if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-5921"]="It was with considerable reluctance that I abandoned in favour of the present undertaking what had long been a favourite project: that of a new edition of Shelton’s “Don Quixote,” which has now become a somewhat scarce book. There are some--and I confess myself to be one--for whom Shelton’s racy old version, with all its defects, has a charm that no modern translation, however skilful or correct, could possess. Shelton had the inestimable advantage of belonging to the same generation as Cervantes; “Don Quixote” had to him a vitality that only a contemporary could feel; it cost him no dramatic effort to see things as Cervantes saw them; there is no anachronism in his language; he put the Spanish of Cervantes into the English of Shakespeare. Shakespeare himself most likely knew the book; he may have carried it home with him in his saddle-bags to Stratford on one of his last journeys, and under the mulberry tree at New Place joined hands with a kindred genius in its pages.\n\nBut it was soon made plain to me that to hope for even a moderate popularity for Shelton was vain. His fine old crusted English would, no doubt, be relished by a minority, but it would be only by a minority. His warmest admirers must admit that he is not a satisfactory representative of Cervantes. His translation of the First Part was very hastily made and was never revised by him. It has all the freshness and vigour, but also a full measure of the faults, of a hasty production. It is often very literal--barbarously literal frequently--but just as often very loose. He had evidently a good colloquial knowledge of Spanish, but apparently not much more. It never seems to occur to him that the same translation of a word will not suit in every case.\n\nIt is often said that we have no satisfactory translation of “Don Quixote.” To those who are familiar with the original, it savours of truism or platitude to say so, for in truth there can be no thoroughly satisfactory translation of “Don Quixote” into English or any other language. It is not that the Spanish idioms are so utterly unmanageable, or that the untranslatable words, numerous enough no doubt, are so superabundant, but rather that the sententious terseness to which the humour of the book owes its flavour is peculiar to Spanish, and can at best be only distantly imitated in any other tongue.\n\nThe history of our English translations of “Don Quixote” is instructive. Shelton’s, the first in any language, was made, apparently, about 1608, but not published till 1612. This of course was only the First Part. It has been asserted that the Second, published in 1620, is not the work of Shelton, but there is nothing to support the assertion save the fact that it has less spirit, less of what we generally understand by “go,” about it than the first, which would be only natural if the first were the work of a young man writing currente calamo, and the second that of a middle-aged man writing for a bookseller. On the other hand, it is closer and more literal, the style is the same, the very same translations, or mistranslations, occur in it, and it is extremely unlikely that a new translator would, by suppressing his name, have allowed Shelton to carry off the credit.\n\nIn 1687 John Phillips, Milton’s nephew, produced a “Don Quixote” “made English,” he says, “according to the humour of our modern language.” His “Quixote” is not so much a translation as a travesty, and a travesty that for coarseness, vulgarity, and buffoonery is almost unexampled even in the literature of that day.\n\nNed Ward’s “Life and Notable Adventures of Don Quixote, merrily translated into Hudibrastic Verse” (1700), can scarcely be reckoned a translation, but it serves to show the light in which “Don Quixote” was regarded at the time.\n\nA further illustration may be found in the version published in 1712 by Peter Motteux, who had then recently combined tea-dealing with literature. It is described as “translated from the original by several hands,” but if so all Spanish flavour has entirely evaporated under the manipulation of the several hands. The flavour that it has, on the other hand, is distinctly Franco-cockney. Anyone who compares it carefully with the original will have little doubt that it is a concoction from Shelton and the French of Filleau de Saint Martin, eked out by borrowings from Phillips, whose mode of treatment it adopts. It is, to be sure, more decent and decorous, but it treats “Don Quixote” in the same fashion as a comic book that cannot be made too comic.\n\nTo attempt to improve the humour of “Don Quixote” by an infusion of cockney flippancy and facetiousness, as Motteux’s operators did, is not merely an impertinence like larding a sirloin of prize beef, but an absolute falsification of the spirit of the book, and it is a proof of the uncritical way in which “Don Quixote” is generally read that this worse than worthless translation--worthless as failing to represent, worse than worthless as misrepresenting--should have been favoured as it has been.\n\nIt had the effect, however, of bringing out a translation undertaken and executed in a very different spirit, that of Charles Jervas, the portrait painter, and friend of Pope, Swift, Arbuthnot, and Gay. Jervas has been allowed little credit for his work, indeed it may be said none, for it is known to the world in general as Jarvis’s. It was not published until after his death, and the printers gave the name according to the current pronunciation of the day. It has been the most freely used and the most freely abused of all the translations. It has seen far more editions than any other, it is admitted on all hands to be by far the most faithful, and yet nobody seems to have a good word to say for it or for its author. Jervas no doubt prejudiced readers against himself in his preface, where among many true words about Shelton, Stevens, and Motteux, he rashly and unjustly charges Shelton with having translated not from the Spanish, but from the Italian version of Franciosini, which did not appear until ten years after Shelton’s first volume. A suspicion of incompetence, too, seems to have attached to him because he was by profession a painter and a mediocre one (though he has given us the best portrait we have of Swift), and this may have been strengthened by Pope’s remark that he “translated ‘Don Quixote’ without understanding Spanish.” He has been also charged with borrowing from Shelton, whom he disparaged. It is true that in a few difficult or obscure passages he has followed Shelton, and gone astray with him; but for one case of this sort, there are fifty where he is right and Shelton wrong. As for Pope’s dictum, anyone who examines Jervas’s version carefully, side by side with the original, will see that he was a sound Spanish scholar, incomparably a better one than Shelton, except perhaps in mere colloquial Spanish. He was, in fact, an honest, faithful, and painstaking translator, and he has left a version which, whatever its shortcomings may be, is singularly free from errors and mistranslations.\n\nThe charge against it is that it is stiff, dry--“wooden” in a word,-and no one can deny that there is a foundation for it. But it may be pleaded for Jervas that a good deal of this rigidity is due to his abhorrence of the light, flippant, jocose style of his predecessors. He was one of the few, very few, translators that have shown any apprehension of the unsmiling gravity which is the essence of Quixotic humour; it seemed to him a crime to bring Cervantes forward smirking and grinning at his own good things, and to this may be attributed in a great measure the ascetic abstinence from everything savouring of liveliness which is the characteristic of his translation. In most modern editions, it should be observed, his style has been smoothed and smartened, but without any reference to the original Spanish, so that if he has been made to read more agreeably he has also been robbed of his chief merit of fidelity.\n\nSmollett’s version, published in 1755, may be almost counted as one of these. At any rate it is plain that in its construction Jervas’s translation was very freely drawn upon, and very little or probably no heed given to the original Spanish.\n\nThe later translations may be dismissed in a few words. George Kelly’s, which appeared in 1769, “printed for the Translator,” was an impudent imposture, being nothing more than Motteux’s version with a few of the words, here and there, artfully transposed; Charles Wilmot’s (1774) was only an abridgment like Florian’s, but not so skilfully executed; and the version published by Miss Smirke in 1818, to accompany her brother’s plates, was merely a patchwork production made out of former translations. On the latest, Mr. A. J. Duffield’s, it would be in every sense of the word impertinent in me to offer an opinion here. I had not even seen it when the present undertaking was proposed to me, and since then I may say vidi tantum, having for obvious reasons resisted the temptation which Mr. Duffield’s reputation and comely volumes hold out to every lover of Cervantes.\n\nFrom the foregoing history of our translations of “Don Quixote,” it will be seen that there are a good many people who, provided they get the mere narrative with its full complement of facts, incidents, and adventures served up to them in a form that amuses them, care very little whether that form is the one in which Cervantes originally shaped his ideas. On the other hand, it is clear that there are many who desire to have not merely the story he tells, but the story as he tells it, so far at least as differences of idiom and circumstances permit, and who will give a preference to the conscientious translator, even though he may have acquitted himself somewhat awkwardly.\n\nBut after all there is no real antagonism between the two classes; there is no reason why what pleases the one should not please the other, or why a translator who makes it his aim to treat “Don Quixote” with the respect due to a great classic, should not be as acceptable even to the careless reader as the one who treats it as a famous old jest-book. It is not a question of caviare to the general, or, if it is, the fault rests with him who makes so. The method by which Cervantes won the ear of the Spanish people ought, mutatis mutandis, to be equally effective with the great majority of English readers. At any rate, even if there are readers to whom it is a matter of indifference, fidelity to the method is as much a part of the translator’s duty as fidelity to the matter. If he can please all parties, so much the better; but his first duty is to those who look to him for as faithful a representation of his author as it is in his power to give them, faithful to the letter so long as fidelity is practicable, faithful to the spirit so far as he can make it.\n\nMy purpose here is not to dogmatise on the rules of translation, but to indicate those I have followed, or at least tried to the best of my ability to follow, in the present instance. One which, it seems to me, cannot be too rigidly followed in translating “Don Quixote,” is to avoid everything that savours of affectation. The book itself is, indeed, in one sense a protest against it, and no man abhorred it more than Cervantes. For this reason, I think, any temptation to use antiquated or obsolete language should be resisted. It is after all an affectation, and one for which there is no warrant or excuse. Spanish has probably undergone less change since the seventeenth century than any language in Europe, and by far the greater and certainly the best part of “Don Quixote” differs but little in language from the colloquial Spanish of the present day. Except in the tales and Don Quixote’s speeches, the translator who uses the simplest and plainest everyday language will almost always be the one who approaches nearest to the original.\n\nSeeing that the story of “Don Quixote” and all its characters and incidents have now been for more than two centuries and a half familiar as household words in English mouths, it seems to me that the old familiar names and phrases should not be changed without good reason. Of course a translator who holds that “Don Quixote” should receive the treatment a great classic deserves, will feel himself bound by the injunction laid upon the Morisco in Chap. IX not to omit or add anything.\n\nFour generations had laughed over “Don Quixote” before it occurred to anyone to ask, who and what manner of man was this Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra whose name is on the title-page; and it was too late for a satisfactory answer to the question when it was proposed to add a life of the author to the London edition published at Lord Carteret’s instance in 1738. All traces of the personality of Cervantes had by that time disappeared. Any floating traditions that may once have existed, transmitted from men who had known him, had long since died out, and of other record there was none; for the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries were incurious as to “the men of the time,” a reproach against which the nineteenth has, at any rate, secured itself, if it has produced no Shakespeare or Cervantes. All that Mayans y Siscar, to whom the task was entrusted, or any of those who followed him, Rios, Pellicer, or Navarrete, could do was to eke out the few allusions Cervantes makes to himself in his various prefaces with such pieces of documentary evidence bearing upon his life as they could find.\n\nThis, however, has been done by the last-named biographer to such good purpose that he has superseded all predecessors. Thoroughness is the chief characteristic of Navarrete’s work. Besides sifting, testing, and methodising with rare patience and judgment what had been previously brought to light, he left, as the saying is, no stone unturned under which anything to illustrate his subject might possibly be found. Navarrete has done all that industry and acumen could do, and it is no fault of his if he has not given us what we want. What Hallam says of Shakespeare may be applied to the almost parallel case of Cervantes: “It is not the register of his baptism, or the draft of his will, or the orthography of his name that we seek; no letter of his writing, no record of his conversation, no character of him drawn ... by a contemporary has been produced.”\n\nIt is only natural, therefore, that the biographers of Cervantes, forced to make brick without straw, should have recourse largely to conjecture, and that conjecture should in some instances come by degrees to take the place of established fact. All that I propose to do here is to separate what is matter of fact from what is matter of conjecture, and leave it to the reader’s judgment to decide whether the data justify the inference or not.\n\nThe men whose names by common consent stand in the front rank of Spanish literature, Cervantes, Lope de Vega, Quevedo, Calderon, Garcilaso de la Vega, the Mendozas, Gongora, were all men of ancient families, and, curiously, all, except the last, of families that traced their origin to the same mountain district in the North of Spain. The family of Cervantes is commonly said to have been of Galician origin, and unquestionably it was in possession of lands in Galicia at a very early date; but I think the balance of the evidence tends to show that the “solar,” the original site of the family, was at Cervatos in the north-west corner of Old Castile, close to the junction of Castile, Leon, and the Asturias. As it happens, there is a complete history of the Cervantes family from the tenth century down to the seventeenth extant under the title of “Illustrious Ancestry, Glorious Deeds, and Noble Posterity of the Famous Nuno Alfonso, Alcaide of Toledo,” written in 1648 by the industrious genealogist Rodrigo Mendez Silva, who availed himself of a manuscript genealogy by Juan de Mena, the poet laureate and historiographer of John II.\n\nThe origin of the name Cervantes is curious. Nuno Alfonso was almost as distinguished in the struggle against the Moors in the reign of Alfonso VII as the Cid had been half a century before in that of Alfonso VI, and was rewarded by divers grants of land in the neighbourhood of Toledo. On one of his acquisitions, about two leagues from the city, he built himself a castle which he called Cervatos, because “he was lord of the solar of Cervatos in the Montana,” as the mountain region extending from the Basque Provinces to Leon was always called. At his death in battle in 1143, the castle passed by his will to his son Alfonso Munio, who, as territorial or local surnames were then coming into vogue in place of the simple patronymic, took the additional name of Cervatos. His eldest son Pedro succeeded him in the possession of the castle, and followed his example in adopting the name, an assumption at which the younger son, Gonzalo, seems to have taken umbrage.\n\nEveryone who has paid even a flying visit to Toledo will remember the ruined castle that crowns the hill above the spot where the bridge of Alcantara spans the gorge of the Tagus, and with its broken outline and crumbling walls makes such an admirable pendant to the square solid Alcazar towering over the city roofs on the opposite side. It was built, or as some say restored, by Alfonso VI shortly after his occupation of Toledo in 1085, and called by him San Servando after a Spanish martyr, a name subsequently modified into San Servan (in which form it appears in the “Poem of the Cid”), San Servantes, and San Cervantes: with regard to which last the “Handbook for Spain” warns its readers against the supposition that it has anything to do with the author of “Don Quixote.” Ford, as all know who have taken him for a companion and counsellor on the roads of Spain, is seldom wrong in matters of literature or history. In this instance, however, he is in error. It has everything to do with the author of “Don Quixote,” for it is in fact these old walls that have given to Spain the name she is proudest of to-day. Gonzalo, above mentioned, it may be readily conceived, did not relish the appropriation by his brother of a name to which he himself had an equal right, for though nominally taken from the castle, it was in reality derived from the ancient territorial possession of the family, and as a set-off, and to distinguish himself (diferenciarse) from his brother, he took as a surname the name of the castle on the bank of the Tagus, in the building of which, according to a family tradition, his great-grandfather had a share.\n\nBoth brothers founded families. The Cervantes branch had more tenacity; it sent offshoots in various directions, Andalusia, Estremadura, Galicia, and Portugal, and produced a goodly line of men distinguished in the service of Church and State. Gonzalo himself, and apparently a son of his, followed Ferdinand III in the great campaign of 1236-48 that gave Cordova and Seville to Christian Spain and penned up the Moors in the kingdom of Granada, and his descendants intermarried with some of the noblest families of the Peninsula and numbered among them soldiers, magistrates, and Church dignitaries, including at least two cardinal-archbishops.\n\nOf the line that settled in Andalusia, Deigo de Cervantes, Commander of the Order of Santiago, married Juana Avellaneda, daughter of Juan Arias de Saavedra, and had several sons, of whom one was Gonzalo Gomez, Corregidor of Jerez and ancestor of the Mexican and Columbian branches of the family; and another, Juan, whose son Rodrigo married Dona Leonor de Cortinas, and by her had four children, Rodrigo, Andrea, Luisa, and Miguel, our author.\n\nThe pedigree of Cervantes is not without its bearing on “Don Quixote.” A man who could look back upon an ancestry of genuine knights-errant extending from well-nigh the time of Pelayo to the siege of Granada was likely to have a strong feeling on the subject of the sham chivalry of the romances. It gives a point, too, to what he says in more than one place about families that have once been great and have tapered away until they have come to nothing, like a pyramid. It was the case of his own.\n\nHe was born at Alcala de Henares and baptised in the church of Santa Maria Mayor on the 9th of October, 1547. Of his boyhood and youth we know nothing, unless it be from the glimpse he gives us in the preface to his “Comedies” of himself as a boy looking on with delight while Lope de Rueda and his company set up their rude plank stage in the plaza and acted the rustic farces which he himself afterwards took as the model of his interludes. This first glimpse, however, is a significant one, for it shows the early development of that love of the drama which exercised such an influence on his life and seems to have grown stronger as he grew older, and of which this very preface, written only a few months before his death, is such a striking proof. He gives us to understand, too, that he was a great reader in his youth; but of this no assurance was needed, for the First Part of “Don Quixote” alone proves a vast amount of miscellaneous reading, romances of chivalry, ballads, popular poetry, chronicles, for which he had no time or opportunity except in the first twenty years of his life; and his misquotations and mistakes in matters of detail are always, it may be noticed, those of a man recalling the reading of his boyhood.\n\nOther things besides the drama were in their infancy when Cervantes was a boy. The period of his boyhood was in every way a transition period for Spain. The old chivalrous Spain had passed away. The new Spain was the mightiest power the world had seen since the Roman Empire and it had not yet been called upon to pay the price of its greatness. By the policy of Ferdinand and Ximenez the sovereign had been made absolute, and the Church and Inquisition adroitly adjusted to keep him so. The nobles, who had always resisted absolutism as strenuously as they had fought the Moors, had been divested of all political power, a like fate had befallen the cities, the free constitutions of Castile and Aragon had been swept away, and the only function that remained to the Cortes was that of granting money at the King’s dictation.\n\nThe transition extended to literature. Men who, like Garcilaso de la Vega and Diego Hurtado de Mendoza, followed the Italian wars, had brought back from Italy the products of the post-Renaissance literature, which took root and flourished and even threatened to extinguish the native growths. Damon and Thyrsis, Phyllis and Chloe had been fairly naturalised in Spain, together with all the devices of pastoral poetry for investing with an air of novelty the idea of a dispairing shepherd and inflexible shepherdess. As a set-off against this, the old historical and traditional ballads, and the true pastorals, the songs and ballads of peasant life, were being collected assiduously and printed in the cancioneros that succeeded one another with increasing rapidity. But the most notable consequence, perhaps, of the spread of printing was the flood of romances of chivalry that had continued to pour from the press ever since Garci Ordonez de Montalvo had resuscitated “Amadis of Gaul” at the beginning of the century.\n\nFor a youth fond of reading, solid or light, there could have been no better spot in Spain than Alcala de Henares in the middle of the sixteenth century. It was then a busy, populous university town, something more than the enterprising rival of Salamanca, and altogether a very different place from the melancholy, silent, deserted Alcala the traveller sees now as he goes from Madrid to Saragossa. Theology and medicine may have been the strong points of the university, but the town itself seems to have inclined rather to the humanities and light literature, and as a producer of books Alcala was already beginning to compete with the older presses of Toledo, Burgos, Salamanca and Seville.\nA pendant to the picture Cervantes has given us of his first playgoings might, no doubt, have been often seen in the streets of Alcala at that time; a bright, eager, tawny-haired boy peering into a book-shop where the latest volumes lay open to tempt the public, wondering, it may be, what that little book with the woodcut of the blind beggar and his boy, that called itself “Vida de Lazarillo de Tormes, segunda impresion,” could be about; or with eyes brimming over with merriment gazing at one of those preposterous portraits of a knight-errant in outrageous panoply and plumes with which the publishers of chivalry romances loved to embellish the title-pages of their folios. If the boy was the father of the man, the sense of the incongruous that was strong at fifty was lively at ten, and some such reflections as these may have been the true genesis of “Don Quixote.”\n\nFor his more solid education, we are told, he went to Salamanca. But why Rodrigo de Cervantes, who was very poor, should have sent his son to a university a hundred and fifty miles away when he had one at his own door, would be a puzzle, if we had any reason for supposing that he did so. The only evidence is a vague statement by Professor Tomas Gonzalez, that he once saw an old entry of the matriculation of a Miguel de Cervantes. This does not appear to have been ever seen again; but even if it had, and if the date corresponded, it would prove nothing, as there were at least two other Miguels born about the middle of the century; one of them, moreover, a Cervantes Saavedra, a cousin, no doubt, who was a source of great embarrassment to the biographers.\n\nThat he was a student neither at Salamanca nor at Alcala is best proved by his own works. No man drew more largely upon experience than he did, and he has nowhere left a single reminiscence of student life-for the “Tia Fingida,” if it be his, is not one--nothing, not even “a college joke,” to show that he remembered days that most men remember best. All that we know positively about his education is that Juan Lopez de Hoyos, a professor of humanities and belles-lettres of some eminence, calls him his “dear and beloved pupil.” This was in a little collection of verses by different hands on the death of Isabel de Valois, second queen of Philip II, published by the professor in 1569, to which Cervantes contributed four pieces, including an elegy, and an epitaph in the form of a sonnet. It is only by a rare chance that a “Lycidas” finds its way into a volume of this sort, and Cervantes was no Milton. His verses are no worse than such things usually are; so much, at least, may be said for them.\n\nBy the time the book appeared he had left Spain, and, as fate ordered it, for twelve years, the most eventful ones of his life. Giulio, afterwards Cardinal, Acquaviva had been sent at the end of 1568 to Philip II by the Pope on a mission, partly of condolence, partly political, and on his return to Rome, which was somewhat brusquely expedited by the King, he took Cervantes with him as his camarero (chamberlain), the office he himself held in the Pope’s household. The post would no doubt have led to advancement at the Papal Court had Cervantes retained it, but in the summer of 1570 he resigned it and enlisted as a private soldier in Captain Diego Urbina’s company, belonging to Don Miguel de Moncada’s regiment, but at that time forming a part of the command of Marc Antony Colonna. What impelled him to this step we know not, whether it was distaste for the career before him, or purely military enthusiasm. It may well have been the latter, for it was a stirring time; the events, however, which led to the alliance between Spain, Venice, and the Pope, against the common enemy, the Porte, and to the victory of the combined fleets at Lepanto, belong rather to the history of Europe than to the life of Cervantes. He was one of those that sailed from Messina, in September 1571, under the command of Don John of Austria; but on the morning of the 7th of October, when the Turkish fleet was sighted, he was lying below ill with fever. At the news that the enemy was in sight he rose, and, in spite of the remonstrances of his comrades and superiors, insisted on taking his post, saying he preferred death in the service of God and the King to health. His galley, the Marquesa, was in the thick of the fight, and before it was over he had received three gunshot wounds, two in the breast and one in the left hand or arm. On the morning after the battle, according to Navarrete, he had an interview with the commander-in-chief, Don John, who was making a personal inspection of the wounded, one result of which was an addition of three crowns to his pay, and another, apparently, the friendship of his general.\n\nHow severely Cervantes was wounded may be inferred from the fact, that with youth, a vigorous frame, and as cheerful and buoyant a temperament as ever invalid had, he was seven months in hospital at Messina before he was discharged. He came out with his left hand permanently disabled; he had lost the use of it, as Mercury told him in the “Viaje del Parnaso” for the greater glory of the right. This, however, did not absolutely unfit him for service, and in April 1572 he joined Manuel Ponce de Leon’s company of Lope de Figueroa’s regiment, in which, it seems probable, his brother Rodrigo was serving, and shared in the operations of the next three years, including the capture of the Goletta and Tunis. Taking advantage of the lull which followed the recapture of these places by the Turks, he obtained leave to return to Spain, and sailed from Naples in September 1575 on board the Sun galley, in company with his brother Rodrigo, Pedro Carrillo de Quesada, late Governor of the Goletta, and some others, and furnished with letters from Don John of Austria and the Duke of Sesa, the Viceroy of Sicily, recommending him to the King for the command of a company, on account of his services; a dono infelice as events proved. On the 26th they fell in with a squadron of Algerine galleys, and after a stout resistance were overpowered and carried into Algiers.\n\nBy means of a ransomed fellow-captive the brothers contrived to inform their family of their condition, and the poor people at Alcala at once strove to raise the ransom money, the father disposing of all he possessed, and the two sisters giving up their marriage portions. But Dali Mami had found on Cervantes the letters addressed to the King by Don John and the Duke of Sesa, and, concluding that his prize must be a person of great consequence, when the money came he refused it scornfully as being altogether insufficient. The owner of Rodrigo, however, was more easily satisfied; ransom was accepted in his case, and it was arranged between the brothers that he should return to Spain and procure a vessel in which he was to come back to Algiers and take off Miguel and as many of their comrades as possible. This was not the first attempt to escape that Cervantes had made. Soon after the commencement of his captivity he induced several of his companions to join him in trying to reach Oran, then a Spanish post, on foot; but after the first day’s journey, the Moor who had agreed to act as their guide deserted them, and they had no choice but to return. The second attempt was more disastrous. In a garden outside the city on the sea-shore, he constructed, with the help of the gardener, a Spaniard, a hiding-place, to which he brought, one by one, fourteen of his fellow-captives, keeping them there in secrecy for several months, and supplying them with food through a renegade known as El Dorador, “the Gilder.” How he, a captive himself, contrived to do all this, is one of the mysteries of the story. Wild as the project may appear, it was very nearly successful. The vessel procured by Rodrigo made its appearance off the coast, and under cover of night was proceeding to take off the refugees, when the crew were alarmed by a passing fishing boat, and beat a hasty retreat. On renewing the attempt shortly afterwards, they, or a portion of them at least, were taken prisoners, and just as the poor fellows in the garden were exulting in the thought that in a few moments more freedom would be within their grasp, they found themselves surrounded by Turkish troops, horse and foot. The Dorador had revealed the whole scheme to the Dey Hassan.\n\nWhen Cervantes saw what had befallen them, he charged his companions to lay all the blame upon him, and as they were being bound he declared aloud that the whole plot was of his contriving, and that nobody else had any share in it. Brought before the Dey, he said the same. He was threatened with impalement and with torture; and as cutting off ears and noses were playful freaks with the Algerines, it may be conceived what their tortures were like; but nothing could make him swerve from his original statement that he and he alone was responsible. The upshot was that the unhappy gardener was hanged by his master, and the prisoners taken possession of by the Dey, who, however, afterwards restored most of them to their masters, but kept Cervantes, paying Dali Mami 500 crowns for him. He felt, no doubt, that a man of such resource, energy, and daring, was too dangerous a piece of property to be left in private hands; and he had him heavily ironed and lodged in his own prison. If he thought that by these means he could break the spirit or shake the resolution of his prisoner, he was soon undeceived, for Cervantes contrived before long to despatch a letter to the Governor of Oran, entreating him to send him some one that could be trusted, to enable him and three other gentlemen, fellow-captives of his, to make their escape; intending evidently to renew his first attempt with a more trustworthy guide. Unfortunately the Moor who carried the letter was stopped just outside Oran, and the letter being found upon him, he was sent back to Algiers, where by the order of the Dey he was promptly impaled as a warning to others, while Cervantes was condemned to receive two thousand blows of the stick, a number which most likely would have deprived the world of “Don Quixote,” had not some persons, who they were we know not, interceded on his behalf.\n\nAfter this he seems to have been kept in still closer confinement than before, for nearly two years passed before he made another attempt. This time his plan was to purchase, by the aid of a Spanish renegade and two Valencian merchants resident in Algiers, an armed vessel in which he and about sixty of the leading captives were to make their escape; but just as they were about to put it into execution one Doctor Juan Blanco de Paz, an ecclesiastic and a compatriot, informed the Dey of the plot. Cervantes by force of character, by his self-devotion, by his untiring energy and his exertions to lighten the lot of his companions in misery, had endeared himself to all, and become the leading spirit in the captive colony, and, incredible as it may seem, jealousy of his influence and the esteem in which he was held, moved this man to compass his destruction by a cruel death. The merchants finding that the Dey knew all, and fearing that Cervantes under torture might make disclosures that would imperil their own lives, tried to persuade him to slip away on board a vessel that was on the point of sailing for Spain; but he told them they had nothing to fear, for no tortures would make him compromise anybody, and he went at once and gave himself up to the Dey.\n\nAs before, the Dey tried to force him to name his accomplices. Everything was made ready for his immediate execution; the halter was put round his neck and his hands tied behind him, but all that could be got from him was that he himself, with the help of four gentlemen who had since left Algiers, had arranged the whole, and that the sixty who were to accompany him were not to know anything of it until the last moment. Finding he could make nothing of him, the Dey sent him back to prison more heavily ironed than before.\n\nThe poverty-stricken Cervantes family had been all this time trying once more to raise the ransom money, and at last a sum of three hundred ducats was got together and entrusted to the Redemptorist Father Juan Gil, who was about to sail for Algiers. The Dey, however, demanded more than double the sum offered, and as his term of office had expired and he was about to sail for Constantinople, taking all his slaves with him, the case of Cervantes was critical. He was already on board heavily ironed, when the Dey at length agreed to reduce his demand by one-half, and Father Gil by borrowing was able to make up the amount, and on September 19, 1580, after a captivity of five years all but a week, Cervantes was at last set free. Before long he discovered that Blanco de Paz, who claimed to be an officer of the Inquisition, was now concocting on false evidence a charge of misconduct to be brought against him on his return to Spain. To checkmate him Cervantes drew up a series of twenty-five questions, covering the whole period of his captivity, upon which he requested Father Gil to take the depositions of credible witnesses before a notary. Eleven witnesses taken from among the principal captives in Algiers deposed to all the facts above stated and to a great deal more besides. There is something touching in the admiration, love, and gratitude we see struggling to find expression in the formal language of the notary, as they testify one after another to the good deeds of Cervantes, how he comforted and helped the weak-hearted, how he kept up their drooping courage, how he shared his poor purse with this deponent, and how “in him this deponent found father and mother.”\n\nOn his return to Spain he found his old regiment about to march for Portugal to support Philip’s claim to the crown, and utterly penniless now, had no choice but to rejoin it. He was in the expeditions to the Azores in 1582 and the following year, and on the conclusion of the war returned to Spain in the autumn of 1583, bringing with him the manuscript of his pastoral romance, the “Galatea,” and probably also, to judge by internal evidence, that of the first portion of “Persiles and Sigismunda.” He also brought back with him, his biographers assert, an infant daughter, the offspring of an amour, as some of them with great circumstantiality inform us, with a Lisbon lady of noble birth, whose name, however, as well as that of the street she lived in, they omit to mention. The sole foundation for all this is that in 1605 there certainly was living in the family of Cervantes a Dona Isabel de Saavedra, who is described in an official document as his natural daughter, and then twenty years of age.\n\nWith his crippled left hand promotion in the army was hopeless, now that Don John was dead and he had no one to press his claims and services, and for a man drawing on to forty life in the ranks was a dismal prospect; he had already a certain reputation as a poet; he made up his mind, therefore, to cast his lot with literature, and for a first venture committed his “Galatea” to the press. It was published, as Salva y Mallen shows conclusively, at Alcala, his own birth-place, in 1585 and no doubt helped to make his name more widely known, but certainly did not do him much good in any other way.\n\nWhile it was going through the press, he married Dona Catalina de Palacios Salazar y Vozmediano, a lady of Esquivias near Madrid, and apparently a friend of the family, who brought him a fortune which may possibly have served to keep the wolf from the door, but if so, that was all. The drama had by this time outgrown market-place stages and strolling companies, and with his old love for it he naturally turned to it for a congenial employment. In about three years he wrote twenty or thirty plays, which he tells us were performed without any throwing of cucumbers or other missiles, and ran their course without any hisses, outcries, or disturbance. In other words, his plays were not bad enough to be hissed off the stage, but not good enough to hold their own upon it. Only two of them have been preserved, but as they happen to be two of the seven or eight he mentions with complacency, we may assume they are favourable specimens, and no one who reads the “Numancia” and the “Trato de Argel” will feel any surprise that they failed as acting dramas. Whatever merits they may have, whatever occasional they may show, they are, as regards construction, incurably clumsy. How completely they failed is manifest from the fact that with all his sanguine temperament and indomitable perseverance he was unable to maintain the struggle to gain a livelihood as a dramatist for more than three years; nor was the rising popularity of Lope the cause, as is often said, notwithstanding his own words to the contrary. When Lope began to write for the stage is uncertain, but it was certainly after Cervantes went to Seville.\n\nAmong the “Nuevos Documentos” printed by Señor Asensio y Toledo is one dated 1592, and curiously characteristic of Cervantes. It is an agreement with one Rodrigo Osorio, a manager, who was to accept six comedies at fifty ducats (about 6l.) apiece, not to be paid in any case unless it appeared on representation that the said comedy was one of the best that had ever been represented in Spain. The test does not seem to have been ever applied; perhaps it was sufficiently apparent to Rodrigo Osorio that the comedies were not among the best that had ever been represented. Among the correspondence of Cervantes there might have been found, no doubt, more than one letter like that we see in the “Rake’s Progress,” “Sir, I have read your play, and it will not doo.”\n\nHe was more successful in a literary contest at Saragossa in 1595 in honour of the canonisation of St. Jacinto, when his composition won the first prize, three silver spoons. The year before this he had been appointed a collector of revenues for the kingdom of Granada. In order to remit the money he had collected more conveniently to the treasury, he entrusted it to a merchant, who failed and absconded; and as the bankrupt’s assets were insufficient to cover the whole, he was sent to prison at Seville in September 1597. The balance against him, however, was a small one, about 26l., and on giving security for it he was released at the end of the year.\n\nIt was as he journeyed from town to town collecting the king’s taxes, that he noted down those bits of inn and wayside life and character that abound in the pages of “Don Quixote:” the Benedictine monks with spectacles and sunshades, mounted on their tall mules; the strollers in costume bound for the next village; the barber with his basin on his head, on his way to bleed a patient; the recruit with his breeches in his bundle, tramping along the road singing; the reapers gathered in the venta gateway listening to “Felixmarte of Hircania” read out to them; and those little Hogarthian touches that he so well knew how to bring in, the ox-tail hanging up with the landlord’s comb stuck in it, the wine-skins at the bed-head, and those notable examples of hostelry art, Helen going off in high spirits on Paris’s arm, and Dido on the tower dropping tears as big as walnuts. Nay, it may well be that on those journeys into remote regions he came across now and then a specimen of the pauper gentleman, with his lean hack and his greyhound and his books of chivalry, dreaming away his life in happy ignorance that the world had changed since his great-grandfather’s old helmet was new. But it was in Seville that he found out his true vocation, though he himself would not by any means have admitted it to be so. It was there, in Triana, that he was first tempted to try his hand at drawing from life, and first brought his humour into play in the exquisite little sketch of “Rinconete y Cortadillo,” the germ, in more ways than one, of “Don Quixote.”\n\nWhere and when that was written, we cannot tell. After his imprisonment all trace of Cervantes in his official capacity disappears, from which it may be inferred that he was not reinstated. That he was still in Seville in November 1598 appears from a satirical sonnet of his on the elaborate catafalque erected to testify the grief of the city at the death of Philip II, but from this up to 1603 we have no clue to his movements. The words in the preface to the First Part of “Don Quixote” are generally held to be conclusive that he conceived the idea of the book, and wrote the beginning of it at least, in a prison, and that he may have done so is extremely likely.\n\nThere is a tradition that Cervantes read some portions of his work to a select audience at the Duke of Bejar’s, which may have helped to make the book known; but the obvious conclusion is that the First Part of “Don Quixote” lay on his hands some time before he could find a publisher bold enough to undertake a venture of so novel a character; and so little faith in it had Francisco Robles of Madrid, to whom at last he sold it, that he did not care to incur the expense of securing the copyright for Aragon or Portugal, contenting himself with that for Castile. The printing was finished in December, and the book came out with the new year, 1605. It is often said that “Don Quixote” was at first received coldly. The facts show just the contrary. No sooner was it in the hands of the public than preparations were made to issue pirated editions at Lisbon and Valencia, and to bring out a second edition with the additional copyrights for Aragon and Portugal, which he secured in February.\n\nNo doubt it was received with something more than coldness by certain sections of the community. Men of wit, taste, and discrimination among the aristocracy gave it a hearty welcome, but the aristocracy in general were not likely to relish a book that turned their favourite reading into ridicule and laughed at so many of their favourite ideas. The dramatists who gathered round Lope as their leader regarded Cervantes as their common enemy, and it is plain that he was equally obnoxious to the other clique, the culto poets who had Gongora for their chief. Navarrete, who knew nothing of the letter above mentioned, tries hard to show that the relations between Cervantes and Lope were of a very friendly sort, as indeed they were until “Don Quixote” was written. Cervantes, indeed, to the last generously and manfully declared his admiration of Lope’s powers, his unfailing invention, and his marvellous fertility; but in the preface of the First Part of “Don Quixote” and in the verses of “Urganda the Unknown,” and one or two other places, there are, if we read between the lines, sly hits at Lope’s vanities and affectations that argue no personal good-will; and Lope openly sneers at “Don Quixote” and Cervantes, and fourteen years after his death gives him only a few lines of cold commonplace in the “Laurel de Apolo,” that seem all the colder for the eulogies of a host of nonentities whose names are found nowhere else.\n\nIn 1601 Valladolid was made the seat of the Court, and at the beginning of 1603 Cervantes had been summoned thither in connection with the balance due by him to the Treasury, which was still outstanding. He remained at Valladolid, apparently supporting himself by agencies and scrivener’s work of some sort; probably drafting petitions and drawing up statements of claims to be presented to the Council, and the like. So, at least, we gather from the depositions taken on the occasion of the death of a gentleman, the victim of a street brawl, who had been carried into the house in which he lived. In these he himself is described as a man who wrote and transacted business, and it appears that his household then consisted of his wife, the natural daughter Isabel de Saavedra already mentioned, his sister Andrea, now a widow, her daughter Constanza, a mysterious Magdalena de Sotomayor calling herself his sister, for whom his biographers cannot account, and a servant-maid.\n\nMeanwhile “Don Quixote” had been growing in favour, and its author’s name was now known beyond the Pyrenees. In 1607 an edition was printed at Brussels. Robles, the Madrid publisher, found it necessary to meet the demand by a third edition, the seventh in all, in 1608. The popularity of the book in Italy was such that a Milan bookseller was led to bring out an edition in 1610; and another was called for in Brussels in 1611. It might naturally have been expected that, with such proofs before him that he had hit the taste of the public, Cervantes would have at once set about redeeming his rather vague promise of a second volume.\n\nBut, to all appearance, nothing was farther from his thoughts. He had still by him one or two short tales of the same vintage as those he had inserted in “Don Quixote” and instead of continuing the adventures of Don Quixote, he set to work to write more of these “Novelas Exemplares” as he afterwards called them, with a view to making a book of them.\n\nThe novels were published in the summer of 1613, with a dedication to the Conde de Lemos, the Maecenas of the day, and with one of those chatty confidential prefaces Cervantes was so fond of. In this, eight years and a half after the First Part of “Don Quixote” had appeared, we get the first hint of a forthcoming Second Part. “You shall see shortly,” he says, “the further exploits of Don Quixote and humours of Sancho Panza.” His idea of “shortly” was a somewhat elastic one, for, as we know by the date to Sancho’s letter, he had barely one-half of the book completed that time twelvemonth.\n\nBut more than poems, or pastorals, or novels, it was his dramatic ambition that engrossed his thoughts. The same indomitable spirit that kept him from despair in the bagnios of Algiers, and prompted him to attempt the escape of himself and his comrades again and again, made him persevere in spite of failure and discouragement in his efforts to win the ear of the public as a dramatist. The temperament of Cervantes was essentially sanguine. The portrait he draws in the preface to the novels, with the aquiline features, chestnut hair, smooth untroubled forehead, and bright cheerful eyes, is the very portrait of a sanguine man. Nothing that the managers might say could persuade him that the merits of his plays would not be recognised at last if they were only given a fair chance. The old soldier of the Spanish Salamis was bent on being the Aeschylus of Spain. He was to found a great national drama, based on the true principles of art, that was to be the envy of all nations; he was to drive from the stage the silly, childish plays, the “mirrors of nonsense and models of folly” that were in vogue through the cupidity of the managers and shortsightedness of the authors; he was to correct and educate the public taste until it was ripe for tragedies on the model of the Greek drama--like the “Numancia” for instance--and comedies that would not only amuse but improve and instruct. All this he was to do, could he once get a hearing: there was the initial difficulty.\n\nHe shows plainly enough, too, that “Don Quixote” and the demolition of the chivalry romances was not the work that lay next his heart. He was, indeed, as he says himself in his preface, more a stepfather than a father to “Don Quixote.” Never was great work so neglected by its author. That it was written carelessly, hastily, and by fits and starts, was not always his fault, but it seems clear he never read what he sent to the press. He knew how the printers had blundered, but he never took the trouble to correct them when the third edition was in progress, as a man who really cared for the child of his brain would have done. He appears to have regarded the book as little more than a mere libro de entretenimiento, an amusing book, a thing, as he says in the “Viaje,” “to divert the melancholy moody heart at any time or season.” No doubt he had an affection for his hero, and was very proud of Sancho Panza. It would have been strange indeed if he had not been proud of the most humorous creation in all fiction. He was proud, too, of the popularity and success of the book, and beyond measure delightful is the naivete with which he shows his pride in a dozen passages in the Second Part. But it was not the success he coveted. In all probability he would have given all the success of “Don Quixote,” nay, would have seen every copy of “Don Quixote” burned in the Plaza Mayor, for one such success as Lope de Vega was enjoying on an average once a week.\n\nAnd so he went on, dawdling over “Don Quixote,” adding a chapter now and again, and putting it aside to turn to “Persiles and Sigismunda”--which, as we know, was to be the most entertaining book in the language, and the rival of “Theagenes and Chariclea”--or finishing off one of his darling comedies; and if Robles asked when “Don Quixote” would be ready, the answer no doubt was: En breve-shortly, there was time enough for that. At sixty-eight he was as full of life and hope and plans for the future as a\n\nboy of eighteen.\nNemesis was coming, however. He had got as far as Chapter LIX, which at his leisurely pace he could hardly have reached before October or November 1614, when there was put into his hand a small octave lately printed at Tarragona, and calling itself “Second Volume of the Ingenious Gentleman Don Quixote of La Mancha: by the Licentiate Alonso Fernandez de Avellaneda of Tordesillas.” The last half of Chapter LIX and most of the following chapters of the Second Part give us some idea of the effect produced upon him, and his irritation was not likely to be lessened by the reflection that he had no one to blame but himself. Had Avellaneda, in fact, been content with merely bringing out a continuation to “Don Quixote,” Cervantes would have had no reasonable grievance. His own intentions were expressed in the very vaguest language at the end of the book; nay, in his last words, “forse altro cantera con miglior plettro,” he seems actually to invite some one else to continue the work, and he made no sign until eight years and a half had gone by; by which time Avellaneda’s volume was no doubt written.\n\nIn fact Cervantes had no case, or a very bad one, as far as the mere continuation was concerned. But Avellaneda chose to write a preface to it, full of such coarse personal abuse as only an ill-conditioned man could pour out. He taunts Cervantes with being old, with having lost his hand, with having been in prison, with being poor, with being friendless, accuses him of envy of Lope’s success, of petulance and querulousness, and so on; and it was in this that the sting lay. Avellaneda’s reason for this personal attack is obvious enough. Whoever he may have been, it is clear that he was one of the dramatists of Lope’s school, for he has the impudence to charge Cervantes with attacking him as well as Lope in his criticism on the drama. His identification has exercised the best critics and baffled all the ingenuity and research that has been brought to bear on it. Navarrete and Ticknor both incline to the belief that Cervantes knew who he was; but I must say I think the anger he shows suggests an invisible assailant; it is like the irritation of a man stung by a mosquito in the dark. Cervantes from certain solecisms of language pronounces him to be an Aragonese, and Pellicer, an Aragonese himself, supports this view and believes him, moreover, to have been an ecclesiastic, a Dominican probably.\n\nAny merit Avellaneda has is reflected from Cervantes, and he is too dull to reflect much. “Dull and dirty” will always be, I imagine, the verdict of the vast majority of unprejudiced readers. He is, at best, a poor plagiarist; all he can do is to follow slavishly the lead given him by Cervantes; his only humour lies in making Don Quixote take inns for castles and fancy himself some legendary or historical personage, and Sancho mistake words, invert proverbs, and display his gluttony; all through he shows a proclivity to coarseness and dirt, and he has contrived to introduce two tales filthier than anything by the sixteenth century novellieri and without their sprightliness.\n\nBut whatever Avellaneda and his book may be, we must not forget the debt we owe them. But for them, there can be no doubt, “Don Quixote” would have come to us a mere torso instead of a complete work. Even if Cervantes had finished the volume he had in hand, most assuredly he would have left off with a promise of a Third Part, giving the further adventures of Don Quixote and humours of Sancho Panza as shepherds. It is plain that he had at one time an intention of dealing with the pastoral romances as he had dealt with the books of chivalry, and but for Avellaneda he would have tried to carry it out. But it is more likely that, with his plans, and projects, and hopefulness, the volume would have remained unfinished till his death, and that we should have never made the acquaintance of the Duke and Duchess, or gone with Sancho to Barataria.\n\nFrom the moment the book came into his hands he seems to have been haunted by the fear that there might be more Avellanedas in the field, and putting everything else aside, he set himself to finish off his task and protect Don Quixote in the only way he could, by killing him. The conclusion is no doubt a hasty and in some places clumsy piece of work and the frequent repetition of the scolding administered to Avellaneda becomes in the end rather wearisome; but it is, at any rate, a conclusion and for that we must thank Avellaneda.\n\nThe new volume was ready for the press in February, but was not printed till the very end of 1615, and during the interval Cervantes put together the comedies and interludes he had written within the last few years, and, as he adds plaintively, found no demand for among the managers, and published them with a preface, worth the book it introduces tenfold, in which he gives an account of the early Spanish stage, and of his own attempts as a dramatist. It is needless to say they were put forward by Cervantes in all good faith and full confidence in their merits. The reader, however, was not to suppose they were his last word or final effort in the drama, for he had in hand a comedy called “Engano a los ojos,” about which, if he mistook not, there would be no question.\n\nOf this dramatic masterpiece the world has no opportunity of judging; his health had been failing for some time, and he died, apparently of dropsy, on the 23rd of April, 1616, the day on which England lost Shakespeare, nominally at least, for the English calendar had not yet been reformed. He died as he had lived, accepting his lot bravely and cheerfully.\n\nWas it an unhappy life, that of Cervantes? His biographers all tell us that it was; but I must say I doubt it. It was a hard life, a life of poverty, of incessant struggle, of toil ill paid, of disappointment, but Cervantes carried within himself the antidote to all these evils. His was not one of those light natures that rise above adversity merely by virtue of their own buoyancy; it was in the fortitude of a high spirit that he was proof against it. It is impossible to conceive Cervantes giving way to despondency or prostrated by dejection. As for poverty, it was with him a thing to be laughed over, and the only sigh he ever allows to escape him is when he says, “Happy he to whom Heaven has given a piece of bread for which he is not bound to give thanks to any but Heaven itself.” Add to all this his vital energy and mental activity, his restless invention and his sanguine temperament, and there will be reason enough to doubt whether his could have been a very unhappy life. He who could take Cervantes’ distresses together with his apparatus for enduring them would not make so bad a bargain, perhaps, as far as happiness in life is concerned.\n\nOf his burial-place nothing is known except that he was buried, in accordance with his will, in the neighbouring convent of Trinitarian nuns, of which it is supposed his daughter, Isabel de Saavedra, was an inmate, and that a few years afterwards the nuns removed to another convent, carrying their dead with them. But whether the remains of Cervantes were included in the removal or not no one knows, and the clue to their resting-place is now lost beyond all hope. This furnishes perhaps the least defensible of the items in the charge of neglect brought against his contemporaries. In some of the others there is a good deal of exaggeration. To listen to most of his biographers one would suppose that all Spain was in league not only against the man but against his memory, or at least that it was insensible to his merits, and left him to live in misery and die of want. To talk of his hard life and unworthy employments in Andalusia is absurd. What had he done to distinguish him from thousands of other struggling men earning a precarious livelihood? True, he was a gallant soldier, who had been wounded and had undergone captivity and suffering in his country’s cause, but there were hundreds of others in the same case. He had written a mediocre specimen of an insipid class of romance, and some plays which manifestly did not comply with the primary condition of pleasing: were the playgoers to patronise plays that did not amuse them, because the author was to produce “Don Quixote” twenty years afterwards?\n\nThe scramble for copies which, as we have seen, followed immediately on the appearance of the book, does not look like general insensibility to its merits. No doubt it was received coldly by some, but if a man writes a book in ridicule of periwigs he must make his account with being coldly received by the periwig wearers and hated by the whole tribe of wigmakers. If Cervantes had the chivalry-romance readers, the sentimentalists, the dramatists, and the poets of the period all against him, it was because “Don Quixote” was what it was; and if the general public did not come forward to make him comfortable for the rest of his days, it is no more to be charged with neglect and ingratitude than the English-speaking public that did not pay off Scott’s liabilities. It did the best it could; it read his book and liked it and bought it, and encouraged the bookseller to pay him well for others.\n\nIt has been also made a reproach to Spain that she has erected no monument to the man she is proudest of; no monument, that is to say, of him; for the bronze statue in the little garden of the Plaza de las Cortes, a fair work of art no doubt, and unexceptionable had it been set up to the local poet in the market-place of some provincial town, is not worthy of Cervantes or of Madrid. But what need has Cervantes of “such weak witness of his name;” or what could a monument do in his case except testify to the self-glorification of those who had put it up? Si monumentum quoeris, circumspice. The nearest bookseller’s shop will show what bathos there would be in a monument to the author of “Don Quixote.”\n\nNine editions of the First Part of “Don Quixote” had already appeared before Cervantes died, thirty thousand copies in all, according to his own estimate, and a tenth was printed at Barcelona the year after his death. So large a number naturally supplied the demand for some time, but by 1634 it appears to have been exhausted; and from that time down to the present day the stream of editions has continued to flow rapidly and regularly. The translations show still more clearly in what request the book has been from the very outset. In seven years from the completion of the work it had been translated into the four leading languages of Europe. Except the Bible, in fact, no book has been so widely diffused as “Don Quixote.” The “Imitatio Christi” may have been translated into as many different languages, and perhaps “Robinson Crusoe” and the “Vicar of Wakefield” into nearly as many, but in multiplicity of translations and editions “Don Quixote” leaves them all far behind.\n\nStill more remarkable is the character of this wide diffusion. “Don Quixote” has been thoroughly naturalised among people whose ideas about knight-errantry, if they had any at all, were of the vaguest, who had never seen or heard of a book of chivalry, who could not possibly feel the humour of the burlesque or sympathise with the author’s purpose. Another curious fact is that this, the most cosmopolitan book in the world, is one of the most intensely national. “Manon Lescaut” is not more thoroughly French, “Tom Jones” not more English, “Rob Roy” not more Scotch, than “Don Quixote” is Spanish, in character, in ideas, in sentiment, in local colour, in everything. What, then, is the secret of this unparalleled popularity, increasing year by year for well-nigh three centuries? One explanation, no doubt, is that of all the books in the world, “Don Quixote” is the most catholic. There is something in it for every sort of reader, young or old, sage or simple, high or low. As Cervantes himself says with a touch of pride, “It is thumbed and read and got by heart by people of all sorts; the children turn its leaves, the young people read it, the grown men understand it, the old folk praise it.”\n\nBut it would be idle to deny that the ingredient which, more than its humour, or its wisdom, or the fertility of invention or knowledge of human nature it displays, has insured its success with the multitude, is the vein of farce that runs through it. It was the attack upon the sheep, the battle with the wine-skins, Mambrino’s helmet, the balsam of Fierabras, Don Quixote knocked over by the sails of the windmill, Sancho tossed in the blanket, the mishaps and misadventures of master and man, that were originally the great attraction, and perhaps are so still to some extent with the majority of readers. It is plain that “Don Quixote” was generally regarded at first, and indeed in Spain for a long time, as little more than a queer droll book, full of laughable incidents and absurd situations, very amusing, but not entitled to much consideration or care. All the editions printed in Spain from 1637 to 1771, when the famous printer Ibarra took it up, were mere trade editions, badly and carelessly printed on vile paper and got up in the style of chap-books intended only for popular use, with, in most instances, uncouth illustrations and clap-trap additions by the publisher.\n\nTo England belongs the credit of having been the first country to recognise the right of “Don Quixote” to better treatment than this. The London edition of 1738, commonly called Lord Carteret’s from having been suggested by him, was not a mere edition de luxe. It produced “Don Quixote” in becoming form as regards paper and type, and embellished with plates which, if not particularly happy as illustrations, were at least well intentioned and well executed, but it also aimed at correctness of text, a matter to which nobody except the editors of the Valencia and Brussels editions had given even a passing thought; and for a first attempt it was fairly successful, for though some of its emendations are inadmissible, a good many of them have been adopted by all subsequent editors.\n\nThe zeal of publishers, editors, and annotators brought about a remarkable change of sentiment with regard to “Don Quixote.” A vast number of its admirers began to grow ashamed of laughing over it. It became almost a crime to treat it as a humorous book. The humour was not entirely denied, but, according to the new view, it was rated as an altogether secondary quality, a mere accessory, nothing more than the stalking-horse under the presentation of which Cervantes shot his philosophy or his satire, or whatever it was he meant to shoot; for on this point opinions varied. All were agreed, however, that the object he aimed at was not the books of chivalry. He said emphatically in the preface to the First Part and in the last sentence of the Second, that he had no other object in view than to discredit these books, and this, to advanced criticism, made it clear that his object must have been something else.\n\nOne theory was that the book was a kind of allegory, setting forth the eternal struggle between the ideal and the real, between the spirit of poetry and the spirit of prose; and perhaps German philosophy never evolved a more ungainly or unlikely camel out of the depths of its inner consciousness. Something of the antagonism, no doubt, is to be found in “Don Quixote,” because it is to be found everywhere in life, and Cervantes drew from life. It is difficult to imagine a community in which the never-ceasing game of cross-purposes between Sancho Panza and Don Quixote would not be recognized as true to nature. In the stone age, among the lake dwellers, among the cave men, there were Don Quixotes and Sancho Panzas; there must have been the troglodyte who never could see the facts before his eyes, and the troglodyte who could see nothing else. But to suppose Cervantes deliberately setting himself to expound any such idea in two stout quarto volumes is to suppose something not only very unlike the age in which he lived, but altogether unlike Cervantes himself, who would have been the first to laugh at an attempt of the sort made by anyone else.\n\nThe extraordinary influence of the romances of chivalry in his day is quite enough to account for the genesis of the book. Some idea of the prodigious development of this branch of literature in the sixteenth century may be obtained from the scrutiny of Chapter VII, if the reader bears in mind that only a portion of the romances belonging to by far the largest group are enumerated. As to its effect upon the nation, there is abundant evidence. From the time when the Amadises and Palmerins began to grow popular down to the very end of the century, there is a steady stream of invective, from men whose character and position lend weight to their words, against the romances of chivalry and the infatuation of their readers. Ridicule was the only besom to sweep away that dust.\n\nThat this was the task Cervantes set himself, and that he had ample provocation to urge him to it, will be sufficiently clear to those who look into the evidence; as it will be also that it was not chivalry itself that he attacked and swept away. Of all the absurdities that, thanks to poetry, will be repeated to the end of time, there is no greater one than saying that “Cervantes smiled Spain’s chivalry away.” In the first place there was no chivalry for him to smile away. Spain’s chivalry had been dead for more than a century. Its work was done when Granada fell, and as chivalry was essentially republican in its nature, it could not live under the rule that Ferdinand substituted for the free institutions of mediaeval Spain. What he did smile away was not chivalry but a degrading mockery of it.\n\nThe true nature of the “right arm” and the “bright array,” before which, according to the poet, “the world gave ground,” and which Cervantes’ single laugh demolished, may be gathered from the words of one of his own countrymen, Don Felix Pacheco, as reported by Captain George Carleton, in his “Military Memoirs from 1672 to 1713.” “Before the appearance in the world of that labour of Cervantes,” he said, “it was next to an impossibility for a man to walk the streets with any delight or without danger. There were seen so many cavaliers prancing and curvetting before the windows of their mistresses, that a stranger would have imagined the whole nation to have been nothing less than a race of knight-errants. But after the world became a little acquainted with that notable history, the man that was seen in that once celebrated drapery was pointed at as a Don Quixote, and found himself the jest of high and low. And I verily believe that to this, and this only, we owe that dampness and poverty of spirit which has run through all our councils for a century past, so little agreeable to those nobler actions of our famous ancestors.”\n\nTo call “Don Quixote” a sad book, preaching a pessimist view of life, argues a total misconception of its drift. It would be so if its moral were that, in this world, true enthusiasm naturally leads to ridicule and discomfiture. But it preaches nothing of the sort; its moral, so far as it can be said to have one, is that the spurious enthusiasm that is born of vanity and self-conceit, that is made an end in itself, not a means to an end, that acts on mere impulse, regardless of circumstances and consequences, is mischievous to its owner, and a very considerable nuisance to the community at large. To those who cannot distinguish between the one kind and the other, no doubt “Don Quixote” is a sad book; no doubt to some minds it is very sad that a man who had just uttered so beautiful a sentiment as that “it is a hard case to make slaves of those whom God and Nature made free,” should be ungratefully pelted by the scoundrels his crazy philanthropy had let loose on society; but to others of a more judicial cast it will be a matter of regret that reckless self-sufficient enthusiasm is not oftener requited in some such way for all the mischief it does in the world.\n\nA very slight examination of the structure of “Don Quixote” will suffice to show that Cervantes had no deep design or elaborate plan in his mind when he began the book. When he wrote those lines in which “with a few strokes of a great master he sets before us the pauper gentleman,” he had no idea of the goal to which his imagination was leading him. There can be little doubt that all he contemplated was a short tale to range with those he had already written, a tale setting forth the ludicrous results that might be expected to follow the attempt of a crazy gentleman to act the part of a knight-errant in modern life.\n\nIt is plain, for one thing, that Sancho Panza did not enter into the original scheme, for had Cervantes thought of him he certainly would not have omitted him in his hero’s outfit, which he obviously meant to be complete. Him we owe to the landlord’s chance remark in Chapter III that knights seldom travelled without squires. To try to think of a Don Quixote without Sancho Panza is like trying to think of a one-bladed pair of scissors.\n\nThe story was written at first, like the others, without any division and without the intervention of Cide Hamete Benengeli; and it seems not unlikely that Cervantes had some intention of bringing Dulcinea, or Aldonza Lorenzo, on the scene in person. It was probably the ransacking of the Don’s library and the discussion on the books of chivalry that first suggested it to him that his idea was capable of development. What, if instead of a mere string of farcical misadventures, he were to make his tale a burlesque of one of these books, caricaturing their style, incidents, and spirit?\n\nIn pursuance of this change of plan, he hastily and somewhat clumsily divided what he had written into chapters on the model of “Amadis,” invented the fable of a mysterious Arabic manuscript, and set up Cide Hamete Benengeli in imitation of the almost invariable practice of the chivalry-romance authors, who were fond of tracing their books to some recondite source. In working out the new ideas, he soon found the value of Sancho Panza. Indeed, the keynote, not only to Sancho’s part, but to the whole book, is struck in the first words Sancho utters when he announces his intention of taking his ass with him. “About the ass,” we are told, “Don Quixote hesitated a little, trying whether he could call to mind any knight-errant taking with him an esquire mounted on ass-back; but no instance occurred to his memory.” We can see the whole scene at a glance, the stolid unconsciousness of Sancho and the perplexity of his master, upon whose perception the incongruity has just forced itself. This is Sancho’s mission throughout the book; he is an unconscious Mephistopheles, always unwittingly making mockery of his master’s aspirations, always exposing the fallacy of his ideas by some unintentional ad absurdum, always bringing him back to the world of fact and commonplace by force of sheer stolidity.\n\nBy the time Cervantes had got his volume of novels off his hands, and summoned up resolution enough to set about the Second Part in earnest, the case was very much altered. Don Quixote and Sancho Panza had not merely found favour, but had already become, what they have never since ceased to be, veritable entities to the popular imagination. There was no occasion for him now to interpolate extraneous matter; nay, his readers told him plainly that what they wanted of him was more Don Quixote and more Sancho Panza, and not novels, tales, or digressions. To himself, too, his creations had become realities, and he had become proud of them, especially of Sancho. He began the Second Part, therefore, under very different conditions, and the difference makes itself manifest at once. Even in translation the style will be seen to be far easier, more flowing, more natural, and more like that of a man sure of himself and of his audience. Don Quixote and Sancho undergo a change also. In the First\n\nPart, Don Quixote has no character or individuality whatever. He is nothing more than a crazy representative of the sentiments of the chivalry romances. In all that he says and does he is simply repeating the lesson he has learned from his books; and therefore, it is absurd to speak of him in the gushing strain of the sentimental critics when they dilate upon his nobleness, disinterestedness, dauntless courage, and so forth. It was the business of a knight-errant to right wrongs, redress injuries, and succour the distressed, and this, as a matter of course, he makes his business when he takes up the part; a knight-errant was bound to be intrepid, and so he feels bound to cast fear aside. Of all Byron’s melodious nonsense about Don Quixote, the most nonsensical statement is that “‘t is his virtue makes him mad!” The exact opposite is the truth; it is his madness makes him virtuous.\n\nIn the Second Part, Cervantes repeatedly reminds the reader, as if it was a point upon which he was anxious there should be no mistake, that his hero’s madness is strictly confined to delusions on the subject of chivalry, and that on every other subject he is discreto, one, in fact, whose faculty of discernment is in perfect order. The advantage of this is that he is enabled to make use of Don Quixote as a mouthpiece for his own reflections, and so, without seeming to digress, allow himself the relief of digression when he requires it, as freely as in a commonplace book.\n\nIt is true the amount of individuality bestowed upon Don Quixote is not very great. There are some natural touches of character about him, such as his mixture of irascibility and placability, and his curious affection for Sancho together with his impatience of the squire’s loquacity and impertinence; but in the main, apart from his craze, he is little more than a thoughtful, cultured gentleman, with instinctive good taste and a great deal of shrewdness and originality of mind.\n\nAs to Sancho, it is plain, from the concluding words of the preface to the First Part, that he was a favourite with his creator even before he had been taken into favour by the public. An inferior genius, taking him in hand a second time, would very likely have tried to improve him by making him more comical, clever, amiable, or virtuous. But Cervantes was too true an artist to spoil his work in this way. Sancho, when he reappears, is the old Sancho with the old familiar features; but with a difference; they have been brought out more distinctly, but at the same time with a careful avoidance of anything like caricature; the outline has been filled in where filling in was necessary, and, vivified by a few touches of a master’s hand, Sancho stands before us as he might in a character portrait by Velazquez. He is a much more important and prominent figure in the Second Part than in the First; indeed, it is his matchless mendacity about Dulcinea that to a great extent supplies the action of the story.\n\nHis development in this respect is as remarkable as in any other. In the First Part he displays a great natural gift of lying. His lies are not of the highly imaginative sort that liars in fiction commonly indulge in; like Falstaff’s, they resemble the father that begets them; they are simple, homely, plump lies; plain working lies, in short. But in the service of such a master as Don Quixote he develops rapidly, as we see when he comes to palm off the three country wenches as Dulcinea and her ladies in waiting. It is worth noticing how, flushed by his success in this instance, he is tempted afterwards to try a flight beyond his powers in his account of the journey on Clavileno.\n\nIn the Second Part it is the spirit rather than the incidents of the chivalry romances that is the subject of the burlesque. Enchantments of the sort travestied in those of Dulcinea and the Trifaldi and the cave of Montesinos play a leading part in the later and inferior romances, and another distinguishing feature is caricatured in Don Quixote’s blind adoration of Dulcinea. In the romances of chivalry love is either a mere animalism or a fantastic idolatry. Only a coarse-minded man would care to make merry with the former, but to one of Cervantes’ humour the latter was naturally an attractive subject for ridicule. Like everything else in these romances, it is a gross exaggeration of the real sentiment of chivalry, but its peculiar extravagance is probably due to the influence of those masters of hyperbole, the Provencal poets. When a troubadour professed his readiness to obey his lady in all things, he made it incumbent upon the next comer, if he wished to avoid the imputation of tameness and commonplace, to declare himself the slave of her will, which the next was compelled to cap by some still stronger declaration; and so expressions of devotion went on rising one above the other like biddings at an auction, and a conventional language of gallantry and theory of love came into being that in time permeated the literature of Southern Europe, and bore fruit, in one direction in the transcendental worship of Beatrice and Laura, and in another in the grotesque idolatry which found exponents in writers like Feliciano de Silva. This is what Cervantes deals with in Don Quixote’s passion for Dulcinea, and in no instance has he carried out the burlesque more happily. By keeping Dulcinea in the background, and making her a vague shadowy being of whose very existence we are left in doubt, he invests Don Quixote’s worship of her virtues and charms with an additional extravagance, and gives still more point to the caricature of the sentiment and language of the romances.\n\nOne of the great merits of “Don Quixote,” and one of the qualities that have secured its acceptance by all classes of readers and made it the most cosmopolitan of books, is its simplicity. There are, of course, points obvious enough to a Spanish seventeenth century audience which do not immediately strike a reader now-a-days, and Cervantes often takes it for granted that an allusion will be generally understood which is only intelligible to a few. For example, on many of his readers in Spain, and most of his readers out of it, the significance of his choice of a country for his hero is completely lost. It would be going too far to say that no one can thoroughly comprehend “Don Quixote” without having seen La Mancha, but undoubtedly even a glimpse of La Mancha will give an insight into the meaning of Cervantes such as no commentator can give. Of all the regions of Spain it is the last that would suggest the idea of romance. Of all the dull central plateau of the Peninsula it is the dullest tract. There is something impressive about the grim solitudes of Estremadura; and if the plains of Leon and Old Castile are bald and dreary, they are studded with old cities renowned in history and rich in relics of the past. But there is no redeeming feature in the Manchegan landscape; it has all the sameness of the desert without its dignity; the few towns and villages that break its monotony are mean and commonplace, there is nothing venerable about them, they have not even the picturesqueness of poverty; indeed, Don Quixote’s own village, Argamasilla, has a sort of oppressive respectability in the prim regularity of its streets and houses; everything is ignoble; the very windmills are the ugliest and shabbiest of the windmill kind.\n\nTo anyone who knew the country well, the mere style and title of “Don Quixote of La Mancha” gave the key to the author’s meaning at once. La Mancha as the knight’s country and scene of his chivalries is of a piece with the pasteboard helmet, the farm-labourer on ass-back for a squire, knighthood conferred by a rascally ventero, convicts taken for victims of oppression, and the rest of the incongruities between Don Quixote’s world and the world he lived in, between things as he saw them and things as they were.\nIt is strange that this element of incongruity, underlying the whole humour and purpose of the book, should have been so little heeded by the majority of those who have undertaken to interpret “Don Quixote.” It has been completely overlooked, for example, by the illustrators. To be sure, the great majority of the artists who illustrated “Don Quixote” knew nothing whatever of Spain. To them a venta conveyed no idea but the abstract one of a roadside inn, and they could not therefore do full justice to the humour of Don Quixote’s misconception in taking it for a castle, or perceive the remoteness of all its realities from his ideal. But even when better informed they seem to have no apprehension of the full force of the discrepancy. Take, for instance, Gustave Dore’s drawing of Don Quixote watching his armour in the inn-yard. Whether or not the Venta de Quesada on the Seville road is, as tradition maintains, the inn described in “Don Quixote,” beyond all question it was just such an inn-yard as the one behind it that Cervantes had in his mind’s eye, and it was on just such a rude stone trough as that beside the primitive draw-well in the corner that he meant Don Quixote to deposit his armour. Gustave Dore makes it an elaborate fountain such as no arriero ever watered his mules at in the corral of any venta in Spain, and thereby entirely misses the point aimed at by Cervantes. It is the mean, prosaic, commonplace character of all the surroundings and circumstances that gives a significance to Don Quixote’s vigil and the ceremony that follows.\n\nCervantes’ humour is for the most part of that broader and simpler sort, the strength of which lies in the perception of the incongruous. It is the incongruity of Sancho in all his ways, words, and works, with the ideas and aims of his master, quite as much as the wonderful vitality and truth to nature of the character, that makes him the most humorous creation in the whole range of fiction. That unsmiling gravity of which Cervantes was the first great master, “Cervantes’ serious air,” which sits naturally on Swift alone, perhaps, of later humourists, is essential to this kind of humour, and here again Cervantes has suffered at the hands of his interpreters. Nothing, unless indeed the coarse buffoonery of Phillips, could be more out of place in an attempt to represent Cervantes, than a flippant, would-be facetious style, like that of Motteux’s version for example, or the sprightly, jaunty air, French translators sometimes adopt. It is the grave matter-of-factness of the narrative, and the apparent unconsciousness of the author that he is saying anything ludicrous, anything but the merest commonplace, that give its peculiar flavour to the humour of Cervantes. His, in fact, is the exact opposite of the humour of Sterne and the self-conscious humourists. Even when Uncle Toby is at his best, you are always aware of “the man Sterne” behind him, watching you over his shoulder to see what effect he is producing. Cervantes always leaves you alone with Don Quixote and Sancho. He and Swift and the great humourists always keep themselves out of sight, or, more properly speaking, never think about themselves at all, unlike our latter-day school of humourists, who seem to have revived the old horse-collar method, and try to raise a laugh by some grotesque assumption of ignorance, imbecility, or bad taste.\n\nIt is true that to do full justice to Spanish humour in any other language is well-nigh an impossibility. There is a natural gravity and a sonorous stateliness about Spanish, be it ever so colloquial, that make an absurdity doubly absurd, and give plausibility to the most preposterous statement. This is what makes Sancho Panza’s drollery the despair of the conscientious translator. Sancho’s curt comments can never fall flat, but they lose half their flavour when transferred from their native Castilian into any other medium. But if foreigners have failed to do justice to the humour of Cervantes, they are no worse than his own countrymen. Indeed, were it not for the Spanish peasant’s relish of “Don Quixote,” one might be tempted to think that the great humourist was not looked upon as a humourist at all in his own country.\n\nThe craze of Don Quixote seems, in some instances, to have communicated itself to his critics, making them see things that are not in the book and run full tilt at phantoms that have no existence save in their own imaginations. Like a good many critics now-a-days, they forget that screams are not criticism, and that it is only vulgar tastes that are influenced by strings of superlatives, three-piled hyperboles, and pompous epithets. But what strikes one as particularly strange is that while they deal in extravagant eulogies, and ascribe all manner of imaginary ideas and qualities to Cervantes, they show no perception of the quality that ninety-nine out of a hundred of his readers would rate highest in him, and hold to be the one that raises him above all rivalry.\n\nTo speak of “Don Quixote” as if it were merely a humorous book would be a manifest misdescription. Cervantes at times makes it a kind of commonplace book for occasional essays and criticisms, or for the observations and reflections and gathered wisdom of a long and stirring life. It is a mine of shrewd observation on mankind and human nature. Among modern novels there may be, here and there, more elaborate studies of character, but there is no book richer in individualised character. What Coleridge said of Shakespeare in minimis is true of Cervantes; he never, even for the most temporary purpose, puts forward a lay figure. There is life and individuality in all his characters, however little they may have to do, or however short a time they may be before the reader. Samson Carrasco, the curate, Teresa Panza, Altisidora, even the two students met on the road to the cave of Montesinos, all live and move and have their being; and it is characteristic of the broad humanity of Cervantes that there is not a hateful one among them all. Even poor Maritornes, with her deplorable morals, has a kind heart of her own and “some faint and distant resemblance to a Christian about her;” and as for Sancho, though on dissection we fail to find a lovable trait in him, unless it be a sort of dog-like affection for his master, who is there that in his heart does not love him?\n\nBut it is, after all, the humour of “Don Quixote” that distinguishes it from all other books of the romance kind. It is this that makes it, as one of the most judicial-minded of modern critics calls it, “the best novel in the world beyond all comparison.” It is its varied humour, ranging from broad farce to comedy as subtle as Shakespeare’s or Moliere’s that has naturalised it in every country where there are readers, and made it a classic in every language that has a literature.\n\nTo the book of Don Quixote of la Mancha\n\nIf to be welcomed by the good, O Book! thou make thy steady aim, No empty chatterer will dare To question or dispute thy claim. But if perchance thou hast a mind To win of idiots approbation, Lost labour will be thy reward, Though they’ll pretend appreciation.\n\nThey say a goodly shade he finds Who shelters ’neath a goodly tree; And such a one thy kindly star In Bejar bath provided thee: A royal tree whose spreading boughs A show of princely fruit display; A tree that bears a noble Duke, The Alexander of his day.\n\nOf a Manchegan gentleman Thy purpose is to tell the story, Relating how he lost his wits O’er idle tales of love and glory, Of “ladies, arms, and cavaliers:” A new Orlando Furioso— Innamorato, rather—who Won Dulcinea del Toboso.\n\nPut no vain emblems on thy shield; All figures—that is bragging play. A modest dedication make, And give no scoffer room to say, “What! Álvaro de Luna here? Or is it Hannibal again? Or does King Francis at Madrid Once more of destiny complain?”\n\nSince Heaven it hath not pleased on thee Deep erudition to bestow, Or black Latino’s gift of tongues, No Latin let thy pages show. Ape not philosophy or wit, Lest one who cannot comprehend, Make a wry face at thee and ask, “Why offer flowers to me, my friend?”\n\nBe not a meddler; no affair Of thine the life thy neighbours lead: Be prudent; oft the random jest Recoils upon the jester’s head. Thy constant labour let it be To earn thyself an honest name, For fooleries preserved in print Are perpetuity of shame.\n\nA further counsel bear in mind: If that thy roof be made of glass, It shows small wit to pick up stones To pelt the people as they pass. Win the attention of the wise, And give the thinker food for thought; Whoso indites frivolities, Will but by simpletons be sought.\n\nAMADIS OF GAUL To Don Quixote of la Mancha\n\nThou that didst imitate that life of mine When I in lonely sadness on the great Rock Peña Pobre sat disconsolate, In self-imposed penance there to pine; Thou, whose sole beverage was the bitter brine Of thine own tears, and who withouten plate Of silver, copper, tin, in lowly state Off the bare earth and on earth’s fruits didst dine; Live thou, of thine eternal glory sure. So long as on the round of the fourth sphere The bright Apollo shall his coursers steer, In thy renown thou shalt remain secure, Thy country’s name in story shall endure, And thy sage author stand without a peer.\n\nDON BELIANIS OF GREECE To Don Quixote of la Mancha\n\nIn slashing, hewing, cleaving, word and deed, I was the foremost knight of chivalry, Stout, bold, expert, as e’er the world did see; Thousands from the oppressor’s wrong I freed; Great were my feats, eternal fame their meed; In love I proved my truth and loyalty; The hugest giant was a dwarf for me; Ever to knighthood’s laws gave I good heed. My mastery the Fickle Goddess owned, And even Chance, submitting to control, Grasped by the forelock, yielded to my will. Yet—though above yon horned moon enthroned My fortune seems to sit—great Quixote, still Envy of thy achievements fills my soul.\n\nTHE LADY OF ORIANA To Dulcinea del Toboso\n\nOh, fairest Dulcinea, could it be! It were a pleasant fancy to suppose so— Could Miraflores change to El Toboso, And London’s town to that which shelters thee! Oh, could mine but acquire that livery Of countless charms thy mind and body show so! Or him, now famous grown—thou mad’st him grow so— Thy knight, in some dread combat could I see! Oh, could I be released from Amadis By exercise of such coy chastity As led thee gentle Quixote to dismiss! Then would my heavy sorrow turn to joy; None would I envy, all would envy me, And happiness be mine without alloy.\n\nGANDALIN, SQUIRE OF AMADIS OF GAUL, To Sancho Panza, squire of Don Quixote\n\nAll hail, illustrious man! Fortune, when she Bound thee apprentice to the esquire trade, Her care and tenderness of thee displayed, Shaping thy course from misadventure free. No longer now doth proud knight-errantry Regard with scorn the sickle and the spade; Of towering arrogance less count is made Than of plain esquire-like simplicity. I envy thee thy Dapple, and thy name, And those alforjas thou wast wont to stuff With comforts that thy providence proclaim. Excellent Sancho! hail to thee again! To thee alone the Ovid of our Spain Does homage with the rustic kiss and cuff.\n\nOn Sancho Panza and Rocinante\n\n# ON SANCHO\n\nI am the esquire Sancho Pan— Who served Don Quixote of La Man—; But from his service I retreat—, Resolved to pass my life discreet—; For Villadiego, called the Si—, Maintained that only in reti— Was found the secret of well-be—, According to the “Celesti—:” A book divine, except for sin— By speech too plain, in my opin—\n\n# ON ROCINANTE\n\nI am that Rocinante fa—, Great-grandson of great Babie—, Who, all for being lean and bon—, Had one Don Quixote for an own—; But if I matched him well in weak—, I never took short commons meek—, But kept myself in corn by steal—, A trick I learned from Lazaril—, When with a piece of straw so neat— The blind man of his wine he cheat—.\n\nORLANDO FURIOSO To Don Quixote of La Mancha\n\nIf thou art not a Peer, peer thou hast none; Among a thousand Peers thou art a peer; Nor is there room for one when thou art near, Unvanquished victor, great unconquered one! Orlando, by Angelica undone, Am I; o’er distant seas condemned to steer, And to Fame’s altars as an offering bear Valour respected by Oblivion. I cannot be thy rival, for thy fame And prowess rise above all rivalry, Albeit both bereft of wits we go. But, though the Scythian or the Moor to tame Was not thy lot, still thou dost rival me: Love binds us in a fellowship of woe.\n\nTo Don Quixote of La Mancha\n\nMy sword was not to be compared with thine Phœbus of Spain, marvel of courtesy, Nor with thy famous arm this hand of mine That smote from east to west as lightnings fly. I scorned all empire, and that monarchy The rosy east held out did I resign For one glance of Claridiana’s eye, The bright Aurora for whose love I pine. A miracle of constancy my love; And banished by her ruthless cruelty, This arm had might the rage of Hell to tame. But, Gothic Quixote, happier thou dost prove, For thou dost live in Dulcinea’s name, And famous, honoured, wise, she lives in thee.\n\nFROM SOLISDAN To Don Quixote of La Mancha\n\nYour fantasies, Sir Quixote, it is true, That crazy brain of yours have quite upset, But aught of base or mean hath never yet Been charged by any in reproach to you. Your deeds are open proof in all men’s view; For you went forth injustice to abate, And for your pains sore drubbings did you get From many a rascally and ruffian crew. If the fair Dulcinea, your heart’s queen, Be unrelenting in her cruelty, If still your woe be powerless to move her, In such hard case your comfort let it be That Sancho was a sorry go-between: A booby he, hard-hearted she, and you no lover.\n\nDIALOGUE Between Babieca and Rocinante\n\nB. “How comes it, Rocinante, you’re so lean?” R. “I’m underfed, with overwork I’m worn.” B. “But what becomes of all the hay and corn?” R. “My master gives me none; he’s much too mean.” B. “Come, come, you show ill-breeding, sir, I ween; ’Tis like an ass your master thus to scorn.” R. He is an ass, will die an ass, an ass was born; Why, he’s in love; what’s plainer to be seen?” B. “To be in love is folly?”—R. “No great sense.” B. “You’re metaphysical.”—R. “From want of food.” B. “Rail at the squire, then.”—R. “Why, what’s the good? I might indeed complain of him, I grant ye, But, squire or master, where’s the difference? They’re both as sorry hacks as Rocinante.”\n\n# THE AUTHOR’S PREFACE\n\nIdle reader: thou mayest believe me without any oath that I would this book, as it is the child of my brain, were the fairest, gayest, and cleverest that could be imagined. But I could not counteract Nature’s law that everything shall beget its like; and what, then, could this sterile, illtilled wit of mine beget but the story of a dry, shrivelled, whimsical offspring, full of thoughts of all sorts and such as never came into any other imagination--just what might be begotten in a prison, where every misery is lodged and every doleful sound makes its dwelling? Tranquillity, a cheerful retreat, pleasant fields, bright skies, murmuring brooks, peace of mind, these are the things that go far to make even the most barren muses fertile, and bring into the world births that fill it with wonder and delight. Sometimes when a father has an ugly, loutish son, the love he bears him so blindfolds his eyes that he does not see his defects, or, rather, takes them for gifts and charms of mind and body, and talks of them to his friends as wit and grace. I, however--for though I pass for the father, I am but the stepfather to “Don Quixote”--have no desire to go with the current of custom, or to implore thee, dearest reader, almost with tears in my eyes, as others do, to pardon or excuse the defects thou wilt perceive in this child of mine. Thou art neither its kinsman nor its friend, thy soul is thine own and thy will as free as any man’s, whate’er he be, thou art in thine own house and master of it as much as the king of his taxes and thou knowest the common saying, “Under my cloak I kill the king;” all which exempts and frees thee from every consideration and obligation, and thou canst say what thou wilt of the story without fear of being abused for any ill or rewarded for any good thou mayest say of it.\n\nMy wish would be simply to present it to thee plain and unadorned, without any embellishment of preface or uncountable muster of customary sonnets, epigrams, and eulogies, such as are commonly put at the beginning of books. For I can tell thee, though composing it cost me some labour, I found none greater than the making of this Preface thou art now reading. Many times did I take up my pen to write it, and many did I lay it down again, not knowing what to write. One of these times, as I was pondering with the paper before me, a pen in my ear, my elbow on the desk, and my cheek in my hand, thinking of what I should say, there came in unexpectedly a certain lively, clever friend of mine, who, seeing me so deep in thought, asked the reason; to which I, making no mystery of it, answered that I was thinking of the Preface I had to make for the story of “Don Quixote,” which so troubled me that I had a mind not to make any at all, nor even publish the achievements of so noble a knight.\n\n“For, how could you expect me not to feel uneasy about what that ancient lawgiver they call the Public will say when it sees me, after slumbering so many years in the silence of oblivion, coming out now with all my years upon my back, and with a book as dry as a rush, devoid of invention, meagre in style, poor in thoughts, wholly wanting in learning and wisdom, without quotations in the margin or annotations at the end, after the fashion of other books I see, which, though all fables and profanity, are so full of maxims from Aristotle, and Plato, and the whole herd of philosophers, that they fill the readers with amazement and convince them that the authors are men of learning, erudition, and eloquence. And then, when they quote the Holy Scriptures!--anyone would say they are St. Thomases or other doctors of the Church, observing as they do a decorum so ingenious that in one sentence they describe a distracted lover and in the next deliver a devout little sermon that it is a pleasure and a treat to hear and read. Of all this there will be nothing in my book, for I have nothing to quote in the margin or to note at the end, and still less do I know what authors I follow in it, to place them at the beginning, as all do, under the letters A, B, C, beginning with Aristotle and ending with Xenophon, or Zoilus, or Zeuxis, though one was a slanderer and the other a painter. Also my book must do without sonnets at the beginning, at least sonnets whose authors are dukes, marquises, counts, bishops, ladies, or famous poets. Though if I were to ask two or three obliging friends, I know they would give me them, and such as the productions of those that have the highest reputation in our Spain could not equal.\n\n“In short, my friend,” I continued, “I am determined that Señor Don Quixote shall remain buried in the archives of his own La Mancha until Heaven provide some one to garnish him with all those things he stands in need of; because I find myself, through my shallowness and want of learning, unequal to supplying them, and because I am by nature shy and careless about hunting for authors to say what I myself can say without them. Hence the cogitation and abstraction you found me in, and reason enough, what you have heard from me.”\n\nHearing this, my friend, giving himself a slap on the forehead and breaking into a hearty laugh, exclaimed, “Before God, Brother, now am I disabused of an error in which I have been living all this long time I have known you, all through which I have taken you to be shrewd and sensible in all you do; but now I see you are as far from that as the heaven is from the earth. Is it possible that things of so little moment and so easy to set right can occupy and perplex a ripe wit like yours, fit to break through and crush far greater obstacles? By my faith, this comes, not of any want of ability, but of too much indolence and too little knowledge of life. Do you want to know if I am telling the truth? Well, then, attend to me, and you will see how, in the opening and shutting of an eye, I sweep away all your difficulties, and supply all those deficiencies which you say check and discourage you from bringing before the world the story of your famous Don Quixote, the light and mirror of all knight-errantry.”\n“Say on,” said I, listening to his talk; “how do you propose to make up for my diffidence, and reduce to order this chaos of perplexity I am in?”\n\nTo which he made answer, “Your first difficulty about the sonnets, epigrams, or complimentary verses which you want for the beginning, and which ought to be by persons of importance and rank, can be removed if you yourself take a little trouble to make them; you can afterwards baptise them, and put any name you like to them, fathering them on Prester John of the Indies or the Emperor of Trebizond, who, to my knowledge, were said to have been famous poets: and even if they were not, and any pedants or bachelors should attack you and question the fact, never care two maravedis for that, for even if they prove a lie against you they cannot cut off the hand you wrote it with.\n\n“As to references in the margin to the books and authors from whom you take the aphorisms and sayings you put into your story, it is only contriving to fit in nicely any sentences or scraps of Latin you may happen to have by heart, or at any rate that will not give you much trouble to look up; so as, when you speak of freedom and captivity, to insert\nNon bene pro toto libertas venditur auro;\n\nand then refer in the margin to Horace, or whoever said it; or, if you allude to the power of death, to come in with--\n\nPallida mors Aequo pulsat pede pauperum tabernas, Regumque turres.\n\n“If it be friendship and the love God bids us bear to our enemy, go at once to the Holy Scriptures, which you can do with a very small amount of research, and quote no less than the words of God himself: Ego autem dico vobis: diligite inimicos vestros. If you speak of evil thoughts, turn to the Gospel: De corde exeunt cogitationes malae. If of the fickleness of friends, there is Cato, who will give you his distich:\n\nDonec eris felix multos numerabis amicos, Tempora si fuerint nubila, solus eris.\n\n“With these and such like bits of Latin they will take you for a grammarian at all events, and that now-a-days is no small honour and profit.\n\n“With regard to adding annotations at the end of the book, you may safely do it in this way. If you mention any giant in your book contrive that it shall be the giant Goliath, and with this alone, which will cost you almost nothing, you have a grand note, for you can put--The giant Golias or Goliath was a Philistine whom the shepherd David slew by a mighty stone-cast in the Terebinth valley, as is related in the Book of Kings--in the chapter where you find it written.\n\n“Next, to prove yourself a man of erudition in polite literature and cosmography, manage that the river Tagus shall be named in your story, and there you are at once with another famous annotation, setting forth--The river Tagus was so called after a King of Spain: it has its source in such and such a place and falls into the ocean, kissing the walls of the famous city of Lisbon, and it is a common belief that it has golden sands, etc. If you should have anything to do with robbers, I will give you the story of Cacus, for I have it by heart; if with loose women, there is the Bishop of Mondonedo, who will give you the loan of Lamia, Laida, and Flora, any reference to whom will bring you great credit; if with hard-hearted ones, Ovid will furnish you with Medea; if with witches or enchantresses, Homer has Calypso, and Virgil Circe; if with valiant captains, Julius Caesar himself will lend you himself in his own ‘Commentaries,’ and Plutarch will give you a thousand Alexanders. If you should deal with love, with two ounces you may know of Tuscan you can go to Leon the Hebrew, who will supply you to your heart’s content; or if you should not care to go to foreign countries you have at home Fonseca’s ‘Of the Love of God,’ in which is condensed all that you or the most imaginative mind can want on the subject. In short, all you have to do is to manage to quote these names, or refer to these stories I have mentioned, and leave it to me to insert the annotations and quotations, and I swear by all that’s good to fill your margins and use up four sheets at the end of the book.\n\n“Now let us come to those references to authors which other books have, and you want for yours. The remedy for this is very simple: You have only to look out for some book that quotes them all, from A to Z as you say yourself, and then insert the very same alphabet in your book, and though the imposition may be plain to see, because you have so little need to borrow from them, that is no matter; there will probably be some simple enough to believe that you have made use of them all in this plain, artless story of yours. At any rate, if it answers no other purpose, this long catalogue of authors will serve to give a surprising look of authority to your book. Besides, no one will trouble himself to verify whether you have followed them or whether you have not, being no way concerned in it; especially as, if I mistake not, this book of yours has no need of any one of those things you say it wants, for it is, from beginning to end, an attack upon the books of chivalry, of which Aristotle never dreamt, nor St. Basil said a word, nor Cicero had any knowledge; nor do the niceties of truth nor the observations of astrology come within the range of its fanciful vagaries; nor have geometrical measurements or refutations of the arguments used in rhetoric anything to do with it; nor does it mean to preach to anybody, mixing up things human and divine, a sort of motley in which no Christian understanding should dress itself. It has only to avail itself of truth to nature in its composition, and the more perfect the imitation the better the work will be. And as this piece of yours aims at nothing more than to destroy the authority and influence which books of chivalry have in the world and with the public, there is no need for you to go a-begging for aphorisms from philosophers, precepts from Holy Scripture, fables from poets, speeches from orators, or miracles from saints; but merely to take care that your style and diction run musically, pleasantly, and plainly, with clear, proper, and well-placed words, setting forth your purpose to the best of your power, and putting your ideas intelligibly, without confusion or obscurity. Strive, too, that in reading your story the melancholy may be moved to laughter, and the merry made merrier still; that the simple shall not be wearied, that the judicious shall admire the invention, that the grave shall not despise it, nor the wise fail to praise it. Finally, keep your aim fixed on the destruction of that ill-founded edifice of the books of chivalry, hated by some and praised by many more; for if you succeed in this you will have achieved no small success.”\n\nIn profound silence I listened to what my friend said, and his observations made such an impression on me that, without attempting to question them, I admitted their soundness, and out of them I determined to make this Preface; wherein, gentle reader, thou wilt perceive my friend’s good sense, my good fortune in finding such an adviser in such a time of need, and what thou hast gained in receiving, without addition or alteration, the story of the famous Don Quixote of La Mancha, who is held by all the inhabitants of the district of the Campo de Montiel to have been the chastest lover and the bravest knight that has for many years been seen in that neighbourhood. I have no desire to magnify the service I render thee in making thee acquainted with so renowned and honoured a knight, but I do desire thy thanks for the acquaintance thou wilt make with the famous Sancho Panza, his squire, in whom, to my thinking, I have given thee condensed all the squirely drolleries that are scattered through the swarm of the vain books of chivalry. And so--may God give thee health, and not forget me. Vale.\n\nTO THE DUKE OF BEJAR, MARQUIS OF GIBRALEON, COUNT OF BENALCAZAR AND BANARES, VICECOUNT OF THE PUEBLA DE ALCOCER, MASTER OF THE TOWNS OF CAPILLA, CURIEL AND BURGUILLOS\n\nIn belief of the good reception and honours that Your Excellency bestows on all sort of books, as prince so inclined to favor good arts, chiefly those who by their nobleness do not submit to the service and bribery of the vulgar, I have determined bringing to light The Ingenious Gentleman Don Quixote of la Mancha, in shelter of Your Excellency’s glamorous name, to whom, with the obeisance I owe to such grandeur, I pray to receive it agreeably under his protection, so that in this shadow, though deprived of that precious ornament of elegance and erudition that clothe the works composed in the houses of those who know, it dares appear with assurance in the judgment of some who, trespassing the bounds of their own ignorance, use to condemn with more rigour and less justice the writings of others. It is my earnest hope that Your Excellency’s good counsel in regard to my honourable purpose, will not disdain the littleness of so humble a service.\n\nMiguel de Cervantes\n\n# CHAPTER I.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF THE CHARACTER AND PURSUITS OF THE FAMOUS GENTLEMAN DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA\nIn a village of La Mancha, the name of which I have no desire to call to mind, there lived not long since one of those gentlemen that keep a lance in the lance-rack, an old buckler, a lean hack, and a greyhound for coursing. An olla of rather more beef than mutton, a salad on most nights, scraps on Saturdays, lentils on Fridays, and a pigeon or so extra on Sundays, made away with three-quarters of his income. The rest of it went in a doublet of fine cloth and velvet breeches and shoes to match for holidays, while on week-days he made a brave figure in his best homespun. He had in his house a housekeeper past forty, a niece under twenty, and a lad for the field and market-place, who used to saddle the hack as well as handle the bill-hook. The age of this gentleman of ours was bordering on fifty; he was of a hardy habit, spare, gaunt-featured, a very early riser and a great sportsman. They will have it his surname was Quixada or Quesada (for here there is some difference of opinion among the authors who write on the subject), although from reasonable conjectures it seems plain that he was called Quexana. This, however, is of but little importance to our tale; it will be enough not to stray a hair’s breadth from the truth in the telling of it.\n\nYou must know, then, that the above-named gentleman whenever he was at leisure (which was mostly all the year round) gave himself up to reading books of chivalry with such ardour and avidity that he almost entirely neglected the pursuit of his field-sports, and even the management of his property; and to such a pitch did his eagerness and infatuation go that he sold many an acre of tillageland to buy books of chivalry to read, and brought home as many of them as he could get. But of all there were none he liked so well as those of the famous Feliciano de Silva’s composition, for their lucidity of style and complicated conceits were as pearls in his sight, particularly when in his reading he came upon courtships and cartels, where he often found passages like “the reason of the unreason with which my reason is afflicted so weakens my reason that with reason I murmur at your beauty;” or again, “the high heavens, that of your divinity divinely fortify you with the stars, render you deserving of the desert your greatness deserves.” Over conceits of this sort the poor gentleman lost his wits, and used to lie awake striving to understand them and worm the meaning out of them; what Aristotle himself could not have made out or extracted had he come to life again for that special purpose. He was not at all easy about the wounds which Don Belianis gave and took, because it seemed to him that, great as were the surgeons who had cured him, he must have had his face and body covered all over with seams and scars. He commended, however, the author’s way of ending his book with the promise of that interminable adventure, and many a time was he tempted to take up his pen and finish it properly as is there proposed, which no doubt he would have done, and made a successful piece of work of it too, had not greater and more absorbing thoughts prevented him.\n\nMany an argument did he have with the curate of his village (a learned man, and a graduate of Siguenza) as to which had been the better knight, Palmerin of England or Amadis of Gaul. Master Nicholas, the village barber, however, used to say that neither of them came up to the Knight of Phoebus, and that if there was any that could compare with him it was Don Galaor, the brother of Amadis of Gaul, because he had a spirit that was equal to every occasion, and was no finikin knight, nor lachrymose like his brother, while in the matter of valour he was not a whit behind him. In short, he became so absorbed in his books that he spent his nights from sunset to sunrise, and his days from dawn to dark, poring over them; and what with little sleep and much reading his brains got so dry that he lost his wits. His fancy grew full of what he used to read about in his books, enchantments, quarrels, battles, challenges, wounds, wooings, loves, agonies, and all sorts of impossible nonsense; and it so possessed his mind that the whole fabric of invention and fancy he read of was true, that to him no history in the world had more reality in it. He used to say the Cid Ruy Diaz was a very good knight, but that he was not to be compared with the Knight of the Burning Sword who with one back-stroke cut in half two fierce and monstrous giants. He thought more of Bernardo del Carpio because at Roncesvalles he slew Roland in spite of enchantments, availing himself of the artifice of Hercules when he strangled Antaeus the son of Terra in his arms. He approved highly of the giant Morgante, because, although of the giant breed which is always arrogant and ill-conditioned, he alone was affable and well-bred. But above all he admired Reinaldos of Montalban, especially when he saw him sallying forth from his castle and robbing everyone he met, and when beyond the seas he stole that image of Mahomet which, as his history says, was entirely of gold. To have a bout of kicking at that traitor of a Ganelon he would have given his housekeeper, and his niece into the bargain.\n\nIn short, his wits being quite gone, he hit upon the strangest notion that ever madman in this world hit upon, and that was that he fancied it was right and requisite, as well for the support of his own honour as for the service of his country, that he should make a knight-errant of himself, roaming the world over in full armour and on horseback in quest of adventures, and putting in practice himself all that he had read of as being the usual practices of knights-errant; righting every kind of wrong, and exposing himself to peril and danger from which, in the issue, he was to reap eternal renown and fame. Already the poor man saw himself crowned by the might of his arm Emperor of Trebizond at least; and so, led away by the intense enjoyment he found in these pleasant fancies, he set himself forthwith to put his scheme into execution.\n\nThe first thing he did was to clean up some armour that had belonged to his great-grandfather, and had been for ages lying forgotten in a corner eaten with rust and covered with mildew. He scoured and polished it as best he could, but he perceived one great defect in it, that it had no closed helmet, nothing but a simple morion. This deficiency, however, his ingenuity supplied, for he contrived a kind of half-helmet of pasteboard which, fitted on to the morion, looked like a whole one. It is true that, in order to see if it was strong and fit to stand a cut, he drew his sword and gave it a couple of slashes, the first of which undid in an instant what had taken him a week to do. The ease with which he had knocked it to pieces disconcerted him somewhat, and to guard against that danger he set to work again, fixing bars of iron on the inside until he was satisfied with its strength; and then, not caring to try any more experiments with it, he passed it and adopted it as a helmet of the most perfect construction.\n\nHe next proceeded to inspect his hack, which, with more quartos than a real and more blemishes than the steed of Gonela, that “tantum pellis et ossa fuit,” surpassed in his eyes the Bucephalus of Alexander or the Babieca of the Cid. Four days were spent in thinking what name to give him, because (as he said to himself) it was not right that a horse belonging to a knight so famous, and one with such merits of his own, should be without some distinctive name, and he strove to adapt it so as to indicate what he had been before belonging to a knight-errant, and what he then was; for it was only reasonable that, his master taking a new character, he should take a new name, and that it should be a distinguished and full-sounding one, befitting the new order and calling he was about to follow. And so, after having composed, struck out, rejected, added to, unmade, and remade a multitude of names out of his memory and fancy, he decided upon calling him Rocinante, a name, to his thinking, lofty, sonorous, and significant of his condition as a hack before he became what he now was, the first and foremost of all the hacks in the world.\n\nHaving got a name for his horse so much to his taste, he was anxious to get one for himself, and he was eight days more pondering over this point, till at last he made up his mind to call himself “Don Quixote,” whence, as has been already said, the authors of this veracious history have inferred that his name must have been beyond a doubt Quixada, and not Quesada as others would have it. Recollecting, however, that the valiant Amadis was not content to call himself curtly Amadis and nothing more, but added the name of his kingdom and country to make it famous, and called himself Amadis of Gaul, he, like a good knight, resolved to add on the name of his, and to style himself Don Quixote of La Mancha, whereby, he considered, he described accurately his origin and country, and did honour to it in taking his surname from it.\n\nSo then, his armour being furbished, his morion turned into a helmet, his hack christened, and he himself confirmed, he came to the conclusion that nothing more was needed now but to look out for a lady to be in love with; for a knight-errant without love was like a tree without leaves or fruit, or a body without a soul. As he said to himself, “If, for my sins, or by my good fortune, I come across some giant hereabouts, a common occurrence with knights-errant, and overthrow him in one onslaught, or cleave him asunder to the waist, or, in short, vanquish and subdue him, will it not be well to have some one I may send him to as a present, that he may come in and fall on his knees before my sweet lady, and in a humble, submissive voice say, ‘I am the giant Caraculiambro, lord of the island of Malindrania, vanquished in single combat by the never sufficiently extolled knight Don Quixote of La Mancha, who has commanded me to present myself before your Grace, that your Highness dispose of me at your pleasure’?” Oh, how our good gentleman enjoyed the delivery of this speech, especially when he had thought of some one to call his Lady! There was, so the story goes, in a village near his own a very good-looking farm-girl with whom he had been at one time in love, though, so far as is known, she never knew it nor gave a thought to the matter. Her name was Aldonza Lorenzo, and upon her he thought fit to confer the title of Lady of his Thoughts; and after some search for a name which should not be out of harmony with her own, and should suggest and indicate that of a princess and great lady, he decided upon calling her Dulcinea del Toboso--she being of El Toboso--a name, to his mind, musical, uncommon, and significant, like all those he had already bestowed upon himself and the things belonging to him.\n\n# CHAPTER II.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF THE FIRST SALLY THE INGENIOUS DON QUIXOTE MADE FROM HOME\nThese preliminaries settled, he did not care to put off any longer the execution of his design, urged on to it by the thought of all the world was losing by his delay, seeing what wrongs he intended to right, grievances to redress, injustices to repair, abuses to remove, and duties to discharge. So, without giving notice of his intention to anyone, and without anybody seeing him, one morning before the dawning of the day (which was one of the hottest of the month of July) he donned his suit of armour, mounted Rocinante with his patched-up helmet on, braced his buckler, took his lance, and by the back door of the yard sallied forth upon the plain in the highest contentment and satisfaction at seeing with what ease he had made a beginning with his grand purpose. But scarcely did he find himself upon the open plain, when a terrible thought struck him, one all but enough to make him abandon the enterprise at the very outset. It occurred to him that he had not been dubbed a knight, and that according to the law of chivalry he neither could nor ought to bear arms against any knight; and that even if he had been, still he ought, as a novice knight, to wear white armour, without a device upon the shield until by his prowess he had earned one. These reflections made him waver in his purpose, but his craze being stronger than any reasoning, he made up his mind to have himself dubbed a knight by the first one he came across, following the example of others in the same case, as he had read in the books that brought him to this pass. As for white armour, he resolved, on the first opportunity, to scour his until it was whiter than an ermine; and so comforting himself he pursued his way, taking that which his horse chose, for in this he believed lay the essence of adventures.\n\nThus setting out, our new-fledged adventurer paced along, talking to himself and saying, “Who knows but that in time to come, when the veracious history of my famous deeds is made known, the sage who writes it, when he has to set forth my first sally in the early morning, will do it after this fashion? ‘Scarce had the rubicund Apollo spread o’er the face of the broad spacious earth the golden threads of his bright hair, scarce had the little birds of painted plumage attuned their notes to hail with dulcet and mellifluous harmony the coming of the rosy Dawn, that, deserting the soft couch of her jealous spouse, was appearing to mortals at the gates and balconies of the Manchegan horizon, when the renowned knight Don Quixote of La Mancha, quitting the lazy down, mounted his celebrated steed Rocinante and began to traverse the ancient and famous Campo de Montiel;’” which in fact he was actually traversing. “Happy the age, happy the time,” he continued, “in which shall be made known my deeds of fame, worthy to be moulded in brass, carved in marble, limned in pictures, for a memorial for ever. And thou, O sage magician, whoever thou art, to whom it shall fall to be the chronicler of this wondrous history, forget not, I entreat thee, my good Rocinante, the constant companion of my ways and wanderings.” Presently he broke out again, as if he were love-stricken in earnest, “O Princess Dulcinea, lady of this captive heart, a grievous wrong hast thou done me to drive me forth with scorn, and with inexorable obduracy banish me from the presence of thy beauty. O lady, deign to hold in remembrance this heart, thy vassal, that thus in anguish pines for love of thee.”\n\nSo he went on stringing together these and other absurdities, all in the style of those his books had taught him, imitating their language as well as he could; and all the while he rode so slowly and the sun mounted so rapidly and with such fervour that it was enough to melt his brains if he had any. Nearly all day he travelled without anything remarkable happening to him, at which he was in despair, for he was anxious to encounter some one at once upon whom to try the might of his strong arm.\n\nWriters there are who say the first adventure he met with was that of Puerto Lapice; others say it was that of the windmills; but what I have ascertained on this point, and what I have found written in the annals of La Mancha, is that he was on the road all day, and towards nightfall his hack and he found themselves dead tired and hungry, when, looking all around to see if he could discover any castle or shepherd’s shanty where he might refresh himself and relieve his sore wants, he perceived not far out of his road an inn, which was as welcome as a star guiding him to the portals, if not the palaces, of his redemption; and quickening his pace he reached it just as night was setting in. At the door were standing two young women, girls of the district as they call them, on their way to Seville with some carriers who had chanced to halt that night at the inn; and as, happen what might to our adventurer, everything he saw or imaged seemed to him to be and to happen after the fashion of what he read of, the moment he saw the inn he pictured it to himself as a castle with its four turrets and pinnacles of shining silver, not forgetting the drawbridge and moat and all the belongings usually ascribed to castles of the sort. To this inn, which to him seemed a castle, he advanced, and at a short distance from it he checked Rocinante, hoping that some dwarf would show himself upon the battlements, and by sound of trumpet give notice that a knight was approaching the castle. But seeing that they were slow about it, and that Rocinante was in a hurry to reach the stable, he made for the inn door, and perceived the two gay damsels who were standing there, and who seemed to him to be two fair maidens or lovely ladies taking their ease at the castle gate.\n\nAt this moment it so happened that a swineherd who was going through the stubbles collecting a drove of pigs (for, without any apology, that is what they are called) gave a blast of his horn to bring them together, and forthwith it seemed to Don Quixote to be what he was expecting, the signal of some dwarf announcing his arrival; and so with prodigious satisfaction he rode up to the inn and to the ladies, who, seeing a man of this sort approaching in full armour and with lance and buckler, were turning in dismay into the inn, when Don Quixote, guessing their fear by their flight, raising his pasteboard visor, disclosed his dry dusty visage, and with courteous bearing and gentle voice addressed them, “Your ladyships need not fly or fear any rudeness, for that it belongs not to the order of knighthood which I profess to offer to anyone, much less to highborn maidens as your appearance proclaims you to be.” The girls were looking at him and straining their eyes to make out the features which the clumsy visor obscured, but when they heard themselves called maidens, a thing so much out of their line, they could not restrain their laughter, which made Don Quixote wax indignant, and say, “Modesty becomes the fair, and moreover laughter that has little cause is great silliness; this, however, I say not to pain or anger you, for my desire is none other than to serve you.”\n\nThe incomprehensible language and the unpromising looks of our cavalier only increased the ladies’ laughter, and that increased his irritation, and matters might have gone farther if at that moment the landlord had not come out, who, being a very fat man, was a very peaceful one. He, seeing this grotesque figure clad in armour that did not match any more than his saddle, bridle, lance, buckler, or corselet, was not at all indisposed to join the damsels in their manifestations of amusement; but, in truth, standing in awe of such a complicated armament, he thought it best to speak him fairly, so he said, “Señor Caballero, if your worship wants lodging, bating the bed (for there is not one in the inn) there is plenty of everything else here.” Don Quixote, observing the respectful bearing of the Alcaide of the fortress (for so innkeeper and inn seemed in his eyes), made answer, “Sir Castellan, for me anything will suffice, for\n\n‘My armour is my only wear, My only rest the fray.’”\n\nThe host fancied he called him Castellan because he took him for a “worthy of Castile,” though he was in fact an Andalusian, and one from the strand of San Lucar, as crafty a thief as Cacus and as full of tricks as a student or a page. “In that case,” said he,\n\n“‘Your bed is on the flinty rock, Your sleep to watch alway;’\n\nand if so, you may dismount and safely reckon upon any quantity of sleeplessness under this roof for a twelvemonth, not to say for a single night.” So saying, he advanced to hold the stirrup for Don Quixote, who got down with great difficulty and exertion (for he had not broken his fast all day), and then charged the host to take great care of his horse, as he was the best bit of flesh that ever ate bread in this world. The landlord eyed him over but did not find him as good as Don Quixote said, nor even half as good; and putting him up in the stable, he returned to see what might be wanted by his guest, whom the damsels, who had by this time made their peace with him, were now relieving of his armour. They had taken off his breastplate and backpiece, but they neither knew nor saw how to open his gorget or remove his make-shift helmet, for he had fastened it with green ribbons, which, as there was no untying the knots, required to be cut. This, however, he would not by any means consent to, so he remained all the evening with his helmet on, the drollest and oddest figure that can be imagined; and while they were removing his armour, taking the baggages who were about it for ladies of high degree belonging to the castle, he said to them with great sprightliness:\n\n“Oh, never, surely, was there knight So served by hand of dame, As served was he, Don Quixote hight, When from his town he came; With maidens waiting on himself, Princesses on his hack--\n\nor Rocinante, for that, ladies mine, is my horse’s name, and Don Quixote of La Mancha is my own; for though I had no intention of declaring myself until my achievements in your service and honour had made me known, the necessity of adapting that old ballad of Lancelot to the present occasion has given you the knowledge of my name altogether prematurely. A time, however, will come for your ladyships to command and me to obey, and then the might of my arm will show my desire to serve you.”\n\nThe girls, who were not used to hearing rhetoric of this sort, had nothing to say in reply; they only asked him if he wanted anything to eat. “I would gladly eat a bit of something,” said Don Quixote, “for I feel it would come very seasonably.” The day happened to be a Friday, and in the whole inn there was nothing but some pieces of the fish they call in Castile “abadejo,” in Andalusia “bacallao,” and in some places “curadillo,” and in others “troutlet;” so they asked him if he thought he could eat troutlet, for there was no other fish to give him. “If there be troutlets enough,” said Don Quixote, “they will be the same thing as a trout; for it is all one to me whether I am given eight reals in small change or a piece of eight; moreover, it may be that these troutlets are like veal, which is better than beef, or kid, which is better than goat. But whatever it be let it come quickly, for the burden and pressure of arms cannot be borne without support to the inside.” They laid a table for him at the door of the inn for the sake of the air, and the host brought him a portion of ill-soaked and worse cooked stockfish, and a piece of bread as black and mouldy as his own armour; but a laughable sight it was to see him eating, for having his helmet on and the beaver up, he could not with his own hands put anything into his mouth unless some one else placed it there, and this service one of the ladies rendered him. But to give him anything to drink was impossible, or would have been so had not the landlord bored a reed, and putting one end in his mouth poured the wine into him through the other; all which he bore with patience rather than sever the ribbons of his helmet.\n\nWhile this was going on there came up to the inn a sowgelder, who, as he approached, sounded his reed pipe four or five times, and thereby completely convinced Don Quixote that he was in some famous castle, and that they were regaling him with music, and that the stockfish was trout, the bread the whitest, the wenches ladies, and the landlord the castellan of the castle; and consequently he held that his enterprise and sally had been to some purpose. But still it distressed him to think he had not been dubbed a knight, for it was plain to him he could not lawfully engage in any adventure without receiving the order of knighthood.\n\n# CHAPTER III.\n\nWHEREIN IS RELATED THE DROLL WAY IN WHICH DON QUIXOTE HAD HIMSELF DUBBED A KNIGHT\nHarassed by this reflection, he made haste with his scanty pothouse supper, and having finished it called the landlord, and shutting himself into the stable with him, fell on his knees before him, saying, “From this spot I rise not, valiant knight, until your courtesy grants me the boon I seek, one that will redound to your praise and the benefit of the human race.” The landlord, seeing his guest at his feet and hearing a speech of this kind, stood staring at him in bewilderment, not knowing what to do or say, and entreating him to rise, but all to no purpose until he had agreed to grant the boon demanded of him. “I looked for no less, my lord, from your High Magnificence,” replied Don Quixote, “and I have to tell you that the boon I have asked and your liberality has granted is that you shall dub me knight to-morrow morning, and that to-night I shall watch my arms in the chapel of this your castle; thus to-morrow, as I have said, will be accomplished what I so much desire, enabling me lawfully to roam through all the four quarters of the world seeking adventures on behalf of those in distress, as is the duty of chivalry and of knights-errant like myself, whose ambition is directed to such deeds.”\n\nThe landlord, who, as has been mentioned, was something of a wag, and had already some suspicion of his guest’s want of wits, was quite convinced of it on hearing talk of this kind from him, and to make sport for the night he determined to fall in with his humour. So he told him he was quite right in pursuing the object he had in view, and that such a motive was natural and becoming in cavaliers as distinguished as he seemed and his gallant bearing showed him to be; and that he himself in his younger days had followed the same honourable calling, roaming in quest of adventures in various parts of the world, among others the Curing-grounds of Malaga, the Isles of Riaran, the Precinct of Seville, the Little Market of Segovia, the Olivera of Valencia, the Rondilla of Granada, the Strand of San Lucar, the Colt of Cordova, the Taverns of Toledo, and divers other quarters, where he had proved the nimbleness of his feet and the lightness of his fingers, doing many wrongs, cheating many widows, ruining maids and swindling minors, and, in short, bringing himself under the notice of almost every tribunal and court of justice in Spain; until at last he had retired to this castle of his, where he was living upon his property and upon that of others; and where he received all knights-errant of whatever rank or condition they might be, all for the great love he bore them and that they might share their substance with him in return for his benevolence. He told him, moreover, that in this castle of his there was no chapel in which he could watch his armour, as it had been pulled down in order to be rebuilt, but that in a case of necessity it might, he knew, be watched anywhere, and he might watch it that night in a courtyard of the castle, and in the morning, God willing, the requisite ceremonies might be performed so as to have him dubbed a knight, and so thoroughly dubbed that nobody could be more so. He asked if he had any money with him, to which Don Quixote replied that he had not a farthing, as in the histories of knights-errant he had never read of any of them carrying any. On this point the landlord told him he was mistaken; for, though not recorded in the histories, because in the author’s opinion there was no need to mention anything so obvious and necessary as money and clean shirts, it was not to be supposed therefore that they did not carry them, and he might regard it as certain and established that all knights-errant (about whom there were so many full and unimpeachable books) carried well-furnished purses in case of emergency, and likewise carried shirts and a little box of ointment to cure the wounds they received. For in those plains and deserts where they engaged in combat and came out wounded, it was not always that there was some one to cure them, unless indeed they had for a friend some sage magician to succour them at once by fetching through the air upon a cloud some damsel or dwarf with a vial of water of such virtue that by tasting one drop of it they were cured of their hurts and wounds in an instant and left as sound as if they had not received any damage whatever. But in case this should not occur, the knights of old took care to see that their squires were provided with money and other requisites, such as lint and ointments for healing purposes; and when it happened that knights had no squires (which was rarely and seldom the case) they themselves carried everything in cunning saddle-bags that were hardly seen on the horse’s croup, as if it were something else of more importance, because, unless for some such reason, carrying saddle-bags was not very favourably regarded among knights-errant. He therefore advised him (and, as his godson so soon to be, he might even command him) never from that time forth to travel without money and the usual requirements, and he would find the advantage of them when he least expected it.\n\nDon Quixote promised to follow his advice scrupulously, and it was arranged forthwith that he should watch his armour in a large yard at one side of the inn; so, collecting it all together, Don Quixote placed it on a trough that stood by the side of a well, and bracing his buckler on his arm he grasped his lance and began with a stately air to march up and down in front of the trough, and as he began his march night began to fall.\n\nThe landlord told all the people who were in the inn about the craze of his guest, the watching of the armour, and the dubbing ceremony he contemplated. Full of wonder at so strange a form of madness, they flocked to see it from a distance, and observed with what composure he sometimes paced up and down, or sometimes, leaning on his lance, gazed on his armour without taking his eyes off it for ever so long; and as the night closed in with a light from the moon so brilliant that it might vie with his that lent it, everything the novice knight did was plainly seen by all.\n\nMeanwhile one of the carriers who were in the inn thought fit to water his team, and it was necessary to remove Don Quixote’s armour as it lay on the trough; but he seeing the other approach hailed him in a loud voice, “O thou, whoever thou art, rash knight that comest to lay hands on the armour of the most valorous errant that ever girt on sword, have a care what thou dost; touch it not unless thou wouldst lay down thy life as the penalty of thy rashness.” The carrier gave no heed to these words (and he would have done better to heed them if he had been heedful of his health), but seizing it by the straps flung the armour some distance from him. Seeing this, Don Quixote raised his eyes to heaven, and fixing his thoughts, apparently, upon his lady Dulcinea, exclaimed, “Aid me, lady mine, in this the first encounter that presents itself to this breast which thou holdest in subjection; let not thy favour and protection fail me in this first jeopardy;” and, with these words and others to the same purpose, dropping his buckler he lifted his lance with both hands and with it smote such a blow on the carrier’s head that he stretched him on the ground, so stunned that had he followed it up with a second there would have been no need of a surgeon to cure him. This done, he picked up his armour and returned to his beat with the same serenity as before.\n\nShortly after this, another, not knowing what had happened (for the carrier still lay senseless), came with the same object of giving water to his mules, and was proceeding to remove the armour in order to clear the trough, when Don Quixote, without uttering a word or imploring aid from anyone, once more dropped his buckler and once more lifted his lance, and without actually breaking the second carrier’s head into pieces, made more than three of it, for he laid it open in four. At the noise all the people of the inn ran to the spot, and among them the landlord. Seeing this, Don Quixote braced his buckler on his arm, and with his hand on his sword exclaimed, “O Lady of Beauty, strength and support of my faint heart, it is time for thee to turn the eyes of thy greatness on this thy captive knight on the brink of so mighty an adventure.” By this he felt himself so inspired that he would not have flinched if all the carriers in the world had assailed him. The comrades of the wounded perceiving the plight they were in began from a distance to shower stones on Don Quixote, who screened himself as best he could with his buckler, not daring to quit the trough and leave his armour unprotected. The landlord shouted to them to leave him alone, for he had already told them that he was mad, and as a madman he would not be accountable even if he killed them all. Still louder shouted Don Quixote, calling them knaves and traitors, and the lord of the castle, who allowed knights-errant to be treated in this fashion, a villain and a low-born knight whom, had he received the order of knighthood, he would call to account for his treachery. “But of you,” he cried, “base and vile rabble, I make no account; fling, strike, come on, do all ye can against me, ye shall see what the reward of your folly and insolence will be.” This he uttered with so much spirit and boldness that he filled his assailants with a terrible fear, and as much for this reason as at the persuasion of the landlord they left off stoning him, and he allowed them to carry off the wounded, and with the same calmness and composure as before resumed the watch over his armour.\n\nBut these freaks of his guest were not much to the liking of the landlord, so he determined to cut matters short and confer upon him at once the unlucky order of knighthood before any further misadventure could occur; so, going up to him, he apologised for the rudeness which, without his knowledge, had been offered to him by these low people, who, however, had been well punished for their audacity. As he had already told him, he said, there was no chapel in the castle, nor was it needed for what remained to be done, for, as he understood the ceremonial of the order, the whole point of being dubbed a knight lay in the accolade and in the slap on the shoulder, and that could be administered in the middle of a field; and that he had now done all that was needful as to watching the armour, for all requirements were satisfied by a watch of two hours only, while he had been more than four about it. Don Quixote believed it all, and told him he stood there ready to obey him, and to make an end of it with as much despatch as possible; for, if he were again attacked, and felt himself to be dubbed knight, he would not, he thought, leave a soul alive in the castle, except such as out of respect he might spare at his bidding.\n\nThus warned and menaced, the castellan forthwith brought out a book in which he used to enter the straw and barley he served out to the carriers, and, with a lad carrying a candle-end, and the two damsels already mentioned, he returned to where Don Quixote stood, and bade him kneel down. Then, reading from his account-book as if he were repeating some devout prayer, in the middle of his delivery he raised his hand and gave him a sturdy blow on the neck, and then, with his own sword, a smart slap on the shoulder, all the while muttering between his teeth as if he was saying his prayers. Having done this, he directed one of the ladies to gird on his sword, which she did with great self-possession and gravity, and not a little was required to prevent a burst of laughter at each stage of the ceremony; but what they had already seen of the novice knight’s prowess kept their laughter within bounds. On girding him with the sword the worthy lady said to him, “May God make your worship a very fortunate knight, and grant you success in battle.” Don Quixote asked her name in order that he might from that time forward know to whom he was beholden for the favour he had received, as he meant to confer upon her some portion of the honour he acquired by the might of his arm. She answered with great humility that she was called La Tolosa, and that she was the daughter of a cobbler of Toledo who lived in the stalls of Sanchobienaya, and that wherever she might be she would serve and esteem him as her lord. Don Quixote said in reply that she would do him a favour if thenceforward she assumed the “Don” and called herself Dona Tolosa. She promised she would, and then the other buckled on his spur, and with her followed almost the same conversation as with the lady of the sword. He asked her name, and she said it was La Molinera, and that she was the daughter of a respectable miller of Antequera; and of her likewise Don Quixote requested that she would adopt the “Don” and call herself Dona Molinera, making offers to her further services and favours.\n\nHaving thus, with hot haste and speed, brought to a conclusion these never-till-now-seen ceremonies, Don Quixote was on thorns until he saw himself on horseback sallying forth in quest of adventures; and saddling Rocinante at once he mounted, and embracing his host, as he returned thanks for his kindness in knighting him, he addressed him in language so extraordinary that it is impossible to convey an idea of it or report it. The landlord, to get him out of the inn, replied with no less rhetoric though with shorter words, and without calling upon him to pay the reckoning let him go with a Godspeed.\n\n# OF WHAT HAPPENED TO OUR KNIGHT WHEN HE LEFT THE INN\n\nDay was dawning when Don Quixote quitted the inn, so happy, so gay, so exhilarated at finding himself now dubbed a knight, that his joy was like to burst his horse-girths. However, recalling the advice of his host as to the requisites he ought to carry with him, especially that referring to money and shirts, he determined to go home and provide himself with all, and also with a squire, for he reckoned upon securing a farm-labourer, a neighbour of his, a poor man with a family, but very well qualified for the office of squire to a knight. With this object he turned his horse’s head towards his village, and Rocinante, thus reminded of his old quarters, stepped out so briskly that he hardly seemed to tread the earth.\n\nHe had not gone far, when out of a thicket on his right there seemed to come feeble cries as of some one in distress, and the instant he heard them he exclaimed, “Thanks be to heaven for the favour it accords me, that it so soon offers me an opportunity of fulfilling the obligation I have undertaken, and gathering the fruit of my ambition. These cries, no doubt, come from some man or woman in want of help, and needing my aid and protection;” and wheeling, he turned Rocinante in the direction whence the cries seemed to proceed. He had gone but a few paces into the wood, when he saw a mare tied to an oak, and tied to another, and stripped from the waist upwards, a youth of about fifteen years of age, from whom the cries came. Nor were they without cause, for a lusty farmer was flogging him with a belt and following up every blow with scoldings and commands, repeating, “Your mouth shut and your eyes open!” while the youth made answer, “I won’t do it again, master mine; by God’s passion I won’t do it again, and I’ll take more care of the flock another time.”\n\nSeeing what was going on, Don Quixote said in an angry voice, “Discourteous knight, it ill becomes you to assail one who cannot defend himself; mount your steed and take your lance” (for there was a lance leaning against the oak to which the mare was tied), “and I will make you know that you are behaving as a coward.” The farmer, seeing before him this figure in full armour brandishing a lance over his head, gave himself up for dead, and made answer meekly, “Sir Knight, this youth that I am chastising is my servant, employed by me to watch a flock of sheep that I have hard by, and he is so careless that I lose one every day, and when I punish him for his carelessness and knavery he says I do it out of niggardliness, to escape paying him the wages I owe him, and before God, and on my soul, he lies.”\n\n“Lies before me, base clown!” said Don Quixote. “By the sun that shines on us I have a mind to run you through with this lance. Pay him at once without another word; if not, by the God that rules us I will make an end of you, and annihilate you on the spot; release him instantly.”\n\nThe farmer hung his head, and without a word untied his servant, of whom Don Quixote asked how much his master owed him.\n\nHe replied, nine months at seven reals a month. Don Quixote added it up, found that it came to sixty-three reals, and told the farmer to pay it down immediately, if he did not want to die for it.\n\nThe trembling clown replied that as he lived and by the oath he had sworn (though he had not sworn any) it was not so much; for there were to be taken into account and deducted three pairs of shoes he had given him, and a real for two blood-lettings when he was sick.\n\n“All that is very well,” said Don Quixote; “but let the shoes and the blood-lettings stand as a setoff against the blows you have given him without any cause; for if he spoiled the leather of the shoes you paid for, you have damaged that of his body, and if the barber took blood from him when he was sick, you have drawn it when he was sound; so on that score he owes you nothing.”\n\n“The difficulty is, Sir Knight, that I have no money here; let Andres come home with me, and I will pay him all, real by real.”\n\n“I go with him!” said the youth. “Nay, God forbid! No, señor, not for the world; for once alone with me, he would flay me like a Saint Bartholomew.”\n\n“He will do nothing of the kind,” said Don Quixote; “I have only to command, and he will obey me; and as he has sworn to me by the order of knighthood which he has received, I leave him free, and I guarantee the payment.”\n\n“Consider what you are saying, señor,” said the youth; “this master of mine is not a knight, nor has he received any order of knighthood; for he is Juan Haldudo the Rich, of Quintanar.”\n\n“That matters little,” replied Don Quixote; “there may be Haldudos knights; moreover, everyone is the son of his works.”\n\n“That is true,” said Andres; “but this master of mine--of what works is he the son, when he refuses me the wages of my sweat and labour?”\n\n“I do not refuse, brother Andres,” said the farmer, “be good enough to come along with me, and I swear by all the orders of knighthood there are in the world to pay you as I have agreed, real by real, and perfumed.”\n\n“For the perfumery I excuse you,” said Don Quixote; “give it to him in reals, and I shall be satisfied; and see that you do as you have sworn; if not, by the same oath I swear to come back and hunt you out and punish you; and I shall find you though you should lie closer than a lizard. And if you desire to know who it is lays this command upon you, that you be more firmly bound to obey it, know that I am the valorous Don Quixote of La Mancha, the undoer of wrongs and injustices; and so, God be with you, and keep in mind what you have promised and sworn under those penalties that have been already declared to you.”\n\nSo saying, he gave Rocinante the spur and was soon out of reach. The farmer followed him with his eyes, and when he saw that he had cleared the wood and was no longer in sight, he turned to his boy Andres, and said, “Come here, my son, I want to pay you what I owe you, as that undoer of wrongs has commanded me.”\n\n“My oath on it,” said Andres, “your worship will be well advised to obey the command of that good knight--may he live a thousand years--for, as he is a valiant and just judge, by Roque, if you do not pay me, he will come back and do as he said.”\n\n“My oath on it, too,” said the farmer; “but as I have a strong affection for you, I want to add to the debt in order to add to the payment;” and seizing him by the arm, he tied him up again, and gave him such a flogging that he left him for dead.\n\n“Now, Master Andres,” said the farmer, “call on the undoer of wrongs; you will find he won’t undo that, though I am not sure that I have quite done with you, for I have a good mind to flay you alive.” But at last he untied him, and gave him leave to go look for his judge in order to put the sentence pronounced into execution.\n\nAndres went off rather down in the mouth, swearing he would go to look for the valiant Don Quixote of La Mancha and tell him exactly what had happened, and that all would have to be repaid him sevenfold; but for all that, he went off weeping, while his master stood laughing.\n\nThus did the valiant Don Quixote right that wrong, and, thoroughly satisfied with what had taken place, as he considered he had made a very happy and noble beginning with his knighthood, he took the road towards his village in perfect self-content, saying in a low voice, “Well mayest thou this day call thyself fortunate above all on earth, O Dulcinea del Toboso, fairest of the fair! since it has fallen to thy lot to hold subject and submissive to thy full will and pleasure a knight so renowned as is and will be Don Quixote of La Mancha, who, as all the world knows, yesterday received the order of knighthood, and hath to-day righted the greatest wrong and grievance that ever injustice conceived and cruelty perpetrated: who hath to-day plucked the rod from the hand of yonder ruthless oppressor so wantonly lashing that tender child.”\n\nHe now came to a road branching in four directions, and immediately he was reminded of those cross-roads where knights-errant used to stop to consider which road they should take. In imitation of them he halted for a while, and after having deeply considered it, he gave Rocinante his head, submitting his own will to that of his hack, who followed out his first intention, which was to make straight for his own stable. After he had gone about two miles Don Quixote perceived a large party of people, who, as afterwards appeared, were some Toledo traders, on their way to buy silk at Murcia. There were six of them coming along under their sunshades, with four servants mounted, and three muleteers on foot. Scarcely had Don Quixote descried them when the fancy possessed him that this must be some new adventure; and to help him to imitate as far as he could those passages he had read of in his books, here seemed to come one made on purpose, which he resolved to attempt. So with a lofty bearing and determination he fixed himself firmly in his stirrups, got his lance ready, brought his buckler before his breast, and planting himself in the middle of the road, stood waiting the approach of these knights-errant, for such he now considered and held them to be; and when they had come near enough to see and hear, he exclaimed with a haughty gesture, “All the world stand, unless all the world confess that in all the world there is no maiden fairer than the Empress of La Mancha, the peerless Dulcinea del Toboso.”\n\nThe traders halted at the sound of this language and the sight of the strange figure that uttered it, and from both figure and language at once guessed the craze of their owner; they wished, however, to learn quietly what was the object of this confession that was demanded of them, and one of them, who was rather fond of a joke and was very sharp-witted, said to him, “Sir Knight, we do not know who this good lady is that you speak of; show her to us, for, if she be of such beauty as you suggest, with all our hearts and without any pressure we will confess the truth that is on your part required of us.”\n\n“If I were to show her to you,” replied Don Quixote, “what merit would you have in confessing a truth so manifest? The essential point is that without seeing her you must believe, confess, affirm, swear, and defend it; else ye have to do with me in battle, ill-conditioned, arrogant rabble that ye are; and come ye on, one by one as the order of knighthood requires, or all together as is the custom and vile usage of your breed, here do I bide and await you relying on the justice of the cause I maintain.”\n\n“Sir Knight,” replied the trader, “I entreat your worship in the name of this present company of princes, that, to save us from charging our consciences with the confession of a thing we have never seen or heard of, and one moreover so much to the prejudice of the Empresses and Queens of the Alcarria and Estremadura, your worship will be pleased to show us some portrait of this lady, though it be no bigger than a grain of wheat; for by the thread one gets at the ball, and in this way we shall be satisfied and easy, and you will be content and pleased; nay, I believe we are already so far agreed with you that even though her portrait should show her blind of one eye, and distilling vermilion and sulphur from the other, we would nevertheless, to gratify your worship, say all in her favour that you desire.”\n\n“She distils nothing of the kind, vile rabble,” said Don Quixote, burning with rage, “nothing of the kind, I say, only ambergris and civet in cotton; nor is she one-eyed or humpbacked, but straighter than a Guadarrama spindle: but ye must pay for the blasphemy ye have uttered against beauty like that of my lady.”\n\nAnd so saying, he charged with levelled lance against the one who had spoken, with such fury and fierceness that, if luck had not contrived that Rocinante should stumble midway and come down, it would have gone hard with the rash trader. Down went Rocinante, and over went his master, rolling along the ground for some distance; and when he tried to rise he was unable, so encumbered was he with lance, buckler, spurs, helmet, and the weight of his old armour; and all the while he was struggling to get up he kept saying, “Fly not, cowards and caitiffs! stay, for not by my fault, but my horse’s, am I stretched here.”\n\nOne of the muleteers in attendance, who could not have had much good nature in him, hearing the poor prostrate man blustering in this style, was unable to refrain from giving him an answer on his ribs; and coming up to him he seized his lance, and having broken it in pieces, with one of them he began so to belabour our Don Quixote that, notwithstanding and in spite of his armour, he milled him like a measure of wheat. His masters called out not to lay on so hard and to leave him alone, but the muleteer’s blood was up, and he did not care to drop the game until he had vented the rest of his wrath, and gathering up the remaining fragments of the lance he finished with a discharge upon the unhappy victim, who all through the storm of sticks that rained on him never ceased threatening heaven, and earth, and the brigands, for such they seemed to him. At last the muleteer was tired, and the traders continued their journey, taking with them matter for talk about the poor fellow who had been cudgelled. He when he found himself alone made another effort to rise; but if he was unable when whole and sound, how was he to rise after having been thrashed and well-nigh knocked to pieces? And yet he esteemed himself fortunate, as it seemed to him that this was a regular knight-errant’s mishap, and entirely, he considered, the fault of his horse. However, battered in body as he was, to rise was beyond his power.\n\n# IN WHICH THE NARRATIVE OF OUR KNIGHT’S MISHAP IS CONTINUED\n\nFinding, then, that, in fact he could not move, he thought himself of having recourse to his usual remedy, which was to think of some passage in his books, and his craze brought to his mind that about Baldwin and the Marquis of Mantua, when Carloto left him wounded on the mountain side, a story known by heart by the children, not forgotten by the young men, and lauded and even believed by the old folk; and for all that not a whit truer than the miracles of Mahomet. This seemed to him to fit exactly the case in which he found himself, so, making a show of severe suffering, he began to roll on the ground and with feeble breath repeat the very words which the wounded knight of the wood is said to have uttered:\n\nWhere art thou, lady mine, that thou My sorrow dost not rue? Thou canst not know it, lady mine, Or else thou art untrue.\n\nAnd so he went on with the ballad as far as the lines:\n\nO noble Marquis of Mantua, My Uncle and liege lord!\n\nAs chance would have it, when he had got to this line there happened to come by a peasant from his own village, a neighbour of his, who had been with a load of wheat to the mill, and he, seeing the man stretched there, came up to him and asked him who he was and what was the matter with him that he complained so dolefully.\n\nDon Quixote was firmly persuaded that this was the Marquis of Mantua, his uncle, so the only answer he made was to go on with his ballad, in which he told the tale of his misfortune, and of the loves of the Emperor’s son and his wife all exactly as the ballad sings it.\n\nThe peasant stood amazed at hearing such nonsense, and relieving him of the visor, already battered to pieces by blows, he wiped his face, which was covered with dust, and as soon as he had done so he recognised him and said, “Señor Quixada” (for so he appears to have been called when he was in his senses and had not yet changed from a quiet country gentleman into a knight-errant), “who has brought your worship to this pass?” But to all questions the other only went on with his ballad.\n\nSeeing this, the good man removed as well as he could his breastplate and backpiece to see if he had any wound, but he could perceive no blood nor any mark whatever. He then contrived to raise him from the ground, and with no little difficulty hoisted him upon his ass, which seemed to him to be the easiest mount for him; and collecting the arms, even to the splinters of the lance, he tied them on Rocinante, and leading him by the bridle and the ass by the halter he took the road for the village, very sad to hear what absurd stuff Don Quixote was talking.\n\nNor was Don Quixote less so, for what with blows and bruises he could not sit upright on the ass, and from time to time he sent up sighs to heaven, so that once more he drove the peasant to ask what ailed him. And it could have been only the devil himself that put into his head tales to match his own adventures, for now, forgetting Baldwin, he bethought himself of the Moor Abindarraez, when the Alcaide of Antequera, Rodrigo de Narvaez, took him prisoner and carried him away to his castle; so that when the peasant again asked him how he was and what ailed him, he gave him for reply the same words and phrases that the captive Abindarraez gave to Rodrigo de Narvaez, just as he had read the story in the “Diana” of Jorge de Montemayor where it is written, applying it to his own case so aptly that the peasant went along cursing his fate that he had to listen to such a lot of nonsense; from which, however, he came to the conclusion that his neighbour was mad, and so made all haste to reach the village to escape the wearisomeness of this harangue of Don Quixote’s; who, at the end of it, said, “Señor Don Rodrigo de Narvaez, your worship must know that this fair Xarifa I have mentioned is now the lovely Dulcinea del Toboso, for whom I have done, am doing, and will do the most famous deeds of chivalry that in this world have been seen, are to be seen, or ever shall be seen.”\n\nTo this the peasant answered, “Señor--sinner that I am!--cannot your worship see that I am not Don Rodrigo de Narvaez nor the Marquis of Mantua, but Pedro Alonso your neighbour, and that your worship is neither Baldwin nor Abindarraez, but the worthy gentleman Señor Quixada?”\n\n“I know who I am,” replied Don Quixote, “and I know that I may be not only those I have named, but all the Twelve Peers of France and even all the Nine Worthies, since my achievements surpass all that they have done all together and each of them on his own account.”\n\nWith this talk and more of the same kind they reached the village just as night was beginning to fall, but the peasant waited until it was a little later that the belaboured gentleman might not be seen riding in such a miserable trim. When it was what seemed to him the proper time he entered the village and went to Don Quixote’s house, which he found all in confusion, and there were the curate and the village barber, who were great friends of Don Quixote, and his housekeeper was saying to them in a loud voice, “What does your worship think can have befallen my master, Señor Licentiate Pero Perez?” for so the curate was called; “it is three days now since anything has been seen of him, or the hack, or the buckler, lance, or armour. Miserable me! I am certain of it, and it is as true as that I was born to die, that these accursed books of chivalry he has, and has got into the way of reading so constantly, have upset his reason; for now I remember having often heard him saying to himself that he would turn knight-errant and go all over the world in quest of adventures. To the devil and Barabbas with such books, that have brought to ruin in this way the finest understanding there was in all La Mancha!”\n\nThe niece said the same, and, more: “You must know, Master Nicholas”--for that was the name of the barber--“it was often my uncle’s way to stay two days and nights together poring over these unholy books of misventures, after which he would fling the book away and snatch up his sword and fall to slashing the walls; and when he was tired out he would say he had killed four giants like four towers; and the sweat that flowed from him when he was weary he said was the blood of the wounds he had received in battle; and then he would drink a great jug of cold water and become calm and quiet, saying that this water was a most precious potion which the sage Esquife, a great magician and friend of his, had brought him. But I take all the blame upon myself for never having told your worships of my uncle’s vagaries, that you might put a stop to them before things had come to this pass, and burn all these accursed books--for he has a great number--that richly deserve to be burned like heretics.”\n\n“So say I too,” said the curate, “and by my faith to-morrow shall not pass without public judgment upon them, and may they be condemned to the flames lest they lead those that read to behave as my good friend seems to have behaved.”\n\nAll this the peasant heard, and from it he understood at last what was the matter with his neighbour, so he began calling aloud, “Open, your worships, to Señor Baldwin and to Señor the Marquis of Mantua, who comes badly wounded, and to Señor Abindarraez, the Moor, whom the valiant Rodrigo de Narvaez, the Alcaide of Antequera, brings captive.”\n\nAt these words they all hurried out, and when they recognised their friend, master, and uncle, who had not yet dismounted from the ass because he could not, they ran to embrace him.\n\n“Hold!” said he, “for I am badly wounded through my horse’s fault; carry me to bed, and if possible send for the wise Urganda to cure and see to my wounds.”\n\n“See there! plague on it!” cried the housekeeper at this: “did not my heart tell the truth as to which foot my master went lame of? To bed with your worship at once, and we will contrive to cure you here without fetching that Hurgada. A curse I say once more, and a hundred times more, on those books of chivalry that have brought your worship to such a pass.”\n\nThey carried him to bed at once, and after searching for his wounds could find none, but he said they were all bruises from having had a severe fall with his horse Rocinante when in combat with ten giants, the biggest and the boldest to be found on earth.\n\n“So, so!” said the curate, “are there giants in the dance? By the sign of the Cross I will burn them to-morrow before the day is over.”\n\nThey put a host of questions to Don Quixote, but his only answer to all was--give him something to eat, and leave him to sleep, for that was what he needed most. They did so, and the curate questioned the peasant at great length as to how he had found Don Quixote. He told him, and the nonsense he had talked when found and on the way home, all which made the licentiate the more eager to do what he did the next day, which was to summon his friend the barber, Master Nicholas, and go with him to Don Quixote’s house.\n\n# CHAPTER VI.\n\nOF THE DIVERTING AND IMPORTANT SCRUTINY WHICH THE CURATE AND THE BARBER MADE IN THE LIBRARY OF OUR INGENIOUS GENTLEMAN\nHe was still sleeping; so the curate asked the niece for the keys of the room where the books, the authors of all the mischief, were, and right willingly she gave them. They all went in, the housekeeper with them, and found more than a hundred volumes of big books very well bound, and some other small ones. The moment the housekeeper saw them she turned about and ran out of the room, and came back immediately with a saucer of holy water and a sprinkler, saying, “Here, your worship, señor licentiate, sprinkle this room; don’t leave any magician of the many there are in these books to bewitch us in revenge for our design of banishing them from the world.”\n\nThe simplicity of the housekeeper made the licentiate laugh, and he directed the barber to give him the books one by one to see what they were about, as there might be some to be found among them that did not deserve the penalty of fire.\n\n“No,” said the niece, “there is no reason for showing mercy to any of them; they have every one of them done mischief; better fling them out of the window into the court and make a pile of them and set fire to them; or else carry them into the yard, and there a bonfire can be made without the smoke giving any annoyance.” The housekeeper said the same, so eager were they both for the slaughter of those innocents, but the curate would not agree to it without first reading at any rate the titles.\n\nThe first that Master Nicholas put into his hand was “The four books of Amadis of Gaul.” “This seems a mysterious thing,” said the curate, “for, as I have heard say, this was the first book of chivalry printed in Spain, and from this all the others derive their birth and origin; so it seems to me that we ought inexorably to condemn it to the flames as the founder of so vile a sect.”\n\n“Nay, sir,” said the barber, “I too, have heard say that this is the best of all the books of this kind that have been written, and so, as something singular in its line, it ought to be pardoned.”\n\n“True,” said the curate; “and for that reason let its life be spared for the present. Let us see that other which is next to it.”\n\n“It is,” said the barber, “the ‘Sergas de Esplandian,’ the lawful son of Amadis of Gaul.”\n\n“Then verily,” said the curate, “the merit of the father must not be put down to the account of the son. Take it, mistress housekeeper; open the window and fling it into the yard and lay the foundation of the pile for the bonfire we are to make.”\n\nThe housekeeper obeyed with great satisfaction, and the worthy “Esplandian” went flying into the yard to await with all patience the fire that was in store for him.\n\n“Proceed,” said the curate.\n\n“This that comes next,” said the barber, “is ‘Amadis of Greece,’ and, indeed, I believe all those on this side are of the same Amadis lineage.”\n\n“Then to the yard with the whole of them,” said the curate; “for to have the burning of Queen Pintiquiniestra, and the shepherd Darinel and his eclogues, and the bedevilled and involved discourses of his author, I would burn with them the father who begot me if he were going about in the guise of a knight-errant.”\n\n“I am of the same mind,” said the barber.\n\n“And so am I,” added the niece.\n\n“In that case,” said the housekeeper, “here, into the yard with them!”\n\nThey were handed to her, and as there were many of them, she spared herself the staircase, and flung them down out of the window.\n\n“Who is that tub there?” said the curate.\n\n“This,” said the barber, “is ‘Don Olivante de Laura.’”\n\n“The author of that book,” said the curate, “was the same that wrote ‘The Garden of Flowers,’ and truly there is no deciding which of the two books is the more truthful, or, to put it better, the less lying; all I can say is, send this one into the yard for a swaggering fool.”\n\n“This that follows is ‘Florismarte of Hircania,’” said the barber.\n\n“Señor Florismarte here?” said the curate; “then by my faith he must take up his quarters in the yard, in spite of his marvellous birth and visionary adventures, for the stiffness and dryness of his style deserve nothing else; into the yard with him and the other, mistress housekeeper.”\n\n“With all my heart, señor,” said she, and executed the order with great delight.\n\n“This,” said the barber, “is The Knight Platir.’”\n\n“An old book that,” said the curate, “but I find no reason for clemency in it; send it after the others without appeal;” which was done.\n\nAnother book was opened, and they saw it was entitled, “The Knight of the Cross.”\n\n“For the sake of the holy name this book has,” said the curate, “its ignorance might be excused; but then, they say, ‘behind the cross there’s the devil; to the fire with it.”\n\nTaking down another book, the barber said, “This is ‘The Mirror of Chivalry.’”\n\n“I know his worship,” said the curate; “that is where Señor Reinaldos of Montalvan figures with his friends and comrades, greater thieves than Cacus, and the Twelve Peers of France with the veracious historian Turpin; however, I am not for condemning them to more than perpetual banishment, because, at any rate, they have some share in the invention of the famous Matteo Boiardo, whence too the Christian poet Ludovico Ariosto wove his web, to whom, if I find him here, and speaking any language but his own, I shall show no respect whatever; but if he speaks his own tongue I will put him upon my head.”\n\n“Well, I have him in Italian,” said the barber, “but I do not understand him.”\n\n“Nor would it be well that you should understand him,” said the curate, “and on that score we might have excused the Captain if he had not brought him into Spain and turned him into Castilian. He robbed him of a great deal of his natural force, and so do all those who try to turn books written in verse into another language, for, with all the pains they take and all the cleverness they show, they never can reach the level of the originals as they were first produced. In short, I say that this book, and all that may be found treating of those French affairs, should be thrown into or deposited in some dry well, until after more consideration it is settled what is to be done with them; excepting always one ‘Bernardo del Carpio’ that is going about, and another called ‘Roncesvalles;’ for these, if they come into my hands, shall pass at once into those of the housekeeper, and from hers into the fire without any reprieve.”\n\nTo all this the barber gave his assent, and looked upon it as right and proper, being persuaded that the curate was so staunch to the Faith and loyal to the Truth that he would not for the world say anything opposed to them. Opening another book he saw it was “Palmerin de Oliva,” and beside it was another called “Palmerin of England,” seeing which the licentiate said, “Let the Olive be made firewood of at once and burned until no ashes even are left; and let that Palm of England be kept and preserved as a thing that stands alone, and let such another case be made for it as that which Alexander found among the spoils of Darius and set aside for the safe keeping of the works of the poet Homer. This book, gossip, is of authority for two reasons, first because it is very good, and secondly because it is said to have been written by a wise and witty king of Portugal. All the adventures at the Castle of Miraguarda are excellent and of admirable contrivance, and the language is polished and clear, studying and observing the style befitting the speaker with propriety and judgment. So then, provided it seems good to you, Master Nicholas, I say let this and ‘Amadis of Gaul’ be remitted the penalty of fire, and as for all the rest, let them perish without further question or query.”\n\n“Nay, gossip,” said the barber, “for this that I have here is the famous ‘Don Belianis.’”\n\n“Well,” said the curate, “that and the second, third, and fourth parts all stand in need of a little rhubarb to purge their excess of bile, and they must be cleared of all that stuff about the Castle of Fame and other greater affectations, to which end let them be allowed the over-seas term, and, according as they mend, so shall mercy or justice be meted out to them; and in the mean time, gossip, do you keep them in your house and let no one read them.”\n\n“With all my heart,” said the barber; and not caring to tire himself with reading more books of chivalry, he told the housekeeper to take all the big ones and throw them into the yard. It was not said to one dull or deaf, but to one who enjoyed burning them more than weaving the broadest and finest web that could be; and seizing about eight at a time, she flung them out of the window.\n\nIn carrying so many together she let one fall at the feet of the barber, who took it up, curious to know whose it was, and found it said, “History of the Famous Knight, Tirante el Blanco.”\n\n“God bless me!” said the curate with a shout, “‘Tirante el Blanco’ here! Hand it over, gossip, for in it I reckon I have found a treasury of enjoyment and a mine of recreation. Here is Don Kyrieleison of Montalvan, a valiant knight, and his brother Thomas of Montalvan, and the knight Fonseca, with the battle the bold Tirante fought with the mastiff, and the witticisms of the damsel Placerdemivida, and the loves and wiles of the widow Reposada, and the empress in love with the squire Hipolito--in truth, gossip, by right of its style it is the best book in the world. Here knights eat and sleep, and die in their beds, and make their wills before dying, and a great deal more of which there is nothing in all the other books. Nevertheless, I say he who wrote it, for deliberately composing such fooleries, deserves to be sent to the galleys for life. Take it home with you and read it, and you will see that what I have said is true.”\n\n“As you will,” said the barber; “but what are we to do with these little books that are left?”\n\n“These must be, not chivalry, but poetry,” said the curate; and opening one he saw it was the “Diana” of Jorge de Montemayor, and, supposing all the others to be of the same sort, “these,” he said, “do not deserve to be burned like the others, for they neither do nor can do the mischief the books of chivalry have done, being books of entertainment that can hurt no one.”\n\n“Ah, señor!” said the niece, “your worship had better order these to be burned as well as the others; for it would be no wonder if, after being cured of his chivalry disorder, my uncle, by reading these, took a fancy to turn shepherd and range the woods and fields singing and piping; or, what would be still worse, to turn poet, which they say is an incurable and infectious malady.”\n\n“The damsel is right,” said the curate, “and it will be well to put this stumbling-block and temptation out of our friend’s way. To begin, then, with the ‘Diana’ of Montemayor. I am of opinion it should not be burned, but that it should be cleared of all that about the sage Felicia and the magic water, and of almost all the longer pieces of verse: let it keep, and welcome, its prose and the honour of being the first of books of the kind.”\n\n“This that comes next,” said the barber, “is the ‘Diana,’ entitled the ‘Second Part, by the Salamancan,’ and this other has the same title, and its author is Gil Polo.”\n\n“As for that of the Salamancan,” replied the curate, “let it go to swell the number of the condemned in the yard, and let Gil Polo’s be preserved as if it came from Apollo himself: but get on, gossip, and make haste, for it is growing late.”\n\n“This book,” said the barber, opening another, “is the ten books of the ‘Fortune of Love,’ written by Antonio de Lofraso, a Sardinian poet.”\n\n“By the orders I have received,” said the curate, “since Apollo has been Apollo, and the Muses have been Muses, and poets have been poets, so droll and absurd a book as this has never been written, and in its way it is the best and the most singular of all of this species that have as yet appeared, and he who has not read it may be sure he has never read what is delightful. Give it here, gossip, for I make more account of having found it than if they had given me a cassock of Florence stuff.”\n\nHe put it aside with extreme satisfaction, and the barber went on, “These that come next are ‘The Shepherd of Iberia,’ ‘Nymphs of Henares,’ and ‘The Enlightenment of Jealousy.’”\n\n“Then all we have to do,” said the curate, “is to hand them over to the secular arm of the housekeeper, and ask me not why, or we shall never have done.”\n\n“This next is the ‘Pastor de Filida.’”\n\n“No Pastor that,” said the curate, “but a highly polished courtier; let it be preserved as a precious jewel.”\n\n“This large one here,” said the barber, “is called ‘The Treasury of various Poems.’”\n\n“If there were not so many of them,” said the curate, “they would be more relished: this book must be weeded and cleansed of certain vulgarities which it has with its excellences; let it be preserved because the author is a friend of mine, and out of respect for other more heroic and loftier works that he has written.”\n\n“This,” continued the barber, “is the ‘Cancionero’ of Lopez de Maldonado.”\n\n“The author of that book, too,” said the curate, “is a great friend of mine, and his verses from his own mouth are the admiration of all who hear them, for such is the sweetness of his voice that he enchants when he chants them: it gives rather too much of its eclogues, but what is good was never yet plentiful: let it be kept with those that have been set apart. But what book is that next it?”\n\n“The ‘Galatea’ of Miguel de Cervantes,” said the barber.\n\n“That Cervantes has been for many years a great friend of mine, and to my knowledge he has had more experience in reverses than in verses. His book has some good invention in it, it presents us with something but brings nothing to a conclusion: we must wait for the Second Part it promises: perhaps with amendment it may succeed in winning the full measure of grace that is now denied it; and in the mean time do you, señor gossip, keep it shut up in your own quarters.”\n\n“Very good,” said the barber; “and here come three together, the ‘Araucana’ of Don Alonso de Ercilla, the ‘Austriada’ of Juan Rufo, Justice of Cordova, and the ‘Montserrate’ of Christobal de Virues, the Valencian poet.”\n\n“These three books,” said the curate, “are the best that have been written in Castilian in heroic verse, and they may compare with the most famous of Italy; let them be preserved as the richest treasures of poetry that Spain possesses.”\n\nThe curate was tired and would not look into any more books, and so he decided that, “contents uncertified,” all the rest should be burned; but just then the barber held open one, called “The Tears of Angelica.”\n\n“I should have shed tears myself,” said the curate when he heard the title, “had I ordered that book to be burned, for its author was one of the famous poets of the world, not to say of Spain, and was very happy in the translation of some of Ovid’s fables.”\n\n# CHAPTER VII.\n\nOF THE SECOND SALLY OF OUR WORTHY KNIGHT DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA\nAt this instant Don Quixote began shouting out, “Here, here, valiant knights! here is need for you to put forth the might of your strong arms, for they of the Court are gaining the mastery in the tourney!” Called away by this noise and outcry, they proceeded no farther with the scrutiny of the remaining books, and so it is thought that “The Carolea,” “The Lion of Spain,” and “The Deeds of the Emperor,” written by Don Luis de Avila, went to the fire unseen and unheard; for no doubt they were among those that remained, and perhaps if the curate had seen them they would not have undergone so severe a sentence.\n\nWhen they reached Don Quixote he was already out of bed, and was still shouting and raving, and slashing and cutting all round, as wide awake as if he had never slept.\n\nThey closed with him and by force got him back to bed, and when he had become a little calm, addressing the curate, he said to him, “Of a truth, Señor Archbishop Turpin, it is a great disgrace for us who call ourselves the Twelve Peers, so carelessly to allow the knights of the Court to gain the victory in this tourney, we the adventurers having carried off the honour on the three former days.”\n\n“Hush, gossip,” said the curate; “please God, the luck may turn, and what is lost to-day may be won to-morrow; for the present let your worship have a care of your health, for it seems to me that you are over-fatigued, if not badly wounded.”\n\n“Wounded no,” said Don Quixote, “but bruised and battered no doubt, for that bastard Don Roland has cudgelled me with the trunk of an oak tree, and all for envy, because he sees that I alone rival him in his achievements. But I should not call myself Reinaldos of Montalvan did he not pay me for it in spite of all his enchantments as soon as I rise from this bed. For the present let them bring me something to eat, for that, I feel, is what will be more to my purpose, and leave it to me to avenge myself.”\n\nThey did as he wished; they gave him something to eat, and once more he fell asleep, leaving them marvelling at his madness.\n\nThat night the housekeeper burned to ashes all the books that were in the yard and in the whole house; and some must have been consumed that deserved preservation in everlasting archives, but their fate and the laziness of the examiner did not permit it, and so in them was verified the proverb that the innocent suffer for the guilty.\n\nOne of the remedies which the curate and the barber immediately applied to their friend’s disorder was to wall up and plaster the room where the books were, so that when he got up he should not find them (possibly the cause being removed the effect might cease), and they might say that a magician had carried them off, room and all; and this was done with all despatch. Two days later Don Quixote got up, and the first thing he did was to go and look at his books, and not finding the room where he had left it, he wandered from side to side looking for it. He came to the place where the door used to be, and tried it with his hands, and turned and twisted his eyes in every direction without saying a word; but after a good while he asked his housekeeper whereabouts was the room that held his books.\n\nThe housekeeper, who had been already well instructed in what she was to answer, said, “What room or what nothing is it that your worship is looking for? There are neither room nor books in this house now, for the devil himself has carried all away.”\n\n“It was not the devil,” said the niece, “but a magician who came on a cloud one night after the day your worship left this, and dismounting from a serpent that he rode he entered the room, and what he did there I know not, but after a little while he made off, flying through the roof, and left the house full of smoke; and when we went to see what he had done we saw neither book nor room: but we remember very well, the housekeeper and I, that on leaving, the old villain said in a loud voice that, for a private grudge he owed the owner of the books and the room, he had done mischief in that house that would be discovered by-and-by: he said too that his name was the Sage Munaton.”\n\n“He must have said Friston,” said Don Quixote.\n\n“I don’t know whether he called himself Friston or Friton,” said the housekeeper, “I only know that his name ended with ‘ton.’”\n\n“So it does,” said Don Quixote, “and he is a sage magician, a great enemy of mine, who has a spite against me because he knows by his arts and lore that in process of time I am to engage in single combat with a knight whom he befriends and that I am to conquer, and he will be unable to prevent it; and for this reason he endeavours to do me all the ill turns that he can; but I promise him it will be hard for him to oppose or avoid what is decreed by Heaven.”\n\n“Who doubts that?” said the niece; “but, uncle, who mixes you up in these quarrels? Would it not be better to remain at peace in your own house instead of roaming the world looking for better bread than ever came of wheat, never reflecting that many go for wool and come back shorn?”\n\n“Oh, niece of mine,” replied Don Quixote, “how much astray art thou in thy reckoning: ere they shear me I shall have plucked away and stripped off the beards of all who dare to touch only the tip of a hair of mine.”\n\nThe two were unwilling to make any further answer, as they saw that his anger was kindling.\n\nIn short, then, he remained at home fifteen days very quietly without showing any signs of a desire to take up with his former delusions, and during this time he held lively discussions with his two gossips, the curate and the barber, on the point he maintained, that knights-errant were what the world stood most in need of, and that in him was to be accomplished the revival of knight-errantry. The curate sometimes contradicted him, sometimes agreed with him, for if he had not observed this precaution he would have been unable to bring him to reason.\n\nMeanwhile Don Quixote worked upon a farm labourer, a neighbour of his, an honest man (if indeed that title can be given to him who is poor), but with very little wit in his pate. In a word, he so talked him over, and with such persuasions and promises, that the poor clown made up his mind to sally forth with him and serve him as esquire. Don Quixote, among other things, told him he ought to be ready to go with him gladly, because any moment an adventure might occur that might win an island in the twinkling of an eye and leave him governor of it. On these and the like promises Sancho Panza (for so the labourer was called) left wife and children, and engaged himself as esquire to his neighbour.\n\nDon Quixote next set about getting some money; and selling one thing and pawning another, and making a bad bargain in every case, he got together a fair sum. He provided himself with a buckler, which he begged as a loan from a friend, and, restoring his battered helmet as best he could, he warned his squire Sancho of the day and hour he meant to set out, that he might provide himself with what he thought most needful. Above all, he charged him to take alforjas with him. The other said he would, and that he meant to take also a very good ass he had, as he was not much given to going on foot. About the ass, Don Quixote hesitated a little, trying whether he could call to mind any knight-errant taking with him an esquire mounted on ass-back, but no instance occurred to his memory. For all that, however, he determined to take him, intending to furnish him with a more honourable mount when a chance of it presented itself, by appropriating the horse of the first discourteous knight he encountered. Himself he provided with shirts and such other things as he could, according to the advice the host had given him; all which being done, without taking leave, Sancho Panza of his wife and children, or Don Quixote of his housekeeper and niece, they sallied forth unseen by anybody from the village one night, and made such good way in the course of it that by daylight they held themselves safe from discovery, even should search be made for them.\n\nSancho rode on his ass like a patriarch, with his alforjas and bota, and longing to see himself soon governor of the island his master had promised him. Don Quixote decided upon taking the same route and road he had taken on his first journey, that over the Campo de Montiel, which he travelled with less discomfort than on the last occasion, for, as it was early morning and the rays of the sun fell on them obliquely, the heat did not distress them.\n\nAnd now said Sancho Panza to his master, “Your worship will take care, Señor Knight-errant, not to forget about the island you have promised me, for be it ever so big I’ll be equal to governing it.”\n\nTo which Don Quixote replied, “Thou must know, friend Sancho Panza, that it was a practice very much in vogue with the knights-errant of old to make their squires governors of the islands or kingdoms they won, and I am determined that there shall be no failure on my part in so liberal a custom; on the contrary, I mean to improve upon it, for they sometimes, and perhaps most frequently, waited until their squires were old, and then when they had had enough of service and hard days and worse nights, they gave them some title or other, of count, or at the most marquis, of some valley or province more or less; but if thou livest and I live, it may well be that before six days are over, I may have won some kingdom that has others dependent upon it, which will be just the thing to enable thee to be crowned king of one of them. Nor needst thou count this wonderful, for things and chances fall to the lot of such knights in ways so unexampled and unexpected that I might easily give thee even more than I promise thee.”\n\n“In that case,” said Sancho Panza, “if I should become a king by one of those miracles your worship speaks of, even Juana Gutierrez, my old woman, would come to be queen and my children infantes.”\n\n“Well, who doubts it?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“I doubt it,” replied Sancho Panza, “because for my part I am persuaded that though God should shower down kingdoms upon earth, not one of them would fit the head of Mari Gutierrez. Let me tell you, señor, she is not worth two maravedis for a queen; countess will fit her better, and that only with God’s help.”\n\n“Leave it to God, Sancho,” returned Don Quixote, “for he will give her what suits her best; but do not undervalue thyself so much as to come to be content with anything less than being governor of a province.”\n\n“I will not, señor,” answered Sancho, “specially as I have a man of such quality for a master in your worship, who will know how to give me all that will be suitable for me and that I can bear.”\n\n# CHAPTER VIII.\n\nOF THE GOOD FORTUNE WHICH THE VALIANT DON QUIXOTE HAD IN THE TERRIBLE AND UNDREAMT-OF ADVENTURE OF THE WINDMILLS, WITH OTHER OCCURRENCES WORTHY TO BE FITLY RECORDED\n\nAt this point they came in sight of thirty or forty windmills that there are on that plain, and as soon as Don Quixote saw them he said to his squire, “Fortune is arranging matters for us better than we could have shaped our desires ourselves, for look there, friend Sancho Panza, where thirty or more monstrous giants present themselves, all of whom I mean to engage in battle and slay, and with whose spoils we shall begin to make our fortunes; for this is righteous warfare, and it is God’s good service to sweep so evil a breed from off the face of the earth.”\n\n“What giants?” said Sancho Panza.\n\n“Those thou seest there,” answered his master, “with the long arms, and some have them nearly two leagues long.”\n\n“Look, your worship,” said Sancho; “what we see there are not giants but windmills, and what seem to be their arms are the sails that turned by the wind make the millstone go.”\n\n“It is easy to see,” replied Don Quixote, “that thou art not used to this business of adventures; those are giants; and if thou art afraid, away with thee out of this and betake thyself to prayer while I engage them in fierce and unequal combat.”\n\nSo saying, he gave the spur to his steed Rocinante, heedless of the cries his squire Sancho sent after him, warning him that most certainly they were windmills and not giants he was going to attack. He, however, was so positive they were giants that he neither heard the cries of Sancho, nor perceived, near as he was, what they were, but made at them shouting, “Fly not, cowards and vile beings, for a single knight attacks you.”\n\nA slight breeze at this moment sprang up, and the great sails began to move, seeing which Don Quixote exclaimed, “Though ye flourish more arms than the giant Briareus, ye have to reckon with me.”\n\nSo saying, and commending himself with all his heart to his lady Dulcinea, imploring her to support him in such a peril, with lance in rest and covered by his buckler, he charged at Rocinante’s fullest gallop and fell upon the first mill that stood in front of him; but as he drove his lance-point into the sail the wind whirled it round with such force that it shivered the lance to pieces, sweeping with it horse and rider, who went rolling over on the plain, in a sorry condition. Sancho hastened to his assistance as fast as his ass could go, and when he came up found him unable to move, with such a shock had Rocinante fallen with him.\n\n“God bless me!” said Sancho, “did I not tell your worship to mind what you were about, for they were only windmills? and no one could have made any mistake about it but one who had something of the same kind in his head.”\n\n“Hush, friend Sancho,” replied Don Quixote, “the fortunes of war more than any other are liable to frequent fluctuations; and moreover I think, and it is the truth, that that same sage Friston who carried off my study and books, has turned these giants into mills in order to rob me of the glory of vanquishing them, such is the enmity he bears me; but in the end his wicked arts will avail but little against my good sword.”\n\n“God order it as he may,” said Sancho Panza, and helping him to rise got him up again on Rocinante, whose shoulder was half out; and then, discussing the late adventure, they followed the road to Puerto Lapice, for there, said Don Quixote, they could not fail to find adventures in abundance and variety, as it was a great thoroughfare. For all that, he was much grieved at the loss of his lance, and saying so to his squire, he added, “I remember having read how a Spanish knight, Diego Perez de Vargas by name, having broken his sword in battle, tore from an oak a ponderous bough or branch, and with it did such things that day, and pounded so many Moors, that he got the surname of Machuca, and he and his descendants from that day forth were called Vargas y Machuca. I mention this because from the first oak I see I mean to rend such another branch, large and stout like that, with which I am determined and resolved to do such deeds that thou mayest deem thyself very fortunate in being found worthy to come and see them, and be an eyewitness of things that will with difficulty be believed.”\n\n“Be that as God will,” said Sancho, “I believe it all as your worship says it; but straighten yourself a little, for you seem all on one side, may be from the shaking of the fall.”\n\n“That is the truth,” said Don Quixote, “and if I make no complaint of the pain it is because knights-errant are not permitted to complain of any wound, even though their bowels be coming out through it.”\n\n“If so,” said Sancho, “I have nothing to say; but God knows I would rather your worship complained when anything ailed you. For my part, I confess I must complain however small the ache may be; unless this rule about not complaining extends to the squires of knights-errant also.”\n\nDon Quixote could not help laughing at his squire’s simplicity, and he assured him he might complain whenever and however he chose, just as he liked, for, so far, he had never read of anything to the contrary in the order of knighthood.\n\nSancho bade him remember it was dinner-time, to which his master answered that he wanted nothing himself just then, but that he might eat when he had a mind. With this permission Sancho settled himself as comfortably as he could on his beast, and taking out of the alforjas what he had stowed away in them, he jogged along behind his master munching deliberately, and from time to time taking a pull at the bota with a relish that the thirstiest tapster in Malaga might have envied; and while he went on in this way, gulping down draught after draught, he never gave a thought to any of the promises his master had made him, nor did he rate it as hardship but rather as recreation going in quest of adventures, however dangerous they might be. Finally they passed the night among some trees, from one of which Don Quixote plucked a dry branch to serve him after a fashion as a lance, and fixed on it the head he had removed from the broken one. All that night Don Quixote lay awake thinking of his lady Dulcinea, in order to conform to what he had read in his books, how many a night in the forests and deserts knights used to lie sleepless supported by the memory of their mistresses. Not so did Sancho Panza spend it, for having his stomach full of something stronger than chicory water he made but one sleep of it, and, if his master had not called him, neither the rays of the sun beating on his face nor all the cheery notes of the birds welcoming the approach of day would have had power to waken him. On getting up he tried the bota and found it somewhat less full than the night before, which grieved his heart because they did not seem to be on the way to remedy the deficiency readily. Don Quixote did not care to break his fast, for, as has been already said, he confined himself to savoury recollections for nourishment.\n\nThey returned to the road they had set out with, leading to Puerto Lapice, and at three in the afternoon they came in sight of it. “Here, brother Sancho Panza,” said Don Quixote when he saw it, “we may plunge our hands up to the elbows in what they call adventures; but observe, even shouldst thou see me in the greatest danger in the world, thou must not put a hand to thy sword in my defence, unless indeed thou perceivest that those who assail me are rabble or base folk; for in that case thou mayest very properly aid me; but if they be knights it is on no account permitted or allowed thee by the laws of knighthood to help me until thou hast been dubbed a knight.”\n\n“Most certainly, señor,” replied Sancho, “your worship shall be fully obeyed in this matter; all the more as of myself I am peaceful and no friend to mixing in strife and quarrels: it is true that as regards the defence of my own person I shall not give much heed to those laws, for laws human and divine allow each one to defend himself against any assailant whatever.”\n\n“That I grant,” said Don Quixote, “but in this matter of aiding me against knights thou must put a restraint upon thy natural impetuosity.”\n\n“I will do so, I promise you,” answered Sancho, “and will keep this precept as carefully as Sunday.”\n\nWhile they were thus talking there appeared on the road two friars of the order of St. Benedict, mounted on two dromedaries, for not less tall were the two mules they rode on. They wore travelling spectacles and carried sunshades; and behind them came a coach attended by four or five persons on horseback and two muleteers on foot. In the coach there was, as afterwards appeared, a Biscay lady on her way to Seville, where her husband was about to take passage for the Indies with an appointment of high honour. The friars, though going the same road, were not in her company; but the moment Don Quixote perceived them he said to his squire, “Either I am mistaken, or this is going to be the most famous adventure that has ever been seen, for those black bodies we see there must be, and doubtless are, magicians who are carrying off some stolen princess in that coach, and with all my might I must undo this wrong.”\n\n“This will be worse than the windmills,” said Sancho. “Look, señor; those are friars of St. Benedict, and the coach plainly belongs to some travellers: I tell you to mind well what you are about and don’t let the devil mislead you.”\n\n“I have told thee already, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote, “that on the subject of adventures thou knowest little. What I say is the truth, as thou shalt see presently.”\n\nSo saying, he advanced and posted himself in the middle of the road along which the friars were coming, and as soon as he thought they had come near enough to hear what he said, he cried aloud, “Devilish and unnatural beings, release instantly the highborn princesses whom you are carrying off by force in this coach, else prepare to meet a speedy death as the just punishment of your evil deeds.”\n\nThe friars drew rein and stood wondering at the appearance of Don Quixote as well as at his words, to which they replied, “Señor Caballero, we are not devilish or unnatural, but two brothers of St. Benedict following our road, nor do we know whether or not there are any captive princesses coming in this coach.”\n\n“No soft words with me, for I know you, lying rabble,” said Don Quixote, and without waiting for a reply he spurred Rocinante and with levelled lance charged the first friar with such fury and determination, that, if the friar had not flung himself off the mule, he would have brought him to the ground against his will, and sore wounded, if not killed outright. The second brother, seeing how his comrade was treated, drove his heels into his castle of a mule and made off across the country faster than the wind.\n\nSancho Panza, when he saw the friar on the ground, dismounting briskly from his ass, rushed towards him and began to strip off his gown. At that instant the friars’ muleteers came up and asked what he was stripping him for. Sancho answered them that this fell to him lawfully as spoil of the battle which his lord Don Quixote had won. The muleteers, who had no idea of a joke and did not understand all this about battles and spoils, seeing that Don Quixote was some distance off talking to the travellers in the coach, fell upon Sancho, knocked him down, and leaving hardly a hair in his beard, belaboured him with kicks and left him stretched breathless and senseless on the ground; and without any more delay helped the friar to mount, who, trembling, terrified, and pale, as soon as he found himself in the saddle, spurred after his companion, who was standing at a distance looking on, watching the result of the onslaught; then, not caring to wait for the end of the affair just begun, they pursued their journey making more crosses than if they had the devil after them.\n\nDon Quixote was, as has been said, speaking to the lady in the coach: “Your beauty, lady mine,” said he, “may now dispose of your person as may be most in accordance with your pleasure, for the pride of your ravishers lies prostrate on the ground through this strong arm of mine; and lest you should be pining to know the name of your deliverer, know that I am called Don Quixote of La Mancha, knight-errant and adventurer, and captive to the peerless and beautiful lady Dulcinea del Toboso: and in return for the service you have received of me I ask no more than that you should return to El Toboso, and on my behalf present yourself before that lady and tell her what I have done to set you free.”\n\nOne of the squires in attendance upon the coach, a Biscayan, was listening to all Don Quixote was saying, and, perceiving that he would not allow the coach to go on, but was saying it must return at once to El Toboso, he made at him, and seizing his lance addressed him in bad Castilian and worse Biscayan after his fashion, “Begone, caballero, and ill go with thee; by the God that made me, unless thou quittest coach, slayest thee as art here a Biscayan.”\n\nDon Quixote understood him quite well, and answered him very quietly, “If thou wert a knight, as thou art none, I should have already chastised thy folly and rashness, miserable creature.” To which the Biscayan returned, “I no gentleman!--I swear to God thou liest as I am Christian: if thou droppest lance and drawest sword, soon shalt thou see thou art carrying water to the cat: Biscayan on land, hidalgo at sea, hidalgo at the devil, and look, if thou sayest otherwise thou liest.”\n\n“‘“You will see presently,” said Agrajes,’” replied Don Quixote; and throwing his lance on the ground he drew his sword, braced his buckler on his arm, and attacked the Biscayan, bent upon taking his life.\n\nThe Biscayan, when he saw him coming on, though he wished to dismount from his mule, in which, being one of those sorry ones let out for hire, he had no confidence, had no choice but to draw his sword; it was lucky for him, however, that he was near the coach, from which he was able to snatch a cushion that served him for a shield; and they went at one another as if they had been two mortal enemies. The others strove to make peace between them, but could not, for the Biscayan declared in his disjointed phrase that if they did not let him finish his battle he would kill his mistress and everyone that strove to prevent him. The lady in the coach, amazed and terrified at what she saw, ordered the coachman to draw aside a little, and set herself to watch this severe struggle, in the course of which the Biscayan smote Don Quixote a mighty stroke on the shoulder over the top of his buckler, which, given to one without armour, would have cleft him to the waist. Don Quixote, feeling the weight of this prodigious blow, cried aloud, saying, “O lady of my soul, Dulcinea, flower of beauty, come to the aid of this your knight, who, in fulfilling his obligations to your beauty, finds himself in this extreme peril.” To say this, to lift his sword, to shelter himself well behind his buckler, and to assail the Biscayan was the work of an instant, determined as he was to venture all upon a single blow. The Biscayan, seeing him come on in this way, was convinced of his courage by his spirited bearing, and resolved to follow his example, so he waited for him keeping well under cover of his cushion, being unable to execute any sort of manoeuvre with his mule, which, dead tired and never meant for this kind of game, could not stir a step.\n\nOn, then, as aforesaid, came Don Quixote against the wary Biscayan, with uplifted sword and a firm intention of splitting him in half, while on his side the Biscayan waited for him sword in hand, and under the protection of his cushion; and all present stood trembling, waiting in suspense the result of blows such as threatened to fall, and the lady in the coach and the rest of her following were making a thousand vows and offerings to all the images and shrines of Spain, that God might deliver her squire and all of them from this great peril in which they found themselves. But it spoils all, that at this point and crisis the author of the history leaves this battle impending, giving as excuse that he could find nothing more written about these achievements of Don Quixote than what has been already set forth. It is true the second author of this work was unwilling to believe that a history so curious could have been allowed to fall under the sentence of oblivion, or that the wits of La Mancha could have been so undiscerning as not to preserve in their archives or registries some documents referring to this famous knight; and this being his persuasion, he did not despair of finding the conclusion of this pleasant history, which, heaven favouring him, he did find in a way that shall be related in the Second Part.\n\n# CHAPTER IX.\n\nIN WHICH IS CONCLUDED AND FINISHED THE TERRIFIC BATTLE BETWEEN THE GALLANT BISCAYAN AND THE VALIANT MANCHEGAN\nIn the First Part of this history we left the valiant Biscayan and the renowned Don Quixote with drawn swords uplifted, ready to deliver two such furious slashing blows that if they had fallen full and fair they would at least have split and cleft them asunder from top to toe and laid them open like a pomegranate; and at this so critical point the delightful history came to a stop and stood cut short without any intimation from the author where what was missing was to be found.\n\nThis distressed me greatly, because the pleasure derived from having read such a small portion turned to vexation at the thought of the poor chance that presented itself of finding the large part that, so it seemed to me, was missing of such an interesting tale. It appeared to me to be a thing impossible and contrary to all precedent that so good a knight should have been without some sage to undertake the task of writing his marvellous achievements; a thing that was never wanting to any of those knights-errant who, they say, went after adventures; for every one of them had one or two sages as if made on purpose, who not only recorded their deeds but described their most trifling thoughts and follies, however secret they might be; and such a good knight could not have been so unfortunate as not to have what Platir and others like him had in abundance. And so I could not bring myself to believe that such a gallant tale had been left maimed and mutilated, and I laid the blame on Time, the devourer and destroyer of all things, that had either concealed or consumed it.\n\nOn the other hand, it struck me that, inasmuch as among his books there had been found such modern ones as “The Enlightenment of Jealousy” and the “Nymphs and Shepherds of Henares,” his story must likewise be modern, and that though it might not be written, it might exist in the memory of the people of his village and of those in the neighbourhood. This reflection kept me perplexed and longing to know really and truly the whole life and wondrous deeds of our famous Spaniard, Don Quixote of La Mancha, light and mirror of Manchegan chivalry, and the first that in our age and in these so evil days devoted himself to the labour and exercise of the arms of knight-errantry, righting wrongs, succouring widows, and protecting damsels of that sort that used to ride about, whip in hand, on their palfreys, with all their virginity about them, from mountain to mountain and valley to valley--for, if it were not for some ruffian, or boor with a hood and hatchet, or monstrous giant, that forced them, there were in days of yore damsels that at the end of eighty years, in all which time they had never slept a day under a roof, went to their graves as much maids as the mothers that bore them. I say, then, that in these and other respects our gallant Don Quixote is worthy of everlasting and notable praise, nor should it be withheld even from me for the labour and pains spent in searching for the conclusion of this delightful history; though I know well that if Heaven, chance and good fortune had not helped me, the world would have remained deprived of an entertainment and pleasure that for a couple of hours or so may well occupy him who shall read it attentively. The discovery of it occurred in this way.\n\nOne day, as I was in the Alcana of Toledo, a boy came up to sell some pamphlets and old papers to a silk mercer, and, as I am fond of reading even the very scraps of paper in the streets, led by this natural bent of mine I took up one of the pamphlets the boy had for sale, and saw that it was in characters which I recognised as Arabic, and as I was unable to read them though I could recognise them, I looked about to see if there were any Spanish-speaking Morisco at hand to read them for me; nor was there any great difficulty in finding such an interpreter, for even had I sought one for an older and better language I should have found him. In short, chance provided me with one, who when I told him what I wanted and put the book into his hands, opened it in the middle and after reading a little in it began to laugh. I asked him what he was laughing at, and he replied that it was at something the book had written in the margin by way of a note. I bade him tell it to me; and he still laughing said, “In the margin, as I told you, this is written: ‘This Dulcinea del Toboso so often mentioned in this history, had, they say, the best hand of any woman in all La Mancha for salting pigs.’”\n\nWhen I heard Dulcinea del Toboso named, I was struck with surprise and amazement, for it occurred to me at once that these pamphlets contained the history of Don Quixote. With this idea I pressed him to read the beginning, and doing so, turning the Arabic offhand into Castilian, he told me it meant, “History of Don Quixote of La Mancha, written by Cide Hamete Benengeli, an Arab historian.” It required great caution to hide the joy I felt when the title of the book reached my ears, and snatching it from the silk mercer, I bought all the papers and pamphlets from the boy for half a real; and if he had had his wits about him and had known how eager I was for them, he might have safely calculated on making more than six reals by the bargain. I withdrew at once with the Morisco into the cloister of the cathedral, and begged him to turn all these pamphlets that related to Don Quixote into the Castilian tongue, without omitting or adding anything to them, offering him whatever payment he pleased. He was satisfied with two arrobas of raisins and two bushels of wheat, and promised to translate them faithfully and with all despatch; but to make the matter easier, and not to let such a precious find out of my hands, I took him to my house, where in little more than a month and a half he translated the whole just as it is set down here.\n\nIn the first pamphlet the battle between Don Quixote and the Biscayan was drawn to the very life, they planted in the same attitude as the history describes, their swords raised, and the one protected by his buckler, the other by his cushion, and the Biscayan’s mule so true to nature that it could be seen to be a hired one a bowshot off. The Biscayan had an inscription under his feet which said, “Don Sancho de Azpeitia,” which no doubt must have been his name; and at the feet of Rocinante was another that said, “Don Quixote.” Rocinante was marvellously portrayed, so long and thin, so lank and lean, with so much backbone and so far gone in consumption, that he showed plainly with what judgment and propriety the name of Rocinante had been bestowed upon him. Near him was Sancho Panza holding the halter of his ass, at whose feet was another label that said, “Sancho Zancas,” and according to the picture, he must have had a big belly, a short body, and long shanks, for which reason, no doubt, the names of Panza and Zancas were given him, for by these two surnames the history several times calls him. Some other trifling particulars might be mentioned, but they are all of slight importance and have nothing to do with the true relation of the history; and no history can be bad so long as it is true.\n\nIf against the present one any objection be raised on the score of its truth, it can only be that its author was an Arab, as lying is a very common propensity with those of that nation; though, as they are such enemies of ours, it is conceivable that there were omissions rather than additions made in the course of it. And this is my own opinion; for, where he could and should give freedom to his pen in praise of so worthy a knight, he seems to me deliberately to pass it over in silence; which is ill done and worse contrived, for it is the business and duty of historians to be exact, truthful, and wholly free from passion, and neither interest nor fear, hatred nor love, should make them swerve from the path of truth, whose mother is history, rival of time, storehouse of deeds, witness for the past, example and counsel for the present, and warning for the future. In this I know will be found all that can be desired in the pleasantest, and if it be wanting in any good quality, I maintain it is the fault of its hound of an author and not the fault of the subject. To be brief, its Second Part, according to the translation, began in this way:\n\nWith trenchant swords upraised and poised on high, it seemed as though the two valiant and wrathful combatants stood threatening heaven, and earth, and hell, with such resolution and determination did they bear themselves. The fiery Biscayan was the first to strike a blow, which was delivered with such force and fury that had not the sword turned in its course, that single stroke would have sufficed to put an end to the bitter struggle and to all the adventures of our knight; but that good fortune which reserved him for greater things, turned aside the sword of his adversary, so that although it smote him upon the left shoulder, it did him no more harm than to strip all that side of its armour, carrying away a great part of his helmet with half of his ear, all which with fearful ruin fell to the ground, leaving him in a sorry plight.\n\nGood God! Who is there that could properly describe the rage that filled the heart of our Manchegan when he saw himself dealt with in this fashion? All that can be said is, it was such that he again raised himself in his stirrups, and, grasping his sword more firmly with both hands, he came down on the Biscayan with such fury, smiting him full over the cushion and over the head, that--even so good a shield proving useless--as if a mountain had fallen on him, he began to bleed from nose, mouth, and ears, reeling as if about to fall backwards from his mule, as no doubt he would have done had he not flung his arms about its neck; at the same time, however, he slipped his feet out of the stirrups and then unclasped his arms, and the mule, taking fright at the terrible blow, made off across the plain, and with a few plunges flung its master to the ground. Don Quixote stood looking on very calmly, and, when he saw him fall, leaped from his horse and with great briskness ran to him, and, presenting the point of his sword to his eyes, bade him surrender, or he would cut his head off. The Biscayan was so bewildered that he was unable to answer a word, and it would have gone hard with him, so blind was Don Quixote, had not the ladies in the coach, who had hitherto been watching the combat in great terror, hastened to where he stood and implored him with earnest entreaties to grant them the great grace and favour of sparing their squire’s life; to which Don Quixote replied with much gravity and dignity, “In truth, fair ladies, I am well content to do what ye ask of me; but it must be on one condition and understanding, which is that this knight promise me to go to the village of El Toboso, and on my behalf present himself before the peerless lady Dulcinea, that she deal with him as shall be most pleasing to her.”\n\nThe terrified and disconsolate ladies, without discussing Don Quixote’s demand or asking who Dulcinea might be, promised that their squire should do all that had been commanded.\n\n“Then, on the faith of that promise,” said Don Quixote, “I shall do him no further harm, though he well deserves it of me.”\n\n# CHAPTER X.\n\nOF THE PLEASANT DISCOURSE THAT PASSED BETWEEN DON QUIXOTE AND HIS SQUIRE SANCHO PANZA\nNow by this time Sancho had risen, rather the worse for the handling of the friars’ muleteers, and stood watching the battle of his master, Don Quixote, and praying to God in his heart that it might be his will to grant him the victory, and that he might thereby win some island to make him governor of, as he had promised. Seeing, therefore, that the struggle was now over, and that his master was returning to mount Rocinante, he approached to hold the stirrup for him, and, before he could mount, he went on his knees before him, and taking his hand, kissed it saying, “May it please your worship, Señor Don Quixote, to give me the government of that island which has been won in this hard fight, for be it ever so big I feel myself in sufficient force to be able to govern it as much and as well as anyone in the world who has ever governed islands.”\n\nTo which Don Quixote replied, “Thou must take notice, brother Sancho, that this adventure and those like it are not adventures of islands, but of cross-roads, in which nothing is got except a broken head or an ear the less: have patience, for adventures will present themselves from which I may make you, not only a governor, but something more.”\n\nSancho gave him many thanks, and again kissing his hand and the skirt of his hauberk, helped him to mount Rocinante, and mounting his ass himself, proceeded to follow his master, who at a brisk pace, without taking leave, or saying anything further to the ladies belonging to the coach, turned into a wood that was hard by. Sancho followed him at his ass’s best trot, but Rocinante stepped out so that, seeing himself left behind, he was forced to call to his master to wait for him. Don Quixote did so, reining in Rocinante until his weary squire came up, who on reaching him said, “It seems to me, señor, it would be prudent in us to go and take refuge in some church, for, seeing how mauled he with whom you fought has been left, it will be no wonder if they give information of the affair to the Holy Brotherhood and arrest us, and, faith, if they do, before we come out of gaol we shall have to sweat for it.”\n\n“Peace,” said Don Quixote; “where hast thou ever seen or heard that a knight-errant has been arraigned before a court of justice, however many homicides he may have committed?”\n\n“I know nothing about omecils,” answered Sancho, “nor in my life have had anything to do with one; I only know that the Holy Brotherhood looks after those who fight in the fields, and in that other matter I do not meddle.”\n\n“Then thou needst have no uneasiness, my friend,” said Don Quixote, “for I will deliver thee out of the hands of the Chaldeans, much more out of those of the Brotherhood. But tell me, as thou livest, hast thou seen a more valiant knight than I in all the known world; hast thou read in history of any who has or had higher mettle in attack, more spirit in maintaining it, more dexterity in wounding or skill in overthrowing?”\n\n“The truth is,” answered Sancho, “that I have never read any history, for I can neither read nor write, but what I will venture to bet is that a more daring master than your worship I have never served in all the days of my life, and God grant that this daring be not paid for where I have said; what I beg of your worship is to dress your wound, for a great deal of blood flows from that ear, and I have here some lint and a little white ointment in the alforjas.”\n\n“All that might be well dispensed with,” said Don Quixote, “if I had remembered to make a vial of the balsam of Fierabras, for time and medicine are saved by one single drop.”\n\n“What vial and what balsam is that?” said Sancho Panza.\n\n“It is a balsam,” answered Don Quixote, “the receipt of which I have in my memory, with which one need have no fear of death, or dread dying of any wound; and so when I make it and give it to thee thou hast nothing to do when in some battle thou seest they have cut me in half through the middle of the body--as is wont to happen frequently,--but neatly and with great nicety, ere the blood congeal, to place that portion of the body which shall have fallen to the ground upon the other half which remains in the saddle, taking care to fit it on evenly and exactly. Then thou shalt give me to drink but two drops of the balsam I have mentioned, and thou shalt see me become sounder than an apple.”\n\n“If that be so,” said Panza, “I renounce henceforth the government of the promised island, and desire nothing more in payment of my many and faithful services than that your worship give me the receipt of this supreme liquor, for I am persuaded it will be worth more than two reals an ounce anywhere, and I want no more to pass the rest of my life in ease and honour; but it remains to be told if it costs much to make it.”\n\n“With less than three reals, six quarts of it may be made,” said Don Quixote.\n\n“Sinner that I am!” said Sancho, “then why does your worship put off making it and teaching it to me?”\n\n“Peace, friend,” answered Don Quixote; “greater secrets I mean to teach thee and greater favours to bestow upon thee; and for the present let us see to the dressing, for my ear pains me more than I could wish.”\n\nSancho took out some lint and ointment from the alforjas; but when Don Quixote came to see his helmet shattered, he was like to lose his senses, and clapping his hand upon his sword and raising his eyes to heaven, be said, “I swear by the Creator of all things and the four Gospels in their fullest extent, to do as the great Marquis of Mantua did when he swore to avenge the death of his nephew Baldwin (and that was not to eat bread from a table-cloth, nor embrace his wife, and other points which, though I cannot now call them to mind, I here grant as expressed) until I take complete vengeance upon him who has committed such an offence against me.”\n\nHearing this, Sancho said to him, “Your worship should bear in mind, Señor Don Quixote, that if the knight has done what was commanded him in going to present himself before my lady Dulcinea del Toboso, he will have done all that he was bound to do, and does not deserve further punishment unless he commits some new offence.”\n\n“Thou hast said well and hit the point,” answered Don Quixote; and so I recall the oath in so far as relates to taking fresh vengeance on him, but I make and confirm it anew to lead the life I have said until such time as I take by force from some knight another helmet such as this and as good; and think not, Sancho, that I am raising smoke with straw in doing so, for I have one to imitate in the matter, since the very same thing to a hair happened in the case of Mambrino’s helmet, which cost Sacripante so dear.”\n\n“Señor,” replied Sancho, “let your worship send all such oaths to the devil, for they are very pernicious to salvation and prejudicial to the conscience; just tell me now, if for several days to come we fall in with no man armed with a helmet, what are we to do? Is the oath to be observed in spite of all the inconvenience and discomfort it will be to sleep in your clothes, and not to sleep in a house, and a thousand other mortifications contained in the oath of that old fool the Marquis of Mantua, which your worship is now wanting to revive? Let your worship observe that there are no men in armour travelling on any of these roads, nothing but carriers and carters, who not only do not wear helmets, but perhaps never heard tell of them all their lives.”\n\n“Thou art wrong there,” said Don Quixote, “for we shall not have been above two hours among these cross-roads before we see more men in armour than came to Albraca to win the fair Angelica.”\n\n“Enough,” said Sancho; “so be it then, and God grant us success, and that the time for winning that island which is costing me so dear may soon come, and then let me die.”\n\n“I have already told thee, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “not to give thyself any uneasiness on that score; for if an island should fail, there is the kingdom of Denmark, or of Sobradisa, which will fit thee as a ring fits the finger, and all the more that, being on terra firma, thou wilt all the better enjoy thyself. But let us leave that to its own time; see if thou hast anything for us to eat in those alforjas, because we must presently go in quest of some castle where we may lodge to-night and make the balsam I told thee of, for I swear to thee by God, this ear is giving me great pain.”\n\n“I have here an onion and a little cheese and a few scraps of bread,” said Sancho, “but they are not victuals fit for a valiant knight like your worship.”\n\n“How little thou knowest about it,” answered Don Quixote; “I would have thee to know, Sancho, that it is the glory of knights-errant to go without eating for a month, and even when they do eat, that it should be of what comes first to hand; and this would have been clear to thee hadst thou read as many histories as I have, for, though they are very many, among them all I have found no mention made of knights-errant eating, unless by accident or at some sumptuous banquets prepared for them, and the rest of the time they passed in dalliance. And though it is plain they could not do without eating and performing all the other natural functions, because, in fact, they were men like ourselves, it is plain too that, wandering as they did the most part of their lives through woods and wilds and without a cook, their most usual fare would be rustic viands such as those thou now offer me; so that, friend Sancho, let not that distress thee which pleases me, and do not seek to make a new world or pervert knight-errantry.”\n\n“Pardon me, your worship,” said Sancho, “for, as I cannot read or write, as I said just now, I neither know nor comprehend the rules of the profession of chivalry: henceforward I will stock the alforjas with every kind of dry fruit for your worship, as you are a knight; and for myself, as I am not one, I will furnish them with poultry and other things more substantial.”\n\n“I do not say, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote, “that it is imperative on knights-errant not to eat anything else but the fruits thou speakest of; only that their more usual diet must be those, and certain herbs they found in the fields which they knew and I know too.”\n\n“A good thing it is,” answered Sancho, “to know those herbs, for to my thinking it will be needful some day to put that knowledge into practice.”\n\nAnd here taking out what he said he had brought, the pair made their repast peaceably and sociably. But anxious to find quarters for the night, they with all despatch made an end of their poor dry fare, mounted at once, and made haste to reach some habitation before night set in; but daylight and the hope of succeeding in their object failed them close by the huts of some goatherds, so they determined to pass the night there, and it was as much to Sancho’s discontent not to have reached a house, as it was to his master’s satisfaction to sleep under the open heaven, for he fancied that each time this happened to him he performed an act of ownership that helped to prove his chivalry.\n\n# WHAT BEFELL DON QUIXOTE WITH CERTAIN GOATHERDS\n\nHe was cordially welcomed by the goatherds, and Sancho, having as best he could put up Rocinante and the ass, drew towards the fragrance that came from some pieces of salted goat simmering in a pot on the fire; and though he would have liked at once to try if they were ready to be transferred from the pot to the stomach, he refrained from doing so as the goatherds removed them from the fire, and laying sheepskins on the ground, quickly spread their rude table, and with signs of hearty good-will invited them both to share what they had. Round the skins six of the men belonging to the fold seated themselves, having first with rough politeness pressed Don Quixote to take a seat upon a trough which they placed for him upside down. Don Quixote seated himself, and Sancho remained standing to serve the cup, which was made of horn. Seeing him standing, his master said to him:\n\n“That thou mayest see, Sancho, the good that knight-errantry contains in itself, and how those who fill any office in it are on the high road to be speedily honoured and esteemed by the world, I desire that thou seat thyself here at my side and in the company of these worthy people, and that thou be one with me who am thy master and natural lord, and that thou eat from my plate and drink from whatever I drink from; for the same may be said of knight-errantry as of love, that it levels all.”\n\n“Great thanks,” said Sancho, “but I may tell your worship that provided I have enough to eat, I can eat it as well, or better, standing, and by myself, than seated alongside of an emperor. And indeed, if the truth is to be told, what I eat in my corner without form or fuss has much more relish for me, even though it be bread and onions, than the turkeys of those other tables where I am forced to chew slowly, drink little, wipe my mouth every minute, and cannot sneeze or cough if I want or do other things that are the privileges of liberty and solitude. So, señor, as for these honours which your worship would put upon me as a servant and follower of knight-errantry, exchange them for other things which may be of more use and advantage to me; for these, though I fully acknowledge them as received, I renounce from this moment to the end of the world.”\n\n“For all that,” said Don Quixote, “thou must seat thyself, because him who humbleth himself God exalteth;” and seizing him by the arm he forced him to sit down beside himself.\n\nThe goatherds did not understand this jargon about squires and knights-errant, and all they did was to eat in silence and stare at their guests, who with great elegance and appetite were stowing away pieces as big as one’s fist. The course of meat finished, they spread upon the sheepskins a great heap of parched acorns, and with them they put down a half cheese harder than if it had been made of mortar. All this while the horn was not idle, for it went round so constantly, now full, now empty, like the bucket of a water-wheel, that it soon drained one of the two wine-skins that were in sight. When Don Quixote had quite appeased his appetite he took up a handful of the acorns, and contemplating them attentively delivered himself somewhat in this fashion:\n\n“Happy the age, happy the time, to which the ancients gave the name of golden, not because in that fortunate age the gold so coveted in this our iron one was gained without toil, but because they that lived in it knew not the two words “mine” and “thine”! In that blessed age all things were in common; to win the daily food no labour was required of any save to stretch forth his hand and gather it from the sturdy oaks that stood generously inviting him with their sweet ripe fruit. The clear streams and running brooks yielded their savoury limpid waters in noble abundance. The busy and sagacious bees fixed their republic in the clefts of the rocks and hollows of the trees, offering without usance the plenteous produce of their fragrant toil to every hand. The mighty cork trees, unenforced save of their own courtesy, shed the broad light bark that served at first to roof the houses supported by rude stakes, a protection against the inclemency of heaven alone. Then all was peace, all friendship, all concord; as yet the dull share of the crooked plough had not dared to rend and pierce the tender bowels of our first mother that without compulsion yielded from every portion of her broad fertile bosom all that could satisfy, sustain, and delight the children that then possessed her. Then was it that the innocent and fair young shepherdess roamed from vale to vale and hill to hill, with flowing locks, and no more garments than were needful modestly to cover what modesty seeks and ever sought to hide. Nor were their ornaments like those in use to-day, set off by Tyrian purple, and silk tortured in endless fashions, but the wreathed leaves of the green dock and ivy, wherewith they went as bravely and becomingly decked as our Court dames with all the rare and far-fetched artifices that idle curiosity has taught them. Then the love-thoughts of the heart clothed themselves simply and naturally as the heart conceived them, nor sought to commend themselves by forced and rambling verbiage. Fraud, deceit, or malice had then not yet mingled with truth and sincerity. Justice held her ground, undisturbed and unassailed by the efforts of favour and of interest, that now so much impair, pervert, and beset her. Arbitrary law had not yet established itself in the mind of the judge, for then there was no cause to judge and no one to be judged. Maidens and modesty, as I have said, wandered at will alone and unattended, without fear of insult from lawlessness or libertine assault, and if they were undone it was of their own will and pleasure. But now in this hateful age of ours not one is safe, not though some new labyrinth like that of Crete conceal and surround her; even there the pestilence of gallantry will make its way to them through chinks or on the air by the zeal of its accursed importunity, and, despite of all seclusion, lead them to ruin. In defence of these, as time advanced and wickedness increased, the order of knights-errant was instituted, to defend maidens, to protect widows and to succour the orphans and the needy. To this order I belong, brother goatherds, to whom I return thanks for the hospitality and kindly welcome ye offer me and my squire; for though by natural law all living are bound to show favour to knights-errant, yet, seeing that without knowing this obligation ye have welcomed and feasted me, it is right that with all the good-will in my power I should thank you for yours.”\n\nAll this long harangue (which might very well have been spared) our knight delivered because the acorns they gave him reminded him of the golden age; and the whim seized him to address all this unnecessary argument to the goatherds, who listened to him gaping in amazement without saying a word in reply. Sancho likewise held his peace and ate acorns, and paid repeated visits to the second wine-skin, which they had hung up on a cork tree to keep the wine cool.\n\nDon Quixote was longer in talking than the supper in finishing, at the end of which one of the goatherds said, “That your worship, señor knight-errant, may say with more truth that we show you hospitality with ready good-will, we will give you amusement and pleasure by making one of our comrades sing: he will be here before long, and he is a very intelligent youth and deep in love, and what is more he can read and write and play on the rebeck to perfection.”\n\nThe goatherd had hardly done speaking, when the notes of the rebeck reached their ears; and shortly after, the player came up, a very good-looking young man of about two-and-twenty. His comrades asked him if he had supped, and on his replying that he had, he who had already made the offer said to him:\n\n“In that case, Antonio, thou mayest as well do us the pleasure of singing a little, that the gentleman, our guest, may see that even in the mountains and woods there are musicians: we have told him of thy accomplishments, and we want thee to show them and prove that we say true; so, as thou livest, pray sit down and sing that ballad about thy love that thy uncle the prebendary made thee, and that was so much liked in the town.”\n\n“With all my heart,” said the young man, and without waiting for more pressing he seated himself on the trunk of a felled oak, and tuning his rebeck, presently began to sing to these words.\n\n# ANTONIO’S BALLAD\n\nThou dost love me well, Olalla; Well I know it, even though Love’s mute tongues, thine eyes, have never By their glances told me so.\n\nFor I know my love thou knowest, Therefore thine to claim I dare: Once it ceases to be secret, Love need never feel despair.\n\nTrue it is, Olalla, sometimes Thou hast all too plainly shown That thy heart is brass in hardness, And thy snowy bosom stone.\n\nYet for all that, in thy coyness, And thy fickle fits between, Hope is there--at least the border Of her garment may be seen.\n\nLures to faith are they, those glimpses, And to faith in thee I hold; Kindness cannot make it stronger, Coldness cannot make it cold.\n\nIf it be that love is gentle, In thy gentleness I see Something holding out assurance To the hope of winning thee.\n\nIf it be that in devotion Lies a power hearts to move, That which every day I show thee, Helpful to my suit should prove.\n\nMany a time thou must have noticed-- If to notice thou dost care-- How I go about on Monday Dressed in all my Sunday wear.\n\nLove’s eyes love to look on brightness; Love loves what is gaily drest; Sunday, Monday, all I care is Thou shouldst see me in my best.\n\nNo account I make of dances, Or of strains that pleased thee so, Keeping thee awake from midnight Till the cocks began to crow;\n\nOr of how I roundly swore it That there’s none so fair as thou; True it is, but as I said it, By the girls I’m hated now.\n\nFor Teresa of the hillside At my praise of thee was sore; Said, “You think you love an angel; It’s a monkey you adore;\n\n“Caught by all her glittering trinkets, And her borrowed braids of hair, And a host of made-up beauties That would Love himself ensnare.”\n\n‘T was a lie, and so I told her, And her cousin at the word Gave me his defiance for it; And what followed thou hast heard.\n\nMine is no high-flown affection, Mine no passion par amours-- As they call it--what I offer Is an honest love, and pure.\n\nCunning cords the holy Church has, Cords of softest silk they be; Put thy neck beneath the yoke, dear; Mine will follow, thou wilt see.\n\nElse--and once for all I swear it By the saint of most renown-- If I ever quit the mountains, ‘T will be in a friar’s gown.\n\nHere the goatherd brought his song to an end, and though Don Quixote entreated him to sing more, Sancho had no mind that way, being more inclined for sleep than for listening to songs; so said he to his master, “Your worship will do well to settle at once where you mean to pass the night, for the labour these good men are at all day does not allow them to spend the night in singing.”\n\n“I understand thee, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote; “I perceive clearly that those visits to the wine-skin demand compensation in sleep rather than in music.”\n\n“It’s sweet to us all, blessed be God,” said Sancho.\n\n“I do not deny it,” replied Don Quixote; “but settle thyself where thou wilt; those of my calling are more becomingly employed in watching than in sleeping; still it would be as well if thou wert to dress this ear for me again, for it is giving me more pain than it need.”\n\nSancho did as he bade him, but one of the goatherds, seeing the wound, told him not to be uneasy, as he would apply a remedy with which it would be soon healed; and gathering some leaves of rosemary, of which there was a great quantity there, he chewed them and mixed them with a little salt, and applying them to the ear he secured them firmly with a bandage, assuring him that no other treatment would be required, and so it proved.\n\n# OF WHAT A GOATHERD RELATED TO THOSE WITH DON QUIXOTE\n\nJust then another young man, one of those who fetched their provisions from the village, came up and said, “Do you know what is going on in the village, comrades?”\n\n“How could we know it?” replied one of them.\n\n“Well, then, you must know,” continued the young man, “this morning that famous student-shepherd called Chrysostom died, and it is rumoured that he died of love for that devil of a village girl the daughter of Guillermo the Rich, she that wanders about the wolds here in the dress of a shepherdess.”\n\n“You mean Marcela?” said one.\n\n“Her I mean,” answered the goatherd; “and the best of it is, he has directed in his will that he is to be buried in the fields like a Moor, and at the foot of the rock where the Cork-tree spring is, because, as the story goes (and they say he himself said so), that was the place where he first saw her. And he has also left other directions which the clergy of the village say should not and must not be obeyed because they savour of paganism. To all which his great friend Ambrosio the student, he who, like him, also went dressed as a shepherd, replies that everything must be done without any omission according to the directions left by Chrysostom, and about this the village is all in commotion; however, report says that, after all, what Ambrosio and all the shepherds his friends desire will be done, and to-morrow they are coming to bury him with great ceremony where I said. I am sure it will be something worth seeing; at least I will not fail to go and see it even if I knew I should not return to the village to-morrow.”\n\n“We will do the same,” answered the goatherds, “and cast lots to see who must stay to mind the goats of all.”\n\n“Thou sayest well, Pedro,” said one, “though there will be no need of taking that trouble, for I will stay behind for all; and don’t suppose it is virtue or want of curiosity in me; it is that the splinter that ran into my foot the other day will not let me walk.”\n\n“For all that, we thank thee,” answered Pedro.\n\nDon Quixote asked Pedro to tell him who the dead man was and who the shepherdess, to which Pedro replied that all he knew was that the dead man was a wealthy gentleman belonging to a village in those mountains, who had been a student at Salamanca for many years, at the end of which he returned to his village with the reputation of being very learned and deeply read. “Above all, they said, he was learned in the science of the stars and of what went on yonder in the heavens and the sun and the moon, for he told us of the cris of the sun and moon to exact time.”\n\n“Eclipse it is called, friend, not cris, the darkening of those two luminaries,” said Don Quixote; but Pedro, not troubling himself with trifles, went on with his story, saying, “Also he foretold when the year was going to be one of abundance or estility.”\n\n“Sterility, you mean,” said Don Quixote.\n\n“Sterility or estility,” answered Pedro, “it is all the same in the end. And I can tell you that by this his father and friends who believed him grew very rich because they did as he advised them, bidding them ‘sow barley this year, not wheat; this year you may sow pulse and not barley; the next there will be a full oil crop, and the three following not a drop will be got.’”\n\n“That science is called astrology,” said Don Quixote.\n\n“I do not know what it is called,” replied Pedro, “but I know that he knew all this and more besides. But, to make an end, not many months had passed after he returned from Salamanca, when one day he appeared dressed as a shepherd with his crook and sheepskin, having put off the long gown he wore as a scholar; and at the same time his great friend, Ambrosio by name, who had been his companion in his studies, took to the shepherd’s dress with him. I forgot to say that Chrysostom, who is dead, was a great man for writing verses, so much so that he made carols for Christmas Eve, and plays for Corpus Christi, which the young men of our village acted, and all said they were excellent. When the villagers saw the two scholars so unexpectedly appearing in shepherd’s dress, they were lost in wonder, and could not guess what had led them to make so extraordinary a change. About this time the father of our Chrysostom died, and he was left heir to a large amount of property in chattels as well as in land, no small number of cattle and sheep, and a large sum of money, of all of which the young man was left dissolute owner, and indeed he was deserving of it all, for he was a very good comrade, and kind-hearted, and a friend of worthy folk, and had a countenance like a benediction. Presently it came to be known that he had changed his dress with no other object than to wander about these wastes after that shepherdess Marcela our lad mentioned a while ago, with whom the deceased Chrysostom had fallen in love. And I must tell you now, for it is well you should know it, who this girl is; perhaps, and even without any perhaps, you will not have heard anything like it all the days of your life, though you should live more years than sarna.”\n\n“Say Sarra,” said Don Quixote, unable to endure the goatherd’s confusion of words.\n\n“The sarna lives long enough,” answered Pedro; “and if, señor, you must go finding fault with words at every step, we shall not make an end of it this twelvemonth.”\n\n“Pardon me, friend,” said Don Quixote; “but, as there is such a difference between sarna and Sarra, I told you of it; however, you have answered very rightly, for sarna lives longer than Sarra: so continue your story, and I will not object any more to anything.”\n\n“I say then, my dear sir,” said the goatherd, “that in our village there was a farmer even richer than the father of Chrysostom, who was named Guillermo, and upon whom God bestowed, over and above great wealth, a daughter at whose birth her mother died, the most respected woman there was in this neighbourhood; I fancy I can see her now with that countenance which had the sun on one side and the moon on the other; and moreover active, and kind to the poor, for which I trust that at the present moment her soul is in bliss with God in the other world. Her husband Guillermo died of grief at the death of so good a wife, leaving his daughter Marcela, a child and rich, to the care of an uncle of hers, a priest and prebendary in our village. The girl grew up with such beauty that it reminded us of her mother’s, which was very great, and yet it was thought that the daughter’s would exceed it; and so when she reached the age of fourteen to fifteen years nobody beheld her but blessed God that had made her so beautiful, and the greater number were in love with her past redemption. Her uncle kept her in great seclusion and retirement, but for all that the fame of her great beauty spread so that, as well for it as for her great wealth, her uncle was asked, solicited, and importuned, to give her in marriage not only by those of our town but of those many leagues round, and by the persons of highest quality in them. But he, being a good Christian man, though he desired to give her in marriage at once, seeing her to be old enough, was unwilling to do so without her consent, not that he had any eye to the gain and profit which the custody of the girl’s property brought him while he put off her marriage; and, faith, this was said in praise of the good priest in more than one set in the town. For I would have you know, Sir Errant, that in these little villages everything is talked about and everything is carped at, and rest assured, as I am, that the priest must be over and above good who forces his parishioners to speak well of him, especially in villages.”\n\n“That is the truth,” said Don Quixote; “but go on, for the story is very good, and you, good Pedro, tell it with very good grace.”\n\n“May that of the Lord not be wanting to me,” said Pedro; “that is the one to have. To proceed; you must know that though the uncle put before his niece and described to her the qualities of each one in particular of the many who had asked her in marriage, begging her to marry and make a choice according to her own taste, she never gave any other answer than that she had no desire to marry just yet, and that being so young she did not think herself fit to bear the burden of matrimony. At these, to all appearance, reasonable excuses that she made, her uncle ceased to urge her, and waited till she was somewhat more advanced in age and could mate herself to her own liking. For, said he--and he said quite right--parents are not to settle children in life against their will. But when one least looked for it, lo and behold! one day the demure Marcela makes her appearance turned shepherdess; and, in spite of her uncle and all those of the town that strove to dissuade her, took to going a-field with the other shepherd-lasses of the village, and tending her own flock. And so, since she appeared in public, and her beauty came to be seen openly, I could not well tell you how many rich youths, gentlemen and peasants, have adopted the costume of Chrysostom, and go about these fields making love to her. One of these, as has been already said, was our deceased friend, of whom they say that he did not love but adore her. But you must not suppose, because Marcela chose a life of such liberty and independence, and of so little or rather no retirement, that she has given any occasion, or even the semblance of one, for disparagement of her purity and modesty; on the contrary, such and so great is the vigilance with which she watches over her honour, that of all those that court and woo her not one has boasted, or can with truth boast, that she has given him any hope however small of obtaining his desire. For although she does not avoid or shun the society and conversation of the shepherds, and treats them courteously and kindly, should any one of them come to declare his intention to her, though it be one as proper and holy as that of matrimony, she flings him from her like a catapult. And with this kind of disposition she does more harm in this country than if the plague had got into it, for her affability and her beauty draw on the hearts of those that associate with her to love her and to court her, but her scorn and her frankness bring them to the brink of despair; and so they know not what to say save to proclaim her aloud cruel and hard-hearted, and other names of the same sort which well describe the nature of her character; and if you should remain here any time, señor, you would hear these hills and valleys resounding with the laments of the rejected ones who pursue her. Not far from this there is a spot where there are a couple of dozen of tall beeches, and there is not one of them but has carved and written on its smooth bark the name of Marcela, and above some a crown carved on the same tree as though her lover would say more plainly that Marcela wore and deserved that of all human beauty. Here one shepherd is sighing, there another is lamenting; there love songs are heard, here despairing elegies. One will pass all the hours of the night seated at the foot of some oak or rock, and there, without having closed his weeping eyes, the sun finds him in the morning bemused and bereft of sense; and another without relief or respite to his sighs, stretched on the burning sand in the full heat of the sultry summer noontide, makes his appeal to the compassionate heavens, and over one and the other, over these and all, the beautiful Marcela triumphs free and careless. And all of us that know her are waiting to see what her pride will come to, and who is to be the happy man that will succeed in taming a nature so formidable and gaining possession of a beauty so supreme. All that I have told you being such well-established truth, I am persuaded that what they say of the cause of Chrysostom’s death, as our lad told us, is the same. And so I advise you, señor, fail not to be present to-morrow at his burial, which will be well worth seeing, for Chrysostom had many friends, and it is not half a league from this place to where he directed he should be buried.”\n\n“I will make a point of it,” said Don Quixote, “and I thank you for the pleasure you have given me by relating so interesting a tale.”\n\n“Oh,” said the goatherd, “I do not know even the half of what has happened to the lovers of Marcela, but perhaps to-morrow we may fall in with some shepherd on the road who can tell us; and now it will be well for you to go and sleep under cover, for the night air may hurt your wound, though with the remedy I have applied to you there is no fear of an untoward result.”\n\nSancho Panza, who was wishing the goatherd’s loquacity at the devil, on his part begged his master to go into Pedro’s hut to sleep. He did so, and passed all the rest of the night in thinking of his lady Dulcinea, in imitation of the lovers of Marcela. Sancho Panza settled himself between Rocinante and his ass, and slept, not like a lover who had been discarded, but like a man who had been soundly kicked.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII.\n\nIN WHICH IS ENDED THE STORY OF THE SHEPHERDESS MARCELA, WITH OTHER INCIDENTS\nBit hardly had day begun to show itself through the balconies of the east, when five of the six goatherds came to rouse Don Quixote and tell him that if he was still of a mind to go and see the famous burial of Chrysostom they would bear him company. Don Quixote, who desired nothing better, rose and ordered Sancho to saddle and pannel at once, which he did with all despatch, and with the same they all set out forthwith. They had not gone a quarter of a league when at the meeting of two paths they saw coming towards them some six shepherds dressed in black sheepskins and with their heads crowned with garlands of cypress and bitter oleander. Each of them carried a stout holly staff in his hand, and along with them there came two men of quality on horseback in handsome travelling dress, with three servants on foot accompanying them. Courteous salutations were exchanged on meeting, and inquiring one of the other which way each party was going, they learned that all were bound for the scene of the burial, so they went on all together.\n\nOne of those on horseback addressing his companion said to him, “It seems to me, Señor Vivaldo, that we may reckon as well spent the delay we shall incur in seeing this remarkable funeral, for remarkable it cannot but be judging by the strange things these shepherds have told us, of both the dead shepherd and homicide shepherdess.”\n\n“So I think too,” replied Vivaldo, “and I would delay not to say a day, but four, for the sake of seeing it.”\n\nDon Quixote asked them what it was they had heard of Marcela and Chrysostom. The traveller answered that the same morning they had met these shepherds, and seeing them dressed in this mournful fashion they had asked them the reason of their appearing in such a guise; which one of them gave, describing the strange behaviour and beauty of a shepherdess called Marcela, and the loves of many who courted her, together with the death of that Chrysostom to whose burial they were going. In short, he repeated all that Pedro had related to Don Quixote.\n\nThis conversation dropped, and another was commenced by him who was called Vivaldo asking Don Quixote what was the reason that led him to go armed in that fashion in a country so peaceful. To which Don Quixote replied, “The pursuit of my calling does not allow or permit me to go in any other fashion; easy life, enjoyment, and repose were invented for soft courtiers, but toil, unrest, and arms were invented and made for those alone whom the world calls knights-errant, of whom I, though unworthy, am the least of all.”\n\nThe instant they heard this all set him down as mad, and the better to settle the point and discover what kind of madness his was, Vivaldo proceeded to ask him what knights-errant meant.\n\n“Have not your worships,” replied Don Quixote, “read the annals and histories of England, in which are recorded the famous deeds of King Arthur, whom we in our popular Castilian invariably call King Artus, with regard to whom it is an ancient tradition, and commonly received all over that kingdom of Great Britain, that this king did not die, but was changed by magic art into a raven, and that in process of time he is to return to reign and recover his kingdom and sceptre; for which reason it cannot be proved that from that time to this any Englishman ever killed a raven? Well, then, in the time of this good king that famous order of chivalry of the Knights of the Round Table was instituted, and the amour of Don Lancelot of the Lake with the Queen Guinevere occurred, precisely as is there related, the go-between and confidante therein being the highly honourable dame Quintanona, whence came that ballad so well known and widely spread in our Spain--\n\nO never surely was there knight So served by hand of dame, As served was he Sir Lancelot hight When he from Britain came—\n\nwith all the sweet and delectable course of his achievements in love and war. Handed down from that time, then, this order of chivalry went on extending and spreading itself over many and various parts of the world; and in it, famous and renowned for their deeds, were the mighty Amadis of Gaul with all his sons and descendants to the fifth generation, and the valiant Felixmarte of Hircania, and the never sufficiently praised Tirante el Blanco, and in our own days almost we have seen and heard and talked with the invincible knight Don Belianis of Greece. This, then, sirs, is to be a knight-errant, and what I have spoken of is the order of his chivalry, of which, as I have already said, I, though a sinner, have made profession, and what the aforesaid knights professed that same do I profess, and so I go through these solitudes and wilds seeking adventures, resolved in soul to oppose my arm and person to the most perilous that fortune may offer me in aid of the weak and needy.”\n\nBy these words of his the travellers were able to satisfy themselves of Don Quixote’s being out of his senses and of the form of madness that overmastered him, at which they felt the same astonishment that all felt on first becoming acquainted with it; and Vivaldo, who was a person of great shrewdness and of a lively temperament, in order to beguile the short journey which they said was required to reach the mountain, the scene of the burial, sought to give him an opportunity of going on with his absurdities. So he said to him, “It seems to me, Señor Knight-errant, that your worship has made choice of one of the most austere professions in the world, and I imagine even that of the Carthusian monks is not so austere.”\n\n“As austere it may perhaps be,” replied our Don Quixote, “but so necessary for the world I am very much inclined to doubt. For, if the truth is to be told, the soldier who executes what his captain orders does no less than the captain himself who gives the order. My meaning, is, that churchmen in peace and quiet pray to Heaven for the welfare of the world, but we soldiers and knights carry into effect what they pray for, defending it with the might of our arms and the edge of our swords, not under shelter but in the open air, a target for the intolerable rays of the sun in summer and the piercing frosts of winter. Thus are we God’s ministers on earth and the arms by which his justice is done therein. And as the business of war and all that relates and belongs to it cannot be conducted without exceeding great sweat, toil, and exertion, it follows that those who make it their profession have undoubtedly more labour than those who in tranquil peace and quiet are engaged in praying to God to help the weak. I do not mean to say, nor does it enter into my thoughts, that the knight-errant’s calling is as good as that of the monk in his cell; I would merely infer from what I endure myself that it is beyond a doubt a more laborious and a more belaboured one, a hungrier and thirstier, a wretcheder, raggeder, and lousier; for there is no reason to doubt that the knights-errant of yore endured much hardship in the course of their lives. And if some of them by the might of their arms did rise to be emperors, in faith it cost them dear in the matter of blood and sweat; and if those who attained to that rank had not had magicians and sages to help them they would have been completely baulked in their ambition and disappointed in their hopes.”\n\n“That is my own opinion,” replied the traveller; “but one thing among many others seems to me very wrong in knights-errant, and that is that when they find themselves about to engage in some mighty and perilous adventure in which there is manifest danger of losing their lives, they never at the moment of engaging in it think of commending themselves to God, as is the duty of every good Christian in like peril; instead of which they commend themselves to their ladies with as much devotion as if these were their gods, a thing which seems to me to savour somewhat of heathenism.”\n\n“Sir,” answered Don Quixote, “that cannot be on any account omitted, and the knight-errant would be disgraced who acted otherwise: for it is usual and customary in knight-errantry that the knight-errant, who on engaging in any great feat of arms has his lady before him, should turn his eyes towards her softly and lovingly, as though with them entreating her to favour and protect him in the hazardous venture he is about to undertake, and even though no one hear him, he is bound to say certain words between his teeth, commending himself to her with all his heart, and of this we have innumerable instances in the histories. Nor is it to be supposed from this that they are to omit commending themselves to God, for there will be time and opportunity for doing so while they are engaged in their task.”\n\n“For all that,” answered the traveller, “I feel some doubt still, because often I have read how words will arise between two knights-errant, and from one thing to another it comes about that their anger kindles and they wheel their horses round and take a good stretch of field, and then without any more ado at the top of their speed they come to the charge, and in mid-career they are wont to commend themselves to their ladies; and what commonly comes of the encounter is that one falls over the haunches of his horse pierced through and through by his antagonist’s lance, and as for the other, it is only by holding on to the mane of his horse that he can help falling to the ground; but I know not how the dead man had time to commend himself to God in the course of such rapid work as this; it would have been better if those words which he spent in commending himself to his lady in the midst of his career had been devoted to his duty and obligation as a Christian. Moreover, it is my belief that all knights-errant have not ladies to commend themselves to, for they are not all in love.”\n\n“That is impossible,” said Don Quixote: “I say it is impossible that there could be a knight-errant without a lady, because to such it is as natural and proper to be in love as to the heavens to have stars: most certainly no history has been seen in which there is to be found a knight-errant without an amour, and for the simple reason that without one he would be held no legitimate knight but a bastard, and one who had gained entrance into the stronghold of the said knighthood, not by the door, but over the wall like a thief and a robber.”\n\n“Nevertheless,” said the traveller, “if I remember rightly, I think I have read that Don Galaor, the brother of the valiant Amadis of Gaul, never had any special lady to whom he might commend himself, and yet he was not the less esteemed, and was a very stout and famous knight.”\n\nTo which our Don Quixote made answer, “Sir, one solitary swallow does not make summer; moreover, I know that knight was in secret very deeply in love; besides which, that way of falling in love with all that took his fancy was a natural propensity which he could not control. But, in short, it is very manifest that he had one alone whom he made mistress of his will, to whom he commended himself very frequently and very secretly, for he prided himself on being a reticent knight.”\n\n“Then if it be essential that every knight-errant should be in love,” said the traveller, “it may be fairly supposed that your worship is so, as you are of the order; and if you do not pride yourself on being as reticent as Don Galaor, I entreat you as earnestly as I can, in the name of all this company and in my own, to inform us of the name, country, rank, and beauty of your lady, for she will esteem herself fortunate if all the world knows that she is loved and served by such a knight as your worship seems to be.”\n\nAt this Don Quixote heaved a deep sigh and said, “I cannot say positively whether my sweet enemy is pleased or not that the world should know I serve her; I can only say in answer to what has been so courteously asked of me, that her name is Dulcinea, her country El Toboso, a village of La Mancha, her rank must be at least that of a princess, since she is my queen and lady, and her beauty superhuman, since all the impossible and fanciful attributes of beauty which the poets apply to their ladies are verified in her; for her hairs are gold, her forehead Elysian fields, her eyebrows rainbows, her eyes suns, her cheeks roses, her lips coral, her teeth pearls, her neck alabaster, her bosom marble, her hands ivory, her fairness snow, and what modesty conceals from sight such, I think and imagine, as rational reflection can only extol, not compare.”\n\n“We should like to know her lineage, race, and ancestry,” said Vivaldo.\n\nTo which Don Quixote replied, “She is not of the ancient Roman Curtii, Caii, or Scipios, nor of the modern Colonnas or Orsini, nor of the Moncadas or Requesenes of Catalonia, nor yet of the Rebellas or Villanovas of Valencia; Palafoxes, Nuzas, Rocabertis, Corellas, Lunas, Alagones, Urreas, Foces, or Gurreas of Aragon; Cerdas, Manriques, Mendozas, or Guzmans of Castile; Alencastros, Pallas, or Meneses of Portugal; but she is of those of El Toboso of La Mancha, a lineage that though modern, may furnish a source of gentle blood for the most illustrious families of the ages that are to come, and this let none dispute with me save on the condition that Zerbino placed at the foot of the trophy of Orlando’s arms, saying,\n\n‘These let none move Who dareth not his might with Roland prove.’”\n\n“Although mine is of the Cachopins of Laredo,” said the traveller, “I will not venture to compare it with that of El Toboso of La Mancha, though, to tell the truth, no such surname has until now ever reached my ears.”\n\n“What!” said Don Quixote, “has that never reached them?”\n\nThe rest of the party went along listening with great attention to the conversation of the pair, and even the very goatherds and shepherds perceived how exceedingly out of his wits our Don Quixote was. Sancho Panza alone thought that what his master said was the truth, knowing who he was and having known him from his birth; and all that he felt any difficulty in believing was that about the fair Dulcinea del Toboso, because neither any such name nor any such princess had ever come to his knowledge though he lived so close to El Toboso. They were going along conversing in this way, when they saw descending a gap between two high mountains some twenty shepherds, all clad in sheepskins of black wool, and crowned with garlands which, as afterwards appeared, were, some of them of yew, some of cypress. Six of the number were carrying a bier covered with a great variety of flowers and branches, on seeing which one of the goatherds said, “Those who come there are the bearers of Chrysostom’s body, and the foot of that mountain is the place where he ordered them to bury him.” They therefore made haste to reach the spot, and did so by the time those who came had laid the bier upon the ground, and four of them with sharp pickaxes were digging a grave by the side of a hard rock. They greeted each other courteously, and then Don Quixote and those who accompanied him turned to examine the bier, and on it, covered with flowers, they saw a dead body in the dress of a shepherd, to all appearance of one thirty years of age, and showing even in death that in life he had been of comely features and gallant bearing. Around him on the bier itself were laid some books, and several papers open and folded; and those who were looking on as well as those who were opening the grave and all the others who were there preserved a strange silence, until one of those who had borne the body said to another, “Observe carefully, Ambrosio if this is the place Chrysostom spoke of, since you are anxious that what he directed in his will should be so strictly complied with.”\n\n“This is the place,” answered Ambrosio “for in it many a time did my poor friend tell me the story of his hard fortune. Here it was, he told me, that he saw for the first time that mortal enemy of the human race, and here, too, for the first time he declared to her his passion, as honourable as it was devoted, and here it was that at last Marcela ended by scorning and rejecting him so as to bring the tragedy of his wretched life to a close; here, in memory of misfortunes so great, he desired to be laid in the bowels of eternal oblivion.” Then turning to Don Quixote and the travellers he went on to say, “That body, sirs, on which you are looking with compassionate eyes, was the abode of a soul on which Heaven bestowed a vast share of its riches. That is the body of Chrysostom, who was unrivalled in wit, unequalled in courtesy, unapproached in gentle bearing, a phoenix in friendship, generous without limit, grave without arrogance, gay without vulgarity, and, in short, first in all that constitutes goodness and second to none in all that makes up misfortune. He loved deeply, he was hated; he adored, he was scorned; he wooed a wild beast, he pleaded with marble, he pursued the wind, he cried to the wilderness, he served ingratitude, and for reward was made the prey of death in the mid-course of life, cut short by a shepherdess whom he sought to immortalise in the memory of man, as these papers which you see could fully prove, had he not commanded me to consign them to the fire after having consigned his body to the earth.”\n\n“You would deal with them more harshly and cruelly than their owner himself,” said Vivaldo, “for it is neither right nor proper to do the will of one who enjoins what is wholly unreasonable; it would not have been reasonable in Augustus Caesar had he permitted the directions left by the divine Mantuan in his will to be carried into effect. So that, Señor Ambrosio while you consign your friend’s body to the earth, you should not consign his writings to oblivion, for if he gave the order in bitterness of heart, it is not right that you should irrationally obey it. On the contrary, by granting life to those papers, let the cruelty of Marcela live for ever, to serve as a warning in ages to come to all men to shun and avoid falling into like danger; or I and all of us who have come here know already the story of this your love-stricken and heart-broken friend, and we know, too, your friendship, and the cause of his death, and the directions he gave at the close of his life; from which sad story may be gathered how great was the cruelty of Marcela, the love of Chrysostom, and the loyalty of your friendship, together with the end awaiting those who pursue rashly the path that insane passion opens to their eyes. Last night we learned the death of Chrysostom and that he was to be buried here, and out of curiosity and pity we left our direct road and resolved to come and see with our eyes that which when heard of had so moved our compassion, and in consideration of that compassion and our desire to prove it if we might by condolence, we beg of you, excellent Ambrosio, or at least I on my own account entreat you, that instead of burning those papers you allow me to carry away some of them.”\n\nAnd without waiting for the shepherd’s answer, he stretched out his hand and took up some of those that were nearest to him; seeing which Ambrosio said, “Out of courtesy, señor, I will grant your request as to those you have taken, but it is idle to expect me to abstain from burning the remainder.”\n\nVivaldo, who was eager to see what the papers contained, opened one of them at once, and saw that its title was “Lay of Despair.”\n\nAmbrosio hearing it said, “That is the last paper the unhappy man wrote; and that you may see, señor, to what an end his misfortunes brought him, read it so that you may be heard, for you will have time enough for that while we are waiting for the grave to be dug.”\n\n“I will do so very willingly,” said Vivaldo; and as all the bystanders were equally eager they gathered round him, and he, reading in a loud voice, found that it ran as follows.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV.\n\nWHEREIN ARE INSERTED THE DESPAIRING VERSES OF THE DEAD SHEPHERD, TOGETHER WITH OTHER INCIDENTS NOT LOOKED FOR\n\n# THE LAY OF CHRYSOSTOM\n\nSince thou dost in thy cruelty desire The ruthless rigour of thy tyranny From tongue to tongue, from land to land proclaimed, The very Hell will I constrain to lend This stricken breast of mine deep notes of woe To serve my need of fitting utterance. And as I strive to body forth the tale Of all I suffer, all that thou hast done, Forth shall the dread voice roll, and bear along Shreds from my vitals torn for greater pain. Then listen, not to dulcet harmony, But to a discord wrung by mad despair Out of this bosom’s depths of bitterness, To ease my heart and plant a sting in thine.\n\nThe lion’s roar, the fierce wolf’s savage howl, The horrid hissing of the scaly snake, The awesome cries of monsters yet unnamed, The crow’s ill-boding croak, the hollow moan Of wild winds wrestling with the restless sea, The wrathful bellow of the vanquished bull, The plaintive sobbing of the widowed dove, The envied owl’s sad note, the wail of woe That rises from the dreary choir of Hell, Commingled in one sound, confusing sense, Let all these come to aid my soul’s complaint, For pain like mine demands new modes of song.\n\nNo echoes of that discord shall be heard Where Father Tagus rolls, or on the banks Of olive-bordered Betis; to the rocks Or in deep caverns shall my plaint be told, And by a lifeless tongue in living words; Or in dark valleys or on lonely shores, Where neither foot of man nor sunbeam falls; Or in among the poison-breathing swarms Of monsters nourished by the sluggish Nile. For, though it be to solitudes remote The hoarse vague echoes of my sorrows sound Thy matchless cruelty, my dismal fate Shall carry them to all the spacious world.\n\nDisdain hath power to kill, and patience dies Slain by suspicion, be it false or true; And deadly is the force of jealousy; Long absence makes of life a dreary void; No hope of happiness can give repose To him that ever fears to be forgot; And death, inevitable, waits in hall. But I, by some strange miracle, live on A prey to absence, jealousy, disdain; Racked by suspicion as by certainty; Forgotten, left to feed my flame alone. And while I suffer thus, there comes no ray Of hope to gladden me athwart the gloom; Nor do I look for it in my despair; But rather clinging to a cureless woe, All hope do I abjure for evermore.\n\nCan there be hope where fear is? Were it well, When far more certain are the grounds of fear? Ought I to shut mine eyes to jealousy, If through a thousand heart-wounds it appears? Who would not give free access to distrust, Seeing disdain unveiled, and--bitter change!-- All his suspicions turned to certainties, And the fair truth transformed into a lie? Oh, thou fierce tyrant of the realms of love, Oh, Jealousy! put chains upon these hands, And bind me with thy strongest cord, Disdain. But, woe is me! triumphant over all, My sufferings drown the memory of you.\n\nAnd now I die, and since there is no hope Of happiness for me in life or death, Still to my fantasy I’ll fondly cling. I’ll say that he is wise who loveth well, And that the soul most free is that most bound In thraldom to the ancient tyrant Love. I’ll say that she who is mine enemy In that fair body hath as fair a mind, And that her coldness is but my desert, And that by virtue of the pain he sends Love rules his kingdom with a gentle sway. Thus, self-deluding, and in bondage sore, And wearing out the wretched shred of life To which I am reduced by her disdain, I’ll give this soul and body to the winds, All hopeless of a crown of bliss in store.\n\nThou whose injustice hath supplied the cause That makes me quit the weary life I loathe, As by this wounded bosom thou canst see How willingly thy victim I become, Let not my death, if haply worth a tear, Cloud the clear heaven that dwells in thy bright eyes; I would not have thee expiate in aught The crime of having made my heart thy prey; But rather let thy laughter gaily ring And prove my death to be thy festival. Fool that I am to bid thee! well I know Thy glory gains by my untimely end.\n\nAnd now it is the time; from Hell’s abyss Come thirsting Tantalus, come Sisyphus Heaving the cruel stone, come Tityus With vulture, and with wheel Ixion come, And come the sisters of the ceaseless toil; And all into this breast transfer their pains, And (if such tribute to despair be due) Chant in their deepest tones a doleful dirge Over a corse unworthy of a shroud. Let the three-headed guardian of the gate, And all the monstrous progeny of hell, The doleful concert join: a lover dead Methinks can have no fitter obsequies.\n\nLay of despair, grieve not when thou art gone Forth from this sorrowing heart: my misery Brings fortune to the cause that gave thee birth; Then banish sadness even in the tomb.\n\nThe “Lay of Chrysostom” met with the approbation of the listeners, though the reader said it did not seem to him to agree with what he had heard of Marcela’s reserve and propriety, for Chrysostom complained in it of jealousy, suspicion, and absence, all to the prejudice of the good name and fame of Marcela; to which Ambrosio replied as one who knew well his friend’s most secret thoughts, “Señor, to remove that doubt I should tell you that when the unhappy man wrote this lay he was away from Marcela, from whom he had voluntarily separated himself, to try if absence would act with him as it is wont; and as everything distresses and every fear haunts the banished lover, so imaginary jealousies and suspicions, dreaded as if they were true, tormented Chrysostom; and thus the truth of what report declares of the virtue of Marcela remains unshaken, and with her envy itself should not and cannot find any fault save that of being cruel, somewhat haughty, and very scornful.”\n\n“That is true,” said Vivaldo; and as he was about to read another paper of those he had preserved from the fire, he was stopped by a marvellous vision (for such it seemed) that unexpectedly presented itself to their eyes; for on the summit of the rock where they were digging the grave there appeared the shepherdess Marcela, so beautiful that her beauty exceeded its reputation. Those who had never till then beheld her gazed upon her in wonder and silence, and those who were accustomed to see her were not less amazed than those who had never seen her before. But the instant Ambrosio saw her he addressed her, with manifest indignation:\n\n“Art thou come, by chance, cruel basilisk of these mountains, to see if in thy presence blood will flow from the wounds of this wretched being thy cruelty has robbed of life; or is it to exult over the cruel work of thy humours that thou art come; or like another pitiless Nero to look down from that height upon the ruin of his Rome in embers; or in thy arrogance to trample on this ill-fated corpse, as the ungrateful daughter trampled on her father Tarquin’s? Tell us quickly for what thou art come, or what it is thou wouldst have, for, as I know the thoughts of Chrysostom never failed to obey thee in life, I will make all these who call themselves his friends obey thee, though he be dead.”\n\n“I come not, Ambrosio for any of the purposes thou hast named,” replied Marcela, “but to defend myself and to prove how unreasonable are all those who blame me for their sorrow and for Chrysostom’s death; and therefore I ask all of you that are here to give me your attention, for it will not take much time or many words to bring the truth home to persons of sense. Heaven has made me, so you say, beautiful, and so much so that in spite of yourselves my beauty leads you to love me; and for the love you show me you say, and even urge, that I am bound to love you. By that natural understanding which God has given me I know that everything beautiful attracts love, but I cannot see how, by reason of being loved, that which is loved for its beauty is bound to love that which loves it; besides, it may happen that the lover of that which is beautiful may be ugly, and ugliness being detestable, it is very absurd to say, “I love thee because thou art beautiful, thou must love me though I be ugly.” But supposing the beauty equal on both sides, it does not follow that the inclinations must be therefore alike, for it is not every beauty that excites love, some but pleasing the eye without winning the affection; and if every sort of beauty excited love and won the heart, the will would wander vaguely to and fro unable to make choice of any; for as there is an infinity of beautiful objects there must be an infinity of inclinations, and true love, I have heard it said, is indivisible, and must be voluntary and not compelled. If this be so, as I believe it to be, why do you desire me to bend my will by force, for no other reason but that you say you love me? Nay--tell me--had Heaven made me ugly, as it has made me beautiful, could I with justice complain of you for not loving me? Moreover, you must remember that the beauty I possess was no choice of mine, for, be it what it may, Heaven of its bounty gave it me without my asking or choosing it; and as the viper, though it kills with it, does not deserve to be blamed for the poison it carries, as it is a gift of nature, neither do I deserve reproach for being beautiful; for beauty in a modest woman is like fire at a distance or a sharp sword; the one does not burn, the other does not cut, those who do not come too near. Honour and virtue are the ornaments of the mind, without which the body, though it be so, has no right to pass for beautiful; but if modesty is one of the virtues that specially lend a grace and charm to mind and body, why should she who is loved for her beauty part with it to gratify one who for his pleasure alone strives with all his might and energy to rob her of it? I was born free, and that I might live in freedom I chose the solitude of the fields; in the trees of the mountains I find society, the clear waters of the brooks are my mirrors, and to the trees and waters I make known my thoughts and charms. I am a fire afar off, a sword laid aside. Those whom I have inspired with love by letting them see me, I have by words undeceived, and if their longings live on hope--and I have given none to Chrysostom or to any other--it cannot justly be said that the death of any is my doing, for it was rather his own obstinacy than my cruelty that killed him; and if it be made a charge against me that his wishes were honourable, and that therefore I was bound to yield to them, I answer that when on this very spot where now his grave is made he declared to me his purity of purpose, I told him that mine was to live in perpetual solitude, and that the earth alone should enjoy the fruits of my retirement and the spoils of my beauty; and if, after this open avowal, he chose to persist against hope and steer against the wind, what wonder is it that he should sink in the depths of his infatuation? If I had encouraged him, I should be false; if I had gratified him, I should have acted against my own better resolution and purpose. He was persistent in spite of warning, he despaired without being hated. Bethink you now if it be reasonable that his suffering should be laid to my charge. Let him who has been deceived complain, let him give way to despair whose encouraged hopes have proved vain, let him flatter himself whom I shall entice, let him boast whom I shall receive; but let not him call me cruel or homicide to whom I make no promise, upon whom I practise no deception, whom I neither entice nor receive. It has not been so far the will of Heaven that I should love by fate, and to expect me to love by choice is idle. Let this general declaration serve for each of my suitors on his own account, and let it be understood from this time forth that if anyone dies for me it is not of jealousy or misery he dies, for she who loves no one can give no cause for jealousy to any, and candour is not to be confounded with scorn. Let him who calls me wild beast and basilisk, leave me alone as something noxious and evil; let him who calls me ungrateful, withhold his service; who calls me wayward, seek not my acquaintance; who calls me cruel, pursue me not; for this wild beast, this basilisk, this ungrateful, cruel, wayward being has no kind of desire to seek, serve, know, or follow them. If Chrysostom’s impatience and violent passion killed him, why should my modest behaviour and circumspection be blamed? If I preserve my purity in the society of the trees, why should he who would have me preserve it among men, seek to rob me of it? I have, as you know, wealth of my own, and I covet not that of others; my taste is for freedom, and I have no relish for constraint; I neither love nor hate anyone; I do not deceive this one or court that, or trifle with one or play with another. The modest converse of the shepherd girls of these hamlets and the care of my goats are my recreations; my desires are bounded by these mountains, and if they ever wander hence it is to contemplate the beauty of the heavens, steps by which the soul travels to its primeval abode.”\n\nWith these words, and not waiting to hear a reply, she turned and passed into the thickest part of a wood that was hard by, leaving all who were there lost in admiration as much of her good sense as of her beauty. Some--those wounded by the irresistible shafts launched by her bright eyes--made as though they would follow her, heedless of the frank declaration they had heard; seeing which, and deeming this a fitting occasion for the exercise of his chivalry in aid of distressed damsels, Don Quixote, laying his hand on the hilt of his sword, exclaimed in a loud and distinct voice:\n\n“Let no one, whatever his rank or condition, dare to follow the beautiful Marcela, under pain of incurring my fierce indignation. She has shown by clear and satisfactory arguments that little or no fault is to be found with her for the death of Chrysostom, and also how far she is from yielding to the wishes of any of her lovers, for which reason, instead of being followed and persecuted, she should in justice be honoured and esteemed by all the good people of the world, for she shows that she is the only woman in it that holds to such a virtuous resolution.”\n\nWhether it was because of the threats of Don Quixote, or because Ambrosio told them to fulfil their duty to their good friend, none of the shepherds moved or stirred from the spot until, having finished the grave and burned Chrysostom’s papers, they laid his body in it, not without many tears from those who stood by. They closed the grave with a heavy stone until a slab was ready which Ambrosio said he meant to have prepared, with an epitaph which was to be to this effect:\n\nBeneath the stone before your eyes The body of a lover lies; In life he was a shepherd swain, In death a victim to disdain. Ungrateful, cruel, coy, and fair, Was she that drove him to despair, And Love hath made her his ally For spreading wide his tyranny.\n\nThey then strewed upon the grave a profusion of flowers and branches, and all expressing their condolence with his friend ambrosio, took their Vivaldo and his companion did the same; and Don Quixote bade farewell to his hosts and to the travellers, who pressed him to come with them to Seville, as being such a convenient place for finding adventures, for they presented themselves in every street and round every corner oftener than anywhere else. Don Quixote thanked them for their advice and for the disposition they showed to do him a favour, and said that for the present he would not, and must not go to Seville until he had cleared all these mountains of highwaymen and robbers, of whom report said they were full. Seeing his good intention, the travellers were unwilling to press him further, and once more bidding him farewell, they left him and pursued their journey, in the course of which they did not fail to discuss the story of Marcela and Chrysostom as well as the madness of Don Quixote. He, on his part, resolved to go in quest of the shepherdess Marcela, and make offer to her of all the service he could render her; but things did not fall out with him as he expected, according to what is related in the course of this veracious history, of which the Second Part ends here.\n\n# CHAPTER XV.\n\nIN WHICH IS RELATED THE UNFORTUNATE ADVENTURE THAT DON QUIXOTE FELL IN WITH WHEN HE FELL OUT WITH CERTAIN HEARTLESS YANGUESANS\n\nThe sage Cide Hamete Benengeli relates that as soon as Don Quixote took leave of his hosts and all who had been present at the burial of Chrysostom, he and his squire passed into the same wood which they had seen the shepherdess Marcela enter, and after having wandered for more than two hours in all directions in search of her without finding her, they came to a halt in a glade covered with tender grass, beside which ran a pleasant cool stream that invited and compelled them to pass there the hours of the noontide heat, which by this time was beginning to come on oppressively. Don Quixote and Sancho dismounted, and turning Rocinante and the ass loose to feed on the grass that was there in abundance, they ransacked the alforjas, and without any ceremony very peacefully and sociably master and man made their repast on what they found in them.\n\nSancho had not thought it worth while to hobble Rocinante, feeling sure, from what he knew of his staidness and freedom from incontinence, that all the mares in the Cordova pastures would not lead him into an impropriety. Chance, however, and the devil, who is not always asleep, so ordained it that feeding in this valley there was a drove of Galician ponies belonging to certain Yanguesan carriers, whose way it is to take their midday rest with their teams in places and spots where grass and water abound; and that where Don Quixote chanced to be suited the Yanguesans’ purpose very well. It so happened, then, that Rocinante took a fancy to disport himself with their ladyships the ponies, and abandoning his usual gait and demeanour as he scented them, he, without asking leave of his master, got up a briskish little trot and hastened to make known his wishes to them; they, however, it seemed, preferred their pasture to him, and received him with their heels and teeth to such effect that they soon broke his girths and left him naked without a saddle to cover him; but what must have been worse to him was that the carriers, seeing the violence he was offering to their mares, came running up armed with stakes, and so belaboured him that they brought him sorely battered to the ground.\n\nBy this time Don Quixote and Sancho, who had witnessed the drubbing of Rocinante, came up panting, and said Don Quixote to Sancho:\n\n“So far as I can see, friend Sancho, these are not knights but base folk of low birth: I mention it because thou canst lawfully aid me in taking due vengeance for the insult offered to Rocinante before our eyes.”\n\n“What the devil vengeance can we take,” answered Sancho, “if they are more than twenty, and we no more than two, or, indeed, perhaps not more than one and a half?”\n\n“I count for a hundred,” replied Don Quixote, and without more words he drew his sword and attacked the Yanguesans and excited and impelled by the example of his master, Sancho did the same; and to begin with, Don Quixote delivered a slash at one of them that laid open the leather jerkin he wore, together with a great portion of his shoulder. The Yanguesans, seeing themselves assaulted by only two men while they were so many, betook themselves to their stakes, and driving the two into the middle they began to lay on with great zeal and energy; in fact, at the second blow they brought Sancho to the ground, and Don Quixote fared the same way, all his skill and high mettle availing him nothing, and fate willed it that he should fall at the feet of Rocinante, who had not yet risen; whereby it may be seen how furiously stakes can pound in angry boorish hands.\n\nThen, seeing the mischief they had done, the Yanguesans with all the haste they could loaded their team and pursued their journey, leaving the two adventurers a sorry sight and in sorrier mood.\n\nSancho was the first to come to, and finding himself close to his master he called to him in a weak and doleful voice, “Señor Don Quixote, ah, Señor Don Quixote!”\n\n“What wouldst thou, brother Sancho?” answered Don Quixote in the same feeble suffering tone as Sancho.\n\n“I would like, if it were possible,” answered Sancho Panza, “your worship to give me a couple of sups of that potion of the fiery Blas, if it be that you have any to hand there; perhaps it will serve for broken bones as well as for wounds.”\n\n“If I only had it here, wretch that I am, what more should we want?” said Don Quixote; “but I swear to thee, Sancho Panza, on the faith of a knight-errant, ere two days are over, unless fortune orders otherwise, I mean to have it in my possession, or my hand will have lost its cunning.”\n\n“But in how many does your worship think we shall have the use of our feet?” answered Sancho Panza.\n\n“For myself I must say I cannot guess how many,” said the battered knight Don Quixote; “but I take all the blame upon myself, for I had no business to put hand to sword against men who where not dubbed knights like myself, and so I believe that in punishment for having transgressed the laws of chivalry the God of battles has permitted this chastisement to be administered to me; for which reason, brother Sancho, it is well thou shouldst receive a hint on the matter which I am now about to mention to thee, for it is of much importance to the welfare of both of us. It is that when thou shalt see rabble of this sort offering us insult thou art not to wait till I draw sword against them, for I shall not do so at all; but do thou draw sword and chastise them to thy heart’s content, and if any knights come to their aid and defence I will take care to defend thee and assail them with all my might; and thou hast already seen by a thousand signs and proofs what the might of this strong arm of mine is equal to”--so uplifted had the poor gentleman become through the victory over the stout Biscayan.\n\nBut Sancho did not so fully approve of his master’s admonition as to let it pass without saying in reply, “Señor, I am a man of peace, meek and quiet, and I can put up with any affront because I have a wife and children to support and bring up; so let it be likewise a hint to your worship, as it cannot be a mandate, that on no account will I draw sword either against clown or against knight, and that here before God I forgive the insults that have been offered me, whether they have been, are, or shall be offered me by high or low, rich or poor, noble or commoner, not excepting any rank or condition whatsoever.”\n\nTo all which his master said in reply, “I wish I had breath enough to speak somewhat easily, and that the pain I feel on this side would abate so as to let me explain to thee, Panza, the mistake thou makest. Come now, sinner, suppose the wind of fortune, hitherto so adverse, should turn in our favour, filling the sails of our desires so that safely and without impediment we put into port in some one of those islands I have promised thee, how would it be with thee if on winning it I made thee lord of it? Why, thou wilt make it well-nigh impossible through not being a knight nor having any desire to be one, nor possessing the courage nor the will to avenge insults or defend thy lordship; for thou must know that in newly conquered kingdoms and provinces the minds of the inhabitants are never so quiet nor so well disposed to the new lord that there is no fear of their making some move to change matters once more, and try, as they say, what chance may do for them; so it is essential that the new possessor should have good sense to enable him to govern, and valour to attack and defend himself, whatever may befall him.”\n\n“In what has now befallen us,” answered Sancho, “I’d have been well pleased to have that good sense and that valour your worship speaks of, but I swear on the faith of a poor man I am more fit for plasters than for arguments. See if your worship can get up, and let us help Rocinante, though he does not deserve it, for he was the main cause of all this thrashing. I never thought it of Rocinante, for I took him to be a virtuous person and as quiet as myself. After all, they say right that it takes a long time to come to know people, and that there is nothing sure in this life. Who would have said that, after such mighty slashes as your worship gave that unlucky knight-errant, there was coming, travelling post and at the very heels of them, such a great storm of sticks as has fallen upon our shoulders?”\n\n“And yet thine, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote, “ought to be used to such squalls; but mine, reared in soft cloth and fine linen, it is plain they must feel more keenly the pain of this mishap, and if it were not that I imagine--why do I say imagine?--know of a certainty that all these annoyances are very necessary accompaniments of the calling of arms, I would lay me down here to die of pure vexation.”\n\nTo this the squire replied, “Señor, as these mishaps are what one reaps of chivalry, tell me if they happen very often, or if they have their own fixed times for coming to pass; because it seems to me that after two harvests we shall be no good for the third, unless God in his infinite mercy helps us.”\n\n“Know, friend Sancho,” answered Don Quixote, “that the life of knights-errant is subject to a thousand dangers and reverses, and neither more nor less is it within immediate possibility for knights-errant to become kings and emperors, as experience has shown in the case of many different knights with whose histories I am thoroughly acquainted; and I could tell thee now, if the pain would let me, of some who simply by might of arm have risen to the high stations I have mentioned; and those same, both before and after, experienced divers misfortunes and miseries; for the valiant Amadis of Gaul found himself in the power of his mortal enemy Arcalaus the magician, who, it is positively asserted, holding him captive, gave him more than two hundred lashes with the reins of his horse while tied to one of the pillars of a court; and moreover there is a certain recondite author of no small authority who says that the Knight of Phoebus, being caught in a certain pitfall, which opened under his feet in a certain castle, on falling found himself bound hand and foot in a deep pit underground, where they administered to him one of those things they call clysters, of sand and snow-water, that well-nigh finished him; and if he had not been succoured in that sore extremity by a sage, a great friend of his, it would have gone very hard with the poor knight; so I may well suffer in company with such worthy folk, for greater were the indignities which they had to suffer than those which we suffer. For I would have thee know, Sancho, that wounds caused by any instruments which happen by chance to be in hand inflict no indignity, and this is laid down in the law of the duel in express words: if, for instance, the cobbler strikes another with the last which he has in his hand, though it be in fact a piece of wood, it cannot be said for that reason that he whom he struck with it has been cudgelled. I say this lest thou shouldst imagine that because we have been drubbed in this affray we have therefore suffered any indignity; for the arms those men carried, with which they pounded us, were nothing more than their stakes, and not one of them, so far as I remember, carried rapier, sword, or dagger.”\n\n“They gave me no time to see that much,” answered Sancho, “for hardly had I laid hand on my tizona when they signed the cross on my shoulders with their sticks in such style that they took the sight out of my eyes and the strength out of my feet, stretching me where I now lie, and where thinking of whether all those stake-strokes were an indignity or not gives me no uneasiness, which the pain of the blows does, for they will remain as deeply impressed on my memory as on my shoulders.”\n\n“For all that let me tell thee, brother Panza,” said Don Quixote, “that there is no recollection which time does not put an end to, and no pain which death does not remove.”\n\n“And what greater misfortune can there be,” replied Panza, “than the one that waits for time to put an end to it and death to remove it? If our mishap were one of those that are cured with a couple of plasters, it would not be so bad; but I am beginning to think that all the plasters in a hospital almost won’t be enough to put us right.”\n\n“No more of that: pluck strength out of weakness, Sancho, as I mean to do,” returned Don Quixote, “and let us see how Rocinante is, for it seems to me that not the least share of this mishap has fallen to the lot of the poor beast.”\n\n“There is nothing wonderful in that,” replied Sancho, “since he is a knight-errant too; what I wonder at is that my beast should have come off scot-free where we come out scotched.”\n\n“Fortune always leaves a door open in adversity in order to bring relief to it,” said Don Quixote; “I say so because this little beast may now supply the want of Rocinante, carrying me hence to some castle where I may be cured of my wounds. And moreover I shall not hold it any dishonour to be so mounted, for I remember having read how the good old Silenus, the tutor and instructor of the gay god of laughter, when he entered the city of the hundred gates, went very contentedly mounted on a handsome ass.”\n\n“It may be true that he went mounted as your worship says,” answered Sancho, “but there is a great difference between going mounted and going slung like a sack of manure.”\n\nTo which Don Quixote replied, “Wounds received in battle confer honour instead of taking it away; and so, friend Panza, say no more, but, as I told thee before, get up as well as thou canst and put me on top of thy beast in whatever fashion pleases thee best, and let us go hence ere night come on and surprise us in these wilds.”\n\n“And yet I have heard your worship say,” observed Panza, “that it is very meet for knights-errant to sleep in wastes and deserts, and that they esteem it very good fortune.”\n\n“That is,” said Don Quixote, “when they cannot help it, or when they are in love; and so true is this that there have been knights who have remained two years on rocks, in sunshine and shade and all the inclemencies of heaven, without their ladies knowing anything of it; and one of these was Amadis, when, under the name of Beltenebros, he took up his abode on the Pena Pobre for--I know not if it was eight years or eight months, for I am not very sure of the reckoning; at any rate he stayed there doing penance for I know not what pique the Princess Oriana had against him; but no more of this now, Sancho, and make haste before a mishap like Rocinante’s befalls the ass.”\n\n“The very devil would be in it in that case,” said Sancho; and letting off thirty “ohs,” and sixty sighs, and a hundred and twenty maledictions and execrations on whomsoever it was that had brought him there, he raised himself, stopping half-way bent like a Turkish bow without power to bring himself upright, but with all his pains he saddled his ass, who too had gone astray somewhat, yielding to the excessive licence of the day; he next raised up Rocinante, and as for him, had he possessed a tongue to complain with, most assuredly neither Sancho nor his master would have been behind him.\n\nTo be brief, Sancho fixed Don Quixote on the ass and secured Rocinante with a leading rein, and taking the ass by the halter, he proceeded more or less in the direction in which it seemed to him the high road might be; and, as chance was conducting their affairs for them from good to better, he had not gone a short league when the road came in sight, and on it he perceived an inn, which to his annoyance and to the delight of Don Quixote must needs be a castle. Sancho insisted that it was an inn, and his master that it was not one, but a castle, and the dispute lasted so long that before the point was settled they had time to reach it, and into it Sancho entered with all his team without any further controversy.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI.\n\nOF WHAT HAPPENED TO THE INGENIOUS GENTLEMAN IN THE INN WHICH HE TOOK TO BE A CASTLE\nThe innkeeper, seeing Don Quixote slung across the ass, asked Sancho what was amiss with him. Sancho answered that it was nothing, only that he had fallen down from a rock and had his ribs a little bruised. The innkeeper had a wife whose disposition was not such as those of her calling commonly have, for she was by nature kind-hearted and felt for the sufferings of her neighbours, so she at once set about tending Don Quixote, and made her young daughter, a very comely girl, help her in taking care of her guest. There was besides in the inn, as servant, an Asturian lass with a broad face, flat poll, and snub nose, blind of one eye and not very sound in the other. The elegance of her shape, to be sure, made up for all her defects; she did not measure seven palms from head to foot, and her shoulders, which overweighted her somewhat, made her contemplate the ground more than she liked. This graceful lass, then, helped the young girl, and the two made up a very bad bed for Don Quixote in a garret that showed evident signs of having formerly served for many years as a straw-loft, in which there was also quartered a carrier whose bed was placed a little beyond our Don Quixote’s, and, though only made of the pack-saddles and cloths of his mules, had much the advantage of it, as Don Quixote’s consisted simply of four rough boards on two not very even trestles, a mattress, that for thinness might have passed for a quilt, full of pellets which, were they not seen through the rents to be wool, would to the touch have seemed pebbles in hardness, two sheets made of buckler leather, and a coverlet the threads of which anyone that chose might have counted without missing one in the reckoning.\n\nOn this accursed bed Don Quixote stretched himself, and the hostess and her daughter soon covered him with plasters from top to toe, while Maritornes--for that was the name of the Asturian--held the light for them, and while plastering him, the hostess, observing how full of wheals Don Quixote was in some places, remarked that this had more the look of blows than of a fall.\n\nIt was not blows, Sancho said, but that the rock had many points and projections, and that each of them had left its mark. “Pray, señora,” he added, “manage to save some tow, as there will be no want of some one to use it, for my loins too are rather sore.”\n\n“Then you must have fallen too,” said the hostess.\n\n“I did not fall,” said Sancho Panza, “but from the shock I got at seeing my master fall, my body aches so that I feel as if I had had a thousand thwacks.”\n\n“That may well be,” said the young girl, “for it has many a time happened to me to dream that I was falling down from a tower and never coming to the ground, and when I awoke from the dream to find myself as weak and shaken as if I had really fallen.”\n\n“There is the point, señora,” replied Sancho Panza, “that I without dreaming at all, but being more awake than I am now, find myself with scarcely less wheals than my master, Don Quixote.”\n\n“How is the gentleman called?” asked Maritornes the Asturian.\n\n“Don Quixote of La Mancha,” answered Sancho Panza, “and he is a knight-adventurer, and one of the best and stoutest that have been seen in the world this long time past.”\n\n“What is a knight-adventurer?” said the lass.\n\n“Are you so new in the world as not to know?” answered Sancho Panza. “Well, then, you must know, sister, that a knight-adventurer is a thing that in two words is seen drubbed and emperor, that is to-day the most miserable and needy being in the world, and to-morrow will have two or three crowns of kingdoms to give his squire.”\n\n“Then how is it,” said the hostess, “that belonging to so good a master as this, you have not, to judge by appearances, even so much as a county?”\n\n“It is too soon yet,” answered Sancho, “for we have only been a month going in quest of adventures, and so far we have met with nothing that can be called one, for it will happen that when one thing is looked for another thing is found; however, if my master Don Quixote gets well of this wound, or fall, and I am left none the worse of it, I would not change my hopes for the best title in Spain.”\n\nTo all this conversation Don Quixote was listening very attentively, and sitting up in bed as well as he could, and taking the hostess by the hand he said to her, “Believe me, fair lady, you may call yourself fortunate in having in this castle of yours sheltered my person, which is such that if I do not myself praise it, it is because of what is commonly said, that self-praise debaseth; but my squire will inform you who I am. I only tell you that I shall preserve for ever inscribed on my memory the service you have rendered me in order to tender you my gratitude while life shall last me; and would to Heaven love held me not so enthralled and subject to its laws and to the eyes of that fair ingrate whom I name between my teeth, but that those of this lovely damsel might be the masters of my liberty.”\n\nThe hostess, her daughter, and the worthy Maritornes listened in bewilderment to the words of the knight-errant; for they understood about as much of them as if he had been talking Greek, though they could perceive they were all meant for expressions of good-will and blandishments; and not being accustomed to this kind of language, they stared at him and wondered to themselves, for he seemed to them a man of a different sort from those they were used to, and thanking him in pothouse phrase for his civility they left him, while the Asturian gave her attention to Sancho, who needed it no less than his master.\n\nThe carrier had made an arrangement with her for recreation that night, and she had given him her word that when the guests were quiet and the family asleep she would come in search of him and meet his wishes unreservedly. And it is said of this good lass that she never made promises of the kind without fulfilling them, even though she made them in a forest and without any witness present, for she plumed herself greatly on being a lady and held it no disgrace to be in such an employment as servant in an inn, because, she said, misfortunes and ill-luck had brought her to that position. The hard, narrow, wretched, rickety bed of Don Quixote stood first in the middle of this star-lit stable, and close beside it Sancho made his, which merely consisted of a rush mat and a blanket that looked as if it was of threadbare canvas rather than of wool. Next to these two beds was that of the carrier, made up, as has been said, of the pack-saddles and all the trappings of the two best mules he had, though there were twelve of them, sleek, plump, and in prime condition, for he was one of the rich carriers of Arevalo, according to the author of this history, who particularly mentions this carrier because he knew him very well, and they even say was in some degree a relation of his; besides which Cide Hamete Benengeli was a historian of great research and accuracy in all things, as is very evident since he would not pass over in silence those that have been already mentioned, however trifling and insignificant they might be, an example that might be followed by those grave historians who relate transactions so curtly and briefly that we hardly get a taste of them, all the substance of the work being left in the inkstand from carelessness, perverseness, or ignorance. A thousand blessings on the author of “Tablante de Ricamonte” and that of the other book in which the deeds of the Conde Tomillas are recounted; with what minuteness they describe everything!\n\nTo proceed, then: after having paid a visit to his team and given them their second feed, the carrier stretched himself on his pack-saddles and lay waiting for his conscientious Maritornes. Sancho was by this time plastered and had lain down, and though he strove to sleep the pain of his ribs would not let him, while Don Quixote with the pain of his had his eyes as wide open as a hare’s.\n\nThe inn was all in silence, and in the whole of it there was no light except that given by a lantern that hung burning in the middle of the gateway. This strange stillness, and the thoughts, always present to our knight’s mind, of the incidents described at every turn in the books that were the cause of his misfortune, conjured up to his imagination as extraordinary a delusion as can well be conceived, which was that he fancied himself to have reached a famous castle (for, as has been said, all the inns he lodged in were castles to his eyes), and that the daughter of the innkeeper was daughter of the lord of the castle, and that she, won by his high-bred bearing, had fallen in love with him, and had promised to come to his bed for a while that night without the knowledge of her parents; and holding all this fantasy that he had constructed as solid fact, he began to feel uneasy and to consider the perilous risk which his virtue was about to encounter, and he resolved in his heart to commit no treason to his lady Dulcinea del Toboso, even though the queen Guinevere herself and the dame Quintanona should present themselves before him.\n\nWhile he was taken up with these vagaries, then, the time and the hour--an unlucky one for him--arrived for the Asturian to come, who in her smock, with bare feet and her hair gathered into a fustian coif, with noiseless and cautious steps entered the chamber where the three were quartered, in quest of the carrier; but scarcely had she gained the door when Don Quixote perceived her, and sitting up in his bed in spite of his plasters and the pain of his ribs, he stretched out his arms to receive his beauteous damsel. The Asturian, who went all doubled up and in silence with her hands before her feeling for her lover, encountered the arms of Don Quixote, who grasped her tightly by the wrist, and drawing her towards him, while she dared not utter a word, made her sit down on the bed. He then felt her smock, and although it was of sackcloth it appeared to him to be of the finest and softest silk: on her wrists she wore some glass beads, but to him they had the sheen of precious Orient pearls: her hair, which in some measure resembled a horse’s mane, he rated as threads of the brightest gold of Araby, whose refulgence dimmed the sun himself: her breath, which no doubt smelt of yesterday’s stale salad, seemed to him to diffuse a sweet aromatic fragrance from her mouth; and, in short, he drew her portrait in his imagination with the same features and in the same style as that which he had seen in his books of the other princesses who, smitten by love, came with all the adornments that are here set down, to see the sorely wounded knight; and so great was the poor gentleman’s blindness that neither touch, nor smell, nor anything else about the good lass that would have made any but a carrier vomit, were enough to undeceive him; on the contrary, he was persuaded he had the goddess of beauty in his arms, and holding her firmly in his grasp he went on to say in low, tender voice:\n\n“Would that I found myself, lovely and exalted lady, in a position to repay such a favour as that which you, by the sight of your great beauty, have granted me; but fortune, which is never weary of persecuting the good, has chosen to place me upon this bed, where I lie so bruised and broken that though my inclination would gladly comply with yours it is impossible; besides, to this impossibility another yet greater is to be added, which is the faith that I have pledged to the peerless Dulcinea del Toboso, sole lady of my most secret thoughts; and were it not that this stood in the way I should not be so insensible a knight as to miss the happy opportunity which your great goodness has offered me.”\n\nMaritornes was fretting and sweating at finding herself held so fast by Don Quixote, and not understanding or heeding the words he addressed to her, she strove without speaking to free herself. The worthy carrier, whose unholy thoughts kept him awake, was aware of his doxy the moment she entered the door, and was listening attentively to all Don Quixote said; and jealous that the Asturian should have broken her word with him for another, drew nearer to Don Quixote’s bed and stood still to see what would come of this talk which he could not understand; but when he perceived the wench struggling to get free and Don Quixote striving to hold her, not relishing the joke he raised his arm and delivered such a terrible cuff on the lank jaws of the amorous knight that he bathed all his mouth in blood, and not content with this he mounted on his ribs and with his feet tramped all over them at a pace rather smarter than a trot. The bed which was somewhat crazy and not very firm on its feet, unable to support the additional weight of the carrier, came to the ground, and at the mighty crash of this the innkeeper awoke and at once concluded that it must be some brawl of Maritornes’, because after calling loudly to her he got no answer. With this suspicion he got up, and lighting a lamp hastened to the quarter where he had heard the disturbance. The wench, seeing that her master was coming and knowing that his temper was terrible, frightened and panic-stricken made for the bed of Sancho Panza, who still slept, and crouching upon it made a ball of herself.\n\nThe innkeeper came in exclaiming, “Where art thou, strumpet? Of course this is some of thy work.” At this Sancho awoke, and feeling this mass almost on top of him fancied he had the nightmare and began to distribute fisticuffs all round, of which a certain share fell upon Maritornes, who, irritated by the pain and flinging modesty aside, paid back so many in return to Sancho that she woke him up in spite of himself. He then, finding himself so handled, by whom he knew not, raising himself up as well as he could, grappled with Maritornes, and he and she between them began the bitterest and drollest scrimmage in the world. The carrier, however, perceiving by the light of the innkeeper candle how it fared with his ladylove, quitting Don Quixote, ran to bring her the help she needed; and the innkeeper did the same but with a different intention, for his was to chastise the lass, as he believed that beyond a doubt she alone was the cause of all the harmony. And so, as the saying is, cat to rat, rat to rope, rope to stick, the carrier pounded Sancho, Sancho the lass, she him, and the innkeeper her, and all worked away so briskly that they did not give themselves a moment’s rest; and the best of it was that the innkeeper’s lamp went out, and as they were left in the dark they all laid on one upon the other in a mass so unmercifully that there was not a sound spot left where a hand could light.\n\nIt so happened that there was lodging that night in the inn a caudrillero of what they call the Old Holy Brotherhood of Toledo, who, also hearing the extraordinary noise of the conflict, seized his staff and the tin case with his warrants, and made his way in the dark into the room crying: “Hold! in the name of the Jurisdiction! Hold! in the name of the Holy Brotherhood!”\n\nThe first that he came upon was the pummelled Don Quixote, who lay stretched senseless on his back upon his broken-down bed, and, his hand falling on the beard as he felt about, he continued to cry, “Help for the Jurisdiction!” but perceiving that he whom he had laid hold of did not move or stir, he concluded that he was dead and that those in the room were his murderers, and with this suspicion he raised his voice still higher, calling out, “Shut the inn gate; see that no one goes out; they have killed a man here!” This cry startled them all, and each dropped the contest at the point at which the voice reached him. The innkeeper retreated to his room, the carrier to his pack-saddles, the lass to her crib; the unlucky Don Quixote and Sancho alone were unable to move from where they were. The cuadrillero on this let go Don Quixote’s beard, and went out to look for a light to search for and apprehend the culprits; but not finding one, as the innkeeper had purposely extinguished the lantern on retreating to his room, he was compelled to have recourse to the hearth, where after much time and trouble he lit another lamp.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII.\n\nIN WHICH ARE CONTAINED THE INNUMERABLE TROUBLES WHICH THE BRAVE DON QUIXOTE AND HIS GOOD SQUIRE SANCHO PANZA ENDURED IN THE INN, WHICH TO HIS MISFORTUNE HE TOOK TO BE A CASTLE\nBy this time Don Quixote had recovered from his swoon; and in the same tone of voice in which he had called to his squire the day before when he lay stretched “in the vale of the stakes,” he began calling to him now, “Sancho, my friend, art thou asleep? sleepest thou, friend Sancho?”\n\n“How can I sleep, curses on it!” returned Sancho discontentedly and bitterly, “when it is plain that all the devils have been at me this night?”\n\n“Thou mayest well believe that,” answered Don Quixote, “because, either I know little, or this castle is enchanted, for thou must know-but this that I am now about to tell thee thou must swear to keep secret until after my death.”\n\n“I swear it,” answered Sancho.\n\n“I say so,” continued Don Quixote, “because I hate taking away anyone’s good name.”\n\n“I say,” replied Sancho, “that I swear to hold my tongue about it till the end of your worship’s days, and God grant I may be able to let it out to-morrow.”\n\n“Do I do thee such injuries, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “that thou wouldst see me dead so soon?”\n\n“It is not for that,” replied Sancho, “but because I hate keeping things long, and I don’t want them to grow rotten with me from over-keeping.”\n\n“At any rate,” said Don Quixote, “I have more confidence in thy affection and good nature; and so I would have thee know that this night there befell me one of the strangest adventures that I could describe, and to relate it to thee briefly thou must know that a little while ago the daughter of the lord of this castle came to me, and that she is the most elegant and beautiful damsel that could be found in the wide world. What I could tell thee of the charms of her person! of her lively wit! of other secret matters which, to preserve the fealty I owe to my lady Dulcinea del Toboso, I shall pass over unnoticed and in silence! I will only tell thee that, either fate being envious of so great a boon placed in my hands by good fortune, or perhaps (and this is more probable) this castle being, as I have already said, enchanted, at the time when I was engaged in the sweetest and most amorous discourse with her, there came, without my seeing or knowing whence it came, a hand attached to some arm of some huge giant, that planted such a cuff on my jaws that I have them all bathed in blood, and then pummelled me in such a way that I am in a worse plight than yesterday when the carriers, on account of Rocinante’s misbehaviour, inflicted on us the injury thou knowest of; whence conjecture that there must be some enchanted Moor guarding the treasure of this damsel’s beauty, and that it is not for me.”\n\n“Not for me either,” said Sancho, “for more than four hundred Moors have so thrashed me that the drubbing of the stakes was cakes and fancy-bread to it. But tell me, señor, what do you call this excellent and rare adventure that has left us as we are left now? Though your worship was not so badly off, having in your arms that incomparable beauty you spoke of; but I, what did I have, except the heaviest whacks I think I had in all my life? Unlucky me and the mother that bore me! for I am not a knight-errant and never expect to be one, and of all the mishaps, the greater part falls to my share.”\n\n“Then thou hast been thrashed too?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“Didn’t I say so? worse luck to my line!” said Sancho.\n\n“Be not distressed, friend,” said Don Quixote, “for I will now make the precious balsam with which we shall cure ourselves in the twinkling of an eye.”\n\nBy this time the cuadrillero had succeeded in lighting the lamp, and came in to see the man that he thought had been killed; and as Sancho caught sight of him at the door, seeing him coming in his shirt, with a cloth on his head, and a lamp in his hand, and a very forbidding countenance, he said to his master, “Señor, can it be that this is the enchanted Moor coming back to give us more castigation if there be anything still left in the ink-bottle?”\n\n“It cannot be the Moor,” answered Don Quixote, “for those under enchantment do not let themselves be seen by anyone.”\n\n“If they don’t let themselves be seen, they let themselves be felt,” said Sancho; “if not, let my shoulders speak to the point.”\n\n“Mine could speak too,” said Don Quixote, “but that is not a sufficient reason for believing that what we see is the enchanted Moor.”\n\nThe officer came up, and finding them engaged in such a peaceful conversation, stood amazed; though Don Quixote, to be sure, still lay on his back unable to move from pure pummelling and plasters. The officer turned to him and said, “Well, how goes it, good man?”\n\n“I would speak more politely if I were you,” replied Don Quixote; “is it the way of this country to address knights-errant in that style, you booby?”\n\nThe cuadrillero finding himself so disrespectfully treated by such a sorry-looking individual, lost his temper, and raising the lamp full of oil, smote Don Quixote such a blow with it on the head that he gave him a badly broken pate; then, all being in darkness, he went out, and Sancho Panza said, “That is certainly the enchanted Moor, Señor, and he keeps the treasure for others, and for us only the cuffs and lamp-whacks.”\n\n“That is the truth,” answered Don Quixote, “and there is no use in troubling oneself about these matters of enchantment or being angry or vexed at them, for as they are invisible and visionary we shall find no one on whom to avenge ourselves, do what we may; rise, Sancho, if thou canst, and call the alcaide of this fortress, and get him to give me a little oil, wine, salt, and rosemary to make the salutiferous balsam, for indeed I believe I have great need of it now, because I am losing much blood from the wound that phantom gave me.”\n\nSancho got up with pain enough in his bones, and went after the innkeeper in the dark, and meeting the officer, who was looking to see what had become of his enemy, he said to him, “Señor, whoever you are, do us the favour and kindness to give us a little rosemary, oil, salt, and wine, for it is wanted to cure one of the best knights-errant on earth, who lies on yonder bed wounded by the hands of the enchanted Moor that is in this inn.”\n\nWhen the officer heard him talk in this way, he took him for a man out of his senses, and as day was now beginning to break, he opened the inn gate, and calling the host, he told him what this good man wanted. The host furnished him with what he required, and Sancho brought it to Don Quixote, who, with his hand to his head, was bewailing the pain of the blow of the lamp, which had done him no more harm than raising a couple of rather large lumps, and what he fancied blood was only the sweat that flowed from him in his sufferings during the late storm. To be brief, he took the materials, of which he made a compound, mixing them all and boiling them a good while until it seemed to him they had come to perfection. He then asked for some vial to pour it into, and as there was not one in the inn, he decided on putting it into a tin oil-bottle or flask of which the host made him a free gift; and over the flask he repeated more than eighty paternosters and as many more ave-marias, salves, and credos, accompanying each word with a cross by way of benediction, at all which there were present Sancho, the innkeeper, and the cuadrillero; for the carrier was now peacefully engaged in attending to the comfort of his mules.\n\nThis being accomplished, he felt anxious to make trial himself, on the spot, of the virtue of this precious balsam, as he considered it, and so he drank near a quart of what could not be put into the flask and remained in the pigskin in which it had been boiled; but scarcely had he done drinking when he began to vomit in such a way that nothing was left in his stomach, and with the pangs and spasms of vomiting he broke into a profuse sweat, on account of which he bade them cover him up and leave him alone. They did so, and he lay sleeping more than three hours, at the end of which he awoke and felt very great bodily relief and so much ease from his bruises that he thought himself quite cured, and verily believed he had hit upon the balsam of Fierabras; and that with this remedy he might thenceforward, without any fear, face any kind of destruction, battle, or combat, however perilous it might be.\n\nSancho Panza, who also regarded the amendment of his master as miraculous, begged him to give him what was left in the pigskin, which was no small quantity. Don Quixote consented, and he, taking it with both hands, in good faith and with a better will, gulped down and drained off very little less than his master. But the fact is, that the stomach of poor Sancho was of necessity not so delicate as that of his master, and so, before vomiting, he was seized with such gripings and retchings, and such sweats and faintness, that verily and truly be believed his last hour had come, and finding himself so racked and tormented he cursed the balsam and the thief that had given it to him.\n\nDon Quixote seeing him in this state said, “It is my belief, Sancho, that this mischief comes of thy not being dubbed a knight, for I am persuaded this liquor cannot be good for those who are not so.”\n\n“If your worship knew that,” returned Sancho--“woe betide me and all my kindred!--why did you let me taste it?”\n\nAt this moment the draught took effect, and the poor squire began to discharge both ways at such a rate that the rush mat on which he had thrown himself and the canvas blanket he had covering him were fit for nothing afterwards. He sweated and perspired with such paroxysms and convulsions that not only he himself but all present thought his end had come. This tempest and tribulation lasted about two hours, at the end of which he was left, not like his master, but so weak and exhausted that he could not stand. Don Quixote, however, who, as has been said, felt himself relieved and well, was eager to take his departure at once in quest of adventures, as it seemed to him that all the time he loitered there was a fraud upon the world and those in it who stood in need of his help and protection, all the more when he had the security and confidence his balsam afforded him; and so, urged by this impulse, he saddled Rocinante himself and put the pack-saddle on his squire’s beast, whom likewise he helped to dress and mount the ass; after which he mounted his horse and turning to a corner of the inn he laid hold of a pike that stood there, to serve him by way of a lance. All that were in the inn, who were more than twenty persons, stood watching him; the innkeeper’s daughter was likewise observing him, and he too never took his eyes off her, and from time to time fetched a sigh that he seemed to pluck up from the depths of his bowels; but they all thought it must be from the pain he felt in his ribs; at any rate they who had seen him plastered the night before thought so.\n\nAs soon as they were both mounted, at the gate of the inn, he called to the host and said in a very grave and measured voice, “Many and great are the favours, Señor Alcaide, that I have received in this castle of yours, and I remain under the deepest obligation to be grateful to you for them all the days of my life; if I can repay them in avenging you of any arrogant foe who may have wronged you, know that my calling is no other than to aid the weak, to avenge those who suffer wrong, and to chastise perfidy. Search your memory, and if you find anything of this kind you need only tell me of it, and I promise you by the order of knighthood which I have received to procure you satisfaction and reparation to the utmost of your desire.”\n\nThe innkeeper replied to him with equal calmness, “Sir Knight, I do not want your worship to avenge me of any wrong, because when any is done me I can take what vengeance seems good to me; the only thing I want is that you pay me the score that you have run up in the inn last night, as well for the straw and barley for your two beasts, as for supper and beds.”\n\n“Then this is an inn?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“And a very respectable one,” said the innkeeper.\n\n“I have been under a mistake all this time,” answered Don Quixote, “for in truth I thought it was a castle, and not a bad one; but since it appears that it is not a castle but an inn, all that can be done now is that you should excuse the payment, for I cannot contravene the rule of knights-errant, of whom I know as a fact (and up to the present I have read nothing to the contrary) that they never paid for lodging or anything else in the inn where they might be; for any hospitality that might be offered them is their due by law and right in return for the insufferable toil they endure in seeking adventures by night and by day, in summer and in winter, on foot and on horseback, in hunger and thirst, cold and heat, exposed to all the inclemencies of heaven and all the hardships of earth.”\n\n“I have little to do with that,” replied the innkeeper; “pay me what you owe me, and let us have no more talk of chivalry, for all I care about is to get my money.”\n\n“You are a stupid, scurvy innkeeper,” said Don Quixote, and putting spurs to Rocinante and bringing his pike to the slope he rode out of the inn before anyone could stop him, and pushed on some distance without looking to see if his squire was following him.\n\nThe innkeeper when he saw him go without paying him ran to get payment of Sancho, who said that as his master would not pay neither would he, because, being as he was squire to a knight-errant, the same rule and reason held good for him as for his master with regard to not paying anything in inns and hostelries. At this the innkeeper waxed very wroth, and threatened if he did not pay to compel him in a way that he would not like. To which Sancho made answer that by the law of chivalry his master had received he would not pay a rap, though it cost him his life; for the excellent and ancient usage of knights-errant was not going to be violated by him, nor should the squires of such as were yet to come into the world ever complain of him or reproach him with breaking so just a privilege.\n\nThe ill-luck of the unfortunate Sancho so ordered it that among the company in the inn there were four woolcarders from Segovia, three needle-makers from the Colt of Cordova, and two lodgers from the Fair of Seville, lively fellows, tender-hearted, fond of a joke, and playful, who, almost as if instigated and moved by a common impulse, made up to Sancho and dismounted him from his ass, while one of them went in for the blanket of the host’s bed; but on flinging him into it they looked up, and seeing that the ceiling was somewhat lower what they required for their work, they decided upon going out into the yard, which was bounded by the sky, and there, putting Sancho in the middle of the blanket, they began to raise him high, making sport with him as they would with a dog at Shrovetide.\n\nThe cries of the poor blanketed wretch were so loud that they reached the ears of his master, who, halting to listen attentively, was persuaded that some new adventure was coming, until he clearly perceived that it was his squire who uttered them. Wheeling about he came up to the inn with a laborious gallop, and finding it shut went round it to see if he could find some way of getting in; but as soon as he came to the wall of the yard, which was not very high, he discovered the game that was being played with his squire. He saw him rising and falling in the air with such grace and nimbleness that, had his rage allowed him, it is my belief he would have laughed. He tried to climb from his horse on to the top of the wall, but he was so bruised and battered that he could not even dismount; and so from the back of his horse he began to utter such maledictions and objurgations against those who were blanketing Sancho as it would be impossible to write down accurately: they, however, did not stay their laughter or their work for this, nor did the flying Sancho cease his lamentations, mingled now with threats, now with entreaties but all to little purpose, or none at all, until from pure weariness they left off. They then brought him his ass, and mounting him on top of it they put his jacket round him; and the compassionate Maritornes, seeing him so exhausted, thought fit to refresh him with a jug of water, and that it might be all the cooler she fetched it from the well. Sancho took it, and as he was raising it to his mouth he was stopped by the cries of his master exclaiming, “Sancho, my son, drink not water; drink it not, my son, for it will kill thee; see, here I have the blessed balsam (and he held up the flask of liquor), and with drinking two drops of it thou wilt certainly be restored.”\n\nAt these words Sancho turned his eyes asquint, and in a still louder voice said, “Can it be your worship has forgotten that I am not a knight, or do you want me to end by vomiting up what bowels I have left after last night? Keep your liquor in the name of all the devils, and leave me to myself!” and at one and the same instant he left off talking and began drinking; but as at the first sup he perceived it was water he did not care to go on with it, and begged Maritornes to fetch him some wine, which she did with right good will, and paid for it with her own money; for indeed they say of her that, though she was in that line of life, there was some faint and distant resemblance to a Christian about her. When Sancho had done drinking he dug his heels into his ass, and the gate of the inn being thrown open he passed out very well pleased at having paid nothing and carried his point, though it had been at the expense of his usual sureties, his shoulders. It is true that the innkeeper detained his alforjas in payment of what was owing to him, but Sancho took his departure in such a flurry that he never missed them. The innkeeper, as soon as he saw him off, wanted to bar the gate close, but the blanketers would not agree to it, for they were fellows who would not have cared two farthings for Don Quixote, even had he been really one of the knights-errant of the Round Table.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII.\n\nIN WHICH IS RELATED THE DISCOURSE SANCHO PANZA HELD WITH HIS MASTER, DON QUIXOTE, AND OTHER ADVENTURES WORTH RELATING\nSancho reached his master so limp and faint that he could not urge on his beast. When Don Quixote saw the state he was in he said, “I have now come to the conclusion, good Sancho, that this castle or inn is beyond a doubt enchanted, because those who have so atrociously diverted themselves with thee, what can they be but phantoms or beings of another world? and I hold this confirmed by having noticed that when I was by the wall of the yard witnessing the acts of thy sad tragedy, it was out of my power to mount upon it, nor could I even dismount from Rocinante, because they no doubt had me enchanted; for I swear to thee by the faith of what I am that if I had been able to climb up or dismount, I would have avenged thee in such a way that those braggart thieves would have remembered their freak for ever, even though in so doing I knew that I contravened the laws of chivalry, which, as I have often told thee, do not permit a knight to lay hands on him who is not one, save in case of urgent and great necessity in defence of his own life and person.”\n\n“I would have avenged myself too if I could,” said Sancho, “whether I had been dubbed knight or not, but I could not; though for my part I am persuaded those who amused themselves with me were not phantoms or enchanted men, as your worship says, but men of flesh and bone like ourselves; and they all had their names, for I heard them name them when they were tossing me, and one was called Pedro Martinez, and another Tenorio Hernandez, and the innkeeper, I heard, was called Juan Palomeque the Left-handed; so that, señor, your not being able to leap over the wall of the yard or dismount from your horse came of something else besides enchantments; and what I make out clearly from all this is, that these adventures we go seeking will in the end lead us into such misadventures that we shall not know which is our right foot; and that the best and wisest thing, according to my small wits, would be for us to return home, now that it is harvest-time, and attend to our business, and give over wandering from Zeca to Mecca and from pail to bucket, as the saying is.”\n\n“How little thou knowest about chivalry, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote; “hold thy peace and have patience; the day will come when thou shalt see with thine own eyes what an honourable thing it is to wander in the pursuit of this calling; nay, tell me, what greater pleasure can there be in the world, or what delight can equal that of winning a battle, and triumphing over one’s enemy? None, beyond all doubt.”\n\n“Very likely,” answered Sancho, “though I do not know it; all I know is that since we have been knights-errant, or since your worship has been one (for I have no right to reckon myself one of so honourable a number) we have never won any battle except the one with the Biscayan, and even out of that your worship came with half an ear and half a helmet the less; and from that till now it has been all cudgellings and more cudgellings, cuffs and more cuffs, I getting the blanketing over and above, and falling in with enchanted persons on whom I cannot avenge myself so as to know what the delight, as your worship calls it, of conquering an enemy is like.”\n\n“That is what vexes me, and what ought to vex thee, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote; “but henceforward I will endeavour to have at hand some sword made by such craft that no kind of enchantments can take effect upon him who carries it, and it is even possible that fortune may procure for me that which belonged to Amadis when he was called ‘The Knight of the Burning Sword,’ which was one of the best swords that ever knight in the world possessed, for, besides having the said virtue, it cut like a razor, and there was no armour, however strong and enchanted it might be, that could resist it.”\n\n“Such is my luck,” said Sancho, “that even if that happened and your worship found some such sword, it would, like the balsam, turn out serviceable and good for dubbed knights only, and as for the squires, they might sup sorrow.”\n\n“Fear not that, Sancho,” said Don Quixote: “Heaven will deal better by thee.”\n\nThus talking, Don Quixote and his squire were going along, when, on the road they were following, Don Quixote perceived approaching them a large and thick cloud of dust, on seeing which he turned to Sancho and said:\n\n“This is the day, Sancho, on which will be seen the boon my fortune is reserving for me; this, I say, is the day on which as much as on any other shall be displayed the might of my arm, and on which I shall do deeds that shall remain written in the book of fame for all ages to come. Seest thou that cloud of dust which rises yonder? Well, then, all that is churned up by a vast army composed of various and countless nations that comes marching there.”\n\n“According to that there must be two,” said Sancho, “for on this opposite side also there rises just such another cloud of dust.”\n\nDon Quixote turned to look and found that it was true, and rejoicing exceedingly, he concluded that they were two armies about to engage and encounter in the midst of that broad plain; for at all times and seasons his fancy was full of the battles, enchantments, adventures, crazy feats, loves, and defiances that are recorded in the books of chivalry, and everything he said, thought, or did had reference to such things. Now the cloud of dust he had seen was raised by two great droves of sheep coming along the same road in opposite directions, which, because of the dust, did not become visible until they drew near, but Don Quixote asserted so positively that they were armies that Sancho was led to believe it and say, “Well, and what are we to do, señor?”\n\n“What?” said Don Quixote: “give aid and assistance to the weak and those who need it; and thou must know, Sancho, that this which comes opposite to us is conducted and led by the mighty emperor Alifanfaron, lord of the great isle of Trapobana; this other that marches behind me is that of his enemy the king of the Garamantas, Pentapolin of the Bare Arm, for he always goes into battle with his right arm bare.”\n\n“But why are these two lords such enemies?”\n\n“They are at enmity,” replied Don Quixote, “because this Alifanfaron is a furious pagan and is in love with the daughter of Pentapolin, who is a very beautiful and moreover gracious lady, and a Christian, and her father is unwilling to bestow her upon the pagan king unless he first abandons the religion of his false prophet Mahomet, and adopts his own.”\n\n“By my beard,” said Sancho, “but Pentapolin does quite right, and I will help him as much as I can.”\n\n“In that thou wilt do what is thy duty, Sancho,” said Don Quixote; “for to engage in battles of this sort it is not requisite to be a dubbed knight.”\n\n“That I can well understand,” answered Sancho; “but where shall we put this ass where we may be sure to find him after the fray is over? for I believe it has not been the custom so far to go into battle on a beast of this kind.”\n\n“That is true,” said Don Quixote, “and what you had best do with him is to leave him to take his chance whether he be lost or not, for the horses we shall have when we come out victors will be so many that even Rocinante will run a risk of being changed for another. But attend to me and observe, for I wish to give thee some account of the chief knights who accompany these two armies; and that thou mayest the better see and mark, let us withdraw to that hillock which rises yonder, whence both armies may be seen.”\n\nThey did so, and placed themselves on a rising ground from which the two droves that Don Quixote made armies of might have been plainly seen if the clouds of dust they raised had not obscured them and blinded the sight; nevertheless, seeing in his imagination what he did not see and what did not exist, he began thus in a loud voice:\n\n“That knight whom thou seest yonder in yellow armour, who bears upon his shield a lion crowned crouching at the feet of a damsel, is the valiant Laurcalco, lord of the Silver Bridge; that one in armour with flowers of gold, who bears on his shield three crowns argent on an azure field, is the dreaded Micocolembo, grand duke of Quirocia; that other of gigantic frame, on his right hand, is the ever dauntless Brandabarbaran de Boliche, lord of the three Arabias, who for armour wears that serpent skin, and has for shield a gate which, according to tradition, is one of those of the temple that Samson brought to the ground when by his death he revenged himself upon his enemies. But turn thine eyes to the other side, and thou shalt see in front and in the van of this other army the ever victorious and never vanquished Timonel of Carcajona, prince of New Biscay, who comes in armour with arms quartered azure, vert, white, and yellow, and bears on his shield a cat or on a field tawny with a motto which says Miau, which is the beginning of the name of his lady, who according to report is the peerless Miaulina, daughter of the duke Alfeniquen of the Algarve; the other, who burdens and presses the loins of that powerful charger and bears arms white as snow and a shield blank and without any device, is a novice knight, a Frenchman by birth, Pierres Papin by name, lord of the baronies of Utrique; that other, who with iron-shod heels strikes the flanks of that nimble parti-coloured zebra, and for arms bears azure vair, is the mighty duke of Nerbia, Espartafilardo del Bosque, who bears for device on his shield an asparagus plant with a motto in Castilian that says, Rastrea mi suerte.” And so he went on naming a number of knights of one squadron or the other out of his imagination, and to all he assigned off-hand their arms, colours, devices, and mottoes, carried away by the illusions of his unheard-of craze; and without a pause, he continued, “People of divers nations compose this squadron in front; here are those that drink of the sweet waters of the famous Xanthus, those that scour the woody Massilian plains, those that sift the pure fine gold of Arabia Felix, those that enjoy the famed cool banks of the crystal Thermodon, those that in many and various ways divert the streams of the golden Pactolus, the Numidians, faithless in their promises, the Persians renowned in archery, the Parthians and the Medes that fight as they fly, the Arabs that ever shift their dwellings, the Scythians as cruel as they are fair, the Ethiopians with pierced lips, and an infinity of other nations whose features I recognise and descry, though I cannot recall their names. In this other squadron there come those that drink of the crystal streams of the olive-bearing Betis, those that make smooth their countenances with the water of the ever rich and golden Tagus, those that rejoice in the fertilising flow of the divine Genil, those that roam the Tartesian plains abounding in pasture, those that take their pleasure in the Elysian meadows of Jerez, the rich Manchegans crowned with ruddy ears of corn, the wearers of iron, old relics of the Gothic race, those that bathe in the Pisuerga renowned for its gentle current, those that feed their herds along the spreading pastures of the winding Guadiana famed for its hidden course, those that tremble with the cold of the pineclad Pyrenees or the dazzling snows of the lofty Apennine; in a word, as many as all Europe includes and contains.”\n\nGood God! what a number of countries and nations he named! giving to each its proper attributes with marvellous readiness; brimful and saturated with what he had read in his lying books! Sancho Panza hung upon his words without speaking, and from time to time turned to try if he could see the knights and giants his master was describing, and as he could not make out one of them he said to him:\n\n“Señor, devil take it if there’s a sign of any man you talk of, knight or giant, in the whole thing; maybe it’s all enchantment, like the phantoms last night.”\n\n“How canst thou say that!” answered Don Quixote; “dost thou not hear the neighing of the steeds, the braying of the trumpets, the roll of the drums?”\n\n“I hear nothing but a great bleating of ewes and sheep,” said Sancho; which was true, for by this time the two flocks had come close.\n\n“The fear thou art in, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “prevents thee from seeing or hearing correctly, for one of the effects of fear is to derange the senses and make things appear different from what they are; if thou art in such fear, withdraw to one side and leave me to myself, for alone I suffice to bring victory to that side to which I shall give my aid;” and so saying he gave Rocinante the spur, and putting the lance in rest, shot down the slope like a thunderbolt. Sancho shouted after him, crying, “Come back, Señor Don Quixote; I vow to God they are sheep and ewes you are charging! Come back! Unlucky the father that begot me! what madness is this! Look, there is no giant, nor knight, nor cats, nor arms, nor shields quartered or whole, nor vair azure or bedevilled. What are you about? Sinner that I am before God!” But not for all these entreaties did Don Quixote turn back; on the contrary he went on shouting out, “Ho, knights, ye who follow and fight under the banners of the valiant emperor Pentapolin of the Bare Arm, follow me all; ye shall see how easily I shall give him his revenge over his enemy Alifanfaron of the Trapobana.”\n\nSo saying, he dashed into the midst of the squadron of ewes, and began spearing them with as much spirit and intrepidity as if he were transfixing mortal enemies in earnest. The shepherds and drovers accompanying the flock shouted to him to desist; seeing it was no use, they ungirt their slings and began to salute his ears with stones as big as one’s fist. Don Quixote gave no heed to the stones, but, letting drive right and left kept saying:\n\n“Where art thou, proud Alifanfaron? Come before me; I am a single knight who would fain prove thy prowess hand to hand, and make thee yield thy life a penalty for the wrong thou dost to the valiant Pentapolin Garamanta.” Here came a sugar-plum from the brook that struck him on the side and buried a couple of ribs in his body. Feeling himself so smitten, he imagined himself slain or badly wounded for certain, and recollecting his liquor he drew out his flask, and putting it to his mouth began to pour the contents into his stomach; but ere he had succeeded in swallowing what seemed to him enough, there came another almond which struck him on the hand and on the flask so fairly that it smashed it to pieces, knocking three or four teeth and grinders out of his mouth in its course, and sorely crushing two fingers of his hand. Such was the force of the first blow and of the second, that the poor knight in spite of himself came down backwards off his horse. The shepherds came up, and felt sure they had killed him; so in all haste they collected their flock together, took up the dead beasts, of which there were more than seven, and made off without waiting to ascertain anything further.\n\nAll this time Sancho stood on the hill watching the crazy feats his master was performing, and tearing his beard and cursing the hour and the occasion when fortune had made him acquainted with him. Seeing him, then, brought to the ground, and that the shepherds had taken themselves off, he ran to him and found him in very bad case, though not unconscious; and said he:\n\n“Did I not tell you to come back, Señor Don Quixote; and that what you were going to attack were not armies but droves of sheep?”\n\n“That’s how that thief of a sage, my enemy, can alter and falsify things,” answered Don Quixote; “thou must know, Sancho, that it is a very easy matter for those of his sort to make us believe what they choose; and this malignant being who persecutes me, envious of the glory he knew I was to win in this battle, has turned the squadrons of the enemy into droves of sheep. At any rate, do this much, I beg of thee, Sancho, to undeceive thyself, and see that what I say is true; mount thy ass and follow them quietly, and thou shalt see that when they have gone some little distance from this they will return to their original shape and, ceasing to be sheep, become men in all respects as I described them to thee at first. But go not just yet, for I want thy help and assistance; come hither, and see how many of my teeth and grinders are missing, for I feel as if there was not one left in my mouth.”\n\nSancho came so close that he almost put his eyes into his mouth; now just at that moment the balsam had acted on the stomach of Don Quixote, so, at the very instant when Sancho came to examine his mouth, he discharged all its contents with more force than a musket, and full into the beard of the compassionate squire.\n\n“Holy Mary!” cried Sancho, “what is this that has happened me? Clearly this sinner is mortally wounded, as he vomits blood from the mouth;” but considering the matter a little more closely he perceived by the colour, taste, and smell, that it was not blood but the balsam from the flask which he had seen him drink; and he was taken with such a loathing that his stomach turned, and he vomited up his inside over his very master, and both were left in a precious state. Sancho ran to his ass to get something wherewith to clean himself, and relieve his master, out of his alforjas; but not finding them, he well-nigh took leave of his senses, and cursed himself anew, and in his heart resolved to quit his master and return home, even though he forfeited the wages of his service and all hopes of the promised island.\n\nDon Quixote now rose, and putting his left hand to his mouth to keep his teeth from falling out altogether, with the other he laid hold of the bridle of Rocinante, who had never stirred from his master’s side--so loyal and well-behaved was he--and betook himself to where the squire stood leaning over his ass with his hand to his cheek, like one in deep dejection. Seeing him in this mood, looking so sad, Don Quixote said to him:\n\n“Bear in mind, Sancho, that one man is no more than another, unless he does more than another; all these tempests that fall upon us are signs that fair weather is coming shortly, and that things will go well with us, for it is impossible for good or evil to last for ever; and hence it follows that the evil having lasted long, the good must be now nigh at hand; so thou must not distress thyself at the misfortunes which happen to me, since thou hast no share in them.”\n\n“How have I not?” replied Sancho; “was he whom they blanketed yesterday perchance any other than my father’s son? and the alforjas that are missing to-day with all my treasures, did they belong to any other but myself?”\n\n“What! are the alforjas missing, Sancho?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“Yes, they are missing,” answered Sancho.\n\n“In that case we have nothing to eat to-day,” replied Don Quixote.\n\n“It would be so,” answered Sancho, “if there were none of the herbs your worship says you know in these meadows, those with which knights-errant as unlucky as your worship are wont to supply such-like shortcomings.”\n\n“For all that,” answered Don Quixote, “I would rather have just now a quarter of bread, or a loaf and a couple of pilchards’ heads, than all the herbs described by Dioscorides, even with Doctor Laguna’s notes. Nevertheless, Sancho the Good, mount thy beast and come along with me, for God, who provides for all things, will not fail us (more especially when we are so active in his service as we are), since he fails not the midges of the air, nor the grubs of the earth, nor the tadpoles of the water, and is so merciful that he maketh his sun to rise on the good and on the evil, and sendeth rain on the unjust and on the just.”\n\n“Your worship would make a better preacher than knight-errant,” said Sancho.\n\n“Knights-errant knew and ought to know everything, Sancho,” said Don Quixote; “for there were knights-errant in former times as well qualified to deliver a sermon or discourse in the middle of an encampment, as if they had graduated in the University of Paris; whereby we may see that the lance has never blunted the pen, nor the pen the lance.”\n\n“Well, be it as your worship says,” replied Sancho; “let us be off now and find some place of shelter for the night, and God grant it may be somewhere where there are no blankets, nor blanketeers, nor phantoms, nor enchanted Moors; for if there are, may the devil take the whole concern.”\n\n“Ask that of God, my son,” said Don Quixote; “and do thou lead on where thou wilt, for this time I leave our lodging to thy choice; but reach me here thy hand, and feel with thy finger, and find out how many of my teeth and grinders are missing from this right side of the upper jaw, for it is there I feel the pain.”\n\nSancho put in his fingers, and feeling about asked him, “How many grinders used your worship have on this side?”\n\n“Four,” replied Don Quixote, “besides the back-tooth, all whole and quite sound.”\n\n“Mind what you are saying, señor.”\n\n“I say four, if not five,” answered Don Quixote, “for never in my life have I had tooth or grinder drawn, nor has any fallen out or been destroyed by any decay or rheum.”\n\n“Well, then,” said Sancho, “in this lower side your worship has no more than two grinders and a half, and in the upper neither a half nor any at all, for it is all as smooth as the palm of my hand.”\n\n“Luckless that I am!” said Don Quixote, hearing the sad news his squire gave him; “I had rather they despoiled me of an arm, so it were not the sword-arm; for I tell thee, Sancho, a mouth without teeth is like a mill without a millstone, and a tooth is much more to be prized than a diamond; but we who profess the austere order of chivalry are liable to all this. Mount, friend, and lead the way, and I will follow thee at whatever pace thou wilt.”\n\nSancho did as he bade him, and proceeded in the direction in which he thought he might find refuge without quitting the high road, which was there very much frequented. As they went along, then, at a slow pace--for the pain in Don Quixote’s jaws kept him uneasy and ill-disposed for speed--Sancho thought it well to amuse and divert him by talk of some kind, and among the things he said to him was that which will be told in the following chapter.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX.\n\nOF THE SHREWD DISCOURSE WHICH SANCHO HELD WITH HIS MASTER, AND OF THE ADVENTURE THAT BEFELL HIM WITH A DEAD BODY, TOGETHER WITH OTHER NOTABLE OCCURRENCES\n“It seems to me, señor, that all these mishaps that have befallen us of late have been without any doubt a punishment for the offence committed by your worship against the order of chivalry in not keeping the oath you made not to eat bread off a tablecloth or embrace the queen, and all the rest of it that your worship swore to observe until you had taken that helmet of Malandrino’s, or whatever the Moor is called, for I do not very well remember.”\n\n“Thou art very right, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “but to tell the truth, it had escaped my memory; and likewise thou mayest rely upon it that the affair of the blanket happened to thee because of thy fault in not reminding me of it in time; but I will make amends, for there are ways of compounding for everything in the order of chivalry.”\n\n“Why! have I taken an oath of some sort, then?” said Sancho.\n\n“It makes no matter that thou hast not taken an oath,” said Don Quixote; “suffice it that I see thou art not quite clear of complicity; and whether or no, it will not be ill done to provide ourselves with a remedy.”\n\n“In that case,” said Sancho, “mind that your worship does not forget this as you did the oath; perhaps the phantoms may take it into their heads to amuse themselves once more with me; or even with your worship if they see you so obstinate.”\n\nWhile engaged in this and other talk, night overtook them on the road before they had reached or discovered any place of shelter; and what made it still worse was that they were dying of hunger, for with the loss of the alforjas they had lost their entire larder and commissariat; and to complete the misfortune they met with an adventure which without any invention had really the appearance of one. It so happened that the night closed in somewhat darkly, but for all that they pushed on, Sancho feeling sure that as the road was the king’s highway they might reasonably expect to find some inn within a league or two. Going along, then, in this way, the night dark, the squire hungry, the master sharp-set, they saw coming towards them on the road they were travelling a great number of lights which looked exactly like stars in motion. Sancho was taken aback at the sight of them, nor did Don Quixote altogether relish them: the one pulled up his ass by the halter, the other his hack by the bridle, and they stood still, watching anxiously to see what all this would turn out to be, and found that the lights were approaching them, and the nearer they came the greater they seemed, at which spectacle Sancho began to shake like a man dosed with mercury, and Don Quixote’s hair stood on end; he, however, plucking up spirit a little, said:\n\n“This, no doubt, Sancho, will be a most mighty and perilous adventure, in which it will be needful for me to put forth all my valour and resolution.”\n\n“Unlucky me!” answered Sancho; “if this adventure happens to be one of phantoms, as I am beginning to think it is, where shall I find the ribs to bear it?”\n\n“Be they phantoms ever so much,” said Don Quixote, “I will not permit them to touch a thread of thy garments; for if they played tricks with thee the time before, it was because I was unable to leap the walls of the yard; but now we are on a wide plain, where I shall be able to wield my sword as I please.”\n\n“And if they enchant and cripple you as they did the last time,” said Sancho, “what difference will it make being on the open plain or not?”\n\n“For all that,” replied Don Quixote, “I entreat thee, Sancho, to keep a good heart, for experience will tell thee what mine is.”\n\n“I will, please God,” answered Sancho, and the two retiring to one side of the road set themselves to observe closely what all these moving lights might be; and very soon afterwards they made out some twenty encamisados, all on horseback, with lighted torches in their hands, the awe-inspiring aspect of whom completely extinguished the courage of Sancho, who began to chatter with his teeth like one in the cold fit of an ague; and his heart sank and his teeth chattered still more when they perceived distinctly that behind them there came a litter covered over with black and followed by six more mounted figures in mourning down to the very feet of their mules--for they could perceive plainly they were not horses by the easy pace at which they went. And as the encamisados came along they muttered to themselves in a low plaintive tone. This strange spectacle at such an hour and in such a solitary place was quite enough to strike terror into Sancho’s heart, and even into his master’s; and (save in Don Quixote’s case) did so, for all Sancho’s resolution had now broken down. It was just the opposite with his master, whose imagination immediately conjured up all this to him vividly as one of the adventures of his books.\n\nHe took it into his head that the litter was a bier on which was borne some sorely wounded or slain knight, to avenge whom was a task reserved for him alone; and without any further reasoning he laid his lance in rest, fixed himself firmly in his saddle, and with gallant spirit and bearing took up his position in the middle of the road where the encamisados must of necessity pass; and as soon as he saw them near at hand he raised his voice and said:\n\n“Halt, knights, or whosoever ye may be, and render me account of who ye are, whence ye come, where ye go, what it is ye carry upon that bier, for, to judge by appearances, either ye have done some wrong or some wrong has been done to you, and it is fitting and necessary that I should know, either that I may chastise you for the evil ye have done, or else that I may avenge you for the injury that has been inflicted upon you.”\n\n“We are in haste,” answered one of the encamisados, “and the inn is far off, and we cannot stop to render you such an account as you demand;” and spurring his mule he moved on.\n\nDon Quixote was mightily provoked by this answer, and seizing the mule by the bridle he said, “Halt, and be more mannerly, and render an account of what I have asked of you; else, take my defiance to combat, all of you.”\n\nThe mule was shy, and was so frightened at her bridle being seized that rearing up she flung her rider to the ground over her haunches. An attendant who was on foot, seeing the encamisado fall, began to abuse Don Quixote, who now moved to anger, without any more ado, laying his lance in rest charged one of the men in mourning and brought him badly wounded to the ground, and as he wheeled round upon the others the agility with which he attacked and routed them was a sight to see, for it seemed just as if wings had that instant grown upon Rocinante, so lightly and proudly did he bear himself. The encamisados were all timid folk and unarmed, so they speedily made their escape from the fray and set off at a run across the plain with their lighted torches, looking exactly like maskers running on some gala or festival night. The mourners, too, enveloped and swathed in their skirts and gowns, were unable to bestir themselves, and so with entire safety to himself Don Quixote belaboured them all and drove them off against their will, for they all thought it was no man but a devil from hell come to carry away the dead body they had in the litter.\n\nSancho beheld all this in astonishment at the intrepidity of his lord, and said to himself, “Clearly this master of mine is as bold and valiant as he says he is.”\n\nA burning torch lay on the ground near the first man whom the mule had thrown, by the light of which Don Quixote perceived him, and coming up to him he presented the point of the lance to his face, calling on him to yield himself prisoner, or else he would kill him; to which the prostrate man replied, “I am prisoner enough as it is; I cannot stir, for one of my legs is broken: I entreat you, if you be a Christian gentleman, not to kill me, which will be committing grave sacrilege, for I am a licentiate and I hold first orders.”\n\n“Then what the devil brought you here, being a churchman?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“What, señor?” said the other. “My bad luck.”\n\n“Then still worse awaits you,” said Don Quixote, “if you do not satisfy me as to all I asked you at first.”\n\n“You shall be soon satisfied,” said the licentiate; “you must know, then, that though just now I said I was a licentiate, I am only a bachelor, and my name is Alonzo Lopez; I am a native of Alcobendas, I come from the city of Baeza with eleven others, priests, the same who fled with the torches, and we are going to the city of Segovia accompanying a dead body which is in that litter, and is that of a gentleman who died in Baeza, where he was interred; and now, as I said, we are taking his bones to their burial-place, which is in Segovia, where he was born.”\n\n“And who killed him?” asked Don Quixote.\n\n“God, by means of a malignant fever that took him,” answered the bachelor.\n\n“In that case,” said Don Quixote, “the Lord has relieved me of the task of avenging his death had any other slain him; but, he who slew him having slain him, there is nothing for it but to be silent, and shrug one’s shoulders; I should do the same were he to slay myself; and I would have your reverence know that I am a knight of La Mancha, Don Quixote by name, and it is my business and calling to roam the world righting wrongs and redressing injuries.”\n\n“I do not know how that about righting wrongs can be,” said the bachelor, “for from straight you have made me crooked, leaving me with a broken leg that will never see itself straight again all the days of its life; and the injury you have redressed in my case has been to leave me injured in such a way that I shall remain injured for ever; and the height of misadventure it was to fall in with you who go in search of adventures.”\n\n“Things do not all happen in the same way,” answered Don Quixote; “it all came, Sir Bachelor Alonzo Lopez, of your going, as you did, by night, dressed in those surplices, with lighted torches, praying, covered with mourning, so that naturally you looked like something evil and of the other world; and so I could not avoid doing my duty in attacking you, and I should have attacked you even had I known positively that you were the very devils of hell, for such I certainly believed and took you to be.”\n\n“As my fate has so willed it,” said the bachelor, “I entreat you, sir knight-errant, whose errand has been such an evil one for me, to help me to get from under this mule that holds one of my legs caught between the stirrup and the saddle.”\n\n“I would have talked on till to-morrow,” said Don Quixote; “how long were you going to wait before telling me of your distress?”\n\nHe at once called to Sancho, who, however, had no mind to come, as he was just then engaged in unloading a sumpter mule, well laden with provender, which these worthy gentlemen had brought with them. Sancho made a bag of his coat, and, getting together as much as he could, and as the bag would hold, he loaded his beast, and then hastened to obey his master’s call, and helped him to remove the bachelor from under the mule; then putting him on her back he gave him the torch, and Don Quixote bade him follow the track of his companions, and beg pardon of them on his part for the wrong which he could not help doing them.\n\nAnd said Sancho, “If by chance these gentlemen should want to know who was the hero that served them so, your worship may tell them that he is the famous Don Quixote of La Mancha, otherwise called the Knight of the Rueful Countenance.”\n\nThe bachelor then took his departure.\n\nI forgot to mention that before he did so he said to Don Quixote, “Remember that you stand excommunicated for having laid violent hands on a holy thing, juxta illud, si quis, suadente diabolo.”\n\n“I do not understand that Latin,” answered Don Quixote, “but I know well I did not lay hands, only this pike; besides, I did not think I was committing an assault upon priests or things of the Church, which, like a Catholic and faithful Christian as I am, I respect and revere, but upon phantoms and spectres of the other world; but even so, I remember how it fared with Cid Ruy Diaz when he broke the chair of the ambassador of that king before his Holiness the Pope, who excommunicated him for the same; and yet the good Roderick of Vivar bore himself that day like a very noble and valiant knight.”\n\nOn hearing this the bachelor took his departure, as has been said, without making any reply; and Don Quixote asked Sancho what had induced him to call him the “Knight of the Rueful Countenance” more then than at any other time.\n\n“I will tell you,” answered Sancho; “it was because I have been looking at you for some time by the light of the torch held by that unfortunate, and verily your worship has got of late the most ill-favoured countenance I ever saw: it must be either owing to the fatigue of this combat, or else to the want of teeth and grinders.”\n\n“It is not that,” replied Don Quixote, “but because the sage whose duty it will be to write the history of my achievements must have thought it proper that I should take some distinctive name as all knights of yore did; one being ‘He of the Burning Sword,’ another ‘He of the Unicorn,’ this one ‘He of the Damsels,’ that ‘He of the Phoenix,’ another ‘The Knight of the Griffin,’ and another ‘He of the Death,’ and by these names and designations they were known all the world round; and so I say that the sage aforesaid must have put it into your mouth and mind just now to call me ‘The Knight of the Rueful Countenance,’ as I intend to call myself from this day forward; and that the said name may fit me better, I mean, when the opportunity offers, to have a very rueful countenance painted on my shield.”\n\n“There is no occasion, señor, for wasting time or money on making that countenance,” said Sancho; “for all that need be done is for your worship to show your own, face to face, to those who look at you, and without anything more, either image or shield, they will call you ‘Him of the Rueful Countenance’ and believe me I am telling you the truth, for I assure you, señor (and in good part be it said), hunger and the loss of your grinders have given you such an ill-favoured face that, as I say, the rueful picture may be very well spared.”\n\nDon Quixote laughed at Sancho’s pleasantry; nevertheless he resolved to call himself by that name, and have his shield or buckler painted as he had devised.\n\nDon Quixote would have looked to see whether the body in the litter were bones or not, but Sancho would not have it, saying:\n\n“Señor, you have ended this perilous adventure more safely for yourself than any of those I have seen: perhaps these people, though beaten and routed, may bethink themselves that it is a single man that has beaten them, and feeling sore and ashamed of it may take heart and come in search of us and give us trouble enough. The ass is in proper trim, the mountains are near at hand, hunger presses, we have nothing more to do but make good our retreat, and, as the saying is, the dead to the grave and the living to the loaf.”\n\nAnd driving his ass before him he begged his master to follow, who, feeling that Sancho was right, did so without replying; and after proceeding some little distance between two hills they found themselves in a wide and retired valley, where they alighted, and Sancho unloaded his beast, and stretched upon the green grass, with hunger for sauce, they breakfasted, dined, lunched, and supped all at once, satisfying their appetites with more than one store of cold meat which the dead man’s clerical gentlemen (who seldom put themselves on short allowance) had brought with them on their sumpter mule. But another piece of ill-luck befell them, which Sancho held the worst of all, and that was that they had no wine to drink, nor even water to moisten their lips; and as thirst tormented them, Sancho, observing that the meadow where they were was full of green and tender grass, said what will be told in the following chapter.\n\n# CHAPTER XX.\n\nOF THE UNEXAMPLED AND UNHEARD-OF ADVENTURE WHICH WAS ACHIEVED BY THE VALIANT DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA WITH LESS PERIL THAN ANY EVER ACHIEVED BY ANY FAMOUS KNIGHT IN THE WORLD\n“It cannot be, señor, but that this grass is a proof that there must be hard by some spring or brook to give it moisture, so it would be well to move a little farther on, that we may find some place where we may quench this terrible thirst that plagues us, which beyond a doubt is more distressing than hunger.”\n\nThe advice seemed good to Don Quixote, and, he leading Rocinante by the bridle and Sancho the ass by the halter, after he had packed away upon him the remains of the supper, they advanced the meadow feeling their way, for the darkness of the night made it impossible to see anything; but they had not gone two hundred paces when a loud noise of water, as if falling from great rocks, struck their ears. The sound cheered them greatly; but halting to make out by listening from what quarter it came they heard unseasonably another noise which spoiled the satisfaction the sound of the water gave them, especially for Sancho, who was by nature timid and faint-hearted. They heard, I say, strokes falling with a measured beat, and a certain rattling of iron and chains that, together with the furious din of the water, would have struck terror into any heart but Don Quixote’s. The night was, as has been said, dark, and they had happened to reach a spot in among some tall trees, whose leaves stirred by a gentle breeze made a low ominous sound; so that, what with the solitude, the place, the darkness, the noise of the water, and the rustling of the leaves, everything inspired awe and dread; more especially as they perceived that the strokes did not cease, nor the wind lull, nor morning approach; to all which might be added their ignorance as to where they were.\n\nBut Don Quixote, supported by his intrepid heart, leaped on Rocinante, and bracing his buckler on his arm, brought his pike to the slope, and said, “Friend Sancho, know that I by Heaven’s will have been born in this our iron age to revive revive in it the age of gold, or the golden as it is called; I am he for whom perils, mighty achievements, and valiant deeds are reserved; I am, I say again, he who is to revive the Knights of the Round Table, the Twelve of France and the Nine Worthies; and he who is to consign to oblivion the Platirs, the Tablantes, the Olivantes and Tirantes, the Phoebuses and Belianises, with the whole herd of famous knights-errant of days gone by, performing in these in which I live such exploits, marvels, and feats of arms as shall obscure their brightest deeds. Thou dost mark well, faithful and trusty squire, the gloom of this night, its strange silence, the dull confused murmur of those trees, the awful sound of that water in quest of which we came, that seems as though it were precipitating and dashing itself down from the lofty mountains of the Moon, and that incessant hammering that wounds and pains our ears; which things all together and each of itself are enough to instil fear, dread, and dismay into the breast of Mars himself, much more into one not used to hazards and adventures of the kind. Well, then, all this that I put before thee is but an incentive and stimulant to my spirit, making my heart burst in my bosom through eagerness to engage in this adventure, arduous as it promises to be; therefore tighten Rocinante’s girths a little, and God be with thee; wait for me here three days and no more, and if in that time I come not back, thou canst return to our village, and thence, to do me a favour and a service, thou wilt go to El Toboso, where thou shalt say to my incomparable lady Dulcinea that her captive knight hath died in attempting things that might make him worthy of being called hers.”\n\nWhen Sancho heard his master’s words he began to weep in the most pathetic way, saying:\n\n“Señor, I know not why your worship wants to attempt this so dreadful adventure; it is night now, no one sees us here, we can easily turn about and take ourselves out of danger, even if we don’t drink for three days to come; and as there is no one to see us, all the less will there be anyone to set us down as cowards; besides, I have many a time heard the curate of our village, whom your worship knows well, preach that he who seeks danger perishes in it; so it is not right to tempt God by trying so tremendous a feat from which there can be no escape save by a miracle, and Heaven has performed enough of them for your worship in delivering you from being blanketed as I was, and bringing you out victorious and safe and sound from among all those enemies that were with the dead man; and if all this does not move or soften that hard heart, let this thought and reflection move it, that you will have hardly quitted this spot when from pure fear I shall yield my soul up to anyone that will take it. I left home and wife and children to come and serve your worship, trusting to do better and not worse; but as covetousness bursts the bag, it has rent my hopes asunder, for just as I had them highest about getting that wretched unlucky island your worship has so often promised me, I see that instead and in lieu of it you mean to desert me now in a place so far from human reach: for God’s sake, master mine, deal not so unjustly by me, and if your worship will not entirely give up attempting this feat, at least put it off till morning, for by what the lore I learned when I was a shepherd tells me it cannot want three hours of dawn now, because the mouth of the Horn is overhead and makes midnight in the line of the left arm.”\n\n“How canst thou see, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “where it makes that line, or where this mouth or this occiput is that thou talkest of, when the night is so dark that there is not a star to be seen in the whole heaven?”\n\n“That’s true,” said Sancho, “but fear has sharp eyes, and sees things underground, much more above in heavens; besides, there is good reason to show that it now wants but little of day.”\n\n“Let it want what it may,” replied Don Quixote, “it shall not be said of me now or at any time that tears or entreaties turned me aside from doing what was in accordance with knightly usage; and so I beg of thee, Sancho, to hold thy peace, for God, who has put it into my heart to undertake now this so unexampled and terrible adventure, will take care to watch over my safety and console thy sorrow; what thou hast to do is to tighten Rocinante’s girths well, and wait here, for I shall come back shortly, alive or dead.”\n\nSancho perceiving it his master’s final resolve, and how little his tears, counsels, and entreaties prevailed with him, determined to have recourse to his own ingenuity and compel him, if he could, to wait till daylight; and so, while tightening the girths of the horse, he quietly and without being felt, with his ass’ halter tied both Rocinante’s legs, so that when Don Quixote strove to go he was unable as the horse could only move by jumps. Seeing the success of his trick, Sancho Panza said:\n\n“See there, señor! Heaven, moved by my tears and prayers, has so ordered it that Rocinante cannot stir; and if you will be obstinate, and spur and strike him, you will only provoke fortune, and kick, as they say, against the pricks.”\n\nDon Quixote at this grew desperate, but the more he drove his heels into the horse, the less he stirred him; and not having any suspicion of the tying, he was fain to resign himself and wait till daybreak or until Rocinante could move, firmly persuaded that all this came of something other than Sancho’s ingenuity. So he said to him, “As it is so, Sancho, and as Rocinante cannot move, I am content to wait till dawn smiles upon us, even though I weep while it delays its coming.”\n\n“There is no need to weep,” answered Sancho, “for I will amuse your worship by telling stories from this till daylight, unless indeed you like to dismount and lie down to sleep a little on the green grass after the fashion of knights-errant, so as to be fresher when day comes and the moment arrives for attempting this extraordinary adventure you are looking forward to.”\n\n“What art thou talking about dismounting or sleeping for?” said Don Quixote. “Am I, thinkest thou, one of those knights that take their rest in the presence of danger? Sleep thou who art born to sleep, or do as thou wilt, for I will act as I think most consistent with my character.”\n\n“Be not angry, master mine,” replied Sancho, “I did not mean to say that;” and coming close to him he laid one hand on the pommel of the saddle and the other on the cantle so that he held his master’s left thigh in his embrace, not daring to separate a finger’s width from him; so much afraid was he of the strokes which still resounded with a regular beat. Don Quixote bade him tell some story to amuse him as he had proposed, to which Sancho replied that he would if his dread of what he heard would let him; “Still,” said he, “I will strive to tell a story which, if I can manage to relate it, and nobody interferes with the telling, is the best of stories, and let your worship give me your attention, for here I begin. What was, was; and may the good that is to come be for all, and the evil for him who goes to look for it--your worship must know that the beginning the old folk used to put to their tales was not just as each one pleased; it was a maxim of Cato Zonzorino the Roman, that says ‘the evil for him that goes to look for it,’ and it comes as pat to the purpose now as ring to finger, to show that your worship should keep quiet and not go looking for evil in any quarter, and that we should go back by some other road, since nobody forces us to follow this in which so many terrors affright us.”\n\n“Go on with thy story, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “and leave the choice of our road to my care.”\n\n“I say then,” continued Sancho, “that in a village of Estremadura there was a goat-shepherd--that is to say, one who tended goats--which shepherd or goatherd, as my story goes, was called Lope Ruiz, and this Lope Ruiz was in love with a shepherdess called Torralva, which shepherdess called Torralva was the daughter of a rich grazier, and this rich grazier—”\n\n“If that is the way thou tellest thy tale, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “repeating twice all thou hast to say, thou wilt not have done these two days; go straight on with it, and tell it like a reasonable man, or else say nothing.”\n\n“Tales are always told in my country in the very way I am telling this,” answered Sancho, “and I cannot tell it in any other, nor is it right of your worship to ask me to make new customs.”\n\n“Tell it as thou wilt,” replied Don Quixote; “and as fate will have it that I cannot help listening to thee, go on.”\n\n“And so, lord of my soul,” continued Sancho, as I have said, this shepherd was in love with Torralva the shepherdess, who was a wild buxom lass with something of the look of a man about her, for she had little moustaches; I fancy I see her now.”\n\n“Then you knew her?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“I did not know her,” said Sancho, “but he who told me the story said it was so true and certain that when I told it to another I might safely declare and swear I had seen it all myself. And so in course of time, the devil, who never sleeps and puts everything in confusion, contrived that the love the shepherd bore the shepherdess turned into hatred and ill-will, and the reason, according to evil tongues, was some little jealousy she caused him that crossed the line and trespassed on forbidden ground; and so much did the shepherd hate her from that time forward that, in order to escape from her, he determined to quit the country and go where he should never set eyes on her again. Torralva, when she found herself spurned by Lope, was immediately smitten with love for him, though she had never loved him before.”\n\n“That is the natural way of women,” said Don Quixote, “to scorn the one that loves them, and love the one that hates them: go on, Sancho.”\n\n“It came to pass,” said Sancho, “that the shepherd carried out his intention, and driving his goats before him took his way across the plains of Estremadura to pass over into the Kingdom of Portugal. Torralva, who knew of it, went after him, and on foot and barefoot followed him at a distance, with a pilgrim’s staff in her hand and a scrip round her neck, in which she carried, it is said, a bit of looking-glass and a piece of a comb and some little pot or other of paint for her face; but let her carry what she did, I am not going to trouble myself to prove it; all I say is, that the shepherd, they say, came with his flock to cross over the river Guadiana, which was at that time swollen and almost overflowing its banks, and at the spot he came to there was neither ferry nor boat nor anyone to carry him or his flock to the other side, at which he was much vexed, for he perceived that Torralva was approaching and would give him great annoyance with her tears and entreaties; however, he went looking about so closely that he discovered a fisherman who had alongside of him a boat so small that it could only hold one person and one goat; but for all that he spoke to him and agreed with him to carry himself and his three hundred goats across. The fisherman got into the boat and carried one goat over; he came back and carried another over; he came back again, and again brought over another--let your worship keep count of the goats the fisherman is taking across, for if one escapes the memory there will be an end of the story, and it will be impossible to tell another word of it. To proceed, I must tell you the landing place on the other side was miry and slippery, and the fisherman lost a great deal of time in going and coming; still he returned for another goat, and another, and another.”\n\n“Take it for granted he brought them all across,” said Don Quixote, “and don’t keep going and coming in this way, or thou wilt not make an end of bringing them over this twelvemonth.”\n\n“How many have gone across so far?” said Sancho.\n\n“How the devil do I know?” replied Don Quixote.\n\n“There it is,” said Sancho, “what I told you, that you must keep a good count; well then, by God, there is an end of the story, for there is no going any farther.”\n\n“How can that be?” said Don Quixote; “is it so essential to the story to know to a nicety the goats that have crossed over, that if there be a mistake of one in the reckoning, thou canst not go on with it?”\n\n“No, señor, not a bit,” replied Sancho; “for when I asked your worship to tell me how many goats had crossed, and you answered you did not know, at that very instant all I had to say passed away out of my memory, and, faith, there was much virtue in it, and entertainment.”\n\n“So, then,” said Don Quixote, “the story has come to an end?”\n\n“As much as my mother has,” said Sancho.\n\n“In truth,” said Don Quixote, “thou hast told one of the rarest stories, tales, or histories, that anyone in the world could have imagined, and such a way of telling it and ending it was never seen nor will be in a lifetime; though I expected nothing else from thy excellent understanding. But I do not wonder, for perhaps those ceaseless strokes may have confused thy wits.”\n\n“All that may be,” replied Sancho, “but I know that as to my story, all that can be said is that it ends there where the mistake in the count of the passage of the goats begins.”\n\n“Let it end where it will, well and good,” said Don Quixote, “and let us see if Rocinante can go;” and again he spurred him, and again Rocinante made jumps and remained where he was, so well tied was he.\n\nJust then, whether it was the cold of the morning that was now approaching, or that he had eaten something laxative at supper, or that it was only natural (as is most likely), Sancho felt a desire to do what no one could do for him; but so great was the fear that had penetrated his heart, he dared not separate himself from his master by as much as the black of his nail; to escape doing what he wanted was, however, also impossible; so what he did for peace’s sake was to remove his right hand, which held the back of the saddle, and with it to untie gently and silently the running string which alone held up his breeches, so that on loosening it they at once fell down round his feet like fetters; he then raised his shirt as well as he could and bared his hind quarters, no slim ones. But, this accomplished, which he fancied was all he had to do to get out of this terrible strait and embarrassment, another still greater difficulty presented itself, for it seemed to him impossible to relieve himself without making some noise, and he ground his teeth and squeezed his shoulders together, holding his breath as much as he could; but in spite of his precautions he was unlucky enough after all to make a little noise, very different from that which was causing him so much fear.\n\nDon Quixote, hearing it, said, “What noise is that, Sancho?”\n\n“I don’t know, señor,” said he; “it must be something new, for adventures and misadventures never begin with a trifle.” Once more he tried his luck, and succeeded so well, that without any further noise or disturbance he found himself relieved of the burden that had given him so much discomfort. But as Don Quixote’s sense of smell was as acute as his hearing, and as Sancho was so closely linked with him that the fumes rose almost in a straight line, it could not be but that some should reach his nose, and as soon as they did he came to its relief by compressing it between his fingers, saying in a rather snuffing tone, “Sancho, it strikes me thou art in great fear.”\n\n“I am,” answered Sancho; “but how does your worship perceive it now more than ever?”\n\n“Because just now thou smellest stronger than ever, and not of ambergris,” answered Don Quixote.\n\n“Very likely,” said Sancho, “but that’s not my fault, but your worship’s, for leading me about at unseasonable hours and at such unwonted paces.”\n\n“Then go back three or four, my friend,” said Don Quixote, all the time with his fingers to his nose; “and for the future pay more attention to thy person and to what thou owest to mine; for it is my great familiarity with thee that has bred this contempt.”\n\n“I’ll bet,” replied Sancho, “that your worship thinks I have done something I ought not with my person.”\n\n“It makes it worse to stir it, friend Sancho,” returned Don Quixote.\n\nWith this and other talk of the same sort master and man passed the night, till Sancho, perceiving that daybreak was coming on apace, very cautiously untied Rocinante and tied up his breeches. As soon as Rocinante found himself free, though by nature he was not at all mettlesome, he seemed to feel lively and began pawing--for as to capering, begging his pardon, he knew not what it meant. Don Quixote, then, observing that Rocinante could move, took it as a good sign and a signal that he should attempt the dread adventure. By this time day had fully broken and everything showed distinctly, and Don Quixote saw that he was among some tall trees, chestnuts, which cast a very deep shade; he perceived likewise that the sound of the strokes did not cease, but could not discover what caused it, and so without any further delay he let Rocinante feel the spur, and once more taking leave of Sancho, he told him to wait for him there three days at most, as he had said before, and if he should not have returned by that time, he might feel sure it had been God’s will that he should end his days in that perilous adventure. He again repeated the message and commission with which he was to go on his behalf to his lady Dulcinea, and said he was not to be uneasy as to the payment of his services, for before leaving home he had made his will, in which he would find himself fully recompensed in the matter of wages in due proportion to the time he had served; but if God delivered him safe, sound, and unhurt out of that danger, he might look upon the promised island as much more than certain. Sancho began to weep afresh on again hearing the affecting words of his good master, and resolved to stay with him until the final issue and end of the business. From these tears and this honourable resolve of Sancho Panza’s the author of this history infers that he must have been of good birth and at least an old Christian; and the feeling he displayed touched his but not so much as to make him show any weakness; on the contrary, hiding what he felt as well as he could, he began to move towards that quarter whence the sound of the water and of the strokes seemed to come.\n\nSancho followed him on foot, leading by the halter, as his custom was, his ass, his constant comrade in prosperity or adversity; and advancing some distance through the shady chestnut trees they came upon a little meadow at the foot of some high rocks, down which a mighty rush of water flung itself. At the foot of the rocks were some rudely constructed houses looking more like ruins than houses, from among which came, they perceived, the din and clatter of blows, which still continued without intermission. Rocinante took fright at the noise of the water and of the blows, but quieting him Don Quixote advanced step by step towards the houses, commending himself with all his heart to his lady, imploring her support in that dread pass and enterprise, and on the way commending himself to God, too, not to forget him. Sancho who never quitted his side, stretched his neck as far as he could and peered between the legs of Rocinante to see if he could now discover what it was that caused him such fear and apprehension. They went it might be a hundred paces farther, when on turning a corner the true cause, beyond the possibility of any mistake, of that dread-sounding and to them awe-inspiring noise that had kept them all the night in such fear and perplexity, appeared plain and obvious; and it was (if, reader, thou art not disgusted and disappointed) six fulling hammers which by their alternate strokes made all the din.\n\nWhen Don Quixote perceived what it was, he was struck dumb and rigid from head to foot. Sancho glanced at him and saw him with his head bent down upon his breast in manifest mortification; and Don Quixote glanced at Sancho and saw him with his cheeks puffed out and his mouth full of laughter, and evidently ready to explode with it, and in spite of his vexation he could not help laughing at the sight of him; and when Sancho saw his master begin he let go so heartily that he had to hold his sides with both hands to keep himself from bursting with laughter. Four times he stopped, and as many times did his laughter break out afresh with the same violence as at first, whereat Don Quixote grew furious, above all when he heard him say mockingly, “Thou must know, friend Sancho, that of Heaven’s will I was born in this our iron age to revive in it the golden or age of gold; I am he for whom are reserved perils, mighty achievements, valiant deeds;” and here he went on repeating the words that Don Quixote uttered the first time they heard the awful strokes.\n\nDon Quixote, then, seeing that Sancho was turning him into ridicule, was so mortified and vexed that he lifted up his pike and smote him two such blows that if, instead of catching them on his shoulders, he had caught them on his head there would have been no wages to pay, unless indeed to his heirs. Sancho seeing that he was getting an awkward return in earnest for his jest, and fearing his master might carry it still further, said to him very humbly, “Calm yourself, sir, for by God I am only joking.”\n\n“Well, then, if you are joking I am not,” replied Don Quixote. “Look here, my lively gentleman, if these, instead of being fulling hammers, had been some perilous adventure, have I not, think you, shown the courage required for the attempt and achievement? Am I, perchance, being, as I am, a gentleman, bound to know and distinguish sounds and tell whether they come from fulling mills or not; and that, when perhaps, as is the case, I have never in my life seen any as you have, low boor as you are, that have been born and bred among them? But turn me these six hammers into six giants, and bring them to beard me, one by one or all together, and if I do not knock them head over heels, then make what mockery you like of me.”\n\n“No more of that, señor,” returned Sancho; “I own I went a little too far with the joke. But tell me, your worship, now that peace is made between us (and may God bring you out of all the adventures that may befall you as safe and sound as he has brought you out of this one), was it not a thing to laugh at, and is it not a good story, the great fear we were in?--at least that I was in; for as to your worship I see now that you neither know nor understand what either fear or dismay is.”\n\n“I do not deny,” said Don Quixote, “that what happened to us may be worth laughing at, but it is not worth making a story about, for it is not everyone that is shrewd enough to hit the right point of a thing.”\n\n“At any rate,” said Sancho, “your worship knew how to hit the right point with your pike, aiming at my head and hitting me on the shoulders, thanks be to God and my own smartness in dodging it. But let that pass; all will come out in the scouring; for I have heard say ‘he loves thee well that makes thee weep;’ and moreover that it is the way with great lords after any hard words they give a servant to give him a pair of breeches; though I do not know what they give after blows, unless it be that knights-errant after blows give islands, or kingdoms on the mainland.”\n\n“It may be on the dice,” said Don Quixote, “that all thou sayest will come true; overlook the past, for thou art shrewd enough to know that our first movements are not in our own control; and one thing for the future bear in mind, that thou curb and restrain thy loquacity in my company; for in all the books of chivalry that I have read, and they are innumerable, I never met with a squire who talked so much to his lord as thou dost to thine; and in fact I feel it to be a great fault of thine and of mine: of thine, that thou hast so little respect for me; of mine, that I do not make myself more respected. There was Gandalin, the squire of Amadis of Gaul, that was Count of the Insula Firme, and we read of him that he always addressed his lord with his cap in his hand, his head bowed down and his body bent double, more turquesco. And then, what shall we say of Gasabal, the squire of Galaor, who was so silent that in order to indicate to us the greatness of his marvellous taciturnity his name is only once mentioned in the whole of that history, as long as it is truthful? From all I have said thou wilt gather, Sancho, that there must be a difference between master and man, between lord and lackey, between knight and squire: so that from this day forward in our intercourse we must observe more respect and take less liberties, for in whatever way I may be provoked with you it will be bad for the pitcher. The favours and benefits that I have promised you will come in due time, and if they do not your wages at least will not be lost, as I have already told you.”\n\n“All that your worship says is very well,” said Sancho, “but I should like to know (in case the time of favours should not come, and it might be necessary to fall back upon wages) how much did the squire of a knight-errant get in those days, and did they agree by the month, or by the day like bricklayers?”\n\n“I do not believe,” replied Don Quixote, “that such squires were ever on wages, but were dependent on favour; and if I have now mentioned thine in the sealed will I have left at home, it was with a view to what may happen; for as yet I know not how chivalry will turn out in these wretched times of ours, and I do not wish my soul to suffer for trifles in the other world; for I would have thee know, Sancho, that in this there is no condition more hazardous than that of adventurers.”\n\n“That is true,” said Sancho, “since the mere noise of the hammers of a fulling mill can disturb and disquiet the heart of such a valiant errant adventurer as your worship; but you may be sure I will not open my lips henceforward to make light of anything of your worship’s, but only to honour you as my master and natural lord.”\n\n“By so doing,” replied Don Quixote, “shalt thou live long on the face of the earth; for next to parents, masters are to be respected as though they were parents.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXI.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF THE EXALTED ADVENTURE AND RICH PRIZE OF MAMBRINO’S HELMET, TOGETHER WITH OTHER THINGS THAT HAPPENED TO OUR INVINCIBLE KNIGHT\n\nIt now began to rain a little, and Sancho was for going into the fulling mills, but Don Quixote had taken such an abhorrence to them on account of the late joke that he would not enter them on any account; so turning aside to right they came upon another road, different from that which they had taken the night before. Shortly afterwards Don Quixote perceived a man on horseback who wore on his head something that shone like gold, and the moment he saw him he turned to Sancho and said:\n\n“I think, Sancho, there is no proverb that is not true, all being maxims drawn from experience itself, the mother of all the sciences, especially that one that says, ‘Where one door shuts, another opens.’ I say so because if last night fortune shut the door of the adventure we were looking for against us, cheating us with the fulling mills, it now opens wide another one for another better and more certain adventure, and if I do not contrive to enter it, it will be my own fault, and I cannot lay it to my ignorance of fulling mills, or the darkness of the night. I say this because, if I mistake not, there comes towards us one who wears on his head the helmet of Mambrino, concerning which I took the oath thou rememberest.”\n\n“Mind what you say, your worship, and still more what you do,” said Sancho, “for I don’t want any more fulling mills to finish off fulling and knocking our senses out.”\n\n“The devil take thee, man,” said Don Quixote; “what has a helmet to do with fulling mills?”\n\n“I don’t know,” replied Sancho, “but, faith, if I might speak as I used, perhaps I could give such reasons that your worship would see you were mistaken in what you say.”\n\n“How can I be mistaken in what I say, unbelieving traitor?” returned Don Quixote; “tell me, seest thou not yonder knight coming towards us on a dappled grey steed, who has upon his head a helmet of gold?”\n\n“What I see and make out,” answered Sancho, “is only a man on a grey ass like my own, who has something that shines on his head.”\n\n“Well, that is the helmet of Mambrino,” said Don Quixote; “stand to one side and leave me alone with him; thou shalt see how, without saying a word, to save time, I shall bring this adventure to an issue and possess myself of the helmet I have so longed for.”\n\n“I will take care to stand aside,” said Sancho; “but God grant, I say once more, that it may be marjoram and not fulling mills.”\n\n“I have told thee, brother, on no account to mention those fulling mills to me again,” said Don Quixote, “or I vow--and I say no more-I’ll full the soul out of you.”\n\nSancho held his peace in dread lest his master should carry out the vow he had hurled like a bowl at him.\n\nThe fact of the matter as regards the helmet, steed, and knight that Don Quixote saw, was this. In that neighbourhood there were two villages, one of them so small that it had neither apothecary’s shop nor barber, which the other that was close to it had, so the barber of the larger served the smaller, and in it there was a sick man who required to be bled and another man who wanted to be shaved, and on this errand the barber was going, carrying with him a brass basin; but as luck would have it, as he was on the way it began to rain, and not to spoil his hat, which probably was a new one, he put the basin on his head, and being clean it glittered at half a league’s distance. He rode upon a grey ass, as Sancho said, and this was what made it seem to Don Quixote to be a dapple-grey steed and a knight and a golden helmet; for everything he saw he made to fall in with his crazy chivalry and ill-errant notions; and when he saw the poor knight draw near, without entering into any parley with him, at Rocinante’s top speed he bore down upon him with the pike pointed low, fully determined to run him through and through, and as he reached him, without checking the fury of his charge, he cried to him:\n\n“Defend thyself, miserable being, or yield me of thine own accord that which is so reasonably my due.”\n\nThe barber, who without any expectation or apprehension of it saw this apparition coming down upon him, had no other way of saving himself from the stroke of the lance but to let himself fall off his ass; and no sooner had he touched the ground than he sprang up more nimbly than a deer and sped away across the plain faster than the wind.\n\nHe left the basin on the ground, with which Don Quixote contented himself, saying that the pagan had shown his discretion and imitated the beaver, which finding itself pressed by the hunters bites and cuts off with its teeth that for which, by its natural instinct, it knows it is pursued.\n\nHe told Sancho to pick up the helmet, and he taking it in his hands said:\n\n“By God the basin is a good one, and worth a real of eight if it is worth a maravedis,” and handed it to his master, who immediately put it on his head, turning it round, now this way, now that, in search of fitment, and not finding it he said, “Clearly the pagan to whose measure this famous head-piece was first forged must have had a very large head; but the worst of it is half of it is wanting.”\n\nWhen Sancho heard him call the basin a headpiece he was unable to restrain his laughter, but remembering his master’s wrath he checked himself in the midst of it.\n\n“What art thou laughing at, Sancho?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“I am laughing,” said he, “to think of the great head the pagan must have had who owned this helmet, for it looks exactly like a regular barber’s basin.”\n\n“Dost thou know what I suspect, Sancho?” said Don Quixote; “that this wonderful piece of this enchanted helmet must by some strange accident have come into the hands of some one who was unable to recognise or realise its value, and who, not knowing what he did, and seeing it to be of the purest gold, must have melted down one half for the sake of what it might be worth, and of the other made this which is like a barber’s basin as thou sayest; but be it as it may, to me who recognise it, its transformation makes no difference, for I will set it to rights at the first village where there is a blacksmith, and in such style that that helmet the god of smithies forged for the god of battles shall not surpass it or even come up to it; and in the meantime I will wear it as well as I can, for something is better than nothing; all the more as it will be quite enough to protect me from any chance blow of a stone.”\n\n“That is,” said Sancho, “if it is not shot with a sling as they were in the battle of the two armies, when they signed the cross on your worship’s grinders and smashed the flask with that blessed draught that made me vomit my bowels up.”\n\n“It does not grieve me much to have lost it,” said Don Quixote, “for thou knowest, Sancho, that I have the receipt in my memory.”\n\n“So have I,” answered Sancho, “but if ever I make it, or try it again as long as I live, may this be my last hour; moreover, I have no intention of putting myself in the way of wanting it, for I mean, with all my five senses, to keep myself from being wounded or from wounding anyone: as to being blanketed again I say nothing, for it is hard to prevent mishaps of that sort, and if they come there is nothing for it but to squeeze our shoulders together, hold our breath, shut our eyes, and let ourselves go where luck and the blanket may send us.”\n\n“Thou art a bad Christian, Sancho,” said Don Quixote on hearing this, “for once an injury has been done thee thou never forgettest it: but know that it is the part of noble and generous hearts not to attach importance to trifles. What lame leg hast thou got by it, what broken rib, what cracked head, that thou canst not forget that jest? For jest and sport it was, properly regarded, and had I not seen it in that light I would have returned and done more mischief in revenging thee than the Greeks did for the rape of Helen, who, if she were alive now, or if my Dulcinea had lived then, might depend upon it she would not be so famous for her beauty as she is;” and here he heaved a sigh and sent it aloft; and said Sancho, “Let it pass for a jest as it cannot be revenged in earnest, but I know what sort of jest and earnest it was, and I know it will never be rubbed out of my memory any more than off my shoulders. But putting that aside, will your worship tell me what are we to do with this dapple-grey steed that looks like a grey ass, which that Martino that your worship overthrew has left deserted here? for, from the way he took to his heels and bolted, he is not likely ever to come back for it; and by my beard but the grey is a good one.”\n\n“I have never been in the habit,” said Don Quixote, “of taking spoil of those whom I vanquish, nor is it the practice of chivalry to take away their horses and leave them to go on foot, unless indeed it be that the victor have lost his own in the combat, in which case it is lawful to take that of the vanquished as a thing won in lawful war; therefore, Sancho, leave this horse, or ass, or whatever thou wilt have it to be; for when its owner sees us gone hence he will come back for it.”\n\n“God knows I should like to take it,” returned Sancho, “or at least to change it for my own, which does not seem to me as good a one: verily the laws of chivalry are strict, since they cannot be stretched to let one ass be changed for another; I should like to know if I might at least change trappings.”\n\n“On that head I am not quite certain,” answered Don Quixote, “and the matter being doubtful, pending better information, I say thou mayest change them, if so be thou hast urgent need of them.”\n\n“So urgent is it,” answered Sancho, “that if they were for my own person I could not want them more;” and forthwith, fortified by this licence, he effected the mutatio capparum, rigging out his beast to the ninety-nines and making quite another thing of it. This done, they broke their fast on the remains of the spoils of war plundered from the sumpter mule, and drank of the brook that flowed from the fulling mills, without casting a look in that direction, in such loathing did they hold them for the alarm they had caused them; and, all anger and gloom removed, they mounted and, without taking any fixed road (not to fix upon any being the proper thing for true knights-errant), they set out, guided by Rocinante’s will, which carried along with it that of his master, not to say that of the ass, which always followed him wherever he led, lovingly and sociably; nevertheless they returned to the high road, and pursued it at a venture without any other aim.\n\nAs they went along, then, in this way Sancho said to his master, “Señor, would your worship give me leave to speak a little to you? For since you laid that hard injunction of silence on me several things have gone to rot in my stomach, and I have now just one on the tip of my tongue that I don’t want to be spoiled.”\n\n“Say, on, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “and be brief in thy discourse, for there is no pleasure in one that is long.”\n\n“Well then, señor,” returned Sancho, “I say that for some days past I have been considering how little is got or gained by going in search of these adventures that your worship seeks in these wilds and cross-roads, where, even if the most perilous are victoriously achieved, there is no one to see or know of them, and so they must be left untold for ever, to the loss of your worship’s object and the credit they deserve; therefore it seems to me it would be better (saving your worship’s better judgment) if we were to go and serve some emperor or other great prince who may have some war on hand, in whose service your worship may prove the worth of your person, your great might, and greater understanding, on perceiving which the lord in whose service we may be will perforce have to reward us, each according to his merits; and there you will not be at a loss for some one to set down your achievements in writing so as to preserve their memory for ever. Of my own I say nothing, as they will not go beyond squirely limits, though I make bold to say that, if it be the practice in chivalry to write the achievements of squires, I think mine must not be left out.”\n\n“Thou speakest not amiss, Sancho,” answered Don Quixote, “but before that point is reached it is requisite to roam the world, as it were on probation, seeking adventures, in order that, by achieving some, name and fame may be acquired, such that when he betakes himself to the court of some great monarch the knight may be already known by his deeds, and that the boys, the instant they see him enter the gate of the city, may all follow him and surround him, crying, ‘This is the Knight of the Sun’-or the Serpent, or any other title under which he may have achieved great deeds. ‘This,’ they will say, ‘is he who vanquished in single combat the gigantic Brocabruno of mighty strength; he who delivered the great Mameluke of Persia out of the long enchantment under which he had been for almost nine hundred years.’ So from one to another they will go proclaiming his achievements; and presently at the tumult of the boys and the others the king of that kingdom will appear at the windows of his royal palace, and as soon as he beholds the knight, recognising him by his arms and the device on his shield, he will as a matter of course say, ‘What ho! Forth all ye, the knights of my court, to receive the flower of chivalry who cometh hither!’ At which command all will issue forth, and he himself, advancing half-way down the stairs, will embrace him closely, and salute him, kissing him on the cheek, and will then lead him to the queen’s chamber, where the knight will find her with the princess her daughter, who will be one of the most beautiful and accomplished damsels that could with the utmost pains be discovered anywhere in the known world. Straightway it will come to pass that she will fix her eyes upon the knight and he his upon her, and each will seem to the other something more divine than human, and, without knowing how or why they will be taken and entangled in the inextricable toils of love, and sorely distressed in their hearts not to see any way of making their pains and sufferings known by speech. Thence they will lead him, no doubt, to some richly adorned chamber of the palace, where, having removed his armour, they will bring him a rich mantle of scarlet wherewith to robe himself, and if he looked noble in his armour he will look still more so in a doublet. When night comes he will sup with the king, queen, and princess; and all the time he will never take his eyes off her, stealing stealthy glances, unnoticed by those present, and she will do the same, and with equal cautiousness, being, as I have said, a damsel of great discretion. The tables being removed, suddenly through the door of the hall there will enter a hideous and diminutive dwarf followed by a fair dame, between two giants, who comes with a certain adventure, the work of an ancient sage; and he who shall achieve it shall be deemed the best knight in the world.\n\n“The king will then command all those present to essay it, and none will bring it to an end and conclusion save the stranger knight, to the great enhancement of his fame, whereat the princess will be overjoyed and will esteem herself happy and fortunate in having fixed and placed her thoughts so high. And the best of it is that this king, or prince, or whatever he is, is engaged in a very bitter war with another as powerful as himself, and the stranger knight, after having been some days at his court, requests leave from him to go and serve him in the said war. The king will grant it very readily, and the knight will courteously kiss his hands for the favour done to him; and that night he will take leave of his lady the princess at the grating of the chamber where she sleeps, which looks upon a garden, and at which he has already many times conversed with her, the go-between and confidante in the matter being a damsel much trusted by the princess. He will sigh, she will swoon, the damsel will fetch water, much distressed because morning approaches, and for the honour of her lady he would not that they were discovered; at last the princess will come to herself and will present her white hands through the grating to the knight, who will kiss them a thousand and a thousand times, bathing them with his tears. It will be arranged between them how they are to inform each other of their good or evil fortunes, and the princess will entreat him to make his absence as short as possible, which he will promise to do with many oaths; once more he kisses her hands, and takes his leave in such grief that he is well-nigh ready to die. He betakes him thence to his chamber, flings himself on his bed, cannot sleep for sorrow at parting, rises early in the morning, goes to take leave of the king, queen, and princess, and, as he takes his leave of the pair, it is told him that the princess is indisposed and cannot receive a visit; the knight thinks it is from grief at his departure, his heart is pierced, and he is hardly able to keep from showing his pain. The confidante is present, observes all, goes to tell her mistress, who listens with tears and says that one of her greatest distresses is not knowing who this knight is, and whether he is of kingly lineage or not; the damsel assures her that so much courtesy, gentleness, and gallantry of bearing as her knight possesses could not exist in any save one who was royal and illustrious; her anxiety is thus relieved, and she strives to be of good cheer lest she should excite suspicion in her parents, and at the end of two days she appears in public. Meanwhile the knight has taken his departure; he fights in the war, conquers the king’s enemy, wins many cities, triumphs in many battles, returns to the court, sees his lady where he was wont to see her, and it is agreed that he shall demand her in marriage of her parents as the reward of his services; the king is unwilling to give her, as he knows not who he is, but nevertheless, whether carried off or in whatever other way it may be, the princess comes to be his bride, and her father comes to regard it as very good fortune; for it so happens that this knight is proved to be the son of a valiant king of some kingdom, I know not what, for I fancy it is not likely to be on the map. The father dies, the princess inherits, and in two words the knight becomes king. And here comes in at once the bestowal of rewards upon his squire and all who have aided him in rising to so exalted a rank. He marries his squire to a damsel of the princess’s, who will be, no doubt, the one who was confidante in their amour, and is daughter of a very great duke.”\n\n“That’s what I want, and no mistake about it!” said Sancho. “That’s what I’m waiting for; for all this, word for word, is in store for your worship under the title of the Knight of the Rueful Countenance.”\n\n“Thou needst not doubt it, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote, “for in the same manner, and by the same steps as I have described here, knights-errant rise and have risen to be kings and emperors; all we want now is to find out what king, Christian or pagan, is at war and has a beautiful daughter; but there will be time enough to think of that, for, as I have told thee, fame must be won in other quarters before repairing to the court. There is another thing, too, that is wanting; for supposing we find a king who is at war and has a beautiful daughter, and that I have won incredible fame throughout the universe, I know not how it can be made out that I am of royal lineage, or even second cousin to an emperor; for the king will not be willing to give me his daughter in marriage unless he is first thoroughly satisfied on this point, however much my famous deeds may deserve it; so that by this deficiency I fear I shall lose what my arm has fairly earned. True it is I am a gentleman of known house, of estate and property, and entitled to the five hundred sueldos mulct; and it may be that the sage who shall write my history will so clear up my ancestry and pedigree that I may find myself fifth or sixth in descent from a king; for I would have thee know, Sancho, that there are two kinds of lineages in the world; some there be tracing and deriving their descent from kings and princes, whom time has reduced little by little until they end in a point like a pyramid upside down; and others who spring from the common herd and go on rising step by step until they come to be great lords; so that the difference is that the one were what they no longer are, and the others are what they formerly were not. And I may be of such that after investigation my origin may prove great and famous, with which the king, my father-in-law that is to be, ought to be satisfied; and should he not be, the princess will so love me that even though she well knew me to be the son of a water-carrier, she will take me for her lord and husband in spite of her father; if not, then it comes to seizing her and carrying her off where I please; for time or death will put an end to the wrath of her parents.”\n\n“It comes to this, too,” said Sancho, “what some naughty people say, ‘Never ask as a favour what thou canst take by force;’ though it would fit better to say, ‘A clear escape is better than good men’s prayers.’ I say so because if my lord the king, your worship’s father-in-law, will not condescend to give you my lady the princess, there is nothing for it but, as your worship says, to seize her and transport her. But the mischief is that until peace is made and you come into the peaceful enjoyment of your kingdom, the poor squire is famishing as far as rewards go, unless it be that the confidante damsel that is to be his wife comes with the princess, and that with her he tides over his bad luck until Heaven otherwise orders things; for his master, I suppose, may as well give her to him at once for a lawful wife.”\n\n“Nobody can object to that,” said Don Quixote.\n\n“Then since that may be,” said Sancho, “there is nothing for it but to commend ourselves to God, and let fortune take what course it will.”\n\n“God guide it according to my wishes and thy wants,” said Don Quixote, “and mean be he who thinks himself mean.”\n\n“In God’s name let him be so,” said Sancho: “I am an old Christian, and to fit me for a count that’s enough.”\n\n“And more than enough for thee,” said Don Quixote; “and even wert thou not, it would make no difference, because I being the king can easily give thee nobility without purchase or service rendered by thee, for when I make thee a count, then thou art at once a gentleman; and they may say what they will, but by my faith they will have to call thee ‘your lordship,’ whether they like it or not.”\n\n“Not a doubt of it; and I’ll know how to support the tittle,” said Sancho.\n\n“Title thou shouldst say, not tittle,” said his master.\n\n“So be it,” answered Sancho. “I say I will know how to behave, for once in my life I was beadle of a brotherhood, and the beadle’s gown sat so well on me that all said I looked as if I was to be steward of the same brotherhood. What will it be, then, when I put a duke’s robe on my back, or dress myself in gold and pearls like a count? I believe they’ll come a hundred leagues to see me.”\n\n“Thou wilt look well,” said Don Quixote, “but thou must shave thy beard often, for thou hast it so thick and rough and unkempt, that if thou dost not shave it every second day at least, they will see what thou art at the distance of a musket shot.”\n\n“What more will it be,” said Sancho, “than having a barber, and keeping him at wages in the house? and even if it be necessary, I will make him go behind me like a nobleman’s equerry.”\n\n“Why, how dost thou know that noblemen have equerries behind them?” asked Don Quixote.\n\n“I will tell you,” answered Sancho. “Years ago I was for a month at the capital and there I saw taking the air a very small gentleman who they said was a very great man, and a man following him on horseback in every turn he took, just as if he was his tail. I asked why this man did not join the other man, instead of always going behind him; they answered me that he was his equerry, and that it was the custom with nobles to have such persons behind them, and ever since then I know it, for I have never forgotten it.”\n\n“Thou art right,” said Don Quixote, “and in the same way thou mayest carry thy barber with thee, for customs did not come into use all together, nor were they all invented at once, and thou mayest be the first count to have a barber to follow him; and, indeed, shaving one’s beard is a greater trust than saddling one’s horse.”\n\n“Let the barber business be my look-out,” said Sancho; “and your worship’s be it to strive to become a king, and make me a count.”\n\n“So it shall be,” answered Don Quixote, and raising his eyes he saw what will be told in the following chapter.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII.\n\nOF THE FREEDOM DON QUIXOTE CONFERRED ON SEVERAL UNFORTUNATES WHO AGAINST THEIR WILL WERE BEING CARRIED WHERE THEY HAD NO WISH TO GO\n\nCide Hamete Benengeli, the Arab and Manchegan author, relates in this most grave, high-sounding, minute, delightful, and original history that after the discussion between the famous Don Quixote of La Mancha and his squire Sancho Panza which is set down at the end of chapter twenty-one, Don Quixote raised his eyes and saw coming along the road he was following some dozen men on foot strung together by the neck, like beads, on a great iron chain, and all with manacles on their hands. With them there came also two men on horseback and two on foot; those on horseback with wheel-lock muskets, those on foot with javelins and swords, and as soon as Sancho saw them he said:\n\n“That is a chain of galley slaves, on the way to the galleys by force of the king’s orders.”\n\n“How by force?” asked Don Quixote; “is it possible that the king uses force against anyone?”\n\n“I do not say that,” answered Sancho, “but that these are people condemned for their crimes to serve by force in the king’s galleys.”\n\n“In fact,” replied Don Quixote, “however it may be, these people are going where they are taking them by force, and not of their own will.”\n\n“Just so,” said Sancho.\n\n“Then if so,” said Don Quixote, “here is a case for the exercise of my office, to put down force and to succour and help the wretched.”\n\n“Recollect, your worship,” said Sancho, “Justice, which is the king himself, is not using force or doing wrong to such persons, but punishing them for their crimes.”\n\nThe chain of galley slaves had by this time come up, and Don Quixote in very courteous language asked those who were in custody of it to be good enough to tell him the reason or reasons for which they were conducting these people in this manner. One of the guards on horseback answered that they were galley slaves belonging to his majesty, that they were going to the galleys, and that was all that was to be said and all he had any business to know.\n\n“Nevertheless,” replied Don Quixote, “I should like to know from each of them separately the reason of his misfortune;” to this he added more to the same effect to induce them to tell him what he wanted so civilly that the other mounted guard said to him:\n\n“Though we have here the register and certificate of the sentence of every one of these wretches, this is no time to take them out or read them; come and ask themselves; they can tell if they choose, and they will, for these fellows take a pleasure in doing and talking about rascalities.”\n\nWith this permission, which Don Quixote would have taken even had they not granted it, he approached the chain and asked the first for what offences he was now in such a sorry case.\n\nHe made answer that it was for being a lover.\n\n“For that only?” replied Don Quixote; “why, if for being lovers they send people to the galleys I might have been rowing in them long ago.”\n\n“The love is not the sort your worship is thinking of,” said the galley slave; “mine was that I loved a washerwoman’s basket of clean linen so well, and held it so close in my embrace, that if the arm of the law had not forced it from me, I should never have let it go of my own will to this moment; I was caught in the act, there was no occasion for torture, the case was settled, they treated me to a hundred lashes on the back, and three years of gurapas besides, and that was the end of it.”\n\n“What are gurapas?” asked Don Quixote.\n\n“Gurapas are galleys,” answered the galley slave, who was a young man of about four-and-twenty, and said he was a native of Piedrahita.\n\nDon Quixote asked the same question of the second, who made no reply, so downcast and melancholy was he; but the first answered for him, and said, “He, sir, goes as a canary, I mean as a musician and a singer.”\n\n“What!” said Don Quixote, “for being musicians and singers are people sent to the galleys too?”\n\n“Yes, sir,” answered the galley slave, “for there is nothing worse than singing under suffering.”\n\n“On the contrary, I have heard say,” said Don Quixote, “that he who sings scares away his woes.”\n\n“Here it is the reverse,” said the galley slave; “for he who sings once weeps all his life.”\n\n“I do not understand it,” said Don Quixote; but one of the guards said to him, “Sir, to sing under suffering means with the non sancta fraternity to confess under torture; they put this sinner to the torture and he confessed his crime, which was being a cuatrero, that is a cattle-stealer, and on his confession they sentenced him to six years in the galleys, besides two hundred lashes that he has already had on the back; and he is always dejected and downcast because the other thieves that were left behind and that march here ill-treat, and snub, and jeer, and despise him for confessing and not having spirit enough to say nay; for, say they, ‘nay’ has no more letters in it than ‘yea,’ and a culprit is well off when life or death with him depends on his own tongue and not on that of witnesses or evidence; and to my thinking they are not very far out.”\n\n“And I think so too,” answered Don Quixote; then passing on to the third he asked him what he had asked the others, and the man answered very readily and unconcernedly, “I am going for five years to their ladyships the gurapas for the want of ten ducats.”\n\n“I will give twenty with pleasure to get you out of that trouble,” said Don Quixote.\n\n“That,” said the galley slave, “is like a man having money at sea when he is dying of hunger and has no way of buying what he wants; I say so because if at the right time I had had those twenty ducats that your worship now offers me, I would have greased the notary’s pen and freshened up the attorney’s wit with them, so that to-day I should be in the middle of the plaza of the Zocodover at Toledo, and not on this road coupled like a greyhound. But God is great; patience--there, that’s enough of it.”\n\nDon Quixote passed on to the fourth, a man of venerable aspect with a white beard falling below his breast, who on hearing himself asked the reason of his being there began to weep without answering a word, but the fifth acted as his tongue and said, “This worthy man is going to the galleys for four years, after having gone the rounds in ceremony and on horseback.”\n\n“That means,” said Sancho Panza, “as I take it, to have been exposed to shame in public.”\n\n“Just so,” replied the galley slave, “and the offence for which they gave him that punishment was having been an ear-broker, nay body-broker; I mean, in short, that this gentleman goes as a pimp, and for having besides a certain touch of the sorcerer about him.”\n\n“If that touch had not been thrown in,” said Don Quixote, “he would not deserve, for mere pimping, to row in the galleys, but rather to command and be admiral of them; for the office of pimp is no ordinary one, being the office of persons of discretion, one very necessary in a well-ordered state, and only to be exercised by persons of good birth; nay, there ought to be an inspector and overseer of them, as in other offices, and recognised number, as with the brokers on change; in this way many of the evils would be avoided which are caused by this office and calling being in the hands of stupid and ignorant people, such as women more or less silly, and pages and jesters of little standing and experience, who on the most urgent occasions, and when ingenuity of contrivance is needed, let the crumbs freeze on the way to their mouths, and know not which is their right hand. I should like to go farther, and give reasons to show that it is advisable to choose those who are to hold so necessary an office in the state, but this is not the fit place for it; some day I will expound the matter to some one able to see to and rectify it; all I say now is, that the additional fact of his being a sorcerer has removed the sorrow it gave me to see these white hairs and this venerable countenance in so painful a position on account of his being a pimp; though I know well there are no sorceries in the world that can move or compel the will as some simple folk fancy, for our will is free, nor is there herb or charm that can force it. All that certain silly women and quacks do is to turn men mad with potions and poisons, pretending that they have power to cause love, for, as I say, it is an impossibility to compel the will.”\n\n“It is true,” said the good old man, “and indeed, sir, as far as the charge of sorcery goes I was not guilty; as to that of being a pimp I cannot deny it; but I never thought I was doing any harm by it, for my only object was that all the world should enjoy itself and live in peace and quiet, without quarrels or troubles; but my good intentions were unavailing to save me from going where I never expect to come back from, with this weight of years upon me and a urinary ailment that never gives me a moment’s ease;” and again he fell to weeping as before, and such compassion did Sancho feel for him that he took out a real of four from his bosom and gave it to him in alms.\n\nDon Quixote went on and asked another what his crime was, and the man answered with no less but rather much more sprightliness than the last one.\n\n“I am here because I carried the joke too far with a couple of cousins of mine, and with a couple of other cousins who were none of mine; in short, I carried the joke so far with them all that it ended in such a complicated increase of kindred that no accountant could make it clear: it was all proved against me, I got no favour, I had no money, I was near having my neck stretched, they sentenced me to the galleys for six years, I accepted my fate, it is the punishment of my fault; I am a young man; let life only last, and with that all will come right. If you, sir, have anything wherewith to help the poor, God will repay it to you in heaven, and we on earth will take care in our petitions to him to pray for the life and health of your worship, that they may be as long and as good as your amiable appearance deserves.”\n\nThis one was in the dress of a student, and one of the guards said he was a great talker and a very elegant Latin scholar.\n\nBehind all these there came a man of thirty, a very personable fellow, except that when he looked, his eyes turned in a little one towards the other. He was bound differently from the rest, for he had to his leg a chain so long that it was wound all round his body, and two rings on his neck, one attached to the chain, the other to what they call a “keep-friend” or “friend’s foot,” from which hung two irons reaching to his waist with two manacles fixed to them in which his hands were secured by a big padlock, so that he could neither raise his hands to his mouth nor lower his head to his hands. Don Quixote asked why this man carried so many more chains than the others. The guard replied that it was because he alone had committed more crimes than all the rest put together, and was so daring and such a villain, that though they marched him in that fashion they did not feel sure of him, but were in dread of his making his escape.\n\n“What crimes can he have committed,” said Don Quixote, “if they have not deserved a heavier punishment than being sent to the galleys?”\n\n“He goes for ten years,” replied the guard, “which is the same thing as civil death, and all that need be said is that this good fellow is the famous Gines de Pasamonte, otherwise called Ginesillo de Parapilla.”\n\n“Gently, señor commissary,” said the galley slave at this, “let us have no fixing of names or surnames; my name is Gines, not Ginesillo, and my family name is Pasamonte, not Parapilla as you say; let each one mind his own business, and he will be doing enough.”\n\n“Speak with less impertinence, master thief of extra measure,” replied the commissary, “if you don’t want me to make you hold your tongue in spite of your teeth.”\n\n“It is easy to see,” returned the galley slave, “that man goes as God pleases, but some one shall know some day whether I am called Ginesillo de Parapilla or not.”\n\n“Don’t they call you so, you liar?” said the guard.\n\n“They do,” returned Gines, “but I will make them give over calling me so, or I will be shaved, where, I only say behind my teeth. If you, sir, have anything to give us, give it to us at once, and God speed you, for you are becoming tiresome with all this inquisitiveness about the lives of others; if you want to know about mine, let me tell you I am Gines de Pasamonte, whose life is written by these fingers.”\n\n“He says true,” said the commissary, “for he has himself written his story as grand as you please, and has left the book in the prison in pawn for two hundred reals.”\n\n“And I mean to take it out of pawn,” said Gines, “though it were in for two hundred ducats.”\n\n“Is it so good?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“So good is it,” replied Gines, “that a fig for ‘Lazarillo de Tormes,’ and all of that kind that have been written, or shall be written compared with it: all I will say about it is that it deals with facts, and facts so neat and diverting that no lies could match them.”\n\n“And how is the book entitled?” asked Don Quixote.\n\n“The ‘Life of Gines de Pasamonte,’” replied the subject of it.\n\n“And is it finished?” asked Don Quixote.\n\n“How can it be finished,” said the other, “when my life is not yet finished? All that is written is from my birth down to the point when they sent me to the galleys this last time.”\n\n“Then you have been there before?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“In the service of God and the king I have been there for four years before now, and I know by this time what the biscuit and courbash are like,” replied Gines; “and it is no great grievance to me to go back to them, for there I shall have time to finish my book; I have still many things left to say, and in the galleys of Spain there is more than enough leisure; though I do not want much for what I have to write, for I have it by heart.”\n\n“You seem a clever fellow,” said Don Quixote.\n\n“And an unfortunate one,” replied Gines, “for misfortune always persecutes good wit.”\n\n“It persecutes rogues,” said the commissary.\n\n“I told you already to go gently, master commissary,” said Pasamonte; “their lordships yonder never gave you that staff to ill-treat us wretches here, but to conduct and take us where his majesty orders you; if not, by the life of—never mind—; it may be that some day the stains made in the inn will come out in the scouring; let everyone hold his tongue and behave well and speak better; and now let us march on, for we have had quite enough of this entertainment.”\n\nThe commissary lifted his staff to strike Pasamonte in return for his threats, but Don Quixote came between them, and begged him not to ill-use him, as it was not too much to allow one who had his hands tied to have his tongue a trifle free; and turning to the whole chain of them he said:\n\n“From all you have told me, dear brethren, I make out clearly that though they have punished you for your faults, the punishments you are about to endure do not give you much pleasure, and that you go to them very much against the grain and against your will, and that perhaps this one’s want of courage under torture, that one’s want of money, the other’s want of advocacy, and lastly the perverted judgment of the judge may have been the cause of your ruin and of your failure to obtain the justice you had on your side. All which presents itself now to my mind, urging, persuading, and even compelling me to demonstrate in your case the purpose for which Heaven sent me into the world and caused me to make profession of the order of chivalry to which I belong, and the vow I took therein to give aid to those in need and under the oppression of the strong. But as I know that it is a mark of prudence not to do by foul means what may be done by fair, I will ask these gentlemen, the guards and commissary, to be so good as to release you and let you go in peace, as there will be no lack of others to serve the king under more favourable circumstances; for it seems to me a hard case to make slaves of those whom God and nature have made free. Moreover, sirs of the guard,” added Don Quixote, “these poor fellows have done nothing to you; let each answer for his own sins yonder; there is a God in Heaven who will not forget to punish the wicked or reward the good; and it is not fitting that honest men should be the instruments of punishment to others, they being therein no way concerned. This request I make thus gently and quietly, that, if you comply with it, I may have reason for thanking you; and, if you will not voluntarily, this lance and sword together with the might of my arm shall compel you to comply with it by force.”\n\n“Nice nonsense!” said the commissary; “a fine piece of pleasantry he has come out with at last! He wants us to let the king’s prisoners go, as if we had any authority to release them, or he to order us to do so! Go your way, sir, and good luck to you; put that basin straight that you’ve got on your head, and don’t go looking for three feet on a cat.”\n\n“‘Tis you that are the cat, rat, and rascal,” replied Don Quixote, and acting on the word he fell upon him so suddenly that without giving him time to defend himself he brought him to the ground sorely wounded with a lance-thrust; and lucky it was for him that it was the one that had the musket. The other guards stood thunderstruck and amazed at this unexpected event, but recovering presence of mind, those on horseback seized their swords, and those on foot their javelins, and attacked Don Quixote, who was waiting for them with great calmness; and no doubt it would have gone badly with him if the galley slaves, seeing the chance before them of liberating themselves, had not effected it by contriving to break the chain on which they were strung. Such was the confusion, that the guards, now rushing at the galley slaves who were breaking loose, now to attack Don Quixote who was waiting for them, did nothing at all that was of any use. Sancho, on his part, gave a helping hand to release Gines de Pasamonte, who was the first to leap forth upon the plain free and unfettered, and who, attacking the prostrate commissary, took from him his sword and the musket, with which, aiming at one and levelling at another, he, without ever discharging it, drove every one of the guards off the field, for they took to flight, as well to escape Pasamonte’s musket, as the showers of stones the now released galley slaves were raining upon them. Sancho was greatly grieved at the affair, because he anticipated that those who had fled would report the matter to the Holy Brotherhood, who at the summons of the alarm-bell would at once sally forth in quest of the offenders; and he said so to his master, and entreated him to leave the place at once, and go into hiding in the sierra that was close by.\n\n“That is all very well,” said Don Quixote, “but I know what must be done now;” and calling together all the galley slaves, who were now running riot, and had stripped the commissary to the skin, he collected them round him to hear what he had to say, and addressed them as follows: “To be grateful for benefits received is the part of persons of good birth, and one of the sins most offensive to God is ingratitude; I say so because, sirs, ye have already seen by manifest proof the benefit ye have received of me; in return for which I desire, and it is my good pleasure that, laden with that chain which I have taken off your necks, ye at once set out and proceed to the city of El Toboso, and there present yourselves before the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, and say to her that her knight, he of the Rueful Countenance, sends to commend himself to her; and that ye recount to her in full detail all the particulars of this notable adventure, up to the recovery of your longed-for liberty; and this done ye may go where ye will, and good fortune attend you.”\n\nGines de Pasamonte made answer for all, saying, “That which you, sir, our deliverer, demand of us, is of all impossibilities the most impossible to comply with, because we cannot go together along the roads, but only singly and separate, and each one his own way, endeavouring to hide ourselves in the bowels of the earth to escape the Holy Brotherhood, which, no doubt, will come out in search of us. What your worship may do, and fairly do, is to change this service and tribute as regards the lady Dulcinea del Toboso for a certain quantity of ave-marias and credos which we will say for your worship’s intention, and this is a condition that can be complied with by night as by day, running or resting, in peace or in war; but to imagine that we are going now to return to the flesh-pots of Egypt, I mean to take up our chain and set out for El Toboso, is to imagine that it is now night, though it is not yet ten in the morning, and to ask this of us is like asking pears of the elm tree.”\n\n“Then by all that’s good,” said Don Quixote (now stirred to wrath), “Don son of a bitch, Don Ginesillo de Paropillo, or whatever your name is, you will have to go yourself alone, with your tail between your legs and the whole chain on your back.”\n\nPasamonte, who was anything but meek (being by this time thoroughly convinced that Don Quixote was not quite right in his head as he had committed such a vagary as to set them free), finding himself abused in this fashion, gave the wink to his companions, and falling back they began to shower stones on Don Quixote at such a rate that he was quite unable to protect himself with his buckler, and poor Rocinante no more heeded the spur than if he had been made of brass. Sancho planted himself behind his ass, and with him sheltered himself from the hailstorm that poured on both of them. Don Quixote was unable to shield himself so well but that more pebbles than I could count struck him full on the body with such force that they brought him to the ground; and the instant he fell the student pounced upon him, snatched the basin from his head, and with it struck three or four blows on his shoulders, and as many more on the ground, knocking it almost to pieces. They then stripped him of a jacket that he wore over his armour, and they would have stripped off his stockings if his greaves had not prevented them. From Sancho they took his coat, leaving him in his shirt-sleeves; and dividing among themselves the remaining spoils of the battle, they went each one his own way, more solicitous about keeping clear of the Holy Brotherhood they dreaded, than about burdening themselves with the chain, or going to present themselves before the lady Dulcinea del Toboso. The ass and Rocinante, Sancho and Don Quixote, were all that were left upon the spot; the ass with drooping head, serious, shaking his ears from time to time as if he thought the storm of stones that assailed them was not yet over; Rocinante stretched beside his master, for he too had been brought to the ground by a stone; Sancho stripped, and trembling with fear of the Holy Brotherhood; and Don Quixote fuming to find himself so served by the very persons for whom he had done so much.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII.\n\nOF WHAT BEFELL DON QUIXOTE IN THE SIERRA MORENA, WHICH WAS ONE OF THE RAREST ADVENTURES RELATED IN THIS VERACIOUS HISTORY\nSeeing himself served in this way, Don Quixote said to his squire, “I have always heard it said, Sancho, that to do good to boors is to throw water into the sea. If I had believed thy words, I should have avoided this trouble; but it is done now, it is only to have patience and take warning for the future.”\n\n“Your worship will take warning as much as I am a Turk,” returned Sancho; “but, as you say this mischief might have been avoided if you had believed me, believe me now, and a still greater one will be avoided; for I tell you chivalry is of no account with the Holy Brotherhood, and they don’t care two maravedis for all the knights-errant in the world; and I can tell you I fancy I hear their arrows whistling past my ears this minute.”\n\n“Thou art a coward by nature, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “but lest thou shouldst say I am obstinate, and that I never do as thou dost advise, this once I will take thy advice, and withdraw out of reach of that fury thou so dreadest; but it must be on one condition, that never, in life or in death, thou art to say to anyone that I retired or withdrew from this danger out of fear, but only in compliance with thy entreaties; for if thou sayest otherwise thou wilt lie therein, and from this time to that, and from that to this, I give thee lie, and say thou liest and wilt lie every time thou thinkest or sayest it; and answer me not again; for at the mere thought that I am withdrawing or retiring from any danger, above all from this, which does seem to carry some little shadow of fear with it, I am ready to take my stand here and await alone, not only that Holy Brotherhood you talk of and dread, but the brothers of the twelve tribes of Israel, and the Seven Maccabees, and Castor and Pollux, and all the brothers and brotherhoods in the world.”\n\n“Señor,” replied Sancho, “to retire is not to flee, and there is no wisdom in waiting when danger outweighs hope, and it is the part of wise men to preserve themselves to-day for to-morrow, and not risk all in one day; and let me tell you, though I am a clown and a boor, I have got some notion of what they call safe conduct; so repent not of having taken my advice, but mount Rocinante if you can, and if not I will help you; and follow me, for my mother-wit tells me we have more need of legs than hands just now.”\n\nDon Quixote mounted without replying, and, Sancho leading the way on his ass, they entered the side of the Sierra Morena, which was close by, as it was Sancho’s design to cross it entirely and come out again at El Viso or Almodovar del Campo, and hide for some days among its crags so as to escape the search of the Brotherhood should they come to look for them. He was encouraged in this by perceiving that the stock of provisions carried by the ass had come safe out of the fray with the galley slaves, a circumstance that he regarded as a miracle, seeing how they pillaged and ransacked.\n\nThat night they reached the very heart of the Sierra Morena, where it seemed prudent to Sancho to pass the night and even some days, at least as many as the stores he carried might last, and so they encamped between two rocks and among some cork trees; but fatal destiny, which, according to the opinion of those who have not the light of the true faith, directs, arranges, and settles everything in its own way, so ordered it that Gines de Pasamonte, the famous knave and thief who by the virtue and madness of Don Quixote had been released from the chain, driven by fear of the Holy Brotherhood, which he had good reason to dread, resolved to take hiding in the mountains; and his fate and fear led him to the same spot to which Don Quixote and Sancho Panza had been led by theirs, just in time to recognise them and leave them to fall asleep: and as the wicked are always ungrateful, and necessity leads to evildoing, and immediate advantage overcomes all considerations of the future, Gines, who was neither grateful nor well-principled, made up his mind to steal Sancho Panza’s ass, not troubling himself about Rocinante, as being a prize that was no good either to pledge or sell. While Sancho slept he stole his ass, and before day dawned he was far out of reach.\n\nAurora made her appearance bringing gladness to the earth but sadness to Sancho Panza, for he found that his Dapple was missing, and seeing himself bereft of him he began the saddest and most doleful lament in the world, so loud that Don Quixote awoke at his exclamations and heard him saying, “O son of my bowels, born in my very house, my children’s plaything, my wife’s joy, the envy of my neighbours, relief of my burdens, and lastly, half supporter of myself, for with the six-and-twenty maravedis thou didst earn me daily I met half my charges.”\n\nDon Quixote, when he heard the lament and learned the cause, consoled Sancho with the best arguments he could, entreating him to be patient, and promising to give him a letter of exchange ordering three out of five ass-colts that he had at home to be given to him. Sancho took comfort at this, dried his tears, suppressed his sobs, and returned thanks for the kindness shown him by Don Quixote. He on his part was rejoiced to the heart on entering the mountains, as they seemed to him to be just the place for the adventures he was in quest of. They brought back to his memory the marvellous adventures that had befallen knights-errant in like solitudes and wilds, and he went along reflecting on these things, so absorbed and carried away by them that he had no thought for anything else.\n\nNor had Sancho any other care (now that he fancied he was travelling in a safe quarter) than to satisfy his appetite with such remains as were left of the clerical spoils, and so he marched behind his master laden with what Dapple used to carry, emptying the sack and packing his paunch, and so long as he could go that way, he would not have given a farthing to meet with another adventure.\n\nWhile so engaged he raised his eyes and saw that his master had halted, and was trying with the point of his pike to lift some bulky object that lay upon the ground, on which he hastened to join him and help him if it were needful, and reached him just as with the point of the pike he was raising a saddle-pad with a valise attached to it, half or rather wholly rotten and torn; but so heavy were they that Sancho had to help to take them up, and his master directed him to see what the valise contained. Sancho did so with great alacrity, and though the valise was secured by a chain and padlock, from its torn and rotten condition he was able to see its contents, which were four shirts of fine holland, and other articles of linen no less curious than clean; and in a handkerchief he found a good lot of gold crowns, and as soon as he saw them he exclaimed:\n\n“Blessed be all Heaven for sending us an adventure that is good for something!”\n\nSearching further he found a little memorandum book richly bound; this Don Quixote asked of him, telling him to take the money and keep it for himself. Sancho kissed his hands for the favour, and cleared the valise of its linen, which he stowed away in the provision sack. Considering the whole matter, Don Quixote observed:\n\n“It seems to me, Sancho--and it is impossible it can be otherwise-that some strayed traveller must have crossed this sierra and been attacked and slain by footpads, who brought him to this remote spot to bury him.”\n\n“That cannot be,” answered Sancho, “because if they had been robbers they would not have left this money.”\n\n“Thou art right,” said Don Quixote, “and I cannot guess or explain what this may mean; but stay; let us see if in this memorandum book there is anything written by which we may be able to trace out or discover what we want to know.”\n\nHe opened it, and the first thing he found in it, written roughly but in a very good hand, was a sonnet, and reading it aloud that Sancho might hear it, he found that it ran as follows:\n\nOr Love is lacking in intelligence, Or to the height of cruelty attains, Or else it is my doom to suffer pains Beyond the measure due to my offence. But if Love be a God, it follows thence That he knows all, and certain it remains No God loves cruelty; then who ordains This penance that enthrals while it torments? It were a falsehood, Chloe, thee to name; Such evil with such goodness cannot live; And against Heaven I dare not charge the blame, I only know it is my fate to die. To him who knows not whence his malady A miracle alone a cure can give.\n\n“There is nothing to be learned from that rhyme,” said Sancho, “unless by that clue there’s in it, one may draw out the ball of the whole matter.”\n\n“What clue is there?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“I thought your worship spoke of a clue in it,” said Sancho.\n\n“I only said Chloe,” replied Don Quixote; “and that no doubt, is the name of the lady of whom the author of the sonnet complains; and, faith, he must be a tolerable poet, or I know little of the craft.”\n\n“Then your worship understands rhyming too?”\n\n“And better than thou thinkest,” replied Don Quixote, “as thou shalt see when thou carriest a letter written in verse from beginning to end to my lady Dulcinea del Toboso, for I would have thee know, Sancho, that all or most of the knights-errant in days of yore were great troubadours and great musicians, for both of these accomplishments, or more properly speaking gifts, are the peculiar property of lovers-errant: true it is that the verses of the knights of old have more spirit than neatness in them.”\n\n“Read more, your worship,” said Sancho, “and you will find something that will enlighten us.”\n\nDon Quixote turned the page and said, “This is prose and seems to be a letter.”\n\n“A correspondence letter, señor?”\n\n“From the beginning it seems to be a love letter,” replied Don Quixote.\n\n“Then let your worship read it aloud,” said Sancho, “for I am very fond of love matters.”\n\n“With all my heart,” said Don Quixote, and reading it aloud as Sancho had requested him, he found it ran thus:\n\nThy false promise and my sure misfortune carry me to a place whence the news of my death will reach thy ears before the words of my complaint. Ungrateful one, thou hast rejected me for one more wealthy, but not more worthy; but if virtue were esteemed wealth I should neither envy the fortunes of others nor weep for misfortunes of my own. What thy beauty raised up thy deeds have laid low; by it I believed thee to be an angel, by them I know thou art a woman. Peace be with thee who hast sent war to me, and Heaven grant that the deceit of thy husband be ever hidden from thee, so that thou repent not of what thou hast done, and I reap not a revenge I would not have.\n\nWhen he had finished the letter, Don Quixote said, “There is less to be gathered from this than from the verses, except that he who wrote it is some rejected lover;” and turning over nearly all the pages of the book he found more verses and letters, some of which he could read, while others he could not; but they were all made up of complaints, laments, misgivings, desires and aversions, favours and rejections, some rapturous, some doleful. While Don Quixote examined the book, Sancho examined the valise, not leaving a corner in the whole of it or in the pad that he did not search, peer into, and explore, or seam that he did not rip, or tuft of wool that he did not pick to pieces, lest anything should escape for want of care and pains; so keen was the covetousness excited in him by the discovery of the crowns, which amounted to near a hundred; and though he found no more booty, he held the blanket flights, balsam vomits, stake benedictions, carriers’ fisticuffs, missing alforjas, stolen coat, and all the hunger, thirst, and weariness he had endured in the service of his good master, cheap at the price; as he considered himself more than fully indemnified for all by the payment he received in the gift of the treasure-trove.\n\nThe Knight of the Rueful Countenance was still very anxious to find out who the owner of the valise could be, conjecturing from the sonnet and letter, from the money in gold, and from the fineness of the shirts, that he must be some lover of distinction whom the scorn and cruelty of his lady had driven to some desperate course; but as in that uninhabited and rugged spot there was no one to be seen of whom he could inquire, he saw nothing else for it but to push on, taking whatever road Rocinante chose--which was where he could make his way--firmly persuaded that among these wilds he could not fail to meet some rare adventure. As he went along, then, occupied with these thoughts, he perceived on the summit of a height that rose before their eyes a man who went springing from rock to rock and from tussock to tussock with marvellous agility. As well as he could make out he was unclad, with a thick black beard, long tangled hair, and bare legs and feet, his thighs were covered by breeches apparently of tawny velvet but so ragged that they showed his skin in several places.\n\nHe was bareheaded, and notwithstanding the swiftness with which he passed as has been described, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance observed and noted all these trifles, and though he made the attempt, he was unable to follow him, for it was not granted to the feebleness of Rocinante to make way over such rough ground, he being, moreover, slow-paced and sluggish by nature. Don Quixote at once came to the conclusion that this was the owner of the saddle-pad and of the valise, and made up his mind to go in search of him, even though he should have to wander a year in those mountains before he found him, and so he directed Sancho to take a short cut over one side of the mountain, while he himself went by the other, and perhaps by this means they might light upon this man who had passed so quickly out of their sight.\n\n“I could not do that,” said Sancho, “for when I separate from your worship fear at once lays hold of me, and assails me with all sorts of panics and fancies; and let what I now say be a notice that from this time forth I am not going to stir a finger’s width from your presence.”\n\n“It shall be so,” said he of the Rueful Countenance, “and I am very glad that thou art willing to rely on my courage, which will never fail thee, even though the soul in thy body fail thee; so come on now behind me slowly as well as thou canst, and make lanterns of thine eyes; let us make the circuit of this ridge; perhaps we shall light upon this man that we saw, who no doubt is no other than the owner of what we found.”\n\nTo which Sancho made answer, “Far better would it be not to look for him, for, if we find him, and he happens to be the owner of the money, it is plain I must restore it; it would be better, therefore, that without taking this needless trouble, I should keep possession of it until in some other less meddlesome and officious way the real owner may be discovered; and perhaps that will be when I shall have spent it, and then the king will hold me harmless.”\n\n“Thou art wrong there, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “for now that we have a suspicion who the owner is, and have him almost before us, we are bound to seek him and make restitution; and if we do not see him, the strong suspicion we have as to his being the owner makes us as guilty as if he were so; and so, friend Sancho, let not our search for him give thee any uneasiness, for if we find him it will relieve mine.”\n\nAnd so saying he gave Rocinante the spur, and Sancho followed him on foot and loaded, and after having partly made the circuit of the mountain they found lying in a ravine, dead and half devoured by dogs and pecked by jackdaws, a mule saddled and bridled, all which still further strengthened their suspicion that he who had fled was the owner of the mule and the saddle-pad.\n\nAs they stood looking at it they heard a whistle like that of a shepherd watching his flock, and suddenly on their left there appeared a great number of goats and behind them on the summit of the mountain the goatherd in charge of them, a man advanced in years. Don Quixote called aloud to him and begged him to come down to where they stood. He shouted in return, asking what had brought them to that spot, seldom or never trodden except by the feet of goats, or of the wolves and other wild beasts that roamed around. Sancho in return bade him come down, and they would explain all to him.\n\nThe goatherd descended, and reaching the place where Don Quixote stood, he said, “I will wager you are looking at that hack mule that lies dead in the hollow there, and, faith, it has been lying there now these six months; tell me, have you come upon its master about here?”\n\n“We have come upon nobody,” answered Don Quixote, “nor on anything except a saddle-pad and a little valise that we found not far from this.”\n\n“I found it too,” said the goatherd, “but I would not lift it nor go near it for fear of some ill-luck or being charged with theft, for the devil is crafty, and things rise up under one’s feet to make one fall without knowing why or wherefore.”\n\n“That’s exactly what I say,” said Sancho; “I found it too, and I would not go within a stone’s throw of it; there I left it, and there it lies just as it was, for I don’t want a dog with a bell.”\n\n“Tell me, good man,” said Don Quixote, “do you know who is the owner of this property?”\n\n“All I can tell you,” said the goatherd, “is that about six months ago, more or less, there arrived at a shepherd’s hut three leagues, perhaps, away from this, a youth of well-bred appearance and manners, mounted on that same mule which lies dead here, and with the same saddle-pad and valise which you say you found and did not touch. He asked us what part of this sierra was the most rugged and retired; we told him that it was where we now are; and so in truth it is, for if you push on half a league farther, perhaps you will not be able to find your way out; and I am wondering how you have managed to come here, for there is no road or path that leads to this spot. I say, then, that on hearing our answer the youth turned about and made for the place we pointed out to him, leaving us all charmed with his good looks, and wondering at his question and the haste with which we saw him depart in the direction of the sierra; and after that we saw him no more, until some days afterwards he crossed the path of one of our shepherds, and without saying a word to him, came up to him and gave him several cuffs and kicks, and then turned to the ass with our provisions and took all the bread and cheese it carried, and having done this made off back again into the sierra with extraordinary swiftness. When some of us goatherds learned this we went in search of him for about two days through the most remote portion of this sierra, at the end of which we found him lodged in the hollow of a large thick cork tree. He came out to meet us with great gentleness, with his dress now torn and his face so disfigured and burned by the sun, that we hardly recognised him but that his clothes, though torn, convinced us, from the recollection we had of them, that he was the person we were looking for. He saluted us courteously, and in a few well-spoken words he told us not to wonder at seeing him going about in this guise, as it was binding upon him in order that he might work out a penance which for his many sins had been imposed upon him. We asked him to tell us who he was, but we were never able to find out from him: we begged of him too, when he was in want of food, which he could not do without, to tell us where we should find him, as we would bring it to him with all good-will and readiness; or if this were not to his taste, at least to come and ask it of us and not take it by force from the shepherds. He thanked us for the offer, begged pardon for the late assault, and promised for the future to ask it in God’s name without offering violence to anybody. As for fixed abode, he said he had no other than that which chance offered wherever night might overtake him; and his words ended in an outburst of weeping so bitter that we who listened to him must have been very stones had we not joined him in it, comparing what we saw of him the first time with what we saw now; for, as I said, he was a graceful and gracious youth, and in his courteous and polished language showed himself to be of good birth and courtly breeding, and rustics as we were that listened to him, even to our rusticity his gentle bearing sufficed to make it plain.\n\n“But in the midst of his conversation he stopped and became silent, keeping his eyes fixed upon the ground for some time, during which we stood still waiting anxiously to see what would come of this abstraction; and with no little pity, for from his behaviour, now staring at the ground with fixed gaze and eyes wide open without moving an eyelid, again closing them, compressing his lips and raising his eyebrows, we could perceive plainly that a fit of madness of some kind had come upon him; and before long he showed that what we imagined was the truth, for he arose in a fury from the ground where he had thrown himself, and attacked the first he found near him with such rage and fierceness that if we had not dragged him off him, he would have beaten or bitten him to death, all the while exclaiming, ‘Oh faithless Fernando, here, here shalt thou pay the penalty of the wrong thou hast done me; these hands shall tear out that heart of thine, abode and dwelling of all iniquity, but of deceit and fraud above all; and to these he added other words all in effect upbraiding this Fernando and charging him with treachery and faithlessness.\n\n“We forced him to release his hold with no little difficulty, and without another word he left us, and rushing off plunged in among these brakes and brambles, so as to make it impossible for us to follow him; from this we suppose that madness comes upon him from time to time, and that some one called Fernando must have done him a wrong of a grievous nature such as the condition to which it had brought him seemed to show. All this has been since then confirmed on those occasions, and they have been many, on which he has crossed our path, at one time to beg the shepherds to give him some of the food they carry, at another to take it from them by force; for when there is a fit of madness upon him, even though the shepherds offer it freely, he will not accept it but snatches it from them by dint of blows; but when he is in his senses he begs it for the love of God, courteously and civilly, and receives it with many thanks and not a few tears. And to tell you the truth, sirs,” continued the goatherd, “it was yesterday that we resolved, I and four of the lads, two of them our servants, and the other two friends of mine, to go in search of him until we find him, and when we do to take him, whether by force or of his own consent, to the town of Almodovar, which is eight leagues from this, and there strive to cure him (if indeed his malady admits of a cure), or learn when he is in his senses who he is, and if he has relatives to whom we may give notice of his misfortune. This, sirs, is all I can say in answer to what you have asked me; and be sure that the owner of the articles you found is he whom you saw pass by with such nimbleness and so naked.”\n\nFor Don Quixote had already described how he had seen the man go bounding along the mountain side, and he was now filled with amazement at what he heard from the goatherd, and more eager than ever to discover who the unhappy madman was; and in his heart he resolved, as he had done before, to search for him all over the mountain, not leaving a corner or cave unexamined until he had found him. But chance arranged matters better than he expected or hoped, for at that very moment, in a gorge on the mountain that opened where they stood, the youth he wished to find made his appearance, coming along talking to himself in a way that would have been unintelligible near at hand, much more at a distance. His garb was what has been described, save that as he drew near, Don Quixote perceived that a tattered doublet which he wore was amber-tanned, from which he concluded that one who wore such garments could not be of very low rank.\n\nApproaching them, the youth greeted them in a harsh and hoarse voice but with great courtesy. Don Quixote returned his salutation with equal politeness, and dismounting from Rocinante advanced with well-bred bearing and grace to embrace him, and held him for some time close in his arms as if he had known him for a long time. The other, whom we may call the Ragged One of the Sorry Countenance, as Don Quixote was of the Rueful, after submitting to the embrace pushed him back a little and, placing his hands on Don Quixote’s shoulders, stood gazing at him as if seeking to see whether he knew him, not less amazed, perhaps, at the sight of the face, figure, and armour of Don Quixote than Don Quixote was at the sight of him. To be brief, the first to speak after embracing was the Ragged One, and he said what will be told farther on.\n\n# IN WHICH IS CONTINUED THE ADVENTURE OF THE SIERRA MORENA\n\nThe history relates that it was with the greatest attention Don Quixote listened to the ragged knight of the Sierra, who began by saying:\n\n“Of a surety, señor, whoever you are, for I know you not, I thank you for the proofs of kindness and courtesy you have shown me, and would I were in a condition to requite with something more than good-will that which you have displayed towards me in the cordial reception you have given me; but my fate does not afford me any other means of returning kindnesses done me save the hearty desire to repay them.”\n\n“Mine,” replied Don Quixote, “is to be of service to you, so much so that I had resolved not to quit these mountains until I had found you, and learned of you whether there is any kind of relief to be found for that sorrow under which from the strangeness of your life you seem to labour; and to search for you with all possible diligence, if search had been necessary. And if your misfortune should prove to be one of those that refuse admission to any sort of consolation, it was my purpose to join you in lamenting and mourning over it, so far as I could; for it is still some comfort in misfortune to find one who can feel for it. And if my good intentions deserve to be acknowledged with any kind of courtesy, I entreat you, señor, by that which I perceive you possess in so high a degree, and likewise conjure you by whatever you love or have loved best in life, to tell me who you are and the cause that has brought you to live or die in these solitudes like a brute beast, dwelling among them in a manner so foreign to your condition as your garb and appearance show. And I swear,” added Don Quixote, “by the order of knighthood which I have received, and by my vocation of knight-errant, if you gratify me in this, to serve you with all the zeal my calling demands of me, either in relieving your misfortune if it admits of relief, or in joining you in lamenting it as I promised to do.”\n\nThe Knight of the Thicket, hearing him of the Rueful Countenance talk in this strain, did nothing but stare at him, and stare at him again, and again survey him from head to foot; and when he had thoroughly examined him, he said to him:\n\n“If you have anything to give me to eat, for God’s sake give it me, and after I have eaten I will do all you ask in acknowledgment of the goodwill you have displayed towards me.”\n\nSancho from his sack, and the goatherd from his pouch, furnished the Ragged One with the means of appeasing his hunger, and what they gave him he ate like a half-witted being, so hastily that he took no time between mouthfuls, gorging rather than swallowing; and while he ate neither he nor they who observed him uttered a word. As soon as he had done he made signs to them to follow him, which they did, and he led them to a green plot which lay a little farther off round the corner of a rock. On reaching it he stretched himself upon the grass, and the others did the same, all keeping silence, until the Ragged One, settling himself in his place, said:\n\n“If it is your wish, sirs, that I should disclose in a few words the surpassing extent of my misfortunes, you must promise not to break the thread of my sad story with any question or other interruption, for the instant you do so the tale I tell will come to an end.”\n\nThese words of the Ragged One reminded Don Quixote of the tale his squire had told him, when he failed to keep count of the goats that had crossed the river and the story remained unfinished; but to return to the Ragged One, he went on to say:\n\n“I give you this warning because I wish to pass briefly over the story of my misfortunes, for recalling them to memory only serves to add fresh ones, and the less you question me the sooner shall I make an end of the recital, though I shall not omit to relate anything of importance in order fully to satisfy your curiosity.”\n\nDon Quixote gave the promise for himself and the others, and with this assurance he began as follows:\n\n“My name is Cardenio, my birthplace one of the best cities of this Andalusia, my family noble, my parents rich, my misfortune so great that my parents must have wept and my family grieved over it without being able by their wealth to lighten it; for the gifts of fortune can do little to relieve reverses sent by Heaven. In that same country there was a heaven in which love had placed all the glory I could desire; such was the beauty of Luscinda, a damsel as noble and as rich as I, but of happier fortunes, and of less firmness than was due to so worthy a passion as mine. This Luscinda I loved, worshipped, and adored from my earliest and tenderest years, and she loved me in all the innocence and sincerity of childhood. Our parents were aware of our feelings, and were not sorry to perceive them, for they saw clearly that as they ripened they must lead at last to a marriage between us, a thing that seemed almost prearranged by the equality of our families and wealth. We grew up, and with our growth grew the love between us, so that the father of Luscinda felt bound for propriety’s sake to refuse me admission to his house, in this perhaps imitating the parents of that Thisbe so celebrated by the poets, and this refusal but added love to love and flame to flame; for though they enforced silence upon our tongues they could not impose it upon our pens, which can make known the heart’s secrets to a loved one more freely than tongues; for many a time the presence of the object of love shakes the firmest will and strikes dumb the boldest tongue. Ah heavens! how many letters did I write her, and how many dainty modest replies did I receive! how many ditties and love-songs did I compose in which my heart declared and made known its feelings, described its ardent longings, revelled in its recollections and dallied with its desires! At length growing impatient and feeling my heart languishing with longing to see her, I resolved to put into execution and carry out what seemed to me the best mode of winning my desired and merited reward, to ask her of her father for my lawful wife, which I did. To this his answer was that he thanked me for the disposition I showed to do honour to him and to regard myself as honoured by the bestowal of his treasure; but that as my father was alive it was his by right to make this demand, for if it were not in accordance with his full will and pleasure, Luscinda was not to be taken or given by stealth. I thanked him for his kindness, reflecting that there was reason in what he said, and that my father would assent to it as soon as I should tell him, and with that view I went the very same instant to let him know what my desires were. When I entered the room where he was I found him with an open letter in his hand, which, before I could utter a word, he gave me, saying, ‘By this letter thou wilt see, Cardenio, the disposition the Duke Ricardo has to serve thee.’ This Duke Ricardo, as you, sirs, probably know already, is a grandee of Spain who has his seat in the best part of this Andalusia. I took and read the letter, which was couched in terms so flattering that even I myself felt it would be wrong in my father not to comply with the request the duke made in it, which was that he would send me immediately to him, as he wished me to become the companion, not servant, of his eldest son, and would take upon himself the charge of placing me in a position corresponding to the esteem in which he held me. On reading the letter my voice failed me, and still more when I heard my father say, ‘Two days hence thou wilt depart, Cardenio, in accordance with the duke’s wish, and give thanks to God who is opening a road to thee by which thou mayest attain what I know thou dost deserve; and to these words he added others of fatherly counsel. The time for my departure arrived; I spoke one night to Luscinda, I told her all that had occurred, as I did also to her father, entreating him to allow some delay, and to defer the disposal of her hand until I should see what the Duke Ricardo sought of me: he gave me the promise, and she confirmed it with vows and swoonings unnumbered. Finally, I presented myself to the duke, and was received and treated by him so kindly that very soon envy began to do its work, the old servants growing envious of me, and regarding the duke’s inclination to show me favour as an injury to themselves. But the one to whom my arrival gave the greatest pleasure was the duke’s second son, Fernando by name, a gallant youth, of noble, generous, and amorous disposition, who very soon made so intimate a friend of me that it was remarked by everybody; for though the elder was attached to me, and showed me kindness, he did not carry his affectionate treatment to the same length as Don Fernando. It so happened, then, that as between friends no secret remains unshared, and as the favour I enjoyed with Don Fernando had grown into friendship, he made all his thoughts known to me, and in particular a love affair which troubled his mind a little. He was deeply in love with a peasant girl, a vassal of his father’s, the daughter of wealthy parents, and herself so beautiful, modest, discreet, and virtuous, that no one who knew her was able to decide in which of these respects she was most highly gifted or most excelled. The attractions of the fair peasant raised the passion of Don Fernando to such a point that, in order to gain his object and overcome her virtuous resolutions, he determined to pledge his word to her to become her husband, for to attempt it in any other way was to attempt an impossibility. Bound to him as I was by friendship, I strove by the best arguments and the most forcible examples I could think of to restrain and dissuade him from such a course; but perceiving I produced no effect I resolved to make the Duke Ricardo, his father, acquainted with the matter; but Don Fernando, being sharp-witted and shrewd, foresaw and apprehended this, perceiving that by my duty as a good servant I was bound not to keep concealed a thing so much opposed to the honour of my lord the duke; and so, to mislead and deceive me, he told me he could find no better way of effacing from his mind the beauty that so enslaved him than by absenting himself for some months, and that he wished the absence to be effected by our going, both of us, to my father’s house under the pretence, which he would make to the duke, of going to see and buy some fine horses that there were in my city, which produces the best in the world. When I heard him say so, even if his resolution had not been so good a one I should have hailed it as one of the happiest that could be imagined, prompted by my affection, seeing what a favourable chance and opportunity it offered me of returning to see my Luscinda. With this thought and wish I commended his idea and encouraged his design, advising him to put it into execution as quickly as possible, as, in truth, absence produced its effect in spite of the most deeply rooted feelings. But, as afterwards appeared, when he said this to me he had already enjoyed the peasant girl under the title of husband, and was waiting for an opportunity of making it known with safety to himself, being in dread of what his father the duke would do when he came to know of his folly. It happened, then, that as with young men love is for the most part nothing more than appetite, which, as its final object is enjoyment, comes to an end on obtaining it, and that which seemed to be love takes to flight, as it cannot pass the limit fixed by nature, which fixes no limit to true love--what I mean is that after Don Fernando had enjoyed this peasant girl his passion subsided and his eagerness cooled, and if at first he feigned a wish to absent himself in order to cure his love, he was now in reality anxious to go to avoid keeping his promise.\n\n“The duke gave him permission, and ordered me to accompany him; we arrived at my city, and my father gave him the reception due to his rank; I saw Luscinda without delay, and, though it had not been dead or deadened, my love gathered fresh life. To my sorrow I told the story of it to Don Fernando, for I thought that in virtue of the great friendship he bore me I was bound to conceal nothing from him. I extolled her beauty, her gaiety, her wit, so warmly, that my praises excited in him a desire to see a damsel adorned by such attractions. To my misfortune I yielded to it, showing her to him one night by the light of a taper at a window where we used to talk to one another. As she appeared to him in her dressing-gown, she drove all the beauties he had seen until then out of his recollection; speech failed him, his head turned, he was spell-bound, and in the end love-smitten, as you will see in the course of the story of my misfortune; and to inflame still further his passion, which he hid from me and revealed to Heaven alone, it so happened that one day he found a note of hers entreating me to demand her of her father in marriage, so delicate, so modest, and so tender, that on reading it he told me that in Luscinda alone were combined all the charms of beauty and understanding that were distributed among all the other women in the world. It is true, and I own it now, that though I knew what good cause Don Fernando had to praise Luscinda, it gave me uneasiness to hear these praises from his mouth, and I began to fear, and with reason to feel distrust of him, for there was no moment when he was not ready to talk of Luscinda, and he would start the subject himself even though he dragged it in unseasonably, a circumstance that aroused in me a certain amount of jealousy; not that I feared any change in the constancy or faith of Luscinda; but still my fate led me to forebode what she assured me against. Don Fernando contrived always to read the letters I sent to Luscinda and her answers to me, under the pretence that he enjoyed the wit and sense of both. It so happened, then, that Luscinda having begged of me a book of chivalry to read, one that she was very fond of, Amadis of Gaul—”\n\nDon Quixote no sooner heard a book of chivalry mentioned, than he said:\n\n“Had your worship told me at the beginning of your story that the Lady Luscinda was fond of books of chivalry, no other laudation would have been requisite to impress upon me the superiority of her understanding, for it could not have been of the excellence you describe had a taste for such delightful reading been wanting; so, as far as I am concerned, you need waste no more words in describing her beauty, worth, and intelligence; for, on merely hearing what her taste was, I declare her to be the most beautiful and the most intelligent woman in the world; and I wish your worship had, along with Amadis of Gaul, sent her the worthy Don Rugel of Greece, for I know the Lady Luscinda would greatly relish Daraida and Garaya, and the shrewd sayings of the shepherd Darinel, and the admirable verses of his bucolics, sung and delivered by him with such sprightliness, wit, and ease; but a time may come when this omission can be remedied, and to rectify it nothing more is needed than for your worship to be so good as to come with me to my village, for there I can give you more than three hundred books which are the delight of my soul and the entertainment of my life;--though it occurs to me that I have not got one of them now, thanks to the spite of wicked and envious enchanters;--but pardon me for having broken the promise we made not to interrupt your discourse; for when I hear chivalry or knights-errant mentioned, I can no more help talking about them than the rays of the sun can help giving heat, or those of the moon moisture; pardon me, therefore, and proceed, for that is more to the purpose now.”\n\nWhile Don Quixote was saying this, Cardenio allowed his head to fall upon his breast, and seemed plunged in deep thought; and though twice Don Quixote bade him go on with his story, he neither looked up nor uttered a word in reply; but after some time he raised his head and said, “I cannot get rid of the idea, nor will anyone in the world remove it, or make me think otherwise--and he would be a blockhead who would hold or believe anything else than that that arrant knave Master Elisabad made free with Queen Madasima.”\n\n“That is not true, by all that’s good,” said Don Quixote in high wrath, turning upon him angrily, as his way was; “and it is a very great slander, or rather villainy. Queen Madasima was a very illustrious lady, and it is not to be supposed that so exalted a princess would have made free with a quack; and whoever maintains the contrary lies like a great scoundrel, and I will give him to know it, on foot or on horseback, armed or unarmed, by night or by day, or as he likes best.”\n\nCardenio was looking at him steadily, and his mad fit having now come upon him, he had no disposition to go on with his story, nor would Don Quixote have listened to it, so much had what he had heard about Madasima disgusted him. Strange to say, he stood up for her as if she were in earnest his veritable born lady; to such a pass had his unholy books brought him. Cardenio, then, being, as I said, now mad, when he heard himself given the lie, and called a scoundrel and other insulting names, not relishing the jest, snatched up a stone that he found near him, and with it delivered such a blow on Don Quixote’s breast that he laid him on his back. Sancho Panza, seeing his master treated in this fashion, attacked the madman with his closed fist; but the Ragged One received him in such a way that with a blow of his fist he stretched him at his feet, and then mounting upon him crushed his ribs to his own satisfaction; the goatherd, who came to the rescue, shared the same fate; and having beaten and pummelled them all he left them and quietly withdrew to his hiding-place on the mountain. Sancho rose, and with the rage he felt at finding himself so belaboured without deserving it, ran to take vengeance on the goatherd, accusing him of not giving them warning that this man was at times taken with a mad fit, for if they had known it they would have been on their guard to protect themselves. The goatherd replied that he had said so, and that if he had not heard him, that was no fault of his. Sancho retorted, and the goatherd rejoined, and the altercation ended in their seizing each other by the beard, and exchanging such fisticuffs that if Don Quixote had not made peace between them, they would have knocked one another to pieces.\n\n“Leave me alone, Sir Knight of the Rueful Countenance,” said Sancho, grappling with the goatherd, “for of this fellow, who is a clown like myself, and no dubbed knight, I can safely take satisfaction for the affront he has offered me, fighting with him hand to hand like an honest man.”\n\n“That is true,” said Don Quixote, “but I know that he is not to blame for what has happened.”\n\nWith this he pacified them, and again asked the goatherd if it would be possible to find Cardenio, as he felt the greatest anxiety to know the end of his story. The goatherd told him, as he had told him before, that there was no knowing of a certainty where his lair was; but that if he wandered about much in that neighbourhood he could not fail to fall in with him either in or out of his senses.\n\n# CHAPTER XXV.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF THE STRANGE THINGS THAT HAPPENED TO THE STOUT KNIGHT OF LA MANCHA IN THE SIERRA MORENA, AND OF HIS IMITATION OF THE PENANCE OF BELTENEBROS\n\nDon Quixote took leave of the goatherd, and once more mounting Rocinante bade Sancho follow him, which he having no ass, did very discontentedly. They proceeded slowly, making their way into the most rugged part of the mountain, Sancho all the while dying to have a talk with his master, and longing for him to begin, so that there should be no breach of the injunction laid upon him; but unable to keep silence so long he said to him:\n\n“Señor Don Quixote, give me your worship’s blessing and dismissal, for I’d like to go home at once to my wife and children with whom I can at any rate talk and converse as much as I like; for to want me to go through these solitudes day and night and not speak to you when I have a mind is burying me alive. If luck would have it that animals spoke as they did in the days of Guisopete, it would not be so bad, because I could talk to Rocinante about whatever came into my head, and so put up with my ill-fortune; but it is a hard case, and not to be borne with patience, to go seeking adventures all one’s life and get nothing but kicks and blanketings, brickbats and punches, and with all this to have to sew up one’s mouth without daring to say what is in one’s heart, just as if one were dumb.”\n\n“I understand thee, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote; “thou art dying to have the interdict I placed upon thy tongue removed; consider it removed, and say what thou wilt while we are wandering in these mountains.”\n\n“So be it,” said Sancho; “let me speak now, for God knows what will happen by-and-by; and to take advantage of the permit at once, I ask, what made your worship stand up so for that Queen Majimasa, or whatever her name is, or what did it matter whether that abbot was a friend of hers or not? for if your worship had let that pass--and you were not a judge in the matter--it is my belief the madman would have gone on with his story, and the blow of the stone, and the kicks, and more than half a dozen cuffs would have been escaped.”\n\n“In faith, Sancho,” answered Don Quixote, “if thou knewest as I do what an honourable and illustrious lady Queen Madasima was, I know thou wouldst say I had great patience that I did not break in pieces the mouth that uttered such blasphemies, for a very great blasphemy it is to say or imagine that a queen has made free with a surgeon. The truth of the story is that that Master Elisabad whom the madman mentioned was a man of great prudence and sound judgment, and served as governor and physician to the queen, but to suppose that she was his mistress is nonsense deserving very severe punishment; and as a proof that Cardenio did not know what he was saying, remember when he said it he was out of his wits.”\n\n“That is what I say,” said Sancho; “there was no occasion for minding the words of a madman; for if good luck had not helped your worship, and he had sent that stone at your head instead of at your breast, a fine way we should have been in for standing up for my lady yonder, God confound her! And then, would not Cardenio have gone free as a madman?”\n\n“Against men in their senses or against madmen,” said Don Quixote, “every knight-errant is bound to stand up for the honour of women, whoever they may be, much more for queens of such high degree and dignity as Queen Madasima, for whom I have a particular regard on account of her amiable qualities; for, besides being extremely beautiful, she was very wise, and very patient under her misfortunes, of which she had many; and the counsel and society of the Master Elisabad were a great help and support to her in enduring her afflictions with wisdom and resignation; hence the ignorant and ill-disposed vulgar took occasion to say and think that she was his mistress; and they lie, I say it once more, and will lie two hundred times more, all who think and say so.”\n\n“I neither say nor think so,” said Sancho; “let them look to it; with their bread let them eat it; they have rendered account to God whether they misbehaved or not; I come from my vineyard, I know nothing; I am not fond of prying into other men’s lives; he who buys and lies feels it in his purse; moreover, naked was I born, naked I find myself, I neither lose nor gain; but if they did, what is that to me? many think there are flitches where there are no hooks; but who can put gates to the open plain? moreover they said of God—”\n\n“God bless me,” said Don Quixote, “what a set of absurdities thou art stringing together! What has what we are talking about got to do with the proverbs thou art threading one after the other? for God’s sake hold thy tongue, Sancho, and henceforward keep to prodding thy ass and don’t meddle in what does not concern thee; and understand with all thy five senses that everything I have done, am doing, or shall do, is well founded on reason and in conformity with the rules of chivalry, for I understand them better than all the world that profess them.”\n\n“Señor,” replied Sancho, “is it a good rule of chivalry that we should go astray through these mountains without path or road, looking for a madman who when he is found will perhaps take a fancy to finish what he began, not his story, but your worship’s head and my ribs, and end by breaking them altogether for us?”\n\n“Peace, I say again, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “for let me tell thee it is not so much the desire of finding that madman that leads me into these regions as that which I have of performing among them an achievement wherewith I shall win eternal name and fame throughout the known world; and it shall be such that I shall thereby set the seal on all that can make a knight-errant perfect and famous.”\n\n“And is it very perilous, this achievement?”\n\n“No,” replied he of the Rueful Countenance; “though it may be in the dice that we may throw deuce-ace instead of sixes; but all will depend on thy diligence.”\n\n“On my diligence!” said Sancho.\n\n“Yes,” said Don Quixote, “for if thou dost return soon from the place where I mean to send thee, my penance will be soon over, and my glory will soon begin. But as it is not right to keep thee any longer in suspense, waiting to see what comes of my words, I would have thee know, Sancho, that the famous Amadis of Gaul was one of the most perfect knights-errant--I am wrong to say he was one; he stood alone, the first, the only one, the lord of all that were in the world in his time. A fig for Don Belianis, and for all who say he equalled him in any respect, for, my oath upon it, they are deceiving themselves! I say, too, that when a painter desires to become famous in his art he endeavours to copy the originals of the rarest painters that he knows; and the same rule holds good for all the most important crafts and callings that serve to adorn a state; thus must he who would be esteemed prudent and patient imitate Ulysses, in whose person and labours Homer presents to us a lively picture of prudence and patience; as Virgil, too, shows us in the person of Æneas the virtue of a pious son and the sagacity of a brave and skilful captain; not representing or describing them as they were, but as they ought to be, so as to leave the example of their virtues to posterity. In the same way Amadis was the polestar, day-star, sun of valiant and devoted knights, whom all we who fight under the banner of love and chivalry are bound to imitate. This, then, being so, I consider, friend Sancho, that the knight-errant who shall imitate him most closely will come nearest to reaching the perfection of chivalry. Now one of the instances in which this knight most conspicuously showed his prudence, worth, valour, endurance, fortitude, and love, was when he withdrew, rejected by the Lady Oriana, to do penance upon the Pena Pobre, changing his name into that of Beltenebros, a name assuredly significant and appropriate to the life which he had voluntarily adopted. So, as it is easier for me to imitate him in this than in cleaving giants asunder, cutting off serpents’ heads, slaying dragons, routing armies, destroying fleets, and breaking enchantments, and as this place is so well suited for a similar purpose, I must not allow the opportunity to escape which now so conveniently offers me its forelock.”\n\n“What is it in reality,” said Sancho, “that your worship means to do in such an out-of-the-way place as this?”\n\n“Have I not told thee,” answered Don Quixote, “that I mean to imitate Amadis here, playing the victim of despair, the madman, the maniac, so as at the same time to imitate the valiant Don Roland, when at the fountain he had evidence of the fair Angelica having disgraced herself with Medoro and through grief thereat went mad, and plucked up trees, troubled the waters of the clear springs, slew shepherds, destroyed flocks, burned down huts, levelled houses, dragged mares after him, and perpetrated a hundred thousand other outrages worthy of everlasting renown and record? And though I have no intention of imitating Roland, or Orlando, or Rotolando (for he went by all these names), step by step in all the mad things he did, said, and thought, I will make a rough copy to the best of my power of all that seems to me most essential; but perhaps I shall content myself with the simple imitation of Amadis, who without giving way to any mischievous madness but merely to tears and sorrow, gained as much fame as the most famous.”\n\n“It seems to me,” said Sancho, “that the knights who behaved in this way had provocation and cause for those follies and penances; but what cause has your worship for going mad? What lady has rejected you, or what evidence have you found to prove that the lady Dulcinea del Toboso has been trifling with Moor or Christian?”\n\n“There is the point,” replied Don Quixote, “and that is the beauty of this business of mine; no thanks to a knight-errant for going mad when he has cause; the thing is to turn crazy without any provocation, and let my lady know, if I do this in the dry, what I would do in the moist; moreover I have abundant cause in the long separation I have endured from my lady till death, Dulcinea del Toboso; for as thou didst hear that shepherd Ambrosio say the other day, in absence all ills are felt and feared; and so, friend Sancho, waste no time in advising me against so rare, so happy, and so unheard-of an imitation; mad I am, and mad I must be until thou returnest with the answer to a letter that I mean to send by thee to my lady Dulcinea; and if it be such as my constancy deserves, my insanity and penance will come to an end; and if it be to the opposite effect, I shall become mad in earnest, and, being so, I shall suffer no more; thus in whatever way she may answer I shall escape from the struggle and affliction in which thou wilt leave me, enjoying in my senses the boon thou bearest me, or as a madman not feeling the evil thou bringest me. But tell me, Sancho, hast thou got Mambrino’s helmet safe? for I saw thee take it up from the ground when that ungrateful wretch tried to break it in pieces but could not, by which the fineness of its temper may be seen.”\n\nTo which Sancho made answer, “By the living God, Sir Knight of the Rueful Countenance, I cannot endure or bear with patience some of the things that your worship says; and from them I begin to suspect that all you tell me about chivalry, and winning kingdoms and empires, and giving islands, and bestowing other rewards and dignities after the custom of knights-errant, must be all made up of wind and lies, and all pigments or figments, or whatever we may call them; for what would anyone think that heard your worship calling a barber’s basin Mambrino’s helmet without ever seeing the mistake all this time, but that one who says and maintains such things must have his brains addled? I have the basin in my sack all dinted, and I am taking it home to have it mended, to trim my beard in it, if, by God’s grace, I am allowed to see my wife and children some day or other.”\n\n“Look here, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “by him thou didst swear by just now I swear thou hast the most limited understanding that any squire in the world has or ever had. Is it possible that all this time thou hast been going about with me thou hast never found out that all things belonging to knights-errant seem to be illusions and nonsense and ravings, and to go always by contraries? And not because it really is so, but because there is always a swarm of enchanters in attendance upon us that change and alter everything with us, and turn things as they please, and according as they are disposed to aid or destroy us; thus what seems to thee a barber’s basin seems to me Mambrino’s helmet, and to another it will seem something else; and rare foresight it was in the sage who is on my side to make what is really and truly Mambrine’s helmet seem a basin to everybody, for, being held in such estimation as it is, all the world would pursue me to rob me of it; but when they see it is only a barber’s basin they do not take the trouble to obtain it; as was plainly shown by him who tried to break it, and left it on the ground without taking it, for, by my faith, had he known it he would never have left it behind. Keep it safe, my friend, for just now I have no need of it; indeed, I shall have to take off all this armour and remain as naked as I was born, if I have a mind to follow Roland rather than Amadis in my penance.”\n\nThus talking they reached the foot of a high mountain which stood like an isolated peak among the others that surrounded it. Past its base there flowed a gentle brook, all around it spread a meadow so green and luxuriant that it was a delight to the eyes to look upon it, and forest trees in abundance, and shrubs and flowers, added to the charms of the spot. Upon this place the Knight of the Rueful Countenance fixed his choice for the performance of his penance, and as he beheld it exclaimed in a loud voice as though he were out of his senses:\n\n“This is the place, oh, ye heavens, that I select and choose for bewailing the misfortune in which ye yourselves have plunged me: this is the spot where the overflowings of mine eyes shall swell the waters of yon little brook, and my deep and endless sighs shall stir unceasingly the leaves of these mountain trees, in testimony and token of the pain my persecuted heart is suffering. Oh, ye rural deities, whoever ye be that haunt this lone spot, give ear to the complaint of a wretched lover whom long absence and brooding jealousy have driven to bewail his fate among these wilds and complain of the hard heart of that fair and ungrateful one, the end and limit of all human beauty! Oh, ye wood nymphs and dryads, that dwell in the thickets of the forest, so may the nimble wanton satyrs by whom ye are vainly wooed never disturb your sweet repose, help me to lament my hard fate or at least weary not at listening to it! Oh, Dulcinea del Toboso, day of my night, glory of my pain, guide of my path, star of my fortune, so may Heaven grant thee in full all thou seekest of it, bethink thee of the place and condition to which absence from thee has brought me, and make that return in kindness that is due to my fidelity! Oh, lonely trees, that from this day forward shall bear me company in my solitude, give me some sign by the gentle movement of your boughs that my presence is not distasteful to you! Oh, thou, my squire, pleasant companion in my prosperous and adverse fortunes, fix well in thy memory what thou shalt see me do here, so that thou mayest relate and report it to the sole cause of all,” and so saying he dismounted from Rocinante, and in an instant relieved him of saddle and bridle, and giving him a slap on the croup, said, “He gives thee freedom who is bereft of it himself, oh steed as excellent in deed as thou art unfortunate in thy lot; begone where thou wilt, for thou bearest written on thy forehead that neither Astolfo’s hippogriff, nor the famed Frontino that cost Bradamante so dear, could equal thee in speed.”\n\nSeeing this Sancho said, “Good luck to him who has saved us the trouble of stripping the pack-saddle off Dapple! By my faith he would not have gone without a slap on the croup and something said in his praise; though if he were here I would not let anyone strip him, for there would be no occasion, as he had nothing of the lover or victim of despair about him, inasmuch as his master, which I was while it was God’s pleasure, was nothing of the sort; and indeed, Sir Knight of the Rueful Countenance, if my departure and your worship’s madness are to come off in earnest, it will be as well to saddle Rocinante again in order that he may supply the want of Dapple, because it will save me time in going and returning: for if I go on foot I don’t know when I shall get there or when I shall get back, as I am, in truth, a bad walker.”\n\n“I declare, Sancho,” returned Don Quixote, “it shall be as thou wilt, for thy plan does not seem to me a bad one, and three days hence thou wilt depart, for I wish thee to observe in the meantime what I do and say for her sake, that thou mayest be able to tell it.”\n\n“But what more have I to see besides what I have seen?” said Sancho.\n\n“Much thou knowest about it!” said Don Quixote. “I have now got to tear up my garments, to scatter about my armour, knock my head against these rocks, and more of the same sort of thing, which thou must witness.”\n\n“For the love of God,” said Sancho, “be careful, your worship, how you give yourself those knocks on the head, for you may come across such a rock, and in such a way, that the very first may put an end to the whole contrivance of this penance; and I should think, if indeed knocks on the head seem necessary to you, and this business cannot be done without them, you might be content--as the whole thing is feigned, and counterfeit, and in joke--you might be content, I say, with giving them to yourself in the water, or against something soft, like cotton; and leave it all to me; for I’ll tell my lady that your worship knocked your head against a point of rock harder than a diamond.”\n\n“I thank thee for thy good intentions, friend Sancho,” answered Don Quixote, “but I would have thee know that all these things I am doing are not in joke, but very much in earnest, for anything else would be a transgression of the ordinances of chivalry, which forbid us to tell any lie whatever under the penalties due to apostasy; and to do one thing instead of another is just the same as lying; so my knocks on the head must be real, solid, and valid, without anything sophisticated or fanciful about them, and it will be needful to leave me some lint to dress my wounds, since fortune has compelled us to do without the balsam we lost.”\n\n“It was worse losing the ass,” replied Sancho, “for with him lint and all were lost; but I beg of your worship not to remind me again of that accursed liquor, for my soul, not to say my stomach, turns at hearing the very name of it; and I beg of you, too, to reckon as past the three days you allowed me for seeing the mad things you do, for I take them as seen already and pronounced upon, and I will tell wonderful stories to my lady; so write the letter and send me off at once, for I long to return and take your worship out of this purgatory where I am leaving you.”\n\n“Purgatory dost thou call it, Sancho?” said Don Quixote, “rather call it hell, or even worse if there be anything worse.”\n\n“For one who is in hell,” said Sancho, “nulla est retentio, as I have heard say.”\n\n“I do not understand what retentio means,” said Don Quixote.\n\n“Retentio,” answered Sancho, “means that whoever is in hell never comes nor can come out of it, which will be the opposite case with your worship or my legs will be idle, that is if I have spurs to enliven Rocinante: let me once get to El Toboso and into the presence of my lady Dulcinea, and I will tell her such things of the follies and madnesses (for it is all one) that your worship has done and is still doing, that I will manage to make her softer than a glove though I find her harder than a cork tree; and with her sweet and honeyed answer I will come back through the air like a witch, and take your worship out of this purgatory that seems to be hell but is not, as there is hope of getting out of it; which, as I have said, those in hell have not, and I believe your worship will not say anything to the contrary.”\n\n“That is true,” said he of the Rueful Countenance, “but how shall we manage to write the letter?”\n\n“And the ass-colt order too,” added Sancho.\n\n“All shall be included,” said Don Quixote; “and as there is no paper, it would be well done to write it on the leaves of trees, as the ancients did, or on tablets of wax; though that would be as hard to find just now as paper. But it has just occurred to me how it may be conveniently and even more than conveniently written, and that is in the note-book that belonged to Cardenio, and thou wilt take care to have it copied on paper, in a good hand, at the first village thou comest to where there is a schoolmaster, or if not, any sacristan will copy it; but see thou give it not to any notary to copy, for they write a law hand that Satan could not make out.”\n\n“But what is to be done about the signature?” said Sancho.\n\n“The letters of Amadis were never signed,” said Don Quixote.\n\n“That is all very well,” said Sancho, “but the order must needs be signed, and if it is copied they will say the signature is false, and I shall be left without ass-colts.”\n\n“The order shall go signed in the same book,” said Don Quixote, “and on seeing it my niece will make no difficulty about obeying it; as to the loveletter thou canst put by way of signature, ‘Yours till death, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance.’ And it will be no great matter if it is in some other person’s hand, for as well as I recollect Dulcinea can neither read nor write, nor in the whole course of her life has she seen handwriting or letter of mine, for my love and hers have been always platonic, not going beyond a modest look, and even that so seldom that I can safely swear I have not seen her four times in all these twelve years I have been loving her more than the light of these eyes that the earth will one day devour; and perhaps even of those four times she has not once perceived that I was looking at her: such is the retirement and seclusion in which her father Lorenzo Corchuelo and her mother Aldonza Nogales have brought her up.”\n\n“So, so!” said Sancho; “Lorenzo Corchuelo’s daughter is the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, otherwise called Aldonza Lorenzo?”\n\n“She it is,” said Don Quixote, “and she it is that is worthy to be lady of the whole universe.”\n\n“I know her well,” said Sancho, “and let me tell you she can fling a crowbar as well as the lustiest lad in all the town. Giver of all good! but she is a brave lass, and a right and stout one, and fit to be helpmate to any knight-errant that is or is to be, who may make her his lady: the whoreson wench, what sting she has and what a voice! I can tell you one day she posted herself on the top of the belfry of the village to call some labourers of theirs that were in a ploughed field of her father’s, and though they were better than half a league off they heard her as well as if they were at the foot of the tower; and the best of her is that she is not a bit prudish, for she has plenty of affability, and jokes with everybody, and has a grin and a jest for everything. So, Sir Knight of the Rueful Countenance, I say you not only may and ought to do mad freaks for her sake, but you have a good right to give way to despair and hang yourself; and no one who knows of it but will say you did well, though the devil should take you; and I wish I were on my road already, simply to see her, for it is many a day since I saw her, and she must be altered by this time, for going about the fields always, and the sun and the air spoil women’s looks greatly. But I must own the truth to your worship, Señor Don Quixote; until now I have been under a great mistake, for I believed truly and honestly that the lady Dulcinea must be some princess your worship was in love with, or some person great enough to deserve the rich presents you have sent her, such as the Biscayan and the galley slaves, and many more no doubt, for your worship must have won many victories in the time when I was not yet your squire. But all things considered, what good can it do the lady Aldonza Lorenzo, I mean the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, to have the vanquished your worship sends or will send coming to her and going down on their knees before her? Because may be when they came she’d be hackling flax or threshing on the threshing floor, and they’d be ashamed to see her, and she’d laugh, or resent the present.”\n\n“I have before now told thee many times, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “that thou art a mighty great chatterer, and that with a blunt wit thou art always striving at sharpness; but to show thee what a fool thou art and how rational I am, I would have thee listen to a short story. Thou must know that a certain widow, fair, young, independent, and rich, and above all free and easy, fell in love with a sturdy strapping young lay-brother; his superior came to know of it, and one day said to the worthy widow by way of brotherly remonstrance, ‘I am surprised, señora, and not without good reason, that a woman of such high standing, so fair, and so rich as you are, should have fallen in love with such a mean, low, stupid fellow as So-and-so, when in this house there are so many masters, graduates, and divinity students from among whom you might choose as if they were a lot of pears, saying this one I’ll take, that I won’t take;’ but she replied to him with great sprightliness and candour, ‘My dear sir, you are very much mistaken, and your ideas are very old-fashioned, if you think that I have made a bad choice in So-and-so, fool as he seems; because for all I want with him he knows as much and more philosophy than Aristotle.’ In the same way, Sancho, for all I want with Dulcinea del Toboso she is just as good as the most exalted princess on earth. It is not to be supposed that all those poets who sang the praises of ladies under the fancy names they give them, had any such mistresses. Thinkest thou that the Amarillises, the Phillises, the Sylvias, the Dianas, the Galateas, the Filidas, and all the rest of them, that the books, the ballads, the barber’s shops, the theatres are full of, were really and truly ladies of flesh and blood, and mistresses of those that glorify and have glorified them? Nothing of the kind; they only invent them for the most part to furnish a subject for their verses, and that they may pass for lovers, or for men valiant enough to be so; and so it suffices me to think and believe that the good Aldonza Lorenzo is fair and virtuous; and as to her pedigree it is very little matter, for no one will examine into it for the purpose of conferring any order upon her, and I, for my part, reckon her the most exalted princess in the world. For thou shouldst know, Sancho, if thou dost not know, that two things alone beyond all others are incentives to love, and these are great beauty and a good name, and these two things are to be found in Dulcinea in the highest degree, for in beauty no one equals her and in good name few approach her; and to put the whole thing in a nutshell, I persuade myself that all I say is as I say, neither more nor less, and I picture her in my imagination as I would have her to be, as well in beauty as in condition; Helen approaches her not nor does Lucretia come up to her, nor any other of the famous women of times past, Greek, Barbarian, or Latin; and let each say what he will, for if in this I am taken to task by the ignorant, I shall not be censured by the critical.”\n\n“I say that your worship is entirely right,” said Sancho, “and that I am an ass. But I know not how the name of ass came into my mouth, for a rope is not to be mentioned in the house of him who has been hanged; but now for the letter, and then, God be with you, I am off.”\n\nDon Quixote took out the note-book, and, retiring to one side, very deliberately began to write the letter, and when he had finished it he called to Sancho, saying he wished to read it to him, so that he might commit it to memory, in case of losing it on the road; for with evil fortune like his anything might be apprehended. To which Sancho replied, “Write it two or three times there in the book and give it to me, and I will carry it very carefully, because to expect me to keep it in my memory is all nonsense, for I have such a bad one that I often forget my own name; but for all that repeat it to me, as I shall like to hear it, for surely it will run as if it was in print.”\n\n“Listen,” said Don Quixote, “this is what it says:\n\n“Sovereign and exalted Lady,--The pierced by the point of absence, the wounded to the heart’s core, sends thee, sweetest Dulcinea del Toboso, the health that he himself enjoys not. If thy beauty despises me, if thy worth is not for me, if thy scorn is my affliction, though I be sufficiently long-suffering, hardly shall I endure this anxiety, which, besides being oppressive, is protracted. My good squire Sancho will relate to thee in full, fair ingrate, dear enemy, the condition to which I am reduced on thy account: if it be thy pleasure to give me relief, I am thine; if not, do as may be pleasing to thee; for by ending my life I shall satisfy thy cruelty and my desire.\n\n“Thine till death,\n\n“The Knight of the Rueful Countenance.”\n\n“By the life of my father,” said Sancho, when he heard the letter, “it is the loftiest thing I ever heard. Body of me! how your worship says everything as you like in it! And how well you fit in ‘The Knight of the Rueful Countenance’ into the signature. I declare your worship is indeed the very devil, and there is nothing you don’t know.”\n\n“Everything is needed for the calling I follow,” said Don Quixote.\n\n“Now then,” said Sancho, “let your worship put the order for the three ass-colts on the other side, and sign it very plainly, that they may recognise it at first sight.”\n\n“With all my heart,” said Don Quixote, and as he had written it he read it to this effect:\n\n“Mistress Niece,--By this first of ass-colts please pay to Sancho Panza, my squire, three of the five I left at home in your charge: said three ass-colts to be paid and delivered for the same number received here in hand, which upon this and upon his receipt shall be duly paid. Done in the heart of the Sierra Morena, the twenty-seventh of August of this present year.”\n\n“That will do,” said Sancho; “now let your worship sign it.”\n\n“There is no need to sign it,” said Don Quixote, “but merely to put my flourish, which is the same as a signature, and enough for three asses, or even three hundred.”\n\n“I can trust your worship,” returned Sancho; “let me go and saddle Rocinante, and be ready to give me your blessing, for I mean to go at once without seeing the fooleries your worship is going to do; I’ll say I saw you do so many that she will not want any more.”\n\n“At any rate, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “I should like--and there is reason for it--I should like thee, I say, to see me stripped to the skin and performing a dozen or two of insanities, which I can get done in less than half an hour; for having seen them with thine own eyes, thou canst then safely swear to the rest that thou wouldst add; and I promise thee thou wilt not tell of as many as I mean to perform.”\n\n“For the love of God, master mine,” said Sancho, “let me not see your worship stripped, for it will sorely grieve me, and I shall not be able to keep from tears, and my head aches so with all I shed last night for Dapple, that I am not fit to begin any fresh weeping; but if it is your worship’s pleasure that I should see some insanities, do them in your clothes, short ones, and such as come readiest to hand; for I myself want nothing of the sort, and, as I have said, it will be a saving of time for my return, which will be with the news your worship desires and deserves. If not, let the lady Dulcinea look to it; if she does not answer reasonably, I swear as solemnly as I can that I will fetch a fair answer out of her stomach with kicks and cuffs; for why should it be borne that a knight-errant as famous as your worship should go mad without rhyme or reason for a--? Her ladyship had best not drive me to say it, for by God I will speak out and let off everything cheap, even if it doesn’t sell: I am pretty good at that! she little knows me; faith, if she knew me she’d be in awe of me.”\n\n“In faith, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “to all appearance thou art no sounder in thy wits than I.”\n\n“I am not so mad,” answered Sancho, “but I am more peppery; but apart from all this, what has your worship to eat until I come back? Will you sally out on the road like Cardenio to force it from the shepherds?”\n\n“Let not that anxiety trouble thee,” replied Don Quixote, “for even if I had it I should not eat anything but the herbs and the fruits which this meadow and these trees may yield me; the beauty of this business of mine lies in not eating, and in performing other mortifications.”\n\n“Do you know what I am afraid of?” said Sancho upon this; “that I shall not be able to find my way back to this spot where I am leaving you, it is such an out-of-the-way place.”\n\n“Observe the landmarks well,” said Don Quixote, “for I will try not to go far from this neighbourhood, and I will even take care to mount the highest of these rocks to see if I can discover thee returning; however, not to miss me and lose thyself, the best plan will be to cut some branches of the broom that is so abundant about here, and as thou goest to lay them at intervals until thou hast come out upon the plain; these will serve thee, after the fashion of the clue in the labyrinth of Theseus, as marks and signs for finding me on thy return.”\n\n“So I will,” said Sancho Panza, and having cut some, he asked his master’s blessing, and not without many tears on both sides, took his leave of him, and mounting Rocinante, of whom Don Quixote charged him earnestly to have as much care as of his own person, he set out for the plain, strewing at intervals the branches of broom as his master had recommended him; and so he went his way, though Don Quixote still entreated him to see him do were it only a couple of mad acts. He had not gone a hundred paces, however, when he returned and said:\n\n“I must say, señor, your worship said quite right, that in order to be able to swear without a weight on my conscience that I had seen you do mad things, it would be well for me to see if it were only one; though in your worship’s remaining here I have seen a very great one.”\n\n“Did I not tell thee so?” said Don Quixote. “Wait, Sancho, and I will do them in the saying of a credo,” and pulling off his breeches in all haste he stripped himself to his skin and his shirt, and then, without more ado, he cut a couple of gambados in the air, and a couple of somersaults, heels over head, making such a display that, not to see it a second time, Sancho wheeled Rocinante round, and felt easy, and satisfied in his mind that he could swear he had left his master mad; and so we will leave him to follow his road until his return, which was a quick one.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVI.\n\nIN WHICH ARE CONTINUED THE REFINEMENTS WHEREWITH DON QUIXOTE PLAYED THE PART OF A LOVER IN THE SIERRA MORENA\nReturning to the proceedings of him of the Rueful Countenance when he found himself alone, the history says that when Don Quixote had completed the performance of the somersaults or capers, naked from the waist down and clothed from the waist up, and saw that Sancho had gone off without waiting to see any more crazy feats, he climbed up to the top of a high rock, and there set himself to consider what he had several times before considered without ever coming to any conclusion on the point, namely whether it would be better and more to his purpose to imitate the outrageous madness of Roland, or the melancholy madness of Amadis; and communing with himself he said:\n\n“What wonder is it if Roland was so good a knight and so valiant as everyone says he was, when, after all, he was enchanted, and nobody could kill him save by thrusting a corking pin into the sole of his foot, and he always wore shoes with seven iron soles? Though cunning devices did not avail him against Bernardo del Carpio, who knew all about them, and strangled him in his arms at Roncesvalles. But putting the question of his valour aside, let us come to his losing his wits, for certain it is that he did lose them in consequence of the proofs he discovered at the fountain, and the intelligence the shepherd gave him of Angelica having slept more than two siestas with Medoro, a little curly-headed Moor, and page to Agramante. If he was persuaded that this was true, and that his lady had wronged him, it is no wonder that he should have gone mad; but I, how am I to imitate him in his madness, unless I can imitate him in the cause of it? For my Dulcinea, I will venture to swear, never saw a Moor in her life, as he is, in his proper costume, and she is this day as the mother that bore her, and I should plainly be doing her a wrong if, fancying anything else, I were to go mad with the same kind of madness as Roland the Furious. On the other hand, I see that Amadis of Gaul, without losing his senses and without doing anything mad, acquired as a lover as much fame as the most famous; for, according to his history, on finding himself rejected by his lady Oriana, who had ordered him not to appear in her presence until it should be her pleasure, all he did was to retire to the Pena Pobre in company with a hermit, and there he took his fill of weeping until Heaven sent him relief in the midst of his great grief and need. And if this be true, as it is, why should I now take the trouble to strip stark naked, or do mischief to these trees which have done me no harm, or why am I to disturb the clear waters of these brooks which will give me to drink whenever I have a mind? Long live the memory of Amadis and let him be imitated so far as is possible by Don Quixote of La Mancha, of whom it will be said, as was said of the other, that if he did not achieve great things, he died in attempting them; and if I am not repulsed or rejected by my Dulcinea, it is enough for me, as I have said, to be absent from her. And so, now to business; come to my memory ye deeds of Amadis, and show me how I am to begin to imitate you. I know already that what he chiefly did was to pray and commend himself to God; but what am I to do for a rosary, for I have not got one?”\n\nAnd then it occurred to him how he might make one, and that was by tearing a great strip off the tail of his shirt which hung down, and making eleven knots on it, one bigger than the rest, and this served him for a rosary all the time he was there, during which he repeated countless ave-marias. But what distressed him greatly was not having another hermit there to confess him and receive consolation from; and so he solaced himself with pacing up and down the little meadow, and writing and carving on the bark of the trees and on the fine sand a multitude of verses all in harmony with his sadness, and some in praise of Dulcinea; but, when he was found there afterwards, the only ones completely legible that could be discovered were those that follow here:\n\nYe on the mountain side that grow, Ye green things all, trees, shrubs, and bushes, Are ye aweary of the woe That this poor aching bosom crushes? If it disturb you, and I owe Some reparation, it may be a Defence for me to let you know Don Quixote’s tears are on the flow, And all for distant Dulcinea Del Toboso.\n\nThe lealest lover time can show, Doomed for a lady-love to languish, Among these solitudes doth go, A prey to every kind of anguish. Why Love should like a spiteful foe Thus use him, he hath no idea, But hogsheads full--this doth he know-- Don Quixote’s tears are on the flow, And all for distant Dulcinea Del Toboso.\n\nAdventure-seeking doth he go Up rugged heights, down rocky valleys, But hill or dale, or high or low, Mishap attendeth all his sallies: Love still pursues him to and fro, And plies his cruel scourge--ah me! a Relentless fate, an endless woe; Don Quixote’s tears are on the flow, And all for distant Dulcinea Del Toboso.\n\nThe addition of “Del Toboso” to Dulcinea’s name gave rise to no little laughter among those who found the above lines, for they suspected Don Quixote must have fancied that unless he added “del Toboso” when he introduced the name of Dulcinea the verse would be unintelligible; which was indeed the fact, as he himself afterwards admitted. He wrote many more, but, as has been said, these three verses were all that could be plainly and perfectly deciphered. In this way, and in sighing and calling on the fauns and satyrs of the woods and the nymphs of the streams, and Echo, moist and mournful, to answer, console, and hear him, as well as in looking for herbs to sustain him, he passed his time until Sancho’s return; and had that been delayed three weeks, as it was three days, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance would have worn such an altered countenance that the mother that bore him would not have known him: and here it will be well to leave him, wrapped up in sighs and verses, to relate how Sancho Panza fared on his mission.\n\nAs for him, coming out upon the high road, he made for El Toboso, and the next day reached the inn where the mishap of the blanket had befallen him. As soon as he recognised it he felt as if he were once more living through the air, and he could not bring himself to enter it though it was an hour when he might well have done so, for it was dinner-time, and he longed to taste something hot as it had been all cold fare with him for many days past. This craving drove him to draw near to the inn, still undecided whether to go in or not, and as he was hesitating there came out two persons who at once recognised him, and said one to the other:\n\n“Señor licentiate, is not he on the horse there Sancho Panza who, our adventurer’s housekeeper told us, went off with her master as esquire?”\n\n“So it is,” said the licentiate, “and that is our friend Don Quixote’s horse;” and if they knew him so well it was because they were the curate and the barber of his own village, the same who had carried out the scrutiny and sentence upon the books; and as soon as they recognised Sancho Panza and Rocinante, being anxious to hear of Don Quixote, they approached, and calling him by his name the curate said, “Friend Sancho Panza, where is your master?”\n\nSancho recognised them at once, and determined to keep secret the place and circumstances where and under which he had left his master, so he replied that his master was engaged in a certain quarter on a certain matter of great importance to him which he could not disclose for the eyes in his head.\n\n“Nay, nay,” said the barber, “if you don’t tell us where he is, Sancho Panza, we will suspect as we suspect already, that you have murdered and robbed him, for here you are mounted on his horse; in fact, you must produce the master of the hack, or else take the consequences.”\n\n“There is no need of threats with me,” said Sancho, “for I am not a man to rob or murder anybody; let his own fate, or God who made him, kill each one; my master is engaged very much to his taste doing penance in the midst of these mountains;” and then, offhand and without stopping, he told them how he had left him, what adventures had befallen him, and how he was carrying a letter to the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, the daughter of Lorenzo Corchuelo, with whom he was over head and ears in love. They were both amazed at what Sancho Panza told them; for though they were aware of Don Quixote’s madness and the nature of it, each time they heard of it they were filled with fresh wonder. They then asked Sancho Panza to show them the letter he was carrying to the lady Dulcinea del Toboso. He said it was written in a note-book, and that his master’s directions were that he should have it copied on paper at the first village he came to. On this the curate said if he showed it to him, he himself would make a fair copy of it. Sancho put his hand into his bosom in search of the note-book but could not find it, nor, if he had been searching until now, could he have found it, for Don Quixote had kept it, and had never given it to him, nor had he himself thought of asking for it. When Sancho discovered he could not find the book his face grew deadly pale, and in great haste he again felt his body all over, and seeing plainly it was not to be found, without more ado he seized his beard with both hands and plucked away half of it, and then, as quick as he could and without stopping, gave himself half a dozen cuffs on the face and nose till they were bathed in blood.\n\nSeeing this, the curate and the barber asked him what had happened him that he gave himself such rough treatment.\n\n“What should happen me?” replied Sancho, “but to have lost from one hand to the other, in a moment, three ass-colts, each of them like a castle?”\n\n“How is that?” said the barber.\n\n“I have lost the note-book,” said Sancho, “that contained the letter to Dulcinea, and an order signed by my master in which he directed his niece to give me three ass-colts out of four or five he had at home;” and he then told them about the loss of Dapple.\n\nThe curate consoled him, telling him that when his master was found he would get him to renew the order, and make a fresh draft on paper, as was usual and customary; for those made in notebooks were never accepted or honoured.\n\nSancho comforted himself with this, and said if that were so the loss of Dulcinea’s letter did not trouble him much, for he had it almost by heart, and it could be taken down from him wherever and whenever they liked.\n\n“Repeat it then, Sancho,” said the barber, “and we will write it down afterwards.”\n\nSancho Panza stopped to scratch his head to bring back the letter to his memory, and balanced himself now on one foot, now the other, one moment staring at the ground, the next at the sky, and after having half gnawed off the end of a finger and kept them in suspense waiting for him to begin, he said, after a long pause, “By God, señor licentiate, devil a thing can I recollect of the letter; but it said at the beginning, ‘Exalted and scrubbing Lady.’”\n\n“It cannot have said ‘scrubbing,’” said the barber, “but ‘superhuman’ or ‘sovereign.’”\n\n“That is it,” said Sancho; “then, as well as I remember, it went on, ‘The wounded, and wanting of sleep, and the pierced, kisses your worship’s hands, ungrateful and very unrecognised fair one; and it said something or other about health and sickness that he was sending her; and from that it went tailing off until it ended with ‘Yours till death, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance.”\n\nIt gave them no little amusement, both of them, to see what a good memory Sancho had, and they complimented him greatly upon it, and begged him to repeat the letter a couple of times more, so that they too might get it by heart to write it out by-and-by. Sancho repeated it three times, and as he did, uttered three thousand more absurdities; then he told them more about his master but he never said a word about the blanketing that had befallen himself in that inn, into which he refused to enter. He told them, moreover, how his lord, if he brought him a favourable answer from the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, was to put himself in the way of endeavouring to become an emperor, or at least a monarch; for it had been so settled between them, and with his personal worth and the might of his arm it was an easy matter to come to be one: and how on becoming one his lord was to make a marriage for him (for he would be a widower by that time, as a matter of course) and was to give him as a wife one of the damsels of the empress, the heiress of some rich and grand state on the mainland, having nothing to do with islands of any sort, for he did not care for them now. All this Sancho delivered with so much composure--wiping his nose from time to time--and with so little common-sense that his two hearers were again filled with wonder at the force of Don Quixote’s madness that could run away with this poor man’s reason. They did not care to take the trouble of disabusing him of his error, as they considered that since it did not in any way hurt his conscience it would be better to leave him in it, and they would have all the more amusement in listening to his simplicities; and so they bade him pray to God for his lord’s health, as it was a very likely and a very feasible thing for him in course of time to come to be an emperor, as he said, or at least an archbishop or some other dignitary of equal rank.\n\nTo which Sancho made answer, “If fortune, sirs, should bring things about in such a way that my master should have a mind, instead of being an emperor, to be an archbishop, I should like to know what archbishops-errant commonly give their squires?”\n\n“They commonly give them,” said the curate, some simple benefice or cure, or some place as sacristan which brings them a good fixed income, not counting the altar fees, which may be reckoned at as much more.”\n\n“But for that,” said Sancho, “the squire must be unmarried, and must know, at any rate, how to help at mass, and if that be so, woe is me, for I am married already and I don’t know the first letter of the A B C. What will become of me if my master takes a fancy to be an archbishop and not an emperor, as is usual and customary with knights-errant?”\n\n“Be not uneasy, friend Sancho,” said the barber, “for we will entreat your master, and advise him, even urging it upon him as a case of conscience, to become an emperor and not an archbishop, because it will be easier for him as he is more valiant than lettered.”\n\n“So I have thought,” said Sancho; “though I can tell you he is fit for anything: what I mean to do for my part is to pray to our Lord to place him where it may be best for him, and where he may be able to bestow most favours upon me.”\n\n“You speak like a man of sense,” said the curate, “and you will be acting like a good Christian; but what must now be done is to take steps to coax your master out of that useless penance you say he is performing; and we had best turn into this inn to consider what plan to adopt, and also to dine, for it is now time.”\n\nSancho said they might go in, but that he would wait there outside, and that he would tell them afterwards the reason why he was unwilling, and why it did not suit him to enter it; but he begged them to bring him out something to eat, and to let it be hot, and also to bring barley for Rocinante. They left him and went in, and presently the barber brought him out something to eat. By-and-by, after they had between them carefully thought over what they should do to carry out their object, the curate hit upon an idea very well adapted to humour Don Quixote, and effect their purpose; and his notion, which he explained to the barber, was that he himself should assume the disguise of a wandering damsel, while the other should try as best he could to pass for a squire, and that they should thus proceed to where Don Quixote was, and he, pretending to be an aggrieved and distressed damsel, should ask a favour of him, which as a valiant knight-errant he could not refuse to grant; and the favour he meant to ask him was that he should accompany her whither she would conduct him, in order to redress a wrong which a wicked knight had done her, while at the same time she should entreat him not to require her to remove her mask, nor ask her any question touching her circumstances until he had righted her with the wicked knight. And he had no doubt that Don Quixote would comply with any request made in these terms, and that in this way they might remove him and take him to his own village, where they would endeavour to find out if his extraordinary madness admitted of any kind of remedy.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVII.\n\nOF HOW THE CURATE AND THE BARBER PROCEEDED WITH THEIR SCHEME; TOGETHER WITH OTHER MATTERS WORTHY OF RECORD IN THIS GREAT HISTORY\n\nThe curate’s plan did not seem a bad one to the barber, but on the contrary so good that they immediately set about putting it in execution. They begged a petticoat and hood of the landlady, leaving her in pledge a new cassock of the curate’s; and the barber made a beard out of a grey-brown or red ox-tail in which the landlord used to stick his comb. The landlady asked them what they wanted these things for, and the curate told her in a few words about the madness of Don Quixote, and how this disguise was intended to get him away from the mountain where he then was. The landlord and landlady immediately came to the conclusion that the madman was their guest, the balsam man and master of the blanketed squire, and they told the curate all that had passed between him and them, not omitting what Sancho had been so silent about. Finally the landlady dressed up the curate in a style that left nothing to be desired; she put on him a cloth petticoat with black velvet stripes a palm broad, all slashed, and a bodice of green velvet set off by a binding of white satin, which as well as the petticoat must have been made in the time of king Wamba. The curate would not let them hood him, but put on his head a little quilted linen cap which he used for a night-cap, and bound his forehead with a strip of black silk, while with another he made a mask with which he concealed his beard and face very well. He then put on his hat, which was broad enough to serve him for an umbrella, and enveloping himself in his cloak seated himself woman-fashion on his mule, while the barber mounted his with a beard down to the waist of mingled red and white, for it was, as has been said, the tail of a clay-red ox.\n\nThey took leave of all, and of the good Maritornes, who, sinner as she was, promised to pray a rosary of prayers that God might grant them success in such an arduous and Christian undertaking as that they had in hand. But hardly had he sallied forth from the inn when it struck the curate that he was doing wrong in rigging himself out in that fashion, as it was an indecorous thing for a priest to dress himself that way even though much might depend upon it; and saying so to the barber he begged him to change dresses, as it was fitter he should be the distressed damsel, while he himself would play the squire’s part, which would be less derogatory to his dignity; otherwise he was resolved to have nothing more to do with the matter, and let the devil take Don Quixote. Just at this moment Sancho came up, and on seeing the pair in such a costume he was unable to restrain his laughter; the barber, however, agreed to do as the curate wished, and, altering their plan, the curate went on to instruct him how to play his part and what to say to Don Quixote to induce and compel him to come with them and give up his fancy for the place he had chosen for his idle penance. The barber told him he could manage it properly without any instruction, and as he did not care to dress himself up until they were near where Don Quixote was, he folded up the garments, and the curate adjusted his beard, and they set out under the guidance of Sancho Panza, who went along telling them of the encounter with the madman they met in the Sierra, saying nothing, however, about the finding of the valise and its contents; for with all his simplicity the lad was a trifle covetous.\n\nThe next day they reached the place where Sancho had laid the broom-branches as marks to direct him to where he had left his master, and recognising it he told them that here was the entrance, and that they would do well to dress themselves, if that was required to deliver his master; for they had already told him that going in this guise and dressing in this way were of the highest importance in order to rescue his master from the pernicious life he had adopted; and they charged him strictly not to tell his master who they were, or that he knew them, and should he ask, as ask he would, if he had given the letter to Dulcinea, to say that he had, and that, as she did not know how to read, she had given an answer by word of mouth, saying that she commanded him, on pain of her displeasure, to come and see her at once; and it was a very important matter for himself, because in this way and with what they meant to say to him they felt sure of bringing him back to a better mode of life and inducing him to take immediate steps to become an emperor or monarch, for there was no fear of his becoming an archbishop. All this Sancho listened to and fixed it well in his memory, and thanked them heartily for intending to recommend his master to be an emperor instead of an archbishop, for he felt sure that in the way of bestowing rewards on their squires emperors could do more than archbishops-errant. He said, too, that it would be as well for him to go on before them to find him, and give him his lady’s answer; for that perhaps might be enough to bring him away from the place without putting them to all this trouble. They approved of what Sancho proposed, and resolved to wait for him until he brought back word of having found his master.\n\nSancho pushed on into the glens of the Sierra, leaving them in one through which there flowed a little gentle rivulet, and where the rocks and trees afforded a cool and grateful shade. It was an August day with all the heat of one, and the heat in those parts is intense, and the hour was three in the afternoon, all which made the spot the more inviting and tempted them to wait there for Sancho’s return, which they did. They were reposing, then, in the shade, when a voice unaccompanied by the notes of any instrument, but sweet and pleasing in its tone, reached their ears, at which they were not a little astonished, as the place did not seem to them likely quarters for one who sang so well; for though it is often said that shepherds of rare voice are to be found in the woods and fields, this is rather a flight of the poet’s fancy than the truth. And still more surprised were they when they perceived that what they heard sung were the verses not of rustic shepherds, but of the polished wits of the city; and so it proved, for the verses they heard were these:\n\nWhat makes my quest of happiness seem vain? Disdain. What bids me to abandon hope of ease? Jealousies. What holds my heart in anguish of suspense? Absence. If that be so, then for my grief Where shall I turn to seek relief, When hope on every side lies slain By Absence, Jealousies, Disdain?\n\nWhat the prime cause of all my woe doth prove? Love. What at my glory ever looks askance? Chance. Whence is permission to afflict me given? Heaven. If that be so, I but await The stroke of a resistless fate, Since, working for my woe, these three, Love, Chance and Heaven, in league I see.\n\nWhat must I do to find a remedy? Die. What is the lure for love when coy and strange? Change. What, if all fail, will cure the heart of sadness? Madness. If that be so, it is but folly To seek a cure for melancholy: Ask where it lies; the answer saith In Change, in Madness, or in Death.\n\nThe hour, the summer season, the solitary place, the voice and skill of the singer, all contributed to the wonder and delight of the two listeners, who remained still waiting to hear something more; finding, however, that the silence continued some little time, they resolved to go in search of the musician who sang with so fine a voice; but just as they were about to do so they were checked by the same voice, which once more fell upon their ears, singing this\n\nWhen heavenward, holy Friendship, thou didst go Soaring to seek thy home beyond the sky, And take thy seat among the saints on high, It was thy will to leave on earth below Thy semblance, and upon it to bestow Thy veil, wherewith at times hypocrisy, Parading in thy shape, deceives the eye, And makes its vileness bright as virtue show. Friendship, return to us, or force the cheat That wears it now, thy livery to restore, By aid whereof sincerity is slain. If thou wilt not unmask thy counterfeit, This earth will be the prey of strife once more, As when primaeval discord held its reign.\n\nThe song ended with a deep sigh, and again the listeners remained waiting attentively for the singer to resume; but perceiving that the music had now turned to sobs and heart-rending moans they determined to find out who the unhappy being could be whose voice was as rare as his sighs were piteous, and they had not proceeded far when on turning the corner of a rock they discovered a man of the same aspect and appearance as Sancho had described to them when he told them the story of Cardenio. He, showing no astonishment when he saw them, stood still with his head bent down upon his breast like one in deep thought, without raising his eyes to look at them after the first glance when they suddenly came upon him. The curate, who was aware of his misfortune and recognised him by the description, being a man of good address, approached him and in a few sensible words entreated and urged him to quit a life of such misery, lest he should end it there, which would be the greatest of all misfortunes. Cardenio was then in his right mind, free from any attack of that madness which so frequently carried him away, and seeing them dressed in a fashion so unusual among the frequenters of those wilds, could not help showing some surprise, especially when he heard them speak of his case as if it were a well-known matter (for the curate’s words gave him to understand as much) so he replied to them thus:\n\n“I see plainly, sirs, whoever you may be, that Heaven, whose care it is to succour the good, and even the wicked very often, here, in this remote spot, cut off from human intercourse, sends me, though I deserve it not, those who seek to draw me away from this to some better retreat, showing me by many and forcible arguments how unreasonably I act in leading the life I do; but as they know, that if I escape from this evil I shall fall into another still greater, perhaps they will set me down as a weak-minded man, or, what is worse, one devoid of reason; nor would it be any wonder, for I myself can perceive that the effect of the recollection of my misfortunes is so great and works so powerfully to my ruin, that in spite of myself I become at times like a stone, without feeling or consciousness; and I come to feel the truth of it when they tell me and show me proofs of the things I have done when the terrible fit overmasters me; and all I can do is bewail my lot in vain, and idly curse my destiny, and plead for my madness by telling how it was caused, to any that care to hear it; for no reasonable beings on learning the cause will wonder at the effects; and if they cannot help me at least they will not blame me, and the repugnance they feel at my wild ways will turn into pity for my woes. If it be, sirs, that you are here with the same design as others have come with, before you proceed with your wise arguments, I entreat you to hear the story of my countless misfortunes, for perhaps when you have heard it you will spare yourselves the trouble you would take in offering consolation to grief that is beyond the reach of it.”\n\nAs they, both of them, desired nothing more than to hear from his own lips the cause of his suffering, they entreated him to tell it, promising not to do anything for his relief or comfort that he did not wish; and thereupon the unhappy gentleman began his sad story in nearly the same words and manner in which he had related it to Don Quixote and the goatherd a few days before, when, through Master Elisabad, and Don Quixote’s scrupulous observance of what was due to chivalry, the tale was left unfinished, as this history has already recorded; but now fortunately the mad fit kept off, allowed him to tell it to the end; and so, coming to the incident of the note which Don Fernando had found in the volume of “Amadis of Gaul,” Cardenio said that he remembered it perfectly and that it was in these words:\n\n“Luscinda to Cardenio.\n\n“Every day I discover merits in you that oblige and compel me to hold you in higher estimation; so if you desire to relieve me of this obligation without cost to my honour, you may easily do so. I have a father who knows you and loves me dearly, who without putting any constraint on my inclination will grant what will be reasonable for you to have, if it be that you value me as you say and as I believe you do.”\n\n“By this letter I was induced, as I told you, to demand Luscinda for my wife, and it was through it that Luscinda came to be regarded by Don Fernando as one of the most discreet and prudent women of the day, and this letter it was that suggested his design of ruining me before mine could be carried into effect. I told Don Fernando that all Luscinda’s father was waiting for was that mine should ask her of him, which I did not dare to suggest to him, fearing that he would not consent to do so; not because he did not know perfectly well the rank, goodness, virtue, and beauty of Luscinda, and that she had qualities that would do honour to any family in Spain, but because I was aware that he did not wish me to marry so soon, before seeing what the Duke Ricardo would do for me. In short, I told him I did not venture to mention it to my father, as well on account of that difficulty, as of many others that discouraged me though I knew not well what they were, only that it seemed to me that what I desired was never to come to pass. To all this Don Fernando answered that he would take it upon himself to speak to my father, and persuade him to speak to Luscinda’s father. O, ambitious Marius! O, cruel Catiline! O, wicked Sylla! O, perfidious Ganelon! O, treacherous Vellido! O, vindictive Julian! O, covetous Judas! Traitor, cruel, vindictive, and perfidious, wherein had this poor wretch failed in his fidelity, who with such frankness showed thee the secrets and the joys of his heart? What offence did I commit? What words did I utter, or what counsels did I give that had not the furtherance of thy honour and welfare for their aim? But, woe is me, wherefore do I complain? for sure it is that when misfortunes spring from the stars, descending from on high they fall upon us with such fury and violence that no power on earth can check their course nor human device stay their coming. Who could have thought that Don Fernando, a highborn gentleman, intelligent, bound to me by gratitude for my services, one that could win the object of his love wherever he might set his affections, could have become so obdurate, as they say, as to rob me of my one ewe lamb that was not even yet in my possession? But laying aside these useless and unavailing reflections, let us take up the broken thread of my unhappy story.\n\n“To proceed, then: Don Fernando finding my presence an obstacle to the execution of his treacherous and wicked design, resolved to send me to his elder brother under the pretext of asking money from him to pay for six horses which, purposely, and with the sole object of sending me away that he might the better carry out his infernal scheme, he had purchased the very day he offered to speak to my father, and the price of which he now desired me to fetch. Could I have anticipated this treachery? Could I by any chance have suspected it? Nay; so far from that, I offered with the greatest pleasure to go at once, in my satisfaction at the good bargain that had been made. That night I spoke with Luscinda, and told her what had been agreed upon with Don Fernando, and how I had strong hopes of our fair and reasonable wishes being realised. She, as unsuspicious as I was of the treachery of Don Fernando, bade me try to return speedily, as she believed the fulfilment of our desires would be delayed only so long as my father put off speaking to hers. I know not why it was that on saying this to me her eyes filled with tears, and there came a lump in her throat that prevented her from uttering a word of many more that it seemed to me she was striving to say to me. I was astonished at this unusual turn, which I never before observed in her, for we always conversed, whenever good fortune and my ingenuity gave us the chance, with the greatest gaiety and cheerfulness, mingling tears, sighs, jealousies, doubts, or fears with our words; it was all on my part a eulogy of my good fortune that Heaven should have given her to me for my mistress; I glorified her beauty, I extolled her worth and her understanding; and she paid me back by praising in me what in her love for me she thought worthy of praise; and besides we had a hundred thousand trifles and doings of our neighbours and acquaintances to talk about, and the utmost extent of my boldness was to take, almost by force, one of her fair white hands and carry it to my lips, as well as the closeness of the low grating that separated us allowed me. But the night before the unhappy day of my departure she wept, she moaned, she sighed, and she withdrew leaving me filled with perplexity and amazement, overwhelmed at the sight of such strange and affecting signs of grief and sorrow in Luscinda; but not to dash my hopes I ascribed it all to the depth of her love for me and the pain that separation gives those who love tenderly. At last I took my departure, sad and dejected, my heart filled with fancies and suspicions, but not knowing well what it was I suspected or fancied; plain omens pointing to the sad event and misfortune that was awaiting me.\n\n“I reached the place whither I had been sent, gave the letter to Don Fernando’s brother, and was kindly received but not promptly dismissed, for he desired me to wait, very much against my will, eight days in some place where the duke his father was not likely to see me, as his brother wrote that the money was to be sent without his knowledge; all of which was a scheme of the treacherous Don Fernando, for his brother had no want of money to enable him to despatch me at once.\n\n“The command was one that exposed me to the temptation of disobeying it, as it seemed to me impossible to endure life for so many days separated from Luscinda, especially after leaving her in the sorrowful mood I have described to you; nevertheless as a dutiful servant I obeyed, though I felt it would be at the cost of my well-being. But four days later there came a man in quest of me with a letter which he gave me, and which by the address I perceived to be from Luscinda, as the writing was hers. I opened it with fear and trepidation, persuaded that it must be something serious that had impelled her to write to me when at a distance, as she seldom did so when I was near. Before reading it I asked the man who it was that had given it to him, and how long he had been upon the road; he told me that as he happened to be passing through one of the streets of the city at the hour of noon, a very beautiful lady called to him from a window, and with tears in her eyes said to him hurriedly, ‘Brother, if you are, as you seem to be, a Christian, for the love of God I entreat you to have this letter despatched without a moment’s delay to the place and person named in the address, all which is well known, and by this you will render a great service to our Lord; and that you may be at no inconvenience in doing so take what is in this handkerchief;’ and said he, ‘with this she threw me a handkerchief out of the window in which were tied up a hundred reals and this gold ring which I bring here together with the letter I have given you. And then without waiting for any answer she left the window, though not before she saw me take the letter and the handkerchief, and I had by signs let her know that I would do as she bade me; and so, seeing myself so well paid for the trouble I would have in bringing it to you, and knowing by the address that it was to you it was sent (for, señor, I know you very well), and also unable to resist that beautiful lady’s tears, I resolved to trust no one else, but to come myself and give it to you, and in sixteen hours from the time when it was given me I have made the journey, which, as you know, is eighteen leagues.’\n\n“All the while the good-natured improvised courier was telling me this, I hung upon his words, my legs trembling under me so that I could scarcely stand. However, I opened the letter and read these words:\n\n“‘The promise Don Fernando gave you to urge your father to speak to mine, he has fulfilled much more to his own satisfaction than to your advantage. I have to tell you, señor, that he has demanded me for a wife, and my father, led away by what he considers Don Fernando’s superiority over you, has favoured his suit so cordially, that in two days hence the betrothal is to take place with such secrecy and so privately that the only witnesses are to be the Heavens above and a few of the household. Picture to yourself the state I am in; judge if it be urgent for you to come; the issue of the affair will show you whether I love you or not. God grant this may come to your hand before mine shall be forced to link itself with his who keeps so ill the faith that he has pledged.’\n\n“Such, in brief, were the words of the letter, words that made me set out at once without waiting any longer for reply or money; for I now saw clearly that it was not the purchase of horses but of his own pleasure that had made Don Fernando send me to his brother. The exasperation I felt against Don Fernando, joined with the fear of losing the prize I had won by so many years of love and devotion, lent me wings; so that almost flying I reached home the same day, by the hour which served for speaking with Luscinda. I arrived unobserved, and left the mule on which I had come at the house of the worthy man who had brought me the letter, and fortune was pleased to be for once so kind that I found Luscinda at the grating that was the witness of our loves. She recognised me at once, and I her, but not as she ought to have recognised me, or I her. But who is there in the world that can boast of having fathomed or understood the wavering mind and unstable nature of a woman? Of a truth no one. To proceed: as soon as Luscinda saw me she said, ‘Cardenio, I am in my bridal dress, and the treacherous Don Fernando and my covetous father are waiting for me in the hall with the other witnesses, who shall be the witnesses of my death before they witness my betrothal. Be not distressed, my friend, but contrive to be present at this sacrifice, and if that cannot be prevented by my words, I have a dagger concealed which will prevent more deliberate violence, putting an end to my life and giving thee a first proof of the love I have borne and bear thee.’ I replied to her distractedly and hastily, in fear lest I should not have time to reply, ‘May thy words be verified by thy deeds, lady; and if thou hast a dagger to save thy honour, I have a sword to defend thee or kill myself if fortune be against us.’\n\n“I think she could not have heard all these words, for I perceived that they called her away in haste, as the bridegroom was waiting. Now the night of my sorrow set in, the sun of my happiness went down, I felt my eyes bereft of sight, my mind of reason. I could not enter the house, nor was I capable of any movement; but reflecting how important it was that I should be present at what might take place on the occasion, I nerved myself as best I could and went in, for I well knew all the entrances and outlets; and besides, with the confusion that in secret pervaded the house no one took notice of me, so, without being seen, I found an opportunity of placing myself in the recess formed by a window of the hall itself, and concealed by the ends and borders of two tapestries, from between which I could, without being seen, see all that took place in the room. Who could describe the agitation of heart I suffered as I stood there--the thoughts that came to me--the reflections that passed through my mind? They were such as cannot be, nor were it well they should be, told. Suffice it to say that the bridegroom entered the hall in his usual dress, without ornament of any kind; as groomsman he had with him a cousin of Luscinda’s and except the servants of the house there was no one else in the chamber. Soon afterwards Luscinda came out from an antechamber, attended by her mother and two of her damsels, arrayed and adorned as became her rank and beauty, and in full festival and ceremonial attire. My anxiety and distraction did not allow me to observe or notice particularly what she wore; I could only perceive the colours, which were crimson and white, and the glitter of the gems and jewels on her head dress and apparel, surpassed by the rare beauty of her lovely auburn hair that vying with the precious stones and the light of the four torches that stood in the hall shone with a brighter gleam than all. Oh memory, mortal foe of my peace! why bring before me now the incomparable beauty of that adored enemy of mine? Were it not better, cruel memory, to remind me and recall what she then did, that stirred by a wrong so glaring I may seek, if not vengeance now, at least to rid myself of life? Be not weary, sirs, of listening to these digressions; my sorrow is not one of those that can or should be told tersely and briefly, for to me each incident seems to call for many words.”\n\nTo this the curate replied that not only were they not weary of listening to him, but that the details he mentioned interested them greatly, being of a kind by no means to be omitted and deserving of the same attention as the main story.\n\n“To proceed, then,” continued Cardenio: “all being assembled in the hall, the priest of the parish came in and as he took the pair by the hand to perform the requisite ceremony, at the words, ‘Will you, Señora Luscinda, take Señor Don Fernando, here present, for your lawful husband, as the holy Mother Church ordains?’ I thrust my head and neck out from between the tapestries, and with eager ears and throbbing heart set myself to listen to Luscinda’s answer, awaiting in her reply the sentence of death or the grant of life. Oh, that I had but dared at that moment to rush forward crying aloud, ‘Luscinda, Luscinda! have a care what thou dost; remember what thou owest me; bethink thee thou art mine and canst not be another’s; reflect that thy utterance of “Yes” and the end of my life will come at the same instant. O, treacherous Don Fernando! robber of my glory, death of my life! What seekest thou? Remember that thou canst not as a Christian attain the object of thy wishes, for Luscinda is my bride, and I am her husband!’ Fool that I am! now that I am far away, and out of danger, I say I should have done what I did not do: now that I have allowed my precious treasure to be robbed from me, I curse the robber, on whom I might have taken vengeance had I as much heart for it as I have for bewailing my fate; in short, as I was then a coward and a fool, little wonder is it if I am now dying shame-stricken, remorseful, and mad.\n\n“The priest stood waiting for the answer of Luscinda, who for a long time withheld it; and just as I thought she was taking out the dagger to save her honour, or struggling for words to make some declaration of the truth on my behalf, I heard her say in a faint and feeble voice, ‘I will:’ Don Fernando said the same, and giving her the ring they stood linked by a knot that could never be loosed. The bridegroom then approached to embrace his bride; and she, pressing her hand upon her heart, fell fainting in her mother’s arms. It only remains now for me to tell you the state I was in when in that consent that I heard I saw all my hopes mocked, the words and promises of Luscinda proved falsehoods, and the recovery of the prize I had that instant lost rendered impossible for ever. I stood stupefied, wholly abandoned, it seemed, by Heaven, declared the enemy of the earth that bore me, the air refusing me breath for my sighs, the water moisture for my tears; it was only the fire that gathered strength so that my whole frame glowed with rage and jealousy. They were all thrown into confusion by Luscinda’s fainting, and as her mother was unlacing her to give her air a sealed paper was discovered in her bosom which Don Fernando seized at once and began to read by the light of one of the torches. As soon as he had read it he seated himself in a chair, leaning his cheek on his hand in the attitude of one deep in thought, without taking any part in the efforts that were being made to recover his bride from her fainting fit.\n\n“Seeing all the household in confusion, I ventured to come out regardless whether I were seen or not, and determined, if I were, to do some frenzied deed that would prove to all the world the righteous indignation of my breast in the punishment of the treacherous Don Fernando, and even in that of the fickle fainting traitress. But my fate, doubtless reserving me for greater sorrows, if such there be, so ordered it that just then I had enough and to spare of that reason which has since been wanting to me; and so, without seeking to take vengeance on my greatest enemies (which might have been easily taken, as all thought of me was so far from their minds), I resolved to take it upon myself, and on myself to inflict the pain they deserved, perhaps with even greater severity than I should have dealt out to them had I then slain them; for sudden pain is soon over, but that which is protracted by tortures is ever slaying without ending life. In a word, I quitted the house and reached that of the man with whom I had left my mule; I made him saddle it for me, mounted without bidding him farewell, and rode out of the city, like another Lot, not daring to turn my head to look back upon it; and when I found myself alone in the open country, screened by the darkness of the night, and tempted by the stillness to give vent to my grief without apprehension or fear of being heard or seen, then I broke silence and lifted up my voice in maledictions upon Luscinda and Don Fernando, as if I could thus avenge the wrong they had done me. I called her cruel, ungrateful, false, thankless, but above all covetous, since the wealth of my enemy had blinded the eyes of her affection, and turned it from me to transfer it to one to whom fortune had been more generous and liberal. And yet, in the midst of this outburst of execration and upbraiding, I found excuses for her, saying it was no wonder that a young girl in the seclusion of her parents’ house, trained and schooled to obey them always, should have been ready to yield to their wishes when they offered her for a husband a gentleman of such distinction, wealth, and noble birth, that if she had refused to accept him she would have been thought out of her senses, or to have set her affection elsewhere, a suspicion injurious to her fair name and fame. But then again, I said, had she declared I was her husband, they would have seen that in choosing me she had not chosen so ill but that they might excuse her, for before Don Fernando had made his offer, they themselves could not have desired, if their desires had been ruled by reason, a more eligible husband for their daughter than I was; and she, before taking the last fatal step of giving her hand, might easily have said that I had already given her mine, for I should have come forward to support any assertion of hers to that effect. In short, I came to the conclusion that feeble love, little reflection, great ambition, and a craving for rank, had made her forget the words with which she had deceived me, encouraged and supported by my firm hopes and honourable passion.\n\n“Thus soliloquising and agitated, I journeyed onward for the remainder of the night, and by daybreak I reached one of the passes of these mountains, among which I wandered for three days more without taking any path or road, until I came to some meadows lying on I know not which side of the mountains, and there I inquired of some herdsmen in what direction the most rugged part of the range lay. They told me that it was in this quarter, and I at once directed my course hither, intending to end my life here; but as I was making my way among these crags, my mule dropped dead through fatigue and hunger, or, as I think more likely, in order to have done with such a worthless burden as it bore in me. I was left on foot, worn out, famishing, without anyone to help me or any thought of seeking help: and so thus I lay stretched on the ground, how long I know not, after which I rose up free from hunger, and found beside me some goatherds, who no doubt were the persons who had relieved me in my need, for they told me how they had found me, and how I had been uttering ravings that showed plainly I had lost my reason; and since then I am conscious that I am not always in full possession of it, but at times so deranged and crazed that I do a thousand mad things, tearing my clothes, crying aloud in these solitudes, cursing my fate, and idly calling on the dear name of her who is my enemy, and only seeking to end my life in lamentation; and when I recover my senses I find myself so exhausted and weary that I can scarcely move. Most commonly my dwelling is the hollow of a cork tree large enough to shelter this miserable body; the herdsmen and goatherds who frequent these mountains, moved by compassion, furnish me with food, leaving it by the wayside or on the rocks, where they think I may perhaps pass and find it; and so, even though I may be then out of my senses, the wants of nature teach me what is required to sustain me, and make me crave it and eager to take it. At other times, so they tell me when they find me in a rational mood, I sally out upon the road, and though they would gladly give it me, I snatch food by force from the shepherds bringing it from the village to their huts. Thus do pass the wretched life that remains to me, until it be Heaven’s will to bring it to a close, or so to order my memory that I no longer recollect the beauty and treachery of Luscinda, or the wrong done me by Don Fernando; for if it will do this without depriving me of life, I will turn my thoughts into some better channel; if not, I can only implore it to have full mercy on my soul, for in myself I feel no power or strength to release my body from this strait in which I have of my own accord chosen to place it.\n\n“Such, sirs, is the dismal story of my misfortune: say if it be one that can be told with less emotion than you have seen in me; and do not trouble yourselves with urging or pressing upon me what reason suggests as likely to serve for my relief, for it will avail me as much as the medicine prescribed by a wise physician avails the sick man who will not take it. I have no wish for health without Luscinda; and since it is her pleasure to be another’s, when she is or should be mine, let it be mine to be a prey to misery when I might have enjoyed happiness. She by her fickleness strove to make my ruin irretrievable; I will strive to gratify her wishes by seeking destruction; and it will show generations to come that I alone was deprived of that of which all others in misfortune have a superabundance, for to them the impossibility of being consoled is itself a consolation, while to me it is the cause of greater sorrows and sufferings, for I think that even in death there will not be an end of them.”\n\nHere Cardenio brought to a close his long discourse and story, as full of misfortune as it was of love; but just as the curate was going to address some words of comfort to him, he was stopped by a voice that reached his ear, saying in melancholy tones what will be told in the Fourth Part of this narrative; for at this point the sage and sagacious historian, Cide Hamete Benengeli, brought the Third to a conclusion.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF THE STRANGE AND DELIGHTFUL ADVENTURE THAT BEFELL THE CURATE AND THE BARBER IN THE SAME SIERRA\nHappy and fortunate were the times when that most daring knight Don Quixote of La Mancha was sent into the world; for by reason of his having formed a resolution so honourable as that of seeking to revive and restore to the world the long-lost and almost defunct order of knight-errantry, we now enjoy in this age of ours, so poor in light entertainment, not only the charm of his veracious history, but also of the tales and episodes contained in it which are, in a measure, no less pleasing, ingenious, and truthful, than the history itself; which, resuming its thread, carded, spun, and wound, relates that just as the curate was going to offer consolation to Cardenio, he was interrupted by a voice that fell upon his ear saying in plaintive tones:\n\n“O God! is it possible I have found a place that may serve as a secret grave for the weary load of this body that I support so unwillingly? If the solitude these mountains promise deceives me not, it is so; ah! woe is me! how much more grateful to my mind will be the society of these rocks and brakes that permit me to complain of my misfortune to Heaven, than that of any human being, for there is none on earth to look to for counsel in doubt, comfort in sorrow, or relief in distress!”\n\nAll this was heard distinctly by the curate and those with him, and as it seemed to them to be uttered close by, as indeed it was, they got up to look for the speaker, and before they had gone twenty paces they discovered behind a rock, seated at the foot of an ash tree, a youth in the dress of a peasant, whose face they were unable at the moment to see as he was leaning forward, bathing his feet in the brook that flowed past. They approached so silently that he did not perceive them, being fully occupied in bathing his feet, which were so fair that they looked like two pieces of shining crystal brought forth among the other stones of the brook. The whiteness and beauty of these feet struck them with surprise, for they did not seem to have been made to crush clods or to follow the plough and the oxen as their owner’s dress suggested; and so, finding they had not been noticed, the curate, who was in front, made a sign to the other two to conceal themselves behind some fragments of rock that lay there; which they did, observing closely what the youth was about. He had on a loose double-skirted dark brown jacket bound tight to his body with a white cloth; he wore besides breeches and gaiters of brown cloth, and on his head a brown montera; and he had the gaiters turned up as far as the middle of the leg, which verily seemed to be of pure alabaster.\n\nAs soon as he had done bathing his beautiful feet, he wiped them with a towel he took from under the montera, on taking off which he raised his face, and those who were watching him had an opportunity of seeing a beauty so exquisite that Cardenio said to the curate in a whisper:\n\n“As this is not Luscinda, it is no human creature but a divine being.”\n\nThe youth then took off the montera, and shaking his head from side to side there broke loose and spread out a mass of hair that the beams of the sun might have envied; by this they knew that what had seemed a peasant was a lovely woman, nay the most beautiful the eyes of two of them had ever beheld, or even Cardenio’s if they had not seen and known Luscinda, for he afterwards declared that only the beauty of Luscinda could compare with this. The long auburn tresses not only covered her shoulders, but such was their length and abundance, concealed her all round beneath their masses, so that except the feet nothing of her form was visible. She now used her hands as a comb, and if her feet had seemed like bits of crystal in the water, her hands looked like pieces of driven snow among her locks; all which increased not only the admiration of the three beholders, but their anxiety to learn who she was. With this object they resolved to show themselves, and at the stir they made in getting upon their feet the fair damsel raised her head, and parting her hair from before her eyes with both hands, she looked to see who had made the noise, and the instant she perceived them she started to her feet, and without waiting to put on her shoes or gather up her hair, hastily snatched up a bundle as though of clothes that she had beside her, and, scared and alarmed, endeavoured to take flight; but before she had gone six paces she fell to the ground, her delicate feet being unable to bear the roughness of the stones; seeing which, the three hastened towards her, and the curate addressing her first said:\n\n“Stay, señora, whoever you may be, for those whom you see here only desire to be of service to you; you have no need to attempt a flight so heedless, for neither can your feet bear it, nor we allow it.”\n\nTaken by surprise and bewildered, she made no reply to these words. They, however, came towards her, and the curate taking her hand went on to say:\n\n“What your dress would hide, señora, is made known to us by your hair; a clear proof that it can be no trifling cause that has disguised your beauty in a garb so unworthy of it, and sent it into solitudes like these where we have had the good fortune to find you, if not to relieve your distress, at least to offer you comfort; for no distress, so long as life lasts, can be so oppressive or reach such a height as to make the sufferer refuse to listen to comfort offered with good intention. And so, señora, or señor, or whatever you prefer to be, dismiss the fears that our appearance has caused you and make us acquainted with your good or evil fortunes, for from all of us together, or from each one of us, you will receive sympathy in your trouble.”\n\nWhile the curate was speaking, the disguised damsel stood as if spell-bound, looking at them without opening her lips or uttering a word, just like a village rustic to whom something strange that he has never seen before has been suddenly shown; but on the curate addressing some further words to the same effect to her, sighing deeply she broke silence and said:\n\n“Since the solitude of these mountains has been unable to conceal me, and the escape of my dishevelled tresses will not allow my tongue to deal in falsehoods, it would be idle for me now to make any further pretence of what, if you were to believe me, you would believe more out of courtesy than for any other reason. This being so, I say I thank you, sirs, for the offer you have made me, which places me under the obligation of complying with the request you have made of me; though I fear the account I shall give you of my misfortunes will excite in you as much concern as compassion, for you will be unable to suggest anything to remedy them or any consolation to alleviate them. However, that my honour may not be left a matter of doubt in your minds, now that you have discovered me to be a woman, and see that I am young, alone, and in this dress, things that taken together or separately would be enough to destroy any good name, I feel bound to tell what I would willingly keep secret if I could.”\n\nAll this she who was now seen to be a lovely woman delivered without any hesitation, with so much ease and in so sweet a voice that they were not less charmed by her intelligence than by her beauty, and as they again repeated their offers and entreaties to her to fulfil her promise, she without further pressing, first modestly covering her feet and gathering up her hair, seated herself on a stone with the three placed around her, and, after an effort to restrain some tears that came to her eyes, in a clear and steady voice began her story thus:\n\n“In this Andalusia there is a town from which a duke takes a title which makes him one of those that are called Grandees of Spain. This nobleman has two sons, the elder heir to his dignity and apparently to his good qualities; the younger heir to I know not what, unless it be the treachery of Vellido and the falsehood of Ganelon. My parents are this lord’s vassals, lowly in origin, but so wealthy that if birth had conferred as much on them as fortune, they would have had nothing left to desire, nor should I have had reason to fear trouble like that in which I find myself now; for it may be that my ill fortune came of theirs in not having been nobly born. It is true they are not so low that they have any reason to be ashamed of their condition, but neither are they so high as to remove from my mind the impression that my mishap comes of their humble birth. They are, in short, peasants, plain homely people, without any taint of disreputable blood, and, as the saying is, old rusty Christians, but so rich that by their wealth and free-handed way of life they are coming by degrees to be considered gentlefolk by birth, and even by position; though the wealth and nobility they thought most of was having me for their daughter; and as they have no other child to make their heir, and are affectionate parents, I was one of the most indulged daughters that ever parents indulged.\n\n“I was the mirror in which they beheld themselves, the staff of their old age, and the object in which, with submission to Heaven, all their wishes centred, and mine were in accordance with theirs, for I knew their worth; and as I was mistress of their hearts, so was I also of their possessions. Through me they engaged or dismissed their servants; through my hands passed the accounts and returns of what was sown and reaped; the oil-mills, the wine-presses, the count of the flocks and herds, the beehives, all in short that a rich farmer like my father has or can have, I had under my care, and I acted as steward and mistress with an assiduity on my part and satisfaction on theirs that I cannot well describe to you. The leisure hours left to me after I had given the requisite orders to the head-shepherds, overseers, and other labourers, I passed in such employments as are not only allowable but necessary for young girls, those that the needle, embroidery cushion, and spinning wheel usually afford, and if to refresh my mind I quitted them for a while, I found recreation in reading some devotional book or playing the harp, for experience taught me that music soothes the troubled mind and relieves weariness of spirit. Such was the life I led in my parents’ house and if I have depicted it thus minutely, it is not out of ostentation, or to let you know that I am rich, but that you may see how, without any fault of mine, I have fallen from the happy condition I have described, to the misery I am in at present. The truth is, that while I was leading this busy life, in a retirement that might compare with that of a monastery, and unseen as I thought by any except the servants of the house (for when I went to Mass it was so early in the morning, and I was so closely attended by my mother and the women of the household, and so thickly veiled and so shy, that my eyes scarcely saw more ground than I trod on), in spite of all this, the eyes of love, or idleness, more properly speaking, that the lynx’s cannot rival, discovered me, with the help of the assiduity of Don Fernando; for that is the name of the younger son of the duke I told of.”\n\nThe moment the speaker mentioned the name of Don Fernando, Cardenio changed colour and broke into a sweat, with such signs of emotion that the curate and the barber, who observed it, feared that one of the mad fits which they heard attacked him sometimes was coming upon him; but Cardenio showed no further agitation and remained quiet, regarding the peasant girl with fixed attention, for he began to suspect who she was. She, however, without noticing the excitement of Cardenio, continuing her story, went on to say:\n\n“And they had hardly discovered me, when, as he owned afterwards, he was smitten with a violent love for me, as the manner in which it displayed itself plainly showed. But to shorten the long recital of my woes, I will pass over in silence all the artifices employed by Don Fernando for declaring his passion for me. He bribed all the household, he gave and offered gifts and presents to my parents; every day was like a holiday or a merry-making in our street; by night no one could sleep for the music; the love letters that used to come to my hand, no one knew how, were innumerable, full of tender pleadings and pledges, containing more promises and oaths than there were letters in them; all which not only did not soften me, but hardened my heart against him, as if he had been my mortal enemy, and as if everything he did to make me yield were done with the opposite intention. Not that the high-bred bearing of Don Fernando was disagreeable to me, or that I found his importunities wearisome; for it gave me a certain sort of satisfaction to find myself so sought and prized by a gentleman of such distinction, and I was not displeased at seeing my praises in his letters (for however ugly we women may be, it seems to me it always pleases us to hear ourselves called beautiful) but that my own sense of right was opposed to all this, as well as the repeated advice of my parents, who now very plainly perceived Don Fernando’s purpose, for he cared very little if all the world knew it. They told me they trusted and confided their honour and good name to my virtue and rectitude alone, and bade me consider the disparity between Don Fernando and myself, from which I might conclude that his intentions, whatever he might say to the contrary, had for their aim his own pleasure rather than my advantage; and if I were at all desirous of opposing an obstacle to his unreasonable suit, they were ready, they said, to marry me at once to anyone I preferred, either among the leading people of our own town, or of any of those in the neighbourhood; for with their wealth and my good name, a match might be looked for in any quarter. This offer, and their sound advice strengthened my resolution, and I never gave Don Fernando a word in reply that could hold out to him any hope of success, however remote.\n\n“All this caution of mine, which he must have taken for coyness, had apparently the effect of increasing his wanton appetite--for that is the name I give to his passion for me; had it been what he declared it to be, you would not know of it now, because there would have been no occasion to tell you of it. At length he learned that my parents were contemplating marriage for me in order to put an end to his hopes of obtaining possession of me, or at least to secure additional protectors to watch over me, and this intelligence or suspicion made him act as you shall hear. One night, as I was in my chamber with no other companion than a damsel who waited on me, with the doors carefully locked lest my honour should be imperilled through any carelessness, I know not nor can conceive how it happened, but, with all this seclusion and these precautions, and in the solitude and silence of my retirement, I found him standing before me, a vision that so astounded me that it deprived my eyes of sight, and my tongue of speech. I had no power to utter a cry, nor, I think, did he give me time to utter one, as he immediately approached me, and taking me in his arms (for, overwhelmed as I was, I was powerless, I say, to help myself), he began to make such professions to me that I know not how falsehood could have had the power of dressing them up to seem so like truth; and the traitor contrived that his tears should vouch for his words, and his sighs for his sincerity.\n\n“I, a poor young creature alone, ill versed among my people in cases such as this, began, I know not how, to think all these lying protestations true, though without being moved by his sighs and tears to anything more than pure compassion; and so, as the first feeling of bewilderment passed away, and I began in some degree to recover myself, I said to him with more courage than I thought I could have possessed, ‘If, as I am now in your arms, señor, I were in the claws of a fierce lion, and my deliverance could be procured by doing or saying anything to the prejudice of my honour, it would no more be in my power to do it or say it, than it would be possible that what was should not have been; so then, if you hold my body clasped in your arms, I hold my soul secured by virtuous intentions, very different from yours, as you will see if you attempt to carry them into effect by force. I am your vassal, but I am not your slave; your nobility neither has nor should have any right to dishonour or degrade my humble birth; and low-born peasant as I am, I have my self-respect as much as you, a lord and gentleman: with me your violence will be to no purpose, your wealth will have no weight, your words will have no power to deceive me, nor your sighs or tears to soften me: were I to see any of the things I speak of in him whom my parents gave me as a husband, his will should be mine, and mine should be bounded by his; and my honour being preserved even though my inclinations were not would willingly yield him what you, señor, would now obtain by force; and this I say lest you should suppose that any but my lawful husband shall ever win anything of me.’ ‘If that,’ said this disloyal gentleman, ‘be the only scruple you feel, fairest Dorothea’ (for that is the name of this unhappy being), ‘see here I give you my hand to be yours, and let Heaven, from which nothing is hid, and this image of Our Lady you have here, be witnesses of this pledge.’”\n\nWhen Cardenio heard her say she was called Dorothea, he showed fresh agitation and felt convinced of the truth of his former suspicion, but he was unwilling to interrupt the story, and wished to hear the end of what he already all but knew, so he merely said:\n\n“What! is Dorothea your name, señora? I have heard of another of the same name who can perhaps match your misfortunes. But proceed; by-and-by I may tell you something that will astonish you as much as it will excite your compassion.”\n\nDorothea was struck by Cardenio’s words as well as by his strange and miserable attire, and begged him if he knew anything concerning her to tell it to her at once, for if fortune had left her any blessing it was courage to bear whatever calamity might fall upon her, as she felt sure that none could reach her capable of increasing in any degree what she endured already.\n\n“I would not let the occasion pass, señora,” replied Cardenio, “of telling you what I think, if what I suspect were the truth, but so far there has been no opportunity, nor is it of any importance to you to know it.”\n\n“Be it as it may,” replied Dorothea, “what happened in my story was that Don Fernando, taking an image that stood in the chamber, placed it as a witness of our betrothal, and with the most binding words and extravagant oaths gave me his promise to become my husband; though before he had made an end of pledging himself I bade him consider well what he was doing, and think of the anger his father would feel at seeing him married to a peasant girl and one of his vassals; I told him not to let my beauty, such as it was, blind him, for that was not enough to furnish an excuse for his transgression; and if in the love he bore me he wished to do me any kindness, it would be to leave my lot to follow its course at the level my condition required; for marriages so unequal never brought happiness, nor did they continue long to afford the enjoyment they began with.\n\n“All this that I have now repeated I said to him, and much more which I cannot recollect; but it had no effect in inducing him to forego his purpose; he who has no intention of paying does not trouble himself about difficulties when he is striking the bargain. At the same time I argued the matter briefly in my own mind, saying to myself, ‘I shall not be the first who has risen through marriage from a lowly to a lofty station, nor will Don Fernando be the first whom beauty or, as is more likely, a blind attachment, has led to mate himself below his rank. Then, since I am introducing no new usage or practice, I may as well avail myself of the honour that chance offers me, for even though his inclination for me should not outlast the attainment of his wishes, I shall be, after all, his wife before God. And if I strive to repel him by scorn, I can see that, fair means failing, he is in a mood to use force, and I shall be left dishonoured and without any means of proving my innocence to those who cannot know how innocently I have come to be in this position; for what arguments would persuade my parents that this gentleman entered my chamber without my consent?’\n\n“All these questions and answers passed through my mind in a moment; but the oaths of Don Fernando, the witnesses he appealed to, the tears he shed, and lastly the charms of his person and his high-bred grace, which, accompanied by such signs of genuine love, might well have conquered a heart even more free and coy than mine--these were the things that more than all began to influence me and lead me unawares to my ruin. I called my waiting-maid to me, that there might be a witness on earth besides those in Heaven, and again Don Fernando renewed and repeated his oaths, invoked as witnesses fresh saints in addition to the former ones, called down upon himself a thousand curses hereafter should he fail to keep his promise, shed more tears, redoubled his sighs and pressed me closer in his arms, from which he had never allowed me to escape; and so I was left by my maid, and ceased to be one, and he became a traitor and a perjured man.\n\n“The day which followed the night of my misfortune did not come so quickly, I imagine, as Don Fernando wished, for when desire has attained its object, the greatest pleasure is to fly from the scene of pleasure. I say so because Don Fernando made all haste to leave me, and by the adroitness of my maid, who was indeed the one who had admitted him, gained the street before daybreak; but on taking leave of me he told me, though not with as much earnestness and fervour as when he came, that I might rest assured of his faith and of the sanctity and sincerity of his oaths; and to confirm his words he drew a rich ring off his finger and placed it upon mine. He then took his departure and I was left, I know not whether sorrowful or happy; all I can say is, I was left agitated and troubled in mind and almost bewildered by what had taken place, and I had not the spirit, or else it did not occur to me, to chide my maid for the treachery she had been guilty of in concealing Don Fernando in my chamber; for as yet I was unable to make up my mind whether what had befallen me was for good or evil. I told Don Fernando at parting, that as I was now his, he might see me on other nights in the same way, until it should be his pleasure to let the matter become known; but, except the following night, he came no more, nor for more than a month could I catch a glimpse of him in the street or in church, while I wearied myself with watching for one; although I knew he was in the town, and almost every day went out hunting, a pastime he was very fond of. I remember well how sad and dreary those days and hours were to me; I remember well how I began to doubt as they went by, and even to lose confidence in the faith of Don Fernando; and I remember, too, how my maid heard those words in reproof of her audacity that she had not heard before, and how I was forced to put a constraint on my tears and on the expression of my countenance, not to give my parents cause to ask me why I was so melancholy, and drive me to invent falsehoods in reply. But all this was suddenly brought to an end, for the time came when all such considerations were disregarded, and there was no further question of honour, when my patience gave way and the secret of my heart became known abroad. The reason was, that a few days later it was reported in the town that Don Fernando had been married in a neighbouring city to a maiden of rare beauty, the daughter of parents of distinguished position, though not so rich that her portion would entitle her to look for so brilliant a match; it was said, too, that her name was Luscinda, and that at the betrothal some strange things had happened.”\n\nCardenio heard the name of Luscinda, but he only shrugged his shoulders, bit his lips, bent his brows, and before long two streams of tears escaped from his eyes. Dorothea, however, did not interrupt her story, but went on in these words:\n\n“This sad intelligence reached my ears, and, instead of being struck with a chill, with such wrath and fury did my heart burn that I scarcely restrained myself from rushing out into the streets, crying aloud and proclaiming openly the perfidy and treachery of which I was the victim; but this transport of rage was for the time checked by a resolution I formed, to be carried out the same night, and that was to assume this dress, which I got from a servant of my father’s, one of the zagals, as they are called in farmhouses, to whom I confided the whole of my misfortune, and whom I entreated to accompany me to the city where I heard my enemy was. He, though he remonstrated with me for my boldness, and condemned my resolution, when he saw me bent upon my purpose, offered to bear me company, as he said, to the end of the world. I at once packed up in a linen pillow-case a woman’s dress, and some jewels and money to provide for emergencies, and in the silence of the night, without letting my treacherous maid know, I sallied forth from the house, accompanied by my servant and abundant anxieties, and on foot set out for the city, but borne as it were on wings by my eagerness to reach it, if not to prevent what I presumed to be already done, at least to call upon Don Fernando to tell me with what conscience he had done it. I reached my destination in two days and a half, and on entering the city inquired for the house of Luscinda’s parents. The first person I asked gave me more in reply than I sought to know; he showed me the house, and told me all that had occurred at the betrothal of the daughter of the family, an affair of such notoriety in the city that it was the talk of every knot of idlers in the street. He said that on the night of Don Fernando’s betrothal with Luscinda, as soon as she had consented to be his bride by saying ‘Yes,’ she was taken with a sudden fainting fit, and that on the bridegroom approaching to unlace the bosom of her dress to give her air, he found a paper in her own handwriting, in which she said and declared that she could not be Don Fernando’s bride, because she was already Cardenio’s, who, according to the man’s account, was a gentleman of distinction of the same city; and that if she had accepted Don Fernando, it was only in obedience to her parents. In short, he said, the words of the paper made it clear she meant to kill herself on the completion of the betrothal, and gave her reasons for putting an end to herself all which was confirmed, it was said, by a dagger they found somewhere in her clothes. On seeing this, Don Fernando, persuaded that Luscinda had befooled, slighted, and trifled with him, assailed her before she had recovered from her swoon, and tried to stab her with the dagger that had been found, and would have succeeded had not her parents and those who were present prevented him. It was said, moreover, that Don Fernando went away at once, and that Luscinda did not recover from her prostration until the next day, when she told her parents how she was really the bride of that Cardenio I have mentioned. I learned besides that Cardenio, according to report, had been present at the betrothal; and that upon seeing her betrothed contrary to his expectation, he had quitted the city in despair, leaving behind him a letter declaring the wrong Luscinda had done him, and his intention of going where no one should ever see him again. All this was a matter of notoriety in the city, and everyone spoke of it; especially when it became known that Luscinda was missing from her father’s house and from the city, for she was not to be found anywhere, to the distraction of her parents, who knew not what steps to take to recover her. What I learned revived my hopes, and I was better pleased not to have found Don Fernando than to find him married, for it seemed to me that the door was not yet entirely shut upon relief in my case, and I thought that perhaps Heaven had put this impediment in the way of the second marriage, to lead him to recognise his obligations under the former one, and reflect that as a Christian he was bound to consider his soul above all human objects. All this passed through my mind, and I strove to comfort myself without comfort, indulging in faint and distant hopes of cherishing that life that I now abhor.\n\n“But while I was in the city, uncertain what to do, as I could not find Don Fernando, I heard notice given by the public crier offering a great reward to anyone who should find me, and giving the particulars of my age and of the very dress I wore; and I heard it said that the lad who came with me had taken me away from my father’s house; a thing that cut me to the heart, showing how low my good name had fallen, since it was not enough that I should lose it by my flight, but they must add with whom I had fled, and that one so much beneath me and so unworthy of my consideration. The instant I heard the notice I quitted the city with my servant, who now began to show signs of wavering in his fidelity to me, and the same night, for fear of discovery, we entered the most thickly wooded part of these mountains. But, as is commonly said, one evil calls up another and the end of one misfortune is apt to be the beginning of one still greater, and so it proved in my case; for my worthy servant, until then so faithful and trusty when he found me in this lonely spot, moved more by his own villainy than by my beauty, sought to take advantage of the opportunity which these solitudes seemed to present him, and with little shame and less fear of God and respect for me, began to make overtures to me; and finding that I replied to the effrontery of his proposals with justly severe language, he laid aside the entreaties which he had employed at first, and began to use violence.\n\n“But just Heaven, that seldom fails to watch over and aid good intentions, so aided mine that with my slight strength and with little exertion I pushed him over a precipice, where I left him, whether dead or alive I know not; and then, with greater speed than seemed possible in my terror and fatigue, I made my way into the mountains, without any other thought or purpose save that of hiding myself among them, and escaping my father and those despatched in search of me by his orders. It is now I know not how many months since with this object I came here, where I met a herdsman who engaged me as his servant at a place in the heart of this Sierra, and all this time I have been serving him as herd, striving to keep always afield to hide these locks which have now unexpectedly betrayed me. But all my care and pains were unavailing, for my master made the discovery that I was not a man, and harboured the same base designs as my servant; and as fortune does not always supply a remedy in cases of difficulty, and I had no precipice or ravine at hand down which to fling the master and cure his passion, as I had in the servant’s case, I thought it a lesser evil to leave him and again conceal myself among these crags, than make trial of my strength and argument with him. So, as I say, once more I went into hiding to seek for some place where I might with sighs and tears implore Heaven to have pity on my misery, and grant me help and strength to escape from it, or let me die among the solitudes, leaving no trace of an unhappy being who, by no fault of hers, has furnished matter for talk and scandal at home and abroad.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF THE DROLL DEVICE AND METHOD ADOPTED TO EXTRICATE OUR LOVE-STRICKEN KNIGHT FROM THE SEVERE PENANCE HE HAD IMPOSED UPON HIMSELF\n“Such, sirs, is the true story of my sad adventures; judge for yourselves now whether the sighs and lamentations you heard, and the tears that flowed from my eyes, had not sufficient cause even if I had indulged in them more freely; and if you consider the nature of my misfortune you will see that consolation is idle, as there is no possible remedy for it. All I ask of you is, what you may easily and reasonably do, to show me where I may pass my life unharassed by the fear and dread of discovery by those who are in search of me; for though the great love my parents bear me makes me feel sure of being kindly received by them, so great is my feeling of shame at the mere thought that I cannot present myself before them as they expect, that I had rather banish myself from their sight for ever than look them in the face with the reflection that they beheld mine stripped of that purity they had a right to expect in me.”\n\nWith these words she became silent, and the colour that overspread her face showed plainly the pain and shame she was suffering at heart. In theirs the listeners felt as much pity as wonder at her misfortunes; but as the curate was just about to offer her some consolation and advice Cardenio forestalled him, saying, “So then, señora, you are the fair Dorothea, the only daughter of the rich Clenardo?” Dorothea was astonished at hearing her father’s name, and at the miserable appearance of him who mentioned it, for it has been already said how wretchedly clad Cardenio was; so she said to him:\n\n“And who may you be, brother, who seem to know my father’s name so well? For so far, if I remember rightly, I have not mentioned it in the whole story of my misfortunes.”\n\n“I am that unhappy being, señora,” replied Cardenio, “whom, as you have said, Luscinda declared to be her husband; I am the unfortunate Cardenio, whom the wrong-doing of him who has brought you to your present condition has reduced to the state you see me in, bare, ragged, bereft of all human comfort, and what is worse, of reason, for I only possess it when Heaven is pleased for some short space to restore it to me. I, Dorothea, am he who witnessed the wrong done by Don Fernando, and waited to hear the ‘Yes’ uttered by which Luscinda owned herself his betrothed: I am he who had not courage enough to see how her fainting fit ended, or what came of the paper that was found in her bosom, because my heart had not the fortitude to endure so many strokes of ill-fortune at once; and so losing patience I quitted the house, and leaving a letter with my host, which I entreated him to place in Luscinda’s hands, I betook myself to these solitudes, resolved to end here the life I hated as if it were my mortal enemy. But fate would not rid me of it, contenting itself with robbing me of my reason, perhaps to preserve me for the good fortune I have had in meeting you; for if that which you have just told us be true, as I believe it to be, it may be that Heaven has yet in store for both of us a happier termination to our misfortunes than we look for; because seeing that Luscinda cannot marry Don Fernando, being mine, as she has herself so openly declared, and that Don Fernando cannot marry her as he is yours, we may reasonably hope that Heaven will restore to us what is ours, as it is still in existence and not yet alienated or destroyed. And as we have this consolation springing from no very visionary hope or wild fancy, I entreat you, señora, to form new resolutions in your better mind, as I mean to do in mine, preparing yourself to look forward to happier fortunes; for I swear to you by the faith of a gentleman and a Christian not to desert you until I see you in possession of Don Fernando, and if I cannot by words induce him to recognise his obligation to you, in that case to avail myself of the right which my rank as a gentleman gives me, and with just cause challenge him on account of the injury he has done you, not regarding my own wrongs, which I shall leave to Heaven to avenge, while I on earth devote myself to yours.”\n\nCardenio’s words completed the astonishment of Dorothea, and not knowing how to return thanks for such an offer, she attempted to kiss his feet; but Cardenio would not permit it, and the licentiate replied for both, commended the sound reasoning of Cardenio, and lastly, begged, advised, and urged them to come with him to his village, where they might furnish themselves with what they needed, and take measures to discover Don Fernando, or restore Dorothea to her parents, or do what seemed to them most advisable. Cardenio and Dorothea thanked him, and accepted the kind offer he made them; and the barber, who had been listening to all attentively and in silence, on his part some kindly words also, and with no less good-will than the curate offered his services in any way that might be of use to them. He also explained to them in a few words the object that had brought them there, and the strange nature of Don Quixote’s madness, and how they were waiting for his squire, who had gone in search of him. Like the recollection of a dream, the quarrel he had had with Don Quixote came back to Cardenio’s memory, and he described it to the others; but he was unable to say what the dispute was about.\n\nAt this moment they heard a shout, and recognised it as coming from Sancho Panza, who, not finding them where he had left them, was calling aloud to them. They went to meet him, and in answer to their inquiries about Don Quixote, he told them how he had found him stripped to his shirt, lank, yellow, half dead with hunger, and sighing for his lady Dulcinea; and although he had told him that she commanded him to quit that place and come to El Toboso, where she was expecting him, he had answered that he was determined not to appear in the presence of her beauty until he had done deeds to make him worthy of her favour; and if this went on, Sancho said, he ran the risk of not becoming an emperor as in duty bound, or even an archbishop, which was the least he could be; for which reason they ought to consider what was to be done to get him away from there. The licentiate in reply told him not to be uneasy, for they would fetch him away in spite of himself. He then told Cardenio and Dorothea what they had proposed to do to cure Don Quixote, or at any rate take him home; upon which Dorothea said that she could play the distressed damsel better than the barber; especially as she had there the dress in which to do it to the life, and that they might trust to her acting the part in every particular requisite for carrying out their scheme, for she had read a great many books of chivalry, and knew exactly the style in which afflicted damsels begged boons of knights-errant.\n\n“In that case,” said the curate, “there is nothing more required than to set about it at once, for beyond a doubt fortune is declaring itself in our favour, since it has so unexpectedly begun to open a door for your relief, and smoothed the way for us to our object.”\n\nDorothea then took out of her pillow-case a complete petticoat of some rich stuff, and a green mantle of some other fine material, and a necklace and other ornaments out of a little box, and with these in an instant she so arrayed herself that she looked like a great and rich lady. All this, and more, she said, she had taken from home in case of need, but that until then she had had no occasion to make use of it. They were all highly delighted with her grace, air, and beauty, and declared Don Fernando to be a man of very little taste when he rejected such charms. But the one who admired her most was Sancho Panza, for it seemed to him (what indeed was true) that in all the days of his life he had never seen such a lovely creature; and he asked the curate with great eagerness who this beautiful lady was, and what she wanted in these out-of-the-way quarters.\n\n“This fair lady, brother Sancho,” replied the curate, “is no less a personage than the heiress in the direct male line of the great kingdom of Micomicon, who has come in search of your master to beg a boon of him, which is that he redress a wrong or injury that a wicked giant has done her; and from the fame as a good knight which your master has acquired far and wide, this princess has come from Guinea to seek him.”\n\n“A lucky seeking and a lucky finding!” said Sancho Panza at this; “especially if my master has the good fortune to redress that injury, and right that wrong, and kill that son of a bitch of a giant your worship speaks of; as kill him he will if he meets him, unless, indeed, he happens to be a phantom; for my master has no power at all against phantoms. But one thing among others I would beg of you, señor licentiate, which is, that, to prevent my master taking a fancy to be an archbishop, for that is what I’m afraid of, your worship would recommend him to marry this princess at once; for in this way he will be disabled from taking archbishop’s orders, and will easily come into his empire, and I to the end of my desires; I have been thinking over the matter carefully, and by what I can make out I find it will not do for me that my master should become an archbishop, because I am no good for the Church, as I am married; and for me now, having as I have a wife and children, to set about obtaining dispensations to enable me to hold a place of profit under the Church, would be endless work; so that, señor, it all turns on my master marrying this lady at once--for as yet I do not know her grace, and so I cannot call her by her name.”\n\n“She is called the Princess Micomicona,” said the curate; “for as her kingdom is Micomicon, it is clear that must be her name.”\n\n“There’s no doubt of that,” replied Sancho, “for I have known many to take their name and title from the place where they were born and call themselves Pedro of Alcala, Juan of Ubeda, and Diego of Valladolid; and it may be that over there in Guinea queens have the same way of taking the names of their kingdoms.”\n\n“So it may,” said the curate; “and as for your master’s marrying, I will do all in my power towards it:” with which Sancho was as much pleased as the curate was amazed at his simplicity and at seeing what a hold the absurdities of his master had taken of his fancy, for he had evidently persuaded himself that he was going to be an emperor.\n\nBy this time Dorothea had seated herself upon the curate’s mule, and the barber had fitted the ox-tail beard to his face, and they now told Sancho to conduct them to where Don Quixote was, warning him not to say that he knew either the licentiate or the barber, as his master’s becoming an emperor entirely depended on his not recognising them; neither the curate nor Cardenio, however, thought fit to go with them; Cardenio lest he should remind Don Quixote of the quarrel he had with him, and the curate as there was no necessity for his presence just yet, so they allowed the others to go on before them, while they themselves followed slowly on foot. The curate did not forget to instruct Dorothea how to act, but she said they might make their minds easy, as everything would be done exactly as the books of chivalry required and described.\n\nThey had gone about three-quarters of a league when they discovered Don Quixote in a wilderness of rocks, by this time clothed, but without his armour; and as soon as Dorothea saw him and was told by Sancho that that was Don Quixote, she whipped her palfrey, the well-bearded barber following her, and on coming up to him her squire sprang from his mule and came forward to receive her in his arms, and she dismounting with great ease of manner advanced to kneel before the feet of Don Quixote; and though he strove to raise her up, she without rising addressed him in this fashion:\n\n“From this spot I will not rise, valiant and doughty knight, until your goodness and courtesy grant me a boon, which will redound to the honour and renown of your person and render a service to the most disconsolate and afflicted damsel the sun has seen; and if the might of your strong arm corresponds to the repute of your immortal fame, you are bound to aid the helpless being who, led by the savour of your renowned name, hath come from far distant lands to seek your aid in her misfortunes.”\n\n“I will not answer a word, beauteous lady,” replied Don Quixote, “nor will I listen to anything further concerning you, until you rise from the earth.”\n\n“I will not rise, señor,” answered the afflicted damsel, “unless of your courtesy the boon I ask is first granted me.”\n\n“I grant and accord it,” said Don Quixote, “provided without detriment or prejudice to my king, my country, or her who holds the key of my heart and freedom, it may be complied with.”\n\n“It will not be to the detriment or prejudice of any of them, my worthy lord,” said the afflicted damsel; and here Sancho Panza drew close to his master’s ear and said to him very softly, “Your worship may very safely grant the boon she asks; it’s nothing at all; only to kill a big giant; and she who asks it is the exalted Princess Micomicona, queen of the great kingdom of Micomicon of Ethiopia.”\n\n“Let her be who she may,” replied Don Quixote, “I will do what is my bounden duty, and what my conscience bids me, in conformity with what I have professed;” and turning to the damsel he said, “Let your great beauty rise, for I grant the boon which you would ask of me.”\n\n“Then what I ask,” said the damsel, “is that your magnanimous person accompany me at once whither I will conduct you, and that you promise not to engage in any other adventure or quest until you have avenged me of a traitor who against all human and divine law, has usurped my kingdom.”\n\n“I repeat that I grant it,” replied Don Quixote; “and so, lady, you may from this day forth lay aside the melancholy that distresses you, and let your failing hopes gather new life and strength, for with the help of God and of my arm you will soon see yourself restored to your kingdom, and seated upon the throne of your ancient and mighty realm, notwithstanding and despite of the felons who would gainsay it; and now hands to the work, for in delay there is apt to be danger.”\n\nThe distressed damsel strove with much pertinacity to kiss his hands; but Don Quixote, who was in all things a polished and courteous knight, would by no means allow it, but made her rise and embraced her with great courtesy and politeness, and ordered Sancho to look to Rocinante’s girths, and to arm him without a moment’s delay. Sancho took down the armour, which was hung up on a tree like a trophy, and having seen to the girths armed his master in a trice, who as soon as he found himself in his armour exclaimed:\n\n“Let us be gone in the name of God to bring aid to this great lady.”\n\nThe barber was all this time on his knees at great pains to hide his laughter and not let his beard fall, for had it fallen maybe their fine scheme would have come to nothing; but now seeing the boon granted, and the promptitude with which Don Quixote prepared to set out in compliance with it, he rose and took his lady’s hand, and between them they placed her upon the mule. Don Quixote then mounted Rocinante, and the barber settled himself on his beast, Sancho being left to go on foot, which made him feel anew the loss of his Dapple, finding the want of him now. But he bore all with cheerfulness, being persuaded that his master had now fairly started and was just on the point of becoming an emperor; for he felt no doubt at all that he would marry this princess, and be king of Micomicon at least. The only thing that troubled him was the reflection that this kingdom was in the land of the blacks, and that the people they would give him for vassals would be all black; but for this he soon found a remedy in his fancy, and said he to himself, “What is it to me if my vassals are blacks? What more have I to do than make a cargo of them and carry them to Spain, where I can sell them and get ready money for them, and with it buy some title or some office in which to live at ease all the days of my life? Not unless you go to sleep and haven’t the wit or skill to turn things to account and sell three, six, or ten thousand vassals while you would be talking about it! By God I will stir them up, big and little, or as best I can, and let them be ever so black I’ll turn them into white or yellow. Come, come, what a fool I am!” And so he jogged on, so occupied with his thoughts and easy in his mind that he forgot all about the hardship of travelling on foot.\n\nCardenio and the curate were watching all this from among some bushes, not knowing how to join company with the others; but the curate, who was very fertile in devices, soon hit upon a way of effecting their purpose, and with a pair of scissors he had in a case he quickly cut off Cardenio’s beard, and putting on him a grey jerkin of his own he gave him a black cloak, leaving himself in his breeches and doublet, while Cardenio’s appearance was so different from what it had been that he would not have known himself had he seen himself in a mirror. Having effected this, although the others had gone on ahead while they were disguising themselves, they easily came out on the high road before them, for the brambles and awkward places they encountered did not allow those on horseback to go as fast as those on foot. They then posted themselves on the level ground at the outlet of the Sierra, and as soon as Don Quixote and his companions emerged from it the curate began to examine him very deliberately, as though he were striving to recognise him, and after having stared at him for some time he hastened towards him with open arms exclaiming, “A happy meeting with the mirror of chivalry, my worthy compatriot Don Quixote of La Mancha, the flower and cream of high breeding, the protection and relief of the distressed, the quintessence of knights-errant!” And so saying he clasped in his arms the knee of Don Quixote’s left leg. He, astonished at the stranger’s words and behaviour, looked at him attentively, and at length recognised him, very much surprised to see him there, and made great efforts to dismount. This, however, the curate would not allow, on which Don Quixote said, “Permit me, señor licentiate, for it is not fitting that I should be on horseback and so reverend a person as your worship on foot.”\n\n“On no account will I allow it,” said the curate; “your mightiness must remain on horseback, for it is on horseback you achieve the greatest deeds and adventures that have been beheld in our age; as for me, an unworthy priest, it will serve me well enough to mount on the haunches of one of the mules of these gentlefolk who accompany your worship, if they have no objection, and I will fancy I am mounted on the steed Pegasus, or on the zebra or charger that bore the famous Moor, Muzaraque, who to this day lies enchanted in the great hill of Zulema, a little distance from the great Complutum.”\n\n“Nor even that will I consent to, señor licentiate,” answered Don Quixote, “and I know it will be the good pleasure of my lady the princess, out of love for me, to order her squire to give up the saddle of his mule to your worship, and he can sit behind if the beast will bear it.”\n\n“It will, I am sure,” said the princess, “and I am sure, too, that I need not order my squire, for he is too courteous and considerate to allow a Churchman to go on foot when he might be mounted.”\n\n“That he is,” said the barber, and at once alighting, he offered his saddle to the curate, who accepted it without much entreaty; but unfortunately as the barber was mounting behind, the mule, being as it happened a hired one, which is the same thing as saying ill-conditioned, lifted its hind hoofs and let fly a couple of kicks in the air, which would have made Master Nicholas wish his expedition in quest of Don Quixote at the devil had they caught him on the breast or head. As it was, they so took him by surprise that he came to the ground, giving so little heed to his beard that it fell off, and all he could do when he found himself without it was to cover his face hastily with both his hands and moan that his teeth were knocked out. Don Quixote when he saw all that bundle of beard detached, without jaws or blood, from the face of the fallen squire, exclaimed:\n\n“By the living God, but this is a great miracle! it has knocked off and plucked away the beard from his face as if it had been shaved off designedly.”\n\nThe curate, seeing the danger of discovery that threatened his scheme, at once pounced upon the beard and hastened with it to where Master Nicholas lay, still uttering moans, and drawing his head to his breast had it on in an instant, muttering over him some words which he said were a certain special charm for sticking on beards, as they would see; and as soon as he had it fixed he left him, and the squire appeared well bearded and whole as before, whereat Don Quixote was beyond measure astonished, and begged the curate to teach him that charm when he had an opportunity, as he was persuaded its virtue must extend beyond the sticking on of beards, for it was clear that where the beard had been stripped off the flesh must have remained torn and lacerated, and when it could heal all that it must be good for more than beards.\n\n“And so it is,” said the curate, and he promised to teach it to him on the first opportunity. They then agreed that for the present the curate should mount, and that the three should ride by turns until they reached the inn, which might be about six leagues from where they were.\n\nThree then being mounted, that is to say, Don Quixote, the princess, and the curate, and three on foot, Cardenio, the barber, and Sancho Panza, Don Quixote said to the damsel:\n\n“Let your highness, lady, lead on whithersoever is most pleasing to you;” but before she could answer the licentiate said:\n\n“Towards what kingdom would your ladyship direct our course? Is it perchance towards that of Micomicon? It must be, or else I know little about kingdoms.”\n\nShe, being ready on all points, understood that she was to answer “Yes,” so she said “Yes, señor, my way lies towards that kingdom.”\n\n“In that case,” said the curate, “we must pass right through my village, and there your worship will take the road to Cartagena, where you will be able to embark, fortune favouring; and if the wind be fair and the sea smooth and tranquil, in somewhat less than nine years you may come in sight of the great lake Meona, I mean Meotides, which is little more than a hundred days’ journey this side of your highness’s kingdom.”\n\n“Your worship is mistaken, señor,” said she; “for it is not two years since I set out from it, and though I never had good weather, nevertheless I am here to behold what I so longed for, and that is my lord Don Quixote of La Mancha, whose fame came to my ears as soon as I set foot in Spain and impelled me to go in search of him, to commend myself to his courtesy, and entrust the justice of my cause to the might of his invincible arm.”\n\n“Enough; no more praise,” said Don Quixote at this, “for I hate all flattery; and though this may not be so, still language of the kind is offensive to my chaste ears. I will only say, señora, that whether it has might or not, that which it may or may not have shall be devoted to your service even to death; and now, leaving this to its proper season, I would ask the señor licentiate to tell me what it is that has brought him into these parts, alone, unattended, and so lightly clad that I am filled with amazement.”\n\n“I will answer that briefly,” replied the curate; “you must know then, Señor Don Quixote, that Master Nicholas, our friend and barber, and I were going to Seville to receive some money that a relative of mine who went to the Indies many years ago had sent me, and not such a small sum but that it was over sixty thousand pieces of eight, full weight, which is something; and passing by this place yesterday we were attacked by four footpads, who stripped us even to our beards, and them they stripped off so that the barber found it necessary to put on a false one, and even this young man here”--pointing to Cardenio--“they completely transformed. But the best of it is, the story goes in the neighbourhood that those who attacked us belong to a number of galley slaves who, they say, were set free almost on the very same spot by a man of such valour that, in spite of the commissary and of the guards, he released the whole of them; and beyond all doubt he must have been out of his senses, or he must be as great a scoundrel as they, or some man without heart or conscience to let the wolf loose among the sheep, the fox among the hens, the fly among the honey. He has defrauded justice, and opposed his king and lawful master, for he opposed his just commands; he has, I say, robbed the galleys of their feet, stirred up the Holy Brotherhood which for many years past has been quiet, and, lastly, has done a deed by which his soul may be lost without any gain to his body.” Sancho had told the curate and the barber of the adventure of the galley slaves, which, so much to his glory, his master had achieved, and hence the curate in alluding to it made the most of it to see what would be said or done by Don Quixote; who changed colour at every word, not daring to say that it was he who had been the liberator of those worthy people. “These, then,” said the curate, “were they who robbed us; and God in his mercy pardon him who would not let them go to the punishment they deserved.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXX.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF ADDRESS DISPLAYED BY THE FAIR DOROTHEA, WITH OTHER MATTERS PLEASANT AND AMUSING\nThe curate had hardly ceased speaking, when Sancho said, “In faith, then, señor licentiate, he who did that deed was my master; and it was not for want of my telling him beforehand and warning him to mind what he was about, and that it was a sin to set them at liberty, as they were all on the march there because they were special scoundrels.”\n\n“Blockhead!” said Don Quixote at this, “it is no business or concern of knights-errant to inquire whether any persons in affliction, in chains, or oppressed that they may meet on the high roads go that way and suffer as they do because of their faults or because of their misfortunes. It only concerns them to aid them as persons in need of help, having regard to their sufferings and not to their rascalities. I encountered a chaplet or string of miserable and unfortunate people, and did for them what my sense of duty demands of me, and as for the rest be that as it may; and whoever takes objection to it, saving the sacred dignity of the señor licentiate and his honoured person, I say he knows little about chivalry and lies like a whoreson villain, and this I will give him to know to the fullest extent with my sword;” and so saying he settled himself in his stirrups and pressed down his morion; for the barber’s basin, which according to him was Mambrino’s helmet, he carried hanging at the saddle-bow until he could repair the damage done to it by the galley slaves.\n\nDorothea, who was shrewd and sprightly, and by this time thoroughly understood Don Quixote’s crazy turn, and that all except Sancho Panza were making game of him, not to be behind the rest said to him, on observing his irritation, “Sir Knight, remember the boon you have promised me, and that in accordance with it you must not engage in any other adventure, be it ever so pressing; calm yourself, for if the licentiate had known that the galley slaves had been set free by that unconquered arm he would have stopped his mouth thrice over, or even bitten his tongue three times before he would have said a word that tended towards disrespect of your worship.”\n\n“That I swear heartily,” said the curate, “and I would have even plucked off a moustache.”\n\n“I will hold my peace, señora,” said Don Quixote, “and I will curb the natural anger that had arisen in my breast, and will proceed in peace and quietness until I have fulfilled my promise; but in return for this consideration I entreat you to tell me, if you have no objection to do so, what is the nature of your trouble, and how many, who, and what are the persons of whom I am to require due satisfaction, and on whom I am to take vengeance on your behalf?”\n\n“That I will do with all my heart,” replied Dorothea, “if it will not be wearisome to you to hear of miseries and misfortunes.”\n\n“It will not be wearisome, señora,” said Don Quixote; to which Dorothea replied, “Well, if that be so, give me your attention.” As soon as she said this, Cardenio and the barber drew close to her side, eager to hear what sort of story the quick-witted Dorothea would invent for herself; and Sancho did the same, for he was as much taken in by her as his master; and she having settled herself comfortably in the saddle, and with the help of coughing and other preliminaries taken time to think, began with great sprightliness of manner in this fashion.\n\n“First of all, I would have you know, sirs, that my name is-” and here she stopped for a moment, for she forgot the name the curate had given her; but he came to her relief, seeing what her difficulty was, and said, “It is no wonder, señora, that your highness should be confused and embarrassed in telling the tale of your misfortunes; for such afflictions often have the effect of depriving the sufferers of memory, so that they do not even remember their own names, as is the case now with your ladyship, who has forgotten that she is called the Princess Micomicona, lawful heiress of the great kingdom of Micomicon; and with this cue your highness may now recall to your sorrowful recollection all you may wish to tell us.”\n\n“That is the truth,” said the damsel; “but I think from this on I shall have no need of any prompting, and I shall bring my true story safe into port, and here it is. The king my father, who was called Tinacrio the Sapient, was very learned in what they call magic arts, and became aware by his craft that my mother, who was called Queen Jaramilla, was to die before he did, and that soon after he too was to depart this life, and I was to be left an orphan without father or mother. But all this, he declared, did not so much grieve or distress him as his certain knowledge that a prodigious giant, the lord of a great island close to our kingdom, Pandafilando of the Scowl by name--for it is averred that, though his eyes are properly placed and straight, he always looks askew as if he squinted, and this he does out of malignity, to strike fear and terror into those he looks at--that he knew, I say, that this giant on becoming aware of my orphan condition would overrun my kingdom with a mighty force and strip me of all, not leaving me even a small village to shelter me; but that I could avoid all this ruin and misfortune if I were willing to marry him; however, as far as he could see, he never expected that I would consent to a marriage so unequal; and he said no more than the truth in this, for it has never entered my mind to marry that giant, or any other, let him be ever so great or enormous. My father said, too, that when he was dead, and I saw Pandafilando about to invade my kingdom, I was not to wait and attempt to defend myself, for that would be destructive to me, but that I should leave the kingdom entirely open to him if I wished to avoid the death and total destruction of my good and loyal vassals, for there would be no possibility of defending myself against the giant’s devilish power; and that I should at once with some of my followers set out for Spain, where I should obtain relief in my distress on finding a certain knight-errant whose fame by that time would extend over the whole kingdom, and who would be called, if I remember rightly, Don Azote or Don Gigote.”\n\n“‘Don Quixote,’ he must have said, señora,” observed Sancho at this, “otherwise called the Knight of the Rueful Countenance.”\n\n“That is it,” said Dorothea; “he said, moreover, that he would be tall of stature and lank featured; and that on his right side under the left shoulder, or thereabouts, he would have a grey mole with hairs like bristles.”\n\nOn hearing this, Don Quixote said to his squire, “Here, Sancho my son, bear a hand and help me to strip, for I want to see if I am the knight that sage king foretold.”\n\n“What does your worship want to strip for?” said Dorothea.\n\n“To see if I have that mole your father spoke of,” answered Don Quixote.\n\n“There is no occasion to strip,” said Sancho; “for I know your worship has just such a mole on the middle of your backbone, which is the mark of a strong man.”\n\n“That is enough,” said Dorothea, “for with friends we must not look too closely into trifles; and whether it be on the shoulder or on the backbone matters little; it is enough if there is a mole, be it where it may, for it is all the same flesh; no doubt my good father hit the truth in every particular, and I have made a lucky hit in commending myself to Don Quixote; for he is the one my father spoke of, as the features of his countenance correspond with those assigned to this knight by that wide fame he has acquired not only in Spain but in all La Mancha; for I had scarcely landed at Osuna when I heard such accounts of his achievements, that at once my heart told me he was the very one I had come in search of.”\n\n“But how did you land at Osuna, señora,” asked Don Quixote, “when it is not a seaport?”\n\nBut before Dorothea could reply the curate anticipated her, saying, “The princess meant to say that after she had landed at Malaga the first place where she heard of your worship was Osuna.”\n\n“That is what I meant to say,” said Dorothea.\n\n“And that would be only natural,” said the curate. “Will your majesty please proceed?”\n\n“There is no more to add,” said Dorothea, “save that in finding Don Quixote I have had such good fortune, that I already reckon and regard myself queen and mistress of my entire dominions, since of his courtesy and magnanimity he has granted me the boon of accompanying me whithersoever I may conduct him, which will be only to bring him face to face with Pandafilando of the Scowl, that he may slay him and restore to me what has been unjustly usurped by him: for all this must come to pass satisfactorily since my good father Tinacrio the Sapient foretold it, who likewise left it declared in writing in Chaldee or Greek characters (for I cannot read them), that if this predicted knight, after having cut the giant’s throat, should be disposed to marry me I was to offer myself at once without demur as his lawful wife, and yield him possession of my kingdom together with my person.”\n\n“What thinkest thou now, friend Sancho?” said Don Quixote at this. “Hearest thou that? Did I not tell thee so? See how we have already got a kingdom to govern and a queen to marry!”\n\n“On my oath it is so,” said Sancho; “and foul fortune to him who won’t marry after slitting Señor Pandahilado’s windpipe! And then, how ill-favoured the queen is! I wish the fleas in my bed were that sort!”\nAnd so saying he cut a couple of capers in the air with every sign of extreme satisfaction, and then ran to seize the bridle of Dorothea’s mule, and checking it fell on his knees before her, begging her to give him her hand to kiss in token of his acknowledgment of her as his queen and mistress. Which of the bystanders could have helped laughing to see the madness of the master and the simplicity of the servant? Dorothea therefore gave her hand, and promised to make him a great lord in her kingdom, when Heaven should be so good as to permit her to recover and enjoy it, for which Sancho returned thanks in words that set them all laughing again.\n\n“This, sirs,” continued Dorothea, “is my story; it only remains to tell you that of all the attendants I took with me from my kingdom I have none left except this well-bearded squire, for all were drowned in a great tempest we encountered when in sight of port; and he and I came to land on a couple of planks as if by a miracle; and indeed the whole course of my life is a miracle and a mystery as you may have observed; and if I have been over minute in any respect or not as precise as I ought, let it be accounted for by what the licentiate said at the beginning of my tale, that constant and excessive troubles deprive the sufferers of their memory.”\n\n“They shall not deprive me of mine, exalted and worthy princess,” said Don Quixote, “however great and unexampled those which I shall endure in your service may be; and here I confirm anew the boon I have promised you, and I swear to go with you to the end of the world until I find myself in the presence of your fierce enemy, whose haughty head I trust by the aid of my arm to cut off with the edge of this--I will not say good sword, thanks to Gines de Pasamonte who carried away mine”--(this he said between his teeth, and then continued), “and when it has been cut off and you have been put in peaceful possession of your realm it shall be left to your own decision to dispose of your person as may be most pleasing to you; for so long as my memory is occupied, my will enslaved, and my understanding enthralled by her-I say no more--it is impossible for me for a moment to contemplate marriage, even with a Phoenix.”\n\nThe last words of his master about not wanting to marry were so disagreeable to Sancho that raising his voice he exclaimed with great irritation:\n\n“By my oath, Señor Don Quixote, you are not in your right senses; for how can your worship possibly object to marrying such an exalted princess as this? Do you think Fortune will offer you behind every stone such a piece of luck as is offered you now? Is my lady Dulcinea fairer, perchance? Not she; nor half as fair; and I will even go so far as to say she does not come up to the shoe of this one here. A poor chance I have of getting that county I am waiting for if your worship goes looking for dainties in the bottom of the sea. In the devil’s name, marry, marry, and take this kingdom that comes to hand without any trouble, and when you are king make me a marquis or governor of a province, and for the rest let the devil take it all.”\n\nDon Quixote, when he heard such blasphemies uttered against his lady Dulcinea, could not endure it, and lifting his pike, without saying anything to Sancho or uttering a word, he gave him two such thwacks that he brought him to the ground; and had it not been that Dorothea cried out to him to spare him he would have no doubt taken his life on the spot.\n\n“Do you think,” he said to him after a pause, “you scurvy clown, that you are to be always interfering with me, and that you are to be always offending and I always pardoning? Don’t fancy it, impious scoundrel, for that beyond a doubt thou art, since thou hast set thy tongue going against the peerless Dulcinea. Know you not, lout, vagabond, beggar, that were it not for the might that she infuses into my arm I should not have strength enough to kill a flea? Say, scoffer with a viper’s tongue, what think you has won this kingdom and cut off this giant’s head and made you a marquis (for all this I count as already accomplished and decided), but the might of Dulcinea, employing my arm as the instrument of her achievements? She fights in me and conquers in me, and I live and breathe in her, and owe my life and being to her. O whoreson scoundrel, how ungrateful you are, you see yourself raised from the dust of the earth to be a titled lord, and the return you make for so great a benefit is to speak evil of her who has conferred it upon you!”\n\nSancho was not so stunned but that he heard all his master said, and rising with some degree of nimbleness he ran to place himself behind Dorothea’s palfrey, and from that position he said to his master:\n\n“Tell me, señor; if your worship is resolved not to marry this great princess, it is plain the kingdom will not be yours; and not being so, how can you bestow favours upon me? That is what I complain of. Let your worship at any rate marry this queen, now that we have got her here as if showered down from heaven, and afterwards you may go back to my lady Dulcinea; for there must have been kings in the world who kept mistresses. As to beauty, I have nothing to do with it; and if the truth is to be told, I like them both; though I have never seen the lady Dulcinea.”\n\n“How! never seen her, blasphemous traitor!” exclaimed Don Quixote; “hast thou not just now brought me a message from her?”\n\n“I mean,” said Sancho, “that I did not see her so much at my leisure that I could take particular notice of her beauty, or of her charms piecemeal; but taken in the lump I like her.”\n\n“Now I forgive thee,” said Don Quixote; “and do thou forgive me the injury I have done thee; for our first impulses are not in our control.”\n\n“That I see,” replied Sancho, “and with me the wish to speak is always the first impulse, and I cannot help saying, once at any rate, what I have on the tip of my tongue.”\n\n“For all that, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “take heed of what thou sayest, for the pitcher goes so often to the well--I need say no more to thee.”\n\n“Well, well,” said Sancho, “God is in heaven, and sees all tricks, and will judge who does most harm, I in not speaking right, or your worship in not doing it.”\n\n“That is enough,” said Dorothea; “run, Sancho, and kiss your lord’s hand and beg his pardon, and henceforward be more circumspect with your praise and abuse; and say nothing in disparagement of that lady Toboso, of whom I know nothing save that I am her servant; and put your trust in God, for you will not fail to obtain some dignity so as to live like a prince.”\n\nSancho advanced hanging his head and begged his master’s hand, which Don Quixote with dignity presented to him, giving him his blessing as soon as he had kissed it; he then bade him go on ahead a little, as he had questions to ask him and matters of great importance to discuss with him. Sancho obeyed, and when the two had gone some distance in advance Don Quixote said to him, “Since thy return I have had no opportunity or time to ask thee many particulars touching thy mission and the answer thou hast brought back, and now that chance has granted us the time and opportunity, deny me not the happiness thou canst give me by such good news.”\n\n“Let your worship ask what you will,” answered Sancho, “for I shall find a way out of all as as I found a way in; but I implore you, señor, not not to be so revengeful in future.”\n\n“Why dost thou say that, Sancho?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“I say it,” he returned, “because those blows just now were more because of the quarrel the devil stirred up between us both the other night, than for what I said against my lady Dulcinea, whom I love and reverence as I would a relic--though there is nothing of that about her--merely as something belonging to your worship.”\n\n“Say no more on that subject for thy life, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “for it is displeasing to me; I have already pardoned thee for that, and thou knowest the common saying, ‘for a fresh sin a fresh penance.’”\n\nWhile this was going on they saw coming along the road they were following a man mounted on an ass, who when he came close seemed to be a gipsy; but Sancho Panza, whose eyes and heart were there wherever he saw asses, no sooner beheld the man than he knew him to be Gines de Pasamonte; and by the thread of the gipsy he got at the ball, his ass, for it was, in fact, Dapple that carried Pasamonte, who to escape recognition and to sell the ass had disguised himself as a gipsy, being able to speak the gipsy language, and many more, as well as if they were his own. Sancho saw him and recognised him, and the instant he did so he shouted to him, “Ginesillo, you thief, give up my treasure, release my life, embarrass thyself not with my repose, quit my ass, leave my delight, be off, rip, get thee gone, thief, and give up what is not thine.”\n\nThere was no necessity for so many words or objurgations, for at the first one Gines jumped down, and at a like racing speed made off and got clear of them all. Sancho hastened to his Dapple, and embracing him he said, “How hast thou fared, my blessing, Dapple of my eyes, my comrade?” all the while kissing him and caressing him as if he were a human being. The ass held his peace, and let himself be kissed and caressed by Sancho without answering a single word. They all came up and congratulated him on having found Dapple, Don Quixote especially, who told him that notwithstanding this he would not cancel the order for the three ass-colts, for which Sancho thanked him.\n\nWhile the two had been going along conversing in this fashion, the curate observed to Dorothea that she had shown great cleverness, as well in the story itself as in its conciseness, and the resemblance it bore to those of the books of chivalry. She said that she had many times amused herself reading them; but that she did not know the situation of the provinces or seaports, and so she had said at haphazard that she had landed at Osuna.\n\n“So I saw,” said the curate, “and for that reason I made haste to say what I did, by which it was all set right. But is it not a strange thing to see how readily this unhappy gentleman believes all these figments and lies, simply because they are in the style and manner of the absurdities of his books?”\n\n“So it is,” said Cardenio; “and so uncommon and unexampled, that were one to attempt to invent and concoct it in fiction, I doubt if there be any wit keen enough to imagine it.”\n\n“But another strange thing about it,” said the curate, “is that, apart from the silly things which this worthy gentleman says in connection with his craze, when other subjects are dealt with, he can discuss them in a perfectly rational manner, showing that his mind is quite clear and composed; so that, provided his chivalry is not touched upon, no one would take him to be anything but a man of thoroughly sound understanding.”\n\nWhile they were holding this conversation Don Quixote continued his with Sancho, saying:\n\n“Friend Panza, let us forgive and forget as to our quarrels, and tell me now, dismissing anger and irritation, where, how, and when didst thou find Dulcinea? What was she doing? What didst thou say to her? What did she answer? How did she look when she was reading my letter? Who copied it out for thee? and everything in the matter that seems to thee worth knowing, asking, and learning; neither adding nor falsifying to give me pleasure, nor yet curtailing lest you should deprive me of it.”\n\n“Señor,” replied Sancho, “if the truth is to be told, nobody copied out the letter for me, for I carried no letter at all.”\n\n“It is as thou sayest,” said Don Quixote, “for the note-book in which I wrote it I found in my own possession two days after thy departure, which gave me very great vexation, as I knew not what thou wouldst do on finding thyself without any letter; and I made sure thou wouldst return from the place where thou didst first miss it.”\n\n“So I should have done,” said Sancho, “if I had not got it by heart when your worship read it to me, so that I repeated it to a sacristan, who copied it out for me from hearing it, so exactly that he said in all the days of his life, though he had read many a letter of excommunication, he had never seen or read so pretty a letter as that.”\n\n“And hast thou got it still in thy memory, Sancho?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“No, señor,” replied Sancho, “for as soon as I had repeated it, seeing there was no further use for it, I set about forgetting it; and if I recollect any of it, it is that about ‘Scrubbing,’ I mean to say ‘Sovereign Lady,’ and the end ‘Yours till death, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance;’ and between these two I put into it more than three hundred ‘my souls’ and ‘my life’s’ and ‘my eyes.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI.\n\nOF THE DELECTABLE DISCUSSION BETWEEN DON QUIXOTE AND SANCHO PANZA, HIS SQUIRE, TOGETHER WITH OTHER INCIDENTS\n“All that is not unsatisfactory to me,” said Don Quixote. “Go on; thou didst reach her; and what was that queen of beauty doing? Surely thou didst find her stringing pearls, or embroidering some device in gold thread for this her enslaved knight.”\n\n“I did not,” said Sancho, “but I found her winnowing two bushels of wheat in the yard of her house.”\n\n“Then depend upon it,” said Don Quixote, “the grains of that wheat were pearls when touched by her hands; and didst thou look, friend? was it white wheat or brown?”\n\n“It was neither, but red,” said Sancho.\n\n“Then I promise thee,” said Don Quixote, “that, winnowed by her hands, beyond a doubt the bread it made was of the whitest; but go on; when thou gavest her my letter, did she kiss it? Did she place it on her head? Did she perform any ceremony befitting it, or what did she do?”\n\n“When I went to give it to her,” replied Sancho, “she was hard at it swaying from side to side with a lot of wheat she had in the sieve, and she said to me, ‘Lay the letter, friend, on the top of that sack, for I cannot read it until I have done sifting all this.”\n\n“Discreet lady!” said Don Quixote; “that was in order to read it at her leisure and enjoy it; proceed, Sancho; while she was engaged in her occupation what converse did she hold with thee? What did she ask about me, and what answer didst thou give? Make haste; tell me all, and let not an atom be left behind in the ink-bottle.”\n\n“She asked me nothing,” said Sancho; “but I told her how your worship was left doing penance in her service, naked from the waist up, in among these mountains like a savage, sleeping on the ground, not eating bread off a tablecloth nor combing your beard, weeping and cursing your fortune.”\n\n“In saying I cursed my fortune thou saidst wrong,” said Don Quixote; “for rather do I bless it and shall bless it all the days of my life for having made me worthy of aspiring to love so lofty a lady as Dulcinea del Toboso.”\n\n“And so lofty she is,” said Sancho, “that she overtops me by more than a hand’s-breadth.”\n\n“What! Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “didst thou measure with her?”\n\n“I measured in this way,” said Sancho; “going to help her to put a sack of wheat on the back of an ass, we came so close together that I could see she stood more than a good palm over me.”\n\n“Well!” said Don Quixote, “and doth she not of a truth accompany and adorn this greatness with a thousand million charms of mind! But one thing thou wilt not deny, Sancho; when thou camest close to her didst thou not perceive a Sabaean odour, an aromatic fragrance, a, I know not what, delicious, that I cannot find a name for; I mean a redolence, an exhalation, as if thou wert in the shop of some dainty glover?”\n\n“All I can say is,” said Sancho, “that I did perceive a little odour, something goaty; it must have been that she was all in a sweat with hard work.”\n\n“It could not be that,” said Don Quixote, “but thou must have been suffering from cold in the head, or must have smelt thyself; for I know well what would be the scent of that rose among thorns, that lily of the field, that dissolved amber.”\n\n“Maybe so,” replied Sancho; “there often comes from myself that same odour which then seemed to me to come from her grace the lady Dulcinea; but that’s no wonder, for one devil is like another.”\n\n“Well then,” continued Don Quixote, “now she has done sifting the corn and sent it to the mill; what did she do when she read the letter?”\n\n“As for the letter,” said Sancho, “she did not read it, for she said she could neither read nor write; instead of that she tore it up into small pieces, saying that she did not want to let anyone read it lest her secrets should become known in the village, and that what I had told her by word of mouth about the love your worship bore her, and the extraordinary penance you were doing for her sake, was enough; and, to make an end of it, she told me to tell your worship that she kissed your hands, and that she had a greater desire to see you than to write to you; and that therefore she entreated and commanded you, on sight of this present, to come out of these thickets, and to have done with carrying on absurdities, and to set out at once for El Toboso, unless something else of greater importance should happen, for she had a great desire to see your worship. She laughed greatly when I told her how your worship was called The Knight of the Rueful Countenance; I asked her if that Biscayan the other day had been there; and she told me he had, and that he was an honest fellow; I asked her too about the galley slaves, but she said she had not seen any as yet.”\n\n“So far all goes well,” said Don Quixote; “but tell me what jewel was it that she gave thee on taking thy leave, in return for thy tidings of me? For it is a usual and ancient custom with knights and ladies errant to give the squires, damsels, or dwarfs who bring tidings of their ladies to the knights, or of their knights to the ladies, some rich jewel as a guerdon for good news,’ and acknowledgment of the message.”\n\n“That is very likely,” said Sancho, “and a good custom it was, to my mind; but that must have been in days gone by, for now it would seem to be the custom only to give a piece of bread and cheese; because that was what my lady Dulcinea gave me over the top of the yard-wall when I took leave of her; and more by token it was sheep’s-milk cheese.”\n\n“She is generous in the extreme,” said Don Quixote, “and if she did not give thee a jewel of gold, no doubt it must have been because she had not one to hand there to give thee; but sleeves are good after Easter; I shall see her and all shall be made right. But knowest thou what amazes me, Sancho? It seems to me thou must have gone and come through the air, for thou hast taken but little more than three days to go to El Toboso and return, though it is more than thirty leagues from here to there. From which I am inclined to think that the sage magician who is my friend, and watches over my interests (for of necessity there is and must be one, or else I should not be a right knight-errant), that this same, I say, must have helped thee to travel without thy knowledge; for some of these sages will catch up a knight-errant sleeping in his bed, and without his knowing how or in what way it happened, he wakes up the next day more than a thousand leagues away from the place where he went to sleep. And if it were not for this, knights-errant would not be able to give aid to one another in peril, as they do at every turn. For a knight, maybe, is fighting in the mountains of Armenia with some dragon, or fierce serpent, or another knight, and gets the worst of the battle, and is at the point of death; but when he least looks for it, there appears over against him on a cloud, or chariot of fire, another knight, a friend of his, who just before had been in England, and who takes his part, and delivers him from death; and at night he finds himself in his own quarters supping very much to his satisfaction; and yet from one place to the other will have been two or three thousand leagues. And all this is done by the craft and skill of the sage enchanters who take care of those valiant knights; so that, friend Sancho, I find no difficulty in believing that thou mayest have gone from this place to El Toboso and returned in such a short time, since, as I have said, some friendly sage must have carried thee through the air without thee perceiving it.”\n\n“That must have been it,” said Sancho, “for indeed Rocinante went like a gipsy’s ass with quicksilver in his ears.”\n\n“Quicksilver!” said Don Quixote, “aye and what is more, a legion of devils, folk that can travel and make others travel without being weary, exactly as the whim seizes them. But putting this aside, what thinkest thou I ought to do about my lady’s command to go and see her? For though I feel that I am bound to obey her mandate, I feel too that I am debarred by the boon I have accorded to the princess that accompanies us, and the law of chivalry compels me to have regard for my word in preference to my inclination; on the one hand the desire to see my lady pursues and harasses me, on the other my solemn promise and the glory I shall win in this enterprise urge and call me; but what I think I shall do is to travel with all speed and reach quickly the place where this giant is, and on my arrival I shall cut off his head, and establish the princess peacefully in her realm, and forthwith I shall return to behold the light that lightens my senses, to whom I shall make such excuses that she will be led to approve of my delay, for she will see that it entirely tends to increase her glory and fame; for all that I have won, am winning, or shall win by arms in this life, comes to me of the favour she extends to me, and because I am hers.”\n\n“Ah! what a sad state your worship’s brains are in!” said Sancho. “Tell me, señor, do you mean to travel all that way for nothing, and to let slip and lose so rich and great a match as this where they give as a portion a kingdom that in sober truth I have heard say is more than twenty thousand leagues round about, and abounds with all things necessary to support human life, and is bigger than Portugal and Castile put together? Peace, for the love of God! Blush for what you have said, and take my advice, and forgive me, and marry at once in the first village where there is a curate; if not, here is our licentiate who will do the business beautifully; remember, I am old enough to give advice, and this I am giving comes pat to the purpose; for a sparrow in the hand is better than a vulture on the wing, and he who has the good to his hand and chooses the bad, that the good he complains of may not come to him.”\n\n“Look here, Sancho,” said Don Quixote. “If thou art advising me to marry, in order that immediately on slaying the giant I may become king, and be able to confer favours on thee, and give thee what I have promised, let me tell thee I shall be able very easily to satisfy thy desires without marrying; for before going into battle I will make it a stipulation that, if I come out of it victorious, even I do not marry, they shall give me a portion portion of the kingdom, that I may bestow it upon whomsoever I choose, and when they give it to me upon whom wouldst thou have me bestow it but upon thee?”\n\n“That is plain speaking,” said Sancho; “but let your worship take care to choose it on the seacoast, so that if I don’t like the life, I may be able to ship off my black vassals and deal with them as I have said; don’t mind going to see my lady Dulcinea now, but go and kill this giant and let us finish off this business; for by God it strikes me it will be one of great honour and great profit.”\n\n“I hold thou art in the right of it, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “and I will take thy advice as to accompanying the princess before going to see Dulcinea; but I counsel thee not to say anything to any one, or to those who are with us, about what we have considered and discussed, for as Dulcinea is so decorous that she does not wish her thoughts to be known it is not right that I or anyone for me should disclose them.”\n\n“Well then, if that be so,” said Sancho, “how is it that your worship makes all those you overcome by your arm go to present themselves before my lady Dulcinea, this being the same thing as signing your name to it that you love her and are her lover? And as those who go must perforce kneel before her and say they come from your worship to submit themselves to her, how can the thoughts of both of you be hid?”\n\n“O, how silly and simple thou art!” said Don Quixote; “seest thou not, Sancho, that this tends to her greater exaltation? For thou must know that according to our way of thinking in chivalry, it is a high honour to a lady to have many knights-errant in her service, whose thoughts never go beyond serving her for her own sake, and who look for no other reward for their great and true devotion than that she should be willing to accept them as her knights.”\n\n“It is with that kind of love,” said Sancho, “I have heard preachers say we ought to love our Lord, for himself alone, without being moved by the hope of glory or the fear of punishment; though for my part, I would rather love and serve him for what he could do.”\n\n“The devil take thee for a clown!” said Don Quixote, “and what shrewd things thou sayest at times! One would think thou hadst studied.”\n\n“In faith, then, I cannot even read.”\n\nMaster Nicholas here called out to them to wait a while, as they wanted to halt and drink at a little spring there was there. Don Quixote drew up, not a little to the satisfaction of Sancho, for he was by this time weary of telling so many lies, and in dread of his master catching him tripping, for though he knew that Dulcinea was a peasant girl of El Toboso, he had never seen her in all his life. Cardenio had now put on the clothes which Dorothea was wearing when they found her, and though they were not very good, they were far better than those he put off. They dismounted together by the side of the spring, and with what the curate had provided himself with at the inn they appeased, though not very well, the keen appetite they all of them brought with them.\n\nWhile they were so employed there happened to come by a youth passing on his way, who stopping to examine the party at the spring, the next moment ran to Don Quixote and clasping him round the legs, began to weep freely, saying, “O, señor, do you not know me? Look at me well; I am that lad Andres that your worship released from the oak-tree where I was tied.”\n\nDon Quixote recognised him, and taking his hand he turned to those present and said: “That your worships may see how important it is to have knights-errant to redress the wrongs and injuries done by tyrannical and wicked men in this world, I may tell you that some days ago passing through a wood, I heard cries and piteous complaints as of a person in pain and distress; I immediately hastened, impelled by my bounden duty, to the quarter whence the plaintive accents seemed to me to proceed, and I found tied to an oak this lad who now stands before you, which in my heart I rejoice at, for his testimony will not permit me to depart from the truth in any particular. He was, I say, tied to an oak, naked from the waist up, and a clown, whom I afterwards found to be his master, was scarifying him by lashes with the reins of his mare. As soon as I saw him I asked the reason of so cruel a flagellation. The boor replied that he was flogging him because he was his servant and because of carelessness that proceeded rather from dishonesty than stupidity; on which this boy said, ‘Señor, he flogs me only because I ask for my wages.’ The master made I know not what speeches and explanations, which, though I listened to them, I did not accept. In short, I compelled the clown to unbind him, and to swear he would take him with him, and pay him real by real, and perfumed into the bargain. Is not all this true, Andres my son? Didst thou not mark with what authority I commanded him, and with what humility he promised to do all I enjoined, specified, and required of him? Answer without hesitation; tell these gentlemen what took place, that they may see that it is as great an advantage as I say to have knights-errant abroad.”\n\n“All that your worship has said is quite true,” answered the lad; “but the end of the business turned out just the opposite of what your worship supposes.”\n\n“How! the opposite?” said Don Quixote; “did not the clown pay thee then?”\n\n“Not only did he not pay me,” replied the lad, “but as soon as your worship had passed out of the wood and we were alone, he tied me up again to the same oak and gave me a fresh flogging, that left me like a flayed Saint Bartholomew; and every stroke he gave me he followed up with some jest or gibe about having made a fool of your worship, and but for the pain I was suffering I should have laughed at the things he said. In short he left me in such a condition that I have been until now in a hospital getting cured of the injuries which that rascally clown inflicted on me then; for all which your worship is to blame; for if you had gone your own way and not come where there was no call for you, nor meddled in other people’s affairs, my master would have been content with giving me one or two dozen lashes, and would have then loosed me and paid me what he owed me; but when your worship abused him so out of measure, and gave him so many hard words, his anger was kindled; and as he could not revenge himself on you, as soon as he saw you had left him the storm burst upon me in such a way, that I feel as if I should never be a man again.”\n\n“The mischief,” said Don Quixote, “lay in my going away; for I should not have gone until I had seen thee paid; because I ought to have known well by long experience that there is no clown who will keep his word if he finds it will not suit him to keep it; but thou rememberest, Andres, that I swore if he did not pay thee I would go and seek him, and find him though he were to hide himself in the whale’s belly.”\n\n“That is true,” said Andres; “but it was of no use.”\n\n“Thou shalt see now whether it is of use or not,” said Don Quixote; and so saying, he got up hastily and bade Sancho bridle Rocinante, who was browsing while they were eating. Dorothea asked him what he meant to do. He replied that he meant to go in search of this clown and chastise him for such iniquitous conduct, and see Andres paid to the last maravedi, despite and in the teeth of all the clowns in the world. To which she replied that he must remember that in accordance with his promise he could not engage in any enterprise until he had concluded hers; and that as he knew this better than anyone, he should restrain his ardour until his return from her kingdom.\n\n“That is true,” said Don Quixote, “and Andres must have patience until my return as you say, señora; but I once more swear and promise not to stop until I have seen him avenged and paid.”\n\n“I have no faith in those oaths,” said Andres; “I would rather have now something to help me to get to Seville than all the revenges in the world; if you have here anything to eat that I can take with me, give it me, and God be with your worship and all knights-errant; and may their errands turn out as well for themselves as they have for me.”\n\nSancho took out from his store a piece of bread and another of cheese, and giving them to the lad he said, “Here, take this, brother Andres, for we have all of us a share in your misfortune.”\n\n“Why, what share have you got?”\n\n“This share of bread and cheese I am giving you,” answered Sancho; “and God knows whether I shall feel the want of it myself or not; for I would have you know, friend, that we squires to knights-errant have to bear a great deal of hunger and hard fortune, and even other things more easily felt than told.”\n\nAndres seized his bread and cheese, and seeing that nobody gave him anything more, bent his head, and took hold of the road, as the saying is. However, before leaving he said, “For the love of God, sir knight-errant, if you ever meet me again, though you may see them cutting me to pieces, give me no aid or succour, but leave me to my misfortune, which will not be so great but that a greater will come to me by being helped by your worship, on whom and all the knights-errant that have ever been born God send his curse.”\n\nDon Quixote was getting up to chastise him, but he took to his heels at such a pace that no one attempted to follow him; and mightily chapfallen was Don Quixote at Andres’ story, and the others had to take great care to restrain their laughter so as not to put him entirely out of countenance.\n\n# WHICH TREATS OF WHAT BEFELL DON QUIXOTE’S PARTY AT THE INN\n\nTheir dainty repast being finished, they saddled at once, and without any adventure worth mentioning they reached next day the inn, the object of Sancho Panza’s fear and dread; but though he would have rather not entered it, there was no help for it. The landlady, the landlord, their daughter, and Maritornes, when they saw Don Quixote and Sancho coming, went out to welcome them with signs of hearty satisfaction, which Don Quixote received with dignity and gravity, and bade them make up a better bed for him than the last time: to which the landlady replied that if he paid better than he did the last time she would give him one fit for a prince. Don Quixote said he would, so they made up a tolerable one for him in the same garret as before; and he lay down at once, being sorely shaken and in want of sleep.\n\nNo sooner was the door shut upon him than the landlady made at the barber, and seizing him by the beard, said:\n\n“By my faith you are not going to make a beard of my tail any longer; you must give me back my tail, for it is a shame the way that thing of my husband’s goes tossing about on the floor; I mean the comb that I used to stick in my good tail.”\n\nBut for all she tugged at it the barber would not give it up until the licentiate told him to let her have it, as there was now no further occasion for that stratagem, because he might declare himself and appear in his own character, and tell Don Quixote that he had fled to this inn when those thieves the galley slaves robbed him; and should he ask for the princess’s squire, they could tell him that she had sent him on before her to give notice to the people of her kingdom that she was coming, and bringing with her the deliverer of them all. On this the barber cheerfully restored the tail to the landlady, and at the same time they returned all the accessories they had borrowed to effect Don Quixote’s deliverance. All the people of the inn were struck with astonishment at the beauty of Dorothea, and even at the comely figure of the shepherd Cardenio. The curate made them get ready such fare as there was in the inn, and the landlord, in hope of better payment, served them up a tolerably good dinner. All this time Don Quixote was asleep, and they thought it best not to waken him, as sleeping would now do him more good than eating.\n\nWhile at dinner, the company consisting of the landlord, his wife, their daughter, Maritornes, and all the travellers, they discussed the strange craze of Don Quixote and the manner in which he had been found; and the landlady told them what had taken place between him and the carrier; and then, looking round to see if Sancho was there, when she saw he was not, she gave them the whole story of his blanketing, which they received with no little amusement. But on the curate observing that it was the books of chivalry which Don Quixote had read that had turned his brain, the landlord said:\n\n“I cannot understand how that can be, for in truth to my mind there is no better reading in the world, and I have here two or three of them, with other writings that are the very life, not only of myself but of plenty more; for when it is harvest-time, the reapers flock here on holidays, and there is always one among them who can read and who takes up one of these books, and we gather round him, thirty or more of us, and stay listening to him with a delight that makes our grey hairs grow young again. At least I can say for myself that when I hear of what furious and terrible blows the knights deliver, I am seized with the longing to do the same, and I would like to be hearing about them night and day.”\n\n“And I just as much,” said the landlady, “because I never have a quiet moment in my house except when you are listening to some one reading; for then you are so taken up that for the time being you forget to scold.”\n\n“That is true,” said Maritornes; “and, faith, I relish hearing these things greatly too, for they are very pretty; especially when they describe some lady or another in the arms of her knight under the orange trees, and the duenna who is keeping watch for them half dead with envy and fright; all this I say is as good as honey.”\n\n“And you, what do you think, young lady?” said the curate turning to the landlord’s daughter.\n\n“I don’t know indeed, señor,” said she; “I listen too, and to tell the truth, though I do not understand it, I like hearing it; but it is not the blows that my father likes that I like, but the laments the knights utter when they are separated from their ladies; and indeed they sometimes make me weep with the pity I feel for them.”\n\n“Then you would console them if it was for you they wept, young lady?” said Dorothea.\n\n“I don’t know what I should do,” said the girl; “I only know that there are some of those ladies so cruel that they call their knights tigers and lions and a thousand other foul names: and Jesus! I don’t know what sort of folk they can be, so unfeeling and heartless, that rather than bestow a glance upon a worthy man they leave him to die or go mad. I don’t know what is the good of such prudery; if it is for honour’s sake, why not marry them? That’s all they want.”\n\n“Hush, child,” said the landlady; “it seems to me thou knowest a great deal about these things, and it is not fit for girls to know or talk so much.”\n\n“As the gentleman asked me, I could not help answering him,” said the girl.\n\n“Well then,” said the curate, “bring me these books, señor landlord, for I should like to see them.”\n\n“With all my heart,” said he, and going into his own room he brought out an old valise secured with a little chain, on opening which the curate found in it three large books and some manuscripts written in a very good hand. The first that he opened he found to be “Don Cirongilio of Thrace,” and the second “Don Felixmarte of Hircania,” and the other the “History of the Great Captain Gonzalo Hernandez de Cordova, with the Life of Diego Garcia de Paredes.”\n\nWhen the curate read the two first titles he looked over at the barber and said, “We want my friend’s housekeeper and niece here now.”\n\n“Nay,” said the barber, “I can do just as well to carry them to the yard or to the hearth, and there is a very good fire there.”\n\n“What! your worship would burn my books!” said the landlord.\n\n“Only these two,” said the curate, “Don Cirongilio, and Felixmarte.”\n\n“Are my books, then, heretics or phlegmatics that you want to burn them?” said the landlord.\n\n“Schismatics you mean, friend,” said the barber, “not phlegmatics.”\n\n“That’s it,” said the landlord; “but if you want to burn any, let it be that about the Great Captain and that Diego Garcia; for I would rather have a child of mine burnt than either of the others.”\n\n“Brother,” said the curate, “those two books are made up of lies, and are full of folly and nonsense; but this of the Great Captain is a true history, and contains the deeds of Gonzalo Hernandez of Cordova, who by his many and great achievements earned the title all over the world of the Great Captain, a famous and illustrious name, and deserved by him alone; and this Diego Garcia de Paredes was a distinguished knight of the city of Trujillo in Estremadura, a most gallant soldier, and of such bodily strength that with one finger he stopped a mill-wheel in full motion; and posted with a two-handed sword at the foot of a bridge he kept the whole of an immense army from passing over it, and achieved such other exploits that if, instead of his relating them himself with the modesty of a knight and of one writing his own history, some free and unbiassed writer had recorded them, they would have thrown into the shade all the deeds of the Hectors, Achilleses, and Rolands.”\n\n“Tell that to my father,” said the landlord. “There’s a thing to be astonished at! Stopping a mill-wheel! By God your worship should read what I have read of Felixmarte of Hircania, how with one single backstroke he cleft five giants asunder through the middle as if they had been made of bean-pods like the little friars the children make; and another time he attacked a very great and powerful army, in which there were more than a million six hundred thousand soldiers, all armed from head to foot, and he routed them all as if they had been flocks of sheep.\n\n“And then, what do you say to the good Cirongilio of Thrace, that was so stout and bold; as may be seen in the book, where it is related that as he was sailing along a river there came up out of the midst of the water against him a fiery serpent, and he, as soon as he saw it, flung himself upon it and got astride of its scaly shoulders, and squeezed its throat with both hands with such force that the serpent, finding he was throttling it, had nothing for it but to let itself sink to the bottom of the river, carrying with it the knight who would not let go his hold; and when they got down there he found himself among palaces and gardens so pretty that it was a wonder to see; and then the serpent changed itself into an old ancient man, who told him such things as were never heard. Hold your peace, señor; for if you were to hear this you would go mad with delight. A couple of figs for your Great Captain and your Diego Garcia!”\n\nHearing this Dorothea said in a whisper to Cardenio, “Our landlord is almost fit to play a second part to Don Quixote.”\n\n“I think so,” said Cardenio, “for, as he shows, he accepts it as a certainty that everything those books relate took place exactly as it is written down; and the barefooted friars themselves would not persuade him to the contrary.”\n\n“But consider, brother,” said the curate once more, “there never was any Felixmarte of Hircania in the world, nor any Cirongilio of Thrace, or any of the other knights of the same sort, that the books of chivalry talk of; the whole thing is the fabrication and invention of idle wits, devised by them for the purpose you describe of beguiling the time, as your reapers do when they read; for I swear to you in all seriousness there never were any such knights in the world, and no such exploits or nonsense ever happened anywhere.”\n\n“Try that bone on another dog,” said the landlord; “as if I did not know how many make five, and where my shoe pinches me; don’t think to feed me with pap, for by God I am no fool. It is a good joke for your worship to try and persuade me that everything these good books say is nonsense and lies, and they printed by the license of the Lords of the Royal Council, as if they were people who would allow such a lot of lies to be printed all together, and so many battles and enchantments that they take away one’s senses.”\n\n“I have told you, friend,” said the curate, “that this is done to divert our idle thoughts; and as in well-ordered states games of chess, fives, and billiards are allowed for the diversion of those who do not care, or are not obliged, or are unable to work, so books of this kind are allowed to be printed, on the supposition that, what indeed is the truth, there can be nobody so ignorant as to take any of them for true stories; and if it were permitted me now, and the present company desired it, I could say something about the qualities books of chivalry should possess to be good ones, that would be to the advantage and even to the taste of some; but I hope the time will come when I can communicate my ideas to some one who may be able to mend matters; and in the meantime, señor landlord, believe what I have said, and take your books, and make up your mind about their truth or falsehood, and much good may they do you; and God grant you may not fall lame of the same foot your guest Don Quixote halts on.”\n\n“No fear of that,” returned the landlord; “I shall not be so mad as to make a knight-errant of myself; for I see well enough that things are not now as they used to be in those days, when they say those famous knights roamed about the world.”\n\nSancho had made his appearance in the middle of this conversation, and he was very much troubled and cast down by what he heard said about knights-errant being now no longer in vogue, and all books of chivalry being folly and lies; and he resolved in his heart to wait and see what came of this journey of his master’s, and if it did not turn out as happily as his master expected, he determined to leave him and go back to his wife and children and his ordinary labour.\n\nThe landlord was carrying away the valise and the books, but the curate said to him, “Wait; I want to see what those papers are that are written in such a good hand.” The landlord taking them out handed them to him to read, and he perceived they were a work of about eight sheets of manuscript, with, in large letters at the beginning, the title of “Novel of the Ill-advised Curiosity.” The curate read three or four lines to himself, and said, “I must say the title of this novel does not seem to me a bad one, and I feel an inclination to read it all.” To which the landlord replied, “Then your reverence will do well to read it, for I can tell you that some guests who have read it here have been much pleased with it, and have begged it of me very earnestly; but I would not give it, meaning to return it to the person who forgot the valise, books, and papers here, for maybe he will return here some time or other; and though I know I shall miss the books, faith I mean to return them; for though I am an innkeeper, still I am a Christian.”\n\n“You are very right, friend,” said the curate; “but for all that, if the novel pleases me you must let me copy it.”\n\n“With all my heart,” replied the host.\n\nWhile they were talking Cardenio had taken up the novel and begun to read it, and forming the same opinion of it as the curate, he begged him to read it so that they might all hear it.\n\n“I would read it,” said the curate, “if the time would not be better spent in sleeping.”\n\n“It will be rest enough for me,” said Dorothea, “to while away the time by listening to some tale, for my spirits are not yet tranquil enough to let me sleep when it would be seasonable.”\n\n“Well then, in that case,” said the curate, “I will read it, if it were only out of curiosity; perhaps it may contain something pleasant.”\n\nMaster Nicholas added his entreaties to the same effect, and Sancho too; seeing which, and considering that he would give pleasure to all, and receive it himself, the curate said, “Well then, attend to me everyone, for the novel begins thus.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII.\n\nIN WHICH IS RELATED THE NOVEL OF “THE ILL-ADVISED CURIOSITY”\nIn Florence, a rich and famous city of Italy in the province called Tuscany, there lived two gentlemen of wealth and quality, Anselmo and Lothario, such great friends that by way of distinction they were called by all that knew them “The Two Friends.” They were unmarried, young, of the same age and of the same tastes, which was enough to account for the reciprocal friendship between them. Anselmo, it is true, was somewhat more inclined to seek pleasure in love than Lothario, for whom the pleasures of the chase had more attraction; but on occasion Anselmo would forego his own tastes to yield to those of Lothario, and Lothario would surrender his to fall in with those of Anselmo, and in this way their inclinations kept pace one with the other with a concord so perfect that the best regulated clock could not surpass it.\n\nAnselmo was deep in love with a high-born and beautiful maiden of the same city, the daughter of parents so estimable, and so estimable herself, that he resolved, with the approval of his friend Lothario, without whom he did nothing, to ask her of them in marriage, and did so, Lothario being the bearer of the demand, and conducting the negotiation so much to the satisfaction of his friend that in a short time he was in possession of the object of his desires, and Camilla so happy in having won Anselmo for her husband, that she gave thanks unceasingly to heaven and to Lothario, by whose means such good fortune had fallen to her. The first few days, those of a wedding being usually days of merry-making, Lothario frequented his friend Anselmo’s house as he had been wont, striving to do honour to him and to the occasion, and to gratify him in every way he could; but when the wedding days were over and the succession of visits and congratulations had slackened, he began purposely to leave off going to the house of Anselmo, for it seemed to him, as it naturally would to all men of sense, that friends’ houses ought not to be visited after marriage with the same frequency as in their masters’ bachelor days: because, though true and genuine friendship cannot and should not be in any way suspicious, still a married man’s honour is a thing of such delicacy that it is held liable to injury from brothers, much more from friends. Anselmo remarked the cessation of Lothario’s visits, and complained of it to him, saying that if he had known that marriage was to keep him from enjoying his society as he used, he would have never married; and that, if by the thorough harmony that subsisted between them while he was a bachelor they had earned such a sweet name as that of “The Two Friends,” he should not allow a title so rare and so delightful to be lost through a needless anxiety to act circumspectly; and so he entreated him, if such a phrase was allowable between them, to be once more master of his house and to come in and go out as formerly, assuring him that his wife Camilla had no other desire or inclination than that which he would wish her to have, and that knowing how sincerely they loved one another she was grieved to see such coldness in him.\n\nTo all this and much more that Anselmo said to Lothario to persuade him to come to his house as he had been in the habit of doing, Lothario replied with so much prudence, sense, and judgment, that Anselmo was satisfied of his friend’s good intentions, and it was agreed that on two days in the week, and on holidays, Lothario should come to dine with him; but though this arrangement was made between them Lothario resolved to observe it no further than he considered to be in accordance with the honour of his friend, whose good name was more to him than his own. He said, and justly, that a married man upon whom heaven had bestowed a beautiful wife should consider as carefully what friends he brought to his house as what female friends his wife associated with, for what cannot be done or arranged in the market-place, in church, at public festivals or at stations (opportunities that husbands cannot always deny their wives), may be easily managed in the house of the female friend or relative in whom most confidence is reposed. Lothario said, too, that every married man should have some friend who would point out to him any negligence he might be guilty of in his conduct, for it will sometimes happen that owing to the deep affection the husband bears his wife either he does not caution her, or, not to vex her, refrains from telling her to do or not to do certain things, doing or avoiding which may be a matter of honour or reproach to him; and errors of this kind he could easily correct if warned by a friend. But where is such a friend to be found as Lothario would have, so judicious, so loyal, and so true?\n\nOf a truth I know not; Lothario alone was such a one, for with the utmost care and vigilance he watched over the honour of his friend, and strove to diminish, cut down, and reduce the number of days for going to his house according to their agreement, lest the visits of a young man, wealthy, high-born, and with the attractions he was conscious of possessing, at the house of a woman so beautiful as Camilla, should be regarded with suspicion by the inquisitive and malicious eyes of the idle public. For though his integrity and reputation might bridle slanderous tongues, still he was unwilling to hazard either his own good name or that of his friend; and for this reason most of the days agreed upon he devoted to some other business which he pretended was unavoidable; so that a great portion of the day was taken up with complaints on one side and excuses on the other. It happened, however, that on one occasion when the two were strolling together outside the city, Anselmo addressed the following words to Lothario.\n\n“Thou mayest suppose, Lothario my friend, that I am unable to give sufficient thanks for the favours God has rendered me in making me the son of such parents as mine were, and bestowing upon me with no niggard hand what are called the gifts of nature as well as those of fortune, and above all for what he has done in giving me thee for a friend and Camilla for a wife--two treasures that I value, if not as highly as I ought, at least as highly as I am able. And yet, with all these good things, which are commonly all that men need to enable them to live happily, I am the most discontented and dissatisfied man in the whole world; for, I know not how long since, I have been harassed and oppressed by a desire so strange and so unusual, that I wonder at myself and blame and chide myself when I am alone, and strive to stifle it and hide it from my own thoughts, and with no better success than if I were endeavouring deliberately to publish it to all the world; and as, in short, it must come out, I would confide it to thy safe keeping, feeling sure that by this means, and by thy readiness as a true friend to afford me relief, I shall soon find myself freed from the distress it causes me, and that thy care will give me happiness in the same degree as my own folly has caused me misery.”\n\nThe words of Anselmo struck Lothario with astonishment, unable as he was to conjecture the purport of such a lengthy preamble; and though be strove to imagine what desire it could be that so troubled his friend, his conjectures were all far from the truth, and to relieve the anxiety which this perplexity was causing him, he told him he was doing a flagrant injustice to their great friendship in seeking circuitous methods of confiding to him his most hidden thoughts, for he well knew he might reckon upon his counsel in diverting them, or his help in carrying them into effect.\n\n“That is the truth,” replied Anselmo, “and relying upon that I will tell thee, friend Lothario, that the desire which harasses me is that of knowing whether my wife Camilla is as good and as perfect as I think her to be; and I cannot satisfy myself of the truth on this point except by testing her in such a way that the trial may prove the purity of her virtue as the fire proves that of gold; because I am persuaded, my friend, that a woman is virtuous only in proportion as she is or is not tempted; and that she alone is strong who does not yield to the promises, gifts, tears, and importunities of earnest lovers; for what thanks does a woman deserve for being good if no one urges her to be bad, and what wonder is it that she is reserved and circumspect to whom no opportunity is given of going wrong and who knows she has a husband that will take her life the first time he detects her in an impropriety? I do not therefore hold her who is virtuous through fear or want of opportunity in the same estimation as her who comes out of temptation and trial with a crown of victory; and so, for these reasons and many others that I could give thee to justify and support the opinion I hold, I am desirous that my wife Camilla should pass this crisis, and be refined and tested by the fire of finding herself wooed and by one worthy to set his affections upon her; and if she comes out, as I know she will, victorious from this struggle, I shall look upon my good fortune as unequalled, I shall be able to say that the cup of my desire is full, and that the virtuous woman of whom the sage says ‘Who shall find her?’ has fallen to my lot. And if the result be the contrary of what I expect, in the satisfaction of knowing that I have been right in my opinion, I shall bear without complaint the pain which my so dearly bought experience will naturally cause me. And, as nothing of all thou wilt urge in opposition to my wish will avail to keep me from carrying it into effect, it is my desire, friend Lothario, that thou shouldst consent to become the instrument for effecting this purpose that I am bent upon, for I will afford thee opportunities to that end, and nothing shall be wanting that I may think necessary for the pursuit of a virtuous, honourable, modest and high-minded woman. And among other reasons, I am induced to entrust this arduous task to thee by the consideration that if Camilla be conquered by thee the conquest will not be pushed to extremes, but only far enough to account that accomplished which from a sense of honour will be left undone; thus I shall not be wronged in anything more than intention, and my wrong will remain buried in the integrity of thy silence, which I know well will be as lasting as that of death in what concerns me. If, therefore, thou wouldst have me enjoy what can be called life, thou wilt at once engage in this love struggle, not lukewarmly nor slothfully, but with the energy and zeal that my desire demands, and with the loyalty our friendship assures me of.”\n\nSuch were the words Anselmo addressed to Lothario, who listened to them with such attention that, except to say what has been already mentioned, he did not open his lips until the other had finished. Then perceiving that he had no more to say, after regarding him for awhile, as one would regard something never before seen that excited wonder and amazement, he said to him, “I cannot persuade myself, Anselmo my friend, that what thou hast said to me is not in jest; if I thought that thou wert speaking seriously I would not have allowed thee to go so far; so as to put a stop to thy long harangue by not listening to thee I verily suspect that either thou dost not know me, or I do not know thee; but no, I know well thou art Anselmo, and thou knowest that I am Lothario; the misfortune is, it seems to me, that thou art not the Anselmo thou wert, and must have thought that I am not the Lothario I should be; for the things that thou hast said to me are not those of that Anselmo who was my friend, nor are those that thou demandest of me what should be asked of the Lothario thou knowest. True friends will prove their friends and make use of them, as a poet has said, usque ad aras; whereby he meant that they will not make use of their friendship in things that are contrary to God’s will. If this, then, was a heathen’s feeling about friendship, how much more should it be a Christian’s, who knows that the divine must not be forfeited for the sake of any human friendship? And if a friend should go so far as to put aside his duty to Heaven to fulfil his duty to his friend, it should not be in matters that are trifling or of little moment, but in such as affect the friend’s life and honour. Now tell me, Anselmo, in which of these two art thou imperilled, that I should hazard myself to gratify thee, and do a thing so detestable as that thou seekest of me? Neither forsooth; on the contrary, thou dost ask of me, so far as I understand, to strive and labour to rob thee of honour and life, and to rob myself of them at the same time; for if I take away thy honour it is plain I take away thy life, as a man without honour is worse than dead; and being the instrument, as thou wilt have it so, of so much wrong to thee, shall not I, too, be left without honour, and consequently without life? Listen to me, Anselmo my friend, and be not impatient to answer me until I have said what occurs to me touching the object of thy desire, for there will be time enough left for thee to reply and for me to hear.”\n\n“Be it so,” said Anselmo, “say what thou wilt.”\n\nLothario then went on to say, “It seems to me, Anselmo, that thine is just now the temper of mind which is always that of the Moors, who can never be brought to see the error of their creed by quotations from the Holy Scriptures, or by reasons which depend upon the examination of the understanding or are founded upon the articles of faith, but must have examples that are palpable, easy, intelligible, capable of proof, not admitting of doubt, with mathematical demonstrations that cannot be denied, like, ‘If equals be taken from equals, the remainders are equal:’ and if they do not understand this in words, and indeed they do not, it has to be shown to them with the hands, and put before their eyes, and even with all this no one succeeds in convincing them of the truth of our holy religion. This same mode of proceeding I shall have to adopt with thee, for the desire which has sprung up in thee is so absurd and remote from everything that has a semblance of reason, that I feel it would be a waste of time to employ it in reasoning with thy simplicity, for at present I will call it by no other name; and I am even tempted to leave thee in thy folly as a punishment for thy pernicious desire; but the friendship I bear thee, which will not allow me to desert thee in such manifest danger of destruction, keeps me from dealing so harshly by thee. And that thou mayest clearly see this, say, Anselmo, hast thou not told me that I must force my suit upon a modest woman, decoy one that is virtuous, make overtures to one that is pure-minded, pay court to one that is prudent? Yes, thou hast told me so. Then, if thou knowest that thou hast a wife, modest, virtuous, pure-minded and prudent, what is it that thou seekest? And if thou believest that she will come forth victorious from all my attacks--as doubtless she would--what higher titles than those she possesses now dost thou think thou canst bestow upon her then, or in what will she be better then than she is now? Either thou dost not hold her to be what thou sayest, or thou knowest not what thou dost demand. If thou dost not hold her to be what thou why dost thou seek to prove her instead of treating her as guilty in the way that may seem best to thee? but if she be as virtuous as thou believest, it is an uncalled-for proceeding to make trial of truth itself, for, after trial, it will but be in the same estimation as before. Thus, then, it is conclusive that to attempt things from which harm rather than advantage may come to us is the part of unreasoning and reckless minds, more especially when they are things which we are not forced or compelled to attempt, and which show from afar that it is plainly madness to attempt them.\n\n“Difficulties are attempted either for the sake of God or for the sake of the world, or for both; those undertaken for God’s sake are those which the saints undertake when they attempt to live the lives of angels in human bodies; those undertaken for the sake of the world are those of the men who traverse such a vast expanse of water, such a variety of climates, so many strange countries, to acquire what are called the blessings of fortune; and those undertaken for the sake of God and the world together are those of brave soldiers, who no sooner do they see in the enemy’s wall a breach as wide as a cannon ball could make, than, casting aside all fear, without hesitating, or heeding the manifest peril that threatens them, borne onward by the desire of defending their faith, their country, and their king, they fling themselves dauntlessly into the midst of the thousand opposing deaths that await them. Such are the things that men are wont to attempt, and there is honour, glory, gain, in attempting them, however full of difficulty and peril they may be; but that which thou sayest it is thy wish to attempt and carry out will not win thee the glory of God nor the blessings of fortune nor fame among men; for even if the issue he as thou wouldst have it, thou wilt be no happier, richer, or more honoured than thou art this moment; and if it be otherwise thou wilt be reduced to misery greater than can be imagined, for then it will avail thee nothing to reflect that no one is aware of the misfortune that has befallen thee; it will suffice to torture and crush thee that thou knowest it thyself. And in confirmation of the truth of what I say, let me repeat to thee a stanza made by the famous poet Luigi Tansillo at the end of the first part of his ‘Tears of Saint Peter,’ which says thus:\n\nThe anguish and the shame but greater grew In Peter’s heart as morning slowly came; No eye was there to see him, well he knew, Yet he himself was to himself a shame; Exposed to all men’s gaze, or screened from view, A noble heart will feel the pang the same; A prey to shame the sinning soul will be, Though none but heaven and earth its shame can see.\n\nThus by keeping it secret thou wilt not escape thy sorrow, but rather thou wilt shed tears unceasingly, if not tears of the eyes, tears of blood from the heart, like those shed by that simple doctor our poet tells us of, that tried the test of the cup, which the wise Rinaldo, better advised, refused to do; for though this may be a poetic fiction it contains a moral lesson worthy of attention and study and imitation. Moreover by what I am about to say to thee thou wilt be led to see the great error thou wouldst commit.\n\n“Tell me, Anselmo, if Heaven or good fortune had made thee master and lawful owner of a diamond of the finest quality, with the excellence and purity of which all the lapidaries that had seen it had been satisfied, saying with one voice and common consent that in purity, quality, and fineness, it was all that a stone of the kind could possibly be, thou thyself too being of the same belief, as knowing nothing to the contrary, would it be reasonable in thee to desire to take that diamond and place it between an anvil and a hammer, and by mere force of blows and strength of arm try if it were as hard and as fine as they said? And if thou didst, and if the stone should resist so silly a test, that would add nothing to its value or reputation; and if it were broken, as it might be, would not all be lost? Undoubtedly it would, leaving its owner to be rated as a fool in the opinion of all. Consider, then, Anselmo my friend, that Camilla is a diamond of the finest quality as well in thy estimation as in that of others, and that it is contrary to reason to expose her to the risk of being broken; for if she remains intact she cannot rise to a higher value than she now possesses; and if she give way and be unable to resist, bethink thee now how thou wilt be deprived of her, and with what good reason thou wilt complain of thyself for having been the cause of her ruin and thine own. Remember there is no jewel in the world so precious as a chaste and virtuous woman, and that the whole honour of women consists in reputation; and since thy wife’s is of that high excellence that thou knowest, wherefore shouldst thou seek to call that truth in question? Remember, my friend, that woman is an imperfect animal, and that impediments are not to be placed in her way to make her trip and fall, but that they should be removed, and her path left clear of all obstacles, so that without hindrance she may run her course freely to attain the desired perfection, which consists in being virtuous. Naturalists tell us that the ermine is a little animal which has a fur of purest white, and that when the hunters wish to take it, they make use of this artifice. Having ascertained the places which it frequents and passes, they stop the way to them with mud, and then rousing it, drive it towards the spot, and as soon as the ermine comes to the mud it halts, and allows itself to be taken captive rather than pass through the mire, and spoil and sully its whiteness, which it values more than life and liberty. The virtuous and chaste woman is an ermine, and whiter and purer than snow is the virtue of modesty; and he who wishes her not to lose it, but to keep and preserve it, must adopt a course different from that employed with the ermine; he must not put before her the mire of the gifts and attentions of persevering lovers, because perhaps--and even without a perhaps--she may not have sufficient virtue and natural strength in herself to pass through and tread under foot these impediments; they must be removed, and the brightness of virtue and the beauty of a fair fame must be put before her. A virtuous woman, too, is like a mirror, of clear shining crystal, liable to be tarnished and dimmed by every breath that touches it. She must be treated as relics are; adored, not touched. She must be protected and prized as one protects and prizes a fair garden full of roses and flowers, the owner of which allows no one to trespass or pluck a blossom; enough for others that from afar and through the iron grating they may enjoy its fragrance and its beauty. Finally let me repeat to thee some verses that come to my mind; I heard them in a modern comedy, and it seems to me they bear upon the point we are discussing. A prudent old man was giving advice to another, the father of a young girl, to lock her up, watch over her and keep her in seclusion, and among other arguments he used these:\n\nWoman is a thing of glass; But her brittleness ‘tis best Not too curiously to test: Who knows what may come to pass?\n\nBreaking is an easy matter, And it’s folly to expose What you cannot mend to blows; What you can’t make whole to shatter.\n\nThis, then, all may hold as true, And the reason’s plain to see; For if Danaes there be, There are golden showers too.\n\n“All that I have said to thee so far, Anselmo, has had reference to what concerns thee; now it is right that I should say something of what regards myself; and if I be prolix, pardon me, for the labyrinth into which thou hast entered and from which thou wouldst have me extricate thee makes it necessary.\n\n“Thou dost reckon me thy friend, and thou wouldst rob me of honour, a thing wholly inconsistent with friendship; and not only dost thou aim at this, but thou wouldst have me rob thee of it also. That thou wouldst rob me of it is clear, for when Camilla sees that I pay court to her as thou requirest, she will certainly regard me as a man without honour or right feeling, since I attempt and do a thing so much opposed to what I owe to my own position and thy friendship. That thou wouldst have me rob thee of it is beyond a doubt, for Camilla, seeing that I press my suit upon her, will suppose that I have perceived in her something light that has encouraged me to make known to her my base desire; and if she holds herself dishonoured, her dishonour touches thee as belonging to her; and hence arises what so commonly takes place, that the husband of the adulterous woman, though he may not be aware of or have given any cause for his wife’s failure in her duty, or (being careless or negligent) have had it in his power to prevent his dishonour, nevertheless is stigmatised by a vile and reproachful name, and in a manner regarded with eyes of contempt instead of pity by all who know of his wife’s guilt, though they see that he is unfortunate not by his own fault, but by the lust of a vicious consort. But I will tell thee why with good reason dishonour attaches to the husband of the unchaste wife, though he know not that she is so, nor be to blame, nor have done anything, or given any provocation to make her so; and be not weary with listening to me, for it will be for thy good.\n\n“When God created our first parent in the earthly paradise, the Holy Scripture says that he infused sleep into Adam and while he slept took a rib from his left side of which he formed our mother Eve, and when Adam awoke and beheld her he said, ‘This is flesh of my flesh, and bone of my bone.’ And God said ‘For this shall a man leave his father and his mother, and they shall be two in one flesh; and then was instituted the divine sacrament of marriage, with such ties that death alone can loose them. And such is the force and virtue of this miraculous sacrament that it makes two different persons one and the same flesh; and even more than this when the virtuous are married; for though they have two souls they have but one will. And hence it follows that as the flesh of the wife is one and the same with that of her husband the stains that may come upon it, or the injuries it incurs fall upon the husband’s flesh, though he, as has been said, may have given no cause for them; for as the pain of the foot or any member of the body is felt by the whole body, because all is one flesh, as the head feels the hurt to the ankle without having caused it, so the husband, being one with her, shares the dishonour of the wife; and as all worldly honour or dishonour comes of flesh and blood, and the erring wife’s is of that kind, the husband must needs bear his part of it and be held dishonoured without knowing it. See, then, Anselmo, the peril thou art encountering in seeking to disturb the peace of thy virtuous consort; see for what an empty and ill-advised curiosity thou wouldst rouse up passions that now repose in quiet in the breast of thy chaste wife; reflect that what thou art staking all to win is little, and what thou wilt lose so much that I leave it undescribed, not having the words to express it. But if all I have said be not enough to turn thee from thy vile purpose, thou must seek some other instrument for thy dishonour and misfortune; for such I will not consent to be, though I lose thy friendship, the greatest loss that I can conceive.”\n\nHaving said this, the wise and virtuous Lothario was silent, and Anselmo, troubled in mind and deep in thought, was unable for a while to utter a word in reply; but at length he said, “I have listened, Lothario my friend, attentively, as thou hast seen, to what thou hast chosen to say to me, and in thy arguments, examples, and comparisons I have seen that high intelligence thou dost possess, and the perfection of true friendship thou hast reached; and likewise I see and confess that if I am not guided by thy opinion, but follow my own, I am flying from the good and pursuing the evil. This being so, thou must remember that I am now labouring under that infirmity which women sometimes suffer from, when the craving seizes them to eat clay, plaster, charcoal, and things even worse, disgusting to look at, much more to eat; so that it will be necessary to have recourse to some artifice to cure me; and this can be easily effected if only thou wilt make a beginning, even though it be in a lukewarm and make-believe fashion, to pay court to Camilla, who will not be so yielding that her virtue will give way at the first attack: with this mere attempt I shall rest satisfied, and thou wilt have done what our friendship binds thee to do, not only in giving me life, but in persuading me not to discard my honour. And this thou art bound to do for one reason alone, that, being, as I am, resolved to apply this test, it is not for thee to permit me to reveal my weakness to another, and so imperil that honour thou art striving to keep me from losing; and if thine may not stand as high as it ought in the estimation of Camilla while thou art paying court to her, that is of little or no importance, because ere long, on finding in her that constancy which we expect, thou canst tell her the plain truth as regards our stratagem, and so regain thy place in her esteem; and as thou art venturing so little, and by the venture canst afford me so much satisfaction, refuse not to undertake it, even if further difficulties present themselves to thee; for, as I have said, if thou wilt only make a beginning I will acknowledge the issue decided.”\n\nLothario seeing the fixed determination of Anselmo, and not knowing what further examples to offer or arguments to urge in order to dissuade him from it, and perceiving that he threatened to confide his pernicious scheme to some one else, to avoid a greater evil resolved to gratify him and do what he asked, intending to manage the business so as to satisfy Anselmo without corrupting the mind of Camilla; so in reply he told him not to communicate his purpose to any other, for he would undertake the task himself, and would begin it as soon as he pleased. Anselmo embraced him warmly and affectionately, and thanked him for his offer as if he had bestowed some great favour upon him; and it was agreed between them to set about it the next day, Anselmo affording opportunity and time to Lothario to converse alone with Camilla, and furnishing him with money and jewels to offer and present to her. He suggested, too, that he should treat her to music, and write verses in her praise, and if he was unwilling to take the trouble of composing them, he offered to do it himself. Lothario agreed to all with an intention very different from what Anselmo supposed, and with this understanding they returned to Anselmo’s house, where they found Camilla awaiting her husband anxiously and uneasily, for he was later than usual in returning that day. Lothario repaired to his own house, and Anselmo remained in his, as well satisfied as Lothario was troubled in mind; for he could see no satisfactory way out of this ill-advised business. That night, however, he thought of a plan by which he might deceive Anselmo without any injury to Camilla. The next day he went to dine with his friend, and was welcomed by Camilla, who received and treated him with great cordiality, knowing the affection her husband felt for him. When dinner was over and the cloth removed, Anselmo told Lothario to stay there with Camilla while he attended to some pressing business, as he would return in an hour and a half. Camilla begged him not to go, and Lothario offered to accompany him, but nothing could persuade Anselmo, who on the contrary pressed Lothario to remain waiting for him as he had a matter of great importance to discuss with him. At the same time he bade Camilla not to leave Lothario alone until he came back. In short he contrived to put so good a face on the reason, or the folly, of his absence that no one could have suspected it was a pretence.\n\nAnselmo took his departure, and Camilla and Lothario were left alone at the table, for the rest of the household had gone to dinner. Lothario saw himself in the lists according to his friend’s wish, and facing an enemy that could by her beauty alone vanquish a squadron of armed knights; judge whether he had good reason to fear; but what he did was to lean his elbow on the arm of the chair, and his cheek upon his hand, and, asking Camilla’s pardon for his ill manners, he said he wished to take a little sleep until Anselmo returned. Camilla in reply said he could repose more at his ease in the reception-room than in his chair, and begged of him to go in and sleep there; but Lothario declined, and there he remained asleep until the return of Anselmo, who finding Camilla in her own room, and Lothario asleep, imagined that he had stayed away so long as to have afforded them time enough for conversation and even for sleep, and was all impatience until Lothario should wake up, that he might go out with him and question him as to his success. Everything fell out as he wished; Lothario awoke, and the two at once left the house, and Anselmo asked what he was anxious to know, and Lothario in answer told him that he had not thought it advisable to declare himself entirely the first time, and therefore had only extolled the charms of Camilla, telling her that all the city spoke of nothing else but her beauty and wit, for this seemed to him an excellent way of beginning to gain her good-will and render her disposed to listen to him with pleasure the next time, thus availing himself of the device the devil has recourse to when he would deceive one who is on the watch; for he being the angel of darkness transforms himself into an angel of light, and, under cover of a fair seeming, discloses himself at length, and effects his purpose if at the beginning his wiles are not discovered. All this gave great satisfaction to Anselmo, and he said he would afford the same opportunity every day, but without leaving the house, for he would find things to do at home so that Camilla should not detect the plot.\n\nThus, then, several days went by, and Lothario, without uttering a word to Camilla, reported to Anselmo that he had talked with her and that he had never been able to draw from her the slightest indication of consent to anything dishonourable, nor even a sign or shadow of hope; on the contrary, he said she would inform her husband of it.\n\n“So far well,” said Anselmo; “Camilla has thus far resisted words; we must now see how she will resist deeds. I will give you to-morrow two thousand crowns in gold for you to offer or even present, and as many more to buy jewels to lure her, for women are fond of being becomingly attired and going gaily dressed, and all the more so if they are beautiful, however chaste they may be; and if she resists this temptation, I will rest satisfied and will give you no more trouble.”\n\nLothario replied that now he had begun he would carry on the undertaking to the end, though he perceived he was to come out of it wearied and vanquished. The next day he received the four thousand crowns, and with them four thousand perplexities, for he knew not what to say by way of a new falsehood; but in the end he made up his mind to tell him that Camilla stood as firm against gifts and promises as against words, and that there was no use in taking any further trouble, for the time was all spent to no purpose.\n\nBut chance, directing things in a different manner, so ordered it that Anselmo, having left Lothario and Camilla alone as on other occasions, shut himself into a chamber and posted himself to watch and listen through the keyhole to what passed between them, and perceived that for more than half an hour Lothario did not utter a word to Camilla, nor would utter a word though he were to be there for an age; and he came to the conclusion that what his friend had told him about the replies of Camilla was all invention and falsehood, and to ascertain if it were so, he came out, and calling Lothario aside asked him what news he had and in what humour Camilla was. Lothario replied that he was not disposed to go on with the business, for she had answered him so angrily and harshly that he had no heart to say anything more to her.\n\n“Ah, Lothario, Lothario,” said Anselmo, “how ill dost thou meet thy obligations to me, and the great confidence I repose in thee! I have been just now watching through this keyhole, and I have seen that thou has not said a word to Camilla, whence I conclude that on the former occasions thou hast not spoken to her either, and if this be so, as no doubt it is, why dost thou deceive me, or wherefore seekest thou by craft to deprive me of the means I might find of attaining my desire?”\n\nAnselmo said no more, but he had said enough to cover Lothario with shame and confusion, and he, feeling as it were his honour touched by having been detected in a lie, swore to Anselmo that he would from that moment devote himself to satisfying him without any deception, as he would see if he had the curiosity to watch; though he need not take the trouble, for the pains he would take to satisfy him would remove all suspicions from his mind. Anselmo believed him, and to afford him an opportunity more free and less liable to surprise, he resolved to absent himself from his house for eight days, betaking himself to that of a friend of his who lived in a village not far from the city; and, the better to account for his departure to Camilla, he so arranged it that the friend should send him a very pressing invitation.\n\nUnhappy, shortsighted Anselmo, what art thou doing, what art thou plotting, what art thou devising? Bethink thee thou art working against thyself, plotting thine own dishonour, devising thine own ruin. Thy wife Camilla is virtuous, thou dost possess her in peace and quietness, no one assails thy happiness, her thoughts wander not beyond the walls of thy house, thou art her heaven on earth, the object of her wishes, the fulfilment of her desires, the measure wherewith she measures her will, making it conform in all things to thine and Heaven’s. If, then, the mine of her honour, beauty, virtue, and modesty yields thee without labour all the wealth it contains and thou canst wish for, why wilt thou dig the earth in search of fresh veins, of new unknown treasure, risking the collapse of all, since it but rests on the feeble props of her weak nature? Bethink thee that from him who seeks impossibilities that which is possible may with justice be withheld, as was better expressed by a poet who said:\n\n‘Tis mine to seek for life in death, Health in disease seek I, I seek in prison freedom’s breath, In traitors loyalty. So Fate that ever scorns to grant Or grace or boon to me, Since what can never be I want, Denies me what might be.\n\nThe next day Anselmo took his departure for the village, leaving instructions with Camilla that during his absence Lothario would come to look after his house and to dine with her, and that she was to treat him as she would himself. Camilla was distressed, as a discreet and right-minded woman would be, at the orders her husband left her, and bade him remember that it was not becoming that anyone should occupy his seat at the table during his absence, and if he acted thus from not feeling confidence that she would be able to manage his house, let him try her this time, and he would find by experience that she was equal to greater responsibilities. Anselmo replied that it was his pleasure to have it so, and that she had only to submit and obey. Camilla said she would do so, though against her will.\n\nAnselmo went, and the next day Lothario came to his house, where he was received by Camilla with a friendly and modest welcome; but she never suffered Lothario to see her alone, for she was always attended by her men and women servants, especially by a handmaid of hers, Leonela by name, to whom she was much attached (for they had been brought up together from childhood in her father’s house), and whom she had kept with her after her marriage with Anselmo. The first three days Lothario did not speak to her, though he might have done so when they removed the cloth and the servants retired to dine hastily; for such were Camilla’s orders; nay more, Leonela had directions to dine earlier than Camilla and never to leave her side. She, however, having her thoughts fixed upon other things more to her taste, and wanting that time and opportunity for her own pleasures, did not always obey her mistress’s commands, but on the contrary left them alone, as if they had ordered her to do so; but the modest bearing of Camilla, the calmness of her countenance, the composure of her aspect were enough to bridle the tongue of Lothario. But the influence which the many virtues of Camilla exerted in imposing silence on Lothario’s tongue proved mischievous for both of them, for if his tongue was silent his thoughts were busy, and could dwell at leisure upon the perfections of Camilla’s goodness and beauty one by one, charms enough to warm with love a marble statue, not to say a heart of flesh. Lothario gazed upon her when he might have been speaking to her, and thought how worthy of being loved she was; and thus reflection began little by little to assail his allegiance to Anselmo, and a thousand times he thought of withdrawing from the city and going where Anselmo should never see him nor he see Camilla. But already the delight he found in gazing on her interposed and held him fast. He put a constraint upon himself, and struggled to repel and repress the pleasure he found in contemplating Camilla; when alone he blamed himself for his weakness, called himself a bad friend, nay a bad Christian; then he argued the matter and compared himself with Anselmo; always coming to the conclusion that the folly and rashness of Anselmo had been worse than his faithlessness, and that if he could excuse his intentions as easily before God as with man, he had no reason to fear any punishment for his offence.\n\nIn short the beauty and goodness of Camilla, joined with the opportunity which the blind husband had placed in his hands, overthrew the loyalty of Lothario; and giving heed to nothing save the object towards which his inclinations led him, after Anselmo had been three days absent, during which he had been carrying on a continual struggle with his passion, he began to make love to Camilla with so much vehemence and warmth of language that she was overwhelmed with amazement, and could only rise from her place and retire to her room without answering him a word. But the hope which always springs up with love was not weakened in Lothario by this repelling demeanour; on the contrary his passion for Camilla increased, and she discovering in him what she had never expected, knew not what to do; and considering it neither safe nor right to give him the chance or opportunity of speaking to her again, she resolved to send, as she did that very night, one of her servants with a letter to Anselmo, in which she addressed the following words to him.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIV.\n\nIN WHICH IS CONTINUED THE NOVEL OF “THE ILL-ADVISED CURIOSITY”\n“It is commonly said that an army looks ill without its general and a castle without its castellan, and I say that a young married woman looks still worse without her husband unless there are very good reasons for it. I find myself so ill at ease without you, and so incapable of enduring this separation, that unless you return quickly I shall have to go for relief to my parents’ house, even if I leave yours without a protector; for the one you left me, if indeed he deserved that title, has, I think, more regard to his own pleasure than to what concerns you: as you are possessed of discernment I need say no more to you, nor indeed is it fitting I should say more.”\n\nAnselmo received this letter, and from it he gathered that Lothario had already begun his task and that Camilla must have replied to him as he would have wished; and delighted beyond measure at such intelligence he sent word to her not to leave his house on any account, as he would very shortly return. Camilla was astonished at Anselmo’s reply, which placed her in greater perplexity than before, for she neither dared to remain in her own house, nor yet to go to her parents’; for in remaining her virtue was imperilled, and in going she was opposing her husband’s commands. Finally she decided upon what was the worse course for her, to remain, resolving not to fly from the presence of Lothario, that she might not give food for gossip to her servants; and she now began to regret having written as she had to her husband, fearing he might imagine that Lothario had perceived in her some lightness which had impelled him to lay aside the respect he owed her; but confident of her rectitude she put her trust in God and in her own virtuous intentions, with which she hoped to resist in silence all the solicitations of Lothario, without saying anything to her husband so as not to involve him in any quarrel or trouble; and she even began to consider how to excuse Lothario to Anselmo when he should ask her what it was that induced her to write that letter. With these resolutions, more honourable than judicious or effectual, she remained the next day listening to Lothario, who pressed his suit so strenuously that Camilla’s firmness began to waver, and her virtue had enough to do to come to the rescue of her eyes and keep them from showing signs of a certain tender compassion which the tears and appeals of Lothario had awakened in her bosom. Lothario observed all this, and it inflamed him all the more. In short he felt that while Anselmo’s absence afforded time and opportunity he must press the siege of the fortress, and so he assailed her self-esteem with praises of her beauty, for there is nothing that more quickly reduces and levels the castle towers of fair women’s vanity than vanity itself upon the tongue of flattery. In fact with the utmost assiduity he undermined the rock of her purity with such engines that had Camilla been of brass she must have fallen. He wept, he entreated, he promised, he flattered, he importuned, he pretended with so much feeling and apparent sincerity, that he overthrew the virtuous resolves of Camilla and won the triumph he least expected and most longed for. Camilla yielded, Camilla fell; but what wonder if the friendship of Lothario could not stand firm? A clear proof to us that the passion of love is to be conquered only by flying from it, and that no one should engage in a struggle with an enemy so mighty; for divine strength is needed to overcome his human power. Leonela alone knew of her mistress’s weakness, for the two false friends and new lovers were unable to conceal it. Lothario did not care to tell Camilla the object Anselmo had in view, nor that he had afforded him the opportunity of attaining such a result, lest she should undervalue his love and think that it was by chance and without intending it and not of his own accord that he had made love to her.\n\nA few days later Anselmo returned to his house and did not perceive what it had lost, that which he so lightly treated and so highly prized. He went at once to see Lothario, and found him at home; they embraced each other, and Anselmo asked for the tidings of his life or his death.\n\n“The tidings I have to give thee, Anselmo my friend,” said Lothario, “are that thou dost possess a wife that is worthy to be the pattern and crown of all good wives. The words that I have addressed to her were borne away on the wind, my promises have been despised, my presents have been refused, such feigned tears as I shed have been turned into open ridicule. In short, as Camilla is the essence of all beauty, so is she the treasure-house where purity dwells, and gentleness and modesty abide with all the virtues that can confer praise, honour, and happiness upon a woman. Take back thy money, my friend; here it is, and I have had no need to touch it, for the chastity of Camilla yields not to things so base as gifts or promises. Be content, Anselmo, and refrain from making further proof; and as thou hast passed dryshod through the sea of those doubts and suspicions that are and may be entertained of women, seek not to plunge again into the deep ocean of new embarrassments, or with another pilot make trial of the goodness and strength of the bark that Heaven has granted thee for thy passage across the sea of this world; but reckon thyself now safe in port, moor thyself with the anchor of sound reflection, and rest in peace until thou art called upon to pay that debt which no nobility on earth can escape paying.”\n\nAnselmo was completely satisfied by the words of Lothario, and believed them as fully as if they had been spoken by an oracle; nevertheless he begged of him not to relinquish the undertaking, were it but for the sake of curiosity and amusement; though thenceforward he need not make use of the same earnest endeavours as before; all he wished him to do was to write some verses to her, praising her under the name of Chloris, for he himself would give her to understand that he was in love with a lady to whom he had given that name to enable him to sing her praises with the decorum due to her modesty; and if Lothario were unwilling to take the trouble of writing the verses he would compose them himself.\n\n“That will not be necessary,” said Lothario, “for the muses are not such enemies of mine but that they visit me now and then in the course of the year. Do thou tell Camilla what thou hast proposed about a pretended amour of mine; as for the verses will make them, and if not as good as the subject deserves, they shall be at least the best I can produce.” An agreement to this effect was made between the friends, the ill-advised one and the treacherous, and Anselmo returning to his house asked Camilla the question she already wondered he had not asked before--what it was that had caused her to write the letter she had sent him. Camilla replied that it had seemed to her that Lothario looked at her somewhat more freely than when he had been at home; but that now she was undeceived and believed it to have been only her own imagination, for Lothario now avoided seeing her, or being alone with her. Anselmo told her she might be quite easy on the score of that suspicion, for he knew that Lothario was in love with a damsel of rank in the city whom he celebrated under the name of Chloris, and that even if he were not, his fidelity and their great friendship left no room for fear. Had not Camilla, however, been informed beforehand by Lothario that this love for Chloris was a pretence, and that he himself had told Anselmo of it in order to be able sometimes to give utterance to the praises of Camilla herself, no doubt she would have fallen into the despairing toils of jealousy; but being forewarned she received the startling news without uneasiness.\n\nThe next day as the three were at table Anselmo asked Lothario to recite something of what he had composed for his mistress Chloris; for as Camilla did not know her, he might safely say what he liked.\n\n“Even did she know her,” returned Lothario, “I would hide nothing, for when a lover praises his lady’s beauty, and charges her with cruelty, he casts no imputation upon her fair name; at any rate, all I can say is that yesterday I made a sonnet on the ingratitude of this Chloris, which goes thus:\n\nAt midnight, in the silence, when the eyes Of happier mortals balmy slumbers close, The weary tale of my unnumbered woes To Chloris and to Heaven is wont to rise. And when the light of day returning dyes The portals of the east with tints of rose, With undiminished force my sorrow flows In broken accents and in burning sighs. And when the sun ascends his star-girt throne, And on the earth pours down his midday beams, Noon but renews my wailing and my tears; And with the night again goes up my moan. Yet ever in my agony it seems To me that neither Heaven nor Chloris hears.”\n\nThe sonnet pleased Camilla, and still more Anselmo, for he praised it and said the lady was excessively cruel who made no return for sincerity so manifest. On which Camilla said, “Then all that love-smitten poets say is true?”\n\n“As poets they do not tell the truth,” replied Lothario; “but as lovers they are not more defective in expression than they are truthful.”\n\n“There is no doubt of that,” observed Anselmo, anxious to support and uphold Lothario’s ideas with Camilla, who was as regardless of his design as she was deep in love with Lothario; and so taking delight in anything that was his, and knowing that his thoughts and writings had her for their object, and that she herself was the real Chloris, she asked him to repeat some other sonnet or verses if he recollected any.\n\n“I do,” replied Lothario, “but I do not think it as good as the first one, or, more correctly speaking, less bad; but you can easily judge, for it is this.\n\nI know that I am doomed; death is to me As certain as that thou, ungrateful fair, Dead at thy feet shouldst see me lying, ere My heart repented of its love for thee. If buried in oblivion I should be, Bereft of life, fame, favour, even there It would be found that I thy image bear Deep graven in my breast for all to see. This like some holy relic do I prize To save me from the fate my truth entails, Truth that to thy hard heart its vigour owes. Alas for him that under lowering skies, In peril o’er a trackless ocean sails, Where neither friendly port nor pole-star shows.”\n\nAnselmo praised this second sonnet too, as he had praised the first; and so he went on adding link after link to the chain with which he was binding himself and making his dishonour secure; for when Lothario was doing most to dishonour him he told him he was most honoured; and thus each step that Camilla descended towards the depths of her abasement, she mounted, in his opinion, towards the summit of virtue and fair fame.\n\nIt so happened that finding herself on one occasion alone with her maid, Camilla said to her, “I am ashamed to think, my dear Leonela, how lightly I have valued myself that I did not compel Lothario to purchase by at least some expenditure of time that full possession of me that I so quickly yielded him of my own free will. I fear that he will think ill of my pliancy or lightness, not considering the irresistible influence he brought to bear upon me.”\n\n“Let not that trouble you, my lady,” said Leonela, “for it does not take away the value of the thing given or make it the less precious to give it quickly if it be really valuable and worthy of being prized; nay, they are wont to say that he who gives quickly gives twice.”\n\n“They say also,” said Camilla, “that what costs little is valued less.”\n\n“That saying does not hold good in your case,” replied Leonela, “for love, as I have heard say, sometimes flies and sometimes walks; with this one it runs, with that it moves slowly; some it cools, others it burns; some it wounds, others it slays; it begins the course of its desires, and at the same moment completes and ends it; in the morning it will lay siege to a fortress and by night will have taken it, for there is no power that can resist it; so what are you in dread of, what do you fear, when the same must have befallen Lothario, love having chosen the absence of my lord as the instrument for subduing you? and it was absolutely necessary to complete then what love had resolved upon, without affording the time to let Anselmo return and by his presence compel the work to be left unfinished; for love has no better agent for carrying out his designs than opportunity; and of opportunity he avails himself in all his feats, especially at the outset. All this I know well myself, more by experience than by hearsay, and some day, señora, I will enlighten you on the subject, for I am of your flesh and blood too. Moreover, lady Camilla, you did not surrender yourself or yield so quickly but that first you saw Lothario’s whole soul in his eyes, in his sighs, in his words, his promises and his gifts, and by it and his good qualities perceived how worthy he was of your love. This, then, being the case, let not these scrupulous and prudish ideas trouble your imagination, but be assured that Lothario prizes you as you do him, and rest content and satisfied that as you are caught in the noose of love it is one of worth and merit that has taken you, and one that has not only the four S’s that they say true lovers ought to have, but a complete alphabet; only listen to me and you will see how I can repeat it by rote. He is to my eyes and thinking, Amiable, Brave, Courteous, Distinguished, Elegant, Fond, Gay, Honourable, Illustrious, Loyal, Manly, Noble, Open, Polite, Quickwitted, Rich, and the S’s according to the saying, and then Tender, Veracious: X does not suit him, for it is a rough letter; Y has been given already; and Z Zealous for your honour.”\n\nCamilla laughed at her maid’s alphabet, and perceived her to be more experienced in love affairs than she said, which she admitted, confessing to Camilla that she had love passages with a young man of good birth of the same city. Camilla was uneasy at this, dreading lest it might prove the means of endangering her honour, and asked whether her intrigue had gone beyond words, and she with little shame and much effrontery said it had; for certain it is that ladies’ imprudences make servants shameless, who, when they see their mistresses make a false step, think nothing of going astray themselves, or of its being known. All that Camilla could do was to entreat Leonela to say nothing about her doings to him whom she called her lover, and to conduct her own affairs secretly lest they should come to the knowledge of Anselmo or of Lothario. Leonela said she would, but kept her word in such a way that she confirmed Camilla’s apprehension of losing her reputation through her means; for this abandoned and bold Leonela, as soon as she perceived that her mistress’s demeanour was not what it was wont to be, had the audacity to introduce her lover into the house, confident that even if her mistress saw him she would not dare to expose him; for the sins of mistresses entail this mischief among others; they make themselves the slaves of their own servants, and are obliged to hide their laxities and depravities; as was the case with Camilla, who though she perceived, not once but many times, that Leonela was with her lover in some room of the house, not only did not dare to chide her, but afforded her opportunities for concealing him and removed all difficulties, lest he should be seen by her husband. She was unable, however, to prevent him from being seen on one occasion, as he sallied forth at daybreak, by Lothario, who, not knowing who he was, at first took him for a spectre; but, as soon as he saw him hasten away, muffling his face with his cloak and concealing himself carefully and cautiously, he rejected this foolish idea, and adopted another, which would have been the ruin of all had not Camilla found a remedy. It did not occur to Lothario that this man he had seen issuing at such an untimely hour from Anselmo’s house could have entered it on Leonela’s account, nor did he even remember there was such a person as Leonela; all he thought was that as Camilla had been light and yielding with him, so she had been with another; for this further penalty the erring woman’s sin brings with it, that her honour is distrusted even by him to whose overtures and persuasions she has yielded; and he believes her to have surrendered more easily to others, and gives implicit credence to every suspicion that comes into his mind. All Lothario’s good sense seems to have failed him at this juncture; all his prudent maxims escaped his memory; for without once reflecting rationally, and without more ado, in his impatience and in the blindness of the jealous rage that gnawed his heart, and dying to revenge himself upon Camilla, who had done him no wrong, before Anselmo had risen he hastened to him and said to him, “Know, Anselmo, that for several days past I have been struggling with myself, striving to withhold from thee what it is no longer possible or right that I should conceal from thee. Know that Camilla’s fortress has surrendered and is ready to submit to my will; and if I have been slow to reveal this fact to thee, it was in order to see if it were some light caprice of hers, or if she sought to try me and ascertain if the love I began to make to her with thy permission was made with a serious intention. I thought, too, that she, if she were what she ought to be, and what we both believed her, would have ere this given thee information of my addresses; but seeing that she delays, I believe the truth of the promise she has given me that the next time thou art absent from the house she will grant me an interview in the closet where thy jewels are kept (and it was true that Camilla used to meet him there); but I do not wish thee to rush precipitately to take vengeance, for the sin is as yet only committed in intention, and Camilla’s may change perhaps between this and the appointed time, and repentance spring up in its place. As hitherto thou hast always followed my advice wholly or in part, follow and observe this that I will give thee now, so that, without mistake, and with mature deliberation, thou mayest satisfy thyself as to what may seem the best course; pretend to absent thyself for two or three days as thou hast been wont to do on other occasions, and contrive to hide thyself in the closet; for the tapestries and other things there afford great facilities for thy concealment, and then thou wilt see with thine own eyes and I with mine what Camilla’s purpose may be. And if it be a guilty one, which may be feared rather than expected, with silence, prudence, and discretion thou canst thyself become the instrument of punishment for the wrong done thee.”\n\nAnselmo was amazed, overwhelmed, and astounded at the words of Lothario, which came upon him at a time when he least expected to hear them, for he now looked upon Camilla as having triumphed over the pretended attacks of Lothario, and was beginning to enjoy the glory of her victory. He remained silent for a considerable time, looking on the ground with fixed gaze, and at length said, “Thou hast behaved, Lothario, as I expected of thy friendship: I will follow thy advice in everything; do as thou wilt, and keep this secret as thou seest it should be kept in circumstances so unlooked for.”\n\nLothario gave him his word, but after leaving him he repented altogether of what he had said to him, perceiving how foolishly he had acted, as he might have revenged himself upon Camilla in some less cruel and degrading way. He cursed his want of sense, condemned his hasty resolution, and knew not what course to take to undo the mischief or find some ready escape from it. At last he decided upon revealing all to Camilla, and, as there was no want of opportunity for doing so, he found her alone the same day; but she, as soon as she had the chance of speaking to him, said, “Lothario my friend, I must tell thee I have a sorrow in my heart which fills it so that it seems ready to burst; and it will be a wonder if it does not; for the audacity of Leonela has now reached such a pitch that every night she conceals a gallant of hers in this house and remains with him till morning, at the expense of my reputation; inasmuch as it is open to anyone to question it who may see him quitting my house at such unseasonable hours; but what distresses me is that I cannot punish or chide her, for her privity to our intrigue bridles my mouth and keeps me silent about hers, while I am dreading that some catastrophe will come of it.”\n\nAs Camilla said this Lothario at first imagined it was some device to delude him into the idea that the man he had seen going out was Leonela’s lover and not hers; but when he saw how she wept and suffered, and begged him to help her, he became convinced of the truth, and the conviction completed his confusion and remorse; however, he told Camilla not to distress herself, as he would take measures to put a stop to the insolence of Leonela. At the same time he told her what, driven by the fierce rage of jealousy, he had said to Anselmo, and how he had arranged to hide himself in the closet that he might there see plainly how little she preserved her fidelity to him; and he entreated her pardon for this madness, and her advice as to how to repair it, and escape safely from the intricate labyrinth in which his imprudence had involved him. Camilla was struck with alarm at hearing what Lothario said, and with much anger, and great good sense, she reproved him and rebuked his base design and the foolish and mischievous resolution he had made; but as woman has by nature a nimbler wit than man for good and for evil, though it is apt to fail when she sets herself deliberately to reason, Camilla on the spur of the moment thought of a way to remedy what was to all appearance irremediable, and told Lothario to contrive that the next day Anselmo should conceal himself in the place he mentioned, for she hoped from his concealment to obtain the means of their enjoying themselves for the future without any apprehension; and without revealing her purpose to him entirely she charged him to be careful, as soon as Anselmo was concealed, to come to her when Leonela should call him, and to all she said to him to answer as he would have answered had he not known that Anselmo was listening. Lothario pressed her to explain her intention fully, so that he might with more certainty and precaution take care to do what he saw to be needful.\n\n“I tell you,” said Camilla, “there is nothing to take care of except to answer me what I shall ask you;” for she did not wish to explain to him beforehand what she meant to do, fearing lest he should be unwilling to follow out an idea which seemed to her such a good one, and should try or devise some other less practicable plan.\n\nLothario then retired, and the next day Anselmo, under pretence of going to his friend’s country house, took his departure, and then returned to conceal himself, which he was able to do easily, as Camilla and Leonela took care to give him the opportunity; and so he placed himself in hiding in the state of agitation that it may be imagined he would feel who expected to see the vitals of his honour laid bare before his eyes, and found himself on the point of losing the supreme blessing he thought he possessed in his beloved Camilla. Having made sure of Anselmo’s being in his hiding-place, Camilla and Leonela entered the closet, and the instant she set foot within it Camilla said, with a deep sigh, “Ah! dear Leonela, would it not be better, before I do what I am unwilling you should know lest you should seek to prevent it, that you should take Anselmo’s dagger that I have asked of you and with it pierce this vile heart of mine? But no; there is no reason why I should suffer the punishment of another’s fault. I will first know what it is that the bold licentious eyes of Lothario have seen in me that could have encouraged him to reveal to me a design so base as that which he has disclosed regardless of his friend and of my honour. Go to the window, Leonela, and call him, for no doubt he is in the street waiting to carry out his vile project; but mine, cruel it may be, but honourable, shall be carried out first.”\n\n“Ah, señora,” said the crafty Leonela, who knew her part, “what is it you want to do with this dagger? Can it be that you mean to take your own life, or Lothario’s? for whichever you mean to do, it will lead to the loss of your reputation and good name. It is better to dissemble your wrong and not give this wicked man the chance of entering the house now and finding us alone; consider, señora, we are weak women and he is a man, and determined, and as he comes with such a base purpose, blind and urged by passion, perhaps before you can put yours into execution he may do what will be worse for you than taking your life. Ill betide my master, Anselmo, for giving such authority in his house to this shameless fellow! And supposing you kill him, señora, as I suspect you mean to do, what shall we do with him when he is dead?”\n\n“What, my friend?” replied Camilla, “we shall leave him for Anselmo to bury him; for in reason it will be to him a light labour to hide his own infamy under ground. Summon him, make haste, for all the time I delay in taking vengeance for my wrong seems to me an offence against the loyalty I owe my husband.”\n\nAnselmo was listening to all this, and every word that Camilla uttered made him change his mind; but when he heard that it was resolved to kill Lothario his first impulse was to come out and show himself to avert such a disaster; but in his anxiety to see the issue of a resolution so bold and virtuous he restrained himself, intending to come forth in time to prevent the deed. At this moment Camilla, throwing herself upon a bed that was close by, swooned away, and Leonela began to weep bitterly, exclaiming, “Woe is me! that I should be fated to have dying here in my arms the flower of virtue upon earth, the crown of true wives, the pattern of chastity!” with more to the same effect, so that anyone who heard her would have taken her for the most tender-hearted and faithful handmaid in the world, and her mistress for another persecuted Penelope.\n\nCamilla was not long in recovering from her fainting fit and on coming to herself she said, “Why do you not go, Leonela, to call hither that friend, the falsest to his friend the sun ever shone upon or night concealed? Away, run, haste, speed! lest the fire of my wrath burn itself out with delay, and the righteous vengeance that I hope for melt away in menaces and maledictions.”\n\n“I am just going to call him, señora,” said Leonela; “but you must first give me that dagger, lest while I am gone you should by means of it give cause to all who love you to weep all their lives.”\n\n“Go in peace, dear Leonela, I will not do so,” said Camilla, “for rash and foolish as I may be, to your mind, in defending my honour, I am not going to be so much so as that Lucretia who they say killed herself without having done anything wrong, and without having first killed him on whom the guilt of her misfortune lay. I shall die, if I am to die; but it must be after full vengeance upon him who has brought me here to weep over audacity that no fault of mine gave birth to.”\n\nLeonela required much pressing before she would go to summon Lothario, but at last she went, and while awaiting her return Camilla continued, as if speaking to herself, “Good God! would it not have been more prudent to have repulsed Lothario, as I have done many a time before, than to allow him, as I am now doing, to think me unchaste and vile, even for the short time I must wait until I undeceive him? No doubt it would have been better; but I should not be avenged, nor the honour of my husband vindicated, should he find so clear and easy an escape from the strait into which his depravity has led him. Let the traitor pay with his life for the temerity of his wanton wishes, and let the world know (if haply it shall ever come to know) that Camilla not only preserved her allegiance to her husband, but avenged him of the man who dared to wrong him. Still, I think it might be better to disclose this to Anselmo. But then I have called his attention to it in the letter I wrote to him in the country, and, if he did nothing to prevent the mischief I there pointed out to him, I suppose it was that from pure goodness of heart and trustfulness he would not and could not believe that any thought against his honour could harbour in the breast of so stanch a friend; nor indeed did I myself believe it for many days, nor should I have ever believed it if his insolence had not gone so far as to make it manifest by open presents, lavish promises, and ceaseless tears. But why do I argue thus? Does a bold determination stand in need of arguments? Surely not. Then traitors avaunt! Vengeance to my aid! Let the false one come, approach, advance, die, yield up his life, and then befall what may. Pure I came to him whom Heaven bestowed upon me, pure I shall leave him; and at the worst bathed in my own chaste blood and in the foul blood of the falsest friend that friendship ever saw in the world;” and as she uttered these words she paced the room holding the unsheathed dagger, with such irregular and disordered steps, and such gestures that one would have supposed her to have lost her senses, and taken her for some violent desperado instead of a delicate woman.\n\nAnselmo, concealed behind some tapestries where he had hidden himself, beheld and was amazed at all, and already felt that what he had seen and heard was a sufficient answer to even greater suspicions; and he would have been now well pleased if the proof afforded by Lothario’s coming were dispensed with, as he feared some sudden mishap; but as he was on the point of showing himself and coming forth to embrace and undeceive his wife he paused as he saw Leonela returning, leading Lothario. Camilla when she saw him, drawing a long line in front of her on the floor with the dagger, said to him, “Lothario, pay attention to what I say to thee: if by any chance thou darest to cross this line thou seest, or even approach it, the instant I see thee attempt it that same instant will I pierce my bosom with this dagger that I hold in my hand; and before thou answerest me a word I desire thee to listen to a few from me, and afterwards thou shalt reply as may please thee. First, I desire thee to tell me, Lothario, if thou knowest my husband Anselmo, and in what light thou regardest him; and secondly I desire to know if thou knowest me too. Answer me this, without embarrassment or reflecting deeply what thou wilt answer, for they are no riddles I put to thee.”\n\nLothario was not so dull but that from the first moment when Camilla directed him to make Anselmo hide himself he understood what she intended to do, and therefore he fell in with her idea so readily and promptly that between them they made the imposture look more true than truth; so he answered her thus: “I did not think, fair Camilla, that thou wert calling me to ask questions so remote from the object with which I come; but if it is to defer the promised reward thou art doing so, thou mightst have put it off still longer, for the longing for happiness gives the more distress the nearer comes the hope of gaining it; but lest thou shouldst say that I do not answer thy questions, I say that I know thy husband Anselmo, and that we have known each other from our earliest years; I will not speak of what thou too knowest, of our friendship, that I may not compel myself to testify against the wrong that love, the mighty excuse for greater errors, makes me inflict upon him. Thee I know and hold in the same estimation as he does, for were it not so I had not for a lesser prize acted in opposition to what I owe to my station and the holy laws of true friendship, now broken and violated by me through that powerful enemy, love.”\n\n“If thou dost confess that,” returned Camilla, “mortal enemy of all that rightly deserves to be loved, with what face dost thou dare to come before one whom thou knowest to be the mirror wherein he is reflected on whom thou shouldst look to see how unworthily thou him? But, woe is me, I now comprehend what has made thee give so little heed to what thou owest to thyself; it must have been some freedom of mine, for I will not call it immodesty, as it did not proceed from any deliberate intention, but from some heedlessness such as women are guilty of through inadvertence when they think they have no occasion for reserve. But tell me, traitor, when did I by word or sign give a reply to thy prayers that could awaken in thee a shadow of hope of attaining thy base wishes? When were not thy professions of love sternly and scornfully rejected and rebuked? When were thy frequent pledges and still more frequent gifts believed or accepted? But as I am persuaded that no one can long persevere in the attempt to win love unsustained by some hope, I am willing to attribute to myself the blame of thy assurance, for no doubt some thoughtlessness of mine has all this time fostered thy hopes; and therefore will I punish myself and inflict upon myself the penalty thy guilt deserves. And that thou mayest see that being so relentless to myself I cannot possibly be otherwise to thee, I have summoned thee to be a witness of the sacrifice I mean to offer to the injured honour of my honoured husband, wronged by thee with all the assiduity thou wert capable of, and by me too through want of caution in avoiding every occasion, if I have given any, of encouraging and sanctioning thy base designs. Once more I say the suspicion in my mind that some imprudence of mine has engendered these lawless thoughts in thee, is what causes me most distress and what I desire most to punish with my own hands, for were any other instrument of punishment employed my error might become perhaps more widely known; but before I do so, in my death I mean to inflict death, and take with me one that will fully satisfy my longing for the revenge I hope for and have; for I shall see, wheresoever it may be that I go, the penalty awarded by inflexible, unswerving justice on him who has placed me in a position so desperate.”\n\nAs she uttered these words, with incredible energy and swiftness she flew upon Lothario with the naked dagger, so manifestly bent on burying it in his breast that he was almost uncertain whether these demonstrations were real or feigned, for he was obliged to have recourse to all his skill and strength to prevent her from striking him; and with such reality did she act this strange farce and mystification that, to give it a colour of truth, she determined to stain it with her own blood; for perceiving, or pretending, that she could not wound Lothario, she said, “Fate, it seems, will not grant my just desire complete satisfaction, but it will not be able to keep me from satisfying it partially at least;” and making an effort to free the hand with the dagger which Lothario held in his grasp, she released it, and directing the point to a place where it could not inflict a deep wound, she plunged it into her left side high up close to the shoulder, and then allowed herself to fall to the ground as if in a faint.\n\nLeonela and Lothario stood amazed and astounded at the catastrophe, and seeing Camilla stretched on the ground and bathed in her blood they were still uncertain as to the true nature of the act. Lothario, terrified and breathless, ran in haste to pluck out the dagger; but when he saw how slight the wound was he was relieved of his fears and once more admired the subtlety, coolness, and ready wit of the fair Camilla; and the better to support the part he had to play he began to utter profuse and doleful lamentations over her body as if she were dead, invoking maledictions not only on himself but also on him who had been the means of placing him in such a position: and knowing that his friend Anselmo heard him he spoke in such a way as to make a listener feel much more pity for him than for Camilla, even though he supposed her dead. Leonela took her up in her arms and laid her on the bed, entreating Lothario to go in quest of some one to attend to her wound in secret, and at the same time asking his advice and opinion as to what they should say to Anselmo about his lady’s wound if he should chance to return before it was healed. He replied they might say what they liked, for he was not in a state to give advice that would be of any use; all he could tell her was to try and stanch the blood, as he was going where he should never more be seen; and with every appearance of deep grief and sorrow he left the house; but when he found himself alone, and where there was nobody to see him, he crossed himself unceasingly, lost in wonder at the adroitness of Camilla and the consistent acting of Leonela. He reflected how convinced Anselmo would be that he had a second Portia for a wife, and he looked forward anxiously to meeting him in order to rejoice together over falsehood and truth the most craftily veiled that could be imagined.\n\nLeonela, as he told her, stanched her lady’s blood, which was no more than sufficed to support her deception; and washing the wound with a little wine she bound it up to the best of her skill, talking all the time she was tending her in a strain that, even if nothing else had been said before, would have been enough to assure Anselmo that he had in Camilla a model of purity. To Leonela’s words Camilla added her own, calling herself cowardly and wanting in spirit, since she had not enough at the time she had most need of it to rid herself of the life she so much loathed. She asked her attendant’s advice as to whether or not she ought to inform her beloved husband of all that had happened, but the other bade her say nothing about it, as she would lay upon him the obligation of taking vengeance on Lothario, which he could not do but at great risk to himself; and it was the duty of a true wife not to give her husband provocation to quarrel, but, on the contrary, to remove it as far as possible from him.\n\nCamilla replied that she believed she was right and that she would follow her advice, but at any rate it would be well to consider how she was to explain the wound to Anselmo, for he could not help seeing it; to which Leonela answered that she did not know how to tell a lie even in jest.\n\n“How then can I know, my dear?” said Camilla, “for I should not dare to forge or keep up a falsehood if my life depended on it. If we can think of no escape from this difficulty, it will be better to tell him the plain truth than that he should find us out in an untrue story.”\n\n“Be not uneasy, señora,” said Leonela; “between this and to-morrow I will think of what we must say to him, and perhaps the wound being where it is it can be hidden from his sight, and Heaven will be pleased to aid us in a purpose so good and honourable. Compose yourself, señora, and endeavour to calm your excitement lest my lord find you agitated; and leave the rest to my care and God’s, who always supports good intentions.”\n\nAnselmo had with the deepest attention listened to and seen played out the tragedy of the death of his honour, which the performers acted with such wonderfully effective truth that it seemed as if they had become the realities of the parts they played. He longed for night and an opportunity of escaping from the house to go and see his good friend Lothario, and with him give vent to his joy over the precious pearl he had gained in having established his wife’s purity. Both mistress and maid took care to give him time and opportunity to get away, and taking advantage of it he made his escape, and at once went in quest of Lothario, and it would be impossible to describe how he embraced him when he found him, and the things he said to him in the joy of his heart, and the praises he bestowed upon Camilla; all which Lothario listened to without being able to show any pleasure, for he could not forget how deceived his friend was, and how dishonourably he had wronged him; and though Anselmo could see that Lothario was not glad, still he imagined it was only because he had left Camilla wounded and had been himself the cause of it; and so among other things he told him not to be distressed about Camilla’s accident, for, as they had agreed to hide it from him, the wound was evidently trifling; and that being so, he had no cause for fear, but should henceforward be of good cheer and rejoice with him, seeing that by his means and adroitness he found himself raised to the greatest height of happiness that he could have ventured to hope for, and desired no better pastime than making verses in praise of Camilla that would preserve her name for all time to come. Lothario commended his purpose, and promised on his own part to aid him in raising a monument so glorious.\n\nAnd so Anselmo was left the most charmingly hoodwinked man there could be in the world. He himself, persuaded he was conducting the instrument of his glory, led home by the hand him who had been the utter destruction of his good name; whom Camilla received with averted countenance, though with smiles in her heart. The deception was carried on for some time, until at the end of a few months Fortune turned her wheel and the guilt which had been until then so skilfully concealed was published abroad, and Anselmo paid with his life the penalty of his ill-advised curiosity.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF THE HEROIC AND PRODIGIOUS BATTLE DON QUIXOTE HAD WITH CERTAIN SKINS OF RED WINE, AND BRINGS THE NOVEL OF “THE ILL-ADVISED CURIOSITY” TO A CLOSE\nThere remained but little more of the novel to be read, when Sancho Panza burst forth in wild excitement from the garret where Don Quixote was lying, shouting, “Run, sirs! quick; and help my master, who is in the thick of the toughest and stiffest battle I ever laid eyes on. By the living God he has given the giant, the enemy of my lady the Princess Micomicona, such a slash that he has sliced his head clean off as if it were a turnip.”\n\n“What are you talking about, brother?” said the curate, pausing as he was about to read the remainder of the novel. “Are you in your senses, Sancho? How the devil can it be as you say, when the giant is two thousand leagues away?”\n\nHere they heard a loud noise in the chamber, and Don Quixote shouting out, “Stand, thief, brigand, villain; now I have got thee, and thy scimitar shall not avail thee!” And then it seemed as though he were slashing vigorously at the wall.\n\n“Don’t stop to listen,” said Sancho, “but go in and part them or help my master: though there is no need of that now, for no doubt the giant is dead by this time and giving account to God of his past wicked life; for I saw the blood flowing on the ground, and the head cut off and fallen on one side, and it is as big as a large wine-skin.”\n\n“May I die,” said the landlord at this, “if Don Quixote or Don Devil has not been slashing some of the skins of red wine that stand full at his bed’s head, and the spilt wine must be what this good fellow takes for blood;” and so saying he went into the room and the rest after him, and there they found Don Quixote in the strangest costume in the world. He was in his shirt, which was not long enough in front to cover his thighs completely and was six fingers shorter behind; his legs were very long and lean, covered with hair, and anything but clean; on his head he had a little greasy red cap that belonged to the host, round his left arm he had rolled the blanket of the bed, to which Sancho, for reasons best known to himself, owed a grudge, and in his right hand he held his unsheathed sword, with which he was slashing about on all sides, uttering exclamations as if he were actually fighting some giant: and the best of it was his eyes were not open, for he was fast asleep, and dreaming that he was doing battle with the giant. For his imagination was so wrought upon by the adventure he was going to accomplish, that it made him dream he had already reached the kingdom of Micomicon, and was engaged in combat with his enemy; and believing he was laying on the giant, he had given so many sword cuts to the skins that the whole room was full of wine. On seeing this the landlord was so enraged that he fell on Don Quixote, and with his clenched fist began to pummel him in such a way, that if Cardenio and the curate had not dragged him off, he would have brought the war of the giant to an end. But in spite of all the poor gentleman never woke until the barber brought a great pot of cold water from the well and flung it with one dash all over his body, on which Don Quixote woke up, but not so completely as to understand what was the matter. Dorothea, seeing how short and slight his attire was, would not go in to witness the battle between her champion and her opponent. As for Sancho, he went searching all over the floor for the head of the giant, and not finding it he said, “I see now that it’s all enchantment in this house; for the last time, on this very spot where I am now, I got ever so many thumps without knowing who gave them to me, or being able to see anybody; and now this head is not to be seen anywhere about, though I saw it cut off with my own eyes and the blood running from the body as if from a fountain.”\n\n“What blood and fountains are you talking about, enemy of God and his saints?” said the landlord. “Don’t you see, you thief, that the blood and the fountain are only these skins here that have been stabbed and the red wine swimming all over the room?--and I wish I saw the soul of him that stabbed them swimming in hell.”\n\n“I know nothing about that,” said Sancho; “all I know is it will be my bad luck that through not finding this head my county will melt away like salt in water;”--for Sancho awake was worse than his master asleep, so much had his master’s promises addled his wits.\n\nThe landlord was beside himself at the coolness of the squire and the mischievous doings of the master, and swore it should not be like the last time when they went without paying; and that their privileges of chivalry should not hold good this time to let one or other of them off without paying, even to the cost of the plugs that would have to be put to the damaged wine-skins. The curate was holding Don Quixote’s hands, who, fancying he had now ended the adventure and was in the presence of the Princess Micomicona, knelt before the curate and said, “Exalted and beauteous lady, your highness may live from this day forth fearless of any harm this base being could do you; and I too from this day forth am released from the promise I gave you, since by the help of God on high and by the favour of her by whom I live and breathe, I have fulfilled it so successfully.”\n\n“Did not I say so?” said Sancho on hearing this. “You see I wasn’t drunk; there you see my master has already salted the giant; there’s no doubt about the bulls; my county is all right!”\n\nWho could have helped laughing at the absurdities of the pair, master and man? And laugh they did, all except the landlord, who cursed himself; but at length the barber, Cardenio, and the curate contrived with no small trouble to get Don Quixote on the bed, and he fell asleep with every appearance of excessive weariness. They left him to sleep, and came out to the gate of the inn to console Sancho Panza on not having found the head of the giant; but much more work had they to appease the landlord, who was furious at the sudden death of his wine-skins; and said the landlady half scolding, half crying, “At an evil moment and in an unlucky hour he came into my house, this knight-errant--would that I had never set eyes on him, for dear he has cost me; the last time he went off with the overnight score against him for supper, bed, straw, and barley, for himself and his squire and a hack and an ass, saying he was a knight adventurer--God send unlucky adventures to him and all the adventurers in the world--and therefore not bound to pay anything, for it was so settled by the knight-errantry tariff: and then, all because of him, came the other gentleman and carried off my tail, and gives it back more than two cuartillos the worse, all stripped of its hair, so that it is no use for my husband’s purpose; and then, for a finishing touch to all, to burst my wine-skins and spill my wine! I wish I saw his own blood spilt! But let him not deceive himself, for, by the bones of my father and the shade of my mother, they shall pay me down every quarto; or my name is not what it is, and I am not my father’s daughter.” All this and more to the same effect the landlady delivered with great irritation, and her good maid Maritornes backed her up, while the daughter held her peace and smiled from time to time. The curate smoothed matters by promising to make good all losses to the best of his power, not only as regarded the wine-skins but also the wine, and above all the depreciation of the tail which they set such store by. Dorothea comforted Sancho, telling him that she pledged herself, as soon as it should appear certain that his master had decapitated the giant, and she found herself peacefully established in her kingdom, to bestow upon him the best county there was in it. With this Sancho consoled himself, and assured the princess she might rely upon it that he had seen the head of the giant, and more by token it had a beard that reached to the girdle, and that if it was not to be seen now it was because everything that happened in that house went by enchantment, as he himself had proved the last time he had lodged there. Dorothea said she fully believed it, and that he need not be uneasy, for all would go well and turn out as he wished. All therefore being appeased, the curate was anxious to go on with the novel, as he saw there was but little more left to read. Dorothea and the others begged him to finish it, and he, as he was willing to please them, and enjoyed reading it himself, continued the tale in these words:\n\nThe result was, that from the confidence Anselmo felt in Camilla’s virtue, he lived happy and free from anxiety, and Camilla purposely looked coldly on Lothario, that Anselmo might suppose her feelings towards him to be the opposite of what they were; and the better to support the position, Lothario begged to be excused from coming to the house, as the displeasure with which Camilla regarded his presence was plain to be seen. But the befooled Anselmo said he would on no account allow such a thing, and so in a thousand ways he became the author of his own dishonour, while he believed he was insuring his happiness. Meanwhile the satisfaction with which Leonela saw herself empowered to carry on her amour reached such a height that, regardless of everything else, she followed her inclinations unrestrainedly, feeling confident that her mistress would screen her, and even show her how to manage it safely. At last one night Anselmo heard footsteps in Leonela’s room, and on trying to enter to see who it was, he found that the door was held against him, which made him all the more determined to open it; and exerting his strength he forced it open, and entered the room in time to see a man leaping through the window into the street. He ran quickly to seize him or discover who he was, but he was unable to effect either purpose, for Leonela flung her arms round him crying, “Be calm, señor; do not give way to passion or follow him who has escaped from this; he belongs to me, and in fact he is my husband.”\n\nAnselmo would not believe it, but blind with rage drew a dagger and threatened to stab Leonela, bidding her tell the truth or he would kill her. She, in her fear, not knowing what she was saying, exclaimed, “Do not kill me, señor, for I can tell you things more important than any you can imagine.”\n\n“Tell me then at once or thou diest,” said Anselmo.\n\n“It would be impossible for me now,” said Leonela, “I am so agitated: leave me till to-morrow, and then you shall hear from me what will fill you with astonishment; but rest assured that he who leaped through the window is a young man of this city, who has given me his promise to become my husband.”\n\nAnselmo was appeased with this, and was content to wait the time she asked of him, for he never expected to hear anything against Camilla, so satisfied and sure of her virtue was he; and so he quitted the room, and left Leonela locked in, telling her she should not come out until she had told him all she had to make known to him. He went at once to see Camilla, and tell her, as he did, all that had passed between him and her handmaid, and the promise she had given him to inform him matters of serious importance.\n\nThere is no need of saying whether Camilla was agitated or not, for so great was her fear and dismay, that, making sure, as she had good reason to do, that Leonela would tell Anselmo all she knew of her faithlessness, she had not the courage to wait and see if her suspicions were confirmed; and that same night, as soon as she thought that Anselmo was asleep, she packed up the most valuable jewels she had and some money, and without being observed by anybody escaped from the house and betook herself to Lothario’s, to whom she related what had occurred, imploring him to convey her to some place of safety or fly with her where they might be safe from Anselmo. The state of perplexity to which Camilla reduced Lothario was such that he was unable to utter a word in reply, still less to decide upon what he should do. At length he resolved to conduct her to a convent of which a sister of his was prioress; Camilla agreed to this, and with the speed which the circumstances demanded, Lothario took her to the convent and left her there, and then himself quitted the city without letting anyone know of his departure.\n\nAs soon as daylight came Anselmo, without missing Camilla from his side, rose cager to learn what Leonela had to tell him, and hastened to the room where he had locked her in. He opened the door, entered, but found no Leonela; all he found was some sheets knotted to the window, a plain proof that she had let herself down from it and escaped. He returned, uneasy, to tell Camilla, but not finding her in bed or anywhere in the house he was lost in amazement. He asked the servants of the house about her, but none of them could give him any explanation. As he was going in search of Camilla it happened by chance that he observed her boxes were lying open, and that the greater part of her jewels were gone; and now he became fully aware of his disgrace, and that Leonela was not the cause of his misfortune; and, just as he was, without delaying to dress himself completely, he repaired, sad at heart and dejected, to his friend Lothario to make known his sorrow to him; but when he failed to find him and the servants reported that he had been absent from his house all night and had taken with him all the money he had, he felt as though he were losing his senses; and to make all complete on returning to his own house he found it deserted and empty, not one of all his servants, male or female, remaining in it. He knew not what to think, or say, or do, and his reason seemed to be deserting him little by little. He reviewed his position, and saw himself in a moment left without wife, friend, or servants, abandoned, he felt, by the heaven above him, and more than all robbed of his honour, for in Camilla’s disappearance he saw his own ruin. After long reflection he resolved at last to go to his friend’s village, where he had been staying when he afforded opportunities for the contrivance of this complication of misfortune. He locked the doors of his house, mounted his horse, and with a broken spirit set out on his journey; but he had hardly gone half-way when, harassed by his reflections, he had to dismount and tie his horse to a tree, at the foot of which he threw himself, giving vent to piteous heartrending sighs; and there he remained till nearly nightfall, when he observed a man approaching on horseback from the city, of whom, after saluting him, he asked what was the news in Florence.\n\nThe citizen replied, “The strangest that have been heard for many a day; for it is reported abroad that Lothario, the great friend of the wealthy Anselmo, who lived at San Giovanni, carried off last night Camilla, the wife of Anselmo, who also has disappeared. All this has been told by a maid-servant of Camilla’s, whom the governor found last night lowering herself by a sheet from the windows of Anselmo’s house. I know not indeed, precisely, how the affair came to pass; all I know is that the whole city is wondering at the occurrence, for no one could have expected a thing of the kind, seeing the great and intimate friendship that existed between them, so great, they say, that they were called ‘The Two Friends.’”\n\n“Is it known at all,” said Anselmo, “what road Lothario and Camilla took?”\n\n“Not in the least,” said the citizen, “though the governor has been very active in searching for them.”\n\n“God speed you, señor,” said Anselmo.\n\n“God be with you,” said the citizen and went his way.\n\nThis disastrous intelligence almost robbed Anselmo not only of his senses but of his life. He got up as well as he was able and reached the house of his friend, who as yet knew nothing of his misfortune, but seeing him come pale, worn, and haggard, perceived that he was suffering some heavy affliction. Anselmo at once begged to be allowed to retire to rest, and to be given writing materials. His wish was complied with and he was left lying down and alone, for he desired this, and even that the door should be locked. Finding himself alone he so took to heart the thought of his misfortune that by the signs of death he felt within him he knew well his life was drawing to a close, and therefore he resolved to leave behind him a declaration of the cause of his strange end. He began to write, but before he had put down all he meant to say, his breath failed him and he yielded up his life, a victim to the suffering which his ill-advised curiosity had entailed upon him. The master of the house observing that it was now late and that Anselmo did not call, determined to go in and ascertain if his indisposition was increasing, and found him lying on his face, his body partly in the bed, partly on the writing-table, on which he lay with the written paper open and the pen still in his hand. Having first called to him without receiving any answer, his host approached him, and taking him by the hand, found that it was cold, and saw that he was dead. Greatly surprised and distressed he summoned the household to witness the sad fate which had befallen Anselmo; and then he read the paper, the handwriting of which he recognised as his, and which contained these words:\n\n“A foolish and ill-advised desire has robbed me of life. If the news of my death should reach the ears of Camilla, let her know that I forgive her, for she was not bound to perform miracles, nor ought I to have required her to perform them; and since I have been the author of my own dishonour, there is no reason why-”\n\nSo far Anselmo had written, and thus it was plain that at this point, before he could finish what he had to say, his life came to an end. The next day his friend sent intelligence of his death to his relatives, who had already ascertained his misfortune, as well as the convent where Camilla lay almost on the point of accompanying her husband on that inevitable journey, not on account of the tidings of his death, but because of those she received of her lover’s departure. Although she saw herself a widow, it is said she refused either to quit the convent or take the veil, until, not long afterwards, intelligence reached her that Lothario had been killed in a battle in which M. de Lautrec had been recently engaged with the Great Captain Gonzalo Fernandez de Cordova in the kingdom of Naples, whither her too late repentant lover had repaired. On learning this Camilla took the veil, and shortly afterwards died, worn out by grief and melancholy. This was the end of all three, an end that came of a thoughtless beginning.\n\n“I like this novel,” said the curate; “but I cannot persuade myself of its truth; and if it has been invented, the author’s invention is faulty, for it is impossible to imagine any husband so foolish as to try such a costly experiment as Anselmo’s. If it had been represented as occurring between a gallant and his mistress it might pass; but between husband and wife there is something of an impossibility about it. As to the way in which the story is told, however, I have no fault to find.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVI.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF MORE CURIOUS INCIDENTS THAT OCCURRED AT THE INN\n\n# WHICH TREATS OF MORE CURIOUS INCIDENTS THAT OCCURRED AT THE INN\n\nJust at that instant the landlord, who was standing at the gate of the inn, exclaimed, “Here comes a fine troop of guests; if they stop here we may say gaudeamus.”\n\n“What are they?” said Cardenio.\n\n“Four men,” said the landlord, “riding a la jineta, with lances and bucklers, and all with black veils, and with them there is a woman in white on a side-saddle, whose face is also veiled, and two attendants on foot.”\n\n“Are they very near?” said the curate.\n\n“So near,” answered the landlord, “that here they come.”\n\nHearing this Dorothea covered her face, and Cardenio retreated into Don Quixote’s room, and they hardly had time to do so before the whole party the host had described entered the inn, and the four that were on horseback, who were of highbred appearance and bearing, dismounted, and came forward to take down the woman who rode on the side-saddle, and one of them taking her in his arms placed her in a chair that stood at the entrance of the room where Cardenio had hidden himself. All this time neither she nor they had removed their veils or spoken a word, only on sitting down on the chair the woman gave a deep sigh and let her arms fall like one that was ill and weak. The attendants on foot then led the horses away to the stable. Observing this the curate, curious to know who these people in such a dress and preserving such silence were, went to where the servants were standing and put the question to one of them, who answered him.\n\n“Faith, sir, I cannot tell you who they are, I only know they seem to be people of distinction, particularly he who advanced to take the lady you saw in his arms; and I say so because all the rest show him respect, and nothing is done except what he directs and orders.”\n\n“And the lady, who is she?” asked the curate.\n\n“That I cannot tell you either,” said the servant, “for I have not seen her face all the way: I have indeed heard her sigh many times and utter such groans that she seems to be giving up the ghost every time; but it is no wonder if we do not know more than we have told you, as my comrade and I have only been in their company two days, for having met us on the road they begged and persuaded us to accompany them to Andalusia, promising to pay us well.”\n\n“And have you heard any of them called by his name?” asked the curate.\n\n“No, indeed,” replied the servant; “they all preserve a marvellous silence on the road, for not a sound is to be heard among them except the poor lady’s sighs and sobs, which make us pity her; and we feel sure that wherever it is she is going, it is against her will, and as far as one can judge from her dress she is a nun or, what is more likely, about to become one; and perhaps it is because taking the vows is not of her own free will, that she is so unhappy as she seems to be.”\n\n“That may well be,” said the curate, and leaving them he returned to where Dorothea was, who, hearing the veiled lady sigh, moved by natural compassion drew near to her and said, “What are you suffering from, señora? If it be anything that women are accustomed and know how to relieve, I offer you my services with all my heart.”\n\nTo this the unhappy lady made no reply; and though Dorothea repeated her offers more earnestly she still kept silence, until the gentleman with the veil, who, the servant said, was obeyed by the rest, approached and said to Dorothea, “Do not give yourself the trouble, señora, of making any offers to that woman, for it is her way to give no thanks for anything that is done for her; and do not try to make her answer unless you want to hear some lie from her lips.”\n\n“I have never told a lie,” was the immediate reply of her who had been silent until now; “on the contrary, it is because I am so truthful and so ignorant of lying devices that I am now in this miserable condition; and this I call you yourself to witness, for it is my unstained truth that has made you false and a liar.”\n\nCardenio heard these words clearly and distinctly, being quite close to the speaker, for there was only the door of Don Quixote’s room between them, and the instant he did so, uttering a loud exclamation he cried, “Good God! what is this I hear? What voice is this that has reached my ears?” Startled at the voice the lady turned her head; and not seeing the speaker she stood up and attempted to enter the room; observing which the gentleman held her back, preventing her from moving a step. In her agitation and sudden movement the silk with which she had covered her face fell off and disclosed a countenance of incomparable and marvellous beauty, but pale and terrified; for she kept turning her eyes, everywhere she could direct her gaze, with an eagerness that made her look as if she had lost her senses, and so marked that it excited the pity of Dorothea and all who beheld her, though they knew not what caused it. The gentleman grasped her firmly by the shoulders, and being so fully occupied with holding her back, he was unable to put a hand to his veil which was falling off, as it did at length entirely, and Dorothea, who was holding the lady in her arms, raising her eyes saw that he who likewise held her was her husband, Don Fernando. The instant she recognised him, with a prolonged plaintive cry drawn from the depths of her heart, she fell backwards fainting, and but for the barber being close by to catch her in his arms, she would have fallen completely to the ground. The curate at once hastened to uncover her face and throw water on it, and as he did so Don Fernando, for he it was who held the other in his arms, recognised her and stood as if death-stricken by the sight; not, however, relaxing his grasp of Luscinda, for it was she that was struggling to release herself from his hold, having recognised Cardenio by his voice, as he had recognised her. Cardenio also heard Dorothea’s cry as she fell fainting, and imagining that it came from his Luscinda burst forth in terror from the room, and the first thing he saw was Don Fernando with Luscinda in his arms. Don Fernando, too, knew Cardenio at once; and all three, Luscinda, Cardenio, and Dorothea, stood in silent amazement scarcely knowing what had happened to them.\n\nThey gazed at one another without speaking, Dorothea at Don Fernando, Don Fernando at Cardenio, Cardenio at Luscinda, and Luscinda at Cardenio. The first to break silence was Luscinda, who thus addressed Don Fernando: “Leave me, Señor Don Fernando, for the sake of what you owe to yourself; if no other reason will induce you, leave me to cling to the wall of which I am the ivy, to the support from which neither your importunities, nor your threats, nor your promises, nor your gifts have been able to detach me. See how Heaven, by ways strange and hidden from our sight, has brought me face to face with my true husband; and well you know by dear-bought experience that death alone will be able to efface him from my memory. May this plain declaration, then, lead you, as you can do nothing else, to turn your love into rage, your affection into resentment, and so to take my life; for if I yield it up in the presence of my beloved husband I count it well bestowed; it may be by my death he will be convinced that I kept my faith to him to the last moment of life.”\n\nMeanwhile Dorothea had come to herself, and had heard Luscinda’s words, by means of which she divined who she was; but seeing that Don Fernando did not yet release her or reply to her, summoning up her resolution as well as she could she rose and knelt at his feet, and with a flood of bright and touching tears addressed him thus:\n\n“If, my lord, the beams of that sun that thou holdest eclipsed in thine arms did not dazzle and rob thine eyes of sight thou wouldst have seen by this time that she who kneels at thy feet is, so long as thou wilt have it so, the unhappy and unfortunate Dorothea. I am that lowly peasant girl whom thou in thy goodness or for thy pleasure wouldst raise high enough to call herself thine; I am she who in the seclusion of innocence led a contented life until at the voice of thy importunity, and thy true and tender passion, as it seemed, she opened the gates of her modesty and surrendered to thee the keys of her liberty; a gift received by thee but thanklessly, as is clearly shown by my forced retreat to the place where thou dost find me, and by thy appearance under the circumstances in which I see thee. Nevertheless, I would not have thee suppose that I have come here driven by my shame; it is only grief and sorrow at seeing myself forgotten by thee that have led me. It was thy will to make me thine, and thou didst so follow thy will, that now, even though thou repentest, thou canst not help being mine. Bethink thee, my lord, the unsurpassable affection I bear thee may compensate for the beauty and noble birth for which thou wouldst desert me. Thou canst not be the fair Luscinda’s because thou art mine, nor can she be thine because she is Cardenio’s; and it will be easier, remember, to bend thy will to love one who adores thee, than to lead one to love thee who abhors thee now. Thou didst address thyself to my simplicity, thou didst lay siege to my virtue, thou wert not ignorant of my station, well dost thou know how I yielded wholly to thy will; there is no ground or reason for thee to plead deception, and if it be so, as it is, and if thou art a Christian as thou art a gentleman, why dost thou by such subterfuges put off making me as happy at last as thou didst at first? And if thou wilt not have me for what I am, thy true and lawful wife, at least take and accept me as thy slave, for so long as I am thine I will count myself happy and fortunate. Do not by deserting me let my shame become the talk of the gossips in the streets; make not the old age of my parents miserable; for the loyal services they as faithful vassals have ever rendered thine are not deserving of such a return; and if thou thinkest it will debase thy blood to mingle it with mine, reflect that there is little or no nobility in the world that has not travelled the same road, and that in illustrious lineages it is not the woman’s blood that is of account; and, moreover, that true nobility consists in virtue, and if thou art wanting in that, refusing me what in justice thou owest me, then even I have higher claims to nobility than thine. To make an end, señor, these are my last words to thee: whether thou wilt, or wilt not, I am thy wife; witness thy words, which must not and ought not to be false, if thou dost pride thyself on that for want of which thou scornest me; witness the pledge which thou didst give me, and witness Heaven, which thou thyself didst call to witness the promise thou hadst made me; and if all this fail, thy own conscience will not fail to lift up its silent voice in the midst of all thy gaiety, and vindicate the truth of what I say and mar thy highest pleasure and enjoyment.”\n\nAll this and more the injured Dorothea delivered with such earnest feeling and such tears that all present, even those who came with Don Fernando, were constrained to join her in them. Don Fernando listened to her without replying, until, ceasing to speak, she gave way to such sobs and sighs that it must have been a heart of brass that was not softened by the sight of so great sorrow. Luscinda stood regarding her with no less compassion for her sufferings than admiration for her intelligence and beauty, and would have gone to her to say some words of comfort to her, but was prevented by Don Fernando’s grasp which held her fast. He, overwhelmed with confusion and astonishment, after regarding Dorothea for some moments with a fixed gaze, opened his arms, and, releasing Luscinda, exclaimed:\n\n“Thou hast conquered, fair Dorothea, thou hast conquered, for it is impossible to have the heart to deny the united force of so many truths.”\n\nLuscinda in her feebleness was on the point of falling to the ground when Don Fernando released her, but Cardenio, who stood near, having retreated behind Don Fernando to escape recognition, casting fear aside and regardless of what might happen, ran forward to support her, and said as he clasped her in his arms, “If Heaven in its compassion is willing to let thee rest at last, mistress of my heart, true, constant, and fair, nowhere canst thou rest more safely than in these arms that now receive thee, and received thee before when fortune permitted me to call thee mine.”\n\nAt these words Luscinda looked up at Cardenio, at first beginning to recognise him by his voice and then satisfying herself by her eyes that it was he, and hardly knowing what she did, and heedless of all considerations of decorum, she flung her arms around his neck and pressing her face close to his, said, “Yes, my dear lord, you are the true master of this your slave, even though adverse fate interpose again, and fresh dangers threaten this life that hangs on yours.”\n\nA strange sight was this for Don Fernando and those that stood around, filled with surprise at an incident so unlooked for. Dorothea fancied that Don Fernando changed colour and looked as though he meant to take vengeance on Cardenio, for she observed him put his hand to his sword; and the instant the idea struck her, with wonderful quickness she clasped him round the knees, and kissing them and holding him so as to prevent his moving, she said, while her tears continued to flow, “What is it thou wouldst do, my only refuge, in this unforeseen event? Thou hast thy wife at thy feet, and she whom thou wouldst have for thy wife is in the arms of her husband: reflect whether it will be right for thee, whether it will be possible for thee to undo what Heaven has done, or whether it will be becoming in thee to seek to raise her to be thy mate who in spite of every obstacle, and strong in her truth and constancy, is before thine eyes, bathing with the tears of love the face and bosom of her lawful husband. For God’s sake I entreat of thee, for thine own I implore thee, let not this open manifestation rouse thy anger; but rather so calm it as to allow these two lovers to live in peace and quiet without any interference from thee so long as Heaven permits them; and in so doing thou wilt prove the generosity of thy lofty noble spirit, and the world shall see that with thee reason has more influence than passion.”\n\nAll the time Dorothea was speaking, Cardenio, though he held Luscinda in his arms, never took his eyes off Don Fernando, determined, if he saw him make any hostile movement, to try and defend himself and resist as best he could all who might assail him, though it should cost him his life. But now Don Fernando’s friends, as well as the curate and the barber, who had been present all the while, not forgetting the worthy Sancho Panza, ran forward and gathered round Don Fernando, entreating him to have regard for the tears of Dorothea, and not suffer her reasonable hopes to be disappointed, since, as they firmly believed, what she said was but the truth; and bidding him observe that it was not, as it might seem, by accident, but by a special disposition of Providence that they had all met in a place where no one could have expected a meeting. And the curate bade him remember that only death could part Luscinda from Cardenio; that even if some sword were to separate them they would think their death most happy; and that in a case that admitted of no remedy his wisest course was, by conquering and putting a constraint upon himself, to show a generous mind, and of his own accord suffer these two to enjoy the happiness Heaven had granted them. He bade him, too, turn his eyes upon the beauty of Dorothea and he would see that few if any could equal much less excel her; while to that beauty should be added her modesty and the surpassing love she bore him. But besides all this, he reminded him that if he prided himself on being a gentleman and a Christian, he could not do otherwise than keep his plighted word; and that in doing so he would obey God and meet the approval of all sensible people, who know and recognised it to be the privilege of beauty, even in one of humble birth, provided virtue accompany it, to be able to raise itself to the level of any rank, without any slur upon him who places it upon an equality with himself; and furthermore that when the potent sway of passion asserts itself, so long as there be no mixture of sin in it, he is not to be blamed who gives way to it.\n\nTo be brief, they added to these such other forcible arguments that Don Fernando’s manly heart, being after all nourished by noble blood, was touched, and yielded to the truth which, even had he wished it, he could not gainsay; and he showed his submission, and acceptance of the good advice that had been offered to him, by stooping down and embracing Dorothea, saying to her, “Rise, dear lady, it is not right that what I hold in my heart should be kneeling at my feet; and if until now I have shown no sign of what I own, it may have been by Heaven’s decree in order that, seeing the constancy with which you love me, I may learn to value you as you deserve. What I entreat of you is that you reproach me not with my transgression and grievous wrong-doing; for the same cause and force that drove me to make you mine impelled me to struggle against being yours; and to prove this, turn and look at the eyes of the now happy Luscinda, and you will see in them an excuse for all my errors: and as she has found and gained the object of her desires, and I have found in you what satisfies all my wishes, may she live in peace and contentment as many happy years with her Cardenio, as on my knees I pray Heaven to allow me to live with my Dorothea;” and with these words he once more embraced her and pressed his face to hers with so much tenderness that he had to take great heed to keep his tears from completing the proof of his love and repentance in the sight of all. Not so Luscinda, and Cardenio, and almost all the others, for they shed so many tears, some in their own happiness, some at that of the others, that one would have supposed a heavy calamity had fallen upon them all. Even Sancho Panza was weeping; though afterwards he said he only wept because he saw that Dorothea was not as he fancied the queen Micomicona, of whom he expected such great favours. Their wonder as well as their weeping lasted some time, and then Cardenio and Luscinda went and fell on their knees before Don Fernando, returning him thanks for the favour he had rendered them in language so grateful that he knew not how to answer them, and raising them up embraced them with every mark of affection and courtesy.\n\nHe then asked Dorothea how she had managed to reach a place so far removed from her own home, and she in a few fitting words told all that she had previously related to Cardenio, with which Don Fernando and his companions were so delighted that they wished the story had been longer; so charmingly did Dorothea describe her misadventures. When she had finished Don Fernando recounted what had befallen him in the city after he had found in Luscinda’s bosom the paper in which she declared that she was Cardenio’s wife, and never could be his. He said he meant to kill her, and would have done so had he not been prevented by her parents, and that he quitted the house full of rage and shame, and resolved to avenge himself when a more convenient opportunity should offer. The next day he learned that Luscinda had disappeared from her father’s house, and that no one could tell whither she had gone. Finally, at the end of some months he ascertained that she was in a convent and meant to remain there all the rest of her life, if she were not to share it with Cardenio; and as soon as he had learned this, taking these three gentlemen as his companions, he arrived at the place where she was, but avoided speaking to her, fearing that if it were known he was there stricter precautions would be taken in the convent; and watching a time when the porter’s lodge was open he left two to guard the gate, and he and the other entered the convent in quest of Luscinda, whom they found in the cloisters in conversation with one of the nuns, and carrying her off without giving her time to resist, they reached a place with her where they provided themselves with what they required for taking her away; all which they were able to do in complete safety, as the convent was in the country at a considerable distance from the city. He added that when Luscinda found herself in his power she lost all consciousness, and after returning to herself did nothing but weep and sigh without speaking a word; and thus in silence and tears they reached that inn, which for him was reaching heaven where all the mischances of earth are over and at an end.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII.\n\nIN WHICH IS CONTINUED THE STORY OF THE FAMOUS PRINCESS MICOMICONA, WITH OTHER DROLL ADVENTURES\nTo all this Sancho listened with no little sorrow at heart to see how his hopes of dignity were fading away and vanishing in smoke, and how the fair Princess Micomicona had turned into Dorothea, and the giant into Don Fernando, while his master was sleeping tranquilly, totally unconscious of all that had come to pass. Dorothea was unable to persuade herself that her present happiness was not all a dream; Cardenio was in a similar state of mind, and Luscinda’s thoughts ran in the same direction. Don Fernando gave thanks to Heaven for the favour shown to him and for having been rescued from the intricate labyrinth in which he had been brought so near the destruction of his good name and of his soul; and in short everybody in the inn was full of contentment and satisfaction at the happy issue of such a complicated and hopeless business. The curate as a sensible man made sound reflections upon the whole affair, and congratulated each upon his good fortune; but the one that was in the highest spirits and good humour was the landlady, because of the promise Cardenio and the curate had given her to pay for all the losses and damage she had sustained through Don Quixote’s means. Sancho, as has been already said, was the only one who was distressed, unhappy, and dejected; and so with a long face he went in to his master, who had just awoke, and said to him:\n\n“Sir Rueful Countenance, your worship may as well sleep on as much as you like, without troubling yourself about killing any giant or restoring her kingdom to the princess; for that is all over and settled now.”\n\n“I should think it was,” replied Don Quixote, “for I have had the most prodigious and stupendous battle with the giant that I ever remember having had all the days of my life; and with one back-stroke-swish!--I brought his head tumbling to the ground, and so much blood gushed forth from him that it ran in rivulets over the earth like water.”\n\n“Like red wine, your worship had better say,” replied Sancho; “for I would have you know, if you don’t know it, that the dead giant is a hacked wine-skin, and the blood four-and-twenty gallons of red wine that it had in its belly, and the cut-off head is the bitch that bore me; and the devil take it all.”\n\n“What art thou talking about, fool?” said Don Quixote; “art thou in thy senses?”\n\n“Let your worship get up,” said Sancho, “and you will see the nice business you have made of it, and what we have to pay; and you will see the queen turned into a private lady called Dorothea, and other things that will astonish you, if you understand them.”\n\n“I shall not be surprised at anything of the kind,” returned Don Quixote; “for if thou dost remember the last time we were here I told thee that everything that happened here was a matter of enchantment, and it would be no wonder if it were the same now.”\n\n“I could believe all that,” replied Sancho, “if my blanketing was the same sort of thing also; only it wasn’t, but real and genuine; for I saw the landlord, who is here to-day, holding one end of the blanket and jerking me up to the skies very neatly and smartly, and with as much laughter as strength; and when it comes to be a case of knowing people, I hold for my part, simple and sinner as I am, that there is no enchantment about it at all, but a great deal of bruising and bad luck.”\n\n“Well, well, God will give a remedy,” said Don Quixote; “hand me my clothes and let me go out, for I want to see these transformations and things thou speakest of.”\n\nSancho fetched him his clothes; and while he was dressing, the curate gave Don Fernando and the others present an account of Don Quixote’s madness and of the stratagem they had made use of to withdraw him from that Pena Pobre where he fancied himself stationed because of his lady’s scorn. He described to them also nearly all the adventures that Sancho had mentioned, at which they marvelled and laughed not a little, thinking it, as all did, the strangest form of madness a crazy intellect could be capable of. But now, the curate said, that the lady Dorothea’s good fortune prevented her from proceeding with their purpose, it would be necessary to devise or discover some other way of getting him home.\n\nCardenio proposed to carry out the scheme they had begun, and suggested that Luscinda would act and support Dorothea’s part sufficiently well.\n\n“No,” said Don Fernando, “that must not be, for I want Dorothea to follow out this idea of hers; and if the worthy gentleman’s village is not very far off, I shall be happy if I can do anything for his relief.”\n\n“It is not more than two days’ journey from this,” said the curate.\n\n“Even if it were more,” said Don Fernando, “I would gladly travel so far for the sake of doing so good a work.”\n\nAt this moment Don Quixote came out in full panoply, with Mambrino’s helmet, all dinted as it was, on his head, his buckler on his arm, and leaning on his staff or pike. The strange figure he presented filled Don Fernando and the rest with amazement as they contemplated his lean yellow face half a league long, his armour of all sorts, and the solemnity of his deportment. They stood silent waiting to see what he would say, and he, fixing his eyes on the fair Dorothea, addressed her with great gravity and composure:\n\n“I am informed, fair lady, by my squire here that your greatness has been annihilated and your being abolished, since, from a queen and lady of high degree as you used to be, you have been turned into a private maiden. If this has been done by the command of the magician king your father, through fear that I should not afford you the aid you need and are entitled to, I may tell you he did not know and does not know half the mass, and was little versed in the annals of chivalry; for, if he had read and gone through them as attentively and deliberately as I have, he would have found at every turn that knights of less renown than mine have accomplished things more difficult: it is no great matter to kill a whelp of a giant, however arrogant he may be; for it is not many hours since I myself was engaged with one, and-I will not speak of it, that they may not say I am lying; time, however, that reveals all, will tell the tale when we least expect it.”\n\n“You were engaged with a couple of wine-skins, and not a giant,” said the landlord at this; but Don Fernando told him to hold his tongue and on no account interrupt Don Quixote, who continued, “I say in conclusion, high and disinherited lady, that if your father has brought about this metamorphosis in your person for the reason I have mentioned, you ought not to attach any importance to it; for there is no peril on earth through which my sword will not force a way, and with it, before many days are over, I will bring your enemy’s head to the ground and place on yours the crown of your kingdom.”\n\nDon Quixote said no more, and waited for the reply of the princess, who aware of Don Fernando’s determination to carry on the deception until Don Quixote had been conveyed to his home, with great ease of manner and gravity made answer, “Whoever told you, valiant Knight of the Rueful Countenance, that I had undergone any change or transformation did not tell you the truth, for I am the same as I was yesterday. It is true that certain strokes of good fortune, that have given me more than I could have hoped for, have made some alteration in me; but I have not therefore ceased to be what I was before, or to entertain the same desire I have had all through of availing myself of the might of your valiant and invincible arm. And so, señor, let your goodness reinstate the father that begot me in your good opinion, and be assured that he was a wise and prudent man, since by his craft he found out such a sure and easy way of remedying my misfortune; for I believe, señor, that had it not been for you I should never have lit upon the good fortune I now possess; and in this I am saying what is perfectly true; as most of these gentlemen who are present can fully testify. All that remains is to set out on our journey to-morrow, for to-day we could not make much way; and for the rest of the happy result I am looking forward to, I trust to God and the valour of your heart.”\n\nSo said the sprightly Dorothea, and on hearing her Don Quixote turned to Sancho, and said to him, with an angry air, “I declare now, little Sancho, thou art the greatest little villain in Spain. Say, thief and vagabond, hast thou not just now told me that this princess had been turned into a maiden called Dorothea, and that the head which I am persuaded I cut off from a giant was the bitch that bore thee, and other nonsense that put me in the greatest perplexity I have ever been in all my life? I vow” (and here he looked to heaven and ground his teeth) “I have a mind to play the mischief with thee, in a way that will teach sense for the future to all lying squires of knights-errant in the world.”\n\n“Let your worship be calm, señor,” returned Sancho, “for it may well be that I have been mistaken as to the change of the lady princess Micomicona; but as to the giant’s head, or at least as to the piercing of the wine-skins, and the blood being red wine, I make no mistake, as sure as there is a God; because the wounded skins are there at the head of your worship’s bed, and the wine has made a lake of the room; if not you will see when the eggs come to be fried; I mean when his worship the landlord calls for all the damages: for the rest, I am heartily glad that her ladyship the queen is as she was, for it concerns me as much as anyone.”\n\n“I tell thee again, Sancho, thou art a fool,” said Don Quixote; “forgive me, and that will do.”\n\n“That will do,” said Don Fernando; “let us say no more about it; and as her ladyship the princess proposes to set out to-morrow because it is too late to-day, so be it, and we will pass the night in pleasant conversation, and to-morrow we will all accompany Señor Don Quixote; for we wish to witness the valiant and unparalleled achievements he is about to perform in the course of this mighty enterprise which he has undertaken.”\n\n“It is I who shall wait upon and accompany you,” said Don Quixote; “and I am much gratified by the favour that is bestowed upon me, and the good opinion entertained of me, which I shall strive to justify or it shall cost me my life, or even more, if it can possibly cost me more.”\n\nMany were the compliments and expressions of politeness that passed between Don Quixote and Don Fernando; but they were brought to an end by a traveller who at this moment entered the inn, and who seemed from his attire to be a Christian lately come from the country of the Moors, for he was dressed in a short-skirted coat of blue cloth with half-sleeves and without a collar; his breeches were also of blue cloth, and his cap of the same colour, and he wore yellow buskins and had a Moorish cutlass slung from a baldric across his breast. Behind him, mounted upon an ass, there came a woman dressed in Moorish fashion, with her face veiled and a scarf on her head, and wearing a little brocaded cap, and a mantle that covered her from her shoulders to her feet. The man was of a robust and well-proportioned frame, in age a little over forty, rather swarthy in complexion, with long moustaches and a full beard, and, in short, his appearance was such that if he had been well dressed he would have been taken for a person of quality and good birth. On entering he asked for a room, and when they told him there was none in the inn he seemed distressed, and approaching her who by her dress seemed to be a Moor he her down from saddle in his arms. Luscinda, Dorothea, the landlady, her daughter and Maritornes, attracted by the strange, and to them entirely new costume, gathered round her; and Dorothea, who was always kindly, courteous, and quick-witted, perceiving that both she and the man who had brought her were annoyed at not finding a room, said to her, “Do not be put out, señora, by the discomfort and want of luxuries here, for it is the way of road-side inns to be without them; still, if you will be pleased to share our lodging with us (pointing to Luscinda) perhaps you will have found worse accommodation in the course of your journey.”\n\nTo this the veiled lady made no reply; all she did was to rise from her seat, crossing her hands upon her bosom, bowing her head and bending her body as a sign that she returned thanks. From her silence they concluded that she must be a Moor and unable to speak a Christian tongue.\n\nAt this moment the captive came up, having been until now otherwise engaged, and seeing that they all stood round his companion and that she made no reply to what they addressed to her, he said, “Ladies, this damsel hardly understands my language and can speak none but that of her own country, for which reason she does not and cannot answer what has been asked of her.”\n\n“Nothing has been asked of her,” returned Luscinda; “she has only been offered our company for this evening and a share of the quarters we occupy, where she shall be made as comfortable as the circumstances allow, with the good-will we are bound to show all strangers that stand in need of it, especially if it be a woman to whom the service is rendered.”\n\n“On her part and my own, señora,” replied the captive, “I kiss your hands, and I esteem highly, as I ought, the favour you have offered, which, on such an occasion and coming from persons of your appearance, is, it is plain to see, a very great one.”\n\n“Tell me, señor,” said Dorothea, “is this lady a Christian or a Moor? for her dress and her silence lead us to imagine that she is what we could wish she was not.”\n\n“In dress and outwardly,” said he, “she is a Moor, but at heart she is a thoroughly good Christian, for she has the greatest desire to become one.”\n\n“Then she has not been baptised?” returned Luscinda.\n\n“There has been no opportunity for that,” replied the captive, “since she left Algiers, her native country and home; and up to the present she has not found herself in any such imminent danger of death as to make it necessary to baptise her before she has been instructed in all the ceremonies our holy mother Church ordains; but, please God, ere long she shall be baptised with the solemnity befitting her which is higher than her dress or mine indicates.”\n\nBy these words he excited a desire in all who heard him, to know who the Moorish lady and the captive were, but no one liked to ask just then, seeing that it was a fitter moment for helping them to rest themselves than for questioning them about their lives. Dorothea took the Moorish lady by the hand and leading her to a seat beside herself, requested her to remove her veil. She looked at the captive as if to ask him what they meant and what she was to do. He said to her in Arabic that they asked her to take off her veil, and thereupon she removed it and disclosed a countenance so lovely, that to Dorothea she seemed more beautiful than Luscinda, and to Luscinda more beautiful than Dorothea, and all the bystanders felt that if any beauty could compare with theirs it was the Moorish lady’s, and there were even those who were inclined to give it somewhat the preference. And as it is the privilege and charm of beauty to win the heart and secure good-will, all forthwith became eager to show kindness and attention to the lovely Moor.\n\nDon Fernando asked the captive what her name was, and he replied that it was Lela Zoraida; but the instant she heard him, she guessed what the Christian had asked, and said hastily, with some displeasure and energy, “No, not Zoraida; Maria, Maria!” giving them to understand that she was called “Maria” and not “Zoraida.” These words, and the touching earnestness with which she uttered them, drew more than one tear from some of the listeners, particularly the women, who are by nature tender-hearted and compassionate. Luscinda embraced her affectionately, saying, “Yes, yes, Maria, Maria,” to which the Moor replied, “Yes, yes, Maria; Zoraida macange,” which means “not Zoraida.”\n\nNight was now approaching, and by the orders of those who accompanied Don Fernando the landlord had taken care and pains to prepare for them the best supper that was in his power. The hour therefore having arrived they all took their seats at a long table like a refectory one, for round or square table there was none in the inn, and the seat of honour at the head of it, though he was for refusing it, they assigned to Don Quixote, who desired the lady Micomicona to place herself by his side, as he was her protector. Luscinda and Zoraida took their places next her, opposite to them were Don Fernando and Cardenio, and next the captive and the other gentlemen, and by the side of the ladies, the curate and the barber. And so they supped in high enjoyment, which was increased when they observed Don Quixote leave off eating, and, moved by an impulse like that which made him deliver himself at such length when he supped with the goatherds, begin to address them:\n\n“Verily, gentlemen, if we reflect upon it, great and marvellous are the things they see, who make profession of the order of knight-errantry. Say, what being is there in this world, who entering the gate of this castle at this moment, and seeing us as we are here, would suppose or imagine us to be what we are? Who would say that this lady who is beside me was the great queen that we all know her to be, or that I am that Knight of the Rueful Countenance, trumpeted far and wide by the mouth of Fame? Now, there can be no doubt that this art and calling surpasses all those that mankind has invented, and is the more deserving of being held in honour in proportion as it is the more exposed to peril. Away with those who assert that letters have the preeminence over arms; I will tell them, whosoever they may be, that they know not what they say. For the reason which such persons commonly assign, and upon which they chiefly rest, is, that the labours of the mind are greater than those of the body, and that arms give employment to the body alone; as if the calling were a porter’s trade, for which nothing more is required than sturdy strength; or as if, in what we who profess them call arms, there were not included acts of vigour for the execution of which high intelligence is requisite; or as if the soul of the warrior, when he has an army, or the defence of a city under his care, did not exert itself as much by mind as by body. Nay; see whether by bodily strength it be possible to learn or divine the intentions of the enemy, his plans, stratagems, or obstacles, or to ward off impending mischief; for all these are the work of the mind, and in them the body has no share whatever. Since, therefore, arms have need of the mind, as much as letters, let us see now which of the two minds, that of the man of letters or that of the warrior, has most to do; and this will be seen by the end and goal that each seeks to attain; for that purpose is the more estimable which has for its aim the nobler object. The end and goal of letters--I am not speaking now of divine letters, the aim of which is to raise and direct the soul to Heaven; for with an end so infinite no other can be compared--I speak of human letters, the end of which is to establish distributive justice, give to every man that which is his, and see and take care that good laws are observed: an end undoubtedly noble, lofty, and deserving of high praise, but not such as should be given to that sought by arms, which have for their end and object peace, the greatest boon that men can desire in this life. The first good news the world and mankind received was that which the angels announced on the night that was our day, when they sang in the air, ‘Glory to God in the highest, and peace on earth to men of good-will;’ and the salutation which the great Master of heaven and earth taught his disciples and chosen followers when they entered any house, was to say, ‘Peace be on this house;’ and many other times he said to them, ‘My peace I give unto you, my peace I leave you, peace be with you;’ a jewel and a precious gift given and left by such a hand: a jewel without which there can be no happiness either on earth or in heaven. This peace is the true end of war; and war is only another name for arms. This, then, being admitted, that the end of war is peace, and that so far it has the advantage of the end of letters, let us turn to the bodily labours of the man of letters, and those of him who follows the profession of arms, and see which are the greater.”\n\nDon Quixote delivered his discourse in such a manner and in such correct language, that for the time being he made it impossible for any of his hearers to consider him a madman; on the contrary, as they were mostly gentlemen, to whom arms are an appurtenance by birth, they listened to him with great pleasure as he continued: “Here, then, I say is what the student has to undergo; first of all poverty: not that all are poor, but to put the case as strongly as possible: and when I have said that he endures poverty, I think nothing more need be said about his hard fortune, for he who is poor has no share of the good things of life. This poverty he suffers from in various ways, hunger, or cold, or nakedness, or all together; but for all that it is not so extreme but that he gets something to eat, though it may be at somewhat unseasonable hours and from the leavings of the rich; for the greatest misery of the student is what they themselves call ‘going out for soup,’ and there is always some neighbour’s brazier or hearth for them, which, if it does not warm, at least tempers the cold to them, and lastly, they sleep comfortably at night under a roof. I will not go into other particulars, as for example want of shirts, and no superabundance of shoes, thin and threadbare garments, and gorging themselves to surfeit in their voracity when good luck has treated them to a banquet of some sort. By this road that I have described, rough and hard, stumbling here, falling there, getting up again to fall again, they reach the rank they desire, and that once attained, we have seen many who have passed these Syrtes and Scyllas and Charybdises, as if borne flying on the wings of favouring fortune; we have seen them, I say, ruling and governing the world from a chair, their hunger turned into satiety, their cold into comfort, their nakedness into fine raiment, their sleep on a mat into repose in holland and damask, the justly earned reward of their virtue; but, contrasted and compared with what the warrior undergoes, all they have undergone falls far short of it, as I am now about to show.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVIII.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF THE CURIOUS DISCOURSE DON QUIXOTE DELIVERED ON ARMS AND LETTERS\nContinuing his discourse Don Quixote said: “As we began in the student’s case with poverty and its accompaniments, let us see now if the soldier is richer, and we shall find that in poverty itself there is no one poorer; for he is dependent on his miserable pay, which comes late or never, or else on what he can plunder, seriously imperilling his life and conscience; and sometimes his nakedness will be so great that a slashed doublet serves him for uniform and shirt, and in the depth of winter he has to defend himself against the inclemency of the weather in the open field with nothing better than the breath of his mouth, which I need not say, coming from an empty place, must come out cold, contrary to the laws of nature. To be sure he looks forward to the approach of night to make up for all these discomforts on the bed that awaits him, which, unless by some fault of his, never sins by being over narrow, for he can easily measure out on the ground as he likes, and roll himself about in it to his heart’s content without any fear of the sheets slipping away from him. Then, after all this, suppose the day and hour for taking his degree in his calling to have come; suppose the day of battle to have arrived, when they invest him with the doctor’s cap made of lint, to mend some bullet-hole, perhaps, that has gone through his temples, or left him with a crippled arm or leg. Or if this does not happen, and merciful Heaven watches over him and keeps him safe and sound, it may be he will be in the same poverty he was in before, and he must go through more engagements and more battles, and come victorious out of all before he betters himself; but miracles of that sort are seldom seen. For tell me, sirs, if you have ever reflected upon it, by how much do those who have gained by war fall short of the number of those who have perished in it? No doubt you will reply that there can be no comparison, that the dead cannot be numbered, while the living who have been rewarded may be summed up with three figures. All which is the reverse in the case of men of letters; for by skirts, to say nothing of sleeves, they all find means of support; so that though the soldier has more to endure, his reward is much less. But against all this it may be urged that it is easier to reward two thousand soldiers, for the former may be remunerated by giving them places, which must perforce be conferred upon men of their calling, while the latter can only be recompensed out of the very property of the master they serve; but this impossibility only strengthens my argument.\n\n“Putting this, however, aside, for it is a puzzling question for which it is difficult to find a solution, let us return to the superiority of arms over letters, a matter still undecided, so many are the arguments put forward on each side; for besides those I have mentioned, letters say that without them arms cannot maintain themselves, for war, too, has its laws and is governed by them, and laws belong to the domain of letters and men of letters. To this arms make answer that without them laws cannot be maintained, for by arms states are defended, kingdoms preserved, cities protected, roads made safe, seas cleared of pirates; and, in short, if it were not for them, states, kingdoms, monarchies, cities, ways by sea and land would be exposed to the violence and confusion which war brings with it, so long as it lasts and is free to make use of its privileges and powers. And then it is plain that whatever costs most is valued and deserves to be valued most. To attain to eminence in letters costs a man time, watching, hunger, nakedness, headaches, indigestions, and other things of the sort, some of which I have already referred to. But for a man to come in the ordinary course of things to be a good soldier costs him all the student suffers, and in an incomparably higher degree, for at every step he runs the risk of losing his life. For what dread of want or poverty that can reach or harass the student can compare with what the soldier feels, who finds himself beleaguered in some stronghold mounting guard in some ravelin or cavalier, knows that the enemy is pushing a mine towards the post where he is stationed, and cannot under any circumstances retire or fly from the imminent danger that threatens him? All he can do is to inform his captain of what is going on so that he may try to remedy it by a counter-mine, and then stand his ground in fear and expectation of the moment when he will fly up to the clouds without wings and descend into the deep against his will. And if this seems a trifling risk, let us see whether it is equalled or surpassed by the encounter of two galleys stem to stem, in the midst of the open sea, locked and entangled one with the other, when the soldier has no more standing room than two feet of the plank of the spur; and yet, though he sees before him threatening him as many ministers of death as there are cannon of the foe pointed at him, not a lance length from his body, and sees too that with the first heedless step he will go down to visit the profundities of Neptune’s bosom, still with dauntless heart, urged on by honour that nerves him, he makes himself a target for all that musketry, and struggles to cross that narrow path to the enemy’s ship. And what is still more marvellous, no sooner has one gone down into the depths he will never rise from till the end of the world, than another takes his place; and if he too falls into the sea that waits for him like an enemy, another and another will succeed him without a moment’s pause between their deaths: courage and daring the greatest that all the chances of war can show. Happy the blest ages that knew not the dread fury of those devilish engines of artillery, whose inventor I am persuaded is in hell receiving the reward of his diabolical invention, by which he made it easy for a base and cowardly arm to take the life of a gallant gentleman; and that, when he knows not how or whence, in the height of the ardour and enthusiasm that fire and animate brave hearts, there should come some random bullet, discharged perhaps by one who fled in terror at the flash when he fired off his accursed machine, which in an instant puts an end to the projects and cuts off the life of one who deserved to live for ages to come. And thus when I reflect on this, I am almost tempted to say that in my heart I repent of having adopted this profession of knight-errant in so detestable an age as we live in now; for though no peril can make me fear, still it gives me some uneasiness to think that powder and lead may rob me of the opportunity of making myself famous and renowned throughout the known earth by the might of my arm and the edge of my sword. But Heaven’s will be done; if I succeed in my attempt I shall be all the more honoured, as I have faced greater dangers than the knights-errant of yore exposed themselves to.”\n\nAll this lengthy discourse Don Quixote delivered while the others supped, forgetting to raise a morsel to his lips, though Sancho more than once told him to eat his supper, as he would have time enough afterwards to say all he wanted. It excited fresh pity in those who had heard him to see a man of apparently sound sense, and with rational views on every subject he discussed, so hopelessly wanting in all, when his wretched unlucky chivalry was in question. The curate told him he was quite right in all he had said in favour of arms, and that he himself, though a man of letters and a graduate, was of the same opinion.\n\nThey finished their supper, the cloth was removed, and while the hostess, her daughter, and Maritornes were getting Don Quixote of La Mancha’s garret ready, in which it was arranged that the women were to be quartered by themselves for the night, Don Fernando begged the captive to tell them the story of his life, for it could not fail to be strange and interesting, to judge by the hints he had let fall on his arrival in company with Zoraida. To this the captive replied that he would very willingly yield to his request, only he feared his tale would not give them as much pleasure as he wished; nevertheless, not to be wanting in compliance, he would tell it. The curate and the others thanked him and added their entreaties, and he finding himself so pressed said there was no occasion to ask, where a command had such weight, and added, “If your worships will give me your attention you will hear a true story which, perhaps, fictitious ones constructed with ingenious and studied art cannot come up to.” These words made them settle themselves in their places and preserve a deep silence, and he seeing them waiting on his words in mute expectation, began thus in a pleasant quiet voice.\n\n# WHEREIN THE CAPTIVE RELATES HIS LIFE AND ADVENTURES\n\nMy family had its origin in a village in the mountains of Leon, and nature had been kinder and more generous to it than fortune; though in the general poverty of those communities my father passed for being even a rich man; and he would have been so in reality had he been as clever in preserving his property as he was in spending it. This tendency of his to be liberal and profuse he had acquired from having been a soldier in his youth, for the soldier’s life is a school in which the niggard becomes free-handed and the free-handed prodigal; and if any soldiers are to be found who are misers, they are monsters of rare occurrence. My father went beyond liberality and bordered on prodigality, a disposition by no means advantageous to a married man who has children to succeed to his name and position. My father had three, all sons, and all of sufficient age to make choice of a profession. Finding, then, that he was unable to resist his propensity, he resolved to divest himself of the instrument and cause of his prodigality and lavishness, to divest himself of wealth, without which Alexander himself would have seemed parsimonious; and so calling us all three aside one day into a room, he addressed us in words somewhat to the following effect:\n\n“My sons, to assure you that I love you, no more need be known or said than that you are my sons; and to encourage a suspicion that I do not love you, no more is needed than the knowledge that I have no self-control as far as preservation of your patrimony is concerned; therefore, that you may for the future feel sure that I love you like a father, and have no wish to ruin you like a stepfather, I propose to do with you what I have for some time back meditated, and after mature deliberation decided upon. You are now of an age to choose your line of life or at least make choice of a calling that will bring you honour and profit when you are older; and what I have resolved to do is to divide my property into four parts; three I will give to you, to each his portion without making any difference, and the other I will retain to live upon and support myself for whatever remainder of life Heaven may be pleased to grant me. But I wish each of you on taking possession of the share that falls to him to follow one of the paths I shall indicate. In this Spain of ours there is a proverb, to my mind very true--as they all are, being short aphorisms drawn from long practical experience--and the one I refer to says, ‘The church, or the sea, or the king’s house;’ as much as to say, in plainer language, whoever wants to flourish and become rich, let him follow the church, or go to sea, adopting commerce as his calling, or go into the king’s service in his household, for they say, ‘Better a king’s crumb than a lord’s favour.’ I say so because it is my will and pleasure that one of you should follow letters, another trade, and the third serve the king in the wars, for it is a difficult matter to gain admission to his service in his household, and if war does not bring much wealth it confers great distinction and fame. Eight days hence I will give you your full shares in money, without defrauding you of a farthing, as you will see in the end. Now tell me if you are willing to follow out my idea and advice as I have laid it before you.”\n\nHaving called upon me as the eldest to answer, I, after urging him not to strip himself of his property but to spend it all as he pleased, for we were young men able to gain our living, consented to comply with his wishes, and said that mine were to follow the profession of arms and thereby serve God and my king. My second brother having made the same proposal, decided upon going to the Indies, embarking the portion that fell to him in trade. The youngest, and in my opinion the wisest, said he would rather follow the church, or go to complete his studies at Salamanca. As soon as we had come to an understanding, and made choice of our professions, my father embraced us all, and in the short time he mentioned carried into effect all he had promised; and when he had given to each his share, which as well as I remember was three thousand ducats apiece in cash (for an uncle of ours bought the estate and paid for it down, not to let it go out of the family), we all three on the same day took leave of our good father; and at the same time, as it seemed to me inhuman to leave my father with such scanty means in his old age, I induced him to take two of my three thousand ducats, as the remainder would be enough to provide me with all a soldier needed. My two brothers, moved by my example, gave him each a thousand ducats, so that there was left for my father four thousand ducats in money, besides three thousand, the value of the portion that fell to him which he preferred to retain in land instead of selling it. Finally, as I said, we took leave of him, and of our uncle whom I have mentioned, not without sorrow and tears on both sides, they charging us to let them know whenever an opportunity offered how we fared, whether well or ill. We promised to do so, and when he had embraced us and given us his blessing, one set out for Salamanca, the other for Seville, and I for Alicante, where I had heard there was a Genoese vessel taking in a cargo of wool for Genoa.\n\nIt is now some twenty-two years since I left my father’s house, and all that time, though I have written several letters, I have had no news whatever of him or of my brothers; my own adventures during that period I will now relate briefly. I embarked at Alicante, reached Genoa after a prosperous voyage, and proceeded thence to Milan, where I provided myself with arms and a few soldier’s accoutrements; thence it was my intention to go and take service in Piedmont, but as I was already on the road to Alessandria della Paglia, I learned that the great Duke of Alva was on his way to Flanders. I changed my plans, joined him, served under him in the campaigns he made, was present at the deaths of the Counts Egmont and Horn, and was promoted to be ensign under a famous captain of Guadalajara, Diego de Urbina by name. Some time after my arrival in Flanders news came of the league that his Holiness Pope Pius V of happy memory, had made with Venice and Spain against the common enemy, the Turk, who had just then with his fleet taken the famous island of Cyprus, which belonged to the Venetians, a loss deplorable and disastrous. It was known as a fact that the Most Serene Don John of Austria, natural brother of our good king Don Philip, was coming as commander-in-chief of the allied forces, and rumours were abroad of the vast warlike preparations which were being made, all which stirred my heart and filled me with a longing to take part in the campaign which was expected; and though I had reason to believe, and almost certain promises, that on the first opportunity that presented itself I should be promoted to be captain, I preferred to leave all and betake myself, as I did, to Italy; and it was my good fortune that Don John had just arrived at Genoa, and was going on to Naples to join the Venetian fleet, as he afterwards did at Messina. I may say, in short, that I took part in that glorious expedition, promoted by this time to be a captain of infantry, to which honourable charge my good luck rather than my merits raised me; and that day--so fortunate for Christendom, because then all the nations of the earth were disabused of the error under which they lay in imagining the Turks to be invincible on sea--on that day, I say, on which the Ottoman pride and arrogance were broken, among all that were there made happy (for the Christians who died that day were happier than those who remained alive and victorious) I alone was miserable; for, instead of some naval crown that I might have expected had it been in Roman times, on the night that followed that famous day I found myself with fetters on my feet and manacles on my hands.\n\nIt happened in this way: El Uchali, the king of Algiers, a daring and successful corsair, having attacked and taken the leading Maltese galley (only three knights being left alive in it, and they badly wounded), the chief galley of John Andrea, on board of which I and my company were placed, came to its relief, and doing as was bound to do in such a case, I leaped on board the enemy’s galley, which, sheering off from that which had attacked it, prevented my men from following me, and so I found myself alone in the midst of my enemies, who were in such numbers that I was unable to resist; in short I was taken, covered with wounds; El Uchali, as you know, sirs, made his escape with his entire squadron, and I was left a prisoner in his power, the only sad being among so many filled with joy, and the only captive among so many free; for there were fifteen thousand Christians, all at the oar in the Turkish fleet, that regained their longed-for liberty that day.\n\nThey carried me to Constantinople, where the Grand Turk, Selim, made my master general at sea for having done his duty in the battle and carried off as evidence of his bravery the standard of the Order of Malta. The following year, which was the year seventy-two, I found myself at Navarino rowing in the leading galley with the three lanterns. There I saw and observed how the opportunity of capturing the whole Turkish fleet in harbour was lost; for all the marines and janizzaries that belonged to it made sure that they were about to be attacked inside the very harbour, and had their kits and pasamaques, or shoes, ready to flee at once on shore without waiting to be assailed, in so great fear did they stand of our fleet. But Heaven ordered it otherwise, not for any fault or neglect of the general who commanded on our side, but for the sins of Christendom, and because it was God’s will and pleasure that we should always have instruments of punishment to chastise us. As it was, El Uchali took refuge at Modon, which is an island near Navarino, and landing forces fortified the mouth of the harbour and waited quietly until Don John retired. On this expedition was taken the galley called the Prize, whose captain was a son of the famous corsair Barbarossa. It was taken by the chief Neapolitan galley called the She-wolf, commanded by that thunderbolt of war, that father of his men, that successful and unconquered captain Don Alvaro de Bazan, Marquis of Santa Cruz; and I cannot help telling you what took place at the capture of the Prize.\n\nThe son of Barbarossa was so cruel, and treated his slaves so badly, that, when those who were at the oars saw that the She-wolf galley was bearing down upon them and gaining upon them, they all at once dropped their oars and seized their captain who stood on the stage at the end of the gangway shouting to them to row lustily; and passing him on from bench to bench, from the poop to the prow, they so bit him that before he had got much past the mast his soul had already got to hell; so great, as I said, was the cruelty with which he treated them, and the hatred with which they hated him.\n\nWe returned to Constantinople, and the following year, seventy-three, it became known that Don John had seized Tunis and taken the kingdom from the Turks, and placed Muley Hamet in possession, putting an end to the hopes which Muley Hamida, the cruelest and bravest Moor in the world, entertained of returning to reign there. The Grand Turk took the loss greatly to heart, and with the cunning which all his race possess, he made peace with the Venetians (who were much more eager for it than he was), and the following year, seventy-four, he attacked the Goletta and the fort which Don John had left half built near Tunis. While all these events were occurring, I was labouring at the oar without any hope of freedom; at least I had no hope of obtaining it by ransom, for I was firmly resolved not to write to my father telling him of my misfortunes. At length the Goletta fell, and the fort fell, before which places there were seventy-five thousand regular Turkish soldiers, and more than four hundred thousand Moors and Arabs from all parts of Africa, and in the train of all this great host such munitions and engines of war, and so many pioneers that with their hands they might have covered the Goletta and the fort with handfuls of earth. The first to fall was the Goletta, until then reckoned impregnable, and it fell, not by any fault of its defenders, who did all that they could and should have done, but because experiment proved how easily entrenchments could be made in the desert sand there; for water used to be found at two palms depth, while the Turks found none at two yards; and so by means of a quantity of sandbags they raised their works so high that they commanded the walls of the fort, sweeping them as if from a cavalier, so that no one was able to make a stand or maintain the defence.\n\nIt was a common opinion that our men should not have shut themselves up in the Goletta, but should have waited in the open at the landing-place; but those who say so talk at random and with little knowledge of such matters; for if in the Goletta and in the fort there were barely seven thousand soldiers, how could such a small number, however resolute, sally out and hold their own against numbers like those of the enemy? And how is it possible to help losing a stronghold that is not relieved, above all when surrounded by a host of determined enemies in their own country? But many thought, and I thought so too, that it was special favour and mercy which Heaven showed to Spain in permitting the destruction of that source and hiding place of mischief, that devourer, sponge, and moth of countless money, fruitlessly wasted there to no other purpose save preserving the memory of its capture by the invincible Charles V; as if to make that eternal, as it is and will be, these stones were needed to support it. The fort also fell; but the Turks had to win it inch by inch, for the soldiers who defended it fought so gallantly and stoutly that the number of the enemy killed in twenty-two general assaults exceeded twenty-five thousand. Of three hundred that remained alive not one was taken unwounded, a clear and manifest proof of their gallantry and resolution, and how sturdily they had defended themselves and held their post. A small fort or tower which was in the middle of the lagoon under the command of Don Juan Zanoguera, a Valencian gentleman and a famous soldier, capitulated upon terms. They took prisoner Don Pedro Puertocarrero, commandant of the Goletta, who had done all in his power to defend his fortress, and took the loss of it so much to heart that he died of grief on the way to Constantinople, where they were carrying him a prisoner. They also took the commandant of the fort, Gabrio Cerbellon by name, a Milanese gentleman, a great engineer and a very brave soldier. In these two fortresses perished many persons of note, among whom was Pagano Doria, knight of the Order of St. John, a man of generous disposition, as was shown by his extreme liberality to his brother, the famous John Andrea Doria; and what made his death the more sad was that he was slain by some Arabs to whom, seeing that the fort was now lost, he entrusted himself, and who offered to conduct him in the disguise of a Moor to Tabarca, a small fort or station on the coast held by the Genoese employed in the coral fishery. These Arabs cut off his head and carried it to the commander of the Turkish fleet, who proved on them the truth of our Castilian proverb, that “though the treason may please, the traitor is hated;” for they say he ordered those who brought him the present to be hanged for not having brought him alive.\n\nAmong the Christians who were taken in the fort was one named Don Pedro de Aguilar, a native of some place, I know not what, in Andalusia, who had been ensign in the fort, a soldier of great repute and rare intelligence, who had in particular a special gift for what they call poetry. I say so because his fate brought him to my galley and to my bench, and made him a slave to the same master; and before we left the port this gentleman composed two sonnets by way of epitaphs, one on the Goletta and the other on the fort; indeed, I may as well repeat them, for I have them by heart, and I think they will be liked rather than disliked.\n\nThe instant the captive mentioned the name of Don Pedro de Aguilar, Don Fernando looked at his companions and they all three smiled; and when he came to speak of the sonnets one of them said, “Before your worship proceeds any further I entreat you to tell me what became of that Don Pedro de Aguilar you have spoken of.”\n\n“All I know is,” replied the captive, “that after having been in Constantinople two years, he escaped in the disguise of an Arnaut, in company with a Greek spy; but whether he regained his liberty or not I cannot tell, though I fancy he did, because a year afterwards I saw the Greek at Constantinople, though I was unable to ask him what the result of the journey was.”\n\n“Well then, you are right,” returned the gentleman, “for that Don Pedro is my brother, and he is now in our village in good health, rich, married, and with three children.”\n\n“Thanks be to God for all the mercies he has shown him,” said the captive; “for to my mind there is no happiness on earth to compare with recovering lost liberty.”\n\n“And what is more,” said the gentleman, “I know the sonnets my brother made.”\n\n“Then let your worship repeat them,” said the captive, “for you will recite them better than I can.”\n\n“With all my heart,” said the gentleman; “that on the Goletta runs thus.”\n\n“Blest souls, that, from this mortal husk set free, In guerdon of brave deeds beatified, Above this lowly orb of ours abide Made heirs of heaven and immortality, With noble rage and ardour glowing ye Your strength, while strength was yours, in battle plied, And with your own blood and the foeman’s dyed The sandy soil and the encircling sea. It was the ebbing life-blood first that failed The weary arms; the stout hearts never quailed. Though vanquished, yet ye earned the victor’s crown: Though mourned, yet still triumphant was your fall For there ye won, between the sword and wall, In Heaven glory and on earth renown.” “That is it exactly, according to my recollection,” said the captive.\n\n“Well then, that on the fort,” said the gentleman, “if my memory serves me, goes thus:\n\n“Up from this wasted soil, this shattered shell, Whose walls and towers here in ruin lie, Three thousand soldier souls took wing on high, In the bright mansions of the blest to dwell. The onslaught of the foeman to repel By might of arm all vainly did they try, And when at length ‘twas left them but to die, Wearied and few the last defenders fell. And this same arid soil hath ever been A haunt of countless mournful memories, As well in our day as in days of yore. But never yet to Heaven it sent, I ween, From its hard bosom purer souls than these, Or braver bodies on its surface bore.”\n\nThe sonnets were not disliked, and the captive was rejoiced at the tidings they gave him of his comrade, and continuing his tale, he went on to say:\n\nThe Goletta and the fort being thus in their hands, the Turks gave orders to dismantle the Goletta--for the fort was reduced to such a state that there was nothing left to level--and to do the work more quickly and easily they mined it in three places; but nowhere were they able to blow up the part which seemed to be the least strong, that is to say, the old walls, while all that remained standing of the new fortifications that the Fratin had made came to the ground with the greatest ease. Finally the fleet returned victorious and triumphant to Constantinople, and a few months later died my master, El Uchali, otherwise Uchali Fartax, which means in Turkish “the scabby renegade;” for that he was; it is the practice with the Turks to name people from some defect or virtue they may possess; the reason being that there are among them only four surnames belonging to families tracing their descent from the Ottoman house, and the others, as I have said, take their names and surnames either from bodily blemishes or moral qualities. This “scabby one” rowed at the oar as a slave of the Grand Signor’s for fourteen years, and when over thirty-four years of age, in resentment at having been struck by a Turk while at the oar, turned renegade and renounced his faith in order to be able to revenge himself; and such was his valour that, without owing his advancement to the base ways and means by which most favourites of the Grand Signor rise to power, he came to be king of Algiers, and afterwards general-on-sea, which is the third place of trust in the realm. He was a Calabrian by birth, and a worthy man morally, and he treated his slaves with great humanity. He had three thousand of them, and after his death they were divided, as he directed by his will, between the Grand Signor (who is heir of all who die and shares with the children of the deceased) and his renegades. I fell to the lot of a Venetian renegade who, when a cabin boy on board a ship, had been taken by Uchali and was so much beloved by him that he became one of his most favoured youths. He came to be the most cruel renegade I ever saw: his name was Hassan Aga, and he grew very rich and became king of Algiers. With him I went there from Constantinople, rather glad to be so near Spain, not that I intended to write to anyone about my unhappy lot, but to try if fortune would be kinder to me in Algiers than in Constantinople, where I had attempted in a thousand ways to escape without ever finding a favourable time or chance; but in Algiers I resolved to seek for other means of effecting the purpose I cherished so dearly; for the hope of obtaining my liberty never deserted me; and when in my plots and schemes and attempts the result did not answer my expectations, without giving way to despair I immediately began to look out for or conjure up some new hope to support me, however faint or feeble it might be.\n\nIn this way I lived on immured in a building or prison called by the Turks a bano in which they confine the Christian captives, as well those that are the king’s as those belonging to private individuals, and also what they call those of the Almacen, which is as much as to say the slaves of the municipality, who serve the city in the public works and other employments; but captives of this kind recover their liberty with great difficulty, for, as they are public property and have no particular master, there is no one with whom to treat for their ransom, even though they may have the means. To these banos, as I have said, some private individuals of the town are in the habit of bringing their captives, especially when they are to be ransomed; because there they can keep them in safety and comfort until their ransom arrives. The king’s captives also, that are on ransom, do not go out to work with the rest of the crew, unless when their ransom is delayed; for then, to make them write for it more pressingly, they compel them to work and go for wood, which is no light labour.\n\nI, however, was one of those on ransom, for when it was discovered that I was a captain, although I declared my scanty means and want of fortune, nothing could dissuade them from including me among the gentlemen and those waiting to be ransomed. They put a chain on me, more as a mark of this than to keep me safe, and so I passed my life in that bano with several other gentlemen and persons of quality marked out as held to ransom; but though at times, or rather almost always, we suffered from hunger and scanty clothing, nothing distressed us so much as hearing and seeing at every turn the unexampled and unheard-of cruelties my master inflicted upon the Christians. Every day he hanged a man, impaled one, cut off the ears of another; and all with so little provocation, or so entirely without any, that the Turks acknowledged he did it merely for the sake of doing it, and because he was by nature murderously disposed towards the whole human race. The only one that fared at all well with him was a Spanish soldier, something de Saavedra by name, to whom he never gave a blow himself, or ordered a blow to be given, or addressed a hard word, although he had done things that will dwell in the memory of the people there for many a year, and all to recover his liberty; and for the least of the many things he did we all dreaded that he would be impaled, and he himself was in fear of it more than once; and only that time does not allow, I could tell you now something of what that soldier did, that would interest and astonish you much more than the narration of my own tale.\n\nTo go on with my story; the courtyard of our prison was overlooked by the windows of the house belonging to a wealthy Moor of high position; and these, as is usual in Moorish houses, were rather loopholes than windows, and besides were covered with thick and close lattice-work. It so happened, then, that as I was one day on the terrace of our prison with three other comrades, trying, to pass away the time, how far we could leap with our chains, we being alone, for all the other Christians had gone out to work, I chanced to raise my eyes, and from one of these little closed windows I saw a reed appear with a cloth attached to the end of it, and it kept waving to and fro, and moving as if making signs to us to come and take it. We watched it, and one of those who were with me went and stood under the reed to see whether they would let it drop, or what they would do, but as he did so the reed was raised and moved from side to side, as if they meant to say “no” by a shake of the head. The Christian came back, and it was again lowered, making the same movements as before. Another of my comrades went, and with him the same happened as with the first, and then the third went forward, but with the same result as the first and second. Seeing this I did not like not to try my luck, and as soon as I came under the reed it was dropped and fell inside the bano at my feet. I hastened to untie the cloth, in which I perceived a knot, and in this were ten cianis, which are coins of base gold, current among the Moors, and each worth ten reals of our money.\n\nIt is needless to say I rejoiced over this godsend, and my joy was not less than my wonder as I strove to imagine how this good fortune could have come to us, but to me specially; for the evident unwillingness to drop the reed for any but me showed that it was for me the favour was intended. I took my welcome money, broke the reed, and returned to the terrace, and looking up at the window, I saw a very white hand put out that opened and shut very quickly. From this we gathered or fancied that it must be some woman living in that house that had done us this kindness, and to show that we were grateful for it, we made salaams after the fashion of the Moors, bowing the head, bending the body, and crossing the arms on the breast. Shortly afterwards at the same window a small cross made of reeds was put out and immediately withdrawn. This sign led us to believe that some Christian woman was a captive in the house, and that it was she who had been so good to us; but the whiteness of the hand and the bracelets we had perceived made us dismiss that idea, though we thought it might be one of the Christian renegades whom their masters very often take as lawful wives, and gladly, for they prefer them to the women of their own nation. In all our conjectures we were wide of the truth; so from that time forward our sole occupation was watching and gazing at the window where the cross had appeared to us, as if it were our pole-star; but at least fifteen days passed without our seeing either it or the hand, or any other sign and though meanwhile we endeavoured with the utmost pains to ascertain who it was that lived in the house, and whether there were any Christian renegade in it, nobody could ever tell us anything more than that he who lived there was a rich Moor of high position, Hadji Morato by name, formerly alcaide of La Pata, an office of high dignity among them. But when we least thought it was going to rain any more cianis from that quarter, we saw the reed suddenly appear with another cloth tied in a larger knot attached to it, and this at a time when, as on the former occasion, the bano was deserted and unoccupied.\n\nWe made trial as before, each of the same three going forward before I did; but the reed was delivered to none but me, and on my approach it was let drop. I untied the knot and I found forty Spanish gold crowns with a paper written in Arabic, and at the end of the writing there was a large cross drawn. I kissed the cross, took the crowns and returned to the terrace, and we all made our salaams; again the hand appeared, I made signs that I would read the paper, and then the window was closed. We were all puzzled, though filled with joy at what had taken place; and as none of us understood Arabic, great was our curiosity to know what the paper contained, and still greater the difficulty of finding some one to read it. At last I resolved to confide in a renegade, a native of Murcia, who professed a very great friendship for me, and had given pledges that bound him to keep any secret I might entrust to him; for it is the custom with some renegades, when they intend to return to Christian territory, to carry about them certificates from captives of mark testifying, in whatever form they can, that such and such a renegade is a worthy man who has always shown kindness to Christians, and is anxious to escape on the first opportunity that may present itself. Some obtain these testimonials with good intentions, others put them to a cunning use; for when they go to pillage on Christian territory, if they chance to be cast away, or taken prisoners, they produce their certificates and say that from these papers may be seen the object they came for, which was to remain on Christian ground, and that it was to this end they joined the Turks in their foray. In this way they escape the consequences of the first outburst and make their peace with the Church before it does them any harm, and then when they have the chance they return to Barbary to become what they were before. Others, however, there are who procure these papers and make use of them honestly, and remain on Christian soil. This friend of mine, then, was one of these renegades that I have described; he had certificates from all our comrades, in which we testified in his favour as strongly as we could; and if the Moors had found the papers they would have burned him alive.\n\nI knew that he understood Arabic very well, and could not only speak but also write it; but before I disclosed the whole matter to him, I asked him to read for me this paper which I had found by accident in a hole in my cell. He opened it and remained some time examining it and muttering to himself as he translated it. I asked him if he understood it, and he told me he did perfectly well, and that if I wished him to tell me its meaning word for word, I must give him pen and ink that he might do it more satisfactorily. We at once gave him what he required, and he set about translating it bit by bit, and when he had done he said:\n\n“All that is here in Spanish is what the Moorish paper contains, and you must bear in mind that when it says ‘Lela Marien’ it means ‘Our Lady the Virgin Mary.’”\n\nWe read the paper and it ran thus:\n\n“When I was a child my father had a slave who taught me to pray the Christian prayer in my own language, and told me many things about Lela Marien. The Christian died, and I know that she did not go to the fire, but to Allah, because since then I have seen her twice, and she told me to go to the land of the Christians to see Lela Marien, who had great love for me. I know not how to go. I have seen many Christians, but except thyself none has seemed to me to be a gentleman. I am young and beautiful, and have plenty of money to take with me. See if thou canst contrive how we may go, and if thou wilt thou shalt be my husband there, and if thou wilt not it will not distress me, for Lela Marien will find me some one to marry me. I myself have written this: have a care to whom thou givest it to read: trust no Moor, for they are all perfidious. I am greatly troubled on this account, for I would not have thee confide in anyone, because if my father knew it he would at once fling me down a well and cover me with stones. I will put a thread to the reed; tie the answer to it, and if thou hast no one to write for thee in Arabic, tell it to me by signs, for Lela Marien will make me understand thee. She and Allah and this cross, which I often kiss as the captive bade me, protect thee.”\n\nJudge, sirs, whether we had reason for surprise and joy at the words of this paper; and both one and the other were so great, that the renegade perceived that the paper had not been found by chance, but had been in reality addressed to some one of us, and he begged us, if what he suspected were the truth, to trust him and tell him all, for he would risk his life for our freedom; and so saying he took out from his breast a metal crucifix, and with many tears swore by the God the image represented, in whom, sinful and wicked as he was, he truly and faithfully believed, to be loyal to us and keep secret whatever we chose to reveal to him; for he thought and almost foresaw that by means of her who had written that paper, he and all of us would obtain our liberty, and he himself obtain the object he so much desired, his restoration to the bosom of the Holy Mother Church, from which by his own sin and ignorance he was now severed like a corrupt limb. The renegade said this with so many tears and such signs of repentance, that with one consent we all agreed to tell him the whole truth of the matter, and so we gave him a full account of all, without hiding anything from him. We pointed out to him the window at which the reed appeared, and he by that means took note of the house, and resolved to ascertain with particular care who lived in it. We agreed also that it would be advisable to answer the Moorish lady’s letter, and the renegade without a moment’s delay took down the words I dictated to him, which were exactly what I shall tell you, for nothing of importance that took place in this affair has escaped my memory, or ever will while life lasts. This, then, was the answer returned to the Moorish lady:\n\n“The true Allah protect thee, Lady, and that blessed Marien who is the true mother of God, and who has put it into thy heart to go to the land of the Christians, because she loves thee. Entreat her that she be pleased to show thee how thou canst execute the command she gives thee, for she will, such is her goodness. On my own part, and on that of all these Christians who are with me, I promise to do all that we can for thee, even to death. Fail not to write to me and inform me what thou dost mean to do, and I will always answer thee; for the great Allah has given us a Christian captive who can speak and write thy language well, as thou mayest see by this paper; without fear, therefore, thou canst inform us of all thou wouldst. As to what thou sayest, that if thou dost reach the land of the Christians thou wilt be my wife, I give thee my promise upon it as a good Christian; and know that the Christians keep their promises better than the Moors. Allah and Marien his mother watch over thee, my Lady.”\n\nThe paper being written and folded I waited two days until the bano was empty as before, and immediately repaired to the usual walk on the terrace to see if there were any sign of the reed, which was not long in making its appearance. As soon as I saw it, although I could not distinguish who put it out, I showed the paper as a sign to attach the thread, but it was already fixed to the reed, and to it I tied the paper; and shortly afterwards our star once more made its appearance with the white flag of peace, the little bundle. It was dropped, and I picked it up, and found in the cloth, in gold and silver coins of all sorts, more than fifty crowns, which fifty times more strengthened our joy and doubled our hope of gaining our liberty. That very night our renegade returned and said he had learned that the Moor we had been told of lived in that house, that his name was Hadji Morato, that he was enormously rich, that he had one only daughter the heiress of all his wealth, and that it was the general opinion throughout the city that she was the most beautiful woman in Barbary, and that several of the viceroys who came there had sought her for a wife, but that she had been always unwilling to marry; and he had learned, moreover, that she had a Christian slave who was now dead; all which agreed with the contents of the paper. We immediately took counsel with the renegade as to what means would have to be adopted in order to carry off the Moorish lady and bring us all to Christian territory; and in the end it was agreed that for the present we should wait for a second communication from Zoraida (for that was the name of her who now desires to be called Maria), because we saw clearly that she and no one else could find a way out of all these difficulties. When we had decided upon this the renegade told us not to be uneasy, for he would lose his life or restore us to liberty. For four days the bano was filled with people, for which reason the reed delayed its appearance for four days, but at the end of that time, when the bano was, as it generally was, empty, it appeared with the cloth so bulky that it promised a happy birth. Reed and cloth came down to me, and I found another paper and a hundred crowns in gold, without any other coin. The renegade was present, and in our cell we gave him the paper to read, which was to this effect:\n\n“I cannot think of a plan, señor, for our going to Spain, nor has Lela Marien shown me one, though I have asked her. All that can be done is for me to give you plenty of money in gold from this window. With it ransom yourself and your friends, and let one of you go to the land of the Christians, and there buy a vessel and come back for the others; and he will find me in my father’s garden, which is at the Babazon gate near the seashore, where I shall be all this summer with my father and my servants. You can carry me away from there by night without any danger, and bring me to the vessel. And remember thou art to be my husband, else I will pray to Marien to punish thee. If thou canst not trust anyone to go for the vessel, ransom thyself and do thou go, for I know thou wilt return more surely than any other, as thou art a gentleman and a Christian. Endeavour to make thyself acquainted with the garden; and when I see thee walking yonder I shall know that the bano is empty and I will give thee abundance of money. Allah protect thee, señor.”\n\nThese were the words and contents of the second paper, and on hearing them, each declared himself willing to be the ransomed one, and promised to go and return with scrupulous good faith; and I too made the same offer; but to all this the renegade objected, saying that he would not on any account consent to one being set free before all went together, as experience had taught him how ill those who have been set free keep promises which they made in captivity; for captives of distinction frequently had recourse to this plan, paying the ransom of one who was to go to Valencia or Majorca with money to enable him to arm a bark and return for the others who had ransomed him, but who never came back; for recovered liberty and the dread of losing it again efface from the memory all the obligations in the world. And to prove the truth of what he said, he told us briefly what had happened to a certain Christian gentleman almost at that very time, the strangest case that had ever occurred even there, where astonishing and marvellous things are happening every instant. In short, he ended by saying that what could and ought to be done was to give the money intended for the ransom of one of us Christians to him, so that he might with it buy a vessel there in Algiers under the pretence of becoming a merchant and trader at Tetuan and along the coast; and when master of the vessel, it would be easy for him to hit on some way of getting us all out of the bano and putting us on board; especially if the Moorish lady gave, as she said, money enough to ransom all, because once free it would be the easiest thing in the world for us to embark even in open day; but the greatest difficulty was that the Moors do not allow any renegade to buy or own any craft, unless it be a large vessel for going on roving expeditions, because they are afraid that anyone who buys a small vessel, especially if he be a Spaniard, only wants it for the purpose of escaping to Christian territory. This however he could get over by arranging with a Tagarin Moor to go shares with him in the purchase of the vessel, and in the profit on the cargo; and under cover of this he could become master of the vessel, in which case he looked upon all the rest as accomplished. But though to me and my comrades it had seemed a better plan to send to Majorca for the vessel, as the Moorish lady suggested, we did not dare to oppose him, fearing that if we did not do as he said he would denounce us, and place us in danger of losing all our lives if he were to disclose our dealings with Zoraida, for whose life we would have all given our own. We therefore resolved to put ourselves in the hands of God and in the renegade’s; and at the same time an answer was given to Zoraida, telling her that we would do all she recommended, for she had given as good advice as if Lela Marien had delivered it, and that it depended on her alone whether we were to defer the business or put it in execution at once. I renewed my promise to be her husband; and thus the next day that the bano chanced to be empty she at different times gave us by means of the reed and cloth two thousand gold crowns and a paper in which she said that the next Juma, that is to say Friday, she was going to her father’s garden, but that before she went she would give us more money; and if it were not enough we were to let her know, as she would give us as much as we asked, for her father had so much he would not miss it, and besides she kept all the keys.\n\nWe at once gave the renegade five hundred crowns to buy the vessel, and with eight hundred I ransomed myself, giving the money to a Valencian merchant who happened to be in Algiers at the time, and who had me released on his word, pledging it that on the arrival of the first ship from Valencia he would pay my ransom; for if he had given the money at once it would have made the king suspect that my ransom money had been for a long time in Algiers, and that the merchant had for his own advantage kept it secret. In fact my master was so difficult to deal with that I dared not on any account pay down the money at once. The Thursday before the Friday on which the fair Zoraida was to go to the garden she gave us a thousand crowns more, and warned us of her departure, begging me, if I were ransomed, to find out her father’s garden at once, and by all means to seek an opportunity of going there to see her. I answered in a few words that I would do so, and that she must remember to commend us to Lela Marien with all the prayers the captive had taught her. This having been done, steps were taken to ransom our three comrades, so as to enable them to quit the bano, and lest, seeing me ransomed and themselves not, though the money was forthcoming, they should make a disturbance about it and the devil should prompt them to do something that might injure Zoraida; for though their position might be sufficient to relieve me from this apprehension, nevertheless I was unwilling to run any risk in the matter; and so I had them ransomed in the same way as I was, handing over all the money to the merchant so that he might with safety and confidence give security; without, however, confiding our arrangement and secret to him, which might have been dangerous.\n\n# IN WHICH THE CAPTIVE STILL CONTINUES HIS ADVENTURES\n\nBefore fifteen days were over our renegade had already purchased an excellent vessel with room for more than thirty persons; and to make the transaction safe and lend a colour to it, he thought it well to make, as he did, a voyage to a place called Shershel, twenty leagues from Algiers on the Oran side, where there is an extensive trade in dried figs. Two or three times he made this voyage in company with the Tagarin already mentioned. The Moors of Aragon are called Tagarins in Barbary, and those of Granada Mudejars; but in the Kingdom of Fez they call the Mudejars Elches, and they are the people the king chiefly employs in war. To proceed: every time he passed with his vessel he anchored in a cove that was not two crossbow shots from the garden where Zoraida was waiting; and there the renegade, together with the two Moorish lads that rowed, used purposely to station himself, either going through his prayers, or else practising as a part what he meant to perform in earnest. And thus he would go to Zoraida’s garden and ask for fruit, which her father gave him, not knowing him; but though, as he afterwards told me, he sought to speak to Zoraida, and tell her who he was, and that by my orders he was to take her to the land of the Christians, so that she might feel satisfied and easy, he had never been able to do so; for the Moorish women do not allow themselves to be seen by any Moor or Turk, unless their husband or father bid them: with Christian captives they permit freedom of intercourse and communication, even more than might be considered proper. But for my part I should have been sorry if he had spoken to her, for perhaps it might have alarmed her to find her affairs talked of by renegades. But God, who ordered it otherwise, afforded no opportunity for our renegade’s well-meant purpose; and he, seeing how safely he could go to Shershel and return, and anchor when and how and where he liked, and that the Tagarin his partner had no will but his, and that, now I was ransomed, all we wanted was to find some Christians to row, told me to look out for any I should be willing to take with me, over and above those who had been ransomed, and to engage them for the next Friday, which he fixed upon for our departure. On this I spoke to twelve Spaniards, all stout rowers, and such as could most easily leave the city; but it was no easy matter to find so many just then, because there were twenty ships out on a cruise and they had taken all the rowers with them; and these would not have been found were it not that their master remained at home that summer without going to sea in order to finish a galliot that he had upon the stocks. To these men I said nothing more than that the next Friday in the evening they were to come out stealthily one by one and hang about Hadji Morato’s garden, waiting for me there until I came. These directions I gave each one separately, with orders that if they saw any other Christians there they were not to say anything to them except that I had directed them to wait at that spot.\n\nThis preliminary having been settled, another still more necessary step had to be taken, which was to let Zoraida know how matters stood that she might be prepared and forewarned, so as not to be taken by surprise if we were suddenly to seize upon her before she thought the Christians’ vessel could have returned. I determined, therefore, to go to the garden and try if I could speak to her; and the day before my departure I went there under the pretence of gathering herbs. The first person I met was her father, who addressed me in the language that all over Barbary and even in Constantinople is the medium between captives and Moors, and is neither Morisco nor Castilian, nor of any other nation, but a mixture of all languages, by means of which we can all understand one another. In this sort of language, I say, he asked me what I wanted in his garden, and to whom I belonged. I replied that I was a slave of the Arnaut Mami (for I knew as a certainty that he was a very great friend of his), and that I wanted some herbs to make a salad. He asked me then whether I were on ransom or not, and what my master demanded for me. While these questions and answers were proceeding, the fair Zoraida, who had already perceived me some time before, came out of the house in the garden, and as Moorish women are by no means particular about letting themselves be seen by Christians, or, as I have said before, at all coy, she had no hesitation in coming to where her father stood with me; moreover her father, seeing her approaching slowly, called to her to come. It would be beyond my power now to describe to you the great beauty, the high-bred air, the brilliant attire of my beloved Zoraida as she presented herself before my eyes. I will content myself with saying that more pearls hung from her fair neck, her ears, and her hair than she had hairs on her head. On her ankles, which as is customary were bare, she had carcajes (for so bracelets or anklets are called in Morisco) of the purest gold, set with so many diamonds that she told me afterwards her father valued them at ten thousand doubloons, and those she had on her wrists were worth as much more. The pearls were in profusion and very fine, for the highest display and adornment of the Moorish women is decking themselves with rich pearls and seed-pearls; and of these there are therefore more among the Moors than among any other people. Zoraida’s father had to the reputation of possessing a great number, and the purest in all Algiers, and of possessing also more than two hundred thousand Spanish crowns; and she, who is now mistress of me only, was mistress of all this. Whether thus adorned she would have been beautiful or not, and what she must have been in her prosperity, may be imagined from the beauty remaining to her after so many hardships; for, as everyone knows, the beauty of some women has its times and its seasons, and is increased or diminished by chance causes; and naturally the emotions of the mind will heighten or impair it, though indeed more frequently they totally destroy it. In a word she presented herself before me that day attired with the utmost splendour, and supremely beautiful; at any rate, she seemed to me the most beautiful object I had ever seen; and when, besides, I thought of all I owed to her I felt as though I had before me some heavenly being come to earth to bring me relief and happiness.\n\nAs she approached her father told her in his own language that I was a captive belonging to his friend the Arnaut Mami, and that I had come for salad.\n\nShe took up the conversation, and in that mixture of tongues I have spoken of she asked me if I was a gentleman, and why I was not ransomed.\n\nI answered that I was already ransomed, and that by the price it might be seen what value my master set on me, as I had given one thousand five hundred zoltanis for me; to which she replied, “Hadst thou been my father’s, I can tell thee, I would not have let him part with thee for twice as much, for you Christians always tell lies about yourselves and make yourselves out poor to cheat the Moors.”\n\n“That may be, lady,” said I; “but indeed I dealt truthfully with my master, as I do and mean to do with everybody in the world.”\n\n“And when dost thou go?” said Zoraida.\n\n“To-morrow, I think,” said I, “for there is a vessel here from France which sails to-morrow, and I think I shall go in her.”\n\n“Would it not be better,” said Zoraida, “to wait for the arrival of ships from Spain and go with them and not with the French who are not your friends?”\n\n“No,” said I; “though if there were intelligence that a vessel were now coming from Spain it is true I might, perhaps, wait for it; however, it is more likely I shall depart to-morrow, for the longing I feel to return to my country and to those I love is so great that it will not allow me to wait for another opportunity, however more convenient, if it be delayed.”\n\n“No doubt thou art married in thine own country,” said Zoraida, “and for that reason thou art anxious to go and see thy wife.”\n\n“I am not married,” I replied, “but I have given my promise to marry on my arrival there.”\n\n“And is the lady beautiful to whom thou hast given it?” said Zoraida.\n\n“So beautiful,” said I, “that, to describe her worthily and tell thee the truth, she is very like thee.”\n\nAt this her father laughed very heartily and said, “By Allah, Christian, she must be very beautiful if she is like my daughter, who is the most beautiful woman in all this kingdom: only look at her well and thou wilt see I am telling the truth.”\n\nZoraida’s father as the better linguist helped to interpret most of these words and phrases, for though she spoke the bastard language, that, as I have said, is employed there, she expressed her meaning more by signs than by words.\n\nWhile we were still engaged in this conversation, a Moor came running up, exclaiming that four Turks had leaped over the fence or wall of the garden, and were gathering the fruit though it was not yet ripe. The old man was alarmed and Zoraida too, for the Moors commonly, and, so to speak, instinctively have a dread of the Turks, but particularly of the soldiers, who are so insolent and domineering to the Moors who are under their power that they treat them worse than if they were their slaves. Her father said to Zoraida, “Daughter, retire into the house and shut thyself in while I go and speak to these dogs; and thou, Christian, pick thy herbs, and go in peace, and Allah bring thee safe to thy own country.”\n\nI bowed, and he went away to look for the Turks, leaving me alone with Zoraida, who made as if she were about to retire as her father bade her; but the moment he was concealed by the trees of the garden, turning to me with her eyes full of tears she said, “Tameji, cristiano, tameji?” that is to say, “Art thou going, Christian, art thou going?”\n\nI made answer, “Yes, lady, but not without thee, come what may: be on the watch for me on the next Juma, and be not alarmed when thou seest us; for most surely we shall go to the land of the Christians.”\n\nThis I said in such a way that she understood perfectly all that passed between us, and throwing her arm round my neck she began with feeble steps to move towards the house; but as fate would have it (and it might have been very unfortunate if Heaven had not otherwise ordered it), just as we were moving on in the manner and position I have described, with her arm round my neck, her father, as he returned after having sent away the Turks, saw how we were walking and we perceived that he saw us; but Zoraida, ready and quickwitted, took care not to remove her arm from my neck, but on the contrary drew closer to me and laid her head on my breast, bending her knees a little and showing all the signs and tokens of fainting, while I at the same time made it seem as though I were supporting her against my will. Her father came running up to where we were, and seeing his daughter in this state asked what was the matter with her; she, however, giving no answer, he said, “No doubt she has fainted in alarm at the entrance of those dogs,” and taking her from mine he drew her to his own breast, while she sighing, her eyes still wet with tears, said again, “Ameji, cristiano, ameji”--“Go, Christian, go.” To this her father replied, “There is no need, daughter, for the Christian to go, for he has done thee no harm, and the Turks have now gone; feel no alarm, there is nothing to hurt thee, for as I say, the Turks at my request have gone back the way they came.”\n\n“It was they who terrified her, as thou hast said, señor,” said I to her father; “but since she tells me to go, I have no wish to displease her: peace be with thee, and with thy leave I will come back to this garden for herbs if need be, for my master says there are nowhere better herbs for salad then here.”\n\n“Come back for any thou hast need of,” replied Hadji Morato; “for my daughter does not speak thus because she is displeased with thee or any Christian: she only meant that the Turks should go, not thou; or that it was time for thee to look for thy herbs.”\n\nWith this I at once took my leave of both; and she, looking as though her heart were breaking, retired with her father. While pretending to look for herbs I made the round of the garden at my ease, and studied carefully all the approaches and outlets, and the fastenings of the house and everything that could be taken advantage of to make our task easy.\n\nHaving done so I went and gave an account of all that had taken place to the renegade and my comrades, and looked forward with impatience to the hour when, all fear at an end, I should find myself in possession of the prize which fortune held out to me in the fair and lovely Zoraida. The time passed at length, and the appointed day we so longed for arrived; and, all following out the arrangement and plan which, after careful consideration and many a long discussion, we had decided upon, we succeeded as fully as we could have wished; for on the Friday following the day upon which I spoke to Zoraida in the garden, the renegade anchored his vessel at nightfall almost opposite the spot where she was. The Christians who were to row were ready and in hiding in different places round about, all waiting for me, anxious and elated, and eager to attack the vessel they had before their eyes; for they did not know the renegade’s plan, but expected that they were to gain their liberty by force of arms and by killing the Moors who were on board the vessel. As soon, then, as I and my comrades made our appearance, all those that were in hiding seeing us came and joined us. It was now the time when the city gates are shut, and there was no one to be seen in all the space outside. When we were collected together we debated whether it would be better first to go for Zoraida, or to make prisoners of the Moorish rowers who rowed in the vessel; but while we were still uncertain our renegade came up asking us what kept us, as it was now the time, and all the Moors were off their guard and most of them asleep. We told him why we hesitated, but he said it was of more importance first to secure the vessel, which could be done with the greatest ease and without any danger, and then we could go for Zoraida. We all approved of what he said, and so without further delay, guided by him we made for the vessel, and he leaping on board first, drew his cutlass and said in Morisco, “Let no one stir from this if he does not want it to cost him his life.” By this almost all the Christians were on board, and the Moors, who were fainthearted, hearing their captain speak in this way, were cowed, and without any one of them taking to his arms (and indeed they had few or hardly any) they submitted without saying a word to be bound by the Christians, who quickly secured them, threatening them that if they raised any kind of outcry they would be all put to the sword. This having been accomplished, and half of our party being left to keep guard over them, the rest of us, again taking the renegade as our guide, hastened towards Hadji Morato’s garden, and as good luck would have it, on trying the gate it opened as easily as if it had not been locked; and so, quite quietly and in silence, we reached the house without being perceived by anybody. The lovely Zoraida was watching for us at a window, and as soon as she perceived that there were people there, she asked in a low voice if we were “Nizarani,” as much as to say or ask if we were Christians. I answered that we were, and begged her to come down. As soon as she recognised me she did not delay an instant, but without answering a word came down immediately, opened the door and presented herself before us all, so beautiful and so richly attired that I cannot attempt to describe her. The moment I saw her I took her hand and kissed it, and the renegade and my two comrades did the same; and the rest, who knew nothing of the circumstances, did as they saw us do, for it only seemed as if we were returning thanks to her, and recognising her as the giver of our liberty. The renegade asked her in the Morisco language if her father was in the house. She replied that he was and that he was asleep.\n\n“Then it will be necessary to waken him and take him with us,” said the renegade, “and everything of value in this fair mansion.”\n\n“Nay,” said she, “my father must not on any account be touched, and there is nothing in the house except what I shall take, and that will be quite enough to enrich and satisfy all of you; wait a little and you shall see,” and so saying she went in, telling us she would return immediately and bidding us keep quiet making any noise.\n\nI asked the renegade what had passed between them, and when he told me, I declared that nothing should be done except in accordance with the wishes of Zoraida, who now came back with a little trunk so full of gold crowns that she could scarcely carry it. Unfortunately her father awoke while this was going on, and hearing a noise in the garden, came to the window, and at once perceiving that all those who were there were Christians, raising a prodigiously loud outcry, he began to call out in Arabic, “Christians, Christians! thieves, thieves!” by which cries we were all thrown into the greatest fear and embarrassment; but the renegade seeing the danger we were in and how important it was for him to effect his purpose before we were heard, mounted with the utmost quickness to where Hadji Morato was, and with him went some of our party; I, however, did not dare to leave Zoraida, who had fallen almost fainting in my arms. To be brief, those who had gone upstairs acted so promptly that in an instant they came down, carrying Hadji Morato with his hands bound and a napkin tied over his mouth, which prevented him from uttering a word, warning him at the same time that to attempt to speak would cost him his life. When his daughter caught sight of him she covered her eyes so as not to see him, and her father was horror-stricken, not knowing how willingly she had placed herself in our hands. But it was now most essential for us to be on the move, and carefully and quickly we regained the vessel, where those who had remained on board were waiting for us in apprehension of some mishap having befallen us. It was barely two hours after night set in when we were all on board the vessel, where the cords were removed from the hands of Zoraida’s father, and the napkin from his mouth; but the renegade once more told him not to utter a word, or they would take his life. He, when he saw his daughter there, began to sigh piteously, and still more when he perceived that I held her closely embraced and that she lay quiet without resisting or complaining, or showing any reluctance; nevertheless he remained silent lest they should carry into effect the repeated threats the renegade had addressed to him.\n\nFinding herself now on board, and that we were about to give way with the oars, Zoraida, seeing her father there, and the other Moors bound, bade the renegade ask me to do her the favour of releasing the Moors and setting her father at liberty, for she would rather drown herself in the sea than suffer a father that had loved her so dearly to be carried away captive before her eyes and on her account. The renegade repeated this to me, and I replied that I was very willing to do so; but he replied that it was not advisable, because if they were left there they would at once raise the country and stir up the city, and lead to the despatch of swift cruisers in pursuit, and our being taken, by sea or land, without any possibility of escape; and that all that could be done was to set them free on the first Christian ground we reached. On this point we all agreed; and Zoraida, to whom it was explained, together with the reasons that prevented us from doing at once what she desired, was satisfied likewise; and then in glad silence and with cheerful alacrity each of our stout rowers took his oar, and commending ourselves to God with all our hearts, we began to shape our course for the island of Majorca, the nearest Christian land. Owing, however, to the Tramontana rising a little, and the sea growing somewhat rough, it was impossible for us to keep a straight course for Majorca, and we were compelled to coast in the direction of Oran, not without great uneasiness on our part lest we should be observed from the town of Shershel, which lies on that coast, not more than sixty miles from Algiers. Moreover we were afraid of meeting on that course one of the galliots that usually come with goods from Tetuan; although each of us for himself and all of us together felt confident that, if we were to meet a merchant galliot, so that it were not a cruiser, not only should we not be lost, but that we should take a vessel in which we could more safely accomplish our voyage. As we pursued our course Zoraida kept her head between my hands so as not to see her father, and I felt that she was praying to Lela Marien to help us.\n\nWe might have made about thirty miles when daybreak found us some three musket-shots off the land, which seemed to us deserted, and without anyone to see us. For all that, however, by hard rowing we put out a little to sea, for it was now somewhat calmer, and having gained about two leagues the word was given to row by batches, while we ate something, for the vessel was well provided; but the rowers said it was not a time to take any rest; let food be served out to those who were not rowing, but they would not leave their oars on any account. This was done, but now a stiff breeze began to blow, which obliged us to leave off rowing and make sail at once and steer for Oran, as it was impossible to make any other course. All this was done very promptly, and under sail we ran more than eight miles an hour without any fear, except that of coming across some vessel out on a roving expedition. We gave the Moorish rowers some food, and the renegade comforted them by telling them that they were not held as captives, as we should set them free on the first opportunity.\n\nThe same was said to Zoraida’s father, who replied, “Anything else, Christian, I might hope for or think likely from your generosity and good behaviour, but do not think me so simple as to imagine you will give me my liberty; for you would have never exposed yourselves to the danger of depriving me of it only to restore it to me so generously, especially as you know who I am and the sum you may expect to receive on restoring it; and if you will only name that, I here offer you all you require for myself and for my unhappy daughter there; or else for her alone, for she is the greatest and most precious part of my soul.”\n\nAs he said this he began to weep so bitterly that he filled us all with compassion and forced Zoraida to look at him, and when she saw him weeping she was so moved that she rose from my feet and ran to throw her arms round him, and pressing her face to his, they both gave way to such an outburst of tears that several of us were constrained to keep them company.\n\nBut when her father saw her in full dress and with all her jewels about her, he said to her in his own language, “What means this, my daughter? Last night, before this terrible misfortune in which we are plunged befell us, I saw thee in thy everyday and indoor garments; and now, without having had time to attire thyself, and without my bringing thee any joyful tidings to furnish an occasion for adorning and bedecking thyself, I see thee arrayed in the finest attire it would be in my power to give thee when fortune was most kind to us. Answer me this; for it causes me greater anxiety and surprise than even this misfortune itself.”\n\nThe renegade interpreted to us what the Moor said to his daughter; she, however, returned him no answer. But when he observed in one corner of the vessel the little trunk in which she used to keep her jewels, which he well knew he had left in Algiers and had not brought to the garden, he was still more amazed, and asked her how that trunk had come into our hands, and what there was in it. To which the renegade, without waiting for Zoraida to reply, made answer, “Do not trouble thyself by asking thy daughter Zoraida so many questions, señor, for the one answer I will give thee will serve for all; I would have thee know that she is a Christian, and that it is she who has been the file for our chains and our deliverer from captivity. She is here of her own free will, as glad, I imagine, to find herself in this position as he who escapes from darkness into the light, from death to life, and from suffering to glory.”\n\n“Daughter, is this true, what he says?” cried the Moor.\n\n“It is,” replied Zoraida.\n\n“That thou art in truth a Christian,” said the old man, “and that thou hast given thy father into the power of his enemies?”\n\nTo which Zoraida made answer, “A Christian I am, but it is not I who have placed thee in this position, for it never was my wish to leave thee or do thee harm, but only to do good to myself.”\n\n“And what good hast thou done thyself, daughter?” said he.\n\n“Ask thou that,” said she, “of Lela Marien, for she can tell thee better than I.”\n\nThe Moor had hardly heard these words when with marvellous quickness he flung himself headforemost into the sea, where no doubt he would have been drowned had not the long and full dress he wore held him up for a little on the surface of the water. Zoraida cried aloud to us to save him, and we all hastened to help, and seizing him by his robe we drew him in half drowned and insensible, at which Zoraida was in such distress that she wept over him as piteously and bitterly as though he were already dead. We turned him upon his face and he voided a great quantity of water, and at the end of two hours came to himself. Meanwhile, the wind having changed we were compelled to head for the land, and ply our oars to avoid being driven on shore; but it was our good fortune to reach a creek that lies on one side of a small promontory or cape, called by the Moors that of the “Cava rumia,” which in our language means “the wicked Christian woman;” for it is a tradition among them that La Cava, through whom Spain was lost, lies buried at that spot; “cava” in their language meaning “wicked woman,” and “rumia” “Christian;” moreover, they count it unlucky to anchor there when necessity compels them, and they never do so otherwise. For us, however, it was not the resting-place of the wicked woman but a haven of safety for our relief, so much had the sea now got up. We posted a look-out on shore, and never let the oars out of our hands, and ate of the stores the renegade had laid in, imploring God and Our Lady with all our hearts to help and protect us, that we might give a happy ending to a beginning so prosperous. At the entreaty of Zoraida orders were given to set on shore her father and the other Moors who were still bound, for she could not endure, nor could her tender heart bear to see her father in bonds and her fellow-countrymen prisoners before her eyes. We promised her to do this at the moment of departure, for as it was uninhabited we ran no risk in releasing them at that place.\n\nOur prayers were not so far in vain as to be unheard by Heaven, for after a while the wind changed in our favour, and made the sea calm, inviting us once more to resume our voyage with a good heart. Seeing this we unbound the Moors, and one by one put them on shore, at which they were filled with amazement; but when we came to land Zoraida’s father, who had now completely recovered his senses, he said:\n\n“Why is it, think ye, Christians, that this wicked woman is rejoiced at your giving me my liberty? Think ye it is because of the affection she bears me? Nay verily, it is only because of the hindrance my presence offers to the execution of her base designs. And think not that it is her belief that yours is better than ours that has led her to change her religion; it is only because she knows that immodesty is more freely practised in your country than in ours.” Then turning to Zoraida, while I and another of the Christians held him fast by both arms, lest he should do some mad act, he said to her, “Infamous girl, misguided maiden, whither in thy blindness and madness art thou going in the hands of these dogs, our natural enemies? Cursed be the hour when I begot thee! Cursed the luxury and indulgence in which I reared thee!”\n\nBut seeing that he was not likely soon to cease I made haste to put him on shore, and thence he continued his maledictions and lamentations aloud; calling on Mohammed to pray to Allah to destroy us, to confound us, to make an end of us; and when, in consequence of having made sail, we could no longer hear what he said we could see what he did; how he plucked out his beard and tore his hair and lay writhing on the ground. But once he raised his voice to such a pitch that we were able to hear what he said. “Come back, dear daughter, come back to shore; I forgive thee all; let those men have the money, for it is theirs now, and come back to comfort thy sorrowing father, who will yield up his life on this barren strand if thou dost leave him.”\n\nAll this Zoraida heard, and heard with sorrow and tears, and all she could say in answer was, “Allah grant that Lela Marien, who has made me become a Christian, give thee comfort in thy sorrow, my father. Allah knows that I could not do otherwise than I have done, and that these Christians owe nothing to my will; for even had I wished not to accompany them, but remain at home, it would have been impossible for me, so eagerly did my soul urge me on to the accomplishment of this purpose, which I feel to be as righteous as to thee, dear father, it seems wicked.”\n\nBut neither could her father hear her nor we see him when she said this; and so, while I consoled Zoraida, we turned our attention to our voyage, in which a breeze from the right point so favoured us that we made sure of finding ourselves off the coast of Spain on the morrow by daybreak. But, as good seldom or never comes pure and unmixed, without being attended or followed by some disturbing evil that gives a shock to it, our fortune, or perhaps the curses which the Moor had hurled at his daughter (for whatever kind of father they may come from these are always to be dreaded), brought it about that when we were now in mid-sea, and the night about three hours spent, as we were running with all sail set and oars lashed, for the favouring breeze saved us the trouble of using them, we saw by the light of the moon, which shone brilliantly, a square-rigged vessel in full sail close to us, luffing up and standing across our course, and so close that we had to strike sail to avoid running foul of her, while they too put the helm hard up to let us pass. They came to the side of the ship to ask who we were, whither we were bound, and whence we came, but as they asked this in French our renegade said, “Let no one answer, for no doubt these are French corsairs who plunder all comers.”\n\nActing on this warning no one answered a word, but after we had gone a little ahead, and the vessel was now lying to leeward, suddenly they fired two guns, and apparently both loaded with chain-shot, for with one they cut our mast in half and brought down both it and the sail into the sea, and the other, discharged at the same moment, sent a ball into our vessel amidships, staving her in completely, but without doing any further damage. We, however, finding ourselves sinking began to shout for help and call upon those in the ship to pick us up as we were beginning to fill. They then lay to, and lowering a skiff or boat, as many as a dozen Frenchmen, well armed with match-locks, and their matches burning, got into it and came alongside; and seeing how few we were, and that our vessel was going down, they took us in, telling us that this had come to us through our incivility in not giving them an answer. Our renegade took the trunk containing Zoraida’s wealth and dropped it into the sea without anyone perceiving what he did. In short we went on board with the Frenchmen, who, after having ascertained all they wanted to know about us, rifled us of everything we had, as if they had been our bitterest enemies, and from Zoraida they took even the anklets she wore on her feet; but the distress they caused her did not distress me so much as the fear I was in that from robbing her of her rich and precious jewels they would proceed to rob her of the most precious jewel that she valued more than all. The desires, however, of those people do not go beyond money, but of that their covetousness is insatiable, and on this occasion it was carried to such a pitch that they would have taken even the clothes we wore as captives if they had been worth anything to them. It was the advice of some of them to throw us all into the sea wrapped up in a sail; for their purpose was to trade at some of the ports of Spain, giving themselves out as Bretons, and if they brought us alive they would be punished as soon as the robbery was discovered; but the captain (who was the one who had plundered my beloved Zoraida) said he was satisfied with the prize he had got, and that he would not touch at any Spanish port, but pass the Straits of Gibraltar by night, or as best he could, and make for La Rochelle, from which he had sailed. So they agreed by common consent to give us the skiff belonging to their ship and all we required for the short voyage that remained to us, and this they did the next day on coming in sight of the Spanish coast, with which, and the joy we felt, all our sufferings and miseries were as completely forgotten as if they had never been endured by us, such is the delight of recovering lost liberty.\n\nIt may have been about mid-day when they placed us in the boat, giving us two kegs of water and some biscuit; and the captain, moved by I know not what compassion, as the lovely Zoraida was about to embark, gave her some forty gold crowns, and would not permit his men to take from her those same garments which she has on now. We got into the boat, returning them thanks for their kindness to us, and showing ourselves grateful rather than indignant. They stood out to sea, steering for the straits; we, without looking to any compass save the land we had before us, set ourselves to row with such energy that by sunset we were so near that we might easily, we thought, land before the night was far advanced. But as the moon did not show that night, and the sky was clouded, and as we knew not whereabouts we were, it did not seem to us a prudent thing to make for the shore, as several of us advised, saying we ought to run ourselves ashore even if it were on rocks and far from any habitation, for in this way we should be relieved from the apprehensions we naturally felt of the prowling vessels of the Tetuan corsairs, who leave Barbary at nightfall and are on the Spanish coast by daybreak, where they commonly take some prize, and then go home to sleep in their own houses. But of the conflicting counsels the one which was adopted was that we should approach gradually, and land where we could if the sea were calm enough to permit us. This was done, and a little before midnight we drew near to the foot of a huge and lofty mountain, not so close to the sea but that it left a narrow space on which to land conveniently. We ran our boat up on the sand, and all sprang out and kissed the ground, and with tears of joyful satisfaction returned thanks to God our Lord for all his incomparable goodness to us on our voyage. We took out of the boat the provisions it contained, and drew it up on the shore, and then climbed a long way up the mountain, for even there we could not feel easy in our hearts, or persuade ourselves that it was Christian soil that was now under our feet.\n\nThe dawn came, more slowly, I think, than we could have wished; we completed the ascent in order to see if from the summit any habitation or any shepherds’ huts could be discovered, but strain our eyes as we might, neither dwelling, nor human being, nor path nor road could we perceive. However, we determined to push on farther, as it could not but be that ere long we must see some one who could tell us where we were. But what distressed me most was to see Zoraida going on foot over that rough ground; for though I once carried her on my shoulders, she was more wearied by my weariness than rested by the rest; and so she would never again allow me to undergo the exertion, and went on very patiently and cheerfully, while I led her by the hand. We had gone rather less than a quarter of a league when the sound of a little bell fell on our ears, a clear proof that there were flocks hard by, and looking about carefully to see if any were within view, we observed a young shepherd tranquilly and unsuspiciously trimming a stick with his knife at the foot of a cork tree. We called to him, and he, raising his head, sprang nimbly to his feet, for, as we afterwards learned, the first who presented themselves to his sight were the renegade and Zoraida, and seeing them in Moorish dress he imagined that all the Moors of Barbary were upon him; and plunging with marvellous swiftness into the thicket in front of him, he began to raise a prodigious outcry, exclaiming, “The Moors--the Moors have landed! To arms, to arms!” We were all thrown into perplexity by these cries, not knowing what to do; but reflecting that the shouts of the shepherd would raise the country and that the mounted coast-guard would come at once to see what was the matter, we agreed that the renegade must strip off his Turkish garments and put on a captive’s jacket or coat which one of our party gave him at once, though he himself was reduced to his shirt; and so commending ourselves to God, we followed the same road which we saw the shepherd take, expecting every moment that the coast-guard would be down upon us. Nor did our expectation deceive us, for two hours had not passed when, coming out of the brushwood into the open ground, we perceived some fifty mounted men swiftly approaching us at a hand-gallop. As soon as we saw them we stood still, waiting for them; but as they came close and, instead of the Moors they were in quest of, saw a set of poor Christians, they were taken aback, and one of them asked if it could be we who were the cause of the shepherd having raised the call to arms. I said “Yes,” and as I was about to explain to him what had occurred, and whence we came and who we were, one of the Christians of our party recognised the horseman who had put the question to us, and before I could say anything more he exclaimed:\n\n“Thanks be to God, sirs, for bringing us to such good quarters; for, if I do not deceive myself, the ground we stand on is that of Velez Malaga unless, indeed, all my years of captivity have made me unable to recollect that you, señor, who ask who we are, are Pedro de Bustamante, my uncle.”\n\nThe Christian captive had hardly uttered these words, when the horseman threw himself off his horse, and ran to embrace the young man, crying:\n\n“Nephew of my soul and life! I recognise thee now; and long have I mourned thee as dead, I, and my sister, thy mother, and all thy kin that are still alive, and whom God has been pleased to preserve that they may enjoy the happiness of seeing thee. We knew long since that thou wert in Algiers, and from the appearance of thy garments and those of all this company, I conclude that ye have had a miraculous restoration to liberty.”\n\n“It is true,” replied the young man, “and by-and-by we will tell you all.”\n\nAs soon as the horsemen understood that we were Christian captives, they dismounted from their horses, and each offered his to carry us to the city of Velez Malaga, which was a league and a half distant. Some of them went to bring the boat to the city, we having told them where we had left it; others took us up behind them, and Zoraida was placed on the horse of the young man’s uncle. The whole town came out to meet us, for they had by this time heard of our arrival from one who had gone on in advance. They were not astonished to see liberated captives or captive Moors, for people on that coast are well used to see both one and the other; but they were astonished at the beauty of Zoraida, which was just then heightened, as well by the exertion of travelling as by joy at finding herself on Christian soil, and relieved of all fear of being lost; for this had brought such a glow upon her face, that unless my affection for her were deceiving me, I would venture to say that there was not a more beautiful creature in the world--at least, that I had ever seen. We went straight to the church to return thanks to God for the mercies we had received, and when Zoraida entered it she said there were faces there like Lela Marien’s. We told her they were her images; and as well as he could the renegade explained to her what they meant, that she might adore them as if each of them were the very same Lela Marien that had spoken to her; and she, having great intelligence and a quick and clear instinct, understood at once all he said to her about them. Thence they took us away and distributed us all in different houses in the town; but as for the renegade, Zoraida, and myself, the Christian who came with us brought us to the house of his parents, who had a fair share of the gifts of fortune, and treated us with as much kindness as they did their own son.\n\nWe remained six days in Velez, at the end of which the renegade, having informed himself of all that was requisite for him to do, set out for the city of Granada to restore himself to the sacred bosom of the Church through the medium of the Holy Inquisition. The other released captives took their departures, each the way that seemed best to him, and Zoraida and I were left alone, with nothing more than the crowns which the courtesy of the Frenchman had bestowed upon Zoraida, out of which I bought the beast on which she rides; and, I for the present attending her as her father and squire and not as her husband, we are now going to ascertain if my father is living, or if any of my brothers has had better fortune than mine has been; though, as Heaven has made me the companion of Zoraida, I think no other lot could be assigned to me, however happy, that I would rather have. The patience with which she endures the hardships that poverty brings with it, and the eagerness she shows to become a Christian, are such that they fill me with admiration, and bind me to serve her all my life; though the happiness I feel in seeing myself hers, and her mine, is disturbed and marred by not knowing whether I shall find any corner to shelter her in my own country, or whether time and death may not have made such changes in the fortunes and lives of my father and brothers, that I shall hardly find anyone who knows me, if they are not alive.\n\nI have no more of my story to tell you, gentlemen; whether it be an interesting or a curious one let your better judgments decide; all I can say is I would gladly have told it to you more briefly; although my fear of wearying you has made me leave out more than one circumstance.\n\n# CHAPTER XLII.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF WHAT FURTHER TOOK PLACE IN THE INN, AND OF SEVERAL OTHER THINGS WORTH KNOWING\nWith these words the captive held his peace, and Don Fernando said to him, “In truth, captain, the manner in which you have related this remarkable adventure has been such as befitted the novelty and strangeness of the matter. The whole story is curious and uncommon, and abounds with incidents that fill the hearers with wonder and astonishment; and so great is the pleasure we have found in listening to it that we should be glad if it were to begin again, even though to-morrow were to find us still occupied with the same tale.” And while he said this Cardenio and the rest of them offered to be of service to him in any way that lay in their power, and in words and language so kindly and sincere that the captain was much gratified by their good-will. In particular Don Fernando offered, if he would go back with him, to get his brother the marquis to become godfather at the baptism of Zoraida, and on his own part to provide him with the means of making his appearance in his own country with the credit and comfort he was entitled to. For all this the captive returned thanks very courteously, although he would not accept any of their generous offers.\n\nBy this time night closed in, and as it did, there came up to the inn a coach attended by some men on horseback, who demanded accommodation; to which the landlady replied that there was not a hand’s breadth of the whole inn unoccupied.\n\n“Still, for all that,” said one of those who had entered on horseback, “room must be found for his lordship the Judge here.”\n\nAt this name the landlady was taken aback, and said, “Señor, the fact is I have no beds; but if his lordship the Judge carries one with him, as no doubt he does, let him come in and welcome; for my husband and I will give up our room to accommodate his worship.”\n\n“Very good, so be it,” said the squire; but in the meantime a man had got out of the coach whose dress indicated at a glance the office and post he held, for the long robe with ruffled sleeves that he wore showed that he was, as his servant said, a Judge of appeal. He led by the hand a young girl in a travelling dress, apparently about sixteen years of age, and of such a high-bred air, so beautiful and so graceful, that all were filled with admiration when she made her appearance, and but for having seen Dorothea, Luscinda, and Zoraida, who were there in the inn, they would have fancied that a beauty like that of this maiden’s would have been hard to find. Don Quixote was present at the entrance of the Judge with the young lady, and as soon as he saw him he said, “Your worship may with confidence enter and take your ease in this castle; for though the accommodation be scanty and poor, there are no quarters so cramped or inconvenient that they cannot make room for arms and letters; above all if arms and letters have beauty for a guide and leader, as letters represented by your worship have in this fair maiden, to whom not only ought castles to throw themselves open and yield themselves up, but rocks should rend themselves asunder and mountains divide and bow themselves down to give her a reception. Enter, your worship, I say, into this paradise, for here you will find stars and suns to accompany the heaven your worship brings with you, here you will find arms in their supreme excellence, and beauty in its highest perfection.”\n\nThe Judge was struck with amazement at the language of Don Quixote, whom he scrutinized very carefully, no less astonished by his figure than by his talk; and before he could find words to answer him he had a fresh surprise, when he saw opposite to him Luscinda, Dorothea, and Zoraida, who, having heard of the new guests and of the beauty of the young lady, had come to see her and welcome her; Don Fernando, Cardenio, and the curate, however, greeted him in a more intelligible and polished style. In short, the Judge made his entrance in a state of bewilderment, as well with what he saw as what he heard, and the fair ladies of the inn gave the fair damsel a cordial welcome. On the whole he could perceive that all who were there were people of quality; but with the figure, countenance, and bearing of Don Quixote he was at his wits’ end; and all civilities having been exchanged, and the accommodation of the inn inquired into, it was settled, as it had been before settled, that all the women should retire to the garret that has been already mentioned, and that the men should remain outside as if to guard them; the Judge, therefore, was very well pleased to allow his daughter, for such the damsel was, to go with the ladies, which she did very willingly; and with part of the host’s narrow bed and half of what the Judge had brought with him, they made a more comfortable arrangement for the night than they had expected.\n\nThe captive, whose heart had leaped within him the instant he saw the Judge, telling him somehow that this was his brother, asked one of the servants who accompanied him what his name was, and whether he knew from what part of the country he came. The servant replied that he was called the Licentiate Juan Perez de Viedma, and that he had heard it said he came from a village in the mountains of Leon. From this statement, and what he himself had seen, he felt convinced that this was his brother who had adopted letters by his father’s advice; and excited and rejoiced, he called Don Fernando and Cardenio and the curate aside, and told them how the matter stood, assuring them that the judge was his brother. The servant had further informed him that he was now going to the Indies with the appointment of Judge of the Supreme Court of Mexico; and he had learned, likewise, that the young lady was his daughter, whose mother had died in giving birth to her, and that he was very rich in consequence of the dowry left to him with the daughter. He asked their advice as to what means he should adopt to make himself known, or to ascertain beforehand whether, when he had made himself known, his brother, seeing him so poor, would be ashamed of him, or would receive him with a warm heart.\n\n“Leave it to me to find out that,” said the curate; “though there is no reason for supposing, señor captain, that you will not be kindly received, because the worth and wisdom that your brother’s bearing shows him to possess do not make it likely that he will prove haughty or insensible, or that he will not know how to estimate the accidents of fortune at their proper value.”\n\n“Still,” said the captain, “I would not make myself known abruptly, but in some indirect way.”\n\n“I have told you already,” said the curate, “that I will manage it in a way to satisfy us all.”\n\nBy this time supper was ready, and they all took their seats at the table, except the captive, and the ladies, who supped by themselves in their own room. In the middle of supper the curate said:\n\n“I had a comrade of your worship’s name, Señor Judge, in Constantinople, where I was a captive for several years, and that same comrade was one of the stoutest soldiers and captains in the whole Spanish infantry; but he had as large a share of misfortune as he had of gallantry and courage.”\n\n“And how was the captain called, señor?” asked the Judge.\n\n“He was called Ruy Perez de Viedma,” replied the curate, “and he was born in a village in the mountains of Leon; and he mentioned a circumstance connected with his father and his brothers which, had it not been told me by so truthful a man as he was, I should have set down as one of those fables the old women tell over the fire in winter; for he said his father had divided his property among his three sons and had addressed words of advice to them sounder than any of Cato’s. But I can say this much, that the choice he made of going to the wars was attended with such success, that by his gallant conduct and courage, and without any help save his own merit, he rose in a few years to be captain of infantry, and to see himself on the high-road and in position to be given the command of a corps before long; but Fortune was against him, for where he might have expected her favour he lost it, and with it his liberty, on that glorious day when so many recovered theirs, at the battle of Lepanto. I lost mine at the Goletta, and after a variety of adventures we found ourselves comrades at Constantinople. Thence he went to Algiers, where he met with one of the most extraordinary adventures that ever befell anyone in the world.”\n\nHere the curate went on to relate briefly his brother’s adventure with Zoraida; to all which the Judge gave such an attentive hearing that he never before had been so much of a hearer. The curate, however, only went so far as to describe how the Frenchmen plundered those who were in the boat, and the poverty and distress in which his comrade and the fair Moor were left, of whom he said he had not been able to learn what became of them, or whether they had reached Spain, or been carried to France by the Frenchmen.\n\nThe captain, standing a little to one side, was listening to all the curate said, and watching every movement of his brother, who, as soon as he perceived the curate had made an end of his story, gave a deep sigh and said with his eyes full of tears, “Oh, señor, if you only knew what news you have given me and how it comes home to me, making me show how I feel it with these tears that spring from my eyes in spite of all my worldly wisdom and self-restraint! That brave captain that you speak of is my eldest brother, who, being of a bolder and loftier mind than my other brother or myself, chose the honourable and worthy calling of arms, which was one of the three careers our father proposed to us, as your comrade mentioned in that fable you thought he was telling you. I followed that of letters, in which God and my own exertions have raised me to the position in which you see me. My second brother is in Peru, so wealthy that with what he has sent to my father and to me he has fully repaid the portion he took with him, and has even furnished my father’s hands with the means of gratifying his natural generosity, while I too have been enabled to pursue my studies in a more becoming and creditable fashion, and so to attain my present standing. My father is still alive, though dying with anxiety to hear of his eldest son, and he prays God unceasingly that death may not close his eyes until he has looked upon those of his son; but with regard to him what surprises me is, that having so much common sense as he had, he should have neglected to give any intelligence about himself, either in his troubles and sufferings, or in his prosperity, for if his father or any of us had known of his condition he need not have waited for that miracle of the reed to obtain his ransom; but what now disquiets me is the uncertainty whether those Frenchmen may have restored him to liberty, or murdered him to hide the robbery. All this will make me continue my journey, not with the satisfaction in which I began it, but in the deepest melancholy and sadness. Oh dear brother! that I only knew where thou art now, and I would hasten to seek thee out and deliver thee from thy sufferings, though it were to cost me suffering myself! Oh that I could bring news to our old father that thou art alive, even wert thou the deepest dungeon of Barbary; for his wealth and my brother’s and mine would rescue thee thence! Oh beautiful and generous Zoraida, that I could repay thy good goodness to a brother! That I could be present at the new birth of thy soul, and at thy bridal that would give us all such happiness!”\n\nAll this and more the Judge uttered with such deep emotion at the news he had received of his brother that all who heard him shared in it, showing their sympathy with his sorrow. The curate, seeing, then, how well he had succeeded in carrying out his purpose and the captain’s wishes, had no desire to keep them unhappy any longer, so he rose from the table and going into the room where Zoraida was he took her by the hand, Luscinda, Dorothea, and the Judge’s daughter following her. The captain was waiting to see what the curate would do, when the latter, taking him with the other hand, advanced with both of them to where the Judge and the other gentlemen were and said, “Let your tears cease to flow, Señor Judge, and the wish of your heart be gratified as fully as you could desire, for you have before you your worthy brother and your good sister-in-law. He whom you see here is the Captain Viedma, and this is the fair Moor who has been so good to him. The Frenchmen I told you of have reduced them to the state of poverty you see that you may show the generosity of your kind heart.”\n\nThe captain ran to embrace his brother, who placed both hands on his breast so as to have a good look at him, holding him a little way off but as soon as he had fully recognised him he clasped him in his arms so closely, shedding such tears of heartfelt joy, that most of those present could not but join in them. The words the brothers exchanged, the emotion they showed can scarcely be imagined, I fancy, much less put down in writing. They told each other in a few words the events of their lives; they showed the true affection of brothers in all its strength; then the judge embraced Zoraida, putting all he possessed at her disposal; then he made his daughter embrace her, and the fair Christian and the lovely Moor drew fresh tears from every eye. And there was Don Quixote observing all these strange proceedings attentively without uttering a word, and attributing the whole to chimeras of knight-errantry. Then they agreed that the captain and Zoraida should return with his brother to Seville, and send news to his father of his having been delivered and found, so as to enable him to come and be present at the marriage and baptism of Zoraida, for it was impossible for the Judge to put off his journey, as he was informed that in a month from that time the fleet was to sail from Seville for New Spain, and to miss the passage would have been a great inconvenience to him. In short, everybody was well pleased and glad at the captive’s good fortune; and as now almost two-thirds of the night were past, they resolved to retire to rest for the remainder of it. Don Quixote offered to mount guard over the castle lest they should be attacked by some giant or other malevolent scoundrel, covetous of the great treasure of beauty the castle contained. Those who understood him returned him thanks for this service, and they gave the Judge an account of his extraordinary humour, with which he was not a little amused. Sancho Panza alone was fuming at the lateness of the hour for retiring to rest; and he of all was the one that made himself most comfortable, as he stretched himself on the trappings of his ass, which, as will be told farther on, cost him so dear.\n\nThe ladies, then, having retired to their chamber, and the others having disposed themselves with as little discomfort as they could, Don Quixote sallied out of the inn to act as sentinel of the castle as he had promised. It happened, however, that a little before the approach of dawn a voice so musical and sweet reached the ears of the ladies that it forced them all to listen attentively, but especially Dorothea, who had been awake, and by whose side Dona Clara de Viedma, for so the Judge’s daughter was called, lay sleeping. No one could imagine who it was that sang so sweetly, and the voice was unaccompanied by any instrument. At one moment it seemed to them as if the singer were in the courtyard, at another in the stable; and as they were all attention, wondering, Cardenio came to the door and said, “Listen, whoever is not asleep, and you will hear a muleteer’s voice that enchants as it chants.”\n\n“We are listening to it already, señor,” said Dorothea; on which Cardenio went away; and Dorothea, giving all her attention to it, made out the words of the song to be these:\n\n# CHAPTER XLIII.\n\nWHEREIN IS RELATED THE PLEASANT STORY OF THE MULETEER, TOGETHER WITH OTHER STRANGE THINGS THAT CAME TO PASS IN THE INN\n\nAh me, Love’s mariner am I On Love’s deep ocean sailing; I know not where the haven lies, I dare not hope to gain it.\n\nOne solitary distant star Is all I have to guide me, A brighter orb than those of old That Palinurus lighted.\n\nAnd vaguely drifting am I borne, I know not where it leads me; I fix my gaze on it alone, Of all beside it heedless.\n\nBut over-cautious prudery, And coyness cold and cruel, When most I need it, these, like clouds, Its longed-for light refuse me.\n\nBright star, goal of my yearning eyes As thou above me beamest, When thou shalt hide thee from my sight I’ll know that death is near me.\n\nThe singer had got so far when it struck Dorothea that it was not fair to let Clara miss hearing such a sweet voice, so, shaking her from side to side, she woke her, saying:\n\n“Forgive me, child, for waking thee, but I do so that thou mayest have the pleasure of hearing the best voice thou hast ever heard, perhaps, in all thy life.”\n\nClara awoke quite drowsy, and not understanding at the moment what Dorothea said, asked her what it was; she repeated what she had said, and Clara became attentive at once; but she had hardly heard two lines, as the singer continued, when a strange trembling seized her, as if she were suffering from a severe attack of quartan ague, and throwing her arms round Dorothea she said:\n\n“Ah, dear lady of my soul and life! why did you wake me? The greatest kindness fortune could do me now would be to close my eyes and ears so as neither to see or hear that unhappy musician.”\n\n“What art thou talking about, child?” said Dorothea. “Why, they say this singer is a muleteer!”\n\n“Nay, he is the lord of many places,” replied Clara, “and that one in my heart which he holds so firmly shall never be taken from him, unless he be willing to surrender it.”\n\nDorothea was amazed at the ardent language of the girl, for it seemed to be far beyond such experience of life as her tender years gave any promise of, so she said to her:\n\n“You speak in such a way that I cannot understand you, Señora Clara; explain yourself more clearly, and tell me what is this you are saying about hearts and places and this musician whose voice has so moved you? But do not tell me anything now; I do not want to lose the pleasure I get from listening to the singer by giving my attention to your transports, for I perceive he is beginning to sing a new strain and a new air.”\n\n“Let him, in Heaven’s name,” returned Clara; and not to hear him she stopped both ears with her hands, at which Dorothea was again surprised; but turning her attention to the song she found that it ran in this fashion:\n\nSweet Hope, my stay, That onward to the goal of thy intent Dost make thy way, Heedless of hindrance or impediment, Have thou no fear If at each step thou findest death is near.\n\nNo victory, No joy of triumph doth the faint heart know; Unblest is he That a bold front to Fortune dares not show, But soul and sense In bondage yieldeth up to indolence.\n\nIf Love his wares Do dearly sell, his right must be contest; What gold compares With that whereon his stamp he hath imprest? And all men know What costeth little that we rate but low.\n\nLove resolute Knows not the word “impossibility;” And though my suit Beset by endless obstacles I see, Yet no despair Shall hold me bound to earth while heaven is there.\n\nHere the voice ceased and Clara’s sobs began afresh, all which excited Dorothea’s curiosity to know what could be the cause of singing so sweet and weeping so bitter, so she again asked her what it was she was going to say before. On this Clara, afraid that Luscinda might overhear her, winding her arms tightly round Dorothea put her mouth so close to her ear that she could speak without fear of being heard by anyone else, and said:\n\n“This singer, dear señora, is the son of a gentleman of Aragon, lord of two villages, who lives opposite my father’s house at Madrid; and though my father had curtains to the windows of his house in winter, and lattice-work in summer, in some way--I know not how--this gentleman, who was pursuing his studies, saw me, whether in church or elsewhere, I cannot tell, and, in fact, fell in love with me, and gave me to know it from the windows of his house, with so many signs and tears that I was forced to believe him, and even to love him, without knowing what it was he wanted of me. One of the signs he used to make me was to link one hand in the other, to show me he wished to marry me; and though I should have been glad if that could be, being alone and motherless I knew not whom to open my mind to, and so I left it as it was, showing him no favour, except when my father, and his too, were from home, to raise the curtain or the lattice a little and let him see me plainly, at which he would show such delight that he seemed as if he were going mad. Meanwhile the time for my father’s departure arrived, which he became aware of, but not from me, for I had never been able to tell him of it. He fell sick, of grief I believe, and so the day we were going away I could not see him to take farewell of him, were it only with the eyes. But after we had been two days on the road, on entering the posada of a village a day’s journey from this, I saw him at the inn door in the dress of a muleteer, and so well disguised, that if I did not carry his image graven on my heart it would have been impossible for me to recognise him. But I knew him, and I was surprised, and glad; he watched me, unsuspected by my father, from whom he always hides himself when he crosses my path on the road, or in the posadas where we halt; and, as I know what he is, and reflect that for love of me he makes this journey on foot in all this hardship, I am ready to die of sorrow; and where he sets foot there I set my eyes. I know not with what object he has come; or how he could have got away from his father, who loves him beyond measure, having no other heir, and because he deserves it, as you will perceive when you see him. And moreover, I can tell you, all that he sings is out of his own head; for I have heard them say he is a great scholar and poet; and what is more, every time I see him or hear him sing I tremble all over, and am terrified lest my father should recognise him and come to know of our loves. I have never spoken a word to him in my life; and for all that I love him so that I could not live without him. This, dear señora, is all I have to tell you about the musician whose voice has delighted you so much; and from it alone you might easily perceive he is no muleteer, but a lord of hearts and towns, as I told you already.”\n\n“Say no more, Dona Clara,” said Dorothea at this, at the same time kissing her a thousand times over, “say no more, I tell you, but wait till day comes; when I trust in God to arrange this affair of yours so that it may have the happy ending such an innocent beginning deserves.”\n\n“Ah, señora,” said Dona Clara, “what end can be hoped for when his father is of such lofty position, and so wealthy, that he would think I was not fit to be even a servant to his son, much less wife? And as to marrying without the knowledge of my father, I would not do it for all the world. I would not ask anything more than that this youth should go back and leave me; perhaps with not seeing him, and the long distance we shall have to travel, the pain I suffer now may become easier; though I daresay the remedy I propose will do me very little good. I don’t know how the devil this has come about, or how this love I have for him got in; I such a young girl, and he such a mere boy; for I verily believe we are both of an age, and I am not sixteen yet; for I will be sixteen Michaelmas Day, next, my father says.”\n\nDorothea could not help laughing to hear how like a child Dona Clara spoke. “Let us go to sleep now, señora,” said she, “for the little of the night that I fancy is left to us: God will soon send us daylight, and we will set all to rights, or it will go hard with me.”\n\nWith this they fell asleep, and deep silence reigned all through the inn. The only persons not asleep were the landlady’s daughter and her servant Maritornes, who, knowing the weak point of Don Quixote’s humour, and that he was outside the inn mounting guard in armour and on horseback, resolved, the pair of them, to play some trick upon him, or at any rate to amuse themselves for a while by listening to his nonsense. As it so happened there was not a window in the whole inn that looked outwards except a hole in the wall of a straw-loft through which they used to throw out the straw. At this hole the two demi-damsels posted themselves, and observed Don Quixote on his horse, leaning on his pike and from time to time sending forth such deep and doleful sighs, that he seemed to pluck up his soul by the roots with each of them; and they could hear him, too, saying in a soft, tender, loving tone, “Oh my lady Dulcinea del Toboso, perfection of all beauty, summit and crown of discretion, treasure house of grace, depositary of virtue, and finally, ideal of all that is good, honourable, and delectable in this world! What is thy grace doing now? Art thou, perchance, mindful of thy enslaved knight who of his own free will hath exposed himself to so great perils, and all to serve thee? Give me tidings of her, oh luminary of the three faces! Perhaps at this moment, envious of hers, thou art regarding her, either as she paces to and fro some gallery of her sumptuous palaces, or leans over some balcony, meditating how, whilst preserving her purity and greatness, she may mitigate the tortures this wretched heart of mine endures for her sake, what glory should recompense my sufferings, what repose my toil, and lastly what death my life, and what reward my services? And thou, oh sun, that art now doubtless harnessing thy steeds in haste to rise betimes and come forth to see my lady; when thou seest her I entreat of thee to salute her on my behalf: but have a care, when thou shalt see her and salute her, that thou kiss not her face; for I shall be more jealous of thee than thou wert of that light-footed ingrate that made thee sweat and run so on the plains of Thessaly, or on the banks of the Peneus (for I do not exactly recollect where it was thou didst run on that occasion) in thy jealousy and love.”\n\nDon Quixote had got so far in his pathetic speech when the landlady’s daughter began to signal to him, saying, “Señor, come over here, please.”\n\nAt these signals and voice Don Quixote turned his head and saw by the light of the moon, which then was in its full splendour, that some one was calling to him from the hole in the wall, which seemed to him to be a window, and what is more, with a gilt grating, as rich castles, such as he believed the inn to be, ought to have; and it immediately suggested itself to his imagination that, as on the former occasion, the fair damsel, the daughter of the lady of the castle, overcome by love for him, was once more endeavouring to win his affections; and with this idea, not to show himself discourteous, or ungrateful, he turned Rocinante’s head and approached the hole, and as he perceived the two wenches he said:\n\n“I pity you, beauteous lady, that you should have directed your thoughts of love to a quarter from whence it is impossible that such a return can be made to you as is due to your great merit and gentle birth, for which you must not blame this unhappy knight-errant whom love renders incapable of submission to any other than her whom, the first moment his eyes beheld her, he made absolute mistress of his soul. Forgive me, noble lady, and retire to your apartment, and do not, by any further declaration of your passion, compel me to show myself more ungrateful; and if, of the love you bear me, you should find that there is anything else in my power wherein I can gratify you, provided it be not love itself, demand it of me; for I swear to you by that sweet absent enemy of mine to grant it this instant, though it be that you require of me a lock of Medusa’s hair, which was all snakes, or even the very beams of the sun shut up in a vial.”\n\n“My mistress wants nothing of that sort, sir knight,” said Maritornes at this.\n\n“What then, discreet dame, is it that your mistress wants?” replied Don Quixote.\n\n“Only one of your fair hands,” said Maritornes, “to enable her to vent over it the great passion passion which has brought her to this loophole, so much to the risk of her honour; for if the lord her father had heard her, the least slice he would cut off her would be her ear.”\n\n“I should like to see that tried,” said Don Quixote; “but he had better beware of that, if he does not want to meet the most disastrous end that ever father in the world met for having laid hands on the tender limbs of a love-stricken daughter.”\n\nMaritornes felt sure that Don Quixote would present the hand she had asked, and making up her mind what to do, she got down from the hole and went into the stable, where she took the halter of Sancho Panza’s ass, and in all haste returned to the hole, just as Don Quixote had planted himself standing on Rocinante’s saddle in order to reach the grated window where he supposed the lovelorn damsel to be; and giving her his hand, he said, “Lady, take this hand, or rather this scourge of the evil-doers of the earth; take, I say, this hand which no other hand of woman has ever touched, not even hers who has complete possession of my entire body. I present it to you, not that you may kiss it, but that you may observe the contexture of the sinews, the close network of the muscles, the breadth and capacity of the veins, whence you may infer what must be the strength of the arm that has such a hand.”\n\n“That we shall see presently,” said Maritornes, and making a running knot on the halter, she passed it over his wrist and coming down from the hole tied the other end very firmly to the bolt of the door of the straw-loft.\n\nDon Quixote, feeling the roughness of the rope on his wrist, exclaimed, “Your grace seems to be grating rather than caressing my hand; treat it not so harshly, for it is not to blame for the offence my resolution has given you, nor is it just to wreak all your vengeance on so small a part; remember that one who loves so well should not revenge herself so cruelly.”\n\nBut there was nobody now to listen to these words of Don Quixote’s, for as soon as Maritornes had tied him she and the other made off, ready to die with laughing, leaving him fastened in such a way that it was impossible for him to release himself.\n\nHe was, as has been said, standing on Rocinante, with his arm passed through the hole and his wrist tied to the bolt of the door, and in mighty fear and dread of being left hanging by the arm if Rocinante were to stir one side or the other; so he did not dare to make the least movement, although from the patience and imperturbable disposition of Rocinante, he had good reason to expect that he would stand without budging for a whole century. Finding himself fast, then, and that the ladies had retired, he began to fancy that all this was done by enchantment, as on the former occasion when in that same castle that enchanted Moor of a carrier had belaboured him; and he cursed in his heart his own want of sense and judgment in venturing to enter the castle again, after having come off so badly the first time; it being a settled point with knights-errant that when they have tried an adventure, and have not succeeded in it, it is a sign that it is not reserved for them but for others, and that therefore they need not try it again. Nevertheless he pulled his arm to see if he could release himself, but it had been made so fast that all his efforts were in vain. It is true he pulled it gently lest Rocinante should move, but try as he might to seat himself in the saddle, he had nothing for it but to stand upright or pull his hand off. Then it was he wished for the sword of Amadis, against which no enchantment whatever had any power; then he cursed his ill fortune; then he magnified the loss the world would sustain by his absence while he remained there enchanted, for that he believed he was beyond all doubt; then he once more took to thinking of his beloved Dulcinea del Toboso; then he called to his worthy squire Sancho Panza, who, buried in sleep and stretched upon the pack-saddle of his ass, was oblivious, at that moment, of the mother that bore him; then he called upon the sages Lirgandeo and Alquife to come to his aid; then he invoked his good friend Urganda to succour him; and then, at last, morning found him in such a state of desperation and perplexity that he was bellowing like a bull, for he had no hope that day would bring any relief to his suffering, which he believed would last for ever, inasmuch as he was enchanted; and of this he was convinced by seeing that Rocinante never stirred, much or little, and he felt persuaded that he and his horse were to remain in this state, without eating or drinking or sleeping, until the malign influence of the stars was overpast, or until some other more sage enchanter should disenchant him.\n\nBut he was very much deceived in this conclusion, for daylight had hardly begun to appear when there came up to the inn four men on horseback, well equipped and accoutred, with firelocks across their saddle-bows. They called out and knocked loudly at the gate of the inn, which was still shut; on seeing which, Don Quixote, even there where he was, did not forget to act as sentinel, and said in a loud and imperious tone, “Knights, or squires, or whatever ye be, ye have no right to knock at the gates of this castle; for it is plain enough that they who are within are either asleep, or else are not in the habit of throwing open the fortress until the sun’s rays are spread over the whole surface of the earth. Withdraw to a distance, and wait till it is broad daylight, and then we shall see whether it will be proper or not to open to you.”\n\n“What the devil fortress or castle is this,” said one, “to make us stand on such ceremony? If you are the innkeeper bid them open to us; we are travellers who only want to feed our horses and go on, for we are in haste.”\n\n“Do you think, gentlemen, that I look like an innkeeper?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“I don’t know what you look like,” replied the other; “but I know that you are talking nonsense when you call this inn a castle.”\n\n“A castle it is,” returned Don Quixote, “nay, more, one of the best in this whole province, and it has within it people who have had the sceptre in the hand and the crown on the head.”\n\n“It would be better if it were the other way,” said the traveller, “the sceptre on the head and the crown in the hand; but if so, may be there is within some company of players, with whom it is a common thing to have those crowns and sceptres you speak of; for in such a small inn as this, and where such silence is kept, I do not believe any people entitled to crowns and sceptres can have taken up their quarters.”\n\n“You know but little of the world,” returned Don Quixote, “since you are ignorant of what commonly occurs in knight-errantry.”\n\nBut the comrades of the spokesman, growing weary of the dialogue with Don Quixote, renewed their knocks with great vehemence, so much so that the host, and not only he but everybody in the inn, awoke, and he got up to ask who knocked. It happened at this moment that one of the horses of the four who were seeking admittance went to smell Rocinante, who melancholy, dejected, and with drooping ears stood motionless, supporting his sorely stretched master; and as he was, after all, flesh, though he looked as if he were made of wood, he could not help giving way and in return smelling the one who had come to offer him attentions. But he had hardly moved at all when Don Quixote lost his footing; and slipping off the saddle, he would have come to the ground, but for being suspended by the arm, which caused him such agony that he believed either his wrist would be cut through or his arm torn off; and he hung so near the ground that he could just touch it with his feet, which was all the worse for him; for, finding how little was wanted to enable him to plant his feet firmly, he struggled and stretched himself as much as he could to gain a footing; just like those undergoing the torture of the strappado, when they are fixed at “touch and no touch,” who aggravate their own sufferings by their violent efforts to stretch themselves, deceived by the hope which makes them fancy that with a very little more they will reach the ground.\n\n# IN WHICH ARE CONTINUED THE UNHEARD-OF ADVENTURES OF THE INN\n\nSo loud, in fact, were the shouts of Don Quixote, that the landlord opening the gate of the inn in all haste, came out in dismay, and ran to see who was uttering such cries, and those who were outside joined him. Maritornes, who had been by this time roused up by the same outcry, suspecting what it was, ran to the loft and, without anyone seeing her, untied the halter by which Don Quixote was suspended, and down he came to the ground in the sight of the landlord and the travellers, who approaching asked him what was the matter with him that he shouted so. He without replying a word took the rope off his wrist, and rising to his feet leaped upon Rocinante, braced his buckler on his arm, put his lance in rest, and making a considerable circuit of the plain came back at a half-gallop exclaiming:\n\n“Whoever shall say that I have been enchanted with just cause, provided my lady the Princess Micomicona grants me permission to do so, I give him the lie, challenge him and defy him to single combat.”\n\nThe newly arrived travellers were amazed at the words of Don Quixote; but the landlord removed their surprise by telling them who he was, and not to mind him as he was out of his senses. They then asked the landlord if by any chance a youth of about fifteen years of age had come to that inn, one dressed like a muleteer, and of such and such an appearance, describing that of Dona Clara’s lover. The landlord replied that there were so many people in the inn he had not noticed the person they were inquiring for; but one of them observing the coach in which the Judge had come, said, “He is here no doubt, for this is the coach he is following: let one of us stay at the gate, and the rest go in to look for him; or indeed it would be as well if one of us went round the inn, lest he should escape over the wall of the yard.” “So be it,” said another; and while two of them went in, one remained at the gate and the other made the circuit of the inn; observing all which, the landlord was unable to conjecture for what reason they were taking all these precautions, though he understood they were looking for the youth whose description they had given him.\n\nIt was by this time broad daylight; and for that reason, as well as in consequence of the noise Don Quixote had made, everybody was awake and up, but particularly Dona Clara and Dorothea; for they had been able to sleep but badly that night, the one from agitation at having her lover so near her, the other from curiosity to see him. Don Quixote, when he saw that not one of the four travellers took any notice of him or replied to his challenge, was furious and ready to die with indignation and wrath; and if he could have found in the ordinances of chivalry that it was lawful for a knight-errant to undertake or engage in another enterprise, when he had plighted his word and faith not to involve himself in any until he had made an end of the one to which he was pledged, he would have attacked the whole of them, and would have made them return an answer in spite of themselves. But considering that it would not become him, nor be right, to begin any new emprise until he had established Micomicona in her kingdom, he was constrained to hold his peace and wait quietly to see what would be the upshot of the proceedings of those same travellers; one of whom found the youth they were seeking lying asleep by the side of a muleteer, without a thought of anyone coming in search of him, much less finding him.\n\nThe man laid hold of him by the arm, saying, “It becomes you well indeed, Señor Don Luis, to be in the dress you wear, and well the bed in which I find you agrees with the luxury in which your mother reared you.”\n\nThe youth rubbed his sleepy eyes and stared for a while at him who held him, but presently recognised him as one of his father’s servants, at which he was so taken aback that for some time he could not find or utter a word; while the servant went on to say, “There is nothing for it now, Señor Don Luis, but to submit quietly and return home, unless it is your wish that my lord, your father, should take his departure for the other world, for nothing else can be the consequence of the grief he is in at your absence.”\n\n“But how did my father know that I had gone this road and in this dress?” said Don Luis.\n\n“It was a student to whom you confided your intentions,” answered the servant, “that disclosed them, touched with pity at the distress he saw your father suffer on missing you; he therefore despatched four of his servants in quest of you, and here we all are at your service, better pleased than you can imagine that we shall return so soon and be able to restore you to those eyes that so yearn for you.”\n\n“That shall be as I please, or as heaven orders,” returned Don Luis.\n\n“What can you please or heaven order,” said the other, “except to agree to go back? Anything else is impossible.”\n\nAll this conversation between the two was overheard by the muleteer at whose side Don Luis lay, and rising, he went to report what had taken place to Don Fernando, Cardenio, and the others, who had by this time dressed themselves; and told them how the man had addressed the youth as “Don,” and what words had passed, and how he wanted him to return to his father, which the youth was unwilling to do. With this, and what they already knew of the rare voice that heaven had bestowed upon him, they all felt very anxious to know more particularly who he was, and even to help him if it was attempted to employ force against him; so they hastened to where he was still talking and arguing with his servant. Dorothea at this instant came out of her room, followed by Dona Clara all in a tremor; and calling Cardenio aside, she told him in a few words the story of the musician and Dona Clara, and he at the same time told her what had happened, how his father’s servants had come in search of him; but in telling her so, he did not speak low enough but that Dona Clara heard what he said, at which she was so much agitated that had not Dorothea hastened to support her she would have fallen to the ground. Cardenio then bade Dorothea return to her room, as he would endeavour to make the whole matter right, and they did as he desired. All the four who had come in quest of Don Luis had now come into the inn and surrounded him, urging him to return and console his father at once and without a moment’s delay. He replied that he could not do so on any account until he had concluded some business in which his life, honour, and heart were at stake. The servants pressed him, saying that most certainly they would not return without him, and that they would take him away whether he liked it or not.\n\n“You shall not do that,” replied Don Luis, “unless you take me dead; though however you take me, it will be without life.”\n\nBy this time most of those in the inn had been attracted by the dispute, but particularly Cardenio, Don Fernando, his companions, the Judge, the curate, the barber, and Don Quixote; for he now considered there was no necessity for mounting guard over the castle any longer. Cardenio being already acquainted with the young man’s story, asked the men who wanted to take him away, what object they had in seeking to carry off this youth against his will.\n\n“Our object,” said one of the four, “is to save the life of his father, who is in danger of losing it through this gentleman’s disappearance.”\n\nUpon this Don Luis exclaimed, “There is no need to make my affairs public here; I am free, and I will return if I please; and if not, none of you shall compel me.”\n\n“Reason will compel your worship,” said the man, “and if it has no power over you, it has power over us, to make us do what we came for, and what it is our duty to do.”\n\n“Let us hear what the whole affair is about,” said the Judge at this; but the man, who knew him as a neighbour of theirs, replied, “Do you not know this gentleman, Señor Judge? He is the son of your neighbour, who has run away from his father’s house in a dress so unbecoming his rank, as your worship may perceive.”\n\nThe judge on this looked at him more carefully and recognised him, and embracing him said, “What folly is this, Señor Don Luis, or what can have been the cause that could have induced you to come here in this way, and in this dress, which so ill becomes your condition?”\n\nTears came into the eyes of the young man, and he was unable to utter a word in reply to the Judge, who told the four servants not to be uneasy, for all would be satisfactorily settled; and then taking Don Luis by the hand, he drew him aside and asked the reason of his having come there.\n\nBut while he was questioning him they heard a loud outcry at the gate of the inn, the cause of which was that two of the guests who had passed the night there, seeing everybody busy about finding out what it was the four men wanted, had conceived the idea of going off without paying what they owed; but the landlord, who minded his own affairs more than other people’s, caught them going out of the gate and demanded his reckoning, abusing them for their dishonesty with such language that he drove them to reply with their fists, and so they began to lay on him in such a style that the poor man was forced to cry out, and call for help. The landlady and her daughter could see no one more free to give aid than Don Quixote, and to him the daughter said, “Sir knight, by the virtue God has given you, help my poor father, for two wicked men are beating him to a mummy.”\n\nTo which Don Quixote very deliberately and phlegmatically replied, “Fair damsel, at the present moment your request is inopportune, for I am debarred from involving myself in any adventure until I have brought to a happy conclusion one to which my word has pledged me; but that which I can do for you is what I will now mention: run and tell your father to stand his ground as well as he can in this battle, and on no account to allow himself to be vanquished, while I go and request permission of the Princess Micomicona to enable me to succour him in his distress; and if she grants it, rest assured I will relieve him from it.”\n\n“Sinner that I am,” exclaimed Maritornes, who stood by; “before you have got your permission my master will be in the other world.”\n\n“Give me leave, señora, to obtain the permission I speak of,” returned Don Quixote; “and if I get it, it will matter very little if he is in the other world; for I will rescue him thence in spite of all the same world can do; or at any rate I will give you such a revenge over those who shall have sent him there that you will be more than moderately satisfied;” and without saying anything more he went and knelt before Dorothea, requesting her Highness in knightly and errant phrase to be pleased to grant him permission to aid and succour the castellan of that castle, who now stood in grievous jeopardy. The princess granted it graciously, and he at once, bracing his buckler on his arm and drawing his sword, hastened to the inn-gate, where the two guests were still handling the landlord roughly; but as soon as he reached the spot he stopped short and stood still, though Maritornes and the landlady asked him why he hesitated to help their master and husband.\n\n“I hesitate,” said Don Quixote, “because it is not lawful for me to draw sword against persons of squirely condition; but call my squire Sancho to me; for this defence and vengeance are his affair and business.”\n\nThus matters stood at the inn-gate, where there was a very lively exchange of fisticuffs and punches, to the sore damage of the landlord and to the wrath of Maritornes, the landlady, and her daughter, who were furious when they saw the pusillanimity of Don Quixote, and the hard treatment their master, husband and father was undergoing. But let us leave him there; for he will surely find some one to help him, and if not, let him suffer and hold his tongue who attempts more than his strength allows him to do; and let us go back fifty paces to see what Don Luis said in reply to the Judge whom we left questioning him privately as to his reasons for coming on foot and so meanly dressed.\n\nTo which the youth, pressing his hand in a way that showed his heart was troubled by some great sorrow, and shedding a flood of tears, made answer:\n\n“Señor, I have no more to tell you than that from the moment when, through heaven’s will and our being near neighbours, I first saw Dona Clara, your daughter and my lady, from that instant I made her the mistress of my will, and if yours, my true lord and father, offers no impediment, this very day she shall become my wife. For her I left my father’s house, and for her I assumed this disguise, to follow her whithersoever she may go, as the arrow seeks its mark or the sailor the pole-star. She knows nothing more of my passion than what she may have learned from having sometimes seen from a distance that my eyes were filled with tears. You know already, señor, the wealth and noble birth of my parents, and that I am their sole heir; if this be a sufficient inducement for you to venture to make me completely happy, accept me at once as your son; for if my father, influenced by other objects of his own, should disapprove of this happiness I have sought for myself, time has more power to alter and change things, than human will.”\n\nWith this the love-smitten youth was silent, while the Judge, after hearing him, was astonished, perplexed, and surprised, as well at the manner and intelligence with which Don Luis had confessed the secret of his heart, as at the position in which he found himself, not knowing what course to take in a matter so sudden and unexpected. All the answer, therefore, he gave him was to bid him to make his mind easy for the present, and arrange with his servants not to take him back that day, so that there might be time to consider what was best for all parties. Don Luis kissed his hands by force, nay, bathed them with his tears, in a way that would have touched a heart of marble, not to say that of the Judge, who, as a shrewd man, had already perceived how advantageous the marriage would be to his daughter; though, were it possible, he would have preferred that it should be brought about with the consent of the father of Don Luis, who he knew looked for a title for his son.\n\nThe guests had by this time made peace with the landlord, for, by persuasion and Don Quixote’s fair words more than by threats, they had paid him what he demanded, and the servants of Don Luis were waiting for the end of the conversation with the Judge and their master’s decision, when the devil, who never sleeps, contrived that the barber, from whom Don Quixote had taken Mambrino’s helmet, and Sancho Panza the trappings of his ass in exchange for those of his own, should at this instant enter the inn; which said barber, as he led his ass to the stable, observed Sancho Panza engaged in repairing something or other belonging to the pack-saddle; and the moment he saw it he knew it, and made bold to attack Sancho, exclaiming, “Ho, sir thief, I have caught you! hand over my basin and my pack-saddle, and all my trappings that you robbed me of.”\n\nSancho, finding himself so unexpectedly assailed, and hearing the abuse poured upon him, seized the pack-saddle with one hand, and with the other gave the barber a cuff that bathed his teeth in blood. The barber, however, was not so ready to relinquish the prize he had made in the pack-saddle; on the contrary, he raised such an outcry that everyone in the inn came running to know what the noise and quarrel meant. “Here, in the name of the king and justice!” he cried, “this thief and highwayman wants to kill me for trying to recover my property.”\n\n“You lie,” said Sancho, “I am no highwayman; it was in fair war my master Don Quixote won these spoils.”\n\nDon Quixote was standing by at the time, highly pleased to see his squire’s stoutness, both offensive and defensive, and from that time forth he reckoned him a man of mettle, and in his heart resolved to dub him a knight on the first opportunity that presented itself, feeling sure that the order of chivalry would be fittingly bestowed upon him.\n\nIn the course of the altercation, among other things the barber said, “Gentlemen, this pack-saddle is mine as surely as I owe God a death, and I know it as well as if I had given birth to it, and here is my ass in the stable who will not let me lie; only try it, and if it does not fit him like a glove, call me a rascal; and what is more, the same day I was robbed of this, they robbed me likewise of a new brass basin, never yet handselled, that would fetch a crown any day.”\n\nAt this Don Quixote could not keep himself from answering; and interposing between the two, and separating them, he placed the pack-saddle on the ground, to lie there in sight until the truth was established, and said, “Your worships may perceive clearly and plainly the error under which this worthy squire lies when he calls a basin which was, is, and shall be the helmet of Mambrino which I won from him in air war, and made myself master of by legitimate and lawful possession. With the pack-saddle I do not concern myself; but I may tell you on that head that my squire Sancho asked my permission to strip off the caparison of this vanquished poltroon’s steed, and with it adorn his own; I allowed him, and he took it; and as to its having been changed from a caparison into a pack-saddle, I can give no explanation except the usual one, that such transformations will take place in adventures of chivalry. To confirm all which, run, Sancho my son, and fetch hither the helmet which this good fellow calls a basin.”\n\n“Egad, master,” said Sancho, “if we have no other proof of our case than what your worship puts forward, Mambrino’s helmet is just as much a basin as this good fellow’s caparison is a pack-saddle.”\n\n“Do as I bid thee,” said Don Quixote; “it cannot be that everything in this castle goes by enchantment.”\n\nSancho hastened to where the basin was, and brought it back with him, and when Don Quixote saw it, he took hold of it and said:\n\n“Your worships may see with what a face this squire can assert that this is a basin and not the helmet I told you of; and I swear by the order of chivalry I profess, that this helmet is the identical one I took from him, without anything added to or taken from it.”\n\n“There is no doubt of that,” said Sancho, “for from the time my master won it until now he has only fought one battle in it, when he let loose those unlucky men in chains; and if had not been for this basin-helmet he would not have come off over well that time, for there was plenty of stone-throwing in that affair.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLV.\n\nIN WHICH THE DOUBTFUL QUESTION OF MAMBRINO’S HELMET AND THE PACK-SADDLE IS FINALLY SETTLED, WITH OTHER ADVENTURES THAT OCCURRED IN TRUTH AND EARNEST\n“What do you think now, gentlemen,” said the barber, “of what these gentles say, when they want to make out that this is a helmet?”\n\n“And whoever says the contrary,” said Don Quixote, “I will let him know he lies if he is a knight, and if he is a squire that he lies again a thousand times.”\n\nOur own barber, who was present at all this, and understood Don Quixote’s humour so thoroughly, took it into his head to back up his delusion and carry on the joke for the general amusement; so addressing the other barber he said:\n\n“Señor barber, or whatever you are, you must know that I belong to your profession too, and have had a licence to practise for more than twenty years, and I know the implements of the barber craft, every one of them, perfectly well; and I was likewise a soldier for some time in the days of my youth, and I know also what a helmet is, and a morion, and a headpiece with a visor, and other things pertaining to soldiering, I meant to say to soldiers’ arms; and I say-saving better opinions and always with submission to sounder judgments--that this piece we have now before us, which this worthy gentleman has in his hands, not only is no barber’s basin, but is as far from being one as white is from black, and truth from falsehood; I say, moreover, that this, although it is a helmet, is not a complete helmet.”\n\n“Certainly not,” said Don Quixote, “for half of it is wanting, that is to say the beaver.”\n\n“It is quite true,” said the curate, who saw the object of his friend the barber; and Cardenio, Don Fernando and his companions agreed with him, and even the Judge, if his thoughts had not been so full of Don Luis’s affair, would have helped to carry on the joke; but he was so taken up with the serious matters he had on his mind that he paid little or no attention to these facetious proceedings.\n\n“God bless me!” exclaimed their butt the barber at this; “is it possible that such an honourable company can say that this is not a basin but a helmet? Why, this is a thing that would astonish a whole university, however wise it might be! That will do; if this basin is a helmet, why, then the pack-saddle must be a horse’s caparison, as this gentleman has said.”\n\n“To me it looks like a pack-saddle,” said Don Quixote; “but I have already said that with that question I do not concern myself.”\n\n“As to whether it be pack-saddle or caparison,” said the curate, “it is only for Señor Don Quixote to say; for in these matters of chivalry all these gentlemen and I bow to his authority.”\n\n“By God, gentlemen,” said Don Quixote, “so many strange things have happened to me in this castle on the two occasions on which I have sojourned in it, that I will not venture to assert anything positively in reply to any question touching anything it contains; for it is my belief that everything that goes on within it goes by enchantment. The first time, an enchanted Moor that there is in it gave me sore trouble, nor did Sancho fare well among certain followers of his; and last night I was kept hanging by this arm for nearly two hours, without knowing how or why I came by such a mishap. So that now, for me to come forward to give an opinion in such a puzzling matter, would be to risk a rash decision. As regards the assertion that this is a basin and not a helmet I have already given an answer; but as to the question whether this is a pack-saddle or a caparison I will not venture to give a positive opinion, but will leave it to your worships’ better judgment. Perhaps as you are not dubbed knights like myself, the enchantments of this place have nothing to do with you, and your faculties are unfettered, and you can see things in this castle as they really and truly are, and not as they appear to me.”\n\n“There can be no question,” said Don Fernando on this, “but that Señor Don Quixote has spoken very wisely, and that with us rests the decision of this matter; and that we may have surer ground to go on, I will take the votes of the gentlemen in secret, and declare the result clearly and fully.”\n\nTo those who were in the secret of Don Quixote’s humour all this afforded great amusement; but to those who knew nothing about it, it seemed the greatest nonsense in the world, in particular to the four servants of Don Luis, as well as to Don Luis himself, and to three other travellers who had by chance come to the inn, and had the appearance of officers of the Holy Brotherhood, as indeed they were; but the one who above all was at his wits’ end, was the barber basin, there before his very eyes, had been turned into Mambrino’s helmet, and whose pack-saddle he had no doubt whatever was about to become a rich caparison for a horse. All laughed to see Don Fernando going from one to another collecting the votes, and whispering to them to give him their private opinion whether the treasure over which there had been so much fighting was a pack-saddle or a caparison; but after he had taken the votes of those who knew Don Quixote, he said aloud, “The fact is, my good fellow, that I am tired collecting such a number of opinions, for I find that there is not one of whom I ask what I desire to know, who does not tell me that it is absurd to say that this is the pack-saddle of an ass, and not the caparison of a horse, nay, of a thoroughbred horse; so you must submit, for, in spite of you and your ass, this is a caparison and no pack-saddle, and you have stated and proved your case very badly.”\n\n“May I never share heaven,” said the poor barber, “if your worships are not all mistaken; and may my soul appear before God as that appears to me a pack-saddle and not a caparison; but, ‘laws go,’-I say no more; and indeed I am not drunk, for I am fasting, except it be from sin.”\n\nThe simple talk of the barber did not afford less amusement than the absurdities of Don Quixote, who now observed:\n\n“There is no more to be done now than for each to take what belongs to him, and to whom God has given it, may St. Peter add his blessing.”\n\nBut said one of the four servants, “Unless, indeed, this is a deliberate joke, I cannot bring myself to believe that men so intelligent as those present are, or seem to be, can venture to declare and assert that this is not a basin, and that not a pack-saddle; but as I perceive that they do assert and declare it, I can only come to the conclusion that there is some mystery in this persistence in what is so opposed to the evidence of experience and truth itself; for I swear by”--and here he rapped out a round oath-“all the people in the world will not make me believe that this is not a barber’s basin and that a jackass’s pack-saddle.”\n\n“It might easily be a she-ass’s,” observed the curate.\n\n“It is all the same,” said the servant; “that is not the point; but whether it is or is not a pack-saddle, as your worships say.”\n\nOn hearing this one of the newly arrived officers of the Brotherhood, who had been listening to the dispute and controversy, unable to restrain his anger and impatience, exclaimed, “It is a pack-saddle as sure as my father is my father, and whoever has said or will say anything else must be drunk.”\n\n“You lie like a rascally clown,” returned Don Quixote; and lifting his pike, which he had never let out of his hand, he delivered such a blow at his head that, had not the officer dodged it, it would have stretched him at full length. The pike was shivered in pieces against the ground, and the rest of the officers, seeing their comrade assaulted, raised a shout, calling for help for the Holy Brotherhood. The landlord, who was of the fraternity, ran at once to fetch his staff of office and his sword, and ranged himself on the side of his comrades; the servants of Don Luis clustered round him, lest he should escape from them in the confusion; the barber, seeing the house turned upside down, once more laid hold of his pack-saddle and Sancho did the same; Don Quixote drew his sword and charged the officers; Don Luis cried out to his servants to leave him alone and go and help Don Quixote, and Cardenio and Don Fernando, who were supporting him; the curate was shouting at the top of his voice, the landlady was screaming, her daughter was wailing, Maritornes was weeping, Dorothea was aghast, Luscinda terror-stricken, and Dona Clara in a faint. The barber cudgelled Sancho, and Sancho pommelled the barber; Don Luis gave one of his servants, who ventured to catch him by the arm to keep him from escaping, a cuff that bathed his teeth in blood; the Judge took his part; Don Fernando had got one of the officers down and was belabouring him heartily; the landlord raised his voice again calling for help for the Holy Brotherhood; so that the whole inn was nothing but cries, shouts, shrieks, confusion, terror, dismay, mishaps, sword-cuts, fisticuffs, cudgellings, kicks, and bloodshed; and in the midst of all this chaos, complication, and general entanglement, Don Quixote took it into his head that he had been plunged into the thick of the discord of Agramante’s camp; and, in a voice that shook the inn like thunder, he cried out:\n\n“Hold all, let all sheathe their swords, let all be calm and attend to me as they value their lives!”\n\nAll paused at his mighty voice, and he went on to say, “Did I not tell you, sirs, that this castle was enchanted, and that a legion or so of devils dwelt in it? In proof whereof I call upon you to behold with your own eyes how the discord of Agramante’s camp has come hither, and been transferred into the midst of us. See how they fight, there for the sword, here for the horse, on that side for the eagle, on this for the helmet; we are all fighting, and all at cross purposes. Come then, you, Señor Judge, and you, señor curate; let the one represent King Agramante and the other King Sobrino, and make peace among us; for by God Almighty it is a sorry business that so many persons of quality as we are should slay one another for such trifling cause.” The officers, who did not understand Don Quixote’s mode of speaking, and found themselves roughly handled by Don Fernando, Cardenio, and their companions, were not to be appeased; the barber was, however, for both his beard and his pack-saddle were the worse for the struggle; Sancho like a good servant obeyed the slightest word of his master; while the four servants of Don Luis kept quiet when they saw how little they gained by not being so. The landlord alone insisted upon it that they must punish the insolence of this madman, who at every turn raised a disturbance in the inn; but at length the uproar was stilled for the present; the pack-saddle remained a caparison till the day of judgment, and the basin a helmet and the inn a castle in Don Quixote’s imagination.\n\nAll having been now pacified and made friends by the persuasion of the Judge and the curate, the servants of Don Luis began again to urge him to return with them at once; and while he was discussing the matter with them, the Judge took counsel with Don Fernando, Cardenio, and the curate as to what he ought to do in the case, telling them how it stood, and what Don Luis had said to him. It was agreed at length that Don Fernando should tell the servants of Don Luis who he was, and that it was his desire that Don Luis should accompany him to Andalusia, where he would receive from the marquis his brother the welcome his quality entitled him to; for, otherwise, it was easy to see from the determination of Don Luis that he would not return to his father at present, though they tore him to pieces. On learning the rank of Don Fernando and the resolution of Don Luis the four then settled it between themselves that three of them should return to tell his father how matters stood, and that the other should remain to wait upon Don Luis, and not leave him until they came back for him, or his father’s orders were known. Thus by the authority of Agramante and the wisdom of King Sobrino all this complication of disputes was arranged; but the enemy of concord and hater of peace, feeling himself slighted and made a fool of, and seeing how little he had gained after having involved them all in such an elaborate entanglement, resolved to try his hand once more by stirring up fresh quarrels and disturbances.\n\nIt came about in this wise: the officers were pacified on learning the rank of those with whom they had been engaged, and withdrew from the contest, considering that whatever the result might be they were likely to get the worst of the battle; but one of them, the one who had been thrashed and kicked by Don Fernando, recollected that among some warrants he carried for the arrest of certain delinquents, he had one against Don Quixote, whom the Holy Brotherhood had ordered to be arrested for setting the galley slaves free, as Sancho had, with very good reason, apprehended. Suspecting how it was, then, he wished to satisfy himself as to whether Don Quixote’s features corresponded; and taking a parchment out of his bosom he lit upon what he was in search of, and setting himself to read it deliberately, for he was not a quick reader, as he made out each word he fixed his eyes on Don Quixote, and went on comparing the description in the warrant with his face, and discovered that beyond all doubt he was the person described in it. As soon as he had satisfied himself, folding up the parchment, he took the warrant in his left hand and with his right seized Don Quixote by the collar so tightly that he did not allow him to breathe, and shouted aloud, “Help for the Holy Brotherhood! and that you may see I demand it in earnest, read this warrant which says this highwayman is to be arrested.”\n\nThe curate took the warrant and saw that what the officer said was true, and that it agreed with Don Quixote’s appearance, who, on his part, when he found himself roughly handled by this rascally clown, worked up to the highest pitch of wrath, and all his joints cracking with rage, with both hands seized the officer by the throat with all his might, so that had he not been helped by his comrades he would have yielded up his life ere Don Quixote released his hold. The landlord, who had perforce to support his brother officers, ran at once to aid them. The landlady, when she saw her husband engaged in a fresh quarrel, lifted up her voice afresh, and its note was immediately caught up by Maritornes and her daughter, calling upon heaven and all present for help; and Sancho, seeing what was going on, exclaimed, “By the Lord, it is quite true what my master says about the enchantments of this castle, for it is impossible to live an hour in peace in it!”\n\nDon Fernando parted the officer and Don Quixote, and to their mutual contentment made them relax the grip by which they held, the one the coat collar, the other the throat of his adversary; for all this, however, the officers did not cease to demand their prisoner and call on them to help, and deliver him over bound into their power, as was required for the service of the King and of the Holy Brotherhood, on whose behalf they again demanded aid and assistance to effect the capture of this robber and footpad of the highways.\n\nDon Quixote smiled when he heard these words, and said very calmly, “Come now, base, ill-born brood; call ye it highway robbery to give freedom to those in bondage, to release the captives, to succour the miserable, to raise up the fallen, to relieve the needy? Infamous beings, who by your vile grovelling intellects deserve that heaven should not make known to you the virtue that lies in knight-errantry, or show you the sin and ignorance in which ye lie when ye refuse to respect the shadow, not to say the presence, of any knight-errant! Come now; band, not of officers, but of thieves; footpads with the licence of the Holy Brotherhood; tell me who was the ignoramus who signed a warrant of arrest against such a knight as I am? Who was he that did not know that knights-errant are independent of all jurisdictions, that their law is their sword, their charter their prowess, and their edicts their will? Who, I say again, was the fool that knows not that there are no letters patent of nobility that confer such privileges or exemptions as a knight-errant acquires the day he is dubbed a knight, and devotes himself to the arduous calling of chivalry? What knight-errant ever paid poll-tax, duty, queen’s pin-money, king’s dues, toll or ferry? What tailor ever took payment of him for making his clothes? What castellan that received him in his castle ever made him pay his shot? What king did not seat him at his table? What damsel was not enamoured of him and did not yield herself up wholly to his will and pleasure? And, lastly, what knight-errant has there been, is there, or will there ever be in the world, not bold enough to give, single-handed, four hundred cudgellings to four hundred officers of the Holy Brotherhood if they come in his way?”\n\n# CHAPTER XLVI.\n\nOF THE END OF THE NOTABLE ADVENTURE OF THE OFFICERS OF THE HOLY BROTHERHOOD; AND OF THE GREAT FEROCITY OF OUR WORTHY KNIGHT, DON QUIXOTE\nWhile Don Quixote was talking in this strain, the curate was endeavouring to persuade the officers that he was out of his senses, as they might perceive by his deeds and his words, and that they need not press the matter any further, for even if they arrested him and carried him off, they would have to release him by-and-by as a madman; to which the holder of the warrant replied that he had nothing to do with inquiring into Don Quixote’s madness, but only to execute his superior’s orders, and that once taken they might let him go three hundred times if they liked.\n\n“For all that,” said the curate, “you must not take him away this time, nor will he, it is my opinion, let himself be taken away.”\n\nIn short, the curate used such arguments, and Don Quixote did such mad things, that the officers would have been more mad than he was if they had not perceived his want of wits, and so they thought it best to allow themselves to be pacified, and even to act as peacemakers between the barber and Sancho Panza, who still continued their altercation with much bitterness. In the end they, as officers of justice, settled the question by arbitration in such a manner that both sides were, if not perfectly contented, at least to some extent satisfied; for they changed the pack-saddles, but not the girths or head-stalls; and as to Mambrino’s helmet, the curate, under the rose and without Don Quixote’s knowing it, paid eight reals for the basin, and the barber executed a full receipt and engagement to make no further demand then or thenceforth for evermore, amen. These two disputes, which were the most important and gravest, being settled, it only remained for the servants of Don Luis to consent that three of them should return while one was left to accompany him whither Don Fernando desired to take him; and good luck and better fortune, having already begun to solve difficulties and remove obstructions in favour of the lovers and warriors of the inn, were pleased to persevere and bring everything to a happy issue; for the servants agreed to do as Don Luis wished; which gave Dona Clara such happiness that no one could have looked into her face just then without seeing the joy of her heart. Zoraida, though she did not fully comprehend all she saw, was grave or gay without knowing why, as she watched and studied the various countenances, but particularly her Spaniard’s, whom she followed with her eyes and clung to with her soul. The gift and compensation which the curate gave the barber had not escaped the landlord’s notice, and he demanded Don Quixote’s reckoning, together with the amount of the damage to his wine-skins, and the loss of his wine, swearing that neither Rocinante nor Sancho’s ass should leave the inn until he had been paid to the very last farthing. The curate settled all amicably, and Don Fernando paid; though the Judge had also very readily offered to pay the score; and all became so peaceful and quiet that the inn no longer reminded one of the discord of Agramante’s camp, as Don Quixote said, but of the peace and tranquillity of the days of Octavianus: for all which it was the universal opinion that their thanks were due to the great zeal and eloquence of the curate, and to the unexampled generosity of Don Fernando.\n\nFinding himself now clear and quit of all quarrels, his squire’s as well as his own, Don Quixote considered that it would be advisable to continue the journey he had begun, and bring to a close that great adventure for which he had been called and chosen; and with this high resolve he went and knelt before Dorothea, who, however, would not allow him to utter a word until he had risen; so to obey her he rose, and said, “It is a common proverb, fair lady, that ‘diligence is the mother of good fortune,’ and experience has often shown in important affairs that the earnestness of the negotiator brings the doubtful case to a successful termination; but in nothing does this truth show itself more plainly than in war, where quickness and activity forestall the devices of the enemy, and win the victory before the foe has time to defend himself. All this I say, exalted and esteemed lady, because it seems to me that for us to remain any longer in this castle now is useless, and may be injurious to us in a way that we shall find out some day; for who knows but that your enemy the giant may have learned by means of secret and diligent spies that I am going to destroy him, and if the opportunity be given him he may seize it to fortify himself in some impregnable castle or stronghold, against which all my efforts and the might of my indefatigable arm may avail but little? Therefore, lady, let us, as I say, forestall his schemes by our activity, and let us depart at once in quest of fair fortune; for your highness is only kept from enjoying it as fully as you could desire by my delay in encountering your adversary.”\n\nDon Quixote held his peace and said no more, calmly awaiting the reply of the beauteous princess, who, with commanding dignity and in a style adapted to Don Quixote’s own, replied to him in these words, “I give you thanks, sir knight, for the eagerness you, like a good knight to whom it is a natural obligation to succour the orphan and the needy, display to afford me aid in my sore trouble; and heaven grant that your wishes and mine may be realised, so that you may see that there are women in this world capable of gratitude; as to my departure, let it be forthwith, for I have no will but yours; dispose of me entirely in accordance with your good pleasure; for she who has once entrusted to you the defence of her person, and placed in your hands the recovery of her dominions, must not think of offering opposition to that which your wisdom may ordain.”\n\n“On, then, in God’s name,” said Don Quixote; “for, when a lady humbles herself to me, I will not lose the opportunity of raising her up and placing her on the throne of her ancestors. Let us depart at once, for the common saying that in delay there is danger, lends spurs to my eagerness to take the road; and as neither heaven has created nor hell seen any that can daunt or intimidate me, saddle Rocinante, Sancho, and get ready thy ass and the queen’s palfrey, and let us take leave of the castellan and these gentlemen, and go hence this very instant.”\n\nSancho, who was standing by all the time, said, shaking his head, “Ah! master, master, there is more mischief in the village than one hears of, begging all good bodies’ pardon.”\n\n“What mischief can there be in any village, or in all the cities of the world, you booby, that can hurt my reputation?” said Don Quixote.\n\n“If your worship is angry,” replied Sancho, “I will hold my tongue and leave unsaid what as a good squire I am bound to say, and what a good servant should tell his master.”\n\n“Say what thou wilt,” returned Don Quixote, “provided thy words be not meant to work upon my fears; for thou, if thou fearest, art behaving like thyself; but I like myself, in not fearing.”\n\n“It is nothing of the sort, as I am a sinner before God,” said Sancho, “but that I take it to be sure and certain that this lady, who calls herself queen of the great kingdom of Micomicon, is no more so than my mother; for, if she was what she says, she would not go rubbing noses with one that is here every instant and behind every door.”\n\nDorothea turned red at Sancho’s words, for the truth was that her husband Don Fernando had now and then, when the others were not looking, gathered from her lips some of the reward his love had earned, and Sancho seeing this had considered that such freedom was more like a courtesan than a queen of a great kingdom; she, however, being unable or not caring to answer him, allowed him to proceed, and he continued, “This I say, señor, because, if after we have travelled roads and highways, and passed bad nights and worse days, one who is now enjoying himself in this inn is to reap the fruit of our labours, there is no need for me to be in a hurry to saddle Rocinante, put the pad on the ass, or get ready the palfrey; for it will be better for us to stay quiet, and let every jade mind her spinning, and let us go to dinner.”\n\nGood God, what was the indignation of Don Quixote when he heard the audacious words of his squire! So great was it, that in a voice inarticulate with rage, with a stammering tongue, and eyes that flashed living fire, he exclaimed, “Rascally clown, boorish, insolent, and ignorant, ill-spoken, foul-mouthed, impudent backbiter and slanderer! Hast thou dared to utter such words in my presence and in that of these illustrious ladies? Hast thou dared to harbour such gross and shameless thoughts in thy muddled imagination? Begone from my presence, thou born monster, storehouse of lies, hoard of untruths, garner of knaveries, inventor of scandals, publisher of absurdities, enemy of the respect due to royal personages! Begone, show thyself no more before me under pain of my wrath;” and so saying he knitted his brows, puffed out his cheeks, gazed around him, and stamped on the ground violently with his right foot, showing in every way the rage that was pent up in his heart; and at his words and furious gestures Sancho was so scared and terrified that he would have been glad if the earth had opened that instant and swallowed him, and his only thought was to turn round and make his escape from the angry presence of his master.\n\nBut the ready-witted Dorothea, who by this time so well understood Don Quixote’s humour, said, to mollify his wrath, “Be not irritated at the absurdities your good squire has uttered, Sir Knight of the Rueful Countenance, for perhaps he did not utter them without cause, and from his good sense and Christian conscience it is not likely that he would bear false witness against anyone. We may therefore believe, without any hesitation, that since, as you say, sir knight, everything in this castle goes and is brought about by means of enchantment, Sancho, I say, may possibly have seen, through this diabolical medium, what he says he saw so much to the detriment of my modesty.”\n\n“I swear by God Omnipotent,” exclaimed Don Quixote at this, “your highness has hit the point; and that some vile illusion must have come before this sinner of a Sancho, that made him see what it would have been impossible to see by any other means than enchantments; for I know well enough, from the poor fellow’s goodness and harmlessness, that he is incapable of bearing false witness against anybody.”\n\n“True, no doubt,” said Don Fernando, “for which reason, Señor Don Quixote, you ought to forgive him and restore him to the bosom of your favour, sicut erat in principio, before illusions of this sort had taken away his senses.”\n\nDon Quixote said he was ready to pardon him, and the curate went for Sancho, who came in very humbly, and falling on his knees begged for the hand of his master, who having presented it to him and allowed him to kiss it, gave him his blessing and said, “Now, Sancho my son, thou wilt be convinced of the truth of what I have many a time told thee, that everything in this castle is done by means of enchantment.”\n\n“So it is, I believe,” said Sancho, “except the affair of the blanket, which came to pass in reality by ordinary means.”\n\n“Believe it not,” said Don Quixote, “for had it been so, I would have avenged thee that instant, or even now; but neither then nor now could I, nor have I seen anyone upon whom to avenge thy wrong.”\n\nThey were all eager to know what the affair of the blanket was, and the landlord gave them a minute account of Sancho’s flights, at which they laughed not a little, and at which Sancho would have been no less out of countenance had not his master once more assured him it was all enchantment. For all that his simplicity never reached so high a pitch that he could persuade himself it was not the plain and simple truth, without any deception whatever about it, that he had been blanketed by beings of flesh and blood, and not by visionary and imaginary phantoms, as his master believed and protested.\n\nThe illustrious company had now been two days in the inn; and as it seemed to them time to depart, they devised a plan so that, without giving Dorothea and Don Fernando the trouble of going back with Don Quixote to his village under pretence of restoring Queen Micomicona, the curate and the barber might carry him away with them as they proposed, and the curate be able to take his madness in hand at home; and in pursuance of their plan they arranged with the owner of an oxcart who happened to be passing that way to carry him after this fashion. They constructed a kind of cage with wooden bars, large enough to hold Don Quixote comfortably; and then Don Fernando and his companions, the servants of Don Luis, and the officers of the Brotherhood, together with the landlord, by the directions and advice of the curate, covered their faces and disguised themselves, some in one way, some in another, so as to appear to Don Quixote quite different from the persons he had seen in the castle. This done, in profound silence they entered the room where he was asleep, taking his his rest after the past frays, and advancing to where he was sleeping tranquilly, not dreaming of anything of the kind happening, they seized him firmly and bound him fast hand and foot, so that, when he awoke startled, he was unable to move, and could only marvel and wonder at the strange figures he saw before him; upon which he at once gave way to the idea which his crazed fancy invariably conjured up before him, and took it into his head that all these shapes were phantoms of the enchanted castle, and that he himself was unquestionably enchanted as he could neither move nor help himself; precisely what the curate, the concoctor of the scheme, expected would happen. Of all that were there Sancho was the only one who was at once in his senses and in his own proper character, and he, though he was within very little of sharing his master’s infirmity, did not fail to perceive who all these disguised figures were; but he did not dare to open his lips until he saw what came of this assault and capture of his master; nor did the latter utter a word, waiting to the upshot of his mishap; which was that bringing in the cage, they shut him up in it and nailed the bars so firmly that they could not be easily burst open.\n\nThey then took him on their shoulders, and as they passed out of the room an awful voice--as much so as the barber, not he of the pack-saddle but the other, was able to make it--was heard to say, “O Knight of the Rueful Countenance, let not this captivity in which thou art placed afflict thee, for this must needs be, for the more speedy accomplishment of the adventure in which thy great heart has engaged thee; the which shall be accomplished when the raging Manchegan lion and the white Tobosan dove shall be linked together, having first humbled their haughty necks to the gentle yoke of matrimony. And from this marvellous union shall come forth to the light of the world brave whelps that shall rival the ravening claws of their valiant father; and this shall come to pass ere the pursuer of the flying nymph shall in his swift natural course have twice visited the starry signs. And thou, O most noble and obedient squire that ever bore sword at side, beard on face, or nose to smell with, be not dismayed or grieved to see the flower of knight-errantry carried away thus before thy very eyes; for soon, if it so please the Framer of the universe, thou shalt see thyself exalted to such a height that thou shalt not know thyself, and the promises which thy good master has made thee shall not prove false; and I assure thee, on the authority of the sage Mentironiana, that thy wages shall be paid thee, as thou shalt see in due season. Follow then the footsteps of the valiant enchanted knight, for it is expedient that thou shouldst go to the destination assigned to both of you; and as it is not permitted to me to say more, God be with thee; for I return to that place I wot of;” and as he brought the prophecy to a close he raised his voice to a high pitch, and then lowered it to such a soft tone, that even those who knew it was all a joke were almost inclined to take what they heard seriously.\n\nDon Quixote was comforted by the prophecy he heard, for he at once comprehended its meaning perfectly, and perceived it was promised to him that he should see himself united in holy and lawful matrimony with his beloved Dulcinea del Toboso, from whose blessed womb should proceed the whelps, his sons, to the eternal glory of La Mancha; and being thoroughly and firmly persuaded of this, he lifted up his voice, and with a deep sigh exclaimed, “Oh thou, whoever thou art, who hast foretold me so much good, I implore of thee that on my part thou entreat that sage enchanter who takes charge of my interests, that he leave me not to perish in this captivity in which they are now carrying me away, ere I see fulfilled promises so joyful and incomparable as those which have been now made me; for, let this but come to pass, and I shall glory in the pains of my prison, find comfort in these chains wherewith they bind me, and regard this bed whereon they stretch me, not as a hard battle-field, but as a soft and happy nuptial couch; and touching the consolation of Sancho Panza, my squire, I rely upon his goodness and rectitude that he will not desert me in good or evil fortune; for if, by his ill luck or mine, it may not happen to be in my power to give him the island I have promised, or any equivalent for it, at least his wages shall not be lost; for in my will, which is already made, I have declared the sum that shall be paid to him, measured, not by his many faithful services, but by the means at my disposal.”\n\nSancho bowed his head very respectfully and kissed both his hands, for, being tied together, he could not kiss one; and then the apparitions lifted the cage upon their shoulders and fixed it upon the ox-cart.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII.\n\nOF THE STRANGE MANNER IN WHICH DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA WAS CARRIED AWAY ENCHANTED, TOGETHER WITH OTHER REMARKABLE INCIDENTS\nWhen Don Quixote saw himself caged and hoisted on the cart in this way, he said, “Many grave histories of knights-errant have I read; but never yet have I read, seen, or heard of their carrying off enchanted knights-errant in this fashion, or at the slow pace that these lazy, sluggish animals promise; for they always take them away through the air with marvellous swiftness, enveloped in a dark thick cloud, or on a chariot of fire, or it may be on some hippogriff or other beast of the kind; but to carry me off like this on an ox-cart! By God, it puzzles me! But perhaps the chivalry and enchantments of our day take a different course from that of those in days gone by; and it may be, too, that as I am a new knight in the world, and the first to revive the already forgotten calling of knight-adventurers, they may have newly invented other kinds of enchantments and other modes of carrying off the enchanted. What thinkest thou of the matter, Sancho my son?”\n\n“I don’t know what to think,” answered Sancho, “not being as well read as your worship in errant writings; but for all that I venture to say and swear that these apparitions that are about us are not quite catholic.”\n\n“Catholic!” said Don Quixote. “Father of me! how can they be Catholic when they are all devils that have taken fantastic shapes to come and do this, and bring me to this condition? And if thou wouldst prove it, touch them, and feel them, and thou wilt find they have only bodies of air, and no consistency except in appearance.”\n\n“By God, master,” returned Sancho, “I have touched them already; and that devil, that goes about there so busily, has firm flesh, and another property very different from what I have heard say devils have, for by all accounts they all smell of brimstone and other bad smells; but this one smells of amber half a league off.” Sancho was here speaking of Don Fernando, who, like a gentleman of his rank, was very likely perfumed as Sancho said.\n\n“Marvel not at that, Sancho my friend,” said Don Quixote; “for let me tell thee devils are crafty; and even if they do carry odours about with them, they themselves have no smell, because they are spirits; or, if they have any smell, they cannot smell of anything sweet, but of something foul and fetid; and the reason is that as they carry hell with them wherever they go, and can get no ease whatever from their torments, and as a sweet smell is a thing that gives pleasure and enjoyment, it is impossible that they can smell sweet; if, then, this devil thou speakest of seems to thee to smell of amber, either thou art deceiving thyself, or he wants to deceive thee by making thee fancy he is not a devil.”\n\nSuch was the conversation that passed between master and man; and Don Fernando and Cardenio, apprehensive of Sancho’s making a complete discovery of their scheme, towards which he had already gone some way, resolved to hasten their departure, and calling the landlord aside, they directed him to saddle Rocinante and put the pack-saddle on Sancho’s ass, which he did with great alacrity. In the meantime the curate had made an arrangement with the officers that they should bear them company as far as his village, he paying them so much a day. Cardenio hung the buckler on one side of the bow of Rocinante’s saddle and the basin on the other, and by signs commanded Sancho to mount his ass and take Rocinante’s bridle, and at each side of the cart he placed two officers with their muskets; but before the cart was put in motion, out came the landlady and her daughter and Maritornes to bid Don Quixote farewell, pretending to weep with grief at his misfortune; and to them Don Quixote said:\n\n“Weep not, good ladies, for all these mishaps are the lot of those who follow the profession I profess; and if these reverses did not befall me I should not esteem myself a famous knight-errant; for such things never happen to knights of little renown and fame, because nobody in the world thinks about them; to valiant knights they do, for these are envied for their virtue and valour by many princes and other knights who compass the destruction of the worthy by base means. Nevertheless, virtue is of herself so mighty, that, in spite of all the magic that Zoroaster its first inventor knew, she will come victorious out of every trial, and shed her light upon the earth as the sun does upon the heavens. Forgive me, fair ladies, if, through inadvertence, I have in aught offended you; for intentionally and wittingly I have never done so to any; and pray to God that he deliver me from this captivity to which some malevolent enchanter has consigned me; and should I find myself released therefrom, the favours that ye have bestowed upon me in this castle shall be held in memory by me, that I may acknowledge, recognise, and requite them as they deserve.”\n\nWhile this was passing between the ladies of the castle and Don Quixote, the curate and the barber bade farewell to Don Fernando and his companions, to the captain, his brother, and the ladies, now all made happy, and in particular to Dorothea and Luscinda. They all embraced one another, and promised to let each other know how things went with them, and Don Fernando directed the curate where to write to him, to tell him what became of Don Quixote, assuring him that there was nothing that could give him more pleasure than to hear of it, and that he too, on his part, would send him word of everything he thought he would like to know, about his marriage, Zoraida’s baptism, Don Luis’s affair, and Luscinda’s return to her home. The curate promised to comply with his request carefully, and they embraced once more, and renewed their promises.\n\nThe landlord approached the curate and handed him some papers, saying he had discovered them in the lining of the valise in which the novel of “The Ill-advised Curiosity” had been found, and that he might take them all away with him as their owner had not since returned; for, as he could not read, he did not want them himself. The curate thanked him, and opening them he saw at the beginning of the manuscript the words, “Novel of Rinconete and Cortadillo,” by which he perceived that it was a novel, and as that of “The Ill-advised Curiosity” had been good he concluded this would be so too, as they were both probably by the same author; so he kept it, intending to read it when he had an opportunity. He then mounted and his friend the barber did the same, both masked, so as not to be recognised by Don Quixote, and set out following in the rear of the cart. The order of march was this: first went the cart with the owner leading it; at each side of it marched the officers of the Brotherhood, as has been said, with their muskets; then followed Sancho Panza on his ass, leading Rocinante by the bridle; and behind all came the curate and the barber on their mighty mules, with faces covered, as aforesaid, and a grave and serious air, measuring their pace to suit the slow steps of the oxen. Don Quixote was seated in the cage, with his hands tied and his feet stretched out, leaning against the bars as silent and as patient as if he were a stone statue and not a man of flesh. Thus slowly and silently they made, it might be, two leagues, until they reached a valley which the carter thought a convenient place for resting and feeding his oxen, and he said so to the curate, but the barber was of opinion that they ought to push on a little farther, as at the other side of a hill which appeared close by he knew there was a valley that had more grass and much better than the one where they proposed to halt; and his advice was taken and they continued their journey.\n\nJust at that moment the curate, looking back, saw coming on behind them six or seven mounted men, well found and equipped, who soon overtook them, for they were travelling, not at the sluggish, deliberate pace of oxen, but like men who rode canons’ mules, and in haste to take their noontide rest as soon as possible at the inn which was in sight not a league off. The quick travellers came up with the slow, and courteous salutations were exchanged; and one of the new comers, who was, in fact, a canon of Toledo and master of the others who accompanied him, observing the regular order of the procession, the cart, the officers, Sancho, Rocinante, the curate and the barber, and above all Don Quixote caged and confined, could not help asking what was the meaning of carrying the man in that fashion; though, from the badges of the officers, he already concluded that he must be some desperate highwayman or other malefactor whose punishment fell within the jurisdiction of the Holy Brotherhood. One of the officers to whom he had put the question, replied, “Let the gentleman himself tell you the meaning of his going this way, señor, for we do not know.”\n\nDon Quixote overheard the conversation and said, “Haply, gentlemen, you are versed and learned in matters of errant chivalry? Because if you are I will tell you my misfortunes; if not, there is no good in my giving myself the trouble of relating them;” but here the curate and the barber, seeing that the travellers were engaged in conversation with Don Quixote, came forward, in order to answer in such a way as to save their stratagem from being discovered.\n\nThe canon, replying to Don Quixote, said, “In truth, brother, I know more about books of chivalry than I do about Villalpando’s elements of logic; so if that be all, you may safely tell me what you please.”\n\n“In God’s name, then, señor,” replied Don Quixote; “if that be so, I would have you know that I am held enchanted in this cage by the envy and fraud of wicked enchanters; for virtue is more persecuted by the wicked than loved by the good. I am a knight-errant, and not one of those whose names Fame has never thought of immortalising in her record, but of those who, in defiance and in spite of envy itself, and all the magicians that Persia, or Brahmans that India, or Gymnosophists that Ethiopia ever produced, will place their names in the temple of immortality, to serve as examples and patterns for ages to come, whereby knights-errant may see the footsteps in which they must tread if they would attain the summit and crowning point of honour in arms.”\n\n“What Señor Don Quixote of La Mancha says,” observed the curate, “is the truth; for he goes enchanted in this cart, not from any fault or sins of his, but because of the malevolence of those to whom virtue is odious and valour hateful. This, señor, is the Knight of the Rueful Countenance, if you have ever heard him named, whose valiant achievements and mighty deeds shall be written on lasting brass and imperishable marble, notwithstanding all the efforts of envy to obscure them and malice to hide them.”\n\nWhen the canon heard both the prisoner and the man who was at liberty talk in such a strain he was ready to cross himself in his astonishment, and could not make out what had befallen him; and all his attendants were in the same state of amazement.\n\nAt this point Sancho Panza, who had drawn near to hear the conversation, said, in order to make everything plain, “Well, sirs, you may like or dislike what I am going to say, but the fact of the matter is, my master, Don Quixote, is just as much enchanted as my mother. He is in his full senses, he eats and he drinks, and he has his calls like other men and as he had yesterday, before they caged him. And if that’s the case, what do they mean by wanting me to believe that he is enchanted? For I have heard many a one say that enchanted people neither eat, nor sleep, nor talk; and my master, if you don’t stop him, will talk more than thirty lawyers.” Then turning to the curate he exclaimed, “Ah, señor curate, señor curate! do you think I don’t know you? Do you think I don’t guess and see the drift of these new enchantments? Well then, I can tell you I know you, for all your face is covered, and I can tell you I am up to you, however you may hide your tricks. After all, where envy reigns virtue cannot live, and where there is niggardliness there can be no liberality. Ill betide the devil! if it had not been for your worship my master would be married to the Princess Micomicona this minute, and I should be a count at least; for no less was to be expected, as well from the goodness of my master, him of the Rueful Countenance, as from the greatness of my services. But I see now how true it is what they say in these parts, that the wheel of fortune turns faster than a mill-wheel, and that those who were up yesterday are down to-day. I am sorry for my wife and children, for when they might fairly and reasonably expect to see their father return to them a governor or viceroy of some island or kingdom, they will see him come back a horse-boy. I have said all this, señor curate, only to urge your paternity to lay to your conscience your ill-treatment of my master; and have a care that God does not call you to account in another life for making a prisoner of him in this way, and charge against you all the succours and good deeds that my lord Don Quixote leaves undone while he is shut up.\n\n“Trim those lamps there!” exclaimed the barber at this; “so you are of the same fraternity as your master, too, Sancho? By God, I begin to see that you will have to keep him company in the cage, and be enchanted like him for having caught some of his humour and chivalry. It was an evil hour when you let yourself be got with child by his promises, and that island you long so much for found its way into your head.”\n\n“I am not with child by anyone,” returned Sancho, “nor am I a man to let myself be got with child, if it was by the King himself. Though I am poor I am an old Christian, and I owe nothing to nobody, and if I long for an island, other people long for worse. Each of us is the son of his own works; and being a man I may come to be pope, not to say governor of an island, especially as my master may win so many that he will not know whom to give them to. Mind how you talk, master barber; for shaving is not everything, and there is some difference between Peter and Peter. I say this because we all know one another, and it will not do to throw false dice with me; and as to the enchantment of my master, God knows the truth; leave it as it is; it only makes it worse to stir it.”\n\nThe barber did not care to answer Sancho lest by his plain speaking he should disclose what the curate and he himself were trying so hard to conceal; and under the same apprehension the curate had asked the canon to ride on a little in advance, so that he might tell him the mystery of this man in the cage, and other things that would amuse him. The canon agreed, and going on ahead with his servants, listened with attention to the account of the character, life, madness, and ways of Don Quixote, given him by the curate, who described to him briefly the beginning and origin of his craze, and told him the whole story of his adventures up to his being confined in the cage, together with the plan they had of taking him home to try if by any means they could discover a cure for his madness. The canon and his servants were surprised anew when they heard Don Quixote’s strange story, and when it was finished he said, “To tell the truth, señor curate, I for my part consider what they call books of chivalry to be mischievous to the State; and though, led by idle and false taste, I have read the beginnings of almost all that have been printed, I never could manage to read any one of them from beginning to end; for it seems to me they are all more or less the same thing; and one has nothing more in it than another; this no more than that. And in my opinion this sort of writing and composition is of the same species as the fables they call the Milesian, nonsensical tales that aim solely at giving amusement and not instruction, exactly the opposite of the apologue fables which amuse and instruct at the same time. And though it may be the chief object of such books to amuse, I do not know how they can succeed, when they are so full of such monstrous nonsense. For the enjoyment the mind feels must come from the beauty and harmony which it perceives or contemplates in the things that the eye or the imagination brings before it; and nothing that has any ugliness or disproportion about it can give any pleasure. What beauty, then, or what proportion of the parts to the whole, or of the whole to the parts, can there be in a book or fable where a lad of sixteen cuts down a giant as tall as a tower and makes two halves of him as if he was an almond cake? And when they want to give us a picture of a battle, after having told us that there are a million of combatants on the side of the enemy, let the hero of the book be opposed to them, and we have perforce to believe, whether we like it or not, that the said knight wins the victory by the single might of his strong arm. And then, what shall we say of the facility with which a born queen or empress will give herself over into the arms of some unknown wandering knight? What mind, that is not wholly barbarous and uncultured, can find pleasure in reading of how a great tower full of knights sails away across the sea like a ship with a fair wind, and will be to-night in Lombardy and to-morrow morning in the land of Prester John of the Indies, or some other that Ptolemy never described nor Marco Polo saw? And if, in answer to this, I am told that the authors of books of the kind write them as fiction, and therefore are not bound to regard niceties of truth, I would reply that fiction is all the better the more it looks like truth, and gives the more pleasure the more probability and possibility there is about it. Plots in fiction should be wedded to the understanding of the reader, and be constructed in such a way that, reconciling impossibilities, smoothing over difficulties, keeping the mind on the alert, they may surprise, interest, divert, and entertain, so that wonder and delight joined may keep pace one with the other; all which he will fail to effect who shuns verisimilitude and truth to nature, wherein lies the perfection of writing. I have never yet seen any book of chivalry that puts together a connected plot complete in all its numbers, so that the middle agrees with the beginning, and the end with the beginning and middle; on the contrary, they construct them with such a multitude of members that it seems as though they meant to produce a chimera or monster rather than a well-proportioned figure. And besides all this they are harsh in their style, incredible in their achievements, licentious in their amours, uncouth in their courtly speeches, prolix in their battles, silly in their arguments, absurd in their travels, and, in short, wanting in everything like intelligent art; for which reason they deserve to be banished from the Christian commonwealth as a worthless breed.”\n\nThe curate listened to him attentively and felt that he was a man of sound understanding, and that there was good reason in what he said; so he told him that, being of the same opinion himself, and bearing a grudge to books of chivalry, he had burned all Don Quixote’s, which were many; and gave him an account of the scrutiny he had made of them, and of those he had condemned to the flames and those he had spared, with which the canon was not a little amused, adding that though he had said so much in condemnation of these books, still he found one good thing in them, and that was the opportunity they afforded to a gifted intellect for displaying itself; for they presented a wide and spacious field over which the pen might range freely, describing shipwrecks, tempests, combats, battles, portraying a valiant captain with all the qualifications requisite to make one, showing him sagacious in foreseeing the wiles of the enemy, eloquent in speech to encourage or restrain his soldiers, ripe in counsel, rapid in resolve, as bold in biding his time as in pressing the attack; now picturing some sad tragic incident, now some joyful and unexpected event; here a beauteous lady, virtuous, wise, and modest; there a Christian knight, brave and gentle; here a lawless, barbarous braggart; there a courteous prince, gallant and gracious; setting forth the devotion and loyalty of vassals, the greatness and generosity of nobles. “Or again,” said he, “the author may show himself to be an astronomer, or a skilled cosmographer, or musician, or one versed in affairs of state, and sometimes he will have a chance of coming forward as a magician if he likes. He can set forth the craftiness of Ulysses, the piety of Æneas, the valour of Achilles, the misfortunes of Hector, the treachery of Sinon, the friendship of Euryalus, the generosity of Alexander, the boldness of Caesar, the clemency and truth of Trajan, the fidelity of Zopyrus, the wisdom of Cato, and in short all the faculties that serve to make an illustrious man perfect, now uniting them in one individual, again distributing them among many; and if this be done with charm of style and ingenious invention, aiming at the truth as much as possible, he will assuredly weave a web of bright and varied threads that, when finished, will display such perfection and beauty that it will attain the worthiest object any writing can seek, which, as I said before, is to give instruction and pleasure combined; for the unrestricted range of these books enables the author to show his powers, epic, lyric, tragic, or comic, and all the moods the sweet and winning arts of poesy and oratory are capable of; for the epic may be written in prose just as well as in verse.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII.\n\nIN WHICH THE CANON PURSUES THE SUBJECT OF THE BOOKS OF CHIVALRY, WITH OTHER MATTERS WORTHY OF HIS WIT\n“It is as you say, señor canon,” said the curate; “and for that reason those who have hitherto written books of the sort deserve all the more censure for writing without paying any attention to good taste or the rules of art, by which they might guide themselves and become as famous in prose as the two princes of Greek and Latin poetry are in verse.”\n\n“I myself, at any rate,” said the canon, “was once tempted to write a book of chivalry in which all the points I have mentioned were to be observed; and if I must own the truth I have more than a hundred sheets written; and to try if it came up to my own opinion of it, I showed them to persons who were fond of this kind of reading, to learned and intelligent men as well as to ignorant people who cared for nothing but the pleasure of listening to nonsense, and from all I obtained flattering approval; nevertheless I proceeded no farther with it, as well because it seemed to me an occupation inconsistent with my profession, as because I perceived that the fools are more numerous than the wise; and, though it is better to be praised by the wise few than applauded by the foolish many, I have no mind to submit myself to the stupid judgment of the silly public, to whom the reading of such books falls for the most part.\n\n“But what most of all made me hold my hand and even abandon all idea of finishing it was an argument I put to myself taken from the plays that are acted now-a-days, which was in this wise: if those that are now in vogue, as well those that are pure invention as those founded on history, are, all or most of them, downright nonsense and things that have neither head nor tail, and yet the public listens to them with delight, and regards and cries them up as perfection when they are so far from it; and if the authors who write them, and the players who act them, say that this is what they must be, for the public wants this and will have nothing else; and that those that go by rule and work out a plot according to the laws of art will only find some half-dozen intelligent people to understand them, while all the rest remain blind to the merit of their composition; and that for themselves it is better to get bread from the many than praise from the few; then my book will fare the same way, after I have burnt off my eyebrows in trying to observe the principles I have spoken of, and I shall be ‘the tailor of the corner.’ And though I have sometimes endeavoured to convince actors that they are mistaken in this notion they have adopted, and that they would attract more people, and get more credit, by producing plays in accordance with the rules of art, than by absurd ones, they are so thoroughly wedded to their own opinion that no argument or evidence can wean them from it.\n\n“I remember saying one day to one of these obstinate fellows, ‘Tell me, do you not recollect that a few years ago, there were three tragedies acted in Spain, written by a famous poet of these kingdoms, which were such that they filled all who heard them with admiration, delight, and interest, the ignorant as well as the wise, the masses as well as the higher orders, and brought in more money to the performers, these three alone, than thirty of the best that have been since produced?’\n\n“‘No doubt,’ replied the actor in question, ‘you mean the “Isabella,” the “Phyllis,” and the “Alexandra.”’\n\n“‘Those are the ones I mean,’ said I; ‘and see if they did not observe the principles of art, and if, by observing them, they failed to show their superiority and please all the world; so that the fault does not lie with the public that insists upon nonsense, but with those who don’t know how to produce something else. “The Ingratitude Revenged” was not nonsense, nor was there any in “The Numantia,” nor any to be found in “The Merchant Lover,” nor yet in “The Friendly Fair Foe,” nor in some others that have been written by certain gifted poets, to their own fame and renown, and to the profit of those that brought them out;’ some further remarks I added to these, with which, I think, I left him rather dumbfoundered, but not so satisfied or convinced that I could disabuse him of his error.”\n\n“You have touched upon a subject, señor canon,” observed the curate here, “that has awakened an old enmity I have against the plays in vogue at the present day, quite as strong as that which I bear to the books of chivalry; for while the drama, according to Tully, should be the mirror of human life, the model of manners, and the image of the truth, those which are presented now-a-days are mirrors of nonsense, models of folly, and images of lewdness. For what greater nonsense can there be in connection with what we are now discussing than for an infant to appear in swaddling clothes in the first scene of the first act, and in the second a grown-up bearded man? Or what greater absurdity can there be than putting before us an old man as a swashbuckler, a young man as a poltroon, a lackey using fine language, a page giving sage advice, a king plying as a porter, a princess who is a kitchen-maid? And then what shall I say of their attention to the time in which the action they represent may or can take place, save that I have seen a play where the first act began in Europe, the second in Asia, the third finished in Africa, and no doubt, had it been in four acts, the fourth would have ended in America, and so it would have been laid in all four quarters of the globe? And if truth to life is the main thing the drama should keep in view, how is it possible for any average understanding to be satisfied when the action is supposed to pass in the time of King Pepin or Charlemagne, and the principal personage in it they represent to be the Emperor Heraclius who entered Jerusalem with the cross and won the Holy Sepulchre, like Godfrey of Bouillon, there being years innumerable between the one and the other? or, if the play is based on fiction and historical facts are introduced, or bits of what occurred to different people and at different times mixed up with it, all, not only without any semblance of probability, but with obvious errors that from every point of view are inexcusable? And the worst of it is, there are ignorant people who say that this is perfection, and that anything beyond this is affected refinement. And then if we turn to sacred dramas--what miracles they invent in them! What apocryphal, ill-devised incidents, attributing to one saint the miracles of another! And even in secular plays they venture to introduce miracles without any reason or object except that they think some such miracle, or transformation as they call it, will come in well to astonish stupid people and draw them to the play. All this tends to the prejudice of the truth and the corruption of history, nay more, to the reproach of the wits of Spain; for foreigners who scrupulously observe the laws of the drama look upon us as barbarous and ignorant, when they see the absurdity and nonsense of the plays we produce. Nor will it be a sufficient excuse to say that the chief object well-ordered governments have in view when they permit plays to be performed in public is to entertain the people with some harmless amusement occasionally, and keep it from those evil humours which idleness is apt to engender; and that, as this may be attained by any sort of play, good or bad, there is no need to lay down laws, or bind those who write or act them to make them as they ought to be made, since, as I say, the object sought for may be secured by any sort. To this I would reply that the same end would be, beyond all comparison, better attained by means of good plays than by those that are not so; for after listening to an artistic and properly constructed play, the hearer will come away enlivened by the jests, instructed by the serious parts, full of admiration at the incidents, his wits sharpened by the arguments, warned by the tricks, all the wiser for the examples, inflamed against vice, and in love with virtue; for in all these ways a good play will stimulate the mind of the hearer be he ever so boorish or dull; and of all impossibilities the greatest is that a play endowed with all these qualities will not entertain, satisfy, and please much more than one wanting in them, like the greater number of those which are commonly acted now-a-days. Nor are the poets who write them to be blamed for this; for some there are among them who are perfectly well aware of their faults, and know what they ought to do; but as plays have become a salable commodity, they say, and with truth, that the actors will not buy them unless they are after this fashion; and so the poet tries to adapt himself to the requirements of the actor who is to pay him for his work. And that this is the truth may be seen by the countless plays that a most fertile wit of these kingdoms has written, with so much brilliancy, so much grace and gaiety, such polished versification, such choice language, such profound reflections, and in a word, so rich in eloquence and elevation of style, that he has filled the world with his fame; and yet, in consequence of his desire to suit the taste of the actors, they have not all, as some of them have, come as near perfection as they ought. Others write plays with such heedlessness that, after they have been acted, the actors have to fly and abscond, afraid of being punished, as they often have been, for having acted something offensive to some king or other, or insulting to some noble family. All which evils, and many more that I say nothing of, would be removed if there were some intelligent and sensible person at the capital to examine all plays before they were acted, not only those produced in the capital itself, but all that were intended to be acted in Spain; without whose approval, seal, and signature, no local magistracy should allow any play to be acted. In that case actors would take care to send their plays to the capital, and could act them in safety, and those who write them would be more careful and take more pains with their work, standing in awe of having to submit it to the strict examination of one who understood the matter; and so good plays would be produced and the objects they aim at happily attained; as well the amusement of the people, as the credit of the wits of Spain, the interest and safety of the actors, and the saving of trouble in inflicting punishment on them. And if the same or some other person were authorised to examine the newly written books of chivalry, no doubt some would appear with all the perfections you have described, enriching our language with the gracious and precious treasure of eloquence, and driving the old books into obscurity before the light of the new ones that would come out for the harmless entertainment, not merely of the idle but of the very busiest; for the bow cannot be always bent, nor can weak human nature exist without some lawful amusement.”\n\nThe canon and the curate had proceeded thus far with their conversation, when the barber, coming forward, joined them, and said to the curate, “This is the spot, señor licentiate, that I said was a good one for fresh and plentiful pasture for the oxen, while we take our noontide rest.”\n\n“And so it seems,” returned the curate, and he told the canon what he proposed to do, on which he too made up his mind to halt with them, attracted by the aspect of the fair valley that lay before their eyes; and to enjoy it as well as the conversation of the curate, to whom he had begun to take a fancy, and also to learn more particulars about the doings of Don Quixote, he desired some of his servants to go on to the inn, which was not far distant, and fetch from it what eatables there might be for the whole party, as he meant to rest for the afternoon where he was; to which one of his servants replied that the sumpter mule, which by this time ought to have reached the inn, carried provisions enough to make it unnecessary to get anything from the inn except barley.\n\n“In that case,” said the canon, “take all the beasts there, and bring the sumpter mule back.”\n\nWhile this was going on, Sancho, perceiving that he could speak to his master without having the curate and the barber, of whom he had his suspicions, present all the time, approached the cage in which Don Quixote was placed, and said, “Señor, to ease my conscience I want to tell you the state of the case as to your enchantment, and that is that these two here, with their faces covered, are the curate of our village and the barber; and I suspect they have hit upon this plan of carrying you off in this fashion, out of pure envy because your worship surpasses them in doing famous deeds; and if this be the truth it follows that you are not enchanted, but hoodwinked and made a fool of. And to prove this I want to ask you one thing; and if you answer me as I believe you will answer, you will be able to lay your finger on the trick, and you will see that you are not enchanted but gone wrong in your wits.”\n\n“Ask what thou wilt, Sancho my son,” returned Don Quixote, “for I will satisfy thee and answer all thou requirest. As to what thou sayest, that these who accompany us yonder are the curate and the barber, our neighbours and acquaintances, it is very possible that they may seem to be those same persons; but that they are so in reality and in fact, believe it not on any account; what thou art to believe and think is that, if they look like them, as thou sayest, it must be that those who have enchanted me have taken this shape and likeness; for it is easy for enchanters to take any form they please, and they may have taken those of our friends in order to make thee think as thou dost, and lead thee into a labyrinth of fancies from which thou wilt find no escape though thou hadst the cord of Theseus; and they may also have done it to make me uncertain in my mind, and unable to conjecture whence this evil comes to me; for if on the one hand thou dost tell me that the barber and curate of our village are here in company with us, and on the other I find myself shut up in a cage, and know in my heart that no power on earth that was not supernatural would have been able to shut me in, what wouldst thou have me say or think, but that my enchantment is of a sort that transcends all I have ever read of in all the histories that deal with knights-errant that have been enchanted? So thou mayest set thy mind at rest as to the idea that they are what thou sayest, for they are as much so as I am a Turk. But touching thy desire to ask me something, say on, and I will answer thee, though thou shouldst ask questions from this till to-morrow morning.”\n\n“May Our Lady be good to me!” said Sancho, lifting up his voice; “and is it possible that your worship is so thick of skull and so short of brains that you cannot see that what I say is the simple truth, and that malice has more to do with your imprisonment and misfortune than enchantment? But as it is so, I will prove plainly to you that you are not enchanted. Now tell me, so may God deliver you from this affliction, and so may you find yourself when you least expect it in the arms of my lady Dulcinea—”\n\n“Leave off conjuring me,” said Don Quixote, “and ask what thou wouldst know; I have already told thee I will answer with all possible precision.”\n\n“That is what I want,” said Sancho; “and what I would know, and have you tell me, without adding or leaving out anything, but telling the whole truth as one expects it to be told, and as it is told, by all who profess arms, as your worship professes them, under the title of knights-errant—”\n\n“I tell thee I will not lie in any particular,” said Don Quixote; “finish thy question; for in truth thou weariest me with all these asseverations, requirements, and precautions, Sancho.”\n\n“Well, I rely on the goodness and truth of my master,” said Sancho; “and so, because it bears upon what we are talking about, I would ask, speaking with all reverence, whether since your worship has been shut up and, as you think, enchanted in this cage, you have felt any desire or inclination to go anywhere, as the saying is?”\n\n“I do not understand ‘going anywhere,’” said Don Quixote; “explain thyself more clearly, Sancho, if thou wouldst have me give an answer to the point.”\n\n“Is it possible,” said Sancho, “that your worship does not understand ‘going anywhere’? Why, the schoolboys know that from the time they were babes. Well then, you must know I mean have you had any desire to do what cannot be avoided?”\n\n“Ah! now I understand thee, Sancho,” said Don Quixote; “yes, often, and even this minute; get me out of this strait, or all will not go right.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLIX.\n\nWHICH TREATS OF THE SHREWD CONVERSATION WHICH SANCHO PANZA HELD WITH HIS MASTER DON QUIXOTE\n“Aha, I have caught you,” said Sancho; “this is what in my heart and soul I was longing to know. Come now, señor, can you deny what is commonly said around us, when a person is out of humour, ‘I don’t know what ails so-and-so, that he neither eats, nor drinks, nor sleeps, nor gives a proper answer to any question; one would think he was enchanted’? From which it is to be gathered that those who do not eat, or drink, or sleep, or do any of the natural acts I am speaking of-that such persons are enchanted; but not those that have the desire your worship has, and drink when drink is given them, and eat when there is anything to eat, and answer every question that is asked them.”\n\n“What thou sayest is true, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote; “but I have already told thee there are many sorts of enchantments, and it may be that in the course of time they have been changed one for another, and that now it may be the way with enchanted people to do all that I do, though they did not do so before; so it is vain to argue or draw inferences against the usage of the time. I know and feel that I am enchanted, and that is enough to ease my conscience; for it would weigh heavily on it if I thought that I was not enchanted, and that in a faint-hearted and cowardly way I allowed myself to lie in this cage, defrauding multitudes of the succour I might afford to those in need and distress, who at this very moment may be in sore want of my aid and protection.”\n\n“Still for all that,” replied Sancho, “I say that, for your greater and fuller satisfaction, it would be well if your worship were to try to get out of this prison (and I promise to do all in my power to help, and even to take you out of it), and see if you could once more mount your good Rocinante, who seems to be enchanted too, he is so melancholy and dejected; and then we might try our chance in looking for adventures again; and if we have no luck there will be time enough to go back to the cage; in which, on the faith of a good and loyal squire, I promise to shut myself up along with your worship, if so be you are so unfortunate, or I so stupid, as not to be able to carry out my plan.”\n\n“I am content to do as thou sayest, brother Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “and when thou seest an opportunity for effecting my release I will obey thee absolutely; but thou wilt see, Sancho, how mistaken thou art in thy conception of my misfortune.”\n\nThe knight-errant and the ill-errant squire kept up their conversation till they reached the place where the curate, the canon, and the barber, who had already dismounted, were waiting for them. The carter at once unyoked the oxen and left them to roam at large about the pleasant green spot, the freshness of which seemed to invite, not enchanted people like Don Quixote, but wide-awake, sensible folk like his squire, who begged the curate to allow his master to leave the cage for a little; for if they did not let him out, the prison might not be as clean as the propriety of such a gentleman as his master required. The curate understood him, and said he would very gladly comply with his request, only that he feared his master, finding himself at liberty, would take to his old courses and make off where nobody could ever find him again.\n\n“I will answer for his not running away,” said Sancho.\n\n“And I also,” said the canon, “especially if he gives me his word as a knight not to leave us without our consent.”\n\nDon Quixote, who was listening to all this, said, “I give it;-moreover one who is enchanted as I am cannot do as he likes with himself; for he who had enchanted him could prevent his moving from one place for three ages, and if he attempted to escape would bring him back flying.”--And that being so, they might as well release him, particularly as it would be to the advantage of all; for, if they did not let him out, he protested he would be unable to avoid offending their nostrils unless they kept their distance.\n\nThe canon took his hand, tied together as they both were, and on his word and promise they unbound him, and rejoiced beyond measure he was to find himself out of the cage. The first thing he did was to stretch himself all over, and then he went to where Rocinante was standing and giving him a couple of slaps on the haunches said, “I still trust in God and in his blessed mother, O flower and mirror of steeds, that we shall soon see ourselves, both of us, as we wish to be, thou with thy master on thy back, and I mounted upon thee, following the calling for which God sent me into the world.” And so saying, accompanied by Sancho, he withdrew to a retired spot, from which he came back much relieved and more eager than ever to put his squire’s scheme into execution.\n\nThe canon gazed at him, wondering at the extraordinary nature of his madness, and that in all his remarks and replies he should show such excellent sense, and only lose his stirrups, as has been already said, when the subject of chivalry was broached. And so, moved by compassion, he said to him, as they all sat on the green grass awaiting the arrival of the provisions:\n\n“Is it possible, gentle sir, that the nauseous and idle reading of books of chivalry can have had such an effect on your worship as to upset your reason so that you fancy yourself enchanted, and the like, all as far from the truth as falsehood itself is? How can there be any human understanding that can persuade itself there ever was all that infinity of Amadises in the world, or all that multitude of famous knights, all those emperors of Trebizond, all those Felixmartes of Hircania, all those palfreys, and damsels-errant, and serpents, and monsters, and giants, and marvellous adventures, and enchantments of every kind, and battles, and prodigious encounters, splendid costumes, love-sick princesses, squires made counts, droll dwarfs, love letters, billings and cooings, swashbuckler women, and, in a word, all that nonsense the books of chivalry contain? For myself, I can only say that when I read them, so long as I do not stop to think that they are all lies and frivolity, they give me a certain amount of pleasure; but when I come to consider what they are, I fling the very best of them at the wall, and would fling it into the fire if there were one at hand, as richly deserving such punishment as cheats and impostors out of the range of ordinary toleration, and as founders of new sects and modes of life, and teachers that lead the ignorant public to believe and accept as truth all the folly they contain. And such is their audacity, they even dare to unsettle the wits of gentlemen of birth and intelligence, as is shown plainly by the way they have served your worship, when they have brought you to such a pass that you have to be shut up in a cage and carried on an ox-cart as one would carry a lion or a tiger from place to place to make money by showing it. Come, Señor Don Quixote, have some compassion for yourself, return to the bosom of common sense, and make use of the liberal share of it that heaven has been pleased to bestow upon you, employing your abundant gifts of mind in some other reading that may serve to benefit your conscience and add to your honour. And if, still led away by your natural bent, you desire to read books of achievements and of chivalry, read the Book of Judges in the Holy Scriptures, for there you will find grand reality, and deeds as true as they are heroic. Lusitania had a Viriatus, Rome a Caesar, Carthage a Hannibal, Greece an Alexander, Castile a Count Fernan Gonzalez, Valencia a Cid, Andalusia a Gonzalo Fernandez, Estremadura a Diego Garcia de Paredes, Jerez a Garci Perez de Vargas, Toledo a Garcilaso, Seville a Don Manuel de Leon, to read of whose valiant deeds will entertain and instruct the loftiest minds and fill them with delight and wonder. Here, Señor Don Quixote, will be reading worthy of your sound understanding; from which you will rise learned in history, in love with virtue, strengthened in goodness, improved in manners, brave without rashness, prudent without cowardice; and all to the honour of God, your own advantage and the glory of La Mancha, whence, I am informed, your worship derives your birth.”\n\nDon Quixote listened with the greatest attention to the canon’s words, and when he found he had finished, after regarding him for some time, he replied to him:\n\n“It appears to me, gentle sir, that your worship’s discourse is intended to persuade me that there never were any knights-errant in the world, and that all the books of chivalry are false, lying, mischievous and useless to the State, and that I have done wrong in reading them, and worse in believing them, and still worse in imitating them, when I undertook to follow the arduous calling of knight-errantry which they set forth; for you deny that there ever were Amadises of Gaul or of Greece, or any other of the knights of whom the books are full.”\n\n“It is all exactly as you state it,” said the canon; to which Don Quixote returned, “You also went on to say that books of this kind had done me much harm, inasmuch as they had upset my senses, and shut me up in a cage, and that it would be better for me to reform and change my studies, and read other truer books which would afford more pleasure and instruction.”\n\n“Just so,” said the canon.\n\n“Well then,” returned Don Quixote, “to my mind it is you who are the one that is out of his wits and enchanted, as you have ventured to utter such blasphemies against a thing so universally acknowledged and accepted as true that whoever denies it, as you do, deserves the same punishment which you say you inflict on the books that irritate you when you read them. For to try to persuade anybody that Amadis, and all the other knights-adventurers with whom the books are filled, never existed, would be like trying to persuade him that the sun does not yield light, or ice cold, or earth nourishment. What wit in the world can persuade another that the story of the Princess Floripes and Guy of Burgundy is not true, or that of Fierabras and the bridge of Mantible, which happened in the time of Charlemagne? For by all that is good it is as true as that it is daylight now; and if it be a lie, it must be a lie too that there was a Hector, or Achilles, or Trojan war, or Twelve Peers of France, or Arthur of England, who still lives changed into a raven, and is unceasingly looked for in his kingdom. One might just as well try to make out that the history of Guarino Mezquino, or of the quest of the Holy Grail, is false, or that the loves of Tristram and the Queen Yseult are apocryphal, as well as those of Guinevere and Lancelot, when there are persons who can almost remember having seen the Dame Quintanona, who was the best cupbearer in Great Britain. And so true is this, that I recollect a grandmother of mine on the father’s side, whenever she saw any dame in a venerable hood, used to say to me, ‘Grandson, that one is like Dame Quintanona,’ from which I conclude that she must have known her, or at least had managed to see some portrait of her. Then who can deny that the story of Pierres and the fair Magalona is true, when even to this day may be seen in the king’s armoury the pin with which the valiant Pierres guided the wooden horse he rode through the air, and it is a trifle bigger than the pole of a cart? And alongside of the pin is Babieca’s saddle, and at Roncesvalles there is Roland’s horn, as large as a large beam; whence we may infer that there were Twelve Peers, and a Pierres, and a Cid, and other knights like them, of the sort people commonly call adventurers. Or perhaps I shall be told, too, that there was no such knight-errant as the valiant Lusitanian Juan de Merlo, who went to Burgundy and in the city of Arras fought with the famous lord of Charny, Mosen Pierres by name, and afterwards in the city of Basle with Mosen Enrique de Remesten, coming out of both encounters covered with fame and honour; or adventures and challenges achieved and delivered, also in Burgundy, by the valiant Spaniards Pedro Barba and Gutierre Quixada (of whose family I come in the direct male line), when they vanquished the sons of the Count of San Polo. I shall be told, too, that Don Fernando de Guevara did not go in quest of adventures to Germany, where he engaged in combat with Micer George, a knight of the house of the Duke of Austria. I shall be told that the jousts of Suero de Quinones, him of the ‘Paso,’ and the emprise of Mosen Luis de Falces against the Castilian knight, Don Gonzalo de Guzman, were mere mockeries; as well as many other achievements of Christian knights of these and foreign realms, which are so authentic and true, that, I repeat, he who denies them must be totally wanting in reason and good sense.”\n\nThe canon was amazed to hear the medley of truth and fiction Don Quixote uttered, and to see how well acquainted he was with everything relating or belonging to the achievements of his knight-errantry; so he said in reply:\n\n“I cannot deny, Señor Don Quixote, that there is some truth in what you say, especially as regards the Spanish knights-errant; and I am willing to grant too that the Twelve Peers of France existed, but I am not disposed to believe that they did all the things that the Archbishop Turpin relates of them. For the truth of the matter is they were knights chosen by the kings of France, and called ‘Peers’ because they were all equal in worth, rank and prowess (at least if they were not they ought to have been), and it was a kind of religious order like those of Santiago and Calatrava in the present day, in which it is assumed that those who take it are valiant knights of distinction and good birth; and just as we say now a Knight of St. John, or of Alcantara, they used to say then a Knight of the Twelve Peers, because twelve equals were chosen for that military order. That there was a Cid, as well as a Bernardo del Carpio, there can be no doubt; but that they did the deeds people say they did, I hold to be very doubtful. In that other matter of the pin of Count Pierres that you speak of, and say is near Babieca’s saddle in the Armoury, I confess my sin; for I am either so stupid or so short-sighted, that, though I have seen the saddle, I have never been able to see the pin, in spite of it being as big as your worship says it is.”\n\n“For all that it is there, without any manner of doubt,” said Don Quixote; “and more by token they say it is inclosed in a sheath of cowhide to keep it from rusting.”\n\n“All that may be,” replied the canon; “but, by the orders I have received, I do not remember seeing it. However, granting it is there, that is no reason why I am bound to believe the stories of all those Amadises and of all that multitude of knights they tell us about, nor is it reasonable that a man like your worship, so worthy, and with so many good qualities, and endowed with such a good understanding, should allow himself to be persuaded that such wild crazy things as are written in those absurd books of chivalry are really true.”\n\n# CHAPTER L.\n\nOF THE SHREWD CONTROVERSY WHICH DON QUIXOTE AND THE CANON HELD, TOGETHER WITH OTHER INCIDENTS\n“A good joke, that!” returned Don Quixote. “Books that have been printed with the king’s licence, and with the approbation of those to whom they have been submitted, and read with universal delight, and extolled by great and small, rich and poor, learned and ignorant, gentle and simple, in a word by people of every sort, of whatever rank or condition they may be--that these should be lies! And above all when they carry such an appearance of truth with them; for they tell us the father, mother, country, kindred, age, place, and the achievements, step by step, and day by day, performed by such a knight or knights! Hush, sir; utter not such blasphemy; trust me I am advising you now to act as a sensible man should; only read them, and you will see the pleasure you will derive from them. For, come, tell me, can there be anything more delightful than to see, as it were, here now displayed before us a vast lake of bubbling pitch with a host of snakes and serpents and lizards, and ferocious and terrible creatures of all sorts swimming about in it, while from the middle of the lake there comes a plaintive voice saying: ‘Knight, whosoever thou art who beholdest this dread lake, if thou wouldst win the prize that lies hidden beneath these dusky waves, prove the valour of thy stout heart and cast thyself into the midst of its dark burning waters, else thou shalt not be worthy to see the mighty wonders contained in the seven castles of the seven Fays that lie beneath this black expanse;’ and then the knight, almost ere the awful voice has ceased, without stopping to consider, without pausing to reflect upon the danger to which he is exposing himself, without even relieving himself of the weight of his massive armour, commending himself to God and to his lady, plunges into the midst of the boiling lake, and when he little looks for it, or knows what his fate is to be, he finds himself among flowery meadows, with which the Elysian fields are not to be compared.\n\n“The sky seems more transparent there, and the sun shines with a strange brilliancy, and a delightful grove of green leafy trees presents itself to the eyes and charms the sight with its verdure, while the ear is soothed by the sweet untutored melody of the countless birds of gay plumage that flit to and fro among the interlacing branches. Here he sees a brook whose limpid waters, like liquid crystal, ripple over fine sands and white pebbles that look like sifted gold and purest pearls. There he perceives a cunningly wrought fountain of many-coloured jasper and polished marble; here another of rustic fashion where the little mussel-shells and the spiral white and yellow mansions of the snail disposed in studious disorder, mingled with fragments of glittering crystal and mock emeralds, make up a work of varied aspect, where art, imitating nature, seems to have outdone it.\n\n“Suddenly there is presented to his sight a strong castle or gorgeous palace with walls of massy gold, turrets of diamond and gates of jacinth; in short, so marvellous is its structure that though the materials of which it is built are nothing less than diamonds, carbuncles, rubies, pearls, gold, and emeralds, the workmanship is still more rare. And after having seen all this, what can be more charming than to see how a bevy of damsels comes forth from the gate of the castle in gay and gorgeous attire, such that, were I to set myself now to depict it as the histories describe it to us, I should never have done; and then how she who seems to be the first among them all takes the bold knight who plunged into the boiling lake by the hand, and without addressing a word to him leads him into the rich palace or castle, and strips him as naked as when his mother bore him, and bathes him in lukewarm water, and anoints him all over with sweet-smelling unguents, and clothes him in a shirt of the softest sendal, all scented and perfumed, while another damsel comes and throws over his shoulders a mantle which is said to be worth at the very least a city, and even more? How charming it is, then, when they tell us how, after all this, they lead him to another chamber where he finds the tables set out in such style that he is filled with amazement and wonder; to see how they pour out water for his hands distilled from amber and sweet-scented flowers; how they seat him on an ivory chair; to see how the damsels wait on him all in profound silence; how they bring him such a variety of dainties so temptingly prepared that the appetite is at a loss which to select; to hear the music that resounds while he is at table, by whom or whence produced he knows not. And then when the repast is over and the tables removed, for the knight to recline in the chair, picking his teeth perhaps as usual, and a damsel, much lovelier than any of the others, to enter unexpectedly by the chamber door, and herself by his side, and begin to tell him what the castle is, and how she is held enchanted there, and other things that amaze the knight and astonish the readers who are perusing his history.\n\n“But I will not expatiate any further upon this, as it may be gathered from it that whatever part of whatever history of a knight-errant one reads, it will fill the reader, whoever he be, with delight and wonder; and take my advice, sir, and, as I said before, read these books and you will see how they will banish any melancholy you may feel and raise your spirits should they be depressed. For myself I can say that since I have been a knight-errant I have become valiant, polite, generous, well-bred, magnanimous, courteous, dauntless, gentle, patient, and have learned to bear hardships, imprisonments, and enchantments; and though it be such a short time since I have seen myself shut up in a cage like a madman, I hope by the might of my arm, if heaven aid me and fortune thwart me not, to see myself king of some kingdom where I may be able to show the gratitude and generosity that dwell in my heart; for by my faith, señor, the poor man is incapacitated from showing the virtue of generosity to anyone, though he may possess it in the highest degree; and gratitude that consists of disposition only is a dead thing, just as faith without works is dead. For this reason I should be glad were fortune soon to offer me some opportunity of making myself an emperor, so as to show my heart in doing good to my friends, particularly to this poor Sancho Panza, my squire, who is the best fellow in the world; and I would gladly give him a county I have promised him this ever so long, only that I am afraid he has not the capacity to govern his realm.”\n\nSancho partly heard these last words of his master, and said to him, “Strive hard you, Señor Don Quixote, to give me that county so often promised by you and so long looked for by me, for I promise you there will be no want of capacity in me to govern it; and even if there is, I have heard say there are men in the world who farm seigniories, paying so much a year, and they themselves taking charge of the government, while the lord, with his legs stretched out, enjoys the revenue they pay him, without troubling himself about anything else. That’s what I’ll do, and not stand haggling over trifles, but wash my hands at once of the whole business, and enjoy my rents like a duke, and let things go their own way.”\n\n“That, brother Sancho,” said the canon, “only holds good as far as the enjoyment of the revenue goes; but the lord of the seigniory must attend to the administration of justice, and here capacity and sound judgment come in, and above all a firm determination to find out the truth; for if this be wanting in the beginning, the middle and the end will always go wrong; and God as commonly aids the honest intentions of the simple as he frustrates the evil designs of the crafty.”\n\n“I don’t understand those philosophies,” returned Sancho Panza; “all I know is I would I had the county as soon as I shall know how to govern it; for I have as much soul as another, and as much body as anyone, and I shall be as much king of my realm as any other of his; and being so I should do as I liked, and doing as I liked I should please myself, and pleasing myself I should be content, and when one is content he has nothing more to desire, and when one has nothing more to desire there is an end of it; so let the county come, and God he with you, and let us see one another, as one blind man said to the other.”\n\n“That is not bad philosophy thou art talking, Sancho,” said the canon; “but for all that there is a good deal to be said on this matter of counties.”\n\nTo which Don Quixote returned, “I know not what more there is to be said; I only guide myself by the example set me by the great Amadis of Gaul, when he made his squire count of the Insula Firme; and so, without any scruples of conscience, I can make a count of Sancho Panza, for he is one of the best squires that ever knight-errant had.”\n\nThe canon was astonished at the methodical nonsense (if nonsense be capable of method) that Don Quixote uttered, at the way in which he had described the adventure of the knight of the lake, at the impression that the deliberate lies of the books he read had made upon him, and lastly he marvelled at the simplicity of Sancho, who desired so eagerly to obtain the county his master had promised him.\n\nBy this time the canon’s servants, who had gone to the inn to fetch the sumpter mule, had returned, and making a carpet and the green grass of the meadow serve as a table, they seated themselves in the shade of some trees and made their repast there, that the carter might not be deprived of the advantage of the spot, as has been already said. As they were eating they suddenly heard a loud noise and the sound of a bell that seemed to come from among some brambles and thick bushes that were close by, and the same instant they observed a beautiful goat, spotted all over black, white, and brown, spring out of the thicket with a goatherd after it, calling to it and uttering the usual cries to make it stop or turn back to the fold. The fugitive goat, scared and frightened, ran towards the company as if seeking their protection and then stood still, and the goatherd coming up seized it by the horns and began to talk to it as if it were possessed of reason and understanding: “Ah wanderer, wanderer, Spotty, Spotty; how have you gone limping all this time? What wolves have frightened you, my daughter? Won’t you tell me what is the matter, my beauty? But what else can it be except that you are a she, and cannot keep quiet? A plague on your humours and the humours of those you take after! Come back, come back, my darling; and if you will not be so happy, at any rate you will be safe in the fold or with your companions; for if you who ought to keep and lead them, go wandering astray, what will become of them?”\n\nThe goatherd’s talk amused all who heard it, but especially the canon, who said to him, “As you live, brother, take it easy, and be not in such a hurry to drive this goat back to the fold; for, being a female, as you say, she will follow her natural instinct in spite of all you can do to prevent it. Take this morsel and drink a sup, and that will soothe your irritation, and in the meantime the goat will rest herself,” and so saying, he handed him the loins of a cold rabbit on a fork.\n\nThe goatherd took it with thanks, and drank and calmed himself, and then said, “I should be sorry if your worships were to take me for a simpleton for having spoken so seriously as I did to this animal; but the truth is there is a certain mystery in the words I used. I am a clown, but not so much of one but that I know how to behave to men and to beasts.”\n\n“That I can well believe,” said the curate, “for I know already by experience that the woods breed men of learning, and shepherds’ harbour philosophers.”\n\n“At all events, señor,” returned the goatherd, “they shelter men of experience; and that you may see the truth of this and grasp it, though I may seem to put myself forward without being asked, I will, if it will not tire you, gentlemen, and you will give me your attention for a little, tell you a true story which will confirm this gentleman’s word (and he pointed to the curate) as well as my own.”\n\nTo this Don Quixote replied, “Seeing that this affair has a certain colour of chivalry about it, I for my part, brother, will hear you most gladly, and so will all these gentlemen, from the high intelligence they possess and their love of curious novelties that interest, charm, and entertain the mind, as I feel quite sure your story will do. So begin, friend, for we are all prepared to listen.”\n\n“I draw my stakes,” said Sancho, “and will retreat with this pasty to the brook there, where I mean to victual myself for three days; for I have heard my lord, Don Quixote, say that a knight-errant’s squire should eat until he can hold no more, whenever he has the chance, because it often happens them to get by accident into a wood so thick that they cannot find a way out of it for six days; and if the man is not well filled or his alforjas well stored, there he may stay, as very often he does, turned into a dried mummy.”\n\n“Thou art in the right of it, Sancho,” said Don Quixote; “go where thou wilt and eat all thou canst, for I have had enough, and only want to give my mind its refreshment, as I shall by listening to this good fellow’s story.”\n\n“It is what we shall all do,” said the canon; and then begged the goatherd to begin the promised tale.\n\nThe goatherd gave the goat which he held by the horns a couple of slaps on the back, saying, “Lie down here beside me, Spotty, for we have time enough to return to our fold.” The goat seemed to understand him, for as her master seated himself, she stretched herself quietly beside him and looked up in his face to show him she was all attention to what he was going to say, and then in these words he began his story.\n\n# CHAPTER LI.\n\nWHICH DEALS WITH WHAT THE GOATHERD TOLD THOSE WHO WERE CARRYING OFF DON QUIXOTE\nThree leagues from this valley there is a village which, though small, is one of the richest in all this neighbourhood, and in it there lived a farmer, a very worthy man, and so much respected that, although to be so is the natural consequence of being rich, he was even more respected for his virtue than for the wealth he had acquired. But what made him still more fortunate, as he said himself, was having a daughter of such exceeding beauty, rare intelligence, gracefulness, and virtue, that everyone who knew her and beheld her marvelled at the extraordinary gifts with which heaven and nature had endowed her. As a child she was beautiful, she continued to grow in beauty, and at the age of sixteen she was most lovely. The fame of her beauty began to spread abroad through all the villages around--but why do I say the villages around, merely, when it spread to distant cities, and even made its way into the halls of royalty and reached the ears of people of every class, who came from all sides to see her as if to see something rare and curious, or some wonder-working image?\n\nHer father watched over her and she watched over herself; for there are no locks, or guards, or bolts that can protect a young girl better than her own modesty. The wealth of the father and the beauty of the daughter led many neighbours as well as strangers to seek her for a wife; but he, as one might well be who had the disposal of so rich a jewel, was perplexed and unable to make up his mind to which of her countless suitors he should entrust her. I was one among the many who felt a desire so natural, and, as her father knew who I was, and I was of the same town, of pure blood, in the bloom of life, and very rich in possessions, I had great hopes of success. There was another of the same place and qualifications who also sought her, and this made her father’s choice hang in the balance, for he felt that on either of us his daughter would be well bestowed; so to escape from this state of perplexity he resolved to refer the matter to Leandra (for that is the name of the rich damsel who has reduced me to misery), reflecting that as we were both equal it would be best to leave it to his dear daughter to choose according to her inclination--a course that is worthy of imitation by all fathers who wish to settle their children in life. I do not mean that they ought to leave them to make a choice of what is contemptible and bad, but that they should place before them what is good and then allow them to make a good choice as they please. I do not know which Leandra chose; I only know her father put us both off with the tender age of his daughter and vague words that neither bound him nor dismissed us. My rival is called Anselmo and I myself Eugenio--that you may know the names of the personages that figure in this tragedy, the end of which is still in suspense, though it is plain to see it must be disastrous.\n\nAbout this time there arrived in our town one Vicente de la Roca, the son of a poor peasant of the same town, the said Vicente having returned from service as a soldier in Italy and divers other parts. A captain who chanced to pass that way with his company had carried him off from our village when he was a boy of about twelve years, and now twelve years later the young man came back in a soldier’s uniform, arrayed in a thousand colours, and all over glass trinkets and fine steel chains. To-day he would appear in one gay dress, to-morrow in another; but all flimsy and gaudy, of little substance and less worth. The peasant folk, who are naturally malicious, and when they have nothing to do can be malice itself, remarked all this, and took note of his finery and jewellery, piece by piece, and discovered that he had three suits of different colours, with garters and stockings to match; but he made so many arrangements and combinations out of them, that if they had not counted them, anyone would have sworn that he had made a display of more than ten suits of clothes and twenty plumes. Do not look upon all this that I am telling you about the clothes as uncalled for or spun out, for they have a great deal to do with the story. He used to seat himself on a bench under the great poplar in our plaza, and there he would keep us all hanging open-mouthed on the stories he told us of his exploits. There was no country on the face of the globe he had not seen, nor battle he had not been engaged in; he had killed more Moors than there are in Morocco and Tunis, and fought more single combats, according to his own account, than Garcilaso, Diego Garcia de Paredes and a thousand others he named, and out of all he had come victorious without losing a drop of blood. On the other hand he showed marks of wounds, which, though they could not be made out, he said were gunshot wounds received in divers encounters and actions. Lastly, with monstrous impudence he used to say “you” to his equals and even those who knew what he was, and declare that his arm was his father and his deeds his pedigree, and that being a soldier he was as good as the king himself. And to add to these swaggering ways he was a trifle of a musician, and played the guitar with such a flourish that some said he made it speak; nor did his accomplishments end here, for he was something of a poet too, and on every trifle that happened in the town he made a ballad a league long.\n\nThis soldier, then, that I have described, this Vicente de la Roca, this bravo, gallant, musician, poet, was often seen and watched by Leandra from a window of her house which looked out on the plaza. The glitter of his showy attire took her fancy, his ballads bewitched her (for he gave away twenty copies of every one he made), the tales of his exploits which he told about himself came to her ears; and in short, as the devil no doubt had arranged it, she fell in love with him before the presumption of making love to her had suggested itself to him; and as in love-affairs none are more easily brought to an issue than those which have the inclination of the lady for an ally, Leandra and Vicente came to an understanding without any difficulty; and before any of her numerous suitors had any suspicion of her design, she had already carried it into effect, having left the house of her dearly beloved father (for mother she had none), and disappeared from the village with the soldier, who came more triumphantly out of this enterprise than out of any of the large number he laid claim to. All the village and all who heard of it were amazed at the affair; I was aghast, Anselmo thunderstruck, her father full of grief, her relations indignant, the authorities all in a ferment, the officers of the Brotherhood in arms. They scoured the roads, they searched the woods and all quarters, and at the end of three days they found the flighty Leandra in a mountain cave, stript to her shift, and robbed of all the money and precious jewels she had carried away from home with her. They brought her back to her unhappy father, and questioned her as to her misfortune, and she confessed without pressure that Vicente de la Roca had deceived her, and under promise of marrying her had induced her to leave her father’s house, as he meant to take her to the richest and most delightful city in the whole world, which was Naples; and that she, ill-advised and deluded, had believed him, and robbed her father, and handed over all to him the night she disappeared; and that he had carried her away to a rugged mountain and shut her up in the cave where they had found her. She said, moreover, that the soldier, without robbing her of her honour, had taken from her everything she had, and made off, leaving her in the cave, a thing that still further surprised everybody. It was not easy for us to credit the young man’s continence, but she asserted it with such earnestness that it helped to console her distressed father, who thought nothing of what had been taken since the jewel that once lost can never be recovered had been left to his daughter. The same day that Leandra made her appearance her father removed her from our sight and took her away to shut her up in a convent in a town near this, in the hope that time may wear away some of the disgrace she has incurred. Leandra’s youth furnished an excuse for her fault, at least with those to whom it was of no consequence whether she was good or bad; but those who knew her shrewdness and intelligence did not attribute her misdemeanour to ignorance but to wantonness and the natural disposition of women, which is for the most part flighty and ill-regulated.\n\nLeandra withdrawn from sight, Anselmo’s eyes grew blind, or at any rate found nothing to look at that gave them any pleasure, and mine were in darkness without a ray of light to direct them to anything enjoyable while Leandra was away. Our melancholy grew greater, our patience grew less; we cursed the soldier’s finery and railed at the carelessness of Leandra’s father. At last Anselmo and I agreed to leave the village and come to this valley; and, he feeding a great flock of sheep of his own, and I a large herd of goats of mine, we pass our life among the trees, giving vent to our sorrows, together singing the fair Leandra’s praises, or upbraiding her, or else sighing alone, and to heaven pouring forth our complaints in solitude. Following our example, many more of Leandra’s lovers have come to these rude mountains and adopted our mode of life, and they are so numerous that one would fancy the place had been turned into the pastoral Arcadia, so full is it of shepherds and sheep-folds; nor is there a spot in it where the name of the fair Leandra is not heard. Here one curses her and calls her capricious, fickle, and immodest, there another condemns her as frail and frivolous; this pardons and absolves her, that spurns and reviles her; one extols her beauty, another assails her character, and in short all abuse her, and all adore her, and to such a pitch has this general infatuation gone that there are some who complain of her scorn without ever having exchanged a word with her, and even some that bewail and mourn the raging fever of jealousy, for which she never gave anyone cause, for, as I have already said, her misconduct was known before her passion. There is no nook among the rocks, no brookside, no shade beneath the trees that is not haunted by some shepherd telling his woes to the breezes; wherever there is an echo it repeats the name of Leandra; the mountains ring with “Leandra,” “Leandra” murmur the brooks, and Leandra keeps us all bewildered and bewitched, hoping without hope and fearing without knowing what we fear. Of all this silly set the one that shows the least and also the most sense is my rival Anselmo, for having so many other things to complain of, he only complains of separation, and to the accompaniment of a rebeck, which he plays admirably, he sings his complaints in verses that show his ingenuity. I follow another, easier, and to my mind wiser course, and that is to rail at the frivolity of women, at their inconstancy, their double dealing, their broken promises, their unkept pledges, and in short the want of reflection they show in fixing their affections and inclinations. This, sirs, was the reason of words and expressions I made use of to this goat when I came up just now; for as she is a female I have a contempt for her, though she is the best in all my fold. This is the story I promised to tell you, and if I have been tedious in telling it, I will not be slow to serve you; my hut is close by, and I have fresh milk and dainty cheese there, as well as a variety of toothsome fruit, no less pleasing to the eye than to the palate.\n\n# CHAPTER LII.\n\nOF THE QUARREL THAT DON QUIXOTE HAD WITH THE GOATHERD, TOGETHER WITH THE RARE ADVENTURE OF THE PENITENTS, WHICH WITH AN EXPENDITURE OF SWEAT HE BROUGHT TO A HAPPY CONCLUSION\nThe goatherd’s tale gave great satisfaction to all the hearers, and the canon especially enjoyed it, for he had remarked with particular attention the manner in which it had been told, which was as unlike the manner of a clownish goatherd as it was like that of a polished city wit; and he observed that the curate had been quite right in saying that the woods bred men of learning. They all offered their services to Eugenio but he who showed himself most liberal in this way was Don Quixote, who said to him, “Most assuredly, brother goatherd, if I found myself in a position to attempt any adventure, I would, this very instant, set out on your behalf, and would rescue Leandra from that convent (where no doubt she is kept against her will), in spite of the abbess and all who might try to prevent me, and would place her in your hands to deal with her according to your will and pleasure, observing, however, the laws of chivalry which lay down that no violence of any kind is to be offered to any damsel. But I trust in God our Lord that the might of one malignant enchanter may not prove so great but that the power of another better disposed may prove superior to it, and then I promise you my support and assistance, as I am bound to do by my profession, which is none other than to give aid to the weak and needy.”\n\nThe goatherd eyed him, and noticing Don Quixote’s sorry appearance and looks, he was filled with wonder, and asked the barber, who was next him, “Señor, who is this man who makes such a figure and talks in such a strain?”\n\n“Who should it be,” said the barber, “but the famous Don Quixote of La Mancha, the undoer of injustice, the righter of wrongs, the protector of damsels, the terror of giants, and the winner of battles?”\n\n“That,” said the goatherd, “sounds like what one reads in the books of the knights-errant, who did all that you say this man does; though it is my belief that either you are joking, or else this gentleman has empty lodgings in his head.”\n\n“You are a great scoundrel,” said Don Quixote, “and it is you who are empty and a fool. I am fuller than ever was the whoreson bitch that bore you;” and passing from words to deeds, he caught up a loaf that was near him and sent it full in the goatherd’s face, with such force that he flattened his nose; but the goatherd, who did not understand jokes, and found himself roughly handled in such good earnest, paying no respect to carpet, tablecloth, or diners, sprang upon Don Quixote, and seizing him by the throat with both hands would no doubt have throttled him, had not Sancho Panza that instant come to the rescue, and grasping him by the shoulders flung him down on the table, smashing plates, breaking glasses, and upsetting and scattering everything on it. Don Quixote, finding himself free, strove to get on top of the goatherd, who, with his face covered with blood, and soundly kicked by Sancho, was on all fours feeling about for one of the table-knives to take a bloody revenge with. The canon and the curate, however, prevented him, but the barber so contrived it that he got Don Quixote under him, and rained down upon him such a shower of fisticuffs that the poor knight’s face streamed with blood as freely as his own. The canon and the curate were bursting with laughter, the officers were capering with delight, and both the one and the other hissed them on as they do dogs that are worrying one another in a fight. Sancho alone was frantic, for he could not free himself from the grasp of one of the canon’s servants, who kept him from going to his master’s assistance.\n\nAt last, while they were all, with the exception of the two bruisers who were mauling each other, in high glee and enjoyment, they heard a trumpet sound a note so doleful that it made them all look in the direction whence the sound seemed to come. But the one that was most excited by hearing it was Don Quixote, who though sorely against his will he was under the goatherd, and something more than pretty well pummelled, said to him, “Brother devil (for it is impossible but that thou must be one since thou hast had might and strength enough to overcome mine), I ask thee to agree to a truce for but one hour for the solemn note of yonder trumpet that falls on our ears seems to me to summon me to some new adventure.” The goatherd, who was by this time tired of pummelling and being pummelled, released him at once, and Don Quixote rising to his feet and turning his eyes to the quarter where the sound had been heard, suddenly saw coming down the slope of a hill several men clad in white like penitents.\n\nThe fact was that the clouds had that year withheld their moisture from the earth, and in all the villages of the district they were organising processions, rogations, and penances, imploring God to open the hands of his mercy and send the rain; and to this end the people of a village that was hard by were going in procession to a holy hermitage there was on one side of that valley. Don Quixote when he saw the strange garb of the penitents, without reflecting how often he had seen it before, took it into his head that this was a case of adventure, and that it fell to him alone as a knight-errant to engage in it; and he was all the more confirmed in this notion, by the idea that an image draped in black they had with them was some illustrious lady that these villains and discourteous thieves were carrying off by force. As soon as this occurred to him he ran with all speed to Rocinante who was grazing at large, and taking the bridle and the buckler from the saddle-bow, he had him bridled in an instant, and calling to Sancho for his sword he mounted Rocinante, braced his buckler on his arm, and in a loud voice exclaimed to those who stood by, “Now, noble company, ye shall see how important it is that there should be knights in the world professing the of knight-errantry; now, I say, ye shall see, by the deliverance of that worthy lady who is borne captive there, whether knights-errant deserve to be held in estimation,” and so saying he brought his legs to bear on Rocinante--for he had no spurs--and at a full canter (for in all this veracious history we never read of Rocinante fairly galloping) set off to encounter the penitents, though the curate, the canon, and the barber ran to prevent him. But it was out of their power, nor did he even stop for the shouts of Sancho calling after him, “Where are you going, Señor Don Quixote? What devils have possessed you to set you on against our Catholic faith? Plague take me! mind, that is a procession of penitents, and the lady they are carrying on that stand there is the blessed image of the immaculate Virgin. Take care what you are doing, señor, for this time it may be safely said you don’t know what you are about.” Sancho laboured in vain, for his master was so bent on coming to quarters with these sheeted figures and releasing the lady in black that he did not hear a word; and even had he heard, he would not have turned back if the king had ordered him. He came up with the procession and reined in Rocinante, who was already anxious enough to slacken speed a little, and in a hoarse, excited voice he exclaimed, “You who hide your faces, perhaps because you are not good subjects, pay attention and listen to what I am about to say to you.” The first to halt were those who were carrying the image, and one of the four ecclesiastics who were chanting the Litany, struck by the strange figure of Don Quixote, the leanness of Rocinante, and the other ludicrous peculiarities he observed, said in reply to him, “Brother, if you have anything to say to us say it quickly, for these brethren are whipping themselves, and we cannot stop, nor is it reasonable we should stop to hear anything, unless indeed it is short enough to be said in two words.”\n\n“I will say it in one,” replied Don Quixote, “and it is this; that at once, this very instant, ye release that fair lady whose tears and sad aspect show plainly that ye are carrying her off against her will, and that ye have committed some scandalous outrage against her; and I, who was born into the world to redress all such like wrongs, will not permit you to advance another step until you have restored to her the liberty she pines for and deserves.”\n\nFrom these words all the hearers concluded that he must be a madman, and began to laugh heartily, and their laughter acted like gunpowder on Don Quixote’s fury, for drawing his sword without another word he made a rush at the stand. One of those who supported it, leaving the burden to his comrades, advanced to meet him, flourishing a forked stick that he had for propping up the stand when resting, and with this he caught a mighty cut Don Quixote made at him that severed it in two; but with the portion that remained in his hand he dealt such a thwack on the shoulder of Don Quixote’s sword arm (which the buckler could not protect against the clownish assault) that poor Don Quixote came to the ground in a sad plight.\n\nSancho Panza, who was coming on close behind puffing and blowing, seeing him fall, cried out to his assailant not to strike him again, for he was poor enchanted knight, who had never harmed anyone all the days of his life; but what checked the clown was, not Sancho’s shouting, but seeing that Don Quixote did not stir hand or foot; and so, fancying he had killed him, he hastily hitched up his tunic under his girdle and took to his heels across the country like a deer.\n\nBy this time all Don Quixote’s companions had come up to where he lay; but the processionists seeing them come running, and with them the officers of the Brotherhood with their crossbows, apprehended mischief, and clustering round the image, raised their hoods, and grasped their scourges, as the priests did their tapers, and awaited the attack, resolved to defend themselves and even to take the offensive against their assailants if they could. Fortune, however, arranged the matter better than they expected, for all Sancho did was to fling himself on his master’s body, raising over him the most doleful and laughable lamentation that ever was heard, for he believed he was dead. The curate was known to another curate who walked in the procession, and their recognition of one another set at rest the apprehensions of both parties; the first then told the other in two words who Don Quixote was, and he and the whole troop of penitents went to see if the poor gentleman was dead, and heard Sancho Panza saying, with tears in his eyes, “Oh flower of chivalry, that with one blow of a stick hast ended the course of thy well-spent life! Oh pride of thy race, honour and glory of all La Mancha, nay, of all the world, that for want of thee will be full of evil-doers, no longer in fear of punishment for their misdeeds! Oh thou, generous above all the Alexanders, since for only eight months of service thou hast given me the best island the sea girds or surrounds! Humble with the proud, haughty with the humble, encounterer of dangers, endurer of outrages, enamoured without reason, imitator of the good, scourge of the wicked, enemy of the mean, in short, knight-errant, which is all that can be said!”\n\nAt the cries and moans of Sancho, Don Quixote came to himself, and the first word he said was, “He who lives separated from you, sweetest Dulcinea, has greater miseries to endure than these. Aid me, friend Sancho, to mount the enchanted cart, for I am not in a condition to press the saddle of Rocinante, as this shoulder is all knocked to pieces.”\n\n“That I will do with all my heart, señor,” said Sancho; “and let us return to our village with these gentlemen, who seek your good, and there we will prepare for making another sally, which may turn out more profitable and creditable to us.”\n\n“Thou art right, Sancho,” returned Don Quixote; “It will be wise to let the malign influence of the stars which now prevails pass off.”\n\nThe canon, the curate, and the barber told him he would act very wisely in doing as he said; and so, highly amused at Sancho Panza’s simplicities, they placed Don Quixote in the cart as before. The procession once more formed itself in order and proceeded on its road; the goatherd took his leave of the party; the officers of the Brotherhood declined to go any farther, and the curate paid them what was due to them; the canon begged the curate to let him know how Don Quixote did, whether he was cured of his madness or still suffered from it, and then begged leave to continue his journey; in short, they all separated and went their ways, leaving to themselves the curate and the barber, Don Quixote, Sancho Panza, and the good Rocinante, who regarded everything with as great resignation as his master. The carter yoked his oxen and made Don Quixote comfortable on a truss of hay, and at his usual deliberate pace took the road the curate directed, and at the end of six days they reached Don Quixote’s village, and entered it about the middle of the day, which it so happened was a Sunday, and the people were all in the plaza, through which Don Quixote’s cart passed. They all flocked to see what was in the cart, and when they recognised their townsman they were filled with amazement, and a boy ran off to bring the news to his housekeeper and his niece that their master and uncle had come back all lean and yellow and stretched on a truss of hay on an ox-cart. It was piteous to hear the cries the two good ladies raised, how they beat their breasts and poured out fresh maledictions on those accursed books of chivalry; all which was renewed when they saw Don Quixote coming in at the gate.\n\nAt the news of Don Quixote’s arrival Sancho Panza’s wife came running, for she by this time knew that her husband had gone away with him as his squire, and on seeing Sancho, the first thing she asked him was if the ass was well. Sancho replied that he was, better than his master was.\n\n“Thanks be to God,” said she, “for being so good to me; but now tell me, my friend, what have you made by your squirings? What gown have you brought me back? What shoes for your children?”\n\n“I bring nothing of that sort, wife,” said Sancho; “though I bring other things of more consequence and value.”\n\n“I am very glad of that,” returned his wife; “show me these things of more value and consequence, my friend; for I want to see them to cheer my heart that has been so sad and heavy all these ages that you have been away.”\n\n“I will show them to you at home, wife,” said Sancho; “be content for the present; for if it please God that we should again go on our travels in search of adventures, you will soon see me a count, or governor of an island, and that not one of those everyday ones, but the best that is to be had.”\n\n“Heaven grant it, husband,” said she, “for indeed we have need of it. But tell me, what’s this about islands, for I don’t understand it?”\n\n“Honey is not for the mouth of the ass,” returned Sancho; “all in good time thou shalt see, wife--nay, thou wilt be surprised to hear thyself called ‘your ladyship’ by all thy vassals.”\n\n“What are you talking about, Sancho, with your ladyships, islands, and vassals?” returned Teresa Panza--for so Sancho’s wife was called, though they were not relations, for in La Mancha it is customary for wives to take their husbands’ surnames.\n\n“Don’t be in such a hurry to know all this, Teresa,” said Sancho; “it is enough that I am telling you the truth, so shut your mouth. But I may tell you this much by the way, that there is nothing in the world more delightful than to be a person of consideration, squire to a knight-errant, and a seeker of adventures. To be sure most of those one finds do not end as pleasantly as one could wish, for out of a hundred, ninety-nine will turn out cross and contrary. I know it by experience, for out of some I came blanketed, and out of others belaboured. Still, for all that, it is a fine thing to be on the look-out for what may happen, crossing mountains, searching woods, climbing rocks, visiting castles, putting up at inns, all at free quarters, and devil take the maravedi to pay.”\n\nWhile this conversation passed between Sancho Panza and his wife, Don Quixote’s housekeeper and niece took him in and undressed him and laid him in his old bed. He eyed them askance, and could not make out where he was. The curate charged his niece to be very careful to make her uncle comfortable and to keep a watch over him lest he should make his escape from them again, telling her what they had been obliged to do to bring him home. On this the pair once more lifted up their voices and renewed their maledictions upon the books of chivalry, and implored heaven to plunge the authors of such lies and nonsense into the midst of the bottomless pit. They were, in short, kept in anxiety and dread lest their uncle and master should give them the slip the moment he found himself somewhat better, and as they feared so it fell out.\n\nBut the author of this history, though he has devoted research and industry to the discovery of the deeds achieved by Don Quixote in his third sally, has been unable to obtain any information respecting them, at any rate derived from authentic documents; tradition has merely preserved in the memory of La Mancha the fact that Don Quixote, the third time he sallied forth from his home, betook himself to Saragossa, where he was present at some famous jousts which came off in that city, and that he had adventures there worthy of his valour and high intelligence. Of his end and death he could learn no particulars, nor would he have ascertained it or known of it, if good fortune had not produced an old physician for him who had in his possession a leaden box, which, according to his account, had been discovered among the crumbling foundations of an ancient hermitage that was being rebuilt; in which box were found certain parchment manuscripts in Gothic character, but in Castilian verse, containing many of his achievements, and setting forth the beauty of Dulcinea, the form of Rocinante, the fidelity of Sancho Panza, and the burial of Don Quixote himself, together with sundry epitaphs and eulogies on his life and character; but all that could be read and deciphered were those which the trustworthy author of this new and unparalleled history here presents. And the said author asks of those that shall read it nothing in return for the vast toil which it has cost him in examining and searching the Manchegan archives in order to bring it to light, save that they give him the same credit that people of sense give to the books of chivalry that pervade the world and are so popular; for with this he will consider himself amply paid and fully satisfied, and will be encouraged to seek out and produce other histories, if not as truthful, at least equal in invention and not less entertaining. The first words written on the parchment found in the leaden box were these:\n\nTHE ACADEMICIANS OF ARGAMASILLA, A VILLAGE OF LA MANCHA, ON THE LIFE AND DEATH OF DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA, HOC SCRIPSERUNT MONICONGO, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA,\n\n# ON THE TOMB OF DON QUIXOTE EPITAPH\n\nThe scatterbrain that gave La Mancha more Rich spoils than Jason’s; who a point so keen Had to his wit, and happier far had been If his wit’s weathercock a blunter bore; The arm renowned far as Gaeta’s shore, Cathay, and all the lands that lie between; The muse discreet and terrible in mien As ever wrote on brass in days of yore; He who surpassed the Amadises all, And who as naught the Galaors accounted, Supported by his love and gallantry: Who made the Belianises sing small, And sought renown on Rocinante mounted; Here, underneath this cold stone, doth he lie.\n\nPANIAGUADO, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, IN LAUDEM DULCINEAE DEL TOBOSO\n\nShe, whose full features may be here descried, High-bosomed, with a bearing of disdain, Is Dulcinea, she for whom in vain The great Don Quixote of La Mancha sighed. For her, Toboso’s queen, from side to side He traversed the grim sierra, the champaign Of Aranjuez, and Montiel’s famous plain: On Rocinante oft a weary ride. Malignant planets, cruel destiny, Pursued them both, the fair Manchegan dame, And the unconquered star of chivalry. Nor youth nor beauty saved her from the claim Of death; he paid love’s bitter penalty, And left the marble to preserve his name.\n\nCAPRICHOSO, A MOST ACUTE ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, IN PRAISE OF ROCINANTE, STEED OF DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA\n\nOn that proud throne of diamantine sheen, Which the blood-reeking feet of Mars degrade, The mad Manchegan’s banner now hath been By him in all its bravery displayed. There hath he hung his arms and trenchant blade Wherewith, achieving deeds till now unseen, He slays, lays low, cleaves, hews; but art hath made A novel style for our new paladin. If Amadis be the proud boast of Gaul, If by his progeny the fame of Greece Through all the regions of the earth be spread, Great Quixote crowned in grim Bellona’s hall To-day exalts La Mancha over these, And above Greece or Gaul she holds her head. Nor ends his glory here, for his good steed Doth Brillador and Bayard far exceed; As mettled steeds compared with Rocinante, The reputation they have won is scanty.\n\nThe worthy Sancho Panza here you see; A great soul once was in that body small, Nor was there squire upon this earthly ball So plain and simple, or of guile so free. Within an ace of being Count was he, And would have been but for the spite and gall Of this vile age, mean and illiberal, That cannot even let a donkey be. For mounted on an ass (excuse the word), By Rocinante’s side this gentle squire Was wont his wandering master to attend. Delusive hopes that lure the common herd With promises of ease, the heart’s desire, In shadows, dreams, and smoke ye always end.\n\nCACHIDIABLO, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DON QUIXOTE EPITAPH\n\n# CACHIDIABLO, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DON QUIXOTE EPITAPH\n\nThe knight lies here below, Ill-errant and bruised sore, Whom Rocinante bore In his wanderings to and fro. By the side of the knight is laid Stolid man Sancho too, Than whom a squire more true Was not in the esquire trade.\n\nTIQUITOC, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DULCINEA DEL TOBOSO\n\n# TIQUITOC, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DULCINEA DEL TOBOSO\n\nEPITAPH Here Dulcinea lies. Plump was she and robust: Now she is ashes and dust: The end of all flesh that dies. A lady of high degree, With the port of a lofty dame, And the great Don Quixote’s flame, And the pride of her village was she.\n\nThese were all the verses that could be deciphered; the rest, the writing being worm-eaten, were handed over to one of the Academicians to make out their meaning conjecturally. We have been informed that at the cost of many sleepless nights and much toil he has succeeded, and that he means to publish them in hopes of Don Quixote’s third sally.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-5921"]="오랫동안 내가 가장 아끼던 계획을 포기하고 지금의 작업을 택하게 된 것은 적잖은 망설임 끝의 일이었다. 그 계획이란 이제는 다소 구하기 어려운 책이 되어 버린 셸턴의 「돈키호테」를 새로이 펴내는 일이었다. 셸턴의 그 생기 넘치는 옛 번역은 온갖 결점에도 불구하고, 아무리 능숙하거나 정확한 현대 번역이라 해도 결코 지니지 못할 매력을 품고 있다고 여기는 사람들이 있는데, 나 자신도 그중 하나임을 고백한다. 셸턴은 세르반테스와 같은 세대에 속했다는, 값으로 따질 수 없는 이점을 누렸다. 그에게 「돈키호테」는 동시대인만이 느낄 수 있는 생명력을 지녔으며, 세르반테스가 본 그대로 사물을 보는 데 극적인 노력을 들일 필요가 없었다. 그의 언어에는 시대착오가 없으니, 그는 세르반테스의 스페인어를 셰익스피어의 영어로 옮겨 놓았다. 셰익스피어 자신도 이 책을 알고 있었을 가능성이 매우 높다. 어쩌면 그는 마지막 여행 중 어느 길에서 안장주머니에 이 책을 넣어 스트랫퍼드의 집으로 가져갔을지도 모르고, 뉴플레이스의 뽕나무 아래에서 그 책장들 속의 한 핏줄과도 같은 천재와 손을 맞잡았을지도 모른다.\n\n그러나 셸턴이 어느 정도의 인기조차 누리리라 기대하는 것이 헛된 일임은 곧 내게 분명해졌다. 그의 멋들어진, 켜켜이 묵은 영어는 의심할 바 없이 소수의 사람들에게는 음미되겠지만, 어디까지나 소수에 그칠 것이다. 그를 가장 열렬히 흠모하는 이들조차 그가 세르반테스를 만족스럽게 대변하지 못한다는 점은 인정해야 한다. 그의 제1부 번역은 몹시 서둘러 이루어졌고 그 자신이 한 번도 손보지 않았다. 거기에는 서둘러 만들어진 작품의 신선함과 활력이 모두 깃들어 있으나, 동시에 그러한 작품의 결점 또한 차고 넘친다. 그것은 종종 매우 직역에 치우쳐 — 빈번히 야만스러우리만치 직역인 — 가 하면, 그에 못지않게 종종 매우 느슨하다. 그는 분명 스페인어 구어에는 능통했으나, 그 이상은 별로 알지 못했던 듯하다. 한 단어를 똑같이 번역한 것이 모든 경우에 들어맞지는 않으리라는 생각은 그에게 결코 떠오르지 않는 듯하다.\n\n우리에게는 만족할 만한 「돈키호테」 번역이 없다는 말을 흔히 듣는다. 원전에 익숙한 이들에게는 그런 말이 자명한 이치나 진부한 말처럼 들린다. 사실 「돈키호테」를 영어로든 다른 어떤 언어로든 완벽하게 만족스럽게 옮기는 일은 있을 수 없기 때문이다. 그것은 스페인어 관용구가 도무지 다룰 수 없을 만큼 까다로워서도 아니고, 번역 불가능한 단어들이 — 분명 적지 않게 많기는 하지만 — 그토록 넘쳐 나서도 아니다. 오히려 이 책의 해학에 그 풍미를 입혀 주는 그 격언 같은 간결함이 스페인어 특유의 것이어서, 다른 어떤 언어로도 기껏해야 멀찍이서 흉내 낼 수 있을 뿐이기 때문이다.\n\n「돈키호테」의 영역사(英譯史)는 시사하는 바가 많다. 어떤 언어로든 최초의 번역이었던 셸턴의 것은 1608년경에 이루어진 듯하나 1612년에야 출간되었다. 물론 이것은 제1부에 한한 것이었다. 1620년에 나온 제2부는 셸턴의 작업이 아니라는 주장이 제기되어 왔으나, 그 주장을 뒷받침할 만한 근거라고는 제1부보다 기백이 덜하고, 우리가 흔히 \"활기\"라 이해하는 것이 적다는 사실뿐이다. 그런데 제1부가 거침없이 붓을 놀려 쓴 젊은이의 작업이고 제2부가 서적상을 위해 쓴 중년 남자의 작업이라면, 그것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이리라. 한편 제2부는 더 충실하고 더 직역에 가까우며 문체가 동일하고, 똑같은 번역, 혹은 똑같은 오역이 그 안에 나타난다. 게다가 새 번역자가 자기 이름을 감추면서까지 셸턴에게 공을 가로채도록 내버려 두었으리라고는 극히 보기 어렵다.\n\n1687년 밀턴의 조카인 존 필립스는 자기 말로 \"우리 현대 언어의 정취에 맞추어 영어로 옮긴\" 「돈키호테」를 내놓았다. 그의 「돈키호테」는 번역이라기보다 희화화에 가까우며, 그 조잡함과 저속함과 익살로 말하자면 그 시대의 문학에서조차 거의 유례를 찾기 어려운 희화화이다.\n\n네드 워드의 「돈키호테의 생애와 주목할 만한 모험들, 휴디브래스풍 운문으로 유쾌하게 옮김」(1700)은 번역이라 보기 어려우나, 당시 「돈키호테」가 어떤 시각으로 여겨졌는지를 보여 주는 데에는 도움이 된다.\n\n또 한 가지 예는 1712년 피터 모토가 펴낸 판본에서 찾을 수 있는데, 그는 당시 막 차(茶) 장사와 문필업을 겸하고 있었다. 이 판본은 \"여러 사람의 손으로 원전에서 번역됨\"이라 일컬어지나, 만일 그렇다면 그 여러 손의 손길을 거치는 동안 스페인적 풍미는 죄다 증발해 버린 셈이다. 반면 그것이 지닌 풍미는 분명 프랑스풍 런던내기의 것이다. 누구든 이것을 원전과 꼼꼼히 견주어 보면, 그것이 셸턴과 피요 드 생마르탱의 프랑스어 번역을 뒤섞은 혼합물이며, 거기에 필립스에게서 빌려 온 것들로 채워 넣고 그의 처리 방식을 따왔음을 거의 의심하지 않으리라. 분명 그것은 더 점잖고 더 품위 있기는 하나, 「돈키호테」를 아무리 우스꽝스럽게 만들어도 지나치지 않은 익살책처럼 다루고 있다.\n\n모토의 일꾼들이 한 것처럼 런던내기 특유의 경박함과 익살을 주입해서 「돈키호테」의 해학을 더 낫게 만들려는 시도는, 상등급 소고기 등심에 비계를 끼워 넣는 것 같은 한낱 주제넘은 짓일 뿐 아니라, 이 책의 정신을 완전히 왜곡하는 일이다. 그리고 대변하지 못한다는 점에서 무가치하고 잘못 대변한다는 점에서 무가치한 것보다 더 못한 이 번역이, 그래 온 만큼이나 호의를 받아 왔다는 사실은 「돈키호테」가 대체로 얼마나 무비판적으로 읽히는가를 보여 주는 증거이다.\n\n그러나 그것은 전혀 다른 정신으로 착수되고 수행된 번역, 즉 초상화가이자 포프, 스위프트, 아버스넛, 게이의 벗이었던 찰스 저바스의 번역을 낳는 효과를 가져왔다. 저바스는 자기 작업에 대해 거의 인정받지 못했고, 사실상 전혀 인정받지 못했다 해도 좋다. 세상에는 대체로 자비스의 번역으로 알려져 있기 때문이다. 그것은 그가 죽은 뒤에야 출간되었고, 인쇄업자들이 당시의 통상적인 발음에 따라 그 이름을 붙였던 것이다. 그것은 모든 번역 가운데 가장 거리낌 없이 이용되었고 또 가장 거리낌 없이 욕먹은 번역이었다. 다른 어떤 것보다 훨씬 많은 판을 거듭했고, 단연 가장 충실하다는 점은 누구나 인정하는 바이나, 그럼에도 그것이나 그 저자를 위해 좋은 말 한마디 해 주는 이는 아무도 없는 듯하다. 저바스는 분명 자신의 서문에서 독자들이 자신에게 편견을 품게 만들었다. 그는 그 서문에서 셸턴, 스티븐스, 모토에 관해 옳은 말을 많이 하는 가운데, 셸턴이 스페인어가 아니라 프란초시니의 이탈리아어 번역에서 옮겼다고 경솔하고도 부당하게 비난했던 것이다. 그런데 그 이탈리아어 번역은 셸턴의 첫 권이 나온 지 십 년이 지나서야 등장했다. 또한 그가 직업이 화가였고 그것도 평범한 화가였다는 이유로(비록 그는 우리에게 스위프트의 가장 훌륭한 초상화를 남겨 주었지만) 무능하다는 의심이 그에게 따라붙은 듯하며, 이는 그가 \"스페인어를 이해하지도 못하면서 「돈키호테」를 번역했다\"는 포프의 언급으로 더 굳어졌을 법하다. 그는 또한 자신이 깎아내렸던 셸턴에게서 빌려 왔다는 비난도 받았다. 그가 어렵거나 모호한 몇몇 구절에서 셸턴을 따랐다가 그와 함께 길을 잘못 든 것은 사실이다. 그러나 이런 경우가 하나라면, 그가 옳고 셸턴이 틀린 경우는 쉰 개나 된다. 포프의 단언으로 말하자면, 저바스의 번역을 원전과 나란히 놓고 꼼꼼히 살펴보는 사람이라면 누구든, 그가 — 어쩌면 순전한 스페인어 구어에서를 제외하고는 — 셸턴보다 비할 바 없이 더 나은, 견실한 스페인어 학자였음을 알게 될 것이다. 사실 그는 정직하고 충실하며 공을 들이는 번역자였고, 그 결점이 무엇이든 간에 오류와 오역에서 유난히 자유로운 번역을 남겼다.\n\n이 번역에 대한 비난은 그것이 뻣뻣하고 메마르다는 것 — 한마디로 \"딱딱하다\"는 것 — 인데, 거기에 근거가 있음을 부인할 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없다. 그러나 저바스를 위해 변론하자면, 이 경직됨의 상당 부분은 그가 선배들의 가볍고 경박하며 익살스러운 문체를 몹시 혐오한 데서 비롯된 것이다. 그는 돈키호테식 해학의 본질인 그 미소 없는 진중함을 조금이나마 헤아렸던 극소수의, 정말 극소수의 번역자 가운데 하나였다. 세르반테스를 자기가 한 멋진 말에 히죽거리며 이를 드러내고 웃는 모습으로 내세우는 것이 그에게는 죄처럼 여겨졌으니, 그의 번역의 특징인, 활기를 띤 모든 것을 금욕적으로 절제하는 태도는 상당 부분 이에서 비롯된 것이라 하겠다. 다만 유의할 점은, 대부분의 현대 판본에서 그의 문체가 매끄럽게 다듬어지고 멋스럽게 손질되었으나 원래의 스페인어와는 아무런 대조 없이 그리되었다는 사실이다. 그리하여 그는 더 듣기 좋게 읽히도록 만들어진 대신, 그의 으뜸가는 미덕인 충실성을 강탈당하고 말았다.\n\n1755년에 출간된 스몰릿의 번역도 이런 것들 가운데 하나로 거의 꼽을 만하다. 어쨌든 그것을 구성하는 데 저바스의 번역이 매우 거리낌 없이 가져다 쓰였고, 원래의 스페인어에는 거의, 혹은 아마도 전혀 주의를 기울이지 않았음은 분명하다.\n\n이후의 번역들은 몇 마디로 처리해도 좋다. 1769년에 \"번역자를 위해 인쇄됨\"이라 하여 나온 조지 켈리의 번역은 뻔뻔한 사기였으니, 모토의 번역에서 여기저기 몇몇 단어를 교묘히 자리바꿈한 것에 지나지 않았다. 찰스 윌멋의 번역(1774)은 플로리앙의 것처럼 축약본에 불과했으나 솜씨는 그만 못했다. 그리고 1818년에 스머크 양이 자기 오라비의 도판에 곁들이려고 펴낸 판본은 이전 번역들을 짜깁기해 만든 것에 지나지 않았다. 가장 최근의 것인 A. J. 더필드 씨의 번역에 대해서는, 여기서 내가 의견을 내놓는 것이 모든 의미에서 주제넘은 일이 되리라. 이 작업을 내게 제안했을 때 나는 그것을 본 적조차 없었고, 그 뒤로는 그저 vidi tantum, 곧 보기만 했다고 할 수 있겠으니, 더필드 씨의 명성과 보기 좋은 책들이 세르반테스를 사랑하는 모든 이에게 내미는 유혹을 뻔한 이유로 뿌리쳐 왔기 때문이다.\n\n* vidi tantum: 라틴어로 \"그저 보기만 했다\"는 뜻.\n「돈키호테」 번역의 앞선 역사를 보면, 사실과 사건과 모험을 온전히 갖춘 단순한 줄거리가 자기들을 즐겁게 해 주는 형식으로 차려져 나오기만 하면, 그 형식이 세르반테스가 본디 자신의 생각을 빚어낸 그 형식인지 아닌지에는 거의 신경 쓰지 않는 사람들이 꽤 많음을 알 수 있다. 한편으로는, 그가 들려주는 이야기뿐 아니라 그가 들려주는 그대로의 이야기를 — 적어도 관용구와 정황의 차이가 허락하는 한에서는 — 갖고자 하며, 다소 어설프게 해냈을지언정 양심적인 번역자를 더 선호할 사람들도 많음이 분명하다.\n\n그러나 결국 이 두 부류 사이에 진정한 대립은 없다. 한쪽을 기쁘게 하는 것이 다른 쪽을 기쁘게 하지 못할 까닭이 없고, 「돈키호테」를 위대한 고전에 합당한 존중으로 다루기를 목표로 삼는 번역자가 그것을 유명한 옛 우스갯거리 책으로 다루는 자만큼이나 부주의한 독자에게도 받아들여지지 못할 까닭도 없다. 그것은 대중에게 캐비아 같은 고급 취향의 문제가 아니며, 설령 그렇다 해도 그 잘못은 그렇게 만드는 자에게 있다. 세르반테스가 스페인 사람들의 귀를 사로잡은 방법은, 사정에 맞게 고친다면(mutatis mutandis), 영어권 독자 대다수에게도 똑같이 효력을 발휘할 것이다. 어쨌든 그것을 아무래도 상관없는 일로 여기는 독자가 있다 하더라도, 방법에 충실한 것은 내용에 충실한 것 못지않게 번역자의 의무의 일부이다. 모든 부류를 다 기쁘게 할 수 있다면 그보다 좋은 일은 없겠지만, 그의 첫 번째 의무는 자기 능력이 닿는 한 가장 충실하게 저자를 대변해 주기를 그에게 기대하는 사람들에 대한 것이다. 충실함이 실행 가능한 한에서는 자구에 충실하고, 그가 할 수 있는 한에서는 정신에 충실하게 말이다.\n\n* mutatis mutandis: 라틴어로 \"바꾸어야 할 것을 바꾸어\"라는 뜻.\n여기서 내 목적은 번역의 규칙을 두고 독단을 부리려는 것이 아니라, 이번 경우에 내가 따랐던, 적어도 내 능력껏 따르려 애썼던 규칙들을 일러두려는 것이다. 「돈키호테」를 번역할 때 아무리 엄격히 따라도 지나치지 않으리라 여겨지는 한 가지 규칙은, 가식의 기미가 있는 모든 것을 피하는 일이다. 사실 이 책 자체가 어떤 의미에서는 가식에 대한 항의이며, 세르반테스보다 그것을 더 혐오한 사람은 없었다. 그러므로 옛스럽거나 낡아 빠진 언어를 쓰고 싶은 유혹은 어떤 것이든 뿌리쳐야 한다고 나는 생각한다. 그것은 결국 하나의 가식이며, 그에 대해서는 아무런 정당성도 변명도 없다. 스페인어는 아마도 유럽의 어떤 언어보다도 17세기 이래 변화가 적었으며, 「돈키호테」의 더 큰 부분, 그리고 확실히 가장 좋은 부분은 오늘날의 스페인어 구어와 언어상 거의 차이가 없다. 이야기들과 돈키호테의 연설들을 제외하면, 가장 단순하고 평이한 일상의 언어를 쓰는 번역자가 거의 언제나 원전에 가장 가까이 다가가는 사람이 될 것이다.\n\n「돈키호테」의 이야기와 그 모든 인물과 사건이 이제 이백오십 년 넘게 영국인들의 입에 일상의 말처럼 친숙해져 왔음을 생각하면, 옛부터 친숙한 이름과 표현은 충분한 이유 없이 바꾸지 말아야 한다고 나는 생각한다. 물론 「돈키호테」가 위대한 고전에 합당한 대접을 받아야 한다고 여기는 번역자라면, 제9장에서 무어인에게 내려진, 어떤 것도 빼거나 더하지 말라는 그 명령에 자신이 매여 있음을 느끼리라.\nII: 세르반테스와 돈키호테에 관하여\n\n네 세대가 「돈키호테」를 두고 웃은 뒤에야 비로소 누군가가, 표지에 그 이름이 적힌 이 미겔 데 세르반테스 사아베드라가 대체 누구이며 어떤 사람이었던가를 물을 생각을 하게 되었다. 그리고 1738년 카터릿 경의 주선으로 런던에서 펴낸 판본에 저자의 생애를 덧붙이자는 제안이 나왔을 때는, 그 물음에 만족스러운 답을 내놓기에 이미 너무 늦은 뒤였다. 그때쯤에는 세르반테스의 인간됨에 관한 자취가 모조리 사라지고 없었다. 한때 존재했을지도 모를, 그를 알았던 사람들에게서 전해 내려온 떠도는 전승들은 이미 오래전에 스러졌고, 다른 기록이라고는 전혀 없었다. 16세기와 17세기는 \"당대의 사람들\"에 대해 무관심했으니, 19세기는 비록 셰익스피어나 세르반테스를 낳지는 못했어도 적어도 그러한 비난에서만큼은 스스로를 지켜 냈다. 이 일을 맡은 마얀스 이 시스카르나, 그의 뒤를 이은 리오스, 펠리세르, 나바레테 가운데 누구도 할 수 있었던 일이라고는, 세르반테스가 여러 서문에서 자신에 관해 언급한 몇 안 되는 대목을, 그들이 찾아낼 수 있었던 그의 생애에 관한 약간의 문헌적 증거들로 메워 보는 것뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 마지막에 언급한 그 전기 작가는 이 일을 매우 훌륭히 해내어 앞선 모든 이를 넘어섰다. 철저함이야말로 나바레테 작업의 으뜸가는 특징이다. 그는 이전에 밝혀진 것들을 보기 드문 인내와 판단력으로 가려내고 검증하고 체계화했을 뿐 아니라, 흔히들 말하듯, 자기 주제를 밝혀 줄 만한 것이 행여 숨어 있을 법한 돌이라면 하나도 뒤집어 보지 않은 것이 없었다. 나바레테는 근면과 통찰이 할 수 있는 모든 일을 해냈으니, 우리가 원하는 것을 그가 주지 못했다면 그것은 그의 잘못이 아니다. 핼럼이 셰익스피어에 관해 한 말은 그와 거의 평행을 이루는 세르반테스의 경우에도 적용될 수 있다. \"우리가 찾는 것은 그의 세례 기록이나 유언장 초안이나 그 이름의 철자법이 아니다. 그가 쓴 편지 한 통도, 그의 대화에 관한 기록도, 동시대인이 그려 낸 그의 성품에 관한 글도 …… 나온 적이 없다.\"\n\n그러니 짚 없이 벽돌을 만들어야 하는 처지에 몰린 세르반테스의 전기 작가들이 추측에 크게 기대게 되고, 그 추측이 어떤 경우에는 차츰차츰 확정된 사실의 자리를 차지하게 된 것도 지극히 당연한 일이다. 여기서 내가 하려는 일이라고는 사실에 속하는 것을 추측에 속하는 것에서 갈라내고, 그 자료들이 추론을 정당화하는지 아닌지를 가리는 일은 독자의 판단에 맡기는 것뿐이다.\n\n스페인 문학의 첫 줄에 선다고 모두가 입을 모으는 이들, 곧 세르반테스, 로페 데 베가, 케베도, 칼데론, 가르실라소 데 라 베가, 멘도사 가문 사람들, 공고라는 모두 유서 깊은 가문 출신이었으며, 묘하게도 마지막 한 사람을 제외하면 모두가 스페인 북부의 같은 산악 지방에 그 기원을 둔 가문 출신이었다. 세르반테스 가문은 흔히 갈리시아 태생이라고들 하며, 의심할 바 없이 그 가문은 아주 이른 시기부터 갈리시아에 토지를 소유하고 있었다. 그러나 내가 보기에 증거의 무게는, 그 가문의 본디 터전인 \"솔라르\"가 옛 카스티야의 북서쪽 모퉁이, 카스티야와 레온과 아스투리아스가 만나는 지점 가까이의 세르바토스에 있었음을 보여 주는 쪽으로 기운다. 마침 세르반테스 가문의 완전한 역사가 10세기부터 17세기까지 전해 내려오는데, 「톨레도의 알카이데였던 저 유명한 누뇨 알폰소의 명문 가계와 영광스러운 행적과 고귀한 후손」이라는 제목으로, 부지런한 계보학자 로드리고 멘데스 실바가 1648년에 쓴 것이다. 그는 후안 2세의 계관시인이자 사관(史官)이었던 후안 데 메나의 필사본 계보를 이용했다.\n\n세르반테스라는 이름의 유래는 흥미롭다. 누뇨 알폰소는 알폰소 7세 치세에 무어인과의 투쟁에서, 반세기 전 알폰소 6세 치세에 엘 시드가 그러했던 것에 거의 못지않게 두드러진 인물이었고, 그 공으로 톨레도 인근의 여러 토지를 하사받았다. 그는 도시에서 두 레구아쯤 떨어진 자기 영지 한 곳에 성을 쌓고 그것을 세르바토스라 불렀는데, 그가 \"몬타냐의 세르바토스 솔라르의 영주\"였기 때문이다. 바스크 지방에서 레온까지 뻗은 그 산악 지방은 늘 몬타냐라 불렸다. 1143년 그가 전투에서 죽자 그 성은 유언에 따라 아들 알폰소 무니오에게 넘어갔는데, 그는 당시 단순한 부칭(父稱) 대신 영지나 지역에서 따온 성(姓)이 유행하기 시작하던 터라 세르바토스라는 이름을 덧붙여 가졌다. 그의 맏아들 페드로가 성의 소유를 물려받고 그 이름을 따른 아비의 본을 좇았는데, 둘째 아들 곤살로는 이러한 차지에 못마땅해했던 듯하다.\n\n톨레도를 잠깐이라도 들른 적이 있는 사람이라면 누구나, 알칸타라 다리가 타호강의 협곡 위로 걸쳐진 곳, 그 위 언덕을 이고 선 폐허가 된 성을 기억할 것이다. 그 성은 부서진 윤곽과 허물어져 가는 벽으로, 맞은편 도시 지붕들 위로 우뚝 솟은 네모지고 단단한 알카사르와 더없이 훌륭한 한 쌍을 이룬다. 그것은 알폰소 6세가 1085년 톨레도를 점령한 직후에 지었다고도 하고 더러는 복원했다고도 하는데, 그는 그것을 스페인의 한 순교자를 기려 산 세르반도라 불렀다. 이 이름은 나중에 산 세르반(「엘 시드의 노래」에 이 형태로 나온다), 산 세르반테스, 산 세르반테스로 바뀌었다. 그 마지막 이름에 관하여 「스페인 안내서」는 그것이 「돈키호테」의 저자와 무슨 관련이 있으리라는 생각을 경계하라고 독자들에게 일러둔다. 스페인의 길에서 그를 길동무이자 조언자로 삼아 본 이라면 누구나 알듯, 포드는 문학이나 역사 문제에서 좀처럼 틀리는 법이 없다. 그러나 이 경우에는 그가 틀렸다. 그것은 「돈키호테」의 저자와 모든 관련이 있으니, 사실 오늘날 스페인이 가장 자랑스러워하는 그 이름을 스페인에 준 것이 바로 이 옛 성벽이기 때문이다. 앞서 말한 곤살로가, 명목상으로는 성에서 따온 것이라 해도 실은 그 가문의 오래된 영지에서 비롯된, 자신에게도 똑같은 권리가 있는 이름을 형이 차지한 것을 달가워하지 않았으리라는 것은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 그래서 이를 상쇄하고 형과 자신을 구별하기(diferenciarse) 위해, 그는 타호강 강변에 선 그 성의 이름을 성(姓)으로 삼았으니, 한 가문의 전승에 따르면 그 성을 짓는 데 그의 증조부가 한몫했다고 한다.\n\n두 형제 모두 가문을 일으켰다. 세르반테스 쪽 가지가 더 끈질겨서, 안달루시아, 에스트레마두라, 갈리시아, 포르투갈 등 여러 방향으로 곁가지를 뻗었고, 교회와 나라를 섬기는 데 두드러진 인물들을 줄줄이 배출했다. 곤살로 자신과, 또 분명 그의 한 아들은 1236년부터 1248년까지 이어진, 코르도바와 세비야를 기독교 스페인에 안기고 무어인을 그라나다 왕국에 몰아넣은 저 대원정에서 페르난도 3세를 따랐으며, 그의 후손들은 이베리아반도의 가장 고귀한 몇몇 가문과 혼인을 맺어 그 가운데 군인과 행정관과 교회의 고위 성직자를 배출했으니, 적어도 두 명의 추기경 대주교가 그 안에 들어 있었다.\n\n안달루시아에 정착한 가계 가운데, 산티아고 기사단의 기사단장이었던 디에고 데 세르반테스는 후안 아리아스 데 사아베드라의 딸 후아나 아베야네다와 혼인하여 여러 아들을 두었다. 그 가운데 하나가 헤레스의 행정관이자 이 가문의 멕시코 분가와 콜롬비아 분가의 조상이 된 곤살로 고메스였고, 또 하나가 후안이었는데, 그의 아들 로드리고가 도냐 레오노르 데 코르티나스와 혼인하여 그녀에게서 로드리고, 안드레아, 루이사, 그리고 우리의 저자 미겔이라는 네 자녀를 두었다.\n\n세르반테스의 가계는 「돈키호테」와 무관하지 않다. 거의 펠라요의 시대부터 그라나다 공방전에 이르기까지 뻗어 있는, 진정한 편력 기사들의 혈통을 돌아볼 수 있는 사람이라면 기사도 소설의 가짜 기사도라는 주제에 대해 강한 감정을 품었을 법하다. 그것은 또한 한때 위대했다가 점점 가늘어져 마침내 피라미드처럼 끄트머리에 이르러서는 아무것도 아닌 것이 되어 버린 가문들에 관해 그가 여러 곳에서 한 말에 무게를 더해 준다. 그것이 바로 그 자신의 가문이 처한 형편이었던 것이다.\n\n그는 알칼라 데 에나레스에서 태어나 1547년 10월 9일 산타 마리아 마요르 성당에서 세례를 받았다. 그의 소년 시절과 청년 시절에 대해서는, 「희극집」 서문에서 그가 보여 주는 한 장면 — 로페 데 루에다와 그 일행이 광장에 투박한 널빤지 무대를 세우고 시골 소극을 공연하는 것을, 소년인 그가 즐거워하며 구경하던 장면, 훗날 그 자신이 자기 막간극의 본보기로 삼은 그 소극을 — 을 제외하면 우리는 아무것도 알지 못한다. 그러나 이 첫 장면은 의미심장하다. 그의 생애에 그토록 큰 영향을 끼친, 그리고 그가 나이 들수록 더 강해진 듯한 그 연극에 대한 사랑이 일찍부터 자라났음을 보여 주기 때문이다. 그가 죽기 불과 몇 달 전에 쓴 바로 이 서문이 그 사랑의 두드러진 증거이다. 그는 또한 젊은 시절 자신이 대단한 독서가였음을 우리에게 일러 주는데, 이에 대해서는 따로 확인할 필요도 없다. 「돈키호테」 제1부 하나만으로도 방대한 양의 잡다한 독서 — 기사도 소설, 발라드, 통속시, 연대기 — 가 입증되는데, 이는 그가 생애의 첫 스무 해를 빼고는 그럴 시간도 기회도 없었던 것들이다. 그리고 세부적인 문제에서 그가 저지른 잘못된 인용과 착오는, 눈여겨보건대, 언제나 소년 시절의 독서를 되새기는 사람의 그것이다.\n\n세르반테스가 소년이었을 무렵에는 연극 말고도 다른 여러 가지가 갓 태동하던 참이었다. 그의 소년기는 모든 면에서 스페인에게 과도기였다. 옛 기사도의 스페인은 스러져 갔다. 새로운 스페인은 로마 제국 이래 세상이 본 가장 막강한 세력이었으나, 아직 그 위대함의 대가를 치르라는 요구를 받지는 않았다. 페르난도와 히메네스의 정책에 따라 군주는 절대 권력을 쥐게 되었고, 교회와 종교 재판소는 군주를 그렇게 유지하도록 교묘히 맞추어졌다. 무어인과 싸운 만큼이나 늘 끈질기게 절대주의에 맞서 온 귀족들은 모든 정치적 권력을 빼앗겼고, 도시들도 같은 운명을 맞았으며, 카스티야와 아라곤의 자유로운 헌정은 휩쓸려 사라졌고, 코르테스에 남은 유일한 기능이라고는 왕이 명하는 대로 돈을 내주는 것뿐이었다.\n\n그 과도기는 문학에까지 미쳤다. 가르실라소 데 라 베가와 디에고 우르타도 데 멘도사처럼 이탈리아 전쟁에 종군했던 이들은 이탈리아에서 르네상스 이후 문학의 산물을 들여왔고, 그것이 뿌리내려 번성하더니 급기야 토착의 산물을 질식시킬 지경에 이르렀다. 다몬과 티르시스, 필리스와 클로에가 스페인에 그럭저럭 토착화되었으며, 절망에 빠진 목동과 냉담한 목녀라는 발상에 새로움의 기색을 입히려는 목가시의 온갖 장치들도 함께 들어왔다. 이에 대한 상쇄로, 옛 역사 발라드와 전승 발라드, 그리고 진정한 목가, 곧 농민의 삶을 노래한 노래와 발라드가 부지런히 수집되어, 점점 더 빠르게 잇따라 나온 가요집(cancionero)에 인쇄되었다. 그러나 인쇄술 보급의 가장 주목할 만한 결과는 아마도, 가르시 오르도녜스 데 몬탈보가 세기 초에 「아마디스 데 가울라」를 되살려 낸 이래 인쇄기에서 줄곧 쏟아져 나온 기사도 소설의 홍수였을 것이다.\n\n진지한 것이든 가벼운 것이든 독서를 좋아하는 젊은이에게, 16세기 중엽의 알칼라 데 에나레스보다 스페인에서 더 나은 곳은 없었을 것이다. 그곳은 당시 분주하고 인구 많은 대학 도시였으며, 살라망카의 진취적인 경쟁자 그 이상이었고, 오늘날 마드리드에서 사라고사로 가는 여행자가 보게 되는 우울하고 적막하고 버려진 알칼라와는 전혀 다른 곳이었다. 대학의 강점은 신학과 의학이었을지 모르나, 도시 자체는 오히려 인문학과 가벼운 문학 쪽으로 기울었던 듯하며, 책의 산지로서 알칼라는 이미 톨레도, 부르고스, 살라망카, 세비야의 더 오래된 인쇄소들과 겨루기 시작하고 있었다. 세르반테스가 우리에게 들려준, 그가 처음 연극 구경을 가던 그림에 한 쌍이 될 만한 장면이, 분명 그 무렵 알칼라의 거리에서 자주 보였을 법하다. 영민하고 열성적이며 황갈색 머리칼을 한 소년이, 대중을 꾈 양으로 최신 책들이 펼쳐져 놓인 서점 안을 들여다보면서, 어쩌면 눈먼 거지와 그 소년의 목판화가 실리고 \"라사리요 데 토르메스의 생애, 재판(再版)\"이라 제목 붙은 저 작은 책이 대체 무슨 내용일까 궁금해하거나, 아니면 기사도 소설 출판업자들이 자기네 2절판 표지를 꾸미기 좋아하던, 터무니없는 갑주와 깃털 장식을 두른 편력 기사의 저 우스꽝스러운 초상화 가운데 하나를 즐거움에 넘쳐흐르는 눈으로 바라보던 장면 말이다. 소년이 어른의 아버지라면, 쉰 살에 강했던 부조화에 대한 감각이 열 살 때 이미 생생했을 것이며, 이와 같은 어떤 상념들이야말로 「돈키호테」의 진정한 발단이었을지도 모른다.\n\n좀 더 본격적인 교육을 위해 그는 살라망카로 갔다고 전한다. 그러나 매우 가난했던 로드리고 데 세르반테스가 제집 문 앞에 대학이 있는데도 어째서 아들을 백오십 마일이나 떨어진 대학으로 보냈을까 하는 것은, 그가 정말 그렇게 했다고 여길 만한 무슨 근거라도 있다면 하나의 수수께끼가 될 것이다. 유일한 증거라고는, 토마스 곤살레스 교수가 미겔 데 세르반테스라는 사람의 입학 등록 옛 기록을 한 번 본 적이 있다는 막연한 진술뿐이다. 이것은 그 뒤로 다시 본 사람이 없는 듯하다. 그러나 설령 다시 보였다 하더라도, 또 그 날짜가 들어맞았다 하더라도, 그것은 아무것도 입증하지 못할 것이니, 그 세기 중엽 무렵에 태어난 미겔이 적어도 둘은 더 있었기 때문이다. 게다가 그 가운데 하나는 세르반테스 사아베드라로, 분명 사촌이었을 그 사람은 전기 작가들에게 큰 골칫거리의 원천이었다.\n\n그가 살라망카에서도 알칼라에서도 학생이 아니었다는 사실은 그 자신의 작품들이 가장 잘 입증한다. 어떤 사람도 그만큼 경험에 크게 기댄 이가 없건만, 그는 학생 시절의 추억이라고는 어디에도 단 하나 남기지 않았으니 — 「티아 핑히다」가 그의 작품이라 하더라도 그것은 추억이 아니다 — 대부분의 사람이 가장 잘 기억하는 그 시절을 그가 기억했음을 보여 줄 만한 것은, \"대학 시절의 농담\" 하나조차 없다. 그의 교육에 관해 우리가 확실히 아는 전부는, 어느 정도 명망 있는 인문학과 순문학 교수였던 후안 로페스 데 오요스가 그를 자신의 \"사랑하고 아끼는 제자\"라 부른다는 것이다. 이는 펠리페 2세의 둘째 왕비 이사벨 데 발로이스의 죽음에 부쳐 여러 사람의 손으로 지어진 시를 모은 작은 시집에 나오는 말로, 그 교수가 1569년에 펴낸 것인데, 세르반테스는 거기에 비가(悲歌) 한 편과 소네트 형식의 묘비명을 포함하여 네 편을 기고했다. 「리시다스」 같은 작품이 이런 종류의 시집에 끼어드는 일은 드문 우연이며, 세르반테스는 밀턴이 아니었다. 그의 시는 보통 이런 것들이 그러한 정도보다 더 나쁘지는 않다. 적어도 그 정도는 말해 줄 수 있겠다.\n\n이 책이 나올 무렵 그는 스페인을 떠나 있었고, 운명이 그렇게 정한 대로 십이 년 동안, 그의 생애에서 가장 파란만장한 세월을 떠나 있게 되었다. 훗날 추기경이 된 줄리오 아콰비바는 1568년 말에 교황의 명을 받아 펠리페 2세에게 일부는 조의를, 일부는 정치적 임무를 띠고 파견되었는데, 왕의 다소 무뚝뚝한 재촉으로 서둘러 로마로 돌아갈 때 세르반테스를 자신의 카마레로(시종)로 데려갔으니, 그것은 그 자신이 교황의 궁정에서 맡고 있던 직책이었다. 만일 세르반테스가 그 자리를 지켰더라면 교황청에서 분명 출세로 이어졌겠지만, 1570년 여름 그는 그 자리를 내놓고 디에고 우르비나 대위의 중대에 일개 병사로 입대했다. 그 중대는 돈 미겔 데 몬카다의 연대 소속이었으나 당시에는 마르크안토니오 콜론나의 지휘 아래 있었다. 무엇이 그를 이런 일로 내몰았는지는 알 수 없으니, 그것이 앞에 놓인 출셋길에 대한 싫증이었는지, 아니면 순전한 군인다운 열정이었는지 우리는 모른다. 어쩌면 후자였을 법도 하니, 가슴 설레는 시절이었기 때문이다. 그러나 스페인과 베네치아와 교황이 공동의 적 오스만 제국에 맞서 동맹을 맺고 레판토에서 연합 함대가 승리를 거두기에 이른 그 사건들은 세르반테스의 생애보다는 차라리 유럽의 역사에 속한다. 그는 1571년 9월 돈 후안 데 아우스트리아의 지휘 아래 메시나에서 출항한 이들 가운데 하나였다. 그러나 10월 7일 아침 튀르크 함대가 시야에 들어왔을 때, 그는 갑판 아래에서 열병으로 앓아누워 있었다. 적이 보인다는 소식에 그는 일어났고, 동료들과 상관들의 만류에도 자기 자리를 지키겠다고 고집하며, 건강보다 하느님과 왕을 섬기다 죽는 편을 택하겠노라 말했다. 그가 탄 갤리선 마르케사호는 전투의 한복판에 있었고, 전투가 끝나기 전에 그는 총상을 셋 입었으니, 둘은 가슴에, 하나는 왼손인지 왼팔인지에 맞았다. 나바레테에 따르면, 전투 다음 날 아침 그는 부상자들을 몸소 살피던 총사령관 돈 후안과 만났는데, 그 만남의 결과 하나는 그의 봉급에 세 크라운이 더해진 것이었고, 또 하나는 보아하니 그 장군의 우정이었다.\n\n세르반테스가 얼마나 심하게 다쳤는지는, 젊음과 튼튼한 몸, 그리고 일찍이 어떤 환자도 지녀 본 적 없을 만큼 쾌활하고 활기찬 기질을 지녔으면서도, 퇴원하기까지 메시나의 병원에서 일곱 달을 보냈다는 사실에서 미루어 알 수 있다. 그는 왼손을 영영 못 쓰게 된 채로 나왔다. 「파르나소 여행기」에서 메르쿠리우스가 그에게 일러 주었듯, 그는 오른손의 더 큰 영광을 위해 왼손의 쓰임을 잃은 것이었다. 그러나 이것이 그를 복무에서 완전히 부적격으로 만들지는 않아, 1572년 4월 그는 로페 데 피게로아 연대의 마누엘 폰세 데 레온 중대에 합류했는데, 그의 형 로드리고도 거기서 복무하고 있었을 공산이 크며, 골레타와 튀니스 점령을 포함하여 이후 삼 년간의 작전에 참여했다. 튀르크인들이 이곳들을 다시 빼앗은 뒤 찾아온 소강상태를 틈타, 그는 스페인으로 돌아갈 허가를 얻어, 1575년 9월 형 로드리고, 골레타의 전 총독 페드로 카리요 데 케사다, 그리고 몇몇 사람과 함께 솔호라는 갤리선을 타고 나폴리에서 출항했다. 그는 자신의 공로를 인정받아 한 중대의 지휘권을 맡도록 왕에게 자신을 천거하는, 돈 후안 데 아우스트리아와 시칠리아 부왕 세사 공작의 추천서를 지니고 있었으니, 그것은 사건들이 입증했듯 불행한 선물(dono infelice)이었다. 26일에 그들은 알제리 갤리선 한 무리와 마주쳤고, 굳센 저항 끝에 제압되어 알제로 끌려갔다.\n\n몸값을 치르고 풀려난 한 동료 포로를 통해 형제는 가까스로 가족에게 자기들의 처지를 알렸고, 알칼라의 그 가엾은 사람들은 곧장 몸값을 마련하려 애써, 아버지는 가진 것을 모조리 처분하고 두 누이는 자기들의 결혼 지참금을 내놓았다. 그러나 달리 마미는 세르반테스에게서 돈 후안과 세사 공작이 왕에게 보내는 편지를 발견하고는, 자기 포로가 대단히 중요한 인물임이 틀림없다고 결론지어, 돈이 왔을 때 그것을 턱없이 모자란다며 멸시하듯 거절했다. 그러나 로드리고의 주인은 더 쉽게 만족하여, 그의 경우에는 몸값이 받아들여졌다. 형제 사이에는, 로드리고가 스페인으로 돌아가 배를 한 척 구해서 그 배로 알제에 돌아와 미겔과, 가능한 한 많은 동료를 데리고 나오기로 합의가 이루어졌다. 이것이 세르반테스가 시도한 첫 번째 탈출은 아니었다. 포로 생활이 시작된 직후 그는 동료 몇몇을 꾀어, 당시 스페인의 전초지였던 오란까지 걸어서 가 보려 했다. 그러나 첫날 여정이 끝나자 길잡이 노릇을 하기로 했던 무어인이 그들을 버리고 달아나, 그들은 돌아오는 수밖에 없었다. 두 번째 시도는 더 참담했다. 그는 해안가 도시 밖 한 정원에, 스페인 사람인 정원사의 도움을 받아 은신처를 만들고, 거기로 동료 포로 열넷을 하나씩 데려와 여러 달 동안 비밀리에 머물게 하면서, \"엘 도라도르\", 곧 \"도금장이\"라 알려진 한 배교자를 통해 그들에게 음식을 대 주었다. 자신도 포로인 그가 어떻게 이 모든 일을 해냈는지는 이 이야기의 수수께끼 가운데 하나이다. 그 계획이 아무리 무모해 보일지라도, 그것은 거의 성공할 뻔했다. 로드리고가 구한 배가 해안 앞바다에 나타나 밤을 틈타 도망자들을 태우려 다가오던 차에, 선원들이 지나가던 고깃배에 놀라 황급히 물러났다. 얼마 뒤 다시 시도했을 때, 그들은, 적어도 그들 가운데 일부는 포로로 붙잡혔고, 정원에 있던 그 가엾은 이들이 이제 잠시만 지나면 자유가 손안에 들어오리라는 생각에 들떠 있던 바로 그때, 자기들이 튀르크 군대에, 기병과 보병에 에워싸여 있음을 알게 되었다. 도라도르가 그 계획 전부를 데이 하산에게 밀고했던 것이다.\n\n세르반테스는 자기들에게 닥친 일을 보자, 동료들에게 모든 죄를 자기에게 뒤집어씌우라고 당부했고, 그들이 결박당하는 동안 음모 전부가 자기가 꾸민 것이며 다른 누구도 거기에 가담하지 않았노라고 큰 소리로 선언했다. 데이 앞에 끌려가서도 그는 똑같이 말했다. 그는 꼬챙이에 꿰이는 형과 고문으로 위협받았다. 귀와 코를 베는 것이 알제리 사람들에게는 장난스러운 짓거리였으니, 그들의 고문이 어떠했을지는 가히 짐작할 만하다. 그러나 어떤 것으로도 그를, 자기, 오직 자기 혼자만이 책임이 있다는 처음의 진술에서 벗어나게 할 수 없었다. 그 결말로, 가엾은 정원사는 그 주인에게 교수형을 당했고, 포로들은 데이가 차지했는데, 데이는 그러나 나중에 그들 대부분을 주인들에게 돌려주되 세르반테스만은 달리 마미에게 5백 크라운을 치르고 데리고 있었다. 그는 분명, 그토록 수완과 정력과 대담함을 갖춘 사람을 사사로운 손에 맡겨 두기에는 너무 위험한 재산이라고 여겼던 것이다. 그래서 그를 무거운 쇠고랑에 채워 자기 감옥에 가두었다. 만일 이런 수단으로 자기 포로의 기개를 꺾거나 결심을 흔들 수 있으리라 생각했다면, 그는 곧 깨우침을 얻었다. 세르반테스가 머지않아 오란의 총독에게 편지 한 통을 보내는 데 성공했기 때문이다. 그 편지에서 그는 믿을 만한 사람을 보내, 자신과 동료 포로인 다른 세 신사가 탈출할 수 있게 해 달라고 간청했으니, 분명 더 믿음직한 길잡이를 데리고 첫 번째 시도를 되풀이할 작정이었다. 불행히도 편지를 가져간 무어인이 오란 바로 밖에서 붙잡혔고, 편지가 그에게서 발견되자 그는 알제로 돌려보내져 데이의 명령에 따라 다른 이들에게 경고로 삼고자 즉시 꼬챙이에 꿰였으며, 세르반테스는 곤장 이천 대를 맞으라는 선고를 받았다. 그 정도면 십중팔구 세상에서 「돈키호테」를 앗아 갔을 숫자였으나, 누구인지는 모르나 몇 사람이 그를 위해 나서서 간청해 준 덕에 그리되지 않았다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤 그는 전보다 더 엄중히 갇혀 지낸 듯하니, 그가 또 한 번 시도하기까지 거의 두 해가 지났기 때문이다. 이번에 그의 계획은, 한 스페인 배교자와 알제에 거주하는 두 발렌시아 상인의 도움을 받아 무장한 배 한 척을 사서, 그 배로 자신과 주요 포로 예순 명가량이 함께 탈출하려는 것이었다. 그러나 그것을 막 실행에 옮기려던 차에, 후안 블랑코 데 파스 박사라는, 성직자이자 동포인 자가 데이에게 그 음모를 밀고했다. 세르반테스는 굳센 성품과 헌신, 그리고 비참한 처지에 놓인 동료들의 운명을 가볍게 해 주려는 지칠 줄 모르는 정력과 노력으로 모두에게 사랑받게 되었고 포로 사회의 중심인물이 되었는데, 믿기 어려울지 모르나, 그의 영향력과 그가 받는 존경에 대한 시기심이 이 사람을 움직여 잔혹한 죽음으로 그를 파멸시키려 들게 했던 것이다. 상인들은 데이가 모든 것을 알고 있음을 알아채고, 세르반테스가 고문 아래 자기들의 목숨을 위태롭게 할 무언가를 발설할까 두려워, 막 스페인으로 떠나려던 배에 올라 슬며시 빠져나가라고 그를 설득하려 했다. 그러나 그는 그들에게 두려워할 것 없으니 어떤 고문으로도 자신은 누구도 연루시키지 않으리라 말하고는, 곧장 가서 데이에게 자수했다.\n\n전과 마찬가지로 데이는 그에게 공범들의 이름을 대라고 강요하려 했다. 그를 즉시 처형할 채비가 모두 갖추어져, 올가미가 그의 목에 둘러지고 손이 등 뒤로 묶였으나, 그에게서 얻어 낼 수 있었던 것이라고는, 그 뒤 알제를 떠난 네 신사의 도움을 받아 자기 혼자서 모든 일을 꾸몄으며, 자기와 동행할 예순 명은 마지막 순간까지 그에 관해 아무것도 모르게 되어 있었다는 말뿐이었다. 그에게서 아무것도 알아낼 수 없음을 안 데이는, 그를 전보다 더 무거운 쇠고랑에 채워 감옥으로 돌려보냈다.\n\n가난에 찌든 세르반테스 가족은 이 모든 시간 동안 또 한 번 몸값을 마련하려 애썼고, 마침내 삼백 두카트의 돈이 모아져, 막 알제로 떠나려던 속량 수도회의 후안 힐 신부에게 맡겨졌다. 그러나 데이는 제시된 액수의 두 배가 넘게 요구했고, 그의 임기가 끝나 그가 모든 노예를 데리고 콘스탄티노플로 막 떠나려던 참이었기에, 세르반테스의 처지는 위태로웠다. 무거운 쇠고랑을 찬 채 그가 이미 배에 올라 있을 때, 데이가 마침내 요구를 절반으로 줄이는 데 동의했고, 힐 신부가 빌려서 그 액수를 채울 수 있었던 덕에, 일주일을 뺀 오 년의 포로 생활 끝에 1580년 9월 19일 세르반테스는 마침내 자유의 몸이 되었다. 머지않아 그는, 종교 재판소의 관리라 자처하던 블랑코 데 파스가, 그가 스페인으로 돌아갔을 때 그에게 씌울 비행(非行) 혐의를 거짓 증거로 꾸며 내고 있음을 알아챘다. 그를 꼼짝 못하게 만들고자 세르반테스는 포로 생활 전 기간을 아우르는 스물다섯 가지 질문을 작성하고, 그것에 관해 공증인 앞에서 믿을 만한 증인들의 진술을 받아 달라고 힐 신부에게 청했다. 알제의 주요 포로들 가운데서 뽑힌 열한 명의 증인이 앞서 진술한 모든 사실과, 그 밖에도 훨씬 많은 것을 증언했다. 그들이 하나씩 차례로 세르반테스의 선행을 증언하면서 — 그가 마음 약한 이들을 어떻게 위로하고 도왔는지, 꺾여 가는 그들의 용기를 어떻게 북돋웠는지, 자신의 가난한 지갑을 이 증언자와 어떻게 나누었는지, 그리고 \"이 증언자가 그에게서 아버지와 어머니를 발견했다\"는 것을 — 공증인의 격식 차린 언어 속에서 표현될 길을 찾으려 몸부림치는 그 흠모와 사랑과 감사에는 어딘가 가슴을 울리는 데가 있다.\n\n스페인으로 돌아온 그는, 자신의 옛 연대가 펠리페의 왕위 요구를 지지하고자 포르투갈로 진군하려는 참임을 알았고, 이제 완전히 빈털터리였던 터라 거기에 다시 합류하는 수밖에 없었다. 그는 1582년과 그 이듬해 아소레스 원정에 참가했고, 전쟁이 끝나자 1583년 가을 스페인으로 돌아오면서 자신의 목가 소설 「갈라테아」의 원고를, 그리고 내적 증거로 미루어 보건대 아마도 「페르실레스와 시히스문다」 첫 부분의 원고도 함께 가져왔다. 그의 전기 작가들이 주장하는 바로는, 그는 또한 어떤 정사(情事)의 소생인 어린 딸을 데리고 돌아왔는데, 그들 가운데 일부가 매우 자세하게 일러 주는 바로는 그 상대가 리스본의 고귀한 가문 출신 여인이었으나, 그 여인의 이름은 물론 그녀가 살던 거리의 이름조차 그들은 밝히지 않는다. 이 모든 이야기의 유일한 근거는, 1605년에 세르반테스 가족 가운데 도냐 이사벨 데 사아베드라라는 여인이 분명 살고 있었으며, 그녀가 한 공식 문서에 그의 사생아이자 당시 스무 살이라 기록되어 있다는 사실뿐이다.\n\n불구가 된 왼손으로는 군에서 진급할 가망이 없었으니, 이제 돈 후안은 죽고 그의 청원과 공로를 밀어줄 사람이 아무도 없는 데다, 마흔을 바라보는 사람에게 졸병 신세는 음울한 앞날이었다. 그는 이미 시인으로서 어느 정도 명성을 얻고 있었으므로, 문학에 운명을 걸기로 마음먹고, 그 첫 시도로 「갈라테아」를 인쇄에 부쳤다. 살바 이 마옌이 결정적으로 밝혀 보였듯, 그것은 1585년 그 자신의 출생지인 알칼라에서 출간되었고, 분명 그의 이름을 더 널리 알리는 데 도움이 되었으나, 다른 어떤 면에서도 그에게 별 이득을 주지는 못한 것이 확실하다.\n\n그 책이 인쇄에 부쳐지는 동안 그는 마드리드 인근 에스키비아스의 여인이자 보아하니 그 가족의 벗이었던 도냐 카탈리나 데 팔라시오스 살라사르 이 보스메디아노와 혼인했는데, 그녀는 그에게 어쩌면 끼니 걱정을 면하게 해 주었을 법한 재산을 가져왔으나, 설령 그렇다 해도 그게 다였다. 이 무렵 연극은 장터의 무대와 떠돌이 극단을 넘어설 만큼 자라나 있었고, 그것에 대한 오랜 사랑으로 그는 자연히 마음 맞는 일거리를 찾아 연극으로 눈을 돌렸다. 약 삼 년 동안 그는 스무 편인지 서른 편인지의 희곡을 썼는데, 그의 말로는 오이나 다른 물건이 날아드는 일 없이 공연되었고, 야유나 고함이나 소동 없이 무사히 막을 내렸다고 한다. 다시 말해, 그의 희곡들은 무대에서 야유받아 쫓겨날 만큼 나쁘지는 않았으나, 무대에서 제힘으로 버틸 만큼 좋지도 않았던 것이다. 그 가운데 단 두 편만이 전해지는데, 마침 그것들이 그가 흐뭇하게 언급한 일고여덟 편 가운데 둘이므로, 우리는 그것들이 좋은 본보기라고 추정할 수 있겠으나, 「누만시아」와 「알제 거래기」를 읽는 사람이라면 누구도 그것들이 상연용 극으로서 실패한 것에 조금도 놀라지 않을 것이다. 그것들이 어떤 장점을 지녔든, 이따금 어떤 번득임을 보여 주든, 구성에 관한 한 그것들은 구제 불능으로 서투르다. 그것들이 얼마나 완벽하게 실패했는가는, 그토록 낙천적인 기질과 불굴의 끈기를 지닌 그가 극작가로서 생계를 꾸리려는 그 싸움을 삼 년 넘게 버텨 내지 못했다는 사실에서 분명히 드러난다. 또한 흔히들 말하듯 떠오르던 로페의 인기가 그 원인은 아니었으니, 그 자신은 그와 반대되는 말을 했어도 그렇다. 로페가 언제 무대를 위해 글을 쓰기 시작했는지는 분명치 않으나, 그것이 세르반테스가 세비야로 간 뒤였음은 확실하다.\n\n아센시오 이 톨레도 씨가 인쇄한 「새 문서들」 가운데 1592년 자(字)의 것이 하나 있는데, 세르반테스의 특징을 묘하게 잘 보여 준다. 그것은 흥행주인 로드리고 오소리오라는 자와 맺은 계약으로, 그는 희극 여섯 편을 각각 오십 두카트(약 6파운드)에 받기로 했으되, 상연되었을 때 그 희극이 일찍이 스페인에서 상연된 가장 훌륭한 작품 가운데 하나임이 드러나지 않는 한 어떤 경우에도 값을 치르지 않기로 한 것이었다. 그 시험은 끝내 한 번도 적용되지 않은 듯하다. 어쩌면 그 희극들이 일찍이 상연된 가장 훌륭한 작품 축에 들지 못한다는 것이 로드리고 오소리오에게 충분히 빤히 드러났는지도 모른다. 세르반테스의 서신 가운데에는, 「난봉꾼의 행각」에 나오는 \"선생, 당신 희곡을 읽어 보았소만, 이건 안 되겠소\" 같은 편지가 분명 한 통 이상 있었을 법하다.\n\n1595년 사라고사에서 성 야신토의 시성(諡聖)을 기리는 한 문학 경연에서 그는 더 큰 성공을 거두어, 그의 작품이 일등상인 은수저 세 개를 탔다. 이보다 한 해 전에 그는 그라나다 왕국의 세금 징수원에 임명되어 있었다. 거두어들인 돈을 국고로 더 편하게 송금하려고 그는 그것을 한 상인에게 맡겼는데, 그 상인이 파산하여 종적을 감추었다. 그리고 그 파산자의 재산이 전액을 메우기에 모자랐던 탓에, 그는 1597년 9월 세비야의 감옥에 갇혔다. 그러나 그가 진 차액은 약 26파운드로 적은 액수였고, 그것에 대한 담보를 제공하자 그는 그해 말에 풀려났다.\n\n왕의 세금을 거두느라 이 고을 저 고을을 떠도는 동안, 그는 「돈키호테」의 페이지마다 넘쳐 나는 그 여인숙과 길가의 삶과 인물상의 단편들을 적어 두었다. 안경을 쓰고 양산을 받친 채 키 큰 노새에 올라탄 베네딕트회 수도사들, 다음 마을로 가는 분장한 광대들, 환자에게 사혈을 하러 가는 길에 대야를 머리에 인 이발사, 반바지를 봇짐에 넣고 노래 부르며 길을 터벅터벅 걷는 신병, 여인숙 문간에 모여 「이르카니아의 펠릭스마르테」를 낭독하는 것을 듣는 추수꾼들, 그리고 그가 그토록 잘 끌어들일 줄 알았던 호가스풍의 자잘한 손길들 — 여관 주인의 빗이 꽂힌 채 매달린 소꼬리, 침대 머리맡의 포도주 부대, 그리고 여인숙 예술의 저 주목할 만한 본보기들, 곧 파리스의 팔에 매달려 한껏 들떠 떠나가는 헬레네와, 탑 위에서 호두알만 한 눈물을 떨구는 디도 같은 것들 말이다. 아니, 외진 고장으로 가는 그 여정에서 그가 이따금 빈궁한 신사의 한 표본과 마주쳤을 법도 하다. 비쩍 마른 말과 사냥개와 기사도 서적을 갖추고, 자기 증조부의 옛 투구가 새것이던 시절 이래 세상이 변했음을 행복하게 모른 채 일생을 꿈결로 흘려보내는 그런 신사 말이다. 그러나 그가 자신의 진정한 천직을 발견한 곳은 세비야였으니, 그 자신은 결코 그렇다고 인정하려 들지 않았겠지만 말이다. 바로 그곳, 트리아나에서 그는 처음으로 삶에서 직접 묘사를 시도해 보고 싶은 유혹을 느꼈고, 「린코네테와 코르타디요」라는 더없이 빼어난 작은 소품 — 여러 면에서 「돈키호테」의 씨앗인 — 에서 처음으로 자신의 해학을 발휘해 보였다.\n\n그것이 어디서 언제 쓰였는지 우리는 알 수 없다. 그가 투옥된 뒤로 공직자로서의 세르반테스의 자취는 모두 사라지는데, 이로 미루어 그가 복직되지 못했다고 추정할 수 있다. 그가 1598년 11월에도 여전히 세비야에 있었음은, 펠리페 2세의 죽음에 즈음하여 도시의 슬픔을 나타내고자 세운 정교한 영구대(靈柩臺)에 부친 그의 풍자 소네트 한 편에서 드러나지만, 이때부터 1603년까지는 그의 행적에 관한 단서가 우리에게 없다. 「돈키호테」 제1부 서문의 말은, 그가 이 책의 착상을 떠올리고 적어도 그 첫머리만은 감옥에서 썼다는 것을 결정적으로 보여 주는 것으로 흔히들 여기며, 그가 그랬을 가능성은 매우 높다.\n\n세르반테스가 베하르 공작의 저택에서 가려 뽑은 청중에게 자기 작품의 일부를 읽어 주었다는 전승이 있는데, 이것이 그 책을 알리는 데 도움이 되었을지도 모른다. 그러나 분명한 결론은, 「돈키호테」 제1부가 그토록 새로운 성격의 모험을 떠맡을 만큼 대담한 출판업자를 그가 찾아내기까지 한동안 그의 수중에 머물러 있었다는 것이다. 그리고 마침내 그가 그것을 판 마드리드의 프란시스코 로블레스는 그 책을 거의 믿지 않아, 아라곤이나 포르투갈의 판권을 확보하는 비용을 들일 마음조차 없이 카스티야의 판권으로 만족했다. 인쇄는 12월에 끝났고, 책은 새해, 곧 1605년에 나왔다. 「돈키호테」가 처음에는 냉담하게 받아들여졌다고들 흔히 말한다. 사실은 정반대를 보여 준다. 그것이 대중의 손에 들어가기가 무섭게 리스본과 발렌시아에서 해적판을 낼 채비가 갖추어졌고, 그가 2월에 확보한 아라곤과 포르투갈의 추가 판권을 더한 재판을 낼 준비가 이루어졌다.\n\n물론 그것은 사회의 어떤 부류들에게는 냉담함 그 이상의 무언가로 받아들여졌다. 귀족 가운데 재치와 안목과 분별을 갖춘 이들은 그것을 진심으로 환영했으나, 귀족 일반은 자기들이 가장 좋아하는 읽을거리를 조롱거리로 만들고 자기들이 아끼는 그 많은 관념을 비웃는 책을 달가워할 리 없었다. 로페를 우두머리로 떠받들며 그 둘레에 모여든 극작가들은 세르반테스를 자기들 공동의 적으로 여겼고, 공고라를 우두머리로 둔 또 다른 패거리, 곧 기교파 시인들에게도 그가 똑같이 미움받았음이 분명하다. 앞서 언급한 그 편지에 대해서는 전혀 알지 못한 나바레테는, 세르반테스와 로페 사이의 관계가 매우 우호적인 것이었음을 애써 보이려 하는데, 「돈키호테」가 쓰이기 전까지는 실제로 그러했다. 사실 세르반테스는 마지막까지 너그럽고도 사내답게 로페의 역량과 그 마르지 않는 착상, 그 놀라운 다작을 칭송한다고 공언했다. 그러나 「돈키호테」 제1부 서문에서, 그리고 「알 수 없는 우르간다」의 시구에서, 또 한두 군데 다른 곳에서, 행간을 읽어 보면, 아무런 개인적 호의도 내비치지 않는, 로페의 허영과 가식에 대한 은근한 꼬집음이 있다. 그리고 로페는 「돈키호테」와 세르반테스를 드러내 놓고 비웃었으며, 그가 죽은 지 십사 년 뒤 「아폴로의 월계관」에서 그에게 차갑고 진부한 몇 줄만을 바쳤을 뿐인데, 그것은 다른 어디에서도 그 이름을 찾을 수 없는 한 무리의 보잘것없는 자들에게 바친 찬사들 탓에 더더욱 차갑게 느껴진다.\n\n1601년 바야돌리드가 궁정의 소재지가 되었고, 1603년 초에 세르반테스는 아직 미결로 남아 있던, 그가 국고에 진 차액과 관련하여 그리로 소환되었다. 그는 바야돌리드에 머물렀는데, 보아하니 대리 업무와 모종의 대서(代書) 일로 생계를 꾸린 듯하다. 아마도 심의회에 제출할 청원서를 작성하고 청구 진술서를 꾸미는 따위의 일이었을 것이다. 적어도 우리는, 거리의 난투극의 희생자가 되어 그가 살던 집으로 옮겨진 한 신사의 죽음에 즈음하여 받아 둔 진술에서 그렇게 미루어 본다. 그 진술에서 그 자신은 글을 쓰고 업무를 처리하는 사람으로 묘사되어 있으며, 당시 그의 집안은 아내, 앞서 언급한 사생아 딸 이사벨 데 사아베드라, 이제 과부가 된 누이 안드레아, 그녀의 딸 콘스탄사, 그리고 자신을 그의 누이라 일컬었으나 전기 작가들이 그 내력을 밝히지 못하는 수수께끼 같은 막달레나 데 소토마요르, 그리고 하녀 하나로 이루어져 있었던 듯하다.\n\n그러는 동안 「돈키호테」는 점점 더 호평을 받아 갔고, 그 저자의 이름은 이제 피레네산맥 너머에까지 알려졌다. 1607년 브뤼셀에서 한 판이 인쇄되었다. 마드리드의 출판업자 로블레스는 1608년 세 번째 판, 곧 통틀어 일곱 번째 판으로 그 수요를 맞춰야 할 형편이었다. 이탈리아에서 그 책의 인기가 어찌나 대단했던지 밀라노의 한 서적상이 1610년에 한 판을 내기에 이르렀고, 1611년에는 브뤼셀에서 또 한 판이 요청되었다. 자신이 대중의 취향을 적중시켰다는 그러한 증거들이 눈앞에 있었으니, 세르반테스가 다소 막연한 두 번째 권의 약속을 곧장 이행하는 데 착수했으리라 자연히 기대했을 법하다.\n\n그러나 보아하니, 그의 생각에서 그보다 더 먼 것은 없었다. 그에게는 「돈키호테」에 끼워 넣었던 것들과 같은 부류의 짤막한 이야기가 아직 한두 편 남아 있었고, 돈키호테의 모험을 이어 가는 대신 그는 그것들로 책 한 권을 묶을 작정으로, 훗날 그가 「모범 소설집」이라 부른 그 「노벨라스 에헴플라레스」를 더 써 나가기 시작했다.\n\n그 소설들은 1613년 여름에 출간되었는데, 당대의 마이케나스였던 레모스 백작에게 바치는 헌사와, 세르반테스가 그토록 좋아하던 그 친근하고 허물없는 서문 가운데 하나가 곁들여졌다. 이 서문에서, 「돈키호테」 제1부가 나온 지 팔 년 반 만에, 우리는 곧 나올 제2부에 대한 첫 암시를 얻는다. \"머지않아 여러분은,\" 그가 말하기를, \"돈키호테의 더 많은 활약과 산초 판사의 익살을 보게 될 것입니다.\" 그가 말하는 \"머지않아\"란 다소 신축성 있는 것이었으니, 산초의 편지에 적힌 날짜로 우리가 알 수 있듯, 그는 그로부터 일 년이 지나서야 그 책의 겨우 절반을 완성한 정도였기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 시든, 목가든, 소설이든, 그 무엇보다 그의 마음을 사로잡은 것은 극작에 대한 야심이었다. 알제의 감옥에서 그를 절망으로부터 지켜 주고, 자신과 동료들의 탈출을 거듭거듭 시도하도록 그를 부추겼던 바로 그 불굴의 정신이, 극작가로서 대중의 귀를 사로잡으려는 노력에서 실패와 좌절에도 아랑곳없이 그를 끈질기게 버티게 했다. 세르반테스의 기질은 본질적으로 낙천적이었다. 소설집 서문에서 그가 그려 보인 초상 — 매부리코의 이목구비, 밤색 머리칼, 매끄럽고 근심 없는 이마, 그리고 밝고 쾌활한 눈 — 은 영락없는 낙천가의 초상이다. 흥행주들이 무슨 말을 하든, 자기 희곡들이 한 번이라도 공정한 기회만 얻는다면 그 진가가 끝내 인정받으리라는 생각을 그에게서 거둘 수는 없었다. 스페인 살라미스의 그 늙은 병사는 스페인의 아이스킬로스가 되기로 작정한 것이었다. 그는 예술의 참된 원리에 바탕을 둔, 모든 나라의 부러움을 살 위대한 국민극을 세우려 했다. 흥행주들의 탐욕과 작가들의 근시안 탓에 유행하던 그 어리석고 유치한 연극들, 그 \"헛소리의 거울이요 어리석음의 본보기\"인 것들을 무대에서 몰아내려 했고, 대중의 취향을 — 이를테면 「누만시아」 같은, 그리스 극을 본뜬 비극과, 즐겁게 할 뿐 아니라 나아지게 하고 가르치기까지 할 희극을 받아들일 만큼 무르익을 때까지 — 바로잡고 길러 내려 했다. 이 모든 것을 그는, 한 번이라도 들어줄 사람을 얻기만 한다면, 해내려 했다. 거기에 첫 번째 난관이 있었다.\n\n그는 또한, 「돈키호테」와 기사도 소설의 타파가 그의 가슴에 가장 가까이 자리한 일거리가 아니었음을 충분히 분명하게 드러내 보인다. 사실 그는 자신의 서문에서 스스로 말하듯, 「돈키호테」에게 아버지라기보다 의붓아버지에 더 가까웠다. 일찍이 어떤 위대한 작품도 그 저자에게 그토록 소홀히 다루어진 적이 없었다. 그것이 부주의하게, 서둘러, 띄엄띄엄 쓰였다는 것이 늘 그의 잘못만은 아니었으나, 그가 인쇄소에 보낸 것을 한 번도 읽어 보지 않았음은 분명해 보인다. 그는 인쇄공들이 어떻게 일을 그르쳤는지 알고 있었으나, 세 번째 판이 진행 중일 때조차, 자기 머리에서 나온 자식을 진정으로 아끼는 사람이라면 했을 법한, 그것들을 바로잡는 수고를 결코 기울이지 않았다. 그는 이 책을 한낱 \"엔트레테니미엔토의 책\", 곧 재미난 책, 「여행기」에서 그가 말하듯 \"어느 때 어느 철에든 우울하고 침울한 마음을 달래 주는\" 것 정도로밖에 여기지 않은 듯하다. 분명 그는 자신의 주인공에게 애정을 품었고 산초 판사를 매우 자랑스러워했다. 모든 소설을 통틀어 가장 해학적인 창조물을 자랑스러워하지 않았다면 그것이야말로 참으로 이상한 일이었으리라. 그는 또한 그 책의 인기와 성공도 자랑스러워했으니, 제2부의 열두어 군데 대목에서 그가 그 자부심을 내보이는 그 천진함은 더없이 흐뭇하다. 그러나 그것은 그가 갈망하던 성공이 아니었다. 십중팔구 그는, 로페 데 베가가 평균 일주일에 한 번꼴로 누리던 그런 성공 한 번을 위해서라면, 「돈키호테」의 모든 성공을 다 내주었을 것이며, 아니 「돈키호테」의 모든 부수가 마요르 광장에서 불타는 것을 보았을 것이다.\n\n그리하여 그는 「돈키호테」를 가지고 꾸물거리며, 이따금 한 장(章)씩 보태고는 그것을 제쳐 두고서 「페르실레스와 시히스문다」 — 우리가 알다시피 그 언어로 쓰인 가장 재미난 책이자 「테아게네스와 카리클레아」의 맞수가 될 책 — 로 눈을 돌리거나, 자신이 끔찍이 아끼는 희극 가운데 하나를 마무리 짓곤 했다. 그리고 로블레스가 「돈키호테」가 언제 준비되겠느냐고 물으면, 그 대답은 분명 이러했을 것이다. 엔 브레베, 곧 머지않아서요, 그건 시간이 넉넉하니까요. 예순여덟의 나이에도 그는 마치……처럼 생기와 희망과 앞날의 계획으로 가득 차 있었다.\n\n네메시스는 다가오고 있었다. 그는 제59장까지 써 내려갔는데, 그의 그 느긋한 속도로 보아 1614년 10월이나 11월 이전에는 도저히 거기에 이르지 못했을 터인데, 그때 그의 손에 작은 8절판 한 권이 쥐어졌으니, 최근 타라고나에서 인쇄된 것으로 스스로를 「기발한 신사 라만차의 돈키호테 제2권: 토르데시야스의 학사 알론소 페르난데스 데 아베야네다 지음」이라 일컫는 책이었다. 제59장의 후반부와 제2부의 이어지는 여러 장은 이 책이 그에게 어떤 영향을 끼쳤는지를 어느 정도 짐작하게 해 주는데, 자기 자신 말고는 탓할 사람이 아무도 없다는 생각이 그의 분노를 누그러뜨려 주었을 리는 없다. 사실 만일 아베야네다가 단지 「돈키호테」의 속편을 펴내는 것에 만족했더라면, 세르반테스로서는 정당하게 불평할 거리가 없었을 것이다. 그 자신의 의도는 책 끄트머리에서 더없이 모호한 언어로 표현되어 있었으니, 아니 그가 마지막에 남긴 말 \"어쩌면 다른 이가 더 나은 가락으로 노래하리라(forse altro cantera con miglior plettro)\"를 보면, 그는 실로 누군가 다른 이에게 이 작업을 이어 가라고 청하는 듯하며, 게다가 그는 팔 년 반이라는 세월이 흐르도록 아무런 기척도 보이지 않았으니, 그 무렵이면 아베야네다의 책은 의심할 바 없이 이미 쓰여 있었을 것이다.\n\n* forse altro cantera con miglior plettro: 이탈리아어로 \"어쩌면 다른 이가 더 나은 가락으로 노래하리라\"라는 뜻으로, 아리오스토의 「광란의 오를란도」에 나오는 구절이다.\n사실 단순히 속편이라는 점에 관한 한 세르반테스에게는 따질 명분이 없었거나, 있다 해도 매우 빈약한 것이었다. 그러나 아베야네다는 거기에 서문을 쓰기로 작정했으니, 그것은 오직 심성이 비뚤어진 자만이 쏟아 낼 수 있을 법한 그런 거칠고 인신공격적인 욕설로 가득 차 있었다. 그는 세르반테스를 두고 늙었다느니, 한 손을 잃었다느니, 감옥살이를 했다느니, 가난하다느니, 벗이 없다느니 하며 조롱하고, 로페의 성공을 시기한다느니, 성마르고 불평이 많다느니 하며 비난하는데, 바로 여기에 그 가시가 박혀 있었다. 아베야네다가 이런 인신공격을 한 까닭은 충분히 분명하다. 그가 누구였든 간에, 그가 로페 학파의 극작가 가운데 하나였음은 명백하니, 그는 뻔뻔스럽게도 세르반테스가 연극에 관한 비평에서 로페뿐 아니라 자신까지 공격했다고 몰아세우고 있기 때문이다. 그의 정체를 밝히는 일은 가장 뛰어난 비평가들조차 골머리를 앓게 했고, 이에 동원된 온갖 재간과 연구를 다 좌절시켰다. 나바레테와 티크너는 둘 다 세르반테스가 그가 누구인지 알고 있었다는 쪽으로 기우나, 나로서는 그가 드러내는 분노가 보이지 않는 가해자를 시사한다고 말할 수밖에 없다. 그것은 어둠 속에서 모기에게 쏘인 사람의 짜증과도 같다. 세르반테스는 몇몇 어법상의 잘못을 근거로 그를 아라곤 사람이라 단정하며, 자신도 아라곤 사람인 펠리세르는 이 견해를 지지하고 나아가 그가 성직자, 아마도 도미니쿠스회 수도사였으리라고 믿는다.\n\n아베야네다에게 어떤 장점이 있든 그것은 세르반테스에게서 반사된 것이며, 그는 그나마도 많이 반사하기에는 너무 둔하다. \"둔하고 추잡하다\"는 것이 편견 없는 독자 대다수의 평결이 되리라고 나는 생각한다. 그는 기껏해야 형편없는 표절꾼이니, 그가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 세르반테스가 그에게 내준 길을 노예처럼 따라가는 것뿐이다. 그의 유일한 해학이라고는 돈키호테로 하여금 여관을 성으로 알게 하고 자신을 어떤 전설적 혹은 역사적 인물로 착각하게 만들며, 산초로 하여금 말을 잘못 알아듣고 속담을 거꾸로 뒤집고 자기 식탐을 드러내게 하는 데 있을 뿐이다. 그는 처음부터 끝까지 천박함과 추잡함으로 기우는 성벽을 보이며, 16세기 이탈리아 단편 작가들의 그 어떤 것보다 더 더러우면서도 그들의 발랄함이라고는 없는 이야기 두 편을 끼워 넣는 솜씨를 부렸다.\n\n* 이탈리아 단편 작가들(novellieri): 보카치오의 「데카메론」 전통을 잇는 16세기 이탈리아의 단편 소설 작가들을 가리킨다.\n그러나 아베야네다와 그의 책이 어떠하든, 우리가 그들에게 진 빚을 잊어서는 안 된다. 그들이 없었더라면 「돈키호테」가 완성된 작품이 아니라 한낱 토르소로 우리에게 전해졌으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 설령 세르반테스가 손에 쥐고 있던 그 책을 끝마쳤다 하더라도, 그는 거의 틀림없이 돈키호테의 더 많은 모험과 양치기가 된 산초 판사의 익살을 담은 제3부에 대한 약속을 남기며 붓을 놓았을 것이다. 그가 한때 기사도 책을 다루었던 것처럼 목가 소설을 다루려는 의도를 품었던 것은 분명하며, 아베야네다가 없었더라면 그는 그것을 실행에 옮기려 애썼을 것이다. 그러나 그의 계획과 구상과 낙천적 기질로 미루어 보건대, 그 책은 그가 죽을 때까지 미완으로 남았을 가능성이 더 크며, 그리하여 우리는 공작 부부와 알게 되지도 못했을 것이고, 산초와 함께 바라타리아로 가 보지도 못했을 것이다.\n\n그 책이 자기 손에 들어온 순간부터 그는 들판에 아베야네다가 더 있을지도 모른다는 두려움에 사로잡혔던 듯하며, 다른 모든 것을 제쳐 두고 자기 과업을 마무리하고 자신이 할 수 있는 유일한 방법으로, 곧 돈키호테를 죽임으로써 그를 지키는 일에 착수했다. 그 결말이 서둘러 마무리되고 군데군데 서투른 작업임은 의심할 바 없으며, 아베야네다에게 퍼붓는 꾸지람이 거듭 되풀이되는 것은 결국 다소 진력나는 일이 되고 만다. 그러나 그것은 어쨌든 하나의 결말이며, 그 점에 대해서는 우리가 아베야네다에게 고마워해야 한다.\n\n새 책은 2월에 인쇄에 부칠 준비가 되었으나 1615년이 거의 다 저물어서야 인쇄되었고, 그 사이 세르반테스는 지난 몇 해 동안 써 두었던, 그리고 그가 구슬프게 덧붙이듯 극단 지배인들 사이에서 아무런 수요도 찾지 못했던 희극과 막간극들을 한데 모아, 그것이 소개하는 책보다 열 배는 값진 서문을 붙여 펴냈으니, 그 서문에서 그는 초기 스페인 무대의 사정과 극작가로서 자신이 시도했던 일들에 관해 이야기한다. 세르반테스가 그것들을 그 가치에 대한 온전한 신의와 충만한 확신을 품고 내놓았음은 말할 필요도 없다. 그러나 독자는 그것이 연극에 관한 그의 마지막 말이나 최종적인 노력이라고 여겨서는 안 되었으니, 그는 「눈속임」이라는 희극을 손에 쥐고 있었고, 그가 잘못 안 것이 아니라면 그것에 관해서는 아무런 이론도 없으리라는 것이었다.\n* 눈속임(Engano a los ojos): \"눈을 속임\"이라는 뜻의 스페인어로, 세르반테스가 언급한 이 희극은 전해지지 않는다.\n\n세상은 이 극적 걸작을 판단할 기회를 갖지 못했으니, 그의 건강은 한동안 쇠해 가고 있었고, 그는 1616년 4월 23일 명백히 수종으로 세상을 떠났으니, 적어도 명목상으로는 잉글랜드가 셰익스피어를 잃은 바로 그날이었다. 잉글랜드의 달력은 아직 개정되지 않았던 것이다. 그는 살아온 그대로 죽었으니, 자기 운명을 용감하고 명랑하게 받아들이며 떠났다.\n\n* 잉글랜드 달력 개정: 당시 잉글랜드는 율리우스력을, 스페인은 그레고리력을 사용하여 날짜 표기에 차이가 있었으므로, 같은 날짜라 하나 실제로는 다른 날이었다.\n세르반테스의 삶은 불행한 것이었던가? 그의 전기 작가들은 한결같이 그러했노라 말하나, 나로서는 그것이 의심스럽다고 말할 수밖에 없다. 그것은 고된 삶, 가난의 삶, 끊임없는 투쟁의 삶, 보수가 박한 노고의 삶, 실망의 삶이었으나, 세르반테스는 이 모든 해악에 맞설 해독제를 제 안에 지니고 있었다. 그의 천성은 오로지 제 부력만으로 역경 위로 떠오르는 그런 가벼운 천성이 아니었다. 그가 역경을 견뎌 낼 수 있었던 것은 고매한 정신의 굳건함에 의해서였다. 세르반테스가 낙담에 굴복하거나 의기소침함에 짓눌리는 모습이란 도무지 상상할 수 없다. 가난으로 말하자면, 그에게 그것은 웃어넘길 거리였으니, 그가 스스로에게 허락한 단 한 번의 한숨은 이런 말을 할 때뿐이었다. \"하늘 말고는 누구에게도 감사할 필요 없는 빵 한 조각을 하늘로부터 받은 자는 복되도다.\" 여기에 그의 왕성한 활력과 정신적 활동, 그의 쉼 없는 창의와 낙천적 기질을 더하면, 그의 삶이 과연 매우 불행한 것이었을까를 의심할 만한 충분한 이유가 될 것이다. 세르반테스의 고난을, 그것을 견디게 해 준 장치와 함께 통째로 떠안을 수 있는 사람이라면, 삶의 행복에 관한 한 그리 나쁜 거래를 하는 것은 아닐지도 모른다.\n\n그가 묻힌 곳에 관해서는, 그가 자신의 유언에 따라 이웃한 삼위일체회 수녀원에 묻혔다는 것 — 그곳에는 그의 딸 이사벨 데 사아베드라가 수녀로 있었으리라 짐작된다 — 과, 몇 해 뒤 수녀들이 다른 수녀원으로 옮겨 가면서 자기들의 죽은 이들을 함께 모셔 갔다는 것 외에는 알려진 바가 없다. 그러나 세르반테스의 유해가 그 이장에 포함되었는지 아닌지는 아무도 모르며, 그가 잠든 곳을 찾을 실마리는 이제 모든 희망을 넘어 잃어버리고 말았다. 이는 그의 동시대인들에게 가해진 무관심이라는 비난의 항목들 가운데 아마도 가장 변호하기 어려운 대목일 것이다. 다른 몇몇 항목에는 상당한 과장이 섞여 있다. 그의 전기 작가 대부분의 말을 듣고 있노라면, 온 스페인이 그 사람뿐 아니라 그에 대한 기억에까지 맞서 결탁이라도 했거나, 적어도 그의 가치에 무감각하여 그를 비참하게 살다가 궁핍 속에 죽게 내버려 두었다고 여기게 될 것이다. 안달루시아에서의 그의 고된 삶과 보잘것없는 일자리를 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하는 것은 터무니없다. 불안정한 생계를 꾸려 가던 다른 수천 명의 분투하는 사람들과 그를 구별 지을 만한 무슨 일을 그가 했단 말인가? 사실 그는 부상을 입고 조국의 대의를 위해 포로살이와 고난을 겪은 용맹한 군인이었으나, 똑같은 처지에 놓인 사람이 수백 명이나 있었다. 그는 김빠진 부류의 소설의 평범한 표본 하나와, 즐거움을 준다는 기본 조건에 명백히 부합하지 못한 희곡 몇 편을 썼을 뿐이니, 작가가 이십 년 뒤에 「돈키호테」를 내놓을 사람이라 하여, 관객들이 자기들을 즐겁게 해 주지도 못하는 연극을 후원해야 했단 말인가?\n\n우리가 본 바와 같이 책이 나오자마자 곧바로 뒤따른 그 사본 쟁탈전은 그 가치에 대한 전반적인 무감각처럼 보이지는 않는다. 의심할 바 없이 그것을 냉담하게 맞은 이들도 더러 있었으나, 가발을 조롱하는 책을 쓰는 사람이라면 가발 쓴 자들에게 냉대받고 가발 만드는 무리 전체에게 미움받을 각오를 해야 마땅하다. 세르반테스가 기사도 소설 독자, 감상주의자, 극작가, 그리고 그 시대의 시인들을 모조리 적으로 두었다면, 그것은 「돈키호테」가 바로 그런 책이었기 때문이다. 그리고 만일 일반 대중이 그의 여생을 편안하게 해 주려 나서지 않았다 하더라도, 그것은 스콧의 빚을 갚아 주지 않은 영어권 대중에게 무관심과 배은망덕의 죄를 물을 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로, 그들에게 그런 죄를 물을 일이 아니다. 그들은 할 수 있는 최선을 다했으니, 그의 책을 읽고 좋아하고 사들였으며, 서적상으로 하여금 다른 작품들에 대해 그에게 후한 값을 치르도록 부추겼다.\n\n또한 스페인이 가장 자랑스러워하는 사람을 위해 아무런 기념비도 세우지 않았다는 것이 그 나라에 대한 비난거리가 되어 왔다. 다시 말해 그를 기리는 기념비가 없다는 것이니, 코르테스 광장의 작은 정원에 있는 청동상은 의심할 바 없이 제법 훌륭한 예술 작품이며 어느 지방 소도시의 장터에 그 고장 시인을 기려 세웠더라면 나무랄 데 없었겠지만, 세르반테스나 마드리드에 어울리는 것은 못 된다. 그러나 세르반테스에게 \"그의 이름에 대한 그런 미약한 증언\"이 무슨 소용이 있겠으며, 그의 경우에 기념비가 그것을 세운 자들의 자기 과시를 증언하는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가? 기념비를 찾고자 하거든 둘러보라(Si monumentum quoeris, circumspice). 가장 가까운 서점이 「돈키호테」의 작가에게 바치는 기념비가 얼마나 용두사미가 될지를 보여 줄 것이다.\n\n* Si monumentum quoeris, circumspice: 라틴어로 \"기념비를 찾고자 하거든 둘러보라\"라는 뜻으로, 런던 세인트폴 대성당의 건축가 크리스토퍼 렌의 묘비명이다.\n세르반테스가 죽기 전에 이미 「돈키호테」 제1부는 아홉 판을 거듭했으니, 그 자신의 추산으로는 모두 삼만 부에 달했고, 그가 죽은 이듬해에는 바르셀로나에서 열 번째 판이 인쇄되었다. 이만큼 많은 부수가 한동안은 자연히 수요를 채워 주었으나, 1634년 무렵에는 동난 듯하다. 그리고 그때부터 오늘날에 이르기까지 판본의 물줄기는 빠르고 꾸준하게 흘러왔다. 번역본들은 이 책이 맨 처음부터 얼마나 큰 수요를 누렸는지를 한층 더 분명히 보여 준다. 작품이 완성된 지 칠 년 만에 그것은 유럽의 주요 네 언어로 번역되었다. 사실 성경을 제외하면 「돈키호테」만큼 널리 퍼진 책은 없다. 「그리스도를 본받아」도 그만큼 여러 언어로 번역되었을지 모르고, 어쩌면 「로빈슨 크루소」와 「웨이크필드의 목사」도 거의 그만큼 많은 언어로 번역되었을지 모르나, 번역과 판본의 다양함에서 「돈키호테」는 그것들을 모두 멀찍이 따돌린다.\n\n이 폭넓은 보급의 성격은 한층 더 주목할 만하다. 「돈키호테」는 편력 기사도에 관한 관념이라고는 — 설령 조금이라도 있었다 한들 — 더없이 흐릿했던 사람들, 기사도 책이라고는 한 번도 보거나 들어 본 적이 없었던 사람들, 그 풍자극의 해학을 도무지 느낄 수도 작가의 의도에 공감할 수도 없었던 사람들 사이에서도 완전히 토착화되었다. 또 한 가지 묘한 사실은, 세상에서 가장 세계주의적인 이 책이 동시에 가장 강렬하게 민족적인 책 가운데 하나라는 점이다. 「마농 레스코」가 그보다 더 철저히 프랑스적이지 않고, 「톰 존스」가 그보다 더 영국적이지 않으며, 「롭 로이」가 그보다 더 스코틀랜드적이지 않으니, 「돈키호테」는 성격에서나 사상에서나 정서에서나 향토색에서나 그 모든 것에서 스페인적이다. 그렇다면 거의 삼 세기에 걸쳐 해마다 더해 온 이 유례없는 인기의 비밀은 무엇인가? 한 가지 설명은 의심할 바 없이, 세상의 모든 책 가운데 「돈키호테」가 가장 보편적인 책이라는 것이다. 그 안에는 젊든 늙든, 현명하든 단순하든, 신분이 높든 낮든, 온갖 부류의 독자를 위한 무언가가 들어 있다. 세르반테스 자신이 약간의 자부심을 담아 말하듯, \"그것은 온갖 부류의 사람들이 손때 묻혀 가며 읽고 외우니, 아이들은 그 책장을 넘기고, 젊은이들은 그것을 읽고, 어른들은 그것을 이해하고, 노인들은 그것을 칭송한다.\"\n\n그러나 그 해학이나 지혜, 혹은 그것이 보여 주는 창의의 풍부함이나 인간 본성에 대한 통찰보다도, 대중에게서 그 성공을 확실히 보장해 준 요소가 그 책 전체에 흐르는 익살의 맥이라는 점을 부인한다면 그것은 부질없는 일이리라. 처음에 큰 매력이었고 어쩌면 지금도 어느 정도 독자 대다수에게 그러한 것은, 양 떼를 향한 공격, 포도주 부대와의 싸움, 맘브리노의 투구, 피에라브라스의 영약, 풍차 날개에 나가떨어지는 돈키호테, 담요에 키질당하는 산초, 그리고 주인과 종이 겪는 봉변과 재난들이었다. 「돈키호테」는 처음에, 그리고 실로 스페인에서는 오랫동안, 대체로 우스운 사건과 터무니없는 상황으로 가득한, 매우 재미나기는 하나 그다지 깊은 고려나 정성을 들일 가치는 없는 한낱 기이하고 익살스러운 책쯤으로 여겨졌음이 분명하다. 1637년부터, 저 유명한 인쇄업자 이바라가 손을 댄 1771년에 이르기까지 스페인에서 인쇄된 모든 판본은 한낱 장삿속 판본이었으니, 형편없는 종이에 조잡하고 부주의하게 인쇄되고, 오로지 대중적 용도만을 위한 싸구려 이야기책의 모양새로 꾸며졌으며, 대개의 경우 거친 삽화와 출판업자가 덧붙인 눈요깃거리들이 들어 있었다.\n\n이러한 것보다 더 나은 대접을 받을 「돈키호테」의 권리를 처음으로 인정한 나라가 잉글랜드라는 공로는 그 나라에 돌아간다. 흔히 카터릿 경의 제안으로 이루어졌다 하여 그의 이름으로 불리는 1738년 런던판은 한낱 호화판이 아니었다. 그것은 종이와 활자 면에서 「돈키호테」를 걸맞은 모양새로 펴내고, 삽화로서 특별히 잘된 것은 아니라 해도 적어도 의도가 좋고 솜씨 있게 제작된 도판들로 꾸몄을 뿐 아니라, 본문의 정확성을 목표로 삼았으니, 이는 발렌시아판과 브뤼셀판의 편집자들 말고는 아무도 스쳐 가는 생각조차 해 본 적 없는 문제였다. 그리고 첫 시도치고는 제법 성공적이었으니, 그 교정 가운데 몇몇은 받아들일 수 없는 것이지만 상당수는 이후의 모든 편집자에게 채택되었기 때문이다.\n\n출판업자와 편집자와 주석가들의 열의는 「돈키호테」에 관한 정서에 주목할 만한 변화를 불러왔다. 그것을 흠모하는 수많은 이들이 그것을 두고 웃는 일을 부끄러워하기 시작했다. 그것을 해학적인 책으로 다루는 것이 거의 죄처럼 되어 버렸다. 그 해학이 전적으로 부정된 것은 아니나, 새로운 견해에 따르면 그것은 어디까지나 부차적인 자질, 한낱 부속물, 세르반테스가 자신의 철학이나 풍자, 혹은 그가 쏘아 맞히려 했던 것이 무엇이든 그것을 쏘기 위해 내세운 위장용 말에 지나지 않는 것으로 평가되었다. 이 점에 관해서는 의견이 갈렸기 때문이다. 그러나 그가 겨냥한 목표가 기사도 책이 아니었다는 데에는 모두가 의견을 같이했다. 그는 제1부 서문에서, 그리고 제2부의 마지막 문장에서, 자신에게는 이런 책들의 신용을 떨어뜨리는 것 말고는 다른 목적이 없었노라고 힘주어 말했는데, 진보한 비평에는 이것이 도리어 그의 목적이 무언가 다른 것이었음에 틀림없다는 점을 분명히 해 주었다.\n\n한 가지 학설은 이 책이 일종의 우의(寓意)로서, 이상과 현실 사이, 시의 정신과 산문의 정신 사이의 영원한 투쟁을 그려 낸다는 것이었다. 그리고 어쩌면 독일 철학은 제 내적 의식의 심연에서 이보다 더 볼품없고 더 어울리지 않는 낙타를 끌어낸 적이 없을 것이다. 의심할 바 없이 그러한 대립의 일면은 「돈키호테」에서 발견되니, 그것이 삶의 도처에서 발견되기 때문이며, 세르반테스는 삶에서 길어 올렸기 때문이다. 산초 판사와 돈키호테 사이의 끊임없는 동상이몽의 유희가 자연 그대로의 참된 모습으로 인정받지 못할 공동체란 상상하기 어렵다. 석기 시대에도, 호상 가옥 거주자들 사이에서도, 동굴 인간들 사이에서도 돈키호테와 산초 판사는 있었다. 제 눈앞의 사실을 결코 보지 못하는 혈거인이 있었을 것이고, 그것 말고는 아무것도 보지 못하는 혈거인도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 세르반테스가 두 권의 두툼한 4절판으로 그런 관념을 풀어내고자 일부러 작정하고 나섰으리라 가정하는 것은, 그가 살았던 시대와 매우 동떨어졌을 뿐 아니라 세르반테스 자신과는 더더욱 동떨어진 무언가를 가정하는 일이니, 그라면 누군가 다른 이가 그런 시도를 했을 때 누구보다 먼저 웃어넘겼을 사람이다.\n\n그의 시대에 기사도 소설이 끼친 그 비상한 영향만으로도 이 책의 발생을 설명하기에 충분하다. 16세기에 이 문학 갈래가 얼마나 엄청나게 발전했는지에 대한 어느 정도의 관념은 제7장의 검열 장면에서 얻을 수 있으니, 다만 독자는 단연 가장 큰 무리에 속하는 소설들 가운데 일부만이 열거되어 있음을 염두에 두어야 한다. 그것이 이 민족에게 끼친 영향에 관해서는 증거가 차고 넘친다. 아마디스류와 팔메린류가 인기를 얻기 시작한 때부터 그 세기가 거의 끝날 무렵까지, 그 성품과 지위가 그 말에 무게를 실어 주는 사람들에게서 기사도 소설과 그 독자들의 광적인 도취에 대한 비난이 끊임없이 쏟아졌다. 그 먼지를 쓸어 낼 유일한 빗자루는 조롱이었다.\n\n이것이 세르반테스가 스스로에게 부과한 과업이었으며, 그가 그 일에 나서도록 부추긴 충분한 자극이 있었음은 그 증거를 들여다보는 이들에게 충분히 분명해질 것이다. 또한 그가 공격하여 쓸어 낸 것이 기사도 자체가 아니었다는 점도 마찬가지로 분명해질 것이다. 시(詩) 덕분에 시간이 다할 때까지 되풀이될 온갖 터무니없는 말 가운데, \"세르반테스가 미소 한 번으로 스페인의 기사도를 사라지게 했다\"는 말보다 더 큰 것은 없다. 우선 그가 미소로 사라지게 할 기사도란 애초에 없었다. 스페인의 기사도는 이미 한 세기 넘게 죽어 있었다. 그라나다가 함락되었을 때 그 소임은 끝났으며, 기사도란 본질적으로 그 성격이 공화주의적인 것이었기에, 페르난도가 중세 스페인의 자유로운 제도를 대신해 세운 통치 아래서는 살아남을 수 없었다. 그가 미소로 사라지게 한 것은 기사도가 아니라 그것을 욕되게 하는 조롱거리였다.\n\n시인의 말에 따르면 그 앞에서 \"온 세상이 물러섰다\"는, 그리고 세르반테스의 단 한 번의 웃음이 무너뜨렸다는 그 \"강한 팔\"과 \"빛나는 위용\"의 참모습은, 조지 칼턴 대위가 그의 「1672년부터 1713년까지의 군 회고록」에서 전하는, 세르반테스 자신의 동포인 돈 펠릭스 파체코의 말에서 헤아릴 수 있다. 그는 이렇게 말했다. \"세르반테스의 그 노작이 세상에 나오기 전에는, 사람이 거리를 조금이라도 즐겁게, 혹은 위험 없이 걷는다는 것은 거의 불가능한 일에 가까웠습니다. 제 연인의 창문 앞에서 말을 껑충거리며 재롱떠는 기사들이 어찌나 많이 보였던지, 외지 사람이라면 이 나라 전체가 다름 아닌 편력 기사의 종족이라고 여겼을 것입니다. 그러나 세상이 그 주목할 만한 이야기를 조금 알게 된 뒤로는, 한때 떠받들어지던 그 차림새로 보이는 자는 돈키호테라 손가락질당하고 위아래 할 것 없이 모두의 웃음거리가 되는 신세가 되었습니다. 그리고 저는 진실로 믿거니와, 우리의 저 이름난 선조들의 그 고귀한 행동들과는 그토록 어울리지 않게 지난 한 세기 동안 우리의 모든 의회에 스며든 그 위축됨과 정신의 빈곤은, 바로 이것에, 오직 이것에 우리가 빚지고 있는 것입니다.\"\n\n「돈키호테」를 두고 삶에 대한 비관적 견해를 설파하는 슬픈 책이라 부르는 것은 그 취지를 완전히 오해하는 것이다. 만일 그 교훈이, 이 세상에서 참된 열정은 자연히 조롱과 낭패로 이어진다는 것이라면 그럴 법도 하다. 그러나 그것은 그런 따위를 설파하지 않는다. 그것에 교훈이 있다 할 수 있는 한, 그 교훈이란, 허영과 자만에서 태어나, 수단이 아니라 그 자체가 목적이 되어 버리고, 상황과 결과를 아랑곳하지 않고 한낱 충동에 따라 움직이는 거짓된 열정은 그 주인에게 해롭고 공동체 전체에는 적잖이 성가신 존재라는 것이다. 이 한 부류와 다른 부류를 구별하지 못하는 이들에게는 의심할 바 없이 「돈키호테」가 슬픈 책이다. 의심할 바 없이 어떤 이들의 마음에는, \"하느님과 자연이 자유로이 만든 이들을 노예로 삼는 것은 가혹한 처사\"라는 그토록 아름다운 정서를 방금 토로한 사람이, 자신의 미친 박애가 사회에 풀어놓은 그 악당들에게 배은망덕하게 돌팔매질당하는 것이 매우 슬픈 일이리라. 그러나 좀 더 분별 있는 기질을 지닌 다른 이들에게는, 무모하고 독선적인 열정이 그것이 세상에 끼치는 온갖 해악에 대해 그런 식으로 보답받는 일이 더 잦지 않다는 것이 안타까운 일이 되리라.\n\n「돈키호테」의 구조를 아주 조금만 살펴보아도, 세르반테스가 이 책을 시작할 때 마음속에 어떤 깊은 설계나 정교한 계획을 품고 있지 않았음을 보여 주기에 충분할 것이다. \"대가의 몇 차례 붓놀림으로 그 가난한 신사를 우리 앞에 세워 놓는\" 그 구절들을 썼을 때, 그는 자신의 상상력이 자기를 이끌어 가던 그 목적지를 알지 못했다. 그가 염두에 둔 것이라고는 이미 써 둔 것들과 어깨를 나란히 할 짧은 이야기, 곧 정신 나간 신사가 현대의 삶에서 편력 기사 노릇을 하려고 시도할 때 뒤따르리라 예상할 수 있는 우스꽝스러운 결과들을 펼쳐 보이는 이야기였음에는 거의 의심의 여지가 없다.\n\n우선 한 가지 분명한 것은, 산초 판사가 애초의 구상에는 들어 있지 않았다는 점이다. 만일 세르반테스가 그를 생각했더라면, 그는 분명 완벽하게 갖추려 했던 자기 주인공의 채비에서 그를 빠뜨리지 않았을 것이기 때문이다. 우리가 그를 얻은 것은 제3장에서 기사들이 종자 없이 여행하는 일은 드물다고 한 여관 주인의 우연한 한마디 덕분이다. 산초 판사 없는 돈키호테를 떠올려 보려는 것은 날 하나뿐인 가위를 떠올려 보려는 것과 같다.\n\n그 이야기는 처음에는 다른 것들과 마찬가지로 아무런 장 구분도 없이, 그리고 시데 아메테 베넹헬리의 개입도 없이 쓰였다. 그리고 세르반테스가 둘시네아, 즉 알돈사 로렌소를 직접 무대에 등장시킬 어떤 의도를 품고 있었을 가능성도 없지 않아 보인다. 그에게 자신의 착상이 발전될 여지가 있음을 처음 시사한 것은 아마도 돈키호테의 서재를 샅샅이 뒤지는 장면과 기사도 책에 관한 논의였을 것이다. 한낱 익살스러운 봉변들을 줄줄이 엮는 대신, 자기 이야기를 이런 책들 가운데 하나를 풍자하는 것으로 만들어, 그것들의 문체와 사건과 정신을 희화화한다면 어떨까?\n\n이 계획 변경에 따라 그는 서둘러, 그리고 다소 서투르게 자기가 써 둔 것을 「아마디스」를 본떠 여러 장으로 나누고, 어떤 알 수 없는 아라비아어 사본이라는 우화를 지어내고, 자기들의 책을 무언가 심오한 출처에서 끌어왔다고 밝히기를 즐겼던 기사도 소설 작가들의 거의 변함없는 관행을 흉내 내어 시데 아메테 베넹헬리를 내세웠다. 새로운 착상을 풀어 가는 가운데 그는 곧 산초 판사의 가치를 발견했다. 실로 산초의 역할뿐 아니라 책 전체의 으뜸음은 산초가 자기 당나귀를 데려가겠다는 뜻을 밝히며 처음 내뱉는 말에서 울린다. \"당나귀에 대해서는,\" 우리에게 전하기를, \"돈키호테는 잠시 망설이며, 종자를 당나귀에 태워 데리고 다닌 편력 기사를 하나라도 떠올릴 수 있을까 애써 보았으나, 그 어떤 본보기도 그의 기억에 떠오르지 않았다.\" 우리는 그 장면 전체를 한눈에 볼 수 있으니, 산초의 멍하니 아무것도 모르는 모습과, 방금 그 어긋남이 제 인식에 들이닥친 그 주인의 당혹스러움이 그것이다. 이것이 책 전체에 걸친 산초의 사명이다. 그는 무의식적인 메피스토펠레스로서, 언제나 본의 아니게 제 주인의 열망을 조롱거리로 만들고, 언제나 어떤 의도치 않은 귀류법으로 그 주인의 생각의 허점을 드러내며, 언제나 순전한 우둔함의 힘으로 그를 사실과 평범함의 세계로 도로 끌어온다.\n\n세르반테스가 자기 소설집을 손에서 털어 내고, 제2부에 진지하게 착수할 만큼 결심을 다잡았을 무렵에는, 사정이 크게 달라져 있었다. 돈키호테와 산초 판사는 한낱 호의를 얻은 정도가 아니라, 이미 그 이후로 줄곧 그래 왔듯이 대중의 상상 속에서 실재하는 존재가 되어 있었다. 이제 그로서는 무관한 내용을 끼워 넣을 까닭이 없었다. 아니, 그의 독자들은 자기들이 그에게 바라는 것은 더 많은 돈키호테와 더 많은 산초 판사이지 소설이나 이야기나 여담이 아니라고 그에게 대놓고 말했다. 그 자신에게도 그의 창조물들은 실재가 되어 있었고, 그는 그들을, 특히 산초를 자랑스러워하게 되었다. 그리하여 그는 매우 다른 조건 아래서 제2부를 시작했으며, 그 차이는 곧바로 스스로를 드러낸다. 번역으로도 그 문체가 훨씬 수월하고, 더 유려하고, 더 자연스러우며, 자기 자신과 자기 독자를 확신하는 사람의 문체와 더 비슷함을 알아볼 수 있을 것이다. 돈키호테와 산초도 변화를 겪는다. 제1부에서는\n\n제1부에서 돈키호테는 아무런 성격도 개성도 지니고 있지 않다. 그는 기사도 로맨스의 감정을 미친 모습으로 대변하는 자에 지나지 않는다. 그가 말하고 행하는 모든 것에서 그는 단지 자신의 책에서 배운 교훈을 되풀이하고 있을 뿐이다. 그러므로 감상적인 비평가들이 그의 고결함과 사심 없음과 두려움 없는 용기 따위를 늘어놓으며 그를 두고 넘쳐흐르는 어조로 떠들어 대는 것은 어리석은 일이다. 잘못을 바로잡고 상처를 치유하며 곤경에 처한 이를 돕는 것은 편력 기사의 본분이었으니, 그가 그 역할을 떠맡았을 때 이것을 자기 본분으로 삼는 것은 당연한 노릇이다. 편력 기사는 마땅히 담대해야 했으므로, 그도 두려움을 떨쳐 버려야 한다고 느끼는 것이다. 돈키호테에 관한 바이런의 그 모든 듣기 좋은 헛소리 가운데 가장 터무니없는 단언은 \"그를 미치게 한 것은 그의 미덕이다!\"라는 것이다. 진실은 그 정반대이니, 그를 덕스럽게 만든 것은 바로 그의 광기인 것이다.\n\n제2부에서 세르반테스는, 마치 거기에 오해가 있어서는 안 된다고 애써 강조하기라도 하듯 독자에게 거듭 일러두기를, 자기 주인공의 광기는 오로지 기사도라는 주제에 관한 망상에만 엄격히 국한되어 있으며 다른 모든 주제에 관해서는 그가 분별 있는 사람, 곧 사실상 판단력이 온전히 작동하는 사람이라고 한다. 이 점의 이로움은, 그가 돈키호테를 자신의 사색을 풀어놓는 대변자로 삼을 수 있다는 데 있다. 그리하여 그는 옆길로 새는 듯 보이지 않으면서도, 필요할 때면 비망록에서처럼 거리낌 없이 여담의 여유를 스스로에게 허락하는 것이다.\n\n돈키호테에게 부여된 개성의 양이 그리 크지 않다는 것은 사실이다. 그에게는 천성적인 성격의 손길이 몇 가지 있으니, 이를테면 성마름과 너그러움이 뒤섞인 점, 그리고 시종의 수다와 무례함에 대한 조바심과 더불어 산초에 대한 묘한 애정 같은 것이 그러하다. 그러나 대체로 그의 광기를 제쳐 두면, 그는 본능적인 좋은 취향과 다분한 명민함, 정신의 독창성을 지닌 사려 깊고 교양 있는 신사 그 이상은 별로 아니다.\n\n산초로 말하자면, 제1부 서문의 맺음말에서 분명히 드러나듯, 그는 대중의 총애를 받기도 전에 이미 그를 창조한 자의 사랑을 받고 있었다. 못한 재능을 지닌 자라면 그를 두 번째로 다룰 때, 그를 더 우스꽝스럽거나 영리하거나 상냥하거나 덕스럽게 만들어 개선하려 들었을 것이다. 그러나 세르반테스는 그런 식으로 자기 작품을 망치기에는 너무나 참된 예술가였다. 산초는 다시 등장할 때, 낯익은 옛 면모를 지닌 옛 산초 그대로이다. 다만 차이가 있으니, 그 면모가 한층 또렷이 드러났으되, 동시에 희화처럼 보일 만한 것은 무엇이든 조심스레 피했다. 윤곽은 채워 넣어야 할 곳에 채워졌고, 대가의 손이 몇 번 스쳐 생기를 얻어, 산초는 마치 벨라스케스의 인물 초상화 속에서처럼 우리 앞에 서 있다. 그는 제1부에서보다 제2부에서 훨씬 더 중요하고 두드러진 인물이며, 실로 둘시네아에 관한 그의 비할 데 없는 거짓말이 상당 부분 이야기의 줄거리를 채워 준다.\n\n이 점에서 그의 발전은 다른 어떤 면에서 못지않게 놀랍다. 제1부에서 그는 거짓말에 관한 대단한 천부적 재능을 드러낸다. 그의 거짓말은 소설 속 거짓말쟁이들이 흔히 즐기는 그런 풍부한 상상력의 종류가 아니다. 폴스타프의 것처럼 그의 거짓말은 그것을 낳은 아비를 닮았으니, 단순하고 소박하며 통통한 거짓말, 한마디로 평범한 일상의 거짓말이다. 그러나 돈키호테 같은 주인을 섬기면서 그는 빠르게 발전하는데, 시골 처녀 셋을 둘시네아와 그 시녀들로 둔갑시켜 떠넘기는 대목에서 우리는 이를 보게 된다. 이 일에서의 성공에 우쭐해진 그가 그 뒤 클라빌레뇨를 타고 떠난 여행을 이야기하면서 제 능력을 넘어서는 비상을 시도하도록 부추겨지는 점은 주목할 만하다.\n\n제2부에서 익살의 대상이 되는 것은 기사도 로맨스의 사건들이라기보다 오히려 그 정신이다. 둘시네아와 트리팔디, 몬테시노스 동굴의 마법에서 풍자된 그런 종류의 마법은 후기의 못한 로맨스들에서 주된 역할을 하며, 또 하나의 두드러진 특징은 돈키호테가 둘시네아를 맹목적으로 숭배하는 데서 희화화된다. 기사도 로맨스에서 사랑은 한낱 동물적 욕정이거나 환상적인 우상 숭배이다. 거친 마음을 지닌 자라면 전자를 두고 즐거워하려 들겠지만, 세르반테스 같은 해학을 지닌 이에게는 후자가 자연히 조롱의 매력적인 대상이었다. 이 로맨스들의 다른 모든 것과 마찬가지로, 그것은 진짜 기사도 감정을 거칠게 과장한 것이나, 그 독특한 터무니없음은 아마도 과장의 대가인 프로방스 시인들의 영향에서 비롯된 듯하다. 한 음유시인이 모든 일에서 자기 귀부인에게 복종하겠노라 공언하면, 다음 차례의 시인은 밋밋하고 진부하다는 비난을 피하려면 자신을 그녀의 뜻에 매인 노예라 선언해야만 했고, 그다음 시인은 또 한층 더 강한 선언으로 그것을 능가해야만 했다. 그리하여 헌신의 표현들은 경매장의 호가처럼 서로 위로 치솟아 올라, 사랑에 관한 관습적인 언어와 이론, 곧 풍류의 어법이 생겨나, 마침내 남유럽의 문학에 스며들어 한쪽으로는 베아트리체와 라우라에 대한 초월적 숭배로, 다른 한쪽으로는 펠리시아노 데 실바 같은 작가들이 대변한 기괴한 우상 숭배로 열매를 맺었다. 이것이 바로 세르반테스가 돈키호테의 둘시네아를 향한 열정에서 다루는 것이며, 그는 어느 경우에도 이보다 더 멋들어지게 익살을 펼친 적이 없다. 둘시네아를 배경에 묻어 두고 그녀를 그 존재 자체마저 의심스러운 어렴풋한 그림자 같은 존재로 만듦으로써, 그는 돈키호테가 그녀의 미덕과 매력을 숭배하는 데 한층 더한 터무니없음을 입히고, 로맨스의 감정과 언어에 대한 희화에 더욱 날을 세운다.\n\n「돈키호테」의 큰 미덕 가운데 하나이자, 모든 계층의 독자에게 받아들여지게 하여 가장 세계적인 책으로 만든 자질 가운데 하나는 바로 그 단순함이다. 물론 17세기 스페인 청중에게는 충분히 자명했으나 오늘날의 독자에게는 곧바로 와닿지 않는 점들이 있으며, 세르반테스는 흔히 소수에게만 이해될 암시를 두루 이해되리라 여기고 넘어가곤 한다. 예컨대 스페인 안의 많은 독자, 그리고 스페인 밖의 대다수 독자에게는 그가 주인공의 고장으로 어떤 지방을 택했는지의 의미가 완전히 사라져 버린다. 라만차를 보지 않고서는 「돈키호테」를 온전히 이해할 수 없다고 말하면 지나치겠으나, 라만차를 한번 흘긋 보기만 해도 어떤 주석가도 줄 수 없는 세르반테스의 의미에 관한 통찰을 얻게 되리라는 것만은 의심할 바 없다. 스페인의 모든 지방 가운데 라만차야말로 로맨스의 관념을 떠올리게 할 마지막 곳이다. 반도의 그 단조롭기 그지없는 중앙 고원 전체에서도 가장 단조로운 지역이다. 에스트레마두라의 음산한 적막에는 무언가 인상적인 데가 있고, 레온과 옛 카스티야의 평원이 헐벗고 황량하다 해도 거기에는 역사로 이름난, 과거의 유적이 풍성한 옛 도시들이 점점이 박혀 있다. 그러나 라만차의 풍경에는 그 어떤 위안이 될 만한 특징도 없으니, 사막의 한결같음을 다 지녔으되 그 위엄은 없다. 그 단조로움을 깨뜨리는 몇 안 되는 도시와 마을은 초라하고 평범하여, 거기에는 유서 깊은 구석이라곤 없고 가난의 그림 같은 정취조차 없다. 실로 돈키호테 자신의 마을 아르가마시야는 그 거리와 집들의 깐깐한 규칙성에서 일종의 답답한 고상함을 풍긴다. 모든 것이 천박하고, 풍차마저도 풍차 종류 가운데 가장 추하고 가장 남루한 것들이다. 이 고장을 잘 아는 사람이라면 누구든 「라만차의 돈키호테」라는 문체와 제목만으로도 단번에 작가의 의도를 푸는 열쇠를 얻었다. 기사의 고장이자 그의 기사도 행각의 무대인 라만차는, 마분지 투구, 시종 노릇을 하는 당나귀 탄 농사꾼, 비열한 객줏집 주인이 내려 주는 기사 작위, 압제의 희생자로 오인된 죄수들, 그리고 돈키호테의 세계와 그가 살던 세계 사이의, 그가 본 그대로의 사물과 실제 사물 사이의 그 밖의 온갖 부조화와 한 묶음을 이룬다.\n\n이 책의 해학과 목적 전체에 깔려 있는 이 부조화의 요소가, 「돈키호테」를 해석하겠다고 나선 이들 대다수에게 그토록 거의 주목받지 못했다는 것은 이상한 일이다. 예컨대 삽화가들은 그것을 완전히 간과해 버렸다. 사실 「돈키호테」에 삽화를 그린 화가들의 절대다수는 스페인에 관해 아무것도 알지 못했다. 그들에게 객줏집이란 한낱 길가 여인숙이라는 추상적 관념밖에 전해 주지 못했으니, 그들은 그것을 성으로 오인하는 돈키호테의 착각이 지닌 해학을 온전히 살려 낼 수도 없었고, 그 객줏집의 모든 실상이 그의 이상에서 얼마나 동떨어졌는지 알아챌 수도 없었다. 그러나 더 잘 알고 있을 때조차 그들은 그 어긋남의 온전한 힘을 헤아리지 못하는 듯하다. 예컨대 돈키호테가 여인숙 마당에서 자기 갑옷을 지켜보는 귀스타브 도레의 그림을 보라. 세비야 가도에 있는 케사다 객줏집이 전승이 주장하는 대로 「돈키호테」에 묘사된 그 여인숙인지 아닌지는 차치하더라도, 세르반테스가 마음의 눈으로 그린 것이 바로 그 뒤편에 있는 것과 똑같은 여인숙 마당이었음은, 그리고 그가 돈키호테에게 갑옷을 내려놓게 하려 한 곳이 모퉁이의 원시적인 두레우물 곁에 놓인 것과 똑같은 투박한 돌구유였음은 추호도 의심할 바 없다. 귀스타브 도레는 그것을, 스페인의 어떤 객줏집 마당에서도 노새몰이꾼이 자기 노새에게 물을 먹인 적 없을 법한 정교한 분수로 만들어 버려, 세르반테스가 겨눈 핵심을 완전히 놓치고 만다. 돈키호테의 밤샘과 그 뒤에 이어지는 의식에 의미를 부여하는 것은, 바로 그 모든 주변 환경과 정황의 초라하고 산문적이며 평범한 성격인 것이다.\n\n세르반테스의 해학은 대부분 부조화의 인식에 그 힘이 있는, 그 폭넓고 단순한 종류의 것이다. 산초를 통틀어 소설의 전 영역에서 가장 해학적인 창조물로 만드는 것은, 그 성격의 놀라운 생명력과 자연에 대한 충실함 못지않게, 그의 모든 행동과 말과 행위가 주인의 관념 및 목표와 빚어내는 부조화이다. 세르반테스가 최초의 위대한 대가였던 그 미소 없는 진중함, 곧 후대의 해학가 가운데 어쩌면 스위프트에게만 자연스럽게 어울리는 \"세르반테스의 진지한 분위기\"는 이런 종류의 해학에 필수적인 것인데, 여기서도 세르반테스는 해석자들의 손에 시달려 왔다. 필립스의 거친 익살을 제외한다면, 세르반테스를 대변하려는 시도에서 예컨대 모토의 번역에서 보이는 것 같은 경박하고 우스개를 부리려는 문체, 혹은 프랑스 번역자들이 이따금 취하는 발랄하고 까부는 분위기보다 더 어울리지 않는 것은 없을 것이다. 세르반테스의 해학에 그 독특한 풍미를 주는 것은, 그 서술의 진중하고 사실적인 태도와, 자신이 우스운 것을, 가장 평범한 것 외에는 그 무엇을 말하고 있음을 의식하지 못하는 듯한 작가의 태도이다. 실로 그의 해학은 스턴과 자의식 강한 해학가들의 해학과 정반대이다. 토비 삼촌이 한창 신명에 차 있을 때조차, 당신은 그 뒤에서 어깨 너머로 당신을 지켜보며 자신이 어떤 효과를 내고 있는지 살피는 \"스턴이라는 사내\"의 존재를 늘 의식하게 된다. 세르반테스는 언제나 당신을 돈키호테와 산초와 단둘이 남겨 둔다. 그와 스위프트와 위대한 해학가들은 언제나 자신을 시야 밖에 두거나, 더 정확히 말하면 자기 자신에 관해서는 도무지 생각하지 않으니, 옛 마구(馬具) 익살의 방식을 되살린 듯, 무지나 우둔함이나 저속한 취향을 기괴하게 꾸며 보이며 웃음을 자아내려 드는 우리 근래의 해학가 무리와는 다르다.\n\n스페인의 해학을 다른 어떤 언어로든 온전히 살려 내는 것이 거의 불가능에 가깝다는 것은 사실이다. 스페인어에는 아무리 구어적이라 해도 천성적인 진중함과 낭랑한 위엄이 깃들어 있어, 그것이 어리석은 말을 갑절로 어리석게 만들고 가장 터무니없는 단언에도 그럴듯함을 부여한다. 바로 이것이 산초 판사의 익살을 양심적인 번역자에게 절망거리로 만드는 것이다. 산초의 퉁명스러운 논평은 결코 김빠지는 법이 없으나, 그 본고장의 카스티야어에서 다른 어떤 매체로 옮겨지면 그 풍미의 절반을 잃고 만다. 그러나 외국인들이 세르반테스의 해학을 제대로 살려 내지 못했다 해도, 그들이 그의 동포보다 더 못한 것은 아니다. 실로 스페인 농부가 「돈키호테」를 음미하지 않았더라면, 그 위대한 해학가가 자기 나라에서는 해학가로 여겨지지조차 않았다고 생각하고 싶어질 정도이다.\n\n돈키호테의 광기는 어떤 경우에는 그의 비평가들에게까지 옮아간 듯하여, 그들로 하여금 책에 없는 것을 보게 하고, 오로지 그들 자신의 상상 속에만 존재하는 허깨비를 향해 전속력으로 돌진하게 만든다. 요즘의 적잖은 비평가들처럼, 그들은 비명이 비평이 아니라는 것을, 그리고 줄줄이 늘어선 최상급과 세 겹으로 쌓아 올린 과장과 거창한 수식어에 흔들리는 것은 오로지 천박한 취향뿐이라는 것을 잊고 있다. 그러나 특히 이상하게 다가오는 점은, 그들이 과장된 찬사를 늘어놓고 온갖 상상 속의 관념과 자질을 세르반테스에게 돌리면서도, 그의 독자 백 명 가운데 아흔아홉이 그에게서 가장 높이 평가하고 그를 모든 경쟁자 위에 올려놓는 바로 그 자질로 여길 그 점에 대해서는 아무런 인식도 보이지 않는다는 것이다.\n\n「돈키호테」를 한낱 해학적인 책인 양 말하는 것은 명백한 그릇된 규정이 되리라. 세르반테스는 때때로 그것을 이따금의 수필과 비평, 혹은 길고 파란만장한 생애에서 얻은 관찰과 성찰과 응축된 지혜를 담는 일종의 비망록으로 삼는다. 그것은 인류와 인간 본성에 관한 명민한 관찰의 보고이다. 근래의 소설들 가운데 여기저기 더 정교한 성격 연구가 있을지 몰라도, 개성을 부여받은 인물에서 이 책보다 풍부한 책은 없다. 콜리지가 셰익스피어를 두고 지극히 사소한 것에 이르기까지 한 말은 세르반테스에게도 들어맞으니, 그는 결코, 아무리 일시적인 목적을 위해서라도 허수아비 인물을 내세우는 법이 없다. 그의 모든 인물에는, 그들이 아무리 할 일이 적고 아무리 짧은 시간 독자 앞에 머문다 해도, 생명과 개성이 깃들어 있다. 산손 카라스코, 신부, 테레사 판사, 알티시도라, 심지어 몬테시노스 동굴로 가는 길에서 만난 두 학생까지, 모두가 살아 움직이며 제 존재를 지닌다. 그리고 그 모두 가운데 미워할 자가 하나도 없다는 것이 세르반테스의 폭넓은 인간애의 특징이다. 개탄스러운 행실의 가엾은 마리토르네스조차 제 나름의 어진 마음을 지니고 있고 \"어딘가 희미하고 아득하게 그리스도교도를 닮은 데\"가 있다. 그리고 산초로 말하자면, 비록 해부해 보면 주인에 대한 일종의 개 같은 애정 말고는 사랑스러운 면모를 찾을 수 없다 해도, 마음속으로 그를 사랑하지 않을 이가 어디 있겠는가?\n그러나 결국 「돈키호테」를 다른 모든 로맨스류 책과 구별 짓는 것은 바로 그 해학이다. 근래의 가장 공정한 판단력을 지닌 비평가 가운데 한 사람이 일컬은 대로 그것을 \"비할 데 없이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 소설\"로 만드는 것이 바로 이것이다. 그것을 독자가 있는 모든 나라에서 토착화시키고, 문학을 지닌 모든 언어에서 고전으로 만든 것은, 거친 익살극에서 셰익스피어나 몰리에르의 것만큼이나 미묘한 희극에 이르기까지 펼쳐지는 그 다채로운 해학이다. 추천하는 시 몇 편 미지의 우르간다 라만차의 돈키호테의 책에게 만일 선한 이들에게 환영받기를, 오 책이여! 그대 한결같이 목표로 삼는다면, 빈 수다쟁이 그 누구도 감히 그대의 자격을 묻거나 다투지 못하리라. 허나 행여 그대가 마음에 품기를 바보들의 인정을 얻고자 한다면, 헛된 수고만이 그대의 보답이 되리니, 겉으로는 칭찬하는 척하리라. 좋은 나무 아래 깃드는 자가 좋은 그늘을 찾는다 사람들은 말하네. 그러한 자에게 그대의 자애로운 별이 베하르에 그늘을 마련해 주었구나. 가지를 활짝 펼쳐 군왕다운 열매를 내보이는 한 그루 왕가의 나무여, 당대의 알렉산드로스인 고귀한 공작을 품은 한 그루 나무로구나.\n\n라만차의 한 신사가 어찌하여 그 정신을 잃었는지, 사랑과 영광에 관한 부질없는 이야기들에 빠져 그 이야기를 들려줌이 그대의 뜻이로다. \"귀부인과 무기와 기사들\"에 관한— 새로운 광란의 오를란도여— 차라리 사랑에 빠진 오를란도라 하리, 그가 엘 토보소의 둘시네아를 얻었으니. 그대의 방패에 헛된 문장(紋章)을 새기지 말라. 온갖 형상이란—허세 부리는 장난일 뿐. 겸손한 헌사를 지어 올려, 조롱꾼에게 이렇게 말할 틈을 주지 말라. \"뭐라! 여기 알바로 데 루나인가? 아니면 다시 한니발인가?\n\n아니면 마드리드의 프랑수아 왕이\n\n또다시 제 운명을 한탄함인가?\" 하늘이 그대에게 깊은 학식을 내려 주기를 즐거워하지 않으셨고, 흑인 라티노의 언어 재능도 주지 않으셨으니, 그대의 책장에 라틴어를 내보이지 말라. 철학이나 기지를 흉내 내지 말라, 이해하지 못하는 자가 그대를 향해\n\n찡그린 얼굴로 이렇게 묻지 않도록, \"어찌 내게 꽃을 권하는가, 벗이여?\" 참견하는 자가 되지 말라. 이웃이 어떤 삶을 사는지는 그대의 일이 아니니. 신중하라. 함부로 던진 농담이 흔히 농담한 자의 머리 위로 되돌아오는 법이라. 그대의 한결같은 수고를 다하여\n\n정직한 이름을 스스로 얻도록 하라, 활자로 박혀 보존된 어리석은 짓이란 영원토록 이어지는 수치이니라. 또 하나 충고를 마음에 새기라. 그대의 지붕이 유리로 되어 있다면, 지나가는 사람들에게 던지려고 돌을 주워 드는 것은 지각없는 짓이니라.\n\n지혜로운 이들의 관심을 얻고, 사색하는 이에게 생각할 거리를 주라. 경박한 것을 써 내려가는 자는 한낱 어리석은 자들에게나 찾아지리라. 골의 아마디스 라만차의 돈키호테에게 소네트 내가 외로운 슬픔에 잠겨 저 거대한 페냐 포브레 바위 위에 풀죽어 앉아,\n\n스스로 부과한 고행 속에서 야위어 가던 그 나의 삶을 그대가 본받았으니, 그대, 유일한 음료가 제 눈물의 쓴 소금물이었고, 은이나 구리나 주석 접시도 없이 비천한 처지로 맨땅 위에서 땅의 열매를 먹던 그대여, 그대의 영원한 영광을 확신하며 살라.\n\n저 넷째 천구의 궤도 위에서 밝은 아폴로가 그 준마들을 모는 한, 그대의 명성 속에 그대는 안전히 남으리니, 그대 고장의 이름은 이야기 속에 길이 남고, 그대의 현명한 작가는 견줄 이 없이 우뚝 서리라. 그리스의 돈 벨리아니스 라만차의 돈키호테에게 소네트\n\n베고 찍고 가르는 일에, 말과 행동에, 나는 기사도의 으뜸가는 기사였으니, 세상이 본 그 어느 누구 못지않게 굳세고 담대하며 능란했노라. 수천의 사람을 압제자의 횡포에서 풀어 주었고, 위업은 컸으며 영원한 명성이 그 보답이었네. 사랑에서 나는 진실과 충성을 증명했고,\n\n가장 거대한 거인도 내게는 난쟁이였으며,\n\n언제나 기사도의 법도에 잘 귀 기울였노라. 변덕스러운 여신도 내 솜씨를 인정했고, 운명조차도 다스림에 굴복하여, 앞머리를 붙들린 채 내 뜻에 따랐네. 허나—저 뿔 돋은 달 위에 옥좌를 둔 듯 내 운수가 앉아 있는 듯 보이건만—위대한 키호테여, 그래도 그대의 위업에 대한 시기심이 내 영혼을 채우는구나. 오리아나 부인 엘 토보소의 둘시네아에게 소네트 오, 가장 아리따운 둘시네아여, 그럴 수만 있다면! 그리 상상함은 즐거운 공상이리니— 미라플로레스가 엘 토보소로 바뀔 수 있다면, 런던의 도성이 그대를 품은 그곳으로 바뀔 수 있다면! 오, 그대의 마음과 몸이 그토록 드러내는\n\n헤아릴 수 없는 매력의 그 제복을 내가 얻을 수 있다면!\n\n아니면 이제 이름 떨친 그를—그대가 그리 키웠으니— 그대의 기사를 어느 무시무시한 결투에서 내가 볼 수 있다면! 오, 그대가 점잖은 키호테를 물리치게 했던 그 수줍은 정절을 발휘하여, 내가 아마디스에게서 풀려날 수 있다면! 그러면 나의 무거운 슬픔이 기쁨으로 바뀌리니, 나는 아무도 부러워하지 않고, 모두가 나를 부러워하며, 티 없는 행복이 내 것이 되리라. 골의 아마디스의 시종 간달린이 돈키호테의 시종 산초 판사에게 소네트 만세, 이름 높은 사내여! 운명의 여신이, 그대를 시종 일에 도제로 묶어 두었을 때, 그대에 대한 보살핌과 자애를 드러내어, 불운에서 벗어나도록 그대의 길을 빚어 놓았구나.\n\n이제는 더 이상 오만한 편력 기사도가\n\n낫과 삽을 멸시의 눈으로 보지 않으니, 드높은 거만함이 소박한 시종다운 순박함보다 덜 셈해지는 법이라. 나는 그대의 잿빛 당나귀와 그대의 이름을 부러워하노라, 그리고 그대가 즐겨 가득 채우던 그 안장주머니를, 그대의 선견지명을 알려 주는 안락한 것들로 말일세. 뛰어난 산초여! 그대에게 다시 만세를 외치노라! 오직 그대에게만 우리 스페인의 오비디우스가 촌스러운 입맞춤과 한 대 쥐어박기로 경의를 표하노라. 얼룩덜룩한 시인 엘 도노소가 산초 판사와 로시난테에 대하여 산초에 대하여 나는 시종 산초 판—, 라 만—의 돈키호테를 섬겼노라.\n\n허나 그를 섬기는 일에서 물러나—, 남은 생을 신중히 보내기로 결심했네—. 시(枾)라 불린 비야디에—가\n\n오직 물러남 속에서만— 잘 사는 비결이 있다고 주장했음이라, 「라 셀레스티—」에 따르면—. 그것은 신성한 책이로되, 다만 죄가 되는— 내 생각에는 너무 노골적인 말투만 빼면—. 로시난테에 대하여 나는 저 유명한 로시난—, 위대한 바비에—의 증손, 오로지 야위고 뼈만 앙상한 탓에—, 돈키호테라는 자를 주인으로 두었노라—. 허나 내가 허약함에서 그와 잘 어울렸다 해도—, 나는 결코 적은 먹이를 고분고분 받지 않았으니—, 훔쳐서라도 제 곡식을 챙겼노라—, 라사리—에게서 배운 수법이니—, 그가 짚 한 오라기를 그토록 깔끔히 써서— 눈먼 이의 포도주를 속여 먹었을 때 말일세—.\n\n광란의 오를란도\n\n# ON SANCHO\n\n라만차의 돈키호테에게 소네트 그대가 동료 기사가 아니라면, 그대에겐 견줄 이 없으리니, 천(千)의 동료 가운데서도 그대는 으뜸가는 동료라, 그대가 곁에 있을 때 다른 이가 들어설 자리 없으니, 패한 적 없는 승리자, 정복당하지 않은 위대한 이여! 나는 안젤리카에게 무너진 오를란도라, 먼 바다를 건너도록 단죄받았고,\n\n# ON ROCINANTE\n\n망각에게 존중받는 용맹을 명성의 제단에 제물로 바치러 가는 신세로다. 나는 그대의 경쟁자가 될 수 없으니, 그대의 명성과 무용이 모든 경쟁 위로 솟아 있음이라, 비록 우리 둘 다 정신을 잃은 채 떠도는 신세이나. 허나 스키타이인이나 무어인을 길들이는 것이 그대의 운명은 아니었어도, 그래도 그대는 나와 견주리니, 사랑이 우리를 비탄의 동지로 묶어 놓는구나.\n\n포이보스의 기사\n\n라만차의 돈키호테에게 내 칼은 그대의 것에 견줄 바가 아니었으니, 스페인의 포이보스여, 예절의 경이로움이여, 번개처럼 동에서 서로 내리치던 이 나의 손도 그대의 이름난 팔에는 견줄 바가 아니었네. 나는 모든 제국을 멸시했고, 장밋빛 동방이 내밀던 그 군주의 자리도 단념했노라, 클라리디아나의 눈길 한 번을 위하여, 내가 그 사랑에 애태우는 그 밝은 새벽의 여신을 위하여. 내 사랑은 한결같음의 기적이라, 그녀의 무자비한 잔혹함에 추방당하니, 이 팔은 지옥의 분노조차 길들일 힘을 지녔노라. 허나, 고트족의 키호테여, 그대가 더 행복함을 증명하나니,\n\n그대는 둘시네아의 이름 속에 살아 있고,\n\n이름 높고 존경받고 현명한 그녀가 그대 안에 살아 있음이라. 솔리스단으로부터 라만차의 돈키호테에게 소네트 키호테 경이여, 그대의 망상들이 그대의 미친 머리를 온통 뒤집어 놓았음은 사실이오, 허나 비천하거나 옹졸한 그 무엇도 아직껏 그 누구도 그대를 비난하여 들씌운 적이 없소이다. 그대의 행적은 만인의 눈앞에 펼쳐진 명백한 증거이니, 그대는 불의를 없애려 길을 나섰고, 그 수고의 대가로 비열하고 무뢰한 무리에게서 호된 몽둥이찜질을 숱하게 받았소이다. 그대 마음의 여왕인 아리따운 둘시네아가 그 잔혹함에 누그러지지 않는다 해도,\n\n그대의 비탄이 여전히 그녀를 움직일 힘이 없다 해도,\n\n그토록 딱한 처지에서 이것을 그대의 위안으로 삼으시오. 산초는 시원찮은 중매쟁이였고, 그는 멍청이요 그녀는 모진 여인이며 그대는 연인답지 못했음을. 대화 바비에카와 로시난테 사이의 소네트 바: \"어찌 된 일인가, 로시난테, 그대 이리 야위었으니?\" 로: \"먹이는 부족하고, 일에 시달려 닳아빠졌소.\" 바: \"허나 그 많은 건초와 곡식은 다 어찌 되었누?\" 로: \"주인이 내게 하나도 주지 않으니, 너무 인색하오.\" 바: \"이보시오, 그대 본데없는 짓을 보이는구려, 당나귀처럼 제 주인을 그리 멸시하다니.\" 로: \"그는 당나귀요, 당나귀로 죽을 것이며, 당나귀로 태어났소.\n\n뭐냐 하면, 그가 사랑에 빠졌으니, 이보다 더 뻔한 게 어디 있겠소?\"\n\n바: \"사랑에 빠짐이 어리석은 짓이오?\"—로: \"그리 대단한 분별은 아니지요.\" 바: \"그대 형이상학을 논하는구려.\"—로: \"먹을 게 없어서라오.\" 바: \"그럼 그 시종을 욕하시오.\"—로: \"원, 그래 봐야 무슨 소용이오? 그를 두고 불평할 수야 있겠소만, 인정하리다, 허나 시종이든 주인이든, 무슨 차이가 있겠소? 둘 다 로시난테만큼이나 시원찮은 말이거늘.\" 작가의 서문\n\n# THE AUTHOR’S PREFACE\n\n한가한 독자여, 이 책이 내 머릿속의 자식인 만큼, 상상할 수 있는 가장 아름답고 가장 유쾌하며 가장 영리한 책이기를 내가 바랐으리라는 것은 굳이 맹세하지 않아도 그대가 믿어 주리라. 그러나 모든 것은 제 닮은 것을 낳는다는 자연의 법칙을 나로서는 거스를 수 없었으니, 그렇다면 이 메마르고 변변히 갈지 못한 내 재능이 낳을 수 있는 것이라곤, 온갖 종류의 생각으로 가득 찬, 그 어떤 다른 상상에도 결코 떠오른 적 없는, 메마르고 쪼그라들고 별난 자식의 이야기 말고 무엇이 있겠는가—온갖 비참함이 깃들고 온갖 구슬픈 소리가 거처를 삼는 감옥에서나 잉태될 법한 바로 그런 것 말일세. 평온함, 즐거운 안식처, 쾌적한 들판, 밝은 하늘, 졸졸 흐르는 시내, 마음의 평화, 이런 것들이야말로 가장 척박한 뮤즈마저 비옥하게 만들고, 세상을 경이와 기쁨으로 채우는 자식들을 세상에 내놓게 하는 것이라네. 때때로 아비가 못생기고 미련한 아들을 두면, 그를 향한 사랑이 그 눈을 가려 결점이 보이지 않게 하거나, 오히려 그것을 정신과 육체의 재능이며 매력으로 여겨, 벗들에게 그것을 기지요 우아함이라 떠벌리기도 하는 법일세. 그러나 나는—비록 아비로 통하지만 「돈키호테」의 의붓아비에 지나지 않으므로—관습의 흐름을 따를 마음도, 다른 이들이 그러하듯 거의 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며, 이 내 자식에게서 그대가 보게 될 결점을 용서하거나 너그러이 봐 달라고 그대에게, 더없이 소중한 독자여, 애원할 마음도 없네. 그대는 이 책의 친척도 벗도 아니요, 그대의 영혼은 그대 자신의 것이며 그대의 의지는 그 누구든 어떤 사람 못지않게 자유로우니, 그대는 그대 자신의 집 안에 있어 마치 임금이 제 세금의 주인이듯 그 집의 주인이며, \"내 망토 아래에서는 임금도 죽일 수 있다\"는 흔한 속담도 알고 있으렷다. 이 모든 것이 그대를 온갖 고려와 의무에서 면제해 주고 풀어 주니, 그대는 이 이야기를 두고 무엇이든 그대 좋을 대로 말할 수 있으며, 험담을 한대서 욕먹을 두려움도, 좋은 말을 한대서 상을 받을 기대도 없으렷다.\n\n내 바람이라면 그저 이 책을, 서문의 그 어떤 꾸밈도 없이, 책의 첫머리에 흔히 놓이는 관습적인 소네트며 경구며 찬사를 헤아릴 수 없이 늘어세우는 일도 없이, 그대에게 꾸밈없이 그대로 내놓는 것이라네. 사실 이 책을 짓는 데 어느 정도 수고가 들기는 했으나, 지금 그대가 읽고 있는 이 서문을 짓는 것보다 더 큰 수고는 없었음을 그대에게 일러둘 수 있네. 이것을 쓰려고 여러 번 붓을 들었다가, 무엇을 써야 할지 몰라 여러 번 다시 내려놓았다네. 그러던 어느 날, 종이를 앞에 놓고 펜을 귀에 꽂고 팔꿈치를 책상에 괴고 뺨을 손에 받친 채, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 골똘히 생각하고 있노라니, 뜻밖에 활달하고 영리한 어느 벗이 들어왔는데, 내가 그토록 깊은 생각에 잠긴 것을 보고 그 까닭을 물었네. 그래서 나는 굳이 감추지 않고, 「돈키호테」 이야기에 붙여야 할 서문을 생각하고 있었는데, 그것이 어찌나 골치 아픈지 아예 서문 같은 건 짓지 않을 마음이요, 그토록 고귀한 기사의 위업을 세상에 내놓지 않을 마음마저 들었노라고 대답했네.\n\n\"이보게, 사람들이 대중이라 부르는 저 오래된 입법자가, 내가 그토록 여러 해 동안 망각의 침묵 속에 잠들어 있다가, 이제 와서 등에 온 세월을 짊어진 채, 골풀처럼 메마르고 창의라곤 없으며 문체는 빈약하고 사상은 보잘것없으며 학식과 지혜라곤 도무지 찾아볼 수 없고, 여백에 인용도 없고 끝에 주석도 없는 책을 들고 나오는 꼴을 본다면 무어라 할지, 내가 어찌 마음 졸이지 않겠는가? 내가 보는 다른 책들은 죄다 꾸며 낸 이야기와 속된 것들뿐이면서도, 아리스토텔레스며 플라톤이며 철학자 무리 전체의 격언으로 가득 차 있어, 독자들을 경탄케 하고 그 저자들이 학식과 박식과 웅변을 갖춘 사람들이라 확신케 하는데 말일세. 게다가 그들이 성경을 인용하기라도 하면!—누구든 그들을 성 토마스나 그 밖의 교회 박사들이라 하겠으니, 어찌나 교묘하게 격식을 차리는지, 한 문장에서는 실연한 연인을 그려 내고 다음 문장에서는 듣고 읽기에 즐겁고 흐뭇한 경건한 작은 설교를 늘어놓는다네. 이 모든 것이 내 책에는 하나도 없을 터이니, 나는 여백에 인용할 것도 끝에 주석으로 달 것도 없거니와, 모두가 그러하듯 첫머리에 A, B, C 차례로 아리스토텔레스에서 시작하여 크세노폰이나 조일루스나 제욱시스로 끝맺도록 늘어놓을, 내가 따른 작가가 누구인지조차 더더욱 알지 못하니 말일세. 한쪽은 비방가요 다른 한쪽은 화가였는데도 말이야. 또한 내 책은 첫머리의 소네트 없이 견뎌야 하니, 적어도 공작이나 후작이나 백작이나 주교나 귀부인이나 이름난 시인을 지은이로 둔 소네트는 없을 걸세. 하기야 친절한 벗 두엇이나 서넛에게 부탁한다면, 그들이 내게 그런 것들을 주리라는 걸 나도 아네, 그것도 우리 스페인에서 가장 드높은 명성을 지닌 이들의 작품도 따르지 못할 만한 것들로 말일세.\n\n\"한마디로 말하자면, 이보게,\" 나는 말을 이었네. \"내가 결심한 바는, 돈키호테 나리께서 그분께 필요한 그 모든 것으로 치장해 줄 누군가를 하늘이 마련해 주실 때까지, 그저 그분 자신의 라만차의 문서고 속에 묻혀 계시도록 하겠다는 것일세. 나는 내 천박함과 학식의 부족으로 그것들을 갖추어 드리기에 미치지 못함을 깨닫고 있고, 또 천성이 수줍고 게을러서 나 스스로 그들 없이도 할 수 있는 말을 하려고 작가들을 뒤지고 다니는 일에 무심하기 때문일세. 그래서 자네가 본 그 골똘함과 망연함에 내가 빠져 있었던 게야, 자네가 내게서 들은 것만으로도 그럴 만한 충분한 까닭이 되지 않겠나.\"\n\n이 말을 듣더니 내 벗은 제 이마를 탁 치며 호탕하게 웃음을 터뜨리고는 이렇게 외쳤네. \"맙소사, 이 사람아, 자네를 알고 지낸 그 오랜 세월 내내 내가 빠져 있던 한 가지 오해에서 이제야 벗어났구먼. 그동안 내내 나는 자네가 하는 모든 일에서 자네를 명민하고 분별 있는 사람으로 여겨 왔는데, 이제 보니 자네가 그것과는 하늘이 땅에서 먼 만큼이나 멀구먼. 그토록 하찮고 바로잡기도 쉬운 일이, 훨씬 더 큰 장애물도 뚫고 부술 만한 자네 같은 무르익은 재능을 사로잡고 쩔쩔매게 한다니, 이게 될 법한 일인가? 정말이지, 이건 능력이 모자라서가 아니라, 게으름이 지나치고 세상 물정에 너무 어두운 탓일세. 내가 진실을 말하고 있는지 알고 싶은가? 그렇다면 내 말을 들어 보게나, 그러면 눈 한 번 떴다 감을 사이에 내가 어떻게 자네의 온갖 어려움을 쓸어 없애고, 자네의 이름난 돈키호테, 곧 모든 편력 기사도의 빛이요 거울인 그 이야기를 세상에 내놓는 것을 가로막고 주저하게 만든다는 그 모든 부족함을 채워 주는지 보게 될 걸세.\" \"어디 말해 보게,\" 나는 그의 말에 귀를 기울이며 말했네. \"어떻게 내 망설임을 메우고, 내가 빠져 있는 이 혼란의 아수라장을 정돈하겠다는 건가?\"\n\n이에 그가 대답하기를, \"첫째로 자네가 첫머리에 두고 싶어 하는, 마땅히 신분 높고 지체 있는 이들이 지어야 할 그 소네트며 경구며 찬사의 시들에 관한 어려움은, 자네가 직접 약간의 수고를 들여 그것들을 짓는다면 없어질 걸세. 그런 다음 거기에 세례를 주듯 자네 좋을 대로 아무 이름이나 붙이고, 인도의 사제왕 요한이나 트레비손드의 황제를 그 지은이로 내세우면 되네. 내가 알기로 그들은 이름난 시인이었다고들 했으니 말일세. 설령 그들이 시인이 아니었다 하더라도, 그래서 어떤 현학자나 학사들이 자네를 공격하며 그 사실에 시비를 걸더라도, 그런 건 마라베디 두 푼어치도 신경 쓰지 말게. 그들이 자네에게 거짓이라는 걸 입증한다 해도, 자네가 그것을 쓴 손을 잘라 낼 수는 없을 테니 말일세.\n* 마라베디: 옛 스페인의 동전으로, 매우 적은 액수를 가리킬 때 쓰였다.\n\n\"자네가 이야기 속에 집어넣는 경구와 명언을 가져온 책과 작가를 여백에 표시하는 일로 말하자면, 그저 자네가 어쩌다 외고 있거나 적어도 찾아보는 데 그리 수고롭지 않을 라틴어 문구나 토막들을 알맞게 끼워 넣도록 궁리하기만 하면 되네. 그러니 이를테면 자유와 구속을 이야기할 때면 이렇게 넣는 거지. 자유는 온 세상의 황금을 다 주고도 잘 팔리지 않는 법.\n\n그러고는 여백에 호라티우스, 혹은 그 말을 한 자가 누구든 그 이름을 표시하면 되네. 혹은 죽음의 위력을 암시하려거든 이렇게 들이대는 거야—\n\n창백한 죽음은 공평한 발걸음으로 가난한 자의 오두막과\n임금의 탑을 두드리노라.\n\n\"만일 그것이 우정과, 신께서 우리에게 명하신, 원수를 사랑하라는 그 사랑에 관한 것이라면, 곧장 성경으로 가게나. 아주 약간만 찾아보면 될 일이고, 다름 아닌 하느님 자신의 말씀을 인용할 수 있으니 말일세. '그러나 나는 너희에게 이르노니, 너희 원수를 사랑하라.' 만일 자네가 악한 생각을 이야기한다면, 복음서로 가게나. '마음에서 악한 생각들이 나오느니라.' 벗들의 변덕스러움을 이야기한다면, 카토가 있어 자네에게 그의 대구(對句)를 줄 걸세.\n\n그대가 행복할 동안에는 숱한 벗을 헤아리려니와,\n\n세월이 흐려지면 그대 홀로 남으리라. \"이런 라틴어 토막들로 그들은 어쨌든 자네를 문법가로 여길 터이니, 요즘 세상에 그것은 적잖은 명예요 이득이라네.\n\n\"책 끝에 주석을 다는 일로 말하자면, 자네는 이런 식으로 안심하고 해도 좋네. 자네 책에 거인을 하나라도 언급하거든, 그것이 골리앗이라는 거인이 되도록 꾸미게. 그러면 거의 아무 비용도 들지 않을 이것 하나만으로도 자네는 멋들어진 주석을 얻게 되니, 이렇게 적을 수 있음이라—골리아스, 혹은 골리앗이라는 거인은, 양치기 다윗이 테레빈 골짜기에서 힘찬 돌팔매질로 죽인 블레셋 사람이니, 「열왕기」에 적힌 바와 같다—그것이 적혀 있는 장(章)을 찾아 적으면 되네.\n\n\"다음으로, 자네가 교양 있는 문학과 우주지리학에 박식한 사람임을 증명하려거든, 자네 이야기에 타호강이 이름을 올리도록 손을 쓰게. 그러면 곧장 또 하나의 이름난 주석을 얻게 되니, 이렇게 펼쳐 적는 거지—타호강은 스페인의 어느 임금의 이름을 따서 그리 불렸으며, 그 발원지는 이러이러한 곳이요 대양으로 흘러들어 가, 이름난 리스본 도시의 성벽에 입 맞추며, 그 모래가 황금이라는 것이 통설이다, 운운. 자네가 도둑들과 얽힐 일이 있거든, 내가 카쿠스의 이야기를 줄 터이니, 그건 내가 외고 있다네. 헤픈 여자들과 얽힐 일이 있거든, 몬도녜도의 주교가 있어 자네에게 라미아와 라이다와 플로라를 빌려줄 터이니, 그들 누구를 언급하든 자네에게 큰 명성을 가져다줄 걸세. 모진 마음을 지닌 여자들과 얽힐 일이라면, 오비디우스가 자네에게 메데이아를 마련해 줄 것이요, 마녀나 마법사들과 얽힐 일이라면, 호메로스에게 칼립소가 있고 베르길리우스에게 키르케가 있다네. 용맹한 장수들과 얽힐 일이라면, 율리우스 카이사르 자신이 그의 「갈리아 전기」에서 자기 자신을 자네에게 빌려줄 것이요, 플루타르코스가 자네에게 천 명의 알렉산드로스를 줄 걸세. 자네가 사랑을 다룬다면, 토스카나 말을 두어 온스만 알아도 헤브라이오스 레온에게 갈 수 있으니, 그가 자네 마음껏 채워 줄 걸세. 혹은 외국까지 가고 싶지 않다면, 자네 집안에 폰세카의 「하느님의 사랑에 관하여」가 있어, 그 주제에 관해 자네나 가장 상상력 풍부한 정신이 바랄 수 있는 모든 것이 거기 응축되어 있다네. 한마디로 자네가 해야 할 일이라곤 이 이름들을 인용하도록, 혹은 내가 말한 이 이야기들을 언급하도록 손을 쓰는 것뿐이고, 주석과 인용을 끼워 넣는 일은 내게 맡기게. 자네 여백을 가득 채우고 책 끝에 넉 장을 다 써 버리겠노라고, 좋은 것 모두를 걸고 맹세하네.\n\n\"이제 다른 책들에는 있고 자네 책에도 두고 싶어 하는 그 작가 참조 목록으로 와 보세. 이에 대한 해법은 아주 간단하네. 자네가 직접 말한 대로 A에서 Z까지 그들을 죄다 인용하는 책 하나를 찾아내기만 하면 되네. 그러고 나서 바로 그 알파벳 목록을 자네 책에 옮겨 넣는 거지. 자네가 그들에게서 빌릴 필요가 그토록 적으니 그 속임수가 빤히 들여다보일지라도, 그건 아무래도 좋아. 자네가 이 꾸밈없고 소박한 이야기 속에서 그들 모두를 활용했다고 믿어 줄 만큼 단순한 이들이 아마 있을 테니 말일세. 어쨌든 다른 쓸모가 없다 해도, 이 긴 작가 목록은 자네 책에 놀라우리만치 권위 있어 보이는 풍모를 더해 주는 데는 쓸모가 있을 걸세. 게다가 자네가 그들을 따랐는지 안 따랐는지 굳이 확인해 보려고 수고할 사람은 아무도 없네. 거기 얽힐 일이 전혀 없으니 말일세. 더구나, 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 이 자네 책은 자네가 부족하다고 말한 그것들 가운데 어느 하나도 필요로 하지 않네. 처음부터 끝까지 그것은 기사도 책들에 대한 공격이니, 아리스토텔레스는 꿈에도 생각지 못했고 성 바실리오는 한마디도 하지 않았으며 키케로는 알지도 못한 것이라네. 또한 진리의 정밀함도, 점성술의 관찰도 그 공상적인 갈팡질팡 속에 들어설 자리가 없고, 기하학적 측정이나 수사학에서 쓰이는 논증의 반박도 그것과는 아무 상관이 없네. 또한 그것은 인간적인 것과 신적인 것을 뒤섞어, 그 어떤 그리스도교적 지성도 차려입어서는 안 될 일종의 얼룩덜룩한 옷을 입고 누구에게도 설교하려 들지 않네. 그것은 그저 그 구성에서 자연에 대한 충실함을 활용하기만 하면 되고, 모방이 완벽할수록 작품도 그만큼 더 나아질 걸세. 그리고 이 자네 작품이 기사도 책들이 세상에서, 그리고 대중에게서 지닌 권위와 영향력을 무너뜨리는 것 말고는 아무것도 노리지 않으니, 자네는 철학자들에게서 경구를, 성경에서 가르침을, 시인들에게서 우화를, 웅변가들에게서 연설을, 성인들에게서 기적을 구걸하러 다닐 필요가 없네. 그저 자네의 문체와 어법이 음악처럼, 유쾌하게, 평이하게 흐르도록, 명료하고 적확하며 제자리에 놓인 말들로 자네의 뜻을 힘껏 펼쳐 보이고, 혼란이나 모호함 없이 자네 생각을 알아듣기 쉽게 풀어내도록 마음 쓰기만 하면 되네. 또한 자네 이야기를 읽으며 우울한 이는 웃음에 이끌리고, 즐거운 이는 더욱 즐거워지도록, 단순한 이는 지루해하지 않고, 분별 있는 이는 그 창의에 감탄하며, 진중한 이는 그것을 멸시하지 않고, 지혜로운 이는 그것을 칭찬하기를 마지않도록 애쓰게. 끝으로, 어떤 이들은 미워하고 그보다 더 많은 이들은 칭송하는, 그 토대가 부실한 기사도 책들의 건물을 무너뜨리는 데 자네의 겨냥을 단단히 고정하게나. 이 일에 자네가 성공한다면, 적잖은 성취를 이룬 셈이 될 테니 말일세.\"\n\n내 벗이 한 말을 깊은 침묵 속에서 들었는데, 그 견해가 어찌나 깊은 인상을 남겼던지, 나는 그것에 시비를 걸어 볼 엄두도 내지 않고 그 타당함을 인정했으며, 그것들로부터 이 서문을 짓기로 결심했다네. 점잖은 독자여, 그대는 여기서 내 벗의 훌륭한 분별을, 그토록 절박한 때에 그런 조언자를 만난 나의 행운을, 그리고 이름난 라만차의 돈키호테 이야기를—더하거나 고친 것 없이—그대로 받음으로써 그대가 얻은 것을 알아보게 되리라. 그분으로 말하자면, 캄포 데 몬티엘 지방의 모든 주민이 여러 해 동안 그 인근에서 본 가장 정결한 연인이자 가장 용감한 기사였다고 입을 모은다네. 그토록 이름 높고 존귀한 기사와 그대를 사귀게 해 주는 데 내가 베푸는 봉사를 부풀릴 마음은 없으나, 그분의 시종인 그 이름난 산초 판사와 사귀게 됨에 대해서만은 그대의 감사를 바라네. 내 생각에 나는 그 안에, 그 부질없는 기사도 책 무리 속에 흩어져 있는 온갖 시종다운 익살을 응축하여 그대에게 안겨 주었으니 말일세. 그러니—하느님께서 그대에게 건강을 주시기를, 그리고 나를 잊지 마시기를. 잘 있게나.\n\n* 잘 있게나(Vale): 라틴어로 작별 인사이다. 제1부 헌사\n\n베하르 공작이시며, 히브랄레온 후작이시며, 베날카사르와 바냐레스의 백작이시며, 푸에블라 데 알코세르의 자작이시며, 카피야와 쿠리엘과 부르기요스 영지의 영주이신 분께 각하께서 온갖 종류의 책에 베푸시는 호의로운 영접과 영예를, 좋은 학예에, 특히 그 고귀함으로 인해 비속한 자들의 비위 맞추기와 매수에 굴하지 않는 책들에 그토록 기우신 군주이심을 믿사옵고, 소인은 각하의 빛나는 존명의 비호 아래 『기발한 이달고 라만차의 돈키호테』를 세상에 내놓기로 결심하였사옵니다. 그 위대하심에 마땅히 바쳐야 할 경의를 다하여, 각하께서 그것을 기꺼이 그 보호 아래 받아 주시기를 비오니, 이로써 이 그늘 속에서, 비록 식견 있는 이들의 집에서 지어진 작품들을 입히는 그 우아함과 박식이라는 값진 장식은 빼앗겼을지언정, 제 무지의 경계를 넘어 남의 글을 더 가혹하고 덜 정의롭게 단죄하기 일쑤인 몇몇 이들의 심판 앞에서도 확신을 가지고 감히 나설 수 있게 되기를 바라옵니다. 소인의 명예로운 뜻에 대한 각하의 어진 조언이 이토록 보잘것없는 봉사의 작음을 업신여기지 않으시기를 간절히 바라옵나이다.\n\n미겔 데 세르반테스\n\n# CHAPTER I.\n\n라만차의 어느 마을, 그 이름은 굳이 떠올리고 싶지 않은 그곳에 그리 오래지 않은 옛날 한 시골 양반이 살고 있었으니, 창걸이에는 창을 꽂아 두고, 오래된 방패와 비쩍 마른 말 한 필, 그리고 사냥용 그레이하운드 한 마리를 거느린 그런 부류의 사람이었다. 양고기보다 쇠고기가 좀 더 많이 든 스튜 한 냄비, 거의 매일 밤의 샐러드, 토요일의 자투리 고기 요리, 금요일의 렌즈콩, 일요일에 곁들이는 비둘기 한두 마리가 그의 수입 가운데 사분의 삼을 축냈다. 나머지는 명절날 입을 고운 천 더블릿과 벨벳 반바지, 거기에 어울리는 신발로 들어갔으며, 평일에는 가장 좋은 손수 짠 옷을 차려입고 의젓한 모습을 했다. 그의 집에는 마흔이 넘은 가정부와 스무 살이 채 안 된 조카딸, 그리고 들일과 장보기를 맡아 말에 안장을 얹기도 하고 가지치기 낫을 다루기도 하는 머슴 아이가 하나 있었다. 우리 이 양반의 나이는 쉰 줄에 다다랐으며, 다부진 체질에 야위고 핼쑥한 얼굴을 했고, 무척 일찍 일어나는 데다 사냥을 몹시 즐겼다. 사람들은 그의 성이 키하다 혹은 케사다였다고 하는데(이 점에 대해서는 이 주제를 다룬 작가들 사이에 견해가 다소 갈린다), 그럴듯한 추측으로 미루어 보면 케하나라 불렸음이 분명한 듯하다. 그러나 이는 우리 이야기에 그리 중요한 일이 아니니, 이야기를 풀어 나가는 동안 진실에서 터럭만큼도 벗어나지 않으면 그것으로 족하다.\n\n그러니 알아 두어야 할 것은, 앞서 말한 이 양반이 한가할 때면(거의 일 년 내내 한가했지만) 어찌나 열렬하고 게걸스럽게 기사도 책 읽기에 빠져들었던지, 사냥하는 일도 거의 까맣게 잊고 심지어 제 재산을 챙기는 일조차 등한히 했다는 사실이다. 그 열망과 광기가 어느 지경에 이르렀던가 하면, 읽을 기사도 책을 사려고 경작지를 여러 에이커씩 팔아 치웠으며, 손에 넣을 수 있는 책이라면 모조리 집으로 가져왔다. 그러나 그 모든 책 가운데서도 그가 가장 좋아한 것은 저 유명한 펠리시아노 데 실바가 지은 책들이었으니, 그 명료한 문체와 복잡하게 얽힌 기발한 표현들이 그의 눈에는 진주처럼 보였던 까닭이요, 특히 책을 읽다가 구애의 글과 도전장에 이르면 더욱 그러했다. 거기서 그는 흔히 이런 구절들을 만나곤 했다. \"내 이성을 괴롭히는 까닭 없는 까닭이 내 이성을 너무도 약하게 만드는지라, 까닭이 있어 그대의 아름다움을 두고 한탄하노라.\" 또 이런 구절도 있었다. \"그대의 신성으로 그대를 별들로써 신성하게 떠받치는 드높은 하늘이, 그대의 위대함이 마땅히 받을 만한 그 마땅함을 그대로 하여금 누리게 하노라.\" 이런 종류의 기발한 표현 앞에서 가엾은 양반은 그만 넋을 잃었으니, 밤잠을 설쳐 가며 그것들을 이해하고 그 속뜻을 캐내려 애썼다. 설령 아리스토텔레스가 바로 그 일을 위해 다시 살아 돌아온다 한들 그 뜻을 헤아리거나 끌어내지 못했을 것이다. 그는 돈 벨리아니스가 주고받은 상처들에 대해서는 도무지 마음을 놓지 못했으니, 그를 치료한 외과의들이 아무리 뛰어났다 해도 그의 얼굴과 몸은 온통 꿰맨 자국과 흉터로 뒤덮여 있을 게 분명하다고 여겼기 때문이다. 그래도 그는 작가가 그 끝없는 모험을 약속하는 것으로 책을 맺은 방식만큼은 칭찬했으며, 거기 제안된 대로 펜을 들어 제대로 마무리 짓고 싶은 마음에 여러 번 사로잡혔다. 더 크고 더 사로잡는 생각들이 그를 막아서지만 않았더라면, 그는 틀림없이 그 일을 해냈을 것이고, 또 그것을 훌륭한 작품으로 완성했을 것이다.\n\n그는 자기 마을의 신부와(시구엔사의 학사 출신인 박식한 사람이었다) 영국의 팔메린과 골의 아마디스 가운데 어느 쪽이 더 나은 기사였던가를 두고 여러 차례 논쟁을 벌이곤 했다. 그러나 마을 이발사인 니콜라스 선생은 그 둘 중 누구도 태양의 기사에는 미치지 못한다고 말하곤 했으며, 만일 그에 견줄 만한 자가 있다면 그것은 골의 아마디스의 동생인 돈 갈라오르일 거라고 했다. 그가 어떤 상황에도 거뜬히 맞설 만한 기개를 지녔고, 그 형처럼 까다롭게 구는 기사도, 눈물이나 짜는 기사도 아니었으며, 용맹함에서도 형에게 조금도 뒤지지 않았기 때문이다. 요컨대 그는 책에 너무도 깊이 빠져들어, 밤이면 해 질 녘부터 동틀 녘까지, 낮이면 새벽부터 어두워질 때까지 책에 코를 박고 지냈다. 그렇게 잠은 적게 자고 책은 많이 읽다 보니 머릿속이 바싹 말라붙어 그예 정신을 잃고 말았다. 그의 머릿속은 책에서 읽던 것들로 가득 차게 되었으니, 마법이며 다툼이며 전투며 도전이며 상처며 구애며 사랑이며 고뇌며 온갖 터무니없는 황당무계한 일들이 그것이었다. 이런 것들이 그의 마음을 어찌나 사로잡았던지, 그가 읽은 그 모든 꾸며 낸 환상의 직물이 진짜라고 여기게 되었으며, 그에게는 세상의 그 어떤 역사책도 이보다 더 사실적인 것이 없었다. 그는 엘 시드 루이 디아스가 매우 훌륭한 기사이긴 하나, 단 한 번의 되받아치는 일격으로 사납고 흉측한 두 거인을 두 동강 낸 불타는 검의 기사에는 견줄 바가 못 된다고 말하곤 했다. 그는 베르나르도 델 카르피오를 더 높이 샀는데, 그가 론세스바예스에서 마법에도 굴하지 않고 롤단을 죽였기 때문이며, 그때 헤라클레스가 대지의 아들 안타이오스를 두 팔로 끌어안아 목 졸라 죽인 그 술책을 써먹었기 때문이다. 그는 거인 모르간테도 크게 칭찬했으니, 그가 늘 거만하고 못돼 먹기 마련인 거인족이면서도 홀로 상냥하고 예의 발랐기 때문이다. 그러나 무엇보다도 그는 몬탈반의 레이날도스를 흠모했으니, 특히 그가 제 성에서 뛰쳐나와 마주치는 사람마다 모조리 약탈하는 모습과, 바다 건너 저편에서 그 마호메트 우상을 훔쳐 내는 모습을 볼 때면 더욱 그러했다. 그 우상은 그의 이야기에 따르면 온통 순금으로 되어 있었다. 저 배신자 가넬론을 실컷 걷어차 줄 수만 있다면 그는 자기 가정부는 물론이요 조카딸까지 덤으로 내주었을 것이다.\n\n요컨대 그는 정신이 완전히 나가 버린 나머지, 이 세상의 어떤 미치광이도 떠올린 적 없는 가장 기이한 생각에 이르렀으니, 그것은 곧 제 명예를 지키기 위해서나 나라에 봉사하기 위해서나 자신이 편력 기사가 되어, 갑옷을 온전히 갖춰 입고 말에 올라 온 세상을 떠돌며 모험을 찾아다니고, 편력 기사들이 으레 행한다고 책에서 읽은 그 모든 일을 몸소 실천하는 것이 옳고도 마땅한 일이라고 여긴 것이었다. 온갖 종류의 불의를 바로잡고, 위험과 곤경에 스스로를 내던지되, 결국에는 그로써 영원한 명성과 영예를 거두려는 것이었다. 이 가엾은 사내는 벌써 제 팔의 힘으로 적어도 트레비손다의 황제로 등극한 자신의 모습을 그려 보고 있었다. 그리하여 이 즐거운 공상에서 맛본 그 강렬한 기쁨에 끌려, 그는 곧장 자신의 계획을 실행에 옮기기로 했다.\n\n그가 가장 먼저 한 일은 증조부의 것이었던 갑옷 한 벌을 손질하는 일이었다. 그 갑옷은 오랜 세월 한구석에 잊힌 채 녹이 슬고 곰팡이로 뒤덮여 있었다. 그는 할 수 있는 한 그것을 닦고 광을 냈으나, 거기서 한 가지 큰 결함을 발견했으니, 닫히는 투구는 없고 그저 단순한 투구 모양의 모리온뿐이라는 점이었다. 그러나 그의 재간이 이 부족함을 메워 주었으니, 그는 마분지로 반쪽짜리 투구 같은 것을 만들어 모리온에 끼워 맞추자 온전한 투구처럼 보이게 했다. 사실 그것이 단단한지, 칼질을 견딜 만한지 보려고 그가 칼을 뽑아 두어 번 내리쳐 보았는데, 첫 번째 일격으로 한 주일 동안 공들인 것이 순식간에 망가져 버렸다. 그토록 손쉽게 산산조각 나 버린 것에 그는 적잖이 당황했으며, 그 위험에 대비하고자 다시 작업에 착수해 안쪽에 쇠막대들을 덧대어 그 견고함에 흡족할 때까지 손을 보았다. 그러고는 더 이상 그것으로 어떤 실험도 해 볼 마음이 없어, 그것을 가장 완벽하게 만들어진 투구로 인정하고 받아들였다.\n\n그는 다음으로 자기 말을 살펴보았는데, 이 말은 레알 동전보다 더 많은 흠을 지녔고, 곤넬라의 말, 곧 \"온통 가죽과 뼈뿐이었던\" 그 말보다 더 많은 결점을 지녔으나, 그의 눈에는 알렉산드로스의 부케팔로스나 엘 시드의 바비에카를 능가하는 것이었다. 그는 말에게 어떤 이름을 지어 줄까 궁리하느라 나흘을 보냈으니, (그가 스스로 되뇌었듯이) 그토록 이름난 기사의 말이, 그것도 제 나름의 빼어남을 갖춘 말이 어떤 특별한 이름도 없이 지내는 것은 옳지 않은 일이었기 때문이다. 그는 그 이름을 잘 다듬어 그 말이 편력 기사의 것이 되기 전에는 어떤 말이었으며 지금은 어떤 말인지를 드러내려 애썼다. 주인이 새로운 신분을 얻었으니 말도 새 이름을 얻는 것이, 그것도 그가 이제 막 따르려는 새로운 기사단과 직분에 걸맞게 격조 높고 우렁차게 울리는 이름을 얻는 것이 사리에 맞는 일이었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그는 기억과 공상에서 숱한 이름을 지어냈다가 지우고, 물리쳤다가 보태고, 허물었다가 다시 짜 맞춘 끝에, 마침내 그 말을 로시난테라 부르기로 마음먹었다. 그가 생각하기에 이는 격조 높고 우렁차게 울리며, 그 말이 지금의 모습, 곧 세상 모든 짐말 가운데 으뜸가는 말이 되기 전에는 한낱 짐말이었던 그 처지를 잘 드러내 주는 이름이었다.\n\n이렇듯 제 마음에 쏙 드는 이름을 말에게 지어 주고 나니, 그는 자기 자신에게도 이름 하나를 지어 주고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그리하여 이 점을 두고 다시 여드레를 곰곰이 생각한 끝에, 마침내 자신을 \"돈키호테\"라 부르기로 마음을 정했다. 이로부터, 앞서 말한 바와 같이, 이 진실한 역사의 작가들은 그의 성이 다른 이들이 우긴 케사다가 아니라 의심할 바 없이 키하다였음이 틀림없다고 추론한 것이다. 그러나 용맹한 아마디스가 그저 무뚝뚝하게 아마디스라고만 부르는 데 만족하지 않고, 제 이름을 떨치고자 거기에 자기 왕국과 고향의 이름을 덧붙여 스스로를 골의 아마디스라 일컬었음을 떠올린 그는, 훌륭한 기사답게 자기 고장의 이름을 덧붙이기로 결심하고는 스스로를 라만차의 돈키호테라 칭하기로 했다. 이로써 그는 자신의 가문과 고향을 정확히 드러내며, 그곳에서 성을 따옴으로써 그곳에 영예를 돌린다고 여겼다.\n\n그리하여 갑옷을 말끔히 손질하고, 모리온을 투구로 바꾸고, 짐말에게 세례명을 지어 주고, 자기 자신도 새 이름으로 확정 짓고 나니, 그는 이제 더 필요한 것이라고는 사랑에 빠질 귀부인을 하나 찾는 일뿐이라는 결론에 이르렀다. 사랑 없는 편력 기사란 잎도 열매도 없는 나무나, 영혼 없는 육신과 같았기 때문이다. 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 되뇌었다. \"내 죄업 탓이든 내 행운 덕분이든, 만일 내가 이 근방에서 어떤 거인과 마주친다면, 이는 편력 기사들에게는 흔한 일이거니와, 단 한 번의 공격으로 그를 거꾸러뜨리거나, 허리께까지 두 동강을 내거나, 요컨대 그를 무찔러 굴복시킨다면, 그를 선물로 보낼 누군가가 있는 편이 좋지 않겠는가. 그리하여 그가 들어와 내 어여쁜 귀부인 앞에 무릎 꿇고, 겸손하고 순종하는 목소리로 이렇게 아뢰는 것이다. '저는 거인 카라쿨리암브로, 말린드라니아 섬의 영주이옵니다. 아무리 칭송해도 모자랄 기사 라만차의 돈키호테 님과의 일대일 결투에서 패하였으며, 그분께서 제게 명하시기를 마님 앞에 나아가 마님께서 마음대로 저를 처분하시도록 하라 하셨나이다.'\" 오, 우리의 선량한 양반은 이 대사를 읊으면서 얼마나 즐거워했던가, 특히 제 귀부인이라 부를 누군가를 떠올렸을 때는 더욱 그러했다! 전해지는 이야기에 따르면, 그의 마을 가까운 한 동네에 무척 어여쁜 농사꾼 처녀가 있었으니, 그가 한때 사랑했던 여인이었다. 다만 알려진 바로는, 그녀는 그 사실을 결코 알지 못했고 그런 일에 마음을 둔 적도 없었다. 그녀의 이름은 알돈사 로렌소였고, 그는 그녀에게 자기 마음속 귀부인이라는 칭호를 내리는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼다. 그리하여 그녀의 본래 이름과 어긋나지 않으면서도 어느 공주나 위대한 귀부인의 이름을 떠올리게 하고 또 암시할 만한 이름을 한참 궁리한 끝에, 그는 그녀를 토보소의 둘시네아라 부르기로 했다. 그녀가 엘 토보소 출신이었기 때문이다. 그가 보기에 이는 음악처럼 울리고, 흔치 않으며, 뜻이 깊은 이름이었으니, 이미 자신과 제게 딸린 것들에 지어 준 다른 모든 이름이 그러했듯이 말이다.\n\n# CHAPTER II.\n\n이런 사전 준비를 마치자, 그는 자신의 계획을 실행에 옮기는 일을 더는 미루고 싶지 않았다. 자신이 바로잡으려는 부당함, 시정하려는 억울함, 보상하려는 불의, 없애려는 폐단, 다해야 할 의무들을 생각하니, 자신이 지체하는 동안 온 세상이 잃고 있는 것을 떠올렸기에 더욱 마음이 다급해졌다. 그리하여 그는 누구에게도 자신의 의향을 알리지 않고, 또 아무도 그를 보지 못하게 한 채, 어느 날 아침 동이 트기도 전에(때는 7월 중에서도 가장 무더운 날 가운데 하루였다) 갑옷을 차려입고, 기워 붙인 투구를 쓰고서 로시난테에 올라타, 방패를 단단히 동여매고 창을 집어 들고는, 마당 뒷문으로 들판을 향해 출정했다. 그는 자신의 원대한 목적을 이토록 손쉽게 시작했다는 데에 더없는 흡족함과 만족감을 느꼈다. 그러나 탁 트인 들판에 막 들어서자마자, 무시무시한 생각 하나가 그를 사로잡았으니, 그것은 출발하는 바로 그 순간 이 모험을 포기하게 만들 뻔할 만큼 강력한 것이었다. 그는 자신이 아직 기사 서임을 받지 못했다는 사실, 그리고 기사도의 법에 따르면 자신은 어떤 기사를 상대로도 무기를 들 수 없고 또 들어서도 안 된다는 사실을 깨달았던 것이다. 게다가 설령 서임을 받았다 하더라도, 신참 기사로서 자신의 무용으로 문장(紋章)을 얻어 낼 때까지는 방패에 아무런 문장도 새기지 않은 흰 갑옷을 입어야 한다는 점 또한 떠올랐다. 이런 생각들이 그의 결심을 흔들리게 했으나, 그의 광기는 그 어떤 이치보다도 강했기에, 그는 이런 처지에 놓인 다른 이들의 본을 따라, 자신을 이 지경에 이르게 한 책들에서 읽은 대로, 처음 마주치는 사람에게서 기사 서임을 받기로 마음을 굳혔다. 흰 갑옷에 관해서는, 첫 번째 기회가 오면 담비 털보다 더 희어질 때까지 자기 갑옷을 닦으리라 작정했다. 그렇게 스스로를 위로하며 그는 가던 길을 계속 갔는데, 말이 골라 가는 대로 내맡겼으니, 바로 거기에 모험의 본질이 깃들어 있다고 믿었기 때문이다.\n\n이렇게 길을 나선 우리의 풋내기 모험가는 혼자 중얼거리며 천천히 나아갔다. \"누가 알겠는가, 훗날 나의 그 빛나는 업적들에 관한 진실한 역사가 세상에 알려질 때, 그것을 기록하는 현자가 이른 아침의 나의 첫 출정을 펼쳐 보일 적에, 이런 식으로 쓰지 않으리라고? '불그레한 아폴로가 넓디넓은 대지의 얼굴 위로 그 찬란한 머리카락의 황금빛 실타래를 채 펼치기도 전에, 알록달록한 깃털을 단 작은 새들이 그 감미롭고 꿀처럼 달콤한 가락으로, 질투심 많은 남편의 보드라운 잠자리를 버리고 라만차 지평선의 문과 발코니에서 인간들에게 모습을 드러내는 장밋빛 새벽의 도래를 맞으려 그 노래를 미처 고르기도 전에, 이름 높은 기사 라만차의 돈키호테는 게으른 새털 이불을 떨치고 일어나, 그 유명한 준마 로시난테에 올라타 유서 깊고 명성 자자한 몬티엘 들판을 가로지르기 시작했더라.'\" 사실 그는 정말로 그 들판을 가로지르고 있었다. \"행복하여라 그 시대여, 행복하여라 그 시절이여.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"청동에 빚어지고 대리석에 새겨지며 그림으로 그려져 영원토록 기념될 만한, 명성에 빛나는 나의 업적들이 세상에 알려질 그 시대여. 그리고 그대, 오 현명한 마법사여, 그대가 누구이든, 이 경이로운 역사의 기록자가 될 운명을 맡은 그대여, 부디 잊지 말아 다오, 내 간청하노니, 나의 길과 방랑의 한결같은 동반자인 나의 충직한 로시난테를.\" 곧이어 그는 마치 진정으로 사랑에 빠진 사람처럼 다시 외쳤다. \"오 둘시네아 공주여, 이 사로잡힌 마음의 여인이여, 그대는 나를 멸시하며 내쫓고, 그 굽힐 줄 모르는 매정함으로 그대의 아름다움 앞에서 나를 추방하여 내게 모진 잘못을 저질렀도다. 오 여인이여, 이 마음을, 그대를 향한 사랑에 이토록 괴로이 애태우는 그대의 종복인 이 마음을, 부디 기억해 주소서.\"\n\n그는 이런 식으로, 그리고 또 다른 갖가지 터무니없는 말들을 줄줄이 엮어 나갔으니, 모두가 자기 책들이 가르쳐 준 그대로의 문체였고, 할 수 있는 한 그 언어를 흉내 낸 것이었다. 그러는 내내 그는 어찌나 느릿느릿 말을 몰았던지, 그리고 해는 어찌나 빠르게, 또 어찌나 뜨거운 기세로 솟아올랐던지, 그에게 골이라는 것이 있었다면 그것을 녹여 버리기에 충분할 정도였다. 그는 거의 온종일 길을 갔으나 이렇다 할 일은 하나도 일어나지 않았으니, 그는 절망에 빠졌다. 한시바삐 누군가를 만나 자기 억센 팔의 힘을 시험해 보고 싶어 안달이 났기 때문이다.\n\n그가 처음 마주친 모험이 푸에르토 라피세의 모험이었다고 말하는 작가들이 있고, 또 풍차의 모험이었다고 말하는 이들도 있다. 그러나 이 점에 관하여 내가 확인한 바, 그리고 라만차의 연대기에 적힌 것을 찾아낸 바로는, 그는 온종일 길을 갔으며, 해 질 무렵이 되자 그의 말과 그는 죽도록 지치고 허기진 처지가 되었으니, 사방을 두루 살피며 몸을 추스르고 쓰라린 궁핍을 달랠 만한 성이나 양치기의 오두막이라도 찾을 수 있을까 했다. 그러던 중 길에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 주막 하나가 눈에 띄었는데, 그것은 마치 그를 그의 구원의 궁전까지는 아니더라도 그 문간으로 인도하는 별처럼 반가운 것이었다. 그가 걸음을 재촉하여 그곳에 다다른 것은 막 밤이 깃들 무렵이었다. 문 앞에는 젊은 여인 둘이 서 있었는데, 그 고장 말로 부르는 이른바 들병이들로서, 그날 밤 마침 그 주막에 들른 어느 노새몰이꾼 일행과 함께 세비야로 가는 길이었다. 우리의 모험가에게는 무슨 일이 일어나든 간에, 그가 보거나 떠올리는 모든 것이 자기가 읽은 그대로의 모습이고 그대로 일어나는 일처럼 여겨졌으므로, 그는 주막을 본 그 순간 그것을 네 개의 망루와 번쩍이는 은빛 첨탑을 갖춘 성으로 그려 냈으며, 도개교며 해자며 그런 부류의 성에 으레 따르는 온갖 부속물도 빼놓지 않았다. 그에게 성처럼 보이는 이 주막을 향해 그는 나아갔고, 거기서 얼마쯤 떨어진 데에서 로시난테를 멈춰 세웠으니, 어느 난쟁이가 흉벽 위에 나타나 나팔 소리로 어느 기사가 성에 다가오고 있음을 알려 주기를 바랐던 것이다. 그러나 그들이 그렇게 하기를 꾸물거리는 데다 로시난테가 마구간에 닿고 싶어 안달인 것을 보고, 그는 주막 문으로 향했고, 거기 서 있던 두 명의 들뜬 처녀를 보았으니, 그에게는 그들이 성문 앞에서 한가로이 쉬고 있는 두 명의 아리따운 아가씨나 사랑스러운 귀부인으로 보였다.\n\n바로 이 순간, 마침 어느 돼지치기가 그루터기밭을 가로지르며 돼지 떼를 (어떤 변명도 없이 말하자면, 그것들이 그렇게 불리는 까닭이다) 모으느라 그것들을 한데 끌어모으려고 뿔나팔을 한 번 불었는데, 돈키호테에게는 즉시 그것이 자신이 기다리던 바로 그것, 곧 자신의 도착을 알리는 어느 난쟁이의 신호처럼 여겨졌다. 그리하여 그는 엄청난 만족감에 젖어 주막으로, 그 귀부인들에게로 말을 몰아갔는데, 그들은 이런 차림의 사내가 갑옷을 온전히 갖춰 입고 창과 방패를 든 채 다가오는 것을 보고는 기겁하여 주막 안으로 들어가려던 참이었다. 돈키호테는 그들이 달아나는 것으로 그들의 두려움을 알아채고, 판지로 만든 면갑을 들어 올려 마르고 먼지 낀 얼굴을 드러내고는, 정중한 태도와 부드러운 목소리로 그들에게 말을 건넸다. \"아가씨들께서는 달아나실 것도, 무슨 무례함을 두려워하실 것도 없습니다. 어느 누구에게도 무례를 범하는 것은 제가 받든 기사도의 법도에 어긋나는 일이며, 더구나 그대들의 자태가 드러내 보이는 대로 고귀한 집안의 아가씨들께라면 더더욱 그러합니다.\" 처녀들은 그를 바라보며, 그 볼품없는 면갑에 가려진 이목구비를 알아보려 눈을 잔뜩 부릅떴으나, 자신들이 아가씨라 불리는 것을 듣자, 그것은 그들의 처지와는 너무도 동떨어진 일이었기에 도무지 웃음을 참을 수 없었으니, 그 바람에 돈키호테는 분개하여 이렇게 말했다. \"정숙함은 아리따운 이에게 어울리는 법이요, 더구나 별 까닭 없는 웃음은 크나큰 어리석음이외다. 그러나 이는 그대들을 아프게 하거나 노엽게 하려고 하는 말이 아니니, 내 바람은 오직 그대들을 섬기는 것 외에 다른 것이 아니기 때문입니다.\"\n\n우리 기사의 알아들을 수 없는 말과 미덥지 못한 행색은 그저 귀부인들의 웃음을 더 키울 뿐이었고, 그것은 그의 짜증을 더 돋우었으니, 만일 그 순간 주막 주인이 나오지 않았더라면 일이 더 커졌을지도 몰랐다. 그는 몹시 뚱뚱한 사람인지라 매우 온순한 사람이었다. 그는 안장이며 굴레며 창이며 방패며 흉갑이며 무엇 하나 서로 어울리지 않는 갑옷을 걸친 이 괴상한 형상을 보고, 처녀들과 한데 어울려 그 우스워하는 기색을 드러내고 싶은 마음이 전혀 없지는 않았으나, 사실은 그토록 복잡하게 무장한 모습에 두려움을 느낀 터라, 좋은 말로 대하는 것이 상책이라 여겨 이렇게 말했다. \"기사 나리, 만일 나리께서 묵을 곳을 찾으신다면, 침대만 빼고는(주막에 침대라곤 하나도 없으니 말입니다) 다른 것은 여기 무엇이든 넉넉히 있습니다.\" 돈키호테는 그 요새의 성주가(그의 눈에는 주막 주인과 주막이 그렇게 보였으니) 보이는 공손한 태도를 살피고는 이렇게 답했다. \"성주님, 제게는 무엇이든 충분하옵니다. 어인즉\n\n'갑옷이 나의 유일한 옷이요, 싸움터가 나의 유일한 안식이니라.'\"\n\n주막 주인은 그가 자신을 카스티야 사람으로 여겨 성주라 불렀다고 생각했으나, 실은 그는 안달루시아 사람이었고, 그것도 산루카르 해변 출신으로, 카쿠스 못지않게 교활한 도둑이요 학생이나 시동만큼이나 잔꾀로 가득 찬 자였다. \"그렇다면,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"'그대의 잠자리는 단단한 바위 위요, 그대의 잠은 늘 깨어 지키는 것이니라.'\n\n그러하시다면, 말에서 내리셔도 좋습니다. 하룻밤은 말할 것도 없고 일 년 내내라도 이 지붕 아래서 얼마든지 잠 못 이룰 것을 톡톡히 보장받으실 테니까요.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 돈키호테의 등자를 잡아 주려고 다가갔고, 돈키호테는 하루 종일 아무것도 입에 대지 못한 터라 무척이나 힘겹고 안간힘을 쓰며 말에서 내렸다. 그러고는 주인에게 자기 말을 각별히 잘 보살펴 달라고 당부했으니, 그것이야말로 이 세상에서 빵을 먹어 본 짐승 가운데 가장 빼어난 놈이라는 것이었다. 주막 주인은 그 말을 찬찬히 뜯어보았으나, 돈키호테가 말한 만큼 좋기는커녕 그 절반만큼도 좋아 보이지 않았다. 그는 말을 마구간에 들여놓고는, 손님이 무엇을 원하는지 보러 돌아왔는데, 그때쯤 손님과 화해를 한 처녀들이 마침 그의 갑옷을 벗겨 주고 있던 참이었다. 그들은 가슴받이와 등받이는 벗겨 냈으나, 목가리개를 푸는 법도, 임시변통으로 만든 투구를 벗기는 법도 알지 못했고 또 알아낼 수도 없었으니, 그가 그것을 초록색 리본으로 동여매 놓았던 데다, 그 매듭은 도무지 풀 수가 없어 잘라 내야만 했기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 이를 결코 허락하려 들지 않았으므로, 저녁 내내 투구를 쓴 채로 있었으니, 가히 상상할 수 있는 가장 익살맞고 괴상한 형상이었다. 그리고 그들이 갑옷을 벗기는 동안, 그는 그 일을 거들던 들병이들을 성에 속한 지체 높은 귀부인들로 여기고는, 한껏 들뜬 기색으로 그들에게 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 일찍이 어느 기사도 귀부인의 손에 이토록 받들어진 적 없었으리, 돈키호테라 이름하는 그이가 고향 마을을 떠나왔을 적에 받든 것만큼은. 아가씨들이 몸소 그를 시중들고, 공주들이 그의 말을 돌보았으니—\n\n로시난테 말입니다. 어인즉, 나의 귀부인들이여, 그것이 내 말의 이름이요, 라만차의 돈키호테는 바로 나의 이름이올시다. 비록 나는 그대들을 섬기고 받드는 가운데 이룬 위업으로 내가 알려지기 전까지는 내 정체를 밝힐 뜻이 없었으나, 저 옛 랜슬롯의 노래를 지금 이 자리에 맞추어 고쳐 부를 수밖에 없었던 까닭에, 그대들이 너무 일찍 내 이름을 알게 되고 말았습니다. 그러나 머지않아 그대들께서 명하시고 내가 받드는 때가 올 것이며, 그때에는 내 팔의 힘이 그대들을 섬기고자 하는 내 소망을 보여 줄 것입니다.\"\n\n이런 식의 미사여구를 들어 본 적이 없던 처녀들은 무어라 대꾸할 말이 없었으니, 그저 그에게 뭐 좀 먹고 싶은 것이 없느냐고 물었을 뿐이다. \"기꺼이 뭐라도 좀 먹었으면 합니다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"마침 때맞춰 잘된 일이라 여겨지니 말입니다.\" 그날은 마침 금요일이었고, 주막을 통틀어 카스티야에서는 '아바데호', 안달루시아에서는 '바칼라오', 또 어떤 곳에서는 '쿠라디요', 또 다른 곳에서는 '송어 새끼'라 부르는 생선 토막 몇 점밖에는 없었다. 그래서 그들은 그에게 송어 새끼를 먹을 수 있겠느냐고 물었으니, 달리 줄 만한 생선이 없었기 때문이다. \"송어 새끼가 넉넉히 있다면야,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그것은 송어 한 마리나 다름없을 것이오. 내게는 잔돈으로 8레알을 받든 8레알짜리 한 닢을 받든 매한가지이기 때문이오. 게다가 어쩌면 이 송어 새끼들은 송아지 고기 같은 것일지도 모르니, 그것은 쇠고기보다 낫고, 또 새끼 염소 고기 같은 것일지도 모르니, 그것은 다 자란 염소 고기보다 낫지요. 그러나 그것이 무엇이든 어서 가져오시오, 무기의 무게와 압박이란 뱃속을 받쳐 주지 않고서는 견뎌 낼 수 없는 것이니 말이오.\" 그들은 바람을 쐬게 하려고 주막 문간에 그를 위한 상을 차렸고, 주인은 그에게 제대로 불리지도, 제대로 익히지도 못한 마른 대구 한 토막과, 제 갑옷만큼이나 거뭇하고 곰팡내 나는 빵 한 조각을 가져다주었다. 그러나 그가 먹는 모습을 보는 것은 가관이었으니, 투구를 쓴 채 면갑을 올리고 있던 터라, 누군가 다른 사람이 입에 넣어 주지 않고서는 제 손으로 무엇 하나 입에 넣을 수가 없었기 때문이다. 이 시중은 귀부인 가운데 한 사람이 들어 주었다. 그러나 마실 것을 주는 일은 도무지 불가능했으니, 주막 주인이 갈대 하나를 뚫어 한쪽 끝을 그의 입에 물리고 다른 쪽 끝으로 포도주를 부어 넣어 주지 않았더라면 정말로 그러했을 것이다. 이 모든 것을 그는 투구의 리본을 끊느니 차라리 참아 내는 편을 택했다.\n\n이런 일이 벌어지고 있는 동안, 주막에 어느 돼지 불까는 자가 다가왔는데, 그는 가까이 오면서 갈대 피리를 네댓 번 불었으니, 그로 인해 돈키호테는 자기가 어느 이름난 성에 있으며, 사람들이 음악으로 자신을 대접하고 있고, 마른 대구는 송어요, 빵은 더없이 흰 빵이며, 들병이들은 귀부인이고, 주막 주인은 그 성의 성주라고 완전히 믿어 버렸다. 그리하여 그는 자신의 출정과 모험이 어느 정도 보람이 있었다고 여겼다. 그러나 그는 자신이 아직 기사 서임을 받지 못했다는 생각에 여전히 마음이 괴로웠으니, 기사도의 서임을 받지 않고서는 어떤 모험에도 정당하게 나설 수 없다는 것이 그에게는 분명한 일이었기 때문이다.\n\n# CHAPTER III.\n\n이런 생각에 시달리던 그는 보잘것없는 주막 저녁 식사를 서둘러 끝냈고, 다 먹고 나자 주막 주인을 불러 함께 마구간으로 들어가 문을 닫고는 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. \"용맹하신 기사님, 제가 청하는 은혜를 당신의 너그러움으로 베풀어 주시기 전에는 이 자리에서 일어나지 않겠나이다. 그것은 당신의 명예를 드높이고 인류 전체에 이로움을 가져다줄 은혜이옵니다.\" 손님이 발치에 꿇어앉아 이런 말을 늘어놓는 것을 본 주막 주인은 어찌할 바를 몰라 멍하니 바라보며, 어떻게 하거나 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알지 못한 채 그저 일어나라고 간청할 뿐이었으나, 청한 은혜를 들어주겠노라 동의하기 전까지는 도무지 소용이 없었다. \"지체 높으신 분께 그 이상은 바라지도 않았나이다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"이제 말씀드리건대, 제가 청하고 당신의 후한 인심이 베풀어 주신 그 은혜란, 내일 아침 저를 기사로 서임해 주시는 것, 그리고 오늘 밤 제가 이 성의 예배당에서 제 무구를 지키며 밤을 새우는 것이옵니다. 그리하여 제가 말씀드린 대로 내일이면 제가 그토록 갈망하는 바가 이루어져, 곤경에 처한 이들을 위하여 세상 사방을 정정당당히 누비며 모험을 찾아다닐 수 있게 될 것이니, 이는 기사도의 의무이자 저와 같은 편력 기사의 의무이며, 그러한 위업이야말로 제 포부가 향하는 바이옵니다.\"\n\n앞서 말했듯이 다소 익살꾼 기질이 있었던 데다 이미 손님의 정신이 온전치 못하다는 의심을 품고 있던 주막 주인은, 그에게서 이런 말을 듣자 그 의심을 완전히 확신하게 되었고, 밤의 심심풀이 삼아 그의 비위를 맞춰 주기로 작정했다. 그래서 그는 손님에게, 그대가 마음에 둔 목적을 좇는 것은 지극히 옳은 일이며, 그러한 동기는 그대처럼 기품 있어 보이고 또 늠름한 몸가짐이 입증하는 그토록 빼어난 기사에게 자연스럽고도 어울리는 것이라고 말했다. 또한 자기 자신도 젊은 시절에 똑같은 명예로운 소명을 따라 세상 곳곳을 모험을 찾아 떠돌았으니, 그 가운데에는 말라가의 건어장, 리아란의 섬들, 세비야의 구역, 세고비아의 작은 시장, 발렌시아의 올리베라, 그라나다의 론디야, 산루카르의 해변, 코르도바의 망아지 광장, 톨레도의 주막들을 비롯한 여러 동네가 있었고, 그곳들에서 그는 발의 날쌤과 손가락의 가벼움을 입증해 보였으니, 숱한 못된 짓을 저지르고 숱한 과부를 속이고 처녀들을 망치고 미성년자들을 등쳐 먹어, 한마디로 스페인의 거의 모든 재판소와 법정에 이름이 오르내리는 처지에 이르렀으며, 마침내는 이 성으로 물러나 자기 재산과 남의 재산으로 살아가고 있고, 그곳에서 신분이나 지체가 어떠하든 모든 편력 기사를 맞아들이는데, 이는 오로지 그들에게 품은 크나큰 사랑에서 비롯된 것이요, 그들이 그의 자비에 보답하여 자기네 재물을 그와 나누어 가지도록 하기 위함이라고 했다. 그는 또한 이 성에는 무구를 지키며 밤을 새울 예배당이 없다고 했으니, 다시 짓느라 헐어 버린 까닭이지만, 부득이한 경우에는 어디서든 그 일을 할 수 있음을 잘 안다고 했고, 그러니 오늘 밤은 성의 안뜰에서 무구를 지키면 되고, 아침이 되어 하느님이 허락하시면 필요한 의식을 치러 그를 기사로 서임할 터이며, 그것도 누구보다도 더할 나위 없이 철저하게 서임하겠노라고 했다. 그는 돈키호테에게 돈을 지니고 있느냐고 물었고, 이에 돈키호테는 편력 기사 이야기에서 그들 가운데 누구도 돈을 지니고 다녔다는 것을 읽은 적이 없으므로 한 푼도 없다고 대답했다. 이 점에 대해 주막 주인은 그가 잘못 알고 있다고 일러 주었으니, 비록 이야기에는 기록되어 있지 않으나, 그것은 작가의 생각에 돈이나 깨끗한 셔츠처럼 그토록 뻔하고 필수적인 것은 굳이 언급할 필요가 없었기 때문일 뿐, 그렇다고 해서 그들이 그런 것을 지니고 다니지 않았다고 여겨서는 안 되며, 모든 편력 기사가(이들에 관해서는 그토록 충실하고 흠잡을 데 없는 책들이 많지 않은가) 만일의 경우에 대비해 두둑한 지갑을 지니고 다녔고, 또한 셔츠와 입은 상처를 치료할 연고 한 통도 지니고 다녔음을 확실하고도 확정된 사실로 여겨도 좋다고 했다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그들이 싸움을 벌이고 상처를 입은 채 빠져나오던 그 들판과 사막에서는 그들을 치료해 줄 누군가가 늘 있는 것은 아니었으니, 다만 그들에게 어떤 현명한 마법사가 벗으로 있어, 구름을 타고 공중을 가로질러 어떤 처녀나 난쟁이를 데려와, 그 효험이 어찌나 대단한지 한 방울만 맛보아도 상처와 부상이 순식간에 나아 마치 아무런 해도 입지 않은 것처럼 멀쩡해지는 그런 물병 하나를 들려 보내 즉시 구해 주는 경우라면 모를까 말이다. 그러나 이런 일이 일어나지 않을 경우에 대비하여, 옛 기사들은 자기 종자들이 돈과 그 밖의 필수품, 이를테면 치료에 쓸 무명실과 연고 따위를 갖추도록 빈틈없이 챙겼고, 기사에게 종자가 없는 경우에는(이는 드물고 좀처럼 없는 일이었지만) 자기네가 직접 모든 것을 교묘한 안장주머니에 넣어 다녔는데, 그 주머니는 마치 무언가 더 중요한 물건인 양 말 엉덩이 위에서도 좀처럼 눈에 띄지 않았으니, 그도 그럴 것이 그런 까닭이라도 있지 않고서는 안장주머니를 지니고 다니는 일이 편력 기사들 사이에서 그리 곱게 여겨지지 않았기 때문이다. 그리하여 그는 돈키호테에게, 앞으로는 결코 돈과 흔히 챙기는 필수품 없이 길을 떠나지 말라고 충고했고(또한 이제 곧 그의 대자가 될 처지였으니 명령하다시피 할 수도 있었다), 그러면 가장 뜻하지 않은 때에 그것들의 이로움을 알게 되리라고 했다.\n\n돈키호테는 그 충고를 어김없이 따르겠노라 약속했고, 이내 그가 주막 한쪽의 넓은 마당에서 무구를 지키며 밤을 새우기로 정해졌다. 그리하여 돈키호테는 무구를 죄다 한데 모아 우물 곁에 놓인 여물통 위에 올려놓고는, 방패를 팔에 단단히 끼고 창을 움켜쥔 채 위풍당당한 태도로 여물통 앞을 오락가락 행군하기 시작했으니, 그가 행군을 시작할 무렵 밤이 내리기 시작했다.\n\n주막 주인은 주막에 있던 모든 사람에게 손님의 광기에 대해, 무구를 지키며 밤을 새우는 일과 그가 꾀하고 있는 서임식에 대해 이야기했다. 그토록 기이한 형태의 광기에 잔뜩 놀란 사람들은 멀찍이서 그것을 구경하려고 몰려들었고, 그가 때로는 얼마나 침착하게 오락가락 거니는지, 또 때로는 창에 몸을 기댄 채 한참이 지나도록 눈을 떼지 않고 자기 무구를 응시하는지를 지켜보았으니, 밤이 깊어지면서 달이 어찌나 환하게 빛을 내뿜었던지 그 빛을 빌려 준 태양과 견줄 만하여, 풋내기 기사가 하는 모든 일이 사람들 눈에 또렷이 보였다.\n\n그러는 사이 주막에 있던 짐꾼 가운데 하나가 자기 노새들에게 물을 먹여야겠다 생각했는데, 그러자면 여물통 위에 놓인 돈키호테의 무구를 치워야 했다. 하지만 그자가 다가오는 것을 본 돈키호테는 큰 소리로 그를 불러 세웠다. \"오, 그대가 누구든, 일찍이 칼을 차 본 가장 용맹한 편력 기사의 무구에 손을 대려 다가오는 무모한 기사여, 그대가 하는 짓을 조심하라. 그대의 무모함에 대한 대가로 목숨을 내놓을 작정이 아니라면 그것에 손대지 마라.\" 짐꾼은 이 말에 아랑곳하지 않았으나(자기 건강을 생각했더라면 귀 기울이는 편이 나았을 것이다), 가죽끈을 붙잡아 무구를 저만치 멀리 내던졌다. 이를 본 돈키호테는 하늘로 눈을 들어, 보아하니 자신의 여인 둘시네아에게 생각을 모으는 듯, 이렇게 외쳤다. \"나의 여인이시여, 그대가 굴복시켜 거느린 이 가슴에 처음으로 닥쳐온 이 싸움에서 저를 도우소서. 이 첫 위기에서 그대의 은총과 보호가 저를 저버리지 않게 하소서.\" 그러고는 이 말과 같은 뜻의 다른 말들을 내뱉으며 방패를 떨어뜨리고 두 손으로 창을 들어 올려, 그것으로 짐꾼의 머리를 어찌나 세게 후려쳤는지 그를 땅바닥에 뻗게 만들었으니, 그가 너무도 정신을 잃어 만일 두 번째 일격을 가했더라면 그를 치료해 줄 의사가 필요 없었을 정도였다. 이 일을 마친 그는 무구를 집어 들고 전과 똑같이 태연하게 자기 보초 자리로 돌아갔다.\n\n이 일이 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않아 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 모르는 또 다른 자가(짐꾼은 여전히 정신을 잃은 채 누워 있었다) 똑같이 자기 노새들에게 물을 먹이려는 목적으로 다가와, 여물통을 비우려고 무구를 치우려던 참이었다. 그러자 돈키호테는 한마디 말도 없이, 누구에게도 도움을 청하지 않은 채, 또다시 방패를 떨어뜨리고 또다시 창을 들어 올려, 두 번째 짐꾼의 머리를 산산조각 내지는 않았으나 네 조각으로 쪼개 놓았으니 세 조각보다 더 만든 셈이었다. 그 소란에 주막의 사람들이 모두 그 자리로 달려왔는데, 그 가운데에는 주막 주인도 있었다. 이를 본 돈키호테는 방패를 팔에 단단히 끼고 칼자루에 손을 얹은 채 외쳤다. \"오, 아름다움의 여인이시여, 제 가냘픈 마음의 힘이요 버팀목이시여, 이토록 거대한 모험의 문턱에 선 그대의 포로 기사에게 그대 위대함의 눈길을 돌리실 때가 되었나이다.\" 이로써 그는 세상의 모든 짐꾼이 덤벼든다 해도 결코 물러서지 않을 만큼 스스로 고무됨을 느꼈다. 부상자의 동료들은 자기네 처지를 알아채고는 멀찍이서 돈키호테에게 돌을 퍼붓기 시작했고, 그는 방패로 할 수 있는 한 몸을 가리며, 여물통을 떠나 무구를 무방비로 내버려 둘 엄두를 내지 못했다. 주막 주인은 그를 내버려 두라고 그들에게 소리쳤으니, 이미 그가 미친 사람이라고 일러 두었던 터라 미친 사람으로서 설령 그들을 모조리 죽인다 해도 책임을 물을 수 없으리라는 것이었다. 돈키호테는 더더욱 큰 소리로 외치며 그들을 악당이요 배신자라 불렀고, 편력 기사들이 이런 식으로 다루어지도록 내버려 둔 성주를 가리켜 비열한 자요 천한 핏줄의 기사라 하면서, 만일 그가 기사 서품을 받은 자였다면 그 배신에 대해 책임을 물었으리라고 했다. \"허나 너희,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"비천하고 천박한 무리들은 안중에도 없도다. 던지고, 때리고, 덤벼라, 나를 향해 할 수 있는 모든 짓을 다 해 보아라, 너희의 어리석음과 무례함이 어떤 대가를 치르는지 보게 되리라.\" 그가 이 말을 어찌나 기개 있고 대담하게 내뱉었는지 공격하던 자들을 무시무시한 두려움으로 가득 채웠고, 이런 까닭에서나 주막 주인의 설득에서나 그들은 돌팔매질을 그만두었으며, 그는 그들이 부상자들을 데려가도록 허락하고는, 전과 똑같이 차분하고 침착하게 다시 무구를 지키는 보초로 돌아갔다.\n\n그러나 손님의 이런 별난 짓거리가 주막 주인의 마음에 그리 들지 않았으므로, 그는 더 이상의 불상사가 일어나기 전에 일을 짧게 마무리 짓고 당장 그 불운한 기사 서품을 그에게 내려 주기로 작정했다. 그리하여 그에게 다가가, 자기도 모르는 사이에 이 천한 자들이 무례를 범한 데 대해 사과하면서, 그래도 그들이 그 뻔뻔함에 대해 톡톡히 벌을 받지 않았느냐고 했다. 이미 말씀드린 대로 성에는 예배당이 없으나 앞으로 남은 일을 위해서는 그것이 필요치 않으니, 자신이 그 의식의 절차를 이해하기로는 기사로 서임되는 일의 핵심은 모두 목덜미를 치는 일과 어깨를 두드리는 일에 있고, 그것은 들판 한복판에서도 행할 수 있다고 했다. 또한 무구를 지키는 일에 관해서라면 이제 필요한 바를 모두 마쳤으니, 단 두 시간의 보초만으로도 모든 요건이 충족되거늘 그는 이미 네 시간이 넘도록 그것을 지켰다고 했다. 돈키호테는 그 모든 말을 곧이곧대로 믿고는, 자신은 그의 분부를 따를 준비가 되어 있으니 되도록 빨리 일을 마무리 지어 달라고 했다. 만일 자신이 또다시 공격을 받는다면, 그리고 자신이 기사로 서임되었음을 느낀다면, 자기 생각으로는 존경하는 마음에서 그의 명에 따라 살려 줄 자들을 제외하고는 성안에 한 사람도 살려 두지 않으리라는 것이었다.\n\n이렇게 경고받고 위협당한 성주는 곧바로 책 한 권을 꺼내 왔으니, 그것은 짐꾼들에게 내준 짚과 보리를 적어 두던 장부였다. 그리고 양초 동강을 든 사내아이와 앞서 말한 두 처녀를 데리고 돈키호테가 서 있는 곳으로 돌아와 무릎을 꿇으라고 일렀다. 그러고는 마치 무슨 경건한 기도라도 외우는 양 자기 회계 장부를 읽어 내려가다가, 읊조리던 도중에 손을 들어 그의 목덜미에 묵직한 일격을 가하고, 이어서 그 자신의 칼로 어깨를 찰싹 후려쳤으니, 그러는 내내 마치 기도를 올리듯 이를 사이로 무어라 중얼거렸다. 이 일을 마치고 그는 처녀 가운데 하나에게 칼을 채워 주라고 지시했고, 그녀는 더없이 침착하고 진중하게 그 일을 해냈으니, 의식의 단계마다 터져 나오려는 웃음을 참기 위해 적잖은 노력이 필요했으나, 풋내기 기사의 무용을 이미 목격한 터라 그 웃음을 가까스로 억눌렀다. 칼을 채워 주면서 그 갸륵한 처녀가 그에게 말했다. \"하느님께서 나리를 매우 운 좋은 기사로 만드시고, 싸움에서 승리를 내려 주시기를 비옵니다.\" 돈키호테는 그녀의 이름을 물었으니, 앞으로 자신이 받은 그 은혜를 누구에게 신세 지고 있는지 알기 위함이요, 자기 팔의 힘으로 얻을 명예의 일부를 그녀에게 나누어 줄 생각에서였다. 그녀는 더없이 겸손하게, 자신은 라 톨로사라 불리며 산초비에나야 노점에서 살던 톨레도의 한 구두장이의 딸이고, 어디에 있든 그를 자기 주인으로 섬기고 우러르겠노라고 대답했다. 이에 돈키호테는, 앞으로는 \"돈\"을 붙여 도냐 톨로사라 자칭한다면 자기에게 호의를 베푸는 것이 되리라고 답했다. 그녀가 그러겠노라 약속하자, 다른 처녀가 그에게 박차를 채워 주었는데, 칼을 채워 준 처녀와 거의 똑같은 대화가 오갔다. 그가 이름을 묻자 그녀는 라 몰리네라라고 했고, 안테케라의 점잖은 방앗간 주인의 딸이라고 했다. 돈키호테는 그녀에게도 \"돈\"을 붙여 도냐 몰리네라라 자칭해 달라고 청하며, 앞으로 더 봉사하고 호의를 베풀겠노라 약속했다.\n\n이렇게 부랴부랴 서둘러 지금껏 본 적 없는 이 의식들을 마무리 짓고 나자, 돈키호테는 말에 올라 모험을 찾아 출정하는 자기 모습을 보기 전까지는 바늘방석에 앉은 듯 안절부절못했다. 그리하여 당장 로시난테에 안장을 얹어 올라타고는, 자신을 기사로 서임해 준 그 친절에 사례하며 주인을 끌어안았으니, 어찌나 기상천외한 말로 그에게 인사를 건넸던지 도무지 그 뜻을 전하거나 옮길 길이 없을 정도였다. 주막 주인은 그를 주막에서 내보내려고, 못지않게 웅변조이나 더 짧은 말로 답했으며, 셈을 치르라고 청하지도 않은 채 잘 가라는 인사와 함께 그를 보내 주었다.\n\n# OF WHAT HAPPENED TO OUR KNIGHT WHEN HE LEFT THE INN\n\n돈키호테가 주막을 떠난 뒤 우리의 기사에게 일어난 일에 관하여\n돈키호테가 주막을 나섰을 때는 동이 터 오고 있었고, 이제 자신이 기사로 서임되었음을 깨달은 그는 어찌나 행복하고 들뜨고 기뻐했던지, 그 기쁨에 말의 뱃대끈이 터져 버릴 지경이었다. 그러나 그는 지녀야 할 필수품들에 관하여, 특히 돈과 셔츠에 관하여 주막 주인이 일러 준 충고를 떠올리고는, 집으로 돌아가 그 모든 것을 마련하고 종자도 하나 구하기로 마음먹었다. 그는 자기 이웃에 사는 농사꾼 하나를 점찍어 두고 있었으니, 그는 딸린 식구가 있는 가난한 사람이었으나 기사의 종자 노릇을 하기에는 더없이 알맞은 사람이었다. 이런 생각으로 그는 말 머리를 자기 마을 쪽으로 돌렸고, 옛 보금자리를 떠올린 로시난테는 어찌나 경쾌하게 발을 내딛던지 땅을 밟는 것 같지도 않을 정도였다.\n\n얼마 가지 않았을 때, 오른편 덤불숲에서 누군가 곤경에 빠진 듯한 가냘픈 비명 소리가 들려오는 것 같았고, 그 소리를 듣자마자 그는 외쳤다. \"내가 떠맡은 의무를 이행하고 내 야망의 결실을 거둘 기회를, 이토록 빨리 내려 주신 하늘의 은총에 감사드리노라. 이 비명은 분명 도움을 필요로 하는 어떤 남자나 여자에게서, 나의 구원과 보호가 절실한 누군가에게서 나오는 것이렷다.\" 그러고는 방향을 틀어 로시난테를 비명이 들려오는 듯한 쪽으로 몰았다. 숲속으로 몇 걸음 들어서자, 떡갈나무에 매여 있는 암말 한 마리가 보였고, 또 다른 나무에는 허리 위로 옷이 벗겨진 채 매여 있는 열다섯 살가량의 소년이 보였는데, 비명은 바로 그에게서 나오고 있었다. 그럴 만도 한 것이, 건장한 농사꾼 하나가 허리띠로 그를 매질하며 한 대 칠 때마다 꾸짖고 명령하기를 거듭하면서 \"주둥이는 닫고 눈은 똑바로 뜨렷다!\" 하고 외치는데, 소년은 \"다시는 안 그러겠어요, 주인님. 하느님의 수난을 두고 맹세코 다시는 안 그러고, 다음번엔 양 떼를 더 잘 챙길게요\" 하고 대답하는 것이었다.\n\n이 광경을 본 돈키호테는 성난 목소리로 말했다. \"무례한 기사여, 스스로를 지킬 수 없는 자를 공격하다니 그대에게 어울리지 않는 짓이로다. 말에 올라 그대의 창을 들라.\" (암말이 매여 있는 떡갈나무에 창 한 자루가 기대어 있었던 것이다.) \"그리하면 그대가 비겁자처럼 굴고 있음을 내 똑똑히 알게 해 주리라.\" 농사꾼은 자기 앞에 갑옷을 온전히 갖춰 입은 이 형상이 창을 머리 위로 휘두르는 것을 보고는 이제 죽었구나 싶어, 고분고분 대답했다. \"기사 나리, 제가 매질하고 있는 이 녀석은 제 하인입니다요. 이 근처에 둔 양 떼를 지키라고 부리는 놈인데, 어찌나 칠칠치 못한지 날마다 한 마리씩 잃어버립지요. 그 부주의함과 못된 짓을 벌하면, 제가 줘야 할 품삯을 떼먹으려고 인색하게 군다고 하니, 하느님 앞에서, 제 영혼을 걸고, 저놈이 거짓말을 하는 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"내 앞에서 거짓말이라니, 천한 촌놈 같으니!\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"우리를 비추는 저 태양을 두고 맹세컨대, 이 창으로 그대를 꿰뚫어 버리고 싶구나. 두말 말고 당장 저 아이에게 품삯을 치르라. 그러지 않으면, 우리를 다스리시는 하느님을 두고 맹세코, 그대를 끝장내고 이 자리에서 없애 버리리라. 당장 저 아이를 풀어 주라.\"\n\n농사꾼은 고개를 떨구고 한마디 말도 없이 자기 하인을 풀어 주었고, 돈키호테는 소년에게 주인이 얼마를 빚졌는지 물었다.\n\n소년은 한 달에 7레알씩 아홉 달 치라고 대답했다. 돈키호테는 그것을 합산하여 63레알이 됨을 알아내고는, 농사꾼에게 죽고 싶지 않거든 당장 그 돈을 치르라고 일렀다.\n\n벌벌 떨던 촌놈은, 자기 목숨을 걸고 또 자신이 한 맹세를 두고(실은 아무 맹세도 한 적이 없었지만) 그렇게 많지는 않다고 대답했으니, 그 까닭은 그에게 신발 세 켤레를 사 준 것을 셈에 넣어 빼야 하고, 또 그가 아팠을 때 두 번 피를 뽑아 준 값으로 1레알도 빼야 하기 때문이라는 것이었다.\n\n\"그것도 다 좋다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"하지만 신발 값과 피 뽑은 값은 그대가 아무 까닭 없이 저 아이에게 가한 매질의 상쇄로 치게 하라. 만약 저 아이가 그대가 값을 치른 신발의 가죽을 망가뜨렸다면, 그대는 저 아이 몸뚱이의 가죽을 망가뜨렸으며, 이발사가 저 아이가 아팠을 때 피를 뽑았다면, 그대는 멀쩡할 때 그 피를 뽑은 셈이니, 그 셈으로 따지면 저 아이가 그대에게 빚진 것은 아무것도 없도다.\"\n\n\"그게 곤란한 게요, 기사 나리. 지금 제겐 여기 돈이 없습니다요. 안드레스를 저와 함께 집으로 가게 해 주십쇼. 그럼 1레알씩 한 푼도 빠짐없이 다 치르겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"내가 저자랑 가다니요!\" 소년이 말했다. \"안 됩니다요, 천만에요! 안 됩니다, 나리. 절대로 안 돼요. 한번 저랑 단둘이 있게 되면, 성 바르톨로메오처럼 제 살가죽을 벗겨 버릴걸요.\"\n\n\"그자는 그런 짓을 하지 못할 것이다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 명령만 하면 그자는 내게 복종할 것이며, 그자가 자기가 받은 기사도의 이름을 걸고 내게 맹세했으니, 나는 그자를 자유로이 풀어 주고 품삯의 지불을 보증하노라.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀을 하시는지 헤아려 보십쇼, 나리.\" 소년이 말했다. \"이 제 주인은 기사가 아니고, 무슨 기사 서임을 받은 적도 없는걸요. 이 사람은 킨타나르에 사는 부자 후안 알두도란 사람입니다요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 대수더냐.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"알두도 가문에도 기사가 있을 수 있느니라. 게다가 사람은 누구나 제 행실의 자식이니라.\"\n\n\"그건 맞는 말씀입죠.\" 안드레스가 말했다. \"한데 이 제 주인은—제가 땀 흘려 일한 품삯을 안 주는 걸 보면, 대체 무슨 행실의 자식이란 말입니까요?\"\n\n\"안 주는 게 아니다, 안드레스야.\" 농사꾼이 말했다. \"제발 나랑 같이 가자꾸나. 이 세상에 있는 모든 기사도를 두고 맹세컨대, 약속한 대로 1레알씩 한 푼도 빠짐없이, 게다가 향내까지 풍기게 해서 치러 주마.\"\n\n\"향내는 됐으니 그건 면해 주마.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그저 레알로 치러 주면 나는 그것으로 만족하겠노라. 그리고 그대가 맹세한 대로 행할지어다. 그러지 않으면, 같은 맹세를 두고 내 다시 돌아와 그대를 찾아내어 벌하리라. 그대가 도마뱀보다 더 꼭꼭 숨는다 해도 내 기어이 찾아낼 것이니라. 또 그대가 이 명령을 더욱 굳게 따르도록, 그대에게 이 명령을 내리는 자가 누구인지 알고 싶거든, 알지어다, 나는 용맹한 라만차의 돈키호테이며, 그릇됨과 불의를 바로잡는 자로다. 그러니 하느님이 그대와 함께하시기를, 그리고 이미 그대에게 일러 준 그 형벌들을 명심하여 그대가 약속하고 맹세한 바를 잊지 말지어다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 그는 로시난테에 박차를 가하여 이내 손이 닿지 않을 만큼 멀어졌다. 농사꾼은 눈으로 그를 좇다가, 그가 숲을 벗어나 더는 보이지 않게 되자, 자기 아이 안드레스에게 돌아서서 말했다. \"이리 오너라, 얘야. 저 그릇됨을 바로잡는 자가 내게 명령한 대로, 내가 네게 빚진 것을 치러 주마.\"\n\n\"맹세코 말씀드리는데요.\" 안드레스가 말했다. \"그 훌륭하신 기사 나리의—천 년만큼 오래 사시기를—명령에 따르시는 게 나리한테도 현명한 일일 겁니다요. 그분은 용맹하고 공정한 재판관이시니까, 로케 성인을 두고 말하건대, 만약 제게 품삯을 안 주시면 그분이 돌아와서 말씀하신 대로 하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"나도 맹세코 그럴 테다.\" 농사꾼이 말했다. \"한데 내가 너를 어찌나 끔찍이 아끼는지, 더 많이 갚으려고 빚을 더 늘려 줘야겠구나.\" 그러고는 소년의 팔을 붙잡아 다시 묶어 놓고는, 그가 죽었나 싶을 만큼 흠씬 매질을 해 댔다.\n\n\"자, 안드레스 도련님.\" 농사꾼이 말했다. \"그릇됨을 바로잡는 자를 불러 보시지. 그자가 이건 바로잡지 못할 게다. 하긴 내가 너랑 아주 끝장을 봤는지는 모르겠다만. 네놈 살가죽을 산 채로 벗겨 버리고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같으니 말이다.\" 그러나 마침내 그는 소년을 풀어 주고는, 선고된 판결을 집행하도록 그 재판관을 찾아가 보라며 떠나게 해 주었다.\n\n안드레스는 다소 풀이 죽은 채로 떠나면서, 용맹한 라만차의 돈키호테를 찾아가 일어난 일을 그대로 일러바칠 것이며, 그러면 일곱 곱절로 다 갚아 주게 되리라고 별렀다. 그러면서도 그는 울며 떠났고, 그동안 그의 주인은 웃고 서 있었다.\n\n이렇게 용맹한 돈키호테는 그 그릇됨을 바로잡았으니, 일어난 일에 더없이 만족하고, 자신의 기사도를 매우 행복하고 고귀하게 시작했노라 여기면서, 그는 완벽한 자족감에 잠겨 자기 마을을 향한 길을 잡고는 낮은 목소리로 이렇게 읊조렸다. \"오, 더없이 아름다운 이 중에서도 가장 아름다운 엘 토보소의 둘시네아여, 그대는 오늘 스스로를 이 땅의 그 누구보다도 행복하다 일컬어도 좋으리라! 온 세상이 알다시피 어제 기사 서임을 받았으며 오늘 일찍이 불의가 꾸미고 잔혹함이 저지른 가장 큰 그릇됨과 횡포를 바로잡은, 지금도 또 앞으로도 그토록 이름 높을 라만차의 돈키호테와 같은 기사를, 그대의 온전한 뜻과 기쁨에 종속시키고 순종케 하는 운명이 그대에게 떨어졌으니 말이다. 그 기사는 오늘 저 어린 아이를 그토록 무자비하게 매질하던 잔혹한 압제자의 손에서 매를 빼앗아 버렸도다.\"\n\n이제 그는 네 갈래로 갈라지는 길에 이르렀고, 곧바로 편력 기사들이 어느 길로 갈지 헤아리려고 멈춰 서곤 하던 그 갈림길들이 떠올랐다. 그들을 흉내 내어 그는 잠시 멈추었고, 깊이 생각한 끝에 로시난테에게 고삐를 내맡겨 자기 의지를 자기 말에게 맡겼으니, 말은 처음 품었던 뜻, 곧 곧장 제 마구간으로 향하려는 뜻을 그대로 좇았다. 두 마일쯤 갔을 때 돈키호테는 한 무리의 사람들을 발견했는데, 나중에 밝혀진 바로는 무르시아에 비단을 사러 가는 톨레도의 상인들이었다. 그들은 여섯이서 양산을 받쳐 들고 오는 길이었고, 말을 탄 하인 넷과 걸어서 따라오는 노새몰이꾼 셋이 함께였다. 돈키호테는 그들을 발견하자마자 이것이 무언가 새로운 모험임이 틀림없다는 공상에 사로잡혔으니, 자신이 책에서 읽은 그 대목들을 할 수 있는 한 흉내 내는 데 보탬이 되도록 마치 일부러 마련된 듯한 모험 하나가 여기 찾아오는 듯하여, 그는 이를 시도하기로 결심했다. 그리하여 그는 위풍당당한 자세와 결의로 등자에 두 발을 굳게 디디고, 창을 가다듬고, 방패를 가슴 앞으로 끌어당기고는, 길 한복판에 버티고 서서 이 편력 기사들이—이제 그는 그들을 그렇게 여기고 또 그렇게 믿었으니—다가오기를 기다렸다. 그들이 보고 들을 수 있을 만큼 가까이 다가오자, 그는 오만한 몸짓으로 외쳤다. \"온 세상이 멈추라. 온 세상이 라만차의 여제, 비할 데 없는 엘 토보소의 둘시네아보다 더 아름다운 처녀는 온 세상에 없음을 고백하지 않는 한 말이다.\"\n\n상인들은 이런 말소리와 그 말을 내뱉는 기이한 형상을 보고 걸음을 멈추었고, 그 형상과 말투에서 단번에 그 임자가 미쳤음을 알아챘다. 그러나 그들은 자기들에게 요구되는 이 고백이 무엇을 노리는 것인지 조용히 알아보고자 했고, 그들 가운데 농담을 꽤 즐기고 매우 영민한 자 하나가 그에게 말했다. \"기사 나리, 우리는 나리가 말씀하시는 이 훌륭한 부인이 누구신지 모릅니다. 그분을 우리에게 보여 주십시오. 만약 나리가 말씀하시는 만큼 그토록 아름다우시다면, 우리는 진심으로, 아무런 강요 없이도, 나리가 우리에게 요구하시는 그 진실을 고백하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"내가 그대들에게 그분을 보여 준다면,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그토록 명백한 진실을 고백하는 데 그대들에게 무슨 공이 있겠는가? 핵심은 그분을 보지 않고도 그대들이 믿고, 고백하고, 단언하고, 맹세하고, 옹호해야 한다는 것이다. 그러지 않으면 그대들은 나와 싸움을 벌여야 하리니, 이 못돼 먹고 오만한 무리들아. 자, 기사도의 법도가 요구하는 대로 하나씩 덤비든, 아니면 너희 족속의 관습이자 비열한 버릇대로 다 함께 덤비든 하라. 나는 여기서, 내가 지키는 대의의 정당함을 믿고서, 버티고 서서 너희를 기다리노라.\"\n\n\"기사 나리,\" 상인이 대답했다. \"여기 모인 이 왕후들의 무리를 대신하여 나리께 간청드립니다. 우리가 보지도 듣지도 못한 것을, 더구나 알카리아와 에스트레마두라의 여제들과 여왕들께 그토록 큰 누가 되는 것을 고백함으로써 우리 양심에 짐을 지우는 일을 면하게 해 주시고자, 부디 이 부인의 초상 하나만이라도 우리에게 보여 주십시오. 밀알 한 톨만 한 크기라도 좋습니다. 실 한 가닥으로 실타래 전체를 알 수 있는 법이니, 이렇게 하면 우리도 흡족하고 마음이 놓일 것이며, 나리께서도 만족하고 기뻐하실 겁니다. 아니, 우리는 이미 나리와 어지간히 뜻이 맞은 듯하여, 설령 그 초상이 그분이 한쪽 눈이 멀고 다른 쪽 눈에서 주홍빛과 유황빛 진물을 흘리는 모습을 보여 준다 해도, 그래도 우리는 나리를 기쁘게 해 드리고자 나리가 바라시는 대로 그분을 칭송하는 온갖 말을 다 하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그분은 그런 것을 흘리지 않느니라, 비열한 무리들아.\" 분노로 활활 타오르며 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그런 것은 흘리지 않는다, 내 말하노니, 다만 솜에 싸인 용연향과 사향만을 풍기실 뿐이며, 한쪽 눈이 먼 것도 곱사등이도 아니요, 과다라마의 물렛가락보다 더 곧으시니라. 허나 너희는 내 여인과 같은 그런 아름다움을 모독한 죄의 값을 치러야 하리라.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 그는 창을 겨눈 채, 말을 했던 그자를 향해 어찌나 맹렬하고 사납게 돌진했던지, 만약 운수가 로시난테로 하여금 도중에 비틀거려 넘어지도록 꾸미지 않았더라면, 그 경솔한 상인은 큰 봉변을 당했을 것이다. 로시난테가 넘어지고 그 주인도 나동그라져 한참을 땅바닥에서 뒹굴었으니, 일어나려 했으나 도무지 일어날 수가 없었다. 창과 방패와 박차와 투구에, 거기다 낡은 갑옷의 무게까지 더해져 그토록 거추장스러웠던 것이다. 그러면서도 그는 일어나려 안간힘을 쓰는 내내 이렇게 외쳤다. \"달아나지 마라, 이 비겁자들, 이 비열한 놈들아! 멈춰라. 내가 여기 뻗어 있는 것은 내 잘못이 아니라 내 말의 잘못이니라.\"\n\n수행하던 노새몰이꾼 가운데 하나가, 그리 너그러운 천성이라곤 없었던 듯, 가엾게 나동그라진 사내가 이런 식으로 큰소리치는 것을 듣고는, 그 갈빗대에 한마디 대꾸를 해 주지 않고는 못 배겼다. 그래서 그에게 다가가 창을 붙잡아 산산조각 낸 다음, 그 한 도막으로 우리의 돈키호테를 어찌나 두들겨 패기 시작했던지, 갑옷을 둘렀음에도 불구하고 그를 밀가루 한 됫박처럼 빻아 놓았다. 그의 주인들은 그렇게 세게 치지 말고 내버려 두라고 소리쳤으나, 노새몰이꾼은 피가 끓어올라 남은 분이 다 풀릴 때까지는 이 놀음을 그만둘 마음이 없었으니, 부러진 창의 남은 조각들을 그러모아 가엾은 희생자에게 한바탕 쏟아붓는 것으로 마무리했다. 그러는 내내 빗발치듯 쏟아지는 몽둥이질 속에서도 그는 하늘과 땅과, 자기 눈에 산적으로 보이던 그 도적들을 향해 위협하기를 그치지 않았다. 마침내 노새몰이꾼은 지쳤고, 상인들은 흠씬 두들겨 맞은 가엾은 사내에 관한 이야깃거리를 안고 가던 길을 계속 갔다. 혼자가 된 그는 다시 한번 일어나려 애를 썼다. 그러나 멀쩡하고 성할 때도 일어나지 못했거늘, 두들겨 맞고 거의 산산조각이 나다시피 한 뒤에 어찌 일어날 수 있었겠는가? 그럼에도 그는 자신을 운 좋은 사람이라 여겼으니, 이것이야말로 편력 기사에게 으레 따르는 재난이며, 그가 생각하기에 전적으로 자기 말의 잘못이라 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 몸뚱이가 그토록 두들겨 맞은 터라, 일어나는 일은 그의 힘으로는 어림없었다.\n\n# IN WHICH THE NARRATIVE OF OUR KNIGHT’S MISHAP IS CONTINUED\n\n그리하여 그는 자신이 도무지 몸을 움직일 수 없음을 깨닫고는, 늘 쓰던 처방, 곧 자기 책 속의 어느 대목을 떠올리는 방법에 의지하기로 했으니, 그의 광기는 발도비노스와 만투아 후작에 관한 이야기, 곧 카를로토가 산기슭에 그를 상처 입은 채 버려두고 떠났던 그 이야기를 그의 머릿속에 불러왔다. 아이들이 줄줄 외우고, 젊은이들도 잊지 않았으며, 노인들이 칭송하고 더러는 믿기까지 하는 이야기였으나, 그러면서도 마호메트의 기적만큼이나 한 치도 더 진실하지는 않은 이야기였다. 이 이야기가 자신이 처한 사정에 꼭 들어맞는 듯 여겨지자, 그는 심한 고통에 시달리는 시늉을 하며 땅바닥을 뒹굴기 시작했고, 가냘픈 숨결로 숲의 상처 입은 기사가 했다는 바로 그 말을 읊조렸다.\n내 그대는 어디 있는가, 나의 여인이여,\n\n내 슬픔을 가엾이 여기지 않으니?\n그대 그것을 알지 못하는 게요, 나의 여인이여,\n\n아니면 그대 신의를 저버린 게요.\n그렇게 그는 다음 구절에 이르기까지 그 노래를 이어 갔다.\n\n오, 고귀하신 만투아 후작이시여,\n나의 숙부이시며 군주이신 분이여!\n\n마침 그가 이 구절에 다다랐을 때, 같은 마을에 사는 그의 이웃 농부 하나가 우연히 지나가게 되었으니, 밀 한 짐을 방앗간에 싣고 갔다 오던 길이었다. 그는 그 자리에 사람이 뻗어 있는 것을 보고 다가가, 누구이며 무슨 일로 그토록 구슬프게 신음하느냐고 물었다.\n\n돈키호테는 이 사람이 자기 숙부인 만투아 후작이라고 굳게 믿었던지라, 그가 한 대답이라고는 자기 노래를 계속 이어 가는 것뿐이었으니, 그 노래 속에서 그는 자신의 불운한 사연과 황제의 아들과 그 아내의 사랑 이야기를, 모두 노래가 읊는 그대로 들려주었다.\n\n농부는 이런 헛소리를 듣고 어안이 벙벙해져서, 이미 매질로 산산조각이 난 면갑을 벗겨 주고, 먼지투성이가 된 그의 얼굴을 닦아 주었다. 그러고 나자마자 그를 알아보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"키하다 나리,\" (제정신일 때, 그러니까 조용한 시골 향사에서 편력 기사로 변신하기 전에는 이렇게 불렸던 모양이다) \"대체 누가 나리를 이 지경으로 만들었답니까?\" 그러나 무엇을 물어도 상대는 그저 자기 노래만 계속할 뿐이었다.\n\n이를 본 착한 사내는 혹시 상처라도 있는지 보려고 가슴받이와 등받이를 할 수 있는 한 벗겨 보았으나, 피도, 무슨 자국 하나도 찾아낼 수 없었다. 그러고는 그를 땅에서 일으켜 세우는 데 가까스로 성공하여, 그가 보기에 가장 타기 편할 듯한 자기 당나귀에 적잖이 애를 먹으며 그를 들어 올려 태웠다. 그리고 무구들을, 부러진 창 조각까지 죄다 그러모아 로시난테에 묶고는, 로시난테는 고삐로, 당나귀는 굴레로 끌면서 마을로 가는 길로 접어들었으니, 돈키호테가 지껄이는 터무니없는 소리를 듣자니 마음이 몹시 무거웠다.\n\n돈키호테 역시 못지않게 괴로웠으니, 얻어맞고 멍든 탓에 당나귀 위에 똑바로 앉아 있을 수가 없어 이따금 하늘을 향해 한숨을 내쉬었고, 그 바람에 다시 한번 농부로 하여금 어디가 아프냐고 묻게 만들었다. 그리고 그의 모험에 들어맞는 이야기들을 그의 머릿속에 집어넣은 것은 오로지 악마 자신이었음이 분명하니, 이제 발도비노스를 잊어버리고는 무어인 아빈다라에스를, 곧 안테케라의 성주 로드리고 데 나르바에스가 그를 포로로 사로잡아 자기 성으로 끌고 갔던 그 이야기를 떠올렸기 때문이다. 그리하여 농부가 다시 그에게 어떠냐고, 어디가 아프냐고 묻자, 그는 포로가 된 아빈다라에스가 로드리고 데 나르바에스에게 했던 것과 똑같은 말과 표현으로 대답했으니, 호르헤 데 몬테마요르의 「디아나」에서 그 이야기가 쓰인 그대로 읽었던 것을 자기 처지에 어찌나 절묘하게 갖다 붙이던지, 농부는 이런 허튼소리 무더기를 들어야만 하는 자기 팔자를 저주하며 길을 갔다. 그러나 그로부터 그는 자기 이웃이 미쳤다는 결론에 이르렀고, 그래서 돈키호테의 이 장광설이 주는 진력남에서 벗어나고자 마을에 닿으려고 한껏 서둘렀다. 한데 돈키호테는 그 끝에 가서 이렇게 말했다. \"로드리고 데 나르바에스 나리, 제가 말씀드린 이 아리따운 하리파가 지금은 사랑스러운 둘시네아 델 토보소라는 것을 나리께서는 아셔야 합니다. 그분을 위하여 저는 이 세상에서 일찍이 보였고, 지금 보이고 있으며, 앞으로 보일, 또 보이게 될 가장 이름 높은 무훈을 이루었고, 이루고 있으며, 또 이룰 것입니다.\"\n\n이에 농부가 대답했다. \"나리, 이 죄 많은 놈을 좀 보십쇼! 제가 로드리고 데 나르바에스 나리도, 만투아 후작도 아니고, 나리의 이웃인 페드로 알론소이며, 나리께서도 발도비노스도 아빈다라에스도 아니고, 훌륭하신 향사 키하다 나리시라는 것이 안 보이십니까요?\"\n\n\"나는 내가 누구인지 안다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그리고 나는 내가 방금 댄 그자들뿐 아니라, 프랑스의 열두 기사 전부, 나아가 아홉 위인 전부일 수도 있다는 것을 안다. 나의 위업은 그들이 모두 합쳐 이룬 것이며 저마다 제 힘으로 이룬 것을 죄다 능가하기 때문이다.\"\n\n이런 이야기, 또 이런 종류의 이야기를 더 주고받는 사이에 그들은 막 밤이 깔리기 시작할 무렵 마을에 다다랐으나, 농부는 그 두들겨 맞은 향사가 그토록 비참한 꼴로 말을 타고 가는 모습을 들키지 않도록 좀 더 늦은 때까지 기다렸다. 그가 보기에 적당한 때가 되자 마을로 들어가 돈키호테의 집으로 갔는데, 집 안은 온통 발칵 뒤집혀 있었고, 그곳에는 돈키호테와 막역한 벗이던 신부와 마을 이발사가 와 있었다. 가정부가 그들에게 큰 소리로 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"신학사 페로 페레스 나리, 제 주인 어른께 무슨 일이 닥쳤을 것 같으십니까요?\" 신부는 그렇게 불렸던 것이다. \"어른은 물론이고 그 말이며, 둥근 방패며, 창이며, 갑옷도 코빼기조차 안 보인 지 사흘이 됐답니다. 가엾은 이 몸! 분명합니다요, 제가 죽으려고 태어났다는 것만큼이나 확실해요. 어른이 갖고 계시면서 줄곧 읽는 버릇이 든 그 빌어먹을 기사도 책들이 어른의 정신을 망쳐 놓은 거랍니다. 이제야 생각이 나는데, 어른이 혼잣말로 편력 기사가 되어 모험을 찾아 온 세상을 돌아다니겠다고 하시는 걸 여러 번 들었거든요. 라만차 천지에서 둘도 없이 멀쩡하던 그 양반의 머리를 이 꼴로 망쳐 놓은 그런 책들일랑 악마하고 바라바한테나 줘 버려요!\"\n\n조카딸도 같은 말을 했고, 게다가 한마디 더 보탰다. \"니콜라스 선생님,\" 이발사의 이름이 그러했다. \"외숙부께서는 그 망측한 재앙의 책들을 들여다보느라 꼬박 이틀 밤낮을 보내시기 일쑤였답니다. 그러고 나면 책을 내던지고 칼을 움켜쥐고는 벽을 마구 베어 대셨어요. 그러다 기진맥진하시면 탑 네 개만 한 거인 넷을 죽였노라 하셨고, 지쳐서 흘리신 땀을 두고는 싸움에서 입은 상처에서 나온 피라고 하셨지요. 그러고는 차가운 물을 한 항아리 가득 들이켜고 잠잠하고 차분해지셔서, 이 물이야말로 위대한 마법사이자 당신 벗인 현자 에스키페가 가져다준 더없이 귀한 영약이라 하셨답니다. 하지만 외숙부의 이런 변덕을 진작에 나리들께 말씀드려서, 일이 이 지경에 이르기 전에 막으시고 그 빌어먹을 책들을 죄다 태워 버리시도록 하지 못한 죄는 모두 제가 짊어지겠어요. 책이 무지 많은데, 죄다 이단자처럼 화형당해 마땅한 것들이거든요.\"\n\n\"나도 같은 말이오.\" 신부가 말했다. \"내 믿음을 걸고, 내일이 가기 전에 그 책들에 공개 재판을 내리리다. 그것들을 읽는 자들이 나의 좋은 벗이 한 것처럼 행동하게 만들지 못하도록, 부디 화형에 처해지기를.\"\n\n이 모든 것을 농부가 듣고는, 마침내 자기 이웃에게 무슨 일이 일어난 것인지 알아차렸다. 그래서 그는 큰 소리로 외치기 시작했다. \"나리들, 발도비노스 나리와 만투아 후작 나리께 문을 여시오. 후작께서 큰 상처를 입고 오시며, 또 무어인 아빈다라에스 나리께도 문을 여시오. 용맹한 안테케라 성주 로드리고 데 나르바에스가 그분을 포로로 끌고 오신답니다.\"\n\n이 말에 모두가 황급히 뛰쳐나왔고, 자신들의 벗이자 주인이자 외숙부를 알아보고는, 그가 아직 내릴 수가 없어 당나귀에서 내리지 못한 채 있는 것을 보고 그를 껴안으려고 달려갔다.\n\n\"멈추시오!\" 그가 말했다. \"내 말의 잘못으로 큰 상처를 입었으니 말이오. 나를 침상으로 옮겨 주시오. 그리고 가능하다면 현자 우르간다를 불러 내 상처를 치료하고 돌보게 해 주시오.\"\n\n\"저것 좀 보세요! 이를 어쩌나!\" 가정부가 이 말에 소리쳤다. \"어른께서 어느 발을 절며 들어오실지 제 가슴이 진작에 일러 준 게 사실이 아니던가요? 어서 침상으로 드시지요, 그 우르가다인지 뭔지를 데려오지 않고도 여기서 어른을 치료해 드릴 방도를 마련하겠습니다요. 어른을 이런 꼴로 만든 그 기사도 책들에 다시 한번, 아니 백 번이라도 저주를 퍼붓겠어요.\"\n\n그들은 곧 그를 침상으로 옮겼고, 상처를 찾아보았으나 하나도 찾을 수 없었는데, 그는 그것이 전부 땅 위에서 가장 크고 가장 대담한 거인 열 명과 싸우다가 자기 말 로시난테와 함께 호되게 넘어진 탓에 생긴 멍이라고 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 이런!\" 신부가 말했다. \"춤판에 거인까지 끼어 있단 말인가? 성호를 걸고, 내일 해가 지기 전에 그것들을 태워 버리겠소.\"\n\n그들은 돈키호테에게 숱한 질문을 퍼부었으나, 그 모든 것에 그가 내놓은 대답이라고는 — 먹을 것을 좀 주고, 잠을 자게 내버려 두라는 것뿐이었으니, 그것이야말로 그에게 가장 필요한 것이었기 때문이다. 그들은 그대로 했고, 신부는 농부에게 어떻게 돈키호테를 발견했는지 길게 캐물었다. 농부는 그에게 그 사연과, 발견했을 때와 집으로 오는 길에 그가 지껄인 헛소리를 모조리 들려주었으니, 이 모든 것이 신학사로 하여금 이튿날 그가 한 일을 더욱 하고 싶게 만들었다. 그것은 곧 그의 벗인 이발사 니콜라스 선생을 불러 함께 돈키호테의 집으로 가는 일이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI.\n\n잠은 아직 깨지 않고 있었다. 그래서 신부는 조카딸에게 그 모든 화근의 장본인인 책들이 있는 방의 열쇠를 청했고, 그녀는 기꺼이 그것을 내주었다. 모두가 안으로 들어갔는데, 가정부도 함께였다. 그들은 매우 잘 제본된 큰 책이 백 권이 넘고, 작은 책도 몇 권 있는 것을 보았다. 그 책들을 보자마자 가정부는 몸을 돌려 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다가 곧장 성수가 담긴 작은 접시와 살수기를 들고 돌아오더니 이렇게 말했다. \"여기, 나리, 학사님, 이 방에 성수를 뿌려 주세요. 이 책들 속에 그토록 많은 마법사들이 들었으니, 우리가 그들을 세상에서 쫓아내려 한다고 그 보복으로 우리를 흘려 놓지 못하도록 하나도 빠짐없이요.\"\n\n가정부의 순진함에 학사는 웃음을 터뜨리고는, 이발사에게 책을 한 권씩 건네 무엇에 관한 것인지 살펴보라고 일렀다. 그 가운데 불태움의 형벌을 받을 만하지 않은 것이 있을지도 모르기 때문이었다.\n\n\"안 돼요.\" 조카딸이 말했다. \"그중 어느 하나에도 자비를 베풀 까닭이 없어요. 죄다 화근을 일으켰으니까요. 차라리 창문 밖 안마당으로 죄다 던져 무더기로 쌓아 놓고 불을 지르는 편이 나아요. 아니면 뒤뜰로 가지고 가서, 연기가 누구도 성가시게 하지 않을 곳에서 모닥불을 피우든가요.\" 가정부도 똑같이 말했으니, 두 사람 다 그 무고한 것들을 살육하는 데 그토록 안달이 나 있었던 것이다. 그러나 신부는 적어도 제목만이라도 먼저 읽어 보지 않고서는 동의하려 하지 않았다.\n\n니콜라스 선생이 그의 손에 처음으로 쥐여 준 것은 「갈리아의 아마디스 네 권」이었다. \"이건 묘한 일이로군.\" 신부가 말했다. \"내가 듣기로 이것이 스페인에서 인쇄된 최초의 기사도 책이고, 다른 모든 책들이 여기서 그 탄생과 기원을 끌어냈다 하니 말일세. 그러니 이토록 천박한 종파의 창시자로서 이 책을 가차 없이 불길에 처해야 마땅하다고 보네.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 신부님.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"저 역시 이것이 이런 종류로 쓰인 모든 책 가운데 으뜸이라고 들었습니다. 그러니 그 분야에서 독보적인 것으로서 사면받아야 마땅하지요.\"\n\n\"옳은 말일세.\" 신부가 말했다. \"바로 그런 까닭에 당분간은 그 목숨을 살려 두기로 하지. 그 옆에 있는 저것을 좀 보세.\"\n\n\"이것은,\" 이발사가 말했다. \"「에스플란디안의 무훈」으로, 갈리아의 아마디스의 적자입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 참으로,\" 신부가 말했다. \"아비의 공덕을 자식의 셈으로 돌려서는 안 되지. 가정부, 이걸 받게. 창문을 열고 뒤뜰로 던져, 우리가 피울 모닥불의 토대로 삼게나.\"\n\n가정부는 큰 만족감으로 시키는 대로 했고, 갸륵한 「에스플란디안」은 자기를 위해 마련된 불을 한껏 인내심을 가지고 기다리고자 뒤뜰로 날아갔다.\n\n\"계속하게.\" 신부가 말했다.\n\n\"다음으로 나오는 이것은,\" 이발사가 말했다. \"「그리스의 아마디스」입니다. 그리고 정말이지, 이쪽에 있는 것들은 모조리 같은 아마디스 혈통인 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 죄다 뒤뜰로 보내게.\" 신부가 말했다. \"핀티키니에스트라 여왕과 목동 다리넬과 그의 목가, 그리고 그 작자의 도깨비 같고 뒤엉킨 잡소리들을 불태우는 일이라면, 나를 낳아 주신 아버지라 해도 편력 기사 차림으로 돌아다닌다면 함께 불태워 버리겠네.\"\n\n\"저도 같은 생각입니다.\" 이발사가 말했다.\n\n\"저도 그렇고요.\" 조카딸이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 가정부가 말했다. \"자, 뒤뜰로 던져 버려요!\"\n\n책들이 그녀에게 건네졌고, 그 수가 워낙 많았던지라 그녀는 계단을 오르내리는 수고를 덜고자 창문 밖으로 내던졌다.\n\n\"저기 저 통짜배기는 뭔가?\" 신부가 말했다.\n\n\"이것은,\" 이발사가 말했다. \"「라우라의 돈 올리반테」입니다.\"\n\n\"그 책의 작자는,\" 신부가 말했다. \"「꽃들의 정원」을 쓴 바로 그 사람일세. 그런데 정말이지 두 책 가운데 어느 쪽이 더 진실한지, 아니 더 정확히 말해 덜 거짓되었는지 가릴 수가 없네. 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤 허풍쟁이 바보로서 이걸 뒤뜰로 보내라는 것뿐이야.\"\n\n\"다음에 나오는 이것은 「히르카니아의 플로리스마르테」입니다.\" 이발사가 말했다.\n\n\"플로리스마르테 님께서 여기 계셨군?\" 신부가 말했다. \"그렇다면 단언컨대 그 경이로운 출생과 환상적인 모험에도 불구하고 뒤뜰에 거처를 잡아야겠네. 그 문체의 뻣뻣함과 메마름은 그 외에 아무것도 받을 자격이 없으니 말일세. 가정부, 이것도 저것과 함께 뒤뜰로 보내게.\"\n\n\"기꺼이요, 나리.\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하고는 큰 기쁨으로 명을 받들었다.\n\n\"이것은,\" 이발사가 말했다. \"「기사 플라티르」입니다.\"\n\n\"오래된 책이로군.\" 신부가 말했다. \"허나 자비를 베풀 까닭이 보이질 않네. 두말없이 다른 것들 뒤를 따라 보내게.\" 그리하여 그대로 되었다.\n\n또 다른 책이 펼쳐졌고, 그들은 그것의 제목이 「십자가의 기사」임을 보았다.\n\n\"이 책이 지닌 그 거룩한 이름을 보아,\" 신부가 말했다. \"그 무지를 용서해 줄 법도 하네. 하지만 사람들 말마따나 '십자가 뒤에 악마가 있다'고 하지 않던가. 불 속으로 던져 버리게.\"\n\n또 다른 책을 집어 들고 이발사가 말했다. \"이것은 「기사도의 거울」입니다.\"\n\n\"그 나리는 내가 잘 알지.\" 신부가 말했다. \"거기에 몬탈반의 레이날도스 님이 등장하시는데, 그 친구들 패거리와 더불어 카쿠스보다 더한 도둑들이지. 또 프랑스의 십이 기사들과 진실한 역사가 투르핀도 나오고. 하지만 나는 이들을 영구 추방 이상으로 단죄할 생각은 없네. 어쨌든 이들은 저 유명한 마테오 보이아르도의 창작에 얼마간 몫이 있고, 거기서 또 기독교 시인 루도비코 아리오스토가 자기 베틀로 천을 짰으니 말일세. 만일 그자가 여기 있고 자기 언어가 아닌 다른 언어를 지껄인다면 나는 그에게 어떤 존경도 표하지 않겠네. 허나 자기 모국어로 말한다면 내 머리 위에 떠받들겠네.\"\n\n\"음, 저는 그걸 이탈리아어로 가지고 있습니다만,\" 이발사가 말했다. \"이해하지는 못합니다.\"\n\n\"자네가 그걸 이해한다면 그게 더 좋지 않은 일이지.\" 신부가 말했다. \"그 점에서는, 만일 그 대위가 그를 스페인으로 데려와 카스티야어로 바꿔 놓지만 않았더라면 그를 용서해 줄 수도 있었을 걸세. 그는 그에게서 그 타고난 힘을 상당 부분 앗아 갔으니, 운문으로 쓰인 책을 다른 언어로 옮기려 드는 자들이 죄다 그러하네. 아무리 공을 들이고 아무리 재주를 부린들, 그들은 결코 원작이 처음 빚어졌던 그 수준에는 이르지 못하니 말일세. 요컨대 내가 하는 말은, 이 책과 저 프랑스 사정을 다룬 책으로 발견될 모든 것들은, 더 숙고한 끝에 그것들을 어찌할지 정해지기까지 어느 마른 우물에 던져 넣거나 보관해 두어야 한다는 걸세. 단, 떠돌아다니는 「베르나르도 델 카르피오」 한 권과 「론세스바예스」라 불리는 또 한 권은 언제나 예외로 두지. 이것들이 내 손에 들어온다면 즉시 가정부의 손으로 넘어갈 것이요, 거기서 다시 한 치의 유예도 없이 불 속으로 들어갈 테니 말일세.\"\n\n이 모든 것에 이발사는 동의하며 그것을 옳고 마땅한 일로 여겼으니, 신부가 신앙에 그토록 충실하고 진리에 그토록 충직하여 결코 그것들에 어긋나는 말은 한마디도 하지 않으리라 확신했기 때문이다. 또 다른 책을 펼치고 그는 그것이 「올리바의 팔메린」임을 보았고, 그 옆에는 「잉글랜드의 팔메린」이라 불리는 또 다른 책이 있었는데, 이를 보고 학사가 말했다. \"그 올리바는 당장 장작으로 만들어 재 한 톨 남지 않을 때까지 불태우게. 그리고 저 잉글랜드의 팔메린은 독보적인 것으로서 간직하고 보존하되, 알렉산드로스가 다리우스의 전리품 가운데서 찾아내어 시인 호메로스의 작품을 안전히 보관하려고 따로 챙겨 둔 그런 함과 같은 또 하나의 함을 만들어 주게나. 친구, 이 책은 두 가지 이유로 권위가 있다네. 첫째는 매우 훌륭하기 때문이요, 둘째는 포르투갈의 어느 지혜롭고 재치 있는 왕이 썼다고 전해지기 때문이지. 미라과르다 성에서 벌어지는 모든 모험은 빼어나고 감탄스러운 솜씨로 짜여 있으며, 언어는 세련되고 명료하여, 적절함과 분별을 가지고 화자에게 어울리는 문체를 연구하고 지키고 있네. 그러니, 니콜라스 선생, 자네에게 좋게 여겨진다면, 나는 이 책과 「갈리아의 아마디스」는 불태우는 형벌을 면해 주고, 나머지 전부는 더 묻거나 따질 것도 없이 멸하자고 말하겠네.\"\n\n\"아니요, 친구.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"여기 제가 들고 있는 이건 저 유명한 「돈 벨리아니스」니까요.\"\n\n\"음,\" 신부가 말했다. \"그것과 그 제2부, 제3부, 제4부는 모조리 넘치는 담즙을 씻어 내릴 대황 약간이 필요하고, 「명성의 성」에 관한 그 모든 군더더기와 다른 더 큰 허세들을 말끔히 도려내야 하네. 그러기 위해 그것들에게 해외 추방의 유예를 허락해 주고, 그것들이 고쳐지는 정도에 따라 자비를 베풀든 정의를 내리든 하기로 하지. 그동안은, 친구, 자네 집에 그것들을 두고 누구도 읽지 못하게 하게나.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요.\" 이발사가 말했다. 그러고는 기사도 책을 더 읽느라 지치고 싶지 않았던 그는 가정부에게 큰 책들을 전부 가져다 뒤뜰로 던져 버리라고 일렀다. 그 말은 둔하거나 귀먹은 사람에게 한 것이 아니라, 세상에서 가장 넓고 고운 천을 짜는 일보다 그것들을 불태우는 일을 더 즐기는 사람에게 한 것이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 한 번에 여덟 권쯤 움켜쥐고는 창문 밖으로 내던졌다.\n\n그토록 많은 책을 한꺼번에 나르다가 그녀는 한 권을 이발사의 발치에 떨어뜨렸는데, 그는 그것이 누구의 것인지 궁금하여 집어 들었고, 거기에 「유명한 기사 티란테 엘 블랑코의 이야기」라 적힌 것을 발견했다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 신부가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"「티란테 엘 블랑코」가 여기 있다니! 친구, 그걸 이리 건네주게. 그 안에서 나는 즐거움의 보물 창고와 오락의 광맥을 찾았다고 보네. 여기 용맹한 기사 몬탈반의 돈 키리엘레이손이 있고, 그의 동생 몬탈반의 토마스가, 그리고 기사 폰세카가 있네. 또 대담한 티란테가 마스티프와 벌인 싸움이며, 처녀 플라세르데미비다의 재담이며, 과부 레포사다의 사랑과 술책이며, 종자 이폴리토에게 반한 황후까지 — 정말이지, 친구, 그 문체의 권위로 따지면 이건 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 책일세. 여기서는 기사들이 먹고 자며, 자기 침대에서 죽고, 죽기 전에 유언장을 작성하니, 다른 모든 책에는 전혀 없는 것들이 이 밖에도 수두룩하다네. 그렇지만 나는 말하네, 이런 어리석은 짓들을 작정하고 지어낸 자는 평생 갤리선으로 보내져 마땅하다고. 집에 가지고 가서 읽어 보게나, 내가 한 말이 사실임을 알게 될 테니.\"\n\n\"분부대로 하지요.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"그런데 여기 남은 이 작은 책들은 어찌할까요?\"\n\n\"이것들은 기사도가 아니라 시가가 틀림없네.\" 신부가 말했다. 그러고는 한 권을 펼치니 그것이 호르헤 데 몬테마요르의 「디아나」임을 알아보았고, 나머지도 모두 같은 종류이리라 짐작하며 이렇게 말했다. \"이것들은 다른 것들처럼 불태울 자격이 없네. 누구도 해칠 수 없는 오락거리 책들이니, 기사도 책들이 끼친 화근을 이것들은 끼치지도 않거니와 끼칠 수도 없거든.\"\n\n\"아, 신부님!\" 조카딸이 말했다. \"이것들도 다른 것들과 함께 불태우라 명하시는 편이 나을 거예요. 삼촌이 그 기사도 병에서 나으신 뒤에 이것들을 읽고는 목동이 되어 노래하고 피리 불며 숲과 들을 떠돌고 싶어 하셔도 이상할 게 없을 테니까요. 아니면 더 나쁘게는 시인이 되고 싶어 하실 수도 있는데, 사람들 말로는 그게 불치이자 전염되는 병이라잖아요.\"\n\n\"이 처녀의 말이 옳네.\" 신부가 말했다. \"우리 친구의 앞길에서 이 걸림돌이자 유혹을 치워 두는 게 좋겠어. 그럼 우선 몬테마요르의 「디아나」부터 시작하지. 내 생각에 이건 불태울 게 아니라, 현자 펠리시아와 마법의 물에 관한 것 전부와, 거의 모든 긴 운문 대목을 깨끗이 도려내야 하네. 그러니 그 산문과, 이런 종류의 책들 가운데 첫째라는 영예는 부디 간직하게 두지.\"\n\n\"다음에 나오는 이것은,\" 이발사가 말했다. \"「디아나」인데, 「살라망카 사람이 쓴 제2부」라는 제목이고, 이 다른 것도 같은 제목인데 작자는 힐 폴로입니다.\"\n\n\"그 살라망카 사람의 것으로 말하자면,\" 신부가 답했다. \"뒤뜰에서 단죄받은 무리의 수를 불리러 가게 하고, 힐 폴로의 것은 마치 아폴론 자신에게서 나온 양 보존하게. 하지만 친구, 어서 서두르게나, 시간이 늦어지고 있으니.\"\n\n\"이 책은,\" 이발사가 또 다른 책을 펼치며 말했다. \"사르데냐의 시인 안토니오 데 로프라소가 쓴 「사랑의 행운」 열 권입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 받은 성직에 걸고 말하건대,\" 신부가 말했다. \"아폴론이 아폴론이 된 이래, 뮤즈들이 뮤즈가 된 이래, 시인들이 시인이 된 이래, 이토록 우스꽝스럽고 터무니없는 책은 일찍이 쓰인 적이 없네. 그 나름으로 이것은 지금껏 나온 이런 부류 가운데 으뜸이자 가장 독보적인 것이며, 이걸 읽어 보지 않은 자는 진정 유쾌한 것을 읽어 본 적이 없다고 단언해도 좋네. 이리 주게, 친구. 나는 피렌체산 천으로 지은 사제복을 받은 것보다 이걸 찾아낸 것을 더 값지게 여긴다네.\"\n\n그는 더할 나위 없는 만족감으로 그것을 따로 챙겨 두었고, 이발사는 계속했다. \"다음에 나오는 이것들은 「이베리아의 목동」과 「에나레스의 님프들」과 「질투의 깨우침」입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 우리가 할 일이라곤,\" 신부가 말했다. \"그것들을 가정부의 세속적 처형의 팔에 넘기는 것뿐일세. 왜냐고는 묻지 말게, 안 그러면 우린 결코 끝을 보지 못할 테니.\"\n\n\"이다음 것은 「필리다의 목동」입니다.\"\n\n\"그건 목동이 아닐세.\" 신부가 말했다. \"고도로 세련된 궁정인이지. 귀한 보석처럼 보존하게.\"\n\n\"여기 이 큰 것은,\" 이발사가 말했다. \"「갖가지 시가의 보고」라 불립니다.\"\n\n\"수가 그렇게 많지만 않았더라면,\" 신부가 말했다. \"더 음미되었을 텐데. 이 책은 그 빼어남과 더불어 지닌 어떤 저속한 대목들을 솎아 내고 깨끗이 해야 하네. 작자가 내 벗이고, 그가 쓴 다른 더 영웅적이고 더 고매한 작품들을 존중하는 뜻에서 보존하기로 하지.\"\n\n\"이것은,\" 이발사가 이어 말했다. \"로페스 데 말도나도의 「가요집」입니다.\"\n\n\"그 책의 작자 또한,\" 신부가 말했다. \"나의 절친한 벗이라네. 그의 입에서 직접 나오는 시구들은 듣는 모든 이의 감탄을 자아내니, 그 목소리가 어찌나 감미로운지 노래로 읊을 때면 듣는 이를 홀려 버린다네. 목가가 좀 지나치게 많기는 하나, 좋은 것이 풍성했던 적은 일찍이 없으니. 따로 떼어 둔 것들과 함께 간직하게. 그런데 그 옆의 저 책은 무엇인가?\"\n\n\"미겔 데 세르반테스의 「갈라테아」입니다.\" 이발사가 말했다.\n\n\"그 세르반테스는 여러 해 동안 나의 절친한 벗이었네. 그런데 내가 알기로 그는 시구를 짓는 일보다 역경을 겪는 일에 더 경험이 많지. 그의 책에는 좋은 창작이 얼마간 들어 있어, 우리에게 무언가를 내놓기는 하나 어떤 것도 결말에 이르게 하지는 못하네. 그것이 약속한 제2부를 우리는 기다려야 하지. 어쩌면 손질을 거치면, 지금은 거부당하고 있는 그 온전한 은총을 마침내 얻어 낼지도 모르겠네. 그러니 그동안은, 친구, 그것을 자네 거처에 가두어 두게나.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"그리고 여기 세 권이 함께 나오는군요. 돈 알론소 데 에르시야의 「라 아라우카나」, 코르도바의 법관 후안 루포의 「라 아우스트리아다」, 그리고 발렌시아의 시인 크리스토발 데 비루에스의 「엘 몬세라테」입니다.\"\n\n\"이 세 책은,\" 신부가 말했다. \"카스티야어로 영웅 운율로 쓰인 것들 가운데 으뜸이며, 이탈리아의 가장 유명한 것들과도 견줄 만하네. 스페인이 지닌 가장 풍요로운 시가의 보물로서 보존하게.\"\n\n신부는 지친 데다 더는 어떤 책도 들여다보고 싶지 않았기에, \"내용 미확인\"으로 나머지는 모조리 불태우기로 정했다. 그런데 바로 그때 이발사가 한 권을 펼쳐 들었으니, 「안젤리카의 눈물」이라 불리는 책이었다.\n\n\"내가 그 책을 불태우라 명했더라면,\" 제목을 듣고 신부가 말했다. \"나 자신이 눈물을 흘렸을 걸세. 그 작자는 스페인은 말할 것도 없고 세계에서 손꼽히는 시인 중 하나였고, 오비디우스의 우화 몇 편을 옮기는 데 매우 뛰어났으니 말일세.\"\n\n# CHAPTER VII.\n\n이 순간 돈키호테가 고함을 지르기 시작했다. “이리로, 이리로 오시오, 용맹한 기사들이여! 그대들의 억센 팔의 힘을 떨쳐야 할 때가 바로 지금이오. 궁정의 무리가 마상 시합에서 승기를 잡아 가고 있단 말이오!” 이 소란과 외침에 불려 가느라 그들은 남은 책들의 검열을 더 진행하지 못했고, 그리하여 돈 루이스 데 아빌라가 쓴 「카롤레아」와 「스페인의 사자」와 「황제의 업적」이 보이지도 들리지도 않은 채 불 속으로 들어간 것이라 여겨진다. 의심할 바 없이 그것들은 남아 있던 책들 가운데 있었을 터이며, 어쩌면 신부가 그것들을 보았더라면 그토록 가혹한 선고를 받지는 않았으리라.\n\n그들이 돈키호테에게 다다랐을 때 그는 이미 침대에서 나와 여전히 고함치고 헛소리를 지르며 사방을 베고 찌르고 있었으니, 한숨도 잔 적이 없는 사람처럼 말짱하게 깨어 있었다.\n\n그들은 그를 붙들고 억지로 다시 침대에 눕혔고, 그가 조금 진정되었을 때 그가 신부를 향해 말했다. “참으로, 투르핀 대주교 예하, 우리 십이 기사라 자처하는 이들이 마상 시합에서 궁정의 기사들에게 승리를 그토록 무심히 내주다니 이는 우리에게 크나큰 수치입니다. 앞선 사흘 동안은 우리 모험 기사들이 영예를 차지했는데도 말입니다.”\n\n“쉿, 친구.” 신부가 말했다. “하느님 뜻이라면 운수가 돌아설 것이고, 오늘 잃은 것을 내일 되찾을 수도 있을 것이오. 당장은 그대의 몸이나 돌보시오. 심하게 다치지는 않았더라도 너무 지친 듯이 보이니 말이오.”\n\n“다치다니, 아니오.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “하나 멍들고 두들겨 맞은 것은 의심할 바 없소. 그 후레자식 돈 롤단이 떡갈나무 둥치로 나를 후려쳤으니 말이오. 모두 시기심에서 비롯된 짓이오. 그가 이룬 업적에서 나 홀로 그와 어깨를 겨룬다는 걸 보았기 때문이지. 그러나 내가 이 침대에서 일어나는 즉시 그의 온갖 마법에도 불구하고 그가 그 대가를 치르게 하지 못한다면, 나는 나를 몬탈반의 레이날도스라 부르지 않겠소. 당장은 먹을 것이나 좀 가져다주시오. 그것이 지금 내게 가장 요긴한 것이라 느껴지니 말이오. 복수하는 일은 내게 맡겨 두시오.”\n\n그들은 그가 바라는 대로 했다. 먹을 것을 가져다주었고, 그는 다시 한번 잠이 들었으며, 그들은 그의 광기에 놀라움을 금치 못한 채 자리를 떴다.\n\n그날 밤 가정부는 마당에 있던 책과 온 집 안에 있던 책을 모조리 태워 재로 만들었다. 그 가운데는 영원한 기록 보관소에 길이 간직될 만한 책들도 분명 타 버렸을 터이나, 그것들의 운명과 검열자의 게으름이 그것을 허락하지 않았으니, 죄지은 자 때문에 죄 없는 자가 고초를 겪는다는 속담이 그것들에게서 그대로 입증된 셈이었다.\n\n신부와 이발사가 벗의 병에 즉각 적용한 처방 가운데 하나는 책들이 있던 방을 벽으로 막고 회칠을 해 버리는 것이었다. 그가 일어났을 때 그 방을 찾지 못하게 하려는 것으로(어쩌면 원인이 사라지면 결과도 그치리라 여긴 것이리라), 어느 마법사가 방째로 책을 죄다 가져가 버렸다고 둘러댈 셈이었다. 이 일은 한껏 서둘러 이루어졌다. 이틀 뒤 돈키호테가 일어나 맨 먼저 한 일은 자기 책들을 보러 가는 것이었으나, 두고 갔던 그 방을 찾지 못하자 그는 이리저리 헤매며 방을 찾아다녔다. 문이 있던 자리에 이르러 그는 손으로 더듬어 보고, 한마디 말도 없이 눈을 사방으로 굴리고 또 굴려 보았다. 그러나 한참 뒤에야 그는 자기 책들이 들어 있던 방이 어디쯤이냐고 가정부에게 물었다.\n\n무어라 대답할지 이미 단단히 일러 받은 가정부가 말했다. “나리께서 찾으시는 게 무슨 방이고 또 무슨 아무것도 아닌 것이옵니까? 이 집에는 이제 방도 책도 없습죠. 악마가 직접 와서 죄다 가져가 버렸으니까요.”\n\n“악마가 아니었어요.” 조카딸이 말했다. “마법사였답니다. 외삼촌이 이곳을 떠나신 다음 날 밤에 구름을 타고 와서, 타고 온 뱀에서 내리더니 그 방으로 들어갔어요. 거기서 무슨 짓을 했는지는 모르겠지만, 잠시 후에 지붕을 뚫고 날아 달아나면서 집 안을 온통 연기로 가득 채워 놓았답니다. 그가 무슨 짓을 했나 보러 갔더니 책도 방도 보이지 않았어요. 다만 가정부와 저는 똑똑히 기억해요. 그 늙은 악당이 떠나면서 큰 소리로 말하기를, 책과 방의 주인에게 사사로운 원한이 있어 이 집에 해코지를 해 두었으니 머지않아 드러나리라고 했어요. 또 제 이름이 현자 무나톤이라고도 했답니다.”\n\n“프리스톤이라고 했을 게다.” 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n“프리스톤이라 했는지 프리톤이라 했는지는 모르겠어요.” 가정부가 말했다. “그저 이름이 ‘톤’으로 끝났다는 것만 알 뿐이지요.”\n\n“그렇지.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “그자는 현자 마법사이며 나의 큰 원수다. 그자는 제 술법과 학식으로, 머지않아 내가 자기가 비호하는 어느 기사와 일대일로 맞붙어 그를 무찌르게 되리라는 것을, 그리고 그것을 자기가 막을 수 없으리라는 것을 알기에 나에게 앙심을 품고 있는 것이다. 이런 까닭에 그자는 할 수 있는 온갖 해코지를 내게 하려 드는 것이지. 그러나 하늘이 정한 바를 거스르거나 피하기란 그자에게 쉽지 않으리라는 것을 내 장담하마.”\n\n“누가 그걸 의심하겠어요?” 조카딸이 말했다. “하지만 외삼촌, 누가 외삼촌을 이런 싸움에 끌어들였답니까? 밀에서 난 적도 없는 더 좋은 빵을 찾아 세상을 떠돌아다니느니 차라리 외삼촌 댁에서 편안히 지내시는 게 낫지 않겠어요? 양털을 구하러 갔다가 제 털까지 깎이고 돌아오는 이가 한둘이 아니라는 걸 통 헤아리지 못하시고요.”\n\n“오, 내 조카딸아.” 돈키호테가 대답했다. “네 셈은 어찌 그리도 빗나갔느냐. 그들이 내 털을 깎기 전에, 내 머리카락 한 올의 끄트머리라도 감히 건드리려는 자들의 수염을 죄다 쥐어뜯어 송두리째 벗겨 놓을 것이니라.”\n\n두 사람은 그의 노여움이 타오르는 것을 보고 더는 아무 대꾸도 하려 들지 않았다.\n\n요컨대 그는 보름 동안 집에 머물며 매우 조용히 지냈으니, 예전의 망상을 다시 붙들고 싶어 하는 기색이라고는 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 그동안 그는 두 친구인 신부와 이발사와 더불어, 자기가 주장하는 한 가지 논점을 두고 활기찬 토론을 벌였다. 곧 세상이 가장 절실히 필요로 하는 것이 편력 기사이며, 편력 기사도의 부흥이 바로 자기 안에서 이루어지리라는 것이었다. 신부는 때로는 그를 반박하고 때로는 그에게 동조했으니, 이렇게 조심하지 않았더라면 그를 도무지 이치로 돌이킬 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n그러는 사이 돈키호테는 이웃에 사는 한 농사꾼을 구슬렸다. 그는 정직한 사람이었으나(가난한 사람에게 그런 칭호를 붙일 수 있다면 말이다), 머릿속에 든 재치라고는 거의 없는 자였다. 한마디로 돈키호테는 어찌나 잘 구슬리고 어찌나 그럴듯한 설득과 약속을 늘어놓았던지, 그 가엾은 촌뜨기는 마침내 그와 함께 길을 나서 종자로 섬기기로 마음먹었다. 무엇보다 돈키호테는, 어느 순간 눈 깜짝할 사이에 섬 하나를 손에 넣어 그를 그 섬의 영주로 앉혀 줄 만한 모험이 닥칠 수 있으니 기꺼이 따라나설 채비를 해 두라고 일러 주었다. 이런저런 약속에 솔깃하여 산초 판사는(그 농사꾼은 그렇게 불렸다) 처자식을 두고 이웃 사람의 종자가 되기로 한 것이다.\n\n다음으로 돈키호테는 약간의 돈을 마련하는 일에 착수했다. 이것저것 팔고 또 이것저것 저당 잡히면서 매번 손해 보는 거래를 한 끝에 그는 그럭저럭 적잖은 돈을 모았다. 그는 한 벗에게 꾸어 얻은 둥근 방패를 마련했고, 망가진 투구를 할 수 있는 데까지 손본 다음, 종자 산초에게 자기가 떠나려는 날과 시각을 일러 주며 가장 요긴하다 여기는 것을 챙겨 두라고 당부했다. 무엇보다 그는 산초에게 안장주머니를 챙겨 오라고 단단히 일렀다. 산초는 그러겠노라 했고, 자기가 가진 아주 좋은 당나귀도 데려갈 작정이라 했으니, 걸어 다니는 데에는 그리 익숙하지 않았기 때문이다. 당나귀 일을 두고 돈키호테는 잠시 망설이며, 종자를 당나귀에 태워 데리고 다닌 편력 기사가 있었던지 떠올려 보려 했으나 기억나는 사례라고는 하나도 없었다. 그럼에도 그는 산초를 데려가기로 마음먹었으니, 기회가 닿는 대로 처음 마주치는 무례한 기사의 말을 빼앗아 더 명예로운 탈것을 마련해 줄 작정이었던 것이다. 자신을 위해서는 셔츠 몇 벌과 그 밖에 할 수 있는 만큼의 것들을, 객줏집 주인이 일러 준 조언에 따라 챙겼다. 이 모든 일을 마치고서, 산초 판사는 처자식에게, 돈키호테는 가정부와 조카딸에게 작별 인사도 없이, 어느 날 밤 마을에서 아무에게도 들키지 않고 길을 나섰다. 그날 밤 그들은 어찌나 잘 나아갔던지, 날이 밝을 무렵에는 행여 누가 자기들을 찾아 나선다 해도 들킬 염려가 없으리라 여겼다.\n\n산초는 안장주머니와 술 부대를 챙긴 채 마치 족장이라도 된 양 당나귀를 타고 갔으니, 주인이 약속한 그 섬의 영주가 된 자기 모습을 어서 보고 싶어 안달이었다. 돈키호테는 첫 여행에서 지나갔던 그 길, 곧 몬티엘 들판을 가로지르는 길을 그대로 택하기로 했는데, 지난번보다는 한결 덜 고생스럽게 지나갔으니, 이른 아침이라 햇살이 비스듬히 내리쬐어 더위가 그들을 괴롭히지 않았던 까닭이다.\n\n이때 산초 판사가 주인에게 말했다. “편력 기사 나리, 제게 약속하신 그 섬을 잊지 않도록 잘 챙기셔야 합니다요. 아무리 크다 해도 저는 그걸 다스릴 만하니까요.”\n\n이에 돈키호테가 대답했다. “벗 산초 판사여, 알아 두어라. 옛 편력 기사들은 자기가 얻은 섬이나 왕국의 영주로 종자를 앉히는 관행을 무척이나 즐겨 따랐으니, 나는 이토록 후한 관습에서 내 몫을 결코 소홀히 하지 않으리라 마음먹었다. 오히려 나는 그것을 한층 낫게 만들 작정이다. 그들은 더러, 아니 어쩌면 가장 흔하게는, 종자가 늙기를 기다렸다가 섬김에 진력나고 고된 낮과 더 고된 밤을 실컷 겪은 뒤에야 비로소 백작이라느니 기껏해야 후작이라느니 하는 칭호를, 그것도 어느 골짜기나 변변찮은 지방의 칭호를 내려 주었다. 그러나 네가 살아 있고 나도 살아 있다면, 엿새가 채 지나기 전에 다른 여러 왕국을 거느린 어느 왕국을 손에 넣을 수도 있을 것이며, 그리되면 너를 그중 한 왕국의 왕으로 즉위시키기에 안성맞춤일 것이다. 이를 두고 놀라워할 것 없으니, 이런 기사들에게는 사물과 기회가 유례없고 뜻밖의 방식으로 굴러들어 오기에, 내가 네게 약속한 것보다 더한 것조차 손쉽게 내려 줄 수 있을 것이다.”\n\n“그렇다면야.” 산초 판사가 말했다. “나리께서 말씀하시는 그런 기적 가운데 하나로 제가 왕이 된다면, 제 마누라 후아나 구티에레스도 왕비가 되고 제 자식들은 왕자가 되겠습니다요.”\n\n“글쎄, 누가 그걸 의심하겠느냐?” 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n“제가 의심합니다요.” 산초 판사가 대답했다. “저로 말씀드리자면, 설령 하느님이 이 땅에 왕국들을 비처럼 쏟아부으신다 해도 그중 어느 하나도 마리 구티에레스의 머리에는 맞지 않으리라 굳게 믿고 있으니까요. 나리, 말씀드리는데, 그 사람은 왕비 노릇 하기엔 두 푼어치도 못 됩니다요. 백작 부인이라면 좀 더 어울리겠지요. 그것도 하느님이 도우셔야 말입니다요.”\n\n“그건 하느님께 맡겨 두어라, 산초.” 돈키호테가 대꾸했다. “그분이 그 사람에게 가장 알맞은 것을 주실 테니 말이다. 다만 너 자신을 너무 낮추어 한 지방의 영주가 되는 것보다 못한 것에 만족하는 처지에 이르지는 마라.”\n\n“그러지 않겠습니다요, 나리.” 산초가 대답했다. “더구나 저는 나리 같은 훌륭한 분을 주인으로 모시고 있으니, 나리께서 제게 알맞고 제가 감당할 수 있는 만큼을 다 베푸실 줄 아실 테니까요.”\n\n# CHAPTER VIII.\n\n용감한 돈키호테가 풍차라는 그 무시무시하고 꿈에도 생각지 못한 모험에서 거둔 행운과, 마땅히 기록해 둘 만한 그 밖의 사건들에 관하여\n\n이때 두 사람은 그 들판에 서 있는 서른 대인가 마흔 대의 풍차를 보게 되었는데, 돈키호테는 풍차를 보자마자 자기 종자에게 말했다. \"운명이 우리가 바랄 수 있었던 것보다 더 좋게 일을 마련해 주는구나. 보아라, 산초 판사 친구야, 저기에 서른 명이 넘는 괴물 같은 거인들이 모습을 드러내고 있으니, 나는 저들 모두와 싸워 죽일 작정이며, 그 전리품으로 우리는 부를 쌓기 시작할 것이다. 이것이야말로 정의로운 싸움이요, 저토록 사악한 족속을 땅 위에서 쓸어 내는 것은 하느님을 섬기는 갸륵한 일이니라.\"\n\n\"무슨 거인 말입니까?\" 산초 판사가 말했다.\n\n\"저기 네가 보는 저들 말이다.\" 주인이 대답했다. \"기다란 팔을 가진 자들로, 어떤 놈은 그 팔이 거의 이 레구아나 되느니라.\"\n\n\"보십시오, 나리.\" 산초가 말했다. \"저기 보이는 것은 거인이 아니라 풍차이고, 팔처럼 보이는 것은 바람을 받아 돌면서 맷돌을 돌리는 날개입니다.\"\n\n\"네가 이 모험이라는 일에 익숙하지 못하다는 것이 쉬이 드러나는구나.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"저것들은 거인이다. 만일 네가 두렵거든 여기서 물러나 기도나 드리고 있거라, 내가 저들과 사납고도 불공평한 싸움을 벌이는 동안 말이다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고는, 풍차이지 거인이 아니니 공격하려는 것은 분명 풍차라고 뒤에서 외치며 경고하는 종자 산초의 소리에는 아랑곳하지 않고, 그는 자기 말 로시난테에게 박차를 가했다. 그는 저것들이 거인이라고 어찌나 확신하던지, 산초의 외침도 듣지 못하고 그토록 가까이 있으면서도 그것이 무엇인지 알아보지 못한 채, \"달아나지 마라, 비겁하고 천한 것들아, 너희를 공격하는 것은 단 한 명의 기사이니라\" 하고 소리치며 그것들을 향해 달려들었다.\n\n이때 가벼운 산들바람이 일어 큰 날개들이 움직이기 시작하니, 그것을 본 돈키호테가 외쳤다. \"네놈들이 거인 브리아레오스보다 더 많은 팔을 휘두른다 해도, 나와 셈을 치러야 하리라.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고는, 온 마음을 다해 자기 여인 둘시네아에게 자신을 맡기며 이런 위험 속에서 도와 달라고 간청한 뒤, 창을 겨누고 방패로 몸을 가린 채 로시난테가 낼 수 있는 가장 빠른 속도로 돌진하여, 앞에 서 있던 첫 번째 풍차에 덤벼들었다. 그러나 그가 날개에 창끝을 박아 넣자 바람이 어찌나 세게 그것을 휘돌렸던지 창이 산산조각으로 부서지면서, 말과 기사를 함께 휩쓸어 가 버려, 둘은 가련한 꼴로 들판을 데굴데굴 굴렀다. 산초가 나귀가 갈 수 있는 한 서둘러 도우러 달려가서 다다라 보니, 그가 꼼짝도 못 하고 있었으니, 로시난테가 그와 함께 그토록 심하게 곤두박질친 까닭이었다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 산초가 말했다. \"제가 나리께 무슨 일을 하시려는지 조심하시라고, 저건 그저 풍차일 뿐이라고 말씀드리지 않았습니까? 머릿속에 그와 같은 무언가가 들어 있는 사람이 아니고서야 누구도 그것을 잘못 볼 리 없었을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"조용히 하거라, 산초 친구야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"전쟁의 운수란 다른 어떤 일보다도 자주 부침을 겪기 마련이다. 게다가 내 생각에는, 그리고 이것은 진실인데, 내 서재와 책들을 훔쳐 간 바로 그 현자 프리스톤이 이 거인들을 풍차로 둔갑시킨 것이니, 이는 내가 그들을 무찌르는 영광을 빼앗으려는 것으로, 그자가 내게 품은 적의가 그러하기 때문이다. 그러나 결국 그자의 사악한 술수도 내 좋은 검 앞에서는 별 소용이 없으리라.\"\n\n\"하느님께서 뜻대로 하시기를.\" 산초 판사가 말하고는, 그가 일어서도록 거들어 한쪽 어깨가 거의 빠진 로시난테에 다시 태웠다. 그러고 나서 방금 겪은 모험을 이야기하며 두 사람은 푸에르토 라피세로 가는 길을 따라갔으니, 거기는 큰길목인지라 풍성하고 다채로운 모험을 틀림없이 만나게 될 것이라고 돈키호테가 말했던 까닭이다. 그럼에도 그는 창을 잃은 것이 몹시 애석하여, 종자에게 그 말을 하고는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"내 어디선가 읽은 것을 기억하는데, 디에고 페레스 데 바르가스라는 이름의 한 스페인 기사가 전투에서 칼이 부러지자 떡갈나무에서 묵직한 가지 하나를 비틀어 꺾어, 그것으로 그날 어찌나 대단한 일들을 해내고 어찌나 많은 무어인을 두들겨 팼던지, 마추카라는 별명을 얻어 그와 그 후손들이 그날 이후로 바르가스 이 마추카라 불리게 되었다지. 내가 이 말을 하는 까닭은, 처음 눈에 띄는 떡갈나무에서 바로 그와 같은 가지를, 크고 튼튼한 가지를 하나 꺾을 작정이기 때문이니, 그것으로 어찌나 대단한 일들을 해낼 결심과 각오가 서 있는지, 너는 그것들을 보러 와서 그것을 볼 자격이 있다고 여겨진 것을 매우 운 좋게 여기고, 좀처럼 믿기 어려운 일들을 직접 목격한 증인이 되게 될 것이다.\"\n\n\"그야 하느님 뜻대로 되겠지요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리께서 말씀하시는 대로 저는 다 믿습니다. 하지만 몸을 좀 바로 세우십시오, 한쪽으로 온통 기울어 보이시는데, 아마 떨어지면서 흔들린 탓인가 봅니다.\"\n\n\"그것이 사실이다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그런데 내가 그 아픔을 두고 아무 불평도 하지 않는 것은, 편력 기사란 비록 창자가 상처로 비어져 나온다 해도 어떤 부상에 대해서도 불평하는 것이 허락되지 않기 때문이니라.\"\n\n\"그러시다면,\" 산초가 말했다. \"저는 더 드릴 말씀이 없습니다. 그래도 하느님은 아시겠지만, 저는 나리께서 무언가 편찮으실 때에는 차라리 불평을 하셨으면 합니다. 저로 말하자면, 고백하건대 아무리 작은 아픔이라도 불평하지 않고는 못 배기겠습니다. 다만 이 불평하지 않는 규칙이 편력 기사의 종자에게까지 미치는 것이 아니라면 말입니다.\"\n\n돈키호테는 종자의 순박함에 웃음을 참을 수 없어, 그가 원할 때면 언제든 어떤 식으로든 마음 내키는 대로 불평해도 좋다고, 이제껏 기사도의 규율에서 그와 어긋나는 어떤 것도 읽은 적이 없으니 그래도 좋다고 일러 주었다.\n\n산초가 지금이 식사 때라고 일깨우자, 주인은 자기는 지금 아무것도 먹고 싶지 않으나 너는 마음 내킬 때 먹어도 좋다고 대답했다. 이 허락을 받은 산초는 제 나귀 위에 될 수 있는 한 편안히 자리를 잡고 앉아, 안장주머니에 챙겨 넣어 두었던 것을 꺼내 천천히 우물거리며 주인 뒤를 어슬렁어슬렁 따라갔으며, 이따금 술자루를 들이켤 때면 그 맛깔스러움이라니 말라가의 가장 목마른 술집 사환이라도 부러워했을 정도였다. 그렇게 한 모금 또 한 모금 들이켜며 가는 동안, 그는 주인이 자기에게 했던 약속 따위는 하나도 떠올리지 않았고, 아무리 위험할지언정 모험을 찾아 나서는 일을 고생이라기보다 오히려 소일거리로 여겼다. 마침내 두 사람은 어느 나무들 사이에서 밤을 보냈는데, 돈키호테는 그중 한 나무에서 마른 가지 하나를 꺾어 그럭저럭 창 대신으로 삼고, 거기에 부러진 창에서 떼어 낸 창날을 박았다. 그날 밤 내내 돈키호테는 자기 여인 둘시네아를 생각하며 잠 못 이루고 누워 있었으니, 이는 기사들이 숲속과 황야에서 그 연인의 기억에 의지하여 잠 못 이루는 밤을 얼마나 많이 보냈던가를 자기 책들에서 읽은 것에 따르려는 것이었다. 산초 판사는 그렇게 밤을 보내지 않았으니, 치커리 물보다 더 독한 무언가로 배를 채운 그는 단숨에 한잠을 푹 자 버려, 만일 주인이 부르지 않았던들 얼굴에 내리쬐는 햇살도, 날이 밝아 옴을 반기는 새들의 그 모든 흥겨운 지저귐도 그를 깨울 힘이 없었을 것이다. 일어나서 그가 술자루를 시험해 보니 간밤보다 다소 덜 찬 것을 알고는 마음이 아팠으니, 그 모자람을 곧바로 메울 길에 들어선 것 같지 않았기 때문이다. 돈키호테는 아침 끼니를 들 생각이 없었으니, 앞서 말한 대로 그는 향긋한 추억만으로 영양을 삼는 데 만족했던 까닭이다.\n\n두 사람은 처음에 나섰던, 푸에르토 라피세로 이어지는 길로 되돌아왔고, 오후 세 시에 그곳이 눈에 들어왔다. \"여기서, 산초 판사 형제여,\" 그것을 보고 돈키호테가 말했다. \"우리는 이른바 모험이라는 것에 팔꿈치까지 손을 푹 담글 수 있을 것이다. 그러나 명심하거라, 설령 네가 나를 세상에서 가장 큰 위험에 빠진 것을 보더라도, 나를 지키려고 네 칼에 손을 대서는 아니 된다. 다만 나를 공격하는 자들이 천한 무리나 비천한 자들임을 알아챘을 경우에는 예외이니, 그런 경우라면 네가 나를 도와도 지극히 마땅하다. 그러나 만일 그들이 기사라면, 네가 기사로 서임받기 전에는 기사도의 법도가 네게 나를 돕는 것을 결코 허락하거나 용납하지 않느니라.\"\n\n\"지극히 당연하옵니다, 나리.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"이 일에서 나리께 온전히 따르겠습니다. 더구나 저로 말하면 본디 평화로운 사람이라 다툼이나 싸움에 끼어드는 것을 좋아하지 않으니 더욱 그렇습니다. 다만 제 한 몸을 지키는 일에 관해서라면 그 법도에 그리 마음을 두지 않겠으니, 인간의 법이든 하느님의 법이든 누구나 어떤 공격자에 맞서서든 제 몸을 지키는 것은 허락하기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"그것은 내가 인정한다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그러나 기사들에 맞서 나를 돕는 이 일에서는 네 타고난 성급함을 억눌러야 하느니라.\"\n\n\"그리하겠습니다, 약속드립니다.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"이 계율을 주일만큼이나 정성껏 지키겠습니다.\"\n\n이렇게 이야기를 나누고 있을 때 길 위에 성 베네딕트 수도회의 수사 둘이 나타났는데, 그들이 타고 있는 두 마리 노새가 어찌나 컸던지 단봉낙타라 해도 그보다 작지 않을 정도였다. 그들은 여행용 안경을 쓰고 양산을 들고 있었으며, 그 뒤로 말 탄 네댓 명과 걸어가는 노새몰이꾼 둘이 시중드는 마차 한 대가 따라왔다. 마차 안에는, 나중에 드러나기로, 세비야로 가는 한 비스카야 부인이 타고 있었으니, 그 남편이 거기서 높은 영예의 직책을 받아 서인도로 가는 배를 타려던 참이었다. 수사들은 같은 길을 가고 있기는 했으나 그 부인의 일행은 아니었다. 그러나 돈키호테는 그들을 알아보자마자 종자에게 말했다. \"내가 잘못 본 것이 아니라면, 이것은 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 이름난 모험이 될 것이다. 저기 보이는 저 검은 형체들은 틀림없이, 의심할 바 없이, 저 마차에 어떤 납치한 공주를 싣고 가는 마법사들이니, 나는 온 힘을 다해 이 잘못을 바로잡아야 하느니라.\"\n\n\"이건 풍차보다 더 고약하겠습니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"보십시오, 나리, 저들은 성 베네딕트 수도회의 수사들이고, 마차는 누가 봐도 어떤 여행자들의 것입니다. 부디 무슨 일을 하시려는지 잘 살피시고 악마에게 홀리지 마시라고 말씀드립니다.\"\n\n\"내 이미 네게 말하지 않았느냐, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"모험이라는 주제에 관해서는 네가 아는 것이 적다고 말이다. 내가 하는 말이 진실임을 곧 보게 될 것이다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고는 그가 앞으로 나아가 수사들이 오고 있는 길 한복판에 자리를 잡고 서서, 그들이 자기 말을 들을 만큼 충분히 가까이 왔다 싶자 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"악마 같고 본성을 거스르는 것들아, 너희가 이 마차에 강제로 싣고 가는 고귀한 신분의 공주들을 당장 풀어 주어라, 그러지 않으면 너희 악행에 대한 마땅한 벌로 당장 죽음을 맞을 각오를 하여라.\"\n\n수사들은 고삐를 당겨 세우고, 돈키호테의 행색에 그가 한 말까지 더해 어리둥절해하며 이렇게 대답했다. \"기사 나리, 우리는 악마도 아니고 본성을 거스르는 것들도 아니오라, 제 길을 가는 성 베네딕트 수도회의 수사 둘일 뿐이며, 이 마차에 사로잡힌 공주들이 타고 오는지 어떤지도 우리는 알지 못합니다.\"\n\n\"내게 부드러운 말은 통하지 않는다, 나는 너희를 알기 때문이다, 거짓말하는 무리들아.\" 돈키호테는 이렇게 말하고는, 대답도 기다리지 않고 로시난테에 박차를 가해 창을 겨눈 채 첫 번째 수사를 향해 어찌나 사납고 단호하게 돌진했던지, 만일 그 수사가 노새에서 몸을 던지지 않았던들 그를 본의 아니게 땅에 떨어뜨려, 죽이지는 못했더라도 심하게 부상을 입혔을 것이다. 두 번째 수사는 제 동료가 어떤 꼴을 당하는지 보고, 성채만 한 제 노새의 옆구리에 발꿈치를 박아 바람보다 빠르게 들판을 가로질러 달아났다.\n\n산초 판사는 수사가 땅바닥에 쓰러진 것을 보자 제 나귀에서 잽싸게 내려 그에게 달려가서 그 수도복을 벗기기 시작했다. 바로 그때 수사들의 노새몰이꾼들이 다가와 어찌하여 그의 옷을 벗기느냐고 물었다. 산초는 이것이 제 주인 돈키호테가 거둔 전투의 전리품으로서 자기에게 정당히 돌아오는 것이라고 그들에게 대답했다. 농담이라고는 알지도 못하고 이 전투니 전리품이니 하는 것을 도무지 이해하지 못한 노새몰이꾼들은, 돈키호테가 멀찍이 떨어져 마차의 여행자들과 이야기하고 있는 것을 보고, 산초에게 달려들어 그를 때려눕히고 수염이 한 올도 남지 않을 정도로 쥐어뜯고는 발길질로 흠씬 두들겨 패서, 숨도 못 쉬고 정신도 잃은 채 땅바닥에 뻗어 놓았다. 그러고는 더 지체하지 않고 수사가 노새에 오르도록 거들었으니, 그 수사는 부들부들 떨며 겁에 질려 새파랗게 질린 채로 안장에 올라앉기 무섭게, 멀찍이 서서 이 공격의 결과를 지켜보며 구경하고 있던 동료를 뒤쫓아 박차를 가했다. 그러고 나서 그들은 막 시작된 이 일의 끝을 기다릴 마음도 없이, 마치 등 뒤에 악마가 쫓아오기라도 하는 듯 성호를 거듭 그으며 제 갈 길을 재촉했다.\n\n돈키호테는, 앞서 말한 대로, 마차 안의 부인에게 말을 건네고 있었다. \"그대의 아름다움이여, 나의 부인이여,\" 그가 말했다. \"이제 그대의 뜻에 가장 합당한 대로 그대 자신을 처분하셔도 좋습니다, 그대를 납치한 자들의 오만이 이 내 굳센 팔로 인해 땅바닥에 엎어졌으니 말입니다. 그리고 그대를 구한 자의 이름을 애타게 알고 싶으실까 하여 알려 드리노니, 나는 라만차의 돈키호테라 불리며, 편력 기사이자 모험가요, 비할 데 없이 아름다운 여인 엘 토보소의 둘시네아에게 사로잡힌 몸입니다. 그리고 그대가 나로부터 받은 봉사의 보답으로 내가 바라는 것은 다만, 그대가 엘 토보소로 돌아가 나를 대신해 그 여인 앞에 나서서, 내가 그대를 자유롭게 하기 위해 한 일을 그분께 전해 주는 것뿐입니다.\"\n\n마차에 수행하던 종자 가운데 한 사람인 비스카야 사람이 돈키호테가 하는 말을 모두 듣고 있다가, 그가 마차를 더 가게 두지 않으려 하며 당장 엘 토보소로 돌아가야 한다고 말하는 것을 알아채고는, 그에게 달려들어 그의 창을 움켜쥐고 제 식대로 형편없는 카스티야 말과 그보다 더 형편없는 비스카야 말로 그에게 내뱉었다. \"물러가라, 기사 놈아, 너에게 화가 미칠지니. 나를 만드신 하느님께 맹세코, 마차를 놓지 않으면, 너를 죽인다 여기 비스카야 사람 있으니.\"\n\n돈키호테는 그의 말을 아주 잘 알아듣고 매우 침착하게 대답했다. \"네가 기사라면, 비록 기사도 아니다만, 나는 이미 네 어리석음과 무모함을 응징했을 것이다, 가련한 것아.\" 이에 비스카야 사람이 되받았다. \"내가 신사 아니다!—하느님께 맹세코 내가 기독교인인 만큼 네가 거짓말한다. 네가 창 떨어뜨리고 칼 뽑으면, 곧 너 고양이한테 물 길어다 주는 줄 알게 된다. 비스카야 사람 땅에서, 이달고 바다에서, 이달고 악마한테서, 그리고 봐라, 네가 달리 말하면 거짓말한다.\"\n\n\"'\"곧 보게 될 것이다, 라고 아그라헤스가 말했다.\"'\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. 그러고는 창을 땅에 내던지고 칼을 뽑아 방패를 팔에 단단히 죄고는, 그의 목숨을 거둘 작정으로 비스카야 사람에게 덤벼들었다.\n\n비스카야 사람은 그가 덤벼드는 것을 보고, 비록 제 노새에서 내리고 싶었으나—삯으로 빌려 주는 그 시원찮은 것들 가운데 하나라 도무지 믿음이 가지 않았던 까닭이다—칼을 뽑는 수밖에 도리가 없었다. 그러나 그에게 다행이었던 것은 마차 가까이 있어서, 거기서 방패 삼을 방석 하나를 낚아챌 수 있었다는 점이다. 그리하여 두 사람은 마치 불구대천의 원수라도 된 듯 서로에게 덤벼들었다. 다른 이들이 둘 사이를 화해시키려 애썼으나 그럴 수 없었으니, 비스카야 사람이 그 토막 난 말투로, 자기가 이 싸움을 끝내게 두지 않으면 제 여주인이고 자기를 막으려는 모든 자고 다 죽여 버리겠다고 선언했기 때문이다. 마차 안의 부인은 자기가 본 광경에 아연실색하고 겁에 질려, 마부에게 한쪽으로 좀 비켜서라 이르고는, 이 격렬한 싸움을 지켜보려 마음먹었는데, 그 와중에 비스카야 사람이 돈키호테의 방패 위쪽 어깨에 어찌나 세찬 일격을 내리쳤던지, 갑옷을 입지 않은 사람에게 가해졌더라면 허리까지 두 동강을 냈을 정도였다. 돈키호테는 이 엄청난 타격의 무게를 느끼고 큰 소리로 외쳐 말했다. \"오 내 영혼의 여인이여, 둘시네아여, 아름다움의 꽃이여, 그대의 아름다움에 진 의무를 다하느라 이 극한의 위험에 처한 이 그대의 기사를 도우러 오소서.\" 이 말을 하고, 칼을 들어 올리고, 방패 뒤로 몸을 잘 숨기고, 비스카야 사람에게 덤벼드는 것이 순식간의 일이었으니, 그는 단 한 번의 일격에 모든 것을 걸 작정이었던 것이다. 비스카야 사람은 그가 이렇게 덤벼드는 것을 보고 그 기세등등한 태도로 그의 용기를 확신하여 그 본을 따르기로 마음먹고는, 방석으로 몸을 잘 가린 채 그를 기다렸으니, 제 노새로는 어떤 작전도 펼칠 수 없었던 까닭이다. 그 노새는 죽도록 지친 데다 본래 이런 놀음에는 맞지 않는 짐승이라 한 발짝도 움직일 수 없었다.\n\n그리하여, 앞서 말한 대로, 돈키호테는 칼을 치켜들고 상대를 두 동강 낼 굳은 작정으로 신중한 비스카야 사람을 향해 다가갔고, 비스카야 사람 쪽에서도 칼을 손에 든 채 방석의 보호를 받으며 그를 기다렸으며, 그 자리에 있던 모든 이들은 부들부들 떨면서 곧 떨어질 듯한 그 일격의 결과를 마음 졸이며 기다렸고, 마차 안의 부인과 나머지 수행원들은 하느님께서 자기네 종자와 그들 모두를 이 처한 큰 위험에서 건져 주시기를 빌며 스페인의 모든 성상과 성소에 천 가지 서원과 봉헌을 올리고 있었다. 그러나 모든 것을 그르치는 것은, 바로 이 대목, 이 절박한 고비에서 이 이야기의 작가가 이 전투를 일촉즉발의 상태로 남겨 둔다는 점인데, 그 변명으로 내세우는 것이 돈키호테의 이 위업들에 관하여 앞서 이미 적어 놓은 것 말고는 더 적힌 것을 찾을 수 없었다는 것이다. 사실 이 작품의 두 번째 작가는 이토록 흥미로운 이야기가 망각의 선고 아래 떨어지도록 내버려졌으리라고는, 혹은 라만차의 식자들이 이 이름난 기사에 관한 어떤 문서를 그들의 문서고나 기록부에 보존해 두지 못할 만큼 그토록 분별없었으리라고는 믿고 싶지 않았다. 그리고 이런 확신이 있었기에, 그는 이 유쾌한 이야기의 결말을 찾는 일을 포기하지 않았으니, 하늘의 도움으로 그는 마침내 그것을 찾아내었고, 그 경위는 제2부에서 이야기될 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER IX.\n\n이 이야기의 제1부에서 우리는 용맹한 비스카야인과 그 이름 높은 돈키호테가 칼을 뽑아 높이 치켜든 채, 만일 그 일격이 빈틈없이 제대로 떨어졌더라면 적어도 두 사람을 머리끝에서 발끝까지 쪼개고 갈라, 석류처럼 활짝 열어젖혔을 만큼 사납게 내리치려 하는 장면에서 헤어졌다. 그런데 바로 이토록 아슬아슬한 대목에서 이 유쾌한 이야기는 뚝 멎어 버렸고, 빠진 부분을 어디서 찾아야 할지 작가의 아무런 귀띔도 없이 끊긴 채로 머물러 있었다.\n\n이 일은 나를 몹시 괴롭게 했으니, 그토록 적은 분량을 읽고 얻은 즐거움이, 이토록 흥미로운 이야기에서 빠져 있는 듯한 그 많은 부분을 찾아낼 가망이 가뭇없다는 생각에 이르자 그만 짜증으로 바뀌어 버렸기 때문이다. 이렇게 훌륭한 기사가 그 경이로운 위업을 기록하는 일을 떠맡을 어떤 현자도 두지 못했다는 것은, 내게는 있을 수 없는 일이요 모든 선례에 어긋나는 일로 여겨졌다. 그런 현자란 모험을 찾아 나섰다고들 하는 그 어느 편력 기사에게도 결코 없었던 적이 없었으니, 그들 하나하나에게는 마치 일부러 마련된 듯 한둘의 현자가 있어, 기사의 행적을 기록했을 뿐 아니라 제아무리 은밀한 것이라도 가장 사소한 생각과 어리석은 짓까지 낱낱이 묘사했던 것이다. 그러니 이토록 훌륭한 기사가 플라티르나 그와 비슷한 자들이 넘치도록 누렸던 것을 갖지 못할 만큼 불운했을 리는 없었다. 그리하여 나는 이토록 멋들어진 이야기가 동강 나고 토막 난 채로 남겨졌으리라고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었고, 만물을 집어삼키고 부수는 자, 곧 그것을 감추었거나 먹어 치웠을 시간에게 그 허물을 돌렸다.\n* 플라티르: 16세기 기사도 소설의 주인공.\n\n다른 한편으로, 그의 책들 가운데 「질투의 깨달음」이라든가 「에나레스의 님프와 목동」 같은 근래의 책들이 있었던 것을 보면, 그의 이야기 또한 근래의 것일 터이며, 글로 쓰이지는 않았더라도 그의 마을 사람들과 인근 사람들의 기억 속에 남아 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 이 생각은 나를 계속 곤혹스럽게 했고, 우리의 이름난 스페인 사람, 라만차의 돈키호테, 곧 라만차 기사도의 빛이요 거울이며, 우리 시대, 이토록 사악한 나날에 처음으로 편력 기사도의 무예를 갈고닦는 노고에 몸을 바쳐, 그릇된 것을 바로잡고 과부를 돕고, 손에 채찍을 쥐고 순결을 고스란히 지닌 채 자기 말을 타고 이 산에서 저 산으로, 이 골짜기에서 저 골짜기로 떠돌아다니던 그런 부류의 처녀들을 보호한 그의 온전한 생애와 경이로운 행적을 참되고 진실하게 알고 싶은 마음을 갈망케 했다. 옛날에는 어떤 불한당이나, 두건을 쓰고 도끼를 든 촌놈이나, 흉측한 거인이 그녀들을 억지로 범하지 않는 한, 여든 살이 되도록 단 하루도 지붕 아래서 자 본 적 없이 떠돌다가 자기를 낳아 준 어머니만큼이나 순결한 처녀로 무덤에 들어간 처녀들이 있었으니 말이다. 그러므로 나는 말하노니, 이런저런 점에서 우리의 용맹한 돈키호테는 영원하고도 두드러진 칭송을 받아 마땅하며, 이 유쾌한 이야기의 결말을 찾느라 들인 수고와 노고에 대해서는 나에게조차 그 칭송을 거두어서는 안 될 것이다. 비록 하늘과 우연과 행운이 나를 돕지 않았던들, 그것을 주의 깊게 읽는 이라면 두어 시간쯤은 너끈히 차지할 만한 이 오락과 즐거움을 세상이 빼앗긴 채로 남았으리라는 것을 나는 잘 알고 있긴 하지만 말이다. 그것을 찾아낸 경위는 이러했다.\n\n어느 날 톨레도의 알카나에 있을 때, 한 소년이 비단 상인에게 소책자 몇 권과 헌 종이를 팔러 다가왔는데, 나는 길거리에 떨어진 종잇조각까지도 읽기를 즐기는 사람인지라, 이 타고난 성향에 이끌려 소년이 팔려고 내놓은 소책자 가운데 하나를 집어 들었다. 그러자 그것이 내가 아랍 문자임을 알아본 글자로 되어 있는 것이 보였는데, 알아보기는 해도 읽을 수는 없었으므로, 그것을 읽어 줄 만한 스페인어를 하는 모리스코가 곁에 있는지 둘러보았다. 그런 통역자를 찾는 데에는 별 어려움이 없었으니, 설령 더 오래되고 더 훌륭한 언어를 다룰 사람을 구했다 해도 찾아냈을 것이다. 요컨대 우연이 내게 한 사람을 마련해 주었으니, 내가 원하는 바를 일러 주고 그 책을 그의 손에 쥐여 주자, 그는 책을 가운데쯤 펼쳐 잠시 읽더니 웃기 시작했다. 무엇 때문에 웃느냐고 물었더니, 책의 여백에 주석으로 적힌 어떤 것 때문이라고 대답했다. 그것을 내게 말해 달라고 했더니, 그는 여전히 웃으면서 말했다. \"여백에, 말씀드린 대로, 이렇게 적혀 있습니다. '이 이야기에 그토록 자주 언급되는 이 엘 토보소의 둘시네아는, 사람들 말로는, 돼지를 소금에 절이는 데에 온 라만차의 어떤 여인보다도 솜씨가 좋았다고 한다.'\"\n* 모리스코: 기독교로 개종한 무어인, 혹은 그 후손.\n\n엘 토보소의 둘시네아라는 이름을 들었을 때, 나는 놀라움과 경탄에 사로잡혔으니, 이 소책자들이 돈키호테의 이야기를 담고 있다는 생각이 단번에 떠올랐기 때문이다. 이런 생각으로 나는 그에게 첫머리를 읽어 달라고 다그쳤고, 그는 아랍어를 즉석에서 카스티야어로 옮기며 이렇게 읽어 주었으니, 그 뜻은 \"아랍 역사가 시데 아메테 베넹헬리가 쓴, 라만차의 돈키호테 이야기\"라는 것이었다. 책의 제목이 내 귀에 닿았을 때 느낀 기쁨을 감추는 데에는 적잖은 조심성이 필요했으니, 나는 그것을 비단 상인에게서 낚아채어 반 레알에 그 소년에게서 종이와 소책자를 죄다 사들였다. 만일 그가 약삭빨라서 내가 그것들을 얼마나 간절히 원하는지 알아챘더라면, 그 거래로 능히 여섯 레알이 넘는 이문을 셈할 수 있었으리라. 나는 곧장 그 모리스코와 함께 대성당 회랑으로 물러나, 돈키호테에 관한 이 소책자들을 무엇 하나 빼거나 더하지 말고 카스티야어로 옮겨 달라고 청하며, 그가 원하는 대로 삯을 치르겠노라 했다. 그는 건포도 두 아로바와 밀 두 부셸로 만족하고는, 충실하게 그리고 온 힘을 다해 신속히 옮겨 주겠노라 약속했다. 그러나 일을 한결 수월케 하고, 이토록 값진 발견물을 내 손에서 놓치지 않으려고, 나는 그를 내 집으로 데려갔으니, 거기서 그는 한 달 반 남짓 만에 여기 적힌 그대로 전부를 옮겨 냈다.\n\n* 레알, 아로바, 부셸: 당시 스페인의 화폐 및 도량형 단위.\n첫 번째 소책자에는 돈키호테와 비스카야인의 싸움이 그야말로 살아 있는 듯이 그려져 있었으니, 두 사람은 이야기가 묘사한 그대로의 자세로, 칼을 치켜든 채, 하나는 작은 방패로, 다른 하나는 방석으로 몸을 가리고 있었으며, 비스카야인의 노새는 어찌나 실물에 충실한지 화살 한 바탕 거리에서도 세낸 놈임을 알아볼 수 있을 정도였다. 비스카야인의 발밑에는 \"돈 산초 데 아스페이티아\"라는 글귀가 적혀 있었는데, 의심할 바 없이 그것이 그의 이름이었을 것이며, 로시난테의 발치에는 \"돈키호테\"라고 적힌 또 다른 글귀가 있었다. 로시난테는 어찌나 길고 야위었으며, 어찌나 비쩍 마르고 가냘프며, 등뼈가 어찌나 도드라지고 폐병이 어찌나 깊이 든 모습으로 절묘하게 그려졌는지, 로시난테라는 이름이 어떤 판단과 합당함으로 그에게 붙여졌는지를 또렷이 보여 주었다. 그 곁에는 산초 판사가 자기 당나귀의 고삐를 쥐고 있었으며, 그 발치에는 \"산초 산카스\"라고 적힌 또 하나의 표찰이 있었으니, 그림으로 보건대 그는 배가 불룩하고 몸통이 짧으며 정강이가 길었던 것이 틀림없는데, 의심할 바 없이 그래서 판사니 산카스니 하는 이름이 그에게 붙여졌을 것이다. 이야기가 그를 여러 차례 이 두 별명으로 부르고 있기 때문이다. 다른 자질구레한 세부도 몇 가지 들 수 있겠으나, 그것들은 죄다 별 중요치 않은 것들이요 이야기의 참된 서술과는 아무 상관이 없으니, 어떤 이야기든 참되기만 하다면 나쁠 수 없는 법이다.\n\n* 판사, 산카스: 각각 스페인어로 '배', '긴 정강이'를 뜻한다.\n혹여 이 이야기에 대해 그 진실성을 두고 어떤 이의가 제기된다면, 그것은 다만 그 작가가 아랍인이었다는 것뿐일 터이니, 거짓말이란 그 민족 사람들에게 매우 흔한 성벽이기 때문이다. 다만 그들이 우리의 그토록 큰 적인지라, 이야기를 옮기는 과정에서 무엇을 더하기보다는 오히려 빼먹은 것이 있으리라 짐작할 만하다. 그리고 이것이 나 자신의 의견이다. 그가 이토록 훌륭한 기사를 기리는 데에 마땅히 붓을 자유로이 놀릴 수 있고 또 그래야 할 대목에서, 내게는 그가 일부러 그것을 잠자코 지나쳐 버리는 것처럼 보이니, 이는 잘못 행한 일이요 더 잘못 꾸민 일이라 하겠다. 정확하고 진실하며 온갖 격정에서 온전히 벗어나는 것이야말로 역사가의 본분이요 의무이며, 이해관계도 두려움도, 미움도 사랑도 그들로 하여금 진실의 길에서 벗어나게 해서는 안 되기 때문이다. 진실의 어머니는 역사이니, 역사는 시간의 맞수요, 행적의 곳간이며, 과거의 증인이요, 현재의 본보기이자 조언이며, 미래에 대한 경고이다. 이 이야기 안에는 가장 즐거운 이야기에서 바랄 수 있는 모든 것이 담겨 있음을 나는 알거니와, 만일 어떤 훌륭한 자질이 모자란다면, 그것은 그 개 같은 작가의 허물이지 소재의 허물이 아니라고 나는 단언하는 바이다. 간추려 말하면, 번역에 따르건대 이 이야기의 제2부는 이렇게 시작되었다.\n\n날카로운 칼을 치켜들어 허공에 겨눈 채, 두 용맹하고 노기 띤 싸움꾼은 하늘과 땅과 지옥을 위협하며 서 있는 듯했으니, 그토록 굳은 결의와 단호함으로 몸가짐을 가다듬고 있었던 것이다. 불같은 비스카야인이 먼저 일격을 날렸는데, 어찌나 힘차고 사납게 떨어졌던지, 만일 칼이 도중에 빗나가지 않았던들 그 단 한 번의 내리침으로 이 모진 싸움도, 우리 기사의 온갖 모험도 끝장을 보기에 넉넉했을 것이다. 그러나 그를 더 큰 일들을 위해 남겨 두었던 그 행운이 적의 칼을 비껴가게 하니, 칼이 그의 왼쪽 어깨를 후려치기는 했어도, 그쪽 옆구리의 갑옷을 죄다 벗겨 내고 투구의 상당 부분을 한쪽 귀의 절반과 함께 앗아 간 것 말고는 더 큰 해를 입히지 못했고, 이 모든 것이 무시무시한 잔해가 되어 땅바닥에 떨어져 그를 가련한 몰골로 만들었을 뿐이다.\n\n오, 하느님! 우리의 라만차 사람이 자신이 이런 꼴을 당하는 것을 보았을 때 그의 가슴을 가득 채운 분노를 제대로 그려 낼 자 그 누구이겠는가? 말할 수 있는 것이라고는 다만 이러했다는 것뿐이니, 그것은 그가 다시 등자를 딛고 몸을 일으켜, 두 손으로 칼을 더욱 단단히 거머쥐고는, 어찌나 사납게 비스카야인에게 덮쳐들어 그 방석과 머리통을 정통으로 후려쳤던지 — 그토록 좋은 방패조차 무용지물임이 드러나면서 — 마치 산이 하나 그 위로 무너져 내린 것처럼, 비스카야인은 코와 입과 귀에서 피를 흘리기 시작하며, 금방이라도 노새에서 뒤로 나동그라질 듯 비틀거렸다. 만일 그가 노새의 목을 두 팔로 부둥켜안지 않았던들 의심할 바 없이 그리되었을 것이다. 그러나 동시에 그는 두 발을 등자에서 빼고 두 팔을 풀었고, 그 무시무시한 일격에 질겁한 노새는 들판을 가로질러 내달아 몇 번 펄쩍 뛰어오르더니 주인을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤다. 돈키호테는 매우 침착하게 지켜보고 서 있다가, 그가 떨어지는 것을 보자 말에서 뛰어내려 잽싸게 그에게 달려가서는, 칼끝을 그의 두 눈에 겨누며 항복하지 않으면 목을 베어 버리겠노라 명했다. 비스카야인은 어찌나 얼이 빠졌던지 한마디도 대답할 수가 없었고, 돈키호테가 그토록 눈이 멀어 있던 터라 그에게는 험한 꼴이 닥쳤을 것이었으나, 그때까지 큰 공포 속에 싸움을 지켜보던 마차 안의 귀부인들이 그가 서 있는 곳으로 황급히 다가와, 자기들 종자의 목숨을 살려 주는 크나큰 은혜와 호의를 베풀어 달라고 간곡한 청으로 애원했다. 이에 돈키호테는 자못 엄숙하고 위엄 있게 대답했다. \"참으로, 아름다운 귀부인들이여, 나는 그대들이 내게 청하는 바를 기꺼이 들어 드리겠소. 다만 한 가지 조건과 다짐이 있어야 하니, 그것은 이 기사가 엘 토보소 마을로 가서, 나를 대신하여 둘도 없는 둘시네아 아가씨 앞에 나아가, 그분이 가장 뜻에 맞으시는 대로 그를 처분하시도록 하겠노라 내게 약속하는 것이오.\"\n\n겁에 질리고 낙담한 귀부인들은, 돈키호테의 요구를 따져 보거나 둘시네아가 누구인지 묻지도 않은 채, 자기들 종자가 분부받은 모든 것을 행하겠노라 약속했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 그 약속을 믿고,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"비록 그가 내게 마땅히 받아야 할 대접이긴 하나, 나는 그에게 더는 해를 끼치지 않겠소.\"\n\n# CHAPTER X.\n\n그 무렵 산초는 수도사들을 모시던 노새몰이꾼들에게 험한 꼴을 당한 탓에 다소 성치 않은 몸을 일으켜 세우고는, 주인 돈키호테의 싸움을 지켜보며 마음속으로 하느님께 그분이 승리를 거두시기를, 그리하여 약속하신 대로 자신을 총독으로 앉혀 줄 섬 하나를 얻으시기를 빌고 있었다. 그리하여 싸움이 이제 끝나고 주인이 로시난테에 다시 오르려고 돌아서는 것을 보자, 그는 등자를 잡아 드리려 다가갔다가, 주인이 미처 오르기도 전에 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇고는 그 손을 잡아 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"돈키호테 나리, 이 고된 싸움에서 얻으신 그 섬의 통치를 제게 내려 주십사 청하옵니다. 아무리 크다 한들 저는 세상에서 일찍이 섬을 다스려 본 그 누구 못지않게, 그만큼 훌륭히 다스릴 만한 힘이 제게 있다고 느끼고 있으니까요.\"\n\n이에 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"산초 형제여, 잘 새겨 두게나. 이번 모험이나 이와 비슷한 것들은 섬을 얻는 모험이 아니라 갈림길의 모험이니, 거기서 얻을 것이라곤 머리가 깨지거나 귀 한쪽을 잃는 것밖에 없다네. 참고 기다리게. 자네를 총독뿐 아니라 그 이상으로도 만들어 줄 모험들이 절로 나타날 것이니.\"\n\n산초는 그에게 거듭 감사를 드리고는, 다시 그의 손과 갑옷 자락에 입을 맞춘 뒤 로시난테에 오르도록 거들었으며, 자신도 나귀에 올라타 주인의 뒤를 따랐다. 주인은 마차에 딸린 귀부인들에게 작별 인사도, 더는 아무 말도 건네지 않고 빠른 걸음으로 가까이 있는 숲속으로 접어들었다. 산초는 제 나귀가 낼 수 있는 가장 빠른 걸음으로 뒤를 따랐으나, 로시난테가 어찌나 성큼성큼 내딛는지 자신이 뒤처지는 것을 보고는 어쩔 수 없이 주인에게 기다려 달라고 외쳐야 했다. 돈키호테는 그 말대로 로시난테의 고삐를 당겨, 지친 종자가 따라붙을 때까지 멈춰 섰다. 종자는 그에게 다가와 이렇게 말했다. \"나리, 제 생각에는 우리가 어느 성당으로 피신하는 것이 현명한 일일 듯합니다. 나리께서 싸우신 그자가 어찌나 만신창이가 되었는지를 보면, 그자들이 이 일을 성스러운 형제단에 알려 우리를 붙잡아 가더라도 조금도 이상할 게 없을 테니까요. 그리고 정말이지 그렇게 되면, 감옥에서 나오기까지 우리는 단단히 진땀을 빼야 할 겁니다.\"\n* 성스러운 형제단(Santa Hermandad): 15세기 스페인에서 시골 길의 치안을 맡아 노상 범죄를 단속하던 무장 자경 조직.\n\n\"가만있게나.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"편력 기사가 아무리 많은 살인을 저질렀다 한들, 법정에 끌려가 심문받았다는 말을 자네는 대체 어디서 보거나 들은 적이 있는가?\"\n\n\"살인이니 뭐니 하는 것은 저는 도무지 모릅니다.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"평생 그런 것과는 아무 상관이 없었으니까요. 제가 아는 것이라곤 그저 성스러운 형제단이 들판에서 싸움질하는 자들을 단속한다는 것뿐이고, 그 밖의 일에는 끼어들지 않습니다요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 마음 졸일 것 없네, 벗이여.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 자네를 칼데아인들의 손에서도 건져 낼 터인데, 하물며 형제단의 손에서야 더 말할 것도 없지. 한데 살아 있는 목숨을 두고 말해 보게나. 알려진 온 세상을 통틀어 나보다 더 용맹한 기사를 본 적이 있는가. 공격에서 이보다 드높은 기개를 지녔거나 지녔던 자, 그것을 밀어붙이는 데 이보다 더한 투지를 보인 자, 상처 입히는 데 이보다 더한 솜씨나 쓰러뜨리는 데 이보다 더한 기량을 지닌 자를 역사책에서 읽은 적이 있는가?\"\n\n* 칼데아인들: 구약성서에서 바빌론의 점성술사·마법사를 가리키는 말. 돈키호테가 과장된 어법으로 끌어들인 표현이다.\n\"솔직히 말씀드리면,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"저는 역사책이라곤 한 번도 읽어 본 적이 없습니다. 읽을 줄도 쓸 줄도 모르니까요. 하지만 감히 내기를 걸 수 있는 것은, 제 평생 나리보다 더 무모한 주인은 모신 적이 없다는 겁니다. 그리고 부디 하느님께서 이 무모함의 값을 제가 아까 말씀드린 그곳에서 치르게 하지 않으시기를 빕니다. 제가 나리께 청하는 것은 그 상처를 좀 싸매시라는 겁니다. 그 귀에서 피가 어찌나 많이 흐르는지요. 여기 안장주머니에 무명실하고 흰 연고가 조금 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그 모든 것은 없어도 좋았을 텐데.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 피에라브라스의 영약을 한 병 만들어 둘 생각을 했더라면 말일세. 그것은 단 한 방울로 시간과 약을 함께 아껴 주니 말이야.\"\n\n* 피에라브라스의 영약: 기사도 소설에 등장하는 만병통치의 신비한 약. 피에라브라스는 사라센의 거인 기사 이름이다.\n\n\"그 병이라는 게 무엇이고, 그 영약이라는 게 무엇입니까?\" 산초 판사가 말했다.\n\"그것은 영약이라네.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그 제조법을 내가 머릿속에 담아 두고 있는데, 그것만 있으면 죽음을 두려워할 것도, 어떤 상처로 목숨을 잃을까 겁낼 것도 없지. 그러니 내가 그것을 만들어 자네에게 건네주고 나면, 어느 싸움에서 그들이 내 몸뚱이를 한가운데서 두 동강으로 갈라 놓은 것을 — 흔히 일어나는 일이지만 — 보게 되었을 때, 자네가 할 일이라곤 이것뿐일세. 피가 굳기 전에, 깔끔하고 더없이 정밀하게, 땅에 떨어진 그 몸의 한쪽을 안장에 남아 있는 다른 절반 위에 얹되, 어긋남 없이 정확히 맞물리도록 조심해서 갖다 붙이는 것이지. 그러고 나서 내가 말한 그 영약을 단 두 방울만 내게 마시게 해 주면, 자네는 내가 사과보다 더 멀쩡해지는 것을 보게 될 걸세.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇다면,\" 판사가 말했다. \"저는 이제부터 약속하신 그 섬의 통치를 단념하겠습니다. 그리고 저의 수많은 충직한 봉사의 대가로 나리께서 그 더없는 영약의 제조법을 제게 일러 주시는 것 말고는 더 바랄 것이 없습니다. 어디서든 그것은 한 온스에 두 레알 넘는 값이 나가리라 믿으니, 저는 그것만 있으면 남은 평생을 편안하고 영예롭게 보내는 데 더 바랄 것이 없습니다. 다만 그것을 만드는 데 비용이 많이 드는지는 아직 말씀해 주셔야겠지요.\"\n* 레알: 당대 스페인의 은화 단위.\n\n\"삼 레알도 채 안 되는 돈으로 여섯 쿠아르토는 만들 수 있지.\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n* 쿠아르토: 액체의 부피를 재던 단위.\n\"이 죄 많은 놈을 보십시오!\" 산초가 말했다. \"그렇다면 나리께서는 어찌하여 그것을 만들어 제게 가르쳐 주시는 일을 미루고 계신답니까?\"\n\n\"가만있게, 벗이여.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그보다 더 큰 비밀들을 내 자네에게 가르쳐 줄 작정이고, 그보다 더 큰 은혜들을 내려 줄 작정이라네. 그러니 지금은 우선 이 상처나 싸매도록 하세. 귀가 내 바라는 것보다 더 아프구먼.\"\n\n산초는 안장주머니에서 무명실과 연고를 조금 꺼냈다. 그러나 돈키호테는 자기 투구가 박살 난 것을 보자 정신을 잃을 지경이 되어, 칼자루에 손을 얹고 두 눈을 하늘로 치켜올리며 이렇게 말했다. \"만물의 창조주와 그 온전한 뜻을 다 갖춘 사복음서를 두고 맹세하노니, 만토바의 위대한 후작이 자기 조카 발도비노스의 죽음을 복수하겠다고 맹세하며 했던 그대로 하리라. 즉 식탁보를 깔고 빵을 먹지 않을 것이며, 아내를 품지 않을 것이며, 지금은 다 떠올리지 못하나 여기서 그대로 말한 것으로 갈음하는 다른 여러 조항을 지키리니, 이는 내게 이토록 모진 모욕을 가한 자에게 온전한 복수를 거둘 때까지로다.\"\n\n* 만토바의 후작과 발도비노스: 카롤링거 계열 기사도 이야기와 옛 로맨스에 나오는 인물들.\n이 말을 듣고 산초가 그에게 말했다. \"돈키호테 나리, 한 가지 유념하셔야 할 것이 있습니다. 그 기사가 명하신 대로 토보소의 둘시네아 마님 앞에 가서 자기를 바쳤다면, 그는 자기가 마땅히 해야 할 바를 다 한 것이니, 새로운 죄를 저지르지 않는 한 더 벌받을 까닭이 없지요.\"\n\n\"옳은 말일세, 정곡을 찔렀구먼.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그러니 그자에게 새로이 복수를 거두는 일에 관해서는 그 맹세를 거두어들이겠네. 하지만 내가 말한 그런 삶을 살겠다는 맹세만큼은 다시 세우고 다짐하노니, 어느 기사에게서 이만큼 훌륭한 또 다른 투구를 힘으로 빼앗을 때까지 그리하리라. 그리고 산초여, 내가 이렇게 한다고 해서 짚으로 연기를 피우는 헛수고를 한다고는 여기지 말게. 이 일에서 본받을 선례가 내게 있으니, 사크리판테에게 그토록 비싼 대가를 치르게 했던 맘브리노의 투구의 경우에도 털끝 하나 다르지 않게 바로 이와 똑같은 일이 일어났기 때문일세.\"\n\n* 맘브리노의 투구: 아리오스토의 「광란의 오를란도」 등에 나오는, 누구도 다치게 할 수 없는 마법의 황금 투구.\n\"나리,\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"그런 맹세 따위는 죄다 악마에게나 보내 버리십시오. 그것들은 구원에 몹시 해롭고 양심에도 거치적거리니까요. 그러니 이제 말씀해 보십시오. 앞으로 며칠 동안 투구를 쓴 사람을 하나도 만나지 못하면 우리는 어찌해야 합니까? 옷을 입은 채 자야 하고, 집 안에서는 자지 못해야 하며, 나리께서 지금 되살리려 하시는 저 늙다리 만토바 후작의 맹세에 담긴 천 가지 다른 고행을 감수해야 하는데, 그 온갖 불편과 괴로움을 무릅쓰고도 그 맹세를 지켜야 한단 말입니까? 보십시오, 이 길들 어디에도 갑옷 입고 다니는 자들은 없고, 짐꾼이나 마차꾼밖에 없습니다. 그자들은 투구를 쓰기는커녕 아마 평생 그런 말조차 들어 본 적이 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그 점에서는 자네가 틀렸네.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"우리가 이 갈림길에 들어선 지 두 시간도 채 안 되어, 아름다운 안젤리카를 차지하려고 알브라카로 몰려들었던 것보다 더 많은 갑옷 입은 자들을 보게 될 테니 말일세.\"\n\n* 안젤리카와 알브라카: 「광란의 오를란도」와 보이아르도의 「사랑에 빠진 오를란도」에 나오는 미녀 공주와 그녀가 농성한 성.\n\n\"알겠습니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그렇다면 그리되도록 하지요. 부디 하느님께서 우리에게 성공을 내려 주시고, 제게 이토록 값비싼 대가를 치르게 하는 그 섬을 얻을 때가 어서 오게 해 주시기를 빕니다. 그러고 나면 저는 죽어도 좋습니다.\"\n\"내 이미 자네에게 말하지 않았는가, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그 일로는 조금도 마음을 졸이지 말게. 설령 섬이 어그러진다 해도 덴마크 왕국이나 소브라디사 왕국이 있어, 반지가 손가락에 꼭 맞듯 자네에게 딱 들어맞을 테니 말일세. 더구나 그것들은 뭍에 있으니 자네가 한층 더 즐기게 될 것이고. 하지만 그 일은 때가 오거든 그때 가서 보세. 우선 그 안장주머니에 우리가 먹을 만한 것이 좀 있는지 살펴보게. 곧장 오늘 밤 묵을 만한 성을 찾아 나서서, 내가 말한 그 영약을 만들어야 하니 말일세. 하느님께 맹세코, 이 귀가 나를 몹시 아프게 하는구먼.\"\n\n\"여기 양파 하나하고 치즈 조금, 빵 부스러기가 몇 개 있습니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하지만 나리 같은 용맹한 기사께 어울리는 음식은 못 됩니다요.\"\n\n\"자네가 그 일을 얼마나 모르는가!\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"산초여, 내 자네에게 일러 두거니와, 한 달을 먹지 않고 지내는 것, 그리고 설령 먹는다 해도 손에 가장 먼저 잡히는 것으로 때우는 것이야말로 편력 기사의 영광일세. 내가 읽은 만큼 자네도 역사책을 많이 읽었더라면 이 점이 자네에게도 분명했을 텐데. 그 책들이 무척 많기는 하나, 그 모든 것을 통틀어도 우연히 먹게 되거나 그들을 위해 차려진 어느 호화로운 연회에서가 아니고서는 편력 기사들이 먹었다는 언급은 찾아본 적이 없고, 나머지 시간은 사랑놀음으로 보냈다고 되어 있으니 말일세. 물론 그들이 먹지 않고서, 또 다른 온갖 자연스러운 일들을 치르지 않고서 지낼 수 없었으리라는 것은 분명하네. 사실 그들도 우리와 같은 사람이었으니까. 하지만 그들이 평생의 대부분을 숲과 황야를 떠돌며 요리사도 없이 지낸 것 또한 분명한즉, 그들의 가장 흔한 끼니란 자네가 지금 내게 내미는 것과 같은 시골스러운 음식이었을 걸세. 그러니 산초 벗이여, 나를 기쁘게 하는 것이 자네를 괴롭히지 않도록 하게. 새로운 세상을 만들어 내려 들거나 편력 기사도를 비틀려 들지 말게.\"\n\n\"용서하십시오, 나리.\" 산초가 말했다. \"아까 말씀드렸다시피 저는 읽을 줄도 쓸 줄도 모르니, 기사라는 직분의 규칙을 알지도 깨닫지도 못합니다. 앞으로는 나리께서 기사이시니, 나리를 위해서는 온갖 말린 과일로 안장주머니를 채워 드리겠습니다. 그리고 저는 기사가 아니니, 저를 위해서는 가금류며 그 밖에 좀 더 든든한 것들로 채워 두겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"산초여, 내 말은,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"편력 기사가 자네 말하는 그 과일 말고는 아무것도 먹어서는 안 된다는 것이 아닐세. 다만 그들의 더 흔한 끼니가 그런 것들과, 그들이 들판에서 찾아낸 어떤 풀들이었으리라는 것이지. 그들도 그 풀들을 알았고 나 또한 알고 있다네.\"\n\n\"그런 풀들을 안다는 건 좋은 일이지요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제 생각에는 언젠가 그 지식을 써먹어야 할 날이 올 듯하니까요.\"\n\n그러고는 산초가 가져왔다고 한 것을 꺼내 놓자, 두 사람은 평화롭고 정답게 끼니를 들었다. 그러나 밤에 묵을 곳을 서둘러 찾고 싶은 마음에, 그들은 부랴부랴 그 변변찮은 마른 음식을 마저 먹어 치우고는 곧장 말에 올라, 날이 저물기 전에 어느 거처에 닿으려고 서둘렀다. 그러나 어느 염소지기들의 오두막 가까이에 이르렀을 때 햇빛도, 목적을 이루리라는 희망도 그들을 저버렸으니, 그들은 그곳에서 밤을 지내기로 했다. 집에 닿지 못한 것이 산초에게는 그만큼 불만스러운 일이었으나, 한데서 별을 이고 자는 것은 그만큼 주인에게는 흡족한 일이었다. 그는 이런 일이 자신에게 일어날 때마다 자신의 기사도를 입증해 주는 소유의 행위 하나를 행하는 것이라 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n# WHAT BEFELL DON QUIXOTE WITH CERTAIN GOATHERDS\n\n돈키호테는 염소지기들에게 따뜻한 환대를 받았고, 산초는 최선을 다해 로시난테와 나귀를 매어 둔 뒤, 불 위에 올려진 솥에서 보글보글 끓고 있는 소금에 절인 염소 고기 몇 점에서 풍겨 오는 향내 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그것들이 솥에서 위장으로 옮겨질 만큼 익었는지 당장 시험해 보고 싶었으나, 염소지기들이 그것을 불에서 내려 양가죽을 땅에 깔고 재빨리 거친 식탁을 차린 다음, 진심에서 우러난 호의의 몸짓으로 두 사람에게 자기들이 가진 것을 함께 나누자고 청했기에 그는 참았다. 우리에 딸린 사내 여섯이 양가죽 둘레에 자리를 잡았는데, 그들은 먼저 투박한 정중함으로 돈키호테에게 권하여 그를 위해 거꾸로 엎어 놓은 여물통 위에 앉게 했다. 돈키호테는 자리에 앉았고, 산초는 뿔로 만든 잔에 술을 따르려고 선 채로 있었다. 그가 서 있는 것을 보고 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"산초야, 편력 기사도 안에 어떤 좋은 점이 깃들어 있는지, 또 그 안에서 어떤 직책이든 맡은 자들이 어찌하여 세상으로부터 속히 존경받고 떠받들리는 큰길에 들어서게 되는지를 네가 알도록, 나는 네가 여기 내 곁에, 이 훌륭한 사람들과 함께 앉기를 바라며, 너의 주인이자 타고난 영주인 나와 하나가 되어, 내 접시에서 먹고 내가 마시는 그 무엇에서든 함께 마시기를 바라노라. 편력 기사도에 대해서도 사랑에 관해 하는 말과 똑같은 말을 할 수 있으니, 그것이 모든 것을 평등하게 만든다는 말이다.\"\n\n\"참으로 고마운 말씀입니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하오나 나리께 한 말씀 드리자면, 먹을 것만 넉넉하다면 황제 곁에 앉아서보다 차라리 서서, 혼자서 먹는 편이 못지않게, 아니 더 잘 먹을 수 있습니다요. 솔직히 말씀드리면, 격식도 법석도 없이 제 구석에서 먹는 것은 그것이 빵에 양파뿐이라 해도, 천천히 씹어야 하고 조금밖에 못 마시며 시도 때도 없이 입을 닦아야 하고, 재채기나 기침이 나도 못 하고, 자유와 고독이 주는 특권인 다른 짓들도 못 하는 저 다른 식탁의 칠면조 요리보다 제게는 훨씬 더 맛이 있습니다요. 그러니 나리, 편력 기사도의 종이요 추종자라 하여 나리께서 제게 베푸시려는 이런 영예는, 제게 더 쓸모 있고 더 이득이 될 다른 것들과 바꿔 주십시오. 이것들은 받은 것으로 충분히 인정은 하지만, 지금 이 순간부터 세상 끝날까지 사양하겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"그렇더라도,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"너는 앉아야 한다. 스스로를 낮추는 자를 하느님께서 높이시는 법이니라.\" 그러고는 그의 팔을 붙잡아 억지로 자기 곁에 앉혔다.\n\n염소지기들은 종자니 편력 기사니 하는 이 알아들을 수 없는 말을 이해하지 못했고, 그저 묵묵히 먹으며 손님들을 빤히 쳐다볼 뿐이었는데, 그 손님들은 더할 나위 없는 우아함과 식욕으로 주먹만 한 고깃점들을 챙겨 넣고 있었다. 고기 요리가 끝나자 그들은 양가죽 위에 볶은 도토리를 한 무더기 쏟아 놓았고, 그와 함께 회반죽으로 만든 것보다 더 단단한 치즈 반 덩이를 내려놓았다. 그러는 내내 뿔잔도 놀고 있지 않았으니, 그것은 물레방아의 두레박처럼 가득 찼다가 비었다가 하며 쉴 새 없이 돌고 돌아, 눈에 보이던 두 개의 술 부대 가운데 하나를 이내 비워 버렸다. 돈키호테는 식욕을 충분히 채우고 나서 도토리 한 줌을 집어 들고는 그것을 골똘히 바라보며 대략 이런 식으로 말을 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"행복하도다, 옛사람들이 황금이라 이름 붙인 그 시대와 그 세월이여. 그 복된 시절에 우리의 이 무쇠 같은 시대에서 그토록 탐내는 황금이 수고 없이 얻어졌기 때문이 아니라, 그때 살던 이들이 '내 것'과 '네 것'이라는 두 마디 말을 알지 못했기 때문이로다! 그 축복받은 시대에는 모든 것이 공유물이었으니, 날마다의 양식을 얻는 데에는 손을 뻗어, 달콤하고 잘 익은 열매로 너그러이 손짓하며 서 있는 튼튼한 떡갈나무에서 그것을 따 모으는 일 외에 아무런 노동도 누구에게 요구되지 않았다. 맑은 시내와 흐르는 개울은 그 향긋하고 투명한 물을 고귀하게도 넘치도록 내어 주었다. 부지런하고 영민한 벌들은 바위틈과 나무 구멍에 그들의 공화국을 세워, 그 향긋한 노고의 풍성한 산물을 아무런 대가도 없이 모든 손길에 내밀었다. 힘센 코르크나무는 제 호의 외에는 아무런 강요도 받지 않고 그 넓적한 가벼운 껍질을 벗어 주었으니, 그것은 처음에 거친 말뚝으로 떠받친 집들의 지붕이 되어 오직 하늘의 험한 날씨에 대한 보호막이 되어 주었다. 그때는 모든 것이 평화요, 모든 것이 우정이요, 모든 것이 화합이었다. 아직 굽은 쟁기의 무딘 보습이 우리의 첫 어머니의 부드러운 속살을 감히 찢고 꿰뚫지 못했으니, 그 어머니는 강요받지 않고도 그 넓고 비옥한 품의 구석구석에서, 그때 그를 차지하던 자식들을 만족시키고 지탱하며 기쁘게 할 모든 것을 내어 주었다. 그때야말로 순결하고 어여쁜 젊은 양치기 처녀가, 머리채를 풀어헤치고, 정숙함이 가리고자 했고 또 늘 가리고자 해 온 것을 단정히 덮을 만큼의 옷가지만 걸친 채, 골짜기에서 골짜기로, 언덕에서 언덕으로 거닐던 시절이었다. 그들의 장신구도 오늘날 쓰이는 것들, 곧 티레산 자줏빛으로 꾸미고 끝없는 모양으로 비단을 비틀어 만든 것들과는 달랐으니, 푸른 소루쟁이와 담쟁이덩굴의 잎을 엮은 것이었고, 그들은 그것으로, 한가한 호기심이 우리 궁정의 귀부인들에게 가르쳐 준 온갖 진귀하고 머나먼 데서 들여온 기교만큼이나 멋지고 어울리게 치장하고 다녔다. 그때는 마음의 사랑의 정념도 마음이 품은 그대로 소박하고 자연스럽게 옷을 입었을 뿐, 억지스럽고 두서없는 말장난으로 스스로를 내세우려 하지 않았다. 사기나 기만이나 악의도 아직 진실과 진심에 섞여 들지 않았다. 정의는 그 자리를 지켜, 지금은 정의를 그토록 심하게 훼손하고 왜곡하며 에워싸는 저 정실과 사욕의 책동에 시달리거나 침해당하지 않았다. 자의적인 법도 아직 재판관의 마음에 자리 잡지 못했으니, 그때는 재판할 거리도 없었고 재판받을 자도 없었기 때문이다. 처녀들과 정숙함은, 내가 말했듯이, 무법자의 모욕이나 난봉꾼의 습격을 두려워하지 않고 홀로 거리낌 없이 시중도 없이 거닐었으며, 만일 그들이 몸을 망쳤다면 그것은 그들 자신의 뜻과 즐거움에서였다. 그러나 이제 우리의 이 가증스러운 시대에는 어느 누구도 안전하지 못하니, 비록 크레타의 그것과 같은 새로운 미로가 그를 감추고 둘러싼다 해도 그러하다. 그곳에서조차 색정의 역병은 틈새를 비집고, 혹은 그 저주받은 집요함의 열의로 공기를 타고 그들에게로 길을 내어, 온갖 격리에도 불구하고 그들을 파멸로 이끌 것이다. 세월이 흐르고 사악함이 늘어남에 따라 이런 이들을 지키고자, 처녀들을 보호하고 과부들을 지키며 고아들과 궁핍한 자들을 구원하기 위해 편력 기사단이 세워졌다. 염소지기 형제들이여, 나는 그 기사단에 속한 자이니, 그대들이 나와 내 종자에게 베푸는 환대와 다정한 영접에 감사를 드리노라. 자연의 법으로 보면 살아 있는 모든 자가 편력 기사에게 호의를 베풀 의무가 있으나, 그대들은 이 의무를 알지 못하고도 나를 맞아들여 잔치를 베풀었으니, 내가 가진 온 호의를 다하여 그대들의 호의에 감사하는 것이 마땅하도다.\"\n\n우리의 기사가 이 길고 긴 일장 연설을(없어도 아주 좋았을) 늘어놓은 것은, 그들이 준 도토리가 그에게 황금시대를 떠올리게 했기 때문이며, 이 쓸데없는 논변을 모조리 염소지기들에게 늘어놓고 싶은 변덕이 그를 사로잡았기 때문인데, 그들은 입을 떡 벌린 채 어리둥절하여 한마디 대꾸도 없이 그의 말에 귀를 기울였다. 산초 또한 입을 다물고 도토리를 먹으며, 술을 시원하게 두려고 코르크나무에 매달아 둔 두 번째 술 부대를 거듭거듭 찾았다.\n\n돈키호테가 말을 늘어놓는 시간이 저녁 식사가 끝나는 시간보다 길었는데, 그것이 끝나자 염소지기 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"편력 기사 나리, 저희가 기꺼운 호의로 나리를 환대한다는 것을 나리께서 더 진실되게 말씀하실 수 있도록, 저희 동무 가운데 하나에게 노래를 시켜 즐거움과 흥을 돋워 드리겠습니다요. 그 친구는 곧 이리로 올 텐데, 아주 영리한 젊은이인 데다 사랑에 깊이 빠져 있고, 게다가 글을 읽고 쓸 줄도 알며 레베크도 더할 나위 없이 잘 켭니다요.\"\n\n염소지기가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에 레베크 소리가 그들의 귀에 닿았고, 잠시 후 그 연주자가 다가왔는데, 스물두 살쯤 된 매우 잘생긴 젊은이였다. 동무들이 그에게 저녁을 먹었느냐고 묻자 그는 먹었다고 대답했고, 앞서 청을 했던 사내가 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 안토니오, 자네가 노래 한 곡을 불러 우리를 즐겁게 해 주는 것도 좋겠네그려. 손님이신 이 신사 어른께서 산속과 숲속에도 악사가 있음을 아시도록 말일세. 우리가 자네의 재주를 어른께 일러두었으니, 자네가 그것을 보여 우리 말이 참됨을 증명해 주었으면 하네. 그러니 제발, 자리에 앉아 자네 삼촌인 참사회원 어른이 자네에게 지어 주신, 마을에서 그토록 사랑받은 자네 사랑 이야기 그 가락을 한번 불러 보게나.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요.\" 젊은이가 말했고, 더 보채기를 기다리지도 않고 베어 낸 떡갈나무 둥치 위에 앉아 레베크를 조율하더니, 이내 이런 가사에 맞추어 노래하기 시작했다.\n안토니오의 노래\n\n# ANTONIO’S BALLAD\n\n그대 나를 진정 사랑하네, 올랄라여, 잘 알고 있다네, 비록 사랑의 말 없는 혀, 그대의 눈이, 그 눈길로 내게 한 번도 그리 일러 준 적 없을지라도.\n\n내 사랑을 그대가 안다는 것을 내 알기에, 그대의 사랑도 내 것이라 감히 주장하리니, 사랑이 한번 비밀이기를 그치면 결코 절망을 느낄 까닭 없으리라.\n\n참으로 그러하네, 올랄라여, 때때로 그대는 너무도 분명히 보여 주었지, 그대 마음이 놋쇠처럼 단단하고 그대 눈 같은 가슴이 돌과 같음을.\n\n허나 그 모든 것에도, 그대의 새침함 속에, 그대의 변덕스러운 발작 사이로, 희망이 있으니 — 적어도 그 옷자락 가장자리만은 엿보이나니.\n\n신뢰로 이끄는 미끼들이라, 그 언뜻 보이는 것들은, 그래서 나 그대에 대한 믿음을 붙드네, 다정함도 그것을 더 굳세게 못 하고, 냉담함도 그것을 식히지 못하리.\n\n사랑이 만일 부드러운 것이라면, 그대의 부드러움 속에서 나는 보네, 그대를 얻으리라는 희망에게 무언가 확신을 내미는 것을.\n\n헌신 속에 마음을 움직이는 힘이 깃들어 있는 것이라면, 내 날마다 그대에게 보이는 그것이, 내 구애에 도움이 되어야 마땅하리.\n\n여러 번 그대도 분명 알아챘으리니 — 알아채려 마음을 쓴다면 말일세 — 내가 어찌하여 월요일에도 주일날 차림을 죄다 갖추고 다니는지를.\n\n사랑의 눈은 환한 것 바라보길 좋아하고, 사랑은 곱게 차려입은 것을 사랑하니, 주일이건 월요일이건 내 마음은 오직 그대가 내 가장 좋은 모습을 봐 주기를.\n\n춤 따위는 내 안중에도 없고, 그대를 그토록 기쁘게 한 가락도, 한밤중부터 닭이 울기 시작할 때까지 그대를 잠 못 들게 한 그것도 마찬가지라네.\n\n그대만큼 어여쁜 이 없노라고 내가 호기롭게 맹세한 것도 그러하네, 참말이긴 하나, 그 말을 한 탓에 마을 처녀들이 이제 나를 미워한다네.\n\n언덕배기의 테레사는 내가 그대를 칭찬하자 속이 상하여 이렇게 말했지, \"넌 천사를 사랑하는 줄 알지만 네가 떠받드는 건 원숭이라네.\n\n번쩍이는 싸구려 장신구에 홀리고, 빌려 온 가짜 머리채에 홀리고, 사랑의 신마저 옭아맬 꾸며 댄 미인 떼거리에 홀린 게지.\"\n\n거짓말이라고, 나는 그 애에게 그리 일렀고, 그 말에 그 애 사촌이 나에게 결투를 청했으니, 그 뒤에 일어난 일은 그대도 들어 알리라.\n\n내 것은 드높이 띄운 애정이 아니요, 내 것은 — 사람들이 그리 부르는 — 정념의 사랑도 아니라네. 내가 바치는 것은 정직하고 순결한 사랑이니라.\n\n거룩한 교회에는 끈이 있으니, 더없이 부드러운 비단 끈이라네, 그 멍에 아래 그대 목을 들이밀게나, 임이여, 내 목도 뒤따르는 것을 그대 보게 되리니.\n\n그러지 않으면 — 단 한 번 맹세하노니 가장 이름 높은 성인을 두고 — 내 만일 이 산을 떠난다면, 그것은 수도사의 옷을 입고서이리라.\n\n여기서 염소지기는 노래를 끝맺었고, 돈키호테는 더 불러 달라고 청했으나, 산초는 노래를 듣기보다 잠 쪽으로 더 기울어 있어 그럴 마음이 없었다. 그래서 그가 주인에게 말했다. \"나리께서는 오늘 밤 어디서 지내실지를 당장 정하시는 게 좋겠습니다요. 이 선량한 사람들이 온종일 하는 노동이 밤을 노래로 지새우도록 내버려 두질 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 알겠다, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"저 술 부대를 그렇게 찾아간 것이 음악보다는 잠으로 보상받기를 요구한다는 것을 똑똑히 알겠구나.\"\n\n\"하느님 덕분에, 그건 우리 모두에게 달콤한 일입지요.\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"부인하지 않겠다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"허나 너는 어디든 마음 내키는 데에 자리를 잡으려무나. 나 같은 직분의 사람은 잠자기보다 망보는 일에 더 어울리게 쓰여야 하느니라. 다만 네가 이 귀를 다시 한번 손봐 주면 좋겠구나. 필요 이상으로 아프니 말이다.\"\n\n산초는 그가 시키는 대로 했는데, 염소지기 가운데 하나가 그 상처를 보고는, 곧 나을 약을 발라 줄 테니 걱정 말라고 일렀다. 그러고는 그곳에 잔뜩 자라 있던 로즈메리 잎을 몇 개 따서 씹은 다음 소금을 약간 섞어 그것을 귀에 갖다 대고 붕대로 단단히 동여매면서, 다른 치료는 필요 없으리라고 장담했는데, 과연 그러했다.\n* 레베크: 중세와 르네상스 시기에 쓰인, 활로 켜는 세 줄짜리 현악기.\n\n# OF WHAT A GOATHERD RELATED TO THOSE WITH DON QUIXOTE\n\n바로 그때 마을에서 양식을 가져오던 젊은이들 가운데 하나가 다가와 말했다. \"여보게들, 마을에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 아나?\"\n\n\"우리가 그걸 어찌 알겠나?\" 그들 가운데 하나가 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 들어 보게나.\" 젊은이가 말을 이었다. \"오늘 아침에 그 유명한 학생 목동 크리소스토모가 죽었다네. 소문으로는 부자 기예르모의 딸, 양치기 처녀 차림으로 이 들판을 떠돌아다니는 그 마을의 마녀 같은 계집한테 반해서 죽었다는구먼.\"\n\n\"마르셀라 말인가?\" 하나가 물었다.\n\n\"바로 그 계집 말이야.\" 염소지기가 대답했다. \"게다가 더 기막힌 건, 유언으로 자기를 무어인처럼 들판에, 코르크나무 샘이 있는 바위 밑에 묻으라고 일러 두었다는 거야. 전해지는 이야기로는(그가 직접 그렇게 말했다더군) 거기가 그 계집을 처음 본 자리였대. 게다가 마을 신부님들이 이교도 냄새가 난다며 따라서는 안 되고 따를 수도 없다고 하는 다른 분부들도 남겨 두었다네. 이 모든 일에 대해, 그처럼 양치기 차림으로 다니던 그의 절친한 벗 학생 암브로시오는 크리소스토모가 남긴 분부대로 하나도 빠짐없이 다 해야 한다고 대답하니, 이 일로 마을이 온통 들끓고 있어. 그래도 소문으로는 결국 암브로시오와 그의 벗인 목동들이 바라는 대로 될 것이고, 내일 내가 말한 그곳에 그를 성대히 묻으러 온다더군. 분명 한번 볼 만한 일일 거야. 적어도 나는 내일 마을로 돌아오지 못한다 해도 꼭 가서 봐야겠어.\"\n\n\"우리도 그렇게 하세.\" 염소지기들이 대답했다. \"그리고 누가 남아서 모두의 염소를 지킬지 제비를 뽑세나.\"\n\n\"옳은 말일세, 페드로.\" 하나가 말했다. \"하지만 그런 수고를 할 필요는 없겠네. 내가 다들 대신해서 남을 테니까. 그렇다고 이게 무슨 덕이 있어서나 호기심이 없어서라고 여기지는 말게. 며칠 전 발에 박힌 가시 때문에 걸을 수가 없어서 그러는 거니까.\"\n\n\"그래도 고맙네.\" 페드로가 대답했다.\n\n돈키호테는 페드로에게 그 죽은 자가 누구이며 양치기 처녀는 또 누구인지 일러 달라고 청했다. 이에 페드로는, 자기가 아는 거라곤 그 죽은 자가 저 산속 어느 마을에 속한 부유한 신사이며, 여러 해 동안 살라망카에서 공부하다가 그 끝에 학식이 매우 깊고 박식하다는 평판을 안고 마을로 돌아왔다는 것뿐이라고 대답했다. \"무엇보다도, 그분은 별들의 학문과 저 위 하늘과 해와 달에서 일어나는 일에 밝았다고들 합디다. 우리한테 해와 달의 그릇침을 정확한 시각까지 일러 주셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 식(蝕)이라고 부른다네, 친구. 그릇침이 아니라. 그 두 빛나는 것이 어두워지는 걸 말일세.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. 그러나 페드로는 그런 사소한 것에 마음 쓰지 않고 이야기를 계속 이어 갔다. \"그분은 또 그해가 풍년이 될지 흉헌이 될지도 미리 알려 주셨지요.\"\n\n\"흉년 말이겠지.\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"흉년이든 흉헌이든.\" 페드로가 대답했다. \"결국 다 마찬가지 아닙니까. 그래서 말씀드리는데, 그분 말씀을 믿은 아버님과 친구들은 그분이 일러 준 대로 했기에 큰 부자가 되었답니다. '올해는 밀 말고 보리를 심어라. 올해는 보리 말고 콩을 심어도 된다. 내년에는 기름이 넘치도록 날 것이고, 그다음 삼 년은 한 방울도 얻지 못할 것이다' 하고 일러 주셨거든요.\"\n\n\"그 학문은 점성술이라 부른다네.\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 뭐라 불리는지는 모르겠습니다만.\" 페드로가 대답했다. \"그분이 이걸 다 알고, 그밖에도 더 많이 알고 계셨다는 건 압니다. 어쨌든 이야기를 끝맺자면, 그분이 살라망카에서 돌아온 지 몇 달 지나지 않은 어느 날, 학자로서 입던 긴 가운을 벗어 던지고 지팡이와 양가죽을 걸친 목동 차림으로 나타나셨답니다. 그와 동시에 그분의 절친한 벗으로, 함께 공부했던 암브로시오라는 이도 그분을 따라 목동 차림을 했지요. 깜빡 잊고 말씀 안 드렸는데, 죽은 크리소스토모는 시 짓는 데 뛰어난 분이라, 성탄 전야의 캐럴이며 성체 축일의 연극까지 지었고, 우리 마을 젊은이들이 그걸 공연했는데 다들 훌륭하다고 칭찬했답니다. 마을 사람들이 그 두 학자가 그토록 느닷없이 목동 차림으로 나타난 걸 보고는, 얼이 빠져서 무엇 때문에 그런 엄청난 변화를 했는지 짐작조차 못 했지요. 이 무렵에 우리 크리소스토모의 아버님이 돌아가셨고, 그분은 동산이며 토지며, 적지 않은 소와 양 떼, 그리고 큰돈에 이르기까지 막대한 재산을 물려받게 되었습니다. 이 젊은이가 그 모든 것의 거리낌 없는 주인이 된 셈인데, 사실 그분은 그 모든 것을 받을 만한 분이었어요. 더없이 좋은 동무였고, 마음씨가 따뜻했으며, 점잖은 이들의 벗이었고, 얼굴은 축복처럼 환했으니까요. 머지않아, 그분이 옷차림을 바꾼 데는 다른 까닭이 아니라, 아까 우리 젊은이가 말한 그 양치기 처녀 마르셀라를 좇아 이 황무지를 떠돌기 위해서였음이 알려졌지요. 죽은 크리소스토모가 그 처녀한테 반해 버렸던 겁니다. 이제 그 계집이 누구인지 말씀드려야겠습니다. 아셔 두시는 게 좋을 테니까요. 어쩌면, 아니 어쩌면이랄 것도 없이, 사르나보다 더 오래 사신다 해도 평생 이런 이야기는 못 들어 보셨을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"사라라고 하게.\" 염소지기의 뒤죽박죽 말을 견디지 못한 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"사르나도 충분히 오래 삽니다.\" 페드로가 대답했다. \"그리고 나리, 한 걸음마다 말꼬리를 잡으려 드시면 우리는 올 한 해가 다 가도록 이야기를 끝내지 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"미안하네, 친구.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"다만 사르나와 사라는 그토록 다르기에 일러 준 것일세. 그래도 자네 대답은 참으로 옳네. 사르나가 사라보다 오래 사니까. 그러니 이야기를 계속하게. 더는 어떤 것에도 토를 달지 않겠네.\"\n\n* 사르나(sarna): 스페인어로 옴, 피부병을 뜻함. 페드로는 구약성서의 인물 사라(Sarra)를 사르나로 잘못 발음하고 있다.\n\"그러면 말씀드리지요, 나리.\" 염소지기가 말했다. \"우리 마을에 크리소스토모의 아버님보다도 더 부유한 농부가 있었는데, 이름이 기예르모였습니다. 하느님은 그에게 큰 재산 말고도 딸을 하나 점지해 주셨는데, 그 아이를 낳다가 어미가 죽었지요. 이 근방에서 가장 존경받던 여인이었답니다. 한쪽엔 해를, 다른 한쪽엔 달을 단 듯한 그 얼굴이 지금도 눈에 선합니다. 게다가 부지런하고 가난한 이들에게 친절했으니, 지금쯤 그 영혼은 저세상에서 하느님과 함께 복락을 누리고 있으리라 믿습니다. 그 남편 기예르모는 그토록 착한 아내의 죽음을 슬퍼하다 세상을 떠나면서, 어리고 부유한 딸 마르셀라를 자기 처삼촌 되는, 우리 마을의 신부이자 성당 참사회원에게 맡겼지요. 그 아이는 어찌나 아리땁게 자랐는지, 그 대단했던 어미의 미모를 떠올리게 했고, 그런데도 딸의 미모가 어미를 넘어서리라는 말까지 나왔답니다. 그래서 그 애가 열네댓 살에 이르자, 그 아이를 보는 사람마다 이토록 아름답게 빚으신 하느님을 찬미했고, 대다수는 도저히 헤어날 수 없을 만큼 그 애한테 반해 버렸지요. 삼촌은 그 애를 깊이 들어앉혀 두고 바깥출입을 삼가게 했지만, 그래도 그 대단한 미모의 명성이 어찌나 퍼졌던지, 그 미모 때문에든 그 막대한 재산 때문에든, 우리 고장 사람들뿐 아니라 여러 십 리 밖 사람들까지, 게다가 그중에서도 가장 지체 높은 이들까지 그 애를 아내로 달라고 삼촌에게 청하고 졸라 대고 들볶았답니다. 하지만 삼촌은 착실한 기독교인인지라, 그 애가 시집갈 만한 나이가 된 걸 보고 당장이라도 시집보내고 싶었지만, 본인의 승낙 없이는 그러려 하지 않았지요. 결혼을 미루는 동안 그 애 재산을 맡아 두며 얻는 이득과 잇속에 눈독을 들였던 게 결코 아니었습니다. 정말이지, 이 점은 마을 곳곳에서 한자리 건너 그 착한 신부님을 칭송하는 말로 회자되었답니다. 편력 기사 나리, 아셔 두셔야 할 게, 이런 작은 마을에서는 무엇이든 입에 오르고 무엇이든 흠잡히는 법이라, 저처럼 나리도 믿어 두시는 게 좋습니다. 교구민들이 자기를 좋게 말하도록 만드는 신부라면, 그야말로 더없이 착한 사람일 수밖에 없다는 걸요. 더구나 마을에서야 말할 것도 없고요.\"\n\n\"그건 사실일세.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그러니 계속하게. 이야기가 참으로 좋고, 자네, 착한 페드로도 참으로 멋들어지게 들려주는구먼.\"\n\n\"주님의 은총이 제게 모자라지 않기를 빕니다.\" 페드로가 말했다. \"꼭 지녀야 할 것이 바로 그것이지요. 이야기를 계속하자면, 아셔 두셔야 할 게, 삼촌이 청혼한 그 많은 이들 하나하나의 됨됨이를 조카딸 앞에 늘어놓고 일러 주면서, 제발 자기 마음에 드는 이를 골라 시집을 가라고 사정했건만, 그 애는 늘 똑같은 대답만 했답니다. 아직은 시집갈 마음이 없고, 너무 어려서 혼인의 짐을 감당할 만하다고 여기지 않는다고요. 겉보기에는 그럴듯한 이런 핑계를 대니, 삼촌은 그 애를 더 다그치기를 그만두고, 좀 더 나이가 들어 제 마음에 드는 짝을 만날 수 있을 때까지 기다리기로 했지요. 부모가 자식을 그 뜻을 거슬러 가며 짝지어 줄 일은 아니라고 했으니까요. 참으로 옳은 말이었습니다. 그런데 누구도 예기치 못한 어느 날, 보십시오! 그 얌전하던 마르셀라가 양치기 처녀로 변신해서 나타난 겁니다. 삼촌이며 그러지 말라고 애써 말리던 온 마을 사람들에도 아랑곳없이, 마을의 다른 양치기 처녀들과 함께 들판에 나가 제 양 떼를 돌보기 시작했지요. 그렇게 그 애가 사람들 앞에 모습을 드러내고 그 미모가 훤히 드러나자, 얼마나 많은 부잣집 젊은이들이, 신사며 농사꾼이며 할 것 없이 크리소스토모의 차림새를 따라 입고는 이 들판을 돌아다니며 그 애한테 구애했는지 이루 다 말씀드릴 수 없을 정돕니다. 그중 하나가, 앞서 말씀드렸듯, 우리의 죽은 벗이었는데, 사람들 말로는 그분이 그 애를 사랑한 게 아니라 흠모했다더군요. 하지만, 마르셀라가 그토록 자유롭고 거리낌 없는 삶을, 바깥출입을 삼가기는커녕 도무지 삼가지 않는 삶을 택했다고 해서, 그 애가 자기 정절과 정숙함을 헐뜯을 만한 빌미를, 아니 그런 빌미 비슷한 것조차 준 적이 있다고 여기시면 안 됩니다. 오히려 그 애가 제 명예를 지키는 경계심이 어찌나 대단한지, 그 애한테 구애하고 추근대는 모든 이들 가운데 누구도, 제 바람을 이루리라는 희망을 그 애가 아무리 작게나마 주었노라고 으스댄 적이 없고, 또 사실대로 으스댈 수도 없답니다. 그 애는 목동들과 어울리고 이야기 나누기를 피하거나 꺼리지 않고, 그들을 정중하고 친절하게 대하지만, 그들 가운데 누구라도 제 속내를, 그것이 혼인처럼 떳떳하고 거룩한 것일지라도 그 애한테 털어놓으려 들면, 투석기로 쏘듯 그자를 내쳐 버립니다. 이런 성미 탓에 그 애는 이 고장에 역병이 든 것보다 더 큰 해를 끼치고 있지요. 그 애의 상냥함과 미모는 곁에 어울리는 이들의 마음을 끌어다 그 애를 사랑하고 구애하게 만들지만, 그 애의 멸시와 솔직함은 그자들을 절망의 벼랑 끝으로 내모니까요. 그래서 그자들은 그 애를 두고 큰 소리로 모질다느니 매정하다느니, 그 성품을 잘 드러내는 그런 부류의 다른 이름들로 외쳐 대는 것 말고는 무어라 할 말을 모릅니다. 나리, 여기에 얼마간 머무시면, 그 애를 좇다 내쳐진 이들의 탄식으로 이 언덕과 골짜기가 울려 퍼지는 소리를 들으실 겁니다. 여기서 멀지 않은 곳에 키 큰 너도밤나무가 스무 그루 남짓 있는데, 그 매끈한 껍질마다 마르셀라의 이름이 새겨지고 적혀 있지 않은 게 하나도 없고, 몇몇 나무 위에는 같은 나무에 왕관까지 새겨져 있어서, 마치 그 애를 사랑하는 이가, 마르셀라야말로 인간의 모든 미모의 왕관을 쓰고 또 받을 만하다고 더욱 또렷이 말하려는 듯하답니다. 여기서는 한 목동이 한숨짓고, 저기서는 또 한 목동이 탄식하지요. 저기서는 사랑 노래가, 여기서는 절망의 비가가 들려옵니다. 어떤 이는 참나무나 바위 밑에 앉아 밤을 꼬박 지새우고, 우는 눈을 감지도 못한 채로 아침 햇살이 멍하니 넋 나간 그자를 비추기도 하지요. 또 어떤 이는 한숨에 잠시의 쉼도 없이, 무더운 한여름 정오의 이글거리는 열기 속 타는 모래밭에 드러누워 자비로운 하늘에 호소하기도 합니다. 그렇게 이 사람 저 사람, 이들 모두를 짓밟으며 아리따운 마르셀라는 자유롭고 무심하게 군림하고 있지요. 그 애를 아는 우리 모두는, 그 도도함이 어디까지 가는지, 그토록 무시무시한 성정을 길들이고 그토록 빼어난 미모를 손에 넣게 될 행복한 사내가 누구일지 지켜보고 있는 중입니다. 제가 말씀드린 모든 게 이토록 확실한 사실인 만큼, 아까 우리 젊은이가 들려준, 크리소스토모의 죽음의 까닭에 대한 사람들의 말도 그대로 사실이리라 믿습니다. 그러니 나리, 내일 그분의 장례에 꼭 참석하시길 권합니다. 한번 볼 만할 거예요. 크리소스토모한테는 벗이 많았고, 그분이 묻히라고 일러 둔 곳도 여기서 반 리도 안 되는 거리니까요.\"\n\n\"꼭 그리하겠네.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그토록 흥미로운 이야기를 들려주어 즐거움을 주었으니 고맙구먼.\"\n\n\"오,\" 염소지기가 말했다. \"마르셀라를 사랑한 이들한테 일어난 일이라면 저는 그 절반도 모릅니다. 하지만 어쩌면 내일 길에서 우리한테 들려줄 만한 목동을 마주칠지도 모르지요. 자, 이제 나리는 지붕 밑에 들어가 주무시는 게 좋겠습니다. 밤공기가 상처에 해로울 수도 있으니까요. 제가 발라 드린 약이 있으니 잘못될 염려야 없습니다만.\"\n\n염소지기의 수다를 악마에게나 보내 버리고 싶던 산초 판사는, 제 나름으로 주인에게 페드로의 오두막에 들어가 주무시라고 청했다. 돈키호테는 그리하여, 마르셀라의 연인들을 본받아 남은 밤을 온통 그의 여인 둘시네아를 생각하며 보냈다. 산초 판사는 로시난테와 제 나귀 사이에 자리를 잡고는, 버림받은 연인처럼이 아니라 흠씬 걷어차인 사내처럼 잠들었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII.\n\n날이 동녘의 발코니 사이로 막 모습을 드러내기 시작할 무렵, 여섯 명의 염소지기 가운데 다섯이 돈키호테를 깨우러 와서, 그가 아직도 크리소스토모의 그 유명한 장례식을 보러 갈 마음이 있다면 자기들이 동행하겠노라고 말했다. 그보다 더 바랄 것이 없던 돈키호테는 일어나 산초에게 당장 안장과 길마를 얹으라 일렀고, 산초는 부지런히 그 일을 마쳤으며, 곧 모두가 함께 길을 나섰다. 그들이 채 사분의 일 레과도 못 갔을 때, 두 갈래 길이 만나는 곳에서 검은 양가죽 옷을 입고 사이프러스와 쓴 협죽도로 엮은 화관을 머리에 쓴 여섯쯤 되는 양치기가 자기들 쪽으로 오는 것을 보았다. 그들은 저마다 손에 굵직한 호랑가시나무 지팡이를 들었고, 그들과 더불어 단정한 여행복 차림을 한 말 탄 신사 두 사람이 왔으며, 걸어서 그들을 따르는 하인 셋도 함께 있었다. 만나면서 정중한 인사가 오갔고, 서로 어느 쪽으로 가는 길이냐고 묻고는 모두가 장례식이 치러지는 곳을 향하고 있음을 알게 되어, 그들은 다 같이 길을 이어 갔다.\n\n말 탄 사람 하나가 자기 일행을 향해 이렇게 말했다. \"비발도 님, 이 보기 드문 장례식을 보느라 지체하는 것은 제대로 쓴 시간이라 쳐도 좋을 듯합니다. 저 양치기들이 들려준, 죽은 양치기와 사람 잡은 양치기 처녀에 관한 그 기이한 이야기들로 미루어 보건대, 보기 드문 장례가 아닐 수 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"저도 그리 생각합니다.\" 비발도가 대답했다. \"그것을 보기 위해서라면 하루가 아니라 나흘이라도 지체하겠습니다.\"\n\n돈키호테는 그들에게 마르셀라와 크리소스토모에 관해 무엇을 들었느냐고 물었다. 여행객은 바로 그날 아침 이 양치기들을 만났는데, 그들이 이렇게 상복 차림을 한 것을 보고 어찌하여 그런 모양새로 나섰는지 까닭을 물었다고 대답했다. 그러자 그들 중 하나가 그 까닭을 일러 주며, 마르셀라라는 양치기 처녀의 기이한 행실과 아름다움, 그녀에게 구애한 숱한 이들의 사랑, 그리고 자기들이 지금 장례식에 가고 있는 저 크리소스토모의 죽음을 이야기해 주었다는 것이었다. 요컨대 그는 페드로가 돈키호테에게 들려준 것을 모두 그대로 되풀이했다.\n\n이 대화가 끊어지자, 비발도라 불리는 사람이 또 다른 대화를 시작하여, 이토록 평화로운 고장에서 어찌하여 그런 차림으로 무장하고 다니는 까닭이 무엇이냐고 돈키호테에게 물었다. 이에 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내 천직을 좇는 일은 다른 어떤 차림으로도 다니는 것을 허락하지도 용납하지도 않소이다. 안락한 생활과 향락과 휴식은 나약한 궁정인들을 위해 만들어진 것이고, 고생과 불안과 무기는 오로지 세상이 편력 기사라 부르는 이들만을 위해 생겨나고 만들어진 것이니, 나는 비록 그 자격이 없으나 그들 가운데 가장 보잘것없는 자올시다.\"\n\n이 말을 듣자마자 모두가 그를 미친 사람으로 단정했고, 그 점을 더 확실히 매듭짓고 그의 광기가 어떤 종류인지 알아보고자, 비발도는 편력 기사가 무엇을 뜻하는지 그에게 물어 나갔다.\n\n\"그대들께서는,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"영국의 연대기와 역사를 읽어 본 적이 없으시오? 거기에는 우리가 평범한 카스티야 말로 으레 아르투스 왕이라 부르는 아서 왕의 그 유명한 행적이 기록되어 있는데, 그분에 관해서는 예부터 전해 내려오고 저 대브리튼 왕국 전역에 두루 받아들여진 전설이 있으니, 이 왕은 죽은 것이 아니라 마법의 술법으로 까마귀가 되었으며, 세월이 흐른 뒤 돌아와 다시 다스리며 자신의 왕국과 왕홀을 되찾으리라는 것이외다. 그런 까닭에 그때부터 이날까지 영국인 누구도 까마귀를 죽인 일이 없다는 것은 증명할 수 없는 사실이오. 그런데 바로 이 선량한 왕의 시대에, 저 유명한 원탁의 기사단이라는 기사회가 세워졌고, 호반의 기사 란슬롯과 왕비 귀네비어의 사랑이 거기 적힌 그대로 일어났으니, 그 사이를 잇고 비밀을 나눈 이가 바로 그 더없이 존귀한 부인 킨타뇨나였소. 거기서 우리 스페인에 그토록 잘 알려지고 널리 퍼진 그 노래가 나왔으니—\n\n오, 일찍이 그 어떤 기사도 부인의 손에 이만큼 시중받지 못하였네, 란슬롯 경이 받은 그 시중만큼은, 그가 브리튼에서 왔을 적에—\n\n그의 사랑과 무용에 관한 온갖 달콤하고 아름다운 행적들이 함께 어우러져 있소. 그리하여 그때부터 전해 내려온 이 기사회는 세상의 많고 다양한 곳으로 점점 뻗어 나가고 퍼져 나갔으니, 그 안에서 행적으로 이름을 떨치고 명성을 얻은 이들로는 다섯 대에 걸친 그 모든 아들과 후손을 거느린 강대한 골의 아마디스가 있었고, 용맹한 이르카니아의 펠릭스마르테가 있었으며, 아무리 칭송해도 모자란 티란테 엘 블랑코가 있었고, 거의 우리 당대에 와서는 그리스의 무적 기사 돈 벨리아니스를 우리가 보고 그 이야기를 듣고 더불어 말까지 나누다시피 하였소. 자, 그러니 여러분, 이것이 바로 편력 기사가 된다는 것이며, 내가 말씀드린 것이 그 기사회의 법도이니, 이미 말한 대로 나는 비록 죄 많은 자이나 그것을 직분으로 삼았으며, 앞서 말한 기사들이 직분으로 삼은 그것을 나 또한 직분으로 삼고 있소. 그리하여 나는 이 외딴곳들과 황야를 누비며 모험을 찾아다니고, 약하고 궁핍한 이들을 돕고자 운명이 내게 던져 줄 가장 위험한 일에 내 팔과 이 몸을 맞세우기로 마음속 깊이 결심하고 있는 것이외다.\"\n\n이러한 그의 말로 여행객들은 돈키호테가 제정신이 아님과, 그를 사로잡은 광기의 형태가 어떤 것인지를 충분히 헤아릴 수 있었으니, 처음 그것을 알게 된 모두가 느낀 것과 똑같은 놀라움을 그들도 느꼈다. 그리고 매우 영리하고 활달한 기질의 사람이던 비발도는, 장례식이 치러지는 산까지 가는 데 필요하다던 그 짧은 여정의 무료함을 달래고자, 그가 계속해서 그 터무니없는 소리를 늘어놓을 거리를 마련해 주려 했다. 그래서 그는 그에게 말했다. \"편력 기사 나리, 제 보기에 나리께서는 세상에서 가장 엄격한 직분 가운데 하나를 택하신 듯하니, 카르투지오회 수도사들의 직분조차 그토록 엄격하지는 않으리라 여겨집니다.\"\n\n\"그만큼 엄격할지는 모르나,\" 우리의 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그것이 세상에 그만큼 필요한지는 나로서는 자못 의심스럽소. 진실을 말하자면, 자기 대장이 명하는 것을 실행하는 병사가 그 명을 내리는 대장 자신보다 결코 못한 일을 하는 것이 아니기 때문이오. 내 말인즉, 성직자들은 평화롭고 고요한 가운데 세상의 안녕을 위해 하늘에 기도하나, 우리 병사와 기사들은 그들이 기도하는 바를 실제로 이루어 내니, 지붕 아래가 아니라 한데에서, 여름이면 견딜 수 없는 햇살의 표적이 되고 겨울이면 살을 에는 서리의 표적이 되어, 우리 팔의 힘과 칼날로 그것을 지켜 내는 것이외다. 이리하여 우리는 이 땅 위에서 하느님의 일꾼이며, 그분의 정의가 이 땅에서 이루어지게 하는 그 팔이오. 그리고 전쟁의 일과 그에 관계되고 속한 모든 것은 그지없이 큰 땀과 노고와 분투 없이는 치를 수 없는 것이니, 그것을 직분으로 삼은 자들이 고요한 평화와 안식 속에서 약한 이를 도와 달라고 하느님께 기도하는 자들보다 의심할 바 없이 더 많은 수고를 한다는 결론이 나오는 것이외다. 나는 편력 기사의 천직이 수도실에 든 수도사의 그것만큼 훌륭하다고 말하려는 것도 아니요, 그런 생각이 내 머릿속에 떠오른 것도 아니오. 다만 내가 몸소 견뎌 내는 것에서 미루어 보아, 편력 기사의 천직이 의심할 바 없이 더 고되고 더 두들겨 맞는 것이며, 더 굶주리고 더 목마른 것이며, 더 비참하고 더 누더기를 걸치고 더 이가 들끓는 것임을 헤아릴 따름이오. 옛날의 편력 기사들이 그 한평생에 숱한 고초를 견뎠음은 의심할 까닭이 없기 때문이오. 그리고 그들 가운데 더러가 제 팔의 힘으로 황제의 자리에까지 올랐다 하더라도, 참으로 그것은 피와 땀으로 톡톡히 치른 대가였소. 또한 그 지위에 오른 이들에게 그들을 도와줄 마법사와 현자가 없었더라면, 그들은 야망에서 완전히 좌절하고 희망에서 실망하고 말았을 것이외다.\"\n\n\"저 또한 그리 생각합니다.\" 여행객이 대답했다. \"허나 여러 가지 가운데 한 가지, 편력 기사들에게 제가 보기에 매우 잘못된 점이 있으니, 그것은 그들이 목숨을 잃을 위험이 빤히 보이는 어떤 대단하고 위험한 모험에 막 뛰어들려 할 때, 막상 그 일에 임하는 순간 같은 위험에 처한 선량한 그리스도교도라면 누구나 마땅히 해야 할 바, 곧 하느님께 자신을 의탁할 생각은 결코 하지 않고, 그 대신 마치 자기들의 여신이라도 되는 양 지극한 정성으로 자기 귀부인에게 자신을 의탁한다는 점입니다. 제게는 그것이 어딘지 이교도의 냄새를 풍기는 일로 여겨집니다.\"\n\n\"이보시오,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그것은 무슨 일이 있어도 빠뜨릴 수 없는 일이니, 그러지 않고 행동하는 편력 기사는 망신을 당할 것이오. 편력 기사도에서는 어떤 큰 무술의 위업에 임하면서 자기 귀부인을 눈앞에 모신 편력 기사가, 자신이 막 떠맡으려는 위태로운 일에서 자기를 어여삐 보아 지켜 달라고 그 눈으로 간청하듯, 부드럽고 사랑스럽게 그녀에게 눈길을 돌리는 것이 통례이자 관습이외다. 또한 아무도 그의 말을 듣지 못한다 하더라도, 그는 이 사이로 어떤 말을 읊조리며 온 마음을 다해 그녀에게 자신을 의탁해야 하는 법이니, 이에 관한 사례는 그 역사들 속에 헤아릴 수 없이 많소. 그렇다고 이것으로 미루어 그들이 하느님께 자신을 의탁하는 것을 빠뜨린다고 여겨서는 아니 되니, 그 일에 임하는 동안에도 그리할 시간과 기회는 있을 것이기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다 하더라도,\" 여행객이 대답했다. \"저는 여전히 얼마간 의심스럽습니다. 두 편력 기사 사이에 말다툼이 일어나, 이 일 저 일로 번지다가 분노에 불이 붙어 두 사람이 말머리를 돌려 들판을 한참 벌려 잡고는, 그다음 더 말할 것도 없이 전속력으로 마주 돌격하여, 그 한가운데서 자기 귀부인에게 자신을 의탁하곤 하는 대목을 자주 읽었기 때문입니다. 그런데 그 맞부딪침에서 흔히 벌어지는 일이란, 한 사람은 적의 창에 몸을 꿰뚫린 채 말 엉덩이 너머로 떨어지고, 다른 한 사람은 말갈기를 부여잡아야만 겨우 땅에 떨어지는 것을 면하는 일입니다. 한데 죽은 자가 이렇듯 순식간에 벌어진 일 가운데 어느 겨를에 하느님께 자신을 의탁할 시간이 있었는지 저로서는 모르겠습니다. 돌격 한복판에서 귀부인에게 자신을 의탁하느라 쓴 그 말들을, 차라리 그리스도교도로서의 본분과 의무에 바쳤더라면 더 나았을 것입니다. 게다가 제 믿음으로는, 편력 기사가 모두 자신을 의탁할 귀부인을 둔 것은 아닌 듯하니, 그들이 모두 사랑에 빠진 것은 아니기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"그것은 있을 수 없는 일이오.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"귀부인 없는 편력 기사란 있을 수 없다고 나는 단언하오. 하늘이 별을 가지는 것이 당연하고 마땅하듯, 그런 이에게 사랑에 빠지는 것이 그만큼 당연하고 마땅한 일이기 때문이오. 사랑 없는 편력 기사가 나오는 역사란 분명 본 적이 없으니, 그 까닭은 단순하오. 사랑이 없으면 그는 적법한 기사가 아니라 사생아로 여겨질 것이며, 그 기사도의 성채에 문으로 들어선 것이 아니라 도둑이나 강도처럼 담을 넘어 들어온 자로 여겨질 것이기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"그렇기는 하나,\" 여행객이 말했다. \"제 기억이 옳다면, 용맹한 골의 아마디스의 아우인 돈 갈라오르는 자신을 의탁할 만한 특정한 귀부인을 둔 적이 한 번도 없었으되, 그럼에도 결코 덜 존경받지 않았으며 매우 굳세고 이름난 기사였다고 읽은 듯합니다.\"\n\n이에 우리의 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"이보시오, 제비 한 마리가 왔다고 여름이 오는 것은 아니오. 게다가 나는 그 기사가 남몰래 깊이 사랑에 빠져 있었음을 알고 있소. 그뿐 아니라 마음에 드는 모든 여인에게 사랑을 느끼던 그 버릇은, 그가 어찌할 수 없던 타고난 성향이었소. 허나 요컨대, 그가 자기 뜻의 주인으로 삼은 단 한 사람을 두고 그녀에게 매우 자주, 그리고 매우 은밀히 자신을 의탁했음은 지극히 분명하니, 그는 입이 무거운 기사임을 자랑으로 삼았기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 편력 기사라면 누구나 사랑에 빠져 있어야 한다는 것이 필수라면,\" 여행객이 말했다. \"나리께서도 그 기사회에 속하셨으니 그러하시리라 짐작해도 무방하겠지요. 그리고 나리께서 돈 갈라오르처럼 입이 무거운 것을 자랑으로 삼지 않으신다면, 이 모든 일행의 이름으로, 또 저 자신의 이름으로 더없이 간곡히 청하오니, 나리의 귀부인의 이름과 고향과 신분과 아름다움을 저희에게 알려 주십시오. 나리 같은 기사에게 사랑받고 섬김을 받는다는 것을 온 세상이 안다면, 그분께서는 스스로를 행운아로 여기실 테니까요.\"\n\n이에 돈키호테가 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"내 달콤한 원수가 내가 그녀를 섬긴다는 것을 세상이 아는 것을 기뻐하는지 아닌지는 단언할 수 없소. 그토록 정중히 물어 주신 데 대해 답할 수 있는 것이라곤 다만 이것뿐이오. 그녀의 이름은 둘시네아요, 고향은 라만차의 한 마을 엘 토보소이며, 신분은 적어도 공주는 되어야 하니 그녀가 내 여왕이요 귀부인이기 때문이오. 그리고 그녀의 아름다움은 인간을 넘어선 것이니, 시인들이 자기들 귀부인에게 갖다 붙이는 그 불가능하고 허황된 온갖 아름다움의 속성이 모두 그녀에게서 사실로 증명되기 때문이오. 그녀의 머리카락은 황금이요, 이마는 엘리시온의 들판이요, 눈썹은 무지개요, 두 눈은 태양이요, 두 뺨은 장미요, 입술은 산호요, 이는 진주요, 목은 설화석고요, 가슴은 대리석이요, 두 손은 상아요, 흰 살결은 눈이며, 정숙함이 눈에서 가려 둔 것들은, 내 생각건대 또 짐작건대, 이성적인 헤아림으로 다만 기릴 수 있을 뿐 견주지는 못할 것이외다.\"\n\n\"그분의 가문과 혈통과 조상을 알고 싶습니다.\" 비발도가 말했다.\n\n이에 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그녀는 고대 로마의 쿠르티우스가나 카이우스가나 스키피오가 출신도 아니요, 근래의 콜론나가나 오르시니가 출신도 아니며, 카탈루냐의 몬카다가나 레케세네스가 출신도 아니요, 발렌시아의 레베야가나 비야노바가 출신도 아니외다. 또한 아라곤의 팔라폭스가, 누사가, 로카베르티가, 코레야가, 루나가, 알라곤가, 우레아가, 포세가, 구레아가 출신도 아니며, 카스티야의 세르다가, 만리케가, 멘도사가, 구스만가 출신도 아니요, 포르투갈의 알렌카스트로가, 파야스가, 메네세스가 출신도 아니외다. 다만 그녀는 라만차 엘 토보소 사람들 가운데 하나이니, 비록 근래의 가문이기는 하나 앞으로 올 시대의 가장 빛나는 가문들에 고귀한 피의 원천을 마련해 줄 수 있는 혈통이외다. 이에 대해서는, 오를란도의 무기로 세운 전승비 발치에 제르비노가 적어 둔 그 조건으로가 아니라면, 그 누구도 나와 다투지 못할 것이오. 그가 이르기를,\n\n이것을 그 누구도 옮기지 말라, 롤랑과 제 힘을 겨룰 엄두 없는 자는.\"\n\n\"제 가문은 라레도의 카초핀가 출신입니다만,\" 여행객이 말했다. \"라만차 엘 토보소 가문과 감히 견주려 들지는 않겠습니다. 솔직히 말씀드리면, 그런 성씨는 지금껏 제 귀에 한 번도 닿은 적이 없긴 합니다만.\"\n\n\"무엇이라!\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그것이 한 번도 그대 귀에 닿은 적이 없다는 말이오?\"\n\n나머지 일행은 두 사람의 대화에 잔뜩 귀를 기울이며 따라가고 있었고, 염소지기와 양치기들조차 우리의 돈키호테가 얼마나 심하게 제정신이 아닌지를 알아차렸다. 오직 산초 판사만이 자기 주인이 누구인지를 알고 태어날 때부터 그를 알아 온 터라, 주인이 한 말이 진실이라고 여겼다. 다만 그가 도무지 믿기 어려운 것이 하나 있었으니, 바로 아름다운 둘시네아 델 토보소에 관한 대목이었다. 자신이 엘 토보소 가까이 살았음에도 그런 이름도, 그런 공주도 도무지 들어 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그들이 이렇게 이야기를 나누며 가고 있을 때, 두 높은 산 사이의 골짜기로 양치기 스무 명쯤이 내려오는 것이 보였으니, 모두 검은 양털 가죽을 걸치고 화관을 머리에 쓰고 있었는데, 나중에 보니 더러는 주목으로, 더러는 사이프러스로 엮은 것이었다. 그 가운데 여섯이 갖가지 꽃과 나뭇가지로 덮인 관을 메고 있었다. 이를 본 염소지기 하나가 말했다. \"저기 오는 자들이 크리소스토모의 시신을 메고 오는 이들이고, 저 산기슭이 바로 그가 자기를 묻어 달라고 명한 곳이라오.\" 그리하여 그들은 서둘러 그곳에 다다랐고, 도착했을 무렵에는 시신을 메고 온 이들이 관을 땅에 내려놓았으며, 그들 가운데 넷이 날카로운 곡괭이로 단단한 바위 옆에 무덤을 파고 있었다. 그들은 서로 정중히 인사를 나누었고, 그러고 나서 돈키호테와 그를 따라온 이들은 관을 살펴보려 몸을 돌렸는데, 꽃으로 덮인 그 위에는 양치기 차림을 한 시신이 보였으니, 겉보기에 서른 살가량 되어 보였고, 죽어서도 살아생전에 그 이목구비가 준수하고 풍채가 늠름하였음을 드러내고 있었다. 관 위 그의 둘레에는 책 몇 권과 펼쳐지거나 접힌 여러 장의 종이가 놓여 있었다. 그리고 지켜보는 이들도, 무덤을 파는 이들도, 그곳에 있던 다른 모든 이도 이상한 침묵을 지키고 있었으니, 마침내 시신을 메고 온 이들 가운데 하나가 다른 이에게 말했다. \"암브로시오, 여기가 크리소스토모가 말하던 곳인지 잘 살펴보시오. 그가 유언에서 지시한 것이 어김없이 지켜지기를 그대가 그토록 바라고 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"여기가 그곳이오.\" 암브로시오가 대답했다. \"이곳에서 여러 번, 내 가엾은 벗은 자기 가혹한 운명의 이야기를 내게 들려주었소. 바로 여기서 그는 인류의 저 철천지원수를 처음으로 보았다고 했고, 또한 여기서 처음으로 그녀에게 자기 연정을 고백했다 했으니, 그것은 정성스러운 만큼이나 떳떳한 것이었소. 그리고 바로 여기서 끝내 마르셀라가 그를 비웃고 물리쳐, 그 비참한 한평생의 비극에 종지부를 찍게 했소. 이토록 큰 불행을 기리고자, 그는 여기, 영원한 망각의 품속에 누이기를 바랐던 것이오.\" 그러고는 돈키호테와 여행객들을 향해 돌아서서 말을 이었다. \"여러분, 여러분이 가엾이 여기는 눈으로 바라보고 있는 저 시신은, 하늘이 그 풍성한 재능을 듬뿍 내려 준 한 영혼의 거처였소. 저것이 바로 크리소스토모의 시신이니, 그는 재치에서 견줄 이가 없었고, 예의에서 맞설 이가 없었으며, 점잖은 풍채에서 따를 이가 없었고, 우정에서는 불사조였으며, 한없이 너그러웠고, 거만함 없이 진중했고, 천박함 없이 유쾌했으니, 요컨대 선함을 이루는 모든 것에서 으뜸이었고, 불행을 이루는 모든 것에서도 그 누구에게 뒤지지 않았소. 그는 깊이 사랑했으나 미움을 받았고, 흠모했으나 멸시당했으며, 들짐승에게 구애했고, 대리석에게 호소했으며, 바람을 좇았고, 황야를 향해 부르짖었으며, 배은망덕을 섬겼으니, 그 대가로 한창나이에 죽음의 먹이가 되어 한 양치기 처녀의 손에 목숨을 잘렸소. 그가 사람들의 기억 속에 길이 남기고자 했던 그 처녀 말이오. 여러분이 보시는 이 종이들이 그것을 넉넉히 증명해 줄 수 있을 터이나, 그가 자기 시신을 흙에 맡긴 다음에는 이것들을 불에 맡기라고 내게 명하였소.\"\n\n\"그것들을 그 임자보다 더 모질고 잔인하게 다루시려는 게로군요.\" 비발도가 말했다. \"전혀 분별없는 것을 명하는 자의 뜻을 따르는 것은 옳지도 마땅하지도 않은 일이니까요. 만일 아우구스투스 카이사르가 저 신과 같은 만토바 사람이 유언에 남긴 지시를 실행하도록 허락했더라면, 그것은 분별 있는 일이 못 되었을 것입니다. 그러니 암브로시오 님, 그대가 벗의 시신을 흙에 맡기더라도 그의 글까지 망각에 맡겨서는 안 됩니다. 그가 마음의 쓰라림 속에서 그런 명을 내렸다 하더라도, 그대가 분별없이 그것을 따르는 것은 옳지 않으니까요. 도리어 그 종이들에 생명을 줌으로써, 마르셀라의 잔혹함이 길이 살아남게 하여, 다가올 세대의 모든 사람에게 그와 같은 위험에 빠지는 것을 피하고 멀리하라는 경계로 삼게 하십시오. 이미 저와, 여기 온 저희 모두는 사랑에 사로잡혀 가슴이 찢긴 이 그대의 벗 이야기를 알고 있고, 또한 그대의 우정과, 그의 죽음의 까닭과, 그가 생의 끝에 남긴 지시도 알고 있습니다. 그 슬픈 이야기에서, 마르셀라의 잔혹함이 얼마나 컸고, 크리소스토모의 사랑이 얼마나 컸으며, 그대 우정의 충직함이 얼마나 컸는지, 더불어 미친 듯한 열정이 눈앞에 열어 주는 그 길을 무모하게 좇는 이들을 기다리는 결말이 어떠한지를 헤아릴 수 있습니다. 어젯밤 저희는 크리소스토모의 죽음과 그가 여기 묻히리라는 것을 알았고, 호기심과 연민에서 곧은길을 벗어나, 들었을 때 그토록 저희 동정심을 움직인 것을 두 눈으로 직접 보러 오기로 마음먹었습니다. 그리고 그 동정심을 헤아려, 또한 가능하다면 애도로써 그것을 입증하고픈 저희 바람을 헤아려, 훌륭한 암브로시오 님께 청하오니, 아니, 적어도 저 자신의 몫으로 그대에게 간청하오니, 저 종이들을 불태우는 대신 그중 몇 장을 제가 가져갈 수 있도록 허락해 주십시오.\"\n\n그러고는 양치기의 대답도 기다리지 않고 손을 뻗어 자기에게 가장 가까이 있던 것 몇 장을 집어 들었다. 이를 본 암브로시오가 말했다. \"이보시오, 예의를 지켜, 그대가 집어 든 것에 대해서는 그대 청을 들어주겠소만, 나머지를 불태우는 것을 내가 그만두기를 바라는 것은 헛된 일이오.\"\n\n종이에 무슨 내용이 담겼는지 보고 싶어 안달이 난 비발도는 그중 하나를 곧장 펼쳤고, 그 표제가 「절망의 노래」임을 보았다.\n\n암브로시오가 그것을 듣고 말했다. \"그것이 그 가엾은 이가 쓴 마지막 종이올시다. 그러니, 이보시오, 그의 불행이 그를 어떤 결말로 몰아갔는지 보시도록, 모두가 들을 수 있게 그것을 읽어 주시오. 무덤이 다 파이기를 기다리는 동안 그럴 시간은 넉넉할 것이오.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다.\" 비발도가 말했다. 곁에 선 모두가 똑같이 듣고 싶어 했으므로 그를 둘러싸고 모여들었고, 그는 큰 소리로 읽어 내려가니, 그것은 다음과 같았다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV.\n\n그대 잔인함으로 그토록 바라거니와 그대 폭정의 무자비한 가혹함을 입에서 입으로, 땅에서 땅으로 퍼뜨리려 하기에,\n\n# THE LAY OF CHRYSOSTOM\n\n나는 지옥마저 강제하여 이 짓밟힌 가슴에 깊은 비탄의 가락을 빌리리니, 마땅한 말을 토해 낼 내 절박함을 채우기 위함이라. 내가 견디는 모든 것, 그대가 저지른 모든 것을 이야기로 빚어내려 애쓰는 동안, 저 무시무시한 목소리는 굽이쳐 흘러, 더 큰 고통을 위해 갈가리 찢긴 내 오장의 조각을 실어 가리라. 그러니 들으라, 감미로운 화음이 아니라, 이 가슴 깊은 쓰라림에서 미친 절망이 비틀어 짜낸 불협화음을, 내 마음을 달래고 그대 마음에 가시를 심으려 함이니. 사자의 포효, 사나운 늑대의 야만스러운 울부짖음, 비늘 덮인 뱀의 소름 끼치는 쉭쉭거림, 아직 이름조차 없는 괴물들의 끔찍한 비명,\n\n까마귀의 불길한 까악거림, 쉼 없는 바다와 씨름하는 거센 바람의 공허한 신음, 패배한 황소의 분노에 찬 울부짖음, 홀로 남은 비둘기의 애처로운 흐느낌, 부러움 산 올빼미의 슬픈 가락, 지옥의 음울한 합창에서 솟아오르는 비탄의 절규, 이 모두가 한 소리로 뒤섞여 분별을 어지럽히니, 이 모든 것이 와서 내 영혼의 하소연을 도우라, 나 같은 고통은 새로운 노래의 가락을 요구하므로. 그 불협화음의 메아리는 타호 아버지 강이 굽이치는 곳에서도, 올리브 늘어선 베티스 강의 기슭에서도 들리지 않으리니, 바위에게\n\n혹은 깊은 동굴 속에서 내 하소연을 들려주리라, 생기 없는 혀로 살아 있는 말을 빌려서. 혹은 어두운 골짜기에서, 혹은 인적 없는 바닷가에서, 사람의 발길도 햇살도 닿지 않는 곳에서. 혹은 느릿한 나일 강이 길러 낸 독을 뿜는 괴물들의 무리 가운데서. 비록 외딴 적막한 곳을 향해 내 슬픔의 쉰, 흐릿한 메아리가 울려 퍼질지라도, 그대의 견줄 데 없는 잔인함과 나의 음울한 운명은 그것을 이 너른 온 세상으로 실어 가리라. 멸시는 죽일 힘이 있고, 인내는 거짓이든 참이든 의심에 베여 죽으며, 질투의 위력은 목숨을 앗아 가고, 오랜 부재는 삶을 황량한 공허로 만드니,\n\n행복의 희망도, 잊힐까 늘 두려워하는 자에게는 평안을 줄 수 없으며, 피할 길 없는 죽음이 문간에서 기다린다. 그러나 나는, 어떤 기이한 기적으로, 부재와 질투와 멸시의 먹이가 되어 살아가니, 확신에 시달리듯 의심에 시달리고, 잊힌 채 홀로 내 불꽃을 키우도록 버려졌도다. 이렇게 견디는 동안, 음울함을 가로질러 나를 기쁘게 할 희망의 빛 한 줄기도 오지 않으며, 내 절망 속에서 그것을 바라지도 않고, 오히려 고칠 길 없는 비탄에 매달려, 영영토록 모든 희망을 저버리노라. 두려움 있는 곳에 희망이 있을 수 있으랴? 그것이 옳으랴, 두려움의 근거가 훨씬 더 확실할진대? 천 군데 마음의 상처를 통해 드러나는데도\n\n내 어찌 질투에 눈을 감아야 하랴? 멸시가 베일을 벗는 것을 보고서, 그리고 — 쓰라린 변화여! — 그 모든 의심이 확신으로 바뀌고, 아름다운 진실이 거짓으로 둔갑하는 것을 보고서, 누군들 불신에 길을 열어 주지 않으랴? 오, 그대 사랑의 왕국의 사나운 폭군이여, 오, 질투여! 이 두 손에 사슬을 채우라, 그리고 그대의 가장 질긴 밧줄로 나를 묶으라, 멸시여. 그러나, 가엾어라! 그 모든 것을 이기고서 내 고통이 그대들에 대한 기억을 잠겨 버리는구나. 이제 나는 죽노라, 삶에서도 죽음에서도 내게 행복의 희망이라곤 없으니, 여전히 내 환상에 애틋하게 매달리리라.\n\n잘 사랑하는 자가 지혜롭다 말하리라, 그리고 가장 자유로운 영혼이란 오래된 폭군 사랑에 가장 깊이 예속된 영혼이라 말하리라. 나의 원수인 그 여인이 저 고운 몸에 그만큼 고운 마음을 지녔다 말하리라, 그녀의 차가움은 다만 내가 받아 마땅한 것이며, 그가 내리는 고통의 힘으로 사랑이 부드러운 통치로 그 왕국을 다스린다 말하리라. 이렇게 스스로를 속이고, 모진 속박에 매여, 그녀의 멸시로 내가 처하게 된 이 비참한 삶의 누더기를 닳아 없애며, 저장된 축복의 관에 대한 모든 희망을 버린 채, 이 영혼과 몸을 바람에 내어 주리라. 그대의 부당함이 빌미가 되어 내가 혐오하는 이 고단한 삶을 버리게 하니, 이 상처 입은 가슴으로 그대가 볼 수 있듯,\n\n내가 얼마나 기꺼이 그대의 제물이 되는지를, 내 죽음이 행여 눈물 한 방울의 값이 있을지라도, 그대 빛나는 두 눈에 깃든 맑은 하늘을 흐리지 말라. 내 마음을 그대의 먹이로 삼은 그 죄를 그대가 조금이라도 속죄하기를 나는 바라지 않으니, 오히려 그대의 웃음이 흥겹게 울려 퍼지게 하여 내 죽음이 그대의 축제임을 증명하라. 그대에게 이런 부탁을 하다니 나는 어리석구나! 잘 아노라, 나의 때 이른 종말로 그대의 영광이 더해짐을. 이제 그때가 왔도다. 지옥의 심연에서 목마른 탄탈로스여 오라, 잔혹한 돌을 떠받치는\n\n시시포스여 오라, 독수리를 거느린 티티오스여, 수레바퀴를 거느린 익시온이여 오라, 그리고 그칠 줄 모르는 노역의 자매들이여 오라. 그 모든 고통을 이 가슴에 옮겨 놓고서, (절망에 그런 공물이 마땅하다면) 수의조차 받을 자격 없는 주검을 두고 가장 깊은 가락으로 구슬픈 만가를 부르라. 문을 지키는 머리 셋 달린 파수꾼과, 지옥의 그 모든 괴물 같은 무리도 이 애처로운 합주에 함께하라. 죽은 연인에게는 이보다 더 어울리는 장례가 없으리라 여겨지나니. 절망의 노래여, 이 슬퍼하는 가슴을 떠나 나아갈 때 슬퍼하지 말라. 내 비참함은\n\n너를 낳은 그 까닭에 행운을 가져다주나니, 그러니 무덤 속에서조차 슬픔을 떨쳐 버리라.\n\n「크리소스토모의 노래」는 듣는 이들의 호평을 받았으나, 낭송한 자는 자신이 들어 온 마르셀라의 정숙함과 단정함과는 어울리지 않는 듯하다고 말했으니, 크리소스토모가 그 안에서 질투와 의심과 부재를 탓하며, 그 모두가 마르셀라의 좋은 이름과 명성을 해치는 것이었기 때문이다. 이에 친구의 가장 은밀한 속생각까지 잘 아는 사람으로서 암브로시오가 대답했다. \"나리, 그 의심을 풀어 드리고자 말씀드리자면, 이 가엾은 사람이 이 노래를 지었을 때 그는 마르셀라에게서 떨어져 있었으니, 부재가 흔히 그러하듯 자기에게도 효력을 발휘하는지 시험해 보려고 스스로 그녀에게서 멀어졌던 것입니다. 그리고 추방당한 연인에게는 모든 것이 괴로움을 안기고 온갖 두려움이 따라다니듯, 참인 양 두려워하던 상상 속의 질투와 의심이 크리소스토모를 괴롭혔던 것이지요. 그러니 마르셀라의 미덕에 관해 소문이 전하는 그대로의 진실은 흔들림이 없으며, 그녀에 대해서는 질투조차도 잔인하고 다소 거만하며 무척 쌀쌀맞다는 것 말고는 어떤 흠도 잡아서는 안 되고 잡을 수도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이오.\" 비발도가 말했다. 그러고는 불에서 건져 낸 종이 가운데 또 한 장을 읽으려던 참에, 뜻밖에도 그들의 눈앞에 나타난 어떤 놀라운 환영(그렇게 보였으므로)에 의해 멈추게 되었으니, 그들이 무덤을 파고 있던 바위 꼭대기에 양치기 처녀 마르셀라가 나타난 것인데, 어찌나 아름다웠던지 그 아름다움이 소문보다 더하였다. 그때까지 그녀를 한 번도 본 적 없는 이들은 경탄과 침묵 속에 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀를 늘 보아 오던 이들도 한 번도 본 적 없는 이들 못지않게 놀라워했다. 그러나 암브로시오는 그녀를 보는 순간, 역연한 분노에 차서 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"네가 혹여, 이 산악의 잔인한 바실리스크여, 네 잔인함이 목숨을 앗아 간 이 가엾은 자의 상처에서 네가 있으면 피가 흐르는지 보려고 왔느냐, 아니면 네 변덕이 빚어낸 잔혹한 소행을 두고 의기양양해하려고 왔느냐, 아니면 또 다른 무자비한 네로처럼 저 높은 곳에서 잿더미가 된 자기 로마의 폐허를 내려다보듯 하려고, 혹은 네 오만함으로 이 불운한 주검을 짓밟으려고 왔느냐, 마치 그 배은망덕한 딸이 제 아비 타르퀴니우스를 짓밟았듯이? 네가 무엇 하러 왔는지, 혹은 무엇을 원하는지 어서 말하라. 크리소스토모의 생각이 살아생전 너에게 복종하기를 한 번도 어기지 않았음을 내가 아는 만큼, 그가 죽었어도 스스로 그의 벗이라 일컫는 이 모든 이가 너에게 복종하도록 내가 만들 터이니.\"\n\n\"암브로시오여, 나는 그대가 말한 그 어떤 목적으로 온 것이 아니라,\" 마르셀라가 대답했다. \"오직 나 자신을 변호하고, 자기들의 슬픔과 크리소스토모의 죽음을 두고 나를 탓하는 모든 이가 얼마나 이치에 어긋나는지를 밝히려고 왔을 뿐입니다. 그러니 여기 계신 모든 분께 청하노니 내게 귀를 기울여 주십시오. 분별 있는 사람들에게 진실을 깨우치는 데에는 많은 시간도 많은 말도 필요치 않을 것입니다. 그대들 말로는 하늘이 나를 아름답게 지으셨다 하며, 그것도 어찌나 아름다운지 그대들이 어쩌지 못한 채 내 아름다움에 이끌려 나를 사랑하게 된다 합니다. 그리고 그대들이 내게 보이는 사랑 때문에, 내가 그대들을 사랑해야 마땅하다고 말하며 심지어 다그치기까지 합니다. 하느님께서 내게 주신 그 타고난 분별로 나는 아름다운 모든 것이 사랑을 끌어당김을 알지만, 사랑받는다는 이유로 그 아름다움 때문에 사랑받는 것이 자기를 사랑하는 자를 사랑해야 할 의무가 어떻게 생기는지는 알 수가 없습니다. 게다가 아름다운 것을 사랑하는 자가 추할 수도 있는 노릇이요, 추함이란 혐오스러운 것이니, '내 그대 아름다우니 그대를 사랑하노라, 내 비록 추하나 그대는 나를 사랑해야 하느니라' 하고 말하는 것은 참으로 어리석은 일입니다. 그러나 설령 양쪽의 아름다움이 동등하다 하더라도, 그렇다고 마음의 끌림까지 같아야 한다는 법은 없으니, 모든 아름다움이 다 사랑을 불러일으키는 것은 아니어서 어떤 것은 마음을 사로잡지 못한 채 눈만 즐겁게 할 뿐입니다. 만일 온갖 종류의 아름다움이 다 사랑을 불러일으키고 마음을 사로잡는다면, 의지는 어느 하나도 택하지 못한 채 정처 없이 이리저리 헤맬 터이니, 아름다운 대상이 무수히 많은 만큼 마음의 끌림도 무수히 많아야 할 것이기 때문입니다. 그런데 내가 듣기로 참된 사랑은 나뉠 수 없는 것이며, 강요된 것이 아니라 자발적인 것이어야 합니다. 내가 믿는 바대로 만일 이것이 참이라면, 그대들이 나를 사랑한다고 말한다는 것 말고는 아무 까닭도 없이, 어찌하여 내가 억지로 내 의지를 굽히기를 바라는 것입니까? 아니, 말해 보십시오, 하늘이 나를 아름답게 지으신 것처럼 추하게 지으셨다면, 나를 사랑하지 않는다고 내가 그대들을 정당하게 탓할 수 있었겠습니까? 더욱이 그대들은 기억해야 합니다. 내가 지닌 이 아름다움은 내가 택한 것이 아니니, 그것이 어떤 것이든 하늘이 그 너그러움으로 내가 청하지도 택하지도 않았는데 내게 주신 것입니다. 그리고 독사가 비록 그 독으로 죽이기는 하나, 그 독은 자연이 준 선물인지라 그것을 지녔다고 탓받아 마땅하지 않은 것처럼, 나 또한 아름답다는 이유로 비난받아 마땅하지 않습니다. 정숙한 여인에게 아름다움이란 멀리 있는 불이나 날 선 칼과 같아서, 너무 가까이 오지 않는 이들은 그 하나에 데지도 않고 그 다른 하나에 베이지도 않기 때문입니다. 명예와 미덕은 마음의 장식이니, 그것이 없으면 몸이 비록 아름답다 한들 아름답다 일컬어질 자격이 없습니다. 그런데 만일 정숙함이 마음과 몸에 우아함과 매력을 더해 주는 미덕 가운데 하나라면, 어찌하여 그 아름다움 때문에 사랑받는 여인이, 오직 자기 쾌락만을 위해 온 힘과 기운을 다해 그것을 빼앗으려 드는 자를 만족시키자고 그것을 내어 주어야 하겠습니까? 나는 자유롭게 태어났고, 자유롭게 살고자 들판의 고적함을 택했습니다. 산의 나무들에서 나는 벗을 찾고, 시냇물의 맑은 물은 나의 거울이며, 나무들과 물에게 나는 내 생각과 매력을 털어놓습니다. 나는 멀리 있는 불이요, 치워 둔 칼입니다. 나를 보게 하여 사랑을 품게 한 이들을 나는 말로써 깨우쳐 주었으니, 만일 그들의 갈망이 희망을 양식 삼아 살아간다면 — 나는 크리소스토모에게도 다른 누구에게도 그런 희망을 준 적이 없거니와 — 누군가의 죽음이 내 소행이라고 정당하게 말할 수는 없습니다. 그를 죽인 것은 나의 잔인함이라기보다 오히려 그 자신의 고집이었기 때문입니다. 그리고 만일 그의 소원이 떳떳한 것이었으니 내가 그에 응했어야 마땅하다고 나를 책망한다면, 나는 이렇게 답하겠습니다. 지금 그의 무덤이 만들어진 바로 이 자리에서 그가 자기 뜻의 순결함을 내게 밝혔을 때, 나는 그에게 내 뜻은 영원한 고독 속에 사는 것이며, 내 은둔의 열매와 내 아름다움의 전리품은 오직 대지만이 누리게 하리라고 말했다고. 이렇게 터놓고 고백한 뒤에도 그가 희망을 거슬러 고집을 부리고 바람을 거슬러 키를 잡기로 택했다면, 그가 자신의 미망의 심연에 빠져든 것이 무어 그리 놀랄 일이겠습니까? 만일 내가 그를 부추겼다면 나는 거짓된 사람이요, 만일 내가 그를 만족시켰다면 나는 나 자신의 더 나은 결심과 뜻을 거슬러 행동한 셈이 됩니다. 경고를 받고도 그는 고집을 부렸고, 미움을 받지 않고도 그는 절망했습니다. 이제 그의 고통을 내 탓으로 돌리는 것이 과연 이치에 맞는지 헤아려 보십시오. 속은 자는 하소연하라 하고, 부추겨진 희망이 헛되이 끝난 자는 절망에 빠지라 하고, 내가 꾈 자는 우쭐대라 하고, 내가 받아 줄 자는 뽐내라 하십시오. 그러나 내가 아무 약속도 하지 않고, 아무 속임수도 부리지 않으며, 꾀지도 받아 주지도 않는 자는 나를 잔인하다거나 살인자라 부르지 말라 하십시오. 내가 운명에 의해 사랑하는 것은 아직 하늘의 뜻이 아니었으며, 내가 선택에 의해 사랑하기를 기대하는 것은 부질없는 일입니다. 이 일반적인 선언이 내 구혼자들 저마다에게 제 몫으로 쓰이게 하고, 또 이제부터는 이렇게 이해되게 하십시오. 누군가 나 때문에 죽는다 해도 그것은 질투나 비참함 때문에 죽는 것이 아니니, 아무도 사랑하지 않는 여인은 누구에게도 질투의 빌미를 줄 수 없으며, 솔직함을 멸시와 혼동해서는 안 되기 때문입니다. 나를 들짐승이라 바실리스크라 부르는 자는 나를 해롭고 악한 것으로 여겨 내버려 두라 하고, 나를 배은망덕하다 부르는 자는 그 봉사를 거두라 하고, 나를 변덕스럽다 부르는 자는 나와 사귀려 하지 말라 하고, 나를 잔인하다 부르는 자는 나를 좇지 말라 하십시오. 이 들짐승, 이 바실리스크, 이 배은망덕하고 잔인하며 변덕스러운 존재는 그들을 찾거나 섬기거나 알거나 좇으려는 욕망이 조금도 없기 때문입니다. 크리소스토모의 조급함과 격렬한 열정이 그를 죽였다면, 어찌하여 내 정숙한 처신과 신중함이 비난받아야 합니까? 내가 나무들과 더불어 내 순결을 지키는데, 어찌하여 사람들 가운데서 내가 그것을 지키기를 바라는 자가 그것을 내게서 빼앗으려 드는 것입니까? 그대들도 알다시피 나는 나 자신의 재산이 있어 남의 것을 탐하지 않으며, 내 취향은 자유에 있고 속박에는 정나미가 없습니다. 나는 누구를 사랑하지도 미워하지도 않으며, 이 사람을 속이거나 저 사람에게 추파를 던지지도, 한 사람을 농락하거나 다른 사람을 가지고 놀지도 않습니다. 이 마을 양치기 처녀들과의 정숙한 담소와 내 염소들을 돌보는 일이 내 즐거움이며, 내 바람은 이 산들에 갇혀 있으니, 혹여 그것이 이곳을 벗어나 헤맨다면 그것은 하늘의 아름다움을 우러르기 위함이요, 그것은 영혼이 그 태초의 거처로 나아가는 계단입니다.\"\n\n이 말과 함께, 대답을 들으려고도 하지 않은 채 그녀는 몸을 돌려 바로 곁에 있던 숲의 가장 우거진 곳으로 들어가 버렸으니, 거기 있던 모든 이는 그녀의 아름다움 못지않게 그녀의 분별에도 넋을 잃었다. 더러는 — 그녀의 빛나는 두 눈이 쏘아 보낸 거역할 수 없는 화살에 맞은 자들은 — 자기들이 들은 그 솔직한 선언에도 아랑곳없이 마치 그녀를 따라가려는 듯하였다. 이를 본 돈키호테는, 곤경에 처한 처녀들을 돕고자 자신의 기사도를 발휘하기에 알맞은 기회라 여기고서, 칼자루에 손을 얹은 채 크고 또렷한 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"누구든 그 신분이나 처지가 어떠하든, 아름다운 마르셀라를 감히 따라가지 말라, 나의 사나운 분노를 사고 싶지 않거든. 그녀는 크리소스토모의 죽음에 대해 자기에게 잘못이 거의, 아니 전혀 없음을 명백하고도 흡족한 논리로 보여 주었고, 또한 자신이 그 어떤 연인의 바람에도 응하기를 얼마나 멀리하는지도 보여 주었다. 그러므로 그녀는 쫓기고 핍박받을 것이 아니라, 마땅히 세상의 모든 선한 이에게 존경받고 떠받들어져야 한다. 이토록 정숙한 결심을 지키는 여인은 세상에 오직 그녀뿐임을 그녀가 보여 주었기 때문이다.\"\n\n돈키호테의 위협 때문이었는지, 아니면 암브로시오가 그들에게 좋은 벗에 대한 도리를 다하라고 일렀기 때문인지, 양치기들 가운데 누구도 그 자리에서 움직이거나 꼼짝하지 않았고, 마침내 무덤을 다 파고 크리소스토모의 종이들을 불사른 뒤에 그의 시신을 그 안에 뉘었으니, 곁에 선 이들의 숱한 눈물이 없지 않았다. 그들은 무거운 돌로 무덤을 덮어, 암브로시오가 준비시키겠다고 말한 묘비석이 마련될 때까지 두기로 했는데, 그 비석에 새길 비명(碑銘)은 이런 뜻이었다.\n\n그대 눈앞 이 돌 아래에 한 연인의 몸이 누웠노라.\n\n살아서는 양치기 총각이었고 죽어서는 멸시의 제물이었네. 그를 절망으로 내몬 여인은 배은망덕, 잔인, 쌀쌀, 그리고 아리따웠으니, 사랑이 그녀를 한패로 삼아 제 폭정을 널리 펼쳤도다. 그러고 나서 그들은 무덤 위에 꽃과 나뭇가지를 가득 흩뿌리고, 모두가 그의 벗 암브로시오에게 조의를 표한 뒤 작별을 고했다. 비발도와 그의 일행도 그리하였고, 돈키호테는 자신을 맞아 준 이들과 나그네들에게 작별을 고했는데, 나그네들은 세비야로 함께 가자고 그에게 권했으니, 거기가 모험을 찾기에 더없이 알맞은 곳이라, 모험이란 것이 다른 어디보다도 거리거리마다 길모퉁이마다 더 자주 나타나기 때문이라고 했다. 돈키호테는 그들의 조언과 자기에게 호의를 베풀려는 그 마음에 감사하며, 지금으로서는 이 모든 산에서, 소문에 우글거린다는 노상강도와 도둑들을 죄다 쓸어 없애기 전에는 세비야로 가지 않을 것이며 가서도 안 된다고 말했다. 그의 갸륵한 뜻을 본 나그네들은 그를 더 채근하려 하지 않았고, 다시 한번 작별을 고하고서 그를 떠나 길을 갔는데, 그 길에서 그들은 마르셀라와 크리소스토모의 이야기는 물론 돈키호테의 광기에 대해서도 빠짐없이 이야기를 나누었다. 그는 그대로, 양치기 처녀 마르셀라를 찾아 나서서 자기가 그녀에게 베풀 수 있는 온갖 봉사를 바치기로 결심했다. 그러나 이 진실한 이야기가 전하는 바에 따르면, 일은 그가 기대한 대로 풀리지 않았으니, 제2부는 여기서 끝난다.\n\n# CHAPTER XV.\n\n무정한 양구에스 사람들과 시비가 붙어 돈키호테가 맞닥뜨린 불운한 모험에 관하여\n\n현자 시데 아메테 베넹헬리는 이렇게 전한다. 돈키호테가 자신을 맞아 준 이들과 크리소스토모의 장례에 와 있던 모든 사람과 작별하자마자, 그와 그의 종자는 양치기 처녀 마르셀라가 들어가는 것을 보았던 바로 그 숲으로 들어섰는데, 그녀를 찾으려고 두 시간 넘게 사방을 헤매고도 끝내 찾지 못하자, 부드러운 풀이 덮인 어느 빈터에 다다라 발길을 멈추었으니, 그 곁으로는 시원하고 상쾌한 시냇물이 흘러, 이맘때면 벌써 견디기 힘들 만큼 거세지기 시작하는 한낮의 더위가 가실 동안 그곳에서 쉬어 가도록 그들을 청하고 또 떠밀었던 것이다. 돈키호테와 산초는 말에서 내려, 로시난테와 당나귀를 풀어 그곳에 무성히 자란 풀을 뜯게 하고는, 안장주머니를 뒤져 격식 차릴 것 없이 매우 평화롭고 정답게 주인과 하인이 그 안에서 찾아낸 것으로 끼니를 때웠다.\n\n산초는 로시난테의 다리를 묶어 둘 필요까지는 없다고 여겼으니, 그가 워낙 점잖고 욕정을 다스릴 줄 아는 말임을 알기에, 코르도바 목장의 암말을 죄다 데려다 놓아도 그를 무례한 짓으로 이끌지는 못하리라 굳게 믿었던 것이다. 그러나 우연이, 그리고 결코 늘 잠들어 있지만은 않은 악마가 일을 이렇게 꾸며 놓았으니, 이 골짜기에는 어느 양구에스 마부들이 부리는 갈리시아산 조랑말 떼가 풀을 뜯고 있었는데, 이 마부들은 풀과 물이 넉넉한 곳과 장소에서 자기네 짐승들과 더불어 한낮의 휴식을 취하는 것이 관례였고, 돈키호테가 마침 머문 그곳이 양구에스 사람들의 목적에 딱 들어맞았던 것이다. 그리하여 일이 이렇게 벌어졌으니, 로시난테가 그 조랑말 마님들과 어울려 놀아 보고 싶은 마음이 동하여, 그네들의 냄새를 맡자 평소의 걸음걸이와 몸가짐을 내팽개치고는, 주인의 허락도 구하지 않은 채 제법 활기찬 종종걸음을 일으켜 자기 소망을 그네들에게 알리고자 서둘러 달려갔으나, 그네들은 아무래도 그보다 풀이 더 좋았던 듯, 발굽과 이빨로 그를 어찌나 호되게 맞이했던지 곧 그의 뱃대끈을 끊어 버려 그를 안장도 없는 알몸으로 만들어 놓았다. 그러나 그에게 더 고약했을 일은, 마부들이 그가 자기네 암말들에게 가하는 행패를 보고는 몽둥이로 무장한 채 달려와 그를 어찌나 두들겨 댔던지 그를 흠씬 얻어맞은 꼴로 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨려 놓은 것이었다.\n\n이때 로시난테가 매질당하는 광경을 목격한 돈키호테와 산초가 헐떡이며 달려왔고, 돈키호테가 산초에게 말했다.\n\n\"산초 친구, 내가 보기에 이자들은 기사가 아니라 천한 태생의 비루한 무리일세. 내가 이 말을 꺼내는 까닭은, 우리 눈앞에서 로시난테에게 가해진 모욕에 대해 마땅한 복수를 하는 데 자네가 정당하게 나를 도울 수 있기 때문이라네.\"\n\n\"대체 무슨 빌어먹을 복수를 한단 말입니까.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"저자들은 스무 명이 넘고 우리는 둘밖에, 아니 어쩌면 한 명하고 반밖에 안 되는데 말입니다.\"\n\n\"나는 백 명 몫일세.\" 돈키호테가 대꾸하고는, 더는 말없이 칼을 뽑아 양구에스 사람들에게 달려들었고, 주인의 본보기에 자극받고 떠밀린 산초도 그와 똑같이 했다. 그리하여 우선 돈키호테가 그중 한 사람에게 일격을 가하여, 그가 걸친 가죽 윗옷을 어깨의 상당 부분과 함께 갈라 놓았다. 양구에스 사람들은 그토록 수가 많은데도 고작 두 사람에게 공격당하는 꼴을 보고는 저마다 몽둥이를 집어 들고, 두 사람을 한복판으로 몰아넣은 다음 대단한 열의와 기운으로 두들겨 패기 시작했다. 사실 두 번째 가격에 그들은 산초를 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸고, 돈키호테도 똑같은 꼴을 당했으니, 그의 온갖 무예와 드높은 기개도 아무 소용이 없었다. 게다가 운명은 그가 아직 일어나지도 못한 로시난테의 발치에 쓰러지도록 정해 놓았으니, 이를 보면 성난 촌놈들의 손에 들린 몽둥이가 얼마나 사납게 후려칠 수 있는지를 알 만하다.\n\n그러고 나서 양구에스 사람들은 자기들이 저질러 놓은 행패를 보고는 부랴부랴 짐승들에게 짐을 싣고 길을 재촉하여, 두 모험가를 가련한 몰골에다 더욱 가련한 심정으로 남겨 두고 떠나 버렸다.\n\n먼저 정신을 차린 것은 산초였으니, 자기 주인이 가까이 있는 것을 알고는 힘없고 애처로운 목소리로 그를 불렀다. \"돈키호테 나리, 아, 돈키호테 나리!\"\n\n\"무슨 일인가, 산초 형제?\" 돈키호테가 산초와 똑같이 기운 없고 괴로워하는 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"할 수만 있다면 말입니다.\" 산초 판사가 대답했다. \"나리께서 그 불의 블라스인지 하는 물약을 두어 모금 제게 주셨으면 합니다요, 혹시 거기 가진 게 있으시다면 말입니다. 어쩌면 상처에만이 아니라 부러진 뼈에도 잘 들을지 모르지요.\"\n\n\"이 가련한 신세에 그것이 여기 있기만 하다면야 더 바랄 게 무어 있겠나?\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 산초 판사, 편력 기사의 신의를 걸고 자네에게 맹세하노니, 운명이 달리 정하지 않는 한 이틀이 지나기 전에 나는 그것을 내 손에 넣고야 말 것이며, 그러지 못한다면 내 손이 그 솜씨를 잃은 것이리라.\"\n\n\"그런데 나리, 우리 발을 다시 쓸 수 있게 되기까지는 며칠이나 걸릴 것 같습니까?\" 산초 판사가 대답했다.\n\n\"나로 말하자면 며칠일지 도무지 짐작이 안 가는구나.\" 흠씬 두들겨 맞은 기사 돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 나는 모든 잘못을 내 탓으로 돌리네. 나처럼 기사 서임을 받지 못한 자들에게 칼을 들이대서는 안 되는 일이었으니 말일세. 그러니 내가 기사도의 법을 어긴 벌로, 싸움의 신께서 이 징벌이 내게 내려지도록 허락하신 것이라 믿네. 그러한 까닭에, 산초 형제여, 이제 내가 자네에게 일러 둘 일에 대해 한 가지 귀띔을 받아 두는 것이 좋겠으니, 이는 우리 둘 모두의 안녕에 매우 중요한 일이라네. 그것은 곧, 이런 부류의 천한 무리가 우리에게 모욕을 가하는 것을 자네가 보거든, 내가 그들에게 칼을 뽑을 때까지 기다리지 말라는 것일세. 나는 결코 그러지 않을 것이기 때문이지. 자네가 칼을 뽑아 마음껏 그들을 응징하게나. 그러다 만약 어떤 기사들이 그들을 돕고 지키러 나선다면, 그때는 내가 온 힘을 다해 자네를 지키고 그들을 공격해 줄 것이니, 이 강한 내 팔의 힘이 어디까지 미치는지는 자네도 이미 수천 가지 표적과 증거로 보았을 걸세.\" 그 억센 비스카야인을 이긴 승리로 이 가련한 양반은 그토록 우쭐해져 있었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 산초는 주인의 그 훈계를 아무 대꾸 없이 그냥 넘길 만큼 흔쾌히 받아들이지는 않았으니, 이렇게 답하여 말했다. \"나리, 저는 평화로운 사람이라 온순하고 조용하며, 어떤 모욕도 참아 넘길 수 있는데, 이는 제게 부양하고 길러야 할 아내와 자식들이 있기 때문입니다요. 그러니 명령은 될 수 없을 테고 그저 귀띔으로나마 나리께 일러 두겠습니다만, 저는 어떤 일이 있어도 촌놈에게든 기사에게든 칼을 뽑는 일은 결코 없을 것이며, 여기 하느님 앞에서, 신분이 높든 낮든, 부자든 가난뱅이든, 귀족이든 평민이든, 그 어떤 지위나 처지의 사람도 가리지 않고, 제게 가해졌거나, 가해지고 있거나, 앞으로 가해질 모든 모욕을 용서하겠습니다요.\"\n\n이 모든 말에 그의 주인이 답하여 말했다. \"내게 좀 수월하게 말할 만큼 숨이 붙어 있고, 이 옆구리에 느껴지는 통증이 가라앉아 자네의 그 잘못을 일러 줄 수 있으면 좋으련만. 자, 이 죄 많은 친구야, 여태껏 그토록 우리에게 등을 돌렸던 운명의 바람이 우리 편으로 돌아서서, 우리 소망의 돛을 가득 채워 아무 탈도 거침도 없이 내가 자네에게 약속한 그 섬들 가운데 하나로 무사히 입항한다고 치세. 만약 그것을 손에 넣어 내가 자네를 그곳의 영주로 삼는다면 자네 처지가 어찌 되겠나? 아니, 자네는 기사도 아니고 기사가 되려는 마음도 없으며, 모욕에 복수하거나 자기 영지를 지킬 용기도 의지도 없으니, 그 일을 거의 불가능하게 만들고 말 걸세. 자네도 알아 두어야 할 것이, 새로 정복한 왕국이나 속주에서는 주민들의 마음이 새 영주에게 결코 그리 평온하지도, 그리 우호적으로 기울지도 않아, 다시 한번 형세를 뒤엎으려 어떤 움직임을 꾀하고는, 사람들 말마따나 운수가 자기들에게 무엇을 가져다줄지 시험해 보려 할 우려가 늘 있다네. 그러니 새 영주는 다스릴 수 있는 분별력과, 무슨 일이 닥치든 공격하고 자신을 지킬 수 있는 용맹을 갖추는 것이 필수란 말일세.\"\n\n\"방금 우리에게 닥친 일에서야말로,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"나리께서 말씀하시는 그 분별력과 그 용맹을 제가 지녔더라면 참 기뻤을 텐데요, 허나 가난뱅이의 신의를 걸고 맹세하건대 저는 논쟁보다는 고약(膏藥)이 더 어울리는 사람입니다요. 나리께서 일어나실 수 있는지 보시고, 우리 로시난테를 좀 도와줍시다. 그럴 자격도 없는 놈이긴 합니다만, 이 매타작의 주된 원인이 그놈이었으니 말입니다. 저는 로시난테가 그럴 줄은 정말 몰랐습니다요. 저는 그놈을 점잖은 인물에다 저처럼 조용한 녀석이라 여겼거든요. 결국 사람을 알기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸리고, 이 세상에 확실한 건 아무것도 없다는 말이 옳습니다요. 나리께서 그 운수 사나운 편력 기사에게 그토록 어마어마한 칼질을 하신 다음에, 그 뒤를 바짝 쫓아 우편마차처럼 빨리, 우리 어깨 위로 이런 몽둥이 폭풍이 쏟아져 내릴 줄을 누가 알았겠습니까요?\"\n\n\"그래도 자네 어깨는, 산초,\" 돈키호테가 대꾸했다. \"그런 돌풍에는 이골이 났을 게 분명하네. 허나 부드러운 천과 고운 아마포 속에서 자라난 내 어깨는, 이 봉변의 고통을 더 사무치게 느낄 수밖에 없을 게 뻔하지. 만약 내가 이 모든 성가신 일이 무예라는 소명에 반드시 따라붙는 것임을 상상하지 않는다면 — 아니, 상상이라니 무슨 말을, 확실히 안다고 해야지 — 나는 순전히 분한 마음에 여기 드러누워 죽어 버렸을 걸세.\"\n\n이에 종자가 답했다. \"나리, 이런 봉변이 기사도에서 거두는 수확이라면, 그것이 아주 자주 일어나는지, 아니면 일어나는 데 저 나름의 정해진 철이라도 있는지 일러 주십시오. 제가 보기엔 두 차례 수확이 끝나고 나면, 하느님께서 그 무한한 자비로 우리를 도와주시지 않는 한, 세 번째 수확에는 우리가 아무짝에도 쓸모없게 될 것 같으니 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"알아 두게, 산초 친구,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"편력 기사의 삶이란 수천 가지 위험과 좌절을 겪게 마련이지만, 그에 못지않게 편력 기사가 왕이나 황제가 되는 일도 당장 일어날 수 있는 일이라네. 이는 내가 그 행적을 속속들이 꿰고 있는 여러 다른 기사들의 경우에서 경험으로 드러난 바이지. 통증만 허락한다면 지금이라도 자네에게 들려줄 수 있으련만, 오로지 팔의 힘만으로 내가 말한 그 높은 지위에 오른 이들의 이야기를 말일세. 그러나 그들 또한 그 전이나 그 후에 갖가지 불운과 고초를 겪었다네. 용맹한 갈리아의 아마디스는 자신의 철천지원수인 마법사 아르칼라우스의 손아귀에 떨어졌는데, 단언컨대 아르칼라우스는 그를 포로로 잡아 궁정의 기둥 하나에 묶어 놓고는 말고삐로 이백 대가 넘게 후려쳤다고 하네. 더욱이 적잖은 권위를 지닌 어느 박학한 작가가 말하기를, 포에보스의 기사는 어느 성에서 발밑이 꺼지며 벌어진 어떤 함정에 빠져, 떨어지고 보니 땅속 깊은 구덩이에서 손발이 묶인 채였는데, 그곳에서 그들이 그에게 모래와 눈 녹인 물로 만든, 이른바 관장(灌腸)이라는 것을 베풀어 그를 거의 끝장낼 뻔했다는 것일세. 만약 그 위급한 지경에서 그의 절친한 벗인 한 현자가 그를 구해 주지 않았더라면, 그 가련한 기사는 매우 험한 꼴을 당했을 걸세. 그러니 나도 그토록 훌륭한 분들과 더불어 고초를 겪는 것쯤이야 능히 감내할 수 있지, 그분들이 견뎌야 했던 모욕은 우리가 견디는 것보다 더 큰 것이었으니 말이네. 산초, 자네가 알아 두어야 할 것이, 우연히 손에 잡힌 어떤 도구로 입은 상처는 결코 모욕이 되지 않는데, 이는 결투의 법에 명백한 말로 규정되어 있다네. 이를테면 구두장이가 손에 든 골틀로 남을 친다면, 그것이 사실은 한낱 나무토막일지언정, 그것으로 얻어맞은 자가 몽둥이질을 당했다고는 말할 수 없는 것이지. 내가 이 말을 하는 까닭은, 우리가 이번 싸움에서 두들겨 맞았다고 해서 무슨 모욕을 당한 것이라고 자네가 여길까 봐서일세. 우리를 두들긴 그자들이 들고 있던 무기란 그저 몽둥이에 지나지 않았으며, 내 기억으로는 그들 중 누구도 양날칼이나 검이나 단검을 지니지 않았으니 말이네.\"\n\n\"제겐 그런 것까지 살펴볼 틈도 주지 않았습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제가 미처 제 티소나에 손을 대기도 전에 그자들이 몽둥이로 제 어깨 위에 어찌나 멋들어지게 십자가를 그어 댔는지, 제 눈에선 시야를 앗아 가고 제 다리에선 힘을 앗아 가, 지금 제가 누워 있는 이 자리에 뻗어 버리게 만들었거든요. 그래서 그 몽둥이질이 다 모욕이었는지 아니었는지를 생각하는 일은 제게 아무 걱정거리도 안 됩니다요만, 매 맞은 고통은 그렇지가 않으니, 그것이 제 어깨에 깊이 새겨진 만큼이나 제 기억에도 깊이 새겨져 남을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그래도 한마디 일러 두겠네, 판사 형제,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"세월이 끝장내지 못하는 기억이란 없고, 죽음이 거두어 가지 못하는 고통이란 없는 법일세.\"\n\n\"한데 세월이 끝장내 주기를, 죽음이 거두어 가 주기를 기다려야 하는 것보다 더 큰 불행이 어디 있겠습니까요?\" 판사가 대꾸했다. \"우리 봉변이 고약 두어 장이면 낫는 그런 것이라면 그리 나쁘진 않을 텐데요, 병원에 있는 고약을 죄다 갖다 붙여도 우리를 멀쩡하게 만들기엔 거의 모자랄 것 같다는 생각이 들기 시작하는걸요.\"\n\n\"그만하게. 산초, 약함 속에서 힘을 끌어내게나, 나도 그러려 하니.\" 돈키호테가 응수했다. \"그리고 로시난테가 어떤지 좀 보세. 이 봉변의 몫 가운데 적지 않은 것이 저 가련한 짐승에게 떨어진 듯하니 말일세.\"\n\n\"그야 이상할 게 없지요.\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"그놈도 편력 기사이니 말입니다요. 제가 이상하게 여기는 건, 우리는 이렇게 흠씬 두들겨 맞았는데 제 당나귀 녀석은 멀쩡하게 빠져나왔다는 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"운명은 역경 속에서도 그것을 덜어 줄 길을 가져다주려고 늘 문 하나를 열어 두는 법일세.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 이렇게 말하는 까닭은, 이 작은 짐승이 이제 로시난테의 빈자리를 메워, 내 상처를 치료받을 수 있는 어느 성으로 나를 데려다줄 수 있기 때문이지. 더욱이 나는 그렇게 올라타는 것을 조금도 불명예로 여기지 않을 것이니, 옛 선량한 실레노스가 — 그 흥겨운 웃음의 신을 가르친 스승이자 인도자 말일세 — 백 개의 성문이 있는 도시에 들어갈 때, 멋진 당나귀에 올라타고 매우 흡족하게 갔다는 글을 읽은 기억이 나기 때문이라네.\"\n\n\"나리 말씀대로 그분이 올라타고 갔다는 건 사실일지 몰라도,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"올라타고 가는 것과 거름 자루처럼 둘러메여 가는 것 사이에는 큰 차이가 있습죠.\"\n\n이에 돈키호테가 답했다. \"싸움에서 입은 상처는 명예를 앗아 가기는커녕 오히려 명예를 안겨 주는 법일세. 그러니 판사 친구, 더는 말 말고, 앞서 자네에게 말했듯 할 수 있는 만큼 일어나 자네 마음에 가장 드는 대로 나를 자네 짐승 위에 얹게나. 그리고 밤이 닥쳐 이 황야에서 우리를 덮치기 전에 어서 떠나세.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 나리께서 말씀하시는 걸 들은 적이 있는데요,\" 판사가 한마디 했다. \"편력 기사가 황무지나 사막에서 잠자는 것은 매우 마땅한 일이며, 그것을 큰 행운으로 여긴다고 하시지 않았습니까요.\"\n\n\"그건,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"어쩔 도리가 없을 때나, 사랑에 빠져 있을 때 그렇다는 말일세. 이는 어찌나 사실인지, 어떤 기사들은 자기 여인이 그것을 까맣게 모르는 채로, 볕과 그늘과 하늘의 온갖 모진 날씨를 견디며 바위 위에서 두 해를 지낸 적도 있다네. 그중 한 사람이 바로 아마디스인데, 그는 벨테네브로스라는 이름으로 페냐 포브레에 거처를 잡고 — 그것이 여덟 해였는지 여덟 달이었는지는 모르겠으나, 셈에 그리 자신이 없어서 말일세 — 어쨌든 거기 머물며 고행을 했는데, 오리아나 공주가 그에게 품은 무슨 노여움 때문이었는지는 나도 잘 모르겠네. 허나 이제 그 이야기는 그만하세, 산초, 그리고 로시난테 같은 봉변이 당나귀에게 닥치기 전에 어서 서두르게.\"\n\n\"그렇게 된다면야 정말 악마가 끼어든 셈이겠지요.\" 산초가 말했다. 그러고는 \"아이고\" 소리를 서른 번, 한숨을 예순 번, 그리고 자기를 이런 데로 데려온 자가 누구든 간에 그에게 퍼붓는 저주와 악담을 백스무 번이나 쏟아 낸 뒤, 몸을 일으켰으나 중간에서 터키 활처럼 굽은 채 멈추어 몸을 곧추세울 힘이 없었지만, 온갖 고통을 무릅쓰고 당나귀에 안장을 얹었으니, 그 녀석 또한 그날의 지나친 방종에 못 이겨 다소 길을 벗어났던 참이었다. 그다음 그는 로시난테를 일으켜 세웠는데, 그놈으로 말하자면 하소연할 혀라도 있었더라면 분명 산초도 그 주인도 그에 뒤지지 않았을 것이다.\n\n간단히 말하자면, 산초는 돈키호테를 당나귀 위에 앉히고 로시난테는 끌채에 매어 둔 다음, 당나귀의 고삐를 잡고서 큰길이 있을 법한 방향이라 여겨지는 쪽으로 얼추 나아갔다. 그런데 우연이 그들의 일을 나쁜 데서 좋은 데로 이끌어 가던 터라, 한참도 못 가 길이 눈에 들어왔고, 그 길 위로 어느 객줏집이 보였는데, 산초에게는 분하게도, 돈키호테에게는 기쁘게도, 그것은 영락없이 성이어야만 했다. 산초는 그것이 객줏집이라 우겼고, 그의 주인은 객줏집이 아니라 성이라 우겨, 그 다툼이 어찌나 오래 끌었던지 결판이 나기도 전에 그들은 거기 다다를 만큼의 시간이 흘렀고, 산초는 더는 옥신각신할 것 없이 자기 짐승들을 죄다 거느리고 그 안으로 들어갔다.\n* 티소나: 엘 시드의 명검 이름으로, 여기서는 산초가 익살스럽게 자기 칼을 일컫는 말이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI.\n\n돈키호테가 당나귀에 가로질러 실려 오는 것을 본 객줏집 주인은 산초에게 그에게 무슨 일이 있었느냐고 물었다. 산초는 별일 아니며, 다만 바위에서 떨어져 갈비뼈를 좀 다쳤을 뿐이라고 대답했다. 객줏집 주인에게는 아내가 하나 있었는데, 같은 일을 하는 여인네들이 흔히 그러한 성정과는 사뭇 다른 사람이었다. 천성이 마음씨가 곱고 이웃의 고통을 함께 아파할 줄 알았기에, 그녀는 곧장 돈키호테를 돌보기 시작했으며, 무척 어여쁜 처녀인 어린 딸에게 손님을 보살피는 일을 거들게 했다. 그 밖에도 객줏집에는 하녀로 일하는 아스투리아스 처녀 하나가 있었는데, 얼굴이 넓적하고 뒤통수가 납작하며 코가 들창코였고, 한쪽 눈은 멀었으며 다른 쪽 눈도 그리 성치 않았다. 그래도 그 몸매의 우아함이 모든 결점을 메워 주었으니, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 일곱 뼘이 채 되지 않았고, 그녀를 다소 짓누르는 어깨 탓에 그녀는 본의 아니게 자주 땅바닥을 들여다보게 되었다. 어쨌든 이 맵시 있는 처녀가 어린 딸을 도왔고, 둘은 함께 돈키호테에게 어느 다락방에 몹시 형편없는 잠자리를 마련해 주었다. 그 다락방은 오랜 세월 짚 곳간으로 쓰였던 흔적이 역력했으며, 거기에는 마부 한 사람도 묵고 있었는데, 그의 잠자리는 우리의 돈키호테 것보다 조금 떨어진 곳에 놓여 있었고, 비록 노새들의 길마와 천 조각들로만 꾸민 것이었으나 돈키호테의 것보다는 훨씬 나았다. 돈키호테의 잠자리란 그저 그리 평평하지도 않은 두 받침대 위에 거친 널빤지 네 장을 얹은 것에, 얇기로 말하자면 누비이불이라 해도 될 만한 매트리스 하나를 깐 것이었으니, 그 매트리스는 솜뭉치로 가득 찼으되 그것이 찢어진 틈으로 양털임을 보지 못했더라면 손끝에는 자갈처럼 단단하게 느껴졌을 정도였고, 방패 가죽으로 만든 듯한 홑이불 두 장과, 그 올을 세려는 사람이라면 누구든 하나도 빠뜨리지 않고 셀 수 있을 만한 덮개 한 장이 전부였다.\n\n이 저주받은 잠자리 위에 돈키호테가 몸을 뉘었고, 안주인과 그 딸은 곧 머리끝에서 발끝까지 그에게 고약을 발라 주었으며, 그동안 마리토르네스는 — 이것이 그 아스투리아스 처녀의 이름이었다 — 그들을 위해 등불을 들어 주었다. 그리고 고약을 바르던 안주인은 돈키호테의 몇몇 군데가 멍울투성이인 것을 보고는, 이것이 떨어진 것이라기보다는 매를 맞은 자국에 가깝다고 한마디 했다.\n\n매를 맞은 것이 아니라, 바위에 모서리와 돌출부가 많아 그 하나하나가 자국을 남긴 것이라고 산초는 말했다. \"부인,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"마(麻)를 좀 남겨 두시지요. 쓸 사람이 없지 않을 테니까요. 제 허리도 꽤 쑤신답니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 댁도 떨어진 모양이군요.\" 안주인이 말했다.\n\n\"전 떨어지지 않았습니다.\" 산초 판사가 말했다. \"다만 제 주인 나리가 떨어지는 걸 본 충격에 온몸이 쑤셔서, 마치 매를 천 대쯤 맞은 것 같은 기분입니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 만도 하지요.\" 어린 처녀가 말했다. \"저도 탑에서 떨어지면서 땅에는 도무지 닿지 않는 꿈을 여러 번 꾸었는데, 그 꿈에서 깨어나 보면 정말로 떨어지기라도 한 양 몸이 나른하고 흔들렸거든요.\"\n\n\"바로 그 점입니다, 부인.\" 산초 판사가 대꾸했다. \"저는 꿈 따위는 전혀 꾸지 않고, 지금보다도 더 멀쩡히 깨어 있었는데도, 제 주인 돈키호테 나리에 못지않게 멍울투성이가 되었으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그 양반은 어떻게 불리시오?\" 아스투리아스 처녀 마리토르네스가 물었다.\n\n\"라만차의 돈키호테시지요.\" 산초 판사가 대답했다. \"방랑 기사이신데, 오랜 세월 이 세상에서 볼 수 있었던 이들 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 굳센 분들 중 하나시랍니다.\"\n\n\"방랑 기사가 뭔데요?\" 처녀가 말했다.\n\n\"세상 물정에 그리도 어두워 그걸 모르시나?\" 산초 판사가 대답했다. \"그럼, 들어 보시게, 아가씨. 방랑 기사란 한마디로 말하자면, 두들겨 맞은 신세였다가 황제가 되기도 하는 그런 존재라네. 오늘은 세상에서 가장 비참하고 가장 궁핍한 자였다가, 내일은 자기 종자에게 나누어 줄 왕국의 왕관을 두세 개씩 가지게 되는 그런 분이지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 어찌하여,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"이렇게 훌륭한 주인을 모시는 분이, 겉모습으로 보아하니 백작령 하나조차 가지지 못한 거요?\"\n\n\"아직 너무 이르지요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"모험을 찾아 길을 나선 지 겨우 한 달밖에 안 됐으니까요. 게다가 지금껏 모험이라 할 만한 건 하나도 만나지 못했답니다. 한 가지를 찾다 보면 다른 게 걸리기 마련이니 말이지요. 어쨌든 제 주인 돈키호테 나리께서 이 상처인지 낙상인지에서 쾌차하시고, 제가 그로 인해 더 나빠지지만 않는다면, 저는 제 희망을 스페인에서 가장 좋은 작위와도 바꾸지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n이 모든 대화를 돈키호테는 무척 주의 깊게 듣고 있었으며, 할 수 있는 한 잠자리에서 몸을 일으켜 앉아 안주인의 손을 잡고는 이렇게 말했다. \"고운 부인, 내 말을 믿으시오. 당신은 이 성에서 이 몸을 거두어 주었으니 스스로를 행운아라 여기셔도 좋소. 내 입으로 이 몸을 칭송하지 않는 것은, 흔히들 말하듯 제 자랑은 스스로를 깎아내린다고 하기 때문이오. 허나 내가 누구인지는 내 종자가 일러 줄 것이오. 다만 내 이르노니, 당신이 내게 베푼 그 수고를 내 기억에 영원히 새겨 두어, 목숨이 다하는 날까지 그 은혜에 보답하리다. 만일 사랑이 나를 그 법도에, 그리고 내가 이 사이로 가만히 이름을 읊조리는 그 아리따운 무정한 여인의 눈동자에 이토록 옭아매어 종으로 삼지만 않았던들, 이 사랑스러운 아가씨의 눈동자야말로 내 자유의 주인이 되었으련만, 하늘이여 굽어살피소서.\"\n\n안주인과 그 딸, 그리고 갸륵한 마리토르네스는 이 방랑 기사의 말을 어리둥절하게 들었다. 그가 그리스어로라도 떠드는 양 그 말을 거의 알아듣지 못했으나, 그것이 모두 호의와 아첨의 표현임은 짐작할 수 있었기 때문이다. 이런 종류의 말투에 익숙하지 않았던 까닭에, 그녀들은 그를 빤히 바라보며 속으로 의아해했으니, 그가 자기들이 익히 보아 온 자들과는 사뭇 다른 부류의 사람으로 여겨졌던 것이다. 그리고 그의 정중함에 술집에서 쓰는 투의 말로 고마움을 표하고는 그를 남겨 둔 채 자리를 떴고, 한편 아스투리아스 처녀는 주인 못지않게 보살핌이 필요했던 산초를 돌보았다.\n\n마부는 그날 밤 그녀와 한바탕 즐기기로 약조를 해 두었으며, 그녀는 손님들이 잠잠해지고 집안 식구들이 잠들면 그를 찾아와 그의 바람을 거리낌 없이 들어주겠노라 언약했다. 그리고 이 갸륵한 처녀로 말하자면, 비록 숲속에서 증인 하나 없이 한 약조라 할지라도 한번 그런 약속을 하면 결코 어기는 법이 없었다고 전해진다. 자신이 귀부인이라는 점을 크게 자랑으로 여겼으며, 객줏집 하녀 노릇 같은 일을 부끄럽게 여기지 않았으니, 그녀의 말에 따르면 불운과 박복함이 자기를 그 처지로 몰아넣었을 뿐이라는 것이었다. 돈키호테의 딱딱하고 비좁고 비참하며 삐걱대는 잠자리가 이 별빛 비치는 마구간 한복판에 첫 번째로 놓여 있었고, 그 바로 곁에 산초가 자기 잠자리를 마련했으니, 그것이라고는 부들자리 하나와, 양털이라기보다는 올이 다 닳은 천에 가까워 보이는 담요 한 장이 전부였다. 이 두 잠자리 다음에 마부의 것이 있었는데, 앞서 말했듯이 길마와 그가 가진 가장 좋은 노새 두 마리의 마구를 죄다 모아 꾸민 것이었다. 노새는 모두 열두 마리였으며, 모두 윤기가 흐르고 토실토실하며 더없이 좋은 상태였으니, 이 이야기의 저자에 따르면 그가 아레발로의 부유한 마부들 가운데 하나였기 때문이다. 저자가 이 마부를 특별히 언급하는 것은 그를 매우 잘 알았기 때문이며, 심지어 어느 정도 그의 친척이었다는 말까지 있다. 게다가 시데 아메테 베넹헬리는 모든 일에 대해 깊이 파고들고 정확하기로 이름난 사학자였으니, 이는 앞서 언급된 일들이 아무리 사소하고 하찮은 것이라 해도 그가 그것들을 잠자코 지나치려 하지 않았다는 점에서 매우 분명하다. 이는 일을 너무도 짤막하고 간략하게 늘어놓아 우리가 그 맛을 거의 보지도 못하게 하고, 작품의 알맹이는 죄다 잉크병 속에 남겨 두는 — 게으름 탓이든 심술 탓이든 무지 탓이든 — 저 근엄한 사학자들이 본받을 만한 본보기이다. 『리카몬테의 타블란테』의 저자와, 톰미야스 백작의 행적을 늘어놓은 또 다른 책의 저자에게 천 번의 축복이 있기를. 그들은 얼마나 세세하게 모든 것을 묘사하는가!\n\n그럼 이야기를 계속하자면, 마부는 자기 노새들을 둘러보고 두 번째 먹이를 준 뒤, 길마 위에 몸을 뻗고 누워 자신의 신실한 마리토르네스를 기다렸다. 그즈음 산초는 고약을 다 바르고 누웠으나, 아무리 자려 애써도 갈비뼈의 통증이 그를 잠들지 못하게 했고, 돈키호테 역시 자기 갈비뼈의 통증으로 산토끼처럼 두 눈을 활짝 뜬 채였다.\n\n객줏집은 온통 고요에 잠겼고, 그 전체에서 빛이라고는 대문 한복판에 걸려 타오르는 등 하나뿐이었다. 이 야릇한 정적과, 우리 기사의 마음에 늘 떠나지 않던, 그의 불행의 원인이 된 그 책들 속 굽이굽이마다 그려진 사건들에 대한 생각이, 그의 상상에 능히 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 기상천외한 망상을 불러일으켰다. 그것인즉 자기가 어느 이름난 성에 다다랐다고 (앞서 말했듯이, 그가 묵는 객줏집은 죄다 그의 눈에는 성으로 보였다) 여기고, 객줏집 주인의 딸이 그 성주의 딸이며, 그녀가 자신의 고귀한 거동에 반해 사랑에 빠졌고, 부모 몰래 그날 밤 잠시 자기 잠자리로 찾아오겠노라 약조했다고 상상한 것이었다. 그리고 스스로 꾸며 낸 이 온갖 공상을 확고한 사실로 받아들이고는, 자신의 정절이 곧 맞닥뜨릴 위태로운 위기를 헤아리며 불안해지기 시작했으니, 설령 귀네비어 왕비와 킨타뇨나 시녀가 직접 그 앞에 나타난다 해도 자신의 여인 엘 토보소의 둘시네아에게 결코 배신을 저지르지 않으리라 마음속으로 굳게 다짐했다.\n\n이렇듯 그가 이 망상들에 골몰해 있는 동안, 그에게는 불운하기 짝이 없는 그 시각, 그 순간이 다가와 아스투리아스 처녀가 들어왔다. 그녀는 속적삼 차림에 맨발이었고, 머리는 면 두건 속에 그러모은 채, 소리 없이 조심스러운 걸음으로 그 셋이 묵고 있는 방에 마부를 찾아 들어섰다. 그러나 그녀가 문턱에 이르기가 무섭게 돈키호테가 그녀를 알아채고는, 고약과 갈비뼈의 통증에도 아랑곳없이 잠자리에서 몸을 일으켜, 자신의 어여쁜 아가씨를 맞으려 두 팔을 뻗었다. 몸을 잔뜩 웅크리고 소리 없이 두 손을 앞으로 내밀어 정인을 더듬던 아스투리아스 처녀는 돈키호테의 두 팔과 마주쳤고, 그는 그녀의 손목을 단단히 움켜쥐고 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨, 그녀가 감히 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 못하는 사이에 잠자리에 앉혔다. 그러고는 그녀의 속적삼을 만져 보았는데, 그것이 비록 거친 삼베였으나 그에게는 더없이 곱고 부드러운 비단처럼 느껴졌다. 그녀의 손목에는 유리구슬 몇 알이 걸려 있었으나, 그에게는 그것이 진귀한 동방 진주의 광채로 보였다. 어느 정도는 말갈기를 닮은 그녀의 머리카락을, 그는 해님조차 빛바래게 하는 아라비아의 가장 찬란한 황금 실로 쳐 주었다. 틀림없이 어제 먹다 남긴 시어진 샐러드 냄새를 풍겼을 그녀의 숨결은, 그에게는 그녀의 입에서 달콤하고 향긋한 향기를 퍼뜨리는 듯 여겨졌다. 요컨대 그는 사랑에 사로잡혀 여기 적힌 온갖 치장을 하고 중상을 입은 기사를 보러 찾아왔다는, 그가 책에서 보았던 다른 공주들의 모습 그대로의 이목구비와 그대로의 양식으로, 그녀의 초상을 자신의 상상 속에 그려 냈다. 이 가엾은 양반의 눈먼 정도가 어찌나 심했던지, 마부가 아닌 누구라도 토하게 만들었을 그 갸륵한 처녀의 감촉도, 냄새도, 그 밖의 무엇도 그의 미망을 깨우기에는 부족했다. 도리어 그는 자신이 미의 여신을 품에 안고 있다고 굳게 믿어, 그녀를 단단히 움켜쥔 채 낮고 부드러운 목소리로 이렇게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"아름답고 고귀한 부인, 당신이 그 빼어난 미모를 보여 줌으로써 내게 베푼 이 은혜에 보답할 수 있는 처지라면 얼마나 좋겠소. 허나 선한 이를 핍박하기에 결코 지칠 줄 모르는 운명이 나를 이 잠자리에 눕히기로 정하여, 내 이리도 멍들고 부서진 채 누워 있으니, 비록 내 마음이야 기꺼이 당신의 뜻을 따르고 싶으나 도무지 불가능하구려. 게다가 이 불가능에 더하여 또 한 가지 더 큰 불가능이 있으니, 그것은 곧 내 가장 은밀한 생각의 유일한 여인인, 견줄 데 없는 엘 토보소의 둘시네아에게 내가 바친 맹세요. 만일 이것이 가로막지만 않았던들, 나는 당신의 그 크나큰 후의가 내게 베푼 이 행복한 기회를 놓칠 만큼 무딘 기사는 아니었을 것이오.\"\n\n마리토르네스는 돈키호테에게 그토록 단단히 붙들린 처지에 애가 타고 땀을 흘리며, 그가 자기에게 건네는 말은 알아듣지도 귀담아듣지도 못한 채, 아무 말 없이 그저 벗어나려 발버둥쳤다. 한편 부정한 생각으로 잠 못 이루던 갸륵한 마부는, 자기 정부가 문에 들어선 순간 이미 그녀를 알아채고는 돈키호테가 하는 말을 죄다 주의 깊게 듣고 있었으며, 아스투리아스 처녀가 자기와의 약조를 깨고 딴 사내에게 갔다는 데에 질투가 일어, 돈키호테의 잠자리 가까이 다가가 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 이 수작이 어찌 되어 가는지 가만히 지켜보고 섰다. 그러나 그 계집이 벗어나려고 발버둥치고 돈키호테가 붙들려고 안간힘을 쓰는 꼴을 보자, 그 장난이 영 마뜩잖아 팔을 치켜들어 그 사랑에 빠진 기사의 야윈 턱주가리에 어찌나 무시무시한 주먹을 한 방 먹였던지, 그의 입안이 온통 피로 흥건해졌다. 그러고도 성에 차지 않아 그의 갈비뼈 위에 올라타 종종걸음보다 좀 빠른 박자로 두 발로 온통 짓밟아 댔다. 다소 삐걱대고 발이 그리 든든하지 못하던 잠자리는, 마부의 무게까지 더해진 것을 견디지 못하고 그만 바닥으로 폭삭 주저앉았으며, 그 우렁찬 굉음에 객줏집 주인이 잠에서 깨어 그것이 필시 마리토르네스가 벌인 무슨 소동이려니 단박에 결론지었으니, 큰 소리로 그녀를 불러도 대답이 없었기 때문이다. 이런 의심을 품고 그는 일어나 등불을 켜고는 소란이 들려온 쪽으로 서둘러 갔다. 자기 주인이 오는 것을 보고 그 성미가 무시무시함을 알던 계집은, 겁에 질려 혼비백산하여 여태 잠들어 있던 산초 판사의 잠자리로 달려가 그 위에 웅크리고는 공처럼 몸을 말았다.\n\n객줏집 주인이 소리치며 들어왔다. \"어디 있느냐, 이 화냥년아? 이건 필시 네년 짓이렷다.\" 이때 산초가 잠에서 깼고, 거의 자기 몸 위에 얹힌 그 덩어리를 느끼고는 가위에 눌린 줄로만 알아 사방으로 주먹을 휘두르기 시작했으니, 그중 몇 대가 마리토르네스에게 떨어졌다. 통증에 약이 오른 그녀는 부끄러움도 내던지고 산초에게 어찌나 많은 주먹을 되돌려 주었던지, 본의 아니게 그를 완전히 깨워 놓고 말았다. 그러자 산초는 누구인지도 모를 자에게 자기가 이리도 험하게 다루어지는 것을 알고, 할 수 있는 한 몸을 일으켜 마리토르네스와 맞붙었으니, 그와 그녀 둘이 어우러져 세상에서 가장 처절하고도 가장 우스꽝스러운 난투극을 벌이기 시작했다. 그러나 마부는 객줏집 주인의 촛불 빛으로 자기 연인이 어찌 되어 가는지를 알아채고는 돈키호테를 내버려 둔 채 그녀에게 필요한 도움을 주려 달려갔고, 객줏집 주인 역시 달려갔으나 그 속셈은 달랐으니, 그는 이 모든 야단법석의 원흉이 의심할 바 없이 그 계집 하나뿐이라고 믿어 그녀를 혼내 주려는 것이었다. 그리하여 속담에 이르듯 고양이는 쥐에게, 쥐는 밧줄에게, 밧줄은 막대기에게 달려들듯, 마부는 산초를 두들기고, 산초는 계집을, 계집은 그를, 객줏집 주인은 그녀를 두들겼으니, 모두가 어찌나 부지런히 손발을 놀렸던지 잠시도 쉴 틈을 주지 않았다. 그런데 무엇보다 기막힌 것은 객줏집 주인의 등불이 꺼져 버린 일이라, 다들 어둠 속에 남겨진 채 한 덩어리로 뒤엉켜 어찌나 사정없이 서로를 두들겨 댔던지 손이 닿는 곳에 성한 자리라고는 한 군데도 남지 않았다.\n\n마침 그날 밤 객줏집에는 톨레도의 이른바 옛 성스러운 형제단의 순찰관 하나가 묵고 있었는데, 그 또한 이 격투의 심상찮은 소음을 듣고는 자기 지팡이와 영장이 든 양철 통을 집어 들고 어둠을 헤치며 방 안으로 들어와 외쳤다. \"멈춰라! 사법권의 이름으로! 멈춰라! 성스러운 형제단의 이름으로!\"\n\n그가 맨 먼저 마주친 것은 두들겨 맞은 돈키호테였으니, 그는 부서진 잠자리 위에 정신을 잃은 채 벌렁 자빠져 있었다. 순찰관은 더듬거리다 그 수염에 손이 닿자 계속해서 외쳤다. \"사법권을 도우라!\" 그러나 자기가 붙든 자가 꿈쩍도 하지 않는 것을 보고는, 그가 죽었으며 방 안에 있는 자들이 그 살인자들이라고 결론지었다. 이런 의심을 품고 그는 목소리를 한층 더 높여 소리쳤다. \"객줏집 대문을 닫아라. 아무도 나가지 못하게 하라. 여기서 사람이 살해당했다!\" 이 외침에 모두가 화들짝 놀라, 저마다 그 목소리가 자기에게 닿은 그 지점에서 싸움을 그만두었다. 객줏집 주인은 자기 방으로, 마부는 자기 길마로, 계집은 자기 잠자리로 물러갔고, 불운한 돈키호테와 산초만이 있던 자리에서 옴짝달싹할 수 없었다. 이에 순찰관은 돈키호테의 수염을 놓고, 죄인들을 찾아 잡아들이려고 불을 구하러 나갔다. 그러나 객줏집 주인이 제 방으로 물러나면서 일부러 등불을 꺼 버린 탓에 불을 찾지 못해, 그는 하는 수 없이 화덕에 의지할 수밖에 없었으니, 한참 동안 애를 먹은 끝에야 거기서 또 다른 등불 하나를 밝혔다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII.\n\n이때쯤 돈키호테는 기절에서 깨어났다. 그리고 전날 그가 \"말뚝의 골짜기\"에 길게 뻗어 누워 있을 때 종자를 불렀던 것과 똑같은 목소리로, 이제 다시 그를 부르기 시작했다. \"산초, 이보게, 자고 있는가? 잠들었는가, 산초?\"\n\n\"제기랄, 어떻게 잠을 잡니까!\" 산초가 불만스럽고 쓰라리게 대답했다. \"오늘 밤 온갖 악마들이 죄다 저를 덮친 게 뻔한데요?\"\n\n\"자네는 그 말을 충분히 믿어도 좋네.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내가 아는 게 적든지, 아니면 이 성이 마법에 걸렸든지 둘 중 하나일 테니. 알아 두게 — 다만 지금 내가 자네에게 말하려는 이것은, 내가 죽은 뒤까지 비밀로 지키겠다고 맹세해야 하네.\"\n\n\"맹세합니다.\" 산초가 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 이렇게 말하는 까닭은,\" 돈키호테가 이어 말했다. \"누구의 명예든 깎아내리는 일을 내가 몹시 싫어하기 때문일세.\"\n\n\"그럼 저는,\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"나리께서 살아 계시는 동안에는 이 일에 대해 입을 다물겠다고 맹세합니다. 그리고 하느님께서 내일 그걸 털어놓을 수 있게 해 주시기를 바랄 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 자네에게 그렇게 못된 짓을 했단 말인가, 산초,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그래서 그토록 빨리 내가 죽기를 바라는 게냐?\"\n\n\"그게 아닙니다.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"다만 저는 무언가를 오래 간직하는 걸 싫어하고, 너무 오래 두어 제 안에서 썩어 버리는 게 싫어서 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"어쨌든,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"나는 자네의 애정과 착한 천성을 더 믿네. 그러니 알아 두게, 오늘 밤 내게 내가 묘사할 수 있는 가장 기이한 모험 중 하나가 일어났네. 자네에게 그것을 간략히 들려주자면 이렇네. 조금 전에 이 성주의 따님이 내게 왔는데, 그 처자는 이 넓은 세상에서 찾아볼 수 있는 가장 우아하고 아름다운 아가씨였네. 그녀의 자태가 지닌 매력에 대해 내가 자네에게 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠는가! 그 생기 넘치는 재치에 대해서는! 그리고 또 다른 은밀한 일들에 대해서는, 내가 나의 여인 둘시네아 델 토보소에게 바쳐야 할 충절을 지키고자, 눈감고 침묵 속에 묻어 두겠네! 다만 자네에게 이것만 말하겠네. 운명이 행운으로 내 손에 쥐여진 그토록 큰 은총을 시샘했든지, 아니면 (이쪽이 더 그럴듯하지만) 이 성이, 내가 이미 말했듯이 마법에 걸려 있어서든지, 내가 그녀와 더없이 달콤하고 정겨운 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 내가 보지도 못하고 어디서 왔는지도 모르는 사이에, 어느 거대한 거인의 팔에 달린 손 하나가 나타나 내 턱에 어찌나 세게 주먹을 날렸던지 온통 피투성이가 되었고, 그러고는 나를 어찌나 두들겨 팼던지, 어제 짐꾼들이 로시난테의 무례한 짓을 빌미로 우리에게 자네가 아는 그 봉변을 안겼을 때보다도 더 심한 꼴이 되었네. 그러니 이 아가씨의 미모라는 보물을 지키는 마법에 걸린 무어인이 어딘가에 있고, 그 미모는 나를 위한 것이 아니라고 짐작할 만하지.\"\n\n\"저를 위한 것도 아니지요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"사백 명도 넘는 무어인들이 저를 어찌나 두들겨 팼던지, 그에 비하면 말뚝으로 얻어맞은 것은 과자에다 고급 빵이었습니다. 그런데 나리, 우리를 지금 이 꼴로 만들어 놓은 이 훌륭하고 진귀한 모험을 대체 뭐라고 부르시렵니까? 하기야 나리께서는 그렇게까지 형편이 나쁘진 않으셨지요, 말씀하신 그 비할 데 없는 미녀를 품에 안고 계셨으니까요. 하지만 저는, 제 평생 받아 본 것 중에 가장 묵직한 매질 말고 무엇을 얻었답니까? 운도 지지리 없는 저와 저를 낳으신 어머니라니! 저는 편력 기사도 아니고 앞으로도 결코 될 일이 없는데, 온갖 불행 중에서 더 큰 몫은 다 제 차지로 떨어지니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그러니까 자네도 두들겨 맞았단 말인가?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 그렇게 말씀드리지 않았습니까? 제 핏줄에 더 큰 불운이 있기를!\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"괴로워하지 말게, 이보게.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 이제 그 귀한 영약을 만들 테니, 그것으로 우리는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 나을 걸세.\"\n\n이때쯤 경관은 등불을 켜는 데 성공해서, 죽었으리라 여긴 사내를 보러 들어왔다. 산초는 문간에서 그를 언뜻 보았는데, 셔츠 바람으로 머리에 천을 두르고 손에 등불을 든 채 매우 험상궂은 얼굴로 들어오는 그를 보고는, 주인에게 말했다. \"나리, 잉크병에 아직 무언가 남아 있으면 우리에게 더 매질을 가하려고 저 마법에 걸린 무어인이 다시 온 게 아닐까요?\"\n\n\"무어인일 리 없네.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"마법에 걸린 자들은 누구에게도 제 모습을 보이지 않는 법이니까.\"\n\n\"제 모습을 보이지 않더라도, 제 손길은 느끼게 하지요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"아니라면, 제 어깨더러 한마디 하라고 하십시오.\"\n\n\"내 어깨도 한마디 할 수 있네.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"하지만 그것이 우리가 보는 것이 마법에 걸린 무어인이라고 믿기에 충분한 이유는 아닐세.\"\n\n경관이 다가와, 그들이 그렇게 평화로운 대화에 빠져 있는 것을 보고 놀라 멈춰 섰다. 물론 돈키호테는 순전히 두들겨 맞고 고약을 발라 댄 탓에 꼼짝도 못 한 채 여전히 등을 대고 누워 있었다. 경관은 그를 돌아보며 말했다. \"그래, 좀 어떤가, 이 양반아?\"\n\n\"내가 자네라면 좀 더 정중히 말하겠네.\" 돈키호테가 대꾸했다. \"이 나라에서는 편력 기사에게 그런 식으로 말을 거는 것이 풍습인가, 이 멍청한 작자야?\"\n\n경관은 그토록 초라해 보이는 자에게 그토록 무례한 대접을 받자 화가 치밀어, 기름이 가득 찬 등불을 치켜들고는 돈키호테의 머리를 한 대 후려쳐서 머리통을 몹시 깨뜨려 놓았다. 그러고는 모든 것이 어둠에 잠긴 가운데 밖으로 나갔고, 산초 판사가 말했다. \"저게 바로 마법에 걸린 무어인이 틀림없습니다, 나리. 보물은 다른 자들을 위해 지키고, 우리한테는 주먹질과 등불질만 하는군요.\"\n\n\"그게 진실일세.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"이 마법의 일들로 골머리를 앓거나 그것에 화내고 속 끓여 봐야 아무 소용이 없네. 그것들은 눈에 보이지 않고 환영 같은 것이라, 우리가 무슨 짓을 해도 복수할 상대를 찾지 못할 테니. 일어나게, 산초, 일어날 수 있거든 이 요새의 성주를 불러서, 기름과 포도주와 소금과 로즈메리를 조금 가져다 달라고 하게. 그 치유의 영약을 만들어야겠네. 정말이지 지금 그것이 몹시 필요한 것 같으니, 저 유령이 낸 상처에서 피를 많이 흘리고 있기 때문일세.\"\n\n산초는 뼈마디가 충분히 쑤시는 채로 일어나, 어둠 속에서 여관 주인을 찾아 나섰다. 그러다 제 적이 어떻게 되었는지 살펴보던 경관과 마주치자, 그에게 말했다. \"나리, 당신이 누구시든, 우리에게 로즈메리와 기름과 소금과 포도주를 조금 베풀어 주시는 친절을 부탁드립니다. 저쪽 침상에 누워, 이 여관에 있는 마법에 걸린 무어인의 손에 부상을 입은, 이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 편력 기사 중 한 분을 치료하는 데 필요한 것이라서요.\"\n\n경관은 그가 이런 식으로 말하는 것을 듣고는 정신 나간 자로 여겼고, 마침 날이 밝아 오기 시작하던 터라 여관 문을 열고 주인을 불러서 이 선량한 사내가 원하는 것을 일러 주었다. 주인은 그가 필요로 하는 것을 마련해 주었고, 산초는 그것을 돈키호테에게 가져갔다. 돈키호테는 머리에 손을 짚고 등불에 맞은 통증을 한탄하고 있었는데, 그 매는 사실 제법 커다란 혹 두 개를 돋게 한 것 말고는 그에게 별다른 해를 끼치지 않았고, 그가 피라고 여긴 것은 지난 소동을 겪는 동안 고통 속에서 그에게서 흘러나온 땀에 지나지 않았다. 간단히 말하면, 그는 재료들을 받아 그것들을 모두 섞어 한참 동안 끓여서 합제(合劑)를 만들었고, 마침내 완성되었다 싶을 때까지 그렇게 했다. 그러고는 그것을 담을 약병을 청했는데, 여관에 그런 것이 하나도 없어서, 주인이 그에게 선뜻 내준 양철 기름병이나 술병에 담기로 했다. 그러고는 그 병 위에 주기도문을 여든 번 넘게, 또 그만큼의 성모송과 찬미경과 사도신경을 거듭 외우면서, 말 한마디마다 축복의 표시로 십자가를 그었다. 이 모든 일에 산초와 여관 주인과 경관이 함께 있었으니, 짐꾼은 이제 평화로이 제 노새들을 편안케 돌보는 데 여념이 없었기 때문이다.\n\n이 일을 마치자 그는 자신이 귀하게 여기는 이 영약의 효험을 그 자리에서 직접 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 간절해져서, 병에 담지 못하고 끓이던 돼지 가죽 부대에 남아 있던 것을 거의 한 쿼트가량 들이켰다. 그런데 다 마시기가 무섭게 어찌나 토하기 시작했던지 위장에 아무것도 남지 않았고, 토하는 고통과 경련 속에서 비 오듯 땀을 흘렸으니, 그 까닭에 그는 사람들에게 자기를 덮어 주고 혼자 있게 해 달라고 청했다. 그들이 그리하자 그는 세 시간 넘게 잠들어 있었고, 그 끝에 깨어나서는 몸이 무척 가뿐해진 것을 느꼈으며, 멍든 곳도 어찌나 후련해졌던지 자신이 완전히 나았다고 여겼고, 자신이 정말로 피에라브라스의 영약을 알아맞혔다고 진정으로 믿었다. 그리고 이 약만 있으면 앞으로는 어떤 종류의 파멸이든 전투든 결투든, 그것이 아무리 위험할지라도 아무런 두려움 없이 맞설 수 있으리라 믿었다.\n\n자기 주인이 회복된 것을 기적이라 여긴 산초 판사도 돼지 가죽 부대에 남은 것을 자기에게 달라고 청했는데, 남은 양이 적지 않았다. 돈키호테가 허락하자 그는 두 손으로 그것을 받아 들고, 굳은 믿음과 한층 더 굳센 의지로 꿀꺽꿀꺽 들이켜 제 주인이 마신 것보다 별로 적지 않은 양을 비웠다. 그러나 사실인즉, 가엾은 산초의 위장은 어쩔 수 없이 제 주인의 것만큼 섬세하지 못했던지라, 토하기도 전에 어찌나 심한 뒤틀림과 구역질에, 또 어찌나 많은 땀과 어지럼증에 사로잡혔던지, 그는 정말이지 진심으로 자기 마지막 순간이 왔다고 믿었고, 자신이 그토록 쥐어짜이고 시달리는 것을 깨닫고는 그 영약과 그것을 자기에게 준 도둑놈을 저주했다.\n\n돈키호테가 그가 이런 상태에 있는 것을 보고 말했다. \"산초, 내 생각에는 이 곤욕이 자네가 기사로 서임되지 않았기 때문에 생기는 것 같네. 이 약은 그렇지 못한 자들에게는 좋을 수가 없다고 나는 확신하기 때문일세.\"\n\n\"나리께서 그걸 알고 계셨으면서,\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"— 저와 제 일가친척 모두에게 화가 미치기를! — 어째서 제게 맛보게 하셨습니까?\"\n\n이 순간 마신 약이 효력을 발하기 시작했고, 가엾은 종자는 양쪽으로 어찌나 격렬히 쏟아 내기 시작했던지, 그가 몸을 던졌던 골풀 자리도, 덮고 있던 거친 천 담요도 그 뒤로는 아무짝에도 쓸모없게 되어 버렸다. 그는 어찌나 발작과 경련을 일으키며 땀을 줄줄 흘렸던지, 그 자신뿐 아니라 그 자리에 있던 모두가 그의 최후가 왔다고 여겼다. 이 폭풍 같은 고난은 두 시간쯤 이어졌고, 그 끝에 그는 자기 주인처럼이 아니라, 어찌나 기진하고 탈진했던지 일어설 수조차 없는 지경으로 남겨졌다. 그러나 앞서 말했듯이 몸이 후련하고 멀쩡해진 것을 느낀 돈키호테는, 당장 모험을 찾아 떠나고 싶어 안달이었다. 그가 거기서 빈둥거리는 동안의 모든 시간이 세상에 대한, 그리고 그의 도움과 보호를 필요로 하는 세상 사람들에 대한 사기처럼 여겨졌기 때문이며, 영약이 그에게 안겨 준 안심과 자신감이 있는 마당이라 더더욱 그러했다. 그리하여 이 충동에 떠밀려 그는 손수 로시난테에 안장을 얹고, 종자의 짐승에게도 짐안장을 얹어 주었으며, 종자가 옷을 차려입고 당나귀에 오르는 것도 거들어 주었다. 그런 다음 자기 말에 올라타고 여관 한구석으로 가서, 거기 세워져 있던 창 한 자루를 집어 들어 자신의 창 대신 쓰기로 했다. 여관에 있던 사람들은, 스무 명이 넘었는데, 모두 서서 그를 지켜보고 있었다. 여관 주인의 딸도 마찬가지로 그를 바라보고 있었고, 그 역시 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 이따금 제 배 속 깊은 데서 끌어올린 듯한 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러나 사람들은 모두 그것이 갈비뼈가 아파서 그러는 것이려니 여겼다. 적어도 전날 밤 그가 고약을 바른 모습을 본 자들은 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n둘 다 말에 올라 여관 문간에 이르자, 그는 주인을 불러 매우 엄숙하고 절제된 목소리로 말했다. \"성주 나리, 제가 이 성에서 받은 은혜가 많고도 큽니다. 그래서 저는 평생토록 그 모든 것에 감사해야 할 깊은 의무를 지고 있습니다. 혹여 당신께 해를 끼친 어떤 오만한 적을 응징해 그 은혜를 갚을 수 있다면, 부디 아십시오, 저의 소명은 다름 아니라 약한 자를 돕고, 부당한 일을 당한 이들의 원수를 갚으며, 배신을 응징하는 것입니다. 기억을 더듬어 보시고, 만일 이런 종류의 일이 떠오르거든 그저 제게 말씀만 하십시오. 그러면 제가 받은 기사도의 이름으로 약속하건대, 당신이 바라는 바를 끝까지 충족시켜 드리고 그 보상을 받게 해 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n여관 주인도 그에 못지않게 차분히 그에게 대답했다. \"기사 나리, 저는 나리께서 제 어떤 원한이든 갚아 주시길 바라지 않습니다. 제게 무슨 일이 생기면 제가 알아서 적당한 복수를 할 수 있으니까요. 제가 바라는 단 한 가지는, 어젯밤 이 여관에서 쌓인 외상값을 치러 주시는 겁니다. 두 짐승에게 준 짚과 보리값에다, 저녁 식사와 잠자리값까지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 여기가 여관이란 말인가?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"그것도 아주 번듯한 여관이지요.\" 여관 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"내가 그동안 줄곧 착각하고 있었구먼.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"정말이지 나는 이곳이 성이라고, 그것도 나쁘지 않은 성이라고 여겼으니. 하지만 이곳이 성이 아니라 여관임이 드러난 이상, 이제 할 수 있는 일이라고는 자네가 그 셈을 면해 주는 것뿐일세. 나로서는 편력 기사의 규율을 어길 수가 없으니. 내가 사실로 아는 바로는(그리고 지금까지 그와 다른 것을 읽은 적이 없네) 그들은 머무는 여관에서 잠자리값이든 무엇이든 결코 치르지 않았네. 그들에게 베풀어지는 어떤 환대든, 그들이 밤낮으로, 여름이건 겨울이건, 걸어서든 말 타고든, 굶주림과 목마름 속에, 추위와 더위 속에, 하늘의 온갖 모진 날씨와 땅의 온갖 고난에 내맡겨진 채 모험을 찾아 견뎌 내는 그 견디기 힘든 노고의 대가로, 법도와 권리에 따라 마땅히 받아야 할 그들의 몫이기 때문일세.\"\n\n\"그건 저와 별 상관이 없습니다.\" 여관 주인이 대꾸했다. \"제게 빚진 것이나 치르시고, 기사도 얘기는 더 그만둡시다. 제가 신경 쓰는 것이라고는 오직 제 돈을 받는 것뿐이니까요.\"\n\n\"이 어리석고 천박한 여관 주인 같으니.\" 돈키호테는 이렇게 말하고는 로시난테에 박차를 가하고 창을 비스듬히 내리며 아무도 그를 막을 새 없이 여관에서 말을 몰아 나가, 종자가 따라오는지 돌아보지도 않은 채 얼마쯤 앞으로 내달렸다.\n\n여관 주인은 그가 값을 치르지 않고 떠나는 것을 보고 산초에게 셈을 받으러 달려갔다. 산초는 제 주인이 치르지 않으니 자기도 치르지 않겠노라고 했으니, 자신은 편력 기사의 종자인 만큼 여관이나 숙소에서 아무것도 치르지 않는 일에 관해서는 주인과 똑같은 규율과 이치가 자신에게도 그대로 적용된다는 것이었다. 이에 여관 주인은 몹시 노발대발하며, 치르지 않으면 좋아하지 않을 방식으로 강제하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 산초는 이에 대해, 제 주인이 받은 기사도의 법도에 따라 자신은 목숨이 달아나는 한이 있어도 한 푼도 치르지 않겠노라고 대답했다. 편력 기사들의 그 훌륭하고 유서 깊은 관례를 자신이 어기는 일은 없을 것이며, 앞으로 세상에 나올 그런 기사들의 종자들이 자신에 대해 불평하거나 그토록 정당한 특권을 깨뜨렸다고 자신을 책망하는 일도 결코 없으리라는 것이었다.\n\n불운한 산초의 액운이 이렇게 일을 꾸몄으니, 여관에 있던 무리 가운데 세고비아에서 온 양털 빗는 직공 넷, 코르도바의 망아지 광장에서 온 바늘 만드는 직공 셋, 세비야 장터에서 온 손님 둘이 있었던 것이다. 이들은 활기차고 마음 여리며 농담을 좋아하고 장난기 많은 친구들이었는데, 거의 같은 충동에 부추겨지고 움직인 듯이 산초에게 달려들어 그를 당나귀에서 끌어 내렸고, 그중 하나가 주인 침대의 담요를 가지러 들어갔다. 그러나 그를 담요에 던져 넣다가 위를 올려다보니 천장이 그들의 작업에 필요한 것보다 다소 낮은지라, 하늘이 곧 천장이 되는 마당으로 나가기로 했다. 그러고는 거기서 산초를 담요 한가운데에 놓고 그를 높이 띄워 올리기 시작했으니, 마치 사육제 때 개를 가지고 노는 것처럼 그를 가지고 장난쳤다.\n\n담요에 띄워진 가엾은 자의 비명이 어찌나 컸던지 그의 주인의 귀에까지 닿았고, 주인은 멈춰 서서 귀 기울여 듣고는 무슨 새로운 모험이 다가오고 있구나 확신했는데, 마침내 그 소리를 지르는 것이 제 종자임을 똑똑히 알아차렸다. 그는 말 머리를 돌려 힘겨운 질주로 여관에 다다랐고, 문이 닫혀 있는 것을 보고는 들어갈 만한 길이 없는지 둘러보려고 여관 둘레를 돌았다. 그러나 그다지 높지 않은 마당 담벼락에 이르자마자, 제 종자를 상대로 벌어지고 있는 놀음을 발견했다. 그는 산초가 어찌나 우아하고 날렵하게 공중에서 오르내리는지를 보았으니, 분노가 허락만 했더라면 그가 웃음을 터뜨렸으리라고 나는 믿는다. 그는 말에서 담벼락 꼭대기로 기어오르려 했으나, 어찌나 멍들고 두들겨 맞았던지 말에서 내릴 수조차 없었다. 그래서 그는 말 등에 앉은 채 산초를 담요질하는 자들에게, 정확히 받아 적기란 불가능할 만큼 온갖 저주와 욕설을 퍼붓기 시작했다. 그러나 그들은 이 때문에 웃음도 작업도 멈추지 않았고, 날아오르는 산초도 이제는 위협을, 또 이제는 애원을 뒤섞은 한탄을 그치지 않았으나, 다 부질없거나 아무 소용이 없었으며, 마침내 그들은 순전히 지쳐서야 그만두었다. 그러고는 그에게 당나귀를 가져다주고 그 위에 그를 태운 다음, 그의 윗도리를 둘러 주었다. 그리고 그가 그토록 기진한 것을 본 가엾이 여기는 마음의 마리토르네스는, 그에게 물 한 단지로 기운을 차리게 해 주는 것이 좋겠다 싶어, 그것이 한결 더 시원하도록 우물에서 길어 왔다. 산초는 그것을 받아 입에 가져가려다, 이렇게 외치는 제 주인의 고함에 멈췄다. \"산초, 내 아들아, 물을 마시지 말게. 마시지 말게, 내 아들아, 그것은 자네를 죽일 걸세. 보게, 여기 그 축복받은 영약이 있지 않은가(그러면서 그는 약병을 치켜들었다). 이것을 두 방울만 마시면 자네는 틀림없이 원기를 회복할 걸세.\"\n\n이 말에 산초는 곁눈질로 그를 흘겨보며 한층 더 큰 목소리로 말했다. \"나리께서는 제가 기사가 아니라는 걸 잊으셨습니까, 아니면 어젯밤 이후로 제게 남은 창자나마 다 토해 내며 끝장나기를 바라십니까? 그놈의 약일랑 온갖 악마들의 이름으로 나리나 가지시고, 저는 제멋대로 내버려 두십시오!\" 그러고는 말을 그치는 바로 그 순간에 마시기 시작했다. 그러나 첫 모금에 그것이 물인 것을 알아채고는 더 이어 마실 마음이 없어져, 마리토르네스에게 포도주를 좀 가져다 달라고 청했고, 그녀는 흔쾌히 그리해 주었으며 게다가 제 돈으로 값을 치렀다. 사람들이 그녀에 대해 하는 말로는, 그녀가 비록 그런 처지의 삶을 살고는 있었으나 그녀에게는 어딘가 희미하고 아득하게나마 기독교인다운 데가 남아 있었다고 한다. 산초는 다 마시고 나서 당나귀 옆구리에 발뒤꿈치를 박았고, 여관 문이 활짝 열려 있던 터라 아무것도 치르지 않고 제 뜻을 관철한 것에 무척 흡족해하며 빠져나갔다. 비록 그것이 늘 그의 담보가 되어 주던 어깨를 대가로 치른 것이긴 했지만 말이다. 사실 여관 주인은 그가 빚진 값으로 그의 안장주머니를 붙들어 두었으나, 산초는 어찌나 허둥지둥 떠났던지 그것이 없어진 것조차 알아채지 못했다. 여관 주인은 그가 떠나는 것을 보자마자 문을 단단히 걸어 잠그려 했으나, 담요질하던 자들이 이에 동의하지 않았으니, 그들은 돈키호테가 설령 정말로 원탁의 편력 기사 중 하나였다 한들 그를 두 푼어치만큼도 대수롭게 여기지 않을 부류였기 때문이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII.\n\n산초는 너무도 기진맥진하고 몸이 늘어진 채 주인에게 다다라서, 제 짐승을 몰아붙일 기력조차 없었다. 돈키호테는 그 꼴을 보고 말했다. \"착한 산초여, 이제 나는 이런 결론에 이르렀느니라. 이 성, 아니 이 객줏집은 의심할 여지없이 마법에 걸린 것이다. 너를 그토록 잔인하게 가지고 논 자들이 유령이거나 다른 세계의 존재가 아니라면 대체 무엇이겠느냐? 그리고 이를 더욱 확실히 믿게 된 까닭이 있으니, 내가 안마당 담장 곁에서 너의 그 슬픈 비극을 지켜보고 섰을 때, 도무지 그 담장을 기어오를 수도 없었고, 로시난테에서 내려설 수조차 없었다. 그자들이 분명 나를 마법에 걸어 놓았던 게야. 내 이 신분의 이름을 걸고 너에게 맹세하노니, 만일 내가 담을 넘거나 말에서 내릴 수 있었더라면, 그 허풍선이 도둑놈들이 자기들 장난질을 영원토록 잊지 못하도록 너의 원수를 갚아 주었을 것이다. 비록 그리하는 것이, 내가 너에게 누차 일렀듯이, 기사가 아닌 자에게 손을 대는 것을 금하는 기사도의 법도를 어기는 줄 알면서도 말이다. 다만 제 목숨과 몸을 지키기 위한 절박하고 큰 위급함이 있을 때는 예외이니라.\"\n\n\"저도 할 수만 있었다면 제 원수를 갚았을 겁니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"기사 서임을 받았든 안 받았든 말입니다요. 하지만 그럴 수가 없었습니다요. 그래도 제 생각으로는, 저를 가지고 논 자들은 나리께서 말씀하시는 유령이나 마법에 걸린 자들이 아니라, 우리들처럼 살과 뼈로 된 사람들이었습니다요. 다들 이름이 있었거든요. 저를 던져 올릴 때 서로 이름 부르는 걸 제가 들었습죠. 한 놈은 페드로 마르티네스라 불렀고, 또 한 놈은 테노리오 에르난데스, 그리고 주막 주인은 왼손잡이 후안 팔로메케라고 부르는 걸 들었습니다요. 그러니 나리, 나리께서 안마당 담을 뛰어넘지도 못하고 말에서 내리지도 못하신 건 마법 말고 다른 무언가 때문이었습죠. 그리고 제가 이 모든 일에서 분명히 깨달은 건, 우리가 찾아 헤매는 이런 모험들이 결국엔 우리를 어떤 봉변으로 끌고 들어가서, 어느 쪽이 우리 오른발인지조차 분간 못하게 만들리란 겁니다요. 제 짧은 머리로 생각하기엔, 지금이 추수철이니 가장 좋고 현명한 일은 우리가 집으로 돌아가 제 할 일에 매달리는 것입죠. 속담대로, 세카에서 메카로, 이 양동이에서 저 양동이로 떠돌아다니는 짓일랑 그만두는 거 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"산초, 너는 기사도에 대해 어찌 그리도 아는 게 없느냐.\" 돈키호테가 대꾸했다. \"입을 다물고 참을성을 가지거라. 언젠가는 이 천직을 좇아 떠도는 것이 얼마나 명예로운 일인지 네 두 눈으로 보게 될 날이 오리라. 아니, 말해 보아라, 세상에 이보다 더 큰 즐거움이 어디 있으며, 싸움에서 이겨 적을 무찌르는 그 기쁨에 견줄 만한 환희가 어디 있겠느냐? 의심할 바 없이 그런 건 없느니라.\"\n\n\"그럴 법도 합니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"하지만 저는 모르겠는뎁쇼. 제가 아는 거라곤, 우리가 편력 기사가 된 이래, 아니 나리께서 편력 기사가 되신 이래(저 같은 놈이 그토록 명예로운 무리 축에 든다고 할 권리는 없으니 말입죠) 우리는 비스카야 사람과 벌인 그 한 판 말고는 어떤 싸움에서도 이긴 적이 없습니다요. 게다가 그것에서조차 나리께서는 귀 한쪽과 투구 반쪽을 잃고 나오셨지요. 그때부터 지금까지 줄곧 몽둥이질에 또 몽둥이질, 주먹질에 또 주먹질뿐이었고, 저는 그 위에 담요 키질까지 덤으로 받았습죠. 그러고는 마법에 걸린 자들이나 만나니, 그자들한테는 원수를 갚을 수도 없어서 나리께서 말씀하시는 그 적을 무찌르는 기쁨이라는 게 어떤 건지 알 도리가 없습니다요.\"\n\n\"그것이 바로 나를 괴롭게 하는 것이며, 너 또한 괴롭게 해야 마땅한 것이니라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"허나 이제부터 나는 어떤 마법도 그것을 지닌 자에게 효력을 부리지 못하도록 신묘한 솜씨로 만든 검 한 자루를 손에 넣도록 애써 보겠다. 어쩌면 운명이 내게, '불타는 검의 기사'라 불리던 시절 아마디스가 지녔던 그 검을 마련해 줄지도 모르지. 그것은 세상의 어떤 기사가 가졌던 것 중에서도 으뜸가는 검 가운데 하나였으니, 앞서 말한 그 신묘한 효험을 지녔을 뿐 아니라 면도날처럼 잘 들어, 제아무리 튼튼하고 마법에 걸린 갑옷이라도 그것을 당해 내지 못했느니라.\"\n\n\"제 팔자가 그렇습죠.\" 산초가 말했다. \"설령 그런 일이 일어나 나리께서 그런 검을 손에 넣으신다 해도, 그건 그 영약처럼 서임받은 기사들한테만 쓸모 있고 좋은 것으로 판명될 테고, 종자들로 말하자면 그저 시름이나 안주 삼아 들이켜야 할 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"그것은 두려워 말거라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"하늘이 너를 더 나은 길로 이끌어 줄 것이니라.\"\n\n이렇게 이야기를 나누며 돈키호테와 그의 종자가 길을 가고 있을 때, 그들이 따라가던 길 위로 돈키호테는 크고 짙은 먼지구름이 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 알아챘고, 그것을 보고는 산초를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"산초, 오늘이 바로 내 운명이 나를 위해 간직해 둔 은혜가 드러날 날이로다. 다시 말하노니, 오늘이야말로 그 어느 날 못지않게 내 팔의 위력이 펼쳐질 날이며, 내가 오는 모든 세대에 걸쳐 명예의 책에 길이 적힐 위업을 이룰 날이니라. 저기 솟아오르는 저 먼지구름이 보이느냐? 자, 그러니까 저것은 여러 갈래의 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 민족으로 이루어진 어마어마한 군대가 저리로 행군해 오면서 일으킨 것이니라.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 둘이 있는 게 틀림없습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"이쪽 맞은편에서도 꼭 그만한 먼지구름이 하나 솟아오르고 있거든요.\"\n\n돈키호테가 돌아서서 바라보니 과연 그것이 사실이라, 그는 몹시 기뻐하며 그 두 군대가 저 드넓은 벌판 한가운데서 맞붙어 싸우려 하는 것이라고 결론지었다. 언제 어느 때고 그의 공상은 기사도 책에 적힌 전투와 마법과 모험과 미친 듯한 무훈과 사랑과 도전으로 가득 차 있었고, 그가 말하고 생각하고 행하는 모든 것이 그런 것들과 관련되어 있었기 때문이다. 그런데 그가 본 그 먼지구름이란 같은 길을 따라 서로 반대 방향에서 오던 양 떼 두 무리가 일으킨 것이었고, 먼지 때문에 가까이 다가오기 전까지는 보이지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 돈키호테가 그것이 군대라고 너무도 확신에 차서 단언하는 통에 산초도 그 말을 믿게 되어 이렇게 말했다. \"그래, 그럼 우리는 어찌해야 합니까요, 나리?\"\n\n\"어찌하다니?\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"약한 자들과 도움이 필요한 자들을 돕고 거드는 게지. 그리고 산초, 너는 알아 두어라, 우리 맞은편에서 오는 저것은 위대한 트라포바나 섬의 군주, 강대한 황제 알리판파론이 이끌고 통솔하는 것이며, 내 등 뒤에서 행군해 오는 저 다른 것은 그의 적인 가라만타의 왕, 맨팔의 펜타폴린의 것이니라. 그는 늘 오른팔을 드러내고 전장에 나서기에 그리 불리는 게야.\"\n\n\"한데 이 두 군주는 어찌하여 그토록 원수지간입니까요?\"\n\n\"그들이 서로 원수가 된 까닭은,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"이 알리판파론이 광포한 이교도이며 펜타폴린의 딸을 사랑하기 때문이니라. 그 딸은 매우 아름답고 게다가 우아한 귀부인이며 그리스도교도인데, 그 아비가 거짓 예언자 마호메트의 종교를 먼저 버리고 자기 종교를 받아들이지 않는 한 그 이교도 왕에게 딸을 내주려 하지 않는 게지.\"\n\n\"제 수염을 걸고,\" 산초가 말했다. \"펜타폴린이 아주 옳게 하는 겁니다요. 저도 힘닿는 데까지 그를 돕겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"그리하면 너는 마땅히 할 도리를 다하는 것이니라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이런 종류의 싸움에 끼어드는 데는 서임받은 기사일 필요가 없으니까 말이다.\"\n\n\"그건 저도 잘 알아듣겠습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"한데 이 당나귀를 어디다 두어야 싸움이 끝난 뒤에 틀림없이 다시 찾을 수 있을깝쇼? 제가 알기로 여태껏 이런 짐승을 타고 전장에 나가는 건 관례가 아니었던 것 같아서 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"그건 맞는 말이다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 그놈을 두고 할 수 있는 최선은, 잃어버리든 말든 운에 맡겨 내버려 두는 것이니라. 우리가 승리자로 나올 때 얻게 될 말이 어찌나 많을지, 로시난테조차 다른 말로 바꿔질 위험에 처할 정도일 테니까. 허나 내 말을 새겨듣고 잘 보아 두어라. 이 두 군대에 함께한 으뜸가는 기사들에 관해 너에게 좀 일러 주고 싶구나. 그리고 네가 더 잘 보고 새길 수 있도록, 저기 솟아 있는 저 언덕으로 물러가자. 거기서라면 두 군대가 다 보일 게다.\"\n\n그들은 그렇게 하여, 돈키호테가 군대로 여긴 그 두 떼가 일으킨 먼지구름이 가리고 시야를 가로막지만 않았더라면 또렷이 보였을 그 솟은 땅에 자리를 잡았다. 그럼에도 돈키호테는 보이지 않는 것, 존재하지도 않는 것을 자기 상상 속에서 보면서 이렇게 큰 소리로 말하기 시작했다.\n\n\"네가 저기 보는, 노란 갑옷을 입고 처녀의 발치에 웅크린 왕관 쓴 사자를 방패에 그려 넣은 저 기사는 은(銀)의 다리의 군주, 용맹한 라우르칼코니라. 금빛 꽃무늬 갑옷을 입고 하늘빛 바탕에 은빛 왕관 셋을 방패에 그려 넣은 저자는 키로시아의 대공, 두려운 미코콜렘보이며, 그 오른편에 있는 저 거인 같은 몸집의 사내는 결코 겁먹는 법이 없는 세 아라비아의 군주, 브란다바르바란 데 볼리체이니, 갑옷으로 저 뱀 가죽을 두르고 방패로는 성문 하나를 들었는데, 전해 오는 이야기로는 그것이 삼손이 죽음으로써 자기 원수들에게 복수할 때 무너뜨린 그 신전의 성문 가운데 하나라 하느니라. 허나 눈을 다른 쪽으로 돌려 보아라, 그러면 이 다른 군대의 맨 앞 선봉에서 늘 승리하고 결코 패한 적 없는 카르카호나의 티모넬, 새 비스카야의 왕자를 보게 될 것이다. 그는 하늘빛, 초록빛, 흰빛, 노란빛으로 네 등분한 문장의 갑옷을 입고 왔으며, 황갈색 바탕에 금빛 고양이 한 마리를 방패에 그려 넣고 '미아우'라는 표어를 새겼는데, 이는 그가 모시는 귀부인의 이름 첫머리이니라. 소문에 따르면 그 귀부인은 둘도 없는 미아울리나로, 알가르베의 공작 알페니켄의 딸이라 하느니라. 저 힘센 군마의 허리를 짓누르고 눌러 대며 눈처럼 흰 갑옷에, 아무런 문양도 없는 빈 방패를 든 다른 한 사람은 프랑스 태생의 풋내기 기사로, 이름은 피에르 파팽, 우트리케 영지의 영주이니라. 또 쇠를 댄 발꿈치로 저 날렵한 얼룩무늬 얼룩말의 옆구리를 차며, 갑옷으로 하늘빛 다람쥐 털 문장을 두른 저 다른 사람은 강대한 네르비아의 공작, 에스파르타필라르도 델 보스케이니, 방패의 문장으로 아스파라거스 한 포기를 그리고 카스티야어로 '내 운수를 좇는다'는 표어를 새겼느니라.\" 이렇게 그는 자기 상상 속에서 이쪽 부대, 저쪽 부대의 기사들을 잇따라 호명해 나갔고, 그 들어 본 적 없는 광기의 환상에 휩쓸린 채 모든 이에게 그 자리에서 곧장 갑옷이며 빛깔이며 문양이며 표어를 붙여 주었다. 그러고는 잠시도 멈추지 않고 이어 갔다. \"여러 갈래의 민족이 앞에 있는 이 부대를 이루고 있느니라. 여기 이름난 크산토스의 단물을 마시는 자들이 있고, 숲 우거진 마실리아의 평원을 누비는 자들이 있으며, 아라비아 펠릭스의 순수하고 고운 황금을 가려내는 자들이 있고, 수정 같은 테르모돈의 이름난 서늘한 강기슭을 즐기는 자들이 있으며, 갖가지 방식으로 황금빛 팍톨로스의 물줄기를 갈라놓는 자들이 있고, 약속에 신의가 없는 누미디아인들, 활쏘기로 이름난 페르시아인들, 달아나면서 싸우는 파르티아인들과 메디아인들, 늘 거처를 옮기는 아랍인들, 아름다운 만큼이나 잔혹한 스키타이인들, 입술에 구멍을 뚫은 에티오피아인들, 그리고 그 이름은 기억나지 않으나 그 생김새는 알아보고 분간할 수 있는 무수히 많은 다른 민족들이 있느니라. 이 다른 부대에는 올리브 무성한 베티스의 수정 같은 물줄기를 마시는 자들이 오고, 늘 풍요롭고 황금빛인 타호의 물로 얼굴을 다듬는 자들, 신성한 헤닐의 기름진 물줄기를 기뻐하는 자들, 목초 넘치는 타르테소스의 평원을 누비는 자들, 헤레스의 엘리시온 같은 초원에서 즐기는 자들, 붉게 익은 보리 이삭으로 머리를 두른 부유한 라만차 사람들, 고트 종족의 오랜 유물인 쇠갑옷을 두른 자들, 부드러운 물살로 이름난 피수에르가에서 멱 감는 자들, 숨겨진 물길로 이름난 굽이진 과디아나의 드넓은 목초지를 따라 가축을 먹이는 자들, 솔숲 우거진 피레네의 추위나 드높은 아펜니노의 눈부신 눈발에 떠는 자들, 한마디로 온 유럽이 품고 거느린 모든 자들이 오느니라.\"\n\n맙소사, 그가 호명한 나라와 민족이 얼마나 많았던가! 거짓투성이 책에서 읽은 것으로 가득 차 넘쳐 흠뻑 젖은 채, 놀라우리만치 거침없이 저마다에 어울리는 특성을 붙여 주었다! 산초 판사는 말없이 그의 말에 매달려 있다가, 이따금 돌아보며 자기 주인이 묘사하는 그 기사들과 거인들을 볼 수 있을까 하고 살폈으나, 그 가운데 하나도 분간해 낼 수 없자 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"나리, 빌어먹을, 나리께서 말씀하시는 그 누구의 그림자도, 기사든 거인이든 도통 보이질 않습니다요. 어쩌면 이게 죄다 마법인지도 모르겠습죠, 어젯밤 그 유령들처럼 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"어찌 그런 말을 하느냐!\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"군마들의 울음소리며 나팔 소리, 북소리가 들리지 않느냐?\"\n\n\"양 떼와 새끼 양들이 어마어마하게 우는 소리 말고는 아무것도 들리지 않습니다요.\" 산초가 말했는데, 그것은 사실이었으니, 이때쯤에는 두 양 떼가 가까이 다가와 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"산초, 네가 빠져 있는 두려움이,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"너로 하여금 똑바로 보지도 듣지도 못하게 하는 게다. 두려움의 작용 가운데 하나가 바로 감각을 어지럽혀 사물을 본디와 다르게 보이도록 만드는 것이니라. 네가 그토록 두렵거든 한쪽으로 물러나 나를 혼자 있게 두어라. 내가 내 도움을 베풀 그쪽 편에 승리를 안겨 주는 데는 나 혼자로도 족하니라.\" 이렇게 말하고는 로시난테에게 박차를 가하고 창을 옆구리에 비껴 든 채 벼락처럼 비탈을 내달려 내려갔다. 산초가 그 뒤에다 대고 소리쳤다. \"돌아오십쇼, 돈키호테 나리. 하느님께 맹세코 나리께서 들이치시는 건 양 떼와 새끼 양들입니다요! 돌아오십쇼! 저를 낳은 아비가 불쌍하지! 이게 무슨 미친 짓입니까요! 보십쇼, 거기 거인도, 기사도, 고양이도, 갑옷도, 네 등분한 방패도 온전한 방패도, 하늘빛 다람쥐 털 문장도 무슨 귀신 들린 것도 없습니다요. 대체 뭘 하시는 겁니까요? 하느님 앞에 죄인인 이놈!\" 그러나 이 모든 애원에도 돈키호테는 돌아서지 않았다. 도리어 계속 소리쳐 댔다. \"오, 기사들이여, 용맹한 맨팔의 황제 펜타폴린의 깃발 아래 따르며 싸우는 그대들이여, 모두 나를 따르라. 내가 그의 적 트라포바나의 알리판파론에게 얼마나 손쉽게 그의 원수를 갚아 주는지 보게 되리라.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고는 그는 새끼 양 떼 한가운데로 돌진해 들어가, 진짜로 죽일 듯한 적들을 꿰뚫기라도 하는 듯 어찌나 기세 좋고 겁 없이 창으로 양들을 찔러 대기 시작했는지 모른다. 양 떼를 따라온 목동들과 몰이꾼들이 그만두라고 그에게 소리쳤으나, 소용없음을 보자 그들은 투석기를 풀어 주먹만 한 돌멩이로 그의 귀를 향해 인사를 보내기 시작했다. 돈키호테는 돌멩이 따위에는 아랑곳하지 않고 좌우로 마구 휘둘러 대며 줄곧 외쳤다.\n\n\"오만한 알리판파론, 어디 있느냐? 내 앞으로 나오라. 나는 너의 무용을 일대일로 시험해 보고, 용맹한 펜타폴린 가라만타에게 저지른 네 잘못의 대가로 네 목숨을 내놓게 하려는 한 사람의 기사이니라.\" 이때 시냇가에서 사탕 알 하나가 날아와 그의 옆구리를 쳐서 갈빗대 두 대를 그의 몸속에 박아 넣었다. 그토록 얻어맞고 보니 그는 자신이 분명 죽었거나 심하게 다쳤다고 여겨, 자기 영약을 떠올리며 호리병을 꺼내어 입에 갖다 대고 그 속을 위장 속으로 부어 넣기 시작했다. 그러나 그가 보기에 충분하다 싶을 만큼 미처 삼키기도 전에 또 다른 아몬드 알이 날아와 그의 손과 호리병을 어찌나 정통으로 맞혔는지, 호리병을 산산조각 내고 지나가는 길에 그의 입에서 이와 어금니 서너 개를 떨궈 내고 그의 손가락 두 개를 호되게 으스러뜨렸다. 첫 번째 가격과 두 번째 가격의 힘이 어찌나 셌던지, 가엾은 기사는 자기 뜻과는 달리 말에서 뒤로 떨어지고 말았다. 목동들이 달려와서는 그를 죽였다고 확신했다. 그리하여 그들은 부랴부랴 양 떼를 한데 모으고, 일곱 마리가 넘는 죽은 짐승들을 거둬들인 뒤, 더는 아무것도 알아보려 기다리지 않고 달아나 버렸다.\n\n이 모든 동안 산초는 언덕에 서서 자기 주인이 벌이는 미친 짓을 지켜보며, 제 수염을 쥐어뜯고 운명이 자기를 그와 알게 만든 그 시각과 그 계기를 저주하고 있었다. 그러다가 주인이 땅바닥에 나뒹굴고 목동들도 자취를 감춘 것을 보자, 그에게 달려가 그가 정신은 잃지 않았으나 몹시 안 좋은 꼴인 것을 발견하고는 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 돌아오시라고 말씀드리지 않았습니까요, 돈키호테 나리. 그리고 나리께서 들이치시려던 게 군대가 아니라 양 떼라고 말씀드리지 않았습니까요?\"\n\n\"그게 바로 그 도둑놈 같은 마법사, 내 원수가 사물을 바꿔치기하고 거짓으로 둔갑시키는 수법이니라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"산초, 너는 알아 두어라, 그런 부류의 자들에게는 자기들이 원하는 것을 우리가 믿게 만드는 일쯤은 아주 손쉬운 노릇이다. 그리고 나를 핍박하는 이 악의에 찬 존재가, 내가 이 싸움에서 거두리라는 걸 알고 있던 그 영광을 시샘하여, 적의 부대를 양 떼로 바꿔 놓은 게다. 어쨌든 산초, 부탁이니 이만큼은 해 다오. 네 스스로 미혹에서 벗어나 내 말이 참인지 확인해 보아라. 네 당나귀에 올라타 조용히 그들을 따라가 보아라. 그러면 그들이 여기서 조금 멀어졌을 때, 본래의 모습으로 되돌아가 양이기를 그치고 내가 처음에 너에게 묘사해 준 그대로 모든 면에서 사람이 되는 것을 보게 될 게다. 허나 아직은 가지 말거라, 네 도움과 거듦이 필요하니까. 이리 와서, 내 이와 어금니가 몇 개나 빠졌는지 보아라, 입안에 하나도 남지 않은 것만 같구나.\"\n\n산초가 어찌나 바짝 다가왔는지 거의 자기 눈을 주인의 입속에 들이밀 지경이었다. 그런데 바로 그 순간 영약이 돈키호테의 위장에서 효력을 부려, 산초가 그의 입을 살펴보러 온 바로 그 찰나에, 그는 그 속에 든 것을 화승총보다 더한 힘으로 죄다 쏟아 냈으니, 그것이 고스란히 가엾은 종자의 수염에 정통으로 쏟아졌다.\n\n\"성모 마리아여!\" 산초가 외쳤다. \"이게 내게 무슨 일이람? 이 죄인 같은 양반이 입에서 피를 토하는 걸 보니 영락없이 죽을 만큼 다치셨구나.\" 그러나 그것을 조금 더 자세히 살펴보고는, 빛깔과 맛과 냄새로 보아 그것이 피가 아니라 주인이 마시는 것을 자기가 보았던 그 호리병의 영약임을 알아챘다. 그러자 어찌나 메스꺼움이 치밀어 오르던지 속이 뒤집혀, 제 뱃속의 것을 바로 그 주인에게 토해 냈고, 둘 다 그야말로 볼만한 꼴이 되고 말았다. 산초는 자기 안장주머니에서 몸을 닦고 주인을 추슬러 줄 무언가를 꺼내려고 당나귀에게로 달려갔으나, 그것을 찾지 못하자 거의 정신이 나갈 지경이 되어 다시금 제 신세를 저주했고, 마음속으로는 봉사의 품삯과 약속받은 섬에 대한 온갖 희망을 모두 잃는 한이 있더라도 주인을 떠나 집으로 돌아가리라 결심했다.\n\n이제 돈키호테가 몸을 일으켜, 이가 죄다 빠져 버리지 않도록 왼손을 입에 갖다 대고, 다른 손으로는 — 어찌나 충직하고 얌전한지 주인 곁을 한 번도 떠나지 않았던 — 로시난테의 고삐를 붙잡고는, 종자가 깊은 실의에 빠진 사람처럼 한 손으로 뺨을 괴고 당나귀에 기대 서 있는 곳으로 갔다. 그가 그런 기분에 잠겨 그토록 슬픈 표정을 짓고 있는 것을 보고 돈키호테가 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"산초, 명심하거라, 사람이란 남보다 더한 일을 하지 않는 한 다른 누구보다 나을 게 없느니라. 우리에게 들이닥치는 이 모든 폭풍은 머지않아 맑은 날씨가 오리라는, 그리고 우리에게 일이 잘 풀리리라는 징조이니라. 좋은 일이든 궂은일이든 영원히 갈 수는 없는 법이거든. 그러니 궂은일이 오래 이어졌으니 이제 좋은 일이 가까이 와 있음이 마땅하니라. 그러니 너는 나에게 닥치는 불운에 괴로워하지 말거라. 네가 그 불운에 몫이 있는 것도 아니니 말이다.\"\n\n\"제가 어찌 몫이 없습니까요?\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"어제 담요질 당한 게 혹시 제 아비의 아들이 아닌 다른 누구였답니까요? 그리고 오늘 제 온갖 재산과 함께 없어진 안장주머니가 저 말고 다른 누구의 것이었답니까요?\"\n\n\"무어라! 안장주머니가 없어졌단 말이냐, 산초?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"예, 없어졌습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 우리는 오늘 먹을 것이 없겠구나.\" 돈키호테가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그렇게 되겠습죠.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"이 풀밭에 나리께서 안다고 말씀하시는 그 풀들이, 나리처럼 불운한 편력 기사들이 그런 결핍을 메우곤 한다는 그 풀들이 없다면 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"그렇기는 해도,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"나는 지금 디오스코리데스가 적어 놓은 온갖 풀들을, 라구나 박사의 주석까지 곁들인 것을 다 준다 해도, 그보다는 빵 한 조각이나 빵 한 덩이에 정어리 대가리 두엇을 더 가지고 싶구나. 그래도 착한 산초여, 네 짐승에 올라타 나와 함께 가자꾸나. 만물을 돌보시는 하느님께서 우리를 저버리지 않으실 게다(더구나 우리처럼 그분의 일에 이토록 부지런할 때야 말할 것도 없지). 그분은 공중의 하루살이도, 땅속의 굼벵이도, 물속의 올챙이도 저버리지 않으시며, 어찌나 자비로우신지 선한 자와 악한 자 위에 똑같이 해를 떠오르게 하시고, 의로운 자와 불의한 자에게 똑같이 비를 내려 주시느니라.\"\n\n\"나리께서는 편력 기사보다 설교자가 되시는 편이 더 나으실 것 같습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"편력 기사는 모든 것을 알았고, 또 마땅히 알아야 하느니라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"옛날에는 마치 파리 대학을 졸업하기라도 한 듯, 진영 한가운데서 설교나 강론을 늘어놓을 만한 자질을 갖춘 편력 기사들이 있었느니라. 이로써 우리는 창이 결코 붓을 무디게 한 적이 없고, 붓 또한 창을 무디게 한 적이 없음을 알 수 있느니라.\"\n\n\"좋습니다요, 나리 말씀대로 하십죠.\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"이제 떠나서 밤을 지낼 만한 안식처를 찾아봅시다요. 부디 하느님께서 그곳이 담요도, 담요질하는 자들도, 유령도, 마법에 걸린 무어인도 없는 어딘가가 되게 해 주시기를. 만일 그런 게 있다면 그 일판 전부를 악마가 가져가 버리라지요.\"\n\n\"그것은 하느님께 빌거라, 아들아.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그리고 네가 원하는 곳으로 앞장서 가거라. 이번에는 우리 잠자리를 네 선택에 맡기마. 허나 여기 네 손을 좀 내밀어, 손가락으로 더듬어 이 위턱 오른쪽에서 내 이와 어금니가 몇 개나 빠졌는지 알아봐 다오. 통증이 느껴지는 게 바로 거기니까.\"\n\n산초가 손가락을 넣어 이리저리 더듬어 보며 그에게 물었다. \"이쪽에 어금니가 몇 개나 있으셨습니까요, 나리?\"\n\n\"네 개니라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"맨 뒤 어금니 말고도 말이다. 전부 온전하고 아주 튼튼했지.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀을 하시는지 잘 생각해 보십쇼, 나리.\"\n\n\"네 개라고 했다, 다섯 개가 아니라면 말이다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내 평생 이나 어금니를 뽑은 적이 없고, 충치나 진물 때문에 빠지거나 망가진 적도 없으니까.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 산초가 말했다. \"이 아래쪽에 나리께서는 어금니가 두 개 반밖에 없으시고, 위쪽에는 반쪽도 하나도 없습니다요. 죄다 제 손바닥처럼 매끈하니까요.\"\n\n\"가련한 이 신세로다!\" 종자가 전하는 그 슬픈 소식을 들으며 돈키호테가 말했다. \"차라리 검을 쥐는 팔만 아니라면 한쪽 팔을 빼앗기는 편이 나았을 것을. 산초, 너에게 이르노니, 이가 없는 입은 맷돌 없는 방앗간과 같고, 이 하나는 다이아몬드보다 훨씬 귀히 여겨야 하느니라. 허나 엄혹한 기사도의 길을 천직으로 삼은 우리는 이 모든 것을 다 겪게 마련이니라. 올라타거라, 벗이여, 그리고 앞장서거라. 네가 원하는 어떤 속도로든 내 너를 따르마.\"\n\n산초는 그가 시킨 대로 하여, 사람들이 무척 많이 다니는 큰길을 벗어나지 않으면서도 안식처를 찾을 만하다고 여겨지는 방향으로 나아갔다. 그리하여 그들이 천천히 — 돈키호테는 턱의 통증 때문에 불편하여 빨리 가고 싶은 마음이 없었으므로 — 길을 가는 동안, 산초는 무언가 이야깃거리로 주인을 즐겁게 하고 기분을 풀어 주는 것이 좋겠다고 생각했는데, 그가 그에게 한 이야기들 가운데 하나가 다음 장에서 들려줄 내용이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX.\n\n“제 생각엔 나리, 요즘 들어 우리에게 닥친 이 모든 불운은 의심할 여지 없이 나리께서 기사도의 율법을 어기신 죄에 대한 벌인 것 같습니다요. 식탁보 위에 놓인 빵을 먹지 않겠다느니 왕비를 끌어안지 않겠다느니, 그 말란드리노인지 뭔지 하는 무어인의 투구를 손에 넣기 전까지 지키겠다고 맹세하신 그 나머지 것들을 다 어기셨잖습니까. 그 이름이 정확히 기억나진 않습니다만요.”\n\n“네 말이 아주 옳다, 산초.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “사실대로 말하자면 그게 내 기억에서 빠져나가 버렸구나. 또한 담요 사건이 네게 일어난 것도 제때 그 일을 일깨워 주지 못한 네 잘못 때문이라 여겨도 좋다. 허나 내 바로잡으마. 기사도의 율법 안에는 무엇이든 보상할 방도가 있는 법이니라.”\n\n“아니! 그럼 제가 무슨 맹세라도 한 거란 말씀입니까?” 산초가 말했다.\n\n“네가 맹세를 하지 않았다는 건 문제가 되지 않는다.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “네가 그 공모에서 아주 깨끗하지는 않다는 걸 내가 보는 것으로 족하니라. 어느 쪽이든 간에 우리 스스로 대비책을 마련해 두는 것이 나쁘지는 않을 게다.”\n\n“그렇다면,” 산초가 말했다. “나리께서 그 맹세를 잊으셨던 것처럼 이번 일도 잊지 않도록 조심하십시오. 어쩌면 그 유령들이 다시 한번 저를 가지고 노는 데 재미를 붙일지도 모르고, 나리께서 그렇게 고집을 부리시는 걸 보면 나리까지도 그럴지 모릅니다요.”\n\n이런저런 이야기에 빠져 있는 동안, 두 사람은 묵을 곳을 찾거나 발견하기도 전에 길 위에서 밤을 맞고 말았다. 게다가 사정을 더욱 나쁘게 만든 것은 둘이 배고픔으로 죽을 지경이었다는 점이니, 안장주머니를 잃어버리는 바람에 식량 창고와 군량을 통째로 잃은 셈이었다. 그리고 불운을 완성이라도 하려는 듯 그들은 어떤 모험과 마주쳤는데, 그것은 꾸며 낸 구석이 전혀 없으면서도 정말로 모험 같은 모양새를 갖추고 있었다. 마침 밤이 다소 어둑하게 깔렸으나 그럼에도 그들은 앞으로 나아갔으니, 이 길이 왕의 큰길인 만큼 한두 레과 안에 어떤 주막을 찾으리라 기대해도 좋겠다고 산초가 확신했기 때문이다. 그렇게 어두운 밤길을, 종자는 배고프고 주인은 시장한 채 나아가다가, 그들은 자신들이 가던 길 위로 자기들을 향해 다가오는 수많은 불빛을 보았으니, 그것은 영락없이 움직이는 별들처럼 보였다. 산초는 그 광경에 흠칫 놀랐고, 돈키호테 역시 그것이 그리 달갑지만은 않았다. 한 사람은 고삐를 잡아 당나귀를 멈춰 세우고, 다른 한 사람은 굴레를 당겨 자기 말을 멈춰 세운 채, 이 모든 것이 무엇으로 드러날지 마음 졸이며 지켜보고 섰는데, 불빛들이 그들에게 가까이 다가올수록 점점 더 커지는 것이었다. 그 광경에 산초는 수은을 먹은 사람처럼 떨기 시작했고, 돈키호테의 머리카락은 곤두섰다. 그러나 그는 약간이나마 기운을 차리고 말했다.\n\n“산초야, 이것은 의심할 바 없이 더없이 강력하고 위험천만한 모험일 게다. 내 모든 용맹과 결단을 쏟아부어야 할 모험 말이다.”\n\n“아이고, 박복한 신세!” 산초가 대답했다. “이 모험이 제가 짐작하기 시작한 대로 유령들의 모험이라면, 그걸 견뎌 낼 갈빗대를 제가 어디서 찾는단 말입니까?”\n\n“그것들이 제아무리 유령이라 한들,” 돈키호테가 말했다. “네 옷자락 한 올도 건드리지 못하게 하리라. 지난번에 그것들이 네게 장난을 친 것은 내가 마당의 담을 뛰어넘을 수 없었기 때문이다. 허나 지금 우리는 너른 들판에 있으니, 나는 마음껏 검을 휘두를 수 있을 게다.”\n\n“그러다 지난번처럼 그것들이 나리께 마법을 걸어 꼼짝 못 하게 만들면,” 산초가 말했다. “너른 들판에 있고 없고가 무슨 차이가 있단 말입니까?”\n\n“그래도,” 돈키호테가 대답했다. “산초야, 부디 마음을 굳게 먹어라. 내가 어떤 사람인지는 겪어 보면 알게 되리라.”\n\n“그러겠습니다, 하느님이 도우신다면요.” 산초가 대답했다. 두 사람이 길 한쪽으로 물러나 이 움직이는 불빛들이 대체 무엇일지 유심히 살피기 시작하자, 머지않아 그들은 스무 명쯤 되는 흰옷 입은 자들이 모두 말을 타고 손에 불 켜진 횃불을 든 모습을 알아보게 되었다. 그 위압적인 광경은 산초의 용기를 완전히 꺼뜨려, 그는 학질에 걸려 오한이 든 사람처럼 이를 딱딱 부딪치기 시작했다. 그리고 그 뒤로 검은 천을 뒤집어쓴 가마 하나가 따라오고, 그 뒤를 또 노새 발끝까지 닿는 상복을 입은 여섯 명이 말에 탄 모습으로 뒤따르는 것을 똑똑히 알아보았을 때, 그의 가슴은 더욱 내려앉고 이는 더 심하게 딱딱거렸다. 그 짐승들이 가는 편안한 걸음새로 보아 말이 아님을 분명히 알 수 있었던 것이다. 그리고 흰옷 입은 자들은 다가오면서 낮고 구슬픈 어조로 무어라 중얼거렸다. 이런 시각에 이토록 외진 곳에서 벌어진 이 기이한 광경은 산초의, 아니 그 주인의 가슴에까지 공포를 심어 넣기에 충분했고, (돈키호테의 경우를 제외하고는) 실제로 그렇게 했으니, 산초의 결의는 이제 완전히 무너지고 말았다. 그의 주인은 정반대였으니, 그의 상상력은 즉시 이 모든 것을 자기 책 속 모험들 가운데 하나로 생생하게 떠올려 냈다.\n\n그는 그 가마가 곧 어느 중상을 입었거나 살해된 기사를 실은 상여이며, 그의 원수를 갚는 일은 오직 자기에게만 맡겨진 과업이라고 머릿속에 떠올렸다. 그리고 더 이상 따져 보지도 않은 채 창을 옆구리에 단단히 끼우고 안장에 몸을 굳게 고정하고는, 늠름한 기백과 자태로 흰옷 입은 자들이 반드시 지나갈 수밖에 없는 길 한복판에 자리를 잡았다. 그리고 그들이 가까이 다가온 것을 보자마자 목소리를 높여 말했다.\n\n“멈추어라, 기사들이여, 그대들이 누구이든 간에. 그리고 내게 고하라. 그대들이 누구이며, 어디서 와서 어디로 가는지, 저 상여 위에 무엇을 싣고 가는지를. 겉으로 보아 그대들이 어떤 잘못을 저질렀거나, 아니면 그대들에게 어떤 잘못이 저질러진 것이니, 내가 마땅히 또 반드시 알아야 할 일이로다. 그대들이 저지른 악행에 대해 그대들을 응징하기 위해서든, 아니면 그대들에게 가해진 해악에 대해 그대들의 원수를 갚아 주기 위해서든 말이다.”\n\n“우리는 급한 길이오.” 흰옷 입은 자들 가운데 하나가 대답했다. “주막은 멀고, 당신이 요구하는 그런 설명을 늘어놓느라 멈춰 설 수가 없소.” 그러고는 노새에 박차를 가하며 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n이 대답에 몹시 화가 치민 돈키호테는 노새의 굴레를 움켜잡으며 말했다. “멈추어라. 좀 더 예의를 갖추어 내가 물은 바를 고하라. 그러지 않으면 그대들 모두에게 결투를 신청하노라.”\n\n노새는 겁이 많은 짐승이라, 굴레를 잡히자 어찌나 놀랐던지 앞발을 치켜들며 등 너머로 탄 사람을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐 버렸다. 걸어서 따라오던 시종 하나가 흰옷 입은 자가 떨어지는 것을 보고 돈키호테에게 욕설을 퍼붓기 시작했다. 그러자 분노에 휩싸인 돈키호테는 더는 지체하지 않고 창을 옆구리에 끼운 채 상복 입은 자들 가운데 하나에게 돌격하여 그를 심하게 다친 채 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다. 그리고 나머지 사람들을 향해 말을 돌려 공격하고 패주시키는 그 날렵함은 가히 볼만한 광경이었으니, 마치 그 순간 로시난테에게 날개라도 돋친 듯 그토록 가뿐하고 당당하게 몸을 놀렸기 때문이다. 흰옷 입은 자들은 모두 겁 많고 무기도 없는 무리였던지라, 재빨리 난투에서 빠져나와 불 켜진 횃불을 들고 들판을 가로질러 달아나기 시작했으니, 영락없이 어느 잔칫날이나 축제의 밤에 달려가는 가장 행렬 같았다. 상복을 입은 자들 역시 치마와 옷자락에 휩싸이고 둘둘 감겨 옴짝달싹할 수가 없었으니, 돈키호테는 아무런 위험 없이 그들 모두를 두들겨 패고 마지못해 달아나게 만들었다. 그들 모두 이것이 사람이 아니라 가마에 실린 시신을 채 가려고 지옥에서 온 악마라고 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n산초는 자기 주인의 대담함에 놀라 이 모든 것을 지켜보며 속으로 중얼거렸다. “분명 우리 주인 나리는 자기가 말하는 대로 용감하고 용맹하시구나.”\n\n노새가 내동댕이친 첫 번째 사람 곁에는 불타는 횃불 하나가 땅에 놓여 있었고, 그 불빛 덕에 돈키호테는 그를 알아보았다. 그에게 다가가 창끝을 그의 얼굴에 들이대며 항복하여 포로가 되라고, 그러지 않으면 죽이겠다고 외쳤다. 이에 땅에 엎어진 사람이 대답했다. “저는 이미 충분히 포로 신세입니다. 다리 하나가 부러져 꼼짝할 수가 없습니다. 청하건대, 당신이 기독교를 믿는 신사라면 저를 죽이지 마십시오. 그것은 중대한 신성 모독을 저지르는 일이 될 것입니다. 저는 학사이며 하급 성직을 받은 몸이니까요.”\n\n“그럼 성직자인 당신이 대체 무슨 빌어먹을 일로 여기까지 온 것이오?” 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n“무슨 일이냐고요, 나리?” 그가 말했다. “제 박복한 운수 때문이지요.”\n\n“그렇다면 더욱 나쁜 일이 당신을 기다리고 있을 것이오.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “내가 처음에 물은 모든 것에 대해 나를 만족시키지 못한다면 말이오.”\n\n“곧 만족하시게 될 겁니다.” 학사가 말했다. “그러니까, 방금 제가 학사라고 말씀드렸지만 사실은 학사 후보생일 뿐이고, 이름은 알론소 로페스입니다. 저는 알코벤다스 태생이고, 다른 열한 사람의 사제들과 함께 바에사 시에서 오는 길입니다. 횃불을 들고 도망친 바로 그 사람들이지요. 저희는 저 가마에 실린 시신을 모시고 세고비아 시로 가는 길인데, 그 시신은 바에사에서 죽어 그곳에 안장되었던 한 신사의 것입니다. 그래서 지금, 말씀드린 대로 저희는 그분의 유골을 그분이 태어나신 세고비아의 매장지로 모셔 가는 중입니다.”\n\n“그럼 누가 그를 죽였소?” 돈키호테가 물었다.\n\n“하느님께서지요. 그를 덮친 악성 열병을 통해서 말입니다.” 학사 후보생이 대답했다.\n\n“그렇다면,” 돈키호테가 말했다. “다른 누가 그를 죽였다면 그 죽음의 원수를 갚는 일이 내 몫이었겠으나, 주님께서 나를 그 과업에서 면해 주셨구려. 허나 그를 죽인 분이 그를 죽이셨으니, 입을 다물고 어깨를 으쓱하는 것 외에는 도리가 없겠소. 설령 그분이 나 자신을 죽이신다 해도 나는 똑같이 할 것이오. 그리고 신부께서 알아 두시기를, 나는 라만차의 기사로 돈키호테라는 이름을 가졌으며, 세상을 떠돌며 그릇된 일을 바로잡고 부당함을 시정하는 것이 나의 업이자 소명이오.”\n\n“그릇된 일을 바로잡는다는 게 어떻게 가능한지 저는 모르겠습니다.” 학사 후보생이 말했다. “당신은 멀쩡한 저를 비뚤어지게 만들어, 평생토록 두 번 다시 똑바로 서지 못할 부러진 다리를 남겨 주셨으니까요. 그리고 제 경우에 당신이 시정해 주신 부당함이란 게, 영영 부당한 처지로 남게끔 부당하게 만들어 놓으신 것입니다. 모험을 찾아 떠도는 당신과 마주친 것이야말로 더없는 불운이었습니다.”\n\n“모든 일이 다 똑같은 식으로 일어나는 것은 아니오.” 돈키호테가 대답했다. “알론소 로페스 학사 후보생 양반, 이 모든 것은 당신이 그렇게 밤중에, 그런 중백의를 걸치고, 불 켜진 횃불을 들고, 기도를 올리며, 상복에 휩싸여 다닌 탓이오. 그러니 당신은 자연히 무언가 사악한 것, 저세상의 것처럼 보였단 말이오. 그래서 나는 당신들을 공격하는 내 의무를 피할 수 없었던 것이고, 설령 당신들이 지옥의 바로 그 악마라는 것을 확실히 알았다 해도 나는 당신들을 공격했을 것이오. 사실 나는 분명 당신들을 그런 것이라 믿고 그렇게 여겼으니 말이오.”\n\n“제 운명이 그렇게 정해 놓은 것이니,” 학사 후보생이 말했다. “편력 기사님, 당신의 행차가 제게 이토록 사악한 것이 되고 말았습니다만, 부디 등자와 안장 사이에 제 다리 하나를 물고 있는 이 노새 밑에서 저를 빼내 주시기를 청합니다.”\n\n“나는 내일까지도 계속 이야기를 늘어놓을 뻔했구려.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “당신의 곤경을 내게 말해 주기까지 대체 얼마나 기다릴 작정이었소?”\n\n그는 곧바로 산초를 불렀으나, 산초는 올 생각이 없었다. 마침 그때 그 점잖은 신사들이 가지고 온, 먹을거리가 잔뜩 실린 짐 노새 한 마리의 짐을 내리느라 바빴기 때문이다. 산초는 자기 외투로 자루를 만들어 담을 수 있는 만큼, 자루에 들어갈 만큼 잔뜩 그러모아 자기 짐승에 실은 다음, 서둘러 주인의 부름에 응하여 학사 후보생을 노새 밑에서 빼내는 일을 거들었다. 그러고는 그를 노새 등에 올려 횃불을 건네주었고, 돈키호테는 그에게 일행의 자취를 따라가, 자신이 어쩔 수 없이 그들에게 끼친 잘못에 대해 자기를 대신해 용서를 빌라고 일렀다.\n\n그러자 산초가 말했다. “혹시라도 저분들이 자기들을 그렇게 혼내 준 영웅이 누구인지 알고 싶어 한다면, 그분은 라만차의 유명한 돈키호테, 일명 슬픈 몰골의 기사라고 나리께서 일러 주셔도 좋겠습니다.”\n\n그러고 나서 학사 후보생은 길을 떠났다.\n\n그가 떠나기 전에 돈키호테에게 이렇게 말했다는 것을 깜빡하고 빠뜨렸다. “당신은 성스러운 것에 폭력의 손을 댔으니 파문당한 몸임을 명심하시오. 그 율법에 이르기를, '누구든 악마의 꾐에 빠져' 운운하였소.”\n\n* 그 라틴어 원문은 juxta illud, si quis, suadente diabolo로, 교회법에서 성직자에게 폭력을 가한 자를 파문한다는 조항의 첫머리이다.\n“나는 그 라틴어를 알아듣지 못하오.” 돈키호테가 대답했다. “허나 내가 손을 댄 것이 아니라 오직 이 창을 댔다는 것만은 잘 알고 있소. 게다가 나는 내가 사제들이나 교회의 것들에게 공격을 가하고 있다고는 생각지 않았소. 나는 가톨릭교도이자 충실한 기독교도로서 그런 것들을 존경하고 숭상하니 말이오. 다만 저세상의 유령과 망령들에게 공격을 가하는 것이라 여겼을 뿐이오. 허나 설령 그렇다 해도, 나는 시드 루이 디아스가 교황 성하 앞에서 어느 왕의 사절이 앉은 의자를 부수었을 때 어떤 일을 겪었는지를 떠올리오. 교황은 그 일로 그를 파문하였으나, 그럼에도 비바르의 선량한 로드리고는 그날 더없이 고귀하고 용맹한 기사답게 처신하였소.”\n\n이 말을 듣고 학사 후보생은, 앞서 말한 대로 아무런 대꾸도 없이 길을 떠났다. 그러자 돈키호테는 산초에게, 어찌하여 다른 어느 때도 아닌 지금 자기를 '슬픈 몰골의 기사'라 불렀는지 물었다.\n\n“말씀드리지요.” 산초가 대답했다. “그건 제가 한동안 저 불쌍한 사람이 든 횃불 빛으로 나리를 바라보았기 때문입니다. 정말이지 나리께서는 요즘 들어 제가 여태껏 본 중에 가장 볼품없는 몰골이 되셨거든요. 이 결투의 피로 탓이거나, 아니면 이빨과 어금니가 빠진 탓일 겁니다.”\n\n“그게 아니다.” 돈키호테가 대답했다. “내 위업의 역사를 기록할 임무를 맡은 현자가, 옛 기사들이 모두 그러했듯 내가 어떤 독특한 이름을 가지는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼기 때문이다. 한 사람은 '불타는 검의 기사', 또 한 사람은 '일각수의 기사', 이 사람은 '처녀들의 기사', 저 사람은 '불사조의 기사', 또 한 사람은 '그리핀의 기사', 또 다른 이는 '죽음의 기사'였으니, 이런 이름과 칭호로 그들은 온 세상에 알려졌느니라. 그러니 내 말은, 방금 그 현자가 나를 '슬픈 몰골의 기사'라 부르도록 네 입과 마음에 그것을 불어넣었다는 것이며, 나는 오늘부터 나 자신을 그렇게 부를 작정이다. 그리고 그 이름이 내게 더 잘 어울리도록, 기회가 닿는 대로 내 방패에 더없이 슬픈 몰골 하나를 그려 넣게 할 생각이다.”\n\n“나리, 그런 몰골을 그리느라 시간이나 돈을 허비하실 필요가 없습니다.” 산초가 말했다. “나리께서 그저 나리 얼굴을 보는 사람들에게 직접, 얼굴을 맞대고 보여 주시기만 하면 됩니다요. 그러면 그림이든 방패든 다른 아무것도 없이도 사람들이 나리를 '슬픈 몰골의 기사'라 부를 테니까요. 제 말을 믿으십시오, 제가 진실을 말씀드리는 겁니다. 분명히 말씀드리지만 나리(좋은 뜻으로 드리는 말씀입니다요), 배고픔과 어금니가 빠진 탓에 나리 얼굴이 어찌나 볼품없어지셨는지, 제가 말씀드린 대로 그 슬픈 그림은 충분히 생략하셔도 좋겠습니다.”\n\n돈키호테는 산초의 농담에 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그럼에도 그는 자신을 그 이름으로 부르기로, 그리고 자기가 궁리한 대로 방패나 작은 방패에 그림을 그려 넣기로 결심했다.\n\n돈키호테는 가마에 실린 것이 유골인지 아닌지 살펴보려 했으나, 산초가 한사코 말렸다.\n\n“나리, 나리께서는 제가 본 어떤 모험보다도 이 위험천만한 모험을 나리 자신에게 더 안전하게 마무리하셨습니다. 어쩌면 저 사람들은 두들겨 맞고 패주했으면서도, 자기들을 때려눕힌 게 단 한 사람이라는 데 생각이 미쳐, 분하고 부끄러워 다시 기운을 차리고 우리를 찾아와 단단히 곤란을 끼칠지도 모릅니다. 당나귀는 만반의 채비가 되어 있고, 산은 가까이 있으며, 배고픔은 다그치니, 우리가 할 일이라곤 무사히 물러나는 것밖에 없습니다. 속담에도 있듯이, 죽은 자는 무덤으로, 산 자는 빵으로 가는 법이지요.”\n\n그러고는 당나귀를 앞세워 몰며 주인에게 따라오기를 청했다. 돈키호테는 산초의 말이 옳다고 여겨 아무 대꾸 없이 그를 따랐다. 두 언덕 사이로 얼마쯤 나아간 끝에 그들은 너르고 외진 골짜기에 이르렀고, 거기서 말과 당나귀에서 내렸다. 산초가 짐승의 짐을 내리자, 둘은 배고픔을 양념 삼아 푸른 풀밭에 드러누워 아침과 점심과 새참과 저녁을 한꺼번에 먹어 치웠으니, 죽은 자의 성직자 신사들이(이들은 좀처럼 인색하게 끼니를 줄이는 법이 없었다) 짐 노새에 싣고 온 한 무더기 넘는 식은 고기로 배를 채웠다. 그러나 그들에게 또 다른 불운이 닥쳤으니, 산초가 그 모든 것 중에 가장 고약하다고 여긴 그것은 바로 마실 포도주가 없을뿐더러 입술을 적실 물조차 없다는 것이었다. 갈증이 그들을 괴롭히자, 산초는 자기들이 있는 풀밭에 푸르고 연한 풀이 가득한 것을 보고는, 다음 장에서 이야기될 말을 하였다.\n\n# CHAPTER XX.\n\n\"나리, 이 풀이 자라난 걸 보면 틀림없이 근처에 물기를 대 주는 샘이나 시냇물이 있다는 증거가 아니겠습니까요. 그러니 좀 더 멀리 가 보는 것이 좋겠습니다요. 우리를 괴롭히는 이 지독한 갈증을 풀 만한 곳을 찾을 수 있을 테니까요. 갈증은 분명 굶주림보다 더 견디기 힘든 것 아니겠습니까요.\"\n\n그 충고가 돈키호테에게는 그럴듯하게 여겨졌고, 그래서 그는 로시난테의 고삐를 잡고 산초는 저녁 식사의 남은 것들을 잔뜩 실은 당나귀의 굴레를 끌면서, 손으로 더듬어 가며 풀밭을 나아갔다. 밤이 어두워 아무것도 분간할 수 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 이백 보도 채 가지 않았을 때, 마치 커다란 바위에서 떨어지는 듯한 요란한 물소리가 그들의 귀를 때렸다. 그 소리에 두 사람은 크게 기운을 얻었다. 그러나 어느 쪽에서 들려오는지 귀 기울여 가늠하려고 걸음을 멈추었을 때, 때마침 또 다른 소리가 들려와 물소리가 안겨 준 만족감을 망쳐 버렸으니, 특히 천성이 겁 많고 소심한 산초에게는 더욱 그러했다. 다시 말하건대 그들은 일정한 박자로 떨어지는 타격 소리와, 쇠붙이와 사슬이 부딪치는 듯한 어떤 절그럭거리는 소리를 들었는데, 이는 사납게 휘몰아치는 물소리와 어우러져 돈키호테가 아닌 그 누구의 가슴에라도 공포를 심어 놓을 만한 것이었다. 앞서 말했듯이 밤은 어두웠고, 그들은 마침 키 큰 나무들 사이의 어느 지점에 이르러 있었는데, 그 잎사귀들이 부드러운 산들바람에 흔들리며 나직하고 불길한 소리를 내고 있었다. 그리하여 적막함이며 장소며 어둠이며 물소리며 잎사귀의 바스락거림이며, 그 모든 것이 경외감과 두려움을 불러일으켰다. 더욱이 타격 소리가 멎지 않고, 바람도 잦아들지 않으며, 아침도 다가오지 않음을 알아차렸을 때는 더더욱 그러했다. 게다가 자기들이 어디에 있는지조차 모른다는 사실까지 더해질 수 있었다.\n\n그러나 돈키호테는 그 담대한 가슴에 힘입어 로시난테 위로 뛰어올라, 방패를 팔에 단단히 끼고 창을 비스듬히 겨누며 말했다. \"산초여, 알아 두어라. 나는 하늘의 뜻으로 이 무쇠의 시대에 태어나, 그 안에서 황금의 시대를, 흔히 말하는 그 황금기를 되살리려 하노라. 위험과 위대한 업적과 용맹한 행적이 마련되어 있는 자가 바로 나이니라. 다시 말하노니, 원탁의 기사들과 프랑스의 열두 기사와 아홉 위인을 되살릴 자가 바로 나이며, 플라티르며 타블란테며 올리반테며 티란테며 페보며 벨리아니스며, 지난날 이름 높던 편력 기사의 무리 전부를 망각 속으로 보내 버릴 자가 바로 나이니라. 내가 사는 이 시대에 그들의 가장 찬란한 행적조차 무색하게 만들 위업과 경이와 무용을 펼치면서 말이다. 충실하고 미더운 종자여, 그대는 이 밤의 음울함과 그 기이한 정적, 저 나무들의 둔탁하고 어수선한 웅성거림, 우리가 찾아 나선 저 물의 무시무시한 소리를 잘 새겨 두라. 그 소리는 마치 달의 높은 산봉우리에서 곤두박질치며 쏟아져 내리는 듯하구나. 그리고 우리 귀를 찌르고 아프게 하는 저 끊임없는 망치질도 마찬가지이니, 이 모든 것이 함께든 따로든 군신 마르스 자신의 가슴에라도 두려움과 공포와 경악을 심어 넣기에 충분하거늘, 하물며 이런 위험과 모험에 익숙지 못한 자에게야 오죽하겠느냐. 그런데 내가 그대 앞에 늘어놓은 이 모든 것이 내 영혼에는 오히려 자극이요 격려가 되어, 험난하기 그지없으리라 짐작되는 이 모험에 뛰어들고 싶은 열망으로 가슴이 터질 듯하구나. 그러니 로시난테의 뱃대끈을 조금 조여 다오. 그리고 신이 그대와 함께하시기를. 여기서 사흘만, 그 이상은 말고 나를 기다려라. 그동안 내가 돌아오지 않거든 그대는 우리 마을로 돌아가도 좋으니, 그곳에서 나를 위해 호의이자 봉사로 엘 토보소로 가서, 비할 데 없는 나의 귀부인 둘시네아께 그분의 포로된 기사가 그분의 것이라 불릴 만한 자격을 얻고자 시도하다가 죽었노라 전해 다오.\"\n\n산초는 주인의 말을 듣고 더없이 애처롭게 울며 말하기 시작했다.\n\n\"나리, 어찌하여 나리께서는 이토록 끔찍한 모험을 해 보려 하시는지 저는 도무지 모르겠습니다요. 지금은 밤이라 여기서 우리를 보는 사람도 없으니, 앞으로 사흘 동안 물을 마시지 못한다 하더라도 손쉽게 발길을 돌려 위험에서 빠져나갈 수 있지 않습니까요. 우리를 보는 이가 아무도 없으니 우리를 겁쟁이라 흉볼 사람은 더더욱 없을 테고요. 게다가 나리께서도 잘 아시는 우리 마을 신부님께서, 위험을 찾는 자는 그 안에서 죽는다고 설교하시는 걸 저는 여러 번 들었습니다요. 그러니 기적 말고는 빠져나올 길이 없는 이런 엄청난 일을 시도해서 하느님을 시험하는 것은 옳지 않습니다요. 하느님께서는 이미 나리를 위해 충분히 기적을 행하셨지 않습니까요. 저처럼 담요에 키질당하지 않도록 나리를 구해 주셨고, 그 시체와 함께 있던 그 모든 적들 가운데서 나리를 승리자로, 안전하고 멀쩡하게 빼내 주셨으니까요. 그래도 이 모든 것이 그 단단한 마음을 움직이거나 누그러뜨리지 못한다면, 부디 이 생각이라도 그 마음을 움직이게 하십시오. 나리께서 이 자리를 떠나시기가 무섭게, 저는 순전한 두려움에 제 영혼을 가져갈 자 누구에게든 내어 줄 것이라는 생각 말입니다요. 저는 더 잘되어 보려고, 결코 더 못되려고가 아니라 나리를 모시려고 집과 처자식을 버리고 떠나왔습니다요. 그러나 욕심이 자루를 터뜨린다더니, 그 욕심이 제 희망을 갈가리 찢어 놓았습니다요. 나리께서 그토록 자주 약속하신 그 비참하고 운 없는 섬을 얻으리라는 기대가 한껏 부풀어 올랐던 바로 그때, 그 대신, 그것 대신에 나리께서는 사람의 손길이 미치지 못하는 이런 외딴곳에 저를 버리려 하시지 않습니까요. 제발, 나리, 저에게 이토록 부당하게 대하지 마십시오. 정 이 일을 시도하기를 완전히 그만두지 못하시겠거든, 적어도 아침까지만이라도 미루십시오. 제가 양치기 시절에 배운 지식에 따르면 이제 동틀 때까지 세 시간도 채 남지 않았으니까요. 작은곰자리의 입이 머리 위에 떠 있고 왼팔 쪽 선에서 한밤중을 가리키고 있거든요.\"\n\n\"산초야,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"온 하늘에 별 하나 보이지 않을 만큼 밤이 어두운데, 그것이 어디서 그 선을 긋는지, 또 네가 말하는 그 입이며 뒤통수가 어디 있는지 어떻게 볼 수 있단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"그건 그렇습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하지만 두려움은 눈이 밝아서 땅속도 보거늘, 하물며 하늘 위야 더 잘 보지요. 게다가 이제 날이 밝을 때가 얼마 남지 않았음을 보여 줄 그럴듯한 까닭도 있고요.\"\n\n\"얼마가 남았든 상관없다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"지금이든 언제든, 눈물이나 간청 때문에 내가 기사도의 관습에 맞는 일을 하기를 그만두었다는 말을 듣게 되지는 않을 것이다. 그러니 산초여, 청하노니 입을 다물라. 이토록 유례없고 무시무시한 모험을 이제 떠맡으라고 내 마음에 그것을 넣어 주신 하느님께서, 내 안전을 살피시고 그대의 슬픔을 달래 주시리라. 그대가 해야 할 일은 로시난테의 뱃대끈을 잘 조이고 여기서 기다리는 것뿐이니, 나는 살아서든 죽어서든 곧 돌아올 것이다.\"\n\n산초는 주인의 결심이 확고하며, 자기 눈물과 충고와 간청이 그에게 거의 통하지 않음을 알아차리고는, 자기 나름의 꾀에 기대어 어떻게든 그를 날이 밝을 때까지 기다리게 만들기로 작정했다. 그리하여 말의 뱃대끈을 조이는 척하면서, 슬그머니 들키지 않게 당나귀의 굴레로 로시난테의 두 다리를 묶어 버렸다. 그래서 돈키호테가 가려고 애를 써도 갈 수가 없었으니, 말은 그저 껑충껑충 뛰는 것밖에 할 수 없었다. 자기 꾀가 들어맞은 것을 보고 산초 판사가 말했다.\n\n\"보십시오, 나리! 제 눈물과 기도에 마음이 움직이신 하늘이, 로시난테가 꼼짝 못하도록 그렇게 정해 놓으셨습니다요. 그러니 고집을 부려 박차를 가하고 때리신다면, 그것은 운명을 거스르는 것이요, 흔히 말하듯 가시를 향해 발길질하는 격이 될 뿐입니다요.\"\n\n이에 돈키호테는 절망에 빠졌으나, 말에 발뒤꿈치를 박으면 박을수록 말은 더더욱 움직이지 않았다. 다리가 묶인 것을 전혀 의심하지 못한 그는, 날이 밝거나 로시난테가 움직일 수 있게 될 때까지 체념하고 기다리는 수밖에 없었으니, 이 모든 것이 산초의 꾀가 아닌 다른 무언가에서 비롯되었다고 굳게 믿었던 것이다. 그래서 그가 산초에게 말했다. \"사정이 이러하고 로시난테가 움직일 수 없으니, 산초여, 나는 새벽이 우리에게 미소 지을 때까지 기다리는 것으로 만족하겠다. 비록 그것이 오기를 더디 끄는 동안 내가 눈물을 흘릴지라도 말이다.\"\n\n\"우실 필요 없습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"지금부터 날이 밝을 때까지 제가 이야기를 들려드려 나리를 즐겁게 해 드릴 테니까요. 정 그러시다면 편력 기사들의 풍습대로 말에서 내려 푸른 풀밭 위에 누워 잠을 좀 청하시는 것도 좋겠습니다요. 그래야 날이 밝고 나리께서 그토록 고대하시는 이 비범한 모험을 시도할 순간이 왔을 때 더 생기 있으실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"내려서 자라니, 그게 무슨 소리냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 위험 앞에서 휴식을 취하는 그런 기사들 가운데 하나라고 너는 생각하느냐? 잠은 자도록 태어난 너나 자거라. 아니면 네 좋을 대로 하든지. 나는 내 본분에 가장 어울린다고 여기는 대로 할 것이니.\"\n\n\"화내지 마십시오, 나리.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"그런 뜻으로 드린 말씀이 아닙니다요.\" 그러고는 주인 곁으로 바짝 다가가 한 손은 안장 앞머리에, 다른 한 손은 안장 뒷머리에 얹어, 주인의 왼쪽 허벅지를 끌어안듯 붙들고는 감히 손가락 한 마디만큼도 떨어지려 하지 않았다. 여전히 규칙적인 박자로 울려 퍼지는 그 타격 소리가 그토록 두려웠던 것이다. 돈키호테가 아까 말한 대로 자기를 즐겁게 해 줄 이야기를 하라고 하자, 산초는 자기가 듣고 있는 소리에 대한 두려움이 허락한다면 그러겠노라 대답하고는 말했다. \"하지만 이야기 하나를 들려드리려 애써 보겠습니다요. 제가 제대로 풀어낼 수만 있고 아무도 그 이야기에 끼어들지만 않는다면, 그것은 둘도 없이 훌륭한 이야기입니다요. 그러니 나리께서는 귀를 기울여 주십시오. 자, 시작합니다요. 있던 일은 있던 일이고, 앞으로 올 좋은 일은 모두에게, 나쁜 일은 그것을 찾아 나서는 자에게 가기를……. 나리께서도 아셔야 할 것이, 옛 어른들이 이야기를 시작할 때 붙이던 그 말은 그저 제멋대로 갖다 붙인 게 아니었습니다요. '나쁜 일은 그것을 찾아 나서는 자에게'라고 한 그 말은 로마 사람 카토 손소리노의 격언인데, 지금 이 경우에 손가락에 반지가 꼭 맞듯 딱 들어맞는 말입니다요. 곧 나리께서는 가만히 계셔야 하고 어느 쪽으로도 나쁜 일을 찾아 나서지 말아야 하며, 아무도 우리에게 그토록 많은 공포가 우리를 떨게 하는 이 길을 가라고 강요하지 않으니 우리는 다른 길로 돌아가야 한다는 뜻이지요.\"\n\n\"네 이야기나 계속해라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"어느 길로 갈지는 내가 알아서 할 일이니.\"\n\n\"그럼 말씀드리지요.\" 산초가 말을 이었다. \"에스트레마두라의 어느 마을에 염소 치는 목동이, 그러니까 염소를 돌보는 자가 하나 있었습니다요. 제 이야기에 따르면 이 목동, 곧 염소지기는 로페 루이스라 불렸는데, 이 로페 루이스는 토랄바라는 양치기 처녀를 사랑하고 있었습죠. 토랄바라는 이 양치기 처녀는 어느 부유한 목축업자의 딸이었는데, 이 부유한 목축업자가——\"\n\n\"산초야, 네가 그런 식으로, 할 말마다 두 번씩 되풀이하며 이야기를 한다면,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이틀이 지나도 끝내지 못할 것이다. 곧장 이야기하라. 분별 있는 사람답게 들려주든지, 아니면 아예 입을 다물든지 해라.\"\n\n\"제 고장에서는 이야기를 늘 제가 지금 하는 바로 이런 식으로 합니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"저는 다른 식으로는 할 줄을 모르고, 새로운 풍습을 만들라고 하시는 것은 나리께서 옳지 못한 일입니다요.\"\n\n\"네 마음대로 해라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"운명이 그러하여 내가 너를 듣지 않을 수 없으니, 계속하라.\"\n\n\"그래서 말입니다요, 제 영혼의 주인이시여,\" 산초가 말을 이었다. \"제가 말씀드린 대로 이 목동은 양치기 처녀 토랄바를 사랑하고 있었습죠. 그 처녀는 사내 같은 데가 좀 있는 거칠고 통통한 아가씨였는데, 자그마한 콧수염까지 나 있었지요. 지금도 눈에 선합니다요.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네가 그 여자를 알았단 말인가?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 그 여자를 안 건 아닙니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하지만 제게 이 이야기를 들려준 사람이, 이것이 너무도 진실하고 확실하니 제가 남에게 이 이야기를 들려줄 때 마치 제 눈으로 다 본 것처럼 마음 놓고 단언하고 맹세해도 좋다고 했습죠. 그리하여 세월이 흐르는 동안, 결코 잠들지 않으며 만사를 어지럽히는 악마가 농간을 부려, 목동이 양치기 처녀에게 품었던 사랑이 미움과 악감정으로 변해 버렸습니다요. 험담꾼들 말에 따르면 그 까닭은, 그 처녀가 도를 넘어 금지된 영역을 침범하여 그에게 약간의 질투를 불러일으킨 데 있었다지요. 그때부터 목동이 그 처녀를 얼마나 미워했던지, 그 여자에게서 벗어나려고 고향을 떠나 다시는 그 여자를 두 번 다시 보지 않을 곳으로 가기로 작정했습니다요. 토랄바는 로페에게 버림받았음을 알게 되자, 전에는 한 번도 그를 사랑한 적이 없었으면서 곧바로 그에 대한 사랑에 사로잡혔습죠.\"\n\n\"그것이 여자들의 타고난 방식이지.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"자기를 사랑하는 자는 멸시하고, 자기를 미워하는 자는 사랑하니 말이다. 계속하라, 산초.\"\n\n\"일이 이렇게 되었습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"목동이 자기 뜻을 실행에 옮겨, 염소들을 앞세워 몰고 포르투갈 왕국으로 넘어가려고 에스트레마두라 평원을 가로질러 길을 떠났습죠. 그 사실을 알게 된 토랄바는 그의 뒤를 쫓아, 맨발로 걸어서 멀찍이 그를 따라갔는데, 손에는 순례자의 지팡이를 들고 목에는 바랑을 둘렀습니다요. 그 안에는 거울 조각 하나와 빗 조각 하나, 그리고 얼굴에 바를 무슨 화장품 단지 같은 것을 넣어 가지고 다녔다지요. 하지만 그 여자가 무엇을 가지고 다녔든, 저는 그걸 증명하려고 애쓸 생각은 없습니다요. 제가 말씀드리려는 건 다만, 사람들 말로 그 목동이 가축 떼를 몰고 과디아나강을 건너러 왔는데, 그 강은 그때 물이 불어나 둑을 거의 넘칠 지경이었다는 겁니다요. 그가 다다른 그 자리에는 나룻배도 배도, 그와 그의 가축 떼를 건너편으로 실어다 줄 사람도 없었으니, 그는 몹시 속이 상했습죠. 토랄바가 다가오고 있으며 눈물과 간청으로 자기를 몹시 성가시게 하리라는 것을 알아차렸기 때문입니다요. 하지만 그는 어찌나 샅샅이 살폈는지, 마침 곁에 배를 댄 어부 한 사람을 찾아냈습니다요. 그 배는 어찌나 작은지 사람 하나에 염소 한 마리밖에 태울 수 없었지요. 그래도 그는 어부에게 말을 걸어, 자기와 자기 염소 삼백 마리를 건너편으로 실어다 주기로 합의를 보았습니다요. 어부는 배에 올라 염소 한 마리를 건너편으로 실어다 주었고, 돌아와서 또 한 마리를 실어다 주었고, 다시 돌아와서 또 한 마리를 실어다 주었습죠——나리, 어부가 건네는 염소 수를 잘 세고 계셔야 합니다요. 한 마리라도 기억에서 빠져나가면 이야기가 끝나 버려서 더 이상 한마디도 이어 갈 수 없게 되니까요. 계속하자면, 건너편 나루터가 진흙투성이에 미끄러워서 어부가 오가는 데 시간을 무척 많이 허비했다는 말씀을 드려야겠습니다요. 그래도 그는 또 한 마리를, 또 한 마리를, 또 한 마리를 실으러 돌아왔지요.\"\n\n\"염소를 전부 다 건넸다고 치자.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그렇게 오락가락하지 마라. 안 그러면 일 년이 지나도 다 건너게 하지 못할 테니.\"\n\n\"지금까지 몇 마리나 건넜습니까요?\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 그걸 어찌 안단 말이냐?\" 돈키호테가 대답했다.\n\n\"바로 그겁니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"제가 잘 세고 계셔야 한다고 말씀드리지 않았습니까요. 그러니 정말이지, 이야기는 끝났습니다요. 더는 이어 갈 수가 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"어찌 그럴 수가 있느냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"건너간 염소 수를 정확히 아는 것이 이야기에 그토록 긴요하단 말이냐? 셈에 한 마리라도 틀리면 이야기를 이어 갈 수 없다는 게냐?\"\n\n\"아닙니다요, 나리, 천만에요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제가 나리께 염소가 몇 마리나 건넜는지 여쭈었을 때 나리께서 모른다고 대답하시는 바로 그 순간, 제가 할 말이 죄다 머릿속에서 사라져 버렸습니다요. 정말이지, 그 안에는 미덕도 많고 재미도 많았는데요.\"\n\n\"그러면,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이야기가 끝나 버렸단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"제 어머니가 끝나신 것만큼이나 끝났습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"참으로,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"너는 이 세상 누구도 상상해 낼 수 없을 만큼 진기한 이야기, 옛날이야기, 혹은 사화(史話) 가운데 하나를 들려주었구나. 그리고 그것을 풀어내고 끝맺는 그런 방식은 평생토록 본 적도 없고 앞으로도 보지 못할 것이다. 하기야 너의 뛰어난 분별력에서 다른 것을 기대하지도 않았다만. 그래도 놀랍지는 않구나. 어쩌면 저 끊임없는 타격 소리가 네 정신을 어지럽혔는지도 모르니.\"\n\n\"다 그럴지도 모르지요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"하지만 제 이야기로 말하자면, 염소 건너는 셈의 착오가 시작되는 바로 거기서 이야기도 끝난다는 것밖에는 더 할 말이 없다는 걸 저는 압니다요.\"\n\n\"어디서 끝나든 좋다, 그만하면 됐다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그럼 로시난테가 갈 수 있는지 보자.\" 그러고는 다시 박차를 가했으나, 로시난테는 또 껑충 뛸 뿐 제자리에 머물러 있었으니, 그만큼 단단히 묶여 있었던 것이다.\n\n바로 그때, 이제 다가오는 아침의 추위 때문인지, 저녁 식사 때 무언가 배 속을 무르게 하는 것을 먹었기 때문인지, 아니면 (가장 그럴듯하게는) 그저 자연스러운 생리 현상 때문인지, 산초는 아무도 자기를 대신해 줄 수 없는 일을 하고 싶은 욕구를 느꼈다. 그러나 그의 가슴에 스며든 두려움이 어찌나 컸던지, 그는 감히 손톱 끝의 검은 때만큼도 주인에게서 떨어지려 하지 않았다. 그렇다고 하고 싶은 일을 안 하고 넘어가는 것 또한 불가능했다. 그래서 평온을 위해 그가 한 일은, 안장 뒷머리를 붙들고 있던 오른손을 떼어, 그것으로 자기 바지를 받쳐 주던 단 하나의 졸라맨 끈을 슬그머니 소리 없이 푸는 것이었다. 그러자 끈을 풀기가 무섭게 바지가 곧장 차꼬처럼 발치로 흘러내렸다. 이어 그는 셔츠를 할 수 있는 한 걷어 올려, 결코 빈약하지 않은 엉덩이를 드러냈다. 그러나 이 끔찍한 곤경과 난처함에서 벗어나려면 이것이 할 일의 전부라고 여겼던 그 일을 마치고 나자, 더욱 큰 난관이 또 하나 닥쳐왔다. 소리를 내지 않고서는 도무지 볼일을 볼 수 없을 것 같았던 것이다. 그래서 그는 이를 악물고 어깨를 잔뜩 움츠리며 숨을 최대한 참았다. 그러나 이런 조심에도 불구하고, 결국 그는 운 나쁘게도 작은 소리를 내고야 말았으니, 그를 그토록 두려움에 떨게 하던 그 소리와는 사뭇 다른 소리였다.\n\n그 소리를 들은 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그게 무슨 소리냐, 산초?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다요, 나리.\" 산초가 말했다. \"무언가 새로운 일이 틀림없습니다요. 모험이든 불행이든 결코 사소한 것으로 시작되는 법은 없으니까요.\" 그는 다시 한번 운을 시험해 보았는데, 어찌나 잘되었던지 더 이상의 소리나 소란 없이, 그를 그토록 거북하게 만들던 짐을 덜어 낼 수 있었다. 그러나 돈키호테의 후각은 청각만큼이나 예민했고, 산초가 그와 어찌나 바짝 붙어 있었던지 그 냄새가 거의 일직선으로 솟아올랐으니, 그 일부가 그의 코에 닿지 않을 수 없었다. 냄새가 닿기가 무섭게 그는 손가락으로 코를 틀어쥐어 코를 구해 주고는, 다소 코맹맹이 소리로 말했다. \"산초야, 보아하니 네가 몹시 두려운 모양이로구나.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"하지만 나리께서는 어찌하여 그걸 어느 때보다 지금 더 잘 아십니까요?\"\n\n\"방금 네가 그 어느 때보다 더 독한 냄새를 풍기는데, 용연향 냄새는 아니기 때문이다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럴 만도 합니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하지만 그건 제 잘못이 아니라 나리 잘못입니다요. 이런 때아닌 시각에, 이런 익숙지 않은 걸음으로 저를 끌고 다니시니 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"그러면 서너 걸음 물러나거라, 이 사람아.\" 돈키호테가 줄곧 코에 손가락을 댄 채 말했다. \"그리고 앞으로는 네 몸가짐과, 나에 대해 네가 지켜야 할 도리에 좀 더 신경을 쓰거라. 너에게 너무 허물없이 대한 탓에 이런 무례가 생겨난 것이니.\"\n\n\"내기를 걸어도 좋습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"나리께서는 제가 제 몸으로 하지 말아야 할 무슨 일을 했다고 여기시는 거지요.\"\n\n\"그것을 들쑤시면 더 나빠질 뿐이니라, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 대꾸했다.\n\n이런저런 같은 종류의 이야기를 나누며 주인과 하인은 밤을 지새웠다. 그러다 산초는 새벽이 빠르게 다가오는 것을 알아차리고, 아주 조심스럽게 로시난테를 풀어 주고 자기 바지를 추슬러 올렸다. 로시난테는 자유로워진 것을 알자, 천성이 전혀 혈기 왕성하지는 않았으나 생기를 느끼는 듯 앞발로 땅을 긁기 시작했으니——껑충거리는 것으로 말하자면, 미안한 말이지만 그게 무엇인지조차 몰랐기 때문이다. 그리하여 돈키호테는 로시난테가 움직일 수 있게 된 것을 보고, 이를 좋은 징조이자 그 무시무시한 모험을 시도해야 한다는 신호로 받아들였다. 이때쯤에는 날이 완전히 밝아 모든 것이 또렷이 드러났는데, 돈키호테는 자기가 무척 짙은 그늘을 드리우는 키 큰 나무들, 곧 밤나무들 사이에 있음을 보았다. 그는 또한 타격 소리가 멎지 않고 있음을 알아차렸으나, 그것이 무엇 때문인지는 알아낼 수 없었다. 그래서 더는 지체하지 않고 로시난테에게 박차를 느끼게 하고는, 다시 한번 산초에게 작별을 고하며, 앞서 말한 대로 길어야 사흘만 거기서 자기를 기다리라고, 만일 그때까지 자기가 돌아오지 않거든 자기가 그 위험천만한 모험에서 생을 마감하는 것이 하느님의 뜻이었다고 확신해도 좋다고 일렀다. 그는 자기를 대신해 귀부인 둘시네아께 전할 전언과 부탁을 다시 한번 되풀이하고는, 봉사의 대가에 대해서는 걱정하지 말라고, 집을 떠나기 전에 유언장을 작성해 두었으니 거기서 그가 봉사한 기간에 정당히 비례하여 품삯이 충분히 보상되어 있음을 알게 되리라고 말했다. 그러나 하느님께서 자기를 그 위험에서 안전하고 멀쩡하게, 다친 데 없이 건져 주신다면, 그는 약속받은 섬을 확실한 것보다 훨씬 더 확실한 것으로 여겨도 좋다고 했다. 산초는 자기 선량한 주인의 그 가슴 저미는 말을 다시 듣고는 새로이 울기 시작했고, 이 일의 마지막 결말과 끝까지 그와 함께 머물기로 결심했다. 산초 판사의 이 눈물과 이 갸륵한 결심에서, 이 이야기의 작가는 그가 좋은 집안 출신이며 적어도 유서 깊은 기독교도였음에 틀림없다고 추론한다. 그가 드러낸 그 감정은 주인의 마음을 움직였으나, 그가 어떤 나약함을 내보일 만큼은 아니었다. 오히려 그는 자기가 느끼는 바를 할 수 있는 한 잘 감추고는, 물소리와 타격 소리가 들려오는 듯한 그 방향으로 움직이기 시작했다.\n\n산초는 늘 하던 대로 굴레를 끌어, 잘되든 못되든 한결같은 동무인 당나귀를 데리고 걸어서 그의 뒤를 따랐다. 그늘진 밤나무들 사이로 얼마쯤 나아가자, 그들은 어떤 높은 바위들 발치의 자그마한 풀밭에 이르렀는데, 그 바위 아래로 엄청난 물줄기가 쏟아져 내리고 있었다. 그 바위 발치에는 집이라기보다는 폐허에 가까운, 조잡하게 지어진 집 몇 채가 있었고, 그 집들 사이에서 여전히 쉴 새 없이 이어지는 타격 소리와 요란한 소음이 들려오는 것을 그들은 알아차렸다. 로시난테는 물소리와 타격 소리에 겁을 먹었으나, 돈키호테는 그를 달래며 한 걸음 한 걸음 그 집들을 향해 나아갔다. 그는 온 마음을 다해 귀부인께 자신을 의탁하며 그 무시무시한 고비와 위업에서 그분의 도움을 간청하고, 가는 길에 하느님께도 자신을 의탁하며 자기를 잊지 말아 주십사 빌었다. 산초는 한시도 그의 곁을 떠나지 않고, 목을 최대한 길게 빼어 로시난테의 다리 사이로 빠끔히 내다보며, 이제 자기를 그토록 두려움과 불안에 떨게 한 것이 무엇인지 알아낼 수 있을까 살폈다. 그들이 백 걸음쯤 더 나아갔을까, 어느 모퉁이를 돌자, 밤새도록 그들을 그토록 두려움과 당혹 속에 가두어 두었던, 그 무시무시하게 울리고 그들에게 경외감을 자아내던 소음의 진정한 원인이 어떤 착오의 여지도 없이 또렷하고 분명하게 드러났다. 그것은 (독자여, 그대가 역겨워하거나 실망하지 않는다면) 여섯 개의 축융기 망치였으니, 그것들이 번갈아 두드리며 그 모든 소음을 내고 있었던 것이다.\n\n* 축융기: 모직물을 두드려 다지고 빨아 천을 촘촘하게 만드는 물레방아식 기계.\n돈키호테는 그것이 무엇인지 알아차리자, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 말문이 막히고 몸이 뻣뻣하게 굳어 버렸다. 산초가 그를 흘끗 보니, 그는 분명한 굴욕감에 고개를 가슴팍까지 떨군 채였다. 그리고 돈키호테가 산초를 흘끗 보니, 그는 두 볼을 잔뜩 부풀리고 입은 웃음으로 가득 차, 금방이라도 웃음을 터뜨릴 듯한 기색이 역력했다. 그 꼴을 보고 돈키호테는 분한 와중에도 웃음을 참지 못했고, 산초는 주인이 웃기 시작하는 것을 보자 어찌나 신나게 웃어 젖혔던지 웃다가 배가 터지지 않게 두 손으로 옆구리를 부여잡아야 할 지경이었다. 네 번이나 웃음을 멈추었으나, 그때마다 처음과 똑같은 격렬함으로 다시금 웃음이 터져 나왔다. 이에 돈키호테는 격노했으니, 무엇보다 산초가 비웃듯이 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었을 때 그러했다. \"산초여, 알아 두어라. 나는 하늘의 뜻으로 이 무쇠의 시대에 태어나, 그 안에서 황금기, 곧 황금의 시대를 되살리려 하노라. 위험과 위대한 업적과 용맹한 행적이 마련되어 있는 자가 바로 나이니라.\" 그러면서 그는 그 무시무시한 타격 소리를 처음 들었을 때 돈키호테가 했던 말을 줄줄이 되풀이했다.\n\n그리하여 돈키호테는 산초가 자기를 조롱거리로 삼는 것을 보고 어찌나 굴욕스럽고 분했던지, 창을 들어 올려 그를 두 번이나 후려쳤으니, 만일 그가 어깨로 그것을 받지 않고 머리로 받았더라면 품삯을 치를 일도 없었으리라——하기야 그의 상속인들에게 치를 일은 있었겠지만. 산초는 자기 농담에 대한 답례로 진짜 호된 대접을 받게 되자, 주인이 그것을 더 멀리 끌고 갈까 두려워 아주 공손하게 말했다. \"진정하십시오, 나리. 정말이지 저는 그저 농담을 했을 뿐입니다요.\"\n\n\"네가 농담을 하고 있다면, 나는 농담이 아니다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"이보게, 익살맞은 양반아, 만일 이것이 축융기 망치가 아니라 무슨 위험천만한 모험이었다면, 내가 그것에 도전하고 이루어 내는 데 필요한 용기를 보여 주지 않았다고 생각하느냐? 어디 그래, 신사인 내가 소리를 알아듣고 분간하여 그것이 축융기에서 나는 것인지 아닌지 가려낼 의무라도 있단 말이냐? 더구나 어쩌면, 실제로 그러하듯이, 나는 평생토록 그런 것을 한 번도 본 적이 없거늘 말이다. 그런 것들 틈에서 나고 자란 너 같은 천한 촌놈이야 보았겠지만. 그러나 이 망치 여섯 개를 거인 여섯으로 바꾸어, 하나씩이든 다 함께든 나에게 덤벼들게 해 보아라. 만일 내가 그것들을 거꾸로 나동그라지게 만들지 못한다면, 그때는 네 멋대로 나를 조롱해도 좋다.\"\n\n\"그만하겠습니다요, 나리.\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"농담이 좀 지나쳤음을 인정합니다요. 하지만 말씀해 보십시오, 나리. 이제 우리 사이에 화해가 이루어졌으니 말입니다요(그리고 하느님께서 이번 모험에서 나리를 무사히 멀쩡하게 빼내 주셨듯, 앞으로 닥칠 온갖 모험에서도 그리 빼내 주시기를), 우리가 빠졌던 그 엄청난 두려움이, 적어도 제가 빠졌던 그 두려움이 웃음거리가 아니었습니까요? 그리고 그것이 좋은 이야깃거리가 아닙니까요? 나리로 말하자면, 이제 저는 나리께서 두려움이나 경악이 무엇인지 알지도 이해하지도 못하신다는 걸 알겠으니 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"우리에게 일어난 일이 웃을 만한 것이라는 점은 부정하지 않겠다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"하지만 그것을 이야깃거리로 삼을 만하지는 않다. 어떤 일의 핵심을 제대로 짚어 낼 만큼 영민한 자가 누구나 다 있는 것은 아니니까.\"\n\n\"어쨌든,\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리께서는 창으로 핵심을 제대로 짚으실 줄 아셨습니다요. 제 머리를 겨누고서 제 어깨를 맞히셨으니 말입니다요. 그것도 하느님 덕분이요, 제가 잽싸게 피한 덕분이지만요. 하지만 그건 그렇다 치고요. 모든 건 빨면서 드러나는 법입죠. '울게 만드는 자가 너를 진정 사랑하느니라'라는 말을 저는 들은 적이 있습니다요. 게다가 위대한 영주들은 하인에게 모진 말을 하고 나면 바지 한 벌을 주는 것이 관례라지요. 다만 매질을 하고 나서는 무엇을 주는지 저는 모르겠습니다요. 편력 기사들이 매질 뒤에 섬이나, 본토에 있는 왕국이라도 주는 게 아니라면 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"주사위를 굴려 봐야 알겠지.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 하는 말이 모두 들어맞을지도 모르지. 지난 일은 눈감아 다오. 우리의 첫 충동이 우리 마음대로 되는 것이 아님은 너도 알 만큼 영민하니 말이다. 그리고 앞으로 한 가지 명심할 것이 있다. 내 앞에서는 그 수다스러움을 억제하고 삼가야 한다는 것이다. 내가 읽은, 그것도 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 그 모든 기사도 책에서, 너처럼 자기 주인에게 그토록 많은 말을 늘어놓는 종자를 나는 한 번도 만난 적이 없다. 사실 나는 이것이 너의 큰 잘못이자 나의 큰 잘못이라 여긴다. 너의 잘못이란 네가 나를 그토록 존경하지 않는 것이요, 나의 잘못이란 내가 나 자신을 더 존경받도록 만들지 못하는 것이다. 골의 아마디스의 종자였던 간달린은 인술라 피르메의 백작이었는데, 우리가 읽기로 그는 늘 모자를 손에 들고, 고개를 숙이고, 몸을 두 겹으로 굽혀, 무어인의 방식대로 자기 주인에게 말을 올렸다. 또 갈라오르의 종자였던 가사발은 어떠하냐? 그는 어찌나 말이 없었던지, 그의 그 경이로운 과묵함의 위대함을 우리에게 일러 주기 위해, 그토록 진실한 그 이야기 전체에서 그의 이름이 단 한 번만 언급될 정도였다. 산초여, 내가 한 이 모든 말에서 너는, 주인과 하인 사이에, 영주와 종복 사이에, 기사와 종자 사이에는 반드시 차이가 있어야 한다는 것을 깨닫게 될 것이다. 그러니 오늘부터 우리 사이에서는 서로 더 많은 존경을 지키고 덜 허물없이 굴어야 할 것이다. 내가 어떤 식으로든 너에게 화가 나면, 그것은 항아리에 좋지 못한 일이 될 테니 말이다. 내가 너에게 약속한 은혜와 혜택은 때가 되면 올 것이고, 설령 오지 않더라도, 앞서 말했듯 적어도 네 품삯만큼은 잃지 않을 것이다.\"\n* 인술라 피르메: '굳건한 섬'이라는 뜻으로, 기사도 소설 『골의 아마디스』에 나오는 가상의 영지.\n\n\"나리께서 하시는 말씀은 모두 지당하십니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하지만 한 가지 알고 싶은 것이 있습니다요(혹시 은혜가 베풀어질 때가 오지 않아 품삯에 기대야 할 경우를 대비해서요). 그 시절 편력 기사의 종자는 얼마나 받았습니까요? 그리고 벽돌공처럼 달로 쳐서 합의했습니까요, 아니면 날로 쳐서 했습니까요?\"\n\n\"내 생각에는,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그런 종자들이 품삯을 받은 적은 한 번도 없고, 다만 주인의 은총에 기댔던 것 같다. 그리고 내가 집에 남겨 둔 봉인된 유언장에 너의 품삯을 언급해 두었다면, 그것은 무슨 일이 일어날지 모르기 때문이었다. 이 비참한 시대에 기사도가 어떻게 될지 나는 아직 모르거니와, 사소한 일로 저세상에서 내 영혼이 고통받기를 원치 않기 때문이다. 산초여, 너는 알아 두어라. 이 세상에서 모험가의 처지보다 더 위태로운 신분은 없다는 것을.\"\n\n\"그건 사실입니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"축융기 망치 소리만으로도 나리처럼 용맹한 편력 모험가의 가슴이 흔들리고 불안해질 수 있으니 말입니다요. 하지만 이것만은 믿으셔도 좋습니다요. 앞으로 저는 나리의 일이라면 무엇이든 가볍게 여기려고 입을 열지는 않을 것이며, 오직 저의 주인이자 타고난 영주이신 나리를 받들기 위해서만 입을 열겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"그리한다면,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"너는 이 땅 위에서 오래 살리라. 부모 다음으로 주인은 마치 부모인 것처럼 존경받아야 하는 법이니.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXI.\n\n무적의 기사에게 일어난 다른 일들과 더불어, 맘브리노 투구라는 드높은 모험과 값진 전리품에 관하여\n\n이제 비가 조금씩 내리기 시작하여 산초는 축융 공장 안으로 들어가자고 했으나, 돈키호테는 지난밤의 그 농지거리 때문에 그곳을 어찌나 혐오하게 되었던지 무슨 일이 있어도 들어가려 하지 않았다. 그리하여 오른쪽으로 비켜 가다가 그들은 전날 밤 택했던 길과는 다른 또 하나의 길에 들어섰다. 얼마 지나지 않아 돈키호테는 머리에 황금처럼 번쩍이는 무언가를 쓴 말 탄 사내를 발견했고, 그를 보자마자 산초를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"산초, 내 생각에 진실하지 않은 속담은 하나도 없으니, 모두가 모든 학문의 어머니인 경험 그 자체에서 길어 올린 격언이기 때문이다. 특히 '한 문이 닫히면 다른 문이 열린다'고 하는 그 속담이 그러하다. 내가 이렇게 말하는 까닭은, 어젯밤 운명이 축융 공장으로 우리를 속여 가며 우리가 찾던 모험의 문을 우리에게 닫아 버렸다면, 이제 더 낫고 더 확실한 또 하나의 모험을 위해 다른 문을 활짝 열어 주고 있기 때문이다. 만일 내가 거기에 들어가는 데 실패한다면 그것은 오롯이 내 잘못일 것이요, 축융 공장에 대한 무지나 밤의 어둠 탓으로 돌릴 수는 없으리라. 내가 이렇게 말하는 것은, 내 잘못 본 것이 아니라면 머리에 맘브리노의 투구를 쓴 자가 우리 쪽으로 다가오고 있기 때문인데, 그것에 관해서는 네가 기억하는 그 맹세를 내가 했지 않느냐.\"\n\n\"나리, 말씀하시는 것을 조심하시고, 행하시는 것은 더더욱 조심하십시오.\" 산초가 말했다. \"우리 정신을 마저 두들겨 빼낼 축융 공장 따위는 더는 사양하고 싶으니 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"이 사람아, 악마가 너를 잡아가겠구나.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"투구가 축융 공장과 무슨 상관이 있단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"하지만 정말이지, 제가 예전처럼 말해도 된다면, 나리께서 하시는 말씀이 잘못되셨다는 걸 아실 만한 이유들을 댈 수 있을지도 모르겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"이 믿음 없는 배신자야, 내가 하는 말이 어찌 잘못될 수 있단 말이냐?\" 돈키호테가 받아쳤다. \"말해 보아라, 저기 얼룩진 잿빛 준마를 타고 머리에 황금 투구를 쓴 채 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 기사가 네 눈에 보이지 않느냐?\"\n\n\"제가 보고 알아볼 수 있는 거라곤,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제 것과 똑같은 잿빛 당나귀에 탄 사내 하나가 머리에 뭔가 번쩍이는 걸 얹고 있다는 것뿐입니다요.\"\n\n\"바로 그것이 맘브리노의 투구다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"너는 한쪽으로 비켜서서 나 혼자 그자를 상대하게 두어라. 시간을 아끼려고 한마디도 하지 않고 내가 어떻게 이 모험을 결말짓고, 그토록 갈망하던 투구를 손에 넣는지 보게 될 것이다.\"\n\"비켜서 있도록 조심하겠습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하지만 다시 한번 말씀드리건대, 부디 그것이 축융 공장이 아니라 마요라나이기를 하느님께 비옵니다요.\"\n\n* 마요라나: 향신료로 쓰이는 풀로, 여기서는 '제발 좋은 일이기를'이라는 뜻의 관용적 표현으로 쓰였다.\n\n\"이 사람아, 무슨 일이 있어도 그 축융 공장 얘기는 다시 내게 꺼내지 말라고 했거늘,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"맹세컨대 — 더는 말하지 않겠다만 — 네놈의 영혼을 축융해서 빼내 버리겠다.\"\n\n산초는 주인이 마치 공처럼 자기에게 내던진 그 맹세를 정말로 실행에 옮길까 두려워 입을 다물었다.\n\n돈키호테가 본 투구와 준마와 기사에 관한 실상은 이러했다. 그 근방에는 마을이 둘 있었는데, 그중 하나는 어찌나 작은지 약방도 이발사도 없는 반면, 가까이 있는 다른 마을에는 둘 다 있었다. 그래서 큰 마을의 이발사가 작은 마을까지 보살펴 주었으니, 그 작은 마을에는 피를 뽑아야 할 병자 하나와 면도를 받고 싶어 하는 사내 하나가 있었다. 이발사는 이 볼일로 놋대야를 가지고 가는 길이었는데, 운수 사납게도 가는 도중에 비가 내리기 시작했고, 아마 새것이었을 모자를 버리지 않으려고 머리에 대야를 얹었던 것이다. 깨끗이 닦인 그 대야는 반 레과 거리에서도 번쩍였다. 산초가 말한 대로 그는 잿빛 당나귀를 타고 있었으니, 바로 이것이 돈키호테에게는 얼룩진 잿빛 준마와 기사와 황금 투구로 보이게 된 연유였다. 그는 자기가 보는 모든 것을 자신의 미친 기사도와 빗나간 편력의 망상에 끼워 맞추었던 것이다. 가엾은 기사가 가까이 다가오는 것을 보자, 그는 그자와 한마디 담판도 나누지 않고 로시난테의 전속력으로 창을 낮게 겨눈 채 돌진하여, 그자를 꿰뚫어 버리겠다고 단단히 작정하고서, 그에게 다다르자 돌격의 기세를 늦추지도 않고 외쳤다.\n\n\"가련한 자여, 네 몸을 지키든가, 아니면 마땅히 내 것이 될 그것을 순순히 내게 내놓아라.\"\n\n이발사는 아무런 예상도 두려움도 없이 이 환영 같은 것이 자기에게 들이닥치는 것을 보고, 창의 일격에서 몸을 구할 다른 길이라곤 당나귀에서 굴러떨어지는 것밖에 없었다. 땅에 닿기가 무섭게 그는 사슴보다 날렵하게 벌떡 일어나 바람보다 빠르게 들판을 가로질러 내달렸다.\n\n그는 대야를 땅바닥에 남겨 두었고, 돈키호테는 그것으로 만족하며 말하기를, 저 이교도가 분별을 보여 비버를 흉내 냈다고 했다. 비버란 사냥꾼들에게 쫓기게 되면, 자신의 본능으로 자기가 무엇 때문에 쫓기는지를 알고서, 바로 그것을 제 이빨로 물어뜯어 잘라 버리는 짐승이라는 것이었다.\n\n그는 산초에게 투구를 주워 오라 일렀고, 산초는 그것을 손에 들고 말했다.\n\n\"맹세코 이 대야는 좋은 물건입니다요. 1마라베디만큼의 값어치라도 있다면 8레알짜리 은화만큼은 나갈 겁니다요.\" 그러고는 그것을 주인에게 건넸고, 주인은 곧장 그것을 머리에 쓰고는 맞는 자리를 찾느라 이리저리 돌려 보았으나 끝내 찾지 못하고 말했다. \"이 유명한 투구가 처음 벼려졌을 때 그 치수의 주인이었던 이교도는 머리가 무척 컸던 게 분명하다. 다만 가장 고약한 것은 절반이 없어졌다는 것이다.\"\n\n산초는 주인이 대야를 투구라 부르는 것을 듣고 웃음을 참지 못했으나, 주인의 노여움을 떠올리고는 웃음 한가운데서 그것을 억눌렀다.\n\n\"산초, 무엇을 그리 웃느냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"웃는 까닭은,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 투구의 주인이었던 이교도가 머리가 얼마나 컸을까 생각하니 우스워서입니다요. 영락없이 보통 이발사의 대야처럼 생겼지 않습니까요.\"\n\n\"산초, 내가 무엇을 의심하는지 아느냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이 마법에 걸린 투구의 놀라운 물건이 어떤 기이한 사고로, 그 값어치를 알아보거나 헤아릴 줄 모르는 누군가의 손에 들어갔던 게 틀림없다. 그자는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 모른 채, 그것이 순금임을 보고서 값나가는 만큼 챙기려고 절반을 녹여 버리고, 나머지 절반으로 네 말마따나 이발사 대야 같은 이것을 만들었을 것이다. 허나 어찌 되었든, 그것을 알아보는 나에게는 그 변형이 아무런 차이가 없으니, 대장장이가 있는 첫 마을에서 제대로 고쳐 놓을 것이며, 그것도 대장간의 신이 전쟁의 신을 위해 벼려 준 그 투구조차 능가하지도, 견주지도 못할 만한 솜씨로 고쳐 놓으리라. 그동안은 할 수 있는 대로 쓰고 다닐 것이니, 없는 것보다는 무엇이라도 있는 편이 나은 법이요, 더구나 돌멩이가 어쩌다 날아드는 일격쯤은 막아 주기에 충분할 테니 말이다.\"\n\n\"그것은,\" 산초가 말했다. \"두 군대의 전투에서처럼 무릿매로 쏘아 대지만 않는다면 그렇겠지요. 그때 놈들이 나리의 어금니에 십자가를 그어 놓고, 제 내장을 다 게워 내게 만든 그 거룩한 약물 병을 박살내 버렸지 않습니까요.\"\n\n\"그것을 잃어버린 게 그리 애통하지는 않다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"산초, 너도 알다시피 그 처방은 내 기억 속에 있으니 말이다.\"\n\n\"저도 알고 있습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"하지만 제가 살아 있는 동안 다시 그것을 만들거나 시험해 본다면, 그때가 제 마지막 순간이 되기를 바랍니다요. 게다가 저는 그게 필요해질 상황에 저를 내던질 생각이 전혀 없으니, 제 오관을 모두 동원해서 다치지도 않고 누구를 다치게 하지도 않으려고 단단히 마음먹고 있습니다요. 다시 담요 키질을 당하는 일에 대해서는 아무 말도 않겠습니다요. 그런 변고는 막기가 어려운 것이라, 만일 닥쳐 온다면 어깨를 움츠리고 숨을 참고 눈을 감은 채, 운수와 담요가 보내는 곳으로 몸을 내맡기는 수밖에 별도리가 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"산초, 너는 나쁜 기독교도로구나.\" 이 말을 듣고 돈키호테가 말했다. \"한번 당한 모욕은 결코 잊지 않으니 말이다. 허나 알아 두어라, 사소한 것에 마음을 두지 않는 것이야말로 고귀하고 너그러운 마음이 할 일이다. 그 일로 네가 무슨 다리를 절게 되었느냐, 무슨 갈비뼈가 부러졌느냐, 무슨 머리가 깨졌기에 그 장난을 잊지 못한단 말이냐? 제대로 따져 보면 그것은 한낱 장난이요 놀이였으니, 내가 그렇게 보지 않았던들 돌아가서 너의 원수를 갚느라, 그리스인들이 헬레네를 빼앗긴 일로 저지른 것보다 더한 해코지를 했으리라. 그 헬레네로 말하자면, 지금 살아 있거나 혹은 그 시절에 내 둘시네아가 살아 있었다면, 그녀가 지금처럼 아름다움으로 그토록 이름나지는 못했으리라는 것을 믿어도 좋으리라.\" 여기서 그는 한숨을 내쉬어 하늘 높이 띄워 보냈다. 그러자 산초가 말했다. \"진심으로 원수를 갚을 수 없는 일이니 장난으로 치고 넘어가지요. 하지만 그것이 어떤 종류의 장난이고 진심이었는지 저는 잘 알고 있으며, 그것이 제 어깨에서 지워지지 않듯 제 기억에서도 결코 지워지지 않으리란 것도 압니다요. 그건 그렇다 치고, 나리께서 쓰러뜨리신 그 마르티노라는 자가 여기 버려두고 간, 잿빛 당나귀처럼 생긴 이 얼룩진 잿빛 준마를 우리가 어떻게 해야 할지 말씀해 주시겠습니까요? 그자가 줄행랑을 친 그 꼴로 보아, 이걸 찾으러 다시 돌아올 성싶지는 않으니 말입니다요. 게다가 제 수염을 걸고 말하건대, 이 잿빛은 좋은 놈입니다요.\"\n\n\"나는,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 무찌른 자들의 전리품을 챙기는 버릇이 한 번도 없었으며, 그들의 말을 빼앗아 그들을 걸어가게 만드는 것도 기사도의 관례가 아니다. 다만 승자가 싸움에서 자기 말을 잃었을 경우에는, 정당한 전쟁에서 얻은 것으로 패자의 말을 취하는 것이 합당하다. 그러니 산초, 이 말이든 당나귀든 네가 무엇이라 부르고 싶든 간에 그것을 그냥 두어라. 주인이 우리가 여기서 떠나는 것을 보면 그것을 찾으러 돌아올 것이다.\"\n\n\"하느님께서 아시지만 저는 그걸 갖고 싶습니다요.\" 산초가 받아 말했다. \"아니면 적어도 제 것과 바꾸기라도 했으면 좋겠는데, 제 것은 그만큼 좋아 보이지 않으니 말입니다요. 참으로 기사도의 법이란 엄격하군요. 당나귀 한 마리를 다른 것과 바꾸도록 늘여 줄 수조차 없다니 말입니다요. 적어도 마구라도 바꿔도 되는지 알고 싶습니다요.\"\n\n\"그 점에 관해서는 나도 그리 확실치 않다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그러니 일이 의심스러운 만큼, 더 나은 정보를 얻기 전까지는, 네게 절실히 필요하다면 마구는 바꿔도 좋다고 말하겠다.\"\n\n\"어찌나 절실한지,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"그것이 제 몸에 걸칠 것이라 해도 이보다 더 절실하지는 못할 겁니다요.\" 그러고는 이 허락에 힘입어 곧장 마구 바꾸기를 실행하여, 제 짐승을 더할 나위 없이 잘 차려 입히고 아주 딴것으로 만들어 놓았다. 이 일을 끝내고서 그들은 짐 나르던 노새에게서 노략한 전리품의 남은 것으로 요기를 했고, 축융 공장에서 흘러나오는 개울물을 마셨는데, 그 공장이 자기들에게 안겨 준 놀라움 때문에 어찌나 그것을 진저리 치며 미워했던지 그쪽으로는 눈길 한 번 던지지 않았다. 그리하여 노여움과 울적함을 모두 떨쳐 버리고 그들은 말에 올라, 정해진 길도 잡지 않고서 — 진정한 편력 기사에게는 어떤 길도 정하지 않는 것이 마땅한 일이므로 — 로시난테의 뜻에 이끌려 길을 나섰으니, 로시난테의 뜻이 곧 주인의 뜻을 함께 끌고 갔고, 더구나 당나귀의 뜻 또한 그러하여 다정하고 붙임성 있게 어디로든 로시난테가 이끄는 대로 따라갔다. 그럼에도 그들은 다시 큰길로 돌아와, 다른 아무런 목적도 없이 되는대로 그 길을 따라갔다.\n\n이렇게 길을 가던 중에 산초가 주인에게 말했다. \"나리, 잠시 나리께 말씀 좀 여쭤도 괜찮겠습니까요? 나리께서 제게 침묵하라는 그 가혹한 분부를 내리신 뒤로 여러 가지가 제 뱃속에서 썩어 가고 있는데, 지금 막 혀끝에 하나가 걸려 있어서 그것이 상해 버리는 건 원치 않으니 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"말해 보아라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"다만 이야기를 짧게 하여라. 긴 이야기에는 즐거움이 없는 법이니.\"\n\n\"그러면 말씀드리지요, 나리.\" 산초가 받아 말했다. \"지난 며칠 동안 제가 곰곰이 생각해 본 것은, 나리께서 이런 황무지와 갈림길에서 찾아 헤매시는 이 모험들로 얻거나 이루는 것이 얼마나 보잘것없는가 하는 점입니다요. 여기서는 아무리 위험천만한 모험을 승리로 이루어 낸다 해도 보거나 알아줄 이가 아무도 없으니, 그것들은 영영 이야기되지 못한 채 묻혀 버려야 하고, 그리하여 나리의 본뜻도 그것들이 받아 마땅한 명예도 잃고 마는 것입지요. 그러니 제 생각에는 (나리의 더 나은 판단을 거스르는 게 아니라면) 전쟁을 벌이고 있는 어느 황제나 다른 위대한 군주를 찾아가 섬기는 편이 더 나을 듯합니다요. 그분을 섬기며 나리께서 나리 자신의 가치와 위대한 무력, 그리고 더 위대한 지력을 입증해 보이시면, 그것을 알아본 그 주군은 우리 각자의 공에 따라 부득불 상을 내리지 않을 수 없을 테고, 거기서는 나리의 업적을 글로 적어 그 기억을 영원히 보존해 줄 누군가가 없어 곤란하실 일도 없을 겁니다요. 제 업적에 대해서는 아무 말도 않겠습니다요. 종자의 한계를 넘지 못할 테니 말입지요. 다만 감히 말씀드리자면, 기사도에서 종자들의 업적도 적는 것이 관례라면 제 것도 빠뜨려서는 안 되리라 생각합니다요.\"\n\n\"네 말이 그르지 않다, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"허나 그 지경에 이르기 전에 먼저, 말하자면 시험 삼아 세상을 떠돌며 모험을 찾아다녀야 하느니, 몇 가지를 이루어 냄으로써 이름과 명성을 얻기 위함이다. 그리하여 어느 위대한 군주의 궁정으로 갈 때쯤이면 그 기사는 이미 자신의 행적으로 알려져 있어서, 도성의 성문에 들어서는 것을 보자마자 사내아이들이 모두 그를 뒤따르며 에워싸고 외칠 것이다. '이분이 바로 태양의 기사다.' 혹은 그가 위대한 공적을 이룬 어떤 칭호든 간에, 뱀의 기사라거나 다른 무엇이라거나 외치며 말이다. '이분이,' 그들이 말하리라. '엄청난 힘을 지닌 거인 브로카브루노를 일대일 결투에서 무찌른 분이며, 거의 구백 년 동안이나 걸려 있던 기나긴 마법에서 페르시아의 위대한 마멜루크를 풀어 준 분이다.' 이렇게 이 입에서 저 입으로 그의 업적을 외쳐 알릴 것이다. 그러다가 사내아이들과 다른 이들이 일으키는 소란에 그 왕국의 임금이 자기 궁전의 창가에 모습을 드러내고, 기사를 보자마자 그 무기와 방패의 문장으로 그를 알아보고는 으레 이렇게 말하리라. '여봐라! 내 궁정의 기사들이여, 모두 나와서 이리로 오시는 기사도의 정화를 맞이하라!' 이 분부에 모두가 나아갈 것이며, 임금 자신도 계단을 반쯤 내려와 그를 꼭 끌어안고, 뺨에 입 맞추며 그를 맞이한 뒤, 왕비의 처소로 데려가리라. 거기서 기사는 왕비와 더불어 그 딸인 공주를 보게 될 것이니, 그 공주는 알려진 온 세계를 아무리 애써 뒤져도 찾을 수 있을 가장 아름답고 기품 있는 처녀들 가운데 하나일 것이다. 곧바로 공주는 기사에게, 기사는 공주에게 서로 눈길을 고정하게 되고, 둘은 서로에게 인간이라기보다 신에 가까운 무언가로 보일 것이며, 어찌 된 영문인지도 모른 채 풀 수 없는 사랑의 그물에 사로잡혀 얽히고, 자신들의 괴로움과 고통을 말로써 알릴 길이 보이지 않아 가슴이 몹시 아릴 것이다. 거기서 그들은 분명 기사를 궁전의 호화롭게 꾸며진 어느 방으로 데려가, 갑옷을 벗기고 몸에 두를 진홍빛 화려한 망토를 가져다줄 것이니, 갑옷을 입었을 때도 고귀해 보였던 그가 더블릿 차림으로는 한층 더 고귀해 보이리라. 밤이 오면 그는 임금과 왕비와 공주와 함께 저녁을 들 것이며, 내내 공주에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 그 자리의 사람들 모르게 슬며시 곁눈질을 훔쳐볼 것이요, 공주 또한 똑같이, 그리고 똑같이 조심스럽게 그리할 것이니, 앞서 말했듯 매우 분별 있는 처녀이기 때문이다. 식탁이 치워지면, 별안간 그 방의 문으로 흉측하고 자그마한 난쟁이 하나가 들어오고, 그 뒤를 두 거인 사이에 낀 아리따운 귀부인이 따라 들어올 것인데, 그녀는 어느 옛 현자가 꾸며 낸 어떤 모험을 가지고 오니, 그것을 이루어 내는 자가 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 기사로 여겨지게 될 것이다.\n\n\"그러면 임금은 그 자리에 있는 모든 이에게 그것을 시도해 보라 명할 것이나, 그 이방의 기사 말고는 아무도 그것을 끝맺어 결말짓지 못할 것이니, 이는 그의 명성을 크게 드높이는 일이 될 것이다. 그리하여 공주는 더할 나위 없이 기뻐하며, 자신의 마음을 그토록 높은 곳에 두고 자리 잡게 한 것을 행복하고 다행스럽게 여길 것이다. 그리고 가장 좋은 것은, 이 임금인지 군주인지 무엇이든 간에 그가 자기만큼 강력한 다른 자와 매우 격렬한 전쟁에 휘말려 있다는 점이다. 그래서 이방의 기사는 궁정에서 며칠을 지낸 뒤, 그 전쟁에서 그를 섬기러 가게 해 달라고 청하리라. 임금은 기꺼이 그것을 허락할 것이며, 기사는 베풀어진 은혜에 정중히 그 손에 입 맞출 것이다. 그날 밤 그는 공주가 잠드는 방의 창살 너머로 자신의 연인인 공주에게 작별을 고할 것이니, 그 방은 정원을 향해 나 있고, 그곳에서 그는 이미 여러 차례 공주와 이야기를 나누었으며, 그 일의 다리 놓는 이이자 속내를 나눈 사람은 공주가 깊이 신뢰하는 한 처녀였다. 그가 한숨지으면 공주는 기절할 것이요, 처녀는 물을 가지러 갈 것이니, 아침이 다가오는 데다 자기 여주인의 명예를 위해 둘이 발각되는 것을 원치 않아 몹시 애를 태우리라. 마침내 공주가 정신을 차려 창살 너머로 기사에게 흰 손을 내밀면, 기사는 천 번이고 또 천 번이고 거기에 입 맞추며 눈물로 그 손을 적시리라. 둘 사이에는 서로의 길흉을 어떻게 알릴지가 정해질 것이며, 공주는 그에게 가능한 한 짧게 떠나 있어 달라 애원하고, 그는 숱한 맹세로 그리하겠노라 약속하리라. 다시 한번 그는 공주의 손에 입 맞추고, 거의 죽을 지경에 이를 만큼 비통한 심정으로 작별을 고한다. 거기서 그는 제 방으로 가 침대에 몸을 내던지나, 이별의 슬픔에 잠들지 못하다가, 아침 일찍 일어나 임금과 왕비와 공주에게 작별을 고하러 간다. 그가 두 분께 작별을 고할 때, 공주가 몸이 편치 않아 방문을 받을 수 없다는 말을 듣게 되리라. 기사는 그것이 자신의 떠남을 슬퍼하는 탓이라 여겨 가슴이 찔리고, 자신의 고통을 드러내지 않으려 안간힘을 다하나 거의 견디기 어려울 것이다. 속내를 나눈 그 처녀가 그 자리에 있어 모든 것을 살피고는, 자기 여주인에게 알리러 가니, 공주는 눈물을 흘리며 듣고서, 자신의 가장 큰 괴로움 가운데 하나는 이 기사가 누구인지, 또 그가 왕가의 혈통인지 아닌지를 알지 못하는 것이라 말하리라. 처녀는 자기 기사가 지닌 그만한 예의와 온화함과 의젓한 기품은, 왕족이며 명문가 출신이 아니고서는 있을 수 없는 것이라 그녀를 안심시키니, 그리하여 공주의 근심은 풀리고, 부모에게 의심을 사지 않으려 애써 명랑하게 지내다가 이틀 뒤에 사람들 앞에 모습을 드러낸다. 그동안 기사는 떠나간 터, 그는 전쟁에서 싸워 임금의 적을 무찌르고, 많은 도시를 손에 넣으며, 숱한 전투에서 개선하고, 궁정으로 돌아와 늘 보던 그곳에서 자신의 연인을 보게 된다. 그리하여 그가 자신의 공로에 대한 보답으로 부모에게 공주를 아내로 청하기로 정해진다. 임금은 그가 누구인지 알지 못하므로 공주를 내주기를 꺼리나, 그럼에도 빼돌리든 다른 어떤 방법으로든 공주는 결국 그의 신부가 되고, 공주의 아버지도 그것을 큰 행운으로 여기게 되리라. 그도 그럴 것이, 이 기사가 어느 왕국인지 — 아무래도 지도에는 없을 듯하다만 — 그 어느 왕국의 용맹한 임금의 아들임이 밝혀지기 때문이다. 그 아버지가 죽으면 공주가 왕위를 물려받고, 두 마디로 말해 기사는 임금이 된다. 그리고 여기서 곧장 그의 종자와, 그가 그토록 드높은 자리에 오르도록 도운 모든 이에게 상을 내리는 일이 따라온다. 그는 자신의 종자를 공주의 시녀와 혼인시킬 것이니, 그 시녀는 분명 그들의 사랑에서 속내를 나누어 준 바로 그 처녀로, 어느 대단한 공작의 딸일 것이다.\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다요. 두말할 것도 없습니다요!\" 산초가 말했다. \"바로 그걸 제가 기다리고 있는 겁니다요. 이 모든 게 한 마디 한 마디 그대로, '슬픈 몰골의 기사'라는 칭호 아래 나리께 마련되어 있는 것이니 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"의심할 것 없다, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"바로 이런 식으로, 그리고 내가 여기서 말한 것과 똑같은 단계를 밟아 편력 기사들이 임금과 황제의 자리에 오르고, 또 올라 왔으니 말이다. 이제 우리가 알아내야 할 것이라곤, 기독교도든 이교도든 전쟁을 벌이고 있고 아름다운 딸을 둔 임금이 누구인가 하는 것뿐이다. 허나 그것을 생각할 시간은 넉넉히 있으니, 앞서 말했듯 궁정으로 가기 전에 다른 곳에서 명성을 얻어야 하기 때문이다. 또 한 가지 모자란 것이 있으니, 전쟁 중에 아름다운 딸을 둔 임금을 우리가 찾아내고, 또 내가 온 우주에 걸쳐 믿기 어려운 명성을 얻었다 치더라도, 내가 왕가의 혈통이라거나 황제의 육촌이라도 된다는 것을 어떻게 입증할지 알 수가 없다. 임금은 이 점에 대해 먼저 충분히 만족하지 않고서는, 내 이름난 행적이 아무리 그럴 만한 값어치가 있다 해도 내게 딸을 아내로 내주려 하지 않을 테니 말이다. 그러니 이 결함 탓에, 내 팔이 정당하게 얻어 낸 것을 잃게 되지나 않을까 두렵다. 하기야 내가 이름난 가문의, 토지와 재산을 지닌, 오백 수엘도의 벌금을 받을 자격을 지닌 신사인 것은 사실이다. 어쩌면 내 이야기를 쓸 현자가 내 가계와 혈통을 그토록 환히 밝혀 주어, 내가 어느 임금의 오대(代)나 육대 후손임을 알게 될지도 모른다. 산초, 알아 두어라, 세상에는 두 종류의 혈통이 있느니, 그 하나는 임금과 군주에게서 가계를 끌어내고 이어받았으나 세월이 조금씩 깎아내려 마침내 거꾸로 선 피라미드처럼 한 점으로 끝나는 것이요, 다른 하나는 평민 무리에서 솟아나와 한 걸음 한 걸음 올라가 마침내 대귀족이 되는 것이다. 그러니 그 차이란, 한쪽은 지금은 아닌 것이 예전에는 그러했고, 다른 쪽은 예전에는 아니었던 것이 지금은 그러하다는 것이다. 나는 그러한 부류여서, 알아보고 나면 내 출신이 위대하고 이름난 것으로 드러날지도 모르니, 그것이라면 장차 내 장인이 될 임금도 마땅히 만족해야 하리라. 설령 그가 만족하지 않더라도, 공주는 나를 너무도 사랑하여 내가 물장수의 아들임을 빤히 안다 해도 그 아버지를 거슬러 나를 자신의 주인이요 남편으로 맞아들일 것이다. 만일 그렇지 않다면, 그때는 공주를 붙들어 내가 원하는 곳으로 데려가 버리는 데까지 이르리니, 세월이나 죽음이 그 부모의 노여움에 끝을 내어 줄 것이다.\"\n\n\"여기에 또,\" 산초가 말했다. \"심술궂은 사람들이 하는 그 말도 들어맞습니다요. '힘으로 빼앗을 수 있는 것을 은혜로 청하지 말라.' 하기야 '깨끗이 달아나는 것이 선한 이들의 기도보다 낫다'고 하는 편이 더 들어맞겠습니다요. 제가 이렇게 말씀드리는 까닭은, 나리의 장인이신 임금님께서 제 마님이신 공주님을 나리께 내주기를 마다하신다면, 나리 말씀대로 공주님을 붙들어 모셔 가는 수밖에 없기 때문입니다요. 다만 고약한 것은, 화친이 이루어지고 나리께서 왕국을 평화로이 누리시게 되기까지, 가엾은 종자는 상을 받기는커녕 쫄쫄 굶고 있다는 점입니다요. 다만 그의 아내가 될 그 속내를 나눈 시녀가 공주님과 함께 와서, 하늘이 달리 일을 마련해 주실 때까지 그녀와 함께 그 불운한 시절을 견뎌 내는 것이라면 또 모르겠습니다요. 제 생각엔 그 주인이 곧장 그녀를 정당한 아내로 종자에게 내주어도 무방할 듯하니 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"누구도 거기에 이의를 달 수 없지.\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그리 될 수 있는 일이니,\" 산초가 말했다. \"우리 자신을 하느님께 맡기고, 운명이 가고 싶은 대로 가게 두는 수밖에 없겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"하느님께서 내 소망과 네 바람에 따라 그것을 이끌어 주시기를.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그리고 스스로를 미천하다 여기는 자야말로 미천한 자이니라.\"\n\n\"하느님의 이름으로 그러하기를 빕니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"저는 오랜 기독교도이니, 백작이 되기에는 그것만으로 충분합니다요.\"\n\n\"네게는 충분하고도 남음이 있지.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"설령 그렇지 않더라도 아무 차이가 없을 것이니, 임금이 된 내가 네 편에서 사들이거나 봉사한 바 없이도 손쉽게 너에게 귀족 신분을 줄 수 있기 때문이다. 내가 너를 백작으로 삼으면, 그 즉시 너는 신사가 되는 것이요, 사람들이 무어라 떠들든 간에, 맹세코 그들은 좋든 싫든 너를 '나리'라 불러야 할 것이다.\"\n\n\"의심할 것도 없습지요. 저도 그 칭허를 떠받칠 줄 알게 될 겁니다요.\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"칭허가 아니라 칭호라 해야지.\" 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"그러지요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제 말씀은, 저도 처신할 줄 알게 되리란 겁니다요. 제 평생에 한 번, 어느 형제회의 관리인 노릇을 한 적이 있었는데, 그 관리인 예복이 어찌나 잘 어울렸던지 다들 제가 그 형제회의 집사라도 될 것 같다고들 했지요. 그러니 제 등에 공작의 예복을 걸치거나, 백작처럼 금과 진주로 차려입는다면 어떻겠습니까요? 사람들이 저를 보겠다고 백 레과 길을 마다 않고 올 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"보기에 좋겠지.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 너는 수염을 자주 깎아야 하느니, 네 수염이 어찌나 빽빽하고 거칠고 텁수룩한지, 적어도 이틀에 한 번씩 깎지 않으면 머스킷 총알이 날아갈 거리에서도 사람들이 네 본바탕을 알아볼 것이기 때문이다.\"\n\n\"이발사 하나를 두고 봉급을 주어 집에 거느리는 것 말고 더 무슨 일이 있겠습니까요?\" 산초가 말했다. \"정 필요하다면 귀족의 마부장처럼 그를 제 뒤에 따라다니게 하겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"아니, 귀족들이 마부장을 뒤에 거느린다는 것을 너는 어찌 아느냐?\" 돈키호테가 물었다.\n\n\"말씀드리지요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"몇 해 전에 제가 한 달 동안 도성에 머문 적이 있었는데, 거기서 바람을 쐬러 나온 아주 자그마한 신사 하나를 보았습니다요. 사람들이 그분을 아주 대단한 분이라 하더군요. 그런데 그분이 가는 곳마다 한 사내가 말을 타고 꼭 그분의 꼬리라도 되는 양 뒤를 따라가지 뭡니까요. 저는 어째서 저 사내가 늘 뒤에만 가지 다른 분과 나란히 가지 않느냐고 물었습니다요. 사람들이 대답하기를, 그 사내가 그분의 마부장인데, 귀족들이 그런 사람을 뒤에 거느리는 것이 관례라더군요. 그때부터 저는 그것을 알게 되었고, 그 뒤로 한 번도 잊은 적이 없습니다요.\"\n\n\"네 말이 옳다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그와 마찬가지로 너도 이발사를 데리고 다닐 수 있느니, 관습이란 것이 한꺼번에 생겨난 것도 아니요, 모두가 일시에 만들어진 것도 아니기 때문이다. 그러니 너는 이발사를 뒤에 거느린 최초의 백작이 될 수도 있으렷다. 게다가 수염을 깎이는 일은 말에 안장을 얹는 일보다 더 큰 신임을 맡기는 일이니 말이다.\"\n\n\"이발사 일은 제게 맡겨 두십시오.\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리께서는 임금이 되시려 애쓰시고, 저를 백작으로 삼아 주시는 것이나 맡아 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그리 될 것이다.\" 돈키호테가 대답하고는, 눈을 들어 다음 장에서 이야기될 것을 보았다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII.\n\n자기들이 가고 싶지 않은 곳으로 본인의 뜻을 거슬러 끌려가던 여러 불운한 자들에게 돈키호테가 베푼 자유에 관하여\n\n아랍인이자 라만차 사람인 작가 시데 아메테 베넹헬리는, 더없이 진중하고 장중하며 세밀하고 유쾌하며 독창적인 이 이야기 속에서 다음과 같이 전하고 있다. 제21장 끝머리에 적힌, 저 유명한 라만차의 돈키호테와 그의 종자 산초 판사 사이의 대화가 있은 뒤에, 돈키호테는 눈을 들어 자기가 따라가던 길을 따라 한 무리의 사람들이 오는 것을 보았으니, 열두어 명쯤 되는 사내들이 큼직한 쇠사슬에 목이 줄줄이 꿰여 마치 염주알처럼 엮인 채 걸어왔고 모두 손에 쇠고랑을 차고 있었다. 그들과 함께 말을 탄 사내 둘과 걸어오는 사내 둘도 있었는데, 말을 탄 자들은 톱니바퀴식 화승총을 들었고 걸어오는 자들은 투창과 칼을 지니고 있었다. 산초는 그들을 보자마자 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"저건 갤리선으로 끌려가는 노예 사슬입죠. 임금님 명령에 따라 억지로 갤리선으로 가는 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"억지라니, 무슨 말이냐?\" 돈키호테가 물었다. \"임금이 누군가에게 강제를 쓴다는 것이 있을 수 있는 일이냐?\"\n\n\"그런 말은 아닙죠.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"저자들은 제 죄 때문에 형을 받아 임금님의 갤리선에서 억지로 노역을 하게 된 자들이라는 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"요컨대,\" 돈키호테가 받았다. \"어찌 되었든 저 사람들은 제 뜻이 아니라 끌려서 억지로 가는 것이로구나.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습죠.\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"여기야말로 내 직분을 발휘할 자리가 아니냐. 강제를 누르고 가련한 자들을 구원하고 돕는 일 말이다.\"\n\n\"잘 헤아려 보십시오, 나리.\" 산초가 말했다. \"정의, 곧 임금님 그 자신은 저런 사람들에게 강제를 쓰거나 해를 끼치는 것이 아니라 그 죄를 벌하는 것입니다요.\"\n\n이즈음 갤리선 노예들의 사슬이 가까이 다가왔고, 돈키호테는 더없이 정중한 말씨로 그들을 호송하는 자들에게, 어찌하여 이 사람들을 이런 모양으로 끌고 가는지 그 까닭이나 사연을 부디 일러 달라고 청했다. 말을 탄 호위병 하나가 대답하기를, 이들은 폐하께 속한 갤리선 노예들이며 갤리선으로 가는 길이고, 할 말은 그것뿐이며 자기가 알아야 할 일도 그뿐이라 했다.\n\n\"그렇더라도,\" 돈키호테가 받았다. \"저들 한 사람 한 사람에게서 그 불행의 까닭을 따로따로 듣고 싶소.\" 그러고는 같은 취지의 말을 더 보태어 자기가 알고자 하는 바를 일러 달라고 하도, 어찌나 공손하게 청했던지 다른 말 탄 호위병이 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"이 가엾은 자들 하나하나의 판결 기록과 증서를 여기 갖고 있긴 하나, 지금은 그것을 꺼내거나 읽을 때가 아니오. 가서 직접 물어보시구려. 마음만 내키면 저들이 일러 줄 것이고, 또 그러기도 할 거요. 저런 작자들은 못된 짓을 하고 그것을 떠벌리는 데서 재미를 보는 자들이니 말이오.\"\n\n이 허락이 있자 — 설령 허락하지 않았더라도 돈키호테는 그리했을 테지만 — 그는 사슬로 다가가 첫 번째 사내에게 무슨 죄를 지어 이런 딱한 처지가 되었느냐고 물었다.\n\n사내는 사랑을 한 죄라고 대답했다.\n\n\"고작 그것 때문에?\" 돈키호테가 받았다. \"아니, 사랑을 했다고 사람을 갤리선으로 보낸다면 나도 진작에 거기서 노를 젓고 있어야 했겠구나.\"\n\n\"나리께서 생각하시는 그런 사랑이 아닙니다요.\" 갤리선 노예가 말했다. \"제 사랑이란, 빨래꾼 아낙의 깨끗한 빨랫감이 든 바구니를 어찌나 사랑했던지, 그놈을 어찌나 꼭 끌어안았던지, 법의 팔뚝이 제게서 억지로 빼앗지 않았다면 지금 이 순간까지도 제 발로는 결코 놓지 않았을 그런 사랑이지요. 현행범으로 붙들렸으니 고문할 것도 없었고, 사건은 곧장 마무리되어 등짝에 채찍 백 대를 안기고 거기에 구라파스 삼 년까지 얹어 주더이다. 그게 끝이었습니다요.\"\n\n\"구라파스란 게 무엇이냐?\" 돈키호테가 물었다.\n\n\"구라파스란 곧 갤리선입죠.\" 갤리선 노예가 대답했는데, 그는 스물네 살쯤 된 젊은이로 자기가 피에드라이타 태생이라 했다.\n\n돈키호테가 두 번째 사내에게 같은 것을 물었으나, 그는 어찌나 풀이 죽고 시름에 잠겼던지 아무 대답이 없었다. 그러나 첫 번째 사내가 그를 대신해 이렇게 말했다. \"저자는요, 나리, 카나리아로 가는 겁니다요. 다시 말해 악사이자 가수로 말입죠.\"\n\n\"무어라!\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"악사이고 가수라고 사람을 갤리선으로 보내기도 한단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다요, 나리.\" 갤리선 노예가 대답했다. \"고통 속에서 노래하는 것보다 더 고약한 일은 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"그 반대로, 나는 이런 말을 들었다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"노래하는 자는 제 시름을 쫓아 버린다고.\"\n\n\"여기선 거꾸롭니다요.\" 갤리선 노예가 말했다. \"한 번 노래한 자는 평생을 우니까요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠구나.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. 그러자 호위병 하나가 그에게 말했다. \"나리, 고통 속에서 노래한다는 건 저 못된 패거리들 사이에서 고문을 받고 자백한다는 뜻이오. 이 죄인은 고문을 당하자 제 죄를 자백했는데, 그 죄란 쿠아트레로, 곧 소도둑이었소. 자백을 했기에 등짝에 이미 받은 채찍 이백 대 말고도 갤리선 육 년형을 선고받은 거요. 저자가 늘 저렇게 의기소침하고 풀이 죽어 있는 건, 뒤에 남았거나 여기 함께 끌려가는 다른 도둑들이 자백을 했다고, 끝까지 아니라고 버틸 만한 배짱이 없었다고 저자를 학대하고 면박을 주고 비웃고 멸시하기 때문이오. 저들 말로는 '아니다'에 든 글자나 '그렇다'에 든 글자나 그 수가 같다는 거요. 게다가 죄인이란 제 목숨과 죽음이 증인이나 증거가 아니라 제 혀에 달려 있을 때야말로 형편이 좋은 법인데, 내 생각에도 저들 말이 그리 틀린 건 아니오.\"\n\n\"내 생각도 그렇소.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. 그러고는 세 번째 사내에게로 옮겨 가 다른 자들에게 물었던 것을 물으니, 그자는 거리낌도 무심하게도 선뜻 대답했다. \"전 두카도 열 닢이 없어서 그 마님이신 구라파스님께로 오 년을 가는 길입니다요.\"\n\n\"그 곤경에서 자네를 꺼내 줄 수만 있다면 내 기꺼이 스무 닢을 주겠네.\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"그건요,\" 갤리선 노예가 말했다. \"굶어 죽어 가면서 바다 한복판에서 돈을 쥐고 있어도 사고 싶은 걸 살 길이 없는 거나 마찬가집죠. 이렇게 말씀드리는 건, 나리께서 지금 제게 내미시는 그 두카도 스무 닢이 마침맞은 때에 제게 있었더라면, 그것으로 서기의 붓에 기름칠을 하고 변호사의 머리를 매끈하게 굴려 놓았을 테고, 그랬다면 오늘 저는 사냥개처럼 줄줄이 엮여 이 길 위에 있는 게 아니라 톨레도의 소코도베르 광장 한복판에 있었을 게 아닙니까. 허나 하느님은 위대하시니, 참는 수밖에요. 자, 그 얘긴 그쯤 해 두지요.\"\n\n돈키호테는 네 번째 사내에게로 옮겨 갔는데, 가슴께까지 흘러내린 흰 수염을 기른 풍채 점잖은 사내였다. 그는 어찌하여 여기 있게 되었느냐는 물음을 듣자 한 마디 대답도 없이 울기 시작했으나, 다섯 번째 사내가 그의 혀를 대신해 말했다. \"이 점잖은 어른은 격식을 갖춰 말에 태워져 거리를 한 바퀴 돌고 나서 갤리선으로 사 년을 가는 길입니다요.\"\n\n\"제가 알기로 그건,\" 산초 판사가 말했다. \"사람들 앞에서 망신을 당했다는 뜻이죠.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습죠.\" 갤리선 노예가 받았다. \"그런 벌을 받게 된 죄목이란 귀 흥정꾼, 아니 몸뚱이 흥정꾼 노릇을 했다는 거지요. 한마디로 이 양반은 뚜쟁이로 가는 거고, 게다가 마법사 기운이 좀 묻어 있어서 그렇답니다요.\"\n\n\"그 기운만 끼지 않았더라면,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"한낱 뚜쟁이 노릇만으로는 갤리선에서 노를 저을 게 아니라 차라리 그것을 지휘하는 제독이 되어 마땅했을 것이다. 뚜쟁이라는 직분은 예사로운 것이 아니어서 사려 깊은 자들이 맡는 직분이며, 잘 다스려진 나라에서는 매우 긴요하여 좋은 가문 출신만이 행할 수 있는 것이기 때문이다. 아니, 다른 직분들에서처럼 그들을 감독하고 살피는 감찰관이 있어야 하고, 거래소의 중개인들처럼 정해진 정원도 있어야 한다. 그리하면 이 직분과 소임이 어리석고 무지한 자들의 손에 있어 생기는 숱한 폐단을 피할 수 있을 것이다. 가령 다소 모자란 여인들이나, 위상도 경험도 변변찮은 시동과 어릿광대 따위가 그러한데, 이들은 더없이 다급한 순간, 재치 있는 꾀가 필요할 때 빵 부스러기가 입으로 가는 길에서 얼어붙도록 내버려 두고는 제 오른손이 어느 쪽인지조차 알지 못한다. 나는 더 나아가, 나라에 이토록 긴요한 직분을 맡을 자들을 가려 뽑는 것이 마땅하다는 까닭을 들어 보이고 싶으나, 여기는 그럴 자리가 아니다. 언젠가 이 일을 살피고 바로잡을 만한 누군가에게 그 이치를 풀어 보이리라. 지금 내가 말할 것은 다만, 그가 마법사이기도 하다는 사실이 보태진 덕에, 한낱 뚜쟁이 노릇 때문에 이 흰 머리와 이 점잖은 얼굴이 이토록 고통스러운 처지에 놓인 것을 보며 내가 느꼈던 슬픔이 가셨다는 것이다. 비록 나는 세상에 어떤 마법도 — 어수룩한 자들이 상상하듯 — 사람의 의지를 움직이거나 강요할 수 없음을 잘 알고 있긴 하지만 말이다. 우리의 의지는 자유로워서 그것을 강제할 수 있는 약초도 부적도 없다. 어떤 어리석은 여인들과 돌팔이들이 하는 짓이라고는, 사랑을 일으킬 힘이 있는 척하며 미약과 독약으로 사내를 미치게 만드는 것뿐이다. 내가 말했듯이 의지를 강제한다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이기 때문이다.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다.\" 선량한 노인이 말했다. \"참으로 나리, 마법을 부렸다는 죄목에 관해서라면 저는 죄가 없었습니다요. 뚜쟁이 노릇을 했다는 것은 부인할 수 없으나, 저는 그것으로 누구에게 해를 끼친다고는 한 번도 생각지 않았습죠. 제 유일한 바람이란 온 세상 사람이 다툼이나 근심 없이 즐겁게 평온히 사는 것이었으니까요. 허나 그 선한 뜻도, 이 나이의 무게를 짊어지고, 잠시도 편할 새 없는 오줌소태를 안은 채, 다시는 돌아오지 못할 곳으로 끌려가는 것에서 저를 구해 주지는 못했습니다요.\" 그러고는 다시 아까처럼 울음을 터뜨리니, 산초가 어찌나 측은한 마음이 들었던지 품에서 사 레알짜리 한 닢을 꺼내 적선으로 그에게 주었다.\n\n돈키호테는 계속 나아가 또 다른 사내에게 무슨 죄를 지었느냐고 물으니, 그자는 앞의 사내 못지않게, 아니 오히려 훨씬 더 발랄하게 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 여기 있는 건요, 제 사촌 누이 둘하고, 또 제 사촌도 아닌 다른 처자 둘하고 농을 좀 지나치게 친 탓입니다요. 한마디로, 모두하고 농을 어찌나 지나치게 쳤던지, 그 끝에 친척이 어찌나 복잡하게 불어났는지 어떤 회계사도 셈을 분명히 가려낼 수 없을 지경이었지요. 죄다 제게 불리하게 입증되었고, 봐주는 이도 없었고, 돈도 없었으니, 하마터면 목이 늘어날 뻔했습니다요. 그래서 갤리선 육 년형을 선고받았고, 저는 제 운명을 받아들였습죠. 제 잘못에 대한 벌이니까요. 저는 젊은 놈이니, 목숨만 붙어 있으면 그것으로 다 잘 풀릴 겁니다요. 나리께서 가난한 자를 도울 무엇이라도 지니셨다면, 하느님께서 하늘에서 갚아 주실 것이고, 저희도 이 땅에서 기도드릴 때 나리의 목숨과 건강을 위해, 그 인자하신 풍채에 걸맞게 그것이 길고도 복되기를 빌어 드리도록 잊지 않겠습니다요.\"\n\n이자는 학생 차림을 하고 있었는데, 호위병 하나가 말하기를 그는 말솜씨가 대단하고 라틴어에 매우 능통한 학자라 했다.\n\n이 모든 자들의 뒤로 서른쯤 된 사내 하나가 왔는데, 풍채가 아주 그럴듯했으나 다만 그가 무얼 볼 때면 두 눈이 약간 안쪽으로 한데 몰리는 것이 흠이었다. 그는 다른 자들과는 다르게 묶여 있었으니, 다리에는 어찌나 긴 사슬이 채워졌는지 그것이 온몸을 칭칭 감았고, 목에는 고리 둘이 걸려 하나는 사슬에 이어졌고 다른 하나는 이른바 '동무지킴이' 또는 '동무의 발'이라 부르는 것에 이어졌는데, 거기서 쇠붙이 둘이 허리께까지 내려와 거기에 박힌 쇠고랑 둘에 그의 두 손이 큼직한 자물쇠로 채워져 있어, 손을 입까지 올릴 수도 고개를 손까지 숙일 수도 없었다. 돈키호테가 어찌하여 이 사내는 다른 자들보다 이토록 많은 사슬을 차고 있느냐고 물었다. 호위병이 대답하기를, 그것은 이 사내 하나가 나머지 모두를 합친 것보다 더 많은 죄를 지었고, 어찌나 대담하고 악질인지 저런 꼴로 끌고 가면서도 도무지 안심이 되지 않아 그가 달아날까 두렵기 때문이라 했다.\n\n\"갤리선으로 보내지는 것보다 더 무거운 벌을 받지 않았다면,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"대체 무슨 죄를 지었기에 그렇소?\"\n\n\"저자는 십 년형으로 가는데,\" 호위병이 대답했다. \"그건 시민으로서의 죽음이나 다름없소. 더 말할 것도 없이, 이 잘난 작자가 바로 그 유명한 히네스 데 파사몬테, 일명 히네시요 데 파라피야라는 자요.\"\n\n\"점잖게 좀 하시오, 위원 나리.\" 이 말에 갤리선 노예가 끼어들었다. \"이름이니 성이니 함부로 들먹이지 맙시다. 내 이름은 히네스지 히네시요가 아니고, 집안 성은 파사몬테지 당신 말처럼 파라피야가 아니오. 저마다 제 일이나 챙기면 그것으로 충분할 거요.\"\n\n\"버르장머리 없는 소리 작작해라, 이 분에 넘치는 도둑놈아.\" 위원이 받았다. \"내가 네놈 이빨이 어쩌든 입을 다물게 만들어 주랴?\"\n\n\"사람이 하느님 뜻대로 굴러가는 거야 빤히 보이는 노릇이지.\" 갤리선 노예가 되받았다. \"허나 내가 히네시요 데 파라피야로 불리는지 아닌지는 언젠가 누군가가 알게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"그렇게들 부르지 않더냐, 이 거짓말쟁이야?\" 호위병이 말했다.\n\n\"부르긴 하지.\" 히네스가 받았다. \"허나 내 그렇게 못 부르게 만들고 말 테요, 그러지 못하면 내 머리를 깎겠소. 어디를 깎을지는 내 이빨 사이에 담아 둘 뿐이오. 나리, 저희에게 주실 게 있거든 당장 주시고, 하느님께서 길을 인도하시기를 빕니다요. 남의 인생을 이렇게 꼬치꼬치 캐물으시니 지겨워지는군요. 제 인생을 알고 싶으시거든 일러 드리지요. 저는 히네스 데 파사몬테이고, 그 인생은 바로 이 손가락들로 쓰여 있습니다요.\"\n\n\"맞는 말이오.\" 위원이 말했다. \"저자가 제 손으로 제 이야기를 더없이 멋들어지게 써 놓고는, 그 책을 감옥에 이백 레알에 저당 잡혀 놓고 왔으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그리고 난 그걸 도로 찾아낼 작정이오.\" 히네스가 말했다. \"이백 두카도에 잡혔다 해도 말이오.\"\n\n\"그토록 훌륭하단 말이냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"어찌나 훌륭한지,\" 히네스가 대답했다. \"그것에 견주면 '라사리요 데 토르메스'며, 그런 부류로 쓰였거나 앞으로 쓰일 책이란 죄다 시시할 뿐이오. 그것에 관해 한마디만 하자면, 그건 사실을 다룬 것인데, 그 사실들이 어찌나 깔끔하고 흥겨운지 어떤 거짓말도 그에 미치지 못한다는 거요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 책의 제목은 무엇이냐?\" 돈키호테가 물었다.\n\n\"'히네스 데 파사몬테의 생애'요.\" 그 주인공이 대답했다.\n\n\"그래 다 끝났느냐?\" 돈키호테가 물었다.\n\n\"어찌 끝이 났겠소,\" 그자가 말했다. \"내 인생이 아직 끝나지 않았는데. 쓰인 것은 다 내가 태어난 때부터 이번에 마지막으로 갤리선에 보내진 대목까지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 전에도 거기 있었더란 말이냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"하느님과 임금님을 섬기느라 전에도 사 년을 거기 있었고, 비스킷과 채찍이 어떤 것인지는 이제 알 만큼 알지요.\" 히네스가 대답했다. \"다시 그리로 돌아가는 게 그리 원통하지도 않은 게, 거기 가면 내 책을 마저 끝낼 짬이 날 테니까요. 아직 할 말이 많이 남았는데, 스페인의 갤리선에는 그러고도 남을 만큼 한가한 틈이 있거든요. 하긴 쓸 거리에 별 시간이 들지도 않소. 다 외고 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"꽤 영리한 자로구나.\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 불운한 자지요.\" 히네스가 받았다. \"불운이란 늘 좋은 재주를 따라다니며 괴롭히는 법이니까요.\"\n\n\"불운은 악당을 따라다니며 괴롭히지.\" 위원이 말했다.\n\n\"점잖게 하라고 진작 일러뒀잖소, 위원 나리.\" 파사몬테가 말했다. \"저 윗분들이 그 지팡이를 당신한테 준 건 여기 가엾은 우리를 학대하라고 준 게 아니라, 폐하께서 명하신 곳으로 우릴 데려가라고 준 거요. 안 그러면, 맹세코—그만둡시다—. 언젠가 여관에서 진 얼룩이 빨래에서 드러날 날이 올지도 모르지. 다들 입 다물고 얌전히 굴고 말은 곱게 하시오. 자, 이제 가십시다. 이 흥겨운 구경은 실컷 했으니 말이오.\"\n\n위원이 그 위협에 갚으려고 파사몬테를 치려 지팡이를 쳐들었으나, 돈키호테가 그 사이에 끼어들어 그를 학대하지 말라고 청하며, 두 손이 묶인 자가 혀나마 조금 자유롭게 두는 것이 그리 과한 일은 아니지 않으냐고 했다. 그러고는 사슬에 엮인 모두를 향해 돌아서서 말했다.\n\n\"그대들이 내게 들려준 모든 것으로 미루어, 사랑하는 형제들이여, 나는 분명히 알겠노라. 그대들이 비록 제 잘못으로 벌을 받기는 했으나, 앞으로 견뎌야 할 그 벌이 그대들에게 그리 즐거운 것이 못 되며, 그대들의 뜻을 몹시 거슬러 마지못해 그리로 끌려간다는 것을. 그리고 어쩌면 이자는 고문 앞에서의 용기가 모자랐던 탓에, 저자는 돈이 없었던 탓에, 또 다른 자는 변호가 없었던 탓에, 끝으로는 판관의 그릇된 판단 탓에 그대들이 파멸하고, 그대들 편에 있던 정의를 얻지 못하게 되었을 것임을. 이 모든 것이 지금 내 마음에 떠올라, 하늘이 나를 세상에 보내시고 내가 속한 기사도의 수도회에 입문케 하신 그 목적을, 그리고 그 안에서 곤경에 처한 자들과 강자의 억압 아래 신음하는 자들을 돕겠노라 세운 그 맹세를 그대들의 경우에 증명해 보이라고 나를 재촉하고 설득하며 강요하는도다. 허나 정정당당히 해낼 수 있는 일을 비열한 수단으로 하지 않는 것이 신중함의 표라는 것을 내 아는바, 나는 이 신사분들, 곧 호위병들과 위원께 부디 그대들을 풀어 주어 평안히 가게 해 주시기를 청하겠노라. 더 유리한 처지에서 임금을 섬길 자들이야 얼마든지 있을 것이기 때문이다. 하느님과 자연이 자유롭게 지으신 자들을 노예로 삼는다는 것은 내게 가혹한 일로 여겨지기 때문이로다. 게다가, 호위병 나리들이여,\" 돈키호테가 덧붙였다. \"이 가엾은 자들이 그대들에게 무슨 짓을 한 것도 아니지 않소. 제 죄는 저마다 저세상에서 갚게 하시오. 하늘에 하느님이 계셔서 악한 자를 벌하고 선한 자에게 상 주기를 잊지 않으실 터, 정직한 사람이 자기와는 아무 상관도 없는 일에 끼어들어 남을 벌하는 도구가 되는 것은 마땅치 않소. 이 청을 내가 이토록 점잖고 조용히 드리는 것이니, 그대들이 응한다면 내 그대들에게 고마워할 까닭이 있겠고, 만약 스스로 응하지 않겠다면 이 창과 칼이, 그리고 내 팔의 힘이 그대들로 하여금 억지로라도 응하게 만들 것이오.\"\n\n\"별 시답잖은 소리를 다 하는군!\" 위원이 말했다. \"마침내 그럴듯한 익살 한 자락을 늘어놓는구먼! 우리한테 임금님의 죄수들을 놓아주라니, 마치 우리한테 저들을 풀어 줄 권한이라도 있는 것처럼, 또 자기한테 우리더러 그러라고 명할 권한이라도 있는 것처럼 말이오! 가시던 길이나 가시오, 나리, 행운이나 비오. 머리에 얹은 그 대야나 똑바로 놓고, 고양이한테서 발 세 개 찾는 짓일랑 마시오.\"\n\n\"고양이고 쥐새끼고 악당인 건 바로 네놈이다.\" 돈키호테가 대꾸하고는, 말이 떨어지기 무섭게 어찌나 느닷없이 그에게 덤벼들었던지, 그자가 미처 막을 틈도 주지 않고 창으로 찔러 호되게 상처를 입혀 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다. 그것이 마침 화승총을 든 자였던 것이 그에게는 다행이었다. 다른 호위병들은 이 뜻밖의 사태에 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 어안이 벙벙하여 멍하니 서 있다가, 정신을 차리고는 말 탄 자들은 칼을, 걸어온 자들은 투창을 집어 들고 돈키호테에게 달려들었으니, 돈키호테는 더없이 침착하게 그들을 기다리고 있었다. 만약 갤리선 노예들이 눈앞에 제 몸을 풀어 자유로워질 기회가 온 것을 보고, 자기들을 엮은 사슬을 끊어 그것을 이뤄 내지 않았더라면, 돈키호테에게 일이 험하게 돌아갔으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 어찌나 어수선했던지, 호위병들은 이제 사슬을 끊고 풀려나는 갤리선 노예들에게 달려들었다가, 다시 자기들을 기다리는 돈키호테를 치려 했다가 하면서 무엇 하나 쓸모 있는 일을 해내지 못했다. 산초는 산초대로 히네스 데 파사몬테를 풀어 주는 데 손을 보탰으니, 그는 가장 먼저 자유롭게 족쇄를 벗은 몸으로 들판으로 뛰쳐나와, 땅에 쓰러진 위원에게 덤벼들어 그 칼과 화승총을 빼앗았다. 그러고는 이자를 겨누었다 저자에게 들이댔다 하며, 한 번도 쏘지는 않으면서 호위병들을 모조리 들판에서 몰아냈으니, 그들은 파사몬테의 화승총을 피하려고, 또 이제 막 풀려난 갤리선 노예들이 퍼붓는 돌 세례를 피하려고 줄행랑을 쳤기 때문이다. 산초는 이 일이 몹시 걱정스러웠으니, 달아난 자들이 이 사건을 성스러운 형제단에 알릴 것이고, 그러면 형제단이 경종 소리에 곧장 출동하여 죄인들을 찾아 나설 것을 짐작했기 때문이다. 그는 주인에게 그렇게 말하며, 당장 이곳을 떠나 바로 가까이에 있는 산속에 몸을 숨기자고 간청했다.\n\n\"그것도 다 좋은 말이다만,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"지금 무엇을 해야 할지는 내가 잘 알고 있다.\" 그러고는 이제 마구 날뛰며 위원의 옷을 알몸이 되도록 벗겨 놓은 갤리선 노예들을 모조리 한데 불러 모아, 자기 할 말을 듣게끔 둘러서게 하고는 이렇게 일렀다. \"받은 은혜에 감사할 줄 아는 것은 좋은 가문 출신인 자의 도리이며, 하느님께 가장 거슬리는 죄 가운데 하나가 곧 배은망덕이니라. 내가 이리 말하는 것은, 여러분, 그대들이 이미 명백한 증거로써 내게서 받은 은혜를 보았기 때문이다. 그 보답으로 나는 바라노니, 또 그것이 내 기쁨이니, 그대들은 내가 그대들의 목에서 벗겨 준 그 사슬을 짊어지고 곧장 길을 떠나 엘 토보소 시로 가서, 거기서 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님 앞에 그대들 자신을 보이고, 그분의 기사, 곧 슬픈 몰골의 기사가 그분께 안부를 전한다고 여쭈어라. 그리고 그대들이 그토록 갈망하던 자유를 되찾기까지, 이 놀라운 모험의 자초지종을 빠짐없이 그분께 낱낱이 아뢰어라. 이 일을 마치고 나면 그대들은 어디로 가든 좋으니, 행운이 그대들과 함께하기를 비노라.\"\n\n히네스 데 파사몬테가 모두를 대신해 대답하여 말했다. \"나리, 저희를 구해 주신 분이여, 나리께서 저희에게 요구하시는 그것은 온갖 불가능 가운데서도 가장 불가능한 일입니다. 저희는 길을 따라 함께 갈 수가 없고 오직 하나씩 따로따로, 저마다 제 갈 길로 가야 하며, 틀림없이 저희를 찾아 나설 성스러운 형제단을 피해 땅속 뱃구레에라도 숨어들려 애써야 하니까요. 나리께서 하실 수 있는, 또 마땅히 하실 수 있는 일이란, 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님께 바칠 이 봉사며 공물을, 저희가 나리의 뜻을 위해 바칠 일정한 수의 아베마리아와 크레도로 바꾸어 주시는 것입니다. 이것이라면 밤이든 낮이든, 달아나든 쉬든, 평화로울 때든 전쟁 통이든 능히 행할 수 있는 조건이지요. 허나 저희가 이제 이집트의 고깃솥으로 돌아가리라, 곧 사슬을 도로 짊어지고 엘 토보소로 떠나리라 생각하시는 것은, 아침 열 시도 채 안 되었는데 지금이 밤이라 여기시는 것과 같으며, 저희에게 이런 것을 청하시는 것은 느릅나무에서 배를 따 달라는 것과 같습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 좋은 것들 죄다 걸고 맹세코,\" 돈키호테가 (이제 노여움이 치솟아) 말했다. \"이 후레자식 같은 돈 히네시요 데 파로피요, 아니 네놈 이름이 무엇이든, 너는 꽁지를 사타구니에 끼고 그 사슬을 죄다 등에 진 채 혼자서 가야 하리라.\"\n\n파사몬테는 도무지 온순한 자가 아니었던지라(이때쯤에는 돈키호테가 자기들을 풀어 주는 따위의 변덕을 부린 것으로 보아 머리가 좀 온전치 못한 자라고 단단히 믿게 된 터였다), 이런 식으로 욕을 먹자 동료들에게 눈짓을 보냈고, 그들은 뒤로 물러서며 돈키호테에게 어찌나 빠른 속도로 돌을 퍼붓기 시작했던지 그는 도무지 작은 방패로 제 몸을 막을 수가 없었고, 가엾은 로시난테는 박차를 가해도 마치 놋쇠로 만들어진 것처럼 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다. 산초는 제 당나귀 뒤에 몸을 박고 그 뒤에 숨어 두 사람에게 쏟아지는 우박 같은 돌 세례를 피했다. 돈키호테는 그만큼 제 몸을 잘 가리지 못해, 내가 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 많은 자갈이 어찌나 세차게 온몸에 정통으로 박혔던지 그를 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨리고 말았다. 그가 쓰러지는 순간 학생이 그에게 덤벼들어 머리에서 대야를 낚아채더니, 그것으로 그의 어깨를 서너 번 후려치고 다시 땅바닥에 그만큼 더 내리쳐 거의 박살을 내 버렸다. 그러고는 그가 갑옷 위에 걸친 윗도리를 벗겨 냈고, 정강이받이만 없었더라면 양말까지 벗겨 갔을 것이다. 산초에게서는 윗옷을 빼앗아 셔츠 바람으로 남겨 두었다. 그러고는 싸움의 남은 노획물을 저희끼리 나눠 가진 뒤 저마다 제 갈 길로 가 버렸으니, 사슬을 짊어지거나 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님 앞에 가 제 몸을 보이는 일보다는, 두려워하는 성스러운 형제단을 멀찍이 피하는 데 더 마음을 썼던 것이다. 당나귀와 로시난테, 산초와 돈키호테만이 그 자리에 덩그러니 남았다. 당나귀는 고개를 떨군 채 심각하게, 자기들을 덮친 돌 폭풍이 아직 끝나지 않았다고 여기기라도 하듯 이따금 귀를 흔들고 있었고, 로시난테는 저 역시 돌에 맞아 땅에 쓰러진 터라 주인 곁에 뻗어 있었으며, 산초는 옷이 벗겨진 채 성스러운 형제단이 두려워 벌벌 떨고 있었고, 돈키호테는 그토록 많은 것을 베풀어 준 바로 그 자들에게 이런 꼴을 당한 것에 분을 삭이지 못하고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII.\n\n이런 꼴을 당한 돈키호테가 종자에게 말했다. \"산초야, 나는 시골뜨기에게 선을 베푸는 것은 바다에 물을 끼얹는 것과 같다는 말을 늘 들어 왔다. 네 말을 믿었더라면 이런 곤욕은 피했을 것을. 허나 이미 벌어진 일이니, 그저 참고 견디며 앞일에 대한 교훈으로 삼는 수밖에 없구나.\"\n\n\"나리께서 교훈을 삼으신다면야 제가 터키 사람이 되겠습죠.\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 나리 말씀처럼 저를 믿으셨더라면 이 화는 피할 수 있었던 것이니, 이번에는 제 말을 믿으십시오. 그러면 더 큰 화도 피할 수 있을 겝니다. 분명히 말씀드리지만 성스러운 형제단에게 기사도란 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 것이라, 그들은 세상의 어떤 편력 기사에게도 동전 두 닢어치의 관심도 두지 않습니다요. 그리고 분명히 말씀드리는데, 지금 이 순간에도 그들의 화살이 제 귓전을 스쳐 가며 휙휙거리는 소리가 들리는 것만 같습니다요.\"\n\n\"너는 천성이 겁쟁이로구나, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그러나 내가 고집불통이라느니, 네 충고를 한 번도 따르지 않는다느니 네가 말하지 못하도록 이번 한 번만큼은 네 충고를 받아들여, 네가 그토록 두려워하는 그 분노가 미치지 못하는 곳으로 물러나마. 다만 한 가지 조건이 있으니, 살아서든 죽어서든 결코 누구에게도 내가 두려워서 이 위험에서 물러났다거나 후퇴했다고 말해서는 안 된다. 오직 네 간청에 응하여 그리했다고만 해야 한다. 만약 네가 달리 말한다면 그것은 거짓이니, 지금부터 그때까지, 또 그때부터 지금까지, 네가 그것을 생각하거나 입에 올릴 때마다 나는 너를 거짓말쟁이라 부르고, 네가 거짓말을 하고 있으며 또 거짓말을 할 것이라 말하리라. 그리고 두 번 다시 내게 대꾸하지 말라. 내가 어떤 위험에서, 무엇보다도 약간의 두려움의 그림자를 드리우고 있는 듯한 이 위험에서 물러나거나 후퇴한다는 생각만으로도, 나는 여기 버티고 서서 홀로 기다릴 각오가 되어 있으니, 네가 떠들어 대며 두려워하는 그 성스러운 형제단뿐 아니라, 이스라엘 열두 지파의 형제들과 일곱 마카베오 형제와 카스토르와 폴룩스, 그리고 세상의 모든 형제와 형제단을 다 상대하겠노라.\"\n\n\"나리,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"물러나는 것은 도망가는 것이 아니고, 위험이 희망을 넘어설 때 기다리는 것은 지혜로운 일이 아닙니다요. 또한 오늘을 보전하여 내일을 도모하고 하루에 모든 것을 걸지 않는 것이 현명한 사람들의 도리입지요. 그리고 비록 제가 무지렁이에 시골뜨기이긴 하나, 사람들이 말하는 안전 통행이란 게 무엇인지 어렴풋이 알고는 있습니다요. 그러니 제 충고를 받아들이신 것을 후회하지 마시고, 하실 수 있거든 로시난테에 오르시고, 못 하시겠거든 제가 돕겠습니다요. 그리고 저를 따라오십시오. 지금은 손보다 다리가 더 요긴하다고 제 타고난 분별이 일러 주는군요.\"\n\n돈키호테는 아무 대꾸 없이 말에 올랐고, 산초가 당나귀를 타고 앞장서서 바로 옆에 있던 시에라 모레나로 들어섰다. 산초의 계획은 그 산을 완전히 가로질러 엘 비소나 알모도바르 델 캄포로 빠져나가는 것이었고, 형제단이 그들을 찾으러 올 경우 그 추적을 피하기 위해 며칠 동안 바위틈에 숨어 있으려는 것이었다. 갤리선 노예들과의 난리 통에서도 당나귀가 싣고 있던 식량이 무사히 빠져나온 것을 보고 그는 더욱 용기를 얻었으니, 그들이 어떻게 노략질하고 뒤졌는지를 생각하면 그것은 기적과도 같은 일이라 여겨졌던 것이다.\n\n그날 밤 그들은 시에라 모레나의 한복판에 이르렀는데, 산초가 보기에는 그곳에서 밤을 지내고, 적어도 가지고 있는 식량이 버텨 주는 날수만큼은 며칠을 더 머무는 것이 분별 있는 일 같았다. 그리하여 그들은 두 바위 사이의 코르크나무들 가운데 자리를 잡았다. 그러나 참된 신앙의 빛을 지니지 못한 자들의 견해에 따르면 만사를 제 나름의 방식으로 지휘하고 배치하고 결정짓는다는 그 잔혹한 운명은, 돈키호테의 미덕과 광기 덕분에 쇠사슬에서 풀려난 저 이름난 악당이자 도둑인 히네스 데 파사몬테가, 마땅히 두려워할 만한 이유가 있는 성스러운 형제단에 대한 공포에 쫓겨 산속에 숨기로 마음먹게끔 일을 꾸몄다. 그리고 그의 운명과 두려움은, 돈키호테와 산초 판사가 저들의 운명과 두려움에 이끌려 온 바로 그 자리로 그를 인도하였으니, 마침 그들을 알아보고 잠들게 내버려 둘 만한 때였다. 사악한 자들은 언제나 배은망덕하고, 궁핍은 악행으로 이끌며, 눈앞의 이득은 미래에 대한 모든 고려를 압도하는 법이라, 감사할 줄도 모르고 양심도 없는 히네스는 산초 판사의 당나귀를 훔치기로 작정하였으니, 로시난테로 말하자면 저당 잡히거나 팔기에 아무런 쓸모가 없는 노획물이라 거들떠보지도 않았다. 산초가 잠든 사이 그는 당나귀를 훔쳤고, 날이 밝기도 전에 멀리 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 달아나 버렸다.\n\n새벽의 여신 아우로라가 모습을 드러내어 대지에는 기쁨을 가져다주었으나 산초 판사에게는 슬픔을 안겼으니, 그가 자신의 잿빛 당나귀가 사라진 것을 알아챘기 때문이다. 당나귀를 잃은 자신을 깨닫고 그는 세상에서 가장 구슬프고 애처로운 통곡을 시작했는데, 어찌나 큰 소리였던지 그 외침에 돈키호테가 잠에서 깨어 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"오, 내 창자에서 나온 아들아, 바로 내 집에서 태어나, 내 자식들의 놀잇감이요, 내 아내의 기쁨이요, 이웃들의 부러움이요, 내 짐의 덜어 줌이요, 마지막으로 나 자신을 절반은 먹여 살리던 것아. 네가 매일 내게 벌어다 주던 스물여섯 닢의 마라베디로 내 비용의 절반을 충당했거늘.\"\n\n돈키호테는 그 통곡을 듣고 그 까닭을 알게 되자, 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 말로 산초를 위로하며 참을 것을 당부하고, 집에 있는 다섯 마리의 새끼 당나귀 가운데 세 마리를 그에게 주라는 환어음을 써 주겠노라 약속했다. 산초는 이에 위안을 얻어 눈물을 닦고 흐느낌을 억누르며 돈키호테가 베푼 친절에 감사를 표했다. 돈키호테는 산속으로 들어서며 마음속 깊이 기뻐했으니, 그곳이야말로 그가 찾아 헤매던 모험에 꼭 알맞은 곳으로 여겨졌기 때문이다. 그곳은 그와 같은 외딴곳과 황무지에서 편력 기사들에게 일어났던 놀라운 모험들을 그의 기억 속에 되살려 냈고, 그는 이런 것들을 곱씹으며 길을 갔으니, 어찌나 거기에 몰두하고 사로잡혔던지 다른 어떤 것도 생각할 겨를이 없었다.\n\n산초 또한 (이제 안전한 곳을 지나고 있다고 여겼으므로) 성직자에게서 노획한 물건 가운데 남은 것들로 식욕을 채우는 것 말고는 다른 걱정이 없었으니, 잿빛 당나귀가 싣고 다니던 짐을 짊어진 채 주인의 뒤를 따라 행군하며 자루를 비우고 제 배를 채웠다. 그렇게 갈 수만 있다면 그는 또 다른 모험을 만나는 데 한 푼도 내놓지 않았을 것이다.\n\n그렇게 분주한 와중에 그가 눈을 들어 보니 주인이 멈춰 서서 창끝으로 땅바닥에 놓인 어떤 부피 큰 물건을 들어 올리려 애쓰고 있었다. 이에 그는 필요하다면 거들려고 서둘러 주인에게 다가갔는데, 마침 주인이 창끝으로 안장 깔개를 들어 올리던 참이었고, 거기에는 여행용 가방이 하나 매달려 있었으니, 절반이, 아니 사실은 완전히 썩고 찢어진 것이었다. 그러나 어찌나 무거웠던지 산초가 들어 올리는 것을 거들어야 했고, 주인은 그에게 가방 속에 무엇이 들었는지 살펴보라고 일렀다. 산초는 몹시 민첩하게 그 일을 했는데, 가방이 쇠사슬과 자물쇠로 잠겨 있긴 했으나 찢기고 썩은 상태라 그 속에 든 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그 안에는 고운 네덜란드 삼베 셔츠 네 벌과, 그에 못지않게 진기하고 깨끗한 다른 아마포 물품들이 들어 있었고, 손수건 속에서는 제법 많은 금화를 발견했으니, 그것을 보자마자 그는 이렇게 외쳤다.\n\n\"무언가에 쓸 만한 모험을 우리에게 보내 주신 온 하늘에 축복이 있기를!\"\n\n더 뒤져 보다가 그는 호화롭게 장정된 자그마한 수첩 하나를 찾아냈다. 돈키호테는 그것을 자신에게 달라고 하면서, 돈은 산초에게 가지라고 일렀다. 산초는 그 은혜에 그의 손에 입을 맞추고는, 가방 속의 아마포를 비워 식량 자루 속에 챙겨 넣었다. 이 모든 일을 곰곰이 헤아리고 나서 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 보기에는, 산초야 — 달리는 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이다만 — 어떤 길 잃은 나그네가 이 산을 가로질러 가다가 노상강도들에게 습격당해 죽임을 당했고, 그자들이 그를 묻으려고 이 외딴곳으로 끌고 온 것이 분명하다.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가 없습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"도둑들이었다면 이 돈을 남겨 두지 않았을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"네 말이 옳다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그러니 이게 무슨 영문인지 나도 짐작도 설명도 못 하겠구나. 그러나 잠깐, 이 수첩에 우리가 알고자 하는 것을 추적하거나 알아낼 수 있을 만한 무언가가 적혀 있는지 보자꾸나.\"\n\n그가 수첩을 펼치니 거기서 맨 처음 발견한 것은, 거칠게 그러나 매우 훌륭한 필체로 쓰인 소네트 한 편이었다. 산초가 들을 수 있도록 큰 소리로 읽어 보니 그 내용은 다음과 같았다.\n소네트\n\n사랑이 지혜를 결여하였거나, 아니면 잔혹함의 극치에 이르렀거나, 그도 아니면 내 죄에 마땅한 정도를 넘어선 고통을 겪는 것이 내 운명이리라. 허나 사랑이 신이라면, 그로부터 따라 나오는 것은 그가 모든 것을 안다는 것이니, 또한 확실히 남는 것은 어떤 신도 잔혹함을 사랑하지 않는다는 것이라. 그렇다면 누가 명하였단 말인가 괴롭히면서 사로잡는 이 고행을? 그것이 그대라 하는 것은 거짓이리라, 클로에여. 그런 악이 그런 선과 더불어 살 수는 없으니. 또한 나는 감히 하늘에 그 탓을 돌리지 못하노라. 오직 내가 죽는 것이 내 운명임을 알 뿐. 제 병이 어디서 왔는지 알지 못하는 자에게는 오직 기적만이 치유를 베풀 수 있으리.\n\n\"그 시에서는 배울 게 아무것도 없네요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그 안에 들어 있는 실마리를 가지고 일의 전모라는 실타래를 풀어낼 수 있다면 또 모르겠지만요.\"\n\n\"무슨 실마리가 있다는 게냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"나리께서 그 안에 무슨 실마리가 있다고 말씀하신 줄로 알았는데요.\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그저 클로에라고 했을 뿐이다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그것은 틀림없이 이 소네트의 작자가 한탄하고 있는 귀부인의 이름이렷다. 그리고 정말이지, 이 사람은 제법 쓸 만한 시인이거나, 아니면 내가 이 재주에 대해 아는 게 별로 없는 게로구나.\"\n\n\"그럼 나리께서는 시 짓는 것도 아십니까요?\"\n\n\"네가 생각하는 것보다 더 잘 안다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"처음부터 끝까지 운문으로 쓴 편지를 네가 내 귀부인 둘시네아 델 토보소에게 가져갈 때 알게 될 것이다. 산초야, 알아 두어라. 옛날의 편력 기사들은 모두, 아니 대부분이 위대한 음유 시인이요 위대한 악사였으니, 이 두 가지 재능은, 더 정확히 말하자면 천부의 재주는 편력하는 연인들에게 고유한 것이니라. 다만 옛 기사들의 시구에는 정교함보다 기백이 더 많이 깃들어 있는 것이 사실이지.\"\n\n\"더 읽어 보십시오, 나리.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그러면 우리를 깨우쳐 줄 무언가를 찾으실 겝니다요.\"\n\n돈키호테는 책장을 넘기고 말했다. \"이건 산문이고, 편지인 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"왕래하던 편지 말씀입니까요, 나리?\"\n\n\"첫머리를 보니 연애편지인 듯하다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 나리께서 큰 소리로 읽어 주십시오.\" 산초가 말했다. \"저는 연애 이야기를 무척 좋아하거든요.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러마.\" 돈키호테가 말하고는 산초가 청한 대로 큰 소리로 읽으니, 그 내용은 이러했다.\n\n그대의 거짓된 약속과 나의 확실한 불행이 나를 어떤 곳으로 데려가니, 그곳에서는 내 한탄의 말보다 내 죽음의 소식이 먼저 그대의 귀에 닿으리라. 매정한 이여, 그대는 나를 버리고 나보다 더 부유한, 그러나 더 가치 있지는 않은 자를 택하였소. 그러나 미덕이 부로 여겨졌다면 나는 남의 재산을 부러워하지도, 내 자신의 불행을 슬퍼하지도 않았으리라. 그대의 아름다움이 일으켜 세운 것을 그대의 행실이 무너뜨렸소. 그 아름다움으로 인해 나는 그대를 천사라 믿었으나, 그 행실로 인해 나는 그대가 한낱 여인임을 알게 되었소. 내게 전쟁을 보낸 그대에게 평화가 있기를. 그리고 그대 남편의 기만이 그대에게 영영 감추어지기를 하늘이 허락하시어, 그대가 한 일을 후회하지 않게 되고, 나 또한 원치 않는 복수를 거두지 않게 되기를.\n\n편지를 다 읽고 나서 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이것을 쓴 사람이 버림받은 어떤 연인이라는 것 말고는, 이 편지에서 얻어 낼 것은 그 시구에서보다도 적구나.\" 그러고는 책의 거의 모든 책장을 넘겨 보니 더 많은 시와 편지가 나왔는데, 어떤 것은 읽을 수 있었고 어떤 것은 읽을 수 없었다. 그러나 그것들은 모두 한탄과 비탄과 의혹과 갈망과 혐오, 호의와 거절로 이루어져 있었으니, 어떤 것은 황홀하고 어떤 것은 구슬펐다. 돈키호테가 책을 살펴보는 동안 산초는 가방을 살펴보았으니, 가방 전체와 깔개에서 뒤지고 들여다보고 헤집지 않은 구석이 하나도 없었고, 뜯어보지 않은 솔기 하나, 낱낱이 풀어 헤쳐 보지 않은 양털 뭉치 하나 없었으니, 소홀함과 게으름 탓에 무엇 하나라도 놓칠세라 그리한 것이었다. 백 닢에 가까운 그 금화의 발견이 그의 마음속에 그토록 날카로운 탐욕을 불러일으켰던 것이다. 비록 더 이상의 노획물은 찾지 못했으나, 그는 담요에 키질당한 일이며, 영약을 토해 낸 일이며, 몽둥이세례를 받은 축복이며, 짐꾼들의 주먹질이며, 잃어버린 안장 자루며, 도둑맞은 외투며, 자신의 훌륭한 주인을 섬기며 견뎌 낸 그 모든 굶주림과 갈증과 피로를, 그 대가치고는 싸게 친 셈이라 여겼다. 뜻밖에 얻은 보물이라는 선물로 받은 보상이 그 모든 것에 대해 충분하고도 남는 배상이라고 생각했기 때문이다.\n\n슬픈 몰골의 기사는 여전히 그 가방의 주인이 누구일지 몹시 알고 싶어 했으니, 소네트와 편지로 보아, 또 금화로 보아, 또 셔츠의 고운 질감으로 보아, 그가 어떤 지체 높은 연인으로서 귀부인의 멸시와 잔혹함이 그를 어떤 절망적인 처지로 몰아넣은 것이 분명하다고 짐작했다. 그러나 사람이 살지 않는 그 험준한 곳에는 물어볼 만한 사람이 아무도 보이지 않았으므로, 로시난테가 택하는 대로 — 그것은 곧 갈 수 있는 길이었다 — 어떤 길이든 따라 앞으로 나아가는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었으니, 이 황야에서라면 진기한 모험을 반드시 만나게 되리라 굳게 믿었던 것이다. 그리하여 이런 생각에 잠겨 길을 가던 중, 그는 눈앞에 솟아오른 어느 높은 봉우리의 꼭대기에서, 놀라운 민첩함으로 바위에서 바위로, 풀숲에서 풀숲으로 뛰어다니는 한 사내를 보았다. 그가 알아볼 수 있는 한, 그 사내는 헐벗은 채였고, 짙은 검은 수염과 길고 헝클어진 머리에 다리와 발이 맨살이었으며, 허벅지는 황갈색 벨벳으로 보이는 바지로 가려져 있었으나 어찌나 누덕누덕하던지 여러 군데로 살갗이 드러나 보였다.\n\n그는 맨머리였고, 앞서 말한 대로 날쌔게 지나갔음에도 슬픈 몰골의 기사는 이 모든 자잘한 것들을 관찰하고 새겨 두었다. 그러나 그를 뒤쫓아 보려 했지만 그럴 수가 없었으니, 그토록 험한 땅을 헤쳐 나가는 일은 로시난테의 허약함에 허락된 바가 아니었고, 게다가 그놈은 천성이 걸음이 느리고 굼떴기 때문이다. 돈키호테는 곧장 이자가 안장 깔개와 가방의 주인이라는 결론에 이르렀고, 그를 찾아 나서기로 마음먹었으니, 설령 그를 찾기까지 그 산속에서 일 년을 헤매야 한다 해도 그리하리라 작정했다. 그리하여 그는 산초에게 산의 한쪽 면을 가로질러 지름길로 가게 하고, 자신은 다른 쪽으로 갔으니, 어쩌면 이렇게 하면 그토록 빠르게 시야에서 사라진 그 사내와 마주칠 수 있을지도 몰랐다.\n\n\"저는 그렇게 못 하겠습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리 곁에서 떨어지면 곧장 두려움이 저를 사로잡고 온갖 공포와 망상으로 저를 덮치니까요. 그러니 지금부터는 나리 곁에서 손가락 한 마디 너비만큼도 움직이지 않겠다는 것을 제가 지금 드리는 말씀으로 알아 두십시오.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하자꾸나.\" 슬픈 몰골의 기사가 말했다. \"네가 내 용기에 기대려 하니 나는 무척 기쁘구나. 비록 네 몸속의 영혼이 너를 저버린다 해도 내 용기는 결코 너를 저버리지 않으리라. 그러니 이제 네가 할 수 있는 한 천천히 내 뒤를 따라오너라. 그리고 두 눈을 등불로 삼아라. 이 산등성이를 한 바퀴 돌아보자꾸나. 어쩌면 우리가 본 그 사내와 마주칠지도 모르니, 그자는 틀림없이 우리가 발견한 물건의 주인일 것이다.\"\n\n이에 산초가 대답했다. \"차라리 그를 찾지 않는 편이 훨씬 나을 겝니다요. 만약 그를 찾았는데 그가 그 돈의 주인이라면 제가 그것을 돌려줘야 하는 게 뻔하니까요. 그러니 이런 쓸데없는 수고를 들이지 말고, 다른 좀 덜 성가시고 덜 참견스러운 방법으로 진짜 주인이 밝혀질 때까지 제가 그걸 갖고 있는 편이 낫겠습니다요. 그리고 그게 밝혀질 때쯤이면 아마 제가 그걸 다 써 버린 뒤일 테고, 그러면 임금님께서도 저를 무사하게 봐주실 겝니다요.\"\n\n\"그건 네가 틀렸다, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이제 우리가 그 주인이 누구인지 짐작하게 되었고, 그가 거의 우리 눈앞에 있으니, 우리는 마땅히 그를 찾아 돌려주어야 한다. 그리고 우리가 그를 보지 못한다 해도, 그가 주인이라는 강한 짐작만으로도 그가 실제로 주인인 것과 다름없이 우리를 죄인으로 만드는 것이니라. 그러니 산초야, 우리가 그를 찾는 일로 마음을 불편해하지 마라. 그를 찾는다면 내 마음이 한결 가벼워질 것이니 말이다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 그는 로시난테에 박차를 가했고, 산초는 짐을 진 채 걸어서 그를 뒤따랐다. 그렇게 산을 얼마쯤 돌아보고 나서, 그들은 어느 협곡에 죽어 자빠진 채 개들에게 절반쯤 뜯어 먹히고 까마귀들에게 쪼인, 안장과 굴레를 갖춘 노새 한 마리를 발견했으니, 이 모든 것이 달아난 그 사내가 노새와 안장 깔개의 주인이라는 그들의 짐작을 한층 더 굳혀 주었다.\n\n그것을 바라보며 서 있는데, 양 떼를 지키는 목동이 부는 듯한 휘파람 소리가 들렸고, 갑자기 그들의 왼편에 수많은 염소 떼가 나타나더니, 그 뒤로 산꼭대기에 그것들을 지키는 염소지기가 모습을 드러냈으니, 나이가 지긋한 사내였다. 돈키호테가 큰 소리로 그를 불러 자기들이 서 있는 곳으로 내려와 달라고 청했다. 그러자 그가 마주 소리치며, 염소들의 발길이나, 이 근처를 어슬렁거리는 늑대며 다른 들짐승들의 발길 말고는 좀처럼, 아니 결코 밟힌 적 없는 이곳에 무슨 일로 왔느냐고 물었다. 산초가 마주 내려오라고 청하며, 모든 것을 그에게 설명해 주겠노라고 했다.\n\n염소지기가 내려와 돈키호테가 서 있는 곳에 이르러 말했다. \"내기를 걸어도 좋소만, 댁들은 저 우묵한 데 죽어 자빠져 있는 짐 노새를 보고 있는 게로구먼. 정말이지 그놈은 벌써 여섯 달째 거기 누워 있다오. 어디 말해 보시오, 이 근처에서 그놈의 주인을 만났소?\"\n\n\"우리는 아무도 만나지 못했소.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"여기서 멀지 않은 데서 발견한 안장 깔개와 자그마한 가방 하나 말고는 아무것도 만나지 못했소.\"\n\n\"나도 그걸 발견했다오.\" 염소지기가 말했다. \"하지만 무슨 불운이 닥칠까, 아니면 도둑으로 몰릴까 두려워서 그걸 들어 올리지도, 가까이 가지도 않았소. 악마란 교활한 놈이라, 영문도 까닭도 모르게 사람을 넘어뜨리려고 발밑에서 물건들이 솟아오르는 법이니 말이오.\"\n\n\"바로 제가 하는 말이 그겁니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"저도 그걸 발견했지만 돌 던질 거리 안으로도 가까이 안 갔습니다요. 거기 그대로 두었으니, 그건 있던 그대로 거기 있을 겝니다요. 저는 방울 달린 개는 원치 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"여보시오, 좋은 양반,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이 물건들의 주인이 누구인지 아시오?\"\n\n\"내가 말해 줄 수 있는 거라곤 이것뿐이오.\" 염소지기가 말했다. \"한 여섯 달쯤 전에, 더 됐을 수도 덜 됐을 수도 있소만, 여기서 한 삼 레과쯤 떨어진 어느 목동의 오두막에, 점잖은 외모와 몸가짐을 한 한 젊은이가 도착했는데, 바로 여기 죽어 있는 저 노새를 타고, 댁들이 발견했으되 손대지 않았다는 바로 그 안장 깔개와 가방을 지니고 있었소. 그가 우리에게 이 시에라에서 가장 험하고 외진 곳이 어디냐고 묻기에, 우리는 지금 우리가 있는 이곳이라고 일러 주었소. 사실이 그러하니, 여기서 반 레과만 더 들어가면 아마 빠져나갈 길을 찾지 못할 것이오. 나는 댁들이 어떻게 여기까지 올 수 있었는지 의아할 따름이오. 이곳으로 이어지는 길이며 오솔길이라곤 없으니 말이오. 어쨌든 그 젊은이는 우리 대답을 듣더니 몸을 돌려 우리가 가리킨 곳으로 향했는데, 우리는 모두 그의 잘생긴 외모에 매혹된 채, 그의 물음과 그가 시에라 쪽으로 떠나는 그 서두름에 의아해하며 남았소. 그러고는 그를 다시 보지 못했는데, 며칠 뒤 우리 목동 하나의 길과 마주쳤다오. 그런데 그에게 한마디 말도 없이 다가오더니 여러 차례 주먹질과 발길질을 하고는, 우리 식량을 실은 당나귀에게 돌아서서 그놈이 싣고 있던 빵과 치즈를 모조리 빼앗았소. 그러고는 그 일을 마치자마자 다시 어마어마한 날쌤으로 시에라 속으로 달아나 버렸소. 우리 염소지기 몇이 이 일을 알고 나서, 이틀가량 이 시에라의 가장 외진 곳을 두루 뒤지며 그를 찾아다녔는데, 끝에 가서 우리는 그가 크고 두꺼운 코르크나무의 빈 속에 깃들어 있는 것을 발견했소. 그가 매우 온순하게 우리를 맞으러 나왔는데, 옷은 이제 다 찢어졌고 얼굴은 햇볕에 어찌나 일그러지고 그을렸던지 우리는 그를 거의 알아보지 못했으나, 비록 찢어졌어도 그 옷이, 우리가 기억하던 것에 비추어, 그가 우리가 찾던 바로 그 사람임을 확인시켜 주었소. 그가 우리에게 정중히 인사하고는, 또박또박 몇 마디 말로, 자기가 이런 몰골로 돌아다니는 것을 의아해하지 말라 하면서, 자신의 많은 죄로 인해 부과된 고행을 치르기 위해 그렇게 하지 않을 수 없노라고 했소. 우리가 그에게 당신이 누구냐고 말해 달라 청했으나, 끝내 그에게서 알아낼 수가 없었소. 또 우리는 그에게, 먹을 것이 궁할 때면 — 그것 없이는 살 수 없으니 — 어디로 가면 그를 찾을 수 있을지 말해 달라 부탁했으니, 우리가 기꺼이 흔쾌히 가져다주겠노라 했소. 만약 그것이 그의 마음에 들지 않거든, 적어도 와서 우리에게 청하고 목동들에게서 강제로 빼앗지는 말아 달라고 했소. 그는 그 제안에 감사하며, 지난번 습격을 용서해 달라 빌고, 앞으로는 누구에게도 폭력을 쓰지 않고 하느님의 이름으로 청하겠노라 약속했소. 정해진 거처로 말하자면, 밤이 자기를 어디서 덮치든 우연이 마련해 주는 곳 말고는 다른 거처가 없다고 했소. 그리고 그의 말은 어찌나 비통한 통곡으로 끝맺었던지, 그의 말을 듣던 우리가 그와 함께 울지 않았다면 돌멩이나 다름없었을 것이오. 우리가 처음 보았을 때의 그와 지금 본 그의 모습을 견주어 보면서 말이오. 앞서 말했듯이 그는 우아하고 품위 있는 젊은이였고, 그 정중하고 세련된 말씨로 보아 좋은 가문 태생에 궁정의 교양을 갖춘 사람임을 드러냈으니, 그의 말을 듣던 우리 같은 촌뜨기들에게조차, 우리 같은 무지렁이에게조차 그의 점잖은 거동만으로 그것이 분명히 드러났소.\n\n\"그런데 그가 이야기를 하던 중에 멈추더니 입을 다물고는, 한동안 눈을 땅바닥에 고정한 채 있었으니, 그동안 우리는 이 넋 나간 모습이 어찌 될지 마음 졸이며 가만히 서서 지켜보았소. 또한 적잖은 연민으로 그리하였으니, 그의 행동으로 보아 — 이제는 눈을 부릅뜨고 눈꺼풀 하나 까딱 않고 땅을 뚫어지게 응시하다가, 다시 눈을 감고는 입술을 꼭 다물고 눈썹을 치켜올리는 것을 보고 — 우리는 어떤 종류의 광기 발작이 그에게 닥쳤음을 똑똑히 알아챌 수 있었소. 그리고 머지않아 그는 우리가 짐작한 것이 사실임을 보여 주었으니, 자기가 몸을 던졌던 땅바닥에서 광분하여 벌떡 일어나, 곁에 있던 첫 번째 사람에게 어찌나 격노와 사나움으로 달려들었던지, 우리가 그를 떼어 놓지 않았다면 그자를 때려죽이거나 물어 죽였을 것이오. 그러면서 내내 이렇게 외쳤소. '오, 신의 없는 페르난도, 여기서, 바로 여기서 네가 내게 저지른 잘못의 대가를 치르리라. 이 두 손이 모든 죄악의, 그중에서도 무엇보다 기만과 사기의 거처요 보금자리인 그 네 심장을 뜯어내리라.' 그러고는 여기에 다른 말들을 덧붙였는데, 모두 이 페르난도를 책망하며 그를 배신과 신의 없음으로 고발하는 내용이었소.\n\n\"우리는 적잖은 어려움 끝에 그가 손을 놓게 만들었고, 그는 더 말 한마디 없이 우리를 떠나, 쏜살같이 달려가 이 덤불과 가시나무 속으로 뛰어들었으니, 우리로서는 그를 뒤따르는 것이 불가능했소. 이로 미루어 우리는 때때로 광기가 그에게 닥치고, 페르난도라는 어떤 자가 그를 그토록 비참한 처지로 몰아넣은 것으로 보아 무언가 심각한 잘못을 그에게 저질렀음이 틀림없다고 짐작하오. 이 모든 것은 그 후로 그가 우리 길과 마주친 여러 번의 — 그런 일이 여러 번 있었소 — 경우에 확인되었으니, 어떤 때는 목동들에게 그들이 지닌 음식을 좀 달라고 청하기도 하고, 또 어떤 때는 그들에게서 강제로 빼앗기도 했소. 광기 발작이 그에게 닥쳤을 때는 목동들이 기꺼이 내주어도 받지 않고 매질을 해서 빼앗지만, 제정신일 때는 하느님의 사랑을 들어 정중하고 예의 바르게 청하며, 숱한 감사와 적잖은 눈물로 그것을 받소. 그리고 사실대로 말씀드리자면, 여러분,\" 염소지기가 말을 이었다. \"우리가, 그러니까 나와 네 명의 젊은이가 — 둘은 우리 하인이고 다른 둘은 내 친구들인데 — 그를 찾을 때까지 찾아 나서기로, 그리고 찾으면 강제로든 그의 동의로든 그를 여기서 여덟 레과 떨어진 알모도바르 마을로 데려가, 거기서 (그의 병이 정말로 치유될 수 있는 것이라면) 그를 고쳐 보거나, 아니면 그가 제정신일 때 그가 누구인지, 그의 불행을 알릴 만한 친척이 있는지 알아보기로 결심한 것이 바로 어제였소. 여러분, 이것이 여러분이 내게 물은 것에 대해 내가 말할 수 있는 전부요. 그리고 여러분이 발견한 물건들의 주인은 바로 그토록 날쌔게, 그토록 헐벗은 채로 지나가는 것을 본 그 사람임을 분명히 알아 두시오.\"\n\n돈키호테는 이미 그 사내가 산비탈을 따라 껑충껑충 뛰어가는 것을 보았노라 이야기한 터였고, 이제 염소지기에게서 들은 이야기에 놀라움으로 가득 차, 그 불행한 광인이 누구인지 그 어느 때보다도 간절히 알아내고 싶어졌다. 그리하여 그는 마음속으로, 전에도 그랬듯이, 그를 찾을 때까지 산 구석구석과 동굴 하나 살피지 않은 데 없이 온 산을 뒤져 그를 찾아내리라 결심했다. 그러나 우연은 그가 예상하거나 바란 것보다 더 잘 일을 마련해 주었으니, 바로 그 순간, 그들이 서 있는 곳으로 열린 산속 협곡에서, 그가 찾고자 하던 그 젊은이가 모습을 드러냈으니, 가까이서도 알아듣기 어려운, 하물며 멀리서는 더욱 그러한 투로 혼잣말을 하며 다가오고 있었다. 그의 차림새는 앞서 묘사한 그대로였으나, 다만 그가 가까이 다가옴에 따라 돈키호테는 그가 입은 누덕누덕한 윗옷이 호박색으로 무두질된 것임을 알아챘고, 이로부터 그런 옷을 입은 자가 그리 미천한 신분일 리 없다고 결론지었다.\n\n그들에게 다가온 젊은이는 거칠고 쉰 목소리로, 그러나 매우 정중하게 그들에게 인사했다. 돈키호테는 똑같이 정중하게 그 인사에 답하고는, 로시난테에서 내려 점잖은 거동과 우아함으로 그를 끌어안으러 나아가, 마치 오랫동안 알고 지내던 사이인 양 한동안 그를 두 팔로 꼭 끌어안았다. 슬픈 몰골의 기사라 불리던 돈키호테처럼 가련한 몰골의 누더기 사내라 부를 만한 그 다른 이는, 그 포옹을 받아들인 뒤 그를 조금 밀쳐 내고는, 두 손을 돈키호테의 어깨에 얹은 채, 마치 그를 아는지 알아보려는 듯 그를 빤히 바라보았으니, 그가 돈키호테의 얼굴과 풍모와 갑옷을 보고 놀란 것은, 어쩌면 돈키호테가 그를 보고 놀란 것에 못지않았다. 간단히 말하자면, 포옹 뒤에 먼저 입을 연 것은 누더기 사내였고, 그가 한 말은 뒤에 이야기될 것이다.\n\n# IN WHICH IS CONTINUED THE ADVENTURE OF THE SIERRA MORENA\n\n이야기에 따르면, 돈키호테는 더없는 주의를 기울여 시에라의 누더기 기사가 하는 말에 귀를 기울였으니, 그 기사는 이렇게 운을 떼었다.\n\n\"분명히 말씀드리건대, 나리, 당신이 누구신지는 모르겠으나, 제게 베풀어 주신 친절과 예의의 증표에 감사드리며, 저를 다정하게 맞아 주신 그 후의에 대해 그저 호의가 아닌 무언가 더한 것으로 보답할 처지가 되었으면 하는 바람입니다. 허나 제 운명은 제게 베풀어진 친절을 갚을 다른 어떤 방도도 허락하지 않으니, 오로지 그것을 갚고자 하는 진심 어린 소망뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"내 운명은,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"당신께 도움이 되고자 하는 것이니, 어찌나 그러한지 나는 당신을 찾아내고, 또 그 기이한 삶으로 보아 당신이 겪고 있는 듯한 그 슬픔에 무슨 위안거리라도 있는지 당신에게서 알아내기 전까지는 이 산을 떠나지 않으리라 결심했고, 만일 찾는 일이 필요했다면 온갖 정성을 다해 당신을 찾았을 것이오. 그리고 만일 당신의 불행이 그 어떤 위로도 받아들이지 않는 종류의 것이라면, 내가 할 수 있는 한 당신과 함께 그것을 탄식하고 애도하는 것이 내 뜻이었소. 불행 가운데서도 그것을 함께 느껴 줄 이를 찾는 것은 그래도 얼마간의 위안이 되는 법이니 말이오. 그리고 만일 나의 선한 뜻이 어떤 예우로든 인정받을 만하다면, 부탁하건대, 나리, 당신이 그토록 높은 정도로 지니신 그것에 걸고, 또한 당신이 삶에서 사랑하거나 가장 사랑했던 그 무엇에 걸고 간청하노니, 당신이 누구이며, 무슨 까닭으로 이 황량한 곳에서 짐승처럼 살거나 죽기에 이르렀는지, 그리고 당신의 옷차림과 행색이 보여 주듯 당신의 신분에 그토록 어울리지 않는 모습으로 이런 곳에 머물게 되었는지를 내게 말해 주시오. 그리고 내 맹세하건대,\" 돈키호테가 덧붙였다. \"내가 받은 기사의 서약에 걸고, 또 편력 기사라는 내 천직에 걸고, 만일 당신이 이 청을 들어준다면, 내 소명이 요구하는 온 열의를 다해 당신을 섬기리니, 당신의 불행이 구제될 수 있다면 그것을 덜어 주는 일에서든, 아니면 내가 약속한 대로 당신과 함께 그것을 탄식하는 일에서든 그리하겠소.\"\n\n덤불의 기사는 슬픈 몰골의 기사가 이런 식으로 말하는 것을 듣고는 그저 그를 빤히 바라보고, 또 빤히 바라보고, 다시 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그를 훑어볼 뿐이었다. 그리고 그를 샅샅이 살펴본 뒤 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"제게 먹을 것을 무엇이든 주실 수 있다면, 부디 그것을 주십시오. 먹고 난 다음에는 제게 베풀어 주신 호의에 보답하고자 당신이 청하시는 바를 모두 하겠습니다.\"\n\n산초는 자기 자루에서, 염소지기는 자기 주머니에서 누더기 사내에게 허기를 달랠 거리를 꺼내 주었고, 그는 그들이 준 것을 얼빠진 자처럼 먹어 댔는데, 어찌나 허겁지겁 먹는지 한입과 다음 한입 사이에 짬도 두지 않고 삼킨다기보다 게걸스레 처넣었다. 그가 먹는 동안 그도, 그를 지켜보던 이들도 한마디도 입을 떼지 않았다. 다 먹자마자 그는 자기를 따라오라는 손짓을 했고, 그들이 그렇게 하자 그는 그들을 조금 더 떨어진 바위 모퉁이를 돌아 있는 푸른 풀밭으로 데려갔다. 그곳에 다다르자 그는 풀밭 위에 몸을 뉘었고, 다른 이들도 그리하여 모두가 침묵을 지켰는데, 마침내 누더기 사내가 제자리를 잡고 앉아 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"여러분, 제 불행의 그 엄청난 깊이를 몇 마디로 털어놓기를 바라신다면, 어떤 질문이나 다른 끼어듦으로도 제 슬픈 이야기의 실타래를 끊지 않겠다고 약속하셔야 합니다. 그렇게 하시는 순간 제가 들려드리는 이야기는 끝나 버릴 테니까요.\"\n\n누더기 사내의 이 말은 돈키호테에게 자기 종자가 들려주었던 이야기, 곧 강을 건넌 염소들의 수를 헤아리지 못해 끝맺지 못한 채 남았던 그 이야기를 떠올리게 했다. 그러나 다시 누더기 사내로 돌아가자면, 그는 이렇게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"제가 이런 당부를 드리는 것은 제 불행의 이야기를 짤막하게 지나가고 싶어서입니다. 그것들을 기억에 되살리는 일은 그저 새로운 불행을 더할 뿐이니까요. 그리고 여러분이 제게 덜 물으실수록 저는 더 빨리 이 이야기를 끝맺을 수 있을 겁니다. 다만 여러분의 호기심을 온전히 채워 드리기 위해 무엇이든 중요한 것은 빠뜨리지 않고 들려드리겠습니다.\"\n\n돈키호테는 자신과 다른 이들을 대신해 그러겠노라 약속했고, 이렇게 다짐을 받자 그는 다음과 같이 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n\"제 이름은 카르데니오이고, 제가 태어난 곳은 이 안달루시아에서 손꼽히는 도시들 가운데 하나이며, 제 가문은 고귀하고, 제 부모는 부유하며, 제 불행은 어찌나 큰지 제 부모는 틀림없이 그것을 두고 눈물 흘렸을 것이고 제 가문은 그것을 슬퍼했을 것이나, 그 재력으로도 그것을 덜어 줄 수는 없었습니다. 하늘이 내린 역경을 덜어 주는 데에 운명의 선물이 할 수 있는 일이란 거의 없으니까요. 같은 그 고장에 사랑이 제가 바랄 수 있는 온갖 영광을 두어 둔 하늘이 하나 있었으니, 바로 루신다의 아름다움이 그러했습니다. 그녀는 저만큼이나 고귀하고 부유한 처녀였으나, 운명은 더 행복했고, 저의 그토록 값진 열정에 어울릴 만큼의 굳건함은 덜한 처녀였습니다. 이 루신다를 저는 가장 어리고 여린 시절부터 사랑하고 흠모하고 떠받들었으며, 그녀도 어린아이의 온갖 순진함과 진심으로 저를 사랑했습니다. 우리 부모들은 우리의 마음을 알고 있었고, 그것을 알아차리고도 언짢아하지 않았으니, 그 마음이 무르익으면 마침내 우리 둘의 혼인으로 이어지리라는 것을 분명히 보았기 때문입니다. 그것은 우리 두 집안과 재산이 대등하기에 거의 미리 정해진 일처럼 여겨졌습니다. 우리는 자랐고, 우리가 자라남에 따라 우리 사이의 사랑도 자라났으니, 그리하여 루신다의 아버지는 체면을 지키기 위해 제가 자기 집에 드나드는 것을 막아야겠다고 느꼈는데, 어쩌면 이 일로 시인들이 그토록 기린 저 티스베의 부모를 흉내 낸 셈이었습니다. 그런데 이 거절은 사랑에 사랑을, 불꽃에 불꽃을 더할 뿐이었으니, 그들이 우리의 혀에는 침묵을 강요할 수 있었어도 우리의 펜에는 그것을 강요할 수 없었기 때문입니다. 펜은 혀보다도 더 거리낌 없이 마음속 비밀을 사랑하는 이에게 알릴 수 있는 법이니, 사랑하는 대상이 곁에 있으면 그 존재가 더없이 굳건한 의지를 흔들어 놓고 가장 대담한 혀마저 말문을 막아 버리는 일이 여러 번이기 때문입니다. 아, 하늘이시여! 제가 그녀에게 얼마나 많은 편지를 썼으며, 또 얼마나 많은 곱고도 정숙한 답장을 받았던지요! 제가 얼마나 많은 노래와 사랑의 시를 지었던지요. 그 안에서 제 마음은 자신의 감정을 토로하고 알렸으며, 자신의 타오르는 갈망을 그렸고, 자신의 추억에 흠뻑 젖었으며, 자신의 욕망과 어울려 노닐었습니다! 마침내 더는 견디지 못하고 그녀를 보고픈 그리움에 제 마음이 시들어 가는 것을 느낀 저는, 제가 바라고 또 받아 마땅하다 여긴 보상을 얻을 최선의 길이라 생각된 바를 실행에 옮기기로 결심했으니, 곧 그녀의 아버지께 그녀를 제 정식 아내로 달라 청하는 것이었고, 저는 그렇게 했습니다. 이에 대한 그분의 대답인즉, 자신을 영예롭게 하고 또 그분의 보배를 받음으로써 제 자신이 영예를 입었다 여기려는 제 마음씨에는 감사하나, 제 아버지가 살아 계시니 이 청을 하는 것은 그분의 권리이며, 그것이 그분의 온전한 뜻과 흡족함에 따른 것이 아니라면 루신다는 몰래 데려가거나 내줄 수 있는 사람이 아니라는 것이었습니다. 저는 그분의 친절에 감사드리며 그분 말씀에 일리가 있다고, 또 제 아버지께서도 제가 말씀드리는 즉시 동의하시리라 생각했고, 그런 마음으로 바로 그 순간 제 소망이 무엇인지 알려 드리려 아버지께 갔습니다. 그분이 계신 방에 들어섰을 때 저는 그분이 펼쳐진 편지 한 통을 손에 들고 계신 것을 보았는데, 제가 미처 한마디 꺼내기도 전에 그분은 그것을 제게 건네며 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다. '카르데니오야, 이 편지로 너는 리카르도 공작께서 너를 위해 베푸시려는 호의를 알게 될 것이다.' 이 리카르도 공작은, 여러분도 이미 아실 테지만, 이 안달루시아에서 가장 좋은 곳에 영지를 둔 스페인의 대공입니다. 저는 그 편지를 받아 읽었는데, 어찌나 입에 발린 말로 쓰였던지 저 자신조차 제 아버지께서 그 안에 적힌 공작의 청에 응하지 않으시는 것이 잘못이라 느껴질 정도였습니다. 그 청인즉, 저를 즉시 자신에게 보내 달라는 것이었으니, 공작은 저를 자기 맏아들의 하인이 아니라 벗으로 삼고자 하셨고, 저를 높이 여기시는 그 존중에 걸맞은 자리에 앉히는 일을 손수 떠맡으시겠다는 것이었습니다. 그 편지를 읽으며 저는 목소리가 나오지 않았고, 제 아버지께서 이렇게 말씀하시는 것을 들었을 때는 더욱 그러했습니다. '카르데니오야, 이틀 뒤에 너는 공작의 뜻에 따라 떠날 것이니, 네가 받아 마땅하다고 내가 아는 바를 얻을 길을 너에게 열어 주시는 하느님께 감사드려라.' 그리고 이 말씀에 아버지로서의 다른 충고들을 덧붙이셨습니다. 제가 떠날 때가 다가왔습니다. 저는 어느 날 밤 루신다에게 말하여 일어난 모든 일을 들려주었고, 그녀의 아버지께도 그렇게 하면서 얼마간 미루어 주시기를, 그리고 리카르도 공작이 제게 무엇을 바라는지 알게 될 때까지 그녀의 혼사를 늦추어 주시기를 간청했습니다. 그분은 제게 약속하셨고, 그녀는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 맹세와 까무러침으로 그것을 굳게 다짐했습니다. 마침내 저는 공작 앞에 나아갔고, 그분에게 어찌나 친절히 맞아지고 대접받았던지 머지않아 시기가 그 일을 시작했으니, 오래된 하인들이 저를 시샘하며 공작이 제게 호의를 보이려는 마음을 자기들에 대한 모욕으로 여겼습니다. 그러나 저의 도착을 가장 기뻐한 이는 공작의 둘째 아들로, 페르난도라는 이름의 늠름한 젊은이였는데, 고귀하고 너그러우며 다정다감한 천성을 지녀 머지않아 저를 어찌나 가까운 벗으로 삼았던지 모두가 그것을 입에 올릴 정도였습니다. 맏아들도 제게 정을 두고 친절을 베풀었으나, 돈 페르난도만큼 그 다정한 대접을 멀리까지 끌고 가지는 않았기 때문입니다. 그러던 차에, 벗들 사이에는 함께 나누지 못할 비밀이 없는 법이고, 또 제가 돈 페르난도에게서 누리던 총애가 우정으로 자라난 터라, 그는 제게 자신의 온갖 생각을 털어놓았는데, 특히 그의 마음을 조금 어지럽히던 어떤 연정에 관한 것이었습니다. 그는 어느 농촌 처녀, 곧 자기 아버지의 영지에 딸린 백성으로 부유한 부모의 딸이며, 그 자신이 어찌나 아름답고 정숙하며 분별 있고 정숙한지 그녀를 아는 누구도 그녀가 이 가운데 어느 점에서 가장 빼어나게 타고났는지, 어느 점에서 가장 뛰어난지 가늠할 수 없는 처녀와 깊은 사랑에 빠져 있었습니다. 그 아리따운 농촌 처녀의 매력은 돈 페르난도의 열정을 어찌나 부추겼던지, 그는 자기 목적을 이루고 그녀의 정숙한 결심을 꺾기 위해 그녀에게 남편이 되겠다는 약조를 하기로 마음먹었으니, 다른 어떤 방법으로 시도하는 것은 불가능한 일을 시도하는 셈이었기 때문입니다. 우정으로 그에게 매여 있던 저는 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 좋은 논리와 가장 설득력 있는 본보기를 들어 그를 그런 길에서 말리고 단념시키려 애썼습니다. 그러나 제가 아무런 효과도 거두지 못하는 것을 알고는 그의 아버지 리카르도 공작께 이 일을 알리기로 결심했습니다. 그런데 돈 페르난도는 영리하고 약빠른지라 이를 미리 내다보고 염려했으니, 충실한 하인으로서의 제 도리상 제가 제 주군이신 공작의 명예에 그토록 어긋나는 일을 숨겨 두지 않으리라는 것을 알아챘기 때문입니다. 그리하여 저를 호리고 속이려고 그는, 자기를 그토록 노예로 만든 그 아름다움을 마음에서 지우는 데에 몇 달 동안 그곳을 떠나 있는 것보다 더 좋은 길은 없겠다고 제게 말했고, 그 떠나 있음을 우리 둘이 함께 제 아버지의 집으로 가는 식으로 이루고 싶다며, 그 핑계로 — 공작께는 이렇게 둘러대겠다고 했습니다 — 세상에서 가장 좋은 말을 내는 제 고향 도시에 있는 몇 마리 훌륭한 말을 보고 사러 간다고 하겠다는 것이었습니다. 그가 그렇게 말하는 것을 들었을 때, 설령 그의 결심이 그토록 좋은 것이 아니었다 해도 저는 제 애정에 이끌려 그것을 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 행복한 결심 가운데 하나로 반겼을 것이니, 그것이 제게 제 루신다를 다시 볼 더없이 좋은 기회와 호기를 마련해 주는 것을 보았기 때문입니다. 이런 생각과 바람으로 저는 그의 착상을 칭찬하고 그의 계획을 부추기며, 참으로 떠나 있음이 가장 깊이 뿌리내린 감정조차 누르고 효력을 발휘하니 되도록 빨리 그것을 실행에 옮기라고 권했습니다. 그러나 나중에 드러난 바로는, 그가 제게 이런 말을 했을 때 그는 이미 남편이라는 명목으로 그 농촌 처녀를 차지한 뒤였고, 자기 어리석음을 알게 되었을 때 아버지 공작이 어찌하실지 두려워하며 자신에게 안전하게 그 일을 알릴 기회를 기다리고 있었던 것입니다. 그러니 젊은이들에게 사랑이란 대개 욕정에 지나지 않아, 그 마지막 목적이 향락인지라 그것을 얻고 나면 끝나 버리고, 사랑처럼 보이던 것은 달아나 버리는 법이니, 그것은 자연이 정해 놓은 한계를 넘지 못하기 때문이며, 그 자연은 참사랑에는 아무런 한계도 두지 않는 법입니다. 제가 말하려는 바는, 돈 페르난도가 이 농촌 처녀를 차지하고 난 뒤 그의 열정은 가라앉고 그의 갈망은 식어 버렸으니, 처음에는 자기 사랑을 고치려 떠나 있고 싶은 척했으나, 이제는 실은 자기 약속을 지키지 않으려고 떠나기를 안달하고 있었다는 것입니다.\n\n\"공작은 그에게 허락하고 제게 그를 따라가라 명하셨습니다. 우리는 제 고향 도시에 도착했고, 제 아버지는 그의 신분에 걸맞은 대접으로 그를 맞이하셨습니다. 저는 지체 없이 루신다를 보았고, 제 사랑은 죽지도 시들지도 않았으나 새로운 생명을 얻었습니다. 슬프게도 저는 그 이야기를 돈 페르난도에게 들려주었으니, 그가 제게 품은 그 두터운 우정에 비추어 그에게는 무엇도 숨기지 말아야 한다고 여겼기 때문입니다. 저는 그녀의 아름다움과 명랑함과 재치를 어찌나 뜨겁게 추켜세웠던지, 제 찬사가 그의 마음에 그토록 매력으로 단장된 처녀를 보고 싶다는 욕망을 불러일으켰습니다. 불행히도 저는 그것에 굴복하여, 어느 날 밤 우리가 늘 서로 이야기를 나누던 창가에서 등잔불 빛으로 그녀를 그에게 보여 주었습니다. 그녀가 잠옷 차림으로 그에게 나타나자, 그녀는 그가 그때까지 보았던 모든 미인을 그의 기억에서 몰아내 버렸습니다. 그는 말문이 막히고, 머리가 어질해지고, 넋이 나갔으며, 마침내는 사랑에 사로잡혔으니, 이는 제 불행의 이야기가 흘러가는 가운데 보시게 될 것입니다. 그리고 그가 제게는 숨기고 오직 하늘에게만 드러낸 그의 열정을 한층 더 타오르게 하려는 듯, 어느 날 그는 우연히 그녀가 쓴 쪽지를 발견했는데, 거기에는 그녀의 아버지께 그녀를 아내로 달라 청해 달라는 간청이 어찌나 섬세하고 정숙하며 다정하게 적혀 있던지, 그것을 읽고 그는 제게 말하기를, 세상 모든 다른 여인들에게 나뉘어 있는 아름다움과 슬기의 온갖 매력이 오직 루신다 한 사람에게 한데 모여 있다고 했습니다. 사실이고, 이제 와 저는 그것을 인정하건대, 돈 페르난도가 루신다를 칭송할 만한 합당한 까닭을 제가 알고 있었음에도, 그의 입에서 이런 찬사를 듣는 것은 제게 불안을 안겨 주었고, 저는 그를 두려워하며 또 그를 미덥지 못하게 여길 만한 까닭이 있어 그렇게 느끼기 시작했으니, 그가 루신다를 입에 올릴 채비가 되어 있지 않은 순간이 없었고, 어울리지 않게 억지로 끌어들여서라도 그 화제를 자기가 꺼내곤 했기 때문인데, 이는 제 안에 얼마간의 질투를 불러일으킨 사정이었습니다. 루신다의 한결같음이나 신의에 어떤 변함이 있을까 두려워서가 아니었습니다. 그래도 제 운명은 그녀가 제게 그런 일은 없으리라 다짐한 바로 그것을 예감하게 만들었습니다. 돈 페르난도는 제가 루신다에게 보내는 편지와 그녀가 제게 보내는 답장을, 그 둘의 재치와 분별을 즐긴다는 핑계로 언제나 용케 읽어 보았습니다. 그러던 차에, 루신다가 제게 자기가 무척 좋아하는 기사도 책 한 권, 곧 『아마디스 데 가울라』를 읽게 빌려 달라고 청한 일이 있었는데—\"\n\n돈키호테는 기사도 책이 언급되는 것을 듣자마자 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"댁께서 이야기를 시작할 적에 루신다 아가씨가 기사도 책을 좋아한다고 내게 일러 주셨더라면, 그녀의 빼어난 지성을 내게 새겨 주는 데에 다른 어떤 칭송도 필요치 않았을 것이오. 그토록 즐거운 읽을거리에 대한 취미가 없었다면 댁이 그리는 그 탁월함을 지녔을 리 없으니 말이오. 그러니 나로 말하자면, 댁은 그녀의 아름다움과 됨됨이와 지성을 그리는 데에 더는 말을 허비할 필요가 없소. 그저 그녀의 취미가 무엇인지를 듣는 것만으로도 나는 그녀가 세상에서 가장 아름답고 가장 지성 있는 여인이라 선언하는 바이오. 그리고 댁께서 『아마디스 데 가울라』와 더불어 그녀에게 저 훌륭한 『그리스의 돈 루헬』도 보내 주셨더라면 좋았을 것을. 루신다 아가씨라면 다라이다와 가라야를, 또 목동 다리넬의 슬기로운 말들을, 그리고 그가 어찌나 발랄하고 재치 있고 거침없이 노래하고 읊었던지 그 감탄스러운 전원시의 시구들을 크게 즐겼으리라는 것을 내가 아니까 말이오. 허나 이 빠뜨림을 바로잡을 때가 언젠가 올지도 모르니, 그것을 바로잡는 데에는 댁께서 부디 나와 함께 내 마을로 와 주시기만 하면 더 바랄 것이 없소. 거기서 나는 댁께 내 영혼의 기쁨이요 내 삶의 즐거움인 책을 삼백 권도 넘게 드릴 수 있으니까요. 허나 사악하고 시기심 많은 마법사들의 심술 탓에 지금은 그 가운데 한 권도 내게 남아 있지 않다는 생각이 드는구려. 어쨌든 댁의 이야기를 끊지 않겠다고 우리가 한 약속을 깬 것을 용서하시오. 기사도나 편력 기사 이야기가 들릴 때면, 나는 해의 빛살이 열기를 내지 않고는 못 배기고 달의 빛살이 습기를 내지 않고는 못 배기듯, 그것을 입에 올리지 않고는 못 배기니 말이오. 그러니 용서하고 계속하시오. 지금은 그것이 더 긴요한 일이니.\"\n\n돈키호테가 이렇게 말하는 동안, 카르데니오는 고개를 가슴께로 떨구고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯했다. 그리고 돈키호테가 이야기를 계속하라고 두 번이나 일렀건만, 그는 고개를 들지도, 대꾸 한마디 하지도 않았다. 그러나 얼마쯤 뒤 그는 고개를 들고 말했다. \"그 생각을 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없고, 세상 누구도 그것을 없애거나 나를 달리 생각하게 만들지 못할 것이오. 그리고 저 천하의 악당 엘리사밧 선생이 마다시마 왕비와 놀아났다는 것 말고 다른 것을 우기거나 믿는 자는 멍청이일 것이오.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이 아니오, 온갖 선한 것에 걸고 말이오.\" 돈키호테가 평소 버릇대로 그에게 사납게 돌아서며 격분하여 말했다. \"그건 아주 크나큰 비방, 아니 차라리 악행이오. 마다시마 왕비는 더없이 고귀한 부인이셨으니, 그토록 지체 높은 공주께서 돌팔이 따위와 놀아났으리라고는 생각할 수 없소. 그 반대를 우기는 자는 누구든 천하의 악당처럼 거짓말을 하는 것이며, 나는 그자에게 그것을 알게 해 주겠소. 걸어서든 말 위에서든, 무장을 했든 안 했든, 밤이든 낮이든, 그자가 가장 좋아하는 대로 말이오.\"\n\n카르데니오는 그를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었는데, 이제 그의 미친 발작이 도진지라 이야기를 계속할 마음이 없었고, 돈키호테 또한 마다시마에 관해 들은 바에 어찌나 비위가 상했던지 그것을 들어 줄 리 없었다. 묘하게도 그는 그녀가 정말 자기의 진짜 타고난 귀부인이라도 되는 양 그녀를 두둔했으니, 그 불경한 책들이 그를 이 지경까지 몰고 온 것이었다. 그리하여 앞서 말했듯 이제 미쳐 버린 카르데니오는, 자기가 거짓말쟁이로 몰리고 악당이며 그 밖의 모욕적인 이름으로 불리는 것을 듣자, 그 농을 달가워하지 않고 곁에 있던 돌멩이 하나를 집어 들어 그것으로 돈키호테의 가슴을 어찌나 세게 후려쳤던지 그를 벌렁 자빠뜨려 놓았다. 산초 판사는 제 주인이 이런 꼴을 당하는 것을 보고 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 그 미치광이에게 덤벼들었으나, 누더기 사내가 어찌나 잘 맞아 주었던지 주먹 한 방으로 그를 제 발치에 뻗어 놓고는, 그 위에 올라타 제 분이 풀릴 때까지 그의 갈비뼈를 짓이겼다. 구하러 온 염소지기도 같은 운명을 겪었다. 그리고 그들을 모조리 두들기고 패고 나서 그는 그들을 버려두고 산속 자기 은신처로 조용히 물러갔다. 산초는 일어나, 받을 까닭도 없이 그토록 두들겨 맞은 것에 대한 분노로 염소지기에게 앙갚음하려 달려들었으니, 이 사내가 이따금 미친 발작을 일으킨다고 미리 일러 주지 않았다며 그를 나무랐다. 알았더라면 자기들을 지키려 경계했을 것이라면서 말이다. 염소지기는 자기가 그렇게 말했노라고, 그리고 산초가 그것을 듣지 못했다면 그것은 자기 잘못이 아니라고 대꾸했다. 산초가 되받아치고 염소지기가 다시 응수하니, 그 말다툼은 끝내 서로 수염을 움켜잡고 어찌나 주먹질을 주고받는 것으로 끝났던지, 돈키호테가 둘 사이를 말리지 않았더라면 그들은 서로를 산산조각 내고 말았을 것이다.\n\n\"내버려 두십시오, 슬픈 몰골의 기사님.\" 산초가 염소지기와 맞붙은 채 말했다. \"이 녀석은 저와 똑같은 촌놈이지 작위 받은 기사가 아니니, 제게 가한 모욕에 대해서는 정직한 사내답게 이놈과 맨손으로 맞붙어 싸워 제가 마음 놓고 분풀이를 할 수 있습니다요.\"\n\n\"그건 옳은 말이오.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 일어난 일에 대해서는 저 사람에게 잘못이 없다는 것을 내가 아오.\"\n\n이 말로 그는 그들을 달랜 뒤, 다시 염소지기에게 카르데니오를 찾아낼 수 있겠느냐고 물었으니, 그의 이야기의 결말을 알고 싶은 마음이 더없이 간절했기 때문이다. 염소지기는 앞서 그에게 일렀던 대로, 그의 소굴이 어디인지 확실히 알 길은 없으나, 그 부근을 많이 돌아다니다 보면 제정신이든 아니든 그와 마주치지 않을 수 없을 것이라고 말했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXV.\n\n시에라 모레나에서 라만차의 용맹한 기사에게 일어난 기이한 일들과, 그가 벨테네브로스의 고행을 본떠 행한 일에 관하여\n\n돈키호테는 염소지기에게 작별을 고하고 다시 로시난테에 올라 산초에게 따라오라 명했는데, 당나귀가 없는 산초는 몹시 못마땅한 기색으로 그 뒤를 따랐다. 두 사람은 천천히 나아가 산속의 가장 험한 곳으로 들어섰는데, 그동안 산초는 주인과 이야기를 나누고 싶어 죽을 지경이라, 자신에게 내려진 그 함구령을 어기지 않으려고 주인이 먼저 입을 열어 주기만을 애타게 기다렸다. 그러나 그토록 오래 침묵을 지킬 수가 없어 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n\"돈키호테 나리, 제게 축복과 작별 인사를 내려 주십시오. 저는 당장에라도 집으로 돌아가 마누라와 자식들 곁으로 가고 싶습니다. 거기서는 적어도 제가 하고 싶은 만큼 마음껏 떠들고 이야기를 나눌 수 있으니까요. 밤낮없이 이런 황량한 곳을 헤매면서 하고 싶은 말이 있어도 나리께 말 한마디 못 하게 하시는 건 산 사람을 생매장하는 것이나 다름없습니다. 만약 운이 좋아서 기소페테 시절처럼 짐승들이 말을 할 줄 안다면야 이렇게까지 나쁘진 않을 겁니다. 그러면 머릿속에 떠오르는 건 뭐든 로시난테한테 떠들어 대면서 제 박복한 신세를 견뎌 낼 수 있을 테니까요. 하지만 평생 모험을 찾아 헤매면서 얻는 거라곤 발길질에 담요 키질에, 벽돌 세례에 주먹질뿐인데, 거기다 벙어리라도 된 것처럼 가슴속에 있는 말도 감히 못 하고 입을 꿰매고 다녀야 한다니, 이건 도무지 참을 수 없는 고약한 일입니다.\"\n\n\"네 마음을 알겠다, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내가 네 혀에 내린 금령을 풀고 싶어 죽을 지경이로구나. 그 금령은 풀린 것으로 여겨라. 그러니 우리가 이 산속을 헤매는 동안에는 하고 싶은 말을 하거라.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하지요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"지금 말하게 해 주십시오. 이따가 무슨 일이 벌어질지는 하느님만 아실 일이니까요. 그럼 당장 그 허락을 써먹어서 여쭙겠습니다. 대체 나리께서는 무엇 때문에 그 마히마사 여왕인지 뭔지 하는 여인 편을 그토록 들어 주셨으며, 그 수도원장이 그 여인의 친구였든 아니었든 나리와 무슨 상관이 있단 말입니까? 만약 나리께서 그 일을 그냥 지나치셨더라면 — 나리께서 그 일의 재판관도 아니셨으니 — 제 생각엔 그 미치광이가 자기 이야기를 계속했을 테고, 돌멩이에 얻어맞는 일도, 발길질도, 대여섯 대도 넘는 주먹질도 다 피할 수 있었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"마다시마 여왕이 얼마나 고귀하고 명망 높은 부인이었는지 나만큼 네가 알았더라면, 그런 모욕적인 말을 내뱉은 그 입을 산산조각 내지 않은 내가 참으로 인내심이 깊었다고 너도 말할 것이다. 여왕이 외과 의사와 부정을 저질렀다고 말하거나 상상하는 것은 실로 엄청난 모독이니라. 이야기의 진실은 이렇다. 그 미치광이가 입에 올린 엘리사밧 선생이라는 분은 신중하고 분별 있는 사람으로, 여왕의 시종이자 의사로 봉직했을 뿐이다. 그런데 그가 여왕의 정부였다고 짐작하는 것은 지극히 엄한 벌을 받아 마땅한 헛소리이니라. 그리고 카르데니오가 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지조차 몰랐다는 증거로는, 그가 그 말을 할 때 제정신이 아니었다는 사실을 떠올려 보아라.\"\n\n\"제 말이 바로 그겁니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"미치광이의 말 따위에 신경 쓸 필요가 없었다는 거지요. 만약 행운이 나리를 돕지 않아서 그자가 나리의 가슴이 아니라 머리에다 그 돌을 던졌더라면, 저기 그 마님 편을 들어 준 대가로 우리 꼴이 참 볼만했을 겁니다. 하느님이 그 여인을 벌하시기를! 그러고 나면 카르데니오는 미치광이라고 무사히 풀려나지 않았겠습니까?\"\n\n\"제정신인 자들에 맞서든 미치광이들에 맞서든,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"편력 기사라면 누구나 여인의 명예를 지켜야 할 의무가 있느니라. 그 여인이 누구든 말이다. 하물며 마다시마 여왕처럼 그토록 높은 신분과 위엄을 갖춘 여왕이라면 더더욱 그러하니, 나는 그분의 어진 품성 때문에 그분을 각별히 존경하느니라. 그분은 빼어나게 아름다울 뿐만 아니라 매우 지혜로웠으며, 숱한 불행 속에서도 참으로 인내심이 깊으셨다. 그리고 엘리사밧 선생의 조언과 벗함은 그분이 그 고난을 지혜와 체념으로 견뎌 내는 데 큰 도움이자 버팀목이 되어 주었다. 바로 이 때문에 무지하고 심술궂은 무리가 그분이 그의 정부라고 떠들고 생각할 빌미를 잡은 것이다. 다시 한번 말하노니, 그리고 이백 번을 더 말하더라도, 그렇게 생각하고 말하는 자들은 모두 거짓말을 하는 것이니라.\"\n\n\"저는 그렇게 말하지도 생각하지도 않습니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그건 그자들이 알아서 할 일이지요. 자기들이 만든 빵은 자기들이 먹는 법입니다. 잘못을 저질렀든 아니든 그자들은 이미 하느님께 셈을 치렀을 테고요. 저는 제 포도밭에서 온 사람이라 아무것도 모릅니다. 남의 인생을 캐고 다니는 건 좋아하지도 않고요. 사서 거짓말하는 자는 제 지갑으로 그 대가를 치르는 법이지요. 게다가 저는 벌거숭이로 태어나 지금도 벌거숭이 신세니, 잃을 것도 얻을 것도 없습니다. 하지만 그자들이 정말 그랬다 한들 그게 저와 무슨 상관입니까? 갈고리도 없는데 베이컨이 걸려 있는 줄 아는 사람이 많지요. 그렇지만 누가 탁 트인 벌판에 문을 달 수 있겠습니까? 더구나 하느님에 대해서도 사람들은 이러쿵저러쿵—\"\n\n\"이런, 맙소사.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"대체 무슨 그런 말도 안 되는 소리를 줄줄이 늘어놓는 게냐! 우리가 지금 하는 이야기가 네가 하나씩 꿰어 대는 그 속담들과 무슨 상관이 있단 말이냐? 제발 입 좀 다물어라, 산초. 그리고 앞으로는 네 당나귀나 몰면서 너와 상관없는 일에는 끼어들지 마라. 그리고 네 다섯 가지 감각을 다 동원해서 알아 두어라. 내가 해 온 일, 하고 있는 일, 앞으로 할 일은 모두 이치에 단단히 뿌리박고 있으며 기사도의 법도에 들어맞느니라. 나는 그 법도를 세상의 어느 기사보다도 더 잘 알기 때문이니라.\"\n\n\"나리,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"길도 없는 이 산속을 헤매면서 미치광이를 찾아다니는 게 그래 좋은 기사도의 법도란 말입니까? 게다가 그자를 찾아내면 어쩌면 제 멋대로 끝맺음을 하려 들지도 모릅니다. 자기 이야기 말고요, 나리의 머리통이며 제 갈비뼈를 말입니다. 그래서 결국 우리 둘을 아주 박살 내 버릴지도 모르지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"다시 한번 말하노니 조용히 하거라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 너에게 말해 두건대, 나를 이 지역으로 이끄는 것은 그 미치광이를 찾으려는 마음이라기보다는, 이곳에서 한 가지 위업을 이루어 온 세상에 영원한 이름과 명성을 떨치려는 마음이니라. 그리고 그것은 편력 기사를 완전하고 명성 높게 만드는 데 필요한 모든 것에 내가 인장을 찍게 될 만큼 대단한 일이 될 것이다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 위업이라는 게 아주 위험한 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아니다.\" 슬픈 몰골의 기사가 대답했다. \"비록 주사위에서 육 대신 한둘이 나올 수도 있겠으나, 모든 것은 네 부지런함에 달려 있느니라.\"\n\n\"제 부지런함에요!\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 내가 보낼 곳에서 일찍 돌아온다면, 내 고행도 곧 끝날 것이고, 내 영광도 곧 시작될 것이기 때문이다. 그러나 내 말이 무슨 결과를 낳을지 보려고 너를 더 오래 애태우게 하는 것은 옳지 못하니, 산초야, 네가 알아 두어야 할 것이 있다. 그 유명한 갈리아의 아마디스는 가장 완벽한 편력 기사 가운데 하나였으니 — 아니, 하나였다고 말하는 것은 잘못이로구나. 그는 홀로 우뚝 섰으니, 으뜸이요, 유일한 존재요, 자기 시대에 세상에 있던 모든 이의 군주였느니라. 돈 벨리아니스 따위는 아무것도 아니며, 그가 어떤 점에서든 아마디스에 견줄 만하다고 말하는 자들도 다 마찬가지다. 내 맹세코, 그자들은 스스로를 속이고 있느니라! 또 내가 말하건대, 화가가 자기 기예로 이름을 떨치고자 할 때는 자기가 아는 가장 빼어난 화가들의 원작을 본뜨려고 애쓰는 법이다. 그리고 이 법칙은 나라를 빛내는 데 이바지하는 가장 중요한 모든 기예와 직업에도 똑같이 적용되느니라. 그러니 신중하고 인내심 깊다는 평을 듣고자 하는 자는 율리시스를 본떠야 하니, 호메로스는 그 인물과 그의 노고를 통해 신중함과 인내의 생생한 그림을 우리에게 보여 주었다. 베르길리우스 또한 아이네아스라는 인물을 통해 효성스러운 아들의 미덕과 용맹하고 노련한 장수의 지혜를 우리에게 보여 주었으니, 그들을 있는 그대로가 아니라 마땅히 있어야 할 모습으로 그리고 묘사함으로써 그 미덕의 본보기를 후세에 남긴 것이니라. 이와 마찬가지로 아마디스는 용맹하고 헌신적인 기사들의 북극성이요, 샛별이요, 태양이었으니, 사랑과 기사도의 깃발 아래 싸우는 우리 모두는 그를 본받아야 하느니라. 그러하니, 친구 산초야, 나는 그를 가장 가까이 본받는 편력 기사야말로 기사도의 완성에 가장 가까이 다가가는 자라고 여기노라. 그런데 이 기사가 자신의 신중함과 가치, 용맹, 인내, 불굴, 사랑을 가장 두드러지게 보여 준 본보기 가운데 하나는, 오리아나 부인에게 거절당하자 물러나 페냐 포브레에서 고행을 하며 자기 이름을 벨테네브로스로 바꾸었을 때이니, 그 이름이야말로 그가 스스로 택한 삶에 어울리는 뜻깊고 적절한 것이었느니라. 그러니 거인을 두 동강 내고, 뱀의 머리를 베고, 용을 죽이고, 군대를 무찌르고, 함대를 부수고, 마법을 푸는 일보다는 이 일에서 그를 본받는 것이 내게 더 쉬운 일이요, 또 이곳이 그런 목적에 더없이 잘 들어맞으니, 지금 이렇게 편리하게 앞머리를 내미는 이 기회를 놓쳐서는 안 되겠노라.\"\n\n\"나리께서는 이런 외진 곳에서 실제로 무엇을 하시려는 겁니까?\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"내 너에게 말하지 않았더냐.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내가 여기서 아마디스를 본받아 절망에 빠진 자, 미치광이, 광인 노릇을 하려 한다고 말이다. 그러면서 동시에 용맹한 돈 롤단도 본받으려 하노라. 그는 샘가에서 아름다운 안젤리카가 메도로와 함께 몸을 더럽혔다는 증거를 보고는, 그 슬픔에 미쳐 버려 나무를 뽑고, 맑은 샘물을 휘젓고, 양치기들을 죽이고, 가축 떼를 망치고, 오두막을 불태우고, 집들을 허물고, 암말들을 끌고 다니며, 영원히 기리고 기록할 만한 수만 가지 폭거를 저질렀느니라. 비록 내가 롤단, 혹은 오를란도, 혹은 로톨란도(그는 이 모든 이름으로 불렸느니라)가 행하고 말하고 생각한 그 모든 미친 짓을 하나하나 그대로 본받을 생각은 없으나, 내게 가장 본질적으로 여겨지는 모든 것을 내 힘껏 대강이나마 본떠 보리라. 어쩌면 나는 아마디스를 본받는 단순한 일로 만족할지도 모르겠구나. 그는 어떤 해로운 광기에도 빠지지 않고 그저 눈물과 슬픔으로써 가장 이름난 자들 못지않은 명성을 얻었으니 말이다.\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔,\" 산초가 말했다. \"이런 식으로 행동한 기사들에게는 그런 어리석은 짓과 고행을 할 만한 자극과 까닭이 있었던 것 같습니다. 그런데 나리께는 미쳐 버릴 무슨 까닭이 있단 말입니까? 어떤 부인이 나리를 거절했습니까, 아니면 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님이 무어인이나 기독교인과 놀아났다는 무슨 증거라도 찾으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그 점이니라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그리고 그것이 내 이 일의 묘미이니라. 까닭이 있어서 미치는 편력 기사라면 무슨 고마울 게 있겠느냐. 핵심은 아무런 자극도 없이 미쳐 버리는 것이며, 내가 메마른 데서도 이러할진대 젖은 데서는 무엇을 할지 내 부인이 알게 하는 것이니라. 더구나 내게는 충분한 까닭이 있으니, 죽음에 이르도록 사랑하는 내 부인 둘시네아 델 토보소와 오래 떨어져 견뎌 온 일이 그것이다. 일전에 그 양치기 암브로시오가 한 말을 너도 들었듯이, 떨어져 있으면 온갖 고통이 느껴지고 두려워지는 법이니라. 그러니 친구 산초야, 이토록 보기 드물고, 이토록 행복하고, 이토록 전대미문의 본받음을 그만두라고 나를 설득하느라 시간을 낭비하지 말거라. 나는 미쳤으며, 내 부인 둘시네아에게 보낼 편지의 답을 네가 가지고 돌아올 때까지 계속 미쳐 있어야 하느니라. 그 답이 내 한결같은 마음에 걸맞은 것이라면 내 광기와 고행은 끝날 것이고, 만약 그 반대라면 나는 정말로 미쳐 버려, 그리되면 더는 아무 고통도 느끼지 못할 것이니라. 이리하여 그분이 어떤 식으로 답하든 나는 네가 나를 두고 떠날 그 몸부림과 괴로움에서 벗어나게 될 것이니, 제정신으로 네가 가져올 은혜를 누리거나, 아니면 미치광이로서 네가 가져올 화를 느끼지 못하거나 둘 중 하나일 것이다. 그건 그렇고 산초야, 맘브리노의 투구는 잘 간수하고 있느냐? 그 배은망덕한 자가 그것을 부수려다 부수지 못했을 때 네가 그것을 땅에서 주워 드는 것을 내가 보았느니라. 그것으로 그 강철의 단단함을 알 수 있느니라.\"\n\n이에 산초가 대답했다. \"살아 계신 하느님께 두고 말씀드리건대, 슬픈 몰골의 기사 나리, 저는 나리께서 하시는 말씀 가운데 어떤 것들은 도무지 참고 견딜 수가 없습니다. 그런 말씀을 들으니 나리께서 제게 들려주시는 기사도며, 왕국과 제국을 얻는 일이며, 섬을 주신다는 일이며, 편력 기사들의 관습에 따라 온갖 상과 작위를 내려 주신다는 그 모든 이야기가 다 바람과 거짓말로 지어낸 것이요, 죄다 헛것이거나 꾸며 낸 것이거나, 뭐라 부르든 그런 게 아닌가 의심이 들기 시작합니다. 이발사의 대야를 맘브리노의 투구라고 부르시면서 여태껏 그 잘못을 한 번도 깨닫지 못하시는 걸 누가 듣는다면, 그런 말을 하고 우기는 사람은 머릿속이 뒤죽박죽이 된 게 분명하다고 여기지 않겠습니까? 그 대야는 다 찌그러진 채 제 자루 속에 들어 있고, 저는 그걸 집으로 가져가서 고쳐다가 수염을 다듬는 데 쓸 작정입니다. 하느님의 은총으로 언젠가 제 마누라와 자식들을 다시 보게 된다면 말이지요.\"\n\n\"이보게, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 방금 두고 맹세한 그분께 두고 나도 맹세하건대, 너는 세상의 어느 종자가 지녔거나 지녔던 것보다도 가장 한정된 머리를 가졌느니라. 네가 이토록 오랫동안 나와 함께 다니면서도, 편력 기사에 속한 모든 것이 환상이요 헛소리요 광기처럼 보이며 늘 거꾸로 돌아간다는 사실을 여태 깨닫지 못했단 말이냐? 그것이 정말로 그래서가 아니라, 우리를 돕거나 망치려는 마음에 따라 우리와 함께하는 모든 것을 바꾸고 뒤틀어, 제멋대로 사물을 변하게 하는 마법사 무리가 늘 우리를 따라다니기 때문이니라. 그리하여 너에게는 이발사의 대야로 보이는 것이 내게는 맘브리노의 투구로 보이고, 또 다른 사람에게는 다른 무엇으로 보일 것이다. 내 편을 드는 그 현자가 참으로 진정한 맘브리노의 투구를 모든 사람에게 대야로 보이게 만든 것은 보기 드문 선견지명이었느니라. 그것이 워낙 귀한 것으로 여겨지니, 온 세상이 그것을 빼앗으려고 나를 쫓아올 테지만, 그저 이발사의 대야로만 보이면 아무도 그것을 손에 넣으려는 수고를 하지 않을 테니 말이다. 그것을 부수려다 손에 넣지 않고 땅바닥에 내버려 둔 자가 이를 분명히 보여 주었으니, 내 믿음을 걸고 말하건대, 그가 그것이 무엇인지 알았더라면 결코 두고 가지 않았을 것이다. 잘 간수하거라, 친구여. 지금은 그것이 내게 필요하지 않으니 말이다. 사실 내가 고행에서 아마디스보다 롤단을 따르기로 마음먹는다면, 이 갑옷을 모조리 벗어 던지고 태어났을 때처럼 벌거숭이로 있어야 할 테니까.\"\n\n이렇게 이야기를 나누며 두 사람은 한 높은 산기슭에 이르렀는데, 그 산은 주위를 둘러싼 다른 산들 가운데 우뚝 솟은 외딴 봉우리처럼 서 있었다. 그 기슭으로는 부드러운 시냇물이 흘렀고, 둘레에는 온통 푸르고 무성한 풀밭이 펼쳐져 바라보는 눈을 즐겁게 했으며, 울창한 숲의 나무들과 관목과 꽃들이 그곳의 아름다움을 더해 주었다. 슬픈 몰골의 기사는 자신의 고행을 행할 곳으로 이곳을 택했고, 그곳을 바라보며 마치 제정신이 아닌 듯 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 하늘이여, 이곳이야말로 그대들 자신이 나를 빠뜨린 이 불행을 통곡하기 위해 내가 가려 뽑은 곳이로다. 이곳이야말로 내 두 눈에서 넘쳐흐르는 눈물이 저 작은 시냇물을 불어나게 하고, 깊고 끝없는 내 한숨이 박해받는 내 마음이 겪는 고통의 증거이자 표징으로 이 산속 나무들의 잎사귀를 쉼 없이 흔들 곳이로다. 오, 이 외딴곳을 떠도는 시골의 신들이여, 그대들이 누구든, 오랜 이별과 끓어오르는 질투에 떠밀려 이 황야에서 제 운명을 통곡하며 인간의 모든 아름다움의 끝이자 한계인 그 아리땁고도 무정한 여인의 모진 마음을 한탄하는 가련한 연인의 하소연에 귀 기울여 주오! 오, 숲속 덤불에 깃들어 사는 물의 요정과 나무의 요정들이여, 그대들에게 헛되이 구애하는 날랜 음탕한 사티로스들이 그대들의 달콤한 안식을 결코 어지럽히지 않기를. 부디 내가 모진 운명을 한탄하는 것을 도와주거나, 적어도 그것을 듣는 데 지치지나 말아 주오! 오, 둘시네아 델 토보소여, 내 밤의 낮이여, 내 고통의 영광이여, 내 길의 길잡이여, 내 운명의 별이여, 하늘이 그대가 구하는 모든 것을 가득 채워 주시기를. 그대와 떨어져 있음으로 내가 이르게 된 이곳과 이 처지를 헤아려 주고, 내 충실함에 마땅한 보답을 친절로써 베풀어 주오! 오, 외로운 나무들이여, 오늘 이후로 내 고독 속에서 나와 벗이 되어 줄 그대들이여, 그대들의 가지를 부드럽게 흔들어 내 있음이 그대들에게 거슬리지 않는다는 어떤 표시라도 보여 주오! 오, 그대여, 내 종자여, 내 순탄한 운명과 역경의 길에서 즐거운 동반자여, 내가 여기서 하는 일을 잘 기억해 두어, 그 모든 것의 유일한 원인이신 분께 그것을 이야기하고 전할 수 있게 하라.\" 이렇게 말하고는 그는 로시난테에서 내려, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 안장과 굴레를 벗기고 엉덩이를 한 번 철썩 때리며 말했다. \"스스로 자유를 빼앗긴 자가 너에게 자유를 주노라, 오, 행적에서는 더없이 빼어나나 그 운명에서는 더없이 박복한 준마여. 네가 가고 싶은 곳으로 가거라. 네 이마에는 아스톨포의 히포그리프도, 브라다만테에게 그토록 값비싼 대가를 치르게 한 그 이름난 프론티노도 빠르기에서 너를 따를 수 없다고 쓰여 있느니라.\"\n\n이를 본 산초가 말했다. \"잿빛이 녀석의 길마를 벗기는 수고를 우리한테 덜어 준 분에게 복이 있기를! 정말이지 그 녀석도 엉덩이를 한 번 철썩 맞고 칭찬 한마디 듣지 않고는 못 떠났을 텐데. 하지만 그 녀석이 여기 있었다면 저는 누구도 그놈의 길마를 벗기지 못하게 했을 겁니다. 그럴 까닭이 없으니까요. 그놈은 연인이나 절망에 빠진 자 같은 구석이라곤 하나도 없었거든요. 하느님 뜻에 따라 한때 그놈의 주인이었던 저부터가 그런 사람이 아니었으니 말입니다. 정말이지, 슬픈 몰골의 기사 나리, 만약 제가 떠나는 일과 나리께서 미치시는 일이 정말로 벌어질 거라면, 로시난테에 다시 안장을 얹는 게 좋겠습니다. 그래야 잿빛 녀석의 빈자리를 메울 수 있고, 오가는 시간도 아낄 수 있을 테니까요. 걸어서 가다가는 제가 언제 거기 도착할지, 언제 돌아올지 알 수가 없습니다. 사실 저는 걸음이 영 신통치 않은 사람이라서요.\"\n\n\"그래, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"네 뜻대로 하거라. 네 생각이 나쁘지 않은 듯하니 말이다. 사흘 뒤에 떠나도록 하여라. 그동안 내가 그분을 위해 무엇을 하고 무엇을 말하는지 잘 보아 두기를 바라기 때문이니, 그래야 네가 그것을 전할 수 있을 것이다.\"\n\n\"그런데 제가 이미 본 것 말고 더 봐야 할 게 뭐가 있단 말입니까?\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"네가 그것을 잘도 알겠구나!\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이제 나는 내 옷을 갈가리 찢고, 갑옷을 여기저기 흩뿌리고, 이 바위들에 머리를 부딪고, 그 밖에도 그와 같은 일들을 더 해야 하니, 너는 그것을 지켜보아야 하느니라.\"\n\n\"제발 부탁이니,\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리, 머리를 부딪으실 때 부디 조심하십시오. 자칫 그런 바위에, 그런 식으로 부딪치셨다가는 바로 그 첫 번째 부딪침에 이 고행의 모든 계획이 끝장나 버릴 수도 있으니까요. 그리고 제 생각엔, 정말로 머리를 부딪는 일이 나리께 꼭 필요하고 이 일을 그것 없이는 할 수 없다 하더라도 — 어차피 이 모든 게 꾸며 낸 것이고, 가짜이고, 장난이니까 — 그저 물속에다 부딪거나, 솜처럼 부드러운 것에다 부딪는 것으로 만족하셔도 되지 않겠습니까. 그리고 나머지는 다 제게 맡기십시오. 제가 마님께 나리께서 다이아몬드보다 더 단단한 바위 모서리에 머리를 부딪으셨다고 말씀드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"네 좋은 뜻은 고맙다, 친구 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"허나 네가 알아 두어야 할 것은, 내가 하는 이 모든 일이 장난이 아니라 지극히 진지한 것이라는 점이니라. 그렇지 않으면 기사도의 법도를 어기는 것이 되기 때문이다. 기사도의 법도는 우리에게 배교에 해당하는 벌을 받을 각오 없이는 어떠한 거짓말도 하지 못하게 금하느니라. 그리고 한 가지 일 대신 다른 일을 하는 것은 거짓말을 하는 것과 똑같으니라. 그러니 내가 머리를 부딪는 것은 진짜요, 단단하고, 정당한 것이어야 하며, 거기에 거짓되거나 꾸며 낸 구석이 있어서는 안 되느니라. 그리고 우리가 잃어버린 영약 없이 지낼 수밖에 없게 된 처지이니, 내 상처를 싸맬 헝겊을 좀 남겨 두어야겠구나.\"\n\n\"당나귀를 잃은 게 더 큰일이었지요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"그놈과 함께 헝겊이고 뭐고 다 잃어버렸으니까요. 하지만 나리께 부탁드리건대, 그 저주받을 물약 이야기는 다시는 제게 꺼내지 말아 주십시오. 그 이름만 들어도 제 위장은 말할 것도 없고 영혼까지 뒤집어지니까요. 그리고 또 한 가지 부탁드리는데, 나리께서 미친 짓을 하시는 것을 보라고 제게 주신 그 사흘은 이미 지나간 것으로 쳐 주십시오. 저는 그걸 이미 다 보고 판단한 것으로 여기고, 마님께 놀라운 이야기들을 들려드릴 작정이니까요. 그러니 편지를 쓰셔서 저를 당장 떠나보내 주십시오. 어서 돌아와서 제가 나리를 두고 가는 이 연옥에서 나리를 구해 드리고 싶으니까요.\"\n\n\"이곳을 연옥이라 부르느냐, 산초?\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"차라리 지옥이라 부르거나, 그보다 더한 것이 있다면 그것이라 부르거라.\"\n\n\"지옥에 있는 자에게는,\" 산초가 말했다. \"'눌라 에스트 레텐티오'라고 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"레텐티오가 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠구나.\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"레텐티오란,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"지옥에 있는 자는 누구든 결코 그곳에서 나오지도, 나올 수도 없다는 뜻입니다. 나리의 경우는 그 반대가 될 텐데요, 그게 아니라면 제 다리가 게으름을 피운 탓이겠지요. 물론 로시난테에 박차를 가할 박차가 있어야겠지만요. 일단 제가 엘 토보소에 가서 둘시네아 마님 앞에 서기만 하면, 나리께서 하셨고 또 지금도 하고 계신 그 어리석은 짓들과 미친 짓들(어차피 다 같은 거지만)을 마님께 어찌나 잘 들려드릴지, 코르크나무보다 더 딱딱한 분이라도 장갑보다 더 부드럽게 만들어 보이겠습니다. 그리고 마님의 달콤하고 꿀 같은 답을 받아 들고 마녀처럼 하늘을 날아 돌아와서, 지옥처럼 보이지만 실은 지옥이 아닌 이 연옥에서 나리를 꺼내 드리겠습니다. 빠져나갈 희망이 있으니 지옥이 아닌 거지요. 제가 말씀드렸듯이 지옥에 있는 자들에게는 그런 희망이 없으니까요. 이건 나리께서도 달리 토를 달지 못하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이다.\" 슬픈 몰골의 기사가 말했다. \"그런데 그 편지는 어떻게 쓰면 좋겠느냐?\"\n\n\"당나귀 새끼 인도장도요.\" 산초가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"모두 다 함께 쓰마.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그리고 종이가 없으니, 옛사람들이 그랬듯이 나뭇잎에다 쓰거나 밀랍 서판에다 쓰는 것도 좋겠구나. 다만 그것도 지금은 종이만큼이나 구하기 어렵겠지. 한데 마침 좋은 생각이 떠올랐으니, 편리하게, 아니 편리한 것 이상으로 쓸 방법이 있느니라. 바로 카르데니오의 것이었던 그 수첩에다 쓰는 것이다. 그러면 너는 학교 선생이 있는 첫 번째 마을에 닿거든 그것을 좋은 글씨로 종이에 베껴 쓰게 하거라. 선생이 없거든 성당지기라도 베껴 줄 것이다. 다만 어떤 공증인에게도 베끼게 하지는 마라. 그자들은 사탄도 알아보지 못할 법률 글씨를 쓰니까 말이다.\"\n\n\"그런데 서명은 어떻게 합니까?\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"아마디스의 편지에는 결코 서명이 없었느니라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 다 좋습니다만,\" 산초가 말했다. \"인도장에는 반드시 서명이 있어야 합니다. 베껴 쓴 거라면 사람들이 서명이 가짜라고 할 테고, 그러면 저는 당나귀 새끼도 못 받게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n\"인도장은 같은 수첩에 서명을 해서 보내마.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그것을 보면 내 조카딸이 군말 없이 따를 것이다. 연애편지로 말하자면, 서명 대신 이렇게 적으면 되느니라. '죽을 때까지 그대의 것인, 슬픈 몰골의 기사가.' 그리고 그것이 다른 사람의 글씨로 쓰여 있다 한들 별일 아니다. 내 기억으로는 둘시네아는 읽을 줄도 쓸 줄도 모르며, 평생 한 번도 내 글씨나 편지를 본 적이 없기 때문이니라. 나와 그분의 사랑은 늘 정신적인 것이어서, 정숙한 눈길 한 번 주고받는 것을 넘은 적이 없었고, 그조차 어찌나 드물었던지, 내가 그분을 사랑해 온 이 십이 년 동안 — 언젠가 흙이 삼켜 버릴 이 두 눈의 빛보다 더 사랑해 온 그 세월 동안 — 그분을 통틀어 네 번도 채 보지 못했다고 떳떳이 맹세할 수 있느니라. 게다가 그 네 번 가운데서도, 어쩌면 그분은 내가 자신을 바라보고 있다는 것을 단 한 번도 알아채지 못했을지도 모르느니라. 그분의 아버지 로렌소 코르추엘로와 어머니 알돈사 노갈레스가 그분을 그토록 깊이 숨기고 가두어 길렀기 때문이니라.\"\n\n\"저런, 저런!\" 산초가 말했다. \"그러니까 로렌소 코르추엘로의 딸이 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님이라는 말씀이군요. 달리 알돈사 로렌소라고도 불리는 그 여자 말입니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그 여인이니라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그리고 온 우주의 주인이 될 만한 자격이 있는 분이 바로 그 여인이니라.\"\n\n\"제가 그 여자를 잘 압니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"말씀드리지만 그 여자는 온 마을에서 가장 기운 센 사내 못지않게 쇠막대를 잘 던집니다. 모든 좋은 것을 주시는 분이시여! 정말이지 그 여자는 씩씩하고 다부진 처녀라서, 자기를 부인으로 삼을 어떤 편력 기사에게든, 지금 있는 기사든 앞으로 나올 기사든 짝이 되어 줄 만합니다. 그 발칙한 계집애가 어찌나 톡톡 쏘아 대고 목청이 큰지! 말씀드리지만 한번은 그 여자가 마을 종탑 꼭대기에 올라가서, 자기 아버지의 밭에서 일하던 일꾼들을 부른 적이 있는데, 그자들이 반 레과나 떨어진 곳에 있었는데도 마치 탑 바로 아래 있는 것처럼 그 소리를 다 들었답니다. 게다가 그 여자의 가장 좋은 점은 조금도 새침을 떨지 않는다는 겁니다. 붙임성이 넘쳐서 누구하고나 농담을 주고받고, 무슨 일에나 히죽 웃으며 한마디 던지지요. 그러니 슬픈 몰골의 기사 나리, 제가 말씀드리건대, 나리께서는 그 여자를 위해 미친 짓을 하셔도 될 뿐만 아니라 하셔야 마땅하며, 절망에 빠져 목을 매다실 권리도 충분하십니다. 이 일을 아는 사람이라면 누구나 나리께서 잘하셨다고 말할 겁니다. 악마가 나리를 데려간다 해도 말입니다. 그리고 저는 어서 길을 떠나고 싶습니다. 그저 그 여자를 보고 싶어서요. 본 지가 워낙 오래됐으니, 지금쯤이면 많이 변했을 겁니다. 늘 들판을 쏘다니니 햇볕이며 바람이 여자의 얼굴을 크게 망쳐 놓기 마련이거든요. 그런데 나리께 진실을 털어놓아야겠습니다, 돈키호테 나리. 지금까지 저는 크게 잘못 알고 있었습니다. 저는 둘시네아 마님이 나리께서 반하신 어느 공주님이거나, 적어도 나리께서 보내신 그 푸짐한 선물들을 받을 만큼 대단한 분이라고 진심으로 정직하게 믿고 있었거든요. 비스카야 사람이며 갤리선 노예들이며, 분명 그 밖에도 많았겠지요. 제가 아직 나리의 종자가 되기 전에 나리께서 숱한 승리를 거두셨을 테니까요. 하지만 모든 걸 따져 보건대, 알돈사 로렌소 마님, 그러니까 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님께서 나리가 보내셨거나 앞으로 보내실 패배자들이 자기 앞에 와서 무릎을 꿇은들 무슨 좋을 게 있겠습니까? 어쩌면 그자들이 도착했을 때 마님은 삼을 빗고 있거나 타작마당에서 타작을 하고 있을지도 모르고, 그러면 그자들은 마님을 보고 부끄러워할 테고, 마님은 웃거나 그 선물을 못마땅해할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"내 전에도 여러 번 너에게 말하지 않았느냐, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"너는 엄청난 수다쟁이이며, 무딘 재치로 늘 날카로움을 부리려 애쓴다고 말이다. 허나 네가 얼마나 어리석은지, 그리고 내가 얼마나 사리에 밝은지 보여 주기 위해, 짧은 이야기 하나를 네게 들려주마. 너는 알아 두어야 한다. 아름답고 젊고 자유로운 데다 부유하고, 무엇보다 거리낌 없고 활달한 어느 과부가 건장하고 헌칠한 젊은 평수사에게 반했느니라. 그 수사의 윗사람이 이를 알게 되어, 어느 날 그 갸륵한 과부에게 형제로서 타이르는 뜻으로 이렇게 말했다. '부인, 부인처럼 그토록 지체 높고, 그토록 아름답고, 그토록 부유한 여인이 아무개처럼 그렇게 천하고 비루하고 어리석은 자에게 반하셨다니, 정말이지 까닭이 없는 게 아니라 참으로 놀랍습니다. 이 집에는 박사며 학사며 신학생들이 그토록 많아서, 마치 배를 고르듯이 이건 가지겠다, 저건 안 가지겠다 하며 그중에서 고르실 수도 있는데 말입니다.' 그러자 과부가 매우 발랄하고 솔직하게 그에게 대답했다. '선생님, 선생님은 크게 잘못 아셨고, 생각도 아주 구식이십니다. 제가 아무개를 택한 게 잘못된 선택이라고 여기신다면 말이지요. 아무리 그가 어리석어 보여도, 제가 그에게 바라는 모든 일에서는 그가 아리스토텔레스만큼이나, 아니 그 이상으로 철학을 알고 있으니까요.' 마찬가지로, 산초야, 내가 둘시네아 델 토보소에게 바라는 모든 일에서 그분은 세상에서 가장 고귀한 공주만큼이나 훌륭하니라. 부인들을 제멋대로 지어낸 이름으로 부르며 그 미덕을 노래한 그 모든 시인이 정말로 그런 연인을 두었다고 여겨서는 안 되느니라. 책이며, 노래며, 이발소며, 극장이 가득 채우고 있는 그 아마릴리스, 필리스, 실비아, 디아나, 갈라테아, 필리다, 그 밖의 모든 여인이 정말로 살과 피를 가진 진짜 부인이었고, 그들을 찬미하고 또 찬미해 온 자들의 연인이었다고 생각하느냐? 천만의 말씀이다. 그들은 대개 자기 시구의 소재를 마련하고, 자신이 연인으로, 혹은 연인이 될 만큼 용맹한 사내로 통하게 하려고 그런 여인들을 지어낸 것뿐이니라. 그러니 나로서는 그 착한 알돈사 로렌소가 아름답고 정숙하다고 생각하고 믿는 것으로 충분하니라. 그분의 가문으로 말하자면 그것은 거의 중요하지 않으니, 그분에게 어떤 작위를 내리려고 그것을 조사할 사람은 아무도 없을 테고, 나로서는 그분을 세상에서 가장 고귀한 공주로 여기노라. 산초야, 네가 모른다면 알아 두어야 할 것이니, 다른 무엇보다도 사랑을 불러일으키는 두 가지가 있으니, 그것은 빼어난 아름다움과 좋은 평판이니라. 그리고 이 두 가지가 둘시네아에게는 더없이 높은 정도로 갖추어져 있으니, 아름다움에서는 아무도 그분에 견줄 수 없고, 좋은 평판에서는 그분에 가까이 다가서는 이가 드무니라. 한마디로 요약하건대, 나는 내가 말하는 모든 것이 더도 덜도 아니라 내가 말하는 그대로라고 스스로 믿으며, 아름다움에서나 신분에서나 내가 바라는 모습 그대로 그분을 내 상상 속에 그려 내노라. 헬레네도 그분에게 미치지 못하고, 루크레티아도 그분에 이르지 못하며, 그리스든 이방이든 라틴이든 옛 시대의 그 어떤 이름난 여인도 마찬가지이니라. 그러니 저마다 하고 싶은 말을 하라지. 이 일로 무지한 자들에게 내가 책잡힌다 한들, 안목 있는 자들에게는 비난받지 않을 테니 말이다.\"\n\n\"나리 말씀이 전적으로 옳다고 생각합니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그리고 저는 당나귀입니다. 하지만 어쩌다 당나귀란 말이 제 입에 올랐는지 모르겠군요. 목매달린 자의 집에서는 밧줄 이야기를 꺼내는 게 아닌데 말입니다. 이제 편지를 주십시오. 그럼 하느님과 함께 가시기를, 저는 떠나겠습니다.\"\n\n돈키호테는 수첩을 꺼내 한쪽으로 물러나 매우 신중하게 편지를 쓰기 시작했고, 다 쓰고 나자 산초를 불러 그것을 읽어 주겠다고 말했다. 길에서 그것을 잃어버릴 경우에 대비해 그가 외워 두게 하기 위해서였으니, 그의 운수처럼 사나운 운수라면 무슨 일이든 염려스러웠던 것이다. 이에 산초가 대답했다. \"그 수첩에다 두세 번 옮겨 써서 제게 주십시오. 그러면 제가 아주 조심스럽게 가져가겠습니다. 제가 그걸 기억에 담아 두기를 바라시는 건 다 부질없는 일이니까요. 저는 기억력이 어찌나 형편없는지 제 이름조차 종종 잊어버릴 정도거든요. 하지만 그래도 제게 한번 읽어 주십시오. 듣고 싶으니까요. 분명 인쇄된 것처럼 술술 읽힐 테니 말입니다.\"\n\"들어 보아라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"여기 이렇게 적혀 있느니라.\n\n둘시네아 델 토보소에게 보내는 돈키호테의 편지\n\n지존하고 고귀한 부인이여. 이별의 칼끝에 찔리고 마음 깊은 곳까지 상처 입은 자가, 더없이 다정한 둘시네아 델 토보소여, 그대에게 자신은 누리지 못하는 건강을 보냅니다. 그대의 아름다움이 나를 멸시하고, 그대의 가치가 나를 위한 것이 아니며, 그대의 경멸이 내 괴로움이라면, 내가 비록 충분히 오래 견디는 자라 할지라도, 이 괴로움을 견디기 어려울 것입니다. 그것은 짓누르는 것일 뿐 아니라 오래 끄는 것이기 때문입니다. 나의 충직한 종자 산초가 그대에게 낱낱이 들려드릴 것입니다, 아리따운 배은망덕한 이여, 사랑스러운 원수여, 그대 때문에 내가 이르게 된 처지를. 만약 내게 위안을 베푸는 것이 그대의 기쁨이라면 나는 그대의 것이요, 그렇지 않다면 그대의 마음에 드는 대로 하시오. 내 목숨을 끊음으로써 나는 그대의 잔혹함과 내 소망을 모두 채울 것이니 말입니다.\n\n죽을 때까지 그대의 것인,\n\n슬픈 몰골의 기사.\"\n\n\"제 아버지의 목숨을 두고 말씀드리건대,\" 편지를 들은 산초가 말했다. \"여태껏 제가 들어 본 것 중 가장 고상한 것입니다. 이런 세상에! 나리께서는 어찌나 하고 싶은 말을 그 안에 다 담으셨는지요! 그리고 '슬픈 몰골의 기사'를 서명에 어찌나 잘 끼워 넣으셨는지요. 정말이지 나리께서는 진짜 악마이시고, 모르시는 게 없으십니다.\"\n\n\"내가 따르는 소명에는 모든 것이 필요하니라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그러면,\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리께서는 그 뒷면에다 당나귀 새끼 세 마리에 대한 인도장을 적으시고, 한눈에 알아볼 수 있도록 아주 또렷하게 서명해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러마.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. 그러고는 다 쓰고 나서 이런 내용으로 그것을 읽어 주었다.\n\n\"조카딸에게. 이 첫 번째 당나귀 새끼 인도장으로, 내가 집에 두고 너에게 맡긴 다섯 마리 가운데 세 마리를 내 종자 산초 판사에게 내어 주거라. 이 세 마리 당나귀 새끼는 이곳에서 직접 받은 같은 수에 대한 대가로 지불하고 인도되어야 하며, 이 인도장과 그의 영수를 근거로 응당 지불될 것이다. 시에라 모레나 한복판에서, 올해 팔월 이십칠일에 작성함.\"\n\n\"그거면 됩니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"이제 나리께서 서명해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"서명할 필요는 없고,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그저 내 화필 장식만 그려 넣으면 되느니라. 그것이 서명이나 마찬가지이니, 당나귀 세 마리는 물론이요 삼백 마리라도 충분하니라.\"\n\n\"저는 나리를 믿겠습니다.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제가 가서 로시난테에 안장을 얹을 테니, 제게 축복을 내려 주실 채비를 해 주십시오. 저는 나리께서 하실 그 어리석은 짓들을 보지 않고 당장 떠날 작정이니까요. 제가 그토록 많은 짓을 하시는 걸 보았다고 말씀드리면, 마님께서도 더는 듣고 싶어 하지 않으실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"어쨌든, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"나는 네가 — 거기엔 까닭이 있느니라 — 내가 알몸이 되도록 옷을 벗고 한두 다스쯤 미친 짓을 하는 것을 보았으면 한다. 그것은 반 시간도 안 되어 해낼 수 있느니라. 네 눈으로 직접 그것을 보고 나면, 네가 거기에 보태고 싶은 나머지에 대해서도 떳떳이 맹세할 수 있을 테니 말이다. 그리고 내 약속하건대, 너는 내가 행하려는 것만큼 많이 이야기하지는 못할 것이다.\"\n\n\"제발 부탁이니, 주인 나리.\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리께서 옷을 벗으시는 건 보지 않게 해 주십시오. 그걸 보면 저는 몹시 슬퍼져서 눈물을 참을 수가 없을 겁니다. 게다가 어젯밤 잿빛 녀석 때문에 어찌나 울었던지 머리가 다 아파서, 새로 또 울 처지가 못 됩니다. 하지만 제가 미친 짓을 좀 봐야 하는 게 나리의 뜻이라면, 옷을 입은 채로, 짧게, 손쉽게 할 수 있는 것들로 해 주십시오. 저로서는 그런 게 전혀 필요 없거니와, 말씀드렸듯이 그래야 제가 돌아가는 시간을 아낄 수 있으니까요. 나리께서 바라시고 또 받으실 만한 소식을 가지고 돌아오겠습니다. 만약 그렇지 못하면, 둘시네아 마님이 알아서 하셔야겠지요. 마님이 사리에 맞게 답하지 않으신다면, 제가 할 수 있는 가장 엄숙한 맹세를 걸고 말씀드리건대, 발길질과 주먹질로 마님 뱃속에서라도 제대로 된 답을 끄집어내겠습니다. 나리처럼 이름난 편력 기사가 일개— 때문에 까닭도 없이 미쳐 버린다는 게 어디 말이나 됩니까? 마님께서 제가 그 말을 입에 올리게 만들지 않으시는 게 좋을 겁니다. 하느님께 맹세코 저는 다 털어놓고, 팔리지 않더라도 죄다 헐값에 쏟아 낼 테니까요. 저는 그런 데는 제법 능합니다! 마님은 저를 잘 모르시지요. 정말이지 마님이 저를 아셨더라면 저를 어려워하셨을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"겉보기로는 너도 나 못지않게 제정신이 아닌 듯하구나.\"\n\n\"저는 그렇게 미치진 않았습니다.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"다만 성미가 좀 더 매울 뿐이지요. 그건 그렇고, 제가 돌아올 때까지 나리께서는 무얼 잡수실 작정입니까? 카르데니오처럼 길로 나가서 양치기들한테서 빼앗으실 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그런 걱정일랑 너를 괴롭히지 못하게 하여라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"설령 먹을 것이 있다 해도, 나는 이 풀밭과 이 나무들이 내게 내어 줄 풀과 열매 말고는 아무것도 먹지 않을 것이니라. 이 일의 묘미는 먹지 않는 것과 그 밖의 고행을 행하는 데 있느니라.\"\n\n\"제가 무얼 두려워하는지 아십니까?\" 이에 산초가 말했다. \"나리를 두고 떠나는 이 자리로 되돌아오는 길을 찾지 못할까 봐서입니다. 이렇게 외진 곳이니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"표지물을 잘 보아 두어라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"나는 이 부근에서 멀리 벗어나지 않도록 애쓸 것이고, 심지어 이 바위들 가운데 가장 높은 곳에 올라가 네가 돌아오는지 살펴보기까지 할 것이다. 허나 나를 놓치고 길을 잃지 않으려면, 이 근방에 흔하디흔한 금작화 가지를 좀 베어, 가다가 들판에 나설 때까지 군데군데 놓아두는 것이 가장 좋은 방법이겠구나. 그것이 테세우스의 미로에서의 실타래처럼, 네가 돌아올 때 나를 찾는 표시이자 길잡이가 되어 줄 것이다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하겠습니다.\" 산초 판사가 말하고는 금작화 가지를 몇 개 벤 다음, 주인에게 축복을 청하고, 양쪽 다 적잖은 눈물을 흘리며 그에게 작별을 고했다. 그러고는 돈키호테가 자기 몸처럼 잘 보살피라고 간곡히 당부한 로시난테에 올라, 주인이 일러 준 대로 군데군데 금작화 가지를 흩뿌리며 들판을 향해 길을 나섰다. 그렇게 그는 제 갈 길을 갔는데, 그동안에도 돈키호테는 단 두어 가지라도 미친 짓을 하는 것을 보고 가라고 그에게 계속 간청했다. 그러나 그가 백 걸음도 채 가지 않아 되돌아와서 말했다.\n\n\"말씀드려야겠습니다, 나리. 나리 말씀이 참으로 옳았습니다. 나리께서 미친 짓을 하시는 걸 제가 보았다고 양심에 거리낌 없이 맹세하려면, 단 한 가지라도 보아 두는 것이 좋겠지요. 물론 나리께서 이곳에 남아 계신다는 것만으로도 저는 이미 아주 대단한 미친 짓 하나를 본 셈이지만 말입니다.\"\n\n\"내 그렇게 말하지 않았더냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"기다려라, 산초, 사도신경 한 번 욀 사이에 해 보이마.\" 그러고는 부랴부랴 바지를 벗어 알몸에 셔츠 차림만 남기더니, 더 지체할 것도 없이 공중으로 두 차례 깡충 뛰어오르고, 머리를 아래로 발을 위로 하여 두 차례 공중제비를 넘으며 어찌나 볼만하게 굴었는지, 그것을 두 번 다시 보지 않으려고 산초는 로시난테를 돌려 세웠으니, 이제 마음이 놓이고 흡족해져서 주인을 미친 사람으로 두고 떠났다고 떳떳이 맹세할 수 있게 되었다. 그러니 그가 돌아올 때까지 우리는 그를 제 갈 길을 가도록 내버려 두기로 하자. 그것은 빠른 걸음이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVI.\n\n혼자 남게 된 슬픈 몰골의 기사가 어떻게 행동했는지로 돌아가 보면, 역사가 전하는 바에 따르면 돈키호테는 허리 아래로는 벌거벗고 허리 위로는 옷을 걸친 채 공중제비와 뜀뛰기를 다 마치고 나서, 산초가 더 이상의 미친 짓을 보지도 않고 떠나 버린 것을 알아챘다. 그러자 그는 높은 바위 꼭대기로 기어올라가, 이전에도 여러 번 곱씹었으나 끝내 아무런 결론에도 이르지 못했던 문제를 다시금 헤아리기 시작했으니, 곧 롤단의 광포한 광기를 흉내 내는 것이 더 나을지, 아니면 아마디스의 우울한 광기를 흉내 내는 것이 자기 목적에 더 어울릴지 하는 것이었다. 그러고는 혼자 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"롤단이 모두가 말하듯 그토록 훌륭한 기사이자 그토록 용맹한 자였다 한들 무에 그리 놀랄 일이겠는가? 따지고 보면 그는 마법에 걸려 있어 발바닥에 굵은 핀을 찔러 넣지 않고서는 아무도 그를 죽일 수 없었고, 그래서 그는 늘 일곱 겹의 쇠바닥을 댄 신을 신고 다녔으니 말이다. 하기야 그 모든 것을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있던 베르나르도 델 카르피오에게는 그 교묘한 술책도 통하지 않았으니, 베르나르도는 론세스바예스에서 그를 두 팔로 끌어안아 목 졸라 죽였더라. 그러나 그의 용맹은 잠시 제쳐 두고, 그가 정신을 잃은 대목으로 가 보자. 그가 정신을 잃은 것은 분명 샘가에서 발견한 증거들과, 안헬리카가 아그라만테의 시동인 곱슬머리 어린 무어인 메도로와 두 낮잠 이상의 시간을 함께 잤다고 양치기가 일러 준 소식 때문이었다. 만일 그가 이것이 사실이며 자기 여인이 자기를 배신했다고 믿게 되었다면, 그가 미쳐 버린 것도 놀랄 일이 아니다. 하지만 나는, 그 미침의 원인까지 흉내 낼 수 없다면 어찌 그의 광기를 흉내 낼 수 있겠는가? 내 둘시네아로 말하자면, 감히 맹세컨대 평생 무어인을 그 본디 차림새 그대로 본 적이 한 번도 없으며, 그녀는 오늘날까지도 그녀를 낳은 어머니 그대로일 터이니, 만일 내가 무엇인가 다른 것을 상상하여 광란의 롤단과 똑같은 종류의 광기에 빠진다면 나는 명백히 그녀에게 부당한 짓을 저지르는 것이 되리라. 다른 한편으로, 나는 갈리아의 아마디스가 분별을 잃지도 않고 미친 짓이라곤 하나도 하지 않고서도, 연인으로서 가장 이름 높은 자 못지않은 명성을 얻었음을 본다. 그의 이야기에 따르면, 그는 자기 여인 오리아나에게 거절당하여, 그녀가 허락할 때까지는 자기 앞에 나타나지 말라는 명을 받게 되자, 그가 한 일이라곤 어느 은자와 더불어 페냐 포브레로 물러나, 하늘이 그 크나큰 슬픔과 고난 가운데서 위안을 보내 줄 때까지 실컷 울었던 것뿐이었다. 이것이 사실이라면, 또 사실이거니와, 어찌하여 내가 이제 와서 알몸으로 발가벗는 수고를 하거나, 내게 아무 해도 끼치지 않은 이 나무들에 행패를 부리거나, 내가 마시고 싶을 때면 언제든 마실 물을 내어 줄 이 시냇물의 맑은 물을 어지럽혀야 한단 말인가? 아마디스의 기억이여 길이 살아남으라, 그리고 라만차의 돈키호테가 할 수 있는 한 그를 본받게 하라. 그에 관해서는 다른 이에 관해 일컬어졌던 그대로, 비록 위대한 일을 이루지는 못했어도 그것을 이루고자 애쓰다 죽었노라고 일컬어지리라. 그리고 내가 내 둘시네아에게 퇴짜를 맞거나 거절당한 것이 아닌 바에야, 내가 말했듯 그녀와 떨어져 있는 것만으로 내게는 충분하다. 자, 그러면 이제 일에 착수하자. 아마디스의 행적들이여 내 기억으로 와서, 내가 어떻게 그대들을 본받기 시작해야 할지 보여 다오. 그가 무엇보다 먼저 한 일이 기도하며 하느님께 자신을 의탁하는 것이었음은 내 이미 알고 있다. 하지만 묵주는 어찌한단 말인가, 내겐 묵주가 없는데?\"\n\n그때 그에게 묵주를 만들 방도가 떠올랐으니, 그것은 늘어진 셔츠 자락의 끝을 길게 한 가닥 찢어 내어 거기에 매듭 열한 개를 짓되 그중 하나는 나머지보다 더 크게 만드는 것이었다. 이것이 그가 그곳에 머무는 내내 묵주 노릇을 했고, 그동안 그는 수도 없이 많은 성모송을 외웠다. 그러나 그를 몹시 괴롭힌 것은, 그곳에 자기 고해를 들어 주고 위안을 베풀어 줄 또 다른 은자가 없다는 사실이었다. 그리하여 그는 작은 풀밭을 이리저리 거닐며, 나무껍질과 고운 모래 위에 자신의 슬픔에 어울리는 수많은 시구를, 더러는 둘시네아를 찬미하는 시구를 쓰고 새기며 스스로를 달랬다. 하지만 나중에 그가 그곳에서 발견되었을 때, 온전히 읽어 낼 수 있었던 것이라고는 다음에 나오는 것들뿐이었다.\n\n산허리에 자라나는 그대들이여, 푸르른 것들이여, 나무며 떨기며 덤불이여, 이 가련하고 쓰린 가슴을 짓누르는 그 비탄에 그대들도 지쳐 버렸는가? 그것이 그대들을 어지럽히고, 내가 무언가 보상해야 할 빚이 있다면, 이 한마디 알려 줌이 내겐 변명이 되리라. 돈키호테의 눈물이 흐르고 있노라, 그 모두가 머나먼 둘시네아 델 토보소를 위함이라네.\n\n세월이 보여 줄 수 있는 가장 충직한 연인이, 한 여인을 사모하다 시들도록 운명 지어져, 이 적막한 곳들 사이를 헤매며 가노라, 온갖 종류의 고뇌에 사로잡힌 채로. 어찌하여 사랑이 심술궂은 원수처럼 그를 이리 부려 먹는지 그는 알지 못하나, 큰 술통 가득—이것만은 그도 안다네— 돈키호테의 눈물이 흐르고 있노라, 그 모두가 머나먼 둘시네아 델 토보소를 위함이라네.\n\n모험을 좇아 그는 가노라, 험준한 봉우리를 오르고, 바위투성이 골짜기를 내려가며, 허나 언덕이든 골짜기든, 높든 낮든, 그의 모든 출정엔 불운이 따라붙네. 사랑은 여전히 그를 이리저리 쫓으며, 그 잔인한 채찍을 휘두르나니—아, 슬프도다! 가차 없는 운명이여, 끝없는 비탄이여. 돈키호테의 눈물이 흐르고 있노라, 그 모두가 머나먼 둘시네아 델 토보소를 위함이라네.\n\n둘시네아의 이름에 \"델 토보소\"를 덧붙인 것은 위의 시구를 발견한 이들 사이에서 적잖은 웃음을 자아냈으니, 그들은 돈키호테가 둘시네아라는 이름을 끌어들일 때 \"델 토보소\"를 덧붙이지 않으면 시구가 알아들을 수 없게 되리라 여겼던 것이 틀림없다고 짐작했기 때문이다. 그리고 그것은 과연 사실이었으니, 그 자신도 훗날 인정한 바이다. 그는 이보다 훨씬 많은 시구를 썼으나, 앞서 말했듯이 또렷하고 완전하게 판독할 수 있었던 것은 이 세 편이 전부였다. 이런 식으로, 또 한숨지으며 숲의 파우누스와 사티로스들과 시냇물의 님프들과, 촉촉하고 구슬픈 메아리를 불러내어 자기에게 답하고 위로하고 귀 기울여 달라 청하며, 또한 자신을 지탱해 줄 풀들을 찾아다니며, 그는 산초가 돌아올 때까지 시간을 보냈다. 그리고 산초의 귀환이 사흘 대신 삼 주나 늦어졌더라면, 슬픈 몰골의 기사는 그를 낳은 어머니조차 알아보지 못할 만큼 몰골이 바뀌어 있었으리라. 그러니 여기서는 그를 한숨과 시구에 파묻힌 채 내버려 두고, 산초 판사가 그 사명을 어떻게 치렀는지 이야기하는 편이 좋겠다.\n\n산초로 말하자면, 큰길로 나서서 엘 토보소를 향했고, 이튿날에는 담요 키질의 봉변을 당했던 그 객줏집에 다다랐다. 그곳을 알아보자마자 그는 다시 한번 공중을 떠다니는 것만 같은 기분이 들었고, 들어가도 좋을 만한 시각이었음에도 — 마침 저녁 식사 때라 며칠째 줄곧 찬 음식만 먹어 온 터에 무언가 뜨거운 것을 맛보고 싶어 안달이었음에도 — 도무지 안으로 들어설 마음이 나지 않았다. 이 갈망이 그를 객줏집 가까이로 이끌었으나, 그는 여전히 들어갈지 말지 마음을 정하지 못하고 머뭇거렸다. 그렇게 망설이고 있는데 두 사람이 밖으로 나오더니, 곧바로 그를 알아보고 서로에게 말했다.\n\n\"학사 양반, 저기 말 위에 있는 자가, 우리네 모험가의 가정부가 일러 주기를 그 주인과 함께 종자로 떠났다던 산초 판사가 아니오?\"\n\n\"바로 그자요.\" 학사가 말했다. \"그리고 저것은 우리 친구 돈키호테의 말이로구먼.\" 그들이 그를 그토록 잘 알아본 것은, 그들이 바로 그가 사는 마을의 신부와 이발사였기 때문이니, 책들에 대해 심문과 선고를 집행했던 바로 그 사람들이었다. 산초 판사와 로시난테를 알아보자마자 돈키호테의 소식이 몹시 궁금했던 그들은 다가와서 그의 이름을 부르며, 신부가 말했다. \"산초 판사 친구, 자네 주인은 어디 있나?\"\n\n산초는 곧바로 그들을 알아보았으나, 자기 주인을 어디에, 어떤 사정으로 두고 왔는지는 비밀로 해 두기로 마음먹고, 자기 주인은 어느 곳에서 그에게 매우 중대한 어떤 일에 매여 있는데, 자기 머릿속의 두 눈을 걸고서라도 그것을 발설할 수 없노라고 대답했다.\n\n\"안 되네, 안 돼.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"그가 어디 있는지 우리에게 말하지 않으면, 산초 판사, 우리는 이미 의심하고 있는 대로 자네가 그를 죽이고 그 물건을 빼앗았다고 의심하겠네. 자네가 지금 그의 말을 타고 있으니 말이야. 정말이지 자네는 이 짐말의 주인을 내놓든지, 아니면 그 책임을 지든지 해야 하네.\"\n\n\"제게 협박은 필요 없습니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"저는 남을 빼앗거나 죽일 사람이 아니니까요. 각자의 운명이, 혹은 그를 만드신 하느님이 각자를 죽이게 두십시오. 제 주인은 이 산속에서 더없이 마음에 들어 하며 고행을 치르고 계십니다.\" 그러고는 거침없이 멈추지도 않고, 어떻게 그를 두고 왔는지, 무슨 모험들이 그에게 닥쳤는지, 그리고 자기가 어떻게 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님께, 곧 주인이 머리끝까지 사랑에 빠진 로렌소 코르추엘로의 딸에게 보내는 편지를 지니고 가는 길인지를 그들에게 들려주었다. 산초 판사가 들려준 이야기에 둘은 모두 깜짝 놀랐으니, 돈키호테의 광기와 그 본질을 알고 있었음에도 그 이야기를 들을 때마다 새삼스러운 경탄에 사로잡혔던 것이다. 이어서 그들은 산초 판사에게 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님께 가져가는 그 편지를 보여 달라고 청했다. 그는 그것이 수첩에 적혀 있으며, 처음 다다르는 마을에서 종이에 베껴 써야 한다는 것이 주인의 지시였다고 말했다. 그러자 신부는 그것을 자기에게 보여 주면 자기가 직접 정서해 주겠노라고 말했다. 산초는 수첩을 찾으려고 품 안에 손을 넣었으나 찾을 수 없었으니, 설령 지금까지 뒤졌다 한들 찾아낼 수 없었을 것이다. 돈키호테가 그것을 자기가 지니고 있으면서 그에게 건넨 적이 없었고, 산초 자신도 그것을 달라고 할 생각을 한 적이 없었기 때문이다. 수첩을 찾을 수 없음을 알아챈 산초의 얼굴은 죽은 사람처럼 새파랗게 질렸고, 그는 몹시 다급하게 다시 한번 온몸을 더듬어 보았으나 도무지 찾을 수 없음이 분명해지자, 더 볼 것도 없이 두 손으로 자기 수염을 움켜쥐고 절반을 쥐어뜯더니, 이어서 할 수 있는 한 빠르게 멈추지도 않고 제 얼굴과 코를 예닐곱 차례 후려쳐서 온통 피범벅이 되도록 만들었다.\n\n이 광경을 본 신부와 이발사는 그에게 무슨 일이 있었기에 그토록 거칠게 자신을 다루느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"제게 무슨 일이 있었느냐고요?\" 산초가 대답했다. \"한순간에, 손에서 손으로, 저마다 성채만 한 새끼 나귀 세 마리를 잃은 것밖에 더 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그게 어찌 된 일인가?\" 이발사가 말했다.\n\n\"수첩을 잃어버렸지 뭡니까.\" 산초가 말했다. \"둘시네아께 보내는 편지가 들어 있고, 또 주인님이 서명한 증서가 들어 있었는데, 그 증서에서 주인님은 조카딸에게 집에 있는 네댓 마리 가운데 새끼 나귀 세 마리를 제게 주라고 일러 두셨거든요.\" 그러고는 잿빛 당나귀를 잃어버린 일에 대해서도 그들에게 들려주었다.\n\n신부는 그를 위로하며, 주인이 발견되면 그더러 그 증서를 다시 쓰게 하여, 으레 그러하듯 종이에 새로 작성하도록 시키겠노라고 말했다. 수첩에 쓴 것은 결코 받아들여지거나 효력을 인정받지 못하는 법이라면서 말이다.\n\n산초는 이 말에 마음을 놓고는, 그렇다면 둘시네아의 편지를 잃은 것은 그리 걱정되지 않노라고, 그것을 거의 외우다시피 하고 있으니 그들이 원하는 곳에서 원하는 때에 받아 적게 할 수 있다고 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 외워 보게, 산초.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"그러면 우리가 나중에 그것을 받아 적겠네.\"\n\n산초 판사는 편지를 기억해 내려고 머리를 긁적이며 멈춰 서서, 한 발로 섰다가 다른 발로 섰다가 하며 몸을 가누었고, 한순간은 땅바닥을 노려보다가 다음 순간엔 하늘을 올려다보았다. 그렇게 손가락 끝을 절반쯤 물어뜯어 가며 두 사람이 그가 시작하기를 애타게 기다리도록 만든 끝에, 한참을 뜸 들이고 나서 그가 말했다. \"맙소사, 학사 양반, 편지 내용이라곤 도무지 한 자도 떠오르질 않습니다. 그래도 첫머리는 '드높고 빨래하시는 마님'이라고 되어 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"'빨래하시는'일 리가 없네.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"'초인적인'이거나 '지엄하신'이겠지.\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그런 다음 제 기억으로는 이렇게 이어졌습니다. '상처 입고, 잠 못 이루고, 꿰뚫린 자가 그대의 손에 입 맞추나이다, 은혜를 모르고 도무지 알아주지 않는 어여쁜 이여.' 그러고는 자기가 그분께 보내는 건강이며 병환이며 하는 무엇무엇을 어쩌고저쩌고 말했고, 거기서부터는 꼬리를 끌듯 흐릿해지다가 마침내 '죽을 때까지 그대의 것, 슬픈 몰골의 기사'로 끝을 맺었습니다.\"\n\n산초의 기억력이 이토록 좋은 것을 보고 두 사람은 적잖이 즐거워하며 그를 크게 칭찬했고, 자기들도 그것을 외워 두었다가 이내 받아 적을 수 있도록 편지를 두어 번 더 외워 달라고 청했다. 산초는 세 번 외웠고, 그러면서 또 삼천 가지나 되는 터무니없는 소리를 늘어놓았다. 그러고는 주인에 관해 더 많은 이야기를 들려주었으나, 그 객줏집에서 자기가 당했던 담요 키질 봉변에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 그는 그곳에 들어가기를 한사코 거부했던 것이다. 그는 또한, 만일 자기 주인이 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님에게서 좋은 답을 받아 온다면 황제, 적어도 군주가 되려고 길을 나설 작정이라고 들려주었다. 둘 사이에 그렇게 정해 둔 일이었고, 그의 인품과 그 팔의 위력이라면 그리되는 것쯤은 손쉬운 일이라는 것이었다. 그리고 그리되면 자기 주인은 그를 위해 혼처를 마련해 주기로 — 당연히 그때쯤이면 주인은 홀아비가 되어 있을 터이므로 — 했으며, 황후의 시녀들 가운데 하나를, 곧 본토에 있는 어느 부유하고 으리으리한 영지의 상속녀를 아내로 주기로 했다고, 자기는 이제 섬 따위에는 아무런 관심도 없으니 어떤 종류의 섬과도 상관없는 처녀를 주기로 했다고 했다. 이 모든 것을 산초는 — 이따금 코를 닦아 가며 — 어찌나 태연하게, 어찌나 분별없이 늘어놓는지, 두 청자는 이 가련한 사내의 이성마저 앗아 갈 수 있는 돈키호테의 광기의 위력에 다시금 경탄을 금치 못했다. 그들은 그의 잘못을 굳이 일깨워 주려 들지 않았으니, 그것이 그의 양심을 조금도 해치지 않는 이상 그대로 두는 편이 낫겠다고 여겼고, 또 그래야 그의 어수룩한 소리를 듣는 재미가 그만큼 더 쏠쏠하리라 생각했기 때문이다. 그리하여 그들은 그더러 주인의 건강을 위해 하느님께 기도하라고 일렀으니, 그가 말한 대로 머잖아 황제가 되거나, 적어도 대주교나 그에 맞먹는 어떤 고관이 되는 것이 충분히 있을 법하고 또 충분히 이루어질 만한 일이라면서 말이다.\n\n이에 산초가 대답했다. \"여러분, 만일 운명이 일을 그렇게 풀어 가서 제 주인님이 황제 대신 대주교가 되실 마음을 품으신다면, 떠도는 대주교님들이 보통 그 종자들에게 무엇을 내려 주는지 알고 싶습니다그려.\"\n\n\"보통 내려 주기를,\" 신부가 말했다. \"성직록이나 본당 자리, 혹은 성구지기 같은 자리를 주는데, 그것이 짭짤하게 고정된 수입을 안겨 주지. 제단 봉헌금은 따로 치고도 말일세. 그 봉헌금도 어림잡아 그만큼은 되거든.\"\n\n\"하지만 그러려면,\" 산초가 말했다. \"종자가 미혼이어야 하고, 적어도 미사 거드는 법쯤은 알아야 할 텐데요. 그런데 그렇다면, 아이고 내 신세야, 저는 이미 장가든 몸인 데다 가나다라의 첫 글자도 모르거든요. 떠도는 기사님들이 으레 그러하듯, 제 주인님이 황제가 아니라 대주교가 되실 마음을 품으시면 저는 어찌 됩니까?\"\n\n\"마음 졸이지 말게, 산초 친구.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"우리가 자네 주인께 간청하고 조언하여, 양심의 문제로까지 들이대 가며 대주교가 아니라 황제가 되시라고 강권하겠네. 그분은 글재주보다 무용이 더 뛰어나시니 그편이 그분께도 더 수월할 테니 말이야.\"\n\n\"저도 그리 생각했습니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하기야 그분이 무슨 일에든 능하시다는 건 제가 장담할 수 있습니다만. 제가 제 몫으로 하려는 일이라곤, 그분께 가장 좋은 자리에, 그리고 제게 가장 많은 은혜를 베풀어 주실 수 있는 자리에 그분을 두어 달라고 우리 주님께 기도드리는 것뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"분별 있는 사람처럼 말하는구먼.\" 신부가 말했다. \"또 착한 기독교인답게 행동하는 것일세. 그러나 지금 해야 할 일은, 자네 말로는 자네 주인이 치르고 있다는 그 부질없는 고행에서 그분을 살살 꾀어내도록 손을 쓰는 것일세. 어떤 방책을 취할지 궁리도 하고, 또 식사도 할 겸 — 이제 그럴 때가 되었으니 — 우리는 이 객줏집으로 들어가는 게 좋겠네.\"\n\n산초는 두 사람더러 들어가도 좋으나 자기는 밖에서 기다리겠다고, 어째서 들어가기 싫은지, 어째서 그곳에 들어가는 것이 자기에게 맞지 않는지는 나중에 일러 주겠노라고 말했다. 다만 그는 먹을 것을 좀 내다 달라고, 그것도 뜨거운 것으로 해 달라고, 또 로시난테에게 줄 보리도 가져다 달라고 부탁했다. 그들은 그를 두고 안으로 들어갔고, 이내 이발사가 그에게 먹을 것을 내다 주었다. 얼마 뒤, 두 사람이 자기들 목적을 이루기 위해 무엇을 해야 할지 함께 곰곰이 궁리한 끝에, 신부가 돈키호테의 비위를 맞추면서도 자기들 뜻을 이루기에 매우 알맞은 한 가지 묘안을 떠올렸다. 그가 이발사에게 설명한 그 생각인즉, 자기는 떠도는 처녀로 변장하고 다른 한쪽은 되도록 종자로 보이도록 꾸민 뒤, 그렇게 돈키호테가 있는 곳으로 가서, 신부가 억울하고 곤경에 처한 처녀인 척 그에게 한 가지 청을 드리되, 용맹한 떠돌이 기사로서 그가 차마 거절할 수 없는 청을 드린다는 것이었다. 그가 청하려는 그 부탁이란, 어느 사악한 기사가 그녀에게 저지른 잘못을 바로잡기 위해 그녀가 그를 데려가려는 곳으로 동행해 달라는 것이었고, 그러면서 동시에 그 사악한 기사에게서 자기를 위해 정의를 세워 줄 때까지는 가면을 벗으라고 요구하지도, 자기 사정에 관해 어떤 질문도 하지 말아 달라고 간청한다는 것이었다. 그리고 그는 돈키호테가 이런 식으로 올린 어떤 청이든 들어주리라는 데 추호의 의심도 없었으며, 이런 방법으로 그를 끌어내어 그의 마을로 데려갈 수 있으리라고, 그곳에서 그의 유별난 광기에 어떤 종류든 치유책이 통할 여지가 있는지 알아보리라고 생각했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVII.\n\n신부의 계획에 대한 이발사의 생각과, 이 위대한 이야기에 기록할 만한 다른 사정들에 관하여\n\n신부의 계획은 이발사에게 나쁘게 여겨지지 않았다. 도리어 어찌나 좋게 여겨졌던지 두 사람은 곧장 그것을 실행에 옮기기 시작했다. 그들은 여관 안주인에게 치마와 두건을 빌려 달라고 청하면서 그 담보로 신부의 새 사제복을 맡겨 두었고, 이발사는 여관 주인이 빗을 꽂아 두곤 하던 잿빛 도는 갈색, 혹은 붉은 황소 꼬리로 수염을 만들었다. 안주인이 그 물건들을 어디에 쓸 작정이냐고 묻자, 신부는 돈키호테의 광기에 대해 몇 마디로 일러 주고, 이 변장이 그가 지금 머물고 있는 산에서 그를 끌어내기 위한 것임을 설명했다. 여관 주인 내외는 즉시 그 미치광이가 다름 아닌 자기네 손님, 곧 그 영약을 가진 사내이자 담요 키질을 당한 종자의 주인임을 알아차렸고, 산초가 그토록 입을 다물어 온 일까지 빠뜨리지 않고, 그와 자기들 사이에 오갔던 모든 일을 신부에게 일러 주었다. 마침내 안주인은 더할 나위 없을 만큼 그럴듯한 차림으로 신부를 꾸며 주었다. 그에게 한 뼘 너비의 검은 우단 띠를 죽 두른, 온통 칼집을 낸 천 치마를 입히고, 흰 공단으로 가장자리를 두른 초록 우단 보디스를 걸쳐 주었는데, 이 보디스도 치마도 틀림없이 왐바 왕 시절에 지어진 것이리라. 신부는 두건만은 쓰려 하지 않고, 머리에는 자기가 잠잘 때 쓰던 누비 삼베 모자를 얹고, 검은 비단 끈으로 이마를 동여맸으며, 또 다른 끈으로는 수염과 얼굴을 아주 잘 가려 주는 가리개를 만들었다. 그러고는 양산으로 써도 좋을 만큼 챙이 넓은 모자를 쓰고, 외투로 몸을 휘감은 채 여인네처럼 모로 앉아 노새에 올랐다. 이발사도 자기 노새에 올랐는데, 허리까지 내려오는 붉고 흰 빛깔이 뒤섞인 수염을 달았으니, 앞서 말했듯 그것은 진흙빛 붉은 황소의 꼬리였던 것이다. * 왐바 왕: 7세기 서고트 왕국의 왕으로, 까마득히 오래된 시절을 가리키는 관용 표현.\n\n그들은 모두에게, 그리고 비록 죄 많은 여인이긴 하나 그들이 떠맡은 그토록 고되고 그리스도교적인 일에 하느님께서 성공을 내려 주시기를 묵주 기도로 빌어 주겠노라 약속한 착한 마리토르네스에게 작별을 고했다. 그런데 신부가 여관을 나서자마자, 자신이 그런 식으로 차려입은 것이 잘못이라는 생각이 그를 찔렀다. 비록 거기에 많은 것이 달려 있다 한들 사제가 이런 차림을 하는 것은 점잖지 못한 일이었기 때문이다. 그래서 이발사에게 그 말을 하며 옷을 바꿔 입자고 청했으니, 이발사가 곤경에 처한 처녀 노릇을 하는 편이 더 어울리고, 자기는 종자 역을 맡는 편이 자기 품위를 덜 손상시키리라는 것이었다. 그렇지 않으면 이 일에서 손을 떼고 돈키호테 따위는 악마에게나 넘겨 버리겠다고 마음먹은 터였다. 바로 그 순간 산초가 다가왔는데, 그런 차림을 한 두 사람을 보고는 웃음을 참을 수가 없었다. 그러나 이발사는 신부가 바라는 대로 하기로 했고, 계획을 바꾼 신부는 그가 어떻게 역을 해내야 하는지, 또 돈키호테가 자기가 게으른 고행을 위해 골라잡은 그곳을 단념하고 자기들과 함께 가도록 꾀고 다그치려면 그에게 무어라 말해야 하는지를 일러 주기 시작했다. 이발사는 아무 지시 없이도 제대로 해낼 수 있노라 말했고, 돈키호테가 있는 곳 가까이 이르기 전에는 변장을 하고 싶지 않다며 옷가지를 접어 두었다. 신부는 수염을 손보았고, 두 사람은 산초 판사의 안내를 받아 길을 나섰다. 산초는 길을 가면서 시에라에서 만난 미치광이와 마주쳤던 일을 그들에게 이야기했으나, 여행 가방과 그 속에 든 것을 주운 일에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 그 어수룩함에도 불구하고 이 젊은이에게는 욕심이 조금 있었던 것이다.\n\n이튿날 그들은 산초가 주인을 두고 떠난 곳으로 자신을 인도하려고 표지 삼아 금작화 가지들을 놓아 두었던 자리에 다다랐다. 그곳을 알아본 산초는 여기가 입구라고 일러 주면서, 주인을 구해 내는 데 변장이 필요하다면 옷을 차려입는 것이 좋겠다고 말했다. 그도 그럴 것이 그들은 이미 산초에게, 그의 주인을 그가 택한 해로운 삶에서 구해 내자면 이런 모습으로 가서 이렇게 차려입는 것이 더없이 중요하다고 일러 둔 터였다. 그리고 그들은 산초에게 자기들이 누구인지, 또 그가 자기들을 안다는 것을 결코 주인에게 말하지 말라고 단단히 일렀으며, 주인이 둘시네아에게 편지를 전했느냐고 물으면 — 틀림없이 물을 터인데 — 전했노라 대답하고, 그분은 글을 읽을 줄 모르시기에 말로 답을 주셨으니, 노여움을 사고 싶지 않거든 당장 자기를 보러 오라 명하시더라고 말하라고 일렀다. 이것은 그 자신에게도 매우 중요한 일이었으니, 이런 식으로 또 그들이 그에게 하려는 말로써 주인을 더 나은 삶의 길로 데려오고, 황제나 군주가 되기 위한 조치를 당장 취하도록 꾈 수 있으리라 확신했기 때문이다. 대주교가 될 염려야 없는 일이었으니 말이다. 산초는 이 모든 것을 귀담아듣고 머릿속에 단단히 새겨 두었으며, 자기 주인을 대주교가 아니라 황제가 되도록 권할 작정이라는 데 진심으로 고마워했으니, 종자에게 상을 내리는 일에 있어서는 황제가 편력 대주교보다 더 많은 것을 해 줄 수 있으리라 확신했기 때문이다. 그는 또한 자기가 먼저 앞서 가서 주인을 찾아 그 귀부인의 답을 전하는 편이 좋겠다고 말했으니, 어쩌면 그것만으로도 이런 수고를 다 들이지 않고도 주인을 그곳에서 데려오기에 충분하리라는 것이었다. 그들은 산초가 내놓은 제안을 받아들였고, 그가 주인을 찾았다는 기별을 가지고 돌아올 때까지 기다리기로 했다.\n\n산초는 시에라의 골짜기들 안으로 밀고 들어갔고, 두 사람은 자그마한 시냇물이 부드럽게 흐르고 바위와 나무들이 서늘하고 고마운 그늘을 드리운 골짜기 하나에 남겨 두었다. 때는 한여름의 온갖 더위를 다 갖춘 팔월의 어느 날이었고, 그 지방의 더위는 모질었으며, 시각은 오후 세 시였으니, 이 모든 것이 그 자리를 한층 마음 끌리게 만들어 거기서 산초가 돌아오기를 기다리도록 그들을 꾀었고, 그들은 그렇게 했다. 그리하여 그들이 그늘에서 쉬고 있는데, 어떤 악기의 가락도 곁들이지 않았으나 그 음색이 감미롭고 듣기 좋은 목소리 하나가 그들의 귀에 닿았으니, 노래를 그토록 잘 부르는 이가 머물 만한 곳으로는 그곳이 도무지 여겨지지 않았기에 그들은 적잖이 놀랐다. 흔히들 숲과 들에서 보기 드물게 고운 목소리의 목동들을 만날 수 있다고들 하지만, 이는 진실이라기보다 시인의 상상이 빚어낸 비약에 가깝기 때문이다. 그리고 그들이 들은 노래가 시골 목동의 시구가 아니라 도회의 세련된 재사들의 시구임을 알아차렸을 때 그들은 한층 더 놀랐다. 과연 그러했으니, 그들이 들은 시구는 이러했다.\n\n내 행복을 향한 추구를 헛되이 보이게 하는 것은 무엇인가? 멸시로다. 내게 안식의 희망을 버리라 명하는 것은 무엇인가? 질투로다. 내 가슴을 애타는 불안 속에 붙들어 두는 것은 무엇인가? 부재로다. 그렇다면, 내 슬픔을 달랠 위안을 어디서 찾아야 하랴, 멸시와 질투와 부재로 말미암아 희망이 사방에서 살해당해 누웠거늘?\n\n내 온갖 비탄의 으뜸가는 원인임이 드러나는 것은 무엇인가? 사랑이로다. 내 영광을 늘 곁눈으로 흘겨보는 것은 무엇인가? 운명이로다. 나를 괴롭힐 허락이 어디서 내려오는가? 하늘이로다. 그렇다면 나는 그저 거역할 수 없는 운명의 일격을 기다릴 뿐, 내 비탄을 위해 손잡고 일하는 이 셋, 사랑과 운명과 하늘을 보고 있으니.\n\n치유책을 찾으려면 나는 무엇을 해야 하는가? 죽음이로다. 수줍고 매정한 사랑을 꾈 미끼는 무엇인가? 변심이로다. 모두 헛되이 끝난다면, 무엇이 슬픈 가슴을 낫게 하랴? 광기로다. 그렇다면 우울을 치유할 약을 찾는다는 것은 한낱 어리석은 짓, 그것이 어디 있느냐 물으면, 대답은 이러하니 변심에, 광기에, 아니면 죽음에 있노라.\n\n그 시각, 여름철, 외딴 자리, 노래하는 이의 목소리와 솜씨, 이 모든 것이 두 청자의 경탄과 기쁨에 한몫하였으니, 그들은 무언가 더 들으려고 잠자코 기다렸다. 그러나 침묵이 한동안 이어지자 그들은 그토록 고운 목소리로 노래하는 그 악사를 찾아 나서기로 했다. 그런데 막 그러려는 참에 똑같은 목소리에 발이 묶이고 말았으니, 그 목소리가 다시 한 번 그들의 귀에 떨어지며 이런 소네트를 노래했던 것이다.\n소네트\n\n거룩한 우정이여, 그대 하늘로 올라 하늘 너머 그대의 집을 찾아 날아오르고 드높이 성인들 가운데 자리를 잡으려 할 적에, 그대는 이 땅 아래에 그대의 형상을 남기고 그 위에 그대의 너울을 베푸는 것이 그대 뜻이었으니, 위선이 때로 그 너울을 두르고 그대의 모습으로 활보하며 눈을 속이고 그 추악함을 미덕인 양 밝게 꾸며 보이는도다. 우정이여, 우리에게 돌아오라, 아니면 지금 그것을 두른 그 사기꾼더러 그대의 제복을 돌려 놓도록 다그치라, 그것의 힘을 빌려 진실이 살해당하고 있으니. 그대가 그대의 가짜를 벗겨 내지 않으려거든, 이 땅은 다시 한 번 다툼의 먹이가 되리라, 태초의 불화가 그 지배를 떨치던 그때처럼.\n\n노래는 깊은 한숨과 더불어 끝났고, 청자들은 다시 노래하는 이가 이어 가기를 골똘히 귀 기울이며 기다렸다. 그러나 이제 그 노랫소리가 흐느낌과 가슴을 에는 신음으로 변한 것을 알아차린 그들은, 그 목소리가 보기 드물게 곱듯이 그 한숨도 그토록 가련한, 그 불행한 존재가 대체 누구인지 알아내기로 마음먹었다. 그들이 얼마 가지 않아 어느 바위 모퉁이를 돌았을 때, 산초가 카르데니오의 이야기를 들려주며 묘사했던 것과 똑같은 행색과 모습의 한 사내를 발견했다. 그는 그들을 보고도 아무런 놀라움을 드러내지 않고, 깊은 생각에 잠긴 사람처럼 고개를 가슴께로 푹 숙인 채 가만히 서 있었으며, 그들이 불쑥 나타났을 때 처음 한 번 흘긋 본 뒤로는 눈을 들어 그들을 쳐다보지도 않았다. 그의 불행을 알고 있던 신부는 그 묘사로 그를 알아보고는, 말솜씨가 좋은 사람인지라 그에게 다가가 분별 있는 몇 마디로, 그토록 비참한 삶을 버리라고 간청하고 다그쳤다. 자칫 그곳에서 그 삶을 끝장내기라도 한다면 그것이야말로 모든 불행 가운데 가장 큰 불행이 되리라는 것이었다. 카르데니오는 그때 제정신이어서, 그토록 자주 그를 휩쓸어 가던 그 광기의 발작에서 벗어나 있었다. 그래서 그 황량한 곳을 드나드는 이들 사이에서는 좀처럼 보기 힘든 차림을 한 그들을 보고는 얼마간 놀라움을 드러내지 않을 수 없었으니, 더구나 그들이 마치 자기 사정을 잘 아는 일인 양 이야기하는 것을 들었을 때는 더욱 그러했다(신부의 말이 그에게 그만큼을 짐작하게 했던 것이다). 그리하여 그는 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"여러분, 그대들이 누구이시든, 저는 똑똑히 알겠습니다. 착한 이들을 — 그리고 흔히는 악한 이들마저 — 보살피시는 것을 소임으로 삼으시는 하늘이, 인간의 왕래가 끊긴 이 외진 곳에, 그럴 자격도 없는 저에게, 이곳에서 저를 끌어내어 더 나은 안식처로 데려가려는 분들을 보내 주셨음을 말입니다. 제가 이런 삶을 사는 것이 얼마나 이치에 어긋나는지를 여러 강력한 논거로 보여 주시면서 말이지요. 그러나 그분들은, 제가 이 불행에서 벗어난다 해도 더 큰 또 다른 불행에 빠지리라는 것을 모르기에, 어쩌면 저를 마음 약한 자, 아니 더 나쁘게는 분별을 잃은 자로 여길지도 모릅니다. 그렇다 해도 이상할 것은 없으니, 저 자신도 알 수 있기 때문입니다. 제 불행을 떠올리는 일이 제게 미치는 영향이 어찌나 크고 제 파멸을 향해 어찌나 세차게 작용하는지, 저는 저도 모르게 때때로 돌처럼 되어 아무 느낌도 의식도 없어지고 맙니다. 그리고 그 끔찍한 발작이 저를 사로잡았을 때 제가 저지른 짓들을 그분들이 일러 주고 증거를 보여 줄 때면, 저는 그것이 사실임을 비로소 느끼게 됩니다. 그럴 때 제가 할 수 있는 일이라고는, 헛되이 제 신세를 한탄하고 부질없이 제 운명을 저주하며, 들어 주고자 하는 이라면 누구에게든 그 광기가 어떻게 비롯되었는지를 이야기함으로써 그것을 변명하는 것뿐입니다. 이성을 갖춘 이라면 그 원인을 알고 나서는 그 결과에 놀라지 않을 것이고, 설령 저를 돕지는 못한다 해도 적어도 저를 탓하지는 않을 것이며, 제 사나운 행실에 느끼던 거부감이 제 비탄에 대한 연민으로 바뀔 것이기 때문입니다. 여러분, 만일 그대들이 다른 이들이 가졌던 것과 같은 뜻을 품고 이곳에 오신 것이라면, 그 현명한 논거를 펼치시기에 앞서, 부디 제 헤아릴 수 없는 불행의 이야기를 들어 주시기를 간청합니다. 어쩌면 그것을 다 듣고 나면, 위안의 손길이 닿지 않는 슬픔에 위로를 건네느라 들이실 수고를 스스로 덜게 되실 테니까요.\"\n\n두 사람은 다른 무엇보다도 그의 고통의 원인을 그 자신의 입으로 듣기를 바랐던 터라, 그가 원치 않는 일은 그의 위안이나 평안을 위해서라도 아무것도 하지 않겠노라 약속하며 그것을 이야기해 달라고 간청했다. 그러자 이 불행한 신사는 며칠 전 돈키호테와 염소지기에게 들려주었던 것과 거의 같은 말과 방식으로 자신의 슬픈 이야기를 시작했다. 그때는 엘리사바드 선생을 둘러싸고, 또 기사도에 합당한 도리를 향한 돈키호테의 깐깐한 고집으로 말미암아, 이 이야기에 이미 적힌 대로 그 이야기가 끝맺지 못한 채로 남았었다. 그러나 이번에는 다행히 광기의 발작이 비켜 가 그가 끝까지 이야기할 수 있게 해 주었다. 그리하여 돈 페르난도가 「아마디스 데 가울라」 책 속에서 찾아냈던 그 쪽지의 일에 이르러, 카르데니오는 그것을 똑똑히 기억하고 있으며 그 내용이 이러했노라 말했다.\n\n\"루신다가 카르데니오께.\n\n날마다 저는 당신에게서, 당신을 더 높이 우러르지 않을 수 없게 하는 미덕들을 발견합니다. 그러니 제 명예를 손상하지 않으면서 이 의무에서 저를 풀어 주고자 하신다면, 당신은 손쉽게 그리하실 수 있습니다. 제게는 당신을 알고 저를 끔찍이 아끼시는 아버지가 계신데, 제 마음에 아무런 강요도 하지 않으시며, 당신이 말씀하시고 또 제가 믿는 대로 당신이 정녕 저를 귀히 여기신다면, 당신이 가져 마땅한 것을 허락해 주실 것입니다.\"\n\n\"이 편지로 말미암아, 앞서 말씀드렸듯, 저는 루신다를 제 아내로 청하게 되었고, 또 이 편지로 말미암아 루신다는 돈 페르난도에게 당대에 가장 사려 깊고 슬기로운 여인 가운데 하나로 여겨지게 되었습니다. 그리고 제 일이 이루어지기 전에 저를 먼저 파멸시키려는 그의 계략을 부추긴 것도 바로 이 편지였습니다. 저는 돈 페르난도에게, 루신다의 아버지가 기다리고 계신 것은 다름 아니라 제 아버지가 그분께 루신다를 청하시는 일뿐이라고 말했습니다. 그런데 그 일을 제 아버지께 감히 입에 올리지 못했으니, 아버지가 응하지 않으실까 두려웠기 때문입니다. 그것은 아버지가 루신다의 신분과 착함과 미덕과 아름다움을 더없이 잘 아셨기 때문이지 — 그녀는 스페인의 어느 가문에라도 영예가 될 만한 자질을 지닌 여인이었으니까요 — 그런 까닭이 아니라, 리카르도 공작께서 저를 위해 무엇을 해 주실지 보시기 전에는 제가 그토록 일찍 혼인하기를 원치 않으심을 제가 알고 있었기 때문입니다. 한마디로, 저는 그에게 제 아버지께 그 말을 꺼낼 엄두가 나지 않노라 했으니, 그 어려움 때문이기도 했고, 또 무엇인지 저로서도 잘 알 수 없으면서 저를 주눅 들게 하던 여러 다른 일들 때문이기도 했습니다. 다만 제가 바라는 일이 결코 이루어지지 못할 것만 같았던 것이지요. 이 모든 것에 돈 페르난도는, 자기가 나서서 제 아버지께 말씀드리고, 또 아버지를 설득해 루신다의 아버지께 말씀하시도록 하겠노라 대답했습니다. 오, 야심에 찬 마리우스여! 오, 잔혹한 카틸리나여! 오, 사악한 술라여! 오, 배신자 가넬론이여! 오, 음험한 베이도여! 오, 앙심 깊은 훌리안이여! 오, 탐욕스러운 유다여! 배신자, 잔혹한 자, 앙심 깊은 자, 음험한 자여, 그토록 솔직하게 제 마음속 비밀과 기쁨을 그대에게 보여 준 이 가련한 자가 그 충심에서 무엇을 잘못했단 말인가? 내가 무슨 죄를 지었던가? 내가 무슨 말을 입에 담았으며, 그대의 명예와 안녕을 도모하려는 것 말고 다른 목적을 둔 무슨 충고를 했단 말인가? 그러나 아아, 가련한 신세여, 어이하여 나는 한탄하는가? 불행이 별에서 솟아날 적에는 높은 곳에서 내려와 그토록 사납고 거센 기세로 우리에게 덮쳐 오니, 이 땅의 어떤 힘도 그 진로를 막을 수 없고 인간의 어떤 꾀로도 그 닥쳐옴을 멈출 수 없음이 분명하기에 하는 말입니다. 돈 페르난도가 — 가문 높은 신사요, 총명하며, 제가 베푼 도움에 대한 고마움으로 저와 맺어진 사람이요, 어디에 마음을 두든 자기가 사랑하는 대상을 얻어 낼 수 있는 사람인 그가 — 흔히 말하듯, 아직 제 손에 들어오지도 않은 단 한 마리의 새끼 암양마저 제게서 빼앗을 만큼 그토록 모질어질 수 있으리라고 그 누가 생각이나 했겠습니까? 그러나 이 부질없고 쓸데없는 넋두리는 접어 두고, 제 불행한 이야기의 끊어진 실타래를 다시 잡아 보겠습니다.\n\n이어 말씀드리자면, 돈 페르난도는 자신의 음흉하고 사악한 계획을 실행하는 데 제가 곁에 있는 것이 걸림돌임을 알고는, 자기 형에게 돈을 청하러 간다는 핑계로 저를 형에게 보내기로 마음먹었습니다. 말 여섯 필 값을 치를 돈이라고 했는데, 그 말들은 그가 일부러, 오직 저를 멀리 쫓아 보내어 자기 흉악한 계략을 더 수월히 꾸미려는 단 하나의 목적으로, 바로 제 아버지께 말씀드리겠노라 제안하던 그날 사들인 것이었습니다. 그러고는 이제 그 값을 제가 가서 받아 오기를 바란 것이지요. 제가 이 배신을 미리 내다볼 수 있었겠습니까? 무슨 수로 그것을 의심할 수 있었겠습니까? 천만에, 그러기는커녕 저는 흥정이 잘되었다는 만족감에 기꺼이 당장 가겠노라 자청했습니다. 그날 밤 저는 루신다와 이야기를 나누며, 돈 페르난도와 합의한 바를 그녀에게 일러 주고, 우리의 어여쁘고 온당한 소망이 이루어지리라는 큰 희망을 품고 있노라 말했습니다. 그녀도 저처럼 돈 페르난도의 배신을 조금도 의심하지 못한 채, 어서 속히 돌아오도록 애써 달라고 당부했으니, 우리 소망의 성취가 늦어지는 것은 오직 제 아버지가 그녀의 아버지께 말씀하시기를 미루는 동안뿐이리라 믿었던 것입니다. 그런데 그녀가 제게 이 말을 할 때 어찌하여 두 눈에 눈물이 가득 고이고 목이 메어, 제게 더 하려는 듯싶던 많은 말들 가운데 단 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 못했는지, 저는 까닭을 알지 못했습니다. 저는 이 예사롭지 않은 모습에 놀랐으니, 전에는 그녀에게서 한 번도 보지 못한 것이었기 때문입니다. 우리는 행운과 제 꾀가 기회를 줄 때면 언제나 더없는 즐거움과 명랑함으로 이야기를 나누며, 그 말들 속에 눈물과 한숨과 질투와 의심과 두려움을 뒤섞곤 했습니다. 제 쪽에서는 모든 것이, 하늘이 그녀를 제 연인으로 주셨다는 제 행운에 대한 찬미였습니다. 저는 그녀의 아름다움을 기리고, 그녀의 가치와 분별을 드높였으며, 그녀는 저를 향한 사랑으로 제게서 칭찬받을 만하다 여긴 것을 칭찬함으로써 제게 되갚아 주었습니다. 게다가 우리에게는 이웃들과 아는 이들에 관한 수십만 가지 자질구레한 이야깃거리며 일들이 있었지요. 그리고 제 대담함이 미친 최대치라고는, 우리를 갈라놓은 낮은 격자창의 좁은 틈이 허락하는 한, 그 어여쁘고 흰 손 하나를 거의 억지로 잡아 입술로 가져가는 것뿐이었습니다. 그러나 제가 떠나야 할 그 불행한 날의 전날 밤, 그녀는 울고, 신음하고, 한숨지었으며, 저를 당혹과 경악에 휩싸인 채 남겨 두고 물러갔으니, 루신다에게서 그토록 야릇하고 가슴 저미는 슬픔과 비탄의 기색을 본 저는 압도되고 말았습니다. 그러나 제 희망을 꺾지 않으려고, 저는 그 모든 것을 저를 향한 그녀의 깊은 사랑과, 애틋이 사랑하는 이들에게 이별이 안기는 아픔 탓으로 돌렸습니다. 마침내 저는 슬프고 의기소침한 채, 가슴은 온갖 망상과 의혹으로 가득 찬 채 길을 떠났으나, 제가 무엇을 의심하고 무엇을 망상하는지 저 자신도 잘 몰랐습니다. 다만 저를 기다리고 있던 그 슬픈 사건과 불행을 가리키는 또렷한 전조들이 있었을 뿐입니다.\n\n저는 보내진 곳에 다다라 돈 페르난도의 형에게 편지를 건넸고, 친절한 대접을 받았으나 곧바로 돌려보내지지는 않았습니다. 그가 제게, 몹시 제 뜻에 거슬리는 일이었지만, 자기 아버지인 공작의 눈에 띄지 않을 만한 어느 곳에서 여드레를 기다리라고 했기 때문입니다. 그의 동생이 편지에 그 돈을 아버지 모르게 보내라고 적었던 까닭이었지요. 이 모든 것이 음흉한 돈 페르난도의 계략이었으니, 그의 형에게는 저를 당장 떠나보낼 돈이 부족할 리 없었기 때문입니다.\n\n그 명령은 제가 그것을 어기고픈 유혹에 빠지게 하는 것이었으니, 루신다와 그토록 여러 날을 떨어져 산다는 것이 저로서는 견딜 수 없는 일로 여겨졌기 때문입니다. 더구나 앞서 말씀드린 그 슬픈 기색 속에 그녀를 두고 떠나온 뒤였으니 말이지요. 그러나 도리를 지키는 종복으로서 저는 복종했으니, 비록 그것이 제 행복을 대가로 치르는 일이 되리라 느끼면서도 그리했습니다. 그런데 나흘 뒤 한 사내가 저를 찾아와 편지 한 통을 건넸는데, 겉봉으로 보아 루신다에게서 온 것임을 알아차렸으니, 글씨가 그녀의 것이었기 때문입니다. 저는 두려움과 떨림에 휩싸여 그것을 열었으니, 제가 가까이 있을 때는 좀처럼 그러지 않던 그녀가 먼 곳에 있는 제게 편지를 쓰도록 떠민 것이라면 틀림없이 무언가 심상찮은 일이리라 여겼던 것입니다. 읽기에 앞서 저는 그 사내에게, 누가 그것을 그에게 주었으며 길에 나선 지 얼마나 되었는지 물었습니다. 그는 마침 정오 무렵 그 도시의 어느 거리를 지나다가, 매우 아름다운 한 부인이 창문에서 자기를 불러, 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 다급히 이렇게 말하더랍니다. '형제여, 보아하니 그대가 그리스도교인인 듯하니, 하느님의 사랑으로 그대에게 간청합니다. 부디 이 편지를 한시도 지체 말고 겉봉에 적힌 곳과 사람에게 보내 주세요. 그곳은 잘 알려진 데이며, 이로써 그대는 우리 주님께 큰 봉사를 드리게 될 것입니다. 또 그리하시는 데 불편함이 없도록, 이 손수건 안에 든 것을 받으세요.' 그러고는 '이렇게 말하며 그 부인이 창밖으로 손수건 하나를 던져 주었는데, 그 안에는 백 레알과, 제가 여기 이 편지와 함께 가져온 이 금가락지가 묶여 있었습니다. 그러고는 답을 기다리지도 않고 창에서 물러났는데, 다만 제가 편지와 손수건을 받는 것을 보고, 또 제가 손짓으로 그분이 시키신 대로 하겠다고 알린 뒤에야 그리했습니다. 그리하여 그것을 나리께 가져다 드리는 수고에 대한 삯을 그토록 후하게 받은 데다, 겉봉으로 그것이 나리께 보내진 것임을 알았고(나리, 저는 나리를 아주 잘 압니다), 또 그 아름다운 부인의 눈물을 차마 외면할 수도 없어, 저는 다른 누구에게도 맡기지 않고 몸소 와서 나리께 드리기로 마음먹었습니다. 그래서 그것을 받은 때로부터 열여섯 시간 만에 이 길을 왔으니, 나리도 아시다시피 십팔 레과나 되는 길이지요'라고 했습니다.\n\n이 마음씨 좋은 즉석 전령이 제게 이 이야기를 하는 동안 내내, 저는 그의 말 한마디 한마디에 매달려 있었고, 두 다리가 후들거려 제대로 서 있기조차 힘들 지경이었습니다. 그래도 저는 편지를 열어 이런 말을 읽었습니다.\n\n'돈 페르난도가 당신께, 당신의 아버지를 다그쳐 제 아버지께 말씀하시도록 하겠다고 한 약속을, 그는 당신께 이로운 쪽보다 자기 자신에게 흡족한 쪽으로 훨씬 더 잘 지켰습니다. 당신께 말씀드려야겠으니, 그가 저를 아내로 청했고, 돈 페르난도가 당신보다 낫다고 여기시는 데 마음이 끌린 제 아버지가 그의 청혼을 어찌나 흔쾌히 받아들이셨던지, 이제 이틀 뒤면 약혼식이 치러진다 합니다. 그것도 어찌나 은밀하고 남몰래 행해지는지, 증인이라고는 위로는 하늘이요, 집안의 몇몇 사람뿐입니다. 제가 어떤 처지에 놓여 있는지 그려 보세요. 당신이 와 주셔야 할 일이 얼마나 급한지 헤아려 보세요. 이 일의 결말이 제가 당신을 사랑하는지 아닌지를 당신께 보여 드릴 것입니다. 부디 이것이, 맹세한 신의를 그토록 못되게 지키는 그자와 제 손이 억지로 맺어지기 전에 당신 손에 닿기를 하느님께 빕니다.'\n\n이것이, 간추리자면, 그 편지의 내용이었으니, 이 말들에 저는 답장도 돈도 더 기다리지 않고 당장 길을 나섰습니다. 이제 저는 돈 페르난도가 저를 자기 형에게 보낸 것이 말을 사기 위해서가 아니라 제 욕망을 채우기 위해서였음을 똑똑히 보았기 때문입니다 — 아니, 그 자신의 욕망을 채우기 위해서였지요. 돈 페르난도에게 느낀 격분이 그토록 여러 해의 사랑과 헌신으로 얻은 상을 잃을지도 모른다는 두려움과 한데 어우러져 제게 날개를 달아 주었으니, 그리하여 저는 거의 날다시피 하여 그날 안으로, 루신다와 이야기 나누기에 알맞은 시각에 맞춰 집에 다다랐습니다. 저는 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 도착하여, 타고 온 노새를 제게 편지를 가져다준 그 착한 사내의 집에 두었습니다. 그리고 운명이 이번만큼은 다행히 친절하여, 저는 우리 사랑의 증인이었던 그 격자창 앞에 있는 루신다를 보게 되었습니다. 그녀는 곧 저를 알아보았고, 저도 그녀를 알아보았으나, 그녀가 저를 알아보아야 마땅했을 그 모습으로도, 제가 그녀를 알아보아야 마땅했을 그 모습으로도 아니었습니다. 그러나 세상에 그 누가 여인의 흔들리는 마음과 변덕스러운 천성을 헤아렸다거나 알았노라 뽐낼 수 있겠습니까? 진실로 아무도 없습니다. 이어 말씀드리지요. 루신다는 저를 보자마자 이렇게 말했습니다. '카르데니오, 저는 신부 차림을 하고 있고, 음흉한 돈 페르난도와 탐욕스러운 제 아버지가 다른 증인들과 함께 홀에서 저를 기다리고 있습니다. 그러나 그들은 제 약혼을 보기에 앞서 제 죽음의 증인이 될 것입니다. 괴로워 마세요, 벗이여, 부디 이 제물 바치는 자리에 와 주세요. 그리고 만일 제 말로써 그것을 막을 수 없거든, 제게 숨겨 둔 단검이 있어 그 한층 작정된 폭력을 막아 줄 것이니, 제 목숨을 끊어 제가 당신께 품어 왔고 또 품고 있는 사랑의 첫 증거를 당신께 드리겠습니다.' 저는 답할 틈조차 없을까 두려워, 제정신이 아닌 채 다급히 그녀에게 답했습니다. '그대의 말이 그대의 행동으로 증명되기를, 부인이여. 그리고 그대에게 명예를 지킬 단검이 있다면, 제게는 그대를 지키거나, 운명이 우리를 거스른다면 저 자신을 베어 죽일 칼이 있습니다.'\n\n그녀가 이 모든 말을 다 듣지는 못했으리라 생각합니다. 신랑이 기다리고 있던 터라 그녀를 다급히 불러 가는 것을 제가 보았기 때문입니다. 이제 제 슬픔의 밤이 깃들고, 제 행복의 해가 저물었으며, 저는 두 눈이 시력을 잃고 정신이 이성을 잃는 것을 느꼈습니다. 저는 그 집에 들어갈 수도 없었고, 어떤 움직임도 할 수 없었습니다. 그러나 그 자리에서 벌어질지도 모를 일에 제가 함께 있는 것이 얼마나 중요한지를 헤아리고는, 할 수 있는 한 마음을 다잡아 안으로 들어갔으니, 모든 드나드는 문과 길목을 제가 잘 알고 있었기 때문입니다. 게다가 은밀하게 집안에 퍼져 있던 혼란 덕분에 아무도 저를 눈여겨보지 않았습니다. 그리하여 저는 들키지 않고, 바로 그 홀의 창이 이룬 후미진 자리에 몸을 둘 기회를 얻었으니, 두 휘장의 끝자락과 가장자리에 가려져 그 사이로 들키지 않고 방 안에서 벌어지는 모든 일을 볼 수 있었습니다. 거기 서 있는 동안 제가 겪은 가슴의 동요를 — 제게 밀려든 생각들을, 제 머릿속을 스쳐 간 상념들을 — 그 누가 그려 낼 수 있겠습니까? 그것들은 입에 담을 수 없는 것들이며, 입에 담는다 한들 좋을 것 없는 것들입니다. 신랑이 여느 때 차림 그대로, 아무런 장식도 없이 홀로 들어왔다는 것만 말씀드려도 충분하겠지요. 들러리로는 루신다의 사촌 하나를 데리고 있었고, 집안의 하인들 말고는 방 안에 다른 사람은 아무도 없었습니다. 곧이어 루신다가 그녀의 어머니와 두 시녀의 시중을 받으며 곁방에서 나왔는데, 그 신분과 아름다움에 걸맞게 단장하고 꾸민, 한껏 차린 예복 차림이었습니다. 제 불안과 심란함은 그녀가 무엇을 걸쳤는지 자세히 살피거나 눈여겨보도록 허락하지 않았습니다. 저는 다만 그 빛깔이 진홍과 흰빛임을, 그리고 그녀의 머리 장식과 의상에 박힌 보석과 패물의 반짝임을 알아볼 수 있었을 뿐인데, 그 빛남마저 그녀의 어여쁜 적갈색 머리칼의 보기 드문 아름다움에 압도되고 말았으니, 그 머리칼은 보석들과 홀에 세워진 네 자루 횃불의 빛과 겨루며 그 모두보다 더 밝은 광채로 빛났습니다. 오, 기억이여, 내 평안의 죽음을 부르는 원수여! 어이하여 너는 지금 내 앞에, 나의 그 흠모하던 원수의 비할 데 없는 아름다움을 끌어다 놓는가? 차라리, 잔혹한 기억이여, 그때 그녀가 한 일을 내게 일깨우고 떠올려 주는 편이 낫지 않겠는가, 그토록 명백한 잘못에 자극받아 내가, 지금 복수는 못할지언정 적어도 이 목숨이나마 떨쳐 버리려 애쓰도록 말이다. 여러분, 이 곁가지 이야기들을 듣느라 지치지 마십시오. 제 슬픔은 간결하고 짤막하게 이야기할 수 있거나 이야기해야 마땅한 그런 부류의 것이 아니니, 제게는 사건 하나하나가 많은 말을 부르는 것만 같기 때문입니다.\"\n\n이에 신부는, 그의 이야기를 듣는 것이 지겹기는커녕 그가 언급하는 세세한 일들이 자기들의 흥미를 크게 끄니, 결코 빠뜨려서는 안 될 종류의 것이요 본 이야기와 똑같은 주의를 기울일 만한 것이라고 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 이어 말씀드리지요.\" 카르데니오가 말을 이었다. \"모두가 홀에 모이자, 그 교구의 신부가 들어와 정해진 예식을 치르려고 두 사람의 손을 맞잡았습니다. 그리고 '루신다 양, 거룩한 어머니 교회가 정하신 대로, 여기 계신 돈 페르난도 님을 그대의 합법적인 남편으로 맞이하겠습니까?'라는 말에 이르렀을 때, 저는 휘장 사이로 머리와 목을 내밀고, 안달하는 귀와 두근거리는 가슴으로 루신다의 대답에 귀를 곤두세웠으니, 그 대답 속에서 사형 선고든 목숨의 허락이든 기다렸던 것입니다. 오, 그 순간 제가 감히 앞으로 뛰쳐나가 큰 소리로 외칠 수 있었더라면! '루신다, 루신다! 그대가 하는 일을 조심하시오. 그대가 내게 진 빚을 기억하시오. 그대는 내 사람이며 다른 이의 사람이 될 수 없음을 헤아리시오. 그대가 「예」라고 말하는 것과 내 목숨이 끝나는 것이 같은 순간에 닥치리라는 것을 생각하시오. 오, 음흉한 돈 페르난도여! 내 영광을 빼앗는 도적이요, 내 목숨의 죽음이여! 그대는 무엇을 노리는가? 그대가 그리스도교인으로서는 그대 소망의 대상을 얻을 수 없음을 기억하라, 루신다는 내 신부요 나는 그녀의 남편이니!'라고 말입니다. 어리석은 저여! 이제 멀리 떨어져 위험에서 벗어난 지금에야, 저는 제가 하지 않았던 일을 했어야 했다고 말합니다. 이제 제 소중한 보물을 빼앗기도록 내버려 둔 지금에야, 저는 그 도적을 저주합니다. 한탄할 마음만큼이나 복수할 마음이 제게 있었더라면 그자에게 복수할 수도 있었을 텐데 말이지요. 한마디로, 그때 제가 겁쟁이요 바보였으니, 제가 지금 수치심에 사로잡혀, 회한에 젖어, 미쳐서 죽어 가고 있다 한들 이상할 것이 무엇이겠습니까.\n\n신부는 루신다의 대답을 기다리며 서 있었고, 그녀는 오랫동안 그것을 미루었습니다. 그래서 제가 막 그녀가 명예를 지키려고 단검을 꺼내거나, 저를 위해 진실을 밝힐 무슨 선언을 하려고 말을 찾느라 애쓰는 줄로 여기던 바로 그때, 저는 그녀가 가냘프고 힘없는 목소리로 '네, 그러겠습니다'라고 말하는 것을 들었습니다. 돈 페르난도도 같은 말을 했고, 그녀에게 가락지를 끼워 주자 두 사람은 결코 풀 수 없는 매듭으로 맺어졌습니다. 이어 신랑이 신부를 안으려 다가오자, 그녀는 손을 가슴에 누른 채 어머니의 품 안으로 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌습니다. 이제 제가 여러분께 말씀드릴 것은, 제가 들은 그 승낙 속에서 제 모든 희망이 조롱당하고, 루신다의 말과 약속이 거짓으로 드러나며, 그 순간 잃은 상을 되찾는 일이 영영 불가능해진 것을 보았을 때, 제가 어떤 처지에 놓였는가 하는 것뿐입니다. 저는 멍하니 넋이 나간 채 서 있었으니, 하늘에 온전히 버림받은 듯하였고, 저를 낳아 준 이 땅의 원수로 선언된 듯하였으며, 대기는 제 한숨에 숨결을 내주기를 마다하고, 물은 제 눈물에 물기를 내주기를 마다하는 듯하였습니다. 오직 불만이 기세를 더해 제 온몸이 분노와 질투로 이글거렸습니다. 모두가 루신다의 졸도로 혼란에 빠졌고, 그녀의 어머니가 바람을 통하게 하려고 옷끈을 풀던 중, 그녀의 품속에서 봉인된 쪽지 한 장이 드러났습니다. 돈 페르난도가 그것을 단번에 낚아채 횃불 하나의 불빛에 비춰 읽기 시작했습니다. 그것을 다 읽자마자 그는 의자에 앉아, 한 손에 뺨을 괴고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 사람의 자세로, 졸도한 신부를 깨어나게 하려는 갖은 애씀에는 아무런 손도 보태지 않았습니다.\n\n집안 모두가 혼란에 빠진 것을 본 저는, 들키든 말든 개의치 않고 감히 밖으로 나서기로 했고, 만일 들킨다면 음흉한 돈 페르난도를 벌하고, 나아가 그 변덕스럽게 졸도한 배신녀까지 벌함으로써, 제 가슴속의 의로운 분노를 온 세상에 보일 무슨 미친 짓이라도 저지르기로 작정했습니다. 그러나 제 운명은, 의심할 바 없이 더 큰 슬픔들이 — 그런 것이 있다면 — 저를 기다리도록 남겨 두려는 듯, 마침 그때 제게는 그 뒤로 줄곧 모자랐던 그 이성이 차고도 남도록 있게 하였습니다. 그리하여 저는 — 제 생각이 그들의 머릿속에서 까마득히 멀었던지라 손쉽게 할 수 있었을 — 가장 큰 원수들에게 복수하려 들지 않고, 그것을 제 자신에게 떠맡겨, 그들이 받아 마땅한 고통을 제 자신에게 가하기로 마음먹었습니다. 어쩌면 그때 제가 그들을 죽였더라면 안겨 주었을 것보다 한층 더 혹독하게 말이지요. 갑작스러운 고통은 이내 끝나지만, 고문으로 질질 끌리는 고통은 목숨을 끊지는 않으면서 끊임없이 죽이는 까닭입니다. 한마디로, 저는 그 집을 나와 제 노새를 맡겨 둔 사내의 집에 이르러, 그에게 노새에 안장을 얹게 하고는 작별 인사도 없이 올라타, 또 한 사람의 롯처럼 감히 고개를 돌려 도시를 돌아보지도 못한 채 그곳을 빠져나왔습니다. 그리고 밤의 어둠에 가려져 너른 들판에 홀로 있게 되었을 때, 들키거나 들릴 염려나 두려움 없이 제 슬픔을 쏟아 내라 부추기는 그 고요함에 이끌려, 저는 비로소 침묵을 깨고 목청을 높여 루신다와 돈 페르난도에게 저주를 퍼부었습니다. 마치 그렇게 함으로써 그들이 제게 저지른 잘못에 복수할 수 있기라도 한 양 말입니다. 저는 그녀를 잔혹하다, 은혜를 모른다, 거짓되다, 배은망덕하다 불렀고, 무엇보다도 탐욕스럽다 불렀으니, 제 원수의 재물이 그녀의 애정 어린 눈을 멀게 하여 그것을 저에게서 거두어, 운명이 더 후하고 너그러이 대해 준 자에게로 옮겨 버렸기 때문입니다. 그러나 이 저주와 책망을 쏟아 내는 와중에도, 저는 그녀를 위한 변명을 찾아내고 있었습니다. 부모의 슬하에 갇혀 늘 부모에게 복종하도록 길러지고 가르침받은 어린 처녀가, 그토록 지체 높고 부유하며 가문 좋은 신사를 남편으로 권할 때, 기꺼이 그 뜻에 따르려 한 것은 이상할 것이 없다고 말입니다. 만일 그녀가 그를 받아들이기를 마다했더라면 제정신이 아니라 여겨지거나, 다른 데 마음을 두었다 여겨졌을 테니, 그것은 그녀의 어여쁜 이름과 명성에 해가 되는 의심이었겠지요. 그러나 또 한편으로 저는, 만일 그녀가 제가 자기 남편이라고 밝혔더라면, 그들도 그녀가 저를 택한 것이 그리 나쁜 선택이 아니어서 그녀를 너그러이 보아 줄 만하다고 여겼으리라 말했습니다. 돈 페르난도가 청혼하기 전이었다면, 그들 자신도 — 그 바람이 이성에 따라 다스려졌더라면 — 저보다 그 딸에게 더 알맞은 남편감을 바랄 수는 없었을 테니까요. 그리고 그녀도, 손을 내주는 그 마지막 치명적인 한 걸음을 내딛기에 앞서, 제가 이미 제 손을 그녀에게 주었노라고 손쉽게 말할 수 있었을 것입니다. 그 말을 뒷받침하려고 제가 나섰을 테니 말입니다. 한마디로, 저는 가냘픈 사랑과, 모자란 분별과, 큰 야심과, 지체를 향한 갈망이, 제 굳건한 희망과 떳떳한 정열로 북돋우고 떠받친 가운데 저를 속였던 그 말들을 그녀로 하여금 잊게 했다는 결론에 이르렀습니다.\n\n이렇게 혼잣말을 되뇌며 심란해진 채, 저는 그 밤의 나머지 동안 내쳐 길을 갔고, 동틀 무렵 이 산들의 어느 고갯길에 다다랐습니다. 그 산들 사이를 저는 사흘이나 더, 어떤 오솔길이나 큰길도 잡지 않은 채 헤맸으니, 마침내 산의 어느 쪽인지도 모를 곳에 펼쳐진 풀밭에 이르렀고, 거기서 어느 목자들에게 이 산줄기에서 가장 험한 데가 어느 쪽이냐고 물었습니다. 그들이 이쪽이라고 일러 주기에, 저는 곧 이리로 길을 잡았으니, 여기서 제 목숨을 끝낼 작정이었습니다. 그러나 이 바위투성이 길을 헤쳐 가던 중 제 노새가 지친 데다 굶주려 쓰러져 죽었습니다. 아니, 제 생각으로는, 제 몸뚱이처럼 쓸모없는 짐을 진 노릇을 그만 끝내려고 그랬던 것이 더 그럴듯하지요. 저는 두 발로 남겨졌고, 기진맥진하고 굶주린 채, 저를 도와줄 이도 없거니와 도움을 구할 생각조차 없었습니다. 그리하여 저는 얼마나 오래인지도 모른 채 땅바닥에 뻗어 누웠다가, 그 뒤에 굶주림에서 벗어나 일어나, 제 곁에 어떤 염소지기들이 있는 것을 보았습니다. 그들이 분명 곤경에 처한 저를 거두어 준 이들이었으니, 자기들이 저를 어떻게 발견했는지, 또 제가 어떻게 분별을 잃었음이 또렷이 드러나는 헛소리를 지껄이고 있었는지를 제게 일러 주었기 때문입니다. 그리고 그때 이래로 저는 제가 늘 온전한 분별을 지니고 있지는 못하며, 때로는 어찌나 흐트러지고 미쳐 버리는지 천 가지 미친 짓을 저지른다는 것을 의식하고 있습니다. 옷을 찢고, 이 황량한 곳에서 큰 소리로 울부짖고, 제 운명을 저주하고, 제 원수인 그녀의 정다운 이름을 부질없이 부르며, 그저 한탄 속에 목숨을 끝내려고만 하지요. 그러다 제정신이 돌아오면 저는 너무도 기진하고 지쳐 있어 거의 움직일 수조차 없습니다. 가장 흔히 제 거처가 되는 곳은 이 가련한 몸뚱이를 가려 줄 만큼 큼지막한 코르크나무의 구멍입니다. 이 산들을 드나드는 소치기들과 염소지기들이 측은한 마음에 길가나 바위 위에, 제가 어쩌면 지나다 발견할 만한 곳에 먹을 것을 놓아 두어 저를 먹입니다. 그리하여 그때 제가 비록 제정신이 아니더라도, 자연의 욕구가 저를 지탱하는 데 무엇이 필요한지를 가르쳐 주어, 그것을 갈망하고 기꺼이 집어 들게 합니다. 또 어떤 때는, 그들이 저를 제정신인 때에 발견하고 일러 주는 바에 따르면, 제가 길로 뛰쳐나가, 그들이 기꺼이 내주려는데도, 마을에서 오두막으로 먹을 것을 나르는 양치기들에게서 그것을 억지로 빼앗는다 합니다. 이렇게 저는 제게 남은 비참한 삶을, 하늘이 그것을 끝내 주실 뜻을 품으시거나, 아니면 제 기억을 다스리시어 제가 더는 루신다의 아름다움과 배신도, 돈 페르난도가 제게 저지른 잘못도 떠올리지 않게 해 주실 때까지 보내고 있습니다. 만일 하늘이 제 목숨을 앗지 않으면서 그리해 주신다면, 저는 제 생각을 더 나은 길로 돌리겠습니다. 그렇지 않다면, 저는 그저 하늘이 제 영혼에 가득한 자비를 베풀어 주시기를 빌 수밖에 없으니, 제 자신 안에는 제 발로 들어앉기를 택한 이 곤경에서 제 몸을 풀어 줄 아무런 힘도 기력도 느껴지지 않기 때문입니다.\n\n여러분, 이것이 제 불행의 음울한 이야기입니다. 이것이 여러분께서 제게서 보신 것보다 덜한 동요로 이야기될 수 있는 것인지 말씀해 보십시오. 그리고 이성이 제 위안에 도움이 되리라 일러 주는 것을 제게 권하거나 들이대시느라 수고하지 마십시오. 그것은, 약을 먹지 않으려는 병자에게 슬기로운 의원이 처방한 약이 소용없는 것만큼이나 제게 소용없을 것이기 때문입니다. 저는 루신다 없는 건강은 바라지 않습니다. 그리고 그녀가, 제 사람이거나 마땅히 제 사람이어야 할 그녀가, 다른 이의 사람이 되기를 기뻐하니, 제가 행복을 누릴 수도 있었던 그때에 비참의 먹이가 되는 것도 제 몫이 되게 하겠습니다. 그녀는 변덕으로 제 파멸을 돌이킬 수 없게 만들려 애썼으니, 저는 파멸을 좇음으로써 그녀의 소망을 이루어 주려 애쓰겠습니다. 그리고 이는 다가올 세대들에게, 불행에 빠진 다른 모든 이가 차고 넘치게 가진 그것을 오직 저만이 빼앗겼음을 보여 줄 것입니다. 그들에게는 위로받을 수 없다는 것 자체가 하나의 위로이지만, 제게는 그것이 더 큰 슬픔과 고통의 원인이기 때문입니다. 죽음에서조차 그것들의 끝은 없으리라 저는 생각하니까요.\"\n\n여기서 카르데니오는, 사랑으로 가득한 만큼이나 불행으로도 가득한 자신의 긴 이야기와 사연을 끝맺었다. 그런데 신부가 막 그에게 위로의 말 몇 마디를 건네려는 참에, 어떤 목소리 하나가 그의 귀에 닿아 그를 멈춰 세웠으니, 그 목소리가 구슬픈 가락으로 무어라 말한 바는 이 이야기의 제4부에서 들려질 것이다. 바로 이 대목에서 슬기롭고 사려 깊은 역사가 시데 아메테 베넹헬리가 제3부를 끝맺었던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII.\n\n그토록 대담한 기사 라만차의 돈키호테가 세상에 보내진 시대는 참으로 행복하고 복된 시대였으니, 오래도록 잊혀 거의 사멸하다시피 한 편력 기사도를 되살려 세상에 회복시키려는 그토록 명예로운 결심을 그가 품은 덕분에, 가벼운 즐길 거리가 이토록 빈약한 우리 시대에 우리는 그의 진실된 이야기가 주는 매혹뿐 아니라, 그 안에 담긴 이야기들과 삽화들이 주는 매혹까지도 누리게 되었으니, 그것들은 어느 모로 보아도 그 이야기 자체에 못지않게 즐겁고 기발하며 진실하다. 그 이야기는 이제 다시 그 실타래를 이어, 솔질하고 잣고 감아 가며, 신부가 막 카르데니오에게 위로의 말을 건네려던 바로 그 순간, 구슬픈 목소리가 그의 귀에 와 닿아 말을 가로막았다고 전한다.\n\n\"오 하느님! 그토록 내키지 않게 떠메고 다니는 이 지친 몸뚱이의 짐을, 남몰래 묻을 무덤으로 삼을 만한 곳을 제가 정녕 찾아낸 것이옵니까? 이 산들이 약속하는 고독이 저를 속이는 것이 아니라면, 정말 그러하옵니다. 아아, 가엾은 신세여! 제 불행을 하늘에 하소연하도록 허락해 주는 이 바위와 덤불의 벗함이, 그 어떤 사람의 벗함보다 제 마음에 얼마나 더 고마운 것인지요. 이 땅 위에는 의심스러울 때 의논할 사람도, 슬플 때 위로해 줄 사람도, 괴로울 때 구해 줄 사람도 없으니 말이옵니다!\"\n\n이 모든 말은 신부와 그 일행에게 또렷이 들렸으며, 그들에게는 그 말이 바로 가까이에서 — 실제로도 그러했다 — 나온 듯하였기에, 말하는 이를 찾으려고 일어섰다. 그리고 스무 걸음도 가기 전에, 그들은 바위 뒤 물푸레나무 밑에 앉아 있는 한 젊은이를 발견했는데, 농부 차림을 한 그는 몸을 앞으로 숙여 곁으로 흐르는 시냇물에 발을 담그고 있던 터라 그 순간에는 얼굴을 볼 수가 없었다. 그들은 어찌나 조용히 다가갔던지, 발 씻기에 온전히 몰두해 있던 그는 그들을 알아차리지 못했는데, 그 발이 어찌나 고운지 시냇물의 다른 돌들 사이에서 빛나는 두 개의 수정 조각이라도 솟아난 듯하였다. 이 발의 희고 아름다움에 그들은 놀라움을 금치 못했으니, 그것은 그 주인의 차림새가 일러 주듯 흙덩이를 부수거나 쟁기와 소를 따라다니도록 만들어진 발로는 도무지 보이지 않았기 때문이다. 그리하여 자기들이 눈에 띄지 않았음을 알고는, 앞에 서 있던 신부가 다른 두 사람에게 거기 놓인 바위 부스러기 뒤에 몸을 숨기라는 신호를 보냈으며, 그들은 그대로 하여 젊은이가 무엇을 하는지 가까이서 살폈다. 그는 흰 천으로 몸에 단단히 동여맨, 자락이 이중으로 늘어진 헐렁한 짙은 갈색 윗옷을 입고 있었으며, 그 밖에 갈색 천으로 만든 반바지와 각반을 차고, 머리에는 갈색 몬테라를 쓰고 있었다. 그리고 각반을 다리 한가운데까지 걷어 올렸는데, 그 다리는 정녕 순백의 설화석고로 빚은 듯하였다.\n\n* 몬테라: 사냥꾼이나 농부가 쓰던 천으로 만든 챙 없는 모자.\n그 아름다운 발을 다 씻고 나자 젊은이는 몬테라 밑에서 꺼낸 수건으로 발을 닦았는데, 몬테라를 벗으면서 얼굴을 들어 올렸기에, 그를 지켜보던 이들은 더없이 빼어난 아름다움을 볼 기회를 얻었으니, 카르데니오가 신부에게 속삭이며 이렇게 말할 정도였다.\n\n\"이 사람이 루스신다가 아니라면, 그것은 사람이 아니라 신적인 존재가 틀림없습니다.\"\n\n이윽고 젊은이가 몬테라를 벗고 고개를 좌우로 흔들자, 햇살마저 시샘했을 법한 한 무더기의 머리채가 풀려 흘러내려 퍼졌다. 이로써 그들은 농부로 여겼던 이가 사실은 어여쁜 여인임을, 아니 그들 가운데 두 사람이, 혹은 카르데니오마저도 — 루스신다를 보고 알지 못했더라면 — 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 아름다운 여인임을 알게 되었으니, 그는 훗날 오직 루스신다의 아름다움만이 이에 견줄 수 있다고 말했던 것이다. 그 길고 적갈색인 머리채는 어깨를 덮었을 뿐 아니라, 그 길이와 풍성함이 어찌나 대단한지 온몸을 그 더미 밑에 죄다 감추어, 발을 제외하고는 그 몸의 어느 곳도 보이지 않았다. 이제 그가 두 손을 빗 삼아 쓰니, 발이 물속의 수정 조각 같았다면 손은 머리채 사이의 흩날리는 눈송이 같았으며, 이 모든 것이 세 구경꾼의 감탄뿐 아니라 그가 누구인지 알고자 하는 그들의 조바심까지 더하게 하였다. 그리하여 그들은 모습을 드러내기로 마음먹었고, 그들이 일어서며 부스럭거리는 소리에 그 어여쁜 처녀가 고개를 들어, 두 손으로 눈앞의 머리채를 가르고 누가 그 소리를 냈는지 보려 하였다. 그리고 그들을 알아차린 순간 벌떡 일어서서, 신발을 신거나 머리를 추스를 겨를도 없이, 곁에 두었던 옷가지인 듯한 보따리를 황급히 낚아채고는, 겁에 질려 놀란 채로 달아나려 했다. 그러나 여섯 걸음도 가기 전에 그 연약한 발이 돌의 거칢을 견디지 못해 땅에 쓰러지고 말았다. 이를 보고 세 사람이 그에게로 달려갔으며, 신부가 먼저 그에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"멈추시오, 세뇨라. 그대가 누구이든, 여기 보이는 이들은 오직 그대에게 도움이 되기를 바랄 뿐이오. 그토록 무모하게 달아나려 할 까닭이 없으니, 그대의 발도 그것을 견디지 못할뿐더러, 우리도 그것을 허락하지 않을 것이오.\"\n\n뜻밖의 일에 놀라 어쩔 줄 모르던 그녀는 이 말에 아무런 대꾸도 하지 못했다. 그러나 그들이 그녀에게 다가오자, 신부가 그녀의 손을 잡고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그대의 옷차림이 감추려 한 것을, 세뇨라, 그대의 머리채가 우리에게 알려 주었소. 이는 그대의 아름다움을 이토록 그에 어울리지 않는 옷으로 변장시켜, 우리가 다행히 그대를 발견한 이런 외진 곳으로 보낸 것이 결코 사소한 까닭이 아니었음을 분명히 보여 주는 증거요. 그대의 괴로움을 덜어 주지는 못할지언정 적어도 위로는 드리고자 함이니, 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 어떤 괴로움도 선한 뜻으로 건네는 위로에 귀 닫게 할 만큼 짓누르거나 드높을 수는 없는 법이오. 그러니 세뇨라든, 세뇨르든, 그대가 무엇으로 불리기를 원하든, 우리의 모습이 그대에게 일으킨 두려움을 떨쳐 버리고, 그대의 좋고 나쁜 운명을 우리에게 들려주시오. 우리 모두에게서든, 우리 한 사람 한 사람에게서든, 그대는 그 곤경에 동정을 받게 될 것이오.\"\n\n신부가 말하는 동안 변장한 처녀는 마치 무엇에 홀린 듯 입을 떼거나 한마디도 내뱉지 않은 채 그들을 바라보고 서 있었으니, 마치 한 번도 본 적 없는 낯선 무언가를 별안간 보게 된 시골뜨기와 같았다. 그러나 신부가 같은 취지의 말을 몇 마디 더 건네자, 깊이 한숨을 쉬며 침묵을 깨고 입을 열었다.\n\n\"이 산들의 고독도 저를 숨겨 주지 못하였고, 풀어 헤쳐진 제 머리채가 빠져나와 제 혀가 거짓을 꾸미도록 내버려 두지 않으니, 이제 와서 제가 더 무슨 가식을 부린들 부질없는 일일 테고, 설령 여러분이 제 말을 믿어 주신다 해도 그것은 다른 어떤 까닭에서가 아니라 예의에서 믿어 주시는 것일 테지요. 그러하오니 말씀드리거니와, 여러분, 제게 베풀어 주신 호의에 감사드리며, 그 호의는 여러분께서 제게 청하신 바를 따라야 할 의무를 제게 지우는 것이옵니다. 다만 제가 들려드릴 불행의 사연이 여러분께 동정만큼이나 근심을 불러일으키지 않을까 염려스럽사오니, 여러분께서는 그것을 고칠 만한 방도도, 덜어 줄 만한 위안도 떠올리지 못하실 터이기 때문이옵니다. 그러나 이제 여러분께서 제가 여자임을 알아내시고, 제가 젊고, 홀로이며, 이런 차림을 하고 있음을 — 함께 묶든 따로 떼든 어떤 좋은 이름이라도 무너뜨리기에 충분한 이런 것들을 — 보신 마당이니, 제 명예가 여러분의 마음속에 의혹으로 남지 않도록, 할 수만 있다면 기꺼이 비밀로 묻어 두었을 일을 털어놓아야겠다고 느끼옵니다.\"\n\n이제 어여쁜 여인임이 드러난 그녀는 이 모든 말을 조금의 망설임도 없이, 너무도 편안하게, 그토록 감미로운 목소리로 풀어놓았기에, 그들은 그녀의 아름다움 못지않게 그녀의 총명함에도 매혹되었다. 그리고 그들이 약속을 지켜 달라는 청과 간곡한 부탁을 다시금 거듭하자, 그녀는 더 채근할 것도 없이 먼저 다소곳이 발을 가리고 머리채를 추스른 다음, 세 사람을 둘레에 두고 돌 위에 앉아, 눈에 차오르는 눈물을 애써 억누른 뒤, 맑고 차분한 목소리로 그 사연을 이렇게 시작했다.\n\n\"이곳 안달루시아에 한 고을이 있는데, 어느 공작이 그곳에서 작위를 따왔고, 그 작위로 하여 그는 스페인의 대공들이라 불리는 이들 가운데 하나가 되었습니다. 이 귀인에게는 아들이 둘 있는데, 맏이는 그의 지위와 보아하니 그의 좋은 성품까지 물려받을 후사이고, 둘째는 — 베이도의 배신과 가넬론의 거짓 말고 무엇을 물려받을지 저로서는 알 수 없는 — 후사이옵니다. 제 부모님은 이 영주의 신하로, 출신은 미천하나 어찌나 부유한지, 출생이 운명만큼이나 그분들께 많은 것을 주었더라면 더 바랄 것이 없었을 것이고, 저 또한 지금 제가 처한 이런 곤경을 두려워할 까닭이 없었을 것이옵니다. 어쩌면 저의 불운은 그분들이 고귀하게 태어나지 못한 데서 온 것일지도 모르니까요. 사실 그분들은 자기 처지를 부끄러워할 만큼 그렇게 낮지도 않으나, 그렇다고 저의 불행이 그분들의 미천한 출생에서 온다는 생각을 제 마음에서 지워 줄 만큼 높지도 않으옵니다. 한마디로 그분들은 농부이고, 꾸밈없고 수수한 사람들이며, 추잡한 피가 한 방울도 섞이지 않은, 흔히 말하듯 오래되고 녹슨 기독교인들이지만, 너무도 부유한지라 그 재산과 후한 살림살이로 차츰 태생부터 양반인 사람으로, 심지어 지위에서도 양반으로 여겨지기에 이르렀습니다. 다만 그분들이 가장 자랑스럽게 여긴 재산이자 고귀함은 저를 딸로 둔 것이었으니, 후사로 삼을 다른 자식이 없는 데다 정 많은 부모이신 까닭에, 저는 일찍이 어떤 부모가 응석을 받아 준 딸들보다도 더 응석받이로 자란 딸 가운데 하나였습니다.\n* 베이도와 가넬론: 베이도 돌포스는 스페인 전설에서 산초 2세 왕을 배신해 죽인 인물이며, 가넬론은 「롤랑의 노래」에 나오는 배신자.\n\n\"저는 그분들이 자신을 비추어 보던 거울이요, 노년의 지팡이요, 하늘의 뜻을 따르는 가운데 그분들의 모든 바람이 모여 있던 대상이었으며, 저의 바람 또한 그분들의 그것과 한결같았으니, 저는 그분들의 가치를 알았기 때문입니다. 그리하여 제가 그분들의 마음의 주인이었던 것처럼, 저는 또한 그분들 재산의 주인이기도 하였습니다. 제 손으로 하인들을 들이고 내보냈으며, 무엇을 뿌리고 거두었는지에 대한 셈과 수입이 제 손을 거쳤고, 기름틀이며 포도주 짜는 기계며 양 떼와 가축 떼의 수효며 벌통이며, 한마디로 제 아버지 같은 부유한 농부가 가지거나 가질 수 있는 모든 것이 제 보살핌 아래 있었으니, 저는 한쪽에서는 부지런함으로, 다른 쪽에서는 그분들의 흡족함으로, 제가 여러분께 잘 그려 드릴 수 없을 만큼 살림 맡은 안주인 노릇을 하였습니다. 목자장이며 감독이며 다른 일꾼들에게 필요한 지시를 내린 뒤에 제게 남은 한가한 시간은, 어린 처녀들에게 허락될 뿐 아니라 필요하기도 한 그런 일들, 곧 바늘과 수틀과 물레가 으레 제공하는 일들로 보냈으며, 마음을 새롭게 하고자 그것들을 잠시 떠날 때는, 신앙서 한 권을 읽거나 하프를 타며 즐거움을 찾았으니, 음악이 어지러운 마음을 달래 주고 지친 정신을 풀어 준다는 것을 경험으로 배웠기 때문입니다. 이것이 제가 부모님 집에서 보낸 삶이었으며, 제가 이렇듯 시시콜콜 그려 보였다면, 그것은 자랑하려거나 제가 부유하다는 것을 알리려는 것이 아니라, 제가 아무 잘못도 없이 어떻게 제가 말씀드린 행복한 처지에서 지금 처한 이 비참함으로 떨어졌는지를 여러분께서 아시도록 하려는 것이옵니다. 사실인즉, 이렇듯 분주한 삶을 수도원에 견줄 만한 은둔 속에서 살아가며, 집안 하인들 말고는 — 미사에 갈 때는 아침이 아주 일렀고, 어머니와 집안 여인들이 어찌나 가까이서 저를 따라붙고, 또 제가 어찌나 두껍게 너울을 쓰고 수줍어했던지, 제 두 눈은 제가 밟는 땅보다 더 많은 땅을 거의 보지 못할 정도였기에 — 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않는다고 여겼건만, 이 모든 것에도 불구하고, 살쾡이의 눈도 따르지 못할 사랑의 눈이, 아니 더 정확히 말하면 한가함의 눈이, 돈 페르난도의 끈질김의 도움을 받아 저를 찾아내고 말았으니, 페르난도란 바로 제가 말씀드린 그 공작의 둘째 아들의 이름이옵니다.\"\n\n말하는 이가 돈 페르난도의 이름을 입에 올리는 순간, 카르데니오는 낯빛이 바뀌고 식은땀을 흘리며 어찌나 동요하는 기색을 보였던지, 이를 지켜보던 신부와 이발사는 그가 이따금 발작한다고 들었던 광기의 발작 중 하나가 그에게 닥쳐오는 것이 아닐까 두려워했다. 그러나 카르데니오는 더는 격앙되는 기색 없이 조용히 있으면서, 그 농부 처녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았으니, 그녀가 누구인지 짐작하기 시작했던 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 카르데니오의 흥분을 알아차리지 못한 채 이야기를 이어 나갔다.\n\n\"그분들이 저를 발견하자마자, 그가 훗날 스스로 인정했듯이, 그는 제게 격렬한 사랑에 사로잡혔으니, 그것이 드러난 방식이 그것을 분명히 보여 주었습니다. 그러나 제 슬픔의 긴 사설을 줄이기 위해, 돈 페르난도가 제게 자기 열정을 고백하려고 동원한 온갖 술수는 입 다물고 넘어가겠습니다. 그는 집안사람들을 죄다 매수했고, 제 부모님께 선물과 예물을 주고 또 권했으며, 날마다 우리 거리는 명절이나 잔칫날 같았고, 밤이면 음악 때문에 아무도 잠들 수 없었으며, 어떻게 들어왔는지 아무도 모를 연애편지들이 제 손에 들어왔는데 그 수가 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼이었고, 다정한 호소와 맹세로 가득하여, 그 안의 글자 수보다 약속과 서약이 더 많을 지경이었습니다. 이 모든 것이 저를 누그러뜨리기는커녕 도리어 그에 맞서 제 마음을 굳게 하였으니, 마치 그가 저의 철천지원수라도 되는 듯, 마치 저를 굴복시키려고 그가 한 모든 일이 그 반대의 의도로 행해지기라도 한 듯하였습니다. 돈 페르난도의 고상한 태도가 제게 거슬렸다거나, 그의 끈질긴 구애가 진력났다는 것이 아닙니다. 그토록 지체 높은 신사에게 그처럼 추구받고 귀히 여겨지는 제 자신을 보는 것은 일종의 만족감을 주었고, 그의 편지에서 저에 대한 찬사를 보는 것이 싫지 않았으니까요 (우리 여자들이 아무리 못생겼다 해도, 자기가 아름답다는 말을 듣는 것은 언제나 기분 좋은 일인 듯하옵니다). 다만 제 자신의 올바름에 대한 감각이 이 모든 것에 맞섰고, 또한 부모님의 거듭된 충고도 그러하였으니, 이제 부모님은 돈 페르난도의 속셈을 아주 분명히 알아차리셨던 것입니다. 그는 온 세상이 그것을 안다 해도 거의 개의치 않았으니까요. 부모님은 당신들의 명예와 좋은 이름을 오직 저의 정숙함과 올곧음에만 맡기고 의탁한다고 제게 말씀하셨으며, 돈 페르난도와 저 사이의 신분 차이를 헤아려 보라고 이르셨으니, 거기서 저는 그가 입으로는 무어라 반대로 말하든 그의 의도가 저의 이익보다는 제 자신의 쾌락을 노린 것임을 헤아릴 수 있었습니다. 그리고 제가 그의 분별없는 구애에 조금이라도 걸림돌을 놓고 싶다면, 부모님은 당장 저를 제가 원하는 누구에게든 — 우리 고을의 지체 높은 사람들이든, 이웃 어느 고을의 사람들이든 — 시집보낼 채비가 되어 있다고 하셨으니, 그분들의 재산과 저의 좋은 이름이면 어느 쪽에서든 혼처를 구할 수 있다는 것이었습니다. 이 제안과 그분들의 분별 있는 충고가 제 결심을 굳혔고, 저는 돈 페르난도에게 아무리 막연하게나마 성공의 희망을 줄 만한 어떤 말도 결코 답으로 건네지 않았습니다.\n\n\"그가 새침함으로 여겼을 이 모든 저의 조심성은, 보아하니 그의 음탕한 욕심을 — 제게 품은 그의 열정을 저는 그렇게 부르겠습니다 — 부추기는 효과만 낸 듯하였으니, 만약 그것이 그가 공언한 그런 것이었다면, 여러분은 지금 그것을 알지 못하셨을 것입니다. 그것을 여러분께 말씀드릴 일이 아예 없었을 테니까요. 마침내 그는 제 부모님이 저를 차지하려는 그의 희망을 끝장내려고, 아니 적어도 저를 지켜 줄 보호자를 더 두려고 제 혼사를 궁리하고 있음을 알게 되었고, 이 소식인지 의심인지로 인해 그는 여러분께서 곧 들으실 그런 짓을 저지르고 말았습니다. 어느 날 밤, 저는 제 방에서 저를 시중드는 하녀 말고는 다른 벗 없이, 행여 부주의로 제 명예가 위태로워질까 하여 문을 단단히 잠근 채로 있었는데, 어찌하여 그런 일이 일어났는지 저는 알 수도 없고 짐작할 수도 없으나, 이 모든 칩거와 이 모든 경계에도 불구하고, 제 은둔처의 고독과 고요 속에서, 저는 그가 제 앞에 서 있는 것을 발견했으니, 그 광경은 저를 어찌나 아연하게 했던지 제 두 눈에서는 시력을, 제 혀에서는 말을 앗아 가 버렸습니다. 저는 소리를 지를 힘도 없었고, 제 생각에 그도 제게 소리 지를 틈을 주지 않았으니, 그가 곧바로 제게 다가와 저를 두 팔에 안았기 때문입니다 (압도당한 저는, 다시 말씀드리거니와, 저 자신을 도울 힘이 없었으니까요). 그러고는 그는 제게 어찌나 고백을 늘어놓던지, 거짓이 어떻게 그것들을 진실처럼 보이도록 그토록 꾸며 낼 힘을 가졌는지 저로서는 알 수 없을 정도였으며, 그 배신자는 자기 눈물로 자기 말을, 자기 한숨으로 자기 진심을 증언하도록 꾀했습니다.\n\n\"가엾고 외로운 어린 것이요, 이런 경우에 대해 제 사람들 가운데서 제대로 배운 바도 없는 저는, 어찌하여 그랬는지 이 모든 거짓 맹세를 참이라 여기기 시작했으나, 그의 한숨과 눈물에 움직여 순전한 동정심 이상의 무언가에 이른 것은 아니었습니다. 그리하여 처음의 당혹감이 가시고 제가 어느 정도 정신을 차리기 시작하자, 저는 제가 가졌으리라 생각한 것보다 더 큰 용기를 내어 그에게 말했습니다. '세뇨르, 제가 지금 당신의 두 팔에 안겨 있듯이 만약 사나운 사자의 발톱에 잡혀 있고, 제 명예를 해치는 무언가를 행하거나 말함으로써 거기서 벗어날 수 있다 해도, 이미 있었던 일이 없었던 일이 될 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로, 그것을 행하거나 말하는 것은 제 힘으로 도무지 할 수 없는 일일 것입니다. 그러니 당신이 제 몸을 두 팔에 안고 있다 한들, 저는 정숙한 뜻으로 제 영혼을 단단히 지키고 있으니, 그것은 당신의 뜻과는 아주 다른 것이라, 당신이 힘으로 그것을 이루려 든다면 알게 되실 것입니다. 저는 당신의 신하이나 당신의 종은 아닙니다. 당신의 고귀함은 제 미천한 출생을 욕보이거나 깎아내릴 권리를 가지고 있지도, 가져서도 안 됩니다. 그리고 비록 미천하게 태어난 농부일지언정 저는 영주이자 신사인 당신 못지않게 제 자존심을 지니고 있습니다. 제게는 당신의 폭력도 소용없을 것이며, 당신의 재산도 무게를 갖지 못할 것이고, 당신의 말도 저를 속일 힘이 없을 것이며, 당신의 한숨이나 눈물도 저를 누그러뜨리지 못할 것입니다. 제 부모님이 제게 남편으로 주신 사람에게서 제가 말한 그런 것들 가운데 무엇이라도 보게 된다면, 그의 뜻이 곧 제 뜻이 될 것이고, 제 뜻은 그의 뜻 안에 매일 것입니다. 그리고 제 명예가 지켜진다면, 비록 제 마음이 내키지 않더라도, 당신이 지금 세뇨르 힘으로 얻으려 하는 것을 그에게는 기꺼이 내어 줄 것입니다. 이것을 말씀드리는 까닭은, 제 합법적인 남편 말고는 그 누구도 결코 제게서 무언가를 얻지 못하리라는 것을 당신이 헤아리시도록 하려는 것입니다.' 이 부정한 신사가 말했습니다. '그것이 그대가 느끼는 유일한 거리낌이라면, 더없이 아리따운 도로테아여' (이 불행한 사람의 이름이 바로 그것이옵니다), '보시오, 여기 내가 그대의 것이 되도록 손을 내미니, 아무것도 숨길 수 없는 하늘과, 여기 그대가 모셔 둔 성모님의 이 상이 이 맹세의 증인이 되게 하시오.'\"\n\n카르데니오는 그녀가 자기 이름이 도로테아라고 말하는 것을 듣자 새로이 동요하는 기색을 보이며 앞서 품었던 의심이 사실임을 확신했으나, 이야기를 가로막고 싶지 않았고, 이미 거의 다 알고 있는 일의 결말을 듣고 싶었기에, 그저 이렇게만 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요! 그대의 이름이 도로테아입니까, 세뇨라? 같은 이름을 가진 또 다른 사람에 대해 들은 적이 있는데, 어쩌면 그녀가 그대의 불행에 견줄 만할지도 모릅니다. 그러나 계속하시지요. 머지않아 그대의 동정심을 자아내는 만큼이나 그대를 놀라게 할 무언가를 제가 들려드릴 수도 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n도로테아는 카르데니오의 말뿐 아니라 그의 기이하고 비참한 차림새에도 놀라, 만약 그가 자기에 관해 무언가 아는 것이 있다면 당장 들려 달라고 청했으니, 만약 운명이 자기에게 어떤 축복이라도 남겨 두었다면 그것은 자기에게 닥칠 어떤 재앙이든 견뎌 낼 용기였고, 자기가 이미 겪고 있는 것을 조금이라도 더할 수 있는 것은 아무것도 자기에게 닿을 수 없으리라 확신했기 때문이다.\n\n\"세뇨라,\" 카르데니오가 답했다. \"제가 의심하는 것이 사실이라면, 제 생각을 그대에게 말씀드릴 이 기회를 놓치지 않을 것입니다만, 아직까지는 그럴 기회도 없었고, 또 그대가 그것을 아는 것이 그대에게 무슨 중요한 일도 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그것이야 어찌 됐든,\" 도로테아가 답했다. \"제 이야기에서 벌어진 일인즉, 돈 페르난도가 방에 놓여 있던 성상 하나를 집어 우리 약혼의 증인으로 세우고, 더없이 구속력 있는 말과 터무니없는 맹세로 제 남편이 되겠노라 약속했다는 것입니다. 다만 그가 맹세를 다 마치기 전에 저는 그에게, 지금 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 잘 헤아려 보고, 농부 처녀이자 자기 신하인 사람과 혼인한 것을 보고 그 아버지가 느낄 노여움을 생각해 보라고 일렀습니다. 저는 그에게, 보잘것없으나마 제 아름다움에 눈멀지 말라고, 그것이 그의 잘못을 변명할 구실이 되기에는 충분치 않다고 말했으며, 만약 그가 제게 품은 사랑으로 제게 무슨 친절이라도 베풀고자 한다면, 그것은 제 분수가 요구하는 처지에 맞게 제 운명이 흘러가도록 내버려 두는 것일 거라고 했습니다. 그토록 어울리지 않는 혼인은 결코 행복을 가져온 적이 없고, 처음에 누렸던 즐거움을 오래도록 이어 주지도 못하니까요.\n\n\"제가 지금 되풀이한 이 모든 것을 저는 그에게 말했고, 지금은 기억나지 않는 더 많은 말도 했습니다만, 그가 자기 뜻을 단념하도록 이끄는 데에는 아무 효과가 없었으니, 갚을 마음이 없는 자는 흥정을 할 때 어려움 따위로 자신을 괴롭히지 않는 법입니다. 동시에 저는 제 마음속에서 그 일을 짤막하게 따져 보며 혼잣말을 했습니다. '혼인으로 미천한 처지에서 높은 지위로 올라간 것이 내가 처음은 아닐 것이고, 아름다움이, 아니 더 그럴듯하게는 눈먼 애착이, 자기 신분 아래로 짝지어지게 만든 것도 돈 페르난도가 처음은 아닐 것이다. 그러니 내가 새로운 관습이나 관행을 끌어들이는 것도 아닌 이상, 우연이 내게 베푼 이 명예를 누려도 좋지 않겠는가. 비록 나에 대한 그의 마음이 자기 소망을 이룬 뒤까지 이어지지 않는다 해도, 나는 어쨌든 하느님 앞에서 그의 아내일 것이다. 그리고 내가 멸시로 그를 물리치려 애쓴다면, 좋은 방법이 통하지 않을 때 그가 힘을 쓸 작정인 것이 내 눈에 보이니, 나는 명예를 잃고도, 내가 얼마나 죄 없이 이런 처지에 놓이게 되었는지 알 길 없는 이들에게 내 결백을 증명할 아무런 방도도 없이 남겨질 것이다. 이 신사가 내 동의 없이 내 방에 들어왔다고 어떤 논리가 내 부모님을 설득할 수 있겠는가?'\n\n\"이 모든 물음과 답이 한순간에 제 마음을 스쳐 지나갔습니다. 그러나 돈 페르난도의 맹세, 그가 호소한 증인들, 그가 흘린 눈물, 그리고 마지막으로 그 사람됨의 매력과 고상한 기품이 — 이러한 참된 사랑의 표시들을 동반했으니 제 마음보다 더 자유롭고 더 새침한 마음마저 능히 정복했을 법한 것들이 — 그 무엇보다도 저를 움직이기 시작하여, 저도 모르게 저를 파멸로 이끌어 갔습니다. 저는 하늘에 있는 증인들 말고도 이 땅에 증인이 있도록 시중드는 하녀를 제게 불렀고, 돈 페르난도는 다시금 자기 맹세를 새로이 거듭하며, 앞서 부른 성인들에 더하여 새로운 성인들을 증인으로 불러내고, 만약 자기 약속을 지키지 못한다면 앞으로 자기 머리 위에 천 가지 저주가 내리기를 빌었으며, 더 많은 눈물을 흘리고, 한숨을 갑절로 늘리고, 한 번도 빠져나가도록 허락한 적 없는 그 두 팔로 저를 더 바싹 끌어안았습니다. 그리하여 저는 하녀에게 버려졌고, 처녀이기를 그쳤으며, 그는 배신자요 위증한 자가 되었습니다.\n\n\"제 불행의 그 밤에 뒤이은 날은, 제 짐작에, 돈 페르난도가 바랐던 만큼 빨리 오지는 않았으니, 욕망이 그 대상을 얻고 나면 가장 큰 즐거움은 그 쾌락의 자리에서 달아나는 것이기 때문입니다. 이렇게 말씀드리는 까닭은, 돈 페르난도가 저를 떠나려고 무던히도 서둘렀고, 사실은 그를 들여보냈던 장본인인 제 하녀의 솜씨로 동트기 전에 거리로 빠져나갔기 때문입니다. 다만 제게 작별을 고할 때 그는, 올 때만큼의 간곡함과 열정은 아니었으나, 자기 신의와, 자기 맹세의 신성함과 진실함을 제가 믿어도 좋다고 말했으며, 자기 말을 다지려고 손가락에서 값진 반지 하나를 빼내어 제 손가락에 끼워 주었습니다. 그러고는 그가 떠나고, 저는 — 슬펐는지 기뻤는지 저도 모를 채로 — 남겨졌습니다. 제가 말할 수 있는 것은, 저는 마음이 동요하고 어지러운 채, 벌어진 일에 거의 얼이 빠진 채로 남겨졌다는 것이며, 돈 페르난도를 제 방에 숨겨 준 하녀의 배신을 꾸짖을 만한 기력도, 아니 그럴 생각조차 떠오르지 않았으니, 아직 제게 닥친 일이 좋은 일인지 나쁜 일인지 마음을 정할 수 없었기 때문입니다. 헤어질 때 저는 돈 페르난도에게, 이제 제가 그의 것이 되었으니 그가 이 일을 알리고자 할 때까지 다른 밤에도 같은 방식으로 저를 볼 수 있다고 말했습니다. 그러나 그는 그다음 날 밤 말고는 다시 오지 않았고, 한 달이 넘도록 저는 거리에서도 성당에서도 그를 한 번도 얼핏 볼 수 없었으니, 그러느라 저는 그를 지켜보며 지쳐 갔습니다. 그가 고을에 있다는 것을, 그리고 그가 무척 좋아하는 소일거리인 사냥을 거의 날마다 나간다는 것을 알고 있었는데도 말입니다. 그 나날과 시간들이 제게 얼마나 슬프고 음울했는지 저는 잘 기억합니다. 시간이 흐르면서 제가 어떻게 의심하기 시작했는지, 심지어 돈 페르난도의 신의에 대한 믿음을 잃기 시작했는지도 잘 기억합니다. 그리고 제 하녀가 전에는 들은 적 없는, 자기 무모함을 꾸짖는 그런 말들을 어떻게 듣게 되었는지, 또 제가 어찌하여 어머니가 왜 그토록 우울하냐고 물으실 빌미를 드리지 않으려고, 그리하여 그 답으로 거짓을 지어내게 되지 않으려고, 제 눈물과 얼굴 표정에 어떻게 억지로 자제를 가해야 했는지도 잘 기억합니다. 그러나 이 모든 것이 별안간 끝나 버렸으니, 이런 온갖 고려가 무시되고 명예 따위는 더 이상 문제 되지 않으며, 제 인내가 무너지고 제 마음의 비밀이 세상에 알려지게 된 그때가 왔기 때문입니다. 그 까닭인즉, 며칠 뒤 돈 페르난도가 이웃 도시에서 보기 드물게 아름다운 어느 처녀와 혼인했다는 소문이 고을에 돌았다는 것이니, 그녀는 지체 높은 부모의 딸이었으나, 그 지참금으로 그토록 빛나는 혼처를 바랄 만큼 부유하지는 않았다고 했습니다. 또한 그녀의 이름이 루스신다라고 했고, 약혼식에서 몇 가지 기이한 일이 일어났다고도 했습니다.\"\n\n카르데니오는 루스신다의 이름을 들었으나, 그저 어깨를 으쓱하고 입술을 깨물며 눈썹을 찌푸렸을 뿐인데, 이내 그의 두 눈에서 두 줄기 눈물이 흘러내렸다. 그러나 도로테아는 이야기를 멈추지 않고 이런 말로 계속해 나갔다.\n\n\"이 슬픈 소식이 제 귀에 닿았으나, 저는 오싹해지기는커녕 제 가슴이 어찌나 분노와 격분으로 타올랐던지, 거리로 뛰쳐나가 큰 소리로 외치며 제가 그 희생자가 된 그 배반과 배신을 공공연히 알리고 싶은 것을 가까스로 억눌렀습니다. 그러나 이 분노의 격발은 같은 날 밤 실행에 옮길 결심을 하면서 일단 가라앉았으니, 그것은 바로 이 옷차림을 하는 것이었습니다. 이 옷은 제 아버지의 하인 하나, 농가에서 부르는 말로 사갈 가운데 하나에게서 얻은 것인데, 저는 그에게 제 불행을 죄다 털어놓고, 제 원수가 있다고 들은 도시까지 동행해 달라고 간청했습니다. 그는 제 대담함에 항의하고 제 결심을 나무랐으나, 제가 뜻을 굳힌 것을 보고는, 자기 말로 세상 끝까지 동행하겠다고 했습니다. 저는 곧장 베갯잇 하나에 여자 옷 한 벌과, 비상시에 대비할 약간의 패물과 돈을 챙겨 넣고, 밤의 고요 속에서 제 배신자 하녀에게는 알리지 않은 채, 제 하인과 넘치는 근심을 동무 삼아 집을 빠져나와, 걸어서 그 도시로 출발했습니다. 다만 이미 끝났으리라 짐작되는 일을 막지는 못할지언정, 적어도 돈 페르난도에게 무슨 양심으로 그런 짓을 했는지 따져 묻고자 하는 조급함에, 마치 날개라도 단 듯이 실려 갔습니다. 저는 이틀 반 만에 목적지에 닿았고, 도시에 들어서서 루스신다 부모의 집을 물었습니다. 제가 처음 물은 사람은 제가 알고자 한 것보다 더 많은 것을 답으로 주었으니, 그는 제게 그 집을 가리켜 보였고, 그 집 딸의 약혼식에서 일어난 모든 일을 들려주었습니다. 그것은 도시에서 어찌나 떠들썩한 일이었던지 거리의 한가한 무리마다 입에 올리는 이야깃거리였습니다. 그가 말하기를, 돈 페르난도와 루스신다의 약혼식 날 밤, 그녀가 '예'라고 말하여 그의 신부가 되기를 승낙하자마자 별안간 기절하는 발작을 일으켰고, 신랑이 다가가 그녀에게 바람을 통하게 하려고 옷의 가슴 부분을 풀자, 그녀가 자기 손으로 쓴 종이 한 장을 발견했다는 것입니다. 그 종이에서 그녀는, 자기는 이미 카르데니오의 것이므로 돈 페르난도의 신부가 될 수 없다고 말하고 또 선언했는데, 그 사람의 말에 따르면 카르데니오는 같은 도시의 지체 높은 신사였다고 합니다. 그리고 자기가 돈 페르난도를 받아들인 것은 오직 부모에게 순종해서였다는 것입니다. 한마디로, 그가 말하기를, 그 종이의 글은 그녀가 약혼식이 끝나는 대로 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 했음을 분명히 보여 주었고, 자결하려는 까닭까지 밝혀 두었는데, 그 모든 것은 그녀의 옷 어딘가에서 발견된 단검으로 확증되었다고 했습니다. 이를 본 돈 페르난도는, 루스신다가 자기를 우롱하고 멸시하며 가지고 놀았다고 여겨, 그녀가 실신에서 깨어나기도 전에 달려들어 발견된 그 단검으로 그녀를 찌르려 했고, 그녀의 부모와 그 자리에 있던 이들이 막지 않았더라면 성공했으리라는 것입니다. 더욱이 돈 페르난도는 곧바로 떠나 버렸고, 루스신다는 이튿날에야 쓰러진 데서 회복하여, 자기가 사실은 제가 말씀드린 그 카르데니오의 신부라고 부모에게 말했다고 했습니다. 그 밖에 제가 알게 된 것은, 소문에 따르면 카르데니오가 약혼식 자리에 있었고, 자기 기대와는 달리 그녀가 약혼하는 것을 보고는 절망에 빠져 도시를 떠나 버렸으며, 루스신다가 자기에게 저지른 잘못과, 다시는 누구도 자기를 보지 못할 곳으로 가겠다는 자기 뜻을 밝힌 편지 한 통을 남겨 두었다는 것입니다. 이 모든 것이 도시에서 떠들썩한 일이었고, 누구나 그것을 입에 올렸는데, 특히 루스신다가 아버지의 집에서도 도시에서도 사라졌다는 것이 알려지면서 더욱 그러했으니, 그녀가 어디에서도 발견되지 않아 그 부모가 어찌할 바를 모르고 정신이 나갈 지경이었기 때문입니다. 제가 알게 된 것은 제 희망을 되살렸으니, 저는 돈 페르난도가 혼인한 것을 보느니 차라리 그를 못 찾은 것이 더 다행스러웠습니다. 제 처지에서 구원의 문이 아직 완전히 닫힌 것은 아니라는 생각이 들었고, 어쩌면 하늘이 그가 앞선 의무를 깨닫고, 기독교인으로서 그 어떤 인간사보다 자기 영혼을 우선해야 함을 돌아보게 하려고, 이 두 번째 혼인의 길에 이런 걸림돌을 놓아둔 것일지도 모른다고 생각했기 때문입니다. 이 모든 것이 제 마음을 스쳐 지나갔고, 저는 이제는 혐오하는 그 목숨을 부지하려는 희미하고 아득한 희망에 매달리며, 위안 없는 위안을 얻으려 애썼습니다.\n\n* 사갈: 스페인 농가에서 일하던 젊은 일꾼이나 머슴을 이르는 말.\n\"그러나 제가 그 도시에 있으면서, 돈 페르난도를 찾을 수 없어 어찌해야 할지 막막해하던 차에, 저를 찾아내는 사람에게 큰 상을 내리겠다는 관의 포고가 외쳐지는 것을 들었으니, 그 포고는 제 나이와 제가 입은 바로 그 옷차림의 세세한 점까지 알려 주고 있었습니다. 그리고 저와 함께 온 젊은이가 제 아버지의 집에서 저를 데려갔다는 말이 도는 것을 들었으니, 이는 제 가슴을 도려내는 일이었습니다. 제 좋은 이름이 얼마나 추락했는지를 보여 주는 것이, 제가 달아나면서 그것을 잃는 것으로도 모자라, 게다가 제가 누구와 함께 달아났는지까지 — 그것도 저보다 그토록 신분이 낮고 제 고려에 그토록 값하지 못하는 자와 — 덧붙여야 했기 때문입니다. 그 포고를 듣는 순간 저는 제 하인과 함께 도시를 떠났으니, 그는 이제 제게 대한 자기 충성이 흔들리는 기색을 보이기 시작했습니다. 그리고 발각될까 두려워 그날 밤 우리는 이 산들 가운데 가장 빽빽하게 우거진 곳으로 들어갔습니다. 그러나 흔히 말하듯, 한 가지 재앙이 다른 재앙을 불러오고, 한 불행의 끝이 더 큰 불행의 시작이 되기 쉬운데, 제 경우에도 그것이 사실로 드러났습니다. 그때까지 그토록 충실하고 미덥던 제 변변한 하인이 이 외진 곳에서 저를 보자, 제 아름다움보다는 제 자신의 사악함에 더 이끌려, 이 적막함이 그에게 베풀어 주는 듯한 기회를 이용하려 했으니, 부끄러움도 거의 없이, 하느님에 대한 두려움이나 저에 대한 존경은 더더욱 없이, 제게 수작을 걸기 시작했습니다. 그리고 그가 자기 제안의 뻔뻔함에 제가 마땅히 준엄한 말로 답하는 것을 보고는, 처음에 썼던 간청을 내려놓고 폭력을 쓰기 시작했습니다.\n\n\"그러나 선한 뜻을 지키고 도우시기를 좀처럼 게을리하지 않으시는 의로운 하늘이 제 뜻을 어찌나 도우셨던지, 보잘것없는 힘으로도, 별 애를 쓰지 않고도 저는 그를 벼랑 너머로 밀어 버렸으며, 그가 죽었는지 살았는지는 모르나 거기에 남겨 두었습니다. 그러고는 두려움과 피로 속에서 가능하리라 여겨지던 것보다 더 빠른 걸음으로, 산속에 몸을 숨기고, 아버지와 그의 명으로 저를 찾으러 파견된 이들에게서 벗어나는 것 말고는 다른 어떤 생각이나 목적도 없이, 저는 산속으로 길을 나아갔습니다. 이 목적으로 제가 이곳에 온 지 이제 몇 달이 되었는지 모르겠습니다만, 여기서 저는 한 목자를 만났고, 그는 이 시에라 한복판의 어느 곳에서 저를 자기 하인으로 들였으며, 그동안 줄곧 저는 그의 양치기 노릇을 하며, 이제 뜻밖에 저를 배신한 이 머리채를 숨기려고 늘 들판에 나가 있으려 애썼습니다. 그러나 저의 온갖 조심과 수고도 헛되었으니, 제 주인이 제가 남자가 아님을 알아내고, 제 하인이 품었던 것과 똑같은 비열한 속셈을 품었던 것입니다. 그리고 운명이 곤경에 처할 때마다 늘 해결책을 마련해 주는 것은 아니어서, 하인의 경우처럼 주인을 굴려 떨어뜨려 그 욕정을 고쳐 줄 벼랑이나 골짜기가 손닿는 곳에 없었기에, 저는 그와 힘과 논쟁을 겨뤄 보느니 차라리 그를 떠나 다시 이 바위들 사이에 몸을 숨기는 것이 덜한 화라고 여겼습니다. 그리하여, 말씀드렸듯이, 저는 한숨과 눈물로 하늘에 제 비참함을 가엾이 여겨 주십사, 거기서 벗어날 도움과 힘을 주십사 빌거나, 아니면 이 적막함 속에서 죽어, 자기 잘못도 없이 안팎에서 입길에 오르내리고 추문거리를 제공한 한 불행한 여인의 자취조차 남기지 않게 해 주십사 빌 만한 어떤 곳을 찾으려고 다시 한번 숨어들었던 것입니다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX.\n\n\"여러분, 이것이 제 슬픈 모험에 관한 진실된 이야기랍니다. 여러분이 들으신 그 한숨과 탄식, 그리고 제 두 눈에서 흘러내린 그 눈물이, 설령 제가 그것을 더 마음껏 쏟아 냈다 한들 충분한 까닭이 없었는지 이제 스스로 판단해 보십시오. 그리고 제 불행의 본질을 헤아려 보신다면, 거기에는 어떤 처방도 있을 수 없으니 위로란 한낱 헛된 일임을 아시게 될 것입니다. 제가 여러분께 청하는 것이라고는, 여러분께서 손쉽고도 사리에 맞게 해 주실 수 있는 일, 곧 저를 찾아 헤매는 자들에게 발각될 두려움과 공포에 시달리지 않고 제 삶을 보낼 수 있는 곳이 어디인지 일러 주시는 것뿐입니다. 비록 제 부모님께서 저를 향해 품으신 그 크나큰 사랑 덕분에 그분들께 따뜻이 받아들여지리라는 것은 확실히 느끼지만, 그분들이 기대하시는 모습으로 제 자신을 그 앞에 내놓을 수 없다는 그 생각만으로도 제 부끄러움이 너무도 크기에, 그분들이 마땅히 제게 바랐던 그 순결을 빼앗긴 제 얼굴을 그분들이 바라본다는 생각으로 그분들의 얼굴을 마주하느니, 차라리 그분들의 시야에서 영영 저 자신을 추방해 버리는 편이 낫겠습니다.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그녀는 입을 다물었고, 그녀의 얼굴을 뒤덮은 홍조는 그녀가 마음속에서 겪고 있는 고통과 수치를 또렷이 드러내 보였다. 듣고 있던 이들은 그녀의 불행에 놀라움 못지않게 큰 연민을 느꼈으나, 신부가 막 그녀에게 위로와 조언을 건네려는 참에 카르데니오가 그를 앞질러 말했다. \"그렇다면 아가씨, 당신이 바로 부유한 클레나르도의 외동딸인 아름다운 도로테아로군요?\" 도로테아는 자기 아버지의 이름을 듣고서, 또 그 이름을 입에 올린 자의 비참한 행색을 보고서 깜짝 놀랐으니, 카르데니오가 얼마나 초라하게 옷을 걸치고 있었는지는 이미 앞서 말한 바와 같다. 그리하여 그녀는 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"제 아버지의 이름을 그토록 잘 아시는 듯한 당신은 대체 누구신가요, 오라버니? 제 기억이 옳다면, 지금까지 제 불행에 관한 이야기 전체에서 저는 그 이름을 언급한 적이 없거든요.\"\n\n\"아가씨, 저는 바로 그 불행한 자랍니다.\" 카르데니오가 대답했다. \"당신이 말씀하신 대로, 루신다가 자기 남편이라고 선언했던 그 사람 말입니다. 저는 불운한 카르데니오로, 당신을 지금의 처지로 몰아넣은 그자의 악행 때문에 당신이 보시는 이 꼴로 전락한 사람입니다. 헐벗고 누더기를 걸치고 인간다운 온갖 위안을 빼앗겼으며, 더욱 나쁘게는 이성마저 빼앗겼으니, 하늘이 기뻐하시어 잠시잠깐 그것을 제게 되돌려 주실 때에만 저는 이성을 지닐 뿐입니다. 도로테아, 저는 돈 페르난도가 저지른 악행을 목격하고, 루신다가 그의 약혼녀임을 스스로 인정하며 내뱉은 그 '예'라는 말을 들으려고 기다리던 사람입니다. 저는 그녀의 실신이 어떻게 끝났는지, 그녀의 가슴에서 발견된 그 종이가 어찌 되었는지를 끝까지 지켜볼 만한 용기조차 없었던 사람입니다. 제 심장이 그토록 많은 불운의 타격을 한꺼번에 견뎌 낼 만한 굳셈을 지니지 못했기 때문이지요. 그리하여 인내심을 잃고 그 집을 떠났으며, 주인에게 편지 한 통을 맡겨 부디 루신다의 손에 전해 달라고 간청한 뒤, 이 황량한 곳으로 몸을 옮겨 와, 마치 불구대천의 원수를 대하듯 미워하던 이 목숨을 여기서 끝내기로 결심했습니다. 그러나 운명은 제게서 그것을 거두어 가려 하지 않고, 그저 제 이성을 앗아 가는 것으로 만족했으니, 아마도 당신을 만나는 행운을 위해 저를 살려 두려 한 것이겠지요. 방금 당신이 우리에게 들려주신 것이 사실이라면, 제가 그것을 사실이라 믿거니와, 어쩌면 하늘은 우리 두 사람 모두에게, 우리가 바라는 것보다 더 행복한 불행의 결말을 아직 마련해 두셨을지도 모릅니다. 루신다가 그토록 공공연히 스스로 선언한 대로 제 사람이니 돈 페르난도와 혼인할 수 없고, 돈 페르난도 또한 당신 사람이니 그녀와 혼인할 수 없음을 보건대, 우리 것이 아직 존재하며 아직 양도되거나 파괴되지 않은 이상, 하늘이 우리에게 우리 것을 되돌려 주시리라 충분히 바랄 수 있겠지요. 그리고 우리에게 이 위안이 어떤 허황된 희망이나 터무니없는 공상에서 솟아난 것이 아니니, 아가씨, 부디 더 나은 마음으로 새로운 결심을 세우시기를 간청합니다. 저 또한 제 마음에 그리하여 더 행복한 운명을 내다볼 채비를 하려 하니까요. 저는 신사이자 그리스도교인의 신의를 걸고 맹세하건대, 당신이 돈 페르난도를 차지하는 것을 볼 때까지 당신을 저버리지 않겠습니다. 만일 제가 말로써 그가 당신에 대한 의무를 인정하도록 이끌 수 없다면, 그때에는 신사로서 제 신분이 제게 부여하는 권리를 행사하여, 그가 당신에게 끼친 해악을 들어 정당한 명분으로 그에게 결투를 청하겠습니다. 제 자신이 입은 부당함은 돌아보지 않을 것이며, 그것은 하늘이 갚아 주시도록 맡겨 두고, 저는 이 땅에서 당신의 일에 제 자신을 바치겠습니다.\"\n\n카르데니오의 말은 도로테아의 놀라움을 극에 달하게 했으니, 그러한 제안에 어떻게 감사를 표해야 할지 알지 못한 그녀는 그의 발에 입을 맞추려 했으나, 카르데니오는 그것을 허락하지 않았다. 학사가 두 사람을 대신해 대답하며 카르데니오의 건전한 논리를 칭찬하고는, 마침내 그들에게 자기 마을로 함께 가자고 청하고 권하고 재촉했으니, 거기서 필요한 것을 갖추고, 돈 페르난도를 찾아낼 방도를 강구하거나, 도로테아를 그 부모에게 되돌려 보내거나, 아니면 그들에게 가장 좋다고 여겨지는 일을 할 수 있으리라는 것이었다. 카르데니오와 도로테아는 그에게 감사하며 그가 베푼 친절한 제안을 받아들였다. 그리고 그동안 모든 것을 주의 깊게 묵묵히 듣고 있던 이발사도 제 나름대로 다정한 말 몇 마디를 건네고는, 신부에 못지않은 호의로 그들에게 도움이 될 만한 일이라면 무엇이든 자기 힘을 보태겠다고 했다. 그는 또한 자기들을 그곳으로 데려온 목적과 돈키호테의 광기가 지닌 기이한 성질, 그리고 그를 찾아 나선 그의 종자를 그들이 기다리고 있다는 사정을 몇 마디로 설명해 주었다. 그러자 마치 꿈을 떠올리듯, 자기가 돈키호테와 벌였던 다툼이 카르데니오의 기억 속에 되살아났고, 그는 그것을 다른 이들에게 들려주었으나 그 다툼이 무엇에 관한 것이었는지는 말할 수 없었다.\n\n이때 그들은 고함 소리를 듣고, 그것이 산초 판사에게서 나는 것임을 알아차렸다. 그를 두고 떠났던 자리에서 그들을 찾지 못한 산초가 큰 소리로 그들을 부르고 있었던 것이다. 그들이 그를 맞으러 나가, 돈키호테에 관해 묻자, 그는 자기가 어떻게 셔츠 바람으로 깡마르고 누렇게 뜬 채 굶주림으로 반쯤 죽어 가며 그의 귀부인 둘시네아를 향해 한숨짓고 있는 주인을 발견했는지 들려주었다. 그리고 비록 그녀께서 그곳을 떠나, 그를 기다리고 있는 엘 토보소로 오라 명하셨다고 일렀건만, 주인은 그녀의 총애를 받을 만한 행적을 이루기 전에는 그 미모 앞에 나서지 않기로 결심했노라 대답했다는 것이다. 산초의 말로는, 이런 식으로 가다가는 주인이 마땅히 되어야 할 황제는 고사하고, 적어도 될 수 있는 최소한의 자리인 대주교조차 되지 못할 위험이 있으니, 그를 거기서 빼내 올 방도를 궁리해야 한다는 것이었다. 학사는 이에 대답하여, 마음 졸이지 말라고, 그가 원하든 원치 않든 데려올 것이라고 했다. 그러고는 카르데니오와 도로테아에게, 돈키호테를 고치거나 적어도 집으로 데려가기 위해 자기들이 무엇을 하기로 했는지를 일러 주었다. 그러자 도로테아는, 자기가 이발사보다 곤경에 빠진 처녀 역을 더 잘할 수 있노라고 했으니, 더욱이 그렇게 실감 나게 해 보일 옷차림을 가지고 있을 뿐 아니라, 그들의 계략을 실행하는 데 필요한 모든 세부 대목에서 그 역을 연기하는 일을 자기에게 맡겨도 좋다고 했다. 그녀는 기사도 책을 매우 많이 읽었기에, 고난에 처한 처녀들이 편력 기사들에게 은혜를 청하는 그 방식을 정확히 알고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 신부가 말했다. \"당장 착수하는 것 말고는 더 필요한 것이 없겠소이다. 운명이 이렇듯 뜻밖에도 당신의 구제를 위해 문을 열어 주기 시작하고, 우리가 목적에 이르는 길을 평탄하게 닦아 주었으니, 운명이 우리 편을 들고 있음에 의심의 여지가 없으니 말이오.\"\n\n그러자 도로테아는 베갯잇에서 값진 천으로 지은 페티코트 한 벌과 또 다른 고운 옷감으로 만든 초록 망토를 꺼내고, 작은 상자에서 목걸이와 다른 장신구들을 꺼내더니, 이것들로 순식간에 단장하여 위엄 있고 부유한 귀부인처럼 보였다. 이 모든 것을, 아니 그 이상을 만일을 대비해 집에서 챙겨 왔으나, 그때까지 쓸 일이 없었노라고 그녀는 말했다. 그녀의 우아함과 자태와 아름다움에 모두가 무척이나 기뻐하며, 돈 페르난도가 이런 매력을 마다했다니 안목이 보잘것없는 사내라고 입을 모았다. 그러나 그녀를 가장 감탄하며 바라본 이는 산초 판사였으니, 그가 보기에는 (실제로도 그러했거니와) 평생토록 그토록 사랑스러운 여인을 본 적이 없는 듯했다. 그리하여 그는 무척이나 열을 올리며 신부에게, 이 아름다운 귀부인은 누구이며, 이런 외딴 두메에서 무엇을 원하는지 물었다.\n\n\"산초 형제여, 이 아름다운 귀부인은,\" 신부가 대답했다. \"다름이 아니라, 위대한 미코미콘 왕국을 직계 남성 혈통으로 물려받은 상속녀로, 당신의 주인을 찾아와 그에게 은혜를 청하시려는 분이라네. 그 은혜란, 사악한 거인이 그녀에게 저지른 잘못이나 해악을 바로잡아 달라는 것이지. 당신의 주인이 훌륭한 기사로서 사방에 떨친 명성을 듣고, 이 공주께서 기니에서부터 그를 찾아 오신 것이라네.\"\n\n\"운 좋게 찾아오셔서 운 좋게 만나셨군요!\" 산초 판사가 이 말에 대꾸했다. \"특히 제 주인님께서 운 좋게 그 해악을 바로잡고, 그 잘못을 바루며, 나리께서 말씀하시는 그 개자식 같은 거인을 죽이실 수 있다면 말입니다. 그놈을 만나기만 하면 죽이실 게 틀림없지요. 다만 그놈이 유령이라면 모를 일이지만요. 제 주인님은 유령에게는 도무지 힘을 쓰지 못하시거든요. 그런데 다른 무엇보다도 나리께 한 가지 청드리고 싶은 게 있습니다, 학사 나리. 제 주인님이 대주교가 되겠다는 마음을 먹지 않도록 — 바로 그게 제가 두려워하는 바입니다만 — 나리께서 그분께 이 공주님과 당장 혼인하시라고 권해 주십사 하는 것입니다. 이렇게 하면 그분은 대주교 서품을 받을 수 없게 되고, 손쉽게 자기 제국을 얻으실 테고, 저도 제 소원의 끝에 다다를 테니까요. 제가 이 일을 곰곰이 따져 보았는데, 제가 헤아릴 수 있는 한, 제 주인님이 대주교가 되시는 건 제게 좋을 게 없다는 걸 알았습니다. 저는 처자식이 있는 몸이라 교회에는 쓸모가 없거든요. 게다가 지금 저처럼 아내와 자식들이 있는 사람이 교회 밑에서 돈벌이 되는 자리를 차지할 수 있도록 면제를 얻어 내려 하자면 끝이 없는 일이 될 테니까요. 그러니 나리, 모든 것은 제 주인님이 이 귀부인과 당장 혼인하시느냐에 달렸습니다. 저는 아직 이분의 존함을 모르니, 이름으로 불러 드릴 수가 없군요.\"\n\n\"이분은 미코미코나 공주라고 불린다네.\" 신부가 말했다. \"이분의 왕국이 미코미콘이니, 그게 이분의 이름임이 분명하지.\"\n\n\"그건 의심할 바 없는 일이군요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"저는 태어난 곳에서 이름과 칭호를 따서 알칼라의 페드로, 우베다의 후안, 바야돌리드의 디에고라고 스스로를 부르는 이들을 많이 알고 있거든요. 저 멀리 기니에서도 왕비님들이 마찬가지로 자기 왕국의 이름을 따는 풍습이 있는지도 모를 일이지요.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지.\" 신부가 말했다. \"그리고 당신 주인의 혼인에 관해서라면, 내가 할 수 있는 모든 힘을 다하겠네.\" 이 말에 산초는 무척이나 기뻐했고, 신부는 그의 순박함과, 그가 자기 주인의 황당무계한 짓에 그토록 사로잡힌 모습을 보고 어리둥절해했으니, 산초가 자기 주인이 황제가 되리라고 스스로를 분명히 납득시킨 것이 틀림없었기 때문이다.\n\n이때쯤 도로테아는 신부의 노새에 올라앉았고, 이발사는 소꼬리 수염을 얼굴에 붙였다. 그러고는 산초에게, 자기들을 돈키호테가 있는 곳으로 안내하되, 학사나 이발사를 안다는 말은 하지 말라고 일렀으니, 주인이 황제가 되는 것은 오로지 그가 그들을 알아보지 못하는 데 달렸다는 것이었다. 그러나 신부도 카르데니오도 그들과 함께 가는 것이 좋겠다고 여기지는 않았다. 카르데니오는 돈키호테에게 그와 벌였던 다툼을 떠올리게 할까 봐서였고, 신부는 아직은 자기가 그 자리에 있을 필요가 없어서였다. 그리하여 그들은 다른 이들을 앞서 가게 하고, 자기들은 천천히 걸어서 뒤따랐다. 신부는 도로테아에게 어떻게 처신해야 할지 일러 주는 것을 잊지 않았으나, 그녀는 모든 것을 기사도 책이 요구하고 묘사하는 그대로 정확히 해낼 테니 안심해도 좋다고 했다.\n\n그들이 사분의 삼 레과쯤 갔을 때, 바위투성이 황무지에서 돈키호테를 발견했는데, 이때쯤 그는 옷은 걸쳤으나 갑옷은 입지 않은 차림이었다. 도로테아는 그를 보고, 산초에게서 저이가 돈키호테라는 말을 듣자마자 자기 작은 말에 채찍질을 했고, 수염을 잘 붙인 이발사가 그 뒤를 따랐다. 그에게 다가가자 그녀의 종자가 노새에서 뛰어내려 두 팔로 그녀를 받으려 나아왔고, 그녀는 무척이나 우아한 몸가짐으로 내려서서 돈키호테의 발 앞에 무릎을 꿇으려 나아갔다. 그가 그녀를 일으켜 세우려 애썼으나, 그녀는 일어나지 않은 채 이렇게 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"용맹하고 굳센 기사님, 당신의 선량함과 정중함이 제게 한 가지 은혜를 베풀어 주시기 전에는, 저는 이 자리에서 일어나지 않겠습니다. 그 은혜는 당신의 명예와 명성을 드높일 것이며, 해 아래 가장 비탄에 잠기고 고난에 빠진 처녀에게 봉사를 베푸는 일이 될 것입니다. 당신의 굳센 팔의 힘이 당신의 불멸하는 명성의 평판에 걸맞다면, 당신은 당신의 드높은 이름의 향기에 이끌려 자신의 불행 속에서 당신의 도움을 구하고자 머나먼 땅에서 찾아온 이 의지가지없는 존재를 도와주실 의무가 있습니다.\"\n\n\"아름다운 귀부인이여, 그대가 땅에서 일어서기 전에는,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"나는 한마디도 답하지 않을 것이며, 그대에 관한 그 어떤 말도 더는 듣지 않으리다.\"\n\n\"기사님, 청컨대 당신의 정중함으로 제가 청하는 그 은혜를 먼저 베풀어 주시기 전에는, 저는 일어나지 않겠습니다.\" 고난에 처한 처녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 그것을 허락하고 응낙하노라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"다만 나의 임금, 나의 조국, 또는 내 마음과 자유의 열쇠를 쥐고 있는 그분께 해롭거나 누가 되지 않는 한에서 이루어질 수 있는 것이어야 하오.\"\n\n\"그 누구에게도 해롭거나 누가 되지 않을 것입니다, 거룩하신 나리.\" 고난에 처한 처녀가 말했다. 이때 산초 판사가 주인의 귀에 바싹 다가가 아주 나직이 말했다. \"나리, 이분이 청하시는 은혜는 아무 거리낌 없이 베풀어 주셔도 됩니다. 아무것도 아니에요. 그저 큰 거인 하나를 죽이는 일일 뿐이거든요. 이걸 청하시는 분은 고귀하신 미코미코나 공주님으로, 에티오피아의 위대한 미코미콘 왕국의 여왕이시랍니다.\"\n\n\"그분이 누구이든 간에,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"나는 내가 맹세한 바에 따라, 마땅히 해야 할 나의 의무와 내 양심이 명하는 바를 행하리라.\" 그러고는 처녀를 향해 돌아서서 말했다. \"그대의 위대한 아름다움이여, 일어나시오. 그대가 내게 청하려는 은혜를 허락하노니.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제가 청하는 바는,\" 처녀가 말했다. \"당신의 너그러우신 몸이 제가 인도하는 곳으로 당장 저와 함께 가 주시는 것, 그리고 인간의 법과 신의 법을 모조리 어기고 제 왕국을 찬탈한 한 반역자에게서 제 원수를 갚아 주시기 전에는 그 어떤 다른 모험이나 탐색에도 나서지 않으시겠다고 약속해 주시는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"거듭 말하거니와, 내가 그것을 허락하노라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그러니 귀부인이여, 그대는 오늘부터 그대를 짓누르는 우수를 벗어 던지고, 그대의 시들어 가던 희망에 새로운 생명과 힘을 불어넣으시오. 하느님과 나의 팔의 도움으로, 그대는 머지않아 그대의 왕국을 되찾고, 그것을 가로막으려는 흉악한 무리들에도 불구하고 그대의 유서 깊고 강대한 영토의 옥좌에 다시 앉은 그대 자신을 보게 될 터이니. 자, 이제 일에 착수합시다. 지체함에는 흔히 위험이 따르는 법이니.\"\n\n고난에 처한 처녀는 무척이나 끈질기게 그의 두 손에 입을 맞추려 애썼으나, 만사에 세련되고 정중한 기사였던 돈키호테는 결코 그것을 허락하지 않고, 그녀를 일으켜 세워 무척이나 정중하고 공손하게 그녀를 끌어안았다. 그러고는 산초에게 로시난테의 뱃대끈을 살피고 한시도 지체 없이 자기에게 갑옷을 입히라고 명했다. 산초는 마치 전리품처럼 나무에 걸려 있던 갑옷을 내리고, 뱃대끈을 살핀 다음 눈 깜짝할 새에 주인에게 갑옷을 입혔다. 갑옷을 차려입은 자신을 보자마자 돈키호테가 외쳤다.\n\n\"하느님의 이름으로, 이 위대한 귀부인을 도우러 떠납시다.\"\n\n이발사는 그동안 줄곧 무릎을 꿇은 채 웃음을 감추고 수염이 떨어지지 않게 하느라 무척이나 애를 먹고 있었으니, 만일 그것이 떨어졌더라면 그들의 멋진 계략이 허사가 되었을지도 모르기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 은혜가 허락되고, 그것을 이행하려 돈키호테가 채비하는 그 민첩함을 보고서, 그는 일어나 자기 귀부인의 손을 잡았고, 둘이서 함께 그녀를 노새에 태웠다. 그러고 나서 돈키호테는 로시난테에 올라타고, 이발사는 제 짐승 위에 자리를 잡았으나, 산초는 걸어서 가게 되었으니, 이제 와서 회색 당나귀가 없는 것을 절감하며 잃어버린 그놈이 새삼 아쉬워졌다. 그래도 그는 모든 것을 흔쾌히 견뎌 냈으니, 자기 주인이 이제 제대로 첫발을 내디뎌 막 황제가 되려는 참이라고 믿었기 때문이다. 그는 주인이 이 공주와 혼인하여 적어도 미코미콘의 왕은 되리라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 다만 한 가지 그를 괴롭히는 것은, 이 왕국이 흑인들의 땅에 있어, 그들이 자기에게 신하로 내줄 백성들이 모두 흑인일 것이라는 생각이었다. 그러나 이에 대해서도 그는 곧 자기 공상 속에서 해결책을 찾아내고는 혼잣말을 했다. \"내 신하들이 흑인이라 한들 그게 나와 무슨 상관이람? 그놈들을 한 배에 실어 스페인으로 데려가, 거기서 팔아 현금을 챙기고, 그 돈으로 작위나 관직을 하나 사서 평생 편안히 살면 그뿐 아니겠어? 잠이나 자고, 머리도 재주도 없어서 일을 잘 굴려 가며 떠드는 사이에 신하 삼천, 육천, 만 명을 팔아 치우지 못하는 게 아니라면야! 맹세코 나는 크든 작든 그놈들을 죄다 휘저어 놓을 테고, 아무리 검다 한들 흰둥이나 누렁이로 둔갑시켜 버리겠어. 자, 자, 내가 이 무슨 멍청이람!\" 이렇게 그는 제 생각에 골몰하여 마음 편히 터덜터덜 걸으며, 걸어서 가는 고생일랑 까맣게 잊고 있었다.\n\n카르데니오와 신부는 덤불 사이에서 이 모든 것을 지켜보며 어떻게 다른 이들과 합류할지 알지 못하고 있었으나, 꾀가 무척이나 많은 신부는 곧 자기들의 목적을 이룰 방도를 생각해 냈다. 그는 갑에 넣어 둔 가위 한 자루로 재빨리 카르데니오의 수염을 잘라 내고, 자기 회색 윗옷을 그에게 입힌 다음 검은 망토를 건네주고는, 자기는 반바지와 더블릿 차림으로 남았다. 그리하여 카르데니오의 행색이 전과는 너무도 달라져, 그가 거울에 비친 자신을 보았더라도 알아보지 못했을 정도였다. 이렇게 변장을 마친 뒤, 변장하는 동안 다른 이들이 앞서 가 버렸으나, 그들은 손쉽게 그들보다 앞질러 큰길로 나설 수 있었으니, 그들이 마주친 가시덤불과 험한 곳들 때문에 말을 탄 이들이 걷는 이들만큼 빨리 가지 못했기 때문이다. 그러고 나서 그들은 시에라 어귀의 평지에 자리를 잡았고, 돈키호테와 그 일행이 거기서 모습을 드러내자마자 신부는 마치 그를 알아보려 애쓰는 듯 무척이나 찬찬히 그를 살펴보았다. 그러고는 한참 동안 그를 빤히 바라본 끝에 두 팔을 벌리고 그에게 달려가며 외쳤다. \"기사도의 거울이신, 나의 거룩한 동향 분, 라만차의 돈키호테 님을 이렇게 만나 뵙다니 얼마나 다행한 일입니까! 고귀한 가문의 꽃이자 정수요, 고난에 빠진 이들의 보호자요 구원자며, 편력 기사들의 진수이신 분을 말입니다!\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 돈키호테의 왼쪽 다리 무릎을 두 팔로 끌어안았다. 돈키호테는 이 낯선 이의 말과 행동에 깜짝 놀라 주의 깊게 그를 바라보다가, 마침내 그를 알아보고는, 그를 거기서 보게 되어 무척이나 놀라며 말에서 내리려 갖은 애를 썼다. 그러나 신부는 이를 허락하지 않았으니, 이에 돈키호테가 말했다. \"학사 나리, 허락해 주시오. 내가 말을 탄 채 있고 나리처럼 존경스러운 분이 걸어서 가시는 것은 온당치 못한 일이니.\"\n\n\"결코 허락할 수 없습니다.\" 신부가 말했다. \"기사님께서는 말 위에 그대로 계셔야 합니다. 우리 시대에 보아 온 가장 위대한 위업과 모험을 기사님께서 이루시는 곳이 바로 말 위이니 말입니다. 저로 말하자면, 보잘것없는 사제이니, 기사님을 모시는 이 분들의 노새 한 마리의 엉덩이에 올라타는 것으로도 충분합니다. 만일 그분들이 마다하지 않으신다면 말이지요. 그러면 저는 페가수스 준마에, 아니 저 위대한 콤플루툼에서 그리 멀지 않은 술레마의 큰 언덕에 오늘날까지도 마법에 걸려 누워 있는 그 유명한 무어인 무사라케를 태우던 얼룩말이나 군마에 올라탄 셈 치겠습니다.\"\n\n\"학사 나리, 그것조차도 나는 동의할 수 없소이다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그리고 나의 귀부인이신 공주께서 나를 향한 사랑으로, 자신의 종자에게 그 노새의 안장을 나리께 내드리라 기꺼이 명하시리라는 것을 나는 알고 있으며, 그 종자는 짐승이 견뎌 낸다면 뒤에 앉아 갈 수 있을 것이오.\"\n\n\"그리하고말고요.\" 공주가 말했다. \"그리고 제가 굳이 제 종자에게 명할 필요도 없으리라 봅니다. 그는 너무도 정중하고 사려 깊은 자라, 자기가 탈 수 있는데도 성직자를 걸어가게 두지는 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요.\" 이발사가 말하고는 곧장 내려서서 신부에게 자기 안장을 내주었고, 신부는 별다른 간청 없이 그것을 받아들였다. 그러나 불행히도 이발사가 뒤에 올라타려는 참에, 마침 빌려 온 노새였으니 — 이는 곧 성질이 못된 놈이라는 말과 같은데 — 그놈이 뒷발굽을 쳐들어 허공에 두어 차례 발길질을 해 댔다. 만일 그것이 가슴이나 머리를 맞혔더라면 니콜라스 영감은 돈키호테를 찾아 나선 이 원정을 악마에게나 줘 버렸으면 싶었을 것이다. 어찌 됐든 그것이 그를 어찌나 불시에 덮쳤던지 그는 땅바닥에 나동그라졌고, 자기 수염에는 거의 신경도 쓰지 못하는 바람에 수염이 떨어져 버렸다. 수염 없는 자신을 발견하고 그가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 황급히 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 이빨이 부러졌다고 신음하는 것뿐이었다. 턱뼈도 피도 없이 그 수염 뭉치가 넘어진 종자의 얼굴에서 떨어져 나간 것을 본 돈키호테가 외쳤다.\n\n\"살아 계신 하느님께 맹세코, 이건 참으로 대단한 기적이로다! 마치 일부러 면도해 낸 것처럼 그의 얼굴에서 수염을 떼어 내 뽑아 버렸으니 말이다.\"\n\n신부는 자기 계략을 위협하는 발각의 위험을 보고, 곧장 수염에 달려들어 그것을 들고서 여전히 신음을 내뱉고 있는 니콜라스 영감이 누운 곳으로 서둘러 갔다. 그러고는 그의 머리를 자기 가슴께로 끌어당겨 순식간에 수염을 도로 붙이며, 그 위로 무어라 몇 마디를 중얼거렸는데, 그것은 곧 보게 되겠지만 수염을 붙이는 어떤 특별한 주문이라는 것이었다. 수염을 고정하자마자 그는 그를 떠났고, 종자는 전과 다름없이 수염이 멀쩡하고 온전한 모습으로 나타났다. 이에 돈키호테는 이루 말할 수 없이 놀라며, 기회가 닿으면 그 주문을 자기에게 가르쳐 달라고 신부에게 청했으니, 그 효험이 수염을 붙이는 일을 넘어설 것이 틀림없다고 확신했기 때문이다. 수염이 떨어져 나간 곳에는 살이 찢기고 째진 채로 남아 있었을 것이 분명한데, 그 모든 것을 낫게 할 수 있다면 그것은 수염 이상의 일에도 쓸모가 있을 것이 분명하다는 것이었다.\n\n\"과연 그렇습니다.\" 신부가 말하고는, 첫 기회가 닿는 대로 그것을 가르쳐 주겠노라 약속했다. 그러고는 우선은 신부가 말에 타고, 세 사람이 객줏집에 이를 때까지 번갈아 타고 가기로 합의했으니, 그 객줏집은 그들이 있는 곳에서 한 여섯 레과쯤 떨어져 있을 듯했다.\n\n이렇게 세 사람, 곧 돈키호테와 공주와 신부가 말에 타고, 세 사람, 곧 카르데니오와 이발사와 산초 판사가 걸어서 가게 되자, 돈키호테가 처녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"귀부인이여, 그대께서 가장 마음에 드는 곳이 어디든 그리로 앞장서 인도하시오.\" 그러나 그녀가 미처 대답하기도 전에 학사가 말했다.\n\n\"마님께서는 우리의 행로를 어느 왕국으로 이끄시려는지요? 혹여 미코미콘 왕국이 아닙니까? 그곳이 분명할 것이외다. 아니라면 내가 왕국에 관해 아는 바가 거의 없는 셈이지요.\"\n\n매사에 빈틈없던 그녀는, 자기가 \"예\"라고 답해야 함을 알아차리고 이렇게 말했다. \"예, 나리, 제 갈 길은 그 왕국을 향해 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 신부가 말했다. \"우리는 내 마을을 곧장 지나가야 할 것이며, 거기서 기사님께서는 카르타헤나로 가는 길을 잡으셔야 합니다. 운만 따라 준다면 거기서 배에 오르실 수 있을 테니까요. 그리고 바람이 순조롭고 바다가 잔잔하고 고요하다면, 한 구 년 남짓이면 위대한 메오나 호수, 아니 메오티데스 호수가 보이는 곳에 이르실 수 있을 것입니다. 그곳은 마님의 왕국에서 이쪽으로 백 일 남짓의 여정밖에 안 되는 곳이지요.\"\n\n\"나리, 잘못 아셨습니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 그곳을 떠난 지 두 해도 채 되지 않았으니까요. 한 번도 날씨가 좋았던 적은 없었지만, 그래도 저는 이렇게 제가 그토록 간절히 바라던 것을 보러 여기에 와 있습니다. 그것은 바로 저의 주인이신 라만차의 돈키호테 님으로, 제가 스페인 땅에 발을 들여놓자마자 그분의 명성이 제 귀에 들려와 저로 하여금 그분을 찾아 나서게 했지요. 그분의 정중함에 제 자신을 의탁하고, 제 대의의 정의를 그분의 무적의 팔의 힘에 맡기고자 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그만하시오, 더는 칭찬하지 마시오.\" 돈키호테가 이에 말했다. \"나는 온갖 아첨을 싫어하니, 이것이 설령 아첨은 아니라 할지라도, 그런 식의 말은 나의 정결한 귀에 거슬리오. 다만 한마디 하리다, 귀부인이여. 그 팔에 힘이 있든 없든, 그것이 지녔거나 지니지 못한 것은 죽음에 이르도록 그대를 섬기는 데 바쳐질 것이오. 자, 이 일은 마땅한 때로 미뤄 두고, 나는 학사 나리께서 무엇이 그대를 이런 곳으로, 그것도 혼자서, 시종도 없이, 내가 놀라움을 금치 못할 만큼 가벼운 차림으로 데려왔는지 일러 주시기를 청하오.\"\n\n\"그것은 간단히 답하지요.\" 신부가 대답했다. \"돈키호테 님, 그러니까 우리의 벗이자 이발사인 니콜라스 영감과 제가 세비야로 가던 길이었습니다. 여러 해 전 인도 제도로 건너간 제 친척이 제게 보낸 돈을 받으러 가는 길이었지요. 그것도 적은 액수가 아니라, 묵직한 무게의 팔레알 은화로 육만 닢이 넘는 돈이었으니, 대단한 액수지요. 그런데 어제 이곳을 지나다가 노상강도 넷에게 습격을 당해, 그놈들이 우리 수염까지 죄다 벗겨 갔습니다. 수염을 어찌나 깎아 가 버렸던지 이발사는 가짜 수염을 붙여야 할 지경이었고, 여기 이 젊은이까지도\" — 카르데니오를 가리키며 — \"그놈들이 완전히 딴사람으로 만들어 놓았답니다. 그런데 무엇보다 기막힌 것은, 이 근방에 도는 소문에 따르면, 우리를 습격한 자들이 갤리선 노예 무리에 속한 자들이라는 겁니다. 그놈들은 바로 이 자리 근처에서, 호송관과 호위병들이 있는데도 어찌나 용맹한 자였는지 그 사내가 그놈들을 죄다 풀어 주었다고 하더군요. 그자는 의심할 바 없이 제정신이 아니었거나, 아니면 그놈들 못지않은 악당이었거나, 아니면 양 떼 사이에 늑대를, 닭들 사이에 여우를, 꿀단지에 파리를 풀어놓을 만큼 가슴도 양심도 없는 자가 분명합니다. 그자는 정의를 기만하고, 자기 임금이자 정당한 주군께 맞섰으니, 그분의 정당한 명령에 대항한 것이지요. 다시 말해 그자는 갤리선에서 그 다리를 빼앗고, 여러 해 동안 잠잠했던 성스러운 형제단을 들쑤셔 놓았으며, 끝내는 제 몸에는 아무런 이득도 없이 제 영혼만 잃을 수도 있는 짓을 저지른 것입니다.\" 산초가 신부와 이발사에게 갤리선 노예들의 모험, 곧 자기 주인이 그토록 영광스럽게 이뤄 낸 그 일을 일러 주었던 터라, 신부는 그것을 언급하면서 돈키호테가 무어라 말하거나 어찌 행동하는지 보려고 일부러 잔뜩 부풀린 것이었다. 그러자 돈키호테는 한마디 한마디마다 안색이 변했으나, 그 갸륵한 자들을 풀어 준 자가 바로 자신이라고는 감히 말하지 못했다. \"그러니까,\" 신부가 말했다. \"우리를 턴 자들이 바로 그놈들이었던 셈이지요. 마땅히 받아야 할 벌을 받으러 가도록 그놈들을 내버려 두지 않은 자를, 하느님께서 자비로이 용서해 주시기를.\"\n* 팔레알 은화로 육만 닢: 원문은 \"pieces of eight\", 곧 당대 스페인의 8레알짜리 은화를 가리킨다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXX.\n\n신부가 말을 마치기가 무섭게 산초가 입을 열었다. \"그렇다면 솔직히 말씀드리는데, 신부님, 그 짓을 한 분이 바로 제 주인 나리입니다요. 미리 말씀드리고 무슨 짓을 하시는지 조심하시라고, 그자들을 풀어 주는 건 죄라고, 다들 워낙 못된 악당들이라 그래서 끌려가던 길이라고 일러 드리지 않은 것도 아닙니다요.\"\n\n\"이 멍청아.\" 이 말에 돈키호테가 끼어들었다. \"큰길에서 마주치는, 고통받거나 사슬에 묶이거나 억눌린 자들이 자기 죄 때문에 그리 가며 그런 고초를 겪는지, 아니면 불운 때문인지를 따져 묻는 것은 편력 기사가 할 일도 관여할 바도 아니니라. 기사가 마음 쓸 일이라고는 그들의 악행이 아니라 그들의 고난을 살펴, 도움이 필요한 자로서 그들을 돕는 것뿐이다. 나는 비참하고 불운한 자들의 묵주 알처럼 줄지어 엮인 무리를 만났고, 내 의무감이 요구하는 바를 그들에게 행했으며, 그 나머지야 어찌 되었든 상관없는 일이니라. 그리고 누구든 이에 트집을 잡는 자가 있다면, 신부님의 거룩한 위엄과 그 존귀한 신분을 제외하고는, 나는 그자가 기사도에 관해 아는 바가 거의 없으며 매춘부의 자식 같은 악당처럼 거짓을 지껄인다고 말하겠으며, 이를 내 검으로 끝까지 똑똑히 알게 해 주리라.\" 이렇게 말하면서 그는 등자를 굳게 딛고 투구를 눌러썼다. 그의 말로는 맘브리노의 투구라는 그 이발사의 대야는, 갤리선 노예들이 그것에 입힌 손상을 고칠 수 있을 때까지 안장 앞머리에 매달아 두고 있었던 것이다.\n\n영리하고 발랄한 도로테아는 이때쯤 돈키호테의 미친 기질을 완전히 꿰뚫고 있었으며, 산초 판사를 제외한 모두가 그를 놀려 먹고 있다는 것도 알고 있었기에, 남들에게 뒤지지 않으려고 그가 짜증을 내는 것을 보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"기사님, 제게 약속하신 은혜를 기억하시고, 그에 따라 아무리 다급한 일이라 할지라도 다른 모험에는 관여하지 않으셔야 합니다. 마음을 가라앉히세요. 신부님께서 그 무적의 팔이 갤리선 노예들을 풀어 주었다는 것을 아셨더라면, 기사님께 무례가 될 만한 말씀을 입에 올리기 전에 세 번이고 입을 다무셨을 것이며, 아니 혀를 세 번이라도 깨무셨을 거예요.\"\n\n\"진심으로 맹세하건대 그렇습니다.\" 신부가 말했다. \"콧수염이라도 한 가닥 뽑아 버렸을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"잠자코 있으리다, 부인.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내 가슴속에 일었던 천성적인 분노를 억누르고, 약속을 이행할 때까지 평화롭고 조용히 나아가리다. 다만 이 배려에 대한 보답으로 부탁드리니, 거리낌이 없으시다면 부인의 고난이 어떠한 것인지, 그리고 내가 부인을 대신하여 마땅한 보상을 요구하고 복수를 행해야 할 자들이 몇이며, 누구이며, 어떤 자들인지를 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그리하지요.\" 도로테아가 대답했다. \"비참한 일과 불운한 일들을 듣는 것이 기사님께 지루하지만 않다면요.\"\n\n\"지루할 리가 있겠습니까, 부인.\" 돈키호테가 말하자, 도로테아가 대답했다. \"그렇다면, 제 말에 귀 기울여 주세요.\" 그녀가 이렇게 말하자마자 카르데니오와 이발사가 그녀 곁으로 바짝 다가들었으니, 재치 넘치는 도로테아가 자신을 위해 어떤 이야기를 지어낼지 듣고 싶어 안달이 났던 것이다. 산초도 똑같이 다가들었으니, 그도 제 주인 못지않게 그녀에게 속아 넘어가 있었던 까닭이다. 그녀는 안장에 편안히 자리를 잡고, 헛기침과 그 밖의 사전 동작들로 생각할 틈을 벌어 두고는, 매우 발랄한 태도로 이런 식으로 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n\"우선 여러분께서 알아 두셔야 할 것은, 제 이름이……\" 여기서 그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추었으니, 신부가 지어 준 이름을 잊어버렸던 것이다. 그러나 신부가 그녀의 곤경을 알아채고 도와주러 나서서 말했다. \"부인, 공주님께서 불운한 사연을 이야기하시며 당황하고 혼란스러워하시는 것도 무리가 아닙니다. 그런 고통은 흔히 겪는 이에게서 기억을 앗아 가, 제 이름조차 떠올리지 못하게 만드는 법이지요. 지금 공주님께서도 그러하시니, 위대한 미코미콘 왕국의 정당한 상속녀이신 미코미코나 공주라 불리신다는 것을 잊으셨군요. 그러나 이 실마리를 잡으시면 이제 공주님께서는 저희에게 들려주고자 하시는 모든 것을 그 슬픈 기억 속에서 다시 떠올리실 수 있을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 처녀가 말했다. \"하지만 이제부터는 일러 주실 필요가 없을 것 같아요. 제 진짜 이야기를 무사히 항구로 끌고 들어갈 테니까요. 자, 이렇답니다. 제 아버지이신 왕께서는 현자 티나크리오라 불리셨고, 사람들이 마법이라 부르는 것에 매우 능하셔서, 그 재주로 제 어머니이신 하라미야 왕비께서 당신보다 먼저 돌아가시리라는 것과, 그 후 곧 당신께서도 이 세상을 떠나, 제가 아버지도 어머니도 없는 고아가 되리라는 것을 아셨습니다. 하지만 이 모든 것보다도 아버지를 더 슬프게 하고 괴롭힌 것은, 우리 왕국에 가까이 있는 큰 섬의 영주인 어마어마한 거인 하나가, 이름하여 찌푸린 얼굴의 판다필란도라는 자가―들리는 말로는, 그의 눈은 제대로 똑바로 박혀 있는데도 늘 사팔뜨기처럼 비뚜름하게 본다는데, 이는 악의에서 그러는 것으로, 바라보는 이들에게 두려움과 공포를 심으려는 것이랍니다―말하자면 그 거인이 제가 고아가 된 것을 알게 되면 막강한 병력으로 제 왕국을 짓밟고 모든 것을 빼앗아, 제가 몸을 의지할 작은 마을 하나조차 남겨 두지 않으리라는 것을 아버지께서 확실히 아셨다는 점이었습니다. 다만 제가 그자와 결혼할 마음만 있다면 이 모든 파멸과 불운을 피할 수 있다고 하셨지요. 그렇지만 아버지께서 보시기에, 제가 그토록 어울리지 않는 결혼에 동의하리라고는 결코 기대하지 않으셨습니다. 이 점에서 아버지는 진실 그대로를 말씀하셨으니, 저는 그 거인이든 다른 누구든, 아무리 위대하고 거대한 자라 할지라도 그와 결혼할 생각은 한 번도 품어 본 적이 없으니까요. 아버지께서는 또한 당신께서 돌아가신 뒤 판다필란도가 제 왕국을 침략하려는 것을 제가 보거든, 기다리거나 스스로를 지키려 들지 말라고 하셨습니다. 그것은 제게 파멸을 가져올 뿐이니, 선량하고 충실한 신하들의 죽음과 전멸을 피하고자 한다면 왕국을 거인에게 온전히 내주라고 하셨지요. 거인의 악마 같은 힘에 맞서 스스로를 지킬 가능성은 없을 것이기 때문입니다. 그리고 저는 즉시 몇몇 추종자를 데리고 스페인으로 떠나야 하며, 그곳에서 한 편력 기사를 만나 제 고난에서 구원을 얻으리라고 하셨습니다. 그 무렵이면 그의 명성이 온 왕국에 퍼져 있을 것이며, 제 기억이 옳다면 그는 돈 아소테, 아니면 돈 히고테라 불릴 것이라 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"'돈키호테'라 하셨을 겁니다요, 부인.\" 이때 산초가 끼어들며 말했다. \"다른 이름으로는 슬픈 몰골의 기사라고도 하지요.\"\n\n\"그거예요.\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"게다가 아버지께서는 그가 키가 크고 얼굴이 홀쭉할 것이며, 오른쪽 옆구리, 왼쪽 어깨 아래쯤에 뻣뻣한 털이 난 잿빛 점이 있을 것이라고도 하셨어요.\"\n\n이를 듣자 돈키호테가 종자에게 말했다. \"이리 오너라, 산초 내 아들아, 손을 빌려 옷 벗는 것을 좀 도와다오. 내가 그 현명한 왕께서 예언하신 기사인지 보고 싶구나.\"\n\n\"옷은 무엇 하러 벗으시려고요?\" 도로테아가 물었다.\n\n\"부인의 부친께서 말씀하신 그 점이 내게 있는지 보려는 것이오.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다.\n\n\"옷을 벗으실 필요 없습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리 등뼈 한가운데에 바로 그런 점이 있다는 걸 제가 아니까요. 그게 바로 힘센 사내의 표시지요.\"\n\n\"그것으로 충분합니다.\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"벗들 사이에 사소한 것을 너무 꼼꼼히 따져서는 안 되지요. 그것이 어깨에 있든 등뼈에 있든 별 상관없어요. 어디에 있든 점만 있으면 충분하니, 다 같은 살이니까요. 제 선량하신 아버지께서는 모든 점에서 진실을 맞히셨고, 저 역시 돈키호테 님께 제 몸을 의탁하기로 한 것은 운이 좋았던 일이지요. 이분이야말로 아버지께서 말씀하신 그분이니, 그 얼굴의 생김새가 스페인뿐 아니라 온 라만차에 떨치신 그 드넓은 명성으로 이 기사께 부여된 특징들과 들어맞으니까요. 제가 오수나에 막 상륙하자마자 그분의 위업에 관한 이야기를 어찌나 많이 들었던지, 그 즉시 제 마음이 이분이 바로 제가 찾아온 그분이라고 일러 주었답니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 어찌 오수나에 상륙하셨소, 부인?\" 돈키호테가 물었다. \"그곳은 항구가 아닌데 말이오.\"\n\n그러나 도로테아가 대답하기도 전에 신부가 앞질러 말했다. \"공주님께서 하시려던 말씀은, 말라가에 상륙하신 뒤 기사님에 관한 소문을 처음 들으신 곳이 오수나였다는 뜻입니다.\"\n\n\"그게 제가 하려던 말이에요.\" 도로테아가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 지극히 당연한 일이지요.\" 신부가 말했다. \"공주님, 계속하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"더 보탤 것은 없어요.\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"다만 돈키호테 님을 만난 것이 제게 얼마나 큰 행운이었던지, 저는 이미 제 영토 전체의 여왕이자 주인이 된 것이나 다름없다 여기고 있다는 것뿐이에요. 그분께서 예의와 아량으로, 제가 어디로 이끌든 저와 동행해 주시는 은혜를 베푸셨으니까요. 제가 그분을 이끌 곳이라고는 오직 찌푸린 얼굴의 판다필란도와 정면으로 마주하게 하여, 그를 죽이고 그가 부당하게 빼앗은 것을 제게 되찾아 주시게 하는 것뿐입니다. 이 모든 일은 만족스럽게 이루어질 것이니, 제 선량하신 아버지 현자 티나크리오께서 그리 예언하셨기 때문이지요. 아버지께서는 또한 칼데아 문자인지 그리스 문자인지로(저는 읽을 줄 모르니까요) 글로 남겨 선언하시기를, 이 예언된 기사가 거인의 목을 벤 후에 저와 결혼할 마음이 있거든, 저는 망설임 없이 즉시 그분의 정당한 아내가 되겠노라 제 몸을 바치고, 제 몸과 함께 제 왕국의 소유권을 그분께 넘겨 드려야 한다고 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"이제 어떠하냐, 친구 산초야?\" 이때 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그 말을 들었느냐? 내가 그리 말하지 않았느냐? 보아라, 우리는 벌써 다스릴 왕국과 혼인할 여왕을 얻지 않았느냐!\"\n\n\"맹세코 그렇습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"판다힐라도 나리의 숨통을 끊어 놓고도 결혼하지 않는다면 그런 자한테는 빌어먹을 불운이 따라야지요! 게다가 저 여왕님이 얼마나 박색이신지! 제 침대의 벼룩들도 저런 종류라면 좋겠네요!\" 그러고는 더할 나위 없이 만족스러운 기색으로 공중에 두어 번 펄쩍 뛰어오르더니, 도로테아의 노새 고삐를 붙잡으러 달려가, 노새를 멈추어 세우고는 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 그녀를 제 여왕이자 주인으로 인정한다는 표시로 입 맞출 손을 달라고 청했다. 주인의 광기와 하인의 순박함을 보고 그 자리에 있던 누군들 웃음을 참을 수 있었으랴? 그리하여 도로테아는 손을 내밀고, 하늘이 은혜를 베풀어 자신이 왕국을 되찾고 누리게 해 주신다면 산초를 자기 왕국의 대귀족으로 만들어 주겠다고 약속했으며, 산초는 좌중을 또다시 웃음 짓게 만드는 말로 감사를 표했다.\n\n\"여러분, 이것이.\" 도로테아가 말을 이었다. \"제 이야기예요. 이제 한 가지만 말씀드리면 됩니다. 제 왕국에서 데리고 온 시종들 가운데, 이 수염 멋진 종자 한 사람을 빼고는 아무도 남지 않았다는 거예요. 항구가 눈앞에 보이는 곳에서 큰 폭풍을 만나 모두 물에 빠져 죽었거든요. 그와 저는 기적이라도 일어난 듯 널빤지 두 장에 의지해 뭍에 닿았답니다. 사실 제 인생 전체가 여러분께서 짐작하셨다시피 기적이자 신비랍니다. 그러니 제가 어느 대목에서 너무 자질구레했거나 마땅히 정확해야 할 만큼 정확하지 못했다 하더라도, 신부님께서 제 이야기 처음에 하셨던 말씀, 곧 끊임없고 지나친 고난은 겪는 이에게서 기억을 앗아 간다는 그 말씀으로 헤아려 주시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"고귀하고 존귀하신 공주여, 그 고난들이 제게서 기억을 앗아 가지는 못할 것입니다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"공주를 섬기며 제가 견뎌야 할 고난이 아무리 크고 유례없는 것일지라도 말이지요. 여기서 저는 공주께 약속드린 은혜를 새로이 확약하며, 공주의 사나운 적과 마주할 때까지 세상 끝까지 공주와 함께 가리라 맹세합니다. 이 팔의 도움으로 그 오만한 목을 이―좋은 검이라고는 말하지 않겠습니다, 제 검을 빼앗아 간 히네스 데 파사몬테 덕분에 말이지요―이 검날로 잘라 내기를 바라마지않습니다.\" (이 말은 이를 악물고 했고, 이어서 말을 계속했다.) \"그리고 그 목을 베어 공주께서 평화로이 왕국을 차지하게 되신 뒤에는, 공주의 몸을 가장 마음에 드시는 대로 처분하시는 일은 공주 자신의 결정에 맡기겠습니다. 제 기억이 사로잡혀 있고, 제 의지가 노예가 되어 있으며, 제 이성이 그분에게―더는 말하지 않겠습니다―얽매여 있는 한, 설령 불사조라 할지라도 제가 한순간이나마 혼인을 생각한다는 것은 불가능하기 때문입니다.\"\n\n혼인할 마음이 없다는 주인의 마지막 말이 산초에게는 어찌나 못마땅했던지, 그는 목소리를 높여 잔뜩 짜증을 내며 외쳤다.\n\n\"맹세코 말씀드리는데, 돈키호테 나리, 나리는 제정신이 아니십니다요. 어찌 이렇게 고귀하신 공주님과 혼인하기를 마다하실 수가 있단 말입니까요? 운명이 지금 나리께 내놓는 것 같은 이런 행운을 돌멩이마다 그 뒤에 마련해 두고 있을 거라 여기십니까요? 둘시네아 마님이 혹 더 어여쁘기라도 합니까요? 천만에요. 절반도 안 어여쁘십니다요. 아니, 더 나아가 그분은 여기 이분의 신발에도 못 미친다고까지 말씀드리겠습니다요. 나리께서 바다 밑바닥에서 별미를 찾으신다면, 제가 기다리고 있는 그 백작령을 얻을 가망은 영 글렀습니다요. 빌어먹을, 결혼하세요, 결혼하시라고요, 아무 수고도 없이 굴러 들어온 이 왕국을 차지하세요. 그래서 왕이 되시거든 저를 후작이나 어느 지방의 총독으로 삼아 주시고, 나머지는 죄다 악마더러 가져가라 하시면 됩니다요.\"\n\n돈키호테는 자기 마님 둘시네아에게 그런 모독이 퍼부어지는 것을 듣고 도저히 참을 수가 없어, 산초에게 아무 말도 한마디 내뱉지 않고 창을 들어 어찌나 호되게 두 번을 후려쳤던지 그를 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨리고 말았다. 만일 도로테아가 그에게 산초를 살려 달라고 외치지 않았더라면, 의심할 바 없이 그 자리에서 산초의 목숨을 앗았을 것이다.\n\n\"이 비열한 촌놈아.\" 돈키호테가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 그에게 말했다. \"너는 늘 나를 가로막고, 늘 나를 거스르며, 나는 늘 너를 용서해야 한다고 여기느냐? 그런 생각일랑 말아라, 이 불경한 악당아. 너는 의심할 여지 없이 그런 놈이니, 견줄 데 없는 둘시네아를 두고 혀를 놀려 댔으니 말이다. 이 무지렁이, 부랑자, 거지 같은 놈아, 그분이 내 팔에 불어넣어 주시는 힘이 아니었다면 내게는 벼룩 한 마리 죽일 힘도 없으리라는 것을 모른단 말이냐? 말해 보아라, 독사 같은 혀를 놀리는 조롱꾼아, 이 왕국을 손에 넣고 이 거인의 목을 베어 너를 후작으로 만든 것이(이 모든 것을 나는 이미 이루어지고 결정된 일로 친다) 그분의 위업을 이루는 도구로 내 팔을 부리시는 둘시네아의 힘이 아니고 무엇이라 여기느냐? 그분이 내 안에서 싸우고 내 안에서 정복하시며, 나는 그분 안에서 살고 숨 쉬며, 내 목숨과 존재를 그분께 빚지고 있느니라. 오, 매춘부의 자식 같은 악당아, 너는 어찌 이리 배은망덕하단 말이냐. 너는 흙먼지에서 일으켜져 작위 있는 영주가 되었으면서, 그토록 큰 은혜에 대한 보답이라는 것이 그 은혜를 베푸신 분을 험담하는 것이라니!\"\n\n산초는 그렇게까지 정신을 잃은 것은 아니어서 주인이 한 말을 죄다 알아들었고, 제법 날쌔게 일어나 도로테아의 작은 말 뒤로 달려가 몸을 숨기더니, 그 자리에서 주인에게 말했다.\n\n\"말씀해 보세요, 나리. 나리께서 이 위대하신 공주님과 혼인하지 않기로 결심하셨다면, 왕국이 나리 것이 되지 못할 게 뻔합니다요. 그리되지 못한다면, 어떻게 제게 은혜를 베풀어 주실 수 있겠습니까요? 제가 한탄하는 게 바로 그겁니다요. 어쨌거나 하늘에서 쏟아져 내리기라도 한 듯 이렇게 여기 모셔 온 이 여왕님과 혼인이나 하시고, 그런 다음에 둘시네아 마님께 돌아가시면 되지 않습니까요. 세상에는 첩을 둔 왕들도 분명 있었으니까요. 미모로 말하자면 저는 상관할 바 아니고, 솔직히 말해 저는 두 분 다 마음에 듭니다요. 둘시네아 마님은 한 번도 뵌 적이 없지만요.\"\n\n\"뭐라! 한 번도 본 적이 없다고, 이 신성 모독을 일삼는 배신자 놈아!\" 돈키호테가 외쳤다. \"방금 그분에게서 받은 전갈을 내게 가져오지 않았느냐?\"\n\n\"제 말씀은.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그분의 미모를 일일이 눈여겨보거나 그 매력을 하나하나 살필 만큼 한가로이 뵙지는 못했다는 겁니다요. 하지만 통째로 보아서는 그분이 마음에 든단 말씀입니다요.\"\n\n\"이제 너를 용서하마.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그리고 너도 내가 네게 입힌 상처를 용서해 다오. 우리의 첫 충동이란 우리 마음대로 다스릴 수 있는 것이 아니니라.\"\n\n\"그건 저도 알겠습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"저로 말씀드리면 말하고 싶은 마음이 늘 첫 충동이라서, 적어도 한 번은 혀끝에 걸린 것을 내뱉지 않고서는 못 배긴답니다요.\"\n\n\"그렇다 하더라도, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 하는 말을 조심하여라, 물동이가 우물에 너무 자주 가다 보면―더는 네게 말하지 않으마.\"\n\n\"네, 네.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하느님께서 하늘에 계시며 온갖 꼼수를 다 보고 계시니, 누가 더 해를 끼치는지를 판단해 주실 겁니다요. 말을 바르게 못 하는 저인지, 아니면 그걸 행동으로 옮기지 못하는 나리인지를요.\"\n\n\"그것으로 됐어요.\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"어서요, 산초, 달려가서 주인님의 손에 입 맞추고 용서를 비세요. 그리고 앞으로는 칭찬을 하든 욕을 하든 더 신중하시고, 제가 그저 그분의 종임을 알 뿐 그 밖에는 아무것도 모르는 그 토보소의 마님을 깎아내리는 말은 한마디도 하지 마세요. 그리고 하느님을 믿으세요. 산초는 왕자처럼 살 만한 어떤 높은 자리든 틀림없이 얻게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n산초는 고개를 떨군 채 다가가 주인의 손을 청했고, 돈키호테는 위엄 있게 그것을 내밀어 산초가 입을 맞추자마자 축복을 내려 주었다. 그러고는 산초에게 물어볼 것과 함께 의논할 매우 중대한 일이 있으니 조금 앞서 가라고 일렀다. 산초가 그 말을 따랐고, 두 사람이 어느 정도 앞서 나아가자 돈키호테가 그에게 말했다. \"네가 돌아온 뒤로 네 임무며 네가 가져온 답에 관해 여러 가지를 자세히 물어볼 겨를도 틈도 없었구나. 이제 운이 우리에게 시간과 기회를 베풀어 주었으니, 그런 좋은 소식으로 네가 줄 수 있는 행복을 내게 마다하지 말아라.\"\n\n\"무엇이든 물어보십시오, 나리.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"들어간 길을 찾았듯이 나오는 길도 다 찾아낼 테니까요. 다만 간청드리건대, 나리, 앞으로는 그렇게 복수심에 불타지 말아 주십시오.\"\n\n\"어찌하여 그런 말을 하느냐, 산초야?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 그리 말씀드리는 까닭은요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"방금 그 매질이, 제가 둘시네아 마님께 거슬리는 말을 한 탓이라기보다는, 지난밤 악마가 우리 둘 사이에 일으킨 다툼 때문이었기 때문입니다요. 그분으로 말씀드리면, 저는 그분을 성물처럼 사랑하고 받들고 있습니다요―그분께 그런 구석이 있어서가 아니라 그저 나리께 속한 무언가라서요.\"\n\n\"그 이야기는 목숨을 걸고 더 꺼내지 말아라, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내게는 거슬리는 일이니라. 그 일에 대해서는 이미 너를 용서했고, 너도 '새로운 죄에는 새로운 속죄'라는 흔한 말을 알고 있지 않느냐.\"\n\n이런 이야기가 오가는 동안, 그들은 자기들이 가던 길을 따라 당나귀를 탄 사내 하나가 다가오는 것을 보았는데, 가까이 다가오자 그는 집시처럼 보였다. 그러나 당나귀를 보면 어디서든 눈과 마음이 그쪽으로 쏠리는 산초 판사는, 그 사내를 보자마자 그가 히네스 데 파사몬테임을 알아보았다. 그리고 집시라는 실마리를 따라 실타래를 풀어 가니, 그것은 곧 자기 당나귀였다. 사실 파사몬테를 태우고 있던 것이 바로 잿빛이었던 것이다. 파사몬테는 정체가 드러나는 것을 피하고 당나귀를 팔아 치우려고 집시로 변장하고 있었으니, 집시 말을 비롯해 여러 언어를 마치 제 모국어처럼 구사할 수 있었던 것이다. 산초는 그를 보고 알아보았고, 알아보자마자 그에게 소리쳤다. \"히네시요, 이 도둑놈아, 내 보물을 내놓아라, 내 목숨을 풀어 다오, 내 안식을 가지고 성가시게 굴지 마라, 내 당나귀를 버리고, 내 기쁨을 떠나라, 꺼져라, 이 망나니야, 썩 물러가라, 도둑놈아, 네 것이 아닌 것을 내놓아라.\"\n\n이렇게 많은 말이나 욕설을 늘어놓을 필요도 없었으니, 첫마디에 히네스가 펄쩍 뛰어내려 그만한 빠른 속도로 줄행랑을 쳐서 그들 모두에게서 벗어나 버렸다. 산초는 자기 잿빛에게 서둘러 달려가 끌어안으며 말했다. \"그동안 어떻게 지냈느냐, 내 보물아, 내 눈 같은 잿빛아, 내 동무야?\" 그러면서 마치 사람이라도 되는 양 줄곧 입 맞추고 어루만졌다. 당나귀는 잠자코, 산초가 입 맞추고 어루만지는 것을 한마디 대꾸도 없이 가만히 받아들였다. 모두 다가와 잿빛을 되찾은 것을 축하해 주었고, 특히 돈키호테는 그럼에도 새끼 당나귀 세 마리를 주겠다는 약속은 취소하지 않겠노라고 일러 주었으며, 산초는 이에 감사를 표했다.\n\n이 두 사람이 이런 식으로 이야기를 나누며 가는 동안, 신부는 도로테아에게 이야기 자체에서나 그 간결함에서나, 또 그것이 기사도 책들의 이야기와 닮은 점에서 대단한 영리함을 보여 주었다고 말했다. 그녀는 그런 책들을 읽으며 여러 번 즐긴 적이 있다고 했다. 다만 지방이나 항구의 위치를 알지 못해, 그래서 닥치는 대로 오수나에 상륙했다고 말해 버렸다는 것이었다.\n\n\"그래서 제가 알아챘지요.\" 신부가 말했다. \"그래서 서둘러 제가 했던 말을 했던 것이고, 그것으로 다 바로잡혔던 겁니다. 그런데 이 가엾은 분이 단지 그것이 자기가 읽은 책들의 터무니없는 것들과 같은 문체요 방식이라는 이유만으로, 이 모든 허구와 거짓을 얼마나 쉽사리 믿어 버리는지를 보는 것은 참 묘한 일이 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"정말 그렇지요.\" 카르데니오가 말했다. \"어찌나 보기 드물고 유례없는 일인지, 누군가 이것을 허구로 지어내고 꾸며 보려 한다 해도, 과연 이를 상상해 낼 만큼 예리한 재치가 있을지 의심스러울 정도입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그것에 관해 또 한 가지 묘한 점은.\" 신부가 말했다. \"이 훌륭한 양반이 자기 광증과 관련해서 어리석은 소리들을 하는 것을 제쳐 두면, 다른 주제를 다룰 때는 더없이 이성적인 태도로 그것들을 논할 수 있어, 그 정신이 자못 맑고 차분하다는 것을 보여 준다는 점입니다. 그러니 기사도만 건드리지 않는다면, 누구도 그를 더없이 건전한 분별력을 갖춘 사람으로밖에는 여기지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n그들이 이런 대화를 나누는 동안, 돈키호테는 산초와의 대화를 이어 가며 말했다.\n\n\"친구 판사야, 우리의 다툼일랑 용서하고 잊어버리자. 그리고 이제 노여움과 짜증일랑 떨쳐 버리고 말해 보아라. 너는 둘시네아를 어디서, 어떻게, 언제 만났느냐? 그분은 무엇을 하고 계셨느냐? 너는 그분께 무슨 말을 했느냐? 그분은 무어라 대답하셨느냐? 내 편지를 읽으실 때 표정이 어떠하셨느냐? 그것을 누가 너를 위해 베껴 주었느냐? 그 밖에 이 일에서 알고 묻고 새겨 둘 만하다 여겨지는 모든 것을 말이다. 나를 기쁘게 하려고 보태거나 거짓을 꾸미지도 말고, 그렇다고 나에게서 그 기쁨을 앗아 갈까 줄이지도 말고서.\"\n\n\"나리.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"솔직히 말씀드리면, 아무도 그 편지를 저를 위해 베껴 주지 않았습니다요. 제가 편지라곤 아예 지니고 가지 않았으니까요.\"\n\n\"네 말이 맞다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그것을 적어 두었던 수첩을 네가 떠난 지 이틀 만에 내 손에서 발견했으니, 그 일로 나는 몹시 속이 상했더랬다. 편지가 없는 것을 알면 네가 어찌할지 몰랐기 때문이지. 나는 네가 그것이 없음을 처음 알아챈 그 자리에서 돌아오리라 확신했었다.\"\n\n\"그랬을 겁니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리께서 제게 읽어 주실 때 외워 두지 않았더라면 말이지요. 그래서 그것을 어느 성당지기에게 읊어 주었더니, 그가 듣고서 어찌나 정확하게 베껴 주던지, 평생 동안 파문장이라면 숱하게 읽어 보았어도 그렇게 예쁜 편지는 한 번도 보거나 읽어 본 적이 없다고 하더군요.\"\n\n\"그래서 너는 아직도 그것을 기억하고 있느냐, 산초야?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 나리.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"다 읊고 나니 더는 쓸데가 없는 것을 보고 곧장 잊어버리기 시작했으니까요. 그래도 조금 기억나는 게 있다면, '문질러 닦는'이라는 건데, 그러니까 '지존하신 마님' 말씀이고요, 끝에 가서는 '죽도록 그대의 것인, 슬픈 몰골의 기사'라는 거지요. 그리고 이 둘 사이에는 '내 영혼'이며 '내 생명'이며 '내 눈'이라는 말을 삼백 개도 넘게 집어넣었더랬습니다요.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI.\n\n“그 모든 것이 내게 불만스럽지는 않구나.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “계속하거라. 그래 네가 그분께 이르렀으렷다. 그래 그 미의 여왕께서는 무엇을 하고 계셨더냐? 분명 진주를 꿰고 계시거나, 이 사로잡힌 기사를 위해 금실로 무슨 문양을 수놓고 계셨을 테지.”\n\n“아니었습니다요.” 산초가 말했다. “자기 집 마당에서 밀 두 부셸을 까부르고 계시던데요.”\n\n“그렇다면 틀림없으니라.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “그 밀알들은 그분의 손길이 닿는 순간 진주가 되었을 게야. 한데 잘 보았느냐, 이 사람아? 흰 밀이더냐, 갈색 밀이더냐?”\n\n“이것도 저것도 아니고, 붉은 밀이었습니다요.” 산초가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 내 약속하마.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “그분의 손에 까불린 그 밀로 만든 빵은 의심할 바 없이 더없이 흰 빵이었을 테지. 한데 계속하거라. 네가 그분께 내 편지를 드렸을 때, 그분께서 입을 맞추셨더냐? 머리 위에 얹으셨더냐? 그에 걸맞은 어떤 예를 갖추셨더냐, 아니면 어떻게 하셨더냐?”\n\n“편지를 드리려 갔을 때요,” 산초가 대꾸했다. “체에 담긴 밀을 잔뜩 이쪽저쪽으로 흔드시느라 한창 바쁘셨는데, 제게 이러시더라고요. ‘이 사람아, 편지는 저 자루 위에 얹어 두구려. 이걸 다 까부르기 전에는 못 읽으니까.’”\n\n“현명한 분이로다!” 돈키호테가 말했다. “그건 한가할 때 천천히 읽고 음미하시려는 뜻이었느니라. 계속하거라, 산초. 그분께서 일에 매여 계시는 동안 너와 무슨 이야기를 나누셨더냐? 나에 관해 무엇을 물으셨고, 너는 무슨 대답을 했느냐? 어서, 모두 말해 보거라. 잉크병 속에 티끌 하나도 남기지 말고.”\n\n“제게 아무것도 안 물으셨습니다요.” 산초가 말했다. “하지만 제가 말씀드렸지요. 나리께서 그분을 섬기느라 고행을 하고 계시는데, 허리 위로는 벌거벗은 채 이 산속에서 야만인처럼 땅바닥에서 주무시고, 식탁보에 빵을 놓고 잡숫지도 않으시고, 수염도 빗지 않으시고, 울며 당신 운명을 저주하고 계신다고요.”\n\n“내가 운명을 저주한다 말한 것은 잘못 말한 게야.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “오히려 나는 그것을 축복하며 평생 동안 축복할 것이니, 엘 토보소의 둘시네아처럼 그토록 고귀한 귀부인을 사랑하려는 열망을 품을 자격을 내게 주었기 때문이니라.”\n\n“그래서 그분이 어찌나 고귀하신지요,” 산초가 말했다. “저보다 한 뼘도 더 크시더라니까요.”\n\n“뭐라! 산초,” 돈키호테가 말했다. “네가 그분과 키를 재 보았더란 말이냐?”\n\n“이렇게 재 봤습니다요.” 산초가 말했다. “밀 자루를 당나귀 등에 얹는 걸 도와드리려고 바짝 다가갔는데, 그분이 저보다 족히 한 뼘은 더 크신 게 보이더라고요.”\n\n“과연!” 돈키호테가 말했다. “그분이 정녕 그 위대함에 더하여 헤아릴 수 없는 천만 가지 마음의 매력으로 그것을 꾸미고 계시지 않더냐! 한데 한 가지는 네가 부인하지 못하리라, 산초. 그분께 바짝 다가갔을 때 사바의 향내, 방향(芳香)의 향기, 뭐라 이름 붙일 수 없는, 그 무어라 형언할 수 없이 감미로운 것을 느끼지 못했느냐? 그러니까 어느 고운 장갑 가게에 들어선 듯한 그런 그윽한 내음, 그런 향취 말이다.”\n\n“제가 드릴 수 있는 말씀이라곤요,” 산초가 말했다. “약간 냄새를 느끼긴 했는데, 좀 염소 냄새 같은 거였습니다요. 아마 그분이 고된 일로 온통 땀에 젖으셔서 그랬겠지요.”\n\n“그럴 리 없느니라.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “네가 코감기에 걸렸거나, 아니면 네 몸 냄새를 맡았던 게 분명하다. 가시덤불 속의 그 장미, 들판의 그 백합, 녹아내린 그 호박(琥珀)의 향기가 어떠한지는 내가 잘 아니 말이다.”\n\n“그럴지도 모르지요.” 산초가 대꾸했다. “그때 둘시네아 마님 마님께 나는 줄 알았던 그 냄새가 제 몸에서 자주 나곤 하니까요. 하지만 놀랄 일도 아니지요. 마귀나 마귀나 다 거기서 거기니까요.”\n\n“그래 그러면,” 돈키호테가 말을 이었다. “이제 그분께서 밀을 다 까부르시고 방앗간으로 보내셨겠다. 편지를 읽으시고는 무엇을 하시더냐?”\n\n“편지로 말씀드리면요,” 산초가 말했다. “읽지 않으셨습니다요. 글도 못 읽고 쓰지도 못한다고 하시면서요. 그러는 대신 잘게 찢어 버리시던데요. 마을에 비밀이 새어 나갈까 봐 아무한테도 못 읽히겠다 하시면서, 나리께서 그분께 품으신 사랑이며 그분을 위해 하고 계시는 그 대단한 고행에 대해 제가 말로 전해 드린 걸로 충분하다고요. 그러고는 끝으로, 나리께 손에 입맞춤을 보낸다고, 그리고 편지를 쓰기보다 만나 뵙고 싶은 마음이 더 크다고 전하라 하시더군요. 그러니 이 전갈을 받으시는 즉시 이 덤불숲에서 나오셔서, 그 터무니없는 짓들을 그만두시고, 더 중대한 다른 일이 생기지 않는 한 당장 엘 토보소로 떠나시기를 간청하고 또 명하신다고요. 나리를 무척 뵙고 싶으시다면서요. 나리께서 ‘슬픈 몰골의 기사’라 불린다고 말씀드렸더니 그분이 무척 웃으셨습니다요. 저는 며칠 전 그 비스카야인이 거기 왔었는지 여쭤봤는데, 왔었다고, 정직한 친구더라고 하시더군요. 갤리선 노예들에 대해서도 여쭤봤는데, 아직 아무도 못 봤다고 하셨습니다요.”\n\n“여기까지는 모든 게 잘되었구나.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “한데 말해 보거라. 작별할 때, 나에 관한 소식을 전한 대가로 그분께서 네게 무슨 보석을 주시더냐? 기사와 귀부인을 섬기는 종자나 시녀나 난쟁이가 귀부인의 소식을 기사에게, 또는 기사의 소식을 귀부인에게 전하면, 좋은 소식에 대한 보답이자 전갈을 받았다는 표시로 값진 보석을 주는 것이 편력 기사와 귀부인들의 오래되고도 통상적인 관례이니라.”\n\n“그럴 법한 일이지요.” 산초가 말했다. “제 생각에도 좋은 관례였고요. 한데 그건 옛날 옛적 일이었나 봅니다요. 지금은 빵과 치즈 한 조각을 주는 게 관례인 모양이니까요. 제가 작별할 때 둘시네아 마님이 마당 담장 너머로 제게 주신 게 그거였거든요. 게다가 더 말씀드리자면 양젖 치즈였고요.”\n\n“그분은 더없이 너그러우시니,” 돈키호테가 말했다. “네게 황금 보석을 주지 않으셨다면, 그건 분명 그 자리에 줄 만한 것이 손에 없으셨기 때문일 게다. 하나 부활절 뒤에 받는 소매도 좋은 법이니, 내가 그분을 뵈면 모든 게 바로잡힐 것이니라. 한데 산초, 무엇이 나를 놀라게 하는지 아느냐? 네가 공중을 가로질러 오가고 온 게 틀림없어 보인다. 여기서 엘 토보소까지 삼십 레과가 넘는데도, 다녀오는 데 사흘 남짓밖에 안 걸렸으니 말이다. 그래서 내 친구이자 내 일을 돌봐 주는 현명한 마법사가(편력 기사라면 반드시 그런 자가 있게 마련이고 또 있어야 하니, 그렇지 않으면 나는 진정한 편력 기사가 아닐 게다), 바로 그자가 네가 알지 못하는 사이에 너를 여행하도록 도왔으리라 생각하는 쪽으로 마음이 기우는구나. 이런 현인들 가운데 더러는 자기 침대에서 자고 있는 편력 기사를 낚아채어, 그 기사가 어떻게 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지도 모르는 사이에, 잠든 곳에서 천 레과도 넘게 떨어진 곳에서 이튿날 깨어나게 한단다. 이것이 아니라면, 편력 기사들이 위기에 처한 서로를, 늘 그러듯 도와줄 수 없을 것이니라. 어느 기사가 아르메니아 산중에서 용이나 사나운 뱀이나 또 다른 기사와 싸우다가 전세가 불리해져 죽음의 문턱에 이르렀다 치자. 그런데 전혀 예기치 못한 순간에, 그를 마주하여 구름 위나 불수레 위에 또 다른 기사가, 그의 벗이, 바로 직전까지 잉글랜드에 있던 자가 나타나, 그의 편을 들어 죽음에서 그를 건져 내느니라. 그러고는 밤이면 그자가 제 거처에서 흡족하게 저녁을 들고 있는 자신을 발견하게 되는데, 그럼에도 한곳에서 다른 곳까지는 이삼천 레과나 되었을 것이니라. 이 모든 일이 그 용맹한 기사들을 돌보는 현명한 마법사들의 술책과 솜씨로 이루어지는 게야. 그러니 산초, 네가 이곳에서 엘 토보소까지 다녀오는 데 그토록 짧은 시간이 걸렸다는 것을 나는 조금도 믿기 어렵지 않구나. 내가 말했듯, 어떤 친절한 현인이 네가 알아채지 못하는 사이에 너를 공중으로 실어 날랐을 게 틀림없으니 말이다.”\n\n“그게 틀림없습니다요.” 산초가 말했다. “정말이지 로시난테가 귀에 수은을 넣은 집시 당나귀처럼 내달렸으니까요.”\n\n“수은이라!” 돈키호테가 말했다. “그렇고말고, 게다가 한 무리의 마귀들이렷다. 변덕이 동하는 대로 지치지도 않고 다니며 남도 다니게 만드는 족속들 말이다. 한데 이 일은 접어 두고, 그분을 뵈러 오라는 내 귀부인의 명에 대해 내가 어찌해야 마땅하다고 보느냐? 그분의 명령에 따라야 마땅하다고 느끼면서도, 우리와 함께하는 공주에게 베푼 은혜로 인해 막혀 있음 또한 느끼는구나. 기사도의 법은 내가 내 마음보다 내 약속을 더 중히 여기도록 강요하느니라. 한편으로는 내 귀부인을 뵙고 싶은 열망이 나를 쫓고 들볶으며, 다른 한편으로는 내 엄숙한 약속과 이 위업에서 얻을 영광이 나를 재촉하고 부른다. 하나 내가 하려는 바는 온 힘을 다해 서둘러 이 거인이 있는 곳에 빨리 다다라, 도착하는 즉시 그놈의 목을 베고 공주를 그 왕국에 평화로이 앉히는 것이며, 그런 뒤 곧장 돌아와 내 감각을 밝혀 주는 그 빛을 다시 우러르는 것이니라. 그분께는 이러한 변명을 드릴 것인즉, 그분께서 내 지체를 흔쾌히 받아들이시게 될 게다. 그것이 오롯이 그분의 영광과 명성을 드높이는 데로 향한다는 것을 그분께서 아실 테니 말이다. 내가 이 생에서 무기로 얻었고, 얻고 있고, 또 얻을 모든 것은 그분께서 내게 베푸시는 은총에서 비롯되며, 내가 그분의 것이기 때문이니라.”\n\n“아이고, 나리의 머릿속이 어찌 이리 딱한 지경이람!” 산초가 말했다. “말씀해 보세요, 나리. 그 먼 길을 헛걸음하시면서, 이렇게 부유하고 대단한 혼처를 놓치고 잃어버리실 작정이세요? 결혼 지참금으로 한 왕국을 준다잖아요. 제가 듣기로 솔직히 그 둘레가 이만 레과가 넘는다는데, 인간이 살아가는 데 필요한 온갖 것이 넘쳐 나고, 포르투갈과 카스티야를 합친 것보다도 크다는데요. 제발 그만하세요, 하느님 사랑으로요! 하신 말씀을 부끄러워하시고, 제 충고를 받아들이세요. 그리고 저를 용서하시고, 사제가 있는 첫 마을에서 당장 결혼하세요. 그게 아니면 여기 우리 학사 나리가 그 일을 멋들어지게 해 주실 겁니다요. 명심하세요, 저도 충고할 만큼 나이를 먹었고, 지금 드리는 이 충고는 딱 들어맞는 거랍니다요. 날아가는 독수리보다 손안의 참새가 나은 법이고, 좋은 것을 손에 쥐고도 나쁜 것을 고르는 자는, 그 한탄하는 좋은 것이 제게 오지 않을 테니까요.”\n\n“이보게, 산초,” 돈키호테가 말했다. “네가 내게 결혼하라고 권하는 것이, 거인을 죽이는 즉시 내가 왕이 되어 네게 은혜를 베풀고 약속한 것을 줄 수 있게 하려는 뜻이라면, 내 말하노니, 결혼하지 않고도 네 소원을 아주 쉽게 들어줄 수 있느니라. 싸움에 나서기 전에 한 가지 조건을 걸어 둘 터인즉, 만일 내가 승리하여 거기서 나온다면, 설령 내가 결혼하지 않더라도 왕국의 한 부분을 내게 떼어 주어 내가 원하는 누구에게든 그것을 줄 수 있게 하라는 것이니라. 그리고 그것을 내게 주면 내가 너 말고 누구에게 주겠느냐?”\n\n“그거 솔직한 말씀이네요.” 산초가 말했다. “한데 나리, 부디 바닷가에 있는 걸로 골라 주세요. 그래야 그 삶이 마음에 안 들면 제 검은 신하들을 배에 실어, 제가 말씀드린 대로 처분할 수 있을 테니까요. 둘시네아 마님은 지금 뵈러 가실 것 없이, 가셔서 이 거인을 죽이고 이 일을 끝장내시지요. 맹세코 제 생각엔 이게 큰 명예와 큰 이득이 될 일 같으니까요.”\n\n“네 말이 옳다고 본다, 산초.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “둘시네아를 뵈러 가기 전에 공주와 동행하라는 네 충고를 따르마. 하나 우리가 헤아리고 의논한 바에 대해서는 아무에게도, 우리와 함께 있는 이들에게도 한마디 하지 말라고 일러 두겠노라. 둘시네아는 워낙 정숙하셔서 자기 생각이 알려지길 원치 않으시니, 나든 나를 대신한 누구든 그것을 드러내는 것은 옳지 않으니라.”\n\n“그래 그게 그렇다면요,” 산초가 말했다. “나리께서 그 팔로 무찌른 자들을 모조리 둘시네아 마님 앞에 나가 자기를 바치라고 하시는 건 어찌 된 일입니까요? 그건 나리가 그분을 사랑하며 그분의 연인이라고 거기다 서명하시는 것과 똑같은 일 아니겠습니까요? 그리고 가야 하는 자들이 별수 없이 그분 앞에 무릎 꿇고 나리께서 보내셔서 복종하러 왔다고 말해야 하는데, 어떻게 두 분의 생각이 감춰지겠습니까요?”\n\n“오, 너는 어찌 이리 어리석고 단순하냐!” 돈키호테가 말했다. “산초, 그것이 그분을 더욱 드높이는 일임을 모르겠느냐? 기사도에서 우리가 생각하는 방식에 따르면, 한 귀부인에게 여러 편력 기사가 봉사하는 것은 그분께 더없는 영예가 되는 일이니라. 그 기사들의 생각이 오로지 그분 자체를 위해 그분을 섬기는 데서 한 걸음도 벗어나지 않으며, 그 위대하고 진실한 헌신에 대한 다른 어떤 보답도 바라지 않고, 다만 그분께서 그들을 당신의 기사로 받아들여 주시기만을 바라기 때문이니라.”\n\n“바로 그런 사랑으로요,” 산초가 말했다. “설교자들이 우리가 주님을 사랑해야 한다고 말씀하시는 걸 들었습죠. 영광의 희망이나 벌의 두려움에 흔들리지 말고, 오직 그분 자체를 위해서요. 하긴 제 경우엔, 그분이 해 주실 수 있는 것 때문에 그분을 사랑하고 섬기는 편이 낫겠습니다만요.”\n\n“이 촌뜨기 같은 놈, 마귀나 잡아가라!” 돈키호테가 말했다. “한데 때로 어쩌면 그리 영리한 말을 하느냐! 네가 공부깨나 한 줄 알겠구나.”\n\n“참말로요, 저는 글도 못 읽는걸요.”\n\n이때 니콜라스 선생이 그들에게 잠깐 멈추라고 외쳤다. 거기 있는 작은 샘에서 멈춰 물을 마시려 했기 때문이다. 돈키호테가 말을 세우니 산초로서는 적잖이 흡족했는데, 이즈음 그는 그토록 많은 거짓말을 늘어놓느라 지친 데다, 주인이 자신이 헛디딘 것을 잡아챌까 봐 겁이 났던 것이다. 둘시네아가 엘 토보소의 농사꾼 처녀라는 것은 알았으나, 평생 한 번도 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 카르데니오는 어느덧 그들이 도로테아를 발견했을 때 그녀가 입고 있던 옷으로 갈아입었는데, 그리 좋은 옷은 아니었으나 그가 벗어 던진 것보다는 훨씬 나았다. 그들은 다 함께 샘가에 내려, 사제가 주막에서 챙겨 온 것으로, 비록 넉넉히는 아니었으나, 다들 가져온 왕성한 식욕을 달랬다.\n\n그들이 그러고 있는 동안, 마침 한 젊은이가 길을 가다 지나치게 되었는데, 샘가의 일행을 살피려고 멈춰 섰다가, 다음 순간 돈키호테에게 달려와 그의 다리를 끌어안고는 거침없이 울기 시작하며 말했다. “오, 나리, 저를 모르시겠어요? 잘 보세요. 저는 나리께서 묶여 있던 그 떡갈나무에서 풀어 주신 그 안드레스라는 아이입니다요.”\n\n돈키호테는 그를 알아보고, 그의 손을 잡고서 좌중을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. “이 세상에서 포악하고 사악한 자들이 저지르는 부당함과 해악을 바로잡는 데 편력 기사를 두는 것이 얼마나 중요한지를 여러분이 알 수 있도록 말씀드리겠소이다. 며칠 전 어느 숲을 지나는데, 고통과 곤경에 처한 사람의 것인 듯한 비명과 애처로운 하소연이 들려왔소. 나는 즉시 마땅한 의무에 떠밀려, 그 애처로운 소리가 들려오는 듯한 쪽으로 서둘러 갔더니, 지금 여러분 앞에 서 있는 이 아이가 떡갈나무에 묶여 있는 것을 발견했소이다. 마음속으로 기쁘기 그지없으니, 이 아이의 증언이 내가 어떤 점에서도 진실에서 벗어나지 못하게 할 것이기 때문이오. 말하건대, 이 아이는 허리 위로 벗은 채 떡갈나무에 묶여 있었고, 나중에 그 주인임을 알게 된 한 촌뜨기가 암말 고삐로 채찍질을 하며 살갗이 벗겨지도록 매질하고 있었소. 그를 보자마자 나는 어찌하여 그토록 잔혹하게 매질하는지 까닭을 물었소. 그 무지렁이는, 이 아이가 자기 하인이고, 어리석음보다는 부정직함에서 비롯된 부주의 때문에 매질한다고 대답했소. 그러자 이 소년이 ‘나리, 제가 품삯을 달라고 했다는 이유만으로 매질하는 겁니다요’라고 했소이다. 그 주인은 무어라 변명인지 해명인지를 늘어놓았으나, 나는 그것을 듣기는 했어도 받아들이지 않았소. 요컨대 나는 그 촌뜨기에게 아이를 풀어 주게 하고, 그를 데려가 한 닢 한 닢 품삯을 치르되 향수까지 곁들여 주겠다고 맹세하게 했소. 그렇지 아니하냐, 안드레스 내 아들아? 내가 얼마나 위엄 있게 그에게 명했고, 그가 얼마나 공손하게 내가 명하고 지시하고 요구한 모든 것을 하겠다고 약속했는지 보지 않았느냐? 망설이지 말고 대답하거라. 이 신사분들께 무슨 일이 있었는지 말씀드려라. 편력 기사가 세상에 나도는 것이 내가 말한 만큼이나 큰 이득임을 그분들이 알 수 있도록 말이다.”\n\n“나리께서 말씀하신 모든 게 다 사실입니다요.” 소년이 대답했다. “하지만 그 일의 끝은 나리께서 생각하시는 것과 정반대로 되어 버렸답니다요.”\n\n“뭐라! 정반대라고?” 돈키호테가 말했다. “그러면 그 촌뜨기가 네게 품삯을 치르지 않았더란 말이냐?”\n\n“품삯을 치르지 않았을 뿐 아니라요,” 소년이 대답했다. “나리께서 숲을 빠져나가시고 우리 둘만 남게 되자마자, 저를 다시 그 똑같은 떡갈나무에 묶어 놓고는 새로 매질을 해서, 살가죽이 벗겨진 성 바르톨로메 꼴로 만들어 버렸습니다요. 한 대 칠 때마다 나리를 골탕 먹였다고 농지거리며 비웃음을 곁들였는데, 제가 겪던 고통만 아니었어도 그가 하는 말에 웃음이 터질 뻔했지요. 요컨대 그가 저를 그런 꼴로 만들어 놓는 바람에, 그 못된 촌뜨기가 그때 입힌 상처를 치료하느라 저는 지금까지 병원 신세를 졌습니다요. 이 모든 게 나리 탓이에요. 나리께서 가시던 길이나 가시고, 부르지도 않은 곳에 오시지 않고 남의 일에 끼어들지만 않으셨어도, 제 주인은 매 한두 다스 치는 걸로 만족하고는, 저를 풀어 주고 빚진 품삯을 치렀을 거예요. 그런데 나리께서 그를 그토록 도를 넘어 모욕하시고 그토록 심한 말을 많이 퍼부으시니, 그의 화가 불붙은 거지요. 나리한테는 앙갚음을 할 수 없으니, 나리께서 떠나신 걸 보자마자 그 폭풍이 제게 몰아쳤고, 그래서 저는 영영 사내구실을 못하게 될 것만 같습니다요.”\n\n“화근은,” 돈키호테가 말했다. “내가 떠나 버린 데 있었구나. 네가 품삯 받는 것을 보기 전에는 떠나지 말았어야 했어. 오랜 경험으로 미루어, 약속을 지키는 게 제게 이롭지 않다고 여기면 약속을 지킬 촌뜨기란 하나도 없다는 걸 진작 잘 알았어야 했는데 말이다. 하나 안드레스, 그가 네게 품삯을 치르지 않으면 내가 그를 찾아 나서서, 설령 그가 고래 뱃속에 숨더라도 기어이 찾아내겠다고 맹세한 것을 너도 기억할 게다.”\n\n“그건 사실이에요.” 안드레스가 말했다. “하지만 아무 소용없었지요.”\n\n“그게 소용이 있는지 없는지 이제 보게 되리라.” 돈키호테가 이렇게 말하고는 황급히 일어나, 그들이 먹는 동안 풀을 뜯고 있던 로시난테에게 굴레를 씌우라고 산초에게 일렀다. 도로테아가 그에게 무엇을 하려는 것이냐고 물었다. 그는 그 촌뜨기를 찾아 나서서 그토록 사악한 짓을 한 것을 벌하고, 세상의 모든 촌뜨기가 반대하고 가로막더라도 안드레스가 마지막 한 푼까지 품삯을 받는 것을 지켜보려는 것이라고 대답했다. 이에 그녀는, 그가 한 약속에 따라 자신의 일을 마치기 전에는 어떤 위업에도 나설 수 없음을 명심해야 하며, 그가 이를 누구보다 잘 아는 만큼, 자기 왕국에서 돌아올 때까지 그 열정을 억눌러야 한다고 대꾸했다.\n\n“그건 사실이오.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “하여 안드레스는, 귀부인께서 말씀하신 대로 내가 돌아올 때까지 인내해야 한다. 하나 나는 다시 한번 맹세하고 약속하노니, 그가 원수를 갚고 품삯을 받는 것을 보기까지는 멈추지 않을 것이니라.”\n\n“저는 그런 맹세들 안 믿어요.” 안드레스가 말했다. “세상의 그 모든 복수보다, 지금 세비야까지 갈 수 있게 도와줄 무언가가 차라리 더 낫겠어요. 여기 제가 가져갈 만한 먹을 게 있으면 주세요. 그리고 나리와 모든 편력 기사들에게 하느님이 함께하시기를요. 그들의 일이 제게 그랬던 만큼이나 그들 자신에게도 그렇게 잘 풀리기를 바랍니다요.”\n\n산초가 자기 짐에서 빵 한 조각과 치즈 한 조각을 꺼내 소년에게 주며 말했다. “자, 이걸 받게, 안드레스 형제. 우리 모두가 자네의 불행에 몫을 나눠 가졌으니 말이야.”\n\n“아니, 무슨 몫을 가졌다는 거예요?”\n\n“내가 자네한테 주는 이 빵과 치즈 몫이지.” 산초가 대답했다. “이게 나중에 나한테 아쉬울지 어떨지는 하느님만 아실 일이고. 이보게 친구, 자네가 알아야 할 게, 우리처럼 편력 기사를 섬기는 종자들은 숱한 굶주림과 박복한 운명을, 게다가 말로 하기보다 몸으로 느끼기가 더 쉬운 다른 것들까지도 견뎌 내야 한다네.”\n\n안드레스는 빵과 치즈를 움켜쥐었고, 아무도 더 주는 것이 없음을 보자, 고개를 숙이고는, 흔히 말하듯 길을 잡아 나섰다. 그러나 떠나기 전에 그가 말했다. “하느님 사랑으로 부탁드리는데요, 편력 기사 나리, 혹시라도 다시 저를 만나시거든, 설령 사람들이 저를 갈가리 찢는 걸 보시더라도 제게 아무 도움도 구원도 베풀지 마시고, 그저 제 불행에 저를 내버려 두세요. 그래 봤자 나리께 도움받아 더 큰 불행이 제게 닥치는 것만큼 큰일은 아닐 테니까요. 나리와, 이제껏 태어난 모든 편력 기사들에게 하느님이 저주를 내리시기를요.”\n\n돈키호테가 그를 벌하려고 일어서려 했으나, 그가 어찌나 빠른 걸음으로 줄행랑을 쳤던지 아무도 뒤쫓으려 들지 않았다. 안드레스의 이야기에 돈키호테는 풀이 잔뜩 죽었고, 나머지 사람들은 그를 아주 무안하게 만들지 않으려고 웃음을 참느라 무척 애를 써야 했다.\n\n# WHICH TREATS OF WHAT BEFELL DON QUIXOTE’S PARTY AT THE INN\n\n맛깔스러운 식사를 마치고 그들은 곧장 말에 안장을 얹었고, 이렇다 할 모험 하나 없이 이튿날 산초 판사가 두려워하고 무서워하던 그 객줏집에 다다랐다. 산초는 차라리 들어가지 않았으면 했으나 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 안주인과 주인, 그들의 딸과 마리토르네스는 돈키호테와 산초가 오는 것을 보고는 진심 어린 만족의 기색을 띠며 그들을 맞으러 나왔고, 돈키호테는 이를 위엄과 무게를 갖추어 받아들이며 지난번보다 더 나은 잠자리를 마련해 달라고 일렀다. 이에 안주인은 지난번보다 셈을 더 잘 치른다면 왕자에게나 어울릴 자리를 내드리겠다고 대답했다. 돈키호테가 그러마고 하자, 그들은 전과 같은 다락방에 그런대로 쓸 만한 잠자리를 마련해 주었고, 그는 몹시 시달린 데다 잠이 절실했던지라 곧바로 드러누웠다.\n\n그의 등 뒤로 문이 닫히기가 무섭게 안주인은 이발사에게 달려들어 그의 수염을 움켜잡고 말했다.\n\n\"맹세코 더는 내 꼬리로 당신 수염 노릇을 시킬 순 없어요. 내 꼬리를 돌려줘요. 내 남편의 그 물건이 바닥에 나뒹구는 꼴이 영 창피하니 말이에요. 내가 그 멀쩡한 꼬리에 꽂아 두던 빗 말이에요.\"\n\n그러나 그녀가 아무리 잡아당겨도 이발사는 그것을 내주려 하지 않았는데, 마침내 석사가 이제는 그 술책이 더는 필요 없으니 그녀에게 돌려주라고 일렀다. 이제 그는 자기 정체를 밝히고 본래 모습으로 나서서, 그 갤리선 노예 도둑들이 자신을 털었을 때 이 객줏집으로 피신해 왔노라고 돈키호테에게 말하면 되었고, 만일 돈키호테가 공주의 종자를 찾거든, 공주가 자신이 모두의 구원자를 모시고 오고 있음을 미리 알리려고 그를 자기 나라 백성들에게 앞서 보냈다고 일러 주면 그만이었다. 이에 이발사는 흔쾌히 꼬리를 안주인에게 돌려주었고, 동시에 그들은 돈키호테를 구해 내려고 빌렸던 온갖 소품을 모두 돌려주었다. 객줏집 사람들은 모두 도로테아의 아름다움에, 그리고 양치기로 분장한 카르데니오의 준수한 모습에까지 놀라움을 금치 못했다. 신부는 객줏집에 있는 대로 음식을 차리게 했고, 주인은 셈을 후하게 받을 기대로 그런대로 괜찮은 저녁을 차려 냈다. 이러는 내내 돈키호테는 잠들어 있었고, 사람들은 그를 깨우지 않는 편이 낫다고 여겼는데, 이제는 먹는 것보다 자는 편이 그에게 더 이로울 것이었기 때문이다.\n\n저녁을 들며, 주인과 그 아내, 딸, 마리토르네스, 그리고 모든 길손이 함께한 자리에서, 그들은 돈키호테의 기이한 광기와 그가 발견된 경위를 두고 이야기를 나누었다. 안주인은 그와 짐꾼 사이에 있었던 일을 들려주었고, 이어 산초가 거기 있는지 둘러보다가 그가 없는 것을 보고는, 그가 담요로 키질을 당한 이야기를 죄다 들려주었으니, 사람들은 이를 적잖이 재미있어하며 들었다. 그러나 신부가 돈키호테가 읽은 기사도 책들이 그의 머리를 돌게 만든 것이라고 하자, 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"어찌 그럴 수가 있는지 도무지 모르겠습니다. 사실 제 생각으로는 세상에 그보다 나은 읽을거리가 없거든요. 저한테도 그런 책이 두세 권 있는데, 다른 글들과 더불어 그것들이야말로 저뿐 아니라 숱한 사람들의 그야말로 삶의 낙입니다. 추수철이 되면 일꾼들이 쉬는 날마다 여기로 몰려드는데, 그중에는 늘 글을 읽을 줄 아는 사람이 하나쯤 있어서 이런 책 한 권을 집어 들면, 우리 서른 명 넘는 사람이 그 둘레에 모여 앉아 그가 읽는 것을 듣지요. 그러면 어찌나 신이 나는지 우리 흰머리가 다시 젊어지는 것만 같습니다. 적어도 저로 말씀드리자면, 기사들이 얼마나 사납고 무시무시한 일격을 날리는지 들을 때면 저도 똑같이 해 보고 싶은 갈망에 사로잡혀, 밤이고 낮이고 그 이야기를 듣고 싶어진답니다.\"\n\n\"저도 꼭 그래요.\" 안주인이 말했다. \"이 집에서 제가 마음 편한 때라곤 당신이 누군가 읽어 주는 걸 듣고 있을 때뿐이거든요. 그럴 때면 당신이 어찌나 푹 빠져 있는지 그동안만큼은 잔소리하는 것도 잊어버리니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 맞아요.\" 마리토르네스가 말했다. \"그리고, 정말이지 저도 이런 이야기를 듣는 게 무척 좋아요. 어찌나 어여쁜지요. 특히 어느 귀부인이 오렌지나무 아래서 자기 기사의 품에 안겨 있고, 곁을 지키던 시녀는 부러움과 두려움으로 반쯤 넋이 나가 있는 대목을 그릴 때가 그래요. 그런 건 죄다 꿀처럼 달콤하다니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 아가씨는 어떻게 생각하나요?\" 신부가 주인의 딸 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"저야 정말 모르겠어요, 나리.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저도 듣긴 하는데, 솔직히 말씀드리면 잘 알아듣지는 못해도 듣는 건 좋아요. 다만 저는 아버지가 좋아하시는 그런 일격이 좋은 게 아니라, 기사들이 자기 귀부인과 헤어져서 토해 내는 그 한탄이 좋아요. 정말이지 가끔은 그들이 가엾어서 눈물이 다 난다니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 만일 그들이 아가씨를 위해 우는 거라면, 아가씨가 그들을 위로해 주겠군요?\" 도로테아가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 어떻게 할지는 모르겠어요.\" 처녀가 말했다. \"다만 그런 귀부인들 중에는 어찌나 모진 이들이 있는지, 자기 기사들을 호랑이니 사자니, 그 밖에도 온갖 험한 이름으로 부른다는 것만은 알아요. 아이고 맙소사! 그렇게 무정하고 매정해서, 의젓한 남자에게 눈길 한 번 던져 주느니 차라리 그를 죽게 내버려 두거나 미치게 만드는 그런 여자들이 대체 어떤 부류인지 저는 도무지 모르겠어요. 그런 내숭이 무슨 소용인지 모르겠어요. 그게 명예 때문이라면, 차라리 그들과 혼인하면 될 게 아니에요? 그 남자들이 바라는 건 그게 다인데 말이에요.\"\n\n\"쉿, 얘야.\" 안주인이 말했다. \"네가 이런 일들을 너무 많이 아는 것 같구나. 처녀가 그렇게 많이 알거나 떠들어 대는 건 합당치 않은 일이야.\"\n\n\"나리가 물으시니 대답하지 않을 수가 없었던 거예요.\" 처녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 어디,\" 신부가 말했다. \"그 책들을 내게 가져와 보시오, 주인 양반. 한번 보고 싶구려.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요.\" 그가 말하고는 자기 방으로 들어가 작은 사슬로 잠긴 낡은 여행 가방을 들고 나왔는데, 그것을 열어 보니 신부는 그 안에서 커다란 책 세 권과 아주 좋은 필체로 쓴 원고 몇 장을 찾아냈다. 처음 펼친 것은 「트라키아의 돈 시롱힐리오」였고, 두 번째는 「이르카니아의 돈 펠릭스마르테」였으며, 나머지 하나는 「위대한 장수 곤살로 에르난데스 데 코르도바의 일대기, 디에고 가르시아 데 파레데스의 생애를 덧붙임」이었다.\n\n신부가 앞의 두 제목을 읽고는 이발사 쪽을 건너다보며 말했다. \"지금 여기 우리 친구의 가정부와 조카딸이 있었으면 좋겠구려.\"\n\n\"아닐세.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"이것들을 마당으로든 화덕으로든 가져가는 건 나 혼자서도 충분히 할 수 있네. 마침 저기 불도 아주 잘 피워져 있고 말이야.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 나리께서 제 책을 태우시겠다고요!\" 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"이 두 권만이오.\" 신부가 말했다. \"「돈 시롱힐리오」와 「펠릭스마르테」 말이오.\"\n\n\"그럼 제 책들이 이단자나 점액질이라도 된단 말입니까, 태워 버리시게요?\" 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"분파주의자를 말하는 게지, 이 친구야.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"점액질이 아니라.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 그거예요.\" 주인이 말했다. \"하지만 굳이 태우시려거든 저 위대한 장수와 디에고 가르시아에 관한 걸 태우세요. 저는 다른 두 권 중 하나가 타느니 차라리 제 자식이 타는 걸 보는 편이 낫겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이보시오.\" 신부가 말했다. \"그 두 권은 죄다 거짓으로 지어낸 것이라 어리석음과 헛소리로 가득하오. 그러나 이 위대한 장수에 관한 것은 참된 역사로서, 코르도바의 곤살로 에르난데스의 행적을 담고 있소. 그는 그 수많은 위대한 공적으로 온 세상에서 '위대한 장수'라는 칭호를 얻었으니, 이는 명망 높고 빛나는 이름이며 오로지 그만이 받아 마땅한 것이오. 그리고 이 디에고 가르시아 데 파레데스는 에스트레마두라의 트루히요 시 출신의 이름난 기사로, 더없이 용맹한 군인이요, 그 몸의 힘이 어찌나 셌던지 한껏 돌아가는 방앗간 바퀴를 손가락 하나로 멈춰 세웠다 하오. 또한 양손검을 들고 다리 어귀에 버티고 서서 거대한 군대 전체가 그 다리를 건너지 못하게 막았으며, 그 밖에도 갖가지 위업을 이루었으니, 만일 그가 기사다운 겸손과, 제 손으로 제 역사를 쓰는 자의 겸손으로 몸소 그것을 이야기한 것이 아니라 자유롭고 공평한 어느 필자가 그것을 기록했더라면, 헥토르며 아킬레우스며 롤랑의 모든 행적을 무색하게 만들었을 것이오.\"\n\n\"그런 얘긴 우리 아버지한테나 하시지요.\" 주인이 말했다. \"참 놀라울 일도 많네요! 방앗간 바퀴를 멈춰 세웠다니! 맹세코 나리께서는 제가 읽은 「이르카니아의 펠릭스마르테」를 읽으셔야 합니다. 그는 단 한 번의 후려치기로 거인 다섯을 한복판으로 두 동강 냈는데, 마치 아이들이 콩깍지로 만드는 그 작은 수도사 인형이라도 되는 양 쪼개 버렸지요. 또 한번은 어마어마하게 크고 강력한 군대를 공격했는데, 거기엔 머리부터 발끝까지 무장한 병사가 백육십만 명도 넘게 있었건만, 그는 마치 양 떼라도 되는 양 그들을 모조리 무찔러 버렸답니다.\n\n게다가, 그 어찌나 굳세고 담대했던 착한 트라키아의 시롱힐리오는 또 어떻고요? 그 책에 다 나와 있지요. 그가 어느 강을 따라 항해하고 있는데, 물 한가운데서 불을 뿜는 뱀이 그에게 덤벼들었답니다. 그러자 그는 그것을 보자마자 그 위로 몸을 날려 비늘 덮인 어깨에 올라타고는, 두 손으로 그 목을 어찌나 세게 졸랐던지, 뱀은 자기가 목이 졸리는 걸 알고 별수 없이 강바닥으로 가라앉을 수밖에 없었는데, 그러면서 끝내 손을 놓지 않은 그 기사를 함께 끌고 내려갔지요. 그렇게 바닥에 다다르니 그는 보기에도 놀라울 만큼 아름다운 궁전과 정원들 사이에 있게 되었고, 그러자 뱀은 늙디늙은 노인으로 변해서는 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 이야기들을 그에게 들려주었답니다. 입 다물고 계시지요, 나리. 이걸 들으시면 너무 좋아서 미쳐 버리실 테니까요. 나리의 그 위대한 장수니 디에고 가르시아니 하는 것들은 무화과 두 알 값어치도 안 됩니다요!\"\n\n이 말을 듣고 도로테아가 카르데니오에게 속삭여 말했다. \"우리 주인장은 돈키호테 다음 자리를 맡아도 거의 손색이 없겠어요.\"\n\n\"제 생각도 그렇소.\" 카르데니오가 말했다. \"보다시피 저 사람은 그 책들이 이야기하는 모든 것이 적힌 그대로 정확히 일어났다고 틀림없는 사실로 받아들이고 있으니, 맨발의 수도사들이 와도 그 반대를 믿게 하지는 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 잘 생각해 보시오, 이 친구.\" 신부가 다시 한번 말했다. \"세상에 이르카니아의 펠릭스마르테 같은 자도, 트라키아의 시롱힐리오 같은 자도, 기사도 책들이 떠들어 대는 그런 부류의 다른 어떤 기사도 결코 있었던 적이 없소. 그 모든 것은 한가한 재주꾼들이 꾸며 내고 지어낸 것이니, 당신네 일꾼들이 책을 읽으며 그러듯, 바로 당신이 말한 그 목적, 곧 시간을 즐겁게 때우려는 목적으로 그들이 고안한 것이오. 진실로 맹세코 말하건대, 세상에 그런 기사는 결코 없었고, 그런 위업이나 헛소리는 어디서도 일어난 적이 없소.\"\n\n\"그런 뼈다귀는 다른 개한테나 던져 보시지요.\" 주인이 말했다. \"제가 마치 다섯이 얼마인지도, 제 신발 어디가 발을 죄는지도 모르는 줄 아십니까. 저를 미음으로 먹여 어르려 마시지요. 맹세코 저는 바보가 아닙니다. 이 훌륭한 책들이 하는 말이 죄다 헛소리요 거짓이라고 저를 설득하려 드시다니, 나리도 참 농담도 잘하시네요. 게다가 이것들은 왕실 심의회 나리들의 허가를 받아 인쇄된 책들 아닙니까. 마치 그분들이 그런 거짓말 덩어리를 한꺼번에, 사람 정신을 쏙 빼놓는 그 많은 전투와 마법들을 죄다 인쇄하도록 허락할 분들이기라도 한 것처럼 말이에요.\"\n\n\"내가 말하지 않았소, 이 친구.\" 신부가 말했다. \"이런 것은 우리의 한가한 생각을 달래 주려고 만들어진 것이라고 말이오. 잘 다스려진 나라에서 일하기 싫어하거나, 일할 의무가 없거나, 일할 수 없는 이들의 소일거리로 장기며 공놀이며 당구가 허용되듯이, 이런 부류의 책들도 인쇄가 허용되는 것이오. 누구든 그것들을 참된 이야기로 받아들일 만큼 무지한 자는 없으리라는 전제 위에서 말인데, 사실이 진정 그렇소. 그리고 만일 지금 내게 허락이 주어지고 여기 계신 분들이 원하신다면, 좋은 기사도 책이 갖추어야 할 자질에 관해 한마디 할 수도 있겠으니, 그것이 어떤 이들에게는 유익하고 또 입맛에도 맞으리다. 하지만 언젠가 이 일을 바로잡을 수 있을 만한 누군가에게 내 생각을 전할 때가 오기를 바라오. 그동안에는, 주인 양반, 내가 한 말을 믿고 당신 책들을 도로 가져가서, 그것들의 진위에 대해서는 스스로 마음을 정하시오. 부디 그 책들이 당신께 큰 보탬이 되기를 바라며, 당신 손님 돈키호테가 절뚝거리는 바로 그 발로 당신이 절름발이가 되지 않도록 하느님께서 지켜 주시기를.\"\n\n\"그런 걱정은 마십시오.\" 주인이 대답했다. \"제가 저 자신을 편력 기사로 만들 만큼 미치지는 않을 테니까요. 저 유명한 기사들이 세상을 떠돌아다녔다는 그 시절과 지금은 사정이 같지 않다는 걸 저도 잘 알고 있거든요.\"\n\n이 대화가 한창일 때 산초가 모습을 드러냈는데, 이제 편력 기사는 더 이상 유행이 아니며 기사도 책들은 죄다 어리석음이요 거짓이라는 말을 듣고는 몹시 심란해하고 풀이 죽었다. 그래서 그는 주인의 이번 여정이 어찌 되는지 두고 보기로 마음속으로 작정했고, 만일 그것이 주인이 기대하는 만큼 행복하게 풀리지 않는다면, 주인을 떠나 아내와 자식들과 본래의 생업으로 돌아가기로 결심했다.\n\n주인이 여행 가방과 책들을 들고 가려는데, 신부가 그에게 말했다. \"잠깐만 기다리시오. 그렇게 좋은 필체로 쓰인 저 종이들이 무엇인지 보고 싶구려.\" 주인이 그것들을 꺼내 읽어 보라며 그에게 건네주자, 그는 그것이 여덟 장쯤 되는 원고로, 첫머리에 큰 글씨로 「무모한 호기심에 관한 소설」이라는 제목이 붙어 있음을 알아보았다. 신부가 혼자 서너 줄을 읽고는 말했다. \"확실히 이 소설의 제목이 내게는 나빠 보이지 않는구려. 전부 읽어 보고 싶은 마음이 드는데.\" 이에 주인이 대답했다. \"그럼 나리께서 읽어 보시는 게 좋겠습니다. 여기서 그걸 읽은 몇몇 손님들이 무척 흡족해하며 그것을 제게 간곡히 졸라 댔다는 걸 말씀드릴 수 있거든요. 하지만 저는 내주지 않았는데, 여기다 여행 가방과 책들과 종이들을 두고 간 사람에게 돌려주려는 생각에서지요. 그 사람이 언젠가 다시 이리로 돌아올지도 모르니까요. 책들이 아쉽긴 하겠지만, 정말이지 저는 그걸 돌려줄 작정입니다. 제가 객줏집 주인이긴 해도 그래도 기독교인이니까요.\"\n\n\"참으로 옳은 말이오, 이 친구.\" 신부가 말했다. \"하지만 그래도 그 소설이 내 마음에 들거든 베껴 가게는 해 주셔야 하오.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요.\" 주인이 대답했다.\n\n그들이 이야기를 나누는 동안 카르데니오는 그 소설을 집어 들어 읽기 시작했고, 신부와 똑같은 의견에 이르자, 모두가 함께 들을 수 있도록 그것을 읽어 달라고 신부에게 청했다.\n\n\"읽어 드리겠소.\" 신부가 말했다. \"그 시간을 차라리 자는 데 쓰는 편이 낫지 않다면 말이오.\"\n\n\"제게는 충분한 쉼이 될 거예요.\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"어떤 이야기를 들으며 시간을 보내는 게요. 제 마음이 아직, 마땅히 잘 시간에 잠을 청할 만큼 평온하지가 않거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 좋소, 그렇다면,\" 신부가 말했다. \"읽어 드리리다. 그저 호기심에서라도 말이오. 어쩌면 뭔가 즐거운 것이 담겨 있을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n니콜라스 선생도 같은 뜻으로 간청을 보탰고, 산초도 그러했다. 이를 보고, 또 모두에게 즐거움을 주고 자신도 즐거움을 얻으리라 헤아린 신부가 말했다. \"그럼 좋소, 모두들 내게 귀를 기울이시오. 소설은 이렇게 시작하오.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII.\n\n이탈리아의 토스카나라 불리는 지방에 자리한 부유하고 이름난 도시 피렌체에, 안셀모와 로타리오라는 부와 가문을 겸비한 두 신사가 살았는데, 둘은 어찌나 막역한 사이였던지 그들을 아는 모든 이가 구별 삼아 \"두 친구\"라 불렀다. 둘은 미혼이었고, 젊었으며, 나이가 같고 취향도 같았으니, 그것만으로도 두 사람 사이의 우정이 서로 오가는 까닭을 충분히 설명할 만했다. 사실 안셀모는 로타리오보다 사랑에서 즐거움을 찾으려는 성향이 다소 강한 편이었고, 로타리오는 사냥의 즐거움에 더 끌렸으나, 때로는 안셀모가 자신의 취향을 접고 로타리오의 취향을 따라 주었고, 로타리오 또한 자신의 것을 양보하여 안셀모의 취향에 맞추어 주었으니, 이렇게 두 사람의 마음 기우는 바가 서로 발맞추어 나아가는 그 조화로움은 아무리 정교하게 맞춘 시계라도 따라올 수 없을 정도였다.\n\n안셀모는 같은 도시에 사는 고귀한 가문 출신의 아름다운 처녀를 깊이 사랑하고 있었으니, 그녀는 더없이 훌륭한 부모의 딸이었고 그녀 자신도 더없이 훌륭하여, 안셀모는 자신이 무슨 일을 하든 빠뜨리지 않던 벗 로타리오의 동의를 얻어 그 부모에게 그녀를 아내로 청하기로 결심했고, 실제로 그리하였는데, 로타리오가 그 청혼을 전하는 사자가 되어 벗이 흡족해할 만큼 일을 잘 매듭지어 준 덕분에 안셀모는 머지않아 그토록 바라던 바를 손에 넣게 되었으며, 카밀라 또한 안셀모를 남편으로 얻은 것이 어찌나 행복했던지, 그러한 행운이 자신에게 굴러든 것을 두고 하늘과 로타리오에게 끊임없이 감사를 드렸다. 처음 며칠 동안은, 혼례 무렵이라는 것이 으레 즐거운 날들이기 마련인지라, 로타리오는 예전에 그러했듯 벗 안셀모의 집을 자주 드나들며 그를 빛내 주고 그 경사를 빛내 주려, 또 할 수 있는 온갖 방법으로 그를 기쁘게 해 주려 애썼다. 그러나 혼례의 날들이 다 지나가고 줄을 잇던 방문과 축하가 뜸해지자, 그는 일부러 안셀모의 집에 발길을 끊기 시작했으니, 분별 있는 모든 사람이 으레 그러하듯 그에게도 이렇게 여겨졌던 까닭이다. 즉 결혼한 뒤의 벗의 집은 그 주인이 총각이던 시절과 같은 빈도로 드나들 곳이 아니라는 것이었다. 참되고 진실한 우정이란 어떤 식으로도 의심을 품을 수 없고 또 품어서도 안 되는 것이지만, 그럼에도 결혼한 남자의 명예란 워낙 미묘한 것이어서 형제로 인해서도 상하기 쉽고, 벗으로 인해서는 더더욱 그러하다고 여겨지기 때문이었다. 안셀모는 로타리오의 발길이 끊긴 것을 알아채고 그에게 그것을 두고 불평하며 말하기를, 결혼이 자신을 그의 벗과 어울리는 즐거움에서 멀어지게 할 줄 알았더라면 자기는 결코 결혼하지 않았을 것이며, 자신이 총각이던 시절 둘 사이에 흐르던 그 더할 나위 없는 조화로 인해 \"두 친구\"라는 그토록 달콤한 이름을 얻었던 터에, 신중하게 처신하려는 부질없는 걱정 때문에 그토록 드물고 그토록 기쁜 칭호를 잃어버리도록 내버려 두어서는 안 된다 하였다. 그러면서 둘 사이에 그런 표현이 허락된다면, 다시 한번 자기 집의 주인이 되어 예전처럼 드나들어 달라고 간청하면서, 자기 아내 카밀라는 그가 그녀에게 바라는 그 마음 외에는 다른 어떤 바람이나 기울임도 없으며, 두 사람이 얼마나 진심으로 서로를 아끼는지 알기에 그에게서 그토록 냉랭함을 보는 것이 슬프다고 단언했다.\n\n이 모든 말에, 그리고 예전처럼 자기 집에 드나들도록 그를 설득하려고 안셀모가 로타리오에게 한 그보다 더 많은 말에, 로타리오는 어찌나 신중하고 사리에 밝으며 분별 있게 대답했던지, 안셀모는 벗의 선의를 믿게 되었고, 두 사람은 로타리오가 일주일에 이틀, 그리고 축일에 그와 함께 식사하러 오기로 합의를 보았다. 그러나 둘 사이에 이런 약조가 맺어졌어도, 로타리오는 자기 자신의 명예보다도 더 소중히 여기는 벗의 명예에 부합한다고 여겨지는 선을 넘어서는 그 약조를 지키지 않기로 마음먹었다. 그는 이렇게 말했으니, 그 말은 옳았다. 하늘이 아름다운 아내를 점지해 준 결혼한 남자라면, 자기 아내가 어떤 여자 벗들과 어울리는지를 살피는 것만큼이나 자기가 어떤 친구들을 집에 들이는지도 신중히 헤아려야 한다는 것이었으니, 광장에서나 교회에서나 공공의 축제에서나 행렬 참배에서는 (남편이 늘 아내에게 거절할 수만은 없는 그런 기회들에서는) 도무지 할 수도 꾸밀 수도 없는 일이, 가장 미더운 여자 벗이나 친척의 집에서는 손쉽게 이루어질 수 있기 때문이었다. 로타리오는 또 말하기를, 결혼한 남자라면 누구나 자기 처신에서 저지를지 모를 어떤 부주의든 일러 줄 벗 하나쯤은 두어야 한다고 했으니, 이따금 남편이 아내에게 품은 깊은 정 때문에 그녀에게 주의를 주지 않거나, 그녀의 비위를 상하게 하지 않으려고 어떤 일을 하라거나 하지 말라거나 일러 주기를 삼가는 일이 생기는데, 그것을 하느냐 마느냐가 그에게 명예가 되기도 비난거리가 되기도 하기 때문이며, 이런 종류의 잘못은 벗이 경고해 준다면 손쉽게 바로잡을 수 있기 때문이었다. 그러나 로타리오가 바라 마지않는, 그토록 분별 있고 그토록 충직하며 그토록 진실한 벗을 대체 어디서 찾을 수 있단 말인가?\n\n* usque ad aras: 라틴어로 \"제단에 이르기까지\"라는 뜻으로, 신의 뜻에 어긋나지 않는 한계까지라는 의미.\n참으로 나는 알지 못한다. 로타리오만이 그러한 벗이었으니, 그는 더없는 정성과 경계심으로 벗의 명예를 지켜보며, 약조한 대로 그 집에 가는 날의 수를 줄이고 깎고 덜어 내려 애썼으니, 부유하고 고귀한 가문 출신에다 자신이 지녔다고 자각하는 매력까지 갖춘 젊은이가 카밀라처럼 아름다운 여인의 집을 드나드는 것이, 한가로운 세상 사람들의 캐묻기 좋아하고 심술궂은 눈에 의심스럽게 비칠까 저어한 까닭이었다. 비록 그의 올곧음과 평판이 헐뜯는 혀를 잠재울 수 있을지언정, 그는 자기 자신의 좋은 이름도 벗의 좋은 이름도 위태롭게 하고 싶지 않았던 것이다. 이런 까닭에 약조한 날들의 대부분을 그는 피치 못할 일이라 둘러댄 어떤 다른 볼일에 쏟았으니, 하루의 큰 부분이 한쪽에서는 불평으로, 다른 쪽에서는 변명으로 채워지곤 했다. 그러던 어느 날, 두 사람이 도시 밖을 함께 거닐고 있을 때, 안셀모가 로타리오에게 다음과 같은 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"벗 로타리오여, 자네는 짐작하겠지. 내가, 내 부모 같은 분들의 아들로 태어나게 해 주시고, 흔히 자연의 선물이라 일컫는 것들과 운명의 선물이라 일컫는 것들을 인색함 없이 베풀어 주신 것, 그리고 무엇보다도 자네를 벗으로, 카밀라를 아내로 주신 것 — 내가 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼은 아닐지언정 적어도 내가 할 수 있는 만큼은 소중히 여기는 두 보배를 주신 것 — 에 대해 하느님께 드린 그 은혜에 충분한 감사를 드릴 길이 없다는 것을 말일세. 그런데도, 사람이 행복하게 살아가는 데 흔히 필요한 모든 것이라 할 이 온갖 좋은 것들을 다 가지고도, 나는 온 세상에서 가장 만족하지 못하고 가장 흡족하지 못한 사람일세. 언제부터인지는 모르겠으나, 어찌나 기이하고 어찌나 유별난 욕망에 시달리고 짓눌려 왔던지, 나는 나 자신이 놀랍기만 하고, 혼자 있을 때면 나 자신을 탓하고 꾸짖으며, 그것을 억누르고 내 생각에서조차 감추려 애쓰지만, 그것을 작정하고 온 세상에 떠벌리려 애쓰는 것보다 나을 게 없는 결과만 얻을 뿐일세. 그러니 결국 그것은 밖으로 나오고야 말 것이기에, 나는 그것을 자네의 미더운 간직에 맡기려 하네. 이렇게 하면, 그리고 참된 벗으로서 기꺼이 나를 덜어 주려는 자네의 마음으로, 나는 머지않아 그것이 내게 끼치는 괴로움에서 벗어나게 되리라 확신하며, 내 어리석음이 내게 안긴 비참함만큼이나 자네의 보살핌이 내게 행복을 주리라 믿네.\"\n\n안셀모의 말은 로타리오를 놀라움에 빠뜨렸으니, 그토록 긴 머리말의 속뜻을 도무지 짐작할 수 없었던 까닭이다. 그는 무슨 욕망이 자기 벗을 그토록 괴롭히는 것인지 헤아려 보려 애썼으나, 그 짐작은 모두 진실과는 거리가 멀었고, 이 어리둥절함이 자아내는 불안을 덜고자, 그는 안셀모에게 말하기를, 자신의 가장 은밀한 생각을 자기에게 털어놓는 데 빙빙 둘러 가는 방법을 쓰는 것은 둘의 위대한 우정에 대한 명백한 모욕을 저지르는 일이라 했으니, 그러한 생각을 떨쳐 버리는 데는 자신의 충고를, 또 그것을 이루는 데는 자신의 도움을 기댈 수 있음을 안셀모가 잘 알고 있는 까닭이었다.\n\n\"그것이 맞는 말일세.\" 안셀모가 대답했다. \"바로 그것을 믿기에, 벗 로타리오여, 자네에게 말하겠네. 나를 시달리게 하는 그 욕망이란, 내 아내 카밀라가 내가 생각하는 만큼 선하고 완전한지 알고 싶다는 것일세. 그런데 나는 그녀를 어떤 식으로든 시험해 보지 않고는, 곧 불이 금의 순도를 증명하듯 그 시험이 그녀의 미덕의 순결함을 증명하지 않고는, 이 점에 관한 진실을 두고 마음을 놓을 수가 없네. 벗이여, 나는 여자란 유혹을 받느냐 받지 않느냐에 비례해서만 정숙한 것이고, 간곡한 구애자들의 약속과 선물과 눈물과 끈질긴 졸라댐에 굴하지 않는 여자만이 강한 여자라고 확신하기 때문일세. 아무도 나쁜 짓을 하라고 부추기지 않는데 선하게 군다고 해서 여자가 무슨 감사를 받을 만하며, 그릇될 기회조차 주어지지 않고, 단 한 번이라도 부적절한 짓을 들켰다 하면 목숨을 앗아 갈 남편이 있음을 아는 여자가 조심스럽고 신중하게 군다고 해서 그게 무어 놀라운 일이겠는가? 그러니 나는 두려움 때문에, 혹은 기회가 없어서 정숙한 여자를, 유혹과 시험을 거쳐 승리의 면류관을 쓰고 나온 여자와 같은 반열에 두지 않네. 그러므로 이런 이유들과, 내가 품은 견해를 정당화하고 떠받치기 위해 자네에게 댈 수 있는 다른 여러 이유로 인해, 나는 내 아내 카밀라가 이 고비를 넘기를, 그리고 자신이 구애를 받고 있음을, 그것도 그녀에게 마음을 둘 만한 자격 있는 자에게서 구애받고 있음을 깨닫는 그 불길로 단련되고 시험받기를 바라네. 만일 그녀가, 내가 알기로 그러하리라 믿듯이, 이 싸움에서 승리하고 나온다면, 나는 내 행운을 견줄 데 없는 것으로 여길 것이며, 내 욕망의 잔이 가득 찼노라고, 그리고 현자가 '누가 그런 여인을 찾으랴?' 하고 말한 그 정숙한 여인이 내 몫으로 떨어졌노라고 말할 수 있을 것이네. 그리고 만일 그 결과가 내 기대와 반대로 나온다 하더라도, 내 견해가 옳았음을 알게 되는 만족 속에서, 그토록 비싼 값을 치르고 얻은 경험이 으레 내게 안길 그 고통을 불평 없이 견디겠네. 그리고 내 바람에 맞서 자네가 내놓을 어떤 말도 내가 그것을 실행에 옮기는 것을 막지는 못할 터이니, 벗 로타리오여, 나는 자네가 내가 마음먹은 이 목적을 이루는 도구가 되어 주기를 바라네. 내가 그 일을 위해 자네에게 기회를 마련해 줄 것이고, 정숙하고 명예롭고 정결하며 고결한 여인을 좇는 데 필요하다고 내가 여기는 것이라면 무엇 하나 모자람이 없게 할 것이네. 그리고 여러 이유 가운데서도, 내가 이 고된 일을 자네에게 맡길 마음이 든 것은, 만일 카밀라가 자네에게 정복당한다 해도 그 정복이 끝까지 밀어붙여지지는 않을 것이고, 명예심에서 비롯되어 끝내 하지 않을 일이 이루어진 것으로 칠 만큼만 나아가리라는 점을 헤아렸기 때문일세. 그리하여 나는 의도 외에는 아무것도 더 침해받지 않을 것이고, 내가 입은 침해는 자네의 침묵의 올곧음 속에 묻힐 것인데, 나와 관계된 일에 관한 한 그 침묵이 죽음의 침묵만큼이나 오래가리라는 것을 나는 잘 아네. 그러니 자네가 나로 하여금 삶이라 부를 만한 것을 누리게 하려거든, 미적지근하게도 게으르게도 말고, 내 욕망이 요구하는 그 열의와 정성으로, 그리고 우리의 우정이 내게 확신케 하는 그 충직함으로, 당장 이 사랑의 싸움에 나서 주게.\"\n\n이것이 안셀모가 로타리오에게 건넨 말이었으니, 로타리오는 어찌나 주의 깊게 그 말을 들었던지, 앞서 언급한 것을 말한 것 외에는 상대가 다 마칠 때까지 입을 열지 않았다. 그러다 상대가 더 할 말이 없음을 알아채고는, 이전에 한 번도 본 적 없어 경이와 놀라움을 자아내는 무언가를 바라보듯 잠시 그를 바라보고 나서, 그에게 말했다. \"벗 안셀모여, 자네가 내게 한 말이 농담이 아니라고는 도무지 믿어지지가 않네. 만일 자네가 진지하게 말하고 있다고 여겼다면 나는 자네가 그토록 멀리까지 가도록 내버려 두지 않았을 것이네. 그래서 자네의 말을 듣지 않음으로써 자네의 긴 일장 연설을 멈추게 하려 했던 것일세. 정말이지 의심스럽군. 자네가 나를 모르거나, 아니면 내가 자네를 모르거나 둘 중 하나가 아닌가 싶네. 하지만 아닐세, 나는 자네가 안셀모임을 잘 알고, 자네는 내가 로타리오임을 알지. 불행한 것은, 내가 보기엔, 자네가 예전의 그 안셀모가 아니라는 것이고, 또 내가 마땅히 그러해야 할 그 로타리오가 아니라고 자네가 생각했음이 틀림없다는 것일세. 자네가 내게 한 말들은 내 벗이던 그 안셀모의 말이 아니며, 자네가 내게 요구하는 것들도 자네가 아는 그 로타리오에게 청할 만한 것이 아니기 때문일세. 참된 벗들은 시인이 말한 대로 '제단에 이르기까지(usque ad aras)' 서로를 시험하고 서로를 활용하는 법인데, 그 말로 그가 뜻한 바는, 그들이 하느님의 뜻에 어긋나는 일에는 우정을 쓰지 않으리라는 것이었네. 그러니 이것이 이교도가 우정에 대해 품은 마음이었다면, 인간의 어떤 우정을 위해서도 신성한 것을 저버려서는 안 됨을 아는 그리스도교도에게는 얼마나 더 그러해야 하겠는가? 그리고 벗이 자기 벗에 대한 의무를 다하고자 하늘에 대한 의무를 제쳐 두는 데까지 나아간다 하더라도, 그것은 하찮거나 사소한 일에서가 아니라, 벗의 목숨과 명예에 관계된 일에서여야 마땅하네. 자, 말해 보게, 안셀모. 이 둘 가운데 어느 것이 자네에게 위태로워서, 내가 자네를 기쁘게 해 주려고 나 자신을 위태롭게 하고, 자네가 내게 구하는 그토록 가증스러운 일을 해야 한단 말인가? 정말이지 둘 다 아닐세. 오히려, 내가 이해하는 한, 자네는 내게 자네의 명예와 목숨을 앗고, 동시에 나 자신의 명예와 목숨까지 앗으려 애쓰고 수고하라고 청하고 있는 것일세. 내가 자네의 명예를 앗는다면 자네의 목숨을 앗는 것이 분명하니, 명예 없는 사람은 죽은 자보다 못하기 때문일세. 그리고 자네가 그러기를 바라는 대로 내가 자네에게 그토록 큰 해악을 끼치는 도구가 된다면, 나 역시 명예 없이, 따라서 목숨 없이 남게 되지 않겠는가? 벗 안셀모여, 내 말을 들어 보게. 그리고 자네 욕망의 대상에 관해 내게 떠오르는 바를 다 말할 때까지는 답하려 조급해하지 말게. 자네가 답하고 내가 들을 시간은 충분히 남아 있을 테니 말일세.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 안셀모가 말했다. \"하고 싶은 말을 다 하게.\"\n\n그러자 로타리오가 말을 이었다. \"안셀모, 내가 보기에 자네는 지금 무어인들이 늘 지니는 그런 마음 상태에 있는 듯하네. 그들은 성경의 인용으로도, 깨달음의 검토에 기대거나 신앙 조항에 근거한 논리로도 결코 자기네 교리의 그릇됨을 깨닫게 할 수가 없고, 오직 손에 잡히고 쉽고 알기 쉬우며 증명할 수 있고 의심의 여지가 없는, '같은 것에서 같은 것을 빼면 그 나머지도 같다' 같은, 부인할 수 없는 수학적 논증을 갖춘 본보기라야만 받아들이지. 그리고 그것을 말로 알아듣지 못하면 — 실제로 알아듣지 못하지 — 손으로 보여 주고, 눈앞에 들이대야 하는데, 그렇게 다 해 줘도 아무도 그들에게 우리의 거룩한 종교의 진리를 납득시키지 못하네. 자네에게도 나는 바로 이런 식으로 해야겠네. 자네 안에서 솟아난 그 욕망이 어찌나 터무니없고 이성의 그림자라 할 만한 모든 것에서 동떨어져 있는지, 자네의 어리석음 — 지금으로선 그것을 달리 부를 이름이 없으니 — 을 두고 논리를 펴는 데 시간을 들이는 것이 헛수고이리라 여겨질 정도일세. 자네의 그 해로운 욕망에 대한 벌로 자네를 그 어리석음 속에 그대로 내버려 두고 싶은 마음마저 드네. 하지만 그토록 명백한 파멸의 위험 속에 자네를 저버리도록 두지 않는, 자네에 대한 내 우정이, 자네를 그토록 매정하게 대하지 못하게 막는구먼. 그리고 자네가 이를 똑똑히 알도록 말해 보게, 안셀모. 자네는 내게, 정숙한 여인에게 억지로 구애를 들이대고, 미덕 있는 여인을 꾀어내고, 정결한 마음을 지닌 여인에게 수작을 걸고, 신중한 여인에게 구애하라고 말하지 않았는가? 그래, 자네는 내게 그렇게 말했네. 그렇다면, 자네가 정숙하고 미덕 있고 정결한 마음에 신중한 아내를 두었음을 안다면, 대체 자네가 구하는 것이 무엇이란 말인가? 그리고 그녀가 내 모든 공세에서 승리하고 나오리라 — 의심할 바 없이 그러하겠지만 — 믿는다면, 그때 자네는 그녀가 지금 지닌 것보다 더 높은 무슨 칭호를 그녀에게 내릴 수 있다고 생각하며, 그때 그녀가 지금보다 무엇이 더 나아진단 말인가? 자네는 그녀를 자네가 말하는 그런 여인으로 여기지 않거나, 아니면 자네가 무엇을 구하는지를 모르거나 둘 중 하날세. 자네가 그녀를 자네가 말하는 그런 여인으로 여기지 않는다면, 어찌하여 자네가 보기에 가장 좋을 방식으로 그녀를 죄 있는 자로 다루지 않고 굳이 그녀를 시험하려 든단 말인가? 하지만 그녀가 자네가 믿는 만큼 미덕 있는 여인이라면, 진리 그 자체를 시험해 보려는 것은 쓸데없는 짓일세. 시험을 거친 뒤에도 그것은 그저 이전과 같은 평가에 머물 것이기 때문이지. 그러니 결론은 분명하네. 이득보다 해가 올 법한 일을 시도하는 것은, 더구나 그것이 우리가 시도하도록 강요받거나 떠밀린 것도 아니고, 멀리서 보아도 시도하는 것이 명백히 미친 짓임이 드러나는 일이라면, 그것은 사리를 분별 못 하고 무모한 자들이나 하는 짓이라는 것일세.\n\n\"어려운 일은 하느님을 위해서든, 세상을 위해서든, 아니면 그 둘 다를 위해서든 시도되는 법일세. 하느님을 위해 떠맡는 일이란 성인들이 인간의 몸으로 천사의 삶을 살고자 할 때 시도하는 일이고, 세상을 위해 떠맡는 일이란 흔히 운명의 축복이라 일컫는 것을 얻으려고 그 광막한 바다와 그토록 다양한 기후, 그토록 많은 낯선 나라를 가로지르는 사람들의 일이며, 하느님과 세상을 함께 위해 떠맡는 일이란 용감한 병사들의 일이니, 그들은 적의 성벽에서 포탄이 뚫을 만한 너비의 구멍을 보자마자 온갖 두려움을 떨쳐 버리고, 망설임도 없이, 자신들을 위협하는 명백한 위험도 아랑곳없이, 자기 신앙과 조국과 임금을 지키려는 욕망에 떠밀려, 자신들을 기다리는 수천 가지 죽음의 한복판으로 겁 없이 몸을 던지지. 사람들이 으레 시도하는 일이란 바로 이런 것들이고, 아무리 어려움과 위험으로 가득하다 해도 그런 일을 시도하는 데는 명예와 영광과 이득이 따르네. 하지만 자네가 시도하고 이루기를 바란다고 말하는 그 일은 자네에게 하느님의 영광도, 운명의 축복도, 사람들 사이의 명성도 가져다주지 못할 것일세. 설령 그 결말이 자네 바라는 대로 나온다 해도, 자네는 지금 이 순간보다 더 행복해지지도, 더 부유해지지도, 더 명예로워지지도 않을 것이고, 만일 그렇지 않게 나온다면 자네는 상상할 수 있는 것보다 더 큰 비참함에 빠지게 될 걸세. 그때는 자네에게 닥친 불행을 아무도 모른다고 곱씹어 봤자 아무 소용이 없을 것이고, 자네 자신이 그것을 안다는 사실만으로도 자네를 괴롭히고 짓누르기에 충분할 테니 말일세. 그리고 내가 하는 말의 진실을 뒷받침하고자, 이름난 시인 루이지 탄실로가 그의 '성 베드로의 눈물' 제1부 끝에 지은 한 연을 자네에게 들려주겠네. 그 시는 이러하네.\n\n아침이 더디 밝아 올수록 베드로의 가슴속 번민과 수치는 더욱 자라났네. 보는 눈 하나 없음을 그는 잘 알았으나, 그 자신이 그 자신에게 수치였으니, 뭇사람의 눈앞에 드러나든, 시야에서 가려지든, 고귀한 마음은 한결같이 그 아픔을 느끼리라. 죄지은 영혼은 수치의 먹이가 되리니, 오직 하늘과 땅만이 그 수치를 본다 할지라도.\n\n이처럼 그것을 비밀로 간직한다 해도 자네는 자네의 슬픔에서 벗어나지 못할 것이며, 오히려 끊임없이 눈물을 흘릴 것이네. 눈의 눈물이 아니라면, 가슴에서 흐르는 피눈물을 말일세. 마치 우리 시인이 들려준 그 어수룩한 의사처럼 말이야. 더 분별 있던 현명한 리날도는 마다했던 그 잔의 시험을 그자는 해 보려 들었지. 비록 이것이 시인의 허구라 하더라도, 거기에는 주의와 연구와 본받음에 값할 만한 도덕적 교훈이 담겨 있네. 게다가 내가 이제 자네에게 하려는 말을 들으면, 자네는 자네가 저지르려는 그 큰 잘못을 보게 될 걸세.\n\n\"말해 보게, 안셀모. 만일 하늘이나 행운이 자네를 더없이 빼어난 품질의 다이아몬드의 임자이자 합당한 주인으로 만들어 주었고, 그것을 본 모든 보석 세공인이 그 빼어남과 순도에 만족하여 한목소리로 한뜻이 되어, 순도와 품질과 정교함에서 그것이 그런 종류의 보석이 될 수 있는 모든 것을 다 갖추었다고 말했으며, 자네 자신도 달리 알 길이 없어 같은 믿음을 지녔다 치세. 그런데 자네가 그 다이아몬드를 가져다 모루와 망치 사이에 끼워 놓고, 그저 두들기는 힘과 팔의 완력으로, 그것이 사람들 말대로 그토록 단단하고 그토록 빼어난지 시험해 보고 싶어 한다면, 그게 자네에게 사리에 맞는 일이겠는가? 그리고 자네가 그리했는데, 그 돌이 그토록 어리석은 시험을 견뎌 낸다 한들, 그것이 그 값이나 명성에 보태 주는 것은 아무것도 없을 것이며, 만일 그것이 깨진다면 — 그럴 수도 있을 테니 — 모든 것이 사라지지 않겠는가? 의심할 바 없이 그러할 것이고, 그 임자는 모든 이의 눈에 바보로 평가받게 될 걸세. 그러니 벗 안셀모여, 헤아려 보게. 카밀라는 자네의 평가에서나 다른 이들의 평가에서나 더없이 빼어난 품질의 다이아몬드라는 것을, 그리고 그녀를 깨질 위험에 내모는 것은 이치에 어긋난다는 것을 말일세. 그녀가 온전히 남는다 해도 지금 지닌 것보다 더 높은 값으로 오를 수는 없고, 만일 그녀가 무너져 버텨 내지 못한다면, 그때 자네가 어떻게 그녀를 잃게 될지, 그리고 그녀와 자네 자신의 파멸의 원인이 된 것을 두고 얼마나 마땅히 자네 자신을 원망하게 될지 지금 곰곰이 생각해 보게. 정숙하고 미덕 있는 여인만큼 세상에 귀한 보석은 없으며, 여자의 명예란 모두 평판에 달려 있음을 기억하게. 그리고 자네 아내의 평판이 자네가 아는 그토록 높은 경지에 있는데, 어찌하여 자네는 그 진실을 의문에 부치려 든단 말인가? 벗이여, 기억하게. 여자란 불완전한 동물이어서, 그녀가 발을 헛디뎌 넘어지게 할 걸림돌을 그녀의 길에 놓아서는 안 되고, 오히려 그것들을 치워, 그녀의 길에서 온갖 장애를 비워 주어, 거리낌 없이 자기 길을 자유로이 달려가 바라던 완성에 — 곧 미덕 있는 여인이 되는 데에 — 이르게 해야 한다는 것을 말일세. 박물학자들은 흰담비라는 작은 짐승이 더없이 새하얀 털을 지녔다고 일러 주는데, 사냥꾼들이 그것을 잡으려 할 때 이런 술책을 쓴다고 하네. 그것이 자주 드나드는 길목을 알아낸 다음, 그 길을 진흙으로 막아 놓고, 그것을 몰아세워 그 자리로 내모는 것이지. 그러면 흰담비는 진흙에 이르자마자 멈춰 서서, 그 진창을 지나 자기 흰빛을 망치고 더럽히느니 차라리 사로잡히기를 택한다네. 그것은 자기 흰빛을 목숨과 자유보다 더 소중히 여기기 때문이지. 미덕 있고 정숙한 여인은 흰담비와 같고, 정결의 미덕은 눈보다 더 희고 더 깨끗하네. 그러니 그녀가 그것을 잃지 않고 지니고 간직하기를 바라는 자라면, 흰담비에게 쓰는 방법과는 다른 방법을 택해야 하네. 끈질긴 구애자들의 선물과 정성이라는 진창을 그녀 앞에 놓아서는 안 되네. 어쩌면 — 아니 '어쩌면'이랄 것도 없이 — 그녀 자신에게 이 걸림돌들을 지나 발밑에 짓밟을 만한 미덕과 타고난 힘이 충분치 않을지도 모르기 때문일세. 그것들은 치워야 하고, 미덕의 광채와 아름다운 명예의 아름다움을 그녀 앞에 놓아 주어야 하네. 미덕 있는 여인은 또한 거울과 같아서, 맑게 빛나는 수정 같아, 그것에 닿는 입김 하나하나에 흐려지고 어두워지기 쉽다네. 그녀는 성유물처럼 다루어야 하니, 흠모하되 만지지는 말아야 하지. 그녀는 장미와 꽃이 가득한 아름다운 정원을 지키고 아끼듯 지키고 아껴야 하니, 그 정원의 임자는 누구도 들어와 꽃 한 송이 꺾지 못하게 하고, 다른 이들은 그저 멀리서 쇠창살 사이로 그 향기와 아름다움을 즐기는 것으로 족하게 하지. 끝으로, 내게 떠오르는 시 몇 구절을 자네에게 들려주겠네. 어느 현대 희극에서 들은 것인데, 우리가 논하는 요점에 들어맞는 듯하네. 한 신중한 노인이 어린 딸을 둔 아버지인 다른 이에게, 딸을 가두어 놓고 지켜보며 칩거시키라고 충고하면서, 여러 논거 가운데 이런 말들을 썼다네.\n여인은 유리로 된 것, 허나 그 깨지기 쉬움을 너무 꼬치꼬치 시험하지 않는 것이 상책이라. 무슨 일이 벌어질지 누가 알랴?\n\n깨뜨리기란 손쉬운 일, 고칠 수 없는 것을 매질에 내맡기고, 온전케 할 수 없는 것을 산산조각 내려는 건 어리석은 짓이라.\n\n그러니 이것은 누구나 참으로 여길 만하고, 그 까닭은 보기에도 분명하니, 다나에가 있는 곳에는 황금의 소나기 또한 있는 법이라.\n\n* 다나에: 그리스 신화의 인물로, 아버지가 그녀를 청동 탑에 가두었으나 제우스가 황금 비로 변해 들어가 임신하게 했다.\n\n\"안셀모, 지금껏 자네에게 한 말은 모두 자네에게 관계된 것이었네. 이제 나 자신에게 관계된 것을 좀 말하는 것이 옳겠지. 그리고 내 말이 장황하더라도 너그러이 봐 주게. 자네가 들어선, 그리고 나더러 자네를 끌어내 달라고 하는 그 미궁이 그것을 어쩔 수 없게 만드니 말일세.\n\n\"자네는 나를 자네의 벗으로 여기면서, 우정과는 전혀 어울리지 않는 것, 곧 내게서 명예를 앗으려 하고 있네. 그리고 자네는 그것만 노리는 것이 아니라, 나로 하여금 자네에게서도 명예를 앗게 하려 드는구먼. 자네가 내게서 명예를 앗으려 한다는 것은 분명하네. 카밀라가 내가 자네 요구대로 그녀에게 구애하는 것을 보면, 그녀는 분명 나를 명예도 올바른 마음도 없는 사람으로 여길 테니, 내가 나 자신의 처지와 자네의 우정에 진 빚에 그토록 어긋나는 일을 시도하고 저지르는 셈이기 때문일세. 자네가 나로 하여금 자네에게서도 명예를 앗게 하려 든다는 것 또한 의심할 바 없네. 카밀라는 내가 그녀에게 구애를 들이대는 것을 보면, 내가 그녀에게서 무언가 가벼운 구석을 알아채어 그것이 나로 하여금 내 천한 욕망을 그녀에게 드러내도록 부추겼다고 생각할 것이며, 만일 그녀가 자신이 욕보았다고 여긴다면, 그녀의 불명예는 그녀에게 속한 자네에게도 닿는 것일세. 그래서 흔히 일어나는 그 일이 생겨나는 것이지. 곧, 간음한 여자의 남편은, 자기 아내가 본분을 저버린 것을 알지 못했거나 그럴 빌미를 준 적이 없거나, 혹은 (부주의하거나 게을러서) 자기 불명예를 막을 힘이 있었음에도 그러지 못했더라도, 그럼에도 천하고 비난받을 이름으로 낙인찍히며, 자기 아내의 죄를 아는 모든 이에게서 동정 대신 멸시의 눈초리로, 어떤 의미에서, 여겨지게 되는 일 말일세. 그가 자기 잘못이 아니라 음란한 배우자의 욕정 때문에 불운을 당했음을 그들이 빤히 보면서도 말이지. 하지만 정숙치 못한 아내의 남편이, 그녀가 그러한 줄 알지 못하고, 탓할 데도 없으며, 그녀를 그리 만들 어떤 짓도 빌미도 준 적이 없는데도, 어찌하여 마땅한 이유로 불명예가 그에게 따라붙는지를 말해 주겠네. 듣기에 지치지 말게, 그것이 자네에게 이로울 테니 말일세.\n\n\"하느님이 지상 낙원에서 우리 최초의 조상을 창조하셨을 때, 성경은 하느님이 아담에게 잠을 불어넣으시고, 그가 잠든 사이 그의 왼쪽 갈비뼈 하나를 취하여 그것으로 우리 어머니 하와를 빚으셨으며, 아담이 깨어나 그녀를 보고는 '이는 내 살 중의 살이요, 내 뼈 중의 뼈로다' 하고 말했다고 하네. 그리고 하느님이 말씀하시기를 '이러므로 남자가 그 아비와 어미를 떠나, 둘이 한 몸을 이룰지니라' 하셨네. 그리하여 결혼이라는 거룩한 성사가 세워졌으니, 오직 죽음만이 풀 수 있는 그런 매듭으로 맺어진 것일세. 그리고 이 기적과도 같은 성사의 힘과 효험이 어찌나 큰지, 그것은 서로 다른 두 사람을 하나의 똑같은 살로 만들어 주며, 미덕 있는 이들이 결혼하면 그 이상이 되니, 그들은 두 영혼을 가졌으되 단 하나의 의지만을 가지기 때문일세. 그리하여 따라 나오는 결론은, 아내의 살이 그 남편의 살과 하나이자 똑같은 것이므로, 그 살에 끼칠지 모를 얼룩이나 그것이 입는 손상이 남편의 살에 떨어진다는 것이며, 비록 그가, 앞서 말했듯, 그 빌미를 준 적이 없을지라도 그러하다는 것이네. 발이나 몸의 어느 부위의 아픔을 온몸이 느끼는 것은 모두가 한 몸이기 때문이고, 머리가 발목의 상처를 — 자기가 그것을 일으키지 않았는데도 — 느끼는 것처럼, 남편도 그녀와 하나이기에 아내의 불명예를 함께 나눠 지는 것일세. 그리고 모든 세속의 명예나 불명예가 살과 피에서 비롯되고, 그릇된 아내의 명예도 그러한 종류인지라, 남편은 어쩔 수 없이 그 몫을 짊어지고, 자신도 모르는 새 불명예를 짊어진 자로 여겨지게 되는 것일세. 그러니 보게, 안셀모. 자네가 자네 미덕 있는 배우자의 평안을 어지럽히려 함으로써 무릅쓰는 위험을 말일세. 자네가 그 얼마나 공허하고 분별없는 호기심 때문에 자네 정숙한 아내의 가슴속에 지금 고요히 잠들어 있는 정념들을 흔들어 깨우려 하는지를 말일세. 곰곰이 생각해 보게. 자네가 모든 것을 걸고 얻으려는 것은 보잘것없고, 자네가 잃을 것은 어찌나 큰지, 나는 그것을 표현할 말이 없어 차마 묘사하지 못하고 그냥 두겠네. 하지만 내가 한 말 모두가 자네를 그 천한 목적에서 돌이키기에 충분치 않다면, 자네는 자네 불명예와 불행을 위한 다른 도구를 찾아야 할 것이네. 나는 비록 내가 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 상실인 자네의 우정을 잃는다 해도, 그러한 도구가 되는 데는 동의하지 않을 테니 말일세.\"\n\n이 말을 하고 나서 현명하고 미덕 있는 로타리오는 입을 다물었고, 안셀모는 마음이 어지럽고 깊은 생각에 잠겨 한동안 대답이라고 한마디도 내놓지 못했으나, 마침내 입을 열었다. \"벗 로타리오여, 자네가 본 대로 나는 자네가 내게 들려주기로 한 말을 주의 깊게 들었네. 그리고 자네의 논거와 본보기와 비유 속에서 나는 자네가 지닌 그 높은 지성과, 자네가 다다른 참된 우정의 완벽함을 보았네. 또한 내가 자네의 견해를 따르지 않고 내 견해를 좇는다면, 나는 선에서 달아나 악을 좇는 셈임을 나는 보고 또 인정하네. 사정이 그러하니, 자네는 기억해 주게. 내가 지금 여자들이 이따금 시달리는 그 병을 앓고 있다는 것을 말일세. 흙이나 회반죽이나 숯, 그보다 더 고약한, 보기에도 역겹고 먹기에는 더더욱 역겨운 것들을 먹고 싶은 갈망이 그들을 사로잡을 때의 그 병 말일세. 그러니 나를 낫게 하려면 어떤 술책에 기댈 수밖에 없는데, 자네가 그저 시작만 해 준다면, 비록 그것이 미적지근하고 흉내만 내는 식이라 해도, 카밀라에게 구애를 시작해 주기만 한다면 그것은 손쉽게 이루어질 것이네. 그녀는 첫 공격에 미덕이 무너질 만큼 호락호락하지 않을 테니 말일세. 이 시도만으로도 나는 만족할 것이고, 자네는 우리 우정이 자네에게 지운 의무를 다하게 될 것이니, 곧 내게 생명을 주는 것뿐 아니라, 내 명예를 버리지 말라고 나를 설득하는 일까지 해 주는 셈일세. 그리고 자네가 이를 해야만 하는 데는 단 한 가지 이유가 있으니, 곧 내가 지금처럼 이 시험을 해 보기로 작정한 이상, 자네가 나로 하여금 내 약점을 다른 이에게 드러내게 하여, 자네가 내가 잃지 않도록 애쓰는 그 명예를 위태롭게 두어서는 안 된다는 것일세. 그리고 자네가 그녀에게 구애하는 동안 자네 명예가 카밀라의 평가에서 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 높이 서지 못한다 해도, 그것은 거의, 아니 전혀 중요치 않네. 머지않아 그녀에게서 우리가 기대하는 그 한결같음을 발견하게 되면, 자네는 우리의 계략에 관한 있는 그대로의 진실을 그녀에게 일러 줄 수 있을 것이고, 그리하여 그녀의 존중 속에서 자네 자리를 되찾을 수 있을 테니 말일세. 그러니 자네가 거는 것은 그토록 적고, 그 모험으로 내게 그토록 큰 만족을 줄 수 있으니, 설령 더한 어려움이 자네 앞에 나타난다 해도 그것을 떠맡기를 마다하지 말아 주게. 내가 말했듯, 자네가 시작만 해 준다면 나는 그 일이 결판난 것으로 받아들이겠네.\"\n\n로타리오는 안셀모의 확고한 결심을 보고, 그를 거기서 단념시키기 위해 더 내놓을 본보기도 들이댈 논거도 알지 못했으며, 그가 자기 그 해로운 계획을 다른 누군가에게 털어놓겠다고 으름장 놓는 것을 알아채고는, 더 큰 화를 막고자 그를 흡족하게 해 주고 그가 청한 바를 들어주기로 마음먹었으니, 카밀라의 마음을 더럽히지 않으면서 안셀모를 만족시키도록 그 일을 처리할 작정이었다. 그리하여 그는 답하기를, 그 뜻을 다른 누구에게도 알리지 말라고 하면서, 그 일을 자신이 떠맡아 그가 원하는 대로 곧 시작하겠노라 했다. 안셀모는 그를 따뜻하고 다정하게 껴안으며, 마치 그가 자기에게 무슨 큰 은혜라도 베푼 양 그 제안에 고마워했다. 그리고 두 사람 사이에 이튿날부터 일에 착수하기로 합의가 이루어졌으니, 안셀모가 로타리오에게 카밀라와 단둘이 이야기 나눌 기회와 시간을 마련해 주고, 그녀에게 내밀고 선사할 돈과 보석을 대 주기로 했다. 안셀모는 또한 그가 그녀에게 음악을 들려주고, 그녀를 기리는 시구를 지어야 한다고 일렀으며, 만일 그가 시 짓는 수고를 마다하면 자기가 직접 짓겠다고 했다. 로타리오는 안셀모가 짐작하는 것과는 사뭇 다른 속셈으로 모든 것에 동의했고, 이런 양해 아래 두 사람은 안셀모의 집으로 돌아왔는데, 거기서 카밀라가 남편을 애타고 불안하게 기다리고 있는 것을 보았으니, 그날따라 그가 평소보다 늦게 돌아온 까닭이었다. 로타리오는 제집으로 물러갔고, 안셀모는 제집에 남았으니, 로타리오가 마음이 어지러운 만큼이나 그는 흡족해 있었다. 로타리오로서는 이 분별없는 일에서 빠져나갈 만족스러운 길이 보이지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 그날 밤 그는 카밀라에게 아무런 해도 끼치지 않으면서 안셀모를 속일 만한 계책 하나를 생각해 냈다. 이튿날 그는 벗과 함께 식사하러 갔고, 남편이 그에게 품은 정을 아는 카밀라에게서 매우 다정한 환대를 받았다. 식사가 끝나고 상보가 걷히자, 안셀모는 로타리오에게, 자기가 급한 볼일을 보고 와야 하니 카밀라와 함께 거기 있으라고 하면서, 한 시간 반이면 돌아오겠다고 했다. 카밀라는 그에게 가지 말라고 청했고, 로타리오는 그를 따라가겠다고 했으나, 무엇으로도 안셀모를 설득할 수 없었으니, 그는 오히려 자기가 그와 의논할 매우 중대한 일이 있다며 로타리오더러 자기를 기다리며 남아 있으라고 다그쳤다. 동시에 그는 카밀라에게, 자기가 돌아올 때까지 로타리오를 혼자 두지 말라고 일렀다. 요컨대 그는 자기 부재의 이유, 곧 그 어리석음에 어찌나 그럴듯한 낯빛을 입혔던지, 그것이 꾸며 낸 일이라고는 누구도 의심할 수 없었다.\n\n안셀모가 떠나고, 카밀라와 로타리오는 식탁에 단둘이 남았으니, 집안의 나머지 사람들은 식사하러 갔던 것이다. 로타리오는 벗의 바람대로 자신이 결투장에 들어선 처지임을, 그것도 그 아름다움만으로 무장한 기사 한 부대를 무찌를 수 있는 적을 마주하고 있음을 알았으니, 그가 두려워할 만한 까닭이 있었는지 어떤지는 판단해 보라. 허나 그가 한 일이란, 의자 팔걸이에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손에 뺨을 기댄 채, 카밀라에게 무례를 용서해 달라고 청하며, 안셀모가 돌아올 때까지 잠을 좀 자고 싶다고 한 것이었다. 카밀라는 대답하기를, 의자에서보다는 응접실에서 더 편히 쉴 수 있을 테니 그리로 들어가 주무시라고 청했으나, 로타리오는 사양하고는 안셀모가 돌아올 때까지 거기서 잠들어 있었다. 안셀모는 카밀라가 제 방에 있고 로타리오는 잠들어 있는 것을 보고, 자기가 그토록 오래 자리를 비워 둘에게 이야기를 나누고도 잠까지 잘 만큼 넉넉한 시간을 마련해 주었거니 생각하고는, 로타리오가 깨어나기를 못내 조바심치며 기다렸으니, 그와 함께 나가 성과가 어떠했는지 캐물으려는 것이었다. 모든 일이 그가 바라던 대로 되어 갔다. 로타리오가 깨어나자 두 사람은 곧장 집을 나섰고, 안셀모는 알고 싶어 안달하던 것을 물었으며, 로타리오는 답하기를, 첫 번에 자신을 온전히 드러내는 것은 마땅치 않다고 여겼기에, 그저 카밀라의 매력을 칭송하면서, 온 도시가 그녀의 아름다움과 재치 외에는 아무것도 입에 올리지 않는다고 그녀에게 말해 주었을 뿐이라 했으니, 이것이 그녀의 호감을 사 다음번에 자신의 말을 기껍게 듣도록 마음을 기울이게 하는 데 더없이 좋은 시작이라 여겨진 까닭이며, 그리하여 그는 경계하고 있는 자를 속이려 할 때 악마가 기대는 그 술책을 써먹은 셈이었다. 악마는 어둠의 천사이면서도 빛의 천사로 둔갑하여, 그럴듯한 겉모습 아래 마침내 제 모습을 드러내고, 처음에 그 간계가 들통나지 않으면 제 목적을 이루는 법이니 말이다. 이 모든 것이 안셀모를 매우 흡족하게 했고, 그는 날마다 같은 기회를 마련해 주되, 집을 비우지는 않겠다고 했으니, 카밀라가 그 음모를 알아채지 못하도록 집 안에서 할 일들을 찾아내겠다는 것이었다.\n\n이리하여 여러 날이 지나갔고, 로타리오는 카밀라에게 한마디도 건네지 않으면서, 안셀모에게는 자기가 그녀와 이야기를 나누었으나 명예롭지 못한 어떤 일에 대해서도 그녀에게서 동의의 기미라곤 손톱만큼도, 아니 희망의 표징이나 그림자조차도 끌어낼 수 없었으며, 오히려 그녀가 그것을 남편에게 일러바치겠다고 했다고 보고했다.\n\n\"여기까지는 좋아.\" 안셀모가 말했다. \"카밀라가 지금껏 말에는 버텨 냈으니, 이제 그녀가 행동에는 어떻게 버티는지 봐야겠네. 내일 자네에게 금화 이천 크라운을 줄 테니 그것을 내밀거나 아예 선사하고, 그만큼 더 줄 테니 그녀를 꾀어낼 보석을 사도록 하게. 여자란 곱게 차려입고 화사하게 꾸미고 다니는 것을 좋아하는 법이고, 아름다운 여자일수록 더더욱 그러하니, 아무리 정숙하다 해도 그렇지. 그러니 그녀가 이 유혹에도 버텨 낸다면, 나는 만족하고 자네를 더는 성가시게 하지 않겠네.\"\n\n로타리오는 답하기를, 이제 시작한 이상 끝까지 그 일을 밀고 나가겠으나, 자기가 지치고 패하여 거기서 빠져나오게 되리라는 것을 알겠노라 했다. 이튿날 그는 사천 크라운을 받았고, 그와 함께 사천 가지 난감함도 받았으니, 새로운 거짓말로 무어라 둘러대야 할지 알 수 없었던 까닭이다. 그러나 끝내 그는 마음을 정하고, 카밀라가 말에 버틴 것만큼이나 선물과 약속에도 굳건히 버티고 있으니, 더 수고할 필요가 없고, 시간만 헛되이 다 흘러가고 있다고 그에게 말하기로 했다.\n\n그런데 우연이 일을 다른 식으로 이끌어, 안셀모가 다른 때처럼 카밀라와 로타리오를 단둘이 남겨 둔 다음, 어느 방에 틀어박혀 열쇠 구멍으로 둘 사이에 오가는 일을 지켜보고 엿듣도록 자리를 잡았는데, 반 시간이 넘도록 로타리오가 카밀라에게 한마디도 건네지 않으며, 한평생 거기 있는다 해도 한마디도 건네지 않으리라는 것을 알아챘다. 그리하여 그는 카밀라의 대답에 관해 벗이 자기에게 들려준 것이 모두 꾸며 낸 거짓말이라는 결론에 이르렀고, 정말 그러한지 확인하려고 밖으로 나와 로타리오를 따로 불러내, 무슨 소식이 있으며 카밀라는 어떤 심기였는지 물었다. 로타리오는 답하기를, 자기는 그 일을 더 끌고 나갈 마음이 없으니, 그녀가 어찌나 노엽고 매몰차게 대답했던지 그녀에게 더 무슨 말을 할 엄두가 나지 않는다 했다.\n\n\"아, 로타리오, 로타리오.\" 안셀모가 말했다. \"자네는 나에 대한 의무를, 그리고 내가 자네에게 거는 그 큰 믿음을 어찌 이리도 못 갚는가! 나는 방금 이 열쇠 구멍으로 지켜보았는데, 자네가 카밀라에게 한마디도 하지 않는 것을 보았네. 그러니 나는 이전에도 자네가 그녀에게 말을 건넨 적이 없다고 결론짓네. 그리고 그것이, 의심할 바 없이 그러하듯, 사실이라면, 어찌하여 자네는 나를 속이며, 어찌하여 자네는 술수를 부려 내가 내 욕망을 이룰 수단을 찾을 길을 앗아 가려 든단 말인가?\"\n\n안셀모는 더는 말하지 않았으나, 로타리오를 수치와 당혹으로 뒤덮기에는 충분한 말을 한 셈이었으니, 그는 거짓말이 들통난 탓에 제 명예가 건드려진 듯 느끼고는, 안셀모에게 맹세하기를, 만일 그가 궁금하여 지켜본다면 알게 되겠지만, 그 순간부터 어떤 속임수도 없이 그를 만족시키는 일에 전념하겠다 했으며, 다만 그가 그런 수고를 할 것까지는 없으니, 자기가 그를 만족시키려 들이는 정성이 그의 마음에서 온갖 의심을 걷어 낼 것이라 했다. 안셀모는 그를 믿었고, 그에게 더 자유롭고 들킬 염려가 덜한 기회를 마련해 주고자, 도시에서 멀지 않은 마을에 사는 자기 벗의 집으로 가, 여드레 동안 집을 비우기로 마음먹었다. 그리고 카밀라에게 자기가 떠나는 까닭을 더 그럴듯하게 둘러대려고, 그 벗으로 하여금 자기에게 매우 간곡한 초대장을 보내도록 일을 꾸몄다.\n\n불행하고 앞 못 보는 안셀모여, 그대는 무슨 짓을 하고 있는가, 무엇을 꾀하고 있는가, 무엇을 꾸미고 있는가? 곰곰이 생각해 보라. 그대는 그대 자신을 거슬러 일하고 있고, 그대 자신의 불명예를 꾀하고 있으며, 그대 자신의 파멸을 꾸미고 있음을. 그대의 아내 카밀라는 미덕 있고, 그대는 평화롭고 고요하게 그녀를 가졌으며, 아무도 그대의 행복을 넘보지 않고, 그녀의 생각은 그대 집의 담장 너머로 떠도는 일이 없으며, 그대는 그녀에게 지상의 하늘이요, 그녀의 바람의 대상이요, 그녀의 욕망의 성취요, 그녀가 자기 뜻을 재는 자이니, 만사에서 그것을 그대의 뜻과 하늘의 뜻에 맞추는도다. 그러하거늘, 그녀의 명예와 아름다움과 미덕과 정숙함이라는 광맥이 그대에게 수고 없이 그것이 품은 온갖 부와 그대가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 내어 주는데, 어찌하여 그대는 새로운 광맥을, 알지 못하던 새 보화를 찾아 땅을 파헤쳐, 그것이 오직 그녀의 약한 천성이라는 무른 받침대에 얹혀 있을 뿐인데, 모든 것의 무너짐을 무릅쓰려 하는가? 곰곰이 생각해 보라. 불가능한 것을 구하는 자에게는 가능한 것마저 마땅히 거두어들여질 수 있음을. 어느 시인이 더 잘 표현했으니, 그는 이렇게 말했다.\n\n죽음 속에서 생명을 찾는 것이 내 일이요, 병 속에서 건강을 나는 찾으며, 감옥 속에서 자유의 숨결을, 배신자들 속에서 충성을 찾노라. 하여 내게 은총도 은혜도 베풀기를 언제나 마다하는 운명은, 결코 있을 수 없는 것을 내가 바라기에, 있을 법한 것마저 내게 거절하는도다.\n\n이튿날 안셀모는 마을로 떠나면서 카밀라에게 일러두기를, 자기가 없는 동안 로타리오가 집을 돌보고 그녀와 함께 식사하러 올 것이니, 그를 자기 대하듯 대접하라고 했다. 카밀라는 신중하고 올바른 마음을 지닌 여자가 으레 그러하듯, 남편이 자기에게 남긴 분부에 마음이 괴로워, 자기가 없는 동안 누군가가 식탁에서 그의 자리를 차지하는 것은 온당치 않다고 그에게 일러 주면서, 만일 그가 자기더러 집안을 꾸려 낼 수 있다는 믿음이 없어 이러는 것이라면, 이번 한 번 자기를 시험해 보라고, 그러면 자기가 더 큰 책임도 감당할 수 있음을 몸소 알게 되리라 했다. 안셀모는 답하기를, 그렇게 하는 것이 자기 뜻이니, 그녀는 그저 순순히 따르기만 하면 된다고 했다. 카밀라는 비록 제 뜻과는 어긋나나 그리하겠노라 했다.\n\n안셀모가 떠나고, 이튿날 로타리오가 그의 집으로 왔으니, 카밀라는 그를 다정하고 정숙한 환대로 맞이했으나, 결코 로타리오가 자기를 단둘이서 보도록 두지 않았으니, 그녀는 늘 남녀 하인들을, 특히 레오넬라라는 자기 시녀를 곁에 두었던 까닭이다. 그녀는 그 시녀를 무척 아꼈는데(어릴 적부터 친정에서 함께 자란 사이였기에), 안셀모와 결혼한 뒤에도 그녀를 곁에 두고 있었다. 처음 사흘 동안 로타리오는 그녀에게 말을 건네지 않았으니, 상보를 걷고 하인들이 서둘러 식사하러 물러갔을 때라면 그럴 수도 있었으련만, 그러지 않았다. 카밀라의 분부가 그러했기 때문이다. 아니 그뿐 아니라, 레오넬라는 카밀라보다 먼저 식사하고 결코 그녀 곁을 떠나지 말라는 분부를 받았던 것이다. 그러나 레오넬라는 제 마음에 더 드는 다른 일에 생각이 쏠려 있었고, 자기 즐거움을 위해 그 시간과 기회가 탐났던지라, 늘 제 안주인의 명을 따르지는 않았고, 오히려 그러라는 분부라도 받은 양 둘을 단둘이 남겨 두곤 했다. 그러나 카밀라의 정숙한 몸가짐과, 차분한 낯빛과, 의젓한 자태는 로타리오의 혀를 묶어 두기에 충분했다. 그러나 카밀라의 그 많은 미덕이 로타리오의 혀에 침묵을 강요하는 데 미친 그 영향은 두 사람 모두에게 해로운 것이 되고 말았으니, 그의 혀가 잠잠한 동안 그의 생각은 분주하여, 카밀라의 선함과 아름다움의 빼어남을 하나하나 한가로이 곱씹을 수 있었던 까닭이다. 그것은 살로 된 가슴은 말할 것도 없고 대리석상이라도 사랑으로 데우기에 족한 매력이었다. 로타리오는 그녀에게 말을 건넬 수도 있었을 그때에 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀가 얼마나 사랑받을 만한 여인인지 생각했다. 그리하여 그 헤아림은 안셀모에 대한 그의 충정을 차츰차츰 갉아먹기 시작했고, 그는 수천 번이나 도시를 떠나, 안셀모가 결코 자기를 보지 못하고 자기도 카밀라를 보지 못할 곳으로 가 버릴까 생각했다. 그러나 이미 그녀를 바라보는 데서 찾는 그 기쁨이 끼어들어 그를 단단히 붙들었다. 그는 자신을 다잡고, 카밀라를 바라보는 데서 얻는 그 즐거움을 물리치고 억누르려 안간힘을 썼다. 혼자 있을 때면 자기 약함을 탓하며 스스로를 못된 벗이라, 아니 못된 그리스도교도라 일컬었다. 그러다가는 그 문제를 따져 보고 자신을 안셀모와 견주어 보았는데, 늘 이르는 결론은 안셀모의 어리석음과 무모함이 자기 신의 없음보다 더 고약했다는 것이며, 만일 자기 의도를 사람 앞에서만큼이나 하느님 앞에서도 손쉽게 변명할 수 있다면, 자기 잘못에 대해 어떤 벌도 두려워할 이유가 없다는 것이었다.\n\n요컨대 카밀라의 아름다움과 선함이, 앞 못 보는 남편이 그의 손에 쥐여 준 기회와 어우러져, 로타리오의 충직함을 무너뜨리고 말았다. 그리하여 그는 자기 마음 기우는 바가 이끄는 그 대상 외에는 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 않게 되었고, 안셀모가 떠난 지 사흘이 지나, 그 사흘 동안 자기 정념과 끊임없는 싸움을 벌인 끝에, 어찌나 격렬하고 뜨거운 말로 카밀라에게 사랑을 호소하기 시작했던지, 그녀는 놀라움에 압도되어, 그저 제자리에서 일어나 그에게 한마디 대꾸도 없이 제 방으로 물러갈 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 사랑과 더불어 늘 솟아나는 그 희망은, 이 물리치는 태도에도 로타리오 안에서 수그러들지 않았고, 오히려 카밀라를 향한 그의 정념은 더해 갔으며, 그녀는 그에게서 결코 예상치 못한 것을 발견하고는 어찌할 바를 몰랐다. 그리고 그에게 다시 자기에게 말을 건넬 틈이나 기회를 주는 것은 안전하지도 옳지도 않다고 여겨, 바로 그날 밤 자기 하인 하나를 시켜 안셀모에게 편지를 보내기로 마음먹었으니, 거기서 그녀는 그에게 다음과 같은 말을 적어 보냈다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIV.\n\n흔히들 군대는 장수가 없으면 볼품없고 성은 성주가 없으면 초라하다고 말하는데, 저는 갓 결혼한 젊은 아내가 남편 없이 지내는 모습이야말로, 그럴 만한 아주 합당한 사정이 없는 한 그보다 더 초라한 법이라 말하고 싶습니다. 저는 당신 없이 지내는 것이 너무도 괴롭고 이 이별을 견딜 힘조차 없어, 당신이 어서 돌아오지 않으신다면 당신의 집을 지킬 사람 하나 없이 비워 두는 한이 있더라도 친정으로 가서 위안을 구해야만 할 듯합니다. 당신이 제게 남겨 두신 그 사람으로 말하자면, 정녕 그런 호칭을 받을 자격이 있는지 모르겠으나, 제 생각에 그는 당신과 관계된 일보다 제 한 몸의 즐거움에 더 마음을 두는 사람이옵니다. 당신은 분별 있는 분이시니 더는 말씀드릴 필요가 없을 것이며, 또한 제가 더 말하는 것이 온당치도 않을 것입니다.\n\n안셀모는 이 편지를 받고서, 로타리오가 이미 그 임무에 착수했으며 카밀라가 그가 바라던 대로 그에게 응답했음이 틀림없다고 헤아렸다. 그리고 그러한 소식에 더없이 기뻐하며, 자신이 곧 돌아갈 터이니 무슨 일이 있어도 집을 떠나지 말라는 전갈을 그녀에게 보냈다. 카밀라는 안셀모의 답신에 깜짝 놀랐으니, 그 답신이 그녀를 전보다 더 큰 곤혹 속으로 몰아넣었기 때문이다. 그녀는 제 집에 머물 엄두도, 친정으로 갈 엄두도 나지 않았다. 머무르자니 그녀의 정절이 위태로웠고, 떠나자니 남편의 명령을 거스르는 일이었다. 마침내 그녀는 자신에게 더 나쁜 길, 곧 머무르는 쪽을 택하기로 하고, 하인들에게 입길에 오를 빌미를 주지 않으려 로타리오 앞에서 달아나지 않기로 마음먹었다. 그러고는 남편에게 그런 편지를 쓴 것을 후회하기 시작했으니, 혹여 로타리오가 자신에게서 어떤 경박함을 알아채고 그로 인해 그가 마땅히 지켜야 할 예의를 저버리게 되었다고 남편이 상상할까 두려웠던 것이다. 그러나 자신의 올곧음을 믿었기에 그녀는 하느님과 자신의 정숙한 뜻에 모든 것을 맡겼으며, 그것으로써 로타리오의 온갖 유혹에 묵묵히 맞서되, 남편을 어떤 다툼이나 곤경에 끌어들이지 않으려 그에게는 아무 말도 하지 않으리라 바랐다. 심지어 그녀는, 안셀모가 무엇 때문에 그런 편지를 쓰게 되었느냐고 물어 올 때 로타리오를 어떻게 변호해 줄지까지 궁리하기 시작했다. 이처럼 분별 있다거나 실효가 있다기보다는 차라리 갸륵하다 할 만한 결심들을 품은 채, 그녀는 이튿날 로타리오의 말에 귀를 기울이며 머물렀는데, 그가 어찌나 끈질기게 구애하던지 카밀라의 굳센 마음이 흔들리기 시작했고, 로타리오의 눈물과 호소가 그녀의 가슴속에 일깨운 어떤 애틋한 연민의 기색이 그녀의 두 눈에 드러나지 않도록 막아 내느라 그녀의 정절은 진땀을 빼야 했다. 로타리오는 이 모든 것을 눈여겨보았고, 그것이 그를 더욱 불타오르게 했다. 요컨대 그는 안셀모의 부재가 시간과 기회를 주는 동안 이 요새의 공략에 박차를 가해야 한다고 느꼈으며, 그리하여 그녀의 미모를 찬미함으로써 그녀의 자존심을 공략했으니, 아첨하는 혀에 실린 허영심만큼 아름다운 여인들의 허영이라는 성루를 그토록 빠르게 무너뜨리고 허무는 것은 없기 때문이다. 실로 그는 더없는 집요함으로, 카밀라가 청동으로 빚어졌다 한들 끝내 무너지고 말았을 그런 공성 기계로 그녀의 순결이라는 바위를 허물어 갔다. 그는 울었고, 애원했고, 약속했고, 아첨했고, 졸라 댔고, 어찌나 절절하고 진실해 보이는 태도로 꾸며 댔던지, 끝내 카밀라의 정숙한 결심을 무너뜨리고 자신이 가장 기대하지 못했으면서도 가장 갈망하던 승리를 거두었다. 카밀라는 굴복했고, 카밀라는 쓰러졌다. 그러나 로타리오의 우정이 굳건히 버티지 못한 것이 무에 그리 놀랄 일이겠는가? 이는 우리에게, 사랑의 정념이란 오직 그것에서 달아남으로써만 이겨 낼 수 있으며, 그토록 막강한 적과는 그 누구도 싸움을 벌여서는 안 된다는 명백한 증거이니, 그의 인간적인 힘을 이겨 내자면 신성한 힘이 필요하기 때문이다. 안주인의 약점을 안 사람은 레오넬라뿐이었으니, 이 거짓된 두 벗이자 새로운 연인은 그것을 도무지 감출 수 없었던 것이다. 로타리오는 안셀모가 무엇을 노렸는지, 또 안셀모가 그에게 그런 결과에 이를 기회를 마련해 주었다는 사실을 카밀라에게 말하고 싶지 않았으니, 그녀가 그의 사랑을 하찮게 여겨, 그가 그녀에게 구애한 것이 우연이며 본의가 아니라 제 뜻에서 비롯된 것이 아니라고 생각할까 염려했기 때문이다.\n\n며칠 뒤 안셀모는 제 집으로 돌아왔으나, 그토록 가벼이 다루면서도 그토록 높이 떠받들던 그것을 자신이 잃었음을 알아채지 못했다. 그는 곧장 로타리오를 보러 가서 집에 있는 그를 만났다. 두 사람은 서로 끌어안았고, 안셀모는 자신의 삶이냐 죽음이냐를 가를 소식을 물었다.\n\n\"내가 자네에게 전할 소식은 말일세, 안셀모, 나의 벗이여,\" 로타리오가 말했다. \"자네가 모든 어진 아내의 본보기요 면류관이 될 만한 아내를 두었다는 것일세. 내가 그녀에게 건넨 말들은 바람결에 흩날려 가 버렸고, 내 약속은 멸시당했으며, 내 선물은 거절당했고, 내가 흘린 거짓 눈물은 노골적인 비웃음으로 돌아왔다네. 요컨대 카밀라가 모든 아름다움의 정수이듯, 그녀는 또한 순결이 깃들어 사는 보고이자, 한 여인에게 칭송과 명예와 행복을 안겨 줄 수 있는 온갖 미덕과 더불어 온유함과 정숙함이 머무는 곳일세. 자네 돈을 도로 가져가게, 나의 벗이여. 여기 있네, 손댈 일조차 없었으니, 카밀라의 정절은 선물이나 약속 따위의 천한 것에는 굴하지 않는다네. 만족하게, 안셀모, 더는 시험하려 들지 말게. 그리고 자네가 여자들에 대해 품고 있고 또 품을 수 있는 그 온갖 의심과 의혹의 바다를 발도 적시지 않고 건넜으니, 다시 새로운 곤혹의 깊은 대양으로 뛰어들려 하지도 말고, 또 다른 키잡이를 데려와 하늘이 자네에게 이 세상의 바다를 건너라고 허락하신 그 배의 견고함과 굳셈을 시험해 보려 하지도 말게. 도리어 이제 자네는 항구에 무사히 들었다 여기고, 건전한 성찰이라는 닻으로 자신을 단단히 매어 두고서, 이 땅의 어떤 고귀함으로도 면할 수 없는 그 빚을 갚으라는 부름을 받을 때까지 평안히 쉬게나.\"\n\n안셀모는 로타리오의 말에 완전히 흡족해하며, 그것이 마치 신탁에서 나온 말이기라도 한 듯 온전히 믿었다. 그럼에도 그는, 단지 호기심과 즐길 거리를 위해서라도 그 일을 그만두지 말아 달라고 그에게 청했다. 다만 이후로는 전처럼 진지한 노력을 기울일 필요는 없으며, 그가 바라는 것은 그저 클로리스라는 이름으로 그녀를 찬미하는 시 몇 편을 써 달라는 것뿐이라고 했다. 자신이 카밀라에게, 자기가 어떤 귀부인을 사랑하고 있는데 그녀의 정숙함에 걸맞은 예의를 갖춰 찬미할 수 있도록 그 부인에게 클로리스라는 이름을 붙여 주었노라 일러 두겠다는 것이었다. 그러면서 만일 로타리오가 그 시를 쓰는 수고를 들이기 싫다면 자신이 직접 짓겠노라고 했다.\n\n\"그럴 것까지는 없네,\" 로타리오가 말했다. \"내가 시신(詩神)들과 그리 척진 사이는 아니어서, 그들이 한 해에 한두 번쯤은 나를 찾아오니 말일세. 자네는 카밀라에게 내가 가짜 연애를 하고 있다고 자네가 일러 두기로 한 그 일을 전해 두게. 시로 말하자면 내가 지어 줄 터이니, 그 주제에 걸맞을 만큼 훌륭하지는 못하더라도 적어도 내가 지을 수 있는 최선의 것은 되리라.\" 분별없는 자와 배신하는 자, 이 두 벗 사이에 이런 취지의 약조가 맺어졌고, 안셀모는 제 집으로 돌아가 카밀라에게, 그녀가 진작 묻지 않은 것을 이상히 여기던 바로 그 물음을 던졌으니, 곧 무엇이 그녀로 하여금 자신에게 보낸 그 편지를 쓰게 했느냐는 것이었다. 카밀라는, 로타리오가 그가 집에 있을 때보다 다소 거리낌 없이 자신을 쳐다보는 듯이 느껴졌으나, 이제는 오해가 풀려 그것이 그저 제 상상에 지나지 않았다고 믿게 되었다고 답했으니, 로타리오가 이제는 자신과 마주치거나 단둘이 있는 것을 피하기 때문이라 했다. 안셀모는 그녀에게 그런 의심일랑 마음 놓아도 좋다고 일렀으니, 자신이 알기로 로타리오는 시내의 어느 지체 높은 처녀와 사랑에 빠져 그녀를 클로리스라는 이름으로 노래하고 있으며, 설령 그렇지 않다 해도 그의 신의와 두 사람의 두터운 우정으로 보아 두려워할 것이 조금도 없다고 했다. 그런데 만일 카밀라가 이 클로리스에 대한 사랑이 거짓이며, 로타리오가 때때로 카밀라 자신을 찬미하는 말을 입에 올릴 수 있도록 그 일을 안셀모에게 미리 일러 둔 것이라는 사실을 로타리오에게서 진작 전해 듣지 못했더라면, 그녀는 틀림없이 질투의 절망스러운 올가미에 빠지고 말았으리라. 그러나 미리 귀띔을 받은 터라 그녀는 그 놀라운 소식을 아무 불안 없이 받아들였다.\n\n이튿날 세 사람이 식탁에 둘러앉았을 때 안셀모는 로타리오에게, 연인 클로리스를 위해 지은 것 가운데 무엇이든 한 편 읊어 달라고 청했으니, 카밀라가 그녀를 알지 못하므로 무슨 말을 해도 거리낄 것이 없으리라는 것이었다.\n\n\"그녀가 카밀라 부인을 안다 해도,\" 로타리오가 답했다. \"나는 아무것도 감추지 않을 걸세. 연인이 제 여인의 아름다움을 찬미하고 그녀의 냉정함을 탓하는 것은 그녀의 고운 이름에 누를 끼치는 일이 아니니 말일세. 어쨌든 내가 말할 수 있는 것은, 어제 이 클로리스의 무정함을 두고 소네트 한 편을 지었는데 그것이 이러하다는 것일세.\"\n\n소네트\n한밤중, 고요 속에, 더 복된 인간들의 두 눈이 달콤한 잠으로 감길 적에, 헤아릴 길 없는 내 슬픔의 지친 사연은 클로리스에게로, 또 하늘로 솟아오르네. 그리고 돌아온 낮의 빛이 동녘의 문을 장밋빛으로 물들일 때면, 내 비탄은 줄지 않은 기세로 끊긴 가락과 타는 한숨이 되어 흐르네. 태양이 별들로 둘러싸인 옥좌에 오르고, 대지 위에 한낮의 빛살을 쏟아부을 적에, 정오는 그저 내 통곡과 눈물을 새로이 할 뿐. 그리고 밤과 더불어 또다시 내 신음이 솟아오르나, 내 고뇌 속에서 줄곧 내게 여겨지기로는 하늘도 클로리스도 듣지 않는 듯하구나.\n\n소네트는 카밀라를 흡족하게 했고, 안셀모는 더욱 그러했으니, 그는 그것을 칭찬하며 이토록 분명한 진심에 아무 응답도 하지 않는 그 여인은 지나치게 무정하다고 말했다. 그러자 카밀라가 말했다. \"그렇다면 사랑에 빠진 시인들이 하는 말은 다 사실인가요?\"\n\n\"시인으로서야 그들이 진실을 말하지는 않지요,\" 로타리오가 답했다. \"허나 연인으로서 그들은 그 표현이 모자란 만큼이나 진실하답니다.\"\n\n\"그건 의심할 여지가 없지,\" 안셀모가 거들었으니, 카밀라 앞에서 로타리오의 생각을 받쳐 주고 떠받들고자 애가 탄 것이었다. 그러나 카밀라는 그의 속셈에는 아랑곳하지 않았으니, 그만큼 그녀는 로타리오에게 깊이 빠져 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 그에게 속한 것이라면 무엇이든 기뻐하고, 그의 생각과 글이 자신을 향한 것이며 자신이 바로 진짜 클로리스임을 알았기에, 그녀는 그에게 혹 기억나는 다른 소네트나 시가 있거든 한 편 더 읊어 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"있긴 하지요,\" 로타리오가 답했다. \"다만 첫 번째 것만큼 훌륭하지는, 아니 더 정확히 말하면 그만큼 형편없지는 않은 듯합니다. 허나 부인께서 쉽게 판단하실 수 있을 터이니, 그것이 이러합니다.\"\n\n소네트\n나는 아노라, 내 운명이 정해졌음을. 죽음은 내게 그대, 은혜 모르는 미인이여, 내 마음이 그대 향한 사랑을 뉘우치기 전에 그대 발치에 쓰러진 나를 보게 되리란 것만큼 확실하구나. 설령 망각 속에 파묻혀 목숨도, 명성도, 은총도 빼앗긴다 한들, 거기서조차 모두가 보도록 내 가슴 깊이 새겨진 그대의 모습을 내가 지니고 있음이 드러나리라. 이것을 나는 어떤 거룩한 성물처럼 소중히 여기노니, 내 진실이 불러온 운명에서 나를 건지려 함이라. 그 진실은 그대의 모진 마음에서 그 기세를 얻은 것이로다. 아아, 가엾어라, 어둑한 하늘 아래, 자취 없는 대양 위를 위태로이 항해하는 자여, 정다운 항구도, 길잡이 북극성도 보이지 않는 그곳을.\n\n안셀모는 이 두 번째 소네트도 첫 번째 것을 칭찬했듯 칭찬했고, 이렇게 그는 자신을 옭아매며 제 불명예를 확실히 다지는 그 사슬에 한 고리 또 한 고리를 잇따라 보태어 갔다. 로타리오가 그를 가장 욕되게 하고 있을 때 그는 도리어 그에게 자신이 가장 명예롭다고 말했으며, 그리하여 카밀라가 제 타락의 나락으로 한 발짝씩 내려갈 때마다, 그의 생각으로는 그녀가 미덕과 고운 명성의 정점을 향해 한 발짝씩 올라가고 있었던 것이다.\n\n어느 날 카밀라는 하녀와 단둘이 있게 되자 그녀에게 이렇게 말했다. \"레오넬라, 내 사랑하는 아이야, 생각하면 부끄럽구나. 내가 어찌 그리 스스로를 가벼이 여겼던지, 적어도 얼마간의 시간이라도 들이게 하여 로타리오가 나를 온전히 차지하도록 하지 않고, 그토록 빨리 내 발로 그에게 내주고 말았으니 말이다. 그가 내게 끼친 그 거역할 수 없는 힘은 헤아리지 않은 채, 내 무름이나 경박함을 나쁘게 여길까 두렵구나.\"\n\n\"마님, 그런 일로 심려치 마옵소서,\" 레오넬라가 말했다. \"정녕 값지고 귀히 여길 만한 것이라면, 그것을 빨리 내준다 하여 그 값어치가 깎이거나 덜 귀해지는 법은 없사옵니다. 아니, 외려 빨리 주는 자는 두 번 주는 것이라고들 하지 않사옵니까.\"\n\n\"허나 또 이런 말도 있지,\" 카밀라가 말했다. \"값싸게 얻은 것은 덜 귀히 여겨진다고.\"\n\n\"그 말은 마님의 경우에는 들어맞지 않사옵니다,\" 레오넬라가 답했다. \"사랑이란, 제가 듣기로는, 때로는 날고 때로는 걷는 것이어서, 이 사람에게는 내달리고 저 사람에게는 더디 가며, 누구는 식히고 누구는 불태우며, 누구에게는 상처를 입히고 누구는 죽이옵니다. 또 제 욕망의 길을 떠나는 바로 그 순간에 그것을 마치고 끝내기도 하며, 아침에 요새를 에워싸서는 밤이면 그것을 함락하기도 하니, 그것을 당해 낼 힘이란 없는 까닭이옵니다. 그러니 마님께서 무엇을 겁내시고 무엇을 두려워하시옵니까, 사랑이 나리의 부재를 마님을 굴복시킬 도구로 골랐으니 로타리오 님께도 똑같은 일이 닥쳤을 것이 분명한데 말이옵니다. 게다가 그때 사랑이 작정한 바를 그 자리에서 이루는 것은 절대로 필요한 일이었으니, 안셀모 나리께서 돌아오시어 그 존재로 일이 마무리되지 못한 채 남겨지도록 만들 틈을 주어서는 안 되었기 때문이옵니다. 사랑이 제 뜻을 이루는 데에 기회보다 더 나은 일꾼은 없으며, 사랑은 제 온갖 위업에서, 특히 그 시작에서 기회를 십분 이용하옵니다. 이 모든 것을 저 자신이 잘 아니, 풍문으로보다는 경험으로 아는 것이옵니다. 그리고 언젠가, 마님, 제가 이 일에 대해 마님을 깨우쳐 드리겠나이다. 저 또한 마님과 같은 살과 피를 지닌 사람이니 말이옵니다. 더구나, 카밀라 마님, 마님께서 그리 빨리 자신을 내주거나 굴복하신 것이 아니오라, 먼저 로타리오 님의 온 영혼을 그 두 눈에서, 그 한숨에서, 그 말과 약속과 선물에서 보셨고, 그것으로, 또 그분의 훌륭한 자질로 그분이 마님의 사랑을 받을 만큼 얼마나 합당한 분인지를 알아보셨던 것이옵니다. 사정이 이러하오니 그런 깐깐하고 새침한 생각으로 마님의 마음을 어지럽히지 마옵시고, 로타리오 님께서도 마님께서 그분을 귀히 여기시듯 마님을 귀히 여기심을 확신하시옵소서. 또 마님께서 사랑의 올가미에 걸리셨으되, 마님을 사로잡은 것이 값지고 빼어난 사람이며, 그것도 참된 연인이라면 갖추어야 한다는 그 네 가지 S뿐 아니라 알파벳 전부를 갖춘 사람임을 흡족히 여기시고 마음 놓으시옵소서. 제 말을 들어 보시면 제가 그것을 줄줄 외울 수 있음을 아시게 될 것이옵니다. 제 눈과 생각으로 그분은 다정하고(Amiable), 용감하고(Brave), 정중하고(Courteous), 출중하고(Distinguished), 우아하고(Elegant), 애틋하고(Fond), 쾌활하고(Gay), 명예롭고(Honourable), 빛나고(Illustrious), 충직하고(Loyal), 남자답고(Manly), 고결하고(Noble), 솔직하고(Open), 점잖고(Polite), 재치 있고(Quickwitted), 부유하며(Rich), 그리고 그 속담에 나오는 S들이 있고, 그다음으로 다정하고(Tender), 진실하옵니다(Veracious). X는 거친 글자라 그분께 어울리지 않사옵고, Y는 이미 나왔으며, Z는 마님의 명예를 위해 열성을 다하는(Zealous) 것이옵니다.\"\n* 네 가지 S: 당시 스페인에서 참된 연인이 갖추어야 한다고 일컬어지던 네 덕목으로, 모두 S로 시작하는 단어였다(현명하다sabio, 한결같다solo, 진중하다solícito, 비밀을 지킨다secreto 등으로 전해진다).\n\n카밀라는 하녀의 알파벳에 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 그녀가 스스로 말한 것보다 연애에 더 노련함을 알아챘으니, 레오넬라도 이를 인정하여 같은 도시의 좋은 가문 출신 젊은이와 연애한 일이 있노라 카밀라에게 털어놓았다. 카밀라는 이 일로 마음이 편치 않았으니, 그것이 자신의 명예를 위태롭게 하는 빌미가 될까 두려워, 그 정분이 말로만 그친 것인지를 물었다. 그러자 레오넬라는 부끄러운 기색이라곤 거의 없이 뻔뻔스럽게 그 이상이었노라 답했으니, 안주인들의 경솔함이 하녀들을 파렴치하게 만드는 것은 분명한 일이어서, 하녀들은 안주인이 그릇된 발을 내딛는 것을 보면 저 자신이 엇나가는 것도, 그것이 알려지는 것도 대수롭지 않게 여기기 때문이다. 카밀라가 할 수 있는 일이라곤, 레오넬라에게 자신의 행실에 관해서는 그녀가 연인이라 부르는 자에게 아무 말도 하지 말 것이며, 그녀 자신의 일도 안셀모나 로타리오의 귀에 들어가지 않도록 은밀히 처신하라고 당부하는 것뿐이었다. 레오넬라는 그러겠노라 했으나, 그 약속을 지킨 방식이라는 것이, 자신으로 말미암아 평판을 잃게 되리라는 카밀라의 우려를 도리어 굳혀 주는 식이었다. 이 막돼먹고 뻔뻔한 레오넬라는 안주인의 행실이 예전 같지 않음을 알아채자마자, 안주인이 제 연인을 본다 한들 감히 그를 들춰내지 못하리라 믿고, 대담하게도 제 연인을 집 안에 끌어들였던 것이다. 안주인들의 죄는 여러 폐해 가운데 이런 화근도 불러오니, 곧 안주인들이 제 하인들의 노예가 되어 제 방종과 타락을 감추지 않을 수 없게 만드는 것이다. 카밀라가 바로 그러했으니, 그녀는 레오넬라가 집 안 어느 방에서 제 연인과 함께 있는 것을 한 번도 아니고 여러 번 알아챘으면서도, 감히 그녀를 꾸짖기는커녕 그를 숨겨 줄 기회를 마련해 주고 온갖 어려움을 치워 주었으니, 행여 남편의 눈에 그가 띌까 함이었다. 그러나 어느 날 동틀 무렵 그가 밖으로 빠져나가는 것을 로타리오에게 들키는 것까지는 막을 수 없었다. 로타리오는 그가 누구인지 알지 못해 처음에는 그를 유령으로 여겼으나, 망토로 얼굴을 가린 채 조심조심 신중하게 몸을 숨기며 서둘러 사라지는 것을 보고는 그 어리석은 생각을 떨쳐 버리고 다른 생각을 품게 되었으니, 카밀라가 방책을 마련해 내지 않았더라면 그것은 모든 것을 파멸로 이끌었을 생각이었다. 그처럼 부적절한 시각에 안셀모의 집에서 나오는 이 사내가 레오넬라 때문에 거기 들어왔을 수도 있다는 생각은 로타리오에게 떠오르지 않았고, 레오넬라라는 사람이 있다는 것조차 그는 기억하지 못했다. 그가 떠올린 것은 오직, 카밀라가 자신에게 경박하고 무르게 굴었듯이 다른 사내에게도 그러했으리라는 것뿐이었다. 길을 그르친 여인의 죄는 이런 더한 벌까지 함께 불러오니, 곧 그녀가 그 구애와 설득에 굴복해 준 바로 그 사내에게조차 그녀의 명예가 의심을 받게 되는 것이다. 그는 그녀가 다른 사내들에게는 더 쉽게 몸을 내주었으리라 믿고, 제 마음에 떠오르는 온갖 의심을 고스란히 믿어 버린다. 이 고비에서 로타리오의 분별력은 죄다 그를 저버린 듯했고, 그의 신중한 격언들은 모조리 기억에서 빠져나가 버린 듯했다. 한 번도 이성적으로 헤아려 보지 않은 채, 다시 두말없이, 제 마음을 갉아먹는 질투의 광기로 눈이 멀어 조급해진 그는, 아무런 잘못도 저지르지 않은 카밀라에게 복수하지 못해 안달이 나, 안셀모가 잠자리에서 일어나기도 전에 그에게 달려가 이렇게 말했다.\n\"알아 두게, 안셀모, 며칠 전부터 나는 자신과 씨름하며, 이제는 더 이상 자네에게 감출 수도, 감추는 것이 옳지도 않은 것을 자네에게 알리지 않으려 안간힘을 써 왔다네. 알아 두게, 카밀라의 요새가 함락되어 내 뜻에 따를 채비가 되어 있다네. 그리고 내가 이 사실을 자네에게 알리기를 더뎌한 것은, 그것이 그녀의 어떤 가벼운 변덕에 지나지 않는지, 아니면 그녀가 나를 떠보아 자네의 허락을 받아 내가 그녀에게 건네기 시작한 그 사랑이 진심에서 우러난 것인지를 알아내려는 것인지 살펴보고자 함이었다네. 또 나는, 그녀가 마땅히 그래야 할 사람이고 우리 둘 다 그녀를 그렇게 믿어 온 사람이라면, 진작 자네에게 내 구애를 일러바쳤으리라 생각했네. 그런데 그녀가 그러기를 미루는 것을 보니, 다음번에 자네가 집을 비우면 자네의 보석을 간직해 둔 골방에서 나와 밀회하겠노라 내게 한 그 약속이 참말이라 믿게 되었네(카밀라가 그곳에서 그와 만나곤 했던 것은 사실이었다). 허나 나는 자네가 황급히 복수에 나서기를 바라지는 않네. 그 죄가 아직은 마음속에서만 저질러진 것이며, 카밀라의 마음은 어쩌면 지금부터 약속한 그 시각 사이에 바뀌어 그 자리에 뉘우침이 싹틀 수도 있기 때문일세. 자네가 이제껏 늘 내 충고를 전부든 일부든 따라 왔으니, 이제 내가 일러 줄 이것도 따르고 새겨 두게. 그리하여 그릇됨 없이, 충분히 숙고하여, 무엇이 최선의 길로 보이는지 자네 스스로 흡족하게 판단할 수 있도록 말일세. 다른 때처럼 이삼 일 집을 비우는 척하고, 그 골방에 몸을 숨기게나. 그곳의 벽걸이며 다른 물건들이 자네가 숨기에 안성맞춤이니 말일세. 그러면 카밀라의 속셈이 무엇인지 자네는 자네 눈으로, 나는 내 눈으로 보게 될 걸세. 만일 그것이 죄지은 속셈이라면 — 바라기보다는 두려워해야 할 일이지만 — 자네는 침묵과 신중함과 분별로 자네 자신이 자네에게 가해진 그릇됨을 응징하는 도구가 될 수 있을 걸세.\"\n\n안셀모는 로타리오의 말에 어안이 벙벙해지고, 압도되고, 망연자실했으니, 그 말이 그가 그것을 들으리라 가장 기대하지 않던 때에 들이닥친 까닭이었다. 그도 그럴 것이 그는 이제 카밀라가 로타리오의 거짓 공세를 물리치고 승리를 거두었다고 여겨, 그 승리의 영광을 누리기 시작하던 참이었던 것이다. 그는 한동안 말없이 눈길을 땅에 박은 채 가만히 있더니, 마침내 이렇게 말했다. \"자네는, 로타리오, 내가 자네의 우정에 기대했던 그대로 처신했네. 나는 모든 일에서 자네 충고를 따르겠네. 자네 뜻대로 하게, 그리고 이토록 뜻밖의 상황에서 마땅히 지켜야 할 만큼 이 비밀을 지켜 주게.\"\n\n로타리오는 그에게 약속했으나, 그를 떠난 뒤로는 자신이 그에게 한 말을 송두리째 후회하게 되었으니, 자신이 얼마나 어리석게 굴었는지를 깨달은 것이다. 카밀라에게 그보다 덜 잔인하고 덜 모욕적인 어떤 방식으로도 복수할 수 있었을 터이기 때문이다. 그는 자신의 몰지각함을 저주하고, 성급한 결심을 책망했으나, 그 화근을 돌이키거나 거기서 빠져나갈 손쉬운 방도를 찾을 길을 알지 못했다. 마침내 그는 카밀라에게 모든 것을 털어놓기로 작정했고, 그럴 기회야 모자랄 것이 없었기에 바로 그날 단둘이 있는 그녀를 만났다. 그런데 그녀는 그에게 말할 기회가 생기자마자 이렇게 말했다. \"로타리오, 나의 벗이여, 당신에게 말해야겠어요. 내 마음속에 슬픔이 가득 차서 터질 듯하니, 정말로 터지지 않으면 다행일 거예요. 레오넬라의 뻔뻔함이 이제 극에 달해서, 밤마다 제 정부를 이 집에 숨겨 두고 아침까지 그와 함께 있으니, 내 평판을 희생시키고 있는 거예요. 그처럼 부적절한 시각에 그가 내 집을 나서는 걸 보는 사람이라면 누구든 내 평판을 의심할 수 있을 테니까요. 하지만 나를 가장 괴롭히는 건, 내가 그 애를 벌하거나 꾸짖을 수 없다는 거예요. 그 애가 우리 정분을 알고 있으니 내 입을 옭아매어 그 애 일에 대해 입을 다물게 하고, 그러는 사이 나는 이 일로 무슨 끔찍한 변고가 생길까 두려워하고 있어요.\"\n\n카밀라가 이렇게 말하자 로타리오는 처음에는, 그것이 자신이 본 그 나가던 사내가 그녀의 정부가 아니라 레오넬라의 정부였다는 생각으로 자신을 속이려는 어떤 술책이려니 짐작했다. 그러나 그녀가 어찌나 흐느끼며 괴로워하고 또 자신에게 도와 달라 애원하는지를 보고는 그 말이 참임을 확신하게 되었고, 그 확신이 그의 혼란과 회한을 한층 더 깊게 했다. 그래도 그는 카밀라에게 너무 괴로워 말라고, 자신이 레오넬라의 무엄함을 막을 조치를 취하겠노라 일렀다. 그와 동시에 그는, 사나운 질투의 광기에 휘몰려 자신이 안셀모에게 무슨 말을 했는지, 또 그녀가 그에 대한 신의를 얼마나 지키지 못하는지 거기서 똑똑히 보겠다며 어떻게 골방에 숨기로 일을 꾸몄는지를 그녀에게 털어놓았다. 그러고는 이 광기에 대해 그녀의 용서를 빌고, 그것을 어떻게 바로잡을지, 또 자신의 경솔함이 자신을 빠뜨린 그 얽히고설킨 미궁에서 어떻게 무사히 벗어날지에 대한 그녀의 조언을 청했다. 카밀라는 로타리오의 말을 듣고 경악에 사로잡혔으나, 큰 노여움과 더불어 빼어난 분별로 그를 나무라며 그의 비열한 속셈과 그가 내린 어리석고 해로운 결단을 꾸짖었다. 그러나 여자는 선이든 악이든 본래 남자보다 더 날렵한 기지를 타고났으되, 다만 작정하고 차근차근 따져 들 때면 그것이 곧잘 어그러지는 법이라, 카밀라는 그 자리에서 누가 봐도 돌이킬 수 없을 일을 바로잡을 방도를 떠올리고는, 로타리오에게 이튿날 안셀모가 그가 말한 그 장소에 숨도록 일을 꾸미라고 일렀으니, 그가 숨어 있음으로써 앞으로 아무 두려움 없이 둘이 즐길 수 있는 길을 얻으리라 바란 까닭이었다. 그러고는 자신의 속셈을 그에게 온전히 밝히지는 않은 채, 안셀모가 숨자마자 레오넬라가 부르거든 자신에게로 오라고, 그리고 자신이 그에게 하는 모든 말에 대해, 안셀모가 듣고 있음을 모르는 것처럼 대답하라고 그에게 단단히 일렀다. 로타리오는 자신이 마땅히 해야 할 일을 한층 더 확실하고 빈틈없이 챙길 수 있도록 그녀에게 그 속셈을 온전히 밝혀 달라고 졸랐다.\n\n\"당신에게 말하지만,\" 카밀라가 말했다. \"내가 묻는 말에 대답하는 것 말고는 챙길 일이 전혀 없어요.\" 그녀가 무엇을 하려는지 미리 그에게 설명하고 싶지 않았던 것은, 자신에게 그토록 훌륭해 보이는 생각을 그가 따르려 하지 않고 무언가 다른, 실행하기 어려운 계획을 꾀하거나 짜낼까 염려한 까닭이었다.\n\n이윽고 로타리오가 물러갔고, 이튿날 안셀모는 친구의 시골 별장에 간다는 핑계로 길을 떠났다가 몸을 숨기러 되돌아왔는데, 카밀라와 레오넬라가 기회를 마련해 주려 마음 쓴 덕에 그 일은 어렵잖게 이루어졌다. 그리하여 그는, 제 명예의 급소가 제 눈앞에 발가벗겨지는 것을 보리라 예상하고, 사랑하는 카밀라에게서 자신이 지녔다 여기던 더없는 복을 잃기 직전에 처한 자라면 누구나 느낄 법한 그런 동요에 휩싸인 채 몸을 숨겼다. 안셀모가 은신처에 든 것을 확인한 뒤 카밀라와 레오넬라가 골방으로 들어왔고, 카밀라는 그 안에 발을 들여놓는 순간 깊은 한숨과 함께 말했다. \"아! 사랑하는 레오넬라, 네가 막으려 들까 봐 알리고 싶지 않은 그 일을 내가 하기 전에, 차라리 네게 가져오라 한 안셀모의 단검을 받아 들고 그것으로 이 천한 내 심장을 꿰뚫는 편이 낫지 않겠느냐? 하지만 아니다. 내가 남의 잘못에 대한 벌을 받을 까닭은 없지. 나는 먼저, 로타리오의 그 대담하고 음탕한 눈이 내게서 무엇을 보았기에 제 벗도 내 명예도 아랑곳없이 내게 드러낸 그토록 비열한 속셈을 감히 털어놓을 마음을 먹게 되었는지부터 알아야겠다. 창가로 가서 그를 부르거라, 레오넬라. 그가 제 더러운 계략을 실행하려고 길에서 기다리고 있는 것이 분명하니. 하지만 내 계략은, 잔인할지언정 명예로운 것이니, 그것이 먼저 실행되어야 한다.\"\n\n\"아, 마님,\" 제 배역을 잘 알고 있던 교활한 레오넬라가 말했다. \"이 단검으로 무엇을 하시려는 것이옵니까? 마님 자신의 목숨을, 아니면 로타리오 님의 목숨을 거두려 하시는 것이옵니까? 어느 쪽이든 그것은 마님의 평판과 명성을 잃는 일로 이어질 것이옵니다. 차라리 마님의 그릇됨을 감추시고, 이 사악한 자에게 지금 집에 들어와 우리가 단둘이 있는 것을 보게 할 빌미를 주지 마옵소서. 헤아려 보옵소서, 마님, 우리는 약한 여인들이고 그는 사내이며, 더구나 작심한 사내인 데다, 그토록 비열한 속셈을 품고 눈이 멀어 정념에 내몰려 오는 터라, 어쩌면 마님께서 마님의 뜻을 실행하시기도 전에 그가 마님께 목숨을 거두는 것보다 더 끔찍한 짓을 저지를지도 모르옵니다. 이 파렴치한 자에게 제 집에서 그런 권한을 내주신 제 주인 안셀모 나리께 화가 미치기를! 그리고 마님께서, 제가 짐작하기로 하시려는 대로, 그를 죽이신다 한들, 그가 죽고 나면 우리가 그를 어찌하오리까?\"\n\n\"어찌하다니, 얘야?\" 카밀라가 답했다. \"그를 안셀모가 묻도록 남겨 두면 되지. 제 불명예를 땅속에 감추는 일쯤이야 그에게는 응당 수월한 수고가 될 테니. 그를 불러 오너라, 어서 서둘러라. 내 그릇됨에 복수하기를 미루는 동안의 모든 시간이 내게는 남편에게 마땅히 바쳐야 할 충절을 거스르는 일로 여겨지는구나.\"\n\n안셀모는 이 모든 것을 듣고 있었고, 카밀라가 입에 올리는 말 한마디 한마디가 그의 마음을 돌려놓았다. 그러나 로타리오를 죽이기로 작정했다는 말을 듣자 그의 첫 충동은 밖으로 나가 모습을 드러내어 그런 참사를 막는 것이었다. 그러나 그토록 대담하고 정숙한 결심의 결말을 보고 싶은 마음에 그는 자신을 억눌렀으니, 그 일이 벌어지는 것을 막기에 알맞은 때에 나설 작정이었다. 이때 카밀라가 곁에 있던 침상에 몸을 던지며 까무러쳤고, 레오넬라는 비통하게 울기 시작하며 외쳤다. \"아아, 가엾어라! 이 땅 위 미덕의 꽃이요, 참된 아내들의 면류관이요, 정절의 본보기이신 분이 내 품에서 숨을 거두시도록 정해졌다니!\" 이런 식으로 더 많은 말을 늘어놓았으니, 그 소리를 들은 사람이라면 누구나 그녀를 세상에서 가장 마음 따뜻하고 충직한 하녀로, 그 안주인을 또 한 사람의 박해받는 페넬로페로 여겼으리라.\n* 페넬로페: 호메로스의 「오디세이아」에 나오는 오디세우스의 아내로, 남편이 떠난 오랜 세월 동안 숱한 구혼자들을 물리치며 정절을 지킨 여인.\n\n카밀라는 오래지 않아 실신에서 깨어났고, 정신을 차리자 말했다. \"어찌 가지 않느냐, 레오넬라, 해가 비춘 적 있고 밤이 가린 적 있는 그 어떤 자보다도 제 벗에게 가장 거짓된 그 벗을 이리로 부르러 말이다. 어서 가거라, 뛰거라, 서둘러라, 빨리! 미적거리는 사이에 내 분노의 불길이 스스로 사그라들고, 내가 바라는 의로운 복수가 위협과 저주 속에 녹아 사라지지 않도록.\"\n\n\"지금 부르러 가옵니다, 마님,\" 레오넬라가 말했다. \"허나 먼저 제게 그 단검을 주셔야 하옵니다. 제가 가 있는 동안 그것으로 마님을 사랑하는 모든 이가 평생 울게 할 빌미를 마님께서 만드실까 두렵사오니.\"\n\n\"평안히 가거라, 사랑하는 레오넬라, 그러지 않으마,\" 카밀라가 말했다. \"네 생각에 내가 내 명예를 지키느라 무모하고 어리석을지 모르나, 나는 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않고서, 또 제 불행의 죄가 얹힌 그 사내를 먼저 죽이지도 않고서 스스로 목숨을 끊었다는 그 루크레티아만큼은 되지 않으련다. 죽어야 한다면 죽겠지만, 아무런 내 잘못도 낳지 않은 그 만행을 두고 나를 이리로 끌어와 울게 만든 그자에게 온전히 복수한 다음이라야 하느니라.\"\n\n* 루크레티아: 고대 로마의 정숙한 여인으로, 능욕을 당한 뒤 가족에게 복수를 당부하고 스스로 목숨을 끊었다. 그녀의 죽음은 로마 왕정을 무너뜨리는 계기가 되었다고 전해진다.\n레오넬라는 로타리오를 부르러 가기까지 한참을 졸라야 했으나, 마침내 그녀가 떠났고, 그녀가 돌아오기를 기다리는 동안 카밀라는 혼잣말하듯 말을 이었다. \"맙소사! 내가 전에 여러 번 그랬듯 로타리오를 물리치는 편이, 지금처럼 그가 나를 부정하고 천한 여자로 여기게 — 그의 오해를 풀어 줄 때까지 그 잠깐 동안일지언정 — 내버려 두는 것보다 더 현명하지 않았겠는가? 의심할 바 없이 그편이 나았으리라. 그러나 그러면 나는 복수를 못 했을 것이고, 그가 제 타락이 자신을 몰아넣은 그 궁지에서 그토록 빤하고 손쉬운 빠져나갈 길을 찾아낸다면 내 남편의 명예도 씻기지 못했으리라. 그 배신자로 하여금 제 음탕한 욕망의 무모함을 목숨으로 갚게 하고, 카밀라가 남편에 대한 충절을 지켰을 뿐 아니라 감히 남편을 욕보이려 한 자에게 남편의 원수를 갚았음을 세상이 알게 하리라(혹여 언젠가 세상이 그것을 알게 된다면). 그래도 이 일을 안셀모에게 털어놓는 편이 나으리라는 생각이 드는구나. 하지만 시골에 있는 그에게 쓴 편지에서 내가 이미 그 일에 주의를 환기시켰는데, 그가 내가 거기서 일러 준 그 화근을 막으려 아무것도 하지 않았다면, 그것은 순전한 마음의 선량함과 미더움에서, 그토록 굳건한 벗의 가슴속에 제 명예를 거스르는 어떤 생각이 깃들 수 있으리라고는 믿으려 하지도, 믿을 수도 없었던 까닭이리라. 사실 나 자신도 여러 날 동안 그것을 믿지 않았고, 그의 무엄함이 노골적인 선물과 아낌없는 약속과 그칠 줄 모르는 눈물로 그것을 드러낼 만큼 멀리 나가지 않았더라면 결코 믿지 않았으리라. 그러나 어찌 내가 이리 따지고만 있는가? 대담한 결단에 따짐 따위가 필요하던가? 결코 그렇지 않지. 그러니 배신자들이여, 물러가라! 복수여, 나를 도우라! 그 거짓된 자로 하여금 오게 하라, 다가오게 하라, 나아오게 하라, 죽게 하라, 제 목숨을 내놓게 하라, 그런 다음에는 무슨 일이 일어나든 일어나라. 하늘이 내게 내려 주신 그분께 나는 정결하게 왔고, 정결하게 그분 곁을 떠나리라. 최악의 경우라도 나 자신의 정결한 피와, 우정이 이 세상에서 본 가장 거짓된 벗의 더러운 피에 젖은 채로.\" 그리고 그녀는 이 말을 입에 올리며, 칼집에서 뺀 단검을 든 채 어찌나 불규칙하고 어지러운 걸음으로, 또 어찌나 격한 몸짓으로 방 안을 거니는지, 누가 보았다면 그녀가 제정신을 잃었다 여기고, 가녀린 여인이 아니라 어떤 사나운 광인으로 여겼으리라.\n\n벽걸이 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 안셀모는 이 모든 것을 지켜보며 경탄을 금치 못했고, 자신이 보고 들은 것이 그보다 더 큰 의심에까지 넉넉한 답이 됨을 이미 절감했다. 그리하여 그는 로타리오가 와서 보여 줄 증거 따위는 없어도 좋겠다 싶었으니, 무슨 갑작스러운 변고가 생길까 두려웠던 것이다. 그러나 그가 막 모습을 드러내어 밖으로 나가 아내를 끌어안고 오해를 풀어 주려던 찰나, 레오넬라가 로타리오를 데리고 돌아오는 것을 보고는 멈칫했다. 카밀라는 그를 보자 단검으로 바닥에 자기 앞으로 길게 줄을 하나 그으며 그에게 말했다. \"로타리오, 내가 당신에게 하는 말을 새겨들어요. 혹시라도 당신이 감히 저 줄을 넘거나 그것에 다가오기라도 한다면, 당신이 그러려는 것을 내가 보는 그 순간 나는 내 손에 쥔 이 단검으로 내 가슴을 꿰뚫겠어요. 그리고 당신이 내게 한마디 대답하기 전에, 나는 당신이 내 말 몇 마디를 듣기를 바라요. 그런 다음에 당신 좋을 대로 대답하면 돼요. 먼저, 로타리오, 당신에게 묻겠어요. 당신이 내 남편 안셀모를 아는지, 또 그를 어떻게 여기는지 말해 주세요. 그리고 둘째로, 당신이 나 또한 아는지 알고 싶어요. 이에 답해 주세요, 당황하지도 말고 무어라 답할지 깊이 헤아리지도 말고. 내가 당신에게 던지는 것은 수수께끼가 아니니까요.\"\n\n로타리오는 그리 둔한 자가 아니어서, 카밀라가 그에게 안셀모를 숨도록 만들라고 지시한 첫 순간부터 그녀가 무엇을 하려는지 알아챘던 터라, 그녀의 생각에 어찌나 선뜻 척척 맞장구를 쳤던지 둘이서 그 거짓을 진실보다 더 진실해 보이게 만들었다. 그리하여 그는 이렇게 답했다. \"아름다운 카밀라, 나는 당신이 내가 온 목적과는 그토록 동떨어진 물음을 던지려고 나를 부른 줄은 몰랐소. 그러나 만일 약속한 보상을 미루려 그러는 것이라면, 더 오래 미뤄도 좋았으리다. 행복에 대한 갈망이란 그것을 얻을 희망이 가까워질수록 더 큰 괴로움을 안기는 법이니 말이오. 허나 당신이, 내가 당신의 물음에 답하지 않는다고 하지 않도록, 내 말하리다. 나는 당신의 남편 안셀모를 알며, 우리는 가장 어린 시절부터 서로를 알아 왔소. 우리의 우정에 대해서는, 당신도 아는 그것에 대해서는 말하지 않으리니, 더 큰 잘못들에 대한 막강한 핑계인 사랑이 그에게 끼치게 만든 그 그릇됨을 거슬러 나 스스로 증언하도록 나를 몰아세우지 않으려 함이오. 당신을 나는 알며, 그가 당신을 여기는 것과 똑같이 여기고 있소. 만일 그렇지 않았더라면, 나는 더 못한 보상을 위해서라면 내 신분에 진 의무와, 이제 그 막강한 적 사랑으로 말미암아 내가 깨뜨리고 짓밟은 참된 우정의 거룩한 법도를 거스르며 행동하지는 않았을 것이오.\"\n\n\"당신이 그것을 시인한다면,\" 카밀라가 응수했다. \"마땅히 사랑받아야 할 모든 것의 철천지원수여, 무슨 낯으로 감히, 당신이 마땅히 우러러보아야 할 그분이 비치는 거울임을, 그리하여 당신이 얼마나 그분께 부당하게 굴었는지를 비추어 보아야 할 그 거울임을 당신이 아는 사람 앞에 나설 수 있단 말이에요? 그러나, 아아 가엾어라, 무엇이 당신으로 하여금 당신 자신이 마땅히 지켜야 할 바를 그토록 가벼이 여기게 했는지 이제 알겠어요. 그것은 분명 내 어떤 거리낌 없는 행동이었을 거예요. 그것을 부정함이라 부르지는 않겠어요. 어떤 작정한 의도에서 나온 것이 아니라, 여자들이 굳이 삼갈 필요가 없다 여길 때 무심결에 저지르는 그런 부주의에서 나온 것이니까요. 하지만 말해 봐요, 배신자여, 내가 언제 말이나 몸짓으로 당신의 그 비열한 욕망을 이룰 수 있으리라는 한 가닥 희망의 그림자라도 당신 속에 일깨울 만한 응답을 당신의 간청에 한 적이 있던가요? 당신의 사랑 고백이 언제 매몰차고 경멸스럽게 거절당하고 책망당하지 않았던가요? 당신의 잦은 맹세와 그보다 더 잦은 선물이 언제 믿어지거나 받아들여진 적이 있던가요? 그러나 나는 어떤 희망에 떠받쳐지지 않고서는 누구도 오래도록 사랑을 얻으려는 시도를 이어 갈 수 없다고 여기는지라, 당신의 그 뻔뻔함의 탓을 나 자신에게 돌리려 해요. 분명 내 어떤 경솔함이 이제껏 줄곧 당신의 희망을 키워 주었을 테니까요. 그러니 나는 나 자신을 벌하고, 당신의 죄가 마땅히 받아야 할 그 벌을 나 자신에게 내리겠어요. 그리고 내가 나 자신에게 이토록 가차 없는 만큼 당신에게도 결코 다르게 굴 수 없음을 당신이 알도록, 나는 당신을 불러, 당신이 할 수 있는 온갖 집요함으로 욕보였고 또 나 역시 — 내가 빌미를 주었다면 — 당신의 비열한 속셈을 부추기고 묵인할 만한 빌미를 매번 피하지 못한 부주의로 욕보인, 내 존귀한 남편의 다친 명예에 바칠 그 제물의 증인이 되게 하려는 거예요. 다시 한번 말하건대, 내 어떤 경솔함이 당신 속에 이 무도한 생각들을 낳았으리라는 내 마음속 의심, 그것이 나를 가장 괴롭히는 것이며 내 손으로 가장 벌하고 싶은 것이에요. 다른 어떤 응징의 도구를 쓴다면 내 잘못이 어쩌면 더 널리 알려질지도 모르니까요. 그러나 그러기 전에, 나는 내 죽음 속에서 죽음을 내려, 내가 바라고 또 품은 그 복수에 대한 갈망을 온전히 채워 줄 자를 데려가겠어요. 내가 어디로 가든 그곳에서, 나를 이토록 절망스러운 처지에 몰아넣은 자에게 굽힘 없고 흔들림 없는 정의가 내린 그 형벌을 보게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n이 말을 입에 올리며 그녀는 믿기 어려운 기세와 날쌤으로, 칼집에서 뺀 단검을 들고 로타리오에게 달려들었는데, 어찌나 대놓고 그것을 그의 가슴에 박으려 드는지 그는 이 거동이 참인지 거짓인지 도무지 가늠할 수 없을 지경이었으니, 그녀가 자신을 찌르지 못하도록 막느라 제 온갖 솜씨와 힘에 기대지 않을 수 없었던 것이다. 그리고 그녀가 이 기이한 광대극이자 속임수를 어찌나 진짜처럼 연기했던지, 그녀는 그것에 진실의 빛깔을 입히려 자기 자신의 피로 그것을 물들이기로 작정했다. 로타리오에게 상처를 입힐 수 없음을 알아채자, 혹은 알아챈 척하며 그녀가 말했다. \"운명이, 보아하니, 내 정당한 소원을 온전히 채워 주지는 않으려나 보다. 그러나 적어도 그 일부나마 채우는 것까지 막지는 못하리라.\" 그러고는 로타리오가 움켜쥐고 있던, 단검 든 손을 빼내려 안간힘을 써서 그것을 풀어내고는, 칼끝을 깊은 상처를 입힐 수 없는 자리로 향하게 하여 어깨에 바싹 붙은 왼쪽 옆구리 위쪽에 그것을 찔러 넣고, 그런 다음 까무러친 듯 바닥에 쓰러졌다.\n\n레오넬라와 로타리오는 이 참변에 어안이 벙벙하고 망연자실하여, 카밀라가 제 피에 젖은 채 바닥에 뻗어 있는 것을 보고도 그 행위의 참된 본질이 무엇인지 여전히 가늠하지 못했다. 로타리오는 겁에 질려 숨을 헐떡이며 단검을 뽑으려 황급히 달려갔으나, 상처가 얼마나 얕은지를 보고는 두려움이 가셨고, 다시 한번 아름다운 카밀라의 영민함과 침착함과 재빠른 기지에 감탄했다. 그러고는 자신이 맡은 배역을 한층 더 잘 떠받치고자, 마치 그녀가 죽기라도 한 듯 그녀의 몸 위로 넘쳐흐르는 구슬픈 비탄을 쏟아 내기 시작했으니, 자기 자신뿐 아니라 자신을 이런 처지에 몰아넣은 장본인에게까지 저주를 퍼부었다. 그리고 자신의 벗 안셀모가 듣고 있음을 알았기에, 그는 듣는 이로 하여금 — 비록 그가 카밀라를 죽었다 여길지라도 — 카밀라보다 자신을 훨씬 더 가엾이 여기게 만들 만한 식으로 말했다. 레오넬라는 그녀를 팔에 안아 침상에 누이고, 로타리오에게 누군가를 찾아가 은밀히 그녀의 상처를 돌보아 줄 사람을 데려와 달라고 애원하는 한편, 혹여 상처가 아물기 전에 안셀모가 돌아온다면 그의 부인의 상처에 대해 뭐라 말해야 할지 그의 조언과 의견을 구했다. 그는 좋을 대로 말하면 된다고, 자신은 쓸모 있을 만한 조언을 해 줄 형편이 못 된다고 답했다. 그가 그녀에게 일러 줄 수 있는 것이라곤 그저 피를 멎게 해 보라는 것뿐이며, 자신은 다시는 모습을 보이지 않을 곳으로 가겠노라 했다. 그러고는 깊은 비탄과 슬픔에 잠긴 온갖 기색을 띤 채 집을 나섰다. 그러나 홀로, 아무도 자신을 보지 않는 곳에 이르자 그는 카밀라의 능란함과 레오넬라의 한결같은 연기에 경탄하여 정신을 못 차린 채 쉴 새 없이 성호를 그었다. 그는 안셀모가 자신이 또 한 사람의 포르티아를 아내로 두었다고 얼마나 굳게 믿을지를 곱씹으며, 상상할 수 있는 가장 교묘하게 가려진 거짓과 진실을 두고 함께 기뻐할 수 있도록 그를 만날 일을 애타게 고대했다.\n\n* 포르티아: 고대 로마의 정치가 카토의 딸이자 브루투스의 아내로, 정절과 굳센 절개로 이름난 여인이다.\n레오넬라는 그가 일러 준 대로 안주인의 피를 멎게 했는데, 그것은 그녀의 속임수를 떠받치는 데 꼭 필요한 만큼에 지나지 않았다. 그러고는 포도주를 조금 발라 상처를 씻고 제 솜씨껏 그것을 동여매면서, 안주인을 돌보는 내내, 설령 앞서 다른 말이 일절 없었다 한들 그것만으로도 카밀라가 순결의 본보기임을 안셀모에게 확신시키기에 넉넉했을 그런 어조로 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 레오넬라의 말에 카밀라는 자기 말을 보태어, 자신을 겁쟁이요 기개가 모자란 여자라 일컬었으니, 그토록 진저리 나는 목숨을 떨쳐 버려야 할 가장 절박한 그 순간에 그럴 만한 기개가 없었다는 것이었다. 그녀는 일어난 모든 일을 사랑하는 남편에게 알려야 할지 말지 시중드는 하녀의 조언을 구했으나, 레오넬라는 그것에 대해 아무 말도 말라고 일렀으니, 그러면 남편에게 로타리오에게 복수해야 할 의무를 지우게 될 터인데, 그가 그 일을 하자면 제 한 몸이 큰 위험을 무릅써야 하기 때문이며, 참된 아내의 도리란 남편에게 다툼의 빌미를 주는 것이 아니라 도리어 그것을 그에게서 되도록 멀리 치워 주는 것이라는 까닭에서였다.\n\n카밀라는 그녀의 말이 옳다 여기며 그 조언을 따르겠노라 답했으나, 어쨌든 안셀모에게 그 상처를 어떻게 둘러댈지 궁리해 두는 것이 좋겠다고, 그가 그것을 보지 않을 수 없을 터이기 때문이라고 했다. 이에 레오넬라는 자신은 농담으로라도 거짓말을 할 줄 모른다고 답했다.\n\n\"그럼 나라고 어찌 알겠느냐, 얘야?\" 카밀라가 말했다. \"나는 목숨이 달렸다 한들 거짓을 꾸미거나 둘러댈 엄두조차 못 낼 텐데. 이 곤경에서 빠져나갈 길을 도무지 떠올릴 수 없다면, 그가 우리의 거짓 이야기를 들춰내게 하느니 차라리 그에게 있는 그대로 사실을 말하는 편이 나으리라.\"\n\n\"심려치 마옵소서, 마님,\" 레오넬라가 말했다. \"오늘부터 내일 사이에 제가 나리께 무어라 말씀드려야 할지 궁리해 두겠나이다. 어쩌면 상처가 있는 자리가 그러하니 나리의 눈에 띄지 않게 감출 수 있을 것이고, 이토록 선하고 명예로운 뜻을 이루도록 하늘이 기꺼이 우리를 도우실 것이옵니다. 마음을 가다듬으소서, 마님, 그리고 나리께서 마님의 동요하신 모습을 보시지 않도록 흥분을 가라앉히도록 애써 보옵소서. 나머지는 제 손과 하느님께 맡기소서, 그분은 언제나 선한 뜻을 지지하시니 말이옵니다.\"\n\n안셀모는 더없는 주의를 기울여, 자기 명예의 죽음이라는 그 비극이 펼쳐지는 것을 듣고 또 지켜보았으니, 그 연기자들이 어찌나 놀랍도록 실감 나는 진실로 그 비극을 연기했던지 그들이 제가 맡은 배역 그 자체가 되어 버린 듯했다. 그는 밤이, 그리고 집을 빠져나가 자신의 좋은 벗 로타리오를 만나러 가서, 그와 더불어 아내의 순결을 입증한 데서 얻은 그 값진 진주를 두고 기쁨을 쏟아 낼 기회가 오기를 애타게 바랐다. 안주인과 하녀 둘 다 그가 빠져나갈 시간과 기회를 마련해 주려 마음을 썼고, 그는 그것을 틈타 빠져나가 곧장 로타리오를 찾아 나섰는데, 그를 찾아냈을 때 어떻게 그를 끌어안았는지, 또 마음의 기쁨에 겨워 그에게 무슨 말들을 했는지, 카밀라에게 어떤 찬사를 바쳤는지는 이루 다 형용할 수 없으리라. 로타리오는 이 모든 것을 들으면서도 아무런 기쁨도 내비치지 못했으니, 제 벗이 얼마나 속고 있는지, 또 자신이 얼마나 불명예스럽게 그를 욕보였는지를 잊을 수 없었던 까닭이다. 그리고 안셀모는 로타리오가 기뻐하지 않음을 알아챌 수 있었으되, 그저 그가 카밀라에게 상처를 입힌 채 두고 왔고 또 자신이 그 장본인이었던 탓이려니 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 여러 말 가운데, 카밀라의 사고를 두고 괴로워 말라고, 그것을 자기에게 숨기기로 둘이 합의한 것을 보면 그 상처가 분명 대수롭지 않은 것일 테니, 그렇다면 두려워할 까닭이 없고, 이제부터는 기운을 내어 자기와 더불어 기뻐해야 마땅하다고 그에게 일렀다. 그의 솜씨와 능란함 덕에 자신이 감히 바랄 수 있었던 행복의 최고봉에 올랐음을 알게 되었으니, 카밀라의 이름을 길이길이 남길, 그녀를 찬미하는 시를 짓는 것보다 더 좋은 소일거리는 바라지 않는다는 것이었다. 로타리오는 그 뜻을 칭송하며, 그토록 영광스러운 기념비를 세우는 데 제 몫을 다해 그를 돕겠노라 약속했다.\n\n그리하여 안셀모는 이 세상에 다시없을 만큼 더없이 곱게 눈이 가려진 사내로 남게 되었다. 제 영광의 도구를 이끌어 가고 있다고 믿은 그 자신이, 제 좋은 명성을 송두리째 무너뜨린 장본인의 손을 잡고 집으로 데려갔으니, 카밀라는 마음속으로는 미소를 머금은 채 고개를 돌린 얼굴로 그를 맞이했다. 이 속임수는 얼마간 이어졌으나, 몇 달이 지난 끝에 운명이 제 수레바퀴를 돌리니, 그때껏 그토록 교묘히 감추어졌던 그 죄가 만천하에 드러났고, 안셀모는 제 분별없는 호기심의 대가를 목숨으로 치렀다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV.\n\n소설은 이제 읽을 것이 얼마 남지 않았는데, 그때 산초 판사가 돈키호테가 누워 있던 다락방에서 격한 흥분에 휩싸여 뛰쳐나오며 소리쳤다. \"여러분, 어서 달려가세요! 빨리, 우리 주인님을 도와주세요. 제가 평생 본 적 없는 가장 지독하고 사나운 싸움 한복판에 계십니다. 살아 계신 하느님께 맹세코, 우리 미코미코나 공주님의 원수인 그 거인에게 어찌나 칼을 휘두르셨는지, 무 자르듯 단번에 머리를 싹둑 베어 버리셨다니까요.\"\n\n\"이 사람아, 대체 무슨 소리를 하는 게냐?\" 소설의 나머지를 막 읽으려던 신부가 멈추고 말했다. \"산초, 정신이 있는 게냐? 그 거인이 이천 레과나 떨어진 곳에 있는데, 어떻게 자네 말대로 될 수 있단 말이냐?\"\n\n이때 방 안에서 요란한 소리가 들리더니, 돈키호테가 고함치는 것이었다. \"멈춰라, 도둑놈아, 강도야, 악당아. 이제 내 손에 잡혔으니, 네 언월도 따위는 아무 소용이 없을 것이다!\" 그러더니 마치 벽을 향해 힘차게 칼질을 해 대는 듯한 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"가만히 서서 듣고 계실 게 아니라요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"들어가서 둘을 떼어 놓든지 우리 주인님을 도우시라고요. 하기야 이제 그럴 것도 없겠네요. 분명 거인은 지금쯤 죽어서, 지나온 죄 많은 생을 하느님께 고하고 있을 테니까요. 제가 봤어요, 피가 바닥에 흐르고 잘려 나간 머리가 한쪽에 나뒹구는데, 그게 큼직한 포도주 가죽 부대만큼이나 컸다니까요.\"\n\n이 말에 주인이 말했다. \"내가 죽어도 좋소이다, 만일 저 돈키호테인지 돈악마인지 하는 자가 침대 머리맡에 그득히 세워 둔 적포도주 가죽 부대들을 칼질해 놓지 않았다면 말이오. 그러니 저 양반이 피라고 여기는 게 틀림없이 쏟아진 포도주일 게요.\" 이렇게 말하면서 그는 방으로 들어갔고 나머지 사람들도 뒤따랐다. 거기서 그들은 세상에서 가장 기이한 차림새를 한 돈키호테를 발견했다. 그는 셔츠 바람이었는데, 그 셔츠는 앞쪽이 넓적다리를 다 덮을 만큼 길지 못했고 뒤쪽은 여섯 손가락만큼이나 더 짧았다. 그의 다리는 무척 길고 비쩍 말랐으며 털로 덮여 있어 도무지 깨끗하다고는 할 수 없었다. 머리에는 주인의 것인 기름때 묻은 작고 빨간 모자를 쓰고 있었고, 왼팔에는 침대 담요를 둘둘 감고 있었으니, 산초가 자기만 아는 이유로 원한을 품고 있던 바로 그 담요였다. 오른손에는 칼집에서 뽑은 칼을 들고 사방으로 휘둘러 대며, 마치 실제로 어떤 거인과 싸우기라도 하는 양 고함을 질러 댔다. 그런데 무엇보다 가관인 것은 그의 두 눈이 감겨 있었다는 사실이니, 그는 곤히 잠들어 거인과 전투를 벌이는 꿈을 꾸고 있었던 것이다. 자신이 이루어 낼 모험에 그의 상상력이 어찌나 강하게 사로잡혔던지, 그는 이미 미코미콘 왕국에 다다라 원수와 전투를 벌이고 있는 꿈을 꾸기에 이르렀고, 거인에게 칼질을 퍼붓고 있다고 믿으며 가죽 부대들에 어찌나 많은 칼을 휘둘렀던지 방 안이 온통 포도주 천지가 되어 버린 것이었다. 이 광경을 본 주인은 어찌나 분이 치밀었던지 돈키호테에게 달려들어 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 마구 후려치기 시작했으니, 만일 카르데니오와 신부가 그를 끌어내지 않았더라면 그가 거인과의 전쟁을 끝장내 버렸을 것이다. 그러나 이 모든 일에도 불구하고 가엾은 양반은 도무지 깨어나지 않았으니, 이발사가 우물에서 차가운 물을 큰 항아리로 길어 와 그의 온몸에 단번에 끼얹고 나서야 돈키호테는 잠에서 깨어났다. 다만 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지를 알아차릴 만큼 온전히 깬 것은 아니었다. 도로테아는 그의 옷차림이 어찌나 짧고 빈약한지를 보고는, 자신의 기사와 자신의 적수 사이의 싸움을 구경하러 들어가려 하지 않았다. 산초로 말하자면, 거인의 머리를 찾느라 온 바닥을 헤집고 다녔으나 끝내 찾지 못하자 이렇게 말했다. \"이제야 알겠네요, 이 집은 죄다 마법이 걸려 있다니까요. 지난번에도 바로 지금 제가 서 있는 이 자리에서, 누가 때리는지도 모르고 아무도 보이지 않는 채로 무수히 두들겨 맞았잖습니까. 그런데 지금 이 머리도 어디에서도 보이질 않네요, 제 두 눈으로 똑똑히 잘려 나가는 걸 보았고 몸에서 샘솟듯 피가 흐르는 것도 보았는데 말입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 피며 무슨 샘 타령이냐, 이 하느님과 그 성인들의 원수 같은 놈아?\" 주인이 말했다. \"이 도둑놈아, 그 피니 샘이니 하는 게 죄다 여기 칼에 찔린 이 가죽 부대들이고 방 안에 온통 떠다니는 적포도주라는 게 보이지 않느냐? 차라리 이걸 찔러 놓은 놈의 영혼이 지옥에서 헤엄치는 꼴을 보았으면 좋겠다.\"\n\n\"전 그런 건 아무것도 모릅니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"제가 아는 거라고는, 이 머리를 못 찾는 바람에 제 영지가 소금이 물에 녹듯 사라져 버린다면 그게 제 운수 사나운 팔자라는 것뿐이에요.\" 깨어 있는 산초가 잠든 주인보다 더 딱한 지경이었으니, 주인의 약속이 그토록 그의 정신을 흐려 놓았던 것이다.\n\n주인은 종자의 천연덕스러움과 그 주인의 고약한 짓거리에 제정신이 아니었다. 그는 지난번에 셈도 치르지 않고 떠나 버린 것처럼 되지는 않을 것이며, 이번에는 기사도의 특권 따위가 통하여 둘 중 누구도 셈을 치르지 않고 빠져나가게 두지 않겠노라고, 망가진 가죽 부대에 박아 넣어야 할 마개 값까지도 받아 내겠노라고 맹세했다. 신부는 돈키호테의 두 손을 붙잡고 있었는데, 돈키호테는 이제 모험을 끝내고 미코미코나 공주 앞에 있다고 여기며 신부 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 말했다. \"고귀하고 아름다우신 공주님, 전하께서는 오늘부터 이 비천한 자가 끼칠 그 어떤 해도 두려워하지 않으시고 살아가실 수 있나이다. 그리고 저 또한 오늘부터 공주님께 드린 약속에서 풀려났으니, 높으신 하느님의 도움과, 제가 살아 숨 쉬는 까닭이 되는 그분의 은총으로 그 약속을 이토록 훌륭히 완수하였기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"제가 그렇게 말씀드리지 않았습니까?\" 이 말을 듣고 산초가 말했다. \"보세요, 전 취하지 않았다니까요. 저기 보시다시피 우리 주인님께서 벌써 거인을 소금에 절여 놓으셨잖습니까. 황소들은 의심할 여지가 없고, 제 영지도 멀쩡합니다!\"\n\n주인과 하인, 이 한 쌍의 터무니없는 짓거리에 누군들 웃음을 참을 수 있었으랴? 과연 다들 웃었으니, 스스로를 저주하던 주인만은 예외였다. 그러나 마침내 이발사와 카르데니오와 신부는 적잖은 수고 끝에 돈키호테를 침대에 눕히는 데 성공했고, 그는 몹시 지친 기색이 역력한 채 잠이 들었다. 그들은 그를 자도록 두고 여관 문간으로 나와, 거인의 머리를 찾지 못한 산초 판사를 위로했다. 그러나 그보다 훨씬 더 애를 먹은 것은 자기 가죽 부대들의 갑작스러운 죽음에 노발대발하는 주인을 달래는 일이었다. 안주인은 반은 꾸짖고 반은 울먹이며 말했다. \"이 편력 기사가 우리 집에 들어온 게 흉한 순간이요 불길한 시각이었지. 차라리 그자에게 눈길 한 번 주지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을, 그자 때문에 큰 손해를 봤으니 말이오. 지난번에는 저 자신과 종자, 말과 당나귀 몫으로 든 저녁 식사며 잠자리며 짚이며 보리 값을 외상으로 달아 놓고 떠나 버렸지. 자기는 모험을 찾아다니는 기사라며—하느님께서 그자와 세상 모든 모험가에게 불운한 모험을 내려 주시길—그러니 아무것도 갚을 의무가 없다는 거였소, 기사도 요금표에 그렇게 정해져 있다나. 그러더니 죄다 그자 때문에 그 다른 양반이 와서 내 꼬리를 가져가더니, 털이 죄다 벗겨진 채 두 쿠아르티요어치도 넘게 망가져서 돌려주는 바람에 우리 남편이 쓰려던 데에는 아무 쓸모도 없게 돼 버렸지. 게다가 모든 일에 마무리 손질이라도 하듯, 내 가죽 부대를 터뜨리고 포도주를 쏟아 버리다니! 차라리 그자 제 피가 쏟아지는 꼴을 보았으면 좋겠소! 하지만 그자가 착각은 말아야 할 거요. 내 아버지의 유골과 내 어머니의 혼령에 걸고, 한 푼도 빠짐없이 받아 내고야 말 테니까. 안 그러면 내 이름이 내 이름이 아니고, 내가 내 아버지의 딸이 아닐 거요.\" 안주인은 이 모든 것과 그 비슷한 말을 더 늘어놓으며 잔뜩 골을 냈고, 착한 하녀 마리토르네스도 거들었으며, 딸은 잠자코 이따금 미소만 지었다. 신부는 가죽 부대뿐 아니라 포도주, 그리고 무엇보다 그들이 그토록 애지중지하던 꼬리의 손상까지 모든 손실을 자기 능력껏 보상해 주겠노라 약속하여 일을 무마했다. 도로테아는 산초를 위로하며, 그의 주인이 거인의 목을 베었음이 확실해지고 자신이 자기 왕국에 평화로이 자리 잡게 되는 즉시, 그 안에서 가장 좋은 영지를 그에게 내리겠노라 다짐했다. 이에 산초는 마음을 달랬고, 자기는 거인의 머리를 분명히 보았으며, 더구나 허리띠까지 닿는 수염이 달려 있더라고, 그리고 지금 그것이 보이지 않는다면 그건 이 집에서 벌어지는 모든 일이 마법으로 돌아가기 때문이며, 지난번에 그곳에 묵었을 때 자신이 직접 겪어 본 바이기도 하다고 공주에게 장담했다. 도로테아는 그 말을 충분히 믿으며, 모든 것이 잘되어 그가 바라는 대로 풀릴 테니 걱정할 것 없다고 말했다. 이리하여 모두가 가라앉자, 신부는 이제 읽을 것이 얼마 남지 않은 것을 보고 소설을 계속 읽어 나가고 싶어 했다. 도로테아와 다른 이들이 끝까지 읽어 달라 청하니, 그도 그들을 기쁘게 해 주고 싶었고 자신도 읽는 것이 즐거웠던지라, 이런 말로 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n그 결과, 카밀라의 정절에 대한 안셀모의 믿음으로 그는 행복하게 근심 없이 살았고, 카밀라는 안셀모가 자신의 감정을 실제와는 정반대로 여기도록 일부러 로타리오를 냉랭하게 대했다. 그리고 그 처지를 더 잘 떠받치기 위해 로타리오는, 카밀라가 자신의 존재를 못마땅해하는 기색이 뻔히 보인다며 그 집에 드나드는 일을 면제해 달라고 청했다. 그러나 어리석게 속아 넘어간 안셀모는 그런 일은 결코 용납할 수 없다고 했으니, 이리하여 그는 자신의 행복을 보장하고 있다고 믿으면서 천 가지 방식으로 제 불명예의 장본인이 되어 갔다. 한편 레오넬라는 자신이 정사를 이어 갈 권한을 쥐었다고 여기는 데서 오는 만족감이 어찌나 높아졌던지, 다른 모든 것을 아랑곳하지 않고 거리낌 없이 제 욕망을 좇았으니, 제 안주인이 자신을 감싸 주고 심지어 어떻게 하면 안전하게 일을 처리할 수 있는지까지 일러 주리라 자신했던 것이다. 마침내 어느 날 밤 안셀모는 레오넬라의 방에서 발소리를 들었고, 누구인지 보려고 들어가려 했으나 문이 안에서 막혀 있는 것을 알고는 더욱 기를 쓰고 열려 했다. 그가 힘을 써서 문을 밀어젖히고 방에 들어선 순간, 마침 한 남자가 창문을 넘어 길거리로 뛰어내리는 것이 보였다. 그는 그자를 붙잡거나 누구인지 알아내려고 재빨리 달려갔으나 둘 다 이루지 못했으니, 레오넬라가 그에게 두 팔을 두르고 매달리며 외쳤기 때문이다. \"진정하세요, 나리. 분에 못 이겨 저기 도망친 자를 뒤쫓지 마세요. 그이는 제 사람, 사실은 제 남편이랍니다.\"\n\n안셀모는 그 말을 믿지 않고 분노로 눈이 멀어 단검을 뽑아 들고는 레오넬라를 찌르겠다고 위협하며, 사실대로 말하지 않으면 죽여 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 그녀는 두려움에 사로잡혀 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 외쳤다. \"저를 죽이지 마세요, 나리. 나리께서 상상하실 수 있는 그 어떤 것보다도 더 중대한 일들을 말씀드릴 수 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당장 말하라, 아니면 죽으리라.\" 안셀모가 말했다.\n\n\"지금은 도저히 안 되겠어요.\" 레오넬라가 말했다. \"제가 너무 떨려서요. 내일까지 저를 내버려 두세요, 그러면 나리를 깜짝 놀라게 할 이야기를 제게서 들으실 거예요. 다만 창문으로 뛰어내린 자가 이 도시의 청년이며, 제 남편이 되기로 약속한 사람이라는 것만은 믿어 주세요.\"\n\n안셀모는 이 말에 마음이 가라앉아 그녀가 청한 시간을 기다리는 데 만족했으니, 그는 카밀라의 정절을 그토록 흡족해하고 확신하던 터라 그녀에게 불리한 말은 무엇도 들으리라고는 결코 예상하지 못했던 것이다. 그리하여 그는 방을 나서며 레오넬라를 가둔 채, 자기에게 밝혀야 할 모든 것을 다 말하기 전까지는 나오지 말라고 일러두었다. 그는 곧장 카밀라를 만나러 가서, 자기와 그녀의 하녀 사이에 오간 모든 일과, 중대한 일들을 알려 주겠다던 그녀의 약속을 그대로 들려주었다.\n\n카밀라가 동요했는지 아닌지는 말할 필요도 없으니, 그녀의 두려움과 낭패감이 어찌나 컸던지, 레오넬라가 자신의 부정에 관해 아는 모든 것을 안셀모에게 일러바칠 것이라고—그럴 만한 충분한 이유가 있었으므로—확신하고는, 제 의심이 사실로 드러나는지를 차마 기다려 볼 용기가 없었던 것이다. 그리하여 바로 그날 밤, 안셀모가 잠들었다 싶자마자 그녀는 가진 것 중 가장 값진 보석들과 약간의 돈을 챙겨, 아무에게도 들키지 않고 집을 빠져나가 로타리오에게로 달려갔다. 그녀는 그에게 벌어진 일을 이야기하며, 자신을 어딘가 안전한 곳으로 데려다주거나 함께 달아나 안셀모의 손이 미치지 못할 곳으로 가 달라고 애원했다. 카밀라가 로타리오를 몰아넣은 당혹스러움이 어찌나 컸던지, 그는 대답으로 한마디도 내뱉지 못했으며 하물며 무엇을 해야 할지조차 정하지 못했다. 마침내 그는 자기 누이가 원장으로 있는 한 수녀원으로 그녀를 데려가기로 결심했다. 카밀라가 이에 동의하자, 로타리오는 사정이 요구하는 신속함으로 그녀를 수녀원으로 데려가 그곳에 남겨 두고는, 자신은 누구에게도 떠난다는 사실을 알리지 않은 채 도시를 떠나 버렸다.\n\n날이 밝자마자 안셀모는 곁에 카밀라가 없는 것도 알아차리지 못한 채, 레오넬라가 자기에게 들려줄 이야기를 알고 싶은 마음에 일어나 그녀를 가두어 둔 방으로 서둘러 갔다. 그는 문을 열고 들어갔으나 레오넬라는 보이지 않았다. 그가 발견한 것이라고는 창문에 매듭지어 묶인 시트 몇 장뿐이었으니, 그녀가 그것을 타고 내려가 도망쳤다는 명백한 증거였다. 그는 불안해하며 카밀라에게 알리러 돌아갔으나, 침대에도 집 안 어디에도 그녀가 보이지 않자 어찌할 바를 몰랐다. 그는 집안의 하인들에게 그녀에 관해 물었으나 누구도 설명해 주지 못했다. 카밀라를 찾아 나서던 중 우연히 그는 그녀의 상자들이 열려 있고 보석 대부분이 사라진 것을 보게 되었다. 그제야 그는 자신의 치욕을 온전히 깨달았으며, 레오넬라가 자기 불행의 원인이 아니라는 것도 알게 되었다. 그리하여 그는 옷도 채 제대로 갖춰 입지 않은 그대로, 슬픔에 잠기고 의기소침하여 자기 벗 로타리오에게 그 슬픔을 털어놓으러 갔다. 그러나 그를 찾지 못하고, 하인들이 그가 밤새 집을 비웠으며 가진 돈을 모조리 가지고 갔다고 전하자, 그는 제정신을 잃는 듯한 기분이 들었다. 그리고 모든 것의 마무리를 짓듯, 제 집으로 돌아왔을 때 그는 집이 텅 비어 버려 남녀 하인 가운데 하나도 남아 있지 않은 것을 보았다. 그는 무엇을 생각해야 할지, 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 무엇을 해야 할지 알 수 없었고, 그의 이성은 조금씩 그를 떠나가는 듯했다. 그는 자신의 처지를 돌아보며, 한순간에 아내도 벗도 하인도 없이 버려진 자신을, 머리 위의 하늘에마저 버림받았다고 느끼는 자신을, 그리고 그 무엇보다도 명예를 빼앗긴 자신을 보았으니, 카밀라가 사라진 데서 그는 곧 제 자신의 파멸을 보았던 것이다. 오랜 숙고 끝에 그는 마침내, 이 불행의 얽힘이 꾸며질 빌미를 스스로 마련하던 동안 머물렀던 벗의 마을로 가기로 결심했다. 그는 집 문들을 잠그고 말에 올라, 꺾인 마음으로 길을 나섰다. 그러나 절반도 채 가지 못해 제 상념에 시달린 나머지, 말에서 내려 나무에 말을 매어 두고 그 밑동에 몸을 던지고는 가슴 미어지는 가련한 한숨을 토해 내야 했다. 그는 거의 해 질 녘까지 그곳에 머물렀는데, 그때 도시 쪽에서 한 남자가 말을 타고 다가오는 것이 보였다. 그는 그에게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 피렌체에 무슨 소식이 있는지를 물었다.\n\n그 시민이 대답했다. \"여러 날 만에 들은 가장 기이한 소식입죠. 산조반니에 살던 부유한 안셀모의 둘도 없는 벗 로타리오가 지난밤 안셀모의 아내 카밀라를 데리고 달아났는데, 그 아내도 함께 사라졌다고 온 동네에 소문이 자자합니다. 이 모든 게 카밀라의 하녀 입에서 나온 이야긴데, 그 하녀를 총독이 지난밤 안셀모의 집 창문에서 시트를 타고 내려오던 걸 붙잡았답니다. 사실 그 일이 어찌 된 영문인지는 저도 정확히 모릅니다. 제가 아는 거라곤 온 도시가 이 일에 놀라워한다는 것뿐인데요, 그 둘 사이에 오갔던 그 크고도 깊은 우정으로 보아 누구도 이런 일을 예상치 못했을 테니까요. 그 우정이 어찌나 컸던지 사람들이 그들을 '두 친구'라고 불렀다지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"로타리오와 카밀라가 어느 길로 갔는지는 도무지 알 수 없소?\" 안셀모가 물었다.\n\n\"전혀요.\" 시민이 말했다. \"총독이 그들을 찾느라 무척 분주하긴 했습니다만.\"\n\n\"하느님의 가호가 함께하시길, 나리.\" 안셀모가 말했다.\n\n\"하느님이 당신과 함께하시길.\" 시민은 이렇게 말하고 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n이 참담한 소식은 안셀모에게서 제정신뿐 아니라 목숨마저 거의 앗아 갈 뻔했다. 그는 간신히 몸을 일으켜 벗의 집에 다다랐는데, 그 벗은 아직 안셀모의 불행을 전혀 모르고 있었으나, 그가 창백하고 초췌하며 수척한 모습으로 오는 것을 보고는 그가 어떤 무거운 고통을 겪고 있음을 알아차렸다. 안셀모는 곧장 쉬게 해 달라고, 그리고 글 쓸 도구를 달라고 청했다. 그의 청은 받아들여졌고, 그가 그러기를 바랐던지라—심지어 문도 잠가 주기를 바랐던지라—그는 홀로 누운 채 남겨졌다. 홀로 있게 되자 그는 제 불행에 대한 생각을 어찌나 마음 깊이 새겼던지, 제 안에서 느껴지는 죽음의 징후로 자기 목숨이 끝나 가고 있음을 잘 알게 되었고, 그리하여 자신의 기이한 죽음의 원인을 밝히는 글을 남기기로 결심했다. 그는 글을 쓰기 시작했으나, 하려던 말을 다 적어 내려가기도 전에 숨이 끊어져, 그릇된 호기심이 그에게 안긴 고통의 희생양이 되어 목숨을 내놓고 말았다. 집주인은 이제 시간이 늦었는데도 안셀모가 부르지 않는 것을 보고, 들어가서 그의 병세가 더해 가는지 알아보기로 마음먹었다. 들어가 보니 그는 엎드린 채, 몸의 일부는 침대에, 일부는 글 쓰던 책상 위에 걸쳐져 있었고, 그 책상 위에는 글 쓴 종이가 펼쳐져 있었으며 펜은 여전히 그의 손에 쥐어져 있었다. 주인은 먼저 그를 불러 보았으나 아무 대답이 없자, 다가가 그의 손을 잡아 보고는 그것이 차갑게 식은 것을 느꼈고, 그가 죽었음을 알았다. 크게 놀라고 비탄에 잠긴 그는 집안 식구들을 불러 안셀모에게 닥친 슬픈 운명을 함께 지켜보게 했다. 그러고 나서 그는 그 종이를 읽었는데, 그 필체가 안셀모의 것임을 알아보았으며, 거기에는 이런 말이 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"어리석고 그릇된 욕망이 내게서 목숨을 앗아 갔다. 내 죽음의 소식이 카밀라의 귀에 닿거든, 내가 그녀를 용서한다는 것을 알게 하라. 그녀는 기적을 행할 의무가 없었고, 나 또한 그녀에게 기적을 행하라 요구하지 말았어야 했으니. 그리고 내가 내 불명예의 장본인이었으니, 무슨 까닭으로—\"\n\n안셀모는 여기까지 썼으니, 이로써 분명한 것은 하려던 말을 채 끝맺기도 전에 이 대목에서 그의 목숨이 다했다는 사실이다. 이튿날 그의 벗은 그의 죽음을 친척들에게 알렸는데, 그들은 이미 그의 불행을 알고 있었으며, 카밀라가 누워 있는 수녀원에도 알렸으니, 그녀는 거의 남편을 따라 그 피할 수 없는 여정에 오를 지경에 이르러 있었다. 그것은 남편의 죽음을 알리는 소식 때문이 아니라, 자기 연인이 떠났다는 소식을 받았기 때문이었다. 비록 자신이 과부가 된 처지였으나, 그녀는 수녀원을 떠나는 것도 베일을 쓰는 것도 거부했다고 전한다. 그러다 얼마 지나지 않아, 너무 늦게 뉘우친 그녀의 연인이 찾아갔던 나폴리 왕국에서, 라우트레크 공이 위대한 장군 곤살로 페르난데스 데 코르도바와 근래 벌인 전투 중에 로타리오가 전사했다는 소식이 그녀에게 닿았다. 이를 알게 된 카밀라는 베일을 썼고, 얼마 후 슬픔과 우울에 시달리다 세상을 떠났다. 이것이 세 사람 모두의 끝이었으니, 분별없는 시작에서 비롯된 끝이었다.\n\n\"나는 이 소설이 마음에 드네.\" 신부가 말했다. \"허나 그것이 사실이라고는 도무지 믿어지지 않으이. 그리고 만일 지어낸 것이라면 작자의 창작에 결함이 있는 것이, 안셀모처럼 그토록 값비싼 시험을 해 보려는 어리석은 남편이 있으리라고는 도저히 상상할 수 없기 때문일세. 만일 한량과 그 정부 사이에 일어난 일로 그려졌다면 그럴듯하게 넘어갈 만하지. 허나 남편과 아내 사이라니, 거기에는 불가능한 구석이 있단 말일세. 다만 이야기를 풀어 나간 방식으로 말하자면, 나로서는 흠잡을 데가 없네.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVI.\n\n바로 그 순간, 객줏집 문간에 서 있던 주인이 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"근사한 손님 한 무리가 오고 있구먼. 저 사람들이 여기서 묵는다면 가우데아무스라 할 만하지.\"\n\n# WHICH TREATS OF MORE CURIOUS INCIDENTS THAT OCCURRED AT THE INN\n\n* 가우데아무스(gaudeamus): 라틴어로 \"기뻐하자\"라는 뜻.\n\n\"어떤 사람들이오?\" 카르데니오가 물었다.\n\n\"네 사람이오.\" 주인이 말했다. \"라 히네타식으로 말을 타고, 창과 둥근 방패를 들었으며, 모두 검은 베일을 쓰고 있소. 그들과 함께 흰옷을 입은 여인이 한 사람 곁안장에 앉아 있는데 그 얼굴 역시 베일에 가려져 있고, 걸어서 따라오는 시종이 둘이오.\"\n* 라 히네타(a la jineta): 등자를 짧게 하고 무릎을 굽혀 타는 무어식 승마법.\n\n\"아주 가까이 왔소?\" 신부가 물었다.\n\n\"어찌나 가까운지,\" 주인이 대답했다. \"벌써 이리 들어오는구먼.\"\n\n이 말을 듣고 도로테아는 얼굴을 가렸고, 카르데니오는 돈키호테의 방으로 물러났으니, 그들이 그리할 겨를도 채 없이 주인이 묘사한 그 무리 전체가 객줏집으로 들어섰다. 말을 타고 있던 네 사람은 기품 있는 용모와 태도를 지녔는데, 말에서 내려 곁안장에 탄 여인을 부축해 내리려고 앞으로 나섰다. 그중 한 사람이 여인을 두 팔로 받아 안아, 카르데니오가 몸을 숨긴 방 입구에 놓인 의자에 앉혔다. 그동안 내내 여인도 그들도 베일을 벗지 않았고 한마디도 하지 않았으며, 다만 의자에 앉으면서 여인이 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 병들고 쇠약한 사람처럼 두 팔을 늘어뜨릴 뿐이었다. 걸어서 따라온 시종들은 그러고 나서 말들을 마구간으로 끌고 갔다. 이를 지켜보던 신부는 이런 차림에 이토록 침묵을 지키는 사람들이 대체 누구인지 궁금하여, 하인들이 서 있는 곳으로 가서 그중 한 사람에게 물었고, 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"솔직히 말씀드리자면, 나리, 저들이 누구인지 저로서는 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다. 다만 신분이 높은 분들 같다는 것만 알지요. 특히 보신 그 부인을 두 팔로 안아 내린 분이 그렇습니다. 이렇게 말씀드리는 까닭은 나머지 사람들이 모두 그분께 경의를 표하고, 그분이 지시하고 명령하는 대로만 모든 일이 이루어지기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 그 부인은 누구요?\" 신부가 물었다.\n\n\"그것 역시 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다.\" 하인이 말했다. \"오는 길 내내 그분의 얼굴을 보지 못했으니까요. 한숨을 여러 번 내쉬고 매번 숨이 넘어갈 듯한 신음을 토하는 것은 분명히 들었습니다만, 저희가 말씀드린 것 이상을 모른다 해도 이상할 게 없습지요. 제 동료와 저는 저분들과 함께한 지 겨우 이틀밖에 안 됐으니 말입니다. 길에서 저희를 만나, 보수를 두둑이 주겠노라 약속하며 안달루시아까지 동행해 달라 간청하고 설득했거든요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그들 가운데 누가 이름으로 불리는 것을 들은 적이 있소?\" 신부가 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 정말로.\" 하인이 대답했다. \"길에서 다들 놀랍도록 침묵을 지켜서, 그 가엾은 부인의 한숨과 흐느낌 말고는 그들 사이에서 아무 소리도 들리지 않습니다. 그 소리가 저희를 가엾게 만들지요. 그리고 저희는 그분이 어디로 가든 본인의 뜻과는 어긋나는 길이라고 확신합니다. 차림새로 미루어 판단하건대 그분은 수녀이거나, 더 그럴듯하게는 수녀가 되려는 분 같습니다. 어쩌면 서원하는 것이 본인의 자유로운 뜻이 아니어서 저렇게 불행해 보이는지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"능히 그럴 만하오.\" 신부는 이렇게 말하고 그들을 떠나 도로테아가 있는 곳으로 돌아왔다. 도로테아는 베일을 쓴 부인이 한숨 쉬는 소리를 듣고 타고난 연민에 이끌려 그녀에게 다가가 말했다. \"무엇 때문에 그리 괴로워하십니까, 세뇨라? 여인들이 으레 덜어 드릴 줄 아는 그런 것이라면, 진심을 다해 제 도움을 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n이 말에 불행한 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 도로테아가 더욱 간곡히 거듭 청해도 여전히 침묵을 지켰다. 그러다 마침내, 하인이 나머지 사람들이 복종한다고 한 그 베일 쓴 신사가 다가와 도로테아에게 말했다. \"수고스럽게 저 여인에게 무엇을 베풀려 애쓰지 마십시오, 세뇨라. 누가 무엇을 해 주어도 고마워할 줄 모르는 것이 저 여인의 버릇입니다. 그리고 입에서 거짓말이나 듣고 싶지 않거든 대답을 시키려 들지 마십시오.\"\n\n\"저는 한 번도 거짓을 말한 적이 없습니다.\" 지금껏 침묵하던 여인이 즉각 대답했다. \"오히려 제가 너무도 진실하고 거짓을 꾸미는 술수에 너무도 어두운 탓에 지금 이 비참한 처지에 빠진 것이지요. 이 점은 당신 자신을 증인으로 부르겠습니다. 당신을 거짓되고 거짓말쟁이로 만든 것은 다름 아닌 더럽혀지지 않은 저의 진실이니까요.\"\n\n카르데니오는 그 말을 또렷하고 분명하게 들었으니, 말하는 이와 아주 가까이 있었던 데다 그들 사이에는 돈키호테 방의 문 하나밖에 없었기 때문이다. 그는 그 말을 듣는 순간 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"맙소사! 이게 무슨 소리인가? 내 귀에 들려온 이 목소리는 대체 누구의 것인가?\" 그 목소리에 놀란 부인은 고개를 돌렸으나, 말한 이가 보이지 않자 자리에서 일어나 방으로 들어가려 했다. 이를 본 신사가 그녀를 붙잡아 한 발짝도 움직이지 못하게 막았다. 그녀가 동요하여 갑작스레 몸을 움직이는 바람에 얼굴을 가렸던 비단이 흘러내려, 비할 데 없이 놀랍도록 아름다우나 창백하고 겁에 질린 얼굴이 드러났다. 그녀는 눈길이 닿는 곳마다 두 눈을 이리저리 돌렸는데, 그 간절함이 마치 제정신을 잃은 듯 보일 만큼 두드러져, 영문을 알지 못하면서도 도로테아와 그 모습을 본 모든 이의 연민을 자아냈다. 신사는 그녀의 양어깨를 단단히 붙들었고, 그녀를 막느라 온통 정신이 팔린 나머지, 흘러내리는 자신의 베일에는 손을 쓸 수가 없어 마침내 그것이 완전히 떨어지고 말았다. 그 부인을 두 팔로 안고 있던 도로테아가 눈을 들어 보니, 마찬가지로 그녀를 붙들고 있는 이가 바로 자기 남편 돈 페르난도였다. 그를 알아본 순간, 도로테아는 가슴 깊은 곳에서 길게 끌려 나온 애처로운 비명을 지르며 뒤로 넘어가 정신을 잃었으니, 마침 가까이 있던 이발사가 두 팔로 받아 안지 않았더라면 그대로 땅바닥에 쓰러지고 말았을 것이다. 신부가 곧장 달려가 그녀의 얼굴을 드러내고 물을 끼얹었는데, 그가 그리하는 동안 다른 여인을 안고 있던 돈 페르난도가 — 바로 그였다 — 도로테아를 알아보고는, 그 광경에 죽은 사람처럼 굳어 서 있었다. 그러면서도 루신다를 붙든 손은 늦추지 않았으니, 카르데니오가 자신을 알아본 것처럼 그녀 역시 목소리로 카르데니오를 알아보고는 그의 손아귀에서 벗어나려 몸부림치고 있었기 때문이다. 카르데니오도 도로테아가 기절하며 쓰러질 때 지른 비명을 듣고는, 그것이 자기 루신다에게서 나온 것이라 여겨 공포에 사로잡혀 방에서 뛰쳐나왔는데, 가장 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은 루신다를 두 팔로 안고 있는 돈 페르난도였다. 돈 페르난도 역시 카르데니오를 단번에 알아보았다. 그리하여 루신다, 카르데니오, 도로테아 셋은 자기들에게 무슨 일이 일어난 것인지 거의 알지 못한 채 말없이 놀라 서 있었다.\n\n그들은 말 한마디 없이 서로를 바라보았으니, 도로테아는 돈 페르난도를, 돈 페르난도는 카르데니오를, 카르데니오는 루신다를, 루신다는 카르데니오를 바라보았다. 침묵을 처음으로 깨뜨린 이는 루신다였으니, 그녀는 돈 페르난도에게 이렇게 말했다. \"저를 놓아주세요, 세뇨르 돈 페르난도, 당신 자신에게 진 도리를 봐서라도요. 다른 어떤 이유로도 마음이 움직이지 않으신다면, 제가 담쟁이덩굴로서 매달려 있는 그 벽에, 당신의 끈질긴 청도, 위협도, 약속도, 선물도 저를 떼어 내지 못한 그 버팀목에 제가 매달리도록 놓아주세요. 보세요, 하늘이 우리 눈에는 낯설고 감추어진 방식으로 저를 제 진정한 남편과 얼굴을 마주하도록 이끌어 주었으니까요. 그리고 죽음만이 그를 제 기억에서 지울 수 있으리라는 것을, 당신은 비싼 값을 치르고 얻은 경험으로 잘 알고 계시지요. 그러니 이 명백한 선언이, 달리 어쩔 도리가 없는 당신을 움직여, 당신의 사랑을 분노로, 당신의 애정을 원한으로 바꾸어 제 목숨을 거두시게 하기를 바랍니다. 제가 사랑하는 남편 앞에서 목숨을 내놓을 수 있다면 그것은 값지게 바쳐진 것이라 여기겠습니다. 제 죽음으로 그가, 제가 생의 마지막 순간까지 그에 대한 신의를 지켰음을 확신하게 될지도 모르니까요.\"\n\n그러는 사이 도로테아는 정신을 차려 루신다의 말을 들었고, 그 말로써 그녀가 누구인지 짐작했다. 그러나 돈 페르난도가 여전히 그녀를 놓아주지도, 그녀에게 대답하지도 않는 것을 보고는, 할 수 있는 한 결심을 가다듬어 일어나 그의 발 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 맑고 가슴을 울리는 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 그에게 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"나리, 그대가 두 팔 안에 가두어 빛을 가린 저 태양의 광채가 그대의 눈을 부시게 하여 시력을 앗아 가지 않았다면, 그대는 이미 그대의 발 앞에 무릎 꿇은 이 여인이, 그대가 그리 원하시는 한 불행하고 가엾은 도로테아임을 알아보셨을 것입니다. 저는 그대가 선의로, 혹은 그대의 즐거움을 위해 그대의 것이라 일컬을 만큼 높이 끌어올리려 하셨던 그 미천한 시골 처녀입니다. 저는 그대의 집요한 청과, 진실하고 다정해 보이던 그 열정의 목소리에 정숙의 문을 열고 그대에게 제 자유의 열쇠를 내어 줄 때까지, 순결의 은둔 속에서 만족스러운 삶을 살던 바로 그 여인입니다. 그대가 받은 그 선물에 아무런 고마움도 표하지 않았음은, 그대가 저를 발견한 이곳으로 제가 떠밀려 와야 했다는 사실로, 또 제가 지금 보는 이 처지에 그대가 나타났다는 사실로 명백히 드러나지요. 그렇지만 제가 수치심에 떠밀려 이곳에 왔다고 여기지는 말아 주십시오. 저를 이끈 것은 오직 그대에게 잊혔다는 것을 보는 데서 오는 비탄과 슬픔뿐입니다. 저를 그대의 것으로 삼는 것이 그대의 뜻이었고, 그대가 그 뜻을 따랐으니, 이제 그대가 후회한다 해도 제 것이 되지 않을 수 없습니다. 생각해 보세요, 나리, 제가 그대에게 품은 더없는 애정이, 그대가 저를 버리고 좇으려는 아름다움과 고귀한 혈통을 보상해 줄 수 있을지도 모릅니다. 그대는 제 것이니 아름다운 루신다의 것이 될 수 없고, 그녀는 카르데니오의 것이니 그대의 것이 될 수 없습니다. 그러니 기억하세요, 지금 그대를 혐오하는 이를 그대를 사랑하도록 이끄는 것보다, 그대를 흠모하는 이를 사랑하도록 그대의 뜻을 굽히는 편이 더 쉬울 것입니다. 그대는 저의 순진함을 노렸고, 저의 정절을 포위했으며, 저의 신분을 모르지 않았고, 제가 그대의 뜻에 온전히 굴복한 것을 잘 알고 계십니다. 그러니 그대가 속았다고 호소할 근거도 이유도 없습니다. 만일 그렇다면, 정말 그러하니, 그대가 신사인 만큼 그리스도교도라면, 어찌하여 그러한 핑계로, 처음에 저를 행복하게 했던 것처럼 끝내 저를 행복하게 해 주는 일을 미루십니까? 그리고 제가 진정하고 적법한 그대의 아내라는 본모습으로 저를 받아들이지 않으시겠다면, 적어도 저를 그대의 노예로라도 받아 주세요. 제가 그대의 것인 한, 저는 스스로를 행복하고 운 좋은 사람으로 여길 테니까요. 저를 버려 제 수치가 거리의 입방아꾼들의 이야깃거리가 되게 하지 마세요. 제 부모님의 노년을 비참하게 만들지 마세요. 충실한 봉신으로서 그분들이 그대의 가문에 늘 바쳐 온 충성스러운 섬김은 그런 보답을 받을 만한 것이 아니니까요. 그리고 그대의 피를 제 피와 섞는 것이 그대의 혈통을 천하게 만든다고 여기신다면, 같은 길을 걷지 않은 고귀한 가문이 세상에 거의 혹은 전혀 없음을, 또 명문가에서 중요한 것은 여자의 피가 아님을 헤아려 주세요. 더욱이 참된 고귀함은 덕에 있으니, 그대가 그 덕을 결여하여 마땅히 제게 갚아야 할 것을 거부한다면, 그렇다면 저야말로 그대보다 고귀함에 대한 더 높은 권리를 지닌 셈입니다. 마지막으로 말씀드립니다, 세뇨르, 이것이 그대에게 드리는 저의 마지막 말입니다. 그대가 원하든 원치 않든, 저는 그대의 아내입니다. 그 결여 때문에 그대가 저를 업신여기는 바로 그것, 곧 그대가 자랑으로 삼는다면 거짓이어서는 안 되고 또 그래서도 안 될 그대의 말이 증인입니다. 그대가 제게 준 맹세가 증인이며, 그대가 제게 한 약속의 증인으로 그대 자신이 불러낸 하늘이 증인입니다. 이 모든 것이 무력하다 해도, 그대 자신의 양심이 그대의 온갖 환락 한가운데서 그 말없는 목소리를 높여, 제가 한 말의 진실을 입증하고 그대의 더없는 쾌락과 향락을 망치는 일만큼은 결코 그치지 않을 것입니다.\"\n\n상처 입은 도로테아는 이 모든 말과 그 이상을 너무도 간절한 심정으로, 너무도 많은 눈물과 함께 토해 내어, 돈 페르난도와 함께 온 자들까지 그 자리에 있던 모든 이가 그녀와 더불어 눈물 흘리지 않을 수 없었다. 돈 페르난도는 아무 대답도 없이 그녀의 말을 듣고만 있다가, 그녀가 말을 마치고 흐느낌과 한숨을 터뜨리니, 그토록 큰 슬픔의 광경에 누그러지지 않는다면 그것은 청동으로 된 심장이라 할 만했다. 루신다는 그녀의 고통을 향한 연민과 그녀의 지성과 미모를 향한 감탄을 똑같이 품고 그녀를 바라보았으며, 위로의 말이라도 건네려고 다가가려 했으나, 자신을 단단히 붙든 돈 페르난도의 손에 가로막혔다. 그는 당혹과 경악에 압도되어 한동안 도로테아를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 두 팔을 벌려 루신다를 놓아주며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그대가 이겼소, 아름다운 도로테아, 그대가 이겼소. 이토록 많은 진실이 한데 모인 힘을 모질게 부정할 마음이란 도무지 가질 수 없으니 말이오.\"\n\n돈 페르난도가 놓아주자 루신다는 기력이 다해 막 땅에 쓰러지려 했으나, 알아볼까 두려워 돈 페르난도 뒤로 물러나 가까이 서 있던 카르데니오가 두려움을 떨치고 무슨 일이 벌어지든 아랑곳없이 달려 나와 그녀를 부축하고는, 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안으며 말했다. \"하늘이 자비를 베풀어 그대가 마침내 쉬게 하려 하신다면, 내 마음의 주인이여, 진실하고 한결같으며 아름다운 이여, 지금 그대를 받아 안고, 운명이 그대를 내 것이라 부르도록 허락했던 그때에도 그대를 받아 안았던 이 두 팔 안에서보다 더 안전하게 쉴 곳은 어디에도 없으리다.\"\n\n이 말에 루신다는 카르데니오를 올려다보았으니, 처음에는 목소리로 그를 알아보기 시작하다가 이윽고 두 눈으로 그가 틀림없음을 확인하고는, 자기가 무엇을 하는지도 거의 모른 채, 또 예법에 관한 온갖 고려도 아랑곳없이 두 팔을 그의 목에 두르고 얼굴을 그의 얼굴에 바짝 대고 말했다. \"그래요, 나의 사랑하는 주인이여, 비록 모진 운명이 다시 끼어들어 당신의 목숨에 매달린 이 목숨을 새로운 위험들이 위협한다 해도, 당신은 이 노예의 진정한 주인이십니다.\"\n\n이는 돈 페르난도와 둘레에 서 있던 이들에게 기이한 광경이었으니, 그토록 뜻밖의 사건에 모두 놀라움으로 가득 찼다. 도로테아는 돈 페르난도의 안색이 변하며 카르데니오에게 복수하려는 듯이 보인다고 여겼으니, 그가 칼에 손을 가져가는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 그 생각이 떠오르는 순간 그녀는 놀라운 민첩함으로 그의 무릎을 끌어안고, 거기에 입을 맞추며 그가 움직이지 못하게 붙들고는, 여전히 눈물을 흘리며 말했다. \"이 뜻밖의 일에 무엇을 하시려는 것입니까, 저의 유일한 피난처여? 그대의 아내가 그대의 발 앞에 있고, 그대가 아내로 삼으려던 이는 자기 남편의 품에 안겨 있습니다. 하늘이 이룬 일을 그대가 되돌리는 것이 옳은 일일지, 아니 그것이 그대에게 가능한 일이기나 한지, 또 온갖 장애에도 불구하고 자신의 진실과 한결같음으로 굳건하여 그대의 눈앞에서 사랑의 눈물로 적법한 남편의 얼굴과 가슴을 적시고 있는 그 여인을 그대의 짝으로 끌어올리려 애쓰는 것이 그대에게 어울리는 일일지 헤아려 보세요. 하느님의 이름으로 그대에게 간청하며, 그대 자신을 위해 그대에게 애원합니다. 이 공공연한 사실의 드러남이 그대의 분노를 일깨우지 않게 하시고, 오히려 그 분노를 가라앉혀, 하늘이 허락하는 한 이 두 연인이 그대의 어떤 간섭도 없이 평화롭고 조용히 살아가도록 해 주세요. 그리하시면 그대는 고귀하고 드높은 정신의 너그러움을 입증하실 것이며, 그대에게는 정념보다 이성이 더 큰 힘을 지녔음을 온 세상이 보게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n도로테아가 말하는 내내, 카르데니오는 루신다를 두 팔에 안고 있으면서도 돈 페르난도에게서 눈을 떼지 않았으니, 그가 적대적인 움직임을 보이기만 하면 제 목숨을 내놓는 한이 있더라도 스스로를 지키려 애쓰고, 자신을 공격할지 모르는 모든 자에게 힘닿는 데까지 맞서리라 작정하고 있었다. 그러나 이제 돈 페르난도의 벗들은 물론, 줄곧 그 자리에 있던 신부와 이발사가, 또 충직한 산초 판사도 잊지 않고 달려 나와 돈 페르난도를 빙 둘러싸고는, 도로테아의 눈물을 헤아려 그녀의 정당한 희망이 좌절되지 않게 해 달라고 간청했으니, 그들이 굳게 믿기로 그녀가 한 말은 다름 아닌 진실이었기 때문이다. 그리고 그들은 그에게 일러두기를, 이렇게 보일지 모르나 그들이 아무도 만남을 기대할 수 없었던 한 장소에서 다 함께 마주친 것은 우연이 아니라 섭리의 특별한 안배에 의한 것임을 헤아리라고 했다. 또 신부는 그에게 일깨우기를, 오직 죽음만이 루신다를 카르데니오에게서 떼어 놓을 수 있으며, 설령 어떤 칼이 그들을 갈라놓는다 해도 그들은 그 죽음을 더없이 행복하게 여길 것이라 했다. 그러니 아무런 처방도 없는 경우에 그가 취할 가장 현명한 길은, 스스로를 이기고 절제함으로써 너그러운 마음을 보여, 자진하여 이 두 사람이 하늘이 베푼 행복을 누리게 하는 것이라 했다. 또 그는 돈 페르난도에게 도로테아의 아름다움에 눈을 돌려 보라 했으니, 그러면 그녀에 견줄 만한 이조차 거의 없거니와 그녀를 넘어설 이는 더더욱 없음을 알게 되리라 했다. 게다가 그 아름다움에 더하여 그녀의 정숙함과 그녀가 그에게 품은 그지없는 사랑까지 헤아려야 한다고 했다. 그러나 이 모든 것에 더하여 신부는 그에게, 그가 신사이자 그리스도교도임을 자랑으로 삼는다면 맹세한 말을 지키는 것 외에 달리 할 도리가 없음을 일깨웠다. 그리고 그리함으로써 그는 하느님께 순종하고 분별 있는 모든 이의 인정을 얻게 되리라 했으니, 그들은 미천한 가문의 여인일지라도 덕이 함께한다면 그 아름다움이, 자신과 동등한 자리에 그녀를 두는 이에게 아무런 흠도 되지 않으면서 어떤 신분의 수준에라도 스스로를 끌어올릴 수 있음이 아름다움의 특권임을 알고 또 인정하는 사람들이었다. 더 나아가 그는, 정념의 강력한 힘이 작용할 때, 거기에 죄가 섞여 있지 않은 한 그에 굴복하는 자는 비난받을 일이 아니라고 했다.\n\n요컨대 그들은 이런 말에 다른 강력한 논거들까지 보태었고, 결국 고귀한 피로 길러진 돈 페르난도의 사내다운 마음은 감동하여, 설령 원했다 해도 거스를 수 없었을 진실에 굴복했다. 그는 몸을 굽혀 도로테아를 끌어안음으로써, 자신에게 베풀어진 좋은 충고를 받아들이고 굴복했음을 보이며 그녀에게 말했다. \"일어나시오, 친애하는 부인. 내가 가슴에 품은 이가 내 발 앞에 무릎 꿇고 있는 것은 옳지 않소. 그리고 지금까지 내가 마음에 품은 것을 아무런 내색도 하지 않았다면, 그것은 어쩌면 하늘의 뜻이었을지도 모르오. 그대가 나를 사랑하는 그 한결같음을 보고, 그대가 받아 마땅한 만큼 그대를 귀히 여기는 법을 내가 배우도록 말이오. 내가 그대에게 간청하는 것은, 내 잘못과 모진 악행을 두고 나를 책망하지 말아 달라는 것이오. 나로 하여금 그대를 내 것으로 삼게 한 바로 그 까닭과 힘이, 그대의 것이 되지 않으려 발버둥치게 만들었던 것이니 말이오. 이를 입증하려거든, 돌아서서 이제 행복해진 루신다의 눈을 보시오. 그러면 그 눈 속에서 내 모든 잘못에 대한 변명을 보게 될 것이오. 그녀가 자신이 바라던 바를 찾아 얻었고, 나 또한 그대에게서 내 모든 소원을 채워 줄 것을 찾았으니, 그녀가 자기 카르데니오와 더불어 평화롭고 만족스럽게 오래도록 행복한 세월을 살기를 바라며, 나도 내 도로테아와 더불어 살도록 하늘이 허락해 주기를 무릎 꿇고 비는 바이오.\" 이렇게 말하고 그는 다시 한번 그녀를 끌어안고는 너무도 다정하게 자기 얼굴을 그녀의 얼굴에 댔으니, 모두가 보는 앞에서 눈물이 그의 사랑과 뉘우침의 증거를 마저 완성해 버리지 않도록 무던히도 조심해야 했다. 루신다와 카르데니오, 그리고 나머지 거의 모든 사람은 그렇지 않았으니, 어떤 이는 제 행복에, 어떤 이는 남의 행복에 어찌나 많은 눈물을 흘렸던지, 그들 모두에게 무거운 재앙이 닥쳤다고 여겨질 정도였다. 산초 판사조차 울고 있었다. 다만 그는 나중에 말하기를, 도로테아가 자기가 생각하던 미코미코나 여왕이 아니라는 것을 알았기 때문에 운 것일 뿐이라 했으니, 그는 그 여왕에게서 그토록 큰 은혜를 기대하고 있었던 것이다. 그들의 놀라움도, 눈물도 한동안 이어지다가, 이윽고 카르데니오와 루신다가 돈 페르난도 앞에 무릎 꿇고, 그가 베풀어 준 은혜에 너무도 감사에 찬 말로 사례하니, 그는 무어라 대답해야 할지 알지 못한 채 그들을 일으켜 세워 온갖 애정과 예의의 표시로 끌어안았다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 도로테아에게, 어떻게 자기 집에서 그토록 멀리 떨어진 곳까지 올 수 있었는지 물었고, 그녀는 앞서 카르데니오에게 들려준 그 모든 이야기를 몇 마디 알맞은 말로 전했다. 그 이야기에 돈 페르난도와 그의 동행들은 어찌나 즐거워했던지 이야기가 더 길었더라면 좋았겠다고 바랄 정도였으니, 그만큼 도로테아는 자신의 불운한 일들을 매혹적으로 풀어놓았다. 그녀가 이야기를 마치자 돈 페르난도는, 루신다의 가슴속에서 그녀가 카르데니오의 아내이며 결코 자신의 아내가 될 수 없다고 선언한 그 종이를 발견한 뒤 도시에서 자신에게 일어난 일을 이야기했다. 그는 그녀를 죽이려 했고, 그녀의 부모가 막지 않았더라면 그리했을 것이며, 분노와 수치심에 가득 차 그 집을 떠나 더 알맞은 기회가 오면 복수하리라 마음먹었다고 했다. 이튿날 그는 루신다가 아버지의 집에서 사라졌고 아무도 그녀가 어디로 갔는지 모른다는 것을 알게 되었다. 마침내 몇 달이 지난 끝에 그는, 그녀가 한 수녀원에 있으며 카르데니오와 함께 살 수 없다면 평생 그곳에 머무를 작정이라는 것을 알아냈다. 그리고 이를 알자마자 그는 이 세 신사를 동행으로 삼아 그녀가 있는 곳에 다다랐으나, 자신이 거기 있음이 알려지면 수녀원에서 더 엄중한 경계를 펼칠까 두려워 그녀에게 말을 거는 것은 피했다. 그러다 문지기의 방이 열려 있는 때를 노려 둘을 남겨 문을 지키게 하고, 자신과 나머지 한 사람이 루신다를 찾아 수녀원으로 들어갔으니, 그녀가 수녀 한 사람과 회랑에서 이야기를 나누고 있는 것을 발견하여, 저항할 틈도 주지 않고 그녀를 데리고 나와, 그녀를 데려가는 데 필요한 것들을 갖춘 한 장소에 이르렀다고 했다. 이 모든 일을 그들이 완벽히 안전하게 해낼 수 있었던 것은, 그 수녀원이 도시에서 상당히 떨어진 시골에 있었기 때문이다. 그는 덧붙이기를, 루신다가 자신이 그의 손아귀에 들어왔음을 알았을 때 온통 정신을 잃었고, 정신을 차린 뒤로는 한마디 말도 없이 울고 한숨짓는 일밖에 하지 않았다고 했다. 그리하여 그들은 침묵과 눈물 속에서 그 객줏집에 다다랐으니, 그에게 그곳은 땅 위의 온갖 불행이 다 끝나고 마는 천국에 다다른 것이나 마찬가지였다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII.\n\n이 모든 것을 산초는 적잖이 가슴 아파하며 듣고 있었으니, 자신의 영화로운 신분에 대한 희망이 연기처럼 스러져 사라지는 것을, 그리고 아름다운 미코미코나 공주가 도로테아로 변하고 거인이 돈 페르난도로 변해 버린 것을 지켜보았기 때문이다. 한편 그의 주인은 그동안 벌어진 모든 일을 까맣게 모른 채 평온히 잠들어 있었다. 도로테아는 지금의 이 행복이 한낱 꿈이 아니라는 사실을 스스로도 믿을 수가 없었고, 카르데니오 역시 비슷한 심정이었으며, 루신다의 생각도 같은 곳을 향해 달려가고 있었다. 돈 페르난도는 자신에게 베풀어진 은총에 대해, 그리고 자신의 명예와 영혼을 파멸 직전까지 몰아넣었던 그 얽히고설킨 미궁에서 구원받은 것에 대해 하늘에 감사를 드렸다. 요컨대 객줏집에 있던 모든 이가 이토록 복잡하고 가망 없던 일이 행복한 결말에 이른 것에 만족과 흡족함으로 가득 차 있었다. 분별 있는 사람인 신부는 이 모든 사태에 대해 건전한 성찰을 펼치며 저마다의 행운을 축하해 주었다. 그러나 가장 들뜨고 기분 좋아한 이는 객줏집 안주인이었으니, 돈키호테 탓에 입은 손실과 손해를 모두 갚아 주겠노라 카르데니오와 신부가 약속해 준 까닭이었다. 앞서 말했듯이 괴로워하고 불행해하며 풀이 죽은 이는 오직 산초뿐이었다. 그리하여 그는 침울한 얼굴로, 막 잠에서 깨어난 주인에게 들어가 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"슬픈 몰골의 기사 나리, 나리께서야 마음껏 더 주무셔도 되겠습니다요. 거인을 죽이느니 공주님께 왕국을 되찾아 주느니 하는 일로 더는 골치 썩이실 것 없으니까요. 그건 다 끝났고 죄다 정리됐거든요.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 여기노라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내 평생 기억하는 한 가장 어마어마하고 엄청난 싸움을 그 거인과 벌였느니라. 단번에 내리치는 한 칼에 — 휙! — 그 머리를 땅바닥에 굴러떨어지게 했으니, 그자에게서 어찌나 피가 솟구쳐 나왔던지 물처럼 땅 위를 개울 이루며 흘렀느니라.\"\n\n\"붉은 포도주처럼이라고 하시는 게 나을 겁니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"혹시 모르신다면 일러 드리지요. 그 죽은 거인이라는 건 칼질당한 포도주 부대였고, 그 피라는 건 부대 속에 들었던 붉은 포도주 스물네 갤런이었습니다요. 게다가 잘려 나간 머리란 건 저를 낳아 준 어미 암캐였으니, 그 모든 게 악마한테나 가 버리라죠.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리를 지껄이는 게냐, 이 멍청아?\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"네 제정신이냐?\"\n\n\"나리, 일어나 보시지요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그러면 나리께서 무슨 멋들어진 일을 저질러 놓으셨는지, 또 우리가 뭘 물어 줘야 하는지 보실 수 있을 겁니다요. 그러면 여왕이 도로테아라는 평범한 귀부인으로 변한 것도 보실 테고, 알아들으시기만 한다면 깜짝 놀라실 다른 일들도 보시게 될 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"나는 그 따위 일에는 전혀 놀라지 않을 것이니라.\" 돈키호테가 대꾸했다. \"우리가 지난번에 여기 왔을 때 내가 네게 말하지 않았더냐, 여기서 일어나는 모든 일은 마법의 소관이라고. 그러니 지금도 똑같다 한들 조금도 이상할 게 없느니라.\"\n\n\"저도 그 모든 걸 믿을 수 있겠습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제가 담요 키질당한 것도 그런 종류의 일이었다면 말입죠. 하지만 그건 그렇지가 않았고, 진짜배기에 틀림없는 일이었습니다요. 오늘도 여기 있는 객줏집 주인이 담요 한쪽 끝을 붙잡고서 저를 아주 깔끔하고 날렵하게, 힘쓰는 만큼이나 웃어 가며 하늘로 휙휙 차올리는 걸 제 눈으로 봤거든요. 그리고 사람을 알아본다는 점에 대해서 말씀드리자면, 저야 단순하고 죄 많은 인간입니다만, 거기엔 마법이라곤 전혀 없고 오로지 멍투성이에다 지독한 불운만 잔뜩 있었다는 게 제 생각입니다요.\"\n\n\"좋다, 좋아. 하느님께서 해결책을 주시리라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내 옷을 가져오너라, 그리고 나가게 해 다오. 네가 말하는 그 변신이니 뭐니 하는 것들을 보고 싶으니라.\"\n\n산초는 그에게 옷을 가져다주었다. 그가 옷을 입는 동안 신부는 돈 페르난도와 그 자리에 있던 다른 이들에게 돈키호테의 광기에 대해, 그리고 그가 자기 귀부인의 멸시 때문에 머물러 있노라 여겼던 그 페냐 포브레에서 그를 빼내려고 자기들이 썼던 계략에 대해 이야기해 주었다. 그는 또한 산초가 언급했던 거의 모든 모험을 그들에게 들려주었고, 그들은 그 이야기에 적잖이 놀라며 웃어 댔으니, 정신 나간 자의 머리가 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 기이한 형태의 광기라고 모두가 입을 모아 여겼다. 그러나 이제, 신부가 말하기를, 도로테아 부인의 행운으로 인해 본래의 계획을 더 진행할 수 없게 되었으니, 그를 집으로 데려갈 다른 방도를 궁리하거나 찾아내는 것이 필요하리라고 했다.\n\n카르데니오는 자기들이 시작했던 계획을 그대로 밀고 나가자고 제안하면서, 루신다가 도로테아의 배역을 충분히 잘 연기하고 떠받쳐 줄 거라고 의견을 냈다.\n\n\"아니오.\" 돈 페르난도가 말했다. \"그건 안 될 일이오. 나는 도로테아가 자기의 이 생각을 끝까지 이어 가기를 바라니까요. 그리고 이 훌륭하신 양반의 마을이 그리 멀지 않다면, 그분을 구하기 위해 내가 뭔가 할 수 있다면 기쁘겠소이다.\"\n\n\"여기서 이틀 길밖에 안 됩니다.\" 신부가 말했다.\n\n\"설령 더 멀다 해도,\" 돈 페르난도가 말했다. \"이렇게 좋은 일을 위해서라면 그만한 거리야 기꺼이 가겠소이다.\"\n\n바로 그 순간 돈키호테가 완전 무장을 갖추고 나왔으니, 온통 우그러진 그대로의 맘브리노 투구를 머리에 쓰고, 둥근 방패를 팔에 끼고, 자기 지팡이인지 창인지에 몸을 기댄 채였다. 그가 드러낸 기이한 형상은 돈 페르난도와 나머지 사람들을 경탄으로 가득 채웠으니, 반 레구아는 됨 직하게 길쭉한 그의 비쩍 마른 누런 얼굴과, 온갖 종류가 뒤섞인 갑주와, 그 거동의 엄숙함을 바라보면서였다. 그들은 그가 무슨 말을 할지 보려고 잠자코 기다렸고, 그는 아름다운 도로테아에게 두 눈을 고정한 채 더없이 무게 있고 침착한 태도로 그녀에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"아름다운 부인이여, 여기 있는 제 종자에게서 듣자 하니, 부인의 위대함이 산산이 무너지고 부인의 존재가 폐기되었다 하더이다. 한때 그러하셨던 여왕이자 고귀한 신분의 귀부인에서, 평범한 처녀로 변하셨다 하니 말이외다. 만일 이것이 마법사인 부왕의 명령으로 이루어진 일이라면, 제가 부인께 마땅히 드려야 할, 또 부인께서 받으실 자격이 있는 도움을 드리지 못할까 두려워서 그리하신 것이라면, 한 말씀 올리건대 그분께서는 미사의 절반도 알지 못하셨고 지금도 알지 못하시며, 기사도의 연대기에 거의 통달하지 못하셨던 것이외다. 그분께서 만일 제가 한 만큼 주의 깊고 신중하게 그것들을 읽고 통독하셨더라면, 저보다 명성이 덜한 기사들도 더 어려운 일들을 이루어 냈음을 곳곳에서 발견하셨을 터이니 말이외다. 거인 새끼 한 마리쯤 죽이는 일은, 그자가 아무리 오만방자할지언정 대단한 일이 아니외다. 저 자신이 한 거인과 맞붙은 지가 채 몇 시간도 지나지 않았으니 말이외다. 한데 — 그 이야기는 하지 않으리다. 제가 거짓말을 한다고들 할까 싶어서이외다. 허나 모든 것을 드러내는 시간이, 우리가 가장 뜻하지 않은 때에 그 사연을 일러 줄 것이외다.\"\n\n\"당신이 맞붙은 건 포도주 부대 두 짝이지 거인이 아니었소.\" 이때 객줏집 주인이 말했다. 그러나 돈 페르난도가 그에게 입을 다물라고, 무슨 일이 있어도 돈키호테의 말을 끊지 말라고 일렀고, 돈키호테는 말을 이었다. \"결론으로 말씀드리건대, 고귀하시되 상속권을 빼앗기신 부인이여, 만일 부왕께서 제가 말씀드린 그 이유로 부인의 몸에 이런 변신을 일으키신 것이라면, 부인께서는 그것에 조금도 의미를 두지 마셔야 하외다. 이 땅 위에 제 검이 길을 뚫지 못할 위험이란 없으며, 그 검으로써 며칠 지나지 않아 부인의 적의 머리를 땅바닥에 굴러떨어지게 하고 부인의 머리 위에 왕국의 왕관을 씌워 드리겠나이다.\"\n\n돈키호테는 더 말하지 않고 공주의 대답을 기다렸다. 공주는 돈키호테가 집으로 옮겨질 때까지 이 속임수를 이어 가려는 돈 페르난도의 결심을 알아챘기에, 더없이 편안한 태도와 무게 있는 자세로 대답했다. \"용감하신 슬픈 몰골의 기사님, 제가 무슨 변화나 변신을 겪었다고 말씀드린 자가 누구든 간에 그자는 기사님께 진실을 말씀드린 것이 아닙니다. 저는 어제와 똑같은 사람이니까요. 제가 바랄 수 있었던 것 이상을 안겨 준 행운의 어떤 손길들이 제게 약간의 변화를 일으킨 것은 사실입니다. 허나 그렇다고 제가 이전의 저이기를 그만둔 것도 아니며, 기사님의 용맹하고 무적인 팔의 힘을 빌리고자 줄곧 품어 온 그 한결같은 소망을 거둔 것도 아닙니다. 그러니 기사님, 부디 너그러우심으로 저를 낳아 주신 아버지를 다시 좋게 보아 주시고, 그분이 현명하고 신중한 분이셨음을 믿어 주십시오. 그분의 술책으로 제 불행을 바로잡을 그토록 확실하고 손쉬운 방도를 찾아내신 것이니까요. 기사님, 저는 믿습니다. 기사님이 아니셨던들 지금 제가 누리는 이 행운에 결코 다다르지 못했으리라고 말입니다. 이 점에서 저는 완벽한 진실을 말씀드리고 있으며, 여기 계신 이분들 대부분이 충분히 증언해 주실 수 있을 것입니다. 이제 남은 일이라곤 내일 우리의 여정을 떠나는 것뿐입니다. 오늘은 멀리 갈 수 없을 테니까요. 그리고 제가 고대하는 그 행복한 결말의 나머지에 대해서는, 하느님과 기사님 마음속의 용맹을 믿겠습니다.\"\n\n발랄한 도로테아가 이렇게 말하자, 그 말을 들은 돈키호테는 산초를 돌아보며 노여운 기색으로 말했다. \"꼬마 산초야, 내 이제 단언하노니, 너는 스페인에서 가장 큰 꼬마 악당이로다. 말해 보아라, 이 도둑놈에 떠돌이 부랑자야, 방금 네놈이 내게 이 공주가 도로테아라는 처녀로 변했다고, 그리고 내가 거인에게서 베어 냈다고 굳게 믿고 있는 그 머리가 너를 낳은 어미 암캐였다고, 그밖에 내 평생 가장 큰 혼란에 빠뜨릴 온갖 헛소리를 지껄이지 않았더냐? 내 맹세하노니\" (여기서 그는 하늘을 우러러보며 이를 갈았다) \"내 너를 흠씬 혼내 줄 마음이 있으니, 그리하여 앞으로 이 세상 편력기사들의 거짓말쟁이 종자들 모두에게 분별을 가르쳐 줄 작정이로다.\"\n\n\"나리, 부디 진정하십시오.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제가 미코미코나 공주 마님의 변화에 대해서는 착각했을 수도 있습니다요. 허나 거인의 머리에 대해서는, 아니 적어도 포도주 부대를 꿰뚫은 일이며 그 피가 붉은 포도주였던 일에 대해서는 하느님이 계신 것만큼이나 확실하게 저는 착각하지 않았습니다요. 그 찢어진 부대들이 바로 나리 침대 머리맡에 있고, 포도주가 방 안을 호수로 만들어 놨거든요. 못 믿으시겠거든 달걀이 부쳐질 때 보시면 됩니다요. 그러니까 객줏집 주인 나리께서 손해 배상을 죄다 청구하실 때 말입죠. 그밖의 일에 대해서는, 여왕 마님께서 본래 그대로이신 게 진심으로 기쁩니다요. 그게 누구 못지않게 저한테도 상관있는 일이거든요.\"\n\n\"다시 말하노니 산초야, 너는 멍청이로다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"나를 용서해 다오, 그러면 됐느니라.\"\n\n\"그러면 됐습니다.\" 돈 페르난도가 말했다. \"그 일에 대해서는 더 말하지 맙시다. 그리고 공주 마님께서 오늘은 너무 늦었으니 내일 출발하기로 제안하셨으니, 그렇게 합시다. 우리는 즐거운 대화로 밤을 지새우고, 내일 우리 모두 돈키호테 님을 모시고 가겠소이다. 그분께서 떠맡으신 이 위대한 과업을 펼치시는 동안 행하실 그 용감하고 비할 데 없는 위업을 우리가 직접 보고 싶으니 말이외다.\"\n\n\"여러분을 받들어 모시고 동행할 사람은 바로 저이외다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"제게 베풀어진 호의와, 제게 품어 주신 좋은 생각에 깊이 감사드리며, 그것에 부응하고자 애쓰겠나이다. 그러지 못한다면 제 목숨을 내놓겠으며, 목숨보다 더한 것을 내놓을 수 있다면 그것마저 내놓겠나이다.\"\n\n돈키호테와 돈 페르난도 사이에 오간 찬사와 정중한 인사말은 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 많았다. 그러나 그것들은 바로 그 순간 객줏집으로 들어선 한 나그네로 인해 끝나고 말았으니, 그는 차림새로 보아 무어인들의 나라에서 막 건너온 기독교도인 듯했다. 그는 반소매에 깃이 없는, 자락이 짧은 푸른 천 외투를 걸치고 있었고, 바지 역시 푸른 천이었으며, 모자도 같은 빛깔이었다. 발에는 노란 반장화를 신고 있었고, 가슴을 가로지른 어깨띠에 무어인의 언월도를 매달고 있었다. 그의 뒤로는 한 여인이 당나귀에 올라타 따라왔는데, 무어식 차림에 얼굴은 베일로 가리고 머리에는 스카프를 둘렀으며, 작은 비단 모자를 쓰고 어깨에서 발까지 덮는 망토를 두르고 있었다. 그 남자는 다부지고 균형 잡힌 체격이었고, 나이는 마흔을 조금 넘긴 듯했으며, 안색은 다소 거뭇하고, 긴 콧수염에 풍성한 턱수염을 길렀으니, 요컨대 그의 풍모는 옷만 잘 차려입었던들 지체 높고 가문 좋은 인물로 여겨질 만한 것이었다. 그는 들어서며 방을 청했고, 객줏집에 빈방이 없다는 말을 듣자 난처해하는 기색이더니, 차림새로 보아 무어 여인인 듯한 그녀에게 다가가 두 팔로 안아 안장에서 내려놓았다. 루신다와 도로테아, 객줏집 안주인과 그 딸, 그리고 마리토르네스는 그 기이하고 자기들에게는 전혀 새로운 차림새에 이끌려 그녀 주위로 모여들었다. 그리고 언제나 친절하고 정중하며 재치 빠른 도로테아는, 그녀와 그녀를 데려온 남자가 방을 찾지 못해 곤란해함을 알아채고는 그녀에게 말했다. \"부인, 여기 불편하고 호사스럽지 못한 것에 마음 쓰지 마십시오. 길가 객줏집이란 으레 그런 것들이 없게 마련이니까요. 그래도 부인께서 저희와 숙소를 함께 쓰는 것을 좋아해 주신다면(그녀는 루신다를 가리켰다), 아마 여행 중에 이보다 못한 잠자리를 만나신 적도 있으실 겁니다.\"\n\n이에 베일을 쓴 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그녀가 한 일이라곤 자리에서 일어나 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고 고개를 숙이며 몸을 굽혀, 감사를 표한다는 표시를 하는 것뿐이었다. 그 침묵으로 미루어 사람들은 그녀가 무어인인 데다 기독교도의 말을 할 줄 모르는 것이 틀림없다고 결론지었다.\n\n바로 그 순간 그때까지 다른 일에 매여 있던 그 포로가 다가왔고, 모두가 자기 동행 곁에 둘러서 있는데도 그녀가 자기들이 건넨 말에 아무 대답도 하지 않는 것을 보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"부인들, 이 처녀는 제 말을 거의 알아듣지 못하고 자기 나라 말밖에는 할 줄 모릅니다. 그런 까닭에 여러분께서 물으신 것에 대답하지 못하고, 또 대답할 수도 없는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"저희가 그녀에게 물은 것은 아무것도 없습니다.\" 루신다가 대답했다. \"그저 오늘 저녁 저희와 함께 지내자고, 저희가 묵는 자리를 나누자고 권했을 뿐입니다. 그곳에서 형편이 허락하는 한 편안히 지내시게 해 드릴 것이며, 도움이 필요한 모든 나그네에게, 특히 그 도움을 받는 이가 여인이라면 마땅히 베풀어야 할 호의로써 그리할 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그녀를 대신해서, 또 저 자신을 위해서, 부인\" 포로가 대답했다. \"부인의 손에 입 맞추며, 베풀어 주신 호의를 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 높이 받들겠습니다. 이러한 처지에, 또 부인 같은 풍모의 분들에게서 받은 것이니, 보다시피 참으로 크나큰 호의가 아닐 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"한 가지 여쭙겠는데요, 선생님\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"이 부인은 기독교도인가요, 아니면 무어인인가요? 그 차림새와 침묵으로 미루어, 저희가 바라지 않는 쪽이 아닐까 짐작하게 되어서요.\"\n\n\"옷차림과 겉모습으로는\" 그가 말했다. \"무어인입니다. 하지만 마음속으로는 더없이 훌륭한 기독교도입니다. 기독교도가 되고 싶은 간절한 소망을 품고 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 아직 세례를 받지 못했나요?\" 루신다가 되물었다.\n\n\"그럴 기회가 없었습니다.\" 포로가 대답했다. \"그녀가 자기 고향이자 보금자리인 알제를 떠난 이래로 말입니다. 그리고 지금까지 우리 거룩한 어머니이신 교회가 정한 모든 의식을 다 배우기 전에 서둘러 세례를 베풀어야 할 만큼 임박한 죽음의 위험에 처한 적도 없었습니다. 다만 하느님께서 허락하신다면, 머지않아 그녀의 옷차림이나 제 옷차림이 가리키는 것보다 훨씬 높은, 그녀에게 걸맞은 엄숙함으로 세례를 받게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n이 말로 그는 듣는 모든 이에게, 그 무어 부인과 포로가 누구인지 알고 싶은 욕구를 불러일으켰다. 그러나 그때만큼은 아무도 선뜻 묻고 싶어 하지 않았으니, 그들의 삶에 대해 캐물을 때라기보다는 그들이 좀 쉬도록 도와줄 더 알맞은 때라고 여긴 까닭이었다. 도로테아는 무어 부인의 손을 잡아 자기 곁의 자리로 이끌고는 베일을 벗어 달라고 청했다. 그녀는 그게 무슨 뜻인지, 자기가 어찌해야 하는지 묻는 듯 포로를 바라보았다. 그가 아랍어로 베일을 벗어 달라는 청이라고 말해 주자, 그녀는 베일을 벗었고, 그러자 어찌나 사랑스러운 얼굴이 드러났던지, 도로테아에게는 그녀가 루신다보다 더 아름다워 보였고, 루신다에게는 도로테아보다 더 아름다워 보였으며, 곁에 있던 모든 이가 자기들의 아름다움에 견줄 만한 아름다움이 있다면 그것은 바로 이 무어 부인의 것이라고 느꼈고, 심지어 그쪽에 다소 우위를 두고 싶어 하는 이들마저 있었다. 그리고 마음을 사로잡고 호의를 얻어 내는 것이야말로 아름다움의 특권이자 매력인지라, 모두가 곧장 이 사랑스러운 무어 여인에게 친절과 관심을 보이고 싶어 안달이 났다.\n\n돈 페르난도가 포로에게 그녀의 이름이 무엇이냐고 묻자, 그는 렐라 소라이다라고 대답했다. 그러나 그 말을 듣는 순간 그녀는 그 기독교도가 무엇을 물었는지 알아채고는, 약간의 언짢음과 격한 기색으로 황급히 말했다. \"아뇨, 소라이다 아녜요. 마리아, 마리아!\" 자신은 \"소라이다\"가 아니라 \"마리아\"라고 불린다는 뜻을 알린 것이었다. 이 말과, 그녀가 그것을 내뱉는 그 가슴 뭉클한 진심은 듣고 있던 몇몇 사람에게서, 특히 천성이 다정하고 가여워할 줄 아는 여인들에게서 한 방울 이상의 눈물을 자아냈다. 루신다가 그녀를 다정하게 끌어안으며 \"그래요, 그래요, 마리아, 마리아\"라고 말하자, 무어 여인은 \"그래요, 그래요, 마리아. 소라이다 마칸헤\"라고 대답했으니, 이는 \"소라이다 아니에요\"라는 뜻이었다.\n\n이제 밤이 다가오고 있었고, 돈 페르난도를 수행한 이들의 분부에 따라 객줏집 주인은 자기 능력껏 가장 훌륭한 저녁상을 차리려고 정성과 수고를 기울였다. 그리하여 때가 이르자 모두 수도원 식당의 것 같은 긴 식탁에 자리를 잡았으니, 객줏집에는 둥글거나 네모난 식탁이 없었던 까닭이다. 식탁 상석의 영예로운 자리는, 그가 한사코 사양했음에도 돈키호테에게 배정되었고, 그는 미코미코나 부인에게 자기 곁에 앉으라고 청했으니, 자신이 그녀의 보호자였기 때문이다. 루신다와 소라이다가 그녀 옆에 자리를 잡았고, 그들 맞은편에는 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오가, 그 옆에는 포로와 다른 신사들이, 그리고 부인들 옆에는 신부와 이발사가 앉았다. 그리하여 그들은 더없이 즐겁게 저녁을 들었는데, 그 즐거움은 돈키호테가 식사를 멈추고서, 염소지기들과 저녁을 들 때 그를 그토록 길게 늘어놓게 만들었던 것과 같은 충동에 이끌려 그들에게 말을 건네기 시작하는 것을 보았을 때 한층 더해졌다.\n\n\"진실로, 여러분, 우리가 곰곰이 생각해 보면, 편력기사도의 길에 몸담은 자들이 보는 것들이란 위대하고 경이로운 것이외다. 말해 보시오, 이 세상에 어떤 존재가, 바로 이 순간 이 성의 문을 들어서서 여기 있는 우리를 본다면, 우리를 지금의 우리와 같은 사람들이라고 짐작하거나 상상할 수 있겠소이까? 제 곁에 있는 이 부인이 우리 모두가 아는 그 위대한 여왕이라고, 또는 제가 명성의 입을 통해 멀리까지 나팔처럼 울려 퍼진 그 슬픈 몰골의 기사라고 누가 말하겠소이까? 자, 이 기예와 소명이 인류가 고안해 낸 다른 모든 것을 능가한다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없으며, 위험에 더 많이 노출되는 만큼 더더욱 영예롭게 여겨질 자격이 있는 것이외다. 학문이 무예보다 우월하다고 주장하는 자들은 물러가시오. 그자들이 누구든 간에 제가 일러 주겠소이다. 그들은 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지조차 모른다고 말이외다. 그런 자들이 흔히 내세우는, 또 주로 거기에 기대는 이유란, 정신의 노고가 육체의 노고보다 크며 무예는 육체에만 일거리를 준다는 것이외다. 마치 그 소명이 짐꾼의 일이어서 억센 힘 말고는 더 필요한 게 없다는 듯이, 또는 우리 무예를 업으로 삼는 자들이 무예라 부르는 것 안에 높은 지력을 요하는 용맹한 행위들이 들어 있지 않다는 듯이 말이외다. 또는 전사가 군대를 거느리거나 한 도시의 방어를 맡았을 때 그 영혼이 육체만큼이나 정신으로도 진력하지 않는다는 듯이 말이외다. 천만에! 육체의 힘만으로 적의 의도며 계획이며 책략이며 장애물을 배우거나 알아챌 수 있는지, 또는 닥쳐오는 화를 막아 낼 수 있는지 보시오. 이 모든 것은 정신의 일이며, 거기에 육체는 아무런 몫도 없는 것이외다. 그러므로 무예가 학문 못지않게 정신을 필요로 하니, 이제 둘 가운데 어느 정신이, 즉 학자의 정신과 전사의 정신 가운데 어느 쪽이 할 일이 더 많은지 봅시다. 이것은 저마다가 다다르려고 하는 목적과 목표를 보면 알 수 있을 것이외다. 더 고귀한 대상을 목표로 삼는 목적이 더 가치 있는 법이니 말이외다. 학문의 목적과 목표는 — 지금 저는 신성한 학문을 말하는 게 아니외다. 그 목표는 영혼을 일으켜 하늘로 인도하는 것이니, 그토록 무한한 목적에는 다른 무엇도 견줄 수 없는 까닭이외다 — 인간의 학문을 말하는 것인데, 그 목적은 분배적 정의를 세우고, 각 사람에게 그의 몫을 주며, 좋은 법이 지켜지는지 보살피는 것이외다. 이는 의심할 바 없이 고귀하고 드높으며 큰 칭송을 받을 만한 목적이외다. 허나 무예가 추구하는 것에 돌려야 할 칭송에는 미치지 못하니, 무예는 그 목적이자 대상으로 평화를, 곧 인간이 이 삶에서 바랄 수 있는 가장 큰 축복을 두는 까닭이외다. 세상과 인류가 받은 첫 기쁜 소식은 천사들이 우리에게 낮이 된 그 밤에 알린 것이니, 그들이 공중에서 '지극히 높은 곳에서는 하느님께 영광이요, 땅에서는 선의의 사람들에게 평화로다'라고 노래했을 때 말이외다. 또한 하늘과 땅의 위대한 스승께서 그분의 제자들과 택하신 추종자들에게 어느 집에 들어가든 하라고 가르치신 인사도, '이 집에 평화가 깃들지어다'라는 것이었으며, 그분은 그밖에도 여러 번 그들에게 '내 평화를 너희에게 주노라, 내 평화를 너희에게 남기노라, 평화가 너희와 함께할지어다'라고 말씀하셨으니, 그러한 손으로 주고 남기신 보석이자 귀한 선물이외다. 그것이 없이는 땅에서도 하늘에서도 행복이 있을 수 없는 보석이외다. 이 평화야말로 전쟁의 참된 목적이며, 전쟁이란 무예의 또 다른 이름일 뿐이외다. 그러니 전쟁의 목적이 평화이며, 그 점에서 무예가 학문의 목적보다 우위에 있음을 인정한다면, 이제 학자의 육체적 노고와 무예의 길을 따르는 자의 육체적 노고로 눈을 돌려, 어느 쪽이 더 큰지 봅시다.\"\n\n돈키호테는 자기 일장 연설을 어찌나 그럴듯한 태도로, 또 어찌나 정확한 언어로 펼쳤던지, 적어도 그동안만큼은 듣는 이들 가운데 누구도 그를 미치광이로 여길 수 없게 만들었다. 오히려 그들 대부분이 무예가 태생적으로 따라붙는 신사들이었던지라, 그가 말을 이어 가는 동안 큰 즐거움으로 그에게 귀 기울였다. \"그러니 여기서, 제가 말씀드리건대, 학생이 견뎌야 할 것은 이것이외다. 무엇보다도 먼저 가난이외다. 모든 학생이 가난하다는 게 아니라, 경우를 가능한 한 강하게 들자는 것이외다. 그리고 그가 가난을 견딘다고 말했으니, 그의 가혹한 운명에 대해서는 더 말할 게 없으리라 봅니다. 가난한 자는 삶의 좋은 것들을 누리지 못하는 법이니 말이외다. 이 가난을 그는 갖가지 모양으로 겪으니, 굶주림이든 추위든 헐벗음이든, 혹은 그 모두든 말이외다. 허나 그렇다 해도 그것이 그토록 극심하지는 않으니, 다소 때아닌 시각에, 또 부자들이 먹다 남긴 것에서일망정 먹을 것은 좀 얻게 되는 까닭이외다. 학생의 가장 큰 비참함이란 그들 자신이 '수프 얻으러 나간다'고 부르는 것인데, 그들에게는 언제나 이웃의 화로나 화덕이 있어, 비록 몸을 데워 주지는 못해도 적어도 추위를 누그러뜨려는 주고, 끝으로 밤에는 지붕 아래서 편안히 잠드는 것이외다. 그밖의 자질구레한 것들, 이를테면 셔츠가 없다거나 신발이 넉넉지 못하다거나 옷이 얇고 다 해졌다거나, 운 좋게 어떤 잔치에 초대받기라도 하면 게걸스럽게 배 터지도록 처먹는다거나 하는 것들에 대해서는 더 들어가지 않겠소이다. 제가 묘사한 이 길, 거칠고 험난한 이 길로, 여기서 비틀거리고 저기서 넘어지고 다시 일어났다가 또 넘어지면서, 그들은 바라던 지위에 다다르며, 일단 그것에 이르면, 우리는 그 시르티스와 스킬라와 카립디스를 마치 순풍의 행운이 날개를 달아 준 듯 헤쳐 나간 이들을 많이 보아 왔소이다. 제가 말하건대, 그들이 의자에 앉아 세상을 다스리고 통치하며, 그 굶주림이 포만으로, 그 추위가 안락으로, 그 헐벗음이 고운 의복으로, 거적 위의 잠이 고운 삼베와 다마스크 천 위의 안식으로 바뀐 것을 보아 왔으니, 이는 그들의 덕에 대한 정당히 얻은 보상이외다. 허나 전사가 견뎌 내는 것과 견주고 비교해 보면, 그들이 견뎌 낸 모든 것은 거기에 훨씬 못 미치니, 이제 제가 그것을 보여 드리겠소이다.\"\n* 시르티스, 스킬라, 카립디스: 고대 지중해 항해의 위험한 곳들로, 시르티스는 북아프리카 연안의 모래톱이고, 스킬라와 카립디스는 그리스 신화에 나오는 좁은 해협의 두 괴물이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVIII.\n\n돈키호테는 이야기를 이어 가며 말했다. \"학생의 경우를 가난과 그에 딸린 것들에서 시작했으니, 이제 군인이 더 부유한지 어디 한번 보십시다. 그러면 가난 그 자체에서도 군인보다 더 가난한 자는 없음을 알게 될 것입니다. 군인은 늦게 오거나 아예 오지 않는 그 변변찮은 봉급에 매여 있거나, 아니면 제 목숨과 양심을 심히 위태롭게 하며 약탈할 수 있는 것에 의지해야 하니 말입니다. 게다가 때로는 헐벗음이 어찌나 심한지 갈라진 더블릿 한 벌이 군복이자 셔츠 노릇을 하고, 한겨울에는 허허벌판에서 제 입김 말고는 더 나은 것도 없이 혹독한 날씨에 맞서 몸을 지켜야 하니, 굳이 말할 것도 없거니와 텅 빈 곳에서 나오는 그 입김은 자연의 법칙을 거슬러 차갑게 나올 수밖에 없는 것입니다. 그래도 그는 밤이 오기를 손꼽아 기다립니다. 자신을 기다리는 침상에서 이 모든 불편을 메우려는 것이지요. 그 침상은 그 자신의 잘못이 아니고서야 결코 너무 좁다는 죄를 짓는 법이 없으니, 땅바닥에 마음대로 제 몸을 재어 누울 수 있고, 시트가 흘러내릴까 봐 두려워할 일도 없이 마음껏 몸을 뒤척일 수 있기 때문입니다. 그런데 이 모든 것을 겪은 뒤, 이제 자기 직업에서 학위를 받을 날과 시각이 왔다고 칩시다. 곧 전투의 날이 와서, 어쩌면 관자놀이를 꿰뚫고 지나간 어떤 총탄 구멍을 꿰매기 위해, 혹은 그를 한쪽 팔이나 다리 못 쓰는 불구로 만들 거즈로 지은 박사모를 그에게 씌워 주는 날이 왔다고 칩시다. 혹 이런 일이 일어나지 않고 자비로운 하늘이 그를 지켜보며 무사히 멀쩡하게 보살펴 준다 하더라도, 그는 전과 똑같은 가난 속에 있을 것이며, 더 많은 교전과 더 많은 전투를 거치고 그 모든 것에서 승리하여 나와야만 비로소 형편이 나아질 것이나, 그런 종류의 기적은 좀처럼 보기 어려운 법입니다. 여러분, 한번 곰곰이 생각해 본 적이 있다면 말씀해 보십시오. 전쟁으로 이득을 본 자들의 수가 거기서 죽어 간 자들의 수에 비해 얼마나 모자란지 말입니다. 의심할 바 없이 여러분은, 비교 자체가 안 된다고, 죽은 자들은 헤아릴 수조차 없는데 상을 받은 산 자들은 세 자릿수면 다 셀 수 있다고 답하실 것입니다. 이 모든 것이 학식 있는 자들의 경우에는 정반대입니다. 그들은 소매는 말할 것도 없고 옷자락만으로도 다들 먹고살 방도를 찾으니 말입니다. 그러니 군인이 더 많이 견뎌야 하면서도 그 보상은 훨씬 적습니다. 그러나 이 모든 것에 맞서, 군인 이천 명에게 상을 주기가 더 쉽다는 반론을 펼 수도 있습니다. 전자는 자리를 줌으로써 보상할 수 있고, 그 자리는 마땅히 그들의 직업에 종사하는 자들에게 내려져야 하는 것이나, 후자는 그들이 섬기는 주인의 바로 그 재산에서밖에 보상받을 길이 없기 때문입니다. 하지만 이 불가능함이야말로 오히려 내 논지를 굳혀 줄 따름입니다.\n\n\"그러나 이 문제는 풀어내기 어려운 까다로운 물음이니 한쪽으로 제쳐 두고, 무기가 학문보다 우월하다는 문제로 돌아갑시다. 이것은 아직 결판이 나지 않은 문제이니, 양쪽에서 내세우는 논거가 그토록 많기 때문입니다. 내가 이미 언급한 것들 말고도, 학문은 자기 없이는 무기가 스스로를 지탱할 수 없다고 말합니다. 전쟁에도 그 나름의 법이 있어 그 법의 지배를 받는데, 법이란 학문과 학식 있는 자들의 영역에 속하기 때문이라는 것이지요. 이에 무기는, 자기 없이는 법이 유지될 수 없다고 답합니다. 무기로써 나라가 지켜지고, 왕국이 보전되며, 도시가 보호받고, 길이 안전해지고, 바다에서 해적이 소탕되기 때문입니다. 한마디로, 무기가 없다면 나라도, 왕국도, 군주국도, 도시도, 바다와 육지의 길도, 전쟁이 지속되며 그 특권과 권력을 마음껏 휘두르는 한, 전쟁이 몰고 오는 폭력과 혼란에 고스란히 내맡겨지고 말 것이라는 말이지요. 그리고 가장 값비싼 것이 가장 귀하게 여겨지고 또 가장 귀하게 여겨질 만하다는 것은 명백한 이치입니다. 학문에서 탁월함에 이르려면 시간과 밤샘과 굶주림과 헐벗음과 두통과 소화불량을, 그리고 이런 종류의 다른 것들을 — 그중 몇 가지는 이미 내가 언급한 바 있거니와 — 치러야 합니다. 그러나 한 사람이 통상적인 과정을 밟아 훌륭한 군인이 되기까지는, 학생이 겪는 모든 것을 그것도 비할 바 없이 더한 정도로 치러야 하니, 걸음마다 제 목숨을 잃을 위험을 무릅쓰기 때문입니다. 학생을 덮치거나 괴롭힐 수 있는 그 어떤 궁핍과 가난의 두려움인들, 어느 요새에 갇힌 채 어느 반월보(半月堡)나 능보(稜堡)에서 보초를 서다가, 적이 자기가 배치된 초소를 향해 갱도를 파 들어오고 있음을 알면서도, 어떤 경우에도 임박한 위험에서 물러나거나 달아날 수 없는 군인이 느끼는 것에 견줄 수 있겠습니까? 그가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 자기 대장에게 알려, 대장이 맞갱도를 파서 막아 보도록 하는 것뿐이며, 그러고는 날개도 없이 구름 위로 솟구쳐 올랐다가 제 뜻과는 어긋나게 깊은 땅속으로 떨어질 그 순간을 두려움과 기다림 속에서 버티고 서 있는 것뿐입니다. 만일 이것이 하찮은 위험으로 보인다면, 망망대해 한복판에서 뱃머리와 뱃머리를 맞댄 두 갤리선이, 서로 맞물리고 뒤엉킨 채 맞부딪치는 일이 이에 견줄 만한지, 아니 이를 능가하는지 어디 한번 봅시다. 그때 군인은 충각(衝角) 발판의 두 자 너비밖에 디딜 자리가 없는데도, 자기 몸에서 창 한 자루 길이도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 적의 대포가 겨눠진 그 수만큼이나 많은 죽음의 사자(使者)들이 눈앞에서 자신을 위협하는 것을 보면서도, 또 첫걸음을 부주의하게 내디뎠다가는 넵투누스 품속의 심연으로 곤두박질쳐 들어가리라는 것을 보면서도, 여전히 두려움 없는 가슴으로, 자신을 다잡아 주는 명예에 떠밀려, 그 모든 총탄의 표적이 되기를 마다하지 않고 적선으로 건너가려 그 좁은 길을 가로지르려 발버둥 칩니다. 더욱 놀라운 것은, 한 사람이 세상이 끝나는 날까지 다시는 떠오르지 못할 심연으로 가라앉기가 무섭게 또 다른 자가 그 자리를 차지하고, 그자 또한 적처럼 자기를 기다리는 바닷속으로 떨어지면, 또 다른 자가, 그리고 또 다른 자가 그 죽음들 사이에 한순간의 틈도 없이 뒤를 잇는다는 것입니다. 전쟁의 온갖 운수가 보여 줄 수 있는 가장 위대한 용기와 담력이지요. 저 악마 같은 포병 기계들의 무시무시한 광포함을 알지 못했던, 복받은 시대들은 행복했습니다. 그것을 발명한 자는, 내 확신컨대, 자신의 악마 같은 발명에 대한 응보를 받으며 지옥에 있을 것입니다. 그자는 그 발명으로, 비천하고 비겁한 팔이 용맹한 신사의 목숨을 빼앗기 쉽도록 만들어 놓았으니, 용감한 가슴을 불태우고 살아 움직이게 하는 그 열정과 격정이 한껏 타오르는 와중에, 어디서 어떻게 날아오는지도 모르는 어떤 난데없는 총알이 — 어쩌면 그 저주받은 기계를 쏘고 나서 그 섬광에 겁먹어 달아난 자가 쏘았을 그 총알이 — 날아들어, 다가올 여러 세대를 살 자격이 있는 자의 계획을 순식간에 끝장내고 그 목숨을 끊어 버리는 것입니다. 그리하여 이를 곰곰이 헤아릴 때면, 나는 지금 우리가 살고 있는 이토록 가증스러운 시대에 편력 기사라는 이 직업을 택한 것을 내 마음속으로 후회한다고 거의 말하고 싶어집니다. 그 어떤 위험도 나를 두렵게 할 수는 없으나, 화약과 납덩이가 내 팔의 힘과 내 칼날로써 온 세상에 이름을 떨치고 명성을 얻을 기회를 앗아 갈지도 모른다는 생각을 하면 다소 마음이 편치 않기 때문입니다. 그러나 하늘의 뜻대로 될지니, 만일 내가 뜻을 이룬다면 나는 옛날의 편력 기사들이 무릅썼던 것보다 더 큰 위험에 맞섰던 만큼 그만큼 더 큰 영예를 누리게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n이 기나긴 이야기를 돈키호테는 다른 이들이 저녁을 먹는 동안 늘어놓았으니, 산초가 한 번도 아니고 여러 번이나 하고 싶은 말은 나중에 얼마든지 할 시간이 있을 테니 어서 저녁이나 드시라고 일렀건만, 그는 음식 한 조각 입에 가져가는 것조차 잊고 있었다. 그의 말을 듣던 이들은 새삼 연민에 사로잡혔으니, 분별이 멀쩡해 보이고 논하는 모든 주제마다 사리에 맞는 견해를 지닌 사람이, 그 가련하고 운수 사나운 기사도가 화제에 오르기만 하면 어쩌면 그토록 가망 없이 모든 분별을 잃어버리는지를 보았기 때문이다. 신부는 그가 무기를 옹호하여 한 말이 모두 지당하며, 자신 또한 학식 있는 사람이요 학위를 받은 자이지만 같은 의견이라고 말해 주었다.\n\n그들은 저녁 식사를 마치고 식탁보를 걷었으며, 여관 안주인과 그녀의 딸과 마리토르네스가 라만차의 돈키호테가 묵을 다락방을 — 그날 밤 여자들끼리만 그곳에서 묵기로 정해져 있었다 — 준비하는 동안, 돈 페르난도는 포로에게 그의 생애 이야기를 들려 달라고 청했다. 그가 소라이다와 함께 도착했을 때 흘린 몇 마디 암시로 미루어 보건대 그 이야기가 기이하고 흥미롭지 않을 리 없었기 때문이다. 이에 포로는 기꺼이 그 청에 응하겠으나, 다만 자기 이야기가 그들이 바라는 만큼 즐거움을 주지는 못할까 봐 염려된다고 답했다. 그럼에도 응하는 데 인색하지 않으려 이야기를 들려주겠노라 했다. 신부와 다른 이들이 고마워하며 거듭 청을 더하자, 그는 이토록 간곡히 청하는 마당에, 그것도 명령이 이토록 무게를 지니는 자리에서 굳이 청할 것까지도 없다고 하면서 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"여러분께서 귀를 기울여 주신다면, 어쩌면 기발하고 공들인 기교로 지어낸 허구의 이야기들도 따라오지 못할 진실한 이야기를 듣게 되실 것입니다.\" 이 말에 모두는 제자리에 자세를 고쳐 앉고는 깊은 침묵을 지켰으며, 그는 그들이 말없는 기대 속에 자신의 말을 기다리는 것을 보고는 유쾌하고 차분한 목소리로 이렇게 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n# WHEREIN THE CAPTIVE RELATES HIS LIFE AND ADVENTURES\n\n우리 집안은 레온 산악 지방의 어느 마을에서 비롯되었는데, 자연은 운명보다 우리 집안에 더 너그럽고 더 후하였다. 그렇지만 그런 고장의 전반적인 가난 속에서도 내 아버지는 부유한 사람으로까지 통하였으니, 재산을 지키는 데도 재산을 쓰는 데 못지않게 영리하였더라면 실제로 그러하였을 것이다. 후하고 헤프게 베푸는 이 성향은 젊은 시절 군인으로 지낸 데서 몸에 밴 것이었다. 군인의 삶이란 인색한 자를 손이 큰 사람으로, 손이 큰 사람을 낭비가로 만드는 학교이기 때문이다. 혹여 구두쇠인 군인이 있다 하더라도 그것은 좀처럼 보기 드문 괴물 같은 존재이다. 내 아버지는 후함을 넘어 낭비벽에 가까웠으니, 이는 자신의 이름과 지위를 이어받을 자식들을 둔 결혼한 남자에게는 결코 이로울 것이 없는 기질이었다. 내 아버지에게는 세 자식이 있었는데, 모두 아들이었고 모두 직업을 택할 만큼 나이가 들어 있었다. 그리하여 자신의 성벽을 도무지 거스를 수 없음을 깨달은 아버지는, 그 낭비와 사치의 도구이자 원인이 되는 것에서 스스로를 떼어 내기로, 곧 알렉산드로스 자신조차 인색해 보이게 만들 그 재산에서 스스로를 떼어 내기로 결심하였다. 그리하여 어느 날 우리 셋을 모두 한 방으로 불러들여 대략 다음과 같은 말로 우리에게 이야기하였다.\n\n\"내 아들들아, 내가 너희를 사랑한다는 것을 확실히 알려 주는 데는 너희가 내 아들이라는 사실 외에 더 알거나 말할 것이 없으며, 내가 너희를 사랑하지 않는다는 의심을 품게 하는 데는 너희 유산을 지키는 일에 관한 한 내게 자제력이 없다는 사실을 아는 것 외에 더 필요한 것이 없다. 그러므로 너희가 앞으로 내가 너희를 아버지로서 사랑하며 계부처럼 너희를 망치고자 하는 마음이 없음을 확실히 느끼도록, 나는 얼마 전부터 곰곰이 생각해 오다가 충분히 숙고한 끝에 결정한 바를 너희에게 행하고자 한다. 너희는 이제 살아갈 길을 택할 나이, 적어도 나이가 들었을 때 명예와 이익을 가져다줄 천직을 택할 나이가 되었다. 내가 하기로 결심한 것은 내 재산을 네 몫으로 나누는 일이니, 세 몫은 차별 없이 너희 각자에게 그 몫을 주고, 나머지 한 몫은 하늘이 내게 베풀어 주실 남은 생이 얼마든 그동안 살아가며 나 자신을 부양하는 데 쓰려고 남겨 두겠다. 다만 나는 너희가 각자 자기에게 떨어진 몫을 차지하거든 내가 일러 줄 길 가운데 하나를 따르기를 바란다. 우리 이 스페인에는 내 생각에 매우 참된 — 모든 속담이 다 그러하듯, 오랜 실제 경험에서 끌어낸 짧은 격언이기에 — 속담이 하나 있으니, 내가 말하려는 그것은 이렇게 이른다. '교회냐, 바다냐, 임금의 집이냐.' 더 쉬운 말로 하자면, 번창하여 부자가 되고자 하는 자는 교회를 따르거나, 상업을 천직으로 삼아 바다로 나가거나, 임금의 궁에서 봉직하라는 뜻이다. 사람들이 말하기를 '귀족의 호의보다 임금의 빵 부스러기가 낫다'고 하기 때문이다. 내가 이렇게 말하는 까닭은, 너희 가운데 하나는 학문을 따르고, 또 하나는 장사를, 셋째는 전쟁에서 임금을 섬기기를 내가 바라고 또 그것이 내 기쁨이기 때문이다. 임금의 궁 안에서 봉직 자리를 얻기란 어려운 일이며, 전쟁이 큰 재물을 가져다주지는 않더라도 큰 명망과 명성을 안겨 주기 때문이다. 지금부터 여드레 후에 내가 너희에게 한 푼도 속이지 않고 돈으로 너희 몫을 온전히 내주겠으니, 끝에 가서 너희가 보게 될 것이다. 자, 이제 내가 너희 앞에 펼쳐 놓은 대로 내 생각과 충고를 따를 마음이 있는지 말해 보아라.\"\n\n맏이인 내게 먼저 답하라 하셨기에, 나는 아버지께 재산을 다 떨어내지 마시고 마음껏 다 쓰시라고, 우리는 제 손으로 벌어 살아갈 수 있는 젊은이들이라고 청한 끝에, 아버지의 뜻을 따르기로 하고 내 뜻은 무기의 길을 따라 그로써 하느님과 임금을 섬기는 데 있다고 말하였다. 둘째 형제도 같은 제안을 받아들여 자기에게 떨어진 몫을 장사에 실어 인도 제도로 가기로 정하였다. 막내, 내 생각에는 가장 현명한 그 아이는 차라리 교회를 따르거나 살라망카에 가서 학업을 마치겠다고 하였다. 우리가 합의에 이르러 각자의 직업을 택하자마자 아버지는 우리 모두를 껴안으셨고, 말씀하신 그 짧은 기간 안에 약속한 모든 것을 실행에 옮기셨다. 그리하여 각자에게 그 몫을 — 내 기억으로는 각각 현금 삼천 두카도였다(우리 외삼촌 한 분이 재산이 집안 밖으로 나가지 않도록 그 토지를 사들여 즉석에서 값을 치렀기 때문이다) — 내주셨을 때, 우리 셋은 같은 날 우리의 인자하신 아버지께 작별을 고하였다. 그와 동시에, 그토록 빈약한 형편으로 노년의 아버지를 두고 떠나는 것이 인정 없는 일로 여겨졌기에, 나는 내 삼천 두카도 가운데 이천 두카도를 받으시도록 아버지를 설득하였으니, 나머지만으로도 군인에게 필요한 모든 것을 갖추기에 넉넉할 터였기 때문이다. 두 형제도 내 본보기에 마음이 움직여 각각 천 두카도씩 아버지께 드렸고, 그리하여 아버지께는 현금 사천 두카도가, 그 밖에 토지로 남겨 두기를 택하신 자기 몫의 값인 삼천 두카도가 남게 되었다. 마침내 앞서 말한 대로 우리는 양쪽 모두 슬픔과 눈물 없이는 아니게, 아버지와 또 내가 말한 외삼촌께 작별을 고하였다. 그분들은 우리에게 잘 지내든 못 지내든 기회가 닿는 대로 어찌 지내는지 알려 달라고 당부하셨다. 우리는 그러마고 약속하였고, 아버지께서 우리를 껴안고 축복을 내려 주신 뒤, 하나는 살라망카로, 또 하나는 세비야로, 나는 제노바로 양모를 싣는 제노바 배가 있다고 들은 알리칸테로 떠났다.\n\n내가 아버지의 집을 떠난 지도 이제 한 스물두 해가 되었는데, 그동안 편지를 여러 통 썼는데도 아버지나 형제들에 관한 소식은 전혀 듣지 못하였다. 그 세월 동안 나 자신이 겪은 모험을 이제 간략히 이야기하겠다. 나는 알리칸테에서 배를 타고 순조로운 항해 끝에 제노바에 닿았고, 거기서 밀라노로 나아가 거기서 무기와 약간의 군인 장비를 갖추었다. 거기서 나는 피에몬테로 가서 복무할 생각이었으나, 이미 알레산드리아 델라 팔리아로 가는 길에 들어섰을 때, 위대한 알바 공작이 플랑드르로 가는 중이라는 소식을 들었다. 나는 계획을 바꾸어 그에게 합류하여 그가 벌인 전역에서 그의 휘하에 복무하였고, 에흐몬트 백작과 호른 백작의 죽음을 곁에서 보았으며, 과달라하라의 이름난 대장 디에고 데 우르비나라는 분 밑에서 기수로 승진하였다. 내가 플랑드르에 도착한 지 얼마 뒤, 복된 기억의 교황 성하 피우스 5세께서 공동의 적인 튀르크인에 맞서 베네치아 및 스페인과 동맹을 맺었다는 소식이 전해졌다. 그 튀르크인은 마침 그때 베네치아인의 소유였던 이름난 키프로스 섬을 함대로 빼앗았으니, 통탄스럽고 참담한 손실이었다. 우리 인자하신 임금 펠리페 폐하의 서출 동생인 지존하신 돈 후안 데 아우스트리아께서 동맹군의 총사령관으로 오신다는 것이 사실로 알려졌고, 엄청난 전쟁 준비가 이루어지고 있다는 소문이 떠돌았으니, 이 모든 것이 내 가슴을 들쑤셔 예상되는 전역에 참가하고 싶은 갈망으로 나를 가득 채웠다. 비록 첫 기회가 닿는 대로 대장으로 승진하리라 믿을 만한 이유가, 거의 확실한 약속이 있었음에도, 나는 모든 것을 버리고 이탈리아로 가는 쪽을 택하여 실제로 그리하였다. 그리고 운 좋게도 돈 후안께서 마침 제노바에 막 도착하시어 나폴리로 가서 베네치아 함대에 합류하려 하셨으니, 훗날 메시나에서 실제로 그리하셨다. 한마디로, 나는 그 영광스러운 원정에 참가하였는데, 그때쯤에는 보병 대장으로 승진하여 있었으니, 내 공로보다는 내 행운이 나를 그 명예로운 직책으로 끌어올린 것이었다. 그리고 그날 — 그리스도교 세계에 그토록 다행스러웠던 그날, 그때 비로소 땅 위의 모든 민족이 튀르크인이 바다에서 무적이라 여기던 그 오류에서 깨어났기 때문이다 — 내가 말하건대 오스만의 오만과 거만이 꺾인 그날, 거기서 행복을 얻은 모든 이들 가운데(그날 죽은 그리스도교도들은 살아남아 승리한 자들보다 더 행복하였으니) 나 홀로 비참하였다. 로마 시대였다면 내가 기대하였을 법한 어떤 해전의 관(冠) 대신, 그 이름난 날의 뒤를 이은 밤에 나는 발에 족쇄를 차고 손에 수갑을 찬 신세가 되어 있었던 것이다.\n\n일은 이렇게 일어났다. 알제의 왕이자 대담하고 성공적인 해적인 엘 우찰리가 선두에 선 몰타의 갤리선을 공격하여 빼앗았는데(그 안에는 세 기사만이 살아남았고, 그들도 심한 부상을 입었다), 나와 내 부대가 타고 있던 후안 안드레아의 기함이 그것을 구원하러 다가갔다. 그러한 경우에 마땅히 해야 할 바를 행하여, 나는 적의 갤리선으로 뛰어올랐는데, 그 배가 공격해 오던 배에서 비켜 가는 바람에 내 부하들이 나를 따라오지 못하게 되었고, 그리하여 나는 적들 한가운데 홀로 남게 되었으니, 그 수가 너무도 많아 도저히 맞설 수가 없었다. 한마디로 나는 온몸에 상처를 입은 채 사로잡혔다. 여러분도 아시다시피 엘 우찰리는 자기 함대 전부를 이끌고 달아났고, 나는 그의 손아귀에 포로로 남겨졌으니, 그토록 많은 기쁨에 찬 이들 가운데 홀로 슬픈 자였고, 그토록 많은 자유로운 이들 가운데 홀로 사로잡힌 자였다. 그날 튀르크 함대에서 노를 젓던 그리스도교도 일만 오천 명이 그토록 갈망하던 자유를 되찾았기 때문이다.\n\n그들은 나를 콘스탄티노플로 끌고 갔고, 거기서 대(大)튀르크인 셀림은 내 주인을 해전에서 임무를 다하고 그 용맹의 증거로 몰타 기사단의 군기를 빼앗아 온 공로로 해군 총사령관에 앉혔다. 그 이듬해, 곧 칠십이 년에 나는 나바리노에서 등불 셋을 단 선두 갤리선에서 노를 젓고 있었다. 거기서 나는 항구 안에 든 튀르크 함대 전부를 사로잡을 기회가 어떻게 사라졌는지를 보고 또 지켜보았다. 거기 속한 모든 해병과 예니체리들이 바로 그 항구 안에서 공격을 받으리라 확신하고는, 공격당하기를 기다리지도 않고 즉시 뭍으로 달아나려고 짐과 파사마케스, 곧 신발을 준비해 두고 있었으니, 그만큼 우리 함대를 크게 두려워하고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 하늘은 그것을 달리 정하셨으니, 우리 편을 지휘한 사령관의 어떤 잘못이나 태만 때문이 아니라, 그리스도교 세계의 죄 때문이요, 우리를 징벌할 회초리를 우리가 늘 지니게 하시는 것이 하느님의 뜻이요 기쁨이었기 때문이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 엘 우찰리는 나바리노 가까운 섬인 모돈으로 피신하여, 병력을 상륙시켜 항구 어귀를 요새화하고는 돈 후안이 물러갈 때까지 조용히 기다렸다. 이 원정에서는 '프레미오'라 불리는 갤리선이 나포되었는데, 그 선장은 이름난 해적 바르바로사의 아들이었다. 그것을 나포한 것은 '암늑대'라 불리는 나폴리의 기함으로, 전쟁의 벼락이자 부하들의 아버지요 성공적이며 한 번도 정복당하지 않은 대장, 산타크루스 후작 돈 알바로 데 바산이 지휘하는 배였다. 나는 그 '프레미오'의 나포 때 일어난 일을 여러분께 말씀드리지 않을 수 없다.\n\n바르바로사의 아들은 어찌나 잔인하고 노예들을 어찌나 모질게 다루었던지, 노를 젓던 자들이 '암늑대' 갤리선이 자기들을 향해 짓쳐 와 거리를 좁혀 오는 것을 보자, 일제히 노를 놓아 버리고는, 통로 끝 발판 위에 서서 힘껏 노를 저으라고 고함치던 자기 선장을 붙잡아, 고물에서 이물까지 자리에서 자리로 넘겨 가며 어찌나 물어뜯었던지, 그가 돛대를 채 많이 지나기도 전에 그 영혼은 이미 지옥에 가 있었다. 내가 말한 대로, 그가 그들을 다룬 잔인함과 그들이 그를 미워한 증오가 그만큼 컸던 것이다.\n\n우리는 콘스탄티노플로 돌아왔고, 그 이듬해 칠십삼 년에 돈 후안이 튀니스를 빼앗아 튀르크인에게서 그 왕국을 거두고 물레이 하메트를 그 자리에 앉혀, 세상에서 가장 잔인하고 가장 용맹한 무어인 물레이 하미다가 거기서 다시 다스리고자 품던 희망에 종지부를 찍었다는 것이 알려졌다. 대튀르크인은 그 손실을 몹시 가슴 아파하였고, 그 종족 모두가 지닌 교활함으로 베네치아인과 화친을 맺었으며(그들이 그보다 화친에 훨씬 더 열을 올렸다), 그 이듬해 칠십사 년에 골레타와 돈 후안이 튀니스 가까이에 반쯤 짓다 만 요새를 공격하였다. 이 모든 일이 벌어지는 동안 나는 자유에 대한 아무런 희망도 없이 노에 매여 일하고 있었다. 적어도 몸값으로 자유를 얻을 희망은 없었으니, 내 불행을 알리는 편지를 아버지께 쓰지 않겠다고 굳게 결심하고 있었기 때문이다. 마침내 골레타가 함락되고 요새가 함락되었는데, 그 진지들 앞에는 정규 튀르크 병사 칠만 오천 명과, 아프리카 곳곳에서 온 사만 명이 넘는 무어인과 아랍인이 있었으며, 이 대군의 행렬에는 그토록 많은 군수품과 전쟁 기계, 그리고 그토록 많은 공병이 따라와 그들이 맨손으로도 한 줌씩 흙을 던져 골레타와 요새를 덮어 버릴 수 있을 정도였다. 가장 먼저 함락된 것은 골레타였으니, 그때까지 난공불락으로 여겨지던 곳이었다. 그곳이 함락된 것은 할 수 있고 또 마땅히 해야 할 모든 일을 다 한 수비병들의 어떤 잘못 때문이 아니라, 거기 사막의 모래에서는 참호를 얼마나 쉽게 팔 수 있는지를 경험이 증명하였기 때문이다. 거기서는 두 뼘 깊이만 파도 물이 나오곤 하였으나, 튀르크인은 두 발(척) 깊이를 파도 물을 찾지 못하였다. 그리하여 그들은 수많은 모래 자루로 자기들 진지를 어찌나 높이 쌓아 올렸던지, 요새의 성벽을 내려다보며 마치 기마대(騎馬臺)에서 쏘듯 성벽을 휩쓸었으니, 아무도 버티고 서거나 방어를 지탱할 수 없었다.\n\n우리 편 사람들이 골레타 안에 틀어박힐 것이 아니라 상륙 지점의 탁 트인 곳에서 기다렸어야 했다는 것이 흔한 의견이었다. 그러나 그렇게 말하는 자들은 함부로 지껄이는 것이요 그러한 일에 관한 지식이 거의 없는 것이니, 골레타와 요새 안에 병사가 겨우 칠천 명밖에 없었는데, 그토록 적은 수가 아무리 결연한들 어찌 밖으로 쳐 나가 적의 그런 수효를 상대로 버틸 수 있었겠는가? 또 구원받지 못하는 요새를, 그것도 자기들 땅에서 결연한 적의 대군에 에워싸인 요새를 어찌 잃지 않을 수 있었겠는가? 그러나 많은 이들이 생각하였고 나도 그리 생각하였으니, 그 화근의 근원이요 소굴, 그 게걸스러운 먹보, 해면(海綿), 헤아릴 수 없는 돈을 좀먹는 좀벌레, 무적의 카를 5세께서 그곳을 함락하신 기억을 보존하는 것 말고는 아무런 목적도 없이 부질없이 거기 탕진된 그 돈, 그것의 파괴를 허락하신 것은 하늘이 스페인에 베푸신 각별한 은총이요 자비였다. 마치 그 기억을, 지금도 그러하고 앞으로도 그러할 그 영원함을 떠받치는 데 이 돌들이 필요하기라도 한 양 말이다. 요새도 함락되었으나, 튀르크인은 그것을 한 치 한 치 따내야만 하였으니, 그것을 지키던 병사들이 어찌나 용맹하고 굳세게 싸웠던지 스물두 차례의 전면 공격에서 죽은 적의 수가 이만 오천을 넘었기 때문이다. 살아남은 삼백 명 가운데 부상 없이 사로잡힌 자는 하나도 없었으니, 그들이 얼마나 용맹하고 결연하였는지, 또 얼마나 굳세게 스스로를 지키고 자기 자리를 지켜 냈는지의 분명하고 뚜렷한 증거였다. 석호 한가운데 있던, 발렌시아의 신사이자 이름난 군인 돈 후안 사노게라의 지휘 아래 있던 작은 요새 혹은 망루는 조건을 걸고 항복하였다. 그들은 자기 요새를 지키려 온 힘을 다하였고 그것을 잃은 것을 어찌나 가슴 아파하였던지 포로로 콘스탄티노플로 끌려가던 도중 슬픔에 겨워 죽고 만 골레타의 사령관 돈 페드로 푸에르토카레로를 사로잡았다. 그들은 또 요새의 사령관인 가브리오 세르베욘이라는 자도 사로잡았는데, 밀라노의 신사로 뛰어난 공병이자 매우 용감한 군인이었다. 이 두 요새에서는 명망 있는 인물이 많이 스러졌으니, 그 가운데 성 요한 기사단의 기사 파가노 도리아가 있었다. 그는 너그러운 기질의 인물이었으니, 그 이름난 형 후안 안드레아 도리아에게 베푼 지극한 후함으로 그것이 드러났다. 그의 죽음을 더욱 슬프게 한 것은, 이제 요새가 함락된 것을 보고 그가 몸을 의탁한 어떤 아랍인들에게 죽임을 당하였다는 사실이다. 그들은 그를 무어인으로 변장시켜, 산호 채취에 종사하는 제노바인들이 차지하고 있던 해안의 작은 요새 혹은 거점인 타바르카까지 데려다 주겠다고 제안하였다. 이 아랍인들은 그의 목을 베어 튀르크 함대 사령관에게 가져갔는데, 그 사령관은 그들에게 우리 카스티야 속담, 곧 \"반역은 마음에 들지언정 반역자는 미움을 받는다\"는 말이 참됨을 입증해 보였다. 그 선물을 가져온 자들을 그를 산 채로 데려오지 않았다는 이유로 목매달라 명하였다고들 하기 때문이다.\n\n요새에서 사로잡힌 그리스도교도들 가운데 돈 페드로 데 아길라르라는 이가 있었는데, 안달루시아의 어느 곳인지는 모르겠으나 그 출신으로, 요새의 기수였으며, 명망이 높고 보기 드문 지성을 갖춘 군인이요, 특히 이른바 시(詩)에 각별한 재능을 지닌 자였다. 내가 이렇게 말하는 까닭은 그의 운명이 그를 내 갤리선, 내 노 젓는 자리로 데려와 같은 주인의 노예로 만들었기 때문이다. 그리고 우리가 그 항구를 떠나기 전 이 신사는 비문(碑文) 삼아 소네트 두 편을 지었으니, 하나는 골레타에 관한 것이요 다른 하나는 요새에 관한 것이었다. 사실 내가 그것을 읊어 드리는 것도 좋겠으니, 외고 있는 데다, 싫어하기보다는 좋아하시리라 생각하기 때문이다.\n\n포로가 돈 페드로 데 아길라르라는 이름을 입에 올리는 순간, 돈 페르난도는 일행을 바라보았고 세 사람 모두 미소 지었다. 그리고 그가 소네트 이야기에 이르렀을 때 그들 가운데 하나가 말하였다. \"더 이야기를 이어 가시기 전에, 말씀하신 그 돈 페드로 데 아길라르가 어찌 되었는지 제게 일러 주시기를 청합니다.\"\n\n\"제가 아는 것이라고는,\" 포로가 대답하였다. \"그가 콘스탄티노플에서 두 해를 지낸 뒤 어느 그리스인 첩자와 함께 아르나우트로 변장하여 달아났다는 것뿐입니다. 그가 자유를 되찾았는지 어떤지는 말씀드릴 수 없으나, 그러하였으리라 짐작합니다. 한 해 뒤에 콘스탄티노플에서 그 그리스인을 보았기 때문인데, 다만 그 여정의 결말이 어찌 되었는지는 물어볼 수가 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그대 말이 옳습니다.\" 그 신사가 대답하였다. \"그 돈 페드로는 제 형제이니, 지금 우리 마을에서 건강히, 부유하게, 결혼하여 세 자식을 두고 지내고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그분께 베푸신 모든 자비에 대해 하느님께 감사드립니다.\" 포로가 말하였다. \"제 생각에는 잃었던 자유를 되찾는 것에 견줄 만한 행복이 이 땅에는 없기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"게다가 더한 것은,\" 그 신사가 말하였다. \"제 형제가 지은 그 소네트들을 제가 알고 있다는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그것을 읊어 주십시오.\" 포로가 말하였다. \"저보다 그대께서 더 잘 읊으실 테니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요.\" 그 신사가 말하였다. \"골레타에 관한 것은 이렇습니다.\"\n\n“이 죽음의 껍데기에서 벗어나 자유로워진 복된 영혼들이여, 용맹한 행적의 보답으로 축복받아, 우리네 이 비천한 땅덩이를 넘어 저 위에 머물러 하늘과 불멸의 상속자가 되었도다, 고귀한 분노와 불타는 열정으로 빛나며 그대들은 힘이 있는 동안 그 힘을 전장에서 떨쳤고, 그대들 자신의 피와 적의 피로 모래 흙과 사방을 두른 바다를 물들였노라. 먼저 다한 것은 지쳐 가던 핏기였으니 지친 팔이 먼저 꺾였을 뿐, 굳센 심장은 결코 떨지 않았도다. 패하였어도 그대들은 승자의 관을 얻었으며, 애도받으면서도 그대들의 쓰러짐은 여전히 개선이었으니, 칼과 성벽 사이에서 그대들은 그곳에서 하늘에서는 영광을, 땅에서는 명성을 얻었음이라.”\n“바로 그것이오, 내 기억으로는.” 포로가 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 요새에 관한 것은,” 그 신사가 말했다, “내 기억이 맞다면 이렇게 가오.\n\n“황폐해진 이 땅, 산산이 부서진 이 껍데기, 그 성벽과 망루가 여기 폐허로 누워 있는 이곳으로부터 삼천의 병사 혼들이 높이 날아올라, 복된 자들의 밝은 거처에 깃들었도다. 적의 맹습을 물리치고자 팔의 힘으로 헛되이 애를 다 썼으나, 마침내 죽는 일 외에는 남은 것이 없게 되었을 때, 지치고 얼마 남지 않은 마지막 수호자들이 쓰러졌노라. 그리고 이 메마른 땅은 예나 지금이나 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 슬픈 추억의 터전이었으되, 우리 시대에도 옛 시절에도 한결같았도다. 허나 그 단단한 품에서 하늘로 보낸 것 중에 이들보다 더 순결한 혼은 없었으리라, 짐작건대, 또한 그 표면에 이보다 더 용맹한 육신을 떠받친 적도 없었으리라.”\n\n소네트들은 미움을 받지 않았고, 포로는 그것들이 전해 주는 자기 전우의 소식에 기뻐하며, 이야기를 이어 가 이렇게 말했다.\n\n골레타와 요새가 이렇게 그들의 손에 들어가자, 튀르크인들은 골레타를 헐어 버리라는 명령을 내렸으니 — 요새는 이미 평평하게 할 것이 하나도 남지 않을 만큼 무너져 버린 상태였기 때문이오 — 일을 더 빠르고 쉽게 하려고 세 군데에 폭약을 묻었으나, 가장 약해 보이던 부분, 곧 옛 성벽만은 어디서도 날려 버릴 수가 없었고, 반면 프라틴이 쌓아 올린 새 방어 시설 가운데 서 있던 것은 모조리 더없이 쉽게 땅바닥에 주저앉았소. 마침내 함대는 승리와 개선을 안고 콘스탄티노플로 돌아갔고, 몇 달 뒤 내 주인이 죽었으니, 곧 엘 우찰리, 다른 이름으로는 우찰리 파르탁스라 불리는 자인데, 그것은 튀르크 말로 \"딱지투성이 배교자\"라는 뜻이오. 실제로 그러했소. 튀르크인들은 사람이 지닌 어떤 결점이나 미덕을 따서 이름을 붙이는 관습이 있는데, 그 까닭은 그들 가운데 오스만 가문에서 혈통을 잇는 가문에 속한 성은 단 네 개뿐이고, 나머지는 내가 말한 대로 신체의 흠이나 도덕적 자질에서 이름과 성을 따 오기 때문이오. 이 \"딱지투성이\"는 십사 년 동안 대제후의 노예로 노를 저었고, 서른네 살이 넘었을 무렵, 노를 젓던 중에 어느 튀르크인에게 얻어맞은 데 앙심을 품고는, 복수할 수 있도록 배교하여 자기 신앙을 저버렸소. 그리고 그 용맹이 어찌나 대단했던지, 대제후의 총신 대부분이 권력에 오르는 그 비천한 방편과 수단에 기대지 않고도 알제의 왕이 되었고, 그 후에는 해군 총사령관이 되었으니, 이는 그 왕국에서 세 번째로 높은 신임의 자리요. 그는 태생이 칼라브리아 사람으로 도덕적으로는 훌륭한 인물이었으며, 노예들을 매우 인간적으로 대했소. 그에게는 노예가 삼천 명 있었는데, 그가 죽은 뒤 유언으로 지시한 대로 대제후(죽는 모든 자의 상속자가 되어 고인의 자식들과 함께 나누어 가지는 자요)와 그의 배교자들 사이에 나뉘었소. 나는 어느 베네치아 출신 배교자의 몫으로 떨어졌는데, 그자는 배에서 사환 노릇을 하던 중 우찰리에게 붙잡혀 그토록 총애를 받아 그의 가장 사랑받는 젊은이 가운데 하나가 되었던 인물이오. 그는 내가 본 가운데 가장 잔인한 배교자가 되었으니, 이름은 하산 아가요, 큰 부자가 되어 알제의 왕이 되었소. 나는 그와 함께 콘스탄티노플에서 그곳으로 갔는데, 스페인에 그렇게 가까이 가게 되어 차라리 기뻤소. 내 불행한 처지를 누군가에게 편지로 알리려는 뜻에서가 아니라, 콘스탄티노플에서보다 알제에서 운이 좀 더 너그러워질지 시험해 보려는 것이었소. 콘스탄티노플에서는 천 가지 방법으로 달아나려 했으나 끝내 적당한 때나 기회를 찾지 못했기 때문이오. 그러나 알제에서는 내가 그토록 간절히 품어 온 목적을 이룰 다른 수단을 찾아보기로 결심했으니, 자유를 얻으리라는 희망이 결코 나를 떠난 적이 없었기 때문이오. 그래서 내 음모와 계략과 시도가 기대에 부응하지 못했을 때도, 절망에 빠지는 일 없이 나는 곧장 나를 지탱해 줄 어떤 새로운 희망을, 그것이 아무리 희미하고 가냘프더라도 찾거나 지어내기 시작했소.\n\n이렇게 나는 튀르크인들이 바뇨라 부르는 건물, 곧 감옥에 갇혀 살았는데, 거기에는 그리스도교도 포로들을 가두어 두니, 왕에게 속한 자들이나 사사로운 개인에게 속한 자들이나, 또 그들이 알마센이라 부르는 자들도 함께였소. 알마센이란 말하자면 시 소속 노예들로, 공공 토목 공사나 그 밖의 일에 동원되어 도시에 봉사하는 자들이오. 다만 이런 부류의 포로들은 자유를 되찾기가 몹시 어려우니, 그들은 공공의 재산이라 특정한 주인이 없어, 설령 몸값을 치를 방편이 있다 해도 그 몸값을 두고 흥정할 상대가 없기 때문이오. 내가 말했듯이 이 바뇨에는 마을의 사사로운 개인들이 자기 포로들을, 특히 몸값을 받고 풀어 줄 포로들을 데려다 두는 관습이 있었소. 거기서라면 몸값이 도착할 때까지 그들을 안전하고 편안하게 둘 수 있었기 때문이오. 몸값이 걸린 왕의 포로들 역시 다른 선원들과 함께 일하러 나가지 않았으나, 몸값이 지체될 경우에는 예외였소. 그럴 때는 몸값을 더 다급하게 재촉하는 편지를 쓰게 하려고 그들을 일하게 하고 땔나무를 하러 보냈는데, 그것은 결코 가벼운 노역이 아니었소.\n\n그러나 나는 몸값이 걸린 포로 가운데 하나였소. 내가 대위라는 것이 발각되자, 비록 내 빈약한 형편과 재산 없음을 밝혔건만, 무엇으로도 그들을 설득하여 나를 신사들과 몸값을 기다리는 자들 무리에서 빼낼 수가 없었기 때문이오. 그들은 내게 사슬을 채웠는데, 안전하게 붙잡아 두려는 것보다는 이런 표시로서의 의미가 더 컸소. 그렇게 나는 그 바뇨에서 몸값에 걸린 자로 지목된 다른 여러 신사와 지체 높은 사람들과 더불어 삶을 이어 갔소. 그러나 때때로, 아니 거의 언제나 우리는 굶주림과 헐벗음에 시달렸으되, 무엇보다 우리를 괴롭힌 것은 내 주인이 그리스도교도들에게 가하는, 유례없고 듣도 보도 못한 잔혹함을 사사건건 듣고 보는 일이었소. 그는 날마다 한 사람을 목매달고, 한 사람을 꼬챙이에 꿰고, 또 한 사람의 귀를 잘랐소. 그것도 어찌나 하찮은 도발에, 아니 전혀 아무런 도발도 없이 그러했던지, 튀르크인들조차 그가 그저 그 짓을 하기 위해, 또 천성이 온 인류를 향해 살의에 차 있어서 그러는 것이라고 인정했소. 그자에게서 그나마 잘 대접받은 유일한 사람은 데 사아베드라라는 이름의 어느 스페인 병사였는데, 그자는 결코 그에게 직접 매를 들거나 매를 들게 시키거나 모진 말 한마디 던지지 않았소. 그가 자기 자유를 되찾으려고, 그곳 사람들의 기억 속에 여러 해 남을 만한 일들을 저질렀건만 말이오. 그가 한 그 많은 일 가운데 가장 사소한 것만으로도 우리는 모두 그가 꼬챙이에 꿰이지나 않을까 두려워했고, 그 자신도 한두 번 그것을 겁냈소. 다만 시간이 허락지 않으니 그렇지, 그 병사가 한 일에 관해서는 지금 내 이야기를 늘어놓는 것보다 여러분에게 훨씬 더 흥미롭고 놀라울 무언가를 들려 드릴 수 있을 것이오.\n\n이야기를 계속하자면, 우리 감옥의 안뜰은 지체 높은 어느 부유한 무어인의 집 창문들에서 내려다보였소. 그 창문들은 무어인 집에서 흔히 그렇듯 창이라기보다는 총안에 가까웠고, 게다가 두껍고 촘촘한 격자로 덮여 있었소. 그러던 어느 날, 내가 다른 세 전우와 함께 우리 감옥 옥상에서 시간을 보내려고 사슬을 찬 채 얼마나 멀리 뛸 수 있는지를 겨루고 있었는데 — 다른 그리스도교도들은 모두 일하러 나가고 우리만 있었소 — 마침 눈을 들어 보니, 그 작고 닫힌 창문 가운데 하나에서 갈대가 하나 나타났고 그 끝에는 천이 매여 있었으며, 그것이 이리저리 흔들리며 우리더러 와서 가져가라고 신호하는 듯 움직였소. 우리는 그것을 지켜보았고, 나와 함께 있던 자들 가운데 하나가 가서 갈대 아래에 서서 그것을 떨어뜨릴지, 아니면 어떻게 하는지를 보려 했소. 그러나 그가 그러자 갈대는 들어 올려져 좌우로 흔들렸으니, 마치 고개를 저어 \"아니다\"라고 말하려는 듯했소. 그 그리스도교도가 돌아오자 갈대는 다시 내려졌고 전과 같은 움직임을 했소. 내 다른 전우 하나가 갔으나 첫째와 똑같은 일이 벌어졌고, 그다음에 셋째가 나아갔으되 첫째와 둘째와 똑같은 결과였소. 이를 보고 나는 내 운을 시험해 보고 싶어졌고, 내가 갈대 아래로 다가서자마자 그것이 떨어져 내 발치에, 바뇨 안쪽으로 떨어졌소. 나는 서둘러 천을 풀었는데, 거기 매듭이 하나 있는 것을 알아챘고, 그 안에는 시아니 열 닢이 들어 있었소. 그것은 무어인들 사이에 통용되는 질 낮은 금화로, 한 닢이 우리 돈으로 십 레알 값어치가 나가는 것이었소.\n\n이 뜻밖의 선물에 내가 기뻐했음은 말할 것도 없거니와, 이 행운이 어찌하여 우리에게, 특히 내게 찾아왔는지 짐작해 보려 애쓰는 동안 그 기쁨은 놀라움 못지않았소. 나를 제외한 누구에게도 갈대를 떨어뜨리지 않으려는 그 명백한 거부가, 그 호의가 나를 향한 것임을 보여 주었기 때문이오. 나는 그 반가운 돈을 받아 갈대를 부러뜨리고 옥상으로 돌아왔는데, 창문을 올려다보니 아주 흰 손 하나가 나와 매우 재빨리 열렸다 닫혔소. 이로써 우리는 이런 친절을 베푼 것이 그 집에 사는 어떤 여인임이 틀림없다고 짐작하거나 상상했고, 우리가 그것을 고마워한다는 것을 보이려고 무어인들의 방식을 따라 머리를 숙이고 몸을 굽히고 두 팔을 가슴에 엇갈려 절을 했소. 잠시 후 같은 창문에서 갈대로 만든 작은 십자가가 나왔다가 곧 거두어졌소. 이 표시로 우리는 어떤 그리스도교도 여인이 그 집에 포로로 있으며, 우리에게 그토록 친절을 베푼 이가 바로 그 여인이라고 믿게 되었소. 그러나 우리가 본 그 손의 흰빛과 팔찌들은 그 생각을 떨치게 했으니, 그래도 우리는 그 여인이 주인들이 흔히, 그것도 기꺼이 정실로 삼는 그리스도교도 배교 여인 가운데 하나일지도 모른다고 여겼소. 그들은 그런 여인을 자기 나라 여인보다 더 좋아하기 때문이오. 우리의 온갖 추측은 진실과는 한참 동떨어진 것이었소. 그리하여 그때부터 우리의 유일한 일거리는 십자가가 우리에게 나타났던 그 창문을, 마치 우리의 북극성인 양 지켜보며 응시하는 것이었소. 그러나 적어도 보름이 지나도록 우리는 그것도, 그 손도, 그 어떤 다른 표시도 보지 못했소. 그동안 우리는 그 집에 사는 이가 누구인지, 그 안에 그리스도교도 배교자가 있는지를 알아내려고 온갖 애를 다 썼으나, 거기 사는 자가 하지 모라토라는 이름의 지체 높은 부유한 무어인으로, 전에는 라 파타의 알카이데를 지냈고 그것이 그들 사이에서 매우 높은 지위의 직책이라는 것 외에는 아무도 더 일러 줄 수 있는 자가 없었소. 그런데 그 방면에서 더는 시아니가 비처럼 쏟아지지 않으리라 생각하던 바로 그때, 우리는 갈대가 불쑥 나타나 거기에 또 다른 천이 더 큰 매듭으로 매여 있는 것을 보았으니, 이번에도 앞서처럼 바뇨가 텅 비어 아무도 없을 때였소.\n\n우리는 전처럼 시험을 해 보았는데, 나보다 먼저 같은 세 사람이 차례로 나아갔소. 그러나 갈대는 나 말고는 누구에게도 건네지지 않았고, 내가 다가서자 그것이 떨어졌소. 나는 매듭을 풀어 스페인 금화 사십 에스쿠도와 함께 아랍어로 적힌 종이 한 장을 발견했는데, 그 글의 끝에는 큰 십자가가 하나 그려져 있었소. 나는 그 십자가에 입을 맞추고 에스쿠도를 챙긴 뒤 옥상으로 돌아왔고, 우리 모두 절을 했소. 다시 그 손이 나타났고, 나는 종이를 읽겠다는 신호를 보냈으며, 그러자 창문이 닫혔소. 우리는 벌어진 일에 기쁨이 가득하면서도 모두 어리둥절했소. 우리 가운데 누구도 아랍어를 알지 못했기에, 그 종이에 무엇이 담겨 있는지 알고 싶은 호기심이 컸고, 그것을 읽어 줄 누군가를 찾는 어려움은 더 컸소. 마침내 나는 무르시아 태생의 어느 배교자에게 털어놓기로 결심했소. 그자는 내게 대단한 우정을 표했고, 내가 맡길지 모를 어떤 비밀이든 지키겠다고 다짐하는 담보를 준 적이 있었소. 어떤 배교자들은 그리스도교 영토로 돌아갈 작정일 때, 이름난 포로들에게서 받은 증명서를 몸에 지니고 다니는 관습이 있는데, 그 증명서에는 가능한 어떤 형식으로든 아무개라는 배교자가 늘 그리스도교도들에게 친절을 베풀어 온 훌륭한 사람이며, 기회가 닿는 대로 달아나기를 간절히 바라고 있다고 증언되어 있소. 어떤 자들은 좋은 뜻으로 이런 증명서를 얻지만, 어떤 자들은 그것을 교활하게 써먹소. 그들이 그리스도교 영토로 약탈하러 갔다가 어쩌다 난파하거나 포로로 붙잡히면, 그 증명서를 내밀며 이 종이들로 자기가 온 목적을 알 수 있으리라 말하니, 곧 그리스도교 땅에 머물려는 것이었고, 바로 이 목적을 위해 튀르크인들의 약탈 행각에 가담했다는 것이오. 이런 식으로 그들은 첫 분노의 결과를 피하고 그것이 해를 끼치기 전에 교회와 화해한 다음, 기회가 닿으면 다시 바르바리로 돌아가 이전의 자기로 되돌아가는 것이오. 그러나 이런 종이를 구해 정직하게 써먹어 그리스도교 땅에 머무는 자들도 있소. 그러니 내 이 친구는 내가 말한 그런 배교자 가운데 하나였소. 그는 우리 전우 모두에게서 받은 증명서를 가지고 있었는데, 거기서 우리는 할 수 있는 한 강력하게 그를 위해 증언했고, 만일 무어인들이 그 종이를 발견했더라면 그를 산 채로 불태웠을 것이오.\n\n나는 그가 아랍어를 매우 잘 이해하고, 말할 뿐 아니라 쓸 줄도 안다는 것을 알고 있었소. 그러나 그에게 모든 일을 털어놓기 전에, 나는 내 감방의 어느 구멍에서 우연히 발견한 이 종이를 읽어 달라고 청했소. 그는 그것을 펼치고는 한동안 그것을 살피며 번역하느라 혼자 중얼거렸소. 나는 그에게 그것을 이해하겠느냐고 물었고, 그는 완벽하게 이해한다며, 만일 그 뜻을 한 마디 한 마디 일러 주기를 바란다면 더 만족스럽게 할 수 있도록 펜과 잉크를 달라고 했소. 우리는 즉시 그가 청한 것을 주었고, 그는 그것을 한 토막씩 번역하기 시작했으며, 다 마치고 나서 이렇게 말했소.\n\n“이 무어인 종이에 담긴 내용을 모두 스페인어로 옮겼소. 그리고 ‘렐라 마리엔’이라 한 곳은 ‘우리의 성모 마리아님’을 뜻한다는 것을 명심해 두시오.”\n\n우리는 그 종이를 읽었는데, 이렇게 적혀 있었소.\n\n“제가 어릴 적에 제 아버지에게는 노예가 하나 있었는데, 그 여자가 제게 제 말로 그리스도교의 기도를 외는 법을 가르쳐 주었고 렐라 마리엔에 관해 많은 것을 일러 주었습니다. 그 그리스도교도 여자는 죽었으나, 저는 그 여자가 불로 가지 않고 알라께로 갔음을 압니다. 그 뒤로 제가 그 여자를 두 번 보았는데, 저더러 그리스도교도들의 땅으로 가서 저를 무척 사랑하시는 렐라 마리엔을 뵈라고 말했기 때문입니다. 저는 어떻게 가야 할지 모릅니다. 그리스도교도를 많이 보았으나, 당신 말고는 누구도 신사처럼 보이지 않았습니다. 저는 젊고 아름다우며, 가지고 갈 돈이 넉넉합니다. 우리가 어떻게 갈 수 있을지 당신이 궁리해 낼 수 있을지 보십시오. 그러기를 바라신다면 당신은 그곳에서 제 남편이 될 것이고, 바라지 않으셔도 저는 괴롭지 않을 것이니, 렐라 마리엔께서 저와 혼인할 누군가를 찾아 주실 것이기 때문입니다. 이것은 제가 손수 썼습니다. 누구에게 이것을 읽게 하는지 조심하십시오. 어떤 무어인도 믿지 마십시오, 그들은 모두 배신을 일삼는 자들이니까요. 저는 이 일로 몹시 근심하고 있으니, 당신이 누구에게도 털어놓지 않으셨으면 합니다. 제 아버지가 이를 알면 당장 저를 우물에 던지고 돌로 덮어 버릴 것이기 때문입니다. 제가 갈대에 실을 매어 두겠으니, 거기에 답을 매십시오. 그리고 당신에게 아랍어로 써 줄 사람이 없으면 신호로 제게 일러 주십시오, 렐라 마리엔께서 제가 당신을 알아듣게 해 주실 것입니다. 그분과 알라와, 포로가 일러 준 대로 제가 자주 입 맞추는 이 십자가가 당신을 지켜 주시기를.”\n\n여러분, 이 종이의 말에 우리가 놀라고 기뻐할 만했는지 헤아려 보시오. 그 놀라움과 기쁨이 어찌나 컸던지, 배교자는 그 종이가 우연히 발견된 것이 아니라 실은 우리 가운데 누군가에게 보내진 것임을 알아챘소. 그래서 그는 자기가 의심하는 바가 사실이라면 자기를 믿고 모든 것을 말해 달라며, 우리의 자유를 위해서라면 목숨을 걸겠다고 간청했소. 그러면서 그는 가슴에서 쇠로 된 십자가상을 꺼내 많은 눈물을 흘리며, 그 형상이 표상하는 하느님께, 죄 많고 사악한 자신이지만 진실하고 충실하게 믿는 그 하느님께 맹세하여, 우리에게 충실하고 우리가 밝히기로 택한 것은 무엇이든 비밀로 지키겠다고 했소. 그가 그렇게 생각하고 거의 예감하기를, 그 종이를 쓴 그 여인을 통해 자기와 우리 모두가 자유를 얻고, 자신 또한 그토록 갈망하던 것, 곧 제 죄와 무지로 인해 지금은 썩은 가지처럼 잘려 나가 있는 거룩한 어머니 교회의 품으로 되돌아가는 일을 이루리라 했기 때문이오. 배교자는 어찌나 많은 눈물과 참회의 기색으로 이 말을 했던지, 우리는 한마음으로 이 일의 모든 진실을 그에게 말하기로 동의했고, 그리하여 그에게 아무것도 숨기지 않고 모든 것을 낱낱이 들려주었소. 우리는 그에게 갈대가 나타났던 창문을 가리켜 보였고, 그는 그것으로 그 집을 눈여겨 두었으며, 거기에 누가 사는지 각별히 주의를 기울여 알아내기로 결심했소. 또한 우리는 그 무어인 여인의 편지에 답하는 것이 좋겠다고 합의했고, 배교자는 한순간도 지체 없이 내가 불러 주는 말을 받아 적었으니, 그것은 바로 내가 여러분에게 들려 드릴 그대로요. 이 일에서 일어난 중요한 일 가운데 무엇 하나 내 기억에서 빠져나간 것이 없고, 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 결코 빠져나가지 않을 것이기 때문이오. 그러니 무어인 여인에게 보낸 답은 이러했소.\n\n“참된 알라께서 당신을 지켜 주시기를, 부인이여, 그리고 하느님의 참된 어머니이시며 당신을 사랑하시기에 그리스도교도들의 땅으로 가도록 당신 마음에 그 뜻을 심어 주신 저 복된 마리엔께서 지켜 주시기를. 그분께서 당신에게 내리신 그 명을 당신이 어떻게 이행할 수 있는지 보여 주시기를 그분께 간청하십시오. 그분의 선하심이 그러하시니 반드시 그리하실 것입니다. 저로 말하자면, 또 저와 함께 있는 이 모든 그리스도교도를 대신하여, 죽음에 이르기까지 당신을 위해 우리가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 하겠다고 약속합니다. 제게 편지하기를 거르지 마시고 당신이 어찌하실 작정인지 알려 주십시오. 그러면 저는 늘 답하겠습니다. 위대하신 알라께서 당신의 언어를 잘 말하고 쓸 줄 아는 그리스도교도 포로를 우리에게 주셨으니, 이 종이로 보시는 바와 같습니다. 그러니 두려움 없이 당신은 원하는 바를 모두 우리에게 알리실 수 있습니다. 당신이 그리스도교도들의 땅에 닿으면 제 아내가 되겠다 하신 말씀에 대해서는, 선량한 그리스도교도로서 그것을 약속드립니다. 그리스도교도는 무어인보다 약속을 더 잘 지킨다는 것을 아셔야 합니다. 알라와 그분의 어머니 마리엔께서 당신을 보살피시기를, 부인이여.”\n\n종이를 써서 접은 뒤 나는 이틀을 기다려 바뇨가 전처럼 텅 비기를 기다렸고, 곧장 옥상의 늘 다니던 산책길로 가서 갈대의 기미가 있는지 살폈는데, 그것은 오래지 않아 나타났소. 나는 그것을 보자마자, 누가 그것을 내미는지는 분간할 수 없었으나, 실을 매라는 신호로 종이를 들어 보였소. 그러나 실은 이미 갈대에 매여 있었고, 거기에 나는 종이를 묶었소. 그리고 잠시 후 우리의 별이 다시금 나타났으니, 평화의 흰 깃발인 그 작은 꾸러미와 함께였소. 그것이 떨어졌고 나는 그것을 집어 들었는데, 천 안에서 온갖 종류의 금화와 은화로 오십 에스쿠도가 넘는 돈을 발견했소. 그것은 우리의 기쁨을 쉰 배나 더 굳세게 했고 자유를 얻으리라는 우리의 희망을 갑절로 키웠소. 바로 그날 밤 우리 배교자가 돌아와 말하기를, 우리가 들은 그 무어인이 그 집에 사는데, 이름은 하지 모라토이고, 엄청난 부자이며, 그 모든 재산의 상속녀인 외동딸이 하나 있는데, 그 도시 전체의 한결같은 평판으로 그 여인이 바르바리에서 가장 아름다운 여인이라 하고, 그곳에 부임한 여러 부왕이 그 여인을 아내로 구했으나 그 여인은 늘 혼인하기를 마다해 왔다는 것을 알아냈다 했소. 게다가 그 여인에게는 그리스도교도 노예 여자가 하나 있었는데 지금은 죽었다는 것도 알아냈으니, 이 모든 것이 그 종이의 내용과 들어맞았소. 우리는 곧장 배교자와 더불어 그 무어인 여인을 빼내어 우리 모두를 그리스도교 영토로 데려가려면 어떤 수단을 써야 할지 의논했고, 결국 당분간은 소라이다(지금은 마리아라 불리기를 바라는 그 여인의 이름이 그러하오)에게서 두 번째 연락이 오기를 기다리기로 합의했으니, 우리는 이 모든 난관에서 빠져나갈 길을 찾을 수 있는 이는 그 여인이지 다른 누구도 아님을 똑똑히 보았기 때문이오. 이렇게 결정하고 나자 배교자는 우리더러 마음을 졸이지 말라며, 목숨을 잃든지 아니면 우리를 자유로 되돌려 놓겠다고 했소. 나흘 동안 바뇨가 사람들로 가득 찼던 까닭에 갈대는 나흘이나 나타나기를 미루었으나, 그 기간이 끝나고 바뇨가 으레 그렇듯 텅 비었을 때, 갈대가 천을 어찌나 불룩하게 매단 채 나타났던지 행복한 출산을 예고하는 듯했소. 갈대와 천이 내게 내려왔고, 나는 또 다른 종이 한 장과 함께 다른 동전은 하나 없이 금화로 백 에스쿠도를 발견했소. 배교자가 그 자리에 있어, 우리 감방에서 그에게 읽으라고 종이를 건넸는데, 그 내용은 이러했소.\n\n“세뇨르, 저는 우리가 스페인으로 갈 방도를 생각해 낼 수가 없으며, 렐라 마리엔께서도 비록 제가 여쭈었으나 한 가지도 보여 주지 않으셨습니다. 할 수 있는 일이라고는 제가 이 창문에서 당신에게 금화를 넉넉히 드리는 것뿐입니다. 그것으로 당신과 당신 벗들의 몸값을 치르고, 당신들 가운데 하나가 그리스도교도들의 땅으로 가서 거기서 배를 한 척 사 다른 이들을 위해 돌아오게 하십시오. 그러면 그는 저를 제 아버지의 정원에서 찾게 될 것이니, 그것은 바닷가 가까운 바바손 문 곁에 있고, 저는 이번 여름 내내 제 아버지와 하인들과 함께 그곳에 있을 것입니다. 당신은 거기서 아무 위험 없이 밤에 저를 데리고 나와 배로 데려갈 수 있습니다. 그리고 당신이 제 남편이 되어야 함을 기억하십시오, 그러지 않으면 저는 마리엔께 당신을 벌하시라 기도할 것입니다. 만일 배를 가지러 갈 누군가를 믿을 수 없다면, 당신 자신의 몸값을 치르고 당신이 가십시오. 당신은 신사이며 그리스도교도이니, 다른 누구보다 더 확실히 돌아오시리라는 것을 제가 알기 때문입니다. 그 정원을 잘 알아 두도록 애쓰십시오. 그리고 당신이 저쪽에서 거니는 것을 제가 보면, 바뇨가 비었음을 알고 당신에게 돈을 넉넉히 드리겠습니다. 알라께서 당신을 지켜 주시기를, 세뇨르.”\n\n이것이 두 번째 종이의 말과 내용이었는데, 그것을 듣자 각자 자기가 몸값으로 풀려나는 자가 되겠노라 하며, 한 치의 어김없는 신의로 가서 돌아오겠다고 약속했소. 나 역시 같은 제안을 했소. 그러나 이 모든 것에 배교자가 반대하며 말하기를, 모두가 함께 떠나기 전에 한 사람이 먼저 풀려나는 것에는 어떤 일이 있어도 동의할 수 없다고 했소. 자유를 얻은 자들이 사로잡혀 있을 때 한 약속을 얼마나 형편없이 지키는지 경험으로 알았기 때문이라 했소. 지체 높은 포로들이 흔히 이런 방책에 의지하여, 발렌시아나 마요르카로 가서 배 한 척을 무장시키고 자기 몸값을 치러 준 다른 이들을 위해 돌아올 수 있도록 돈과 함께 한 사람의 몸값을 치렀으나, 그자가 결코 돌아오지 않는 일이 있었으니, 되찾은 자유와 그것을 다시 잃을 두려움이 세상의 온갖 의무를 기억에서 지워 버리기 때문이라 했소. 그리고 자기 말이 진실임을 입증하려고, 그는 거의 바로 그 무렵 어느 그리스도교도 신사에게 일어난 일을, 놀랍고 신기한 일이 매 순간 벌어지는 그곳에서조차 일찍이 일어난 가장 기이한 사건을 간략히 들려주었소. 요컨대 그는 결론짓기를, 할 수 있고 또 마땅히 해야 할 일은, 우리 그리스도교도 가운데 하나의 몸값으로 쓰려던 돈을 자기에게 주어, 자기가 테투안과 그 연안을 오가는 상인이자 무역상이 되는 척하며 그것으로 여기 알제에서 배를 한 척 사는 것이라 했소. 그리하여 배의 주인이 되면, 우리 모두를 바뇨에서 빼내어 배에 태울 어떤 방도를 찾는 것이 그에게는 쉬운 일일 것이라 했소. 특히 그 무어인 여인이 말한 대로 모두의 몸값을 치를 만한 돈을 준다면 더욱 그러하니, 일단 자유로워지면 우리가 환한 대낮에라도 배에 오르는 일은 세상에서 가장 쉬운 일일 것이기 때문이라 했소. 다만 가장 큰 어려움은, 무어인들은 어떤 배교자에게도 배를 사거나 소유하는 것을 허락하지 않는다는 점인데, 약탈 원정을 나가는 큰 배라면 또 모를까, 작은 배를 사는 자는, 특히 스페인 사람이라면, 오로지 그리스도교 영토로 달아나려는 목적으로 그것을 원할 뿐이라 두려워하기 때문이라 했소. 그러나 이것은 타가린 무어인 하나와 함께 배 구입과 화물의 이문을 나누기로 약정하여 넘어설 수 있다고 했소. 그리고 이를 빌미로 그가 배의 주인이 될 수 있을 것이니, 그렇게 되면 나머지 모든 일은 다 이루어진 것이나 다름없다고 보았소. 그러나 나와 내 전우들에게는 그 무어인 여인이 제안한 대로 마요르카로 사람을 보내 배를 구하는 편이 더 나은 계획으로 여겨졌으되, 우리는 감히 그에게 반대하지 못했소. 만일 그가 말한 대로 하지 않으면 그가 우리를 밀고하여 우리 모두 목숨을 잃을 위험에 빠뜨릴지도 모른다는 두려움 때문이었으니, 그가 소라이다와 우리의 거래를 폭로하기라도 한다면 — 그 여인의 목숨을 위해서라면 우리 모두 제 목숨을 다 내놓았을 것이오 — 큰일이었기 때문이오. 그리하여 우리는 우리 자신을 하느님과 배교자의 손에 맡기기로 결심했소. 동시에 소라이다에게 답을 보내, 우리가 그녀가 권한 모든 것을 하겠으며, 그녀가 마치 렐라 마리엔께서 전해 주신 것처럼 훌륭한 조언을 주었으니, 일을 미룰지 곧장 실행에 옮길지는 오직 그녀에게 달렸다고 일렀소. 나는 그녀의 남편이 되겠다는 약속을 다시 새겼소. 그리하여 이튿날 바뇨가 마침 비었을 때, 그녀는 갈대와 천을 통해 여러 차례에 걸쳐 우리에게 금화 이천 에스쿠도와 함께 종이 한 장을 주었는데, 거기서 그녀는 다음 후마, 곧 금요일에 자기 아버지의 정원으로 가는데, 그 전에 우리에게 돈을 더 주겠다고 했소. 그리고 그것으로 부족하면 알려 달라며, 우리가 청하는 만큼 주겠으니, 자기 아버지가 가진 것이 하도 많아 알아채지도 못할 것이고, 게다가 모든 열쇠를 자기가 지니고 있다고 했소.\n\n우리는 곧장 배교자에게 배를 사라고 오백 에스쿠도를 주었고, 팔백 에스쿠도로 나는 내 몸값을 치렀으니, 마침 그때 알제에 있던 발렌시아의 어느 상인에게 돈을 주었소. 그는 자기 말만 믿고 나를 풀려나게 해 주었으니, 발렌시아에서 첫 배가 도착하면 내 몸값을 치르겠다고 약조한 것이오. 만일 그가 돈을 당장 치렀더라면, 왕은 내 몸값이 오래전부터 알제에 있었는데도 상인이 제 잇속을 챙기려고 그것을 비밀로 했다고 의심했을 것이오. 사실 내 주인이 어찌나 상대하기 까다로운 자였던지, 나는 어떤 일이 있어도 감히 돈을 당장 내려놓을 수 없었소. 아름다운 소라이다가 정원으로 가기로 한 금요일 전 목요일에, 그녀는 우리에게 천 에스쿠도를 더 주고 자기가 떠난다는 것을 일러 주며, 내가 몸값을 치르고 풀려났거든 곧장 자기 아버지의 정원을 알아내어 무슨 수를 써서라도 거기로 가 자기를 만날 기회를 찾으라고 간청했소. 나는 몇 마디로 그리하겠노라 답하고, 포로가 그녀에게 가르쳐 준 모든 기도를 다해 우리를 렐라 마리엔께 부탁드리는 것을 잊지 말라고 했소. 이 일이 이루어지고 나서, 우리 세 전우의 몸값을 치를 조치를 취하여 그들이 바뇨를 떠날 수 있게 했소. 행여 내가 몸값을 치르고 풀려나는데 자기들은 돈이 마련되어 있는데도 그러지 못하는 것을 보고 그 일로 소동을 일으켜, 악마가 그들을 부추겨 소라이다를 해칠 무슨 짓을 하게 할까 염려해서였소. 비록 그들의 신분으로 보아 이런 우려에서 벗어날 만했으나, 그래도 나는 이 일에서 어떤 위험도 무릅쓰고 싶지 않았소. 그래서 나는 그들도 나와 같은 방식으로 몸값을 치르게 하여, 그 상인에게 모든 돈을 넘겨 그가 안전하고 자신 있게 담보를 설 수 있도록 했소. 다만 우리의 계획과 비밀은 그에게 털어놓지 않았으니, 그것은 위험할 수도 있었기 때문이오.\n\n# IN WHICH THE CAPTIVE STILL CONTINUES HIS ADVENTURES\n\n보름이 채 지나기도 전에 우리의 배교자는 벌써 서른 명도 더 태울 수 있는 훌륭한 배 한 척을 사들였다. 그리고 그 거래를 안전하게 만들고 그럴듯한 명분을 붙이기 위해 그는 알제에서 오랑 쪽으로 스무 레과쯤 떨어진 셰르셀이라는 곳으로 항해를 다녀오는 것이 좋겠다고 여겨 실제로 그리했는데, 그곳은 말린 무화과 거래가 활발한 곳이었다. 그는 두세 차례 앞서 말한 타가린 사람과 함께 이 항해를 했다. 아라곤의 무어인들을 바르바리아에서는 타가린이라 부르고 그라나다의 무어인들은 무데하르라 부르나, 페스 왕국에서는 무데하르를 엘체라 부르며, 이들은 왕이 전쟁에 주로 부리는 사람들이다. 이야기를 이어 가자면, 그는 배를 몰고 지날 때마다 소라이다가 기다리는 정원에서 석궁 두 번 쏠 거리도 안 되는 어느 후미에 닻을 내렸다. 그리고 거기서 배교자는 노를 젓는 두 무어 청년과 함께 일부러 머무르며 기도를 올리거나, 아니면 본격적으로 행하려는 일을 미리 연습 삼아 해 보곤 했다. 그렇게 그는 소라이다의 정원으로 가서 과일을 청했고, 그녀의 아버지는 그를 알아보지 못한 채 그것을 내주었다. 그러나 그가 나중에 내게 말하기로는, 소라이다에게 말을 걸어 자기가 누구인지, 그리고 내 명령에 따라 그녀를 기독교도들의 땅으로 데려가기로 되어 있으니 안심하고 마음 놓아도 좋다는 것을 일러 주려 애썼으나 끝내 그리할 수 없었다고 했다. 무어 여인들은 남편이나 아버지가 시키지 않는 한 어떤 무어인이나 튀르크인에게도 자기 모습을 보이지 않기 때문이다. 다만 기독교도 포로들과는 격의 없이 어울리고 이야기 나누는 것을 허락하니, 어쩌면 온당하다 여겨질 정도를 넘어설 만큼이다. 그러나 내 처지에서는 그가 그녀에게 말을 걸지 않은 것이 차라리 다행스러웠을 것이니, 자기 일이 배교자들 입에 오르내리는 것을 알면 그녀가 놀랐을지도 모르기 때문이다. 그러나 일을 달리 마련하신 하느님께서는 우리 배교자의 선의에 찬 뜻이 이루어질 기회를 주지 않으셨다. 그리고 그는 셰르셀을 얼마나 안전하게 오갈 수 있는지, 또 언제 어떻게 어디서든 마음대로 닻을 내릴 수 있는지, 동료인 타가린이 오로지 자기 뜻만을 따른다는 것을, 그리고 내 몸값이 치러진 지금 우리에게 필요한 것은 노를 저을 기독교도 몇 사람을 찾는 일뿐임을 알고는, 몸값을 치른 자들 외에 내가 함께 데려갈 만한 자들이 있으면 눈여겨봐 두었다가 다음 금요일을 기해 그들을 끌어들이라고 내게 말했다. 그는 그날을 우리의 출발일로 정했던 것이다. 그래서 나는 모두가 건장한 노잡이이고 도시를 가장 수월하게 빠져나올 수 있는 열두 명의 스페인 사람에게 이야기를 했다. 그러나 바로 그때 그만한 수를 찾는 것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었으니, 스무 척의 배가 순항을 나가 노잡이들을 죄다 데려갔기 때문이다. 만일 그들의 주인이 선대에 올려놓은 갤리엇 한 척을 마무리하느라 그해 여름 바다에 나가지 않고 집에 머물지 않았더라면, 이들조차 찾지 못했을 것이다. 나는 이 사람들에게 다만 다음 금요일 저녁에 한 사람씩 몰래 빠져나와 하지 모라토의 정원 근처를 서성이며 내가 올 때까지 거기서 기다리라고만 일렀다. 이 지시를 나는 각자에게 따로따로 주었으며, 만일 거기서 다른 기독교도들을 보더라도 내가 그 자리에서 기다리라고 시켰다는 말 외에는 아무 말도 하지 말라고 명했다.\n\n이 준비를 마치고 나니, 그보다 더 긴요한 또 한 가지 일을 해 두어야 했다. 그것은 일이 어찌 돌아가는지를 소라이다에게 알려, 그녀가 미리 대비하고 마음의 준비를 갖추어, 기독교도의 배가 돌아올 수 있으리라 생각지도 못한 때에 우리가 갑작스레 그녀를 붙들더라도 놀라지 않게 하는 것이었다. 그리하여 나는 정원에 가서 그녀에게 말을 걸 수 있을지 시험해 보기로 마음먹고, 출발 전날 약초를 캔다는 핑계로 그리로 갔다. 거기서 처음 마주친 사람은 그녀의 아버지였는데, 그는 바르바리아 전역, 심지어 콘스탄티노플에서도 포로와 무어인 사이의 소통 수단으로 쓰이는 언어로 내게 말을 걸었다. 그 언어는 모리스코 말도 카스티야 말도, 다른 어느 나라 말도 아니요 모든 언어가 뒤섞인 것으로, 이를 통해 우리는 모두 서로를 알아들을 수 있다. 그러니까 그는 이런 식의 언어로 내가 자기 정원에서 무엇을 원하는지, 또 누구의 소속인지를 물었다. 나는 아르나우트 마미의 노예라고 대답했다(나는 그가 이 사람의 아주 절친한 벗임을 확실히 알고 있었기 때문이다). 그리고 샐러드를 만들 약초를 좀 얻고 싶다고 했다. 그러자 그는 내가 몸값을 치르기로 되어 있는지, 또 내 주인이 나를 두고 얼마를 요구하는지 물었다. 이런 문답이 오가는 동안, 얼마 전부터 이미 나를 알아본 아리따운 소라이다가 정원 안의 집에서 나왔다. 무어 여인들은 기독교도에게 자기 모습을 보이는 것을 전혀 꺼리지 않으며, 앞서 말했듯이 조금도 수줍어하지 않으므로, 그녀는 망설임 없이 자기 아버지가 나와 함께 서 있는 곳으로 다가왔다. 게다가 그녀의 아버지도 그녀가 천천히 다가오는 것을 보고 어서 오라고 불렀다. 그녀가 내 눈앞에 모습을 드러냈을 때의 그 대단한 아름다움, 그 기품 어린 자태, 그 눈부신 차림새를 지금 여러분께 묘사하는 일은 내 능력 밖일 것이다. 다만 그녀의 고운 목과 귀와 머리에 머리카락 수보다 더 많은 진주가 매달려 있었다고 말하는 것으로 만족하겠다. 관습대로 맨살을 드러낸 발목에는 가장 순도 높은 황금으로 만든 카르카헤(모리스코 말로 팔찌나 발찌를 그렇게 부른다)를 차고 있었는데, 거기에 박힌 다이아몬드가 어찌나 많은지 그녀가 나중에 말하기로는 자기 아버지가 그것을 만 도블론으로 쳤다 했으며, 그녀의 손목에 찬 것도 그만한 값어치가 나갔다. 진주는 무수히 많고 매우 훌륭했으니, 무어 여인들이 가장 으뜸으로 치는 과시와 치장은 값진 진주와 잔진주로 몸을 꾸미는 것이어서, 그렇기에 진주는 다른 어느 민족보다도 무어인들 사이에 더 많다. 소라이다의 아버지는 알제에서 가장 많고 가장 순도 높은 진주를 가졌다는 평판이 있었으며, 또한 이십만 스페인 크라운이 넘는 재산을 지녔다는 평판도 있었다. 그리고 이제는 오로지 나만의 주인인 그녀가 이 모든 것의 주인이었다. 이렇듯 치장한 그녀가 아름다웠을지 아닐지, 또 그녀가 영화로운 시절에 어떠했을지는, 그토록 많은 고초를 겪고도 그녀에게 남아 있는 아름다움으로 미루어 짐작할 수 있으리라. 누구나 알다시피 어떤 여인들의 아름다움은 때와 철을 타며, 우연한 사정으로 더해지기도 덜해지기도 하기 때문이다. 그리고 마음의 격정은 당연히 그것을 드높이기도 망치기도 하지만, 실로 더 흔하게는 그것을 송두리째 무너뜨린다. 한마디로 그날 그녀는 더없이 화려하게 차려입고 지극히 아름다운 모습으로 내 앞에 나타났다. 어쨌든 내게는 그녀가 일찍이 본 가장 아름다운 것으로 여겨졌으며, 게다가 내가 그녀에게 진 모든 빚을 떠올리자, 나는 마치 내게 위안과 행복을 가져다주러 땅으로 내려온 어떤 천상의 존재를 눈앞에 둔 듯한 기분이 들었다.\n\n그녀가 다가오자 아버지는 자기네 말로, 내가 자기 벗 아르나우트 마미에게 속한 포로이며 샐러드 거리를 얻으러 왔다고 그녀에게 일러 주었다.\n\n그녀는 대화를 이어받아, 내가 앞서 말한 그 뒤섞인 언어로 내게 양반인지, 또 왜 몸값을 치르지 않았는지 물었다.\n\n나는 이미 몸값을 치렀으며, 그 값으로 미루어 내 주인이 나를 얼마나 값있게 여겼는지 알 수 있을 것이라고, 내가 천오백 솔타니를 치렀노라고 대답했다. 그러자 그녀가 대답하기를, \"그대가 내 아버지의 것이었다면, 내 그대에게 말해 두거니와, 그 갑절을 준대도 그분이 그대를 내놓게 하지 않았을 거예요. 그대들 기독교도는 늘 자기 자신에 대해 거짓을 늘어놓고, 무어인을 속이려고 스스로를 가난한 척하니까요\" 했다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요, 아가씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"그러나 저는 정말로 제 주인에게 진실되게 대했습니다. 세상 모든 이에게 그러하듯, 또 앞으로도 그러려 하듯 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그대는 언제 떠나죠?\" 소라이다가 말했다.\n\n\"내일인 듯합니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"여기 프랑스에서 온 배 한 척이 내일 떠나는데, 저는 그 배로 갈까 합니다.\"\n\n\"스페인에서 오는 배들이 도착하기를 기다렸다가 그들과 함께 가는 편이 낫지 않겠어요?\" 소라이다가 말했다. \"그대들의 벗도 아닌 프랑스 사람들과 가느니 말이에요.\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 내가 말했다. \"지금 스페인에서 배가 오고 있다는 소식이라도 있다면야 어쩌면 기다릴 수도 있겠지요. 그러나 아무래도 내일 떠날 듯합니다. 제 나라와 제가 사랑하는 이들에게 돌아가고픈 그리움이 너무도 커서, 설령 더 편한 기회라 해도 그것이 늦춰진다면 그것을 기다리도록 저를 내버려 두지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그대는 분명 제 나라에 아내를 두고 왔겠지요.\" 소라이다가 말했다. \"그래서 가서 아내를 보고 싶어 안달하는 거고요.\"\n\n\"저는 혼인하지 않았습니다.\" 내가 대답했다. \"다만 그곳에 도착하면 혼인하겠노라 약조를 해 두었지요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그대가 약조한 그 여인은 아름다운가요?\" 소라이다가 말했다.\n\n\"어찌나 아름다운지,\" 내가 말했다. \"그녀를 합당하게 묘사하고 그대에게 진실을 말하자면, 그녀는 그대와 아주 닮았습니다.\"\n\n이 말에 그녀의 아버지가 자못 흐뭇하게 웃으며 말했다. \"알라께 맹세코, 기독교도여, 내 딸을 닮았다면 그녀는 분명 매우 아름답겠구먼. 내 딸은 이 왕국 전체에서 가장 아름다운 여인이거든. 그저 그 아이를 잘 보기만 해도 내가 진실을 말하고 있음을 알 게야.\"\n\n소라이다의 아버지는 더 나은 통역자였던지라 이 말과 표현들의 대부분을 풀어 주는 데 도움을 주었다. 앞서 말했듯 그녀가 그곳에서 쓰이는 그 잡탕 언어를 말하기는 했어도, 그녀는 자기 뜻을 말보다는 손짓으로 더 많이 나타냈기 때문이다.\n\n우리가 아직 이런 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 한 무어인이 뛰어와 외치기를, 튀르크인 넷이 정원의 울타리인지 담을 넘어 들어와 아직 익지도 않은 과일을 따 모으고 있다는 것이었다. 노인은 놀랐고 소라이다도 그러했으니, 무어인들은 으레, 말하자면 본능적으로 튀르크인을 두려워하기 때문이며, 특히 그 군졸들을 두려워한다. 그들은 자기 세력 아래 있는 무어인들에게 어찌나 오만방자하고 위세를 부리는지, 노예보다도 더 못하게 대하는 것이다. 그녀의 아버지가 소라이다에게 말했다. \"얘야, 내가 가서 저 개들에게 한마디 하는 동안 집 안으로 들어가 문을 걸어 잠그고 있거라. 그리고 그대, 기독교도여, 약초를 캐서 평안히 가게나. 알라께서 그대를 그대의 고향까지 무사히 데려다주시길 비네.\"\n\n나는 절을 했고, 그는 튀르크인들을 찾으러 가 버려, 나를 소라이다와 단둘이 남겨 두었다. 그녀는 아버지의 분부대로 물러나려는 양했으나, 그가 정원의 나무들에 가려 보이지 않게 된 순간, 눈물이 가득한 눈으로 내게 돌아서며 말했다. \"타메히, 크리스티아노, 타메히?\" 다시 말해 \"가시나요, 기독교도여, 가시나요?\" 하는 뜻이었다.\n\n나는 대답했다. \"그렇습니다, 아가씨. 그러나 무슨 일이 있어도 그대 없이는 가지 않겠습니다. 다음 주마에 저를 기다려 주십시오. 그리고 우리를 보거든 놀라지 마십시오. 우리는 틀림없이 기독교도들의 땅으로 갈 것이니까요.\"\n\n나는 우리 사이에 오간 모든 말을 그녀가 온전히 알아듣도록 이렇게 말했다. 그러자 그녀는 내 목에 팔을 두르고 비척거리는 걸음으로 집을 향해 걷기 시작했다. 그러나 운명이 그렇게 정한 탓에(하늘이 달리 마련해 주시지 않았더라면 매우 불운한 일이 될 뻔했다), 내가 묘사한 그대로의 모양과 자세로, 곧 그녀가 내 목에 팔을 두른 채 우리가 막 걸음을 옮기던 바로 그때, 튀르크인들을 돌려보내고 돌아오던 그녀의 아버지가 우리가 어떻게 걷고 있는지를 보았고, 우리도 그가 우리를 본 것을 알아챘다. 그러나 재빠르고 기민한 소라이다는 내 목에서 팔을 떼지 않으려 조심했고, 오히려 내게 더 바싹 다가와 머리를 내 가슴에 기대고는 무릎을 살짝 굽혀 기절하는 온갖 기색과 시늉을 보였다. 나는 그 사이 마치 마지못해 그녀를 부축하는 척했다. 그녀의 아버지가 우리가 있는 곳으로 달려와 자기 딸이 이런 상태인 것을 보고 무슨 일이냐고 물었다. 그러나 그녀가 아무 대답도 하지 않자 그가 말했다. \"저 개들이 들어오는 바람에 놀라 기절한 게 틀림없구나.\" 그러고는 그녀를 내게서 떼어 자기 가슴에 끌어안았다. 그녀는 여전히 눈물에 젖은 눈으로 한숨지으며 다시 말했다. \"아메히, 크리스티아노, 아메히.\" 곧 \"가세요, 기독교도여, 가세요\"라는 뜻이었다. 이에 그녀의 아버지가 대답했다. \"얘야, 기독교도가 갈 필요는 없다. 그는 네게 아무런 해도 끼치지 않았고, 튀르크인들은 이제 가 버렸으니 말이다. 놀라지 말거라, 너를 해칠 것은 아무것도 없으니. 내가 말했듯이 튀르크인들은 내 청대로 왔던 길로 돌아갔단다.\"\n\n\"말씀하신 대로 그녀를 놀라게 한 것은 그들입니다, 나리.\" 내가 그녀의 아버지에게 말했다. \"그러나 그녀가 가라고 하니 저는 그녀의 뜻을 거스르고 싶지 않습니다. 평안하시기를 빌며, 허락하신다면 필요할 때 이 정원으로 약초를 캐러 다시 오겠습니다. 제 주인께서 샐러드용 약초로는 여기보다 더 나은 데가 없다고 하시니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"필요한 것이 있거든 언제든 다시 오게나.\" 하지 모라토가 대답했다. \"내 딸이 이렇게 말하는 것은 그대나 다른 어떤 기독교도가 못마땅해서가 아닐세. 그저 그대가 아니라 튀르크인들더러 가라는 뜻이었거나, 아니면 그대가 약초를 찾으러 갈 때가 되었다는 말이었을 뿐이지.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 나는 곧 두 사람에게 작별을 고했다. 그리고 그녀는 가슴이 찢어질 듯한 얼굴로 아버지와 함께 물러갔다. 나는 약초를 찾는 척하면서 정원을 여유롭게 한 바퀴 돌며, 모든 진입로와 출구, 집의 잠금장치, 그리고 우리의 일을 수월하게 할 수 있도록 이용할 만한 모든 것을 주의 깊게 살펴 두었다.\n\n그렇게 하고 나서 나는 가서 일어난 모든 일을 배교자와 동료들에게 일러 주었고, 두려움이 모두 사라지고 운명이 아리땁고 사랑스러운 소라이다라는 모습으로 내게 내미는 그 상을 손에 넣게 될 시각을 애타게 기다렸다. 마침내 시간이 흘러 우리가 그토록 고대하던 정해진 날이 왔다. 우리는 신중한 숙고와 숱한 긴 논의 끝에 결정한 그 절차와 계획을 모두 따랐고, 바랄 수 있는 만큼 온전히 성공했다. 내가 정원에서 소라이다와 이야기를 나눈 날 이후 다음 금요일, 배교자는 해 질 무렵 그녀가 있던 곳 거의 맞은편에 배의 닻을 내렸다. 노를 저을 기독교도들은 준비를 갖추고 사방 여기저기에 숨어, 모두 나를 기다리며 초조하면서도 들떠 있었고, 눈앞에 있는 배를 덮치고 싶어 안달했다. 그들은 배교자의 계획을 알지 못한 채, 무력으로, 그리고 배에 타고 있는 무어인들을 죽여 자유를 얻으리라 여겼던 것이다. 그리하여 나와 동료들이 모습을 드러내자, 숨어 있던 자들이 모두 우리를 보고 나와 우리에게 합류했다. 이제 도시의 성문이 닫히는 시각이라, 성문 밖 온 공간에는 보이는 사람이 하나도 없었다. 우리가 한데 모이자 우리는 먼저 소라이다를 데리러 가는 것이 나을지, 아니면 배에서 노를 젓는 무어 노잡이들을 포로로 잡는 것이 나을지를 의논했다. 그러나 우리가 아직 결정을 못 내리고 있을 때 배교자가 다가와 무엇 때문에 지체하느냐고, 이제 때가 되었으며 무어인들이 모두 방심하고 대부분 잠들어 있다고 물었다. 우리가 망설이는 까닭을 일러 주자, 그는 먼저 배를 확보하는 것이 더 중요하다고, 그것은 더없이 손쉽게 아무런 위험 없이 해낼 수 있는 일이며 그러고 나서 소라이다를 데리러 가면 된다고 말했다. 우리는 모두 그의 말에 찬성했고, 그리하여 더 지체하지 않고 그의 인도를 받아 배로 향했다. 그가 먼저 배에 뛰어올라 단검을 빼 들고는 모리스코 말로 말했다. \"목숨이 아깝거든 누구도 여기서 꼼짝 마라.\" 이 말과 함께 거의 모든 기독교도가 배에 올랐고, 겁이 많은 무어인들은 자기네 선장이 이런 식으로 말하는 것을 듣고는 기가 꺾여, 누구 하나 무기를 드는 일도 없이(실은 무기랄 것도 거의 없었지만) 군소리 없이 기독교도들에게 결박되었다. 기독교도들은 그들을 재빨리 묶으며, 조금이라도 소리를 지르면 모조리 칼로 베어 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 이 일을 끝내고 우리 일행의 절반을 남겨 그들을 지키게 한 뒤, 나머지 우리는 다시 배교자를 길잡이로 삼아 하지 모라토의 정원으로 서둘러 갔다. 운 좋게도 문을 밀어 보니 마치 잠겨 있지 않았던 것처럼 쉽게 열렸다. 그리하여 우리는 아주 조용히, 소리 없이, 아무에게도 들키지 않고 집에 다다랐다. 아리따운 소라이다는 창가에서 우리를 지켜보고 있었고, 거기 사람들이 있는 것을 알아채자마자 낮은 목소리로 우리가 \"니사라니\"냐고 물었으니, 곧 우리가 기독교도냐고 묻는 셈이었다. 나는 그렇다고 대답하며 그녀에게 내려오라고 청했다. 그녀는 나를 알아보자마자 한순간도 지체하지 않고, 한마디 대답도 없이 곧장 내려와 문을 열고 우리 모두 앞에 모습을 드러냈는데, 어찌나 아름답고 어찌나 화려하게 차려입었는지 나로서는 묘사할 엄두조차 나지 않는다. 그녀를 본 순간 나는 그녀의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추었고, 배교자와 내 두 동료도 똑같이 했다. 그리고 사정을 전혀 모르는 나머지 사람들도 우리가 하는 것을 보고 그대로 따라 했으니, 그것은 마치 우리가 그녀에게 감사를 표하며 그녀를 우리 자유의 베푼 이로 알아보는 것처럼만 보였다. 배교자가 모리스코 말로 그녀의 아버지가 집에 있느냐고 물었다. 그녀는 계시며 잠들어 있다고 대답했다.\n\n\"그러면 그분을 깨워 우리와 함께 데려가야 하겠습니다.\" 배교자가 말했다. \"그리고 이 좋은 저택 안의 값진 것은 모조리 말입니다.\"\n\n\"안 됩니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제 아버지께는 무슨 일이 있어도 손을 대서는 안 돼요. 그리고 집 안에는 제가 가져갈 것 말고는 아무것도 없으나, 그것만으로도 여러분 모두를 넉넉히 부유하게 하고 만족시키기에 충분할 거예요. 잠시만 기다리시면 보게 될 거예요.\" 그렇게 말하고 그녀는 안으로 들어가며, 곧 돌아오겠으니 소리를 내지 말고 조용히 있으라고 우리에게 당부했다.\n\n나는 배교자에게 둘 사이에 무슨 말이 오갔는지 물었고, 그가 말해 주자, 나는 무슨 일이든 소라이다의 뜻에 따르지 않고서는 아무것도 해서는 안 된다고 단언했다. 이제 그녀가 황금 크라운이 가득 차서 들기조차 힘들 만큼 묵직한 작은 궤짝 하나를 들고 돌아왔다. 불행히도 이 일이 벌어지는 동안 그녀의 아버지가 잠에서 깨어 정원에서 나는 소리를 듣고 창가로 와서는, 거기 있는 자들이 모두 기독교도임을 단번에 알아채고 엄청나게 큰 소리를 지르며 아랍어로 외치기 시작했다. \"기독교도다, 기독교도! 도둑이다, 도둑이야!\" 이 외침에 우리는 모두 더없는 두려움과 당혹에 빠졌다. 그러나 우리가 처한 위험과, 들키기 전에 자기 목적을 이루는 것이 자신에게 얼마나 중요한지를 안 배교자는 더없이 재빨리 하지 모라토가 있는 곳으로 올라갔고, 그와 함께 우리 일행 몇이 따라 올라갔다. 그러나 나는 거의 기절한 채 내 품에 쓰러져 있던 소라이다를 차마 떠날 수 없었다. 간단히 말해, 위로 올라간 자들이 어찌나 신속하게 움직였던지 눈 깜짝할 사이에 하지 모라토를 데리고 내려왔는데, 그의 두 손은 묶이고 입에는 천이 동여매여 한마디도 내뱉지 못하게 되어 있었으며, 동시에 입을 열려 들면 목숨을 잃게 되리라는 경고도 받은 터였다. 그의 딸은 아버지를 보자 차마 보지 못해 두 눈을 가렸고, 그녀가 얼마나 기꺼이 우리 손에 몸을 맡겼는지 알 리 없는 아버지는 경악에 사로잡혔다. 그러나 이제 우리가 서둘러 움직이는 것이 무엇보다 긴요했으므로, 우리는 조심스럽고도 재빠르게 배로 돌아왔다. 거기서는 배에 남아 있던 자들이 우리에게 무슨 변고라도 생겼을까 염려하며 우리를 기다리고 있었다. 우리가 모두 배에 오른 것은 밤이 든 지 겨우 두 시간쯤 지나서였다. 거기서 소라이다 아버지의 손에서 밧줄을 풀고 입에서 천을 떼어 냈으나, 배교자는 한마디라도 내뱉으면 목숨을 빼앗겠다고 그에게 다시 한번 일렀다. 그는 거기 자기 딸이 있는 것을 보자 가엾게 한숨짓기 시작했고, 내가 그녀를 꼭 끌어안고 있으며 그녀가 저항하거나 하소연하거나 꺼리는 기색도 없이 가만히 안겨 있는 것을 알아챘을 때 더욱더 그러했다. 그럼에도 그는 배교자가 거듭 던진 위협을 그들이 실행에 옮길까 두려워 입을 다물고 있었다.\n\n이제 배에 오른 자신을 발견하고 우리가 막 노를 저으려는 것을 본 소라이다는, 거기 자기 아버지가 있고 다른 무어인들이 묶여 있는 것을 보고는, 배교자를 시켜 내게 부탁하기를, 무어인들을 풀어 주고 자기 아버지를 자유롭게 해 주는 호의를 베풀어 달라고 했다. 자기를 그토록 애틋이 사랑한 아버지가 자기 눈앞에서, 자기 탓에 포로로 끌려가는 것을 보느니 차라리 바다에 몸을 던져 죽는 편이 낫겠다는 것이었다. 배교자가 이를 내게 거듭 전했고, 나는 기꺼이 그리하겠다고 대답했다. 그러나 그는 그것이 현명하지 못하다고 했으니, 만일 그들을 거기 남겨 둔다면 그들이 곧 온 고장을 들쑤시고 도시를 소동에 빠뜨려, 빠른 순항선들을 추격에 내보내게 하고, 그리하여 우리가 바다에서든 뭍에서든 빠져나갈 길 없이 붙잡히게 될 것이기 때문이라고 했다. 그러니 할 수 있는 일이라고는 우리가 처음으로 닿는 기독교 땅에서 그들을 풀어 주는 것뿐이라고 했다. 이 점에 대해 우리는 모두 동의했다. 그리고 그녀가 바라는 일을 당장 할 수 없게 만든 이유와 함께 사정을 설명받은 소라이다도 마찬가지로 수긍했다. 그리하여 기쁜 침묵 속에서, 즐거운 민첩함으로 우리의 건장한 노잡이들이 저마다 노를 잡았고, 우리는 온 마음을 다해 하느님께 우리를 의탁하며 가장 가까운 기독교 땅인 마요르카섬을 향해 뱃길을 잡기 시작했다. 그러나 트라몬타나 바람이 조금 일고 바다가 다소 거칠어진 탓에 마요르카로 곧장 항로를 잡기란 불가능했고, 우리는 오랑 방향을 따라 연안을 항해할 수밖에 없었다. 알제에서 육십 마일도 채 떨어지지 않은 그 연안에 자리한 셰르셀 마을에서 우리가 눈에 띌까 큰 불안을 느끼면서 말이다. 게다가 우리는 그 항로에서, 흔히 테투안에서 물자를 싣고 오는 갤리엇 한 척과 마주칠까 두려웠다. 다만 우리 각자, 또 우리 모두가 한데 모여 자신했던 것은, 만일 우리가 순항선이 아닌 상선 갤리엇과 마주친다면 우리가 잃을 것이 없을 뿐 아니라, 오히려 우리의 항해를 더 안전하게 이룰 수 있는 배 한 척을 빼앗게 되리라는 것이었다. 항로를 따라가는 동안 소라이다는 아버지를 보지 않으려고 내 두 손 사이에 머리를 묻고 있었고, 나는 그녀가 우리를 도와 달라고 렐라 마리엔에게 기도하고 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n우리가 한 삼십 마일쯤 갔을 무렵 동이 트면서 우리가 뭍에서 총 세 방 쏠 거리쯤 떨어져 있음을 알게 되었는데, 뭍은 인적이 끊긴 듯했고 우리를 볼 사람도 없는 듯했다. 그래도 우리는 힘껏 노를 저어 바다 쪽으로 조금 나아갔으니, 이제 다소 잔잔해진 까닭이었다. 두 레과쯤 나아가고 나서, 우리가 무언가를 먹는 동안 패를 나누어 노를 젓자는 말이 나왔으니, 배에는 양식이 넉넉히 갖추어져 있었다. 그러나 노잡이들은 지금은 쉴 때가 아니라고 했다. 노를 젓지 않는 자들에게는 음식을 나누어 주되, 자기들은 무슨 일이 있어도 노를 놓지 않겠다는 것이었다. 그대로 했으나, 이제 거센 바람이 불기 시작해 우리는 노 젓기를 그만두고 곧장 돛을 올려 오랑을 향해 키를 잡을 수밖에 없었으니, 다른 항로를 잡기란 불가능했기 때문이다. 이 모든 것을 매우 신속히 해냈고, 돛을 올린 채 우리는 시속 팔 마일이 넘게 달렸으니, 떠돌이 약탈을 나선 어떤 배와 마주칠까 하는 두려움 외에는 아무 걱정이 없었다. 우리는 무어 노잡이들에게 음식을 좀 주었고, 배교자는 그들이 포로로 잡혀 있는 것이 아니라 첫 기회가 닿는 대로 풀어 주겠노라 일러 주며 그들을 달랬다.\n\n소라이다의 아버지에게도 같은 말을 했더니 그가 대답했다. \"기독교도여, 그대들의 너그러움과 훌륭한 처신에서 다른 것이라면 무엇이든 바라거나 그럴 법하다 여길 수 있겠으나, 그대들이 내게 자유를 주리라 여길 만큼 나를 그리 순진한 자로 보지는 마시오. 그대들은 한낱 그것을 이리도 너그러이 되돌려 주려고 그것을 내게서 빼앗는 위험에 스스로를 내맡겼을 리 없으니, 더구나 내가 누구인지, 또 그것을 되돌려 줌으로써 그대들이 받을 만한 금액이 얼마인지 알고 있으면서 말이오. 그러니 그대들이 그 액수를 말하기만 한다면, 나는 여기서 나 자신과 저기 있는 불행한 내 딸을 위해 그대들이 요구하는 모든 것을 내놓겠소. 아니면 저 아이 하나만을 위해서라도 그러겠소. 저 아이는 내 영혼의 가장 크고 가장 소중한 부분이니 말이오.\"\n\n이 말을 하면서 그는 어찌나 비통하게 울기 시작했던지 우리 모두를 가엾은 마음으로 채웠고, 소라이다로 하여금 그를 바라보지 않을 수 없게 만들었다. 그녀는 아버지가 우는 것을 보자 어찌나 마음이 움직였던지 내 발치에서 일어나 그에게 달려가 두 팔로 끌어안았고, 자기 얼굴을 그의 얼굴에 맞대니, 두 사람은 그토록 격하게 울음을 터뜨려 우리 가운데 여럿도 그들과 함께 울지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 그녀의 아버지는 그녀가 성장(盛裝)을 하고 온갖 보석을 몸에 두른 것을 보자 자기네 말로 그녀에게 말했다. \"이게 무슨 일이냐, 내 딸아? 우리가 빠져든 이 끔찍한 불행이 닥치기 전인 어젯밤만 해도 나는 너의 평상복, 집에서 입는 옷차림을 보았다. 그런데 지금, 옷을 차려입을 겨를도 없었고, 내가 너를 단장하고 꾸밀 구실이 될 만한 어떤 기쁜 소식을 가져다준 것도 아닌데, 운수가 우리에게 가장 후했던 시절 내가 너에게 줄 수 있었을 가장 좋은 차림으로 단장한 너를 보는구나. 내게 이것을 답해 보아라. 이는 이 불행 자체보다도 내게 더 큰 근심과 놀라움을 안기는 일이니라.\"\n\n배교자가 무어인이 자기 딸에게 한 말을 우리에게 통역해 주었다. 그러나 그녀는 그에게 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 한데 그가 배 한구석에서 그녀가 보석을 넣어 두던 그 작은 궤짝을 보았는데, 그것을 알제에 두고 정원으로 가져오지 않았음을 잘 알고 있던 터라, 그는 더욱더 놀라 그 궤짝이 어떻게 우리 손에 들어왔으며 그 안에 무엇이 들었느냐고 그녀에게 물었다. 이에 소라이다가 대답하기를 기다리지 않고 배교자가 대답했다. \"그대의 딸 소라이다에게 그리 많은 것을 묻느라 애쓰지 마시오, 나리. 내가 그대에게 줄 한 가지 대답이 그 모든 것을 갈음할 테니 말이오. 그대가 알아야 할 것은, 저 아이가 기독교도이며, 우리의 사슬을 끊어 준 줄칼이요 포로 신세에서 우리를 건져 낸 구원자가 바로 저 아이라는 사실이오. 저 아이는 제 발로 여기 왔으며, 내 짐작에는, 어둠에서 빛으로, 죽음에서 생명으로, 고통에서 영광으로 벗어난 자처럼 자신이 이 처지에 놓인 것을 기뻐하고 있소.\"\n\n\"딸아, 저자가 하는 말이 정녕 사실이냐?\" 무어인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"사실이에요.\" 소라이다가 대답했다.\n\n\"네가 정녕 기독교도라는 것이,\" 노인이 말했다. \"그리고 네가 네 아비를 그 원수들의 손에 넘겨주었다는 것이?\"\n\n이에 소라이다가 대답했다. \"제가 기독교도인 것은 맞아요. 그러나 아버지를 이 처지에 놓이게 한 것은 제가 아니에요. 저는 결코 아버지를 떠나거나 아버지께 해를 끼치고 싶었던 게 아니라, 다만 제 자신에게 좋은 일을 하고 싶었을 뿐이니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 네가 네 자신에게 무슨 좋은 일을 했단 말이냐, 딸아?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그건,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"렐라 마리엔께 여쭈어 보세요. 저보다 그분이 더 잘 일러 주실 거예요.\"\n\n무어인은 이 말을 듣기가 무섭게 놀랍도록 잽싸게 머리부터 바다에 몸을 던졌고, 그가 입은 길고 풍성한 옷이 잠시 그를 물 위에 떠받쳐 주지 않았더라면 틀림없이 익사했을 것이다. 소라이다가 우리에게 그를 구해 달라고 큰 소리로 외쳤고, 우리는 모두 서둘러 도우러 가서 그의 옷자락을 붙잡아 반쯤 익사해 정신을 잃은 그를 끌어 올렸다. 이에 소라이다는 어찌나 비통해하던지 그가 이미 죽기라도 한 양 가엾고 애절하게 그를 두고 울었다. 우리는 그를 엎어 놓았고 그는 엄청난 양의 물을 토해 냈으며, 두 시간이 지나서야 정신을 차렸다. 그러는 사이 바람이 바뀌어 우리는 뭍을 향해 뱃머리를 돌리고 노를 부지런히 저어 해안으로 떠밀리지 않도록 해야 했다. 그러나 다행히도 우리는 작은 곶이나 갑(岬)의 한쪽에 자리한 후미에 다다랐는데, 무어인들은 그곳을 \"카바 루미아\"의 곶이라 부르니, 우리말로 \"사악한 기독교도 여인\"이라는 뜻이다. 그들 사이에는 스페인이 멸망하게 된 원인인 라 카바가 그 자리에 묻혀 있다는 전설이 있기 때문이다. 그들 말로 \"카바\"는 \"사악한 여인\"을, \"루미아\"는 \"기독교도\"를 뜻한다. 게다가 그들은 어쩔 수 없이 그곳에 닻을 내리는 것을 불길하게 여기며, 그렇지 않고서는 결코 거기 닻을 내리지 않는다. 그러나 우리에게 그곳은 사악한 여인의 안식처가 아니라 우리를 구해 줄 안전한 피난처였으니, 이제 바다가 그만큼 험해진 까닭이었다. 우리는 뭍에 망보는 자를 세워 두고 결코 노를 손에서 놓지 않았으며, 배교자가 마련해 둔 양식을 먹으면서, 이토록 순조롭게 시작한 일에 행복한 결말을 줄 수 있도록 우리를 도와 지켜 주십사 하느님과 성모님께 온 마음으로 빌었다. 소라이다의 간청에 따라, 아직 묶여 있던 그녀의 아버지와 다른 무어인들을 뭍에 내려 주라는 명이 내려졌으니, 그녀는 자기 아버지가 결박당하고 동포들이 자기 눈앞에서 포로가 되어 있는 것을 차마 견딜 수도, 그 여린 마음으로 두고 볼 수도 없었기 때문이다. 우리는 떠날 순간에 그리하겠노라 그녀에게 약속했으니, 그곳은 사람이 살지 않아 거기서 그들을 풀어 주어도 아무런 위험이 없었기 때문이다.\n\n우리의 기도가 하늘에 닿지 않을 만큼 헛되지는 않았던지, 얼마 뒤 바람이 우리에게 유리하게 바뀌어 바다를 잔잔하게 만들었고, 우리더러 다시 한번 기운차게 항해를 이어 가라고 부추겼다. 이를 보고 우리는 무어인들의 결박을 풀어 한 사람씩 뭍에 내려 주었으니, 그들은 경악으로 가득 찼다. 그러나 우리가 이제 완전히 정신을 차린 소라이다의 아버지를 뭍에 내리려 하자 그가 말했다.\n\n\"기독교도들이여, 이 사악한 계집이 그대들이 내게 자유를 주는 것을 기뻐하는 까닭이 무엇이라 생각하오? 저 아이가 내게 품은 정 때문이라 여기오? 천만에, 결코 아니오. 그저 내가 있으면 저 아이의 비열한 음모를 실행하는 데 방해가 되기 때문일 뿐이오. 또 그대들 종교가 우리 것보다 낫다고 믿어서 저 아이가 제 신앙을 바꾸었다고 여기지 마시오. 그저 그대들 나라에서 우리 나라에서보다 음란함이 더 거리낌 없이 행해진다는 것을 저 아이가 알기 때문일 뿐이오.\" 그러고는 내가 또 다른 기독교도 한 사람과 함께, 그가 무슨 미친 짓이라도 할까 봐 그의 두 팔을 단단히 붙든 가운데, 그가 그녀에게 돌아서서 말했다. \"치욕스러운 계집아, 길 잃은 처녀야, 너는 눈멀고 미쳐서 우리의 천적인 이 개들의 손에 끌려 어디로 가려는 것이냐? 너를 낳은 시각이 저주받을지어다! 너를 호사와 응석 속에 길러 낸 일이 저주받을지어다!\"\n\n그러나 그가 좀처럼 멈출 것 같지 않은 것을 보고 나는 서둘러 그를 뭍에 내려놓았고, 거기서도 그는 큰 소리로 저주와 한탄을 이어 갔다. 마호메트더러 우리를 멸하고, 혼란에 빠뜨리고, 끝장내 달라고 알라께 빌라고 부르짖으면서 말이다. 그리고 돛을 올린 탓에 그가 하는 말이 더는 들리지 않게 되었을 때도, 우리는 그가 무엇을 하는지는 볼 수 있었다. 그가 어떻게 제 수염을 쥐어뜯고 머리카락을 잡아 뜯으며 땅바닥에 몸부림치며 누웠는지를 말이다. 그러나 한번은 그가 어찌나 높이 목청을 돋우었던지 우리가 그의 말을 들을 수 있었다. \"돌아오너라, 사랑하는 딸아, 뭍으로 돌아오너라. 내 너의 모든 것을 용서하마. 저 사내들에게 돈은 주어 버려라, 이제 그건 저들의 것이니. 그리고 돌아와 슬픔에 잠긴 네 아비를 위로해 다오. 네가 떠난다면 이 메마른 해변에서 목숨을 내놓고 말 네 아비를 말이다.\"\n\n이 모든 것을 소라이다는 들었으니, 슬픔과 눈물로 들었다. 그리고 그녀가 대답으로 할 수 있었던 말이라고는 이것뿐이었다. \"알라께서 부디, 저를 기독교도가 되게 하신 렐라 마리엔으로 하여금 아버지의 슬픔에 위안을 주시게 하소서. 알라께서는 제가 한 것 말고는 달리 어쩔 수 없었음을, 그리고 이 기독교도들이 제 뜻에 빚진 것이 아무것도 없음을 아십니다. 제가 그들을 따라가지 않고 집에 남기를 바랐다 한들, 그것은 제게 불가능했을 테니까요. 제 영혼이 어찌나 간절히 저를 이 목적의 성취로 떠밀었던지요. 사랑하는 아버지께는 그것이 사악해 보이는 만큼이나 제게는 그것이 의로운 것으로 느껴지니까요.\"\n\n그러나 그녀가 이 말을 할 때 아버지는 그녀의 말을 들을 수도 없었고 우리도 그를 볼 수 없었다. 그리하여 내가 소라이다를 달래는 동안 우리는 우리의 항해에 마음을 쏟았고, 알맞은 방향에서 부는 바람이 어찌나 우리를 도왔던지 우리는 이튿날 동틀 무렵이면 틀림없이 스페인 연안에 다다라 있으리라 믿어 의심치 않았다. 그러나 좋은 일이란 좀처럼, 아니 결코 순수하고 섞임 없이 오는 법이 없고, 거기에 충격을 주는 어떤 어지러운 재앙이 따르거나 뒤이어 오기 마련이라, 우리의 운명, 아니 어쩌면 무어인이 제 딸에게 퍼부은 저주가(어떤 아비에게서 나온 것이든 이런 저주는 늘 두려워할 만하니) 일을 이렇게 만들었다. 우리가 이제 바다 한복판에 있고 밤이 세 시간쯤 지났을 무렵, 돛을 모두 올리고 노는 묶어 둔 채(순풍이 노를 쓸 수고를 덜어 준 까닭이었다) 달리고 있을 때, 휘영청 밝게 빛나는 달빛으로 우리는 돛을 모두 펼친 사각돛배 한 척이 우리 가까이에서 바람을 거슬러 오르며 우리 항로를 가로질러 다가오는 것을 보았다. 어찌나 가까웠던지 우리는 그 배와 충돌하지 않으려 돛을 내려야 했고, 그들 또한 우리를 지나가게 하려고 키를 한껏 위로 꺾었다. 그들은 배 옆으로 다가와 우리가 누구이며 어디로 향하고 어디서 왔는지를 물었으나, 그것을 프랑스어로 물었으므로 우리 배교자가 말했다. \"아무도 대답하지 마시오. 이들은 틀림없이 오는 자마다 약탈하는 프랑스 해적들이오.\"\n\n이 경고에 따라 아무도 한마디 대답하지 않았으나, 우리가 조금 앞서 나가 그 배가 이제 바람 아래쪽에 놓이게 되었을 때, 갑자기 그들이 대포 두 방을 쏘았는데 둘 다 사슬탄을 장전한 듯했다. 한 방으로는 우리 돛대를 반으로 잘라 돛대와 돛을 함께 바다로 떨어뜨렸고, 동시에 발사된 다른 한 방은 우리 배 한가운데에 포탄을 박아 배를 완전히 부수었으나 더 이상의 손상은 입히지 않았다. 그러나 우리는 배가 가라앉는 것을 알고는 도움을 청해 소리치기 시작했고, 우리가 물에 잠겨 가니 배에 탄 자들에게 우리를 건져 달라고 외쳤다. 그러자 그들은 배를 세우고 작은 거룻배인지 보트를 내렸으며, 화승총으로 단단히 무장하고 화승에 불을 붙인 프랑스인 열두 명쯤이 거기 올라타 우리 곁으로 다가왔다. 그리고 우리 수가 얼마 안 되고 우리 배가 가라앉고 있는 것을 보고는, 이 일이 우리가 자기들에게 대답하지 않은 무례 탓에 닥친 것이라 말하며 우리를 거두어 주었다. 우리 배교자는 소라이다의 재물이 담긴 궤짝을 들어, 자기가 무엇을 하는지 아무도 알아채지 못하게 바다에 떨어뜨렸다. 요컨대 우리는 프랑스인들과 함께 배에 올랐는데, 그들은 우리에 관해 알고 싶은 모든 것을 알아내고 나자, 마치 우리의 가장 지독한 원수이기라도 한 양 우리가 가진 모든 것을 털어 갔으며, 소라이다에게서는 발에 차고 있던 발찌마저 빼앗았다. 그러나 그들이 그녀에게 끼친 괴로움도, 그들이 그녀의 값지고 귀한 보석을 약탈한 데서 더 나아가 그녀가 모든 것보다 더 소중히 여기는 가장 귀한 보석마저 약탈하지나 않을까 하는 내 두려움만큼 나를 괴롭히지는 못했다. 그러나 그자들의 욕망은 돈을 넘어서지 않으며, 그 점에 있어서 그들의 탐욕은 채울 길이 없으니, 이때에도 그것이 어찌나 극에 달했던지, 그들에게 조금이라도 값나가는 것이었다면 우리가 포로로 입고 있던 옷가지마저 빼앗아 갔을 것이다. 그들 가운데 더러는 우리를 모두 돛에 싸서 바다에 던져 버리자고 권했으니, 그들의 속셈은 스페인의 어느 항구들에서 자기들을 브르타뉴 사람으로 내세우며 거래를 하려는 것이었는데, 만일 우리를 산 채로 데려간다면 약탈이 발각되는 즉시 벌을 받게 되리라는 것이었다. 그러나 선장은(내 사랑하는 소라이다를 약탈한 바로 그자였다) 자기가 얻은 노획물에 만족한다고, 자기는 스페인의 어떤 항구에도 들르지 않고 밤에, 혹은 할 수 있는 한 어떻게든 지브롤터 해협을 지나, 자기가 출항한 라로셸로 향하겠다고 말했다. 그리하여 그들은 공동의 합의로, 자기들 배에 딸린 거룻배와 우리에게 남은 짧은 항해에 필요한 모든 것을 우리에게 주기로 했다. 그들은 스페인 연안이 보이게 된 이튿날 그리해 주었고, 그 연안과 우리가 느낀 기쁨에 우리의 모든 고초와 비참함은 마치 우리가 단 한 번도 그것을 견딘 적 없었던 양 완전히 잊혀졌으니, 잃었던 자유를 되찾는다는 것은 그토록 큰 기쁨이다.\n\n그들이 우리를 보트에 태운 것은 정오 무렵이었을 것이며, 물 두 통과 비스킷 약간을 주었다. 그리고 선장은 무슨 측은한 마음이 일었던지, 아리따운 소라이다가 막 배에 오르려 할 때 그녀에게 황금 크라운 마흔 닢쯤을 주었고, 부하들이 그녀가 지금 입고 있는 바로 그 옷가지를 빼앗는 것을 허락하지 않았다. 우리는 그들이 우리에게 베푼 친절에 고마움을 표하며, 분개하기보다 감사하는 모습을 보이며 보트에 올랐다. 그들은 해협을 향해 키를 잡고 바다로 나아갔다. 우리는 눈앞에 둔 뭍 말고는 어떤 나침반도 보지 않은 채, 어찌나 힘차게 노를 저었던지 해 질 무렵에는 밤이 깊기 전에 쉬이 상륙할 수 있으리라 여길 만큼 가까이 다가가 있었다. 그러나 그날 밤은 달이 뜨지 않았고 하늘은 구름에 덮였으며, 우리가 대체 어디쯤에 있는지 알 수 없었던지라, 우리 가운데 여럿이 권한 대로 해안으로 향하는 것은 현명한 일로 여겨지지 않았다. 그들은 설령 바위투성이에 인가에서 멀리 떨어진 곳일지라도 배를 뭍에 올려야 한다고 했으니, 이렇게 하면 우리가 으레 느끼던 두려움, 곧 어두워질 무렵 바르바리아를 떠나 동틀 녘이면 스페인 연안에 와 있는 테투안 해적들의 떠도는 배들에 대한 두려움에서 벗어날 수 있다는 것이었다. 그자들은 그곳에서 흔히 노획물을 얻고는 제집에서 잠을 자러 돌아간다. 그러나 엇갈린 권고들 가운데 채택된 것은, 우리가 차츰차츰 다가가, 바다가 잔잔해 허락한다면 가능한 곳에 상륙하자는 것이었다. 그대로 했고, 자정이 조금 못 되어 우리는 어느 거대하고 우뚝한 산기슭에 다가갔는데, 바다에 그리 바싹 붙어 있지는 않아 상륙하기에 알맞은 좁은 자리를 남겨 두고 있었다. 우리는 보트를 모래밭에 올리고 모두 뛰어내려 땅에 입을 맞추었으며, 기쁨에 찬 흡족함의 눈물을 흘리며 우리 항해 내내 우리에게 베푸신 비할 데 없는 자비를 두고 우리 주 하느님께 감사를 드렸다. 우리는 보트에서 거기 실린 양식을 꺼내고 보트를 뭍으로 끌어 올린 다음, 산을 따라 한참을 올라갔으니, 거기서조차 마음을 놓을 수도, 이제 우리 발밑에 있는 것이 기독교 땅임을 스스로 믿을 수도 없었기 때문이다.\n\n동이 텄으니, 내 생각에는 우리가 바랐을 법한 것보다 더 더디게 텄다. 우리는 정상에서 어떤 인가나 양치기들의 오두막이라도 보이는지 알아보려고 끝까지 올라갔으나, 아무리 눈을 부릅떠도 살림집도, 사람도, 오솔길도, 큰길도 보이지 않았다. 그래도 우리는 더 멀리 나아가기로 했으니, 머지않아 우리가 어디 있는지 일러 줄 누군가를 틀림없이 만나게 되리라 여긴 까닭이었다. 그러나 무엇보다 나를 가장 괴롭힌 것은 소라이다가 그 험한 땅을 걸어서 가는 것을 보는 일이었다. 한번은 내가 그녀를 어깨에 메었으나, 그녀는 쉼으로 쉰 것보다 내 고단함에 더 고단해했으므로, 그녀는 다시는 내게 그 수고를 하게 하려 들지 않았고, 내가 손을 잡고 이끄는 동안 매우 참을성 있고 명랑하게 걸어갔다. 우리가 사 분의 일 레과쯤 채 안 되게 갔을 때 작은 방울 소리가 우리 귀에 들려왔으니, 가까운 데 가축 떼가 있다는 분명한 증거였다. 그래서 보이는 데 무엇이 있나 주의 깊게 둘러보니, 한 젊은 양치기가 코르크나무 아래에서 태평스럽고 아무 의심 없이 칼로 막대기를 다듬고 있는 것이 보였다. 우리가 그를 부르자, 그는 고개를 들고 날렵하게 벌떡 일어났다. 나중에 알게 된 바로는, 그의 눈에 처음 들어온 것이 배교자와 소라이다였고, 그들이 무어인 차림을 한 것을 보고는 바르바리아의 무어인들이 죄다 자기에게 들이닥치는 줄로 여겼던 것이다. 그리하여 그는 놀라운 빠르기로 앞에 있는 덤불 속으로 뛰어들며 엄청난 비명을 지르기 시작했다. \"무어인이다, 무어인이 상륙했다! 무기를 들어라, 무기를!\" 이 외침에 우리는 모두 어찌할 바를 몰라 당혹에 빠졌으나, 양치기의 고함이 온 고장을 들쑤셔 말을 탄 연안 경비대가 무슨 일인지 보러 곧장 들이닥치리라 생각하고는, 배교자가 튀르크 옷을 벗고 우리 일행 가운데 한 사람이 곧 그에게 내준 포로의 윗옷이나 외투를 입어야 한다고 의견을 모았다. 비록 그 옷을 내준 자 자신은 셔츠 바람이 되고 말았지만 말이다. 그리하여 우리는 하느님께 우리를 의탁하며, 양치기가 간 것과 같은 길을 따라가면서, 연안 경비대가 금방이라도 우리를 덮치리라 시시각각 예상했다. 우리의 예상은 빗나가지 않았으니, 두 시간이 채 지나기도 전에 우리가 덤불에서 빈 땅으로 나섰을 때 쉰 명쯤 되는 기마병이 가벼운 구보로 우리를 향해 빠르게 다가오는 것을 보았던 것이다. 그들을 보자마자 우리는 가만히 서서 그들을 기다렸다. 그러나 그들은 가까이 다가와, 자기들이 찾던 무어인 대신 가련한 기독교도 한 무리를 보고는 어리둥절해했고, 그중 하나가 양치기로 하여금 무기를 들라 외치게 한 원인이 우리냐고 물었다. 내가 \"그렇소\"라고 하며 무슨 일이 있었는지, 또 우리가 어디서 왔으며 누구인지를 그에게 설명하려는데, 우리 일행 가운데 한 기독교도가 우리에게 그 물음을 던진 기마병을 알아보고는, 내가 한마디 더 하기도 전에 외쳤다.\n\n\"하느님께 감사드립니다, 여러분, 우리를 이리도 좋은 곳으로 데려다주셔서요. 제가 틀리지 않았다면, 우리가 서 있는 이 땅은 벨레스말라가의 땅입니다. 하기야 그 숱한 포로살이의 세월이 제 기억을 흐려 놓지 않았다면 말이지요. 우리가 누구인지 물으시는 나리, 당신은 바로 제 외삼촌 페드로 데 부스타만테이십니다.\"\n\n기독교도 포로가 이 말을 채 입에 올리기 무섭게, 그 기마병은 말에서 몸을 던져 내려 젊은이를 끌어안으러 달려가며 외쳤다.\n\n\"내 영혼이요 생명인 조카야! 이제야 너를 알아보겠구나. 오랫동안 나는 너를 죽은 줄 알고 애통해했단다, 나도, 네 어미인 내 누이도, 아직 살아 있는 네 모든 일가붙이도 말이다. 하느님께서 그들을 살려 두신 것은 너를 보는 기쁨을 누리게 하시려는 뜻이었구나. 우리는 네가 알제에 있다는 것을 오래전부터 알고 있었다. 그리고 네 옷차림과 이 일행 모두의 옷차림을 보니, 너희가 기적처럼 자유를 되찾았으리라 짐작이 가는구나.\"\n\n\"사실입니다.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. \"이따가 모두 말씀드리지요.\"\n\n기마병들이 우리가 기독교도 포로들임을 알아채자마자, 그들은 말에서 내려 저마다 자기 말을 내주며 우리를 한 레과 반쯤 떨어진 벨레스말라가 시로 데려다주려 했다. 그중 몇은 우리가 보트를 둔 곳을 일러 주자 그것을 시로 가져오러 갔고, 다른 자들은 우리를 자기들 뒤에 태웠으며, 소라이다는 젊은이의 외삼촌의 말에 태워졌다. 온 마을 사람들이 우리를 맞으러 나왔으니, 이때쯤에는 앞서 달려간 자에게서 우리의 도착 소식을 들었던 것이다. 그들은 풀려난 포로들이나 포로가 된 무어인들을 보고 놀라지는 않았으니, 그 연안 사람들은 이쪽도 저쪽도 보는 데 익숙했기 때문이다. 그러나 그들은 소라이다의 아름다움에 놀랐는데, 그 아름다움은 마침 그때 여행의 노고와 더불어 자신이 기독교 땅에 와 있음을, 또 길을 잃을 모든 두려움에서 벗어났음을 알게 된 기쁨으로 한껏 돋보였던 것이다. 이것이 그녀의 얼굴에 그토록 환한 빛을 가져다주었으니, 그녀를 향한 내 애정이 나를 속이는 것이 아니라면, 나는 감히 세상에 그녀보다 더 아름다운 존재는 없다고, 적어도 내가 본 적이 있는 한에서는 그렇다고 말하리라. 우리는 우리가 받은 자비를 두고 하느님께 감사를 드리러 곧장 성당으로 갔는데, 소라이다는 성당에 들어서자 거기 렐라 마리엔 같은 얼굴들이 있다고 말했다. 우리는 그것이 그녀의 성상(聖像)이라고 일러 주었고, 배교자는 할 수 있는 한, 그녀가 그것들 하나하나를 마치 자기에게 말을 건넸던 바로 그 렐라 마리엔처럼 경배할 수 있도록, 그것들이 무엇을 뜻하는지 설명해 주었다. 그녀는 빼어난 지력과 빠르고 또렷한 직관을 지녔던지라, 그것들에 관해 그가 한 말을 단번에 모두 알아들었다. 거기서 사람들은 우리를 데려가 마을의 여러 집에 나누어 머물게 했으나, 배교자와 소라이다와 나로 말하자면, 우리와 함께 온 그 기독교도가 우리를 자기 부모의 집으로 데려갔으니, 그들은 운수의 선물을 어지간히 누리는 처지였고 우리를 제 친자식 대하듯 친절히 대접해 주었다.\n\n우리는 벨레스에서 엿새를 머물렀고, 그 끝에 배교자는 자기가 해야 할 모든 일을 알아 둔 뒤, 성스러운 종교 재판소를 통해 자신을 거룩한 교회의 품으로 되돌리려고 그라나다 시로 떠났다. 풀려난 다른 포로들도 저마다 자기에게 가장 좋아 보이는 길로 떠나갔고, 소라이다와 나만 남았는데, 가진 것이라고는 그 프랑스인의 호의가 소라이다에게 베푼 크라운뿐이었으니, 나는 그것으로 그녀가 타고 다니는 짐승을 샀다. 그리고 나는 지금으로서는 그녀를 남편으로서가 아니라 아버지요 종자(從者)로서 모시면서, 우리는 이제 내 아버지가 살아 계신지, 또는 내 형제들 가운데 누가 나보다 나은 운을 누렸는지를 알아보러 가는 길이다. 비록 하늘이 나를 소라이다의 길동무로 삼아 주셨으니, 아무리 행복한 다른 운명이 내게 주어진다 한들 이보다 더 갖고 싶은 것은 없으리라 여기지만 말이다. 그녀가 가난이 안기는 고초를 견뎌 내는 그 인내심과, 기독교도가 되고자 보이는 그 간절함은 나를 경탄으로 가득 채우고, 평생 그녀를 섬기도록 나를 묶어 놓을 만한 것이다. 비록 내가 그녀의 것이요 그녀가 내 것임을 보는 데서 느끼는 행복은, 내 고향에 그녀를 깃들게 할 한 귀퉁이라도 찾을 수 있을지, 또는 세월과 죽음이 내 아버지와 형제들의 살림과 목숨에 어떤 변화를 일으켜, 그들이 살아 있지 않다면 나를 알아볼 이를 좀처럼 찾지 못하게 되지나 않았을지 알 수 없어 흐트러지고 망쳐지기는 하지만 말이다.\n\n여러분, 제 이야기는 이제 더 들려드릴 것이 없습니다. 그것이 흥미로운 이야기인지 진기한 이야기인지는 여러분의 더 나은 판단에 맡기겠습니다. 다만 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 것은, 저로서는 기꺼이 더 짧게 들려드리고 싶었다는 것입니다. 비록 여러분을 지루하게 할까 하는 두려움 탓에 한 가지 이상의 정황을 빼놓고 말하기는 했지만 말입니다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLII.\n\n포로가 이 말과 함께 입을 다물자, 돈 페르난도가 그에게 말했다. \"정말이지, 대위님, 이 보기 드문 모험을 들려주신 방식이야말로 그 사안의 새로움과 기이함에 꼭 어울리는 것이었습니다. 그 이야기는 처음부터 끝까지 신기하고 보기 드물며, 듣는 이들을 경이와 놀라움으로 가득 채우는 사건들로 넘쳐납니다. 그리고 우리가 그것을 듣는 데서 얻은 즐거움이 어찌나 큰지, 내일까지 같은 이야기에 매달려 있게 된다 해도 다시 처음부터 시작해 주신다면 기쁘기 그지없겠습니다.\" 그가 이렇게 말하는 동안 카르데니오와 나머지 사람들도 자기들의 힘이 닿는 한 어떤 식으로든 그를 돕겠노라 나섰는데, 그 말과 표현이 어찌나 다정하고 진실되던지 대위는 그들의 호의에 크게 감사한 마음이 들었다. 특히 돈 페르난도는, 만약 그가 자신과 함께 돌아간다면 형인 후작을 소라이다의 세례 대부로 모시게 해 주겠으며, 자신으로서는 그가 마땅히 누려야 할 명예와 안락을 갖추어 고향에 모습을 드러낼 수 있도록 방편을 마련해 주겠노라 제안했다. 이 모든 것에 대해 포로는 매우 정중히 감사의 뜻을 표했으나, 그들의 너그러운 제안 가운데 어느 것도 받아들이려 하지 않았다.\n\n이러는 사이 밤이 깊어졌고, 어둠이 내려앉을 무렵 마차 한 대가 말 탄 사람 몇을 거느리고 객줏집으로 다가와 묵을 곳을 청했다. 이에 안주인은 객줏집 전체에 손바닥 한 뼘만 한 빈자리도 없다고 답했다.\n\n\"그래도 어쨌든,\" 말을 타고 들어선 사람들 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"여기 판관 나리를 모실 방은 마련되어야 하오.\"\n\n이 호칭에 안주인은 흠칫 놀라며 말했다. \"나리, 사실 침대가 하나도 없습니다요. 하지만 판관 나리께서 분명 그러하시듯 침대를 지니고 다니신다면, 어서 드시지요. 저와 제 남편이 나리께 자리를 내어 드리고자 저희 방을 비우겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"좋소, 그리합시다.\" 종자가 말했다. 그러나 그러는 사이 한 남자가 마차에서 내렸는데, 그 차림새만 보아도 그가 어떤 직책과 자리에 있는 사람인지 한눈에 알 수 있었으니, 그가 걸친 주름 소맷자락이 달린 긴 법복이 그가 종자의 말대로 항소 판관임을 드러내고 있었기 때문이다. 그는 여행복 차림에 열여섯 살쯤 되어 보이는 한 어린 처녀를 손으로 이끌고 있었는데, 그 처녀가 어찌나 기품 있고 어찌나 아름다우며 어찌나 우아하던지 그녀가 모습을 드러내자 모두가 감탄으로 가득 찼고, 만일 그 객줏집에 있던 도로테아와 루신다와 소라이다를 보지 못했더라면 이 처녀의 아름다움 같은 것은 좀처럼 찾아보기 어려우리라 여겼을 것이다. 돈키호테는 판관이 그 젊은 처녀와 함께 들어설 때 그 자리에 있었는데, 그를 보자마자 이렇게 말했다. \"나리께서는 안심하시고 이 성으로 드셔서 편히 쉬시지요. 비록 묵을 곳이 비좁고 누추하나, 무예와 학문을 위해 자리를 내어 주지 못할 만큼 옹색하거나 불편한 거처란 없는 법이니 말입니다. 더구나 무예와 학문이 아름다움을 길잡이요 인도자로 삼고 있다면야 더욱 그러하니, 나리께서 대변하시는 학문이 이 아리따운 처녀를 인도자로 두고 있은즉, 그녀에게는 성들이 마땅히 활짝 문을 열고 스스로를 내어 줄 뿐 아니라, 바위들이 갈라져 쪼개지고 산들이 둘로 나뉘어 몸을 숙여 그녀를 맞아들여야 할 것입니다. 드시지요, 나리, 이 낙원으로 드시지요. 여기서 나리께서는 나리께서 함께 가져오신 하늘을 수행할 별들과 태양들을 보시게 될 것이며, 여기서 나리께서는 그 지극한 탁월함에 이른 무예와 그 최고의 완성에 다다른 아름다움을 보시게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n판관은 돈키호테의 말에 깜짝 놀라, 그의 말 못지않게 그의 모습에도 적잖이 놀라며 그를 매우 찬찬히 뜯어보았다. 그리고 미처 그에게 답할 말을 찾기도 전에 또 한 번 놀랄 일이 생겼으니, 자기 맞은편에 루신다와 도로테아와 소라이다가 있는 것을 본 것이다. 그들은 새 손님들과 어린 처녀의 아름다움에 관한 소식을 듣고 그녀를 보고 또 맞이하려고 나와 있었던 것이다. 그러나 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오와 신부는 그를 보다 알아들을 만하고 세련된 투로 맞이했다. 요컨대 판관은 자기가 본 것에도 들은 것에도 어리둥절해하며 안으로 들어섰고, 객줏집의 아리따운 부인들은 그 아리따운 아가씨를 따뜻이 맞아 주었다. 대체로 그는 거기 있는 이들이 모두 신분 있는 사람들임을 알아챌 수 있었으나, 돈키호테의 모습과 얼굴과 거동만큼은 도무지 종잡을 수가 없었다. 모든 인사가 오가고 객줏집의 잠자리에 관한 의논이 끝나자, 앞서 정해진 대로 모든 여인은 이미 언급된 그 다락방으로 물러가고 남자들은 마치 그들을 지키기라도 하듯 바깥에 머물기로 결정되었다. 그리하여 판관은 자기 딸을 — 그 아가씨가 바로 그의 딸이었던 것이다 — 기꺼이 부인들과 함께 가도록 허락했고, 딸도 매우 흔쾌히 그리했다. 그리고 주인의 좁은 침대 일부와 판관이 가져온 것의 절반으로 그들은 예상했던 것보다 더 편안한 잠자리를 마련했다.\n\n포로는 판관을 보는 순간 가슴이 뛰며 어쩐지 이 사람이 자기 형이라는 느낌이 들었던 터라, 그를 따라온 하인들 가운데 하나에게 그의 이름이 무엇이며 어느 고장 출신인지 아느냐고 물었다. 하인은 그가 후안 페레스 데 비에드마 학사라 불리며, 레온 산지의 어느 마을 출신이라는 말을 들은 적이 있다고 답했다. 이 말과 그 자신이 직접 본 것으로 미루어, 그는 이 사람이 아버지의 권유로 학문의 길을 택한 자기 형임을 확신하게 되었다. 그리하여 흥분과 기쁨에 차서 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오와 신부를 한쪽으로 불러 사정이 어떠한지를 말하고, 그 판관이 자기 형이라고 단언했다. 하인은 또한 그가 지금 멕시코 대법원 판관으로 임명되어 인도 제도로 가는 길이라는 것과, 그 어린 처녀가 그의 딸인데 그 어머니는 딸을 낳다가 세상을 떠났으며, 딸과 함께 남겨진 지참금 덕분에 그가 매우 부유하다는 것도 알려 주었다. 그는 자기를 어떻게 알려야 할지, 혹은 자기를 알린 뒤에 그토록 가난한 자기 모습을 보고 형이 자기를 부끄러워할지 아니면 따뜻한 마음으로 맞아 줄지를 미리 알아내려면 어떤 방법을 써야 할지에 관해 그들의 조언을 구했다.\n\n\"그건 제가 알아내도록 맡겨 주십시오.\" 신부가 말했다. \"하기야, 대위님, 형님께서 따뜻이 맞아 주지 않으시리라 짐작할 이유라곤 없습니다. 형님의 거동에 드러난 그 인품과 지혜로 보건대 그분이 거만하거나 무정하게 굴거나, 운명의 부침을 그 마땅한 값으로 헤아릴 줄 모르실 분 같지는 않으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그래도,\" 대위가 말했다. \"제 신분을 불쑥 드러내기보다는 어떤 에둘러 가는 방식으로 하고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"이미 말씀드렸지 않습니까.\" 신부가 말했다. \"우리 모두가 만족할 만한 방식으로 제가 처리하겠습니다.\"\n\n이러는 사이 저녁 식사가 준비되어, 포로와 부인들을 제외한 모두가 식탁에 둘러앉았다. 부인들은 자기들 방에서 따로 식사를 했다. 식사 도중에 신부가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 여러 해 포로로 있던 콘스탄티노플에서, 판관님과 같은 이름을 가진 동료가 한 사람 있었습니다. 그 동료는 스페인 보병 전체에서 가장 굳세고 용맹한 군인이자 대위 가운데 하나였지요. 하지만 그는 용맹과 용기를 지닌 만큼이나 큰 몫의 불운도 지니고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 대위는 어떻게 불렸습니까, 나리?\" 판관이 물었다.\n\n\"루이 페레스 데 비에드마라 불렸습니다.\" 신부가 답했다. \"그리고 레온 산지의 어느 마을에서 태어났지요. 그는 자기 아버지와 형제들에 얽힌 한 가지 사연을 들려주었는데, 만일 그토록 진실한 사람이 직접 들려준 것이 아니었더라면 저는 그것을 겨울철 노파들이 화롯가에서 늘어놓는 옛이야기 가운데 하나로 치부했을 것입니다. 그가 말하기를, 자기 아버지가 세 아들에게 재산을 나누어 주면서 카토의 그 어떤 가르침보다도 더 사리에 맞는 충고의 말을 들려주었다는 것입니다. 다만 이것만은 말할 수 있으니, 그가 전쟁터로 나가기로 한 선택은 그토록 큰 성공이 따라서, 그 용맹한 처신과 용기로, 그리고 오로지 자신의 공적 외에는 아무런 도움도 없이, 그는 몇 해 만에 보병 대위에 올랐고, 머지않아 군단의 지휘권을 받게 될 출셋길에 오른 자기 모습을 보게 되었습니다. 그러나 운명은 그를 거슬렀으니, 그 운명의 은총을 기대할 만한 바로 그곳에서 그는 그것을 잃고 말았고, 그와 더불어 자유까지 잃었습니다. 그토록 많은 이들이 자유를 되찾은 그 영광스러운 날, 레판토 해전에서 말입니다. 저는 라 골레타에서 제 자유를 잃었고, 여러 곡절을 겪은 끝에 우리는 콘스탄티노플에서 동료가 되었습니다. 거기서 그는 알제로 갔는데, 그곳에서 세상 누구에게도 일찍이 일어난 적 없는 더없이 기이한 모험 가운데 하나를 겪었습니다.\"\n\n여기서 신부는 자기 형이 소라이다와 겪은 모험을 간추려 이야기해 나갔다. 판관은 그 모든 것에 어찌나 귀 기울여 듣던지 일찍이 그처럼 열심히 들어 본 적이 없을 정도였다. 그러나 신부는 프랑스인들이 보트에 탄 이들의 것을 약탈한 일과, 그의 동료와 아리따운 무어 여인이 빠지게 된 가난과 곤경을 묘사하는 데까지만 이야기했고, 그들이 어떻게 되었는지, 스페인에 닿았는지, 아니면 프랑스인들에게 프랑스로 끌려갔는지는 알아낼 수 없었노라고 말했다.\n\n대위는 한쪽으로 조금 물러나 서서 신부가 하는 말을 모두 듣고 있었고, 자기 형의 일거수일투족을 지켜보고 있었다. 형은 신부가 이야기를 끝냈음을 알아채자마자 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 눈에 눈물을 가득 머금은 채 말했다. \"오, 나리, 나리께서 제게 어떤 소식을 주셨는지, 그것이 제게 얼마나 사무치는지 아신다면! 제 온갖 세상 지혜와 자제력에도 불구하고 제 눈에서 솟구치는 이 눈물로 제 심정을 드러내고 마는군요! 나리께서 말씀하신 그 용감한 대위는 제 맏형입니다. 그는 저나 제 다른 형제보다 더 담대하고 더 고상한 뜻을 품고 있어서, 아버지께서 우리에게 제시하신 세 가지 길 가운데 명예롭고 떳떳한 무예의 길을 택했지요. 나리의 그 동료분이, 나리께서 옛이야기를 들려주는 줄로 여기셨던 그 사연 속에서 말한 그대로입니다. 저는 학문의 길을 따랐는데, 하느님과 저 자신의 노력으로 지금 나리께서 보시는 이 자리에까지 오르게 되었습니다. 제 둘째 형은 페루에 있는데, 어찌나 부유한지 아버지와 제게 보내 준 것으로 자기가 가지고 간 몫을 충분히 갚고도, 아버지께서 타고난 너그러움을 베푸실 방편까지 손에 쥐여 드렸으며, 저 또한 보다 격에 맞고 떳떳한 방식으로 학업을 이어 가 지금의 지위에 이를 수 있었습니다. 아버지께서는 아직 살아 계시나, 맏아들의 소식을 듣고자 하는 애간장에 거의 돌아가실 지경이며, 아들의 두 눈을 한 번 보기 전에는 죽음이 자기 두 눈을 감기지 않게 해 달라고 하느님께 쉼 없이 기도드리고 계십니다. 그런데 형에 관해 제가 놀라운 것은, 그토록 분별 있는 사람이었으면서 자기 고난과 고통 속에서든 형통할 때든 자신에 관해 아무런 소식도 전하기를 소홀히 했다는 점입니다. 만일 아버지나 우리 가운데 누구라도 그의 처지를 알았더라면 그는 몸값을 마련하려고 그 갈대의 기적 따위를 기다릴 필요도 없었을 테니 말입니다. 그러나 지금 저를 불안하게 하는 것은, 그 프랑스인들이 형을 풀어 주었는지, 아니면 도둑질을 숨기려고 죽여 버렸는지 알 수 없다는 것입니다. 이 모든 것 때문에 저는 처음 시작했을 때의 그 만족감이 아니라 더없이 깊은 우수와 슬픔 속에서 제 여정을 이어 가게 될 것입니다. 오, 사랑하는 형이여! 형이 지금 어디 있는지 알기만 한다면, 비록 제 자신이 고통을 치르더라도 서둘러 형을 찾아 그 고통에서 건져 낼 텐데! 오, 형이 살아 있다는 소식을 우리 늙으신 아버지께 전할 수만 있다면, 설령 형이 바르바리의 가장 깊은 토굴에 갇혀 있다 한들 형의 재산과 제 재산으로 거기서 형을 구해 낼 텐데! 오, 아름답고 너그러운 소라이다여, 한 형제에게 베푼 그대의 선한 덕에 보답할 수만 있다면! 그대 영혼의 새로운 탄생에, 그리고 우리 모두에게 그토록 큰 행복을 안겨 줄 그대의 혼례에 함께할 수만 있다면!\"\n\n판관은 형에 관해 들은 소식에 어찌나 깊이 감격했던지 이 모든 말과 그 이상의 말을 토해 냈고, 듣는 이들 모두가 그 마음을 함께 나누며 그의 슬픔에 동조하는 빛을 보였다. 그러자 신부는 자기 목적과 대위의 바람을 얼마나 훌륭히 이루어 냈는가를 보고는 더 이상 그들을 슬픔 속에 두고 싶지 않아, 식탁에서 일어나 소라이다가 있는 방으로 가서 그녀의 손을 잡았고, 루신다와 도로테아와 판관의 딸이 그 뒤를 따랐다. 대위는 신부가 어찌하나 지켜보며 기다리고 있었는데, 신부가 다른 손으로 그를 붙들고는 두 사람을 데리고 판관과 다른 신사들이 있는 곳으로 나아가 말했다. \"판관님, 이제 눈물을 거두십시오. 그리고 바라시던 만큼 흡족하게 마음의 소원을 이루십시오. 바로 판관님 앞에 그 훌륭한 형님과 어진 형수님이 계시니 말입니다. 여기 보시는 이분이 비에드마 대위이며, 이 사람이 그에게 그토록 잘해 준 그 아리따운 무어 여인입니다. 제가 말씀드린 그 프랑스인들이 이들을 지금 보시는 가난한 처지로 몰아넣었으니, 판관님의 어진 마음의 너그러움을 베푸시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n대위는 달려가 형을 끌어안으려 했으나, 형은 그를 잘 바라보려고 두 손을 그의 가슴에 짚어 조금 떼어 놓았다. 그러나 그가 누구인지 온전히 알아보자마자 형은 두 팔로 그를 어찌나 꼭 껴안고 진심에서 우러난 기쁨의 눈물을 어찌나 쏟던지, 거기 있던 이들 대부분이 함께 눈물을 흘리지 않을 수 없었다. 두 형제가 주고받은 말과 그들이 드러낸 감격은 짐작하기조차 어려우리니, 글로 적어 내기는 더더욱 어려우리라 여겨진다. 그들은 몇 마디로 서로 자기 삶에 일어난 일들을 들려주었고, 형제의 참된 애정을 그 온 힘을 다해 드러내 보였다. 이윽고 판관은 소라이다를 껴안으며 자기가 가진 모든 것을 그녀가 마음대로 쓰도록 내어 주었고, 그런 다음 자기 딸로 하여금 그녀를 껴안게 하니, 그 아리따운 기독교 처녀와 사랑스러운 무어 여인의 모습에 다시금 모든 이의 눈에서 새 눈물이 솟았다. 그리고 그 자리에는 돈키호테가 있어, 이 모든 기이한 일들을 한마디 말도 없이 유심히 지켜보며 그 전부를 편력 기사도의 환영(幻影)들 탓으로 돌리고 있었다. 이윽고 그들은, 대위와 소라이다가 형과 함께 세비야로 돌아가, 그가 풀려나 찾아졌다는 소식을 아버지께 전하여 아버지가 와서 소라이다의 혼례와 세례에 함께할 수 있도록 하기로 합의했다. 판관으로서는 여정을 미루기가 불가능했으니, 그때부터 한 달 뒤에 함대가 세비야에서 누에바 에스파냐를 향해 출항한다는 통지를 받은 터라, 그 항해를 놓치면 그에게 큰 낭패가 되었을 것이기 때문이다. 요컨대, 모두가 포로의 좋은 운수에 흡족해하고 기뻐했다. 그리고 이제 밤이 거의 삼분의 이가 지난지라, 그들은 남은 밤 동안 잠자리에 들어 쉬기로 했다. 돈키호테는 성안에 든 그 막대한 아름다움의 보물을 탐낸 어떤 거인이나 다른 악독한 자가 그들을 습격하지 못하도록 성을 지키며 보초를 서겠노라 자청했다. 그를 아는 이들은 이 봉사에 감사를 표했고, 판관에게 그의 별난 기질에 관해 이야기해 주니 판관도 적잖이 흥겨워했다. 오직 산초 판사만은 잠자리에 들 시각이 너무 늦은 데 부아가 나 있었다. 그러면서도 그는 누구보다 더 편안히 자리를 잡았으니, 자기 당나귀의 마구 위에 몸을 쭉 뻗었던 것인데, 뒤에 이야기되겠지만 이것이 그에게 톡톡히 값비싼 대가를 치르게 했다.\n\n그리하여 부인들이 자기들 방으로 물러가고 나머지 사람들도 되도록 불편하지 않게 자리를 잡고 나자, 돈키호테는 약속한 대로 성의 보초를 서려고 객줏집 밖으로 나갔다. 그런데 동이 트기 조금 전, 어찌나 곱고 감미로운 목소리가 부인들의 귀에 닿았던지, 모두가 그것에 귀를 곤두세우지 않을 수 없었는데, 특히나 잠이 깨어 있던 도로테아가 그러했으니, 그녀 곁에는 도냐 클라라 데 비에드마가 — 판관의 딸이 그렇게 불렸던 것이다 — 잠들어 누워 있었다. 그토록 감미롭게 노래하는 이가 누구인지 아무도 짐작할 수 없었고, 그 목소리에는 어떤 악기의 반주도 곁들여 있지 않았다. 어느 순간에는 노래하는 이가 안뜰에 있는 것 같다가도, 또 어느 순간에는 마구간에 있는 것 같았다. 그렇게 모두가 어리둥절해하며 온통 귀를 기울이고 있을 때, 카르데니오가 문간으로 와서 말했다. \"잠들지 않은 사람은 들어 보시오. 노래하면서 사람을 호리는 어느 노새몰이꾼의 목소리를 듣게 될 테니.\"\n\n\"이미 듣고 있답니다, 나리.\" 도로테아가 말했다. 이에 카르데니오는 자리를 떴고, 도로테아는 그 목소리에 온통 정신을 쏟아 그 노랫말이 이러함을 알아들었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIII.\n\n노새 몰이꾼의 유쾌한 이야기와 더불어 여인숙에서 일어난 다른 기이한 일들에 관하여\n\n아, 나는 사랑의 뱃사람, 사랑의 깊은 바다를 항해하는 자. 어디에 항구가 있는지 알지 못하고, 그곳에 닿으리라 감히 바라지도 못하네.\n\n외로이 멀리 떠 있는 별 하나가 나를 인도하는 전부이니, 옛날 팔리누루스를 비추던 그 어떤 별보다도 밝구나.\n\n정처 없이 떠밀려 가는 나는, 그것이 나를 어디로 데려가는지 알지 못한 채, 오직 그 별 하나에만 눈길을 박고, 그 밖의 모든 것엔 무심하기만 하네.\n\n허나 지나치게 조심스러운 정숙함과 차갑고 모진 새침함이, 내가 가장 그것을 필요로 할 때면, 구름처럼 그토록 갈망하던 그 빛을 내게서 가려 버리네.\n\n밝은 별이여, 내 그리워하는 눈길의 목표여, 그대 내 머리 위에서 빛날 때, 그대 내 시야에서 몸을 감추는 그날이면 죽음이 내게 가까이 왔음을 나는 알리라.\n\n노래하는 이가 여기까지 불렀을 때, 도로테아는 클라라가 이토록 감미로운 목소리를 듣지 못하고 놓치게 두는 것이 온당치 못하다는 생각이 들어, 그녀를 이리저리 흔들어 깨우며 말했다.\n\n\"얘야, 깨워서 미안하구나. 허나 네 평생에 들어 본 것 중 가장 아름다운 목소리일지도 모를 노래를 듣는 즐거움을 네게 주려고 그러는 거란다.\"\n\n클라라는 잠에 몹시 취한 채로 깨어나, 그 순간 도로테아가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아듣지 못해 무슨 일이냐고 물었다. 도로테아가 했던 말을 되풀이하자, 클라라는 이내 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 노래하는 이가 계속 부르는 두 줄을 채 듣기도 전에, 마치 심한 사일열에 시달리기라도 하듯 야릇한 떨림이 그녀를 사로잡았고, 그녀는 도로테아에게 두 팔을 두르며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 제 영혼과 생명의 다정하신 분이여! 어찌하여 저를 깨우셨나요? 지금 운명이 제게 베풀 수 있는 가장 큰 자비라면, 제 눈과 귀를 닫아 저 불행한 악사를 보지도 듣지도 못하게 하는 것일 텐데요.\"\n\n\"얘야, 대체 무슨 말을 하는 게냐?\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"아니, 저 노래하는 이는 노새몰이꾼이라던데!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그분은 여러 영지의 주인이세요.\" 클라라가 대답했다. \"그리고 제 마음속의 한 영지는 그분이 그토록 굳건히 차지하고 계시니, 그분 스스로 내어 주실 마음이 없는 한 결코 빼앗기지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n도로테아는 그 소녀의 열렬한 말에 깜짝 놀랐으니, 그것은 그 어린 나이가 약속할 법한 인생의 경험을 한참 넘어선 듯 보였기에, 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"클라라 아가씨, 무슨 말씀인지 알아들을 수가 없게 말씀하시는군요. 좀 더 분명하게 일러 주세요. 마음이며 영지며, 그리고 그 목소리가 아가씨를 그토록 흔들어 놓은 이 악사에 관해 무슨 말씀을 하시는지 말이에요. 하지만 지금은 아무 말도 하지 마세요. 아가씨의 그 격정에 정신을 쏟느라 노래하는 이의 노래를 듣는 즐거움을 잃고 싶지 않으니까요. 보아하니 그가 새로운 가락과 새로운 곡조를 부르기 시작하는 모양이네요.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 그러시든지요.\" 클라라가 대꾸했다. 그러고는 그의 노래를 듣지 않으려고 두 손으로 양쪽 귀를 틀어막았으니, 이에 도로테아는 다시금 놀랐다. 그러나 노래에 주의를 돌리자, 그 가락이 이러하게 흘러가는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n감미로운 희망이여, 나의 버팀목이여, 그대가 품은 뜻의 목표를 향해 앞으로 길을 헤쳐 나아가며, 방해도 장애도 아랑곳하지 않는구나. 두려워 말지니, 한 걸음마다 죽음이 가까이 있음을 보더라도.\n\n어떤 승리도, 승전의 어떤 기쁨도 약한 마음은 알지 못하나니, 복되지 못한 자로다, 운명에 담대한 낯을 감히 내보이지 못하고, 영혼과 감각을 나태에 묶어 내어 주는 자는.\n\n사랑이 제 물건을 비싸게 판다면, 그 값어치는 다투어 마땅하니, 사랑이 제 도장을 찍어 놓은 것에 어떤 황금이 견주리오? 또한 모든 이가 알거니, 값싸게 얻은 것은 우리가 하찮게 여기는 법.\n\n결연한 사랑은 \"불가능\"이라는 말을 알지 못하나니, 내 구애가 끝없는 장애에 둘러싸여 있음을 보더라도, 어떤 절망도 하늘이 저기 있는 한 나를 땅에 묶어 두지 못하리라.\n\n여기서 목소리가 그쳤고 클라라의 흐느낌이 다시 시작되었으니, 이 모든 것이 도로테아의 호기심을 부추겨, 어찌하여 노래는 그리도 감미롭고 울음은 그리도 쓰라린지 그 까닭을 알고 싶게 했다. 그래서 도로테아는 아까 무슨 말을 하려 했었는지 다시 물었다. 이에 클라라는 루신다가 엿들을까 두려워, 두 팔을 도로테아에게 단단히 감고 입을 그녀의 귀에 바짝 갖다 대어 다른 누구에게도 들릴 염려 없이 말할 수 있게 한 뒤 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"다정하신 부인, 이 노래하는 이는 아라곤 출신 어느 신사의 아들로, 마을 두 곳의 영주이신 분의 자제예요. 그 신사는 마드리드에 있는 제 아버지 집 맞은편에 사시는데요, 비록 제 아버지가 겨울이면 집 창문에 커튼을, 여름이면 격자창을 쳐 두셨음에도, 어찌 된 영문인지 — 저도 모르겠어요 — 학업을 닦고 있던 이 신사분이, 교회에서였는지 다른 곳에서였는지는 말씀드릴 수 없지만, 아무튼 저를 보셨고, 그러다 저와 사랑에 빠지셨어요. 그리고 자기 집 창문에서 그토록 많은 신호와 눈물로 그 마음을 제게 알리시기에, 저는 그분을 믿지 않을 수 없었고, 심지어 그분이 제게서 무엇을 바라는지도 모른 채 그분을 사랑하지 않을 수 없었어요. 그분이 제게 보내곤 하던 신호 중 하나는 한 손을 다른 손에 맞잡아, 저와 혼인하고 싶다는 뜻을 보이는 것이었어요. 그렇게 될 수만 있다면 저야 기뻤겠지만, 외톨이에다 어머니도 없는 처지라 누구에게 제 마음을 털어놓아야 할지 몰라, 그저 그대로 두고 그분께 아무 호의도 보이지 않았어요. 다만 제 아버지와 그분의 아버지가 모두 집을 비우셨을 때면, 커튼이나 격자창을 조금 들어 올려 그분이 저를 똑똑히 볼 수 있게 해 드렸는데, 그러면 그분은 미쳐 버릴 것만 같은 기쁨을 내보이셨어요. 그러는 사이 제 아버지가 떠나실 때가 다가왔고, 그분이 그것을 알게 되셨지만 제게서 들으신 건 아니었어요. 저는 그것을 그분께 도무지 알릴 수가 없었으니까요. 그분은, 제 생각엔 슬픔 때문에 병이 나셨고, 그래서 저희가 떠나던 날 저는 그분을 뵙고 작별 인사를 — 그저 눈으로라도 — 드릴 수가 없었어요. 그런데 길을 떠난 지 이틀이 지나, 여기서 하루 거리에 있는 어느 마을의 여인숙에 들어서는데, 저는 여관 문간에서 노새몰이꾼 차림을 한 그분을 보았어요. 어찌나 변장을 잘하셨던지, 제가 그분의 모습을 마음에 새겨 두고 다니지 않았더라면 알아보지 못했을 거예요. 하지만 저는 그분을 알아보았고, 놀라우면서도 기뻤어요. 그분은 제 아버지 모르게 저를 지켜보셨는데, 길에서나 저희가 묵는 여인숙에서나 제 앞을 지날 때면 늘 아버지를 피해 몸을 숨기시거든요. 그분이 어떤 분인지 알고, 또 저를 사랑하는 마음에 이 모든 고생을 무릅쓰며 걸어서 이 여행을 하신다는 걸 떠올리면, 저는 슬픔에 죽을 것만 같아요. 그래서 그분이 발을 딛는 곳마다 제 눈길을 두지요. 그분이 무슨 뜻으로 오셨는지, 또 어떻게 자기 아버지에게서 빠져나오실 수 있었는지 저는 모르겠어요. 그 아버지는 다른 상속자도 없거니와 그분이 그럴 만한 자식이기에 한없이 그분을 사랑하시거든요. 그건 그분을 보시면 부인께서도 아시게 될 거예요. 게다가 말씀드릴 수 있는 건, 그분이 부르는 노래는 죄다 그분의 머릿속에서 나온 것이라는 거예요. 사람들이 그분을 대단한 학자이자 시인이라 말하는 것을 제가 들었거든요. 더구나 저는 그분을 보거나 그분의 노래를 들을 때마다 온몸이 떨리고, 제 아버지가 그분을 알아보고 저희 사랑을 알게 되실까 봐 겁이 나요. 저는 평생 그분과 단 한 마디도 나눈 적이 없어요. 그런데도 그분 없이는 살 수 없을 만큼 그분을 사랑한답니다. 다정하신 부인, 이것이 그 목소리로 부인을 그토록 기쁘게 한 그 악사에 관해 제가 드릴 수 있는 이야기 전부예요. 그리고 이것만으로도 그분이 노새몰이꾼이 아니라, 제가 이미 말씀드렸듯 마음과 마을의 주인이라는 것을 부인께서는 어렵잖이 아실 수 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"클라라 아가씨, 더는 말씀하지 마세요.\" 이 말에 도로테아가 그녀에게 천 번이나 입 맞추며 말했다. \"더는 말씀하지 마시라니까요. 날이 밝을 때까지 기다리세요. 그러면 저는 하느님께서 이 아가씨의 일을 마련해 주시리라 믿어요. 이토록 순결한 시작에 어울리는 행복한 결말을 맺도록 말이에요.\"\n\n\"아, 부인.\" 도냐 클라라가 말했다. \"그분의 아버지가 그토록 높은 지위에 계시고 그토록 부유하시어, 저 같은 건 그분 아들의 아내는커녕 하녀가 될 자격조차 없다 여기실 텐데, 무슨 결말을 바랄 수 있겠어요? 그리고 제 아버지 몰래 혼인하는 일은, 세상을 다 준다 해도 하지 않을 거예요. 제가 바라는 건 그저 이 젊은이가 돌아가서 저를 떠나는 것뿐이에요. 어쩌면 그분을 보지 않고, 또 저희가 가야 할 그 먼 길을 가다 보면, 지금 제가 겪는 이 고통이 한결 가벼워질지도 몰라요. 하긴 제가 내놓은 그 처방이 제게 별 도움이 안 되리란 건 짐작하지만요. 어쩌다 이런 일이 생겼는지, 그분을 향한 이 사랑이 어떻게 들어왔는지 도무지 모르겠어요. 저는 이렇게 어린 계집애이고 그분은 이렇게 한낱 소년인데요. 정말이지 저희 둘은 동갑이라고 생각해요. 저는 아직 열여섯도 안 됐거든요. 아버지 말씀으로는 다가오는 미카엘 축일에야 열여섯이 된다고 하셨어요.\"\n\n도로테아는 도냐 클라라가 어찌나 어린아이처럼 말하는지 웃지 않을 수 없었다. \"이제 잠을 청합시다, 아가씨.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"밤이 얼마 안 남은 듯하니까요. 하느님께서 이내 날을 밝혀 주실 테고, 그러면 우리가 만사를 바로잡지요. 그러지 못하면 제 탓이 클 거예요.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 두 사람은 잠이 들었고, 깊은 정적이 여인숙 전체를 다스렸다. 잠들지 않은 사람이라곤 여관 안주인의 딸과 그 하녀 마리토르네스뿐이었으니, 둘은 돈키호테의 별난 기질의 약점을 익히 알고 있던 데다, 그가 갑옷을 입고 말에 올라 여인숙 밖에서 보초를 서고 있다는 것도 알았기에, 그에게 무슨 장난을 치거나, 아니면 적어도 그의 허튼소리를 들으며 한동안 재미나 보자고 둘이서 작정했다. 마침 여인숙을 통틀어 바깥을 향해 난 창이라곤, 짚을 던져 내보내는 데 쓰던 짚 광 벽의 구멍 하나밖에 없었다. 이 구멍에 두 명의 반쪽짜리 아가씨가 자리를 잡고, 말 위에 앉아 창을 짚은 채 이따금 어찌나 깊고 구슬픈 한숨을 내쉬는지 그때마다 제 영혼을 뿌리째 뽑아내는 듯한 돈키호테를 살펴보았다. 또한 그가 부드럽고 다정하고 사랑이 담긴 어조로 이렇게 말하는 것도 들을 수 있었다. \"오, 나의 여인 둘시네아 델 토보소여, 온갖 아름다움의 극치요, 분별의 정점이자 면류관이며, 우아함의 보고요, 미덕의 곳간이며, 끝으로 이 세상 온갖 선하고 명예롭고 즐거운 것의 이상이시여! 그대 고귀하신 분께서는 지금 무엇을 하고 계신지요? 혹여 그대는, 오로지 그대를 섬기기 위해 자청하여 이토록 큰 위험에 몸을 내던진, 그대에게 사로잡힌 기사를 마음에 두고 계신지요? 오, 세 얼굴을 가진 빛이여, 그녀의 소식을 내게 전해 다오! 어쩌면 이 순간, 그녀를 시샘하며 그대는 그녀를 바라보고 있을지도 모르지. 그녀가 호화로운 궁전의 어느 회랑을 이리저리 거닐든, 어느 발코니에 기대어, 자신의 순결과 위엄을 지키면서도 어떻게 하면 그녀를 위해 견디는 이 가련한 내 가슴의 고통을 덜어 줄 수 있을지, 어떤 영광이 내 고난을 보답할지, 어떤 안식이 내 수고를, 끝으로 어떤 죽음이 내 삶을, 또 어떤 보상이 내 봉사를 갚아 줄지 골똘히 헤아리는 동안에 말이지. 그리고 그대, 오, 태양이여, 그대는 지금 의심할 바 없이 일찍 떠올라 내 여인을 보러 나오려고 서둘러 그대의 준마들에 마구를 채우고 있으렷다. 그대가 그녀를 보거든 부디 내 대신 그녀에게 인사를 전해 다오. 허나 조심할지니, 그녀를 보고 인사할 때 그녀의 얼굴에 입 맞추지는 말지어다. 그러지 않으면 나는, 그대가 질투와 사랑에 빠져 테살리아 들판에서, 아니면 페네우스 강가에서(그때 그대가 어디서 달렸는지 정확히 기억나지는 않지만) 그대를 그토록 땀 흘리며 달리게 했던 그 발 빠른 배은망덕한 여인에게 품었던 것보다 더한 질투를 그대에게 품게 될 테니까.\"\n\n돈키호테가 그 애절한 연설을 여기까지 이어 갔을 때, 여관 안주인의 딸이 그에게 신호를 보내기 시작하며 말했다. \"기사님, 이리로 좀 와 주세요.\"\n\n이 신호와 목소리에 돈키호테는 고개를 돌렸고, 마침 휘영청 만월의 광채로 빛나던 달빛에 비추어, 누군가가 벽의 그 구멍에서 자기를 부르고 있음을 보았다. 그에게는 그것이 창문처럼 보였고, 더구나 — 그가 여인숙을 그렇게 믿었듯 — 화려한 성이라면 으레 갖추었을 법한 금빛 격자가 달린 창문으로 보였다. 그러자 즉시, 지난번처럼, 이 성주의 따님인 아름다운 아가씨가 그를 향한 사랑에 사로잡혀 다시 한번 그의 애정을 얻으려 애쓰고 있다는 생각이 그의 상상에 떠올랐다. 이런 생각에, 무례하거나 배은망덕하게 보이지 않으려고, 그는 로시난테의 머리를 돌려 그 구멍으로 다가갔고, 두 처녀를 알아보자 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"아름다운 아가씨여, 그대의 큰 덕망과 고귀한 가문에 마땅한 보답을 도무지 돌려드릴 수 없는 자에게 사랑의 정념을 두셨다니, 가엾기 그지없습니다. 허나 그 일로 이 불행한 편력 기사를 탓하셔서는 아니 됩니다. 사랑이 이 기사로 하여금, 처음 눈으로 보았던 그 순간에 자기 영혼의 절대적 여주인으로 삼은 그분 외에는 누구에게도 굴복할 수 없게 만들었기 때문입니다. 고귀한 아가씨여, 저를 용서하시고 그대의 처소로 물러가소서. 그리고 그대의 정념을 더는 토로하시어 저로 하여금 더한층 배은망덕한 모습을 보이도록 몰아가지 마소서. 만일 저를 향한 그 사랑으로, 사랑 그 자체만 아니라면, 제 능력이 닿는 한도 안에서 그대를 만족시켜 드릴 수 있는 다른 무언가가 있다 여기신다면, 제게 청하소서. 제 그 감미롭고도 멀리 떨어진 적을 두고 그대에게 맹세하노니, 비록 그대가 온통 뱀으로 된 메두사의 머리카락 한 타래를 요구하시든, 아니 병 속에 가둔 태양의 빛줄기 그 자체를 요구하시든, 당장 이 순간 들어드리겠나이다.\"\n\n\"기사님, 저희 아씨께서는 그런 건 아무것도 원하지 않으세요.\" 이때 마리토르네스가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 분별 있는 부인, 그대의 아씨께서 원하시는 것이 무엇이오?\" 돈키호테가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그저 기사님의 고운 손 한쪽이에요.\" 마리토르네스가 말했다. \"그 손에다 아씨께서, 그 큰 정념을 — 이 구멍까지 이끌려 나와 명예를 그토록 위태롭게 하면서까지 품게 된 그 정념을 — 쏟아 내실 수 있게요. 만일 영주이신 아버님께서 아씨의 소리를 들으셨더라면, 잘라 내실 것 중 가장 작은 것이 아씨의 귀였을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그런 짓을 한번 시도하는 꼴을 보았으면 좋겠소.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 그자는 그런 짓을 삼가는 편이 나을 거요. 사랑에 빠진 딸의 보드라운 사지에 손을 댔다가, 세상의 어떤 아비도 맞은 적 없는 가장 처참한 최후를 맞고 싶지 않다면 말이오.\"\n\n마리토르네스는 돈키호테가 자기가 청한 손을 내밀 것이라 확신하고, 무엇을 할지 마음을 정한 뒤, 구멍에서 내려와 마구간으로 들어가 산초 판사의 당나귀 고삐를 집어 들고는, 마침 돈키호테가 사랑에 애태우는 아가씨가 있다고 여긴 그 격자창에 닿으려고 로시난테의 안장 위에 올라선 바로 그 순간에, 한껏 서둘러 구멍으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 그에게 손을 내밀며 그가 말했다. \"아가씨여, 이 손을, 아니 차라리 땅 위 악인들을 응징하는 이 채찍을 받으소서. 자, 받으소서. 이 손은 그 어떤 여인의 손도 닿은 적이 없으니, 내 온몸을 완전히 차지하고 계신 그분의 손조차도 닿은 적이 없는 손입니다. 입 맞추라고 이 손을 내미는 것이 아니라, 힘줄의 짜임새와 근육의 촘촘한 얽힘과 핏줄의 너비와 굵기를 살펴보시라고 내미는 것이니, 그로써 그대는 이런 손을 가진 팔의 힘이 어떠할지 헤아릴 수 있으리다.\"\n\n\"그건 곧 알게 되겠지요.\" 마리토르네스가 말하고는, 고삐에 매듭 올가미를 지어 그것을 그의 손목에 걸고, 구멍에서 내려와 그 다른 끝을 짚 광 문빗장에 아주 단단히 잡아맸다.\n\n손목에 닿는 밧줄의 거칠음을 느낀 돈키호테가 부르짖었다. \"그대께서는 제 손을 어루만지신다기보다 갈아 대시는 듯하구려. 그토록 모질게 다루지 마소서. 제 결심이 그대를 노엽게 한 그 잘못에 이 손은 죄가 없으며, 그토록 작은 부분에 그대의 온갖 복수를 퍼붓는 것도 옳지 않소이다. 그토록 깊이 사랑하는 이라면 그토록 잔혹하게 복수해서는 아니 됨을 기억하소서.\"\n\n그러나 이제 돈키호테의 이 말을 들어 줄 사람은 아무도 없었으니, 마리토르네스는 그를 묶자마자 다른 처녀와 함께 웃다 죽을 지경이 되어 달아나 버렸고, 그가 스스로 풀려나는 것이 도무지 불가능한 꼴로 그를 매어 둔 채 떠난 것이다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이 그는 로시난테 위에 서 있었고, 팔은 구멍으로 통과시킨 채 손목은 문빗장에 묶여 있어, 로시난테가 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 한 발짝이라도 움직이면 팔에 매달린 신세가 될까 봐 극심한 두려움과 공포에 떨었다. 그리하여 그는 손톱만큼도 움직이려 들지 못했다. 비록 로시난테의 참을성 있고 흔들림 없는 성품으로 보아, 한 세기 내내 꿈쩍 않고 서 있으리라 충분히 기대할 만했지만 말이다. 자기가 단단히 묶여 있고 아가씨들이 물러갔음을 알게 되자, 그는 이 모든 것이 마법으로 벌어진 일이라 여기기 시작했으니, 지난번 바로 그 성에서 그 마법에 걸린 무어인 같은 짐꾼이 자기를 흠씬 두들겨 팼던 때처럼 말이다. 그리고 그는, 처음에 그토록 호되게 당하고서도 또다시 이 성에 들어오는 모험을 무릅쓴 자신의 분별없음과 판단력 부족을 마음속으로 저주했다. 편력 기사들 사이에서는, 일단 어떤 모험을 시도했다가 성공하지 못하면 그것은 그들을 위한 것이 아니라 다른 이들을 위해 마련된 모험이라는 표시이므로 다시 시도할 필요가 없다는 것이 정해진 이치였기 때문이다. 그럼에도 그는 풀려날 수 있을까 하여 팔을 잡아당겨 보았으나, 너무도 단단히 매여 있어 온갖 애를 써 보았자 헛수고였다. 사실 그는 로시난테가 움직일까 봐 조심스레 잡아당기긴 했으나, 안장에 다시 앉아 보려고 아무리 애를 써도 별수 없이 그저 꼿꼿이 서 있거나 손을 잡아 떼어 내는 수밖에 없었다. 바로 그때 그는, 그 어떤 마법도 힘을 쓰지 못하던 아마디스의 검을 갈망했다. 그러고는 자기 불운을 저주했다. 그러고는 자기가 여기 마법에 걸려 있는 동안 세상이 입게 될 손실이 얼마나 클지 부풀려 생각했으니, 자기가 의심할 바 없이 마법에 걸려 있다고 믿었기 때문이다. 그러고는 다시 한번 사랑하는 둘시네아 델 토보소를 떠올리기 시작했다. 그러고는 충직한 종자 산초 판사를 불렀으나, 그는 당나귀의 짐안장 위에 큰대자로 뻗어 잠에 파묻힌 채 그 순간 자기를 낳아 준 어미조차 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 그러고는 현자 리르간데오와 알키페에게 도와달라고 외쳤다. 그러고는 자기의 좋은 벗 우르간다에게 구원을 청했다. 그러고는 마침내, 아침이 되도록 그는 절망과 당혹에 빠져 황소처럼 울부짖고 있었으니, 그날이 자기 고통에 어떤 위안도 가져다주지 못하리라 여겼기 때문이다. 자기가 마법에 걸린 이상 그 고통이 영원히 계속되리라 믿었던 것이다. 로시난테가 많이든 적게든 도무지 꿈쩍하지 않는 것을 보고 그는 이 믿음을 더욱 굳혔으니, 자기와 자기 말이 흉성(凶星)의 사악한 기운이 다 지나가거나, 혹은 더 현명한 어떤 마법사가 마법을 풀어 줄 때까지 먹지도 마시지도 자지도 못한 채 이 꼴로 남아 있어야 하리라 믿어 버린 것이다.\n\n그러나 그는 이 결론에서 크게 속고 있었으니, 날이 채 밝기도 전에 잘 갖춰 입고 무장한 네 명의 사내가 말을 타고 안장 앞에 화승총을 가로질러 걸친 채 여인숙으로 다가왔기 때문이다. 그들은 아직 닫혀 있던 여인숙 문을 향해 큰 소리로 외치며 요란하게 두드렸다. 이를 본 돈키호테는, 자기가 있던 그곳에서도 보초 노릇 하기를 잊지 않고, 크고 위엄 있는 어조로 말했다. \"기사들이든, 종자들이든, 그대들이 무엇이든 간에, 그대들에게는 이 성의 문을 두드릴 권리가 없소. 안에 있는 이들이 잠들어 있거나, 아니면 햇살이 온 땅 위에 두루 퍼지기 전에는 이 요새를 열어젖히는 법이 없음이 자명하지 않소. 멀찍이 물러가, 날이 환히 밝을 때까지 기다리시오. 그런 다음에야 그대들에게 문을 여는 것이 마땅한지 아닌지 우리가 보게 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"대체 이게 무슨 빌어먹을 요새고 성이라고,\" 한 사람이 말했다. \"우리한테 이런 격식을 차리게 한단 말이오? 당신이 여관 주인이거든 문을 열라고 일러 주시오. 우리는 그저 말에게 여물이나 먹이고 가던 길을 가려는 나그네들이고, 갈 길이 바쁘오.\"\n\n\"나리들, 내가 여관 주인처럼 보이시오?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 무엇처럼 보이는지는 모르겠소.\" 그 사람이 대답했다. \"허나 이 여인숙을 성이라 부르다니 허튼소리를 하고 있다는 건 알겠소.\"\n\n\"성이 맞소.\" 돈키호테가 받아쳤다. \"아니, 그뿐 아니라 이 지방 전체에서 손꼽히는 성 중 하나요. 그리고 그 안에는 손에 왕홀을 쥐고 머리에 왕관을 얹어 본 사람들이 있소.\"\n\n\"차라리 그 반대라면 나을 텐데,\" 나그네가 말했다. \"머리에 왕홀을, 손에 왕관을 말이오. 하지만 그렇다면 안에 어쩌면 어느 배우 패거리가 있는 모양이군. 그들이야 당신이 말하는 그 왕관이며 왕홀을 갖고 다니는 게 흔한 일이니 말이오. 이렇게 조그만 여인숙에, 게다가 이토록 조용한 곳에, 왕관과 왕홀을 누릴 자격이 있는 사람들이 묵고 있으리라곤 믿기지 않으니까.\"\n\n\"그대는 세상을 거의 모르는구려.\" 돈키호테가 되받았다. \"편력 기사도에서 흔히 일어나는 일을 모르는 걸 보니 말이오.\"\n\n그러나 말하던 사내의 동료들은 돈키호테와의 입씨름에 진력이 나, 다시 어찌나 거세게 문을 두드렸던지, 주인은 물론이고 여인숙 안의 모든 이가 잠에서 깨어났고, 주인은 일어나 누가 문을 두드리는지 물으려 했다. 마침 이 순간, 안으로 들어가기를 청하던 네 사람의 말 가운데 한 마리가 로시난테의 냄새를 맡으러 다가갔다. 로시난테는 우울하고 풀 죽은 채 귀를 늘어뜨리고 꿈쩍도 않고 서서, 호되게 늘어진 주인을 떠받치고 있었다. 그러나 나무로 만들어진 것처럼 보일지언정 결국 살과 피로 된 짐승인지라, 어쩔 수 없이 그만 몸을 내주어, 자기에게 관심을 보이러 온 그 말에게 답례로 냄새를 맡았다. 그런데 그가 조금이라도 움직이자마자 돈키호테는 발 디딘 곳을 잃고 안장에서 미끄러져 땅에 떨어졌을 것이나, 팔로 매달린 덕에 그러지 못했다. 그 바람에 그는 손목이 잘려 나가거나 팔이 뜯겨 나가리라 여겨질 만큼 극심한 고통을 겪었다. 그리고 그는 땅에서 어찌나 가까이 매달려 있던지 발끝으로 겨우 땅에 닿을 정도였는데, 이것이 그에게는 오히려 더 나빴다. 발을 단단히 디디는 데 얼마나 모자라는지를 알아채자, 그는 발 디딜 자리를 얻으려고 안간힘을 다해 버둥거리며 몸을 뻗었으니, 마치 스트라파도 고문을 당하는 자들이 \"닿을 듯 말 듯한\" 높이에 매달려, 조금만 더 하면 땅에 닿으리라는 헛된 희망에 속아 미친 듯이 몸을 뻗으며 제 고통을 한층 키우는 꼴과 꼭 같았다. * 스트라파도: 두 손을 뒤로 묶어 도르래로 매달아 올렸다가 떨어뜨리는 옛 고문 방식.\n\n# IN WHICH ARE CONTINUED THE UNHEARD-OF ADVENTURES OF THE INN\n\n이렇듯 돈키호테가 지른 고함 소리가 어찌나 컸던지, 여관 주인이 부랴부랴 여관 문을 열고 당황한 얼굴로 뛰쳐나와 누가 그런 고함을 지르는지 보려 달려갔고, 바깥에 있던 자들도 그를 뒤따랐다. 그 무렵 같은 고함 소리에 잠이 깬 마리토르네스는 무슨 일인지 짐작하고 다락으로 달려가, 아무도 보지 못하게 돈키호테가 매달려 있던 밧줄을 풀어 주었다. 그리하여 그는 여관 주인과 길손들이 보는 앞에서 땅으로 떨어졌고, 그들이 다가와 무슨 일로 그토록 고함을 질렀느냐고 물었다. 그는 한마디 대꾸도 없이 손목에 묶인 밧줄을 풀고는 벌떡 일어나 로시난테에 뛰어올라, 방패를 팔에 끼고 창을 겨드랑이에 받친 채 들판을 한참 빙 돌더니 절반쯤 질주하며 돌아와 외쳤다.\n\n\"누구든 내가 마땅한 이유로 마법에 걸렸다고 말하는 자가 있다면, 미코미코나 공주 마님께서 내게 허락해 주시는 한, 나는 그가 거짓말쟁이라 선언하고, 일대일 결투를 청하며 도전하노라.\"\n\n새로 도착한 길손들은 돈키호테의 말에 어리둥절했으나, 여관 주인이 그가 누구인지 일러 주며 제정신이 아니니 신경 쓰지 말라 하여 그들의 놀라움을 풀어 주었다. 그러자 그들은 여관 주인에게 혹시 열다섯 살쯤 된 젊은이가 이 여관에 들지 않았는지, 노새몰이꾼 차림에 이러이러한 모습을 한 자가 오지 않았는지 물으며 도냐 클라라의 연인의 생김새를 일러 주었다. 여관 주인은 여관에 사람이 하도 많아 그들이 찾는 자를 미처 눈여겨보지 못했노라 대답했다. 그런데 그들 가운데 하나가 판사가 타고 온 마차를 알아보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"그자는 틀림없이 여기 있소. 이게 바로 그가 따라온 마차니 말이오. 우리 중 하나는 문간에 남고 나머지는 안으로 들어가 그를 찾읍시다. 아니, 그자가 마당 담을 넘어 달아나지 못하게 누군가가 여관을 한 바퀴 도는 것이 좋겠소.\" \"그럽시다.\" 다른 자가 말했다. 그리하여 둘은 안으로 들어가고, 하나는 문간에 남고 또 하나는 여관 둘레를 돌았는데, 이 모든 것을 지켜보던 여관 주인은 그들이 일러 준 생김새의 젊은이를 찾고 있다는 것은 알았으나, 무슨 까닭에 이토록 온갖 조심을 다하는지는 도무지 짐작할 수가 없었다.\n\n이 무렵 날은 훤히 밝았다. 그런 까닭에, 또한 돈키호테가 일으킨 소동 탓에 모두가 잠에서 깨어 일어나 있었는데, 특히 도냐 클라라와 도로테아가 그러했으니, 둘은 그날 밤 잠을 제대로 이루지 못했던 것이다. 하나는 연인이 그토록 가까이 있다는 데서 오는 설렘 때문이었고, 다른 하나는 그를 보고 싶은 호기심 때문이었다. 돈키호테는 네 명의 길손 가운데 어느 누구도 자기를 거들떠보지 않고 도전에 응하지도 않는 것을 보자 분통이 터져, 분노와 노여움으로 죽을 지경이 되었다. 만일 그가 기사도의 법규 가운데, 편력 기사가 어떤 임무에 자신의 말과 신의를 걸어 그 일을 끝마치기 전에는 다른 어떤 일에도 얽혀들지 않겠다고 맹세한 처지에서도 또 다른 일을 떠맡거나 거기에 뛰어드는 것이 마땅하다는 조항을 찾아낼 수 있었더라면, 그는 그들 전부를 공격하여 그들로 하여금 본의 아니게라도 대답하게 만들었으리라. 그러나 미코미코나를 그 왕국에 복위시키기 전에는 새로운 일을 시작하는 것이 자신에게 어울리지도, 옳지도 않다는 데 생각이 미치자, 그는 입을 다물고 가만히 기다리며 그 길손들의 일이 어찌 결말이 날지 지켜볼 수밖에 없었다. 그들 가운데 하나가 찾던 젊은이를 발견했는데, 그는 누가 자기를 찾으러 오리라고는, 더더구나 찾아내리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못한 채 노새몰이꾼 곁에서 곤히 잠들어 있었다.\n\n그 사내는 그의 팔을 붙잡고 말했다. \"돈 루이스 도련님, 참으로 잘 어울리는 차림새를 하고 계시는군요. 어머님께서 호사스럽게 길러 주신 분이 누우신 잠자리치고는 어찌나 걸맞은지요.\"\n\n젊은이는 졸린 눈을 비비며 자기를 붙든 자를 한참 바라보다가, 이내 그가 아버지의 하인 가운데 하나임을 알아보고는 어찌나 놀랐던지 한동안 한마디도 찾아 입에 올리지 못했다. 그러는 사이 하인은 말을 이었다. \"돈 루이스 도련님, 이제는 별수 없으니 순순히 따라 집으로 돌아가시지요. 도련님의 부재로 상심에 잠기신 아버님께서 저세상으로 떠나시기를 바라지 않으신다면 말입니다. 그러지 않으시면 그것 말고 다른 결말은 있을 수 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"한데 내가 이 길로, 이런 차림으로 떠난 줄을 아버지께서 어찌 아셨단 말이냐?\" 돈 루이스가 말했다.\n\n\"도련님이 속내를 털어놓으셨던 그 학생입니다.\" 하인이 대답했다. \"그자가 도련님을 잃고 괴로워하시는 아버님의 모습에 측은한 마음이 들어 다 일러바쳤지요. 그래서 아버님께서 하인 넷을 도련님을 찾으라 보내셨으니, 이제 저희 모두 도련님을 모시려 여기 와 있습니다. 이토록 빨리 돌아가 도련님을 그토록 애타게 기다리는 그 눈앞에 다시 모실 수 있게 되었으니, 상상도 못 하실 만큼 기쁘기 그지없습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 내 뜻에 달렸거나, 하늘이 정하는 대로 될 일이다.\" 돈 루이스가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"돌아가는 데 동의하시는 것 말고 도련님이 무엇을 뜻하실 수 있으며, 하늘인들 무엇을 정할 수 있겠습니까?\" 하인이 말했다. \"그 밖의 일은 있을 수 없습니다.\"\n\n돈 루이스가 곁에 누워 있던 노새몰이꾼이 두 사람의 이 모든 대화를 엿들었는데, 그는 일어나 그동안 옷을 차려입은 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오와 다른 이들에게 가서 무슨 일이 있었는지 알렸다. 그 사내가 젊은이를 \"돈\"이라 부른 것이며, 오간 말들이며, 사내가 젊은이를 아버지에게 돌려보내려 하나 젊은이는 그러기를 꺼린다는 것을 일러 주었다. 이 이야기에다, 하늘이 그에게 내려 준 그 보기 드문 목소리에 관해 이미 알고 있던 터라, 그들 모두 그가 누구인지 좀 더 자세히 알고 싶은 마음이 간절해졌고, 만일 그에게 무력을 쓰려 한다면 그를 도와주고 싶기까지 했다. 그리하여 그들은 그가 아직 하인과 이야기를 나누며 옥신각신하고 있는 곳으로 서둘러 갔다. 바로 그때 도로테아가 자기 방에서 나왔고, 온몸을 떠는 도냐 클라라가 그 뒤를 따랐다. 도로테아는 카르데니오를 따로 불러 몇 마디로 그 악사와 도냐 클라라의 사연을 들려주었고, 카르데니오 역시 그녀에게 무슨 일이 있었는지, 그의 아버지의 하인들이 그를 찾으러 왔다는 것을 일러 주었는데, 그렇게 말하면서 목소리를 충분히 낮추지 못한 탓에 도냐 클라라가 그 말을 듣고 말았다. 그녀가 어찌나 동요했던지 도로테아가 얼른 부축하지 않았더라면 땅바닥에 쓰러질 뻔했다. 그러자 카르데니오는 도로테아에게 자기가 이 모든 일을 잘 수습해 보겠노라며 방으로 돌아가라 일렀고, 그들은 그의 말대로 했다. 돈 루이스를 찾으러 온 네 사람은 이제 모두 여관 안으로 들어와 그를 에워싸고, 당장 한시도 지체 없이 돌아가 아버지를 위로해 드리라고 다그쳤다. 그는 자신의 목숨과 명예와 마음이 걸린 어떤 일을 매듭짓기 전에는 무슨 일이 있어도 그럴 수 없다고 대답했다. 하인들은 그를 두고는 결단코 돌아가지 않을 것이며, 그가 좋든 싫든 데려가겠다며 그를 몰아세웠다.\n\n\"그렇게는 못 한다.\" 돈 루이스가 대꾸했다. \"나를 죽여서 데려간다면 모를까. 어떤 식으로 데려가든 살아 있는 나를 데려가지는 못할 것이다.\"\n\n이 무렵에는 여관 안에 있던 대부분의 사람이 이 다툼에 이끌려 모여들었는데, 특히 카르데니오와 돈 페르난도와 그의 일행, 판사, 신부, 이발사, 그리고 돈키호테가 그러했다. 돈키호테는 이제 더는 성을 지키고 망볼 필요가 없다고 여겼던 것이다. 카르데니오는 이미 젊은이의 사연을 알고 있던 터라, 그를 데려가려는 사내들에게 어찌하여 이 젊은이를 본인의 뜻을 거슬러 끌고 가려 하느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"저희 목적은,\" 네 사람 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"이 도련님의 실종으로 목숨을 잃을 지경에 이르신 그분의 아버님 목숨을 구하는 것입니다.\"\n\n이 말에 돈 루이스가 소리쳤다. \"여기서 내 일을 떠벌릴 필요는 없다. 나는 자유로운 몸이니, 내가 원하면 돌아갈 것이고, 원치 않으면 너희 가운데 누구도 나를 억지로 끌고 가지 못한다.\"\n\n\"이치가 도련님을 끌고 갈 것입니다.\" 사내가 말했다. \"이치가 도련님께는 힘을 못 쓴다 해도 저희에게는 힘을 쓸 테니, 저희가 온 까닭, 저희가 마땅히 해야 할 일을 하게 만들 것입니다.\"\n\n\"대체 이게 다 무슨 일인지 들어 봅시다.\" 이때 판사가 말했다. 그러자 그를 이웃으로 알아본 사내가 대답했다. \"판사님, 이분을 모르시겠습니까? 댁의 이웃 어른의 아드님이신데, 보시다시피 신분에 도무지 어울리지 않는 차림으로 아버님 댁에서 도망쳐 나온 것입니다.\"\n\n이 말에 판사는 그를 더 유심히 살펴 알아보고는 그를 끌어안으며 말했다. \"돈 루이스, 이게 대체 무슨 어리석은 짓인가, 무슨 까닭이 있어 이런 모양으로, 자네 처지에 그토록 어울리지 않는 이런 차림으로 여기까지 오게 되었단 말인가?\"\n\n젊은이는 눈에 눈물이 차올라 판사의 물음에 한마디도 대꾸하지 못했다. 판사는 네 하인에게 마음 놓으라며, 모든 일이 흡족하게 매듭지어질 것이라 일렀다. 그러고는 돈 루이스의 손을 잡고 한쪽으로 데려가 그가 여기까지 온 까닭을 물었다.\n\n그런데 그가 그렇게 묻고 있는 사이, 여관 문간에서 요란한 고함 소리가 들려왔다. 그 까닭인즉, 그곳에서 밤을 보낸 손님 둘이 모두가 네 사내가 무엇을 원하는지 알아내느라 분주한 틈을 타, 외상값을 치르지 않고 슬그머니 떠날 궁리를 했던 것이다. 그러나 남의 일보다 제 일을 더 챙기는 여관 주인이 문간을 나서는 그들을 붙잡아 셈을 치르라 요구하며 그들의 파렴치함을 어찌나 험한 말로 욕했던지, 그들이 주먹으로 응수하게 만들었고, 그리하여 그들은 가엾은 주인이 비명을 지르며 도움을 청하지 않을 수 없을 만큼 호되게 두들겨 패기 시작했다. 여관 안주인과 그 딸은 돈키호테말고는 도움을 줄 만큼 한가한 사람이 아무도 없다고 보았기에, 딸이 그에게 말했다. \"기사 나리, 하느님께서 나리께 주신 그 힘으로 부디 가엾은 제 아버지를 도와주세요. 못된 사내 둘이 아버지를 떡이 되도록 두들겨 패고 있어요.\"\n\n이에 돈키호테는 자못 차분하고 태연하게 대꾸했다. \"고운 아가씨, 지금 이 순간 그대의 청은 때에 맞지 않소. 나는 내 말이 걸린 어떤 일을 행복한 결말로 이끌기 전에는 어떤 모험에도 얽혀드는 것이 금지되어 있기 때문이오. 다만 그대를 위해 내가 할 수 있는 것은 이제 말해 줄 터이니, 어서 달려가 그대의 아버지에게 이 싸움에서 힘닿는 대로 버티며 결코 패배를 당하지 말라 이르시오. 그동안 나는 미코미코나 공주께 가서 그분의 곤경에서 그를 구할 수 있도록 허락을 청하겠소. 만일 공주께서 허락하신다면, 내 반드시 그를 그 곤경에서 건져 내리라 믿어도 좋소.\"\n\n\"이 죄 많은 몸이여!\" 곁에 서 있던 마리토르네스가 소리쳤다. \"나리가 그 허락을 얻기도 전에 우리 주인은 저세상에 가 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"부인, 내가 말한 그 허락을 얻을 수 있게 해 주시오.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내가 허락을 얻기만 한다면, 그가 저세상에 가 있다 한들 별 상관이 없소. 온 세상이 막아선다 해도 내가 그를 거기서 구해 낼 것이며, 적어도 그를 그리로 보낸 자들에게 그대가 어지간히는 만족하고도 남을 만큼 톡톡히 원수를 갚아 주겠소.\" 그러고는 더 말하지 않고 도로테아 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 편력 기사다운 격식 갖춘 말로 공주 마님께, 지금 참혹한 위기에 처한 그 성의 성주를 돕고 구할 수 있도록 허락해 주시기를 청했다. 공주는 흔쾌히 허락해 주었고, 그는 즉시 방패를 팔에 끼고 칼을 빼어 들고는 여관 문간으로 달려갔는데, 그곳에서는 여전히 두 손님이 여관 주인을 거칠게 다루고 있었다. 그러나 그는 그 자리에 이르자마자 우뚝 멈춰 꼼짝 않고 서 있었으니, 마리토르네스와 안주인이 어찌하여 주인이자 남편을 돕기를 머뭇거리느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"내가 머뭇거리는 것은,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"종자 신분의 자들을 상대로 칼을 뽑는 것이 나에게는 허락되지 않은 일이기 때문이오. 그러니 내 종자 산초를 불러 주시오. 이 방어와 복수는 그의 소관이요 그가 할 일이니 말이오.\"\n\n여관 문간의 사정은 이러했으니, 그곳에서는 주먹질과 매질이 자못 활기차게 오가 여관 주인은 심하게 상하고, 마리토르네스와 안주인과 그 딸은 노여워했다. 그들은 돈키호테의 비겁함과, 주인이자 남편이자 아버지인 자가 당하는 모진 꼴을 보고 분통이 터졌던 것이다. 그러나 그는 거기 내버려 두자. 그를 도와줄 누군가가 분명 나타날 터이고, 그렇지 않다면 제 힘이 닿지 않는 일을 무리하게 떠맡으려는 자는 고통을 겪으며 입을 다물고 있어야 하리라. 우리는 쉰 걸음쯤 뒤로 돌아가, 우리가 두고 온, 판사가 따로 그를 데려가 무슨 까닭으로 걸어서 그토록 초라한 차림으로 왔느냐고 캐묻던 곳에서 돈 루이스가 무어라 대답했는지 살펴보기로 하자.\n\n이에 젊은이는, 어떤 크나큰 슬픔으로 마음이 괴롭다는 것을 드러내듯 판사의 손을 꼭 쥐고 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 대답했다.\n\n\"나리, 제가 더 드릴 말씀은 없습니다. 다만 하늘의 뜻으로, 또 저희가 가까운 이웃인 덕분에, 제가 나리의 따님이자 제 연인이신 도냐 클라라를 처음 본 그 순간부터 저는 그분을 제 마음의 주인으로 삼았으며, 진정한 제 어른이자 아버지이신 나리께서 가로막지만 않으신다면, 바로 오늘 그분을 제 아내로 맞고자 합니다. 그분을 위해 저는 아버지의 집을 떠났고, 그분을 위해 이 변장을 했으니, 화살이 과녁을 좇고 뱃사람이 북극성을 좇듯 그분이 어디로 가시든 따르려 함이었습니다. 그분은 제 열정에 대해, 이따금 멀리서 제 눈에 눈물이 가득 고인 것을 보아 알아챘을 법한 것 말고는 아무것도 모르십니다. 나리께서는 이미 제 부모의 재산과 고귀한 가문, 그리고 제가 그 외아들이라는 것을 알고 계십니다. 만일 이것이 나리께서 저를 온전히 행복하게 해 주시려 마음먹으실 만한 충분한 이유가 된다면, 부디 저를 지금 당장 사위로 받아 주십시오. 설령 제 아버지께서 당신만의 다른 뜻에 이끌려 제가 스스로 구한 이 행복을 못마땅하게 여기신다 해도, 세월은 인간의 의지보다 더 큰 힘으로 일을 뒤바꾸고 변화시키는 법이니까요.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 사랑에 빠진 젊은이는 입을 다물었고, 그 말을 들은 판사는 돈 루이스가 제 마음속 비밀을 털어놓은 그 태도와 영민함에도, 또 자신이 처하게 된 그 입장에도 깜짝 놀라며 어리둥절하고 당혹스러워했으니, 이토록 갑작스럽고 뜻밖의 일에 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 알 수 없었던 것이다. 그리하여 그가 그에게 해 준 대답이라고는, 지금으로서는 마음을 편히 가지고, 하인들과 잘 의논하여 그날은 자기를 데려가지 않도록 해서, 모두에게 무엇이 가장 좋을지 헤아릴 여유를 두라는 것뿐이었다. 돈 루이스는 억지로 그의 손에 입을 맞추었을 뿐 아니라, 대리석 같은 마음이라도 — 영민한 사람으로서 이 혼인이 제 딸에게 얼마나 이로운지 이미 알아챈 판사의 마음은 말할 것도 없고 — 감동시킬 만큼 눈물로 그 손을 적셨다. 다만 가능하기만 하다면, 판사는 아들에게 작위를 바라고 있음을 그가 아는 돈 루이스의 아버지의 동의를 얻어 이 혼인이 이루어지기를 더 바랐다.\n\n그 무렵 손님들은 이미 여관 주인과 화해한 뒤였으니, 협박보다는 설득과 돈키호테의 그럴듯한 말솜씨 덕분에 그가 요구한 셈을 치렀던 것이다. 돈 루이스의 하인들은 판사와의 대화가 끝나고 주인의 결정이 내려지기를 기다리고 있었는데, 결코 잠드는 법이 없는 악마가 일을 꾸며, 돈키호테가 맘브리노의 투구를 빼앗고 산초 판사가 제 당나귀 마구와 맞바꿔 마구를 가져왔던 그 이발사가 바로 이 순간 여관으로 들어서게 만들었다. 그 이발사는 당나귀를 마구간으로 끌고 가다가, 산초 판사가 길마에 딸린 무언가를 손보고 있는 것을 보았는데, 그것을 보는 순간 한눈에 알아보고는 대담하게 산초에게 달려들며 소리쳤다. \"어이, 이 도둑놈아, 내가 너를 잡았다! 네놈이 나한테서 훔쳐 간 내 대야와 길마와 내 마구를 죄다 내놓아라.\"\n\n산초는 이렇게 느닷없이 공격을 당하고 자기에게 퍼붓는 욕설을 듣자, 한 손으로는 길마를 움켜쥐고 다른 손으로는 이발사에게 한 대 갈겨 그 이를 피로 적셨다. 그러나 이발사는 길마라는 전리품을 그리 쉽사리 내줄 생각이 없었으니, 오히려 어찌나 큰 소리로 고함을 질렀던지 여관 안의 모두가 무슨 소란이며 다툼인가 알아보려 달려왔다. \"여기, 국왕과 정의의 이름으로!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이 도둑놈, 노상강도가 제 물건을 되찾으려는 나를 죽이려 합니다!\"\n\n\"거짓말 마라.\" 산초가 말했다. \"나는 노상강도가 아니다. 우리 주인 돈키호테께서 정당한 싸움으로 이 전리품을 얻으신 것이다.\"\n\n마침 곁에 서 있던 돈키호테는 제 종자가 공격에서나 방어에서나 야무진 것을 보고 무척 흐뭇해하며, 그때부터 그를 기개 있는 사내로 여기고는, 기회가 오는 대로 첫 번째 기회에 그를 기사로 서임하리라 마음속으로 작정했으니, 기사도의 위계가 그에게 합당하게 내려지리라 확신했던 것이다.\n\n옥신각신하는 가운데 이발사는 다른 말들과 함께 이렇게 말했다. \"여러분, 제가 하느님께 죽음을 빚지고 있는 것만큼이나 이 길마는 분명 제 것이며, 제가 직접 낳기라도 한 듯 훤히 압니다. 여기 마구간에 제 당나귀가 있으니 그놈이 거짓이 아님을 증명해 줄 겁니다. 한번 채워 보시지요, 만일 장갑처럼 꼭 맞지 않거든 저를 악당이라 부르십시오. 게다가 이것을 도둑맞은 바로 그날, 그놈들은 제 새 놋쇠 대야도 함께 훔쳐 갔는데, 아직 한 번도 써 본 적 없는, 어느 날이든 1크라운은 족히 받을 만한 물건이었습니다.\"\n\n이 말에 돈키호테는 대꾸하지 않을 수 없어, 두 사람 사이에 끼어들어 그들을 떼어 놓고는 진실이 가려질 때까지 모두가 볼 수 있도록 길마를 땅에 내려놓으며 말했다. \"여러분께서는 이 훌륭한 종자가 빠져 있는 잘못을 똑똑히, 그리고 분명히 아실 수 있을 것이오. 그는 내가 정당한 싸움에서 그에게서 빼앗아 적법하고 합법적인 소유로 내 것이 된, 과거에도 맘브리노의 투구였고 지금도 그러하며 앞으로도 그러할 것을 대야라고 부르고 있소. 길마에 대해서는 내가 관여할 바 아니나, 다만 그 점에 관해 말할 수 있는 것은, 내 종자 산초가 이 패배한 겁쟁이의 말에서 마구를 벗겨 제 것을 꾸미게 해 달라고 내게 청하기에 내가 허락했고 그가 그것을 가져갔다는 사실이오. 그것이 마구에서 길마로 바뀐 데 대해서는, 기사도의 모험에서는 그런 변신이 일어나기 마련이라는 흔한 설명 말고는 달리 드릴 말씀이 없소. 이 모든 것을 확인하기 위해, 산초 내 아들아, 어서 달려가 이 선량한 자가 대야라 부르는 그 투구를 이리로 가져오너라.\"\n\n\"이런, 나리.\" 산초가 말했다. \"우리 처지를 증명할 거리가 나리께서 내놓으시는 것밖에 없다면, 맘브리노의 투구는 이 선량한 자의 마구가 길마인 것만큼이나 영락없이 대야입니다요.\"\n\n\"시키는 대로 하여라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이 성의 모든 것이 마법으로 돌아간다고는 할 수 없을 테니 말이다.\"\n\n산초는 대야가 있는 곳으로 달려가 그것을 가지고 돌아왔고, 돈키호테는 그것을 보자 손에 들고 말했다.\n\n\"여러분께서는 이 종자가 무슨 낯짝으로 이것이 대야이지 내가 말한 그 투구가 아니라고 우길 수 있는지 보실 것이오. 그리고 내가 받든 기사도의 위계를 걸고 맹세하건대, 이 투구는 내가 그에게서 빼앗은 바로 그것으로, 거기에 무엇 하나 더하거나 덜어 낸 것이 없소.\"\n\n\"그건 의심할 여지가 없지요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"주인님이 그것을 얻으신 때부터 지금까지 그것을 쓰고 싸우신 전투라고는 단 한 번뿐이니, 그 불운한 쇠사슬에 묶인 자들을 풀어 주셨을 때 말입니다요. 만일 이 대야 투구가 아니었다면 그때 주인님은 그리 무사히 빠져나오지 못하셨을 겁니다요. 그 일에서는 돌팔매질이 어찌나 쏟아졌는지 모르니까요.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XLV.\n\n이발사가 말했다. \"여러분, 이제 어떻게들 생각하십니까? 이 양반들이 이것을 투구라고 우기려 드는 이 일을 두고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"누구든 그 반대를 말하는 자가 있다면,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그가 기사라면 거짓을 말하고 있음을 알려 주겠고, 종자라면 천 번이고 거짓을 말하고 있음을 알려 주겠노라.\"\n\n이 모든 일에 함께 있으면서 돈키호테의 별난 기질을 속속들이 꿰뚫고 있던 우리 편 이발사는, 모두의 흥을 돋우기 위해 그의 망상에 장단을 맞추어 이 익살을 계속 끌고 가야겠다는 생각이 들었다. 그래서 다른 이발사를 향해 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"이발사 양반, 아니 댁이 뭐든 간에, 알아 두시오. 나도 댁과 같은 직업에 몸담은 사람으로, 이십 년 넘게 면허를 받아 일해 왔으니, 이발 일에 쓰는 연장이라면 하나도 빠짐없이 더할 나위 없이 잘 안다오. 그뿐 아니라 젊은 시절 한동안 군인 노릇도 했던지라, 투구가 무엇인지, 챙 없는 투구가 무엇인지, 면갑이 달린 투구가 무엇인지, 그 밖에 군대에 관한 것들, 아니 군인들의 무장에 관한 것들도 잘 안다오. 그래서 내가 말하는데 — 더 나은 견해는 존중하며, 언제나 더 건전한 판단에는 따른다는 전제하에 — 지금 우리 앞에 있는 이 물건, 이 훌륭한 양반이 손에 들고 있는 이것은 이발사의 대야가 아닐 뿐 아니라, 흰빛이 검은빛과 다르고 진실이 거짓과 다른 만큼이나 대야와는 거리가 멀다오. 게다가 내가 말하건대, 이것은 비록 투구이기는 하나 온전한 투구는 아니라오.\"\n\n\"그야 물론 아니지요.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그 절반, 그러니까 턱받이가 빠져 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"정말 맞는 말이군요.\" 친구인 이발사가 무엇을 노리는지 알아챈 신부가 말했다. 카르데니오와 돈 페르난도와 그 일행도 그의 말에 맞장구를 쳤고, 판관조차도 돈 루이스의 일로 머릿속이 그토록 가득 차 있지만 않았더라면 이 익살을 거드는 데 한몫 거들었으련만, 그는 마음에 담아 둔 진지한 문제에 너무나 골몰해 있던 터라 이 우스꽝스러운 일에는 거의, 아니 전혀 관심을 두지 않았다.\n\n이때 그들의 놀림감이 된 이발사가 외쳤다. \"맙소사! 이렇게 점잖은 분들이 이것을 대야가 아니라 투구라고 말씀하시다니, 이게 있을 법한 일입니까? 이건 대학 전체를, 그것이 아무리 지혜롭다 한들 깜짝 놀라게 할 일이올시다! 됐습니다. 이 대야가 투구라면, 그럼 이 양반이 말한 대로 길마는 말의 마갑(馬甲)일 수밖에 없겠군요.\"\n\n\"내게는 길마로 보이는구려.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 그 문제에는 내가 관여하지 않겠다고 이미 말한 바 있소.\"\n\n\"그것이 길마인지 마갑인지에 관해서는,\" 신부가 말했다. \"오직 돈키호테 님께서 판단하실 일입니다. 이런 기사도에 관한 문제에 있어서는 여기 계신 모든 분들과 나는 그분의 권위에 머리를 숙이니까요.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 여러분,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 이 성에 두 번 머무는 동안 하도 기이한 일들을 많이 겪은지라, 이 안에 있는 무엇에 관한 물음이든 단정 지어 답하기를 감히 시도하지 않겠소이다. 그 안에서 벌어지는 모든 일이 마법으로 일어난다는 것이 내 믿음이기 때문이오. 처음에는 이 안에 있는 마법에 걸린 무어인 하나가 나를 심히 괴롭혔고, 산초 또한 어떤 무리의 손에 봉변을 당했소. 그리고 어젯밤에는 이 팔로 두 시간 가까이 매달려 있었으되, 어찌하여 또 무슨 까닭으로 그런 봉변을 당했는지 알지 못하오. 그러니 지금 내가 나서서 이토록 어려운 문제에 의견을 내놓는다면, 이는 경솔한 판단을 무릅쓰는 일이 되겠소. 이것이 투구가 아니라 대야라는 주장에 관해서는 이미 답을 드렸소만, 이것이 길마인지 마갑인지에 관해서는 감히 단정적인 의견을 내놓지 않고 여러분의 더 나은 판단에 맡기겠소. 어쩌면 여러분은 나처럼 기사로 서임되지 않았기에 이곳의 마법이 여러분과는 아무 상관이 없고, 그래서 여러분의 분별력이 얽매이지 않아 이 성 안의 사물을 내게 보이는 대로가 아니라 참되고 진실한 모습 그대로 볼 수 있을지도 모르겠소.\"\n\n이에 돈 페르난도가 말했다. \"돈키호테 님께서 참으로 지혜롭게 말씀하셨고, 이 문제의 판결이 우리에게 달려 있다는 데에는 의문의 여지가 없습니다. 우리가 더 확실한 근거 위에 설 수 있도록, 내가 여러분의 의견을 은밀히 모아 그 결과를 분명하고 빠짐없이 선언하겠습니다.\"\n\n돈키호테의 별난 기질을 속속들이 아는 이들에게는 이 모든 것이 큰 즐거움을 주었으나, 그것을 전혀 모르는 이들에게는 세상에서 가장 터무니없는 헛소리로만 보였으니, 특히 돈 루이스의 하인 넷과 돈 루이스 본인, 그리고 우연히 객줏집에 들른 다른 세 나그네에게 그러했다. 그 세 사람은 산타 에르만다드의 관원처럼 보였는데 실제로도 그러했다. 그러나 그 누구보다도 어찌할 바를 모른 것은 그 이발사였으니, 바로 그의 눈앞에서 자기 대야가 맘브리노의 투구로 둔갑하고, 자기 길마가 의심할 여지 없이 곧 호화로운 말의 마갑으로 바뀌려는 참이었던 것이다. 돈 페르난도가 이 사람 저 사람을 돌며 표를 거두면서, 그토록 다투어 온 이 보물이 길마인지 마갑인지 각자의 은밀한 의견을 일러 달라고 귓속말로 속삭이는 모습을 보고 모두가 웃었다. 그런데 돈키호테를 아는 이들의 표를 다 거둔 뒤 그는 큰 소리로 말했다. \"이보게, 실은 이렇게 많은 의견을 거두느라 지쳤네. 내가 알고자 하는 바를 묻는 이마다 하나같이, 이것을 말의 마갑이, 그것도 순종 말의 마갑이 아니라 당나귀의 길마라고 하는 것은 터무니없는 소리라고 답하지 않는 이가 없으니 말일세. 그러니 자네는 승복해야 하네. 자네와 자네 당나귀가 아무리 떼를 써도 이것은 길마가 아니라 마갑이며, 자네는 자네 주장을 아주 형편없이 펼치고 또 입증한 셈이니 말일세.\"\n\n\"제가 천국을 누리지 못해도 좋습니다.\" 가엾은 이발사가 말했다. \"여러분 모두가 잘못 보신 게 아니라면 말입니다. 또 제 영혼이 하느님 앞에 나아가지 못해도 좋습니다. 제게 저것이 마갑이 아니라 길마로 보이지 않는다면 말입니다. 하지만 '법은 법대로 가는 법'이니 — 더는 말하지 않겠습니다. 그리고 저는 정말이지 취하지 않았습니다. 저는 단식 중이거든요, 죄에서 단식하는 것만 빼면 말입니다.\"\n\n이발사의 천진한 말은 돈키호테의 터무니없는 소리 못지않은 즐거움을 안겨 주었는데, 이번에는 돈키호테가 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"이제 더 할 일이라고는, 각자 자기 것을 가져가는 것뿐이오. 하느님께서 그것을 누구에게 주셨든, 성 베드로께서 그분의 축복을 더해 주시기를.\"\n\n그러나 네 하인 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"이것이 작정하고 꾸민 농담이 아닌 다음에야, 여기 계신 분들처럼 지혜롭거나 지혜로워 보이는 분들이 감히 이것을 대야가 아니라거니 저것을 길마가 아니라거니 선언하고 단언하실 수 있다는 것을, 저로서는 도무지 믿을 수가 없습니다. 하지만 그분들이 정말로 그렇게 단언하고 선언하시는 걸 보니, 저는 다만, 경험과 진실 그 자체의 증거에 그토록 어긋나는 것을 이렇게 고집하는 데에는 무슨 까닭이 숨어 있으리라는 결론에 이를 수밖에 없군요. 맹세코\" — 여기서 그는 걸쭉한 욕설을 한바탕 내뱉었다 — \"세상 모든 사람이 달려들어도 저로 하여금 이것이 이발사의 대야가 아니고 저것이 당나귀의 길마가 아니라고 믿게 만들지는 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그게 암나귀 것일 수도 있지요.\" 신부가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그게 그거지요.\" 하인이 말했다. \"그게 요점이 아니라, 여러분 말씀처럼 그것이 길마냐 아니냐 하는 게 요점이지요.\"\n\n이 말을 듣고, 그 다툼과 옥신각신을 듣고 있던, 새로 도착한 산타 에르만다드의 관원 가운데 하나가 분노와 조바심을 누르지 못하고 외쳤다. \"내 아버지가 내 아버지인 것만큼이나 분명히 이건 길마요. 그리고 다른 소리를 했거나 앞으로 할 자는 술에 취한 게 틀림없소.\"\n\n\"이 비열한 촌놈 같으니, 거짓을 지껄이는구나.\" 돈키호테가 받아쳤다. 그러고는 한 번도 손에서 놓지 않았던 장창을 치켜들어 관원의 머리를 향해 어찌나 세게 후려쳤던지, 관원이 몸을 피하지 않았더라면 그를 그 자리에 길게 뻗게 만들었을 것이다. 장창은 땅바닥에 부딪쳐 산산조각이 났고, 동료가 공격당하는 것을 본 나머지 관원들은 고함을 지르며 산타 에르만다드에 도움을 청했다. 그 형제단의 일원이던 객줏집 주인은 즉시 직권을 나타내는 지팡이와 칼을 가지러 달려가 동료들 편에 가담했다. 돈 루이스의 하인들은 그가 이 혼란을 틈타 빠져나가지 못하도록 그를 빙 둘러쌌다. 집 안이 발칵 뒤집힌 것을 본 이발사는 다시 한번 자기 길마를 움켜잡았고 산초도 똑같이 했다. 돈키호테는 칼을 빼어 들고 관원들에게 돌진했다. 돈 루이스는 하인들에게 자기를 내버려 두고 가서 돈키호테와, 그를 거들고 있는 카르데니오와 돈 페르난도를 도우라고 소리쳤다. 신부는 목청껏 고함을 지르고, 안주인은 비명을 질러 대고, 그 딸은 울부짖고, 마리토르네스는 흐느끼고, 도로테아는 아연실색하고, 루신다는 공포에 사로잡히고, 도냐 클라라는 까무러쳤다. 이발사는 산초를 몽둥이질하고, 산초는 이발사를 주먹으로 쥐어박았다. 돈 루이스는 자기를 붙잡아 도망치지 못하게 하려고 감히 팔을 움켜쥔 하인 하나에게 주먹을 한 방 먹여 그 이빨을 피로 적셨다. 판관이 그를 거들었다. 돈 페르난도는 관원 하나를 쓰러뜨려 흠씬 두들겨 패고 있었다. 객줏집 주인은 다시 목소리를 높여 산타 에르만다드에 도움을 청했다. 그리하여 객줏집 안은 온통 울부짖음과 고함과 비명과 혼란과 공포와 경악과 봉변과 칼부림과 주먹질과 몽둥이질과 발길질과 유혈뿐이었다. 그리고 이 모든 아수라장과 뒤엉킴과 총체적 난장판 한가운데서, 돈키호테는 자신이 아그라만테 진영의 불화 한복판에 던져졌다는 생각이 머릿속에 들었다. 그래서 객줏집을 천둥처럼 뒤흔드는 목소리로 그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"모두 멈추라, 모두 칼을 거두라, 모두 진정하고, 목숨을 아낀다면 내 말에 귀를 기울이라!\"\n\n그 우렁찬 목소리에 모두가 멈추자, 그는 이어서 말했다. \"내가 여러분에게 말하지 않았소이까, 여러분, 이 성이 마법에 걸렸으며 그 안에 일개 군단쯤 되는 악마들이 깃들어 있다고 말이오? 그 증거로, 아그라만테 진영의 불화가 어떻게 이리로 옮겨 와 우리 한복판에 자리 잡았는지 여러분의 두 눈으로 직접 보시라고 청하는 바요. 보시오, 저들이 어떻게 싸우는지를. 저기서는 칼을 두고, 여기서는 말을 두고, 저쪽에서는 독수리를 두고, 이쪽에서는 투구를 두고 싸우고 있소. 우리 모두가 싸우고 있고, 모두가 서로 엇갈려 있소. 자, 그러니 판관 님과 신부 님께서 이리 오시오. 한 분은 아그라만테 왕을, 다른 한 분은 소브리노 왕을 맡으셔서 우리 사이에 화평을 이루어 주시오. 전능하신 하느님께 맹세코, 우리처럼 지체 높은 사람들이 이토록 하찮은 까닭으로 서로를 죽이려 든다는 것은 참으로 한심한 노릇이외다.\" 돈키호테의 말투를 알아듣지 못하고, 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오와 그 일행에게 호되게 당한 처지였던 관원들은 좀처럼 진정하려 들지 않았다. 그러나 이발사는 진정했으니, 수염도 길마도 그 다툼으로 더 망가진 탓이었다. 산초는 충실한 종답게 주인의 말 한마디 한마디에 고분고분 따랐고, 돈 루이스의 네 하인은 그러지 않아 봐야 득 될 것이 없음을 보고 잠잠해졌다. 오직 객줏집 주인만이, 걸핏하면 객줏집에 소동을 일으키는 이 미친자의 무례함을 반드시 벌해야 한다고 우겨 댔다. 그러나 마침내 소란은 당분간 잠잠해졌고, 길마는 최후의 심판 날까지 마갑으로 남았으며, 대야는 투구로, 객줏집은 성으로 돈키호테의 상상 속에 남았다.\n\n판관과 신부의 설득으로 모두가 진정되어 화해한 뒤, 돈 루이스의 하인들은 다시 그에게 당장 자기들과 함께 돌아가자고 재촉하기 시작했다. 그가 그들과 그 문제를 의논하는 동안, 판관은 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오와 신부에게 이 일을 어떻게 처리해야 할지 의논을 구하며, 사정이 어떠한지와 돈 루이스가 자기에게 한 말을 일러 주었다. 마침내, 돈 페르난도가 돈 루이스의 하인들에게 자신이 누구인지를 밝히고, 돈 루이스가 자기를 따라 안달루시아로 가는 것이 자기 뜻이며, 거기서 그가 자기 형인 후작에게서 그의 지체에 걸맞은 환대를 받게 되리라고 말하기로 합의가 이루어졌다. 그러지 않으면, 돈 루이스가 비록 갈가리 찢긴다 해도 지금은 아버지에게 돌아가지 않으리라는 그 단호함으로 보아, 사정이 어떨지는 빤히 알 수 있었기 때문이다. 돈 페르난도의 지위와 돈 루이스의 결심을 알게 된 네 하인은 자기들끼리 이렇게 정했으니, 그중 셋은 돌아가 아버지에게 사정이 어떠한지 알리고, 나머지 하나는 남아서 돈 루이스의 시중을 들며, 그들이 그를 데리러 돌아오거나 아버지의 분부를 알게 될 때까지 그의 곁을 떠나지 않기로 했다. 이렇게 아그라만테의 권위와 소브리노 왕의 지혜로 이 모든 얽히고설킨 다툼이 가지런히 정리되었다. 그러나 화합의 적이요 평화를 미워하는 자는, 자신이 무시당하고 우롱당했다고 느끼며, 그 모두를 그토록 공들인 뒤엉킴 속에 몰아넣고도 얻은 것이 거의 없음을 보고서, 다시 한번 새로운 다툼과 소동을 부추겨 솜씨를 발휘해 보기로 작정했다.\n\n일은 이렇게 벌어졌다. 관원들은 자기들이 맞붙은 상대의 지위를 알고는 진정하여, 결과가 어찌 되든 이 싸움에서 자기들이 가장 큰 손해를 보기 쉽다고 여겨 다툼에서 물러났다. 그러나 그들 가운데 하나, 곧 돈 페르난도에게 두들겨 맞고 걷어차인 그 관원이, 자기가 지닌 몇몇 범죄자 체포 영장 가운데 돈키호테를 잡으라는 영장이 하나 있다는 사실을 떠올렸다. 산초가 아주 그럴 만한 까닭으로 염려했던 대로, 산타 에르만다드는 갤리선 노예들을 풀어 준 죄로 돈키호테를 체포하라고 명했던 것이다. 그래서 사정이 어떠한지를 짐작한 그는 돈키호테의 용모가 일치하는지 확인하고 싶어졌다. 그는 품에서 양피지 한 장을 꺼내어 찾던 것을 찾아내고는, 글 읽는 솜씨가 빠르지 못한 터라 천천히 읽기 시작하여, 한 단어 한 단어 더듬어 가며 돈키호테에게 눈길을 고정하고는, 영장에 적힌 인상착의를 그의 얼굴과 견주어 보았다. 그리하여 그가 의심할 여지 없이 거기 묘사된 바로 그 인물임을 알아냈다. 확신을 얻자마자 그는 양피지를 접어 왼손에 영장을 들고 오른손으로 돈키호테의 멱살을 어찌나 단단히 거머쥐었던지 숨도 쉬지 못하게 하고는 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"산타 에르만다드에 도움을 청하노라! 그리고 내가 진심으로 청하는 것임을 알고자 한다면, 이 노상강도를 체포하라고 적힌 이 영장을 읽어 보시오.\"\n\n신부가 영장을 받아 보니 관원의 말이 사실이며 돈키호테의 모습과 일치했다. 한편 돈키호테로 말하자면, 이 비열한 촌놈에게 거칠게 다루어지는 처지가 되자 분노가 극에 달해, 격노로 온 관절이 우두둑거리며 두 손으로 있는 힘껏 관원의 목을 거머쥐었으니, 만일 동료들이 돕지 않았더라면 그는 돈키호테가 손을 놓기도 전에 목숨을 내놓고 말았을 것이다. 부득이 동료 관원들을 거들어야 했던 객줏집 주인이 즉시 그들을 도우러 달려갔다. 남편이 또다시 싸움에 휘말린 것을 본 안주인은 새삼 목소리를 높였고, 그 가락을 마리토르네스와 그 딸이 곧장 받아, 하늘과 그 자리에 있는 모든 이에게 도움을 청했다. 벌어지는 일을 본 산초가 외쳤다. \"맹세코, 이 성의 마법에 관한 우리 주인 나리 말씀이 다 옳구먼. 여기서는 단 한 시간도 편안히 살 수가 없으니 말이야!\"\n\n돈 페르난도가 관원과 돈키호테를 떼어 놓고, 양쪽 다 만족하도록 한쪽은 멱살을, 다른 쪽은 상대의 목을 쥐고 있던 손아귀의 힘을 풀게 했다. 그래도 관원들은 자기들의 죄수를 내놓으라고 요구하기를 멈추지 않고 도와 달라 외치며, 국왕과 산타 에르만다드를 위해 요구되는 대로 그를 결박하여 자기들 손에 넘기라고 했다. 그러면서 그들은 이 도둑이자 노상강도를 붙잡을 수 있도록 다시 한번 산타 에르만다드를 대신하여 지원과 협조를 요청했다.\n\n돈키호테는 이 말을 듣고 빙긋 웃더니 아주 침착하게 말했다. \"자, 이리 와 보라, 비천하고 천한 핏줄의 무리여. 속박된 자들에게 자유를 주고, 사로잡힌 이들을 풀어 주고, 비참한 이들을 구하고, 쓰러진 이들을 일으켜 세우고, 궁핍한 이들을 돕는 것을 너희가 노상강도질이라 부르느냐? 파렴치한 것들이여, 너희의 그 천박하고 비루한 지력 때문에, 하늘이 너희에게 편력 기사도에 깃든 미덕을 알려 주지 않아 마땅하고, 어떤 편력 기사의 현존은 말할 것도 없고 그 그림자조차 존중하기를 거부하는 너희가 어떤 죄와 무지 속에 빠져 있는지를 보여 주지 않아 마땅하도다! 자, 이리 와 보라. 관원이 아니라 도둑의 무리여, 산타 에르만다드의 면허를 받은 노상강도들이여. 나 같은 기사를 잡으라는 체포 영장에 서명한 그 무지렁이가 대체 누구인지 내게 말해 보라. 편력 기사가 모든 사법권에서 벗어나 있으며, 그들의 법은 그들의 칼이요, 그들의 헌장은 그들의 무용이요, 그들의 칙령은 그들의 의지임을 모르는 자가 대체 누구더냐? 다시 묻거니와, 편력 기사가 기사로 서임되어 기사도라는 그 고된 소명에 헌신하는 그날 얻게 되는 특권이나 면제만 한 것을 부여하는 귀족 특허장은 어디에도 없음을 모르는 그 어리석은 자가 대체 누구더냐? 어느 편력 기사가 인두세나, 통행세나, 왕비의 용돈이나, 국왕의 세금이나, 통과세나, 나룻세를 낸 적이 있더냐? 어느 재단사가 그의 옷을 지어 주고 삯을 받은 적이 있더냐? 자기 성에 그를 맞아들인 어느 성주가 그에게 셈을 치르게 한 적이 있더냐? 어느 왕이 그를 자기 식탁에 앉히지 않았더냐? 어느 처녀가 그에게 반하여 자신을 온전히 그의 뜻과 기쁨에 내맡기지 않았더냐? 그리고 마지막으로, 산타 에르만다드의 관원 사백 명이 제 앞길을 막아선다면 혼자서 그 사백 명에게 사백 번의 몽둥이질을 안겨 줄 만큼 담대하지 못한 편력 기사가 이 세상에 있었던 적이, 또 있을 적이 대체 어디 있더냐?\"\n\n# CHAPTER XLVI.\n\n돈키호테가 이런 투로 이야기하는 동안, 신부는 그가 제정신이 아님을 그의 행동과 말에서 알 수 있을 것이라며 성동포단 관원들을 설득하려 애썼고, 더 이상 이 일을 밀어붙일 필요가 없다고, 설령 그를 체포해 데려간다 한들 미치광이라 하여 머지않아 풀어 주어야 할 터이니 그렇다고 일렀다. 이에 영장을 쥔 자는 돈키호테가 미쳤는지 아닌지를 따지는 일은 자기와 상관없는 노릇이며, 자기는 그저 상관의 명령을 집행할 뿐이고, 일단 잡아넣은 뒤에는 그쪽에서 원한다면 삼백 번이라도 놓아주든 말든 마음대로 하라고 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그렇다 해도,\" 신부가 말했다. \"이번만은 그를 데려가서는 안 될 것이오. 또 내 생각엔 그가 순순히 끌려가도록 가만있지도 않을 것이고.\"\n\n요컨대 신부가 이런저런 논리를 펴고 돈키호테가 어찌나 미친 짓들을 해 댔던지, 관원들이 만일 그의 모자란 지각을 알아채지 못했다면 그보다 더 미친 자들이 되었을 판이었다. 그리하여 그들은 마음을 누그러뜨리는 편이 상책이라 여겼고, 나아가 아직도 모진 악다구니로 옥신각신하고 있던 이발사와 산초 판사 사이를 중재하는 노릇까지 맡기로 했다. 결국 그들은 법을 집행하는 관원답게 양쪽이 완전히 만족하지는 못하더라도 적어도 어느 정도는 흡족하도록 중재로 문제를 매듭지었으니, 길마는 서로 바꾸되 뱃대끈이나 굴레는 바꾸지 않기로 했다. 그리고 맘브리노의 투구에 대해서는, 신부가 돈키호테 모르게 슬그머니 대야 값으로 8레알을 치렀고, 이발사는 영수증을 끝까지 작성하여 지금이나 앞으로나 영원토록 더는 아무런 청구도 하지 않겠노라 다짐했으니, 아멘이었다. 가장 중대하고 심각하던 이 두 분쟁이 해결되고 나자, 이제 남은 일이라곤 돈루이스의 하인들이 셋은 돌아가고 하나만 남아 돈페르난도가 데려가고자 하는 곳까지 그를 따르도록 동의하는 것뿐이었다. 그리고 행운과 더 나은 운명이 이미 객줏집의 연인들과 무사들을 위하여 곤경을 풀고 장애를 치워 주기 시작했던 터라, 끝까지 그 일을 밀고 나가 모든 것을 행복한 결말로 이끌어 주기를 기꺼워했으니, 하인들이 돈루이스가 바라는 대로 하기로 동의했던 것이다. 이에 도냐클라라는 어찌나 행복했던지, 그 순간 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다본 사람이라면 누구든 그 가슴속 기쁨을 보지 않을 수 없었을 것이다. 소라이다는 비록 자기가 보는 모든 것을 온전히 헤아리지는 못했으나, 까닭도 모른 채 진지해지기도 하고 즐거워지기도 하면서, 여러 사람의 얼굴을, 특히 자기 눈으로 좇고 영혼으로 매달리던 그 에스파냐 사람의 얼굴을 지켜보며 살폈다. 신부가 이발사에게 준 선물이자 보상은 주인장의 눈을 벗어나지 못했으니, 그는 돈키호테의 셈과 더불어 자기 가죽 부대가 입은 손해와 흘려 버린 포도주 값까지 청구하면서, 마지막 한 푼까지 받아 내기 전에는 로시난테도 산초의 나귀도 객줏집을 떠나지 못하리라 맹세했다. 신부가 모든 것을 원만히 처리하고 돈페르난도가 값을 치렀다. 비록 판사도 기꺼이 셈을 치르겠노라 선뜻 나섰지만 말이다. 그리하여 모두가 어찌나 평화롭고 고요해졌던지, 돈키호테의 말마따나 객줏집은 더 이상 아그라만테 진영의 불화를 떠올리게 하지 않고 옥타비아누스 시절의 평화와 안녕을 떠올리게 했다. 이 모든 일에 대해 그 공이 신부의 크나큰 열성과 능변에, 그리고 돈페르난도의 유례없는 너그러움에 돌아가야 한다는 것이 모두의 한결같은 의견이었다.\n\n이제 자기 것이든 종자의 것이든 모든 다툼에서 깨끗이 풀려난 것을 알게 된 돈키호테는, 시작했던 여정을 계속하여 자기가 부름받고 뽑힌 그 위대한 모험을 끝맺는 것이 좋겠다고 생각했다. 이 드높은 결심을 품고 그는 도로테아 앞에 나아가 무릎을 꿇었는데, 그녀는 그가 일어서기 전에는 한마디도 입을 떼지 못하게 했다. 하여 그 명을 따라 그는 일어나서 말했다. \"아름다운 공주님, '근면은 행운의 어머니'라는 흔한 속담이 있고, 또 중대한 일들에서 협상하는 자의 열성이 의심스러운 사안을 성공적인 결말로 이끈다는 것을 경험이 자주 보여 주었습니다. 그러나 이 진리가 가장 분명히 드러나는 것은 다름 아닌 전쟁에서이니, 거기서는 신속함과 민첩함이 적의 책략을 미리 막아 내고, 적이 미처 방비할 틈을 갖기도 전에 승리를 거두게 마련입니다. 고귀하고 존경받는 공주님, 제가 이 모든 말씀을 드리는 까닭은, 우리가 지금 이 성에 더 머무는 것이 무익할뿐더러 언젠가 알게 될 어떤 방식으로 우리에게 해로울 수도 있다고 여겨지기 때문입니다. 누가 압니까, 공주님의 원수인 그 거인이 은밀하고 부지런한 첩자들을 통해 제가 자기를 무찌르러 간다는 것을 알아챘을지, 그리고 기회가 주어진다면 그 틈을 타 난공불락의 성이나 요새에 틀어박혀, 저의 온갖 노력과 지칠 줄 모르는 제 팔의 힘으로도 어쩌지 못하게 만들지 말입니다. 그러니 공주님, 제가 말씀드린 대로 우리의 민첩함으로 그자의 책략을 미리 막고, 당장 떠나 행운을 찾아 나섭시다. 공주님께서 바라시는 만큼 흡족하게 그 행운을 누리지 못하시는 것은 오로지 제가 공주님의 적수와 맞붙기를 미루고 있는 탓이니 말입니다.\"\n\n돈키호테는 입을 다물고 더는 말하지 않은 채, 아리따운 공주의 대답을 차분히 기다렸다. 공주는 위엄을 갖춘 당당한 태도로, 돈키호테 자신의 말투에 걸맞은 투로 이렇게 답했다. \"기사님, 고아와 궁핍한 자를 돕는 것이 천성의 의무인 훌륭한 기사답게, 이 쓰라린 곤경에 처한 저를 도우려 보여 주시는 그 열의에 감사드립니다. 부디 하늘이 기사님과 저의 소원을 이루어 주시어, 이 세상에 감사할 줄 아는 여인이 있음을 기사님께서 보실 수 있기를 바랍니다. 제 출발로 말하자면, 당장 떠나도록 하지요. 저에게는 기사님의 뜻 외에 다른 뜻이 없으니, 기사님 좋으실 대로 저를 온전히 처분하소서. 일찍이 기사님께 제 한 몸의 보호를 맡기고 제 영토의 회복을 그 손에 맡긴 여인으로서, 기사님의 지혜가 명하시는 바에 거스를 생각은 결코 품지 않을 것이옵니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 하느님의 이름으로 나아갑시다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"한 귀부인이 제게 몸을 낮추실 때, 저는 그분을 일으켜 세워 조상의 옥좌에 앉혀 드릴 기회를 놓치지 않겠습니다. 당장 떠납시다. 지체하는 데 위험이 도사린다는 흔한 말이 길을 떠나려는 제 열의에 박차를 가하니 말입니다. 하늘도 저를 두렵게 하거나 겁먹게 할 자를 창조하지 않았고 지옥도 그런 자를 본 적이 없으니, 산초야, 로시난테에 안장을 얹고, 네 나귀와 여왕님의 말도 채비하여, 성주님과 이 신사분들께 작별을 고하고 바로 이 순간 떠나도록 하자.\"\n\n내내 곁에 서 있던 산초가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"아이고, 주인 나리, 나리, 착한 분들께는 죄송한 말씀이지만, 동네에는 소문으로 들리는 것보다 훨씬 더 못된 일들이 있는 법이라고요.\"\n\n\"이 멍청아, 어느 동네에서든, 아니 세상의 모든 도시를 통틀어서든, 내 명예를 해칠 만한 못된 일이 무엇이 있단 말이냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다.\n\n\"나리께서 화를 내신다면,\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"저는 입을 다물고, 훌륭한 종자로서 마땅히 드려야 할 말씀, 또 충직한 하인이 제 주인께 일러 드려야 할 말씀을 하지 않고 그냥 두겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"무엇이든 하고 싶은 말을 하거라.\" 돈키호테가 받았다. \"다만 네 말이 내 두려움을 부추기려는 것만 아니라면 말이다. 네가 두려워한다면 그건 너다운 처신이요, 나는 두려워하지 않으니 나다운 것이로다.\"\n\n\"하느님 앞의 죄인인 제가 말씀드리건대, 그런 게 결코 아닙니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"다만 제 생각엔, 자기를 위대한 미코미콘 왕국의 여왕이라 일컫는 이 부인이 제 어머니가 여왕인 것이나 진배없다는 게 확실하고 분명하다는 겁니다요. 만약 부인이 정말 그렇게 말씀하시는 분이라면, 여기 매 순간 같이 있는 어떤 사람이랑, 그것도 문짝 뒤에서마다 코를 비벼 대고 있지는 않으실 테니까요.\"\n\n산초의 말에 도로테아의 얼굴이 붉어졌으니, 사실인즉 그녀의 남편 돈페르난도가 이따금 다른 이들이 보지 않을 때 그녀의 입술에서 제 사랑이 얻어 낸 보답의 일부를 거두어들였던 것이고, 이를 본 산초가 그런 스스럼없는 행동은 위대한 왕국의 여왕보다는 화류계 여인에게나 어울린다고 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 그에게 대꾸할 수 없었던 건지 아니면 대꾸하기를 개의치 않았던 건지, 그냥 계속 지껄이게 내버려 두었고, 그는 말을 이었다. \"제가 이런 말씀을 드리는 까닭은요, 나리, 우리가 큰길 작은 길 가리지 않고 두루 돌아다니고, 험한 밤과 더 험한 낮을 겪고 나서, 지금 이 객줏집에서 흥겹게 놀고 있는 어떤 자가 우리 수고의 열매를 거두어 갈 거라면, 제가 서둘러 로시난테에 안장을 얹고 나귀에 길마를 채우고 말을 채비할 필요가 없다는 거지요. 우리는 가만히 머무르면서, 갈보들은 제 실 잣는 일이나 신경 쓰게 두고, 우리는 밥이나 먹으러 가는 편이 더 나을 테니까요.\"\n\n맙소사, 자기 종자의 그 무엄한 말을 들었을 때 돈키호테의 분노가 어떠했겠는가! 어찌나 컸던지, 그는 분노로 말이 제대로 나오지 않는 목소리로, 혀를 더듬거리며, 생생한 불길이 번뜩이는 눈으로 외쳤다. \"이 못돼 먹은 촌놈, 무지렁이에다 건방지고 무식하며, 입버릇 고약하고 입 더러우며, 뻔뻔스러운 험담꾼이요 비방꾼 같으니라고! 네 감히 내 앞에서, 그리고 이 고귀하신 부인들 앞에서 그따위 말을 입에 담느냐? 네 감히 그 흐리멍덩한 머릿속에 그토록 천하고 파렴치한 생각을 품는단 말이냐? 내 앞에서 썩 꺼지거라, 이 타고난 괴물 같은 놈, 거짓의 곳간이요 허위의 창고요 악행의 곡간이며, 추문을 지어내는 자요 망발을 퍼뜨리는 자요, 왕가에 마땅히 바칠 존경의 원수 같은 놈아! 꺼져라, 내 노여움을 사기 싫거든 다시는 내 앞에 모습을 보이지 마라.\" 이렇게 말하면서 그는 눈썹을 찌푸리고 두 볼을 부풀리며 주위를 둘러보더니, 오른발로 땅을 사납게 굴러, 가슴속에 응어리진 분노를 온갖 방식으로 드러냈다. 그의 말과 격분한 몸짓에 산초는 어찌나 겁에 질리고 두려웠던지, 그 순간 땅이 갈라져 자기를 삼켜 주었으면 좋겠다 싶을 지경이었으니, 그의 머릿속에는 오직 돌아서서 노여워하는 주인 앞에서 달아날 생각뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 이때쯤이면 돈키호테의 성미를 너무도 잘 헤아리고 있던 재치 있는 도로테아가 그의 노여움을 누그러뜨리려고 말했다. \"슬픈 몰골의 기사님, 충직한 종자가 내뱉은 망발에 노여워하지 마십시오. 어쩌면 그가 까닭 없이 그런 말을 한 것은 아닐 테고, 그의 건전한 분별력과 기독교도다운 양심으로 보아 누구에게든 거짓 증언을 할 사람 같지는 않으니 말입니다. 그러니 우리는 조금도 망설일 것 없이 이렇게 믿어도 좋을 것입니다. 기사님께서 말씀하신 대로 이 성안의 모든 일이 마법으로 행해지고 이루어지는 것이라면, 산초도 그 악마 같은 매개를 통해 제 정숙함을 그토록 해칠 만한 광경을 정말로 보았을 수도 있다고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"전능하신 하느님께 맹세코,\" 이 말에 돈키호테가 외쳤다. \"공주님께서 정곡을 찌르셨습니다. 이 죄인 같은 산초 앞에 어떤 사악한 환영이 나타나, 마법이 아니고서는 도저히 볼 수 없었을 것을 그에게 보이게 한 게 분명합니다. 저 가엾은 녀석의 선량함과 무해함으로 보아, 그가 누구에게든 거짓 증언을 할 위인이 못 된다는 것을 저는 잘 알고 있으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"의심할 바 없이 옳은 말씀이오.\" 돈페르난도가 말했다. \"그러니 돈키호테 님, 그대는 마땅히 그를 용서하고, 이런 종류의 환영이 그의 지각을 앗아 가기 전 그대로(시쿠트 에라트 인 프린키피오), 그를 그대의 은총의 품으로 되돌려 주셔야 할 것이오.\"\n\n* 시쿠트 에라트 인 프린키피오(sicut erat in principio): 라틴어로 \"처음에 그러했던 것처럼\"이라는 뜻.\n돈키호테가 그를 용서할 마음이 있다고 말하자, 신부가 산초를 데리러 갔다. 산초는 매우 겸손하게 들어와 무릎을 꿇고 주인의 손을 청했고, 주인은 그 손을 내밀어 입맞추게 한 다음, 그에게 축복을 내리며 말했다. \"자, 내 아들 산초야, 이제 너도 내가 너에게 여러 번 일러 준 말의 진실을 확신하게 될 게다. 이 성안의 모든 일은 마법으로 이루어진다는 것을 말이다.\"\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"다만 그 담요 키질 사건만은 빼고요. 그건 평범한 방법으로 실제로 일어난 일이었으니까요.\"\n\n\"믿지 마라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"만일 그게 그랬다면, 나는 그 순간에, 아니 지금이라도 네 원수를 갚아 주었을 게다. 하나 그때도 지금도 나는 그럴 수 없었고, 네 모욕을 갚아 줄 상대를 본 적도 없으니 말이다.\"\n\n모두들 그 담요 키질 사건이 무엇인지 알고 싶어 했고, 주인장이 산초가 공중에 키질당하던 이야기를 시시콜콜 들려주자 다들 적잖이 웃었다. 만일 주인이 다시 한번 그 모든 것이 마법이라고 단언해 주지 않았더라면 산초도 그에 못지않게 무안했을 터였다. 그러나 산초의 단순함이 결코 그렇게까지 높은 경지에 이르지는 못하여, 자기가 주인이 믿고 우기는 대로 환영이나 상상 속의 유령들이 아니라 살과 피를 가진 자들에게 담요 키질을 당했다는 것이, 아무런 속임수도 끼어들지 않은 명백하고 단순한 진실이 아니라고 스스로를 납득시킬 수는 없었다.\n\n이 고귀한 일행은 이제 객줏집에서 이틀을 묵었고, 떠날 때가 된 듯 여겨지자, 도로테아와 돈페르난도가 미코미코나 여왕을 복위시킨다는 구실로 돈키호테와 함께 그의 마을까지 되돌아가는 수고를 끼치지 않으면서도, 신부와 이발사가 애초에 작정했던 대로 그를 데려가, 신부가 집에서 그의 광증을 다스릴 수 있도록 하는 계획을 궁리해 냈다. 그리하여 그 계획에 따라, 마침 그 길로 지나가던 어느 소달구지 주인과 흥정하여 이런 식으로 그를 운반하기로 했다. 그들은 나무 살창으로 우리 같은 것을 만들었는데, 돈키호테를 넉넉히 들어앉힐 만큼 컸다. 그러고 나서 돈페르난도와 그 일행, 돈루이스의 하인들, 성동포단 관원들, 그리고 주인장까지, 신부의 지시와 조언에 따라 얼굴을 가리고 저마다 이렇게 저렇게 변장을 하여, 돈키호테에게는 성안에서 보았던 사람들과는 전혀 딴판으로 보이게 했다. 이렇게 한 다음, 그들은 깊은 침묵 속에서 그가 지난번 싸움들을 치른 뒤 잠들어 쉬고 있는 방으로 들어가, 그가 이런 일이 벌어지리라곤 꿈에도 모른 채 평온히 잠든 곳으로 다가가서, 그를 단단히 붙잡아 손발을 꽁꽁 묶었다. 하여 그가 깜짝 놀라 깨어났을 때는 꼼짝달싹할 수 없었고, 그저 눈앞에 보이는 괴이한 형상들에 어리둥절해 의아해할 따름이었다. 이에 그는 곧장, 그의 미친 상상이 어김없이 떠올리던 그 생각에 빠져들어, 이 모든 형상이 마법에 걸린 성의 유령들이며, 자기 자신도 움직일 수도 어쩔 수도 없으니 의심할 바 없이 마법에 걸린 것이라고 머릿속에 단정해 버렸다. 그것이야말로 이 계략을 꾸민 신부가 일어나리라 예상했던 바로 그것이었다. 거기 있던 모든 이 가운데 산초만이 유일하게 제정신이요 제 본래 성품 그대로였는데, 그는 비록 주인의 병에 거의 함께 빠져들 뻔하긴 했어도, 이 변장한 형상들이 모두 누구인지 알아채지 못하지는 않았다. 그러나 그는 주인을 이렇게 습격하고 사로잡은 일이 어찌 되는지 지켜볼 때까지는 감히 입을 떼지 못했고, 주인 역시 자기에게 닥친 재난의 결말을 기다리며 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그 결말인즉, 그들이 우리를 들여와 그를 그 안에 가두고 살창을 어찌나 단단히 못 박았던지 쉬이 부수고 나올 수 없게 만든 것이었다.\n\n그러고 나서 그들이 그를 어깨에 둘러메고 방을 나서는데, 무시무시한 목소리가 — 길마 다툼을 벌인 그 이발사가 아니라 다른 이발사가 낼 수 있는 한껏 무시무시한 목소리로 — 이렇게 말하는 것이 들려왔다. \"오, 슬픈 몰골의 기사여, 그대가 처한 이 사로잡힘을 괴로워하지 말지어다. 이는 그대의 위대한 가슴이 떠맡은 모험을 더 빨리 이루기 위하여 마땅히 그리되어야 하는 일이니라. 그 모험은 사납게 날뛰는 라만차의 사자와 새하얀 토보소의 비둘기가, 먼저 그 거만한 목을 혼인의 부드러운 멍에에 숙인 다음 서로 맺어질 때 이루어지리라. 그리고 이 경이로운 결합에서 용맹한 아비의 게걸스러운 발톱에 견줄 만한 늠름한 새끼들이 세상의 빛 속으로 태어나리라. 또 이는 달아나는 요정을 쫓는 자가 그 빠른 천체의 운행으로 별자리들을 두 번 거듭 찾기도 전에 이루어지리라. 그리고 그대여, 일찍이 옆구리에 칼을 차고 얼굴에 수염을 기르고 냄새 맡을 코를 지녔던 그 누구보다도 고귀하고 순종하는 종자여, 편력 기사도의 꽃이 그대의 바로 눈앞에서 이렇게 실려 가는 것을 보더라도 낙담하거나 슬퍼하지 말지어다. 머지않아, 만물을 빚으신 분께서 그리 기꺼워하신다면, 그대는 그대 자신도 알아보지 못할 만큼 높은 자리에 오른 그대 자신을 보게 될 것이요, 그대의 훌륭한 주인이 그대에게 한 약속들도 헛되지 않으리라. 또한 나는 현자 멘티로니아나의 권위를 빌려 그대에게 단언하노니, 그대의 품삯은 때가 되면 그대 눈으로 보게 되듯 어김없이 치러지리라. 그러니 그 용맹하고 마법에 걸린 기사의 발자취를 따르라. 그대 두 사람에게 정해진 행선지로 그대가 가는 것이 마땅하니 말이다. 내게 더 말하는 것이 허락되지 않았으므로, 하느님께서 그대와 함께하시기를. 나는 내가 아는 그곳으로 돌아가노라.\" 그러고는 예언을 끝맺으면서 목소리를 높은 가락으로 끌어올렸다가, 다시 어찌나 부드러운 어조로 낮추었던지, 이 모든 것이 한낱 장난임을 아는 이들조차 들리는 말을 진지하게 받아들이고픈 마음이 들 지경이었다.\n\n돈키호테는 들려온 예언에 위로를 받았으니, 곧장 그 뜻을 완벽하게 헤아렸기 때문이다. 그는 자기가 사랑하는 토보소의 둘시네아와 거룩하고 합법적인 혼인으로 맺어진 자신을 보게 되리라는 것, 그리고 그 복된 태에서 새끼들, 곧 그의 아들들이 나와 라만차의 영원한 영광이 되리라는 것을 약속받았음을 알아차렸다. 이를 철저하고 굳건하게 확신한 그는 목소리를 높여 깊은 한숨과 함께 외쳤다. \"오, 그대가 누구이든, 내게 그토록 많은 길사를 예언해 준 그대여, 그대에게 간청하노니, 나를 대신하여 내 안위를 맡아 보살피는 저 현명한 마법사에게 청해 주시오. 지금 그들이 나를 실어 가는 이 사로잡힘 속에서, 방금 내게 주어진 그토록 기쁘고 비할 데 없는 약속들이 이루어지는 것을 내가 보기도 전에, 그가 나를 죽도록 내버려 두지 말기를. 그것만 이루어진다면, 나는 이 감옥의 고통을 영광으로 여기고, 나를 묶은 이 사슬에서 위안을 찾으며, 그들이 나를 뉘어 놓은 이 잠자리도 딱딱한 싸움터가 아니라 부드럽고 행복한 신혼의 잠자리로 여길 것이오. 또 내 종자 산초 판사의 위안으로 말하자면, 나는 그의 선량함과 정직함을 믿으니, 그가 좋은 운에서든 나쁜 운에서든 나를 저버리지 않으리라. 설령 그의 불운으로든 나의 불운으로든, 내가 그에게 약속한 섬이나 그에 맞먹는 무엇을 줄 수 없게 되더라도, 적어도 그의 품삯만은 잃지 않을 것이니, 이미 작성해 둔 내 유언장에, 그의 수많은 충직한 봉사가 아니라 내가 가진 재력에 맞추어 그에게 치러야 할 액수를 밝혀 두었기 때문이오.\"\n\n산초는 매우 공손하게 고개를 숙이고 두 손에 입을 맞추었으니, 두 손이 함께 묶여 있어 한 손에만 입맞출 수는 없었던 까닭이다. 그러고 나서 그 유령들이 우리를 어깨에 둘러메어 소달구지 위에 고정시켰다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII.\n\n돈키호테가 이런 식으로 우리에 갇혀 수레 위에 올려진 자기 모습을 보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"편력 기사에 관한 진중한 역사를 나는 숱하게 읽어 왔으나, 마법에 걸린 편력 기사를 이런 식으로, 또 이 게으르고 굼뜬 짐승들이 약속하는 이런 느린 걸음으로 데려갔다는 이야기는 읽은 적도, 본 적도, 들은 적도 없도다. 본디 마법에 걸린 기사는 언제나 신비로운 신속함으로 검고 짙은 구름에 휩싸인 채 공중을 가로질러, 혹은 불의 전차에 실려, 어쩌면 어떤 히포그리프나 그와 비슷한 짐승에 태워져 끌려가는 법이거늘, 나를 이렇게 황소 수레에 실어 데려가다니! 맙소사, 도무지 알 수가 없구나! 그러나 어쩌면 오늘날의 기사도와 마법은 지난날의 그것과는 다른 길을 따르는지도 모를 일이며, 또 내가 이 세상의 새로운 기사이자 이미 잊힌 모험 기사라는 소명을 되살린 첫 번째 사람이기에, 어쩌면 그들이 새로이 다른 종류의 마법과 마법에 걸린 자를 데려가는 다른 방식을 고안해 낸 것인지도 모르지. 산초야, 너는 이 일을 어찌 생각하느냐?\"\n\n\"무얼 어떻게 생각해야 할지 모르겠습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"저야 나리만큼 편력 기사에 관한 글을 두루 읽지 못했으니까요. 하지만 그래도 한마디 감히 말씀드리고 또 맹세컨대, 우리를 에워싸고 있는 이 유령들은 그리 가톨릭답지가 못합니다요.\"\n\n\"가톨릭답다고!\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이런 한심한 녀석 같으니! 저것들이 어찌 가톨릭답겠느냐? 저것들은 모두 환상 같은 형상을 뒤집어쓰고 와서 이런 짓을 벌이며 나를 이 지경으로 몰아넣은 악마들인 것을. 정 그걸 증명하고 싶거든 저것들을 만져 보고 더듬어 보거라. 그러면 저것들이 한낱 공기로 된 몸뚱이뿐이며, 겉모습 말고는 아무 실체도 없음을 알게 되리라.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 나리.\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"저는 벌써 그것들을 만져 보았습니다요. 그런데 저기서 저리도 분주히 돌아다니는 저 악마는 단단한 살을 지녔고, 게다가 제가 악마에 대해 들어 온 것과는 아주 딴판인 성질을 하나 가지고 있습니다요. 듣자 하니 악마들은 죄다 유황 냄새며 온갖 고약한 냄새를 풍긴다는데, 저 녀석은 반 레과 밖에서도 호박 향을 풍기지 뭡니까요.\" 산초가 여기서 말한 것은 돈 페르난도를 두고 한 것이었으니, 그는 그만한 신분의 신사답게 산초가 말한 대로 향수를 뿌렸을 법했다.\n\n\"그것을 이상히 여기지 마라, 내 벗 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내 너에게 일러두건대, 악마들이란 교활한 것들이라, 설령 향기를 몸에 지니고 다닌다 해도 그것들 자체는 아무 냄새도 없으니, 그것들은 영혼이기 때문이니라. 혹 냄새가 난다 해도 무언가 향긋한 냄새가 날 수는 없고 무언가 더럽고 역겨운 냄새가 날 따름이니, 그 까닭은 이러하다. 그것들은 어디를 가든 지옥을 함께 짊어지고 다니며 그 고통에서 조금도 벗어날 길이 없는데, 향긋한 냄새란 즐거움과 기쁨을 주는 것인즉, 그것들이 향긋한 냄새를 풍긴다는 것은 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이로다. 그러니 네가 말하는 저 악마에게서 호박 향이 나는 듯이 여겨진다면, 네가 스스로를 속이고 있거나, 아니면 그가 제 딴에는 악마가 아니라고 너로 하여금 믿게 하여 너를 속이려는 것이니라.\"\n\n주인과 종 사이에 오간 대화가 이러했다. 한편 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오는 산초가 이미 어느 정도 눈치를 챈 터라 자기들의 계략을 완전히 들춰낼까 염려하여 서둘러 출발하기로 마음먹고, 주막 주인을 한쪽으로 불러 로시난테에 안장을 얹고 산초의 당나귀에 길마를 지우라 일렀더니, 그가 매우 기민하게 그 일을 해치웠다. 그사이 신부는 관원들과 약조를 맺어, 하루에 얼마씩을 지불하는 조건으로 그들이 자기 마을까지 동행하게 하였다. 카르데니오는 로시난테 안장의 앞고리 한쪽에 작은 방패를, 다른 쪽에 대야를 매달고는, 산초에게 손짓으로 명하여 당나귀에 올라타 로시난테의 고삐를 잡게 하였으며, 수레 양옆에는 머스켓 총을 든 관원 둘씩을 배치하였다. 그러나 수레가 움직이기에 앞서 주막 안주인과 그 딸, 그리고 마리토르네스가 나와 돈키호테에게 작별을 고하면서, 그의 불운을 슬퍼하는 척 눈물을 흘리는 시늉을 하니, 돈키호테가 그들에게 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"울지 마시오, 착한 부인들이여, 이 모든 재난은 내가 받드는 이 직업을 따르는 자들의 운명이오. 만일 이러한 역경이 내게 닥치지 않는다면 나는 스스로를 이름난 편력 기사라 여기지 못할 것이오. 명성도 이름도 변변찮은 기사에게는 이런 일이 결코 일어나지 않으니, 세상의 그 누구도 그런 자들은 마음에 두지 않기 때문이오. 그러나 용맹한 기사에게는 이런 일이 일어나는 법이니, 그들의 덕과 용맹을 시기하는 숱한 군주와 다른 기사들이 비열한 수단으로 훌륭한 자들을 파멸시키려 꾀하는 까닭이오. 그럼에도 덕이란 그 자체로 너무도 강대하여, 그 최초의 창시자인 조로아스터가 알았던 온갖 마법에도 불구하고 모든 시련에서 승리하여 나올 것이며, 태양이 하늘에 빛을 뿌리듯 땅 위에 그 빛을 뿌리리다. 아리따운 부인들이여, 혹 내가 부주의로 무엇이든 그대들의 마음을 상하게 한 일이 있다면 부디 용서하시오. 의도적으로 알고서 그런 짓을 한 적은 누구에게도 결코 없었으니 말이오. 그리고 어느 사악한 마법사가 나를 가두어 놓은 이 속박에서 하느님께서 나를 구해 주시기를 기도해 주시오. 만일 내가 여기서 풀려나게 된다면, 그대들이 이 성에서 내게 베풀어 준 은혜를 마음에 새겨 두었다가, 그것이 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼 알아주고 갚아 드리리다.\"\n\n성의 부인들과 돈키호테 사이에 이런 일이 오가는 동안, 신부와 이발사는 돈 페르난도와 그 일행에게, 그리고 대위와 그 형제와 부인들에게, 또 이제 모두가 행복하게 된 그들에게, 특히 도로테아와 루신다에게 작별을 고하였다. 그들은 모두 서로 부둥켜안고, 앞으로 일이 어찌 되어 가는지 서로에게 알리기로 약속하였으며, 돈 페르난도는 돈키호테가 어찌 되었는지 알려 달라고 신부에게 자기에게 편지를 보낼 곳을 일러 주면서, 그 소식을 듣는 것보다 더 기쁜 일은 없으리라 장담하였고, 자기 또한 그의 결혼이며 소라이다의 세례, 돈 루이스의 일, 루신다가 친정으로 돌아간 일 등, 신부가 알고 싶어 할 만한 모든 일을 빠짐없이 전해 주겠노라 하였다. 신부는 그 청을 빈틈없이 따르겠노라 약속하였고, 그들은 다시 한 번 부둥켜안고 약속을 거듭 다졌다.\n\n주막 주인이 신부에게 다가와 종이 몇 장을 건네면서, 「무모한 호기심」이라는 소설이 들어 있던 가방의 안감 속에서 이것들을 발견했는데, 주인이 그 뒤로 돌아오지 않았으니 신부가 죄다 가져가도 된다고 말했다. 자기는 글을 읽을 줄 모르니 그것들이 필요 없다는 것이었다. 신부는 그에게 고마움을 표하고 그것들을 펼쳐 보니, 원고 첫머리에 \"린코네테와 코르타디요 이야기\"라는 글자가 적혀 있어 그것이 소설임을 알아차렸고, 「무모한 호기심」이 훌륭했던 만큼 이것도 그러하리라 짐작하였으니, 둘 다 아마도 같은 작가의 작품일 듯하였기 때문이다. 그리하여 그는 그것을 챙겨 두었다가 틈이 나면 읽으려 하였다. 그러고 나서 그는 말에 올랐고, 그의 벗 이발사도 똑같이 하였는데, 둘 다 돈키호테에게 알아보이지 않으려 얼굴을 가리고서 수레의 뒤를 따라 나섰다. 행렬의 순서는 이러하였다. 맨 앞에는 주인이 끄는 수레가 가고, 그 양옆에는 앞서 말한 대로 머스켓 총을 든 성동포단(聖同胞團)의 관원들이 행진하였으며, 그 뒤로는 산초 판사가 당나귀를 타고 로시난테의 고삐를 잡고 따랐고, 맨 뒤에는 신부와 이발사가 앞서 말한 대로 얼굴을 가린 채, 진중하고 엄숙한 자태로 그 늠름한 노새에 올라타, 황소들의 느린 걸음에 보조를 맞추어 따라왔다. 돈키호테는 두 손이 묶이고 두 발을 쭉 뻗은 채 우리 안에 앉아, 마치 살과 피로 된 사람이 아니라 돌로 된 조각상이기라도 한 양 말없이 참을성 있게 창살에 기대어 있었다. 이렇게 느릿느릿 말없이 그들은 두 레과쯤 나아가, 마침내 어느 골짜기에 이르렀는데, 수레 주인이 보기에 그곳이 쉬면서 황소들에게 먹이를 줄 만한 자리인 듯하여 그렇게 신부에게 말하였으나, 이발사의 생각으로는 조금 더 나아가야 할 듯하였으니, 가까이 보이는 언덕 너머에 그들이 멈추려는 곳보다 풀이 더 많고 훨씬 좋은 골짜기가 있음을 그가 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그의 조언이 받아들여져 그들은 길을 계속 갔다.\n\n바로 그때 신부가 뒤를 돌아보니, 잘 갖추어 입고 채비를 단단히 한 예닐곱 명의 말 탄 사람들이 뒤따라오고 있었는데, 그들은 황소의 굼뜨고 느릿한 걸음이 아니라 참사회원의 노새를 탄 사람들답게 달려왔으니, 한 레과도 채 안 되는 곳에 보이는 주막에서 한시바삐 한낮의 휴식을 취하려고 서두르는 까닭에 이내 그들을 따라잡았다. 발 빠른 나그네들이 느린 일행을 따라잡자 정중한 인사가 오갔는데, 새로 온 이들 가운데 하나는 실은 톨레도의 참사회원으로서 그를 수행하는 다른 이들의 우두머리였으니, 그가 그 행렬의 정연한 차림새며, 수레와 관원들과 산초와 로시난테와 신부와 이발사, 그리고 무엇보다 우리에 갇혀 결박당한 돈키호테를 살펴보고는, 저 사람을 어찌하여 저런 식으로 데려가는지 그 까닭을 묻지 않을 수 없었다. 다만 관원들의 휘장으로 미루어 그는 이미 저 사람이 처벌이 성동포단의 관할에 속하는 어떤 막무가내 노상강도이거나 다른 흉악범이려니 짐작하고 있었다. 그가 물음을 던진 관원 가운데 하나가 대답하였다. \"나리, 저 양반이 어찌하여 이런 식으로 끌려가는지는 저 양반 본인더러 말하게 하십시오. 저희는 모릅니다.\"\n\n돈키호테가 그 말을 엿듣고 말했다. \"여러분, 혹 편력 기사도의 일에 정통하고 박식하신지요? 만일 그러하시다면 제 불운을 들려드리겠으나, 그렇지 않다면 굳이 수고로이 그것을 늘어놓아 봐야 아무 소용이 없을 테니 말입니다.\" 그러나 이때 신부와 이발사는 나그네들이 돈키호테와 대화를 나누는 것을 보고는, 자기들의 계략이 발각되지 않도록 적당히 대답하려고 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n참사회원이 돈키호테에게 대답하여 말했다. \"사실, 형제여, 나는 비얄판도의 논리학 입문서보다 기사도 책에 대해 더 많이 알고 있소. 그러니 그게 전부라면 무엇이든 안심하고 내게 말씀하셔도 좋소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 나리, 하느님의 이름으로 말씀드리겠습니다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"정 그러하시다면 알아 두십시오. 저는 사악한 마법사들의 시기와 술수로 인해 이 우리에 마법으로 갇혀 있는 것입니다. 덕이란 선한 자들에게 사랑받는 것보다 악한 자들에게 핍박받는 일이 더 많은 법이니까요. 저는 편력 기사이되, 명성이 그 기록에 결코 불멸로 남길 생각조차 하지 않은 그런 자들의 하나가 아니라, 시기 그 자체와, 페르시아가 낳은 온갖 마법사며 인도가 낳은 브라만이며 에티오피아가 낳은 나체 수행자들 모두에게 맞서고 또 그들을 무릅쓰고서, 다가올 시대를 위한 본보기와 귀감이 되도록 불멸의 신전에 제 이름을 새길 그런 자들의 하나입니다. 그리하여 편력 기사들이 무예에서 명예의 정점이자 최고의 영광에 이르고자 한다면 마땅히 밟아야 할 발자취를 거기서 보게 할 것입니다.\"\n\n\"라만차의 돈키호테 나리께서 하시는 말씀은,\" 신부가 말했다. \"사실입니다. 그분은 어떤 잘못이나 죄 때문이 아니라, 덕을 가증스러워하고 용맹을 미워하는 자들의 악의 때문에 이 수레에 마법으로 갇혀 가시는 것입니다. 나리, 이분이 바로 슬픈 몰골의 기사이시니, 혹 그 이름을 들어 보신 적이 있다면 아실 것입니다. 그분의 용맹한 위업과 위대한 행적은, 그것을 가리려는 시기와 숨기려는 악의의 온갖 노력에도 불구하고, 길이 남을 청동과 썩지 않을 대리석에 새겨질 것입니다.\"\n\n참사회원은 갇힌 자와 풀려난 자가 다 같이 이런 투로 말하는 것을 듣고는 놀란 나머지 성호를 그을 지경이었으며, 자기에게 무슨 일이 닥쳤는지 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었다. 그를 수행하던 자들도 모두 똑같이 어리둥절한 상태였다.\n\n이때 대화를 들으려고 가까이 다가와 있던 산초 판사가, 모든 것을 분명히 밝히려고 이렇게 말했다. \"여보십시오, 나리들, 제가 이제부터 드릴 말씀이 마음에 드시든 안 드시든 간에, 사실인즉슨 제 주인 돈키호테 나리는 우리 어머니나 마찬가지로 마법에 걸린 게 아닙니다요. 멀쩡한 정신에다 잡수시고 마시시고, 어제 우리에 가두기 전과 똑같이 다른 사람들처럼 볼일도 다 보십니다요. 그런데 사정이 이러한데, 어찌하여 저더러 나리가 마법에 걸렸다고 믿으라는 겁니까요? 저는 마법에 걸린 사람은 먹지도, 자지도, 말하지도 못한다고 하는 말을 숱하게 들었습니다요. 한데 제 주인은 누가 말리지 않으면 변호사 서른 명보다 더 떠들어 대실 분입죠.\" 그러더니 신부 쪽으로 돌아서서 그가 외쳤다. \"아이고, 신부님, 신부님! 제가 신부님을 못 알아볼 줄 아십니까요? 제가 이 새로운 마법인지 뭔지의 속셈을 알아채지도 보지도 못할 줄 아십니까요? 그렇다면 말씀드립니다만, 얼굴을 죄다 가리셨어도 저는 신부님을 알아봤고, 아무리 그 수작을 감추셔도 제가 다 꿰뚫어 보고 있습니다요. 결국 시기가 판치는 곳에 덕이 살아남을 수 없고, 인색함이 있는 곳에 너그러움이 있을 수 없는 법이지요. 망할 놈의 악마 같으니! 신부님만 아니었던들 지금쯤 제 주인은 미코미코나 공주님과 혼인하셨을 테고, 저는 적어도 백작쯤은 되었을 겁니다요. 슬픈 몰골의 기사이신 제 주인의 그 어지심으로 보나, 제가 바친 그 큰 공로로 보나, 그만한 일이야 당연히 기대할 만했으니까요. 한데 이제 보니 이 고장에서 흔히들 하는 말, 즉 운명의 수레바퀴는 물레방아 바퀴보다 더 빨리 돌아가고, 어제 위에 있던 자가 오늘은 아래로 떨어진다는 말이 참으로 옳습니다요. 제 처자식이 가엾기 그지없습니다요. 그것들이 마땅히 제 아비가 어느 섬이나 왕국의 총독이나 부왕이 되어 돌아오기를 기대할 만하였거늘, 도리어 말구종이 되어 돌아오는 꼴을 보게 될 테니 말입니다요. 신부님, 제가 이 모든 말씀을 드리는 것은, 오로지 신부님께서 제 주인을 학대하신 일을 양심에 비추어 보시라고 촉구하기 위함입니다요. 그리고 이렇게 그분을 가둠으로써, 또 제 주인 돈키호테 나리께서 갇혀 계시는 동안 베풀지 못하고 마는 온갖 구원과 선행을 신부님 탓으로 돌려, 하느님께서 저세상에서 신부님께 그 책임을 물으시지 않도록 조심하십시오.\"\n\n\"저기 등잔 심지나 손질해라!\" 이때 이발사가 외쳤다. \"그래, 산초, 너도 네 주인과 한패였더냐? 맙소사, 이제 보니 너도 그 주인의 광기와 기사도 기질을 옮아 받아서, 그와 함께 우리에 갇혀 마법에 걸려야 할 모양이로구나. 네가 그자의 약속에 넘어가 배가 부르도록 내버려 두고, 네가 그토록 갈망하는 그 섬이 네 머릿속에 들어앉은 것이 다 액운이었던 게야.\"\n\n\"저는 누구의 씨로도 배가 부른 적이 없습니다요.\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"설령 임금님이 그러시려 한들 제가 배부르도록 내버려 둘 사람도 아니고요. 비록 가난하나마 저는 유서 깊은 기독교도이고, 누구에게도 빚진 게 없습니다요. 제가 섬을 갈망한다 한들, 남들은 더 못한 것을 갈망하기도 합니다요. 우리는 저마다 제가 한 일의 자식인 법이고, 사람으로 태어난 이상 섬의 총독은 그만두고 교황도 될 수 있는 것 아닙니까요. 더구나 제 주인께서는 하도 많은 섬을 얻으셔서 누구한테 줘야 할지 모르실 지경일 텐데요. 말조심하십시오, 이발사 양반. 면도질이 전부가 아니고, 베드로와 베드로 사이에도 차이가 있는 법이니까요. 제가 이런 말씀을 드리는 것은 우리가 다 서로를 알기 때문이며, 저한테 가짜 주사위를 굴려 봐야 통하지 않는다는 말입니다요. 그리고 제 주인의 마법으로 말할 것 같으면, 진실은 하느님께서 아십니다요. 그냥 내버려 두십시오. 들쑤셔 봐야 더 나빠질 뿐이니까요.\"\n\n이발사는 산초에게 대꾸하고 싶지 않았으니, 그가 직설적으로 떠들어 대다가 신부와 자기가 그토록 애써 감추려는 것을 폭로해 버릴까 염려한 까닭이었다. 그리고 같은 염려에서 신부는 참사회원에게 조금 앞서 가 달라고 청하여, 이 우리에 갇힌 사람의 사연이며 그를 즐겁게 해 줄 다른 일들을 들려주려 하였다. 참사회원이 응하여 종자들과 함께 앞으로 나아가서는, 신부가 들려주는 돈키호테의 성품과 삶과 광기와 행적에 관한 이야기에 귀 기울여 들었다. 신부는 그의 광기의 시작과 기원을 간략히 일러 주고, 그가 우리에 갇히기까지의 모험담을 죄다 들려주었으며, 어떻게든 그 광기를 고칠 방도를 찾을 수 있을까 하여 그를 집으로 데려가려는 계획까지 함께 이야기하였다. 참사회원과 그 종자들은 돈키호테의 기이한 사연을 듣고 새삼 놀랐는데, 이야기가 끝나자 그가 말했다. \"사실대로 말하자면, 신부님, 나로서는 사람들이 기사도 책이라 부르는 것이 나라에 해롭다고 봅니다. 그리고 한가하고 그릇된 취향에 이끌려 인쇄된 거의 모든 책의 첫머리는 읽어 보았으나, 그중 단 한 권도 처음부터 끝까지 읽어 낼 수가 없었습니다. 내가 보기에 그것들은 죄다 크든 작든 똑같은 것이어서, 이것이 저것보다 나을 게 없고, 저것이 이것보다 나을 게 없으니 말입니다. 그리고 내 생각에 이런 종류의 글과 작품은 사람들이 밀레토스풍이라 부르는 우화와 같은 부류로서, 오로지 즐거움만 주고 가르침은 주지 않으려는 터무니없는 이야기들인즉, 즐거움과 가르침을 동시에 주는 교훈 우화와는 정반대입니다. 그리고 설령 그런 책들의 으뜸가는 목적이 즐거움을 주는 것이라 하더라도, 그토록 괴이한 헛소리로 가득 차 있으니 어찌 그 목적을 이룰 수 있을지 모르겠습니다. 마음이 느끼는 즐거움이란 눈이나 상상이 앞에 펼쳐 보이는 사물에서 마음이 지각하거나 관조하는 아름다움과 조화로움에서 비롯되어야 하는 법이며, 무엇이든 추함이나 부조화를 지닌 것은 아무런 즐거움도 줄 수 없습니다. 그렇다면 열여섯 살짜리 소년이 탑처럼 키 큰 거인을 베어 마치 아몬드 과자라도 되는 양 두 동강 내어 버리는 책이나 이야기에 무슨 아름다움이, 또 부분과 전체, 전체와 부분 사이에 무슨 균형이 있을 수 있겠습니까? 또 그들이 전투의 모습을 그려 보이고자 할 때, 적군 쪽에 백만 명의 전사가 있다고 일러 놓고는, 책의 주인공을 그들에게 맞서게 하니, 우리는 좋든 싫든 그 기사가 제 억센 팔의 힘 하나만으로 승리를 거둔다고 어쩔 수 없이 믿어야 합니다. 그리고 또, 타고난 여왕이나 황후가 일면식도 없는 어느 떠돌이 기사의 품에 제 몸을 던져 버리는 그 손쉬움에 대해서는 무어라 해야겠습니까? 기사들로 가득 찬 거대한 탑이 순풍을 받은 배처럼 바다를 가로질러 떠가서, 오늘 밤은 롬바르디아에, 내일 아침은 인도의 사제왕 요한의 나라에, 혹은 프톨레마이오스도 기술한 적 없고 마르코 폴로도 본 적 없는 어떤 다른 나라에 이른다는 이야기를 읽고 즐거움을 느낄 마음이, 도무지 야만스럽고 무지하지 않은 다음에야 어디 있겠습니까? 그리고 만일 이에 대한 답으로, 그런 종류의 책을 쓰는 작가들은 그것을 허구로 쓰는 것이니 진실의 세세한 부분까지 따질 의무는 없다고 한다면, 나는 이렇게 대답하겠습니다. 허구란 진실에 가까워 보일수록 더 훌륭하며, 개연성과 가능성이 있을수록 더 큰 즐거움을 주는 법이라고 말입니다. 허구 속의 줄거리는 독자의 이해에 맞아떨어져야 하며, 불가능한 것을 그럴듯하게 만들고 어려운 것을 매끄럽게 다듬으며 마음을 긴장 속에 붙들어 둠으로써, 놀라게 하고 흥미를 끌고 즐겁게 하고 재미있게 하여, 경탄과 기쁨이 서로 발맞추어 나아가도록 짜여야 합니다. 그런데 글쓰기의 완성이 깃든 핍진성과 자연에 대한 충실함을 외면하는 자는 이 모든 것을 이루어 내지 못할 것입니다. 나는 아직껏 모든 부분이 완결된, 짜임새 있는 줄거리를 갖추어, 중간이 처음과 들어맞고 끝이 처음 및 중간과 들어맞는 기사도 책을 단 한 권도 본 적이 없습니다. 도리어 그들은 그토록 많은 부분으로 그것을 짜 맞추니, 균형 잡힌 형상이 아니라 키마이라나 괴물을 빚어내려는 것처럼 보일 지경입니다. 게다가 이 모든 것에 더하여, 그것들은 문체가 거칠고, 위업은 믿기 어려우며, 연애는 음란하고, 궁정의 언사는 투박하며, 전투는 장황하고, 논변은 어리석으며, 여행은 황당하니, 한마디로 지성적인 기예라 할 만한 모든 것이 결여되어 있습니다. 그런 까닭에 그것들은 쓸모없는 종자(種子)로서 기독교 공화국에서 추방당해 마땅합니다.\"\n\n신부는 그의 말에 주의 깊게 귀 기울이며, 그가 건전한 분별을 갖춘 사람이요 그의 말에 충분한 일리가 있음을 느꼈다. 그리하여 그는 자기도 같은 의견이며 기사도 책에 원한을 품고 있던 터라, 무수히 많던 돈키호테의 책들을 죄다 불태워 버렸노라 일러 주었다. 또 자기가 그것들을 어떻게 점검하였는지, 어떤 것을 불길에 처넣고 어떤 것을 살려 두었는지에 관한 이야기를 들려주니, 참사회원은 적잖이 재미있어하였다. 그러더니 참사회원은, 자기가 그 책들을 그토록 책망하기는 하였으나 그래도 한 가지 좋은 점은 발견하였다고 덧붙였으니, 그것은 바로 재능 있는 지성이 스스로를 펼쳐 보일 기회를 그 책들이 제공한다는 점이었다. 그 책들은 펜이 자유로이 누빌 수 있는 넓고 광활한 들판을 펼쳐 보이는바, 난파며 폭풍이며 격전이며 전투를 그려 내고, 용맹한 대장을 갖추는 데 필요한 온갖 자질을 두루 지닌 그러한 인물을 묘사하되, 적의 술책을 미리 내다보는 데 명민하고, 병사들을 북돋우거나 다잡는 언변에 능하며, 책략에 노련하고, 결단에 신속하며, 때를 기다리는 데도 공격을 밀어붙이는 데도 한결같이 대담한 모습으로 그려 낸다는 것이었다. 또 어느 때는 어떤 슬프고 비극적인 사건을, 또 어느 때는 어떤 기쁘고 뜻밖의 사건을 그려 보이고, 여기서는 아름답고 정숙하며 지혜롭고 겸손한 귀부인을, 저기서는 용감하고 점잖은 기독교도 기사를, 여기서는 무법하고 야만스러운 허풍쟁이를, 저기서는 정중하고 늠름하며 기품 있는 군주를 그려 내며, 봉신들의 헌신과 충성을, 귀족들의 위대함과 너그러움을 펼쳐 보인다는 것이었다. \"혹은 또,\" 그가 말했다. \"작가가 스스로 천문학자임을, 혹은 능숙한 우주지리학자나 음악가, 또는 국정에 통달한 자임을 보여 줄 수도 있고, 때로는 원한다면 마법사로 나설 기회도 가질 것입니다. 그는 율리시스의 교활함, 아이네아스의 효심, 아킬레우스의 용맹, 헥토르의 불운, 시논의 배신, 에우리알루스의 우정, 알렉산드로스의 너그러움, 카이사르의 대담함, 트라야누스의 관용과 진실함, 조피로스의 충성, 카토의 지혜를, 한마디로 한 출중한 인물을 완전하게 만드는 데 이바지하는 온갖 자질을, 어느 때는 한 사람에게 한데 모으고 어느 때는 여럿에게 나누어 펼쳐 보일 수 있습니다. 그리고 만일 이것을 문체의 매력과 기발한 창의로써, 되도록 진실을 겨냥하여 해낸다면, 그는 분명 밝고 갖가지 빛깔의 실로 짠 직물 한 폭을 엮어 낼 것이며, 그것이 완성되면 그 완벽함과 아름다움이 너무도 빛나, 어떤 글이든 추구할 수 있는 가장 값진 목적, 곧 앞서 말한 대로 가르침과 즐거움을 함께 주는 그 목적에 다다를 것입니다. 이런 책들의 거침없는 범위는 작가로 하여금 서사시적, 서정시적, 비극적, 희극적인 자기 역량을, 그리고 시와 웅변의 달콤하고 매혹적인 기예가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 정조(情調)를 펼쳐 보일 수 있게 하니, 서사시란 운문으로 쓸 수 있는 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 산문으로도 쓸 수 있는 것이기 때문입니다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII.\n\n신부가 말했다. \"참사회원 님 말씀이 옳습니다. 바로 그런 이유로 지금까지 그런 부류의 책을 써 온 자들은 더욱 큰 비난을 받아 마땅하지요. 좋은 취향이나 예술의 규칙에는 조금도 마음을 두지 않고 써 댔으니 말입니다. 그 규칙을 길잡이로 삼았더라면 그리스와 라틴 시의 두 거장이 운문에서 그러하듯, 그들도 산문에서 명성을 떨칠 수 있었을 텐데요.\"\n\n참사회원이 말했다. \"저로 말하자면, 한때 제가 말씀드린 그 모든 요점을 지킨 기사도 책을 한 권 써 보고 싶은 유혹을 받은 적이 있습니다. 사실을 털어놓자면 이미 백 장이 넘게 써 두었지요. 그리고 그것이 제 스스로의 평가에 부합하는지 시험해 보려고, 이런 종류의 읽을거리를 좋아하는 사람들에게, 곧 학식 있고 지적인 이들에게나, 허튼소리를 듣는 즐거움밖에는 아무것도 바라지 않는 무지한 자들에게나 두루 보여 주었습니다. 그랬더니 모두에게서 듣기 좋은 칭찬을 얻었지요. 그럼에도 저는 그 일을 더 진척시키지 않았습니다. 한편으로는 그것이 제 직분에 어울리지 않는 일로 여겨졌기 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 어리석은 자가 현명한 자보다 수가 많음을 알았기 때문입니다. 비록 어리석은 다수에게 갈채받는 것보다 현명한 소수에게 칭찬받는 편이 낫다고는 하나, 저는 이런 책을 읽는 일이 대개 떨어지고 마는 그 분별없는 대중의 우둔한 판단에 저 자신을 내맡길 마음이 없습니다.\n\n\"하지만 무엇보다도 제 손을 멈추게 하고 그것을 완성할 생각조차 아예 버리게 만든 것은, 요즘 상연되는 연극을 두고 제 스스로에게 던진 논거였습니다. 그것은 이런 식이었지요. 지금 유행하는 연극들이, 순전한 창작이든 역사에 바탕을 둔 것이든, 그 전부 혹은 대부분이 한낱 허튼소리요 머리도 꼬리도 없는 것들인데도 대중은 그것을 기뻐하며 듣고, 완벽함과는 거리가 멀건만 완벽한 것으로 여기고 떠받들며 칭송한다면, 또 그것을 쓰는 작가들과 그것을 연기하는 배우들이 대중이 이것을 원하고 다른 것은 받아들이려 하지 않으니 마땅히 이래야 한다고 말한다면, 또 규칙에 따라 예술의 법도대로 줄거리를 짜는 자들은 그것을 알아볼 만한 지각 있는 사람을 기껏해야 대여섯이나 찾을 뿐이고 나머지는 모두 그 작품의 가치에 눈먼 채로 남아 있다면, 또 그들로서는 소수에게 칭찬받기보다 다수에게서 빵을 얻는 편이 낫다면, 제가 앞서 말씀드린 원칙을 지키느라 눈썹을 다 태우고 난 뒤에도 제 책 역시 똑같은 길을 걷게 될 것이요, 저는 '길모퉁이의 재단사'* 꼴이 되고 말 것이라는 논거였습니다. 그래서 제가 더러 배우들에게 그들이 받아들인 이 관념이 잘못된 것이라고, 터무니없는 연극보다 예술의 규칙에 맞는 연극을 내놓는 편이 더 많은 사람을 끌어모으고 더 큰 신망을 얻으리라고 설득해 보려 했으나, 그들은 자기네 생각에 어찌나 단단히 매여 있는지 어떤 논거나 증거로도 거기서 떼어 낼 수가 없었습니다.\n\n* 길모퉁이의 재단사: 공짜로 일해 주고도 실을 제 돈으로 댔다는 스페인 속담 속 인물로, 헛수고하는 사람을 빗댄 말.\n\"하루는 그런 고집불통 가운데 한 사람에게 이렇게 말한 기억이 납니다. '말해 보시오, 몇 해 전에 이 왕국의 한 이름난 시인이 쓴 비극 세 편이 스페인에서 상연되었던 것을 기억하지 못하시오? 그것들은 듣는 사람을 모두, 무지한 자나 현명한 자나, 천한 무리나 높은 신분이나 가릴 것 없이 감탄과 기쁨과 흥미로 가득 채웠고, 그 세 편만으로도 이후 나온 가장 훌륭한 연극 서른 편을 합친 것보다 더 많은 돈을 흥행주들에게 벌어다 주지 않았소?'\n\n\"그러자 그 배우가 대답하더군요. '「이사벨라」와 「필리스」와 「알렉산드라」를 두고 하시는 말씀이겠지요.'\n\n\"제가 말했지요. '바로 그것들이오. 그것들이 예술의 원칙을 지키지 않았는지, 또 그 원칙을 지킴으로써 그 우월함을 드러내고 온 세상을 즐겁게 하는 데 실패했는지 보시오. 그러니 허튼소리를 고집하는 것은 대중의 잘못이 아니라, 다른 무언가를 만들어 낼 줄 모르는 자들의 잘못이오. 「복수당한 배은망덕」도 허튼소리가 아니었고, 「누만시아」에도 그런 것은 없었으며, 「상인이 된 연인」에도, 「다정한 어여쁜 원수」에도, 또 어떤 재능 있는 시인들이 자기네의 명성과 영예를 위해, 그리고 그것을 무대에 올린 자들의 이익을 위해 쓴 다른 몇몇 작품에도 그런 것은 없었소.' 여기에 몇 마디 더 덧붙였더니, 제 생각에는 그를 다소 말문이 막히게 만들기는 했으나, 그의 오류를 깨우쳐 줄 만큼 그가 흡족해하거나 수긍한 것은 아니었습니다.\"\n\n여기서 신부가 끼어들었다. \"참사회원 님께서는 한 가지 화제를 건드리셨는데, 그것은 제가 오늘날 유행하는 연극에 대해 품고 있는 해묵은 적개심을 일깨우는군요. 제가 기사도 책에 품은 적개심에 못지않게 강한 것입니다. 툴리우스에 따르면 연극은 인생의 거울이요, 풍속의 모범이며, 진리의 형상이어야 하건만, 요즘 상연되는 것들은 허튼소리의 거울이요, 어리석음의 모범이며, 음란함의 형상이니 말입니다. 우리가 지금 논하는 것과 관련하여, 첫 막 첫 장면에서 강보에 싸인 갓난아이가 등장했다가 두 번째 장면에서 수염 난 다 자란 어른으로 나오는 것보다 더한 허튼소리가 어디 있겠습니까? 또 늙은이를 허세 부리는 칼잡이로, 젊은이를 겁쟁이로, 하인이 고상한 말을 늘어놓고, 시동이 현명한 충고를 하며, 왕이 짐꾼 노릇을 하고, 공주가 부엌데기가 되어 우리 앞에 나서는 것보다 더한 터무니없음이 어디 있겠습니까? 또 그들이 그려 내는 사건이 일어날 법한, 혹은 일어날 수 있는 시간에 그들이 기울이는 주의에 대해서는 무어라 말해야 할까요? 제가 본 어떤 연극은 첫 막이 유럽에서 시작되어, 두 번째 막은 아시아에서, 세 번째 막은 아프리카에서 끝나더군요. 그리고 만일 네 막짜리였다면 네 번째 막은 틀림없이 아메리카에서 끝나, 그리하여 지구의 네 귀퉁이 모두를 무대로 삼았을 것입니다. 또 인생에의 충실함이 연극이 무엇보다 염두에 두어야 할 것이라면, 사건이 페핀 왕이나 샤를마뉴 시대에 일어나는 것으로 설정되어 있는데 그 안의 주인공으로는 부용의 고드프루아처럼 십자가를 들고 예루살렘에 들어가 성묘를 되찾은 헤라클리우스 황제를 내세우니, 그 둘 사이에는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 세월이 가로놓여 있는 터에, 어떻게 평범한 지각을 가진 사람치고 만족할 수 있겠습니까? 또 연극이 허구에 바탕을 두면서 거기에 역사적 사실을 끌어들이거나, 서로 다른 사람들에게 서로 다른 때에 일어났던 일들의 토막을 뒤섞어 놓는데, 그 모든 것이 개연성의 기미라고는 전혀 없을뿐더러, 어느 모로 보나 변명의 여지 없는 명백한 오류들로 가득하다면 어떻겠습니까? 게다가 가장 고약한 것은, 이것이 완벽함이요 이것을 넘어선 것은 무엇이든 꾸며 낸 세련됨이라고 말하는 무지한 자들이 있다는 사실입니다. 그리고 성극으로 눈을 돌리면, 그 안에서 그들은 어떤 기적들을 지어내는지요! 한 성인의 기적을 다른 성인에게 갖다 붙이며, 얼마나 외경스럽고 엉성하게 꾸며 낸 사건들을 만들어 내는지요! 심지어 세속극에서도 그들은 아무런 까닭이나 목적도 없이 기적을 끌어들이는데, 그저 그들이 변신이라 부르는 그런 기적이 어리석은 사람들을 놀라게 하고 연극으로 끌어들이는 데 요긴하리라 여길 따름입니다. 이 모든 것이 진리를 해치고 역사를 그르치며, 더 나아가 스페인 재사들에게 치욕을 안기는 데로 기울지요. 연극의 법도를 빈틈없이 지키는 외국인들이 우리가 내놓는 연극의 터무니없음과 허튼소리를 볼 때면 우리를 야만스럽고 무지한 자들로 여기니 말입니다. 잘 다스려지는 정부가 연극을 대중 앞에 상연하도록 허락할 때 주로 염두에 두는 목적은, 때때로 해롭지 않은 오락거리로 사람들을 즐겁게 하고, 게으름이 자아내기 쉬운 그 사악한 기질로부터 그들을 지키려는 데 있으며, 또 이것은 좋든 나쁘든 어떤 종류의 연극으로든 이룰 수 있으니, 법도를 정하거나 그것을 쓰고 연기하는 자들을 마땅한 방식대로 만들도록 매어 둘 필요가 없다는 변명도 충분치 않습니다. 제가 말씀드렸듯이, 추구하는 목적은 어떤 종류의 연극으로든 확보될 수 있다는 변명 말입니다. 이에 대해 저는 이렇게 답하겠습니다. 그와 똑같은 목적은, 비할 데 없이 더 훌륭하게, 그렇지 못한 연극보다는 좋은 연극을 통해 이루어지리라고요. 예술적이고 제대로 짜인 연극을 듣고 나면, 듣는 이는 익살에 흥이 돋고, 진지한 대목에 가르침을 받으며, 사건들에 감탄으로 가득 차고, 논변으로 지혜가 날카로워지며, 술수에 경각심을 얻고, 본보기로 한층 현명해지며, 악덕에 맞서 분기하고, 미덕을 사랑하며 자리를 뜰 것입니다. 이 모든 길로 좋은 연극은, 듣는 이가 아무리 무뚝뚝하고 둔하다 한들 그의 마음을 자극할 것입니다. 그리고 온갖 불가능한 일 가운데 가장 큰 것은, 이 모든 자질을 갖춘 연극이, 그것이 결여된 연극보다, 곧 요즘 흔히 상연되는 대다수의 연극보다 훨씬 더 즐겁게 하고 만족시키고 기쁘게 하지 못하리라는 것입니다. 또 이 일로 그것을 쓰는 시인들을 탓할 것도 못 됩니다. 그들 가운데에는 자기 결함을 더없이 잘 알고 마땅히 무엇을 해야 하는지를 아는 이들도 있으니 말입니다. 다만 연극이 사고파는 상품이 되어 버린 까닭에, 그들은 배우들이 이런 식으로 된 것이 아니면 사려 하지 않는다고, 그것도 사실대로 말합니다. 그래서 시인은 자기 작품에 값을 치를 배우의 요구에 자신을 맞추려 애쓰는 것이지요. 이것이 진실임은, 이 왕국의 더없이 풍요로운 한 재사가 그토록 찬란하게, 그토록 우아하고 흥겹게, 그토록 다듬어진 운율로, 그토록 정선된 언어로, 그토록 심오한 성찰로, 한마디로 웅변과 격조 높은 문체가 그토록 풍부하게 써낸 수많은 연극을 보면 알 수 있습니다. 그는 그 명성으로 온 세상을 가득 채웠으나, 배우들의 취향에 맞추려는 그의 욕심 탓에, 그 작품들 가운데 몇몇이 그러하듯 모두가 마땅한 만큼 완벽함에 다가가지는 못했지요. 또 어떤 이들은 어찌나 경솔하게 연극을 쓰는지, 그것이 상연된 뒤에 배우들은 어느 왕이나 누군가에게 거슬리거나 어느 귀족 가문에 모욕이 될 만한 것을 연기했다는 이유로 처벌받을까 두려워 달아나 몸을 숨겨야 하는데, 실제로 그런 일이 자주 있었습니다. 이 모든 폐단, 그리고 제가 말씀드리지 않은 더 많은 폐단은, 수도에 모든 연극이 상연되기 전에 검토할 만한 지각 있고 분별 있는 사람이 하나 있다면 사라질 것입니다. 수도 자체에서 만들어진 것뿐 아니라 스페인 전역에서 상연될 모든 연극을 말이지요. 그의 승인과 검인과 서명이 없이는 어떤 지방 관청도 어떤 연극의 상연을 허락하지 않도록 하는 것입니다. 그렇게 되면 배우들은 자기네 연극을 수도로 보내도록 신경 쓸 것이고, 안전하게 그것을 상연할 수 있을 것이며, 그것을 쓰는 자들은 그 일을 아는 자의 엄정한 검토에 자기 작품을 제출해야 한다는 두려움에, 더 신중을 기하고 자기 일에 더 공을 들일 것입니다. 그리하여 좋은 연극이 만들어지고 그것이 노리는 목적도 흐뭇하게 이루어지겠지요. 사람들의 오락도, 스페인 재사들의 신망도, 배우들의 이익과 안전도, 또 그들에게 처벌을 내리는 수고를 더는 일도 말입니다. 그리고 만일 같은 사람이나 다른 누군가에게 새로 쓰인 기사도 책을 검토할 권한이 주어진다면, 틀림없이 참사회원 님께서 묘사하신 그 모든 완벽함을 갖춘 책들이 나타나, 우리 언어를 웅변의 우아하고 값진 보배로 풍요롭게 하고, 새로 나올 책들의 빛 앞에서 낡은 책들을 어둠 속으로 몰아넣을 것입니다. 그 새 책들은 한가한 자들뿐 아니라 더없이 바쁜 자들에게까지도 해롭지 않은 즐거움을 주려고 나올 테니까요. 활시위를 늘 당겨 놓을 수만은 없고, 연약한 인간 본성도 얼마간의 정당한 오락 없이는 존재할 수 없는 법이니 말입니다.\"\n\n참사회원과 신부가 대화를 여기까지 이어 왔을 때, 이발사가 앞으로 나와 그들에게 다가서며 신부에게 말했다. \"면허 신부 님, 여기가 한낮의 휴식을 취하는 동안 소들에게 싱싱하고 넉넉한 꼴을 먹이기에 좋다고 제가 말씀드린 그 자리입니다.\"\n\n\"그런 것 같군.\" 신부가 대답하고는 자기가 하려는 바를 참사회원에게 일러 주었다. 그러자 참사회원도 눈앞에 펼쳐진 아름다운 골짜기의 풍광에 이끌려 그들과 함께 멈추기로 마음을 굳혔다. 그 풍광을 즐기는 동시에, 자기가 차츰 호감을 품게 된 신부와의 대화를 누리고, 또 돈키호테의 행적에 관해 더 자세한 사정을 알기 위해서였다. 그는 하인 몇을 가까이 있는 주막으로 보내, 일행 전부가 먹을 만한 먹을거리를 거기서 가져오게 했다. 자기는 오후 동안 있던 자리에서 쉬려 했기 때문이다. 그러자 하인 하나가 대답하기를, 이맘때면 벌써 주막에 닿았을 짐 노새가 보리 말고는 주막에서 아무것도 구할 필요가 없을 만큼 넉넉한 양식을 싣고 있다고 했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 참사회원이 말했다. \"짐승들을 모두 그리로 데려가고, 짐 노새는 도로 끌고 오너라.\"\n\n이런 일이 벌어지는 동안, 산초는 자기가 의심을 품고 있던 신부와 이발사가 줄곧 곁에 있지 않은 틈을 타 주인에게 말을 건넬 수 있겠다 싶어, 돈키호테가 갇혀 있는 우리로 다가가 말했다. \"나리, 제 양심을 편하게 하려고 나리께 마법에 관한 사정이 어떤지 말씀드리고 싶습니다요. 그게 다름이 아니라, 여기 얼굴을 가린 저 두 사람은 우리 마을 신부님과 이발사거든요. 제 짐작에는, 나리께서 그분들보다 이름난 위업을 더 잘 이루신다는 이유로, 순전한 시샘에서 이런 식으로 나리를 끌고 가려는 꾀를 짜낸 듯합니다요. 그리고 이게 사실이라면, 나리께서는 마법에 걸리신 게 아니라 눈가림을 당하고 바보 취급을 당하시는 셈이지요. 이걸 증명하려고 나리께 한 가지 여쭤보고 싶습니다요. 그래서 제가 믿는 대로 나리께서 대답해 주시면, 나리께서는 그 속임수에 손가락을 짚을 수 있을 것이고, 나리께서 마법에 걸리신 게 아니라 그저 정신이 어긋나셨음을 아시게 될 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"무엇이든 묻고 싶은 대로 물어라, 내 아들 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내 너를 흡족하게 해 주고 네가 청하는 모든 것에 답해 주마. 네가 말하는바, 저기 우리와 동행하는 저들이 우리 이웃이자 아는 사이인 신부와 이발사라는 것은, 그들이 바로 그 사람들로 보일 수도 있는 일이니 충분히 가능하다. 허나 그들이 실제로, 사실상 그 사람들이라는 것은 결코 믿지 마라. 네가 믿고 생각해야 할 것은, 네 말대로 그들이 그들처럼 보인다면, 그것은 필시 나를 마법에 건 자들이 이 모습과 형상을 빌려 쓴 것이라는 점이다. 마법사들이 마음에 드는 어떤 형상이든 빌려 쓰기란 쉬운 일이니, 그들은 네가 지금처럼 생각하도록 만들고, 테세우스의 실타래를 가졌다 한들 빠져나올 수 없는 환상의 미궁으로 너를 끌어들이려고 우리 벗들의 형상을 빌렸을 수도 있다. 또한 그들은 내 마음을 어지럽혀, 이 화가 어디에서 내게 오는지 짐작조차 못 하게 하려고 그리했을 수도 있다. 한편으로 네가 우리 마을 이발사와 신부가 여기 우리와 함께 있다고 말하는데, 다른 한편으로 나 자신은 우리에 갇혀 있고, 초자연적인 것이 아닌 이 세상의 어떤 힘으로도 나를 가둘 수 없었으리라는 것을 내 마음속으로 알고 있으니, 마법에 걸린 편력 기사들을 다룬 그 모든 이야기에서 내가 읽은 모든 것을 뛰어넘는 종류의 마법이 내게 걸린 것이라고 말하고 생각하는 것 말고, 네가 나더러 무어라 말하거나 생각하라는 것이냐? 그러니 그들이 네 말하는 그런 자들이라는 생각일랑 마음을 놓아도 좋다. 내가 터키 사람인 만큼이나 그들도 그러한 자들이니 말이다. 다만 내게 무언가를 묻고 싶다는 네 바람으로 말하자면, 어서 말해 보아라. 네가 지금부터 내일 아침까지 질문을 던진다 한들 내 답해 주마.\"\n\n\"성모님, 저를 어여삐 여기소서!\" 산초가 목소리를 높여 말했다. \"나리께서 머리가 어찌나 둔하고 골이 어찌나 모자라시기에, 제 말이 꾸밈없는 진실이라는 것을, 또 나리께서 갇히고 불행을 겪으시는 데에는 마법보다 악의가 더 큰 몫을 했다는 것을 보지 못하신단 말입니까요? 허나 그러시다니, 나리께서 마법에 걸리지 않으셨음을 제가 환히 증명해 드리겠습니다요. 자, 말씀해 주십시오. 하느님께서 나리를 이 고난에서 건져 주시기를, 또 나리께서 미처 생각지도 못하실 때 둘시네아 마님의 품에 안기시게 되기를 빌면서——\"\n\n\"내게 주문 외우는 짓일랑 그만두어라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그리고 알고 싶은 것을 물어라. 내 이미 더없이 정확하게 답해 주겠다고 일렀느니라.\"\n\n\"바로 그걸 바라는 겁니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그리고 제가 알고 싶고 나리께 말씀해 주십사 하는 것은, 무엇 하나 보태거나 빼지 마시고, 으레 그러리라 기대되는 대로, 또 나리께서 표방하시는 그 편력 기사라는 칭호 아래 무예를 표방하는 모든 이가 으레 그러하듯, 온전한 진실을 말씀해 주시는 것입니다요——\"\n\n\"내 어떤 점에서도 거짓말하지 않으리라 말하지 않았느냐.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"어서 질문을 끝맺어라. 산초야, 정말이지 이 온갖 다짐이며 요구며 조심성으로 네가 나를 지치게 하는구나.\"\n\n\"그럼, 저는 제 주인 나리의 선하심과 진실하심을 믿겠습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그러니, 우리가 이야기하던 것과 관련된 일이라, 더없이 공손하게 여쭙겠습니다요. 나리께서 이 우리에 갇히신 뒤로, 또 나리 생각대로 마법에 걸리신 뒤로, 흔히들 말하듯 어디 좀 가고 싶은 마음이나 욕구를 느끼신 적이 있으신지요?\"\n\n\"'어디 좀 가다'라는 말은 못 알아듣겠구나.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"산초야, 내가 요점에 맞는 답을 해 주기를 바란다면, 좀 더 분명히 말해 보아라.\"\n\n\"이럴 수가 있나요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리께서 '어디 좀 가다'라는 말을 못 알아들으신다니요? 아니, 학교 아이들도 갓난쟁이 적부터 그건 압니다요. 그럼, 이렇게 말씀드리지요. 나리께서 피할 수 없는 그 일을 하고 싶은 마음이 드신 적이 있느냐는 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"아! 이제 알아듣겠다, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그래, 자주 있었고, 바로 이 순간에도 그렇구나. 나를 이 곤경에서 꺼내 다오. 그러지 않으면 만사가 순조롭지 못할 테니.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XLIX.\n\n\"아, 이제 당신을 붙잡았습니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"이것이야말로 제 마음속 깊이 알고 싶어 하던 것입니다. 자, 나리, 우리 주변에서 누군가 기분이 언짢을 때면 흔히들 하는 말을 부정하실 수 있겠습니까? '아무개가 무슨 탈이 났는지 모르겠어, 먹지도 않고, 마시지도 않고, 잠도 안 자고, 무엇을 물어도 제대로 대답을 안 하니. 마법에라도 걸린 게 아닌가 싶어' 하는 그런 말 말입니다. 거기서 알 수 있는 것은, 먹지도 마시지도 자지도 않고, 제가 말씀드리는 그런 자연스러운 일을 하나도 하지 않는 사람들, 그런 사람들이 마법에 걸린 거라는 겁니다. 나리처럼 욕구가 있어서, 마실 것을 주면 마시고, 먹을 것이 있으면 먹고, 묻는 말마다 다 대답하는 사람은 아니란 말이지요.\"\n\n\"네 말이 옳다, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"허나 이미 너에게 말하지 않았느냐, 마법에도 여러 종류가 있으며, 세월이 흐르는 동안 그것들이 서로 뒤바뀌었을 수도 있고, 이제는 마법에 걸린 자들이 내가 하는 모든 일을 하는 것이 그 방식이 되었을지도 모른다고 말이다. 비록 예전에는 그러지 않았다 해도 말이지. 그러니 시대의 관행을 두고 따지거나 추론을 끌어내는 것은 헛된 일이다. 나는 내가 마법에 걸려 있음을 알고 또 느끼고 있으며, 그것으로 내 양심을 편케 하기에 족하다. 만일 내가 마법에 걸린 것이 아니라고, 그저 겁 많고 비겁하게 이 우리 속에 누워 곤경과 고통에 처한 이들에게 베풀 수 있는 도움을 수많은 사람들에게서 빼앗고 있다고 여긴다면, 바로 이 순간에도 내 도움과 보호를 절실히 필요로 하고 있을 그들을 생각하면, 그 무게가 내 양심을 무겁게 짓누를 것이기 때문이다.\"\n\n\"그렇다 해도 말입니다.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"더 크고 더 온전한 만족을 위해서, 나리께서 이 감옥에서 빠져나오려 애써 보시는 게 좋겠습니다(저는 제 힘이 닿는 한 도와드리겠다고, 아니 직접 꺼내 드리겠다고 약속합니다). 그래서 마법에 걸린 것처럼 저리도 우울하고 풀 죽어 보이는 그 훌륭한 로시난테에 다시 한번 올라타실 수 있는지 보시는 겁니다. 그런 다음 우리는 다시 모험을 찾아 운을 시험해 보면 됩니다. 운이 따르지 않으면 그때 우리로 돌아갈 시간은 충분합니다. 만일 나리께서 운이 너무 없으시거나 제가 너무 어리석어 제 계획을 이뤄 내지 못하게 된다면, 착하고 충직한 종자의 신의를 걸고, 저도 나리와 함께 그 안에 갇히겠다고 약속드립니다.\"\n\n\"네 말대로 하기로 하마, 산초 형제여.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 나를 풀어 줄 기회를 보거든 나는 너에게 절대적으로 따르겠다. 허나 산초야, 너는 곧 알게 될 게다, 네가 내 불운을 두고 얼마나 잘못 생각하고 있는지를 말이다.\"\n\n편력 기사와 불운한 종자는 이미 말에서 내려 그들을 기다리고 있던 신부와 참사회원과 이발사가 있는 곳에 다다를 때까지 그렇게 대화를 이어 갔다. 짐수레꾼은 곧 소들의 멍에를 풀어 그 기분 좋은 푸른 풀밭을 마음껏 거닐도록 놓아주었으니, 그 싱그러움은 돈키호테처럼 마법에 걸린 사람이 아니라 그의 종자처럼 정신이 또렷하고 분별 있는 사람을 부르는 듯했다. 종자는 신부에게 자기 주인을 잠시 우리에서 나오게 해 달라고 청했다. 그를 내보내 주지 않으면 그런 신사 분의 체통이 요구하는 만큼 우리가 깨끗이 유지되지 못할 것이기 때문이었다. 신부는 그 뜻을 알아듣고, 기꺼이 청을 들어주고 싶지만 다만 자기 주인이 자유로워지면 옛 버릇이 도져 아무도 다시는 찾지 못할 곳으로 달아날까 두렵다고 말했다.\n\n\"그분이 달아나지 않으리란 것은 제가 책임지겠습니다.\" 산초가 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그러겠소.\" 참사회원이 말했다. \"특히 우리의 동의 없이는 우리를 떠나지 않겠다고 기사로서 약조해 준다면 말이오.\"\n\n이 모든 말을 듣고 있던 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그 약조를 드리겠소. 더구나 나처럼 마법에 걸린 자는 제 뜻대로 제 몸을 어찌할 수가 없소. 그를 마법에 건 자가 그를 세 시대 동안 한곳에서 움직이지 못하게 할 수도 있고, 만일 그가 달아나려 든다면 날아오게 하여 도로 끌고 올 수도 있는 까닭이오.\" 그러니 사정이 그러하므로 그들이 자기를 풀어 주어도 무방하며, 특히 그것이 모두에게 이로운 일이라고 했다. 만일 자기를 내보내 주지 않는다면, 그들이 멀찍이 떨어져 있지 않는 한 그들의 콧구멍을 거스르는 일을 피할 수 없으리라고 그는 단언했다.\n\n참사회원은 둘 다 묶여 있는 채로 그의 손을 잡았고, 그의 말과 약속을 믿고 그를 풀어 주었으니, 그는 우리 밖으로 나오게 된 것이 이루 말할 수 없이 기뻤다. 그가 맨 처음 한 일은 온몸을 쭉 펴는 것이었고, 그런 다음 로시난테가 서 있는 곳으로 가서 그 엉덩이를 두어 번 두드리며 말했다. \"준마들의 꽃이요 거울인 그대여, 나는 여전히 하느님과 그 복되신 어머니를 믿노라. 우리 둘 다 머지않아 바라던 그 모습으로 다시 서게 되리니, 그대는 네 주인을 등에 태우고, 나는 그대 위에 올라타, 하느님께서 나를 이 세상에 보내신 그 소명을 좇으리라.\" 그러고는 그렇게 말하며 산초를 데리고 외진 곳으로 물러갔다가, 한결 홀가분해져서, 종자의 계획을 실행에 옮기고 싶은 마음이 그 어느 때보다 간절해진 채로 돌아왔다.\n\n참사회원은 그를 바라보며, 그 광기의 유별난 성질에 놀랐으니, 그의 모든 말과 대답에서 그토록 훌륭한 분별을 보이다가도, 앞서 말한 대로 기사도라는 주제가 나오기만 하면 그만 등자를 놓치는 것이었다. 그리하여 연민에 사로잡혀, 모두가 푸른 풀밭에 앉아 음식이 도착하기를 기다리는 동안 그는 돈키호테에게 말했다.\n\n\"점잖으신 분이여, 그 메스껍고 부질없는 기사도 책 읽기가 당신의 이성을 뒤엎어 스스로 마법에 걸렸다고 믿게 하고, 거짓 그 자체만큼이나 진실에서 동떨어진 그런 온갖 것들을 믿게 할 만큼 당신에게 그토록 큰 영향을 미쳤다는 것이 가능한 일입니까? 세상에 그 무한히 많은 아마디스가 있었다거나, 그 수많은 이름난 기사들, 그 모든 트레비손다의 황제들, 히르카니아의 펠릭스마르테들, 그 모든 말들과 편력하는 시녀들과 뱀들과 괴물들과 거인들, 온갖 종류의 놀라운 모험과 마법, 전투와 굉장한 격돌, 화려한 의상, 사랑에 빠진 공주들, 백작이 된 종자들, 익살맞은 난쟁이들, 연애편지, 사랑의 속삭임, 칼 휘두르는 여인들, 한마디로 기사도 책에 담긴 그 모든 허튼소리가 다 있었다고 스스로 납득시킬 수 있는 인간의 지성이 어디 있겠습니까? 저로 말하자면, 그것들을 읽을 때 그것들이 모두 거짓이요 부질없는 것임을 멈춰 생각하지만 않는다면 어느 정도 즐거움을 얻는다고는 말할 수 있습니다. 그러나 그것들이 무엇인지를 따져 보기에 이르면, 그중 가장 훌륭한 것이라도 벽에 내던지고, 곁에 불이 있다면 불 속에 던져 넣고 싶어집니다. 보통의 관용 범위를 벗어난 사기꾼이요 협잡꾼으로서, 새로운 종파와 삶의 방식을 세운 자요, 무지한 대중을 이끌어 그 안에 담긴 온갖 어리석음을 진실로 믿고 받아들이게 하는 선생으로서, 그런 벌을 받아 마땅하니 말입니다. 게다가 그 책들의 대담함이 어찌나 큰지, 가문 좋고 지성 있는 신사들의 정신마저 어지럽힐 엄두를 내니, 그것들이 당신을 어떤 지경에 이르게 했는지 — 당신을 우리에 가두고, 돈벌이를 위해 사자나 호랑이를 이곳저곳 끌고 다니며 구경시키듯 소달구지에 실어 옮기게 만든 그 처지가 — 명백히 보여 주는 바입니다. 자, 돈키호테 님, 당신 자신에게 좀 연민을 가지시고, 상식의 품으로 돌아오시어, 하늘이 당신에게 기꺼이 베풀어 주신 그 넉넉한 몫을 활용하십시오. 당신의 풍부한 정신적 재능을 당신의 양심에 이로움을 주고 당신의 명예를 더해 줄 만한 다른 책 읽기에 쓰시면서 말입니다. 그래도 여전히 타고난 성향에 이끌려 무훈과 기사도에 관한 책을 읽고 싶으시거든, 성경의 사사기를 읽으십시오. 거기서 당신은 웅장한 실제와, 영웅적인 만큼이나 진실한 행적들을 보게 될 것입니다. 루시타니아에는 비리아투스가 있었고, 로마에는 카이사르가, 카르타고에는 한니발이, 그리스에는 알렉산드로스가, 카스티야에는 페르난 곤살레스 백작이, 발렌시아에는 시드가, 안달루시아에는 곤살로 페르난데스가, 에스트레마두라에는 디에고 가르시아 데 파레데스가, 헤레스에는 가르시 페레스 데 바르가스가, 톨레도에는 가르실라소가, 세비야에는 돈 마누엘 데 레온이 있었으니, 그들의 용맹한 행적을 읽는 것은 더없이 고매한 정신마저 즐겁게 하고 가르치며 기쁨과 경이로 채워 줄 것입니다. 여기에야말로, 돈키호테 님, 당신의 건전한 지성에 합당한 읽을거리가 있습니다. 그것을 통해 당신은 역사에 박식해지고, 미덕을 사랑하며, 선함이 굳건해지고, 행실이 나아지며, 무모하지 않으면서 용감하고, 비겁하지 않으면서 신중한 사람으로 일어서게 될 것입니다. 이 모든 것이 하느님의 영광이요, 당신 자신의 이로움이며, 제가 듣기로 당신께서 태어나신 곳인 라만차의 영예가 될 것입니다.\"\n\n돈키호테는 참사회원의 말을 더없이 주의 깊게 듣고 있다가, 그가 말을 마쳤음을 알자 한동안 그를 바라본 뒤 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"점잖으신 분이여, 제가 보기에 당신의 말씀은 세상에 편력 기사란 결코 없었으며, 기사도 책이란 모두 거짓되고 허위에 가득 차며 해롭고 나라에 쓸모없는 것이라고, 그래서 제가 그것들을 읽은 것이 잘못이고, 믿은 것은 더 큰 잘못이며, 그 책들이 펼쳐 보인 편력 기사라는 그 험난한 소명을 좇기로 했을 때 그것들을 흉내 낸 것은 더더욱 큰 잘못이라고 저를 설득하려는 데 그 뜻이 있는 듯합니다. 당신은 갈리아의 아마디스나 그리스의 아마디스, 혹은 그 책들에 가득한 다른 어떤 기사도 결코 없었다고 부정하시니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"모두 당신이 말씀하신 그대로입니다.\" 참사회원이 말하자, 돈키호테가 받아 말했다. \"당신은 또한 이런 종류의 책들이 제 정신을 뒤엎고 저를 우리에 가두었으니 제게 큰 해를 끼쳤다고, 그래서 제가 마음을 고쳐 공부를 바꾸어, 더 큰 즐거움과 가르침을 줄 더 진실한 다른 책들을 읽는 편이 나으리라는 말씀까지 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 참사회원이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 돈키호테가 받아 말했다. \"제가 보기에는 오히려 당신이야말로 제정신이 아니고 마법에 걸린 사람입니다. 진실로 그토록 널리 인정되고 받아들여진 것에 대해 그런 신성모독을 감히 입에 올리셨으니 말입니다. 당신처럼 그것을 부정하는 자는 누구든, 당신이 읽다가 거슬리는 책들에 가한다고 말씀하신 바로 그 벌을 받아 마땅합니다. 아마디스와, 그 책들에 가득한 다른 모든 모험 기사들이 결코 존재하지 않았다고 누군가를 설득하려는 것은, 해가 빛을 주지 않는다거나, 얼음이 차갑지 않다거나, 땅이 양식을 내지 않는다고 그를 설득하려는 것과 마찬가지일 것입니다. 세상의 어떤 지혜가, 플로리페스 공주와 부르고뉴의 기 이야기가 진실이 아니라거나, 샤를마뉴 시대에 일어난 피에라브라스와 만티블레 다리 이야기가 진실이 아니라고 남을 납득시킬 수 있겠습니까? 좋은 모든 것을 두고 맹세컨대, 그것은 지금이 대낮인 것만큼이나 진실입니다. 만일 그것이 거짓이라면, 헥토르나 아킬레우스나 트로이 전쟁이나 프랑스의 열두 용사나, 아직도 까마귀로 변한 채 살아 있어 그 왕국에서 끊임없이 사람들이 찾고 있다는 잉글랜드의 아서가 있었다는 것 또한 거짓이어야 할 것입니다. 차라리 과리노 메스키노의 이야기나 성배를 찾아 떠난 탐색의 이야기가 거짓이라거나, 트리스탄과 이졸데 왕비의 사랑이, 그리고 기네비어와 랜슬롯의 사랑이 거짓이라고 우기는 편이 나을 것입니다. 그레이트브리튼 최고의 술 시중꾼이었던 킨타뇨나 부인을 보았던 일을 거의 기억할 수 있는 사람들이 있는데도 말입니다. 이것이 어찌나 진실인지, 저는 친가 쪽 제 할머니 한 분을 기억하는데, 그분은 점잖은 두건을 쓴 부인을 볼 때마다 제게 이렇게 말씀하시곤 했습니다. '얘야, 저분이 킨타뇨나 부인을 닮았구나.' 그래서 저는 할머니께서 그분을 아셨거나, 적어도 그분의 초상을 어떻게든 보신 적이 있었으리라고 결론짓습니다. 그러니 피에레스와 아리따운 마갈로나의 이야기가 진실임을 누가 부정할 수 있겠습니까? 오늘날까지도 왕의 무기고에는 용맹한 피에레스가 공중을 가르며 탔던 그 나무 말을 조종하던 핀이 보이는데, 그것은 짐수레의 끌채보다 조금 더 클 정도입니다. 그 핀 곁에는 바비에카의 안장이 있고, 론세스바예스에는 큰 들보만큼이나 큰 롤단의 뿔나팔이 있으니, 그로 미루어 우리는 열두 용사와 피에레스와 시드와, 흔히 사람들이 모험가라 부르는 그런 부류의 다른 기사들이 있었음을 추론할 수 있습니다. 어쩌면 누군가는 제게, 부르고뉴로 가서 아라스 시에서 샤르니의 이름난 영주, 모센 피에레스라는 자와 싸우고, 그 뒤 바젤 시에서 모센 엔리케 데 레메스텐과 맞붙어 두 격전 모두에서 명성과 영예로 뒤덮여 나온 그 용맹한 루시타니아인 후안 데 메를로 같은 편력 기사는 없었다고 말할지도 모릅니다. 또 역시 부르고뉴에서 산 폴로 백작의 아들들을 무찔렀던 용맹한 스페인 사람 페드로 바르바와 구티에레 키하다(저는 이 가문의 직계 남자 혈통으로 이어집니다)가 이루고 응한 모험과 도전들도 없었다고 말이지요. 또한 제게, 돈 페르난도 데 게바라가 모험을 찾아 독일로 가서 오스트리아 공작 가문의 기사인 미세르 호르헤와 결투를 벌인 일도 없었다고 말할 것입니다. '파소'의 그 사람, 수에로 데 키뇨네스의 마상 시합과, 카스티야 기사 돈 곤살로 데 구스만에 맞선 모센 루이스 데 팔세스의 무훈도 한낱 우스갯거리에 불과했다고 말하겠지요. 그 밖에도 이 나라와 외국 영토의 기독교 기사들이 이룬 수많은 다른 행적들도 그러하다고 말입니다. 그것들은 어찌나 확실하고 진실한지, 거듭 말하거니와, 그것들을 부정하는 자는 이성과 양식이 완전히 결여된 자임이 틀림없습니다.\"\n\n참사회원은 돈키호테가 쏟아 내는 진실과 허구의 뒤범벅을 듣고, 또 그가 자기 편력 기사도의 행적과 관련되거나 거기 속한 모든 것에 대해 얼마나 훤히 꿰고 있는지를 보고 깜짝 놀랐다. 그래서 그는 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"돈키호테 님, 당신 말씀에 어느 정도 진실이 있음은 부정할 수 없습니다. 특히 스페인 편력 기사들에 관한 한 그러합니다. 또 프랑스의 열두 용사가 존재했다는 것도 기꺼이 인정하겠습니다. 다만 대주교 투르핀이 그들에 관해 이야기한 그 모든 일을 그들이 다 했다고는 믿고 싶지 않습니다. 사실인즉슨 그들은 프랑스 왕들이 가려 뽑은 기사들로서, 모두가 가치와 지위와 무용에서 동등했기에(적어도 그렇지 않았다면 응당 그래야 했기에) '용사'라 불렸던 것입니다. 그것은 오늘날의 산티아고나 칼라트라바 같은 일종의 종교 기사단이었으니, 그 단에 드는 자는 명망 있고 가문 좋은 용맹한 기사라고 여겨졌습니다. 또 지금 우리가 성 요한의 기사니 알칸타라의 기사니 하고 말하듯, 그들은 그때 열두 용사의 기사라 말했던 것이니, 그 무사 기단을 위해 동등한 열둘을 가려 뽑았기 때문입니다. 시드가 있었다는 것, 또 베르나르도 델 카르피오가 있었다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 그러나 사람들이 말하는 그런 행적을 그들이 했다는 것은 저로서는 매우 의심스럽게 여겨집니다. 당신이 말씀하신, 무기고에서 바비에카의 안장 곁에 있다는 그 피에레스 백작의 핀이라는 또 다른 문제에 대해서는, 제 죄를 고백하겠습니다. 저는 너무 어리석거나 너무 눈이 어두운 탓인지, 안장은 보았으나, 당신께서 말씀하신 만큼 그토록 크다는데도 그 핀만은 도무지 본 적이 없으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그래도 그것은 그곳에 분명히 있습니다, 조금도 의심할 바 없이.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"더구나 듣자니 그것은 녹슬지 않도록 쇠가죽 칼집에 넣어져 있다고 합니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠지요.\" 참사회원이 대답했다. \"그러나 제가 받은 성직을 두고 말하건대, 저는 그것을 본 기억이 없습니다. 어쨌든 그것이 그곳에 있다고 인정한다 해도, 그것이 그 모든 아마디스들과, 그들이 우리에게 이야기하는 그 수많은 기사들의 이야기를 제가 마땅히 믿어야 할 이유가 되지는 않습니다. 또한 당신처럼 훌륭하고 그토록 많은 좋은 자질을 갖추고, 그토록 뛰어난 지성을 타고난 분이, 그런 허무맹랑한 기사도 책에 적힌 그 거칠고 미친 것들이 정말로 진실이라고 스스로 납득하도록 내버려 두는 것은 도무지 사리에 맞지 않습니다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER L.\n\n“이거 참 우습기도 하구려!” 돈키호테가 대꾸했다. “국왕의 허가를 받아 인쇄되고, 심의를 맡은 이들의 인준을 거쳐, 만인이 즐겁게 읽고, 신분 높은 자나 낮은 자나, 부자나 가난한 자나, 학식 있는 자나 무지한 자나, 양반이나 평민이나, 한마디로 어떤 계급이든 어떤 처지든 온갖 부류의 사람들이 입을 모아 칭송하는 책들이라오. 그런 책들이 거짓이라니! 더구나 그토록 진실의 외양을 갖추고 있는데 말이오. 그 책들은 우리에게 어느 기사 혹은 기사들의 아버지와 어머니, 나라와 친족, 나이와 출신지, 그리고 그들이 한 발짝 한 발짝 날마다 이룬 위업들을 일러 주지 않소! 자, 그만하시오, 선생. 그런 불경한 말을 입에 담지 마시구려. 나를 믿고, 지금 내가 충고하는 대로 분별 있는 사람이 마땅히 할 일을 하시오. 그저 그 책들을 읽어 보시오. 그러면 그 책들에서 얻게 될 즐거움을 알게 될 것이오. 자, 어디 한번 말해 보시오. 바로 지금 우리 눈앞에 이런 광경이 펼쳐지는 것보다 더 흐뭇한 일이 어디 있겠소? 부글부글 끓어오르는 역청의 드넓은 호수가 펼쳐지고, 그 안에는 뱀과 살무사와 도마뱀과 온갖 사납고 무시무시한 짐승들이 우글우글 헤엄쳐 다니고, 그 호수 한복판에서 구슬픈 목소리가 들려오는 것이오. ‘기사여, 그대가 누구이든 이 무시무시한 호수를 바라보는 자여, 만일 그대가 이 어두운 물결 아래 숨겨진 상을 얻고자 한다면, 그대의 굳센 심장의 용맹을 증명하여 이 컴컴하고 불타는 물 한가운데로 몸을 던지라. 그러지 않으면 그대는 이 검은 수면 아래 일곱 요정의 일곱 성에 깃든 위대한 경이들을 볼 자격이 없으리라.’ 그러면 기사는 그 끔찍한 목소리가 채 그치기도 전에, 멈춰 서서 생각해 보지도 않고, 자신이 처할 위험을 헤아려 보려 잠시 멈추지도 않고, 심지어 묵직한 갑옷의 무게마저 벗어 내지 않은 채, 하느님과 자기 귀부인께 자신을 맡기고 그 펄펄 끓는 호수 한복판으로 뛰어든다오. 그리고 미처 예상치도 못하고 자기 운명이 어찌 될지 알지도 못하는 사이에, 엘리시온 들판도 견줄 수 없을 만큼 꽃이 만발한 풀밭 가운데에 있는 자신을 발견하게 되는 것이오.\n\n“그곳에선 하늘이 더욱 투명해 보이고, 태양은 야릇한 광채로 빛나며, 푸르고 잎이 무성한 나무들의 아늑한 숲이 눈앞에 펼쳐져 그 싱그러움으로 시선을 사로잡는다오. 한편 귀로는 얽히고설킨 가지 사이를 이리저리 날아다니는, 화려한 깃털을 두른 수많은 새들의 달콤하고 꾸밈없는 가락이 어루만져 주지요. 여기에는 맑은 물이 액체 수정처럼 고운 모래와, 체로 거른 황금이나 더없이 순결한 진주처럼 보이는 하얀 자갈 위로 잔물결을 일으키며 흐르는 시냇물이 보이고. 저기에는 갖가지 빛깔의 벽옥과 윤을 낸 대리석으로 정교하게 빚어 낸 샘이 눈에 들어오며, 또 여기에는 시골풍으로 만든 또 다른 샘이 있어, 작은 홍합 껍데기와 나선형으로 말린 희고 노란 달팽이 집들이 일부러 흐트러뜨린 듯 배치되어 반짝이는 수정 조각과 가짜 에메랄드와 어우러지니, 그것은 자연을 본뜬 기교가 도리어 자연을 능가한 듯한 다채로운 작품을 이루고 있다오.\n\n“그러다 문득 그의 눈앞에 단단한 성, 혹은 화려한 궁전이 나타나는데, 그 성벽은 묵직한 황금으로 되어 있고, 망루는 다이아몬드, 성문은 히아신스 보석으로 되어 있소. 요컨대 그 구조가 어찌나 경이로운지, 그것을 지은 재료가 다이아몬드, 홍옥, 루비, 진주, 황금, 에메랄드에 다름 아니건만, 그 솜씨는 그보다도 더 희귀하다오. 그리고 이 모든 것을 본 다음, 한 무리의 처녀들이 화려하고 곱게 차려입고 성문에서 걸어 나오는 모습을 보는 것보다 더 흐뭇한 일이 어디 있겠소? 그 차림새는 어찌나 곱던지, 이제 내가 그 옛이야기들이 우리에게 묘사해 주는 그대로 그것을 그려 내려 든다면 결코 끝맺지 못할 것이오. 그러고는 그들 가운데 으뜸으로 보이는 처녀가 펄펄 끓는 호수에 몸을 던진 그 대담한 기사의 손을 잡고, 한마디 말도 건네지 않은 채 그를 으리으리한 궁전, 혹은 성 안으로 이끌어, 어머니가 그를 낳았을 때처럼 발가벗기고는, 미지근한 물에 목욕시키고, 향기로운 연고를 온몸에 발라 주며, 더없이 부드러운 비단 셔츠를 입혀 주는데, 그 셔츠는 온통 향기를 머금고 있소. 그러는 동안 또 다른 처녀가 와서 그의 어깨에 망토를 걸쳐 주는데, 그 망토는 적어도 도시 하나 값어치는 되고, 어쩌면 그보다 더하다고들 한다오. 그러니 이 모든 일이 끝난 뒤에 그 처녀들이 그를 또 다른 방으로 이끌고, 거기에서 그가 어찌나 멋들어지게 차려진 식탁들을 보았던지 경탄과 경이로 가득 차게 되었다는 이야기를 들려줄 때, 그것이 또 얼마나 흐뭇하겠소. 그들이 호박과 향기로운 꽃에서 증류해 낸 물을 그의 두 손에 부어 주는 모습이며, 상아 의자에 그를 앉히는 모습이며, 처녀들이 깊은 침묵 속에서 그의 시중을 드는 모습이며, 너무도 먹음직스럽게 차려진 갖가지 진미를 가져다주어 어느 것을 골라야 할지 입맛이 갈피를 못 잡는 모습이며, 그가 식탁에 앉아 있는 동안 누가 어디서 빚어내는지도 모를 음악이 울려 퍼지는 소리를 듣는 모습까지 말이오. 그러고 나서 식사가 끝나고 식탁들이 치워지면, 기사는 여느 때처럼 어쩌면 이를 쑤시며 의자에 기대어 있고, 다른 어느 처녀보다도 한결 어여쁜 처녀가 뜻밖에 방문으로 들어와 그의 곁에 앉아서는, 이 성이 무엇인지, 그리고 자신이 어떻게 이곳에 마법에 걸려 갇혀 있는지를 이야기하기 시작하고, 그 밖에 기사를 놀라게 하고 그의 이야기를 읽는 독자들을 경탄케 하는 온갖 것들을 들려준다오.\n\n“그러나 이 일을 더 길게 늘어놓지는 않겠소. 다만 이로써 알 수 있는 것은, 편력기사의 그 어떤 이야기든 그 어느 대목을 읽더라도, 읽는 이가 누구든 간에 그를 즐거움과 경이로 가득 채워 주리라는 점이오. 그러니 내 충고를 들으시오, 선생. 앞서 말했듯이 이 책들을 읽어 보시오. 그러면 이 책들이 선생이 느낄 법한 어떤 우울도 어떻게 몰아내 주는지, 의기소침할 때면 어떻게 기운을 북돋아 주는지 알게 될 것이오. 나로 말할 것 같으면, 편력기사가 된 이후로 나는 용감하고 정중하고 너그럽고 예의 바르고 도량이 넓고 공손하고 두려움을 모르고 온화하고 인내심 있는 사람이 되었으며, 온갖 고난과 옥살이와 마법을 견디는 법을 배웠다고 말할 수 있소. 그리고 내가 미치광이처럼 우리에 갇힌 신세가 된 지 비록 이토록 얼마 되지 않았지만, 하늘이 나를 돕고 운명이 나를 가로막지 않는다면, 이 팔의 힘으로 어느 왕국의 왕이 되어 내 마음속에 깃든 감사와 너그러움을 보일 수 있게 되기를 바라오. 정말이지, 선생, 가난한 자는 비록 너그러움의 미덕을 더없이 드높이 지니고 있다 한들 누구에게도 그것을 베풀 수 없는 처지이고, 마음가짐만으로 그치는 감사란 죽은 것이니, 행함이 없는 믿음이 죽은 것과 마찬가지요. 이런 까닭에 운명이 머지않아 내게 황제가 될 어떤 기회를 베풀어 주어, 내가 벗들에게, 특히 세상에서 둘도 없이 좋은 친구인 내 종자 이 가엾은 산초 판사에게 선을 베풀며 내 마음을 보일 수 있게 된다면 더없이 기쁘겠소. 진작부터 그에게 약속해 둔 백작령을 기꺼이 그에게 주고 싶으나, 다만 그가 자기 영지를 다스릴 만한 역량이 없을까 봐 그것이 걱정될 따름이오.”\n\n산초는 주인의 이 마지막 말을 어느 정도 알아듣고는 그에게 말했다. “돈키호테 나리, 그토록 자주 약속하셨고 제가 그토록 오래 기다려 온 그 백작령을 부디 힘써 주십시오. 약속하건대 저는 그것을 다스리는 데 역량이 모자라지 않을 것입니다요. 설령 모자란다 해도, 세상에는 영지를 한 해 얼마씩 바치고 도지로 부쳐서, 정작 통치는 자기들이 도맡아 하고, 영주는 다리를 쭉 뻗고 앉아 그들이 바치는 수익을 누리며 다른 일에는 신경도 쓰지 않는다는 자들이 있다고 들었습니다요. 저도 바로 그렇게 할 작정입니다요. 자질구레한 일로 흥정하며 다투지 않고, 단번에 그 골치 아픈 일에서 손을 씻어 버리고는, 공작님처럼 제 소작료나 누리면서 만사는 제멋대로 흘러가게 둘 작정입니다요.”\n\n“산초 형제,” 사제 참사회원이 말했다. “그것은 어디까지나 수익을 누리는 데에만 들어맞는 이야기일세. 그러나 영지의 영주는 재판을 다스리는 일에 마음을 써야 하는데, 여기에는 역량과 건전한 판단력이 필요하고, 무엇보다도 진실을 밝혀내려는 굳건한 결의가 필요하다네. 처음에 이것이 없으면 중간도 끝도 언제나 어그러지고 말 테니까. 하느님께서는 흔히 단순한 자들의 정직한 뜻을 도우시는 만큼이나, 교활한 자들의 사악한 책략을 좌절시키신다네.”\n\n“저는 그런 철학들은 모르겠습니다요.” 산초 판사가 대꾸했다. “제가 아는 거라곤, 그것을 다스릴 줄 알게 되는 즉시 그 백작령을 갖고 싶다는 것뿐입니다요. 저도 남만큼 영혼이 있고, 누구 못지않게 몸뚱이도 있으니, 다른 왕이 자기 왕국의 왕이듯 저도 제 영지의 왕이 될 것입니다요. 그리되면 저는 하고 싶은 대로 할 것이고, 하고 싶은 대로 하면 제 마음에 들 것이고, 제 마음에 들면 만족할 것이고, 사람이 만족하면 더 바랄 것이 없고, 더 바랄 것이 없으면 그것으로 끝입니다요. 그러니 어서 그 백작령이나 들어오라지요. 그럼 안녕히, 또 봅시다, 어느 장님이 다른 장님에게 말했듯이 말입니다요.”\n\n“자네가 늘어놓는 것이 그리 나쁜 철학은 아닐세, 산초.” 사제 참사회원이 말했다. “하지만 그래도 이 백작령 문제에 관해서는 할 말이 적지 않다네.”\n\n이에 돈키호테가 대꾸했다. “더 할 말이 무엇이 있는지 나는 모르겠소. 나로 말하면 그저 저 위대한 ‘갈리아의 아마디스’가 자기 종자를 인술라 피르메의 백작으로 삼았을 때 내게 보여 준 본보기를 따를 따름이오. 그러니 양심에 아무런 거리낌 없이 나는 산초 판사를 백작으로 삼을 수 있소. 그는 일찍이 편력기사가 거느린 종자 가운데 가장 훌륭한 자 중 하나이기 때문이오.”\n\n사제 참사회원은 돈키호테가 늘어놓은, 조리 있는 헛소리(헛소리에 조리가 있을 수 있다면)에 놀랐고, 그가 호수의 기사 모험을 묘사한 방식에 놀랐으며, 그가 읽은 책들의 그 작정하고 꾸며 낸 거짓말들이 그에게 남긴 인상에 놀랐고, 마지막으로 주인이 약속한 백작령을 그토록 간절히 얻고자 하는 산초의 순박함에 경탄해 마지않았다.\n\n이즈음 짐 싣는 노새를 가지러 주막으로 갔던 사제 참사회원의 하인들이 돌아왔고, 양탄자 한 장과 풀밭의 푸른 잔디를 식탁 삼아 그들은 몇몇 나무 그늘에 자리를 잡고 거기에서 식사를 했는데, 앞서 말한 대로 마부가 그 좋은 자리의 이점을 빼앗기지 않도록 하기 위함이었다. 그들이 먹고 있는데 별안간 큰 소리와 함께 방울 소리가 들려왔으니, 그것은 가까이 있던 가시덤불과 무성한 수풀 사이에서 나는 듯했다. 바로 그 순간 그들은 온통 검고 희고 갈색 얼룩이 진 아름다운 염소 한 마리가 덤불에서 튀어나오고, 그 뒤를 염소지기 하나가 쫓아오며 멈추거나 우리로 돌아오게 하려고 으레 내지르는 소리를 외쳐 대는 것을 보았다. 달아나던 염소는 겁에 질려 마치 그들에게 보호를 청하기라도 하듯 일행 쪽으로 달려오더니 그 자리에 멈춰 섰고, 다가온 염소지기는 그 뿔을 붙잡고는 마치 그 짐승이 이성과 분별을 갖추기라도 한 양 말을 걸기 시작했다. “아, 떠돌이야, 떠돌이야, 얼룩아, 얼룩아, 그동안 어찌하여 이리 절뚝거리며 다녔느냐? 무슨 늑대가 너를 놀라게 했느냐, 내 딸아? 무슨 일인지 내게 말해 주지 않으련, 내 어여쁜 것아? 허나 네가 암컷이어서 가만히 있지 못하는 것 말고 무슨 까닭이 더 있겠느냐? 네 변덕과, 네가 닮은 것들의 변덕에 염병이나 들어라! 돌아오너라, 돌아와, 내 귀여운 것아. 네가 그리 행복하지 못할지언정, 적어도 우리 안에서나 네 동무들과 함께라면 안전할 것이 아니냐. 마땅히 저들을 지키고 이끌어야 할 네가 길을 잃고 헤맨다면, 저들은 대체 어찌 되겠느냐?”\n\n염소지기의 말은 그것을 듣는 모든 이를 즐겁게 했으나, 특히 사제 참사회원이 그러하여 그에게 말했다. “여보게, 자네 목숨을 두고 부탁하건대, 마음을 좀 누그러뜨리고, 이 염소를 우리로 몰아넣으려고 그리 서두르지 말게나. 자네 말마따나 암컷이니, 자네가 아무리 막으려 애쓴들 그 타고난 본성을 따를 것이 아닌가. 이 고기 한 점 들고 한 모금 마시게나. 그러면 화가 가라앉을 것이고, 그동안 염소도 쉴 것일세.” 이렇게 말하며 그는 차게 식은 토끼 뒷다리 살을 포크에 꽂아 그에게 건넸다.\n\n염소지기는 고맙다며 그것을 받아 들고는, 한 모금 마시고 마음을 가라앉힌 뒤에 말했다. “제가 이 짐승에게 그토록 진지하게 말을 걸었다고 하여 나리들께서 저를 얼간이로 여기신다면 섭섭하겠습니다. 하오나 사실인즉, 제가 쓴 그 말에는 어떤 사연이 깃들어 있답니다. 저는 촌사람이지만, 사람과 짐승에게 어떻게 처신해야 할지 모를 만큼 그리 촌스럽지는 않습니다.”\n\n“그야 나도 능히 믿네.” 신부가 말했다. “이미 경험으로 알고 있거니와, 숲은 학식 있는 사람들을 길러 내고, 목동들의 오두막은 철학자들을 품는 법이니까.”\n\n“어쨌든, 나리,” 염소지기가 대꾸했다. “그곳들은 경험 많은 사람들을 품지요. 그리고 이것이 사실임을 나리들께서 보고 또 헤아리실 수 있도록, 비록 청하지도 않았는데 제가 주제넘게 나서는 듯 보일지라도, 나리들께서 싫증 내지 않으시고 잠시 귀를 기울여 주신다면, 저 나리의 말씀과(그러면서 그는 신부를 가리켰다) 제 말을 아울러 입증해 줄 참된 이야기 하나를 들려 드리겠습니다.”\n\n이에 돈키호테가 대답했다. “이 일에 어딘가 기사도의 빛깔이 깃들어 있는 듯하니, 나로서는, 여보게, 자네 이야기를 더없이 기쁘게 듣겠고, 여기 이 신사분들도 모두 그러할 것이오. 이분들은 드높은 지성을 지닌 데다, 마음을 끌고 매혹하고 즐겁게 해 주는 진기한 이야기들을 사랑하는 분들이니 말이오. 자네 이야기가 분명 그러하리라 나는 확신하오. 그러니 시작하시게, 친구. 우리 모두 들을 채비가 되어 있으니.”\n\n“저는 판돈을 거두겠습니다요.” 산초가 말했다. “그러고는 이 고기 파이를 들고 저기 시냇가로 물러가, 사흘치 양식을 든든히 채워 둘 작정입니다요. 우리 주인이신 돈키호테 나리께서 말씀하시기를, 편력기사의 종자는 기회가 닿을 때마다 더는 들어가지 않을 만큼 먹어 두어야 한다고 하셨거든요. 흔히 우연찮게 어찌나 빽빽한 숲에 들어서는지, 엿새가 지나도록 빠져나갈 길을 찾지 못하는 일이 잦은데, 그때 사람이 든든히 배를 채워 두지 않았거나 자루를 잘 채워 두지 않았으면, 거기서 흔히 그렇듯 바싹 마른 미라가 되어 버릴 수도 있다고 하셨으니까요.”\n\n“네 말이 옳다, 산초.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “가고 싶은 데로 가서 먹을 수 있는 만큼 먹어라. 나는 이미 충분하니, 그저 이 착한 친구의 이야기를 들으며 마음에 새 기운을 불어넣어 주고 싶을 따름이다.”\n\n“우리 모두 그렇게 할 것이오.” 사제 참사회원이 말하고는, 염소지기에게 약속한 이야기를 시작해 달라고 청했다.\n\n염소지기는 뿔을 잡고 있던 염소의 등을 두어 번 토닥이며 말했다. “여기 내 곁에 누우려무나, 얼룩아. 우리 우리로 돌아갈 시간은 넉넉하니 말이다.” 염소는 그의 말을 알아듣는 듯했으니, 주인이 자리에 앉자 그 곁에 얌전히 몸을 누이고는, 이제부터 그가 하려는 말에 온통 귀를 기울이고 있음을 보이려는 양 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그러고는 다음과 같은 말로 그는 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n# CHAPTER LI.\n\n이 골짜기에서 삼 레구아 떨어진 곳에 마을이 하나 있는데, 비록 작기는 하나 이 일대에서 가장 부유한 마을 가운데 하나입니다. 그곳에 한 농부가 살았으니, 매우 훌륭한 사람으로 어찌나 존경받았던지, 부유하면 자연히 그리되기 마련이긴 하지만, 그는 자신이 쌓은 재산보다도 그 덕성으로 인해 한층 더 존경을 받았습니다. 그러나 그를 더더욱 복된 사람으로 만든 것은, 그 자신의 말마따나, 빼어난 아름다움과 보기 드문 지혜와 우아함과 덕성을 두루 갖춘 딸을 두었다는 점이었으니, 그녀를 알고 또 본 사람이라면 누구나 하늘과 자연이 그녀에게 베푼 그 비범한 천품에 경탄해 마지않았습니다. 어린아이 적부터 그녀는 아름다웠고, 자라면서 더욱 아름다워졌으며, 열여섯 나이에 이르러서는 더없이 어여뻤습니다. 그녀의 아름다움에 관한 명성이 주변의 모든 마을로 퍼져 나가기 시작했지요. 아니, 어찌 주변 마을이라고만 하겠습니까, 그 명성이 먼 도시들에까지 미치고, 심지어 왕궁의 홀에까지 흘러들어 온갖 계층 사람들의 귀에 닿았으니, 사람들은 무언가 희귀하고 진기한 것이라도, 혹은 기적을 행하는 성상이라도 보러 오듯 사방에서 그녀를 보러 몰려왔던 것입니다.\n\n그녀의 아버지는 그녀를 지켜보았고 그녀 또한 스스로를 지켰으니, 젊은 처녀를 그 자신의 정숙함보다 더 잘 지켜 줄 수 있는 자물쇠도, 감시인도, 빗장도 없는 법이지요. 아버지의 재산과 딸의 아름다움은 같은 마을 이웃들뿐 아니라 외지 사람들까지 그녀를 아내로 삼고자 찾아오게 만들었습니다. 그러나 그토록 값진 보석을 처분할 권한을 쥔 자가 으레 그러하듯, 그는 어찌할 바를 몰라 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 구혼자 가운데 누구에게 딸을 맡겨야 할지 마음을 정하지 못했습니다. 저 또한 이 지극히 자연스러운 소망을 품은 많은 이들 가운데 하나였는데, 그녀의 아버지가 제가 누구인지를 알고 있었던 데다, 저는 같은 마을 사람이요, 순수한 혈통에, 한창나이였으며, 재산도 매우 넉넉했기에 큰 기대를 품고 있었습니다. 같은 마을 출신에 같은 조건을 갖춘 또 다른 사람도 그녀를 구했으니, 이로 인해 그녀 아버지의 선택은 저울 위에 매달린 듯 기울지 못했지요. 그는 우리 둘 중 누구에게든 딸을 잘 시집보내는 것이라 여겼기 때문입니다. 그리하여 이 곤혹스러운 처지에서 벗어나고자 그는 이 일을 레안드라에게 맡기기로 결심했는데(저를 이 비참한 처지로 몰아넣은 그 부유한 처녀의 이름이 바로 레안드라입니다), 우리 둘이 동등하니 사랑하는 딸이 제 마음 가는 대로 고르게 하는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 생각했던 것이지요. 이는 자식을 한세상 자리 잡게 해 주고자 하는 모든 아버지가 본받을 만한 처사입니다. 제 말은 자식들에게 천하고 나쁜 것을 고르게 내버려 두라는 뜻이 아니라, 좋은 것을 그들 앞에 차려 놓고 그런 다음 제 마음껏 좋은 선택을 하도록 허락하라는 뜻입니다. 레안드라가 누구를 골랐는지는 저도 모릅니다. 다만 그녀의 아버지가 딸이 아직 어리다는 핑계와, 자신을 묶지도 않고 우리를 물리치지도 않는 모호한 말로 우리 둘을 미루어 두었다는 것만은 압니다. 제 연적의 이름은 안셀모요, 저 자신은 에우헤니오라 합니다. 이 비극에 등장하는 인물들의 이름을 아시도록 말씀드리는 것이지요. 그 비극의 결말은 아직 매듭지어지지 않았으나, 참담할 수밖에 없으리라는 것은 빤히 내다보입니다.\n\n이 무렵 우리 마을에 비센테 데 라 로카라는 자가 들어왔는데, 그는 같은 마을의 가난한 농부의 아들로, 이탈리아를 비롯한 여러 곳에서 병사로 복무하고 돌아온 자였습니다. 한 대위가 우연히 자기 중대를 이끌고 그곳을 지나다가, 그가 열두 살 남짓한 소년이었을 적에 우리 마을에서 데려갔던 것인데, 이제 열두 해가 지나 그 젊은이가 병사의 군복 차림으로 돌아온 것입니다. 갖가지 빛깔로 치장하고, 온몸에 유리 장신구며 번쩍이는 강철 사슬을 두른 채로 말이지요. 오늘은 이 화려한 옷을 입고 나타나고, 내일은 또 저 옷을 입고 나타나곤 했는데, 하나같이 얄팍하고 번지르르하기만 할 뿐 알맹이도 없고 값어치도 더더욱 없는 것들이었습니다. 천성이 짓궂은 데다 할 일이 없으면 짓궂음 그 자체가 되어 버리는 농사꾼들은 이 모든 것을 눈여겨보아, 그의 화려한 옷과 장신구를 하나하나 따져 보고는, 그가 빛깔이 다른 세 벌의 옷에 거기 맞춘 대님과 양말을 가졌을 뿐임을 알아냈지요. 그런데 그가 그것들을 어찌나 요리조리 짜 맞추고 바꿔 입었던지, 일일이 세어 보지 않았더라면 누구든 그가 열 벌 넘는 옷과 스무 개의 깃 장식을 늘어놓고 자랑했다고 맹세했을 정도였습니다. 제가 옷에 관해 들려드리는 이 모든 이야기를 쓸데없거나 장황한 것으로 여기지는 마십시오, 이 이야기와 큰 관련이 있으니까요. 그는 우리 광장의 큰 포플러나무 아래 의자에 앉아, 자기 무용담을 늘어놓아 우리 모두가 입을 떡 벌린 채 거기에 매달리게 만들곤 했습니다. 그가 가 보지 못한 나라가 이 지구상에 없었고, 끼어들지 않은 전투가 없었습니다. 그는 모로코와 튀니스에 있는 무어인보다 더 많은 무어인을 죽였고, 제 입으로 떠벌리는 바에 따르면 가르실라소며 디에고 가르시아 데 파레데스며 그 밖에 그가 들먹인 천 명의 인물보다 더 많은 일대일 결투를 치렀으되, 그 모든 싸움에서 피 한 방울 흘리지 않고 승리하여 빠져나왔다고 했지요. 한편으로는 상처 자국을 내보이기도 했는데, 분간조차 되지 않는 것을 두고 그는 여러 교전과 전투에서 입은 총상이라고 했습니다. 끝으로 그는 무지막지하게 뻔뻔스럽게도 자기와 동등한 사람들, 심지어 그가 어떤 자인지 다 아는 사람들에게까지 \"너\"라고 불러대며, 제 팔이 곧 제 아비요 제 무공이 곧 제 가문이며, 자기는 병사이니 임금 그 자신만큼이나 훌륭하다고 큰소리쳤습니다. 게다가 이런 허풍에 더해 그는 음악도 조금 할 줄 알아, 기타를 어찌나 멋들어지게 켜는지 어떤 이들은 그가 기타를 말하게 만든다고까지 했지요. 그의 재주는 여기서 그치지 않았으니, 그는 시인 노릇도 조금 했던지라 마을에서 일어나는 사소한 일마다 일 레구아 길이의 발라드를 지어내곤 했습니다.\n자, 그래서 제가 말씀드린 이 병사, 이 비센테 데 라 로카, 이 허풍쟁이요 멋쟁이요 음악가요 시인인 자를, 레안드라는 광장이 내려다보이는 자기 집 창문에서 자주 보고 또 지켜보았습니다. 그의 화려한 옷의 번쩍임이 그녀의 마음을 사로잡았고, 그의 발라드가 그녀를 호렸으며(그는 자기가 지은 발라드마다 스무 부씩 나눠 주었으니까요), 그가 제 입으로 들려주는 무용담이 그녀의 귀에 와닿았습니다. 요컨대, 의심할 바 없이 악마가 그렇게 꾸며 놓은 것이겠지요, 그가 감히 그녀에게 구애할 생각을 떠올리기도 전에 그녀가 먼저 그에게 반해 버린 것입니다. 그런데 사랑의 일에서 여인의 마음이 한편이 되어 주는 것보다 더 쉽게 결말로 치닫는 것은 없는 법이라, 레안드라와 비센테는 아무런 어려움 없이 서로 뜻을 통하게 되었습니다. 그리하여 그녀의 그 숱한 구혼자 가운데 누구도 그녀의 속셈을 눈치채기도 전에, 그녀는 이미 그 일을 실행에 옮겨, 끔찍이도 사랑하는 아버지의 집을 떠나(어머니는 없었으니까요) 그 병사와 함께 마을에서 자취를 감추고 말았으니, 그 병사로 말하면 자신이 떠벌린 그 많은 모험 가운데 어느 것에서보다도 이 거사에서 더 의기양양하게 빠져나온 셈이었지요. 온 마을과 이 일을 들은 모든 이가 이 사건에 경악했습니다. 저는 망연자실했고, 안셀모는 벼락 맞은 듯했으며, 그녀의 아버지는 슬픔에 잠겼고, 친척들은 분개했으며, 관청은 온통 들끓었고, 형제단의 관원들은 무장에 나섰습니다. 그들은 길이란 길은 다 뒤지고, 숲이며 사방을 다 수색하여, 사흘 만에 산속 동굴에서 그 경솔한 레안드라를 찾아냈는데, 속옷만 걸친 채 집에서 가지고 나온 돈과 값진 보석은 죄다 빼앗긴 모습이었습니다.\n\n* 형제단: 산타 에르만다드(Santa Hermandad). 스페인의 시골 지역 치안을 맡던 일종의 무장 자경 조직.\n그들은 그녀를 가엾은 아버지에게 데려와 그 불행에 관해 캐물었는데, 그녀는 다그치지 않아도 순순히 털어놓기를, 비센테 데 라 로카가 자기를 속였으며, 결혼해 주겠다는 약속으로 자기를 꾀어 아버지의 집을 떠나게 했으니, 자기를 온 세상에서 가장 부유하고 가장 즐거운 도시인 나폴리로 데려가겠노라 했다는 것입니다. 그리고 자기는 어리석게도 속아 넘어가 그 말을 믿고는 아버지의 것을 훔쳐, 자취를 감춘 그날 밤 그 모두를 그에게 넘겨주었으며, 그가 자기를 험준한 산으로 끌고 가 그들이 찾아낸 그 동굴 안에 가두었다고 했지요. 그녀는 또 말하기를, 그 병사가 자기의 정조만은 빼앗지 않은 채, 가진 것을 죄다 앗아 가고는 자기를 동굴에 남겨 둔 채 달아났다고 했으니, 이것이 또 모두를 한층 더 놀라게 했습니다. 그 젊은이의 절제를 믿기란 우리로서는 쉬운 일이 아니었으나, 그녀가 어찌나 간곡하게 단언하던지 그것이 비탄에 잠긴 아버지를 위로하는 데에 도움이 되었습니다. 한번 잃으면 결코 되찾을 수 없는 그 보석이 딸에게 남겨졌으니, 빼앗긴 것쯤은 아무것도 아니라고 그가 여겼던 것이지요. 레안드라가 모습을 드러낸 바로 그날, 그녀의 아버지는 그녀를 우리 눈앞에서 거두어, 이 근처의 어느 마을 수도원에 가두려고 데려가 버렸습니다. 그녀가 저지른 불명예를 세월이 얼마간 닳아 없애 주기를 바라면서 말이지요. 레안드라의 어린 나이는 그녀의 잘못에 대한 변명거리가 되어 주었으니, 적어도 그녀가 선한지 악한지가 아무래도 상관없는 이들에게는 그러했습니다. 그러나 그녀의 영악함과 지혜를 아는 이들은 그녀의 잘못을 무지의 탓으로 돌리지 않고, 방종과 여자의 천성 탓으로 돌렸으니, 그 천성이란 대개 들뜨고 절제가 없는 법이지요.\n\n레안드라가 눈앞에서 사라지자 안셀모의 눈은 멀어 버린 듯, 아니 적어도 어떤 즐거움도 주지 못하는 것 외에는 바라볼 것을 찾지 못하게 되었고, 제 눈 또한 레안드라가 떠나 있는 동안 어떤 즐거운 것으로도 이끌어 줄 한 줄기 빛도 없는 어둠 속에 잠겼습니다. 우리의 우울은 더욱 깊어지고, 우리의 인내는 더욱 줄어들었지요. 우리는 그 병사의 화려한 옷을 저주하고, 레안드라 아버지의 부주의함을 욕했습니다. 마침내 안셀모와 저는 마을을 떠나 이 골짜기로 오기로 뜻을 모았으니, 그는 자기 소유의 큰 양 떼를 치고 저는 제 소유의 큰 염소 떼를 치면서, 우리는 나무들 사이에서 우리의 슬픔을 토해 내고, 함께 아름다운 레안드라를 찬미하거나 그녀를 책망하거나, 아니면 홀로 한숨지으며 외로이 하늘을 향해 우리의 하소연을 쏟아 내며 세월을 보냅니다. 우리를 본받아 레안드라의 다른 연인들도 여럿 이 거친 산으로 와서 우리의 생활 방식을 따랐는데, 그 수가 어찌나 많은지 이곳이 목가적인 아르카디아로 변해 버린 듯, 양치기와 양 우리로 가득합니다. 이곳에는 아름다운 레안드라의 이름이 들리지 않는 자리가 한 군데도 없지요. 여기서는 한 사람이 그녀를 저주하며 변덕스럽고 경박하고 정숙하지 못하다 하고, 저기서는 또 한 사람이 그녀를 연약하고 들떴다고 나무랍니다. 이쪽은 그녀를 용서하고 사면하는가 하면, 저쪽은 그녀를 내치고 욕합니다. 한 사람은 그녀의 아름다움을 추켜올리고, 또 한 사람은 그녀의 품성을 헐뜯습니다. 요컨대 모두가 그녀를 욕하면서도 모두가 그녀를 흠모하니, 이 보편적인 미혹이 어찌나 극에 달했던지, 그녀와 단 한마디도 나눠 본 적 없으면서 그녀의 냉대를 한탄하는 자들이 있는가 하면, 그녀가 누구에게도 빌미를 준 적 없는 질투의 격렬한 열병을 슬퍼하며 애통해하는 자들마저 있습니다. 앞서 말씀드렸듯이 그녀의 비행은 그녀의 정열에 앞서 알려졌으니까요. 바위 틈새며 시냇가며 나무 아래 그늘 어디든, 어느 양치기가 산들바람에 제 시름을 풀어놓지 않는 자리가 없습니다. 메아리가 있는 곳이라면 어디든 레안드라의 이름을 되울리고, 산들은 \"레안드라\" 소리로 울려 퍼지며, 시냇물은 \"레안드라\" 하고 속삭이니, 레안드라는 우리 모두를 얼떨떨하게 또 호린 채로 붙들어 두어, 희망 없이 희망하고 무엇을 두려워하는지도 모르면서 두려워하게 만듭니다. 이 어리석은 무리 가운데 가장 분별이 없으면서도 또한 가장 분별이 있는 자가 제 연적 안셀모인데, 그는 한탄할 다른 일이 그토록 많은데도 오로지 이별만을 한탄하며, 자기가 솜씨 좋게 켜는 레벡 반주에 맞추어 제 재치를 드러내는 시로 그 한탄을 노래하지요. 저는 다른, 더 쉬운, 제 생각으로는 더 현명한 길을 택했으니, 그것은 곧 여자들의 경박함과 변덕과 표리부동과 깨어진 약속과 지키지 않은 맹세를, 한마디로 그들이 제 애정과 마음을 정함에 있어 보이는 그 분별 없음을 욕하는 것입니다. 여러분, 이것이 바로 제가 조금 전에 올라오면서 이 염소에게 퍼부었던 말과 표현의 까닭입니다. 이놈이 암컷인지라 제 우리에서 으뜸가는 놈이긴 해도 저는 이놈을 경멸하지요. 이것이 제가 들려드리겠다고 약속한 이야기입니다. 이야기를 들려드리느라 지루하게 해 드렸다면, 여러분을 대접하는 일에는 굼뜨지 않겠습니다. 제 오두막이 바로 가까이에 있으니, 그곳에는 신선한 우유와 맛깔스러운 치즈가 있고, 보기에도 입에도 두루 즐거운 갖가지 맛난 과일도 있습니다.\n* 레벡: 활로 켜는 중세의 현악기.\n\n# CHAPTER LII.\n\n염소지기의 이야기는 듣고 있던 모든 이를 크게 만족시켰는데, 그중에서도 특히 신부 참사회원이 즐거워했으니, 그는 이야기가 펼쳐지는 방식을 각별한 주의를 기울여 눈여겨보았던 것이다. 그것은 촌스러운 염소지기의 말투와는 도무지 닮지 않았고, 오히려 세련된 도회지 재담가의 말투에 가까웠다. 그리하여 그는 숲이 학식 있는 사람들을 길러 낸다고 한 신부의 말이 참으로 옳았다고 여겼다. 모두가 에우헤니오에게 도움을 베풀겠다고 나섰으나, 그중에서도 이 점에서 가장 후하게 자기를 내보인 이는 돈키호테였으니, 그는 그에게 이렇게 말했다. \"분명히 말하건대, 염소지기 형제여, 만일 내가 어떤 모험에 나설 만한 처지에 있었다면, 나는 바로 이 순간 그대를 위하여 길을 떠나, 레안드라를 그 수도원에서 — 틀림없이 제 뜻과는 달리 갇혀 있을 그곳에서 — 구해 냈을 것이오. 원장 수녀를 비롯하여 나를 막으려는 그 누구도 아랑곳하지 않고 말이오. 그러고는 그녀를 그대의 손에 맡겨 그대의 뜻과 마음대로 처분하게 하였을 것이나, 다만 그 어떤 처녀에게도 어떠한 폭력도 행해서는 안 된다고 정한 기사도의 법도만은 지키게 했을 것이오. 그러나 나는 우리 주 하느님을 믿거니와, 한 사악한 마법사의 힘이 제아무리 크다 한들, 더 선한 뜻을 품은 다른 마법사의 힘이 그보다 우월하지 못할 만큼은 아닐 것이오. 그때가 오면 나는 그대에게 나의 지지와 도움을 약속하리니, 이는 내 본분이 명하는 바요, 그 본분이란 다름 아니라 약하고 궁핍한 자를 돕는 일이기 때문이오.\"\n\n염소지기는 그를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 돈키호테의 초라한 행색과 몰골을 알아채고는 경이로움에 사로잡혀, 곁에 있던 이발사에게 물었다. \"나리, 저런 꼴을 하고 저런 투로 말하는 저 사람은 대체 누굽니까?\"\n\n\"누구긴 누구겠나,\" 이발사가 말했다. \"저 유명한 라만차의 돈키호테가 아닌가. 불의를 무너뜨리고, 그릇된 것을 바로잡으며, 처녀들을 보호하고, 거인들을 두려움에 떨게 하며, 싸움마다 이기는 분이지.\"\n\n\"그거 참,\" 염소지기가 말했다. \"편력 기사 이야기책에서나 읽을 법한 말이로군요. 그 기사들이야말로 나리께서 저 사람이 한다고 하신 그 모든 일을 했으니 말입니다. 하지만 제 생각엔, 나리께서 농을 하고 계시거나, 아니면 저 양반 머릿속이 텅 빈 셋방 같은 게 아닐까 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"이 고약한 놈 같으니,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"텅 비고 어리석은 건 바로 너로다. 나로 말하면 너를 낳은 그 화냥년의 암캐보다도 가득 차 있느니라.\" 그러고는 말에서 행동으로 옮겨, 곁에 있던 빵 덩이를 집어 들어 염소지기의 얼굴 한복판에 정통으로 날렸는데, 어찌나 세게 던졌던지 그의 코가 납작해지고 말았다. 그러나 농담을 알아듣지 못하는 데다 정말로 호되게 당했다고 여긴 염소지기는, 깔개도 식탁보도 식사하던 이들도 아랑곳하지 않고 돈키호테에게 달려들어 두 손으로 그의 멱살을 움켜쥐었으니, 만일 그 순간 산초 판사가 구하러 오지 않았던들 틀림없이 그를 목 졸라 죽였으리라. 산초는 그의 어깨를 붙들어 식탁 위로 내동댕이쳤고, 그 바람에 접시가 깨지고 잔이 부서지며 식탁 위의 온갖 것이 뒤엎어져 흩어졌다. 자유로워진 돈키호테는 염소지기 위에 올라타려고 안간힘을 썼고, 얼굴이 온통 피투성이가 되고 산초에게 흠씬 걷어차인 염소지기는 네 발로 기어 다니며 피로 복수하려고 식탁 칼 하나를 더듬어 찾고 있었다. 그러나 신부 참사회원과 신부가 그것을 막았고, 한편 이발사는 용케 솜씨를 부려 돈키호테를 제 밑에 깔고는 그에게 주먹세례를 어찌나 퍼부었던지, 가엾은 기사의 얼굴에서도 제 얼굴 못지않게 피가 줄줄 흘렀다. 신부 참사회원과 신부는 웃음을 터뜨려 배를 잡았고, 형제단 관원들은 좋아라 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰었으며, 이쪽이고 저쪽이고 마치 싸우며 서로 물어뜯는 개들을 부추기듯 그들을 부추겼다. 오직 산초만이 미칠 듯이 안달했으니, 그는 신부 참사회원의 하인 하나가 붙잡고 놓아주지 않아 주인을 도우러 갈 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n마침내, 서로 두들겨 패고 있던 두 싸움꾼을 빼고 모두가 한껏 흥에 겨워 즐기고 있을 때, 나팔 소리가 어찌나 구슬프게 울렸던지 그 모든 이가 소리 나는 듯한 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다. 그러나 그 소리를 듣고 가장 흥분한 이는 돈키호테였으니, 그는 자기 뜻과는 너무도 어긋나게 염소지기 밑에 깔려 어지간히 흠씬 두들겨 맞은 처지였음에도 그에게 이렇게 말했다. \"악마 형제여(그대가 악마가 아니고서야 내 힘을 누를 만큼 강하고 힘셀 수는 없는 노릇이니), 그대에게 청하건대 단 한 시간만 휴전에 동의해 다오. 우리 귀에 들려오는 저 나팔의 엄숙한 소리가 내게는 어떤 새로운 모험으로 나를 부르는 것만 같으니 말이다.\" 이때쯤이면 두들겨 패고 또 두들겨 맞는 데 지칠 대로 지친 염소지기는 즉시 그를 놓아주었고, 돈키호테는 벌떡 일어나 소리가 들려온 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸다가, 문득 언덕 비탈을 내려오는 여러 사람을 보았으니, 그들은 고행자들처럼 흰옷을 입고 있었다.\n\n사실인즉, 그해에는 구름이 땅에 비를 내려 주지 않아, 그 지방의 모든 마을에서 행렬과 기도 행진과 고행을 벌이며 하느님께 자비의 손을 열어 비를 내려 주십사 빌고 있었다. 그리하여 가까이 있던 한 마을 사람들이 그 골짜기 한편에 있는 어느 성스러운 암자로 행렬을 지어 가는 중이었다. 돈키호테는 고행자들의 기이한 차림을 보자, 전에도 그런 모습을 얼마나 자주 보았던가는 돌이켜 생각하지도 않고, 이것이야말로 모험의 한 경우이며 편력 기사인 자기 혼자만이 떠맡아야 할 일이라고 단정해 버렸다. 게다가 그들이 지니고 가는, 검은 천을 두른 어떤 성상(聖像)이 저 비열한 자들과 무례한 도적들이 강제로 끌고 가는 어느 고귀한 부인이라는 생각이 들면서, 이 확신은 한층 더 굳어졌다. 이런 생각이 머리에 떠오르자마자 그는 마음대로 풀밭을 뜯고 있던 로시난테에게 온 힘을 다해 달려가, 안장 앞가지에서 고삐와 방패를 집어 들고는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 말에 굴레를 씌웠으며, 산초를 불러 칼을 받아 들고 로시난테에 올라타 방패를 팔에 동여매고는, 곁에 서 있던 이들에게 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"자, 고귀한 분들이여, 이제 그대들은 편력 기사도를 받드는 기사들이 이 세상에 있다는 것이 얼마나 중요한 일인지 보게 되리라. 이제, 내 말하노니, 그대들은 저기 사로잡혀 끌려가는 저 가련한 부인을 구해 냄으로써, 편력 기사들이 존경받아 마땅한 존재인지 아닌지를 보게 되리라.\" 그러고는 두 다리로 로시난테의 옆구리를 죄었으니 — 박차가 없었던 까닭이다 — 전속력으로 달려(이 진실한 이야기를 통틀어 로시난테가 제대로 질주했다는 대목은 한 번도 읽은 적이 없으므로) 고행자들과 맞서러 나아갔다. 신부와 신부 참사회원과 이발사가 그를 막으려고 달려갔으나, 그것은 그들의 힘에 부치는 일이었고, 산초가 뒤에서 외쳐 부르는 소리에도 그는 멈추지 않았다. \"어디로 가시는 겁니까, 돈키호테 나리? 대체 무슨 악마가 씌었기에 우리 가톨릭 신앙에 맞서려 드십니까? 빌어먹을, 정신 차리세요, 저건 고행자들의 행렬이고, 저 받침대에 모시고 가는 부인은 티 없으신 동정 마리아의 거룩한 성상이란 말입니다. 무슨 짓을 하시는지 조심하세요, 나리. 이번만큼은 나리께서 무슨 일을 벌이시는지도 모르신다고 단언해도 좋을 지경입니다.\" 그러나 산초의 수고는 헛일이었으니, 그의 주인은 이 천을 둘러쓴 무리와 맞붙어 검은 옷의 부인을 구해 내겠다는 일념에 사로잡혀 한마디도 듣지 못했고, 설령 들었다 한들 임금이 명령했다 해도 돌아서지 않았으리라. 그는 행렬에 다가가 로시난테의 고삐를 당겨 세웠는데, 로시난테로서도 이미 걸음을 조금 늦추고 싶어 안달이 나 있던 참이었다. 그러고는 쉬고 들뜬 목소리로 외쳤다. \"그대들, 얼굴을 가린 자들이여, 어쩌면 떳떳한 백성이 못 되어 그러는 것이렷다. 정신 차리고 내가 그대들에게 하려는 말을 들으라.\" 가장 먼저 멈춰 선 이들은 성상을 메고 가던 자들이었고, 호칭 기도를 노래하던 네 명의 성직자 가운데 하나가, 돈키호테의 괴이한 몰골과 로시난테의 비쩍 마른 꼴, 그 밖에 눈에 띈 우스꽝스러운 별난 점들에 놀라 그에게 대답했다. \"형제여, 우리에게 할 말이 있거든 어서 하시오. 이 형제들이 제 몸에 채찍질을 하고 있는 터라 우리는 멈출 수가 없고, 무슨 말을 들으려고 멈추는 것도 도리가 아니오. 정 그렇다면 두 마디로 끝낼 만큼 짧은 것이라야 하오.\"\n\n\"한마디로 하겠소,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그것은 이러하오. 당장, 바로 이 순간에 저 아름다운 부인을 풀어 주시오. 그분의 눈물과 슬픈 모습이 그대들이 그분을 뜻에 거슬러 끌고 가며 그분께 어떤 파렴치한 폭행을 저질렀음을 분명히 드러내고 있소. 그리고 바로 이런 온갖 그릇된 일을 바로잡고자 세상에 태어난 나는, 그대들이 그분께 그분이 애타게 그리며 누려 마땅한 자유를 되돌려 줄 때까지는 단 한 걸음도 더 나아가도록 허락하지 않겠소.\"\n\n이 말을 듣고 모두는 그가 미친 사람임에 틀림없다고 결론짓고는 한바탕 호탕하게 웃기 시작했는데, 그 웃음은 돈키호테의 분노에 화약처럼 불을 댕겼으니, 그는 더 말할 것도 없이 칼을 빼 들고 받침대로 돌진했다. 그것을 떠받치던 자들 가운데 하나가 짐을 동료들에게 맡기고 그를 맞으러 나서서, 받침대를 쉴 때 받쳐 두려고 가지고 있던 끝이 갈라진 막대기를 휘둘렀는데, 이 막대기로 돈키호테가 내리친 굉장한 일격을 받아 막대기가 두 동강이 났다. 그러나 손에 남은 그 토막으로 그는 돈키호테의 칼 잡은 팔 어깨에 어찌나 호된 일격을 가했던지(방패도 이 촌스러운 공격만은 막아 내지 못했다), 가엾은 돈키호테는 비참한 꼴로 땅바닥에 나동그라지고 말았다.\n\n씩씩거리며 헐떡이며 바로 뒤따라오던 산초 판사는 그가 쓰러지는 것을 보고는, 공격한 자에게 더는 치지 말라고 소리쳤으니, 그는 가엾은 마법에 걸린 기사로서 한평생 누구도 해친 적이 없는 분이라는 것이었다. 그러나 그 촌놈을 멈추게 한 것은 산초의 외침이 아니라, 돈키호테가 손도 발도 까딱하지 않는 것을 본 일이었다. 그리하여 그를 죽였다고 여긴 그는 허둥지둥 옷자락을 허리띠 밑으로 걷어 올리고는 사슴처럼 들판을 가로질러 줄행랑을 쳤다.\n\n이때쯤 돈키호테의 일행이 모두 그가 쓰러진 곳으로 달려왔다. 그러나 행렬을 짓던 이들은 그들이 달려오는 것을, 게다가 석궁을 든 형제단 관원들까지 함께 오는 것을 보고는 좋지 않은 일을 짐작하고, 성상 둘레로 모여들어 두건을 치켜올리고 채찍을 움켜쥐었으며, 사제들도 촛대를 굳게 쥐고는 공격에 대비하여, 스스로를 지킬 뿐 아니라 가능하다면 공격해 온 자들에게 맞받아 나설 작정으로 기다렸다. 그러나 운명은 그들이 예상한 것보다 일을 더 잘 풀어 주었으니, 산초가 한 일이라고는 주인의 몸뚱이에 몸을 내던지고 그 위에서 일찍이 들어 본 적 없을 만큼 구슬프고도 우스꽝스러운 통곡을 늘어놓은 것뿐이었으니, 그는 주인이 죽은 줄로만 알았던 것이다. 신부는 행렬 속에 걷던 또 다른 신부와 아는 사이였는데, 두 사람이 서로 알아보면서 양쪽의 불안이 가라앉았다. 그러고 나서 앞의 신부가 뒤의 신부에게 돈키호테가 누구인지를 두 마디로 일러 주자, 그와 고행자 무리 전체가 그 가엾은 양반이 죽었는지 보러 갔다가, 산초 판사가 눈물을 글썽이며 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"오, 기사도의 꽃이시여, 막대기 한 방에 그토록 잘 쓰신 생애의 길을 마치셨단 말입니까! 오, 그대 가문의 자랑이시여, 온 라만차의, 아니 온 세상의 명예요 영광이시여, 그대가 없으니 세상은 악행을 저지르고도 더는 그 죄에 대한 벌을 두려워하지 않는 자들로 가득 차겠나이다! 오, 모든 알렉산드로스보다도 너그러우신 분이시여, 고작 여덟 달의 봉직에 그대는 바다가 두르고 에워싼 가장 좋은 섬을 제게 주셨나이다! 오만한 자에게 겸손하고, 겸손한 자에게 오만하며, 위험에 맞서고, 모욕을 견디며, 까닭 없이 사랑에 빠지고, 선한 이를 본받으며, 악한 자를 채찍질하고, 비열한 자의 적이셨던, 한마디로 편력 기사이셨으니, 그 이상 무슨 말이 더 필요하겠나이까!\"\n\n산초의 울부짖음과 신음 소리에 돈키호테는 정신을 차렸고, 그가 맨 처음 한 말은 이러했다. \"그대에게서 떨어져 사는 자는, 더없이 어여쁜 둘시네아여, 이런 것들보다 더 큰 비참함을 견뎌야 한다오. 산초 친구여, 저 마법에 걸린 수레에 오르도록 나를 도와다오. 이 어깨가 산산조각이 나서 로시난테의 안장을 짓누를 형편이 못 되니 말이다.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다요, 나리,\" 산초가 말했다. \"그리고 나리를 위하는 이분들과 함께 우리 마을로 돌아가시지요. 거기서 또 한 번 출정을 준비하면 우리에게 더 득이 되고 더 명예로운 일이 될지도 모르지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"네 말이 옳다, 산초,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"지금 기승을 부리는 별들의 사악한 기운이 지나가도록 두는 것이 현명한 일이겠지.\"\n\n신부 참사회원과 신부와 이발사는 그가 말한 대로 하는 것이 참으로 현명한 처사라고 일러 주었다. 그리하여 그들은 산초 판사의 어수룩한 말들에 한껏 즐거워하며 돈키호테를 전처럼 수레에 실었다. 행렬은 다시금 차례를 갖추어 제 길을 따라 나아갔고, 염소지기는 일행에게 작별을 고했으며, 형제단 관원들은 더 가지 않겠다고 하여 신부가 그들에게 마땅한 삯을 치렀다. 신부 참사회원은 신부에게 돈키호테의 형편이 어떤지, 그의 광기가 나았는지 아니면 여전히 앓고 있는지 알려 달라고 청한 다음, 길을 마저 가게 해 달라고 양해를 구했다. 요컨대 그들은 모두 헤어져 제 갈 길을 갔고, 신부와 이발사, 돈키호테와 산초 판사, 그리고 제 주인 못지않게 무던히 모든 것을 받아들이던 착한 로시난테만이 남았다. 수레꾼은 소들에 멍에를 메우고 돈키호테를 건초 더미 위에 편안히 뉘인 뒤, 늘 그렇듯 느릿한 걸음으로 신부가 일러 준 길을 잡아 나아갔으며, 엿새 만에 그들은 돈키호테의 마을에 다다라 한낮 무렵 마을로 들어섰다. 마침 그날이 일요일이라 사람들이 모두 광장에 나와 있었는데, 돈키호테의 수레가 그 광장을 가로질러 지나갔다. 모두가 수레에 무엇이 실렸는지 보려고 몰려들었고, 그것이 제 고장 사람임을 알아보고는 경이로움에 사로잡혔으니, 한 소년이 달려가 가정부와 조카딸에게 그들의 주인이자 외삼촌이 비쩍 마르고 누렇게 뜬 몰골로 소달구지의 건초 더미 위에 뻗은 채 돌아왔다는 소식을 전했다. 그 두 착한 여인이 내지른 울부짖음을, 가슴을 치며 저 저주받은 기사도 책들에 새삼스레 악담을 퍼붓는 모습을 듣자니 참으로 가여웠는데, 그 모든 것이 돈키호테가 문으로 들어서는 것을 보자 다시금 터져 나왔다.\n\n돈키호테가 도착했다는 소식에 산초 판사의 아내가 달려왔으니, 그녀는 이미 제 남편이 그의 종자로 따라나섰다는 것을 알고 있던 터라, 산초를 보자마자 맨 처음 물은 것은 나귀가 잘 있느냐는 것이었다. 산초는 나귀가 잘 있노라고, 주인보다 더 잘 있노라고 대답했다.\n\n\"하느님께 감사할 일이네요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내게 이리 잘해 주시니. 하지만 이제 말해 봐요, 여보, 종노릇으로 뭘 벌어 왔어요? 내게 무슨 치마를 가져왔어요? 애들 신발은요?\"\n\n\"그런 건 아무것도 안 가져왔소, 여보,\" 산초가 말했다. \"그래도 더 중하고 값진 다른 것들을 가져왔지.\"\n\n\"그거 참 반가운 소리네요,\" 그의 아내가 대꾸했다. \"그 더 값지고 중하다는 것들을 보여 줘요, 여보. 당신이 떠나 있던 그 오랜 세월 내내 슬프고 무겁기만 했던 내 마음을 좀 달래게 보고 싶으니까요.\"\n\n\"집에 가서 보여 주리다, 여보,\" 산초가 말했다. \"지금은 그쯤으로 만족하구려. 하느님 뜻이 닿아 우리가 다시 모험을 찾아 길을 떠나게 된다면, 머지않아 내가 백작이 되거나 어느 섬의 총독이 된 걸 보게 될 테니. 그것도 흔해 빠진 그런 섬이 아니라 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 섬 말이오.\"\n\n\"하늘이 그리되게 해 주시기를, 여보,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"정말이지 우리에게 그게 필요하니까요. 하지만 말해 봐요, 그 섬이라는 게 대체 무슨 소리예요? 도무지 못 알아듣겠으니.\"\n\n\"꿀이 나귀 입에 어울리는 건 아니지,\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"때가 되면 다 보게 될 거요, 여보. 아니, 당신은 온 신하들이 당신을 '마님'이라 부르는 걸 듣고 깜짝 놀라게 될 거외다.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리예요, 산초, 마님이니 섬이니 신하니 하면서?\" 테레사 판사가 대꾸했다 — 산초의 아내는 그렇게 불렸으니, 둘이 친척 사이는 아니었으나, 라만차에서는 아내가 남편의 성을 따르는 것이 관습이었던 까닭이다.\n\n\"이 모든 걸 그리 서둘러 알려고 하지 마오, 테레사,\" 산초가 말했다. \"내가 당신에게 진실을 말하고 있다는 것으로 족하니, 입을 다물구려. 다만 지나는 말로 이만큼은 일러 줄 수 있소. 세상에 편력 기사의 종자요 모험을 찾아다니는 자로서 존중받는 사람이 되는 것보다 더 즐거운 일은 없다는 것을. 하기야 마주치는 모험들 대부분이 바라는 만큼 유쾌하게 끝나지는 않지. 백 개 가운데 아흔아홉은 어긋나고 뒤틀려 버리니 말이오. 나는 그걸 몸소 겪어 안다오. 어떤 데서는 담요에 싸여 키질을 당해 나왔고, 또 어떤 데서는 흠씬 두들겨 맞고 나왔으니까. 그래도 그 모든 것에도 불구하고, 무슨 일이 일어날까 살피는 것은 멋진 일이오. 산을 넘고, 숲을 뒤지고, 바위를 기어오르고, 성을 찾아가고, 객줏집에 묵으면서, 그 모든 걸 공짜로 하고, 마라베디 한 푼 낼 일은 악마에게나 던져 주는 거지.\"\n\n산초 판사와 그의 아내 사이에 이런 이야기가 오가는 동안, 돈키호테의 가정부와 조카딸은 그를 안으로 들여 옷을 벗기고 옛 침대에 뉘었다. 그는 그들을 곁눈질로 바라보았으나 자기가 어디에 있는지조차 가늠하지 못했다. 신부는 조카딸에게 외삼촌을 편안히 모시는 데 각별히 마음을 쓰고, 그가 다시 그들에게서 달아나지 못하도록 잘 지켜보라고 단단히 당부하면서, 그를 집으로 데려오기 위해 자기들이 어쩔 수 없이 했던 일을 일러 주었다. 이 말에 두 여인은 다시금 목소리를 높여 기사도 책들에 악담을 퍼붓고, 그런 거짓말과 헛소리를 지어낸 자들을 바닥 모를 구렁텅이 한가운데로 처넣어 달라고 하늘에 빌었다. 요컨대 그들은 외삼촌이자 주인이 몸이 좀 나아지는 순간 자기들을 따돌리고 빠져나가지 않을까 하는 근심과 두려움에 사로잡혀 있었는데, 그들이 두려워하던 그대로 일은 벌어지고 말았다.\n\n그러나 이 이야기의 작가는 돈키호테가 세 번째 출정에서 이룬 행적을 밝혀내고자 연구와 노고를 쏟아부었음에도, 적어도 믿을 만한 문헌에서 비롯된 정보라고 할 만한 것은 그 행적에 관하여 무엇 하나 얻어 내지 못했다. 다만 전승이 라만차의 기억 속에 보존해 온 사실이라고는, 돈키호테가 세 번째로 집을 나섰을 때 사라고사로 발걸음을 옮겨, 그 도시에서 열린 몇몇 이름난 마상 시합에 참석했으며, 거기서 그의 용맹과 드높은 지혜에 걸맞은 모험들을 겪었다는 것뿐이다. 그의 최후와 죽음에 관하여는 어떤 자세한 내막도 알아낼 수 없었을 것이며, 만일 행운이 그에게 한 늙은 의사를 보내 주지 않았던들 그것을 확인하지도, 알지도 못했으리라. 그 의사가 한 납 상자를 가지고 있었는데, 그의 말에 따르면 그 상자는 다시 짓고 있던 어느 옛 암자의 허물어진 기초 사이에서 발견되었다고 한다. 그 상자 안에서는 고딕 문자로 쓰였으되 카스티야어 운문으로 된 양피지 사본 몇 점이 나왔는데, 거기에는 그의 행적 가운데 많은 것이 담겨 있었고, 둘시네아의 아름다움과 로시난테의 생김새, 산초 판사의 충직함, 그리고 돈키호테 자신의 매장이, 그의 생애와 성품에 관한 갖가지 묘비명과 찬사와 더불어 적혀 있었다. 그러나 읽고 판독할 수 있었던 것이라고는, 이 새롭고도 견줄 데 없는 이야기의 믿음직한 작가가 여기 내놓는 것들뿐이었다. 그리고 이 작가는, 그것을 세상에 드러내기 위하여 라만차의 문서고를 살피고 뒤지는 데 들인 막대한 노고에 대하여, 그것을 읽을 이들에게 그 어떤 보답도 바라지 않거니와, 다만 세상에 널리 퍼져 그토록 인기를 누리는 기사도 책들에 분별 있는 이들이 보내는 것과 똑같은 신뢰를 자기에게도 보내 주기만을 바랄 뿐이다. 이만하면 그는 스스로 넉넉히 보상받았고 충분히 만족했다고 여길 것이며, 비록 이만큼 진실하지는 못할지언정 적어도 착상에서는 견줄 만하고 재미에서도 못지않은 다른 이야기들을 찾아내어 내놓을 용기를 얻을 것이다. 납 상자에서 발견된 양피지에 맨 처음 적혀 있던 말은 이러했다.\n\n라만차의 한 마을 아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원들이 라만차의 돈키호테의 삶과 죽음에 관하여 이를 적었노라 아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원, 모니콩고,\n\n# ON THE TOMB OF DON QUIXOTE EPITAPH\n\n돈키호테의 무덤에 부쳐 묘비명 이아손의 것보다도 더 풍성한 노획물을 라만차에 안겨 준 그 얼빠진 자, 그 재치 어찌나 날카로웠던지, 차라리 그 재치의 풍향계가 더 무딘 데를 가리켰던들 더 행복했으리. 가에타의 기슭만큼, 카타이만큼, 그 사이에 놓인 온 땅만큼 멀리 떨치던 그 팔뚝. 옛적에 청동에 새겼던 그 어떤 시신(詩神)만큼 신중하고도 그 위세 무시무시한 시신. 사랑과 무용에 떠받쳐 모든 아마디스를 능가하고, 갈라오르 무리를 한낱 아무것도 아닌 듯 여기며, 벨리아니스 무리를 풀 죽게 만들고, 로시난테에 올라 명성을 좇던 그가, 여기, 이 차가운 돌 아래에 누워 있노라.\n\n아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원, 파니아구아도, 엘 토보소의 둘시네아를 기리며\n\n소네트 여기 그려진 두툼한 이목구비, 봉긋한 가슴, 멸시하는 듯한 자태를 지닌 그녀는 둘시네아, 위대한 라만차의 돈키호테가 그녀를 위하여 헛되이 한숨짓던 바로 그녀라. 엘 토보소의 여왕인 그녀를 위하여 그는 이 끝에서 저 끝까지, 험준한 산맥과 아랑후에스의 들판, 몬티엘의 이름난 벌판을 로시난테에 올라 곧잘 고달프게 누볐노라. 사악한 별들, 잔혹한 운명이 저 아름다운 라만차의 부인과 정복되지 않는 기사도의 별, 그 둘을 뒤쫓았더라. 젊음도 미모도 그녀를 죽음의 손아귀에서 구해 내지 못하였고, 그는 사랑의 쓰디쓴 대가를 치르고 제 이름을 간직하라 대리석을 남겼노라.\n\n아르가마시야의 더없이 예리한 아카데미 회원, 카프리초소, 라만차의 돈키호테의 군마 로시난테를 기리며\n\n소네트 전쟁의 신 마르스의 피 냄새 풍기는 발이 욕보이는, 저 금강석처럼 빛나는 드높은 옥좌 위에, 이제 미친 라만차 사나이의 깃발이 그에 의해 그 온갖 위풍으로 펄럭였노라. 거기에 그는 제 무기와 잘 드는 칼날을 걸었으니, 이제껏 보지 못한 위업을 이루며 그것으로 그는 베고, 쓰러뜨리고, 가르고, 찍었더라. 허나 무예는 우리의 새 용사를 위한 새로운 양식을 빚어냈도다. 아마디스가 갈리아의 드높은 자랑이라면, 그의 후예들로 그리스의 명성이 온 누리의 방방곡곡에 퍼졌다면, 무시무시한 벨로나의 전당에서 왕관을 쓴 위대한 키호테는 오늘 이들 위에 라만차를 드높이며, 그녀는 그리스도 갈리아도 위에 고개를 쳐드노라. 그의 영광은 여기서 그치지 않으니, 그의 좋은 군마는 브리야도르와 바야르를 한참 능가하는도다. 혈기 넘치는 군마들도 로시난테에 견주면, 그들이 얻은 명성은 보잘것없으니.\n\n아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원, 부를라도르, 산초 판사에 관하여 소네트 여기 그대가 보는 이는 갸륵한 산초 판사라. 그 작은 몸뚱이 안에 한때 큰 영혼이 깃들었고, 이 지상의 공 위에 그토록 꾸밈없고 소박하며 간계라곤 없는 종자는 다시 없었더라. 백작이 되기 일보 직전까지 갔으니, 이 비열하고 옹졸하며 천박한 시대의 악의와 앙심만 아니었던들 백작이 되었으련만, 이 시대는 나귀 한 마리조차 가만두지 못하는도다. 나귀에 올라(이 말을 용서하시라) 로시난테의 곁에서 이 온순한 종자는 떠도는 제 주인을 따르곤 하였더라. 편안함과 마음의 소망을 약속하며 무리를 꾀어내는 헛된 희망이여, 너희는 늘 그림자와 꿈과 연기 속에서 스러지는도다.\n\n아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원, 카치디아블로, 돈키호테의 무덤에 부쳐 묘비명\n\n# CACHIDIABLO, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DON QUIXOTE EPITAPH\n\n여기 이 아래 기사가 누워 있으니, 편력에 서툴고 호되게 멍든 자라, 로시난테가 그를 태우고 이리저리 떠돌았더라. 기사의 곁에는 우직한 사내 산초 또한 뉘어 있으니, 종자라는 생업에서 그보다 더 진실한 종자는 없었더라.\n\n아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원, 티키톡, 엘 토보소의 둘시네아의 무덤에 부쳐\n\n# TIQUITOC, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DULCINEA DEL TOBOSO\n\n묘비명 여기 둘시네아가 누워 있노라. 토실토실하고 튼실하던 그녀, 이제는 재요 티끌이니, 죽는 모든 육신의 끝이라. 드높은 신분의 부인으로, 당당한 귀부인의 자태를 지니고, 위대한 돈키호테의 연정의 대상이요, 제 마을의 자랑이었더라.\n\n이것이 판독해 낼 수 있었던 시구의 전부였다. 나머지는 글씨가 좀먹은 까닭에, 그 뜻을 어림으로 헤아려 보라고 아카데미 회원 가운데 한 사람에게 넘겨졌다. 우리가 전해 들은 바로는, 그가 숱한 불면의 밤과 많은 노고를 치른 끝에 그 일을 해냈으며, 돈키호테의 세 번째 출정을 기대하며 그것을 펴낼 작정이라고 한다.\n\"어쩌면 다른 이가 더 나은 가락으로 노래하리라.\" * 포르세 알트로 칸테라 콘 밀리오르 플레트로(Forse altro cantera con miglior plectro): 이탈리아어로 \"어쩌면 다른 이가 더 나은 채(撥)로 노래하리라\"라는 뜻으로, 아리오스토의 「광란의 오를란도」에 나오는 구절.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-5921"]=[[["It was with considerable reluctance that I abandoned in favour of the present undertaking what had long been a favourite project: that of a new edition of Shelton’s “Don Quixote,”","오랫동안 내가 가장 아끼던 계획을 포기하고 지금의 작업을 택하게 된 것은 적잖은 망설임 끝의 일이었다. 그 계획이란 이제는 다소 구하기 어려운 책이 되어 버린 셸턴의 「돈키호테」를 새로이 펴내는 일이었다."],["which has now become a somewhat scarce book. There are some--and I confess myself to be one--for whom Shelton’s racy old version, with all its defects, has a charm that no modern translation, however skilful or correct, could possess.","셸턴의 그 생기 넘치는 옛 번역은 온갖 결점에도 불구하고, 아무리 능숙하거나 정확한 현대 번역이라 해도 결코 지니지 못할 매력을 품고 있다고 여기는 사람들이 있는데, 나 자신도 그중 하나임을 고백한다."],["Shelton had the inestimable advantage of belonging to the same generation as Cervantes; “Don Quixote”","셸턴은 세르반테스와 같은 세대에 속했다는, 값으로 따질 수 없는 이점을 누렸다."],["had to him a vitality that only a contemporary could feel; it cost him no dramatic effort to see things as Cervantes saw them; there is no anachronism in his language; he put the Spanish of Cervantes into the English of Shakespeare.","그에게 「돈키호테」는 동시대인만이 느낄 수 있는 생명력을 지녔으며, 세르반테스가 본 그대로 사물을 보는 데 극적인 노력을 들일 필요가 없었다. 그의 언어에는 시대착오가 없으니, 그는 세르반테스의 스페인어를 셰익스피어의 영어로 옮겨 놓았다."],["Shakespeare himself most likely knew the book; he may have carried it home with him in his saddle-bags to Stratford on one of his last journeys, and under the mulberry tree at New Place joined hands with a kindred genius in its pages.","셰익스피어 자신도 이 책을 알고 있었을 가능성이 매우 높다. 어쩌면 그는 마지막 여행 중 어느 길에서 안장주머니에 이 책을 넣어 스트랫퍼드의 집으로 가져갔을지도 모르고, 뉴플레이스의 뽕나무 아래에서 그 책장들 속의 한 핏줄과도 같은 천재와 손을 맞잡았을지도 모른다."]],[["But it was soon made plain to me that to hope for even a moderate popularity for Shelton was vain.","그러나 셸턴이 어느 정도의 인기조차 누리리라 기대하는 것이 헛된 일임은 곧 내게 분명해졌다."],["His fine old crusted English would, no doubt, be relished by a minority, but it would be only by a minority.","그의 멋들어진, 켜켜이 묵은 영어는 의심할 바 없이 소수의 사람들에게는 음미되겠지만, 어디까지나 소수에 그칠 것이다."],["His warmest admirers must admit that he is not a satisfactory representative of Cervantes.","그를 가장 열렬히 흠모하는 이들조차 그가 세르반테스를 만족스럽게 대변하지 못한다는 점은 인정해야 한다."],["His translation of the First Part was very hastily made and was never revised by him.","그의 제1부 번역은 몹시 서둘러 이루어졌고 그 자신이 한 번도 손보지 않았다."],["It has all the freshness and vigour, but also a full measure of the faults, of a hasty production.","거기에는 서둘러 만들어진 작품의 신선함과 활력이 모두 깃들어 있으나, 동시에 그러한 작품의 결점 또한 차고 넘친다."],["It is often very literal--barbarously literal frequently--but just as often very loose.","그것은 종종 매우 직역에 치우쳐 — 빈번히 야만스러우리만치 직역인 — 가 하면, 그에 못지않게 종종 매우 느슨하다."],["He had evidently a good colloquial knowledge of Spanish, but apparently not much more.","그는 분명 스페인어 구어에는 능통했으나, 그 이상은 별로 알지 못했던 듯하다."],["It never seems to occur to him that the same translation of a word will not suit in every case.","한 단어를 똑같이 번역한 것이 모든 경우에 들어맞지는 않으리라는 생각은 그에게 결코 떠오르지 않는 듯하다."]],[["It is often said that we have no satisfactory translation of “Don Quixote.”","우리에게는 만족할 만한 「돈키호테」 번역이 없다는 말을 흔히 듣는다."],["To those who are familiar with the original, it savours of truism or platitude to say so, for in truth there can be no thoroughly satisfactory translation of “Don Quixote”","원전에 익숙한 이들에게는 그런 말이 자명한 이치나 진부한 말처럼 들린다."],["into English or any other language.","사실 「돈키호테」를 영어로든 다른 어떤 언어로든 완벽하게 만족스럽게 옮기는 일은 있을 수 없기 때문이다."],["It is not that the Spanish idioms are so utterly unmanageable, or that the untranslatable words, numerous enough no doubt, are so superabundant, but rather that the sententious terseness to which the humour of the book owes its flavour is peculiar to Spanish, and can at best be only distantly imitated in any other tongue.","그것은 스페인어 관용구가 도무지 다룰 수 없을 만큼 까다로워서도 아니고, 번역 불가능한 단어들이 — 분명 적지 않게 많기는 하지만 — 그토록 넘쳐 나서도 아니다. 오히려 이 책의 해학에 그 풍미를 입혀 주는 그 격언 같은 간결함이 스페인어 특유의 것이어서, 다른 어떤 언어로도 기껏해야 멀찍이서 흉내 낼 수 있을 뿐이기 때문이다."]],[["The history of our English translations of “Don Quixote”","「돈키호테」의 영역사(英譯史)는 시사하는 바가 많다."],["is instructive. Shelton’s, the first in any language, was made, apparently, about 1608, but not published till 1612.","어떤 언어로든 최초의 번역이었던 셸턴의 것은 1608년경에 이루어진 듯하나 1612년에야 출간되었다."],["This of course was only the First Part.","물론 이것은 제1부에 한한 것이었다."],["It has been asserted that the Second, published in 1620, is not the work of Shelton, but there is nothing to support the assertion save the fact that it has less spirit, less of what we generally understand by “go,”","1620년에 나온 제2부는 셸턴의 작업이 아니라는 주장이 제기되어 왔으나, 그 주장을 뒷받침할 만한 근거라고는 제1부보다 기백이 덜하고, 우리가 흔히 \"활기\"라 이해하는 것이 적다는 사실뿐이다."],["about it than the first, which would be only natural if the first were the work of a young man writing currente calamo, and the second that of a middle-aged man writing for a bookseller.","그런데 제1부가 거침없이 붓을 놀려 쓴 젊은이의 작업이고 제2부가 서적상을 위해 쓴 중년 남자의 작업이라면, 그것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이리라."],["On the other hand, it is closer and more literal, the style is the same, the very same translations, or mistranslations, occur in it, and it is extremely unlikely that a new translator would, by suppressing his name, have allowed Shelton to carry off the credit.","한편 제2부는 더 충실하고 더 직역에 가까우며 문체가 동일하고, 똑같은 번역, 혹은 똑같은 오역이 그 안에 나타난다. 게다가 새 번역자가 자기 이름을 감추면서까지 셸턴에게 공을 가로채도록 내버려 두었으리라고는 극히 보기 어렵다."]],[["In 1687 John Phillips, Milton’s nephew, produced a “Don Quixote” “made English,”","1687년 밀턴의 조카인 존 필립스는 자기 말로 \"우리 현대 언어의 정취에 맞추어 영어로 옮긴\""],["he says, “according to the humour of our modern language.”","「돈키호테」를 내놓았다."],["His “Quixote” is not so much a translation as a travesty, and a travesty that for coarseness, vulgarity, and buffoonery is almost unexampled even in the literature of that day.","그의 「돈키호테」는 번역이라기보다 희화화에 가까우며, 그 조잡함과 저속함과 익살로 말하자면 그 시대의 문학에서조차 거의 유례를 찾기 어려운 희화화이다."]],[["Ned Ward’s “Life and Notable Adventures of Don Quixote, merrily translated into Hudibrastic Verse” (1700), can scarcely be reckoned a translation, but it serves to show the light in which “Don Quixote” was regarded at the time.","네드 워드의 「돈키호테의 생애와 주목할 만한 모험들, 휴디브래스풍 운문으로 유쾌하게 옮김」(1700)은 번역이라 보기 어려우나, 당시 「돈키호테」가 어떤 시각으로 여겨졌는지를 보여 주는 데에는 도움이 된다."]],[["A further illustration may be found in the version published in 1712 by Peter Motteux, who had then recently combined tea-dealing with literature.","또 한 가지 예는 1712년 피터 모토가 펴낸 판본에서 찾을 수 있는데, 그는 당시 막 차(茶) 장사와 문필업을 겸하고 있었다."],["It is described as “translated from the original by several hands,” but if so all Spanish flavour has entirely evaporated under the manipulation of the several hands.","이 판본은 \"여러 사람의 손으로 원전에서 번역됨\"이라 일컬어지나, 만일 그렇다면 그 여러 손의 손길을 거치는 동안 스페인적 풍미는 죄다 증발해 버린 셈이다."],["The flavour that it has, on the other hand, is distinctly Franco-cockney.","반면 그것이 지닌 풍미는 분명 프랑스풍 런던내기의 것이다."],["Anyone who compares it carefully with the original will have little doubt that it is a concoction from Shelton and the French of Filleau de Saint Martin, eked out by borrowings from Phillips, whose mode of treatment it adopts.","누구든 이것을 원전과 꼼꼼히 견주어 보면, 그것이 셸턴과 피요 드 생마르탱의 프랑스어 번역을 뒤섞은 혼합물이며, 거기에 필립스에게서 빌려 온 것들로 채워 넣고 그의 처리 방식을 따왔음을 거의 의심하지 않으리라."],["It is, to be sure, more decent and decorous, but it treats “Don Quixote” in the same fashion as a comic book that cannot be made too comic.","분명 그것은 더 점잖고 더 품위 있기는 하나, 「돈키호테」를 아무리 우스꽝스럽게 만들어도 지나치지 않은 익살책처럼 다루고 있다."]],[["To attempt to improve the humour of “Don Quixote” by an infusion of cockney flippancy and facetiousness, as Motteux’s operators did, is not merely an impertinence like larding a sirloin of prize beef, but an absolute falsification of the spirit of the book, and it is a proof of the uncritical way in which “Don Quixote”","모토의 일꾼들이 한 것처럼 런던내기 특유의 경박함과 익살을 주입해서 「돈키호테」의 해학을 더 낫게 만들려는 시도는, 상등급 소고기 등심에 비계를 끼워 넣는 것 같은 한낱 주제넘은 짓일 뿐 아니라, 이 책의 정신을 완전히 왜곡하는 일이다."],["is generally read that this worse than worthless translation--worthless as failing to represent, worse than worthless as misrepresenting--should have been favoured as it has been.","그리고 대변하지 못한다는 점에서 무가치하고 잘못 대변한다는 점에서 무가치한 것보다 더 못한 이 번역이, 그래 온 만큼이나 호의를 받아 왔다는 사실은 「돈키호테」가 대체로 얼마나 무비판적으로 읽히는가를 보여 주는 증거이다."]],[["It had the effect, however, of bringing out a translation undertaken and executed in a very different spirit, that of Charles Jervas, the portrait painter, and friend of Pope, Swift, Arbuthnot, and Gay.","그러나 그것은 전혀 다른 정신으로 착수되고 수행된 번역, 즉 초상화가이자 포프, 스위프트, 아버스넛, 게이의 벗이었던 찰스 저바스의 번역을 낳는 효과를 가져왔다."],["Jervas has been allowed little credit for his work, indeed it may be said none, for it is known to the world in general as Jarvis’s.","저바스는 자기 작업에 대해 거의 인정받지 못했고, 사실상 전혀 인정받지 못했다 해도 좋다. 세상에는 대체로 자비스의 번역으로 알려져 있기 때문이다."],["It was not published until after his death, and the printers gave the name according to the current pronunciation of the day.","그것은 그가 죽은 뒤에야 출간되었고, 인쇄업자들이 당시의 통상적인 발음에 따라 그 이름을 붙였던 것이다."],["It has been the most freely used and the most freely abused of all the translations.","그것은 모든 번역 가운데 가장 거리낌 없이 이용되었고 또 가장 거리낌 없이 욕먹은 번역이었다."],["It has seen far more editions than any other, it is admitted on all hands to be by far the most faithful, and yet nobody seems to have a good word to say for it or for its author.","다른 어떤 것보다 훨씬 많은 판을 거듭했고, 단연 가장 충실하다는 점은 누구나 인정하는 바이나, 그럼에도 그것이나 그 저자를 위해 좋은 말 한마디 해 주는 이는 아무도 없는 듯하다."],["Jervas no doubt prejudiced readers against himself in his preface, where among many true words about Shelton, Stevens, and Motteux, he rashly and unjustly charges Shelton with having translated not from the Spanish, but from the Italian version of Franciosini, which did not appear until ten years after Shelton’s first volume.","저바스는 분명 자신의 서문에서 독자들이 자신에게 편견을 품게 만들었다. 그는 그 서문에서 셸턴, 스티븐스, 모토에 관해 옳은 말을 많이 하는 가운데, 셸턴이 스페인어가 아니라 프란초시니의 이탈리아어 번역에서 옮겼다고 경솔하고도 부당하게 비난했던 것이다."],["A suspicion of incompetence, too, seems to have attached to him because he was by profession a painter and a mediocre one (though he has given us the best portrait we have of Swift), and this may have been strengthened by Pope’s remark that he “translated ‘Don Quixote’ without understanding Spanish.”","그런데 그 이탈리아어 번역은 셸턴의 첫 권이 나온 지 십 년이 지나서야 등장했다. 또한 그가 직업이 화가였고 그것도 평범한 화가였다는 이유로(비록 그는 우리에게 스위프트의 가장 훌륭한 초상화를 남겨 주었지만) 무능하다는 의심이 그에게 따라붙은 듯하며, 이는 그가 \"스페인어를 이해하지도 못하면서 「돈키호테」를 번역했다\"는 포프의 언급으로 더 굳어졌을 법하다."],["He has been also charged with borrowing from Shelton, whom he disparaged.","그는 또한 자신이 깎아내렸던 셸턴에게서 빌려 왔다는 비난도 받았다."],["It is true that in a few difficult or obscure passages he has followed Shelton, and gone astray with him; but for one case of this sort, there are fifty where he is right and Shelton wrong.","그가 어렵거나 모호한 몇몇 구절에서 셸턴을 따랐다가 그와 함께 길을 잘못 든 것은 사실이다. 그러나 이런 경우가 하나라면, 그가 옳고 셸턴이 틀린 경우는 쉰 개나 된다."],["As for Pope’s dictum, anyone who examines Jervas’s version carefully, side by side with the original, will see that he was a sound Spanish scholar, incomparably a better one than Shelton, except perhaps in mere colloquial Spanish.","포프의 단언으로 말하자면, 저바스의 번역을 원전과 나란히 놓고 꼼꼼히 살펴보는 사람이라면 누구든, 그가 — 어쩌면 순전한 스페인어 구어에서를 제외하고는 — 셸턴보다 비할 바 없이 더 나은, 견실한 스페인어 학자였음을 알게 될 것이다."],["He was, in fact, an honest, faithful, and painstaking translator, and he has left a version which, whatever its shortcomings may be, is singularly free from errors and mistranslations.","사실 그는 정직하고 충실하며 공을 들이는 번역자였고, 그 결점이 무엇이든 간에 오류와 오역에서 유난히 자유로운 번역을 남겼다."]],[["The charge against it is that it is stiff, dry--“wooden” in a word,-and no one can deny that there is a foundation for it.","이 번역에 대한 비난은 그것이 뻣뻣하고 메마르다는 것 — 한마디로 \"딱딱하다\"는 것 — 인데, 거기에 근거가 있음을 부인할 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없다."],["But it may be pleaded for Jervas that a good deal of this rigidity is due to his abhorrence of the light, flippant, jocose style of his predecessors.","그러나 저바스를 위해 변론하자면, 이 경직됨의 상당 부분은 그가 선배들의 가볍고 경박하며 익살스러운 문체를 몹시 혐오한 데서 비롯된 것이다."],["He was one of the few, very few, translators that have shown any apprehension of the unsmiling gravity which is the essence of Quixotic humour; it seemed to him a crime to bring Cervantes forward smirking and grinning at his own good things, and to this may be attributed in a great measure the ascetic abstinence from everything savouring of liveliness which is the characteristic of his translation.","그는 돈키호테식 해학의 본질인 그 미소 없는 진중함을 조금이나마 헤아렸던 극소수의, 정말 극소수의 번역자 가운데 하나였다. 세르반테스를 자기가 한 멋진 말에 히죽거리며 이를 드러내고 웃는 모습으로 내세우는 것이 그에게는 죄처럼 여겨졌으니, 그의 번역의 특징인, 활기를 띤 모든 것을 금욕적으로 절제하는 태도는 상당 부분 이에서 비롯된 것이라 하겠다."],["In most modern editions, it should be observed, his style has been smoothed and smartened, but without any reference to the original Spanish, so that if he has been made to read more agreeably he has also been robbed of his chief merit of fidelity.","다만 유의할 점은, 대부분의 현대 판본에서 그의 문체가 매끄럽게 다듬어지고 멋스럽게 손질되었으나 원래의 스페인어와는 아무런 대조 없이 그리되었다는 사실이다. 그리하여 그는 더 듣기 좋게 읽히도록 만들어진 대신, 그의 으뜸가는 미덕인 충실성을 강탈당하고 말았다."]],[["Smollett’s version, published in 1755, may be almost counted as one of these.","1755년에 출간된 스몰릿의 번역도 이런 것들 가운데 하나로 거의 꼽을 만하다."],["At any rate it is plain that in its construction Jervas’s translation was very freely drawn upon, and very little or probably no heed given to the original Spanish.","어쨌든 그것을 구성하는 데 저바스의 번역이 매우 거리낌 없이 가져다 쓰였고, 원래의 스페인어에는 거의, 혹은 아마도 전혀 주의를 기울이지 않았음은 분명하다."]],[["The later translations may be dismissed in a few words.","이후의 번역들은 몇 마디로 처리해도 좋다."],["George Kelly’s, which appeared in 1769, “printed for the Translator,”","1769년에 \"번역자를 위해 인쇄됨\"이라 하여 나온 조지 켈리의 번역은 뻔뻔한 사기였으니, 모토의 번역에서 여기저기 몇몇 단어를 교묘히 자리바꿈한 것에 지나지 않았다."],["was an impudent imposture, being nothing more than Motteux’s version with a few of the words, here and there, artfully transposed; Charles Wilmot’s (1774) was only an abridgment like Florian’s, but not so skilfully executed; and the version published by Miss Smirke in 1818, to accompany her brother’s plates, was merely a patchwork production made out of former translations.","찰스 윌멋의 번역(1774)은 플로리앙의 것처럼 축약본에 불과했으나 솜씨는 그만 못했다. 그리고 1818년에 스머크 양이 자기 오라비의 도판에 곁들이려고 펴낸 판본은 이전 번역들을 짜깁기해 만든 것에 지나지 않았다."],["On the latest, Mr.","가장 최근의 것인 A."],["A. J.","J."],["Duffield’s, it would be in every sense of the word impertinent in me to offer an opinion here.","더필드 씨의 번역에 대해서는, 여기서 내가 의견을 내놓는 것이 모든 의미에서 주제넘은 일이 되리라."],["I had not even seen it when the present undertaking was proposed to me, and since then I may say vidi tantum, having for obvious reasons resisted the temptation which Mr. Duffield’s reputation and comely volumes hold out to every lover of Cervantes.","이 작업을 내게 제안했을 때 나는 그것을 본 적조차 없었고, 그 뒤로는 그저 vidi tantum, 곧 보기만 했다고 할 수 있겠으니, 더필드 씨의 명성과 보기 좋은 책들이 세르반테스를 사랑하는 모든 이에게 내미는 유혹을 뻔한 이유로 뿌리쳐 왔기 때문이다."]],[["From the foregoing history of our translations of “Don Quixote,”","* vidi tantum: 라틴어로 \"그저 보기만 했다\"는 뜻."],["it will be seen that there are a good many people who, provided they get the mere narrative with its full complement of facts, incidents, and adventures served up to them in a form that amuses them, care very little whether that form is the one in which Cervantes originally shaped his ideas.","「돈키호테」 번역의 앞선 역사를 보면, 사실과 사건과 모험을 온전히 갖춘 단순한 줄거리가 자기들을 즐겁게 해 주는 형식으로 차려져 나오기만 하면, 그 형식이 세르반테스가 본디 자신의 생각을 빚어낸 그 형식인지 아닌지에는 거의 신경 쓰지 않는 사람들이 꽤 많음을 알 수 있다."],["On the other hand, it is clear that there are many who desire to have not merely the story he tells, but the story as he tells it, so far at least as differences of idiom and circumstances permit, and who will give a preference to the conscientious translator, even though he may have acquitted himself somewhat awkwardly.","한편으로는, 그가 들려주는 이야기뿐 아니라 그가 들려주는 그대로의 이야기를 — 적어도 관용구와 정황의 차이가 허락하는 한에서는 — 갖고자 하며, 다소 어설프게 해냈을지언정 양심적인 번역자를 더 선호할 사람들도 많음이 분명하다."]],[["But after all there is no real antagonism between the two classes; there is no reason why what pleases the one should not please the other, or why a translator who makes it his aim to treat “Don Quixote”","그러나 결국 이 두 부류 사이에 진정한 대립은 없다. 한쪽을 기쁘게 하는 것이 다른 쪽을 기쁘게 하지 못할 까닭이 없고, 「돈키호테」를 위대한 고전에 합당한 존중으로 다루기를 목표로 삼는 번역자가 그것을 유명한 옛 우스갯거리 책으로 다루는 자만큼이나 부주의한 독자에게도 받아들여지지 못할 까닭도 없다."],["with the respect due to a great classic, should not be as acceptable even to the careless reader as the one who treats it as a famous old jest-book.","그것은 대중에게 캐비아 같은 고급 취향의 문제가 아니며, 설령 그렇다 해도 그 잘못은 그렇게 만드는 자에게 있다."],["It is not a question of caviare to the general, or, if it is, the fault rests with him who makes so. The method by which Cervantes won the ear of the Spanish people ought, mutatis mutandis, to be equally effective with the great majority of English readers.","세르반테스가 스페인 사람들의 귀를 사로잡은 방법은, 사정에 맞게 고친다면(mutatis mutandis), 영어권 독자 대다수에게도 똑같이 효력을 발휘할 것이다."],["At any rate, even if there are readers to whom it is a matter of indifference, fidelity to the method is as much a part of the translator’s duty as fidelity to the matter.","어쨌든 그것을 아무래도 상관없는 일로 여기는 독자가 있다 하더라도, 방법에 충실한 것은 내용에 충실한 것 못지않게 번역자의 의무의 일부이다."],["If he can please all parties, so much the better; but his first duty is to those who look to him for as faithful a representation of his author as it is in his power to give them, faithful to the letter so long as fidelity is practicable, faithful to the spirit so far as he can make it.","모든 부류를 다 기쁘게 할 수 있다면 그보다 좋은 일은 없겠지만, 그의 첫 번째 의무는 자기 능력이 닿는 한 가장 충실하게 저자를 대변해 주기를 그에게 기대하는 사람들에 대한 것이다. 충실함이 실행 가능한 한에서는 자구에 충실하고, 그가 할 수 있는 한에서는 정신에 충실하게 말이다."]],[["My purpose here is not to dogmatise on the rules of translation, but to indicate those I have followed, or at least tried to the best of my ability to follow, in the present instance.","* mutatis mutandis: 라틴어로 \"바꾸어야 할 것을 바꾸어\"라는 뜻. 여기서 내 목적은 번역의 규칙을 두고 독단을 부리려는 것이 아니라, 이번 경우에 내가 따랐던, 적어도 내 능력껏 따르려 애썼던 규칙들을 일러두려는 것이다."],["One which, it seems to me, cannot be too rigidly followed in translating “Don Quixote,” is to avoid everything that savours of affectation.","「돈키호테」를 번역할 때 아무리 엄격히 따라도 지나치지 않으리라 여겨지는 한 가지 규칙은, 가식의 기미가 있는 모든 것을 피하는 일이다."],["The book itself is, indeed, in one sense a protest against it, and no man abhorred it more than Cervantes.","사실 이 책 자체가 어떤 의미에서는 가식에 대한 항의이며, 세르반테스보다 그것을 더 혐오한 사람은 없었다."],["For this reason, I think, any temptation to use antiquated or obsolete language should be resisted.","그러므로 옛스럽거나 낡아 빠진 언어를 쓰고 싶은 유혹은 어떤 것이든 뿌리쳐야 한다고 나는 생각한다."],["It is after all an affectation, and one for which there is no warrant or excuse.","그것은 결국 하나의 가식이며, 그에 대해서는 아무런 정당성도 변명도 없다."],["Spanish has probably undergone less change since the seventeenth century than any language in Europe, and by far the greater and certainly the best part of “Don Quixote” differs but little in language from the colloquial Spanish of the present day.","스페인어는 아마도 유럽의 어떤 언어보다도 17세기 이래 변화가 적었으며, 「돈키호테」의 더 큰 부분, 그리고 확실히 가장 좋은 부분은 오늘날의 스페인어 구어와 언어상 거의 차이가 없다."],["Except in the tales and Don Quixote’s speeches, the translator who uses the simplest and plainest everyday language will almost always be the one who approaches nearest to the original.","이야기들과 돈키호테의 연설들을 제외하면, 가장 단순하고 평이한 일상의 언어를 쓰는 번역자가 거의 언제나 원전에 가장 가까이 다가가는 사람이 될 것이다."]],[["Seeing that the story of “Don Quixote” and all its characters and incidents have now been for more than two centuries and a half familiar as household words in English mouths, it seems to me that the old familiar names and phrases should not be changed without good reason.","「돈키호테」의 이야기와 그 모든 인물과 사건이 이제 이백오십 년 넘게 영국인들의 입에 일상의 말처럼 친숙해져 왔음을 생각하면, 옛부터 친숙한 이름과 표현은 충분한 이유 없이 바꾸지 말아야 한다고 나는 생각한다."],["Of course a translator who holds that “Don Quixote” should receive the treatment a great classic deserves, will feel himself bound by the injunction laid upon the Morisco in Chap.","물론 「돈키호테」가 위대한 고전에 합당한 대접을 받아야 한다고 여기는 번역자라면, 제9장에서 무어인에게 내려진, 어떤 것도 빼거나 더하지 말라는 그 명령에 자신이 매여 있음을 느끼리라."],["IX not to omit or add anything.","II: 세르반테스와 돈키호테에 관하여"]],[["Four generations had laughed over “Don Quixote” before it occurred to anyone to ask, who and what manner of man was this Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra whose name is on the title-page; and it was too late for a satisfactory answer to the question when it was proposed to add a life of the author to the London edition published at Lord Carteret’s instance in 1738.","네 세대가 「돈키호테」를 두고 웃은 뒤에야 비로소 누군가가, 표지에 그 이름이 적힌 이 미겔 데 세르반테스 사아베드라가 대체 누구이며 어떤 사람이었던가를 물을 생각을 하게 되었다."],["All traces of the personality of Cervantes had by that time disappeared.","그리고 1738년 카터릿 경의 주선으로 런던에서 펴낸 판본에 저자의 생애를 덧붙이자는 제안이 나왔을 때는, 그 물음에 만족스러운 답을 내놓기에 이미 너무 늦은 뒤였다."],["Any floating traditions that may once have existed, transmitted from men who had known him, had long since died out, and of other record there was none; for the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries were incurious as to “the men of the time,”","그때쯤에는 세르반테스의 인간됨에 관한 자취가 모조리 사라지고 없었다. 한때 존재했을지도 모를, 그를 알았던 사람들에게서 전해 내려온 떠도는 전승들은 이미 오래전에 스러졌고, 다른 기록이라고는 전혀 없었다."],["a reproach against which the nineteenth has, at any rate, secured itself, if it has produced no Shakespeare or Cervantes.","16세기와 17세기는 \"당대의 사람들\"에 대해 무관심했으니, 19세기는 비록 셰익스피어나 세르반테스를 낳지는 못했어도 적어도 그러한 비난에서만큼은 스스로를 지켜 냈다."],["All that Mayans y Siscar, to whom the task was entrusted, or any of those who followed him, Rios, Pellicer, or Navarrete, could do was to eke out the few allusions Cervantes makes to himself in his various prefaces with such pieces of documentary evidence bearing upon his life as they could find.","이 일을 맡은 마얀스 이 시스카르나, 그의 뒤를 이은 리오스, 펠리세르, 나바레테 가운데 누구도 할 수 있었던 일이라고는, 세르반테스가 여러 서문에서 자신에 관해 언급한 몇 안 되는 대목을, 그들이 찾아낼 수 있었던 그의 생애에 관한 약간의 문헌적 증거들로 메워 보는 것뿐이었다."]],[["This, however, has been done by the last-named biographer to such good purpose that he has superseded all predecessors.","그러나 마지막에 언급한 그 전기 작가는 이 일을 매우 훌륭히 해내어 앞선 모든 이를 넘어섰다."],["Thoroughness is the chief characteristic of Navarrete’s work.","철저함이야말로 나바레테 작업의 으뜸가는 특징이다."],["Besides sifting, testing, and methodising with rare patience and judgment what had been previously brought to light, he left, as the saying is, no stone unturned under which anything to illustrate his subject might possibly be found.","그는 이전에 밝혀진 것들을 보기 드문 인내와 판단력으로 가려내고 검증하고 체계화했을 뿐 아니라, 흔히들 말하듯, 자기 주제를 밝혀 줄 만한 것이 행여 숨어 있을 법한 돌이라면 하나도 뒤집어 보지 않은 것이 없었다. 나바레테는 근면과 통찰이 할 수 있는 모든 일을 해냈으니, 우리가 원하는 것을 그가 주지 못했다면 그것은 그의 잘못이 아니다."],["Navarrete has done all that industry and acumen could do, and it is no fault of his if he has not given us what we want.","핼럼이 셰익스피어에 관해 한 말은 그와 거의 평행을 이루는 세르반테스의 경우에도 적용될 수 있다."],["What Hallam says of Shakespeare may be applied to the almost parallel case of Cervantes: “It is not the register of his baptism, or the draft of his will, or the orthography of his name that we seek; no letter of his writing, no record of his conversation, no character of him drawn ...","\"우리가 찾는 것은 그의 세례 기록이나 유언장 초안이나 그 이름의 철자법이 아니다. 그가 쓴 편지 한 통도, 그의 대화에 관한 기록도, 동시대인이 그려 낸 그의 성품에 관한 글도 ……"],["by a contemporary has been produced.”","나온 적이 없다.\""]],[["It is only natural, therefore, that the biographers of Cervantes, forced to make brick without straw, should have recourse largely to conjecture, and that conjecture should in some instances come by degrees to take the place of established fact.","그러니 짚 없이 벽돌을 만들어야 하는 처지에 몰린 세르반테스의 전기 작가들이 추측에 크게 기대게 되고, 그 추측이 어떤 경우에는 차츰차츰 확정된 사실의 자리를 차지하게 된 것도 지극히 당연한 일이다."],["All that I propose to do here is to separate what is matter of fact from what is matter of conjecture, and leave it to the reader’s judgment to decide whether the data justify the inference or not.","여기서 내가 하려는 일이라고는 사실에 속하는 것을 추측에 속하는 것에서 갈라내고, 그 자료들이 추론을 정당화하는지 아닌지를 가리는 일은 독자의 판단에 맡기는 것뿐이다."]],[["The men whose names by common consent stand in the front rank of Spanish literature, Cervantes, Lope de Vega, Quevedo, Calderon, Garcilaso de la Vega, the Mendozas, Gongora, were all men of ancient families, and, curiously, all, except the last, of families that traced their origin to the same mountain district in the North of Spain.","스페인 문학의 첫 줄에 선다고 모두가 입을 모으는 이들, 곧 세르반테스, 로페 데 베가, 케베도, 칼데론, 가르실라소 데 라 베가, 멘도사 가문 사람들, 공고라는 모두 유서 깊은 가문 출신이었으며, 묘하게도 마지막 한 사람을 제외하면 모두가 스페인 북부의 같은 산악 지방에 그 기원을 둔 가문 출신이었다."],["The family of Cervantes is commonly said to have been of Galician origin, and unquestionably it was in possession of lands in Galicia at a very early date; but I think the balance of the evidence tends to show that the “solar,”","세르반테스 가문은 흔히 갈리시아 태생이라고들 하며, 의심할 바 없이 그 가문은 아주 이른 시기부터 갈리시아에 토지를 소유하고 있었다."],["the original site of the family, was at Cervatos in the north-west corner of Old Castile, close to the junction of Castile, Leon, and the Asturias.","그러나 내가 보기에 증거의 무게는, 그 가문의 본디 터전인 \"솔라르\"가 옛 카스티야의 북서쪽 모퉁이, 카스티야와 레온과 아스투리아스가 만나는 지점 가까이의 세르바토스에 있었음을 보여 주는 쪽으로 기운다."],["As it happens, there is a complete history of the Cervantes family from the tenth century down to the seventeenth extant under the title of “Illustrious Ancestry, Glorious Deeds, and Noble Posterity of the Famous Nuno Alfonso, Alcaide of Toledo,”","마침 세르반테스 가문의 완전한 역사가 10세기부터 17세기까지 전해 내려오는데, 「톨레도의 알카이데였던 저 유명한 누뇨 알폰소의 명문 가계와 영광스러운 행적과 고귀한 후손」이라는 제목으로, 부지런한 계보학자 로드리고 멘데스 실바가 1648년에 쓴 것이다."],["written in 1648 by the industrious genealogist Rodrigo Mendez Silva, who availed himself of a manuscript genealogy by Juan de Mena, the poet laureate and historiographer of John II.","그는 후안 2세의 계관시인이자 사관(史官)이었던 후안 데 메나의 필사본 계보를 이용했다."]],[["The origin of the name Cervantes is curious.","세르반테스라는 이름의 유래는 흥미롭다."],["Nuno Alfonso was almost as distinguished in the struggle against the Moors in the reign of Alfonso VII as the Cid had been half a century before in that of Alfonso VI, and was rewarded by divers grants of land in the neighbourhood of Toledo.","누뇨 알폰소는 알폰소 7세 치세에 무어인과의 투쟁에서, 반세기 전 알폰소 6세 치세에 엘 시드가 그러했던 것에 거의 못지않게 두드러진 인물이었고, 그 공으로 톨레도 인근의 여러 토지를 하사받았다."],["On one of his acquisitions, about two leagues from the city, he built himself a castle which he called Cervatos, because “he was lord of the solar of Cervatos in the Montana,”","그는 도시에서 두 레구아쯤 떨어진 자기 영지 한 곳에 성을 쌓고 그것을 세르바토스라 불렀는데, 그가 \"몬타냐의 세르바토스 솔라르의 영주\"였기 때문이다."],["as the mountain region extending from the Basque Provinces to Leon was always called.","바스크 지방에서 레온까지 뻗은 그 산악 지방은 늘 몬타냐라 불렸다."],["At his death in battle in 1143, the castle passed by his will to his son Alfonso Munio, who, as territorial or local surnames were then coming into vogue in place of the simple patronymic, took the additional name of Cervatos.","1143년 그가 전투에서 죽자 그 성은 유언에 따라 아들 알폰소 무니오에게 넘어갔는데, 그는 당시 단순한 부칭(父稱) 대신 영지나 지역에서 따온 성(姓)이 유행하기 시작하던 터라 세르바토스라는 이름을 덧붙여 가졌다."],["His eldest son Pedro succeeded him in the possession of the castle, and followed his example in adopting the name, an assumption at which the younger son, Gonzalo, seems to have taken umbrage.","그의 맏아들 페드로가 성의 소유를 물려받고 그 이름을 따른 아비의 본을 좇았는데, 둘째 아들 곤살로는 이러한 차지에 못마땅해했던 듯하다."]],[["Everyone who has paid even a flying visit to Toledo will remember the ruined castle that crowns the hill above the spot where the bridge of Alcantara spans the gorge of the Tagus, and with its broken outline and crumbling walls makes such an admirable pendant to the square solid Alcazar towering over the city roofs on the opposite side.","톨레도를 잠깐이라도 들른 적이 있는 사람이라면 누구나, 알칸타라 다리가 타호강의 협곡 위로 걸쳐진 곳, 그 위 언덕을 이고 선 폐허가 된 성을 기억할 것이다. 그 성은 부서진 윤곽과 허물어져 가는 벽으로, 맞은편 도시 지붕들 위로 우뚝 솟은 네모지고 단단한 알카사르와 더없이 훌륭한 한 쌍을 이룬다."],["It was built, or as some say restored, by Alfonso VI shortly after his occupation of Toledo in 1085, and called by him San Servando after a Spanish martyr, a name subsequently modified into San Servan (in which form it appears in the “Poem of the Cid”), San Servantes, and San Cervantes: with regard to which last the “Handbook for Spain”","그것은 알폰소 6세가 1085년 톨레도를 점령한 직후에 지었다고도 하고 더러는 복원했다고도 하는데, 그는 그것을 스페인의 한 순교자를 기려 산 세르반도라 불렀다. 이 이름은 나중에 산 세르반(「엘 시드의 노래」에 이 형태로 나온다), 산 세르반테스, 산 세르반테스로 바뀌었다."],["warns its readers against the supposition that it has anything to do with the author of “Don Quixote.”","그 마지막 이름에 관하여 「스페인 안내서」는 그것이 「돈키호테」의 저자와 무슨 관련이 있으리라는 생각을 경계하라고 독자들에게 일러둔다."],["Ford, as all know who have taken him for a companion and counsellor on the roads of Spain, is seldom wrong in matters of literature or history.","스페인의 길에서 그를 길동무이자 조언자로 삼아 본 이라면 누구나 알듯, 포드는 문학이나 역사 문제에서 좀처럼 틀리는 법이 없다."],["In this instance, however, he is in error.","그러나 이 경우에는 그가 틀렸다."],["It has everything to do with the author of “Don Quixote,” for it is in fact these old walls that have given to Spain the name she is proudest of to-day.","그것은 「돈키호테」의 저자와 모든 관련이 있으니, 사실 오늘날 스페인이 가장 자랑스러워하는 그 이름을 스페인에 준 것이 바로 이 옛 성벽이기 때문이다."],["Gonzalo, above mentioned, it may be readily conceived, did not relish the appropriation by his brother of a name to which he himself had an equal right, for though nominally taken from the castle, it was in reality derived from the ancient territorial possession of the family, and as a set-off, and to distinguish himself (diferenciarse) from his brother, he took as a surname the name of the castle on the bank of the Tagus, in the building of which, according to a family tradition, his great-grandfather had a share.","앞서 말한 곤살로가, 명목상으로는 성에서 따온 것이라 해도 실은 그 가문의 오래된 영지에서 비롯된, 자신에게도 똑같은 권리가 있는 이름을 형이 차지한 것을 달가워하지 않았으리라는 것은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 그래서 이를 상쇄하고 형과 자신을 구별하기(diferenciarse) 위해, 그는 타호강 강변에 선 그 성의 이름을 성(姓)으로 삼았으니, 한 가문의 전승에 따르면 그 성을 짓는 데 그의 증조부가 한몫했다고 한다."]],[["Both brothers founded families.","두 형제 모두 가문을 일으켰다."],["The Cervantes branch had more tenacity; it sent offshoots in various directions, Andalusia, Estremadura, Galicia, and Portugal, and produced a goodly line of men distinguished in the service of Church and State.","세르반테스 쪽 가지가 더 끈질겨서, 안달루시아, 에스트레마두라, 갈리시아, 포르투갈 등 여러 방향으로 곁가지를 뻗었고, 교회와 나라를 섬기는 데 두드러진 인물들을 줄줄이 배출했다."],["Gonzalo himself, and apparently a son of his, followed Ferdinand III in the great campaign of 1236-48 that gave Cordova and Seville to Christian Spain and penned up the Moors in the kingdom of Granada, and his descendants intermarried with some of the noblest families of the Peninsula and numbered among them soldiers, magistrates, and Church dignitaries, including at least two cardinal-archbishops.","곤살로 자신과, 또 분명 그의 한 아들은 1236년부터 1248년까지 이어진, 코르도바와 세비야를 기독교 스페인에 안기고 무어인을 그라나다 왕국에 몰아넣은 저 대원정에서 페르난도 3세를 따랐으며, 그의 후손들은 이베리아반도의 가장 고귀한 몇몇 가문과 혼인을 맺어 그 가운데 군인과 행정관과 교회의 고위 성직자를 배출했으니, 적어도 두 명의 추기경 대주교가 그 안에 들어 있었다."]],[["Of the line that settled in Andalusia, Deigo de Cervantes, Commander of the Order of Santiago, married Juana Avellaneda, daughter of Juan Arias de Saavedra, and had several sons, of whom one was Gonzalo Gomez, Corregidor of Jerez and ancestor of the Mexican and Columbian branches of the family; and another, Juan, whose son Rodrigo married Dona Leonor de Cortinas, and by her had four children, Rodrigo, Andrea, Luisa, and Miguel, our author.","안달루시아에 정착한 가계 가운데, 산티아고 기사단의 기사단장이었던 디에고 데 세르반테스는 후안 아리아스 데 사아베드라의 딸 후아나 아베야네다와 혼인하여 여러 아들을 두었다. 그 가운데 하나가 헤레스의 행정관이자 이 가문의 멕시코 분가와 콜롬비아 분가의 조상이 된 곤살로 고메스였고, 또 하나가 후안이었는데, 그의 아들 로드리고가 도냐 레오노르 데 코르티나스와 혼인하여 그녀에게서 로드리고, 안드레아, 루이사, 그리고 우리의 저자 미겔이라는 네 자녀를 두었다."]],[["The pedigree of Cervantes is not without its bearing on “Don Quixote.”","세르반테스의 가계는 「돈키호테」와 무관하지 않다."],["A man who could look back upon an ancestry of genuine knights-errant extending from well-nigh the time of Pelayo to the siege of Granada was likely to have a strong feeling on the subject of the sham chivalry of the romances.","거의 펠라요의 시대부터 그라나다 공방전에 이르기까지 뻗어 있는, 진정한 편력 기사들의 혈통을 돌아볼 수 있는 사람이라면 기사도 소설의 가짜 기사도라는 주제에 대해 강한 감정을 품었을 법하다."],["It gives a point, too, to what he says in more than one place about families that have once been great and have tapered away until they have come to nothing, like a pyramid.","그것은 또한 한때 위대했다가 점점 가늘어져 마침내 피라미드처럼 끄트머리에 이르러서는 아무것도 아닌 것이 되어 버린 가문들에 관해 그가 여러 곳에서 한 말에 무게를 더해 준다."],["It was the case of his own.","그것이 바로 그 자신의 가문이 처한 형편이었던 것이다."]],[["He was born at Alcala de Henares and baptised in the church of Santa Maria Mayor on the 9th of October, 1547.","그는 알칼라 데 에나레스에서 태어나 1547년 10월 9일 산타 마리아 마요르 성당에서 세례를 받았다."],["Of his boyhood and youth we know nothing, unless it be from the glimpse he gives us in the preface to his “Comedies”","그의 소년 시절과 청년 시절에 대해서는, 「희극집」"],["of himself as a boy looking on with delight while Lope de Rueda and his company set up their rude plank stage in the plaza and acted the rustic farces which he himself afterwards took as the model of his interludes.","서문에서 그가 보여 주는 한 장면 — 로페 데 루에다와 그 일행이 광장에 투박한 널빤지 무대를 세우고 시골 소극을 공연하는 것을, 소년인 그가 즐거워하며 구경하던 장면, 훗날 그 자신이 자기 막간극의 본보기로 삼은 그 소극을 — 을 제외하면 우리는 아무것도 알지 못한다. 그러나 이 첫 장면은 의미심장하다."],["This first glimpse, however, is a significant one, for it shows the early development of that love of the drama which exercised such an influence on his life and seems to have grown stronger as he grew older, and of which this very preface, written only a few months before his death, is such a striking proof.","그의 생애에 그토록 큰 영향을 끼친, 그리고 그가 나이 들수록 더 강해진 듯한 그 연극에 대한 사랑이 일찍부터 자라났음을 보여 주기 때문이다. 그가 죽기 불과 몇 달 전에 쓴 바로 이 서문이 그 사랑의 두드러진 증거이다."],["He gives us to understand, too, that he was a great reader in his youth; but of this no assurance was needed, for the First Part of “Don Quixote”","그는 또한 젊은 시절 자신이 대단한 독서가였음을 우리에게 일러 주는데, 이에 대해서는 따로 확인할 필요도 없다. 「돈키호테」"],["alone proves a vast amount of miscellaneous reading, romances of chivalry, ballads, popular poetry, chronicles, for which he had no time or opportunity except in the first twenty years of his life; and his misquotations and mistakes in matters of detail are always, it may be noticed, those of a man recalling the reading of his boyhood.","제1부 하나만으로도 방대한 양의 잡다한 독서 — 기사도 소설, 발라드, 통속시, 연대기 — 가 입증되는데, 이는 그가 생애의 첫 스무 해를 빼고는 그럴 시간도 기회도 없었던 것들이다. 그리고 세부적인 문제에서 그가 저지른 잘못된 인용과 착오는, 눈여겨보건대, 언제나 소년 시절의 독서를 되새기는 사람의 그것이다."]],[["Other things besides the drama were in their infancy when Cervantes was a boy.","세르반테스가 소년이었을 무렵에는 연극 말고도 다른 여러 가지가 갓 태동하던 참이었다."],["The period of his boyhood was in every way a transition period for Spain.","그의 소년기는 모든 면에서 스페인에게 과도기였다."],["The old chivalrous Spain had passed away.","옛 기사도의 스페인은 스러져 갔다."],["The new Spain was the mightiest power the world had seen since the Roman Empire and it had not yet been called upon to pay the price of its greatness.","새로운 스페인은 로마 제국 이래 세상이 본 가장 막강한 세력이었으나, 아직 그 위대함의 대가를 치르라는 요구를 받지는 않았다."],["By the policy of Ferdinand and Ximenez the sovereign had been made absolute, and the Church and Inquisition adroitly adjusted to keep him so.","페르난도와 히메네스의 정책에 따라 군주는 절대 권력을 쥐게 되었고, 교회와 종교 재판소는 군주를 그렇게 유지하도록 교묘히 맞추어졌다."],["The nobles, who had always resisted absolutism as strenuously as they had fought the Moors, had been divested of all political power, a like fate had befallen the cities, the free constitutions of Castile and Aragon had been swept away, and the only function that remained to the Cortes was that of granting money at the King’s dictation.","무어인과 싸운 만큼이나 늘 끈질기게 절대주의에 맞서 온 귀족들은 모든 정치적 권력을 빼앗겼고, 도시들도 같은 운명을 맞았으며, 카스티야와 아라곤의 자유로운 헌정은 휩쓸려 사라졌고, 코르테스에 남은 유일한 기능이라고는 왕이 명하는 대로 돈을 내주는 것뿐이었다."]],[["The transition extended to literature.","그 과도기는 문학에까지 미쳤다."],["Men who, like Garcilaso de la Vega and Diego Hurtado de Mendoza, followed the Italian wars, had brought back from Italy the products of the post-Renaissance literature, which took root and flourished and even threatened to extinguish the native growths.","가르실라소 데 라 베가와 디에고 우르타도 데 멘도사처럼 이탈리아 전쟁에 종군했던 이들은 이탈리아에서 르네상스 이후 문학의 산물을 들여왔고, 그것이 뿌리내려 번성하더니 급기야 토착의 산물을 질식시킬 지경에 이르렀다."],["Damon and Thyrsis, Phyllis and Chloe had been fairly naturalised in Spain, together with all the devices of pastoral poetry for investing with an air of novelty the idea of a dispairing shepherd and inflexible shepherdess.","다몬과 티르시스, 필리스와 클로에가 스페인에 그럭저럭 토착화되었으며, 절망에 빠진 목동과 냉담한 목녀라는 발상에 새로움의 기색을 입히려는 목가시의 온갖 장치들도 함께 들어왔다."],["As a set-off against this, the old historical and traditional ballads, and the true pastorals, the songs and ballads of peasant life, were being collected assiduously and printed in the cancioneros that succeeded one another with increasing rapidity.","이에 대한 상쇄로, 옛 역사 발라드와 전승 발라드, 그리고 진정한 목가, 곧 농민의 삶을 노래한 노래와 발라드가 부지런히 수집되어, 점점 더 빠르게 잇따라 나온 가요집(cancionero)에 인쇄되었다."],["But the most notable consequence, perhaps, of the spread of printing was the flood of romances of chivalry that had continued to pour from the press ever since Garci Ordonez de Montalvo had resuscitated “Amadis of Gaul” at the beginning of the century.","그러나 인쇄술 보급의 가장 주목할 만한 결과는 아마도, 가르시 오르도녜스 데 몬탈보가 세기 초에 「아마디스 데 가울라」를 되살려 낸 이래 인쇄기에서 줄곧 쏟아져 나온 기사도 소설의 홍수였을 것이다."]],[["For a youth fond of reading, solid or light, there could have been no better spot in Spain than Alcala de Henares in the middle of the sixteenth century.","진지한 것이든 가벼운 것이든 독서를 좋아하는 젊은이에게, 16세기 중엽의 알칼라 데 에나레스보다 스페인에서 더 나은 곳은 없었을 것이다."],["It was then a busy, populous university town, something more than the enterprising rival of Salamanca, and altogether a very different place from the melancholy, silent, deserted Alcala the traveller sees now as he goes from Madrid to Saragossa.","그곳은 당시 분주하고 인구 많은 대학 도시였으며, 살라망카의 진취적인 경쟁자 그 이상이었고, 오늘날 마드리드에서 사라고사로 가는 여행자가 보게 되는 우울하고 적막하고 버려진 알칼라와는 전혀 다른 곳이었다."],["Theology and medicine may have been the strong points of the university, but the town itself seems to have inclined rather to the humanities and light literature, and as a producer of books Alcala was already beginning to compete with the older presses of Toledo, Burgos, Salamanca and Seville.","대학의 강점은 신학과 의학이었을지 모르나, 도시 자체는 오히려 인문학과 가벼운 문학 쪽으로 기울었던 듯하며, 책의 산지로서 알칼라는 이미 톨레도, 부르고스, 살라망카, 세비야의 더 오래된 인쇄소들과 겨루기 시작하고 있었다. 세르반테스가 우리에게 들려준, 그가 처음 연극 구경을 가던 그림에 한 쌍이 될 만한 장면이, 분명 그 무렵 알칼라의 거리에서 자주 보였을 법하다."],["A pendant to the picture Cervantes has given us of his first playgoings might, no doubt, have been often seen in the streets of Alcala at that time; a bright, eager, tawny-haired boy peering into a book-shop where the latest volumes lay open to tempt the public, wondering, it may be, what that little book with the woodcut of the blind beggar and his boy, that called itself “Vida de Lazarillo de Tormes, segunda impresion,” could be about; or with eyes brimming over with merriment gazing at one of those preposterous portraits of a knight-errant in outrageous panoply and plumes with which the publishers of chivalry romances loved to embellish the title-pages of their folios.","영민하고 열성적이며 황갈색 머리칼을 한 소년이, 대중을 꾈 양으로 최신 책들이 펼쳐져 놓인 서점 안을 들여다보면서, 어쩌면 눈먼 거지와 그 소년의 목판화가 실리고 \"라사리요 데 토르메스의 생애, 재판(再版)\"이라 제목 붙은 저 작은 책이 대체 무슨 내용일까 궁금해하거나, 아니면 기사도 소설 출판업자들이 자기네 2절판 표지를 꾸미기 좋아하던, 터무니없는 갑주와 깃털 장식을 두른 편력 기사의 저 우스꽝스러운 초상화 가운데 하나를 즐거움에 넘쳐흐르는 눈으로 바라보던 장면 말이다."],["If the boy was the father of the man, the sense of the incongruous that was strong at fifty was lively at ten, and some such reflections as these may have been the true genesis of “Don Quixote.”","소년이 어른의 아버지라면, 쉰 살에 강했던 부조화에 대한 감각이 열 살 때 이미 생생했을 것이며, 이와 같은 어떤 상념들이야말로 「돈키호테」의 진정한 발단이었을지도 모른다."]],[["For his more solid education, we are told, he went to Salamanca.","좀 더 본격적인 교육을 위해 그는 살라망카로 갔다고 전한다."],["But why Rodrigo de Cervantes, who was very poor, should have sent his son to a university a hundred and fifty miles away when he had one at his own door, would be a puzzle, if we had any reason for supposing that he did so.","그러나 매우 가난했던 로드리고 데 세르반테스가 제집 문 앞에 대학이 있는데도 어째서 아들을 백오십 마일이나 떨어진 대학으로 보냈을까 하는 것은, 그가 정말 그렇게 했다고 여길 만한 무슨 근거라도 있다면 하나의 수수께끼가 될 것이다."],["The only evidence is a vague statement by Professor Tomas Gonzalez, that he once saw an old entry of the matriculation of a Miguel de Cervantes.","유일한 증거라고는, 토마스 곤살레스 교수가 미겔 데 세르반테스라는 사람의 입학 등록 옛 기록을 한 번 본 적이 있다는 막연한 진술뿐이다. 이것은 그 뒤로 다시 본 사람이 없는 듯하다."],["This does not appear to have been ever seen again; but even if it had, and if the date corresponded, it would prove nothing, as there were at least two other Miguels born about the middle of the century; one of them, moreover, a Cervantes Saavedra, a cousin, no doubt, who was a source of great embarrassment to the biographers.","그러나 설령 다시 보였다 하더라도, 또 그 날짜가 들어맞았다 하더라도, 그것은 아무것도 입증하지 못할 것이니, 그 세기 중엽 무렵에 태어난 미겔이 적어도 둘은 더 있었기 때문이다. 게다가 그 가운데 하나는 세르반테스 사아베드라로, 분명 사촌이었을 그 사람은 전기 작가들에게 큰 골칫거리의 원천이었다."]],[["That he was a student neither at Salamanca nor at Alcala is best proved by his own works.","그가 살라망카에서도 알칼라에서도 학생이 아니었다는 사실은 그 자신의 작품들이 가장 잘 입증한다."],["No man drew more largely upon experience than he did, and he has nowhere left a single reminiscence of student life-for the “Tia Fingida,” if it be his, is not one--nothing, not even “a college joke,”","어떤 사람도 그만큼 경험에 크게 기댄 이가 없건만, 그는 학생 시절의 추억이라고는 어디에도 단 하나 남기지 않았으니 — 「티아 핑히다」가 그의 작품이라 하더라도 그것은 추억이 아니다 — 대부분의 사람이 가장 잘 기억하는 그 시절을 그가 기억했음을 보여 줄 만한 것은, \"대학 시절의 농담\""],["to show that he remembered days that most men remember best.","하나조차 없다."],["All that we know positively about his education is that Juan Lopez de Hoyos, a professor of humanities and belles-lettres of some eminence, calls him his “dear and beloved pupil.”","그의 교육에 관해 우리가 확실히 아는 전부는, 어느 정도 명망 있는 인문학과 순문학 교수였던 후안 로페스 데 오요스가 그를 자신의 \"사랑하고 아끼는 제자\"라 부른다는 것이다."],["This was in a little collection of verses by different hands on the death of Isabel de Valois, second queen of Philip II, published by the professor in 1569, to which Cervantes contributed four pieces, including an elegy, and an epitaph in the form of a sonnet.","이는 펠리페 2세의 둘째 왕비 이사벨 데 발로이스의 죽음에 부쳐 여러 사람의 손으로 지어진 시를 모은 작은 시집에 나오는 말로, 그 교수가 1569년에 펴낸 것인데, 세르반테스는 거기에 비가(悲歌) 한 편과 소네트 형식의 묘비명을 포함하여 네 편을 기고했다. 「리시다스」"],["It is only by a rare chance that a “Lycidas” finds its way into a volume of this sort, and Cervantes was no Milton.","같은 작품이 이런 종류의 시집에 끼어드는 일은 드문 우연이며, 세르반테스는 밀턴이 아니었다."],["His verses are no worse than such things usually are; so much, at least, may be said for them.","그의 시는 보통 이런 것들이 그러한 정도보다 더 나쁘지는 않다. 적어도 그 정도는 말해 줄 수 있겠다."]],[["By the time the book appeared he had left Spain, and, as fate ordered it, for twelve years, the most eventful ones of his life.","이 책이 나올 무렵 그는 스페인을 떠나 있었고, 운명이 그렇게 정한 대로 십이 년 동안, 그의 생애에서 가장 파란만장한 세월을 떠나 있게 되었다."],["Giulio, afterwards Cardinal, Acquaviva had been sent at the end of 1568 to Philip II by the Pope on a mission, partly of condolence, partly political, and on his return to Rome, which was somewhat brusquely expedited by the King, he took Cervantes with him as his camarero (chamberlain), the office he himself held in the Pope’s household.","훗날 추기경이 된 줄리오 아콰비바는 1568년 말에 교황의 명을 받아 펠리페 2세에게 일부는 조의를, 일부는 정치적 임무를 띠고 파견되었는데, 왕의 다소 무뚝뚝한 재촉으로 서둘러 로마로 돌아갈 때 세르반테스를 자신의 카마레로(시종)로 데려갔으니, 그것은 그 자신이 교황의 궁정에서 맡고 있던 직책이었다."],["The post would no doubt have led to advancement at the Papal Court had Cervantes retained it, but in the summer of 1570 he resigned it and enlisted as a private soldier in Captain Diego Urbina’s company, belonging to Don Miguel de Moncada’s regiment, but at that time forming a part of the command of Marc Antony Colonna.","만일 세르반테스가 그 자리를 지켰더라면 교황청에서 분명 출세로 이어졌겠지만, 1570년 여름 그는 그 자리를 내놓고 디에고 우르비나 대위의 중대에 일개 병사로 입대했다. 그 중대는 돈 미겔 데 몬카다의 연대 소속이었으나 당시에는 마르크안토니오 콜론나의 지휘 아래 있었다."],["What impelled him to this step we know not, whether it was distaste for the career before him, or purely military enthusiasm.","무엇이 그를 이런 일로 내몰았는지는 알 수 없으니, 그것이 앞에 놓인 출셋길에 대한 싫증이었는지, 아니면 순전한 군인다운 열정이었는지 우리는 모른다."],["It may well have been the latter, for it was a stirring time; the events, however, which led to the alliance between Spain, Venice, and the Pope, against the common enemy, the Porte, and to the victory of the combined fleets at Lepanto, belong rather to the history of Europe than to the life of Cervantes.","어쩌면 후자였을 법도 하니, 가슴 설레는 시절이었기 때문이다. 그러나 스페인과 베네치아와 교황이 공동의 적 오스만 제국에 맞서 동맹을 맺고 레판토에서 연합 함대가 승리를 거두기에 이른 그 사건들은 세르반테스의 생애보다는 차라리 유럽의 역사에 속한다."],["He was one of those that sailed from Messina, in September 1571, under the command of Don John of Austria; but on the morning of the 7th of October, when the Turkish fleet was sighted, he was lying below ill with fever.","그는 1571년 9월 돈 후안 데 아우스트리아의 지휘 아래 메시나에서 출항한 이들 가운데 하나였다. 그러나 10월 7일 아침 튀르크 함대가 시야에 들어왔을 때, 그는 갑판 아래에서 열병으로 앓아누워 있었다."],["At the news that the enemy was in sight he rose, and, in spite of the remonstrances of his comrades and superiors, insisted on taking his post, saying he preferred death in the service of God and the King to health.","적이 보인다는 소식에 그는 일어났고, 동료들과 상관들의 만류에도 자기 자리를 지키겠다고 고집하며, 건강보다 하느님과 왕을 섬기다 죽는 편을 택하겠노라 말했다."],["His galley, the Marquesa, was in the thick of the fight, and before it was over he had received three gunshot wounds, two in the breast and one in the left hand or arm.","그가 탄 갤리선 마르케사호는 전투의 한복판에 있었고, 전투가 끝나기 전에 그는 총상을 셋 입었으니, 둘은 가슴에, 하나는 왼손인지 왼팔인지에 맞았다."],["On the morning after the battle, according to Navarrete, he had an interview with the commander-in-chief, Don John, who was making a personal inspection of the wounded, one result of which was an addition of three crowns to his pay, and another, apparently, the friendship of his general.","나바레테에 따르면, 전투 다음 날 아침 그는 부상자들을 몸소 살피던 총사령관 돈 후안과 만났는데, 그 만남의 결과 하나는 그의 봉급에 세 크라운이 더해진 것이었고, 또 하나는 보아하니 그 장군의 우정이었다."]],[["How severely Cervantes was wounded may be inferred from the fact, that with youth, a vigorous frame, and as cheerful and buoyant a temperament as ever invalid had, he was seven months in hospital at Messina before he was discharged.","세르반테스가 얼마나 심하게 다쳤는지는, 젊음과 튼튼한 몸, 그리고 일찍이 어떤 환자도 지녀 본 적 없을 만큼 쾌활하고 활기찬 기질을 지녔으면서도, 퇴원하기까지 메시나의 병원에서 일곱 달을 보냈다는 사실에서 미루어 알 수 있다. 그는 왼손을 영영 못 쓰게 된 채로 나왔다."],["He came out with his left hand permanently disabled; he had lost the use of it, as Mercury told him in the “Viaje del Parnaso”","「파르나소 여행기」에서 메르쿠리우스가 그에게 일러 주었듯, 그는 오른손의 더 큰 영광을 위해 왼손의 쓰임을 잃은 것이었다."],["for the greater glory of the right. This, however, did not absolutely unfit him for service, and in April 1572 he joined Manuel Ponce de Leon’s company of Lope de Figueroa’s regiment, in which, it seems probable, his brother Rodrigo was serving, and shared in the operations of the next three years, including the capture of the Goletta and Tunis.","그러나 이것이 그를 복무에서 완전히 부적격으로 만들지는 않아, 1572년 4월 그는 로페 데 피게로아 연대의 마누엘 폰세 데 레온 중대에 합류했는데, 그의 형 로드리고도 거기서 복무하고 있었을 공산이 크며, 골레타와 튀니스 점령을 포함하여 이후 삼 년간의 작전에 참여했다."],["Taking advantage of the lull which followed the recapture of these places by the Turks, he obtained leave to return to Spain, and sailed from Naples in September 1575 on board the Sun galley, in company with his brother Rodrigo, Pedro Carrillo de Quesada, late Governor of the Goletta, and some others, and furnished with letters from Don John of Austria and the Duke of Sesa, the Viceroy of Sicily, recommending him to the King for the command of a company, on account of his services; a dono infelice as events proved.","튀르크인들이 이곳들을 다시 빼앗은 뒤 찾아온 소강상태를 틈타, 그는 스페인으로 돌아갈 허가를 얻어, 1575년 9월 형 로드리고, 골레타의 전 총독 페드로 카리요 데 케사다, 그리고 몇몇 사람과 함께 솔호라는 갤리선을 타고 나폴리에서 출항했다. 그는 자신의 공로를 인정받아 한 중대의 지휘권을 맡도록 왕에게 자신을 천거하는, 돈 후안 데 아우스트리아와 시칠리아 부왕 세사 공작의 추천서를 지니고 있었으니, 그것은 사건들이 입증했듯 불행한 선물(dono infelice)이었다."],["On the 26th they fell in with a squadron of Algerine galleys, and after a stout resistance were overpowered and carried into Algiers.","26일에 그들은 알제리 갤리선 한 무리와 마주쳤고, 굳센 저항 끝에 제압되어 알제로 끌려갔다."]],[["By means of a ransomed fellow-captive the brothers contrived to inform their family of their condition, and the poor people at Alcala at once strove to raise the ransom money, the father disposing of all he possessed, and the two sisters giving up their marriage portions.","몸값을 치르고 풀려난 한 동료 포로를 통해 형제는 가까스로 가족에게 자기들의 처지를 알렸고, 알칼라의 그 가엾은 사람들은 곧장 몸값을 마련하려 애써, 아버지는 가진 것을 모조리 처분하고 두 누이는 자기들의 결혼 지참금을 내놓았다."],["But Dali Mami had found on Cervantes the letters addressed to the King by Don John and the Duke of Sesa, and, concluding that his prize must be a person of great consequence, when the money came he refused it scornfully as being altogether insufficient.","그러나 달리 마미는 세르반테스에게서 돈 후안과 세사 공작이 왕에게 보내는 편지를 발견하고는, 자기 포로가 대단히 중요한 인물임이 틀림없다고 결론지어, 돈이 왔을 때 그것을 턱없이 모자란다며 멸시하듯 거절했다."],["The owner of Rodrigo, however, was more easily satisfied; ransom was accepted in his case, and it was arranged between the brothers that he should return to Spain and procure a vessel in which he was to come back to Algiers and take off Miguel and as many of their comrades as possible.","그러나 로드리고의 주인은 더 쉽게 만족하여, 그의 경우에는 몸값이 받아들여졌다. 형제 사이에는, 로드리고가 스페인으로 돌아가 배를 한 척 구해서 그 배로 알제에 돌아와 미겔과, 가능한 한 많은 동료를 데리고 나오기로 합의가 이루어졌다."],["This was not the first attempt to escape that Cervantes had made.","이것이 세르반테스가 시도한 첫 번째 탈출은 아니었다."],["Soon after the commencement of his captivity he induced several of his companions to join him in trying to reach Oran, then a Spanish post, on foot; but after the first day’s journey, the Moor who had agreed to act as their guide deserted them, and they had no choice but to return.","포로 생활이 시작된 직후 그는 동료 몇몇을 꾀어, 당시 스페인의 전초지였던 오란까지 걸어서 가 보려 했다. 그러나 첫날 여정이 끝나자 길잡이 노릇을 하기로 했던 무어인이 그들을 버리고 달아나, 그들은 돌아오는 수밖에 없었다."],["The second attempt was more disastrous.","두 번째 시도는 더 참담했다."],["In a garden outside the city on the sea-shore, he constructed, with the help of the gardener, a Spaniard, a hiding-place, to which he brought, one by one, fourteen of his fellow-captives, keeping them there in secrecy for several months, and supplying them with food through a renegade known as El Dorador, “the Gilder.”","그는 해안가 도시 밖 한 정원에, 스페인 사람인 정원사의 도움을 받아 은신처를 만들고, 거기로 동료 포로 열넷을 하나씩 데려와 여러 달 동안 비밀리에 머물게 하면서, \"엘 도라도르\", 곧 \"도금장이\"라 알려진 한 배교자를 통해 그들에게 음식을 대 주었다."],["How he, a captive himself, contrived to do all this, is one of the mysteries of the story.","자신도 포로인 그가 어떻게 이 모든 일을 해냈는지는 이 이야기의 수수께끼 가운데 하나이다."],["Wild as the project may appear, it was very nearly successful.","그 계획이 아무리 무모해 보일지라도, 그것은 거의 성공할 뻔했다."],["The vessel procured by Rodrigo made its appearance off the coast, and under cover of night was proceeding to take off the refugees, when the crew were alarmed by a passing fishing boat, and beat a hasty retreat.","로드리고가 구한 배가 해안 앞바다에 나타나 밤을 틈타 도망자들을 태우려 다가오던 차에, 선원들이 지나가던 고깃배에 놀라 황급히 물러났다."],["On renewing the attempt shortly afterwards, they, or a portion of them at least, were taken prisoners, and just as the poor fellows in the garden were exulting in the thought that in a few moments more freedom would be within their grasp, they found themselves surrounded by Turkish troops, horse and foot.","얼마 뒤 다시 시도했을 때, 그들은, 적어도 그들 가운데 일부는 포로로 붙잡혔고, 정원에 있던 그 가엾은 이들이 이제 잠시만 지나면 자유가 손안에 들어오리라는 생각에 들떠 있던 바로 그때, 자기들이 튀르크 군대에, 기병과 보병에 에워싸여 있음을 알게 되었다."],["The Dorador had revealed the whole scheme to the Dey Hassan.","도라도르가 그 계획 전부를 데이 하산에게 밀고했던 것이다."]],[["When Cervantes saw what had befallen them, he charged his companions to lay all the blame upon him, and as they were being bound he declared aloud that the whole plot was of his contriving, and that nobody else had any share in it.","세르반테스는 자기들에게 닥친 일을 보자, 동료들에게 모든 죄를 자기에게 뒤집어씌우라고 당부했고, 그들이 결박당하는 동안 음모 전부가 자기가 꾸민 것이며 다른 누구도 거기에 가담하지 않았노라고 큰 소리로 선언했다. 데이 앞에 끌려가서도 그는 똑같이 말했다."],["Brought before the Dey, he said the same.","그는 꼬챙이에 꿰이는 형과 고문으로 위협받았다."],["He was threatened with impalement and with torture; and as cutting off ears and noses were playful freaks with the Algerines, it may be conceived what their tortures were like; but nothing could make him swerve from his original statement that he and he alone was responsible.","귀와 코를 베는 것이 알제리 사람들에게는 장난스러운 짓거리였으니, 그들의 고문이 어떠했을지는 가히 짐작할 만하다. 그러나 어떤 것으로도 그를, 자기, 오직 자기 혼자만이 책임이 있다는 처음의 진술에서 벗어나게 할 수 없었다."],["The upshot was that the unhappy gardener was hanged by his master, and the prisoners taken possession of by the Dey, who, however, afterwards restored most of them to their masters, but kept Cervantes, paying Dali Mami 500 crowns for him.","그 결말로, 가엾은 정원사는 그 주인에게 교수형을 당했고, 포로들은 데이가 차지했는데, 데이는 그러나 나중에 그들 대부분을 주인들에게 돌려주되 세르반테스만은 달리 마미에게 5백 크라운을 치르고 데리고 있었다."],["He felt, no doubt, that a man of such resource, energy, and daring, was too dangerous a piece of property to be left in private hands; and he had him heavily ironed and lodged in his own prison.","그는 분명, 그토록 수완과 정력과 대담함을 갖춘 사람을 사사로운 손에 맡겨 두기에는 너무 위험한 재산이라고 여겼던 것이다. 그래서 그를 무거운 쇠고랑에 채워 자기 감옥에 가두었다."],["If he thought that by these means he could break the spirit or shake the resolution of his prisoner, he was soon undeceived, for Cervantes contrived before long to despatch a letter to the Governor of Oran, entreating him to send him some one that could be trusted, to enable him and three other gentlemen, fellow-captives of his, to make their escape; intending evidently to renew his first attempt with a more trustworthy guide.","만일 이런 수단으로 자기 포로의 기개를 꺾거나 결심을 흔들 수 있으리라 생각했다면, 그는 곧 깨우침을 얻었다. 세르반테스가 머지않아 오란의 총독에게 편지 한 통을 보내는 데 성공했기 때문이다."],["Unfortunately the Moor who carried the letter was stopped just outside Oran, and the letter being found upon him, he was sent back to Algiers, where by the order of the Dey he was promptly impaled as a warning to others, while Cervantes was condemned to receive two thousand blows of the stick, a number which most likely would have deprived the world of “Don Quixote,”","그 편지에서 그는 믿을 만한 사람을 보내, 자신과 동료 포로인 다른 세 신사가 탈출할 수 있게 해 달라고 간청했으니, 분명 더 믿음직한 길잡이를 데리고 첫 번째 시도를 되풀이할 작정이었다. 불행히도 편지를 가져간 무어인이 오란 바로 밖에서 붙잡혔고, 편지가 그에게서 발견되자 그는 알제로 돌려보내져 데이의 명령에 따라 다른 이들에게 경고로 삼고자 즉시 꼬챙이에 꿰였으며, 세르반테스는 곤장 이천 대를 맞으라는 선고를 받았다."],["had not some persons, who they were we know not, interceded on his behalf.","그 정도면 십중팔구 세상에서 「돈키호테」를 앗아 갔을 숫자였으나, 누구인지는 모르나 몇 사람이 그를 위해 나서서 간청해 준 덕에 그리되지 않았다."]],[["After this he seems to have been kept in still closer confinement than before, for nearly two years passed before he made another attempt.","이 일이 있은 뒤 그는 전보다 더 엄중히 갇혀 지낸 듯하니, 그가 또 한 번 시도하기까지 거의 두 해가 지났기 때문이다."],["This time his plan was to purchase, by the aid of a Spanish renegade and two Valencian merchants resident in Algiers, an armed vessel in which he and about sixty of the leading captives were to make their escape; but just as they were about to put it into execution one Doctor Juan Blanco de Paz, an ecclesiastic and a compatriot, informed the Dey of the plot.","이번에 그의 계획은, 한 스페인 배교자와 알제에 거주하는 두 발렌시아 상인의 도움을 받아 무장한 배 한 척을 사서, 그 배로 자신과 주요 포로 예순 명가량이 함께 탈출하려는 것이었다. 그러나 그것을 막 실행에 옮기려던 차에, 후안 블랑코 데 파스 박사라는, 성직자이자 동포인 자가 데이에게 그 음모를 밀고했다."],["Cervantes by force of character, by his self-devotion, by his untiring energy and his exertions to lighten the lot of his companions in misery, had endeared himself to all, and become the leading spirit in the captive colony, and, incredible as it may seem, jealousy of his influence and the esteem in which he was held, moved this man to compass his destruction by a cruel death.","세르반테스는 굳센 성품과 헌신, 그리고 비참한 처지에 놓인 동료들의 운명을 가볍게 해 주려는 지칠 줄 모르는 정력과 노력으로 모두에게 사랑받게 되었고 포로 사회의 중심인물이 되었는데, 믿기 어려울지 모르나, 그의 영향력과 그가 받는 존경에 대한 시기심이 이 사람을 움직여 잔혹한 죽음으로 그를 파멸시키려 들게 했던 것이다."],["The merchants finding that the Dey knew all, and fearing that Cervantes under torture might make disclosures that would imperil their own lives, tried to persuade him to slip away on board a vessel that was on the point of sailing for Spain; but he told them they had nothing to fear, for no tortures would make him compromise anybody, and he went at once and gave himself up to the Dey.","상인들은 데이가 모든 것을 알고 있음을 알아채고, 세르반테스가 고문 아래 자기들의 목숨을 위태롭게 할 무언가를 발설할까 두려워, 막 스페인으로 떠나려던 배에 올라 슬며시 빠져나가라고 그를 설득하려 했다. 그러나 그는 그들에게 두려워할 것 없으니 어떤 고문으로도 자신은 누구도 연루시키지 않으리라 말하고는, 곧장 가서 데이에게 자수했다."]],[["As before, the Dey tried to force him to name his accomplices.","전과 마찬가지로 데이는 그에게 공범들의 이름을 대라고 강요하려 했다."],["Everything was made ready for his immediate execution; the halter was put round his neck and his hands tied behind him, but all that could be got from him was that he himself, with the help of four gentlemen who had since left Algiers, had arranged the whole, and that the sixty who were to accompany him were not to know anything of it until the last moment.","그를 즉시 처형할 채비가 모두 갖추어져, 올가미가 그의 목에 둘러지고 손이 등 뒤로 묶였으나, 그에게서 얻어 낼 수 있었던 것이라고는, 그 뒤 알제를 떠난 네 신사의 도움을 받아 자기 혼자서 모든 일을 꾸몄으며, 자기와 동행할 예순 명은 마지막 순간까지 그에 관해 아무것도 모르게 되어 있었다는 말뿐이었다."],["Finding he could make nothing of him, the Dey sent him back to prison more heavily ironed than before.","그에게서 아무것도 알아낼 수 없음을 안 데이는, 그를 전보다 더 무거운 쇠고랑에 채워 감옥으로 돌려보냈다."]],[["The poverty-stricken Cervantes family had been all this time trying once more to raise the ransom money, and at last a sum of three hundred ducats was got together and entrusted to the Redemptorist Father Juan Gil, who was about to sail for Algiers.","가난에 찌든 세르반테스 가족은 이 모든 시간 동안 또 한 번 몸값을 마련하려 애썼고, 마침내 삼백 두카트의 돈이 모아져, 막 알제로 떠나려던 속량 수도회의 후안 힐 신부에게 맡겨졌다."],["The Dey, however, demanded more than double the sum offered, and as his term of office had expired and he was about to sail for Constantinople, taking all his slaves with him, the case of Cervantes was critical.","그러나 데이는 제시된 액수의 두 배가 넘게 요구했고, 그의 임기가 끝나 그가 모든 노예를 데리고 콘스탄티노플로 막 떠나려던 참이었기에, 세르반테스의 처지는 위태로웠다."],["He was already on board heavily ironed, when the Dey at length agreed to reduce his demand by one-half, and Father Gil by borrowing was able to make up the amount, and on September 19, 1580, after a captivity of five years all but a week, Cervantes was at last set free.","무거운 쇠고랑을 찬 채 그가 이미 배에 올라 있을 때, 데이가 마침내 요구를 절반으로 줄이는 데 동의했고, 힐 신부가 빌려서 그 액수를 채울 수 있었던 덕에, 일주일을 뺀 오 년의 포로 생활 끝에 1580년 9월 19일 세르반테스는 마침내 자유의 몸이 되었다."],["Before long he discovered that Blanco de Paz, who claimed to be an officer of the Inquisition, was now concocting on false evidence a charge of misconduct to be brought against him on his return to Spain.","머지않아 그는, 종교 재판소의 관리라 자처하던 블랑코 데 파스가, 그가 스페인으로 돌아갔을 때 그에게 씌울 비행(非行) 혐의를 거짓 증거로 꾸며 내고 있음을 알아챘다."],["To checkmate him Cervantes drew up a series of twenty-five questions, covering the whole period of his captivity, upon which he requested Father Gil to take the depositions of credible witnesses before a notary.","그를 꼼짝 못하게 만들고자 세르반테스는 포로 생활 전 기간을 아우르는 스물다섯 가지 질문을 작성하고, 그것에 관해 공증인 앞에서 믿을 만한 증인들의 진술을 받아 달라고 힐 신부에게 청했다."],["Eleven witnesses taken from among the principal captives in Algiers deposed to all the facts above stated and to a great deal more besides.","알제의 주요 포로들 가운데서 뽑힌 열한 명의 증인이 앞서 진술한 모든 사실과, 그 밖에도 훨씬 많은 것을 증언했다."],["There is something touching in the admiration, love, and gratitude we see struggling to find expression in the formal language of the notary, as they testify one after another to the good deeds of Cervantes, how he comforted and helped the weak-hearted, how he kept up their drooping courage, how he shared his poor purse with this deponent, and how “in him this deponent found father and mother.”","그들이 하나씩 차례로 세르반테스의 선행을 증언하면서 — 그가 마음 약한 이들을 어떻게 위로하고 도왔는지, 꺾여 가는 그들의 용기를 어떻게 북돋웠는지, 자신의 가난한 지갑을 이 증언자와 어떻게 나누었는지, 그리고 \"이 증언자가 그에게서 아버지와 어머니를 발견했다\"는 것을 — 공증인의 격식 차린 언어 속에서 표현될 길을 찾으려 몸부림치는 그 흠모와 사랑과 감사에는 어딘가 가슴을 울리는 데가 있다."]],[["On his return to Spain he found his old regiment about to march for Portugal to support Philip’s claim to the crown, and utterly penniless now, had no choice but to rejoin it.","스페인으로 돌아온 그는, 자신의 옛 연대가 펠리페의 왕위 요구를 지지하고자 포르투갈로 진군하려는 참임을 알았고, 이제 완전히 빈털터리였던 터라 거기에 다시 합류하는 수밖에 없었다."],["He was in the expeditions to the Azores in 1582 and the following year, and on the conclusion of the war returned to Spain in the autumn of 1583, bringing with him the manuscript of his pastoral romance, the “Galatea,”","그는 1582년과 그 이듬해 아소레스 원정에 참가했고, 전쟁이 끝나자 1583년 가을 스페인으로 돌아오면서 자신의 목가 소설 「갈라테아」의 원고를, 그리고 내적 증거로 미루어 보건대 아마도 「페르실레스와 시히스문다」"],["and probably also, to judge by internal evidence, that of the first portion of “Persiles and Sigismunda.”","첫 부분의 원고도 함께 가져왔다."],["He also brought back with him, his biographers assert, an infant daughter, the offspring of an amour, as some of them with great circumstantiality inform us, with a Lisbon lady of noble birth, whose name, however, as well as that of the street she lived in, they omit to mention.","그의 전기 작가들이 주장하는 바로는, 그는 또한 어떤 정사(情事)의 소생인 어린 딸을 데리고 돌아왔는데, 그들 가운데 일부가 매우 자세하게 일러 주는 바로는 그 상대가 리스본의 고귀한 가문 출신 여인이었으나, 그 여인의 이름은 물론 그녀가 살던 거리의 이름조차 그들은 밝히지 않는다."],["The sole foundation for all this is that in 1605 there certainly was living in the family of Cervantes a Dona Isabel de Saavedra, who is described in an official document as his natural daughter, and then twenty years of age.","이 모든 이야기의 유일한 근거는, 1605년에 세르반테스 가족 가운데 도냐 이사벨 데 사아베드라라는 여인이 분명 살고 있었으며, 그녀가 한 공식 문서에 그의 사생아이자 당시 스무 살이라 기록되어 있다는 사실뿐이다."]],[["With his crippled left hand promotion in the army was hopeless, now that Don John was dead and he had no one to press his claims and services, and for a man drawing on to forty life in the ranks was a dismal prospect; he had already a certain reputation as a poet; he made up his mind, therefore, to cast his lot with literature, and for a first venture committed his “Galatea”","불구가 된 왼손으로는 군에서 진급할 가망이 없었으니, 이제 돈 후안은 죽고 그의 청원과 공로를 밀어줄 사람이 아무도 없는 데다, 마흔을 바라보는 사람에게 졸병 신세는 음울한 앞날이었다. 그는 이미 시인으로서 어느 정도 명성을 얻고 있었으므로, 문학에 운명을 걸기로 마음먹고, 그 첫 시도로 「갈라테아」를 인쇄에 부쳤다."],["to the press. It was published, as Salva y Mallen shows conclusively, at Alcala, his own birth-place, in 1585 and no doubt helped to make his name more widely known, but certainly did not do him much good in any other way.","살바 이 마옌이 결정적으로 밝혀 보였듯, 그것은 1585년 그 자신의 출생지인 알칼라에서 출간되었고, 분명 그의 이름을 더 널리 알리는 데 도움이 되었으나, 다른 어떤 면에서도 그에게 별 이득을 주지는 못한 것이 확실하다."]],[["While it was going through the press, he married Dona Catalina de Palacios Salazar y Vozmediano, a lady of Esquivias near Madrid, and apparently a friend of the family, who brought him a fortune which may possibly have served to keep the wolf from the door, but if so, that was all.","그 책이 인쇄에 부쳐지는 동안 그는 마드리드 인근 에스키비아스의 여인이자 보아하니 그 가족의 벗이었던 도냐 카탈리나 데 팔라시오스 살라사르 이 보스메디아노와 혼인했는데, 그녀는 그에게 어쩌면 끼니 걱정을 면하게 해 주었을 법한 재산을 가져왔으나, 설령 그렇다 해도 그게 다였다."],["The drama had by this time outgrown market-place stages and strolling companies, and with his old love for it he naturally turned to it for a congenial employment.","이 무렵 연극은 장터의 무대와 떠돌이 극단을 넘어설 만큼 자라나 있었고, 그것에 대한 오랜 사랑으로 그는 자연히 마음 맞는 일거리를 찾아 연극으로 눈을 돌렸다."],["In about three years he wrote twenty or thirty plays, which he tells us were performed without any throwing of cucumbers or other missiles, and ran their course without any hisses, outcries, or disturbance.","약 삼 년 동안 그는 스무 편인지 서른 편인지의 희곡을 썼는데, 그의 말로는 오이나 다른 물건이 날아드는 일 없이 공연되었고, 야유나 고함이나 소동 없이 무사히 막을 내렸다고 한다."],["In other words, his plays were not bad enough to be hissed off the stage, but not good enough to hold their own upon it.","다시 말해, 그의 희곡들은 무대에서 야유받아 쫓겨날 만큼 나쁘지는 않았으나, 무대에서 제힘으로 버틸 만큼 좋지도 않았던 것이다."],["Only two of them have been preserved, but as they happen to be two of the seven or eight he mentions with complacency, we may assume they are favourable specimens, and no one who reads the “Numancia” and the “Trato de Argel”","그 가운데 단 두 편만이 전해지는데, 마침 그것들이 그가 흐뭇하게 언급한 일고여덟 편 가운데 둘이므로, 우리는 그것들이 좋은 본보기라고 추정할 수 있겠으나, 「누만시아」와 「알제 거래기」를 읽는 사람이라면 누구도 그것들이 상연용 극으로서 실패한 것에 조금도 놀라지 않을 것이다."],["will feel any surprise that they failed as acting dramas. Whatever merits they may have, whatever occasional they may show, they are, as regards construction, incurably clumsy.","그것들이 어떤 장점을 지녔든, 이따금 어떤 번득임을 보여 주든, 구성에 관한 한 그것들은 구제 불능으로 서투르다."],["How completely they failed is manifest from the fact that with all his sanguine temperament and indomitable perseverance he was unable to maintain the struggle to gain a livelihood as a dramatist for more than three years; nor was the rising popularity of Lope the cause, as is often said, notwithstanding his own words to the contrary.","그것들이 얼마나 완벽하게 실패했는가는, 그토록 낙천적인 기질과 불굴의 끈기를 지닌 그가 극작가로서 생계를 꾸리려는 그 싸움을 삼 년 넘게 버텨 내지 못했다는 사실에서 분명히 드러난다. 또한 흔히들 말하듯 떠오르던 로페의 인기가 그 원인은 아니었으니, 그 자신은 그와 반대되는 말을 했어도 그렇다."],["When Lope began to write for the stage is uncertain, but it was certainly after Cervantes went to Seville.","로페가 언제 무대를 위해 글을 쓰기 시작했는지는 분명치 않으나, 그것이 세르반테스가 세비야로 간 뒤였음은 확실하다."]],[["Among the “Nuevos Documentos”","아센시오 이 톨레도 씨가 인쇄한 「새 문서들」"],["printed by Señor Asensio y Toledo is one dated 1592, and curiously characteristic of Cervantes.","가운데 1592년 자(字)의 것이 하나 있는데, 세르반테스의 특징을 묘하게 잘 보여 준다."],["It is an agreement with one Rodrigo Osorio, a manager, who was to accept six comedies at fifty ducats (about 6l.) apiece, not to be paid in any case unless it appeared on representation that the said comedy was one of the best that had ever been represented in Spain.","그것은 흥행주인 로드리고 오소리오라는 자와 맺은 계약으로, 그는 희극 여섯 편을 각각 오십 두카트(약 6파운드)에 받기로 했으되, 상연되었을 때 그 희극이 일찍이 스페인에서 상연된 가장 훌륭한 작품 가운데 하나임이 드러나지 않는 한 어떤 경우에도 값을 치르지 않기로 한 것이었다."],["The test does not seem to have been ever applied; perhaps it was sufficiently apparent to Rodrigo Osorio that the comedies were not among the best that had ever been represented.","그 시험은 끝내 한 번도 적용되지 않은 듯하다. 어쩌면 그 희극들이 일찍이 상연된 가장 훌륭한 작품 축에 들지 못한다는 것이 로드리고 오소리오에게 충분히 빤히 드러났는지도 모른다."],["Among the correspondence of Cervantes there might have been found, no doubt, more than one letter like that we see in the “Rake’s Progress,”","세르반테스의 서신 가운데에는, 「난봉꾼의 행각」에 나오는 \"선생, 당신 희곡을 읽어 보았소만, 이건 안 되겠소\""],["“Sir, I have read your play, and it will not doo.”","같은 편지가 분명 한 통 이상 있었을 법하다."]],[["He was more successful in a literary contest at Saragossa in 1595 in honour of the canonisation of St. Jacinto, when his composition won the first prize, three silver spoons.","1595년 사라고사에서 성 야신토의 시성(諡聖)을 기리는 한 문학 경연에서 그는 더 큰 성공을 거두어, 그의 작품이 일등상인 은수저 세 개를 탔다."],["The year before this he had been appointed a collector of revenues for the kingdom of Granada.","이보다 한 해 전에 그는 그라나다 왕국의 세금 징수원에 임명되어 있었다."],["In order to remit the money he had collected more conveniently to the treasury, he entrusted it to a merchant, who failed and absconded; and as the bankrupt’s assets were insufficient to cover the whole, he was sent to prison at Seville in September 1597.","거두어들인 돈을 국고로 더 편하게 송금하려고 그는 그것을 한 상인에게 맡겼는데, 그 상인이 파산하여 종적을 감추었다. 그리고 그 파산자의 재산이 전액을 메우기에 모자랐던 탓에, 그는 1597년 9월 세비야의 감옥에 갇혔다."],["The balance against him, however, was a small one, about 26l., and on giving security for it he was released at the end of the year.","그러나 그가 진 차액은 약 26파운드로 적은 액수였고, 그것에 대한 담보를 제공하자 그는 그해 말에 풀려났다."]],[["It was as he journeyed from town to town collecting the king’s taxes, that he noted down those bits of inn and wayside life and character that abound in the pages of “Don Quixote:”","왕의 세금을 거두느라 이 고을 저 고을을 떠도는 동안, 그는 「돈키호테」의 페이지마다 넘쳐 나는 그 여인숙과 길가의 삶과 인물상의 단편들을 적어 두었다."],["the Benedictine monks with spectacles and sunshades, mounted on their tall mules; the strollers in costume bound for the next village; the barber with his basin on his head, on his way to bleed a patient; the recruit with his breeches in his bundle, tramping along the road singing; the reapers gathered in the venta gateway listening to “Felixmarte of Hircania” read out to them; and those little Hogarthian touches that he so well knew how to bring in, the ox-tail hanging up with the landlord’s comb stuck in it, the wine-skins at the bed-head, and those notable examples of hostelry art, Helen going off in high spirits on Paris’s arm, and Dido on the tower dropping tears as big as walnuts.","안경을 쓰고 양산을 받친 채 키 큰 노새에 올라탄 베네딕트회 수도사들, 다음 마을로 가는 분장한 광대들, 환자에게 사혈을 하러 가는 길에 대야를 머리에 인 이발사, 반바지를 봇짐에 넣고 노래 부르며 길을 터벅터벅 걷는 신병, 여인숙 문간에 모여 「이르카니아의 펠릭스마르테」를 낭독하는 것을 듣는 추수꾼들, 그리고 그가 그토록 잘 끌어들일 줄 알았던 호가스풍의 자잘한 손길들 — 여관 주인의 빗이 꽂힌 채 매달린 소꼬리, 침대 머리맡의 포도주 부대, 그리고 여인숙 예술의 저 주목할 만한 본보기들, 곧 파리스의 팔에 매달려 한껏 들떠 떠나가는 헬레네와, 탑 위에서 호두알만 한 눈물을 떨구는 디도 같은 것들 말이다."],["Nay, it may well be that on those journeys into remote regions he came across now and then a specimen of the pauper gentleman, with his lean hack and his greyhound and his books of chivalry, dreaming away his life in happy ignorance that the world had changed since his great-grandfather’s old helmet was new.","아니, 외진 고장으로 가는 그 여정에서 그가 이따금 빈궁한 신사의 한 표본과 마주쳤을 법도 하다. 비쩍 마른 말과 사냥개와 기사도 서적을 갖추고, 자기 증조부의 옛 투구가 새것이던 시절 이래 세상이 변했음을 행복하게 모른 채 일생을 꿈결로 흘려보내는 그런 신사 말이다."],["But it was in Seville that he found out his true vocation, though he himself would not by any means have admitted it to be so.","그러나 그가 자신의 진정한 천직을 발견한 곳은 세비야였으니, 그 자신은 결코 그렇다고 인정하려 들지 않았겠지만 말이다."],["It was there, in Triana, that he was first tempted to try his hand at drawing from life, and first brought his humour into play in the exquisite little sketch of “Rinconete y Cortadillo,” the germ, in more ways than one, of “Don Quixote.”","바로 그곳, 트리아나에서 그는 처음으로 삶에서 직접 묘사를 시도해 보고 싶은 유혹을 느꼈고, 「린코네테와 코르타디요」라는 더없이 빼어난 작은 소품 — 여러 면에서 「돈키호테」의 씨앗인 — 에서 처음으로 자신의 해학을 발휘해 보였다."]],[["Where and when that was written, we cannot tell.","그것이 어디서 언제 쓰였는지 우리는 알 수 없다."],["After his imprisonment all trace of Cervantes in his official capacity disappears, from which it may be inferred that he was not reinstated.","그가 투옥된 뒤로 공직자로서의 세르반테스의 자취는 모두 사라지는데, 이로 미루어 그가 복직되지 못했다고 추정할 수 있다."],["That he was still in Seville in November 1598 appears from a satirical sonnet of his on the elaborate catafalque erected to testify the grief of the city at the death of Philip II, but from this up to 1603 we have no clue to his movements. The words in the preface to the First Part of “Don Quixote”","그가 1598년 11월에도 여전히 세비야에 있었음은, 펠리페 2세의 죽음에 즈음하여 도시의 슬픔을 나타내고자 세운 정교한 영구대(靈柩臺)에 부친 그의 풍자 소네트 한 편에서 드러나지만, 이때부터 1603년까지는 그의 행적에 관한 단서가 우리에게 없다."],["are generally held to be conclusive that he conceived the idea of the book, and wrote the beginning of it at least, in a prison, and that he may have done so is extremely likely.","「돈키호테」 제1부 서문의 말은, 그가 이 책의 착상을 떠올리고 적어도 그 첫머리만은 감옥에서 썼다는 것을 결정적으로 보여 주는 것으로 흔히들 여기며, 그가 그랬을 가능성은 매우 높다."]],[["There is a tradition that Cervantes read some portions of his work to a select audience at the Duke of Bejar’s, which may have helped to make the book known; but the obvious conclusion is that the First Part of “Don Quixote”","세르반테스가 베하르 공작의 저택에서 가려 뽑은 청중에게 자기 작품의 일부를 읽어 주었다는 전승이 있는데, 이것이 그 책을 알리는 데 도움이 되었을지도 모른다. 그러나 분명한 결론은, 「돈키호테」"],["lay on his hands some time before he could find a publisher bold enough to undertake a venture of so novel a character; and so little faith in it had Francisco Robles of Madrid, to whom at last he sold it, that he did not care to incur the expense of securing the copyright for Aragon or Portugal, contenting himself with that for Castile.","제1부가 그토록 새로운 성격의 모험을 떠맡을 만큼 대담한 출판업자를 그가 찾아내기까지 한동안 그의 수중에 머물러 있었다는 것이다. 그리고 마침내 그가 그것을 판 마드리드의 프란시스코 로블레스는 그 책을 거의 믿지 않아, 아라곤이나 포르투갈의 판권을 확보하는 비용을 들일 마음조차 없이 카스티야의 판권으로 만족했다."],["The printing was finished in December, and the book came out with the new year, 1605.","인쇄는 12월에 끝났고, 책은 새해, 곧 1605년에 나왔다."],["It is often said that “Don Quixote” was at first received coldly.","「돈키호테」가 처음에는 냉담하게 받아들여졌다고들 흔히 말한다."],["The facts show just the contrary.","사실은 정반대를 보여 준다."],["No sooner was it in the hands of the public than preparations were made to issue pirated editions at Lisbon and Valencia, and to bring out a second edition with the additional copyrights for Aragon and Portugal, which he secured in February.","그것이 대중의 손에 들어가기가 무섭게 리스본과 발렌시아에서 해적판을 낼 채비가 갖추어졌고, 그가 2월에 확보한 아라곤과 포르투갈의 추가 판권을 더한 재판을 낼 준비가 이루어졌다."]],[["No doubt it was received with something more than coldness by certain sections of the community.","물론 그것은 사회의 어떤 부류들에게는 냉담함 그 이상의 무언가로 받아들여졌다."],["Men of wit, taste, and discrimination among the aristocracy gave it a hearty welcome, but the aristocracy in general were not likely to relish a book that turned their favourite reading into ridicule and laughed at so many of their favourite ideas.","귀족 가운데 재치와 안목과 분별을 갖춘 이들은 그것을 진심으로 환영했으나, 귀족 일반은 자기들이 가장 좋아하는 읽을거리를 조롱거리로 만들고 자기들이 아끼는 그 많은 관념을 비웃는 책을 달가워할 리 없었다."],["The dramatists who gathered round Lope as their leader regarded Cervantes as their common enemy, and it is plain that he was equally obnoxious to the other clique, the culto poets who had Gongora for their chief.","로페를 우두머리로 떠받들며 그 둘레에 모여든 극작가들은 세르반테스를 자기들 공동의 적으로 여겼고, 공고라를 우두머리로 둔 또 다른 패거리, 곧 기교파 시인들에게도 그가 똑같이 미움받았음이 분명하다."],["Navarrete, who knew nothing of the letter above mentioned, tries hard to show that the relations between Cervantes and Lope were of a very friendly sort, as indeed they were until “Don Quixote” was written.","앞서 언급한 그 편지에 대해서는 전혀 알지 못한 나바레테는, 세르반테스와 로페 사이의 관계가 매우 우호적인 것이었음을 애써 보이려 하는데, 「돈키호테」가 쓰이기 전까지는 실제로 그러했다."],["Cervantes, indeed, to the last generously and manfully declared his admiration of Lope’s powers, his unfailing invention, and his marvellous fertility; but in the preface of the First Part of “Don Quixote”","사실 세르반테스는 마지막까지 너그럽고도 사내답게 로페의 역량과 그 마르지 않는 착상, 그 놀라운 다작을 칭송한다고 공언했다. 그러나 「돈키호테」"],["and in the verses of “Urganda the Unknown,” and one or two other places, there are, if we read between the lines, sly hits at Lope’s vanities and affectations that argue no personal good-will; and Lope openly sneers at “Don Quixote”","제1부 서문에서, 그리고 「알 수 없는 우르간다」의 시구에서, 또 한두 군데 다른 곳에서, 행간을 읽어 보면, 아무런 개인적 호의도 내비치지 않는, 로페의 허영과 가식에 대한 은근한 꼬집음이 있다."],["and Cervantes, and fourteen years after his death gives him only a few lines of cold commonplace in the “Laurel de Apolo,” that seem all the colder for the eulogies of a host of nonentities whose names are found nowhere else.","그리고 로페는 「돈키호테」와 세르반테스를 드러내 놓고 비웃었으며, 그가 죽은 지 십사 년 뒤 「아폴로의 월계관」에서 그에게 차갑고 진부한 몇 줄만을 바쳤을 뿐인데, 그것은 다른 어디에서도 그 이름을 찾을 수 없는 한 무리의 보잘것없는 자들에게 바친 찬사들 탓에 더더욱 차갑게 느껴진다."]],[["In 1601 Valladolid was made the seat of the Court, and at the beginning of 1603 Cervantes had been summoned thither in connection with the balance due by him to the Treasury, which was still outstanding.","1601년 바야돌리드가 궁정의 소재지가 되었고, 1603년 초에 세르반테스는 아직 미결로 남아 있던, 그가 국고에 진 차액과 관련하여 그리로 소환되었다."],["He remained at Valladolid, apparently supporting himself by agencies and scrivener’s work of some sort; probably drafting petitions and drawing up statements of claims to be presented to the Council, and the like.","그는 바야돌리드에 머물렀는데, 보아하니 대리 업무와 모종의 대서(代書) 일로 생계를 꾸린 듯하다. 아마도 심의회에 제출할 청원서를 작성하고 청구 진술서를 꾸미는 따위의 일이었을 것이다."],["So, at least, we gather from the depositions taken on the occasion of the death of a gentleman, the victim of a street brawl, who had been carried into the house in which he lived.","적어도 우리는, 거리의 난투극의 희생자가 되어 그가 살던 집으로 옮겨진 한 신사의 죽음에 즈음하여 받아 둔 진술에서 그렇게 미루어 본다."],["In these he himself is described as a man who wrote and transacted business, and it appears that his household then consisted of his wife, the natural daughter Isabel de Saavedra already mentioned, his sister Andrea, now a widow, her daughter Constanza, a mysterious Magdalena de Sotomayor calling herself his sister, for whom his biographers cannot account, and a servant-maid.","그 진술에서 그 자신은 글을 쓰고 업무를 처리하는 사람으로 묘사되어 있으며, 당시 그의 집안은 아내, 앞서 언급한 사생아 딸 이사벨 데 사아베드라, 이제 과부가 된 누이 안드레아, 그녀의 딸 콘스탄사, 그리고 자신을 그의 누이라 일컬었으나 전기 작가들이 그 내력을 밝히지 못하는 수수께끼 같은 막달레나 데 소토마요르, 그리고 하녀 하나로 이루어져 있었던 듯하다."]],[["Meanwhile “Don Quixote” had been growing in favour, and its author’s name was now known beyond the Pyrenees.","그러는 동안 「돈키호테」는 점점 더 호평을 받아 갔고, 그 저자의 이름은 이제 피레네산맥 너머에까지 알려졌다."],["In 1607 an edition was printed at Brussels.","1607년 브뤼셀에서 한 판이 인쇄되었다."],["Robles, the Madrid publisher, found it necessary to meet the demand by a third edition, the seventh in all, in 1608.","마드리드의 출판업자 로블레스는 1608년 세 번째 판, 곧 통틀어 일곱 번째 판으로 그 수요를 맞춰야 할 형편이었다."],["The popularity of the book in Italy was such that a Milan bookseller was led to bring out an edition in 1610; and another was called for in Brussels in 1611.","이탈리아에서 그 책의 인기가 어찌나 대단했던지 밀라노의 한 서적상이 1610년에 한 판을 내기에 이르렀고, 1611년에는 브뤼셀에서 또 한 판이 요청되었다."],["It might naturally have been expected that, with such proofs before him that he had hit the taste of the public, Cervantes would have at once set about redeeming his rather vague promise of a second volume.","자신이 대중의 취향을 적중시켰다는 그러한 증거들이 눈앞에 있었으니, 세르반테스가 다소 막연한 두 번째 권의 약속을 곧장 이행하는 데 착수했으리라 자연히 기대했을 법하다."]],[["But, to all appearance, nothing was farther from his thoughts. He had still by him one or two short tales of the same vintage as those he had inserted in “Don Quixote”","그러나 보아하니, 그의 생각에서 그보다 더 먼 것은 없었다."],["and instead of continuing the adventures of Don Quixote, he set to work to write more of these “Novelas Exemplares” as he afterwards called them, with a view to making a book of them.","그에게는 「돈키호테」에 끼워 넣었던 것들과 같은 부류의 짤막한 이야기가 아직 한두 편 남아 있었고, 돈키호테의 모험을 이어 가는 대신 그는 그것들로 책 한 권을 묶을 작정으로, 훗날 그가 「모범 소설집」이라 부른 그 「노벨라스 에헴플라레스」를 더 써 나가기 시작했다."]],[["The novels were published in the summer of 1613, with a dedication to the Conde de Lemos, the Maecenas of the day, and with one of those chatty confidential prefaces Cervantes was so fond of.","그 소설들은 1613년 여름에 출간되었는데, 당대의 마이케나스였던 레모스 백작에게 바치는 헌사와, 세르반테스가 그토록 좋아하던 그 친근하고 허물없는 서문 가운데 하나가 곁들여졌다."],["In this, eight years and a half after the First Part of “Don Quixote”","이 서문에서, 「돈키호테」"],["had appeared, we get the first hint of a forthcoming Second Part.","제1부가 나온 지 팔 년 반 만에, 우리는 곧 나올 제2부에 대한 첫 암시를 얻는다."],["“You shall see shortly,”","\"머지않아 여러분은,\""],["he says, “the further exploits of Don Quixote and humours of Sancho Panza.”","그가 말하기를, \"돈키호테의 더 많은 활약과 산초 판사의 익살을 보게 될 것입니다.\""],["His idea of “shortly” was a somewhat elastic one, for, as we know by the date to Sancho’s letter, he had barely one-half of the book completed that time twelvemonth.","그가 말하는 \"머지않아\"란 다소 신축성 있는 것이었으니, 산초의 편지에 적힌 날짜로 우리가 알 수 있듯, 그는 그로부터 일 년이 지나서야 그 책의 겨우 절반을 완성한 정도였기 때문이다."]],[["But more than poems, or pastorals, or novels, it was his dramatic ambition that engrossed his thoughts.","그러나 시든, 목가든, 소설이든, 그 무엇보다 그의 마음을 사로잡은 것은 극작에 대한 야심이었다."],["The same indomitable spirit that kept him from despair in the bagnios of Algiers, and prompted him to attempt the escape of himself and his comrades again and again, made him persevere in spite of failure and discouragement in his efforts to win the ear of the public as a dramatist.","알제의 감옥에서 그를 절망으로부터 지켜 주고, 자신과 동료들의 탈출을 거듭거듭 시도하도록 그를 부추겼던 바로 그 불굴의 정신이, 극작가로서 대중의 귀를 사로잡으려는 노력에서 실패와 좌절에도 아랑곳없이 그를 끈질기게 버티게 했다."],["The temperament of Cervantes was essentially sanguine.","세르반테스의 기질은 본질적으로 낙천적이었다."],["The portrait he draws in the preface to the novels, with the aquiline features, chestnut hair, smooth untroubled forehead, and bright cheerful eyes, is the very portrait of a sanguine man.","소설집 서문에서 그가 그려 보인 초상 — 매부리코의 이목구비, 밤색 머리칼, 매끄럽고 근심 없는 이마, 그리고 밝고 쾌활한 눈 — 은 영락없는 낙천가의 초상이다."],["Nothing that the managers might say could persuade him that the merits of his plays would not be recognised at last if they were only given a fair chance.","흥행주들이 무슨 말을 하든, 자기 희곡들이 한 번이라도 공정한 기회만 얻는다면 그 진가가 끝내 인정받으리라는 생각을 그에게서 거둘 수는 없었다."],["The old soldier of the Spanish Salamis was bent on being the Aeschylus of Spain.","스페인 살라미스의 그 늙은 병사는 스페인의 아이스킬로스가 되기로 작정한 것이었다."],["He was to found a great national drama, based on the true principles of art, that was to be the envy of all nations; he was to drive from the stage the silly, childish plays, the “mirrors of nonsense and models of folly”","그는 예술의 참된 원리에 바탕을 둔, 모든 나라의 부러움을 살 위대한 국민극을 세우려 했다. 흥행주들의 탐욕과 작가들의 근시안 탓에 유행하던 그 어리석고 유치한 연극들, 그 \"헛소리의 거울이요 어리석음의 본보기\"인 것들을 무대에서 몰아내려 했고, 대중의 취향을 — 이를테면 「누만시아」"],["that were in vogue through the cupidity of the managers and shortsightedness of the authors; he was to correct and educate the public taste until it was ripe for tragedies on the model of the Greek drama--like the “Numancia”","같은, 그리스 극을 본뜬 비극과, 즐겁게 할 뿐 아니라 나아지게 하고 가르치기까지 할 희극을 받아들일 만큼 무르익을 때까지 — 바로잡고 길러 내려 했다."],["for instance--and comedies that would not only amuse but improve and instruct.","이 모든 것을 그는, 한 번이라도 들어줄 사람을 얻기만 한다면, 해내려 했다."],["All this he was to do, could he once get a hearing: there was the initial difficulty.","거기에 첫 번째 난관이 있었다."]],[["He shows plainly enough, too, that “Don Quixote” and the demolition of the chivalry romances was not the work that lay next his heart.","그는 또한, 「돈키호테」와 기사도 소설의 타파가 그의 가슴에 가장 가까이 자리한 일거리가 아니었음을 충분히 분명하게 드러내 보인다."],["He was, indeed, as he says himself in his preface, more a stepfather than a father to “Don Quixote.”","사실 그는 자신의 서문에서 스스로 말하듯, 「돈키호테」에게 아버지라기보다 의붓아버지에 더 가까웠다."],["Never was great work so neglected by its author.","일찍이 어떤 위대한 작품도 그 저자에게 그토록 소홀히 다루어진 적이 없었다."],["That it was written carelessly, hastily, and by fits and starts, was not always his fault, but it seems clear he never read what he sent to the press.","그것이 부주의하게, 서둘러, 띄엄띄엄 쓰였다는 것이 늘 그의 잘못만은 아니었으나, 그가 인쇄소에 보낸 것을 한 번도 읽어 보지 않았음은 분명해 보인다."],["He knew how the printers had blundered, but he never took the trouble to correct them when the third edition was in progress, as a man who really cared for the child of his brain would have done.","그는 인쇄공들이 어떻게 일을 그르쳤는지 알고 있었으나, 세 번째 판이 진행 중일 때조차, 자기 머리에서 나온 자식을 진정으로 아끼는 사람이라면 했을 법한, 그것들을 바로잡는 수고를 결코 기울이지 않았다."],["He appears to have regarded the book as little more than a mere libro de entretenimiento, an amusing book, a thing, as he says in the “Viaje,”","그는 이 책을 한낱 \"엔트레테니미엔토의 책\", 곧 재미난 책, 「여행기」에서 그가 말하듯 \"어느 때 어느 철에든 우울하고 침울한 마음을 달래 주는\""],["“to divert the melancholy moody heart at any time or season.”","것 정도로밖에 여기지 않은 듯하다."],["No doubt he had an affection for his hero, and was very proud of Sancho Panza.","분명 그는 자신의 주인공에게 애정을 품었고 산초 판사를 매우 자랑스러워했다."],["It would have been strange indeed if he had not been proud of the most humorous creation in all fiction.","모든 소설을 통틀어 가장 해학적인 창조물을 자랑스러워하지 않았다면 그것이야말로 참으로 이상한 일이었으리라."],["He was proud, too, of the popularity and success of the book, and beyond measure delightful is the naivete with which he shows his pride in a dozen passages in the Second Part. But it was not the success he coveted.","그는 또한 그 책의 인기와 성공도 자랑스러워했으니, 제2부의 열두어 군데 대목에서 그가 그 자부심을 내보이는 그 천진함은 더없이 흐뭇하다."],["In all probability he would have given all the success of “Don Quixote,”","그러나 그것은 그가 갈망하던 성공이 아니었다."],["nay, would have seen every copy of “Don Quixote” burned in the Plaza Mayor, for one such success as Lope de Vega was enjoying on an average once a week.","십중팔구 그는, 로페 데 베가가 평균 일주일에 한 번꼴로 누리던 그런 성공 한 번을 위해서라면, 「돈키호테」의 모든 성공을 다 내주었을 것이며, 아니 「돈키호테」의 모든 부수가 마요르 광장에서 불타는 것을 보았을 것이다."]],[["And so he went on, dawdling over “Don Quixote,”","그리하여 그는 「돈키호테」를 가지고 꾸물거리며, 이따금 한 장(章)씩 보태고는 그것을 제쳐 두고서 「페르실레스와 시히스문다」"],["adding a chapter now and again, and putting it aside to turn to “Persiles and Sigismunda”--which, as we know, was to be the most entertaining book in the language, and the rival of “Theagenes and Chariclea”--or finishing off one of his darling comedies; and if Robles asked when “Don Quixote”","— 우리가 알다시피 그 언어로 쓰인 가장 재미난 책이자 「테아게네스와 카리클레아」의 맞수가 될 책 — 로 눈을 돌리거나, 자신이 끔찍이 아끼는 희극 가운데 하나를 마무리 짓곤 했다. 그리고 로블레스가 「돈키호테」가 언제 준비되겠느냐고 물으면, 그 대답은 분명 이러했을 것이다."],["would be ready, the answer no doubt was: En breve-shortly, there was time enough for that.","엔 브레베, 곧 머지않아서요, 그건 시간이 넉넉하니까요."],["At sixty-eight he was as full of life and hope and plans for the future as a","예순여덟의 나이에도 그는 마치……처럼 생기와 희망과 앞날의 계획으로 가득 차 있었다."]],[["boy of eighteen. Nemesis was coming, however.","네메시스는 다가오고 있었다."],["He had got as far as Chapter LIX, which at his leisurely pace he could hardly have reached before October or November 1614, when there was put into his hand a small octave lately printed at Tarragona, and calling itself “Second Volume of the Ingenious Gentleman Don Quixote of La Mancha: by the Licentiate Alonso Fernandez de Avellaneda of Tordesillas.”","그는 제59장까지 써 내려갔는데, 그의 그 느긋한 속도로 보아 1614년 10월이나 11월 이전에는 도저히 거기에 이르지 못했을 터인데, 그때 그의 손에 작은 8절판 한 권이 쥐어졌으니, 최근 타라고나에서 인쇄된 것으로 스스로를 「기발한 신사 라만차의 돈키호테 제2권: 토르데시야스의 학사 알론소 페르난데스 데 아베야네다 지음」이라 일컫는 책이었다."],["The last half of Chapter LIX and most of the following chapters of the Second Part give us some idea of the effect produced upon him, and his irritation was not likely to be lessened by the reflection that he had no one to blame but himself.","제59장의 후반부와 제2부의 이어지는 여러 장은 이 책이 그에게 어떤 영향을 끼쳤는지를 어느 정도 짐작하게 해 주는데, 자기 자신 말고는 탓할 사람이 아무도 없다는 생각이 그의 분노를 누그러뜨려 주었을 리는 없다."],["Had Avellaneda, in fact, been content with merely bringing out a continuation to “Don Quixote,” Cervantes would have had no reasonable grievance.","사실 만일 아베야네다가 단지 「돈키호테」의 속편을 펴내는 것에 만족했더라면, 세르반테스로서는 정당하게 불평할 거리가 없었을 것이다."],["His own intentions were expressed in the very vaguest language at the end of the book; nay, in his last words, “forse altro cantera con miglior plettro,” he seems actually to invite some one else to continue the work, and he made no sign until eight years and a half had gone by; by which time Avellaneda’s volume was no doubt written.","그 자신의 의도는 책 끄트머리에서 더없이 모호한 언어로 표현되어 있었으니, 아니 그가 마지막에 남긴 말 \"어쩌면 다른 이가 더 나은 가락으로 노래하리라(forse altro cantera con miglior plettro)\"를 보면, 그는 실로 누군가 다른 이에게 이 작업을 이어 가라고 청하는 듯하며, 게다가 그는 팔 년 반이라는 세월이 흐르도록 아무런 기척도 보이지 않았으니, 그 무렵이면 아베야네다의 책은 의심할 바 없이 이미 쓰여 있었을 것이다."]],[["In fact Cervantes had no case, or a very bad one, as far as the mere continuation was concerned.","* forse altro cantera con miglior plettro: 이탈리아어로 \"어쩌면 다른 이가 더 나은 가락으로 노래하리라\"라는 뜻으로, 아리오스토의 「광란의 오를란도」에 나오는 구절이다."],["But Avellaneda chose to write a preface to it, full of such coarse personal abuse as only an ill-conditioned man could pour out.","사실 단순히 속편이라는 점에 관한 한 세르반테스에게는 따질 명분이 없었거나, 있다 해도 매우 빈약한 것이었다."],["He taunts Cervantes with being old, with having lost his hand, with having been in prison, with being poor, with being friendless, accuses him of envy of Lope’s success, of petulance and querulousness, and so on; and it was in this that the sting lay.","그러나 아베야네다는 거기에 서문을 쓰기로 작정했으니, 그것은 오직 심성이 비뚤어진 자만이 쏟아 낼 수 있을 법한 그런 거칠고 인신공격적인 욕설로 가득 차 있었다. 그는 세르반테스를 두고 늙었다느니, 한 손을 잃었다느니, 감옥살이를 했다느니, 가난하다느니, 벗이 없다느니 하며 조롱하고, 로페의 성공을 시기한다느니, 성마르고 불평이 많다느니 하며 비난하는데, 바로 여기에 그 가시가 박혀 있었다."],["Avellaneda’s reason for this personal attack is obvious enough.","아베야네다가 이런 인신공격을 한 까닭은 충분히 분명하다."],["Whoever he may have been, it is clear that he was one of the dramatists of Lope’s school, for he has the impudence to charge Cervantes with attacking him as well as Lope in his criticism on the drama.","그가 누구였든 간에, 그가 로페 학파의 극작가 가운데 하나였음은 명백하니, 그는 뻔뻔스럽게도 세르반테스가 연극에 관한 비평에서 로페뿐 아니라 자신까지 공격했다고 몰아세우고 있기 때문이다."],["His identification has exercised the best critics and baffled all the ingenuity and research that has been brought to bear on it.","그의 정체를 밝히는 일은 가장 뛰어난 비평가들조차 골머리를 앓게 했고, 이에 동원된 온갖 재간과 연구를 다 좌절시켰다."],["Navarrete and Ticknor both incline to the belief that Cervantes knew who he was; but I must say I think the anger he shows suggests an invisible assailant; it is like the irritation of a man stung by a mosquito in the dark.","나바레테와 티크너는 둘 다 세르반테스가 그가 누구인지 알고 있었다는 쪽으로 기우나, 나로서는 그가 드러내는 분노가 보이지 않는 가해자를 시사한다고 말할 수밖에 없다. 그것은 어둠 속에서 모기에게 쏘인 사람의 짜증과도 같다."],["Cervantes from certain solecisms of language pronounces him to be an Aragonese, and Pellicer, an Aragonese himself, supports this view and believes him, moreover, to have been an ecclesiastic, a Dominican probably.","세르반테스는 몇몇 어법상의 잘못을 근거로 그를 아라곤 사람이라 단정하며, 자신도 아라곤 사람인 펠리세르는 이 견해를 지지하고 나아가 그가 성직자, 아마도 도미니쿠스회 수도사였으리라고 믿는다."]],[["Any merit Avellaneda has is reflected from Cervantes, and he is too dull to reflect much.","아베야네다에게 어떤 장점이 있든 그것은 세르반테스에게서 반사된 것이며, 그는 그나마도 많이 반사하기에는 너무 둔하다. \"둔하고 추잡하다\"는 것이 편견 없는 독자 대다수의 평결이 되리라고 나는 생각한다."],["“Dull and dirty” will always be, I imagine, the verdict of the vast majority of unprejudiced readers.","그는 기껏해야 형편없는 표절꾼이니, 그가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 세르반테스가 그에게 내준 길을 노예처럼 따라가는 것뿐이다."],["He is, at best, a poor plagiarist; all he can do is to follow slavishly the lead given him by Cervantes; his only humour lies in making Don Quixote take inns for castles and fancy himself some legendary or historical personage, and Sancho mistake words, invert proverbs, and display his gluttony; all through he shows a proclivity to coarseness and dirt, and he has contrived to introduce two tales filthier than anything by the sixteenth century novellieri and without their sprightliness.","그의 유일한 해학이라고는 돈키호테로 하여금 여관을 성으로 알게 하고 자신을 어떤 전설적 혹은 역사적 인물로 착각하게 만들며, 산초로 하여금 말을 잘못 알아듣고 속담을 거꾸로 뒤집고 자기 식탐을 드러내게 하는 데 있을 뿐이다. 그는 처음부터 끝까지 천박함과 추잡함으로 기우는 성벽을 보이며, 16세기 이탈리아 단편 작가들의 그 어떤 것보다 더 더러우면서도 그들의 발랄함이라고는 없는 이야기 두 편을 끼워 넣는 솜씨를 부렸다."]],[["But whatever Avellaneda and his book may be, we must not forget the debt we owe them.","* 이탈리아 단편 작가들(novellieri): 보카치오의 「데카메론」"],["But for them, there can be no doubt, “Don Quixote”","전통을 잇는 16세기 이탈리아의 단편 소설 작가들을 가리킨다."],["would have come to us a mere torso instead of a complete work.","그러나 아베야네다와 그의 책이 어떠하든, 우리가 그들에게 진 빚을 잊어서는 안 된다."],["Even if Cervantes had finished the volume he had in hand, most assuredly he would have left off with a promise of a Third Part, giving the further adventures of Don Quixote and humours of Sancho Panza as shepherds.","그들이 없었더라면 「돈키호테」가 완성된 작품이 아니라 한낱 토르소로 우리에게 전해졌으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 설령 세르반테스가 손에 쥐고 있던 그 책을 끝마쳤다 하더라도, 그는 거의 틀림없이 돈키호테의 더 많은 모험과 양치기가 된 산초 판사의 익살을 담은 제3부에 대한 약속을 남기며 붓을 놓았을 것이다."],["It is plain that he had at one time an intention of dealing with the pastoral romances as he had dealt with the books of chivalry, and but for Avellaneda he would have tried to carry it out.","그가 한때 기사도 책을 다루었던 것처럼 목가 소설을 다루려는 의도를 품었던 것은 분명하며, 아베야네다가 없었더라면 그는 그것을 실행에 옮기려 애썼을 것이다."],["But it is more likely that, with his plans, and projects, and hopefulness, the volume would have remained unfinished till his death, and that we should have never made the acquaintance of the Duke and Duchess, or gone with Sancho to Barataria.","그러나 그의 계획과 구상과 낙천적 기질로 미루어 보건대, 그 책은 그가 죽을 때까지 미완으로 남았을 가능성이 더 크며, 그리하여 우리는 공작 부부와 알게 되지도 못했을 것이고, 산초와 함께 바라타리아로 가 보지도 못했을 것이다."]],[["From the moment the book came into his hands he seems to have been haunted by the fear that there might be more Avellanedas in the field, and putting everything else aside, he set himself to finish off his task and protect Don Quixote in the only way he could, by killing him.","그 책이 자기 손에 들어온 순간부터 그는 들판에 아베야네다가 더 있을지도 모른다는 두려움에 사로잡혔던 듯하며, 다른 모든 것을 제쳐 두고 자기 과업을 마무리하고 자신이 할 수 있는 유일한 방법으로, 곧 돈키호테를 죽임으로써 그를 지키는 일에 착수했다."],["The conclusion is no doubt a hasty and in some places clumsy piece of work and the frequent repetition of the scolding administered to Avellaneda becomes in the end rather wearisome; but it is, at any rate, a conclusion and for that we must thank Avellaneda.","그 결말이 서둘러 마무리되고 군데군데 서투른 작업임은 의심할 바 없으며, 아베야네다에게 퍼붓는 꾸지람이 거듭 되풀이되는 것은 결국 다소 진력나는 일이 되고 만다. 그러나 그것은 어쨌든 하나의 결말이며, 그 점에 대해서는 우리가 아베야네다에게 고마워해야 한다."]],[["The new volume was ready for the press in February, but was not printed till the very end of 1615, and during the interval Cervantes put together the comedies and interludes he had written within the last few years, and, as he adds plaintively, found no demand for among the managers, and published them with a preface, worth the book it introduces tenfold, in which he gives an account of the early Spanish stage, and of his own attempts as a dramatist.","새 책은 2월에 인쇄에 부칠 준비가 되었으나 1615년이 거의 다 저물어서야 인쇄되었고, 그 사이 세르반테스는 지난 몇 해 동안 써 두었던, 그리고 그가 구슬프게 덧붙이듯 극단 지배인들 사이에서 아무런 수요도 찾지 못했던 희극과 막간극들을 한데 모아, 그것이 소개하는 책보다 열 배는 값진 서문을 붙여 펴냈으니, 그 서문에서 그는 초기 스페인 무대의 사정과 극작가로서 자신이 시도했던 일들에 관해 이야기한다."],["It is needless to say they were put forward by Cervantes in all good faith and full confidence in their merits.","세르반테스가 그것들을 그 가치에 대한 온전한 신의와 충만한 확신을 품고 내놓았음은 말할 필요도 없다."],["The reader, however, was not to suppose they were his last word or final effort in the drama, for he had in hand a comedy called “Engano a los ojos,”","그러나 독자는 그것이 연극에 관한 그의 마지막 말이나 최종적인 노력이라고 여겨서는 안 되었으니, 그는 「눈속임」이라는 희극을 손에 쥐고 있었고, 그가 잘못 안 것이 아니라면 그것에 관해서는 아무런 이론도 없으리라는 것이었다."],["about which, if he mistook not, there would be no question.","* 눈속임(Engano a los ojos): \"눈을 속임\"이라는 뜻의 스페인어로, 세르반테스가 언급한 이 희극은 전해지지 않는다."]],[["Of this dramatic masterpiece the world has no opportunity of judging; his health had been failing for some time, and he died, apparently of dropsy, on the 23rd of April, 1616, the day on which England lost Shakespeare, nominally at least, for the English calendar had not yet been reformed.","세상은 이 극적 걸작을 판단할 기회를 갖지 못했으니, 그의 건강은 한동안 쇠해 가고 있었고, 그는 1616년 4월 23일 명백히 수종으로 세상을 떠났으니, 적어도 명목상으로는 잉글랜드가 셰익스피어를 잃은 바로 그날이었다. 잉글랜드의 달력은 아직 개정되지 않았던 것이다."],["He died as he had lived, accepting his lot bravely and cheerfully.","그는 살아온 그대로 죽었으니, 자기 운명을 용감하고 명랑하게 받아들이며 떠났다."]],[["Was it an unhappy life, that of Cervantes? His biographers all tell us that it was; but I must say I doubt it.","* 잉글랜드 달력 개정: 당시 잉글랜드는 율리우스력을, 스페인은 그레고리력을 사용하여 날짜 표기에 차이가 있었으므로, 같은 날짜라 하나 실제로는 다른 날이었다."],["It was a hard life, a life of poverty, of incessant struggle, of toil ill paid, of disappointment, but Cervantes carried within himself the antidote to all these evils.","세르반테스의 삶은 불행한 것이었던가? 그의 전기 작가들은 한결같이 그러했노라 말하나, 나로서는 그것이 의심스럽다고 말할 수밖에 없다."],["His was not one of those light natures that rise above adversity merely by virtue of their own buoyancy; it was in the fortitude of a high spirit that he was proof against it.","그것은 고된 삶, 가난의 삶, 끊임없는 투쟁의 삶, 보수가 박한 노고의 삶, 실망의 삶이었으나, 세르반테스는 이 모든 해악에 맞설 해독제를 제 안에 지니고 있었다. 그의 천성은 오로지 제 부력만으로 역경 위로 떠오르는 그런 가벼운 천성이 아니었다."],["It is impossible to conceive Cervantes giving way to despondency or prostrated by dejection.","그가 역경을 견뎌 낼 수 있었던 것은 고매한 정신의 굳건함에 의해서였다."],["As for poverty, it was with him a thing to be laughed over, and the only sigh he ever allows to escape him is when he says, “Happy he to whom Heaven has given a piece of bread for which he is not bound to give thanks to any but Heaven itself.”","세르반테스가 낙담에 굴복하거나 의기소침함에 짓눌리는 모습이란 도무지 상상할 수 없다. 가난으로 말하자면, 그에게 그것은 웃어넘길 거리였으니, 그가 스스로에게 허락한 단 한 번의 한숨은 이런 말을 할 때뿐이었다."],["Add to all this his vital energy and mental activity, his restless invention and his sanguine temperament, and there will be reason enough to doubt whether his could have been a very unhappy life.","\"하늘 말고는 누구에게도 감사할 필요 없는 빵 한 조각을 하늘로부터 받은 자는 복되도다.\" 여기에 그의 왕성한 활력과 정신적 활동, 그의 쉼 없는 창의와 낙천적 기질을 더하면, 그의 삶이 과연 매우 불행한 것이었을까를 의심할 만한 충분한 이유가 될 것이다."],["He who could take Cervantes’ distresses together with his apparatus for enduring them would not make so bad a bargain, perhaps, as far as happiness in life is concerned.","세르반테스의 고난을, 그것을 견디게 해 준 장치와 함께 통째로 떠안을 수 있는 사람이라면, 삶의 행복에 관한 한 그리 나쁜 거래를 하는 것은 아닐지도 모른다."]],[["Of his burial-place nothing is known except that he was buried, in accordance with his will, in the neighbouring convent of Trinitarian nuns, of which it is supposed his daughter, Isabel de Saavedra, was an inmate, and that a few years afterwards the nuns removed to another convent, carrying their dead with them.","그가 묻힌 곳에 관해서는, 그가 자신의 유언에 따라 이웃한 삼위일체회 수녀원에 묻혔다는 것 — 그곳에는 그의 딸 이사벨 데 사아베드라가 수녀로 있었으리라 짐작된다 — 과, 몇 해 뒤 수녀들이 다른 수녀원으로 옮겨 가면서 자기들의 죽은 이들을 함께 모셔 갔다는 것 외에는 알려진 바가 없다."],["But whether the remains of Cervantes were included in the removal or not no one knows, and the clue to their resting-place is now lost beyond all hope.","그러나 세르반테스의 유해가 그 이장에 포함되었는지 아닌지는 아무도 모르며, 그가 잠든 곳을 찾을 실마리는 이제 모든 희망을 넘어 잃어버리고 말았다."],["This furnishes perhaps the least defensible of the items in the charge of neglect brought against his contemporaries.","이는 그의 동시대인들에게 가해진 무관심이라는 비난의 항목들 가운데 아마도 가장 변호하기 어려운 대목일 것이다."],["In some of the others there is a good deal of exaggeration.","다른 몇몇 항목에는 상당한 과장이 섞여 있다."],["To listen to most of his biographers one would suppose that all Spain was in league not only against the man but against his memory, or at least that it was insensible to his merits, and left him to live in misery and die of want.","그의 전기 작가 대부분의 말을 듣고 있노라면, 온 스페인이 그 사람뿐 아니라 그에 대한 기억에까지 맞서 결탁이라도 했거나, 적어도 그의 가치에 무감각하여 그를 비참하게 살다가 궁핍 속에 죽게 내버려 두었다고 여기게 될 것이다."],["To talk of his hard life and unworthy employments in Andalusia is absurd.","안달루시아에서의 그의 고된 삶과 보잘것없는 일자리를 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하는 것은 터무니없다."],["What had he done to distinguish him from thousands of other struggling men earning a precarious livelihood?","불안정한 생계를 꾸려 가던 다른 수천 명의 분투하는 사람들과 그를 구별 지을 만한 무슨 일을 그가 했단 말인가?"],["True, he was a gallant soldier, who had been wounded and had undergone captivity and suffering in his country’s cause, but there were hundreds of others in the same case.","사실 그는 부상을 입고 조국의 대의를 위해 포로살이와 고난을 겪은 용맹한 군인이었으나, 똑같은 처지에 놓인 사람이 수백 명이나 있었다."],["He had written a mediocre specimen of an insipid class of romance, and some plays which manifestly did not comply with the primary condition of pleasing: were the playgoers to patronise plays that did not amuse them, because the author was to produce “Don Quixote” twenty years afterwards?","그는 김빠진 부류의 소설의 평범한 표본 하나와, 즐거움을 준다는 기본 조건에 명백히 부합하지 못한 희곡 몇 편을 썼을 뿐이니, 작가가 이십 년 뒤에 「돈키호테」를 내놓을 사람이라 하여, 관객들이 자기들을 즐겁게 해 주지도 못하는 연극을 후원해야 했단 말인가?"]],[["The scramble for copies which, as we have seen, followed immediately on the appearance of the book, does not look like general insensibility to its merits.","우리가 본 바와 같이 책이 나오자마자 곧바로 뒤따른 그 사본 쟁탈전은 그 가치에 대한 전반적인 무감각처럼 보이지는 않는다."],["No doubt it was received coldly by some, but if a man writes a book in ridicule of periwigs he must make his account with being coldly received by the periwig wearers and hated by the whole tribe of wigmakers.","의심할 바 없이 그것을 냉담하게 맞은 이들도 더러 있었으나, 가발을 조롱하는 책을 쓰는 사람이라면 가발 쓴 자들에게 냉대받고 가발 만드는 무리 전체에게 미움받을 각오를 해야 마땅하다."],["If Cervantes had the chivalry-romance readers, the sentimentalists, the dramatists, and the poets of the period all against him, it was because “Don Quixote”","세르반테스가 기사도 소설 독자, 감상주의자, 극작가, 그리고 그 시대의 시인들을 모조리 적으로 두었다면, 그것은 「돈키호테」가 바로 그런 책이었기 때문이다."],["was what it was; and if the general public did not come forward to make him comfortable for the rest of his days, it is no more to be charged with neglect and ingratitude than the English-speaking public that did not pay off Scott’s liabilities.","그리고 만일 일반 대중이 그의 여생을 편안하게 해 주려 나서지 않았다 하더라도, 그것은 스콧의 빚을 갚아 주지 않은 영어권 대중에게 무관심과 배은망덕의 죄를 물을 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로, 그들에게 그런 죄를 물을 일이 아니다."],["It did the best it could; it read his book and liked it and bought it, and encouraged the bookseller to pay him well for others.","그들은 할 수 있는 최선을 다했으니, 그의 책을 읽고 좋아하고 사들였으며, 서적상으로 하여금 다른 작품들에 대해 그에게 후한 값을 치르도록 부추겼다."]],[["It has been also made a reproach to Spain that she has erected no monument to the man she is proudest of; no monument, that is to say, of him; for the bronze statue in the little garden of the Plaza de las Cortes, a fair work of art no doubt, and unexceptionable had it been set up to the local poet in the market-place of some provincial town, is not worthy of Cervantes or of Madrid.","또한 스페인이 가장 자랑스러워하는 사람을 위해 아무런 기념비도 세우지 않았다는 것이 그 나라에 대한 비난거리가 되어 왔다. 다시 말해 그를 기리는 기념비가 없다는 것이니, 코르테스 광장의 작은 정원에 있는 청동상은 의심할 바 없이 제법 훌륭한 예술 작품이며 어느 지방 소도시의 장터에 그 고장 시인을 기려 세웠더라면 나무랄 데 없었겠지만, 세르반테스나 마드리드에 어울리는 것은 못 된다."],["But what need has Cervantes of “such weak witness of his name;” or what could a monument do in his case except testify to the self-glorification of those who had put it up?","그러나 세르반테스에게 \"그의 이름에 대한 그런 미약한 증언\"이 무슨 소용이 있겠으며, 그의 경우에 기념비가 그것을 세운 자들의 자기 과시를 증언하는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가?"],["Si monumentum quoeris, circumspice.","기념비를 찾고자 하거든 둘러보라(Si monumentum quoeris, circumspice)."],["The nearest bookseller’s shop will show what bathos there would be in a monument to the author of “Don Quixote.”","가장 가까운 서점이 「돈키호테」의 작가에게 바치는 기념비가 얼마나 용두사미가 될지를 보여 줄 것이다."]],[["Nine editions of the First Part of “Don Quixote” had already appeared before Cervantes died, thirty thousand copies in all, according to his own estimate, and a tenth was printed at Barcelona the year after his death.","* Si monumentum quoeris, circumspice: 라틴어로 \"기념비를 찾고자 하거든 둘러보라\"라는 뜻으로, 런던 세인트폴 대성당의 건축가 크리스토퍼 렌의 묘비명이다."],["So large a number naturally supplied the demand for some time, but by 1634 it appears to have been exhausted; and from that time down to the present day the stream of editions has continued to flow rapidly and regularly.","세르반테스가 죽기 전에 이미 「돈키호테」 제1부는 아홉 판을 거듭했으니, 그 자신의 추산으로는 모두 삼만 부에 달했고, 그가 죽은 이듬해에는 바르셀로나에서 열 번째 판이 인쇄되었다."],["The translations show still more clearly in what request the book has been from the very outset.","이만큼 많은 부수가 한동안은 자연히 수요를 채워 주었으나, 1634년 무렵에는 동난 듯하다."],["In seven years from the completion of the work it had been translated into the four leading languages of Europe.","그리고 그때부터 오늘날에 이르기까지 판본의 물줄기는 빠르고 꾸준하게 흘러왔다."],["Except the Bible, in fact, no book has been so widely diffused as “Don Quixote.” The “Imitatio Christi”","번역본들은 이 책이 맨 처음부터 얼마나 큰 수요를 누렸는지를 한층 더 분명히 보여 준다."],["may have been translated into as many different languages, and perhaps “Robinson Crusoe”","작품이 완성된 지 칠 년 만에 그것은 유럽의 주요 네 언어로 번역되었다."],["and the “Vicar of Wakefield”","사실 성경을 제외하면 「돈키호테」만큼 널리 퍼진 책은 없다."],["into nearly as many, but in multiplicity of translations and editions “Don Quixote” leaves them all far behind.","「그리스도를 본받아」도 그만큼 여러 언어로 번역되었을지 모르고, 어쩌면 「로빈슨 크루소」와 「웨이크필드의 목사」도 거의 그만큼 많은 언어로 번역되었을지 모르나, 번역과 판본의 다양함에서 「돈키호테」는 그것들을 모두 멀찍이 따돌린다."]],[["Still more remarkable is the character of this wide diffusion.","이 폭넓은 보급의 성격은 한층 더 주목할 만하다."],["“Don Quixote” has been thoroughly naturalised among people whose ideas about knight-errantry, if they had any at all, were of the vaguest, who had never seen or heard of a book of chivalry, who could not possibly feel the humour of the burlesque or sympathise with the author’s purpose.","「돈키호테」는 편력 기사도에 관한 관념이라고는 — 설령 조금이라도 있었다 한들 — 더없이 흐릿했던 사람들, 기사도 책이라고는 한 번도 보거나 들어 본 적이 없었던 사람들, 그 풍자극의 해학을 도무지 느낄 수도 작가의 의도에 공감할 수도 없었던 사람들 사이에서도 완전히 토착화되었다."],["Another curious fact is that this, the most cosmopolitan book in the world, is one of the most intensely national. “Manon Lescaut”","또 한 가지 묘한 사실은, 세상에서 가장 세계주의적인 이 책이 동시에 가장 강렬하게 민족적인 책 가운데 하나라는 점이다."],["is not more thoroughly French, “Tom Jones” not more English, “Rob Roy”","「마농 레스코」가 그보다 더 철저히 프랑스적이지 않고, 「톰 존스」가 그보다 더 영국적이지 않으며, 「롭 로이」가 그보다 더 스코틀랜드적이지 않으니, 「돈키호테」는 성격에서나 사상에서나 정서에서나 향토색에서나 그 모든 것에서 스페인적이다."],["not more Scotch, than “Don Quixote” is Spanish, in character, in ideas, in sentiment, in local colour, in everything.","그렇다면 거의 삼 세기에 걸쳐 해마다 더해 온 이 유례없는 인기의 비밀은 무엇인가?"],["What, then, is the secret of this unparalleled popularity, increasing year by year for well-nigh three centuries?","한 가지 설명은 의심할 바 없이, 세상의 모든 책 가운데 「돈키호테」가 가장 보편적인 책이라는 것이다."],["One explanation, no doubt, is that of all the books in the world, “Don Quixote” is the most catholic.","그 안에는 젊든 늙든, 현명하든 단순하든, 신분이 높든 낮든, 온갖 부류의 독자를 위한 무언가가 들어 있다."],["There is something in it for every sort of reader, young or old, sage or simple, high or low. As Cervantes himself says with a touch of pride, “It is thumbed and read and got by heart by people of all sorts; the children turn its leaves, the young people read it, the grown men understand it, the old folk praise it.”","세르반테스 자신이 약간의 자부심을 담아 말하듯, \"그것은 온갖 부류의 사람들이 손때 묻혀 가며 읽고 외우니, 아이들은 그 책장을 넘기고, 젊은이들은 그것을 읽고, 어른들은 그것을 이해하고, 노인들은 그것을 칭송한다.\""]],[["But it would be idle to deny that the ingredient which, more than its humour, or its wisdom, or the fertility of invention or knowledge of human nature it displays, has insured its success with the multitude, is the vein of farce that runs through it.","그러나 그 해학이나 지혜, 혹은 그것이 보여 주는 창의의 풍부함이나 인간 본성에 대한 통찰보다도, 대중에게서 그 성공을 확실히 보장해 준 요소가 그 책 전체에 흐르는 익살의 맥이라는 점을 부인한다면 그것은 부질없는 일이리라."],["It was the attack upon the sheep, the battle with the wine-skins, Mambrino’s helmet, the balsam of Fierabras, Don Quixote knocked over by the sails of the windmill, Sancho tossed in the blanket, the mishaps and misadventures of master and man, that were originally the great attraction, and perhaps are so still to some extent with the majority of readers.","처음에 큰 매력이었고 어쩌면 지금도 어느 정도 독자 대다수에게 그러한 것은, 양 떼를 향한 공격, 포도주 부대와의 싸움, 맘브리노의 투구, 피에라브라스의 영약, 풍차 날개에 나가떨어지는 돈키호테, 담요에 키질당하는 산초, 그리고 주인과 종이 겪는 봉변과 재난들이었다."],["It is plain that “Don Quixote” was generally regarded at first, and indeed in Spain for a long time, as little more than a queer droll book, full of laughable incidents and absurd situations, very amusing, but not entitled to much consideration or care.","「돈키호테」는 처음에, 그리고 실로 스페인에서는 오랫동안, 대체로 우스운 사건과 터무니없는 상황으로 가득한, 매우 재미나기는 하나 그다지 깊은 고려나 정성을 들일 가치는 없는 한낱 기이하고 익살스러운 책쯤으로 여겨졌음이 분명하다."],["All the editions printed in Spain from 1637 to 1771, when the famous printer Ibarra took it up, were mere trade editions, badly and carelessly printed on vile paper and got up in the style of chap-books intended only for popular use, with, in most instances, uncouth illustrations and clap-trap additions by the publisher.","1637년부터, 저 유명한 인쇄업자 이바라가 손을 댄 1771년에 이르기까지 스페인에서 인쇄된 모든 판본은 한낱 장삿속 판본이었으니, 형편없는 종이에 조잡하고 부주의하게 인쇄되고, 오로지 대중적 용도만을 위한 싸구려 이야기책의 모양새로 꾸며졌으며, 대개의 경우 거친 삽화와 출판업자가 덧붙인 눈요깃거리들이 들어 있었다."]],[["To England belongs the credit of having been the first country to recognise the right of “Don Quixote” to better treatment than this.","이러한 것보다 더 나은 대접을 받을 「돈키호테」의 권리를 처음으로 인정한 나라가 잉글랜드라는 공로는 그 나라에 돌아간다."],["The London edition of 1738, commonly called Lord Carteret’s from having been suggested by him, was not a mere edition de luxe. It produced “Don Quixote”","흔히 카터릿 경의 제안으로 이루어졌다 하여 그의 이름으로 불리는 1738년 런던판은 한낱 호화판이 아니었다."],["in becoming form as regards paper and type, and embellished with plates which, if not particularly happy as illustrations, were at least well intentioned and well executed, but it also aimed at correctness of text, a matter to which nobody except the editors of the Valencia and Brussels editions had given even a passing thought; and for a first attempt it was fairly successful, for though some of its emendations are inadmissible, a good many of them have been adopted by all subsequent editors.","그것은 종이와 활자 면에서 「돈키호테」를 걸맞은 모양새로 펴내고, 삽화로서 특별히 잘된 것은 아니라 해도 적어도 의도가 좋고 솜씨 있게 제작된 도판들로 꾸몄을 뿐 아니라, 본문의 정확성을 목표로 삼았으니, 이는 발렌시아판과 브뤼셀판의 편집자들 말고는 아무도 스쳐 가는 생각조차 해 본 적 없는 문제였다. 그리고 첫 시도치고는 제법 성공적이었으니, 그 교정 가운데 몇몇은 받아들일 수 없는 것이지만 상당수는 이후의 모든 편집자에게 채택되었기 때문이다."]],[["The zeal of publishers, editors, and annotators brought about a remarkable change of sentiment with regard to “Don Quixote.”","출판업자와 편집자와 주석가들의 열의는 「돈키호테」에 관한 정서에 주목할 만한 변화를 불러왔다."],["A vast number of its admirers began to grow ashamed of laughing over it.","그것을 흠모하는 수많은 이들이 그것을 두고 웃는 일을 부끄러워하기 시작했다."],["It became almost a crime to treat it as a humorous book.","그것을 해학적인 책으로 다루는 것이 거의 죄처럼 되어 버렸다."],["The humour was not entirely denied, but, according to the new view, it was rated as an altogether secondary quality, a mere accessory, nothing more than the stalking-horse under the presentation of which Cervantes shot his philosophy or his satire, or whatever it was he meant to shoot; for on this point opinions varied.","그 해학이 전적으로 부정된 것은 아니나, 새로운 견해에 따르면 그것은 어디까지나 부차적인 자질, 한낱 부속물, 세르반테스가 자신의 철학이나 풍자, 혹은 그가 쏘아 맞히려 했던 것이 무엇이든 그것을 쏘기 위해 내세운 위장용 말에 지나지 않는 것으로 평가되었다. 이 점에 관해서는 의견이 갈렸기 때문이다."],["All were agreed, however, that the object he aimed at was not the books of chivalry.","그러나 그가 겨냥한 목표가 기사도 책이 아니었다는 데에는 모두가 의견을 같이했다."],["He said emphatically in the preface to the First Part and in the last sentence of the Second, that he had no other object in view than to discredit these books, and this, to advanced criticism, made it clear that his object must have been something else.","그는 제1부 서문에서, 그리고 제2부의 마지막 문장에서, 자신에게는 이런 책들의 신용을 떨어뜨리는 것 말고는 다른 목적이 없었노라고 힘주어 말했는데, 진보한 비평에는 이것이 도리어 그의 목적이 무언가 다른 것이었음에 틀림없다는 점을 분명히 해 주었다."]],[["One theory was that the book was a kind of allegory, setting forth the eternal struggle between the ideal and the real, between the spirit of poetry and the spirit of prose; and perhaps German philosophy never evolved a more ungainly or unlikely camel out of the depths of its inner consciousness.","한 가지 학설은 이 책이 일종의 우의(寓意)로서, 이상과 현실 사이, 시의 정신과 산문의 정신 사이의 영원한 투쟁을 그려 낸다는 것이었다. 그리고 어쩌면 독일 철학은 제 내적 의식의 심연에서 이보다 더 볼품없고 더 어울리지 않는 낙타를 끌어낸 적이 없을 것이다."],["Something of the antagonism, no doubt, is to be found in “Don Quixote,” because it is to be found everywhere in life, and Cervantes drew from life.","의심할 바 없이 그러한 대립의 일면은 「돈키호테」에서 발견되니, 그것이 삶의 도처에서 발견되기 때문이며, 세르반테스는 삶에서 길어 올렸기 때문이다."],["It is difficult to imagine a community in which the never-ceasing game of cross-purposes between Sancho Panza and Don Quixote would not be recognized as true to nature.","산초 판사와 돈키호테 사이의 끊임없는 동상이몽의 유희가 자연 그대로의 참된 모습으로 인정받지 못할 공동체란 상상하기 어렵다."],["In the stone age, among the lake dwellers, among the cave men, there were Don Quixotes and Sancho Panzas; there must have been the troglodyte who never could see the facts before his eyes, and the troglodyte who could see nothing else.","석기 시대에도, 호상 가옥 거주자들 사이에서도, 동굴 인간들 사이에서도 돈키호테와 산초 판사는 있었다. 제 눈앞의 사실을 결코 보지 못하는 혈거인이 있었을 것이고, 그것 말고는 아무것도 보지 못하는 혈거인도 있었을 것이다."],["But to suppose Cervantes deliberately setting himself to expound any such idea in two stout quarto volumes is to suppose something not only very unlike the age in which he lived, but altogether unlike Cervantes himself, who would have been the first to laugh at an attempt of the sort made by anyone else.","그러나 세르반테스가 두 권의 두툼한 4절판으로 그런 관념을 풀어내고자 일부러 작정하고 나섰으리라 가정하는 것은, 그가 살았던 시대와 매우 동떨어졌을 뿐 아니라 세르반테스 자신과는 더더욱 동떨어진 무언가를 가정하는 일이니, 그라면 누군가 다른 이가 그런 시도를 했을 때 누구보다 먼저 웃어넘겼을 사람이다."]],[["The extraordinary influence of the romances of chivalry in his day is quite enough to account for the genesis of the book.","그의 시대에 기사도 소설이 끼친 그 비상한 영향만으로도 이 책의 발생을 설명하기에 충분하다."],["Some idea of the prodigious development of this branch of literature in the sixteenth century may be obtained from the scrutiny of Chapter VII, if the reader bears in mind that only a portion of the romances belonging to by far the largest group are enumerated.","16세기에 이 문학 갈래가 얼마나 엄청나게 발전했는지에 대한 어느 정도의 관념은 제7장의 검열 장면에서 얻을 수 있으니, 다만 독자는 단연 가장 큰 무리에 속하는 소설들 가운데 일부만이 열거되어 있음을 염두에 두어야 한다."],["As to its effect upon the nation, there is abundant evidence.","그것이 이 민족에게 끼친 영향에 관해서는 증거가 차고 넘친다."],["From the time when the Amadises and Palmerins began to grow popular down to the very end of the century, there is a steady stream of invective, from men whose character and position lend weight to their words, against the romances of chivalry and the infatuation of their readers.","아마디스류와 팔메린류가 인기를 얻기 시작한 때부터 그 세기가 거의 끝날 무렵까지, 그 성품과 지위가 그 말에 무게를 실어 주는 사람들에게서 기사도 소설과 그 독자들의 광적인 도취에 대한 비난이 끊임없이 쏟아졌다."],["Ridicule was the only besom to sweep away that dust.","그 먼지를 쓸어 낼 유일한 빗자루는 조롱이었다."]],[["That this was the task Cervantes set himself, and that he had ample provocation to urge him to it, will be sufficiently clear to those who look into the evidence; as it will be also that it was not chivalry itself that he attacked and swept away.","이것이 세르반테스가 스스로에게 부과한 과업이었으며, 그가 그 일에 나서도록 부추긴 충분한 자극이 있었음은 그 증거를 들여다보는 이들에게 충분히 분명해질 것이다. 또한 그가 공격하여 쓸어 낸 것이 기사도 자체가 아니었다는 점도 마찬가지로 분명해질 것이다."],["Of all the absurdities that, thanks to poetry, will be repeated to the end of time, there is no greater one than saying that “Cervantes smiled Spain’s chivalry away.”","시(詩) 덕분에 시간이 다할 때까지 되풀이될 온갖 터무니없는 말 가운데, \"세르반테스가 미소 한 번으로 스페인의 기사도를 사라지게 했다\"는 말보다 더 큰 것은 없다."],["In the first place there was no chivalry for him to smile away.","우선 그가 미소로 사라지게 할 기사도란 애초에 없었다."],["Spain’s chivalry had been dead for more than a century.","스페인의 기사도는 이미 한 세기 넘게 죽어 있었다."],["Its work was done when Granada fell, and as chivalry was essentially republican in its nature, it could not live under the rule that Ferdinand substituted for the free institutions of mediaeval Spain.","그라나다가 함락되었을 때 그 소임은 끝났으며, 기사도란 본질적으로 그 성격이 공화주의적인 것이었기에, 페르난도가 중세 스페인의 자유로운 제도를 대신해 세운 통치 아래서는 살아남을 수 없었다."],["What he did smile away was not chivalry but a degrading mockery of it.","그가 미소로 사라지게 한 것은 기사도가 아니라 그것을 욕되게 하는 조롱거리였다."]],[["The true nature of the “right arm” and the “bright array,”","시인의 말에 따르면 그 앞에서 \"온 세상이 물러섰다\"는, 그리고 세르반테스의 단 한 번의 웃음이 무너뜨렸다는 그 \"강한 팔\"과 \"빛나는 위용\"의 참모습은, 조지 칼턴 대위가 그의 「1672년부터 1713년까지의 군 회고록」에서 전하는, 세르반테스 자신의 동포인 돈 펠릭스 파체코의 말에서 헤아릴 수 있다."],["before which, according to the poet, “the world gave ground,” and which Cervantes’ single laugh demolished, may be gathered from the words of one of his own countrymen, Don Felix Pacheco, as reported by Captain George Carleton, in his “Military Memoirs from 1672 to 1713.”","그는 이렇게 말했다."],["“Before the appearance in the world of that labour of Cervantes,” he said, “it was next to an impossibility for a man to walk the streets with any delight or without danger.","\"세르반테스의 그 노작이 세상에 나오기 전에는, 사람이 거리를 조금이라도 즐겁게, 혹은 위험 없이 걷는다는 것은 거의 불가능한 일에 가까웠습니다."],["There were seen so many cavaliers prancing and curvetting before the windows of their mistresses, that a stranger would have imagined the whole nation to have been nothing less than a race of knight-errants.","제 연인의 창문 앞에서 말을 껑충거리며 재롱떠는 기사들이 어찌나 많이 보였던지, 외지 사람이라면 이 나라 전체가 다름 아닌 편력 기사의 종족이라고 여겼을 것입니다."],["But after the world became a little acquainted with that notable history, the man that was seen in that once celebrated drapery was pointed at as a Don Quixote, and found himself the jest of high and low.","그러나 세상이 그 주목할 만한 이야기를 조금 알게 된 뒤로는, 한때 떠받들어지던 그 차림새로 보이는 자는 돈키호테라 손가락질당하고 위아래 할 것 없이 모두의 웃음거리가 되는 신세가 되었습니다."],["And I verily believe that to this, and this only, we owe that dampness and poverty of spirit which has run through all our councils for a century past, so little agreeable to those nobler actions of our famous ancestors.”","그리고 저는 진실로 믿거니와, 우리의 저 이름난 선조들의 그 고귀한 행동들과는 그토록 어울리지 않게 지난 한 세기 동안 우리의 모든 의회에 스며든 그 위축됨과 정신의 빈곤은, 바로 이것에, 오직 이것에 우리가 빚지고 있는 것입니다.\""]],[["To call “Don Quixote” a sad book, preaching a pessimist view of life, argues a total misconception of its drift.","「돈키호테」를 두고 삶에 대한 비관적 견해를 설파하는 슬픈 책이라 부르는 것은 그 취지를 완전히 오해하는 것이다."],["It would be so if its moral were that, in this world, true enthusiasm naturally leads to ridicule and discomfiture.","만일 그 교훈이, 이 세상에서 참된 열정은 자연히 조롱과 낭패로 이어진다는 것이라면 그럴 법도 하다."],["But it preaches nothing of the sort; its moral, so far as it can be said to have one, is that the spurious enthusiasm that is born of vanity and self-conceit, that is made an end in itself, not a means to an end, that acts on mere impulse, regardless of circumstances and consequences, is mischievous to its owner, and a very considerable nuisance to the community at large.","그러나 그것은 그런 따위를 설파하지 않는다. 그것에 교훈이 있다 할 수 있는 한, 그 교훈이란, 허영과 자만에서 태어나, 수단이 아니라 그 자체가 목적이 되어 버리고, 상황과 결과를 아랑곳하지 않고 한낱 충동에 따라 움직이는 거짓된 열정은 그 주인에게 해롭고 공동체 전체에는 적잖이 성가신 존재라는 것이다."],["To those who cannot distinguish between the one kind and the other, no doubt “Don Quixote”","이 한 부류와 다른 부류를 구별하지 못하는 이들에게는 의심할 바 없이 「돈키호테」가 슬픈 책이다."],["is a sad book; no doubt to some minds it is very sad that a man who had just uttered so beautiful a sentiment as that “it is a hard case to make slaves of those whom God and Nature made free,”","의심할 바 없이 어떤 이들의 마음에는, \"하느님과 자연이 자유로이 만든 이들을 노예로 삼는 것은 가혹한 처사\"라는 그토록 아름다운 정서를 방금 토로한 사람이, 자신의 미친 박애가 사회에 풀어놓은 그 악당들에게 배은망덕하게 돌팔매질당하는 것이 매우 슬픈 일이리라."],["should be ungratefully pelted by the scoundrels his crazy philanthropy had let loose on society; but to others of a more judicial cast it will be a matter of regret that reckless self-sufficient enthusiasm is not oftener requited in some such way for all the mischief it does in the world.","그러나 좀 더 분별 있는 기질을 지닌 다른 이들에게는, 무모하고 독선적인 열정이 그것이 세상에 끼치는 온갖 해악에 대해 그런 식으로 보답받는 일이 더 잦지 않다는 것이 안타까운 일이 되리라."]],[["A very slight examination of the structure of “Don Quixote” will suffice to show that Cervantes had no deep design or elaborate plan in his mind when he began the book.","「돈키호테」의 구조를 아주 조금만 살펴보아도, 세르반테스가 이 책을 시작할 때 마음속에 어떤 깊은 설계나 정교한 계획을 품고 있지 않았음을 보여 주기에 충분할 것이다."],["When he wrote those lines in which “with a few strokes of a great master he sets before us the pauper gentleman,”","\"대가의 몇 차례 붓놀림으로 그 가난한 신사를 우리 앞에 세워 놓는\""],["he had no idea of the goal to which his imagination was leading him.","그 구절들을 썼을 때, 그는 자신의 상상력이 자기를 이끌어 가던 그 목적지를 알지 못했다."],["There can be little doubt that all he contemplated was a short tale to range with those he had already written, a tale setting forth the ludicrous results that might be expected to follow the attempt of a crazy gentleman to act the part of a knight-errant in modern life.","그가 염두에 둔 것이라고는 이미 써 둔 것들과 어깨를 나란히 할 짧은 이야기, 곧 정신 나간 신사가 현대의 삶에서 편력 기사 노릇을 하려고 시도할 때 뒤따르리라 예상할 수 있는 우스꽝스러운 결과들을 펼쳐 보이는 이야기였음에는 거의 의심의 여지가 없다."]],[["It is plain, for one thing, that Sancho Panza did not enter into the original scheme, for had Cervantes thought of him he certainly would not have omitted him in his hero’s outfit, which he obviously meant to be complete.","우선 한 가지 분명한 것은, 산초 판사가 애초의 구상에는 들어 있지 않았다는 점이다. 만일 세르반테스가 그를 생각했더라면, 그는 분명 완벽하게 갖추려 했던 자기 주인공의 채비에서 그를 빠뜨리지 않았을 것이기 때문이다."],["Him we owe to the landlord’s chance remark in Chapter III that knights seldom travelled without squires.","우리가 그를 얻은 것은 제3장에서 기사들이 종자 없이 여행하는 일은 드물다고 한 여관 주인의 우연한 한마디 덕분이다."],["To try to think of a Don Quixote without Sancho Panza is like trying to think of a one-bladed pair of scissors.","산초 판사 없는 돈키호테를 떠올려 보려는 것은 날 하나뿐인 가위를 떠올려 보려는 것과 같다."]],[["The story was written at first, like the others, without any division and without the intervention of Cide Hamete Benengeli; and it seems not unlikely that Cervantes had some intention of bringing Dulcinea, or Aldonza Lorenzo, on the scene in person.","그 이야기는 처음에는 다른 것들과 마찬가지로 아무런 장 구분도 없이, 그리고 시데 아메테 베넹헬리의 개입도 없이 쓰였다. 그리고 세르반테스가 둘시네아, 즉 알돈사 로렌소를 직접 무대에 등장시킬 어떤 의도를 품고 있었을 가능성도 없지 않아 보인다."],["It was probably the ransacking of the Don’s library and the discussion on the books of chivalry that first suggested it to him that his idea was capable of development.","그에게 자신의 착상이 발전될 여지가 있음을 처음 시사한 것은 아마도 돈키호테의 서재를 샅샅이 뒤지는 장면과 기사도 책에 관한 논의였을 것이다."],["What, if instead of a mere string of farcical misadventures, he were to make his tale a burlesque of one of these books, caricaturing their style, incidents, and spirit?","한낱 익살스러운 봉변들을 줄줄이 엮는 대신, 자기 이야기를 이런 책들 가운데 하나를 풍자하는 것으로 만들어, 그것들의 문체와 사건과 정신을 희화화한다면 어떨까?"]],[["In pursuance of this change of plan, he hastily and somewhat clumsily divided what he had written into chapters on the model of “Amadis,” invented the fable of a mysterious Arabic manuscript, and set up Cide Hamete Benengeli in imitation of the almost invariable practice of the chivalry-romance authors, who were fond of tracing their books to some recondite source.","이 계획 변경에 따라 그는 서둘러, 그리고 다소 서투르게 자기가 써 둔 것을 「아마디스」를 본떠 여러 장으로 나누고, 어떤 알 수 없는 아라비아어 사본이라는 우화를 지어내고, 자기들의 책을 무언가 심오한 출처에서 끌어왔다고 밝히기를 즐겼던 기사도 소설 작가들의 거의 변함없는 관행을 흉내 내어 시데 아메테 베넹헬리를 내세웠다."],["In working out the new ideas, he soon found the value of Sancho Panza.","새로운 착상을 풀어 가는 가운데 그는 곧 산초 판사의 가치를 발견했다."],["Indeed, the keynote, not only to Sancho’s part, but to the whole book, is struck in the first words Sancho utters when he announces his intention of taking his ass with him.","실로 산초의 역할뿐 아니라 책 전체의 으뜸음은 산초가 자기 당나귀를 데려가겠다는 뜻을 밝히며 처음 내뱉는 말에서 울린다. \"당나귀에 대해서는,\""],["“About the ass,” we are told, “Don Quixote hesitated a little, trying whether he could call to mind any knight-errant taking with him an esquire mounted on ass-back; but no instance occurred to his memory.”","우리에게 전하기를, \"돈키호테는 잠시 망설이며, 종자를 당나귀에 태워 데리고 다닌 편력 기사를 하나라도 떠올릴 수 있을까 애써 보았으나, 그 어떤 본보기도 그의 기억에 떠오르지 않았다.\""],["We can see the whole scene at a glance, the stolid unconsciousness of Sancho and the perplexity of his master, upon whose perception the incongruity has just forced itself.","우리는 그 장면 전체를 한눈에 볼 수 있으니, 산초의 멍하니 아무것도 모르는 모습과, 방금 그 어긋남이 제 인식에 들이닥친 그 주인의 당혹스러움이 그것이다."],["This is Sancho’s mission throughout the book; he is an unconscious Mephistopheles, always unwittingly making mockery of his master’s aspirations, always exposing the fallacy of his ideas by some unintentional ad absurdum, always bringing him back to the world of fact and commonplace by force of sheer stolidity.","이것이 책 전체에 걸친 산초의 사명이다. 그는 무의식적인 메피스토펠레스로서, 언제나 본의 아니게 제 주인의 열망을 조롱거리로 만들고, 언제나 어떤 의도치 않은 귀류법으로 그 주인의 생각의 허점을 드러내며, 언제나 순전한 우둔함의 힘으로 그를 사실과 평범함의 세계로 도로 끌어온다."]],[["By the time Cervantes had got his volume of novels off his hands, and summoned up resolution enough to set about the Second Part in earnest, the case was very much altered.","세르반테스가 자기 소설집을 손에서 털어 내고, 제2부에 진지하게 착수할 만큼 결심을 다잡았을 무렵에는, 사정이 크게 달라져 있었다."],["Don Quixote and Sancho Panza had not merely found favour, but had already become, what they have never since ceased to be, veritable entities to the popular imagination.","돈키호테와 산초 판사는 한낱 호의를 얻은 정도가 아니라, 이미 그 이후로 줄곧 그래 왔듯이 대중의 상상 속에서 실재하는 존재가 되어 있었다."],["There was no occasion for him now to interpolate extraneous matter; nay, his readers told him plainly that what they wanted of him was more Don Quixote and more Sancho Panza, and not novels, tales, or digressions.","이제 그로서는 무관한 내용을 끼워 넣을 까닭이 없었다. 아니, 그의 독자들은 자기들이 그에게 바라는 것은 더 많은 돈키호테와 더 많은 산초 판사이지 소설이나 이야기나 여담이 아니라고 그에게 대놓고 말했다."],["To himself, too, his creations had become realities, and he had become proud of them, especially of Sancho.","그 자신에게도 그의 창조물들은 실재가 되어 있었고, 그는 그들을, 특히 산초를 자랑스러워하게 되었다."],["He began the Second Part, therefore, under very different conditions, and the difference makes itself manifest at once.","그리하여 그는 매우 다른 조건 아래서 제2부를 시작했으며, 그 차이는 곧바로 스스로를 드러낸다."],["Even in translation the style will be seen to be far easier, more flowing, more natural, and more like that of a man sure of himself and of his audience.","번역으로도 그 문체가 훨씬 수월하고, 더 유려하고, 더 자연스러우며, 자기 자신과 자기 독자를 확신하는 사람의 문체와 더 비슷함을 알아볼 수 있을 것이다."],["Don Quixote and Sancho undergo a change also.","돈키호테와 산초도 변화를 겪는다."],["In the First","제1부에서는"]],[["Part, Don Quixote has no character or individuality whatever.","제1부에서 돈키호테는 아무런 성격도 개성도 지니고 있지 않다."],["He is nothing more than a crazy representative of the sentiments of the chivalry romances.","그는 기사도 로맨스의 감정을 미친 모습으로 대변하는 자에 지나지 않는다."],["In all that he says and does he is simply repeating the lesson he has learned from his books; and therefore, it is absurd to speak of him in the gushing strain of the sentimental critics when they dilate upon his nobleness, disinterestedness, dauntless courage, and so forth.","그가 말하고 행하는 모든 것에서 그는 단지 자신의 책에서 배운 교훈을 되풀이하고 있을 뿐이다. 그러므로 감상적인 비평가들이 그의 고결함과 사심 없음과 두려움 없는 용기 따위를 늘어놓으며 그를 두고 넘쳐흐르는 어조로 떠들어 대는 것은 어리석은 일이다."],["It was the business of a knight-errant to right wrongs, redress injuries, and succour the distressed, and this, as a matter of course, he makes his business when he takes up the part; a knight-errant was bound to be intrepid, and so he feels bound to cast fear aside.","잘못을 바로잡고 상처를 치유하며 곤경에 처한 이를 돕는 것은 편력 기사의 본분이었으니, 그가 그 역할을 떠맡았을 때 이것을 자기 본분으로 삼는 것은 당연한 노릇이다. 편력 기사는 마땅히 담대해야 했으므로, 그도 두려움을 떨쳐 버려야 한다고 느끼는 것이다."],["Of all Byron’s melodious nonsense about Don Quixote, the most nonsensical statement is that “‘t is his virtue makes him mad!”","돈키호테에 관한 바이런의 그 모든 듣기 좋은 헛소리 가운데 가장 터무니없는 단언은 \"그를 미치게 한 것은 그의 미덕이다!\"라는 것이다."],["The exact opposite is the truth; it is his madness makes him virtuous.","진실은 그 정반대이니, 그를 덕스럽게 만든 것은 바로 그의 광기인 것이다."]],[["In the Second Part, Cervantes repeatedly reminds the reader, as if it was a point upon which he was anxious there should be no mistake, that his hero’s madness is strictly confined to delusions on the subject of chivalry, and that on every other subject he is discreto, one, in fact, whose faculty of discernment is in perfect order.","제2부에서 세르반테스는, 마치 거기에 오해가 있어서는 안 된다고 애써 강조하기라도 하듯 독자에게 거듭 일러두기를, 자기 주인공의 광기는 오로지 기사도라는 주제에 관한 망상에만 엄격히 국한되어 있으며 다른 모든 주제에 관해서는 그가 분별 있는 사람, 곧 사실상 판단력이 온전히 작동하는 사람이라고 한다."],["The advantage of this is that he is enabled to make use of Don Quixote as a mouthpiece for his own reflections, and so, without seeming to digress, allow himself the relief of digression when he requires it, as freely as in a commonplace book.","이 점의 이로움은, 그가 돈키호테를 자신의 사색을 풀어놓는 대변자로 삼을 수 있다는 데 있다. 그리하여 그는 옆길로 새는 듯 보이지 않으면서도, 필요할 때면 비망록에서처럼 거리낌 없이 여담의 여유를 스스로에게 허락하는 것이다."]],[["It is true the amount of individuality bestowed upon Don Quixote is not very great.","돈키호테에게 부여된 개성의 양이 그리 크지 않다는 것은 사실이다."],["There are some natural touches of character about him, such as his mixture of irascibility and placability, and his curious affection for Sancho together with his impatience of the squire’s loquacity and impertinence; but in the main, apart from his craze, he is little more than a thoughtful, cultured gentleman, with instinctive good taste and a great deal of shrewdness and originality of mind.","그에게는 천성적인 성격의 손길이 몇 가지 있으니, 이를테면 성마름과 너그러움이 뒤섞인 점, 그리고 시종의 수다와 무례함에 대한 조바심과 더불어 산초에 대한 묘한 애정 같은 것이 그러하다. 그러나 대체로 그의 광기를 제쳐 두면, 그는 본능적인 좋은 취향과 다분한 명민함, 정신의 독창성을 지닌 사려 깊고 교양 있는 신사 그 이상은 별로 아니다."]],[["As to Sancho, it is plain, from the concluding words of the preface to the First Part, that he was a favourite with his creator even before he had been taken into favour by the public.","산초로 말하자면, 제1부 서문의 맺음말에서 분명히 드러나듯, 그는 대중의 총애를 받기도 전에 이미 그를 창조한 자의 사랑을 받고 있었다."],["An inferior genius, taking him in hand a second time, would very likely have tried to improve him by making him more comical, clever, amiable, or virtuous.","못한 재능을 지닌 자라면 그를 두 번째로 다룰 때, 그를 더 우스꽝스럽거나 영리하거나 상냥하거나 덕스럽게 만들어 개선하려 들었을 것이다. 그러나 세르반테스는 그런 식으로 자기 작품을 망치기에는 너무나 참된 예술가였다."],["But Cervantes was too true an artist to spoil his work in this way.","산초는 다시 등장할 때, 낯익은 옛 면모를 지닌 옛 산초 그대로이다."],["Sancho, when he reappears, is the old Sancho with the old familiar features; but with a difference; they have been brought out more distinctly, but at the same time with a careful avoidance of anything like caricature; the outline has been filled in where filling in was necessary, and, vivified by a few touches of a master’s hand, Sancho stands before us as he might in a character portrait by Velazquez.","다만 차이가 있으니, 그 면모가 한층 또렷이 드러났으되, 동시에 희화처럼 보일 만한 것은 무엇이든 조심스레 피했다. 윤곽은 채워 넣어야 할 곳에 채워졌고, 대가의 손이 몇 번 스쳐 생기를 얻어, 산초는 마치 벨라스케스의 인물 초상화 속에서처럼 우리 앞에 서 있다."],["He is a much more important and prominent figure in the Second Part than in the First; indeed, it is his matchless mendacity about Dulcinea that to a great extent supplies the action of the story.","그는 제1부에서보다 제2부에서 훨씬 더 중요하고 두드러진 인물이며, 실로 둘시네아에 관한 그의 비할 데 없는 거짓말이 상당 부분 이야기의 줄거리를 채워 준다."]],[["His development in this respect is as remarkable as in any other.","이 점에서 그의 발전은 다른 어떤 면에서 못지않게 놀랍다."],["In the First Part he displays a great natural gift of lying.","제1부에서 그는 거짓말에 관한 대단한 천부적 재능을 드러낸다."],["His lies are not of the highly imaginative sort that liars in fiction commonly indulge in; like Falstaff’s, they resemble the father that begets them; they are simple, homely, plump lies; plain working lies, in short.","그의 거짓말은 소설 속 거짓말쟁이들이 흔히 즐기는 그런 풍부한 상상력의 종류가 아니다. 폴스타프의 것처럼 그의 거짓말은 그것을 낳은 아비를 닮았으니, 단순하고 소박하며 통통한 거짓말, 한마디로 평범한 일상의 거짓말이다."],["But in the service of such a master as Don Quixote he develops rapidly, as we see when he comes to palm off the three country wenches as Dulcinea and her ladies in waiting.","그러나 돈키호테 같은 주인을 섬기면서 그는 빠르게 발전하는데, 시골 처녀 셋을 둘시네아와 그 시녀들로 둔갑시켜 떠넘기는 대목에서 우리는 이를 보게 된다."],["It is worth noticing how, flushed by his success in this instance, he is tempted afterwards to try a flight beyond his powers in his account of the journey on Clavileno.","이 일에서의 성공에 우쭐해진 그가 그 뒤 클라빌레뇨를 타고 떠난 여행을 이야기하면서 제 능력을 넘어서는 비상을 시도하도록 부추겨지는 점은 주목할 만하다."]],[["In the Second Part it is the spirit rather than the incidents of the chivalry romances that is the subject of the burlesque.","제2부에서 익살의 대상이 되는 것은 기사도 로맨스의 사건들이라기보다 오히려 그 정신이다."],["Enchantments of the sort travestied in those of Dulcinea and the Trifaldi and the cave of Montesinos play a leading part in the later and inferior romances, and another distinguishing feature is caricatured in Don Quixote’s blind adoration of Dulcinea.","둘시네아와 트리팔디, 몬테시노스 동굴의 마법에서 풍자된 그런 종류의 마법은 후기의 못한 로맨스들에서 주된 역할을 하며, 또 하나의 두드러진 특징은 돈키호테가 둘시네아를 맹목적으로 숭배하는 데서 희화화된다."],["In the romances of chivalry love is either a mere animalism or a fantastic idolatry.","기사도 로맨스에서 사랑은 한낱 동물적 욕정이거나 환상적인 우상 숭배이다."],["Only a coarse-minded man would care to make merry with the former, but to one of Cervantes’ humour the latter was naturally an attractive subject for ridicule.","거친 마음을 지닌 자라면 전자를 두고 즐거워하려 들겠지만, 세르반테스 같은 해학을 지닌 이에게는 후자가 자연히 조롱의 매력적인 대상이었다."],["Like everything else in these romances, it is a gross exaggeration of the real sentiment of chivalry, but its peculiar extravagance is probably due to the influence of those masters of hyperbole, the Provencal poets.","이 로맨스들의 다른 모든 것과 마찬가지로, 그것은 진짜 기사도 감정을 거칠게 과장한 것이나, 그 독특한 터무니없음은 아마도 과장의 대가인 프로방스 시인들의 영향에서 비롯된 듯하다."],["When a troubadour professed his readiness to obey his lady in all things, he made it incumbent upon the next comer, if he wished to avoid the imputation of tameness and commonplace, to declare himself the slave of her will, which the next was compelled to cap by some still stronger declaration; and so expressions of devotion went on rising one above the other like biddings at an auction, and a conventional language of gallantry and theory of love came into being that in time permeated the literature of Southern Europe, and bore fruit, in one direction in the transcendental worship of Beatrice and Laura, and in another in the grotesque idolatry which found exponents in writers like Feliciano de Silva.","한 음유시인이 모든 일에서 자기 귀부인에게 복종하겠노라 공언하면, 다음 차례의 시인은 밋밋하고 진부하다는 비난을 피하려면 자신을 그녀의 뜻에 매인 노예라 선언해야만 했고, 그다음 시인은 또 한층 더 강한 선언으로 그것을 능가해야만 했다. 그리하여 헌신의 표현들은 경매장의 호가처럼 서로 위로 치솟아 올라, 사랑에 관한 관습적인 언어와 이론, 곧 풍류의 어법이 생겨나, 마침내 남유럽의 문학에 스며들어 한쪽으로는 베아트리체와 라우라에 대한 초월적 숭배로, 다른 한쪽으로는 펠리시아노 데 실바 같은 작가들이 대변한 기괴한 우상 숭배로 열매를 맺었다."],["This is what Cervantes deals with in Don Quixote’s passion for Dulcinea, and in no instance has he carried out the burlesque more happily.","이것이 바로 세르반테스가 돈키호테의 둘시네아를 향한 열정에서 다루는 것이며, 그는 어느 경우에도 이보다 더 멋들어지게 익살을 펼친 적이 없다."],["By keeping Dulcinea in the background, and making her a vague shadowy being of whose very existence we are left in doubt, he invests Don Quixote’s worship of her virtues and charms with an additional extravagance, and gives still more point to the caricature of the sentiment and language of the romances.","둘시네아를 배경에 묻어 두고 그녀를 그 존재 자체마저 의심스러운 어렴풋한 그림자 같은 존재로 만듦으로써, 그는 돈키호테가 그녀의 미덕과 매력을 숭배하는 데 한층 더한 터무니없음을 입히고, 로맨스의 감정과 언어에 대한 희화에 더욱 날을 세운다."]],[["One of the great merits of “Don Quixote,” and one of the qualities that have secured its acceptance by all classes of readers and made it the most cosmopolitan of books, is its simplicity.","「돈키호테」의 큰 미덕 가운데 하나이자, 모든 계층의 독자에게 받아들여지게 하여 가장 세계적인 책으로 만든 자질 가운데 하나는 바로 그 단순함이다."],["There are, of course, points obvious enough to a Spanish seventeenth century audience which do not immediately strike a reader now-a-days, and Cervantes often takes it for granted that an allusion will be generally understood which is only intelligible to a few.","물론 17세기 스페인 청중에게는 충분히 자명했으나 오늘날의 독자에게는 곧바로 와닿지 않는 점들이 있으며, 세르반테스는 흔히 소수에게만 이해될 암시를 두루 이해되리라 여기고 넘어가곤 한다. 예컨대 스페인 안의 많은 독자, 그리고 스페인 밖의 대다수 독자에게는 그가 주인공의 고장으로 어떤 지방을 택했는지의 의미가 완전히 사라져 버린다."],["For example, on many of his readers in Spain, and most of his readers out of it, the significance of his choice of a country for his hero is completely lost.","라만차를 보지 않고서는 「돈키호테」를 온전히 이해할 수 없다고 말하면 지나치겠으나, 라만차를 한번 흘긋 보기만 해도 어떤 주석가도 줄 수 없는 세르반테스의 의미에 관한 통찰을 얻게 되리라는 것만은 의심할 바 없다."],["It would be going too far to say that no one can thoroughly comprehend “Don Quixote”","스페인의 모든 지방 가운데 라만차야말로 로맨스의 관념을 떠올리게 할 마지막 곳이다."],["without having seen La Mancha, but undoubtedly even a glimpse of La Mancha will give an insight into the meaning of Cervantes such as no commentator can give.","반도의 그 단조롭기 그지없는 중앙 고원 전체에서도 가장 단조로운 지역이다. 에스트레마두라의 음산한 적막에는 무언가 인상적인 데가 있고, 레온과 옛 카스티야의 평원이 헐벗고 황량하다 해도 거기에는 역사로 이름난, 과거의 유적이 풍성한 옛 도시들이 점점이 박혀 있다."],["Of all the regions of Spain it is the last that would suggest the idea of romance.","그러나 라만차의 풍경에는 그 어떤 위안이 될 만한 특징도 없으니, 사막의 한결같음을 다 지녔으되 그 위엄은 없다."],["Of all the dull central plateau of the Peninsula it is the dullest tract.","그 단조로움을 깨뜨리는 몇 안 되는 도시와 마을은 초라하고 평범하여, 거기에는 유서 깊은 구석이라곤 없고 가난의 그림 같은 정취조차 없다."],["There is something impressive about the grim solitudes of Estremadura; and if the plains of Leon and Old Castile are bald and dreary, they are studded with old cities renowned in history and rich in relics of the past.","실로 돈키호테 자신의 마을 아르가마시야는 그 거리와 집들의 깐깐한 규칙성에서 일종의 답답한 고상함을 풍긴다. 모든 것이 천박하고, 풍차마저도 풍차 종류 가운데 가장 추하고 가장 남루한 것들이다."],["But there is no redeeming feature in the Manchegan landscape; it has all the sameness of the desert without its dignity; the few towns and villages that break its monotony are mean and commonplace, there is nothing venerable about them, they have not even the picturesqueness of poverty; indeed, Don Quixote’s own village, Argamasilla, has a sort of oppressive respectability in the prim regularity of its streets and houses; everything is ignoble; the very windmills are the ugliest and shabbiest of the windmill kind.","이 고장을 잘 아는 사람이라면 누구든 「라만차의 돈키호테」라는 문체와 제목만으로도 단번에 작가의 의도를 푸는 열쇠를 얻었다. 기사의 고장이자 그의 기사도 행각의 무대인 라만차는, 마분지 투구, 시종 노릇을 하는 당나귀 탄 농사꾼, 비열한 객줏집 주인이 내려 주는 기사 작위, 압제의 희생자로 오인된 죄수들, 그리고 돈키호테의 세계와 그가 살던 세계 사이의, 그가 본 그대로의 사물과 실제 사물 사이의 그 밖의 온갖 부조화와 한 묶음을 이룬다."]],[["To anyone who knew the country well, the mere style and title of “Don Quixote of La Mancha” gave the key to the author’s meaning at once.","이 책의 해학과 목적 전체에 깔려 있는 이 부조화의 요소가, 「돈키호테」를 해석하겠다고 나선 이들 대다수에게 그토록 거의 주목받지 못했다는 것은 이상한 일이다."],["La Mancha as the knight’s country and scene of his chivalries is of a piece with the pasteboard helmet, the farm-labourer on ass-back for a squire, knighthood conferred by a rascally ventero, convicts taken for victims of oppression, and the rest of the incongruities between Don Quixote’s world and the world he lived in, between things as he saw them and things as they were. It is strange that this element of incongruity, underlying the whole humour and purpose of the book, should have been so little heeded by the majority of those who have undertaken to interpret “Don Quixote.”","예컨대 삽화가들은 그것을 완전히 간과해 버렸다."],["It has been completely overlooked, for example, by the illustrators. To be sure, the great majority of the artists who illustrated “Don Quixote”","사실 「돈키호테」에 삽화를 그린 화가들의 절대다수는 스페인에 관해 아무것도 알지 못했다."],["knew nothing whatever of Spain. To them a venta conveyed no idea but the abstract one of a roadside inn, and they could not therefore do full justice to the humour of Don Quixote’s misconception in taking it for a castle, or perceive the remoteness of all its realities from his ideal.","그들에게 객줏집이란 한낱 길가 여인숙이라는 추상적 관념밖에 전해 주지 못했으니, 그들은 그것을 성으로 오인하는 돈키호테의 착각이 지닌 해학을 온전히 살려 낼 수도 없었고, 그 객줏집의 모든 실상이 그의 이상에서 얼마나 동떨어졌는지 알아챌 수도 없었다."],["But even when better informed they seem to have no apprehension of the full force of the discrepancy.","그러나 더 잘 알고 있을 때조차 그들은 그 어긋남의 온전한 힘을 헤아리지 못하는 듯하다."],["Take, for instance, Gustave Dore’s drawing of Don Quixote watching his armour in the inn-yard.","예컨대 돈키호테가 여인숙 마당에서 자기 갑옷을 지켜보는 귀스타브 도레의 그림을 보라."],["Whether or not the Venta de Quesada on the Seville road is, as tradition maintains, the inn described in “Don Quixote,” beyond all question it was just such an inn-yard as the one behind it that Cervantes had in his mind’s eye, and it was on just such a rude stone trough as that beside the primitive draw-well in the corner that he meant Don Quixote to deposit his armour.","세비야 가도에 있는 케사다 객줏집이 전승이 주장하는 대로 「돈키호테」에 묘사된 그 여인숙인지 아닌지는 차치하더라도, 세르반테스가 마음의 눈으로 그린 것이 바로 그 뒤편에 있는 것과 똑같은 여인숙 마당이었음은, 그리고 그가 돈키호테에게 갑옷을 내려놓게 하려 한 곳이 모퉁이의 원시적인 두레우물 곁에 놓인 것과 똑같은 투박한 돌구유였음은 추호도 의심할 바 없다."],["Gustave Dore makes it an elaborate fountain such as no arriero ever watered his mules at in the corral of any venta in Spain, and thereby entirely misses the point aimed at by Cervantes.","귀스타브 도레는 그것을, 스페인의 어떤 객줏집 마당에서도 노새몰이꾼이 자기 노새에게 물을 먹인 적 없을 법한 정교한 분수로 만들어 버려, 세르반테스가 겨눈 핵심을 완전히 놓치고 만다."],["It is the mean, prosaic, commonplace character of all the surroundings and circumstances that gives a significance to Don Quixote’s vigil and the ceremony that follows.","돈키호테의 밤샘과 그 뒤에 이어지는 의식에 의미를 부여하는 것은, 바로 그 모든 주변 환경과 정황의 초라하고 산문적이며 평범한 성격인 것이다."]],[["Cervantes’ humour is for the most part of that broader and simpler sort, the strength of which lies in the perception of the incongruous.","세르반테스의 해학은 대부분 부조화의 인식에 그 힘이 있는, 그 폭넓고 단순한 종류의 것이다."],["It is the incongruity of Sancho in all his ways, words, and works, with the ideas and aims of his master, quite as much as the wonderful vitality and truth to nature of the character, that makes him the most humorous creation in the whole range of fiction.","산초를 통틀어 소설의 전 영역에서 가장 해학적인 창조물로 만드는 것은, 그 성격의 놀라운 생명력과 자연에 대한 충실함 못지않게, 그의 모든 행동과 말과 행위가 주인의 관념 및 목표와 빚어내는 부조화이다."],["That unsmiling gravity of which Cervantes was the first great master, “Cervantes’ serious air,” which sits naturally on Swift alone, perhaps, of later humourists, is essential to this kind of humour, and here again Cervantes has suffered at the hands of his interpreters.","세르반테스가 최초의 위대한 대가였던 그 미소 없는 진중함, 곧 후대의 해학가 가운데 어쩌면 스위프트에게만 자연스럽게 어울리는 \"세르반테스의 진지한 분위기\"는 이런 종류의 해학에 필수적인 것인데, 여기서도 세르반테스는 해석자들의 손에 시달려 왔다."],["Nothing, unless indeed the coarse buffoonery of Phillips, could be more out of place in an attempt to represent Cervantes, than a flippant, would-be facetious style, like that of Motteux’s version for example, or the sprightly, jaunty air, French translators sometimes adopt.","필립스의 거친 익살을 제외한다면, 세르반테스를 대변하려는 시도에서 예컨대 모토의 번역에서 보이는 것 같은 경박하고 우스개를 부리려는 문체, 혹은 프랑스 번역자들이 이따금 취하는 발랄하고 까부는 분위기보다 더 어울리지 않는 것은 없을 것이다."],["It is the grave matter-of-factness of the narrative, and the apparent unconsciousness of the author that he is saying anything ludicrous, anything but the merest commonplace, that give its peculiar flavour to the humour of Cervantes.","세르반테스의 해학에 그 독특한 풍미를 주는 것은, 그 서술의 진중하고 사실적인 태도와, 자신이 우스운 것을, 가장 평범한 것 외에는 그 무엇을 말하고 있음을 의식하지 못하는 듯한 작가의 태도이다."],["His, in fact, is the exact opposite of the humour of Sterne and the self-conscious humourists.","실로 그의 해학은 스턴과 자의식 강한 해학가들의 해학과 정반대이다."],["Even when Uncle Toby is at his best, you are always aware of “the man Sterne” behind him, watching you over his shoulder to see what effect he is producing.","토비 삼촌이 한창 신명에 차 있을 때조차, 당신은 그 뒤에서 어깨 너머로 당신을 지켜보며 자신이 어떤 효과를 내고 있는지 살피는 \"스턴이라는 사내\"의 존재를 늘 의식하게 된다."],["Cervantes always leaves you alone with Don Quixote and Sancho.","세르반테스는 언제나 당신을 돈키호테와 산초와 단둘이 남겨 둔다."],["He and Swift and the great humourists always keep themselves out of sight, or, more properly speaking, never think about themselves at all, unlike our latter-day school of humourists, who seem to have revived the old horse-collar method, and try to raise a laugh by some grotesque assumption of ignorance, imbecility, or bad taste.","그와 스위프트와 위대한 해학가들은 언제나 자신을 시야 밖에 두거나, 더 정확히 말하면 자기 자신에 관해서는 도무지 생각하지 않으니, 옛 마구(馬具) 익살의 방식을 되살린 듯, 무지나 우둔함이나 저속한 취향을 기괴하게 꾸며 보이며 웃음을 자아내려 드는 우리 근래의 해학가 무리와는 다르다."]],[["It is true that to do full justice to Spanish humour in any other language is well-nigh an impossibility.","스페인의 해학을 다른 어떤 언어로든 온전히 살려 내는 것이 거의 불가능에 가깝다는 것은 사실이다."],["There is a natural gravity and a sonorous stateliness about Spanish, be it ever so colloquial, that make an absurdity doubly absurd, and give plausibility to the most preposterous statement.","스페인어에는 아무리 구어적이라 해도 천성적인 진중함과 낭랑한 위엄이 깃들어 있어, 그것이 어리석은 말을 갑절로 어리석게 만들고 가장 터무니없는 단언에도 그럴듯함을 부여한다."],["This is what makes Sancho Panza’s drollery the despair of the conscientious translator.","바로 이것이 산초 판사의 익살을 양심적인 번역자에게 절망거리로 만드는 것이다."],["Sancho’s curt comments can never fall flat, but they lose half their flavour when transferred from their native Castilian into any other medium.","산초의 퉁명스러운 논평은 결코 김빠지는 법이 없으나, 그 본고장의 카스티야어에서 다른 어떤 매체로 옮겨지면 그 풍미의 절반을 잃고 만다."],["But if foreigners have failed to do justice to the humour of Cervantes, they are no worse than his own countrymen.","그러나 외국인들이 세르반테스의 해학을 제대로 살려 내지 못했다 해도, 그들이 그의 동포보다 더 못한 것은 아니다."],["Indeed, were it not for the Spanish peasant’s relish of “Don Quixote,” one might be tempted to think that the great humourist was not looked upon as a humourist at all in his own country.","실로 스페인 농부가 「돈키호테」를 음미하지 않았더라면, 그 위대한 해학가가 자기 나라에서는 해학가로 여겨지지조차 않았다고 생각하고 싶어질 정도이다."]],[["The craze of Don Quixote seems, in some instances, to have communicated itself to his critics, making them see things that are not in the book and run full tilt at phantoms that have no existence save in their own imaginations.","돈키호테의 광기는 어떤 경우에는 그의 비평가들에게까지 옮아간 듯하여, 그들로 하여금 책에 없는 것을 보게 하고, 오로지 그들 자신의 상상 속에만 존재하는 허깨비를 향해 전속력으로 돌진하게 만든다."],["Like a good many critics now-a-days, they forget that screams are not criticism, and that it is only vulgar tastes that are influenced by strings of superlatives, three-piled hyperboles, and pompous epithets.","요즘의 적잖은 비평가들처럼, 그들은 비명이 비평이 아니라는 것을, 그리고 줄줄이 늘어선 최상급과 세 겹으로 쌓아 올린 과장과 거창한 수식어에 흔들리는 것은 오로지 천박한 취향뿐이라는 것을 잊고 있다."],["But what strikes one as particularly strange is that while they deal in extravagant eulogies, and ascribe all manner of imaginary ideas and qualities to Cervantes, they show no perception of the quality that ninety-nine out of a hundred of his readers would rate highest in him, and hold to be the one that raises him above all rivalry.","그러나 특히 이상하게 다가오는 점은, 그들이 과장된 찬사를 늘어놓고 온갖 상상 속의 관념과 자질을 세르반테스에게 돌리면서도, 그의 독자 백 명 가운데 아흔아홉이 그에게서 가장 높이 평가하고 그를 모든 경쟁자 위에 올려놓는 바로 그 자질로 여길 그 점에 대해서는 아무런 인식도 보이지 않는다는 것이다."]],[["To speak of “Don Quixote”","「돈키호테」를 한낱 해학적인 책인 양 말하는 것은 명백한 그릇된 규정이 되리라."],["as if it were merely a humorous book would be a manifest misdescription.","세르반테스는 때때로 그것을 이따금의 수필과 비평, 혹은 길고 파란만장한 생애에서 얻은 관찰과 성찰과 응축된 지혜를 담는 일종의 비망록으로 삼는다. 그것은 인류와 인간 본성에 관한 명민한 관찰의 보고이다."],["Cervantes at times makes it a kind of commonplace book for occasional essays and criticisms, or for the observations and reflections and gathered wisdom of a long and stirring life.","근래의 소설들 가운데 여기저기 더 정교한 성격 연구가 있을지 몰라도, 개성을 부여받은 인물에서 이 책보다 풍부한 책은 없다. 콜리지가 셰익스피어를 두고 지극히 사소한 것에 이르기까지 한 말은 세르반테스에게도 들어맞으니, 그는 결코, 아무리 일시적인 목적을 위해서라도 허수아비 인물을 내세우는 법이 없다."],["It is a mine of shrewd observation on mankind and human nature.","그의 모든 인물에는, 그들이 아무리 할 일이 적고 아무리 짧은 시간 독자 앞에 머문다 해도, 생명과 개성이 깃들어 있다. 산손 카라스코, 신부, 테레사 판사, 알티시도라, 심지어 몬테시노스 동굴로 가는 길에서 만난 두 학생까지, 모두가 살아 움직이며 제 존재를 지닌다."],["Among modern novels there may be, here and there, more elaborate studies of character, but there is no book richer in individualised character.","그리고 그 모두 가운데 미워할 자가 하나도 없다는 것이 세르반테스의 폭넓은 인간애의 특징이다. 개탄스러운 행실의 가엾은 마리토르네스조차 제 나름의 어진 마음을 지니고 있고 \"어딘가 희미하고 아득하게 그리스도교도를 닮은 데\"가 있다."],["What Coleridge said of Shakespeare in minimis is true of Cervantes; he never, even for the most temporary purpose, puts forward a lay figure.","그리고 산초로 말하자면, 비록 해부해 보면 주인에 대한 일종의 개 같은 애정 말고는 사랑스러운 면모를 찾을 수 없다 해도, 마음속으로 그를 사랑하지 않을 이가 어디 있겠는가? 그러나 결국 「돈키호테」를 다른 모든 로맨스류 책과 구별 짓는 것은 바로 그 해학이다."],["There is life and individuality in all his characters, however little they may have to do, or however short a time they may be before the reader.","근래의 가장 공정한 판단력을 지닌 비평가 가운데 한 사람이 일컬은 대로 그것을 \"비할 데 없이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 소설\"로 만드는 것이 바로 이것이다. 그것을 독자가 있는 모든 나라에서 토착화시키고, 문학을 지닌 모든 언어에서 고전으로 만든 것은, 거친 익살극에서 셰익스피어나 몰리에르의 것만큼이나 미묘한 희극에 이르기까지 펼쳐지는 그 다채로운 해학이다."],["Samson Carrasco, the curate, Teresa Panza, Altisidora, even the two students met on the road to the cave of Montesinos, all live and move and have their being; and it is characteristic of the broad humanity of Cervantes that there is not a hateful one among them all.","추천하는 시 몇 편 미지의 우르간다 라만차의 돈키호테의 책에게 만일 선한 이들에게 환영받기를, 오 책이여! 그대 한결같이 목표로 삼는다면, 빈 수다쟁이 그 누구도 감히 그대의 자격을 묻거나 다투지 못하리라."],["Even poor Maritornes, with her deplorable morals, has a kind heart of her own and “some faint and distant resemblance to a Christian about her;”","허나 행여 그대가 마음에 품기를 바보들의 인정을 얻고자 한다면, 헛된 수고만이 그대의 보답이 되리니, 겉으로는 칭찬하는 척하리라. 좋은 나무 아래 깃드는 자가 좋은 그늘을 찾는다 사람들은 말하네."],["and as for Sancho, though on dissection we fail to find a lovable trait in him, unless it be a sort of dog-like affection for his master, who is there that in his heart does not love him?","그러한 자에게 그대의 자애로운 별이 베하르에 그늘을 마련해 주었구나. 가지를 활짝 펼쳐 군왕다운 열매를 내보이는 한 그루 왕가의 나무여, 당대의 알렉산드로스인 고귀한 공작을 품은 한 그루 나무로구나."]],[["But it is, after all, the humour of “Don Quixote”","라만차의 한 신사가 어찌하여 그 정신을 잃었는지, 사랑과 영광에 관한 부질없는 이야기들에 빠져 그 이야기를 들려줌이 그대의 뜻이로다. \"귀부인과 무기와 기사들\"에 관한— 새로운 광란의 오를란도여— 차라리 사랑에 빠진 오를란도라 하리, 그가 엘 토보소의 둘시네아를 얻었으니."],["that distinguishes it from all other books of the romance kind.","그대의 방패에 헛된 문장(紋章)을 새기지 말라. 온갖 형상이란—허세 부리는 장난일 뿐."],["It is this that makes it, as one of the most judicial-minded of modern critics calls it, “the best novel in the world beyond all comparison.”","겸손한 헌사를 지어 올려, 조롱꾼에게 이렇게 말할 틈을 주지 말라. \"뭐라!"],["It is its varied humour, ranging from broad farce to comedy as subtle as Shakespeare’s or Moliere’s that has naturalised it in every country where there are readers, and made it a classic in every language that has a literature.","여기 알바로 데 루나인가? 아니면 다시 한니발인가?"]],[["To the book of Don Quixote of la Mancha","아니면 마드리드의 프랑수아 왕이"]],[["If to be welcomed by the good, O Book!","또다시 제 운명을 한탄함인가?\""],["thou make thy steady aim, No empty chatterer will dare To question or dispute thy claim.","하늘이 그대에게 깊은 학식을 내려 주기를 즐거워하지 않으셨고, 흑인 라티노의 언어 재능도 주지 않으셨으니, 그대의 책장에 라틴어를 내보이지 말라."],["But if perchance thou hast a mind To win of idiots approbation, Lost labour will be thy reward, Though they’ll pretend appreciation.","철학이나 기지를 흉내 내지 말라, 이해하지 못하는 자가 그대를 향해"]],[["They say a goodly shade he finds Who shelters ’neath a goodly tree; And such a one thy kindly star In Bejar bath provided thee: A royal tree whose spreading boughs A show of princely fruit display; A tree that bears a noble Duke, The Alexander of his day.","찡그린 얼굴로 이렇게 묻지 않도록, \"어찌 내게 꽃을 권하는가, 벗이여?\" 참견하는 자가 되지 말라. 이웃이 어떤 삶을 사는지는 그대의 일이 아니니. 신중하라. 함부로 던진 농담이 흔히 농담한 자의 머리 위로 되돌아오는 법이라. 그대의 한결같은 수고를 다하여"]],[["Of a Manchegan gentleman Thy purpose is to tell the story, Relating how he lost his wits O’er idle tales of love and glory, Of “ladies, arms, and cavaliers:”","정직한 이름을 스스로 얻도록 하라, 활자로 박혀 보존된 어리석은 짓이란 영원토록 이어지는 수치이니라. 또 하나 충고를 마음에 새기라."],["A new Orlando Furioso— Innamorato, rather—who Won Dulcinea del Toboso.","그대의 지붕이 유리로 되어 있다면, 지나가는 사람들에게 던지려고 돌을 주워 드는 것은 지각없는 짓이니라."]],[["Put no vain emblems on thy shield; All figures—that is bragging play.","지혜로운 이들의 관심을 얻고, 사색하는 이에게 생각할 거리를 주라."],["A modest dedication make, And give no scoffer room to say, “What! Álvaro de Luna here?","경박한 것을 써 내려가는 자는 한낱 어리석은 자들에게나 찾아지리라."],["Or is it Hannibal again? Or does King Francis at Madrid Once more of destiny complain?”","골의 아마디스 라만차의 돈키호테에게 소네트 내가 외로운 슬픔에 잠겨 저 거대한 페냐 포브레 바위 위에 풀죽어 앉아,"]],[["Since Heaven it hath not pleased on thee Deep erudition to bestow, Or black Latino’s gift of tongues, No Latin let thy pages show. Ape not philosophy or wit, Lest one who cannot comprehend, Make a wry face at thee and ask, “Why offer flowers to me, my friend?”","스스로 부과한 고행 속에서 야위어 가던 그 나의 삶을 그대가 본받았으니, 그대, 유일한 음료가 제 눈물의 쓴 소금물이었고, 은이나 구리나 주석 접시도 없이 비천한 처지로 맨땅 위에서 땅의 열매를 먹던 그대여, 그대의 영원한 영광을 확신하며 살라."]],[["Be not a meddler; no affair Of thine the life thy neighbours lead: Be prudent; oft the random jest Recoils upon the jester’s head.","저 넷째 천구의 궤도 위에서 밝은 아폴로가 그 준마들을 모는 한, 그대의 명성 속에 그대는 안전히 남으리니, 그대 고장의 이름은 이야기 속에 길이 남고, 그대의 현명한 작가는 견줄 이 없이 우뚝 서리라."],["Thy constant labour let it be To earn thyself an honest name, For fooleries preserved in print Are perpetuity of shame.","그리스의 돈 벨리아니스 라만차의 돈키호테에게 소네트"]],[["A further counsel bear in mind: If that thy roof be made of glass, It shows small wit to pick up stones To pelt the people as they pass.","베고 찍고 가르는 일에, 말과 행동에, 나는 기사도의 으뜸가는 기사였으니, 세상이 본 그 어느 누구 못지않게 굳세고 담대하며 능란했노라."],["Win the attention of the wise, And give the thinker food for thought; Whoso indites frivolities, Will but by simpletons be sought.","수천의 사람을 압제자의 횡포에서 풀어 주었고, 위업은 컸으며 영원한 명성이 그 보답이었네. 사랑에서 나는 진실과 충성을 증명했고,"]],[["AMADIS OF GAUL To Don Quixote of la Mancha","가장 거대한 거인도 내게는 난쟁이였으며,"]],[["Thou that didst imitate that life of mine When I in lonely sadness on the great Rock Peña Pobre sat disconsolate, In self-imposed penance there to pine; Thou, whose sole beverage was the bitter brine Of thine own tears, and who withouten plate Of silver, copper, tin, in lowly state Off the bare earth and on earth’s fruits didst dine; Live thou, of thine eternal glory sure. So long as on the round of the fourth sphere The bright Apollo shall his coursers steer, In thy renown thou shalt remain secure, Thy country’s name in story shall endure, And thy sage author stand without a peer.","언제나 기사도의 법도에 잘 귀 기울였노라. 변덕스러운 여신도 내 솜씨를 인정했고, 운명조차도 다스림에 굴복하여, 앞머리를 붙들린 채 내 뜻에 따랐네. 허나—저 뿔 돋은 달 위에 옥좌를 둔 듯 내 운수가 앉아 있는 듯 보이건만—위대한 키호테여, 그래도 그대의 위업에 대한 시기심이 내 영혼을 채우는구나. 오리아나 부인 엘 토보소의 둘시네아에게 소네트 오, 가장 아리따운 둘시네아여, 그럴 수만 있다면! 그리 상상함은 즐거운 공상이리니— 미라플로레스가 엘 토보소로 바뀔 수 있다면, 런던의 도성이 그대를 품은 그곳으로 바뀔 수 있다면! 오, 그대의 마음과 몸이 그토록 드러내는"]],[["DON BELIANIS OF GREECE To Don Quixote of la Mancha","헤아릴 수 없는 매력의 그 제복을 내가 얻을 수 있다면!"]],[["In slashing, hewing, cleaving, word and deed, I was the foremost knight of chivalry, Stout, bold, expert, as e’er the world did see; Thousands from the oppressor’s wrong I freed; Great were my feats, eternal fame their meed; In love I proved my truth and loyalty; The hugest giant was a dwarf for me; Ever to knighthood’s laws gave I good heed.","아니면 이제 이름 떨친 그를—그대가 그리 키웠으니— 그대의 기사를 어느 무시무시한 결투에서 내가 볼 수 있다면! 오, 그대가 점잖은 키호테를 물리치게 했던 그 수줍은 정절을 발휘하여, 내가 아마디스에게서 풀려날 수 있다면!"],["My mastery the Fickle Goddess owned, And even Chance, submitting to control, Grasped by the forelock, yielded to my will.","그러면 나의 무거운 슬픔이 기쁨으로 바뀌리니, 나는 아무도 부러워하지 않고, 모두가 나를 부러워하며, 티 없는 행복이 내 것이 되리라."],["Yet—though above yon horned moon enthroned My fortune seems to sit—great Quixote, still Envy of thy achievements fills my soul.","골의 아마디스의 시종 간달린이 돈키호테의 시종 산초 판사에게 소네트 만세, 이름 높은 사내여! 운명의 여신이, 그대를 시종 일에 도제로 묶어 두었을 때, 그대에 대한 보살핌과 자애를 드러내어, 불운에서 벗어나도록 그대의 길을 빚어 놓았구나."]],[["THE LADY OF ORIANA To Dulcinea del Toboso","이제는 더 이상 오만한 편력 기사도가"]],[["Oh, fairest Dulcinea, could it be! It were a pleasant fancy to suppose so— Could Miraflores change to El Toboso, And London’s town to that which shelters thee!","낫과 삽을 멸시의 눈으로 보지 않으니, 드높은 거만함이 소박한 시종다운 순박함보다 덜 셈해지는 법이라."],["Oh, could mine but acquire that livery Of countless charms thy mind and body show so!","나는 그대의 잿빛 당나귀와 그대의 이름을 부러워하노라, 그리고 그대가 즐겨 가득 채우던 그 안장주머니를, 그대의 선견지명을 알려 주는 안락한 것들로 말일세."],["Or him, now famous grown—thou mad’st him grow so— Thy knight, in some dread combat could I see!","뛰어난 산초여! 그대에게 다시 만세를 외치노라!"],["Oh, could I be released from Amadis By exercise of such coy chastity As led thee gentle Quixote to dismiss!","오직 그대에게만 우리 스페인의 오비디우스가 촌스러운 입맞춤과 한 대 쥐어박기로 경의를 표하노라."],["Then would my heavy sorrow turn to joy; None would I envy, all would envy me, And happiness be mine without alloy.","얼룩덜룩한 시인 엘 도노소가 산초 판사와 로시난테에 대하여 산초에 대하여 나는 시종 산초 판—, 라 만—의 돈키호테를 섬겼노라."]],[["GANDALIN, SQUIRE OF AMADIS OF GAUL, To Sancho Panza, squire of Don Quixote","허나 그를 섬기는 일에서 물러나—, 남은 생을 신중히 보내기로 결심했네—. 시(枾)라 불린 비야디에—가"]],[["All hail, illustrious man! Fortune, when she Bound thee apprentice to the esquire trade, Her care and tenderness of thee displayed, Shaping thy course from misadventure free.","오직 물러남 속에서만— 잘 사는 비결이 있다고 주장했음이라, 「라 셀레스티—」에 따르면—."],["No longer now doth proud knight-errantry Regard with scorn the sickle and the spade; Of towering arrogance less count is made Than of plain esquire-like simplicity.","그것은 신성한 책이로되, 다만 죄가 되는— 내 생각에는 너무 노골적인 말투만 빼면—."],["I envy thee thy Dapple, and thy name, And those alforjas thou wast wont to stuff With comforts that thy providence proclaim. Excellent Sancho!","로시난테에 대하여 나는 저 유명한 로시난—, 위대한 바비에—의 증손, 오로지 야위고 뼈만 앙상한 탓에—, 돈키호테라는 자를 주인으로 두었노라—."],["hail to thee again! To thee alone the Ovid of our Spain Does homage with the rustic kiss and cuff.","허나 내가 허약함에서 그와 잘 어울렸다 해도—, 나는 결코 적은 먹이를 고분고분 받지 않았으니—, 훔쳐서라도 제 곡식을 챙겼노라—, 라사리—에게서 배운 수법이니—, 그가 짚 한 오라기를 그토록 깔끔히 써서— 눈먼 이의 포도주를 속여 먹었을 때 말일세—."]],[["On Sancho Panza and Rocinante","광란의 오를란도"]],[["# ON SANCHO","# ON SANCHO"]],[["I am the esquire Sancho Pan— Who served Don Quixote of La Man—; But from his service I retreat—, Resolved to pass my life discreet—; For Villadiego, called the Si—, Maintained that only in reti— Was found the secret of well-be—, According to the “Celesti—:”","라만차의 돈키호테에게 소네트 그대가 동료 기사가 아니라면, 그대에겐 견줄 이 없으리니, 천(千)의 동료 가운데서도 그대는 으뜸가는 동료라, 그대가 곁에 있을 때 다른 이가 들어설 자리 없으니, 패한 적 없는 승리자, 정복당하지 않은 위대한 이여!"],["A book divine, except for sin— By speech too plain, in my opin—","나는 안젤리카에게 무너진 오를란도라, 먼 바다를 건너도록 단죄받았고,"]],[["# ON ROCINANTE","# ON ROCINANTE"]],[["I am that Rocinante fa—, Great-grandson of great Babie—, Who, all for being lean and bon—, Had one Don Quixote for an own—; But if I matched him well in weak—, I never took short commons meek—, But kept myself in corn by steal—, A trick I learned from Lazaril—, When with a piece of straw so neat— The blind man of his wine he cheat—.","망각에게 존중받는 용맹을 명성의 제단에 제물로 바치러 가는 신세로다. 나는 그대의 경쟁자가 될 수 없으니, 그대의 명성과 무용이 모든 경쟁 위로 솟아 있음이라, 비록 우리 둘 다 정신을 잃은 채 떠도는 신세이나. 허나 스키타이인이나 무어인을 길들이는 것이 그대의 운명은 아니었어도, 그래도 그대는 나와 견주리니, 사랑이 우리를 비탄의 동지로 묶어 놓는구나."]],[["ORLANDO FURIOSO To Don Quixote of La Mancha","포이보스의 기사"]],[["If thou art not a Peer, peer thou hast none; Among a thousand Peers thou art a peer; Nor is there room for one when thou art near, Unvanquished victor, great unconquered one!","라만차의 돈키호테에게 내 칼은 그대의 것에 견줄 바가 아니었으니, 스페인의 포이보스여, 예절의 경이로움이여, 번개처럼 동에서 서로 내리치던 이 나의 손도 그대의 이름난 팔에는 견줄 바가 아니었네."],["Orlando, by Angelica undone, Am I; o’er distant seas condemned to steer, And to Fame’s altars as an offering bear Valour respected by Oblivion.","나는 모든 제국을 멸시했고, 장밋빛 동방이 내밀던 그 군주의 자리도 단념했노라, 클라리디아나의 눈길 한 번을 위하여, 내가 그 사랑에 애태우는 그 밝은 새벽의 여신을 위하여."],["I cannot be thy rival, for thy fame And prowess rise above all rivalry, Albeit both bereft of wits we go.","내 사랑은 한결같음의 기적이라, 그녀의 무자비한 잔혹함에 추방당하니, 이 팔은 지옥의 분노조차 길들일 힘을 지녔노라."],["But, though the Scythian or the Moor to tame Was not thy lot, still thou dost rival me: Love binds us in a fellowship of woe.","허나, 고트족의 키호테여, 그대가 더 행복함을 증명하나니,"]],[["To Don Quixote of La Mancha","그대는 둘시네아의 이름 속에 살아 있고,"]],[["My sword was not to be compared with thine Phœbus of Spain, marvel of courtesy, Nor with thy famous arm this hand of mine That smote from east to west as lightnings fly.","이름 높고 존경받고 현명한 그녀가 그대 안에 살아 있음이라. 솔리스단으로부터 라만차의 돈키호테에게 소네트 키호테 경이여, 그대의 망상들이 그대의 미친 머리를 온통 뒤집어 놓았음은 사실이오, 허나 비천하거나 옹졸한 그 무엇도 아직껏 그 누구도 그대를 비난하여 들씌운 적이 없소이다."],["I scorned all empire, and that monarchy The rosy east held out did I resign For one glance of Claridiana’s eye, The bright Aurora for whose love I pine. A miracle of constancy my love; And banished by her ruthless cruelty, This arm had might the rage of Hell to tame.","그대의 행적은 만인의 눈앞에 펼쳐진 명백한 증거이니, 그대는 불의를 없애려 길을 나섰고, 그 수고의 대가로 비열하고 무뢰한 무리에게서 호된 몽둥이찜질을 숱하게 받았소이다."],["But, Gothic Quixote, happier thou dost prove, For thou dost live in Dulcinea’s name, And famous, honoured, wise, she lives in thee.","그대 마음의 여왕인 아리따운 둘시네아가 그 잔혹함에 누그러지지 않는다 해도,"]],[["FROM SOLISDAN To Don Quixote of La Mancha","그대의 비탄이 여전히 그녀를 움직일 힘이 없다 해도,"]],[["Your fantasies, Sir Quixote, it is true, That crazy brain of yours have quite upset, But aught of base or mean hath never yet Been charged by any in reproach to you. Your deeds are open proof in all men’s view; For you went forth injustice to abate, And for your pains sore drubbings did you get From many a rascally and ruffian crew. If the fair Dulcinea, your heart’s queen, Be unrelenting in her cruelty, If still your woe be powerless to move her, In such hard case your comfort let it be That Sancho was a sorry go-between: A booby he, hard-hearted she, and you no lover.","그토록 딱한 처지에서 이것을 그대의 위안으로 삼으시오. 산초는 시원찮은 중매쟁이였고, 그는 멍청이요 그녀는 모진 여인이며 그대는 연인답지 못했음을. 대화 바비에카와 로시난테 사이의 소네트 바: \"어찌 된 일인가, 로시난테, 그대 이리 야위었으니?\" 로: \"먹이는 부족하고, 일에 시달려 닳아빠졌소.\" 바: \"허나 그 많은 건초와 곡식은 다 어찌 되었누?\" 로: \"주인이 내게 하나도 주지 않으니, 너무 인색하오.\" 바: \"이보시오, 그대 본데없는 짓을 보이는구려, 당나귀처럼 제 주인을 그리 멸시하다니.\" 로: \"그는 당나귀요, 당나귀로 죽을 것이며, 당나귀로 태어났소."]],[["DIALOGUE Between Babieca and Rocinante","뭐냐 하면, 그가 사랑에 빠졌으니, 이보다 더 뻔한 게 어디 있겠소?\""]],[["B. “How comes it, Rocinante, you’re so lean?” R. “I’m underfed, with overwork I’m worn.” B. “But what becomes of all the hay and corn?” R. “My master gives me none; he’s much too mean.” B. “Come, come, you show ill-breeding, sir, I ween; ’Tis like an ass your master thus to scorn.” R. He is an ass, will die an ass, an ass was born; Why, he’s in love; what’s plainer to be seen?” B. “To be in love is folly?”—R. “No great sense.” B. “You’re metaphysical.”—R. “From want of food.” B. “Rail at the squire, then.”—R. “Why, what’s the good? I might indeed complain of him, I grant ye, But, squire or master, where’s the difference? They’re both as sorry hacks as Rocinante.”","바: \"사랑에 빠짐이 어리석은 짓이오?\"—로: \"그리 대단한 분별은 아니지요.\" 바: \"그대 형이상학을 논하는구려.\"—로: \"먹을 게 없어서라오.\" 바: \"그럼 그 시종을 욕하시오.\"—로: \"원, 그래 봐야 무슨 소용이오? 그를 두고 불평할 수야 있겠소만, 인정하리다, 허나 시종이든 주인이든, 무슨 차이가 있겠소? 둘 다 로시난테만큼이나 시원찮은 말이거늘.\" 작가의 서문"]],[["# THE AUTHOR’S PREFACE","# THE AUTHOR’S PREFACE"]],[["Idle reader: thou mayest believe me without any oath that I would this book, as it is the child of my brain, were the fairest, gayest, and cleverest that could be imagined.","한가한 독자여, 이 책이 내 머릿속의 자식인 만큼, 상상할 수 있는 가장 아름답고 가장 유쾌하며 가장 영리한 책이기를 내가 바랐으리라는 것은 굳이 맹세하지 않아도 그대가 믿어 주리라."],["But I could not counteract Nature’s law that everything shall beget its like; and what, then, could this sterile, illtilled wit of mine beget but the story of a dry, shrivelled, whimsical offspring, full of thoughts of all sorts and such as never came into any other imagination--just what might be begotten in a prison, where every misery is lodged and every doleful sound makes its dwelling?","그러나 모든 것은 제 닮은 것을 낳는다는 자연의 법칙을 나로서는 거스를 수 없었으니, 그렇다면 이 메마르고 변변히 갈지 못한 내 재능이 낳을 수 있는 것이라곤, 온갖 종류의 생각으로 가득 찬, 그 어떤 다른 상상에도 결코 떠오른 적 없는, 메마르고 쪼그라들고 별난 자식의 이야기 말고 무엇이 있겠는가—온갖 비참함이 깃들고 온갖 구슬픈 소리가 거처를 삼는 감옥에서나 잉태될 법한 바로 그런 것 말일세."],["Tranquillity, a cheerful retreat, pleasant fields, bright skies, murmuring brooks, peace of mind, these are the things that go far to make even the most barren muses fertile, and bring into the world births that fill it with wonder and delight.","평온함, 즐거운 안식처, 쾌적한 들판, 밝은 하늘, 졸졸 흐르는 시내, 마음의 평화, 이런 것들이야말로 가장 척박한 뮤즈마저 비옥하게 만들고, 세상을 경이와 기쁨으로 채우는 자식들을 세상에 내놓게 하는 것이라네."],["Sometimes when a father has an ugly, loutish son, the love he bears him so blindfolds his eyes that he does not see his defects, or, rather, takes them for gifts and charms of mind and body, and talks of them to his friends as wit and grace.","때때로 아비가 못생기고 미련한 아들을 두면, 그를 향한 사랑이 그 눈을 가려 결점이 보이지 않게 하거나, 오히려 그것을 정신과 육체의 재능이며 매력으로 여겨, 벗들에게 그것을 기지요 우아함이라 떠벌리기도 하는 법일세."],["I, however--for though I pass for the father, I am but the stepfather to “Don Quixote”--have no desire to go with the current of custom, or to implore thee, dearest reader, almost with tears in my eyes, as others do, to pardon or excuse the defects thou wilt perceive in this child of mine.","그러나 나는—비록 아비로 통하지만 「돈키호테」의 의붓아비에 지나지 않으므로—관습의 흐름을 따를 마음도, 다른 이들이 그러하듯 거의 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며, 이 내 자식에게서 그대가 보게 될 결점을 용서하거나 너그러이 봐 달라고 그대에게, 더없이 소중한 독자여, 애원할 마음도 없네."],["Thou art neither its kinsman nor its friend, thy soul is thine own and thy will as free as any man’s, whate’er he be, thou art in thine own house and master of it as much as the king of his taxes and thou knowest the common saying, “Under my cloak I kill the king;”","그대는 이 책의 친척도 벗도 아니요, 그대의 영혼은 그대 자신의 것이며 그대의 의지는 그 누구든 어떤 사람 못지않게 자유로우니, 그대는 그대 자신의 집 안에 있어 마치 임금이 제 세금의 주인이듯 그 집의 주인이며, \"내 망토 아래에서는 임금도 죽일 수 있다\"는 흔한 속담도 알고 있으렷다."],["all which exempts and frees thee from every consideration and obligation, and thou canst say what thou wilt of the story without fear of being abused for any ill or rewarded for any good thou mayest say of it.","이 모든 것이 그대를 온갖 고려와 의무에서 면제해 주고 풀어 주니, 그대는 이 이야기를 두고 무엇이든 그대 좋을 대로 말할 수 있으며, 험담을 한대서 욕먹을 두려움도, 좋은 말을 한대서 상을 받을 기대도 없으렷다."]],[["My wish would be simply to present it to thee plain and unadorned, without any embellishment of preface or uncountable muster of customary sonnets, epigrams, and eulogies, such as are commonly put at the beginning of books.","내 바람이라면 그저 이 책을, 서문의 그 어떤 꾸밈도 없이, 책의 첫머리에 흔히 놓이는 관습적인 소네트며 경구며 찬사를 헤아릴 수 없이 늘어세우는 일도 없이, 그대에게 꾸밈없이 그대로 내놓는 것이라네."],["For I can tell thee, though composing it cost me some labour, I found none greater than the making of this Preface thou art now reading.","사실 이 책을 짓는 데 어느 정도 수고가 들기는 했으나, 지금 그대가 읽고 있는 이 서문을 짓는 것보다 더 큰 수고는 없었음을 그대에게 일러둘 수 있네."],["Many times did I take up my pen to write it, and many did I lay it down again, not knowing what to write.","이것을 쓰려고 여러 번 붓을 들었다가, 무엇을 써야 할지 몰라 여러 번 다시 내려놓았다네."],["One of these times, as I was pondering with the paper before me, a pen in my ear, my elbow on the desk, and my cheek in my hand, thinking of what I should say, there came in unexpectedly a certain lively, clever friend of mine, who, seeing me so deep in thought, asked the reason; to which I, making no mystery of it, answered that I was thinking of the Preface I had to make for the story of “Don Quixote,”","그러던 어느 날, 종이를 앞에 놓고 펜을 귀에 꽂고 팔꿈치를 책상에 괴고 뺨을 손에 받친 채, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 골똘히 생각하고 있노라니, 뜻밖에 활달하고 영리한 어느 벗이 들어왔는데, 내가 그토록 깊은 생각에 잠긴 것을 보고 그 까닭을 물었네. 그래서 나는 굳이 감추지 않고, 「돈키호테」"],["which so troubled me that I had a mind not to make any at all, nor even publish the achievements of so noble a knight.","이야기에 붙여야 할 서문을 생각하고 있었는데, 그것이 어찌나 골치 아픈지 아예 서문 같은 건 짓지 않을 마음이요, 그토록 고귀한 기사의 위업을 세상에 내놓지 않을 마음마저 들었노라고 대답했네."]],[["“For, how could you expect me not to feel uneasy about what that ancient lawgiver they call the Public will say when it sees me, after slumbering so many years in the silence of oblivion, coming out now with all my years upon my back, and with a book as dry as a rush, devoid of invention, meagre in style, poor in thoughts, wholly wanting in learning and wisdom, without quotations in the margin or annotations at the end, after the fashion of other books I see, which, though all fables and profanity, are so full of maxims from Aristotle, and Plato, and the whole herd of philosophers, that they fill the readers with amazement and convince them that the authors are men of learning, erudition, and eloquence.","\"이보게, 사람들이 대중이라 부르는 저 오래된 입법자가, 내가 그토록 여러 해 동안 망각의 침묵 속에 잠들어 있다가, 이제 와서 등에 온 세월을 짊어진 채, 골풀처럼 메마르고 창의라곤 없으며 문체는 빈약하고 사상은 보잘것없으며 학식과 지혜라곤 도무지 찾아볼 수 없고, 여백에 인용도 없고 끝에 주석도 없는 책을 들고 나오는 꼴을 본다면 무어라 할지, 내가 어찌 마음 졸이지 않겠는가? 내가 보는 다른 책들은 죄다 꾸며 낸 이야기와 속된 것들뿐이면서도, 아리스토텔레스며 플라톤이며 철학자 무리 전체의 격언으로 가득 차 있어, 독자들을 경탄케 하고 그 저자들이 학식과 박식과 웅변을 갖춘 사람들이라 확신케 하는데 말일세."],["And then, when they quote the Holy Scriptures!--anyone would say they are St. Thomases or other doctors of the Church, observing as they do a decorum so ingenious that in one sentence they describe a distracted lover and in the next deliver a devout little sermon that it is a pleasure and a treat to hear and read.","게다가 그들이 성경을 인용하기라도 하면!—누구든 그들을 성 토마스나 그 밖의 교회 박사들이라 하겠으니, 어찌나 교묘하게 격식을 차리는지, 한 문장에서는 실연한 연인을 그려 내고 다음 문장에서는 듣고 읽기에 즐겁고 흐뭇한 경건한 작은 설교를 늘어놓는다네."],["Of all this there will be nothing in my book, for I have nothing to quote in the margin or to note at the end, and still less do I know what authors I follow in it, to place them at the beginning, as all do, under the letters A, B, C, beginning with Aristotle and ending with Xenophon, or Zoilus, or Zeuxis, though one was a slanderer and the other a painter.","이 모든 것이 내 책에는 하나도 없을 터이니, 나는 여백에 인용할 것도 끝에 주석으로 달 것도 없거니와, 모두가 그러하듯 첫머리에 A, B, C 차례로 아리스토텔레스에서 시작하여 크세노폰이나 조일루스나 제욱시스로 끝맺도록 늘어놓을, 내가 따른 작가가 누구인지조차 더더욱 알지 못하니 말일세. 한쪽은 비방가요 다른 한쪽은 화가였는데도 말이야."],["Also my book must do without sonnets at the beginning, at least sonnets whose authors are dukes, marquises, counts, bishops, ladies, or famous poets.","또한 내 책은 첫머리의 소네트 없이 견뎌야 하니, 적어도 공작이나 후작이나 백작이나 주교나 귀부인이나 이름난 시인을 지은이로 둔 소네트는 없을 걸세."],["Though if I were to ask two or three obliging friends, I know they would give me them, and such as the productions of those that have the highest reputation in our Spain could not equal.","하기야 친절한 벗 두엇이나 서넛에게 부탁한다면, 그들이 내게 그런 것들을 주리라는 걸 나도 아네, 그것도 우리 스페인에서 가장 드높은 명성을 지닌 이들의 작품도 따르지 못할 만한 것들로 말일세."]],[["“In short, my friend,”","\"한마디로 말하자면, 이보게,\" 나는 말을 이었네."],["I continued, “I am determined that Señor Don Quixote shall remain buried in the archives of his own La Mancha until Heaven provide some one to garnish him with all those things he stands in need of; because I find myself, through my shallowness and want of learning, unequal to supplying them, and because I am by nature shy and careless about hunting for authors to say what I myself can say without them.","\"내가 결심한 바는, 돈키호테 나리께서 그분께 필요한 그 모든 것으로 치장해 줄 누군가를 하늘이 마련해 주실 때까지, 그저 그분 자신의 라만차의 문서고 속에 묻혀 계시도록 하겠다는 것일세. 나는 내 천박함과 학식의 부족으로 그것들을 갖추어 드리기에 미치지 못함을 깨닫고 있고, 또 천성이 수줍고 게을러서 나 스스로 그들 없이도 할 수 있는 말을 하려고 작가들을 뒤지고 다니는 일에 무심하기 때문일세."],["Hence the cogitation and abstraction you found me in, and reason enough, what you have heard from me.”","그래서 자네가 본 그 골똘함과 망연함에 내가 빠져 있었던 게야, 자네가 내게서 들은 것만으로도 그럴 만한 충분한 까닭이 되지 않겠나.\""]],[["Hearing this, my friend, giving himself a slap on the forehead and breaking into a hearty laugh, exclaimed, “Before God, Brother, now am I disabused of an error in which I have been living all this long time I have known you, all through which I have taken you to be shrewd and sensible in all you do; but now I see you are as far from that as the heaven is from the earth.","이 말을 듣더니 내 벗은 제 이마를 탁 치며 호탕하게 웃음을 터뜨리고는 이렇게 외쳤네. \"맙소사, 이 사람아, 자네를 알고 지낸 그 오랜 세월 내내 내가 빠져 있던 한 가지 오해에서 이제야 벗어났구먼."],["Is it possible that things of so little moment and so easy to set right can occupy and perplex a ripe wit like yours, fit to break through and crush far greater obstacles?","그동안 내내 나는 자네가 하는 모든 일에서 자네를 명민하고 분별 있는 사람으로 여겨 왔는데, 이제 보니 자네가 그것과는 하늘이 땅에서 먼 만큼이나 멀구먼. 그토록 하찮고 바로잡기도 쉬운 일이, 훨씬 더 큰 장애물도 뚫고 부술 만한 자네 같은 무르익은 재능을 사로잡고 쩔쩔매게 한다니, 이게 될 법한 일인가?"],["By my faith, this comes, not of any want of ability, but of too much indolence and too little knowledge of life.","정말이지, 이건 능력이 모자라서가 아니라, 게으름이 지나치고 세상 물정에 너무 어두운 탓일세."],["Do you want to know if I am telling the truth?","내가 진실을 말하고 있는지 알고 싶은가?"],["Well, then, attend to me, and you will see how, in the opening and shutting of an eye, I sweep away all your difficulties, and supply all those deficiencies which you say check and discourage you from bringing before the world the story of your famous Don Quixote, the light and mirror of all knight-errantry.”","그렇다면 내 말을 들어 보게나, 그러면 눈 한 번 떴다 감을 사이에 내가 어떻게 자네의 온갖 어려움을 쓸어 없애고, 자네의 이름난 돈키호테, 곧 모든 편력 기사도의 빛이요 거울인 그 이야기를 세상에 내놓는 것을 가로막고 주저하게 만든다는 그 모든 부족함을 채워 주는지 보게 될 걸세.\""],["“Say on,”","\"어디 말해 보게,\""],["said I, listening to his talk; “how do you propose to make up for my diffidence, and reduce to order this chaos of perplexity I am in?”","나는 그의 말에 귀를 기울이며 말했네. \"어떻게 내 망설임을 메우고, 내가 빠져 있는 이 혼란의 아수라장을 정돈하겠다는 건가?\""]],[["To which he made answer, “Your first difficulty about the sonnets, epigrams, or complimentary verses which you want for the beginning, and which ought to be by persons of importance and rank, can be removed if you yourself take a little trouble to make them; you can afterwards baptise them, and put any name you like to them, fathering them on Prester John of the Indies or the Emperor of Trebizond, who, to my knowledge, were said to have been famous poets: and even if they were not, and any pedants or bachelors should attack you and question the fact, never care two maravedis for that, for even if they prove a lie against you they cannot cut off the hand you wrote it with.","이에 그가 대답하기를, \"첫째로 자네가 첫머리에 두고 싶어 하는, 마땅히 신분 높고 지체 있는 이들이 지어야 할 그 소네트며 경구며 찬사의 시들에 관한 어려움은, 자네가 직접 약간의 수고를 들여 그것들을 짓는다면 없어질 걸세. 그런 다음 거기에 세례를 주듯 자네 좋을 대로 아무 이름이나 붙이고, 인도의 사제왕 요한이나 트레비손드의 황제를 그 지은이로 내세우면 되네. 내가 알기로 그들은 이름난 시인이었다고들 했으니 말일세. 설령 그들이 시인이 아니었다 하더라도, 그래서 어떤 현학자나 학사들이 자네를 공격하며 그 사실에 시비를 걸더라도, 그런 건 마라베디 두 푼어치도 신경 쓰지 말게. 그들이 자네에게 거짓이라는 걸 입증한다 해도, 자네가 그것을 쓴 손을 잘라 낼 수는 없을 테니 말일세. * 마라베디: 옛 스페인의 동전으로, 매우 적은 액수를 가리킬 때 쓰였다."]],[["“As to references in the margin to the books and authors from whom you take the aphorisms and sayings you put into your story, it is only contriving to fit in nicely any sentences or scraps of Latin you may happen to have by heart, or at any rate that will not give you much trouble to look up; so as, when you speak of freedom and captivity, to insert\nNon bene pro toto libertas venditur auro;","\"자네가 이야기 속에 집어넣는 경구와 명언을 가져온 책과 작가를 여백에 표시하는 일로 말하자면, 그저 자네가 어쩌다 외고 있거나 적어도 찾아보는 데 그리 수고롭지 않을 라틴어 문구나 토막들을 알맞게 끼워 넣도록 궁리하기만 하면 되네. 그러니 이를테면 자유와 구속을 이야기할 때면 이렇게 넣는 거지. 자유는 온 세상의 황금을 다 주고도 잘 팔리지 않는 법."]],[["and then refer in the margin to Horace, or whoever said it; or, if you allude to the power of death, to come in with--","그러고는 여백에 호라티우스, 혹은 그 말을 한 자가 누구든 그 이름을 표시하면 되네. 혹은 죽음의 위력을 암시하려거든 이렇게 들이대는 거야—"]],[["Pallida mors Aequo pulsat pede pauperum tabernas, Regumque turres.","창백한 죽음은 공평한 발걸음으로 가난한 자의 오두막과\n임금의 탑을 두드리노라."]],[["“If it be friendship and the love God bids us bear to our enemy, go at once to the Holy Scriptures, which you can do with a very small amount of research, and quote no less than the words of God himself: Ego autem dico vobis: diligite inimicos vestros.","\"만일 그것이 우정과, 신께서 우리에게 명하신, 원수를 사랑하라는 그 사랑에 관한 것이라면, 곧장 성경으로 가게나. 아주 약간만 찾아보면 될 일이고, 다름 아닌 하느님 자신의 말씀을 인용할 수 있으니 말일세."],["If you speak of evil thoughts, turn to the Gospel: De corde exeunt cogitationes malae.","'그러나 나는 너희에게 이르노니, 너희 원수를 사랑하라.' 만일 자네가 악한 생각을 이야기한다면, 복음서로 가게나."],["If of the fickleness of friends, there is Cato, who will give you his distich:","'마음에서 악한 생각들이 나오느니라.' 벗들의 변덕스러움을 이야기한다면, 카토가 있어 자네에게 그의 대구(對句)를 줄 걸세."]],[["Donec eris felix multos numerabis amicos, Tempora si fuerint nubila, solus eris.","그대가 행복할 동안에는 숱한 벗을 헤아리려니와,"]],[["“With these and such like bits of Latin they will take you for a grammarian at all events, and that now-a-days is no small honour and profit.","세월이 흐려지면 그대 홀로 남으리라. \"이런 라틴어 토막들로 그들은 어쨌든 자네를 문법가로 여길 터이니, 요즘 세상에 그것은 적잖은 명예요 이득이라네."]],[["“With regard to adding annotations at the end of the book, you may safely do it in this way.","\"책 끝에 주석을 다는 일로 말하자면, 자네는 이런 식으로 안심하고 해도 좋네."],["If you mention any giant in your book contrive that it shall be the giant Goliath, and with this alone, which will cost you almost nothing, you have a grand note, for you can put--The giant Golias or Goliath was a Philistine whom the shepherd David slew by a mighty stone-cast in the Terebinth valley, as is related in the Book of Kings--in the chapter where you find it written.","자네 책에 거인을 하나라도 언급하거든, 그것이 골리앗이라는 거인이 되도록 꾸미게. 그러면 거의 아무 비용도 들지 않을 이것 하나만으로도 자네는 멋들어진 주석을 얻게 되니, 이렇게 적을 수 있음이라—골리아스, 혹은 골리앗이라는 거인은, 양치기 다윗이 테레빈 골짜기에서 힘찬 돌팔매질로 죽인 블레셋 사람이니, 「열왕기」에 적힌 바와 같다—그것이 적혀 있는 장(章)을 찾아 적으면 되네."]],[["“Next, to prove yourself a man of erudition in polite literature and cosmography, manage that the river Tagus shall be named in your story, and there you are at once with another famous annotation, setting forth--The river Tagus was so called after a King of Spain: it has its source in such and such a place and falls into the ocean, kissing the walls of the famous city of Lisbon, and it is a common belief that it has golden sands, etc. If you should have anything to do with robbers, I will give you the story of Cacus, for I have it by heart; if with loose women, there is the Bishop of Mondonedo, who will give you the loan of Lamia, Laida, and Flora, any reference to whom will bring you great credit; if with hard-hearted ones, Ovid will furnish you with Medea; if with witches or enchantresses, Homer has Calypso, and Virgil Circe; if with valiant captains, Julius Caesar himself will lend you himself in his own ‘Commentaries,’ and Plutarch will give you a thousand Alexanders. If you should deal with love, with two ounces you may know of Tuscan you can go to Leon the Hebrew, who will supply you to your heart’s content; or if you should not care to go to foreign countries you have at home Fonseca’s ‘Of the Love of God,’ in which is condensed all that you or the most imaginative mind can want on the subject. In short, all you have to do is to manage to quote these names, or refer to these stories I have mentioned, and leave it to me to insert the annotations and quotations, and I swear by all that’s good to fill your margins and use up four sheets at the end of the book.","\"다음으로, 자네가 교양 있는 문학과 우주지리학에 박식한 사람임을 증명하려거든, 자네 이야기에 타호강이 이름을 올리도록 손을 쓰게. 그러면 곧장 또 하나의 이름난 주석을 얻게 되니, 이렇게 펼쳐 적는 거지—타호강은 스페인의 어느 임금의 이름을 따서 그리 불렸으며, 그 발원지는 이러이러한 곳이요 대양으로 흘러들어 가, 이름난 리스본 도시의 성벽에 입 맞추며, 그 모래가 황금이라는 것이 통설이다, 운운. 자네가 도둑들과 얽힐 일이 있거든, 내가 카쿠스의 이야기를 줄 터이니, 그건 내가 외고 있다네. 헤픈 여자들과 얽힐 일이 있거든, 몬도녜도의 주교가 있어 자네에게 라미아와 라이다와 플로라를 빌려줄 터이니, 그들 누구를 언급하든 자네에게 큰 명성을 가져다줄 걸세. 모진 마음을 지닌 여자들과 얽힐 일이라면, 오비디우스가 자네에게 메데이아를 마련해 줄 것이요, 마녀나 마법사들과 얽힐 일이라면, 호메로스에게 칼립소가 있고 베르길리우스에게 키르케가 있다네. 용맹한 장수들과 얽힐 일이라면, 율리우스 카이사르 자신이 그의 「갈리아 전기」에서 자기 자신을 자네에게 빌려줄 것이요, 플루타르코스가 자네에게 천 명의 알렉산드로스를 줄 걸세. 자네가 사랑을 다룬다면, 토스카나 말을 두어 온스만 알아도 헤브라이오스 레온에게 갈 수 있으니, 그가 자네 마음껏 채워 줄 걸세. 혹은 외국까지 가고 싶지 않다면, 자네 집안에 폰세카의 「하느님의 사랑에 관하여」가 있어, 그 주제에 관해 자네나 가장 상상력 풍부한 정신이 바랄 수 있는 모든 것이 거기 응축되어 있다네. 한마디로 자네가 해야 할 일이라곤 이 이름들을 인용하도록, 혹은 내가 말한 이 이야기들을 언급하도록 손을 쓰는 것뿐이고, 주석과 인용을 끼워 넣는 일은 내게 맡기게. 자네 여백을 가득 채우고 책 끝에 넉 장을 다 써 버리겠노라고, 좋은 것 모두를 걸고 맹세하네."]],[["“Now let us come to those references to authors which other books have, and you want for yours. The remedy for this is very simple: You have only to look out for some book that quotes them all, from A to Z as you say yourself, and then insert the very same alphabet in your book, and though the imposition may be plain to see, because you have so little need to borrow from them, that is no matter; there will probably be some simple enough to believe that you have made use of them all in this plain, artless story of yours. At any rate, if it answers no other purpose, this long catalogue of authors will serve to give a surprising look of authority to your book. Besides, no one will trouble himself to verify whether you have followed them or whether you have not, being no way concerned in it; especially as, if I mistake not, this book of yours has no need of any one of those things you say it wants, for it is, from beginning to end, an attack upon the books of chivalry, of which Aristotle never dreamt, nor St. Basil said a word, nor Cicero had any knowledge; nor do the niceties of truth nor the observations of astrology come within the range of its fanciful vagaries; nor have geometrical measurements or refutations of the arguments used in rhetoric anything to do with it; nor does it mean to preach to anybody, mixing up things human and divine, a sort of motley in which no Christian understanding should dress itself. It has only to avail itself of truth to nature in its composition, and the more perfect the imitation the better the work will be. And as this piece of yours aims at nothing more than to destroy the authority and influence which books of chivalry have in the world and with the public, there is no need for you to go a-begging for aphorisms from philosophers, precepts from Holy Scripture, fables from poets, speeches from orators, or miracles from saints; but merely to take care that your style and diction run musically, pleasantly, and plainly, with clear, proper, and well-placed words, setting forth your purpose to the best of your power, and putting your ideas intelligibly, without confusion or obscurity. Strive, too, that in reading your story the melancholy may be moved to laughter, and the merry made merrier still; that the simple shall not be wearied, that the judicious shall admire the invention, that the grave shall not despise it, nor the wise fail to praise it. Finally, keep your aim fixed on the destruction of that ill-founded edifice of the books of chivalry, hated by some and praised by many more; for if you succeed in this you will have achieved no small success.”","\"이제 다른 책들에는 있고 자네 책에도 두고 싶어 하는 그 작가 참조 목록으로 와 보세. 이에 대한 해법은 아주 간단하네. 자네가 직접 말한 대로 A에서 Z까지 그들을 죄다 인용하는 책 하나를 찾아내기만 하면 되네. 그러고 나서 바로 그 알파벳 목록을 자네 책에 옮겨 넣는 거지. 자네가 그들에게서 빌릴 필요가 그토록 적으니 그 속임수가 빤히 들여다보일지라도, 그건 아무래도 좋아. 자네가 이 꾸밈없고 소박한 이야기 속에서 그들 모두를 활용했다고 믿어 줄 만큼 단순한 이들이 아마 있을 테니 말일세. 어쨌든 다른 쓸모가 없다 해도, 이 긴 작가 목록은 자네 책에 놀라우리만치 권위 있어 보이는 풍모를 더해 주는 데는 쓸모가 있을 걸세. 게다가 자네가 그들을 따랐는지 안 따랐는지 굳이 확인해 보려고 수고할 사람은 아무도 없네. 거기 얽힐 일이 전혀 없으니 말일세. 더구나, 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 이 자네 책은 자네가 부족하다고 말한 그것들 가운데 어느 하나도 필요로 하지 않네. 처음부터 끝까지 그것은 기사도 책들에 대한 공격이니, 아리스토텔레스는 꿈에도 생각지 못했고 성 바실리오는 한마디도 하지 않았으며 키케로는 알지도 못한 것이라네. 또한 진리의 정밀함도, 점성술의 관찰도 그 공상적인 갈팡질팡 속에 들어설 자리가 없고, 기하학적 측정이나 수사학에서 쓰이는 논증의 반박도 그것과는 아무 상관이 없네. 또한 그것은 인간적인 것과 신적인 것을 뒤섞어, 그 어떤 그리스도교적 지성도 차려입어서는 안 될 일종의 얼룩덜룩한 옷을 입고 누구에게도 설교하려 들지 않네. 그것은 그저 그 구성에서 자연에 대한 충실함을 활용하기만 하면 되고, 모방이 완벽할수록 작품도 그만큼 더 나아질 걸세. 그리고 이 자네 작품이 기사도 책들이 세상에서, 그리고 대중에게서 지닌 권위와 영향력을 무너뜨리는 것 말고는 아무것도 노리지 않으니, 자네는 철학자들에게서 경구를, 성경에서 가르침을, 시인들에게서 우화를, 웅변가들에게서 연설을, 성인들에게서 기적을 구걸하러 다닐 필요가 없네. 그저 자네의 문체와 어법이 음악처럼, 유쾌하게, 평이하게 흐르도록, 명료하고 적확하며 제자리에 놓인 말들로 자네의 뜻을 힘껏 펼쳐 보이고, 혼란이나 모호함 없이 자네 생각을 알아듣기 쉽게 풀어내도록 마음 쓰기만 하면 되네. 또한 자네 이야기를 읽으며 우울한 이는 웃음에 이끌리고, 즐거운 이는 더욱 즐거워지도록, 단순한 이는 지루해하지 않고, 분별 있는 이는 그 창의에 감탄하며, 진중한 이는 그것을 멸시하지 않고, 지혜로운 이는 그것을 칭찬하기를 마지않도록 애쓰게. 끝으로, 어떤 이들은 미워하고 그보다 더 많은 이들은 칭송하는, 그 토대가 부실한 기사도 책들의 건물을 무너뜨리는 데 자네의 겨냥을 단단히 고정하게나. 이 일에 자네가 성공한다면, 적잖은 성취를 이룬 셈이 될 테니 말일세.\""]],[["In profound silence I listened to what my friend said, and his observations made such an impression on me that, without attempting to question them, I admitted their soundness, and out of them I determined to make this Preface; wherein, gentle reader, thou wilt perceive my friend’s good sense, my good fortune in finding such an adviser in such a time of need, and what thou hast gained in receiving, without addition or alteration, the story of the famous Don Quixote of La Mancha, who is held by all the inhabitants of the district of the Campo de Montiel to have been the chastest lover and the bravest knight that has for many years been seen in that neighbourhood.","내 벗이 한 말을 깊은 침묵 속에서 들었는데, 그 견해가 어찌나 깊은 인상을 남겼던지, 나는 그것에 시비를 걸어 볼 엄두도 내지 않고 그 타당함을 인정했으며, 그것들로부터 이 서문을 짓기로 결심했다네. 점잖은 독자여, 그대는 여기서 내 벗의 훌륭한 분별을, 그토록 절박한 때에 그런 조언자를 만난 나의 행운을, 그리고 이름난 라만차의 돈키호테 이야기를—더하거나 고친 것 없이—그대로 받음으로써 그대가 얻은 것을 알아보게 되리라."],["I have no desire to magnify the service I render thee in making thee acquainted with so renowned and honoured a knight, but I do desire thy thanks for the acquaintance thou wilt make with the famous Sancho Panza, his squire, in whom, to my thinking, I have given thee condensed all the squirely drolleries that are scattered through the swarm of the vain books of chivalry.","그분으로 말하자면, 캄포 데 몬티엘 지방의 모든 주민이 여러 해 동안 그 인근에서 본 가장 정결한 연인이자 가장 용감한 기사였다고 입을 모은다네. 그토록 이름 높고 존귀한 기사와 그대를 사귀게 해 주는 데 내가 베푸는 봉사를 부풀릴 마음은 없으나, 그분의 시종인 그 이름난 산초 판사와 사귀게 됨에 대해서만은 그대의 감사를 바라네."],["And so--may God give thee health, and not forget me.","내 생각에 나는 그 안에, 그 부질없는 기사도 책 무리 속에 흩어져 있는 온갖 시종다운 익살을 응축하여 그대에게 안겨 주었으니 말일세. 그러니—하느님께서 그대에게 건강을 주시기를, 그리고 나를 잊지 마시기를."],["Vale.","잘 있게나."]],[["TO THE DUKE OF BEJAR, MARQUIS OF GIBRALEON, COUNT OF BENALCAZAR AND BANARES, VICECOUNT OF THE PUEBLA DE ALCOCER, MASTER OF THE TOWNS OF CAPILLA, CURIEL AND BURGUILLOS","* 잘 있게나(Vale): 라틴어로 작별 인사이다. 제1부 헌사"]],[["In belief of the good reception and honours that Your Excellency bestows on all sort of books, as prince so inclined to favor good arts, chiefly those who by their nobleness do not submit to the service and bribery of the vulgar, I have determined bringing to light The Ingenious Gentleman Don Quixote of la Mancha, in shelter of Your Excellency’s glamorous name, to whom, with the obeisance I owe to such grandeur, I pray to receive it agreeably under his protection, so that in this shadow, though deprived of that precious ornament of elegance and erudition that clothe the works composed in the houses of those who know, it dares appear with assurance in the judgment of some who, trespassing the bounds of their own ignorance, use to condemn with more rigour and less justice the writings of others.","베하르 공작이시며, 히브랄레온 후작이시며, 베날카사르와 바냐레스의 백작이시며, 푸에블라 데 알코세르의 자작이시며, 카피야와 쿠리엘과 부르기요스 영지의 영주이신 분께 각하께서 온갖 종류의 책에 베푸시는 호의로운 영접과 영예를, 좋은 학예에, 특히 그 고귀함으로 인해 비속한 자들의 비위 맞추기와 매수에 굴하지 않는 책들에 그토록 기우신 군주이심을 믿사옵고, 소인은 각하의 빛나는 존명의 비호 아래 『기발한 이달고 라만차의 돈키호테』를 세상에 내놓기로 결심하였사옵니다. 그 위대하심에 마땅히 바쳐야 할 경의를 다하여, 각하께서 그것을 기꺼이 그 보호 아래 받아 주시기를 비오니, 이로써 이 그늘 속에서, 비록 식견 있는 이들의 집에서 지어진 작품들을 입히는 그 우아함과 박식이라는 값진 장식은 빼앗겼을지언정, 제 무지의 경계를 넘어 남의 글을 더 가혹하고 덜 정의롭게 단죄하기 일쑤인 몇몇 이들의 심판 앞에서도 확신을 가지고 감히 나설 수 있게 되기를 바라옵니다."],["It is my earnest hope that Your Excellency’s good counsel in regard to my honourable purpose, will not disdain the littleness of so humble a service.","소인의 명예로운 뜻에 대한 각하의 어진 조언이 이토록 보잘것없는 봉사의 작음을 업신여기지 않으시기를 간절히 바라옵나이다."]],[["Miguel de Cervantes","미겔 데 세르반테스"]],[["# CHAPTER I. WHICH TREATS OF THE CHARACTER AND PURSUITS OF THE FAMOUS GENTLEMAN DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA","# CHAPTER I."]],[["In a village of La Mancha, the name of which I have no desire to call to mind, there lived not long since one of those gentlemen that keep a lance in the lance-rack, an old buckler, a lean hack, and a greyhound for coursing.","라만차의 어느 마을, 그 이름은 굳이 떠올리고 싶지 않은 그곳에 그리 오래지 않은 옛날 한 시골 양반이 살고 있었으니, 창걸이에는 창을 꽂아 두고, 오래된 방패와 비쩍 마른 말 한 필, 그리고 사냥용 그레이하운드 한 마리를 거느린 그런 부류의 사람이었다."],["An olla of rather more beef than mutton, a salad on most nights, scraps on Saturdays, lentils on Fridays, and a pigeon or so extra on Sundays, made away with three-quarters of his income.","양고기보다 쇠고기가 좀 더 많이 든 스튜 한 냄비, 거의 매일 밤의 샐러드, 토요일의 자투리 고기 요리, 금요일의 렌즈콩, 일요일에 곁들이는 비둘기 한두 마리가 그의 수입 가운데 사분의 삼을 축냈다."],["The rest of it went in a doublet of fine cloth and velvet breeches and shoes to match for holidays, while on week-days he made a brave figure in his best homespun.","나머지는 명절날 입을 고운 천 더블릿과 벨벳 반바지, 거기에 어울리는 신발로 들어갔으며, 평일에는 가장 좋은 손수 짠 옷을 차려입고 의젓한 모습을 했다."],["He had in his house a housekeeper past forty, a niece under twenty, and a lad for the field and market-place, who used to saddle the hack as well as handle the bill-hook.","그의 집에는 마흔이 넘은 가정부와 스무 살이 채 안 된 조카딸, 그리고 들일과 장보기를 맡아 말에 안장을 얹기도 하고 가지치기 낫을 다루기도 하는 머슴 아이가 하나 있었다."],["The age of this gentleman of ours was bordering on fifty; he was of a hardy habit, spare, gaunt-featured, a very early riser and a great sportsman.","우리 이 양반의 나이는 쉰 줄에 다다랐으며, 다부진 체질에 야위고 핼쑥한 얼굴을 했고, 무척 일찍 일어나는 데다 사냥을 몹시 즐겼다."],["They will have it his surname was Quixada or Quesada (for here there is some difference of opinion among the authors who write on the subject), although from reasonable conjectures it seems plain that he was called Quexana.","사람들은 그의 성이 키하다 혹은 케사다였다고 하는데(이 점에 대해서는 이 주제를 다룬 작가들 사이에 견해가 다소 갈린다), 그럴듯한 추측으로 미루어 보면 케하나라 불렸음이 분명한 듯하다."],["This, however, is of but little importance to our tale; it will be enough not to stray a hair’s breadth from the truth in the telling of it.","그러나 이는 우리 이야기에 그리 중요한 일이 아니니, 이야기를 풀어 나가는 동안 진실에서 터럭만큼도 벗어나지 않으면 그것으로 족하다."]],[["You must know, then, that the above-named gentleman whenever he was at leisure (which was mostly all the year round) gave himself up to reading books of chivalry with such ardour and avidity that he almost entirely neglected the pursuit of his field-sports, and even the management of his property; and to such a pitch did his eagerness and infatuation go that he sold many an acre of tillageland to buy books of chivalry to read, and brought home as many of them as he could get.","그러니 알아 두어야 할 것은, 앞서 말한 이 양반이 한가할 때면(거의 일 년 내내 한가했지만) 어찌나 열렬하고 게걸스럽게 기사도 책 읽기에 빠져들었던지, 사냥하는 일도 거의 까맣게 잊고 심지어 제 재산을 챙기는 일조차 등한히 했다는 사실이다. 그 열망과 광기가 어느 지경에 이르렀던가 하면, 읽을 기사도 책을 사려고 경작지를 여러 에이커씩 팔아 치웠으며, 손에 넣을 수 있는 책이라면 모조리 집으로 가져왔다."],["But of all there were none he liked so well as those of the famous Feliciano de Silva’s composition, for their lucidity of style and complicated conceits were as pearls in his sight, particularly when in his reading he came upon courtships and cartels, where he often found passages like “the reason of the unreason with which my reason is afflicted so weakens my reason that with reason I murmur at your beauty;”","그러나 그 모든 책 가운데서도 그가 가장 좋아한 것은 저 유명한 펠리시아노 데 실바가 지은 책들이었으니, 그 명료한 문체와 복잡하게 얽힌 기발한 표현들이 그의 눈에는 진주처럼 보였던 까닭이요, 특히 책을 읽다가 구애의 글과 도전장에 이르면 더욱 그러했다. 거기서 그는 흔히 이런 구절들을 만나곤 했다."],["or again, “the high heavens, that of your divinity divinely fortify you with the stars, render you deserving of the desert your greatness deserves.”","\"내 이성을 괴롭히는 까닭 없는 까닭이 내 이성을 너무도 약하게 만드는지라, 까닭이 있어 그대의 아름다움을 두고 한탄하노라.\" 또 이런 구절도 있었다."],["Over conceits of this sort the poor gentleman lost his wits, and used to lie awake striving to understand them and worm the meaning out of them; what Aristotle himself could not have made out or extracted had he come to life again for that special purpose.","\"그대의 신성으로 그대를 별들로써 신성하게 떠받치는 드높은 하늘이, 그대의 위대함이 마땅히 받을 만한 그 마땅함을 그대로 하여금 누리게 하노라.\" 이런 종류의 기발한 표현 앞에서 가엾은 양반은 그만 넋을 잃었으니, 밤잠을 설쳐 가며 그것들을 이해하고 그 속뜻을 캐내려 애썼다."],["He was not at all easy about the wounds which Don Belianis gave and took, because it seemed to him that, great as were the surgeons who had cured him, he must have had his face and body covered all over with seams and scars.","설령 아리스토텔레스가 바로 그 일을 위해 다시 살아 돌아온다 한들 그 뜻을 헤아리거나 끌어내지 못했을 것이다. 그는 돈 벨리아니스가 주고받은 상처들에 대해서는 도무지 마음을 놓지 못했으니, 그를 치료한 외과의들이 아무리 뛰어났다 해도 그의 얼굴과 몸은 온통 꿰맨 자국과 흉터로 뒤덮여 있을 게 분명하다고 여겼기 때문이다."],["He commended, however, the author’s way of ending his book with the promise of that interminable adventure, and many a time was he tempted to take up his pen and finish it properly as is there proposed, which no doubt he would have done, and made a successful piece of work of it too, had not greater and more absorbing thoughts prevented him.","그래도 그는 작가가 그 끝없는 모험을 약속하는 것으로 책을 맺은 방식만큼은 칭찬했으며, 거기 제안된 대로 펜을 들어 제대로 마무리 짓고 싶은 마음에 여러 번 사로잡혔다. 더 크고 더 사로잡는 생각들이 그를 막아서지만 않았더라면, 그는 틀림없이 그 일을 해냈을 것이고, 또 그것을 훌륭한 작품으로 완성했을 것이다."]],[["Many an argument did he have with the curate of his village (a learned man, and a graduate of Siguenza) as to which had been the better knight, Palmerin of England or Amadis of Gaul.","그는 자기 마을의 신부와(시구엔사의 학사 출신인 박식한 사람이었다) 영국의 팔메린과 골의 아마디스 가운데 어느 쪽이 더 나은 기사였던가를 두고 여러 차례 논쟁을 벌이곤 했다."],["Master Nicholas, the village barber, however, used to say that neither of them came up to the Knight of Phoebus, and that if there was any that could compare with him it was Don Galaor, the brother of Amadis of Gaul, because he had a spirit that was equal to every occasion, and was no finikin knight, nor lachrymose like his brother, while in the matter of valour he was not a whit behind him.","그러나 마을 이발사인 니콜라스 선생은 그 둘 중 누구도 태양의 기사에는 미치지 못한다고 말하곤 했으며, 만일 그에 견줄 만한 자가 있다면 그것은 골의 아마디스의 동생인 돈 갈라오르일 거라고 했다. 그가 어떤 상황에도 거뜬히 맞설 만한 기개를 지녔고, 그 형처럼 까다롭게 구는 기사도, 눈물이나 짜는 기사도 아니었으며, 용맹함에서도 형에게 조금도 뒤지지 않았기 때문이다."],["In short, he became so absorbed in his books that he spent his nights from sunset to sunrise, and his days from dawn to dark, poring over them; and what with little sleep and much reading his brains got so dry that he lost his wits.","요컨대 그는 책에 너무도 깊이 빠져들어, 밤이면 해 질 녘부터 동틀 녘까지, 낮이면 새벽부터 어두워질 때까지 책에 코를 박고 지냈다. 그렇게 잠은 적게 자고 책은 많이 읽다 보니 머릿속이 바싹 말라붙어 그예 정신을 잃고 말았다."],["His fancy grew full of what he used to read about in his books, enchantments, quarrels, battles, challenges, wounds, wooings, loves, agonies, and all sorts of impossible nonsense; and it so possessed his mind that the whole fabric of invention and fancy he read of was true, that to him no history in the world had more reality in it.","그의 머릿속은 책에서 읽던 것들로 가득 차게 되었으니, 마법이며 다툼이며 전투며 도전이며 상처며 구애며 사랑이며 고뇌며 온갖 터무니없는 황당무계한 일들이 그것이었다. 이런 것들이 그의 마음을 어찌나 사로잡았던지, 그가 읽은 그 모든 꾸며 낸 환상의 직물이 진짜라고 여기게 되었으며, 그에게는 세상의 그 어떤 역사책도 이보다 더 사실적인 것이 없었다."],["He used to say the Cid Ruy Diaz was a very good knight, but that he was not to be compared with the Knight of the Burning Sword who with one back-stroke cut in half two fierce and monstrous giants.","그는 엘 시드 루이 디아스가 매우 훌륭한 기사이긴 하나, 단 한 번의 되받아치는 일격으로 사납고 흉측한 두 거인을 두 동강 낸 불타는 검의 기사에는 견줄 바가 못 된다고 말하곤 했다."],["He thought more of Bernardo del Carpio because at Roncesvalles he slew Roland in spite of enchantments, availing himself of the artifice of Hercules when he strangled Antaeus the son of Terra in his arms.","그는 베르나르도 델 카르피오를 더 높이 샀는데, 그가 론세스바예스에서 마법에도 굴하지 않고 롤단을 죽였기 때문이며, 그때 헤라클레스가 대지의 아들 안타이오스를 두 팔로 끌어안아 목 졸라 죽인 그 술책을 써먹었기 때문이다."],["He approved highly of the giant Morgante, because, although of the giant breed which is always arrogant and ill-conditioned, he alone was affable and well-bred.","그는 거인 모르간테도 크게 칭찬했으니, 그가 늘 거만하고 못돼 먹기 마련인 거인족이면서도 홀로 상냥하고 예의 발랐기 때문이다."],["But above all he admired Reinaldos of Montalban, especially when he saw him sallying forth from his castle and robbing everyone he met, and when beyond the seas he stole that image of Mahomet which, as his history says, was entirely of gold.","그러나 무엇보다도 그는 몬탈반의 레이날도스를 흠모했으니, 특히 그가 제 성에서 뛰쳐나와 마주치는 사람마다 모조리 약탈하는 모습과, 바다 건너 저편에서 그 마호메트 우상을 훔쳐 내는 모습을 볼 때면 더욱 그러했다. 그 우상은 그의 이야기에 따르면 온통 순금으로 되어 있었다."],["To have a bout of kicking at that traitor of a Ganelon he would have given his housekeeper, and his niece into the bargain.","저 배신자 가넬론을 실컷 걷어차 줄 수만 있다면 그는 자기 가정부는 물론이요 조카딸까지 덤으로 내주었을 것이다."]],[["In short, his wits being quite gone, he hit upon the strangest notion that ever madman in this world hit upon, and that was that he fancied it was right and requisite, as well for the support of his own honour as for the service of his country, that he should make a knight-errant of himself, roaming the world over in full armour and on horseback in quest of adventures, and putting in practice himself all that he had read of as being the usual practices of knights-errant; righting every kind of wrong, and exposing himself to peril and danger from which, in the issue, he was to reap eternal renown and fame.","요컨대 그는 정신이 완전히 나가 버린 나머지, 이 세상의 어떤 미치광이도 떠올린 적 없는 가장 기이한 생각에 이르렀으니, 그것은 곧 제 명예를 지키기 위해서나 나라에 봉사하기 위해서나 자신이 편력 기사가 되어, 갑옷을 온전히 갖춰 입고 말에 올라 온 세상을 떠돌며 모험을 찾아다니고, 편력 기사들이 으레 행한다고 책에서 읽은 그 모든 일을 몸소 실천하는 것이 옳고도 마땅한 일이라고 여긴 것이었다. 온갖 종류의 불의를 바로잡고, 위험과 곤경에 스스로를 내던지되, 결국에는 그로써 영원한 명성과 영예를 거두려는 것이었다."],["Already the poor man saw himself crowned by the might of his arm Emperor of Trebizond at least; and so, led away by the intense enjoyment he found in these pleasant fancies, he set himself forthwith to put his scheme into execution.","이 가엾은 사내는 벌써 제 팔의 힘으로 적어도 트레비손다의 황제로 등극한 자신의 모습을 그려 보고 있었다. 그리하여 이 즐거운 공상에서 맛본 그 강렬한 기쁨에 끌려, 그는 곧장 자신의 계획을 실행에 옮기기로 했다."]],[["The first thing he did was to clean up some armour that had belonged to his great-grandfather, and had been for ages lying forgotten in a corner eaten with rust and covered with mildew.","그가 가장 먼저 한 일은 증조부의 것이었던 갑옷 한 벌을 손질하는 일이었다. 그 갑옷은 오랜 세월 한구석에 잊힌 채 녹이 슬고 곰팡이로 뒤덮여 있었다."],["He scoured and polished it as best he could, but he perceived one great defect in it, that it had no closed helmet, nothing but a simple morion.","그는 할 수 있는 한 그것을 닦고 광을 냈으나, 거기서 한 가지 큰 결함을 발견했으니, 닫히는 투구는 없고 그저 단순한 투구 모양의 모리온뿐이라는 점이었다."],["This deficiency, however, his ingenuity supplied, for he contrived a kind of half-helmet of pasteboard which, fitted on to the morion, looked like a whole one.","그러나 그의 재간이 이 부족함을 메워 주었으니, 그는 마분지로 반쪽짜리 투구 같은 것을 만들어 모리온에 끼워 맞추자 온전한 투구처럼 보이게 했다."],["It is true that, in order to see if it was strong and fit to stand a cut, he drew his sword and gave it a couple of slashes, the first of which undid in an instant what had taken him a week to do.","사실 그것이 단단한지, 칼질을 견딜 만한지 보려고 그가 칼을 뽑아 두어 번 내리쳐 보았는데, 첫 번째 일격으로 한 주일 동안 공들인 것이 순식간에 망가져 버렸다."],["The ease with which he had knocked it to pieces disconcerted him somewhat, and to guard against that danger he set to work again, fixing bars of iron on the inside until he was satisfied with its strength; and then, not caring to try any more experiments with it, he passed it and adopted it as a helmet of the most perfect construction.","그토록 손쉽게 산산조각 나 버린 것에 그는 적잖이 당황했으며, 그 위험에 대비하고자 다시 작업에 착수해 안쪽에 쇠막대들을 덧대어 그 견고함에 흡족할 때까지 손을 보았다. 그러고는 더 이상 그것으로 어떤 실험도 해 볼 마음이 없어, 그것을 가장 완벽하게 만들어진 투구로 인정하고 받아들였다."]],[["He next proceeded to inspect his hack, which, with more quartos than a real and more blemishes than the steed of Gonela, that “tantum pellis et ossa fuit,”","그는 다음으로 자기 말을 살펴보았는데, 이 말은 레알 동전보다 더 많은 흠을 지녔고, 곤넬라의 말, 곧 \"온통 가죽과 뼈뿐이었던\" 그 말보다 더 많은 결점을 지녔으나, 그의 눈에는 알렉산드로스의 부케팔로스나 엘 시드의 바비에카를 능가하는 것이었다."],["surpassed in his eyes the Bucephalus of Alexander or the Babieca of the Cid.","그는 말에게 어떤 이름을 지어 줄까 궁리하느라 나흘을 보냈으니, (그가 스스로 되뇌었듯이) 그토록 이름난 기사의 말이, 그것도 제 나름의 빼어남을 갖춘 말이 어떤 특별한 이름도 없이 지내는 것은 옳지 않은 일이었기 때문이다."],["Four days were spent in thinking what name to give him, because (as he said to himself) it was not right that a horse belonging to a knight so famous, and one with such merits of his own, should be without some distinctive name, and he strove to adapt it so as to indicate what he had been before belonging to a knight-errant, and what he then was; for it was only reasonable that, his master taking a new character, he should take a new name, and that it should be a distinguished and full-sounding one, befitting the new order and calling he was about to follow.","그는 그 이름을 잘 다듬어 그 말이 편력 기사의 것이 되기 전에는 어떤 말이었으며 지금은 어떤 말인지를 드러내려 애썼다. 주인이 새로운 신분을 얻었으니 말도 새 이름을 얻는 것이, 그것도 그가 이제 막 따르려는 새로운 기사단과 직분에 걸맞게 격조 높고 우렁차게 울리는 이름을 얻는 것이 사리에 맞는 일이었기 때문이다."],["And so, after having composed, struck out, rejected, added to, unmade, and remade a multitude of names out of his memory and fancy, he decided upon calling him Rocinante, a name, to his thinking, lofty, sonorous, and significant of his condition as a hack before he became what he now was, the first and foremost of all the hacks in the world.","그리하여 그는 기억과 공상에서 숱한 이름을 지어냈다가 지우고, 물리쳤다가 보태고, 허물었다가 다시 짜 맞춘 끝에, 마침내 그 말을 로시난테라 부르기로 마음먹었다. 그가 생각하기에 이는 격조 높고 우렁차게 울리며, 그 말이 지금의 모습, 곧 세상 모든 짐말 가운데 으뜸가는 말이 되기 전에는 한낱 짐말이었던 그 처지를 잘 드러내 주는 이름이었다."]],[["Having got a name for his horse so much to his taste, he was anxious to get one for himself, and he was eight days more pondering over this point, till at last he made up his mind to call himself “Don Quixote,”","이렇듯 제 마음에 쏙 드는 이름을 말에게 지어 주고 나니, 그는 자기 자신에게도 이름 하나를 지어 주고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그리하여 이 점을 두고 다시 여드레를 곰곰이 생각한 끝에, 마침내 자신을 \"돈키호테\"라 부르기로 마음을 정했다."],["whence, as has been already said, the authors of this veracious history have inferred that his name must have been beyond a doubt Quixada, and not Quesada as others would have it.","이로부터, 앞서 말한 바와 같이, 이 진실한 역사의 작가들은 그의 성이 다른 이들이 우긴 케사다가 아니라 의심할 바 없이 키하다였음이 틀림없다고 추론한 것이다."],["Recollecting, however, that the valiant Amadis was not content to call himself curtly Amadis and nothing more, but added the name of his kingdom and country to make it famous, and called himself Amadis of Gaul, he, like a good knight, resolved to add on the name of his, and to style himself Don Quixote of La Mancha, whereby, he considered, he described accurately his origin and country, and did honour to it in taking his surname from it.","그러나 용맹한 아마디스가 그저 무뚝뚝하게 아마디스라고만 부르는 데 만족하지 않고, 제 이름을 떨치고자 거기에 자기 왕국과 고향의 이름을 덧붙여 스스로를 골의 아마디스라 일컬었음을 떠올린 그는, 훌륭한 기사답게 자기 고장의 이름을 덧붙이기로 결심하고는 스스로를 라만차의 돈키호테라 칭하기로 했다. 이로써 그는 자신의 가문과 고향을 정확히 드러내며, 그곳에서 성을 따옴으로써 그곳에 영예를 돌린다고 여겼다."]],[["So then, his armour being furbished, his morion turned into a helmet, his hack christened, and he himself confirmed, he came to the conclusion that nothing more was needed now but to look out for a lady to be in love with; for a knight-errant without love was like a tree without leaves or fruit, or a body without a soul. As he said to himself, “If, for my sins, or by my good fortune, I come across some giant hereabouts, a common occurrence with knights-errant, and overthrow him in one onslaught, or cleave him asunder to the waist, or, in short, vanquish and subdue him, will it not be well to have some one I may send him to as a present, that he may come in and fall on his knees before my sweet lady, and in a humble, submissive voice say, ‘I am the giant Caraculiambro, lord of the island of Malindrania, vanquished in single combat by the never sufficiently extolled knight Don Quixote of La Mancha, who has commanded me to present myself before your Grace, that your Highness dispose of me at your pleasure’?” Oh, how our good gentleman enjoyed the delivery of this speech, especially when he had thought of some one to call his Lady! There was, so the story goes, in a village near his own a very good-looking farm-girl with whom he had been at one time in love, though, so far as is known, she never knew it nor gave a thought to the matter. Her name was Aldonza Lorenzo, and upon her he thought fit to confer the title of Lady of his Thoughts; and after some search for a name which should not be out of harmony with her own, and should suggest and indicate that of a princess and great lady, he decided upon calling her Dulcinea del Toboso--she being of El Toboso--a name, to his mind, musical, uncommon, and significant, like all those he had already bestowed upon himself and the things belonging to him.","그리하여 갑옷을 말끔히 손질하고, 모리온을 투구로 바꾸고, 짐말에게 세례명을 지어 주고, 자기 자신도 새 이름으로 확정 짓고 나니, 그는 이제 더 필요한 것이라고는 사랑에 빠질 귀부인을 하나 찾는 일뿐이라는 결론에 이르렀다. 사랑 없는 편력 기사란 잎도 열매도 없는 나무나, 영혼 없는 육신과 같았기 때문이다. 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 되뇌었다. \"내 죄업 탓이든 내 행운 덕분이든, 만일 내가 이 근방에서 어떤 거인과 마주친다면, 이는 편력 기사들에게는 흔한 일이거니와, 단 한 번의 공격으로 그를 거꾸러뜨리거나, 허리께까지 두 동강을 내거나, 요컨대 그를 무찔러 굴복시킨다면, 그를 선물로 보낼 누군가가 있는 편이 좋지 않겠는가. 그리하여 그가 들어와 내 어여쁜 귀부인 앞에 무릎 꿇고, 겸손하고 순종하는 목소리로 이렇게 아뢰는 것이다. '저는 거인 카라쿨리암브로, 말린드라니아 섬의 영주이옵니다. 아무리 칭송해도 모자랄 기사 라만차의 돈키호테 님과의 일대일 결투에서 패하였으며, 그분께서 제게 명하시기를 마님 앞에 나아가 마님께서 마음대로 저를 처분하시도록 하라 하셨나이다.'\" 오, 우리의 선량한 양반은 이 대사를 읊으면서 얼마나 즐거워했던가, 특히 제 귀부인이라 부를 누군가를 떠올렸을 때는 더욱 그러했다! 전해지는 이야기에 따르면, 그의 마을 가까운 한 동네에 무척 어여쁜 농사꾼 처녀가 있었으니, 그가 한때 사랑했던 여인이었다. 다만 알려진 바로는, 그녀는 그 사실을 결코 알지 못했고 그런 일에 마음을 둔 적도 없었다. 그녀의 이름은 알돈사 로렌소였고, 그는 그녀에게 자기 마음속 귀부인이라는 칭호를 내리는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼다. 그리하여 그녀의 본래 이름과 어긋나지 않으면서도 어느 공주나 위대한 귀부인의 이름을 떠올리게 하고 또 암시할 만한 이름을 한참 궁리한 끝에, 그는 그녀를 토보소의 둘시네아라 부르기로 했다. 그녀가 엘 토보소 출신이었기 때문이다. 그가 보기에 이는 음악처럼 울리고, 흔치 않으며, 뜻이 깊은 이름이었으니, 이미 자신과 제게 딸린 것들에 지어 준 다른 모든 이름이 그러했듯이 말이다."]],[["# CHAPTER II. WHICH TREATS OF THE FIRST SALLY THE INGENIOUS DON QUIXOTE MADE FROM HOME","# CHAPTER II."]],[["These preliminaries settled, he did not care to put off any longer the execution of his design, urged on to it by the thought of all the world was losing by his delay, seeing what wrongs he intended to right, grievances to redress, injustices to repair, abuses to remove, and duties to discharge.","이런 사전 준비를 마치자, 그는 자신의 계획을 실행에 옮기는 일을 더는 미루고 싶지 않았다. 자신이 바로잡으려는 부당함, 시정하려는 억울함, 보상하려는 불의, 없애려는 폐단, 다해야 할 의무들을 생각하니, 자신이 지체하는 동안 온 세상이 잃고 있는 것을 떠올렸기에 더욱 마음이 다급해졌다."],["So, without giving notice of his intention to anyone, and without anybody seeing him, one morning before the dawning of the day (which was one of the hottest of the month of July) he donned his suit of armour, mounted Rocinante with his patched-up helmet on, braced his buckler, took his lance, and by the back door of the yard sallied forth upon the plain in the highest contentment and satisfaction at seeing with what ease he had made a beginning with his grand purpose.","그리하여 그는 누구에게도 자신의 의향을 알리지 않고, 또 아무도 그를 보지 못하게 한 채, 어느 날 아침 동이 트기도 전에(때는 7월 중에서도 가장 무더운 날 가운데 하루였다) 갑옷을 차려입고, 기워 붙인 투구를 쓰고서 로시난테에 올라타, 방패를 단단히 동여매고 창을 집어 들고는, 마당 뒷문으로 들판을 향해 출정했다. 그는 자신의 원대한 목적을 이토록 손쉽게 시작했다는 데에 더없는 흡족함과 만족감을 느꼈다."],["But scarcely did he find himself upon the open plain, when a terrible thought struck him, one all but enough to make him abandon the enterprise at the very outset.","그러나 탁 트인 들판에 막 들어서자마자, 무시무시한 생각 하나가 그를 사로잡았으니, 그것은 출발하는 바로 그 순간 이 모험을 포기하게 만들 뻔할 만큼 강력한 것이었다."],["It occurred to him that he had not been dubbed a knight, and that according to the law of chivalry he neither could nor ought to bear arms against any knight; and that even if he had been, still he ought, as a novice knight, to wear white armour, without a device upon the shield until by his prowess he had earned one.","그는 자신이 아직 기사 서임을 받지 못했다는 사실, 그리고 기사도의 법에 따르면 자신은 어떤 기사를 상대로도 무기를 들 수 없고 또 들어서도 안 된다는 사실을 깨달았던 것이다. 게다가 설령 서임을 받았다 하더라도, 신참 기사로서 자신의 무용으로 문장(紋章)을 얻어 낼 때까지는 방패에 아무런 문장도 새기지 않은 흰 갑옷을 입어야 한다는 점 또한 떠올랐다."],["These reflections made him waver in his purpose, but his craze being stronger than any reasoning, he made up his mind to have himself dubbed a knight by the first one he came across, following the example of others in the same case, as he had read in the books that brought him to this pass.","이런 생각들이 그의 결심을 흔들리게 했으나, 그의 광기는 그 어떤 이치보다도 강했기에, 그는 이런 처지에 놓인 다른 이들의 본을 따라, 자신을 이 지경에 이르게 한 책들에서 읽은 대로, 처음 마주치는 사람에게서 기사 서임을 받기로 마음을 굳혔다."],["As for white armour, he resolved, on the first opportunity, to scour his until it was whiter than an ermine; and so comforting himself he pursued his way, taking that which his horse chose, for in this he believed lay the essence of adventures.","흰 갑옷에 관해서는, 첫 번째 기회가 오면 담비 털보다 더 희어질 때까지 자기 갑옷을 닦으리라 작정했다. 그렇게 스스로를 위로하며 그는 가던 길을 계속 갔는데, 말이 골라 가는 대로 내맡겼으니, 바로 거기에 모험의 본질이 깃들어 있다고 믿었기 때문이다."]],[["Thus setting out, our new-fledged adventurer paced along, talking to himself and saying, “Who knows but that in time to come, when the veracious history of my famous deeds is made known, the sage who writes it, when he has to set forth my first sally in the early morning, will do it after this fashion?","이렇게 길을 나선 우리의 풋내기 모험가는 혼자 중얼거리며 천천히 나아갔다. \"누가 알겠는가, 훗날 나의 그 빛나는 업적들에 관한 진실한 역사가 세상에 알려질 때, 그것을 기록하는 현자가 이른 아침의 나의 첫 출정을 펼쳐 보일 적에, 이런 식으로 쓰지 않으리라고?"],["‘Scarce had the rubicund Apollo spread o’er the face of the broad spacious earth the golden threads of his bright hair, scarce had the little birds of painted plumage attuned their notes to hail with dulcet and mellifluous harmony the coming of the rosy Dawn, that, deserting the soft couch of her jealous spouse, was appearing to mortals at the gates and balconies of the Manchegan horizon, when the renowned knight Don Quixote of La Mancha, quitting the lazy down, mounted his celebrated steed Rocinante and began to traverse the ancient and famous Campo de Montiel;’”","'불그레한 아폴로가 넓디넓은 대지의 얼굴 위로 그 찬란한 머리카락의 황금빛 실타래를 채 펼치기도 전에, 알록달록한 깃털을 단 작은 새들이 그 감미롭고 꿀처럼 달콤한 가락으로, 질투심 많은 남편의 보드라운 잠자리를 버리고 라만차 지평선의 문과 발코니에서 인간들에게 모습을 드러내는 장밋빛 새벽의 도래를 맞으려 그 노래를 미처 고르기도 전에, 이름 높은 기사 라만차의 돈키호테는 게으른 새털 이불을 떨치고 일어나, 그 유명한 준마 로시난테에 올라타 유서 깊고 명성 자자한 몬티엘 들판을 가로지르기 시작했더라.'\" 사실 그는 정말로 그 들판을 가로지르고 있었다."],["which in fact he was actually traversing.","\"행복하여라 그 시대여, 행복하여라 그 시절이여.\""],["“Happy the age, happy the time,”","그가 말을 이었다."],["he continued, “in which shall be made known my deeds of fame, worthy to be moulded in brass, carved in marble, limned in pictures, for a memorial for ever.","\"청동에 빚어지고 대리석에 새겨지며 그림으로 그려져 영원토록 기념될 만한, 명성에 빛나는 나의 업적들이 세상에 알려질 그 시대여."],["And thou, O sage magician, whoever thou art, to whom it shall fall to be the chronicler of this wondrous history, forget not, I entreat thee, my good Rocinante, the constant companion of my ways and wanderings.”","그리고 그대, 오 현명한 마법사여, 그대가 누구이든, 이 경이로운 역사의 기록자가 될 운명을 맡은 그대여, 부디 잊지 말아 다오, 내 간청하노니, 나의 길과 방랑의 한결같은 동반자인 나의 충직한 로시난테를.\""],["Presently he broke out again, as if he were love-stricken in earnest, “O Princess Dulcinea, lady of this captive heart, a grievous wrong hast thou done me to drive me forth with scorn, and with inexorable obduracy banish me from the presence of thy beauty.","곧이어 그는 마치 진정으로 사랑에 빠진 사람처럼 다시 외쳤다. \"오 둘시네아 공주여, 이 사로잡힌 마음의 여인이여, 그대는 나를 멸시하며 내쫓고, 그 굽힐 줄 모르는 매정함으로 그대의 아름다움 앞에서 나를 추방하여 내게 모진 잘못을 저질렀도다."],["O lady, deign to hold in remembrance this heart, thy vassal, that thus in anguish pines for love of thee.”","오 여인이여, 이 마음을, 그대를 향한 사랑에 이토록 괴로이 애태우는 그대의 종복인 이 마음을, 부디 기억해 주소서.\""]],[["So he went on stringing together these and other absurdities, all in the style of those his books had taught him, imitating their language as well as he could; and all the while he rode so slowly and the sun mounted so rapidly and with such fervour that it was enough to melt his brains if he had any.","그는 이런 식으로, 그리고 또 다른 갖가지 터무니없는 말들을 줄줄이 엮어 나갔으니, 모두가 자기 책들이 가르쳐 준 그대로의 문체였고, 할 수 있는 한 그 언어를 흉내 낸 것이었다. 그러는 내내 그는 어찌나 느릿느릿 말을 몰았던지, 그리고 해는 어찌나 빠르게, 또 어찌나 뜨거운 기세로 솟아올랐던지, 그에게 골이라는 것이 있었다면 그것을 녹여 버리기에 충분할 정도였다."],["Nearly all day he travelled without anything remarkable happening to him, at which he was in despair, for he was anxious to encounter some one at once upon whom to try the might of his strong arm.","그는 거의 온종일 길을 갔으나 이렇다 할 일은 하나도 일어나지 않았으니, 그는 절망에 빠졌다. 한시바삐 누군가를 만나 자기 억센 팔의 힘을 시험해 보고 싶어 안달이 났기 때문이다."]],[["Writers there are who say the first adventure he met with was that of Puerto Lapice; others say it was that of the windmills; but what I have ascertained on this point, and what I have found written in the annals of La Mancha, is that he was on the road all day, and towards nightfall his hack and he found themselves dead tired and hungry, when, looking all around to see if he could discover any castle or shepherd’s shanty where he might refresh himself and relieve his sore wants, he perceived not far out of his road an inn, which was as welcome as a star guiding him to the portals, if not the palaces, of his redemption; and quickening his pace he reached it just as night was setting in.","그가 처음 마주친 모험이 푸에르토 라피세의 모험이었다고 말하는 작가들이 있고, 또 풍차의 모험이었다고 말하는 이들도 있다. 그러나 이 점에 관하여 내가 확인한 바, 그리고 라만차의 연대기에 적힌 것을 찾아낸 바로는, 그는 온종일 길을 갔으며, 해 질 무렵이 되자 그의 말과 그는 죽도록 지치고 허기진 처지가 되었으니, 사방을 두루 살피며 몸을 추스르고 쓰라린 궁핍을 달랠 만한 성이나 양치기의 오두막이라도 찾을 수 있을까 했다."],["At the door were standing two young women, girls of the district as they call them, on their way to Seville with some carriers who had chanced to halt that night at the inn; and as, happen what might to our adventurer, everything he saw or imaged seemed to him to be and to happen after the fashion of what he read of, the moment he saw the inn he pictured it to himself as a castle with its four turrets and pinnacles of shining silver, not forgetting the drawbridge and moat and all the belongings usually ascribed to castles of the sort.","그러던 중 길에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 주막 하나가 눈에 띄었는데, 그것은 마치 그를 그의 구원의 궁전까지는 아니더라도 그 문간으로 인도하는 별처럼 반가운 것이었다. 그가 걸음을 재촉하여 그곳에 다다른 것은 막 밤이 깃들 무렵이었다."],["To this inn, which to him seemed a castle, he advanced, and at a short distance from it he checked Rocinante, hoping that some dwarf would show himself upon the battlements, and by sound of trumpet give notice that a knight was approaching the castle.","문 앞에는 젊은 여인 둘이 서 있었는데, 그 고장 말로 부르는 이른바 들병이들로서, 그날 밤 마침 그 주막에 들른 어느 노새몰이꾼 일행과 함께 세비야로 가는 길이었다. 우리의 모험가에게는 무슨 일이 일어나든 간에, 그가 보거나 떠올리는 모든 것이 자기가 읽은 그대로의 모습이고 그대로 일어나는 일처럼 여겨졌으므로, 그는 주막을 본 그 순간 그것을 네 개의 망루와 번쩍이는 은빛 첨탑을 갖춘 성으로 그려 냈으며, 도개교며 해자며 그런 부류의 성에 으레 따르는 온갖 부속물도 빼놓지 않았다."],["But seeing that they were slow about it, and that Rocinante was in a hurry to reach the stable, he made for the inn door, and perceived the two gay damsels who were standing there, and who seemed to him to be two fair maidens or lovely ladies taking their ease at the castle gate.","그에게 성처럼 보이는 이 주막을 향해 그는 나아갔고, 거기서 얼마쯤 떨어진 데에서 로시난테를 멈춰 세웠으니, 어느 난쟁이가 흉벽 위에 나타나 나팔 소리로 어느 기사가 성에 다가오고 있음을 알려 주기를 바랐던 것이다. 그러나 그들이 그렇게 하기를 꾸물거리는 데다 로시난테가 마구간에 닿고 싶어 안달인 것을 보고, 그는 주막 문으로 향했고, 거기 서 있던 두 명의 들뜬 처녀를 보았으니, 그에게는 그들이 성문 앞에서 한가로이 쉬고 있는 두 명의 아리따운 아가씨나 사랑스러운 귀부인으로 보였다."]],[["At this moment it so happened that a swineherd who was going through the stubbles collecting a drove of pigs (for, without any apology, that is what they are called) gave a blast of his horn to bring them together, and forthwith it seemed to Don Quixote to be what he was expecting, the signal of some dwarf announcing his arrival; and so with prodigious satisfaction he rode up to the inn and to the ladies, who, seeing a man of this sort approaching in full armour and with lance and buckler, were turning in dismay into the inn, when Don Quixote, guessing their fear by their flight, raising his pasteboard visor, disclosed his dry dusty visage, and with courteous bearing and gentle voice addressed them, “Your ladyships need not fly or fear any rudeness, for that it belongs not to the order of knighthood which I profess to offer to anyone, much less to highborn maidens as your appearance proclaims you to be.” The girls were looking at him and straining their eyes to make out the features which the clumsy visor obscured, but when they heard themselves called maidens, a thing so much out of their line, they could not restrain their laughter, which made Don Quixote wax indignant, and say, “Modesty becomes the fair, and moreover laughter that has little cause is great silliness; this, however, I say not to pain or anger you, for my desire is none other than to serve you.”","바로 이 순간, 마침 어느 돼지치기가 그루터기밭을 가로지르며 돼지 떼를 (어떤 변명도 없이 말하자면, 그것들이 그렇게 불리는 까닭이다) 모으느라 그것들을 한데 끌어모으려고 뿔나팔을 한 번 불었는데, 돈키호테에게는 즉시 그것이 자신이 기다리던 바로 그것, 곧 자신의 도착을 알리는 어느 난쟁이의 신호처럼 여겨졌다. 그리하여 그는 엄청난 만족감에 젖어 주막으로, 그 귀부인들에게로 말을 몰아갔는데, 그들은 이런 차림의 사내가 갑옷을 온전히 갖춰 입고 창과 방패를 든 채 다가오는 것을 보고는 기겁하여 주막 안으로 들어가려던 참이었다. 돈키호테는 그들이 달아나는 것으로 그들의 두려움을 알아채고, 판지로 만든 면갑을 들어 올려 마르고 먼지 낀 얼굴을 드러내고는, 정중한 태도와 부드러운 목소리로 그들에게 말을 건넸다. \"아가씨들께서는 달아나실 것도, 무슨 무례함을 두려워하실 것도 없습니다. 어느 누구에게도 무례를 범하는 것은 제가 받든 기사도의 법도에 어긋나는 일이며, 더구나 그대들의 자태가 드러내 보이는 대로 고귀한 집안의 아가씨들께라면 더더욱 그러합니다.\" 처녀들은 그를 바라보며, 그 볼품없는 면갑에 가려진 이목구비를 알아보려 눈을 잔뜩 부릅떴으나, 자신들이 아가씨라 불리는 것을 듣자, 그것은 그들의 처지와는 너무도 동떨어진 일이었기에 도무지 웃음을 참을 수 없었으니, 그 바람에 돈키호테는 분개하여 이렇게 말했다. \"정숙함은 아리따운 이에게 어울리는 법이요, 더구나 별 까닭 없는 웃음은 크나큰 어리석음이외다. 그러나 이는 그대들을 아프게 하거나 노엽게 하려고 하는 말이 아니니, 내 바람은 오직 그대들을 섬기는 것 외에 다른 것이 아니기 때문입니다.\""]],[["The incomprehensible language and the unpromising looks of our cavalier only increased the ladies’ laughter, and that increased his irritation, and matters might have gone farther if at that moment the landlord had not come out, who, being a very fat man, was a very peaceful one. He, seeing this grotesque figure clad in armour that did not match any more than his saddle, bridle, lance, buckler, or corselet, was not at all indisposed to join the damsels in their manifestations of amusement; but, in truth, standing in awe of such a complicated armament, he thought it best to speak him fairly, so he said, “Señor Caballero, if your worship wants lodging, bating the bed (for there is not one in the inn) there is plenty of everything else here.” Don Quixote, observing the respectful bearing of the Alcaide of the fortress (for so innkeeper and inn seemed in his eyes), made answer, “Sir Castellan, for me anything will suffice, for","우리 기사의 알아들을 수 없는 말과 미덥지 못한 행색은 그저 귀부인들의 웃음을 더 키울 뿐이었고, 그것은 그의 짜증을 더 돋우었으니, 만일 그 순간 주막 주인이 나오지 않았더라면 일이 더 커졌을지도 몰랐다. 그는 몹시 뚱뚱한 사람인지라 매우 온순한 사람이었다. 그는 안장이며 굴레며 창이며 방패며 흉갑이며 무엇 하나 서로 어울리지 않는 갑옷을 걸친 이 괴상한 형상을 보고, 처녀들과 한데 어울려 그 우스워하는 기색을 드러내고 싶은 마음이 전혀 없지는 않았으나, 사실은 그토록 복잡하게 무장한 모습에 두려움을 느낀 터라, 좋은 말로 대하는 것이 상책이라 여겨 이렇게 말했다. \"기사 나리, 만일 나리께서 묵을 곳을 찾으신다면, 침대만 빼고는(주막에 침대라곤 하나도 없으니 말입니다) 다른 것은 여기 무엇이든 넉넉히 있습니다.\" 돈키호테는 그 요새의 성주가(그의 눈에는 주막 주인과 주막이 그렇게 보였으니) 보이는 공손한 태도를 살피고는 이렇게 답했다. \"성주님, 제게는 무엇이든 충분하옵니다. 어인즉"]],[["‘My armour is my only wear, My only rest the fray.’”","'갑옷이 나의 유일한 옷이요, 싸움터가 나의 유일한 안식이니라.'\""]],[["The host fancied he called him Castellan because he took him for a “worthy of Castile,” though he was in fact an Andalusian, and one from the strand of San Lucar, as crafty a thief as Cacus and as full of tricks as a student or a page.","주막 주인은 그가 자신을 카스티야 사람으로 여겨 성주라 불렀다고 생각했으나, 실은 그는 안달루시아 사람이었고, 그것도 산루카르 해변 출신으로, 카쿠스 못지않게 교활한 도둑이요 학생이나 시동만큼이나 잔꾀로 가득 찬 자였다."],["“In that case,”","\"그렇다면,\""],["said he,","그가 말했다."]],[["“‘Your bed is on the flinty rock, Your sleep to watch alway;’","\"'그대의 잠자리는 단단한 바위 위요, 그대의 잠은 늘 깨어 지키는 것이니라.'"]],[["and if so, you may dismount and safely reckon upon any quantity of sleeplessness under this roof for a twelvemonth, not to say for a single night.”","그러하시다면, 말에서 내리셔도 좋습니다. 하룻밤은 말할 것도 없고 일 년 내내라도 이 지붕 아래서 얼마든지 잠 못 이룰 것을 톡톡히 보장받으실 테니까요.\""],["So saying, he advanced to hold the stirrup for Don Quixote, who got down with great difficulty and exertion (for he had not broken his fast all day), and then charged the host to take great care of his horse, as he was the best bit of flesh that ever ate bread in this world.","이렇게 말하며 그는 돈키호테의 등자를 잡아 주려고 다가갔고, 돈키호테는 하루 종일 아무것도 입에 대지 못한 터라 무척이나 힘겹고 안간힘을 쓰며 말에서 내렸다. 그러고는 주인에게 자기 말을 각별히 잘 보살펴 달라고 당부했으니, 그것이야말로 이 세상에서 빵을 먹어 본 짐승 가운데 가장 빼어난 놈이라는 것이었다."],["The landlord eyed him over but did not find him as good as Don Quixote said, nor even half as good; and putting him up in the stable, he returned to see what might be wanted by his guest, whom the damsels, who had by this time made their peace with him, were now relieving of his armour.","주막 주인은 그 말을 찬찬히 뜯어보았으나, 돈키호테가 말한 만큼 좋기는커녕 그 절반만큼도 좋아 보이지 않았다. 그는 말을 마구간에 들여놓고는, 손님이 무엇을 원하는지 보러 돌아왔는데, 그때쯤 손님과 화해를 한 처녀들이 마침 그의 갑옷을 벗겨 주고 있던 참이었다."],["They had taken off his breastplate and backpiece, but they neither knew nor saw how to open his gorget or remove his make-shift helmet, for he had fastened it with green ribbons, which, as there was no untying the knots, required to be cut.","그들은 가슴받이와 등받이는 벗겨 냈으나, 목가리개를 푸는 법도, 임시변통으로 만든 투구를 벗기는 법도 알지 못했고 또 알아낼 수도 없었으니, 그가 그것을 초록색 리본으로 동여매 놓았던 데다, 그 매듭은 도무지 풀 수가 없어 잘라 내야만 했기 때문이다."],["This, however, he would not by any means consent to, so he remained all the evening with his helmet on, the drollest and oddest figure that can be imagined; and while they were removing his armour, taking the baggages who were about it for ladies of high degree belonging to the castle, he said to them with great sprightliness:","그러나 그는 이를 결코 허락하려 들지 않았으므로, 저녁 내내 투구를 쓴 채로 있었으니, 가히 상상할 수 있는 가장 익살맞고 괴상한 형상이었다. 그리고 그들이 갑옷을 벗기는 동안, 그는 그 일을 거들던 들병이들을 성에 속한 지체 높은 귀부인들로 여기고는, 한껏 들뜬 기색으로 그들에게 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“Oh, never, surely, was there knight So served by hand of dame, As served was he, Don Quixote hight, When from his town he came; With maidens waiting on himself, Princesses on his hack--","\"오, 일찍이 어느 기사도 귀부인의 손에 이토록 받들어진 적 없었으리, 돈키호테라 이름하는 그이가 고향 마을을 떠나왔을 적에 받든 것만큼은. 아가씨들이 몸소 그를 시중들고, 공주들이 그의 말을 돌보았으니—"]],[["or Rocinante, for that, ladies mine, is my horse’s name, and Don Quixote of La Mancha is my own; for though I had no intention of declaring myself until my achievements in your service and honour had made me known, the necessity of adapting that old ballad of Lancelot to the present occasion has given you the knowledge of my name altogether prematurely.","로시난테 말입니다. 어인즉, 나의 귀부인들이여, 그것이 내 말의 이름이요, 라만차의 돈키호테는 바로 나의 이름이올시다."],["A time, however, will come for your ladyships to command and me to obey, and then the might of my arm will show my desire to serve you.”","비록 나는 그대들을 섬기고 받드는 가운데 이룬 위업으로 내가 알려지기 전까지는 내 정체를 밝힐 뜻이 없었으나, 저 옛 랜슬롯의 노래를 지금 이 자리에 맞추어 고쳐 부를 수밖에 없었던 까닭에, 그대들이 너무 일찍 내 이름을 알게 되고 말았습니다. 그러나 머지않아 그대들께서 명하시고 내가 받드는 때가 올 것이며, 그때에는 내 팔의 힘이 그대들을 섬기고자 하는 내 소망을 보여 줄 것입니다.\""]],[["The girls, who were not used to hearing rhetoric of this sort, had nothing to say in reply; they only asked him if he wanted anything to eat.","이런 식의 미사여구를 들어 본 적이 없던 처녀들은 무어라 대꾸할 말이 없었으니, 그저 그에게 뭐 좀 먹고 싶은 것이 없느냐고 물었을 뿐이다."],["“I would gladly eat a bit of something,”","\"기꺼이 뭐라도 좀 먹었으면 합니다.\""],["said Don Quixote, “for I feel it would come very seasonably.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"마침 때맞춰 잘된 일이라 여겨지니 말입니다.\""],["The day happened to be a Friday, and in the whole inn there was nothing but some pieces of the fish they call in Castile “abadejo,”","그날은 마침 금요일이었고, 주막을 통틀어 카스티야에서는 '아바데호', 안달루시아에서는 '바칼라오', 또 어떤 곳에서는 '쿠라디요', 또 다른 곳에서는 '송어 새끼'라 부르는 생선 토막 몇 점밖에는 없었다. 그래서 그들은 그에게 송어 새끼를 먹을 수 있겠느냐고 물었으니, 달리 줄 만한 생선이 없었기 때문이다."],["in Andalusia “bacallao,”","\"송어 새끼가 넉넉히 있다면야,\""],["and in some places “curadillo,”","돈키호테가 말했다."],["and in others “troutlet;”","\"그것은 송어 한 마리나 다름없을 것이오."],["so they asked him if he thought he could eat troutlet, for there was no other fish to give him. “If there be troutlets enough,”","내게는 잔돈으로 8레알을 받든 8레알짜리 한 닢을 받든 매한가지이기 때문이오."],["said Don Quixote, “they will be the same thing as a trout; for it is all one to me whether I am given eight reals in small change or a piece of eight; moreover, it may be that these troutlets are like veal, which is better than beef, or kid, which is better than goat.","게다가 어쩌면 이 송어 새끼들은 송아지 고기 같은 것일지도 모르니, 그것은 쇠고기보다 낫고, 또 새끼 염소 고기 같은 것일지도 모르니, 그것은 다 자란 염소 고기보다 낫지요. 그러나 그것이 무엇이든 어서 가져오시오, 무기의 무게와 압박이란 뱃속을 받쳐 주지 않고서는 견뎌 낼 수 없는 것이니 말이오.\""],["But whatever it be let it come quickly, for the burden and pressure of arms cannot be borne without support to the inside.”","그들은 바람을 쐬게 하려고 주막 문간에 그를 위한 상을 차렸고, 주인은 그에게 제대로 불리지도, 제대로 익히지도 못한 마른 대구 한 토막과, 제 갑옷만큼이나 거뭇하고 곰팡내 나는 빵 한 조각을 가져다주었다."],["They laid a table for him at the door of the inn for the sake of the air, and the host brought him a portion of ill-soaked and worse cooked stockfish, and a piece of bread as black and mouldy as his own armour; but a laughable sight it was to see him eating, for having his helmet on and the beaver up, he could not with his own hands put anything into his mouth unless some one else placed it there, and this service one of the ladies rendered him.","그러나 그가 먹는 모습을 보는 것은 가관이었으니, 투구를 쓴 채 면갑을 올리고 있던 터라, 누군가 다른 사람이 입에 넣어 주지 않고서는 제 손으로 무엇 하나 입에 넣을 수가 없었기 때문이다. 이 시중은 귀부인 가운데 한 사람이 들어 주었다."],["But to give him anything to drink was impossible, or would have been so had not the landlord bored a reed, and putting one end in his mouth poured the wine into him through the other; all which he bore with patience rather than sever the ribbons of his helmet.","그러나 마실 것을 주는 일은 도무지 불가능했으니, 주막 주인이 갈대 하나를 뚫어 한쪽 끝을 그의 입에 물리고 다른 쪽 끝으로 포도주를 부어 넣어 주지 않았더라면 정말로 그러했을 것이다. 이 모든 것을 그는 투구의 리본을 끊느니 차라리 참아 내는 편을 택했다."]],[["While this was going on there came up to the inn a sowgelder, who, as he approached, sounded his reed pipe four or five times, and thereby completely convinced Don Quixote that he was in some famous castle, and that they were regaling him with music, and that the stockfish was trout, the bread the whitest, the wenches ladies, and the landlord the castellan of the castle; and consequently he held that his enterprise and sally had been to some purpose.","이런 일이 벌어지고 있는 동안, 주막에 어느 돼지 불까는 자가 다가왔는데, 그는 가까이 오면서 갈대 피리를 네댓 번 불었으니, 그로 인해 돈키호테는 자기가 어느 이름난 성에 있으며, 사람들이 음악으로 자신을 대접하고 있고, 마른 대구는 송어요, 빵은 더없이 흰 빵이며, 들병이들은 귀부인이고, 주막 주인은 그 성의 성주라고 완전히 믿어 버렸다. 그리하여 그는 자신의 출정과 모험이 어느 정도 보람이 있었다고 여겼다."],["But still it distressed him to think he had not been dubbed a knight, for it was plain to him he could not lawfully engage in any adventure without receiving the order of knighthood.","그러나 그는 자신이 아직 기사 서임을 받지 못했다는 생각에 여전히 마음이 괴로웠으니, 기사도의 서임을 받지 않고서는 어떤 모험에도 정당하게 나설 수 없다는 것이 그에게는 분명한 일이었기 때문이다."]],[["# CHAPTER III. WHEREIN IS RELATED THE DROLL WAY IN WHICH DON QUIXOTE HAD HIMSELF DUBBED A KNIGHT","# CHAPTER III."]],[["Harassed by this reflection, he made haste with his scanty pothouse supper, and having finished it called the landlord, and shutting himself into the stable with him, fell on his knees before him, saying, “From this spot I rise not, valiant knight, until your courtesy grants me the boon I seek, one that will redound to your praise and the benefit of the human race.”","이런 생각에 시달리던 그는 보잘것없는 주막 저녁 식사를 서둘러 끝냈고, 다 먹고 나자 주막 주인을 불러 함께 마구간으로 들어가 문을 닫고는 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. \"용맹하신 기사님, 제가 청하는 은혜를 당신의 너그러움으로 베풀어 주시기 전에는 이 자리에서 일어나지 않겠나이다."],["The landlord, seeing his guest at his feet and hearing a speech of this kind, stood staring at him in bewilderment, not knowing what to do or say, and entreating him to rise, but all to no purpose until he had agreed to grant the boon demanded of him.","그것은 당신의 명예를 드높이고 인류 전체에 이로움을 가져다줄 은혜이옵니다.\" 손님이 발치에 꿇어앉아 이런 말을 늘어놓는 것을 본 주막 주인은 어찌할 바를 몰라 멍하니 바라보며, 어떻게 하거나 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알지 못한 채 그저 일어나라고 간청할 뿐이었으나, 청한 은혜를 들어주겠노라 동의하기 전까지는 도무지 소용이 없었다."],["“I looked for no less, my lord, from your High Magnificence,”","\"지체 높으신 분께 그 이상은 바라지도 않았나이다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote, “and I have to tell you that the boon I have asked and your liberality has granted is that you shall dub me knight to-morrow morning, and that to-night I shall watch my arms in the chapel of this your castle; thus to-morrow, as I have said, will be accomplished what I so much desire, enabling me lawfully to roam through all the four quarters of the world seeking adventures on behalf of those in distress, as is the duty of chivalry and of knights-errant like myself, whose ambition is directed to such deeds.”","\"이제 말씀드리건대, 제가 청하고 당신의 후한 인심이 베풀어 주신 그 은혜란, 내일 아침 저를 기사로 서임해 주시는 것, 그리고 오늘 밤 제가 이 성의 예배당에서 제 무구를 지키며 밤을 새우는 것이옵니다. 그리하여 제가 말씀드린 대로 내일이면 제가 그토록 갈망하는 바가 이루어져, 곤경에 처한 이들을 위하여 세상 사방을 정정당당히 누비며 모험을 찾아다닐 수 있게 될 것이니, 이는 기사도의 의무이자 저와 같은 편력 기사의 의무이며, 그러한 위업이야말로 제 포부가 향하는 바이옵니다.\""]],[["The landlord, who, as has been mentioned, was something of a wag, and had already some suspicion of his guest’s want of wits, was quite convinced of it on hearing talk of this kind from him, and to make sport for the night he determined to fall in with his humour.","앞서 말했듯이 다소 익살꾼 기질이 있었던 데다 이미 손님의 정신이 온전치 못하다는 의심을 품고 있던 주막 주인은, 그에게서 이런 말을 듣자 그 의심을 완전히 확신하게 되었고, 밤의 심심풀이 삼아 그의 비위를 맞춰 주기로 작정했다."],["So he told him he was quite right in pursuing the object he had in view, and that such a motive was natural and becoming in cavaliers as distinguished as he seemed and his gallant bearing showed him to be; and that he himself in his younger days had followed the same honourable calling, roaming in quest of adventures in various parts of the world, among others the Curing-grounds of Malaga, the Isles of Riaran, the Precinct of Seville, the Little Market of Segovia, the Olivera of Valencia, the Rondilla of Granada, the Strand of San Lucar, the Colt of Cordova, the Taverns of Toledo, and divers other quarters, where he had proved the nimbleness of his feet and the lightness of his fingers, doing many wrongs, cheating many widows, ruining maids and swindling minors, and, in short, bringing himself under the notice of almost every tribunal and court of justice in Spain; until at last he had retired to this castle of his, where he was living upon his property and upon that of others; and where he received all knights-errant of whatever rank or condition they might be, all for the great love he bore them and that they might share their substance with him in return for his benevolence.","그래서 그는 손님에게, 그대가 마음에 둔 목적을 좇는 것은 지극히 옳은 일이며, 그러한 동기는 그대처럼 기품 있어 보이고 또 늠름한 몸가짐이 입증하는 그토록 빼어난 기사에게 자연스럽고도 어울리는 것이라고 말했다. 또한 자기 자신도 젊은 시절에 똑같은 명예로운 소명을 따라 세상 곳곳을 모험을 찾아 떠돌았으니, 그 가운데에는 말라가의 건어장, 리아란의 섬들, 세비야의 구역, 세고비아의 작은 시장, 발렌시아의 올리베라, 그라나다의 론디야, 산루카르의 해변, 코르도바의 망아지 광장, 톨레도의 주막들을 비롯한 여러 동네가 있었고, 그곳들에서 그는 발의 날쌤과 손가락의 가벼움을 입증해 보였으니, 숱한 못된 짓을 저지르고 숱한 과부를 속이고 처녀들을 망치고 미성년자들을 등쳐 먹어, 한마디로 스페인의 거의 모든 재판소와 법정에 이름이 오르내리는 처지에 이르렀으며, 마침내는 이 성으로 물러나 자기 재산과 남의 재산으로 살아가고 있고, 그곳에서 신분이나 지체가 어떠하든 모든 편력 기사를 맞아들이는데, 이는 오로지 그들에게 품은 크나큰 사랑에서 비롯된 것이요, 그들이 그의 자비에 보답하여 자기네 재물을 그와 나누어 가지도록 하기 위함이라고 했다."],["He told him, moreover, that in this castle of his there was no chapel in which he could watch his armour, as it had been pulled down in order to be rebuilt, but that in a case of necessity it might, he knew, be watched anywhere, and he might watch it that night in a courtyard of the castle, and in the morning, God willing, the requisite ceremonies might be performed so as to have him dubbed a knight, and so thoroughly dubbed that nobody could be more so.","그는 또한 이 성에는 무구를 지키며 밤을 새울 예배당이 없다고 했으니, 다시 짓느라 헐어 버린 까닭이지만, 부득이한 경우에는 어디서든 그 일을 할 수 있음을 잘 안다고 했고, 그러니 오늘 밤은 성의 안뜰에서 무구를 지키면 되고, 아침이 되어 하느님이 허락하시면 필요한 의식을 치러 그를 기사로 서임할 터이며, 그것도 누구보다도 더할 나위 없이 철저하게 서임하겠노라고 했다."],["He asked if he had any money with him, to which Don Quixote replied that he had not a farthing, as in the histories of knights-errant he had never read of any of them carrying any.","그는 돈키호테에게 돈을 지니고 있느냐고 물었고, 이에 돈키호테는 편력 기사 이야기에서 그들 가운데 누구도 돈을 지니고 다녔다는 것을 읽은 적이 없으므로 한 푼도 없다고 대답했다."],["On this point the landlord told him he was mistaken; for, though not recorded in the histories, because in the author’s opinion there was no need to mention anything so obvious and necessary as money and clean shirts, it was not to be supposed therefore that they did not carry them, and he might regard it as certain and established that all knights-errant (about whom there were so many full and unimpeachable books) carried well-furnished purses in case of emergency, and likewise carried shirts and a little box of ointment to cure the wounds they received.","이 점에 대해 주막 주인은 그가 잘못 알고 있다고 일러 주었으니, 비록 이야기에는 기록되어 있지 않으나, 그것은 작가의 생각에 돈이나 깨끗한 셔츠처럼 그토록 뻔하고 필수적인 것은 굳이 언급할 필요가 없었기 때문일 뿐, 그렇다고 해서 그들이 그런 것을 지니고 다니지 않았다고 여겨서는 안 되며, 모든 편력 기사가(이들에 관해서는 그토록 충실하고 흠잡을 데 없는 책들이 많지 않은가) 만일의 경우에 대비해 두둑한 지갑을 지니고 다녔고, 또한 셔츠와 입은 상처를 치료할 연고 한 통도 지니고 다녔음을 확실하고도 확정된 사실로 여겨도 좋다고 했다."],["For in those plains and deserts where they engaged in combat and came out wounded, it was not always that there was some one to cure them, unless indeed they had for a friend some sage magician to succour them at once by fetching through the air upon a cloud some damsel or dwarf with a vial of water of such virtue that by tasting one drop of it they were cured of their hurts and wounds in an instant and left as sound as if they had not received any damage whatever.","그도 그럴 것이, 그들이 싸움을 벌이고 상처를 입은 채 빠져나오던 그 들판과 사막에서는 그들을 치료해 줄 누군가가 늘 있는 것은 아니었으니, 다만 그들에게 어떤 현명한 마법사가 벗으로 있어, 구름을 타고 공중을 가로질러 어떤 처녀나 난쟁이를 데려와, 그 효험이 어찌나 대단한지 한 방울만 맛보아도 상처와 부상이 순식간에 나아 마치 아무런 해도 입지 않은 것처럼 멀쩡해지는 그런 물병 하나를 들려 보내 즉시 구해 주는 경우라면 모를까 말이다."],["But in case this should not occur, the knights of old took care to see that their squires were provided with money and other requisites, such as lint and ointments for healing purposes; and when it happened that knights had no squires (which was rarely and seldom the case) they themselves carried everything in cunning saddle-bags that were hardly seen on the horse’s croup, as if it were something else of more importance, because, unless for some such reason, carrying saddle-bags was not very favourably regarded among knights-errant.","그러나 이런 일이 일어나지 않을 경우에 대비하여, 옛 기사들은 자기 종자들이 돈과 그 밖의 필수품, 이를테면 치료에 쓸 무명실과 연고 따위를 갖추도록 빈틈없이 챙겼고, 기사에게 종자가 없는 경우에는(이는 드물고 좀처럼 없는 일이었지만) 자기네가 직접 모든 것을 교묘한 안장주머니에 넣어 다녔는데, 그 주머니는 마치 무언가 더 중요한 물건인 양 말 엉덩이 위에서도 좀처럼 눈에 띄지 않았으니, 그도 그럴 것이 그런 까닭이라도 있지 않고서는 안장주머니를 지니고 다니는 일이 편력 기사들 사이에서 그리 곱게 여겨지지 않았기 때문이다."],["He therefore advised him (and, as his godson so soon to be, he might even command him) never from that time forth to travel without money and the usual requirements, and he would find the advantage of them when he least expected it.","그리하여 그는 돈키호테에게, 앞으로는 결코 돈과 흔히 챙기는 필수품 없이 길을 떠나지 말라고 충고했고(또한 이제 곧 그의 대자가 될 처지였으니 명령하다시피 할 수도 있었다), 그러면 가장 뜻하지 않은 때에 그것들의 이로움을 알게 되리라고 했다."]],[["Don Quixote promised to follow his advice scrupulously, and it was arranged forthwith that he should watch his armour in a large yard at one side of the inn; so, collecting it all together, Don Quixote placed it on a trough that stood by the side of a well, and bracing his buckler on his arm he grasped his lance and began with a stately air to march up and down in front of the trough, and as he began his march night began to fall.","돈키호테는 그 충고를 어김없이 따르겠노라 약속했고, 이내 그가 주막 한쪽의 넓은 마당에서 무구를 지키며 밤을 새우기로 정해졌다. 그리하여 돈키호테는 무구를 죄다 한데 모아 우물 곁에 놓인 여물통 위에 올려놓고는, 방패를 팔에 단단히 끼고 창을 움켜쥔 채 위풍당당한 태도로 여물통 앞을 오락가락 행군하기 시작했으니, 그가 행군을 시작할 무렵 밤이 내리기 시작했다."]],[["The landlord told all the people who were in the inn about the craze of his guest, the watching of the armour, and the dubbing ceremony he contemplated.","주막 주인은 주막에 있던 모든 사람에게 손님의 광기에 대해, 무구를 지키며 밤을 새우는 일과 그가 꾀하고 있는 서임식에 대해 이야기했다."],["Full of wonder at so strange a form of madness, they flocked to see it from a distance, and observed with what composure he sometimes paced up and down, or sometimes, leaning on his lance, gazed on his armour without taking his eyes off it for ever so long; and as the night closed in with a light from the moon so brilliant that it might vie with his that lent it, everything the novice knight did was plainly seen by all.","그토록 기이한 형태의 광기에 잔뜩 놀란 사람들은 멀찍이서 그것을 구경하려고 몰려들었고, 그가 때로는 얼마나 침착하게 오락가락 거니는지, 또 때로는 창에 몸을 기댄 채 한참이 지나도록 눈을 떼지 않고 자기 무구를 응시하는지를 지켜보았으니, 밤이 깊어지면서 달이 어찌나 환하게 빛을 내뿜었던지 그 빛을 빌려 준 태양과 견줄 만하여, 풋내기 기사가 하는 모든 일이 사람들 눈에 또렷이 보였다."]],[["Meanwhile one of the carriers who were in the inn thought fit to water his team, and it was necessary to remove Don Quixote’s armour as it lay on the trough; but he seeing the other approach hailed him in a loud voice, “O thou, whoever thou art, rash knight that comest to lay hands on the armour of the most valorous errant that ever girt on sword, have a care what thou dost; touch it not unless thou wouldst lay down thy life as the penalty of thy rashness.”","그러는 사이 주막에 있던 짐꾼 가운데 하나가 자기 노새들에게 물을 먹여야겠다 생각했는데, 그러자면 여물통 위에 놓인 돈키호테의 무구를 치워야 했다. 하지만 그자가 다가오는 것을 본 돈키호테는 큰 소리로 그를 불러 세웠다."],["The carrier gave no heed to these words (and he would have done better to heed them if he had been heedful of his health), but seizing it by the straps flung the armour some distance from him.","\"오, 그대가 누구든, 일찍이 칼을 차 본 가장 용맹한 편력 기사의 무구에 손을 대려 다가오는 무모한 기사여, 그대가 하는 짓을 조심하라. 그대의 무모함에 대한 대가로 목숨을 내놓을 작정이 아니라면 그것에 손대지 마라.\""],["Seeing this, Don Quixote raised his eyes to heaven, and fixing his thoughts, apparently, upon his lady Dulcinea, exclaimed, “Aid me, lady mine, in this the first encounter that presents itself to this breast which thou holdest in subjection; let not thy favour and protection fail me in this first jeopardy;”","짐꾼은 이 말에 아랑곳하지 않았으나(자기 건강을 생각했더라면 귀 기울이는 편이 나았을 것이다), 가죽끈을 붙잡아 무구를 저만치 멀리 내던졌다. 이를 본 돈키호테는 하늘로 눈을 들어, 보아하니 자신의 여인 둘시네아에게 생각을 모으는 듯, 이렇게 외쳤다."],["and, with these words and others to the same purpose, dropping his buckler he lifted his lance with both hands and with it smote such a blow on the carrier’s head that he stretched him on the ground, so stunned that had he followed it up with a second there would have been no need of a surgeon to cure him.","\"나의 여인이시여, 그대가 굴복시켜 거느린 이 가슴에 처음으로 닥쳐온 이 싸움에서 저를 도우소서. 이 첫 위기에서 그대의 은총과 보호가 저를 저버리지 않게 하소서.\""],["This done, he picked up his armour and returned to his beat with the same serenity as before.","그러고는 이 말과 같은 뜻의 다른 말들을 내뱉으며 방패를 떨어뜨리고 두 손으로 창을 들어 올려, 그것으로 짐꾼의 머리를 어찌나 세게 후려쳤는지 그를 땅바닥에 뻗게 만들었으니, 그가 너무도 정신을 잃어 만일 두 번째 일격을 가했더라면 그를 치료해 줄 의사가 필요 없었을 정도였다. 이 일을 마친 그는 무구를 집어 들고 전과 똑같이 태연하게 자기 보초 자리로 돌아갔다."]],[["Shortly after this, another, not knowing what had happened (for the carrier still lay senseless), came with the same object of giving water to his mules, and was proceeding to remove the armour in order to clear the trough, when Don Quixote, without uttering a word or imploring aid from anyone, once more dropped his buckler and once more lifted his lance, and without actually breaking the second carrier’s head into pieces, made more than three of it, for he laid it open in four.","이 일이 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않아 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 모르는 또 다른 자가(짐꾼은 여전히 정신을 잃은 채 누워 있었다) 똑같이 자기 노새들에게 물을 먹이려는 목적으로 다가와, 여물통을 비우려고 무구를 치우려던 참이었다. 그러자 돈키호테는 한마디 말도 없이, 누구에게도 도움을 청하지 않은 채, 또다시 방패를 떨어뜨리고 또다시 창을 들어 올려, 두 번째 짐꾼의 머리를 산산조각 내지는 않았으나 네 조각으로 쪼개 놓았으니 세 조각보다 더 만든 셈이었다."],["At the noise all the people of the inn ran to the spot, and among them the landlord.","그 소란에 주막의 사람들이 모두 그 자리로 달려왔는데, 그 가운데에는 주막 주인도 있었다."],["Seeing this, Don Quixote braced his buckler on his arm, and with his hand on his sword exclaimed, “O Lady of Beauty, strength and support of my faint heart, it is time for thee to turn the eyes of thy greatness on this thy captive knight on the brink of so mighty an adventure.”","이를 본 돈키호테는 방패를 팔에 단단히 끼고 칼자루에 손을 얹은 채 외쳤다. \"오, 아름다움의 여인이시여, 제 가냘픈 마음의 힘이요 버팀목이시여, 이토록 거대한 모험의 문턱에 선 그대의 포로 기사에게 그대 위대함의 눈길을 돌리실 때가 되었나이다.\""],["By this he felt himself so inspired that he would not have flinched if all the carriers in the world had assailed him.","이로써 그는 세상의 모든 짐꾼이 덤벼든다 해도 결코 물러서지 않을 만큼 스스로 고무됨을 느꼈다."],["The comrades of the wounded perceiving the plight they were in began from a distance to shower stones on Don Quixote, who screened himself as best he could with his buckler, not daring to quit the trough and leave his armour unprotected.","부상자의 동료들은 자기네 처지를 알아채고는 멀찍이서 돈키호테에게 돌을 퍼붓기 시작했고, 그는 방패로 할 수 있는 한 몸을 가리며, 여물통을 떠나 무구를 무방비로 내버려 둘 엄두를 내지 못했다."],["The landlord shouted to them to leave him alone, for he had already told them that he was mad, and as a madman he would not be accountable even if he killed them all.","주막 주인은 그를 내버려 두라고 그들에게 소리쳤으니, 이미 그가 미친 사람이라고 일러 두었던 터라 미친 사람으로서 설령 그들을 모조리 죽인다 해도 책임을 물을 수 없으리라는 것이었다."],["Still louder shouted Don Quixote, calling them knaves and traitors, and the lord of the castle, who allowed knights-errant to be treated in this fashion, a villain and a low-born knight whom, had he received the order of knighthood, he would call to account for his treachery.","돈키호테는 더더욱 큰 소리로 외치며 그들을 악당이요 배신자라 불렀고, 편력 기사들이 이런 식으로 다루어지도록 내버려 둔 성주를 가리켜 비열한 자요 천한 핏줄의 기사라 하면서, 만일 그가 기사 서품을 받은 자였다면 그 배신에 대해 책임을 물었으리라고 했다. \"허나 너희,\""],["“But of you,”","그가 외쳤다."],["he cried, “base and vile rabble, I make no account; fling, strike, come on, do all ye can against me, ye shall see what the reward of your folly and insolence will be.”","\"비천하고 천박한 무리들은 안중에도 없도다. 던지고, 때리고, 덤벼라, 나를 향해 할 수 있는 모든 짓을 다 해 보아라, 너희의 어리석음과 무례함이 어떤 대가를 치르는지 보게 되리라.\""],["This he uttered with so much spirit and boldness that he filled his assailants with a terrible fear, and as much for this reason as at the persuasion of the landlord they left off stoning him, and he allowed them to carry off the wounded, and with the same calmness and composure as before resumed the watch over his armour.","그가 이 말을 어찌나 기개 있고 대담하게 내뱉었는지 공격하던 자들을 무시무시한 두려움으로 가득 채웠고, 이런 까닭에서나 주막 주인의 설득에서나 그들은 돌팔매질을 그만두었으며, 그는 그들이 부상자들을 데려가도록 허락하고는, 전과 똑같이 차분하고 침착하게 다시 무구를 지키는 보초로 돌아갔다."]],[["But these freaks of his guest were not much to the liking of the landlord, so he determined to cut matters short and confer upon him at once the unlucky order of knighthood before any further misadventure could occur; so, going up to him, he apologised for the rudeness which, without his knowledge, had been offered to him by these low people, who, however, had been well punished for their audacity.","그러나 손님의 이런 별난 짓거리가 주막 주인의 마음에 그리 들지 않았으므로, 그는 더 이상의 불상사가 일어나기 전에 일을 짧게 마무리 짓고 당장 그 불운한 기사 서품을 그에게 내려 주기로 작정했다. 그리하여 그에게 다가가, 자기도 모르는 사이에 이 천한 자들이 무례를 범한 데 대해 사과하면서, 그래도 그들이 그 뻔뻔함에 대해 톡톡히 벌을 받지 않았느냐고 했다."],["As he had already told him, he said, there was no chapel in the castle, nor was it needed for what remained to be done, for, as he understood the ceremonial of the order, the whole point of being dubbed a knight lay in the accolade and in the slap on the shoulder, and that could be administered in the middle of a field; and that he had now done all that was needful as to watching the armour, for all requirements were satisfied by a watch of two hours only, while he had been more than four about it.","이미 말씀드린 대로 성에는 예배당이 없으나 앞으로 남은 일을 위해서는 그것이 필요치 않으니, 자신이 그 의식의 절차를 이해하기로는 기사로 서임되는 일의 핵심은 모두 목덜미를 치는 일과 어깨를 두드리는 일에 있고, 그것은 들판 한복판에서도 행할 수 있다고 했다. 또한 무구를 지키는 일에 관해서라면 이제 필요한 바를 모두 마쳤으니, 단 두 시간의 보초만으로도 모든 요건이 충족되거늘 그는 이미 네 시간이 넘도록 그것을 지켰다고 했다."],["Don Quixote believed it all, and told him he stood there ready to obey him, and to make an end of it with as much despatch as possible; for, if he were again attacked, and felt himself to be dubbed knight, he would not, he thought, leave a soul alive in the castle, except such as out of respect he might spare at his bidding.","돈키호테는 그 모든 말을 곧이곧대로 믿고는, 자신은 그의 분부를 따를 준비가 되어 있으니 되도록 빨리 일을 마무리 지어 달라고 했다. 만일 자신이 또다시 공격을 받는다면, 그리고 자신이 기사로 서임되었음을 느낀다면, 자기 생각으로는 존경하는 마음에서 그의 명에 따라 살려 줄 자들을 제외하고는 성안에 한 사람도 살려 두지 않으리라는 것이었다."]],[["Thus warned and menaced, the castellan forthwith brought out a book in which he used to enter the straw and barley he served out to the carriers, and, with a lad carrying a candle-end, and the two damsels already mentioned, he returned to where Don Quixote stood, and bade him kneel down.","이렇게 경고받고 위협당한 성주는 곧바로 책 한 권을 꺼내 왔으니, 그것은 짐꾼들에게 내준 짚과 보리를 적어 두던 장부였다. 그리고 양초 동강을 든 사내아이와 앞서 말한 두 처녀를 데리고 돈키호테가 서 있는 곳으로 돌아와 무릎을 꿇으라고 일렀다."],["Then, reading from his account-book as if he were repeating some devout prayer, in the middle of his delivery he raised his hand and gave him a sturdy blow on the neck, and then, with his own sword, a smart slap on the shoulder, all the while muttering between his teeth as if he was saying his prayers.","그러고는 마치 무슨 경건한 기도라도 외우는 양 자기 회계 장부를 읽어 내려가다가, 읊조리던 도중에 손을 들어 그의 목덜미에 묵직한 일격을 가하고, 이어서 그 자신의 칼로 어깨를 찰싹 후려쳤으니, 그러는 내내 마치 기도를 올리듯 이를 사이로 무어라 중얼거렸다."],["Having done this, he directed one of the ladies to gird on his sword, which she did with great self-possession and gravity, and not a little was required to prevent a burst of laughter at each stage of the ceremony; but what they had already seen of the novice knight’s prowess kept their laughter within bounds.","이 일을 마치고 그는 처녀 가운데 하나에게 칼을 채워 주라고 지시했고, 그녀는 더없이 침착하고 진중하게 그 일을 해냈으니, 의식의 단계마다 터져 나오려는 웃음을 참기 위해 적잖은 노력이 필요했으나, 풋내기 기사의 무용을 이미 목격한 터라 그 웃음을 가까스로 억눌렀다."],["On girding him with the sword the worthy lady said to him, “May God make your worship a very fortunate knight, and grant you success in battle.”","칼을 채워 주면서 그 갸륵한 처녀가 그에게 말했다. \"하느님께서 나리를 매우 운 좋은 기사로 만드시고, 싸움에서 승리를 내려 주시기를 비옵니다.\""],["Don Quixote asked her name in order that he might from that time forward know to whom he was beholden for the favour he had received, as he meant to confer upon her some portion of the honour he acquired by the might of his arm.","돈키호테는 그녀의 이름을 물었으니, 앞으로 자신이 받은 그 은혜를 누구에게 신세 지고 있는지 알기 위함이요, 자기 팔의 힘으로 얻을 명예의 일부를 그녀에게 나누어 줄 생각에서였다."],["She answered with great humility that she was called La Tolosa, and that she was the daughter of a cobbler of Toledo who lived in the stalls of Sanchobienaya, and that wherever she might be she would serve and esteem him as her lord.","그녀는 더없이 겸손하게, 자신은 라 톨로사라 불리며 산초비에나야 노점에서 살던 톨레도의 한 구두장이의 딸이고, 어디에 있든 그를 자기 주인으로 섬기고 우러르겠노라고 대답했다."],["Don Quixote said in reply that she would do him a favour if thenceforward she assumed the “Don” and called herself Dona Tolosa.","이에 돈키호테는, 앞으로는 \"돈\"을 붙여 도냐 톨로사라 자칭한다면 자기에게 호의를 베푸는 것이 되리라고 답했다."],["She promised she would, and then the other buckled on his spur, and with her followed almost the same conversation as with the lady of the sword.","그녀가 그러겠노라 약속하자, 다른 처녀가 그에게 박차를 채워 주었는데, 칼을 채워 준 처녀와 거의 똑같은 대화가 오갔다."],["He asked her name, and she said it was La Molinera, and that she was the daughter of a respectable miller of Antequera; and of her likewise Don Quixote requested that she would adopt the “Don”","그가 이름을 묻자 그녀는 라 몰리네라라고 했고, 안테케라의 점잖은 방앗간 주인의 딸이라고 했다."],["and call herself Dona Molinera, making offers to her further services and favours.","돈키호테는 그녀에게도 \"돈\"을 붙여 도냐 몰리네라라 자칭해 달라고 청하며, 앞으로 더 봉사하고 호의를 베풀겠노라 약속했다."]],[["Having thus, with hot haste and speed, brought to a conclusion these never-till-now-seen ceremonies, Don Quixote was on thorns until he saw himself on horseback sallying forth in quest of adventures; and saddling Rocinante at once he mounted, and embracing his host, as he returned thanks for his kindness in knighting him, he addressed him in language so extraordinary that it is impossible to convey an idea of it or report it.","이렇게 부랴부랴 서둘러 지금껏 본 적 없는 이 의식들을 마무리 짓고 나자, 돈키호테는 말에 올라 모험을 찾아 출정하는 자기 모습을 보기 전까지는 바늘방석에 앉은 듯 안절부절못했다. 그리하여 당장 로시난테에 안장을 얹어 올라타고는, 자신을 기사로 서임해 준 그 친절에 사례하며 주인을 끌어안았으니, 어찌나 기상천외한 말로 그에게 인사를 건넸던지 도무지 그 뜻을 전하거나 옮길 길이 없을 정도였다."],["The landlord, to get him out of the inn, replied with no less rhetoric though with shorter words, and without calling upon him to pay the reckoning let him go with a Godspeed.","주막 주인은 그를 주막에서 내보내려고, 못지않게 웅변조이나 더 짧은 말로 답했으며, 셈을 치르라고 청하지도 않은 채 잘 가라는 인사와 함께 그를 보내 주었다."]],[["# CHAPTER IV. OF WHAT HAPPENED TO OUR KNIGHT WHEN HE LEFT THE INN","# CHAPTER IV."]],[["Day was dawning when Don Quixote quitted the inn, so happy, so gay, so exhilarated at finding himself now dubbed a knight, that his joy was like to burst his horse-girths.","돈키호테가 주막을 나섰을 때는 동이 터 오고 있었고, 이제 자신이 기사로 서임되었음을 깨달은 그는 어찌나 행복하고 들뜨고 기뻐했던지, 그 기쁨에 말의 뱃대끈이 터져 버릴 지경이었다."],["However, recalling the advice of his host as to the requisites he ought to carry with him, especially that referring to money and shirts, he determined to go home and provide himself with all, and also with a squire, for he reckoned upon securing a farm-labourer, a neighbour of his, a poor man with a family, but very well qualified for the office of squire to a knight.","그러나 그는 지녀야 할 필수품들에 관하여, 특히 돈과 셔츠에 관하여 주막 주인이 일러 준 충고를 떠올리고는, 집으로 돌아가 그 모든 것을 마련하고 종자도 하나 구하기로 마음먹었다. 그는 자기 이웃에 사는 농사꾼 하나를 점찍어 두고 있었으니, 그는 딸린 식구가 있는 가난한 사람이었으나 기사의 종자 노릇을 하기에는 더없이 알맞은 사람이었다."],["With this object he turned his horse’s head towards his village, and Rocinante, thus reminded of his old quarters, stepped out so briskly that he hardly seemed to tread the earth.","이런 생각으로 그는 말 머리를 자기 마을 쪽으로 돌렸고, 옛 보금자리를 떠올린 로시난테는 어찌나 경쾌하게 발을 내딛던지 땅을 밟는 것 같지도 않을 정도였다."]],[["He had not gone far, when out of a thicket on his right there seemed to come feeble cries as of some one in distress, and the instant he heard them he exclaimed, “Thanks be to heaven for the favour it accords me, that it so soon offers me an opportunity of fulfilling the obligation I have undertaken, and gathering the fruit of my ambition.","얼마 가지 않았을 때, 오른편 덤불숲에서 누군가 곤경에 빠진 듯한 가냘픈 비명 소리가 들려오는 것 같았고, 그 소리를 듣자마자 그는 외쳤다. \"내가 떠맡은 의무를 이행하고 내 야망의 결실을 거둘 기회를, 이토록 빨리 내려 주신 하늘의 은총에 감사드리노라."],["These cries, no doubt, come from some man or woman in want of help, and needing my aid and protection;”","이 비명은 분명 도움을 필요로 하는 어떤 남자나 여자에게서, 나의 구원과 보호가 절실한 누군가에게서 나오는 것이렷다.\""],["and wheeling, he turned Rocinante in the direction whence the cries seemed to proceed.","그러고는 방향을 틀어 로시난테를 비명이 들려오는 듯한 쪽으로 몰았다."],["He had gone but a few paces into the wood, when he saw a mare tied to an oak, and tied to another, and stripped from the waist upwards, a youth of about fifteen years of age, from whom the cries came.","숲속으로 몇 걸음 들어서자, 떡갈나무에 매여 있는 암말 한 마리가 보였고, 또 다른 나무에는 허리 위로 옷이 벗겨진 채 매여 있는 열다섯 살가량의 소년이 보였는데, 비명은 바로 그에게서 나오고 있었다."],["Nor were they without cause, for a lusty farmer was flogging him with a belt and following up every blow with scoldings and commands, repeating, “Your mouth shut and your eyes open!”","그럴 만도 한 것이, 건장한 농사꾼 하나가 허리띠로 그를 매질하며 한 대 칠 때마다 꾸짖고 명령하기를 거듭하면서 \"주둥이는 닫고 눈은 똑바로 뜨렷다!\" 하고 외치는데, 소년은 \"다시는 안 그러겠어요, 주인님."],["while the youth made answer, “I won’t do it again, master mine; by God’s passion I won’t do it again, and I’ll take more care of the flock another time.”","하느님의 수난을 두고 맹세코 다시는 안 그러고, 다음번엔 양 떼를 더 잘 챙길게요\" 하고 대답하는 것이었다."]],[["Seeing what was going on, Don Quixote said in an angry voice, “Discourteous knight, it ill becomes you to assail one who cannot defend himself; mount your steed and take your lance” (for there was a lance leaning against the oak to which the mare was tied), “and I will make you know that you are behaving as a coward.” The farmer, seeing before him this figure in full armour brandishing a lance over his head, gave himself up for dead, and made answer meekly, “Sir Knight, this youth that I am chastising is my servant, employed by me to watch a flock of sheep that I have hard by, and he is so careless that I lose one every day, and when I punish him for his carelessness and knavery he says I do it out of niggardliness, to escape paying him the wages I owe him, and before God, and on my soul, he lies.”","이 광경을 본 돈키호테는 성난 목소리로 말했다. \"무례한 기사여, 스스로를 지킬 수 없는 자를 공격하다니 그대에게 어울리지 않는 짓이로다. 말에 올라 그대의 창을 들라.\" (암말이 매여 있는 떡갈나무에 창 한 자루가 기대어 있었던 것이다.) \"그리하면 그대가 비겁자처럼 굴고 있음을 내 똑똑히 알게 해 주리라.\" 농사꾼은 자기 앞에 갑옷을 온전히 갖춰 입은 이 형상이 창을 머리 위로 휘두르는 것을 보고는 이제 죽었구나 싶어, 고분고분 대답했다. \"기사 나리, 제가 매질하고 있는 이 녀석은 제 하인입니다요. 이 근처에 둔 양 떼를 지키라고 부리는 놈인데, 어찌나 칠칠치 못한지 날마다 한 마리씩 잃어버립지요. 그 부주의함과 못된 짓을 벌하면, 제가 줘야 할 품삯을 떼먹으려고 인색하게 군다고 하니, 하느님 앞에서, 제 영혼을 걸고, 저놈이 거짓말을 하는 겁니다요.\""]],[["“Lies before me, base clown!”","\"내 앞에서 거짓말이라니, 천한 촌놈 같으니!\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."],["“By the sun that shines on us I have a mind to run you through with this lance.","\"우리를 비추는 저 태양을 두고 맹세컨대, 이 창으로 그대를 꿰뚫어 버리고 싶구나. 두말 말고 당장 저 아이에게 품삯을 치르라."],["Pay him at once without another word; if not, by the God that rules us I will make an end of you, and annihilate you on the spot; release him instantly.”","그러지 않으면, 우리를 다스리시는 하느님을 두고 맹세코, 그대를 끝장내고 이 자리에서 없애 버리리라. 당장 저 아이를 풀어 주라.\""]],[["The farmer hung his head, and without a word untied his servant, of whom Don Quixote asked how much his master owed him.","농사꾼은 고개를 떨구고 한마디 말도 없이 자기 하인을 풀어 주었고, 돈키호테는 소년에게 주인이 얼마를 빚졌는지 물었다."]],[["He replied, nine months at seven reals a month.","소년은 한 달에 7레알씩 아홉 달 치라고 대답했다."],["Don Quixote added it up, found that it came to sixty-three reals, and told the farmer to pay it down immediately, if he did not want to die for it.","돈키호테는 그것을 합산하여 63레알이 됨을 알아내고는, 농사꾼에게 죽고 싶지 않거든 당장 그 돈을 치르라고 일렀다."]],[["The trembling clown replied that as he lived and by the oath he had sworn (though he had not sworn any) it was not so much; for there were to be taken into account and deducted three pairs of shoes he had given him, and a real for two blood-lettings when he was sick.","벌벌 떨던 촌놈은, 자기 목숨을 걸고 또 자신이 한 맹세를 두고(실은 아무 맹세도 한 적이 없었지만) 그렇게 많지는 않다고 대답했으니, 그 까닭은 그에게 신발 세 켤레를 사 준 것을 셈에 넣어 빼야 하고, 또 그가 아팠을 때 두 번 피를 뽑아 준 값으로 1레알도 빼야 하기 때문이라는 것이었다."]],[["“All that is very well,”","\"그것도 다 좋다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “but let the shoes and the blood-lettings stand as a setoff against the blows you have given him without any cause; for if he spoiled the leather of the shoes you paid for, you have damaged that of his body, and if the barber took blood from him when he was sick, you have drawn it when he was sound; so on that score he owes you nothing.”","\"하지만 신발 값과 피 뽑은 값은 그대가 아무 까닭 없이 저 아이에게 가한 매질의 상쇄로 치게 하라. 만약 저 아이가 그대가 값을 치른 신발의 가죽을 망가뜨렸다면, 그대는 저 아이 몸뚱이의 가죽을 망가뜨렸으며, 이발사가 저 아이가 아팠을 때 피를 뽑았다면, 그대는 멀쩡할 때 그 피를 뽑은 셈이니, 그 셈으로 따지면 저 아이가 그대에게 빚진 것은 아무것도 없도다.\""]],[["“The difficulty is, Sir Knight, that I have no money here; let Andres come home with me, and I will pay him all, real by real.”","\"그게 곤란한 게요, 기사 나리. 지금 제겐 여기 돈이 없습니다요. 안드레스를 저와 함께 집으로 가게 해 주십쇼. 그럼 1레알씩 한 푼도 빠짐없이 다 치르겠습니다요.\""]],[["“I go with him!”","\"내가 저자랑 가다니요!\" 소년이 말했다."],["said the youth.","\"안 됩니다요, 천만에요!"],["“Nay, God forbid!","안 됩니다, 나리."],["No, señor, not for the world; for once alone with me, he would flay me like a Saint Bartholomew.”","절대로 안 돼요. 한번 저랑 단둘이 있게 되면, 성 바르톨로메오처럼 제 살가죽을 벗겨 버릴걸요.\""]],[["“He will do nothing of the kind,”","\"그자는 그런 짓을 하지 못할 것이다.\""],["said Don Quixote; “I have only to command, and he will obey me; and as he has sworn to me by the order of knighthood which he has received, I leave him free, and I guarantee the payment.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 명령만 하면 그자는 내게 복종할 것이며, 그자가 자기가 받은 기사도의 이름을 걸고 내게 맹세했으니, 나는 그자를 자유로이 풀어 주고 품삯의 지불을 보증하노라.\""]],[["“Consider what you are saying, señor,”","\"무슨 말씀을 하시는지 헤아려 보십쇼, 나리.\" 소년이 말했다."],["said the youth; “this master of mine is not a knight, nor has he received any order of knighthood; for he is Juan Haldudo the Rich, of Quintanar.”","\"이 제 주인은 기사가 아니고, 무슨 기사 서임을 받은 적도 없는걸요. 이 사람은 킨타나르에 사는 부자 후안 알두도란 사람입니다요.\""]],[["“That matters little,”","\"그게 무슨 대수더냐.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote; “there may be Haldudos knights; moreover, everyone is the son of his works.”","\"알두도 가문에도 기사가 있을 수 있느니라. 게다가 사람은 누구나 제 행실의 자식이니라.\""]],[["“That is true,”","\"그건 맞는 말씀입죠.\""],["said Andres; “but this master of mine--of what works is he the son, when he refuses me the wages of my sweat and labour?”","안드레스가 말했다. \"한데 이 제 주인은—제가 땀 흘려 일한 품삯을 안 주는 걸 보면, 대체 무슨 행실의 자식이란 말입니까요?\""]],[["“I do not refuse, brother Andres,”","\"안 주는 게 아니다, 안드레스야.\" 농사꾼이 말했다."],["said the farmer, “be good enough to come along with me, and I swear by all the orders of knighthood there are in the world to pay you as I have agreed, real by real, and perfumed.”","\"제발 나랑 같이 가자꾸나. 이 세상에 있는 모든 기사도를 두고 맹세컨대, 약속한 대로 1레알씩 한 푼도 빠짐없이, 게다가 향내까지 풍기게 해서 치러 주마.\""]],[["“For the perfumery I excuse you,” said Don Quixote; “give it to him in reals, and I shall be satisfied; and see that you do as you have sworn; if not, by the same oath I swear to come back and hunt you out and punish you; and I shall find you though you should lie closer than a lizard. And if you desire to know who it is lays this command upon you, that you be more firmly bound to obey it, know that I am the valorous Don Quixote of La Mancha, the undoer of wrongs and injustices; and so, God be with you, and keep in mind what you have promised and sworn under those penalties that have been already declared to you.”","\"향내는 됐으니 그건 면해 주마.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그저 레알로 치러 주면 나는 그것으로 만족하겠노라. 그리고 그대가 맹세한 대로 행할지어다. 그러지 않으면, 같은 맹세를 두고 내 다시 돌아와 그대를 찾아내어 벌하리라. 그대가 도마뱀보다 더 꼭꼭 숨는다 해도 내 기어이 찾아낼 것이니라. 또 그대가 이 명령을 더욱 굳게 따르도록, 그대에게 이 명령을 내리는 자가 누구인지 알고 싶거든, 알지어다, 나는 용맹한 라만차의 돈키호테이며, 그릇됨과 불의를 바로잡는 자로다. 그러니 하느님이 그대와 함께하시기를, 그리고 이미 그대에게 일러 준 그 형벌들을 명심하여 그대가 약속하고 맹세한 바를 잊지 말지어다.\""]],[["So saying, he gave Rocinante the spur and was soon out of reach.","이렇게 말하고 그는 로시난테에 박차를 가하여 이내 손이 닿지 않을 만큼 멀어졌다. 농사꾼은 눈으로 그를 좇다가, 그가 숲을 벗어나 더는 보이지 않게 되자, 자기 아이 안드레스에게 돌아서서 말했다."],["The farmer followed him with his eyes, and when he saw that he had cleared the wood and was no longer in sight, he turned to his boy Andres, and said, “Come here, my son, I want to pay you what I owe you, as that undoer of wrongs has commanded me.”","\"이리 오너라, 얘야. 저 그릇됨을 바로잡는 자가 내게 명령한 대로, 내가 네게 빚진 것을 치러 주마.\""]],[["“My oath on it,”","\"맹세코 말씀드리는데요.\" 안드레스가 말했다."],["said Andres, “your worship will be well advised to obey the command of that good knight--may he live a thousand years--for, as he is a valiant and just judge, by Roque, if you do not pay me, he will come back and do as he said.”","\"그 훌륭하신 기사 나리의—천 년만큼 오래 사시기를—명령에 따르시는 게 나리한테도 현명한 일일 겁니다요. 그분은 용맹하고 공정한 재판관이시니까, 로케 성인을 두고 말하건대, 만약 제게 품삯을 안 주시면 그분이 돌아와서 말씀하신 대로 하실 거예요.\""]],[["“My oath on it, too,”","\"나도 맹세코 그럴 테다.\""],["said the farmer; “but as I have a strong affection for you, I want to add to the debt in order to add to the payment;”","농사꾼이 말했다. \"한데 내가 너를 어찌나 끔찍이 아끼는지, 더 많이 갚으려고 빚을 더 늘려 줘야겠구나.\""],["and seizing him by the arm, he tied him up again, and gave him such a flogging that he left him for dead.","그러고는 소년의 팔을 붙잡아 다시 묶어 놓고는, 그가 죽었나 싶을 만큼 흠씬 매질을 해 댔다."]],[["“Now, Master Andres,” said the farmer, “call on the undoer of wrongs; you will find he won’t undo that, though I am not sure that I have quite done with you, for I have a good mind to flay you alive.” But at last he untied him, and gave him leave to go look for his judge in order to put the sentence pronounced into execution.","\"자, 안드레스 도련님.\" 농사꾼이 말했다. \"그릇됨을 바로잡는 자를 불러 보시지. 그자가 이건 바로잡지 못할 게다. 하긴 내가 너랑 아주 끝장을 봤는지는 모르겠다만. 네놈 살가죽을 산 채로 벗겨 버리고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같으니 말이다.\" 그러나 마침내 그는 소년을 풀어 주고는, 선고된 판결을 집행하도록 그 재판관을 찾아가 보라며 떠나게 해 주었다."]],[["Andres went off rather down in the mouth, swearing he would go to look for the valiant Don Quixote of La Mancha and tell him exactly what had happened, and that all would have to be repaid him sevenfold; but for all that, he went off weeping, while his master stood laughing.","안드레스는 다소 풀이 죽은 채로 떠나면서, 용맹한 라만차의 돈키호테를 찾아가 일어난 일을 그대로 일러바칠 것이며, 그러면 일곱 곱절로 다 갚아 주게 되리라고 별렀다. 그러면서도 그는 울며 떠났고, 그동안 그의 주인은 웃고 서 있었다."]],[["Thus did the valiant Don Quixote right that wrong, and, thoroughly satisfied with what had taken place, as he considered he had made a very happy and noble beginning with his knighthood, he took the road towards his village in perfect self-content, saying in a low voice, “Well mayest thou this day call thyself fortunate above all on earth, O Dulcinea del Toboso, fairest of the fair!","이렇게 용맹한 돈키호테는 그 그릇됨을 바로잡았으니, 일어난 일에 더없이 만족하고, 자신의 기사도를 매우 행복하고 고귀하게 시작했노라 여기면서, 그는 완벽한 자족감에 잠겨 자기 마을을 향한 길을 잡고는 낮은 목소리로 이렇게 읊조렸다. \"오, 더없이 아름다운 이 중에서도 가장 아름다운 엘 토보소의 둘시네아여, 그대는 오늘 스스로를 이 땅의 그 누구보다도 행복하다 일컬어도 좋으리라!"],["since it has fallen to thy lot to hold subject and submissive to thy full will and pleasure a knight so renowned as is and will be Don Quixote of La Mancha, who, as all the world knows, yesterday received the order of knighthood, and hath to-day righted the greatest wrong and grievance that ever injustice conceived and cruelty perpetrated: who hath to-day plucked the rod from the hand of yonder ruthless oppressor so wantonly lashing that tender child.”","온 세상이 알다시피 어제 기사 서임을 받았으며 오늘 일찍이 불의가 꾸미고 잔혹함이 저지른 가장 큰 그릇됨과 횡포를 바로잡은, 지금도 또 앞으로도 그토록 이름 높을 라만차의 돈키호테와 같은 기사를, 그대의 온전한 뜻과 기쁨에 종속시키고 순종케 하는 운명이 그대에게 떨어졌으니 말이다. 그 기사는 오늘 저 어린 아이를 그토록 무자비하게 매질하던 잔혹한 압제자의 손에서 매를 빼앗아 버렸도다.\""]],[["He now came to a road branching in four directions, and immediately he was reminded of those cross-roads where knights-errant used to stop to consider which road they should take.","이제 그는 네 갈래로 갈라지는 길에 이르렀고, 곧바로 편력 기사들이 어느 길로 갈지 헤아리려고 멈춰 서곤 하던 그 갈림길들이 떠올랐다. 그들을 흉내 내어 그는 잠시 멈추었고, 깊이 생각한 끝에 로시난테에게 고삐를 내맡겨 자기 의지를 자기 말에게 맡겼으니, 말은 처음 품었던 뜻, 곧 곧장 제 마구간으로 향하려는 뜻을 그대로 좇았다."],["In imitation of them he halted for a while, and after having deeply considered it, he gave Rocinante his head, submitting his own will to that of his hack, who followed out his first intention, which was to make straight for his own stable.","두 마일쯤 갔을 때 돈키호테는 한 무리의 사람들을 발견했는데, 나중에 밝혀진 바로는 무르시아에 비단을 사러 가는 톨레도의 상인들이었다. 그들은 여섯이서 양산을 받쳐 들고 오는 길이었고, 말을 탄 하인 넷과 걸어서 따라오는 노새몰이꾼 셋이 함께였다."],["After he had gone about two miles Don Quixote perceived a large party of people, who, as afterwards appeared, were some Toledo traders, on their way to buy silk at Murcia. There were six of them coming along under their sunshades, with four servants mounted, and three muleteers on foot.","돈키호테는 그들을 발견하자마자 이것이 무언가 새로운 모험임이 틀림없다는 공상에 사로잡혔으니, 자신이 책에서 읽은 그 대목들을 할 수 있는 한 흉내 내는 데 보탬이 되도록 마치 일부러 마련된 듯한 모험 하나가 여기 찾아오는 듯하여, 그는 이를 시도하기로 결심했다."],["Scarcely had Don Quixote descried them when the fancy possessed him that this must be some new adventure; and to help him to imitate as far as he could those passages he had read of in his books, here seemed to come one made on purpose, which he resolved to attempt.","그리하여 그는 위풍당당한 자세와 결의로 등자에 두 발을 굳게 디디고, 창을 가다듬고, 방패를 가슴 앞으로 끌어당기고는, 길 한복판에 버티고 서서 이 편력 기사들이—이제 그는 그들을 그렇게 여기고 또 그렇게 믿었으니—다가오기를 기다렸다. 그들이 보고 들을 수 있을 만큼 가까이 다가오자, 그는 오만한 몸짓으로 외쳤다."],["So with a lofty bearing and determination he fixed himself firmly in his stirrups, got his lance ready, brought his buckler before his breast, and planting himself in the middle of the road, stood waiting the approach of these knights-errant, for such he now considered and held them to be; and when they had come near enough to see and hear, he exclaimed with a haughty gesture, “All the world stand, unless all the world confess that in all the world there is no maiden fairer than the Empress of La Mancha, the peerless Dulcinea del Toboso.”","\"온 세상이 멈추라. 온 세상이 라만차의 여제, 비할 데 없는 엘 토보소의 둘시네아보다 더 아름다운 처녀는 온 세상에 없음을 고백하지 않는 한 말이다.\""]],[["The traders halted at the sound of this language and the sight of the strange figure that uttered it, and from both figure and language at once guessed the craze of their owner; they wished, however, to learn quietly what was the object of this confession that was demanded of them, and one of them, who was rather fond of a joke and was very sharp-witted, said to him, “Sir Knight, we do not know who this good lady is that you speak of; show her to us, for, if she be of such beauty as you suggest, with all our hearts and without any pressure we will confess the truth that is on your part required of us.”","상인들은 이런 말소리와 그 말을 내뱉는 기이한 형상을 보고 걸음을 멈추었고, 그 형상과 말투에서 단번에 그 임자가 미쳤음을 알아챘다. 그러나 그들은 자기들에게 요구되는 이 고백이 무엇을 노리는 것인지 조용히 알아보고자 했고, 그들 가운데 농담을 꽤 즐기고 매우 영민한 자 하나가 그에게 말했다. \"기사 나리, 우리는 나리가 말씀하시는 이 훌륭한 부인이 누구신지 모릅니다. 그분을 우리에게 보여 주십시오. 만약 나리가 말씀하시는 만큼 그토록 아름다우시다면, 우리는 진심으로, 아무런 강요 없이도, 나리가 우리에게 요구하시는 그 진실을 고백하겠습니다.\""]],[["“If I were to show her to you,” replied Don Quixote, “what merit would you have in confessing a truth so manifest? The essential point is that without seeing her you must believe, confess, affirm, swear, and defend it; else ye have to do with me in battle, ill-conditioned, arrogant rabble that ye are; and come ye on, one by one as the order of knighthood requires, or all together as is the custom and vile usage of your breed, here do I bide and await you relying on the justice of the cause I maintain.”","\"내가 그대들에게 그분을 보여 준다면,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그토록 명백한 진실을 고백하는 데 그대들에게 무슨 공이 있겠는가? 핵심은 그분을 보지 않고도 그대들이 믿고, 고백하고, 단언하고, 맹세하고, 옹호해야 한다는 것이다. 그러지 않으면 그대들은 나와 싸움을 벌여야 하리니, 이 못돼 먹고 오만한 무리들아. 자, 기사도의 법도가 요구하는 대로 하나씩 덤비든, 아니면 너희 족속의 관습이자 비열한 버릇대로 다 함께 덤비든 하라. 나는 여기서, 내가 지키는 대의의 정당함을 믿고서, 버티고 서서 너희를 기다리노라.\""]],[["“Sir Knight,” replied the trader, “I entreat your worship in the name of this present company of princes, that, to save us from charging our consciences with the confession of a thing we have never seen or heard of, and one moreover so much to the prejudice of the Empresses and Queens of the Alcarria and Estremadura, your worship will be pleased to show us some portrait of this lady, though it be no bigger than a grain of wheat; for by the thread one gets at the ball, and in this way we shall be satisfied and easy, and you will be content and pleased; nay, I believe we are already so far agreed with you that even though her portrait should show her blind of one eye, and distilling vermilion and sulphur from the other, we would nevertheless, to gratify your worship, say all in her favour that you desire.”","\"기사 나리,\" 상인이 대답했다. \"여기 모인 이 왕후들의 무리를 대신하여 나리께 간청드립니다. 우리가 보지도 듣지도 못한 것을, 더구나 알카리아와 에스트레마두라의 여제들과 여왕들께 그토록 큰 누가 되는 것을 고백함으로써 우리 양심에 짐을 지우는 일을 면하게 해 주시고자, 부디 이 부인의 초상 하나만이라도 우리에게 보여 주십시오. 밀알 한 톨만 한 크기라도 좋습니다. 실 한 가닥으로 실타래 전체를 알 수 있는 법이니, 이렇게 하면 우리도 흡족하고 마음이 놓일 것이며, 나리께서도 만족하고 기뻐하실 겁니다. 아니, 우리는 이미 나리와 어지간히 뜻이 맞은 듯하여, 설령 그 초상이 그분이 한쪽 눈이 멀고 다른 쪽 눈에서 주홍빛과 유황빛 진물을 흘리는 모습을 보여 준다 해도, 그래도 우리는 나리를 기쁘게 해 드리고자 나리가 바라시는 대로 그분을 칭송하는 온갖 말을 다 하겠습니다.\""]],[["“She distils nothing of the kind, vile rabble,”","\"그분은 그런 것을 흘리지 않느니라, 비열한 무리들아.\" 분노로 활활 타오르며 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, burning with rage, “nothing of the kind, I say, only ambergris and civet in cotton; nor is she one-eyed or humpbacked, but straighter than a Guadarrama spindle: but ye must pay for the blasphemy ye have uttered against beauty like that of my lady.”","\"그런 것은 흘리지 않는다, 내 말하노니, 다만 솜에 싸인 용연향과 사향만을 풍기실 뿐이며, 한쪽 눈이 먼 것도 곱사등이도 아니요, 과다라마의 물렛가락보다 더 곧으시니라. 허나 너희는 내 여인과 같은 그런 아름다움을 모독한 죄의 값을 치러야 하리라.\""]],[["And so saying, he charged with levelled lance against the one who had spoken, with such fury and fierceness that, if luck had not contrived that Rocinante should stumble midway and come down, it would have gone hard with the rash trader. Down went Rocinante, and over went his master, rolling along the ground for some distance; and when he tried to rise he was unable, so encumbered was he with lance, buckler, spurs, helmet, and the weight of his old armour; and all the while he was struggling to get up he kept saying, “Fly not, cowards and caitiffs! stay, for not by my fault, but my horse’s, am I stretched here.”","이렇게 말하고 그는 창을 겨눈 채, 말을 했던 그자를 향해 어찌나 맹렬하고 사납게 돌진했던지, 만약 운수가 로시난테로 하여금 도중에 비틀거려 넘어지도록 꾸미지 않았더라면, 그 경솔한 상인은 큰 봉변을 당했을 것이다. 로시난테가 넘어지고 그 주인도 나동그라져 한참을 땅바닥에서 뒹굴었으니, 일어나려 했으나 도무지 일어날 수가 없었다. 창과 방패와 박차와 투구에, 거기다 낡은 갑옷의 무게까지 더해져 그토록 거추장스러웠던 것이다. 그러면서도 그는 일어나려 안간힘을 쓰는 내내 이렇게 외쳤다. \"달아나지 마라, 이 비겁자들, 이 비열한 놈들아! 멈춰라. 내가 여기 뻗어 있는 것은 내 잘못이 아니라 내 말의 잘못이니라.\""]],[["One of the muleteers in attendance, who could not have had much good nature in him, hearing the poor prostrate man blustering in this style, was unable to refrain from giving him an answer on his ribs; and coming up to him he seized his lance, and having broken it in pieces, with one of them he began so to belabour our Don Quixote that, notwithstanding and in spite of his armour, he milled him like a measure of wheat.","수행하던 노새몰이꾼 가운데 하나가, 그리 너그러운 천성이라곤 없었던 듯, 가엾게 나동그라진 사내가 이런 식으로 큰소리치는 것을 듣고는, 그 갈빗대에 한마디 대꾸를 해 주지 않고는 못 배겼다. 그래서 그에게 다가가 창을 붙잡아 산산조각 낸 다음, 그 한 도막으로 우리의 돈키호테를 어찌나 두들겨 패기 시작했던지, 갑옷을 둘렀음에도 불구하고 그를 밀가루 한 됫박처럼 빻아 놓았다."],["His masters called out not to lay on so hard and to leave him alone, but the muleteer’s blood was up, and he did not care to drop the game until he had vented the rest of his wrath, and gathering up the remaining fragments of the lance he finished with a discharge upon the unhappy victim, who all through the storm of sticks that rained on him never ceased threatening heaven, and earth, and the brigands, for such they seemed to him.","그의 주인들은 그렇게 세게 치지 말고 내버려 두라고 소리쳤으나, 노새몰이꾼은 피가 끓어올라 남은 분이 다 풀릴 때까지는 이 놀음을 그만둘 마음이 없었으니, 부러진 창의 남은 조각들을 그러모아 가엾은 희생자에게 한바탕 쏟아붓는 것으로 마무리했다. 그러는 내내 빗발치듯 쏟아지는 몽둥이질 속에서도 그는 하늘과 땅과, 자기 눈에 산적으로 보이던 그 도적들을 향해 위협하기를 그치지 않았다."],["At last the muleteer was tired, and the traders continued their journey, taking with them matter for talk about the poor fellow who had been cudgelled.","마침내 노새몰이꾼은 지쳤고, 상인들은 흠씬 두들겨 맞은 가엾은 사내에 관한 이야깃거리를 안고 가던 길을 계속 갔다."],["He when he found himself alone made another effort to rise; but if he was unable when whole and sound, how was he to rise after having been thrashed and well-nigh knocked to pieces?","혼자가 된 그는 다시 한번 일어나려 애를 썼다. 그러나 멀쩡하고 성할 때도 일어나지 못했거늘, 두들겨 맞고 거의 산산조각이 나다시피 한 뒤에 어찌 일어날 수 있었겠는가?"],["And yet he esteemed himself fortunate, as it seemed to him that this was a regular knight-errant’s mishap, and entirely, he considered, the fault of his horse.","그럼에도 그는 자신을 운 좋은 사람이라 여겼으니, 이것이야말로 편력 기사에게 으레 따르는 재난이며, 그가 생각하기에 전적으로 자기 말의 잘못이라 여겼던 것이다."],["However, battered in body as he was, to rise was beyond his power.","그러나 몸뚱이가 그토록 두들겨 맞은 터라, 일어나는 일은 그의 힘으로는 어림없었다."]],[["# CHAPTER V. IN WHICH THE NARRATIVE OF OUR KNIGHT’S MISHAP IS CONTINUED","# CHAPTER V."]],[["Finding, then, that, in fact he could not move, he thought himself of having recourse to his usual remedy, which was to think of some passage in his books, and his craze brought to his mind that about Baldwin and the Marquis of Mantua, when Carloto left him wounded on the mountain side, a story known by heart by the children, not forgotten by the young men, and lauded and even believed by the old folk; and for all that not a whit truer than the miracles of Mahomet.","그리하여 그는 자신이 도무지 몸을 움직일 수 없음을 깨닫고는, 늘 쓰던 처방, 곧 자기 책 속의 어느 대목을 떠올리는 방법에 의지하기로 했으니, 그의 광기는 발도비노스와 만투아 후작에 관한 이야기, 곧 카를로토가 산기슭에 그를 상처 입은 채 버려두고 떠났던 그 이야기를 그의 머릿속에 불러왔다. 아이들이 줄줄 외우고, 젊은이들도 잊지 않았으며, 노인들이 칭송하고 더러는 믿기까지 하는 이야기였으나, 그러면서도 마호메트의 기적만큼이나 한 치도 더 진실하지는 않은 이야기였다."],["This seemed to him to fit exactly the case in which he found himself, so, making a show of severe suffering, he began to roll on the ground and with feeble breath repeat the very words which the wounded knight of the wood is said to have uttered:","이 이야기가 자신이 처한 사정에 꼭 들어맞는 듯 여겨지자, 그는 심한 고통에 시달리는 시늉을 하며 땅바닥을 뒹굴기 시작했고, 가냘픈 숨결로 숲의 상처 입은 기사가 했다는 바로 그 말을 읊조렸다. 내 그대는 어디 있는가, 나의 여인이여,"]],[["Where art thou, lady mine, that thou My sorrow dost not rue?","내 슬픔을 가엾이 여기지 않으니?"],["Thou canst not know it, lady mine, Or else thou art untrue.","그대 그것을 알지 못하는 게요, 나의 여인이여,"]],[["And so he went on with the ballad as far as the lines:","아니면 그대 신의를 저버린 게요. 그렇게 그는 다음 구절에 이르기까지 그 노래를 이어 갔다."]],[["O noble Marquis of Mantua, My Uncle and liege lord!","오, 고귀하신 만투아 후작이시여,\n나의 숙부이시며 군주이신 분이여!"]],[["As chance would have it, when he had got to this line there happened to come by a peasant from his own village, a neighbour of his, who had been with a load of wheat to the mill, and he, seeing the man stretched there, came up to him and asked him who he was and what was the matter with him that he complained so dolefully.","마침 그가 이 구절에 다다랐을 때, 같은 마을에 사는 그의 이웃 농부 하나가 우연히 지나가게 되었으니, 밀 한 짐을 방앗간에 싣고 갔다 오던 길이었다. 그는 그 자리에 사람이 뻗어 있는 것을 보고 다가가, 누구이며 무슨 일로 그토록 구슬프게 신음하느냐고 물었다."]],[["Don Quixote was firmly persuaded that this was the Marquis of Mantua, his uncle, so the only answer he made was to go on with his ballad, in which he told the tale of his misfortune, and of the loves of the Emperor’s son and his wife all exactly as the ballad sings it.","돈키호테는 이 사람이 자기 숙부인 만투아 후작이라고 굳게 믿었던지라, 그가 한 대답이라고는 자기 노래를 계속 이어 가는 것뿐이었으니, 그 노래 속에서 그는 자신의 불운한 사연과 황제의 아들과 그 아내의 사랑 이야기를, 모두 노래가 읊는 그대로 들려주었다."]],[["The peasant stood amazed at hearing such nonsense, and relieving him of the visor, already battered to pieces by blows, he wiped his face, which was covered with dust, and as soon as he had done so he recognised him and said, “Señor Quixada”","농부는 이런 헛소리를 듣고 어안이 벙벙해져서, 이미 매질로 산산조각이 난 면갑을 벗겨 주고, 먼지투성이가 된 그의 얼굴을 닦아 주었다. 그러고 나자마자 그를 알아보고는 이렇게 말했다."],["(for so he appears to have been called when he was in his senses and had not yet changed from a quiet country gentleman into a knight-errant), “who has brought your worship to this pass?”","\"키하다 나리,\" (제정신일 때, 그러니까 조용한 시골 향사에서 편력 기사로 변신하기 전에는 이렇게 불렸던 모양이다) \"대체 누가 나리를 이 지경으로 만들었답니까?\""],["But to all questions the other only went on with his ballad.","그러나 무엇을 물어도 상대는 그저 자기 노래만 계속할 뿐이었다."]],[["Seeing this, the good man removed as well as he could his breastplate and backpiece to see if he had any wound, but he could perceive no blood nor any mark whatever.","이를 본 착한 사내는 혹시 상처라도 있는지 보려고 가슴받이와 등받이를 할 수 있는 한 벗겨 보았으나, 피도, 무슨 자국 하나도 찾아낼 수 없었다."],["He then contrived to raise him from the ground, and with no little difficulty hoisted him upon his ass, which seemed to him to be the easiest mount for him; and collecting the arms, even to the splinters of the lance, he tied them on Rocinante, and leading him by the bridle and the ass by the halter he took the road for the village, very sad to hear what absurd stuff Don Quixote was talking.","그러고는 그를 땅에서 일으켜 세우는 데 가까스로 성공하여, 그가 보기에 가장 타기 편할 듯한 자기 당나귀에 적잖이 애를 먹으며 그를 들어 올려 태웠다. 그리고 무구들을, 부러진 창 조각까지 죄다 그러모아 로시난테에 묶고는, 로시난테는 고삐로, 당나귀는 굴레로 끌면서 마을로 가는 길로 접어들었으니, 돈키호테가 지껄이는 터무니없는 소리를 듣자니 마음이 몹시 무거웠다."]],[["Nor was Don Quixote less so, for what with blows and bruises he could not sit upright on the ass, and from time to time he sent up sighs to heaven, so that once more he drove the peasant to ask what ailed him. And it could have been only the devil himself that put into his head tales to match his own adventures, for now, forgetting Baldwin, he bethought himself of the Moor Abindarraez, when the Alcaide of Antequera, Rodrigo de Narvaez, took him prisoner and carried him away to his castle; so that when the peasant again asked him how he was and what ailed him, he gave him for reply the same words and phrases that the captive Abindarraez gave to Rodrigo de Narvaez, just as he had read the story in the “Diana” of Jorge de Montemayor where it is written, applying it to his own case so aptly that the peasant went along cursing his fate that he had to listen to such a lot of nonsense; from which, however, he came to the conclusion that his neighbour was mad, and so made all haste to reach the village to escape the wearisomeness of this harangue of Don Quixote’s; who, at the end of it, said, “Señor Don Rodrigo de Narvaez, your worship must know that this fair Xarifa I have mentioned is now the lovely Dulcinea del Toboso, for whom I have done, am doing, and will do the most famous deeds of chivalry that in this world have been seen, are to be seen, or ever shall be seen.”","돈키호테 역시 못지않게 괴로웠으니, 얻어맞고 멍든 탓에 당나귀 위에 똑바로 앉아 있을 수가 없어 이따금 하늘을 향해 한숨을 내쉬었고, 그 바람에 다시 한번 농부로 하여금 어디가 아프냐고 묻게 만들었다. 그리고 그의 모험에 들어맞는 이야기들을 그의 머릿속에 집어넣은 것은 오로지 악마 자신이었음이 분명하니, 이제 발도비노스를 잊어버리고는 무어인 아빈다라에스를, 곧 안테케라의 성주 로드리고 데 나르바에스가 그를 포로로 사로잡아 자기 성으로 끌고 갔던 그 이야기를 떠올렸기 때문이다. 그리하여 농부가 다시 그에게 어떠냐고, 어디가 아프냐고 묻자, 그는 포로가 된 아빈다라에스가 로드리고 데 나르바에스에게 했던 것과 똑같은 말과 표현으로 대답했으니, 호르헤 데 몬테마요르의 「디아나」에서 그 이야기가 쓰인 그대로 읽었던 것을 자기 처지에 어찌나 절묘하게 갖다 붙이던지, 농부는 이런 허튼소리 무더기를 들어야만 하는 자기 팔자를 저주하며 길을 갔다. 그러나 그로부터 그는 자기 이웃이 미쳤다는 결론에 이르렀고, 그래서 돈키호테의 이 장광설이 주는 진력남에서 벗어나고자 마을에 닿으려고 한껏 서둘렀다. 한데 돈키호테는 그 끝에 가서 이렇게 말했다. \"로드리고 데 나르바에스 나리, 제가 말씀드린 이 아리따운 하리파가 지금은 사랑스러운 둘시네아 델 토보소라는 것을 나리께서는 아셔야 합니다. 그분을 위하여 저는 이 세상에서 일찍이 보였고, 지금 보이고 있으며, 앞으로 보일, 또 보이게 될 가장 이름 높은 무훈을 이루었고, 이루고 있으며, 또 이룰 것입니다.\""]],[["To this the peasant answered, “Señor--sinner that I am!--cannot your worship see that I am not Don Rodrigo de Narvaez nor the Marquis of Mantua, but Pedro Alonso your neighbour, and that your worship is neither Baldwin nor Abindarraez, but the worthy gentleman Señor Quixada?”","이에 농부가 대답했다. \"나리, 이 죄 많은 놈을 좀 보십쇼! 제가 로드리고 데 나르바에스 나리도, 만투아 후작도 아니고, 나리의 이웃인 페드로 알론소이며, 나리께서도 발도비노스도 아빈다라에스도 아니고, 훌륭하신 향사 키하다 나리시라는 것이 안 보이십니까요?\""]],[["“I know who I am,”","\"나는 내가 누구인지 안다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote, “and I know that I may be not only those I have named, but all the Twelve Peers of France and even all the Nine Worthies, since my achievements surpass all that they have done all together and each of them on his own account.”","\"그리고 나는 내가 방금 댄 그자들뿐 아니라, 프랑스의 열두 기사 전부, 나아가 아홉 위인 전부일 수도 있다는 것을 안다. 나의 위업은 그들이 모두 합쳐 이룬 것이며 저마다 제 힘으로 이룬 것을 죄다 능가하기 때문이다.\""]],[["With this talk and more of the same kind they reached the village just as night was beginning to fall, but the peasant waited until it was a little later that the belaboured gentleman might not be seen riding in such a miserable trim.","이런 이야기, 또 이런 종류의 이야기를 더 주고받는 사이에 그들은 막 밤이 깔리기 시작할 무렵 마을에 다다랐으나, 농부는 그 두들겨 맞은 향사가 그토록 비참한 꼴로 말을 타고 가는 모습을 들키지 않도록 좀 더 늦은 때까지 기다렸다. 그가 보기에 적당한 때가 되자 마을로 들어가 돈키호테의 집으로 갔는데, 집 안은 온통 발칵 뒤집혀 있었고, 그곳에는 돈키호테와 막역한 벗이던 신부와 마을 이발사가 와 있었다."],["When it was what seemed to him the proper time he entered the village and went to Don Quixote’s house, which he found all in confusion, and there were the curate and the village barber, who were great friends of Don Quixote, and his housekeeper was saying to them in a loud voice, “What does your worship think can have befallen my master, Señor Licentiate Pero Perez?”","가정부가 그들에게 큰 소리로 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"신학사 페로 페레스 나리, 제 주인 어른께 무슨 일이 닥쳤을 것 같으십니까요?\""],["for so the curate was called; “it is three days now since anything has been seen of him, or the hack, or the buckler, lance, or armour.","신부는 그렇게 불렸던 것이다. \"어른은 물론이고 그 말이며, 둥근 방패며, 창이며, 갑옷도 코빼기조차 안 보인 지 사흘이 됐답니다."],["Miserable me!","가엾은 이 몸!"],["I am certain of it, and it is as true as that I was born to die, that these accursed books of chivalry he has, and has got into the way of reading so constantly, have upset his reason; for now I remember having often heard him saying to himself that he would turn knight-errant and go all over the world in quest of adventures.","분명합니다요, 제가 죽으려고 태어났다는 것만큼이나 확실해요. 어른이 갖고 계시면서 줄곧 읽는 버릇이 든 그 빌어먹을 기사도 책들이 어른의 정신을 망쳐 놓은 거랍니다."],["To the devil and Barabbas with such books, that have brought to ruin in this way the finest understanding there was in all La Mancha!”","이제야 생각이 나는데, 어른이 혼잣말로 편력 기사가 되어 모험을 찾아 온 세상을 돌아다니겠다고 하시는 걸 여러 번 들었거든요. 라만차 천지에서 둘도 없이 멀쩡하던 그 양반의 머리를 이 꼴로 망쳐 놓은 그런 책들일랑 악마하고 바라바한테나 줘 버려요!\""]],[["The niece said the same, and, more: “You must know, Master Nicholas”--for that was the name of the barber--“it was often my uncle’s way to stay two days and nights together poring over these unholy books of misventures, after which he would fling the book away and snatch up his sword and fall to slashing the walls; and when he was tired out he would say he had killed four giants like four towers; and the sweat that flowed from him when he was weary he said was the blood of the wounds he had received in battle; and then he would drink a great jug of cold water and become calm and quiet, saying that this water was a most precious potion which the sage Esquife, a great magician and friend of his, had brought him. But I take all the blame upon myself for never having told your worships of my uncle’s vagaries, that you might put a stop to them before things had come to this pass, and burn all these accursed books--for he has a great number--that richly deserve to be burned like heretics.”","조카딸도 같은 말을 했고, 게다가 한마디 더 보탰다. \"니콜라스 선생님,\" 이발사의 이름이 그러했다. \"외숙부께서는 그 망측한 재앙의 책들을 들여다보느라 꼬박 이틀 밤낮을 보내시기 일쑤였답니다. 그러고 나면 책을 내던지고 칼을 움켜쥐고는 벽을 마구 베어 대셨어요. 그러다 기진맥진하시면 탑 네 개만 한 거인 넷을 죽였노라 하셨고, 지쳐서 흘리신 땀을 두고는 싸움에서 입은 상처에서 나온 피라고 하셨지요. 그러고는 차가운 물을 한 항아리 가득 들이켜고 잠잠하고 차분해지셔서, 이 물이야말로 위대한 마법사이자 당신 벗인 현자 에스키페가 가져다준 더없이 귀한 영약이라 하셨답니다. 하지만 외숙부의 이런 변덕을 진작에 나리들께 말씀드려서, 일이 이 지경에 이르기 전에 막으시고 그 빌어먹을 책들을 죄다 태워 버리시도록 하지 못한 죄는 모두 제가 짊어지겠어요. 책이 무지 많은데, 죄다 이단자처럼 화형당해 마땅한 것들이거든요.\""]],[["“So say I too,”","\"나도 같은 말이오.\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “and by my faith to-morrow shall not pass without public judgment upon them, and may they be condemned to the flames lest they lead those that read to behave as my good friend seems to have behaved.”","\"내 믿음을 걸고, 내일이 가기 전에 그 책들에 공개 재판을 내리리다. 그것들을 읽는 자들이 나의 좋은 벗이 한 것처럼 행동하게 만들지 못하도록, 부디 화형에 처해지기를.\""]],[["All this the peasant heard, and from it he understood at last what was the matter with his neighbour, so he began calling aloud, “Open, your worships, to Señor Baldwin and to Señor the Marquis of Mantua, who comes badly wounded, and to Señor Abindarraez, the Moor, whom the valiant Rodrigo de Narvaez, the Alcaide of Antequera, brings captive.”","이 모든 것을 농부가 듣고는, 마침내 자기 이웃에게 무슨 일이 일어난 것인지 알아차렸다. 그래서 그는 큰 소리로 외치기 시작했다. \"나리들, 발도비노스 나리와 만투아 후작 나리께 문을 여시오. 후작께서 큰 상처를 입고 오시며, 또 무어인 아빈다라에스 나리께도 문을 여시오. 용맹한 안테케라 성주 로드리고 데 나르바에스가 그분을 포로로 끌고 오신답니다.\""]],[["At these words they all hurried out, and when they recognised their friend, master, and uncle, who had not yet dismounted from the ass because he could not, they ran to embrace him.","이 말에 모두가 황급히 뛰쳐나왔고, 자신들의 벗이자 주인이자 외숙부를 알아보고는, 그가 아직 내릴 수가 없어 당나귀에서 내리지 못한 채 있는 것을 보고 그를 껴안으려고 달려갔다."]],[["“Hold!” said he, “for I am badly wounded through my horse’s fault; carry me to bed, and if possible send for the wise Urganda to cure and see to my wounds.”","\"멈추시오!\" 그가 말했다. \"내 말의 잘못으로 큰 상처를 입었으니 말이오. 나를 침상으로 옮겨 주시오. 그리고 가능하다면 현자 우르간다를 불러 내 상처를 치료하고 돌보게 해 주시오.\""]],[["“See there!","\"저것 좀 보세요!"],["plague on it!”","이를 어쩌나!\""],["cried the housekeeper at this: “did not my heart tell the truth as to which foot my master went lame of?","가정부가 이 말에 소리쳤다. \"어른께서 어느 발을 절며 들어오실지 제 가슴이 진작에 일러 준 게 사실이 아니던가요?"],["To bed with your worship at once, and we will contrive to cure you here without fetching that Hurgada.","어서 침상으로 드시지요, 그 우르가다인지 뭔지를 데려오지 않고도 여기서 어른을 치료해 드릴 방도를 마련하겠습니다요."],["A curse I say once more, and a hundred times more, on those books of chivalry that have brought your worship to such a pass.”","어른을 이런 꼴로 만든 그 기사도 책들에 다시 한번, 아니 백 번이라도 저주를 퍼붓겠어요.\""]],[["They carried him to bed at once, and after searching for his wounds could find none, but he said they were all bruises from having had a severe fall with his horse Rocinante when in combat with ten giants, the biggest and the boldest to be found on earth.","그들은 곧 그를 침상으로 옮겼고, 상처를 찾아보았으나 하나도 찾을 수 없었는데, 그는 그것이 전부 땅 위에서 가장 크고 가장 대담한 거인 열 명과 싸우다가 자기 말 로시난테와 함께 호되게 넘어진 탓에 생긴 멍이라고 말했다."]],[["“So, so!”","\"이런, 이런!\""],["said the curate, “are there giants in the dance?","신부가 말했다. \"춤판에 거인까지 끼어 있단 말인가?"],["By the sign of the Cross I will burn them to-morrow before the day is over.”","성호를 걸고, 내일 해가 지기 전에 그것들을 태워 버리겠소.\""]],[["They put a host of questions to Don Quixote, but his only answer to all was--give him something to eat, and leave him to sleep, for that was what he needed most.","그들은 돈키호테에게 숱한 질문을 퍼부었으나, 그 모든 것에 그가 내놓은 대답이라고는 — 먹을 것을 좀 주고, 잠을 자게 내버려 두라는 것뿐이었으니, 그것이야말로 그에게 가장 필요한 것이었기 때문이다."],["They did so, and the curate questioned the peasant at great length as to how he had found Don Quixote.","그들은 그대로 했고, 신부는 농부에게 어떻게 돈키호테를 발견했는지 길게 캐물었다."],["He told him, and the nonsense he had talked when found and on the way home, all which made the licentiate the more eager to do what he did the next day, which was to summon his friend the barber, Master Nicholas, and go with him to Don Quixote’s house.","농부는 그에게 그 사연과, 발견했을 때와 집으로 오는 길에 그가 지껄인 헛소리를 모조리 들려주었으니, 이 모든 것이 신학사로 하여금 이튿날 그가 한 일을 더욱 하고 싶게 만들었다. 그것은 곧 그의 벗인 이발사 니콜라스 선생을 불러 함께 돈키호테의 집으로 가는 일이었다."]],[["# CHAPTER VI. OF THE DIVERTING AND IMPORTANT SCRUTINY WHICH THE CURATE AND THE BARBER MADE IN THE LIBRARY OF OUR INGENIOUS GENTLEMAN","# CHAPTER VI."]],[["He was still sleeping; so the curate asked the niece for the keys of the room where the books, the authors of all the mischief, were, and right willingly she gave them. They all went in, the housekeeper with them, and found more than a hundred volumes of big books very well bound, and some other small ones. The moment the housekeeper saw them she turned about and ran out of the room, and came back immediately with a saucer of holy water and a sprinkler, saying, “Here, your worship, señor licentiate, sprinkle this room; don’t leave any magician of the many there are in these books to bewitch us in revenge for our design of banishing them from the world.”","잠은 아직 깨지 않고 있었다. 그래서 신부는 조카딸에게 그 모든 화근의 장본인인 책들이 있는 방의 열쇠를 청했고, 그녀는 기꺼이 그것을 내주었다. 모두가 안으로 들어갔는데, 가정부도 함께였다. 그들은 매우 잘 제본된 큰 책이 백 권이 넘고, 작은 책도 몇 권 있는 것을 보았다. 그 책들을 보자마자 가정부는 몸을 돌려 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다가 곧장 성수가 담긴 작은 접시와 살수기를 들고 돌아오더니 이렇게 말했다. \"여기, 나리, 학사님, 이 방에 성수를 뿌려 주세요. 이 책들 속에 그토록 많은 마법사들이 들었으니, 우리가 그들을 세상에서 쫓아내려 한다고 그 보복으로 우리를 흘려 놓지 못하도록 하나도 빠짐없이요.\""]],[["The simplicity of the housekeeper made the licentiate laugh, and he directed the barber to give him the books one by one to see what they were about, as there might be some to be found among them that did not deserve the penalty of fire.","가정부의 순진함에 학사는 웃음을 터뜨리고는, 이발사에게 책을 한 권씩 건네 무엇에 관한 것인지 살펴보라고 일렀다. 그 가운데 불태움의 형벌을 받을 만하지 않은 것이 있을지도 모르기 때문이었다."]],[["“No,” said the niece, “there is no reason for showing mercy to any of them; they have every one of them done mischief; better fling them out of the window into the court and make a pile of them and set fire to them; or else carry them into the yard, and there a bonfire can be made without the smoke giving any annoyance.” The housekeeper said the same, so eager were they both for the slaughter of those innocents, but the curate would not agree to it without first reading at any rate the titles.","\"안 돼요.\" 조카딸이 말했다. \"그중 어느 하나에도 자비를 베풀 까닭이 없어요. 죄다 화근을 일으켰으니까요. 차라리 창문 밖 안마당으로 죄다 던져 무더기로 쌓아 놓고 불을 지르는 편이 나아요. 아니면 뒤뜰로 가지고 가서, 연기가 누구도 성가시게 하지 않을 곳에서 모닥불을 피우든가요.\" 가정부도 똑같이 말했으니, 두 사람 다 그 무고한 것들을 살육하는 데 그토록 안달이 나 있었던 것이다. 그러나 신부는 적어도 제목만이라도 먼저 읽어 보지 않고서는 동의하려 하지 않았다."]],[["The first that Master Nicholas put into his hand was “The four books of Amadis of Gaul.”","니콜라스 선생이 그의 손에 처음으로 쥐여 준 것은 「갈리아의 아마디스 네 권」이었다."],["“This seems a mysterious thing,”","\"이건 묘한 일이로군.\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “for, as I have heard say, this was the first book of chivalry printed in Spain, and from this all the others derive their birth and origin; so it seems to me that we ought inexorably to condemn it to the flames as the founder of so vile a sect.”","\"내가 듣기로 이것이 스페인에서 인쇄된 최초의 기사도 책이고, 다른 모든 책들이 여기서 그 탄생과 기원을 끌어냈다 하니 말일세. 그러니 이토록 천박한 종파의 창시자로서 이 책을 가차 없이 불길에 처해야 마땅하다고 보네.\""]],[["“Nay, sir,”","\"아닙니다, 신부님.\" 이발사가 말했다."],["said the barber, “I too, have heard say that this is the best of all the books of this kind that have been written, and so, as something singular in its line, it ought to be pardoned.”","\"저 역시 이것이 이런 종류로 쓰인 모든 책 가운데 으뜸이라고 들었습니다. 그러니 그 분야에서 독보적인 것으로서 사면받아야 마땅하지요.\""]],[["“True,”","\"옳은 말일세.\""],["said the curate; “and for that reason let its life be spared for the present.","신부가 말했다. \"바로 그런 까닭에 당분간은 그 목숨을 살려 두기로 하지."],["Let us see that other which is next to it.”","그 옆에 있는 저것을 좀 보세.\""]],[["“It is,”","\"이것은,\""],["said the barber, “the ‘Sergas de Esplandian,’ the lawful son of Amadis of Gaul.”","이발사가 말했다. \"「에스플란디안의 무훈」으로, 갈리아의 아마디스의 적자입니다.\""]],[["“Then verily,”","\"그렇다면 참으로,\""],["said the curate, “the merit of the father must not be put down to the account of the son.","신부가 말했다. \"아비의 공덕을 자식의 셈으로 돌려서는 안 되지."],["Take it, mistress housekeeper; open the window and fling it into the yard and lay the foundation of the pile for the bonfire we are to make.”","가정부, 이걸 받게. 창문을 열고 뒤뜰로 던져, 우리가 피울 모닥불의 토대로 삼게나.\""]],[["The housekeeper obeyed with great satisfaction, and the worthy “Esplandian” went flying into the yard to await with all patience the fire that was in store for him.","가정부는 큰 만족감으로 시키는 대로 했고, 갸륵한 「에스플란디안」은 자기를 위해 마련된 불을 한껏 인내심을 가지고 기다리고자 뒤뜰로 날아갔다."]],[["“Proceed,”","\"계속하게.\""],["said the curate.","신부가 말했다."]],[["“This that comes next,”","\"다음으로 나오는 이것은,\" 이발사가 말했다."],["said the barber, “is ‘Amadis of Greece,’ and, indeed, I believe all those on this side are of the same Amadis lineage.”","\"「그리스의 아마디스」입니다. 그리고 정말이지, 이쪽에 있는 것들은 모조리 같은 아마디스 혈통인 듯합니다.\""]],[["“Then to the yard with the whole of them,”","\"그렇다면 죄다 뒤뜰로 보내게.\""],["said the curate; “for to have the burning of Queen Pintiquiniestra, and the shepherd Darinel and his eclogues, and the bedevilled and involved discourses of his author, I would burn with them the father who begot me if he were going about in the guise of a knight-errant.”","신부가 말했다. \"핀티키니에스트라 여왕과 목동 다리넬과 그의 목가, 그리고 그 작자의 도깨비 같고 뒤엉킨 잡소리들을 불태우는 일이라면, 나를 낳아 주신 아버지라 해도 편력 기사 차림으로 돌아다닌다면 함께 불태워 버리겠네.\""]],[["“I am of the same mind,”","\"저도 같은 생각입니다.\""],["said the barber.","이발사가 말했다."]],[["“And so am I,”","\"저도 그렇고요.\""],["added the niece.","조카딸이 덧붙였다."]],[["“In that case,”","\"그렇다면,\""],["said the housekeeper, “here, into the yard with them!”","가정부가 말했다. \"자, 뒤뜰로 던져 버려요!\""]],[["They were handed to her, and as there were many of them, she spared herself the staircase, and flung them down out of the window.","책들이 그녀에게 건네졌고, 그 수가 워낙 많았던지라 그녀는 계단을 오르내리는 수고를 덜고자 창문 밖으로 내던졌다."]],[["“Who is that tub there?”","\"저기 저 통짜배기는 뭔가?\""],["said the curate.","신부가 말했다."]],[["“This,”","\"이것은,\""],["said the barber, “is ‘Don Olivante de Laura.’”","이발사가 말했다. \"「라우라의 돈 올리반테」입니다.\""]],[["“The author of that book,” said the curate, “was the same that wrote ‘The Garden of Flowers,’ and truly there is no deciding which of the two books is the more truthful, or, to put it better, the less lying; all I can say is, send this one into the yard for a swaggering fool.”","\"그 책의 작자는,\" 신부가 말했다. \"「꽃들의 정원」을 쓴 바로 그 사람일세. 그런데 정말이지 두 책 가운데 어느 쪽이 더 진실한지, 아니 더 정확히 말해 덜 거짓되었는지 가릴 수가 없네. 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤 허풍쟁이 바보로서 이걸 뒤뜰로 보내라는 것뿐이야.\""]],[["“This that follows is ‘Florismarte of Hircania,’”","\"다음에 나오는 이것은 「히르카니아의 플로리스마르테」입니다.\""],["said the barber.","이발사가 말했다."]],[["“Señor Florismarte here?” said the curate; “then by my faith he must take up his quarters in the yard, in spite of his marvellous birth and visionary adventures, for the stiffness and dryness of his style deserve nothing else; into the yard with him and the other, mistress housekeeper.”","\"플로리스마르테 님께서 여기 계셨군?\" 신부가 말했다. \"그렇다면 단언컨대 그 경이로운 출생과 환상적인 모험에도 불구하고 뒤뜰에 거처를 잡아야겠네. 그 문체의 뻣뻣함과 메마름은 그 외에 아무것도 받을 자격이 없으니 말일세. 가정부, 이것도 저것과 함께 뒤뜰로 보내게.\""]],[["“With all my heart, señor,”","\"기꺼이요, 나리.\""],["said she, and executed the order with great delight.","그녀는 그렇게 말하고는 큰 기쁨으로 명을 받들었다."]],[["“This,”","\"이것은,\""],["said the barber, “is The Knight Platir.’”","이발사가 말했다. \"「기사 플라티르」입니다.\""]],[["“An old book that,”","\"오래된 책이로군.\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “but I find no reason for clemency in it; send it after the others without appeal;”","\"허나 자비를 베풀 까닭이 보이질 않네. 두말없이 다른 것들 뒤를 따라 보내게.\""],["which was done.","그리하여 그대로 되었다."]],[["Another book was opened, and they saw it was entitled, “The Knight of the Cross.”","또 다른 책이 펼쳐졌고, 그들은 그것의 제목이 「십자가의 기사」임을 보았다."]],[["“For the sake of the holy name this book has,” said the curate, “its ignorance might be excused; but then, they say, ‘behind the cross there’s the devil; to the fire with it.”","\"이 책이 지닌 그 거룩한 이름을 보아,\" 신부가 말했다. \"그 무지를 용서해 줄 법도 하네. 하지만 사람들 말마따나 '십자가 뒤에 악마가 있다'고 하지 않던가. 불 속으로 던져 버리게.\""]],[["Taking down another book, the barber said, “This is ‘The Mirror of Chivalry.’”","또 다른 책을 집어 들고 이발사가 말했다. \"이것은 「기사도의 거울」입니다.\""]],[["“I know his worship,” said the curate; “that is where Señor Reinaldos of Montalvan figures with his friends and comrades, greater thieves than Cacus, and the Twelve Peers of France with the veracious historian Turpin; however, I am not for condemning them to more than perpetual banishment, because, at any rate, they have some share in the invention of the famous Matteo Boiardo, whence too the Christian poet Ludovico Ariosto wove his web, to whom, if I find him here, and speaking any language but his own, I shall show no respect whatever; but if he speaks his own tongue I will put him upon my head.”","\"그 나리는 내가 잘 알지.\" 신부가 말했다. \"거기에 몬탈반의 레이날도스 님이 등장하시는데, 그 친구들 패거리와 더불어 카쿠스보다 더한 도둑들이지. 또 프랑스의 십이 기사들과 진실한 역사가 투르핀도 나오고. 하지만 나는 이들을 영구 추방 이상으로 단죄할 생각은 없네. 어쨌든 이들은 저 유명한 마테오 보이아르도의 창작에 얼마간 몫이 있고, 거기서 또 기독교 시인 루도비코 아리오스토가 자기 베틀로 천을 짰으니 말일세. 만일 그자가 여기 있고 자기 언어가 아닌 다른 언어를 지껄인다면 나는 그에게 어떤 존경도 표하지 않겠네. 허나 자기 모국어로 말한다면 내 머리 위에 떠받들겠네.\""]],[["“Well, I have him in Italian,”","\"음, 저는 그걸 이탈리아어로 가지고 있습니다만,\""],["said the barber, “but I do not understand him.”","이발사가 말했다. \"이해하지는 못합니다.\""]],[["“Nor would it be well that you should understand him,” said the curate, “and on that score we might have excused the Captain if he had not brought him into Spain and turned him into Castilian. He robbed him of a great deal of his natural force, and so do all those who try to turn books written in verse into another language, for, with all the pains they take and all the cleverness they show, they never can reach the level of the originals as they were first produced. In short, I say that this book, and all that may be found treating of those French affairs, should be thrown into or deposited in some dry well, until after more consideration it is settled what is to be done with them; excepting always one ‘Bernardo del Carpio’ that is going about, and another called ‘Roncesvalles;’ for these, if they come into my hands, shall pass at once into those of the housekeeper, and from hers into the fire without any reprieve.”","\"자네가 그걸 이해한다면 그게 더 좋지 않은 일이지.\" 신부가 말했다. \"그 점에서는, 만일 그 대위가 그를 스페인으로 데려와 카스티야어로 바꿔 놓지만 않았더라면 그를 용서해 줄 수도 있었을 걸세. 그는 그에게서 그 타고난 힘을 상당 부분 앗아 갔으니, 운문으로 쓰인 책을 다른 언어로 옮기려 드는 자들이 죄다 그러하네. 아무리 공을 들이고 아무리 재주를 부린들, 그들은 결코 원작이 처음 빚어졌던 그 수준에는 이르지 못하니 말일세. 요컨대 내가 하는 말은, 이 책과 저 프랑스 사정을 다룬 책으로 발견될 모든 것들은, 더 숙고한 끝에 그것들을 어찌할지 정해지기까지 어느 마른 우물에 던져 넣거나 보관해 두어야 한다는 걸세. 단, 떠돌아다니는 「베르나르도 델 카르피오」 한 권과 「론세스바예스」라 불리는 또 한 권은 언제나 예외로 두지. 이것들이 내 손에 들어온다면 즉시 가정부의 손으로 넘어갈 것이요, 거기서 다시 한 치의 유예도 없이 불 속으로 들어갈 테니 말일세.\""]],[["To all this the barber gave his assent, and looked upon it as right and proper, being persuaded that the curate was so staunch to the Faith and loyal to the Truth that he would not for the world say anything opposed to them.","이 모든 것에 이발사는 동의하며 그것을 옳고 마땅한 일로 여겼으니, 신부가 신앙에 그토록 충실하고 진리에 그토록 충직하여 결코 그것들에 어긋나는 말은 한마디도 하지 않으리라 확신했기 때문이다."],["Opening another book he saw it was “Palmerin de Oliva,” and beside it was another called “Palmerin of England,”","또 다른 책을 펼치고 그는 그것이 「올리바의 팔메린」임을 보았고, 그 옆에는 「잉글랜드의 팔메린」이라 불리는 또 다른 책이 있었는데, 이를 보고 학사가 말했다."],["seeing which the licentiate said, “Let the Olive be made firewood of at once and burned until no ashes even are left; and let that Palm of England be kept and preserved as a thing that stands alone, and let such another case be made for it as that which Alexander found among the spoils of Darius and set aside for the safe keeping of the works of the poet Homer.","\"그 올리바는 당장 장작으로 만들어 재 한 톨 남지 않을 때까지 불태우게. 그리고 저 잉글랜드의 팔메린은 독보적인 것으로서 간직하고 보존하되, 알렉산드로스가 다리우스의 전리품 가운데서 찾아내어 시인 호메로스의 작품을 안전히 보관하려고 따로 챙겨 둔 그런 함과 같은 또 하나의 함을 만들어 주게나."],["This book, gossip, is of authority for two reasons, first because it is very good, and secondly because it is said to have been written by a wise and witty king of Portugal.","친구, 이 책은 두 가지 이유로 권위가 있다네. 첫째는 매우 훌륭하기 때문이요, 둘째는 포르투갈의 어느 지혜롭고 재치 있는 왕이 썼다고 전해지기 때문이지."],["All the adventures at the Castle of Miraguarda are excellent and of admirable contrivance, and the language is polished and clear, studying and observing the style befitting the speaker with propriety and judgment.","미라과르다 성에서 벌어지는 모든 모험은 빼어나고 감탄스러운 솜씨로 짜여 있으며, 언어는 세련되고 명료하여, 적절함과 분별을 가지고 화자에게 어울리는 문체를 연구하고 지키고 있네."],["So then, provided it seems good to you, Master Nicholas, I say let this and ‘Amadis of Gaul’ be remitted the penalty of fire, and as for all the rest, let them perish without further question or query.”","그러니, 니콜라스 선생, 자네에게 좋게 여겨진다면, 나는 이 책과 「갈리아의 아마디스」는 불태우는 형벌을 면해 주고, 나머지 전부는 더 묻거나 따질 것도 없이 멸하자고 말하겠네.\""]],[["“Nay, gossip,”","\"아니요, 친구.\""],["said the barber, “for this that I have here is the famous ‘Don Belianis.’”","이발사가 말했다. \"여기 제가 들고 있는 이건 저 유명한 「돈 벨리아니스」니까요.\""]],[["“Well,” said the curate, “that and the second, third, and fourth parts all stand in need of a little rhubarb to purge their excess of bile, and they must be cleared of all that stuff about the Castle of Fame and other greater affectations, to which end let them be allowed the over-seas term, and, according as they mend, so shall mercy or justice be meted out to them; and in the mean time, gossip, do you keep them in your house and let no one read them.”","\"음,\" 신부가 말했다. \"그것과 그 제2부, 제3부, 제4부는 모조리 넘치는 담즙을 씻어 내릴 대황 약간이 필요하고, 「명성의 성」에 관한 그 모든 군더더기와 다른 더 큰 허세들을 말끔히 도려내야 하네. 그러기 위해 그것들에게 해외 추방의 유예를 허락해 주고, 그것들이 고쳐지는 정도에 따라 자비를 베풀든 정의를 내리든 하기로 하지. 그동안은, 친구, 자네 집에 그것들을 두고 누구도 읽지 못하게 하게나.\""]],[["“With all my heart,”","\"기꺼이 그러지요.\""],["said the barber; and not caring to tire himself with reading more books of chivalry, he told the housekeeper to take all the big ones and throw them into the yard.","이발사가 말했다. 그러고는 기사도 책을 더 읽느라 지치고 싶지 않았던 그는 가정부에게 큰 책들을 전부 가져다 뒤뜰로 던져 버리라고 일렀다."],["It was not said to one dull or deaf, but to one who enjoyed burning them more than weaving the broadest and finest web that could be; and seizing about eight at a time, she flung them out of the window.","그 말은 둔하거나 귀먹은 사람에게 한 것이 아니라, 세상에서 가장 넓고 고운 천을 짜는 일보다 그것들을 불태우는 일을 더 즐기는 사람에게 한 것이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 한 번에 여덟 권쯤 움켜쥐고는 창문 밖으로 내던졌다."]],[["In carrying so many together she let one fall at the feet of the barber, who took it up, curious to know whose it was, and found it said, “History of the Famous Knight, Tirante el Blanco.”","그토록 많은 책을 한꺼번에 나르다가 그녀는 한 권을 이발사의 발치에 떨어뜨렸는데, 그는 그것이 누구의 것인지 궁금하여 집어 들었고, 거기에 「유명한 기사 티란테 엘 블랑코의 이야기」라 적힌 것을 발견했다."]],[["“God bless me!”","\"맙소사!\""],["said the curate with a shout, “‘Tirante el Blanco’ here!","신부가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"「티란테 엘 블랑코」가 여기 있다니!"],["Hand it over, gossip, for in it I reckon I have found a treasury of enjoyment and a mine of recreation.","친구, 그걸 이리 건네주게. 그 안에서 나는 즐거움의 보물 창고와 오락의 광맥을 찾았다고 보네."],["Here is Don Kyrieleison of Montalvan, a valiant knight, and his brother Thomas of Montalvan, and the knight Fonseca, with the battle the bold Tirante fought with the mastiff, and the witticisms of the damsel Placerdemivida, and the loves and wiles of the widow Reposada, and the empress in love with the squire Hipolito--in truth, gossip, by right of its style it is the best book in the world.","여기 용맹한 기사 몬탈반의 돈 키리엘레이손이 있고, 그의 동생 몬탈반의 토마스가, 그리고 기사 폰세카가 있네. 또 대담한 티란테가 마스티프와 벌인 싸움이며, 처녀 플라세르데미비다의 재담이며, 과부 레포사다의 사랑과 술책이며, 종자 이폴리토에게 반한 황후까지 — 정말이지, 친구, 그 문체의 권위로 따지면 이건 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 책일세."],["Here knights eat and sleep, and die in their beds, and make their wills before dying, and a great deal more of which there is nothing in all the other books.","여기서는 기사들이 먹고 자며, 자기 침대에서 죽고, 죽기 전에 유언장을 작성하니, 다른 모든 책에는 전혀 없는 것들이 이 밖에도 수두룩하다네."],["Nevertheless, I say he who wrote it, for deliberately composing such fooleries, deserves to be sent to the galleys for life.","그렇지만 나는 말하네, 이런 어리석은 짓들을 작정하고 지어낸 자는 평생 갤리선으로 보내져 마땅하다고."],["Take it home with you and read it, and you will see that what I have said is true.”","집에 가지고 가서 읽어 보게나, 내가 한 말이 사실임을 알게 될 테니.\""]],[["“As you will,”","\"분부대로 하지요.\""],["said the barber; “but what are we to do with these little books that are left?”","이발사가 말했다. \"그런데 여기 남은 이 작은 책들은 어찌할까요?\""]],[["“These must be, not chivalry, but poetry,”","\"이것들은 기사도가 아니라 시가가 틀림없네.\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate; and opening one he saw it was the “Diana” of Jorge de Montemayor, and, supposing all the others to be of the same sort, “these,”","그러고는 한 권을 펼치니 그것이 호르헤 데 몬테마요르의 「디아나」임을 알아보았고, 나머지도 모두 같은 종류이리라 짐작하며 이렇게 말했다."],["he said, “do not deserve to be burned like the others, for they neither do nor can do the mischief the books of chivalry have done, being books of entertainment that can hurt no one.”","\"이것들은 다른 것들처럼 불태울 자격이 없네. 누구도 해칠 수 없는 오락거리 책들이니, 기사도 책들이 끼친 화근을 이것들은 끼치지도 않거니와 끼칠 수도 없거든.\""]],[["“Ah, señor!” said the niece, “your worship had better order these to be burned as well as the others; for it would be no wonder if, after being cured of his chivalry disorder, my uncle, by reading these, took a fancy to turn shepherd and range the woods and fields singing and piping; or, what would be still worse, to turn poet, which they say is an incurable and infectious malady.”","\"아, 신부님!\" 조카딸이 말했다. \"이것들도 다른 것들과 함께 불태우라 명하시는 편이 나을 거예요. 삼촌이 그 기사도 병에서 나으신 뒤에 이것들을 읽고는 목동이 되어 노래하고 피리 불며 숲과 들을 떠돌고 싶어 하셔도 이상할 게 없을 테니까요. 아니면 더 나쁘게는 시인이 되고 싶어 하실 수도 있는데, 사람들 말로는 그게 불치이자 전염되는 병이라잖아요.\""]],[["“The damsel is right,”","\"이 처녀의 말이 옳네.\""],["said the curate, “and it will be well to put this stumbling-block and temptation out of our friend’s way.","신부가 말했다. \"우리 친구의 앞길에서 이 걸림돌이자 유혹을 치워 두는 게 좋겠어."],["To begin, then, with the ‘Diana’ of Montemayor.","그럼 우선 몬테마요르의 「디아나」부터 시작하지."],["I am of opinion it should not be burned, but that it should be cleared of all that about the sage Felicia and the magic water, and of almost all the longer pieces of verse: let it keep, and welcome, its prose and the honour of being the first of books of the kind.”","내 생각에 이건 불태울 게 아니라, 현자 펠리시아와 마법의 물에 관한 것 전부와, 거의 모든 긴 운문 대목을 깨끗이 도려내야 하네. 그러니 그 산문과, 이런 종류의 책들 가운데 첫째라는 영예는 부디 간직하게 두지.\""]],[["“This that comes next,”","\"다음에 나오는 이것은,\""],["said the barber, “is the ‘Diana,’ entitled the ‘Second Part, by the Salamancan,’ and this other has the same title, and its author is Gil Polo.”","이발사가 말했다. \"「디아나」인데, 「살라망카 사람이 쓴 제2부」라는 제목이고, 이 다른 것도 같은 제목인데 작자는 힐 폴로입니다.\""]],[["“As for that of the Salamancan,”","\"그 살라망카 사람의 것으로 말하자면,\" 신부가 답했다."],["replied the curate, “let it go to swell the number of the condemned in the yard, and let Gil Polo’s be preserved as if it came from Apollo himself: but get on, gossip, and make haste, for it is growing late.”","\"뒤뜰에서 단죄받은 무리의 수를 불리러 가게 하고, 힐 폴로의 것은 마치 아폴론 자신에게서 나온 양 보존하게. 하지만 친구, 어서 서두르게나, 시간이 늦어지고 있으니.\""]],[["“This book,”","\"이 책은,\" 이발사가 또 다른 책을 펼치며 말했다."],["said the barber, opening another, “is the ten books of the ‘Fortune of Love,’ written by Antonio de Lofraso, a Sardinian poet.”","\"사르데냐의 시인 안토니오 데 로프라소가 쓴 「사랑의 행운」 열 권입니다.\""]],[["“By the orders I have received,”","\"내가 받은 성직에 걸고 말하건대,\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “since Apollo has been Apollo, and the Muses have been Muses, and poets have been poets, so droll and absurd a book as this has never been written, and in its way it is the best and the most singular of all of this species that have as yet appeared, and he who has not read it may be sure he has never read what is delightful.","\"아폴론이 아폴론이 된 이래, 뮤즈들이 뮤즈가 된 이래, 시인들이 시인이 된 이래, 이토록 우스꽝스럽고 터무니없는 책은 일찍이 쓰인 적이 없네. 그 나름으로 이것은 지금껏 나온 이런 부류 가운데 으뜸이자 가장 독보적인 것이며, 이걸 읽어 보지 않은 자는 진정 유쾌한 것을 읽어 본 적이 없다고 단언해도 좋네."],["Give it here, gossip, for I make more account of having found it than if they had given me a cassock of Florence stuff.”","이리 주게, 친구. 나는 피렌체산 천으로 지은 사제복을 받은 것보다 이걸 찾아낸 것을 더 값지게 여긴다네.\""]],[["He put it aside with extreme satisfaction, and the barber went on, “These that come next are ‘The Shepherd of Iberia,’ ‘Nymphs of Henares,’ and ‘The Enlightenment of Jealousy.’”","그는 더할 나위 없는 만족감으로 그것을 따로 챙겨 두었고, 이발사는 계속했다. \"다음에 나오는 이것들은 「이베리아의 목동」과 「에나레스의 님프들」과 「질투의 깨우침」입니다.\""]],[["“Then all we have to do,”","\"그렇다면 우리가 할 일이라곤,\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “is to hand them over to the secular arm of the housekeeper, and ask me not why, or we shall never have done.”","\"그것들을 가정부의 세속적 처형의 팔에 넘기는 것뿐일세. 왜냐고는 묻지 말게, 안 그러면 우린 결코 끝을 보지 못할 테니.\""]],[["“This next is the ‘Pastor de Filida.’”","\"이다음 것은 「필리다의 목동」입니다.\""]],[["“No Pastor that,”","\"그건 목동이 아닐세.\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “but a highly polished courtier; let it be preserved as a precious jewel.”","\"고도로 세련된 궁정인이지. 귀한 보석처럼 보존하게.\""]],[["“This large one here,”","\"여기 이 큰 것은,\""],["said the barber, “is called ‘The Treasury of various Poems.’”","이발사가 말했다. \"「갖가지 시가의 보고」라 불립니다.\""]],[["“If there were not so many of them,” said the curate, “they would be more relished: this book must be weeded and cleansed of certain vulgarities which it has with its excellences; let it be preserved because the author is a friend of mine, and out of respect for other more heroic and loftier works that he has written.”","\"수가 그렇게 많지만 않았더라면,\" 신부가 말했다. \"더 음미되었을 텐데. 이 책은 그 빼어남과 더불어 지닌 어떤 저속한 대목들을 솎아 내고 깨끗이 해야 하네. 작자가 내 벗이고, 그가 쓴 다른 더 영웅적이고 더 고매한 작품들을 존중하는 뜻에서 보존하기로 하지.\""]],[["“This,”","\"이것은,\""],["continued the barber, “is the ‘Cancionero’ of Lopez de Maldonado.”","이발사가 이어 말했다. \"로페스 데 말도나도의 「가요집」입니다.\""]],[["“The author of that book, too,” said the curate, “is a great friend of mine, and his verses from his own mouth are the admiration of all who hear them, for such is the sweetness of his voice that he enchants when he chants them: it gives rather too much of its eclogues, but what is good was never yet plentiful: let it be kept with those that have been set apart. But what book is that next it?”","\"그 책의 작자 또한,\" 신부가 말했다. \"나의 절친한 벗이라네. 그의 입에서 직접 나오는 시구들은 듣는 모든 이의 감탄을 자아내니, 그 목소리가 어찌나 감미로운지 노래로 읊을 때면 듣는 이를 홀려 버린다네. 목가가 좀 지나치게 많기는 하나, 좋은 것이 풍성했던 적은 일찍이 없으니. 따로 떼어 둔 것들과 함께 간직하게. 그런데 그 옆의 저 책은 무엇인가?\""]],[["“The ‘Galatea’ of Miguel de Cervantes,”","\"미겔 데 세르반테스의 「갈라테아」입니다.\""],["said the barber.","이발사가 말했다."]],[["“That Cervantes has been for many years a great friend of mine, and to my knowledge he has had more experience in reverses than in verses. His book has some good invention in it, it presents us with something but brings nothing to a conclusion: we must wait for the Second Part it promises: perhaps with amendment it may succeed in winning the full measure of grace that is now denied it; and in the mean time do you, señor gossip, keep it shut up in your own quarters.”","\"그 세르반테스는 여러 해 동안 나의 절친한 벗이었네. 그런데 내가 알기로 그는 시구를 짓는 일보다 역경을 겪는 일에 더 경험이 많지. 그의 책에는 좋은 창작이 얼마간 들어 있어, 우리에게 무언가를 내놓기는 하나 어떤 것도 결말에 이르게 하지는 못하네. 그것이 약속한 제2부를 우리는 기다려야 하지. 어쩌면 손질을 거치면, 지금은 거부당하고 있는 그 온전한 은총을 마침내 얻어 낼지도 모르겠네. 그러니 그동안은, 친구, 그것을 자네 거처에 가두어 두게나.\""]],[["“Very good,”","\"좋습니다.\" 이발사가 말했다."],["said the barber; “and here come three together, the ‘Araucana’ of Don Alonso de Ercilla, the ‘Austriada’ of Juan Rufo, Justice of Cordova, and the ‘Montserrate’ of Christobal de Virues, the Valencian poet.”","\"그리고 여기 세 권이 함께 나오는군요. 돈 알론소 데 에르시야의 「라 아라우카나」, 코르도바의 법관 후안 루포의 「라 아우스트리아다」, 그리고 발렌시아의 시인 크리스토발 데 비루에스의 「엘 몬세라테」입니다.\""]],[["“These three books,”","\"이 세 책은,\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “are the best that have been written in Castilian in heroic verse, and they may compare with the most famous of Italy; let them be preserved as the richest treasures of poetry that Spain possesses.”","\"카스티야어로 영웅 운율로 쓰인 것들 가운데 으뜸이며, 이탈리아의 가장 유명한 것들과도 견줄 만하네. 스페인이 지닌 가장 풍요로운 시가의 보물로서 보존하게.\""]],[["The curate was tired and would not look into any more books, and so he decided that, “contents uncertified,”","신부는 지친 데다 더는 어떤 책도 들여다보고 싶지 않았기에, \"내용 미확인\"으로 나머지는 모조리 불태우기로 정했다."],["all the rest should be burned; but just then the barber held open one, called “The Tears of Angelica.”","그런데 바로 그때 이발사가 한 권을 펼쳐 들었으니, 「안젤리카의 눈물」이라 불리는 책이었다."]],[["“I should have shed tears myself,”","\"내가 그 책을 불태우라 명했더라면,\" 제목을 듣고 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate when he heard the title, “had I ordered that book to be burned, for its author was one of the famous poets of the world, not to say of Spain, and was very happy in the translation of some of Ovid’s fables.”","\"나 자신이 눈물을 흘렸을 걸세. 그 작자는 스페인은 말할 것도 없고 세계에서 손꼽히는 시인 중 하나였고, 오비디우스의 우화 몇 편을 옮기는 데 매우 뛰어났으니 말일세.\""]],[["# CHAPTER VII. OF THE SECOND SALLY OF OUR WORTHY KNIGHT DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA","# CHAPTER VII."]],[["At this instant Don Quixote began shouting out, “Here, here, valiant knights!","이 순간 돈키호테가 고함을 지르기 시작했다. “이리로, 이리로 오시오, 용맹한 기사들이여!"],["here is need for you to put forth the might of your strong arms, for they of the Court are gaining the mastery in the tourney!”","그대들의 억센 팔의 힘을 떨쳐야 할 때가 바로 지금이오. 궁정의 무리가 마상 시합에서 승기를 잡아 가고 있단 말이오!”"],["Called away by this noise and outcry, they proceeded no farther with the scrutiny of the remaining books, and so it is thought that “The Carolea,” “The Lion of Spain,”","이 소란과 외침에 불려 가느라 그들은 남은 책들의 검열을 더 진행하지 못했고, 그리하여 돈 루이스 데 아빌라가 쓴 「카롤레아」와 「스페인의 사자」와 「황제의 업적」이 보이지도 들리지도 않은 채 불 속으로 들어간 것이라 여겨진다."],["and “The Deeds of the Emperor,” written by Don Luis de Avila, went to the fire unseen and unheard; for no doubt they were among those that remained, and perhaps if the curate had seen them they would not have undergone so severe a sentence.","의심할 바 없이 그것들은 남아 있던 책들 가운데 있었을 터이며, 어쩌면 신부가 그것들을 보았더라면 그토록 가혹한 선고를 받지는 않았으리라."]],[["When they reached Don Quixote he was already out of bed, and was still shouting and raving, and slashing and cutting all round, as wide awake as if he had never slept.","그들이 돈키호테에게 다다랐을 때 그는 이미 침대에서 나와 여전히 고함치고 헛소리를 지르며 사방을 베고 찌르고 있었으니, 한숨도 잔 적이 없는 사람처럼 말짱하게 깨어 있었다."]],[["They closed with him and by force got him back to bed, and when he had become a little calm, addressing the curate, he said to him, “Of a truth, Señor Archbishop Turpin, it is a great disgrace for us who call ourselves the Twelve Peers, so carelessly to allow the knights of the Court to gain the victory in this tourney, we the adventurers having carried off the honour on the three former days.”","그들은 그를 붙들고 억지로 다시 침대에 눕혔고, 그가 조금 진정되었을 때 그가 신부를 향해 말했다. “참으로, 투르핀 대주교 예하, 우리 십이 기사라 자처하는 이들이 마상 시합에서 궁정의 기사들에게 승리를 그토록 무심히 내주다니 이는 우리에게 크나큰 수치입니다. 앞선 사흘 동안은 우리 모험 기사들이 영예를 차지했는데도 말입니다.”"]],[["“Hush, gossip,” said the curate; “please God, the luck may turn, and what is lost to-day may be won to-morrow; for the present let your worship have a care of your health, for it seems to me that you are over-fatigued, if not badly wounded.”","“쉿, 친구.” 신부가 말했다. “하느님 뜻이라면 운수가 돌아설 것이고, 오늘 잃은 것을 내일 되찾을 수도 있을 것이오. 당장은 그대의 몸이나 돌보시오. 심하게 다치지는 않았더라도 너무 지친 듯이 보이니 말이오.”"]],[["“Wounded no,” said Don Quixote, “but bruised and battered no doubt, for that bastard Don Roland has cudgelled me with the trunk of an oak tree, and all for envy, because he sees that I alone rival him in his achievements. But I should not call myself Reinaldos of Montalvan did he not pay me for it in spite of all his enchantments as soon as I rise from this bed. For the present let them bring me something to eat, for that, I feel, is what will be more to my purpose, and leave it to me to avenge myself.”","“다치다니, 아니오.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “하나 멍들고 두들겨 맞은 것은 의심할 바 없소. 그 후레자식 돈 롤단이 떡갈나무 둥치로 나를 후려쳤으니 말이오. 모두 시기심에서 비롯된 짓이오. 그가 이룬 업적에서 나 홀로 그와 어깨를 겨룬다는 걸 보았기 때문이지. 그러나 내가 이 침대에서 일어나는 즉시 그의 온갖 마법에도 불구하고 그가 그 대가를 치르게 하지 못한다면, 나는 나를 몬탈반의 레이날도스라 부르지 않겠소. 당장은 먹을 것이나 좀 가져다주시오. 그것이 지금 내게 가장 요긴한 것이라 느껴지니 말이오. 복수하는 일은 내게 맡겨 두시오.”"]],[["They did as he wished; they gave him something to eat, and once more he fell asleep, leaving them marvelling at his madness.","그들은 그가 바라는 대로 했다. 먹을 것을 가져다주었고, 그는 다시 한번 잠이 들었으며, 그들은 그의 광기에 놀라움을 금치 못한 채 자리를 떴다."]],[["That night the housekeeper burned to ashes all the books that were in the yard and in the whole house; and some must have been consumed that deserved preservation in everlasting archives, but their fate and the laziness of the examiner did not permit it, and so in them was verified the proverb that the innocent suffer for the guilty.","그날 밤 가정부는 마당에 있던 책과 온 집 안에 있던 책을 모조리 태워 재로 만들었다. 그 가운데는 영원한 기록 보관소에 길이 간직될 만한 책들도 분명 타 버렸을 터이나, 그것들의 운명과 검열자의 게으름이 그것을 허락하지 않았으니, 죄지은 자 때문에 죄 없는 자가 고초를 겪는다는 속담이 그것들에게서 그대로 입증된 셈이었다."]],[["One of the remedies which the curate and the barber immediately applied to their friend’s disorder was to wall up and plaster the room where the books were, so that when he got up he should not find them (possibly the cause being removed the effect might cease), and they might say that a magician had carried them off, room and all; and this was done with all despatch.","신부와 이발사가 벗의 병에 즉각 적용한 처방 가운데 하나는 책들이 있던 방을 벽으로 막고 회칠을 해 버리는 것이었다. 그가 일어났을 때 그 방을 찾지 못하게 하려는 것으로(어쩌면 원인이 사라지면 결과도 그치리라 여긴 것이리라), 어느 마법사가 방째로 책을 죄다 가져가 버렸다고 둘러댈 셈이었다."],["Two days later Don Quixote got up, and the first thing he did was to go and look at his books, and not finding the room where he had left it, he wandered from side to side looking for it.","이 일은 한껏 서둘러 이루어졌다. 이틀 뒤 돈키호테가 일어나 맨 먼저 한 일은 자기 책들을 보러 가는 것이었으나, 두고 갔던 그 방을 찾지 못하자 그는 이리저리 헤매며 방을 찾아다녔다."],["He came to the place where the door used to be, and tried it with his hands, and turned and twisted his eyes in every direction without saying a word; but after a good while he asked his housekeeper whereabouts was the room that held his books.","문이 있던 자리에 이르러 그는 손으로 더듬어 보고, 한마디 말도 없이 눈을 사방으로 굴리고 또 굴려 보았다. 그러나 한참 뒤에야 그는 자기 책들이 들어 있던 방이 어디쯤이냐고 가정부에게 물었다."]],[["The housekeeper, who had been already well instructed in what she was to answer, said, “What room or what nothing is it that your worship is looking for?","무어라 대답할지 이미 단단히 일러 받은 가정부가 말했다. “나리께서 찾으시는 게 무슨 방이고 또 무슨 아무것도 아닌 것이옵니까?"],["There are neither room nor books in this house now, for the devil himself has carried all away.”","이 집에는 이제 방도 책도 없습죠. 악마가 직접 와서 죄다 가져가 버렸으니까요.”"]],[["“It was not the devil,” said the niece, “but a magician who came on a cloud one night after the day your worship left this, and dismounting from a serpent that he rode he entered the room, and what he did there I know not, but after a little while he made off, flying through the roof, and left the house full of smoke; and when we went to see what he had done we saw neither book nor room: but we remember very well, the housekeeper and I, that on leaving, the old villain said in a loud voice that, for a private grudge he owed the owner of the books and the room, he had done mischief in that house that would be discovered by-and-by: he said too that his name was the Sage Munaton.”","“악마가 아니었어요.” 조카딸이 말했다. “마법사였답니다. 외삼촌이 이곳을 떠나신 다음 날 밤에 구름을 타고 와서, 타고 온 뱀에서 내리더니 그 방으로 들어갔어요. 거기서 무슨 짓을 했는지는 모르겠지만, 잠시 후에 지붕을 뚫고 날아 달아나면서 집 안을 온통 연기로 가득 채워 놓았답니다. 그가 무슨 짓을 했나 보러 갔더니 책도 방도 보이지 않았어요. 다만 가정부와 저는 똑똑히 기억해요. 그 늙은 악당이 떠나면서 큰 소리로 말하기를, 책과 방의 주인에게 사사로운 원한이 있어 이 집에 해코지를 해 두었으니 머지않아 드러나리라고 했어요. 또 제 이름이 현자 무나톤이라고도 했답니다.”"]],[["“He must have said Friston,”","“프리스톤이라고 했을 게다.”"],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“I don’t know whether he called himself Friston or Friton,”","“프리스톤이라 했는지 프리톤이라 했는지는 모르겠어요.”"],["said the housekeeper, “I only know that his name ended with ‘ton.’”","가정부가 말했다. “그저 이름이 ‘톤’으로 끝났다는 것만 알 뿐이지요.”"]],[["“So it does,” said Don Quixote, “and he is a sage magician, a great enemy of mine, who has a spite against me because he knows by his arts and lore that in process of time I am to engage in single combat with a knight whom he befriends and that I am to conquer, and he will be unable to prevent it; and for this reason he endeavours to do me all the ill turns that he can; but I promise him it will be hard for him to oppose or avoid what is decreed by Heaven.”","“그렇지.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “그자는 현자 마법사이며 나의 큰 원수다. 그자는 제 술법과 학식으로, 머지않아 내가 자기가 비호하는 어느 기사와 일대일로 맞붙어 그를 무찌르게 되리라는 것을, 그리고 그것을 자기가 막을 수 없으리라는 것을 알기에 나에게 앙심을 품고 있는 것이다. 이런 까닭에 그자는 할 수 있는 온갖 해코지를 내게 하려 드는 것이지. 그러나 하늘이 정한 바를 거스르거나 피하기란 그자에게 쉽지 않으리라는 것을 내 장담하마.”"]],[["“Who doubts that?”","“누가 그걸 의심하겠어요?”"],["said the niece; “but, uncle, who mixes you up in these quarrels?","조카딸이 말했다. “하지만 외삼촌, 누가 외삼촌을 이런 싸움에 끌어들였답니까?"],["Would it not be better to remain at peace in your own house instead of roaming the world looking for better bread than ever came of wheat, never reflecting that many go for wool and come back shorn?”","밀에서 난 적도 없는 더 좋은 빵을 찾아 세상을 떠돌아다니느니 차라리 외삼촌 댁에서 편안히 지내시는 게 낫지 않겠어요? 양털을 구하러 갔다가 제 털까지 깎이고 돌아오는 이가 한둘이 아니라는 걸 통 헤아리지 못하시고요.”"]],[["“Oh, niece of mine,”","“오, 내 조카딸아.” 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote, “how much astray art thou in thy reckoning: ere they shear me I shall have plucked away and stripped off the beards of all who dare to touch only the tip of a hair of mine.”","“네 셈은 어찌 그리도 빗나갔느냐. 그들이 내 털을 깎기 전에, 내 머리카락 한 올의 끄트머리라도 감히 건드리려는 자들의 수염을 죄다 쥐어뜯어 송두리째 벗겨 놓을 것이니라.”"]],[["The two were unwilling to make any further answer, as they saw that his anger was kindling.","두 사람은 그의 노여움이 타오르는 것을 보고 더는 아무 대꾸도 하려 들지 않았다."]],[["In short, then, he remained at home fifteen days very quietly without showing any signs of a desire to take up with his former delusions, and during this time he held lively discussions with his two gossips, the curate and the barber, on the point he maintained, that knights-errant were what the world stood most in need of, and that in him was to be accomplished the revival of knight-errantry.","요컨대 그는 보름 동안 집에 머물며 매우 조용히 지냈으니, 예전의 망상을 다시 붙들고 싶어 하는 기색이라고는 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 그동안 그는 두 친구인 신부와 이발사와 더불어, 자기가 주장하는 한 가지 논점을 두고 활기찬 토론을 벌였다."],["The curate sometimes contradicted him, sometimes agreed with him, for if he had not observed this precaution he would have been unable to bring him to reason.","곧 세상이 가장 절실히 필요로 하는 것이 편력 기사이며, 편력 기사도의 부흥이 바로 자기 안에서 이루어지리라는 것이었다. 신부는 때로는 그를 반박하고 때로는 그에게 동조했으니, 이렇게 조심하지 않았더라면 그를 도무지 이치로 돌이킬 수 없었을 것이다."]],[["Meanwhile Don Quixote worked upon a farm labourer, a neighbour of his, an honest man (if indeed that title can be given to him who is poor), but with very little wit in his pate.","그러는 사이 돈키호테는 이웃에 사는 한 농사꾼을 구슬렸다. 그는 정직한 사람이었으나(가난한 사람에게 그런 칭호를 붙일 수 있다면 말이다), 머릿속에 든 재치라고는 거의 없는 자였다."],["In a word, he so talked him over, and with such persuasions and promises, that the poor clown made up his mind to sally forth with him and serve him as esquire.","한마디로 돈키호테는 어찌나 잘 구슬리고 어찌나 그럴듯한 설득과 약속을 늘어놓았던지, 그 가엾은 촌뜨기는 마침내 그와 함께 길을 나서 종자로 섬기기로 마음먹었다."],["Don Quixote, among other things, told him he ought to be ready to go with him gladly, because any moment an adventure might occur that might win an island in the twinkling of an eye and leave him governor of it.","무엇보다 돈키호테는, 어느 순간 눈 깜짝할 사이에 섬 하나를 손에 넣어 그를 그 섬의 영주로 앉혀 줄 만한 모험이 닥칠 수 있으니 기꺼이 따라나설 채비를 해 두라고 일러 주었다."],["On these and the like promises Sancho Panza (for so the labourer was called) left wife and children, and engaged himself as esquire to his neighbour.","이런저런 약속에 솔깃하여 산초 판사는(그 농사꾼은 그렇게 불렸다) 처자식을 두고 이웃 사람의 종자가 되기로 한 것이다."]],[["Don Quixote next set about getting some money; and selling one thing and pawning another, and making a bad bargain in every case, he got together a fair sum.","다음으로 돈키호테는 약간의 돈을 마련하는 일에 착수했다. 이것저것 팔고 또 이것저것 저당 잡히면서 매번 손해 보는 거래를 한 끝에 그는 그럭저럭 적잖은 돈을 모았다."],["He provided himself with a buckler, which he begged as a loan from a friend, and, restoring his battered helmet as best he could, he warned his squire Sancho of the day and hour he meant to set out, that he might provide himself with what he thought most needful.","그는 한 벗에게 꾸어 얻은 둥근 방패를 마련했고, 망가진 투구를 할 수 있는 데까지 손본 다음, 종자 산초에게 자기가 떠나려는 날과 시각을 일러 주며 가장 요긴하다 여기는 것을 챙겨 두라고 당부했다."],["Above all, he charged him to take alforjas with him.","무엇보다 그는 산초에게 안장주머니를 챙겨 오라고 단단히 일렀다."],["The other said he would, and that he meant to take also a very good ass he had, as he was not much given to going on foot.","산초는 그러겠노라 했고, 자기가 가진 아주 좋은 당나귀도 데려갈 작정이라 했으니, 걸어 다니는 데에는 그리 익숙하지 않았기 때문이다."],["About the ass, Don Quixote hesitated a little, trying whether he could call to mind any knight-errant taking with him an esquire mounted on ass-back, but no instance occurred to his memory.","당나귀 일을 두고 돈키호테는 잠시 망설이며, 종자를 당나귀에 태워 데리고 다닌 편력 기사가 있었던지 떠올려 보려 했으나 기억나는 사례라고는 하나도 없었다."],["For all that, however, he determined to take him, intending to furnish him with a more honourable mount when a chance of it presented itself, by appropriating the horse of the first discourteous knight he encountered.","그럼에도 그는 산초를 데려가기로 마음먹었으니, 기회가 닿는 대로 처음 마주치는 무례한 기사의 말을 빼앗아 더 명예로운 탈것을 마련해 줄 작정이었던 것이다. 자신을 위해서는 셔츠 몇 벌과 그 밖에 할 수 있는 만큼의 것들을, 객줏집 주인이 일러 준 조언에 따라 챙겼다."],["Himself he provided with shirts and such other things as he could, according to the advice the host had given him; all which being done, without taking leave, Sancho Panza of his wife and children, or Don Quixote of his housekeeper and niece, they sallied forth unseen by anybody from the village one night, and made such good way in the course of it that by daylight they held themselves safe from discovery, even should search be made for them.","이 모든 일을 마치고서, 산초 판사는 처자식에게, 돈키호테는 가정부와 조카딸에게 작별 인사도 없이, 어느 날 밤 마을에서 아무에게도 들키지 않고 길을 나섰다. 그날 밤 그들은 어찌나 잘 나아갔던지, 날이 밝을 무렵에는 행여 누가 자기들을 찾아 나선다 해도 들킬 염려가 없으리라 여겼다."]],[["Sancho rode on his ass like a patriarch, with his alforjas and bota, and longing to see himself soon governor of the island his master had promised him.","산초는 안장주머니와 술 부대를 챙긴 채 마치 족장이라도 된 양 당나귀를 타고 갔으니, 주인이 약속한 그 섬의 영주가 된 자기 모습을 어서 보고 싶어 안달이었다."],["Don Quixote decided upon taking the same route and road he had taken on his first journey, that over the Campo de Montiel, which he travelled with less discomfort than on the last occasion, for, as it was early morning and the rays of the sun fell on them obliquely, the heat did not distress them.","돈키호테는 첫 여행에서 지나갔던 그 길, 곧 몬티엘 들판을 가로지르는 길을 그대로 택하기로 했는데, 지난번보다는 한결 덜 고생스럽게 지나갔으니, 이른 아침이라 햇살이 비스듬히 내리쬐어 더위가 그들을 괴롭히지 않았던 까닭이다."]],[["And now said Sancho Panza to his master, “Your worship will take care, Señor Knight-errant, not to forget about the island you have promised me, for be it ever so big I’ll be equal to governing it.”","이때 산초 판사가 주인에게 말했다. “편력 기사 나리, 제게 약속하신 그 섬을 잊지 않도록 잘 챙기셔야 합니다요. 아무리 크다 해도 저는 그걸 다스릴 만하니까요.”"]],[["To which Don Quixote replied, “Thou must know, friend Sancho Panza, that it was a practice very much in vogue with the knights-errant of old to make their squires governors of the islands or kingdoms they won, and I am determined that there shall be no failure on my part in so liberal a custom; on the contrary, I mean to improve upon it, for they sometimes, and perhaps most frequently, waited until their squires were old, and then when they had had enough of service and hard days and worse nights, they gave them some title or other, of count, or at the most marquis, of some valley or province more or less; but if thou livest and I live, it may well be that before six days are over, I may have won some kingdom that has others dependent upon it, which will be just the thing to enable thee to be crowned king of one of them. Nor needst thou count this wonderful, for things and chances fall to the lot of such knights in ways so unexampled and unexpected that I might easily give thee even more than I promise thee.”","이에 돈키호테가 대답했다. “벗 산초 판사여, 알아 두어라. 옛 편력 기사들은 자기가 얻은 섬이나 왕국의 영주로 종자를 앉히는 관행을 무척이나 즐겨 따랐으니, 나는 이토록 후한 관습에서 내 몫을 결코 소홀히 하지 않으리라 마음먹었다. 오히려 나는 그것을 한층 낫게 만들 작정이다. 그들은 더러, 아니 어쩌면 가장 흔하게는, 종자가 늙기를 기다렸다가 섬김에 진력나고 고된 낮과 더 고된 밤을 실컷 겪은 뒤에야 비로소 백작이라느니 기껏해야 후작이라느니 하는 칭호를, 그것도 어느 골짜기나 변변찮은 지방의 칭호를 내려 주었다. 그러나 네가 살아 있고 나도 살아 있다면, 엿새가 채 지나기 전에 다른 여러 왕국을 거느린 어느 왕국을 손에 넣을 수도 있을 것이며, 그리되면 너를 그중 한 왕국의 왕으로 즉위시키기에 안성맞춤일 것이다. 이를 두고 놀라워할 것 없으니, 이런 기사들에게는 사물과 기회가 유례없고 뜻밖의 방식으로 굴러들어 오기에, 내가 네게 약속한 것보다 더한 것조차 손쉽게 내려 줄 수 있을 것이다.”"]],[["“In that case,”","“그렇다면야.”"],["said Sancho Panza, “if I should become a king by one of those miracles your worship speaks of, even Juana Gutierrez, my old woman, would come to be queen and my children infantes.”","산초 판사가 말했다. “나리께서 말씀하시는 그런 기적 가운데 하나로 제가 왕이 된다면, 제 마누라 후아나 구티에레스도 왕비가 되고 제 자식들은 왕자가 되겠습니다요.”"]],[["“Well, who doubts it?”","“글쎄, 누가 그걸 의심하겠느냐?”"],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“I doubt it,”","“제가 의심합니다요.” 산초 판사가 대답했다."],["replied Sancho Panza, “because for my part I am persuaded that though God should shower down kingdoms upon earth, not one of them would fit the head of Mari Gutierrez.","“저로 말씀드리자면, 설령 하느님이 이 땅에 왕국들을 비처럼 쏟아부으신다 해도 그중 어느 하나도 마리 구티에레스의 머리에는 맞지 않으리라 굳게 믿고 있으니까요. 나리, 말씀드리는데, 그 사람은 왕비 노릇 하기엔 두 푼어치도 못 됩니다요."],["Let me tell you, señor, she is not worth two maravedis for a queen; countess will fit her better, and that only with God’s help.”","백작 부인이라면 좀 더 어울리겠지요. 그것도 하느님이 도우셔야 말입니다요.”"]],[["“Leave it to God, Sancho,”","“그건 하느님께 맡겨 두어라, 산초.” 돈키호테가 대꾸했다."],["returned Don Quixote, “for he will give her what suits her best; but do not undervalue thyself so much as to come to be content with anything less than being governor of a province.”","“그분이 그 사람에게 가장 알맞은 것을 주실 테니 말이다. 다만 너 자신을 너무 낮추어 한 지방의 영주가 되는 것보다 못한 것에 만족하는 처지에 이르지는 마라.”"]],[["“I will not, señor,”","“그러지 않겠습니다요, 나리.”"],["answered Sancho, “specially as I have a man of such quality for a master in your worship, who will know how to give me all that will be suitable for me and that I can bear.”","산초가 대답했다. “더구나 저는 나리 같은 훌륭한 분을 주인으로 모시고 있으니, 나리께서 제게 알맞고 제가 감당할 수 있는 만큼을 다 베푸실 줄 아실 테니까요.”"]],[["# CHAPTER VIII. OF THE GOOD FORTUNE WHICH THE VALIANT DON QUIXOTE HAD IN THE TERRIBLE AND UNDREAMT-OF ADVENTURE OF THE WINDMILLS, WITH OTHER OCCURRENCES WORTHY TO BE FITLY RECORDED","# CHAPTER VIII."]],[["At this point they came in sight of thirty or forty windmills that there are on that plain, and as soon as Don Quixote saw them he said to his squire, “Fortune is arranging matters for us better than we could have shaped our desires ourselves, for look there, friend Sancho Panza, where thirty or more monstrous giants present themselves, all of whom I mean to engage in battle and slay, and with whose spoils we shall begin to make our fortunes; for this is righteous warfare, and it is God’s good service to sweep so evil a breed from off the face of the earth.”","이때 두 사람은 그 들판에 서 있는 서른 대인가 마흔 대의 풍차를 보게 되었는데, 돈키호테는 풍차를 보자마자 자기 종자에게 말했다. \"운명이 우리가 바랄 수 있었던 것보다 더 좋게 일을 마련해 주는구나. 보아라, 산초 판사 친구야, 저기에 서른 명이 넘는 괴물 같은 거인들이 모습을 드러내고 있으니, 나는 저들 모두와 싸워 죽일 작정이며, 그 전리품으로 우리는 부를 쌓기 시작할 것이다. 이것이야말로 정의로운 싸움이요, 저토록 사악한 족속을 땅 위에서 쓸어 내는 것은 하느님을 섬기는 갸륵한 일이니라.\""]],[["“What giants?”","\"무슨 거인 말입니까?\""],["said Sancho Panza.","산초 판사가 말했다."]],[["“Those thou seest there,”","\"저기 네가 보는 저들 말이다.\""],["answered his master, “with the long arms, and some have them nearly two leagues long.”","주인이 대답했다. \"기다란 팔을 가진 자들로, 어떤 놈은 그 팔이 거의 이 레구아나 되느니라.\""]],[["“Look, your worship,”","\"보십시오, 나리.\""],["said Sancho; “what we see there are not giants but windmills, and what seem to be their arms are the sails that turned by the wind make the millstone go.”","산초가 말했다. \"저기 보이는 것은 거인이 아니라 풍차이고, 팔처럼 보이는 것은 바람을 받아 돌면서 맷돌을 돌리는 날개입니다.\""]],[["“It is easy to see,”","\"네가 이 모험이라는 일에 익숙하지 못하다는 것이 쉬이 드러나는구나.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote, “that thou art not used to this business of adventures; those are giants; and if thou art afraid, away with thee out of this and betake thyself to prayer while I engage them in fierce and unequal combat.”","\"저것들은 거인이다. 만일 네가 두렵거든 여기서 물러나 기도나 드리고 있거라, 내가 저들과 사납고도 불공평한 싸움을 벌이는 동안 말이다.\""]],[["So saying, he gave the spur to his steed Rocinante, heedless of the cries his squire Sancho sent after him, warning him that most certainly they were windmills and not giants he was going to attack.","이렇게 말하고는, 풍차이지 거인이 아니니 공격하려는 것은 분명 풍차라고 뒤에서 외치며 경고하는 종자 산초의 소리에는 아랑곳하지 않고, 그는 자기 말 로시난테에게 박차를 가했다."],["He, however, was so positive they were giants that he neither heard the cries of Sancho, nor perceived, near as he was, what they were, but made at them shouting, “Fly not, cowards and vile beings, for a single knight attacks you.”","그는 저것들이 거인이라고 어찌나 확신하던지, 산초의 외침도 듣지 못하고 그토록 가까이 있으면서도 그것이 무엇인지 알아보지 못한 채, \"달아나지 마라, 비겁하고 천한 것들아, 너희를 공격하는 것은 단 한 명의 기사이니라\" 하고 소리치며 그것들을 향해 달려들었다."]],[["A slight breeze at this moment sprang up, and the great sails began to move, seeing which Don Quixote exclaimed, “Though ye flourish more arms than the giant Briareus, ye have to reckon with me.”","이때 가벼운 산들바람이 일어 큰 날개들이 움직이기 시작하니, 그것을 본 돈키호테가 외쳤다. \"네놈들이 거인 브리아레오스보다 더 많은 팔을 휘두른다 해도, 나와 셈을 치러야 하리라.\""]],[["So saying, and commending himself with all his heart to his lady Dulcinea, imploring her to support him in such a peril, with lance in rest and covered by his buckler, he charged at Rocinante’s fullest gallop and fell upon the first mill that stood in front of him; but as he drove his lance-point into the sail the wind whirled it round with such force that it shivered the lance to pieces, sweeping with it horse and rider, who went rolling over on the plain, in a sorry condition.","이렇게 말하고는, 온 마음을 다해 자기 여인 둘시네아에게 자신을 맡기며 이런 위험 속에서 도와 달라고 간청한 뒤, 창을 겨누고 방패로 몸을 가린 채 로시난테가 낼 수 있는 가장 빠른 속도로 돌진하여, 앞에 서 있던 첫 번째 풍차에 덤벼들었다. 그러나 그가 날개에 창끝을 박아 넣자 바람이 어찌나 세게 그것을 휘돌렸던지 창이 산산조각으로 부서지면서, 말과 기사를 함께 휩쓸어 가 버려, 둘은 가련한 꼴로 들판을 데굴데굴 굴렀다."],["Sancho hastened to his assistance as fast as his ass could go, and when he came up found him unable to move, with such a shock had Rocinante fallen with him.","산초가 나귀가 갈 수 있는 한 서둘러 도우러 달려가서 다다라 보니, 그가 꼼짝도 못 하고 있었으니, 로시난테가 그와 함께 그토록 심하게 곤두박질친 까닭이었다."]],[["“God bless me!”","\"맙소사!\""],["said Sancho, “did I not tell your worship to mind what you were about, for they were only windmills?","산초가 말했다. \"제가 나리께 무슨 일을 하시려는지 조심하시라고, 저건 그저 풍차일 뿐이라고 말씀드리지 않았습니까?"],["and no one could have made any mistake about it but one who had something of the same kind in his head.”","머릿속에 그와 같은 무언가가 들어 있는 사람이 아니고서야 누구도 그것을 잘못 볼 리 없었을 것입니다.\""]],[["“Hush, friend Sancho,” replied Don Quixote, “the fortunes of war more than any other are liable to frequent fluctuations; and moreover I think, and it is the truth, that that same sage Friston who carried off my study and books, has turned these giants into mills in order to rob me of the glory of vanquishing them, such is the enmity he bears me; but in the end his wicked arts will avail but little against my good sword.”","\"조용히 하거라, 산초 친구야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"전쟁의 운수란 다른 어떤 일보다도 자주 부침을 겪기 마련이다. 게다가 내 생각에는, 그리고 이것은 진실인데, 내 서재와 책들을 훔쳐 간 바로 그 현자 프리스톤이 이 거인들을 풍차로 둔갑시킨 것이니, 이는 내가 그들을 무찌르는 영광을 빼앗으려는 것으로, 그자가 내게 품은 적의가 그러하기 때문이다. 그러나 결국 그자의 사악한 술수도 내 좋은 검 앞에서는 별 소용이 없으리라.\""]],[["“God order it as he may,”","\"하느님께서 뜻대로 하시기를.\""],["said Sancho Panza, and helping him to rise got him up again on Rocinante, whose shoulder was half out; and then, discussing the late adventure, they followed the road to Puerto Lapice, for there, said Don Quixote, they could not fail to find adventures in abundance and variety, as it was a great thoroughfare.","산초 판사가 말하고는, 그가 일어서도록 거들어 한쪽 어깨가 거의 빠진 로시난테에 다시 태웠다. 그러고 나서 방금 겪은 모험을 이야기하며 두 사람은 푸에르토 라피세로 가는 길을 따라갔으니, 거기는 큰길목인지라 풍성하고 다채로운 모험을 틀림없이 만나게 될 것이라고 돈키호테가 말했던 까닭이다."],["For all that, he was much grieved at the loss of his lance, and saying so to his squire, he added, “I remember having read how a Spanish knight, Diego Perez de Vargas by name, having broken his sword in battle, tore from an oak a ponderous bough or branch, and with it did such things that day, and pounded so many Moors, that he got the surname of Machuca, and he and his descendants from that day forth were called Vargas y Machuca.","그럼에도 그는 창을 잃은 것이 몹시 애석하여, 종자에게 그 말을 하고는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"내 어디선가 읽은 것을 기억하는데, 디에고 페레스 데 바르가스라는 이름의 한 스페인 기사가 전투에서 칼이 부러지자 떡갈나무에서 묵직한 가지 하나를 비틀어 꺾어, 그것으로 그날 어찌나 대단한 일들을 해내고 어찌나 많은 무어인을 두들겨 팼던지, 마추카라는 별명을 얻어 그와 그 후손들이 그날 이후로 바르가스 이 마추카라 불리게 되었다지."],["I mention this because from the first oak I see I mean to rend such another branch, large and stout like that, with which I am determined and resolved to do such deeds that thou mayest deem thyself very fortunate in being found worthy to come and see them, and be an eyewitness of things that will with difficulty be believed.”","내가 이 말을 하는 까닭은, 처음 눈에 띄는 떡갈나무에서 바로 그와 같은 가지를, 크고 튼튼한 가지를 하나 꺾을 작정이기 때문이니, 그것으로 어찌나 대단한 일들을 해낼 결심과 각오가 서 있는지, 너는 그것들을 보러 와서 그것을 볼 자격이 있다고 여겨진 것을 매우 운 좋게 여기고, 좀처럼 믿기 어려운 일들을 직접 목격한 증인이 되게 될 것이다.\""]],[["“Be that as God will,”","\"그야 하느님 뜻대로 되겠지요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “I believe it all as your worship says it; but straighten yourself a little, for you seem all on one side, may be from the shaking of the fall.”","\"나리께서 말씀하시는 대로 저는 다 믿습니다. 하지만 몸을 좀 바로 세우십시오, 한쪽으로 온통 기울어 보이시는데, 아마 떨어지면서 흔들린 탓인가 봅니다.\""]],[["“That is the truth,”","\"그것이 사실이다.\""],["said Don Quixote, “and if I make no complaint of the pain it is because knights-errant are not permitted to complain of any wound, even though their bowels be coming out through it.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그런데 내가 그 아픔을 두고 아무 불평도 하지 않는 것은, 편력 기사란 비록 창자가 상처로 비어져 나온다 해도 어떤 부상에 대해서도 불평하는 것이 허락되지 않기 때문이니라.\""]],[["“If so,”","\"그러시다면,\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “I have nothing to say; but God knows I would rather your worship complained when anything ailed you.","\"저는 더 드릴 말씀이 없습니다. 그래도 하느님은 아시겠지만, 저는 나리께서 무언가 편찮으실 때에는 차라리 불평을 하셨으면 합니다."],["For my part, I confess I must complain however small the ache may be; unless this rule about not complaining extends to the squires of knights-errant also.”","저로 말하자면, 고백하건대 아무리 작은 아픔이라도 불평하지 않고는 못 배기겠습니다. 다만 이 불평하지 않는 규칙이 편력 기사의 종자에게까지 미치는 것이 아니라면 말입니다.\""]],[["Don Quixote could not help laughing at his squire’s simplicity, and he assured him he might complain whenever and however he chose, just as he liked, for, so far, he had never read of anything to the contrary in the order of knighthood.","돈키호테는 종자의 순박함에 웃음을 참을 수 없어, 그가 원할 때면 언제든 어떤 식으로든 마음 내키는 대로 불평해도 좋다고, 이제껏 기사도의 규율에서 그와 어긋나는 어떤 것도 읽은 적이 없으니 그래도 좋다고 일러 주었다."]],[["Sancho bade him remember it was dinner-time, to which his master answered that he wanted nothing himself just then, but that he might eat when he had a mind.","산초가 지금이 식사 때라고 일깨우자, 주인은 자기는 지금 아무것도 먹고 싶지 않으나 너는 마음 내킬 때 먹어도 좋다고 대답했다."],["With this permission Sancho settled himself as comfortably as he could on his beast, and taking out of the alforjas what he had stowed away in them, he jogged along behind his master munching deliberately, and from time to time taking a pull at the bota with a relish that the thirstiest tapster in Malaga might have envied; and while he went on in this way, gulping down draught after draught, he never gave a thought to any of the promises his master had made him, nor did he rate it as hardship but rather as recreation going in quest of adventures, however dangerous they might be.","이 허락을 받은 산초는 제 나귀 위에 될 수 있는 한 편안히 자리를 잡고 앉아, 안장주머니에 챙겨 넣어 두었던 것을 꺼내 천천히 우물거리며 주인 뒤를 어슬렁어슬렁 따라갔으며, 이따금 술자루를 들이켤 때면 그 맛깔스러움이라니 말라가의 가장 목마른 술집 사환이라도 부러워했을 정도였다. 그렇게 한 모금 또 한 모금 들이켜며 가는 동안, 그는 주인이 자기에게 했던 약속 따위는 하나도 떠올리지 않았고, 아무리 위험할지언정 모험을 찾아 나서는 일을 고생이라기보다 오히려 소일거리로 여겼다."],["Finally they passed the night among some trees, from one of which Don Quixote plucked a dry branch to serve him after a fashion as a lance, and fixed on it the head he had removed from the broken one.","마침내 두 사람은 어느 나무들 사이에서 밤을 보냈는데, 돈키호테는 그중 한 나무에서 마른 가지 하나를 꺾어 그럭저럭 창 대신으로 삼고, 거기에 부러진 창에서 떼어 낸 창날을 박았다."],["All that night Don Quixote lay awake thinking of his lady Dulcinea, in order to conform to what he had read in his books, how many a night in the forests and deserts knights used to lie sleepless supported by the memory of their mistresses.","그날 밤 내내 돈키호테는 자기 여인 둘시네아를 생각하며 잠 못 이루고 누워 있었으니, 이는 기사들이 숲속과 황야에서 그 연인의 기억에 의지하여 잠 못 이루는 밤을 얼마나 많이 보냈던가를 자기 책들에서 읽은 것에 따르려는 것이었다."],["Not so did Sancho Panza spend it, for having his stomach full of something stronger than chicory water he made but one sleep of it, and, if his master had not called him, neither the rays of the sun beating on his face nor all the cheery notes of the birds welcoming the approach of day would have had power to waken him.","산초 판사는 그렇게 밤을 보내지 않았으니, 치커리 물보다 더 독한 무언가로 배를 채운 그는 단숨에 한잠을 푹 자 버려, 만일 주인이 부르지 않았던들 얼굴에 내리쬐는 햇살도, 날이 밝아 옴을 반기는 새들의 그 모든 흥겨운 지저귐도 그를 깨울 힘이 없었을 것이다."],["On getting up he tried the bota and found it somewhat less full than the night before, which grieved his heart because they did not seem to be on the way to remedy the deficiency readily.","일어나서 그가 술자루를 시험해 보니 간밤보다 다소 덜 찬 것을 알고는 마음이 아팠으니, 그 모자람을 곧바로 메울 길에 들어선 것 같지 않았기 때문이다."],["Don Quixote did not care to break his fast, for, as has been already said, he confined himself to savoury recollections for nourishment.","돈키호테는 아침 끼니를 들 생각이 없었으니, 앞서 말한 대로 그는 향긋한 추억만으로 영양을 삼는 데 만족했던 까닭이다."]],[["They returned to the road they had set out with, leading to Puerto Lapice, and at three in the afternoon they came in sight of it. “Here, brother Sancho Panza,” said Don Quixote when he saw it, “we may plunge our hands up to the elbows in what they call adventures; but observe, even shouldst thou see me in the greatest danger in the world, thou must not put a hand to thy sword in my defence, unless indeed thou perceivest that those who assail me are rabble or base folk; for in that case thou mayest very properly aid me; but if they be knights it is on no account permitted or allowed thee by the laws of knighthood to help me until thou hast been dubbed a knight.”","두 사람은 처음에 나섰던, 푸에르토 라피세로 이어지는 길로 되돌아왔고, 오후 세 시에 그곳이 눈에 들어왔다. \"여기서, 산초 판사 형제여,\" 그것을 보고 돈키호테가 말했다. \"우리는 이른바 모험이라는 것에 팔꿈치까지 손을 푹 담글 수 있을 것이다. 그러나 명심하거라, 설령 네가 나를 세상에서 가장 큰 위험에 빠진 것을 보더라도, 나를 지키려고 네 칼에 손을 대서는 아니 된다. 다만 나를 공격하는 자들이 천한 무리나 비천한 자들임을 알아챘을 경우에는 예외이니, 그런 경우라면 네가 나를 도와도 지극히 마땅하다. 그러나 만일 그들이 기사라면, 네가 기사로 서임받기 전에는 기사도의 법도가 네게 나를 돕는 것을 결코 허락하거나 용납하지 않느니라.\""]],[["“Most certainly, señor,” replied Sancho, “your worship shall be fully obeyed in this matter; all the more as of myself I am peaceful and no friend to mixing in strife and quarrels: it is true that as regards the defence of my own person I shall not give much heed to those laws, for laws human and divine allow each one to defend himself against any assailant whatever.”","\"지극히 당연하옵니다, 나리.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"이 일에서 나리께 온전히 따르겠습니다. 더구나 저로 말하면 본디 평화로운 사람이라 다툼이나 싸움에 끼어드는 것을 좋아하지 않으니 더욱 그렇습니다. 다만 제 한 몸을 지키는 일에 관해서라면 그 법도에 그리 마음을 두지 않겠으니, 인간의 법이든 하느님의 법이든 누구나 어떤 공격자에 맞서서든 제 몸을 지키는 것은 허락하기 때문입니다.\""]],[["“That I grant,”","\"그것은 내가 인정한다.\""],["said Don Quixote, “but in this matter of aiding me against knights thou must put a restraint upon thy natural impetuosity.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그러나 기사들에 맞서 나를 돕는 이 일에서는 네 타고난 성급함을 억눌러야 하느니라.\""]],[["“I will do so, I promise you,”","\"그리하겠습니다, 약속드립니다.\""],["answered Sancho, “and will keep this precept as carefully as Sunday.”","산초가 대답했다. \"이 계율을 주일만큼이나 정성껏 지키겠습니다.\""]],[["While they were thus talking there appeared on the road two friars of the order of St. Benedict, mounted on two dromedaries, for not less tall were the two mules they rode on.","이렇게 이야기를 나누고 있을 때 길 위에 성 베네딕트 수도회의 수사 둘이 나타났는데, 그들이 타고 있는 두 마리 노새가 어찌나 컸던지 단봉낙타라 해도 그보다 작지 않을 정도였다."],["They wore travelling spectacles and carried sunshades; and behind them came a coach attended by four or five persons on horseback and two muleteers on foot.","그들은 여행용 안경을 쓰고 양산을 들고 있었으며, 그 뒤로 말 탄 네댓 명과 걸어가는 노새몰이꾼 둘이 시중드는 마차 한 대가 따라왔다. 마차 안에는, 나중에 드러나기로, 세비야로 가는 한 비스카야 부인이 타고 있었으니, 그 남편이 거기서 높은 영예의 직책을 받아 서인도로 가는 배를 타려던 참이었다."],["In the coach there was, as afterwards appeared, a Biscay lady on her way to Seville, where her husband was about to take passage for the Indies with an appointment of high honour.","수사들은 같은 길을 가고 있기는 했으나 그 부인의 일행은 아니었다. 그러나 돈키호테는 그들을 알아보자마자 종자에게 말했다."],["The friars, though going the same road, were not in her company; but the moment Don Quixote perceived them he said to his squire, “Either I am mistaken, or this is going to be the most famous adventure that has ever been seen, for those black bodies we see there must be, and doubtless are, magicians who are carrying off some stolen princess in that coach, and with all my might I must undo this wrong.”","\"내가 잘못 본 것이 아니라면, 이것은 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 이름난 모험이 될 것이다. 저기 보이는 저 검은 형체들은 틀림없이, 의심할 바 없이, 저 마차에 어떤 납치한 공주를 싣고 가는 마법사들이니, 나는 온 힘을 다해 이 잘못을 바로잡아야 하느니라.\""]],[["“This will be worse than the windmills,”","\"이건 풍차보다 더 고약하겠습니다.\""],["said Sancho. “Look, señor; those are friars of St.","산초가 말했다."],["Benedict, and the coach plainly belongs to some travellers: I tell you to mind well what you are about and don’t let the devil mislead you.”","\"보십시오, 나리, 저들은 성 베네딕트 수도회의 수사들이고, 마차는 누가 봐도 어떤 여행자들의 것입니다. 부디 무슨 일을 하시려는지 잘 살피시고 악마에게 홀리지 마시라고 말씀드립니다.\""]],[["“I have told thee already, Sancho,”","\"내 이미 네게 말하지 않았느냐, 산초.\""],["replied Don Quixote, “that on the subject of adventures thou knowest little.","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"모험이라는 주제에 관해서는 네가 아는 것이 적다고 말이다."],["What I say is the truth, as thou shalt see presently.”","내가 하는 말이 진실임을 곧 보게 될 것이다.\""]],[["So saying, he advanced and posted himself in the middle of the road along which the friars were coming, and as soon as he thought they had come near enough to hear what he said, he cried aloud, “Devilish and unnatural beings, release instantly the highborn princesses whom you are carrying off by force in this coach, else prepare to meet a speedy death as the just punishment of your evil deeds.”","이렇게 말하고는 그가 앞으로 나아가 수사들이 오고 있는 길 한복판에 자리를 잡고 서서, 그들이 자기 말을 들을 만큼 충분히 가까이 왔다 싶자 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"악마 같고 본성을 거스르는 것들아, 너희가 이 마차에 강제로 싣고 가는 고귀한 신분의 공주들을 당장 풀어 주어라, 그러지 않으면 너희 악행에 대한 마땅한 벌로 당장 죽음을 맞을 각오를 하여라.\""]],[["The friars drew rein and stood wondering at the appearance of Don Quixote as well as at his words, to which they replied, “Señor Caballero, we are not devilish or unnatural, but two brothers of St.","수사들은 고삐를 당겨 세우고, 돈키호테의 행색에 그가 한 말까지 더해 어리둥절해하며 이렇게 대답했다."],["Benedict following our road, nor do we know whether or not there are any captive princesses coming in this coach.”","\"기사 나리, 우리는 악마도 아니고 본성을 거스르는 것들도 아니오라, 제 길을 가는 성 베네딕트 수도회의 수사 둘일 뿐이며, 이 마차에 사로잡힌 공주들이 타고 오는지 어떤지도 우리는 알지 못합니다.\""]],[["“No soft words with me, for I know you, lying rabble,”","\"내게 부드러운 말은 통하지 않는다, 나는 너희를 알기 때문이다, 거짓말하는 무리들아.\""],["said Don Quixote, and without waiting for a reply he spurred Rocinante and with levelled lance charged the first friar with such fury and determination, that, if the friar had not flung himself off the mule, he would have brought him to the ground against his will, and sore wounded, if not killed outright.","돈키호테는 이렇게 말하고는, 대답도 기다리지 않고 로시난테에 박차를 가해 창을 겨눈 채 첫 번째 수사를 향해 어찌나 사납고 단호하게 돌진했던지, 만일 그 수사가 노새에서 몸을 던지지 않았던들 그를 본의 아니게 땅에 떨어뜨려, 죽이지는 못했더라도 심하게 부상을 입혔을 것이다."],["The second brother, seeing how his comrade was treated, drove his heels into his castle of a mule and made off across the country faster than the wind.","두 번째 수사는 제 동료가 어떤 꼴을 당하는지 보고, 성채만 한 제 노새의 옆구리에 발꿈치를 박아 바람보다 빠르게 들판을 가로질러 달아났다."]],[["Sancho Panza, when he saw the friar on the ground, dismounting briskly from his ass, rushed towards him and began to strip off his gown.","산초 판사는 수사가 땅바닥에 쓰러진 것을 보자 제 나귀에서 잽싸게 내려 그에게 달려가서 그 수도복을 벗기기 시작했다. 바로 그때 수사들의 노새몰이꾼들이 다가와 어찌하여 그의 옷을 벗기느냐고 물었다."],["At that instant the friars’ muleteers came up and asked what he was stripping him for.","산초는 이것이 제 주인 돈키호테가 거둔 전투의 전리품으로서 자기에게 정당히 돌아오는 것이라고 그들에게 대답했다."],["Sancho answered them that this fell to him lawfully as spoil of the battle which his lord Don Quixote had won.","농담이라고는 알지도 못하고 이 전투니 전리품이니 하는 것을 도무지 이해하지 못한 노새몰이꾼들은, 돈키호테가 멀찍이 떨어져 마차의 여행자들과 이야기하고 있는 것을 보고, 산초에게 달려들어 그를 때려눕히고 수염이 한 올도 남지 않을 정도로 쥐어뜯고는 발길질로 흠씬 두들겨 패서, 숨도 못 쉬고 정신도 잃은 채 땅바닥에 뻗어 놓았다."],["The muleteers, who had no idea of a joke and did not understand all this about battles and spoils, seeing that Don Quixote was some distance off talking to the travellers in the coach, fell upon Sancho, knocked him down, and leaving hardly a hair in his beard, belaboured him with kicks and left him stretched breathless and senseless on the ground; and without any more delay helped the friar to mount, who, trembling, terrified, and pale, as soon as he found himself in the saddle, spurred after his companion, who was standing at a distance looking on, watching the result of the onslaught; then, not caring to wait for the end of the affair just begun, they pursued their journey making more crosses than if they had the devil after them.","그러고는 더 지체하지 않고 수사가 노새에 오르도록 거들었으니, 그 수사는 부들부들 떨며 겁에 질려 새파랗게 질린 채로 안장에 올라앉기 무섭게, 멀찍이 서서 이 공격의 결과를 지켜보며 구경하고 있던 동료를 뒤쫓아 박차를 가했다. 그러고 나서 그들은 막 시작된 이 일의 끝을 기다릴 마음도 없이, 마치 등 뒤에 악마가 쫓아오기라도 하는 듯 성호를 거듭 그으며 제 갈 길을 재촉했다."]],[["Don Quixote was, as has been said, speaking to the lady in the coach: “Your beauty, lady mine,” said he, “may now dispose of your person as may be most in accordance with your pleasure, for the pride of your ravishers lies prostrate on the ground through this strong arm of mine; and lest you should be pining to know the name of your deliverer, know that I am called Don Quixote of La Mancha, knight-errant and adventurer, and captive to the peerless and beautiful lady Dulcinea del Toboso: and in return for the service you have received of me I ask no more than that you should return to El Toboso, and on my behalf present yourself before that lady and tell her what I have done to set you free.”","돈키호테는, 앞서 말한 대로, 마차 안의 부인에게 말을 건네고 있었다. \"그대의 아름다움이여, 나의 부인이여,\" 그가 말했다. \"이제 그대의 뜻에 가장 합당한 대로 그대 자신을 처분하셔도 좋습니다, 그대를 납치한 자들의 오만이 이 내 굳센 팔로 인해 땅바닥에 엎어졌으니 말입니다. 그리고 그대를 구한 자의 이름을 애타게 알고 싶으실까 하여 알려 드리노니, 나는 라만차의 돈키호테라 불리며, 편력 기사이자 모험가요, 비할 데 없이 아름다운 여인 엘 토보소의 둘시네아에게 사로잡힌 몸입니다. 그리고 그대가 나로부터 받은 봉사의 보답으로 내가 바라는 것은 다만, 그대가 엘 토보소로 돌아가 나를 대신해 그 여인 앞에 나서서, 내가 그대를 자유롭게 하기 위해 한 일을 그분께 전해 주는 것뿐입니다.\""]],[["One of the squires in attendance upon the coach, a Biscayan, was listening to all Don Quixote was saying, and, perceiving that he would not allow the coach to go on, but was saying it must return at once to El Toboso, he made at him, and seizing his lance addressed him in bad Castilian and worse Biscayan after his fashion, “Begone, caballero, and ill go with thee; by the God that made me, unless thou quittest coach, slayest thee as art here a Biscayan.”","마차에 수행하던 종자 가운데 한 사람인 비스카야 사람이 돈키호테가 하는 말을 모두 듣고 있다가, 그가 마차를 더 가게 두지 않으려 하며 당장 엘 토보소로 돌아가야 한다고 말하는 것을 알아채고는, 그에게 달려들어 그의 창을 움켜쥐고 제 식대로 형편없는 카스티야 말과 그보다 더 형편없는 비스카야 말로 그에게 내뱉었다. \"물러가라, 기사 놈아, 너에게 화가 미칠지니. 나를 만드신 하느님께 맹세코, 마차를 놓지 않으면, 너를 죽인다 여기 비스카야 사람 있으니.\""]],[["Don Quixote understood him quite well, and answered him very quietly, “If thou wert a knight, as thou art none, I should have already chastised thy folly and rashness, miserable creature.” To which the Biscayan returned, “I no gentleman!--I swear to God thou liest as I am Christian: if thou droppest lance and drawest sword, soon shalt thou see thou art carrying water to the cat: Biscayan on land, hidalgo at sea, hidalgo at the devil, and look, if thou sayest otherwise thou liest.”","돈키호테는 그의 말을 아주 잘 알아듣고 매우 침착하게 대답했다. \"네가 기사라면, 비록 기사도 아니다만, 나는 이미 네 어리석음과 무모함을 응징했을 것이다, 가련한 것아.\" 이에 비스카야 사람이 되받았다. \"내가 신사 아니다!—하느님께 맹세코 내가 기독교인인 만큼 네가 거짓말한다. 네가 창 떨어뜨리고 칼 뽑으면, 곧 너 고양이한테 물 길어다 주는 줄 알게 된다. 비스카야 사람 땅에서, 이달고 바다에서, 이달고 악마한테서, 그리고 봐라, 네가 달리 말하면 거짓말한다.\""]],[["“‘“You will see presently,” said Agrajes,’”","\"'\"곧 보게 될 것이다, 라고 아그라헤스가 말했다.\"'\""],["replied Don Quixote; and throwing his lance on the ground he drew his sword, braced his buckler on his arm, and attacked the Biscayan, bent upon taking his life.","돈키호테가 대답했다. 그러고는 창을 땅에 내던지고 칼을 뽑아 방패를 팔에 단단히 죄고는, 그의 목숨을 거둘 작정으로 비스카야 사람에게 덤벼들었다."]],[["The Biscayan, when he saw him coming on, though he wished to dismount from his mule, in which, being one of those sorry ones let out for hire, he had no confidence, had no choice but to draw his sword; it was lucky for him, however, that he was near the coach, from which he was able to snatch a cushion that served him for a shield; and they went at one another as if they had been two mortal enemies.","비스카야 사람은 그가 덤벼드는 것을 보고, 비록 제 노새에서 내리고 싶었으나—삯으로 빌려 주는 그 시원찮은 것들 가운데 하나라 도무지 믿음이 가지 않았던 까닭이다—칼을 뽑는 수밖에 도리가 없었다. 그러나 그에게 다행이었던 것은 마차 가까이 있어서, 거기서 방패 삼을 방석 하나를 낚아챌 수 있었다는 점이다."],["The others strove to make peace between them, but could not, for the Biscayan declared in his disjointed phrase that if they did not let him finish his battle he would kill his mistress and everyone that strove to prevent him.","그리하여 두 사람은 마치 불구대천의 원수라도 된 듯 서로에게 덤벼들었다. 다른 이들이 둘 사이를 화해시키려 애썼으나 그럴 수 없었으니, 비스카야 사람이 그 토막 난 말투로, 자기가 이 싸움을 끝내게 두지 않으면 제 여주인이고 자기를 막으려는 모든 자고 다 죽여 버리겠다고 선언했기 때문이다."],["The lady in the coach, amazed and terrified at what she saw, ordered the coachman to draw aside a little, and set herself to watch this severe struggle, in the course of which the Biscayan smote Don Quixote a mighty stroke on the shoulder over the top of his buckler, which, given to one without armour, would have cleft him to the waist.","마차 안의 부인은 자기가 본 광경에 아연실색하고 겁에 질려, 마부에게 한쪽으로 좀 비켜서라 이르고는, 이 격렬한 싸움을 지켜보려 마음먹었는데, 그 와중에 비스카야 사람이 돈키호테의 방패 위쪽 어깨에 어찌나 세찬 일격을 내리쳤던지, 갑옷을 입지 않은 사람에게 가해졌더라면 허리까지 두 동강을 냈을 정도였다."],["Don Quixote, feeling the weight of this prodigious blow, cried aloud, saying, “O lady of my soul, Dulcinea, flower of beauty, come to the aid of this your knight, who, in fulfilling his obligations to your beauty, finds himself in this extreme peril.”","돈키호테는 이 엄청난 타격의 무게를 느끼고 큰 소리로 외쳐 말했다. \"오 내 영혼의 여인이여, 둘시네아여, 아름다움의 꽃이여, 그대의 아름다움에 진 의무를 다하느라 이 극한의 위험에 처한 이 그대의 기사를 도우러 오소서.\""],["To say this, to lift his sword, to shelter himself well behind his buckler, and to assail the Biscayan was the work of an instant, determined as he was to venture all upon a single blow.","이 말을 하고, 칼을 들어 올리고, 방패 뒤로 몸을 잘 숨기고, 비스카야 사람에게 덤벼드는 것이 순식간의 일이었으니, 그는 단 한 번의 일격에 모든 것을 걸 작정이었던 것이다."],["The Biscayan, seeing him come on in this way, was convinced of his courage by his spirited bearing, and resolved to follow his example, so he waited for him keeping well under cover of his cushion, being unable to execute any sort of manoeuvre with his mule, which, dead tired and never meant for this kind of game, could not stir a step.","비스카야 사람은 그가 이렇게 덤벼드는 것을 보고 그 기세등등한 태도로 그의 용기를 확신하여 그 본을 따르기로 마음먹고는, 방석으로 몸을 잘 가린 채 그를 기다렸으니, 제 노새로는 어떤 작전도 펼칠 수 없었던 까닭이다. 그 노새는 죽도록 지친 데다 본래 이런 놀음에는 맞지 않는 짐승이라 한 발짝도 움직일 수 없었다."]],[["On, then, as aforesaid, came Don Quixote against the wary Biscayan, with uplifted sword and a firm intention of splitting him in half, while on his side the Biscayan waited for him sword in hand, and under the protection of his cushion; and all present stood trembling, waiting in suspense the result of blows such as threatened to fall, and the lady in the coach and the rest of her following were making a thousand vows and offerings to all the images and shrines of Spain, that God might deliver her squire and all of them from this great peril in which they found themselves.","그리하여, 앞서 말한 대로, 돈키호테는 칼을 치켜들고 상대를 두 동강 낼 굳은 작정으로 신중한 비스카야 사람을 향해 다가갔고, 비스카야 사람 쪽에서도 칼을 손에 든 채 방석의 보호를 받으며 그를 기다렸으며, 그 자리에 있던 모든 이들은 부들부들 떨면서 곧 떨어질 듯한 그 일격의 결과를 마음 졸이며 기다렸고, 마차 안의 부인과 나머지 수행원들은 하느님께서 자기네 종자와 그들 모두를 이 처한 큰 위험에서 건져 주시기를 빌며 스페인의 모든 성상과 성소에 천 가지 서원과 봉헌을 올리고 있었다."],["But it spoils all, that at this point and crisis the author of the history leaves this battle impending, giving as excuse that he could find nothing more written about these achievements of Don Quixote than what has been already set forth.","그러나 모든 것을 그르치는 것은, 바로 이 대목, 이 절박한 고비에서 이 이야기의 작가가 이 전투를 일촉즉발의 상태로 남겨 둔다는 점인데, 그 변명으로 내세우는 것이 돈키호테의 이 위업들에 관하여 앞서 이미 적어 놓은 것 말고는 더 적힌 것을 찾을 수 없었다는 것이다."],["It is true the second author of this work was unwilling to believe that a history so curious could have been allowed to fall under the sentence of oblivion, or that the wits of La Mancha could have been so undiscerning as not to preserve in their archives or registries some documents referring to this famous knight; and this being his persuasion, he did not despair of finding the conclusion of this pleasant history, which, heaven favouring him, he did find in a way that shall be related in the Second Part.","사실 이 작품의 두 번째 작가는 이토록 흥미로운 이야기가 망각의 선고 아래 떨어지도록 내버려졌으리라고는, 혹은 라만차의 식자들이 이 이름난 기사에 관한 어떤 문서를 그들의 문서고나 기록부에 보존해 두지 못할 만큼 그토록 분별없었으리라고는 믿고 싶지 않았다. 그리고 이런 확신이 있었기에, 그는 이 유쾌한 이야기의 결말을 찾는 일을 포기하지 않았으니, 하늘의 도움으로 그는 마침내 그것을 찾아내었고, 그 경위는 제2부에서 이야기될 것이다."]],[["# CHAPTER IX. IN WHICH IS CONCLUDED AND FINISHED THE TERRIFIC BATTLE BETWEEN THE GALLANT BISCAYAN AND THE VALIANT MANCHEGAN","# CHAPTER IX."]],[["In the First Part of this history we left the valiant Biscayan and the renowned Don Quixote with drawn swords uplifted, ready to deliver two such furious slashing blows that if they had fallen full and fair they would at least have split and cleft them asunder from top to toe and laid them open like a pomegranate; and at this so critical point the delightful history came to a stop and stood cut short without any intimation from the author where what was missing was to be found.","이 이야기의 제1부에서 우리는 용맹한 비스카야인과 그 이름 높은 돈키호테가 칼을 뽑아 높이 치켜든 채, 만일 그 일격이 빈틈없이 제대로 떨어졌더라면 적어도 두 사람을 머리끝에서 발끝까지 쪼개고 갈라, 석류처럼 활짝 열어젖혔을 만큼 사납게 내리치려 하는 장면에서 헤어졌다. 그런데 바로 이토록 아슬아슬한 대목에서 이 유쾌한 이야기는 뚝 멎어 버렸고, 빠진 부분을 어디서 찾아야 할지 작가의 아무런 귀띔도 없이 끊긴 채로 머물러 있었다."]],[["This distressed me greatly, because the pleasure derived from having read such a small portion turned to vexation at the thought of the poor chance that presented itself of finding the large part that, so it seemed to me, was missing of such an interesting tale.","이 일은 나를 몹시 괴롭게 했으니, 그토록 적은 분량을 읽고 얻은 즐거움이, 이토록 흥미로운 이야기에서 빠져 있는 듯한 그 많은 부분을 찾아낼 가망이 가뭇없다는 생각에 이르자 그만 짜증으로 바뀌어 버렸기 때문이다. 이렇게 훌륭한 기사가 그 경이로운 위업을 기록하는 일을 떠맡을 어떤 현자도 두지 못했다는 것은, 내게는 있을 수 없는 일이요 모든 선례에 어긋나는 일로 여겨졌다."],["It appeared to me to be a thing impossible and contrary to all precedent that so good a knight should have been without some sage to undertake the task of writing his marvellous achievements; a thing that was never wanting to any of those knights-errant who, they say, went after adventures; for every one of them had one or two sages as if made on purpose, who not only recorded their deeds but described their most trifling thoughts and follies, however secret they might be; and such a good knight could not have been so unfortunate as not to have what Platir and others like him had in abundance.","그런 현자란 모험을 찾아 나섰다고들 하는 그 어느 편력 기사에게도 결코 없었던 적이 없었으니, 그들 하나하나에게는 마치 일부러 마련된 듯 한둘의 현자가 있어, 기사의 행적을 기록했을 뿐 아니라 제아무리 은밀한 것이라도 가장 사소한 생각과 어리석은 짓까지 낱낱이 묘사했던 것이다. 그러니 이토록 훌륭한 기사가 플라티르나 그와 비슷한 자들이 넘치도록 누렸던 것을 갖지 못할 만큼 불운했을 리는 없었다."],["And so I could not bring myself to believe that such a gallant tale had been left maimed and mutilated, and I laid the blame on Time, the devourer and destroyer of all things, that had either concealed or consumed it.","그리하여 나는 이토록 멋들어진 이야기가 동강 나고 토막 난 채로 남겨졌으리라고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었고, 만물을 집어삼키고 부수는 자, 곧 그것을 감추었거나 먹어 치웠을 시간에게 그 허물을 돌렸다. * 플라티르: 16세기 기사도 소설의 주인공."]],[["On the other hand, it struck me that, inasmuch as among his books there had been found such modern ones as “The Enlightenment of Jealousy”","다른 한편으로, 그의 책들 가운데 「질투의 깨달음」이라든가 「에나레스의 님프와 목동」"],["and the “Nymphs and Shepherds of Henares,” his story must likewise be modern, and that though it might not be written, it might exist in the memory of the people of his village and of those in the neighbourhood.","같은 근래의 책들이 있었던 것을 보면, 그의 이야기 또한 근래의 것일 터이며, 글로 쓰이지는 않았더라도 그의 마을 사람들과 인근 사람들의 기억 속에 남아 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 떠올랐다."],["This reflection kept me perplexed and longing to know really and truly the whole life and wondrous deeds of our famous Spaniard, Don Quixote of La Mancha, light and mirror of Manchegan chivalry, and the first that in our age and in these so evil days devoted himself to the labour and exercise of the arms of knight-errantry, righting wrongs, succouring widows, and protecting damsels of that sort that used to ride about, whip in hand, on their palfreys, with all their virginity about them, from mountain to mountain and valley to valley--for, if it were not for some ruffian, or boor with a hood and hatchet, or monstrous giant, that forced them, there were in days of yore damsels that at the end of eighty years, in all which time they had never slept a day under a roof, went to their graves as much maids as the mothers that bore them.","이 생각은 나를 계속 곤혹스럽게 했고, 우리의 이름난 스페인 사람, 라만차의 돈키호테, 곧 라만차 기사도의 빛이요 거울이며, 우리 시대, 이토록 사악한 나날에 처음으로 편력 기사도의 무예를 갈고닦는 노고에 몸을 바쳐, 그릇된 것을 바로잡고 과부를 돕고, 손에 채찍을 쥐고 순결을 고스란히 지닌 채 자기 말을 타고 이 산에서 저 산으로, 이 골짜기에서 저 골짜기로 떠돌아다니던 그런 부류의 처녀들을 보호한 그의 온전한 생애와 경이로운 행적을 참되고 진실하게 알고 싶은 마음을 갈망케 했다. 옛날에는 어떤 불한당이나, 두건을 쓰고 도끼를 든 촌놈이나, 흉측한 거인이 그녀들을 억지로 범하지 않는 한, 여든 살이 되도록 단 하루도 지붕 아래서 자 본 적 없이 떠돌다가 자기를 낳아 준 어머니만큼이나 순결한 처녀로 무덤에 들어간 처녀들이 있었으니 말이다."],["I say, then, that in these and other respects our gallant Don Quixote is worthy of everlasting and notable praise, nor should it be withheld even from me for the labour and pains spent in searching for the conclusion of this delightful history; though I know well that if Heaven, chance and good fortune had not helped me, the world would have remained deprived of an entertainment and pleasure that for a couple of hours or so may well occupy him who shall read it attentively.","그러므로 나는 말하노니, 이런저런 점에서 우리의 용맹한 돈키호테는 영원하고도 두드러진 칭송을 받아 마땅하며, 이 유쾌한 이야기의 결말을 찾느라 들인 수고와 노고에 대해서는 나에게조차 그 칭송을 거두어서는 안 될 것이다. 비록 하늘과 우연과 행운이 나를 돕지 않았던들, 그것을 주의 깊게 읽는 이라면 두어 시간쯤은 너끈히 차지할 만한 이 오락과 즐거움을 세상이 빼앗긴 채로 남았으리라는 것을 나는 잘 알고 있긴 하지만 말이다."],["The discovery of it occurred in this way.","그것을 찾아낸 경위는 이러했다."]],[["One day, as I was in the Alcana of Toledo, a boy came up to sell some pamphlets and old papers to a silk mercer, and, as I am fond of reading even the very scraps of paper in the streets, led by this natural bent of mine I took up one of the pamphlets the boy had for sale, and saw that it was in characters which I recognised as Arabic, and as I was unable to read them though I could recognise them, I looked about to see if there were any Spanish-speaking Morisco at hand to read them for me; nor was there any great difficulty in finding such an interpreter, for even had I sought one for an older and better language I should have found him. In short, chance provided me with one, who when I told him what I wanted and put the book into his hands, opened it in the middle and after reading a little in it began to laugh. I asked him what he was laughing at, and he replied that it was at something the book had written in the margin by way of a note. I bade him tell it to me; and he still laughing said, “In the margin, as I told you, this is written: ‘This Dulcinea del Toboso so often mentioned in this history, had, they say, the best hand of any woman in all La Mancha for salting pigs.’”","어느 날 톨레도의 알카나에 있을 때, 한 소년이 비단 상인에게 소책자 몇 권과 헌 종이를 팔러 다가왔는데, 나는 길거리에 떨어진 종잇조각까지도 읽기를 즐기는 사람인지라, 이 타고난 성향에 이끌려 소년이 팔려고 내놓은 소책자 가운데 하나를 집어 들었다. 그러자 그것이 내가 아랍 문자임을 알아본 글자로 되어 있는 것이 보였는데, 알아보기는 해도 읽을 수는 없었으므로, 그것을 읽어 줄 만한 스페인어를 하는 모리스코가 곁에 있는지 둘러보았다. 그런 통역자를 찾는 데에는 별 어려움이 없었으니, 설령 더 오래되고 더 훌륭한 언어를 다룰 사람을 구했다 해도 찾아냈을 것이다. 요컨대 우연이 내게 한 사람을 마련해 주었으니, 내가 원하는 바를 일러 주고 그 책을 그의 손에 쥐여 주자, 그는 책을 가운데쯤 펼쳐 잠시 읽더니 웃기 시작했다. 무엇 때문에 웃느냐고 물었더니, 책의 여백에 주석으로 적힌 어떤 것 때문이라고 대답했다. 그것을 내게 말해 달라고 했더니, 그는 여전히 웃으면서 말했다. \"여백에, 말씀드린 대로, 이렇게 적혀 있습니다. '이 이야기에 그토록 자주 언급되는 이 엘 토보소의 둘시네아는, 사람들 말로는, 돼지를 소금에 절이는 데에 온 라만차의 어떤 여인보다도 솜씨가 좋았다고 한다.'\" * 모리스코: 기독교로 개종한 무어인, 혹은 그 후손."]],[["When I heard Dulcinea del Toboso named, I was struck with surprise and amazement, for it occurred to me at once that these pamphlets contained the history of Don Quixote.","엘 토보소의 둘시네아라는 이름을 들었을 때, 나는 놀라움과 경탄에 사로잡혔으니, 이 소책자들이 돈키호테의 이야기를 담고 있다는 생각이 단번에 떠올랐기 때문이다."],["With this idea I pressed him to read the beginning, and doing so, turning the Arabic offhand into Castilian, he told me it meant, “History of Don Quixote of La Mancha, written by Cide Hamete Benengeli, an Arab historian.”","이런 생각으로 나는 그에게 첫머리를 읽어 달라고 다그쳤고, 그는 아랍어를 즉석에서 카스티야어로 옮기며 이렇게 읽어 주었으니, 그 뜻은 \"아랍 역사가 시데 아메테 베넹헬리가 쓴, 라만차의 돈키호테 이야기\"라는 것이었다."],["It required great caution to hide the joy I felt when the title of the book reached my ears, and snatching it from the silk mercer, I bought all the papers and pamphlets from the boy for half a real; and if he had had his wits about him and had known how eager I was for them, he might have safely calculated on making more than six reals by the bargain.","책의 제목이 내 귀에 닿았을 때 느낀 기쁨을 감추는 데에는 적잖은 조심성이 필요했으니, 나는 그것을 비단 상인에게서 낚아채어 반 레알에 그 소년에게서 종이와 소책자를 죄다 사들였다. 만일 그가 약삭빨라서 내가 그것들을 얼마나 간절히 원하는지 알아챘더라면, 그 거래로 능히 여섯 레알이 넘는 이문을 셈할 수 있었으리라."],["I withdrew at once with the Morisco into the cloister of the cathedral, and begged him to turn all these pamphlets that related to Don Quixote into the Castilian tongue, without omitting or adding anything to them, offering him whatever payment he pleased.","나는 곧장 그 모리스코와 함께 대성당 회랑으로 물러나, 돈키호테에 관한 이 소책자들을 무엇 하나 빼거나 더하지 말고 카스티야어로 옮겨 달라고 청하며, 그가 원하는 대로 삯을 치르겠노라 했다."],["He was satisfied with two arrobas of raisins and two bushels of wheat, and promised to translate them faithfully and with all despatch; but to make the matter easier, and not to let such a precious find out of my hands, I took him to my house, where in little more than a month and a half he translated the whole just as it is set down here.","그는 건포도 두 아로바와 밀 두 부셸로 만족하고는, 충실하게 그리고 온 힘을 다해 신속히 옮겨 주겠노라 약속했다. 그러나 일을 한결 수월케 하고, 이토록 값진 발견물을 내 손에서 놓치지 않으려고, 나는 그를 내 집으로 데려갔으니, 거기서 그는 한 달 반 남짓 만에 여기 적힌 그대로 전부를 옮겨 냈다."]],[["In the first pamphlet the battle between Don Quixote and the Biscayan was drawn to the very life, they planted in the same attitude as the history describes, their swords raised, and the one protected by his buckler, the other by his cushion, and the Biscayan’s mule so true to nature that it could be seen to be a hired one a bowshot off.","* 레알, 아로바, 부셸: 당시 스페인의 화폐 및 도량형 단위. 첫 번째 소책자에는 돈키호테와 비스카야인의 싸움이 그야말로 살아 있는 듯이 그려져 있었으니, 두 사람은 이야기가 묘사한 그대로의 자세로, 칼을 치켜든 채, 하나는 작은 방패로, 다른 하나는 방석으로 몸을 가리고 있었으며, 비스카야인의 노새는 어찌나 실물에 충실한지 화살 한 바탕 거리에서도 세낸 놈임을 알아볼 수 있을 정도였다."],["The Biscayan had an inscription under his feet which said, “Don Sancho de Azpeitia,” which no doubt must have been his name; and at the feet of Rocinante was another that said, “Don Quixote.”","비스카야인의 발밑에는 \"돈 산초 데 아스페이티아\"라는 글귀가 적혀 있었는데, 의심할 바 없이 그것이 그의 이름이었을 것이며, 로시난테의 발치에는 \"돈키호테\"라고 적힌 또 다른 글귀가 있었다."],["Rocinante was marvellously portrayed, so long and thin, so lank and lean, with so much backbone and so far gone in consumption, that he showed plainly with what judgment and propriety the name of Rocinante had been bestowed upon him.","로시난테는 어찌나 길고 야위었으며, 어찌나 비쩍 마르고 가냘프며, 등뼈가 어찌나 도드라지고 폐병이 어찌나 깊이 든 모습으로 절묘하게 그려졌는지, 로시난테라는 이름이 어떤 판단과 합당함으로 그에게 붙여졌는지를 또렷이 보여 주었다."],["Near him was Sancho Panza holding the halter of his ass, at whose feet was another label that said, “Sancho Zancas,” and according to the picture, he must have had a big belly, a short body, and long shanks, for which reason, no doubt, the names of Panza and Zancas were given him, for by these two surnames the history several times calls him.","그 곁에는 산초 판사가 자기 당나귀의 고삐를 쥐고 있었으며, 그 발치에는 \"산초 산카스\"라고 적힌 또 하나의 표찰이 있었으니, 그림으로 보건대 그는 배가 불룩하고 몸통이 짧으며 정강이가 길었던 것이 틀림없는데, 의심할 바 없이 그래서 판사니 산카스니 하는 이름이 그에게 붙여졌을 것이다."],["Some other trifling particulars might be mentioned, but they are all of slight importance and have nothing to do with the true relation of the history; and no history can be bad so long as it is true.","이야기가 그를 여러 차례 이 두 별명으로 부르고 있기 때문이다. 다른 자질구레한 세부도 몇 가지 들 수 있겠으나, 그것들은 죄다 별 중요치 않은 것들이요 이야기의 참된 서술과는 아무 상관이 없으니, 어떤 이야기든 참되기만 하다면 나쁠 수 없는 법이다."]],[["If against the present one any objection be raised on the score of its truth, it can only be that its author was an Arab, as lying is a very common propensity with those of that nation; though, as they are such enemies of ours, it is conceivable that there were omissions rather than additions made in the course of it. And this is my own opinion; for, where he could and should give freedom to his pen in praise of so worthy a knight, he seems to me deliberately to pass it over in silence; which is ill done and worse contrived, for it is the business and duty of historians to be exact, truthful, and wholly free from passion, and neither interest nor fear, hatred nor love, should make them swerve from the path of truth, whose mother is history, rival of time, storehouse of deeds, witness for the past, example and counsel for the present, and warning for the future. In this I know will be found all that can be desired in the pleasantest, and if it be wanting in any good quality, I maintain it is the fault of its hound of an author and not the fault of the subject. To be brief, its Second Part, according to the translation, began in this way:","* 판사, 산카스: 각각 스페인어로 '배', '긴 정강이'를 뜻한다. 혹여 이 이야기에 대해 그 진실성을 두고 어떤 이의가 제기된다면, 그것은 다만 그 작가가 아랍인이었다는 것뿐일 터이니, 거짓말이란 그 민족 사람들에게 매우 흔한 성벽이기 때문이다. 다만 그들이 우리의 그토록 큰 적인지라, 이야기를 옮기는 과정에서 무엇을 더하기보다는 오히려 빼먹은 것이 있으리라 짐작할 만하다. 그리고 이것이 나 자신의 의견이다. 그가 이토록 훌륭한 기사를 기리는 데에 마땅히 붓을 자유로이 놀릴 수 있고 또 그래야 할 대목에서, 내게는 그가 일부러 그것을 잠자코 지나쳐 버리는 것처럼 보이니, 이는 잘못 행한 일이요 더 잘못 꾸민 일이라 하겠다. 정확하고 진실하며 온갖 격정에서 온전히 벗어나는 것이야말로 역사가의 본분이요 의무이며, 이해관계도 두려움도, 미움도 사랑도 그들로 하여금 진실의 길에서 벗어나게 해서는 안 되기 때문이다. 진실의 어머니는 역사이니, 역사는 시간의 맞수요, 행적의 곳간이며, 과거의 증인이요, 현재의 본보기이자 조언이며, 미래에 대한 경고이다. 이 이야기 안에는 가장 즐거운 이야기에서 바랄 수 있는 모든 것이 담겨 있음을 나는 알거니와, 만일 어떤 훌륭한 자질이 모자란다면, 그것은 그 개 같은 작가의 허물이지 소재의 허물이 아니라고 나는 단언하는 바이다. 간추려 말하면, 번역에 따르건대 이 이야기의 제2부는 이렇게 시작되었다."]],[["With trenchant swords upraised and poised on high, it seemed as though the two valiant and wrathful combatants stood threatening heaven, and earth, and hell, with such resolution and determination did they bear themselves.","날카로운 칼을 치켜들어 허공에 겨눈 채, 두 용맹하고 노기 띤 싸움꾼은 하늘과 땅과 지옥을 위협하며 서 있는 듯했으니, 그토록 굳은 결의와 단호함으로 몸가짐을 가다듬고 있었던 것이다."],["The fiery Biscayan was the first to strike a blow, which was delivered with such force and fury that had not the sword turned in its course, that single stroke would have sufficed to put an end to the bitter struggle and to all the adventures of our knight; but that good fortune which reserved him for greater things, turned aside the sword of his adversary, so that although it smote him upon the left shoulder, it did him no more harm than to strip all that side of its armour, carrying away a great part of his helmet with half of his ear, all which with fearful ruin fell to the ground, leaving him in a sorry plight.","불같은 비스카야인이 먼저 일격을 날렸는데, 어찌나 힘차고 사납게 떨어졌던지, 만일 칼이 도중에 빗나가지 않았던들 그 단 한 번의 내리침으로 이 모진 싸움도, 우리 기사의 온갖 모험도 끝장을 보기에 넉넉했을 것이다. 그러나 그를 더 큰 일들을 위해 남겨 두었던 그 행운이 적의 칼을 비껴가게 하니, 칼이 그의 왼쪽 어깨를 후려치기는 했어도, 그쪽 옆구리의 갑옷을 죄다 벗겨 내고 투구의 상당 부분을 한쪽 귀의 절반과 함께 앗아 간 것 말고는 더 큰 해를 입히지 못했고, 이 모든 것이 무시무시한 잔해가 되어 땅바닥에 떨어져 그를 가련한 몰골로 만들었을 뿐이다."]],[["Good God!","오, 하느님! 우리의 라만차 사람이 자신이 이런 꼴을 당하는 것을 보았을 때 그의 가슴을 가득 채운 분노를 제대로 그려 낼 자 그 누구이겠는가?"],["Who is there that could properly describe the rage that filled the heart of our Manchegan when he saw himself dealt with in this fashion?","말할 수 있는 것이라고는 다만 이러했다는 것뿐이니, 그것은 그가 다시 등자를 딛고 몸을 일으켜, 두 손으로 칼을 더욱 단단히 거머쥐고는, 어찌나 사납게 비스카야인에게 덮쳐들어 그 방석과 머리통을 정통으로 후려쳤던지 — 그토록 좋은 방패조차 무용지물임이 드러나면서 — 마치 산이 하나 그 위로 무너져 내린 것처럼, 비스카야인은 코와 입과 귀에서 피를 흘리기 시작하며, 금방이라도 노새에서 뒤로 나동그라질 듯 비틀거렸다. 만일 그가 노새의 목을 두 팔로 부둥켜안지 않았던들 의심할 바 없이 그리되었을 것이다."],["All that can be said is, it was such that he again raised himself in his stirrups, and, grasping his sword more firmly with both hands, he came down on the Biscayan with such fury, smiting him full over the cushion and over the head, that--even so good a shield proving useless--as if a mountain had fallen on him, he began to bleed from nose, mouth, and ears, reeling as if about to fall backwards from his mule, as no doubt he would have done had he not flung his arms about its neck; at the same time, however, he slipped his feet out of the stirrups and then unclasped his arms, and the mule, taking fright at the terrible blow, made off across the plain, and with a few plunges flung its master to the ground.","그러나 동시에 그는 두 발을 등자에서 빼고 두 팔을 풀었고, 그 무시무시한 일격에 질겁한 노새는 들판을 가로질러 내달아 몇 번 펄쩍 뛰어오르더니 주인을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤다. 돈키호테는 매우 침착하게 지켜보고 서 있다가, 그가 떨어지는 것을 보자 말에서 뛰어내려 잽싸게 그에게 달려가서는, 칼끝을 그의 두 눈에 겨누며 항복하지 않으면 목을 베어 버리겠노라 명했다."],["Don Quixote stood looking on very calmly, and, when he saw him fall, leaped from his horse and with great briskness ran to him, and, presenting the point of his sword to his eyes, bade him surrender, or he would cut his head off.","비스카야인은 어찌나 얼이 빠졌던지 한마디도 대답할 수가 없었고, 돈키호테가 그토록 눈이 멀어 있던 터라 그에게는 험한 꼴이 닥쳤을 것이었으나, 그때까지 큰 공포 속에 싸움을 지켜보던 마차 안의 귀부인들이 그가 서 있는 곳으로 황급히 다가와, 자기들 종자의 목숨을 살려 주는 크나큰 은혜와 호의를 베풀어 달라고 간곡한 청으로 애원했다. 이에 돈키호테는 자못 엄숙하고 위엄 있게 대답했다."],["The Biscayan was so bewildered that he was unable to answer a word, and it would have gone hard with him, so blind was Don Quixote, had not the ladies in the coach, who had hitherto been watching the combat in great terror, hastened to where he stood and implored him with earnest entreaties to grant them the great grace and favour of sparing their squire’s life; to which Don Quixote replied with much gravity and dignity, “In truth, fair ladies, I am well content to do what ye ask of me; but it must be on one condition and understanding, which is that this knight promise me to go to the village of El Toboso, and on my behalf present himself before the peerless lady Dulcinea, that she deal with him as shall be most pleasing to her.”","\"참으로, 아름다운 귀부인들이여, 나는 그대들이 내게 청하는 바를 기꺼이 들어 드리겠소. 다만 한 가지 조건과 다짐이 있어야 하니, 그것은 이 기사가 엘 토보소 마을로 가서, 나를 대신하여 둘도 없는 둘시네아 아가씨 앞에 나아가, 그분이 가장 뜻에 맞으시는 대로 그를 처분하시도록 하겠노라 내게 약속하는 것이오.\""]],[["The terrified and disconsolate ladies, without discussing Don Quixote’s demand or asking who Dulcinea might be, promised that their squire should do all that had been commanded.","겁에 질리고 낙담한 귀부인들은, 돈키호테의 요구를 따져 보거나 둘시네아가 누구인지 묻지도 않은 채, 자기들 종자가 분부받은 모든 것을 행하겠노라 약속했다."]],[["“Then, on the faith of that promise,”","\"그렇다면, 그 약속을 믿고,\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “I shall do him no further harm, though he well deserves it of me.”","\"비록 그가 내게 마땅히 받아야 할 대접이긴 하나, 나는 그에게 더는 해를 끼치지 않겠소.\""]],[["# CHAPTER X. OF THE PLEASANT DISCOURSE THAT PASSED BETWEEN DON QUIXOTE AND HIS SQUIRE SANCHO PANZA","# CHAPTER X."]],[["Now by this time Sancho had risen, rather the worse for the handling of the friars’ muleteers, and stood watching the battle of his master, Don Quixote, and praying to God in his heart that it might be his will to grant him the victory, and that he might thereby win some island to make him governor of, as he had promised.","그 무렵 산초는 수도사들을 모시던 노새몰이꾼들에게 험한 꼴을 당한 탓에 다소 성치 않은 몸을 일으켜 세우고는, 주인 돈키호테의 싸움을 지켜보며 마음속으로 하느님께 그분이 승리를 거두시기를, 그리하여 약속하신 대로 자신을 총독으로 앉혀 줄 섬 하나를 얻으시기를 빌고 있었다. 그리하여 싸움이 이제 끝나고 주인이 로시난테에 다시 오르려고 돌아서는 것을 보자, 그는 등자를 잡아 드리려 다가갔다가, 주인이 미처 오르기도 전에 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇고는 그 손을 잡아 입을 맞추며 말했다."],["Seeing, therefore, that the struggle was now over, and that his master was returning to mount Rocinante, he approached to hold the stirrup for him, and, before he could mount, he went on his knees before him, and taking his hand, kissed it saying, “May it please your worship, Señor Don Quixote, to give me the government of that island which has been won in this hard fight, for be it ever so big I feel myself in sufficient force to be able to govern it as much and as well as anyone in the world who has ever governed islands.”","\"돈키호테 나리, 이 고된 싸움에서 얻으신 그 섬의 통치를 제게 내려 주십사 청하옵니다. 아무리 크다 한들 저는 세상에서 일찍이 섬을 다스려 본 그 누구 못지않게, 그만큼 훌륭히 다스릴 만한 힘이 제게 있다고 느끼고 있으니까요.\""]],[["To which Don Quixote replied, “Thou must take notice, brother Sancho, that this adventure and those like it are not adventures of islands, but of cross-roads, in which nothing is got except a broken head or an ear the less: have patience, for adventures will present themselves from which I may make you, not only a governor, but something more.”","이에 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"산초 형제여, 잘 새겨 두게나. 이번 모험이나 이와 비슷한 것들은 섬을 얻는 모험이 아니라 갈림길의 모험이니, 거기서 얻을 것이라곤 머리가 깨지거나 귀 한쪽을 잃는 것밖에 없다네. 참고 기다리게. 자네를 총독뿐 아니라 그 이상으로도 만들어 줄 모험들이 절로 나타날 것이니.\""]],[["Sancho gave him many thanks, and again kissing his hand and the skirt of his hauberk, helped him to mount Rocinante, and mounting his ass himself, proceeded to follow his master, who at a brisk pace, without taking leave, or saying anything further to the ladies belonging to the coach, turned into a wood that was hard by. Sancho followed him at his ass’s best trot, but Rocinante stepped out so that, seeing himself left behind, he was forced to call to his master to wait for him. Don Quixote did so, reining in Rocinante until his weary squire came up, who on reaching him said, “It seems to me, señor, it would be prudent in us to go and take refuge in some church, for, seeing how mauled he with whom you fought has been left, it will be no wonder if they give information of the affair to the Holy Brotherhood and arrest us, and, faith, if they do, before we come out of gaol we shall have to sweat for it.”","산초는 그에게 거듭 감사를 드리고는, 다시 그의 손과 갑옷 자락에 입을 맞춘 뒤 로시난테에 오르도록 거들었으며, 자신도 나귀에 올라타 주인의 뒤를 따랐다. 주인은 마차에 딸린 귀부인들에게 작별 인사도, 더는 아무 말도 건네지 않고 빠른 걸음으로 가까이 있는 숲속으로 접어들었다. 산초는 제 나귀가 낼 수 있는 가장 빠른 걸음으로 뒤를 따랐으나, 로시난테가 어찌나 성큼성큼 내딛는지 자신이 뒤처지는 것을 보고는 어쩔 수 없이 주인에게 기다려 달라고 외쳐야 했다. 돈키호테는 그 말대로 로시난테의 고삐를 당겨, 지친 종자가 따라붙을 때까지 멈춰 섰다. 종자는 그에게 다가와 이렇게 말했다. \"나리, 제 생각에는 우리가 어느 성당으로 피신하는 것이 현명한 일일 듯합니다. 나리께서 싸우신 그자가 어찌나 만신창이가 되었는지를 보면, 그자들이 이 일을 성스러운 형제단에 알려 우리를 붙잡아 가더라도 조금도 이상할 게 없을 테니까요. 그리고 정말이지 그렇게 되면, 감옥에서 나오기까지 우리는 단단히 진땀을 빼야 할 겁니다.\" * 성스러운 형제단(Santa Hermandad): 15세기 스페인에서 시골 길의 치안을 맡아 노상 범죄를 단속하던 무장 자경 조직."]],[["“Peace,”","\"가만있게나.\""],["said Don Quixote; “where hast thou ever seen or heard that a knight-errant has been arraigned before a court of justice, however many homicides he may have committed?”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"편력 기사가 아무리 많은 살인을 저질렀다 한들, 법정에 끌려가 심문받았다는 말을 자네는 대체 어디서 보거나 들은 적이 있는가?\""]],[["“I know nothing about omecils,”","\"살인이니 뭐니 하는 것은 저는 도무지 모릅니다.\" 산초가 대답했다."],["answered Sancho, “nor in my life have had anything to do with one; I only know that the Holy Brotherhood looks after those who fight in the fields, and in that other matter I do not meddle.”","\"평생 그런 것과는 아무 상관이 없었으니까요. 제가 아는 것이라곤 그저 성스러운 형제단이 들판에서 싸움질하는 자들을 단속한다는 것뿐이고, 그 밖의 일에는 끼어들지 않습니다요.\""]],[["“Then thou needst have no uneasiness, my friend,”","\"그렇다면 마음 졸일 것 없네, 벗이여.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “for I will deliver thee out of the hands of the Chaldeans, much more out of those of the Brotherhood.","\"내가 자네를 칼데아인들의 손에서도 건져 낼 터인데, 하물며 형제단의 손에서야 더 말할 것도 없지. 한데 살아 있는 목숨을 두고 말해 보게나."],["But tell me, as thou livest, hast thou seen a more valiant knight than I in all the known world; hast thou read in history of any who has or had higher mettle in attack, more spirit in maintaining it, more dexterity in wounding or skill in overthrowing?”","알려진 온 세상을 통틀어 나보다 더 용맹한 기사를 본 적이 있는가. 공격에서 이보다 드높은 기개를 지녔거나 지녔던 자, 그것을 밀어붙이는 데 이보다 더한 투지를 보인 자, 상처 입히는 데 이보다 더한 솜씨나 쓰러뜨리는 데 이보다 더한 기량을 지닌 자를 역사책에서 읽은 적이 있는가?\""]],[["“The truth is,” answered Sancho, “that I have never read any history, for I can neither read nor write, but what I will venture to bet is that a more daring master than your worship I have never served in all the days of my life, and God grant that this daring be not paid for where I have said; what I beg of your worship is to dress your wound, for a great deal of blood flows from that ear, and I have here some lint and a little white ointment in the alforjas.”","* 칼데아인들: 구약성서에서 바빌론의 점성술사·마법사를 가리키는 말. 돈키호테가 과장된 어법으로 끌어들인 표현이다. \"솔직히 말씀드리면,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"저는 역사책이라곤 한 번도 읽어 본 적이 없습니다. 읽을 줄도 쓸 줄도 모르니까요. 하지만 감히 내기를 걸 수 있는 것은, 제 평생 나리보다 더 무모한 주인은 모신 적이 없다는 겁니다. 그리고 부디 하느님께서 이 무모함의 값을 제가 아까 말씀드린 그곳에서 치르게 하지 않으시기를 빕니다. 제가 나리께 청하는 것은 그 상처를 좀 싸매시라는 겁니다. 그 귀에서 피가 어찌나 많이 흐르는지요. 여기 안장주머니에 무명실하고 흰 연고가 조금 있습니다.\""]],[["“All that might be well dispensed with,”","\"그 모든 것은 없어도 좋았을 텐데.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “if I had remembered to make a vial of the balsam of Fierabras, for time and medicine are saved by one single drop.”","\"내가 피에라브라스의 영약을 한 병 만들어 둘 생각을 했더라면 말일세. 그것은 단 한 방울로 시간과 약을 함께 아껴 주니 말이야.\""]],[["“What vial and what balsam is that?”","* 피에라브라스의 영약: 기사도 소설에 등장하는 만병통치의 신비한 약."],["said Sancho Panza.","피에라브라스는 사라센의 거인 기사 이름이다."]],[["“It is a balsam,” answered Don Quixote, “the receipt of which I have in my memory, with which one need have no fear of death, or dread dying of any wound; and so when I make it and give it to thee thou hast nothing to do when in some battle thou seest they have cut me in half through the middle of the body--as is wont to happen frequently,--but neatly and with great nicety, ere the blood congeal, to place that portion of the body which shall have fallen to the ground upon the other half which remains in the saddle, taking care to fit it on evenly and exactly. Then thou shalt give me to drink but two drops of the balsam I have mentioned, and thou shalt see me become sounder than an apple.”","\"그 병이라는 게 무엇이고, 그 영약이라는 게 무엇입니까?\" 산초 판사가 말했다. \"그것은 영약이라네.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그 제조법을 내가 머릿속에 담아 두고 있는데, 그것만 있으면 죽음을 두려워할 것도, 어떤 상처로 목숨을 잃을까 겁낼 것도 없지. 그러니 내가 그것을 만들어 자네에게 건네주고 나면, 어느 싸움에서 그들이 내 몸뚱이를 한가운데서 두 동강으로 갈라 놓은 것을 — 흔히 일어나는 일이지만 — 보게 되었을 때, 자네가 할 일이라곤 이것뿐일세. 피가 굳기 전에, 깔끔하고 더없이 정밀하게, 땅에 떨어진 그 몸의 한쪽을 안장에 남아 있는 다른 절반 위에 얹되, 어긋남 없이 정확히 맞물리도록 조심해서 갖다 붙이는 것이지. 그러고 나서 내가 말한 그 영약을 단 두 방울만 내게 마시게 해 주면, 자네는 내가 사과보다 더 멀쩡해지는 것을 보게 될 걸세.\""]],[["“If that be so,” said Panza, “I renounce henceforth the government of the promised island, and desire nothing more in payment of my many and faithful services than that your worship give me the receipt of this supreme liquor, for I am persuaded it will be worth more than two reals an ounce anywhere, and I want no more to pass the rest of my life in ease and honour; but it remains to be told if it costs much to make it.”","\"정말 그렇다면,\" 판사가 말했다. \"저는 이제부터 약속하신 그 섬의 통치를 단념하겠습니다. 그리고 저의 수많은 충직한 봉사의 대가로 나리께서 그 더없는 영약의 제조법을 제게 일러 주시는 것 말고는 더 바랄 것이 없습니다. 어디서든 그것은 한 온스에 두 레알 넘는 값이 나가리라 믿으니, 저는 그것만 있으면 남은 평생을 편안하고 영예롭게 보내는 데 더 바랄 것이 없습니다. 다만 그것을 만드는 데 비용이 많이 드는지는 아직 말씀해 주셔야겠지요.\" * 레알: 당대 스페인의 은화 단위."]],[["“With less than three reals, six quarts of it may be made,”","\"삼 레알도 채 안 되는 돈으로 여섯 쿠아르토는 만들 수 있지.\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“Sinner that I am!”","* 쿠아르토: 액체의 부피를 재던 단위. \"이 죄 많은 놈을 보십시오!\""],["said Sancho, “then why does your worship put off making it and teaching it to me?”","산초가 말했다. \"그렇다면 나리께서는 어찌하여 그것을 만들어 제게 가르쳐 주시는 일을 미루고 계신답니까?\""]],[["“Peace, friend,” answered Don Quixote; “greater secrets I mean to teach thee and greater favours to bestow upon thee; and for the present let us see to the dressing, for my ear pains me more than I could wish.”","\"가만있게, 벗이여.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그보다 더 큰 비밀들을 내 자네에게 가르쳐 줄 작정이고, 그보다 더 큰 은혜들을 내려 줄 작정이라네. 그러니 지금은 우선 이 상처나 싸매도록 하세. 귀가 내 바라는 것보다 더 아프구먼.\""]],[["Sancho took out some lint and ointment from the alforjas; but when Don Quixote came to see his helmet shattered, he was like to lose his senses, and clapping his hand upon his sword and raising his eyes to heaven, be said, “I swear by the Creator of all things and the four Gospels in their fullest extent, to do as the great Marquis of Mantua did when he swore to avenge the death of his nephew Baldwin (and that was not to eat bread from a table-cloth, nor embrace his wife, and other points which, though I cannot now call them to mind, I here grant as expressed) until I take complete vengeance upon him who has committed such an offence against me.”","산초는 안장주머니에서 무명실과 연고를 조금 꺼냈다. 그러나 돈키호테는 자기 투구가 박살 난 것을 보자 정신을 잃을 지경이 되어, 칼자루에 손을 얹고 두 눈을 하늘로 치켜올리며 이렇게 말했다. \"만물의 창조주와 그 온전한 뜻을 다 갖춘 사복음서를 두고 맹세하노니, 만토바의 위대한 후작이 자기 조카 발도비노스의 죽음을 복수하겠다고 맹세하며 했던 그대로 하리라. 즉 식탁보를 깔고 빵을 먹지 않을 것이며, 아내를 품지 않을 것이며, 지금은 다 떠올리지 못하나 여기서 그대로 말한 것으로 갈음하는 다른 여러 조항을 지키리니, 이는 내게 이토록 모진 모욕을 가한 자에게 온전한 복수를 거둘 때까지로다.\""]],[["Hearing this, Sancho said to him, “Your worship should bear in mind, Señor Don Quixote, that if the knight has done what was commanded him in going to present himself before my lady Dulcinea del Toboso, he will have done all that he was bound to do, and does not deserve further punishment unless he commits some new offence.”","* 만토바의 후작과 발도비노스: 카롤링거 계열 기사도 이야기와 옛 로맨스에 나오는 인물들. 이 말을 듣고 산초가 그에게 말했다. \"돈키호테 나리, 한 가지 유념하셔야 할 것이 있습니다. 그 기사가 명하신 대로 토보소의 둘시네아 마님 앞에 가서 자기를 바쳤다면, 그는 자기가 마땅히 해야 할 바를 다 한 것이니, 새로운 죄를 저지르지 않는 한 더 벌받을 까닭이 없지요.\""]],[["“Thou hast said well and hit the point,” answered Don Quixote; and so I recall the oath in so far as relates to taking fresh vengeance on him, but I make and confirm it anew to lead the life I have said until such time as I take by force from some knight another helmet such as this and as good; and think not, Sancho, that I am raising smoke with straw in doing so, for I have one to imitate in the matter, since the very same thing to a hair happened in the case of Mambrino’s helmet, which cost Sacripante so dear.”","\"옳은 말일세, 정곡을 찔렀구먼.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그러니 그자에게 새로이 복수를 거두는 일에 관해서는 그 맹세를 거두어들이겠네. 하지만 내가 말한 그런 삶을 살겠다는 맹세만큼은 다시 세우고 다짐하노니, 어느 기사에게서 이만큼 훌륭한 또 다른 투구를 힘으로 빼앗을 때까지 그리하리라. 그리고 산초여, 내가 이렇게 한다고 해서 짚으로 연기를 피우는 헛수고를 한다고는 여기지 말게. 이 일에서 본받을 선례가 내게 있으니, 사크리판테에게 그토록 비싼 대가를 치르게 했던 맘브리노의 투구의 경우에도 털끝 하나 다르지 않게 바로 이와 똑같은 일이 일어났기 때문일세.\""]],[["“Señor,” replied Sancho, “let your worship send all such oaths to the devil, for they are very pernicious to salvation and prejudicial to the conscience; just tell me now, if for several days to come we fall in with no man armed with a helmet, what are we to do? Is the oath to be observed in spite of all the inconvenience and discomfort it will be to sleep in your clothes, and not to sleep in a house, and a thousand other mortifications contained in the oath of that old fool the Marquis of Mantua, which your worship is now wanting to revive? Let your worship observe that there are no men in armour travelling on any of these roads, nothing but carriers and carters, who not only do not wear helmets, but perhaps never heard tell of them all their lives.”","* 맘브리노의 투구: 아리오스토의 「광란의 오를란도」 등에 나오는, 누구도 다치게 할 수 없는 마법의 황금 투구. \"나리,\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"그런 맹세 따위는 죄다 악마에게나 보내 버리십시오. 그것들은 구원에 몹시 해롭고 양심에도 거치적거리니까요. 그러니 이제 말씀해 보십시오. 앞으로 며칠 동안 투구를 쓴 사람을 하나도 만나지 못하면 우리는 어찌해야 합니까? 옷을 입은 채 자야 하고, 집 안에서는 자지 못해야 하며, 나리께서 지금 되살리려 하시는 저 늙다리 만토바 후작의 맹세에 담긴 천 가지 다른 고행을 감수해야 하는데, 그 온갖 불편과 괴로움을 무릅쓰고도 그 맹세를 지켜야 한단 말입니까? 보십시오, 이 길들 어디에도 갑옷 입고 다니는 자들은 없고, 짐꾼이나 마차꾼밖에 없습니다. 그자들은 투구를 쓰기는커녕 아마 평생 그런 말조차 들어 본 적이 없을 겁니다.\""]],[["“Thou art wrong there,”","\"그 점에서는 자네가 틀렸네.\""],["said Don Quixote, “for we shall not have been above two hours among these cross-roads before we see more men in armour than came to Albraca to win the fair Angelica.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"우리가 이 갈림길에 들어선 지 두 시간도 채 안 되어, 아름다운 안젤리카를 차지하려고 알브라카로 몰려들었던 것보다 더 많은 갑옷 입은 자들을 보게 될 테니 말일세.\""]],[["“Enough,” said Sancho; “so be it then, and God grant us success, and that the time for winning that island which is costing me so dear may soon come, and then let me die.”","* 안젤리카와 알브라카: 「광란의 오를란도」와 보이아르도의 「사랑에 빠진 오를란도」에 나오는 미녀 공주와 그녀가 농성한 성."]],[["“I have already told thee, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “not to give thyself any uneasiness on that score; for if an island should fail, there is the kingdom of Denmark, or of Sobradisa, which will fit thee as a ring fits the finger, and all the more that, being on terra firma, thou wilt all the better enjoy thyself. But let us leave that to its own time; see if thou hast anything for us to eat in those alforjas, because we must presently go in quest of some castle where we may lodge to-night and make the balsam I told thee of, for I swear to thee by God, this ear is giving me great pain.”","\"알겠습니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그렇다면 그리되도록 하지요. 부디 하느님께서 우리에게 성공을 내려 주시고, 제게 이토록 값비싼 대가를 치르게 하는 그 섬을 얻을 때가 어서 오게 해 주시기를 빕니다. 그러고 나면 저는 죽어도 좋습니다.\" \"내 이미 자네에게 말하지 않았는가, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그 일로는 조금도 마음을 졸이지 말게. 설령 섬이 어그러진다 해도 덴마크 왕국이나 소브라디사 왕국이 있어, 반지가 손가락에 꼭 맞듯 자네에게 딱 들어맞을 테니 말일세. 더구나 그것들은 뭍에 있으니 자네가 한층 더 즐기게 될 것이고. 하지만 그 일은 때가 오거든 그때 가서 보세. 우선 그 안장주머니에 우리가 먹을 만한 것이 좀 있는지 살펴보게. 곧장 오늘 밤 묵을 만한 성을 찾아 나서서, 내가 말한 그 영약을 만들어야 하니 말일세. 하느님께 맹세코, 이 귀가 나를 몹시 아프게 하는구먼.\""]],[["“I have here an onion and a little cheese and a few scraps of bread,”","\"여기 양파 하나하고 치즈 조금, 빵 부스러기가 몇 개 있습니다.\""],["said Sancho, “but they are not victuals fit for a valiant knight like your worship.”","산초가 말했다. \"하지만 나리 같은 용맹한 기사께 어울리는 음식은 못 됩니다요.\""]],[["“How little thou knowest about it,” answered Don Quixote; “I would have thee to know, Sancho, that it is the glory of knights-errant to go without eating for a month, and even when they do eat, that it should be of what comes first to hand; and this would have been clear to thee hadst thou read as many histories as I have, for, though they are very many, among them all I have found no mention made of knights-errant eating, unless by accident or at some sumptuous banquets prepared for them, and the rest of the time they passed in dalliance. And though it is plain they could not do without eating and performing all the other natural functions, because, in fact, they were men like ourselves, it is plain too that, wandering as they did the most part of their lives through woods and wilds and without a cook, their most usual fare would be rustic viands such as those thou now offer me; so that, friend Sancho, let not that distress thee which pleases me, and do not seek to make a new world or pervert knight-errantry.”","\"자네가 그 일을 얼마나 모르는가!\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"산초여, 내 자네에게 일러 두거니와, 한 달을 먹지 않고 지내는 것, 그리고 설령 먹는다 해도 손에 가장 먼저 잡히는 것으로 때우는 것이야말로 편력 기사의 영광일세. 내가 읽은 만큼 자네도 역사책을 많이 읽었더라면 이 점이 자네에게도 분명했을 텐데. 그 책들이 무척 많기는 하나, 그 모든 것을 통틀어도 우연히 먹게 되거나 그들을 위해 차려진 어느 호화로운 연회에서가 아니고서는 편력 기사들이 먹었다는 언급은 찾아본 적이 없고, 나머지 시간은 사랑놀음으로 보냈다고 되어 있으니 말일세. 물론 그들이 먹지 않고서, 또 다른 온갖 자연스러운 일들을 치르지 않고서 지낼 수 없었으리라는 것은 분명하네. 사실 그들도 우리와 같은 사람이었으니까. 하지만 그들이 평생의 대부분을 숲과 황야를 떠돌며 요리사도 없이 지낸 것 또한 분명한즉, 그들의 가장 흔한 끼니란 자네가 지금 내게 내미는 것과 같은 시골스러운 음식이었을 걸세. 그러니 산초 벗이여, 나를 기쁘게 하는 것이 자네를 괴롭히지 않도록 하게. 새로운 세상을 만들어 내려 들거나 편력 기사도를 비틀려 들지 말게.\""]],[["“Pardon me, your worship,” said Sancho, “for, as I cannot read or write, as I said just now, I neither know nor comprehend the rules of the profession of chivalry: henceforward I will stock the alforjas with every kind of dry fruit for your worship, as you are a knight; and for myself, as I am not one, I will furnish them with poultry and other things more substantial.”","\"용서하십시오, 나리.\" 산초가 말했다. \"아까 말씀드렸다시피 저는 읽을 줄도 쓸 줄도 모르니, 기사라는 직분의 규칙을 알지도 깨닫지도 못합니다. 앞으로는 나리께서 기사이시니, 나리를 위해서는 온갖 말린 과일로 안장주머니를 채워 드리겠습니다. 그리고 저는 기사가 아니니, 저를 위해서는 가금류며 그 밖에 좀 더 든든한 것들로 채워 두겠습니다요.\""]],[["“I do not say, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote, “that it is imperative on knights-errant not to eat anything else but the fruits thou speakest of; only that their more usual diet must be those, and certain herbs they found in the fields which they knew and I know too.”","\"산초여, 내 말은,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"편력 기사가 자네 말하는 그 과일 말고는 아무것도 먹어서는 안 된다는 것이 아닐세. 다만 그들의 더 흔한 끼니가 그런 것들과, 그들이 들판에서 찾아낸 어떤 풀들이었으리라는 것이지. 그들도 그 풀들을 알았고 나 또한 알고 있다네.\""]],[["“A good thing it is,”","\"그런 풀들을 안다는 건 좋은 일이지요.\""],["answered Sancho, “to know those herbs, for to my thinking it will be needful some day to put that knowledge into practice.”","산초가 대답했다. \"제 생각에는 언젠가 그 지식을 써먹어야 할 날이 올 듯하니까요.\""]],[["And here taking out what he said he had brought, the pair made their repast peaceably and sociably. But anxious to find quarters for the night, they with all despatch made an end of their poor dry fare, mounted at once, and made haste to reach some habitation before night set in; but daylight and the hope of succeeding in their object failed them close by the huts of some goatherds, so they determined to pass the night there, and it was as much to Sancho’s discontent not to have reached a house, as it was to his master’s satisfaction to sleep under the open heaven, for he fancied that each time this happened to him he performed an act of ownership that helped to prove his chivalry.","그러고는 산초가 가져왔다고 한 것을 꺼내 놓자, 두 사람은 평화롭고 정답게 끼니를 들었다. 그러나 밤에 묵을 곳을 서둘러 찾고 싶은 마음에, 그들은 부랴부랴 그 변변찮은 마른 음식을 마저 먹어 치우고는 곧장 말에 올라, 날이 저물기 전에 어느 거처에 닿으려고 서둘렀다. 그러나 어느 염소지기들의 오두막 가까이에 이르렀을 때 햇빛도, 목적을 이루리라는 희망도 그들을 저버렸으니, 그들은 그곳에서 밤을 지내기로 했다. 집에 닿지 못한 것이 산초에게는 그만큼 불만스러운 일이었으나, 한데서 별을 이고 자는 것은 그만큼 주인에게는 흡족한 일이었다. 그는 이런 일이 자신에게 일어날 때마다 자신의 기사도를 입증해 주는 소유의 행위 하나를 행하는 것이라 여겼기 때문이다."]],[["# CHAPTER XI. WHAT BEFELL DON QUIXOTE WITH CERTAIN GOATHERDS","# CHAPTER XI."]],[["He was cordially welcomed by the goatherds, and Sancho, having as best he could put up Rocinante and the ass, drew towards the fragrance that came from some pieces of salted goat simmering in a pot on the fire; and though he would have liked at once to try if they were ready to be transferred from the pot to the stomach, he refrained from doing so as the goatherds removed them from the fire, and laying sheepskins on the ground, quickly spread their rude table, and with signs of hearty good-will invited them both to share what they had.","돈키호테는 염소지기들에게 따뜻한 환대를 받았고, 산초는 최선을 다해 로시난테와 나귀를 매어 둔 뒤, 불 위에 올려진 솥에서 보글보글 끓고 있는 소금에 절인 염소 고기 몇 점에서 풍겨 오는 향내 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그것들이 솥에서 위장으로 옮겨질 만큼 익었는지 당장 시험해 보고 싶었으나, 염소지기들이 그것을 불에서 내려 양가죽을 땅에 깔고 재빨리 거친 식탁을 차린 다음, 진심에서 우러난 호의의 몸짓으로 두 사람에게 자기들이 가진 것을 함께 나누자고 청했기에 그는 참았다."],["Round the skins six of the men belonging to the fold seated themselves, having first with rough politeness pressed Don Quixote to take a seat upon a trough which they placed for him upside down.","우리에 딸린 사내 여섯이 양가죽 둘레에 자리를 잡았는데, 그들은 먼저 투박한 정중함으로 돈키호테에게 권하여 그를 위해 거꾸로 엎어 놓은 여물통 위에 앉게 했다."],["Don Quixote seated himself, and Sancho remained standing to serve the cup, which was made of horn.","돈키호테는 자리에 앉았고, 산초는 뿔로 만든 잔에 술을 따르려고 선 채로 있었다."],["Seeing him standing, his master said to him:","그가 서 있는 것을 보고 주인이 말했다."]],[["“That thou mayest see, Sancho, the good that knight-errantry contains in itself, and how those who fill any office in it are on the high road to be speedily honoured and esteemed by the world, I desire that thou seat thyself here at my side and in the company of these worthy people, and that thou be one with me who am thy master and natural lord, and that thou eat from my plate and drink from whatever I drink from; for the same may be said of knight-errantry as of love, that it levels all.”","\"산초야, 편력 기사도 안에 어떤 좋은 점이 깃들어 있는지, 또 그 안에서 어떤 직책이든 맡은 자들이 어찌하여 세상으로부터 속히 존경받고 떠받들리는 큰길에 들어서게 되는지를 네가 알도록, 나는 네가 여기 내 곁에, 이 훌륭한 사람들과 함께 앉기를 바라며, 너의 주인이자 타고난 영주인 나와 하나가 되어, 내 접시에서 먹고 내가 마시는 그 무엇에서든 함께 마시기를 바라노라. 편력 기사도에 대해서도 사랑에 관해 하는 말과 똑같은 말을 할 수 있으니, 그것이 모든 것을 평등하게 만든다는 말이다.\""]],[["“Great thanks,”","\"참으로 고마운 말씀입니다요.\""],["said Sancho, “but I may tell your worship that provided I have enough to eat, I can eat it as well, or better, standing, and by myself, than seated alongside of an emperor.","산초가 말했다. \"하오나 나리께 한 말씀 드리자면, 먹을 것만 넉넉하다면 황제 곁에 앉아서보다 차라리 서서, 혼자서 먹는 편이 못지않게, 아니 더 잘 먹을 수 있습니다요."],["And indeed, if the truth is to be told, what I eat in my corner without form or fuss has much more relish for me, even though it be bread and onions, than the turkeys of those other tables where I am forced to chew slowly, drink little, wipe my mouth every minute, and cannot sneeze or cough if I want or do other things that are the privileges of liberty and solitude.","솔직히 말씀드리면, 격식도 법석도 없이 제 구석에서 먹는 것은 그것이 빵에 양파뿐이라 해도, 천천히 씹어야 하고 조금밖에 못 마시며 시도 때도 없이 입을 닦아야 하고, 재채기나 기침이 나도 못 하고, 자유와 고독이 주는 특권인 다른 짓들도 못 하는 저 다른 식탁의 칠면조 요리보다 제게는 훨씬 더 맛이 있습니다요."],["So, señor, as for these honours which your worship would put upon me as a servant and follower of knight-errantry, exchange them for other things which may be of more use and advantage to me; for these, though I fully acknowledge them as received, I renounce from this moment to the end of the world.”","그러니 나리, 편력 기사도의 종이요 추종자라 하여 나리께서 제게 베푸시려는 이런 영예는, 제게 더 쓸모 있고 더 이득이 될 다른 것들과 바꿔 주십시오. 이것들은 받은 것으로 충분히 인정은 하지만, 지금 이 순간부터 세상 끝날까지 사양하겠습니다요.\""]],[["“For all that,”","\"그렇더라도,\""],["said Don Quixote, “thou must seat thyself, because him who humbleth himself God exalteth;”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"너는 앉아야 한다."],["and seizing him by the arm he forced him to sit down beside himself.","스스로를 낮추는 자를 하느님께서 높이시는 법이니라.\" 그러고는 그의 팔을 붙잡아 억지로 자기 곁에 앉혔다."]],[["The goatherds did not understand this jargon about squires and knights-errant, and all they did was to eat in silence and stare at their guests, who with great elegance and appetite were stowing away pieces as big as one’s fist.","염소지기들은 종자니 편력 기사니 하는 이 알아들을 수 없는 말을 이해하지 못했고, 그저 묵묵히 먹으며 손님들을 빤히 쳐다볼 뿐이었는데, 그 손님들은 더할 나위 없는 우아함과 식욕으로 주먹만 한 고깃점들을 챙겨 넣고 있었다."],["The course of meat finished, they spread upon the sheepskins a great heap of parched acorns, and with them they put down a half cheese harder than if it had been made of mortar.","고기 요리가 끝나자 그들은 양가죽 위에 볶은 도토리를 한 무더기 쏟아 놓았고, 그와 함께 회반죽으로 만든 것보다 더 단단한 치즈 반 덩이를 내려놓았다."],["All this while the horn was not idle, for it went round so constantly, now full, now empty, like the bucket of a water-wheel, that it soon drained one of the two wine-skins that were in sight.","그러는 내내 뿔잔도 놀고 있지 않았으니, 그것은 물레방아의 두레박처럼 가득 찼다가 비었다가 하며 쉴 새 없이 돌고 돌아, 눈에 보이던 두 개의 술 부대 가운데 하나를 이내 비워 버렸다."],["When Don Quixote had quite appeased his appetite he took up a handful of the acorns, and contemplating them attentively delivered himself somewhat in this fashion:","돈키호테는 식욕을 충분히 채우고 나서 도토리 한 줌을 집어 들고는 그것을 골똘히 바라보며 대략 이런 식으로 말을 늘어놓았다."]],[["“Happy the age, happy the time, to which the ancients gave the name of golden, not because in that fortunate age the gold so coveted in this our iron one was gained without toil, but because they that lived in it knew not the two words “mine”","\"행복하도다, 옛사람들이 황금이라 이름 붙인 그 시대와 그 세월이여. 그 복된 시절에 우리의 이 무쇠 같은 시대에서 그토록 탐내는 황금이 수고 없이 얻어졌기 때문이 아니라, 그때 살던 이들이 '내 것'과 '네 것'이라는 두 마디 말을 알지 못했기 때문이로다!"],["and “thine”! In that blessed age all things were in common; to win the daily food no labour was required of any save to stretch forth his hand and gather it from the sturdy oaks that stood generously inviting him with their sweet ripe fruit.","그 축복받은 시대에는 모든 것이 공유물이었으니, 날마다의 양식을 얻는 데에는 손을 뻗어, 달콤하고 잘 익은 열매로 너그러이 손짓하며 서 있는 튼튼한 떡갈나무에서 그것을 따 모으는 일 외에 아무런 노동도 누구에게 요구되지 않았다."],["The clear streams and running brooks yielded their savoury limpid waters in noble abundance.","맑은 시내와 흐르는 개울은 그 향긋하고 투명한 물을 고귀하게도 넘치도록 내어 주었다."],["The busy and sagacious bees fixed their republic in the clefts of the rocks and hollows of the trees, offering without usance the plenteous produce of their fragrant toil to every hand.","부지런하고 영민한 벌들은 바위틈과 나무 구멍에 그들의 공화국을 세워, 그 향긋한 노고의 풍성한 산물을 아무런 대가도 없이 모든 손길에 내밀었다."],["The mighty cork trees, unenforced save of their own courtesy, shed the broad light bark that served at first to roof the houses supported by rude stakes, a protection against the inclemency of heaven alone.","힘센 코르크나무는 제 호의 외에는 아무런 강요도 받지 않고 그 넓적한 가벼운 껍질을 벗어 주었으니, 그것은 처음에 거친 말뚝으로 떠받친 집들의 지붕이 되어 오직 하늘의 험한 날씨에 대한 보호막이 되어 주었다."],["Then all was peace, all friendship, all concord; as yet the dull share of the crooked plough had not dared to rend and pierce the tender bowels of our first mother that without compulsion yielded from every portion of her broad fertile bosom all that could satisfy, sustain, and delight the children that then possessed her.","그때는 모든 것이 평화요, 모든 것이 우정이요, 모든 것이 화합이었다. 아직 굽은 쟁기의 무딘 보습이 우리의 첫 어머니의 부드러운 속살을 감히 찢고 꿰뚫지 못했으니, 그 어머니는 강요받지 않고도 그 넓고 비옥한 품의 구석구석에서, 그때 그를 차지하던 자식들을 만족시키고 지탱하며 기쁘게 할 모든 것을 내어 주었다."],["Then was it that the innocent and fair young shepherdess roamed from vale to vale and hill to hill, with flowing locks, and no more garments than were needful modestly to cover what modesty seeks and ever sought to hide.","그때야말로 순결하고 어여쁜 젊은 양치기 처녀가, 머리채를 풀어헤치고, 정숙함이 가리고자 했고 또 늘 가리고자 해 온 것을 단정히 덮을 만큼의 옷가지만 걸친 채, 골짜기에서 골짜기로, 언덕에서 언덕으로 거닐던 시절이었다."],["Nor were their ornaments like those in use to-day, set off by Tyrian purple, and silk tortured in endless fashions, but the wreathed leaves of the green dock and ivy, wherewith they went as bravely and becomingly decked as our Court dames with all the rare and far-fetched artifices that idle curiosity has taught them.","그들의 장신구도 오늘날 쓰이는 것들, 곧 티레산 자줏빛으로 꾸미고 끝없는 모양으로 비단을 비틀어 만든 것들과는 달랐으니, 푸른 소루쟁이와 담쟁이덩굴의 잎을 엮은 것이었고, 그들은 그것으로, 한가한 호기심이 우리 궁정의 귀부인들에게 가르쳐 준 온갖 진귀하고 머나먼 데서 들여온 기교만큼이나 멋지고 어울리게 치장하고 다녔다."],["Then the love-thoughts of the heart clothed themselves simply and naturally as the heart conceived them, nor sought to commend themselves by forced and rambling verbiage.","그때는 마음의 사랑의 정념도 마음이 품은 그대로 소박하고 자연스럽게 옷을 입었을 뿐, 억지스럽고 두서없는 말장난으로 스스로를 내세우려 하지 않았다."],["Fraud, deceit, or malice had then not yet mingled with truth and sincerity.","사기나 기만이나 악의도 아직 진실과 진심에 섞여 들지 않았다."],["Justice held her ground, undisturbed and unassailed by the efforts of favour and of interest, that now so much impair, pervert, and beset her.","정의는 그 자리를 지켜, 지금은 정의를 그토록 심하게 훼손하고 왜곡하며 에워싸는 저 정실과 사욕의 책동에 시달리거나 침해당하지 않았다."],["Arbitrary law had not yet established itself in the mind of the judge, for then there was no cause to judge and no one to be judged.","자의적인 법도 아직 재판관의 마음에 자리 잡지 못했으니, 그때는 재판할 거리도 없었고 재판받을 자도 없었기 때문이다."],["Maidens and modesty, as I have said, wandered at will alone and unattended, without fear of insult from lawlessness or libertine assault, and if they were undone it was of their own will and pleasure.","처녀들과 정숙함은, 내가 말했듯이, 무법자의 모욕이나 난봉꾼의 습격을 두려워하지 않고 홀로 거리낌 없이 시중도 없이 거닐었으며, 만일 그들이 몸을 망쳤다면 그것은 그들 자신의 뜻과 즐거움에서였다."],["But now in this hateful age of ours not one is safe, not though some new labyrinth like that of Crete conceal and surround her; even there the pestilence of gallantry will make its way to them through chinks or on the air by the zeal of its accursed importunity, and, despite of all seclusion, lead them to ruin.","그러나 이제 우리의 이 가증스러운 시대에는 어느 누구도 안전하지 못하니, 비록 크레타의 그것과 같은 새로운 미로가 그를 감추고 둘러싼다 해도 그러하다. 그곳에서조차 색정의 역병은 틈새를 비집고, 혹은 그 저주받은 집요함의 열의로 공기를 타고 그들에게로 길을 내어, 온갖 격리에도 불구하고 그들을 파멸로 이끌 것이다."],["In defence of these, as time advanced and wickedness increased, the order of knights-errant was instituted, to defend maidens, to protect widows and to succour the orphans and the needy.","세월이 흐르고 사악함이 늘어남에 따라 이런 이들을 지키고자, 처녀들을 보호하고 과부들을 지키며 고아들과 궁핍한 자들을 구원하기 위해 편력 기사단이 세워졌다."],["To this order I belong, brother goatherds, to whom I return thanks for the hospitality and kindly welcome ye offer me and my squire; for though by natural law all living are bound to show favour to knights-errant, yet, seeing that without knowing this obligation ye have welcomed and feasted me, it is right that with all the good-will in my power I should thank you for yours.”","염소지기 형제들이여, 나는 그 기사단에 속한 자이니, 그대들이 나와 내 종자에게 베푸는 환대와 다정한 영접에 감사를 드리노라. 자연의 법으로 보면 살아 있는 모든 자가 편력 기사에게 호의를 베풀 의무가 있으나, 그대들은 이 의무를 알지 못하고도 나를 맞아들여 잔치를 베풀었으니, 내가 가진 온 호의를 다하여 그대들의 호의에 감사하는 것이 마땅하도다.\""]],[["All this long harangue (which might very well have been spared) our knight delivered because the acorns they gave him reminded him of the golden age; and the whim seized him to address all this unnecessary argument to the goatherds, who listened to him gaping in amazement without saying a word in reply.","우리의 기사가 이 길고 긴 일장 연설을(없어도 아주 좋았을) 늘어놓은 것은, 그들이 준 도토리가 그에게 황금시대를 떠올리게 했기 때문이며, 이 쓸데없는 논변을 모조리 염소지기들에게 늘어놓고 싶은 변덕이 그를 사로잡았기 때문인데, 그들은 입을 떡 벌린 채 어리둥절하여 한마디 대꾸도 없이 그의 말에 귀를 기울였다."],["Sancho likewise held his peace and ate acorns, and paid repeated visits to the second wine-skin, which they had hung up on a cork tree to keep the wine cool.","산초 또한 입을 다물고 도토리를 먹으며, 술을 시원하게 두려고 코르크나무에 매달아 둔 두 번째 술 부대를 거듭거듭 찾았다."]],[["Don Quixote was longer in talking than the supper in finishing, at the end of which one of the goatherds said, “That your worship, señor knight-errant, may say with more truth that we show you hospitality with ready good-will, we will give you amusement and pleasure by making one of our comrades sing: he will be here before long, and he is a very intelligent youth and deep in love, and what is more he can read and write and play on the rebeck to perfection.”","돈키호테가 말을 늘어놓는 시간이 저녁 식사가 끝나는 시간보다 길었는데, 그것이 끝나자 염소지기 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"편력 기사 나리, 저희가 기꺼운 호의로 나리를 환대한다는 것을 나리께서 더 진실되게 말씀하실 수 있도록, 저희 동무 가운데 하나에게 노래를 시켜 즐거움과 흥을 돋워 드리겠습니다요. 그 친구는 곧 이리로 올 텐데, 아주 영리한 젊은이인 데다 사랑에 깊이 빠져 있고, 게다가 글을 읽고 쓸 줄도 알며 레베크도 더할 나위 없이 잘 켭니다요.\""]],[["The goatherd had hardly done speaking, when the notes of the rebeck reached their ears; and shortly after, the player came up, a very good-looking young man of about two-and-twenty.","염소지기가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에 레베크 소리가 그들의 귀에 닿았고, 잠시 후 그 연주자가 다가왔는데, 스물두 살쯤 된 매우 잘생긴 젊은이였다."],["His comrades asked him if he had supped, and on his replying that he had, he who had already made the offer said to him:","동무들이 그에게 저녁을 먹었느냐고 묻자 그는 먹었다고 대답했고, 앞서 청을 했던 사내가 그에게 말했다."]],[["“In that case, Antonio, thou mayest as well do us the pleasure of singing a little, that the gentleman, our guest, may see that even in the mountains and woods there are musicians: we have told him of thy accomplishments, and we want thee to show them and prove that we say true; so, as thou livest, pray sit down and sing that ballad about thy love that thy uncle the prebendary made thee, and that was so much liked in the town.”","\"그렇다면 안토니오, 자네가 노래 한 곡을 불러 우리를 즐겁게 해 주는 것도 좋겠네그려. 손님이신 이 신사 어른께서 산속과 숲속에도 악사가 있음을 아시도록 말일세. 우리가 자네의 재주를 어른께 일러두었으니, 자네가 그것을 보여 우리 말이 참됨을 증명해 주었으면 하네. 그러니 제발, 자리에 앉아 자네 삼촌인 참사회원 어른이 자네에게 지어 주신, 마을에서 그토록 사랑받은 자네 사랑 이야기 그 가락을 한번 불러 보게나.\""]],[["“With all my heart,”","\"기꺼이 그러지요.\""],["said the young man, and without waiting for more pressing he seated himself on the trunk of a felled oak, and tuning his rebeck, presently began to sing to these words.","젊은이가 말했고, 더 보채기를 기다리지도 않고 베어 낸 떡갈나무 둥치 위에 앉아 레베크를 조율하더니, 이내 이런 가사에 맞추어 노래하기 시작했다. 안토니오의 노래"]],[["# ANTONIO’S BALLAD","# ANTONIO’S BALLAD"]],[["Thou dost love me well, Olalla; Well I know it, even though Love’s mute tongues, thine eyes, have never By their glances told me so.","그대 나를 진정 사랑하네, 올랄라여, 잘 알고 있다네, 비록 사랑의 말 없는 혀, 그대의 눈이, 그 눈길로 내게 한 번도 그리 일러 준 적 없을지라도."]],[["For I know my love thou knowest, Therefore thine to claim I dare: Once it ceases to be secret, Love need never feel despair.","내 사랑을 그대가 안다는 것을 내 알기에, 그대의 사랑도 내 것이라 감히 주장하리니, 사랑이 한번 비밀이기를 그치면 결코 절망을 느낄 까닭 없으리라."]],[["True it is, Olalla, sometimes Thou hast all too plainly shown That thy heart is brass in hardness, And thy snowy bosom stone.","참으로 그러하네, 올랄라여, 때때로 그대는 너무도 분명히 보여 주었지, 그대 마음이 놋쇠처럼 단단하고 그대 눈 같은 가슴이 돌과 같음을."]],[["Yet for all that, in thy coyness, And thy fickle fits between, Hope is there--at least the border Of her garment may be seen.","허나 그 모든 것에도, 그대의 새침함 속에, 그대의 변덕스러운 발작 사이로, 희망이 있으니 — 적어도 그 옷자락 가장자리만은 엿보이나니."]],[["Lures to faith are they, those glimpses, And to faith in thee I hold; Kindness cannot make it stronger, Coldness cannot make it cold.","신뢰로 이끄는 미끼들이라, 그 언뜻 보이는 것들은, 그래서 나 그대에 대한 믿음을 붙드네, 다정함도 그것을 더 굳세게 못 하고, 냉담함도 그것을 식히지 못하리."]],[["If it be that love is gentle, In thy gentleness I see Something holding out assurance To the hope of winning thee.","사랑이 만일 부드러운 것이라면, 그대의 부드러움 속에서 나는 보네, 그대를 얻으리라는 희망에게 무언가 확신을 내미는 것을."]],[["If it be that in devotion Lies a power hearts to move, That which every day I show thee, Helpful to my suit should prove.","헌신 속에 마음을 움직이는 힘이 깃들어 있는 것이라면, 내 날마다 그대에게 보이는 그것이, 내 구애에 도움이 되어야 마땅하리."]],[["Many a time thou must have noticed-- If to notice thou dost care-- How I go about on Monday Dressed in all my Sunday wear.","여러 번 그대도 분명 알아챘으리니 — 알아채려 마음을 쓴다면 말일세 — 내가 어찌하여 월요일에도 주일날 차림을 죄다 갖추고 다니는지를."]],[["Love’s eyes love to look on brightness; Love loves what is gaily drest; Sunday, Monday, all I care is Thou shouldst see me in my best.","사랑의 눈은 환한 것 바라보길 좋아하고, 사랑은 곱게 차려입은 것을 사랑하니, 주일이건 월요일이건 내 마음은 오직 그대가 내 가장 좋은 모습을 봐 주기를."]],[["No account I make of dances, Or of strains that pleased thee so, Keeping thee awake from midnight Till the cocks began to crow;","춤 따위는 내 안중에도 없고, 그대를 그토록 기쁘게 한 가락도, 한밤중부터 닭이 울기 시작할 때까지 그대를 잠 못 들게 한 그것도 마찬가지라네."]],[["Or of how I roundly swore it That there’s none so fair as thou; True it is, but as I said it, By the girls I’m hated now.","그대만큼 어여쁜 이 없노라고 내가 호기롭게 맹세한 것도 그러하네, 참말이긴 하나, 그 말을 한 탓에 마을 처녀들이 이제 나를 미워한다네."]],[["For Teresa of the hillside At my praise of thee was sore; Said, “You think you love an angel; It’s a monkey you adore;","언덕배기의 테레사는 내가 그대를 칭찬하자 속이 상하여 이렇게 말했지, \"넌 천사를 사랑하는 줄 알지만 네가 떠받드는 건 원숭이라네."]],[["“Caught by all her glittering trinkets, And her borrowed braids of hair, And a host of made-up beauties That would Love himself ensnare.”","번쩍이는 싸구려 장신구에 홀리고, 빌려 온 가짜 머리채에 홀리고, 사랑의 신마저 옭아맬 꾸며 댄 미인 떼거리에 홀린 게지.\""]],[["‘T was a lie, and so I told her, And her cousin at the word Gave me his defiance for it; And what followed thou hast heard.","거짓말이라고, 나는 그 애에게 그리 일렀고, 그 말에 그 애 사촌이 나에게 결투를 청했으니, 그 뒤에 일어난 일은 그대도 들어 알리라."]],[["Mine is no high-flown affection, Mine no passion par amours-- As they call it--what I offer Is an honest love, and pure.","내 것은 드높이 띄운 애정이 아니요, 내 것은 — 사람들이 그리 부르는 — 정념의 사랑도 아니라네. 내가 바치는 것은 정직하고 순결한 사랑이니라."]],[["Cunning cords the holy Church has, Cords of softest silk they be; Put thy neck beneath the yoke, dear; Mine will follow, thou wilt see.","거룩한 교회에는 끈이 있으니, 더없이 부드러운 비단 끈이라네, 그 멍에 아래 그대 목을 들이밀게나, 임이여, 내 목도 뒤따르는 것을 그대 보게 되리니."]],[["Else--and once for all I swear it By the saint of most renown-- If I ever quit the mountains, ‘T will be in a friar’s gown.","그러지 않으면 — 단 한 번 맹세하노니 가장 이름 높은 성인을 두고 — 내 만일 이 산을 떠난다면, 그것은 수도사의 옷을 입고서이리라."]],[["Here the goatherd brought his song to an end, and though Don Quixote entreated him to sing more, Sancho had no mind that way, being more inclined for sleep than for listening to songs; so said he to his master, “Your worship will do well to settle at once where you mean to pass the night, for the labour these good men are at all day does not allow them to spend the night in singing.”","여기서 염소지기는 노래를 끝맺었고, 돈키호테는 더 불러 달라고 청했으나, 산초는 노래를 듣기보다 잠 쪽으로 더 기울어 있어 그럴 마음이 없었다. 그래서 그가 주인에게 말했다. \"나리께서는 오늘 밤 어디서 지내실지를 당장 정하시는 게 좋겠습니다요. 이 선량한 사람들이 온종일 하는 노동이 밤을 노래로 지새우도록 내버려 두질 않으니까요.\""]],[["“I understand thee, Sancho,”","\"무슨 말인지 알겠다, 산초야.\""],["replied Don Quixote; “I perceive clearly that those visits to the wine-skin demand compensation in sleep rather than in music.”","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"저 술 부대를 그렇게 찾아간 것이 음악보다는 잠으로 보상받기를 요구한다는 것을 똑똑히 알겠구나.\""]],[["“It’s sweet to us all, blessed be God,”","\"하느님 덕분에, 그건 우리 모두에게 달콤한 일입지요.\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“I do not deny it,” replied Don Quixote; “but settle thyself where thou wilt; those of my calling are more becomingly employed in watching than in sleeping; still it would be as well if thou wert to dress this ear for me again, for it is giving me more pain than it need.”","\"부인하지 않겠다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"허나 너는 어디든 마음 내키는 데에 자리를 잡으려무나. 나 같은 직분의 사람은 잠자기보다 망보는 일에 더 어울리게 쓰여야 하느니라. 다만 네가 이 귀를 다시 한번 손봐 주면 좋겠구나. 필요 이상으로 아프니 말이다.\""]],[["Sancho did as he bade him, but one of the goatherds, seeing the wound, told him not to be uneasy, as he would apply a remedy with which it would be soon healed; and gathering some leaves of rosemary, of which there was a great quantity there, he chewed them and mixed them with a little salt, and applying them to the ear he secured them firmly with a bandage, assuring him that no other treatment would be required, and so it proved.","산초는 그가 시키는 대로 했는데, 염소지기 가운데 하나가 그 상처를 보고는, 곧 나을 약을 발라 줄 테니 걱정 말라고 일렀다. 그러고는 그곳에 잔뜩 자라 있던 로즈메리 잎을 몇 개 따서 씹은 다음 소금을 약간 섞어 그것을 귀에 갖다 대고 붕대로 단단히 동여매면서, 다른 치료는 필요 없으리라고 장담했는데, 과연 그러했다. * 레베크: 중세와 르네상스 시기에 쓰인, 활로 켜는 세 줄짜리 현악기."]],[["# CHAPTER XII. OF WHAT A GOATHERD RELATED TO THOSE WITH DON QUIXOTE","# CHAPTER XII."]],[["Just then another young man, one of those who fetched their provisions from the village, came up and said, “Do you know what is going on in the village, comrades?”","바로 그때 마을에서 양식을 가져오던 젊은이들 가운데 하나가 다가와 말했다. \"여보게들, 마을에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 아나?\""]],[["“How could we know it?”","\"우리가 그걸 어찌 알겠나?\""],["replied one of them.","그들 가운데 하나가 대답했다."]],[["“Well, then, you must know,”","\"그렇다면 들어 보게나.\" 젊은이가 말을 이었다."],["continued the young man, “this morning that famous student-shepherd called Chrysostom died, and it is rumoured that he died of love for that devil of a village girl the daughter of Guillermo the Rich, she that wanders about the wolds here in the dress of a shepherdess.”","\"오늘 아침에 그 유명한 학생 목동 크리소스토모가 죽었다네. 소문으로는 부자 기예르모의 딸, 양치기 처녀 차림으로 이 들판을 떠돌아다니는 그 마을의 마녀 같은 계집한테 반해서 죽었다는구먼.\""]],[["“You mean Marcela?”","\"마르셀라 말인가?\""],["said one.","하나가 물었다."]],[["“Her I mean,”","\"바로 그 계집 말이야.\""],["answered the goatherd; “and the best of it is, he has directed in his will that he is to be buried in the fields like a Moor, and at the foot of the rock where the Cork-tree spring is, because, as the story goes (and they say he himself said so), that was the place where he first saw her.","염소지기가 대답했다. \"게다가 더 기막힌 건, 유언으로 자기를 무어인처럼 들판에, 코르크나무 샘이 있는 바위 밑에 묻으라고 일러 두었다는 거야."],["And he has also left other directions which the clergy of the village say should not and must not be obeyed because they savour of paganism.","전해지는 이야기로는(그가 직접 그렇게 말했다더군) 거기가 그 계집을 처음 본 자리였대. 게다가 마을 신부님들이 이교도 냄새가 난다며 따라서는 안 되고 따를 수도 없다고 하는 다른 분부들도 남겨 두었다네."],["To all which his great friend Ambrosio the student, he who, like him, also went dressed as a shepherd, replies that everything must be done without any omission according to the directions left by Chrysostom, and about this the village is all in commotion; however, report says that, after all, what Ambrosio and all the shepherds his friends desire will be done, and to-morrow they are coming to bury him with great ceremony where I said.","이 모든 일에 대해, 그처럼 양치기 차림으로 다니던 그의 절친한 벗 학생 암브로시오는 크리소스토모가 남긴 분부대로 하나도 빠짐없이 다 해야 한다고 대답하니, 이 일로 마을이 온통 들끓고 있어. 그래도 소문으로는 결국 암브로시오와 그의 벗인 목동들이 바라는 대로 될 것이고, 내일 내가 말한 그곳에 그를 성대히 묻으러 온다더군."],["I am sure it will be something worth seeing; at least I will not fail to go and see it even if I knew I should not return to the village to-morrow.”","분명 한번 볼 만한 일일 거야. 적어도 나는 내일 마을로 돌아오지 못한다 해도 꼭 가서 봐야겠어.\""]],[["“We will do the same,”","\"우리도 그렇게 하세.\""],["answered the goatherds, “and cast lots to see who must stay to mind the goats of all.”","염소지기들이 대답했다. \"그리고 누가 남아서 모두의 염소를 지킬지 제비를 뽑세나.\""]],[["“Thou sayest well, Pedro,” said one, “though there will be no need of taking that trouble, for I will stay behind for all; and don’t suppose it is virtue or want of curiosity in me; it is that the splinter that ran into my foot the other day will not let me walk.”","\"옳은 말일세, 페드로.\" 하나가 말했다. \"하지만 그런 수고를 할 필요는 없겠네. 내가 다들 대신해서 남을 테니까. 그렇다고 이게 무슨 덕이 있어서나 호기심이 없어서라고 여기지는 말게. 며칠 전 발에 박힌 가시 때문에 걸을 수가 없어서 그러는 거니까.\""]],[["“For all that, we thank thee,”","\"그래도 고맙네.\""],["answered Pedro.","페드로가 대답했다."]],[["Don Quixote asked Pedro to tell him who the dead man was and who the shepherdess, to which Pedro replied that all he knew was that the dead man was a wealthy gentleman belonging to a village in those mountains, who had been a student at Salamanca for many years, at the end of which he returned to his village with the reputation of being very learned and deeply read.","돈키호테는 페드로에게 그 죽은 자가 누구이며 양치기 처녀는 또 누구인지 일러 달라고 청했다. 이에 페드로는, 자기가 아는 거라곤 그 죽은 자가 저 산속 어느 마을에 속한 부유한 신사이며, 여러 해 동안 살라망카에서 공부하다가 그 끝에 학식이 매우 깊고 박식하다는 평판을 안고 마을로 돌아왔다는 것뿐이라고 대답했다."],["“Above all, they said, he was learned in the science of the stars and of what went on yonder in the heavens and the sun and the moon, for he told us of the cris of the sun and moon to exact time.”","\"무엇보다도, 그분은 별들의 학문과 저 위 하늘과 해와 달에서 일어나는 일에 밝았다고들 합디다. 우리한테 해와 달의 그릇침을 정확한 시각까지 일러 주셨으니까요.\""]],[["“Eclipse it is called, friend, not cris, the darkening of those two luminaries,” said Don Quixote; but Pedro, not troubling himself with trifles, went on with his story, saying, “Also he foretold when the year was going to be one of abundance or estility.”","\"그건 식(蝕)이라고 부른다네, 친구. 그릇침이 아니라. 그 두 빛나는 것이 어두워지는 걸 말일세.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. 그러나 페드로는 그런 사소한 것에 마음 쓰지 않고 이야기를 계속 이어 갔다. \"그분은 또 그해가 풍년이 될지 흉헌이 될지도 미리 알려 주셨지요.\""]],[["“Sterility, you mean,”","\"흉년 말이겠지.\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“Sterility or estility,” answered Pedro, “it is all the same in the end. And I can tell you that by this his father and friends who believed him grew very rich because they did as he advised them, bidding them ‘sow barley this year, not wheat; this year you may sow pulse and not barley; the next there will be a full oil crop, and the three following not a drop will be got.’”","\"흉년이든 흉헌이든.\" 페드로가 대답했다. \"결국 다 마찬가지 아닙니까. 그래서 말씀드리는데, 그분 말씀을 믿은 아버님과 친구들은 그분이 일러 준 대로 했기에 큰 부자가 되었답니다. '올해는 밀 말고 보리를 심어라. 올해는 보리 말고 콩을 심어도 된다. 내년에는 기름이 넘치도록 날 것이고, 그다음 삼 년은 한 방울도 얻지 못할 것이다' 하고 일러 주셨거든요.\""]],[["“That science is called astrology,”","\"그 학문은 점성술이라 부른다네.\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“I do not know what it is called,”","\"그게 뭐라 불리는지는 모르겠습니다만.\""],["replied Pedro, “but I know that he knew all this and more besides.","페드로가 대답했다. \"그분이 이걸 다 알고, 그밖에도 더 많이 알고 계셨다는 건 압니다."],["But, to make an end, not many months had passed after he returned from Salamanca, when one day he appeared dressed as a shepherd with his crook and sheepskin, having put off the long gown he wore as a scholar; and at the same time his great friend, Ambrosio by name, who had been his companion in his studies, took to the shepherd’s dress with him.","어쨌든 이야기를 끝맺자면, 그분이 살라망카에서 돌아온 지 몇 달 지나지 않은 어느 날, 학자로서 입던 긴 가운을 벗어 던지고 지팡이와 양가죽을 걸친 목동 차림으로 나타나셨답니다. 그와 동시에 그분의 절친한 벗으로, 함께 공부했던 암브로시오라는 이도 그분을 따라 목동 차림을 했지요."],["I forgot to say that Chrysostom, who is dead, was a great man for writing verses, so much so that he made carols for Christmas Eve, and plays for Corpus Christi, which the young men of our village acted, and all said they were excellent.","깜빡 잊고 말씀 안 드렸는데, 죽은 크리소스토모는 시 짓는 데 뛰어난 분이라, 성탄 전야의 캐럴이며 성체 축일의 연극까지 지었고, 우리 마을 젊은이들이 그걸 공연했는데 다들 훌륭하다고 칭찬했답니다. 마을 사람들이 그 두 학자가 그토록 느닷없이 목동 차림으로 나타난 걸 보고는, 얼이 빠져서 무엇 때문에 그런 엄청난 변화를 했는지 짐작조차 못 했지요."],["When the villagers saw the two scholars so unexpectedly appearing in shepherd’s dress, they were lost in wonder, and could not guess what had led them to make so extraordinary a change.","이 무렵에 우리 크리소스토모의 아버님이 돌아가셨고, 그분은 동산이며 토지며, 적지 않은 소와 양 떼, 그리고 큰돈에 이르기까지 막대한 재산을 물려받게 되었습니다. 이 젊은이가 그 모든 것의 거리낌 없는 주인이 된 셈인데, 사실 그분은 그 모든 것을 받을 만한 분이었어요."],["About this time the father of our Chrysostom died, and he was left heir to a large amount of property in chattels as well as in land, no small number of cattle and sheep, and a large sum of money, of all of which the young man was left dissolute owner, and indeed he was deserving of it all, for he was a very good comrade, and kind-hearted, and a friend of worthy folk, and had a countenance like a benediction.","더없이 좋은 동무였고, 마음씨가 따뜻했으며, 점잖은 이들의 벗이었고, 얼굴은 축복처럼 환했으니까요. 머지않아, 그분이 옷차림을 바꾼 데는 다른 까닭이 아니라, 아까 우리 젊은이가 말한 그 양치기 처녀 마르셀라를 좇아 이 황무지를 떠돌기 위해서였음이 알려졌지요."],["Presently it came to be known that he had changed his dress with no other object than to wander about these wastes after that shepherdess Marcela our lad mentioned a while ago, with whom the deceased Chrysostom had fallen in love.","죽은 크리소스토모가 그 처녀한테 반해 버렸던 겁니다. 이제 그 계집이 누구인지 말씀드려야겠습니다."],["And I must tell you now, for it is well you should know it, who this girl is; perhaps, and even without any perhaps, you will not have heard anything like it all the days of your life, though you should live more years than sarna.”","아셔 두시는 게 좋을 테니까요. 어쩌면, 아니 어쩌면이랄 것도 없이, 사르나보다 더 오래 사신다 해도 평생 이런 이야기는 못 들어 보셨을 겁니다.\""]],[["“Say Sarra,”","\"사라라고 하게.\""],["said Don Quixote, unable to endure the goatherd’s confusion of words.","염소지기의 뒤죽박죽 말을 견디지 못한 돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“The sarna lives long enough,”","\"사르나도 충분히 오래 삽니다.\""],["answered Pedro; “and if, señor, you must go finding fault with words at every step, we shall not make an end of it this twelvemonth.”","페드로가 대답했다. \"그리고 나리, 한 걸음마다 말꼬리를 잡으려 드시면 우리는 올 한 해가 다 가도록 이야기를 끝내지 못할 겁니다.\""]],[["“Pardon me, friend,” said Don Quixote; “but, as there is such a difference between sarna and Sarra, I told you of it; however, you have answered very rightly, for sarna lives longer than Sarra: so continue your story, and I will not object any more to anything.”","\"미안하네, 친구.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"다만 사르나와 사라는 그토록 다르기에 일러 준 것일세. 그래도 자네 대답은 참으로 옳네. 사르나가 사라보다 오래 사니까. 그러니 이야기를 계속하게. 더는 어떤 것에도 토를 달지 않겠네.\""]],[["“I say then, my dear sir,” said the goatherd, “that in our village there was a farmer even richer than the father of Chrysostom, who was named Guillermo, and upon whom God bestowed, over and above great wealth, a daughter at whose birth her mother died, the most respected woman there was in this neighbourhood; I fancy I can see her now with that countenance which had the sun on one side and the moon on the other; and moreover active, and kind to the poor, for which I trust that at the present moment her soul is in bliss with God in the other world. Her husband Guillermo died of grief at the death of so good a wife, leaving his daughter Marcela, a child and rich, to the care of an uncle of hers, a priest and prebendary in our village. The girl grew up with such beauty that it reminded us of her mother’s, which was very great, and yet it was thought that the daughter’s would exceed it; and so when she reached the age of fourteen to fifteen years nobody beheld her but blessed God that had made her so beautiful, and the greater number were in love with her past redemption. Her uncle kept her in great seclusion and retirement, but for all that the fame of her great beauty spread so that, as well for it as for her great wealth, her uncle was asked, solicited, and importuned, to give her in marriage not only by those of our town but of those many leagues round, and by the persons of highest quality in them. But he, being a good Christian man, though he desired to give her in marriage at once, seeing her to be old enough, was unwilling to do so without her consent, not that he had any eye to the gain and profit which the custody of the girl’s property brought him while he put off her marriage; and, faith, this was said in praise of the good priest in more than one set in the town. For I would have you know, Sir Errant, that in these little villages everything is talked about and everything is carped at, and rest assured, as I am, that the priest must be over and above good who forces his parishioners to speak well of him, especially in villages.”","* 사르나(sarna): 스페인어로 옴, 피부병을 뜻함. 페드로는 구약성서의 인물 사라(Sarra)를 사르나로 잘못 발음하고 있다. \"그러면 말씀드리지요, 나리.\" 염소지기가 말했다. \"우리 마을에 크리소스토모의 아버님보다도 더 부유한 농부가 있었는데, 이름이 기예르모였습니다. 하느님은 그에게 큰 재산 말고도 딸을 하나 점지해 주셨는데, 그 아이를 낳다가 어미가 죽었지요. 이 근방에서 가장 존경받던 여인이었답니다. 한쪽엔 해를, 다른 한쪽엔 달을 단 듯한 그 얼굴이 지금도 눈에 선합니다. 게다가 부지런하고 가난한 이들에게 친절했으니, 지금쯤 그 영혼은 저세상에서 하느님과 함께 복락을 누리고 있으리라 믿습니다. 그 남편 기예르모는 그토록 착한 아내의 죽음을 슬퍼하다 세상을 떠나면서, 어리고 부유한 딸 마르셀라를 자기 처삼촌 되는, 우리 마을의 신부이자 성당 참사회원에게 맡겼지요. 그 아이는 어찌나 아리땁게 자랐는지, 그 대단했던 어미의 미모를 떠올리게 했고, 그런데도 딸의 미모가 어미를 넘어서리라는 말까지 나왔답니다. 그래서 그 애가 열네댓 살에 이르자, 그 아이를 보는 사람마다 이토록 아름답게 빚으신 하느님을 찬미했고, 대다수는 도저히 헤어날 수 없을 만큼 그 애한테 반해 버렸지요. 삼촌은 그 애를 깊이 들어앉혀 두고 바깥출입을 삼가게 했지만, 그래도 그 대단한 미모의 명성이 어찌나 퍼졌던지, 그 미모 때문에든 그 막대한 재산 때문에든, 우리 고장 사람들뿐 아니라 여러 십 리 밖 사람들까지, 게다가 그중에서도 가장 지체 높은 이들까지 그 애를 아내로 달라고 삼촌에게 청하고 졸라 대고 들볶았답니다. 하지만 삼촌은 착실한 기독교인인지라, 그 애가 시집갈 만한 나이가 된 걸 보고 당장이라도 시집보내고 싶었지만, 본인의 승낙 없이는 그러려 하지 않았지요. 결혼을 미루는 동안 그 애 재산을 맡아 두며 얻는 이득과 잇속에 눈독을 들였던 게 결코 아니었습니다. 정말이지, 이 점은 마을 곳곳에서 한자리 건너 그 착한 신부님을 칭송하는 말로 회자되었답니다. 편력 기사 나리, 아셔 두셔야 할 게, 이런 작은 마을에서는 무엇이든 입에 오르고 무엇이든 흠잡히는 법이라, 저처럼 나리도 믿어 두시는 게 좋습니다. 교구민들이 자기를 좋게 말하도록 만드는 신부라면, 그야말로 더없이 착한 사람일 수밖에 없다는 걸요. 더구나 마을에서야 말할 것도 없고요.\""]],[["“That is the truth,”","\"그건 사실일세.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “but go on, for the story is very good, and you, good Pedro, tell it with very good grace.”","\"그러니 계속하게. 이야기가 참으로 좋고, 자네, 착한 페드로도 참으로 멋들어지게 들려주는구먼.\""]],[["“May that of the Lord not be wanting to me,”","\"주님의 은총이 제게 모자라지 않기를 빕니다.\" 페드로가 말했다."],["said Pedro; “that is the one to have.","\"꼭 지녀야 할 것이 바로 그것이지요."],["To proceed; you must know that though the uncle put before his niece and described to her the qualities of each one in particular of the many who had asked her in marriage, begging her to marry and make a choice according to her own taste, she never gave any other answer than that she had no desire to marry just yet, and that being so young she did not think herself fit to bear the burden of matrimony.","이야기를 계속하자면, 아셔 두셔야 할 게, 삼촌이 청혼한 그 많은 이들 하나하나의 됨됨이를 조카딸 앞에 늘어놓고 일러 주면서, 제발 자기 마음에 드는 이를 골라 시집을 가라고 사정했건만, 그 애는 늘 똑같은 대답만 했답니다. 아직은 시집갈 마음이 없고, 너무 어려서 혼인의 짐을 감당할 만하다고 여기지 않는다고요."],["At these, to all appearance, reasonable excuses that she made, her uncle ceased to urge her, and waited till she was somewhat more advanced in age and could mate herself to her own liking.","겉보기에는 그럴듯한 이런 핑계를 대니, 삼촌은 그 애를 더 다그치기를 그만두고, 좀 더 나이가 들어 제 마음에 드는 짝을 만날 수 있을 때까지 기다리기로 했지요."],["For, said he--and he said quite right--parents are not to settle children in life against their will.","부모가 자식을 그 뜻을 거슬러 가며 짝지어 줄 일은 아니라고 했으니까요. 참으로 옳은 말이었습니다."],["But when one least looked for it, lo and behold!","그런데 누구도 예기치 못한 어느 날, 보십시오!"],["one day the demure Marcela makes her appearance turned shepherdess; and, in spite of her uncle and all those of the town that strove to dissuade her, took to going a-field with the other shepherd-lasses of the village, and tending her own flock.","그 얌전하던 마르셀라가 양치기 처녀로 변신해서 나타난 겁니다. 삼촌이며 그러지 말라고 애써 말리던 온 마을 사람들에도 아랑곳없이, 마을의 다른 양치기 처녀들과 함께 들판에 나가 제 양 떼를 돌보기 시작했지요."],["And so, since she appeared in public, and her beauty came to be seen openly, I could not well tell you how many rich youths, gentlemen and peasants, have adopted the costume of Chrysostom, and go about these fields making love to her.","그렇게 그 애가 사람들 앞에 모습을 드러내고 그 미모가 훤히 드러나자, 얼마나 많은 부잣집 젊은이들이, 신사며 농사꾼이며 할 것 없이 크리소스토모의 차림새를 따라 입고는 이 들판을 돌아다니며 그 애한테 구애했는지 이루 다 말씀드릴 수 없을 정돕니다."],["One of these, as has been already said, was our deceased friend, of whom they say that he did not love but adore her.","그중 하나가, 앞서 말씀드렸듯, 우리의 죽은 벗이었는데, 사람들 말로는 그분이 그 애를 사랑한 게 아니라 흠모했다더군요."],["But you must not suppose, because Marcela chose a life of such liberty and independence, and of so little or rather no retirement, that she has given any occasion, or even the semblance of one, for disparagement of her purity and modesty; on the contrary, such and so great is the vigilance with which she watches over her honour, that of all those that court and woo her not one has boasted, or can with truth boast, that she has given him any hope however small of obtaining his desire.","하지만, 마르셀라가 그토록 자유롭고 거리낌 없는 삶을, 바깥출입을 삼가기는커녕 도무지 삼가지 않는 삶을 택했다고 해서, 그 애가 자기 정절과 정숙함을 헐뜯을 만한 빌미를, 아니 그런 빌미 비슷한 것조차 준 적이 있다고 여기시면 안 됩니다. 오히려 그 애가 제 명예를 지키는 경계심이 어찌나 대단한지, 그 애한테 구애하고 추근대는 모든 이들 가운데 누구도, 제 바람을 이루리라는 희망을 그 애가 아무리 작게나마 주었노라고 으스댄 적이 없고, 또 사실대로 으스댈 수도 없답니다."],["For although she does not avoid or shun the society and conversation of the shepherds, and treats them courteously and kindly, should any one of them come to declare his intention to her, though it be one as proper and holy as that of matrimony, she flings him from her like a catapult.","그 애는 목동들과 어울리고 이야기 나누기를 피하거나 꺼리지 않고, 그들을 정중하고 친절하게 대하지만, 그들 가운데 누구라도 제 속내를, 그것이 혼인처럼 떳떳하고 거룩한 것일지라도 그 애한테 털어놓으려 들면, 투석기로 쏘듯 그자를 내쳐 버립니다. 이런 성미 탓에 그 애는 이 고장에 역병이 든 것보다 더 큰 해를 끼치고 있지요."],["And with this kind of disposition she does more harm in this country than if the plague had got into it, for her affability and her beauty draw on the hearts of those that associate with her to love her and to court her, but her scorn and her frankness bring them to the brink of despair; and so they know not what to say save to proclaim her aloud cruel and hard-hearted, and other names of the same sort which well describe the nature of her character; and if you should remain here any time, señor, you would hear these hills and valleys resounding with the laments of the rejected ones who pursue her.","그 애의 상냥함과 미모는 곁에 어울리는 이들의 마음을 끌어다 그 애를 사랑하고 구애하게 만들지만, 그 애의 멸시와 솔직함은 그자들을 절망의 벼랑 끝으로 내모니까요. 그래서 그자들은 그 애를 두고 큰 소리로 모질다느니 매정하다느니, 그 성품을 잘 드러내는 그런 부류의 다른 이름들로 외쳐 대는 것 말고는 무어라 할 말을 모릅니다."],["Not far from this there is a spot where there are a couple of dozen of tall beeches, and there is not one of them but has carved and written on its smooth bark the name of Marcela, and above some a crown carved on the same tree as though her lover would say more plainly that Marcela wore and deserved that of all human beauty.","나리, 여기에 얼마간 머무시면, 그 애를 좇다 내쳐진 이들의 탄식으로 이 언덕과 골짜기가 울려 퍼지는 소리를 들으실 겁니다. 여기서 멀지 않은 곳에 키 큰 너도밤나무가 스무 그루 남짓 있는데, 그 매끈한 껍질마다 마르셀라의 이름이 새겨지고 적혀 있지 않은 게 하나도 없고, 몇몇 나무 위에는 같은 나무에 왕관까지 새겨져 있어서, 마치 그 애를 사랑하는 이가, 마르셀라야말로 인간의 모든 미모의 왕관을 쓰고 또 받을 만하다고 더욱 또렷이 말하려는 듯하답니다."],["Here one shepherd is sighing, there another is lamenting; there love songs are heard, here despairing elegies.","여기서는 한 목동이 한숨짓고, 저기서는 또 한 목동이 탄식하지요. 저기서는 사랑 노래가, 여기서는 절망의 비가가 들려옵니다."],["One will pass all the hours of the night seated at the foot of some oak or rock, and there, without having closed his weeping eyes, the sun finds him in the morning bemused and bereft of sense; and another without relief or respite to his sighs, stretched on the burning sand in the full heat of the sultry summer noontide, makes his appeal to the compassionate heavens, and over one and the other, over these and all, the beautiful Marcela triumphs free and careless.","어떤 이는 참나무나 바위 밑에 앉아 밤을 꼬박 지새우고, 우는 눈을 감지도 못한 채로 아침 햇살이 멍하니 넋 나간 그자를 비추기도 하지요. 또 어떤 이는 한숨에 잠시의 쉼도 없이, 무더운 한여름 정오의 이글거리는 열기 속 타는 모래밭에 드러누워 자비로운 하늘에 호소하기도 합니다."],["And all of us that know her are waiting to see what her pride will come to, and who is to be the happy man that will succeed in taming a nature so formidable and gaining possession of a beauty so supreme.","그렇게 이 사람 저 사람, 이들 모두를 짓밟으며 아리따운 마르셀라는 자유롭고 무심하게 군림하고 있지요. 그 애를 아는 우리 모두는, 그 도도함이 어디까지 가는지, 그토록 무시무시한 성정을 길들이고 그토록 빼어난 미모를 손에 넣게 될 행복한 사내가 누구일지 지켜보고 있는 중입니다."],["All that I have told you being such well-established truth, I am persuaded that what they say of the cause of Chrysostom’s death, as our lad told us, is the same.","제가 말씀드린 모든 게 이토록 확실한 사실인 만큼, 아까 우리 젊은이가 들려준, 크리소스토모의 죽음의 까닭에 대한 사람들의 말도 그대로 사실이리라 믿습니다. 그러니 나리, 내일 그분의 장례에 꼭 참석하시길 권합니다."],["And so I advise you, señor, fail not to be present to-morrow at his burial, which will be well worth seeing, for Chrysostom had many friends, and it is not half a league from this place to where he directed he should be buried.”","한번 볼 만할 거예요. 크리소스토모한테는 벗이 많았고, 그분이 묻히라고 일러 둔 곳도 여기서 반 리도 안 되는 거리니까요.\""]],[["“I will make a point of it,”","\"꼭 그리하겠네.\""],["said Don Quixote, “and I thank you for the pleasure you have given me by relating so interesting a tale.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그토록 흥미로운 이야기를 들려주어 즐거움을 주었으니 고맙구먼.\""]],[["“Oh,” said the goatherd, “I do not know even the half of what has happened to the lovers of Marcela, but perhaps to-morrow we may fall in with some shepherd on the road who can tell us; and now it will be well for you to go and sleep under cover, for the night air may hurt your wound, though with the remedy I have applied to you there is no fear of an untoward result.”","\"오,\" 염소지기가 말했다. \"마르셀라를 사랑한 이들한테 일어난 일이라면 저는 그 절반도 모릅니다. 하지만 어쩌면 내일 길에서 우리한테 들려줄 만한 목동을 마주칠지도 모르지요. 자, 이제 나리는 지붕 밑에 들어가 주무시는 게 좋겠습니다. 밤공기가 상처에 해로울 수도 있으니까요. 제가 발라 드린 약이 있으니 잘못될 염려야 없습니다만.\""]],[["Sancho Panza, who was wishing the goatherd’s loquacity at the devil, on his part begged his master to go into Pedro’s hut to sleep.","염소지기의 수다를 악마에게나 보내 버리고 싶던 산초 판사는, 제 나름으로 주인에게 페드로의 오두막에 들어가 주무시라고 청했다."],["He did so, and passed all the rest of the night in thinking of his lady Dulcinea, in imitation of the lovers of Marcela.","돈키호테는 그리하여, 마르셀라의 연인들을 본받아 남은 밤을 온통 그의 여인 둘시네아를 생각하며 보냈다."],["Sancho Panza settled himself between Rocinante and his ass, and slept, not like a lover who had been discarded, but like a man who had been soundly kicked.","산초 판사는 로시난테와 제 나귀 사이에 자리를 잡고는, 버림받은 연인처럼이 아니라 흠씬 걷어차인 사내처럼 잠들었다."]],[["# CHAPTER XIII. IN WHICH IS ENDED THE STORY OF THE SHEPHERDESS MARCELA, WITH OTHER INCIDENTS","# CHAPTER XIII."]],[["Bit hardly had day begun to show itself through the balconies of the east, when five of the six goatherds came to rouse Don Quixote and tell him that if he was still of a mind to go and see the famous burial of Chrysostom they would bear him company.","날이 동녘의 발코니 사이로 막 모습을 드러내기 시작할 무렵, 여섯 명의 염소지기 가운데 다섯이 돈키호테를 깨우러 와서, 그가 아직도 크리소스토모의 그 유명한 장례식을 보러 갈 마음이 있다면 자기들이 동행하겠노라고 말했다."],["Don Quixote, who desired nothing better, rose and ordered Sancho to saddle and pannel at once, which he did with all despatch, and with the same they all set out forthwith.","그보다 더 바랄 것이 없던 돈키호테는 일어나 산초에게 당장 안장과 길마를 얹으라 일렀고, 산초는 부지런히 그 일을 마쳤으며, 곧 모두가 함께 길을 나섰다."],["They had not gone a quarter of a league when at the meeting of two paths they saw coming towards them some six shepherds dressed in black sheepskins and with their heads crowned with garlands of cypress and bitter oleander.","그들이 채 사분의 일 레과도 못 갔을 때, 두 갈래 길이 만나는 곳에서 검은 양가죽 옷을 입고 사이프러스와 쓴 협죽도로 엮은 화관을 머리에 쓴 여섯쯤 되는 양치기가 자기들 쪽으로 오는 것을 보았다."],["Each of them carried a stout holly staff in his hand, and along with them there came two men of quality on horseback in handsome travelling dress, with three servants on foot accompanying them.","그들은 저마다 손에 굵직한 호랑가시나무 지팡이를 들었고, 그들과 더불어 단정한 여행복 차림을 한 말 탄 신사 두 사람이 왔으며, 걸어서 그들을 따르는 하인 셋도 함께 있었다."],["Courteous salutations were exchanged on meeting, and inquiring one of the other which way each party was going, they learned that all were bound for the scene of the burial, so they went on all together.","만나면서 정중한 인사가 오갔고, 서로 어느 쪽으로 가는 길이냐고 묻고는 모두가 장례식이 치러지는 곳을 향하고 있음을 알게 되어, 그들은 다 같이 길을 이어 갔다."]],[["One of those on horseback addressing his companion said to him, “It seems to me, Señor Vivaldo, that we may reckon as well spent the delay we shall incur in seeing this remarkable funeral, for remarkable it cannot but be judging by the strange things these shepherds have told us, of both the dead shepherd and homicide shepherdess.”","말 탄 사람 하나가 자기 일행을 향해 이렇게 말했다. \"비발도 님, 이 보기 드문 장례식을 보느라 지체하는 것은 제대로 쓴 시간이라 쳐도 좋을 듯합니다. 저 양치기들이 들려준, 죽은 양치기와 사람 잡은 양치기 처녀에 관한 그 기이한 이야기들로 미루어 보건대, 보기 드문 장례가 아닐 수 없을 테니까요.\""]],[["“So I think too,”","\"저도 그리 생각합니다.\""],["replied Vivaldo, “and I would delay not to say a day, but four, for the sake of seeing it.”","비발도가 대답했다. \"그것을 보기 위해서라면 하루가 아니라 나흘이라도 지체하겠습니다.\""]],[["Don Quixote asked them what it was they had heard of Marcela and Chrysostom.","돈키호테는 그들에게 마르셀라와 크리소스토모에 관해 무엇을 들었느냐고 물었다."],["The traveller answered that the same morning they had met these shepherds, and seeing them dressed in this mournful fashion they had asked them the reason of their appearing in such a guise; which one of them gave, describing the strange behaviour and beauty of a shepherdess called Marcela, and the loves of many who courted her, together with the death of that Chrysostom to whose burial they were going.","여행객은 바로 그날 아침 이 양치기들을 만났는데, 그들이 이렇게 상복 차림을 한 것을 보고 어찌하여 그런 모양새로 나섰는지 까닭을 물었다고 대답했다. 그러자 그들 중 하나가 그 까닭을 일러 주며, 마르셀라라는 양치기 처녀의 기이한 행실과 아름다움, 그녀에게 구애한 숱한 이들의 사랑, 그리고 자기들이 지금 장례식에 가고 있는 저 크리소스토모의 죽음을 이야기해 주었다는 것이었다."],["In short, he repeated all that Pedro had related to Don Quixote.","요컨대 그는 페드로가 돈키호테에게 들려준 것을 모두 그대로 되풀이했다."]],[["This conversation dropped, and another was commenced by him who was called Vivaldo asking Don Quixote what was the reason that led him to go armed in that fashion in a country so peaceful.","이 대화가 끊어지자, 비발도라 불리는 사람이 또 다른 대화를 시작하여, 이토록 평화로운 고장에서 어찌하여 그런 차림으로 무장하고 다니는 까닭이 무엇이냐고 돈키호테에게 물었다. 이에 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["To which Don Quixote replied, “The pursuit of my calling does not allow or permit me to go in any other fashion; easy life, enjoyment, and repose were invented for soft courtiers, but toil, unrest, and arms were invented and made for those alone whom the world calls knights-errant, of whom I, though unworthy, am the least of all.”","\"내 천직을 좇는 일은 다른 어떤 차림으로도 다니는 것을 허락하지도 용납하지도 않소이다. 안락한 생활과 향락과 휴식은 나약한 궁정인들을 위해 만들어진 것이고, 고생과 불안과 무기는 오로지 세상이 편력 기사라 부르는 이들만을 위해 생겨나고 만들어진 것이니, 나는 비록 그 자격이 없으나 그들 가운데 가장 보잘것없는 자올시다.\""]],[["The instant they heard this all set him down as mad, and the better to settle the point and discover what kind of madness his was, Vivaldo proceeded to ask him what knights-errant meant.","이 말을 듣자마자 모두가 그를 미친 사람으로 단정했고, 그 점을 더 확실히 매듭짓고 그의 광기가 어떤 종류인지 알아보고자, 비발도는 편력 기사가 무엇을 뜻하는지 그에게 물어 나갔다."]],[["“Have not your worships,” replied Don Quixote, “read the annals and histories of England, in which are recorded the famous deeds of King Arthur, whom we in our popular Castilian invariably call King Artus, with regard to whom it is an ancient tradition, and commonly received all over that kingdom of Great Britain, that this king did not die, but was changed by magic art into a raven, and that in process of time he is to return to reign and recover his kingdom and sceptre; for which reason it cannot be proved that from that time to this any Englishman ever killed a raven? Well, then, in the time of this good king that famous order of chivalry of the Knights of the Round Table was instituted, and the amour of Don Lancelot of the Lake with the Queen Guinevere occurred, precisely as is there related, the go-between and confidante therein being the highly honourable dame Quintanona, whence came that ballad so well known and widely spread in our Spain--","\"그대들께서는,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"영국의 연대기와 역사를 읽어 본 적이 없으시오? 거기에는 우리가 평범한 카스티야 말로 으레 아르투스 왕이라 부르는 아서 왕의 그 유명한 행적이 기록되어 있는데, 그분에 관해서는 예부터 전해 내려오고 저 대브리튼 왕국 전역에 두루 받아들여진 전설이 있으니, 이 왕은 죽은 것이 아니라 마법의 술법으로 까마귀가 되었으며, 세월이 흐른 뒤 돌아와 다시 다스리며 자신의 왕국과 왕홀을 되찾으리라는 것이외다. 그런 까닭에 그때부터 이날까지 영국인 누구도 까마귀를 죽인 일이 없다는 것은 증명할 수 없는 사실이오. 그런데 바로 이 선량한 왕의 시대에, 저 유명한 원탁의 기사단이라는 기사회가 세워졌고, 호반의 기사 란슬롯과 왕비 귀네비어의 사랑이 거기 적힌 그대로 일어났으니, 그 사이를 잇고 비밀을 나눈 이가 바로 그 더없이 존귀한 부인 킨타뇨나였소. 거기서 우리 스페인에 그토록 잘 알려지고 널리 퍼진 그 노래가 나왔으니—"]],[["O never surely was there knight So served by hand of dame, As served was he Sir Lancelot hight When he from Britain came—","오, 일찍이 그 어떤 기사도 부인의 손에 이만큼 시중받지 못하였네, 란슬롯 경이 받은 그 시중만큼은, 그가 브리튼에서 왔을 적에—"]],[["with all the sweet and delectable course of his achievements in love and war.","그의 사랑과 무용에 관한 온갖 달콤하고 아름다운 행적들이 함께 어우러져 있소."],["Handed down from that time, then, this order of chivalry went on extending and spreading itself over many and various parts of the world; and in it, famous and renowned for their deeds, were the mighty Amadis of Gaul with all his sons and descendants to the fifth generation, and the valiant Felixmarte of Hircania, and the never sufficiently praised Tirante el Blanco, and in our own days almost we have seen and heard and talked with the invincible knight Don Belianis of Greece.","그리하여 그때부터 전해 내려온 이 기사회는 세상의 많고 다양한 곳으로 점점 뻗어 나가고 퍼져 나갔으니, 그 안에서 행적으로 이름을 떨치고 명성을 얻은 이들로는 다섯 대에 걸친 그 모든 아들과 후손을 거느린 강대한 골의 아마디스가 있었고, 용맹한 이르카니아의 펠릭스마르테가 있었으며, 아무리 칭송해도 모자란 티란테 엘 블랑코가 있었고, 거의 우리 당대에 와서는 그리스의 무적 기사 돈 벨리아니스를 우리가 보고 그 이야기를 듣고 더불어 말까지 나누다시피 하였소."],["This, then, sirs, is to be a knight-errant, and what I have spoken of is the order of his chivalry, of which, as I have already said, I, though a sinner, have made profession, and what the aforesaid knights professed that same do I profess, and so I go through these solitudes and wilds seeking adventures, resolved in soul to oppose my arm and person to the most perilous that fortune may offer me in aid of the weak and needy.”","자, 그러니 여러분, 이것이 바로 편력 기사가 된다는 것이며, 내가 말씀드린 것이 그 기사회의 법도이니, 이미 말한 대로 나는 비록 죄 많은 자이나 그것을 직분으로 삼았으며, 앞서 말한 기사들이 직분으로 삼은 그것을 나 또한 직분으로 삼고 있소. 그리하여 나는 이 외딴곳들과 황야를 누비며 모험을 찾아다니고, 약하고 궁핍한 이들을 돕고자 운명이 내게 던져 줄 가장 위험한 일에 내 팔과 이 몸을 맞세우기로 마음속 깊이 결심하고 있는 것이외다.\""]],[["By these words of his the travellers were able to satisfy themselves of Don Quixote’s being out of his senses and of the form of madness that overmastered him, at which they felt the same astonishment that all felt on first becoming acquainted with it; and Vivaldo, who was a person of great shrewdness and of a lively temperament, in order to beguile the short journey which they said was required to reach the mountain, the scene of the burial, sought to give him an opportunity of going on with his absurdities.","이러한 그의 말로 여행객들은 돈키호테가 제정신이 아님과, 그를 사로잡은 광기의 형태가 어떤 것인지를 충분히 헤아릴 수 있었으니, 처음 그것을 알게 된 모두가 느낀 것과 똑같은 놀라움을 그들도 느꼈다. 그리고 매우 영리하고 활달한 기질의 사람이던 비발도는, 장례식이 치러지는 산까지 가는 데 필요하다던 그 짧은 여정의 무료함을 달래고자, 그가 계속해서 그 터무니없는 소리를 늘어놓을 거리를 마련해 주려 했다."],["So he said to him, “It seems to me, Señor Knight-errant, that your worship has made choice of one of the most austere professions in the world, and I imagine even that of the Carthusian monks is not so austere.”","그래서 그는 그에게 말했다. \"편력 기사 나리, 제 보기에 나리께서는 세상에서 가장 엄격한 직분 가운데 하나를 택하신 듯하니, 카르투지오회 수도사들의 직분조차 그토록 엄격하지는 않으리라 여겨집니다.\""]],[["“As austere it may perhaps be,”","\"그만큼 엄격할지는 모르나,\""],["replied our Don Quixote, “but so necessary for the world I am very much inclined to doubt.","우리의 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그것이 세상에 그만큼 필요한지는 나로서는 자못 의심스럽소."],["For, if the truth is to be told, the soldier who executes what his captain orders does no less than the captain himself who gives the order.","진실을 말하자면, 자기 대장이 명하는 것을 실행하는 병사가 그 명을 내리는 대장 자신보다 결코 못한 일을 하는 것이 아니기 때문이오."],["My meaning, is, that churchmen in peace and quiet pray to Heaven for the welfare of the world, but we soldiers and knights carry into effect what they pray for, defending it with the might of our arms and the edge of our swords, not under shelter but in the open air, a target for the intolerable rays of the sun in summer and the piercing frosts of winter.","내 말인즉, 성직자들은 평화롭고 고요한 가운데 세상의 안녕을 위해 하늘에 기도하나, 우리 병사와 기사들은 그들이 기도하는 바를 실제로 이루어 내니, 지붕 아래가 아니라 한데에서, 여름이면 견딜 수 없는 햇살의 표적이 되고 겨울이면 살을 에는 서리의 표적이 되어, 우리 팔의 힘과 칼날로 그것을 지켜 내는 것이외다."],["Thus are we God’s ministers on earth and the arms by which his justice is done therein.","이리하여 우리는 이 땅 위에서 하느님의 일꾼이며, 그분의 정의가 이 땅에서 이루어지게 하는 그 팔이오."],["And as the business of war and all that relates and belongs to it cannot be conducted without exceeding great sweat, toil, and exertion, it follows that those who make it their profession have undoubtedly more labour than those who in tranquil peace and quiet are engaged in praying to God to help the weak.","그리고 전쟁의 일과 그에 관계되고 속한 모든 것은 그지없이 큰 땀과 노고와 분투 없이는 치를 수 없는 것이니, 그것을 직분으로 삼은 자들이 고요한 평화와 안식 속에서 약한 이를 도와 달라고 하느님께 기도하는 자들보다 의심할 바 없이 더 많은 수고를 한다는 결론이 나오는 것이외다. 나는 편력 기사의 천직이 수도실에 든 수도사의 그것만큼 훌륭하다고 말하려는 것도 아니요, 그런 생각이 내 머릿속에 떠오른 것도 아니오."],["I do not mean to say, nor does it enter into my thoughts, that the knight-errant’s calling is as good as that of the monk in his cell; I would merely infer from what I endure myself that it is beyond a doubt a more laborious and a more belaboured one, a hungrier and thirstier, a wretcheder, raggeder, and lousier; for there is no reason to doubt that the knights-errant of yore endured much hardship in the course of their lives.","다만 내가 몸소 견뎌 내는 것에서 미루어 보아, 편력 기사의 천직이 의심할 바 없이 더 고되고 더 두들겨 맞는 것이며, 더 굶주리고 더 목마른 것이며, 더 비참하고 더 누더기를 걸치고 더 이가 들끓는 것임을 헤아릴 따름이오. 옛날의 편력 기사들이 그 한평생에 숱한 고초를 견뎠음은 의심할 까닭이 없기 때문이오."],["And if some of them by the might of their arms did rise to be emperors, in faith it cost them dear in the matter of blood and sweat; and if those who attained to that rank had not had magicians and sages to help them they would have been completely baulked in their ambition and disappointed in their hopes.”","그리고 그들 가운데 더러가 제 팔의 힘으로 황제의 자리에까지 올랐다 하더라도, 참으로 그것은 피와 땀으로 톡톡히 치른 대가였소. 또한 그 지위에 오른 이들에게 그들을 도와줄 마법사와 현자가 없었더라면, 그들은 야망에서 완전히 좌절하고 희망에서 실망하고 말았을 것이외다.\""]],[["“That is my own opinion,”","\"저 또한 그리 생각합니다.\" 여행객이 대답했다."],["replied the traveller; “but one thing among many others seems to me very wrong in knights-errant, and that is that when they find themselves about to engage in some mighty and perilous adventure in which there is manifest danger of losing their lives, they never at the moment of engaging in it think of commending themselves to God, as is the duty of every good Christian in like peril; instead of which they commend themselves to their ladies with as much devotion as if these were their gods, a thing which seems to me to savour somewhat of heathenism.”","\"허나 여러 가지 가운데 한 가지, 편력 기사들에게 제가 보기에 매우 잘못된 점이 있으니, 그것은 그들이 목숨을 잃을 위험이 빤히 보이는 어떤 대단하고 위험한 모험에 막 뛰어들려 할 때, 막상 그 일에 임하는 순간 같은 위험에 처한 선량한 그리스도교도라면 누구나 마땅히 해야 할 바, 곧 하느님께 자신을 의탁할 생각은 결코 하지 않고, 그 대신 마치 자기들의 여신이라도 되는 양 지극한 정성으로 자기 귀부인에게 자신을 의탁한다는 점입니다. 제게는 그것이 어딘지 이교도의 냄새를 풍기는 일로 여겨집니다.\""]],[["“Sir,”","\"이보시오,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["answered Don Quixote, “that cannot be on any account omitted, and the knight-errant would be disgraced who acted otherwise: for it is usual and customary in knight-errantry that the knight-errant, who on engaging in any great feat of arms has his lady before him, should turn his eyes towards her softly and lovingly, as though with them entreating her to favour and protect him in the hazardous venture he is about to undertake, and even though no one hear him, he is bound to say certain words between his teeth, commending himself to her with all his heart, and of this we have innumerable instances in the histories.","\"그것은 무슨 일이 있어도 빠뜨릴 수 없는 일이니, 그러지 않고 행동하는 편력 기사는 망신을 당할 것이오. 편력 기사도에서는 어떤 큰 무술의 위업에 임하면서 자기 귀부인을 눈앞에 모신 편력 기사가, 자신이 막 떠맡으려는 위태로운 일에서 자기를 어여삐 보아 지켜 달라고 그 눈으로 간청하듯, 부드럽고 사랑스럽게 그녀에게 눈길을 돌리는 것이 통례이자 관습이외다."],["Nor is it to be supposed from this that they are to omit commending themselves to God, for there will be time and opportunity for doing so while they are engaged in their task.”","또한 아무도 그의 말을 듣지 못한다 하더라도, 그는 이 사이로 어떤 말을 읊조리며 온 마음을 다해 그녀에게 자신을 의탁해야 하는 법이니, 이에 관한 사례는 그 역사들 속에 헤아릴 수 없이 많소. 그렇다고 이것으로 미루어 그들이 하느님께 자신을 의탁하는 것을 빠뜨린다고 여겨서는 아니 되니, 그 일에 임하는 동안에도 그리할 시간과 기회는 있을 것이기 때문이오.\""]],[["“For all that,” answered the traveller, “I feel some doubt still, because often I have read how words will arise between two knights-errant, and from one thing to another it comes about that their anger kindles and they wheel their horses round and take a good stretch of field, and then without any more ado at the top of their speed they come to the charge, and in mid-career they are wont to commend themselves to their ladies; and what commonly comes of the encounter is that one falls over the haunches of his horse pierced through and through by his antagonist’s lance, and as for the other, it is only by holding on to the mane of his horse that he can help falling to the ground; but I know not how the dead man had time to commend himself to God in the course of such rapid work as this; it would have been better if those words which he spent in commending himself to his lady in the midst of his career had been devoted to his duty and obligation as a Christian. Moreover, it is my belief that all knights-errant have not ladies to commend themselves to, for they are not all in love.”","\"그렇다 하더라도,\" 여행객이 대답했다. \"저는 여전히 얼마간 의심스럽습니다. 두 편력 기사 사이에 말다툼이 일어나, 이 일 저 일로 번지다가 분노에 불이 붙어 두 사람이 말머리를 돌려 들판을 한참 벌려 잡고는, 그다음 더 말할 것도 없이 전속력으로 마주 돌격하여, 그 한가운데서 자기 귀부인에게 자신을 의탁하곤 하는 대목을 자주 읽었기 때문입니다. 그런데 그 맞부딪침에서 흔히 벌어지는 일이란, 한 사람은 적의 창에 몸을 꿰뚫린 채 말 엉덩이 너머로 떨어지고, 다른 한 사람은 말갈기를 부여잡아야만 겨우 땅에 떨어지는 것을 면하는 일입니다. 한데 죽은 자가 이렇듯 순식간에 벌어진 일 가운데 어느 겨를에 하느님께 자신을 의탁할 시간이 있었는지 저로서는 모르겠습니다. 돌격 한복판에서 귀부인에게 자신을 의탁하느라 쓴 그 말들을, 차라리 그리스도교도로서의 본분과 의무에 바쳤더라면 더 나았을 것입니다. 게다가 제 믿음으로는, 편력 기사가 모두 자신을 의탁할 귀부인을 둔 것은 아닌 듯하니, 그들이 모두 사랑에 빠진 것은 아니기 때문입니다.\""]],[["“That is impossible,” said Don Quixote: “I say it is impossible that there could be a knight-errant without a lady, because to such it is as natural and proper to be in love as to the heavens to have stars: most certainly no history has been seen in which there is to be found a knight-errant without an amour, and for the simple reason that without one he would be held no legitimate knight but a bastard, and one who had gained entrance into the stronghold of the said knighthood, not by the door, but over the wall like a thief and a robber.”","\"그것은 있을 수 없는 일이오.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"귀부인 없는 편력 기사란 있을 수 없다고 나는 단언하오. 하늘이 별을 가지는 것이 당연하고 마땅하듯, 그런 이에게 사랑에 빠지는 것이 그만큼 당연하고 마땅한 일이기 때문이오. 사랑 없는 편력 기사가 나오는 역사란 분명 본 적이 없으니, 그 까닭은 단순하오. 사랑이 없으면 그는 적법한 기사가 아니라 사생아로 여겨질 것이며, 그 기사도의 성채에 문으로 들어선 것이 아니라 도둑이나 강도처럼 담을 넘어 들어온 자로 여겨질 것이기 때문이오.\""]],[["“Nevertheless,”","\"그렇기는 하나,\""],["said the traveller, “if I remember rightly, I think I have read that Don Galaor, the brother of the valiant Amadis of Gaul, never had any special lady to whom he might commend himself, and yet he was not the less esteemed, and was a very stout and famous knight.”","여행객이 말했다. \"제 기억이 옳다면, 용맹한 골의 아마디스의 아우인 돈 갈라오르는 자신을 의탁할 만한 특정한 귀부인을 둔 적이 한 번도 없었으되, 그럼에도 결코 덜 존경받지 않았으며 매우 굳세고 이름난 기사였다고 읽은 듯합니다.\""]],[["To which our Don Quixote made answer, “Sir, one solitary swallow does not make summer; moreover, I know that knight was in secret very deeply in love; besides which, that way of falling in love with all that took his fancy was a natural propensity which he could not control. But, in short, it is very manifest that he had one alone whom he made mistress of his will, to whom he commended himself very frequently and very secretly, for he prided himself on being a reticent knight.”","이에 우리의 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"이보시오, 제비 한 마리가 왔다고 여름이 오는 것은 아니오. 게다가 나는 그 기사가 남몰래 깊이 사랑에 빠져 있었음을 알고 있소. 그뿐 아니라 마음에 드는 모든 여인에게 사랑을 느끼던 그 버릇은, 그가 어찌할 수 없던 타고난 성향이었소. 허나 요컨대, 그가 자기 뜻의 주인으로 삼은 단 한 사람을 두고 그녀에게 매우 자주, 그리고 매우 은밀히 자신을 의탁했음은 지극히 분명하니, 그는 입이 무거운 기사임을 자랑으로 삼았기 때문이오.\""]],[["“Then if it be essential that every knight-errant should be in love,” said the traveller, “it may be fairly supposed that your worship is so, as you are of the order; and if you do not pride yourself on being as reticent as Don Galaor, I entreat you as earnestly as I can, in the name of all this company and in my own, to inform us of the name, country, rank, and beauty of your lady, for she will esteem herself fortunate if all the world knows that she is loved and served by such a knight as your worship seems to be.”","\"그렇다면 편력 기사라면 누구나 사랑에 빠져 있어야 한다는 것이 필수라면,\" 여행객이 말했다. \"나리께서도 그 기사회에 속하셨으니 그러하시리라 짐작해도 무방하겠지요. 그리고 나리께서 돈 갈라오르처럼 입이 무거운 것을 자랑으로 삼지 않으신다면, 이 모든 일행의 이름으로, 또 저 자신의 이름으로 더없이 간곡히 청하오니, 나리의 귀부인의 이름과 고향과 신분과 아름다움을 저희에게 알려 주십시오. 나리 같은 기사에게 사랑받고 섬김을 받는다는 것을 온 세상이 안다면, 그분께서는 스스로를 행운아로 여기실 테니까요.\""]],[["At this Don Quixote heaved a deep sigh and said, “I cannot say positively whether my sweet enemy is pleased or not that the world should know I serve her; I can only say in answer to what has been so courteously asked of me, that her name is Dulcinea, her country El Toboso, a village of La Mancha, her rank must be at least that of a princess, since she is my queen and lady, and her beauty superhuman, since all the impossible and fanciful attributes of beauty which the poets apply to their ladies are verified in her; for her hairs are gold, her forehead Elysian fields, her eyebrows rainbows, her eyes suns, her cheeks roses, her lips coral, her teeth pearls, her neck alabaster, her bosom marble, her hands ivory, her fairness snow, and what modesty conceals from sight such, I think and imagine, as rational reflection can only extol, not compare.”","이에 돈키호테가 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"내 달콤한 원수가 내가 그녀를 섬긴다는 것을 세상이 아는 것을 기뻐하는지 아닌지는 단언할 수 없소. 그토록 정중히 물어 주신 데 대해 답할 수 있는 것이라곤 다만 이것뿐이오. 그녀의 이름은 둘시네아요, 고향은 라만차의 한 마을 엘 토보소이며, 신분은 적어도 공주는 되어야 하니 그녀가 내 여왕이요 귀부인이기 때문이오. 그리고 그녀의 아름다움은 인간을 넘어선 것이니, 시인들이 자기들 귀부인에게 갖다 붙이는 그 불가능하고 허황된 온갖 아름다움의 속성이 모두 그녀에게서 사실로 증명되기 때문이오. 그녀의 머리카락은 황금이요, 이마는 엘리시온의 들판이요, 눈썹은 무지개요, 두 눈은 태양이요, 두 뺨은 장미요, 입술은 산호요, 이는 진주요, 목은 설화석고요, 가슴은 대리석이요, 두 손은 상아요, 흰 살결은 눈이며, 정숙함이 눈에서 가려 둔 것들은, 내 생각건대 또 짐작건대, 이성적인 헤아림으로 다만 기릴 수 있을 뿐 견주지는 못할 것이외다.\""]],[["“We should like to know her lineage, race, and ancestry,”","\"그분의 가문과 혈통과 조상을 알고 싶습니다.\""],["said Vivaldo.","비발도가 말했다."]],[["To which Don Quixote replied, “She is not of the ancient Roman Curtii, Caii, or Scipios, nor of the modern Colonnas or Orsini, nor of the Moncadas or Requesenes of Catalonia, nor yet of the Rebellas or Villanovas of Valencia; Palafoxes, Nuzas, Rocabertis, Corellas, Lunas, Alagones, Urreas, Foces, or Gurreas of Aragon; Cerdas, Manriques, Mendozas, or Guzmans of Castile; Alencastros, Pallas, or Meneses of Portugal; but she is of those of El Toboso of La Mancha, a lineage that though modern, may furnish a source of gentle blood for the most illustrious families of the ages that are to come, and this let none dispute with me save on the condition that Zerbino placed at the foot of the trophy of Orlando’s arms, saying,","이에 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그녀는 고대 로마의 쿠르티우스가나 카이우스가나 스키피오가 출신도 아니요, 근래의 콜론나가나 오르시니가 출신도 아니며, 카탈루냐의 몬카다가나 레케세네스가 출신도 아니요, 발렌시아의 레베야가나 비야노바가 출신도 아니외다. 또한 아라곤의 팔라폭스가, 누사가, 로카베르티가, 코레야가, 루나가, 알라곤가, 우레아가, 포세가, 구레아가 출신도 아니며, 카스티야의 세르다가, 만리케가, 멘도사가, 구스만가 출신도 아니요, 포르투갈의 알렌카스트로가, 파야스가, 메네세스가 출신도 아니외다. 다만 그녀는 라만차 엘 토보소 사람들 가운데 하나이니, 비록 근래의 가문이기는 하나 앞으로 올 시대의 가장 빛나는 가문들에 고귀한 피의 원천을 마련해 줄 수 있는 혈통이외다. 이에 대해서는, 오를란도의 무기로 세운 전승비 발치에 제르비노가 적어 둔 그 조건으로가 아니라면, 그 누구도 나와 다투지 못할 것이오. 그가 이르기를,"]],[["‘These let none move Who dareth not his might with Roland prove.’”","이것을 그 누구도 옮기지 말라, 롤랑과 제 힘을 겨룰 엄두 없는 자는.\""]],[["“Although mine is of the Cachopins of Laredo,”","\"제 가문은 라레도의 카초핀가 출신입니다만,\" 여행객이 말했다."],["said the traveller, “I will not venture to compare it with that of El Toboso of La Mancha, though, to tell the truth, no such surname has until now ever reached my ears.”","\"라만차 엘 토보소 가문과 감히 견주려 들지는 않겠습니다. 솔직히 말씀드리면, 그런 성씨는 지금껏 제 귀에 한 번도 닿은 적이 없긴 합니다만.\""]],[["“What!”","\"무엇이라!\""],["said Don Quixote, “has that never reached them?”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그것이 한 번도 그대 귀에 닿은 적이 없다는 말이오?\""]],[["The rest of the party went along listening with great attention to the conversation of the pair, and even the very goatherds and shepherds perceived how exceedingly out of his wits our Don Quixote was.","나머지 일행은 두 사람의 대화에 잔뜩 귀를 기울이며 따라가고 있었고, 염소지기와 양치기들조차 우리의 돈키호테가 얼마나 심하게 제정신이 아닌지를 알아차렸다. 오직 산초 판사만이 자기 주인이 누구인지를 알고 태어날 때부터 그를 알아 온 터라, 주인이 한 말이 진실이라고 여겼다."],["Sancho Panza alone thought that what his master said was the truth, knowing who he was and having known him from his birth; and all that he felt any difficulty in believing was that about the fair Dulcinea del Toboso, because neither any such name nor any such princess had ever come to his knowledge though he lived so close to El Toboso.","다만 그가 도무지 믿기 어려운 것이 하나 있었으니, 바로 아름다운 둘시네아 델 토보소에 관한 대목이었다. 자신이 엘 토보소 가까이 살았음에도 그런 이름도, 그런 공주도 도무지 들어 본 적이 없었기 때문이다."],["They were going along conversing in this way, when they saw descending a gap between two high mountains some twenty shepherds, all clad in sheepskins of black wool, and crowned with garlands which, as afterwards appeared, were, some of them of yew, some of cypress.","그들이 이렇게 이야기를 나누며 가고 있을 때, 두 높은 산 사이의 골짜기로 양치기 스무 명쯤이 내려오는 것이 보였으니, 모두 검은 양털 가죽을 걸치고 화관을 머리에 쓰고 있었는데, 나중에 보니 더러는 주목으로, 더러는 사이프러스로 엮은 것이었다. 그 가운데 여섯이 갖가지 꽃과 나뭇가지로 덮인 관을 메고 있었다."],["Six of the number were carrying a bier covered with a great variety of flowers and branches, on seeing which one of the goatherds said, “Those who come there are the bearers of Chrysostom’s body, and the foot of that mountain is the place where he ordered them to bury him.”","이를 본 염소지기 하나가 말했다. \"저기 오는 자들이 크리소스토모의 시신을 메고 오는 이들이고, 저 산기슭이 바로 그가 자기를 묻어 달라고 명한 곳이라오.\""],["They therefore made haste to reach the spot, and did so by the time those who came had laid the bier upon the ground, and four of them with sharp pickaxes were digging a grave by the side of a hard rock.","그리하여 그들은 서둘러 그곳에 다다랐고, 도착했을 무렵에는 시신을 메고 온 이들이 관을 땅에 내려놓았으며, 그들 가운데 넷이 날카로운 곡괭이로 단단한 바위 옆에 무덤을 파고 있었다. 그들은 서로 정중히 인사를 나누었고, 그러고 나서 돈키호테와 그를 따라온 이들은 관을 살펴보려 몸을 돌렸는데, 꽃으로 덮인 그 위에는 양치기 차림을 한 시신이 보였으니, 겉보기에 서른 살가량 되어 보였고, 죽어서도 살아생전에 그 이목구비가 준수하고 풍채가 늠름하였음을 드러내고 있었다."],["They greeted each other courteously, and then Don Quixote and those who accompanied him turned to examine the bier, and on it, covered with flowers, they saw a dead body in the dress of a shepherd, to all appearance of one thirty years of age, and showing even in death that in life he had been of comely features and gallant bearing.","관 위 그의 둘레에는 책 몇 권과 펼쳐지거나 접힌 여러 장의 종이가 놓여 있었다. 그리고 지켜보는 이들도, 무덤을 파는 이들도, 그곳에 있던 다른 모든 이도 이상한 침묵을 지키고 있었으니, 마침내 시신을 메고 온 이들 가운데 하나가 다른 이에게 말했다."],["Around him on the bier itself were laid some books, and several papers open and folded; and those who were looking on as well as those who were opening the grave and all the others who were there preserved a strange silence, until one of those who had borne the body said to another, “Observe carefully, Ambrosio if this is the place Chrysostom spoke of, since you are anxious that what he directed in his will should be so strictly complied with.”","\"암브로시오, 여기가 크리소스토모가 말하던 곳인지 잘 살펴보시오. 그가 유언에서 지시한 것이 어김없이 지켜지기를 그대가 그토록 바라고 있으니 말이오.\""]],[["“This is the place,”","\"여기가 그곳이오.\" 암브로시오가 대답했다."],["answered Ambrosio “for in it many a time did my poor friend tell me the story of his hard fortune.","\"이곳에서 여러 번, 내 가엾은 벗은 자기 가혹한 운명의 이야기를 내게 들려주었소. 바로 여기서 그는 인류의 저 철천지원수를 처음으로 보았다고 했고, 또한 여기서 처음으로 그녀에게 자기 연정을 고백했다 했으니, 그것은 정성스러운 만큼이나 떳떳한 것이었소."],["Here it was, he told me, that he saw for the first time that mortal enemy of the human race, and here, too, for the first time he declared to her his passion, as honourable as it was devoted, and here it was that at last Marcela ended by scorning and rejecting him so as to bring the tragedy of his wretched life to a close; here, in memory of misfortunes so great, he desired to be laid in the bowels of eternal oblivion.”","그리고 바로 여기서 끝내 마르셀라가 그를 비웃고 물리쳐, 그 비참한 한평생의 비극에 종지부를 찍게 했소. 이토록 큰 불행을 기리고자, 그는 여기, 영원한 망각의 품속에 누이기를 바랐던 것이오.\""],["Then turning to Don Quixote and the travellers he went on to say, “That body, sirs, on which you are looking with compassionate eyes, was the abode of a soul on which Heaven bestowed a vast share of its riches.","그러고는 돈키호테와 여행객들을 향해 돌아서서 말을 이었다. \"여러분, 여러분이 가엾이 여기는 눈으로 바라보고 있는 저 시신은, 하늘이 그 풍성한 재능을 듬뿍 내려 준 한 영혼의 거처였소."],["That is the body of Chrysostom, who was unrivalled in wit, unequalled in courtesy, unapproached in gentle bearing, a phoenix in friendship, generous without limit, grave without arrogance, gay without vulgarity, and, in short, first in all that constitutes goodness and second to none in all that makes up misfortune.","저것이 바로 크리소스토모의 시신이니, 그는 재치에서 견줄 이가 없었고, 예의에서 맞설 이가 없었으며, 점잖은 풍채에서 따를 이가 없었고, 우정에서는 불사조였으며, 한없이 너그러웠고, 거만함 없이 진중했고, 천박함 없이 유쾌했으니, 요컨대 선함을 이루는 모든 것에서 으뜸이었고, 불행을 이루는 모든 것에서도 그 누구에게 뒤지지 않았소. 그는 깊이 사랑했으나 미움을 받았고, 흠모했으나 멸시당했으며, 들짐승에게 구애했고, 대리석에게 호소했으며, 바람을 좇았고, 황야를 향해 부르짖었으며, 배은망덕을 섬겼으니, 그 대가로 한창나이에 죽음의 먹이가 되어 한 양치기 처녀의 손에 목숨을 잘렸소."],["He loved deeply, he was hated; he adored, he was scorned; he wooed a wild beast, he pleaded with marble, he pursued the wind, he cried to the wilderness, he served ingratitude, and for reward was made the prey of death in the mid-course of life, cut short by a shepherdess whom he sought to immortalise in the memory of man, as these papers which you see could fully prove, had he not commanded me to consign them to the fire after having consigned his body to the earth.”","그가 사람들의 기억 속에 길이 남기고자 했던 그 처녀 말이오. 여러분이 보시는 이 종이들이 그것을 넉넉히 증명해 줄 수 있을 터이나, 그가 자기 시신을 흙에 맡긴 다음에는 이것들을 불에 맡기라고 내게 명하였소.\""]],[["“You would deal with them more harshly and cruelly than their owner himself,” said Vivaldo, “for it is neither right nor proper to do the will of one who enjoins what is wholly unreasonable; it would not have been reasonable in Augustus Caesar had he permitted the directions left by the divine Mantuan in his will to be carried into effect. So that, Señor Ambrosio while you consign your friend’s body to the earth, you should not consign his writings to oblivion, for if he gave the order in bitterness of heart, it is not right that you should irrationally obey it. On the contrary, by granting life to those papers, let the cruelty of Marcela live for ever, to serve as a warning in ages to come to all men to shun and avoid falling into like danger; or I and all of us who have come here know already the story of this your love-stricken and heart-broken friend, and we know, too, your friendship, and the cause of his death, and the directions he gave at the close of his life; from which sad story may be gathered how great was the cruelty of Marcela, the love of Chrysostom, and the loyalty of your friendship, together with the end awaiting those who pursue rashly the path that insane passion opens to their eyes. Last night we learned the death of Chrysostom and that he was to be buried here, and out of curiosity and pity we left our direct road and resolved to come and see with our eyes that which when heard of had so moved our compassion, and in consideration of that compassion and our desire to prove it if we might by condolence, we beg of you, excellent Ambrosio, or at least I on my own account entreat you, that instead of burning those papers you allow me to carry away some of them.”","\"그것들을 그 임자보다 더 모질고 잔인하게 다루시려는 게로군요.\" 비발도가 말했다. \"전혀 분별없는 것을 명하는 자의 뜻을 따르는 것은 옳지도 마땅하지도 않은 일이니까요. 만일 아우구스투스 카이사르가 저 신과 같은 만토바 사람이 유언에 남긴 지시를 실행하도록 허락했더라면, 그것은 분별 있는 일이 못 되었을 것입니다. 그러니 암브로시오 님, 그대가 벗의 시신을 흙에 맡기더라도 그의 글까지 망각에 맡겨서는 안 됩니다. 그가 마음의 쓰라림 속에서 그런 명을 내렸다 하더라도, 그대가 분별없이 그것을 따르는 것은 옳지 않으니까요. 도리어 그 종이들에 생명을 줌으로써, 마르셀라의 잔혹함이 길이 살아남게 하여, 다가올 세대의 모든 사람에게 그와 같은 위험에 빠지는 것을 피하고 멀리하라는 경계로 삼게 하십시오. 이미 저와, 여기 온 저희 모두는 사랑에 사로잡혀 가슴이 찢긴 이 그대의 벗 이야기를 알고 있고, 또한 그대의 우정과, 그의 죽음의 까닭과, 그가 생의 끝에 남긴 지시도 알고 있습니다. 그 슬픈 이야기에서, 마르셀라의 잔혹함이 얼마나 컸고, 크리소스토모의 사랑이 얼마나 컸으며, 그대 우정의 충직함이 얼마나 컸는지, 더불어 미친 듯한 열정이 눈앞에 열어 주는 그 길을 무모하게 좇는 이들을 기다리는 결말이 어떠한지를 헤아릴 수 있습니다. 어젯밤 저희는 크리소스토모의 죽음과 그가 여기 묻히리라는 것을 알았고, 호기심과 연민에서 곧은길을 벗어나, 들었을 때 그토록 저희 동정심을 움직인 것을 두 눈으로 직접 보러 오기로 마음먹었습니다. 그리고 그 동정심을 헤아려, 또한 가능하다면 애도로써 그것을 입증하고픈 저희 바람을 헤아려, 훌륭한 암브로시오 님께 청하오니, 아니, 적어도 저 자신의 몫으로 그대에게 간청하오니, 저 종이들을 불태우는 대신 그중 몇 장을 제가 가져갈 수 있도록 허락해 주십시오.\""]],[["And without waiting for the shepherd’s answer, he stretched out his hand and took up some of those that were nearest to him; seeing which Ambrosio said, “Out of courtesy, señor, I will grant your request as to those you have taken, but it is idle to expect me to abstain from burning the remainder.”","그러고는 양치기의 대답도 기다리지 않고 손을 뻗어 자기에게 가장 가까이 있던 것 몇 장을 집어 들었다. 이를 본 암브로시오가 말했다. \"이보시오, 예의를 지켜, 그대가 집어 든 것에 대해서는 그대 청을 들어주겠소만, 나머지를 불태우는 것을 내가 그만두기를 바라는 것은 헛된 일이오.\""]],[["Vivaldo, who was eager to see what the papers contained, opened one of them at once, and saw that its title was “Lay of Despair.”","종이에 무슨 내용이 담겼는지 보고 싶어 안달이 난 비발도는 그중 하나를 곧장 펼쳤고, 그 표제가 「절망의 노래」임을 보았다."]],[["Ambrosio hearing it said, “That is the last paper the unhappy man wrote; and that you may see, señor, to what an end his misfortunes brought him, read it so that you may be heard, for you will have time enough for that while we are waiting for the grave to be dug.”","암브로시오가 그것을 듣고 말했다. \"그것이 그 가엾은 이가 쓴 마지막 종이올시다. 그러니, 이보시오, 그의 불행이 그를 어떤 결말로 몰아갔는지 보시도록, 모두가 들을 수 있게 그것을 읽어 주시오. 무덤이 다 파이기를 기다리는 동안 그럴 시간은 넉넉할 것이오.\""]],[["“I will do so very willingly,”","\"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다.\""],["said Vivaldo; and as all the bystanders were equally eager they gathered round him, and he, reading in a loud voice, found that it ran as follows.","비발도가 말했다. 곁에 선 모두가 똑같이 듣고 싶어 했으므로 그를 둘러싸고 모여들었고, 그는 큰 소리로 읽어 내려가니, 그것은 다음과 같았다."]],[["# CHAPTER XIV. WHEREIN ARE INSERTED THE DESPAIRING VERSES OF THE DEAD SHEPHERD, TOGETHER WITH OTHER INCIDENTS NOT LOOKED FOR","# CHAPTER XIV."]],[["# THE LAY OF CHRYSOSTOM","# THE LAY OF CHRYSOSTOM"]],[["Since thou dost in thy cruelty desire The ruthless rigour of thy tyranny From tongue to tongue, from land to land proclaimed, The very Hell will I constrain to lend This stricken breast of mine deep notes of woe To serve my need of fitting utterance.","그대 잔인함으로 그토록 바라거니와 그대 폭정의 무자비한 가혹함을 입에서 입으로, 땅에서 땅으로 퍼뜨리려 하기에, 나는 지옥마저 강제하여 이 짓밟힌 가슴에 깊은 비탄의 가락을 빌리리니, 마땅한 말을 토해 낼 내 절박함을 채우기 위함이라."],["And as I strive to body forth the tale Of all I suffer, all that thou hast done, Forth shall the dread voice roll, and bear along Shreds from my vitals torn for greater pain.","내가 견디는 모든 것, 그대가 저지른 모든 것을 이야기로 빚어내려 애쓰는 동안, 저 무시무시한 목소리는 굽이쳐 흘러, 더 큰 고통을 위해 갈가리 찢긴 내 오장의 조각을 실어 가리라."],["Then listen, not to dulcet harmony, But to a discord wrung by mad despair Out of this bosom’s depths of bitterness, To ease my heart and plant a sting in thine.","그러니 들으라, 감미로운 화음이 아니라, 이 가슴 깊은 쓰라림에서 미친 절망이 비틀어 짜낸 불협화음을, 내 마음을 달래고 그대 마음에 가시를 심으려 함이니."]],[["The lion’s roar, the fierce wolf’s savage howl, The horrid hissing of the scaly snake, The awesome cries of monsters yet unnamed, The crow’s ill-boding croak, the hollow moan Of wild winds wrestling with the restless sea, The wrathful bellow of the vanquished bull, The plaintive sobbing of the widowed dove, The envied owl’s sad note, the wail of woe That rises from the dreary choir of Hell, Commingled in one sound, confusing sense, Let all these come to aid my soul’s complaint, For pain like mine demands new modes of song.","사자의 포효, 사나운 늑대의 야만스러운 울부짖음, 비늘 덮인 뱀의 소름 끼치는 쉭쉭거림, 아직 이름조차 없는 괴물들의 끔찍한 비명, 까마귀의 불길한 까악거림, 쉼 없는 바다와 씨름하는 거센 바람의 공허한 신음, 패배한 황소의 분노에 찬 울부짖음, 홀로 남은 비둘기의 애처로운 흐느낌, 부러움 산 올빼미의 슬픈 가락, 지옥의 음울한 합창에서 솟아오르는 비탄의 절규, 이 모두가 한 소리로 뒤섞여 분별을 어지럽히니, 이 모든 것이 와서 내 영혼의 하소연을 도우라, 나 같은 고통은 새로운 노래의 가락을 요구하므로."]],[["No echoes of that discord shall be heard Where Father Tagus rolls, or on the banks Of olive-bordered Betis; to the rocks Or in deep caverns shall my plaint be told, And by a lifeless tongue in living words; Or in dark valleys or on lonely shores, Where neither foot of man nor sunbeam falls; Or in among the poison-breathing swarms Of monsters nourished by the sluggish Nile. For, though it be to solitudes remote The hoarse vague echoes of my sorrows sound Thy matchless cruelty, my dismal fate Shall carry them to all the spacious world.","그 불협화음의 메아리는 타호 아버지 강이 굽이치는 곳에서도, 올리브 늘어선 베티스 강의 기슭에서도 들리지 않으리니, 바위에게 혹은 깊은 동굴 속에서 내 하소연을 들려주리라, 생기 없는 혀로 살아 있는 말을 빌려서. 혹은 어두운 골짜기에서, 혹은 인적 없는 바닷가에서, 사람의 발길도 햇살도 닿지 않는 곳에서. 혹은 느릿한 나일 강이 길러 낸 독을 뿜는 괴물들의 무리 가운데서. 비록 외딴 적막한 곳을 향해 내 슬픔의 쉰, 흐릿한 메아리가 울려 퍼질지라도, 그대의 견줄 데 없는 잔인함과 나의 음울한 운명은 그것을 이 너른 온 세상으로 실어 가리라."]],[["Disdain hath power to kill, and patience dies Slain by suspicion, be it false or true; And deadly is the force of jealousy; Long absence makes of life a dreary void; No hope of happiness can give repose To him that ever fears to be forgot; And death, inevitable, waits in hall.","멸시는 죽일 힘이 있고, 인내는 거짓이든 참이든 의심에 베여 죽으며, 질투의 위력은 목숨을 앗아 가고, 오랜 부재는 삶을 황량한 공허로 만드니, 행복의 희망도, 잊힐까 늘 두려워하는 자에게는 평안을 줄 수 없으며, 피할 길 없는 죽음이 문간에서 기다린다."],["But I, by some strange miracle, live on A prey to absence, jealousy, disdain; Racked by suspicion as by certainty; Forgotten, left to feed my flame alone.","그러나 나는, 어떤 기이한 기적으로, 부재와 질투와 멸시의 먹이가 되어 살아가니, 확신에 시달리듯 의심에 시달리고, 잊힌 채 홀로 내 불꽃을 키우도록 버려졌도다."],["And while I suffer thus, there comes no ray Of hope to gladden me athwart the gloom; Nor do I look for it in my despair; But rather clinging to a cureless woe, All hope do I abjure for evermore.","이렇게 견디는 동안, 음울함을 가로질러 나를 기쁘게 할 희망의 빛 한 줄기도 오지 않으며, 내 절망 속에서 그것을 바라지도 않고, 오히려 고칠 길 없는 비탄에 매달려, 영영토록 모든 희망을 저버리노라."]],[["Can there be hope where fear is?","두려움 있는 곳에 희망이 있을 수 있으랴?"],["Were it well, When far more certain are the grounds of fear?","그것이 옳으랴, 두려움의 근거가 훨씬 더 확실할진대?"],["Ought I to shut mine eyes to jealousy, If through a thousand heart-wounds it appears?","천 군데 마음의 상처를 통해 드러나는데도 내 어찌 질투에 눈을 감아야 하랴?"],["Who would not give free access to distrust, Seeing disdain unveiled, and--bitter change!-- All his suspicions turned to certainties, And the fair truth transformed into a lie?","멸시가 베일을 벗는 것을 보고서, 그리고 — 쓰라린 변화여! — 그 모든 의심이 확신으로 바뀌고, 아름다운 진실이 거짓으로 둔갑하는 것을 보고서, 누군들 불신에 길을 열어 주지 않으랴?"],["Oh, thou fierce tyrant of the realms of love, Oh, Jealousy!","오, 그대 사랑의 왕국의 사나운 폭군이여, 오, 질투여!"],["put chains upon these hands, And bind me with thy strongest cord, Disdain.","이 두 손에 사슬을 채우라, 그리고 그대의 가장 질긴 밧줄로 나를 묶으라, 멸시여."],["But, woe is me! triumphant over all, My sufferings drown the memory of you.","그러나, 가엾어라! 그 모든 것을 이기고서 내 고통이 그대들에 대한 기억을 잠겨 버리는구나."]],[["And now I die, and since there is no hope Of happiness for me in life or death, Still to my fantasy I’ll fondly cling.","이제 나는 죽노라, 삶에서도 죽음에서도 내게 행복의 희망이라곤 없으니, 여전히 내 환상에 애틋하게 매달리리라."],["I’ll say that he is wise who loveth well, And that the soul most free is that most bound In thraldom to the ancient tyrant Love.","잘 사랑하는 자가 지혜롭다 말하리라, 그리고 가장 자유로운 영혼이란 오래된 폭군 사랑에 가장 깊이 예속된 영혼이라 말하리라."],["I’ll say that she who is mine enemy In that fair body hath as fair a mind, And that her coldness is but my desert, And that by virtue of the pain he sends Love rules his kingdom with a gentle sway.","나의 원수인 그 여인이 저 고운 몸에 그만큼 고운 마음을 지녔다 말하리라, 그녀의 차가움은 다만 내가 받아 마땅한 것이며, 그가 내리는 고통의 힘으로 사랑이 부드러운 통치로 그 왕국을 다스린다 말하리라."],["Thus, self-deluding, and in bondage sore, And wearing out the wretched shred of life To which I am reduced by her disdain, I’ll give this soul and body to the winds, All hopeless of a crown of bliss in store.","이렇게 스스로를 속이고, 모진 속박에 매여, 그녀의 멸시로 내가 처하게 된 이 비참한 삶의 누더기를 닳아 없애며, 저장된 축복의 관에 대한 모든 희망을 버린 채, 이 영혼과 몸을 바람에 내어 주리라."]],[["Thou whose injustice hath supplied the cause That makes me quit the weary life I loathe, As by this wounded bosom thou canst see How willingly thy victim I become, Let not my death, if haply worth a tear, Cloud the clear heaven that dwells in thy bright eyes; I would not have thee expiate in aught The crime of having made my heart thy prey; But rather let thy laughter gaily ring And prove my death to be thy festival.","그대의 부당함이 빌미가 되어 내가 혐오하는 이 고단한 삶을 버리게 하니, 이 상처 입은 가슴으로 그대가 볼 수 있듯, 내가 얼마나 기꺼이 그대의 제물이 되는지를, 내 죽음이 행여 눈물 한 방울의 값이 있을지라도, 그대 빛나는 두 눈에 깃든 맑은 하늘을 흐리지 말라. 내 마음을 그대의 먹이로 삼은 그 죄를 그대가 조금이라도 속죄하기를 나는 바라지 않으니, 오히려 그대의 웃음이 흥겹게 울려 퍼지게 하여 내 죽음이 그대의 축제임을 증명하라."],["Fool that I am to bid thee!","그대에게 이런 부탁을 하다니 나는 어리석구나!"],["well I know Thy glory gains by my untimely end.","잘 아노라, 나의 때 이른 종말로 그대의 영광이 더해짐을."]],[["And now it is the time; from Hell’s abyss Come thirsting Tantalus, come Sisyphus Heaving the cruel stone, come Tityus With vulture, and with wheel Ixion come, And come the sisters of the ceaseless toil; And all into this breast transfer their pains, And (if such tribute to despair be due) Chant in their deepest tones a doleful dirge Over a corse unworthy of a shroud. Let the three-headed guardian of the gate, And all the monstrous progeny of hell, The doleful concert join: a lover dead Methinks can have no fitter obsequies.","이제 그때가 왔도다. 지옥의 심연에서 목마른 탄탈로스여 오라, 잔혹한 돌을 떠받치는 시시포스여 오라, 독수리를 거느린 티티오스여, 수레바퀴를 거느린 익시온이여 오라, 그리고 그칠 줄 모르는 노역의 자매들이여 오라. 그 모든 고통을 이 가슴에 옮겨 놓고서, (절망에 그런 공물이 마땅하다면) 수의조차 받을 자격 없는 주검을 두고 가장 깊은 가락으로 구슬픈 만가를 부르라. 문을 지키는 머리 셋 달린 파수꾼과, 지옥의 그 모든 괴물 같은 무리도 이 애처로운 합주에 함께하라. 죽은 연인에게는 이보다 더 어울리는 장례가 없으리라 여겨지나니."]],[["Lay of despair, grieve not when thou art gone Forth from this sorrowing heart: my misery Brings fortune to the cause that gave thee birth; Then banish sadness even in the tomb.","절망의 노래여, 이 슬퍼하는 가슴을 떠나 나아갈 때 슬퍼하지 말라. 내 비참함은 너를 낳은 그 까닭에 행운을 가져다주나니, 그러니 무덤 속에서조차 슬픔을 떨쳐 버리라."]],[["The “Lay of Chrysostom” met with the approbation of the listeners, though the reader said it did not seem to him to agree with what he had heard of Marcela’s reserve and propriety, for Chrysostom complained in it of jealousy, suspicion, and absence, all to the prejudice of the good name and fame of Marcela; to which Ambrosio replied as one who knew well his friend’s most secret thoughts, “Señor, to remove that doubt I should tell you that when the unhappy man wrote this lay he was away from Marcela, from whom he had voluntarily separated himself, to try if absence would act with him as it is wont; and as everything distresses and every fear haunts the banished lover, so imaginary jealousies and suspicions, dreaded as if they were true, tormented Chrysostom; and thus the truth of what report declares of the virtue of Marcela remains unshaken, and with her envy itself should not and cannot find any fault save that of being cruel, somewhat haughty, and very scornful.”","「크리소스토모의 노래」는 듣는 이들의 호평을 받았으나, 낭송한 자는 자신이 들어 온 마르셀라의 정숙함과 단정함과는 어울리지 않는 듯하다고 말했으니, 크리소스토모가 그 안에서 질투와 의심과 부재를 탓하며, 그 모두가 마르셀라의 좋은 이름과 명성을 해치는 것이었기 때문이다. 이에 친구의 가장 은밀한 속생각까지 잘 아는 사람으로서 암브로시오가 대답했다. \"나리, 그 의심을 풀어 드리고자 말씀드리자면, 이 가엾은 사람이 이 노래를 지었을 때 그는 마르셀라에게서 떨어져 있었으니, 부재가 흔히 그러하듯 자기에게도 효력을 발휘하는지 시험해 보려고 스스로 그녀에게서 멀어졌던 것입니다. 그리고 추방당한 연인에게는 모든 것이 괴로움을 안기고 온갖 두려움이 따라다니듯, 참인 양 두려워하던 상상 속의 질투와 의심이 크리소스토모를 괴롭혔던 것이지요. 그러니 마르셀라의 미덕에 관해 소문이 전하는 그대로의 진실은 흔들림이 없으며, 그녀에 대해서는 질투조차도 잔인하고 다소 거만하며 무척 쌀쌀맞다는 것 말고는 어떤 흠도 잡아서는 안 되고 잡을 수도 없습니다.\""]],[["“That is true,”","\"그건 사실이오.\""],["said Vivaldo; and as he was about to read another paper of those he had preserved from the fire, he was stopped by a marvellous vision (for such it seemed) that unexpectedly presented itself to their eyes; for on the summit of the rock where they were digging the grave there appeared the shepherdess Marcela, so beautiful that her beauty exceeded its reputation.","비발도가 말했다. 그러고는 불에서 건져 낸 종이 가운데 또 한 장을 읽으려던 참에, 뜻밖에도 그들의 눈앞에 나타난 어떤 놀라운 환영(그렇게 보였으므로)에 의해 멈추게 되었으니, 그들이 무덤을 파고 있던 바위 꼭대기에 양치기 처녀 마르셀라가 나타난 것인데, 어찌나 아름다웠던지 그 아름다움이 소문보다 더하였다."],["Those who had never till then beheld her gazed upon her in wonder and silence, and those who were accustomed to see her were not less amazed than those who had never seen her before.","그때까지 그녀를 한 번도 본 적 없는 이들은 경탄과 침묵 속에 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀를 늘 보아 오던 이들도 한 번도 본 적 없는 이들 못지않게 놀라워했다."],["But the instant Ambrosio saw her he addressed her, with manifest indignation:","그러나 암브로시오는 그녀를 보는 순간, 역연한 분노에 차서 그녀에게 말했다."]],[["“Art thou come, by chance, cruel basilisk of these mountains, to see if in thy presence blood will flow from the wounds of this wretched being thy cruelty has robbed of life; or is it to exult over the cruel work of thy humours that thou art come; or like another pitiless Nero to look down from that height upon the ruin of his Rome in embers; or in thy arrogance to trample on this ill-fated corpse, as the ungrateful daughter trampled on her father Tarquin’s?","\"네가 혹여, 이 산악의 잔인한 바실리스크여, 네 잔인함이 목숨을 앗아 간 이 가엾은 자의 상처에서 네가 있으면 피가 흐르는지 보려고 왔느냐, 아니면 네 변덕이 빚어낸 잔혹한 소행을 두고 의기양양해하려고 왔느냐, 아니면 또 다른 무자비한 네로처럼 저 높은 곳에서 잿더미가 된 자기 로마의 폐허를 내려다보듯 하려고, 혹은 네 오만함으로 이 불운한 주검을 짓밟으려고 왔느냐, 마치 그 배은망덕한 딸이 제 아비 타르퀴니우스를 짓밟았듯이?"],["Tell us quickly for what thou art come, or what it is thou wouldst have, for, as I know the thoughts of Chrysostom never failed to obey thee in life, I will make all these who call themselves his friends obey thee, though he be dead.”","네가 무엇 하러 왔는지, 혹은 무엇을 원하는지 어서 말하라. 크리소스토모의 생각이 살아생전 너에게 복종하기를 한 번도 어기지 않았음을 내가 아는 만큼, 그가 죽었어도 스스로 그의 벗이라 일컫는 이 모든 이가 너에게 복종하도록 내가 만들 터이니.\""]],[["“I come not, Ambrosio for any of the purposes thou hast named,”","\"암브로시오여, 나는 그대가 말한 그 어떤 목적으로 온 것이 아니라,\" 마르셀라가 대답했다."],["replied Marcela, “but to defend myself and to prove how unreasonable are all those who blame me for their sorrow and for Chrysostom’s death; and therefore I ask all of you that are here to give me your attention, for it will not take much time or many words to bring the truth home to persons of sense.","\"오직 나 자신을 변호하고, 자기들의 슬픔과 크리소스토모의 죽음을 두고 나를 탓하는 모든 이가 얼마나 이치에 어긋나는지를 밝히려고 왔을 뿐입니다. 그러니 여기 계신 모든 분께 청하노니 내게 귀를 기울여 주십시오."],["Heaven has made me, so you say, beautiful, and so much so that in spite of yourselves my beauty leads you to love me; and for the love you show me you say, and even urge, that I am bound to love you.","분별 있는 사람들에게 진실을 깨우치는 데에는 많은 시간도 많은 말도 필요치 않을 것입니다. 그대들 말로는 하늘이 나를 아름답게 지으셨다 하며, 그것도 어찌나 아름다운지 그대들이 어쩌지 못한 채 내 아름다움에 이끌려 나를 사랑하게 된다 합니다."],["By that natural understanding which God has given me I know that everything beautiful attracts love, but I cannot see how, by reason of being loved, that which is loved for its beauty is bound to love that which loves it; besides, it may happen that the lover of that which is beautiful may be ugly, and ugliness being detestable, it is very absurd to say, “I love thee because thou art beautiful, thou must love me though I be ugly.”","그리고 그대들이 내게 보이는 사랑 때문에, 내가 그대들을 사랑해야 마땅하다고 말하며 심지어 다그치기까지 합니다. 하느님께서 내게 주신 그 타고난 분별로 나는 아름다운 모든 것이 사랑을 끌어당김을 알지만, 사랑받는다는 이유로 그 아름다움 때문에 사랑받는 것이 자기를 사랑하는 자를 사랑해야 할 의무가 어떻게 생기는지는 알 수가 없습니다."],["But supposing the beauty equal on both sides, it does not follow that the inclinations must be therefore alike, for it is not every beauty that excites love, some but pleasing the eye without winning the affection; and if every sort of beauty excited love and won the heart, the will would wander vaguely to and fro unable to make choice of any; for as there is an infinity of beautiful objects there must be an infinity of inclinations, and true love, I have heard it said, is indivisible, and must be voluntary and not compelled.","게다가 아름다운 것을 사랑하는 자가 추할 수도 있는 노릇이요, 추함이란 혐오스러운 것이니, '내 그대 아름다우니 그대를 사랑하노라, 내 비록 추하나 그대는 나를 사랑해야 하느니라' 하고 말하는 것은 참으로 어리석은 일입니다."],["If this be so, as I believe it to be, why do you desire me to bend my will by force, for no other reason but that you say you love me?","그러나 설령 양쪽의 아름다움이 동등하다 하더라도, 그렇다고 마음의 끌림까지 같아야 한다는 법은 없으니, 모든 아름다움이 다 사랑을 불러일으키는 것은 아니어서 어떤 것은 마음을 사로잡지 못한 채 눈만 즐겁게 할 뿐입니다."],["Nay--tell me--had Heaven made me ugly, as it has made me beautiful, could I with justice complain of you for not loving me?","만일 온갖 종류의 아름다움이 다 사랑을 불러일으키고 마음을 사로잡는다면, 의지는 어느 하나도 택하지 못한 채 정처 없이 이리저리 헤맬 터이니, 아름다운 대상이 무수히 많은 만큼 마음의 끌림도 무수히 많아야 할 것이기 때문입니다. 그런데 내가 듣기로 참된 사랑은 나뉠 수 없는 것이며, 강요된 것이 아니라 자발적인 것이어야 합니다."],["Moreover, you must remember that the beauty I possess was no choice of mine, for, be it what it may, Heaven of its bounty gave it me without my asking or choosing it; and as the viper, though it kills with it, does not deserve to be blamed for the poison it carries, as it is a gift of nature, neither do I deserve reproach for being beautiful; for beauty in a modest woman is like fire at a distance or a sharp sword; the one does not burn, the other does not cut, those who do not come too near.","내가 믿는 바대로 만일 이것이 참이라면, 그대들이 나를 사랑한다고 말한다는 것 말고는 아무 까닭도 없이, 어찌하여 내가 억지로 내 의지를 굽히기를 바라는 것입니까? 아니, 말해 보십시오, 하늘이 나를 아름답게 지으신 것처럼 추하게 지으셨다면, 나를 사랑하지 않는다고 내가 그대들을 정당하게 탓할 수 있었겠습니까?"],["Honour and virtue are the ornaments of the mind, without which the body, though it be so, has no right to pass for beautiful; but if modesty is one of the virtues that specially lend a grace and charm to mind and body, why should she who is loved for her beauty part with it to gratify one who for his pleasure alone strives with all his might and energy to rob her of it?","더욱이 그대들은 기억해야 합니다. 내가 지닌 이 아름다움은 내가 택한 것이 아니니, 그것이 어떤 것이든 하늘이 그 너그러움으로 내가 청하지도 택하지도 않았는데 내게 주신 것입니다."],["I was born free, and that I might live in freedom I chose the solitude of the fields; in the trees of the mountains I find society, the clear waters of the brooks are my mirrors, and to the trees and waters I make known my thoughts and charms.","그리고 독사가 비록 그 독으로 죽이기는 하나, 그 독은 자연이 준 선물인지라 그것을 지녔다고 탓받아 마땅하지 않은 것처럼, 나 또한 아름답다는 이유로 비난받아 마땅하지 않습니다. 정숙한 여인에게 아름다움이란 멀리 있는 불이나 날 선 칼과 같아서, 너무 가까이 오지 않는 이들은 그 하나에 데지도 않고 그 다른 하나에 베이지도 않기 때문입니다."],["I am a fire afar off, a sword laid aside.","명예와 미덕은 마음의 장식이니, 그것이 없으면 몸이 비록 아름답다 한들 아름답다 일컬어질 자격이 없습니다."],["Those whom I have inspired with love by letting them see me, I have by words undeceived, and if their longings live on hope--and I have given none to Chrysostom or to any other--it cannot justly be said that the death of any is my doing, for it was rather his own obstinacy than my cruelty that killed him; and if it be made a charge against me that his wishes were honourable, and that therefore I was bound to yield to them, I answer that when on this very spot where now his grave is made he declared to me his purity of purpose, I told him that mine was to live in perpetual solitude, and that the earth alone should enjoy the fruits of my retirement and the spoils of my beauty; and if, after this open avowal, he chose to persist against hope and steer against the wind, what wonder is it that he should sink in the depths of his infatuation?","그런데 만일 정숙함이 마음과 몸에 우아함과 매력을 더해 주는 미덕 가운데 하나라면, 어찌하여 그 아름다움 때문에 사랑받는 여인이, 오직 자기 쾌락만을 위해 온 힘과 기운을 다해 그것을 빼앗으려 드는 자를 만족시키자고 그것을 내어 주어야 하겠습니까? 나는 자유롭게 태어났고, 자유롭게 살고자 들판의 고적함을 택했습니다."],["If I had encouraged him, I should be false; if I had gratified him, I should have acted against my own better resolution and purpose.","산의 나무들에서 나는 벗을 찾고, 시냇물의 맑은 물은 나의 거울이며, 나무들과 물에게 나는 내 생각과 매력을 털어놓습니다. 나는 멀리 있는 불이요, 치워 둔 칼입니다."],["He was persistent in spite of warning, he despaired without being hated.","나를 보게 하여 사랑을 품게 한 이들을 나는 말로써 깨우쳐 주었으니, 만일 그들의 갈망이 희망을 양식 삼아 살아간다면 — 나는 크리소스토모에게도 다른 누구에게도 그런 희망을 준 적이 없거니와 — 누군가의 죽음이 내 소행이라고 정당하게 말할 수는 없습니다. 그를 죽인 것은 나의 잔인함이라기보다 오히려 그 자신의 고집이었기 때문입니다."],["Bethink you now if it be reasonable that his suffering should be laid to my charge.","그리고 만일 그의 소원이 떳떳한 것이었으니 내가 그에 응했어야 마땅하다고 나를 책망한다면, 나는 이렇게 답하겠습니다. 지금 그의 무덤이 만들어진 바로 이 자리에서 그가 자기 뜻의 순결함을 내게 밝혔을 때, 나는 그에게 내 뜻은 영원한 고독 속에 사는 것이며, 내 은둔의 열매와 내 아름다움의 전리품은 오직 대지만이 누리게 하리라고 말했다고."],["Let him who has been deceived complain, let him give way to despair whose encouraged hopes have proved vain, let him flatter himself whom I shall entice, let him boast whom I shall receive; but let not him call me cruel or homicide to whom I make no promise, upon whom I practise no deception, whom I neither entice nor receive.","이렇게 터놓고 고백한 뒤에도 그가 희망을 거슬러 고집을 부리고 바람을 거슬러 키를 잡기로 택했다면, 그가 자신의 미망의 심연에 빠져든 것이 무어 그리 놀랄 일이겠습니까? 만일 내가 그를 부추겼다면 나는 거짓된 사람이요, 만일 내가 그를 만족시켰다면 나는 나 자신의 더 나은 결심과 뜻을 거슬러 행동한 셈이 됩니다."],["It has not been so far the will of Heaven that I should love by fate, and to expect me to love by choice is idle.","경고를 받고도 그는 고집을 부렸고, 미움을 받지 않고도 그는 절망했습니다. 이제 그의 고통을 내 탓으로 돌리는 것이 과연 이치에 맞는지 헤아려 보십시오."],["Let this general declaration serve for each of my suitors on his own account, and let it be understood from this time forth that if anyone dies for me it is not of jealousy or misery he dies, for she who loves no one can give no cause for jealousy to any, and candour is not to be confounded with scorn.","속은 자는 하소연하라 하고, 부추겨진 희망이 헛되이 끝난 자는 절망에 빠지라 하고, 내가 꾈 자는 우쭐대라 하고, 내가 받아 줄 자는 뽐내라 하십시오. 그러나 내가 아무 약속도 하지 않고, 아무 속임수도 부리지 않으며, 꾀지도 받아 주지도 않는 자는 나를 잔인하다거나 살인자라 부르지 말라 하십시오."],["Let him who calls me wild beast and basilisk, leave me alone as something noxious and evil; let him who calls me ungrateful, withhold his service; who calls me wayward, seek not my acquaintance; who calls me cruel, pursue me not; for this wild beast, this basilisk, this ungrateful, cruel, wayward being has no kind of desire to seek, serve, know, or follow them.","내가 운명에 의해 사랑하는 것은 아직 하늘의 뜻이 아니었으며, 내가 선택에 의해 사랑하기를 기대하는 것은 부질없는 일입니다. 이 일반적인 선언이 내 구혼자들 저마다에게 제 몫으로 쓰이게 하고, 또 이제부터는 이렇게 이해되게 하십시오."],["If Chrysostom’s impatience and violent passion killed him, why should my modest behaviour and circumspection be blamed?","누군가 나 때문에 죽는다 해도 그것은 질투나 비참함 때문에 죽는 것이 아니니, 아무도 사랑하지 않는 여인은 누구에게도 질투의 빌미를 줄 수 없으며, 솔직함을 멸시와 혼동해서는 안 되기 때문입니다. 나를 들짐승이라 바실리스크라 부르는 자는 나를 해롭고 악한 것으로 여겨 내버려 두라 하고, 나를 배은망덕하다 부르는 자는 그 봉사를 거두라 하고, 나를 변덕스럽다 부르는 자는 나와 사귀려 하지 말라 하고, 나를 잔인하다 부르는 자는 나를 좇지 말라 하십시오."],["If I preserve my purity in the society of the trees, why should he who would have me preserve it among men, seek to rob me of it?","이 들짐승, 이 바실리스크, 이 배은망덕하고 잔인하며 변덕스러운 존재는 그들을 찾거나 섬기거나 알거나 좇으려는 욕망이 조금도 없기 때문입니다. 크리소스토모의 조급함과 격렬한 열정이 그를 죽였다면, 어찌하여 내 정숙한 처신과 신중함이 비난받아야 합니까?"],["I have, as you know, wealth of my own, and I covet not that of others; my taste is for freedom, and I have no relish for constraint; I neither love nor hate anyone; I do not deceive this one or court that, or trifle with one or play with another.","내가 나무들과 더불어 내 순결을 지키는데, 어찌하여 사람들 가운데서 내가 그것을 지키기를 바라는 자가 그것을 내게서 빼앗으려 드는 것입니까? 그대들도 알다시피 나는 나 자신의 재산이 있어 남의 것을 탐하지 않으며, 내 취향은 자유에 있고 속박에는 정나미가 없습니다."],["The modest converse of the shepherd girls of these hamlets and the care of my goats are my recreations; my desires are bounded by these mountains, and if they ever wander hence it is to contemplate the beauty of the heavens, steps by which the soul travels to its primeval abode.”","나는 누구를 사랑하지도 미워하지도 않으며, 이 사람을 속이거나 저 사람에게 추파를 던지지도, 한 사람을 농락하거나 다른 사람을 가지고 놀지도 않습니다. 이 마을 양치기 처녀들과의 정숙한 담소와 내 염소들을 돌보는 일이 내 즐거움이며, 내 바람은 이 산들에 갇혀 있으니, 혹여 그것이 이곳을 벗어나 헤맨다면 그것은 하늘의 아름다움을 우러르기 위함이요, 그것은 영혼이 그 태초의 거처로 나아가는 계단입니다.\""]],[["With these words, and not waiting to hear a reply, she turned and passed into the thickest part of a wood that was hard by, leaving all who were there lost in admiration as much of her good sense as of her beauty.","이 말과 함께, 대답을 들으려고도 하지 않은 채 그녀는 몸을 돌려 바로 곁에 있던 숲의 가장 우거진 곳으로 들어가 버렸으니, 거기 있던 모든 이는 그녀의 아름다움 못지않게 그녀의 분별에도 넋을 잃었다."],["Some--those wounded by the irresistible shafts launched by her bright eyes--made as though they would follow her, heedless of the frank declaration they had heard; seeing which, and deeming this a fitting occasion for the exercise of his chivalry in aid of distressed damsels, Don Quixote, laying his hand on the hilt of his sword, exclaimed in a loud and distinct voice:","더러는 — 그녀의 빛나는 두 눈이 쏘아 보낸 거역할 수 없는 화살에 맞은 자들은 — 자기들이 들은 그 솔직한 선언에도 아랑곳없이 마치 그녀를 따라가려는 듯하였다. 이를 본 돈키호테는, 곤경에 처한 처녀들을 돕고자 자신의 기사도를 발휘하기에 알맞은 기회라 여기고서, 칼자루에 손을 얹은 채 크고 또렷한 목소리로 외쳤다."]],[["“Let no one, whatever his rank or condition, dare to follow the beautiful Marcela, under pain of incurring my fierce indignation.","\"누구든 그 신분이나 처지가 어떠하든, 아름다운 마르셀라를 감히 따라가지 말라, 나의 사나운 분노를 사고 싶지 않거든. 그녀는 크리소스토모의 죽음에 대해 자기에게 잘못이 거의, 아니 전혀 없음을 명백하고도 흡족한 논리로 보여 주었고, 또한 자신이 그 어떤 연인의 바람에도 응하기를 얼마나 멀리하는지도 보여 주었다."],["She has shown by clear and satisfactory arguments that little or no fault is to be found with her for the death of Chrysostom, and also how far she is from yielding to the wishes of any of her lovers, for which reason, instead of being followed and persecuted, she should in justice be honoured and esteemed by all the good people of the world, for she shows that she is the only woman in it that holds to such a virtuous resolution.”","그러므로 그녀는 쫓기고 핍박받을 것이 아니라, 마땅히 세상의 모든 선한 이에게 존경받고 떠받들어져야 한다. 이토록 정숙한 결심을 지키는 여인은 세상에 오직 그녀뿐임을 그녀가 보여 주었기 때문이다.\""]],[["Whether it was because of the threats of Don Quixote, or because Ambrosio told them to fulfil their duty to their good friend, none of the shepherds moved or stirred from the spot until, having finished the grave and burned Chrysostom’s papers, they laid his body in it, not without many tears from those who stood by.","돈키호테의 위협 때문이었는지, 아니면 암브로시오가 그들에게 좋은 벗에 대한 도리를 다하라고 일렀기 때문인지, 양치기들 가운데 누구도 그 자리에서 움직이거나 꼼짝하지 않았고, 마침내 무덤을 다 파고 크리소스토모의 종이들을 불사른 뒤에 그의 시신을 그 안에 뉘었으니, 곁에 선 이들의 숱한 눈물이 없지 않았다."],["They closed the grave with a heavy stone until a slab was ready which Ambrosio said he meant to have prepared, with an epitaph which was to be to this effect:","그들은 무거운 돌로 무덤을 덮어, 암브로시오가 준비시키겠다고 말한 묘비석이 마련될 때까지 두기로 했는데, 그 비석에 새길 비명(碑銘)은 이런 뜻이었다."]],[["Beneath the stone before your eyes The body of a lover lies; In life he was a shepherd swain, In death a victim to disdain. Ungrateful, cruel, coy, and fair, Was she that drove him to despair, And Love hath made her his ally For spreading wide his tyranny.","그대 눈앞 이 돌 아래에 한 연인의 몸이 누웠노라. 살아서는 양치기 총각이었고 죽어서는 멸시의 제물이었네. 그를 절망으로 내몬 여인은 배은망덕, 잔인, 쌀쌀, 그리고 아리따웠으니, 사랑이 그녀를 한패로 삼아 제 폭정을 널리 펼쳤도다."]],[["They then strewed upon the grave a profusion of flowers and branches, and all expressing their condolence with his friend ambrosio, took their Vivaldo and his companion did the same; and Don Quixote bade farewell to his hosts and to the travellers, who pressed him to come with them to Seville, as being such a convenient place for finding adventures, for they presented themselves in every street and round every corner oftener than anywhere else.","그러고 나서 그들은 무덤 위에 꽃과 나뭇가지를 가득 흩뿌리고, 모두가 그의 벗 암브로시오에게 조의를 표한 뒤 작별을 고했다. 비발도와 그의 일행도 그리하였고, 돈키호테는 자신을 맞아 준 이들과 나그네들에게 작별을 고했는데, 나그네들은 세비야로 함께 가자고 그에게 권했으니, 거기가 모험을 찾기에 더없이 알맞은 곳이라, 모험이란 것이 다른 어디보다도 거리거리마다 길모퉁이마다 더 자주 나타나기 때문이라고 했다."],["Don Quixote thanked them for their advice and for the disposition they showed to do him a favour, and said that for the present he would not, and must not go to Seville until he had cleared all these mountains of highwaymen and robbers, of whom report said they were full.","돈키호테는 그들의 조언과 자기에게 호의를 베풀려는 그 마음에 감사하며, 지금으로서는 이 모든 산에서, 소문에 우글거린다는 노상강도와 도둑들을 죄다 쓸어 없애기 전에는 세비야로 가지 않을 것이며 가서도 안 된다고 말했다."],["Seeing his good intention, the travellers were unwilling to press him further, and once more bidding him farewell, they left him and pursued their journey, in the course of which they did not fail to discuss the story of Marcela and Chrysostom as well as the madness of Don Quixote.","그의 갸륵한 뜻을 본 나그네들은 그를 더 채근하려 하지 않았고, 다시 한번 작별을 고하고서 그를 떠나 길을 갔는데, 그 길에서 그들은 마르셀라와 크리소스토모의 이야기는 물론 돈키호테의 광기에 대해서도 빠짐없이 이야기를 나누었다."],["He, on his part, resolved to go in quest of the shepherdess Marcela, and make offer to her of all the service he could render her; but things did not fall out with him as he expected, according to what is related in the course of this veracious history, of which the Second Part ends here.","그는 그대로, 양치기 처녀 마르셀라를 찾아 나서서 자기가 그녀에게 베풀 수 있는 온갖 봉사를 바치기로 결심했다. 그러나 이 진실한 이야기가 전하는 바에 따르면, 일은 그가 기대한 대로 풀리지 않았으니, 제2부는 여기서 끝난다."]],[["# CHAPTER XV. IN WHICH IS RELATED THE UNFORTUNATE ADVENTURE THAT DON QUIXOTE FELL IN WITH WHEN HE FELL OUT WITH CERTAIN HEARTLESS YANGUESANS","# CHAPTER XV."]],[["The sage Cide Hamete Benengeli relates that as soon as Don Quixote took leave of his hosts and all who had been present at the burial of Chrysostom, he and his squire passed into the same wood which they had seen the shepherdess Marcela enter, and after having wandered for more than two hours in all directions in search of her without finding her, they came to a halt in a glade covered with tender grass, beside which ran a pleasant cool stream that invited and compelled them to pass there the hours of the noontide heat, which by this time was beginning to come on oppressively.","현자 시데 아메테 베넹헬리는 이렇게 전한다. 돈키호테가 자신을 맞아 준 이들과 크리소스토모의 장례에 와 있던 모든 사람과 작별하자마자, 그와 그의 종자는 양치기 처녀 마르셀라가 들어가는 것을 보았던 바로 그 숲으로 들어섰는데, 그녀를 찾으려고 두 시간 넘게 사방을 헤매고도 끝내 찾지 못하자, 부드러운 풀이 덮인 어느 빈터에 다다라 발길을 멈추었으니, 그 곁으로는 시원하고 상쾌한 시냇물이 흘러, 이맘때면 벌써 견디기 힘들 만큼 거세지기 시작하는 한낮의 더위가 가실 동안 그곳에서 쉬어 가도록 그들을 청하고 또 떠밀었던 것이다."],["Don Quixote and Sancho dismounted, and turning Rocinante and the ass loose to feed on the grass that was there in abundance, they ransacked the alforjas, and without any ceremony very peacefully and sociably master and man made their repast on what they found in them.","돈키호테와 산초는 말에서 내려, 로시난테와 당나귀를 풀어 그곳에 무성히 자란 풀을 뜯게 하고는, 안장주머니를 뒤져 격식 차릴 것 없이 매우 평화롭고 정답게 주인과 하인이 그 안에서 찾아낸 것으로 끼니를 때웠다."]],[["Sancho had not thought it worth while to hobble Rocinante, feeling sure, from what he knew of his staidness and freedom from incontinence, that all the mares in the Cordova pastures would not lead him into an impropriety.","산초는 로시난테의 다리를 묶어 둘 필요까지는 없다고 여겼으니, 그가 워낙 점잖고 욕정을 다스릴 줄 아는 말임을 알기에, 코르도바 목장의 암말을 죄다 데려다 놓아도 그를 무례한 짓으로 이끌지는 못하리라 굳게 믿었던 것이다."],["Chance, however, and the devil, who is not always asleep, so ordained it that feeding in this valley there was a drove of Galician ponies belonging to certain Yanguesan carriers, whose way it is to take their midday rest with their teams in places and spots where grass and water abound; and that where Don Quixote chanced to be suited the Yanguesans’ purpose very well.","그러나 우연이, 그리고 결코 늘 잠들어 있지만은 않은 악마가 일을 이렇게 꾸며 놓았으니, 이 골짜기에는 어느 양구에스 마부들이 부리는 갈리시아산 조랑말 떼가 풀을 뜯고 있었는데, 이 마부들은 풀과 물이 넉넉한 곳과 장소에서 자기네 짐승들과 더불어 한낮의 휴식을 취하는 것이 관례였고, 돈키호테가 마침 머문 그곳이 양구에스 사람들의 목적에 딱 들어맞았던 것이다."],["It so happened, then, that Rocinante took a fancy to disport himself with their ladyships the ponies, and abandoning his usual gait and demeanour as he scented them, he, without asking leave of his master, got up a briskish little trot and hastened to make known his wishes to them; they, however, it seemed, preferred their pasture to him, and received him with their heels and teeth to such effect that they soon broke his girths and left him naked without a saddle to cover him; but what must have been worse to him was that the carriers, seeing the violence he was offering to their mares, came running up armed with stakes, and so belaboured him that they brought him sorely battered to the ground.","그리하여 일이 이렇게 벌어졌으니, 로시난테가 그 조랑말 마님들과 어울려 놀아 보고 싶은 마음이 동하여, 그네들의 냄새를 맡자 평소의 걸음걸이와 몸가짐을 내팽개치고는, 주인의 허락도 구하지 않은 채 제법 활기찬 종종걸음을 일으켜 자기 소망을 그네들에게 알리고자 서둘러 달려갔으나, 그네들은 아무래도 그보다 풀이 더 좋았던 듯, 발굽과 이빨로 그를 어찌나 호되게 맞이했던지 곧 그의 뱃대끈을 끊어 버려 그를 안장도 없는 알몸으로 만들어 놓았다. 그러나 그에게 더 고약했을 일은, 마부들이 그가 자기네 암말들에게 가하는 행패를 보고는 몽둥이로 무장한 채 달려와 그를 어찌나 두들겨 댔던지 그를 흠씬 얻어맞은 꼴로 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨려 놓은 것이었다."]],[["By this time Don Quixote and Sancho, who had witnessed the drubbing of Rocinante, came up panting, and said Don Quixote to Sancho:","이때 로시난테가 매질당하는 광경을 목격한 돈키호테와 산초가 헐떡이며 달려왔고, 돈키호테가 산초에게 말했다."]],[["“So far as I can see, friend Sancho, these are not knights but base folk of low birth: I mention it because thou canst lawfully aid me in taking due vengeance for the insult offered to Rocinante before our eyes.”","\"산초 친구, 내가 보기에 이자들은 기사가 아니라 천한 태생의 비루한 무리일세. 내가 이 말을 꺼내는 까닭은, 우리 눈앞에서 로시난테에게 가해진 모욕에 대해 마땅한 복수를 하는 데 자네가 정당하게 나를 도울 수 있기 때문이라네.\""]],[["“What the devil vengeance can we take,”","\"대체 무슨 빌어먹을 복수를 한단 말입니까.\""],["answered Sancho, “if they are more than twenty, and we no more than two, or, indeed, perhaps not more than one and a half?”","산초가 대답했다. \"저자들은 스무 명이 넘고 우리는 둘밖에, 아니 어쩌면 한 명하고 반밖에 안 되는데 말입니다.\""]],[["“I count for a hundred,”","\"나는 백 명 몫일세.\" 돈키호테가 대꾸하고는, 더는 말없이 칼을 뽑아 양구에스 사람들에게 달려들었고, 주인의 본보기에 자극받고 떠밀린 산초도 그와 똑같이 했다."],["replied Don Quixote, and without more words he drew his sword and attacked the Yanguesans and excited and impelled by the example of his master, Sancho did the same; and to begin with, Don Quixote delivered a slash at one of them that laid open the leather jerkin he wore, together with a great portion of his shoulder.","그리하여 우선 돈키호테가 그중 한 사람에게 일격을 가하여, 그가 걸친 가죽 윗옷을 어깨의 상당 부분과 함께 갈라 놓았다. 양구에스 사람들은 그토록 수가 많은데도 고작 두 사람에게 공격당하는 꼴을 보고는 저마다 몽둥이를 집어 들고, 두 사람을 한복판으로 몰아넣은 다음 대단한 열의와 기운으로 두들겨 패기 시작했다."],["The Yanguesans, seeing themselves assaulted by only two men while they were so many, betook themselves to their stakes, and driving the two into the middle they began to lay on with great zeal and energy; in fact, at the second blow they brought Sancho to the ground, and Don Quixote fared the same way, all his skill and high mettle availing him nothing, and fate willed it that he should fall at the feet of Rocinante, who had not yet risen; whereby it may be seen how furiously stakes can pound in angry boorish hands.","사실 두 번째 가격에 그들은 산초를 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸고, 돈키호테도 똑같은 꼴을 당했으니, 그의 온갖 무예와 드높은 기개도 아무 소용이 없었다. 게다가 운명은 그가 아직 일어나지도 못한 로시난테의 발치에 쓰러지도록 정해 놓았으니, 이를 보면 성난 촌놈들의 손에 들린 몽둥이가 얼마나 사납게 후려칠 수 있는지를 알 만하다."]],[["Then, seeing the mischief they had done, the Yanguesans with all the haste they could loaded their team and pursued their journey, leaving the two adventurers a sorry sight and in sorrier mood.","그러고 나서 양구에스 사람들은 자기들이 저질러 놓은 행패를 보고는 부랴부랴 짐승들에게 짐을 싣고 길을 재촉하여, 두 모험가를 가련한 몰골에다 더욱 가련한 심정으로 남겨 두고 떠나 버렸다."]],[["Sancho was the first to come to, and finding himself close to his master he called to him in a weak and doleful voice, “Señor Don Quixote, ah, Señor Don Quixote!”","먼저 정신을 차린 것은 산초였으니, 자기 주인이 가까이 있는 것을 알고는 힘없고 애처로운 목소리로 그를 불렀다. \"돈키호테 나리, 아, 돈키호테 나리!\""]],[["“What wouldst thou, brother Sancho?”","\"무슨 일인가, 산초 형제?\""],["answered Don Quixote in the same feeble suffering tone as Sancho.","돈키호테가 산초와 똑같이 기운 없고 괴로워하는 목소리로 대답했다."]],[["“I would like, if it were possible,”","\"할 수만 있다면 말입니다.\" 산초 판사가 대답했다."],["answered Sancho Panza, “your worship to give me a couple of sups of that potion of the fiery Blas, if it be that you have any to hand there; perhaps it will serve for broken bones as well as for wounds.”","\"나리께서 그 불의 블라스인지 하는 물약을 두어 모금 제게 주셨으면 합니다요, 혹시 거기 가진 게 있으시다면 말입니다. 어쩌면 상처에만이 아니라 부러진 뼈에도 잘 들을지 모르지요.\""]],[["“If I only had it here, wretch that I am, what more should we want?”","\"이 가련한 신세에 그것이 여기 있기만 하다면야 더 바랄 게 무어 있겠나?\""],["said Don Quixote; “but I swear to thee, Sancho Panza, on the faith of a knight-errant, ere two days are over, unless fortune orders otherwise, I mean to have it in my possession, or my hand will have lost its cunning.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 산초 판사, 편력 기사의 신의를 걸고 자네에게 맹세하노니, 운명이 달리 정하지 않는 한 이틀이 지나기 전에 나는 그것을 내 손에 넣고야 말 것이며, 그러지 못한다면 내 손이 그 솜씨를 잃은 것이리라.\""]],[["“But in how many does your worship think we shall have the use of our feet?”","\"그런데 나리, 우리 발을 다시 쓸 수 있게 되기까지는 며칠이나 걸릴 것 같습니까?\""],["answered Sancho Panza.","산초 판사가 대답했다."]],[["“For myself I must say I cannot guess how many,” said the battered knight Don Quixote; “but I take all the blame upon myself, for I had no business to put hand to sword against men who where not dubbed knights like myself, and so I believe that in punishment for having transgressed the laws of chivalry the God of battles has permitted this chastisement to be administered to me; for which reason, brother Sancho, it is well thou shouldst receive a hint on the matter which I am now about to mention to thee, for it is of much importance to the welfare of both of us. It is that when thou shalt see rabble of this sort offering us insult thou art not to wait till I draw sword against them, for I shall not do so at all; but do thou draw sword and chastise them to thy heart’s content, and if any knights come to their aid and defence I will take care to defend thee and assail them with all my might; and thou hast already seen by a thousand signs and proofs what the might of this strong arm of mine is equal to”--so uplifted had the poor gentleman become through the victory over the stout Biscayan.","\"나로 말하자면 며칠일지 도무지 짐작이 안 가는구나.\" 흠씬 두들겨 맞은 기사 돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 나는 모든 잘못을 내 탓으로 돌리네. 나처럼 기사 서임을 받지 못한 자들에게 칼을 들이대서는 안 되는 일이었으니 말일세. 그러니 내가 기사도의 법을 어긴 벌로, 싸움의 신께서 이 징벌이 내게 내려지도록 허락하신 것이라 믿네. 그러한 까닭에, 산초 형제여, 이제 내가 자네에게 일러 둘 일에 대해 한 가지 귀띔을 받아 두는 것이 좋겠으니, 이는 우리 둘 모두의 안녕에 매우 중요한 일이라네. 그것은 곧, 이런 부류의 천한 무리가 우리에게 모욕을 가하는 것을 자네가 보거든, 내가 그들에게 칼을 뽑을 때까지 기다리지 말라는 것일세. 나는 결코 그러지 않을 것이기 때문이지. 자네가 칼을 뽑아 마음껏 그들을 응징하게나. 그러다 만약 어떤 기사들이 그들을 돕고 지키러 나선다면, 그때는 내가 온 힘을 다해 자네를 지키고 그들을 공격해 줄 것이니, 이 강한 내 팔의 힘이 어디까지 미치는지는 자네도 이미 수천 가지 표적과 증거로 보았을 걸세.\" 그 억센 비스카야인을 이긴 승리로 이 가련한 양반은 그토록 우쭐해져 있었던 것이다."]],[["But Sancho did not so fully approve of his master’s admonition as to let it pass without saying in reply, “Señor, I am a man of peace, meek and quiet, and I can put up with any affront because I have a wife and children to support and bring up; so let it be likewise a hint to your worship, as it cannot be a mandate, that on no account will I draw sword either against clown or against knight, and that here before God I forgive the insults that have been offered me, whether they have been, are, or shall be offered me by high or low, rich or poor, noble or commoner, not excepting any rank or condition whatsoever.”","그러나 산초는 주인의 그 훈계를 아무 대꾸 없이 그냥 넘길 만큼 흔쾌히 받아들이지는 않았으니, 이렇게 답하여 말했다. \"나리, 저는 평화로운 사람이라 온순하고 조용하며, 어떤 모욕도 참아 넘길 수 있는데, 이는 제게 부양하고 길러야 할 아내와 자식들이 있기 때문입니다요. 그러니 명령은 될 수 없을 테고 그저 귀띔으로나마 나리께 일러 두겠습니다만, 저는 어떤 일이 있어도 촌놈에게든 기사에게든 칼을 뽑는 일은 결코 없을 것이며, 여기 하느님 앞에서, 신분이 높든 낮든, 부자든 가난뱅이든, 귀족이든 평민이든, 그 어떤 지위나 처지의 사람도 가리지 않고, 제게 가해졌거나, 가해지고 있거나, 앞으로 가해질 모든 모욕을 용서하겠습니다요.\""]],[["To all which his master said in reply, “I wish I had breath enough to speak somewhat easily, and that the pain I feel on this side would abate so as to let me explain to thee, Panza, the mistake thou makest. Come now, sinner, suppose the wind of fortune, hitherto so adverse, should turn in our favour, filling the sails of our desires so that safely and without impediment we put into port in some one of those islands I have promised thee, how would it be with thee if on winning it I made thee lord of it? Why, thou wilt make it well-nigh impossible through not being a knight nor having any desire to be one, nor possessing the courage nor the will to avenge insults or defend thy lordship; for thou must know that in newly conquered kingdoms and provinces the minds of the inhabitants are never so quiet nor so well disposed to the new lord that there is no fear of their making some move to change matters once more, and try, as they say, what chance may do for them; so it is essential that the new possessor should have good sense to enable him to govern, and valour to attack and defend himself, whatever may befall him.”","이 모든 말에 그의 주인이 답하여 말했다. \"내게 좀 수월하게 말할 만큼 숨이 붙어 있고, 이 옆구리에 느껴지는 통증이 가라앉아 자네의 그 잘못을 일러 줄 수 있으면 좋으련만. 자, 이 죄 많은 친구야, 여태껏 그토록 우리에게 등을 돌렸던 운명의 바람이 우리 편으로 돌아서서, 우리 소망의 돛을 가득 채워 아무 탈도 거침도 없이 내가 자네에게 약속한 그 섬들 가운데 하나로 무사히 입항한다고 치세. 만약 그것을 손에 넣어 내가 자네를 그곳의 영주로 삼는다면 자네 처지가 어찌 되겠나? 아니, 자네는 기사도 아니고 기사가 되려는 마음도 없으며, 모욕에 복수하거나 자기 영지를 지킬 용기도 의지도 없으니, 그 일을 거의 불가능하게 만들고 말 걸세. 자네도 알아 두어야 할 것이, 새로 정복한 왕국이나 속주에서는 주민들의 마음이 새 영주에게 결코 그리 평온하지도, 그리 우호적으로 기울지도 않아, 다시 한번 형세를 뒤엎으려 어떤 움직임을 꾀하고는, 사람들 말마따나 운수가 자기들에게 무엇을 가져다줄지 시험해 보려 할 우려가 늘 있다네. 그러니 새 영주는 다스릴 수 있는 분별력과, 무슨 일이 닥치든 공격하고 자신을 지킬 수 있는 용맹을 갖추는 것이 필수란 말일세.\""]],[["“In what has now befallen us,”","\"방금 우리에게 닥친 일에서야말로,\""],["answered Sancho, “I’d have been well pleased to have that good sense and that valour your worship speaks of, but I swear on the faith of a poor man I am more fit for plasters than for arguments.","산초가 대답했다. \"나리께서 말씀하시는 그 분별력과 그 용맹을 제가 지녔더라면 참 기뻤을 텐데요, 허나 가난뱅이의 신의를 걸고 맹세하건대 저는 논쟁보다는 고약(膏藥)이 더 어울리는 사람입니다요."],["See if your worship can get up, and let us help Rocinante, though he does not deserve it, for he was the main cause of all this thrashing.","나리께서 일어나실 수 있는지 보시고, 우리 로시난테를 좀 도와줍시다. 그럴 자격도 없는 놈이긴 합니다만, 이 매타작의 주된 원인이 그놈이었으니 말입니다."],["I never thought it of Rocinante, for I took him to be a virtuous person and as quiet as myself.","저는 로시난테가 그럴 줄은 정말 몰랐습니다요. 저는 그놈을 점잖은 인물에다 저처럼 조용한 녀석이라 여겼거든요."],["After all, they say right that it takes a long time to come to know people, and that there is nothing sure in this life.","결국 사람을 알기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸리고, 이 세상에 확실한 건 아무것도 없다는 말이 옳습니다요."],["Who would have said that, after such mighty slashes as your worship gave that unlucky knight-errant, there was coming, travelling post and at the very heels of them, such a great storm of sticks as has fallen upon our shoulders?”","나리께서 그 운수 사나운 편력 기사에게 그토록 어마어마한 칼질을 하신 다음에, 그 뒤를 바짝 쫓아 우편마차처럼 빨리, 우리 어깨 위로 이런 몽둥이 폭풍이 쏟아져 내릴 줄을 누가 알았겠습니까요?\""]],[["“And yet thine, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote, “ought to be used to such squalls; but mine, reared in soft cloth and fine linen, it is plain they must feel more keenly the pain of this mishap, and if it were not that I imagine--why do I say imagine?--know of a certainty that all these annoyances are very necessary accompaniments of the calling of arms, I would lay me down here to die of pure vexation.”","\"그래도 자네 어깨는, 산초,\" 돈키호테가 대꾸했다. \"그런 돌풍에는 이골이 났을 게 분명하네. 허나 부드러운 천과 고운 아마포 속에서 자라난 내 어깨는, 이 봉변의 고통을 더 사무치게 느낄 수밖에 없을 게 뻔하지. 만약 내가 이 모든 성가신 일이 무예라는 소명에 반드시 따라붙는 것임을 상상하지 않는다면 — 아니, 상상이라니 무슨 말을, 확실히 안다고 해야지 — 나는 순전히 분한 마음에 여기 드러누워 죽어 버렸을 걸세.\""]],[["To this the squire replied, “Señor, as these mishaps are what one reaps of chivalry, tell me if they happen very often, or if they have their own fixed times for coming to pass; because it seems to me that after two harvests we shall be no good for the third, unless God in his infinite mercy helps us.”","이에 종자가 답했다. \"나리, 이런 봉변이 기사도에서 거두는 수확이라면, 그것이 아주 자주 일어나는지, 아니면 일어나는 데 저 나름의 정해진 철이라도 있는지 일러 주십시오. 제가 보기엔 두 차례 수확이 끝나고 나면, 하느님께서 그 무한한 자비로 우리를 도와주시지 않는 한, 세 번째 수확에는 우리가 아무짝에도 쓸모없게 될 것 같으니 말입니다요.\""]],[["“Know, friend Sancho,” answered Don Quixote, “that the life of knights-errant is subject to a thousand dangers and reverses, and neither more nor less is it within immediate possibility for knights-errant to become kings and emperors, as experience has shown in the case of many different knights with whose histories I am thoroughly acquainted; and I could tell thee now, if the pain would let me, of some who simply by might of arm have risen to the high stations I have mentioned; and those same, both before and after, experienced divers misfortunes and miseries; for the valiant Amadis of Gaul found himself in the power of his mortal enemy Arcalaus the magician, who, it is positively asserted, holding him captive, gave him more than two hundred lashes with the reins of his horse while tied to one of the pillars of a court; and moreover there is a certain recondite author of no small authority who says that the Knight of Phoebus, being caught in a certain pitfall, which opened under his feet in a certain castle, on falling found himself bound hand and foot in a deep pit underground, where they administered to him one of those things they call clysters, of sand and snow-water, that well-nigh finished him; and if he had not been succoured in that sore extremity by a sage, a great friend of his, it would have gone very hard with the poor knight; so I may well suffer in company with such worthy folk, for greater were the indignities which they had to suffer than those which we suffer. For I would have thee know, Sancho, that wounds caused by any instruments which happen by chance to be in hand inflict no indignity, and this is laid down in the law of the duel in express words: if, for instance, the cobbler strikes another with the last which he has in his hand, though it be in fact a piece of wood, it cannot be said for that reason that he whom he struck with it has been cudgelled. I say this lest thou shouldst imagine that because we have been drubbed in this affray we have therefore suffered any indignity; for the arms those men carried, with which they pounded us, were nothing more than their stakes, and not one of them, so far as I remember, carried rapier, sword, or dagger.”","\"알아 두게, 산초 친구,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"편력 기사의 삶이란 수천 가지 위험과 좌절을 겪게 마련이지만, 그에 못지않게 편력 기사가 왕이나 황제가 되는 일도 당장 일어날 수 있는 일이라네. 이는 내가 그 행적을 속속들이 꿰고 있는 여러 다른 기사들의 경우에서 경험으로 드러난 바이지. 통증만 허락한다면 지금이라도 자네에게 들려줄 수 있으련만, 오로지 팔의 힘만으로 내가 말한 그 높은 지위에 오른 이들의 이야기를 말일세. 그러나 그들 또한 그 전이나 그 후에 갖가지 불운과 고초를 겪었다네. 용맹한 갈리아의 아마디스는 자신의 철천지원수인 마법사 아르칼라우스의 손아귀에 떨어졌는데, 단언컨대 아르칼라우스는 그를 포로로 잡아 궁정의 기둥 하나에 묶어 놓고는 말고삐로 이백 대가 넘게 후려쳤다고 하네. 더욱이 적잖은 권위를 지닌 어느 박학한 작가가 말하기를, 포에보스의 기사는 어느 성에서 발밑이 꺼지며 벌어진 어떤 함정에 빠져, 떨어지고 보니 땅속 깊은 구덩이에서 손발이 묶인 채였는데, 그곳에서 그들이 그에게 모래와 눈 녹인 물로 만든, 이른바 관장(灌腸)이라는 것을 베풀어 그를 거의 끝장낼 뻔했다는 것일세. 만약 그 위급한 지경에서 그의 절친한 벗인 한 현자가 그를 구해 주지 않았더라면, 그 가련한 기사는 매우 험한 꼴을 당했을 걸세. 그러니 나도 그토록 훌륭한 분들과 더불어 고초를 겪는 것쯤이야 능히 감내할 수 있지, 그분들이 견뎌야 했던 모욕은 우리가 견디는 것보다 더 큰 것이었으니 말이네. 산초, 자네가 알아 두어야 할 것이, 우연히 손에 잡힌 어떤 도구로 입은 상처는 결코 모욕이 되지 않는데, 이는 결투의 법에 명백한 말로 규정되어 있다네. 이를테면 구두장이가 손에 든 골틀로 남을 친다면, 그것이 사실은 한낱 나무토막일지언정, 그것으로 얻어맞은 자가 몽둥이질을 당했다고는 말할 수 없는 것이지. 내가 이 말을 하는 까닭은, 우리가 이번 싸움에서 두들겨 맞았다고 해서 무슨 모욕을 당한 것이라고 자네가 여길까 봐서일세. 우리를 두들긴 그자들이 들고 있던 무기란 그저 몽둥이에 지나지 않았으며, 내 기억으로는 그들 중 누구도 양날칼이나 검이나 단검을 지니지 않았으니 말이네.\""]],[["“They gave me no time to see that much,”","\"제겐 그런 것까지 살펴볼 틈도 주지 않았습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다."],["answered Sancho, “for hardly had I laid hand on my tizona when they signed the cross on my shoulders with their sticks in such style that they took the sight out of my eyes and the strength out of my feet, stretching me where I now lie, and where thinking of whether all those stake-strokes were an indignity or not gives me no uneasiness, which the pain of the blows does, for they will remain as deeply impressed on my memory as on my shoulders.”","\"제가 미처 제 티소나에 손을 대기도 전에 그자들이 몽둥이로 제 어깨 위에 어찌나 멋들어지게 십자가를 그어 댔는지, 제 눈에선 시야를 앗아 가고 제 다리에선 힘을 앗아 가, 지금 제가 누워 있는 이 자리에 뻗어 버리게 만들었거든요. 그래서 그 몽둥이질이 다 모욕이었는지 아니었는지를 생각하는 일은 제게 아무 걱정거리도 안 됩니다요만, 매 맞은 고통은 그렇지가 않으니, 그것이 제 어깨에 깊이 새겨진 만큼이나 제 기억에도 깊이 새겨져 남을 테니까요.\""]],[["“For all that let me tell thee, brother Panza,”","\"그래도 한마디 일러 두겠네, 판사 형제,\""],["said Don Quixote, “that there is no recollection which time does not put an end to, and no pain which death does not remove.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"세월이 끝장내지 못하는 기억이란 없고, 죽음이 거두어 가지 못하는 고통이란 없는 법일세.\""]],[["“And what greater misfortune can there be,” replied Panza, “than the one that waits for time to put an end to it and death to remove it?","\"한데 세월이 끝장내 주기를, 죽음이 거두어 가 주기를 기다려야 하는 것보다 더 큰 불행이 어디 있겠습니까요?\""],["If our mishap were one of those that are cured with a couple of plasters, it would not be so bad; but I am beginning to think that all the plasters in a hospital almost won’t be enough to put us right.”","판사가 대꾸했다. \"우리 봉변이 고약 두어 장이면 낫는 그런 것이라면 그리 나쁘진 않을 텐데요, 병원에 있는 고약을 죄다 갖다 붙여도 우리를 멀쩡하게 만들기엔 거의 모자랄 것 같다는 생각이 들기 시작하는걸요.\""]],[["“No more of that: pluck strength out of weakness, Sancho, as I mean to do,” returned Don Quixote, “and let us see how Rocinante is, for it seems to me that not the least share of this mishap has fallen to the lot of the poor beast.”","\"그만하게. 산초, 약함 속에서 힘을 끌어내게나, 나도 그러려 하니.\" 돈키호테가 응수했다. \"그리고 로시난테가 어떤지 좀 보세. 이 봉변의 몫 가운데 적지 않은 것이 저 가련한 짐승에게 떨어진 듯하니 말일세.\""]],[["“There is nothing wonderful in that,”","\"그야 이상할 게 없지요.\" 산초가 대꾸했다."],["replied Sancho, “since he is a knight-errant too; what I wonder at is that my beast should have come off scot-free where we come out scotched.”","\"그놈도 편력 기사이니 말입니다요. 제가 이상하게 여기는 건, 우리는 이렇게 흠씬 두들겨 맞았는데 제 당나귀 녀석은 멀쩡하게 빠져나왔다는 겁니다요.\""]],[["“Fortune always leaves a door open in adversity in order to bring relief to it,”","\"운명은 역경 속에서도 그것을 덜어 줄 길을 가져다주려고 늘 문 하나를 열어 두는 법일세.\""],["said Don Quixote; “I say so because this little beast may now supply the want of Rocinante, carrying me hence to some castle where I may be cured of my wounds.","돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 이렇게 말하는 까닭은, 이 작은 짐승이 이제 로시난테의 빈자리를 메워, 내 상처를 치료받을 수 있는 어느 성으로 나를 데려다줄 수 있기 때문이지."],["And moreover I shall not hold it any dishonour to be so mounted, for I remember having read how the good old Silenus, the tutor and instructor of the gay god of laughter, when he entered the city of the hundred gates, went very contentedly mounted on a handsome ass.”","더욱이 나는 그렇게 올라타는 것을 조금도 불명예로 여기지 않을 것이니, 옛 선량한 실레노스가 — 그 흥겨운 웃음의 신을 가르친 스승이자 인도자 말일세 — 백 개의 성문이 있는 도시에 들어갈 때, 멋진 당나귀에 올라타고 매우 흡족하게 갔다는 글을 읽은 기억이 나기 때문이라네.\""]],[["“It may be true that he went mounted as your worship says,”","\"나리 말씀대로 그분이 올라타고 갔다는 건 사실일지 몰라도,\""],["answered Sancho, “but there is a great difference between going mounted and going slung like a sack of manure.”","산초가 대답했다. \"올라타고 가는 것과 거름 자루처럼 둘러메여 가는 것 사이에는 큰 차이가 있습죠.\""]],[["To which Don Quixote replied, “Wounds received in battle confer honour instead of taking it away; and so, friend Panza, say no more, but, as I told thee before, get up as well as thou canst and put me on top of thy beast in whatever fashion pleases thee best, and let us go hence ere night come on and surprise us in these wilds.”","이에 돈키호테가 답했다. \"싸움에서 입은 상처는 명예를 앗아 가기는커녕 오히려 명예를 안겨 주는 법일세. 그러니 판사 친구, 더는 말 말고, 앞서 자네에게 말했듯 할 수 있는 만큼 일어나 자네 마음에 가장 드는 대로 나를 자네 짐승 위에 얹게나. 그리고 밤이 닥쳐 이 황야에서 우리를 덮치기 전에 어서 떠나세.\""]],[["“And yet I have heard your worship say,”","\"그렇지만 나리께서 말씀하시는 걸 들은 적이 있는데요,\""],["observed Panza, “that it is very meet for knights-errant to sleep in wastes and deserts, and that they esteem it very good fortune.”","판사가 한마디 했다. \"편력 기사가 황무지나 사막에서 잠자는 것은 매우 마땅한 일이며, 그것을 큰 행운으로 여긴다고 하시지 않았습니까요.\""]],[["“That is,” said Don Quixote, “when they cannot help it, or when they are in love; and so true is this that there have been knights who have remained two years on rocks, in sunshine and shade and all the inclemencies of heaven, without their ladies knowing anything of it; and one of these was Amadis, when, under the name of Beltenebros, he took up his abode on the Pena Pobre for--I know not if it was eight years or eight months, for I am not very sure of the reckoning; at any rate he stayed there doing penance for I know not what pique the Princess Oriana had against him; but no more of this now, Sancho, and make haste before a mishap like Rocinante’s befalls the ass.”","\"그건,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"어쩔 도리가 없을 때나, 사랑에 빠져 있을 때 그렇다는 말일세. 이는 어찌나 사실인지, 어떤 기사들은 자기 여인이 그것을 까맣게 모르는 채로, 볕과 그늘과 하늘의 온갖 모진 날씨를 견디며 바위 위에서 두 해를 지낸 적도 있다네. 그중 한 사람이 바로 아마디스인데, 그는 벨테네브로스라는 이름으로 페냐 포브레에 거처를 잡고 — 그것이 여덟 해였는지 여덟 달이었는지는 모르겠으나, 셈에 그리 자신이 없어서 말일세 — 어쨌든 거기 머물며 고행을 했는데, 오리아나 공주가 그에게 품은 무슨 노여움 때문이었는지는 나도 잘 모르겠네. 허나 이제 그 이야기는 그만하세, 산초, 그리고 로시난테 같은 봉변이 당나귀에게 닥치기 전에 어서 서두르게.\""]],[["“The very devil would be in it in that case,”","\"그렇게 된다면야 정말 악마가 끼어든 셈이겠지요.\""],["said Sancho; and letting off thirty “ohs,”","산초가 말했다. 그러고는 \"아이고\""],["and sixty sighs, and a hundred and twenty maledictions and execrations on whomsoever it was that had brought him there, he raised himself, stopping half-way bent like a Turkish bow without power to bring himself upright, but with all his pains he saddled his ass, who too had gone astray somewhat, yielding to the excessive licence of the day; he next raised up Rocinante, and as for him, had he possessed a tongue to complain with, most assuredly neither Sancho nor his master would have been behind him.","소리를 서른 번, 한숨을 예순 번, 그리고 자기를 이런 데로 데려온 자가 누구든 간에 그에게 퍼붓는 저주와 악담을 백스무 번이나 쏟아 낸 뒤, 몸을 일으켰으나 중간에서 터키 활처럼 굽은 채 멈추어 몸을 곧추세울 힘이 없었지만, 온갖 고통을 무릅쓰고 당나귀에 안장을 얹었으니, 그 녀석 또한 그날의 지나친 방종에 못 이겨 다소 길을 벗어났던 참이었다. 그다음 그는 로시난테를 일으켜 세웠는데, 그놈으로 말하자면 하소연할 혀라도 있었더라면 분명 산초도 그 주인도 그에 뒤지지 않았을 것이다."]],[["To be brief, Sancho fixed Don Quixote on the ass and secured Rocinante with a leading rein, and taking the ass by the halter, he proceeded more or less in the direction in which it seemed to him the high road might be; and, as chance was conducting their affairs for them from good to better, he had not gone a short league when the road came in sight, and on it he perceived an inn, which to his annoyance and to the delight of Don Quixote must needs be a castle.","간단히 말하자면, 산초는 돈키호테를 당나귀 위에 앉히고 로시난테는 끌채에 매어 둔 다음, 당나귀의 고삐를 잡고서 큰길이 있을 법한 방향이라 여겨지는 쪽으로 얼추 나아갔다. 그런데 우연이 그들의 일을 나쁜 데서 좋은 데로 이끌어 가던 터라, 한참도 못 가 길이 눈에 들어왔고, 그 길 위로 어느 객줏집이 보였는데, 산초에게는 분하게도, 돈키호테에게는 기쁘게도, 그것은 영락없이 성이어야만 했다."],["Sancho insisted that it was an inn, and his master that it was not one, but a castle, and the dispute lasted so long that before the point was settled they had time to reach it, and into it Sancho entered with all his team without any further controversy.","산초는 그것이 객줏집이라 우겼고, 그의 주인은 객줏집이 아니라 성이라 우겨, 그 다툼이 어찌나 오래 끌었던지 결판이 나기도 전에 그들은 거기 다다를 만큼의 시간이 흘렀고, 산초는 더는 옥신각신할 것 없이 자기 짐승들을 죄다 거느리고 그 안으로 들어갔다. * 티소나: 엘 시드의 명검 이름으로, 여기서는 산초가 익살스럽게 자기 칼을 일컫는 말이다."]],[["# CHAPTER XVI. OF WHAT HAPPENED TO THE INGENIOUS GENTLEMAN IN THE INN WHICH HE TOOK TO BE A CASTLE","# CHAPTER XVI."]],[["The innkeeper, seeing Don Quixote slung across the ass, asked Sancho what was amiss with him.","돈키호테가 당나귀에 가로질러 실려 오는 것을 본 객줏집 주인은 산초에게 그에게 무슨 일이 있었느냐고 물었다. 산초는 별일 아니며, 다만 바위에서 떨어져 갈비뼈를 좀 다쳤을 뿐이라고 대답했다."],["Sancho answered that it was nothing, only that he had fallen down from a rock and had his ribs a little bruised.","객줏집 주인에게는 아내가 하나 있었는데, 같은 일을 하는 여인네들이 흔히 그러한 성정과는 사뭇 다른 사람이었다."],["The innkeeper had a wife whose disposition was not such as those of her calling commonly have, for she was by nature kind-hearted and felt for the sufferings of her neighbours, so she at once set about tending Don Quixote, and made her young daughter, a very comely girl, help her in taking care of her guest.","천성이 마음씨가 곱고 이웃의 고통을 함께 아파할 줄 알았기에, 그녀는 곧장 돈키호테를 돌보기 시작했으며, 무척 어여쁜 처녀인 어린 딸에게 손님을 보살피는 일을 거들게 했다. 그 밖에도 객줏집에는 하녀로 일하는 아스투리아스 처녀 하나가 있었는데, 얼굴이 넓적하고 뒤통수가 납작하며 코가 들창코였고, 한쪽 눈은 멀었으며 다른 쪽 눈도 그리 성치 않았다."],["There was besides in the inn, as servant, an Asturian lass with a broad face, flat poll, and snub nose, blind of one eye and not very sound in the other.","그래도 그 몸매의 우아함이 모든 결점을 메워 주었으니, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 일곱 뼘이 채 되지 않았고, 그녀를 다소 짓누르는 어깨 탓에 그녀는 본의 아니게 자주 땅바닥을 들여다보게 되었다."],["The elegance of her shape, to be sure, made up for all her defects; she did not measure seven palms from head to foot, and her shoulders, which overweighted her somewhat, made her contemplate the ground more than she liked.","어쨌든 이 맵시 있는 처녀가 어린 딸을 도왔고, 둘은 함께 돈키호테에게 어느 다락방에 몹시 형편없는 잠자리를 마련해 주었다."],["This graceful lass, then, helped the young girl, and the two made up a very bad bed for Don Quixote in a garret that showed evident signs of having formerly served for many years as a straw-loft, in which there was also quartered a carrier whose bed was placed a little beyond our Don Quixote’s, and, though only made of the pack-saddles and cloths of his mules, had much the advantage of it, as Don Quixote’s consisted simply of four rough boards on two not very even trestles, a mattress, that for thinness might have passed for a quilt, full of pellets which, were they not seen through the rents to be wool, would to the touch have seemed pebbles in hardness, two sheets made of buckler leather, and a coverlet the threads of which anyone that chose might have counted without missing one in the reckoning.","그 다락방은 오랜 세월 짚 곳간으로 쓰였던 흔적이 역력했으며, 거기에는 마부 한 사람도 묵고 있었는데, 그의 잠자리는 우리의 돈키호테 것보다 조금 떨어진 곳에 놓여 있었고, 비록 노새들의 길마와 천 조각들로만 꾸민 것이었으나 돈키호테의 것보다는 훨씬 나았다. 돈키호테의 잠자리란 그저 그리 평평하지도 않은 두 받침대 위에 거친 널빤지 네 장을 얹은 것에, 얇기로 말하자면 누비이불이라 해도 될 만한 매트리스 하나를 깐 것이었으니, 그 매트리스는 솜뭉치로 가득 찼으되 그것이 찢어진 틈으로 양털임을 보지 못했더라면 손끝에는 자갈처럼 단단하게 느껴졌을 정도였고, 방패 가죽으로 만든 듯한 홑이불 두 장과, 그 올을 세려는 사람이라면 누구든 하나도 빠뜨리지 않고 셀 수 있을 만한 덮개 한 장이 전부였다."]],[["On this accursed bed Don Quixote stretched himself, and the hostess and her daughter soon covered him with plasters from top to toe, while Maritornes--for that was the name of the Asturian--held the light for them, and while plastering him, the hostess, observing how full of wheals Don Quixote was in some places, remarked that this had more the look of blows than of a fall.","이 저주받은 잠자리 위에 돈키호테가 몸을 뉘었고, 안주인과 그 딸은 곧 머리끝에서 발끝까지 그에게 고약을 발라 주었으며, 그동안 마리토르네스는 — 이것이 그 아스투리아스 처녀의 이름이었다 — 그들을 위해 등불을 들어 주었다. 그리고 고약을 바르던 안주인은 돈키호테의 몇몇 군데가 멍울투성이인 것을 보고는, 이것이 떨어진 것이라기보다는 매를 맞은 자국에 가깝다고 한마디 했다."]],[["It was not blows, Sancho said, but that the rock had many points and projections, and that each of them had left its mark.","매를 맞은 것이 아니라, 바위에 모서리와 돌출부가 많아 그 하나하나가 자국을 남긴 것이라고 산초는 말했다. \"부인,\""],["“Pray, señora,”","그가 덧붙였다. \"마(麻)를 좀 남겨 두시지요."],["he added, “manage to save some tow, as there will be no want of some one to use it, for my loins too are rather sore.”","쓸 사람이 없지 않을 테니까요. 제 허리도 꽤 쑤신답니다.\""]],[["“Then you must have fallen too,”","\"그렇다면 댁도 떨어진 모양이군요.\""],["said the hostess.","안주인이 말했다."]],[["“I did not fall,”","\"전 떨어지지 않았습니다.\""],["said Sancho Panza, “but from the shock I got at seeing my master fall, my body aches so that I feel as if I had had a thousand thwacks.”","산초 판사가 말했다. \"다만 제 주인 나리가 떨어지는 걸 본 충격에 온몸이 쑤셔서, 마치 매를 천 대쯤 맞은 것 같은 기분입니다.\""]],[["“That may well be,”","\"그럴 만도 하지요.\""],["said the young girl, “for it has many a time happened to me to dream that I was falling down from a tower and never coming to the ground, and when I awoke from the dream to find myself as weak and shaken as if I had really fallen.”","어린 처녀가 말했다. \"저도 탑에서 떨어지면서 땅에는 도무지 닿지 않는 꿈을 여러 번 꾸었는데, 그 꿈에서 깨어나 보면 정말로 떨어지기라도 한 양 몸이 나른하고 흔들렸거든요.\""]],[["“There is the point, señora,”","\"바로 그 점입니다, 부인.\""],["replied Sancho Panza, “that I without dreaming at all, but being more awake than I am now, find myself with scarcely less wheals than my master, Don Quixote.”","산초 판사가 대꾸했다. \"저는 꿈 따위는 전혀 꾸지 않고, 지금보다도 더 멀쩡히 깨어 있었는데도, 제 주인 돈키호테 나리에 못지않게 멍울투성이가 되었으니 말입니다.\""]],[["“How is the gentleman called?”","\"그 양반은 어떻게 불리시오?\""],["asked Maritornes the Asturian.","아스투리아스 처녀 마리토르네스가 물었다."]],[["“Don Quixote of La Mancha,”","\"라만차의 돈키호테시지요.\""],["answered Sancho Panza, “and he is a knight-adventurer, and one of the best and stoutest that have been seen in the world this long time past.”","산초 판사가 대답했다. \"방랑 기사이신데, 오랜 세월 이 세상에서 볼 수 있었던 이들 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 굳센 분들 중 하나시랍니다.\""]],[["“What is a knight-adventurer?”","\"방랑 기사가 뭔데요?\""],["said the lass.","처녀가 말했다."]],[["“Are you so new in the world as not to know?”","\"세상 물정에 그리도 어두워 그걸 모르시나?\" 산초 판사가 대답했다."],["answered Sancho Panza.","\"그럼, 들어 보시게, 아가씨."],["“Well, then, you must know, sister, that a knight-adventurer is a thing that in two words is seen drubbed and emperor, that is to-day the most miserable and needy being in the world, and to-morrow will have two or three crowns of kingdoms to give his squire.”","방랑 기사란 한마디로 말하자면, 두들겨 맞은 신세였다가 황제가 되기도 하는 그런 존재라네. 오늘은 세상에서 가장 비참하고 가장 궁핍한 자였다가, 내일은 자기 종자에게 나누어 줄 왕국의 왕관을 두세 개씩 가지게 되는 그런 분이지.\""]],[["“Then how is it,”","\"그렇다면 어찌하여,\""],["said the hostess, “that belonging to so good a master as this, you have not, to judge by appearances, even so much as a county?”","안주인이 말했다. \"이렇게 훌륭한 주인을 모시는 분이, 겉모습으로 보아하니 백작령 하나조차 가지지 못한 거요?\""]],[["“It is too soon yet,” answered Sancho, “for we have only been a month going in quest of adventures, and so far we have met with nothing that can be called one, for it will happen that when one thing is looked for another thing is found; however, if my master Don Quixote gets well of this wound, or fall, and I am left none the worse of it, I would not change my hopes for the best title in Spain.”","\"아직 너무 이르지요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"모험을 찾아 길을 나선 지 겨우 한 달밖에 안 됐으니까요. 게다가 지금껏 모험이라 할 만한 건 하나도 만나지 못했답니다. 한 가지를 찾다 보면 다른 게 걸리기 마련이니 말이지요. 어쨌든 제 주인 돈키호테 나리께서 이 상처인지 낙상인지에서 쾌차하시고, 제가 그로 인해 더 나빠지지만 않는다면, 저는 제 희망을 스페인에서 가장 좋은 작위와도 바꾸지 않겠습니다.\""]],[["To all this conversation Don Quixote was listening very attentively, and sitting up in bed as well as he could, and taking the hostess by the hand he said to her, “Believe me, fair lady, you may call yourself fortunate in having in this castle of yours sheltered my person, which is such that if I do not myself praise it, it is because of what is commonly said, that self-praise debaseth; but my squire will inform you who I am. I only tell you that I shall preserve for ever inscribed on my memory the service you have rendered me in order to tender you my gratitude while life shall last me; and would to Heaven love held me not so enthralled and subject to its laws and to the eyes of that fair ingrate whom I name between my teeth, but that those of this lovely damsel might be the masters of my liberty.”","이 모든 대화를 돈키호테는 무척 주의 깊게 듣고 있었으며, 할 수 있는 한 잠자리에서 몸을 일으켜 앉아 안주인의 손을 잡고는 이렇게 말했다. \"고운 부인, 내 말을 믿으시오. 당신은 이 성에서 이 몸을 거두어 주었으니 스스로를 행운아라 여기셔도 좋소. 내 입으로 이 몸을 칭송하지 않는 것은, 흔히들 말하듯 제 자랑은 스스로를 깎아내린다고 하기 때문이오. 허나 내가 누구인지는 내 종자가 일러 줄 것이오. 다만 내 이르노니, 당신이 내게 베푼 그 수고를 내 기억에 영원히 새겨 두어, 목숨이 다하는 날까지 그 은혜에 보답하리다. 만일 사랑이 나를 그 법도에, 그리고 내가 이 사이로 가만히 이름을 읊조리는 그 아리따운 무정한 여인의 눈동자에 이토록 옭아매어 종으로 삼지만 않았던들, 이 사랑스러운 아가씨의 눈동자야말로 내 자유의 주인이 되었으련만, 하늘이여 굽어살피소서.\""]],[["The hostess, her daughter, and the worthy Maritornes listened in bewilderment to the words of the knight-errant; for they understood about as much of them as if he had been talking Greek, though they could perceive they were all meant for expressions of good-will and blandishments; and not being accustomed to this kind of language, they stared at him and wondered to themselves, for he seemed to them a man of a different sort from those they were used to, and thanking him in pothouse phrase for his civility they left him, while the Asturian gave her attention to Sancho, who needed it no less than his master.","안주인과 그 딸, 그리고 갸륵한 마리토르네스는 이 방랑 기사의 말을 어리둥절하게 들었다. 그가 그리스어로라도 떠드는 양 그 말을 거의 알아듣지 못했으나, 그것이 모두 호의와 아첨의 표현임은 짐작할 수 있었기 때문이다. 이런 종류의 말투에 익숙하지 않았던 까닭에, 그녀들은 그를 빤히 바라보며 속으로 의아해했으니, 그가 자기들이 익히 보아 온 자들과는 사뭇 다른 부류의 사람으로 여겨졌던 것이다. 그리고 그의 정중함에 술집에서 쓰는 투의 말로 고마움을 표하고는 그를 남겨 둔 채 자리를 떴고, 한편 아스투리아스 처녀는 주인 못지않게 보살핌이 필요했던 산초를 돌보았다."]],[["The carrier had made an arrangement with her for recreation that night, and she had given him her word that when the guests were quiet and the family asleep she would come in search of him and meet his wishes unreservedly.","마부는 그날 밤 그녀와 한바탕 즐기기로 약조를 해 두었으며, 그녀는 손님들이 잠잠해지고 집안 식구들이 잠들면 그를 찾아와 그의 바람을 거리낌 없이 들어주겠노라 언약했다. 그리고 이 갸륵한 처녀로 말하자면, 비록 숲속에서 증인 하나 없이 한 약조라 할지라도 한번 그런 약속을 하면 결코 어기는 법이 없었다고 전해진다."],["And it is said of this good lass that she never made promises of the kind without fulfilling them, even though she made them in a forest and without any witness present, for she plumed herself greatly on being a lady and held it no disgrace to be in such an employment as servant in an inn, because, she said, misfortunes and ill-luck had brought her to that position.","자신이 귀부인이라는 점을 크게 자랑으로 여겼으며, 객줏집 하녀 노릇 같은 일을 부끄럽게 여기지 않았으니, 그녀의 말에 따르면 불운과 박복함이 자기를 그 처지로 몰아넣었을 뿐이라는 것이었다. 돈키호테의 딱딱하고 비좁고 비참하며 삐걱대는 잠자리가 이 별빛 비치는 마구간 한복판에 첫 번째로 놓여 있었고, 그 바로 곁에 산초가 자기 잠자리를 마련했으니, 그것이라고는 부들자리 하나와, 양털이라기보다는 올이 다 닳은 천에 가까워 보이는 담요 한 장이 전부였다."],["The hard, narrow, wretched, rickety bed of Don Quixote stood first in the middle of this star-lit stable, and close beside it Sancho made his, which merely consisted of a rush mat and a blanket that looked as if it was of threadbare canvas rather than of wool.","이 두 잠자리 다음에 마부의 것이 있었는데, 앞서 말했듯이 길마와 그가 가진 가장 좋은 노새 두 마리의 마구를 죄다 모아 꾸민 것이었다. 노새는 모두 열두 마리였으며, 모두 윤기가 흐르고 토실토실하며 더없이 좋은 상태였으니, 이 이야기의 저자에 따르면 그가 아레발로의 부유한 마부들 가운데 하나였기 때문이다."],["Next to these two beds was that of the carrier, made up, as has been said, of the pack-saddles and all the trappings of the two best mules he had, though there were twelve of them, sleek, plump, and in prime condition, for he was one of the rich carriers of Arevalo, according to the author of this history, who particularly mentions this carrier because he knew him very well, and they even say was in some degree a relation of his; besides which Cide Hamete Benengeli was a historian of great research and accuracy in all things, as is very evident since he would not pass over in silence those that have been already mentioned, however trifling and insignificant they might be, an example that might be followed by those grave historians who relate transactions so curtly and briefly that we hardly get a taste of them, all the substance of the work being left in the inkstand from carelessness, perverseness, or ignorance.","저자가 이 마부를 특별히 언급하는 것은 그를 매우 잘 알았기 때문이며, 심지어 어느 정도 그의 친척이었다는 말까지 있다. 게다가 시데 아메테 베넹헬리는 모든 일에 대해 깊이 파고들고 정확하기로 이름난 사학자였으니, 이는 앞서 언급된 일들이 아무리 사소하고 하찮은 것이라 해도 그가 그것들을 잠자코 지나치려 하지 않았다는 점에서 매우 분명하다."],["A thousand blessings on the author of “Tablante de Ricamonte”","이는 일을 너무도 짤막하고 간략하게 늘어놓아 우리가 그 맛을 거의 보지도 못하게 하고, 작품의 알맹이는 죄다 잉크병 속에 남겨 두는 — 게으름 탓이든 심술 탓이든 무지 탓이든 — 저 근엄한 사학자들이 본받을 만한 본보기이다."],["and that of the other book in which the deeds of the Conde Tomillas are recounted; with what minuteness they describe everything!","『리카몬테의 타블란테』의 저자와, 톰미야스 백작의 행적을 늘어놓은 또 다른 책의 저자에게 천 번의 축복이 있기를. 그들은 얼마나 세세하게 모든 것을 묘사하는가!"]],[["To proceed, then: after having paid a visit to his team and given them their second feed, the carrier stretched himself on his pack-saddles and lay waiting for his conscientious Maritornes.","그럼 이야기를 계속하자면, 마부는 자기 노새들을 둘러보고 두 번째 먹이를 준 뒤, 길마 위에 몸을 뻗고 누워 자신의 신실한 마리토르네스를 기다렸다."],["Sancho was by this time plastered and had lain down, and though he strove to sleep the pain of his ribs would not let him, while Don Quixote with the pain of his had his eyes as wide open as a hare’s.","그즈음 산초는 고약을 다 바르고 누웠으나, 아무리 자려 애써도 갈비뼈의 통증이 그를 잠들지 못하게 했고, 돈키호테 역시 자기 갈비뼈의 통증으로 산토끼처럼 두 눈을 활짝 뜬 채였다."]],[["The inn was all in silence, and in the whole of it there was no light except that given by a lantern that hung burning in the middle of the gateway.","객줏집은 온통 고요에 잠겼고, 그 전체에서 빛이라고는 대문 한복판에 걸려 타오르는 등 하나뿐이었다. 이 야릇한 정적과, 우리 기사의 마음에 늘 떠나지 않던, 그의 불행의 원인이 된 그 책들 속 굽이굽이마다 그려진 사건들에 대한 생각이, 그의 상상에 능히 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 기상천외한 망상을 불러일으켰다."],["This strange stillness, and the thoughts, always present to our knight’s mind, of the incidents described at every turn in the books that were the cause of his misfortune, conjured up to his imagination as extraordinary a delusion as can well be conceived, which was that he fancied himself to have reached a famous castle (for, as has been said, all the inns he lodged in were castles to his eyes), and that the daughter of the innkeeper was daughter of the lord of the castle, and that she, won by his high-bred bearing, had fallen in love with him, and had promised to come to his bed for a while that night without the knowledge of her parents; and holding all this fantasy that he had constructed as solid fact, he began to feel uneasy and to consider the perilous risk which his virtue was about to encounter, and he resolved in his heart to commit no treason to his lady Dulcinea del Toboso, even though the queen Guinevere herself and the dame Quintanona should present themselves before him.","그것인즉 자기가 어느 이름난 성에 다다랐다고 (앞서 말했듯이, 그가 묵는 객줏집은 죄다 그의 눈에는 성으로 보였다) 여기고, 객줏집 주인의 딸이 그 성주의 딸이며, 그녀가 자신의 고귀한 거동에 반해 사랑에 빠졌고, 부모 몰래 그날 밤 잠시 자기 잠자리로 찾아오겠노라 약조했다고 상상한 것이었다. 그리고 스스로 꾸며 낸 이 온갖 공상을 확고한 사실로 받아들이고는, 자신의 정절이 곧 맞닥뜨릴 위태로운 위기를 헤아리며 불안해지기 시작했으니, 설령 귀네비어 왕비와 킨타뇨나 시녀가 직접 그 앞에 나타난다 해도 자신의 여인 엘 토보소의 둘시네아에게 결코 배신을 저지르지 않으리라 마음속으로 굳게 다짐했다."]],[["While he was taken up with these vagaries, then, the time and the hour--an unlucky one for him--arrived for the Asturian to come, who in her smock, with bare feet and her hair gathered into a fustian coif, with noiseless and cautious steps entered the chamber where the three were quartered, in quest of the carrier; but scarcely had she gained the door when Don Quixote perceived her, and sitting up in his bed in spite of his plasters and the pain of his ribs, he stretched out his arms to receive his beauteous damsel. The Asturian, who went all doubled up and in silence with her hands before her feeling for her lover, encountered the arms of Don Quixote, who grasped her tightly by the wrist, and drawing her towards him, while she dared not utter a word, made her sit down on the bed. He then felt her smock, and although it was of sackcloth it appeared to him to be of the finest and softest silk: on her wrists she wore some glass beads, but to him they had the sheen of precious Orient pearls: her hair, which in some measure resembled a horse’s mane, he rated as threads of the brightest gold of Araby, whose refulgence dimmed the sun himself: her breath, which no doubt smelt of yesterday’s stale salad, seemed to him to diffuse a sweet aromatic fragrance from her mouth; and, in short, he drew her portrait in his imagination with the same features and in the same style as that which he had seen in his books of the other princesses who, smitten by love, came with all the adornments that are here set down, to see the sorely wounded knight; and so great was the poor gentleman’s blindness that neither touch, nor smell, nor anything else about the good lass that would have made any but a carrier vomit, were enough to undeceive him; on the contrary, he was persuaded he had the goddess of beauty in his arms, and holding her firmly in his grasp he went on to say in low, tender voice:","이렇듯 그가 이 망상들에 골몰해 있는 동안, 그에게는 불운하기 짝이 없는 그 시각, 그 순간이 다가와 아스투리아스 처녀가 들어왔다. 그녀는 속적삼 차림에 맨발이었고, 머리는 면 두건 속에 그러모은 채, 소리 없이 조심스러운 걸음으로 그 셋이 묵고 있는 방에 마부를 찾아 들어섰다. 그러나 그녀가 문턱에 이르기가 무섭게 돈키호테가 그녀를 알아채고는, 고약과 갈비뼈의 통증에도 아랑곳없이 잠자리에서 몸을 일으켜, 자신의 어여쁜 아가씨를 맞으려 두 팔을 뻗었다. 몸을 잔뜩 웅크리고 소리 없이 두 손을 앞으로 내밀어 정인을 더듬던 아스투리아스 처녀는 돈키호테의 두 팔과 마주쳤고, 그는 그녀의 손목을 단단히 움켜쥐고 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨, 그녀가 감히 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 못하는 사이에 잠자리에 앉혔다. 그러고는 그녀의 속적삼을 만져 보았는데, 그것이 비록 거친 삼베였으나 그에게는 더없이 곱고 부드러운 비단처럼 느껴졌다. 그녀의 손목에는 유리구슬 몇 알이 걸려 있었으나, 그에게는 그것이 진귀한 동방 진주의 광채로 보였다. 어느 정도는 말갈기를 닮은 그녀의 머리카락을, 그는 해님조차 빛바래게 하는 아라비아의 가장 찬란한 황금 실로 쳐 주었다. 틀림없이 어제 먹다 남긴 시어진 샐러드 냄새를 풍겼을 그녀의 숨결은, 그에게는 그녀의 입에서 달콤하고 향긋한 향기를 퍼뜨리는 듯 여겨졌다. 요컨대 그는 사랑에 사로잡혀 여기 적힌 온갖 치장을 하고 중상을 입은 기사를 보러 찾아왔다는, 그가 책에서 보았던 다른 공주들의 모습 그대로의 이목구비와 그대로의 양식으로, 그녀의 초상을 자신의 상상 속에 그려 냈다. 이 가엾은 양반의 눈먼 정도가 어찌나 심했던지, 마부가 아닌 누구라도 토하게 만들었을 그 갸륵한 처녀의 감촉도, 냄새도, 그 밖의 무엇도 그의 미망을 깨우기에는 부족했다. 도리어 그는 자신이 미의 여신을 품에 안고 있다고 굳게 믿어, 그녀를 단단히 움켜쥔 채 낮고 부드러운 목소리로 이렇게 말을 이었다."]],[["“Would that I found myself, lovely and exalted lady, in a position to repay such a favour as that which you, by the sight of your great beauty, have granted me; but fortune, which is never weary of persecuting the good, has chosen to place me upon this bed, where I lie so bruised and broken that though my inclination would gladly comply with yours it is impossible; besides, to this impossibility another yet greater is to be added, which is the faith that I have pledged to the peerless Dulcinea del Toboso, sole lady of my most secret thoughts; and were it not that this stood in the way I should not be so insensible a knight as to miss the happy opportunity which your great goodness has offered me.”","\"아름답고 고귀한 부인, 당신이 그 빼어난 미모를 보여 줌으로써 내게 베푼 이 은혜에 보답할 수 있는 처지라면 얼마나 좋겠소. 허나 선한 이를 핍박하기에 결코 지칠 줄 모르는 운명이 나를 이 잠자리에 눕히기로 정하여, 내 이리도 멍들고 부서진 채 누워 있으니, 비록 내 마음이야 기꺼이 당신의 뜻을 따르고 싶으나 도무지 불가능하구려. 게다가 이 불가능에 더하여 또 한 가지 더 큰 불가능이 있으니, 그것은 곧 내 가장 은밀한 생각의 유일한 여인인, 견줄 데 없는 엘 토보소의 둘시네아에게 내가 바친 맹세요. 만일 이것이 가로막지만 않았던들, 나는 당신의 그 크나큰 후의가 내게 베푼 이 행복한 기회를 놓칠 만큼 무딘 기사는 아니었을 것이오.\""]],[["Maritornes was fretting and sweating at finding herself held so fast by Don Quixote, and not understanding or heeding the words he addressed to her, she strove without speaking to free herself.","마리토르네스는 돈키호테에게 그토록 단단히 붙들린 처지에 애가 타고 땀을 흘리며, 그가 자기에게 건네는 말은 알아듣지도 귀담아듣지도 못한 채, 아무 말 없이 그저 벗어나려 발버둥쳤다."],["The worthy carrier, whose unholy thoughts kept him awake, was aware of his doxy the moment she entered the door, and was listening attentively to all Don Quixote said; and jealous that the Asturian should have broken her word with him for another, drew nearer to Don Quixote’s bed and stood still to see what would come of this talk which he could not understand; but when he perceived the wench struggling to get free and Don Quixote striving to hold her, not relishing the joke he raised his arm and delivered such a terrible cuff on the lank jaws of the amorous knight that he bathed all his mouth in blood, and not content with this he mounted on his ribs and with his feet tramped all over them at a pace rather smarter than a trot.","한편 부정한 생각으로 잠 못 이루던 갸륵한 마부는, 자기 정부가 문에 들어선 순간 이미 그녀를 알아채고는 돈키호테가 하는 말을 죄다 주의 깊게 듣고 있었으며, 아스투리아스 처녀가 자기와의 약조를 깨고 딴 사내에게 갔다는 데에 질투가 일어, 돈키호테의 잠자리 가까이 다가가 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 이 수작이 어찌 되어 가는지 가만히 지켜보고 섰다. 그러나 그 계집이 벗어나려고 발버둥치고 돈키호테가 붙들려고 안간힘을 쓰는 꼴을 보자, 그 장난이 영 마뜩잖아 팔을 치켜들어 그 사랑에 빠진 기사의 야윈 턱주가리에 어찌나 무시무시한 주먹을 한 방 먹였던지, 그의 입안이 온통 피로 흥건해졌다."],["The bed which was somewhat crazy and not very firm on its feet, unable to support the additional weight of the carrier, came to the ground, and at the mighty crash of this the innkeeper awoke and at once concluded that it must be some brawl of Maritornes’, because after calling loudly to her he got no answer.","그러고도 성에 차지 않아 그의 갈비뼈 위에 올라타 종종걸음보다 좀 빠른 박자로 두 발로 온통 짓밟아 댔다. 다소 삐걱대고 발이 그리 든든하지 못하던 잠자리는, 마부의 무게까지 더해진 것을 견디지 못하고 그만 바닥으로 폭삭 주저앉았으며, 그 우렁찬 굉음에 객줏집 주인이 잠에서 깨어 그것이 필시 마리토르네스가 벌인 무슨 소동이려니 단박에 결론지었으니, 큰 소리로 그녀를 불러도 대답이 없었기 때문이다."],["With this suspicion he got up, and lighting a lamp hastened to the quarter where he had heard the disturbance.","이런 의심을 품고 그는 일어나 등불을 켜고는 소란이 들려온 쪽으로 서둘러 갔다."],["The wench, seeing that her master was coming and knowing that his temper was terrible, frightened and panic-stricken made for the bed of Sancho Panza, who still slept, and crouching upon it made a ball of herself.","자기 주인이 오는 것을 보고 그 성미가 무시무시함을 알던 계집은, 겁에 질려 혼비백산하여 여태 잠들어 있던 산초 판사의 잠자리로 달려가 그 위에 웅크리고는 공처럼 몸을 말았다."]],[["The innkeeper came in exclaiming, “Where art thou, strumpet?","객줏집 주인이 소리치며 들어왔다. \"어디 있느냐, 이 화냥년아?"],["Of course this is some of thy work.”","이건 필시 네년 짓이렷다.\""],["At this Sancho awoke, and feeling this mass almost on top of him fancied he had the nightmare and began to distribute fisticuffs all round, of which a certain share fell upon Maritornes, who, irritated by the pain and flinging modesty aside, paid back so many in return to Sancho that she woke him up in spite of himself.","이때 산초가 잠에서 깼고, 거의 자기 몸 위에 얹힌 그 덩어리를 느끼고는 가위에 눌린 줄로만 알아 사방으로 주먹을 휘두르기 시작했으니, 그중 몇 대가 마리토르네스에게 떨어졌다. 통증에 약이 오른 그녀는 부끄러움도 내던지고 산초에게 어찌나 많은 주먹을 되돌려 주었던지, 본의 아니게 그를 완전히 깨워 놓고 말았다."],["He then, finding himself so handled, by whom he knew not, raising himself up as well as he could, grappled with Maritornes, and he and she between them began the bitterest and drollest scrimmage in the world.","그러자 산초는 누구인지도 모를 자에게 자기가 이리도 험하게 다루어지는 것을 알고, 할 수 있는 한 몸을 일으켜 마리토르네스와 맞붙었으니, 그와 그녀 둘이 어우러져 세상에서 가장 처절하고도 가장 우스꽝스러운 난투극을 벌이기 시작했다."],["The carrier, however, perceiving by the light of the innkeeper candle how it fared with his ladylove, quitting Don Quixote, ran to bring her the help she needed; and the innkeeper did the same but with a different intention, for his was to chastise the lass, as he believed that beyond a doubt she alone was the cause of all the harmony.","그러나 마부는 객줏집 주인의 촛불 빛으로 자기 연인이 어찌 되어 가는지를 알아채고는 돈키호테를 내버려 둔 채 그녀에게 필요한 도움을 주려 달려갔고, 객줏집 주인 역시 달려갔으나 그 속셈은 달랐으니, 그는 이 모든 야단법석의 원흉이 의심할 바 없이 그 계집 하나뿐이라고 믿어 그녀를 혼내 주려는 것이었다."],["And so, as the saying is, cat to rat, rat to rope, rope to stick, the carrier pounded Sancho, Sancho the lass, she him, and the innkeeper her, and all worked away so briskly that they did not give themselves a moment’s rest; and the best of it was that the innkeeper’s lamp went out, and as they were left in the dark they all laid on one upon the other in a mass so unmercifully that there was not a sound spot left where a hand could light.","그리하여 속담에 이르듯 고양이는 쥐에게, 쥐는 밧줄에게, 밧줄은 막대기에게 달려들듯, 마부는 산초를 두들기고, 산초는 계집을, 계집은 그를, 객줏집 주인은 그녀를 두들겼으니, 모두가 어찌나 부지런히 손발을 놀렸던지 잠시도 쉴 틈을 주지 않았다. 그런데 무엇보다 기막힌 것은 객줏집 주인의 등불이 꺼져 버린 일이라, 다들 어둠 속에 남겨진 채 한 덩어리로 뒤엉켜 어찌나 사정없이 서로를 두들겨 댔던지 손이 닿는 곳에 성한 자리라고는 한 군데도 남지 않았다."]],[["It so happened that there was lodging that night in the inn a caudrillero of what they call the Old Holy Brotherhood of Toledo, who, also hearing the extraordinary noise of the conflict, seized his staff and the tin case with his warrants, and made his way in the dark into the room crying: “Hold!","마침 그날 밤 객줏집에는 톨레도의 이른바 옛 성스러운 형제단의 순찰관 하나가 묵고 있었는데, 그 또한 이 격투의 심상찮은 소음을 듣고는 자기 지팡이와 영장이 든 양철 통을 집어 들고 어둠을 헤치며 방 안으로 들어와 외쳤다. \"멈춰라!"],["in the name of the Jurisdiction!","사법권의 이름으로! 멈춰라!"],["Hold! in the name of the Holy Brotherhood!”","성스러운 형제단의 이름으로!\""]],[["The first that he came upon was the pummelled Don Quixote, who lay stretched senseless on his back upon his broken-down bed, and, his hand falling on the beard as he felt about, he continued to cry, “Help for the Jurisdiction!” but perceiving that he whom he had laid hold of did not move or stir, he concluded that he was dead and that those in the room were his murderers, and with this suspicion he raised his voice still higher, calling out, “Shut the inn gate; see that no one goes out; they have killed a man here!” This cry startled them all, and each dropped the contest at the point at which the voice reached him. The innkeeper retreated to his room, the carrier to his pack-saddles, the lass to her crib; the unlucky Don Quixote and Sancho alone were unable to move from where they were. The cuadrillero on this let go Don Quixote’s beard, and went out to look for a light to search for and apprehend the culprits; but not finding one, as the innkeeper had purposely extinguished the lantern on retreating to his room, he was compelled to have recourse to the hearth, where after much time and trouble he lit another lamp.","그가 맨 먼저 마주친 것은 두들겨 맞은 돈키호테였으니, 그는 부서진 잠자리 위에 정신을 잃은 채 벌렁 자빠져 있었다. 순찰관은 더듬거리다 그 수염에 손이 닿자 계속해서 외쳤다. \"사법권을 도우라!\" 그러나 자기가 붙든 자가 꿈쩍도 하지 않는 것을 보고는, 그가 죽었으며 방 안에 있는 자들이 그 살인자들이라고 결론지었다. 이런 의심을 품고 그는 목소리를 한층 더 높여 소리쳤다. \"객줏집 대문을 닫아라. 아무도 나가지 못하게 하라. 여기서 사람이 살해당했다!\" 이 외침에 모두가 화들짝 놀라, 저마다 그 목소리가 자기에게 닿은 그 지점에서 싸움을 그만두었다. 객줏집 주인은 자기 방으로, 마부는 자기 길마로, 계집은 자기 잠자리로 물러갔고, 불운한 돈키호테와 산초만이 있던 자리에서 옴짝달싹할 수 없었다. 이에 순찰관은 돈키호테의 수염을 놓고, 죄인들을 찾아 잡아들이려고 불을 구하러 나갔다. 그러나 객줏집 주인이 제 방으로 물러나면서 일부러 등불을 꺼 버린 탓에 불을 찾지 못해, 그는 하는 수 없이 화덕에 의지할 수밖에 없었으니, 한참 동안 애를 먹은 끝에야 거기서 또 다른 등불 하나를 밝혔다."]],[["# CHAPTER XVII. IN WHICH ARE CONTAINED THE INNUMERABLE TROUBLES WHICH THE BRAVE DON QUIXOTE AND HIS GOOD SQUIRE SANCHO PANZA ENDURED IN THE INN, WHICH TO HIS MISFORTUNE HE TOOK TO BE A CASTLE","# CHAPTER XVII."]],[["By this time Don Quixote had recovered from his swoon; and in the same tone of voice in which he had called to his squire the day before when he lay stretched “in the vale of the stakes,”","이때쯤 돈키호테는 기절에서 깨어났다. 그리고 전날 그가 \"말뚝의 골짜기\"에 길게 뻗어 누워 있을 때 종자를 불렀던 것과 똑같은 목소리로, 이제 다시 그를 부르기 시작했다."],["he began calling to him now, “Sancho, my friend, art thou asleep?","\"산초, 이보게, 자고 있는가?"],["sleepest thou, friend Sancho?”","잠들었는가, 산초?\""]],[["“How can I sleep, curses on it!”","\"제기랄, 어떻게 잠을 잡니까!\""],["returned Sancho discontentedly and bitterly, “when it is plain that all the devils have been at me this night?”","산초가 불만스럽고 쓰라리게 대답했다. \"오늘 밤 온갖 악마들이 죄다 저를 덮친 게 뻔한데요?\""]],[["“Thou mayest well believe that,”","\"자네는 그 말을 충분히 믿어도 좋네.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["answered Don Quixote, “because, either I know little, or this castle is enchanted, for thou must know-but this that I am now about to tell thee thou must swear to keep secret until after my death.”","\"내가 아는 게 적든지, 아니면 이 성이 마법에 걸렸든지 둘 중 하나일 테니. 알아 두게 — 다만 지금 내가 자네에게 말하려는 이것은, 내가 죽은 뒤까지 비밀로 지키겠다고 맹세해야 하네.\""]],[["“I swear it,”","\"맹세합니다.\""],["answered Sancho.","산초가 대답했다."]],[["“I say so,”","\"내가 이렇게 말하는 까닭은,\""],["continued Don Quixote, “because I hate taking away anyone’s good name.”","돈키호테가 이어 말했다. \"누구의 명예든 깎아내리는 일을 내가 몹시 싫어하기 때문일세.\""]],[["“I say,”","\"그럼 저는,\" 산초가 대꾸했다."],["replied Sancho, “that I swear to hold my tongue about it till the end of your worship’s days, and God grant I may be able to let it out to-morrow.”","\"나리께서 살아 계시는 동안에는 이 일에 대해 입을 다물겠다고 맹세합니다. 그리고 하느님께서 내일 그걸 털어놓을 수 있게 해 주시기를 바랄 뿐입니다.\""]],[["“Do I do thee such injuries, Sancho,”","\"내가 자네에게 그렇게 못된 짓을 했단 말인가, 산초,\""],["said Don Quixote, “that thou wouldst see me dead so soon?”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그래서 그토록 빨리 내가 죽기를 바라는 게냐?\""]],[["“It is not for that,”","\"그게 아닙니다.\""],["replied Sancho, “but because I hate keeping things long, and I don’t want them to grow rotten with me from over-keeping.”","산초가 대답했다. \"다만 저는 무언가를 오래 간직하는 걸 싫어하고, 너무 오래 두어 제 안에서 썩어 버리는 게 싫어서 그렇습니다.\""]],[["“At any rate,”","\"어쨌든,\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “I have more confidence in thy affection and good nature; and so I would have thee know that this night there befell me one of the strangest adventures that I could describe, and to relate it to thee briefly thou must know that a little while ago the daughter of the lord of this castle came to me, and that she is the most elegant and beautiful damsel that could be found in the wide world.","\"나는 자네의 애정과 착한 천성을 더 믿네. 그러니 알아 두게, 오늘 밤 내게 내가 묘사할 수 있는 가장 기이한 모험 중 하나가 일어났네."],["What I could tell thee of the charms of her person!","자네에게 그것을 간략히 들려주자면 이렇네. 조금 전에 이 성주의 따님이 내게 왔는데, 그 처자는 이 넓은 세상에서 찾아볼 수 있는 가장 우아하고 아름다운 아가씨였네."],["of her lively wit!","그녀의 자태가 지닌 매력에 대해 내가 자네에게 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠는가! 그 생기 넘치는 재치에 대해서는!"],["of other secret matters which, to preserve the fealty I owe to my lady Dulcinea del Toboso, I shall pass over unnoticed and in silence!","그리고 또 다른 은밀한 일들에 대해서는, 내가 나의 여인 둘시네아 델 토보소에게 바쳐야 할 충절을 지키고자, 눈감고 침묵 속에 묻어 두겠네! 다만 자네에게 이것만 말하겠네."],["I will only tell thee that, either fate being envious of so great a boon placed in my hands by good fortune, or perhaps (and this is more probable) this castle being, as I have already said, enchanted, at the time when I was engaged in the sweetest and most amorous discourse with her, there came, without my seeing or knowing whence it came, a hand attached to some arm of some huge giant, that planted such a cuff on my jaws that I have them all bathed in blood, and then pummelled me in such a way that I am in a worse plight than yesterday when the carriers, on account of Rocinante’s misbehaviour, inflicted on us the injury thou knowest of; whence conjecture that there must be some enchanted Moor guarding the treasure of this damsel’s beauty, and that it is not for me.”","운명이 행운으로 내 손에 쥐여진 그토록 큰 은총을 시샘했든지, 아니면 (이쪽이 더 그럴듯하지만) 이 성이, 내가 이미 말했듯이 마법에 걸려 있어서든지, 내가 그녀와 더없이 달콤하고 정겨운 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 내가 보지도 못하고 어디서 왔는지도 모르는 사이에, 어느 거대한 거인의 팔에 달린 손 하나가 나타나 내 턱에 어찌나 세게 주먹을 날렸던지 온통 피투성이가 되었고, 그러고는 나를 어찌나 두들겨 팼던지, 어제 짐꾼들이 로시난테의 무례한 짓을 빌미로 우리에게 자네가 아는 그 봉변을 안겼을 때보다도 더 심한 꼴이 되었네. 그러니 이 아가씨의 미모라는 보물을 지키는 마법에 걸린 무어인이 어딘가에 있고, 그 미모는 나를 위한 것이 아니라고 짐작할 만하지.\""]],[["“Not for me either,”","\"저를 위한 것도 아니지요.\""],["said Sancho, “for more than four hundred Moors have so thrashed me that the drubbing of the stakes was cakes and fancy-bread to it.","산초가 말했다. \"사백 명도 넘는 무어인들이 저를 어찌나 두들겨 팼던지, 그에 비하면 말뚝으로 얻어맞은 것은 과자에다 고급 빵이었습니다."],["But tell me, señor, what do you call this excellent and rare adventure that has left us as we are left now?","그런데 나리, 우리를 지금 이 꼴로 만들어 놓은 이 훌륭하고 진귀한 모험을 대체 뭐라고 부르시렵니까?"],["Though your worship was not so badly off, having in your arms that incomparable beauty you spoke of; but I, what did I have, except the heaviest whacks I think I had in all my life?","하기야 나리께서는 그렇게까지 형편이 나쁘진 않으셨지요, 말씀하신 그 비할 데 없는 미녀를 품에 안고 계셨으니까요. 하지만 저는, 제 평생 받아 본 것 중에 가장 묵직한 매질 말고 무엇을 얻었답니까?"],["Unlucky me and the mother that bore me!","운도 지지리 없는 저와 저를 낳으신 어머니라니!"],["for I am not a knight-errant and never expect to be one, and of all the mishaps, the greater part falls to my share.”","저는 편력 기사도 아니고 앞으로도 결코 될 일이 없는데, 온갖 불행 중에서 더 큰 몫은 다 제 차지로 떨어지니 말입니다.\""]],[["“Then thou hast been thrashed too?”","\"그러니까 자네도 두들겨 맞았단 말인가?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“Didn’t I say so?","\"제가 그렇게 말씀드리지 않았습니까?"],["worse luck to my line!”","제 핏줄에 더 큰 불운이 있기를!\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“Be not distressed, friend,”","\"괴로워하지 말게, 이보게.\""],["said Don Quixote, “for I will now make the precious balsam with which we shall cure ourselves in the twinkling of an eye.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 이제 그 귀한 영약을 만들 테니, 그것으로 우리는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 나을 걸세.\""]],[["By this time the cuadrillero had succeeded in lighting the lamp, and came in to see the man that he thought had been killed; and as Sancho caught sight of him at the door, seeing him coming in his shirt, with a cloth on his head, and a lamp in his hand, and a very forbidding countenance, he said to his master, “Señor, can it be that this is the enchanted Moor coming back to give us more castigation if there be anything still left in the ink-bottle?”","이때쯤 경관은 등불을 켜는 데 성공해서, 죽었으리라 여긴 사내를 보러 들어왔다. 산초는 문간에서 그를 언뜻 보았는데, 셔츠 바람으로 머리에 천을 두르고 손에 등불을 든 채 매우 험상궂은 얼굴로 들어오는 그를 보고는, 주인에게 말했다. \"나리, 잉크병에 아직 무언가 남아 있으면 우리에게 더 매질을 가하려고 저 마법에 걸린 무어인이 다시 온 게 아닐까요?\""]],[["“It cannot be the Moor,”","\"무어인일 리 없네.\""],["answered Don Quixote, “for those under enchantment do not let themselves be seen by anyone.”","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"마법에 걸린 자들은 누구에게도 제 모습을 보이지 않는 법이니까.\""]],[["“If they don’t let themselves be seen, they let themselves be felt,”","\"제 모습을 보이지 않더라도, 제 손길은 느끼게 하지요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho; “if not, let my shoulders speak to the point.”","\"아니라면, 제 어깨더러 한마디 하라고 하십시오.\""]],[["“Mine could speak too,”","\"내 어깨도 한마디 할 수 있네.\""],["said Don Quixote, “but that is not a sufficient reason for believing that what we see is the enchanted Moor.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"하지만 그것이 우리가 보는 것이 마법에 걸린 무어인이라고 믿기에 충분한 이유는 아닐세.\""]],[["The officer came up, and finding them engaged in such a peaceful conversation, stood amazed; though Don Quixote, to be sure, still lay on his back unable to move from pure pummelling and plasters.","경관이 다가와, 그들이 그렇게 평화로운 대화에 빠져 있는 것을 보고 놀라 멈춰 섰다. 물론 돈키호테는 순전히 두들겨 맞고 고약을 발라 댄 탓에 꼼짝도 못 한 채 여전히 등을 대고 누워 있었다."],["The officer turned to him and said, “Well, how goes it, good man?”","경관은 그를 돌아보며 말했다. \"그래, 좀 어떤가, 이 양반아?\""]],[["“I would speak more politely if I were you,”","\"내가 자네라면 좀 더 정중히 말하겠네.\""],["replied Don Quixote; “is it the way of this country to address knights-errant in that style, you booby?”","돈키호테가 대꾸했다. \"이 나라에서는 편력 기사에게 그런 식으로 말을 거는 것이 풍습인가, 이 멍청한 작자야?\""]],[["The cuadrillero finding himself so disrespectfully treated by such a sorry-looking individual, lost his temper, and raising the lamp full of oil, smote Don Quixote such a blow with it on the head that he gave him a badly broken pate; then, all being in darkness, he went out, and Sancho Panza said, “That is certainly the enchanted Moor, Señor, and he keeps the treasure for others, and for us only the cuffs and lamp-whacks.”","경관은 그토록 초라해 보이는 자에게 그토록 무례한 대접을 받자 화가 치밀어, 기름이 가득 찬 등불을 치켜들고는 돈키호테의 머리를 한 대 후려쳐서 머리통을 몹시 깨뜨려 놓았다. 그러고는 모든 것이 어둠에 잠긴 가운데 밖으로 나갔고, 산초 판사가 말했다. \"저게 바로 마법에 걸린 무어인이 틀림없습니다, 나리. 보물은 다른 자들을 위해 지키고, 우리한테는 주먹질과 등불질만 하는군요.\""]],[["“That is the truth,” answered Don Quixote, “and there is no use in troubling oneself about these matters of enchantment or being angry or vexed at them, for as they are invisible and visionary we shall find no one on whom to avenge ourselves, do what we may; rise, Sancho, if thou canst, and call the alcaide of this fortress, and get him to give me a little oil, wine, salt, and rosemary to make the salutiferous balsam, for indeed I believe I have great need of it now, because I am losing much blood from the wound that phantom gave me.”","\"그게 진실일세.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"이 마법의 일들로 골머리를 앓거나 그것에 화내고 속 끓여 봐야 아무 소용이 없네. 그것들은 눈에 보이지 않고 환영 같은 것이라, 우리가 무슨 짓을 해도 복수할 상대를 찾지 못할 테니. 일어나게, 산초, 일어날 수 있거든 이 요새의 성주를 불러서, 기름과 포도주와 소금과 로즈메리를 조금 가져다 달라고 하게. 그 치유의 영약을 만들어야겠네. 정말이지 지금 그것이 몹시 필요한 것 같으니, 저 유령이 낸 상처에서 피를 많이 흘리고 있기 때문일세.\""]],[["Sancho got up with pain enough in his bones, and went after the innkeeper in the dark, and meeting the officer, who was looking to see what had become of his enemy, he said to him, “Señor, whoever you are, do us the favour and kindness to give us a little rosemary, oil, salt, and wine, for it is wanted to cure one of the best knights-errant on earth, who lies on yonder bed wounded by the hands of the enchanted Moor that is in this inn.”","산초는 뼈마디가 충분히 쑤시는 채로 일어나, 어둠 속에서 여관 주인을 찾아 나섰다. 그러다 제 적이 어떻게 되었는지 살펴보던 경관과 마주치자, 그에게 말했다. \"나리, 당신이 누구시든, 우리에게 로즈메리와 기름과 소금과 포도주를 조금 베풀어 주시는 친절을 부탁드립니다. 저쪽 침상에 누워, 이 여관에 있는 마법에 걸린 무어인의 손에 부상을 입은, 이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 편력 기사 중 한 분을 치료하는 데 필요한 것이라서요.\""]],[["When the officer heard him talk in this way, he took him for a man out of his senses, and as day was now beginning to break, he opened the inn gate, and calling the host, he told him what this good man wanted.","경관은 그가 이런 식으로 말하는 것을 듣고는 정신 나간 자로 여겼고, 마침 날이 밝아 오기 시작하던 터라 여관 문을 열고 주인을 불러서 이 선량한 사내가 원하는 것을 일러 주었다. 주인은 그가 필요로 하는 것을 마련해 주었고, 산초는 그것을 돈키호테에게 가져갔다."],["The host furnished him with what he required, and Sancho brought it to Don Quixote, who, with his hand to his head, was bewailing the pain of the blow of the lamp, which had done him no more harm than raising a couple of rather large lumps, and what he fancied blood was only the sweat that flowed from him in his sufferings during the late storm.","돈키호테는 머리에 손을 짚고 등불에 맞은 통증을 한탄하고 있었는데, 그 매는 사실 제법 커다란 혹 두 개를 돋게 한 것 말고는 그에게 별다른 해를 끼치지 않았고, 그가 피라고 여긴 것은 지난 소동을 겪는 동안 고통 속에서 그에게서 흘러나온 땀에 지나지 않았다. 간단히 말하면, 그는 재료들을 받아 그것들을 모두 섞어 한참 동안 끓여서 합제(合劑)를 만들었고, 마침내 완성되었다 싶을 때까지 그렇게 했다."],["To be brief, he took the materials, of which he made a compound, mixing them all and boiling them a good while until it seemed to him they had come to perfection.","그러고는 그것을 담을 약병을 청했는데, 여관에 그런 것이 하나도 없어서, 주인이 그에게 선뜻 내준 양철 기름병이나 술병에 담기로 했다."],["He then asked for some vial to pour it into, and as there was not one in the inn, he decided on putting it into a tin oil-bottle or flask of which the host made him a free gift; and over the flask he repeated more than eighty paternosters and as many more ave-marias, salves, and credos, accompanying each word with a cross by way of benediction, at all which there were present Sancho, the innkeeper, and the cuadrillero; for the carrier was now peacefully engaged in attending to the comfort of his mules.","그러고는 그 병 위에 주기도문을 여든 번 넘게, 또 그만큼의 성모송과 찬미경과 사도신경을 거듭 외우면서, 말 한마디마다 축복의 표시로 십자가를 그었다. 이 모든 일에 산초와 여관 주인과 경관이 함께 있었으니, 짐꾼은 이제 평화로이 제 노새들을 편안케 돌보는 데 여념이 없었기 때문이다."]],[["This being accomplished, he felt anxious to make trial himself, on the spot, of the virtue of this precious balsam, as he considered it, and so he drank near a quart of what could not be put into the flask and remained in the pigskin in which it had been boiled; but scarcely had he done drinking when he began to vomit in such a way that nothing was left in his stomach, and with the pangs and spasms of vomiting he broke into a profuse sweat, on account of which he bade them cover him up and leave him alone.","이 일을 마치자 그는 자신이 귀하게 여기는 이 영약의 효험을 그 자리에서 직접 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 간절해져서, 병에 담지 못하고 끓이던 돼지 가죽 부대에 남아 있던 것을 거의 한 쿼트가량 들이켰다. 그런데 다 마시기가 무섭게 어찌나 토하기 시작했던지 위장에 아무것도 남지 않았고, 토하는 고통과 경련 속에서 비 오듯 땀을 흘렸으니, 그 까닭에 그는 사람들에게 자기를 덮어 주고 혼자 있게 해 달라고 청했다."],["They did so, and he lay sleeping more than three hours, at the end of which he awoke and felt very great bodily relief and so much ease from his bruises that he thought himself quite cured, and verily believed he had hit upon the balsam of Fierabras; and that with this remedy he might thenceforward, without any fear, face any kind of destruction, battle, or combat, however perilous it might be.","그들이 그리하자 그는 세 시간 넘게 잠들어 있었고, 그 끝에 깨어나서는 몸이 무척 가뿐해진 것을 느꼈으며, 멍든 곳도 어찌나 후련해졌던지 자신이 완전히 나았다고 여겼고, 자신이 정말로 피에라브라스의 영약을 알아맞혔다고 진정으로 믿었다. 그리고 이 약만 있으면 앞으로는 어떤 종류의 파멸이든 전투든 결투든, 그것이 아무리 위험할지라도 아무런 두려움 없이 맞설 수 있으리라 믿었다."]],[["Sancho Panza, who also regarded the amendment of his master as miraculous, begged him to give him what was left in the pigskin, which was no small quantity.","자기 주인이 회복된 것을 기적이라 여긴 산초 판사도 돼지 가죽 부대에 남은 것을 자기에게 달라고 청했는데, 남은 양이 적지 않았다."],["Don Quixote consented, and he, taking it with both hands, in good faith and with a better will, gulped down and drained off very little less than his master.","돈키호테가 허락하자 그는 두 손으로 그것을 받아 들고, 굳은 믿음과 한층 더 굳센 의지로 꿀꺽꿀꺽 들이켜 제 주인이 마신 것보다 별로 적지 않은 양을 비웠다."],["But the fact is, that the stomach of poor Sancho was of necessity not so delicate as that of his master, and so, before vomiting, he was seized with such gripings and retchings, and such sweats and faintness, that verily and truly be believed his last hour had come, and finding himself so racked and tormented he cursed the balsam and the thief that had given it to him.","그러나 사실인즉, 가엾은 산초의 위장은 어쩔 수 없이 제 주인의 것만큼 섬세하지 못했던지라, 토하기도 전에 어찌나 심한 뒤틀림과 구역질에, 또 어찌나 많은 땀과 어지럼증에 사로잡혔던지, 그는 정말이지 진심으로 자기 마지막 순간이 왔다고 믿었고, 자신이 그토록 쥐어짜이고 시달리는 것을 깨닫고는 그 영약과 그것을 자기에게 준 도둑놈을 저주했다."]],[["Don Quixote seeing him in this state said, “It is my belief, Sancho, that this mischief comes of thy not being dubbed a knight, for I am persuaded this liquor cannot be good for those who are not so.”","돈키호테가 그가 이런 상태에 있는 것을 보고 말했다. \"산초, 내 생각에는 이 곤욕이 자네가 기사로 서임되지 않았기 때문에 생기는 것 같네. 이 약은 그렇지 못한 자들에게는 좋을 수가 없다고 나는 확신하기 때문일세.\""]],[["“If your worship knew that,”","\"나리께서 그걸 알고 계셨으면서,\" 산초가 대꾸했다."],["returned Sancho--“woe betide me and all my kindred!--why did you let me taste it?”","\"— 저와 제 일가친척 모두에게 화가 미치기를! — 어째서 제게 맛보게 하셨습니까?\""]],[["At this moment the draught took effect, and the poor squire began to discharge both ways at such a rate that the rush mat on which he had thrown himself and the canvas blanket he had covering him were fit for nothing afterwards. He sweated and perspired with such paroxysms and convulsions that not only he himself but all present thought his end had come. This tempest and tribulation lasted about two hours, at the end of which he was left, not like his master, but so weak and exhausted that he could not stand. Don Quixote, however, who, as has been said, felt himself relieved and well, was eager to take his departure at once in quest of adventures, as it seemed to him that all the time he loitered there was a fraud upon the world and those in it who stood in need of his help and protection, all the more when he had the security and confidence his balsam afforded him; and so, urged by this impulse, he saddled Rocinante himself and put the pack-saddle on his squire’s beast, whom likewise he helped to dress and mount the ass; after which he mounted his horse and turning to a corner of the inn he laid hold of a pike that stood there, to serve him by way of a lance. All that were in the inn, who were more than twenty persons, stood watching him; the innkeeper’s daughter was likewise observing him, and he too never took his eyes off her, and from time to time fetched a sigh that he seemed to pluck up from the depths of his bowels; but they all thought it must be from the pain he felt in his ribs; at any rate they who had seen him plastered the night before thought so.","이 순간 마신 약이 효력을 발하기 시작했고, 가엾은 종자는 양쪽으로 어찌나 격렬히 쏟아 내기 시작했던지, 그가 몸을 던졌던 골풀 자리도, 덮고 있던 거친 천 담요도 그 뒤로는 아무짝에도 쓸모없게 되어 버렸다. 그는 어찌나 발작과 경련을 일으키며 땀을 줄줄 흘렸던지, 그 자신뿐 아니라 그 자리에 있던 모두가 그의 최후가 왔다고 여겼다. 이 폭풍 같은 고난은 두 시간쯤 이어졌고, 그 끝에 그는 자기 주인처럼이 아니라, 어찌나 기진하고 탈진했던지 일어설 수조차 없는 지경으로 남겨졌다. 그러나 앞서 말했듯이 몸이 후련하고 멀쩡해진 것을 느낀 돈키호테는, 당장 모험을 찾아 떠나고 싶어 안달이었다. 그가 거기서 빈둥거리는 동안의 모든 시간이 세상에 대한, 그리고 그의 도움과 보호를 필요로 하는 세상 사람들에 대한 사기처럼 여겨졌기 때문이며, 영약이 그에게 안겨 준 안심과 자신감이 있는 마당이라 더더욱 그러했다. 그리하여 이 충동에 떠밀려 그는 손수 로시난테에 안장을 얹고, 종자의 짐승에게도 짐안장을 얹어 주었으며, 종자가 옷을 차려입고 당나귀에 오르는 것도 거들어 주었다. 그런 다음 자기 말에 올라타고 여관 한구석으로 가서, 거기 세워져 있던 창 한 자루를 집어 들어 자신의 창 대신 쓰기로 했다. 여관에 있던 사람들은, 스무 명이 넘었는데, 모두 서서 그를 지켜보고 있었다. 여관 주인의 딸도 마찬가지로 그를 바라보고 있었고, 그 역시 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 이따금 제 배 속 깊은 데서 끌어올린 듯한 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러나 사람들은 모두 그것이 갈비뼈가 아파서 그러는 것이려니 여겼다. 적어도 전날 밤 그가 고약을 바른 모습을 본 자들은 그렇게 생각했다."]],[["As soon as they were both mounted, at the gate of the inn, he called to the host and said in a very grave and measured voice, “Many and great are the favours, Señor Alcaide, that I have received in this castle of yours, and I remain under the deepest obligation to be grateful to you for them all the days of my life; if I can repay them in avenging you of any arrogant foe who may have wronged you, know that my calling is no other than to aid the weak, to avenge those who suffer wrong, and to chastise perfidy. Search your memory, and if you find anything of this kind you need only tell me of it, and I promise you by the order of knighthood which I have received to procure you satisfaction and reparation to the utmost of your desire.”","둘 다 말에 올라 여관 문간에 이르자, 그는 주인을 불러 매우 엄숙하고 절제된 목소리로 말했다. \"성주 나리, 제가 이 성에서 받은 은혜가 많고도 큽니다. 그래서 저는 평생토록 그 모든 것에 감사해야 할 깊은 의무를 지고 있습니다. 혹여 당신께 해를 끼친 어떤 오만한 적을 응징해 그 은혜를 갚을 수 있다면, 부디 아십시오, 저의 소명은 다름 아니라 약한 자를 돕고, 부당한 일을 당한 이들의 원수를 갚으며, 배신을 응징하는 것입니다. 기억을 더듬어 보시고, 만일 이런 종류의 일이 떠오르거든 그저 제게 말씀만 하십시오. 그러면 제가 받은 기사도의 이름으로 약속하건대, 당신이 바라는 바를 끝까지 충족시켜 드리고 그 보상을 받게 해 드리겠습니다.\""]],[["The innkeeper replied to him with equal calmness, “Sir Knight, I do not want your worship to avenge me of any wrong, because when any is done me I can take what vengeance seems good to me; the only thing I want is that you pay me the score that you have run up in the inn last night, as well for the straw and barley for your two beasts, as for supper and beds.”","여관 주인도 그에 못지않게 차분히 그에게 대답했다. \"기사 나리, 저는 나리께서 제 어떤 원한이든 갚아 주시길 바라지 않습니다. 제게 무슨 일이 생기면 제가 알아서 적당한 복수를 할 수 있으니까요. 제가 바라는 단 한 가지는, 어젯밤 이 여관에서 쌓인 외상값을 치러 주시는 겁니다. 두 짐승에게 준 짚과 보리값에다, 저녁 식사와 잠자리값까지요.\""]],[["“Then this is an inn?”","\"그럼 여기가 여관이란 말인가?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“And a very respectable one,”","\"그것도 아주 번듯한 여관이지요.\""],["said the innkeeper.","여관 주인이 말했다."]],[["“I have been under a mistake all this time,” answered Don Quixote, “for in truth I thought it was a castle, and not a bad one; but since it appears that it is not a castle but an inn, all that can be done now is that you should excuse the payment, for I cannot contravene the rule of knights-errant, of whom I know as a fact (and up to the present I have read nothing to the contrary) that they never paid for lodging or anything else in the inn where they might be; for any hospitality that might be offered them is their due by law and right in return for the insufferable toil they endure in seeking adventures by night and by day, in summer and in winter, on foot and on horseback, in hunger and thirst, cold and heat, exposed to all the inclemencies of heaven and all the hardships of earth.”","\"내가 그동안 줄곧 착각하고 있었구먼.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"정말이지 나는 이곳이 성이라고, 그것도 나쁘지 않은 성이라고 여겼으니. 하지만 이곳이 성이 아니라 여관임이 드러난 이상, 이제 할 수 있는 일이라고는 자네가 그 셈을 면해 주는 것뿐일세. 나로서는 편력 기사의 규율을 어길 수가 없으니. 내가 사실로 아는 바로는(그리고 지금까지 그와 다른 것을 읽은 적이 없네) 그들은 머무는 여관에서 잠자리값이든 무엇이든 결코 치르지 않았네. 그들에게 베풀어지는 어떤 환대든, 그들이 밤낮으로, 여름이건 겨울이건, 걸어서든 말 타고든, 굶주림과 목마름 속에, 추위와 더위 속에, 하늘의 온갖 모진 날씨와 땅의 온갖 고난에 내맡겨진 채 모험을 찾아 견뎌 내는 그 견디기 힘든 노고의 대가로, 법도와 권리에 따라 마땅히 받아야 할 그들의 몫이기 때문일세.\""]],[["“I have little to do with that,”","\"그건 저와 별 상관이 없습니다.\" 여관 주인이 대꾸했다."],["replied the innkeeper; “pay me what you owe me, and let us have no more talk of chivalry, for all I care about is to get my money.”","\"제게 빚진 것이나 치르시고, 기사도 얘기는 더 그만둡시다. 제가 신경 쓰는 것이라고는 오직 제 돈을 받는 것뿐이니까요.\""]],[["“You are a stupid, scurvy innkeeper,”","\"이 어리석고 천박한 여관 주인 같으니.\""],["said Don Quixote, and putting spurs to Rocinante and bringing his pike to the slope he rode out of the inn before anyone could stop him, and pushed on some distance without looking to see if his squire was following him.","돈키호테는 이렇게 말하고는 로시난테에 박차를 가하고 창을 비스듬히 내리며 아무도 그를 막을 새 없이 여관에서 말을 몰아 나가, 종자가 따라오는지 돌아보지도 않은 채 얼마쯤 앞으로 내달렸다."]],[["The innkeeper when he saw him go without paying him ran to get payment of Sancho, who said that as his master would not pay neither would he, because, being as he was squire to a knight-errant, the same rule and reason held good for him as for his master with regard to not paying anything in inns and hostelries.","여관 주인은 그가 값을 치르지 않고 떠나는 것을 보고 산초에게 셈을 받으러 달려갔다. 산초는 제 주인이 치르지 않으니 자기도 치르지 않겠노라고 했으니, 자신은 편력 기사의 종자인 만큼 여관이나 숙소에서 아무것도 치르지 않는 일에 관해서는 주인과 똑같은 규율과 이치가 자신에게도 그대로 적용된다는 것이었다."],["At this the innkeeper waxed very wroth, and threatened if he did not pay to compel him in a way that he would not like.","이에 여관 주인은 몹시 노발대발하며, 치르지 않으면 좋아하지 않을 방식으로 강제하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다."],["To which Sancho made answer that by the law of chivalry his master had received he would not pay a rap, though it cost him his life; for the excellent and ancient usage of knights-errant was not going to be violated by him, nor should the squires of such as were yet to come into the world ever complain of him or reproach him with breaking so just a privilege.","산초는 이에 대해, 제 주인이 받은 기사도의 법도에 따라 자신은 목숨이 달아나는 한이 있어도 한 푼도 치르지 않겠노라고 대답했다. 편력 기사들의 그 훌륭하고 유서 깊은 관례를 자신이 어기는 일은 없을 것이며, 앞으로 세상에 나올 그런 기사들의 종자들이 자신에 대해 불평하거나 그토록 정당한 특권을 깨뜨렸다고 자신을 책망하는 일도 결코 없으리라는 것이었다."]],[["The ill-luck of the unfortunate Sancho so ordered it that among the company in the inn there were four woolcarders from Segovia, three needle-makers from the Colt of Cordova, and two lodgers from the Fair of Seville, lively fellows, tender-hearted, fond of a joke, and playful, who, almost as if instigated and moved by a common impulse, made up to Sancho and dismounted him from his ass, while one of them went in for the blanket of the host’s bed; but on flinging him into it they looked up, and seeing that the ceiling was somewhat lower what they required for their work, they decided upon going out into the yard, which was bounded by the sky, and there, putting Sancho in the middle of the blanket, they began to raise him high, making sport with him as they would with a dog at Shrovetide.","불운한 산초의 액운이 이렇게 일을 꾸몄으니, 여관에 있던 무리 가운데 세고비아에서 온 양털 빗는 직공 넷, 코르도바의 망아지 광장에서 온 바늘 만드는 직공 셋, 세비야 장터에서 온 손님 둘이 있었던 것이다. 이들은 활기차고 마음 여리며 농담을 좋아하고 장난기 많은 친구들이었는데, 거의 같은 충동에 부추겨지고 움직인 듯이 산초에게 달려들어 그를 당나귀에서 끌어 내렸고, 그중 하나가 주인 침대의 담요를 가지러 들어갔다. 그러나 그를 담요에 던져 넣다가 위를 올려다보니 천장이 그들의 작업에 필요한 것보다 다소 낮은지라, 하늘이 곧 천장이 되는 마당으로 나가기로 했다. 그러고는 거기서 산초를 담요 한가운데에 놓고 그를 높이 띄워 올리기 시작했으니, 마치 사육제 때 개를 가지고 노는 것처럼 그를 가지고 장난쳤다."]],[["The cries of the poor blanketed wretch were so loud that they reached the ears of his master, who, halting to listen attentively, was persuaded that some new adventure was coming, until he clearly perceived that it was his squire who uttered them. Wheeling about he came up to the inn with a laborious gallop, and finding it shut went round it to see if he could find some way of getting in; but as soon as he came to the wall of the yard, which was not very high, he discovered the game that was being played with his squire. He saw him rising and falling in the air with such grace and nimbleness that, had his rage allowed him, it is my belief he would have laughed. He tried to climb from his horse on to the top of the wall, but he was so bruised and battered that he could not even dismount; and so from the back of his horse he began to utter such maledictions and objurgations against those who were blanketing Sancho as it would be impossible to write down accurately: they, however, did not stay their laughter or their work for this, nor did the flying Sancho cease his lamentations, mingled now with threats, now with entreaties but all to little purpose, or none at all, until from pure weariness they left off. They then brought him his ass, and mounting him on top of it they put his jacket round him; and the compassionate Maritornes, seeing him so exhausted, thought fit to refresh him with a jug of water, and that it might be all the cooler she fetched it from the well. Sancho took it, and as he was raising it to his mouth he was stopped by the cries of his master exclaiming, “Sancho, my son, drink not water; drink it not, my son, for it will kill thee; see, here I have the blessed balsam (and he held up the flask of liquor), and with drinking two drops of it thou wilt certainly be restored.”","담요에 띄워진 가엾은 자의 비명이 어찌나 컸던지 그의 주인의 귀에까지 닿았고, 주인은 멈춰 서서 귀 기울여 듣고는 무슨 새로운 모험이 다가오고 있구나 확신했는데, 마침내 그 소리를 지르는 것이 제 종자임을 똑똑히 알아차렸다. 그는 말 머리를 돌려 힘겨운 질주로 여관에 다다랐고, 문이 닫혀 있는 것을 보고는 들어갈 만한 길이 없는지 둘러보려고 여관 둘레를 돌았다. 그러나 그다지 높지 않은 마당 담벼락에 이르자마자, 제 종자를 상대로 벌어지고 있는 놀음을 발견했다. 그는 산초가 어찌나 우아하고 날렵하게 공중에서 오르내리는지를 보았으니, 분노가 허락만 했더라면 그가 웃음을 터뜨렸으리라고 나는 믿는다. 그는 말에서 담벼락 꼭대기로 기어오르려 했으나, 어찌나 멍들고 두들겨 맞았던지 말에서 내릴 수조차 없었다. 그래서 그는 말 등에 앉은 채 산초를 담요질하는 자들에게, 정확히 받아 적기란 불가능할 만큼 온갖 저주와 욕설을 퍼붓기 시작했다. 그러나 그들은 이 때문에 웃음도 작업도 멈추지 않았고, 날아오르는 산초도 이제는 위협을, 또 이제는 애원을 뒤섞은 한탄을 그치지 않았으나, 다 부질없거나 아무 소용이 없었으며, 마침내 그들은 순전히 지쳐서야 그만두었다. 그러고는 그에게 당나귀를 가져다주고 그 위에 그를 태운 다음, 그의 윗도리를 둘러 주었다. 그리고 그가 그토록 기진한 것을 본 가엾이 여기는 마음의 마리토르네스는, 그에게 물 한 단지로 기운을 차리게 해 주는 것이 좋겠다 싶어, 그것이 한결 더 시원하도록 우물에서 길어 왔다. 산초는 그것을 받아 입에 가져가려다, 이렇게 외치는 제 주인의 고함에 멈췄다. \"산초, 내 아들아, 물을 마시지 말게. 마시지 말게, 내 아들아, 그것은 자네를 죽일 걸세. 보게, 여기 그 축복받은 영약이 있지 않은가(그러면서 그는 약병을 치켜들었다). 이것을 두 방울만 마시면 자네는 틀림없이 원기를 회복할 걸세.\""]],[["At these words Sancho turned his eyes asquint, and in a still louder voice said, “Can it be your worship has forgotten that I am not a knight, or do you want me to end by vomiting up what bowels I have left after last night?","이 말에 산초는 곁눈질로 그를 흘겨보며 한층 더 큰 목소리로 말했다. \"나리께서는 제가 기사가 아니라는 걸 잊으셨습니까, 아니면 어젯밤 이후로 제게 남은 창자나마 다 토해 내며 끝장나기를 바라십니까?"],["Keep your liquor in the name of all the devils, and leave me to myself!”","그놈의 약일랑 온갖 악마들의 이름으로 나리나 가지시고, 저는 제멋대로 내버려 두십시오!\" 그러고는 말을 그치는 바로 그 순간에 마시기 시작했다."],["and at one and the same instant he left off talking and began drinking; but as at the first sup he perceived it was water he did not care to go on with it, and begged Maritornes to fetch him some wine, which she did with right good will, and paid for it with her own money; for indeed they say of her that, though she was in that line of life, there was some faint and distant resemblance to a Christian about her.","그러나 첫 모금에 그것이 물인 것을 알아채고는 더 이어 마실 마음이 없어져, 마리토르네스에게 포도주를 좀 가져다 달라고 청했고, 그녀는 흔쾌히 그리해 주었으며 게다가 제 돈으로 값을 치렀다. 사람들이 그녀에 대해 하는 말로는, 그녀가 비록 그런 처지의 삶을 살고는 있었으나 그녀에게는 어딘가 희미하고 아득하게나마 기독교인다운 데가 남아 있었다고 한다."],["When Sancho had done drinking he dug his heels into his ass, and the gate of the inn being thrown open he passed out very well pleased at having paid nothing and carried his point, though it had been at the expense of his usual sureties, his shoulders.","산초는 다 마시고 나서 당나귀 옆구리에 발뒤꿈치를 박았고, 여관 문이 활짝 열려 있던 터라 아무것도 치르지 않고 제 뜻을 관철한 것에 무척 흡족해하며 빠져나갔다. 비록 그것이 늘 그의 담보가 되어 주던 어깨를 대가로 치른 것이긴 했지만 말이다."],["It is true that the innkeeper detained his alforjas in payment of what was owing to him, but Sancho took his departure in such a flurry that he never missed them.","사실 여관 주인은 그가 빚진 값으로 그의 안장주머니를 붙들어 두었으나, 산초는 어찌나 허둥지둥 떠났던지 그것이 없어진 것조차 알아채지 못했다."],["The innkeeper, as soon as he saw him off, wanted to bar the gate close, but the blanketers would not agree to it, for they were fellows who would not have cared two farthings for Don Quixote, even had he been really one of the knights-errant of the Round Table.","여관 주인은 그가 떠나는 것을 보자마자 문을 단단히 걸어 잠그려 했으나, 담요질하던 자들이 이에 동의하지 않았으니, 그들은 돈키호테가 설령 정말로 원탁의 편력 기사 중 하나였다 한들 그를 두 푼어치만큼도 대수롭게 여기지 않을 부류였기 때문이다."]],[["# CHAPTER XVIII. IN WHICH IS RELATED THE DISCOURSE SANCHO PANZA HELD WITH HIS MASTER, DON QUIXOTE, AND OTHER ADVENTURES WORTH RELATING","# CHAPTER XVIII."]],[["Sancho reached his master so limp and faint that he could not urge on his beast. When Don Quixote saw the state he was in he said, “I have now come to the conclusion, good Sancho, that this castle or inn is beyond a doubt enchanted, because those who have so atrociously diverted themselves with thee, what can they be but phantoms or beings of another world? and I hold this confirmed by having noticed that when I was by the wall of the yard witnessing the acts of thy sad tragedy, it was out of my power to mount upon it, nor could I even dismount from Rocinante, because they no doubt had me enchanted; for I swear to thee by the faith of what I am that if I had been able to climb up or dismount, I would have avenged thee in such a way that those braggart thieves would have remembered their freak for ever, even though in so doing I knew that I contravened the laws of chivalry, which, as I have often told thee, do not permit a knight to lay hands on him who is not one, save in case of urgent and great necessity in defence of his own life and person.”","산초는 너무도 기진맥진하고 몸이 늘어진 채 주인에게 다다라서, 제 짐승을 몰아붙일 기력조차 없었다. 돈키호테는 그 꼴을 보고 말했다. \"착한 산초여, 이제 나는 이런 결론에 이르렀느니라. 이 성, 아니 이 객줏집은 의심할 여지없이 마법에 걸린 것이다. 너를 그토록 잔인하게 가지고 논 자들이 유령이거나 다른 세계의 존재가 아니라면 대체 무엇이겠느냐? 그리고 이를 더욱 확실히 믿게 된 까닭이 있으니, 내가 안마당 담장 곁에서 너의 그 슬픈 비극을 지켜보고 섰을 때, 도무지 그 담장을 기어오를 수도 없었고, 로시난테에서 내려설 수조차 없었다. 그자들이 분명 나를 마법에 걸어 놓았던 게야. 내 이 신분의 이름을 걸고 너에게 맹세하노니, 만일 내가 담을 넘거나 말에서 내릴 수 있었더라면, 그 허풍선이 도둑놈들이 자기들 장난질을 영원토록 잊지 못하도록 너의 원수를 갚아 주었을 것이다. 비록 그리하는 것이, 내가 너에게 누차 일렀듯이, 기사가 아닌 자에게 손을 대는 것을 금하는 기사도의 법도를 어기는 줄 알면서도 말이다. 다만 제 목숨과 몸을 지키기 위한 절박하고 큰 위급함이 있을 때는 예외이니라.\""]],[["“I would have avenged myself too if I could,” said Sancho, “whether I had been dubbed knight or not, but I could not; though for my part I am persuaded those who amused themselves with me were not phantoms or enchanted men, as your worship says, but men of flesh and bone like ourselves; and they all had their names, for I heard them name them when they were tossing me, and one was called Pedro Martinez, and another Tenorio Hernandez, and the innkeeper, I heard, was called Juan Palomeque the Left-handed; so that, señor, your not being able to leap over the wall of the yard or dismount from your horse came of something else besides enchantments; and what I make out clearly from all this is, that these adventures we go seeking will in the end lead us into such misadventures that we shall not know which is our right foot; and that the best and wisest thing, according to my small wits, would be for us to return home, now that it is harvest-time, and attend to our business, and give over wandering from Zeca to Mecca and from pail to bucket, as the saying is.”","\"저도 할 수만 있었다면 제 원수를 갚았을 겁니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"기사 서임을 받았든 안 받았든 말입니다요. 하지만 그럴 수가 없었습니다요. 그래도 제 생각으로는, 저를 가지고 논 자들은 나리께서 말씀하시는 유령이나 마법에 걸린 자들이 아니라, 우리들처럼 살과 뼈로 된 사람들이었습니다요. 다들 이름이 있었거든요. 저를 던져 올릴 때 서로 이름 부르는 걸 제가 들었습죠. 한 놈은 페드로 마르티네스라 불렀고, 또 한 놈은 테노리오 에르난데스, 그리고 주막 주인은 왼손잡이 후안 팔로메케라고 부르는 걸 들었습니다요. 그러니 나리, 나리께서 안마당 담을 뛰어넘지도 못하고 말에서 내리지도 못하신 건 마법 말고 다른 무언가 때문이었습죠. 그리고 제가 이 모든 일에서 분명히 깨달은 건, 우리가 찾아 헤매는 이런 모험들이 결국엔 우리를 어떤 봉변으로 끌고 들어가서, 어느 쪽이 우리 오른발인지조차 분간 못하게 만들리란 겁니다요. 제 짧은 머리로 생각하기엔, 지금이 추수철이니 가장 좋고 현명한 일은 우리가 집으로 돌아가 제 할 일에 매달리는 것입죠. 속담대로, 세카에서 메카로, 이 양동이에서 저 양동이로 떠돌아다니는 짓일랑 그만두는 거 말입니다요.\""]],[["“How little thou knowest about chivalry, Sancho,”","\"산초, 너는 기사도에 대해 어찌 그리도 아는 게 없느냐.\" 돈키호테가 대꾸했다."],["replied Don Quixote; “hold thy peace and have patience; the day will come when thou shalt see with thine own eyes what an honourable thing it is to wander in the pursuit of this calling; nay, tell me, what greater pleasure can there be in the world, or what delight can equal that of winning a battle, and triumphing over one’s enemy?","\"입을 다물고 참을성을 가지거라. 언젠가는 이 천직을 좇아 떠도는 것이 얼마나 명예로운 일인지 네 두 눈으로 보게 될 날이 오리라."],["None, beyond all doubt.”","아니, 말해 보아라, 세상에 이보다 더 큰 즐거움이 어디 있으며, 싸움에서 이겨 적을 무찌르는 그 기쁨에 견줄 만한 환희가 어디 있겠느냐? 의심할 바 없이 그런 건 없느니라.\""]],[["“Very likely,” answered Sancho, “though I do not know it; all I know is that since we have been knights-errant, or since your worship has been one (for I have no right to reckon myself one of so honourable a number) we have never won any battle except the one with the Biscayan, and even out of that your worship came with half an ear and half a helmet the less; and from that till now it has been all cudgellings and more cudgellings, cuffs and more cuffs, I getting the blanketing over and above, and falling in with enchanted persons on whom I cannot avenge myself so as to know what the delight, as your worship calls it, of conquering an enemy is like.”","\"그럴 법도 합니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"하지만 저는 모르겠는뎁쇼. 제가 아는 거라곤, 우리가 편력 기사가 된 이래, 아니 나리께서 편력 기사가 되신 이래(저 같은 놈이 그토록 명예로운 무리 축에 든다고 할 권리는 없으니 말입죠) 우리는 비스카야 사람과 벌인 그 한 판 말고는 어떤 싸움에서도 이긴 적이 없습니다요. 게다가 그것에서조차 나리께서는 귀 한쪽과 투구 반쪽을 잃고 나오셨지요. 그때부터 지금까지 줄곧 몽둥이질에 또 몽둥이질, 주먹질에 또 주먹질뿐이었고, 저는 그 위에 담요 키질까지 덤으로 받았습죠. 그러고는 마법에 걸린 자들이나 만나니, 그자들한테는 원수를 갚을 수도 없어서 나리께서 말씀하시는 그 적을 무찌르는 기쁨이라는 게 어떤 건지 알 도리가 없습니다요.\""]],[["“That is what vexes me, and what ought to vex thee, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote; “but henceforward I will endeavour to have at hand some sword made by such craft that no kind of enchantments can take effect upon him who carries it, and it is even possible that fortune may procure for me that which belonged to Amadis when he was called ‘The Knight of the Burning Sword,’ which was one of the best swords that ever knight in the world possessed, for, besides having the said virtue, it cut like a razor, and there was no armour, however strong and enchanted it might be, that could resist it.”","\"그것이 바로 나를 괴롭게 하는 것이며, 너 또한 괴롭게 해야 마땅한 것이니라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"허나 이제부터 나는 어떤 마법도 그것을 지닌 자에게 효력을 부리지 못하도록 신묘한 솜씨로 만든 검 한 자루를 손에 넣도록 애써 보겠다. 어쩌면 운명이 내게, '불타는 검의 기사'라 불리던 시절 아마디스가 지녔던 그 검을 마련해 줄지도 모르지. 그것은 세상의 어떤 기사가 가졌던 것 중에서도 으뜸가는 검 가운데 하나였으니, 앞서 말한 그 신묘한 효험을 지녔을 뿐 아니라 면도날처럼 잘 들어, 제아무리 튼튼하고 마법에 걸린 갑옷이라도 그것을 당해 내지 못했느니라.\""]],[["“Such is my luck,”","\"제 팔자가 그렇습죠.\""],["said Sancho, “that even if that happened and your worship found some such sword, it would, like the balsam, turn out serviceable and good for dubbed knights only, and as for the squires, they might sup sorrow.”","산초가 말했다. \"설령 그런 일이 일어나 나리께서 그런 검을 손에 넣으신다 해도, 그건 그 영약처럼 서임받은 기사들한테만 쓸모 있고 좋은 것으로 판명될 테고, 종자들로 말하자면 그저 시름이나 안주 삼아 들이켜야 할 겁니다요.\""]],[["“Fear not that, Sancho,”","\"그것은 두려워 말거라, 산초.\""],["said Don Quixote: “Heaven will deal better by thee.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"하늘이 너를 더 나은 길로 이끌어 줄 것이니라.\""]],[["Thus talking, Don Quixote and his squire were going along, when, on the road they were following, Don Quixote perceived approaching them a large and thick cloud of dust, on seeing which he turned to Sancho and said:","이렇게 이야기를 나누며 돈키호테와 그의 종자가 길을 가고 있을 때, 그들이 따라가던 길 위로 돈키호테는 크고 짙은 먼지구름이 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 알아챘고, 그것을 보고는 산초를 돌아보며 말했다."]],[["“This is the day, Sancho, on which will be seen the boon my fortune is reserving for me; this, I say, is the day on which as much as on any other shall be displayed the might of my arm, and on which I shall do deeds that shall remain written in the book of fame for all ages to come.","\"산초, 오늘이 바로 내 운명이 나를 위해 간직해 둔 은혜가 드러날 날이로다. 다시 말하노니, 오늘이야말로 그 어느 날 못지않게 내 팔의 위력이 펼쳐질 날이며, 내가 오는 모든 세대에 걸쳐 명예의 책에 길이 적힐 위업을 이룰 날이니라."],["Seest thou that cloud of dust which rises yonder?","저기 솟아오르는 저 먼지구름이 보이느냐?"],["Well, then, all that is churned up by a vast army composed of various and countless nations that comes marching there.”","자, 그러니까 저것은 여러 갈래의 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 민족으로 이루어진 어마어마한 군대가 저리로 행군해 오면서 일으킨 것이니라.\""]],[["“According to that there must be two,”","\"그렇다면 둘이 있는 게 틀림없습니다요.\""],["said Sancho, “for on this opposite side also there rises just such another cloud of dust.”","산초가 말했다. \"이쪽 맞은편에서도 꼭 그만한 먼지구름이 하나 솟아오르고 있거든요.\""]],[["Don Quixote turned to look and found that it was true, and rejoicing exceedingly, he concluded that they were two armies about to engage and encounter in the midst of that broad plain; for at all times and seasons his fancy was full of the battles, enchantments, adventures, crazy feats, loves, and defiances that are recorded in the books of chivalry, and everything he said, thought, or did had reference to such things. Now the cloud of dust he had seen was raised by two great droves of sheep coming along the same road in opposite directions, which, because of the dust, did not become visible until they drew near, but Don Quixote asserted so positively that they were armies that Sancho was led to believe it and say, “Well, and what are we to do, señor?”","돈키호테가 돌아서서 바라보니 과연 그것이 사실이라, 그는 몹시 기뻐하며 그 두 군대가 저 드넓은 벌판 한가운데서 맞붙어 싸우려 하는 것이라고 결론지었다. 언제 어느 때고 그의 공상은 기사도 책에 적힌 전투와 마법과 모험과 미친 듯한 무훈과 사랑과 도전으로 가득 차 있었고, 그가 말하고 생각하고 행하는 모든 것이 그런 것들과 관련되어 있었기 때문이다. 그런데 그가 본 그 먼지구름이란 같은 길을 따라 서로 반대 방향에서 오던 양 떼 두 무리가 일으킨 것이었고, 먼지 때문에 가까이 다가오기 전까지는 보이지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 돈키호테가 그것이 군대라고 너무도 확신에 차서 단언하는 통에 산초도 그 말을 믿게 되어 이렇게 말했다. \"그래, 그럼 우리는 어찌해야 합니까요, 나리?\""]],[["“What?” said Don Quixote: “give aid and assistance to the weak and those who need it; and thou must know, Sancho, that this which comes opposite to us is conducted and led by the mighty emperor Alifanfaron, lord of the great isle of Trapobana; this other that marches behind me is that of his enemy the king of the Garamantas, Pentapolin of the Bare Arm, for he always goes into battle with his right arm bare.”","\"어찌하다니?\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"약한 자들과 도움이 필요한 자들을 돕고 거드는 게지. 그리고 산초, 너는 알아 두어라, 우리 맞은편에서 오는 저것은 위대한 트라포바나 섬의 군주, 강대한 황제 알리판파론이 이끌고 통솔하는 것이며, 내 등 뒤에서 행군해 오는 저 다른 것은 그의 적인 가라만타의 왕, 맨팔의 펜타폴린의 것이니라. 그는 늘 오른팔을 드러내고 전장에 나서기에 그리 불리는 게야.\""]],[["“But why are these two lords such enemies?”","\"한데 이 두 군주는 어찌하여 그토록 원수지간입니까요?\""]],[["“They are at enmity,”","\"그들이 서로 원수가 된 까닭은,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote, “because this Alifanfaron is a furious pagan and is in love with the daughter of Pentapolin, who is a very beautiful and moreover gracious lady, and a Christian, and her father is unwilling to bestow her upon the pagan king unless he first abandons the religion of his false prophet Mahomet, and adopts his own.”","\"이 알리판파론이 광포한 이교도이며 펜타폴린의 딸을 사랑하기 때문이니라. 그 딸은 매우 아름답고 게다가 우아한 귀부인이며 그리스도교도인데, 그 아비가 거짓 예언자 마호메트의 종교를 먼저 버리고 자기 종교를 받아들이지 않는 한 그 이교도 왕에게 딸을 내주려 하지 않는 게지.\""]],[["“By my beard,”","\"제 수염을 걸고,\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “but Pentapolin does quite right, and I will help him as much as I can.”","\"펜타폴린이 아주 옳게 하는 겁니다요. 저도 힘닿는 데까지 그를 돕겠습니다요.\""]],[["“In that thou wilt do what is thy duty, Sancho,”","\"그리하면 너는 마땅히 할 도리를 다하는 것이니라, 산초.\""],["said Don Quixote; “for to engage in battles of this sort it is not requisite to be a dubbed knight.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"이런 종류의 싸움에 끼어드는 데는 서임받은 기사일 필요가 없으니까 말이다.\""]],[["“That I can well understand,”","\"그건 저도 잘 알아듣겠습니다요.\""],["answered Sancho; “but where shall we put this ass where we may be sure to find him after the fray is over?","산초가 대답했다. \"한데 이 당나귀를 어디다 두어야 싸움이 끝난 뒤에 틀림없이 다시 찾을 수 있을깝쇼?"],["for I believe it has not been the custom so far to go into battle on a beast of this kind.”","제가 알기로 여태껏 이런 짐승을 타고 전장에 나가는 건 관례가 아니었던 것 같아서 말입니다요.\""]],[["“That is true,” said Don Quixote, “and what you had best do with him is to leave him to take his chance whether he be lost or not, for the horses we shall have when we come out victors will be so many that even Rocinante will run a risk of being changed for another. But attend to me and observe, for I wish to give thee some account of the chief knights who accompany these two armies; and that thou mayest the better see and mark, let us withdraw to that hillock which rises yonder, whence both armies may be seen.”","\"그건 맞는 말이다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 그놈을 두고 할 수 있는 최선은, 잃어버리든 말든 운에 맡겨 내버려 두는 것이니라. 우리가 승리자로 나올 때 얻게 될 말이 어찌나 많을지, 로시난테조차 다른 말로 바꿔질 위험에 처할 정도일 테니까. 허나 내 말을 새겨듣고 잘 보아 두어라. 이 두 군대에 함께한 으뜸가는 기사들에 관해 너에게 좀 일러 주고 싶구나. 그리고 네가 더 잘 보고 새길 수 있도록, 저기 솟아 있는 저 언덕으로 물러가자. 거기서라면 두 군대가 다 보일 게다.\""]],[["They did so, and placed themselves on a rising ground from which the two droves that Don Quixote made armies of might have been plainly seen if the clouds of dust they raised had not obscured them and blinded the sight; nevertheless, seeing in his imagination what he did not see and what did not exist, he began thus in a loud voice:","그들은 그렇게 하여, 돈키호테가 군대로 여긴 그 두 떼가 일으킨 먼지구름이 가리고 시야를 가로막지만 않았더라면 또렷이 보였을 그 솟은 땅에 자리를 잡았다. 그럼에도 돈키호테는 보이지 않는 것, 존재하지도 않는 것을 자기 상상 속에서 보면서 이렇게 큰 소리로 말하기 시작했다."]],[["“That knight whom thou seest yonder in yellow armour, who bears upon his shield a lion crowned crouching at the feet of a damsel, is the valiant Laurcalco, lord of the Silver Bridge; that one in armour with flowers of gold, who bears on his shield three crowns argent on an azure field, is the dreaded Micocolembo, grand duke of Quirocia; that other of gigantic frame, on his right hand, is the ever dauntless Brandabarbaran de Boliche, lord of the three Arabias, who for armour wears that serpent skin, and has for shield a gate which, according to tradition, is one of those of the temple that Samson brought to the ground when by his death he revenged himself upon his enemies. But turn thine eyes to the other side, and thou shalt see in front and in the van of this other army the ever victorious and never vanquished Timonel of Carcajona, prince of New Biscay, who comes in armour with arms quartered azure, vert, white, and yellow, and bears on his shield a cat or on a field tawny with a motto which says Miau, which is the beginning of the name of his lady, who according to report is the peerless Miaulina, daughter of the duke Alfeniquen of the Algarve; the other, who burdens and presses the loins of that powerful charger and bears arms white as snow and a shield blank and without any device, is a novice knight, a Frenchman by birth, Pierres Papin by name, lord of the baronies of Utrique; that other, who with iron-shod heels strikes the flanks of that nimble parti-coloured zebra, and for arms bears azure vair, is the mighty duke of Nerbia, Espartafilardo del Bosque, who bears for device on his shield an asparagus plant with a motto in Castilian that says, Rastrea mi suerte.” And so he went on naming a number of knights of one squadron or the other out of his imagination, and to all he assigned off-hand their arms, colours, devices, and mottoes, carried away by the illusions of his unheard-of craze; and without a pause, he continued, “People of divers nations compose this squadron in front; here are those that drink of the sweet waters of the famous Xanthus, those that scour the woody Massilian plains, those that sift the pure fine gold of Arabia Felix, those that enjoy the famed cool banks of the crystal Thermodon, those that in many and various ways divert the streams of the golden Pactolus, the Numidians, faithless in their promises, the Persians renowned in archery, the Parthians and the Medes that fight as they fly, the Arabs that ever shift their dwellings, the Scythians as cruel as they are fair, the Ethiopians with pierced lips, and an infinity of other nations whose features I recognise and descry, though I cannot recall their names. In this other squadron there come those that drink of the crystal streams of the olive-bearing Betis, those that make smooth their countenances with the water of the ever rich and golden Tagus, those that rejoice in the fertilising flow of the divine Genil, those that roam the Tartesian plains abounding in pasture, those that take their pleasure in the Elysian meadows of Jerez, the rich Manchegans crowned with ruddy ears of corn, the wearers of iron, old relics of the Gothic race, those that bathe in the Pisuerga renowned for its gentle current, those that feed their herds along the spreading pastures of the winding Guadiana famed for its hidden course, those that tremble with the cold of the pineclad Pyrenees or the dazzling snows of the lofty Apennine; in a word, as many as all Europe includes and contains.”","\"네가 저기 보는, 노란 갑옷을 입고 처녀의 발치에 웅크린 왕관 쓴 사자를 방패에 그려 넣은 저 기사는 은(銀)의 다리의 군주, 용맹한 라우르칼코니라. 금빛 꽃무늬 갑옷을 입고 하늘빛 바탕에 은빛 왕관 셋을 방패에 그려 넣은 저자는 키로시아의 대공, 두려운 미코콜렘보이며, 그 오른편에 있는 저 거인 같은 몸집의 사내는 결코 겁먹는 법이 없는 세 아라비아의 군주, 브란다바르바란 데 볼리체이니, 갑옷으로 저 뱀 가죽을 두르고 방패로는 성문 하나를 들었는데, 전해 오는 이야기로는 그것이 삼손이 죽음으로써 자기 원수들에게 복수할 때 무너뜨린 그 신전의 성문 가운데 하나라 하느니라. 허나 눈을 다른 쪽으로 돌려 보아라, 그러면 이 다른 군대의 맨 앞 선봉에서 늘 승리하고 결코 패한 적 없는 카르카호나의 티모넬, 새 비스카야의 왕자를 보게 될 것이다. 그는 하늘빛, 초록빛, 흰빛, 노란빛으로 네 등분한 문장의 갑옷을 입고 왔으며, 황갈색 바탕에 금빛 고양이 한 마리를 방패에 그려 넣고 '미아우'라는 표어를 새겼는데, 이는 그가 모시는 귀부인의 이름 첫머리이니라. 소문에 따르면 그 귀부인은 둘도 없는 미아울리나로, 알가르베의 공작 알페니켄의 딸이라 하느니라. 저 힘센 군마의 허리를 짓누르고 눌러 대며 눈처럼 흰 갑옷에, 아무런 문양도 없는 빈 방패를 든 다른 한 사람은 프랑스 태생의 풋내기 기사로, 이름은 피에르 파팽, 우트리케 영지의 영주이니라. 또 쇠를 댄 발꿈치로 저 날렵한 얼룩무늬 얼룩말의 옆구리를 차며, 갑옷으로 하늘빛 다람쥐 털 문장을 두른 저 다른 사람은 강대한 네르비아의 공작, 에스파르타필라르도 델 보스케이니, 방패의 문장으로 아스파라거스 한 포기를 그리고 카스티야어로 '내 운수를 좇는다'는 표어를 새겼느니라.\" 이렇게 그는 자기 상상 속에서 이쪽 부대, 저쪽 부대의 기사들을 잇따라 호명해 나갔고, 그 들어 본 적 없는 광기의 환상에 휩쓸린 채 모든 이에게 그 자리에서 곧장 갑옷이며 빛깔이며 문양이며 표어를 붙여 주었다. 그러고는 잠시도 멈추지 않고 이어 갔다. \"여러 갈래의 민족이 앞에 있는 이 부대를 이루고 있느니라. 여기 이름난 크산토스의 단물을 마시는 자들이 있고, 숲 우거진 마실리아의 평원을 누비는 자들이 있으며, 아라비아 펠릭스의 순수하고 고운 황금을 가려내는 자들이 있고, 수정 같은 테르모돈의 이름난 서늘한 강기슭을 즐기는 자들이 있으며, 갖가지 방식으로 황금빛 팍톨로스의 물줄기를 갈라놓는 자들이 있고, 약속에 신의가 없는 누미디아인들, 활쏘기로 이름난 페르시아인들, 달아나면서 싸우는 파르티아인들과 메디아인들, 늘 거처를 옮기는 아랍인들, 아름다운 만큼이나 잔혹한 스키타이인들, 입술에 구멍을 뚫은 에티오피아인들, 그리고 그 이름은 기억나지 않으나 그 생김새는 알아보고 분간할 수 있는 무수히 많은 다른 민족들이 있느니라. 이 다른 부대에는 올리브 무성한 베티스의 수정 같은 물줄기를 마시는 자들이 오고, 늘 풍요롭고 황금빛인 타호의 물로 얼굴을 다듬는 자들, 신성한 헤닐의 기름진 물줄기를 기뻐하는 자들, 목초 넘치는 타르테소스의 평원을 누비는 자들, 헤레스의 엘리시온 같은 초원에서 즐기는 자들, 붉게 익은 보리 이삭으로 머리를 두른 부유한 라만차 사람들, 고트 종족의 오랜 유물인 쇠갑옷을 두른 자들, 부드러운 물살로 이름난 피수에르가에서 멱 감는 자들, 숨겨진 물길로 이름난 굽이진 과디아나의 드넓은 목초지를 따라 가축을 먹이는 자들, 솔숲 우거진 피레네의 추위나 드높은 아펜니노의 눈부신 눈발에 떠는 자들, 한마디로 온 유럽이 품고 거느린 모든 자들이 오느니라.\""]],[["Good God! what a number of countries and nations he named!","맙소사, 그가 호명한 나라와 민족이 얼마나 많았던가!"],["giving to each its proper attributes with marvellous readiness; brimful and saturated with what he had read in his lying books!","거짓투성이 책에서 읽은 것으로 가득 차 넘쳐 흠뻑 젖은 채, 놀라우리만치 거침없이 저마다에 어울리는 특성을 붙여 주었다!"],["Sancho Panza hung upon his words without speaking, and from time to time turned to try if he could see the knights and giants his master was describing, and as he could not make out one of them he said to him:","산초 판사는 말없이 그의 말에 매달려 있다가, 이따금 돌아보며 자기 주인이 묘사하는 그 기사들과 거인들을 볼 수 있을까 하고 살폈으나, 그 가운데 하나도 분간해 낼 수 없자 그에게 말했다."]],[["“Señor, devil take it if there’s a sign of any man you talk of, knight or giant, in the whole thing; maybe it’s all enchantment, like the phantoms last night.”","\"나리, 빌어먹을, 나리께서 말씀하시는 그 누구의 그림자도, 기사든 거인이든 도통 보이질 않습니다요. 어쩌면 이게 죄다 마법인지도 모르겠습죠, 어젯밤 그 유령들처럼 말입니다요.\""]],[["“How canst thou say that!”","\"어찌 그런 말을 하느냐!\""],["answered Don Quixote; “dost thou not hear the neighing of the steeds, the braying of the trumpets, the roll of the drums?”","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"군마들의 울음소리며 나팔 소리, 북소리가 들리지 않느냐?\""]],[["“I hear nothing but a great bleating of ewes and sheep,”","\"양 떼와 새끼 양들이 어마어마하게 우는 소리 말고는 아무것도 들리지 않습니다요.\""],["said Sancho; which was true, for by this time the two flocks had come close.","산초가 말했는데, 그것은 사실이었으니, 이때쯤에는 두 양 떼가 가까이 다가와 있었던 것이다."]],[["“The fear thou art in, Sancho,”","\"산초, 네가 빠져 있는 두려움이,\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “prevents thee from seeing or hearing correctly, for one of the effects of fear is to derange the senses and make things appear different from what they are; if thou art in such fear, withdraw to one side and leave me to myself, for alone I suffice to bring victory to that side to which I shall give my aid;”","\"너로 하여금 똑바로 보지도 듣지도 못하게 하는 게다. 두려움의 작용 가운데 하나가 바로 감각을 어지럽혀 사물을 본디와 다르게 보이도록 만드는 것이니라."],["and so saying he gave Rocinante the spur, and putting the lance in rest, shot down the slope like a thunderbolt.","네가 그토록 두렵거든 한쪽으로 물러나 나를 혼자 있게 두어라. 내가 내 도움을 베풀 그쪽 편에 승리를 안겨 주는 데는 나 혼자로도 족하니라.\""],["Sancho shouted after him, crying, “Come back, Señor Don Quixote; I vow to God they are sheep and ewes you are charging!","이렇게 말하고는 로시난테에게 박차를 가하고 창을 옆구리에 비껴 든 채 벼락처럼 비탈을 내달려 내려갔다. 산초가 그 뒤에다 대고 소리쳤다."],["Come back!","\"돌아오십쇼, 돈키호테 나리."],["Unlucky the father that begot me!","하느님께 맹세코 나리께서 들이치시는 건 양 떼와 새끼 양들입니다요! 돌아오십쇼!"],["what madness is this!","저를 낳은 아비가 불쌍하지! 이게 무슨 미친 짓입니까요!"],["Look, there is no giant, nor knight, nor cats, nor arms, nor shields quartered or whole, nor vair azure or bedevilled.","보십쇼, 거기 거인도, 기사도, 고양이도, 갑옷도, 네 등분한 방패도 온전한 방패도, 하늘빛 다람쥐 털 문장도 무슨 귀신 들린 것도 없습니다요. 대체 뭘 하시는 겁니까요?"],["What are you about?","하느님 앞에 죄인인 이놈!\""],["Sinner that I am before God!”","그러나 이 모든 애원에도 돈키호테는 돌아서지 않았다. 도리어 계속 소리쳐 댔다."],["But not for all these entreaties did Don Quixote turn back; on the contrary he went on shouting out, “Ho, knights, ye who follow and fight under the banners of the valiant emperor Pentapolin of the Bare Arm, follow me all; ye shall see how easily I shall give him his revenge over his enemy Alifanfaron of the Trapobana.”","\"오, 기사들이여, 용맹한 맨팔의 황제 펜타폴린의 깃발 아래 따르며 싸우는 그대들이여, 모두 나를 따르라. 내가 그의 적 트라포바나의 알리판파론에게 얼마나 손쉽게 그의 원수를 갚아 주는지 보게 되리라.\""]],[["So saying, he dashed into the midst of the squadron of ewes, and began spearing them with as much spirit and intrepidity as if he were transfixing mortal enemies in earnest.","이렇게 말하고는 그는 새끼 양 떼 한가운데로 돌진해 들어가, 진짜로 죽일 듯한 적들을 꿰뚫기라도 하는 듯 어찌나 기세 좋고 겁 없이 창으로 양들을 찔러 대기 시작했는지 모른다."],["The shepherds and drovers accompanying the flock shouted to him to desist; seeing it was no use, they ungirt their slings and began to salute his ears with stones as big as one’s fist.","양 떼를 따라온 목동들과 몰이꾼들이 그만두라고 그에게 소리쳤으나, 소용없음을 보자 그들은 투석기를 풀어 주먹만 한 돌멩이로 그의 귀를 향해 인사를 보내기 시작했다."],["Don Quixote gave no heed to the stones, but, letting drive right and left kept saying:","돈키호테는 돌멩이 따위에는 아랑곳하지 않고 좌우로 마구 휘둘러 대며 줄곧 외쳤다."]],[["“Where art thou, proud Alifanfaron?","\"오만한 알리판파론, 어디 있느냐?"],["Come before me; I am a single knight who would fain prove thy prowess hand to hand, and make thee yield thy life a penalty for the wrong thou dost to the valiant Pentapolin Garamanta.”","내 앞으로 나오라. 나는 너의 무용을 일대일로 시험해 보고, 용맹한 펜타폴린 가라만타에게 저지른 네 잘못의 대가로 네 목숨을 내놓게 하려는 한 사람의 기사이니라.\""],["Here came a sugar-plum from the brook that struck him on the side and buried a couple of ribs in his body.","이때 시냇가에서 사탕 알 하나가 날아와 그의 옆구리를 쳐서 갈빗대 두 대를 그의 몸속에 박아 넣었다."],["Feeling himself so smitten, he imagined himself slain or badly wounded for certain, and recollecting his liquor he drew out his flask, and putting it to his mouth began to pour the contents into his stomach; but ere he had succeeded in swallowing what seemed to him enough, there came another almond which struck him on the hand and on the flask so fairly that it smashed it to pieces, knocking three or four teeth and grinders out of his mouth in its course, and sorely crushing two fingers of his hand.","그토록 얻어맞고 보니 그는 자신이 분명 죽었거나 심하게 다쳤다고 여겨, 자기 영약을 떠올리며 호리병을 꺼내어 입에 갖다 대고 그 속을 위장 속으로 부어 넣기 시작했다. 그러나 그가 보기에 충분하다 싶을 만큼 미처 삼키기도 전에 또 다른 아몬드 알이 날아와 그의 손과 호리병을 어찌나 정통으로 맞혔는지, 호리병을 산산조각 내고 지나가는 길에 그의 입에서 이와 어금니 서너 개를 떨궈 내고 그의 손가락 두 개를 호되게 으스러뜨렸다."],["Such was the force of the first blow and of the second, that the poor knight in spite of himself came down backwards off his horse.","첫 번째 가격과 두 번째 가격의 힘이 어찌나 셌던지, 가엾은 기사는 자기 뜻과는 달리 말에서 뒤로 떨어지고 말았다."],["The shepherds came up, and felt sure they had killed him; so in all haste they collected their flock together, took up the dead beasts, of which there were more than seven, and made off without waiting to ascertain anything further.","목동들이 달려와서는 그를 죽였다고 확신했다. 그리하여 그들은 부랴부랴 양 떼를 한데 모으고, 일곱 마리가 넘는 죽은 짐승들을 거둬들인 뒤, 더는 아무것도 알아보려 기다리지 않고 달아나 버렸다."]],[["All this time Sancho stood on the hill watching the crazy feats his master was performing, and tearing his beard and cursing the hour and the occasion when fortune had made him acquainted with him.","이 모든 동안 산초는 언덕에 서서 자기 주인이 벌이는 미친 짓을 지켜보며, 제 수염을 쥐어뜯고 운명이 자기를 그와 알게 만든 그 시각과 그 계기를 저주하고 있었다."],["Seeing him, then, brought to the ground, and that the shepherds had taken themselves off, he ran to him and found him in very bad case, though not unconscious; and said he:","그러다가 주인이 땅바닥에 나뒹굴고 목동들도 자취를 감춘 것을 보자, 그에게 달려가 그가 정신은 잃지 않았으나 몹시 안 좋은 꼴인 것을 발견하고는 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“Did I not tell you to come back, Señor Don Quixote; and that what you were going to attack were not armies but droves of sheep?”","\"제가 돌아오시라고 말씀드리지 않았습니까요, 돈키호테 나리. 그리고 나리께서 들이치시려던 게 군대가 아니라 양 떼라고 말씀드리지 않았습니까요?\""]],[["“That’s how that thief of a sage, my enemy, can alter and falsify things,” answered Don Quixote; “thou must know, Sancho, that it is a very easy matter for those of his sort to make us believe what they choose; and this malignant being who persecutes me, envious of the glory he knew I was to win in this battle, has turned the squadrons of the enemy into droves of sheep. At any rate, do this much, I beg of thee, Sancho, to undeceive thyself, and see that what I say is true; mount thy ass and follow them quietly, and thou shalt see that when they have gone some little distance from this they will return to their original shape and, ceasing to be sheep, become men in all respects as I described them to thee at first. But go not just yet, for I want thy help and assistance; come hither, and see how many of my teeth and grinders are missing, for I feel as if there was not one left in my mouth.”","\"그게 바로 그 도둑놈 같은 마법사, 내 원수가 사물을 바꿔치기하고 거짓으로 둔갑시키는 수법이니라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"산초, 너는 알아 두어라, 그런 부류의 자들에게는 자기들이 원하는 것을 우리가 믿게 만드는 일쯤은 아주 손쉬운 노릇이다. 그리고 나를 핍박하는 이 악의에 찬 존재가, 내가 이 싸움에서 거두리라는 걸 알고 있던 그 영광을 시샘하여, 적의 부대를 양 떼로 바꿔 놓은 게다. 어쨌든 산초, 부탁이니 이만큼은 해 다오. 네 스스로 미혹에서 벗어나 내 말이 참인지 확인해 보아라. 네 당나귀에 올라타 조용히 그들을 따라가 보아라. 그러면 그들이 여기서 조금 멀어졌을 때, 본래의 모습으로 되돌아가 양이기를 그치고 내가 처음에 너에게 묘사해 준 그대로 모든 면에서 사람이 되는 것을 보게 될 게다. 허나 아직은 가지 말거라, 네 도움과 거듦이 필요하니까. 이리 와서, 내 이와 어금니가 몇 개나 빠졌는지 보아라, 입안에 하나도 남지 않은 것만 같구나.\""]],[["Sancho came so close that he almost put his eyes into his mouth; now just at that moment the balsam had acted on the stomach of Don Quixote, so, at the very instant when Sancho came to examine his mouth, he discharged all its contents with more force than a musket, and full into the beard of the compassionate squire.","산초가 어찌나 바짝 다가왔는지 거의 자기 눈을 주인의 입속에 들이밀 지경이었다. 그런데 바로 그 순간 영약이 돈키호테의 위장에서 효력을 부려, 산초가 그의 입을 살펴보러 온 바로 그 찰나에, 그는 그 속에 든 것을 화승총보다 더한 힘으로 죄다 쏟아 냈으니, 그것이 고스란히 가엾은 종자의 수염에 정통으로 쏟아졌다."]],[["“Holy Mary!”","\"성모 마리아여!\""],["cried Sancho, “what is this that has happened me?","산초가 외쳤다. \"이게 내게 무슨 일이람?"],["Clearly this sinner is mortally wounded, as he vomits blood from the mouth;”","이 죄인 같은 양반이 입에서 피를 토하는 걸 보니 영락없이 죽을 만큼 다치셨구나.\""],["but considering the matter a little more closely he perceived by the colour, taste, and smell, that it was not blood but the balsam from the flask which he had seen him drink; and he was taken with such a loathing that his stomach turned, and he vomited up his inside over his very master, and both were left in a precious state.","그러나 그것을 조금 더 자세히 살펴보고는, 빛깔과 맛과 냄새로 보아 그것이 피가 아니라 주인이 마시는 것을 자기가 보았던 그 호리병의 영약임을 알아챘다. 그러자 어찌나 메스꺼움이 치밀어 오르던지 속이 뒤집혀, 제 뱃속의 것을 바로 그 주인에게 토해 냈고, 둘 다 그야말로 볼만한 꼴이 되고 말았다."],["Sancho ran to his ass to get something wherewith to clean himself, and relieve his master, out of his alforjas; but not finding them, he well-nigh took leave of his senses, and cursed himself anew, and in his heart resolved to quit his master and return home, even though he forfeited the wages of his service and all hopes of the promised island.","산초는 자기 안장주머니에서 몸을 닦고 주인을 추슬러 줄 무언가를 꺼내려고 당나귀에게로 달려갔으나, 그것을 찾지 못하자 거의 정신이 나갈 지경이 되어 다시금 제 신세를 저주했고, 마음속으로는 봉사의 품삯과 약속받은 섬에 대한 온갖 희망을 모두 잃는 한이 있더라도 주인을 떠나 집으로 돌아가리라 결심했다."]],[["Don Quixote now rose, and putting his left hand to his mouth to keep his teeth from falling out altogether, with the other he laid hold of the bridle of Rocinante, who had never stirred from his master’s side--so loyal and well-behaved was he--and betook himself to where the squire stood leaning over his ass with his hand to his cheek, like one in deep dejection.","이제 돈키호테가 몸을 일으켜, 이가 죄다 빠져 버리지 않도록 왼손을 입에 갖다 대고, 다른 손으로는 — 어찌나 충직하고 얌전한지 주인 곁을 한 번도 떠나지 않았던 — 로시난테의 고삐를 붙잡고는, 종자가 깊은 실의에 빠진 사람처럼 한 손으로 뺨을 괴고 당나귀에 기대 서 있는 곳으로 갔다."],["Seeing him in this mood, looking so sad, Don Quixote said to him:","그가 그런 기분에 잠겨 그토록 슬픈 표정을 짓고 있는 것을 보고 돈키호테가 그에게 말했다."]],[["“Bear in mind, Sancho, that one man is no more than another, unless he does more than another; all these tempests that fall upon us are signs that fair weather is coming shortly, and that things will go well with us, for it is impossible for good or evil to last for ever; and hence it follows that the evil having lasted long, the good must be now nigh at hand; so thou must not distress thyself at the misfortunes which happen to me, since thou hast no share in them.”","\"산초, 명심하거라, 사람이란 남보다 더한 일을 하지 않는 한 다른 누구보다 나을 게 없느니라. 우리에게 들이닥치는 이 모든 폭풍은 머지않아 맑은 날씨가 오리라는, 그리고 우리에게 일이 잘 풀리리라는 징조이니라. 좋은 일이든 궂은일이든 영원히 갈 수는 없는 법이거든. 그러니 궂은일이 오래 이어졌으니 이제 좋은 일이 가까이 와 있음이 마땅하니라. 그러니 너는 나에게 닥치는 불운에 괴로워하지 말거라. 네가 그 불운에 몫이 있는 것도 아니니 말이다.\""]],[["“How have I not?”","\"제가 어찌 몫이 없습니까요?\""],["replied Sancho; “was he whom they blanketed yesterday perchance any other than my father’s son?","산초가 대꾸했다. \"어제 담요질 당한 게 혹시 제 아비의 아들이 아닌 다른 누구였답니까요?"],["and the alforjas that are missing to-day with all my treasures, did they belong to any other but myself?”","그리고 오늘 제 온갖 재산과 함께 없어진 안장주머니가 저 말고 다른 누구의 것이었답니까요?\""]],[["“What!","\"무어라!"],["are the alforjas missing, Sancho?”","안장주머니가 없어졌단 말이냐, 산초?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“Yes, they are missing,”","\"예, 없어졌습니다요.\""],["answered Sancho.","산초가 대답했다."]],[["“In that case we have nothing to eat to-day,”","\"그렇다면 우리는 오늘 먹을 것이 없겠구나.\""],["replied Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 대꾸했다."]],[["“It would be so,”","\"그렇게 되겠습죠.\""],["answered Sancho, “if there were none of the herbs your worship says you know in these meadows, those with which knights-errant as unlucky as your worship are wont to supply such-like shortcomings.”","산초가 대답했다. \"이 풀밭에 나리께서 안다고 말씀하시는 그 풀들이, 나리처럼 불운한 편력 기사들이 그런 결핍을 메우곤 한다는 그 풀들이 없다면 말입니다요.\""]],[["“For all that,”","\"그렇기는 해도,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["answered Don Quixote, “I would rather have just now a quarter of bread, or a loaf and a couple of pilchards’ heads, than all the herbs described by Dioscorides, even with Doctor Laguna’s notes.","\"나는 지금 디오스코리데스가 적어 놓은 온갖 풀들을, 라구나 박사의 주석까지 곁들인 것을 다 준다 해도, 그보다는 빵 한 조각이나 빵 한 덩이에 정어리 대가리 두엇을 더 가지고 싶구나. 그래도 착한 산초여, 네 짐승에 올라타 나와 함께 가자꾸나."],["Nevertheless, Sancho the Good, mount thy beast and come along with me, for God, who provides for all things, will not fail us (more especially when we are so active in his service as we are), since he fails not the midges of the air, nor the grubs of the earth, nor the tadpoles of the water, and is so merciful that he maketh his sun to rise on the good and on the evil, and sendeth rain on the unjust and on the just.”","만물을 돌보시는 하느님께서 우리를 저버리지 않으실 게다(더구나 우리처럼 그분의 일에 이토록 부지런할 때야 말할 것도 없지). 그분은 공중의 하루살이도, 땅속의 굼벵이도, 물속의 올챙이도 저버리지 않으시며, 어찌나 자비로우신지 선한 자와 악한 자 위에 똑같이 해를 떠오르게 하시고, 의로운 자와 불의한 자에게 똑같이 비를 내려 주시느니라.\""]],[["“Your worship would make a better preacher than knight-errant,”","\"나리께서는 편력 기사보다 설교자가 되시는 편이 더 나으실 것 같습니다요.\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“Knights-errant knew and ought to know everything, Sancho,”","\"편력 기사는 모든 것을 알았고, 또 마땅히 알아야 하느니라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “for there were knights-errant in former times as well qualified to deliver a sermon or discourse in the middle of an encampment, as if they had graduated in the University of Paris; whereby we may see that the lance has never blunted the pen, nor the pen the lance.”","\"옛날에는 마치 파리 대학을 졸업하기라도 한 듯, 진영 한가운데서 설교나 강론을 늘어놓을 만한 자질을 갖춘 편력 기사들이 있었느니라. 이로써 우리는 창이 결코 붓을 무디게 한 적이 없고, 붓 또한 창을 무디게 한 적이 없음을 알 수 있느니라.\""]],[["“Well, be it as your worship says,” replied Sancho; “let us be off now and find some place of shelter for the night, and God grant it may be somewhere where there are no blankets, nor blanketeers, nor phantoms, nor enchanted Moors; for if there are, may the devil take the whole concern.”","\"좋습니다요, 나리 말씀대로 하십죠.\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"이제 떠나서 밤을 지낼 만한 안식처를 찾아봅시다요. 부디 하느님께서 그곳이 담요도, 담요질하는 자들도, 유령도, 마법에 걸린 무어인도 없는 어딘가가 되게 해 주시기를. 만일 그런 게 있다면 그 일판 전부를 악마가 가져가 버리라지요.\""]],[["“Ask that of God, my son,” said Don Quixote; “and do thou lead on where thou wilt, for this time I leave our lodging to thy choice; but reach me here thy hand, and feel with thy finger, and find out how many of my teeth and grinders are missing from this right side of the upper jaw, for it is there I feel the pain.”","\"그것은 하느님께 빌거라, 아들아.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그리고 네가 원하는 곳으로 앞장서 가거라. 이번에는 우리 잠자리를 네 선택에 맡기마. 허나 여기 네 손을 좀 내밀어, 손가락으로 더듬어 이 위턱 오른쪽에서 내 이와 어금니가 몇 개나 빠졌는지 알아봐 다오. 통증이 느껴지는 게 바로 거기니까.\""]],[["Sancho put in his fingers, and feeling about asked him, “How many grinders used your worship have on this side?”","산초가 손가락을 넣어 이리저리 더듬어 보며 그에게 물었다. \"이쪽에 어금니가 몇 개나 있으셨습니까요, 나리?\""]],[["“Four,”","\"네 개니라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote, “besides the back-tooth, all whole and quite sound.”","\"맨 뒤 어금니 말고도 말이다. 전부 온전하고 아주 튼튼했지.\""]],[["“Mind what you are saying, señor.”","\"무슨 말씀을 하시는지 잘 생각해 보십쇼, 나리.\""]],[["“I say four, if not five,”","\"네 개라고 했다, 다섯 개가 아니라면 말이다.\""],["answered Don Quixote, “for never in my life have I had tooth or grinder drawn, nor has any fallen out or been destroyed by any decay or rheum.”","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내 평생 이나 어금니를 뽑은 적이 없고, 충치나 진물 때문에 빠지거나 망가진 적도 없으니까.\""]],[["“Well, then,”","\"그렇다면,\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “in this lower side your worship has no more than two grinders and a half, and in the upper neither a half nor any at all, for it is all as smooth as the palm of my hand.”","\"이 아래쪽에 나리께서는 어금니가 두 개 반밖에 없으시고, 위쪽에는 반쪽도 하나도 없습니다요. 죄다 제 손바닥처럼 매끈하니까요.\""]],[["“Luckless that I am!” said Don Quixote, hearing the sad news his squire gave him; “I had rather they despoiled me of an arm, so it were not the sword-arm; for I tell thee, Sancho, a mouth without teeth is like a mill without a millstone, and a tooth is much more to be prized than a diamond; but we who profess the austere order of chivalry are liable to all this. Mount, friend, and lead the way, and I will follow thee at whatever pace thou wilt.”","\"가련한 이 신세로다!\" 종자가 전하는 그 슬픈 소식을 들으며 돈키호테가 말했다. \"차라리 검을 쥐는 팔만 아니라면 한쪽 팔을 빼앗기는 편이 나았을 것을. 산초, 너에게 이르노니, 이가 없는 입은 맷돌 없는 방앗간과 같고, 이 하나는 다이아몬드보다 훨씬 귀히 여겨야 하느니라. 허나 엄혹한 기사도의 길을 천직으로 삼은 우리는 이 모든 것을 다 겪게 마련이니라. 올라타거라, 벗이여, 그리고 앞장서거라. 네가 원하는 어떤 속도로든 내 너를 따르마.\""]],[["Sancho did as he bade him, and proceeded in the direction in which he thought he might find refuge without quitting the high road, which was there very much frequented.","산초는 그가 시킨 대로 하여, 사람들이 무척 많이 다니는 큰길을 벗어나지 않으면서도 안식처를 찾을 만하다고 여겨지는 방향으로 나아갔다."],["As they went along, then, at a slow pace--for the pain in Don Quixote’s jaws kept him uneasy and ill-disposed for speed--Sancho thought it well to amuse and divert him by talk of some kind, and among the things he said to him was that which will be told in the following chapter.","그리하여 그들이 천천히 — 돈키호테는 턱의 통증 때문에 불편하여 빨리 가고 싶은 마음이 없었으므로 — 길을 가는 동안, 산초는 무언가 이야깃거리로 주인을 즐겁게 하고 기분을 풀어 주는 것이 좋겠다고 생각했는데, 그가 그에게 한 이야기들 가운데 하나가 다음 장에서 들려줄 내용이다."]],[["# CHAPTER XIX. OF THE SHREWD DISCOURSE WHICH SANCHO HELD WITH HIS MASTER, AND OF THE ADVENTURE THAT BEFELL HIM WITH A DEAD BODY, TOGETHER WITH OTHER NOTABLE OCCURRENCES","# CHAPTER XIX."]],[["“It seems to me, señor, that all these mishaps that have befallen us of late have been without any doubt a punishment for the offence committed by your worship against the order of chivalry in not keeping the oath you made not to eat bread off a tablecloth or embrace the queen, and all the rest of it that your worship swore to observe until you had taken that helmet of Malandrino’s, or whatever the Moor is called, for I do not very well remember.”","“제 생각엔 나리, 요즘 들어 우리에게 닥친 이 모든 불운은 의심할 여지 없이 나리께서 기사도의 율법을 어기신 죄에 대한 벌인 것 같습니다요. 식탁보 위에 놓인 빵을 먹지 않겠다느니 왕비를 끌어안지 않겠다느니, 그 말란드리노인지 뭔지 하는 무어인의 투구를 손에 넣기 전까지 지키겠다고 맹세하신 그 나머지 것들을 다 어기셨잖습니까. 그 이름이 정확히 기억나진 않습니다만요.”"]],[["“Thou art very right, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “but to tell the truth, it had escaped my memory; and likewise thou mayest rely upon it that the affair of the blanket happened to thee because of thy fault in not reminding me of it in time; but I will make amends, for there are ways of compounding for everything in the order of chivalry.”","“네 말이 아주 옳다, 산초.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “사실대로 말하자면 그게 내 기억에서 빠져나가 버렸구나. 또한 담요 사건이 네게 일어난 것도 제때 그 일을 일깨워 주지 못한 네 잘못 때문이라 여겨도 좋다. 허나 내 바로잡으마. 기사도의 율법 안에는 무엇이든 보상할 방도가 있는 법이니라.”"]],[["“Why!","“아니!"],["have I taken an oath of some sort, then?”","그럼 제가 무슨 맹세라도 한 거란 말씀입니까?”"],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“It makes no matter that thou hast not taken an oath,”","“네가 맹세를 하지 않았다는 건 문제가 되지 않는다.” 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “suffice it that I see thou art not quite clear of complicity; and whether or no, it will not be ill done to provide ourselves with a remedy.”","“네가 그 공모에서 아주 깨끗하지는 않다는 걸 내가 보는 것으로 족하니라. 어느 쪽이든 간에 우리 스스로 대비책을 마련해 두는 것이 나쁘지는 않을 게다.”"]],[["“In that case,”","“그렇다면,” 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “mind that your worship does not forget this as you did the oath; perhaps the phantoms may take it into their heads to amuse themselves once more with me; or even with your worship if they see you so obstinate.”","“나리께서 그 맹세를 잊으셨던 것처럼 이번 일도 잊지 않도록 조심하십시오. 어쩌면 그 유령들이 다시 한번 저를 가지고 노는 데 재미를 붙일지도 모르고, 나리께서 그렇게 고집을 부리시는 걸 보면 나리까지도 그럴지 모릅니다요.”"]],[["While engaged in this and other talk, night overtook them on the road before they had reached or discovered any place of shelter; and what made it still worse was that they were dying of hunger, for with the loss of the alforjas they had lost their entire larder and commissariat; and to complete the misfortune they met with an adventure which without any invention had really the appearance of one. It so happened that the night closed in somewhat darkly, but for all that they pushed on, Sancho feeling sure that as the road was the king’s highway they might reasonably expect to find some inn within a league or two. Going along, then, in this way, the night dark, the squire hungry, the master sharp-set, they saw coming towards them on the road they were travelling a great number of lights which looked exactly like stars in motion. Sancho was taken aback at the sight of them, nor did Don Quixote altogether relish them: the one pulled up his ass by the halter, the other his hack by the bridle, and they stood still, watching anxiously to see what all this would turn out to be, and found that the lights were approaching them, and the nearer they came the greater they seemed, at which spectacle Sancho began to shake like a man dosed with mercury, and Don Quixote’s hair stood on end; he, however, plucking up spirit a little, said:","이런저런 이야기에 빠져 있는 동안, 두 사람은 묵을 곳을 찾거나 발견하기도 전에 길 위에서 밤을 맞고 말았다. 게다가 사정을 더욱 나쁘게 만든 것은 둘이 배고픔으로 죽을 지경이었다는 점이니, 안장주머니를 잃어버리는 바람에 식량 창고와 군량을 통째로 잃은 셈이었다. 그리고 불운을 완성이라도 하려는 듯 그들은 어떤 모험과 마주쳤는데, 그것은 꾸며 낸 구석이 전혀 없으면서도 정말로 모험 같은 모양새를 갖추고 있었다. 마침 밤이 다소 어둑하게 깔렸으나 그럼에도 그들은 앞으로 나아갔으니, 이 길이 왕의 큰길인 만큼 한두 레과 안에 어떤 주막을 찾으리라 기대해도 좋겠다고 산초가 확신했기 때문이다. 그렇게 어두운 밤길을, 종자는 배고프고 주인은 시장한 채 나아가다가, 그들은 자신들이 가던 길 위로 자기들을 향해 다가오는 수많은 불빛을 보았으니, 그것은 영락없이 움직이는 별들처럼 보였다. 산초는 그 광경에 흠칫 놀랐고, 돈키호테 역시 그것이 그리 달갑지만은 않았다. 한 사람은 고삐를 잡아 당나귀를 멈춰 세우고, 다른 한 사람은 굴레를 당겨 자기 말을 멈춰 세운 채, 이 모든 것이 무엇으로 드러날지 마음 졸이며 지켜보고 섰는데, 불빛들이 그들에게 가까이 다가올수록 점점 더 커지는 것이었다. 그 광경에 산초는 수은을 먹은 사람처럼 떨기 시작했고, 돈키호테의 머리카락은 곤두섰다. 그러나 그는 약간이나마 기운을 차리고 말했다."]],[["“This, no doubt, Sancho, will be a most mighty and perilous adventure, in which it will be needful for me to put forth all my valour and resolution.”","“산초야, 이것은 의심할 바 없이 더없이 강력하고 위험천만한 모험일 게다. 내 모든 용맹과 결단을 쏟아부어야 할 모험 말이다.”"]],[["“Unlucky me!”","“아이고, 박복한 신세!”"],["answered Sancho; “if this adventure happens to be one of phantoms, as I am beginning to think it is, where shall I find the ribs to bear it?”","산초가 대답했다. “이 모험이 제가 짐작하기 시작한 대로 유령들의 모험이라면, 그걸 견뎌 낼 갈빗대를 제가 어디서 찾는단 말입니까?”"]],[["“Be they phantoms ever so much,” said Don Quixote, “I will not permit them to touch a thread of thy garments; for if they played tricks with thee the time before, it was because I was unable to leap the walls of the yard; but now we are on a wide plain, where I shall be able to wield my sword as I please.”","“그것들이 제아무리 유령이라 한들,” 돈키호테가 말했다. “네 옷자락 한 올도 건드리지 못하게 하리라. 지난번에 그것들이 네게 장난을 친 것은 내가 마당의 담을 뛰어넘을 수 없었기 때문이다. 허나 지금 우리는 너른 들판에 있으니, 나는 마음껏 검을 휘두를 수 있을 게다.”"]],[["“And if they enchant and cripple you as they did the last time,”","“그러다 지난번처럼 그것들이 나리께 마법을 걸어 꼼짝 못 하게 만들면,”"],["said Sancho, “what difference will it make being on the open plain or not?”","산초가 말했다. “너른 들판에 있고 없고가 무슨 차이가 있단 말입니까?”"]],[["“For all that,”","“그래도,” 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote, “I entreat thee, Sancho, to keep a good heart, for experience will tell thee what mine is.”","“산초야, 부디 마음을 굳게 먹어라. 내가 어떤 사람인지는 겪어 보면 알게 되리라.”"]],[["“I will, please God,”","“그러겠습니다, 하느님이 도우신다면요.” 산초가 대답했다."],["answered Sancho, and the two retiring to one side of the road set themselves to observe closely what all these moving lights might be; and very soon afterwards they made out some twenty encamisados, all on horseback, with lighted torches in their hands, the awe-inspiring aspect of whom completely extinguished the courage of Sancho, who began to chatter with his teeth like one in the cold fit of an ague; and his heart sank and his teeth chattered still more when they perceived distinctly that behind them there came a litter covered over with black and followed by six more mounted figures in mourning down to the very feet of their mules--for they could perceive plainly they were not horses by the easy pace at which they went.","두 사람이 길 한쪽으로 물러나 이 움직이는 불빛들이 대체 무엇일지 유심히 살피기 시작하자, 머지않아 그들은 스무 명쯤 되는 흰옷 입은 자들이 모두 말을 타고 손에 불 켜진 횃불을 든 모습을 알아보게 되었다. 그 위압적인 광경은 산초의 용기를 완전히 꺼뜨려, 그는 학질에 걸려 오한이 든 사람처럼 이를 딱딱 부딪치기 시작했다."],["And as the encamisados came along they muttered to themselves in a low plaintive tone.","그리고 그 뒤로 검은 천을 뒤집어쓴 가마 하나가 따라오고, 그 뒤를 또 노새 발끝까지 닿는 상복을 입은 여섯 명이 말에 탄 모습으로 뒤따르는 것을 똑똑히 알아보았을 때, 그의 가슴은 더욱 내려앉고 이는 더 심하게 딱딱거렸다. 그 짐승들이 가는 편안한 걸음새로 보아 말이 아님을 분명히 알 수 있었던 것이다."],["This strange spectacle at such an hour and in such a solitary place was quite enough to strike terror into Sancho’s heart, and even into his master’s; and (save in Don Quixote’s case) did so, for all Sancho’s resolution had now broken down.","그리고 흰옷 입은 자들은 다가오면서 낮고 구슬픈 어조로 무어라 중얼거렸다. 이런 시각에 이토록 외진 곳에서 벌어진 이 기이한 광경은 산초의, 아니 그 주인의 가슴에까지 공포를 심어 넣기에 충분했고, (돈키호테의 경우를 제외하고는) 실제로 그렇게 했으니, 산초의 결의는 이제 완전히 무너지고 말았다."],["It was just the opposite with his master, whose imagination immediately conjured up all this to him vividly as one of the adventures of his books.","그의 주인은 정반대였으니, 그의 상상력은 즉시 이 모든 것을 자기 책 속 모험들 가운데 하나로 생생하게 떠올려 냈다."]],[["He took it into his head that the litter was a bier on which was borne some sorely wounded or slain knight, to avenge whom was a task reserved for him alone; and without any further reasoning he laid his lance in rest, fixed himself firmly in his saddle, and with gallant spirit and bearing took up his position in the middle of the road where the encamisados must of necessity pass; and as soon as he saw them near at hand he raised his voice and said:","그는 그 가마가 곧 어느 중상을 입었거나 살해된 기사를 실은 상여이며, 그의 원수를 갚는 일은 오직 자기에게만 맡겨진 과업이라고 머릿속에 떠올렸다. 그리고 더 이상 따져 보지도 않은 채 창을 옆구리에 단단히 끼우고 안장에 몸을 굳게 고정하고는, 늠름한 기백과 자태로 흰옷 입은 자들이 반드시 지나갈 수밖에 없는 길 한복판에 자리를 잡았다. 그리고 그들이 가까이 다가온 것을 보자마자 목소리를 높여 말했다."]],[["“Halt, knights, or whosoever ye may be, and render me account of who ye are, whence ye come, where ye go, what it is ye carry upon that bier, for, to judge by appearances, either ye have done some wrong or some wrong has been done to you, and it is fitting and necessary that I should know, either that I may chastise you for the evil ye have done, or else that I may avenge you for the injury that has been inflicted upon you.”","“멈추어라, 기사들이여, 그대들이 누구이든 간에. 그리고 내게 고하라. 그대들이 누구이며, 어디서 와서 어디로 가는지, 저 상여 위에 무엇을 싣고 가는지를. 겉으로 보아 그대들이 어떤 잘못을 저질렀거나, 아니면 그대들에게 어떤 잘못이 저질러진 것이니, 내가 마땅히 또 반드시 알아야 할 일이로다. 그대들이 저지른 악행에 대해 그대들을 응징하기 위해서든, 아니면 그대들에게 가해진 해악에 대해 그대들의 원수를 갚아 주기 위해서든 말이다.”"]],[["“We are in haste,”","“우리는 급한 길이오.”"],["answered one of the encamisados, “and the inn is far off, and we cannot stop to render you such an account as you demand;”","흰옷 입은 자들 가운데 하나가 대답했다. “주막은 멀고, 당신이 요구하는 그런 설명을 늘어놓느라 멈춰 설 수가 없소.”"],["and spurring his mule he moved on.","그러고는 노새에 박차를 가하며 앞으로 나아갔다."]],[["Don Quixote was mightily provoked by this answer, and seizing the mule by the bridle he said, “Halt, and be more mannerly, and render an account of what I have asked of you; else, take my defiance to combat, all of you.”","이 대답에 몹시 화가 치민 돈키호테는 노새의 굴레를 움켜잡으며 말했다. “멈추어라. 좀 더 예의를 갖추어 내가 물은 바를 고하라. 그러지 않으면 그대들 모두에게 결투를 신청하노라.”"]],[["The mule was shy, and was so frightened at her bridle being seized that rearing up she flung her rider to the ground over her haunches.","노새는 겁이 많은 짐승이라, 굴레를 잡히자 어찌나 놀랐던지 앞발을 치켜들며 등 너머로 탄 사람을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐 버렸다. 걸어서 따라오던 시종 하나가 흰옷 입은 자가 떨어지는 것을 보고 돈키호테에게 욕설을 퍼붓기 시작했다."],["An attendant who was on foot, seeing the encamisado fall, began to abuse Don Quixote, who now moved to anger, without any more ado, laying his lance in rest charged one of the men in mourning and brought him badly wounded to the ground, and as he wheeled round upon the others the agility with which he attacked and routed them was a sight to see, for it seemed just as if wings had that instant grown upon Rocinante, so lightly and proudly did he bear himself.","그러자 분노에 휩싸인 돈키호테는 더는 지체하지 않고 창을 옆구리에 끼운 채 상복 입은 자들 가운데 하나에게 돌격하여 그를 심하게 다친 채 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다. 그리고 나머지 사람들을 향해 말을 돌려 공격하고 패주시키는 그 날렵함은 가히 볼만한 광경이었으니, 마치 그 순간 로시난테에게 날개라도 돋친 듯 그토록 가뿐하고 당당하게 몸을 놀렸기 때문이다."],["The encamisados were all timid folk and unarmed, so they speedily made their escape from the fray and set off at a run across the plain with their lighted torches, looking exactly like maskers running on some gala or festival night.","흰옷 입은 자들은 모두 겁 많고 무기도 없는 무리였던지라, 재빨리 난투에서 빠져나와 불 켜진 횃불을 들고 들판을 가로질러 달아나기 시작했으니, 영락없이 어느 잔칫날이나 축제의 밤에 달려가는 가장 행렬 같았다."],["The mourners, too, enveloped and swathed in their skirts and gowns, were unable to bestir themselves, and so with entire safety to himself Don Quixote belaboured them all and drove them off against their will, for they all thought it was no man but a devil from hell come to carry away the dead body they had in the litter.","상복을 입은 자들 역시 치마와 옷자락에 휩싸이고 둘둘 감겨 옴짝달싹할 수가 없었으니, 돈키호테는 아무런 위험 없이 그들 모두를 두들겨 패고 마지못해 달아나게 만들었다. 그들 모두 이것이 사람이 아니라 가마에 실린 시신을 채 가려고 지옥에서 온 악마라고 여겼기 때문이다."]],[["Sancho beheld all this in astonishment at the intrepidity of his lord, and said to himself, “Clearly this master of mine is as bold and valiant as he says he is.”","산초는 자기 주인의 대담함에 놀라 이 모든 것을 지켜보며 속으로 중얼거렸다. “분명 우리 주인 나리는 자기가 말하는 대로 용감하고 용맹하시구나.”"]],[["A burning torch lay on the ground near the first man whom the mule had thrown, by the light of which Don Quixote perceived him, and coming up to him he presented the point of the lance to his face, calling on him to yield himself prisoner, or else he would kill him; to which the prostrate man replied, “I am prisoner enough as it is; I cannot stir, for one of my legs is broken: I entreat you, if you be a Christian gentleman, not to kill me, which will be committing grave sacrilege, for I am a licentiate and I hold first orders.”","노새가 내동댕이친 첫 번째 사람 곁에는 불타는 횃불 하나가 땅에 놓여 있었고, 그 불빛 덕에 돈키호테는 그를 알아보았다. 그에게 다가가 창끝을 그의 얼굴에 들이대며 항복하여 포로가 되라고, 그러지 않으면 죽이겠다고 외쳤다. 이에 땅에 엎어진 사람이 대답했다. “저는 이미 충분히 포로 신세입니다. 다리 하나가 부러져 꼼짝할 수가 없습니다. 청하건대, 당신이 기독교를 믿는 신사라면 저를 죽이지 마십시오. 그것은 중대한 신성 모독을 저지르는 일이 될 것입니다. 저는 학사이며 하급 성직을 받은 몸이니까요.”"]],[["“Then what the devil brought you here, being a churchman?”","“그럼 성직자인 당신이 대체 무슨 빌어먹을 일로 여기까지 온 것이오?”"],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“What, señor?”","“무슨 일이냐고요, 나리?”"],["said the other.","그가 말했다."],["“My bad luck.”","“제 박복한 운수 때문이지요.”"]],[["“Then still worse awaits you,”","“그렇다면 더욱 나쁜 일이 당신을 기다리고 있을 것이오.”"],["said Don Quixote, “if you do not satisfy me as to all I asked you at first.”","돈키호테가 말했다. “내가 처음에 물은 모든 것에 대해 나를 만족시키지 못한다면 말이오.”"]],[["“You shall be soon satisfied,” said the licentiate; “you must know, then, that though just now I said I was a licentiate, I am only a bachelor, and my name is Alonzo Lopez; I am a native of Alcobendas, I come from the city of Baeza with eleven others, priests, the same who fled with the torches, and we are going to the city of Segovia accompanying a dead body which is in that litter, and is that of a gentleman who died in Baeza, where he was interred; and now, as I said, we are taking his bones to their burial-place, which is in Segovia, where he was born.”","“곧 만족하시게 될 겁니다.” 학사가 말했다. “그러니까, 방금 제가 학사라고 말씀드렸지만 사실은 학사 후보생일 뿐이고, 이름은 알론소 로페스입니다. 저는 알코벤다스 태생이고, 다른 열한 사람의 사제들과 함께 바에사 시에서 오는 길입니다. 횃불을 들고 도망친 바로 그 사람들이지요. 저희는 저 가마에 실린 시신을 모시고 세고비아 시로 가는 길인데, 그 시신은 바에사에서 죽어 그곳에 안장되었던 한 신사의 것입니다. 그래서 지금, 말씀드린 대로 저희는 그분의 유골을 그분이 태어나신 세고비아의 매장지로 모셔 가는 중입니다.”"]],[["“And who killed him?”","“그럼 누가 그를 죽였소?”"],["asked Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 물었다."]],[["“God, by means of a malignant fever that took him,”","“하느님께서지요. 그를 덮친 악성 열병을 통해서 말입니다.”"],["answered the bachelor.","학사 후보생이 대답했다."]],[["“In that case,” said Don Quixote, “the Lord has relieved me of the task of avenging his death had any other slain him; but, he who slew him having slain him, there is nothing for it but to be silent, and shrug one’s shoulders; I should do the same were he to slay myself; and I would have your reverence know that I am a knight of La Mancha, Don Quixote by name, and it is my business and calling to roam the world righting wrongs and redressing injuries.”","“그렇다면,” 돈키호테가 말했다. “다른 누가 그를 죽였다면 그 죽음의 원수를 갚는 일이 내 몫이었겠으나, 주님께서 나를 그 과업에서 면해 주셨구려. 허나 그를 죽인 분이 그를 죽이셨으니, 입을 다물고 어깨를 으쓱하는 것 외에는 도리가 없겠소. 설령 그분이 나 자신을 죽이신다 해도 나는 똑같이 할 것이오. 그리고 신부께서 알아 두시기를, 나는 라만차의 기사로 돈키호테라는 이름을 가졌으며, 세상을 떠돌며 그릇된 일을 바로잡고 부당함을 시정하는 것이 나의 업이자 소명이오.”"]],[["“I do not know how that about righting wrongs can be,” said the bachelor, “for from straight you have made me crooked, leaving me with a broken leg that will never see itself straight again all the days of its life; and the injury you have redressed in my case has been to leave me injured in such a way that I shall remain injured for ever; and the height of misadventure it was to fall in with you who go in search of adventures.”","“그릇된 일을 바로잡는다는 게 어떻게 가능한지 저는 모르겠습니다.” 학사 후보생이 말했다. “당신은 멀쩡한 저를 비뚤어지게 만들어, 평생토록 두 번 다시 똑바로 서지 못할 부러진 다리를 남겨 주셨으니까요. 그리고 제 경우에 당신이 시정해 주신 부당함이란 게, 영영 부당한 처지로 남게끔 부당하게 만들어 놓으신 것입니다. 모험을 찾아 떠도는 당신과 마주친 것이야말로 더없는 불운이었습니다.”"]],[["“Things do not all happen in the same way,” answered Don Quixote; “it all came, Sir Bachelor Alonzo Lopez, of your going, as you did, by night, dressed in those surplices, with lighted torches, praying, covered with mourning, so that naturally you looked like something evil and of the other world; and so I could not avoid doing my duty in attacking you, and I should have attacked you even had I known positively that you were the very devils of hell, for such I certainly believed and took you to be.”","“모든 일이 다 똑같은 식으로 일어나는 것은 아니오.” 돈키호테가 대답했다. “알론소 로페스 학사 후보생 양반, 이 모든 것은 당신이 그렇게 밤중에, 그런 중백의를 걸치고, 불 켜진 횃불을 들고, 기도를 올리며, 상복에 휩싸여 다닌 탓이오. 그러니 당신은 자연히 무언가 사악한 것, 저세상의 것처럼 보였단 말이오. 그래서 나는 당신들을 공격하는 내 의무를 피할 수 없었던 것이고, 설령 당신들이 지옥의 바로 그 악마라는 것을 확실히 알았다 해도 나는 당신들을 공격했을 것이오. 사실 나는 분명 당신들을 그런 것이라 믿고 그렇게 여겼으니 말이오.”"]],[["“As my fate has so willed it,”","“제 운명이 그렇게 정해 놓은 것이니,”"],["said the bachelor, “I entreat you, sir knight-errant, whose errand has been such an evil one for me, to help me to get from under this mule that holds one of my legs caught between the stirrup and the saddle.”","학사 후보생이 말했다. “편력 기사님, 당신의 행차가 제게 이토록 사악한 것이 되고 말았습니다만, 부디 등자와 안장 사이에 제 다리 하나를 물고 있는 이 노새 밑에서 저를 빼내 주시기를 청합니다.”"]],[["“I would have talked on till to-morrow,”","“나는 내일까지도 계속 이야기를 늘어놓을 뻔했구려.”"],["said Don Quixote; “how long were you going to wait before telling me of your distress?”","돈키호테가 말했다. “당신의 곤경을 내게 말해 주기까지 대체 얼마나 기다릴 작정이었소?”"]],[["He at once called to Sancho, who, however, had no mind to come, as he was just then engaged in unloading a sumpter mule, well laden with provender, which these worthy gentlemen had brought with them.","그는 곧바로 산초를 불렀으나, 산초는 올 생각이 없었다. 마침 그때 그 점잖은 신사들이 가지고 온, 먹을거리가 잔뜩 실린 짐 노새 한 마리의 짐을 내리느라 바빴기 때문이다."],["Sancho made a bag of his coat, and, getting together as much as he could, and as the bag would hold, he loaded his beast, and then hastened to obey his master’s call, and helped him to remove the bachelor from under the mule; then putting him on her back he gave him the torch, and Don Quixote bade him follow the track of his companions, and beg pardon of them on his part for the wrong which he could not help doing them.","산초는 자기 외투로 자루를 만들어 담을 수 있는 만큼, 자루에 들어갈 만큼 잔뜩 그러모아 자기 짐승에 실은 다음, 서둘러 주인의 부름에 응하여 학사 후보생을 노새 밑에서 빼내는 일을 거들었다. 그러고는 그를 노새 등에 올려 횃불을 건네주었고, 돈키호테는 그에게 일행의 자취를 따라가, 자신이 어쩔 수 없이 그들에게 끼친 잘못에 대해 자기를 대신해 용서를 빌라고 일렀다."]],[["And said Sancho, “If by chance these gentlemen should want to know who was the hero that served them so, your worship may tell them that he is the famous Don Quixote of La Mancha, otherwise called the Knight of the Rueful Countenance.”","그러자 산초가 말했다. “혹시라도 저분들이 자기들을 그렇게 혼내 준 영웅이 누구인지 알고 싶어 한다면, 그분은 라만차의 유명한 돈키호테, 일명 슬픈 몰골의 기사라고 나리께서 일러 주셔도 좋겠습니다.”"]],[["The bachelor then took his departure.","그러고 나서 학사 후보생은 길을 떠났다."]],[["I forgot to mention that before he did so he said to Don Quixote, “Remember that you stand excommunicated for having laid violent hands on a holy thing, juxta illud, si quis, suadente diabolo.”","그가 떠나기 전에 돈키호테에게 이렇게 말했다는 것을 깜빡하고 빠뜨렸다. “당신은 성스러운 것에 폭력의 손을 댔으니 파문당한 몸임을 명심하시오. 그 율법에 이르기를, '누구든 악마의 꾐에 빠져' 운운하였소.”"]],[["“I do not understand that Latin,” answered Don Quixote, “but I know well I did not lay hands, only this pike; besides, I did not think I was committing an assault upon priests or things of the Church, which, like a Catholic and faithful Christian as I am, I respect and revere, but upon phantoms and spectres of the other world; but even so, I remember how it fared with Cid Ruy Diaz when he broke the chair of the ambassador of that king before his Holiness the Pope, who excommunicated him for the same; and yet the good Roderick of Vivar bore himself that day like a very noble and valiant knight.”","* 그 라틴어 원문은 juxta illud, si quis, suadente diabolo로, 교회법에서 성직자에게 폭력을 가한 자를 파문한다는 조항의 첫머리이다. “나는 그 라틴어를 알아듣지 못하오.” 돈키호테가 대답했다. “허나 내가 손을 댄 것이 아니라 오직 이 창을 댔다는 것만은 잘 알고 있소. 게다가 나는 내가 사제들이나 교회의 것들에게 공격을 가하고 있다고는 생각지 않았소. 나는 가톨릭교도이자 충실한 기독교도로서 그런 것들을 존경하고 숭상하니 말이오. 다만 저세상의 유령과 망령들에게 공격을 가하는 것이라 여겼을 뿐이오. 허나 설령 그렇다 해도, 나는 시드 루이 디아스가 교황 성하 앞에서 어느 왕의 사절이 앉은 의자를 부수었을 때 어떤 일을 겪었는지를 떠올리오. 교황은 그 일로 그를 파문하였으나, 그럼에도 비바르의 선량한 로드리고는 그날 더없이 고귀하고 용맹한 기사답게 처신하였소.”"]],[["On hearing this the bachelor took his departure, as has been said, without making any reply; and Don Quixote asked Sancho what had induced him to call him the “Knight of the Rueful Countenance”","이 말을 듣고 학사 후보생은, 앞서 말한 대로 아무런 대꾸도 없이 길을 떠났다."],["more then than at any other time.","그러자 돈키호테는 산초에게, 어찌하여 다른 어느 때도 아닌 지금 자기를 '슬픈 몰골의 기사'라 불렀는지 물었다."]],[["“I will tell you,” answered Sancho; “it was because I have been looking at you for some time by the light of the torch held by that unfortunate, and verily your worship has got of late the most ill-favoured countenance I ever saw: it must be either owing to the fatigue of this combat, or else to the want of teeth and grinders.”","“말씀드리지요.” 산초가 대답했다. “그건 제가 한동안 저 불쌍한 사람이 든 횃불 빛으로 나리를 바라보았기 때문입니다. 정말이지 나리께서는 요즘 들어 제가 여태껏 본 중에 가장 볼품없는 몰골이 되셨거든요. 이 결투의 피로 탓이거나, 아니면 이빨과 어금니가 빠진 탓일 겁니다.”"]],[["“It is not that,” replied Don Quixote, “but because the sage whose duty it will be to write the history of my achievements must have thought it proper that I should take some distinctive name as all knights of yore did; one being ‘He of the Burning Sword,’ another ‘He of the Unicorn,’ this one ‘He of the Damsels,’ that ‘He of the Phoenix,’ another ‘The Knight of the Griffin,’ and another ‘He of the Death,’ and by these names and designations they were known all the world round; and so I say that the sage aforesaid must have put it into your mouth and mind just now to call me ‘The Knight of the Rueful Countenance,’ as I intend to call myself from this day forward; and that the said name may fit me better, I mean, when the opportunity offers, to have a very rueful countenance painted on my shield.”","“그게 아니다.” 돈키호테가 대답했다. “내 위업의 역사를 기록할 임무를 맡은 현자가, 옛 기사들이 모두 그러했듯 내가 어떤 독특한 이름을 가지는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼기 때문이다. 한 사람은 '불타는 검의 기사', 또 한 사람은 '일각수의 기사', 이 사람은 '처녀들의 기사', 저 사람은 '불사조의 기사', 또 한 사람은 '그리핀의 기사', 또 다른 이는 '죽음의 기사'였으니, 이런 이름과 칭호로 그들은 온 세상에 알려졌느니라. 그러니 내 말은, 방금 그 현자가 나를 '슬픈 몰골의 기사'라 부르도록 네 입과 마음에 그것을 불어넣었다는 것이며, 나는 오늘부터 나 자신을 그렇게 부를 작정이다. 그리고 그 이름이 내게 더 잘 어울리도록, 기회가 닿는 대로 내 방패에 더없이 슬픈 몰골 하나를 그려 넣게 할 생각이다.”"]],[["“There is no occasion, señor, for wasting time or money on making that countenance,” said Sancho; “for all that need be done is for your worship to show your own, face to face, to those who look at you, and without anything more, either image or shield, they will call you ‘Him of the Rueful Countenance’ and believe me I am telling you the truth, for I assure you, señor (and in good part be it said), hunger and the loss of your grinders have given you such an ill-favoured face that, as I say, the rueful picture may be very well spared.”","“나리, 그런 몰골을 그리느라 시간이나 돈을 허비하실 필요가 없습니다.” 산초가 말했다. “나리께서 그저 나리 얼굴을 보는 사람들에게 직접, 얼굴을 맞대고 보여 주시기만 하면 됩니다요. 그러면 그림이든 방패든 다른 아무것도 없이도 사람들이 나리를 '슬픈 몰골의 기사'라 부를 테니까요. 제 말을 믿으십시오, 제가 진실을 말씀드리는 겁니다. 분명히 말씀드리지만 나리(좋은 뜻으로 드리는 말씀입니다요), 배고픔과 어금니가 빠진 탓에 나리 얼굴이 어찌나 볼품없어지셨는지, 제가 말씀드린 대로 그 슬픈 그림은 충분히 생략하셔도 좋겠습니다.”"]],[["Don Quixote laughed at Sancho’s pleasantry; nevertheless he resolved to call himself by that name, and have his shield or buckler painted as he had devised.","돈키호테는 산초의 농담에 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그럼에도 그는 자신을 그 이름으로 부르기로, 그리고 자기가 궁리한 대로 방패나 작은 방패에 그림을 그려 넣기로 결심했다."]],[["Don Quixote would have looked to see whether the body in the litter were bones or not, but Sancho would not have it, saying:","돈키호테는 가마에 실린 것이 유골인지 아닌지 살펴보려 했으나, 산초가 한사코 말렸다."]],[["“Señor, you have ended this perilous adventure more safely for yourself than any of those I have seen: perhaps these people, though beaten and routed, may bethink themselves that it is a single man that has beaten them, and feeling sore and ashamed of it may take heart and come in search of us and give us trouble enough.","“나리, 나리께서는 제가 본 어떤 모험보다도 이 위험천만한 모험을 나리 자신에게 더 안전하게 마무리하셨습니다. 어쩌면 저 사람들은 두들겨 맞고 패주했으면서도, 자기들을 때려눕힌 게 단 한 사람이라는 데 생각이 미쳐, 분하고 부끄러워 다시 기운을 차리고 우리를 찾아와 단단히 곤란을 끼칠지도 모릅니다."],["The ass is in proper trim, the mountains are near at hand, hunger presses, we have nothing more to do but make good our retreat, and, as the saying is, the dead to the grave and the living to the loaf.”","당나귀는 만반의 채비가 되어 있고, 산은 가까이 있으며, 배고픔은 다그치니, 우리가 할 일이라곤 무사히 물러나는 것밖에 없습니다. 속담에도 있듯이, 죽은 자는 무덤으로, 산 자는 빵으로 가는 법이지요.”"]],[["And driving his ass before him he begged his master to follow, who, feeling that Sancho was right, did so without replying; and after proceeding some little distance between two hills they found themselves in a wide and retired valley, where they alighted, and Sancho unloaded his beast, and stretched upon the green grass, with hunger for sauce, they breakfasted, dined, lunched, and supped all at once, satisfying their appetites with more than one store of cold meat which the dead man’s clerical gentlemen (who seldom put themselves on short allowance) had brought with them on their sumpter mule. But another piece of ill-luck befell them, which Sancho held the worst of all, and that was that they had no wine to drink, nor even water to moisten their lips; and as thirst tormented them, Sancho, observing that the meadow where they were was full of green and tender grass, said what will be told in the following chapter.","그러고는 당나귀를 앞세워 몰며 주인에게 따라오기를 청했다. 돈키호테는 산초의 말이 옳다고 여겨 아무 대꾸 없이 그를 따랐다. 두 언덕 사이로 얼마쯤 나아간 끝에 그들은 너르고 외진 골짜기에 이르렀고, 거기서 말과 당나귀에서 내렸다. 산초가 짐승의 짐을 내리자, 둘은 배고픔을 양념 삼아 푸른 풀밭에 드러누워 아침과 점심과 새참과 저녁을 한꺼번에 먹어 치웠으니, 죽은 자의 성직자 신사들이(이들은 좀처럼 인색하게 끼니를 줄이는 법이 없었다) 짐 노새에 싣고 온 한 무더기 넘는 식은 고기로 배를 채웠다. 그러나 그들에게 또 다른 불운이 닥쳤으니, 산초가 그 모든 것 중에 가장 고약하다고 여긴 그것은 바로 마실 포도주가 없을뿐더러 입술을 적실 물조차 없다는 것이었다. 갈증이 그들을 괴롭히자, 산초는 자기들이 있는 풀밭에 푸르고 연한 풀이 가득한 것을 보고는, 다음 장에서 이야기될 말을 하였다."]],[["# CHAPTER XX. OF THE UNEXAMPLED AND UNHEARD-OF ADVENTURE WHICH WAS ACHIEVED BY THE VALIANT DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA WITH LESS PERIL THAN ANY EVER ACHIEVED BY ANY FAMOUS KNIGHT IN THE WORLD","# CHAPTER XX."]],[["“It cannot be, señor, but that this grass is a proof that there must be hard by some spring or brook to give it moisture, so it would be well to move a little farther on, that we may find some place where we may quench this terrible thirst that plagues us, which beyond a doubt is more distressing than hunger.”","\"나리, 이 풀이 자라난 걸 보면 틀림없이 근처에 물기를 대 주는 샘이나 시냇물이 있다는 증거가 아니겠습니까요. 그러니 좀 더 멀리 가 보는 것이 좋겠습니다요. 우리를 괴롭히는 이 지독한 갈증을 풀 만한 곳을 찾을 수 있을 테니까요. 갈증은 분명 굶주림보다 더 견디기 힘든 것 아니겠습니까요.\""]],[["The advice seemed good to Don Quixote, and, he leading Rocinante by the bridle and Sancho the ass by the halter, after he had packed away upon him the remains of the supper, they advanced the meadow feeling their way, for the darkness of the night made it impossible to see anything; but they had not gone two hundred paces when a loud noise of water, as if falling from great rocks, struck their ears. The sound cheered them greatly; but halting to make out by listening from what quarter it came they heard unseasonably another noise which spoiled the satisfaction the sound of the water gave them, especially for Sancho, who was by nature timid and faint-hearted. They heard, I say, strokes falling with a measured beat, and a certain rattling of iron and chains that, together with the furious din of the water, would have struck terror into any heart but Don Quixote’s. The night was, as has been said, dark, and they had happened to reach a spot in among some tall trees, whose leaves stirred by a gentle breeze made a low ominous sound; so that, what with the solitude, the place, the darkness, the noise of the water, and the rustling of the leaves, everything inspired awe and dread; more especially as they perceived that the strokes did not cease, nor the wind lull, nor morning approach; to all which might be added their ignorance as to where they were.","그 충고가 돈키호테에게는 그럴듯하게 여겨졌고, 그래서 그는 로시난테의 고삐를 잡고 산초는 저녁 식사의 남은 것들을 잔뜩 실은 당나귀의 굴레를 끌면서, 손으로 더듬어 가며 풀밭을 나아갔다. 밤이 어두워 아무것도 분간할 수 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 이백 보도 채 가지 않았을 때, 마치 커다란 바위에서 떨어지는 듯한 요란한 물소리가 그들의 귀를 때렸다. 그 소리에 두 사람은 크게 기운을 얻었다. 그러나 어느 쪽에서 들려오는지 귀 기울여 가늠하려고 걸음을 멈추었을 때, 때마침 또 다른 소리가 들려와 물소리가 안겨 준 만족감을 망쳐 버렸으니, 특히 천성이 겁 많고 소심한 산초에게는 더욱 그러했다. 다시 말하건대 그들은 일정한 박자로 떨어지는 타격 소리와, 쇠붙이와 사슬이 부딪치는 듯한 어떤 절그럭거리는 소리를 들었는데, 이는 사납게 휘몰아치는 물소리와 어우러져 돈키호테가 아닌 그 누구의 가슴에라도 공포를 심어 놓을 만한 것이었다. 앞서 말했듯이 밤은 어두웠고, 그들은 마침 키 큰 나무들 사이의 어느 지점에 이르러 있었는데, 그 잎사귀들이 부드러운 산들바람에 흔들리며 나직하고 불길한 소리를 내고 있었다. 그리하여 적막함이며 장소며 어둠이며 물소리며 잎사귀의 바스락거림이며, 그 모든 것이 경외감과 두려움을 불러일으켰다. 더욱이 타격 소리가 멎지 않고, 바람도 잦아들지 않으며, 아침도 다가오지 않음을 알아차렸을 때는 더더욱 그러했다. 게다가 자기들이 어디에 있는지조차 모른다는 사실까지 더해질 수 있었다."]],[["But Don Quixote, supported by his intrepid heart, leaped on Rocinante, and bracing his buckler on his arm, brought his pike to the slope, and said, “Friend Sancho, know that I by Heaven’s will have been born in this our iron age to revive revive in it the age of gold, or the golden as it is called; I am he for whom perils, mighty achievements, and valiant deeds are reserved; I am, I say again, he who is to revive the Knights of the Round Table, the Twelve of France and the Nine Worthies; and he who is to consign to oblivion the Platirs, the Tablantes, the Olivantes and Tirantes, the Phoebuses and Belianises, with the whole herd of famous knights-errant of days gone by, performing in these in which I live such exploits, marvels, and feats of arms as shall obscure their brightest deeds. Thou dost mark well, faithful and trusty squire, the gloom of this night, its strange silence, the dull confused murmur of those trees, the awful sound of that water in quest of which we came, that seems as though it were precipitating and dashing itself down from the lofty mountains of the Moon, and that incessant hammering that wounds and pains our ears; which things all together and each of itself are enough to instil fear, dread, and dismay into the breast of Mars himself, much more into one not used to hazards and adventures of the kind. Well, then, all this that I put before thee is but an incentive and stimulant to my spirit, making my heart burst in my bosom through eagerness to engage in this adventure, arduous as it promises to be; therefore tighten Rocinante’s girths a little, and God be with thee; wait for me here three days and no more, and if in that time I come not back, thou canst return to our village, and thence, to do me a favour and a service, thou wilt go to El Toboso, where thou shalt say to my incomparable lady Dulcinea that her captive knight hath died in attempting things that might make him worthy of being called hers.”","그러나 돈키호테는 그 담대한 가슴에 힘입어 로시난테 위로 뛰어올라, 방패를 팔에 단단히 끼고 창을 비스듬히 겨누며 말했다. \"산초여, 알아 두어라. 나는 하늘의 뜻으로 이 무쇠의 시대에 태어나, 그 안에서 황금의 시대를, 흔히 말하는 그 황금기를 되살리려 하노라. 위험과 위대한 업적과 용맹한 행적이 마련되어 있는 자가 바로 나이니라. 다시 말하노니, 원탁의 기사들과 프랑스의 열두 기사와 아홉 위인을 되살릴 자가 바로 나이며, 플라티르며 타블란테며 올리반테며 티란테며 페보며 벨리아니스며, 지난날 이름 높던 편력 기사의 무리 전부를 망각 속으로 보내 버릴 자가 바로 나이니라. 내가 사는 이 시대에 그들의 가장 찬란한 행적조차 무색하게 만들 위업과 경이와 무용을 펼치면서 말이다. 충실하고 미더운 종자여, 그대는 이 밤의 음울함과 그 기이한 정적, 저 나무들의 둔탁하고 어수선한 웅성거림, 우리가 찾아 나선 저 물의 무시무시한 소리를 잘 새겨 두라. 그 소리는 마치 달의 높은 산봉우리에서 곤두박질치며 쏟아져 내리는 듯하구나. 그리고 우리 귀를 찌르고 아프게 하는 저 끊임없는 망치질도 마찬가지이니, 이 모든 것이 함께든 따로든 군신 마르스 자신의 가슴에라도 두려움과 공포와 경악을 심어 넣기에 충분하거늘, 하물며 이런 위험과 모험에 익숙지 못한 자에게야 오죽하겠느냐. 그런데 내가 그대 앞에 늘어놓은 이 모든 것이 내 영혼에는 오히려 자극이요 격려가 되어, 험난하기 그지없으리라 짐작되는 이 모험에 뛰어들고 싶은 열망으로 가슴이 터질 듯하구나. 그러니 로시난테의 뱃대끈을 조금 조여 다오. 그리고 신이 그대와 함께하시기를. 여기서 사흘만, 그 이상은 말고 나를 기다려라. 그동안 내가 돌아오지 않거든 그대는 우리 마을로 돌아가도 좋으니, 그곳에서 나를 위해 호의이자 봉사로 엘 토보소로 가서, 비할 데 없는 나의 귀부인 둘시네아께 그분의 포로된 기사가 그분의 것이라 불릴 만한 자격을 얻고자 시도하다가 죽었노라 전해 다오.\""]],[["When Sancho heard his master’s words he began to weep in the most pathetic way, saying:","산초는 주인의 말을 듣고 더없이 애처롭게 울며 말하기 시작했다."]],[["“Señor, I know not why your worship wants to attempt this so dreadful adventure; it is night now, no one sees us here, we can easily turn about and take ourselves out of danger, even if we don’t drink for three days to come; and as there is no one to see us, all the less will there be anyone to set us down as cowards; besides, I have many a time heard the curate of our village, whom your worship knows well, preach that he who seeks danger perishes in it; so it is not right to tempt God by trying so tremendous a feat from which there can be no escape save by a miracle, and Heaven has performed enough of them for your worship in delivering you from being blanketed as I was, and bringing you out victorious and safe and sound from among all those enemies that were with the dead man; and if all this does not move or soften that hard heart, let this thought and reflection move it, that you will have hardly quitted this spot when from pure fear I shall yield my soul up to anyone that will take it. I left home and wife and children to come and serve your worship, trusting to do better and not worse; but as covetousness bursts the bag, it has rent my hopes asunder, for just as I had them highest about getting that wretched unlucky island your worship has so often promised me, I see that instead and in lieu of it you mean to desert me now in a place so far from human reach: for God’s sake, master mine, deal not so unjustly by me, and if your worship will not entirely give up attempting this feat, at least put it off till morning, for by what the lore I learned when I was a shepherd tells me it cannot want three hours of dawn now, because the mouth of the Horn is overhead and makes midnight in the line of the left arm.”","\"나리, 어찌하여 나리께서는 이토록 끔찍한 모험을 해 보려 하시는지 저는 도무지 모르겠습니다요. 지금은 밤이라 여기서 우리를 보는 사람도 없으니, 앞으로 사흘 동안 물을 마시지 못한다 하더라도 손쉽게 발길을 돌려 위험에서 빠져나갈 수 있지 않습니까요. 우리를 보는 이가 아무도 없으니 우리를 겁쟁이라 흉볼 사람은 더더욱 없을 테고요. 게다가 나리께서도 잘 아시는 우리 마을 신부님께서, 위험을 찾는 자는 그 안에서 죽는다고 설교하시는 걸 저는 여러 번 들었습니다요. 그러니 기적 말고는 빠져나올 길이 없는 이런 엄청난 일을 시도해서 하느님을 시험하는 것은 옳지 않습니다요. 하느님께서는 이미 나리를 위해 충분히 기적을 행하셨지 않습니까요. 저처럼 담요에 키질당하지 않도록 나리를 구해 주셨고, 그 시체와 함께 있던 그 모든 적들 가운데서 나리를 승리자로, 안전하고 멀쩡하게 빼내 주셨으니까요. 그래도 이 모든 것이 그 단단한 마음을 움직이거나 누그러뜨리지 못한다면, 부디 이 생각이라도 그 마음을 움직이게 하십시오. 나리께서 이 자리를 떠나시기가 무섭게, 저는 순전한 두려움에 제 영혼을 가져갈 자 누구에게든 내어 줄 것이라는 생각 말입니다요. 저는 더 잘되어 보려고, 결코 더 못되려고가 아니라 나리를 모시려고 집과 처자식을 버리고 떠나왔습니다요. 그러나 욕심이 자루를 터뜨린다더니, 그 욕심이 제 희망을 갈가리 찢어 놓았습니다요. 나리께서 그토록 자주 약속하신 그 비참하고 운 없는 섬을 얻으리라는 기대가 한껏 부풀어 올랐던 바로 그때, 그 대신, 그것 대신에 나리께서는 사람의 손길이 미치지 못하는 이런 외딴곳에 저를 버리려 하시지 않습니까요. 제발, 나리, 저에게 이토록 부당하게 대하지 마십시오. 정 이 일을 시도하기를 완전히 그만두지 못하시겠거든, 적어도 아침까지만이라도 미루십시오. 제가 양치기 시절에 배운 지식에 따르면 이제 동틀 때까지 세 시간도 채 남지 않았으니까요. 작은곰자리의 입이 머리 위에 떠 있고 왼팔 쪽 선에서 한밤중을 가리키고 있거든요.\""]],[["“How canst thou see, Sancho,”","\"산초야,\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “where it makes that line, or where this mouth or this occiput is that thou talkest of, when the night is so dark that there is not a star to be seen in the whole heaven?”","\"온 하늘에 별 하나 보이지 않을 만큼 밤이 어두운데, 그것이 어디서 그 선을 긋는지, 또 네가 말하는 그 입이며 뒤통수가 어디 있는지 어떻게 볼 수 있단 말이냐?\""]],[["“That’s true,”","\"그건 그렇습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “but fear has sharp eyes, and sees things underground, much more above in heavens; besides, there is good reason to show that it now wants but little of day.”","\"하지만 두려움은 눈이 밝아서 땅속도 보거늘, 하물며 하늘 위야 더 잘 보지요. 게다가 이제 날이 밝을 때가 얼마 남지 않았음을 보여 줄 그럴듯한 까닭도 있고요.\""]],[["“Let it want what it may,” replied Don Quixote, “it shall not be said of me now or at any time that tears or entreaties turned me aside from doing what was in accordance with knightly usage; and so I beg of thee, Sancho, to hold thy peace, for God, who has put it into my heart to undertake now this so unexampled and terrible adventure, will take care to watch over my safety and console thy sorrow; what thou hast to do is to tighten Rocinante’s girths well, and wait here, for I shall come back shortly, alive or dead.”","\"얼마가 남았든 상관없다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"지금이든 언제든, 눈물이나 간청 때문에 내가 기사도의 관습에 맞는 일을 하기를 그만두었다는 말을 듣게 되지는 않을 것이다. 그러니 산초여, 청하노니 입을 다물라. 이토록 유례없고 무시무시한 모험을 이제 떠맡으라고 내 마음에 그것을 넣어 주신 하느님께서, 내 안전을 살피시고 그대의 슬픔을 달래 주시리라. 그대가 해야 할 일은 로시난테의 뱃대끈을 잘 조이고 여기서 기다리는 것뿐이니, 나는 살아서든 죽어서든 곧 돌아올 것이다.\""]],[["Sancho perceiving it his master’s final resolve, and how little his tears, counsels, and entreaties prevailed with him, determined to have recourse to his own ingenuity and compel him, if he could, to wait till daylight; and so, while tightening the girths of the horse, he quietly and without being felt, with his ass’ halter tied both Rocinante’s legs, so that when Don Quixote strove to go he was unable as the horse could only move by jumps.","산초는 주인의 결심이 확고하며, 자기 눈물과 충고와 간청이 그에게 거의 통하지 않음을 알아차리고는, 자기 나름의 꾀에 기대어 어떻게든 그를 날이 밝을 때까지 기다리게 만들기로 작정했다. 그리하여 말의 뱃대끈을 조이는 척하면서, 슬그머니 들키지 않게 당나귀의 굴레로 로시난테의 두 다리를 묶어 버렸다."],["Seeing the success of his trick, Sancho Panza said:","그래서 돈키호테가 가려고 애를 써도 갈 수가 없었으니, 말은 그저 껑충껑충 뛰는 것밖에 할 수 없었다. 자기 꾀가 들어맞은 것을 보고 산초 판사가 말했다."]],[["“See there, señor!","\"보십시오, 나리!"],["Heaven, moved by my tears and prayers, has so ordered it that Rocinante cannot stir; and if you will be obstinate, and spur and strike him, you will only provoke fortune, and kick, as they say, against the pricks.”","제 눈물과 기도에 마음이 움직이신 하늘이, 로시난테가 꼼짝 못하도록 그렇게 정해 놓으셨습니다요. 그러니 고집을 부려 박차를 가하고 때리신다면, 그것은 운명을 거스르는 것이요, 흔히 말하듯 가시를 향해 발길질하는 격이 될 뿐입니다요.\""]],[["Don Quixote at this grew desperate, but the more he drove his heels into the horse, the less he stirred him; and not having any suspicion of the tying, he was fain to resign himself and wait till daybreak or until Rocinante could move, firmly persuaded that all this came of something other than Sancho’s ingenuity. So he said to him, “As it is so, Sancho, and as Rocinante cannot move, I am content to wait till dawn smiles upon us, even though I weep while it delays its coming.”","이에 돈키호테는 절망에 빠졌으나, 말에 발뒤꿈치를 박으면 박을수록 말은 더더욱 움직이지 않았다. 다리가 묶인 것을 전혀 의심하지 못한 그는, 날이 밝거나 로시난테가 움직일 수 있게 될 때까지 체념하고 기다리는 수밖에 없었으니, 이 모든 것이 산초의 꾀가 아닌 다른 무언가에서 비롯되었다고 굳게 믿었던 것이다. 그래서 그가 산초에게 말했다. \"사정이 이러하고 로시난테가 움직일 수 없으니, 산초여, 나는 새벽이 우리에게 미소 지을 때까지 기다리는 것으로 만족하겠다. 비록 그것이 오기를 더디 끄는 동안 내가 눈물을 흘릴지라도 말이다.\""]],[["“There is no need to weep,” answered Sancho, “for I will amuse your worship by telling stories from this till daylight, unless indeed you like to dismount and lie down to sleep a little on the green grass after the fashion of knights-errant, so as to be fresher when day comes and the moment arrives for attempting this extraordinary adventure you are looking forward to.”","\"우실 필요 없습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"지금부터 날이 밝을 때까지 제가 이야기를 들려드려 나리를 즐겁게 해 드릴 테니까요. 정 그러시다면 편력 기사들의 풍습대로 말에서 내려 푸른 풀밭 위에 누워 잠을 좀 청하시는 것도 좋겠습니다요. 그래야 날이 밝고 나리께서 그토록 고대하시는 이 비범한 모험을 시도할 순간이 왔을 때 더 생기 있으실 테니까요.\""]],[["“What art thou talking about dismounting or sleeping for?”","\"내려서 자라니, 그게 무슨 소리냐?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."],["“Am I, thinkest thou, one of those knights that take their rest in the presence of danger?","\"내가 위험 앞에서 휴식을 취하는 그런 기사들 가운데 하나라고 너는 생각하느냐? 잠은 자도록 태어난 너나 자거라."],["Sleep thou who art born to sleep, or do as thou wilt, for I will act as I think most consistent with my character.”","아니면 네 좋을 대로 하든지. 나는 내 본분에 가장 어울린다고 여기는 대로 할 것이니.\""]],[["“Be not angry, master mine,” replied Sancho, “I did not mean to say that;” and coming close to him he laid one hand on the pommel of the saddle and the other on the cantle so that he held his master’s left thigh in his embrace, not daring to separate a finger’s width from him; so much afraid was he of the strokes which still resounded with a regular beat. Don Quixote bade him tell some story to amuse him as he had proposed, to which Sancho replied that he would if his dread of what he heard would let him; “Still,” said he, “I will strive to tell a story which, if I can manage to relate it, and nobody interferes with the telling, is the best of stories, and let your worship give me your attention, for here I begin. What was, was; and may the good that is to come be for all, and the evil for him who goes to look for it--your worship must know that the beginning the old folk used to put to their tales was not just as each one pleased; it was a maxim of Cato Zonzorino the Roman, that says ‘the evil for him that goes to look for it,’ and it comes as pat to the purpose now as ring to finger, to show that your worship should keep quiet and not go looking for evil in any quarter, and that we should go back by some other road, since nobody forces us to follow this in which so many terrors affright us.”","\"화내지 마십시오, 나리.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"그런 뜻으로 드린 말씀이 아닙니다요.\" 그러고는 주인 곁으로 바짝 다가가 한 손은 안장 앞머리에, 다른 한 손은 안장 뒷머리에 얹어, 주인의 왼쪽 허벅지를 끌어안듯 붙들고는 감히 손가락 한 마디만큼도 떨어지려 하지 않았다. 여전히 규칙적인 박자로 울려 퍼지는 그 타격 소리가 그토록 두려웠던 것이다. 돈키호테가 아까 말한 대로 자기를 즐겁게 해 줄 이야기를 하라고 하자, 산초는 자기가 듣고 있는 소리에 대한 두려움이 허락한다면 그러겠노라 대답하고는 말했다. \"하지만 이야기 하나를 들려드리려 애써 보겠습니다요. 제가 제대로 풀어낼 수만 있고 아무도 그 이야기에 끼어들지만 않는다면, 그것은 둘도 없이 훌륭한 이야기입니다요. 그러니 나리께서는 귀를 기울여 주십시오. 자, 시작합니다요. 있던 일은 있던 일이고, 앞으로 올 좋은 일은 모두에게, 나쁜 일은 그것을 찾아 나서는 자에게 가기를……. 나리께서도 아셔야 할 것이, 옛 어른들이 이야기를 시작할 때 붙이던 그 말은 그저 제멋대로 갖다 붙인 게 아니었습니다요. '나쁜 일은 그것을 찾아 나서는 자에게'라고 한 그 말은 로마 사람 카토 손소리노의 격언인데, 지금 이 경우에 손가락에 반지가 꼭 맞듯 딱 들어맞는 말입니다요. 곧 나리께서는 가만히 계셔야 하고 어느 쪽으로도 나쁜 일을 찾아 나서지 말아야 하며, 아무도 우리에게 그토록 많은 공포가 우리를 떨게 하는 이 길을 가라고 강요하지 않으니 우리는 다른 길로 돌아가야 한다는 뜻이지요.\""]],[["“Go on with thy story, Sancho,”","\"네 이야기나 계속해라, 산초.\""],["said Don Quixote, “and leave the choice of our road to my care.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"어느 길로 갈지는 내가 알아서 할 일이니.\""]],[["“I say then,” continued Sancho, “that in a village of Estremadura there was a goat-shepherd--that is to say, one who tended goats--which shepherd or goatherd, as my story goes, was called Lope Ruiz, and this Lope Ruiz was in love with a shepherdess called Torralva, which shepherdess called Torralva was the daughter of a rich grazier, and this rich grazier—”","\"그럼 말씀드리지요.\" 산초가 말을 이었다. \"에스트레마두라의 어느 마을에 염소 치는 목동이, 그러니까 염소를 돌보는 자가 하나 있었습니다요. 제 이야기에 따르면 이 목동, 곧 염소지기는 로페 루이스라 불렸는데, 이 로페 루이스는 토랄바라는 양치기 처녀를 사랑하고 있었습죠. 토랄바라는 이 양치기 처녀는 어느 부유한 목축업자의 딸이었는데, 이 부유한 목축업자가——\""]],[["“If that is the way thou tellest thy tale, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “repeating twice all thou hast to say, thou wilt not have done these two days; go straight on with it, and tell it like a reasonable man, or else say nothing.”","\"산초야, 네가 그런 식으로, 할 말마다 두 번씩 되풀이하며 이야기를 한다면,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이틀이 지나도 끝내지 못할 것이다. 곧장 이야기하라. 분별 있는 사람답게 들려주든지, 아니면 아예 입을 다물든지 해라.\""]],[["“Tales are always told in my country in the very way I am telling this,”","\"제 고장에서는 이야기를 늘 제가 지금 하는 바로 이런 식으로 합니다요.\""],["answered Sancho, “and I cannot tell it in any other, nor is it right of your worship to ask me to make new customs.”","산초가 대답했다. \"저는 다른 식으로는 할 줄을 모르고, 새로운 풍습을 만들라고 하시는 것은 나리께서 옳지 못한 일입니다요.\""]],[["“Tell it as thou wilt,”","\"네 마음대로 해라.\""],["replied Don Quixote; “and as fate will have it that I cannot help listening to thee, go on.”","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"운명이 그러하여 내가 너를 듣지 않을 수 없으니, 계속하라.\""]],[["“And so, lord of my soul,” continued Sancho, as I have said, this shepherd was in love with Torralva the shepherdess, who was a wild buxom lass with something of the look of a man about her, for she had little moustaches; I fancy I see her now.”","\"그래서 말입니다요, 제 영혼의 주인이시여,\" 산초가 말을 이었다. \"제가 말씀드린 대로 이 목동은 양치기 처녀 토랄바를 사랑하고 있었습죠. 그 처녀는 사내 같은 데가 좀 있는 거칠고 통통한 아가씨였는데, 자그마한 콧수염까지 나 있었지요. 지금도 눈에 선합니다요.\""]],[["“Then you knew her?”","\"그럼 자네가 그 여자를 알았단 말인가?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“I did not know her,”","\"제가 그 여자를 안 건 아닙니다요.\""],["said Sancho, “but he who told me the story said it was so true and certain that when I told it to another I might safely declare and swear I had seen it all myself.","산초가 말했다. \"하지만 제게 이 이야기를 들려준 사람이, 이것이 너무도 진실하고 확실하니 제가 남에게 이 이야기를 들려줄 때 마치 제 눈으로 다 본 것처럼 마음 놓고 단언하고 맹세해도 좋다고 했습죠."],["And so in course of time, the devil, who never sleeps and puts everything in confusion, contrived that the love the shepherd bore the shepherdess turned into hatred and ill-will, and the reason, according to evil tongues, was some little jealousy she caused him that crossed the line and trespassed on forbidden ground; and so much did the shepherd hate her from that time forward that, in order to escape from her, he determined to quit the country and go where he should never set eyes on her again.","그리하여 세월이 흐르는 동안, 결코 잠들지 않으며 만사를 어지럽히는 악마가 농간을 부려, 목동이 양치기 처녀에게 품었던 사랑이 미움과 악감정으로 변해 버렸습니다요. 험담꾼들 말에 따르면 그 까닭은, 그 처녀가 도를 넘어 금지된 영역을 침범하여 그에게 약간의 질투를 불러일으킨 데 있었다지요."],["Torralva, when she found herself spurned by Lope, was immediately smitten with love for him, though she had never loved him before.”","그때부터 목동이 그 처녀를 얼마나 미워했던지, 그 여자에게서 벗어나려고 고향을 떠나 다시는 그 여자를 두 번 다시 보지 않을 곳으로 가기로 작정했습니다요. 토랄바는 로페에게 버림받았음을 알게 되자, 전에는 한 번도 그를 사랑한 적이 없었으면서 곧바로 그에 대한 사랑에 사로잡혔습죠.\""]],[["“That is the natural way of women,”","\"그것이 여자들의 타고난 방식이지.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “to scorn the one that loves them, and love the one that hates them: go on, Sancho.”","\"자기를 사랑하는 자는 멸시하고, 자기를 미워하는 자는 사랑하니 말이다. 계속하라, 산초.\""]],[["“It came to pass,” said Sancho, “that the shepherd carried out his intention, and driving his goats before him took his way across the plains of Estremadura to pass over into the Kingdom of Portugal. Torralva, who knew of it, went after him, and on foot and barefoot followed him at a distance, with a pilgrim’s staff in her hand and a scrip round her neck, in which she carried, it is said, a bit of looking-glass and a piece of a comb and some little pot or other of paint for her face; but let her carry what she did, I am not going to trouble myself to prove it; all I say is, that the shepherd, they say, came with his flock to cross over the river Guadiana, which was at that time swollen and almost overflowing its banks, and at the spot he came to there was neither ferry nor boat nor anyone to carry him or his flock to the other side, at which he was much vexed, for he perceived that Torralva was approaching and would give him great annoyance with her tears and entreaties; however, he went looking about so closely that he discovered a fisherman who had alongside of him a boat so small that it could only hold one person and one goat; but for all that he spoke to him and agreed with him to carry himself and his three hundred goats across. The fisherman got into the boat and carried one goat over; he came back and carried another over; he came back again, and again brought over another--let your worship keep count of the goats the fisherman is taking across, for if one escapes the memory there will be an end of the story, and it will be impossible to tell another word of it. To proceed, I must tell you the landing place on the other side was miry and slippery, and the fisherman lost a great deal of time in going and coming; still he returned for another goat, and another, and another.”","\"일이 이렇게 되었습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"목동이 자기 뜻을 실행에 옮겨, 염소들을 앞세워 몰고 포르투갈 왕국으로 넘어가려고 에스트레마두라 평원을 가로질러 길을 떠났습죠. 그 사실을 알게 된 토랄바는 그의 뒤를 쫓아, 맨발로 걸어서 멀찍이 그를 따라갔는데, 손에는 순례자의 지팡이를 들고 목에는 바랑을 둘렀습니다요. 그 안에는 거울 조각 하나와 빗 조각 하나, 그리고 얼굴에 바를 무슨 화장품 단지 같은 것을 넣어 가지고 다녔다지요. 하지만 그 여자가 무엇을 가지고 다녔든, 저는 그걸 증명하려고 애쓸 생각은 없습니다요. 제가 말씀드리려는 건 다만, 사람들 말로 그 목동이 가축 떼를 몰고 과디아나강을 건너러 왔는데, 그 강은 그때 물이 불어나 둑을 거의 넘칠 지경이었다는 겁니다요. 그가 다다른 그 자리에는 나룻배도 배도, 그와 그의 가축 떼를 건너편으로 실어다 줄 사람도 없었으니, 그는 몹시 속이 상했습죠. 토랄바가 다가오고 있으며 눈물과 간청으로 자기를 몹시 성가시게 하리라는 것을 알아차렸기 때문입니다요. 하지만 그는 어찌나 샅샅이 살폈는지, 마침 곁에 배를 댄 어부 한 사람을 찾아냈습니다요. 그 배는 어찌나 작은지 사람 하나에 염소 한 마리밖에 태울 수 없었지요. 그래도 그는 어부에게 말을 걸어, 자기와 자기 염소 삼백 마리를 건너편으로 실어다 주기로 합의를 보았습니다요. 어부는 배에 올라 염소 한 마리를 건너편으로 실어다 주었고, 돌아와서 또 한 마리를 실어다 주었고, 다시 돌아와서 또 한 마리를 실어다 주었습죠——나리, 어부가 건네는 염소 수를 잘 세고 계셔야 합니다요. 한 마리라도 기억에서 빠져나가면 이야기가 끝나 버려서 더 이상 한마디도 이어 갈 수 없게 되니까요. 계속하자면, 건너편 나루터가 진흙투성이에 미끄러워서 어부가 오가는 데 시간을 무척 많이 허비했다는 말씀을 드려야겠습니다요. 그래도 그는 또 한 마리를, 또 한 마리를, 또 한 마리를 실으러 돌아왔지요.\""]],[["“Take it for granted he brought them all across,”","\"염소를 전부 다 건넸다고 치자.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “and don’t keep going and coming in this way, or thou wilt not make an end of bringing them over this twelvemonth.”","\"그렇게 오락가락하지 마라. 안 그러면 일 년이 지나도 다 건너게 하지 못할 테니.\""]],[["“How many have gone across so far?”","\"지금까지 몇 마리나 건넜습니까요?\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“How the devil do I know?”","\"내가 그걸 어찌 안단 말이냐?\""],["replied Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 대답했다."]],[["“There it is,” said Sancho, “what I told you, that you must keep a good count; well then, by God, there is an end of the story, for there is no going any farther.”","\"바로 그겁니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"제가 잘 세고 계셔야 한다고 말씀드리지 않았습니까요. 그러니 정말이지, 이야기는 끝났습니다요. 더는 이어 갈 수가 없으니까요.\""]],[["“How can that be?”","\"어찌 그럴 수가 있느냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “is it so essential to the story to know to a nicety the goats that have crossed over, that if there be a mistake of one in the reckoning, thou canst not go on with it?”","\"건너간 염소 수를 정확히 아는 것이 이야기에 그토록 긴요하단 말이냐? 셈에 한 마리라도 틀리면 이야기를 이어 갈 수 없다는 게냐?\""]],[["“No, señor, not a bit,”","\"아닙니다요, 나리, 천만에요.\" 산초가 대답했다."],["replied Sancho; “for when I asked your worship to tell me how many goats had crossed, and you answered you did not know, at that very instant all I had to say passed away out of my memory, and, faith, there was much virtue in it, and entertainment.”","\"제가 나리께 염소가 몇 마리나 건넜는지 여쭈었을 때 나리께서 모른다고 대답하시는 바로 그 순간, 제가 할 말이 죄다 머릿속에서 사라져 버렸습니다요. 정말이지, 그 안에는 미덕도 많고 재미도 많았는데요.\""]],[["“So, then,”","\"그러면,\""],["said Don Quixote, “the story has come to an end?”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"이야기가 끝나 버렸단 말이냐?\""]],[["“As much as my mother has,”","\"제 어머니가 끝나신 것만큼이나 끝났습니다요.\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“In truth,” said Don Quixote, “thou hast told one of the rarest stories, tales, or histories, that anyone in the world could have imagined, and such a way of telling it and ending it was never seen nor will be in a lifetime; though I expected nothing else from thy excellent understanding. But I do not wonder, for perhaps those ceaseless strokes may have confused thy wits.”","\"참으로,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"너는 이 세상 누구도 상상해 낼 수 없을 만큼 진기한 이야기, 옛날이야기, 혹은 사화(史話) 가운데 하나를 들려주었구나. 그리고 그것을 풀어내고 끝맺는 그런 방식은 평생토록 본 적도 없고 앞으로도 보지 못할 것이다. 하기야 너의 뛰어난 분별력에서 다른 것을 기대하지도 않았다만. 그래도 놀랍지는 않구나. 어쩌면 저 끊임없는 타격 소리가 네 정신을 어지럽혔는지도 모르니.\""]],[["“All that may be,”","\"다 그럴지도 모르지요.\""],["replied Sancho, “but I know that as to my story, all that can be said is that it ends there where the mistake in the count of the passage of the goats begins.”","산초가 대답했다. \"하지만 제 이야기로 말하자면, 염소 건너는 셈의 착오가 시작되는 바로 거기서 이야기도 끝난다는 것밖에는 더 할 말이 없다는 걸 저는 압니다요.\""]],[["“Let it end where it will, well and good,”","\"어디서 끝나든 좋다, 그만하면 됐다.\""],["said Don Quixote, “and let us see if Rocinante can go;”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그럼 로시난테가 갈 수 있는지 보자.\""],["and again he spurred him, and again Rocinante made jumps and remained where he was, so well tied was he.","그러고는 다시 박차를 가했으나, 로시난테는 또 껑충 뛸 뿐 제자리에 머물러 있었으니, 그만큼 단단히 묶여 있었던 것이다."]],[["Just then, whether it was the cold of the morning that was now approaching, or that he had eaten something laxative at supper, or that it was only natural (as is most likely), Sancho felt a desire to do what no one could do for him; but so great was the fear that had penetrated his heart, he dared not separate himself from his master by as much as the black of his nail; to escape doing what he wanted was, however, also impossible; so what he did for peace’s sake was to remove his right hand, which held the back of the saddle, and with it to untie gently and silently the running string which alone held up his breeches, so that on loosening it they at once fell down round his feet like fetters; he then raised his shirt as well as he could and bared his hind quarters, no slim ones. But, this accomplished, which he fancied was all he had to do to get out of this terrible strait and embarrassment, another still greater difficulty presented itself, for it seemed to him impossible to relieve himself without making some noise, and he ground his teeth and squeezed his shoulders together, holding his breath as much as he could; but in spite of his precautions he was unlucky enough after all to make a little noise, very different from that which was causing him so much fear.","바로 그때, 이제 다가오는 아침의 추위 때문인지, 저녁 식사 때 무언가 배 속을 무르게 하는 것을 먹었기 때문인지, 아니면 (가장 그럴듯하게는) 그저 자연스러운 생리 현상 때문인지, 산초는 아무도 자기를 대신해 줄 수 없는 일을 하고 싶은 욕구를 느꼈다. 그러나 그의 가슴에 스며든 두려움이 어찌나 컸던지, 그는 감히 손톱 끝의 검은 때만큼도 주인에게서 떨어지려 하지 않았다. 그렇다고 하고 싶은 일을 안 하고 넘어가는 것 또한 불가능했다. 그래서 평온을 위해 그가 한 일은, 안장 뒷머리를 붙들고 있던 오른손을 떼어, 그것으로 자기 바지를 받쳐 주던 단 하나의 졸라맨 끈을 슬그머니 소리 없이 푸는 것이었다. 그러자 끈을 풀기가 무섭게 바지가 곧장 차꼬처럼 발치로 흘러내렸다. 이어 그는 셔츠를 할 수 있는 한 걷어 올려, 결코 빈약하지 않은 엉덩이를 드러냈다. 그러나 이 끔찍한 곤경과 난처함에서 벗어나려면 이것이 할 일의 전부라고 여겼던 그 일을 마치고 나자, 더욱 큰 난관이 또 하나 닥쳐왔다. 소리를 내지 않고서는 도무지 볼일을 볼 수 없을 것 같았던 것이다. 그래서 그는 이를 악물고 어깨를 잔뜩 움츠리며 숨을 최대한 참았다. 그러나 이런 조심에도 불구하고, 결국 그는 운 나쁘게도 작은 소리를 내고야 말았으니, 그를 그토록 두려움에 떨게 하던 그 소리와는 사뭇 다른 소리였다."]],[["Don Quixote, hearing it, said, “What noise is that, Sancho?”","그 소리를 들은 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그게 무슨 소리냐, 산초?\""]],[["“I don’t know, señor,”","\"모르겠습니다요, 나리.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said he; “it must be something new, for adventures and misadventures never begin with a trifle.”","\"무언가 새로운 일이 틀림없습니다요. 모험이든 불행이든 결코 사소한 것으로 시작되는 법은 없으니까요.\""],["Once more he tried his luck, and succeeded so well, that without any further noise or disturbance he found himself relieved of the burden that had given him so much discomfort.","그는 다시 한번 운을 시험해 보았는데, 어찌나 잘되었던지 더 이상의 소리나 소란 없이, 그를 그토록 거북하게 만들던 짐을 덜어 낼 수 있었다. 그러나 돈키호테의 후각은 청각만큼이나 예민했고, 산초가 그와 어찌나 바짝 붙어 있었던지 그 냄새가 거의 일직선으로 솟아올랐으니, 그 일부가 그의 코에 닿지 않을 수 없었다."],["But as Don Quixote’s sense of smell was as acute as his hearing, and as Sancho was so closely linked with him that the fumes rose almost in a straight line, it could not be but that some should reach his nose, and as soon as they did he came to its relief by compressing it between his fingers, saying in a rather snuffing tone, “Sancho, it strikes me thou art in great fear.”","냄새가 닿기가 무섭게 그는 손가락으로 코를 틀어쥐어 코를 구해 주고는, 다소 코맹맹이 소리로 말했다. \"산초야, 보아하니 네가 몹시 두려운 모양이로구나.\""]],[["“I am,”","\"그렇습니다요.\""],["answered Sancho; “but how does your worship perceive it now more than ever?”","산초가 대답했다. \"하지만 나리께서는 어찌하여 그걸 어느 때보다 지금 더 잘 아십니까요?\""]],[["“Because just now thou smellest stronger than ever, and not of ambergris,”","\"방금 네가 그 어느 때보다 더 독한 냄새를 풍기는데, 용연향 냄새는 아니기 때문이다.\""],["answered Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 대답했다."]],[["“Very likely,”","\"그럴 만도 합니다요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “but that’s not my fault, but your worship’s, for leading me about at unseasonable hours and at such unwonted paces.”","\"하지만 그건 제 잘못이 아니라 나리 잘못입니다요. 이런 때아닌 시각에, 이런 익숙지 않은 걸음으로 저를 끌고 다니시니 말입니다요.\""]],[["“Then go back three or four, my friend,”","\"그러면 서너 걸음 물러나거라, 이 사람아.\" 돈키호테가 줄곧 코에 손가락을 댄 채 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, all the time with his fingers to his nose; “and for the future pay more attention to thy person and to what thou owest to mine; for it is my great familiarity with thee that has bred this contempt.”","\"그리고 앞으로는 네 몸가짐과, 나에 대해 네가 지켜야 할 도리에 좀 더 신경을 쓰거라. 너에게 너무 허물없이 대한 탓에 이런 무례가 생겨난 것이니.\""]],[["“I’ll bet,”","\"내기를 걸어도 좋습니다요.\""],["replied Sancho, “that your worship thinks I have done something I ought not with my person.”","산초가 대답했다. \"나리께서는 제가 제 몸으로 하지 말아야 할 무슨 일을 했다고 여기시는 거지요.\""]],[["“It makes it worse to stir it, friend Sancho,”","\"그것을 들쑤시면 더 나빠질 뿐이니라, 산초야.\""],["returned Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 대꾸했다."]],[["With this and other talk of the same sort master and man passed the night, till Sancho, perceiving that daybreak was coming on apace, very cautiously untied Rocinante and tied up his breeches.","이런저런 같은 종류의 이야기를 나누며 주인과 하인은 밤을 지새웠다. 그러다 산초는 새벽이 빠르게 다가오는 것을 알아차리고, 아주 조심스럽게 로시난테를 풀어 주고 자기 바지를 추슬러 올렸다."],["As soon as Rocinante found himself free, though by nature he was not at all mettlesome, he seemed to feel lively and began pawing--for as to capering, begging his pardon, he knew not what it meant.","로시난테는 자유로워진 것을 알자, 천성이 전혀 혈기 왕성하지는 않았으나 생기를 느끼는 듯 앞발로 땅을 긁기 시작했으니——껑충거리는 것으로 말하자면, 미안한 말이지만 그게 무엇인지조차 몰랐기 때문이다. 그리하여 돈키호테는 로시난테가 움직일 수 있게 된 것을 보고, 이를 좋은 징조이자 그 무시무시한 모험을 시도해야 한다는 신호로 받아들였다."],["Don Quixote, then, observing that Rocinante could move, took it as a good sign and a signal that he should attempt the dread adventure.","이때쯤에는 날이 완전히 밝아 모든 것이 또렷이 드러났는데, 돈키호테는 자기가 무척 짙은 그늘을 드리우는 키 큰 나무들, 곧 밤나무들 사이에 있음을 보았다. 그는 또한 타격 소리가 멎지 않고 있음을 알아차렸으나, 그것이 무엇 때문인지는 알아낼 수 없었다."],["By this time day had fully broken and everything showed distinctly, and Don Quixote saw that he was among some tall trees, chestnuts, which cast a very deep shade; he perceived likewise that the sound of the strokes did not cease, but could not discover what caused it, and so without any further delay he let Rocinante feel the spur, and once more taking leave of Sancho, he told him to wait for him there three days at most, as he had said before, and if he should not have returned by that time, he might feel sure it had been God’s will that he should end his days in that perilous adventure.","그래서 더는 지체하지 않고 로시난테에게 박차를 느끼게 하고는, 다시 한번 산초에게 작별을 고하며, 앞서 말한 대로 길어야 사흘만 거기서 자기를 기다리라고, 만일 그때까지 자기가 돌아오지 않거든 자기가 그 위험천만한 모험에서 생을 마감하는 것이 하느님의 뜻이었다고 확신해도 좋다고 일렀다. 그는 자기를 대신해 귀부인 둘시네아께 전할 전언과 부탁을 다시 한번 되풀이하고는, 봉사의 대가에 대해서는 걱정하지 말라고, 집을 떠나기 전에 유언장을 작성해 두었으니 거기서 그가 봉사한 기간에 정당히 비례하여 품삯이 충분히 보상되어 있음을 알게 되리라고 말했다."],["He again repeated the message and commission with which he was to go on his behalf to his lady Dulcinea, and said he was not to be uneasy as to the payment of his services, for before leaving home he had made his will, in which he would find himself fully recompensed in the matter of wages in due proportion to the time he had served; but if God delivered him safe, sound, and unhurt out of that danger, he might look upon the promised island as much more than certain.","그러나 하느님께서 자기를 그 위험에서 안전하고 멀쩡하게, 다친 데 없이 건져 주신다면, 그는 약속받은 섬을 확실한 것보다 훨씬 더 확실한 것으로 여겨도 좋다고 했다. 산초는 자기 선량한 주인의 그 가슴 저미는 말을 다시 듣고는 새로이 울기 시작했고, 이 일의 마지막 결말과 끝까지 그와 함께 머물기로 결심했다."],["Sancho began to weep afresh on again hearing the affecting words of his good master, and resolved to stay with him until the final issue and end of the business.","산초 판사의 이 눈물과 이 갸륵한 결심에서, 이 이야기의 작가는 그가 좋은 집안 출신이며 적어도 유서 깊은 기독교도였음에 틀림없다고 추론한다."],["From these tears and this honourable resolve of Sancho Panza’s the author of this history infers that he must have been of good birth and at least an old Christian; and the feeling he displayed touched his but not so much as to make him show any weakness; on the contrary, hiding what he felt as well as he could, he began to move towards that quarter whence the sound of the water and of the strokes seemed to come.","그가 드러낸 그 감정은 주인의 마음을 움직였으나, 그가 어떤 나약함을 내보일 만큼은 아니었다. 오히려 그는 자기가 느끼는 바를 할 수 있는 한 잘 감추고는, 물소리와 타격 소리가 들려오는 듯한 그 방향으로 움직이기 시작했다."]],[["Sancho followed him on foot, leading by the halter, as his custom was, his ass, his constant comrade in prosperity or adversity; and advancing some distance through the shady chestnut trees they came upon a little meadow at the foot of some high rocks, down which a mighty rush of water flung itself.","산초는 늘 하던 대로 굴레를 끌어, 잘되든 못되든 한결같은 동무인 당나귀를 데리고 걸어서 그의 뒤를 따랐다. 그늘진 밤나무들 사이로 얼마쯤 나아가자, 그들은 어떤 높은 바위들 발치의 자그마한 풀밭에 이르렀는데, 그 바위 아래로 엄청난 물줄기가 쏟아져 내리고 있었다."],["At the foot of the rocks were some rudely constructed houses looking more like ruins than houses, from among which came, they perceived, the din and clatter of blows, which still continued without intermission.","그 바위 발치에는 집이라기보다는 폐허에 가까운, 조잡하게 지어진 집 몇 채가 있었고, 그 집들 사이에서 여전히 쉴 새 없이 이어지는 타격 소리와 요란한 소음이 들려오는 것을 그들은 알아차렸다."],["Rocinante took fright at the noise of the water and of the blows, but quieting him Don Quixote advanced step by step towards the houses, commending himself with all his heart to his lady, imploring her support in that dread pass and enterprise, and on the way commending himself to God, too, not to forget him.","로시난테는 물소리와 타격 소리에 겁을 먹었으나, 돈키호테는 그를 달래며 한 걸음 한 걸음 그 집들을 향해 나아갔다. 그는 온 마음을 다해 귀부인께 자신을 의탁하며 그 무시무시한 고비와 위업에서 그분의 도움을 간청하고, 가는 길에 하느님께도 자신을 의탁하며 자기를 잊지 말아 주십사 빌었다."],["Sancho who never quitted his side, stretched his neck as far as he could and peered between the legs of Rocinante to see if he could now discover what it was that caused him such fear and apprehension.","산초는 한시도 그의 곁을 떠나지 않고, 목을 최대한 길게 빼어 로시난테의 다리 사이로 빠끔히 내다보며, 이제 자기를 그토록 두려움과 불안에 떨게 한 것이 무엇인지 알아낼 수 있을까 살폈다."],["They went it might be a hundred paces farther, when on turning a corner the true cause, beyond the possibility of any mistake, of that dread-sounding and to them awe-inspiring noise that had kept them all the night in such fear and perplexity, appeared plain and obvious; and it was (if, reader, thou art not disgusted and disappointed) six fulling hammers which by their alternate strokes made all the din.","그들이 백 걸음쯤 더 나아갔을까, 어느 모퉁이를 돌자, 밤새도록 그들을 그토록 두려움과 당혹 속에 가두어 두었던, 그 무시무시하게 울리고 그들에게 경외감을 자아내던 소음의 진정한 원인이 어떤 착오의 여지도 없이 또렷하고 분명하게 드러났다. 그것은 (독자여, 그대가 역겨워하거나 실망하지 않는다면) 여섯 개의 축융기 망치였으니, 그것들이 번갈아 두드리며 그 모든 소음을 내고 있었던 것이다."]],[["When Don Quixote perceived what it was, he was struck dumb and rigid from head to foot. Sancho glanced at him and saw him with his head bent down upon his breast in manifest mortification; and Don Quixote glanced at Sancho and saw him with his cheeks puffed out and his mouth full of laughter, and evidently ready to explode with it, and in spite of his vexation he could not help laughing at the sight of him; and when Sancho saw his master begin he let go so heartily that he had to hold his sides with both hands to keep himself from bursting with laughter. Four times he stopped, and as many times did his laughter break out afresh with the same violence as at first, whereat Don Quixote grew furious, above all when he heard him say mockingly, “Thou must know, friend Sancho, that of Heaven’s will I was born in this our iron age to revive in it the golden or age of gold; I am he for whom are reserved perils, mighty achievements, valiant deeds;” and here he went on repeating the words that Don Quixote uttered the first time they heard the awful strokes.","* 축융기: 모직물을 두드려 다지고 빨아 천을 촘촘하게 만드는 물레방아식 기계. 돈키호테는 그것이 무엇인지 알아차리자, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 말문이 막히고 몸이 뻣뻣하게 굳어 버렸다. 산초가 그를 흘끗 보니, 그는 분명한 굴욕감에 고개를 가슴팍까지 떨군 채였다. 그리고 돈키호테가 산초를 흘끗 보니, 그는 두 볼을 잔뜩 부풀리고 입은 웃음으로 가득 차, 금방이라도 웃음을 터뜨릴 듯한 기색이 역력했다. 그 꼴을 보고 돈키호테는 분한 와중에도 웃음을 참지 못했고, 산초는 주인이 웃기 시작하는 것을 보자 어찌나 신나게 웃어 젖혔던지 웃다가 배가 터지지 않게 두 손으로 옆구리를 부여잡아야 할 지경이었다. 네 번이나 웃음을 멈추었으나, 그때마다 처음과 똑같은 격렬함으로 다시금 웃음이 터져 나왔다. 이에 돈키호테는 격노했으니, 무엇보다 산초가 비웃듯이 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었을 때 그러했다. \"산초여, 알아 두어라. 나는 하늘의 뜻으로 이 무쇠의 시대에 태어나, 그 안에서 황금기, 곧 황금의 시대를 되살리려 하노라. 위험과 위대한 업적과 용맹한 행적이 마련되어 있는 자가 바로 나이니라.\" 그러면서 그는 그 무시무시한 타격 소리를 처음 들었을 때 돈키호테가 했던 말을 줄줄이 되풀이했다."]],[["Don Quixote, then, seeing that Sancho was turning him into ridicule, was so mortified and vexed that he lifted up his pike and smote him two such blows that if, instead of catching them on his shoulders, he had caught them on his head there would have been no wages to pay, unless indeed to his heirs.","그리하여 돈키호테는 산초가 자기를 조롱거리로 삼는 것을 보고 어찌나 굴욕스럽고 분했던지, 창을 들어 올려 그를 두 번이나 후려쳤으니, 만일 그가 어깨로 그것을 받지 않고 머리로 받았더라면 품삯을 치를 일도 없었으리라——하기야 그의 상속인들에게 치를 일은 있었겠지만. 산초는 자기 농담에 대한 답례로 진짜 호된 대접을 받게 되자, 주인이 그것을 더 멀리 끌고 갈까 두려워 아주 공손하게 말했다."],["Sancho seeing that he was getting an awkward return in earnest for his jest, and fearing his master might carry it still further, said to him very humbly, “Calm yourself, sir, for by God I am only joking.”","\"진정하십시오, 나리. 정말이지 저는 그저 농담을 했을 뿐입니다요.\""]],[["“Well, then, if you are joking I am not,”","\"네가 농담을 하고 있다면, 나는 농담이 아니다.\""],["replied Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 대답했다."],["“Look here, my lively gentleman, if these, instead of being fulling hammers, had been some perilous adventure, have I not, think you, shown the courage required for the attempt and achievement?","\"이보게, 익살맞은 양반아, 만일 이것이 축융기 망치가 아니라 무슨 위험천만한 모험이었다면, 내가 그것에 도전하고 이루어 내는 데 필요한 용기를 보여 주지 않았다고 생각하느냐? 어디 그래, 신사인 내가 소리를 알아듣고 분간하여 그것이 축융기에서 나는 것인지 아닌지 가려낼 의무라도 있단 말이냐?"],["Am I, perchance, being, as I am, a gentleman, bound to know and distinguish sounds and tell whether they come from fulling mills or not; and that, when perhaps, as is the case, I have never in my life seen any as you have, low boor as you are, that have been born and bred among them?","더구나 어쩌면, 실제로 그러하듯이, 나는 평생토록 그런 것을 한 번도 본 적이 없거늘 말이다. 그런 것들 틈에서 나고 자란 너 같은 천한 촌놈이야 보았겠지만."],["But turn me these six hammers into six giants, and bring them to beard me, one by one or all together, and if I do not knock them head over heels, then make what mockery you like of me.”","그러나 이 망치 여섯 개를 거인 여섯으로 바꾸어, 하나씩이든 다 함께든 나에게 덤벼들게 해 보아라. 만일 내가 그것들을 거꾸로 나동그라지게 만들지 못한다면, 그때는 네 멋대로 나를 조롱해도 좋다.\""]],[["“No more of that, señor,” returned Sancho; “I own I went a little too far with the joke. But tell me, your worship, now that peace is made between us (and may God bring you out of all the adventures that may befall you as safe and sound as he has brought you out of this one), was it not a thing to laugh at, and is it not a good story, the great fear we were in?--at least that I was in; for as to your worship I see now that you neither know nor understand what either fear or dismay is.”","\"그만하겠습니다요, 나리.\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"농담이 좀 지나쳤음을 인정합니다요. 하지만 말씀해 보십시오, 나리. 이제 우리 사이에 화해가 이루어졌으니 말입니다요(그리고 하느님께서 이번 모험에서 나리를 무사히 멀쩡하게 빼내 주셨듯, 앞으로 닥칠 온갖 모험에서도 그리 빼내 주시기를), 우리가 빠졌던 그 엄청난 두려움이, 적어도 제가 빠졌던 그 두려움이 웃음거리가 아니었습니까요? 그리고 그것이 좋은 이야깃거리가 아닙니까요? 나리로 말하자면, 이제 저는 나리께서 두려움이나 경악이 무엇인지 알지도 이해하지도 못하신다는 걸 알겠으니 말입니다요.\""]],[["“I do not deny,”","\"우리에게 일어난 일이 웃을 만한 것이라는 점은 부정하지 않겠다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “that what happened to us may be worth laughing at, but it is not worth making a story about, for it is not everyone that is shrewd enough to hit the right point of a thing.”","\"하지만 그것을 이야깃거리로 삼을 만하지는 않다. 어떤 일의 핵심을 제대로 짚어 낼 만큼 영민한 자가 누구나 다 있는 것은 아니니까.\""]],[["“At any rate,” said Sancho, “your worship knew how to hit the right point with your pike, aiming at my head and hitting me on the shoulders, thanks be to God and my own smartness in dodging it. But let that pass; all will come out in the scouring; for I have heard say ‘he loves thee well that makes thee weep;’ and moreover that it is the way with great lords after any hard words they give a servant to give him a pair of breeches; though I do not know what they give after blows, unless it be that knights-errant after blows give islands, or kingdoms on the mainland.”","\"어쨌든,\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리께서는 창으로 핵심을 제대로 짚으실 줄 아셨습니다요. 제 머리를 겨누고서 제 어깨를 맞히셨으니 말입니다요. 그것도 하느님 덕분이요, 제가 잽싸게 피한 덕분이지만요. 하지만 그건 그렇다 치고요. 모든 건 빨면서 드러나는 법입죠. '울게 만드는 자가 너를 진정 사랑하느니라'라는 말을 저는 들은 적이 있습니다요. 게다가 위대한 영주들은 하인에게 모진 말을 하고 나면 바지 한 벌을 주는 것이 관례라지요. 다만 매질을 하고 나서는 무엇을 주는지 저는 모르겠습니다요. 편력 기사들이 매질 뒤에 섬이나, 본토에 있는 왕국이라도 주는 게 아니라면 말입니다요.\""]],[["“It may be on the dice,” said Don Quixote, “that all thou sayest will come true; overlook the past, for thou art shrewd enough to know that our first movements are not in our own control; and one thing for the future bear in mind, that thou curb and restrain thy loquacity in my company; for in all the books of chivalry that I have read, and they are innumerable, I never met with a squire who talked so much to his lord as thou dost to thine; and in fact I feel it to be a great fault of thine and of mine: of thine, that thou hast so little respect for me; of mine, that I do not make myself more respected. There was Gandalin, the squire of Amadis of Gaul, that was Count of the Insula Firme, and we read of him that he always addressed his lord with his cap in his hand, his head bowed down and his body bent double, more turquesco. And then, what shall we say of Gasabal, the squire of Galaor, who was so silent that in order to indicate to us the greatness of his marvellous taciturnity his name is only once mentioned in the whole of that history, as long as it is truthful? From all I have said thou wilt gather, Sancho, that there must be a difference between master and man, between lord and lackey, between knight and squire: so that from this day forward in our intercourse we must observe more respect and take less liberties, for in whatever way I may be provoked with you it will be bad for the pitcher. The favours and benefits that I have promised you will come in due time, and if they do not your wages at least will not be lost, as I have already told you.”","\"주사위를 굴려 봐야 알겠지.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 하는 말이 모두 들어맞을지도 모르지. 지난 일은 눈감아 다오. 우리의 첫 충동이 우리 마음대로 되는 것이 아님은 너도 알 만큼 영민하니 말이다. 그리고 앞으로 한 가지 명심할 것이 있다. 내 앞에서는 그 수다스러움을 억제하고 삼가야 한다는 것이다. 내가 읽은, 그것도 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 그 모든 기사도 책에서, 너처럼 자기 주인에게 그토록 많은 말을 늘어놓는 종자를 나는 한 번도 만난 적이 없다. 사실 나는 이것이 너의 큰 잘못이자 나의 큰 잘못이라 여긴다. 너의 잘못이란 네가 나를 그토록 존경하지 않는 것이요, 나의 잘못이란 내가 나 자신을 더 존경받도록 만들지 못하는 것이다. 골의 아마디스의 종자였던 간달린은 인술라 피르메의 백작이었는데, 우리가 읽기로 그는 늘 모자를 손에 들고, 고개를 숙이고, 몸을 두 겹으로 굽혀, 무어인의 방식대로 자기 주인에게 말을 올렸다. 또 갈라오르의 종자였던 가사발은 어떠하냐? 그는 어찌나 말이 없었던지, 그의 그 경이로운 과묵함의 위대함을 우리에게 일러 주기 위해, 그토록 진실한 그 이야기 전체에서 그의 이름이 단 한 번만 언급될 정도였다. 산초여, 내가 한 이 모든 말에서 너는, 주인과 하인 사이에, 영주와 종복 사이에, 기사와 종자 사이에는 반드시 차이가 있어야 한다는 것을 깨닫게 될 것이다. 그러니 오늘부터 우리 사이에서는 서로 더 많은 존경을 지키고 덜 허물없이 굴어야 할 것이다. 내가 어떤 식으로든 너에게 화가 나면, 그것은 항아리에 좋지 못한 일이 될 테니 말이다. 내가 너에게 약속한 은혜와 혜택은 때가 되면 올 것이고, 설령 오지 않더라도, 앞서 말했듯 적어도 네 품삯만큼은 잃지 않을 것이다.\" * 인술라 피르메: '굳건한 섬'이라는 뜻으로, 기사도 소설 『골의 아마디스』에 나오는 가상의 영지."]],[["“All that your worship says is very well,” said Sancho, “but I should like to know (in case the time of favours should not come, and it might be necessary to fall back upon wages) how much did the squire of a knight-errant get in those days, and did they agree by the month, or by the day like bricklayers?”","\"나리께서 하시는 말씀은 모두 지당하십니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하지만 한 가지 알고 싶은 것이 있습니다요(혹시 은혜가 베풀어질 때가 오지 않아 품삯에 기대야 할 경우를 대비해서요). 그 시절 편력 기사의 종자는 얼마나 받았습니까요? 그리고 벽돌공처럼 달로 쳐서 합의했습니까요, 아니면 날로 쳐서 했습니까요?\""]],[["“I do not believe,” replied Don Quixote, “that such squires were ever on wages, but were dependent on favour; and if I have now mentioned thine in the sealed will I have left at home, it was with a view to what may happen; for as yet I know not how chivalry will turn out in these wretched times of ours, and I do not wish my soul to suffer for trifles in the other world; for I would have thee know, Sancho, that in this there is no condition more hazardous than that of adventurers.”","\"내 생각에는,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그런 종자들이 품삯을 받은 적은 한 번도 없고, 다만 주인의 은총에 기댔던 것 같다. 그리고 내가 집에 남겨 둔 봉인된 유언장에 너의 품삯을 언급해 두었다면, 그것은 무슨 일이 일어날지 모르기 때문이었다. 이 비참한 시대에 기사도가 어떻게 될지 나는 아직 모르거니와, 사소한 일로 저세상에서 내 영혼이 고통받기를 원치 않기 때문이다. 산초여, 너는 알아 두어라. 이 세상에서 모험가의 처지보다 더 위태로운 신분은 없다는 것을.\""]],[["“That is true,” said Sancho, “since the mere noise of the hammers of a fulling mill can disturb and disquiet the heart of such a valiant errant adventurer as your worship; but you may be sure I will not open my lips henceforward to make light of anything of your worship’s, but only to honour you as my master and natural lord.”","\"그건 사실입니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"축융기 망치 소리만으로도 나리처럼 용맹한 편력 모험가의 가슴이 흔들리고 불안해질 수 있으니 말입니다요. 하지만 이것만은 믿으셔도 좋습니다요. 앞으로 저는 나리의 일이라면 무엇이든 가볍게 여기려고 입을 열지는 않을 것이며, 오직 저의 주인이자 타고난 영주이신 나리를 받들기 위해서만 입을 열겠습니다요.\""]],[["“By so doing,”","\"그리한다면,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote, “shalt thou live long on the face of the earth; for next to parents, masters are to be respected as though they were parents.”","\"너는 이 땅 위에서 오래 살리라. 부모 다음으로 주인은 마치 부모인 것처럼 존경받아야 하는 법이니.\""]],[["# CHAPTER XXI. WHICH TREATS OF THE EXALTED ADVENTURE AND RICH PRIZE OF MAMBRINO’S HELMET, TOGETHER WITH OTHER THINGS THAT HAPPENED TO OUR INVINCIBLE KNIGHT","# CHAPTER XXI."]],[["It now began to rain a little, and Sancho was for going into the fulling mills, but Don Quixote had taken such an abhorrence to them on account of the late joke that he would not enter them on any account; so turning aside to right they came upon another road, different from that which they had taken the night before.","이제 비가 조금씩 내리기 시작하여 산초는 축융 공장 안으로 들어가자고 했으나, 돈키호테는 지난밤의 그 농지거리 때문에 그곳을 어찌나 혐오하게 되었던지 무슨 일이 있어도 들어가려 하지 않았다. 그리하여 오른쪽으로 비켜 가다가 그들은 전날 밤 택했던 길과는 다른 또 하나의 길에 들어섰다."],["Shortly afterwards Don Quixote perceived a man on horseback who wore on his head something that shone like gold, and the moment he saw him he turned to Sancho and said:","얼마 지나지 않아 돈키호테는 머리에 황금처럼 번쩍이는 무언가를 쓴 말 탄 사내를 발견했고, 그를 보자마자 산초를 돌아보며 말했다."]],[["“I think, Sancho, there is no proverb that is not true, all being maxims drawn from experience itself, the mother of all the sciences, especially that one that says, ‘Where one door shuts, another opens.’ I say so because if last night fortune shut the door of the adventure we were looking for against us, cheating us with the fulling mills, it now opens wide another one for another better and more certain adventure, and if I do not contrive to enter it, it will be my own fault, and I cannot lay it to my ignorance of fulling mills, or the darkness of the night. I say this because, if I mistake not, there comes towards us one who wears on his head the helmet of Mambrino, concerning which I took the oath thou rememberest.”","\"산초, 내 생각에 진실하지 않은 속담은 하나도 없으니, 모두가 모든 학문의 어머니인 경험 그 자체에서 길어 올린 격언이기 때문이다. 특히 '한 문이 닫히면 다른 문이 열린다'고 하는 그 속담이 그러하다. 내가 이렇게 말하는 까닭은, 어젯밤 운명이 축융 공장으로 우리를 속여 가며 우리가 찾던 모험의 문을 우리에게 닫아 버렸다면, 이제 더 낫고 더 확실한 또 하나의 모험을 위해 다른 문을 활짝 열어 주고 있기 때문이다. 만일 내가 거기에 들어가는 데 실패한다면 그것은 오롯이 내 잘못일 것이요, 축융 공장에 대한 무지나 밤의 어둠 탓으로 돌릴 수는 없으리라. 내가 이렇게 말하는 것은, 내 잘못 본 것이 아니라면 머리에 맘브리노의 투구를 쓴 자가 우리 쪽으로 다가오고 있기 때문인데, 그것에 관해서는 네가 기억하는 그 맹세를 내가 했지 않느냐.\""]],[["“Mind what you say, your worship, and still more what you do,”","\"나리, 말씀하시는 것을 조심하시고, 행하시는 것은 더더욱 조심하십시오.\""],["said Sancho, “for I don’t want any more fulling mills to finish off fulling and knocking our senses out.”","산초가 말했다. \"우리 정신을 마저 두들겨 빼낼 축융 공장 따위는 더는 사양하고 싶으니 말입니다요.\""]],[["“The devil take thee, man,”","\"이 사람아, 악마가 너를 잡아가겠구나.\""],["said Don Quixote; “what has a helmet to do with fulling mills?”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"투구가 축융 공장과 무슨 상관이 있단 말이냐?\""]],[["“I don’t know,”","\"모르겠습니다요.\""],["replied Sancho, “but, faith, if I might speak as I used, perhaps I could give such reasons that your worship would see you were mistaken in what you say.”","산초가 대답했다. \"하지만 정말이지, 제가 예전처럼 말해도 된다면, 나리께서 하시는 말씀이 잘못되셨다는 걸 아실 만한 이유들을 댈 수 있을지도 모르겠습니다요.\""]],[["“How can I be mistaken in what I say, unbelieving traitor?”","\"이 믿음 없는 배신자야, 내가 하는 말이 어찌 잘못될 수 있단 말이냐?\""],["returned Don Quixote; “tell me, seest thou not yonder knight coming towards us on a dappled grey steed, who has upon his head a helmet of gold?”","돈키호테가 받아쳤다. \"말해 보아라, 저기 얼룩진 잿빛 준마를 타고 머리에 황금 투구를 쓴 채 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 기사가 네 눈에 보이지 않느냐?\""]],[["“What I see and make out,”","\"제가 보고 알아볼 수 있는 거라곤,\""],["answered Sancho, “is only a man on a grey ass like my own, who has something that shines on his head.”","산초가 대답했다. \"제 것과 똑같은 잿빛 당나귀에 탄 사내 하나가 머리에 뭔가 번쩍이는 걸 얹고 있다는 것뿐입니다요.\""]],[["“Well, that is the helmet of Mambrino,” said Don Quixote; “stand to one side and leave me alone with him; thou shalt see how, without saying a word, to save time, I shall bring this adventure to an issue and possess myself of the helmet I have so longed for.”","\"바로 그것이 맘브리노의 투구다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"너는 한쪽으로 비켜서서 나 혼자 그자를 상대하게 두어라. 시간을 아끼려고 한마디도 하지 않고 내가 어떻게 이 모험을 결말짓고, 그토록 갈망하던 투구를 손에 넣는지 보게 될 것이다.\" \"비켜서 있도록 조심하겠습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"하지만 다시 한번 말씀드리건대, 부디 그것이 축융 공장이 아니라 마요라나이기를 하느님께 비옵니다요.\""]],[["“I will take care to stand aside,” said Sancho; “but God grant, I say once more, that it may be marjoram and not fulling mills.”","* 마요라나: 향신료로 쓰이는 풀로, 여기서는 '제발 좋은 일이기를'이라는 뜻의 관용적 표현으로 쓰였다."]],[["“I have told thee, brother, on no account to mention those fulling mills to me again,”","\"이 사람아, 무슨 일이 있어도 그 축융 공장 얘기는 다시 내게 꺼내지 말라고 했거늘,\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “or I vow--and I say no more-I’ll full the soul out of you.”","\"맹세컨대 — 더는 말하지 않겠다만 — 네놈의 영혼을 축융해서 빼내 버리겠다.\""]],[["Sancho held his peace in dread lest his master should carry out the vow he had hurled like a bowl at him.","산초는 주인이 마치 공처럼 자기에게 내던진 그 맹세를 정말로 실행에 옮길까 두려워 입을 다물었다."]],[["The fact of the matter as regards the helmet, steed, and knight that Don Quixote saw, was this. In that neighbourhood there were two villages, one of them so small that it had neither apothecary’s shop nor barber, which the other that was close to it had, so the barber of the larger served the smaller, and in it there was a sick man who required to be bled and another man who wanted to be shaved, and on this errand the barber was going, carrying with him a brass basin; but as luck would have it, as he was on the way it began to rain, and not to spoil his hat, which probably was a new one, he put the basin on his head, and being clean it glittered at half a league’s distance. He rode upon a grey ass, as Sancho said, and this was what made it seem to Don Quixote to be a dapple-grey steed and a knight and a golden helmet; for everything he saw he made to fall in with his crazy chivalry and ill-errant notions; and when he saw the poor knight draw near, without entering into any parley with him, at Rocinante’s top speed he bore down upon him with the pike pointed low, fully determined to run him through and through, and as he reached him, without checking the fury of his charge, he cried to him:","돈키호테가 본 투구와 준마와 기사에 관한 실상은 이러했다. 그 근방에는 마을이 둘 있었는데, 그중 하나는 어찌나 작은지 약방도 이발사도 없는 반면, 가까이 있는 다른 마을에는 둘 다 있었다. 그래서 큰 마을의 이발사가 작은 마을까지 보살펴 주었으니, 그 작은 마을에는 피를 뽑아야 할 병자 하나와 면도를 받고 싶어 하는 사내 하나가 있었다. 이발사는 이 볼일로 놋대야를 가지고 가는 길이었는데, 운수 사납게도 가는 도중에 비가 내리기 시작했고, 아마 새것이었을 모자를 버리지 않으려고 머리에 대야를 얹었던 것이다. 깨끗이 닦인 그 대야는 반 레과 거리에서도 번쩍였다. 산초가 말한 대로 그는 잿빛 당나귀를 타고 있었으니, 바로 이것이 돈키호테에게는 얼룩진 잿빛 준마와 기사와 황금 투구로 보이게 된 연유였다. 그는 자기가 보는 모든 것을 자신의 미친 기사도와 빗나간 편력의 망상에 끼워 맞추었던 것이다. 가엾은 기사가 가까이 다가오는 것을 보자, 그는 그자와 한마디 담판도 나누지 않고 로시난테의 전속력으로 창을 낮게 겨눈 채 돌진하여, 그자를 꿰뚫어 버리겠다고 단단히 작정하고서, 그에게 다다르자 돌격의 기세를 늦추지도 않고 외쳤다."]],[["“Defend thyself, miserable being, or yield me of thine own accord that which is so reasonably my due.”","\"가련한 자여, 네 몸을 지키든가, 아니면 마땅히 내 것이 될 그것을 순순히 내게 내놓아라.\""]],[["The barber, who without any expectation or apprehension of it saw this apparition coming down upon him, had no other way of saving himself from the stroke of the lance but to let himself fall off his ass; and no sooner had he touched the ground than he sprang up more nimbly than a deer and sped away across the plain faster than the wind.","이발사는 아무런 예상도 두려움도 없이 이 환영 같은 것이 자기에게 들이닥치는 것을 보고, 창의 일격에서 몸을 구할 다른 길이라곤 당나귀에서 굴러떨어지는 것밖에 없었다. 땅에 닿기가 무섭게 그는 사슴보다 날렵하게 벌떡 일어나 바람보다 빠르게 들판을 가로질러 내달렸다."]],[["He left the basin on the ground, with which Don Quixote contented himself, saying that the pagan had shown his discretion and imitated the beaver, which finding itself pressed by the hunters bites and cuts off with its teeth that for which, by its natural instinct, it knows it is pursued.","그는 대야를 땅바닥에 남겨 두었고, 돈키호테는 그것으로 만족하며 말하기를, 저 이교도가 분별을 보여 비버를 흉내 냈다고 했다. 비버란 사냥꾼들에게 쫓기게 되면, 자신의 본능으로 자기가 무엇 때문에 쫓기는지를 알고서, 바로 그것을 제 이빨로 물어뜯어 잘라 버리는 짐승이라는 것이었다."]],[["He told Sancho to pick up the helmet, and he taking it in his hands said:","그는 산초에게 투구를 주워 오라 일렀고, 산초는 그것을 손에 들고 말했다."]],[["“By God the basin is a good one, and worth a real of eight if it is worth a maravedis,” and handed it to his master, who immediately put it on his head, turning it round, now this way, now that, in search of fitment, and not finding it he said, “Clearly the pagan to whose measure this famous head-piece was first forged must have had a very large head; but the worst of it is half of it is wanting.”","\"맹세코 이 대야는 좋은 물건입니다요. 1마라베디만큼의 값어치라도 있다면 8레알짜리 은화만큼은 나갈 겁니다요.\" 그러고는 그것을 주인에게 건넸고, 주인은 곧장 그것을 머리에 쓰고는 맞는 자리를 찾느라 이리저리 돌려 보았으나 끝내 찾지 못하고 말했다. \"이 유명한 투구가 처음 벼려졌을 때 그 치수의 주인이었던 이교도는 머리가 무척 컸던 게 분명하다. 다만 가장 고약한 것은 절반이 없어졌다는 것이다.\""]],[["When Sancho heard him call the basin a headpiece he was unable to restrain his laughter, but remembering his master’s wrath he checked himself in the midst of it.","산초는 주인이 대야를 투구라 부르는 것을 듣고 웃음을 참지 못했으나, 주인의 노여움을 떠올리고는 웃음 한가운데서 그것을 억눌렀다."]],[["“What art thou laughing at, Sancho?”","\"산초, 무엇을 그리 웃느냐?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“I am laughing,”","\"웃는 까닭은,\" 그가 말했다."],["said he, “to think of the great head the pagan must have had who owned this helmet, for it looks exactly like a regular barber’s basin.”","\"이 투구의 주인이었던 이교도가 머리가 얼마나 컸을까 생각하니 우스워서입니다요. 영락없이 보통 이발사의 대야처럼 생겼지 않습니까요.\""]],[["“Dost thou know what I suspect, Sancho?” said Don Quixote; “that this wonderful piece of this enchanted helmet must by some strange accident have come into the hands of some one who was unable to recognise or realise its value, and who, not knowing what he did, and seeing it to be of the purest gold, must have melted down one half for the sake of what it might be worth, and of the other made this which is like a barber’s basin as thou sayest; but be it as it may, to me who recognise it, its transformation makes no difference, for I will set it to rights at the first village where there is a blacksmith, and in such style that that helmet the god of smithies forged for the god of battles shall not surpass it or even come up to it; and in the meantime I will wear it as well as I can, for something is better than nothing; all the more as it will be quite enough to protect me from any chance blow of a stone.”","\"산초, 내가 무엇을 의심하는지 아느냐?\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이 마법에 걸린 투구의 놀라운 물건이 어떤 기이한 사고로, 그 값어치를 알아보거나 헤아릴 줄 모르는 누군가의 손에 들어갔던 게 틀림없다. 그자는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 모른 채, 그것이 순금임을 보고서 값나가는 만큼 챙기려고 절반을 녹여 버리고, 나머지 절반으로 네 말마따나 이발사 대야 같은 이것을 만들었을 것이다. 허나 어찌 되었든, 그것을 알아보는 나에게는 그 변형이 아무런 차이가 없으니, 대장장이가 있는 첫 마을에서 제대로 고쳐 놓을 것이며, 그것도 대장간의 신이 전쟁의 신을 위해 벼려 준 그 투구조차 능가하지도, 견주지도 못할 만한 솜씨로 고쳐 놓으리라. 그동안은 할 수 있는 대로 쓰고 다닐 것이니, 없는 것보다는 무엇이라도 있는 편이 나은 법이요, 더구나 돌멩이가 어쩌다 날아드는 일격쯤은 막아 주기에 충분할 테니 말이다.\""]],[["“That is,”","\"그것은,\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “if it is not shot with a sling as they were in the battle of the two armies, when they signed the cross on your worship’s grinders and smashed the flask with that blessed draught that made me vomit my bowels up.”","\"두 군대의 전투에서처럼 무릿매로 쏘아 대지만 않는다면 그렇겠지요. 그때 놈들이 나리의 어금니에 십자가를 그어 놓고, 제 내장을 다 게워 내게 만든 그 거룩한 약물 병을 박살내 버렸지 않습니까요.\""]],[["“It does not grieve me much to have lost it,”","\"그것을 잃어버린 게 그리 애통하지는 않다.\""],["said Don Quixote, “for thou knowest, Sancho, that I have the receipt in my memory.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"산초, 너도 알다시피 그 처방은 내 기억 속에 있으니 말이다.\""]],[["“So have I,” answered Sancho, “but if ever I make it, or try it again as long as I live, may this be my last hour; moreover, I have no intention of putting myself in the way of wanting it, for I mean, with all my five senses, to keep myself from being wounded or from wounding anyone: as to being blanketed again I say nothing, for it is hard to prevent mishaps of that sort, and if they come there is nothing for it but to squeeze our shoulders together, hold our breath, shut our eyes, and let ourselves go where luck and the blanket may send us.”","\"저도 알고 있습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"하지만 제가 살아 있는 동안 다시 그것을 만들거나 시험해 본다면, 그때가 제 마지막 순간이 되기를 바랍니다요. 게다가 저는 그게 필요해질 상황에 저를 내던질 생각이 전혀 없으니, 제 오관을 모두 동원해서 다치지도 않고 누구를 다치게 하지도 않으려고 단단히 마음먹고 있습니다요. 다시 담요 키질을 당하는 일에 대해서는 아무 말도 않겠습니다요. 그런 변고는 막기가 어려운 것이라, 만일 닥쳐 온다면 어깨를 움츠리고 숨을 참고 눈을 감은 채, 운수와 담요가 보내는 곳으로 몸을 내맡기는 수밖에 별도리가 없으니까요.\""]],[["“Thou art a bad Christian, Sancho,”","\"산초, 너는 나쁜 기독교도로구나.\" 이 말을 듣고 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote on hearing this, “for once an injury has been done thee thou never forgettest it: but know that it is the part of noble and generous hearts not to attach importance to trifles.","\"한번 당한 모욕은 결코 잊지 않으니 말이다. 허나 알아 두어라, 사소한 것에 마음을 두지 않는 것이야말로 고귀하고 너그러운 마음이 할 일이다."],["What lame leg hast thou got by it, what broken rib, what cracked head, that thou canst not forget that jest?","그 일로 네가 무슨 다리를 절게 되었느냐, 무슨 갈비뼈가 부러졌느냐, 무슨 머리가 깨졌기에 그 장난을 잊지 못한단 말이냐? 제대로 따져 보면 그것은 한낱 장난이요 놀이였으니, 내가 그렇게 보지 않았던들 돌아가서 너의 원수를 갚느라, 그리스인들이 헬레네를 빼앗긴 일로 저지른 것보다 더한 해코지를 했으리라."],["For jest and sport it was, properly regarded, and had I not seen it in that light I would have returned and done more mischief in revenging thee than the Greeks did for the rape of Helen, who, if she were alive now, or if my Dulcinea had lived then, might depend upon it she would not be so famous for her beauty as she is;”","그 헬레네로 말하자면, 지금 살아 있거나 혹은 그 시절에 내 둘시네아가 살아 있었다면, 그녀가 지금처럼 아름다움으로 그토록 이름나지는 못했으리라는 것을 믿어도 좋으리라.\" 여기서 그는 한숨을 내쉬어 하늘 높이 띄워 보냈다."],["and here he heaved a sigh and sent it aloft; and said Sancho, “Let it pass for a jest as it cannot be revenged in earnest, but I know what sort of jest and earnest it was, and I know it will never be rubbed out of my memory any more than off my shoulders.","그러자 산초가 말했다. \"진심으로 원수를 갚을 수 없는 일이니 장난으로 치고 넘어가지요."],["But putting that aside, will your worship tell me what are we to do with this dapple-grey steed that looks like a grey ass, which that Martino that your worship overthrew has left deserted here?","하지만 그것이 어떤 종류의 장난이고 진심이었는지 저는 잘 알고 있으며, 그것이 제 어깨에서 지워지지 않듯 제 기억에서도 결코 지워지지 않으리란 것도 압니다요. 그건 그렇다 치고, 나리께서 쓰러뜨리신 그 마르티노라는 자가 여기 버려두고 간, 잿빛 당나귀처럼 생긴 이 얼룩진 잿빛 준마를 우리가 어떻게 해야 할지 말씀해 주시겠습니까요?"],["for, from the way he took to his heels and bolted, he is not likely ever to come back for it; and by my beard but the grey is a good one.”","그자가 줄행랑을 친 그 꼴로 보아, 이걸 찾으러 다시 돌아올 성싶지는 않으니 말입니다요. 게다가 제 수염을 걸고 말하건대, 이 잿빛은 좋은 놈입니다요.\""]],[["“I have never been in the habit,” said Don Quixote, “of taking spoil of those whom I vanquish, nor is it the practice of chivalry to take away their horses and leave them to go on foot, unless indeed it be that the victor have lost his own in the combat, in which case it is lawful to take that of the vanquished as a thing won in lawful war; therefore, Sancho, leave this horse, or ass, or whatever thou wilt have it to be; for when its owner sees us gone hence he will come back for it.”","\"나는,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 무찌른 자들의 전리품을 챙기는 버릇이 한 번도 없었으며, 그들의 말을 빼앗아 그들을 걸어가게 만드는 것도 기사도의 관례가 아니다. 다만 승자가 싸움에서 자기 말을 잃었을 경우에는, 정당한 전쟁에서 얻은 것으로 패자의 말을 취하는 것이 합당하다. 그러니 산초, 이 말이든 당나귀든 네가 무엇이라 부르고 싶든 간에 그것을 그냥 두어라. 주인이 우리가 여기서 떠나는 것을 보면 그것을 찾으러 돌아올 것이다.\""]],[["“God knows I should like to take it,” returned Sancho, “or at least to change it for my own, which does not seem to me as good a one: verily the laws of chivalry are strict, since they cannot be stretched to let one ass be changed for another; I should like to know if I might at least change trappings.”","\"하느님께서 아시지만 저는 그걸 갖고 싶습니다요.\" 산초가 받아 말했다. \"아니면 적어도 제 것과 바꾸기라도 했으면 좋겠는데, 제 것은 그만큼 좋아 보이지 않으니 말입니다요. 참으로 기사도의 법이란 엄격하군요. 당나귀 한 마리를 다른 것과 바꾸도록 늘여 줄 수조차 없다니 말입니다요. 적어도 마구라도 바꿔도 되는지 알고 싶습니다요.\""]],[["“On that head I am not quite certain,”","\"그 점에 관해서는 나도 그리 확실치 않다.\""],["answered Don Quixote, “and the matter being doubtful, pending better information, I say thou mayest change them, if so be thou hast urgent need of them.”","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그러니 일이 의심스러운 만큼, 더 나은 정보를 얻기 전까지는, 네게 절실히 필요하다면 마구는 바꿔도 좋다고 말하겠다.\""]],[["“So urgent is it,”","\"어찌나 절실한지,\""],["answered Sancho, “that if they were for my own person I could not want them more;”","산초가 대답했다. \"그것이 제 몸에 걸칠 것이라 해도 이보다 더 절실하지는 못할 겁니다요.\""],["and forthwith, fortified by this licence, he effected the mutatio capparum, rigging out his beast to the ninety-nines and making quite another thing of it.","그러고는 이 허락에 힘입어 곧장 마구 바꾸기를 실행하여, 제 짐승을 더할 나위 없이 잘 차려 입히고 아주 딴것으로 만들어 놓았다. 이 일을 끝내고서 그들은 짐 나르던 노새에게서 노략한 전리품의 남은 것으로 요기를 했고, 축융 공장에서 흘러나오는 개울물을 마셨는데, 그 공장이 자기들에게 안겨 준 놀라움 때문에 어찌나 그것을 진저리 치며 미워했던지 그쪽으로는 눈길 한 번 던지지 않았다."],["This done, they broke their fast on the remains of the spoils of war plundered from the sumpter mule, and drank of the brook that flowed from the fulling mills, without casting a look in that direction, in such loathing did they hold them for the alarm they had caused them; and, all anger and gloom removed, they mounted and, without taking any fixed road (not to fix upon any being the proper thing for true knights-errant), they set out, guided by Rocinante’s will, which carried along with it that of his master, not to say that of the ass, which always followed him wherever he led, lovingly and sociably; nevertheless they returned to the high road, and pursued it at a venture without any other aim.","그리하여 노여움과 울적함을 모두 떨쳐 버리고 그들은 말에 올라, 정해진 길도 잡지 않고서 — 진정한 편력 기사에게는 어떤 길도 정하지 않는 것이 마땅한 일이므로 — 로시난테의 뜻에 이끌려 길을 나섰으니, 로시난테의 뜻이 곧 주인의 뜻을 함께 끌고 갔고, 더구나 당나귀의 뜻 또한 그러하여 다정하고 붙임성 있게 어디로든 로시난테가 이끄는 대로 따라갔다. 그럼에도 그들은 다시 큰길로 돌아와, 다른 아무런 목적도 없이 되는대로 그 길을 따라갔다."]],[["As they went along, then, in this way Sancho said to his master, “Señor, would your worship give me leave to speak a little to you?","이렇게 길을 가던 중에 산초가 주인에게 말했다. \"나리, 잠시 나리께 말씀 좀 여쭤도 괜찮겠습니까요?"],["For since you laid that hard injunction of silence on me several things have gone to rot in my stomach, and I have now just one on the tip of my tongue that I don’t want to be spoiled.”","나리께서 제게 침묵하라는 그 가혹한 분부를 내리신 뒤로 여러 가지가 제 뱃속에서 썩어 가고 있는데, 지금 막 혀끝에 하나가 걸려 있어서 그것이 상해 버리는 건 원치 않으니 말입니다요.\""]],[["“Say, on, Sancho,”","\"말해 보아라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “and be brief in thy discourse, for there is no pleasure in one that is long.”","\"다만 이야기를 짧게 하여라. 긴 이야기에는 즐거움이 없는 법이니.\""]],[["“Well then, señor,” returned Sancho, “I say that for some days past I have been considering how little is got or gained by going in search of these adventures that your worship seeks in these wilds and cross-roads, where, even if the most perilous are victoriously achieved, there is no one to see or know of them, and so they must be left untold for ever, to the loss of your worship’s object and the credit they deserve; therefore it seems to me it would be better (saving your worship’s better judgment) if we were to go and serve some emperor or other great prince who may have some war on hand, in whose service your worship may prove the worth of your person, your great might, and greater understanding, on perceiving which the lord in whose service we may be will perforce have to reward us, each according to his merits; and there you will not be at a loss for some one to set down your achievements in writing so as to preserve their memory for ever. Of my own I say nothing, as they will not go beyond squirely limits, though I make bold to say that, if it be the practice in chivalry to write the achievements of squires, I think mine must not be left out.”","\"그러면 말씀드리지요, 나리.\" 산초가 받아 말했다. \"지난 며칠 동안 제가 곰곰이 생각해 본 것은, 나리께서 이런 황무지와 갈림길에서 찾아 헤매시는 이 모험들로 얻거나 이루는 것이 얼마나 보잘것없는가 하는 점입니다요. 여기서는 아무리 위험천만한 모험을 승리로 이루어 낸다 해도 보거나 알아줄 이가 아무도 없으니, 그것들은 영영 이야기되지 못한 채 묻혀 버려야 하고, 그리하여 나리의 본뜻도 그것들이 받아 마땅한 명예도 잃고 마는 것입지요. 그러니 제 생각에는 (나리의 더 나은 판단을 거스르는 게 아니라면) 전쟁을 벌이고 있는 어느 황제나 다른 위대한 군주를 찾아가 섬기는 편이 더 나을 듯합니다요. 그분을 섬기며 나리께서 나리 자신의 가치와 위대한 무력, 그리고 더 위대한 지력을 입증해 보이시면, 그것을 알아본 그 주군은 우리 각자의 공에 따라 부득불 상을 내리지 않을 수 없을 테고, 거기서는 나리의 업적을 글로 적어 그 기억을 영원히 보존해 줄 누군가가 없어 곤란하실 일도 없을 겁니다요. 제 업적에 대해서는 아무 말도 않겠습니다요. 종자의 한계를 넘지 못할 테니 말입지요. 다만 감히 말씀드리자면, 기사도에서 종자들의 업적도 적는 것이 관례라면 제 것도 빠뜨려서는 안 되리라 생각합니다요.\""]],[["“Thou speakest not amiss, Sancho,” answered Don Quixote, “but before that point is reached it is requisite to roam the world, as it were on probation, seeking adventures, in order that, by achieving some, name and fame may be acquired, such that when he betakes himself to the court of some great monarch the knight may be already known by his deeds, and that the boys, the instant they see him enter the gate of the city, may all follow him and surround him, crying, ‘This is the Knight of the Sun’-or the Serpent, or any other title under which he may have achieved great deeds. ‘This,’ they will say, ‘is he who vanquished in single combat the gigantic Brocabruno of mighty strength; he who delivered the great Mameluke of Persia out of the long enchantment under which he had been for almost nine hundred years.’ So from one to another they will go proclaiming his achievements; and presently at the tumult of the boys and the others the king of that kingdom will appear at the windows of his royal palace, and as soon as he beholds the knight, recognising him by his arms and the device on his shield, he will as a matter of course say, ‘What ho! Forth all ye, the knights of my court, to receive the flower of chivalry who cometh hither!’ At which command all will issue forth, and he himself, advancing half-way down the stairs, will embrace him closely, and salute him, kissing him on the cheek, and will then lead him to the queen’s chamber, where the knight will find her with the princess her daughter, who will be one of the most beautiful and accomplished damsels that could with the utmost pains be discovered anywhere in the known world. Straightway it will come to pass that she will fix her eyes upon the knight and he his upon her, and each will seem to the other something more divine than human, and, without knowing how or why they will be taken and entangled in the inextricable toils of love, and sorely distressed in their hearts not to see any way of making their pains and sufferings known by speech. Thence they will lead him, no doubt, to some richly adorned chamber of the palace, where, having removed his armour, they will bring him a rich mantle of scarlet wherewith to robe himself, and if he looked noble in his armour he will look still more so in a doublet. When night comes he will sup with the king, queen, and princess; and all the time he will never take his eyes off her, stealing stealthy glances, unnoticed by those present, and she will do the same, and with equal cautiousness, being, as I have said, a damsel of great discretion. The tables being removed, suddenly through the door of the hall there will enter a hideous and diminutive dwarf followed by a fair dame, between two giants, who comes with a certain adventure, the work of an ancient sage; and he who shall achieve it shall be deemed the best knight in the world.","\"네 말이 그르지 않다, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"허나 그 지경에 이르기 전에 먼저, 말하자면 시험 삼아 세상을 떠돌며 모험을 찾아다녀야 하느니, 몇 가지를 이루어 냄으로써 이름과 명성을 얻기 위함이다. 그리하여 어느 위대한 군주의 궁정으로 갈 때쯤이면 그 기사는 이미 자신의 행적으로 알려져 있어서, 도성의 성문에 들어서는 것을 보자마자 사내아이들이 모두 그를 뒤따르며 에워싸고 외칠 것이다. '이분이 바로 태양의 기사다.' 혹은 그가 위대한 공적을 이룬 어떤 칭호든 간에, 뱀의 기사라거나 다른 무엇이라거나 외치며 말이다. '이분이,' 그들이 말하리라. '엄청난 힘을 지닌 거인 브로카브루노를 일대일 결투에서 무찌른 분이며, 거의 구백 년 동안이나 걸려 있던 기나긴 마법에서 페르시아의 위대한 마멜루크를 풀어 준 분이다.' 이렇게 이 입에서 저 입으로 그의 업적을 외쳐 알릴 것이다. 그러다가 사내아이들과 다른 이들이 일으키는 소란에 그 왕국의 임금이 자기 궁전의 창가에 모습을 드러내고, 기사를 보자마자 그 무기와 방패의 문장으로 그를 알아보고는 으레 이렇게 말하리라. '여봐라! 내 궁정의 기사들이여, 모두 나와서 이리로 오시는 기사도의 정화를 맞이하라!' 이 분부에 모두가 나아갈 것이며, 임금 자신도 계단을 반쯤 내려와 그를 꼭 끌어안고, 뺨에 입 맞추며 그를 맞이한 뒤, 왕비의 처소로 데려가리라. 거기서 기사는 왕비와 더불어 그 딸인 공주를 보게 될 것이니, 그 공주는 알려진 온 세계를 아무리 애써 뒤져도 찾을 수 있을 가장 아름답고 기품 있는 처녀들 가운데 하나일 것이다. 곧바로 공주는 기사에게, 기사는 공주에게 서로 눈길을 고정하게 되고, 둘은 서로에게 인간이라기보다 신에 가까운 무언가로 보일 것이며, 어찌 된 영문인지도 모른 채 풀 수 없는 사랑의 그물에 사로잡혀 얽히고, 자신들의 괴로움과 고통을 말로써 알릴 길이 보이지 않아 가슴이 몹시 아릴 것이다. 거기서 그들은 분명 기사를 궁전의 호화롭게 꾸며진 어느 방으로 데려가, 갑옷을 벗기고 몸에 두를 진홍빛 화려한 망토를 가져다줄 것이니, 갑옷을 입었을 때도 고귀해 보였던 그가 더블릿 차림으로는 한층 더 고귀해 보이리라. 밤이 오면 그는 임금과 왕비와 공주와 함께 저녁을 들 것이며, 내내 공주에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 그 자리의 사람들 모르게 슬며시 곁눈질을 훔쳐볼 것이요, 공주 또한 똑같이, 그리고 똑같이 조심스럽게 그리할 것이니, 앞서 말했듯 매우 분별 있는 처녀이기 때문이다. 식탁이 치워지면, 별안간 그 방의 문으로 흉측하고 자그마한 난쟁이 하나가 들어오고, 그 뒤를 두 거인 사이에 낀 아리따운 귀부인이 따라 들어올 것인데, 그녀는 어느 옛 현자가 꾸며 낸 어떤 모험을 가지고 오니, 그것을 이루어 내는 자가 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 기사로 여겨지게 될 것이다."]],[["“The king will then command all those present to essay it, and none will bring it to an end and conclusion save the stranger knight, to the great enhancement of his fame, whereat the princess will be overjoyed and will esteem herself happy and fortunate in having fixed and placed her thoughts so high.","\"그러면 임금은 그 자리에 있는 모든 이에게 그것을 시도해 보라 명할 것이나, 그 이방의 기사 말고는 아무도 그것을 끝맺어 결말짓지 못할 것이니, 이는 그의 명성을 크게 드높이는 일이 될 것이다. 그리하여 공주는 더할 나위 없이 기뻐하며, 자신의 마음을 그토록 높은 곳에 두고 자리 잡게 한 것을 행복하고 다행스럽게 여길 것이다."],["And the best of it is that this king, or prince, or whatever he is, is engaged in a very bitter war with another as powerful as himself, and the stranger knight, after having been some days at his court, requests leave from him to go and serve him in the said war.","그리고 가장 좋은 것은, 이 임금인지 군주인지 무엇이든 간에 그가 자기만큼 강력한 다른 자와 매우 격렬한 전쟁에 휘말려 있다는 점이다. 그래서 이방의 기사는 궁정에서 며칠을 지낸 뒤, 그 전쟁에서 그를 섬기러 가게 해 달라고 청하리라."],["The king will grant it very readily, and the knight will courteously kiss his hands for the favour done to him; and that night he will take leave of his lady the princess at the grating of the chamber where she sleeps, which looks upon a garden, and at which he has already many times conversed with her, the go-between and confidante in the matter being a damsel much trusted by the princess.","임금은 기꺼이 그것을 허락할 것이며, 기사는 베풀어진 은혜에 정중히 그 손에 입 맞출 것이다. 그날 밤 그는 공주가 잠드는 방의 창살 너머로 자신의 연인인 공주에게 작별을 고할 것이니, 그 방은 정원을 향해 나 있고, 그곳에서 그는 이미 여러 차례 공주와 이야기를 나누었으며, 그 일의 다리 놓는 이이자 속내를 나눈 사람은 공주가 깊이 신뢰하는 한 처녀였다."],["He will sigh, she will swoon, the damsel will fetch water, much distressed because morning approaches, and for the honour of her lady he would not that they were discovered; at last the princess will come to herself and will present her white hands through the grating to the knight, who will kiss them a thousand and a thousand times, bathing them with his tears.","그가 한숨지으면 공주는 기절할 것이요, 처녀는 물을 가지러 갈 것이니, 아침이 다가오는 데다 자기 여주인의 명예를 위해 둘이 발각되는 것을 원치 않아 몹시 애를 태우리라. 마침내 공주가 정신을 차려 창살 너머로 기사에게 흰 손을 내밀면, 기사는 천 번이고 또 천 번이고 거기에 입 맞추며 눈물로 그 손을 적시리라."],["It will be arranged between them how they are to inform each other of their good or evil fortunes, and the princess will entreat him to make his absence as short as possible, which he will promise to do with many oaths; once more he kisses her hands, and takes his leave in such grief that he is well-nigh ready to die.","둘 사이에는 서로의 길흉을 어떻게 알릴지가 정해질 것이며, 공주는 그에게 가능한 한 짧게 떠나 있어 달라 애원하고, 그는 숱한 맹세로 그리하겠노라 약속하리라. 다시 한번 그는 공주의 손에 입 맞추고, 거의 죽을 지경에 이를 만큼 비통한 심정으로 작별을 고한다."],["He betakes him thence to his chamber, flings himself on his bed, cannot sleep for sorrow at parting, rises early in the morning, goes to take leave of the king, queen, and princess, and, as he takes his leave of the pair, it is told him that the princess is indisposed and cannot receive a visit; the knight thinks it is from grief at his departure, his heart is pierced, and he is hardly able to keep from showing his pain.","거기서 그는 제 방으로 가 침대에 몸을 내던지나, 이별의 슬픔에 잠들지 못하다가, 아침 일찍 일어나 임금과 왕비와 공주에게 작별을 고하러 간다. 그가 두 분께 작별을 고할 때, 공주가 몸이 편치 않아 방문을 받을 수 없다는 말을 듣게 되리라."],["The confidante is present, observes all, goes to tell her mistress, who listens with tears and says that one of her greatest distresses is not knowing who this knight is, and whether he is of kingly lineage or not; the damsel assures her that so much courtesy, gentleness, and gallantry of bearing as her knight possesses could not exist in any save one who was royal and illustrious; her anxiety is thus relieved, and she strives to be of good cheer lest she should excite suspicion in her parents, and at the end of two days she appears in public.","기사는 그것이 자신의 떠남을 슬퍼하는 탓이라 여겨 가슴이 찔리고, 자신의 고통을 드러내지 않으려 안간힘을 다하나 거의 견디기 어려울 것이다. 속내를 나눈 그 처녀가 그 자리에 있어 모든 것을 살피고는, 자기 여주인에게 알리러 가니, 공주는 눈물을 흘리며 듣고서, 자신의 가장 큰 괴로움 가운데 하나는 이 기사가 누구인지, 또 그가 왕가의 혈통인지 아닌지를 알지 못하는 것이라 말하리라."],["Meanwhile the knight has taken his departure; he fights in the war, conquers the king’s enemy, wins many cities, triumphs in many battles, returns to the court, sees his lady where he was wont to see her, and it is agreed that he shall demand her in marriage of her parents as the reward of his services; the king is unwilling to give her, as he knows not who he is, but nevertheless, whether carried off or in whatever other way it may be, the princess comes to be his bride, and her father comes to regard it as very good fortune; for it so happens that this knight is proved to be the son of a valiant king of some kingdom, I know not what, for I fancy it is not likely to be on the map.","처녀는 자기 기사가 지닌 그만한 예의와 온화함과 의젓한 기품은, 왕족이며 명문가 출신이 아니고서는 있을 수 없는 것이라 그녀를 안심시키니, 그리하여 공주의 근심은 풀리고, 부모에게 의심을 사지 않으려 애써 명랑하게 지내다가 이틀 뒤에 사람들 앞에 모습을 드러낸다. 그동안 기사는 떠나간 터, 그는 전쟁에서 싸워 임금의 적을 무찌르고, 많은 도시를 손에 넣으며, 숱한 전투에서 개선하고, 궁정으로 돌아와 늘 보던 그곳에서 자신의 연인을 보게 된다."],["The father dies, the princess inherits, and in two words the knight becomes king.","그리하여 그가 자신의 공로에 대한 보답으로 부모에게 공주를 아내로 청하기로 정해진다. 임금은 그가 누구인지 알지 못하므로 공주를 내주기를 꺼리나, 그럼에도 빼돌리든 다른 어떤 방법으로든 공주는 결국 그의 신부가 되고, 공주의 아버지도 그것을 큰 행운으로 여기게 되리라."],["And here comes in at once the bestowal of rewards upon his squire and all who have aided him in rising to so exalted a rank.","그도 그럴 것이, 이 기사가 어느 왕국인지 — 아무래도 지도에는 없을 듯하다만 — 그 어느 왕국의 용맹한 임금의 아들임이 밝혀지기 때문이다. 그 아버지가 죽으면 공주가 왕위를 물려받고, 두 마디로 말해 기사는 임금이 된다."],["He marries his squire to a damsel of the princess’s, who will be, no doubt, the one who was confidante in their amour, and is daughter of a very great duke.”","그리고 여기서 곧장 그의 종자와, 그가 그토록 드높은 자리에 오르도록 도운 모든 이에게 상을 내리는 일이 따라온다. 그는 자신의 종자를 공주의 시녀와 혼인시킬 것이니, 그 시녀는 분명 그들의 사랑에서 속내를 나누어 준 바로 그 처녀로, 어느 대단한 공작의 딸일 것이다.\""]],[["“That’s what I want, and no mistake about it!”","\"바로 그겁니다요. 두말할 것도 없습니다요!\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."],["“That’s what I’m waiting for; for all this, word for word, is in store for your worship under the title of the Knight of the Rueful Countenance.”","\"바로 그걸 제가 기다리고 있는 겁니다요. 이 모든 게 한 마디 한 마디 그대로, '슬픈 몰골의 기사'라는 칭호 아래 나리께 마련되어 있는 것이니 말입니다요.\""]],[["“Thou needst not doubt it, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote, “for in the same manner, and by the same steps as I have described here, knights-errant rise and have risen to be kings and emperors; all we want now is to find out what king, Christian or pagan, is at war and has a beautiful daughter; but there will be time enough to think of that, for, as I have told thee, fame must be won in other quarters before repairing to the court. There is another thing, too, that is wanting; for supposing we find a king who is at war and has a beautiful daughter, and that I have won incredible fame throughout the universe, I know not how it can be made out that I am of royal lineage, or even second cousin to an emperor; for the king will not be willing to give me his daughter in marriage unless he is first thoroughly satisfied on this point, however much my famous deeds may deserve it; so that by this deficiency I fear I shall lose what my arm has fairly earned. True it is I am a gentleman of known house, of estate and property, and entitled to the five hundred sueldos mulct; and it may be that the sage who shall write my history will so clear up my ancestry and pedigree that I may find myself fifth or sixth in descent from a king; for I would have thee know, Sancho, that there are two kinds of lineages in the world; some there be tracing and deriving their descent from kings and princes, whom time has reduced little by little until they end in a point like a pyramid upside down; and others who spring from the common herd and go on rising step by step until they come to be great lords; so that the difference is that the one were what they no longer are, and the others are what they formerly were not. And I may be of such that after investigation my origin may prove great and famous, with which the king, my father-in-law that is to be, ought to be satisfied; and should he not be, the princess will so love me that even though she well knew me to be the son of a water-carrier, she will take me for her lord and husband in spite of her father; if not, then it comes to seizing her and carrying her off where I please; for time or death will put an end to the wrath of her parents.”","\"의심할 것 없다, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"바로 이런 식으로, 그리고 내가 여기서 말한 것과 똑같은 단계를 밟아 편력 기사들이 임금과 황제의 자리에 오르고, 또 올라 왔으니 말이다. 이제 우리가 알아내야 할 것이라곤, 기독교도든 이교도든 전쟁을 벌이고 있고 아름다운 딸을 둔 임금이 누구인가 하는 것뿐이다. 허나 그것을 생각할 시간은 넉넉히 있으니, 앞서 말했듯 궁정으로 가기 전에 다른 곳에서 명성을 얻어야 하기 때문이다. 또 한 가지 모자란 것이 있으니, 전쟁 중에 아름다운 딸을 둔 임금을 우리가 찾아내고, 또 내가 온 우주에 걸쳐 믿기 어려운 명성을 얻었다 치더라도, 내가 왕가의 혈통이라거나 황제의 육촌이라도 된다는 것을 어떻게 입증할지 알 수가 없다. 임금은 이 점에 대해 먼저 충분히 만족하지 않고서는, 내 이름난 행적이 아무리 그럴 만한 값어치가 있다 해도 내게 딸을 아내로 내주려 하지 않을 테니 말이다. 그러니 이 결함 탓에, 내 팔이 정당하게 얻어 낸 것을 잃게 되지나 않을까 두렵다. 하기야 내가 이름난 가문의, 토지와 재산을 지닌, 오백 수엘도의 벌금을 받을 자격을 지닌 신사인 것은 사실이다. 어쩌면 내 이야기를 쓸 현자가 내 가계와 혈통을 그토록 환히 밝혀 주어, 내가 어느 임금의 오대(代)나 육대 후손임을 알게 될지도 모른다. 산초, 알아 두어라, 세상에는 두 종류의 혈통이 있느니, 그 하나는 임금과 군주에게서 가계를 끌어내고 이어받았으나 세월이 조금씩 깎아내려 마침내 거꾸로 선 피라미드처럼 한 점으로 끝나는 것이요, 다른 하나는 평민 무리에서 솟아나와 한 걸음 한 걸음 올라가 마침내 대귀족이 되는 것이다. 그러니 그 차이란, 한쪽은 지금은 아닌 것이 예전에는 그러했고, 다른 쪽은 예전에는 아니었던 것이 지금은 그러하다는 것이다. 나는 그러한 부류여서, 알아보고 나면 내 출신이 위대하고 이름난 것으로 드러날지도 모르니, 그것이라면 장차 내 장인이 될 임금도 마땅히 만족해야 하리라. 설령 그가 만족하지 않더라도, 공주는 나를 너무도 사랑하여 내가 물장수의 아들임을 빤히 안다 해도 그 아버지를 거슬러 나를 자신의 주인이요 남편으로 맞아들일 것이다. 만일 그렇지 않다면, 그때는 공주를 붙들어 내가 원하는 곳으로 데려가 버리는 데까지 이르리니, 세월이나 죽음이 그 부모의 노여움에 끝을 내어 줄 것이다.\""]],[["“It comes to this, too,” said Sancho, “what some naughty people say, ‘Never ask as a favour what thou canst take by force;’ though it would fit better to say, ‘A clear escape is better than good men’s prayers.’ I say so because if my lord the king, your worship’s father-in-law, will not condescend to give you my lady the princess, there is nothing for it but, as your worship says, to seize her and transport her. But the mischief is that until peace is made and you come into the peaceful enjoyment of your kingdom, the poor squire is famishing as far as rewards go, unless it be that the confidante damsel that is to be his wife comes with the princess, and that with her he tides over his bad luck until Heaven otherwise orders things; for his master, I suppose, may as well give her to him at once for a lawful wife.”","\"여기에 또,\" 산초가 말했다. \"심술궂은 사람들이 하는 그 말도 들어맞습니다요. '힘으로 빼앗을 수 있는 것을 은혜로 청하지 말라.' 하기야 '깨끗이 달아나는 것이 선한 이들의 기도보다 낫다'고 하는 편이 더 들어맞겠습니다요. 제가 이렇게 말씀드리는 까닭은, 나리의 장인이신 임금님께서 제 마님이신 공주님을 나리께 내주기를 마다하신다면, 나리 말씀대로 공주님을 붙들어 모셔 가는 수밖에 없기 때문입니다요. 다만 고약한 것은, 화친이 이루어지고 나리께서 왕국을 평화로이 누리시게 되기까지, 가엾은 종자는 상을 받기는커녕 쫄쫄 굶고 있다는 점입니다요. 다만 그의 아내가 될 그 속내를 나눈 시녀가 공주님과 함께 와서, 하늘이 달리 일을 마련해 주실 때까지 그녀와 함께 그 불운한 시절을 견뎌 내는 것이라면 또 모르겠습니다요. 제 생각엔 그 주인이 곧장 그녀를 정당한 아내로 종자에게 내주어도 무방할 듯하니 말입니다요.\""]],[["“Nobody can object to that,”","\"누구도 거기에 이의를 달 수 없지.\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“Then since that may be,”","\"그렇다면 그리 될 수 있는 일이니,\""],["said Sancho, “there is nothing for it but to commend ourselves to God, and let fortune take what course it will.”","산초가 말했다. \"우리 자신을 하느님께 맡기고, 운명이 가고 싶은 대로 가게 두는 수밖에 없겠습니다요.\""]],[["“God guide it according to my wishes and thy wants,”","\"하느님께서 내 소망과 네 바람에 따라 그것을 이끌어 주시기를.\""],["said Don Quixote, “and mean be he who thinks himself mean.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그리고 스스로를 미천하다 여기는 자야말로 미천한 자이니라.\""]],[["“In God’s name let him be so,”","\"하느님의 이름으로 그러하기를 빕니다요.\""],["said Sancho: “I am an old Christian, and to fit me for a count that’s enough.”","산초가 말했다. \"저는 오랜 기독교도이니, 백작이 되기에는 그것만으로 충분합니다요.\""]],[["“And more than enough for thee,”","\"네게는 충분하고도 남음이 있지.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “and even wert thou not, it would make no difference, because I being the king can easily give thee nobility without purchase or service rendered by thee, for when I make thee a count, then thou art at once a gentleman; and they may say what they will, but by my faith they will have to call thee ‘your lordship,’ whether they like it or not.”","\"설령 그렇지 않더라도 아무 차이가 없을 것이니, 임금이 된 내가 네 편에서 사들이거나 봉사한 바 없이도 손쉽게 너에게 귀족 신분을 줄 수 있기 때문이다. 내가 너를 백작으로 삼으면, 그 즉시 너는 신사가 되는 것이요, 사람들이 무어라 떠들든 간에, 맹세코 그들은 좋든 싫든 너를 '나리'라 불러야 할 것이다.\""]],[["“Not a doubt of it; and I’ll know how to support the tittle,”","\"의심할 것도 없습지요. 저도 그 칭허를 떠받칠 줄 알게 될 겁니다요.\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“Title thou shouldst say, not tittle,”","\"칭허가 아니라 칭호라 해야지.\""],["said his master.","주인이 말했다."]],[["“So be it,”","\"그러지요.\""],["answered Sancho.","산초가 대답했다."],["“I say I will know how to behave, for once in my life I was beadle of a brotherhood, and the beadle’s gown sat so well on me that all said I looked as if I was to be steward of the same brotherhood.","\"제 말씀은, 저도 처신할 줄 알게 되리란 겁니다요. 제 평생에 한 번, 어느 형제회의 관리인 노릇을 한 적이 있었는데, 그 관리인 예복이 어찌나 잘 어울렸던지 다들 제가 그 형제회의 집사라도 될 것 같다고들 했지요."],["What will it be, then, when I put a duke’s robe on my back, or dress myself in gold and pearls like a count?","그러니 제 등에 공작의 예복을 걸치거나, 백작처럼 금과 진주로 차려입는다면 어떻겠습니까요?"],["I believe they’ll come a hundred leagues to see me.”","사람들이 저를 보겠다고 백 레과 길을 마다 않고 올 겁니다요.\""]],[["“Thou wilt look well,”","\"보기에 좋겠지.\""],["said Don Quixote, “but thou must shave thy beard often, for thou hast it so thick and rough and unkempt, that if thou dost not shave it every second day at least, they will see what thou art at the distance of a musket shot.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 너는 수염을 자주 깎아야 하느니, 네 수염이 어찌나 빽빽하고 거칠고 텁수룩한지, 적어도 이틀에 한 번씩 깎지 않으면 머스킷 총알이 날아갈 거리에서도 사람들이 네 본바탕을 알아볼 것이기 때문이다.\""]],[["“What more will it be,” said Sancho, “than having a barber, and keeping him at wages in the house?","\"이발사 하나를 두고 봉급을 주어 집에 거느리는 것 말고 더 무슨 일이 있겠습니까요?\""],["and even if it be necessary, I will make him go behind me like a nobleman’s equerry.”","산초가 말했다. \"정 필요하다면 귀족의 마부장처럼 그를 제 뒤에 따라다니게 하겠습니다요.\""]],[["“Why, how dost thou know that noblemen have equerries behind them?”","\"아니, 귀족들이 마부장을 뒤에 거느린다는 것을 너는 어찌 아느냐?\""],["asked Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 물었다."]],[["“I will tell you,”","\"말씀드리지요.\" 산초가 대답했다."],["answered Sancho.","\"몇 해 전에 제가 한 달 동안 도성에 머문 적이 있었는데, 거기서 바람을 쐬러 나온 아주 자그마한 신사 하나를 보았습니다요. 사람들이 그분을 아주 대단한 분이라 하더군요."],["“Years ago I was for a month at the capital and there I saw taking the air a very small gentleman who they said was a very great man, and a man following him on horseback in every turn he took, just as if he was his tail.","그런데 그분이 가는 곳마다 한 사내가 말을 타고 꼭 그분의 꼬리라도 되는 양 뒤를 따라가지 뭡니까요. 저는 어째서 저 사내가 늘 뒤에만 가지 다른 분과 나란히 가지 않느냐고 물었습니다요."],["I asked why this man did not join the other man, instead of always going behind him; they answered me that he was his equerry, and that it was the custom with nobles to have such persons behind them, and ever since then I know it, for I have never forgotten it.”","사람들이 대답하기를, 그 사내가 그분의 마부장인데, 귀족들이 그런 사람을 뒤에 거느리는 것이 관례라더군요. 그때부터 저는 그것을 알게 되었고, 그 뒤로 한 번도 잊은 적이 없습니다요.\""]],[["“Thou art right,” said Don Quixote, “and in the same way thou mayest carry thy barber with thee, for customs did not come into use all together, nor were they all invented at once, and thou mayest be the first count to have a barber to follow him; and, indeed, shaving one’s beard is a greater trust than saddling one’s horse.”","\"네 말이 옳다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그와 마찬가지로 너도 이발사를 데리고 다닐 수 있느니, 관습이란 것이 한꺼번에 생겨난 것도 아니요, 모두가 일시에 만들어진 것도 아니기 때문이다. 그러니 너는 이발사를 뒤에 거느린 최초의 백작이 될 수도 있으렷다. 게다가 수염을 깎이는 일은 말에 안장을 얹는 일보다 더 큰 신임을 맡기는 일이니 말이다.\""]],[["“Let the barber business be my look-out,”","\"이발사 일은 제게 맡겨 두십시오.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho; “and your worship’s be it to strive to become a king, and make me a count.”","\"나리께서는 임금이 되시려 애쓰시고, 저를 백작으로 삼아 주시는 것이나 맡아 주십시오.\""]],[["“So it shall be,”","\"그리 될 것이다.\""],["answered Don Quixote, and raising his eyes he saw what will be told in the following chapter.","돈키호테가 대답하고는, 눈을 들어 다음 장에서 이야기될 것을 보았다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXII. OF THE FREEDOM DON QUIXOTE CONFERRED ON SEVERAL UNFORTUNATES WHO AGAINST THEIR WILL WERE BEING CARRIED WHERE THEY HAD NO WISH TO GO","# CHAPTER XXII."]],[["Cide Hamete Benengeli, the Arab and Manchegan author, relates in this most grave, high-sounding, minute, delightful, and original history that after the discussion between the famous Don Quixote of La Mancha and his squire Sancho Panza which is set down at the end of chapter twenty-one, Don Quixote raised his eyes and saw coming along the road he was following some dozen men on foot strung together by the neck, like beads, on a great iron chain, and all with manacles on their hands.","아랍인이자 라만차 사람인 작가 시데 아메테 베넹헬리는, 더없이 진중하고 장중하며 세밀하고 유쾌하며 독창적인 이 이야기 속에서 다음과 같이 전하고 있다. 제21장 끝머리에 적힌, 저 유명한 라만차의 돈키호테와 그의 종자 산초 판사 사이의 대화가 있은 뒤에, 돈키호테는 눈을 들어 자기가 따라가던 길을 따라 한 무리의 사람들이 오는 것을 보았으니, 열두어 명쯤 되는 사내들이 큼직한 쇠사슬에 목이 줄줄이 꿰여 마치 염주알처럼 엮인 채 걸어왔고 모두 손에 쇠고랑을 차고 있었다."],["With them there came also two men on horseback and two on foot; those on horseback with wheel-lock muskets, those on foot with javelins and swords, and as soon as Sancho saw them he said:","그들과 함께 말을 탄 사내 둘과 걸어오는 사내 둘도 있었는데, 말을 탄 자들은 톱니바퀴식 화승총을 들었고 걸어오는 자들은 투창과 칼을 지니고 있었다. 산초는 그들을 보자마자 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“That is a chain of galley slaves, on the way to the galleys by force of the king’s orders.”","\"저건 갤리선으로 끌려가는 노예 사슬입죠. 임금님 명령에 따라 억지로 갤리선으로 가는 겁니다요.\""]],[["“How by force?”","\"억지라니, 무슨 말이냐?\""],["asked Don Quixote; “is it possible that the king uses force against anyone?”","돈키호테가 물었다. \"임금이 누군가에게 강제를 쓴다는 것이 있을 수 있는 일이냐?\""]],[["“I do not say that,”","\"그런 말은 아닙죠.\""],["answered Sancho, “but that these are people condemned for their crimes to serve by force in the king’s galleys.”","산초가 대답했다. \"저자들은 제 죄 때문에 형을 받아 임금님의 갤리선에서 억지로 노역을 하게 된 자들이라는 말입니다요.\""]],[["“In fact,”","\"요컨대,\""],["replied Don Quixote, “however it may be, these people are going where they are taking them by force, and not of their own will.”","돈키호테가 받았다. \"어찌 되었든 저 사람들은 제 뜻이 아니라 끌려서 억지로 가는 것이로구나.\""]],[["“Just so,”","\"바로 그렇습죠.\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“Then if so,”","\"그렇다면,\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “here is a case for the exercise of my office, to put down force and to succour and help the wretched.”","\"여기야말로 내 직분을 발휘할 자리가 아니냐. 강제를 누르고 가련한 자들을 구원하고 돕는 일 말이다.\""]],[["“Recollect, your worship,”","\"잘 헤아려 보십시오, 나리.\""],["said Sancho, “Justice, which is the king himself, is not using force or doing wrong to such persons, but punishing them for their crimes.”","산초가 말했다. \"정의, 곧 임금님 그 자신은 저런 사람들에게 강제를 쓰거나 해를 끼치는 것이 아니라 그 죄를 벌하는 것입니다요.\""]],[["The chain of galley slaves had by this time come up, and Don Quixote in very courteous language asked those who were in custody of it to be good enough to tell him the reason or reasons for which they were conducting these people in this manner.","이즈음 갤리선 노예들의 사슬이 가까이 다가왔고, 돈키호테는 더없이 정중한 말씨로 그들을 호송하는 자들에게, 어찌하여 이 사람들을 이런 모양으로 끌고 가는지 그 까닭이나 사연을 부디 일러 달라고 청했다."],["One of the guards on horseback answered that they were galley slaves belonging to his majesty, that they were going to the galleys, and that was all that was to be said and all he had any business to know.","말을 탄 호위병 하나가 대답하기를, 이들은 폐하께 속한 갤리선 노예들이며 갤리선으로 가는 길이고, 할 말은 그것뿐이며 자기가 알아야 할 일도 그뿐이라 했다."]],[["“Nevertheless,”","\"그렇더라도,\""],["replied Don Quixote, “I should like to know from each of them separately the reason of his misfortune;”","돈키호테가 받았다. \"저들 한 사람 한 사람에게서 그 불행의 까닭을 따로따로 듣고 싶소.\""],["to this he added more to the same effect to induce them to tell him what he wanted so civilly that the other mounted guard said to him:","그러고는 같은 취지의 말을 더 보태어 자기가 알고자 하는 바를 일러 달라고 하도, 어찌나 공손하게 청했던지 다른 말 탄 호위병이 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“Though we have here the register and certificate of the sentence of every one of these wretches, this is no time to take them out or read them; come and ask themselves; they can tell if they choose, and they will, for these fellows take a pleasure in doing and talking about rascalities.”","\"이 가엾은 자들 하나하나의 판결 기록과 증서를 여기 갖고 있긴 하나, 지금은 그것을 꺼내거나 읽을 때가 아니오. 가서 직접 물어보시구려. 마음만 내키면 저들이 일러 줄 것이고, 또 그러기도 할 거요. 저런 작자들은 못된 짓을 하고 그것을 떠벌리는 데서 재미를 보는 자들이니 말이오.\""]],[["With this permission, which Don Quixote would have taken even had they not granted it, he approached the chain and asked the first for what offences he was now in such a sorry case.","이 허락이 있자 — 설령 허락하지 않았더라도 돈키호테는 그리했을 테지만 — 그는 사슬로 다가가 첫 번째 사내에게 무슨 죄를 지어 이런 딱한 처지가 되었느냐고 물었다."]],[["He made answer that it was for being a lover.","사내는 사랑을 한 죄라고 대답했다."]],[["“For that only?”","\"고작 그것 때문에?\""],["replied Don Quixote; “why, if for being lovers they send people to the galleys I might have been rowing in them long ago.”","돈키호테가 받았다. \"아니, 사랑을 했다고 사람을 갤리선으로 보낸다면 나도 진작에 거기서 노를 젓고 있어야 했겠구나.\""]],[["“The love is not the sort your worship is thinking of,” said the galley slave; “mine was that I loved a washerwoman’s basket of clean linen so well, and held it so close in my embrace, that if the arm of the law had not forced it from me, I should never have let it go of my own will to this moment; I was caught in the act, there was no occasion for torture, the case was settled, they treated me to a hundred lashes on the back, and three years of gurapas besides, and that was the end of it.”","\"나리께서 생각하시는 그런 사랑이 아닙니다요.\" 갤리선 노예가 말했다. \"제 사랑이란, 빨래꾼 아낙의 깨끗한 빨랫감이 든 바구니를 어찌나 사랑했던지, 그놈을 어찌나 꼭 끌어안았던지, 법의 팔뚝이 제게서 억지로 빼앗지 않았다면 지금 이 순간까지도 제 발로는 결코 놓지 않았을 그런 사랑이지요. 현행범으로 붙들렸으니 고문할 것도 없었고, 사건은 곧장 마무리되어 등짝에 채찍 백 대를 안기고 거기에 구라파스 삼 년까지 얹어 주더이다. 그게 끝이었습니다요.\""]],[["“What are gurapas?”","\"구라파스란 게 무엇이냐?\""],["asked Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 물었다."]],[["“Gurapas are galleys,”","\"구라파스란 곧 갤리선입죠.\""],["answered the galley slave, who was a young man of about four-and-twenty, and said he was a native of Piedrahita.","갤리선 노예가 대답했는데, 그는 스물네 살쯤 된 젊은이로 자기가 피에드라이타 태생이라 했다."]],[["Don Quixote asked the same question of the second, who made no reply, so downcast and melancholy was he; but the first answered for him, and said, “He, sir, goes as a canary, I mean as a musician and a singer.”","돈키호테가 두 번째 사내에게 같은 것을 물었으나, 그는 어찌나 풀이 죽고 시름에 잠겼던지 아무 대답이 없었다. 그러나 첫 번째 사내가 그를 대신해 이렇게 말했다. \"저자는요, 나리, 카나리아로 가는 겁니다요. 다시 말해 악사이자 가수로 말입죠.\""]],[["“What!”","\"무어라!\""],["said Don Quixote, “for being musicians and singers are people sent to the galleys too?”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"악사이고 가수라고 사람을 갤리선으로 보내기도 한단 말이냐?\""]],[["“Yes, sir,”","\"그렇습니다요, 나리.\""],["answered the galley slave, “for there is nothing worse than singing under suffering.”","갤리선 노예가 대답했다. \"고통 속에서 노래하는 것보다 더 고약한 일은 없으니까요.\""]],[["“On the contrary, I have heard say,”","\"그 반대로, 나는 이런 말을 들었다.\""],["said Don Quixote, “that he who sings scares away his woes.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"노래하는 자는 제 시름을 쫓아 버린다고.\""]],[["“Here it is the reverse,”","\"여기선 거꾸롭니다요.\""],["said the galley slave; “for he who sings once weeps all his life.”","갤리선 노예가 말했다. \"한 번 노래한 자는 평생을 우니까요.\""]],[["“I do not understand it,” said Don Quixote; but one of the guards said to him, “Sir, to sing under suffering means with the non sancta fraternity to confess under torture; they put this sinner to the torture and he confessed his crime, which was being a cuatrero, that is a cattle-stealer, and on his confession they sentenced him to six years in the galleys, besides two hundred lashes that he has already had on the back; and he is always dejected and downcast because the other thieves that were left behind and that march here ill-treat, and snub, and jeer, and despise him for confessing and not having spirit enough to say nay; for, say they, ‘nay’ has no more letters in it than ‘yea,’ and a culprit is well off when life or death with him depends on his own tongue and not on that of witnesses or evidence; and to my thinking they are not very far out.”","\"무슨 말인지 모르겠구나.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. 그러자 호위병 하나가 그에게 말했다. \"나리, 고통 속에서 노래한다는 건 저 못된 패거리들 사이에서 고문을 받고 자백한다는 뜻이오. 이 죄인은 고문을 당하자 제 죄를 자백했는데, 그 죄란 쿠아트레로, 곧 소도둑이었소. 자백을 했기에 등짝에 이미 받은 채찍 이백 대 말고도 갤리선 육 년형을 선고받은 거요. 저자가 늘 저렇게 의기소침하고 풀이 죽어 있는 건, 뒤에 남았거나 여기 함께 끌려가는 다른 도둑들이 자백을 했다고, 끝까지 아니라고 버틸 만한 배짱이 없었다고 저자를 학대하고 면박을 주고 비웃고 멸시하기 때문이오. 저들 말로는 '아니다'에 든 글자나 '그렇다'에 든 글자나 그 수가 같다는 거요. 게다가 죄인이란 제 목숨과 죽음이 증인이나 증거가 아니라 제 혀에 달려 있을 때야말로 형편이 좋은 법인데, 내 생각에도 저들 말이 그리 틀린 건 아니오.\""]],[["“And I think so too,”","\"내 생각도 그렇소.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["answered Don Quixote; then passing on to the third he asked him what he had asked the others, and the man answered very readily and unconcernedly, “I am going for five years to their ladyships the gurapas for the want of ten ducats.”","그러고는 세 번째 사내에게로 옮겨 가 다른 자들에게 물었던 것을 물으니, 그자는 거리낌도 무심하게도 선뜻 대답했다. \"전 두카도 열 닢이 없어서 그 마님이신 구라파스님께로 오 년을 가는 길입니다요.\""]],[["“I will give twenty with pleasure to get you out of that trouble,”","\"그 곤경에서 자네를 꺼내 줄 수만 있다면 내 기꺼이 스무 닢을 주겠네.\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“That,”","\"그건요,\" 갤리선 노예가 말했다."],["said the galley slave, “is like a man having money at sea when he is dying of hunger and has no way of buying what he wants; I say so because if at the right time I had had those twenty ducats that your worship now offers me, I would have greased the notary’s pen and freshened up the attorney’s wit with them, so that to-day I should be in the middle of the plaza of the Zocodover at Toledo, and not on this road coupled like a greyhound.","\"굶어 죽어 가면서 바다 한복판에서 돈을 쥐고 있어도 사고 싶은 걸 살 길이 없는 거나 마찬가집죠. 이렇게 말씀드리는 건, 나리께서 지금 제게 내미시는 그 두카도 스무 닢이 마침맞은 때에 제게 있었더라면, 그것으로 서기의 붓에 기름칠을 하고 변호사의 머리를 매끈하게 굴려 놓았을 테고, 그랬다면 오늘 저는 사냥개처럼 줄줄이 엮여 이 길 위에 있는 게 아니라 톨레도의 소코도베르 광장 한복판에 있었을 게 아닙니까."],["But God is great; patience--there, that’s enough of it.”","허나 하느님은 위대하시니, 참는 수밖에요. 자, 그 얘긴 그쯤 해 두지요.\""]],[["Don Quixote passed on to the fourth, a man of venerable aspect with a white beard falling below his breast, who on hearing himself asked the reason of his being there began to weep without answering a word, but the fifth acted as his tongue and said, “This worthy man is going to the galleys for four years, after having gone the rounds in ceremony and on horseback.”","돈키호테는 네 번째 사내에게로 옮겨 갔는데, 가슴께까지 흘러내린 흰 수염을 기른 풍채 점잖은 사내였다. 그는 어찌하여 여기 있게 되었느냐는 물음을 듣자 한 마디 대답도 없이 울기 시작했으나, 다섯 번째 사내가 그의 혀를 대신해 말했다. \"이 점잖은 어른은 격식을 갖춰 말에 태워져 거리를 한 바퀴 돌고 나서 갤리선으로 사 년을 가는 길입니다요.\""]],[["“That means,”","\"제가 알기로 그건,\""],["said Sancho Panza, “as I take it, to have been exposed to shame in public.”","산초 판사가 말했다. \"사람들 앞에서 망신을 당했다는 뜻이죠.\""]],[["“Just so,”","\"바로 그렇습죠.\" 갤리선 노예가 받았다."],["replied the galley slave, “and the offence for which they gave him that punishment was having been an ear-broker, nay body-broker; I mean, in short, that this gentleman goes as a pimp, and for having besides a certain touch of the sorcerer about him.”","\"그런 벌을 받게 된 죄목이란 귀 흥정꾼, 아니 몸뚱이 흥정꾼 노릇을 했다는 거지요. 한마디로 이 양반은 뚜쟁이로 가는 거고, 게다가 마법사 기운이 좀 묻어 있어서 그렇답니다요.\""]],[["“If that touch had not been thrown in,” said Don Quixote, “he would not deserve, for mere pimping, to row in the galleys, but rather to command and be admiral of them; for the office of pimp is no ordinary one, being the office of persons of discretion, one very necessary in a well-ordered state, and only to be exercised by persons of good birth; nay, there ought to be an inspector and overseer of them, as in other offices, and recognised number, as with the brokers on change; in this way many of the evils would be avoided which are caused by this office and calling being in the hands of stupid and ignorant people, such as women more or less silly, and pages and jesters of little standing and experience, who on the most urgent occasions, and when ingenuity of contrivance is needed, let the crumbs freeze on the way to their mouths, and know not which is their right hand. I should like to go farther, and give reasons to show that it is advisable to choose those who are to hold so necessary an office in the state, but this is not the fit place for it; some day I will expound the matter to some one able to see to and rectify it; all I say now is, that the additional fact of his being a sorcerer has removed the sorrow it gave me to see these white hairs and this venerable countenance in so painful a position on account of his being a pimp; though I know well there are no sorceries in the world that can move or compel the will as some simple folk fancy, for our will is free, nor is there herb or charm that can force it. All that certain silly women and quacks do is to turn men mad with potions and poisons, pretending that they have power to cause love, for, as I say, it is an impossibility to compel the will.”","\"그 기운만 끼지 않았더라면,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"한낱 뚜쟁이 노릇만으로는 갤리선에서 노를 저을 게 아니라 차라리 그것을 지휘하는 제독이 되어 마땅했을 것이다. 뚜쟁이라는 직분은 예사로운 것이 아니어서 사려 깊은 자들이 맡는 직분이며, 잘 다스려진 나라에서는 매우 긴요하여 좋은 가문 출신만이 행할 수 있는 것이기 때문이다. 아니, 다른 직분들에서처럼 그들을 감독하고 살피는 감찰관이 있어야 하고, 거래소의 중개인들처럼 정해진 정원도 있어야 한다. 그리하면 이 직분과 소임이 어리석고 무지한 자들의 손에 있어 생기는 숱한 폐단을 피할 수 있을 것이다. 가령 다소 모자란 여인들이나, 위상도 경험도 변변찮은 시동과 어릿광대 따위가 그러한데, 이들은 더없이 다급한 순간, 재치 있는 꾀가 필요할 때 빵 부스러기가 입으로 가는 길에서 얼어붙도록 내버려 두고는 제 오른손이 어느 쪽인지조차 알지 못한다. 나는 더 나아가, 나라에 이토록 긴요한 직분을 맡을 자들을 가려 뽑는 것이 마땅하다는 까닭을 들어 보이고 싶으나, 여기는 그럴 자리가 아니다. 언젠가 이 일을 살피고 바로잡을 만한 누군가에게 그 이치를 풀어 보이리라. 지금 내가 말할 것은 다만, 그가 마법사이기도 하다는 사실이 보태진 덕에, 한낱 뚜쟁이 노릇 때문에 이 흰 머리와 이 점잖은 얼굴이 이토록 고통스러운 처지에 놓인 것을 보며 내가 느꼈던 슬픔이 가셨다는 것이다. 비록 나는 세상에 어떤 마법도 — 어수룩한 자들이 상상하듯 — 사람의 의지를 움직이거나 강요할 수 없음을 잘 알고 있긴 하지만 말이다. 우리의 의지는 자유로워서 그것을 강제할 수 있는 약초도 부적도 없다. 어떤 어리석은 여인들과 돌팔이들이 하는 짓이라고는, 사랑을 일으킬 힘이 있는 척하며 미약과 독약으로 사내를 미치게 만드는 것뿐이다. 내가 말했듯이 의지를 강제한다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이기 때문이다.\""]],[["“It is true,” said the good old man, “and indeed, sir, as far as the charge of sorcery goes I was not guilty; as to that of being a pimp I cannot deny it; but I never thought I was doing any harm by it, for my only object was that all the world should enjoy itself and live in peace and quiet, without quarrels or troubles; but my good intentions were unavailing to save me from going where I never expect to come back from, with this weight of years upon me and a urinary ailment that never gives me a moment’s ease;” and again he fell to weeping as before, and such compassion did Sancho feel for him that he took out a real of four from his bosom and gave it to him in alms.","\"옳은 말씀입니다.\" 선량한 노인이 말했다. \"참으로 나리, 마법을 부렸다는 죄목에 관해서라면 저는 죄가 없었습니다요. 뚜쟁이 노릇을 했다는 것은 부인할 수 없으나, 저는 그것으로 누구에게 해를 끼친다고는 한 번도 생각지 않았습죠. 제 유일한 바람이란 온 세상 사람이 다툼이나 근심 없이 즐겁게 평온히 사는 것이었으니까요. 허나 그 선한 뜻도, 이 나이의 무게를 짊어지고, 잠시도 편할 새 없는 오줌소태를 안은 채, 다시는 돌아오지 못할 곳으로 끌려가는 것에서 저를 구해 주지는 못했습니다요.\" 그러고는 다시 아까처럼 울음을 터뜨리니, 산초가 어찌나 측은한 마음이 들었던지 품에서 사 레알짜리 한 닢을 꺼내 적선으로 그에게 주었다."]],[["Don Quixote went on and asked another what his crime was, and the man answered with no less but rather much more sprightliness than the last one.","돈키호테는 계속 나아가 또 다른 사내에게 무슨 죄를 지었느냐고 물으니, 그자는 앞의 사내 못지않게, 아니 오히려 훨씬 더 발랄하게 대답했다."]],[["“I am here because I carried the joke too far with a couple of cousins of mine, and with a couple of other cousins who were none of mine; in short, I carried the joke so far with them all that it ended in such a complicated increase of kindred that no accountant could make it clear: it was all proved against me, I got no favour, I had no money, I was near having my neck stretched, they sentenced me to the galleys for six years, I accepted my fate, it is the punishment of my fault; I am a young man; let life only last, and with that all will come right. If you, sir, have anything wherewith to help the poor, God will repay it to you in heaven, and we on earth will take care in our petitions to him to pray for the life and health of your worship, that they may be as long and as good as your amiable appearance deserves.”","\"제가 여기 있는 건요, 제 사촌 누이 둘하고, 또 제 사촌도 아닌 다른 처자 둘하고 농을 좀 지나치게 친 탓입니다요. 한마디로, 모두하고 농을 어찌나 지나치게 쳤던지, 그 끝에 친척이 어찌나 복잡하게 불어났는지 어떤 회계사도 셈을 분명히 가려낼 수 없을 지경이었지요. 죄다 제게 불리하게 입증되었고, 봐주는 이도 없었고, 돈도 없었으니, 하마터면 목이 늘어날 뻔했습니다요. 그래서 갤리선 육 년형을 선고받았고, 저는 제 운명을 받아들였습죠. 제 잘못에 대한 벌이니까요. 저는 젊은 놈이니, 목숨만 붙어 있으면 그것으로 다 잘 풀릴 겁니다요. 나리께서 가난한 자를 도울 무엇이라도 지니셨다면, 하느님께서 하늘에서 갚아 주실 것이고, 저희도 이 땅에서 기도드릴 때 나리의 목숨과 건강을 위해, 그 인자하신 풍채에 걸맞게 그것이 길고도 복되기를 빌어 드리도록 잊지 않겠습니다요.\""]],[["This one was in the dress of a student, and one of the guards said he was a great talker and a very elegant Latin scholar.","이자는 학생 차림을 하고 있었는데, 호위병 하나가 말하기를 그는 말솜씨가 대단하고 라틴어에 매우 능통한 학자라 했다."]],[["Behind all these there came a man of thirty, a very personable fellow, except that when he looked, his eyes turned in a little one towards the other.","이 모든 자들의 뒤로 서른쯤 된 사내 하나가 왔는데, 풍채가 아주 그럴듯했으나 다만 그가 무얼 볼 때면 두 눈이 약간 안쪽으로 한데 몰리는 것이 흠이었다."],["He was bound differently from the rest, for he had to his leg a chain so long that it was wound all round his body, and two rings on his neck, one attached to the chain, the other to what they call a “keep-friend”","그는 다른 자들과는 다르게 묶여 있었으니, 다리에는 어찌나 긴 사슬이 채워졌는지 그것이 온몸을 칭칭 감았고, 목에는 고리 둘이 걸려 하나는 사슬에 이어졌고 다른 하나는 이른바 '동무지킴이'"],["or “friend’s foot,” from which hung two irons reaching to his waist with two manacles fixed to them in which his hands were secured by a big padlock, so that he could neither raise his hands to his mouth nor lower his head to his hands.","또는 '동무의 발'이라 부르는 것에 이어졌는데, 거기서 쇠붙이 둘이 허리께까지 내려와 거기에 박힌 쇠고랑 둘에 그의 두 손이 큼직한 자물쇠로 채워져 있어, 손을 입까지 올릴 수도 고개를 손까지 숙일 수도 없었다."],["Don Quixote asked why this man carried so many more chains than the others.","돈키호테가 어찌하여 이 사내는 다른 자들보다 이토록 많은 사슬을 차고 있느냐고 물었다."],["The guard replied that it was because he alone had committed more crimes than all the rest put together, and was so daring and such a villain, that though they marched him in that fashion they did not feel sure of him, but were in dread of his making his escape.","호위병이 대답하기를, 그것은 이 사내 하나가 나머지 모두를 합친 것보다 더 많은 죄를 지었고, 어찌나 대담하고 악질인지 저런 꼴로 끌고 가면서도 도무지 안심이 되지 않아 그가 달아날까 두렵기 때문이라 했다."]],[["“What crimes can he have committed,”","\"갤리선으로 보내지는 것보다 더 무거운 벌을 받지 않았다면,\""],["said Don Quixote, “if they have not deserved a heavier punishment than being sent to the galleys?”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"대체 무슨 죄를 지었기에 그렇소?\""]],[["“He goes for ten years,”","\"저자는 십 년형으로 가는데,\" 호위병이 대답했다."],["replied the guard, “which is the same thing as civil death, and all that need be said is that this good fellow is the famous Gines de Pasamonte, otherwise called Ginesillo de Parapilla.”","\"그건 시민으로서의 죽음이나 다름없소. 더 말할 것도 없이, 이 잘난 작자가 바로 그 유명한 히네스 데 파사몬테, 일명 히네시요 데 파라피야라는 자요.\""]],[["“Gently, señor commissary,” said the galley slave at this, “let us have no fixing of names or surnames; my name is Gines, not Ginesillo, and my family name is Pasamonte, not Parapilla as you say; let each one mind his own business, and he will be doing enough.”","\"점잖게 좀 하시오, 위원 나리.\" 이 말에 갤리선 노예가 끼어들었다. \"이름이니 성이니 함부로 들먹이지 맙시다. 내 이름은 히네스지 히네시요가 아니고, 집안 성은 파사몬테지 당신 말처럼 파라피야가 아니오. 저마다 제 일이나 챙기면 그것으로 충분할 거요.\""]],[["“Speak with less impertinence, master thief of extra measure,”","\"버르장머리 없는 소리 작작해라, 이 분에 넘치는 도둑놈아.\""],["replied the commissary, “if you don’t want me to make you hold your tongue in spite of your teeth.”","위원이 받았다. \"내가 네놈 이빨이 어쩌든 입을 다물게 만들어 주랴?\""]],[["“It is easy to see,”","\"사람이 하느님 뜻대로 굴러가는 거야 빤히 보이는 노릇이지.\""],["returned the galley slave, “that man goes as God pleases, but some one shall know some day whether I am called Ginesillo de Parapilla or not.”","갤리선 노예가 되받았다. \"허나 내가 히네시요 데 파라피야로 불리는지 아닌지는 언젠가 누군가가 알게 될 거요.\""]],[["“Don’t they call you so, you liar?”","\"그렇게들 부르지 않더냐, 이 거짓말쟁이야?\""],["said the guard.","호위병이 말했다."]],[["“They do,” returned Gines, “but I will make them give over calling me so, or I will be shaved, where, I only say behind my teeth. If you, sir, have anything to give us, give it to us at once, and God speed you, for you are becoming tiresome with all this inquisitiveness about the lives of others; if you want to know about mine, let me tell you I am Gines de Pasamonte, whose life is written by these fingers.”","\"부르긴 하지.\" 히네스가 받았다. \"허나 내 그렇게 못 부르게 만들고 말 테요, 그러지 못하면 내 머리를 깎겠소. 어디를 깎을지는 내 이빨 사이에 담아 둘 뿐이오. 나리, 저희에게 주실 게 있거든 당장 주시고, 하느님께서 길을 인도하시기를 빕니다요. 남의 인생을 이렇게 꼬치꼬치 캐물으시니 지겨워지는군요. 제 인생을 알고 싶으시거든 일러 드리지요. 저는 히네스 데 파사몬테이고, 그 인생은 바로 이 손가락들로 쓰여 있습니다요.\""]],[["“He says true,”","\"맞는 말이오.\""],["said the commissary, “for he has himself written his story as grand as you please, and has left the book in the prison in pawn for two hundred reals.”","위원이 말했다. \"저자가 제 손으로 제 이야기를 더없이 멋들어지게 써 놓고는, 그 책을 감옥에 이백 레알에 저당 잡혀 놓고 왔으니 말이오.\""]],[["“And I mean to take it out of pawn,”","\"그리고 난 그걸 도로 찾아낼 작정이오.\""],["said Gines, “though it were in for two hundred ducats.”","히네스가 말했다. \"이백 두카도에 잡혔다 해도 말이오.\""]],[["“Is it so good?”","\"그토록 훌륭하단 말이냐?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“So good is it,”","\"어찌나 훌륭한지,\" 히네스가 대답했다."],["replied Gines, “that a fig for ‘Lazarillo de Tormes,’ and all of that kind that have been written, or shall be written compared with it: all I will say about it is that it deals with facts, and facts so neat and diverting that no lies could match them.”","\"그것에 견주면 '라사리요 데 토르메스'며, 그런 부류로 쓰였거나 앞으로 쓰일 책이란 죄다 시시할 뿐이오. 그것에 관해 한마디만 하자면, 그건 사실을 다룬 것인데, 그 사실들이 어찌나 깔끔하고 흥겨운지 어떤 거짓말도 그에 미치지 못한다는 거요.\""]],[["“And how is the book entitled?”","\"그래, 그 책의 제목은 무엇이냐?\""],["asked Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 물었다."]],[["“The ‘Life of Gines de Pasamonte,’”","\"'히네스 데 파사몬테의 생애'요.\""],["replied the subject of it.","그 주인공이 대답했다."]],[["“And is it finished?”","\"그래 다 끝났느냐?\""],["asked Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 물었다."]],[["“How can it be finished,”","\"어찌 끝이 났겠소,\""],["said the other, “when my life is not yet finished?","그자가 말했다. \"내 인생이 아직 끝나지 않았는데."],["All that is written is from my birth down to the point when they sent me to the galleys this last time.”","쓰인 것은 다 내가 태어난 때부터 이번에 마지막으로 갤리선에 보내진 대목까지요.\""]],[["“Then you have been there before?”","\"그렇다면 전에도 거기 있었더란 말이냐?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“In the service of God and the king I have been there for four years before now, and I know by this time what the biscuit and courbash are like,” replied Gines; “and it is no great grievance to me to go back to them, for there I shall have time to finish my book; I have still many things left to say, and in the galleys of Spain there is more than enough leisure; though I do not want much for what I have to write, for I have it by heart.”","\"하느님과 임금님을 섬기느라 전에도 사 년을 거기 있었고, 비스킷과 채찍이 어떤 것인지는 이제 알 만큼 알지요.\" 히네스가 대답했다. \"다시 그리로 돌아가는 게 그리 원통하지도 않은 게, 거기 가면 내 책을 마저 끝낼 짬이 날 테니까요. 아직 할 말이 많이 남았는데, 스페인의 갤리선에는 그러고도 남을 만큼 한가한 틈이 있거든요. 하긴 쓸 거리에 별 시간이 들지도 않소. 다 외고 있으니 말이오.\""]],[["“You seem a clever fellow,”","\"꽤 영리한 자로구나.\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“And an unfortunate one,”","\"그리고 불운한 자지요.\""],["replied Gines, “for misfortune always persecutes good wit.”","히네스가 받았다. \"불운이란 늘 좋은 재주를 따라다니며 괴롭히는 법이니까요.\""]],[["“It persecutes rogues,”","\"불운은 악당을 따라다니며 괴롭히지.\""],["said the commissary.","위원이 말했다."]],[["“I told you already to go gently, master commissary,” said Pasamonte; “their lordships yonder never gave you that staff to ill-treat us wretches here, but to conduct and take us where his majesty orders you; if not, by the life of—never mind—; it may be that some day the stains made in the inn will come out in the scouring; let everyone hold his tongue and behave well and speak better; and now let us march on, for we have had quite enough of this entertainment.”","\"점잖게 하라고 진작 일러뒀잖소, 위원 나리.\" 파사몬테가 말했다. \"저 윗분들이 그 지팡이를 당신한테 준 건 여기 가엾은 우리를 학대하라고 준 게 아니라, 폐하께서 명하신 곳으로 우릴 데려가라고 준 거요. 안 그러면, 맹세코—그만둡시다—. 언젠가 여관에서 진 얼룩이 빨래에서 드러날 날이 올지도 모르지. 다들 입 다물고 얌전히 굴고 말은 곱게 하시오. 자, 이제 가십시다. 이 흥겨운 구경은 실컷 했으니 말이오.\""]],[["The commissary lifted his staff to strike Pasamonte in return for his threats, but Don Quixote came between them, and begged him not to ill-use him, as it was not too much to allow one who had his hands tied to have his tongue a trifle free; and turning to the whole chain of them he said:","위원이 그 위협에 갚으려고 파사몬테를 치려 지팡이를 쳐들었으나, 돈키호테가 그 사이에 끼어들어 그를 학대하지 말라고 청하며, 두 손이 묶인 자가 혀나마 조금 자유롭게 두는 것이 그리 과한 일은 아니지 않으냐고 했다. 그러고는 사슬에 엮인 모두를 향해 돌아서서 말했다."]],[["“From all you have told me, dear brethren, I make out clearly that though they have punished you for your faults, the punishments you are about to endure do not give you much pleasure, and that you go to them very much against the grain and against your will, and that perhaps this one’s want of courage under torture, that one’s want of money, the other’s want of advocacy, and lastly the perverted judgment of the judge may have been the cause of your ruin and of your failure to obtain the justice you had on your side. All which presents itself now to my mind, urging, persuading, and even compelling me to demonstrate in your case the purpose for which Heaven sent me into the world and caused me to make profession of the order of chivalry to which I belong, and the vow I took therein to give aid to those in need and under the oppression of the strong. But as I know that it is a mark of prudence not to do by foul means what may be done by fair, I will ask these gentlemen, the guards and commissary, to be so good as to release you and let you go in peace, as there will be no lack of others to serve the king under more favourable circumstances; for it seems to me a hard case to make slaves of those whom God and nature have made free. Moreover, sirs of the guard,” added Don Quixote, “these poor fellows have done nothing to you; let each answer for his own sins yonder; there is a God in Heaven who will not forget to punish the wicked or reward the good; and it is not fitting that honest men should be the instruments of punishment to others, they being therein no way concerned. This request I make thus gently and quietly, that, if you comply with it, I may have reason for thanking you; and, if you will not voluntarily, this lance and sword together with the might of my arm shall compel you to comply with it by force.”","\"그대들이 내게 들려준 모든 것으로 미루어, 사랑하는 형제들이여, 나는 분명히 알겠노라. 그대들이 비록 제 잘못으로 벌을 받기는 했으나, 앞으로 견뎌야 할 그 벌이 그대들에게 그리 즐거운 것이 못 되며, 그대들의 뜻을 몹시 거슬러 마지못해 그리로 끌려간다는 것을. 그리고 어쩌면 이자는 고문 앞에서의 용기가 모자랐던 탓에, 저자는 돈이 없었던 탓에, 또 다른 자는 변호가 없었던 탓에, 끝으로는 판관의 그릇된 판단 탓에 그대들이 파멸하고, 그대들 편에 있던 정의를 얻지 못하게 되었을 것임을. 이 모든 것이 지금 내 마음에 떠올라, 하늘이 나를 세상에 보내시고 내가 속한 기사도의 수도회에 입문케 하신 그 목적을, 그리고 그 안에서 곤경에 처한 자들과 강자의 억압 아래 신음하는 자들을 돕겠노라 세운 그 맹세를 그대들의 경우에 증명해 보이라고 나를 재촉하고 설득하며 강요하는도다. 허나 정정당당히 해낼 수 있는 일을 비열한 수단으로 하지 않는 것이 신중함의 표라는 것을 내 아는바, 나는 이 신사분들, 곧 호위병들과 위원께 부디 그대들을 풀어 주어 평안히 가게 해 주시기를 청하겠노라. 더 유리한 처지에서 임금을 섬길 자들이야 얼마든지 있을 것이기 때문이다. 하느님과 자연이 자유롭게 지으신 자들을 노예로 삼는다는 것은 내게 가혹한 일로 여겨지기 때문이로다. 게다가, 호위병 나리들이여,\" 돈키호테가 덧붙였다. \"이 가엾은 자들이 그대들에게 무슨 짓을 한 것도 아니지 않소. 제 죄는 저마다 저세상에서 갚게 하시오. 하늘에 하느님이 계셔서 악한 자를 벌하고 선한 자에게 상 주기를 잊지 않으실 터, 정직한 사람이 자기와는 아무 상관도 없는 일에 끼어들어 남을 벌하는 도구가 되는 것은 마땅치 않소. 이 청을 내가 이토록 점잖고 조용히 드리는 것이니, 그대들이 응한다면 내 그대들에게 고마워할 까닭이 있겠고, 만약 스스로 응하지 않겠다면 이 창과 칼이, 그리고 내 팔의 힘이 그대들로 하여금 억지로라도 응하게 만들 것이오.\""]],[["“Nice nonsense!”","\"별 시답잖은 소리를 다 하는군!\""],["said the commissary; “a fine piece of pleasantry he has come out with at last!","위원이 말했다. \"마침내 그럴듯한 익살 한 자락을 늘어놓는구먼!"],["He wants us to let the king’s prisoners go, as if we had any authority to release them, or he to order us to do so!","우리한테 임금님의 죄수들을 놓아주라니, 마치 우리한테 저들을 풀어 줄 권한이라도 있는 것처럼, 또 자기한테 우리더러 그러라고 명할 권한이라도 있는 것처럼 말이오!"],["Go your way, sir, and good luck to you; put that basin straight that you’ve got on your head, and don’t go looking for three feet on a cat.”","가시던 길이나 가시오, 나리, 행운이나 비오. 머리에 얹은 그 대야나 똑바로 놓고, 고양이한테서 발 세 개 찾는 짓일랑 마시오.\""]],[["“‘Tis you that are the cat, rat, and rascal,”","\"고양이고 쥐새끼고 악당인 건 바로 네놈이다.\""],["replied Don Quixote, and acting on the word he fell upon him so suddenly that without giving him time to defend himself he brought him to the ground sorely wounded with a lance-thrust; and lucky it was for him that it was the one that had the musket.","돈키호테가 대꾸하고는, 말이 떨어지기 무섭게 어찌나 느닷없이 그에게 덤벼들었던지, 그자가 미처 막을 틈도 주지 않고 창으로 찔러 호되게 상처를 입혀 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다. 그것이 마침 화승총을 든 자였던 것이 그에게는 다행이었다."],["The other guards stood thunderstruck and amazed at this unexpected event, but recovering presence of mind, those on horseback seized their swords, and those on foot their javelins, and attacked Don Quixote, who was waiting for them with great calmness; and no doubt it would have gone badly with him if the galley slaves, seeing the chance before them of liberating themselves, had not effected it by contriving to break the chain on which they were strung.","다른 호위병들은 이 뜻밖의 사태에 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 어안이 벙벙하여 멍하니 서 있다가, 정신을 차리고는 말 탄 자들은 칼을, 걸어온 자들은 투창을 집어 들고 돈키호테에게 달려들었으니, 돈키호테는 더없이 침착하게 그들을 기다리고 있었다. 만약 갤리선 노예들이 눈앞에 제 몸을 풀어 자유로워질 기회가 온 것을 보고, 자기들을 엮은 사슬을 끊어 그것을 이뤄 내지 않았더라면, 돈키호테에게 일이 험하게 돌아갔으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다."],["Such was the confusion, that the guards, now rushing at the galley slaves who were breaking loose, now to attack Don Quixote who was waiting for them, did nothing at all that was of any use.","어찌나 어수선했던지, 호위병들은 이제 사슬을 끊고 풀려나는 갤리선 노예들에게 달려들었다가, 다시 자기들을 기다리는 돈키호테를 치려 했다가 하면서 무엇 하나 쓸모 있는 일을 해내지 못했다."],["Sancho, on his part, gave a helping hand to release Gines de Pasamonte, who was the first to leap forth upon the plain free and unfettered, and who, attacking the prostrate commissary, took from him his sword and the musket, with which, aiming at one and levelling at another, he, without ever discharging it, drove every one of the guards off the field, for they took to flight, as well to escape Pasamonte’s musket, as the showers of stones the now released galley slaves were raining upon them.","산초는 산초대로 히네스 데 파사몬테를 풀어 주는 데 손을 보탰으니, 그는 가장 먼저 자유롭게 족쇄를 벗은 몸으로 들판으로 뛰쳐나와, 땅에 쓰러진 위원에게 덤벼들어 그 칼과 화승총을 빼앗았다. 그러고는 이자를 겨누었다 저자에게 들이댔다 하며, 한 번도 쏘지는 않으면서 호위병들을 모조리 들판에서 몰아냈으니, 그들은 파사몬테의 화승총을 피하려고, 또 이제 막 풀려난 갤리선 노예들이 퍼붓는 돌 세례를 피하려고 줄행랑을 쳤기 때문이다."],["Sancho was greatly grieved at the affair, because he anticipated that those who had fled would report the matter to the Holy Brotherhood, who at the summons of the alarm-bell would at once sally forth in quest of the offenders; and he said so to his master, and entreated him to leave the place at once, and go into hiding in the sierra that was close by.","산초는 이 일이 몹시 걱정스러웠으니, 달아난 자들이 이 사건을 성스러운 형제단에 알릴 것이고, 그러면 형제단이 경종 소리에 곧장 출동하여 죄인들을 찾아 나설 것을 짐작했기 때문이다. 그는 주인에게 그렇게 말하며, 당장 이곳을 떠나 바로 가까이에 있는 산속에 몸을 숨기자고 간청했다."]],[["“That is all very well,” said Don Quixote, “but I know what must be done now;” and calling together all the galley slaves, who were now running riot, and had stripped the commissary to the skin, he collected them round him to hear what he had to say, and addressed them as follows: “To be grateful for benefits received is the part of persons of good birth, and one of the sins most offensive to God is ingratitude; I say so because, sirs, ye have already seen by manifest proof the benefit ye have received of me; in return for which I desire, and it is my good pleasure that, laden with that chain which I have taken off your necks, ye at once set out and proceed to the city of El Toboso, and there present yourselves before the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, and say to her that her knight, he of the Rueful Countenance, sends to commend himself to her; and that ye recount to her in full detail all the particulars of this notable adventure, up to the recovery of your longed-for liberty; and this done ye may go where ye will, and good fortune attend you.”","\"그것도 다 좋은 말이다만,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"지금 무엇을 해야 할지는 내가 잘 알고 있다.\" 그러고는 이제 마구 날뛰며 위원의 옷을 알몸이 되도록 벗겨 놓은 갤리선 노예들을 모조리 한데 불러 모아, 자기 할 말을 듣게끔 둘러서게 하고는 이렇게 일렀다. \"받은 은혜에 감사할 줄 아는 것은 좋은 가문 출신인 자의 도리이며, 하느님께 가장 거슬리는 죄 가운데 하나가 곧 배은망덕이니라. 내가 이리 말하는 것은, 여러분, 그대들이 이미 명백한 증거로써 내게서 받은 은혜를 보았기 때문이다. 그 보답으로 나는 바라노니, 또 그것이 내 기쁨이니, 그대들은 내가 그대들의 목에서 벗겨 준 그 사슬을 짊어지고 곧장 길을 떠나 엘 토보소 시로 가서, 거기서 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님 앞에 그대들 자신을 보이고, 그분의 기사, 곧 슬픈 몰골의 기사가 그분께 안부를 전한다고 여쭈어라. 그리고 그대들이 그토록 갈망하던 자유를 되찾기까지, 이 놀라운 모험의 자초지종을 빠짐없이 그분께 낱낱이 아뢰어라. 이 일을 마치고 나면 그대들은 어디로 가든 좋으니, 행운이 그대들과 함께하기를 비노라.\""]],[["Gines de Pasamonte made answer for all, saying, “That which you, sir, our deliverer, demand of us, is of all impossibilities the most impossible to comply with, because we cannot go together along the roads, but only singly and separate, and each one his own way, endeavouring to hide ourselves in the bowels of the earth to escape the Holy Brotherhood, which, no doubt, will come out in search of us. What your worship may do, and fairly do, is to change this service and tribute as regards the lady Dulcinea del Toboso for a certain quantity of ave-marias and credos which we will say for your worship’s intention, and this is a condition that can be complied with by night as by day, running or resting, in peace or in war; but to imagine that we are going now to return to the flesh-pots of Egypt, I mean to take up our chain and set out for El Toboso, is to imagine that it is now night, though it is not yet ten in the morning, and to ask this of us is like asking pears of the elm tree.”","히네스 데 파사몬테가 모두를 대신해 대답하여 말했다. \"나리, 저희를 구해 주신 분이여, 나리께서 저희에게 요구하시는 그것은 온갖 불가능 가운데서도 가장 불가능한 일입니다. 저희는 길을 따라 함께 갈 수가 없고 오직 하나씩 따로따로, 저마다 제 갈 길로 가야 하며, 틀림없이 저희를 찾아 나설 성스러운 형제단을 피해 땅속 뱃구레에라도 숨어들려 애써야 하니까요. 나리께서 하실 수 있는, 또 마땅히 하실 수 있는 일이란, 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님께 바칠 이 봉사며 공물을, 저희가 나리의 뜻을 위해 바칠 일정한 수의 아베마리아와 크레도로 바꾸어 주시는 것입니다. 이것이라면 밤이든 낮이든, 달아나든 쉬든, 평화로울 때든 전쟁 통이든 능히 행할 수 있는 조건이지요. 허나 저희가 이제 이집트의 고깃솥으로 돌아가리라, 곧 사슬을 도로 짊어지고 엘 토보소로 떠나리라 생각하시는 것은, 아침 열 시도 채 안 되었는데 지금이 밤이라 여기시는 것과 같으며, 저희에게 이런 것을 청하시는 것은 느릅나무에서 배를 따 달라는 것과 같습니다.\""]],[["“Then by all that’s good,”","\"그렇다면 좋은 것들 죄다 걸고 맹세코,\""],["said Don Quixote (now stirred to wrath), “Don son of a bitch, Don Ginesillo de Paropillo, or whatever your name is, you will have to go yourself alone, with your tail between your legs and the whole chain on your back.”","돈키호테가 (이제 노여움이 치솟아) 말했다. \"이 후레자식 같은 돈 히네시요 데 파로피요, 아니 네놈 이름이 무엇이든, 너는 꽁지를 사타구니에 끼고 그 사슬을 죄다 등에 진 채 혼자서 가야 하리라.\""]],[["Pasamonte, who was anything but meek (being by this time thoroughly convinced that Don Quixote was not quite right in his head as he had committed such a vagary as to set them free), finding himself abused in this fashion, gave the wink to his companions, and falling back they began to shower stones on Don Quixote at such a rate that he was quite unable to protect himself with his buckler, and poor Rocinante no more heeded the spur than if he had been made of brass.","파사몬테는 도무지 온순한 자가 아니었던지라(이때쯤에는 돈키호테가 자기들을 풀어 주는 따위의 변덕을 부린 것으로 보아 머리가 좀 온전치 못한 자라고 단단히 믿게 된 터였다), 이런 식으로 욕을 먹자 동료들에게 눈짓을 보냈고, 그들은 뒤로 물러서며 돈키호테에게 어찌나 빠른 속도로 돌을 퍼붓기 시작했던지 그는 도무지 작은 방패로 제 몸을 막을 수가 없었고, 가엾은 로시난테는 박차를 가해도 마치 놋쇠로 만들어진 것처럼 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다."],["Sancho planted himself behind his ass, and with him sheltered himself from the hailstorm that poured on both of them.","산초는 제 당나귀 뒤에 몸을 박고 그 뒤에 숨어 두 사람에게 쏟아지는 우박 같은 돌 세례를 피했다."],["Don Quixote was unable to shield himself so well but that more pebbles than I could count struck him full on the body with such force that they brought him to the ground; and the instant he fell the student pounced upon him, snatched the basin from his head, and with it struck three or four blows on his shoulders, and as many more on the ground, knocking it almost to pieces.","돈키호테는 그만큼 제 몸을 잘 가리지 못해, 내가 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 많은 자갈이 어찌나 세차게 온몸에 정통으로 박혔던지 그를 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨리고 말았다. 그가 쓰러지는 순간 학생이 그에게 덤벼들어 머리에서 대야를 낚아채더니, 그것으로 그의 어깨를 서너 번 후려치고 다시 땅바닥에 그만큼 더 내리쳐 거의 박살을 내 버렸다."],["They then stripped him of a jacket that he wore over his armour, and they would have stripped off his stockings if his greaves had not prevented them.","그러고는 그가 갑옷 위에 걸친 윗도리를 벗겨 냈고, 정강이받이만 없었더라면 양말까지 벗겨 갔을 것이다."],["From Sancho they took his coat, leaving him in his shirt-sleeves; and dividing among themselves the remaining spoils of the battle, they went each one his own way, more solicitous about keeping clear of the Holy Brotherhood they dreaded, than about burdening themselves with the chain, or going to present themselves before the lady Dulcinea del Toboso.","산초에게서는 윗옷을 빼앗아 셔츠 바람으로 남겨 두었다. 그러고는 싸움의 남은 노획물을 저희끼리 나눠 가진 뒤 저마다 제 갈 길로 가 버렸으니, 사슬을 짊어지거나 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님 앞에 가 제 몸을 보이는 일보다는, 두려워하는 성스러운 형제단을 멀찍이 피하는 데 더 마음을 썼던 것이다."],["The ass and Rocinante, Sancho and Don Quixote, were all that were left upon the spot; the ass with drooping head, serious, shaking his ears from time to time as if he thought the storm of stones that assailed them was not yet over; Rocinante stretched beside his master, for he too had been brought to the ground by a stone; Sancho stripped, and trembling with fear of the Holy Brotherhood; and Don Quixote fuming to find himself so served by the very persons for whom he had done so much.","당나귀와 로시난테, 산초와 돈키호테만이 그 자리에 덩그러니 남았다. 당나귀는 고개를 떨군 채 심각하게, 자기들을 덮친 돌 폭풍이 아직 끝나지 않았다고 여기기라도 하듯 이따금 귀를 흔들고 있었고, 로시난테는 저 역시 돌에 맞아 땅에 쓰러진 터라 주인 곁에 뻗어 있었으며, 산초는 옷이 벗겨진 채 성스러운 형제단이 두려워 벌벌 떨고 있었고, 돈키호테는 그토록 많은 것을 베풀어 준 바로 그 자들에게 이런 꼴을 당한 것에 분을 삭이지 못하고 있었다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXIII. OF WHAT BEFELL DON QUIXOTE IN THE SIERRA MORENA, WHICH WAS ONE OF THE RAREST ADVENTURES RELATED IN THIS VERACIOUS HISTORY","# CHAPTER XXIII."]],[["Seeing himself served in this way, Don Quixote said to his squire, “I have always heard it said, Sancho, that to do good to boors is to throw water into the sea.","이런 꼴을 당한 돈키호테가 종자에게 말했다. \"산초야, 나는 시골뜨기에게 선을 베푸는 것은 바다에 물을 끼얹는 것과 같다는 말을 늘 들어 왔다."],["If I had believed thy words, I should have avoided this trouble; but it is done now, it is only to have patience and take warning for the future.”","네 말을 믿었더라면 이런 곤욕은 피했을 것을. 허나 이미 벌어진 일이니, 그저 참고 견디며 앞일에 대한 교훈으로 삼는 수밖에 없구나.\""]],[["“Your worship will take warning as much as I am a Turk,” returned Sancho; “but, as you say this mischief might have been avoided if you had believed me, believe me now, and a still greater one will be avoided; for I tell you chivalry is of no account with the Holy Brotherhood, and they don’t care two maravedis for all the knights-errant in the world; and I can tell you I fancy I hear their arrows whistling past my ears this minute.”","\"나리께서 교훈을 삼으신다면야 제가 터키 사람이 되겠습죠.\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 나리 말씀처럼 저를 믿으셨더라면 이 화는 피할 수 있었던 것이니, 이번에는 제 말을 믿으십시오. 그러면 더 큰 화도 피할 수 있을 겝니다. 분명히 말씀드리지만 성스러운 형제단에게 기사도란 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 것이라, 그들은 세상의 어떤 편력 기사에게도 동전 두 닢어치의 관심도 두지 않습니다요. 그리고 분명히 말씀드리는데, 지금 이 순간에도 그들의 화살이 제 귓전을 스쳐 가며 휙휙거리는 소리가 들리는 것만 같습니다요.\""]],[["“Thou art a coward by nature, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “but lest thou shouldst say I am obstinate, and that I never do as thou dost advise, this once I will take thy advice, and withdraw out of reach of that fury thou so dreadest; but it must be on one condition, that never, in life or in death, thou art to say to anyone that I retired or withdrew from this danger out of fear, but only in compliance with thy entreaties; for if thou sayest otherwise thou wilt lie therein, and from this time to that, and from that to this, I give thee lie, and say thou liest and wilt lie every time thou thinkest or sayest it; and answer me not again; for at the mere thought that I am withdrawing or retiring from any danger, above all from this, which does seem to carry some little shadow of fear with it, I am ready to take my stand here and await alone, not only that Holy Brotherhood you talk of and dread, but the brothers of the twelve tribes of Israel, and the Seven Maccabees, and Castor and Pollux, and all the brothers and brotherhoods in the world.”","\"너는 천성이 겁쟁이로구나, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그러나 내가 고집불통이라느니, 네 충고를 한 번도 따르지 않는다느니 네가 말하지 못하도록 이번 한 번만큼은 네 충고를 받아들여, 네가 그토록 두려워하는 그 분노가 미치지 못하는 곳으로 물러나마. 다만 한 가지 조건이 있으니, 살아서든 죽어서든 결코 누구에게도 내가 두려워서 이 위험에서 물러났다거나 후퇴했다고 말해서는 안 된다. 오직 네 간청에 응하여 그리했다고만 해야 한다. 만약 네가 달리 말한다면 그것은 거짓이니, 지금부터 그때까지, 또 그때부터 지금까지, 네가 그것을 생각하거나 입에 올릴 때마다 나는 너를 거짓말쟁이라 부르고, 네가 거짓말을 하고 있으며 또 거짓말을 할 것이라 말하리라. 그리고 두 번 다시 내게 대꾸하지 말라. 내가 어떤 위험에서, 무엇보다도 약간의 두려움의 그림자를 드리우고 있는 듯한 이 위험에서 물러나거나 후퇴한다는 생각만으로도, 나는 여기 버티고 서서 홀로 기다릴 각오가 되어 있으니, 네가 떠들어 대며 두려워하는 그 성스러운 형제단뿐 아니라, 이스라엘 열두 지파의 형제들과 일곱 마카베오 형제와 카스토르와 폴룩스, 그리고 세상의 모든 형제와 형제단을 다 상대하겠노라.\""]],[["“Señor,” replied Sancho, “to retire is not to flee, and there is no wisdom in waiting when danger outweighs hope, and it is the part of wise men to preserve themselves to-day for to-morrow, and not risk all in one day; and let me tell you, though I am a clown and a boor, I have got some notion of what they call safe conduct; so repent not of having taken my advice, but mount Rocinante if you can, and if not I will help you; and follow me, for my mother-wit tells me we have more need of legs than hands just now.”","\"나리,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"물러나는 것은 도망가는 것이 아니고, 위험이 희망을 넘어설 때 기다리는 것은 지혜로운 일이 아닙니다요. 또한 오늘을 보전하여 내일을 도모하고 하루에 모든 것을 걸지 않는 것이 현명한 사람들의 도리입지요. 그리고 비록 제가 무지렁이에 시골뜨기이긴 하나, 사람들이 말하는 안전 통행이란 게 무엇인지 어렴풋이 알고는 있습니다요. 그러니 제 충고를 받아들이신 것을 후회하지 마시고, 하실 수 있거든 로시난테에 오르시고, 못 하시겠거든 제가 돕겠습니다요. 그리고 저를 따라오십시오. 지금은 손보다 다리가 더 요긴하다고 제 타고난 분별이 일러 주는군요.\""]],[["Don Quixote mounted without replying, and, Sancho leading the way on his ass, they entered the side of the Sierra Morena, which was close by, as it was Sancho’s design to cross it entirely and come out again at El Viso or Almodovar del Campo, and hide for some days among its crags so as to escape the search of the Brotherhood should they come to look for them.","돈키호테는 아무 대꾸 없이 말에 올랐고, 산초가 당나귀를 타고 앞장서서 바로 옆에 있던 시에라 모레나로 들어섰다. 산초의 계획은 그 산을 완전히 가로질러 엘 비소나 알모도바르 델 캄포로 빠져나가는 것이었고, 형제단이 그들을 찾으러 올 경우 그 추적을 피하기 위해 며칠 동안 바위틈에 숨어 있으려는 것이었다."],["He was encouraged in this by perceiving that the stock of provisions carried by the ass had come safe out of the fray with the galley slaves, a circumstance that he regarded as a miracle, seeing how they pillaged and ransacked.","갤리선 노예들과의 난리 통에서도 당나귀가 싣고 있던 식량이 무사히 빠져나온 것을 보고 그는 더욱 용기를 얻었으니, 그들이 어떻게 노략질하고 뒤졌는지를 생각하면 그것은 기적과도 같은 일이라 여겨졌던 것이다."]],[["That night they reached the very heart of the Sierra Morena, where it seemed prudent to Sancho to pass the night and even some days, at least as many as the stores he carried might last, and so they encamped between two rocks and among some cork trees; but fatal destiny, which, according to the opinion of those who have not the light of the true faith, directs, arranges, and settles everything in its own way, so ordered it that Gines de Pasamonte, the famous knave and thief who by the virtue and madness of Don Quixote had been released from the chain, driven by fear of the Holy Brotherhood, which he had good reason to dread, resolved to take hiding in the mountains; and his fate and fear led him to the same spot to which Don Quixote and Sancho Panza had been led by theirs, just in time to recognise them and leave them to fall asleep: and as the wicked are always ungrateful, and necessity leads to evildoing, and immediate advantage overcomes all considerations of the future, Gines, who was neither grateful nor well-principled, made up his mind to steal Sancho Panza’s ass, not troubling himself about Rocinante, as being a prize that was no good either to pledge or sell. While Sancho slept he stole his ass, and before day dawned he was far out of reach.","그날 밤 그들은 시에라 모레나의 한복판에 이르렀는데, 산초가 보기에는 그곳에서 밤을 지내고, 적어도 가지고 있는 식량이 버텨 주는 날수만큼은 며칠을 더 머무는 것이 분별 있는 일 같았다. 그리하여 그들은 두 바위 사이의 코르크나무들 가운데 자리를 잡았다. 그러나 참된 신앙의 빛을 지니지 못한 자들의 견해에 따르면 만사를 제 나름의 방식으로 지휘하고 배치하고 결정짓는다는 그 잔혹한 운명은, 돈키호테의 미덕과 광기 덕분에 쇠사슬에서 풀려난 저 이름난 악당이자 도둑인 히네스 데 파사몬테가, 마땅히 두려워할 만한 이유가 있는 성스러운 형제단에 대한 공포에 쫓겨 산속에 숨기로 마음먹게끔 일을 꾸몄다. 그리고 그의 운명과 두려움은, 돈키호테와 산초 판사가 저들의 운명과 두려움에 이끌려 온 바로 그 자리로 그를 인도하였으니, 마침 그들을 알아보고 잠들게 내버려 둘 만한 때였다. 사악한 자들은 언제나 배은망덕하고, 궁핍은 악행으로 이끌며, 눈앞의 이득은 미래에 대한 모든 고려를 압도하는 법이라, 감사할 줄도 모르고 양심도 없는 히네스는 산초 판사의 당나귀를 훔치기로 작정하였으니, 로시난테로 말하자면 저당 잡히거나 팔기에 아무런 쓸모가 없는 노획물이라 거들떠보지도 않았다. 산초가 잠든 사이 그는 당나귀를 훔쳤고, 날이 밝기도 전에 멀리 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 달아나 버렸다."]],[["Aurora made her appearance bringing gladness to the earth but sadness to Sancho Panza, for he found that his Dapple was missing, and seeing himself bereft of him he began the saddest and most doleful lament in the world, so loud that Don Quixote awoke at his exclamations and heard him saying, “O son of my bowels, born in my very house, my children’s plaything, my wife’s joy, the envy of my neighbours, relief of my burdens, and lastly, half supporter of myself, for with the six-and-twenty maravedis thou didst earn me daily I met half my charges.”","새벽의 여신 아우로라가 모습을 드러내어 대지에는 기쁨을 가져다주었으나 산초 판사에게는 슬픔을 안겼으니, 그가 자신의 잿빛 당나귀가 사라진 것을 알아챘기 때문이다. 당나귀를 잃은 자신을 깨닫고 그는 세상에서 가장 구슬프고 애처로운 통곡을 시작했는데, 어찌나 큰 소리였던지 그 외침에 돈키호테가 잠에서 깨어 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"오, 내 창자에서 나온 아들아, 바로 내 집에서 태어나, 내 자식들의 놀잇감이요, 내 아내의 기쁨이요, 이웃들의 부러움이요, 내 짐의 덜어 줌이요, 마지막으로 나 자신을 절반은 먹여 살리던 것아. 네가 매일 내게 벌어다 주던 스물여섯 닢의 마라베디로 내 비용의 절반을 충당했거늘.\""]],[["Don Quixote, when he heard the lament and learned the cause, consoled Sancho with the best arguments he could, entreating him to be patient, and promising to give him a letter of exchange ordering three out of five ass-colts that he had at home to be given to him.","돈키호테는 그 통곡을 듣고 그 까닭을 알게 되자, 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 말로 산초를 위로하며 참을 것을 당부하고, 집에 있는 다섯 마리의 새끼 당나귀 가운데 세 마리를 그에게 주라는 환어음을 써 주겠노라 약속했다."],["Sancho took comfort at this, dried his tears, suppressed his sobs, and returned thanks for the kindness shown him by Don Quixote.","산초는 이에 위안을 얻어 눈물을 닦고 흐느낌을 억누르며 돈키호테가 베푼 친절에 감사를 표했다."],["He on his part was rejoiced to the heart on entering the mountains, as they seemed to him to be just the place for the adventures he was in quest of.","돈키호테는 산속으로 들어서며 마음속 깊이 기뻐했으니, 그곳이야말로 그가 찾아 헤매던 모험에 꼭 알맞은 곳으로 여겨졌기 때문이다."],["They brought back to his memory the marvellous adventures that had befallen knights-errant in like solitudes and wilds, and he went along reflecting on these things, so absorbed and carried away by them that he had no thought for anything else.","그곳은 그와 같은 외딴곳과 황무지에서 편력 기사들에게 일어났던 놀라운 모험들을 그의 기억 속에 되살려 냈고, 그는 이런 것들을 곱씹으며 길을 갔으니, 어찌나 거기에 몰두하고 사로잡혔던지 다른 어떤 것도 생각할 겨를이 없었다."]],[["Nor had Sancho any other care (now that he fancied he was travelling in a safe quarter) than to satisfy his appetite with such remains as were left of the clerical spoils, and so he marched behind his master laden with what Dapple used to carry, emptying the sack and packing his paunch, and so long as he could go that way, he would not have given a farthing to meet with another adventure.","산초 또한 (이제 안전한 곳을 지나고 있다고 여겼으므로) 성직자에게서 노획한 물건 가운데 남은 것들로 식욕을 채우는 것 말고는 다른 걱정이 없었으니, 잿빛 당나귀가 싣고 다니던 짐을 짊어진 채 주인의 뒤를 따라 행군하며 자루를 비우고 제 배를 채웠다. 그렇게 갈 수만 있다면 그는 또 다른 모험을 만나는 데 한 푼도 내놓지 않았을 것이다."]],[["While so engaged he raised his eyes and saw that his master had halted, and was trying with the point of his pike to lift some bulky object that lay upon the ground, on which he hastened to join him and help him if it were needful, and reached him just as with the point of the pike he was raising a saddle-pad with a valise attached to it, half or rather wholly rotten and torn; but so heavy were they that Sancho had to help to take them up, and his master directed him to see what the valise contained. Sancho did so with great alacrity, and though the valise was secured by a chain and padlock, from its torn and rotten condition he was able to see its contents, which were four shirts of fine holland, and other articles of linen no less curious than clean; and in a handkerchief he found a good lot of gold crowns, and as soon as he saw them he exclaimed:","그렇게 분주한 와중에 그가 눈을 들어 보니 주인이 멈춰 서서 창끝으로 땅바닥에 놓인 어떤 부피 큰 물건을 들어 올리려 애쓰고 있었다. 이에 그는 필요하다면 거들려고 서둘러 주인에게 다가갔는데, 마침 주인이 창끝으로 안장 깔개를 들어 올리던 참이었고, 거기에는 여행용 가방이 하나 매달려 있었으니, 절반이, 아니 사실은 완전히 썩고 찢어진 것이었다. 그러나 어찌나 무거웠던지 산초가 들어 올리는 것을 거들어야 했고, 주인은 그에게 가방 속에 무엇이 들었는지 살펴보라고 일렀다. 산초는 몹시 민첩하게 그 일을 했는데, 가방이 쇠사슬과 자물쇠로 잠겨 있긴 했으나 찢기고 썩은 상태라 그 속에 든 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그 안에는 고운 네덜란드 삼베 셔츠 네 벌과, 그에 못지않게 진기하고 깨끗한 다른 아마포 물품들이 들어 있었고, 손수건 속에서는 제법 많은 금화를 발견했으니, 그것을 보자마자 그는 이렇게 외쳤다."]],[["“Blessed be all Heaven for sending us an adventure that is good for something!”","\"무언가에 쓸 만한 모험을 우리에게 보내 주신 온 하늘에 축복이 있기를!\""]],[["Searching further he found a little memorandum book richly bound; this Don Quixote asked of him, telling him to take the money and keep it for himself.","더 뒤져 보다가 그는 호화롭게 장정된 자그마한 수첩 하나를 찾아냈다. 돈키호테는 그것을 자신에게 달라고 하면서, 돈은 산초에게 가지라고 일렀다."],["Sancho kissed his hands for the favour, and cleared the valise of its linen, which he stowed away in the provision sack.","산초는 그 은혜에 그의 손에 입을 맞추고는, 가방 속의 아마포를 비워 식량 자루 속에 챙겨 넣었다."],["Considering the whole matter, Don Quixote observed:","이 모든 일을 곰곰이 헤아리고 나서 돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“It seems to me, Sancho--and it is impossible it can be otherwise-that some strayed traveller must have crossed this sierra and been attacked and slain by footpads, who brought him to this remote spot to bury him.”","\"내가 보기에는, 산초야 — 달리는 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이다만 — 어떤 길 잃은 나그네가 이 산을 가로질러 가다가 노상강도들에게 습격당해 죽임을 당했고, 그자들이 그를 묻으려고 이 외딴곳으로 끌고 온 것이 분명하다.\""]],[["“That cannot be,”","\"그럴 리가 없습니다요.\""],["answered Sancho, “because if they had been robbers they would not have left this money.”","산초가 대답했다. \"도둑들이었다면 이 돈을 남겨 두지 않았을 테니까요.\""]],[["“Thou art right,”","\"네 말이 옳다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “and I cannot guess or explain what this may mean; but stay; let us see if in this memorandum book there is anything written by which we may be able to trace out or discover what we want to know.”","\"그러니 이게 무슨 영문인지 나도 짐작도 설명도 못 하겠구나. 그러나 잠깐, 이 수첩에 우리가 알고자 하는 것을 추적하거나 알아낼 수 있을 만한 무언가가 적혀 있는지 보자꾸나.\""]],[["He opened it, and the first thing he found in it, written roughly but in a very good hand, was a sonnet, and reading it aloud that Sancho might hear it, he found that it ran as follows:","그가 수첩을 펼치니 거기서 맨 처음 발견한 것은, 거칠게 그러나 매우 훌륭한 필체로 쓰인 소네트 한 편이었다. 산초가 들을 수 있도록 큰 소리로 읽어 보니 그 내용은 다음과 같았다. 소네트"]],[["Or Love is lacking in intelligence, Or to the height of cruelty attains, Or else it is my doom to suffer pains Beyond the measure due to my offence.","사랑이 지혜를 결여하였거나, 아니면 잔혹함의 극치에 이르렀거나, 그도 아니면 내 죄에 마땅한 정도를 넘어선 고통을 겪는 것이 내 운명이리라. 허나 사랑이 신이라면, 그로부터 따라 나오는 것은 그가 모든 것을 안다는 것이니, 또한 확실히 남는 것은 어떤 신도 잔혹함을 사랑하지 않는다는 것이라."],["But if Love be a God, it follows thence That he knows all, and certain it remains No God loves cruelty; then who ordains This penance that enthrals while it torments?","그렇다면 누가 명하였단 말인가 괴롭히면서 사로잡는 이 고행을? 그것이 그대라 하는 것은 거짓이리라, 클로에여."],["It were a falsehood, Chloe, thee to name; Such evil with such goodness cannot live; And against Heaven I dare not charge the blame, I only know it is my fate to die.","그런 악이 그런 선과 더불어 살 수는 없으니. 또한 나는 감히 하늘에 그 탓을 돌리지 못하노라."],["To him who knows not whence his malady A miracle alone a cure can give.","오직 내가 죽는 것이 내 운명임을 알 뿐. 제 병이 어디서 왔는지 알지 못하는 자에게는 오직 기적만이 치유를 베풀 수 있으리."]],[["“There is nothing to be learned from that rhyme,”","\"그 시에서는 배울 게 아무것도 없네요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “unless by that clue there’s in it, one may draw out the ball of the whole matter.”","\"그 안에 들어 있는 실마리를 가지고 일의 전모라는 실타래를 풀어낼 수 있다면 또 모르겠지만요.\""]],[["“What clue is there?”","\"무슨 실마리가 있다는 게냐?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“I thought your worship spoke of a clue in it,”","\"나리께서 그 안에 무슨 실마리가 있다고 말씀하신 줄로 알았는데요.\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“I only said Chloe,”","\"나는 그저 클로에라고 했을 뿐이다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote; “and that no doubt, is the name of the lady of whom the author of the sonnet complains; and, faith, he must be a tolerable poet, or I know little of the craft.”","\"그것은 틀림없이 이 소네트의 작자가 한탄하고 있는 귀부인의 이름이렷다. 그리고 정말이지, 이 사람은 제법 쓸 만한 시인이거나, 아니면 내가 이 재주에 대해 아는 게 별로 없는 게로구나.\""]],[["“Then your worship understands rhyming too?”","\"그럼 나리께서는 시 짓는 것도 아십니까요?\""]],[["“And better than thou thinkest,” replied Don Quixote, “as thou shalt see when thou carriest a letter written in verse from beginning to end to my lady Dulcinea del Toboso, for I would have thee know, Sancho, that all or most of the knights-errant in days of yore were great troubadours and great musicians, for both of these accomplishments, or more properly speaking gifts, are the peculiar property of lovers-errant: true it is that the verses of the knights of old have more spirit than neatness in them.”","\"네가 생각하는 것보다 더 잘 안다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"처음부터 끝까지 운문으로 쓴 편지를 네가 내 귀부인 둘시네아 델 토보소에게 가져갈 때 알게 될 것이다. 산초야, 알아 두어라. 옛날의 편력 기사들은 모두, 아니 대부분이 위대한 음유 시인이요 위대한 악사였으니, 이 두 가지 재능은, 더 정확히 말하자면 천부의 재주는 편력하는 연인들에게 고유한 것이니라. 다만 옛 기사들의 시구에는 정교함보다 기백이 더 많이 깃들어 있는 것이 사실이지.\""]],[["“Read more, your worship,”","\"더 읽어 보십시오, 나리.\""],["said Sancho, “and you will find something that will enlighten us.”","산초가 말했다. \"그러면 우리를 깨우쳐 줄 무언가를 찾으실 겝니다요.\""]],[["Don Quixote turned the page and said, “This is prose and seems to be a letter.”","돈키호테는 책장을 넘기고 말했다. \"이건 산문이고, 편지인 것 같구나.\""]],[["“A correspondence letter, señor?”","\"왕래하던 편지 말씀입니까요, 나리?\""]],[["“From the beginning it seems to be a love letter,”","\"첫머리를 보니 연애편지인 듯하다.\""],["replied Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 대답했다."]],[["“Then let your worship read it aloud,”","\"그럼 나리께서 큰 소리로 읽어 주십시오.\""],["said Sancho, “for I am very fond of love matters.”","산초가 말했다. \"저는 연애 이야기를 무척 좋아하거든요.\""]],[["“With all my heart,”","\"기꺼이 그러마.\""],["said Don Quixote, and reading it aloud as Sancho had requested him, he found it ran thus:","돈키호테가 말하고는 산초가 청한 대로 큰 소리로 읽으니, 그 내용은 이러했다."]],[["Thy false promise and my sure misfortune carry me to a place whence the news of my death will reach thy ears before the words of my complaint.","그대의 거짓된 약속과 나의 확실한 불행이 나를 어떤 곳으로 데려가니, 그곳에서는 내 한탄의 말보다 내 죽음의 소식이 먼저 그대의 귀에 닿으리라."],["Ungrateful one, thou hast rejected me for one more wealthy, but not more worthy; but if virtue were esteemed wealth I should neither envy the fortunes of others nor weep for misfortunes of my own.","매정한 이여, 그대는 나를 버리고 나보다 더 부유한, 그러나 더 가치 있지는 않은 자를 택하였소. 그러나 미덕이 부로 여겨졌다면 나는 남의 재산을 부러워하지도, 내 자신의 불행을 슬퍼하지도 않았으리라."],["What thy beauty raised up thy deeds have laid low; by it I believed thee to be an angel, by them I know thou art a woman.","그대의 아름다움이 일으켜 세운 것을 그대의 행실이 무너뜨렸소. 그 아름다움으로 인해 나는 그대를 천사라 믿었으나, 그 행실로 인해 나는 그대가 한낱 여인임을 알게 되었소."],["Peace be with thee who hast sent war to me, and Heaven grant that the deceit of thy husband be ever hidden from thee, so that thou repent not of what thou hast done, and I reap not a revenge I would not have.","내게 전쟁을 보낸 그대에게 평화가 있기를. 그리고 그대 남편의 기만이 그대에게 영영 감추어지기를 하늘이 허락하시어, 그대가 한 일을 후회하지 않게 되고, 나 또한 원치 않는 복수를 거두지 않게 되기를."]],[["When he had finished the letter, Don Quixote said, “There is less to be gathered from this than from the verses, except that he who wrote it is some rejected lover;” and turning over nearly all the pages of the book he found more verses and letters, some of which he could read, while others he could not; but they were all made up of complaints, laments, misgivings, desires and aversions, favours and rejections, some rapturous, some doleful. While Don Quixote examined the book, Sancho examined the valise, not leaving a corner in the whole of it or in the pad that he did not search, peer into, and explore, or seam that he did not rip, or tuft of wool that he did not pick to pieces, lest anything should escape for want of care and pains; so keen was the covetousness excited in him by the discovery of the crowns, which amounted to near a hundred; and though he found no more booty, he held the blanket flights, balsam vomits, stake benedictions, carriers’ fisticuffs, missing alforjas, stolen coat, and all the hunger, thirst, and weariness he had endured in the service of his good master, cheap at the price; as he considered himself more than fully indemnified for all by the payment he received in the gift of the treasure-trove.","편지를 다 읽고 나서 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이것을 쓴 사람이 버림받은 어떤 연인이라는 것 말고는, 이 편지에서 얻어 낼 것은 그 시구에서보다도 적구나.\" 그러고는 책의 거의 모든 책장을 넘겨 보니 더 많은 시와 편지가 나왔는데, 어떤 것은 읽을 수 있었고 어떤 것은 읽을 수 없었다. 그러나 그것들은 모두 한탄과 비탄과 의혹과 갈망과 혐오, 호의와 거절로 이루어져 있었으니, 어떤 것은 황홀하고 어떤 것은 구슬펐다. 돈키호테가 책을 살펴보는 동안 산초는 가방을 살펴보았으니, 가방 전체와 깔개에서 뒤지고 들여다보고 헤집지 않은 구석이 하나도 없었고, 뜯어보지 않은 솔기 하나, 낱낱이 풀어 헤쳐 보지 않은 양털 뭉치 하나 없었으니, 소홀함과 게으름 탓에 무엇 하나라도 놓칠세라 그리한 것이었다. 백 닢에 가까운 그 금화의 발견이 그의 마음속에 그토록 날카로운 탐욕을 불러일으켰던 것이다. 비록 더 이상의 노획물은 찾지 못했으나, 그는 담요에 키질당한 일이며, 영약을 토해 낸 일이며, 몽둥이세례를 받은 축복이며, 짐꾼들의 주먹질이며, 잃어버린 안장 자루며, 도둑맞은 외투며, 자신의 훌륭한 주인을 섬기며 견뎌 낸 그 모든 굶주림과 갈증과 피로를, 그 대가치고는 싸게 친 셈이라 여겼다. 뜻밖에 얻은 보물이라는 선물로 받은 보상이 그 모든 것에 대해 충분하고도 남는 배상이라고 생각했기 때문이다."]],[["The Knight of the Rueful Countenance was still very anxious to find out who the owner of the valise could be, conjecturing from the sonnet and letter, from the money in gold, and from the fineness of the shirts, that he must be some lover of distinction whom the scorn and cruelty of his lady had driven to some desperate course; but as in that uninhabited and rugged spot there was no one to be seen of whom he could inquire, he saw nothing else for it but to push on, taking whatever road Rocinante chose--which was where he could make his way--firmly persuaded that among these wilds he could not fail to meet some rare adventure.","슬픈 몰골의 기사는 여전히 그 가방의 주인이 누구일지 몹시 알고 싶어 했으니, 소네트와 편지로 보아, 또 금화로 보아, 또 셔츠의 고운 질감으로 보아, 그가 어떤 지체 높은 연인으로서 귀부인의 멸시와 잔혹함이 그를 어떤 절망적인 처지로 몰아넣은 것이 분명하다고 짐작했다. 그러나 사람이 살지 않는 그 험준한 곳에는 물어볼 만한 사람이 아무도 보이지 않았으므로, 로시난테가 택하는 대로 — 그것은 곧 갈 수 있는 길이었다 — 어떤 길이든 따라 앞으로 나아가는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었으니, 이 황야에서라면 진기한 모험을 반드시 만나게 되리라 굳게 믿었던 것이다."],["As he went along, then, occupied with these thoughts, he perceived on the summit of a height that rose before their eyes a man who went springing from rock to rock and from tussock to tussock with marvellous agility.","그리하여 이런 생각에 잠겨 길을 가던 중, 그는 눈앞에 솟아오른 어느 높은 봉우리의 꼭대기에서, 놀라운 민첩함으로 바위에서 바위로, 풀숲에서 풀숲으로 뛰어다니는 한 사내를 보았다."],["As well as he could make out he was unclad, with a thick black beard, long tangled hair, and bare legs and feet, his thighs were covered by breeches apparently of tawny velvet but so ragged that they showed his skin in several places.","그가 알아볼 수 있는 한, 그 사내는 헐벗은 채였고, 짙은 검은 수염과 길고 헝클어진 머리에 다리와 발이 맨살이었으며, 허벅지는 황갈색 벨벳으로 보이는 바지로 가려져 있었으나 어찌나 누덕누덕하던지 여러 군데로 살갗이 드러나 보였다."]],[["He was bareheaded, and notwithstanding the swiftness with which he passed as has been described, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance observed and noted all these trifles, and though he made the attempt, he was unable to follow him, for it was not granted to the feebleness of Rocinante to make way over such rough ground, he being, moreover, slow-paced and sluggish by nature.","그는 맨머리였고, 앞서 말한 대로 날쌔게 지나갔음에도 슬픈 몰골의 기사는 이 모든 자잘한 것들을 관찰하고 새겨 두었다. 그러나 그를 뒤쫓아 보려 했지만 그럴 수가 없었으니, 그토록 험한 땅을 헤쳐 나가는 일은 로시난테의 허약함에 허락된 바가 아니었고, 게다가 그놈은 천성이 걸음이 느리고 굼떴기 때문이다."],["Don Quixote at once came to the conclusion that this was the owner of the saddle-pad and of the valise, and made up his mind to go in search of him, even though he should have to wander a year in those mountains before he found him, and so he directed Sancho to take a short cut over one side of the mountain, while he himself went by the other, and perhaps by this means they might light upon this man who had passed so quickly out of their sight.","돈키호테는 곧장 이자가 안장 깔개와 가방의 주인이라는 결론에 이르렀고, 그를 찾아 나서기로 마음먹었으니, 설령 그를 찾기까지 그 산속에서 일 년을 헤매야 한다 해도 그리하리라 작정했다. 그리하여 그는 산초에게 산의 한쪽 면을 가로질러 지름길로 가게 하고, 자신은 다른 쪽으로 갔으니, 어쩌면 이렇게 하면 그토록 빠르게 시야에서 사라진 그 사내와 마주칠 수 있을지도 몰랐다."]],[["“I could not do that,”","\"저는 그렇게 못 하겠습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “for when I separate from your worship fear at once lays hold of me, and assails me with all sorts of panics and fancies; and let what I now say be a notice that from this time forth I am not going to stir a finger’s width from your presence.”","\"나리 곁에서 떨어지면 곧장 두려움이 저를 사로잡고 온갖 공포와 망상으로 저를 덮치니까요. 그러니 지금부터는 나리 곁에서 손가락 한 마디 너비만큼도 움직이지 않겠다는 것을 제가 지금 드리는 말씀으로 알아 두십시오.\""]],[["“It shall be so,” said he of the Rueful Countenance, “and I am very glad that thou art willing to rely on my courage, which will never fail thee, even though the soul in thy body fail thee; so come on now behind me slowly as well as thou canst, and make lanterns of thine eyes; let us make the circuit of this ridge; perhaps we shall light upon this man that we saw, who no doubt is no other than the owner of what we found.”","\"그렇게 하자꾸나.\" 슬픈 몰골의 기사가 말했다. \"네가 내 용기에 기대려 하니 나는 무척 기쁘구나. 비록 네 몸속의 영혼이 너를 저버린다 해도 내 용기는 결코 너를 저버리지 않으리라. 그러니 이제 네가 할 수 있는 한 천천히 내 뒤를 따라오너라. 그리고 두 눈을 등불로 삼아라. 이 산등성이를 한 바퀴 돌아보자꾸나. 어쩌면 우리가 본 그 사내와 마주칠지도 모르니, 그자는 틀림없이 우리가 발견한 물건의 주인일 것이다.\""]],[["To which Sancho made answer, “Far better would it be not to look for him, for, if we find him, and he happens to be the owner of the money, it is plain I must restore it; it would be better, therefore, that without taking this needless trouble, I should keep possession of it until in some other less meddlesome and officious way the real owner may be discovered; and perhaps that will be when I shall have spent it, and then the king will hold me harmless.”","이에 산초가 대답했다. \"차라리 그를 찾지 않는 편이 훨씬 나을 겝니다요. 만약 그를 찾았는데 그가 그 돈의 주인이라면 제가 그것을 돌려줘야 하는 게 뻔하니까요. 그러니 이런 쓸데없는 수고를 들이지 말고, 다른 좀 덜 성가시고 덜 참견스러운 방법으로 진짜 주인이 밝혀질 때까지 제가 그걸 갖고 있는 편이 낫겠습니다요. 그리고 그게 밝혀질 때쯤이면 아마 제가 그걸 다 써 버린 뒤일 테고, 그러면 임금님께서도 저를 무사하게 봐주실 겝니다요.\""]],[["“Thou art wrong there, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “for now that we have a suspicion who the owner is, and have him almost before us, we are bound to seek him and make restitution; and if we do not see him, the strong suspicion we have as to his being the owner makes us as guilty as if he were so; and so, friend Sancho, let not our search for him give thee any uneasiness, for if we find him it will relieve mine.”","\"그건 네가 틀렸다, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"이제 우리가 그 주인이 누구인지 짐작하게 되었고, 그가 거의 우리 눈앞에 있으니, 우리는 마땅히 그를 찾아 돌려주어야 한다. 그리고 우리가 그를 보지 못한다 해도, 그가 주인이라는 강한 짐작만으로도 그가 실제로 주인인 것과 다름없이 우리를 죄인으로 만드는 것이니라. 그러니 산초야, 우리가 그를 찾는 일로 마음을 불편해하지 마라. 그를 찾는다면 내 마음이 한결 가벼워질 것이니 말이다.\""]],[["And so saying he gave Rocinante the spur, and Sancho followed him on foot and loaded, and after having partly made the circuit of the mountain they found lying in a ravine, dead and half devoured by dogs and pecked by jackdaws, a mule saddled and bridled, all which still further strengthened their suspicion that he who had fled was the owner of the mule and the saddle-pad.","이렇게 말하고 그는 로시난테에 박차를 가했고, 산초는 짐을 진 채 걸어서 그를 뒤따랐다. 그렇게 산을 얼마쯤 돌아보고 나서, 그들은 어느 협곡에 죽어 자빠진 채 개들에게 절반쯤 뜯어 먹히고 까마귀들에게 쪼인, 안장과 굴레를 갖춘 노새 한 마리를 발견했으니, 이 모든 것이 달아난 그 사내가 노새와 안장 깔개의 주인이라는 그들의 짐작을 한층 더 굳혀 주었다."]],[["As they stood looking at it they heard a whistle like that of a shepherd watching his flock, and suddenly on their left there appeared a great number of goats and behind them on the summit of the mountain the goatherd in charge of them, a man advanced in years.","그것을 바라보며 서 있는데, 양 떼를 지키는 목동이 부는 듯한 휘파람 소리가 들렸고, 갑자기 그들의 왼편에 수많은 염소 떼가 나타나더니, 그 뒤로 산꼭대기에 그것들을 지키는 염소지기가 모습을 드러냈으니, 나이가 지긋한 사내였다."],["Don Quixote called aloud to him and begged him to come down to where they stood.","돈키호테가 큰 소리로 그를 불러 자기들이 서 있는 곳으로 내려와 달라고 청했다."],["He shouted in return, asking what had brought them to that spot, seldom or never trodden except by the feet of goats, or of the wolves and other wild beasts that roamed around.","그러자 그가 마주 소리치며, 염소들의 발길이나, 이 근처를 어슬렁거리는 늑대며 다른 들짐승들의 발길 말고는 좀처럼, 아니 결코 밟힌 적 없는 이곳에 무슨 일로 왔느냐고 물었다."],["Sancho in return bade him come down, and they would explain all to him.","산초가 마주 내려오라고 청하며, 모든 것을 그에게 설명해 주겠노라고 했다."]],[["The goatherd descended, and reaching the place where Don Quixote stood, he said, “I will wager you are looking at that hack mule that lies dead in the hollow there, and, faith, it has been lying there now these six months; tell me, have you come upon its master about here?”","염소지기가 내려와 돈키호테가 서 있는 곳에 이르러 말했다. \"내기를 걸어도 좋소만, 댁들은 저 우묵한 데 죽어 자빠져 있는 짐 노새를 보고 있는 게로구먼. 정말이지 그놈은 벌써 여섯 달째 거기 누워 있다오. 어디 말해 보시오, 이 근처에서 그놈의 주인을 만났소?\""]],[["“We have come upon nobody,”","\"우리는 아무도 만나지 못했소.\""],["answered Don Quixote, “nor on anything except a saddle-pad and a little valise that we found not far from this.”","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"여기서 멀지 않은 데서 발견한 안장 깔개와 자그마한 가방 하나 말고는 아무것도 만나지 못했소.\""]],[["“I found it too,”","\"나도 그걸 발견했다오.\" 염소지기가 말했다."],["said the goatherd, “but I would not lift it nor go near it for fear of some ill-luck or being charged with theft, for the devil is crafty, and things rise up under one’s feet to make one fall without knowing why or wherefore.”","\"하지만 무슨 불운이 닥칠까, 아니면 도둑으로 몰릴까 두려워서 그걸 들어 올리지도, 가까이 가지도 않았소. 악마란 교활한 놈이라, 영문도 까닭도 모르게 사람을 넘어뜨리려고 발밑에서 물건들이 솟아오르는 법이니 말이오.\""]],[["“That’s exactly what I say,” said Sancho; “I found it too, and I would not go within a stone’s throw of it; there I left it, and there it lies just as it was, for I don’t want a dog with a bell.”","\"바로 제가 하는 말이 그겁니다요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"저도 그걸 발견했지만 돌 던질 거리 안으로도 가까이 안 갔습니다요. 거기 그대로 두었으니, 그건 있던 그대로 거기 있을 겝니다요. 저는 방울 달린 개는 원치 않으니까요.\""]],[["“Tell me, good man,”","\"여보시오, 좋은 양반,\""],["said Don Quixote, “do you know who is the owner of this property?”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"이 물건들의 주인이 누구인지 아시오?\""]],[["“All I can tell you,” said the goatherd, “is that about six months ago, more or less, there arrived at a shepherd’s hut three leagues, perhaps, away from this, a youth of well-bred appearance and manners, mounted on that same mule which lies dead here, and with the same saddle-pad and valise which you say you found and did not touch. He asked us what part of this sierra was the most rugged and retired; we told him that it was where we now are; and so in truth it is, for if you push on half a league farther, perhaps you will not be able to find your way out; and I am wondering how you have managed to come here, for there is no road or path that leads to this spot. I say, then, that on hearing our answer the youth turned about and made for the place we pointed out to him, leaving us all charmed with his good looks, and wondering at his question and the haste with which we saw him depart in the direction of the sierra; and after that we saw him no more, until some days afterwards he crossed the path of one of our shepherds, and without saying a word to him, came up to him and gave him several cuffs and kicks, and then turned to the ass with our provisions and took all the bread and cheese it carried, and having done this made off back again into the sierra with extraordinary swiftness. When some of us goatherds learned this we went in search of him for about two days through the most remote portion of this sierra, at the end of which we found him lodged in the hollow of a large thick cork tree. He came out to meet us with great gentleness, with his dress now torn and his face so disfigured and burned by the sun, that we hardly recognised him but that his clothes, though torn, convinced us, from the recollection we had of them, that he was the person we were looking for. He saluted us courteously, and in a few well-spoken words he told us not to wonder at seeing him going about in this guise, as it was binding upon him in order that he might work out a penance which for his many sins had been imposed upon him. We asked him to tell us who he was, but we were never able to find out from him: we begged of him too, when he was in want of food, which he could not do without, to tell us where we should find him, as we would bring it to him with all good-will and readiness; or if this were not to his taste, at least to come and ask it of us and not take it by force from the shepherds. He thanked us for the offer, begged pardon for the late assault, and promised for the future to ask it in God’s name without offering violence to anybody. As for fixed abode, he said he had no other than that which chance offered wherever night might overtake him; and his words ended in an outburst of weeping so bitter that we who listened to him must have been very stones had we not joined him in it, comparing what we saw of him the first time with what we saw now; for, as I said, he was a graceful and gracious youth, and in his courteous and polished language showed himself to be of good birth and courtly breeding, and rustics as we were that listened to him, even to our rusticity his gentle bearing sufficed to make it plain.","\"내가 말해 줄 수 있는 거라곤 이것뿐이오.\" 염소지기가 말했다. \"한 여섯 달쯤 전에, 더 됐을 수도 덜 됐을 수도 있소만, 여기서 한 삼 레과쯤 떨어진 어느 목동의 오두막에, 점잖은 외모와 몸가짐을 한 한 젊은이가 도착했는데, 바로 여기 죽어 있는 저 노새를 타고, 댁들이 발견했으되 손대지 않았다는 바로 그 안장 깔개와 가방을 지니고 있었소. 그가 우리에게 이 시에라에서 가장 험하고 외진 곳이 어디냐고 묻기에, 우리는 지금 우리가 있는 이곳이라고 일러 주었소. 사실이 그러하니, 여기서 반 레과만 더 들어가면 아마 빠져나갈 길을 찾지 못할 것이오. 나는 댁들이 어떻게 여기까지 올 수 있었는지 의아할 따름이오. 이곳으로 이어지는 길이며 오솔길이라곤 없으니 말이오. 어쨌든 그 젊은이는 우리 대답을 듣더니 몸을 돌려 우리가 가리킨 곳으로 향했는데, 우리는 모두 그의 잘생긴 외모에 매혹된 채, 그의 물음과 그가 시에라 쪽으로 떠나는 그 서두름에 의아해하며 남았소. 그러고는 그를 다시 보지 못했는데, 며칠 뒤 우리 목동 하나의 길과 마주쳤다오. 그런데 그에게 한마디 말도 없이 다가오더니 여러 차례 주먹질과 발길질을 하고는, 우리 식량을 실은 당나귀에게 돌아서서 그놈이 싣고 있던 빵과 치즈를 모조리 빼앗았소. 그러고는 그 일을 마치자마자 다시 어마어마한 날쌤으로 시에라 속으로 달아나 버렸소. 우리 염소지기 몇이 이 일을 알고 나서, 이틀가량 이 시에라의 가장 외진 곳을 두루 뒤지며 그를 찾아다녔는데, 끝에 가서 우리는 그가 크고 두꺼운 코르크나무의 빈 속에 깃들어 있는 것을 발견했소. 그가 매우 온순하게 우리를 맞으러 나왔는데, 옷은 이제 다 찢어졌고 얼굴은 햇볕에 어찌나 일그러지고 그을렸던지 우리는 그를 거의 알아보지 못했으나, 비록 찢어졌어도 그 옷이, 우리가 기억하던 것에 비추어, 그가 우리가 찾던 바로 그 사람임을 확인시켜 주었소. 그가 우리에게 정중히 인사하고는, 또박또박 몇 마디 말로, 자기가 이런 몰골로 돌아다니는 것을 의아해하지 말라 하면서, 자신의 많은 죄로 인해 부과된 고행을 치르기 위해 그렇게 하지 않을 수 없노라고 했소. 우리가 그에게 당신이 누구냐고 말해 달라 청했으나, 끝내 그에게서 알아낼 수가 없었소. 또 우리는 그에게, 먹을 것이 궁할 때면 — 그것 없이는 살 수 없으니 — 어디로 가면 그를 찾을 수 있을지 말해 달라 부탁했으니, 우리가 기꺼이 흔쾌히 가져다주겠노라 했소. 만약 그것이 그의 마음에 들지 않거든, 적어도 와서 우리에게 청하고 목동들에게서 강제로 빼앗지는 말아 달라고 했소. 그는 그 제안에 감사하며, 지난번 습격을 용서해 달라 빌고, 앞으로는 누구에게도 폭력을 쓰지 않고 하느님의 이름으로 청하겠노라 약속했소. 정해진 거처로 말하자면, 밤이 자기를 어디서 덮치든 우연이 마련해 주는 곳 말고는 다른 거처가 없다고 했소. 그리고 그의 말은 어찌나 비통한 통곡으로 끝맺었던지, 그의 말을 듣던 우리가 그와 함께 울지 않았다면 돌멩이나 다름없었을 것이오. 우리가 처음 보았을 때의 그와 지금 본 그의 모습을 견주어 보면서 말이오. 앞서 말했듯이 그는 우아하고 품위 있는 젊은이였고, 그 정중하고 세련된 말씨로 보아 좋은 가문 태생에 궁정의 교양을 갖춘 사람임을 드러냈으니, 그의 말을 듣던 우리 같은 촌뜨기들에게조차, 우리 같은 무지렁이에게조차 그의 점잖은 거동만으로 그것이 분명히 드러났소."]],[["“But in the midst of his conversation he stopped and became silent, keeping his eyes fixed upon the ground for some time, during which we stood still waiting anxiously to see what would come of this abstraction; and with no little pity, for from his behaviour, now staring at the ground with fixed gaze and eyes wide open without moving an eyelid, again closing them, compressing his lips and raising his eyebrows, we could perceive plainly that a fit of madness of some kind had come upon him; and before long he showed that what we imagined was the truth, for he arose in a fury from the ground where he had thrown himself, and attacked the first he found near him with such rage and fierceness that if we had not dragged him off him, he would have beaten or bitten him to death, all the while exclaiming, ‘Oh faithless Fernando, here, here shalt thou pay the penalty of the wrong thou hast done me; these hands shall tear out that heart of thine, abode and dwelling of all iniquity, but of deceit and fraud above all; and to these he added other words all in effect upbraiding this Fernando and charging him with treachery and faithlessness.","\"그런데 그가 이야기를 하던 중에 멈추더니 입을 다물고는, 한동안 눈을 땅바닥에 고정한 채 있었으니, 그동안 우리는 이 넋 나간 모습이 어찌 될지 마음 졸이며 가만히 서서 지켜보았소. 또한 적잖은 연민으로 그리하였으니, 그의 행동으로 보아 — 이제는 눈을 부릅뜨고 눈꺼풀 하나 까딱 않고 땅을 뚫어지게 응시하다가, 다시 눈을 감고는 입술을 꼭 다물고 눈썹을 치켜올리는 것을 보고 — 우리는 어떤 종류의 광기 발작이 그에게 닥쳤음을 똑똑히 알아챌 수 있었소. 그리고 머지않아 그는 우리가 짐작한 것이 사실임을 보여 주었으니, 자기가 몸을 던졌던 땅바닥에서 광분하여 벌떡 일어나, 곁에 있던 첫 번째 사람에게 어찌나 격노와 사나움으로 달려들었던지, 우리가 그를 떼어 놓지 않았다면 그자를 때려죽이거나 물어 죽였을 것이오. 그러면서 내내 이렇게 외쳤소. '오, 신의 없는 페르난도, 여기서, 바로 여기서 네가 내게 저지른 잘못의 대가를 치르리라. 이 두 손이 모든 죄악의, 그중에서도 무엇보다 기만과 사기의 거처요 보금자리인 그 네 심장을 뜯어내리라.' 그러고는 여기에 다른 말들을 덧붙였는데, 모두 이 페르난도를 책망하며 그를 배신과 신의 없음으로 고발하는 내용이었소."]],[["“We forced him to release his hold with no little difficulty, and without another word he left us, and rushing off plunged in among these brakes and brambles, so as to make it impossible for us to follow him; from this we suppose that madness comes upon him from time to time, and that some one called Fernando must have done him a wrong of a grievous nature such as the condition to which it had brought him seemed to show.","\"우리는 적잖은 어려움 끝에 그가 손을 놓게 만들었고, 그는 더 말 한마디 없이 우리를 떠나, 쏜살같이 달려가 이 덤불과 가시나무 속으로 뛰어들었으니, 우리로서는 그를 뒤따르는 것이 불가능했소. 이로 미루어 우리는 때때로 광기가 그에게 닥치고, 페르난도라는 어떤 자가 그를 그토록 비참한 처지로 몰아넣은 것으로 보아 무언가 심각한 잘못을 그에게 저질렀음이 틀림없다고 짐작하오."],["All this has been since then confirmed on those occasions, and they have been many, on which he has crossed our path, at one time to beg the shepherds to give him some of the food they carry, at another to take it from them by force; for when there is a fit of madness upon him, even though the shepherds offer it freely, he will not accept it but snatches it from them by dint of blows; but when he is in his senses he begs it for the love of God, courteously and civilly, and receives it with many thanks and not a few tears.","이 모든 것은 그 후로 그가 우리 길과 마주친 여러 번의 — 그런 일이 여러 번 있었소 — 경우에 확인되었으니, 어떤 때는 목동들에게 그들이 지닌 음식을 좀 달라고 청하기도 하고, 또 어떤 때는 그들에게서 강제로 빼앗기도 했소. 광기 발작이 그에게 닥쳤을 때는 목동들이 기꺼이 내주어도 받지 않고 매질을 해서 빼앗지만, 제정신일 때는 하느님의 사랑을 들어 정중하고 예의 바르게 청하며, 숱한 감사와 적잖은 눈물로 그것을 받소."],["And to tell you the truth, sirs,”","그리고 사실대로 말씀드리자면, 여러분,\""],["continued the goatherd, “it was yesterday that we resolved, I and four of the lads, two of them our servants, and the other two friends of mine, to go in search of him until we find him, and when we do to take him, whether by force or of his own consent, to the town of Almodovar, which is eight leagues from this, and there strive to cure him (if indeed his malady admits of a cure), or learn when he is in his senses who he is, and if he has relatives to whom we may give notice of his misfortune.","염소지기가 말을 이었다. \"우리가, 그러니까 나와 네 명의 젊은이가 — 둘은 우리 하인이고 다른 둘은 내 친구들인데 — 그를 찾을 때까지 찾아 나서기로, 그리고 찾으면 강제로든 그의 동의로든 그를 여기서 여덟 레과 떨어진 알모도바르 마을로 데려가, 거기서 (그의 병이 정말로 치유될 수 있는 것이라면) 그를 고쳐 보거나, 아니면 그가 제정신일 때 그가 누구인지, 그의 불행을 알릴 만한 친척이 있는지 알아보기로 결심한 것이 바로 어제였소."],["This, sirs, is all I can say in answer to what you have asked me; and be sure that the owner of the articles you found is he whom you saw pass by with such nimbleness and so naked.”","여러분, 이것이 여러분이 내게 물은 것에 대해 내가 말할 수 있는 전부요. 그리고 여러분이 발견한 물건들의 주인은 바로 그토록 날쌔게, 그토록 헐벗은 채로 지나가는 것을 본 그 사람임을 분명히 알아 두시오.\""]],[["For Don Quixote had already described how he had seen the man go bounding along the mountain side, and he was now filled with amazement at what he heard from the goatherd, and more eager than ever to discover who the unhappy madman was; and in his heart he resolved, as he had done before, to search for him all over the mountain, not leaving a corner or cave unexamined until he had found him.","돈키호테는 이미 그 사내가 산비탈을 따라 껑충껑충 뛰어가는 것을 보았노라 이야기한 터였고, 이제 염소지기에게서 들은 이야기에 놀라움으로 가득 차, 그 불행한 광인이 누구인지 그 어느 때보다도 간절히 알아내고 싶어졌다. 그리하여 그는 마음속으로, 전에도 그랬듯이, 그를 찾을 때까지 산 구석구석과 동굴 하나 살피지 않은 데 없이 온 산을 뒤져 그를 찾아내리라 결심했다."],["But chance arranged matters better than he expected or hoped, for at that very moment, in a gorge on the mountain that opened where they stood, the youth he wished to find made his appearance, coming along talking to himself in a way that would have been unintelligible near at hand, much more at a distance.","그러나 우연은 그가 예상하거나 바란 것보다 더 잘 일을 마련해 주었으니, 바로 그 순간, 그들이 서 있는 곳으로 열린 산속 협곡에서, 그가 찾고자 하던 그 젊은이가 모습을 드러냈으니, 가까이서도 알아듣기 어려운, 하물며 멀리서는 더욱 그러한 투로 혼잣말을 하며 다가오고 있었다."],["His garb was what has been described, save that as he drew near, Don Quixote perceived that a tattered doublet which he wore was amber-tanned, from which he concluded that one who wore such garments could not be of very low rank.","그의 차림새는 앞서 묘사한 그대로였으나, 다만 그가 가까이 다가옴에 따라 돈키호테는 그가 입은 누덕누덕한 윗옷이 호박색으로 무두질된 것임을 알아챘고, 이로부터 그런 옷을 입은 자가 그리 미천한 신분일 리 없다고 결론지었다."]],[["Approaching them, the youth greeted them in a harsh and hoarse voice but with great courtesy.","그들에게 다가온 젊은이는 거칠고 쉰 목소리로, 그러나 매우 정중하게 그들에게 인사했다."],["Don Quixote returned his salutation with equal politeness, and dismounting from Rocinante advanced with well-bred bearing and grace to embrace him, and held him for some time close in his arms as if he had known him for a long time.","돈키호테는 똑같이 정중하게 그 인사에 답하고는, 로시난테에서 내려 점잖은 거동과 우아함으로 그를 끌어안으러 나아가, 마치 오랫동안 알고 지내던 사이인 양 한동안 그를 두 팔로 꼭 끌어안았다."],["The other, whom we may call the Ragged One of the Sorry Countenance, as Don Quixote was of the Rueful, after submitting to the embrace pushed him back a little and, placing his hands on Don Quixote’s shoulders, stood gazing at him as if seeking to see whether he knew him, not less amazed, perhaps, at the sight of the face, figure, and armour of Don Quixote than Don Quixote was at the sight of him.","슬픈 몰골의 기사라 불리던 돈키호테처럼 가련한 몰골의 누더기 사내라 부를 만한 그 다른 이는, 그 포옹을 받아들인 뒤 그를 조금 밀쳐 내고는, 두 손을 돈키호테의 어깨에 얹은 채, 마치 그를 아는지 알아보려는 듯 그를 빤히 바라보았으니, 그가 돈키호테의 얼굴과 풍모와 갑옷을 보고 놀란 것은, 어쩌면 돈키호테가 그를 보고 놀란 것에 못지않았다."],["To be brief, the first to speak after embracing was the Ragged One, and he said what will be told farther on.","간단히 말하자면, 포옹 뒤에 먼저 입을 연 것은 누더기 사내였고, 그가 한 말은 뒤에 이야기될 것이다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXIV. IN WHICH IS CONTINUED THE ADVENTURE OF THE SIERRA MORENA","# CHAPTER XXIV."]],[["The history relates that it was with the greatest attention Don Quixote listened to the ragged knight of the Sierra, who began by saying:","이야기에 따르면, 돈키호테는 더없는 주의를 기울여 시에라의 누더기 기사가 하는 말에 귀를 기울였으니, 그 기사는 이렇게 운을 떼었다."]],[["“Of a surety, señor, whoever you are, for I know you not, I thank you for the proofs of kindness and courtesy you have shown me, and would I were in a condition to requite with something more than good-will that which you have displayed towards me in the cordial reception you have given me; but my fate does not afford me any other means of returning kindnesses done me save the hearty desire to repay them.”","\"분명히 말씀드리건대, 나리, 당신이 누구신지는 모르겠으나, 제게 베풀어 주신 친절과 예의의 증표에 감사드리며, 저를 다정하게 맞아 주신 그 후의에 대해 그저 호의가 아닌 무언가 더한 것으로 보답할 처지가 되었으면 하는 바람입니다. 허나 제 운명은 제게 베풀어진 친절을 갚을 다른 어떤 방도도 허락하지 않으니, 오로지 그것을 갚고자 하는 진심 어린 소망뿐입니다.\""]],[["“Mine,”","\"내 운명은,\""],["replied Don Quixote, “is to be of service to you, so much so that I had resolved not to quit these mountains until I had found you, and learned of you whether there is any kind of relief to be found for that sorrow under which from the strangeness of your life you seem to labour; and to search for you with all possible diligence, if search had been necessary.","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"당신께 도움이 되고자 하는 것이니, 어찌나 그러한지 나는 당신을 찾아내고, 또 그 기이한 삶으로 보아 당신이 겪고 있는 듯한 그 슬픔에 무슨 위안거리라도 있는지 당신에게서 알아내기 전까지는 이 산을 떠나지 않으리라 결심했고, 만일 찾는 일이 필요했다면 온갖 정성을 다해 당신을 찾았을 것이오."],["And if your misfortune should prove to be one of those that refuse admission to any sort of consolation, it was my purpose to join you in lamenting and mourning over it, so far as I could; for it is still some comfort in misfortune to find one who can feel for it.","그리고 만일 당신의 불행이 그 어떤 위로도 받아들이지 않는 종류의 것이라면, 내가 할 수 있는 한 당신과 함께 그것을 탄식하고 애도하는 것이 내 뜻이었소. 불행 가운데서도 그것을 함께 느껴 줄 이를 찾는 것은 그래도 얼마간의 위안이 되는 법이니 말이오."],["And if my good intentions deserve to be acknowledged with any kind of courtesy, I entreat you, señor, by that which I perceive you possess in so high a degree, and likewise conjure you by whatever you love or have loved best in life, to tell me who you are and the cause that has brought you to live or die in these solitudes like a brute beast, dwelling among them in a manner so foreign to your condition as your garb and appearance show.","그리고 만일 나의 선한 뜻이 어떤 예우로든 인정받을 만하다면, 부탁하건대, 나리, 당신이 그토록 높은 정도로 지니신 그것에 걸고, 또한 당신이 삶에서 사랑하거나 가장 사랑했던 그 무엇에 걸고 간청하노니, 당신이 누구이며, 무슨 까닭으로 이 황량한 곳에서 짐승처럼 살거나 죽기에 이르렀는지, 그리고 당신의 옷차림과 행색이 보여 주듯 당신의 신분에 그토록 어울리지 않는 모습으로 이런 곳에 머물게 되었는지를 내게 말해 주시오. 그리고 내 맹세하건대,\""],["And I swear,”","돈키호테가 덧붙였다."],["added Don Quixote, “by the order of knighthood which I have received, and by my vocation of knight-errant, if you gratify me in this, to serve you with all the zeal my calling demands of me, either in relieving your misfortune if it admits of relief, or in joining you in lamenting it as I promised to do.”","\"내가 받은 기사의 서약에 걸고, 또 편력 기사라는 내 천직에 걸고, 만일 당신이 이 청을 들어준다면, 내 소명이 요구하는 온 열의를 다해 당신을 섬기리니, 당신의 불행이 구제될 수 있다면 그것을 덜어 주는 일에서든, 아니면 내가 약속한 대로 당신과 함께 그것을 탄식하는 일에서든 그리하겠소.\""]],[["The Knight of the Thicket, hearing him of the Rueful Countenance talk in this strain, did nothing but stare at him, and stare at him again, and again survey him from head to foot; and when he had thoroughly examined him, he said to him:","덤불의 기사는 슬픈 몰골의 기사가 이런 식으로 말하는 것을 듣고는 그저 그를 빤히 바라보고, 또 빤히 바라보고, 다시 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그를 훑어볼 뿐이었다. 그리고 그를 샅샅이 살펴본 뒤 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“If you have anything to give me to eat, for God’s sake give it me, and after I have eaten I will do all you ask in acknowledgment of the goodwill you have displayed towards me.”","\"제게 먹을 것을 무엇이든 주실 수 있다면, 부디 그것을 주십시오. 먹고 난 다음에는 제게 베풀어 주신 호의에 보답하고자 당신이 청하시는 바를 모두 하겠습니다.\""]],[["Sancho from his sack, and the goatherd from his pouch, furnished the Ragged One with the means of appeasing his hunger, and what they gave him he ate like a half-witted being, so hastily that he took no time between mouthfuls, gorging rather than swallowing; and while he ate neither he nor they who observed him uttered a word.","산초는 자기 자루에서, 염소지기는 자기 주머니에서 누더기 사내에게 허기를 달랠 거리를 꺼내 주었고, 그는 그들이 준 것을 얼빠진 자처럼 먹어 댔는데, 어찌나 허겁지겁 먹는지 한입과 다음 한입 사이에 짬도 두지 않고 삼킨다기보다 게걸스레 처넣었다. 그가 먹는 동안 그도, 그를 지켜보던 이들도 한마디도 입을 떼지 않았다."],["As soon as he had done he made signs to them to follow him, which they did, and he led them to a green plot which lay a little farther off round the corner of a rock.","다 먹자마자 그는 자기를 따라오라는 손짓을 했고, 그들이 그렇게 하자 그는 그들을 조금 더 떨어진 바위 모퉁이를 돌아 있는 푸른 풀밭으로 데려갔다."],["On reaching it he stretched himself upon the grass, and the others did the same, all keeping silence, until the Ragged One, settling himself in his place, said:","그곳에 다다르자 그는 풀밭 위에 몸을 뉘었고, 다른 이들도 그리하여 모두가 침묵을 지켰는데, 마침내 누더기 사내가 제자리를 잡고 앉아 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“If it is your wish, sirs, that I should disclose in a few words the surpassing extent of my misfortunes, you must promise not to break the thread of my sad story with any question or other interruption, for the instant you do so the tale I tell will come to an end.”","\"여러분, 제 불행의 그 엄청난 깊이를 몇 마디로 털어놓기를 바라신다면, 어떤 질문이나 다른 끼어듦으로도 제 슬픈 이야기의 실타래를 끊지 않겠다고 약속하셔야 합니다. 그렇게 하시는 순간 제가 들려드리는 이야기는 끝나 버릴 테니까요.\""]],[["These words of the Ragged One reminded Don Quixote of the tale his squire had told him, when he failed to keep count of the goats that had crossed the river and the story remained unfinished; but to return to the Ragged One, he went on to say:","누더기 사내의 이 말은 돈키호테에게 자기 종자가 들려주었던 이야기, 곧 강을 건넌 염소들의 수를 헤아리지 못해 끝맺지 못한 채 남았던 그 이야기를 떠올리게 했다. 그러나 다시 누더기 사내로 돌아가자면, 그는 이렇게 말을 이었다."]],[["“I give you this warning because I wish to pass briefly over the story of my misfortunes, for recalling them to memory only serves to add fresh ones, and the less you question me the sooner shall I make an end of the recital, though I shall not omit to relate anything of importance in order fully to satisfy your curiosity.”","\"제가 이런 당부를 드리는 것은 제 불행의 이야기를 짤막하게 지나가고 싶어서입니다. 그것들을 기억에 되살리는 일은 그저 새로운 불행을 더할 뿐이니까요. 그리고 여러분이 제게 덜 물으실수록 저는 더 빨리 이 이야기를 끝맺을 수 있을 겁니다. 다만 여러분의 호기심을 온전히 채워 드리기 위해 무엇이든 중요한 것은 빠뜨리지 않고 들려드리겠습니다.\""]],[["Don Quixote gave the promise for himself and the others, and with this assurance he began as follows:","돈키호테는 자신과 다른 이들을 대신해 그러겠노라 약속했고, 이렇게 다짐을 받자 그는 다음과 같이 이야기를 시작했다."]],[["“My name is Cardenio, my birthplace one of the best cities of this Andalusia, my family noble, my parents rich, my misfortune so great that my parents must have wept and my family grieved over it without being able by their wealth to lighten it; for the gifts of fortune can do little to relieve reverses sent by Heaven.","\"제 이름은 카르데니오이고, 제가 태어난 곳은 이 안달루시아에서 손꼽히는 도시들 가운데 하나이며, 제 가문은 고귀하고, 제 부모는 부유하며, 제 불행은 어찌나 큰지 제 부모는 틀림없이 그것을 두고 눈물 흘렸을 것이고 제 가문은 그것을 슬퍼했을 것이나, 그 재력으로도 그것을 덜어 줄 수는 없었습니다. 하늘이 내린 역경을 덜어 주는 데에 운명의 선물이 할 수 있는 일이란 거의 없으니까요."],["In that same country there was a heaven in which love had placed all the glory I could desire; such was the beauty of Luscinda, a damsel as noble and as rich as I, but of happier fortunes, and of less firmness than was due to so worthy a passion as mine.","같은 그 고장에 사랑이 제가 바랄 수 있는 온갖 영광을 두어 둔 하늘이 하나 있었으니, 바로 루신다의 아름다움이 그러했습니다. 그녀는 저만큼이나 고귀하고 부유한 처녀였으나, 운명은 더 행복했고, 저의 그토록 값진 열정에 어울릴 만큼의 굳건함은 덜한 처녀였습니다."],["This Luscinda I loved, worshipped, and adored from my earliest and tenderest years, and she loved me in all the innocence and sincerity of childhood.","이 루신다를 저는 가장 어리고 여린 시절부터 사랑하고 흠모하고 떠받들었으며, 그녀도 어린아이의 온갖 순진함과 진심으로 저를 사랑했습니다. 우리 부모들은 우리의 마음을 알고 있었고, 그것을 알아차리고도 언짢아하지 않았으니, 그 마음이 무르익으면 마침내 우리 둘의 혼인으로 이어지리라는 것을 분명히 보았기 때문입니다."],["Our parents were aware of our feelings, and were not sorry to perceive them, for they saw clearly that as they ripened they must lead at last to a marriage between us, a thing that seemed almost prearranged by the equality of our families and wealth.","그것은 우리 두 집안과 재산이 대등하기에 거의 미리 정해진 일처럼 여겨졌습니다. 우리는 자랐고, 우리가 자라남에 따라 우리 사이의 사랑도 자라났으니, 그리하여 루신다의 아버지는 체면을 지키기 위해 제가 자기 집에 드나드는 것을 막아야겠다고 느꼈는데, 어쩌면 이 일로 시인들이 그토록 기린 저 티스베의 부모를 흉내 낸 셈이었습니다."],["We grew up, and with our growth grew the love between us, so that the father of Luscinda felt bound for propriety’s sake to refuse me admission to his house, in this perhaps imitating the parents of that Thisbe so celebrated by the poets, and this refusal but added love to love and flame to flame; for though they enforced silence upon our tongues they could not impose it upon our pens, which can make known the heart’s secrets to a loved one more freely than tongues; for many a time the presence of the object of love shakes the firmest will and strikes dumb the boldest tongue.","그런데 이 거절은 사랑에 사랑을, 불꽃에 불꽃을 더할 뿐이었으니, 그들이 우리의 혀에는 침묵을 강요할 수 있었어도 우리의 펜에는 그것을 강요할 수 없었기 때문입니다. 펜은 혀보다도 더 거리낌 없이 마음속 비밀을 사랑하는 이에게 알릴 수 있는 법이니, 사랑하는 대상이 곁에 있으면 그 존재가 더없이 굳건한 의지를 흔들어 놓고 가장 대담한 혀마저 말문을 막아 버리는 일이 여러 번이기 때문입니다."],["Ah heavens!","아, 하늘이시여!"],["how many letters did I write her, and how many dainty modest replies did I receive!","제가 그녀에게 얼마나 많은 편지를 썼으며, 또 얼마나 많은 곱고도 정숙한 답장을 받았던지요!"],["how many ditties and love-songs did I compose in which my heart declared and made known its feelings, described its ardent longings, revelled in its recollections and dallied with its desires!","제가 얼마나 많은 노래와 사랑의 시를 지었던지요. 그 안에서 제 마음은 자신의 감정을 토로하고 알렸으며, 자신의 타오르는 갈망을 그렸고, 자신의 추억에 흠뻑 젖었으며, 자신의 욕망과 어울려 노닐었습니다!"],["At length growing impatient and feeling my heart languishing with longing to see her, I resolved to put into execution and carry out what seemed to me the best mode of winning my desired and merited reward, to ask her of her father for my lawful wife, which I did.","마침내 더는 견디지 못하고 그녀를 보고픈 그리움에 제 마음이 시들어 가는 것을 느낀 저는, 제가 바라고 또 받아 마땅하다 여긴 보상을 얻을 최선의 길이라 생각된 바를 실행에 옮기기로 결심했으니, 곧 그녀의 아버지께 그녀를 제 정식 아내로 달라 청하는 것이었고, 저는 그렇게 했습니다."],["To this his answer was that he thanked me for the disposition I showed to do honour to him and to regard myself as honoured by the bestowal of his treasure; but that as my father was alive it was his by right to make this demand, for if it were not in accordance with his full will and pleasure, Luscinda was not to be taken or given by stealth.","이에 대한 그분의 대답인즉, 자신을 영예롭게 하고 또 그분의 보배를 받음으로써 제 자신이 영예를 입었다 여기려는 제 마음씨에는 감사하나, 제 아버지가 살아 계시니 이 청을 하는 것은 그분의 권리이며, 그것이 그분의 온전한 뜻과 흡족함에 따른 것이 아니라면 루신다는 몰래 데려가거나 내줄 수 있는 사람이 아니라는 것이었습니다. 저는 그분의 친절에 감사드리며 그분 말씀에 일리가 있다고, 또 제 아버지께서도 제가 말씀드리는 즉시 동의하시리라 생각했고, 그런 마음으로 바로 그 순간 제 소망이 무엇인지 알려 드리려 아버지께 갔습니다."],["I thanked him for his kindness, reflecting that there was reason in what he said, and that my father would assent to it as soon as I should tell him, and with that view I went the very same instant to let him know what my desires were.","그분이 계신 방에 들어섰을 때 저는 그분이 펼쳐진 편지 한 통을 손에 들고 계신 것을 보았는데, 제가 미처 한마디 꺼내기도 전에 그분은 그것을 제게 건네며 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다. '카르데니오야, 이 편지로 너는 리카르도 공작께서 너를 위해 베푸시려는 호의를 알게 될 것이다.'"],["When I entered the room where he was I found him with an open letter in his hand, which, before I could utter a word, he gave me, saying, ‘By this letter thou wilt see, Cardenio, the disposition the Duke Ricardo has to serve thee.’ This Duke Ricardo, as you, sirs, probably know already, is a grandee of Spain who has his seat in the best part of this Andalusia.","이 리카르도 공작은, 여러분도 이미 아실 테지만, 이 안달루시아에서 가장 좋은 곳에 영지를 둔 스페인의 대공입니다. 저는 그 편지를 받아 읽었는데, 어찌나 입에 발린 말로 쓰였던지 저 자신조차 제 아버지께서 그 안에 적힌 공작의 청에 응하지 않으시는 것이 잘못이라 느껴질 정도였습니다."],["I took and read the letter, which was couched in terms so flattering that even I myself felt it would be wrong in my father not to comply with the request the duke made in it, which was that he would send me immediately to him, as he wished me to become the companion, not servant, of his eldest son, and would take upon himself the charge of placing me in a position corresponding to the esteem in which he held me.","그 청인즉, 저를 즉시 자신에게 보내 달라는 것이었으니, 공작은 저를 자기 맏아들의 하인이 아니라 벗으로 삼고자 하셨고, 저를 높이 여기시는 그 존중에 걸맞은 자리에 앉히는 일을 손수 떠맡으시겠다는 것이었습니다. 그 편지를 읽으며 저는 목소리가 나오지 않았고, 제 아버지께서 이렇게 말씀하시는 것을 들었을 때는 더욱 그러했습니다."],["On reading the letter my voice failed me, and still more when I heard my father say, ‘Two days hence thou wilt depart, Cardenio, in accordance with the duke’s wish, and give thanks to God who is opening a road to thee by which thou mayest attain what I know thou dost deserve; and to these words he added others of fatherly counsel.","'카르데니오야, 이틀 뒤에 너는 공작의 뜻에 따라 떠날 것이니, 네가 받아 마땅하다고 내가 아는 바를 얻을 길을 너에게 열어 주시는 하느님께 감사드려라.' 그리고 이 말씀에 아버지로서의 다른 충고들을 덧붙이셨습니다."],["The time for my departure arrived; I spoke one night to Luscinda, I told her all that had occurred, as I did also to her father, entreating him to allow some delay, and to defer the disposal of her hand until I should see what the Duke Ricardo sought of me: he gave me the promise, and she confirmed it with vows and swoonings unnumbered.","제가 떠날 때가 다가왔습니다. 저는 어느 날 밤 루신다에게 말하여 일어난 모든 일을 들려주었고, 그녀의 아버지께도 그렇게 하면서 얼마간 미루어 주시기를, 그리고 리카르도 공작이 제게 무엇을 바라는지 알게 될 때까지 그녀의 혼사를 늦추어 주시기를 간청했습니다."],["Finally, I presented myself to the duke, and was received and treated by him so kindly that very soon envy began to do its work, the old servants growing envious of me, and regarding the duke’s inclination to show me favour as an injury to themselves.","그분은 제게 약속하셨고, 그녀는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 맹세와 까무러침으로 그것을 굳게 다짐했습니다. 마침내 저는 공작 앞에 나아갔고, 그분에게 어찌나 친절히 맞아지고 대접받았던지 머지않아 시기가 그 일을 시작했으니, 오래된 하인들이 저를 시샘하며 공작이 제게 호의를 보이려는 마음을 자기들에 대한 모욕으로 여겼습니다."],["But the one to whom my arrival gave the greatest pleasure was the duke’s second son, Fernando by name, a gallant youth, of noble, generous, and amorous disposition, who very soon made so intimate a friend of me that it was remarked by everybody; for though the elder was attached to me, and showed me kindness, he did not carry his affectionate treatment to the same length as Don Fernando.","그러나 저의 도착을 가장 기뻐한 이는 공작의 둘째 아들로, 페르난도라는 이름의 늠름한 젊은이였는데, 고귀하고 너그러우며 다정다감한 천성을 지녀 머지않아 저를 어찌나 가까운 벗으로 삼았던지 모두가 그것을 입에 올릴 정도였습니다. 맏아들도 제게 정을 두고 친절을 베풀었으나, 돈 페르난도만큼 그 다정한 대접을 멀리까지 끌고 가지는 않았기 때문입니다."],["It so happened, then, that as between friends no secret remains unshared, and as the favour I enjoyed with Don Fernando had grown into friendship, he made all his thoughts known to me, and in particular a love affair which troubled his mind a little.","그러던 차에, 벗들 사이에는 함께 나누지 못할 비밀이 없는 법이고, 또 제가 돈 페르난도에게서 누리던 총애가 우정으로 자라난 터라, 그는 제게 자신의 온갖 생각을 털어놓았는데, 특히 그의 마음을 조금 어지럽히던 어떤 연정에 관한 것이었습니다."],["He was deeply in love with a peasant girl, a vassal of his father’s, the daughter of wealthy parents, and herself so beautiful, modest, discreet, and virtuous, that no one who knew her was able to decide in which of these respects she was most highly gifted or most excelled.","그는 어느 농촌 처녀, 곧 자기 아버지의 영지에 딸린 백성으로 부유한 부모의 딸이며, 그 자신이 어찌나 아름답고 정숙하며 분별 있고 정숙한지 그녀를 아는 누구도 그녀가 이 가운데 어느 점에서 가장 빼어나게 타고났는지, 어느 점에서 가장 뛰어난지 가늠할 수 없는 처녀와 깊은 사랑에 빠져 있었습니다. 그 아리따운 농촌 처녀의 매력은 돈 페르난도의 열정을 어찌나 부추겼던지, 그는 자기 목적을 이루고 그녀의 정숙한 결심을 꺾기 위해 그녀에게 남편이 되겠다는 약조를 하기로 마음먹었으니, 다른 어떤 방법으로 시도하는 것은 불가능한 일을 시도하는 셈이었기 때문입니다."],["The attractions of the fair peasant raised the passion of Don Fernando to such a point that, in order to gain his object and overcome her virtuous resolutions, he determined to pledge his word to her to become her husband, for to attempt it in any other way was to attempt an impossibility.","우정으로 그에게 매여 있던 저는 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 좋은 논리와 가장 설득력 있는 본보기를 들어 그를 그런 길에서 말리고 단념시키려 애썼습니다. 그러나 제가 아무런 효과도 거두지 못하는 것을 알고는 그의 아버지 리카르도 공작께 이 일을 알리기로 결심했습니다."],["Bound to him as I was by friendship, I strove by the best arguments and the most forcible examples I could think of to restrain and dissuade him from such a course; but perceiving I produced no effect I resolved to make the Duke Ricardo, his father, acquainted with the matter; but Don Fernando, being sharp-witted and shrewd, foresaw and apprehended this, perceiving that by my duty as a good servant I was bound not to keep concealed a thing so much opposed to the honour of my lord the duke; and so, to mislead and deceive me, he told me he could find no better way of effacing from his mind the beauty that so enslaved him than by absenting himself for some months, and that he wished the absence to be effected by our going, both of us, to my father’s house under the pretence, which he would make to the duke, of going to see and buy some fine horses that there were in my city, which produces the best in the world.","그런데 돈 페르난도는 영리하고 약빠른지라 이를 미리 내다보고 염려했으니, 충실한 하인으로서의 제 도리상 제가 제 주군이신 공작의 명예에 그토록 어긋나는 일을 숨겨 두지 않으리라는 것을 알아챘기 때문입니다. 그리하여 저를 호리고 속이려고 그는, 자기를 그토록 노예로 만든 그 아름다움을 마음에서 지우는 데에 몇 달 동안 그곳을 떠나 있는 것보다 더 좋은 길은 없겠다고 제게 말했고, 그 떠나 있음을 우리 둘이 함께 제 아버지의 집으로 가는 식으로 이루고 싶다며, 그 핑계로 — 공작께는 이렇게 둘러대겠다고 했습니다 — 세상에서 가장 좋은 말을 내는 제 고향 도시에 있는 몇 마리 훌륭한 말을 보고 사러 간다고 하겠다는 것이었습니다."],["When I heard him say so, even if his resolution had not been so good a one I should have hailed it as one of the happiest that could be imagined, prompted by my affection, seeing what a favourable chance and opportunity it offered me of returning to see my Luscinda.","그가 그렇게 말하는 것을 들었을 때, 설령 그의 결심이 그토록 좋은 것이 아니었다 해도 저는 제 애정에 이끌려 그것을 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 행복한 결심 가운데 하나로 반겼을 것이니, 그것이 제게 제 루신다를 다시 볼 더없이 좋은 기회와 호기를 마련해 주는 것을 보았기 때문입니다."],["With this thought and wish I commended his idea and encouraged his design, advising him to put it into execution as quickly as possible, as, in truth, absence produced its effect in spite of the most deeply rooted feelings.","이런 생각과 바람으로 저는 그의 착상을 칭찬하고 그의 계획을 부추기며, 참으로 떠나 있음이 가장 깊이 뿌리내린 감정조차 누르고 효력을 발휘하니 되도록 빨리 그것을 실행에 옮기라고 권했습니다."],["But, as afterwards appeared, when he said this to me he had already enjoyed the peasant girl under the title of husband, and was waiting for an opportunity of making it known with safety to himself, being in dread of what his father the duke would do when he came to know of his folly.","그러나 나중에 드러난 바로는, 그가 제게 이런 말을 했을 때 그는 이미 남편이라는 명목으로 그 농촌 처녀를 차지한 뒤였고, 자기 어리석음을 알게 되었을 때 아버지 공작이 어찌하실지 두려워하며 자신에게 안전하게 그 일을 알릴 기회를 기다리고 있었던 것입니다."],["It happened, then, that as with young men love is for the most part nothing more than appetite, which, as its final object is enjoyment, comes to an end on obtaining it, and that which seemed to be love takes to flight, as it cannot pass the limit fixed by nature, which fixes no limit to true love--what I mean is that after Don Fernando had enjoyed this peasant girl his passion subsided and his eagerness cooled, and if at first he feigned a wish to absent himself in order to cure his love, he was now in reality anxious to go to avoid keeping his promise.","그러니 젊은이들에게 사랑이란 대개 욕정에 지나지 않아, 그 마지막 목적이 향락인지라 그것을 얻고 나면 끝나 버리고, 사랑처럼 보이던 것은 달아나 버리는 법이니, 그것은 자연이 정해 놓은 한계를 넘지 못하기 때문이며, 그 자연은 참사랑에는 아무런 한계도 두지 않는 법입니다. 제가 말하려는 바는, 돈 페르난도가 이 농촌 처녀를 차지하고 난 뒤 그의 열정은 가라앉고 그의 갈망은 식어 버렸으니, 처음에는 자기 사랑을 고치려 떠나 있고 싶은 척했으나, 이제는 실은 자기 약속을 지키지 않으려고 떠나기를 안달하고 있었다는 것입니다."]],[["“The duke gave him permission, and ordered me to accompany him; we arrived at my city, and my father gave him the reception due to his rank; I saw Luscinda without delay, and, though it had not been dead or deadened, my love gathered fresh life.","\"공작은 그에게 허락하고 제게 그를 따라가라 명하셨습니다. 우리는 제 고향 도시에 도착했고, 제 아버지는 그의 신분에 걸맞은 대접으로 그를 맞이하셨습니다."],["To my sorrow I told the story of it to Don Fernando, for I thought that in virtue of the great friendship he bore me I was bound to conceal nothing from him.","저는 지체 없이 루신다를 보았고, 제 사랑은 죽지도 시들지도 않았으나 새로운 생명을 얻었습니다. 슬프게도 저는 그 이야기를 돈 페르난도에게 들려주었으니, 그가 제게 품은 그 두터운 우정에 비추어 그에게는 무엇도 숨기지 말아야 한다고 여겼기 때문입니다."],["I extolled her beauty, her gaiety, her wit, so warmly, that my praises excited in him a desire to see a damsel adorned by such attractions.","저는 그녀의 아름다움과 명랑함과 재치를 어찌나 뜨겁게 추켜세웠던지, 제 찬사가 그의 마음에 그토록 매력으로 단장된 처녀를 보고 싶다는 욕망을 불러일으켰습니다."],["To my misfortune I yielded to it, showing her to him one night by the light of a taper at a window where we used to talk to one another.","불행히도 저는 그것에 굴복하여, 어느 날 밤 우리가 늘 서로 이야기를 나누던 창가에서 등잔불 빛으로 그녀를 그에게 보여 주었습니다. 그녀가 잠옷 차림으로 그에게 나타나자, 그녀는 그가 그때까지 보았던 모든 미인을 그의 기억에서 몰아내 버렸습니다."],["As she appeared to him in her dressing-gown, she drove all the beauties he had seen until then out of his recollection; speech failed him, his head turned, he was spell-bound, and in the end love-smitten, as you will see in the course of the story of my misfortune; and to inflame still further his passion, which he hid from me and revealed to Heaven alone, it so happened that one day he found a note of hers entreating me to demand her of her father in marriage, so delicate, so modest, and so tender, that on reading it he told me that in Luscinda alone were combined all the charms of beauty and understanding that were distributed among all the other women in the world.","그는 말문이 막히고, 머리가 어질해지고, 넋이 나갔으며, 마침내는 사랑에 사로잡혔으니, 이는 제 불행의 이야기가 흘러가는 가운데 보시게 될 것입니다. 그리고 그가 제게는 숨기고 오직 하늘에게만 드러낸 그의 열정을 한층 더 타오르게 하려는 듯, 어느 날 그는 우연히 그녀가 쓴 쪽지를 발견했는데, 거기에는 그녀의 아버지께 그녀를 아내로 달라 청해 달라는 간청이 어찌나 섬세하고 정숙하며 다정하게 적혀 있던지, 그것을 읽고 그는 제게 말하기를, 세상 모든 다른 여인들에게 나뉘어 있는 아름다움과 슬기의 온갖 매력이 오직 루신다 한 사람에게 한데 모여 있다고 했습니다."],["It is true, and I own it now, that though I knew what good cause Don Fernando had to praise Luscinda, it gave me uneasiness to hear these praises from his mouth, and I began to fear, and with reason to feel distrust of him, for there was no moment when he was not ready to talk of Luscinda, and he would start the subject himself even though he dragged it in unseasonably, a circumstance that aroused in me a certain amount of jealousy; not that I feared any change in the constancy or faith of Luscinda; but still my fate led me to forebode what she assured me against.","사실이고, 이제 와 저는 그것을 인정하건대, 돈 페르난도가 루신다를 칭송할 만한 합당한 까닭을 제가 알고 있었음에도, 그의 입에서 이런 찬사를 듣는 것은 제게 불안을 안겨 주었고, 저는 그를 두려워하며 또 그를 미덥지 못하게 여길 만한 까닭이 있어 그렇게 느끼기 시작했으니, 그가 루신다를 입에 올릴 채비가 되어 있지 않은 순간이 없었고, 어울리지 않게 억지로 끌어들여서라도 그 화제를 자기가 꺼내곤 했기 때문인데, 이는 제 안에 얼마간의 질투를 불러일으킨 사정이었습니다. 루신다의 한결같음이나 신의에 어떤 변함이 있을까 두려워서가 아니었습니다."],["Don Fernando contrived always to read the letters I sent to Luscinda and her answers to me, under the pretence that he enjoyed the wit and sense of both.","그래도 제 운명은 그녀가 제게 그런 일은 없으리라 다짐한 바로 그것을 예감하게 만들었습니다. 돈 페르난도는 제가 루신다에게 보내는 편지와 그녀가 제게 보내는 답장을, 그 둘의 재치와 분별을 즐긴다는 핑계로 언제나 용케 읽어 보았습니다."],["It so happened, then, that Luscinda having begged of me a book of chivalry to read, one that she was very fond of, Amadis of Gaul—”","그러던 차에, 루신다가 제게 자기가 무척 좋아하는 기사도 책 한 권, 곧 『아마디스 데 가울라』를 읽게 빌려 달라고 청한 일이 있었는데—\""]],[["Don Quixote no sooner heard a book of chivalry mentioned, than he said:","돈키호테는 기사도 책이 언급되는 것을 듣자마자 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“Had your worship told me at the beginning of your story that the Lady Luscinda was fond of books of chivalry, no other laudation would have been requisite to impress upon me the superiority of her understanding, for it could not have been of the excellence you describe had a taste for such delightful reading been wanting; so, as far as I am concerned, you need waste no more words in describing her beauty, worth, and intelligence; for, on merely hearing what her taste was, I declare her to be the most beautiful and the most intelligent woman in the world; and I wish your worship had, along with Amadis of Gaul, sent her the worthy Don Rugel of Greece, for I know the Lady Luscinda would greatly relish Daraida and Garaya, and the shrewd sayings of the shepherd Darinel, and the admirable verses of his bucolics, sung and delivered by him with such sprightliness, wit, and ease; but a time may come when this omission can be remedied, and to rectify it nothing more is needed than for your worship to be so good as to come with me to my village, for there I can give you more than three hundred books which are the delight of my soul and the entertainment of my life;--though it occurs to me that I have not got one of them now, thanks to the spite of wicked and envious enchanters;--but pardon me for having broken the promise we made not to interrupt your discourse; for when I hear chivalry or knights-errant mentioned, I can no more help talking about them than the rays of the sun can help giving heat, or those of the moon moisture; pardon me, therefore, and proceed, for that is more to the purpose now.”","\"댁께서 이야기를 시작할 적에 루신다 아가씨가 기사도 책을 좋아한다고 내게 일러 주셨더라면, 그녀의 빼어난 지성을 내게 새겨 주는 데에 다른 어떤 칭송도 필요치 않았을 것이오. 그토록 즐거운 읽을거리에 대한 취미가 없었다면 댁이 그리는 그 탁월함을 지녔을 리 없으니 말이오. 그러니 나로 말하자면, 댁은 그녀의 아름다움과 됨됨이와 지성을 그리는 데에 더는 말을 허비할 필요가 없소. 그저 그녀의 취미가 무엇인지를 듣는 것만으로도 나는 그녀가 세상에서 가장 아름답고 가장 지성 있는 여인이라 선언하는 바이오. 그리고 댁께서 『아마디스 데 가울라』와 더불어 그녀에게 저 훌륭한 『그리스의 돈 루헬』도 보내 주셨더라면 좋았을 것을. 루신다 아가씨라면 다라이다와 가라야를, 또 목동 다리넬의 슬기로운 말들을, 그리고 그가 어찌나 발랄하고 재치 있고 거침없이 노래하고 읊었던지 그 감탄스러운 전원시의 시구들을 크게 즐겼으리라는 것을 내가 아니까 말이오. 허나 이 빠뜨림을 바로잡을 때가 언젠가 올지도 모르니, 그것을 바로잡는 데에는 댁께서 부디 나와 함께 내 마을로 와 주시기만 하면 더 바랄 것이 없소. 거기서 나는 댁께 내 영혼의 기쁨이요 내 삶의 즐거움인 책을 삼백 권도 넘게 드릴 수 있으니까요. 허나 사악하고 시기심 많은 마법사들의 심술 탓에 지금은 그 가운데 한 권도 내게 남아 있지 않다는 생각이 드는구려. 어쨌든 댁의 이야기를 끊지 않겠다고 우리가 한 약속을 깬 것을 용서하시오. 기사도나 편력 기사 이야기가 들릴 때면, 나는 해의 빛살이 열기를 내지 않고는 못 배기고 달의 빛살이 습기를 내지 않고는 못 배기듯, 그것을 입에 올리지 않고는 못 배기니 말이오. 그러니 용서하고 계속하시오. 지금은 그것이 더 긴요한 일이니.\""]],[["While Don Quixote was saying this, Cardenio allowed his head to fall upon his breast, and seemed plunged in deep thought; and though twice Don Quixote bade him go on with his story, he neither looked up nor uttered a word in reply; but after some time he raised his head and said, “I cannot get rid of the idea, nor will anyone in the world remove it, or make me think otherwise--and he would be a blockhead who would hold or believe anything else than that that arrant knave Master Elisabad made free with Queen Madasima.”","돈키호테가 이렇게 말하는 동안, 카르데니오는 고개를 가슴께로 떨구고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯했다. 그리고 돈키호테가 이야기를 계속하라고 두 번이나 일렀건만, 그는 고개를 들지도, 대꾸 한마디 하지도 않았다. 그러나 얼마쯤 뒤 그는 고개를 들고 말했다. \"그 생각을 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없고, 세상 누구도 그것을 없애거나 나를 달리 생각하게 만들지 못할 것이오. 그리고 저 천하의 악당 엘리사밧 선생이 마다시마 왕비와 놀아났다는 것 말고 다른 것을 우기거나 믿는 자는 멍청이일 것이오.\""]],[["“That is not true, by all that’s good,”","\"그건 사실이 아니오, 온갖 선한 것에 걸고 말이오.\" 돈키호테가 평소 버릇대로 그에게 사납게 돌아서며 격분하여 말했다."],["said Don Quixote in high wrath, turning upon him angrily, as his way was; “and it is a very great slander, or rather villainy.","\"그건 아주 크나큰 비방, 아니 차라리 악행이오. 마다시마 왕비는 더없이 고귀한 부인이셨으니, 그토록 지체 높은 공주께서 돌팔이 따위와 놀아났으리라고는 생각할 수 없소."],["Queen Madasima was a very illustrious lady, and it is not to be supposed that so exalted a princess would have made free with a quack; and whoever maintains the contrary lies like a great scoundrel, and I will give him to know it, on foot or on horseback, armed or unarmed, by night or by day, or as he likes best.”","그 반대를 우기는 자는 누구든 천하의 악당처럼 거짓말을 하는 것이며, 나는 그자에게 그것을 알게 해 주겠소. 걸어서든 말 위에서든, 무장을 했든 안 했든, 밤이든 낮이든, 그자가 가장 좋아하는 대로 말이오.\""]],[["Cardenio was looking at him steadily, and his mad fit having now come upon him, he had no disposition to go on with his story, nor would Don Quixote have listened to it, so much had what he had heard about Madasima disgusted him.","카르데니오는 그를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었는데, 이제 그의 미친 발작이 도진지라 이야기를 계속할 마음이 없었고, 돈키호테 또한 마다시마에 관해 들은 바에 어찌나 비위가 상했던지 그것을 들어 줄 리 없었다."],["Strange to say, he stood up for her as if she were in earnest his veritable born lady; to such a pass had his unholy books brought him.","묘하게도 그는 그녀가 정말 자기의 진짜 타고난 귀부인이라도 되는 양 그녀를 두둔했으니, 그 불경한 책들이 그를 이 지경까지 몰고 온 것이었다."],["Cardenio, then, being, as I said, now mad, when he heard himself given the lie, and called a scoundrel and other insulting names, not relishing the jest, snatched up a stone that he found near him, and with it delivered such a blow on Don Quixote’s breast that he laid him on his back.","그리하여 앞서 말했듯 이제 미쳐 버린 카르데니오는, 자기가 거짓말쟁이로 몰리고 악당이며 그 밖의 모욕적인 이름으로 불리는 것을 듣자, 그 농을 달가워하지 않고 곁에 있던 돌멩이 하나를 집어 들어 그것으로 돈키호테의 가슴을 어찌나 세게 후려쳤던지 그를 벌렁 자빠뜨려 놓았다."],["Sancho Panza, seeing his master treated in this fashion, attacked the madman with his closed fist; but the Ragged One received him in such a way that with a blow of his fist he stretched him at his feet, and then mounting upon him crushed his ribs to his own satisfaction; the goatherd, who came to the rescue, shared the same fate; and having beaten and pummelled them all he left them and quietly withdrew to his hiding-place on the mountain.","산초 판사는 제 주인이 이런 꼴을 당하는 것을 보고 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 그 미치광이에게 덤벼들었으나, 누더기 사내가 어찌나 잘 맞아 주었던지 주먹 한 방으로 그를 제 발치에 뻗어 놓고는, 그 위에 올라타 제 분이 풀릴 때까지 그의 갈비뼈를 짓이겼다. 구하러 온 염소지기도 같은 운명을 겪었다."],["Sancho rose, and with the rage he felt at finding himself so belaboured without deserving it, ran to take vengeance on the goatherd, accusing him of not giving them warning that this man was at times taken with a mad fit, for if they had known it they would have been on their guard to protect themselves.","그리고 그들을 모조리 두들기고 패고 나서 그는 그들을 버려두고 산속 자기 은신처로 조용히 물러갔다. 산초는 일어나, 받을 까닭도 없이 그토록 두들겨 맞은 것에 대한 분노로 염소지기에게 앙갚음하려 달려들었으니, 이 사내가 이따금 미친 발작을 일으킨다고 미리 일러 주지 않았다며 그를 나무랐다."],["The goatherd replied that he had said so, and that if he had not heard him, that was no fault of his.","알았더라면 자기들을 지키려 경계했을 것이라면서 말이다. 염소지기는 자기가 그렇게 말했노라고, 그리고 산초가 그것을 듣지 못했다면 그것은 자기 잘못이 아니라고 대꾸했다."],["Sancho retorted, and the goatherd rejoined, and the altercation ended in their seizing each other by the beard, and exchanging such fisticuffs that if Don Quixote had not made peace between them, they would have knocked one another to pieces.","산초가 되받아치고 염소지기가 다시 응수하니, 그 말다툼은 끝내 서로 수염을 움켜잡고 어찌나 주먹질을 주고받는 것으로 끝났던지, 돈키호테가 둘 사이를 말리지 않았더라면 그들은 서로를 산산조각 내고 말았을 것이다."]],[["“Leave me alone, Sir Knight of the Rueful Countenance,”","\"내버려 두십시오, 슬픈 몰골의 기사님.\""],["said Sancho, grappling with the goatherd, “for of this fellow, who is a clown like myself, and no dubbed knight, I can safely take satisfaction for the affront he has offered me, fighting with him hand to hand like an honest man.”","산초가 염소지기와 맞붙은 채 말했다. \"이 녀석은 저와 똑같은 촌놈이지 작위 받은 기사가 아니니, 제게 가한 모욕에 대해서는 정직한 사내답게 이놈과 맨손으로 맞붙어 싸워 제가 마음 놓고 분풀이를 할 수 있습니다요.\""]],[["“That is true,”","\"그건 옳은 말이오.\""],["said Don Quixote, “but I know that he is not to blame for what has happened.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 일어난 일에 대해서는 저 사람에게 잘못이 없다는 것을 내가 아오.\""]],[["With this he pacified them, and again asked the goatherd if it would be possible to find Cardenio, as he felt the greatest anxiety to know the end of his story.","이 말로 그는 그들을 달랜 뒤, 다시 염소지기에게 카르데니오를 찾아낼 수 있겠느냐고 물었으니, 그의 이야기의 결말을 알고 싶은 마음이 더없이 간절했기 때문이다."],["The goatherd told him, as he had told him before, that there was no knowing of a certainty where his lair was; but that if he wandered about much in that neighbourhood he could not fail to fall in with him either in or out of his senses.","염소지기는 앞서 그에게 일렀던 대로, 그의 소굴이 어디인지 확실히 알 길은 없으나, 그 부근을 많이 돌아다니다 보면 제정신이든 아니든 그와 마주치지 않을 수 없을 것이라고 말했다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXV. WHICH TREATS OF THE STRANGE THINGS THAT HAPPENED TO THE STOUT KNIGHT OF LA MANCHA IN THE SIERRA MORENA, AND OF HIS IMITATION OF THE PENANCE OF BELTENEBROS","# CHAPTER XXV."]],[["Don Quixote took leave of the goatherd, and once more mounting Rocinante bade Sancho follow him, which he having no ass, did very discontentedly.","돈키호테는 염소지기에게 작별을 고하고 다시 로시난테에 올라 산초에게 따라오라 명했는데, 당나귀가 없는 산초는 몹시 못마땅한 기색으로 그 뒤를 따랐다."],["They proceeded slowly, making their way into the most rugged part of the mountain, Sancho all the while dying to have a talk with his master, and longing for him to begin, so that there should be no breach of the injunction laid upon him; but unable to keep silence so long he said to him:","두 사람은 천천히 나아가 산속의 가장 험한 곳으로 들어섰는데, 그동안 산초는 주인과 이야기를 나누고 싶어 죽을 지경이라, 자신에게 내려진 그 함구령을 어기지 않으려고 주인이 먼저 입을 열어 주기만을 애타게 기다렸다. 그러나 그토록 오래 침묵을 지킬 수가 없어 마침내 그가 말했다."]],[["“Señor Don Quixote, give me your worship’s blessing and dismissal, for I’d like to go home at once to my wife and children with whom I can at any rate talk and converse as much as I like; for to want me to go through these solitudes day and night and not speak to you when I have a mind is burying me alive. If luck would have it that animals spoke as they did in the days of Guisopete, it would not be so bad, because I could talk to Rocinante about whatever came into my head, and so put up with my ill-fortune; but it is a hard case, and not to be borne with patience, to go seeking adventures all one’s life and get nothing but kicks and blanketings, brickbats and punches, and with all this to have to sew up one’s mouth without daring to say what is in one’s heart, just as if one were dumb.”","\"돈키호테 나리, 제게 축복과 작별 인사를 내려 주십시오. 저는 당장에라도 집으로 돌아가 마누라와 자식들 곁으로 가고 싶습니다. 거기서는 적어도 제가 하고 싶은 만큼 마음껏 떠들고 이야기를 나눌 수 있으니까요. 밤낮없이 이런 황량한 곳을 헤매면서 하고 싶은 말이 있어도 나리께 말 한마디 못 하게 하시는 건 산 사람을 생매장하는 것이나 다름없습니다. 만약 운이 좋아서 기소페테 시절처럼 짐승들이 말을 할 줄 안다면야 이렇게까지 나쁘진 않을 겁니다. 그러면 머릿속에 떠오르는 건 뭐든 로시난테한테 떠들어 대면서 제 박복한 신세를 견뎌 낼 수 있을 테니까요. 하지만 평생 모험을 찾아 헤매면서 얻는 거라곤 발길질에 담요 키질에, 벽돌 세례에 주먹질뿐인데, 거기다 벙어리라도 된 것처럼 가슴속에 있는 말도 감히 못 하고 입을 꿰매고 다녀야 한다니, 이건 도무지 참을 수 없는 고약한 일입니다.\""]],[["“I understand thee, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote; “thou art dying to have the interdict I placed upon thy tongue removed; consider it removed, and say what thou wilt while we are wandering in these mountains.”","\"네 마음을 알겠다, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내가 네 혀에 내린 금령을 풀고 싶어 죽을 지경이로구나. 그 금령은 풀린 것으로 여겨라. 그러니 우리가 이 산속을 헤매는 동안에는 하고 싶은 말을 하거라.\""]],[["“So be it,” said Sancho; “let me speak now, for God knows what will happen by-and-by; and to take advantage of the permit at once, I ask, what made your worship stand up so for that Queen Majimasa, or whatever her name is, or what did it matter whether that abbot was a friend of hers or not? for if your worship had let that pass--and you were not a judge in the matter--it is my belief the madman would have gone on with his story, and the blow of the stone, and the kicks, and more than half a dozen cuffs would have been escaped.”","\"그렇게 하지요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"지금 말하게 해 주십시오. 이따가 무슨 일이 벌어질지는 하느님만 아실 일이니까요. 그럼 당장 그 허락을 써먹어서 여쭙겠습니다. 대체 나리께서는 무엇 때문에 그 마히마사 여왕인지 뭔지 하는 여인 편을 그토록 들어 주셨으며, 그 수도원장이 그 여인의 친구였든 아니었든 나리와 무슨 상관이 있단 말입니까? 만약 나리께서 그 일을 그냥 지나치셨더라면 — 나리께서 그 일의 재판관도 아니셨으니 — 제 생각엔 그 미치광이가 자기 이야기를 계속했을 테고, 돌멩이에 얻어맞는 일도, 발길질도, 대여섯 대도 넘는 주먹질도 다 피할 수 있었을 겁니다.\""]],[["“In faith, Sancho,” answered Don Quixote, “if thou knewest as I do what an honourable and illustrious lady Queen Madasima was, I know thou wouldst say I had great patience that I did not break in pieces the mouth that uttered such blasphemies, for a very great blasphemy it is to say or imagine that a queen has made free with a surgeon. The truth of the story is that that Master Elisabad whom the madman mentioned was a man of great prudence and sound judgment, and served as governor and physician to the queen, but to suppose that she was his mistress is nonsense deserving very severe punishment; and as a proof that Cardenio did not know what he was saying, remember when he said it he was out of his wits.”","\"맹세코, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"마다시마 여왕이 얼마나 고귀하고 명망 높은 부인이었는지 나만큼 네가 알았더라면, 그런 모욕적인 말을 내뱉은 그 입을 산산조각 내지 않은 내가 참으로 인내심이 깊었다고 너도 말할 것이다. 여왕이 외과 의사와 부정을 저질렀다고 말하거나 상상하는 것은 실로 엄청난 모독이니라. 이야기의 진실은 이렇다. 그 미치광이가 입에 올린 엘리사밧 선생이라는 분은 신중하고 분별 있는 사람으로, 여왕의 시종이자 의사로 봉직했을 뿐이다. 그런데 그가 여왕의 정부였다고 짐작하는 것은 지극히 엄한 벌을 받아 마땅한 헛소리이니라. 그리고 카르데니오가 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지조차 몰랐다는 증거로는, 그가 그 말을 할 때 제정신이 아니었다는 사실을 떠올려 보아라.\""]],[["“That is what I say,”","\"제 말이 바로 그겁니다.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho; “there was no occasion for minding the words of a madman; for if good luck had not helped your worship, and he had sent that stone at your head instead of at your breast, a fine way we should have been in for standing up for my lady yonder, God confound her!","\"미치광이의 말 따위에 신경 쓸 필요가 없었다는 거지요. 만약 행운이 나리를 돕지 않아서 그자가 나리의 가슴이 아니라 머리에다 그 돌을 던졌더라면, 저기 그 마님 편을 들어 준 대가로 우리 꼴이 참 볼만했을 겁니다."],["And then, would not Cardenio have gone free as a madman?”","하느님이 그 여인을 벌하시기를! 그러고 나면 카르데니오는 미치광이라고 무사히 풀려나지 않았겠습니까?\""]],[["“Against men in their senses or against madmen,” said Don Quixote, “every knight-errant is bound to stand up for the honour of women, whoever they may be, much more for queens of such high degree and dignity as Queen Madasima, for whom I have a particular regard on account of her amiable qualities; for, besides being extremely beautiful, she was very wise, and very patient under her misfortunes, of which she had many; and the counsel and society of the Master Elisabad were a great help and support to her in enduring her afflictions with wisdom and resignation; hence the ignorant and ill-disposed vulgar took occasion to say and think that she was his mistress; and they lie, I say it once more, and will lie two hundred times more, all who think and say so.”","\"제정신인 자들에 맞서든 미치광이들에 맞서든,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"편력 기사라면 누구나 여인의 명예를 지켜야 할 의무가 있느니라. 그 여인이 누구든 말이다. 하물며 마다시마 여왕처럼 그토록 높은 신분과 위엄을 갖춘 여왕이라면 더더욱 그러하니, 나는 그분의 어진 품성 때문에 그분을 각별히 존경하느니라. 그분은 빼어나게 아름다울 뿐만 아니라 매우 지혜로웠으며, 숱한 불행 속에서도 참으로 인내심이 깊으셨다. 그리고 엘리사밧 선생의 조언과 벗함은 그분이 그 고난을 지혜와 체념으로 견뎌 내는 데 큰 도움이자 버팀목이 되어 주었다. 바로 이 때문에 무지하고 심술궂은 무리가 그분이 그의 정부라고 떠들고 생각할 빌미를 잡은 것이다. 다시 한번 말하노니, 그리고 이백 번을 더 말하더라도, 그렇게 생각하고 말하는 자들은 모두 거짓말을 하는 것이니라.\""]],[["“I neither say nor think so,” said Sancho; “let them look to it; with their bread let them eat it; they have rendered account to God whether they misbehaved or not; I come from my vineyard, I know nothing; I am not fond of prying into other men’s lives; he who buys and lies feels it in his purse; moreover, naked was I born, naked I find myself, I neither lose nor gain; but if they did, what is that to me? many think there are flitches where there are no hooks; but who can put gates to the open plain? moreover they said of God—”","\"저는 그렇게 말하지도 생각하지도 않습니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그건 그자들이 알아서 할 일이지요. 자기들이 만든 빵은 자기들이 먹는 법입니다. 잘못을 저질렀든 아니든 그자들은 이미 하느님께 셈을 치렀을 테고요. 저는 제 포도밭에서 온 사람이라 아무것도 모릅니다. 남의 인생을 캐고 다니는 건 좋아하지도 않고요. 사서 거짓말하는 자는 제 지갑으로 그 대가를 치르는 법이지요. 게다가 저는 벌거숭이로 태어나 지금도 벌거숭이 신세니, 잃을 것도 얻을 것도 없습니다. 하지만 그자들이 정말 그랬다 한들 그게 저와 무슨 상관입니까? 갈고리도 없는데 베이컨이 걸려 있는 줄 아는 사람이 많지요. 그렇지만 누가 탁 트인 벌판에 문을 달 수 있겠습니까? 더구나 하느님에 대해서도 사람들은 이러쿵저러쿵—\""]],[["“God bless me,” said Don Quixote, “what a set of absurdities thou art stringing together! What has what we are talking about got to do with the proverbs thou art threading one after the other? for God’s sake hold thy tongue, Sancho, and henceforward keep to prodding thy ass and don’t meddle in what does not concern thee; and understand with all thy five senses that everything I have done, am doing, or shall do, is well founded on reason and in conformity with the rules of chivalry, for I understand them better than all the world that profess them.”","\"이런, 맙소사.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"대체 무슨 그런 말도 안 되는 소리를 줄줄이 늘어놓는 게냐! 우리가 지금 하는 이야기가 네가 하나씩 꿰어 대는 그 속담들과 무슨 상관이 있단 말이냐? 제발 입 좀 다물어라, 산초. 그리고 앞으로는 네 당나귀나 몰면서 너와 상관없는 일에는 끼어들지 마라. 그리고 네 다섯 가지 감각을 다 동원해서 알아 두어라. 내가 해 온 일, 하고 있는 일, 앞으로 할 일은 모두 이치에 단단히 뿌리박고 있으며 기사도의 법도에 들어맞느니라. 나는 그 법도를 세상의 어느 기사보다도 더 잘 알기 때문이니라.\""]],[["“Señor,” replied Sancho, “is it a good rule of chivalry that we should go astray through these mountains without path or road, looking for a madman who when he is found will perhaps take a fancy to finish what he began, not his story, but your worship’s head and my ribs, and end by breaking them altogether for us?”","\"나리,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"길도 없는 이 산속을 헤매면서 미치광이를 찾아다니는 게 그래 좋은 기사도의 법도란 말입니까? 게다가 그자를 찾아내면 어쩌면 제 멋대로 끝맺음을 하려 들지도 모릅니다. 자기 이야기 말고요, 나리의 머리통이며 제 갈비뼈를 말입니다. 그래서 결국 우리 둘을 아주 박살 내 버릴지도 모르지 않습니까?\""]],[["“Peace, I say again, Sancho,”","\"다시 한번 말하노니 조용히 하거라, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “for let me tell thee it is not so much the desire of finding that madman that leads me into these regions as that which I have of performing among them an achievement wherewith I shall win eternal name and fame throughout the known world; and it shall be such that I shall thereby set the seal on all that can make a knight-errant perfect and famous.”","\"내가 너에게 말해 두건대, 나를 이 지역으로 이끄는 것은 그 미치광이를 찾으려는 마음이라기보다는, 이곳에서 한 가지 위업을 이루어 온 세상에 영원한 이름과 명성을 떨치려는 마음이니라. 그리고 그것은 편력 기사를 완전하고 명성 높게 만드는 데 필요한 모든 것에 내가 인장을 찍게 될 만큼 대단한 일이 될 것이다.\""]],[["“And is it very perilous, this achievement?”","\"그래, 그 위업이라는 게 아주 위험한 겁니까?\""]],[["“No,”","\"아니다.\""],["replied he of the Rueful Countenance; “though it may be in the dice that we may throw deuce-ace instead of sixes; but all will depend on thy diligence.”","슬픈 몰골의 기사가 대답했다. \"비록 주사위에서 육 대신 한둘이 나올 수도 있겠으나, 모든 것은 네 부지런함에 달려 있느니라.\""]],[["“On my diligence!”","\"제 부지런함에요!\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“Yes,”","\"그렇다.\""],["said Don Quixote, “for if thou dost return soon from the place where I mean to send thee, my penance will be soon over, and my glory will soon begin.","돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 내가 보낼 곳에서 일찍 돌아온다면, 내 고행도 곧 끝날 것이고, 내 영광도 곧 시작될 것이기 때문이다."],["But as it is not right to keep thee any longer in suspense, waiting to see what comes of my words, I would have thee know, Sancho, that the famous Amadis of Gaul was one of the most perfect knights-errant--I am wrong to say he was one; he stood alone, the first, the only one, the lord of all that were in the world in his time.","그러나 내 말이 무슨 결과를 낳을지 보려고 너를 더 오래 애태우게 하는 것은 옳지 못하니, 산초야, 네가 알아 두어야 할 것이 있다. 그 유명한 갈리아의 아마디스는 가장 완벽한 편력 기사 가운데 하나였으니 — 아니, 하나였다고 말하는 것은 잘못이로구나."],["A fig for Don Belianis, and for all who say he equalled him in any respect, for, my oath upon it, they are deceiving themselves!","그는 홀로 우뚝 섰으니, 으뜸이요, 유일한 존재요, 자기 시대에 세상에 있던 모든 이의 군주였느니라. 돈 벨리아니스 따위는 아무것도 아니며, 그가 어떤 점에서든 아마디스에 견줄 만하다고 말하는 자들도 다 마찬가지다."],["I say, too, that when a painter desires to become famous in his art he endeavours to copy the originals of the rarest painters that he knows; and the same rule holds good for all the most important crafts and callings that serve to adorn a state; thus must he who would be esteemed prudent and patient imitate Ulysses, in whose person and labours Homer presents to us a lively picture of prudence and patience; as Virgil, too, shows us in the person of Æneas the virtue of a pious son and the sagacity of a brave and skilful captain; not representing or describing them as they were, but as they ought to be, so as to leave the example of their virtues to posterity.","내 맹세코, 그자들은 스스로를 속이고 있느니라! 또 내가 말하건대, 화가가 자기 기예로 이름을 떨치고자 할 때는 자기가 아는 가장 빼어난 화가들의 원작을 본뜨려고 애쓰는 법이다."],["In the same way Amadis was the polestar, day-star, sun of valiant and devoted knights, whom all we who fight under the banner of love and chivalry are bound to imitate.","그리고 이 법칙은 나라를 빛내는 데 이바지하는 가장 중요한 모든 기예와 직업에도 똑같이 적용되느니라. 그러니 신중하고 인내심 깊다는 평을 듣고자 하는 자는 율리시스를 본떠야 하니, 호메로스는 그 인물과 그의 노고를 통해 신중함과 인내의 생생한 그림을 우리에게 보여 주었다."],["This, then, being so, I consider, friend Sancho, that the knight-errant who shall imitate him most closely will come nearest to reaching the perfection of chivalry.","베르길리우스 또한 아이네아스라는 인물을 통해 효성스러운 아들의 미덕과 용맹하고 노련한 장수의 지혜를 우리에게 보여 주었으니, 그들을 있는 그대로가 아니라 마땅히 있어야 할 모습으로 그리고 묘사함으로써 그 미덕의 본보기를 후세에 남긴 것이니라."],["Now one of the instances in which this knight most conspicuously showed his prudence, worth, valour, endurance, fortitude, and love, was when he withdrew, rejected by the Lady Oriana, to do penance upon the Pena Pobre, changing his name into that of Beltenebros, a name assuredly significant and appropriate to the life which he had voluntarily adopted.","이와 마찬가지로 아마디스는 용맹하고 헌신적인 기사들의 북극성이요, 샛별이요, 태양이었으니, 사랑과 기사도의 깃발 아래 싸우는 우리 모두는 그를 본받아야 하느니라. 그러하니, 친구 산초야, 나는 그를 가장 가까이 본받는 편력 기사야말로 기사도의 완성에 가장 가까이 다가가는 자라고 여기노라."],["So, as it is easier for me to imitate him in this than in cleaving giants asunder, cutting off serpents’ heads, slaying dragons, routing armies, destroying fleets, and breaking enchantments, and as this place is so well suited for a similar purpose, I must not allow the opportunity to escape which now so conveniently offers me its forelock.”","그런데 이 기사가 자신의 신중함과 가치, 용맹, 인내, 불굴, 사랑을 가장 두드러지게 보여 준 본보기 가운데 하나는, 오리아나 부인에게 거절당하자 물러나 페냐 포브레에서 고행을 하며 자기 이름을 벨테네브로스로 바꾸었을 때이니, 그 이름이야말로 그가 스스로 택한 삶에 어울리는 뜻깊고 적절한 것이었느니라. 그러니 거인을 두 동강 내고, 뱀의 머리를 베고, 용을 죽이고, 군대를 무찌르고, 함대를 부수고, 마법을 푸는 일보다는 이 일에서 그를 본받는 것이 내게 더 쉬운 일이요, 또 이곳이 그런 목적에 더없이 잘 들어맞으니, 지금 이렇게 편리하게 앞머리를 내미는 이 기회를 놓쳐서는 안 되겠노라.\""]],[["“What is it in reality,”","\"나리께서는 이런 외진 곳에서 실제로 무엇을 하시려는 겁니까?\""],["said Sancho, “that your worship means to do in such an out-of-the-way place as this?”","산초가 말했다."]],[["“Have I not told thee,” answered Don Quixote, “that I mean to imitate Amadis here, playing the victim of despair, the madman, the maniac, so as at the same time to imitate the valiant Don Roland, when at the fountain he had evidence of the fair Angelica having disgraced herself with Medoro and through grief thereat went mad, and plucked up trees, troubled the waters of the clear springs, slew shepherds, destroyed flocks, burned down huts, levelled houses, dragged mares after him, and perpetrated a hundred thousand other outrages worthy of everlasting renown and record? And though I have no intention of imitating Roland, or Orlando, or Rotolando (for he went by all these names), step by step in all the mad things he did, said, and thought, I will make a rough copy to the best of my power of all that seems to me most essential; but perhaps I shall content myself with the simple imitation of Amadis, who without giving way to any mischievous madness but merely to tears and sorrow, gained as much fame as the most famous.”","\"내 너에게 말하지 않았더냐.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내가 여기서 아마디스를 본받아 절망에 빠진 자, 미치광이, 광인 노릇을 하려 한다고 말이다. 그러면서 동시에 용맹한 돈 롤단도 본받으려 하노라. 그는 샘가에서 아름다운 안젤리카가 메도로와 함께 몸을 더럽혔다는 증거를 보고는, 그 슬픔에 미쳐 버려 나무를 뽑고, 맑은 샘물을 휘젓고, 양치기들을 죽이고, 가축 떼를 망치고, 오두막을 불태우고, 집들을 허물고, 암말들을 끌고 다니며, 영원히 기리고 기록할 만한 수만 가지 폭거를 저질렀느니라. 비록 내가 롤단, 혹은 오를란도, 혹은 로톨란도(그는 이 모든 이름으로 불렸느니라)가 행하고 말하고 생각한 그 모든 미친 짓을 하나하나 그대로 본받을 생각은 없으나, 내게 가장 본질적으로 여겨지는 모든 것을 내 힘껏 대강이나마 본떠 보리라. 어쩌면 나는 아마디스를 본받는 단순한 일로 만족할지도 모르겠구나. 그는 어떤 해로운 광기에도 빠지지 않고 그저 눈물과 슬픔으로써 가장 이름난 자들 못지않은 명성을 얻었으니 말이다.\""]],[["“It seems to me,”","\"제가 보기엔,\""],["said Sancho, “that the knights who behaved in this way had provocation and cause for those follies and penances; but what cause has your worship for going mad?","산초가 말했다. \"이런 식으로 행동한 기사들에게는 그런 어리석은 짓과 고행을 할 만한 자극과 까닭이 있었던 것 같습니다."],["What lady has rejected you, or what evidence have you found to prove that the lady Dulcinea del Toboso has been trifling with Moor or Christian?”","그런데 나리께는 미쳐 버릴 무슨 까닭이 있단 말입니까? 어떤 부인이 나리를 거절했습니까, 아니면 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님이 무어인이나 기독교인과 놀아났다는 무슨 증거라도 찾으셨습니까?\""]],[["“There is the point,” replied Don Quixote, “and that is the beauty of this business of mine; no thanks to a knight-errant for going mad when he has cause; the thing is to turn crazy without any provocation, and let my lady know, if I do this in the dry, what I would do in the moist; moreover I have abundant cause in the long separation I have endured from my lady till death, Dulcinea del Toboso; for as thou didst hear that shepherd Ambrosio say the other day, in absence all ills are felt and feared; and so, friend Sancho, waste no time in advising me against so rare, so happy, and so unheard-of an imitation; mad I am, and mad I must be until thou returnest with the answer to a letter that I mean to send by thee to my lady Dulcinea; and if it be such as my constancy deserves, my insanity and penance will come to an end; and if it be to the opposite effect, I shall become mad in earnest, and, being so, I shall suffer no more; thus in whatever way she may answer I shall escape from the struggle and affliction in which thou wilt leave me, enjoying in my senses the boon thou bearest me, or as a madman not feeling the evil thou bringest me. But tell me, Sancho, hast thou got Mambrino’s helmet safe? for I saw thee take it up from the ground when that ungrateful wretch tried to break it in pieces but could not, by which the fineness of its temper may be seen.”","\"바로 그 점이니라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그리고 그것이 내 이 일의 묘미이니라. 까닭이 있어서 미치는 편력 기사라면 무슨 고마울 게 있겠느냐. 핵심은 아무런 자극도 없이 미쳐 버리는 것이며, 내가 메마른 데서도 이러할진대 젖은 데서는 무엇을 할지 내 부인이 알게 하는 것이니라. 더구나 내게는 충분한 까닭이 있으니, 죽음에 이르도록 사랑하는 내 부인 둘시네아 델 토보소와 오래 떨어져 견뎌 온 일이 그것이다. 일전에 그 양치기 암브로시오가 한 말을 너도 들었듯이, 떨어져 있으면 온갖 고통이 느껴지고 두려워지는 법이니라. 그러니 친구 산초야, 이토록 보기 드물고, 이토록 행복하고, 이토록 전대미문의 본받음을 그만두라고 나를 설득하느라 시간을 낭비하지 말거라. 나는 미쳤으며, 내 부인 둘시네아에게 보낼 편지의 답을 네가 가지고 돌아올 때까지 계속 미쳐 있어야 하느니라. 그 답이 내 한결같은 마음에 걸맞은 것이라면 내 광기와 고행은 끝날 것이고, 만약 그 반대라면 나는 정말로 미쳐 버려, 그리되면 더는 아무 고통도 느끼지 못할 것이니라. 이리하여 그분이 어떤 식으로 답하든 나는 네가 나를 두고 떠날 그 몸부림과 괴로움에서 벗어나게 될 것이니, 제정신으로 네가 가져올 은혜를 누리거나, 아니면 미치광이로서 네가 가져올 화를 느끼지 못하거나 둘 중 하나일 것이다. 그건 그렇고 산초야, 맘브리노의 투구는 잘 간수하고 있느냐? 그 배은망덕한 자가 그것을 부수려다 부수지 못했을 때 네가 그것을 땅에서 주워 드는 것을 내가 보았느니라. 그것으로 그 강철의 단단함을 알 수 있느니라.\""]],[["To which Sancho made answer, “By the living God, Sir Knight of the Rueful Countenance, I cannot endure or bear with patience some of the things that your worship says; and from them I begin to suspect that all you tell me about chivalry, and winning kingdoms and empires, and giving islands, and bestowing other rewards and dignities after the custom of knights-errant, must be all made up of wind and lies, and all pigments or figments, or whatever we may call them; for what would anyone think that heard your worship calling a barber’s basin Mambrino’s helmet without ever seeing the mistake all this time, but that one who says and maintains such things must have his brains addled? I have the basin in my sack all dinted, and I am taking it home to have it mended, to trim my beard in it, if, by God’s grace, I am allowed to see my wife and children some day or other.”","이에 산초가 대답했다. \"살아 계신 하느님께 두고 말씀드리건대, 슬픈 몰골의 기사 나리, 저는 나리께서 하시는 말씀 가운데 어떤 것들은 도무지 참고 견딜 수가 없습니다. 그런 말씀을 들으니 나리께서 제게 들려주시는 기사도며, 왕국과 제국을 얻는 일이며, 섬을 주신다는 일이며, 편력 기사들의 관습에 따라 온갖 상과 작위를 내려 주신다는 그 모든 이야기가 다 바람과 거짓말로 지어낸 것이요, 죄다 헛것이거나 꾸며 낸 것이거나, 뭐라 부르든 그런 게 아닌가 의심이 들기 시작합니다. 이발사의 대야를 맘브리노의 투구라고 부르시면서 여태껏 그 잘못을 한 번도 깨닫지 못하시는 걸 누가 듣는다면, 그런 말을 하고 우기는 사람은 머릿속이 뒤죽박죽이 된 게 분명하다고 여기지 않겠습니까? 그 대야는 다 찌그러진 채 제 자루 속에 들어 있고, 저는 그걸 집으로 가져가서 고쳐다가 수염을 다듬는 데 쓸 작정입니다. 하느님의 은총으로 언젠가 제 마누라와 자식들을 다시 보게 된다면 말이지요.\""]],[["“Look here, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “by him thou didst swear by just now I swear thou hast the most limited understanding that any squire in the world has or ever had. Is it possible that all this time thou hast been going about with me thou hast never found out that all things belonging to knights-errant seem to be illusions and nonsense and ravings, and to go always by contraries? And not because it really is so, but because there is always a swarm of enchanters in attendance upon us that change and alter everything with us, and turn things as they please, and according as they are disposed to aid or destroy us; thus what seems to thee a barber’s basin seems to me Mambrino’s helmet, and to another it will seem something else; and rare foresight it was in the sage who is on my side to make what is really and truly Mambrine’s helmet seem a basin to everybody, for, being held in such estimation as it is, all the world would pursue me to rob me of it; but when they see it is only a barber’s basin they do not take the trouble to obtain it; as was plainly shown by him who tried to break it, and left it on the ground without taking it, for, by my faith, had he known it he would never have left it behind. Keep it safe, my friend, for just now I have no need of it; indeed, I shall have to take off all this armour and remain as naked as I was born, if I have a mind to follow Roland rather than Amadis in my penance.”","\"이보게, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 방금 두고 맹세한 그분께 두고 나도 맹세하건대, 너는 세상의 어느 종자가 지녔거나 지녔던 것보다도 가장 한정된 머리를 가졌느니라. 네가 이토록 오랫동안 나와 함께 다니면서도, 편력 기사에 속한 모든 것이 환상이요 헛소리요 광기처럼 보이며 늘 거꾸로 돌아간다는 사실을 여태 깨닫지 못했단 말이냐? 그것이 정말로 그래서가 아니라, 우리를 돕거나 망치려는 마음에 따라 우리와 함께하는 모든 것을 바꾸고 뒤틀어, 제멋대로 사물을 변하게 하는 마법사 무리가 늘 우리를 따라다니기 때문이니라. 그리하여 너에게는 이발사의 대야로 보이는 것이 내게는 맘브리노의 투구로 보이고, 또 다른 사람에게는 다른 무엇으로 보일 것이다. 내 편을 드는 그 현자가 참으로 진정한 맘브리노의 투구를 모든 사람에게 대야로 보이게 만든 것은 보기 드문 선견지명이었느니라. 그것이 워낙 귀한 것으로 여겨지니, 온 세상이 그것을 빼앗으려고 나를 쫓아올 테지만, 그저 이발사의 대야로만 보이면 아무도 그것을 손에 넣으려는 수고를 하지 않을 테니 말이다. 그것을 부수려다 손에 넣지 않고 땅바닥에 내버려 둔 자가 이를 분명히 보여 주었으니, 내 믿음을 걸고 말하건대, 그가 그것이 무엇인지 알았더라면 결코 두고 가지 않았을 것이다. 잘 간수하거라, 친구여. 지금은 그것이 내게 필요하지 않으니 말이다. 사실 내가 고행에서 아마디스보다 롤단을 따르기로 마음먹는다면, 이 갑옷을 모조리 벗어 던지고 태어났을 때처럼 벌거숭이로 있어야 할 테니까.\""]],[["Thus talking they reached the foot of a high mountain which stood like an isolated peak among the others that surrounded it.","이렇게 이야기를 나누며 두 사람은 한 높은 산기슭에 이르렀는데, 그 산은 주위를 둘러싼 다른 산들 가운데 우뚝 솟은 외딴 봉우리처럼 서 있었다."],["Past its base there flowed a gentle brook, all around it spread a meadow so green and luxuriant that it was a delight to the eyes to look upon it, and forest trees in abundance, and shrubs and flowers, added to the charms of the spot.","그 기슭으로는 부드러운 시냇물이 흘렀고, 둘레에는 온통 푸르고 무성한 풀밭이 펼쳐져 바라보는 눈을 즐겁게 했으며, 울창한 숲의 나무들과 관목과 꽃들이 그곳의 아름다움을 더해 주었다."],["Upon this place the Knight of the Rueful Countenance fixed his choice for the performance of his penance, and as he beheld it exclaimed in a loud voice as though he were out of his senses:","슬픈 몰골의 기사는 자신의 고행을 행할 곳으로 이곳을 택했고, 그곳을 바라보며 마치 제정신이 아닌 듯 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]],[["“This is the place, oh, ye heavens, that I select and choose for bewailing the misfortune in which ye yourselves have plunged me: this is the spot where the overflowings of mine eyes shall swell the waters of yon little brook, and my deep and endless sighs shall stir unceasingly the leaves of these mountain trees, in testimony and token of the pain my persecuted heart is suffering.","\"오, 하늘이여, 이곳이야말로 그대들 자신이 나를 빠뜨린 이 불행을 통곡하기 위해 내가 가려 뽑은 곳이로다. 이곳이야말로 내 두 눈에서 넘쳐흐르는 눈물이 저 작은 시냇물을 불어나게 하고, 깊고 끝없는 내 한숨이 박해받는 내 마음이 겪는 고통의 증거이자 표징으로 이 산속 나무들의 잎사귀를 쉼 없이 흔들 곳이로다."],["Oh, ye rural deities, whoever ye be that haunt this lone spot, give ear to the complaint of a wretched lover whom long absence and brooding jealousy have driven to bewail his fate among these wilds and complain of the hard heart of that fair and ungrateful one, the end and limit of all human beauty!","오, 이 외딴곳을 떠도는 시골의 신들이여, 그대들이 누구든, 오랜 이별과 끓어오르는 질투에 떠밀려 이 황야에서 제 운명을 통곡하며 인간의 모든 아름다움의 끝이자 한계인 그 아리땁고도 무정한 여인의 모진 마음을 한탄하는 가련한 연인의 하소연에 귀 기울여 주오! 오, 숲속 덤불에 깃들어 사는 물의 요정과 나무의 요정들이여, 그대들에게 헛되이 구애하는 날랜 음탕한 사티로스들이 그대들의 달콤한 안식을 결코 어지럽히지 않기를."],["Oh, ye wood nymphs and dryads, that dwell in the thickets of the forest, so may the nimble wanton satyrs by whom ye are vainly wooed never disturb your sweet repose, help me to lament my hard fate or at least weary not at listening to it!","부디 내가 모진 운명을 한탄하는 것을 도와주거나, 적어도 그것을 듣는 데 지치지나 말아 주오! 오, 둘시네아 델 토보소여, 내 밤의 낮이여, 내 고통의 영광이여, 내 길의 길잡이여, 내 운명의 별이여, 하늘이 그대가 구하는 모든 것을 가득 채워 주시기를."],["Oh, Dulcinea del Toboso, day of my night, glory of my pain, guide of my path, star of my fortune, so may Heaven grant thee in full all thou seekest of it, bethink thee of the place and condition to which absence from thee has brought me, and make that return in kindness that is due to my fidelity!","그대와 떨어져 있음으로 내가 이르게 된 이곳과 이 처지를 헤아려 주고, 내 충실함에 마땅한 보답을 친절로써 베풀어 주오! 오, 외로운 나무들이여, 오늘 이후로 내 고독 속에서 나와 벗이 되어 줄 그대들이여, 그대들의 가지를 부드럽게 흔들어 내 있음이 그대들에게 거슬리지 않는다는 어떤 표시라도 보여 주오!"],["Oh, lonely trees, that from this day forward shall bear me company in my solitude, give me some sign by the gentle movement of your boughs that my presence is not distasteful to you!","오, 그대여, 내 종자여, 내 순탄한 운명과 역경의 길에서 즐거운 동반자여, 내가 여기서 하는 일을 잘 기억해 두어, 그 모든 것의 유일한 원인이신 분께 그것을 이야기하고 전할 수 있게 하라.\""],["Oh, thou, my squire, pleasant companion in my prosperous and adverse fortunes, fix well in thy memory what thou shalt see me do here, so that thou mayest relate and report it to the sole cause of all,”","이렇게 말하고는 그는 로시난테에서 내려, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 안장과 굴레를 벗기고 엉덩이를 한 번 철썩 때리며 말했다. \"스스로 자유를 빼앗긴 자가 너에게 자유를 주노라, 오, 행적에서는 더없이 빼어나나 그 운명에서는 더없이 박복한 준마여."],["and so saying he dismounted from Rocinante, and in an instant relieved him of saddle and bridle, and giving him a slap on the croup, said, “He gives thee freedom who is bereft of it himself, oh steed as excellent in deed as thou art unfortunate in thy lot; begone where thou wilt, for thou bearest written on thy forehead that neither Astolfo’s hippogriff, nor the famed Frontino that cost Bradamante so dear, could equal thee in speed.”","네가 가고 싶은 곳으로 가거라. 네 이마에는 아스톨포의 히포그리프도, 브라다만테에게 그토록 값비싼 대가를 치르게 한 그 이름난 프론티노도 빠르기에서 너를 따를 수 없다고 쓰여 있느니라.\""]],[["Seeing this Sancho said, “Good luck to him who has saved us the trouble of stripping the pack-saddle off Dapple! By my faith he would not have gone without a slap on the croup and something said in his praise; though if he were here I would not let anyone strip him, for there would be no occasion, as he had nothing of the lover or victim of despair about him, inasmuch as his master, which I was while it was God’s pleasure, was nothing of the sort; and indeed, Sir Knight of the Rueful Countenance, if my departure and your worship’s madness are to come off in earnest, it will be as well to saddle Rocinante again in order that he may supply the want of Dapple, because it will save me time in going and returning: for if I go on foot I don’t know when I shall get there or when I shall get back, as I am, in truth, a bad walker.”","이를 본 산초가 말했다. \"잿빛이 녀석의 길마를 벗기는 수고를 우리한테 덜어 준 분에게 복이 있기를! 정말이지 그 녀석도 엉덩이를 한 번 철썩 맞고 칭찬 한마디 듣지 않고는 못 떠났을 텐데. 하지만 그 녀석이 여기 있었다면 저는 누구도 그놈의 길마를 벗기지 못하게 했을 겁니다. 그럴 까닭이 없으니까요. 그놈은 연인이나 절망에 빠진 자 같은 구석이라곤 하나도 없었거든요. 하느님 뜻에 따라 한때 그놈의 주인이었던 저부터가 그런 사람이 아니었으니 말입니다. 정말이지, 슬픈 몰골의 기사 나리, 만약 제가 떠나는 일과 나리께서 미치시는 일이 정말로 벌어질 거라면, 로시난테에 다시 안장을 얹는 게 좋겠습니다. 그래야 잿빛 녀석의 빈자리를 메울 수 있고, 오가는 시간도 아낄 수 있을 테니까요. 걸어서 가다가는 제가 언제 거기 도착할지, 언제 돌아올지 알 수가 없습니다. 사실 저는 걸음이 영 신통치 않은 사람이라서요.\""]],[["“I declare, Sancho,” returned Don Quixote, “it shall be as thou wilt, for thy plan does not seem to me a bad one, and three days hence thou wilt depart, for I wish thee to observe in the meantime what I do and say for her sake, that thou mayest be able to tell it.”","\"그래, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"네 뜻대로 하거라. 네 생각이 나쁘지 않은 듯하니 말이다. 사흘 뒤에 떠나도록 하여라. 그동안 내가 그분을 위해 무엇을 하고 무엇을 말하는지 잘 보아 두기를 바라기 때문이니, 그래야 네가 그것을 전할 수 있을 것이다.\""]],[["“But what more have I to see besides what I have seen?”","\"그런데 제가 이미 본 것 말고 더 봐야 할 게 뭐가 있단 말입니까?\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“Much thou knowest about it!”","\"네가 그것을 잘도 알겠구나!\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."],["“I have now got to tear up my garments, to scatter about my armour, knock my head against these rocks, and more of the same sort of thing, which thou must witness.”","\"이제 나는 내 옷을 갈가리 찢고, 갑옷을 여기저기 흩뿌리고, 이 바위들에 머리를 부딪고, 그 밖에도 그와 같은 일들을 더 해야 하니, 너는 그것을 지켜보아야 하느니라.\""]],[["“For the love of God,” said Sancho, “be careful, your worship, how you give yourself those knocks on the head, for you may come across such a rock, and in such a way, that the very first may put an end to the whole contrivance of this penance; and I should think, if indeed knocks on the head seem necessary to you, and this business cannot be done without them, you might be content--as the whole thing is feigned, and counterfeit, and in joke--you might be content, I say, with giving them to yourself in the water, or against something soft, like cotton; and leave it all to me; for I’ll tell my lady that your worship knocked your head against a point of rock harder than a diamond.”","\"제발 부탁이니,\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리, 머리를 부딪으실 때 부디 조심하십시오. 자칫 그런 바위에, 그런 식으로 부딪치셨다가는 바로 그 첫 번째 부딪침에 이 고행의 모든 계획이 끝장나 버릴 수도 있으니까요. 그리고 제 생각엔, 정말로 머리를 부딪는 일이 나리께 꼭 필요하고 이 일을 그것 없이는 할 수 없다 하더라도 — 어차피 이 모든 게 꾸며 낸 것이고, 가짜이고, 장난이니까 — 그저 물속에다 부딪거나, 솜처럼 부드러운 것에다 부딪는 것으로 만족하셔도 되지 않겠습니까. 그리고 나머지는 다 제게 맡기십시오. 제가 마님께 나리께서 다이아몬드보다 더 단단한 바위 모서리에 머리를 부딪으셨다고 말씀드리겠습니다.\""]],[["“I thank thee for thy good intentions, friend Sancho,” answered Don Quixote, “but I would have thee know that all these things I am doing are not in joke, but very much in earnest, for anything else would be a transgression of the ordinances of chivalry, which forbid us to tell any lie whatever under the penalties due to apostasy; and to do one thing instead of another is just the same as lying; so my knocks on the head must be real, solid, and valid, without anything sophisticated or fanciful about them, and it will be needful to leave me some lint to dress my wounds, since fortune has compelled us to do without the balsam we lost.”","\"네 좋은 뜻은 고맙다, 친구 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"허나 네가 알아 두어야 할 것은, 내가 하는 이 모든 일이 장난이 아니라 지극히 진지한 것이라는 점이니라. 그렇지 않으면 기사도의 법도를 어기는 것이 되기 때문이다. 기사도의 법도는 우리에게 배교에 해당하는 벌을 받을 각오 없이는 어떠한 거짓말도 하지 못하게 금하느니라. 그리고 한 가지 일 대신 다른 일을 하는 것은 거짓말을 하는 것과 똑같으니라. 그러니 내가 머리를 부딪는 것은 진짜요, 단단하고, 정당한 것이어야 하며, 거기에 거짓되거나 꾸며 낸 구석이 있어서는 안 되느니라. 그리고 우리가 잃어버린 영약 없이 지낼 수밖에 없게 된 처지이니, 내 상처를 싸맬 헝겊을 좀 남겨 두어야겠구나.\""]],[["“It was worse losing the ass,” replied Sancho, “for with him lint and all were lost; but I beg of your worship not to remind me again of that accursed liquor, for my soul, not to say my stomach, turns at hearing the very name of it; and I beg of you, too, to reckon as past the three days you allowed me for seeing the mad things you do, for I take them as seen already and pronounced upon, and I will tell wonderful stories to my lady; so write the letter and send me off at once, for I long to return and take your worship out of this purgatory where I am leaving you.”","\"당나귀를 잃은 게 더 큰일이었지요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"그놈과 함께 헝겊이고 뭐고 다 잃어버렸으니까요. 하지만 나리께 부탁드리건대, 그 저주받을 물약 이야기는 다시는 제게 꺼내지 말아 주십시오. 그 이름만 들어도 제 위장은 말할 것도 없고 영혼까지 뒤집어지니까요. 그리고 또 한 가지 부탁드리는데, 나리께서 미친 짓을 하시는 것을 보라고 제게 주신 그 사흘은 이미 지나간 것으로 쳐 주십시오. 저는 그걸 이미 다 보고 판단한 것으로 여기고, 마님께 놀라운 이야기들을 들려드릴 작정이니까요. 그러니 편지를 쓰셔서 저를 당장 떠나보내 주십시오. 어서 돌아와서 제가 나리를 두고 가는 이 연옥에서 나리를 구해 드리고 싶으니까요.\""]],[["“Purgatory dost thou call it, Sancho?”","\"이곳을 연옥이라 부르느냐, 산초?\""],["said Don Quixote, “rather call it hell, or even worse if there be anything worse.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"차라리 지옥이라 부르거나, 그보다 더한 것이 있다면 그것이라 부르거라.\""]],[["“For one who is in hell,”","\"지옥에 있는 자에게는,\""],["said Sancho, “nulla est retentio, as I have heard say.”","산초가 말했다. \"'눌라 에스트 레텐티오'라고 들었습니다.\""]],[["“I do not understand what retentio means,”","\"레텐티오가 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠구나.\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“Retentio,” answered Sancho, “means that whoever is in hell never comes nor can come out of it, which will be the opposite case with your worship or my legs will be idle, that is if I have spurs to enliven Rocinante: let me once get to El Toboso and into the presence of my lady Dulcinea, and I will tell her such things of the follies and madnesses (for it is all one) that your worship has done and is still doing, that I will manage to make her softer than a glove though I find her harder than a cork tree; and with her sweet and honeyed answer I will come back through the air like a witch, and take your worship out of this purgatory that seems to be hell but is not, as there is hope of getting out of it; which, as I have said, those in hell have not, and I believe your worship will not say anything to the contrary.”","\"레텐티오란,\" 산초가 대답했다. \"지옥에 있는 자는 누구든 결코 그곳에서 나오지도, 나올 수도 없다는 뜻입니다. 나리의 경우는 그 반대가 될 텐데요, 그게 아니라면 제 다리가 게으름을 피운 탓이겠지요. 물론 로시난테에 박차를 가할 박차가 있어야겠지만요. 일단 제가 엘 토보소에 가서 둘시네아 마님 앞에 서기만 하면, 나리께서 하셨고 또 지금도 하고 계신 그 어리석은 짓들과 미친 짓들(어차피 다 같은 거지만)을 마님께 어찌나 잘 들려드릴지, 코르크나무보다 더 딱딱한 분이라도 장갑보다 더 부드럽게 만들어 보이겠습니다. 그리고 마님의 달콤하고 꿀 같은 답을 받아 들고 마녀처럼 하늘을 날아 돌아와서, 지옥처럼 보이지만 실은 지옥이 아닌 이 연옥에서 나리를 꺼내 드리겠습니다. 빠져나갈 희망이 있으니 지옥이 아닌 거지요. 제가 말씀드렸듯이 지옥에 있는 자들에게는 그런 희망이 없으니까요. 이건 나리께서도 달리 토를 달지 못하실 겁니다.\""]],[["“That is true,”","\"그건 사실이다.\""],["said he of the Rueful Countenance, “but how shall we manage to write the letter?”","슬픈 몰골의 기사가 말했다. \"그런데 그 편지는 어떻게 쓰면 좋겠느냐?\""]],[["“And the ass-colt order too,”","\"당나귀 새끼 인도장도요.\""],["added Sancho.","산초가 덧붙였다."]],[["“All shall be included,” said Don Quixote; “and as there is no paper, it would be well done to write it on the leaves of trees, as the ancients did, or on tablets of wax; though that would be as hard to find just now as paper. But it has just occurred to me how it may be conveniently and even more than conveniently written, and that is in the note-book that belonged to Cardenio, and thou wilt take care to have it copied on paper, in a good hand, at the first village thou comest to where there is a schoolmaster, or if not, any sacristan will copy it; but see thou give it not to any notary to copy, for they write a law hand that Satan could not make out.”","\"모두 다 함께 쓰마.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그리고 종이가 없으니, 옛사람들이 그랬듯이 나뭇잎에다 쓰거나 밀랍 서판에다 쓰는 것도 좋겠구나. 다만 그것도 지금은 종이만큼이나 구하기 어렵겠지. 한데 마침 좋은 생각이 떠올랐으니, 편리하게, 아니 편리한 것 이상으로 쓸 방법이 있느니라. 바로 카르데니오의 것이었던 그 수첩에다 쓰는 것이다. 그러면 너는 학교 선생이 있는 첫 번째 마을에 닿거든 그것을 좋은 글씨로 종이에 베껴 쓰게 하거라. 선생이 없거든 성당지기라도 베껴 줄 것이다. 다만 어떤 공증인에게도 베끼게 하지는 마라. 그자들은 사탄도 알아보지 못할 법률 글씨를 쓰니까 말이다.\""]],[["“But what is to be done about the signature?”","\"그런데 서명은 어떻게 합니까?\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“The letters of Amadis were never signed,”","\"아마디스의 편지에는 결코 서명이 없었느니라.\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“That is all very well,”","\"그건 다 좋습니다만,\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “but the order must needs be signed, and if it is copied they will say the signature is false, and I shall be left without ass-colts.”","\"인도장에는 반드시 서명이 있어야 합니다. 베껴 쓴 거라면 사람들이 서명이 가짜라고 할 테고, 그러면 저는 당나귀 새끼도 못 받게 될 테니까요.\""]],[["“The order shall go signed in the same book,” said Don Quixote, “and on seeing it my niece will make no difficulty about obeying it; as to the loveletter thou canst put by way of signature, ‘Yours till death, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance.’ And it will be no great matter if it is in some other person’s hand, for as well as I recollect Dulcinea can neither read nor write, nor in the whole course of her life has she seen handwriting or letter of mine, for my love and hers have been always platonic, not going beyond a modest look, and even that so seldom that I can safely swear I have not seen her four times in all these twelve years I have been loving her more than the light of these eyes that the earth will one day devour; and perhaps even of those four times she has not once perceived that I was looking at her: such is the retirement and seclusion in which her father Lorenzo Corchuelo and her mother Aldonza Nogales have brought her up.”","\"인도장은 같은 수첩에 서명을 해서 보내마.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그것을 보면 내 조카딸이 군말 없이 따를 것이다. 연애편지로 말하자면, 서명 대신 이렇게 적으면 되느니라. '죽을 때까지 그대의 것인, 슬픈 몰골의 기사가.' 그리고 그것이 다른 사람의 글씨로 쓰여 있다 한들 별일 아니다. 내 기억으로는 둘시네아는 읽을 줄도 쓸 줄도 모르며, 평생 한 번도 내 글씨나 편지를 본 적이 없기 때문이니라. 나와 그분의 사랑은 늘 정신적인 것이어서, 정숙한 눈길 한 번 주고받는 것을 넘은 적이 없었고, 그조차 어찌나 드물었던지, 내가 그분을 사랑해 온 이 십이 년 동안 — 언젠가 흙이 삼켜 버릴 이 두 눈의 빛보다 더 사랑해 온 그 세월 동안 — 그분을 통틀어 네 번도 채 보지 못했다고 떳떳이 맹세할 수 있느니라. 게다가 그 네 번 가운데서도, 어쩌면 그분은 내가 자신을 바라보고 있다는 것을 단 한 번도 알아채지 못했을지도 모르느니라. 그분의 아버지 로렌소 코르추엘로와 어머니 알돈사 노갈레스가 그분을 그토록 깊이 숨기고 가두어 길렀기 때문이니라.\""]],[["“So, so!”","\"저런, 저런!\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho; “Lorenzo Corchuelo’s daughter is the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, otherwise called Aldonza Lorenzo?”","\"그러니까 로렌소 코르추엘로의 딸이 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님이라는 말씀이군요. 달리 알돈사 로렌소라고도 불리는 그 여자 말입니까?\""]],[["“She it is,”","\"바로 그 여인이니라.\""],["said Don Quixote, “and she it is that is worthy to be lady of the whole universe.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그리고 온 우주의 주인이 될 만한 자격이 있는 분이 바로 그 여인이니라.\""]],[["“I know her well,” said Sancho, “and let me tell you she can fling a crowbar as well as the lustiest lad in all the town. Giver of all good! but she is a brave lass, and a right and stout one, and fit to be helpmate to any knight-errant that is or is to be, who may make her his lady: the whoreson wench, what sting she has and what a voice! I can tell you one day she posted herself on the top of the belfry of the village to call some labourers of theirs that were in a ploughed field of her father’s, and though they were better than half a league off they heard her as well as if they were at the foot of the tower; and the best of her is that she is not a bit prudish, for she has plenty of affability, and jokes with everybody, and has a grin and a jest for everything. So, Sir Knight of the Rueful Countenance, I say you not only may and ought to do mad freaks for her sake, but you have a good right to give way to despair and hang yourself; and no one who knows of it but will say you did well, though the devil should take you; and I wish I were on my road already, simply to see her, for it is many a day since I saw her, and she must be altered by this time, for going about the fields always, and the sun and the air spoil women’s looks greatly. But I must own the truth to your worship, Señor Don Quixote; until now I have been under a great mistake, for I believed truly and honestly that the lady Dulcinea must be some princess your worship was in love with, or some person great enough to deserve the rich presents you have sent her, such as the Biscayan and the galley slaves, and many more no doubt, for your worship must have won many victories in the time when I was not yet your squire. But all things considered, what good can it do the lady Aldonza Lorenzo, I mean the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, to have the vanquished your worship sends or will send coming to her and going down on their knees before her? Because may be when they came she’d be hackling flax or threshing on the threshing floor, and they’d be ashamed to see her, and she’d laugh, or resent the present.”","\"제가 그 여자를 잘 압니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"말씀드리지만 그 여자는 온 마을에서 가장 기운 센 사내 못지않게 쇠막대를 잘 던집니다. 모든 좋은 것을 주시는 분이시여! 정말이지 그 여자는 씩씩하고 다부진 처녀라서, 자기를 부인으로 삼을 어떤 편력 기사에게든, 지금 있는 기사든 앞으로 나올 기사든 짝이 되어 줄 만합니다. 그 발칙한 계집애가 어찌나 톡톡 쏘아 대고 목청이 큰지! 말씀드리지만 한번은 그 여자가 마을 종탑 꼭대기에 올라가서, 자기 아버지의 밭에서 일하던 일꾼들을 부른 적이 있는데, 그자들이 반 레과나 떨어진 곳에 있었는데도 마치 탑 바로 아래 있는 것처럼 그 소리를 다 들었답니다. 게다가 그 여자의 가장 좋은 점은 조금도 새침을 떨지 않는다는 겁니다. 붙임성이 넘쳐서 누구하고나 농담을 주고받고, 무슨 일에나 히죽 웃으며 한마디 던지지요. 그러니 슬픈 몰골의 기사 나리, 제가 말씀드리건대, 나리께서는 그 여자를 위해 미친 짓을 하셔도 될 뿐만 아니라 하셔야 마땅하며, 절망에 빠져 목을 매다실 권리도 충분하십니다. 이 일을 아는 사람이라면 누구나 나리께서 잘하셨다고 말할 겁니다. 악마가 나리를 데려간다 해도 말입니다. 그리고 저는 어서 길을 떠나고 싶습니다. 그저 그 여자를 보고 싶어서요. 본 지가 워낙 오래됐으니, 지금쯤이면 많이 변했을 겁니다. 늘 들판을 쏘다니니 햇볕이며 바람이 여자의 얼굴을 크게 망쳐 놓기 마련이거든요. 그런데 나리께 진실을 털어놓아야겠습니다, 돈키호테 나리. 지금까지 저는 크게 잘못 알고 있었습니다. 저는 둘시네아 마님이 나리께서 반하신 어느 공주님이거나, 적어도 나리께서 보내신 그 푸짐한 선물들을 받을 만큼 대단한 분이라고 진심으로 정직하게 믿고 있었거든요. 비스카야 사람이며 갤리선 노예들이며, 분명 그 밖에도 많았겠지요. 제가 아직 나리의 종자가 되기 전에 나리께서 숱한 승리를 거두셨을 테니까요. 하지만 모든 걸 따져 보건대, 알돈사 로렌소 마님, 그러니까 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님께서 나리가 보내셨거나 앞으로 보내실 패배자들이 자기 앞에 와서 무릎을 꿇은들 무슨 좋을 게 있겠습니까? 어쩌면 그자들이 도착했을 때 마님은 삼을 빗고 있거나 타작마당에서 타작을 하고 있을지도 모르고, 그러면 그자들은 마님을 보고 부끄러워할 테고, 마님은 웃거나 그 선물을 못마땅해할 테니까요.\""]],[["“I have before now told thee many times, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “that thou art a mighty great chatterer, and that with a blunt wit thou art always striving at sharpness; but to show thee what a fool thou art and how rational I am, I would have thee listen to a short story. Thou must know that a certain widow, fair, young, independent, and rich, and above all free and easy, fell in love with a sturdy strapping young lay-brother; his superior came to know of it, and one day said to the worthy widow by way of brotherly remonstrance, ‘I am surprised, señora, and not without good reason, that a woman of such high standing, so fair, and so rich as you are, should have fallen in love with such a mean, low, stupid fellow as So-and-so, when in this house there are so many masters, graduates, and divinity students from among whom you might choose as if they were a lot of pears, saying this one I’ll take, that I won’t take;’ but she replied to him with great sprightliness and candour, ‘My dear sir, you are very much mistaken, and your ideas are very old-fashioned, if you think that I have made a bad choice in So-and-so, fool as he seems; because for all I want with him he knows as much and more philosophy than Aristotle.’ In the same way, Sancho, for all I want with Dulcinea del Toboso she is just as good as the most exalted princess on earth. It is not to be supposed that all those poets who sang the praises of ladies under the fancy names they give them, had any such mistresses. Thinkest thou that the Amarillises, the Phillises, the Sylvias, the Dianas, the Galateas, the Filidas, and all the rest of them, that the books, the ballads, the barber’s shops, the theatres are full of, were really and truly ladies of flesh and blood, and mistresses of those that glorify and have glorified them? Nothing of the kind; they only invent them for the most part to furnish a subject for their verses, and that they may pass for lovers, or for men valiant enough to be so; and so it suffices me to think and believe that the good Aldonza Lorenzo is fair and virtuous; and as to her pedigree it is very little matter, for no one will examine into it for the purpose of conferring any order upon her, and I, for my part, reckon her the most exalted princess in the world. For thou shouldst know, Sancho, if thou dost not know, that two things alone beyond all others are incentives to love, and these are great beauty and a good name, and these two things are to be found in Dulcinea in the highest degree, for in beauty no one equals her and in good name few approach her; and to put the whole thing in a nutshell, I persuade myself that all I say is as I say, neither more nor less, and I picture her in my imagination as I would have her to be, as well in beauty as in condition; Helen approaches her not nor does Lucretia come up to her, nor any other of the famous women of times past, Greek, Barbarian, or Latin; and let each say what he will, for if in this I am taken to task by the ignorant, I shall not be censured by the critical.”","\"내 전에도 여러 번 너에게 말하지 않았느냐, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"너는 엄청난 수다쟁이이며, 무딘 재치로 늘 날카로움을 부리려 애쓴다고 말이다. 허나 네가 얼마나 어리석은지, 그리고 내가 얼마나 사리에 밝은지 보여 주기 위해, 짧은 이야기 하나를 네게 들려주마. 너는 알아 두어야 한다. 아름답고 젊고 자유로운 데다 부유하고, 무엇보다 거리낌 없고 활달한 어느 과부가 건장하고 헌칠한 젊은 평수사에게 반했느니라. 그 수사의 윗사람이 이를 알게 되어, 어느 날 그 갸륵한 과부에게 형제로서 타이르는 뜻으로 이렇게 말했다. '부인, 부인처럼 그토록 지체 높고, 그토록 아름답고, 그토록 부유한 여인이 아무개처럼 그렇게 천하고 비루하고 어리석은 자에게 반하셨다니, 정말이지 까닭이 없는 게 아니라 참으로 놀랍습니다. 이 집에는 박사며 학사며 신학생들이 그토록 많아서, 마치 배를 고르듯이 이건 가지겠다, 저건 안 가지겠다 하며 그중에서 고르실 수도 있는데 말입니다.' 그러자 과부가 매우 발랄하고 솔직하게 그에게 대답했다. '선생님, 선생님은 크게 잘못 아셨고, 생각도 아주 구식이십니다. 제가 아무개를 택한 게 잘못된 선택이라고 여기신다면 말이지요. 아무리 그가 어리석어 보여도, 제가 그에게 바라는 모든 일에서는 그가 아리스토텔레스만큼이나, 아니 그 이상으로 철학을 알고 있으니까요.' 마찬가지로, 산초야, 내가 둘시네아 델 토보소에게 바라는 모든 일에서 그분은 세상에서 가장 고귀한 공주만큼이나 훌륭하니라. 부인들을 제멋대로 지어낸 이름으로 부르며 그 미덕을 노래한 그 모든 시인이 정말로 그런 연인을 두었다고 여겨서는 안 되느니라. 책이며, 노래며, 이발소며, 극장이 가득 채우고 있는 그 아마릴리스, 필리스, 실비아, 디아나, 갈라테아, 필리다, 그 밖의 모든 여인이 정말로 살과 피를 가진 진짜 부인이었고, 그들을 찬미하고 또 찬미해 온 자들의 연인이었다고 생각하느냐? 천만의 말씀이다. 그들은 대개 자기 시구의 소재를 마련하고, 자신이 연인으로, 혹은 연인이 될 만큼 용맹한 사내로 통하게 하려고 그런 여인들을 지어낸 것뿐이니라. 그러니 나로서는 그 착한 알돈사 로렌소가 아름답고 정숙하다고 생각하고 믿는 것으로 충분하니라. 그분의 가문으로 말하자면 그것은 거의 중요하지 않으니, 그분에게 어떤 작위를 내리려고 그것을 조사할 사람은 아무도 없을 테고, 나로서는 그분을 세상에서 가장 고귀한 공주로 여기노라. 산초야, 네가 모른다면 알아 두어야 할 것이니, 다른 무엇보다도 사랑을 불러일으키는 두 가지가 있으니, 그것은 빼어난 아름다움과 좋은 평판이니라. 그리고 이 두 가지가 둘시네아에게는 더없이 높은 정도로 갖추어져 있으니, 아름다움에서는 아무도 그분에 견줄 수 없고, 좋은 평판에서는 그분에 가까이 다가서는 이가 드무니라. 한마디로 요약하건대, 나는 내가 말하는 모든 것이 더도 덜도 아니라 내가 말하는 그대로라고 스스로 믿으며, 아름다움에서나 신분에서나 내가 바라는 모습 그대로 그분을 내 상상 속에 그려 내노라. 헬레네도 그분에게 미치지 못하고, 루크레티아도 그분에 이르지 못하며, 그리스든 이방이든 라틴이든 옛 시대의 그 어떤 이름난 여인도 마찬가지이니라. 그러니 저마다 하고 싶은 말을 하라지. 이 일로 무지한 자들에게 내가 책잡힌다 한들, 안목 있는 자들에게는 비난받지 않을 테니 말이다.\""]],[["“I say that your worship is entirely right,” said Sancho, “and that I am an ass. But I know not how the name of ass came into my mouth, for a rope is not to be mentioned in the house of him who has been hanged; but now for the letter, and then, God be with you, I am off.”","\"나리 말씀이 전적으로 옳다고 생각합니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그리고 저는 당나귀입니다. 하지만 어쩌다 당나귀란 말이 제 입에 올랐는지 모르겠군요. 목매달린 자의 집에서는 밧줄 이야기를 꺼내는 게 아닌데 말입니다. 이제 편지를 주십시오. 그럼 하느님과 함께 가시기를, 저는 떠나겠습니다.\""]],[["Don Quixote took out the note-book, and, retiring to one side, very deliberately began to write the letter, and when he had finished it he called to Sancho, saying he wished to read it to him, so that he might commit it to memory, in case of losing it on the road; for with evil fortune like his anything might be apprehended. To which Sancho replied, “Write it two or three times there in the book and give it to me, and I will carry it very carefully, because to expect me to keep it in my memory is all nonsense, for I have such a bad one that I often forget my own name; but for all that repeat it to me, as I shall like to hear it, for surely it will run as if it was in print.”","돈키호테는 수첩을 꺼내 한쪽으로 물러나 매우 신중하게 편지를 쓰기 시작했고, 다 쓰고 나자 산초를 불러 그것을 읽어 주겠다고 말했다. 길에서 그것을 잃어버릴 경우에 대비해 그가 외워 두게 하기 위해서였으니, 그의 운수처럼 사나운 운수라면 무슨 일이든 염려스러웠던 것이다. 이에 산초가 대답했다. \"그 수첩에다 두세 번 옮겨 써서 제게 주십시오. 그러면 제가 아주 조심스럽게 가져가겠습니다. 제가 그걸 기억에 담아 두기를 바라시는 건 다 부질없는 일이니까요. 저는 기억력이 어찌나 형편없는지 제 이름조차 종종 잊어버릴 정도거든요. 하지만 그래도 제게 한번 읽어 주십시오. 듣고 싶으니까요. 분명 인쇄된 것처럼 술술 읽힐 테니 말입니다.\" \"들어 보아라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"여기 이렇게 적혀 있느니라."]],[["“Listen,” said Don Quixote, “this is what it says:","둘시네아 델 토보소에게 보내는 돈키호테의 편지"]],[["“Sovereign and exalted Lady,--The pierced by the point of absence, the wounded to the heart’s core, sends thee, sweetest Dulcinea del Toboso, the health that he himself enjoys not. If thy beauty despises me, if thy worth is not for me, if thy scorn is my affliction, though I be sufficiently long-suffering, hardly shall I endure this anxiety, which, besides being oppressive, is protracted. My good squire Sancho will relate to thee in full, fair ingrate, dear enemy, the condition to which I am reduced on thy account: if it be thy pleasure to give me relief, I am thine; if not, do as may be pleasing to thee; for by ending my life I shall satisfy thy cruelty and my desire.","지존하고 고귀한 부인이여. 이별의 칼끝에 찔리고 마음 깊은 곳까지 상처 입은 자가, 더없이 다정한 둘시네아 델 토보소여, 그대에게 자신은 누리지 못하는 건강을 보냅니다. 그대의 아름다움이 나를 멸시하고, 그대의 가치가 나를 위한 것이 아니며, 그대의 경멸이 내 괴로움이라면, 내가 비록 충분히 오래 견디는 자라 할지라도, 이 괴로움을 견디기 어려울 것입니다. 그것은 짓누르는 것일 뿐 아니라 오래 끄는 것이기 때문입니다. 나의 충직한 종자 산초가 그대에게 낱낱이 들려드릴 것입니다, 아리따운 배은망덕한 이여, 사랑스러운 원수여, 그대 때문에 내가 이르게 된 처지를. 만약 내게 위안을 베푸는 것이 그대의 기쁨이라면 나는 그대의 것이요, 그렇지 않다면 그대의 마음에 드는 대로 하시오. 내 목숨을 끊음으로써 나는 그대의 잔혹함과 내 소망을 모두 채울 것이니 말입니다."]],[["“Thine till death,","죽을 때까지 그대의 것인,"]],[["“The Knight of the Rueful Countenance.”","슬픈 몰골의 기사.\""]],[["“By the life of my father,”","\"제 아버지의 목숨을 두고 말씀드리건대,\""],["said Sancho, when he heard the letter, “it is the loftiest thing I ever heard.","편지를 들은 산초가 말했다. \"여태껏 제가 들어 본 것 중 가장 고상한 것입니다."],["Body of me!","이런 세상에!"],["how your worship says everything as you like in it!","나리께서는 어찌나 하고 싶은 말을 그 안에 다 담으셨는지요!"],["And how well you fit in ‘The Knight of the Rueful Countenance’ into the signature.","그리고 '슬픈 몰골의 기사'를 서명에 어찌나 잘 끼워 넣으셨는지요."],["I declare your worship is indeed the very devil, and there is nothing you don’t know.”","정말이지 나리께서는 진짜 악마이시고, 모르시는 게 없으십니다.\""]],[["“Everything is needed for the calling I follow,”","\"내가 따르는 소명에는 모든 것이 필요하니라.\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“Now then,”","\"자, 그러면,\""],["said Sancho, “let your worship put the order for the three ass-colts on the other side, and sign it very plainly, that they may recognise it at first sight.”","산초가 말했다. \"나리께서는 그 뒷면에다 당나귀 새끼 세 마리에 대한 인도장을 적으시고, 한눈에 알아볼 수 있도록 아주 또렷하게 서명해 주십시오.\""]],[["“With all my heart,”","\"기꺼이 그러마.\""],["said Don Quixote, and as he had written it he read it to this effect:","돈키호테가 말했다. 그러고는 다 쓰고 나서 이런 내용으로 그것을 읽어 주었다."]],[["“Mistress Niece,--By this first of ass-colts please pay to Sancho Panza, my squire, three of the five I left at home in your charge: said three ass-colts to be paid and delivered for the same number received here in hand, which upon this and upon his receipt shall be duly paid.","\"조카딸에게. 이 첫 번째 당나귀 새끼 인도장으로, 내가 집에 두고 너에게 맡긴 다섯 마리 가운데 세 마리를 내 종자 산초 판사에게 내어 주거라."],["Done in the heart of the Sierra Morena, the twenty-seventh of August of this present year.”","이 세 마리 당나귀 새끼는 이곳에서 직접 받은 같은 수에 대한 대가로 지불하고 인도되어야 하며, 이 인도장과 그의 영수를 근거로 응당 지불될 것이다. 시에라 모레나 한복판에서, 올해 팔월 이십칠일에 작성함.\""]],[["“That will do,”","\"그거면 됩니다.\""],["said Sancho; “now let your worship sign it.”","산초가 말했다. \"이제 나리께서 서명해 주십시오.\""]],[["“There is no need to sign it,”","\"서명할 필요는 없고,\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “but merely to put my flourish, which is the same as a signature, and enough for three asses, or even three hundred.”","\"그저 내 화필 장식만 그려 넣으면 되느니라. 그것이 서명이나 마찬가지이니, 당나귀 세 마리는 물론이요 삼백 마리라도 충분하니라.\""]],[["“I can trust your worship,” returned Sancho; “let me go and saddle Rocinante, and be ready to give me your blessing, for I mean to go at once without seeing the fooleries your worship is going to do; I’ll say I saw you do so many that she will not want any more.”","\"저는 나리를 믿겠습니다.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제가 가서 로시난테에 안장을 얹을 테니, 제게 축복을 내려 주실 채비를 해 주십시오. 저는 나리께서 하실 그 어리석은 짓들을 보지 않고 당장 떠날 작정이니까요. 제가 그토록 많은 짓을 하시는 걸 보았다고 말씀드리면, 마님께서도 더는 듣고 싶어 하지 않으실 겁니다.\""]],[["“At any rate, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “I should like--and there is reason for it--I should like thee, I say, to see me stripped to the skin and performing a dozen or two of insanities, which I can get done in less than half an hour; for having seen them with thine own eyes, thou canst then safely swear to the rest that thou wouldst add; and I promise thee thou wilt not tell of as many as I mean to perform.”","\"어쨌든, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"나는 네가 — 거기엔 까닭이 있느니라 — 내가 알몸이 되도록 옷을 벗고 한두 다스쯤 미친 짓을 하는 것을 보았으면 한다. 그것은 반 시간도 안 되어 해낼 수 있느니라. 네 눈으로 직접 그것을 보고 나면, 네가 거기에 보태고 싶은 나머지에 대해서도 떳떳이 맹세할 수 있을 테니 말이다. 그리고 내 약속하건대, 너는 내가 행하려는 것만큼 많이 이야기하지는 못할 것이다.\""]],[["“For the love of God, master mine,” said Sancho, “let me not see your worship stripped, for it will sorely grieve me, and I shall not be able to keep from tears, and my head aches so with all I shed last night for Dapple, that I am not fit to begin any fresh weeping; but if it is your worship’s pleasure that I should see some insanities, do them in your clothes, short ones, and such as come readiest to hand; for I myself want nothing of the sort, and, as I have said, it will be a saving of time for my return, which will be with the news your worship desires and deserves. If not, let the lady Dulcinea look to it; if she does not answer reasonably, I swear as solemnly as I can that I will fetch a fair answer out of her stomach with kicks and cuffs; for why should it be borne that a knight-errant as famous as your worship should go mad without rhyme or reason for a--? Her ladyship had best not drive me to say it, for by God I will speak out and let off everything cheap, even if it doesn’t sell: I am pretty good at that! she little knows me; faith, if she knew me she’d be in awe of me.”","\"제발 부탁이니, 주인 나리.\" 산초가 말했다. \"나리께서 옷을 벗으시는 건 보지 않게 해 주십시오. 그걸 보면 저는 몹시 슬퍼져서 눈물을 참을 수가 없을 겁니다. 게다가 어젯밤 잿빛 녀석 때문에 어찌나 울었던지 머리가 다 아파서, 새로 또 울 처지가 못 됩니다. 하지만 제가 미친 짓을 좀 봐야 하는 게 나리의 뜻이라면, 옷을 입은 채로, 짧게, 손쉽게 할 수 있는 것들로 해 주십시오. 저로서는 그런 게 전혀 필요 없거니와, 말씀드렸듯이 그래야 제가 돌아가는 시간을 아낄 수 있으니까요. 나리께서 바라시고 또 받으실 만한 소식을 가지고 돌아오겠습니다. 만약 그렇지 못하면, 둘시네아 마님이 알아서 하셔야겠지요. 마님이 사리에 맞게 답하지 않으신다면, 제가 할 수 있는 가장 엄숙한 맹세를 걸고 말씀드리건대, 발길질과 주먹질로 마님 뱃속에서라도 제대로 된 답을 끄집어내겠습니다. 나리처럼 이름난 편력 기사가 일개— 때문에 까닭도 없이 미쳐 버린다는 게 어디 말이나 됩니까? 마님께서 제가 그 말을 입에 올리게 만들지 않으시는 게 좋을 겁니다. 하느님께 맹세코 저는 다 털어놓고, 팔리지 않더라도 죄다 헐값에 쏟아 낼 테니까요. 저는 그런 데는 제법 능합니다! 마님은 저를 잘 모르시지요. 정말이지 마님이 저를 아셨더라면 저를 어려워하셨을 겁니다.\""]],[["“In faith, Sancho,”","\"맹세코, 산초야.\""],["said Don Quixote, “to all appearance thou art no sounder in thy wits than I.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"겉보기로는 너도 나 못지않게 제정신이 아닌 듯하구나.\""]],[["“I am not so mad,”","\"저는 그렇게 미치진 않았습니다.\""],["answered Sancho, “but I am more peppery; but apart from all this, what has your worship to eat until I come back?","산초가 대답했다. \"다만 성미가 좀 더 매울 뿐이지요."],["Will you sally out on the road like Cardenio to force it from the shepherds?”","그건 그렇고, 제가 돌아올 때까지 나리께서는 무얼 잡수실 작정입니까? 카르데니오처럼 길로 나가서 양치기들한테서 빼앗으실 겁니까?\""]],[["“Let not that anxiety trouble thee,”","\"그런 걱정일랑 너를 괴롭히지 못하게 하여라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote, “for even if I had it I should not eat anything but the herbs and the fruits which this meadow and these trees may yield me; the beauty of this business of mine lies in not eating, and in performing other mortifications.”","\"설령 먹을 것이 있다 해도, 나는 이 풀밭과 이 나무들이 내게 내어 줄 풀과 열매 말고는 아무것도 먹지 않을 것이니라. 이 일의 묘미는 먹지 않는 것과 그 밖의 고행을 행하는 데 있느니라.\""]],[["“Do you know what I am afraid of?”","\"제가 무얼 두려워하는지 아십니까?\" 이에 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho upon this; “that I shall not be able to find my way back to this spot where I am leaving you, it is such an out-of-the-way place.”","\"나리를 두고 떠나는 이 자리로 되돌아오는 길을 찾지 못할까 봐서입니다. 이렇게 외진 곳이니 말입니다.\""]],[["“Observe the landmarks well,” said Don Quixote, “for I will try not to go far from this neighbourhood, and I will even take care to mount the highest of these rocks to see if I can discover thee returning; however, not to miss me and lose thyself, the best plan will be to cut some branches of the broom that is so abundant about here, and as thou goest to lay them at intervals until thou hast come out upon the plain; these will serve thee, after the fashion of the clue in the labyrinth of Theseus, as marks and signs for finding me on thy return.”","\"표지물을 잘 보아 두어라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"나는 이 부근에서 멀리 벗어나지 않도록 애쓸 것이고, 심지어 이 바위들 가운데 가장 높은 곳에 올라가 네가 돌아오는지 살펴보기까지 할 것이다. 허나 나를 놓치고 길을 잃지 않으려면, 이 근방에 흔하디흔한 금작화 가지를 좀 베어, 가다가 들판에 나설 때까지 군데군데 놓아두는 것이 가장 좋은 방법이겠구나. 그것이 테세우스의 미로에서의 실타래처럼, 네가 돌아올 때 나를 찾는 표시이자 길잡이가 되어 줄 것이다.\""]],[["“So I will,”","\"그렇게 하겠습니다.\""],["said Sancho Panza, and having cut some, he asked his master’s blessing, and not without many tears on both sides, took his leave of him, and mounting Rocinante, of whom Don Quixote charged him earnestly to have as much care as of his own person, he set out for the plain, strewing at intervals the branches of broom as his master had recommended him; and so he went his way, though Don Quixote still entreated him to see him do were it only a couple of mad acts.","산초 판사가 말하고는 금작화 가지를 몇 개 벤 다음, 주인에게 축복을 청하고, 양쪽 다 적잖은 눈물을 흘리며 그에게 작별을 고했다. 그러고는 돈키호테가 자기 몸처럼 잘 보살피라고 간곡히 당부한 로시난테에 올라, 주인이 일러 준 대로 군데군데 금작화 가지를 흩뿌리며 들판을 향해 길을 나섰다."],["He had not gone a hundred paces, however, when he returned and said:","그렇게 그는 제 갈 길을 갔는데, 그동안에도 돈키호테는 단 두어 가지라도 미친 짓을 하는 것을 보고 가라고 그에게 계속 간청했다. 그러나 그가 백 걸음도 채 가지 않아 되돌아와서 말했다."]],[["“I must say, señor, your worship said quite right, that in order to be able to swear without a weight on my conscience that I had seen you do mad things, it would be well for me to see if it were only one; though in your worship’s remaining here I have seen a very great one.”","\"말씀드려야겠습니다, 나리. 나리 말씀이 참으로 옳았습니다. 나리께서 미친 짓을 하시는 걸 제가 보았다고 양심에 거리낌 없이 맹세하려면, 단 한 가지라도 보아 두는 것이 좋겠지요. 물론 나리께서 이곳에 남아 계신다는 것만으로도 저는 이미 아주 대단한 미친 짓 하나를 본 셈이지만 말입니다.\""]],[["“Did I not tell thee so?”","\"내 그렇게 말하지 않았더냐?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."],["“Wait, Sancho, and I will do them in the saying of a credo,”","\"기다려라, 산초, 사도신경 한 번 욀 사이에 해 보이마.\" 그러고는 부랴부랴 바지를 벗어 알몸에 셔츠 차림만 남기더니, 더 지체할 것도 없이 공중으로 두 차례 깡충 뛰어오르고, 머리를 아래로 발을 위로 하여 두 차례 공중제비를 넘으며 어찌나 볼만하게 굴었는지, 그것을 두 번 다시 보지 않으려고 산초는 로시난테를 돌려 세웠으니, 이제 마음이 놓이고 흡족해져서 주인을 미친 사람으로 두고 떠났다고 떳떳이 맹세할 수 있게 되었다."],["and pulling off his breeches in all haste he stripped himself to his skin and his shirt, and then, without more ado, he cut a couple of gambados in the air, and a couple of somersaults, heels over head, making such a display that, not to see it a second time, Sancho wheeled Rocinante round, and felt easy, and satisfied in his mind that he could swear he had left his master mad; and so we will leave him to follow his road until his return, which was a quick one.","그러니 그가 돌아올 때까지 우리는 그를 제 갈 길을 가도록 내버려 두기로 하자. 그것은 빠른 걸음이었다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXVI. IN WHICH ARE CONTINUED THE REFINEMENTS WHEREWITH DON QUIXOTE PLAYED THE PART OF A LOVER IN THE SIERRA MORENA","# CHAPTER XXVI."]],[["Returning to the proceedings of him of the Rueful Countenance when he found himself alone, the history says that when Don Quixote had completed the performance of the somersaults or capers, naked from the waist down and clothed from the waist up, and saw that Sancho had gone off without waiting to see any more crazy feats, he climbed up to the top of a high rock, and there set himself to consider what he had several times before considered without ever coming to any conclusion on the point, namely whether it would be better and more to his purpose to imitate the outrageous madness of Roland, or the melancholy madness of Amadis; and communing with himself he said:","혼자 남게 된 슬픈 몰골의 기사가 어떻게 행동했는지로 돌아가 보면, 역사가 전하는 바에 따르면 돈키호테는 허리 아래로는 벌거벗고 허리 위로는 옷을 걸친 채 공중제비와 뜀뛰기를 다 마치고 나서, 산초가 더 이상의 미친 짓을 보지도 않고 떠나 버린 것을 알아챘다. 그러자 그는 높은 바위 꼭대기로 기어올라가, 이전에도 여러 번 곱씹었으나 끝내 아무런 결론에도 이르지 못했던 문제를 다시금 헤아리기 시작했으니, 곧 롤단의 광포한 광기를 흉내 내는 것이 더 나을지, 아니면 아마디스의 우울한 광기를 흉내 내는 것이 자기 목적에 더 어울릴지 하는 것이었다. 그러고는 혼자 중얼거렸다."]],[["“What wonder is it if Roland was so good a knight and so valiant as everyone says he was, when, after all, he was enchanted, and nobody could kill him save by thrusting a corking pin into the sole of his foot, and he always wore shoes with seven iron soles?","\"롤단이 모두가 말하듯 그토록 훌륭한 기사이자 그토록 용맹한 자였다 한들 무에 그리 놀랄 일이겠는가? 따지고 보면 그는 마법에 걸려 있어 발바닥에 굵은 핀을 찔러 넣지 않고서는 아무도 그를 죽일 수 없었고, 그래서 그는 늘 일곱 겹의 쇠바닥을 댄 신을 신고 다녔으니 말이다."],["Though cunning devices did not avail him against Bernardo del Carpio, who knew all about them, and strangled him in his arms at Roncesvalles.","하기야 그 모든 것을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있던 베르나르도 델 카르피오에게는 그 교묘한 술책도 통하지 않았으니, 베르나르도는 론세스바예스에서 그를 두 팔로 끌어안아 목 졸라 죽였더라."],["But putting the question of his valour aside, let us come to his losing his wits, for certain it is that he did lose them in consequence of the proofs he discovered at the fountain, and the intelligence the shepherd gave him of Angelica having slept more than two siestas with Medoro, a little curly-headed Moor, and page to Agramante.","그러나 그의 용맹은 잠시 제쳐 두고, 그가 정신을 잃은 대목으로 가 보자. 그가 정신을 잃은 것은 분명 샘가에서 발견한 증거들과, 안헬리카가 아그라만테의 시동인 곱슬머리 어린 무어인 메도로와 두 낮잠 이상의 시간을 함께 잤다고 양치기가 일러 준 소식 때문이었다."],["If he was persuaded that this was true, and that his lady had wronged him, it is no wonder that he should have gone mad; but I, how am I to imitate him in his madness, unless I can imitate him in the cause of it?","만일 그가 이것이 사실이며 자기 여인이 자기를 배신했다고 믿게 되었다면, 그가 미쳐 버린 것도 놀랄 일이 아니다. 하지만 나는, 그 미침의 원인까지 흉내 낼 수 없다면 어찌 그의 광기를 흉내 낼 수 있겠는가?"],["For my Dulcinea, I will venture to swear, never saw a Moor in her life, as he is, in his proper costume, and she is this day as the mother that bore her, and I should plainly be doing her a wrong if, fancying anything else, I were to go mad with the same kind of madness as Roland the Furious.","내 둘시네아로 말하자면, 감히 맹세컨대 평생 무어인을 그 본디 차림새 그대로 본 적이 한 번도 없으며, 그녀는 오늘날까지도 그녀를 낳은 어머니 그대로일 터이니, 만일 내가 무엇인가 다른 것을 상상하여 광란의 롤단과 똑같은 종류의 광기에 빠진다면 나는 명백히 그녀에게 부당한 짓을 저지르는 것이 되리라."],["On the other hand, I see that Amadis of Gaul, without losing his senses and without doing anything mad, acquired as a lover as much fame as the most famous; for, according to his history, on finding himself rejected by his lady Oriana, who had ordered him not to appear in her presence until it should be her pleasure, all he did was to retire to the Pena Pobre in company with a hermit, and there he took his fill of weeping until Heaven sent him relief in the midst of his great grief and need.","다른 한편으로, 나는 갈리아의 아마디스가 분별을 잃지도 않고 미친 짓이라곤 하나도 하지 않고서도, 연인으로서 가장 이름 높은 자 못지않은 명성을 얻었음을 본다. 그의 이야기에 따르면, 그는 자기 여인 오리아나에게 거절당하여, 그녀가 허락할 때까지는 자기 앞에 나타나지 말라는 명을 받게 되자, 그가 한 일이라곤 어느 은자와 더불어 페냐 포브레로 물러나, 하늘이 그 크나큰 슬픔과 고난 가운데서 위안을 보내 줄 때까지 실컷 울었던 것뿐이었다."],["And if this be true, as it is, why should I now take the trouble to strip stark naked, or do mischief to these trees which have done me no harm, or why am I to disturb the clear waters of these brooks which will give me to drink whenever I have a mind?","이것이 사실이라면, 또 사실이거니와, 어찌하여 내가 이제 와서 알몸으로 발가벗는 수고를 하거나, 내게 아무 해도 끼치지 않은 이 나무들에 행패를 부리거나, 내가 마시고 싶을 때면 언제든 마실 물을 내어 줄 이 시냇물의 맑은 물을 어지럽혀야 한단 말인가? 아마디스의 기억이여 길이 살아남으라, 그리고 라만차의 돈키호테가 할 수 있는 한 그를 본받게 하라."],["Long live the memory of Amadis and let him be imitated so far as is possible by Don Quixote of La Mancha, of whom it will be said, as was said of the other, that if he did not achieve great things, he died in attempting them; and if I am not repulsed or rejected by my Dulcinea, it is enough for me, as I have said, to be absent from her.","그에 관해서는 다른 이에 관해 일컬어졌던 그대로, 비록 위대한 일을 이루지는 못했어도 그것을 이루고자 애쓰다 죽었노라고 일컬어지리라. 그리고 내가 내 둘시네아에게 퇴짜를 맞거나 거절당한 것이 아닌 바에야, 내가 말했듯 그녀와 떨어져 있는 것만으로 내게는 충분하다."],["And so, now to business; come to my memory ye deeds of Amadis, and show me how I am to begin to imitate you.","자, 그러면 이제 일에 착수하자. 아마디스의 행적들이여 내 기억으로 와서, 내가 어떻게 그대들을 본받기 시작해야 할지 보여 다오."],["I know already that what he chiefly did was to pray and commend himself to God; but what am I to do for a rosary, for I have not got one?”","그가 무엇보다 먼저 한 일이 기도하며 하느님께 자신을 의탁하는 것이었음은 내 이미 알고 있다. 하지만 묵주는 어찌한단 말인가, 내겐 묵주가 없는데?\""]],[["And then it occurred to him how he might make one, and that was by tearing a great strip off the tail of his shirt which hung down, and making eleven knots on it, one bigger than the rest, and this served him for a rosary all the time he was there, during which he repeated countless ave-marias. But what distressed him greatly was not having another hermit there to confess him and receive consolation from; and so he solaced himself with pacing up and down the little meadow, and writing and carving on the bark of the trees and on the fine sand a multitude of verses all in harmony with his sadness, and some in praise of Dulcinea; but, when he was found there afterwards, the only ones completely legible that could be discovered were those that follow here:","그때 그에게 묵주를 만들 방도가 떠올랐으니, 그것은 늘어진 셔츠 자락의 끝을 길게 한 가닥 찢어 내어 거기에 매듭 열한 개를 짓되 그중 하나는 나머지보다 더 크게 만드는 것이었다. 이것이 그가 그곳에 머무는 내내 묵주 노릇을 했고, 그동안 그는 수도 없이 많은 성모송을 외웠다. 그러나 그를 몹시 괴롭힌 것은, 그곳에 자기 고해를 들어 주고 위안을 베풀어 줄 또 다른 은자가 없다는 사실이었다. 그리하여 그는 작은 풀밭을 이리저리 거닐며, 나무껍질과 고운 모래 위에 자신의 슬픔에 어울리는 수많은 시구를, 더러는 둘시네아를 찬미하는 시구를 쓰고 새기며 스스로를 달랬다. 하지만 나중에 그가 그곳에서 발견되었을 때, 온전히 읽어 낼 수 있었던 것이라고는 다음에 나오는 것들뿐이었다."]],[["Ye on the mountain side that grow, Ye green things all, trees, shrubs, and bushes, Are ye aweary of the woe That this poor aching bosom crushes?","산허리에 자라나는 그대들이여, 푸르른 것들이여, 나무며 떨기며 덤불이여, 이 가련하고 쓰린 가슴을 짓누르는 그 비탄에 그대들도 지쳐 버렸는가?"],["If it disturb you, and I owe Some reparation, it may be a Defence for me to let you know Don Quixote’s tears are on the flow, And all for distant Dulcinea Del Toboso.","그것이 그대들을 어지럽히고, 내가 무언가 보상해야 할 빚이 있다면, 이 한마디 알려 줌이 내겐 변명이 되리라. 돈키호테의 눈물이 흐르고 있노라, 그 모두가 머나먼 둘시네아 델 토보소를 위함이라네."]],[["The lealest lover time can show, Doomed for a lady-love to languish, Among these solitudes doth go, A prey to every kind of anguish.","세월이 보여 줄 수 있는 가장 충직한 연인이, 한 여인을 사모하다 시들도록 운명 지어져, 이 적막한 곳들 사이를 헤매며 가노라, 온갖 종류의 고뇌에 사로잡힌 채로."],["Why Love should like a spiteful foe Thus use him, he hath no idea, But hogsheads full--this doth he know-- Don Quixote’s tears are on the flow, And all for distant Dulcinea Del Toboso.","어찌하여 사랑이 심술궂은 원수처럼 그를 이리 부려 먹는지 그는 알지 못하나, 큰 술통 가득—이것만은 그도 안다네— 돈키호테의 눈물이 흐르고 있노라, 그 모두가 머나먼 둘시네아 델 토보소를 위함이라네."]],[["Adventure-seeking doth he go Up rugged heights, down rocky valleys, But hill or dale, or high or low, Mishap attendeth all his sallies: Love still pursues him to and fro, And plies his cruel scourge--ah me!","모험을 좇아 그는 가노라, 험준한 봉우리를 오르고, 바위투성이 골짜기를 내려가며, 허나 언덕이든 골짜기든, 높든 낮든, 그의 모든 출정엔 불운이 따라붙네. 사랑은 여전히 그를 이리저리 쫓으며, 그 잔인한 채찍을 휘두르나니—아, 슬프도다!"],["a Relentless fate, an endless woe; Don Quixote’s tears are on the flow, And all for distant Dulcinea Del Toboso.","가차 없는 운명이여, 끝없는 비탄이여. 돈키호테의 눈물이 흐르고 있노라, 그 모두가 머나먼 둘시네아 델 토보소를 위함이라네."]],[["The addition of “Del Toboso” to Dulcinea’s name gave rise to no little laughter among those who found the above lines, for they suspected Don Quixote must have fancied that unless he added “del Toboso”","둘시네아의 이름에 \"델 토보소\"를 덧붙인 것은 위의 시구를 발견한 이들 사이에서 적잖은 웃음을 자아냈으니, 그들은 돈키호테가 둘시네아라는 이름을 끌어들일 때 \"델 토보소\"를 덧붙이지 않으면 시구가 알아들을 수 없게 되리라 여겼던 것이 틀림없다고 짐작했기 때문이다."],["when he introduced the name of Dulcinea the verse would be unintelligible; which was indeed the fact, as he himself afterwards admitted.","그리고 그것은 과연 사실이었으니, 그 자신도 훗날 인정한 바이다. 그는 이보다 훨씬 많은 시구를 썼으나, 앞서 말했듯이 또렷하고 완전하게 판독할 수 있었던 것은 이 세 편이 전부였다."],["He wrote many more, but, as has been said, these three verses were all that could be plainly and perfectly deciphered.","이런 식으로, 또 한숨지으며 숲의 파우누스와 사티로스들과 시냇물의 님프들과, 촉촉하고 구슬픈 메아리를 불러내어 자기에게 답하고 위로하고 귀 기울여 달라 청하며, 또한 자신을 지탱해 줄 풀들을 찾아다니며, 그는 산초가 돌아올 때까지 시간을 보냈다."],["In this way, and in sighing and calling on the fauns and satyrs of the woods and the nymphs of the streams, and Echo, moist and mournful, to answer, console, and hear him, as well as in looking for herbs to sustain him, he passed his time until Sancho’s return; and had that been delayed three weeks, as it was three days, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance would have worn such an altered countenance that the mother that bore him would not have known him: and here it will be well to leave him, wrapped up in sighs and verses, to relate how Sancho Panza fared on his mission.","그리고 산초의 귀환이 사흘 대신 삼 주나 늦어졌더라면, 슬픈 몰골의 기사는 그를 낳은 어머니조차 알아보지 못할 만큼 몰골이 바뀌어 있었으리라. 그러니 여기서는 그를 한숨과 시구에 파묻힌 채 내버려 두고, 산초 판사가 그 사명을 어떻게 치렀는지 이야기하는 편이 좋겠다."]],[["As for him, coming out upon the high road, he made for El Toboso, and the next day reached the inn where the mishap of the blanket had befallen him.","산초로 말하자면, 큰길로 나서서 엘 토보소를 향했고, 이튿날에는 담요 키질의 봉변을 당했던 그 객줏집에 다다랐다."],["As soon as he recognised it he felt as if he were once more living through the air, and he could not bring himself to enter it though it was an hour when he might well have done so, for it was dinner-time, and he longed to taste something hot as it had been all cold fare with him for many days past.","그곳을 알아보자마자 그는 다시 한번 공중을 떠다니는 것만 같은 기분이 들었고, 들어가도 좋을 만한 시각이었음에도 — 마침 저녁 식사 때라 며칠째 줄곧 찬 음식만 먹어 온 터에 무언가 뜨거운 것을 맛보고 싶어 안달이었음에도 — 도무지 안으로 들어설 마음이 나지 않았다."],["This craving drove him to draw near to the inn, still undecided whether to go in or not, and as he was hesitating there came out two persons who at once recognised him, and said one to the other:","이 갈망이 그를 객줏집 가까이로 이끌었으나, 그는 여전히 들어갈지 말지 마음을 정하지 못하고 머뭇거렸다. 그렇게 망설이고 있는데 두 사람이 밖으로 나오더니, 곧바로 그를 알아보고 서로에게 말했다."]],[["“Señor licentiate, is not he on the horse there Sancho Panza who, our adventurer’s housekeeper told us, went off with her master as esquire?”","\"학사 양반, 저기 말 위에 있는 자가, 우리네 모험가의 가정부가 일러 주기를 그 주인과 함께 종자로 떠났다던 산초 판사가 아니오?\""]],[["“So it is,”","\"바로 그자요.\" 학사가 말했다."],["said the licentiate, “and that is our friend Don Quixote’s horse;”","\"그리고 저것은 우리 친구 돈키호테의 말이로구먼.\" 그들이 그를 그토록 잘 알아본 것은, 그들이 바로 그가 사는 마을의 신부와 이발사였기 때문이니, 책들에 대해 심문과 선고를 집행했던 바로 그 사람들이었다."],["and if they knew him so well it was because they were the curate and the barber of his own village, the same who had carried out the scrutiny and sentence upon the books; and as soon as they recognised Sancho Panza and Rocinante, being anxious to hear of Don Quixote, they approached, and calling him by his name the curate said, “Friend Sancho Panza, where is your master?”","산초 판사와 로시난테를 알아보자마자 돈키호테의 소식이 몹시 궁금했던 그들은 다가와서 그의 이름을 부르며, 신부가 말했다. \"산초 판사 친구, 자네 주인은 어디 있나?\""]],[["Sancho recognised them at once, and determined to keep secret the place and circumstances where and under which he had left his master, so he replied that his master was engaged in a certain quarter on a certain matter of great importance to him which he could not disclose for the eyes in his head.","산초는 곧바로 그들을 알아보았으나, 자기 주인을 어디에, 어떤 사정으로 두고 왔는지는 비밀로 해 두기로 마음먹고, 자기 주인은 어느 곳에서 그에게 매우 중대한 어떤 일에 매여 있는데, 자기 머릿속의 두 눈을 걸고서라도 그것을 발설할 수 없노라고 대답했다."]],[["“Nay, nay,” said the barber, “if you don’t tell us where he is, Sancho Panza, we will suspect as we suspect already, that you have murdered and robbed him, for here you are mounted on his horse; in fact, you must produce the master of the hack, or else take the consequences.”","\"안 되네, 안 돼.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"그가 어디 있는지 우리에게 말하지 않으면, 산초 판사, 우리는 이미 의심하고 있는 대로 자네가 그를 죽이고 그 물건을 빼앗았다고 의심하겠네. 자네가 지금 그의 말을 타고 있으니 말이야. 정말이지 자네는 이 짐말의 주인을 내놓든지, 아니면 그 책임을 지든지 해야 하네.\""]],[["“There is no need of threats with me,”","\"제게 협박은 필요 없습니다.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “for I am not a man to rob or murder anybody; let his own fate, or God who made him, kill each one; my master is engaged very much to his taste doing penance in the midst of these mountains;”","\"저는 남을 빼앗거나 죽일 사람이 아니니까요. 각자의 운명이, 혹은 그를 만드신 하느님이 각자를 죽이게 두십시오."],["and then, offhand and without stopping, he told them how he had left him, what adventures had befallen him, and how he was carrying a letter to the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, the daughter of Lorenzo Corchuelo, with whom he was over head and ears in love.","제 주인은 이 산속에서 더없이 마음에 들어 하며 고행을 치르고 계십니다.\" 그러고는 거침없이 멈추지도 않고, 어떻게 그를 두고 왔는지, 무슨 모험들이 그에게 닥쳤는지, 그리고 자기가 어떻게 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님께, 곧 주인이 머리끝까지 사랑에 빠진 로렌소 코르추엘로의 딸에게 보내는 편지를 지니고 가는 길인지를 그들에게 들려주었다."],["They were both amazed at what Sancho Panza told them; for though they were aware of Don Quixote’s madness and the nature of it, each time they heard of it they were filled with fresh wonder.","산초 판사가 들려준 이야기에 둘은 모두 깜짝 놀랐으니, 돈키호테의 광기와 그 본질을 알고 있었음에도 그 이야기를 들을 때마다 새삼스러운 경탄에 사로잡혔던 것이다."],["They then asked Sancho Panza to show them the letter he was carrying to the lady Dulcinea del Toboso.","이어서 그들은 산초 판사에게 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님께 가져가는 그 편지를 보여 달라고 청했다."],["He said it was written in a note-book, and that his master’s directions were that he should have it copied on paper at the first village he came to.","그는 그것이 수첩에 적혀 있으며, 처음 다다르는 마을에서 종이에 베껴 써야 한다는 것이 주인의 지시였다고 말했다."],["On this the curate said if he showed it to him, he himself would make a fair copy of it.","그러자 신부는 그것을 자기에게 보여 주면 자기가 직접 정서해 주겠노라고 말했다."],["Sancho put his hand into his bosom in search of the note-book but could not find it, nor, if he had been searching until now, could he have found it, for Don Quixote had kept it, and had never given it to him, nor had he himself thought of asking for it.","산초는 수첩을 찾으려고 품 안에 손을 넣었으나 찾을 수 없었으니, 설령 지금까지 뒤졌다 한들 찾아낼 수 없었을 것이다. 돈키호테가 그것을 자기가 지니고 있으면서 그에게 건넨 적이 없었고, 산초 자신도 그것을 달라고 할 생각을 한 적이 없었기 때문이다."],["When Sancho discovered he could not find the book his face grew deadly pale, and in great haste he again felt his body all over, and seeing plainly it was not to be found, without more ado he seized his beard with both hands and plucked away half of it, and then, as quick as he could and without stopping, gave himself half a dozen cuffs on the face and nose till they were bathed in blood.","수첩을 찾을 수 없음을 알아챈 산초의 얼굴은 죽은 사람처럼 새파랗게 질렸고, 그는 몹시 다급하게 다시 한번 온몸을 더듬어 보았으나 도무지 찾을 수 없음이 분명해지자, 더 볼 것도 없이 두 손으로 자기 수염을 움켜쥐고 절반을 쥐어뜯더니, 이어서 할 수 있는 한 빠르게 멈추지도 않고 제 얼굴과 코를 예닐곱 차례 후려쳐서 온통 피범벅이 되도록 만들었다."]],[["Seeing this, the curate and the barber asked him what had happened him that he gave himself such rough treatment.","이 광경을 본 신부와 이발사는 그에게 무슨 일이 있었기에 그토록 거칠게 자신을 다루느냐고 물었다."]],[["“What should happen me?”","\"제게 무슨 일이 있었느냐고요?\""],["replied Sancho, “but to have lost from one hand to the other, in a moment, three ass-colts, each of them like a castle?”","산초가 대답했다. \"한순간에, 손에서 손으로, 저마다 성채만 한 새끼 나귀 세 마리를 잃은 것밖에 더 있겠습니까?\""]],[["“How is that?”","\"그게 어찌 된 일인가?\""],["said the barber.","이발사가 말했다."]],[["“I have lost the note-book,”","\"수첩을 잃어버렸지 뭡니까.\""],["said Sancho, “that contained the letter to Dulcinea, and an order signed by my master in which he directed his niece to give me three ass-colts out of four or five he had at home;”","산초가 말했다. \"둘시네아께 보내는 편지가 들어 있고, 또 주인님이 서명한 증서가 들어 있었는데, 그 증서에서 주인님은 조카딸에게 집에 있는 네댓 마리 가운데 새끼 나귀 세 마리를 제게 주라고 일러 두셨거든요.\""],["and he then told them about the loss of Dapple.","그러고는 잿빛 당나귀를 잃어버린 일에 대해서도 그들에게 들려주었다."]],[["The curate consoled him, telling him that when his master was found he would get him to renew the order, and make a fresh draft on paper, as was usual and customary; for those made in notebooks were never accepted or honoured.","신부는 그를 위로하며, 주인이 발견되면 그더러 그 증서를 다시 쓰게 하여, 으레 그러하듯 종이에 새로 작성하도록 시키겠노라고 말했다. 수첩에 쓴 것은 결코 받아들여지거나 효력을 인정받지 못하는 법이라면서 말이다."]],[["Sancho comforted himself with this, and said if that were so the loss of Dulcinea’s letter did not trouble him much, for he had it almost by heart, and it could be taken down from him wherever and whenever they liked.","산초는 이 말에 마음을 놓고는, 그렇다면 둘시네아의 편지를 잃은 것은 그리 걱정되지 않노라고, 그것을 거의 외우다시피 하고 있으니 그들이 원하는 곳에서 원하는 때에 받아 적게 할 수 있다고 말했다."]],[["“Repeat it then, Sancho,”","\"그럼 외워 보게, 산초.\""],["said the barber, “and we will write it down afterwards.”","이발사가 말했다. \"그러면 우리가 나중에 그것을 받아 적겠네.\""]],[["Sancho Panza stopped to scratch his head to bring back the letter to his memory, and balanced himself now on one foot, now the other, one moment staring at the ground, the next at the sky, and after having half gnawed off the end of a finger and kept them in suspense waiting for him to begin, he said, after a long pause, “By God, señor licentiate, devil a thing can I recollect of the letter; but it said at the beginning, ‘Exalted and scrubbing Lady.’”","산초 판사는 편지를 기억해 내려고 머리를 긁적이며 멈춰 서서, 한 발로 섰다가 다른 발로 섰다가 하며 몸을 가누었고, 한순간은 땅바닥을 노려보다가 다음 순간엔 하늘을 올려다보았다. 그렇게 손가락 끝을 절반쯤 물어뜯어 가며 두 사람이 그가 시작하기를 애타게 기다리도록 만든 끝에, 한참을 뜸 들이고 나서 그가 말했다. \"맙소사, 학사 양반, 편지 내용이라곤 도무지 한 자도 떠오르질 않습니다. 그래도 첫머리는 '드높고 빨래하시는 마님'이라고 되어 있었습니다.\""]],[["“It cannot have said ‘scrubbing,’”","\"'빨래하시는'일 리가 없네.\""],["said the barber, “but ‘superhuman’ or ‘sovereign.’”","이발사가 말했다. \"'초인적인'이거나 '지엄하신'이겠지.\""]],[["“That is it,” said Sancho; “then, as well as I remember, it went on, ‘The wounded, and wanting of sleep, and the pierced, kisses your worship’s hands, ungrateful and very unrecognised fair one; and it said something or other about health and sickness that he was sending her; and from that it went tailing off until it ended with ‘Yours till death, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance.”","\"바로 그겁니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"그런 다음 제 기억으로는 이렇게 이어졌습니다. '상처 입고, 잠 못 이루고, 꿰뚫린 자가 그대의 손에 입 맞추나이다, 은혜를 모르고 도무지 알아주지 않는 어여쁜 이여.' 그러고는 자기가 그분께 보내는 건강이며 병환이며 하는 무엇무엇을 어쩌고저쩌고 말했고, 거기서부터는 꼬리를 끌듯 흐릿해지다가 마침내 '죽을 때까지 그대의 것, 슬픈 몰골의 기사'로 끝을 맺었습니다.\""]],[["It gave them no little amusement, both of them, to see what a good memory Sancho had, and they complimented him greatly upon it, and begged him to repeat the letter a couple of times more, so that they too might get it by heart to write it out by-and-by.","산초의 기억력이 이토록 좋은 것을 보고 두 사람은 적잖이 즐거워하며 그를 크게 칭찬했고, 자기들도 그것을 외워 두었다가 이내 받아 적을 수 있도록 편지를 두어 번 더 외워 달라고 청했다. 산초는 세 번 외웠고, 그러면서 또 삼천 가지나 되는 터무니없는 소리를 늘어놓았다."],["Sancho repeated it three times, and as he did, uttered three thousand more absurdities; then he told them more about his master but he never said a word about the blanketing that had befallen himself in that inn, into which he refused to enter.","그러고는 주인에 관해 더 많은 이야기를 들려주었으나, 그 객줏집에서 자기가 당했던 담요 키질 봉변에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 그는 그곳에 들어가기를 한사코 거부했던 것이다. 그는 또한, 만일 자기 주인이 둘시네아 델 토보소 마님에게서 좋은 답을 받아 온다면 황제, 적어도 군주가 되려고 길을 나설 작정이라고 들려주었다."],["He told them, moreover, how his lord, if he brought him a favourable answer from the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, was to put himself in the way of endeavouring to become an emperor, or at least a monarch; for it had been so settled between them, and with his personal worth and the might of his arm it was an easy matter to come to be one: and how on becoming one his lord was to make a marriage for him (for he would be a widower by that time, as a matter of course) and was to give him as a wife one of the damsels of the empress, the heiress of some rich and grand state on the mainland, having nothing to do with islands of any sort, for he did not care for them now.","둘 사이에 그렇게 정해 둔 일이었고, 그의 인품과 그 팔의 위력이라면 그리되는 것쯤은 손쉬운 일이라는 것이었다. 그리고 그리되면 자기 주인은 그를 위해 혼처를 마련해 주기로 — 당연히 그때쯤이면 주인은 홀아비가 되어 있을 터이므로 — 했으며, 황후의 시녀들 가운데 하나를, 곧 본토에 있는 어느 부유하고 으리으리한 영지의 상속녀를 아내로 주기로 했다고, 자기는 이제 섬 따위에는 아무런 관심도 없으니 어떤 종류의 섬과도 상관없는 처녀를 주기로 했다고 했다."],["All this Sancho delivered with so much composure--wiping his nose from time to time--and with so little common-sense that his two hearers were again filled with wonder at the force of Don Quixote’s madness that could run away with this poor man’s reason.","이 모든 것을 산초는 — 이따금 코를 닦아 가며 — 어찌나 태연하게, 어찌나 분별없이 늘어놓는지, 두 청자는 이 가련한 사내의 이성마저 앗아 갈 수 있는 돈키호테의 광기의 위력에 다시금 경탄을 금치 못했다."],["They did not care to take the trouble of disabusing him of his error, as they considered that since it did not in any way hurt his conscience it would be better to leave him in it, and they would have all the more amusement in listening to his simplicities; and so they bade him pray to God for his lord’s health, as it was a very likely and a very feasible thing for him in course of time to come to be an emperor, as he said, or at least an archbishop or some other dignitary of equal rank.","그들은 그의 잘못을 굳이 일깨워 주려 들지 않았으니, 그것이 그의 양심을 조금도 해치지 않는 이상 그대로 두는 편이 낫겠다고 여겼고, 또 그래야 그의 어수룩한 소리를 듣는 재미가 그만큼 더 쏠쏠하리라 생각했기 때문이다. 그리하여 그들은 그더러 주인의 건강을 위해 하느님께 기도하라고 일렀으니, 그가 말한 대로 머잖아 황제가 되거나, 적어도 대주교나 그에 맞먹는 어떤 고관이 되는 것이 충분히 있을 법하고 또 충분히 이루어질 만한 일이라면서 말이다."]],[["To which Sancho made answer, “If fortune, sirs, should bring things about in such a way that my master should have a mind, instead of being an emperor, to be an archbishop, I should like to know what archbishops-errant commonly give their squires?”","이에 산초가 대답했다. \"여러분, 만일 운명이 일을 그렇게 풀어 가서 제 주인님이 황제 대신 대주교가 되실 마음을 품으신다면, 떠도는 대주교님들이 보통 그 종자들에게 무엇을 내려 주는지 알고 싶습니다그려.\""]],[["“They commonly give them,” said the curate, some simple benefice or cure, or some place as sacristan which brings them a good fixed income, not counting the altar fees, which may be reckoned at as much more.”","\"보통 내려 주기를,\" 신부가 말했다. \"성직록이나 본당 자리, 혹은 성구지기 같은 자리를 주는데, 그것이 짭짤하게 고정된 수입을 안겨 주지. 제단 봉헌금은 따로 치고도 말일세. 그 봉헌금도 어림잡아 그만큼은 되거든.\""]],[["“But for that,”","\"하지만 그러려면,\""],["said Sancho, “the squire must be unmarried, and must know, at any rate, how to help at mass, and if that be so, woe is me, for I am married already and I don’t know the first letter of the A B C.","산초가 말했다. \"종자가 미혼이어야 하고, 적어도 미사 거드는 법쯤은 알아야 할 텐데요."],["What will become of me if my master takes a fancy to be an archbishop and not an emperor, as is usual and customary with knights-errant?”","그런데 그렇다면, 아이고 내 신세야, 저는 이미 장가든 몸인 데다 가나다라의 첫 글자도 모르거든요. 떠도는 기사님들이 으레 그러하듯, 제 주인님이 황제가 아니라 대주교가 되실 마음을 품으시면 저는 어찌 됩니까?\""]],[["“Be not uneasy, friend Sancho,”","\"마음 졸이지 말게, 산초 친구.\" 이발사가 말했다."],["said the barber, “for we will entreat your master, and advise him, even urging it upon him as a case of conscience, to become an emperor and not an archbishop, because it will be easier for him as he is more valiant than lettered.”","\"우리가 자네 주인께 간청하고 조언하여, 양심의 문제로까지 들이대 가며 대주교가 아니라 황제가 되시라고 강권하겠네. 그분은 글재주보다 무용이 더 뛰어나시니 그편이 그분께도 더 수월할 테니 말이야.\""]],[["“So I have thought,”","\"저도 그리 생각했습니다.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho; “though I can tell you he is fit for anything: what I mean to do for my part is to pray to our Lord to place him where it may be best for him, and where he may be able to bestow most favours upon me.”","\"하기야 그분이 무슨 일에든 능하시다는 건 제가 장담할 수 있습니다만. 제가 제 몫으로 하려는 일이라곤, 그분께 가장 좋은 자리에, 그리고 제게 가장 많은 은혜를 베풀어 주실 수 있는 자리에 그분을 두어 달라고 우리 주님께 기도드리는 것뿐입니다.\""]],[["“You speak like a man of sense,” said the curate, “and you will be acting like a good Christian; but what must now be done is to take steps to coax your master out of that useless penance you say he is performing; and we had best turn into this inn to consider what plan to adopt, and also to dine, for it is now time.”","\"분별 있는 사람처럼 말하는구먼.\" 신부가 말했다. \"또 착한 기독교인답게 행동하는 것일세. 그러나 지금 해야 할 일은, 자네 말로는 자네 주인이 치르고 있다는 그 부질없는 고행에서 그분을 살살 꾀어내도록 손을 쓰는 것일세. 어떤 방책을 취할지 궁리도 하고, 또 식사도 할 겸 — 이제 그럴 때가 되었으니 — 우리는 이 객줏집으로 들어가는 게 좋겠네.\""]],[["Sancho said they might go in, but that he would wait there outside, and that he would tell them afterwards the reason why he was unwilling, and why it did not suit him to enter it; but he begged them to bring him out something to eat, and to let it be hot, and also to bring barley for Rocinante.","산초는 두 사람더러 들어가도 좋으나 자기는 밖에서 기다리겠다고, 어째서 들어가기 싫은지, 어째서 그곳에 들어가는 것이 자기에게 맞지 않는지는 나중에 일러 주겠노라고 말했다. 다만 그는 먹을 것을 좀 내다 달라고, 그것도 뜨거운 것으로 해 달라고, 또 로시난테에게 줄 보리도 가져다 달라고 부탁했다."],["They left him and went in, and presently the barber brought him out something to eat.","그들은 그를 두고 안으로 들어갔고, 이내 이발사가 그에게 먹을 것을 내다 주었다."],["By-and-by, after they had between them carefully thought over what they should do to carry out their object, the curate hit upon an idea very well adapted to humour Don Quixote, and effect their purpose; and his notion, which he explained to the barber, was that he himself should assume the disguise of a wandering damsel, while the other should try as best he could to pass for a squire, and that they should thus proceed to where Don Quixote was, and he, pretending to be an aggrieved and distressed damsel, should ask a favour of him, which as a valiant knight-errant he could not refuse to grant; and the favour he meant to ask him was that he should accompany her whither she would conduct him, in order to redress a wrong which a wicked knight had done her, while at the same time she should entreat him not to require her to remove her mask, nor ask her any question touching her circumstances until he had righted her with the wicked knight.","얼마 뒤, 두 사람이 자기들 목적을 이루기 위해 무엇을 해야 할지 함께 곰곰이 궁리한 끝에, 신부가 돈키호테의 비위를 맞추면서도 자기들 뜻을 이루기에 매우 알맞은 한 가지 묘안을 떠올렸다. 그가 이발사에게 설명한 그 생각인즉, 자기는 떠도는 처녀로 변장하고 다른 한쪽은 되도록 종자로 보이도록 꾸민 뒤, 그렇게 돈키호테가 있는 곳으로 가서, 신부가 억울하고 곤경에 처한 처녀인 척 그에게 한 가지 청을 드리되, 용맹한 떠돌이 기사로서 그가 차마 거절할 수 없는 청을 드린다는 것이었다."],["And he had no doubt that Don Quixote would comply with any request made in these terms, and that in this way they might remove him and take him to his own village, where they would endeavour to find out if his extraordinary madness admitted of any kind of remedy.","그가 청하려는 그 부탁이란, 어느 사악한 기사가 그녀에게 저지른 잘못을 바로잡기 위해 그녀가 그를 데려가려는 곳으로 동행해 달라는 것이었고, 그러면서 동시에 그 사악한 기사에게서 자기를 위해 정의를 세워 줄 때까지는 가면을 벗으라고 요구하지도, 자기 사정에 관해 어떤 질문도 하지 말아 달라고 간청한다는 것이었다. 그리고 그는 돈키호테가 이런 식으로 올린 어떤 청이든 들어주리라는 데 추호의 의심도 없었으며, 이런 방법으로 그를 끌어내어 그의 마을로 데려갈 수 있으리라고, 그곳에서 그의 유별난 광기에 어떤 종류든 치유책이 통할 여지가 있는지 알아보리라고 생각했다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXVII. OF HOW THE CURATE AND THE BARBER PROCEEDED WITH THEIR SCHEME; TOGETHER WITH OTHER MATTERS WORTHY OF RECORD IN THIS GREAT HISTORY","# CHAPTER XXVII."]],[["The curate’s plan did not seem a bad one to the barber, but on the contrary so good that they immediately set about putting it in execution.","신부의 계획은 이발사에게 나쁘게 여겨지지 않았다. 도리어 어찌나 좋게 여겨졌던지 두 사람은 곧장 그것을 실행에 옮기기 시작했다."],["They begged a petticoat and hood of the landlady, leaving her in pledge a new cassock of the curate’s; and the barber made a beard out of a grey-brown or red ox-tail in which the landlord used to stick his comb.","그들은 여관 안주인에게 치마와 두건을 빌려 달라고 청하면서 그 담보로 신부의 새 사제복을 맡겨 두었고, 이발사는 여관 주인이 빗을 꽂아 두곤 하던 잿빛 도는 갈색, 혹은 붉은 황소 꼬리로 수염을 만들었다."],["The landlady asked them what they wanted these things for, and the curate told her in a few words about the madness of Don Quixote, and how this disguise was intended to get him away from the mountain where he then was.","안주인이 그 물건들을 어디에 쓸 작정이냐고 묻자, 신부는 돈키호테의 광기에 대해 몇 마디로 일러 주고, 이 변장이 그가 지금 머물고 있는 산에서 그를 끌어내기 위한 것임을 설명했다."],["The landlord and landlady immediately came to the conclusion that the madman was their guest, the balsam man and master of the blanketed squire, and they told the curate all that had passed between him and them, not omitting what Sancho had been so silent about.","여관 주인 내외는 즉시 그 미치광이가 다름 아닌 자기네 손님, 곧 그 영약을 가진 사내이자 담요 키질을 당한 종자의 주인임을 알아차렸고, 산초가 그토록 입을 다물어 온 일까지 빠뜨리지 않고, 그와 자기들 사이에 오갔던 모든 일을 신부에게 일러 주었다."],["Finally the landlady dressed up the curate in a style that left nothing to be desired; she put on him a cloth petticoat with black velvet stripes a palm broad, all slashed, and a bodice of green velvet set off by a binding of white satin, which as well as the petticoat must have been made in the time of king Wamba.","마침내 안주인은 더할 나위 없을 만큼 그럴듯한 차림으로 신부를 꾸며 주었다. 그에게 한 뼘 너비의 검은 우단 띠를 죽 두른, 온통 칼집을 낸 천 치마를 입히고, 흰 공단으로 가장자리를 두른 초록 우단 보디스를 걸쳐 주었는데, 이 보디스도 치마도 틀림없이 왐바 왕 시절에 지어진 것이리라."],["The curate would not let them hood him, but put on his head a little quilted linen cap which he used for a night-cap, and bound his forehead with a strip of black silk, while with another he made a mask with which he concealed his beard and face very well.","신부는 두건만은 쓰려 하지 않고, 머리에는 자기가 잠잘 때 쓰던 누비 삼베 모자를 얹고, 검은 비단 끈으로 이마를 동여맸으며, 또 다른 끈으로는 수염과 얼굴을 아주 잘 가려 주는 가리개를 만들었다. 그러고는 양산으로 써도 좋을 만큼 챙이 넓은 모자를 쓰고, 외투로 몸을 휘감은 채 여인네처럼 모로 앉아 노새에 올랐다."],["He then put on his hat, which was broad enough to serve him for an umbrella, and enveloping himself in his cloak seated himself woman-fashion on his mule, while the barber mounted his with a beard down to the waist of mingled red and white, for it was, as has been said, the tail of a clay-red ox.","이발사도 자기 노새에 올랐는데, 허리까지 내려오는 붉고 흰 빛깔이 뒤섞인 수염을 달았으니, 앞서 말했듯 그것은 진흙빛 붉은 황소의 꼬리였던 것이다. * 왐바 왕: 7세기 서고트 왕국의 왕으로, 까마득히 오래된 시절을 가리키는 관용 표현."]],[["They took leave of all, and of the good Maritornes, who, sinner as she was, promised to pray a rosary of prayers that God might grant them success in such an arduous and Christian undertaking as that they had in hand. But hardly had he sallied forth from the inn when it struck the curate that he was doing wrong in rigging himself out in that fashion, as it was an indecorous thing for a priest to dress himself that way even though much might depend upon it; and saying so to the barber he begged him to change dresses, as it was fitter he should be the distressed damsel, while he himself would play the squire’s part, which would be less derogatory to his dignity; otherwise he was resolved to have nothing more to do with the matter, and let the devil take Don Quixote. Just at this moment Sancho came up, and on seeing the pair in such a costume he was unable to restrain his laughter; the barber, however, agreed to do as the curate wished, and, altering their plan, the curate went on to instruct him how to play his part and what to say to Don Quixote to induce and compel him to come with them and give up his fancy for the place he had chosen for his idle penance. The barber told him he could manage it properly without any instruction, and as he did not care to dress himself up until they were near where Don Quixote was, he folded up the garments, and the curate adjusted his beard, and they set out under the guidance of Sancho Panza, who went along telling them of the encounter with the madman they met in the Sierra, saying nothing, however, about the finding of the valise and its contents; for with all his simplicity the lad was a trifle covetous.","그들은 모두에게, 그리고 비록 죄 많은 여인이긴 하나 그들이 떠맡은 그토록 고되고 그리스도교적인 일에 하느님께서 성공을 내려 주시기를 묵주 기도로 빌어 주겠노라 약속한 착한 마리토르네스에게 작별을 고했다. 그런데 신부가 여관을 나서자마자, 자신이 그런 식으로 차려입은 것이 잘못이라는 생각이 그를 찔렀다. 비록 거기에 많은 것이 달려 있다 한들 사제가 이런 차림을 하는 것은 점잖지 못한 일이었기 때문이다. 그래서 이발사에게 그 말을 하며 옷을 바꿔 입자고 청했으니, 이발사가 곤경에 처한 처녀 노릇을 하는 편이 더 어울리고, 자기는 종자 역을 맡는 편이 자기 품위를 덜 손상시키리라는 것이었다. 그렇지 않으면 이 일에서 손을 떼고 돈키호테 따위는 악마에게나 넘겨 버리겠다고 마음먹은 터였다. 바로 그 순간 산초가 다가왔는데, 그런 차림을 한 두 사람을 보고는 웃음을 참을 수가 없었다. 그러나 이발사는 신부가 바라는 대로 하기로 했고, 계획을 바꾼 신부는 그가 어떻게 역을 해내야 하는지, 또 돈키호테가 자기가 게으른 고행을 위해 골라잡은 그곳을 단념하고 자기들과 함께 가도록 꾀고 다그치려면 그에게 무어라 말해야 하는지를 일러 주기 시작했다. 이발사는 아무 지시 없이도 제대로 해낼 수 있노라 말했고, 돈키호테가 있는 곳 가까이 이르기 전에는 변장을 하고 싶지 않다며 옷가지를 접어 두었다. 신부는 수염을 손보았고, 두 사람은 산초 판사의 안내를 받아 길을 나섰다. 산초는 길을 가면서 시에라에서 만난 미치광이와 마주쳤던 일을 그들에게 이야기했으나, 여행 가방과 그 속에 든 것을 주운 일에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 그 어수룩함에도 불구하고 이 젊은이에게는 욕심이 조금 있었던 것이다."]],[["The next day they reached the place where Sancho had laid the broom-branches as marks to direct him to where he had left his master, and recognising it he told them that here was the entrance, and that they would do well to dress themselves, if that was required to deliver his master; for they had already told him that going in this guise and dressing in this way were of the highest importance in order to rescue his master from the pernicious life he had adopted; and they charged him strictly not to tell his master who they were, or that he knew them, and should he ask, as ask he would, if he had given the letter to Dulcinea, to say that he had, and that, as she did not know how to read, she had given an answer by word of mouth, saying that she commanded him, on pain of her displeasure, to come and see her at once; and it was a very important matter for himself, because in this way and with what they meant to say to him they felt sure of bringing him back to a better mode of life and inducing him to take immediate steps to become an emperor or monarch, for there was no fear of his becoming an archbishop. All this Sancho listened to and fixed it well in his memory, and thanked them heartily for intending to recommend his master to be an emperor instead of an archbishop, for he felt sure that in the way of bestowing rewards on their squires emperors could do more than archbishops-errant. He said, too, that it would be as well for him to go on before them to find him, and give him his lady’s answer; for that perhaps might be enough to bring him away from the place without putting them to all this trouble. They approved of what Sancho proposed, and resolved to wait for him until he brought back word of having found his master.","이튿날 그들은 산초가 주인을 두고 떠난 곳으로 자신을 인도하려고 표지 삼아 금작화 가지들을 놓아 두었던 자리에 다다랐다. 그곳을 알아본 산초는 여기가 입구라고 일러 주면서, 주인을 구해 내는 데 변장이 필요하다면 옷을 차려입는 것이 좋겠다고 말했다. 그도 그럴 것이 그들은 이미 산초에게, 그의 주인을 그가 택한 해로운 삶에서 구해 내자면 이런 모습으로 가서 이렇게 차려입는 것이 더없이 중요하다고 일러 둔 터였다. 그리고 그들은 산초에게 자기들이 누구인지, 또 그가 자기들을 안다는 것을 결코 주인에게 말하지 말라고 단단히 일렀으며, 주인이 둘시네아에게 편지를 전했느냐고 물으면 — 틀림없이 물을 터인데 — 전했노라 대답하고, 그분은 글을 읽을 줄 모르시기에 말로 답을 주셨으니, 노여움을 사고 싶지 않거든 당장 자기를 보러 오라 명하시더라고 말하라고 일렀다. 이것은 그 자신에게도 매우 중요한 일이었으니, 이런 식으로 또 그들이 그에게 하려는 말로써 주인을 더 나은 삶의 길로 데려오고, 황제나 군주가 되기 위한 조치를 당장 취하도록 꾈 수 있으리라 확신했기 때문이다. 대주교가 될 염려야 없는 일이었으니 말이다. 산초는 이 모든 것을 귀담아듣고 머릿속에 단단히 새겨 두었으며, 자기 주인을 대주교가 아니라 황제가 되도록 권할 작정이라는 데 진심으로 고마워했으니, 종자에게 상을 내리는 일에 있어서는 황제가 편력 대주교보다 더 많은 것을 해 줄 수 있으리라 확신했기 때문이다. 그는 또한 자기가 먼저 앞서 가서 주인을 찾아 그 귀부인의 답을 전하는 편이 좋겠다고 말했으니, 어쩌면 그것만으로도 이런 수고를 다 들이지 않고도 주인을 그곳에서 데려오기에 충분하리라는 것이었다. 그들은 산초가 내놓은 제안을 받아들였고, 그가 주인을 찾았다는 기별을 가지고 돌아올 때까지 기다리기로 했다."]],[["Sancho pushed on into the glens of the Sierra, leaving them in one through which there flowed a little gentle rivulet, and where the rocks and trees afforded a cool and grateful shade.","산초는 시에라의 골짜기들 안으로 밀고 들어갔고, 두 사람은 자그마한 시냇물이 부드럽게 흐르고 바위와 나무들이 서늘하고 고마운 그늘을 드리운 골짜기 하나에 남겨 두었다."],["It was an August day with all the heat of one, and the heat in those parts is intense, and the hour was three in the afternoon, all which made the spot the more inviting and tempted them to wait there for Sancho’s return, which they did.","때는 한여름의 온갖 더위를 다 갖춘 팔월의 어느 날이었고, 그 지방의 더위는 모질었으며, 시각은 오후 세 시였으니, 이 모든 것이 그 자리를 한층 마음 끌리게 만들어 거기서 산초가 돌아오기를 기다리도록 그들을 꾀었고, 그들은 그렇게 했다."],["They were reposing, then, in the shade, when a voice unaccompanied by the notes of any instrument, but sweet and pleasing in its tone, reached their ears, at which they were not a little astonished, as the place did not seem to them likely quarters for one who sang so well; for though it is often said that shepherds of rare voice are to be found in the woods and fields, this is rather a flight of the poet’s fancy than the truth.","그리하여 그들이 그늘에서 쉬고 있는데, 어떤 악기의 가락도 곁들이지 않았으나 그 음색이 감미롭고 듣기 좋은 목소리 하나가 그들의 귀에 닿았으니, 노래를 그토록 잘 부르는 이가 머물 만한 곳으로는 그곳이 도무지 여겨지지 않았기에 그들은 적잖이 놀랐다. 흔히들 숲과 들에서 보기 드물게 고운 목소리의 목동들을 만날 수 있다고들 하지만, 이는 진실이라기보다 시인의 상상이 빚어낸 비약에 가깝기 때문이다."],["And still more surprised were they when they perceived that what they heard sung were the verses not of rustic shepherds, but of the polished wits of the city; and so it proved, for the verses they heard were these:","그리고 그들이 들은 노래가 시골 목동의 시구가 아니라 도회의 세련된 재사들의 시구임을 알아차렸을 때 그들은 한층 더 놀랐다. 과연 그러했으니, 그들이 들은 시구는 이러했다."]],[["What makes my quest of happiness seem vain?","내 행복을 향한 추구를 헛되이 보이게 하는 것은 무엇인가?"],["Disdain.","멸시로다."],["What bids me to abandon hope of ease?","내게 안식의 희망을 버리라 명하는 것은 무엇인가?"],["Jealousies.","질투로다."],["What holds my heart in anguish of suspense?","내 가슴을 애타는 불안 속에 붙들어 두는 것은 무엇인가?"],["Absence.","부재로다."],["If that be so, then for my grief Where shall I turn to seek relief, When hope on every side lies slain By Absence, Jealousies, Disdain?","그렇다면, 내 슬픔을 달랠 위안을 어디서 찾아야 하랴, 멸시와 질투와 부재로 말미암아 희망이 사방에서 살해당해 누웠거늘?"]],[["What the prime cause of all my woe doth prove?","내 온갖 비탄의 으뜸가는 원인임이 드러나는 것은 무엇인가? 사랑이로다."],["Love. What at my glory ever looks askance?","내 영광을 늘 곁눈으로 흘겨보는 것은 무엇인가?"],["Chance.","운명이로다."],["Whence is permission to afflict me given?","나를 괴롭힐 허락이 어디서 내려오는가?"],["Heaven.","하늘이로다."],["If that be so, I but await The stroke of a resistless fate, Since, working for my woe, these three, Love, Chance and Heaven, in league I see.","그렇다면 나는 그저 거역할 수 없는 운명의 일격을 기다릴 뿐, 내 비탄을 위해 손잡고 일하는 이 셋, 사랑과 운명과 하늘을 보고 있으니."]],[["What must I do to find a remedy? Die.","치유책을 찾으려면 나는 무엇을 해야 하는가?"],["What is the lure for love when coy and strange?","죽음이로다. 수줍고 매정한 사랑을 꾈 미끼는 무엇인가?"],["Change.","변심이로다."],["What, if all fail, will cure the heart of sadness?","모두 헛되이 끝난다면, 무엇이 슬픈 가슴을 낫게 하랴?"],["Madness.","광기로다."],["If that be so, it is but folly To seek a cure for melancholy: Ask where it lies; the answer saith In Change, in Madness, or in Death.","그렇다면 우울을 치유할 약을 찾는다는 것은 한낱 어리석은 짓, 그것이 어디 있느냐 물으면, 대답은 이러하니 변심에, 광기에, 아니면 죽음에 있노라."]],[["The hour, the summer season, the solitary place, the voice and skill of the singer, all contributed to the wonder and delight of the two listeners, who remained still waiting to hear something more; finding, however, that the silence continued some little time, they resolved to go in search of the musician who sang with so fine a voice; but just as they were about to do so they were checked by the same voice, which once more fell upon their ears, singing this","그 시각, 여름철, 외딴 자리, 노래하는 이의 목소리와 솜씨, 이 모든 것이 두 청자의 경탄과 기쁨에 한몫하였으니, 그들은 무언가 더 들으려고 잠자코 기다렸다. 그러나 침묵이 한동안 이어지자 그들은 그토록 고운 목소리로 노래하는 그 악사를 찾아 나서기로 했다. 그런데 막 그러려는 참에 똑같은 목소리에 발이 묶이고 말았으니, 그 목소리가 다시 한 번 그들의 귀에 떨어지며 이런 소네트를 노래했던 것이다. 소네트"]],[["When heavenward, holy Friendship, thou didst go Soaring to seek thy home beyond the sky, And take thy seat among the saints on high, It was thy will to leave on earth below Thy semblance, and upon it to bestow Thy veil, wherewith at times hypocrisy, Parading in thy shape, deceives the eye, And makes its vileness bright as virtue show.","거룩한 우정이여, 그대 하늘로 올라 하늘 너머 그대의 집을 찾아 날아오르고 드높이 성인들 가운데 자리를 잡으려 할 적에, 그대는 이 땅 아래에 그대의 형상을 남기고 그 위에 그대의 너울을 베푸는 것이 그대 뜻이었으니, 위선이 때로 그 너울을 두르고 그대의 모습으로 활보하며 눈을 속이고 그 추악함을 미덕인 양 밝게 꾸며 보이는도다."],["Friendship, return to us, or force the cheat That wears it now, thy livery to restore, By aid whereof sincerity is slain.","우정이여, 우리에게 돌아오라, 아니면 지금 그것을 두른 그 사기꾼더러 그대의 제복을 돌려 놓도록 다그치라, 그것의 힘을 빌려 진실이 살해당하고 있으니."],["If thou wilt not unmask thy counterfeit, This earth will be the prey of strife once more, As when primaeval discord held its reign.","그대가 그대의 가짜를 벗겨 내지 않으려거든, 이 땅은 다시 한 번 다툼의 먹이가 되리라, 태초의 불화가 그 지배를 떨치던 그때처럼."]],[["The song ended with a deep sigh, and again the listeners remained waiting attentively for the singer to resume; but perceiving that the music had now turned to sobs and heart-rending moans they determined to find out who the unhappy being could be whose voice was as rare as his sighs were piteous, and they had not proceeded far when on turning the corner of a rock they discovered a man of the same aspect and appearance as Sancho had described to them when he told them the story of Cardenio. He, showing no astonishment when he saw them, stood still with his head bent down upon his breast like one in deep thought, without raising his eyes to look at them after the first glance when they suddenly came upon him. The curate, who was aware of his misfortune and recognised him by the description, being a man of good address, approached him and in a few sensible words entreated and urged him to quit a life of such misery, lest he should end it there, which would be the greatest of all misfortunes. Cardenio was then in his right mind, free from any attack of that madness which so frequently carried him away, and seeing them dressed in a fashion so unusual among the frequenters of those wilds, could not help showing some surprise, especially when he heard them speak of his case as if it were a well-known matter (for the curate’s words gave him to understand as much) so he replied to them thus:","노래는 깊은 한숨과 더불어 끝났고, 청자들은 다시 노래하는 이가 이어 가기를 골똘히 귀 기울이며 기다렸다. 그러나 이제 그 노랫소리가 흐느낌과 가슴을 에는 신음으로 변한 것을 알아차린 그들은, 그 목소리가 보기 드물게 곱듯이 그 한숨도 그토록 가련한, 그 불행한 존재가 대체 누구인지 알아내기로 마음먹었다. 그들이 얼마 가지 않아 어느 바위 모퉁이를 돌았을 때, 산초가 카르데니오의 이야기를 들려주며 묘사했던 것과 똑같은 행색과 모습의 한 사내를 발견했다. 그는 그들을 보고도 아무런 놀라움을 드러내지 않고, 깊은 생각에 잠긴 사람처럼 고개를 가슴께로 푹 숙인 채 가만히 서 있었으며, 그들이 불쑥 나타났을 때 처음 한 번 흘긋 본 뒤로는 눈을 들어 그들을 쳐다보지도 않았다. 그의 불행을 알고 있던 신부는 그 묘사로 그를 알아보고는, 말솜씨가 좋은 사람인지라 그에게 다가가 분별 있는 몇 마디로, 그토록 비참한 삶을 버리라고 간청하고 다그쳤다. 자칫 그곳에서 그 삶을 끝장내기라도 한다면 그것이야말로 모든 불행 가운데 가장 큰 불행이 되리라는 것이었다. 카르데니오는 그때 제정신이어서, 그토록 자주 그를 휩쓸어 가던 그 광기의 발작에서 벗어나 있었다. 그래서 그 황량한 곳을 드나드는 이들 사이에서는 좀처럼 보기 힘든 차림을 한 그들을 보고는 얼마간 놀라움을 드러내지 않을 수 없었으니, 더구나 그들이 마치 자기 사정을 잘 아는 일인 양 이야기하는 것을 들었을 때는 더욱 그러했다(신부의 말이 그에게 그만큼을 짐작하게 했던 것이다). 그리하여 그는 이렇게 대답했다."]],[["“I see plainly, sirs, whoever you may be, that Heaven, whose care it is to succour the good, and even the wicked very often, here, in this remote spot, cut off from human intercourse, sends me, though I deserve it not, those who seek to draw me away from this to some better retreat, showing me by many and forcible arguments how unreasonably I act in leading the life I do; but as they know, that if I escape from this evil I shall fall into another still greater, perhaps they will set me down as a weak-minded man, or, what is worse, one devoid of reason; nor would it be any wonder, for I myself can perceive that the effect of the recollection of my misfortunes is so great and works so powerfully to my ruin, that in spite of myself I become at times like a stone, without feeling or consciousness; and I come to feel the truth of it when they tell me and show me proofs of the things I have done when the terrible fit overmasters me; and all I can do is bewail my lot in vain, and idly curse my destiny, and plead for my madness by telling how it was caused, to any that care to hear it; for no reasonable beings on learning the cause will wonder at the effects; and if they cannot help me at least they will not blame me, and the repugnance they feel at my wild ways will turn into pity for my woes. If it be, sirs, that you are here with the same design as others have come with, before you proceed with your wise arguments, I entreat you to hear the story of my countless misfortunes, for perhaps when you have heard it you will spare yourselves the trouble you would take in offering consolation to grief that is beyond the reach of it.”","\"여러분, 그대들이 누구이시든, 저는 똑똑히 알겠습니다. 착한 이들을 — 그리고 흔히는 악한 이들마저 — 보살피시는 것을 소임으로 삼으시는 하늘이, 인간의 왕래가 끊긴 이 외진 곳에, 그럴 자격도 없는 저에게, 이곳에서 저를 끌어내어 더 나은 안식처로 데려가려는 분들을 보내 주셨음을 말입니다. 제가 이런 삶을 사는 것이 얼마나 이치에 어긋나는지를 여러 강력한 논거로 보여 주시면서 말이지요. 그러나 그분들은, 제가 이 불행에서 벗어난다 해도 더 큰 또 다른 불행에 빠지리라는 것을 모르기에, 어쩌면 저를 마음 약한 자, 아니 더 나쁘게는 분별을 잃은 자로 여길지도 모릅니다. 그렇다 해도 이상할 것은 없으니, 저 자신도 알 수 있기 때문입니다. 제 불행을 떠올리는 일이 제게 미치는 영향이 어찌나 크고 제 파멸을 향해 어찌나 세차게 작용하는지, 저는 저도 모르게 때때로 돌처럼 되어 아무 느낌도 의식도 없어지고 맙니다. 그리고 그 끔찍한 발작이 저를 사로잡았을 때 제가 저지른 짓들을 그분들이 일러 주고 증거를 보여 줄 때면, 저는 그것이 사실임을 비로소 느끼게 됩니다. 그럴 때 제가 할 수 있는 일이라고는, 헛되이 제 신세를 한탄하고 부질없이 제 운명을 저주하며, 들어 주고자 하는 이라면 누구에게든 그 광기가 어떻게 비롯되었는지를 이야기함으로써 그것을 변명하는 것뿐입니다. 이성을 갖춘 이라면 그 원인을 알고 나서는 그 결과에 놀라지 않을 것이고, 설령 저를 돕지는 못한다 해도 적어도 저를 탓하지는 않을 것이며, 제 사나운 행실에 느끼던 거부감이 제 비탄에 대한 연민으로 바뀔 것이기 때문입니다. 여러분, 만일 그대들이 다른 이들이 가졌던 것과 같은 뜻을 품고 이곳에 오신 것이라면, 그 현명한 논거를 펼치시기에 앞서, 부디 제 헤아릴 수 없는 불행의 이야기를 들어 주시기를 간청합니다. 어쩌면 그것을 다 듣고 나면, 위안의 손길이 닿지 않는 슬픔에 위로를 건네느라 들이실 수고를 스스로 덜게 되실 테니까요.\""]],[["As they, both of them, desired nothing more than to hear from his own lips the cause of his suffering, they entreated him to tell it, promising not to do anything for his relief or comfort that he did not wish; and thereupon the unhappy gentleman began his sad story in nearly the same words and manner in which he had related it to Don Quixote and the goatherd a few days before, when, through Master Elisabad, and Don Quixote’s scrupulous observance of what was due to chivalry, the tale was left unfinished, as this history has already recorded; but now fortunately the mad fit kept off, allowed him to tell it to the end; and so, coming to the incident of the note which Don Fernando had found in the volume of “Amadis of Gaul,” Cardenio said that he remembered it perfectly and that it was in these words:","두 사람은 다른 무엇보다도 그의 고통의 원인을 그 자신의 입으로 듣기를 바랐던 터라, 그가 원치 않는 일은 그의 위안이나 평안을 위해서라도 아무것도 하지 않겠노라 약속하며 그것을 이야기해 달라고 간청했다. 그러자 이 불행한 신사는 며칠 전 돈키호테와 염소지기에게 들려주었던 것과 거의 같은 말과 방식으로 자신의 슬픈 이야기를 시작했다. 그때는 엘리사바드 선생을 둘러싸고, 또 기사도에 합당한 도리를 향한 돈키호테의 깐깐한 고집으로 말미암아, 이 이야기에 이미 적힌 대로 그 이야기가 끝맺지 못한 채로 남았었다. 그러나 이번에는 다행히 광기의 발작이 비켜 가 그가 끝까지 이야기할 수 있게 해 주었다. 그리하여 돈 페르난도가 「아마디스 데 가울라」 책 속에서 찾아냈던 그 쪽지의 일에 이르러, 카르데니오는 그것을 똑똑히 기억하고 있으며 그 내용이 이러했노라 말했다."]],[["“Luscinda to Cardenio.","\"루신다가 카르데니오께."]],[["“Every day I discover merits in you that oblige and compel me to hold you in higher estimation; so if you desire to relieve me of this obligation without cost to my honour, you may easily do so.","날마다 저는 당신에게서, 당신을 더 높이 우러르지 않을 수 없게 하는 미덕들을 발견합니다. 그러니 제 명예를 손상하지 않으면서 이 의무에서 저를 풀어 주고자 하신다면, 당신은 손쉽게 그리하실 수 있습니다."],["I have a father who knows you and loves me dearly, who without putting any constraint on my inclination will grant what will be reasonable for you to have, if it be that you value me as you say and as I believe you do.”","제게는 당신을 알고 저를 끔찍이 아끼시는 아버지가 계신데, 제 마음에 아무런 강요도 하지 않으시며, 당신이 말씀하시고 또 제가 믿는 대로 당신이 정녕 저를 귀히 여기신다면, 당신이 가져 마땅한 것을 허락해 주실 것입니다.\""]],[["“By this letter I was induced, as I told you, to demand Luscinda for my wife, and it was through it that Luscinda came to be regarded by Don Fernando as one of the most discreet and prudent women of the day, and this letter it was that suggested his design of ruining me before mine could be carried into effect.","\"이 편지로 말미암아, 앞서 말씀드렸듯, 저는 루신다를 제 아내로 청하게 되었고, 또 이 편지로 말미암아 루신다는 돈 페르난도에게 당대에 가장 사려 깊고 슬기로운 여인 가운데 하나로 여겨지게 되었습니다. 그리고 제 일이 이루어지기 전에 저를 먼저 파멸시키려는 그의 계략을 부추긴 것도 바로 이 편지였습니다."],["I told Don Fernando that all Luscinda’s father was waiting for was that mine should ask her of him, which I did not dare to suggest to him, fearing that he would not consent to do so; not because he did not know perfectly well the rank, goodness, virtue, and beauty of Luscinda, and that she had qualities that would do honour to any family in Spain, but because I was aware that he did not wish me to marry so soon, before seeing what the Duke Ricardo would do for me.","저는 돈 페르난도에게, 루신다의 아버지가 기다리고 계신 것은 다름 아니라 제 아버지가 그분께 루신다를 청하시는 일뿐이라고 말했습니다. 그런데 그 일을 제 아버지께 감히 입에 올리지 못했으니, 아버지가 응하지 않으실까 두려웠기 때문입니다."],["In short, I told him I did not venture to mention it to my father, as well on account of that difficulty, as of many others that discouraged me though I knew not well what they were, only that it seemed to me that what I desired was never to come to pass.","그것은 아버지가 루신다의 신분과 착함과 미덕과 아름다움을 더없이 잘 아셨기 때문이지 — 그녀는 스페인의 어느 가문에라도 영예가 될 만한 자질을 지닌 여인이었으니까요 — 그런 까닭이 아니라, 리카르도 공작께서 저를 위해 무엇을 해 주실지 보시기 전에는 제가 그토록 일찍 혼인하기를 원치 않으심을 제가 알고 있었기 때문입니다. 한마디로, 저는 그에게 제 아버지께 그 말을 꺼낼 엄두가 나지 않노라 했으니, 그 어려움 때문이기도 했고, 또 무엇인지 저로서도 잘 알 수 없으면서 저를 주눅 들게 하던 여러 다른 일들 때문이기도 했습니다."],["To all this Don Fernando answered that he would take it upon himself to speak to my father, and persuade him to speak to Luscinda’s father.","다만 제가 바라는 일이 결코 이루어지지 못할 것만 같았던 것이지요. 이 모든 것에 돈 페르난도는, 자기가 나서서 제 아버지께 말씀드리고, 또 아버지를 설득해 루신다의 아버지께 말씀하시도록 하겠노라 대답했습니다."],["O, ambitious Marius!","오, 야심에 찬 마리우스여!"],["O, cruel Catiline!","오, 잔혹한 카틸리나여!"],["O, wicked Sylla!","오, 사악한 술라여!"],["O, perfidious Ganelon!","오, 배신자 가넬론이여!"],["O, treacherous Vellido!","오, 음험한 베이도여!"],["O, vindictive Julian!","오, 앙심 깊은 훌리안이여!"],["O, covetous Judas!","오, 탐욕스러운 유다여!"],["Traitor, cruel, vindictive, and perfidious, wherein had this poor wretch failed in his fidelity, who with such frankness showed thee the secrets and the joys of his heart?","배신자, 잔혹한 자, 앙심 깊은 자, 음험한 자여, 그토록 솔직하게 제 마음속 비밀과 기쁨을 그대에게 보여 준 이 가련한 자가 그 충심에서 무엇을 잘못했단 말인가?"],["What offence did I commit?","내가 무슨 죄를 지었던가?"],["What words did I utter, or what counsels did I give that had not the furtherance of thy honour and welfare for their aim?","내가 무슨 말을 입에 담았으며, 그대의 명예와 안녕을 도모하려는 것 말고 다른 목적을 둔 무슨 충고를 했단 말인가?"],["But, woe is me, wherefore do I complain?","그러나 아아, 가련한 신세여, 어이하여 나는 한탄하는가?"],["for sure it is that when misfortunes spring from the stars, descending from on high they fall upon us with such fury and violence that no power on earth can check their course nor human device stay their coming.","불행이 별에서 솟아날 적에는 높은 곳에서 내려와 그토록 사납고 거센 기세로 우리에게 덮쳐 오니, 이 땅의 어떤 힘도 그 진로를 막을 수 없고 인간의 어떤 꾀로도 그 닥쳐옴을 멈출 수 없음이 분명하기에 하는 말입니다."],["Who could have thought that Don Fernando, a highborn gentleman, intelligent, bound to me by gratitude for my services, one that could win the object of his love wherever he might set his affections, could have become so obdurate, as they say, as to rob me of my one ewe lamb that was not even yet in my possession?","돈 페르난도가 — 가문 높은 신사요, 총명하며, 제가 베푼 도움에 대한 고마움으로 저와 맺어진 사람이요, 어디에 마음을 두든 자기가 사랑하는 대상을 얻어 낼 수 있는 사람인 그가 — 흔히 말하듯, 아직 제 손에 들어오지도 않은 단 한 마리의 새끼 암양마저 제게서 빼앗을 만큼 그토록 모질어질 수 있으리라고 그 누가 생각이나 했겠습니까?"],["But laying aside these useless and unavailing reflections, let us take up the broken thread of my unhappy story.","그러나 이 부질없고 쓸데없는 넋두리는 접어 두고, 제 불행한 이야기의 끊어진 실타래를 다시 잡아 보겠습니다."]],[["“To proceed, then: Don Fernando finding my presence an obstacle to the execution of his treacherous and wicked design, resolved to send me to his elder brother under the pretext of asking money from him to pay for six horses which, purposely, and with the sole object of sending me away that he might the better carry out his infernal scheme, he had purchased the very day he offered to speak to my father, and the price of which he now desired me to fetch.","이어 말씀드리자면, 돈 페르난도는 자신의 음흉하고 사악한 계획을 실행하는 데 제가 곁에 있는 것이 걸림돌임을 알고는, 자기 형에게 돈을 청하러 간다는 핑계로 저를 형에게 보내기로 마음먹었습니다. 말 여섯 필 값을 치를 돈이라고 했는데, 그 말들은 그가 일부러, 오직 저를 멀리 쫓아 보내어 자기 흉악한 계략을 더 수월히 꾸미려는 단 하나의 목적으로, 바로 제 아버지께 말씀드리겠노라 제안하던 그날 사들인 것이었습니다."],["Could I have anticipated this treachery?","그러고는 이제 그 값을 제가 가서 받아 오기를 바란 것이지요."],["Could I by any chance have suspected it?","제가 이 배신을 미리 내다볼 수 있었겠습니까? 무슨 수로 그것을 의심할 수 있었겠습니까?"],["Nay; so far from that, I offered with the greatest pleasure to go at once, in my satisfaction at the good bargain that had been made.","천만에, 그러기는커녕 저는 흥정이 잘되었다는 만족감에 기꺼이 당장 가겠노라 자청했습니다. 그날 밤 저는 루신다와 이야기를 나누며, 돈 페르난도와 합의한 바를 그녀에게 일러 주고, 우리의 어여쁘고 온당한 소망이 이루어지리라는 큰 희망을 품고 있노라 말했습니다."],["That night I spoke with Luscinda, and told her what had been agreed upon with Don Fernando, and how I had strong hopes of our fair and reasonable wishes being realised.","그녀도 저처럼 돈 페르난도의 배신을 조금도 의심하지 못한 채, 어서 속히 돌아오도록 애써 달라고 당부했으니, 우리 소망의 성취가 늦어지는 것은 오직 제 아버지가 그녀의 아버지께 말씀하시기를 미루는 동안뿐이리라 믿었던 것입니다. 그런데 그녀가 제게 이 말을 할 때 어찌하여 두 눈에 눈물이 가득 고이고 목이 메어, 제게 더 하려는 듯싶던 많은 말들 가운데 단 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 못했는지, 저는 까닭을 알지 못했습니다."],["She, as unsuspicious as I was of the treachery of Don Fernando, bade me try to return speedily, as she believed the fulfilment of our desires would be delayed only so long as my father put off speaking to hers.","저는 이 예사롭지 않은 모습에 놀랐으니, 전에는 그녀에게서 한 번도 보지 못한 것이었기 때문입니다. 우리는 행운과 제 꾀가 기회를 줄 때면 언제나 더없는 즐거움과 명랑함으로 이야기를 나누며, 그 말들 속에 눈물과 한숨과 질투와 의심과 두려움을 뒤섞곤 했습니다."],["I know not why it was that on saying this to me her eyes filled with tears, and there came a lump in her throat that prevented her from uttering a word of many more that it seemed to me she was striving to say to me.","제 쪽에서는 모든 것이, 하늘이 그녀를 제 연인으로 주셨다는 제 행운에 대한 찬미였습니다. 저는 그녀의 아름다움을 기리고, 그녀의 가치와 분별을 드높였으며, 그녀는 저를 향한 사랑으로 제게서 칭찬받을 만하다 여긴 것을 칭찬함으로써 제게 되갚아 주었습니다."],["I was astonished at this unusual turn, which I never before observed in her, for we always conversed, whenever good fortune and my ingenuity gave us the chance, with the greatest gaiety and cheerfulness, mingling tears, sighs, jealousies, doubts, or fears with our words; it was all on my part a eulogy of my good fortune that Heaven should have given her to me for my mistress; I glorified her beauty, I extolled her worth and her understanding; and she paid me back by praising in me what in her love for me she thought worthy of praise; and besides we had a hundred thousand trifles and doings of our neighbours and acquaintances to talk about, and the utmost extent of my boldness was to take, almost by force, one of her fair white hands and carry it to my lips, as well as the closeness of the low grating that separated us allowed me.","게다가 우리에게는 이웃들과 아는 이들에 관한 수십만 가지 자질구레한 이야깃거리며 일들이 있었지요. 그리고 제 대담함이 미친 최대치라고는, 우리를 갈라놓은 낮은 격자창의 좁은 틈이 허락하는 한, 그 어여쁘고 흰 손 하나를 거의 억지로 잡아 입술로 가져가는 것뿐이었습니다."],["But the night before the unhappy day of my departure she wept, she moaned, she sighed, and she withdrew leaving me filled with perplexity and amazement, overwhelmed at the sight of such strange and affecting signs of grief and sorrow in Luscinda; but not to dash my hopes I ascribed it all to the depth of her love for me and the pain that separation gives those who love tenderly.","그러나 제가 떠나야 할 그 불행한 날의 전날 밤, 그녀는 울고, 신음하고, 한숨지었으며, 저를 당혹과 경악에 휩싸인 채 남겨 두고 물러갔으니, 루신다에게서 그토록 야릇하고 가슴 저미는 슬픔과 비탄의 기색을 본 저는 압도되고 말았습니다. 그러나 제 희망을 꺾지 않으려고, 저는 그 모든 것을 저를 향한 그녀의 깊은 사랑과, 애틋이 사랑하는 이들에게 이별이 안기는 아픔 탓으로 돌렸습니다."],["At last I took my departure, sad and dejected, my heart filled with fancies and suspicions, but not knowing well what it was I suspected or fancied; plain omens pointing to the sad event and misfortune that was awaiting me.","마침내 저는 슬프고 의기소침한 채, 가슴은 온갖 망상과 의혹으로 가득 찬 채 길을 떠났으나, 제가 무엇을 의심하고 무엇을 망상하는지 저 자신도 잘 몰랐습니다. 다만 저를 기다리고 있던 그 슬픈 사건과 불행을 가리키는 또렷한 전조들이 있었을 뿐입니다."]],[["“I reached the place whither I had been sent, gave the letter to Don Fernando’s brother, and was kindly received but not promptly dismissed, for he desired me to wait, very much against my will, eight days in some place where the duke his father was not likely to see me, as his brother wrote that the money was to be sent without his knowledge; all of which was a scheme of the treacherous Don Fernando, for his brother had no want of money to enable him to despatch me at once.","저는 보내진 곳에 다다라 돈 페르난도의 형에게 편지를 건넸고, 친절한 대접을 받았으나 곧바로 돌려보내지지는 않았습니다. 그가 제게, 몹시 제 뜻에 거슬리는 일이었지만, 자기 아버지인 공작의 눈에 띄지 않을 만한 어느 곳에서 여드레를 기다리라고 했기 때문입니다. 그의 동생이 편지에 그 돈을 아버지 모르게 보내라고 적었던 까닭이었지요. 이 모든 것이 음흉한 돈 페르난도의 계략이었으니, 그의 형에게는 저를 당장 떠나보낼 돈이 부족할 리 없었기 때문입니다."]],[["“The command was one that exposed me to the temptation of disobeying it, as it seemed to me impossible to endure life for so many days separated from Luscinda, especially after leaving her in the sorrowful mood I have described to you; nevertheless as a dutiful servant I obeyed, though I felt it would be at the cost of my well-being. But four days later there came a man in quest of me with a letter which he gave me, and which by the address I perceived to be from Luscinda, as the writing was hers. I opened it with fear and trepidation, persuaded that it must be something serious that had impelled her to write to me when at a distance, as she seldom did so when I was near. Before reading it I asked the man who it was that had given it to him, and how long he had been upon the road; he told me that as he happened to be passing through one of the streets of the city at the hour of noon, a very beautiful lady called to him from a window, and with tears in her eyes said to him hurriedly, ‘Brother, if you are, as you seem to be, a Christian, for the love of God I entreat you to have this letter despatched without a moment’s delay to the place and person named in the address, all which is well known, and by this you will render a great service to our Lord; and that you may be at no inconvenience in doing so take what is in this handkerchief;’ and said he, ‘with this she threw me a handkerchief out of the window in which were tied up a hundred reals and this gold ring which I bring here together with the letter I have given you. And then without waiting for any answer she left the window, though not before she saw me take the letter and the handkerchief, and I had by signs let her know that I would do as she bade me; and so, seeing myself so well paid for the trouble I would have in bringing it to you, and knowing by the address that it was to you it was sent (for, señor, I know you very well), and also unable to resist that beautiful lady’s tears, I resolved to trust no one else, but to come myself and give it to you, and in sixteen hours from the time when it was given me I have made the journey, which, as you know, is eighteen leagues.’","그 명령은 제가 그것을 어기고픈 유혹에 빠지게 하는 것이었으니, 루신다와 그토록 여러 날을 떨어져 산다는 것이 저로서는 견딜 수 없는 일로 여겨졌기 때문입니다. 더구나 앞서 말씀드린 그 슬픈 기색 속에 그녀를 두고 떠나온 뒤였으니 말이지요. 그러나 도리를 지키는 종복으로서 저는 복종했으니, 비록 그것이 제 행복을 대가로 치르는 일이 되리라 느끼면서도 그리했습니다. 그런데 나흘 뒤 한 사내가 저를 찾아와 편지 한 통을 건넸는데, 겉봉으로 보아 루신다에게서 온 것임을 알아차렸으니, 글씨가 그녀의 것이었기 때문입니다. 저는 두려움과 떨림에 휩싸여 그것을 열었으니, 제가 가까이 있을 때는 좀처럼 그러지 않던 그녀가 먼 곳에 있는 제게 편지를 쓰도록 떠민 것이라면 틀림없이 무언가 심상찮은 일이리라 여겼던 것입니다. 읽기에 앞서 저는 그 사내에게, 누가 그것을 그에게 주었으며 길에 나선 지 얼마나 되었는지 물었습니다. 그는 마침 정오 무렵 그 도시의 어느 거리를 지나다가, 매우 아름다운 한 부인이 창문에서 자기를 불러, 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 다급히 이렇게 말하더랍니다. '형제여, 보아하니 그대가 그리스도교인인 듯하니, 하느님의 사랑으로 그대에게 간청합니다. 부디 이 편지를 한시도 지체 말고 겉봉에 적힌 곳과 사람에게 보내 주세요. 그곳은 잘 알려진 데이며, 이로써 그대는 우리 주님께 큰 봉사를 드리게 될 것입니다. 또 그리하시는 데 불편함이 없도록, 이 손수건 안에 든 것을 받으세요.' 그러고는 '이렇게 말하며 그 부인이 창밖으로 손수건 하나를 던져 주었는데, 그 안에는 백 레알과, 제가 여기 이 편지와 함께 가져온 이 금가락지가 묶여 있었습니다. 그러고는 답을 기다리지도 않고 창에서 물러났는데, 다만 제가 편지와 손수건을 받는 것을 보고, 또 제가 손짓으로 그분이 시키신 대로 하겠다고 알린 뒤에야 그리했습니다. 그리하여 그것을 나리께 가져다 드리는 수고에 대한 삯을 그토록 후하게 받은 데다, 겉봉으로 그것이 나리께 보내진 것임을 알았고(나리, 저는 나리를 아주 잘 압니다), 또 그 아름다운 부인의 눈물을 차마 외면할 수도 없어, 저는 다른 누구에게도 맡기지 않고 몸소 와서 나리께 드리기로 마음먹었습니다. 그래서 그것을 받은 때로부터 열여섯 시간 만에 이 길을 왔으니, 나리도 아시다시피 십팔 레과나 되는 길이지요'라고 했습니다."]],[["“All the while the good-natured improvised courier was telling me this, I hung upon his words, my legs trembling under me so that I could scarcely stand.","이 마음씨 좋은 즉석 전령이 제게 이 이야기를 하는 동안 내내, 저는 그의 말 한마디 한마디에 매달려 있었고, 두 다리가 후들거려 제대로 서 있기조차 힘들 지경이었습니다."],["However, I opened the letter and read these words:","그래도 저는 편지를 열어 이런 말을 읽었습니다."]],[["“‘The promise Don Fernando gave you to urge your father to speak to mine, he has fulfilled much more to his own satisfaction than to your advantage.","'돈 페르난도가 당신께, 당신의 아버지를 다그쳐 제 아버지께 말씀하시도록 하겠다고 한 약속을, 그는 당신께 이로운 쪽보다 자기 자신에게 흡족한 쪽으로 훨씬 더 잘 지켰습니다."],["I have to tell you, señor, that he has demanded me for a wife, and my father, led away by what he considers Don Fernando’s superiority over you, has favoured his suit so cordially, that in two days hence the betrothal is to take place with such secrecy and so privately that the only witnesses are to be the Heavens above and a few of the household.","당신께 말씀드려야겠으니, 그가 저를 아내로 청했고, 돈 페르난도가 당신보다 낫다고 여기시는 데 마음이 끌린 제 아버지가 그의 청혼을 어찌나 흔쾌히 받아들이셨던지, 이제 이틀 뒤면 약혼식이 치러진다 합니다. 그것도 어찌나 은밀하고 남몰래 행해지는지, 증인이라고는 위로는 하늘이요, 집안의 몇몇 사람뿐입니다."],["Picture to yourself the state I am in; judge if it be urgent for you to come; the issue of the affair will show you whether I love you or not.","제가 어떤 처지에 놓여 있는지 그려 보세요. 당신이 와 주셔야 할 일이 얼마나 급한지 헤아려 보세요."],["God grant this may come to your hand before mine shall be forced to link itself with his who keeps so ill the faith that he has pledged.’","이 일의 결말이 제가 당신을 사랑하는지 아닌지를 당신께 보여 드릴 것입니다. 부디 이것이, 맹세한 신의를 그토록 못되게 지키는 그자와 제 손이 억지로 맺어지기 전에 당신 손에 닿기를 하느님께 빕니다.'"]],[["“Such, in brief, were the words of the letter, words that made me set out at once without waiting any longer for reply or money; for I now saw clearly that it was not the purchase of horses but of his own pleasure that had made Don Fernando send me to his brother. The exasperation I felt against Don Fernando, joined with the fear of losing the prize I had won by so many years of love and devotion, lent me wings; so that almost flying I reached home the same day, by the hour which served for speaking with Luscinda. I arrived unobserved, and left the mule on which I had come at the house of the worthy man who had brought me the letter, and fortune was pleased to be for once so kind that I found Luscinda at the grating that was the witness of our loves. She recognised me at once, and I her, but not as she ought to have recognised me, or I her. But who is there in the world that can boast of having fathomed or understood the wavering mind and unstable nature of a woman? Of a truth no one. To proceed: as soon as Luscinda saw me she said, ‘Cardenio, I am in my bridal dress, and the treacherous Don Fernando and my covetous father are waiting for me in the hall with the other witnesses, who shall be the witnesses of my death before they witness my betrothal. Be not distressed, my friend, but contrive to be present at this sacrifice, and if that cannot be prevented by my words, I have a dagger concealed which will prevent more deliberate violence, putting an end to my life and giving thee a first proof of the love I have borne and bear thee.’ I replied to her distractedly and hastily, in fear lest I should not have time to reply, ‘May thy words be verified by thy deeds, lady; and if thou hast a dagger to save thy honour, I have a sword to defend thee or kill myself if fortune be against us.’","이것이, 간추리자면, 그 편지의 내용이었으니, 이 말들에 저는 답장도 돈도 더 기다리지 않고 당장 길을 나섰습니다. 이제 저는 돈 페르난도가 저를 자기 형에게 보낸 것이 말을 사기 위해서가 아니라 제 욕망을 채우기 위해서였음을 똑똑히 보았기 때문입니다 — 아니, 그 자신의 욕망을 채우기 위해서였지요. 돈 페르난도에게 느낀 격분이 그토록 여러 해의 사랑과 헌신으로 얻은 상을 잃을지도 모른다는 두려움과 한데 어우러져 제게 날개를 달아 주었으니, 그리하여 저는 거의 날다시피 하여 그날 안으로, 루신다와 이야기 나누기에 알맞은 시각에 맞춰 집에 다다랐습니다. 저는 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 도착하여, 타고 온 노새를 제게 편지를 가져다준 그 착한 사내의 집에 두었습니다. 그리고 운명이 이번만큼은 다행히 친절하여, 저는 우리 사랑의 증인이었던 그 격자창 앞에 있는 루신다를 보게 되었습니다. 그녀는 곧 저를 알아보았고, 저도 그녀를 알아보았으나, 그녀가 저를 알아보아야 마땅했을 그 모습으로도, 제가 그녀를 알아보아야 마땅했을 그 모습으로도 아니었습니다. 그러나 세상에 그 누가 여인의 흔들리는 마음과 변덕스러운 천성을 헤아렸다거나 알았노라 뽐낼 수 있겠습니까? 진실로 아무도 없습니다. 이어 말씀드리지요. 루신다는 저를 보자마자 이렇게 말했습니다. '카르데니오, 저는 신부 차림을 하고 있고, 음흉한 돈 페르난도와 탐욕스러운 제 아버지가 다른 증인들과 함께 홀에서 저를 기다리고 있습니다. 그러나 그들은 제 약혼을 보기에 앞서 제 죽음의 증인이 될 것입니다. 괴로워 마세요, 벗이여, 부디 이 제물 바치는 자리에 와 주세요. 그리고 만일 제 말로써 그것을 막을 수 없거든, 제게 숨겨 둔 단검이 있어 그 한층 작정된 폭력을 막아 줄 것이니, 제 목숨을 끊어 제가 당신께 품어 왔고 또 품고 있는 사랑의 첫 증거를 당신께 드리겠습니다.' 저는 답할 틈조차 없을까 두려워, 제정신이 아닌 채 다급히 그녀에게 답했습니다. '그대의 말이 그대의 행동으로 증명되기를, 부인이여. 그리고 그대에게 명예를 지킬 단검이 있다면, 제게는 그대를 지키거나, 운명이 우리를 거스른다면 저 자신을 베어 죽일 칼이 있습니다.'"]],[["“I think she could not have heard all these words, for I perceived that they called her away in haste, as the bridegroom was waiting.","그녀가 이 모든 말을 다 듣지는 못했으리라 생각합니다. 신랑이 기다리고 있던 터라 그녀를 다급히 불러 가는 것을 제가 보았기 때문입니다."],["Now the night of my sorrow set in, the sun of my happiness went down, I felt my eyes bereft of sight, my mind of reason.","이제 제 슬픔의 밤이 깃들고, 제 행복의 해가 저물었으며, 저는 두 눈이 시력을 잃고 정신이 이성을 잃는 것을 느꼈습니다. 저는 그 집에 들어갈 수도 없었고, 어떤 움직임도 할 수 없었습니다."],["I could not enter the house, nor was I capable of any movement; but reflecting how important it was that I should be present at what might take place on the occasion, I nerved myself as best I could and went in, for I well knew all the entrances and outlets; and besides, with the confusion that in secret pervaded the house no one took notice of me, so, without being seen, I found an opportunity of placing myself in the recess formed by a window of the hall itself, and concealed by the ends and borders of two tapestries, from between which I could, without being seen, see all that took place in the room.","그러나 그 자리에서 벌어질지도 모를 일에 제가 함께 있는 것이 얼마나 중요한지를 헤아리고는, 할 수 있는 한 마음을 다잡아 안으로 들어갔으니, 모든 드나드는 문과 길목을 제가 잘 알고 있었기 때문입니다. 게다가 은밀하게 집안에 퍼져 있던 혼란 덕분에 아무도 저를 눈여겨보지 않았습니다."],["Who could describe the agitation of heart I suffered as I stood there--the thoughts that came to me--the reflections that passed through my mind?","그리하여 저는 들키지 않고, 바로 그 홀의 창이 이룬 후미진 자리에 몸을 둘 기회를 얻었으니, 두 휘장의 끝자락과 가장자리에 가려져 그 사이로 들키지 않고 방 안에서 벌어지는 모든 일을 볼 수 있었습니다. 거기 서 있는 동안 제가 겪은 가슴의 동요를 — 제게 밀려든 생각들을, 제 머릿속을 스쳐 간 상념들을 — 그 누가 그려 낼 수 있겠습니까?"],["They were such as cannot be, nor were it well they should be, told.","그것들은 입에 담을 수 없는 것들이며, 입에 담는다 한들 좋을 것 없는 것들입니다."],["Suffice it to say that the bridegroom entered the hall in his usual dress, without ornament of any kind; as groomsman he had with him a cousin of Luscinda’s and except the servants of the house there was no one else in the chamber.","신랑이 여느 때 차림 그대로, 아무런 장식도 없이 홀로 들어왔다는 것만 말씀드려도 충분하겠지요. 들러리로는 루신다의 사촌 하나를 데리고 있었고, 집안의 하인들 말고는 방 안에 다른 사람은 아무도 없었습니다."],["Soon afterwards Luscinda came out from an antechamber, attended by her mother and two of her damsels, arrayed and adorned as became her rank and beauty, and in full festival and ceremonial attire.","곧이어 루신다가 그녀의 어머니와 두 시녀의 시중을 받으며 곁방에서 나왔는데, 그 신분과 아름다움에 걸맞게 단장하고 꾸민, 한껏 차린 예복 차림이었습니다."],["My anxiety and distraction did not allow me to observe or notice particularly what she wore; I could only perceive the colours, which were crimson and white, and the glitter of the gems and jewels on her head dress and apparel, surpassed by the rare beauty of her lovely auburn hair that vying with the precious stones and the light of the four torches that stood in the hall shone with a brighter gleam than all.","제 불안과 심란함은 그녀가 무엇을 걸쳤는지 자세히 살피거나 눈여겨보도록 허락하지 않았습니다. 저는 다만 그 빛깔이 진홍과 흰빛임을, 그리고 그녀의 머리 장식과 의상에 박힌 보석과 패물의 반짝임을 알아볼 수 있었을 뿐인데, 그 빛남마저 그녀의 어여쁜 적갈색 머리칼의 보기 드문 아름다움에 압도되고 말았으니, 그 머리칼은 보석들과 홀에 세워진 네 자루 횃불의 빛과 겨루며 그 모두보다 더 밝은 광채로 빛났습니다."],["Oh memory, mortal foe of my peace!","오, 기억이여, 내 평안의 죽음을 부르는 원수여!"],["why bring before me now the incomparable beauty of that adored enemy of mine?","어이하여 너는 지금 내 앞에, 나의 그 흠모하던 원수의 비할 데 없는 아름다움을 끌어다 놓는가?"],["Were it not better, cruel memory, to remind me and recall what she then did, that stirred by a wrong so glaring I may seek, if not vengeance now, at least to rid myself of life?","차라리, 잔혹한 기억이여, 그때 그녀가 한 일을 내게 일깨우고 떠올려 주는 편이 낫지 않겠는가, 그토록 명백한 잘못에 자극받아 내가, 지금 복수는 못할지언정 적어도 이 목숨이나마 떨쳐 버리려 애쓰도록 말이다."],["Be not weary, sirs, of listening to these digressions; my sorrow is not one of those that can or should be told tersely and briefly, for to me each incident seems to call for many words.”","여러분, 이 곁가지 이야기들을 듣느라 지치지 마십시오. 제 슬픔은 간결하고 짤막하게 이야기할 수 있거나 이야기해야 마땅한 그런 부류의 것이 아니니, 제게는 사건 하나하나가 많은 말을 부르는 것만 같기 때문입니다.\""]],[["To this the curate replied that not only were they not weary of listening to him, but that the details he mentioned interested them greatly, being of a kind by no means to be omitted and deserving of the same attention as the main story.","이에 신부는, 그의 이야기를 듣는 것이 지겹기는커녕 그가 언급하는 세세한 일들이 자기들의 흥미를 크게 끄니, 결코 빠뜨려서는 안 될 종류의 것이요 본 이야기와 똑같은 주의를 기울일 만한 것이라고 대답했다."]],[["“To proceed, then,”","\"그럼 이어 말씀드리지요.\" 카르데니오가 말을 이었다."],["continued Cardenio: “all being assembled in the hall, the priest of the parish came in and as he took the pair by the hand to perform the requisite ceremony, at the words, ‘Will you, Señora Luscinda, take Señor Don Fernando, here present, for your lawful husband, as the holy Mother Church ordains?’ I thrust my head and neck out from between the tapestries, and with eager ears and throbbing heart set myself to listen to Luscinda’s answer, awaiting in her reply the sentence of death or the grant of life.","\"모두가 홀에 모이자, 그 교구의 신부가 들어와 정해진 예식을 치르려고 두 사람의 손을 맞잡았습니다. 그리고 '루신다 양, 거룩한 어머니 교회가 정하신 대로, 여기 계신 돈 페르난도 님을 그대의 합법적인 남편으로 맞이하겠습니까?'라는 말에 이르렀을 때, 저는 휘장 사이로 머리와 목을 내밀고, 안달하는 귀와 두근거리는 가슴으로 루신다의 대답에 귀를 곤두세웠으니, 그 대답 속에서 사형 선고든 목숨의 허락이든 기다렸던 것입니다."],["Oh, that I had but dared at that moment to rush forward crying aloud, ‘Luscinda, Luscinda!","오, 그 순간 제가 감히 앞으로 뛰쳐나가 큰 소리로 외칠 수 있었더라면! '루신다, 루신다!"],["have a care what thou dost; remember what thou owest me; bethink thee thou art mine and canst not be another’s; reflect that thy utterance of “Yes”","그대가 하는 일을 조심하시오. 그대가 내게 진 빚을 기억하시오."],["and the end of my life will come at the same instant.","그대는 내 사람이며 다른 이의 사람이 될 수 없음을 헤아리시오. 그대가 「예」라고 말하는 것과 내 목숨이 끝나는 것이 같은 순간에 닥치리라는 것을 생각하시오."],["O, treacherous Don Fernando!","오, 음흉한 돈 페르난도여! 내 영광을 빼앗는 도적이요, 내 목숨의 죽음이여!"],["robber of my glory, death of my life!","그대는 무엇을 노리는가? 그대가 그리스도교인으로서는 그대 소망의 대상을 얻을 수 없음을 기억하라, 루신다는 내 신부요 나는 그녀의 남편이니!'라고 말입니다."],["What seekest thou?","어리석은 저여!"],["Remember that thou canst not as a Christian attain the object of thy wishes, for Luscinda is my bride, and I am her husband!’ Fool that I am!","이제 멀리 떨어져 위험에서 벗어난 지금에야, 저는 제가 하지 않았던 일을 했어야 했다고 말합니다. 이제 제 소중한 보물을 빼앗기도록 내버려 둔 지금에야, 저는 그 도적을 저주합니다."],["now that I am far away, and out of danger, I say I should have done what I did not do: now that I have allowed my precious treasure to be robbed from me, I curse the robber, on whom I might have taken vengeance had I as much heart for it as I have for bewailing my fate; in short, as I was then a coward and a fool, little wonder is it if I am now dying shame-stricken, remorseful, and mad.","한탄할 마음만큼이나 복수할 마음이 제게 있었더라면 그자에게 복수할 수도 있었을 텐데 말이지요. 한마디로, 그때 제가 겁쟁이요 바보였으니, 제가 지금 수치심에 사로잡혀, 회한에 젖어, 미쳐서 죽어 가고 있다 한들 이상할 것이 무엇이겠습니까."]],[["“The priest stood waiting for the answer of Luscinda, who for a long time withheld it; and just as I thought she was taking out the dagger to save her honour, or struggling for words to make some declaration of the truth on my behalf, I heard her say in a faint and feeble voice, ‘I will:’ Don Fernando said the same, and giving her the ring they stood linked by a knot that could never be loosed.","신부는 루신다의 대답을 기다리며 서 있었고, 그녀는 오랫동안 그것을 미루었습니다. 그래서 제가 막 그녀가 명예를 지키려고 단검을 꺼내거나, 저를 위해 진실을 밝힐 무슨 선언을 하려고 말을 찾느라 애쓰는 줄로 여기던 바로 그때, 저는 그녀가 가냘프고 힘없는 목소리로 '네, 그러겠습니다'라고 말하는 것을 들었습니다."],["The bridegroom then approached to embrace his bride; and she, pressing her hand upon her heart, fell fainting in her mother’s arms.","돈 페르난도도 같은 말을 했고, 그녀에게 가락지를 끼워 주자 두 사람은 결코 풀 수 없는 매듭으로 맺어졌습니다."],["It only remains now for me to tell you the state I was in when in that consent that I heard I saw all my hopes mocked, the words and promises of Luscinda proved falsehoods, and the recovery of the prize I had that instant lost rendered impossible for ever.","이어 신랑이 신부를 안으려 다가오자, 그녀는 손을 가슴에 누른 채 어머니의 품 안으로 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌습니다. 이제 제가 여러분께 말씀드릴 것은, 제가 들은 그 승낙 속에서 제 모든 희망이 조롱당하고, 루신다의 말과 약속이 거짓으로 드러나며, 그 순간 잃은 상을 되찾는 일이 영영 불가능해진 것을 보았을 때, 제가 어떤 처지에 놓였는가 하는 것뿐입니다."],["I stood stupefied, wholly abandoned, it seemed, by Heaven, declared the enemy of the earth that bore me, the air refusing me breath for my sighs, the water moisture for my tears; it was only the fire that gathered strength so that my whole frame glowed with rage and jealousy.","저는 멍하니 넋이 나간 채 서 있었으니, 하늘에 온전히 버림받은 듯하였고, 저를 낳아 준 이 땅의 원수로 선언된 듯하였으며, 대기는 제 한숨에 숨결을 내주기를 마다하고, 물은 제 눈물에 물기를 내주기를 마다하는 듯하였습니다. 오직 불만이 기세를 더해 제 온몸이 분노와 질투로 이글거렸습니다."],["They were all thrown into confusion by Luscinda’s fainting, and as her mother was unlacing her to give her air a sealed paper was discovered in her bosom which Don Fernando seized at once and began to read by the light of one of the torches.","모두가 루신다의 졸도로 혼란에 빠졌고, 그녀의 어머니가 바람을 통하게 하려고 옷끈을 풀던 중, 그녀의 품속에서 봉인된 쪽지 한 장이 드러났습니다. 돈 페르난도가 그것을 단번에 낚아채 횃불 하나의 불빛에 비춰 읽기 시작했습니다."],["As soon as he had read it he seated himself in a chair, leaning his cheek on his hand in the attitude of one deep in thought, without taking any part in the efforts that were being made to recover his bride from her fainting fit.","그것을 다 읽자마자 그는 의자에 앉아, 한 손에 뺨을 괴고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 사람의 자세로, 졸도한 신부를 깨어나게 하려는 갖은 애씀에는 아무런 손도 보태지 않았습니다."]],[["“Seeing all the household in confusion, I ventured to come out regardless whether I were seen or not, and determined, if I were, to do some frenzied deed that would prove to all the world the righteous indignation of my breast in the punishment of the treacherous Don Fernando, and even in that of the fickle fainting traitress. But my fate, doubtless reserving me for greater sorrows, if such there be, so ordered it that just then I had enough and to spare of that reason which has since been wanting to me; and so, without seeking to take vengeance on my greatest enemies (which might have been easily taken, as all thought of me was so far from their minds), I resolved to take it upon myself, and on myself to inflict the pain they deserved, perhaps with even greater severity than I should have dealt out to them had I then slain them; for sudden pain is soon over, but that which is protracted by tortures is ever slaying without ending life. In a word, I quitted the house and reached that of the man with whom I had left my mule; I made him saddle it for me, mounted without bidding him farewell, and rode out of the city, like another Lot, not daring to turn my head to look back upon it; and when I found myself alone in the open country, screened by the darkness of the night, and tempted by the stillness to give vent to my grief without apprehension or fear of being heard or seen, then I broke silence and lifted up my voice in maledictions upon Luscinda and Don Fernando, as if I could thus avenge the wrong they had done me. I called her cruel, ungrateful, false, thankless, but above all covetous, since the wealth of my enemy had blinded the eyes of her affection, and turned it from me to transfer it to one to whom fortune had been more generous and liberal. And yet, in the midst of this outburst of execration and upbraiding, I found excuses for her, saying it was no wonder that a young girl in the seclusion of her parents’ house, trained and schooled to obey them always, should have been ready to yield to their wishes when they offered her for a husband a gentleman of such distinction, wealth, and noble birth, that if she had refused to accept him she would have been thought out of her senses, or to have set her affection elsewhere, a suspicion injurious to her fair name and fame. But then again, I said, had she declared I was her husband, they would have seen that in choosing me she had not chosen so ill but that they might excuse her, for before Don Fernando had made his offer, they themselves could not have desired, if their desires had been ruled by reason, a more eligible husband for their daughter than I was; and she, before taking the last fatal step of giving her hand, might easily have said that I had already given her mine, for I should have come forward to support any assertion of hers to that effect. In short, I came to the conclusion that feeble love, little reflection, great ambition, and a craving for rank, had made her forget the words with which she had deceived me, encouraged and supported by my firm hopes and honourable passion.","집안 모두가 혼란에 빠진 것을 본 저는, 들키든 말든 개의치 않고 감히 밖으로 나서기로 했고, 만일 들킨다면 음흉한 돈 페르난도를 벌하고, 나아가 그 변덕스럽게 졸도한 배신녀까지 벌함으로써, 제 가슴속의 의로운 분노를 온 세상에 보일 무슨 미친 짓이라도 저지르기로 작정했습니다. 그러나 제 운명은, 의심할 바 없이 더 큰 슬픔들이 — 그런 것이 있다면 — 저를 기다리도록 남겨 두려는 듯, 마침 그때 제게는 그 뒤로 줄곧 모자랐던 그 이성이 차고도 남도록 있게 하였습니다. 그리하여 저는 — 제 생각이 그들의 머릿속에서 까마득히 멀었던지라 손쉽게 할 수 있었을 — 가장 큰 원수들에게 복수하려 들지 않고, 그것을 제 자신에게 떠맡겨, 그들이 받아 마땅한 고통을 제 자신에게 가하기로 마음먹었습니다. 어쩌면 그때 제가 그들을 죽였더라면 안겨 주었을 것보다 한층 더 혹독하게 말이지요. 갑작스러운 고통은 이내 끝나지만, 고문으로 질질 끌리는 고통은 목숨을 끊지는 않으면서 끊임없이 죽이는 까닭입니다. 한마디로, 저는 그 집을 나와 제 노새를 맡겨 둔 사내의 집에 이르러, 그에게 노새에 안장을 얹게 하고는 작별 인사도 없이 올라타, 또 한 사람의 롯처럼 감히 고개를 돌려 도시를 돌아보지도 못한 채 그곳을 빠져나왔습니다. 그리고 밤의 어둠에 가려져 너른 들판에 홀로 있게 되었을 때, 들키거나 들릴 염려나 두려움 없이 제 슬픔을 쏟아 내라 부추기는 그 고요함에 이끌려, 저는 비로소 침묵을 깨고 목청을 높여 루신다와 돈 페르난도에게 저주를 퍼부었습니다. 마치 그렇게 함으로써 그들이 제게 저지른 잘못에 복수할 수 있기라도 한 양 말입니다. 저는 그녀를 잔혹하다, 은혜를 모른다, 거짓되다, 배은망덕하다 불렀고, 무엇보다도 탐욕스럽다 불렀으니, 제 원수의 재물이 그녀의 애정 어린 눈을 멀게 하여 그것을 저에게서 거두어, 운명이 더 후하고 너그러이 대해 준 자에게로 옮겨 버렸기 때문입니다. 그러나 이 저주와 책망을 쏟아 내는 와중에도, 저는 그녀를 위한 변명을 찾아내고 있었습니다. 부모의 슬하에 갇혀 늘 부모에게 복종하도록 길러지고 가르침받은 어린 처녀가, 그토록 지체 높고 부유하며 가문 좋은 신사를 남편으로 권할 때, 기꺼이 그 뜻에 따르려 한 것은 이상할 것이 없다고 말입니다. 만일 그녀가 그를 받아들이기를 마다했더라면 제정신이 아니라 여겨지거나, 다른 데 마음을 두었다 여겨졌을 테니, 그것은 그녀의 어여쁜 이름과 명성에 해가 되는 의심이었겠지요. 그러나 또 한편으로 저는, 만일 그녀가 제가 자기 남편이라고 밝혔더라면, 그들도 그녀가 저를 택한 것이 그리 나쁜 선택이 아니어서 그녀를 너그러이 보아 줄 만하다고 여겼으리라 말했습니다. 돈 페르난도가 청혼하기 전이었다면, 그들 자신도 — 그 바람이 이성에 따라 다스려졌더라면 — 저보다 그 딸에게 더 알맞은 남편감을 바랄 수는 없었을 테니까요. 그리고 그녀도, 손을 내주는 그 마지막 치명적인 한 걸음을 내딛기에 앞서, 제가 이미 제 손을 그녀에게 주었노라고 손쉽게 말할 수 있었을 것입니다. 그 말을 뒷받침하려고 제가 나섰을 테니 말입니다. 한마디로, 저는 가냘픈 사랑과, 모자란 분별과, 큰 야심과, 지체를 향한 갈망이, 제 굳건한 희망과 떳떳한 정열로 북돋우고 떠받친 가운데 저를 속였던 그 말들을 그녀로 하여금 잊게 했다는 결론에 이르렀습니다."]],[["“Thus soliloquising and agitated, I journeyed onward for the remainder of the night, and by daybreak I reached one of the passes of these mountains, among which I wandered for three days more without taking any path or road, until I came to some meadows lying on I know not which side of the mountains, and there I inquired of some herdsmen in what direction the most rugged part of the range lay. They told me that it was in this quarter, and I at once directed my course hither, intending to end my life here; but as I was making my way among these crags, my mule dropped dead through fatigue and hunger, or, as I think more likely, in order to have done with such a worthless burden as it bore in me. I was left on foot, worn out, famishing, without anyone to help me or any thought of seeking help: and so thus I lay stretched on the ground, how long I know not, after which I rose up free from hunger, and found beside me some goatherds, who no doubt were the persons who had relieved me in my need, for they told me how they had found me, and how I had been uttering ravings that showed plainly I had lost my reason; and since then I am conscious that I am not always in full possession of it, but at times so deranged and crazed that I do a thousand mad things, tearing my clothes, crying aloud in these solitudes, cursing my fate, and idly calling on the dear name of her who is my enemy, and only seeking to end my life in lamentation; and when I recover my senses I find myself so exhausted and weary that I can scarcely move. Most commonly my dwelling is the hollow of a cork tree large enough to shelter this miserable body; the herdsmen and goatherds who frequent these mountains, moved by compassion, furnish me with food, leaving it by the wayside or on the rocks, where they think I may perhaps pass and find it; and so, even though I may be then out of my senses, the wants of nature teach me what is required to sustain me, and make me crave it and eager to take it. At other times, so they tell me when they find me in a rational mood, I sally out upon the road, and though they would gladly give it me, I snatch food by force from the shepherds bringing it from the village to their huts. Thus do pass the wretched life that remains to me, until it be Heaven’s will to bring it to a close, or so to order my memory that I no longer recollect the beauty and treachery of Luscinda, or the wrong done me by Don Fernando; for if it will do this without depriving me of life, I will turn my thoughts into some better channel; if not, I can only implore it to have full mercy on my soul, for in myself I feel no power or strength to release my body from this strait in which I have of my own accord chosen to place it.","이렇게 혼잣말을 되뇌며 심란해진 채, 저는 그 밤의 나머지 동안 내쳐 길을 갔고, 동틀 무렵 이 산들의 어느 고갯길에 다다랐습니다. 그 산들 사이를 저는 사흘이나 더, 어떤 오솔길이나 큰길도 잡지 않은 채 헤맸으니, 마침내 산의 어느 쪽인지도 모를 곳에 펼쳐진 풀밭에 이르렀고, 거기서 어느 목자들에게 이 산줄기에서 가장 험한 데가 어느 쪽이냐고 물었습니다. 그들이 이쪽이라고 일러 주기에, 저는 곧 이리로 길을 잡았으니, 여기서 제 목숨을 끝낼 작정이었습니다. 그러나 이 바위투성이 길을 헤쳐 가던 중 제 노새가 지친 데다 굶주려 쓰러져 죽었습니다. 아니, 제 생각으로는, 제 몸뚱이처럼 쓸모없는 짐을 진 노릇을 그만 끝내려고 그랬던 것이 더 그럴듯하지요. 저는 두 발로 남겨졌고, 기진맥진하고 굶주린 채, 저를 도와줄 이도 없거니와 도움을 구할 생각조차 없었습니다. 그리하여 저는 얼마나 오래인지도 모른 채 땅바닥에 뻗어 누웠다가, 그 뒤에 굶주림에서 벗어나 일어나, 제 곁에 어떤 염소지기들이 있는 것을 보았습니다. 그들이 분명 곤경에 처한 저를 거두어 준 이들이었으니, 자기들이 저를 어떻게 발견했는지, 또 제가 어떻게 분별을 잃었음이 또렷이 드러나는 헛소리를 지껄이고 있었는지를 제게 일러 주었기 때문입니다. 그리고 그때 이래로 저는 제가 늘 온전한 분별을 지니고 있지는 못하며, 때로는 어찌나 흐트러지고 미쳐 버리는지 천 가지 미친 짓을 저지른다는 것을 의식하고 있습니다. 옷을 찢고, 이 황량한 곳에서 큰 소리로 울부짖고, 제 운명을 저주하고, 제 원수인 그녀의 정다운 이름을 부질없이 부르며, 그저 한탄 속에 목숨을 끝내려고만 하지요. 그러다 제정신이 돌아오면 저는 너무도 기진하고 지쳐 있어 거의 움직일 수조차 없습니다. 가장 흔히 제 거처가 되는 곳은 이 가련한 몸뚱이를 가려 줄 만큼 큼지막한 코르크나무의 구멍입니다. 이 산들을 드나드는 소치기들과 염소지기들이 측은한 마음에 길가나 바위 위에, 제가 어쩌면 지나다 발견할 만한 곳에 먹을 것을 놓아 두어 저를 먹입니다. 그리하여 그때 제가 비록 제정신이 아니더라도, 자연의 욕구가 저를 지탱하는 데 무엇이 필요한지를 가르쳐 주어, 그것을 갈망하고 기꺼이 집어 들게 합니다. 또 어떤 때는, 그들이 저를 제정신인 때에 발견하고 일러 주는 바에 따르면, 제가 길로 뛰쳐나가, 그들이 기꺼이 내주려는데도, 마을에서 오두막으로 먹을 것을 나르는 양치기들에게서 그것을 억지로 빼앗는다 합니다. 이렇게 저는 제게 남은 비참한 삶을, 하늘이 그것을 끝내 주실 뜻을 품으시거나, 아니면 제 기억을 다스리시어 제가 더는 루신다의 아름다움과 배신도, 돈 페르난도가 제게 저지른 잘못도 떠올리지 않게 해 주실 때까지 보내고 있습니다. 만일 하늘이 제 목숨을 앗지 않으면서 그리해 주신다면, 저는 제 생각을 더 나은 길로 돌리겠습니다. 그렇지 않다면, 저는 그저 하늘이 제 영혼에 가득한 자비를 베풀어 주시기를 빌 수밖에 없으니, 제 자신 안에는 제 발로 들어앉기를 택한 이 곤경에서 제 몸을 풀어 줄 아무런 힘도 기력도 느껴지지 않기 때문입니다."]],[["“Such, sirs, is the dismal story of my misfortune: say if it be one that can be told with less emotion than you have seen in me; and do not trouble yourselves with urging or pressing upon me what reason suggests as likely to serve for my relief, for it will avail me as much as the medicine prescribed by a wise physician avails the sick man who will not take it. I have no wish for health without Luscinda; and since it is her pleasure to be another’s, when she is or should be mine, let it be mine to be a prey to misery when I might have enjoyed happiness. She by her fickleness strove to make my ruin irretrievable; I will strive to gratify her wishes by seeking destruction; and it will show generations to come that I alone was deprived of that of which all others in misfortune have a superabundance, for to them the impossibility of being consoled is itself a consolation, while to me it is the cause of greater sorrows and sufferings, for I think that even in death there will not be an end of them.”","여러분, 이것이 제 불행의 음울한 이야기입니다. 이것이 여러분께서 제게서 보신 것보다 덜한 동요로 이야기될 수 있는 것인지 말씀해 보십시오. 그리고 이성이 제 위안에 도움이 되리라 일러 주는 것을 제게 권하거나 들이대시느라 수고하지 마십시오. 그것은, 약을 먹지 않으려는 병자에게 슬기로운 의원이 처방한 약이 소용없는 것만큼이나 제게 소용없을 것이기 때문입니다. 저는 루신다 없는 건강은 바라지 않습니다. 그리고 그녀가, 제 사람이거나 마땅히 제 사람이어야 할 그녀가, 다른 이의 사람이 되기를 기뻐하니, 제가 행복을 누릴 수도 있었던 그때에 비참의 먹이가 되는 것도 제 몫이 되게 하겠습니다. 그녀는 변덕으로 제 파멸을 돌이킬 수 없게 만들려 애썼으니, 저는 파멸을 좇음으로써 그녀의 소망을 이루어 주려 애쓰겠습니다. 그리고 이는 다가올 세대들에게, 불행에 빠진 다른 모든 이가 차고 넘치게 가진 그것을 오직 저만이 빼앗겼음을 보여 줄 것입니다. 그들에게는 위로받을 수 없다는 것 자체가 하나의 위로이지만, 제게는 그것이 더 큰 슬픔과 고통의 원인이기 때문입니다. 죽음에서조차 그것들의 끝은 없으리라 저는 생각하니까요.\""]],[["Here Cardenio brought to a close his long discourse and story, as full of misfortune as it was of love; but just as the curate was going to address some words of comfort to him, he was stopped by a voice that reached his ear, saying in melancholy tones what will be told in the Fourth Part of this narrative; for at this point the sage and sagacious historian, Cide Hamete Benengeli, brought the Third to a conclusion.","여기서 카르데니오는, 사랑으로 가득한 만큼이나 불행으로도 가득한 자신의 긴 이야기와 사연을 끝맺었다. 그런데 신부가 막 그에게 위로의 말 몇 마디를 건네려는 참에, 어떤 목소리 하나가 그의 귀에 닿아 그를 멈춰 세웠으니, 그 목소리가 구슬픈 가락으로 무어라 말한 바는 이 이야기의 제4부에서 들려질 것이다. 바로 이 대목에서 슬기롭고 사려 깊은 역사가 시데 아메테 베넹헬리가 제3부를 끝맺었던 것이다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXVIII. WHICH TREATS OF THE STRANGE AND DELIGHTFUL ADVENTURE THAT BEFELL THE CURATE AND THE BARBER IN THE SAME SIERRA","# CHAPTER XXVIII."]],[["Happy and fortunate were the times when that most daring knight Don Quixote of La Mancha was sent into the world; for by reason of his having formed a resolution so honourable as that of seeking to revive and restore to the world the long-lost and almost defunct order of knight-errantry, we now enjoy in this age of ours, so poor in light entertainment, not only the charm of his veracious history, but also of the tales and episodes contained in it which are, in a measure, no less pleasing, ingenious, and truthful, than the history itself; which, resuming its thread, carded, spun, and wound, relates that just as the curate was going to offer consolation to Cardenio, he was interrupted by a voice that fell upon his ear saying in plaintive tones:","그토록 대담한 기사 라만차의 돈키호테가 세상에 보내진 시대는 참으로 행복하고 복된 시대였으니, 오래도록 잊혀 거의 사멸하다시피 한 편력 기사도를 되살려 세상에 회복시키려는 그토록 명예로운 결심을 그가 품은 덕분에, 가벼운 즐길 거리가 이토록 빈약한 우리 시대에 우리는 그의 진실된 이야기가 주는 매혹뿐 아니라, 그 안에 담긴 이야기들과 삽화들이 주는 매혹까지도 누리게 되었으니, 그것들은 어느 모로 보아도 그 이야기 자체에 못지않게 즐겁고 기발하며 진실하다. 그 이야기는 이제 다시 그 실타래를 이어, 솔질하고 잣고 감아 가며, 신부가 막 카르데니오에게 위로의 말을 건네려던 바로 그 순간, 구슬픈 목소리가 그의 귀에 와 닿아 말을 가로막았다고 전한다."]],[["“O God!","\"오 하느님!"],["is it possible I have found a place that may serve as a secret grave for the weary load of this body that I support so unwillingly?","그토록 내키지 않게 떠메고 다니는 이 지친 몸뚱이의 짐을, 남몰래 묻을 무덤으로 삼을 만한 곳을 제가 정녕 찾아낸 것이옵니까?"],["If the solitude these mountains promise deceives me not, it is so; ah!","이 산들이 약속하는 고독이 저를 속이는 것이 아니라면, 정말 그러하옵니다."],["woe is me!","아아, 가엾은 신세여!"],["how much more grateful to my mind will be the society of these rocks and brakes that permit me to complain of my misfortune to Heaven, than that of any human being, for there is none on earth to look to for counsel in doubt, comfort in sorrow, or relief in distress!”","제 불행을 하늘에 하소연하도록 허락해 주는 이 바위와 덤불의 벗함이, 그 어떤 사람의 벗함보다 제 마음에 얼마나 더 고마운 것인지요. 이 땅 위에는 의심스러울 때 의논할 사람도, 슬플 때 위로해 줄 사람도, 괴로울 때 구해 줄 사람도 없으니 말이옵니다!\""]],[["All this was heard distinctly by the curate and those with him, and as it seemed to them to be uttered close by, as indeed it was, they got up to look for the speaker, and before they had gone twenty paces they discovered behind a rock, seated at the foot of an ash tree, a youth in the dress of a peasant, whose face they were unable at the moment to see as he was leaning forward, bathing his feet in the brook that flowed past.","이 모든 말은 신부와 그 일행에게 또렷이 들렸으며, 그들에게는 그 말이 바로 가까이에서 — 실제로도 그러했다 — 나온 듯하였기에, 말하는 이를 찾으려고 일어섰다. 그리고 스무 걸음도 가기 전에, 그들은 바위 뒤 물푸레나무 밑에 앉아 있는 한 젊은이를 발견했는데, 농부 차림을 한 그는 몸을 앞으로 숙여 곁으로 흐르는 시냇물에 발을 담그고 있던 터라 그 순간에는 얼굴을 볼 수가 없었다."],["They approached so silently that he did not perceive them, being fully occupied in bathing his feet, which were so fair that they looked like two pieces of shining crystal brought forth among the other stones of the brook.","그들은 어찌나 조용히 다가갔던지, 발 씻기에 온전히 몰두해 있던 그는 그들을 알아차리지 못했는데, 그 발이 어찌나 고운지 시냇물의 다른 돌들 사이에서 빛나는 두 개의 수정 조각이라도 솟아난 듯하였다."],["The whiteness and beauty of these feet struck them with surprise, for they did not seem to have been made to crush clods or to follow the plough and the oxen as their owner’s dress suggested; and so, finding they had not been noticed, the curate, who was in front, made a sign to the other two to conceal themselves behind some fragments of rock that lay there; which they did, observing closely what the youth was about.","이 발의 희고 아름다움에 그들은 놀라움을 금치 못했으니, 그것은 그 주인의 차림새가 일러 주듯 흙덩이를 부수거나 쟁기와 소를 따라다니도록 만들어진 발로는 도무지 보이지 않았기 때문이다. 그리하여 자기들이 눈에 띄지 않았음을 알고는, 앞에 서 있던 신부가 다른 두 사람에게 거기 놓인 바위 부스러기 뒤에 몸을 숨기라는 신호를 보냈으며, 그들은 그대로 하여 젊은이가 무엇을 하는지 가까이서 살폈다."],["He had on a loose double-skirted dark brown jacket bound tight to his body with a white cloth; he wore besides breeches and gaiters of brown cloth, and on his head a brown montera; and he had the gaiters turned up as far as the middle of the leg, which verily seemed to be of pure alabaster.","그는 흰 천으로 몸에 단단히 동여맨, 자락이 이중으로 늘어진 헐렁한 짙은 갈색 윗옷을 입고 있었으며, 그 밖에 갈색 천으로 만든 반바지와 각반을 차고, 머리에는 갈색 몬테라를 쓰고 있었다. 그리고 각반을 다리 한가운데까지 걷어 올렸는데, 그 다리는 정녕 순백의 설화석고로 빚은 듯하였다."]],[["As soon as he had done bathing his beautiful feet, he wiped them with a towel he took from under the montera, on taking off which he raised his face, and those who were watching him had an opportunity of seeing a beauty so exquisite that Cardenio said to the curate in a whisper:","* 몬테라: 사냥꾼이나 농부가 쓰던 천으로 만든 챙 없는 모자. 그 아름다운 발을 다 씻고 나자 젊은이는 몬테라 밑에서 꺼낸 수건으로 발을 닦았는데, 몬테라를 벗으면서 얼굴을 들어 올렸기에, 그를 지켜보던 이들은 더없이 빼어난 아름다움을 볼 기회를 얻었으니, 카르데니오가 신부에게 속삭이며 이렇게 말할 정도였다."]],[["“As this is not Luscinda, it is no human creature but a divine being.”","\"이 사람이 루스신다가 아니라면, 그것은 사람이 아니라 신적인 존재가 틀림없습니다.\""]],[["The youth then took off the montera, and shaking his head from side to side there broke loose and spread out a mass of hair that the beams of the sun might have envied; by this they knew that what had seemed a peasant was a lovely woman, nay the most beautiful the eyes of two of them had ever beheld, or even Cardenio’s if they had not seen and known Luscinda, for he afterwards declared that only the beauty of Luscinda could compare with this.","이윽고 젊은이가 몬테라를 벗고 고개를 좌우로 흔들자, 햇살마저 시샘했을 법한 한 무더기의 머리채가 풀려 흘러내려 퍼졌다. 이로써 그들은 농부로 여겼던 이가 사실은 어여쁜 여인임을, 아니 그들 가운데 두 사람이, 혹은 카르데니오마저도 — 루스신다를 보고 알지 못했더라면 — 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 아름다운 여인임을 알게 되었으니, 그는 훗날 오직 루스신다의 아름다움만이 이에 견줄 수 있다고 말했던 것이다."],["The long auburn tresses not only covered her shoulders, but such was their length and abundance, concealed her all round beneath their masses, so that except the feet nothing of her form was visible.","그 길고 적갈색인 머리채는 어깨를 덮었을 뿐 아니라, 그 길이와 풍성함이 어찌나 대단한지 온몸을 그 더미 밑에 죄다 감추어, 발을 제외하고는 그 몸의 어느 곳도 보이지 않았다. 이제 그가 두 손을 빗 삼아 쓰니, 발이 물속의 수정 조각 같았다면 손은 머리채 사이의 흩날리는 눈송이 같았으며, 이 모든 것이 세 구경꾼의 감탄뿐 아니라 그가 누구인지 알고자 하는 그들의 조바심까지 더하게 하였다."],["She now used her hands as a comb, and if her feet had seemed like bits of crystal in the water, her hands looked like pieces of driven snow among her locks; all which increased not only the admiration of the three beholders, but their anxiety to learn who she was.","그리하여 그들은 모습을 드러내기로 마음먹었고, 그들이 일어서며 부스럭거리는 소리에 그 어여쁜 처녀가 고개를 들어, 두 손으로 눈앞의 머리채를 가르고 누가 그 소리를 냈는지 보려 하였다. 그리고 그들을 알아차린 순간 벌떡 일어서서, 신발을 신거나 머리를 추스를 겨를도 없이, 곁에 두었던 옷가지인 듯한 보따리를 황급히 낚아채고는, 겁에 질려 놀란 채로 달아나려 했다."],["With this object they resolved to show themselves, and at the stir they made in getting upon their feet the fair damsel raised her head, and parting her hair from before her eyes with both hands, she looked to see who had made the noise, and the instant she perceived them she started to her feet, and without waiting to put on her shoes or gather up her hair, hastily snatched up a bundle as though of clothes that she had beside her, and, scared and alarmed, endeavoured to take flight; but before she had gone six paces she fell to the ground, her delicate feet being unable to bear the roughness of the stones; seeing which, the three hastened towards her, and the curate addressing her first said:","그러나 여섯 걸음도 가기 전에 그 연약한 발이 돌의 거칢을 견디지 못해 땅에 쓰러지고 말았다. 이를 보고 세 사람이 그에게로 달려갔으며, 신부가 먼저 그에게 말을 건넸다."]],[["“Stay, señora, whoever you may be, for those whom you see here only desire to be of service to you; you have no need to attempt a flight so heedless, for neither can your feet bear it, nor we allow it.”","\"멈추시오, 세뇨라. 그대가 누구이든, 여기 보이는 이들은 오직 그대에게 도움이 되기를 바랄 뿐이오. 그토록 무모하게 달아나려 할 까닭이 없으니, 그대의 발도 그것을 견디지 못할뿐더러, 우리도 그것을 허락하지 않을 것이오.\""]],[["Taken by surprise and bewildered, she made no reply to these words.","뜻밖의 일에 놀라 어쩔 줄 모르던 그녀는 이 말에 아무런 대꾸도 하지 못했다."],["They, however, came towards her, and the curate taking her hand went on to say:","그러나 그들이 그녀에게 다가오자, 신부가 그녀의 손을 잡고 말을 이었다."]],[["“What your dress would hide, señora, is made known to us by your hair; a clear proof that it can be no trifling cause that has disguised your beauty in a garb so unworthy of it, and sent it into solitudes like these where we have had the good fortune to find you, if not to relieve your distress, at least to offer you comfort; for no distress, so long as life lasts, can be so oppressive or reach such a height as to make the sufferer refuse to listen to comfort offered with good intention. And so, señora, or señor, or whatever you prefer to be, dismiss the fears that our appearance has caused you and make us acquainted with your good or evil fortunes, for from all of us together, or from each one of us, you will receive sympathy in your trouble.”","\"그대의 옷차림이 감추려 한 것을, 세뇨라, 그대의 머리채가 우리에게 알려 주었소. 이는 그대의 아름다움을 이토록 그에 어울리지 않는 옷으로 변장시켜, 우리가 다행히 그대를 발견한 이런 외진 곳으로 보낸 것이 결코 사소한 까닭이 아니었음을 분명히 보여 주는 증거요. 그대의 괴로움을 덜어 주지는 못할지언정 적어도 위로는 드리고자 함이니, 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 어떤 괴로움도 선한 뜻으로 건네는 위로에 귀 닫게 할 만큼 짓누르거나 드높을 수는 없는 법이오. 그러니 세뇨라든, 세뇨르든, 그대가 무엇으로 불리기를 원하든, 우리의 모습이 그대에게 일으킨 두려움을 떨쳐 버리고, 그대의 좋고 나쁜 운명을 우리에게 들려주시오. 우리 모두에게서든, 우리 한 사람 한 사람에게서든, 그대는 그 곤경에 동정을 받게 될 것이오.\""]],[["While the curate was speaking, the disguised damsel stood as if spell-bound, looking at them without opening her lips or uttering a word, just like a village rustic to whom something strange that he has never seen before has been suddenly shown; but on the curate addressing some further words to the same effect to her, sighing deeply she broke silence and said:","신부가 말하는 동안 변장한 처녀는 마치 무엇에 홀린 듯 입을 떼거나 한마디도 내뱉지 않은 채 그들을 바라보고 서 있었으니, 마치 한 번도 본 적 없는 낯선 무언가를 별안간 보게 된 시골뜨기와 같았다. 그러나 신부가 같은 취지의 말을 몇 마디 더 건네자, 깊이 한숨을 쉬며 침묵을 깨고 입을 열었다."]],[["“Since the solitude of these mountains has been unable to conceal me, and the escape of my dishevelled tresses will not allow my tongue to deal in falsehoods, it would be idle for me now to make any further pretence of what, if you were to believe me, you would believe more out of courtesy than for any other reason.","\"이 산들의 고독도 저를 숨겨 주지 못하였고, 풀어 헤쳐진 제 머리채가 빠져나와 제 혀가 거짓을 꾸미도록 내버려 두지 않으니, 이제 와서 제가 더 무슨 가식을 부린들 부질없는 일일 테고, 설령 여러분이 제 말을 믿어 주신다 해도 그것은 다른 어떤 까닭에서가 아니라 예의에서 믿어 주시는 것일 테지요."],["This being so, I say I thank you, sirs, for the offer you have made me, which places me under the obligation of complying with the request you have made of me; though I fear the account I shall give you of my misfortunes will excite in you as much concern as compassion, for you will be unable to suggest anything to remedy them or any consolation to alleviate them.","그러하오니 말씀드리거니와, 여러분, 제게 베풀어 주신 호의에 감사드리며, 그 호의는 여러분께서 제게 청하신 바를 따라야 할 의무를 제게 지우는 것이옵니다. 다만 제가 들려드릴 불행의 사연이 여러분께 동정만큼이나 근심을 불러일으키지 않을까 염려스럽사오니, 여러분께서는 그것을 고칠 만한 방도도, 덜어 줄 만한 위안도 떠올리지 못하실 터이기 때문이옵니다."],["However, that my honour may not be left a matter of doubt in your minds, now that you have discovered me to be a woman, and see that I am young, alone, and in this dress, things that taken together or separately would be enough to destroy any good name, I feel bound to tell what I would willingly keep secret if I could.”","그러나 이제 여러분께서 제가 여자임을 알아내시고, 제가 젊고, 홀로이며, 이런 차림을 하고 있음을 — 함께 묶든 따로 떼든 어떤 좋은 이름이라도 무너뜨리기에 충분한 이런 것들을 — 보신 마당이니, 제 명예가 여러분의 마음속에 의혹으로 남지 않도록, 할 수만 있다면 기꺼이 비밀로 묻어 두었을 일을 털어놓아야겠다고 느끼옵니다.\""]],[["All this she who was now seen to be a lovely woman delivered without any hesitation, with so much ease and in so sweet a voice that they were not less charmed by her intelligence than by her beauty, and as they again repeated their offers and entreaties to her to fulfil her promise, she without further pressing, first modestly covering her feet and gathering up her hair, seated herself on a stone with the three placed around her, and, after an effort to restrain some tears that came to her eyes, in a clear and steady voice began her story thus:","이제 어여쁜 여인임이 드러난 그녀는 이 모든 말을 조금의 망설임도 없이, 너무도 편안하게, 그토록 감미로운 목소리로 풀어놓았기에, 그들은 그녀의 아름다움 못지않게 그녀의 총명함에도 매혹되었다. 그리고 그들이 약속을 지켜 달라는 청과 간곡한 부탁을 다시금 거듭하자, 그녀는 더 채근할 것도 없이 먼저 다소곳이 발을 가리고 머리채를 추스른 다음, 세 사람을 둘레에 두고 돌 위에 앉아, 눈에 차오르는 눈물을 애써 억누른 뒤, 맑고 차분한 목소리로 그 사연을 이렇게 시작했다."]],[["“In this Andalusia there is a town from which a duke takes a title which makes him one of those that are called Grandees of Spain.","\"이곳 안달루시아에 한 고을이 있는데, 어느 공작이 그곳에서 작위를 따왔고, 그 작위로 하여 그는 스페인의 대공들이라 불리는 이들 가운데 하나가 되었습니다."],["This nobleman has two sons, the elder heir to his dignity and apparently to his good qualities; the younger heir to I know not what, unless it be the treachery of Vellido and the falsehood of Ganelon.","이 귀인에게는 아들이 둘 있는데, 맏이는 그의 지위와 보아하니 그의 좋은 성품까지 물려받을 후사이고, 둘째는 — 베이도의 배신과 가넬론의 거짓 말고 무엇을 물려받을지 저로서는 알 수 없는 — 후사이옵니다."],["My parents are this lord’s vassals, lowly in origin, but so wealthy that if birth had conferred as much on them as fortune, they would have had nothing left to desire, nor should I have had reason to fear trouble like that in which I find myself now; for it may be that my ill fortune came of theirs in not having been nobly born.","제 부모님은 이 영주의 신하로, 출신은 미천하나 어찌나 부유한지, 출생이 운명만큼이나 그분들께 많은 것을 주었더라면 더 바랄 것이 없었을 것이고, 저 또한 지금 제가 처한 이런 곤경을 두려워할 까닭이 없었을 것이옵니다. 어쩌면 저의 불운은 그분들이 고귀하게 태어나지 못한 데서 온 것일지도 모르니까요."],["It is true they are not so low that they have any reason to be ashamed of their condition, but neither are they so high as to remove from my mind the impression that my mishap comes of their humble birth.","사실 그분들은 자기 처지를 부끄러워할 만큼 그렇게 낮지도 않으나, 그렇다고 저의 불행이 그분들의 미천한 출생에서 온다는 생각을 제 마음에서 지워 줄 만큼 높지도 않으옵니다. 한마디로 그분들은 농부이고, 꾸밈없고 수수한 사람들이며, 추잡한 피가 한 방울도 섞이지 않은, 흔히 말하듯 오래되고 녹슨 기독교인들이지만, 너무도 부유한지라 그 재산과 후한 살림살이로 차츰 태생부터 양반인 사람으로, 심지어 지위에서도 양반으로 여겨지기에 이르렀습니다."],["They are, in short, peasants, plain homely people, without any taint of disreputable blood, and, as the saying is, old rusty Christians, but so rich that by their wealth and free-handed way of life they are coming by degrees to be considered gentlefolk by birth, and even by position; though the wealth and nobility they thought most of was having me for their daughter; and as they have no other child to make their heir, and are affectionate parents, I was one of the most indulged daughters that ever parents indulged.","다만 그분들이 가장 자랑스럽게 여긴 재산이자 고귀함은 저를 딸로 둔 것이었으니, 후사로 삼을 다른 자식이 없는 데다 정 많은 부모이신 까닭에, 저는 일찍이 어떤 부모가 응석을 받아 준 딸들보다도 더 응석받이로 자란 딸 가운데 하나였습니다. * 베이도와 가넬론: 베이도 돌포스는 스페인 전설에서 산초 2세 왕을 배신해 죽인 인물이며, 가넬론은 「롤랑의 노래」에 나오는 배신자."]],[["“I was the mirror in which they beheld themselves, the staff of their old age, and the object in which, with submission to Heaven, all their wishes centred, and mine were in accordance with theirs, for I knew their worth; and as I was mistress of their hearts, so was I also of their possessions.","\"저는 그분들이 자신을 비추어 보던 거울이요, 노년의 지팡이요, 하늘의 뜻을 따르는 가운데 그분들의 모든 바람이 모여 있던 대상이었으며, 저의 바람 또한 그분들의 그것과 한결같았으니, 저는 그분들의 가치를 알았기 때문입니다. 그리하여 제가 그분들의 마음의 주인이었던 것처럼, 저는 또한 그분들 재산의 주인이기도 하였습니다."],["Through me they engaged or dismissed their servants; through my hands passed the accounts and returns of what was sown and reaped; the oil-mills, the wine-presses, the count of the flocks and herds, the beehives, all in short that a rich farmer like my father has or can have, I had under my care, and I acted as steward and mistress with an assiduity on my part and satisfaction on theirs that I cannot well describe to you.","제 손으로 하인들을 들이고 내보냈으며, 무엇을 뿌리고 거두었는지에 대한 셈과 수입이 제 손을 거쳤고, 기름틀이며 포도주 짜는 기계며 양 떼와 가축 떼의 수효며 벌통이며, 한마디로 제 아버지 같은 부유한 농부가 가지거나 가질 수 있는 모든 것이 제 보살핌 아래 있었으니, 저는 한쪽에서는 부지런함으로, 다른 쪽에서는 그분들의 흡족함으로, 제가 여러분께 잘 그려 드릴 수 없을 만큼 살림 맡은 안주인 노릇을 하였습니다."],["The leisure hours left to me after I had given the requisite orders to the head-shepherds, overseers, and other labourers, I passed in such employments as are not only allowable but necessary for young girls, those that the needle, embroidery cushion, and spinning wheel usually afford, and if to refresh my mind I quitted them for a while, I found recreation in reading some devotional book or playing the harp, for experience taught me that music soothes the troubled mind and relieves weariness of spirit.","목자장이며 감독이며 다른 일꾼들에게 필요한 지시를 내린 뒤에 제게 남은 한가한 시간은, 어린 처녀들에게 허락될 뿐 아니라 필요하기도 한 그런 일들, 곧 바늘과 수틀과 물레가 으레 제공하는 일들로 보냈으며, 마음을 새롭게 하고자 그것들을 잠시 떠날 때는, 신앙서 한 권을 읽거나 하프를 타며 즐거움을 찾았으니, 음악이 어지러운 마음을 달래 주고 지친 정신을 풀어 준다는 것을 경험으로 배웠기 때문입니다."],["Such was the life I led in my parents’ house and if I have depicted it thus minutely, it is not out of ostentation, or to let you know that I am rich, but that you may see how, without any fault of mine, I have fallen from the happy condition I have described, to the misery I am in at present.","이것이 제가 부모님 집에서 보낸 삶이었으며, 제가 이렇듯 시시콜콜 그려 보였다면, 그것은 자랑하려거나 제가 부유하다는 것을 알리려는 것이 아니라, 제가 아무 잘못도 없이 어떻게 제가 말씀드린 행복한 처지에서 지금 처한 이 비참함으로 떨어졌는지를 여러분께서 아시도록 하려는 것이옵니다."],["The truth is, that while I was leading this busy life, in a retirement that might compare with that of a monastery, and unseen as I thought by any except the servants of the house (for when I went to Mass it was so early in the morning, and I was so closely attended by my mother and the women of the household, and so thickly veiled and so shy, that my eyes scarcely saw more ground than I trod on), in spite of all this, the eyes of love, or idleness, more properly speaking, that the lynx’s cannot rival, discovered me, with the help of the assiduity of Don Fernando; for that is the name of the younger son of the duke I told of.”","사실인즉, 이렇듯 분주한 삶을 수도원에 견줄 만한 은둔 속에서 살아가며, 집안 하인들 말고는 — 미사에 갈 때는 아침이 아주 일렀고, 어머니와 집안 여인들이 어찌나 가까이서 저를 따라붙고, 또 제가 어찌나 두껍게 너울을 쓰고 수줍어했던지, 제 두 눈은 제가 밟는 땅보다 더 많은 땅을 거의 보지 못할 정도였기에 — 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않는다고 여겼건만, 이 모든 것에도 불구하고, 살쾡이의 눈도 따르지 못할 사랑의 눈이, 아니 더 정확히 말하면 한가함의 눈이, 돈 페르난도의 끈질김의 도움을 받아 저를 찾아내고 말았으니, 페르난도란 바로 제가 말씀드린 그 공작의 둘째 아들의 이름이옵니다.\""]],[["The moment the speaker mentioned the name of Don Fernando, Cardenio changed colour and broke into a sweat, with such signs of emotion that the curate and the barber, who observed it, feared that one of the mad fits which they heard attacked him sometimes was coming upon him; but Cardenio showed no further agitation and remained quiet, regarding the peasant girl with fixed attention, for he began to suspect who she was.","말하는 이가 돈 페르난도의 이름을 입에 올리는 순간, 카르데니오는 낯빛이 바뀌고 식은땀을 흘리며 어찌나 동요하는 기색을 보였던지, 이를 지켜보던 신부와 이발사는 그가 이따금 발작한다고 들었던 광기의 발작 중 하나가 그에게 닥쳐오는 것이 아닐까 두려워했다. 그러나 카르데니오는 더는 격앙되는 기색 없이 조용히 있으면서, 그 농부 처녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았으니, 그녀가 누구인지 짐작하기 시작했던 것이다."],["She, however, without noticing the excitement of Cardenio, continuing her story, went on to say:","그러나 그녀는 카르데니오의 흥분을 알아차리지 못한 채 이야기를 이어 나갔다."]],[["“And they had hardly discovered me, when, as he owned afterwards, he was smitten with a violent love for me, as the manner in which it displayed itself plainly showed.","\"그분들이 저를 발견하자마자, 그가 훗날 스스로 인정했듯이, 그는 제게 격렬한 사랑에 사로잡혔으니, 그것이 드러난 방식이 그것을 분명히 보여 주었습니다. 그러나 제 슬픔의 긴 사설을 줄이기 위해, 돈 페르난도가 제게 자기 열정을 고백하려고 동원한 온갖 술수는 입 다물고 넘어가겠습니다."],["But to shorten the long recital of my woes, I will pass over in silence all the artifices employed by Don Fernando for declaring his passion for me.","그는 집안사람들을 죄다 매수했고, 제 부모님께 선물과 예물을 주고 또 권했으며, 날마다 우리 거리는 명절이나 잔칫날 같았고, 밤이면 음악 때문에 아무도 잠들 수 없었으며, 어떻게 들어왔는지 아무도 모를 연애편지들이 제 손에 들어왔는데 그 수가 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼이었고, 다정한 호소와 맹세로 가득하여, 그 안의 글자 수보다 약속과 서약이 더 많을 지경이었습니다. 이 모든 것이 저를 누그러뜨리기는커녕 도리어 그에 맞서 제 마음을 굳게 하였으니, 마치 그가 저의 철천지원수라도 되는 듯, 마치 저를 굴복시키려고 그가 한 모든 일이 그 반대의 의도로 행해지기라도 한 듯하였습니다."],["He bribed all the household, he gave and offered gifts and presents to my parents; every day was like a holiday or a merry-making in our street; by night no one could sleep for the music; the love letters that used to come to my hand, no one knew how, were innumerable, full of tender pleadings and pledges, containing more promises and oaths than there were letters in them; all which not only did not soften me, but hardened my heart against him, as if he had been my mortal enemy, and as if everything he did to make me yield were done with the opposite intention.","돈 페르난도의 고상한 태도가 제게 거슬렸다거나, 그의 끈질긴 구애가 진력났다는 것이 아닙니다. 그토록 지체 높은 신사에게 그처럼 추구받고 귀히 여겨지는 제 자신을 보는 것은 일종의 만족감을 주었고, 그의 편지에서 저에 대한 찬사를 보는 것이 싫지 않았으니까요 (우리 여자들이 아무리 못생겼다 해도, 자기가 아름답다는 말을 듣는 것은 언제나 기분 좋은 일인 듯하옵니다)."],["Not that the high-bred bearing of Don Fernando was disagreeable to me, or that I found his importunities wearisome; for it gave me a certain sort of satisfaction to find myself so sought and prized by a gentleman of such distinction, and I was not displeased at seeing my praises in his letters (for however ugly we women may be, it seems to me it always pleases us to hear ourselves called beautiful) but that my own sense of right was opposed to all this, as well as the repeated advice of my parents, who now very plainly perceived Don Fernando’s purpose, for he cared very little if all the world knew it.","다만 제 자신의 올바름에 대한 감각이 이 모든 것에 맞섰고, 또한 부모님의 거듭된 충고도 그러하였으니, 이제 부모님은 돈 페르난도의 속셈을 아주 분명히 알아차리셨던 것입니다. 그는 온 세상이 그것을 안다 해도 거의 개의치 않았으니까요."],["They told me they trusted and confided their honour and good name to my virtue and rectitude alone, and bade me consider the disparity between Don Fernando and myself, from which I might conclude that his intentions, whatever he might say to the contrary, had for their aim his own pleasure rather than my advantage; and if I were at all desirous of opposing an obstacle to his unreasonable suit, they were ready, they said, to marry me at once to anyone I preferred, either among the leading people of our own town, or of any of those in the neighbourhood; for with their wealth and my good name, a match might be looked for in any quarter.","부모님은 당신들의 명예와 좋은 이름을 오직 저의 정숙함과 올곧음에만 맡기고 의탁한다고 제게 말씀하셨으며, 돈 페르난도와 저 사이의 신분 차이를 헤아려 보라고 이르셨으니, 거기서 저는 그가 입으로는 무어라 반대로 말하든 그의 의도가 저의 이익보다는 제 자신의 쾌락을 노린 것임을 헤아릴 수 있었습니다. 그리고 제가 그의 분별없는 구애에 조금이라도 걸림돌을 놓고 싶다면, 부모님은 당장 저를 제가 원하는 누구에게든 — 우리 고을의 지체 높은 사람들이든, 이웃 어느 고을의 사람들이든 — 시집보낼 채비가 되어 있다고 하셨으니, 그분들의 재산과 저의 좋은 이름이면 어느 쪽에서든 혼처를 구할 수 있다는 것이었습니다."],["This offer, and their sound advice strengthened my resolution, and I never gave Don Fernando a word in reply that could hold out to him any hope of success, however remote.","이 제안과 그분들의 분별 있는 충고가 제 결심을 굳혔고, 저는 돈 페르난도에게 아무리 막연하게나마 성공의 희망을 줄 만한 어떤 말도 결코 답으로 건네지 않았습니다."]],[["“All this caution of mine, which he must have taken for coyness, had apparently the effect of increasing his wanton appetite--for that is the name I give to his passion for me; had it been what he declared it to be, you would not know of it now, because there would have been no occasion to tell you of it.","\"그가 새침함으로 여겼을 이 모든 저의 조심성은, 보아하니 그의 음탕한 욕심을 — 제게 품은 그의 열정을 저는 그렇게 부르겠습니다 — 부추기는 효과만 낸 듯하였으니, 만약 그것이 그가 공언한 그런 것이었다면, 여러분은 지금 그것을 알지 못하셨을 것입니다. 그것을 여러분께 말씀드릴 일이 아예 없었을 테니까요."],["At length he learned that my parents were contemplating marriage for me in order to put an end to his hopes of obtaining possession of me, or at least to secure additional protectors to watch over me, and this intelligence or suspicion made him act as you shall hear.","마침내 그는 제 부모님이 저를 차지하려는 그의 희망을 끝장내려고, 아니 적어도 저를 지켜 줄 보호자를 더 두려고 제 혼사를 궁리하고 있음을 알게 되었고, 이 소식인지 의심인지로 인해 그는 여러분께서 곧 들으실 그런 짓을 저지르고 말았습니다."],["One night, as I was in my chamber with no other companion than a damsel who waited on me, with the doors carefully locked lest my honour should be imperilled through any carelessness, I know not nor can conceive how it happened, but, with all this seclusion and these precautions, and in the solitude and silence of my retirement, I found him standing before me, a vision that so astounded me that it deprived my eyes of sight, and my tongue of speech.","어느 날 밤, 저는 제 방에서 저를 시중드는 하녀 말고는 다른 벗 없이, 행여 부주의로 제 명예가 위태로워질까 하여 문을 단단히 잠근 채로 있었는데, 어찌하여 그런 일이 일어났는지 저는 알 수도 없고 짐작할 수도 없으나, 이 모든 칩거와 이 모든 경계에도 불구하고, 제 은둔처의 고독과 고요 속에서, 저는 그가 제 앞에 서 있는 것을 발견했으니, 그 광경은 저를 어찌나 아연하게 했던지 제 두 눈에서는 시력을, 제 혀에서는 말을 앗아 가 버렸습니다."],["I had no power to utter a cry, nor, I think, did he give me time to utter one, as he immediately approached me, and taking me in his arms (for, overwhelmed as I was, I was powerless, I say, to help myself), he began to make such professions to me that I know not how falsehood could have had the power of dressing them up to seem so like truth; and the traitor contrived that his tears should vouch for his words, and his sighs for his sincerity.","저는 소리를 지를 힘도 없었고, 제 생각에 그도 제게 소리 지를 틈을 주지 않았으니, 그가 곧바로 제게 다가와 저를 두 팔에 안았기 때문입니다 (압도당한 저는, 다시 말씀드리거니와, 저 자신을 도울 힘이 없었으니까요). 그러고는 그는 제게 어찌나 고백을 늘어놓던지, 거짓이 어떻게 그것들을 진실처럼 보이도록 그토록 꾸며 낼 힘을 가졌는지 저로서는 알 수 없을 정도였으며, 그 배신자는 자기 눈물로 자기 말을, 자기 한숨으로 자기 진심을 증언하도록 꾀했습니다."]],[["“I, a poor young creature alone, ill versed among my people in cases such as this, began, I know not how, to think all these lying protestations true, though without being moved by his sighs and tears to anything more than pure compassion; and so, as the first feeling of bewilderment passed away, and I began in some degree to recover myself, I said to him with more courage than I thought I could have possessed, ‘If, as I am now in your arms, señor, I were in the claws of a fierce lion, and my deliverance could be procured by doing or saying anything to the prejudice of my honour, it would no more be in my power to do it or say it, than it would be possible that what was should not have been; so then, if you hold my body clasped in your arms, I hold my soul secured by virtuous intentions, very different from yours, as you will see if you attempt to carry them into effect by force. I am your vassal, but I am not your slave; your nobility neither has nor should have any right to dishonour or degrade my humble birth; and low-born peasant as I am, I have my self-respect as much as you, a lord and gentleman: with me your violence will be to no purpose, your wealth will have no weight, your words will have no power to deceive me, nor your sighs or tears to soften me: were I to see any of the things I speak of in him whom my parents gave me as a husband, his will should be mine, and mine should be bounded by his; and my honour being preserved even though my inclinations were not would willingly yield him what you, señor, would now obtain by force; and this I say lest you should suppose that any but my lawful husband shall ever win anything of me.’ ‘If that,’ said this disloyal gentleman, ‘be the only scruple you feel, fairest Dorothea’ (for that is the name of this unhappy being), ‘see here I give you my hand to be yours, and let Heaven, from which nothing is hid, and this image of Our Lady you have here, be witnesses of this pledge.’”","\"가엾고 외로운 어린 것이요, 이런 경우에 대해 제 사람들 가운데서 제대로 배운 바도 없는 저는, 어찌하여 그랬는지 이 모든 거짓 맹세를 참이라 여기기 시작했으나, 그의 한숨과 눈물에 움직여 순전한 동정심 이상의 무언가에 이른 것은 아니었습니다. 그리하여 처음의 당혹감이 가시고 제가 어느 정도 정신을 차리기 시작하자, 저는 제가 가졌으리라 생각한 것보다 더 큰 용기를 내어 그에게 말했습니다. '세뇨르, 제가 지금 당신의 두 팔에 안겨 있듯이 만약 사나운 사자의 발톱에 잡혀 있고, 제 명예를 해치는 무언가를 행하거나 말함으로써 거기서 벗어날 수 있다 해도, 이미 있었던 일이 없었던 일이 될 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로, 그것을 행하거나 말하는 것은 제 힘으로 도무지 할 수 없는 일일 것입니다. 그러니 당신이 제 몸을 두 팔에 안고 있다 한들, 저는 정숙한 뜻으로 제 영혼을 단단히 지키고 있으니, 그것은 당신의 뜻과는 아주 다른 것이라, 당신이 힘으로 그것을 이루려 든다면 알게 되실 것입니다. 저는 당신의 신하이나 당신의 종은 아닙니다. 당신의 고귀함은 제 미천한 출생을 욕보이거나 깎아내릴 권리를 가지고 있지도, 가져서도 안 됩니다. 그리고 비록 미천하게 태어난 농부일지언정 저는 영주이자 신사인 당신 못지않게 제 자존심을 지니고 있습니다. 제게는 당신의 폭력도 소용없을 것이며, 당신의 재산도 무게를 갖지 못할 것이고, 당신의 말도 저를 속일 힘이 없을 것이며, 당신의 한숨이나 눈물도 저를 누그러뜨리지 못할 것입니다. 제 부모님이 제게 남편으로 주신 사람에게서 제가 말한 그런 것들 가운데 무엇이라도 보게 된다면, 그의 뜻이 곧 제 뜻이 될 것이고, 제 뜻은 그의 뜻 안에 매일 것입니다. 그리고 제 명예가 지켜진다면, 비록 제 마음이 내키지 않더라도, 당신이 지금 세뇨르 힘으로 얻으려 하는 것을 그에게는 기꺼이 내어 줄 것입니다. 이것을 말씀드리는 까닭은, 제 합법적인 남편 말고는 그 누구도 결코 제게서 무언가를 얻지 못하리라는 것을 당신이 헤아리시도록 하려는 것입니다.' 이 부정한 신사가 말했습니다. '그것이 그대가 느끼는 유일한 거리낌이라면, 더없이 아리따운 도로테아여' (이 불행한 사람의 이름이 바로 그것이옵니다), '보시오, 여기 내가 그대의 것이 되도록 손을 내미니, 아무것도 숨길 수 없는 하늘과, 여기 그대가 모셔 둔 성모님의 이 상이 이 맹세의 증인이 되게 하시오.'\""]],[["When Cardenio heard her say she was called Dorothea, he showed fresh agitation and felt convinced of the truth of his former suspicion, but he was unwilling to interrupt the story, and wished to hear the end of what he already all but knew, so he merely said:","카르데니오는 그녀가 자기 이름이 도로테아라고 말하는 것을 듣자 새로이 동요하는 기색을 보이며 앞서 품었던 의심이 사실임을 확신했으나, 이야기를 가로막고 싶지 않았고, 이미 거의 다 알고 있는 일의 결말을 듣고 싶었기에, 그저 이렇게만 말했다."]],[["“What!","\"뭐라고요!"],["is Dorothea your name, señora?","그대의 이름이 도로테아입니까, 세뇨라?"],["I have heard of another of the same name who can perhaps match your misfortunes.","같은 이름을 가진 또 다른 사람에 대해 들은 적이 있는데, 어쩌면 그녀가 그대의 불행에 견줄 만할지도 모릅니다."],["But proceed; by-and-by I may tell you something that will astonish you as much as it will excite your compassion.”","그러나 계속하시지요. 머지않아 그대의 동정심을 자아내는 만큼이나 그대를 놀라게 할 무언가를 제가 들려드릴 수도 있을 테니까요.\""]],[["Dorothea was struck by Cardenio’s words as well as by his strange and miserable attire, and begged him if he knew anything concerning her to tell it to her at once, for if fortune had left her any blessing it was courage to bear whatever calamity might fall upon her, as she felt sure that none could reach her capable of increasing in any degree what she endured already.","도로테아는 카르데니오의 말뿐 아니라 그의 기이하고 비참한 차림새에도 놀라, 만약 그가 자기에 관해 무언가 아는 것이 있다면 당장 들려 달라고 청했으니, 만약 운명이 자기에게 어떤 축복이라도 남겨 두었다면 그것은 자기에게 닥칠 어떤 재앙이든 견뎌 낼 용기였고, 자기가 이미 겪고 있는 것을 조금이라도 더할 수 있는 것은 아무것도 자기에게 닿을 수 없으리라 확신했기 때문이다."]],[["“I would not let the occasion pass, señora,”","\"세뇨라,\" 카르데니오가 답했다."],["replied Cardenio, “of telling you what I think, if what I suspect were the truth, but so far there has been no opportunity, nor is it of any importance to you to know it.”","\"제가 의심하는 것이 사실이라면, 제 생각을 그대에게 말씀드릴 이 기회를 놓치지 않을 것입니다만, 아직까지는 그럴 기회도 없었고, 또 그대가 그것을 아는 것이 그대에게 무슨 중요한 일도 아닙니다.\""]],[["“Be it as it may,” replied Dorothea, “what happened in my story was that Don Fernando, taking an image that stood in the chamber, placed it as a witness of our betrothal, and with the most binding words and extravagant oaths gave me his promise to become my husband; though before he had made an end of pledging himself I bade him consider well what he was doing, and think of the anger his father would feel at seeing him married to a peasant girl and one of his vassals; I told him not to let my beauty, such as it was, blind him, for that was not enough to furnish an excuse for his transgression; and if in the love he bore me he wished to do me any kindness, it would be to leave my lot to follow its course at the level my condition required; for marriages so unequal never brought happiness, nor did they continue long to afford the enjoyment they began with.","\"그것이야 어찌 됐든,\" 도로테아가 답했다. \"제 이야기에서 벌어진 일인즉, 돈 페르난도가 방에 놓여 있던 성상 하나를 집어 우리 약혼의 증인으로 세우고, 더없이 구속력 있는 말과 터무니없는 맹세로 제 남편이 되겠노라 약속했다는 것입니다. 다만 그가 맹세를 다 마치기 전에 저는 그에게, 지금 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 잘 헤아려 보고, 농부 처녀이자 자기 신하인 사람과 혼인한 것을 보고 그 아버지가 느낄 노여움을 생각해 보라고 일렀습니다. 저는 그에게, 보잘것없으나마 제 아름다움에 눈멀지 말라고, 그것이 그의 잘못을 변명할 구실이 되기에는 충분치 않다고 말했으며, 만약 그가 제게 품은 사랑으로 제게 무슨 친절이라도 베풀고자 한다면, 그것은 제 분수가 요구하는 처지에 맞게 제 운명이 흘러가도록 내버려 두는 것일 거라고 했습니다. 그토록 어울리지 않는 혼인은 결코 행복을 가져온 적이 없고, 처음에 누렸던 즐거움을 오래도록 이어 주지도 못하니까요."]],[["“All this that I have now repeated I said to him, and much more which I cannot recollect; but it had no effect in inducing him to forego his purpose; he who has no intention of paying does not trouble himself about difficulties when he is striking the bargain.","\"제가 지금 되풀이한 이 모든 것을 저는 그에게 말했고, 지금은 기억나지 않는 더 많은 말도 했습니다만, 그가 자기 뜻을 단념하도록 이끄는 데에는 아무 효과가 없었으니, 갚을 마음이 없는 자는 흥정을 할 때 어려움 따위로 자신을 괴롭히지 않는 법입니다."],["At the same time I argued the matter briefly in my own mind, saying to myself, ‘I shall not be the first who has risen through marriage from a lowly to a lofty station, nor will Don Fernando be the first whom beauty or, as is more likely, a blind attachment, has led to mate himself below his rank.","동시에 저는 제 마음속에서 그 일을 짤막하게 따져 보며 혼잣말을 했습니다. '혼인으로 미천한 처지에서 높은 지위로 올라간 것이 내가 처음은 아닐 것이고, 아름다움이, 아니 더 그럴듯하게는 눈먼 애착이, 자기 신분 아래로 짝지어지게 만든 것도 돈 페르난도가 처음은 아닐 것이다."],["Then, since I am introducing no new usage or practice, I may as well avail myself of the honour that chance offers me, for even though his inclination for me should not outlast the attainment of his wishes, I shall be, after all, his wife before God.","그러니 내가 새로운 관습이나 관행을 끌어들이는 것도 아닌 이상, 우연이 내게 베푼 이 명예를 누려도 좋지 않겠는가. 비록 나에 대한 그의 마음이 자기 소망을 이룬 뒤까지 이어지지 않는다 해도, 나는 어쨌든 하느님 앞에서 그의 아내일 것이다."],["And if I strive to repel him by scorn, I can see that, fair means failing, he is in a mood to use force, and I shall be left dishonoured and without any means of proving my innocence to those who cannot know how innocently I have come to be in this position; for what arguments would persuade my parents that this gentleman entered my chamber without my consent?’","그리고 내가 멸시로 그를 물리치려 애쓴다면, 좋은 방법이 통하지 않을 때 그가 힘을 쓸 작정인 것이 내 눈에 보이니, 나는 명예를 잃고도, 내가 얼마나 죄 없이 이런 처지에 놓이게 되었는지 알 길 없는 이들에게 내 결백을 증명할 아무런 방도도 없이 남겨질 것이다. 이 신사가 내 동의 없이 내 방에 들어왔다고 어떤 논리가 내 부모님을 설득할 수 있겠는가?'"]],[["“All these questions and answers passed through my mind in a moment; but the oaths of Don Fernando, the witnesses he appealed to, the tears he shed, and lastly the charms of his person and his high-bred grace, which, accompanied by such signs of genuine love, might well have conquered a heart even more free and coy than mine--these were the things that more than all began to influence me and lead me unawares to my ruin.","\"이 모든 물음과 답이 한순간에 제 마음을 스쳐 지나갔습니다. 그러나 돈 페르난도의 맹세, 그가 호소한 증인들, 그가 흘린 눈물, 그리고 마지막으로 그 사람됨의 매력과 고상한 기품이 — 이러한 참된 사랑의 표시들을 동반했으니 제 마음보다 더 자유롭고 더 새침한 마음마저 능히 정복했을 법한 것들이 — 그 무엇보다도 저를 움직이기 시작하여, 저도 모르게 저를 파멸로 이끌어 갔습니다."],["I called my waiting-maid to me, that there might be a witness on earth besides those in Heaven, and again Don Fernando renewed and repeated his oaths, invoked as witnesses fresh saints in addition to the former ones, called down upon himself a thousand curses hereafter should he fail to keep his promise, shed more tears, redoubled his sighs and pressed me closer in his arms, from which he had never allowed me to escape; and so I was left by my maid, and ceased to be one, and he became a traitor and a perjured man.","저는 하늘에 있는 증인들 말고도 이 땅에 증인이 있도록 시중드는 하녀를 제게 불렀고, 돈 페르난도는 다시금 자기 맹세를 새로이 거듭하며, 앞서 부른 성인들에 더하여 새로운 성인들을 증인으로 불러내고, 만약 자기 약속을 지키지 못한다면 앞으로 자기 머리 위에 천 가지 저주가 내리기를 빌었으며, 더 많은 눈물을 흘리고, 한숨을 갑절로 늘리고, 한 번도 빠져나가도록 허락한 적 없는 그 두 팔로 저를 더 바싹 끌어안았습니다. 그리하여 저는 하녀에게 버려졌고, 처녀이기를 그쳤으며, 그는 배신자요 위증한 자가 되었습니다."]],[["“The day which followed the night of my misfortune did not come so quickly, I imagine, as Don Fernando wished, for when desire has attained its object, the greatest pleasure is to fly from the scene of pleasure.","\"제 불행의 그 밤에 뒤이은 날은, 제 짐작에, 돈 페르난도가 바랐던 만큼 빨리 오지는 않았으니, 욕망이 그 대상을 얻고 나면 가장 큰 즐거움은 그 쾌락의 자리에서 달아나는 것이기 때문입니다. 이렇게 말씀드리는 까닭은, 돈 페르난도가 저를 떠나려고 무던히도 서둘렀고, 사실은 그를 들여보냈던 장본인인 제 하녀의 솜씨로 동트기 전에 거리로 빠져나갔기 때문입니다."],["I say so because Don Fernando made all haste to leave me, and by the adroitness of my maid, who was indeed the one who had admitted him, gained the street before daybreak; but on taking leave of me he told me, though not with as much earnestness and fervour as when he came, that I might rest assured of his faith and of the sanctity and sincerity of his oaths; and to confirm his words he drew a rich ring off his finger and placed it upon mine.","다만 제게 작별을 고할 때 그는, 올 때만큼의 간곡함과 열정은 아니었으나, 자기 신의와, 자기 맹세의 신성함과 진실함을 제가 믿어도 좋다고 말했으며, 자기 말을 다지려고 손가락에서 값진 반지 하나를 빼내어 제 손가락에 끼워 주었습니다. 그러고는 그가 떠나고, 저는 — 슬펐는지 기뻤는지 저도 모를 채로 — 남겨졌습니다."],["He then took his departure and I was left, I know not whether sorrowful or happy; all I can say is, I was left agitated and troubled in mind and almost bewildered by what had taken place, and I had not the spirit, or else it did not occur to me, to chide my maid for the treachery she had been guilty of in concealing Don Fernando in my chamber; for as yet I was unable to make up my mind whether what had befallen me was for good or evil.","제가 말할 수 있는 것은, 저는 마음이 동요하고 어지러운 채, 벌어진 일에 거의 얼이 빠진 채로 남겨졌다는 것이며, 돈 페르난도를 제 방에 숨겨 준 하녀의 배신을 꾸짖을 만한 기력도, 아니 그럴 생각조차 떠오르지 않았으니, 아직 제게 닥친 일이 좋은 일인지 나쁜 일인지 마음을 정할 수 없었기 때문입니다. 헤어질 때 저는 돈 페르난도에게, 이제 제가 그의 것이 되었으니 그가 이 일을 알리고자 할 때까지 다른 밤에도 같은 방식으로 저를 볼 수 있다고 말했습니다."],["I told Don Fernando at parting, that as I was now his, he might see me on other nights in the same way, until it should be his pleasure to let the matter become known; but, except the following night, he came no more, nor for more than a month could I catch a glimpse of him in the street or in church, while I wearied myself with watching for one; although I knew he was in the town, and almost every day went out hunting, a pastime he was very fond of.","그러나 그는 그다음 날 밤 말고는 다시 오지 않았고, 한 달이 넘도록 저는 거리에서도 성당에서도 그를 한 번도 얼핏 볼 수 없었으니, 그러느라 저는 그를 지켜보며 지쳐 갔습니다. 그가 고을에 있다는 것을, 그리고 그가 무척 좋아하는 소일거리인 사냥을 거의 날마다 나간다는 것을 알고 있었는데도 말입니다."],["I remember well how sad and dreary those days and hours were to me; I remember well how I began to doubt as they went by, and even to lose confidence in the faith of Don Fernando; and I remember, too, how my maid heard those words in reproof of her audacity that she had not heard before, and how I was forced to put a constraint on my tears and on the expression of my countenance, not to give my parents cause to ask me why I was so melancholy, and drive me to invent falsehoods in reply.","그 나날과 시간들이 제게 얼마나 슬프고 음울했는지 저는 잘 기억합니다. 시간이 흐르면서 제가 어떻게 의심하기 시작했는지, 심지어 돈 페르난도의 신의에 대한 믿음을 잃기 시작했는지도 잘 기억합니다."],["But all this was suddenly brought to an end, for the time came when all such considerations were disregarded, and there was no further question of honour, when my patience gave way and the secret of my heart became known abroad.","그리고 제 하녀가 전에는 들은 적 없는, 자기 무모함을 꾸짖는 그런 말들을 어떻게 듣게 되었는지, 또 제가 어찌하여 어머니가 왜 그토록 우울하냐고 물으실 빌미를 드리지 않으려고, 그리하여 그 답으로 거짓을 지어내게 되지 않으려고, 제 눈물과 얼굴 표정에 어떻게 억지로 자제를 가해야 했는지도 잘 기억합니다. 그러나 이 모든 것이 별안간 끝나 버렸으니, 이런 온갖 고려가 무시되고 명예 따위는 더 이상 문제 되지 않으며, 제 인내가 무너지고 제 마음의 비밀이 세상에 알려지게 된 그때가 왔기 때문입니다."],["The reason was, that a few days later it was reported in the town that Don Fernando had been married in a neighbouring city to a maiden of rare beauty, the daughter of parents of distinguished position, though not so rich that her portion would entitle her to look for so brilliant a match; it was said, too, that her name was Luscinda, and that at the betrothal some strange things had happened.”","그 까닭인즉, 며칠 뒤 돈 페르난도가 이웃 도시에서 보기 드물게 아름다운 어느 처녀와 혼인했다는 소문이 고을에 돌았다는 것이니, 그녀는 지체 높은 부모의 딸이었으나, 그 지참금으로 그토록 빛나는 혼처를 바랄 만큼 부유하지는 않았다고 했습니다. 또한 그녀의 이름이 루스신다라고 했고, 약혼식에서 몇 가지 기이한 일이 일어났다고도 했습니다.\""]],[["Cardenio heard the name of Luscinda, but he only shrugged his shoulders, bit his lips, bent his brows, and before long two streams of tears escaped from his eyes.","카르데니오는 루스신다의 이름을 들었으나, 그저 어깨를 으쓱하고 입술을 깨물며 눈썹을 찌푸렸을 뿐인데, 이내 그의 두 눈에서 두 줄기 눈물이 흘러내렸다."],["Dorothea, however, did not interrupt her story, but went on in these words:","그러나 도로테아는 이야기를 멈추지 않고 이런 말로 계속해 나갔다."]],[["“This sad intelligence reached my ears, and, instead of being struck with a chill, with such wrath and fury did my heart burn that I scarcely restrained myself from rushing out into the streets, crying aloud and proclaiming openly the perfidy and treachery of which I was the victim; but this transport of rage was for the time checked by a resolution I formed, to be carried out the same night, and that was to assume this dress, which I got from a servant of my father’s, one of the zagals, as they are called in farmhouses, to whom I confided the whole of my misfortune, and whom I entreated to accompany me to the city where I heard my enemy was.","\"이 슬픈 소식이 제 귀에 닿았으나, 저는 오싹해지기는커녕 제 가슴이 어찌나 분노와 격분으로 타올랐던지, 거리로 뛰쳐나가 큰 소리로 외치며 제가 그 희생자가 된 그 배반과 배신을 공공연히 알리고 싶은 것을 가까스로 억눌렀습니다. 그러나 이 분노의 격발은 같은 날 밤 실행에 옮길 결심을 하면서 일단 가라앉았으니, 그것은 바로 이 옷차림을 하는 것이었습니다."],["He, though he remonstrated with me for my boldness, and condemned my resolution, when he saw me bent upon my purpose, offered to bear me company, as he said, to the end of the world.","이 옷은 제 아버지의 하인 하나, 농가에서 부르는 말로 사갈 가운데 하나에게서 얻은 것인데, 저는 그에게 제 불행을 죄다 털어놓고, 제 원수가 있다고 들은 도시까지 동행해 달라고 간청했습니다. 그는 제 대담함에 항의하고 제 결심을 나무랐으나, 제가 뜻을 굳힌 것을 보고는, 자기 말로 세상 끝까지 동행하겠다고 했습니다."],["I at once packed up in a linen pillow-case a woman’s dress, and some jewels and money to provide for emergencies, and in the silence of the night, without letting my treacherous maid know, I sallied forth from the house, accompanied by my servant and abundant anxieties, and on foot set out for the city, but borne as it were on wings by my eagerness to reach it, if not to prevent what I presumed to be already done, at least to call upon Don Fernando to tell me with what conscience he had done it.","저는 곧장 베갯잇 하나에 여자 옷 한 벌과, 비상시에 대비할 약간의 패물과 돈을 챙겨 넣고, 밤의 고요 속에서 제 배신자 하녀에게는 알리지 않은 채, 제 하인과 넘치는 근심을 동무 삼아 집을 빠져나와, 걸어서 그 도시로 출발했습니다. 다만 이미 끝났으리라 짐작되는 일을 막지는 못할지언정, 적어도 돈 페르난도에게 무슨 양심으로 그런 짓을 했는지 따져 묻고자 하는 조급함에, 마치 날개라도 단 듯이 실려 갔습니다."],["I reached my destination in two days and a half, and on entering the city inquired for the house of Luscinda’s parents.","저는 이틀 반 만에 목적지에 닿았고, 도시에 들어서서 루스신다 부모의 집을 물었습니다."],["The first person I asked gave me more in reply than I sought to know; he showed me the house, and told me all that had occurred at the betrothal of the daughter of the family, an affair of such notoriety in the city that it was the talk of every knot of idlers in the street.","제가 처음 물은 사람은 제가 알고자 한 것보다 더 많은 것을 답으로 주었으니, 그는 제게 그 집을 가리켜 보였고, 그 집 딸의 약혼식에서 일어난 모든 일을 들려주었습니다. 그것은 도시에서 어찌나 떠들썩한 일이었던지 거리의 한가한 무리마다 입에 올리는 이야깃거리였습니다."],["He said that on the night of Don Fernando’s betrothal with Luscinda, as soon as she had consented to be his bride by saying ‘Yes,’ she was taken with a sudden fainting fit, and that on the bridegroom approaching to unlace the bosom of her dress to give her air, he found a paper in her own handwriting, in which she said and declared that she could not be Don Fernando’s bride, because she was already Cardenio’s, who, according to the man’s account, was a gentleman of distinction of the same city; and that if she had accepted Don Fernando, it was only in obedience to her parents.","그가 말하기를, 돈 페르난도와 루스신다의 약혼식 날 밤, 그녀가 '예'라고 말하여 그의 신부가 되기를 승낙하자마자 별안간 기절하는 발작을 일으켰고, 신랑이 다가가 그녀에게 바람을 통하게 하려고 옷의 가슴 부분을 풀자, 그녀가 자기 손으로 쓴 종이 한 장을 발견했다는 것입니다. 그 종이에서 그녀는, 자기는 이미 카르데니오의 것이므로 돈 페르난도의 신부가 될 수 없다고 말하고 또 선언했는데, 그 사람의 말에 따르면 카르데니오는 같은 도시의 지체 높은 신사였다고 합니다."],["In short, he said, the words of the paper made it clear she meant to kill herself on the completion of the betrothal, and gave her reasons for putting an end to herself all which was confirmed, it was said, by a dagger they found somewhere in her clothes.","그리고 자기가 돈 페르난도를 받아들인 것은 오직 부모에게 순종해서였다는 것입니다. 한마디로, 그가 말하기를, 그 종이의 글은 그녀가 약혼식이 끝나는 대로 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 했음을 분명히 보여 주었고, 자결하려는 까닭까지 밝혀 두었는데, 그 모든 것은 그녀의 옷 어딘가에서 발견된 단검으로 확증되었다고 했습니다."],["On seeing this, Don Fernando, persuaded that Luscinda had befooled, slighted, and trifled with him, assailed her before she had recovered from her swoon, and tried to stab her with the dagger that had been found, and would have succeeded had not her parents and those who were present prevented him.","이를 본 돈 페르난도는, 루스신다가 자기를 우롱하고 멸시하며 가지고 놀았다고 여겨, 그녀가 실신에서 깨어나기도 전에 달려들어 발견된 그 단검으로 그녀를 찌르려 했고, 그녀의 부모와 그 자리에 있던 이들이 막지 않았더라면 성공했으리라는 것입니다."],["It was said, moreover, that Don Fernando went away at once, and that Luscinda did not recover from her prostration until the next day, when she told her parents how she was really the bride of that Cardenio I have mentioned.","더욱이 돈 페르난도는 곧바로 떠나 버렸고, 루스신다는 이튿날에야 쓰러진 데서 회복하여, 자기가 사실은 제가 말씀드린 그 카르데니오의 신부라고 부모에게 말했다고 했습니다."],["I learned besides that Cardenio, according to report, had been present at the betrothal; and that upon seeing her betrothed contrary to his expectation, he had quitted the city in despair, leaving behind him a letter declaring the wrong Luscinda had done him, and his intention of going where no one should ever see him again.","그 밖에 제가 알게 된 것은, 소문에 따르면 카르데니오가 약혼식 자리에 있었고, 자기 기대와는 달리 그녀가 약혼하는 것을 보고는 절망에 빠져 도시를 떠나 버렸으며, 루스신다가 자기에게 저지른 잘못과, 다시는 누구도 자기를 보지 못할 곳으로 가겠다는 자기 뜻을 밝힌 편지 한 통을 남겨 두었다는 것입니다."],["All this was a matter of notoriety in the city, and everyone spoke of it; especially when it became known that Luscinda was missing from her father’s house and from the city, for she was not to be found anywhere, to the distraction of her parents, who knew not what steps to take to recover her.","이 모든 것이 도시에서 떠들썩한 일이었고, 누구나 그것을 입에 올렸는데, 특히 루스신다가 아버지의 집에서도 도시에서도 사라졌다는 것이 알려지면서 더욱 그러했으니, 그녀가 어디에서도 발견되지 않아 그 부모가 어찌할 바를 모르고 정신이 나갈 지경이었기 때문입니다."],["What I learned revived my hopes, and I was better pleased not to have found Don Fernando than to find him married, for it seemed to me that the door was not yet entirely shut upon relief in my case, and I thought that perhaps Heaven had put this impediment in the way of the second marriage, to lead him to recognise his obligations under the former one, and reflect that as a Christian he was bound to consider his soul above all human objects.","제가 알게 된 것은 제 희망을 되살렸으니, 저는 돈 페르난도가 혼인한 것을 보느니 차라리 그를 못 찾은 것이 더 다행스러웠습니다. 제 처지에서 구원의 문이 아직 완전히 닫힌 것은 아니라는 생각이 들었고, 어쩌면 하늘이 그가 앞선 의무를 깨닫고, 기독교인으로서 그 어떤 인간사보다 자기 영혼을 우선해야 함을 돌아보게 하려고, 이 두 번째 혼인의 길에 이런 걸림돌을 놓아둔 것일지도 모른다고 생각했기 때문입니다."],["All this passed through my mind, and I strove to comfort myself without comfort, indulging in faint and distant hopes of cherishing that life that I now abhor.","이 모든 것이 제 마음을 스쳐 지나갔고, 저는 이제는 혐오하는 그 목숨을 부지하려는 희미하고 아득한 희망에 매달리며, 위안 없는 위안을 얻으려 애썼습니다."]],[["“But while I was in the city, uncertain what to do, as I could not find Don Fernando, I heard notice given by the public crier offering a great reward to anyone who should find me, and giving the particulars of my age and of the very dress I wore; and I heard it said that the lad who came with me had taken me away from my father’s house; a thing that cut me to the heart, showing how low my good name had fallen, since it was not enough that I should lose it by my flight, but they must add with whom I had fled, and that one so much beneath me and so unworthy of my consideration. The instant I heard the notice I quitted the city with my servant, who now began to show signs of wavering in his fidelity to me, and the same night, for fear of discovery, we entered the most thickly wooded part of these mountains. But, as is commonly said, one evil calls up another and the end of one misfortune is apt to be the beginning of one still greater, and so it proved in my case; for my worthy servant, until then so faithful and trusty when he found me in this lonely spot, moved more by his own villainy than by my beauty, sought to take advantage of the opportunity which these solitudes seemed to present him, and with little shame and less fear of God and respect for me, began to make overtures to me; and finding that I replied to the effrontery of his proposals with justly severe language, he laid aside the entreaties which he had employed at first, and began to use violence.","* 사갈: 스페인 농가에서 일하던 젊은 일꾼이나 머슴을 이르는 말. \"그러나 제가 그 도시에 있으면서, 돈 페르난도를 찾을 수 없어 어찌해야 할지 막막해하던 차에, 저를 찾아내는 사람에게 큰 상을 내리겠다는 관의 포고가 외쳐지는 것을 들었으니, 그 포고는 제 나이와 제가 입은 바로 그 옷차림의 세세한 점까지 알려 주고 있었습니다. 그리고 저와 함께 온 젊은이가 제 아버지의 집에서 저를 데려갔다는 말이 도는 것을 들었으니, 이는 제 가슴을 도려내는 일이었습니다. 제 좋은 이름이 얼마나 추락했는지를 보여 주는 것이, 제가 달아나면서 그것을 잃는 것으로도 모자라, 게다가 제가 누구와 함께 달아났는지까지 — 그것도 저보다 그토록 신분이 낮고 제 고려에 그토록 값하지 못하는 자와 — 덧붙여야 했기 때문입니다. 그 포고를 듣는 순간 저는 제 하인과 함께 도시를 떠났으니, 그는 이제 제게 대한 자기 충성이 흔들리는 기색을 보이기 시작했습니다. 그리고 발각될까 두려워 그날 밤 우리는 이 산들 가운데 가장 빽빽하게 우거진 곳으로 들어갔습니다. 그러나 흔히 말하듯, 한 가지 재앙이 다른 재앙을 불러오고, 한 불행의 끝이 더 큰 불행의 시작이 되기 쉬운데, 제 경우에도 그것이 사실로 드러났습니다. 그때까지 그토록 충실하고 미덥던 제 변변한 하인이 이 외진 곳에서 저를 보자, 제 아름다움보다는 제 자신의 사악함에 더 이끌려, 이 적막함이 그에게 베풀어 주는 듯한 기회를 이용하려 했으니, 부끄러움도 거의 없이, 하느님에 대한 두려움이나 저에 대한 존경은 더더욱 없이, 제게 수작을 걸기 시작했습니다. 그리고 그가 자기 제안의 뻔뻔함에 제가 마땅히 준엄한 말로 답하는 것을 보고는, 처음에 썼던 간청을 내려놓고 폭력을 쓰기 시작했습니다."]],[["“But just Heaven, that seldom fails to watch over and aid good intentions, so aided mine that with my slight strength and with little exertion I pushed him over a precipice, where I left him, whether dead or alive I know not; and then, with greater speed than seemed possible in my terror and fatigue, I made my way into the mountains, without any other thought or purpose save that of hiding myself among them, and escaping my father and those despatched in search of me by his orders.","\"그러나 선한 뜻을 지키고 도우시기를 좀처럼 게을리하지 않으시는 의로운 하늘이 제 뜻을 어찌나 도우셨던지, 보잘것없는 힘으로도, 별 애를 쓰지 않고도 저는 그를 벼랑 너머로 밀어 버렸으며, 그가 죽었는지 살았는지는 모르나 거기에 남겨 두었습니다. 그러고는 두려움과 피로 속에서 가능하리라 여겨지던 것보다 더 빠른 걸음으로, 산속에 몸을 숨기고, 아버지와 그의 명으로 저를 찾으러 파견된 이들에게서 벗어나는 것 말고는 다른 어떤 생각이나 목적도 없이, 저는 산속으로 길을 나아갔습니다."],["It is now I know not how many months since with this object I came here, where I met a herdsman who engaged me as his servant at a place in the heart of this Sierra, and all this time I have been serving him as herd, striving to keep always afield to hide these locks which have now unexpectedly betrayed me.","이 목적으로 제가 이곳에 온 지 이제 몇 달이 되었는지 모르겠습니다만, 여기서 저는 한 목자를 만났고, 그는 이 시에라 한복판의 어느 곳에서 저를 자기 하인으로 들였으며, 그동안 줄곧 저는 그의 양치기 노릇을 하며, 이제 뜻밖에 저를 배신한 이 머리채를 숨기려고 늘 들판에 나가 있으려 애썼습니다."],["But all my care and pains were unavailing, for my master made the discovery that I was not a man, and harboured the same base designs as my servant; and as fortune does not always supply a remedy in cases of difficulty, and I had no precipice or ravine at hand down which to fling the master and cure his passion, as I had in the servant’s case, I thought it a lesser evil to leave him and again conceal myself among these crags, than make trial of my strength and argument with him.","그러나 저의 온갖 조심과 수고도 헛되었으니, 제 주인이 제가 남자가 아님을 알아내고, 제 하인이 품었던 것과 똑같은 비열한 속셈을 품었던 것입니다. 그리고 운명이 곤경에 처할 때마다 늘 해결책을 마련해 주는 것은 아니어서, 하인의 경우처럼 주인을 굴려 떨어뜨려 그 욕정을 고쳐 줄 벼랑이나 골짜기가 손닿는 곳에 없었기에, 저는 그와 힘과 논쟁을 겨뤄 보느니 차라리 그를 떠나 다시 이 바위들 사이에 몸을 숨기는 것이 덜한 화라고 여겼습니다."],["So, as I say, once more I went into hiding to seek for some place where I might with sighs and tears implore Heaven to have pity on my misery, and grant me help and strength to escape from it, or let me die among the solitudes, leaving no trace of an unhappy being who, by no fault of hers, has furnished matter for talk and scandal at home and abroad.”","그리하여, 말씀드렸듯이, 저는 한숨과 눈물로 하늘에 제 비참함을 가엾이 여겨 주십사, 거기서 벗어날 도움과 힘을 주십사 빌거나, 아니면 이 적막함 속에서 죽어, 자기 잘못도 없이 안팎에서 입길에 오르내리고 추문거리를 제공한 한 불행한 여인의 자취조차 남기지 않게 해 주십사 빌 만한 어떤 곳을 찾으려고 다시 한번 숨어들었던 것입니다.\""]],[["# CHAPTER XXIX. WHICH TREATS OF THE DROLL DEVICE AND METHOD ADOPTED TO EXTRICATE OUR LOVE-STRICKEN KNIGHT FROM THE SEVERE PENANCE HE HAD IMPOSED UPON HIMSELF","# CHAPTER XXIX."]],[["“Such, sirs, is the true story of my sad adventures; judge for yourselves now whether the sighs and lamentations you heard, and the tears that flowed from my eyes, had not sufficient cause even if I had indulged in them more freely; and if you consider the nature of my misfortune you will see that consolation is idle, as there is no possible remedy for it. All I ask of you is, what you may easily and reasonably do, to show me where I may pass my life unharassed by the fear and dread of discovery by those who are in search of me; for though the great love my parents bear me makes me feel sure of being kindly received by them, so great is my feeling of shame at the mere thought that I cannot present myself before them as they expect, that I had rather banish myself from their sight for ever than look them in the face with the reflection that they beheld mine stripped of that purity they had a right to expect in me.”","\"여러분, 이것이 제 슬픈 모험에 관한 진실된 이야기랍니다. 여러분이 들으신 그 한숨과 탄식, 그리고 제 두 눈에서 흘러내린 그 눈물이, 설령 제가 그것을 더 마음껏 쏟아 냈다 한들 충분한 까닭이 없었는지 이제 스스로 판단해 보십시오. 그리고 제 불행의 본질을 헤아려 보신다면, 거기에는 어떤 처방도 있을 수 없으니 위로란 한낱 헛된 일임을 아시게 될 것입니다. 제가 여러분께 청하는 것이라고는, 여러분께서 손쉽고도 사리에 맞게 해 주실 수 있는 일, 곧 저를 찾아 헤매는 자들에게 발각될 두려움과 공포에 시달리지 않고 제 삶을 보낼 수 있는 곳이 어디인지 일러 주시는 것뿐입니다. 비록 제 부모님께서 저를 향해 품으신 그 크나큰 사랑 덕분에 그분들께 따뜻이 받아들여지리라는 것은 확실히 느끼지만, 그분들이 기대하시는 모습으로 제 자신을 그 앞에 내놓을 수 없다는 그 생각만으로도 제 부끄러움이 너무도 크기에, 그분들이 마땅히 제게 바랐던 그 순결을 빼앗긴 제 얼굴을 그분들이 바라본다는 생각으로 그분들의 얼굴을 마주하느니, 차라리 그분들의 시야에서 영영 저 자신을 추방해 버리는 편이 낫겠습니다.\""]],[["With these words she became silent, and the colour that overspread her face showed plainly the pain and shame she was suffering at heart.","이 말과 함께 그녀는 입을 다물었고, 그녀의 얼굴을 뒤덮은 홍조는 그녀가 마음속에서 겪고 있는 고통과 수치를 또렷이 드러내 보였다."],["In theirs the listeners felt as much pity as wonder at her misfortunes; but as the curate was just about to offer her some consolation and advice Cardenio forestalled him, saying, “So then, señora, you are the fair Dorothea, the only daughter of the rich Clenardo?”","듣고 있던 이들은 그녀의 불행에 놀라움 못지않게 큰 연민을 느꼈으나, 신부가 막 그녀에게 위로와 조언을 건네려는 참에 카르데니오가 그를 앞질러 말했다. \"그렇다면 아가씨, 당신이 바로 부유한 클레나르도의 외동딸인 아름다운 도로테아로군요?\""],["Dorothea was astonished at hearing her father’s name, and at the miserable appearance of him who mentioned it, for it has been already said how wretchedly clad Cardenio was; so she said to him:","도로테아는 자기 아버지의 이름을 듣고서, 또 그 이름을 입에 올린 자의 비참한 행색을 보고서 깜짝 놀랐으니, 카르데니오가 얼마나 초라하게 옷을 걸치고 있었는지는 이미 앞서 말한 바와 같다. 그리하여 그녀는 그에게 말했다."]],[["“And who may you be, brother, who seem to know my father’s name so well?","\"제 아버지의 이름을 그토록 잘 아시는 듯한 당신은 대체 누구신가요, 오라버니?"],["For so far, if I remember rightly, I have not mentioned it in the whole story of my misfortunes.”","제 기억이 옳다면, 지금까지 제 불행에 관한 이야기 전체에서 저는 그 이름을 언급한 적이 없거든요.\""]],[["“I am that unhappy being, señora,” replied Cardenio, “whom, as you have said, Luscinda declared to be her husband; I am the unfortunate Cardenio, whom the wrong-doing of him who has brought you to your present condition has reduced to the state you see me in, bare, ragged, bereft of all human comfort, and what is worse, of reason, for I only possess it when Heaven is pleased for some short space to restore it to me. I, Dorothea, am he who witnessed the wrong done by Don Fernando, and waited to hear the ‘Yes’ uttered by which Luscinda owned herself his betrothed: I am he who had not courage enough to see how her fainting fit ended, or what came of the paper that was found in her bosom, because my heart had not the fortitude to endure so many strokes of ill-fortune at once; and so losing patience I quitted the house, and leaving a letter with my host, which I entreated him to place in Luscinda’s hands, I betook myself to these solitudes, resolved to end here the life I hated as if it were my mortal enemy. But fate would not rid me of it, contenting itself with robbing me of my reason, perhaps to preserve me for the good fortune I have had in meeting you; for if that which you have just told us be true, as I believe it to be, it may be that Heaven has yet in store for both of us a happier termination to our misfortunes than we look for; because seeing that Luscinda cannot marry Don Fernando, being mine, as she has herself so openly declared, and that Don Fernando cannot marry her as he is yours, we may reasonably hope that Heaven will restore to us what is ours, as it is still in existence and not yet alienated or destroyed. And as we have this consolation springing from no very visionary hope or wild fancy, I entreat you, señora, to form new resolutions in your better mind, as I mean to do in mine, preparing yourself to look forward to happier fortunes; for I swear to you by the faith of a gentleman and a Christian not to desert you until I see you in possession of Don Fernando, and if I cannot by words induce him to recognise his obligation to you, in that case to avail myself of the right which my rank as a gentleman gives me, and with just cause challenge him on account of the injury he has done you, not regarding my own wrongs, which I shall leave to Heaven to avenge, while I on earth devote myself to yours.”","\"아가씨, 저는 바로 그 불행한 자랍니다.\" 카르데니오가 대답했다. \"당신이 말씀하신 대로, 루신다가 자기 남편이라고 선언했던 그 사람 말입니다. 저는 불운한 카르데니오로, 당신을 지금의 처지로 몰아넣은 그자의 악행 때문에 당신이 보시는 이 꼴로 전락한 사람입니다. 헐벗고 누더기를 걸치고 인간다운 온갖 위안을 빼앗겼으며, 더욱 나쁘게는 이성마저 빼앗겼으니, 하늘이 기뻐하시어 잠시잠깐 그것을 제게 되돌려 주실 때에만 저는 이성을 지닐 뿐입니다. 도로테아, 저는 돈 페르난도가 저지른 악행을 목격하고, 루신다가 그의 약혼녀임을 스스로 인정하며 내뱉은 그 '예'라는 말을 들으려고 기다리던 사람입니다. 저는 그녀의 실신이 어떻게 끝났는지, 그녀의 가슴에서 발견된 그 종이가 어찌 되었는지를 끝까지 지켜볼 만한 용기조차 없었던 사람입니다. 제 심장이 그토록 많은 불운의 타격을 한꺼번에 견뎌 낼 만한 굳셈을 지니지 못했기 때문이지요. 그리하여 인내심을 잃고 그 집을 떠났으며, 주인에게 편지 한 통을 맡겨 부디 루신다의 손에 전해 달라고 간청한 뒤, 이 황량한 곳으로 몸을 옮겨 와, 마치 불구대천의 원수를 대하듯 미워하던 이 목숨을 여기서 끝내기로 결심했습니다. 그러나 운명은 제게서 그것을 거두어 가려 하지 않고, 그저 제 이성을 앗아 가는 것으로 만족했으니, 아마도 당신을 만나는 행운을 위해 저를 살려 두려 한 것이겠지요. 방금 당신이 우리에게 들려주신 것이 사실이라면, 제가 그것을 사실이라 믿거니와, 어쩌면 하늘은 우리 두 사람 모두에게, 우리가 바라는 것보다 더 행복한 불행의 결말을 아직 마련해 두셨을지도 모릅니다. 루신다가 그토록 공공연히 스스로 선언한 대로 제 사람이니 돈 페르난도와 혼인할 수 없고, 돈 페르난도 또한 당신 사람이니 그녀와 혼인할 수 없음을 보건대, 우리 것이 아직 존재하며 아직 양도되거나 파괴되지 않은 이상, 하늘이 우리에게 우리 것을 되돌려 주시리라 충분히 바랄 수 있겠지요. 그리고 우리에게 이 위안이 어떤 허황된 희망이나 터무니없는 공상에서 솟아난 것이 아니니, 아가씨, 부디 더 나은 마음으로 새로운 결심을 세우시기를 간청합니다. 저 또한 제 마음에 그리하여 더 행복한 운명을 내다볼 채비를 하려 하니까요. 저는 신사이자 그리스도교인의 신의를 걸고 맹세하건대, 당신이 돈 페르난도를 차지하는 것을 볼 때까지 당신을 저버리지 않겠습니다. 만일 제가 말로써 그가 당신에 대한 의무를 인정하도록 이끌 수 없다면, 그때에는 신사로서 제 신분이 제게 부여하는 권리를 행사하여, 그가 당신에게 끼친 해악을 들어 정당한 명분으로 그에게 결투를 청하겠습니다. 제 자신이 입은 부당함은 돌아보지 않을 것이며, 그것은 하늘이 갚아 주시도록 맡겨 두고, 저는 이 땅에서 당신의 일에 제 자신을 바치겠습니다.\""]],[["Cardenio’s words completed the astonishment of Dorothea, and not knowing how to return thanks for such an offer, she attempted to kiss his feet; but Cardenio would not permit it, and the licentiate replied for both, commended the sound reasoning of Cardenio, and lastly, begged, advised, and urged them to come with him to his village, where they might furnish themselves with what they needed, and take measures to discover Don Fernando, or restore Dorothea to her parents, or do what seemed to them most advisable.","카르데니오의 말은 도로테아의 놀라움을 극에 달하게 했으니, 그러한 제안에 어떻게 감사를 표해야 할지 알지 못한 그녀는 그의 발에 입을 맞추려 했으나, 카르데니오는 그것을 허락하지 않았다. 학사가 두 사람을 대신해 대답하며 카르데니오의 건전한 논리를 칭찬하고는, 마침내 그들에게 자기 마을로 함께 가자고 청하고 권하고 재촉했으니, 거기서 필요한 것을 갖추고, 돈 페르난도를 찾아낼 방도를 강구하거나, 도로테아를 그 부모에게 되돌려 보내거나, 아니면 그들에게 가장 좋다고 여겨지는 일을 할 수 있으리라는 것이었다."],["Cardenio and Dorothea thanked him, and accepted the kind offer he made them; and the barber, who had been listening to all attentively and in silence, on his part some kindly words also, and with no less good-will than the curate offered his services in any way that might be of use to them.","카르데니오와 도로테아는 그에게 감사하며 그가 베푼 친절한 제안을 받아들였다. 그리고 그동안 모든 것을 주의 깊게 묵묵히 듣고 있던 이발사도 제 나름대로 다정한 말 몇 마디를 건네고는, 신부에 못지않은 호의로 그들에게 도움이 될 만한 일이라면 무엇이든 자기 힘을 보태겠다고 했다."],["He also explained to them in a few words the object that had brought them there, and the strange nature of Don Quixote’s madness, and how they were waiting for his squire, who had gone in search of him.","그는 또한 자기들을 그곳으로 데려온 목적과 돈키호테의 광기가 지닌 기이한 성질, 그리고 그를 찾아 나선 그의 종자를 그들이 기다리고 있다는 사정을 몇 마디로 설명해 주었다."],["Like the recollection of a dream, the quarrel he had had with Don Quixote came back to Cardenio’s memory, and he described it to the others; but he was unable to say what the dispute was about.","그러자 마치 꿈을 떠올리듯, 자기가 돈키호테와 벌였던 다툼이 카르데니오의 기억 속에 되살아났고, 그는 그것을 다른 이들에게 들려주었으나 그 다툼이 무엇에 관한 것이었는지는 말할 수 없었다."]],[["At this moment they heard a shout, and recognised it as coming from Sancho Panza, who, not finding them where he had left them, was calling aloud to them. They went to meet him, and in answer to their inquiries about Don Quixote, he told them how he had found him stripped to his shirt, lank, yellow, half dead with hunger, and sighing for his lady Dulcinea; and although he had told him that she commanded him to quit that place and come to El Toboso, where she was expecting him, he had answered that he was determined not to appear in the presence of her beauty until he had done deeds to make him worthy of her favour; and if this went on, Sancho said, he ran the risk of not becoming an emperor as in duty bound, or even an archbishop, which was the least he could be; for which reason they ought to consider what was to be done to get him away from there. The licentiate in reply told him not to be uneasy, for they would fetch him away in spite of himself. He then told Cardenio and Dorothea what they had proposed to do to cure Don Quixote, or at any rate take him home; upon which Dorothea said that she could play the distressed damsel better than the barber; especially as she had there the dress in which to do it to the life, and that they might trust to her acting the part in every particular requisite for carrying out their scheme, for she had read a great many books of chivalry, and knew exactly the style in which afflicted damsels begged boons of knights-errant.","이때 그들은 고함 소리를 듣고, 그것이 산초 판사에게서 나는 것임을 알아차렸다. 그를 두고 떠났던 자리에서 그들을 찾지 못한 산초가 큰 소리로 그들을 부르고 있었던 것이다. 그들이 그를 맞으러 나가, 돈키호테에 관해 묻자, 그는 자기가 어떻게 셔츠 바람으로 깡마르고 누렇게 뜬 채 굶주림으로 반쯤 죽어 가며 그의 귀부인 둘시네아를 향해 한숨짓고 있는 주인을 발견했는지 들려주었다. 그리고 비록 그녀께서 그곳을 떠나, 그를 기다리고 있는 엘 토보소로 오라 명하셨다고 일렀건만, 주인은 그녀의 총애를 받을 만한 행적을 이루기 전에는 그 미모 앞에 나서지 않기로 결심했노라 대답했다는 것이다. 산초의 말로는, 이런 식으로 가다가는 주인이 마땅히 되어야 할 황제는 고사하고, 적어도 될 수 있는 최소한의 자리인 대주교조차 되지 못할 위험이 있으니, 그를 거기서 빼내 올 방도를 궁리해야 한다는 것이었다. 학사는 이에 대답하여, 마음 졸이지 말라고, 그가 원하든 원치 않든 데려올 것이라고 했다. 그러고는 카르데니오와 도로테아에게, 돈키호테를 고치거나 적어도 집으로 데려가기 위해 자기들이 무엇을 하기로 했는지를 일러 주었다. 그러자 도로테아는, 자기가 이발사보다 곤경에 빠진 처녀 역을 더 잘할 수 있노라고 했으니, 더욱이 그렇게 실감 나게 해 보일 옷차림을 가지고 있을 뿐 아니라, 그들의 계략을 실행하는 데 필요한 모든 세부 대목에서 그 역을 연기하는 일을 자기에게 맡겨도 좋다고 했다. 그녀는 기사도 책을 매우 많이 읽었기에, 고난에 처한 처녀들이 편력 기사들에게 은혜를 청하는 그 방식을 정확히 알고 있었던 것이다."]],[["“In that case,”","\"그렇다면,\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “there is nothing more required than to set about it at once, for beyond a doubt fortune is declaring itself in our favour, since it has so unexpectedly begun to open a door for your relief, and smoothed the way for us to our object.”","\"당장 착수하는 것 말고는 더 필요한 것이 없겠소이다. 운명이 이렇듯 뜻밖에도 당신의 구제를 위해 문을 열어 주기 시작하고, 우리가 목적에 이르는 길을 평탄하게 닦아 주었으니, 운명이 우리 편을 들고 있음에 의심의 여지가 없으니 말이오.\""]],[["Dorothea then took out of her pillow-case a complete petticoat of some rich stuff, and a green mantle of some other fine material, and a necklace and other ornaments out of a little box, and with these in an instant she so arrayed herself that she looked like a great and rich lady.","그러자 도로테아는 베갯잇에서 값진 천으로 지은 페티코트 한 벌과 또 다른 고운 옷감으로 만든 초록 망토를 꺼내고, 작은 상자에서 목걸이와 다른 장신구들을 꺼내더니, 이것들로 순식간에 단장하여 위엄 있고 부유한 귀부인처럼 보였다."],["All this, and more, she said, she had taken from home in case of need, but that until then she had had no occasion to make use of it.","이 모든 것을, 아니 그 이상을 만일을 대비해 집에서 챙겨 왔으나, 그때까지 쓸 일이 없었노라고 그녀는 말했다."],["They were all highly delighted with her grace, air, and beauty, and declared Don Fernando to be a man of very little taste when he rejected such charms.","그녀의 우아함과 자태와 아름다움에 모두가 무척이나 기뻐하며, 돈 페르난도가 이런 매력을 마다했다니 안목이 보잘것없는 사내라고 입을 모았다."],["But the one who admired her most was Sancho Panza, for it seemed to him (what indeed was true) that in all the days of his life he had never seen such a lovely creature; and he asked the curate with great eagerness who this beautiful lady was, and what she wanted in these out-of-the-way quarters.","그러나 그녀를 가장 감탄하며 바라본 이는 산초 판사였으니, 그가 보기에는 (실제로도 그러했거니와) 평생토록 그토록 사랑스러운 여인을 본 적이 없는 듯했다. 그리하여 그는 무척이나 열을 올리며 신부에게, 이 아름다운 귀부인은 누구이며, 이런 외딴 두메에서 무엇을 원하는지 물었다."]],[["“This fair lady, brother Sancho,” replied the curate, “is no less a personage than the heiress in the direct male line of the great kingdom of Micomicon, who has come in search of your master to beg a boon of him, which is that he redress a wrong or injury that a wicked giant has done her; and from the fame as a good knight which your master has acquired far and wide, this princess has come from Guinea to seek him.”","\"산초 형제여, 이 아름다운 귀부인은,\" 신부가 대답했다. \"다름이 아니라, 위대한 미코미콘 왕국을 직계 남성 혈통으로 물려받은 상속녀로, 당신의 주인을 찾아와 그에게 은혜를 청하시려는 분이라네. 그 은혜란, 사악한 거인이 그녀에게 저지른 잘못이나 해악을 바로잡아 달라는 것이지. 당신의 주인이 훌륭한 기사로서 사방에 떨친 명성을 듣고, 이 공주께서 기니에서부터 그를 찾아 오신 것이라네.\""]],[["“A lucky seeking and a lucky finding!” said Sancho Panza at this; “especially if my master has the good fortune to redress that injury, and right that wrong, and kill that son of a bitch of a giant your worship speaks of; as kill him he will if he meets him, unless, indeed, he happens to be a phantom; for my master has no power at all against phantoms. But one thing among others I would beg of you, señor licentiate, which is, that, to prevent my master taking a fancy to be an archbishop, for that is what I’m afraid of, your worship would recommend him to marry this princess at once; for in this way he will be disabled from taking archbishop’s orders, and will easily come into his empire, and I to the end of my desires; I have been thinking over the matter carefully, and by what I can make out I find it will not do for me that my master should become an archbishop, because I am no good for the Church, as I am married; and for me now, having as I have a wife and children, to set about obtaining dispensations to enable me to hold a place of profit under the Church, would be endless work; so that, señor, it all turns on my master marrying this lady at once--for as yet I do not know her grace, and so I cannot call her by her name.”","\"운 좋게 찾아오셔서 운 좋게 만나셨군요!\" 산초 판사가 이 말에 대꾸했다. \"특히 제 주인님께서 운 좋게 그 해악을 바로잡고, 그 잘못을 바루며, 나리께서 말씀하시는 그 개자식 같은 거인을 죽이실 수 있다면 말입니다. 그놈을 만나기만 하면 죽이실 게 틀림없지요. 다만 그놈이 유령이라면 모를 일이지만요. 제 주인님은 유령에게는 도무지 힘을 쓰지 못하시거든요. 그런데 다른 무엇보다도 나리께 한 가지 청드리고 싶은 게 있습니다, 학사 나리. 제 주인님이 대주교가 되겠다는 마음을 먹지 않도록 — 바로 그게 제가 두려워하는 바입니다만 — 나리께서 그분께 이 공주님과 당장 혼인하시라고 권해 주십사 하는 것입니다. 이렇게 하면 그분은 대주교 서품을 받을 수 없게 되고, 손쉽게 자기 제국을 얻으실 테고, 저도 제 소원의 끝에 다다를 테니까요. 제가 이 일을 곰곰이 따져 보았는데, 제가 헤아릴 수 있는 한, 제 주인님이 대주교가 되시는 건 제게 좋을 게 없다는 걸 알았습니다. 저는 처자식이 있는 몸이라 교회에는 쓸모가 없거든요. 게다가 지금 저처럼 아내와 자식들이 있는 사람이 교회 밑에서 돈벌이 되는 자리를 차지할 수 있도록 면제를 얻어 내려 하자면 끝이 없는 일이 될 테니까요. 그러니 나리, 모든 것은 제 주인님이 이 귀부인과 당장 혼인하시느냐에 달렸습니다. 저는 아직 이분의 존함을 모르니, 이름으로 불러 드릴 수가 없군요.\""]],[["“She is called the Princess Micomicona,”","\"이분은 미코미코나 공주라고 불린다네.\""],["said the curate; “for as her kingdom is Micomicon, it is clear that must be her name.”","신부가 말했다. \"이분의 왕국이 미코미콘이니, 그게 이분의 이름임이 분명하지.\""]],[["“There’s no doubt of that,”","\"그건 의심할 바 없는 일이군요.\" 산초가 대답했다."],["replied Sancho, “for I have known many to take their name and title from the place where they were born and call themselves Pedro of Alcala, Juan of Ubeda, and Diego of Valladolid; and it may be that over there in Guinea queens have the same way of taking the names of their kingdoms.”","\"저는 태어난 곳에서 이름과 칭호를 따서 알칼라의 페드로, 우베다의 후안, 바야돌리드의 디에고라고 스스로를 부르는 이들을 많이 알고 있거든요. 저 멀리 기니에서도 왕비님들이 마찬가지로 자기 왕국의 이름을 따는 풍습이 있는지도 모를 일이지요.\""]],[["“So it may,”","\"그럴지도 모르지.\""],["said the curate; “and as for your master’s marrying, I will do all in my power towards it:”","신부가 말했다. \"그리고 당신 주인의 혼인에 관해서라면, 내가 할 수 있는 모든 힘을 다하겠네.\""],["with which Sancho was as much pleased as the curate was amazed at his simplicity and at seeing what a hold the absurdities of his master had taken of his fancy, for he had evidently persuaded himself that he was going to be an emperor.","이 말에 산초는 무척이나 기뻐했고, 신부는 그의 순박함과, 그가 자기 주인의 황당무계한 짓에 그토록 사로잡힌 모습을 보고 어리둥절해했으니, 산초가 자기 주인이 황제가 되리라고 스스로를 분명히 납득시킨 것이 틀림없었기 때문이다."]],[["By this time Dorothea had seated herself upon the curate’s mule, and the barber had fitted the ox-tail beard to his face, and they now told Sancho to conduct them to where Don Quixote was, warning him not to say that he knew either the licentiate or the barber, as his master’s becoming an emperor entirely depended on his not recognising them; neither the curate nor Cardenio, however, thought fit to go with them; Cardenio lest he should remind Don Quixote of the quarrel he had with him, and the curate as there was no necessity for his presence just yet, so they allowed the others to go on before them, while they themselves followed slowly on foot. The curate did not forget to instruct Dorothea how to act, but she said they might make their minds easy, as everything would be done exactly as the books of chivalry required and described.","이때쯤 도로테아는 신부의 노새에 올라앉았고, 이발사는 소꼬리 수염을 얼굴에 붙였다. 그러고는 산초에게, 자기들을 돈키호테가 있는 곳으로 안내하되, 학사나 이발사를 안다는 말은 하지 말라고 일렀으니, 주인이 황제가 되는 것은 오로지 그가 그들을 알아보지 못하는 데 달렸다는 것이었다. 그러나 신부도 카르데니오도 그들과 함께 가는 것이 좋겠다고 여기지는 않았다. 카르데니오는 돈키호테에게 그와 벌였던 다툼을 떠올리게 할까 봐서였고, 신부는 아직은 자기가 그 자리에 있을 필요가 없어서였다. 그리하여 그들은 다른 이들을 앞서 가게 하고, 자기들은 천천히 걸어서 뒤따랐다. 신부는 도로테아에게 어떻게 처신해야 할지 일러 주는 것을 잊지 않았으나, 그녀는 모든 것을 기사도 책이 요구하고 묘사하는 그대로 정확히 해낼 테니 안심해도 좋다고 했다."]],[["They had gone about three-quarters of a league when they discovered Don Quixote in a wilderness of rocks, by this time clothed, but without his armour; and as soon as Dorothea saw him and was told by Sancho that that was Don Quixote, she whipped her palfrey, the well-bearded barber following her, and on coming up to him her squire sprang from his mule and came forward to receive her in his arms, and she dismounting with great ease of manner advanced to kneel before the feet of Don Quixote; and though he strove to raise her up, she without rising addressed him in this fashion:","그들이 사분의 삼 레과쯤 갔을 때, 바위투성이 황무지에서 돈키호테를 발견했는데, 이때쯤 그는 옷은 걸쳤으나 갑옷은 입지 않은 차림이었다. 도로테아는 그를 보고, 산초에게서 저이가 돈키호테라는 말을 듣자마자 자기 작은 말에 채찍질을 했고, 수염을 잘 붙인 이발사가 그 뒤를 따랐다. 그에게 다가가자 그녀의 종자가 노새에서 뛰어내려 두 팔로 그녀를 받으려 나아왔고, 그녀는 무척이나 우아한 몸가짐으로 내려서서 돈키호테의 발 앞에 무릎을 꿇으려 나아갔다. 그가 그녀를 일으켜 세우려 애썼으나, 그녀는 일어나지 않은 채 이렇게 그에게 말했다."]],[["“From this spot I will not rise, valiant and doughty knight, until your goodness and courtesy grant me a boon, which will redound to the honour and renown of your person and render a service to the most disconsolate and afflicted damsel the sun has seen; and if the might of your strong arm corresponds to the repute of your immortal fame, you are bound to aid the helpless being who, led by the savour of your renowned name, hath come from far distant lands to seek your aid in her misfortunes.”","\"용맹하고 굳센 기사님, 당신의 선량함과 정중함이 제게 한 가지 은혜를 베풀어 주시기 전에는, 저는 이 자리에서 일어나지 않겠습니다. 그 은혜는 당신의 명예와 명성을 드높일 것이며, 해 아래 가장 비탄에 잠기고 고난에 빠진 처녀에게 봉사를 베푸는 일이 될 것입니다. 당신의 굳센 팔의 힘이 당신의 불멸하는 명성의 평판에 걸맞다면, 당신은 당신의 드높은 이름의 향기에 이끌려 자신의 불행 속에서 당신의 도움을 구하고자 머나먼 땅에서 찾아온 이 의지가지없는 존재를 도와주실 의무가 있습니다.\""]],[["“I will not answer a word, beauteous lady,”","\"아름다운 귀부인이여, 그대가 땅에서 일어서기 전에는,\""],["replied Don Quixote, “nor will I listen to anything further concerning you, until you rise from the earth.”","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"나는 한마디도 답하지 않을 것이며, 그대에 관한 그 어떤 말도 더는 듣지 않으리다.\""]],[["“I will not rise, señor,”","\"기사님, 청컨대 당신의 정중함으로 제가 청하는 그 은혜를 먼저 베풀어 주시기 전에는, 저는 일어나지 않겠습니다.\""],["answered the afflicted damsel, “unless of your courtesy the boon I ask is first granted me.”","고난에 처한 처녀가 대답했다."]],[["“I grant and accord it,”","\"내가 그것을 허락하고 응낙하노라.\""],["said Don Quixote, “provided without detriment or prejudice to my king, my country, or her who holds the key of my heart and freedom, it may be complied with.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"다만 나의 임금, 나의 조국, 또는 내 마음과 자유의 열쇠를 쥐고 있는 그분께 해롭거나 누가 되지 않는 한에서 이루어질 수 있는 것이어야 하오.\""]],[["“It will not be to the detriment or prejudice of any of them, my worthy lord,” said the afflicted damsel; and here Sancho Panza drew close to his master’s ear and said to him very softly, “Your worship may very safely grant the boon she asks; it’s nothing at all; only to kill a big giant; and she who asks it is the exalted Princess Micomicona, queen of the great kingdom of Micomicon of Ethiopia.”","\"그 누구에게도 해롭거나 누가 되지 않을 것입니다, 거룩하신 나리.\" 고난에 처한 처녀가 말했다. 이때 산초 판사가 주인의 귀에 바싹 다가가 아주 나직이 말했다. \"나리, 이분이 청하시는 은혜는 아무 거리낌 없이 베풀어 주셔도 됩니다. 아무것도 아니에요. 그저 큰 거인 하나를 죽이는 일일 뿐이거든요. 이걸 청하시는 분은 고귀하신 미코미코나 공주님으로, 에티오피아의 위대한 미코미콘 왕국의 여왕이시랍니다.\""]],[["“Let her be who she may,”","\"그분이 누구이든 간에,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["replied Don Quixote, “I will do what is my bounden duty, and what my conscience bids me, in conformity with what I have professed;”","\"나는 내가 맹세한 바에 따라, 마땅히 해야 할 나의 의무와 내 양심이 명하는 바를 행하리라.\" 그러고는 처녀를 향해 돌아서서 말했다."],["and turning to the damsel he said, “Let your great beauty rise, for I grant the boon which you would ask of me.”","\"그대의 위대한 아름다움이여, 일어나시오. 그대가 내게 청하려는 은혜를 허락하노니.\""]],[["“Then what I ask,”","\"그렇다면 제가 청하는 바는,\""],["said the damsel, “is that your magnanimous person accompany me at once whither I will conduct you, and that you promise not to engage in any other adventure or quest until you have avenged me of a traitor who against all human and divine law, has usurped my kingdom.”","처녀가 말했다. \"당신의 너그러우신 몸이 제가 인도하는 곳으로 당장 저와 함께 가 주시는 것, 그리고 인간의 법과 신의 법을 모조리 어기고 제 왕국을 찬탈한 한 반역자에게서 제 원수를 갚아 주시기 전에는 그 어떤 다른 모험이나 탐색에도 나서지 않으시겠다고 약속해 주시는 것입니다.\""]],[["“I repeat that I grant it,” replied Don Quixote; “and so, lady, you may from this day forth lay aside the melancholy that distresses you, and let your failing hopes gather new life and strength, for with the help of God and of my arm you will soon see yourself restored to your kingdom, and seated upon the throne of your ancient and mighty realm, notwithstanding and despite of the felons who would gainsay it; and now hands to the work, for in delay there is apt to be danger.”","\"거듭 말하거니와, 내가 그것을 허락하노라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그러니 귀부인이여, 그대는 오늘부터 그대를 짓누르는 우수를 벗어 던지고, 그대의 시들어 가던 희망에 새로운 생명과 힘을 불어넣으시오. 하느님과 나의 팔의 도움으로, 그대는 머지않아 그대의 왕국을 되찾고, 그것을 가로막으려는 흉악한 무리들에도 불구하고 그대의 유서 깊고 강대한 영토의 옥좌에 다시 앉은 그대 자신을 보게 될 터이니. 자, 이제 일에 착수합시다. 지체함에는 흔히 위험이 따르는 법이니.\""]],[["The distressed damsel strove with much pertinacity to kiss his hands; but Don Quixote, who was in all things a polished and courteous knight, would by no means allow it, but made her rise and embraced her with great courtesy and politeness, and ordered Sancho to look to Rocinante’s girths, and to arm him without a moment’s delay.","고난에 처한 처녀는 무척이나 끈질기게 그의 두 손에 입을 맞추려 애썼으나, 만사에 세련되고 정중한 기사였던 돈키호테는 결코 그것을 허락하지 않고, 그녀를 일으켜 세워 무척이나 정중하고 공손하게 그녀를 끌어안았다. 그러고는 산초에게 로시난테의 뱃대끈을 살피고 한시도 지체 없이 자기에게 갑옷을 입히라고 명했다."],["Sancho took down the armour, which was hung up on a tree like a trophy, and having seen to the girths armed his master in a trice, who as soon as he found himself in his armour exclaimed:","산초는 마치 전리품처럼 나무에 걸려 있던 갑옷을 내리고, 뱃대끈을 살핀 다음 눈 깜짝할 새에 주인에게 갑옷을 입혔다. 갑옷을 차려입은 자신을 보자마자 돈키호테가 외쳤다."]],[["“Let us be gone in the name of God to bring aid to this great lady.”","\"하느님의 이름으로, 이 위대한 귀부인을 도우러 떠납시다.\""]],[["The barber was all this time on his knees at great pains to hide his laughter and not let his beard fall, for had it fallen maybe their fine scheme would have come to nothing; but now seeing the boon granted, and the promptitude with which Don Quixote prepared to set out in compliance with it, he rose and took his lady’s hand, and between them they placed her upon the mule.","이발사는 그동안 줄곧 무릎을 꿇은 채 웃음을 감추고 수염이 떨어지지 않게 하느라 무척이나 애를 먹고 있었으니, 만일 그것이 떨어졌더라면 그들의 멋진 계략이 허사가 되었을지도 모르기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 은혜가 허락되고, 그것을 이행하려 돈키호테가 채비하는 그 민첩함을 보고서, 그는 일어나 자기 귀부인의 손을 잡았고, 둘이서 함께 그녀를 노새에 태웠다."],["Don Quixote then mounted Rocinante, and the barber settled himself on his beast, Sancho being left to go on foot, which made him feel anew the loss of his Dapple, finding the want of him now.","그러고 나서 돈키호테는 로시난테에 올라타고, 이발사는 제 짐승 위에 자리를 잡았으나, 산초는 걸어서 가게 되었으니, 이제 와서 회색 당나귀가 없는 것을 절감하며 잃어버린 그놈이 새삼 아쉬워졌다."],["But he bore all with cheerfulness, being persuaded that his master had now fairly started and was just on the point of becoming an emperor; for he felt no doubt at all that he would marry this princess, and be king of Micomicon at least.","그래도 그는 모든 것을 흔쾌히 견뎌 냈으니, 자기 주인이 이제 제대로 첫발을 내디뎌 막 황제가 되려는 참이라고 믿었기 때문이다. 그는 주인이 이 공주와 혼인하여 적어도 미코미콘의 왕은 되리라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다."],["The only thing that troubled him was the reflection that this kingdom was in the land of the blacks, and that the people they would give him for vassals would be all black; but for this he soon found a remedy in his fancy, and said he to himself, “What is it to me if my vassals are blacks?","다만 한 가지 그를 괴롭히는 것은, 이 왕국이 흑인들의 땅에 있어, 그들이 자기에게 신하로 내줄 백성들이 모두 흑인일 것이라는 생각이었다. 그러나 이에 대해서도 그는 곧 자기 공상 속에서 해결책을 찾아내고는 혼잣말을 했다."],["What more have I to do than make a cargo of them and carry them to Spain, where I can sell them and get ready money for them, and with it buy some title or some office in which to live at ease all the days of my life?","\"내 신하들이 흑인이라 한들 그게 나와 무슨 상관이람? 그놈들을 한 배에 실어 스페인으로 데려가, 거기서 팔아 현금을 챙기고, 그 돈으로 작위나 관직을 하나 사서 평생 편안히 살면 그뿐 아니겠어?"],["Not unless you go to sleep and haven’t the wit or skill to turn things to account and sell three, six, or ten thousand vassals while you would be talking about it!","잠이나 자고, 머리도 재주도 없어서 일을 잘 굴려 가며 떠드는 사이에 신하 삼천, 육천, 만 명을 팔아 치우지 못하는 게 아니라면야!"],["By God I will stir them up, big and little, or as best I can, and let them be ever so black I’ll turn them into white or yellow.","맹세코 나는 크든 작든 그놈들을 죄다 휘저어 놓을 테고, 아무리 검다 한들 흰둥이나 누렁이로 둔갑시켜 버리겠어."],["Come, come, what a fool I am!”","자, 자, 내가 이 무슨 멍청이람!\""],["And so he jogged on, so occupied with his thoughts and easy in his mind that he forgot all about the hardship of travelling on foot.","이렇게 그는 제 생각에 골몰하여 마음 편히 터덜터덜 걸으며, 걸어서 가는 고생일랑 까맣게 잊고 있었다."]],[["Cardenio and the curate were watching all this from among some bushes, not knowing how to join company with the others; but the curate, who was very fertile in devices, soon hit upon a way of effecting their purpose, and with a pair of scissors he had in a case he quickly cut off Cardenio’s beard, and putting on him a grey jerkin of his own he gave him a black cloak, leaving himself in his breeches and doublet, while Cardenio’s appearance was so different from what it had been that he would not have known himself had he seen himself in a mirror. Having effected this, although the others had gone on ahead while they were disguising themselves, they easily came out on the high road before them, for the brambles and awkward places they encountered did not allow those on horseback to go as fast as those on foot. They then posted themselves on the level ground at the outlet of the Sierra, and as soon as Don Quixote and his companions emerged from it the curate began to examine him very deliberately, as though he were striving to recognise him, and after having stared at him for some time he hastened towards him with open arms exclaiming, “A happy meeting with the mirror of chivalry, my worthy compatriot Don Quixote of La Mancha, the flower and cream of high breeding, the protection and relief of the distressed, the quintessence of knights-errant!” And so saying he clasped in his arms the knee of Don Quixote’s left leg. He, astonished at the stranger’s words and behaviour, looked at him attentively, and at length recognised him, very much surprised to see him there, and made great efforts to dismount. This, however, the curate would not allow, on which Don Quixote said, “Permit me, señor licentiate, for it is not fitting that I should be on horseback and so reverend a person as your worship on foot.”","카르데니오와 신부는 덤불 사이에서 이 모든 것을 지켜보며 어떻게 다른 이들과 합류할지 알지 못하고 있었으나, 꾀가 무척이나 많은 신부는 곧 자기들의 목적을 이룰 방도를 생각해 냈다. 그는 갑에 넣어 둔 가위 한 자루로 재빨리 카르데니오의 수염을 잘라 내고, 자기 회색 윗옷을 그에게 입힌 다음 검은 망토를 건네주고는, 자기는 반바지와 더블릿 차림으로 남았다. 그리하여 카르데니오의 행색이 전과는 너무도 달라져, 그가 거울에 비친 자신을 보았더라도 알아보지 못했을 정도였다. 이렇게 변장을 마친 뒤, 변장하는 동안 다른 이들이 앞서 가 버렸으나, 그들은 손쉽게 그들보다 앞질러 큰길로 나설 수 있었으니, 그들이 마주친 가시덤불과 험한 곳들 때문에 말을 탄 이들이 걷는 이들만큼 빨리 가지 못했기 때문이다. 그러고 나서 그들은 시에라 어귀의 평지에 자리를 잡았고, 돈키호테와 그 일행이 거기서 모습을 드러내자마자 신부는 마치 그를 알아보려 애쓰는 듯 무척이나 찬찬히 그를 살펴보았다. 그러고는 한참 동안 그를 빤히 바라본 끝에 두 팔을 벌리고 그에게 달려가며 외쳤다. \"기사도의 거울이신, 나의 거룩한 동향 분, 라만차의 돈키호테 님을 이렇게 만나 뵙다니 얼마나 다행한 일입니까! 고귀한 가문의 꽃이자 정수요, 고난에 빠진 이들의 보호자요 구원자며, 편력 기사들의 진수이신 분을 말입니다!\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 돈키호테의 왼쪽 다리 무릎을 두 팔로 끌어안았다. 돈키호테는 이 낯선 이의 말과 행동에 깜짝 놀라 주의 깊게 그를 바라보다가, 마침내 그를 알아보고는, 그를 거기서 보게 되어 무척이나 놀라며 말에서 내리려 갖은 애를 썼다. 그러나 신부는 이를 허락하지 않았으니, 이에 돈키호테가 말했다. \"학사 나리, 허락해 주시오. 내가 말을 탄 채 있고 나리처럼 존경스러운 분이 걸어서 가시는 것은 온당치 못한 일이니.\""]],[["“On no account will I allow it,” said the curate; “your mightiness must remain on horseback, for it is on horseback you achieve the greatest deeds and adventures that have been beheld in our age; as for me, an unworthy priest, it will serve me well enough to mount on the haunches of one of the mules of these gentlefolk who accompany your worship, if they have no objection, and I will fancy I am mounted on the steed Pegasus, or on the zebra or charger that bore the famous Moor, Muzaraque, who to this day lies enchanted in the great hill of Zulema, a little distance from the great Complutum.”","\"결코 허락할 수 없습니다.\" 신부가 말했다. \"기사님께서는 말 위에 그대로 계셔야 합니다. 우리 시대에 보아 온 가장 위대한 위업과 모험을 기사님께서 이루시는 곳이 바로 말 위이니 말입니다. 저로 말하자면, 보잘것없는 사제이니, 기사님을 모시는 이 분들의 노새 한 마리의 엉덩이에 올라타는 것으로도 충분합니다. 만일 그분들이 마다하지 않으신다면 말이지요. 그러면 저는 페가수스 준마에, 아니 저 위대한 콤플루툼에서 그리 멀지 않은 술레마의 큰 언덕에 오늘날까지도 마법에 걸려 누워 있는 그 유명한 무어인 무사라케를 태우던 얼룩말이나 군마에 올라탄 셈 치겠습니다.\""]],[["“Nor even that will I consent to, señor licentiate,”","\"학사 나리, 그것조차도 나는 동의할 수 없소이다.\""],["answered Don Quixote, “and I know it will be the good pleasure of my lady the princess, out of love for me, to order her squire to give up the saddle of his mule to your worship, and he can sit behind if the beast will bear it.”","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그리고 나의 귀부인이신 공주께서 나를 향한 사랑으로, 자신의 종자에게 그 노새의 안장을 나리께 내드리라 기꺼이 명하시리라는 것을 나는 알고 있으며, 그 종자는 짐승이 견뎌 낸다면 뒤에 앉아 갈 수 있을 것이오.\""]],[["“It will, I am sure,”","\"그리하고말고요.\" 공주가 말했다."],["said the princess, “and I am sure, too, that I need not order my squire, for he is too courteous and considerate to allow a Churchman to go on foot when he might be mounted.”","\"그리고 제가 굳이 제 종자에게 명할 필요도 없으리라 봅니다. 그는 너무도 정중하고 사려 깊은 자라, 자기가 탈 수 있는데도 성직자를 걸어가게 두지는 않을 테니까요.\""]],[["“That he is,”","\"그렇고말고요.\""],["said the barber, and at once alighting, he offered his saddle to the curate, who accepted it without much entreaty; but unfortunately as the barber was mounting behind, the mule, being as it happened a hired one, which is the same thing as saying ill-conditioned, lifted its hind hoofs and let fly a couple of kicks in the air, which would have made Master Nicholas wish his expedition in quest of Don Quixote at the devil had they caught him on the breast or head.","이발사가 말하고는 곧장 내려서서 신부에게 자기 안장을 내주었고, 신부는 별다른 간청 없이 그것을 받아들였다. 그러나 불행히도 이발사가 뒤에 올라타려는 참에, 마침 빌려 온 노새였으니 — 이는 곧 성질이 못된 놈이라는 말과 같은데 — 그놈이 뒷발굽을 쳐들어 허공에 두어 차례 발길질을 해 댔다."],["As it was, they so took him by surprise that he came to the ground, giving so little heed to his beard that it fell off, and all he could do when he found himself without it was to cover his face hastily with both his hands and moan that his teeth were knocked out.","만일 그것이 가슴이나 머리를 맞혔더라면 니콜라스 영감은 돈키호테를 찾아 나선 이 원정을 악마에게나 줘 버렸으면 싶었을 것이다. 어찌 됐든 그것이 그를 어찌나 불시에 덮쳤던지 그는 땅바닥에 나동그라졌고, 자기 수염에는 거의 신경도 쓰지 못하는 바람에 수염이 떨어져 버렸다."],["Don Quixote when he saw all that bundle of beard detached, without jaws or blood, from the face of the fallen squire, exclaimed:","수염 없는 자신을 발견하고 그가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 황급히 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 이빨이 부러졌다고 신음하는 것뿐이었다. 턱뼈도 피도 없이 그 수염 뭉치가 넘어진 종자의 얼굴에서 떨어져 나간 것을 본 돈키호테가 외쳤다."]],[["“By the living God, but this is a great miracle!","\"살아 계신 하느님께 맹세코, 이건 참으로 대단한 기적이로다!"],["it has knocked off and plucked away the beard from his face as if it had been shaved off designedly.”","마치 일부러 면도해 낸 것처럼 그의 얼굴에서 수염을 떼어 내 뽑아 버렸으니 말이다.\""]],[["The curate, seeing the danger of discovery that threatened his scheme, at once pounced upon the beard and hastened with it to where Master Nicholas lay, still uttering moans, and drawing his head to his breast had it on in an instant, muttering over him some words which he said were a certain special charm for sticking on beards, as they would see; and as soon as he had it fixed he left him, and the squire appeared well bearded and whole as before, whereat Don Quixote was beyond measure astonished, and begged the curate to teach him that charm when he had an opportunity, as he was persuaded its virtue must extend beyond the sticking on of beards, for it was clear that where the beard had been stripped off the flesh must have remained torn and lacerated, and when it could heal all that it must be good for more than beards.","신부는 자기 계략을 위협하는 발각의 위험을 보고, 곧장 수염에 달려들어 그것을 들고서 여전히 신음을 내뱉고 있는 니콜라스 영감이 누운 곳으로 서둘러 갔다. 그러고는 그의 머리를 자기 가슴께로 끌어당겨 순식간에 수염을 도로 붙이며, 그 위로 무어라 몇 마디를 중얼거렸는데, 그것은 곧 보게 되겠지만 수염을 붙이는 어떤 특별한 주문이라는 것이었다. 수염을 고정하자마자 그는 그를 떠났고, 종자는 전과 다름없이 수염이 멀쩡하고 온전한 모습으로 나타났다. 이에 돈키호테는 이루 말할 수 없이 놀라며, 기회가 닿으면 그 주문을 자기에게 가르쳐 달라고 신부에게 청했으니, 그 효험이 수염을 붙이는 일을 넘어설 것이 틀림없다고 확신했기 때문이다. 수염이 떨어져 나간 곳에는 살이 찢기고 째진 채로 남아 있었을 것이 분명한데, 그 모든 것을 낫게 할 수 있다면 그것은 수염 이상의 일에도 쓸모가 있을 것이 분명하다는 것이었다."]],[["“And so it is,”","\"과연 그렇습니다.\""],["said the curate, and he promised to teach it to him on the first opportunity.","신부가 말하고는, 첫 기회가 닿는 대로 그것을 가르쳐 주겠노라 약속했다."],["They then agreed that for the present the curate should mount, and that the three should ride by turns until they reached the inn, which might be about six leagues from where they were.","그러고는 우선은 신부가 말에 타고, 세 사람이 객줏집에 이를 때까지 번갈아 타고 가기로 합의했으니, 그 객줏집은 그들이 있는 곳에서 한 여섯 레과쯤 떨어져 있을 듯했다."]],[["Three then being mounted, that is to say, Don Quixote, the princess, and the curate, and three on foot, Cardenio, the barber, and Sancho Panza, Don Quixote said to the damsel:","이렇게 세 사람, 곧 돈키호테와 공주와 신부가 말에 타고, 세 사람, 곧 카르데니오와 이발사와 산초 판사가 걸어서 가게 되자, 돈키호테가 처녀에게 말했다."]],[["“Let your highness, lady, lead on whithersoever is most pleasing to you;”","\"귀부인이여, 그대께서 가장 마음에 드는 곳이 어디든 그리로 앞장서 인도하시오.\""],["but before she could answer the licentiate said:","그러나 그녀가 미처 대답하기도 전에 학사가 말했다."]],[["“Towards what kingdom would your ladyship direct our course?","\"마님께서는 우리의 행로를 어느 왕국으로 이끄시려는지요?"],["Is it perchance towards that of Micomicon?","혹여 미코미콘 왕국이 아닙니까? 그곳이 분명할 것이외다."],["It must be, or else I know little about kingdoms.”","아니라면 내가 왕국에 관해 아는 바가 거의 없는 셈이지요.\""]],[["She, being ready on all points, understood that she was to answer “Yes,”","매사에 빈틈없던 그녀는, 자기가 \"예\"라고 답해야 함을 알아차리고 이렇게 말했다."],["so she said “Yes, señor, my way lies towards that kingdom.”","\"예, 나리, 제 갈 길은 그 왕국을 향해 있습니다.\""]],[["“In that case,” said the curate, “we must pass right through my village, and there your worship will take the road to Cartagena, where you will be able to embark, fortune favouring; and if the wind be fair and the sea smooth and tranquil, in somewhat less than nine years you may come in sight of the great lake Meona, I mean Meotides, which is little more than a hundred days’ journey this side of your highness’s kingdom.”","\"그렇다면,\" 신부가 말했다. \"우리는 내 마을을 곧장 지나가야 할 것이며, 거기서 기사님께서는 카르타헤나로 가는 길을 잡으셔야 합니다. 운만 따라 준다면 거기서 배에 오르실 수 있을 테니까요. 그리고 바람이 순조롭고 바다가 잔잔하고 고요하다면, 한 구 년 남짓이면 위대한 메오나 호수, 아니 메오티데스 호수가 보이는 곳에 이르실 수 있을 것입니다. 그곳은 마님의 왕국에서 이쪽으로 백 일 남짓의 여정밖에 안 되는 곳이지요.\""]],[["“Your worship is mistaken, señor,” said she; “for it is not two years since I set out from it, and though I never had good weather, nevertheless I am here to behold what I so longed for, and that is my lord Don Quixote of La Mancha, whose fame came to my ears as soon as I set foot in Spain and impelled me to go in search of him, to commend myself to his courtesy, and entrust the justice of my cause to the might of his invincible arm.”","\"나리, 잘못 아셨습니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 그곳을 떠난 지 두 해도 채 되지 않았으니까요. 한 번도 날씨가 좋았던 적은 없었지만, 그래도 저는 이렇게 제가 그토록 간절히 바라던 것을 보러 여기에 와 있습니다. 그것은 바로 저의 주인이신 라만차의 돈키호테 님으로, 제가 스페인 땅에 발을 들여놓자마자 그분의 명성이 제 귀에 들려와 저로 하여금 그분을 찾아 나서게 했지요. 그분의 정중함에 제 자신을 의탁하고, 제 대의의 정의를 그분의 무적의 팔의 힘에 맡기고자 말입니다.\""]],[["“Enough; no more praise,”","\"그만하시오, 더는 칭찬하지 마시오.\" 돈키호테가 이에 말했다."],["said Don Quixote at this, “for I hate all flattery; and though this may not be so, still language of the kind is offensive to my chaste ears.","\"나는 온갖 아첨을 싫어하니, 이것이 설령 아첨은 아니라 할지라도, 그런 식의 말은 나의 정결한 귀에 거슬리오. 다만 한마디 하리다, 귀부인이여."],["I will only say, señora, that whether it has might or not, that which it may or may not have shall be devoted to your service even to death; and now, leaving this to its proper season, I would ask the señor licentiate to tell me what it is that has brought him into these parts, alone, unattended, and so lightly clad that I am filled with amazement.”","그 팔에 힘이 있든 없든, 그것이 지녔거나 지니지 못한 것은 죽음에 이르도록 그대를 섬기는 데 바쳐질 것이오. 자, 이 일은 마땅한 때로 미뤄 두고, 나는 학사 나리께서 무엇이 그대를 이런 곳으로, 그것도 혼자서, 시종도 없이, 내가 놀라움을 금치 못할 만큼 가벼운 차림으로 데려왔는지 일러 주시기를 청하오.\""]],[["“I will answer that briefly,” replied the curate; “you must know then, Señor Don Quixote, that Master Nicholas, our friend and barber, and I were going to Seville to receive some money that a relative of mine who went to the Indies many years ago had sent me, and not such a small sum but that it was over sixty thousand pieces of eight, full weight, which is something; and passing by this place yesterday we were attacked by four footpads, who stripped us even to our beards, and them they stripped off so that the barber found it necessary to put on a false one, and even this young man here”--pointing to Cardenio--“they completely transformed. But the best of it is, the story goes in the neighbourhood that those who attacked us belong to a number of galley slaves who, they say, were set free almost on the very same spot by a man of such valour that, in spite of the commissary and of the guards, he released the whole of them; and beyond all doubt he must have been out of his senses, or he must be as great a scoundrel as they, or some man without heart or conscience to let the wolf loose among the sheep, the fox among the hens, the fly among the honey. He has defrauded justice, and opposed his king and lawful master, for he opposed his just commands; he has, I say, robbed the galleys of their feet, stirred up the Holy Brotherhood which for many years past has been quiet, and, lastly, has done a deed by which his soul may be lost without any gain to his body.” Sancho had told the curate and the barber of the adventure of the galley slaves, which, so much to his glory, his master had achieved, and hence the curate in alluding to it made the most of it to see what would be said or done by Don Quixote; who changed colour at every word, not daring to say that it was he who had been the liberator of those worthy people. “These, then,” said the curate, “were they who robbed us; and God in his mercy pardon him who would not let them go to the punishment they deserved.”","\"그것은 간단히 답하지요.\" 신부가 대답했다. \"돈키호테 님, 그러니까 우리의 벗이자 이발사인 니콜라스 영감과 제가 세비야로 가던 길이었습니다. 여러 해 전 인도 제도로 건너간 제 친척이 제게 보낸 돈을 받으러 가는 길이었지요. 그것도 적은 액수가 아니라, 묵직한 무게의 팔레알 은화로 육만 닢이 넘는 돈이었으니, 대단한 액수지요. 그런데 어제 이곳을 지나다가 노상강도 넷에게 습격을 당해, 그놈들이 우리 수염까지 죄다 벗겨 갔습니다. 수염을 어찌나 깎아 가 버렸던지 이발사는 가짜 수염을 붙여야 할 지경이었고, 여기 이 젊은이까지도\" — 카르데니오를 가리키며 — \"그놈들이 완전히 딴사람으로 만들어 놓았답니다. 그런데 무엇보다 기막힌 것은, 이 근방에 도는 소문에 따르면, 우리를 습격한 자들이 갤리선 노예 무리에 속한 자들이라는 겁니다. 그놈들은 바로 이 자리 근처에서, 호송관과 호위병들이 있는데도 어찌나 용맹한 자였는지 그 사내가 그놈들을 죄다 풀어 주었다고 하더군요. 그자는 의심할 바 없이 제정신이 아니었거나, 아니면 그놈들 못지않은 악당이었거나, 아니면 양 떼 사이에 늑대를, 닭들 사이에 여우를, 꿀단지에 파리를 풀어놓을 만큼 가슴도 양심도 없는 자가 분명합니다. 그자는 정의를 기만하고, 자기 임금이자 정당한 주군께 맞섰으니, 그분의 정당한 명령에 대항한 것이지요. 다시 말해 그자는 갤리선에서 그 다리를 빼앗고, 여러 해 동안 잠잠했던 성스러운 형제단을 들쑤셔 놓았으며, 끝내는 제 몸에는 아무런 이득도 없이 제 영혼만 잃을 수도 있는 짓을 저지른 것입니다.\" 산초가 신부와 이발사에게 갤리선 노예들의 모험, 곧 자기 주인이 그토록 영광스럽게 이뤄 낸 그 일을 일러 주었던 터라, 신부는 그것을 언급하면서 돈키호테가 무어라 말하거나 어찌 행동하는지 보려고 일부러 잔뜩 부풀린 것이었다. 그러자 돈키호테는 한마디 한마디마다 안색이 변했으나, 그 갸륵한 자들을 풀어 준 자가 바로 자신이라고는 감히 말하지 못했다. \"그러니까,\" 신부가 말했다. \"우리를 턴 자들이 바로 그놈들이었던 셈이지요. 마땅히 받아야 할 벌을 받으러 가도록 그놈들을 내버려 두지 않은 자를, 하느님께서 자비로이 용서해 주시기를.\" * 팔레알 은화로 육만 닢: 원문은 \"pieces of eight\", 곧 당대 스페인의 8레알짜리 은화를 가리킨다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXX. WHICH TREATS OF ADDRESS DISPLAYED BY THE FAIR DOROTHEA, WITH OTHER MATTERS PLEASANT AND AMUSING","# CHAPTER XXX."]],[["The curate had hardly ceased speaking, when Sancho said, “In faith, then, señor licentiate, he who did that deed was my master; and it was not for want of my telling him beforehand and warning him to mind what he was about, and that it was a sin to set them at liberty, as they were all on the march there because they were special scoundrels.”","신부가 말을 마치기가 무섭게 산초가 입을 열었다. \"그렇다면 솔직히 말씀드리는데, 신부님, 그 짓을 한 분이 바로 제 주인 나리입니다요. 미리 말씀드리고 무슨 짓을 하시는지 조심하시라고, 그자들을 풀어 주는 건 죄라고, 다들 워낙 못된 악당들이라 그래서 끌려가던 길이라고 일러 드리지 않은 것도 아닙니다요.\""]],[["“Blockhead!”","\"이 멍청아.\""],["said Don Quixote at this, “it is no business or concern of knights-errant to inquire whether any persons in affliction, in chains, or oppressed that they may meet on the high roads go that way and suffer as they do because of their faults or because of their misfortunes.","이 말에 돈키호테가 끼어들었다. \"큰길에서 마주치는, 고통받거나 사슬에 묶이거나 억눌린 자들이 자기 죄 때문에 그리 가며 그런 고초를 겪는지, 아니면 불운 때문인지를 따져 묻는 것은 편력 기사가 할 일도 관여할 바도 아니니라."],["It only concerns them to aid them as persons in need of help, having regard to their sufferings and not to their rascalities.","기사가 마음 쓸 일이라고는 그들의 악행이 아니라 그들의 고난을 살펴, 도움이 필요한 자로서 그들을 돕는 것뿐이다."],["I encountered a chaplet or string of miserable and unfortunate people, and did for them what my sense of duty demands of me, and as for the rest be that as it may; and whoever takes objection to it, saving the sacred dignity of the señor licentiate and his honoured person, I say he knows little about chivalry and lies like a whoreson villain, and this I will give him to know to the fullest extent with my sword;”","나는 비참하고 불운한 자들의 묵주 알처럼 줄지어 엮인 무리를 만났고, 내 의무감이 요구하는 바를 그들에게 행했으며, 그 나머지야 어찌 되었든 상관없는 일이니라. 그리고 누구든 이에 트집을 잡는 자가 있다면, 신부님의 거룩한 위엄과 그 존귀한 신분을 제외하고는, 나는 그자가 기사도에 관해 아는 바가 거의 없으며 매춘부의 자식 같은 악당처럼 거짓을 지껄인다고 말하겠으며, 이를 내 검으로 끝까지 똑똑히 알게 해 주리라.\""],["and so saying he settled himself in his stirrups and pressed down his morion; for the barber’s basin, which according to him was Mambrino’s helmet, he carried hanging at the saddle-bow until he could repair the damage done to it by the galley slaves.","이렇게 말하면서 그는 등자를 굳게 딛고 투구를 눌러썼다. 그의 말로는 맘브리노의 투구라는 그 이발사의 대야는, 갤리선 노예들이 그것에 입힌 손상을 고칠 수 있을 때까지 안장 앞머리에 매달아 두고 있었던 것이다."]],[["Dorothea, who was shrewd and sprightly, and by this time thoroughly understood Don Quixote’s crazy turn, and that all except Sancho Panza were making game of him, not to be behind the rest said to him, on observing his irritation, “Sir Knight, remember the boon you have promised me, and that in accordance with it you must not engage in any other adventure, be it ever so pressing; calm yourself, for if the licentiate had known that the galley slaves had been set free by that unconquered arm he would have stopped his mouth thrice over, or even bitten his tongue three times before he would have said a word that tended towards disrespect of your worship.”","영리하고 발랄한 도로테아는 이때쯤 돈키호테의 미친 기질을 완전히 꿰뚫고 있었으며, 산초 판사를 제외한 모두가 그를 놀려 먹고 있다는 것도 알고 있었기에, 남들에게 뒤지지 않으려고 그가 짜증을 내는 것을 보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"기사님, 제게 약속하신 은혜를 기억하시고, 그에 따라 아무리 다급한 일이라 할지라도 다른 모험에는 관여하지 않으셔야 합니다. 마음을 가라앉히세요. 신부님께서 그 무적의 팔이 갤리선 노예들을 풀어 주었다는 것을 아셨더라면, 기사님께 무례가 될 만한 말씀을 입에 올리기 전에 세 번이고 입을 다무셨을 것이며, 아니 혀를 세 번이라도 깨무셨을 거예요.\""]],[["“That I swear heartily,”","\"진심으로 맹세하건대 그렇습니다.\""],["said the curate, “and I would have even plucked off a moustache.”","신부가 말했다. \"콧수염이라도 한 가닥 뽑아 버렸을 겁니다.\""]],[["“I will hold my peace, señora,”","\"잠자코 있으리다, 부인.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “and I will curb the natural anger that had arisen in my breast, and will proceed in peace and quietness until I have fulfilled my promise; but in return for this consideration I entreat you to tell me, if you have no objection to do so, what is the nature of your trouble, and how many, who, and what are the persons of whom I am to require due satisfaction, and on whom I am to take vengeance on your behalf?”","\"내 가슴속에 일었던 천성적인 분노를 억누르고, 약속을 이행할 때까지 평화롭고 조용히 나아가리다. 다만 이 배려에 대한 보답으로 부탁드리니, 거리낌이 없으시다면 부인의 고난이 어떠한 것인지, 그리고 내가 부인을 대신하여 마땅한 보상을 요구하고 복수를 행해야 할 자들이 몇이며, 누구이며, 어떤 자들인지를 말씀해 주십시오.\""]],[["“That I will do with all my heart,”","\"기꺼이 그리하지요.\""],["replied Dorothea, “if it will not be wearisome to you to hear of miseries and misfortunes.”","도로테아가 대답했다. \"비참한 일과 불운한 일들을 듣는 것이 기사님께 지루하지만 않다면요.\""]],[["“It will not be wearisome, señora,”","\"지루할 리가 있겠습니까, 부인.\" 돈키호테가 말하자, 도로테아가 대답했다."],["said Don Quixote; to which Dorothea replied, “Well, if that be so, give me your attention.”","\"그렇다면, 제 말에 귀 기울여 주세요.\" 그녀가 이렇게 말하자마자 카르데니오와 이발사가 그녀 곁으로 바짝 다가들었으니, 재치 넘치는 도로테아가 자신을 위해 어떤 이야기를 지어낼지 듣고 싶어 안달이 났던 것이다."],["As soon as she said this, Cardenio and the barber drew close to her side, eager to hear what sort of story the quick-witted Dorothea would invent for herself; and Sancho did the same, for he was as much taken in by her as his master; and she having settled herself comfortably in the saddle, and with the help of coughing and other preliminaries taken time to think, began with great sprightliness of manner in this fashion.","산초도 똑같이 다가들었으니, 그도 제 주인 못지않게 그녀에게 속아 넘어가 있었던 까닭이다. 그녀는 안장에 편안히 자리를 잡고, 헛기침과 그 밖의 사전 동작들로 생각할 틈을 벌어 두고는, 매우 발랄한 태도로 이런 식으로 이야기를 시작했다."]],[["“First of all, I would have you know, sirs, that my name is-” and here she stopped for a moment, for she forgot the name the curate had given her; but he came to her relief, seeing what her difficulty was, and said, “It is no wonder, señora, that your highness should be confused and embarrassed in telling the tale of your misfortunes; for such afflictions often have the effect of depriving the sufferers of memory, so that they do not even remember their own names, as is the case now with your ladyship, who has forgotten that she is called the Princess Micomicona, lawful heiress of the great kingdom of Micomicon; and with this cue your highness may now recall to your sorrowful recollection all you may wish to tell us.”","\"우선 여러분께서 알아 두셔야 할 것은, 제 이름이……\" 여기서 그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추었으니, 신부가 지어 준 이름을 잊어버렸던 것이다. 그러나 신부가 그녀의 곤경을 알아채고 도와주러 나서서 말했다. \"부인, 공주님께서 불운한 사연을 이야기하시며 당황하고 혼란스러워하시는 것도 무리가 아닙니다. 그런 고통은 흔히 겪는 이에게서 기억을 앗아 가, 제 이름조차 떠올리지 못하게 만드는 법이지요. 지금 공주님께서도 그러하시니, 위대한 미코미콘 왕국의 정당한 상속녀이신 미코미코나 공주라 불리신다는 것을 잊으셨군요. 그러나 이 실마리를 잡으시면 이제 공주님께서는 저희에게 들려주고자 하시는 모든 것을 그 슬픈 기억 속에서 다시 떠올리실 수 있을 것입니다.\""]],[["“That is the truth,” said the damsel; “but I think from this on I shall have no need of any prompting, and I shall bring my true story safe into port, and here it is. The king my father, who was called Tinacrio the Sapient, was very learned in what they call magic arts, and became aware by his craft that my mother, who was called Queen Jaramilla, was to die before he did, and that soon after he too was to depart this life, and I was to be left an orphan without father or mother. But all this, he declared, did not so much grieve or distress him as his certain knowledge that a prodigious giant, the lord of a great island close to our kingdom, Pandafilando of the Scowl by name--for it is averred that, though his eyes are properly placed and straight, he always looks askew as if he squinted, and this he does out of malignity, to strike fear and terror into those he looks at--that he knew, I say, that this giant on becoming aware of my orphan condition would overrun my kingdom with a mighty force and strip me of all, not leaving me even a small village to shelter me; but that I could avoid all this ruin and misfortune if I were willing to marry him; however, as far as he could see, he never expected that I would consent to a marriage so unequal; and he said no more than the truth in this, for it has never entered my mind to marry that giant, or any other, let him be ever so great or enormous. My father said, too, that when he was dead, and I saw Pandafilando about to invade my kingdom, I was not to wait and attempt to defend myself, for that would be destructive to me, but that I should leave the kingdom entirely open to him if I wished to avoid the death and total destruction of my good and loyal vassals, for there would be no possibility of defending myself against the giant’s devilish power; and that I should at once with some of my followers set out for Spain, where I should obtain relief in my distress on finding a certain knight-errant whose fame by that time would extend over the whole kingdom, and who would be called, if I remember rightly, Don Azote or Don Gigote.”","\"맞아요.\" 처녀가 말했다. \"하지만 이제부터는 일러 주실 필요가 없을 것 같아요. 제 진짜 이야기를 무사히 항구로 끌고 들어갈 테니까요. 자, 이렇답니다. 제 아버지이신 왕께서는 현자 티나크리오라 불리셨고, 사람들이 마법이라 부르는 것에 매우 능하셔서, 그 재주로 제 어머니이신 하라미야 왕비께서 당신보다 먼저 돌아가시리라는 것과, 그 후 곧 당신께서도 이 세상을 떠나, 제가 아버지도 어머니도 없는 고아가 되리라는 것을 아셨습니다. 하지만 이 모든 것보다도 아버지를 더 슬프게 하고 괴롭힌 것은, 우리 왕국에 가까이 있는 큰 섬의 영주인 어마어마한 거인 하나가, 이름하여 찌푸린 얼굴의 판다필란도라는 자가―들리는 말로는, 그의 눈은 제대로 똑바로 박혀 있는데도 늘 사팔뜨기처럼 비뚜름하게 본다는데, 이는 악의에서 그러는 것으로, 바라보는 이들에게 두려움과 공포를 심으려는 것이랍니다―말하자면 그 거인이 제가 고아가 된 것을 알게 되면 막강한 병력으로 제 왕국을 짓밟고 모든 것을 빼앗아, 제가 몸을 의지할 작은 마을 하나조차 남겨 두지 않으리라는 것을 아버지께서 확실히 아셨다는 점이었습니다. 다만 제가 그자와 결혼할 마음만 있다면 이 모든 파멸과 불운을 피할 수 있다고 하셨지요. 그렇지만 아버지께서 보시기에, 제가 그토록 어울리지 않는 결혼에 동의하리라고는 결코 기대하지 않으셨습니다. 이 점에서 아버지는 진실 그대로를 말씀하셨으니, 저는 그 거인이든 다른 누구든, 아무리 위대하고 거대한 자라 할지라도 그와 결혼할 생각은 한 번도 품어 본 적이 없으니까요. 아버지께서는 또한 당신께서 돌아가신 뒤 판다필란도가 제 왕국을 침략하려는 것을 제가 보거든, 기다리거나 스스로를 지키려 들지 말라고 하셨습니다. 그것은 제게 파멸을 가져올 뿐이니, 선량하고 충실한 신하들의 죽음과 전멸을 피하고자 한다면 왕국을 거인에게 온전히 내주라고 하셨지요. 거인의 악마 같은 힘에 맞서 스스로를 지킬 가능성은 없을 것이기 때문입니다. 그리고 저는 즉시 몇몇 추종자를 데리고 스페인으로 떠나야 하며, 그곳에서 한 편력 기사를 만나 제 고난에서 구원을 얻으리라고 하셨습니다. 그 무렵이면 그의 명성이 온 왕국에 퍼져 있을 것이며, 제 기억이 옳다면 그는 돈 아소테, 아니면 돈 히고테라 불릴 것이라 하셨어요.\""]],[["“‘Don Quixote,’ he must have said, señora,”","\"'돈키호테'라 하셨을 겁니다요, 부인.\""],["observed Sancho at this, “otherwise called the Knight of the Rueful Countenance.”","이때 산초가 끼어들며 말했다. \"다른 이름으로는 슬픈 몰골의 기사라고도 하지요.\""]],[["“That is it,”","\"그거예요.\""],["said Dorothea; “he said, moreover, that he would be tall of stature and lank featured; and that on his right side under the left shoulder, or thereabouts, he would have a grey mole with hairs like bristles.”","도로테아가 말했다. \"게다가 아버지께서는 그가 키가 크고 얼굴이 홀쭉할 것이며, 오른쪽 옆구리, 왼쪽 어깨 아래쯤에 뻣뻣한 털이 난 잿빛 점이 있을 것이라고도 하셨어요.\""]],[["On hearing this, Don Quixote said to his squire, “Here, Sancho my son, bear a hand and help me to strip, for I want to see if I am the knight that sage king foretold.”","이를 듣자 돈키호테가 종자에게 말했다. \"이리 오너라, 산초 내 아들아, 손을 빌려 옷 벗는 것을 좀 도와다오. 내가 그 현명한 왕께서 예언하신 기사인지 보고 싶구나.\""]],[["“What does your worship want to strip for?”","\"옷은 무엇 하러 벗으시려고요?\""],["said Dorothea.","도로테아가 물었다."]],[["“To see if I have that mole your father spoke of,”","\"부인의 부친께서 말씀하신 그 점이 내게 있는지 보려는 것이오.\""],["answered Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 대답했다."]],[["“There is no occasion to strip,”","\"옷을 벗으실 필요 없습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho; “for I know your worship has just such a mole on the middle of your backbone, which is the mark of a strong man.”","\"나리 등뼈 한가운데에 바로 그런 점이 있다는 걸 제가 아니까요. 그게 바로 힘센 사내의 표시지요.\""]],[["“That is enough,” said Dorothea, “for with friends we must not look too closely into trifles; and whether it be on the shoulder or on the backbone matters little; it is enough if there is a mole, be it where it may, for it is all the same flesh; no doubt my good father hit the truth in every particular, and I have made a lucky hit in commending myself to Don Quixote; for he is the one my father spoke of, as the features of his countenance correspond with those assigned to this knight by that wide fame he has acquired not only in Spain but in all La Mancha; for I had scarcely landed at Osuna when I heard such accounts of his achievements, that at once my heart told me he was the very one I had come in search of.”","\"그것으로 충분합니다.\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"벗들 사이에 사소한 것을 너무 꼼꼼히 따져서는 안 되지요. 그것이 어깨에 있든 등뼈에 있든 별 상관없어요. 어디에 있든 점만 있으면 충분하니, 다 같은 살이니까요. 제 선량하신 아버지께서는 모든 점에서 진실을 맞히셨고, 저 역시 돈키호테 님께 제 몸을 의탁하기로 한 것은 운이 좋았던 일이지요. 이분이야말로 아버지께서 말씀하신 그분이니, 그 얼굴의 생김새가 스페인뿐 아니라 온 라만차에 떨치신 그 드넓은 명성으로 이 기사께 부여된 특징들과 들어맞으니까요. 제가 오수나에 막 상륙하자마자 그분의 위업에 관한 이야기를 어찌나 많이 들었던지, 그 즉시 제 마음이 이분이 바로 제가 찾아온 그분이라고 일러 주었답니다.\""]],[["“But how did you land at Osuna, señora,”","\"하지만 어찌 오수나에 상륙하셨소, 부인?\""],["asked Don Quixote, “when it is not a seaport?”","돈키호테가 물었다. \"그곳은 항구가 아닌데 말이오.\""]],[["But before Dorothea could reply the curate anticipated her, saying, “The princess meant to say that after she had landed at Malaga the first place where she heard of your worship was Osuna.”","그러나 도로테아가 대답하기도 전에 신부가 앞질러 말했다. \"공주님께서 하시려던 말씀은, 말라가에 상륙하신 뒤 기사님에 관한 소문을 처음 들으신 곳이 오수나였다는 뜻입니다.\""]],[["“That is what I meant to say,”","\"그게 제가 하려던 말이에요.\""],["said Dorothea.","도로테아가 말했다."]],[["“And that would be only natural,”","\"그렇다면 지극히 당연한 일이지요.\""],["said the curate.","신부가 말했다."],["“Will your majesty please proceed?”","\"공주님, 계속하시겠습니까?\""]],[["“There is no more to add,” said Dorothea, “save that in finding Don Quixote I have had such good fortune, that I already reckon and regard myself queen and mistress of my entire dominions, since of his courtesy and magnanimity he has granted me the boon of accompanying me whithersoever I may conduct him, which will be only to bring him face to face with Pandafilando of the Scowl, that he may slay him and restore to me what has been unjustly usurped by him: for all this must come to pass satisfactorily since my good father Tinacrio the Sapient foretold it, who likewise left it declared in writing in Chaldee or Greek characters (for I cannot read them), that if this predicted knight, after having cut the giant’s throat, should be disposed to marry me I was to offer myself at once without demur as his lawful wife, and yield him possession of my kingdom together with my person.”","\"더 보탤 것은 없어요.\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"다만 돈키호테 님을 만난 것이 제게 얼마나 큰 행운이었던지, 저는 이미 제 영토 전체의 여왕이자 주인이 된 것이나 다름없다 여기고 있다는 것뿐이에요. 그분께서 예의와 아량으로, 제가 어디로 이끌든 저와 동행해 주시는 은혜를 베푸셨으니까요. 제가 그분을 이끌 곳이라고는 오직 찌푸린 얼굴의 판다필란도와 정면으로 마주하게 하여, 그를 죽이고 그가 부당하게 빼앗은 것을 제게 되찾아 주시게 하는 것뿐입니다. 이 모든 일은 만족스럽게 이루어질 것이니, 제 선량하신 아버지 현자 티나크리오께서 그리 예언하셨기 때문이지요. 아버지께서는 또한 칼데아 문자인지 그리스 문자인지로(저는 읽을 줄 모르니까요) 글로 남겨 선언하시기를, 이 예언된 기사가 거인의 목을 벤 후에 저와 결혼할 마음이 있거든, 저는 망설임 없이 즉시 그분의 정당한 아내가 되겠노라 제 몸을 바치고, 제 몸과 함께 제 왕국의 소유권을 그분께 넘겨 드려야 한다고 하셨습니다.\""]],[["“What thinkest thou now, friend Sancho?”","\"이제 어떠하냐, 친구 산초야?\""],["said Don Quixote at this.","이때 돈키호테가 말했다."],["“Hearest thou that?","\"그 말을 들었느냐?"],["Did I not tell thee so?","내가 그리 말하지 않았느냐?"],["See how we have already got a kingdom to govern and a queen to marry!”","보아라, 우리는 벌써 다스릴 왕국과 혼인할 여왕을 얻지 않았느냐!\""]],[["“On my oath it is so,”","\"맹세코 그렇습니다요.\""],["said Sancho; “and foul fortune to him who won’t marry after slitting Señor Pandahilado’s windpipe!","산초가 말했다. \"판다힐라도 나리의 숨통을 끊어 놓고도 결혼하지 않는다면 그런 자한테는 빌어먹을 불운이 따라야지요!"],["And then, how ill-favoured the queen is!","게다가 저 여왕님이 얼마나 박색이신지!"],["I wish the fleas in my bed were that sort!”","제 침대의 벼룩들도 저런 종류라면 좋겠네요!\""],["And so saying he cut a couple of capers in the air with every sign of extreme satisfaction, and then ran to seize the bridle of Dorothea’s mule, and checking it fell on his knees before her, begging her to give him her hand to kiss in token of his acknowledgment of her as his queen and mistress.","그러고는 더할 나위 없이 만족스러운 기색으로 공중에 두어 번 펄쩍 뛰어오르더니, 도로테아의 노새 고삐를 붙잡으러 달려가, 노새를 멈추어 세우고는 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 그녀를 제 여왕이자 주인으로 인정한다는 표시로 입 맞출 손을 달라고 청했다."],["Which of the bystanders could have helped laughing to see the madness of the master and the simplicity of the servant?","주인의 광기와 하인의 순박함을 보고 그 자리에 있던 누군들 웃음을 참을 수 있었으랴?"],["Dorothea therefore gave her hand, and promised to make him a great lord in her kingdom, when Heaven should be so good as to permit her to recover and enjoy it, for which Sancho returned thanks in words that set them all laughing again.","그리하여 도로테아는 손을 내밀고, 하늘이 은혜를 베풀어 자신이 왕국을 되찾고 누리게 해 주신다면 산초를 자기 왕국의 대귀족으로 만들어 주겠다고 약속했으며, 산초는 좌중을 또다시 웃음 짓게 만드는 말로 감사를 표했다."]],[["“This, sirs,” continued Dorothea, “is my story; it only remains to tell you that of all the attendants I took with me from my kingdom I have none left except this well-bearded squire, for all were drowned in a great tempest we encountered when in sight of port; and he and I came to land on a couple of planks as if by a miracle; and indeed the whole course of my life is a miracle and a mystery as you may have observed; and if I have been over minute in any respect or not as precise as I ought, let it be accounted for by what the licentiate said at the beginning of my tale, that constant and excessive troubles deprive the sufferers of their memory.”","\"여러분, 이것이.\" 도로테아가 말을 이었다. \"제 이야기예요. 이제 한 가지만 말씀드리면 됩니다. 제 왕국에서 데리고 온 시종들 가운데, 이 수염 멋진 종자 한 사람을 빼고는 아무도 남지 않았다는 거예요. 항구가 눈앞에 보이는 곳에서 큰 폭풍을 만나 모두 물에 빠져 죽었거든요. 그와 저는 기적이라도 일어난 듯 널빤지 두 장에 의지해 뭍에 닿았답니다. 사실 제 인생 전체가 여러분께서 짐작하셨다시피 기적이자 신비랍니다. 그러니 제가 어느 대목에서 너무 자질구레했거나 마땅히 정확해야 할 만큼 정확하지 못했다 하더라도, 신부님께서 제 이야기 처음에 하셨던 말씀, 곧 끊임없고 지나친 고난은 겪는 이에게서 기억을 앗아 간다는 그 말씀으로 헤아려 주시기 바랍니다.\""]],[["“They shall not deprive me of mine, exalted and worthy princess,” said Don Quixote, “however great and unexampled those which I shall endure in your service may be; and here I confirm anew the boon I have promised you, and I swear to go with you to the end of the world until I find myself in the presence of your fierce enemy, whose haughty head I trust by the aid of my arm to cut off with the edge of this--I will not say good sword, thanks to Gines de Pasamonte who carried away mine”--(this he said between his teeth, and then continued), “and when it has been cut off and you have been put in peaceful possession of your realm it shall be left to your own decision to dispose of your person as may be most pleasing to you; for so long as my memory is occupied, my will enslaved, and my understanding enthralled by her-I say no more--it is impossible for me for a moment to contemplate marriage, even with a Phoenix.”","\"고귀하고 존귀하신 공주여, 그 고난들이 제게서 기억을 앗아 가지는 못할 것입니다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"공주를 섬기며 제가 견뎌야 할 고난이 아무리 크고 유례없는 것일지라도 말이지요. 여기서 저는 공주께 약속드린 은혜를 새로이 확약하며, 공주의 사나운 적과 마주할 때까지 세상 끝까지 공주와 함께 가리라 맹세합니다. 이 팔의 도움으로 그 오만한 목을 이―좋은 검이라고는 말하지 않겠습니다, 제 검을 빼앗아 간 히네스 데 파사몬테 덕분에 말이지요―이 검날로 잘라 내기를 바라마지않습니다.\" (이 말은 이를 악물고 했고, 이어서 말을 계속했다.) \"그리고 그 목을 베어 공주께서 평화로이 왕국을 차지하게 되신 뒤에는, 공주의 몸을 가장 마음에 드시는 대로 처분하시는 일은 공주 자신의 결정에 맡기겠습니다. 제 기억이 사로잡혀 있고, 제 의지가 노예가 되어 있으며, 제 이성이 그분에게―더는 말하지 않겠습니다―얽매여 있는 한, 설령 불사조라 할지라도 제가 한순간이나마 혼인을 생각한다는 것은 불가능하기 때문입니다.\""]],[["The last words of his master about not wanting to marry were so disagreeable to Sancho that raising his voice he exclaimed with great irritation:","혼인할 마음이 없다는 주인의 마지막 말이 산초에게는 어찌나 못마땅했던지, 그는 목소리를 높여 잔뜩 짜증을 내며 외쳤다."]],[["“By my oath, Señor Don Quixote, you are not in your right senses; for how can your worship possibly object to marrying such an exalted princess as this?","\"맹세코 말씀드리는데, 돈키호테 나리, 나리는 제정신이 아니십니다요. 어찌 이렇게 고귀하신 공주님과 혼인하기를 마다하실 수가 있단 말입니까요?"],["Do you think Fortune will offer you behind every stone such a piece of luck as is offered you now?","운명이 지금 나리께 내놓는 것 같은 이런 행운을 돌멩이마다 그 뒤에 마련해 두고 있을 거라 여기십니까요?"],["Is my lady Dulcinea fairer, perchance?","둘시네아 마님이 혹 더 어여쁘기라도 합니까요? 천만에요."],["Not she; nor half as fair; and I will even go so far as to say she does not come up to the shoe of this one here.","절반도 안 어여쁘십니다요. 아니, 더 나아가 그분은 여기 이분의 신발에도 못 미친다고까지 말씀드리겠습니다요."],["A poor chance I have of getting that county I am waiting for if your worship goes looking for dainties in the bottom of the sea.","나리께서 바다 밑바닥에서 별미를 찾으신다면, 제가 기다리고 있는 그 백작령을 얻을 가망은 영 글렀습니다요."],["In the devil’s name, marry, marry, and take this kingdom that comes to hand without any trouble, and when you are king make me a marquis or governor of a province, and for the rest let the devil take it all.”","빌어먹을, 결혼하세요, 결혼하시라고요, 아무 수고도 없이 굴러 들어온 이 왕국을 차지하세요. 그래서 왕이 되시거든 저를 후작이나 어느 지방의 총독으로 삼아 주시고, 나머지는 죄다 악마더러 가져가라 하시면 됩니다요.\""]],[["Don Quixote, when he heard such blasphemies uttered against his lady Dulcinea, could not endure it, and lifting his pike, without saying anything to Sancho or uttering a word, he gave him two such thwacks that he brought him to the ground; and had it not been that Dorothea cried out to him to spare him he would have no doubt taken his life on the spot.","돈키호테는 자기 마님 둘시네아에게 그런 모독이 퍼부어지는 것을 듣고 도저히 참을 수가 없어, 산초에게 아무 말도 한마디 내뱉지 않고 창을 들어 어찌나 호되게 두 번을 후려쳤던지 그를 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨리고 말았다. 만일 도로테아가 그에게 산초를 살려 달라고 외치지 않았더라면, 의심할 바 없이 그 자리에서 산초의 목숨을 앗았을 것이다."]],[["“Do you think,”","\"이 비열한 촌놈아.\""],["he said to him after a pause, “you scurvy clown, that you are to be always interfering with me, and that you are to be always offending and I always pardoning?","돈키호테가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 그에게 말했다. \"너는 늘 나를 가로막고, 늘 나를 거스르며, 나는 늘 너를 용서해야 한다고 여기느냐?"],["Don’t fancy it, impious scoundrel, for that beyond a doubt thou art, since thou hast set thy tongue going against the peerless Dulcinea.","그런 생각일랑 말아라, 이 불경한 악당아. 너는 의심할 여지 없이 그런 놈이니, 견줄 데 없는 둘시네아를 두고 혀를 놀려 댔으니 말이다."],["Know you not, lout, vagabond, beggar, that were it not for the might that she infuses into my arm I should not have strength enough to kill a flea?","이 무지렁이, 부랑자, 거지 같은 놈아, 그분이 내 팔에 불어넣어 주시는 힘이 아니었다면 내게는 벼룩 한 마리 죽일 힘도 없으리라는 것을 모른단 말이냐?"],["Say, scoffer with a viper’s tongue, what think you has won this kingdom and cut off this giant’s head and made you a marquis (for all this I count as already accomplished and decided), but the might of Dulcinea, employing my arm as the instrument of her achievements?","말해 보아라, 독사 같은 혀를 놀리는 조롱꾼아, 이 왕국을 손에 넣고 이 거인의 목을 베어 너를 후작으로 만든 것이(이 모든 것을 나는 이미 이루어지고 결정된 일로 친다) 그분의 위업을 이루는 도구로 내 팔을 부리시는 둘시네아의 힘이 아니고 무엇이라 여기느냐?"],["She fights in me and conquers in me, and I live and breathe in her, and owe my life and being to her.","그분이 내 안에서 싸우고 내 안에서 정복하시며, 나는 그분 안에서 살고 숨 쉬며, 내 목숨과 존재를 그분께 빚지고 있느니라."],["O whoreson scoundrel, how ungrateful you are, you see yourself raised from the dust of the earth to be a titled lord, and the return you make for so great a benefit is to speak evil of her who has conferred it upon you!”","오, 매춘부의 자식 같은 악당아, 너는 어찌 이리 배은망덕하단 말이냐. 너는 흙먼지에서 일으켜져 작위 있는 영주가 되었으면서, 그토록 큰 은혜에 대한 보답이라는 것이 그 은혜를 베푸신 분을 험담하는 것이라니!\""]],[["Sancho was not so stunned but that he heard all his master said, and rising with some degree of nimbleness he ran to place himself behind Dorothea’s palfrey, and from that position he said to his master:","산초는 그렇게까지 정신을 잃은 것은 아니어서 주인이 한 말을 죄다 알아들었고, 제법 날쌔게 일어나 도로테아의 작은 말 뒤로 달려가 몸을 숨기더니, 그 자리에서 주인에게 말했다."]],[["“Tell me, señor; if your worship is resolved not to marry this great princess, it is plain the kingdom will not be yours; and not being so, how can you bestow favours upon me?","\"말씀해 보세요, 나리. 나리께서 이 위대하신 공주님과 혼인하지 않기로 결심하셨다면, 왕국이 나리 것이 되지 못할 게 뻔합니다요."],["That is what I complain of.","그리되지 못한다면, 어떻게 제게 은혜를 베풀어 주실 수 있겠습니까요? 제가 한탄하는 게 바로 그겁니다요."],["Let your worship at any rate marry this queen, now that we have got her here as if showered down from heaven, and afterwards you may go back to my lady Dulcinea; for there must have been kings in the world who kept mistresses.","어쨌거나 하늘에서 쏟아져 내리기라도 한 듯 이렇게 여기 모셔 온 이 여왕님과 혼인이나 하시고, 그런 다음에 둘시네아 마님께 돌아가시면 되지 않습니까요. 세상에는 첩을 둔 왕들도 분명 있었으니까요."],["As to beauty, I have nothing to do with it; and if the truth is to be told, I like them both; though I have never seen the lady Dulcinea.”","미모로 말하자면 저는 상관할 바 아니고, 솔직히 말해 저는 두 분 다 마음에 듭니다요. 둘시네아 마님은 한 번도 뵌 적이 없지만요.\""]],[["“How!","\"뭐라!"],["never seen her, blasphemous traitor!”","한 번도 본 적이 없다고, 이 신성 모독을 일삼는 배신자 놈아!\""],["exclaimed Don Quixote; “hast thou not just now brought me a message from her?”","돈키호테가 외쳤다. \"방금 그분에게서 받은 전갈을 내게 가져오지 않았느냐?\""]],[["“I mean,”","\"제 말씀은.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “that I did not see her so much at my leisure that I could take particular notice of her beauty, or of her charms piecemeal; but taken in the lump I like her.”","\"그분의 미모를 일일이 눈여겨보거나 그 매력을 하나하나 살필 만큼 한가로이 뵙지는 못했다는 겁니다요. 하지만 통째로 보아서는 그분이 마음에 든단 말씀입니다요.\""]],[["“Now I forgive thee,”","\"이제 너를 용서하마.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “and do thou forgive me the injury I have done thee; for our first impulses are not in our control.”","\"그리고 너도 내가 네게 입힌 상처를 용서해 다오. 우리의 첫 충동이란 우리 마음대로 다스릴 수 있는 것이 아니니라.\""]],[["“That I see,”","\"그건 저도 알겠습니다요.\""],["replied Sancho, “and with me the wish to speak is always the first impulse, and I cannot help saying, once at any rate, what I have on the tip of my tongue.”","산초가 대답했다. \"저로 말씀드리면 말하고 싶은 마음이 늘 첫 충동이라서, 적어도 한 번은 혀끝에 걸린 것을 내뱉지 않고서는 못 배긴답니다요.\""]],[["“For all that, Sancho,”","\"그렇다 하더라도, 산초야.\""],["said Don Quixote, “take heed of what thou sayest, for the pitcher goes so often to the well--I need say no more to thee.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"네가 하는 말을 조심하여라, 물동이가 우물에 너무 자주 가다 보면―더는 네게 말하지 않으마.\""]],[["“Well, well,”","\"네, 네.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “God is in heaven, and sees all tricks, and will judge who does most harm, I in not speaking right, or your worship in not doing it.”","\"하느님께서 하늘에 계시며 온갖 꼼수를 다 보고 계시니, 누가 더 해를 끼치는지를 판단해 주실 겁니다요. 말을 바르게 못 하는 저인지, 아니면 그걸 행동으로 옮기지 못하는 나리인지를요.\""]],[["“That is enough,” said Dorothea; “run, Sancho, and kiss your lord’s hand and beg his pardon, and henceforward be more circumspect with your praise and abuse; and say nothing in disparagement of that lady Toboso, of whom I know nothing save that I am her servant; and put your trust in God, for you will not fail to obtain some dignity so as to live like a prince.”","\"그것으로 됐어요.\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"어서요, 산초, 달려가서 주인님의 손에 입 맞추고 용서를 비세요. 그리고 앞으로는 칭찬을 하든 욕을 하든 더 신중하시고, 제가 그저 그분의 종임을 알 뿐 그 밖에는 아무것도 모르는 그 토보소의 마님을 깎아내리는 말은 한마디도 하지 마세요. 그리고 하느님을 믿으세요. 산초는 왕자처럼 살 만한 어떤 높은 자리든 틀림없이 얻게 될 테니까요.\""]],[["Sancho advanced hanging his head and begged his master’s hand, which Don Quixote with dignity presented to him, giving him his blessing as soon as he had kissed it; he then bade him go on ahead a little, as he had questions to ask him and matters of great importance to discuss with him. Sancho obeyed, and when the two had gone some distance in advance Don Quixote said to him, “Since thy return I have had no opportunity or time to ask thee many particulars touching thy mission and the answer thou hast brought back, and now that chance has granted us the time and opportunity, deny me not the happiness thou canst give me by such good news.”","산초는 고개를 떨군 채 다가가 주인의 손을 청했고, 돈키호테는 위엄 있게 그것을 내밀어 산초가 입을 맞추자마자 축복을 내려 주었다. 그러고는 산초에게 물어볼 것과 함께 의논할 매우 중대한 일이 있으니 조금 앞서 가라고 일렀다. 산초가 그 말을 따랐고, 두 사람이 어느 정도 앞서 나아가자 돈키호테가 그에게 말했다. \"네가 돌아온 뒤로 네 임무며 네가 가져온 답에 관해 여러 가지를 자세히 물어볼 겨를도 틈도 없었구나. 이제 운이 우리에게 시간과 기회를 베풀어 주었으니, 그런 좋은 소식으로 네가 줄 수 있는 행복을 내게 마다하지 말아라.\""]],[["“Let your worship ask what you will,”","\"무엇이든 물어보십시오, 나리.\" 산초가 대답했다."],["answered Sancho, “for I shall find a way out of all as as I found a way in; but I implore you, señor, not not to be so revengeful in future.”","\"들어간 길을 찾았듯이 나오는 길도 다 찾아낼 테니까요. 다만 간청드리건대, 나리, 앞으로는 그렇게 복수심에 불타지 말아 주십시오.\""]],[["“Why dost thou say that, Sancho?”","\"어찌하여 그런 말을 하느냐, 산초야?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“I say it,”","\"제가 그리 말씀드리는 까닭은요.\" 산초가 대답했다."],["he returned, “because those blows just now were more because of the quarrel the devil stirred up between us both the other night, than for what I said against my lady Dulcinea, whom I love and reverence as I would a relic--though there is nothing of that about her--merely as something belonging to your worship.”","\"방금 그 매질이, 제가 둘시네아 마님께 거슬리는 말을 한 탓이라기보다는, 지난밤 악마가 우리 둘 사이에 일으킨 다툼 때문이었기 때문입니다요. 그분으로 말씀드리면, 저는 그분을 성물처럼 사랑하고 받들고 있습니다요―그분께 그런 구석이 있어서가 아니라 그저 나리께 속한 무언가라서요.\""]],[["“Say no more on that subject for thy life, Sancho,”","\"그 이야기는 목숨을 걸고 더 꺼내지 말아라, 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “for it is displeasing to me; I have already pardoned thee for that, and thou knowest the common saying, ‘for a fresh sin a fresh penance.’”","\"내게는 거슬리는 일이니라. 그 일에 대해서는 이미 너를 용서했고, 너도 '새로운 죄에는 새로운 속죄'라는 흔한 말을 알고 있지 않느냐.\""]],[["While this was going on they saw coming along the road they were following a man mounted on an ass, who when he came close seemed to be a gipsy; but Sancho Panza, whose eyes and heart were there wherever he saw asses, no sooner beheld the man than he knew him to be Gines de Pasamonte; and by the thread of the gipsy he got at the ball, his ass, for it was, in fact, Dapple that carried Pasamonte, who to escape recognition and to sell the ass had disguised himself as a gipsy, being able to speak the gipsy language, and many more, as well as if they were his own. Sancho saw him and recognised him, and the instant he did so he shouted to him, “Ginesillo, you thief, give up my treasure, release my life, embarrass thyself not with my repose, quit my ass, leave my delight, be off, rip, get thee gone, thief, and give up what is not thine.”","이런 이야기가 오가는 동안, 그들은 자기들이 가던 길을 따라 당나귀를 탄 사내 하나가 다가오는 것을 보았는데, 가까이 다가오자 그는 집시처럼 보였다. 그러나 당나귀를 보면 어디서든 눈과 마음이 그쪽으로 쏠리는 산초 판사는, 그 사내를 보자마자 그가 히네스 데 파사몬테임을 알아보았다. 그리고 집시라는 실마리를 따라 실타래를 풀어 가니, 그것은 곧 자기 당나귀였다. 사실 파사몬테를 태우고 있던 것이 바로 잿빛이었던 것이다. 파사몬테는 정체가 드러나는 것을 피하고 당나귀를 팔아 치우려고 집시로 변장하고 있었으니, 집시 말을 비롯해 여러 언어를 마치 제 모국어처럼 구사할 수 있었던 것이다. 산초는 그를 보고 알아보았고, 알아보자마자 그에게 소리쳤다. \"히네시요, 이 도둑놈아, 내 보물을 내놓아라, 내 목숨을 풀어 다오, 내 안식을 가지고 성가시게 굴지 마라, 내 당나귀를 버리고, 내 기쁨을 떠나라, 꺼져라, 이 망나니야, 썩 물러가라, 도둑놈아, 네 것이 아닌 것을 내놓아라.\""]],[["There was no necessity for so many words or objurgations, for at the first one Gines jumped down, and at a like racing speed made off and got clear of them all.","이렇게 많은 말이나 욕설을 늘어놓을 필요도 없었으니, 첫마디에 히네스가 펄쩍 뛰어내려 그만한 빠른 속도로 줄행랑을 쳐서 그들 모두에게서 벗어나 버렸다."],["Sancho hastened to his Dapple, and embracing him he said, “How hast thou fared, my blessing, Dapple of my eyes, my comrade?”","산초는 자기 잿빛에게 서둘러 달려가 끌어안으며 말했다. \"그동안 어떻게 지냈느냐, 내 보물아, 내 눈 같은 잿빛아, 내 동무야?\""],["all the while kissing him and caressing him as if he were a human being.","그러면서 마치 사람이라도 되는 양 줄곧 입 맞추고 어루만졌다."],["The ass held his peace, and let himself be kissed and caressed by Sancho without answering a single word.","당나귀는 잠자코, 산초가 입 맞추고 어루만지는 것을 한마디 대꾸도 없이 가만히 받아들였다."],["They all came up and congratulated him on having found Dapple, Don Quixote especially, who told him that notwithstanding this he would not cancel the order for the three ass-colts, for which Sancho thanked him.","모두 다가와 잿빛을 되찾은 것을 축하해 주었고, 특히 돈키호테는 그럼에도 새끼 당나귀 세 마리를 주겠다는 약속은 취소하지 않겠노라고 일러 주었으며, 산초는 이에 감사를 표했다."]],[["While the two had been going along conversing in this fashion, the curate observed to Dorothea that she had shown great cleverness, as well in the story itself as in its conciseness, and the resemblance it bore to those of the books of chivalry.","이 두 사람이 이런 식으로 이야기를 나누며 가는 동안, 신부는 도로테아에게 이야기 자체에서나 그 간결함에서나, 또 그것이 기사도 책들의 이야기와 닮은 점에서 대단한 영리함을 보여 주었다고 말했다."],["She said that she had many times amused herself reading them; but that she did not know the situation of the provinces or seaports, and so she had said at haphazard that she had landed at Osuna.","그녀는 그런 책들을 읽으며 여러 번 즐긴 적이 있다고 했다. 다만 지방이나 항구의 위치를 알지 못해, 그래서 닥치는 대로 오수나에 상륙했다고 말해 버렸다는 것이었다."]],[["“So I saw,”","\"그래서 제가 알아챘지요.\""],["said the curate, “and for that reason I made haste to say what I did, by which it was all set right.","신부가 말했다. \"그래서 서둘러 제가 했던 말을 했던 것이고, 그것으로 다 바로잡혔던 겁니다."],["But is it not a strange thing to see how readily this unhappy gentleman believes all these figments and lies, simply because they are in the style and manner of the absurdities of his books?”","그런데 이 가엾은 분이 단지 그것이 자기가 읽은 책들의 터무니없는 것들과 같은 문체요 방식이라는 이유만으로, 이 모든 허구와 거짓을 얼마나 쉽사리 믿어 버리는지를 보는 것은 참 묘한 일이 아닙니까?\""]],[["“So it is,”","\"정말 그렇지요.\""],["said Cardenio; “and so uncommon and unexampled, that were one to attempt to invent and concoct it in fiction, I doubt if there be any wit keen enough to imagine it.”","카르데니오가 말했다. \"어찌나 보기 드물고 유례없는 일인지, 누군가 이것을 허구로 지어내고 꾸며 보려 한다 해도, 과연 이를 상상해 낼 만큼 예리한 재치가 있을지 의심스러울 정도입니다.\""]],[["“But another strange thing about it,”","\"하지만 그것에 관해 또 한 가지 묘한 점은.\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “is that, apart from the silly things which this worthy gentleman says in connection with his craze, when other subjects are dealt with, he can discuss them in a perfectly rational manner, showing that his mind is quite clear and composed; so that, provided his chivalry is not touched upon, no one would take him to be anything but a man of thoroughly sound understanding.”","\"이 훌륭한 양반이 자기 광증과 관련해서 어리석은 소리들을 하는 것을 제쳐 두면, 다른 주제를 다룰 때는 더없이 이성적인 태도로 그것들을 논할 수 있어, 그 정신이 자못 맑고 차분하다는 것을 보여 준다는 점입니다. 그러니 기사도만 건드리지 않는다면, 누구도 그를 더없이 건전한 분별력을 갖춘 사람으로밖에는 여기지 않을 겁니다.\""]],[["While they were holding this conversation Don Quixote continued his with Sancho, saying:","그들이 이런 대화를 나누는 동안, 돈키호테는 산초와의 대화를 이어 가며 말했다."]],[["“Friend Panza, let us forgive and forget as to our quarrels, and tell me now, dismissing anger and irritation, where, how, and when didst thou find Dulcinea?","\"친구 판사야, 우리의 다툼일랑 용서하고 잊어버리자. 그리고 이제 노여움과 짜증일랑 떨쳐 버리고 말해 보아라."],["What was she doing?","너는 둘시네아를 어디서, 어떻게, 언제 만났느냐?"],["What didst thou say to her?","그분은 무엇을 하고 계셨느냐?"],["What did she answer?","너는 그분께 무슨 말을 했느냐?"],["How did she look when she was reading my letter?","그분은 무어라 대답하셨느냐? 내 편지를 읽으실 때 표정이 어떠하셨느냐?"],["Who copied it out for thee?","그것을 누가 너를 위해 베껴 주었느냐?"],["and everything in the matter that seems to thee worth knowing, asking, and learning; neither adding nor falsifying to give me pleasure, nor yet curtailing lest you should deprive me of it.”","그 밖에 이 일에서 알고 묻고 새겨 둘 만하다 여겨지는 모든 것을 말이다. 나를 기쁘게 하려고 보태거나 거짓을 꾸미지도 말고, 그렇다고 나에게서 그 기쁨을 앗아 갈까 줄이지도 말고서.\""]],[["“Señor,”","\"나리.\" 산초가 대답했다."],["replied Sancho, “if the truth is to be told, nobody copied out the letter for me, for I carried no letter at all.”","\"솔직히 말씀드리면, 아무도 그 편지를 저를 위해 베껴 주지 않았습니다요. 제가 편지라곤 아예 지니고 가지 않았으니까요.\""]],[["“It is as thou sayest,” said Don Quixote, “for the note-book in which I wrote it I found in my own possession two days after thy departure, which gave me very great vexation, as I knew not what thou wouldst do on finding thyself without any letter; and I made sure thou wouldst return from the place where thou didst first miss it.”","\"네 말이 맞다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그것을 적어 두었던 수첩을 네가 떠난 지 이틀 만에 내 손에서 발견했으니, 그 일로 나는 몹시 속이 상했더랬다. 편지가 없는 것을 알면 네가 어찌할지 몰랐기 때문이지. 나는 네가 그것이 없음을 처음 알아챈 그 자리에서 돌아오리라 확신했었다.\""]],[["“So I should have done,”","\"그랬을 겁니다요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “if I had not got it by heart when your worship read it to me, so that I repeated it to a sacristan, who copied it out for me from hearing it, so exactly that he said in all the days of his life, though he had read many a letter of excommunication, he had never seen or read so pretty a letter as that.”","\"나리께서 제게 읽어 주실 때 외워 두지 않았더라면 말이지요. 그래서 그것을 어느 성당지기에게 읊어 주었더니, 그가 듣고서 어찌나 정확하게 베껴 주던지, 평생 동안 파문장이라면 숱하게 읽어 보았어도 그렇게 예쁜 편지는 한 번도 보거나 읽어 본 적이 없다고 하더군요.\""]],[["“And hast thou got it still in thy memory, Sancho?”","\"그래서 너는 아직도 그것을 기억하고 있느냐, 산초야?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“No, señor,” replied Sancho, “for as soon as I had repeated it, seeing there was no further use for it, I set about forgetting it; and if I recollect any of it, it is that about ‘Scrubbing,’ I mean to say ‘Sovereign Lady,’ and the end ‘Yours till death, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance;’ and between these two I put into it more than three hundred ‘my souls’ and ‘my life’s’ and ‘my eyes.”","\"아니요, 나리.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"다 읊고 나니 더는 쓸데가 없는 것을 보고 곧장 잊어버리기 시작했으니까요. 그래도 조금 기억나는 게 있다면, '문질러 닦는'이라는 건데, 그러니까 '지존하신 마님' 말씀이고요, 끝에 가서는 '죽도록 그대의 것인, 슬픈 몰골의 기사'라는 거지요. 그리고 이 둘 사이에는 '내 영혼'이며 '내 생명'이며 '내 눈'이라는 말을 삼백 개도 넘게 집어넣었더랬습니다요.\""]],[["# CHAPTER XXXI. OF THE DELECTABLE DISCUSSION BETWEEN DON QUIXOTE AND SANCHO PANZA, HIS SQUIRE, TOGETHER WITH OTHER INCIDENTS","# CHAPTER XXXI."]],[["“All that is not unsatisfactory to me,”","“그 모든 것이 내게 불만스럽지는 않구나.” 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote.","“계속하거라."],["“Go on; thou didst reach her; and what was that queen of beauty doing?","그래 네가 그분께 이르렀으렷다. 그래 그 미의 여왕께서는 무엇을 하고 계셨더냐?"],["Surely thou didst find her stringing pearls, or embroidering some device in gold thread for this her enslaved knight.”","분명 진주를 꿰고 계시거나, 이 사로잡힌 기사를 위해 금실로 무슨 문양을 수놓고 계셨을 테지.”"]],[["“I did not,”","“아니었습니다요.”"],["said Sancho, “but I found her winnowing two bushels of wheat in the yard of her house.”","산초가 말했다. “자기 집 마당에서 밀 두 부셸을 까부르고 계시던데요.”"]],[["“Then depend upon it,”","“그렇다면 틀림없으니라.” 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “the grains of that wheat were pearls when touched by her hands; and didst thou look, friend?","“그 밀알들은 그분의 손길이 닿는 순간 진주가 되었을 게야. 한데 잘 보았느냐, 이 사람아?"],["was it white wheat or brown?”","흰 밀이더냐, 갈색 밀이더냐?”"]],[["“It was neither, but red,”","“이것도 저것도 아니고, 붉은 밀이었습니다요.”"],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“Then I promise thee,”","“그러면 내 약속하마.”"],["said Don Quixote, “that, winnowed by her hands, beyond a doubt the bread it made was of the whitest; but go on; when thou gavest her my letter, did she kiss it?","돈키호테가 말했다. “그분의 손에 까불린 그 밀로 만든 빵은 의심할 바 없이 더없이 흰 빵이었을 테지."],["Did she place it on her head?","한데 계속하거라. 네가 그분께 내 편지를 드렸을 때, 그분께서 입을 맞추셨더냐?"],["Did she perform any ceremony befitting it, or what did she do?”","머리 위에 얹으셨더냐? 그에 걸맞은 어떤 예를 갖추셨더냐, 아니면 어떻게 하셨더냐?”"]],[["“When I went to give it to her,” replied Sancho, “she was hard at it swaying from side to side with a lot of wheat she had in the sieve, and she said to me, ‘Lay the letter, friend, on the top of that sack, for I cannot read it until I have done sifting all this.”","“편지를 드리려 갔을 때요,” 산초가 대꾸했다. “체에 담긴 밀을 잔뜩 이쪽저쪽으로 흔드시느라 한창 바쁘셨는데, 제게 이러시더라고요. ‘이 사람아, 편지는 저 자루 위에 얹어 두구려. 이걸 다 까부르기 전에는 못 읽으니까.’”"]],[["“Discreet lady!”","“현명한 분이로다!” 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “that was in order to read it at her leisure and enjoy it; proceed, Sancho; while she was engaged in her occupation what converse did she hold with thee?","“그건 한가할 때 천천히 읽고 음미하시려는 뜻이었느니라. 계속하거라, 산초."],["What did she ask about me, and what answer didst thou give?","그분께서 일에 매여 계시는 동안 너와 무슨 이야기를 나누셨더냐? 나에 관해 무엇을 물으셨고, 너는 무슨 대답을 했느냐?"],["Make haste; tell me all, and let not an atom be left behind in the ink-bottle.”","어서, 모두 말해 보거라. 잉크병 속에 티끌 하나도 남기지 말고.”"]],[["“She asked me nothing,”","“제게 아무것도 안 물으셨습니다요.” 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho; “but I told her how your worship was left doing penance in her service, naked from the waist up, in among these mountains like a savage, sleeping on the ground, not eating bread off a tablecloth nor combing your beard, weeping and cursing your fortune.”","“하지만 제가 말씀드렸지요. 나리께서 그분을 섬기느라 고행을 하고 계시는데, 허리 위로는 벌거벗은 채 이 산속에서 야만인처럼 땅바닥에서 주무시고, 식탁보에 빵을 놓고 잡숫지도 않으시고, 수염도 빗지 않으시고, 울며 당신 운명을 저주하고 계신다고요.”"]],[["“In saying I cursed my fortune thou saidst wrong,”","“내가 운명을 저주한다 말한 것은 잘못 말한 게야.”"],["said Don Quixote; “for rather do I bless it and shall bless it all the days of my life for having made me worthy of aspiring to love so lofty a lady as Dulcinea del Toboso.”","돈키호테가 말했다. “오히려 나는 그것을 축복하며 평생 동안 축복할 것이니, 엘 토보소의 둘시네아처럼 그토록 고귀한 귀부인을 사랑하려는 열망을 품을 자격을 내게 주었기 때문이니라.”"]],[["“And so lofty she is,”","“그래서 그분이 어찌나 고귀하신지요,”"],["said Sancho, “that she overtops me by more than a hand’s-breadth.”","산초가 말했다. “저보다 한 뼘도 더 크시더라니까요.”"]],[["“What!","“뭐라!"],["Sancho,”","산초,”"],["said Don Quixote, “didst thou measure with her?”","돈키호테가 말했다. “네가 그분과 키를 재 보았더란 말이냐?”"]],[["“I measured in this way,”","“이렇게 재 봤습니다요.”"],["said Sancho; “going to help her to put a sack of wheat on the back of an ass, we came so close together that I could see she stood more than a good palm over me.”","산초가 말했다. “밀 자루를 당나귀 등에 얹는 걸 도와드리려고 바짝 다가갔는데, 그분이 저보다 족히 한 뼘은 더 크신 게 보이더라고요.”"]],[["“Well!”","“과연!” 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “and doth she not of a truth accompany and adorn this greatness with a thousand million charms of mind!","“그분이 정녕 그 위대함에 더하여 헤아릴 수 없는 천만 가지 마음의 매력으로 그것을 꾸미고 계시지 않더냐! 한데 한 가지는 네가 부인하지 못하리라, 산초."],["But one thing thou wilt not deny, Sancho; when thou camest close to her didst thou not perceive a Sabaean odour, an aromatic fragrance, a, I know not what, delicious, that I cannot find a name for; I mean a redolence, an exhalation, as if thou wert in the shop of some dainty glover?”","그분께 바짝 다가갔을 때 사바의 향내, 방향(芳香)의 향기, 뭐라 이름 붙일 수 없는, 그 무어라 형언할 수 없이 감미로운 것을 느끼지 못했느냐? 그러니까 어느 고운 장갑 가게에 들어선 듯한 그런 그윽한 내음, 그런 향취 말이다.”"]],[["“All I can say is,”","“제가 드릴 수 있는 말씀이라곤요,” 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “that I did perceive a little odour, something goaty; it must have been that she was all in a sweat with hard work.”","“약간 냄새를 느끼긴 했는데, 좀 염소 냄새 같은 거였습니다요. 아마 그분이 고된 일로 온통 땀에 젖으셔서 그랬겠지요.”"]],[["“It could not be that,”","“그럴 리 없느니라.” 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “but thou must have been suffering from cold in the head, or must have smelt thyself; for I know well what would be the scent of that rose among thorns, that lily of the field, that dissolved amber.”","“네가 코감기에 걸렸거나, 아니면 네 몸 냄새를 맡았던 게 분명하다. 가시덤불 속의 그 장미, 들판의 그 백합, 녹아내린 그 호박(琥珀)의 향기가 어떠한지는 내가 잘 아니 말이다.”"]],[["“Maybe so,” replied Sancho; “there often comes from myself that same odour which then seemed to me to come from her grace the lady Dulcinea; but that’s no wonder, for one devil is like another.”","“그럴지도 모르지요.” 산초가 대꾸했다. “그때 둘시네아 마님 마님께 나는 줄 알았던 그 냄새가 제 몸에서 자주 나곤 하니까요. 하지만 놀랄 일도 아니지요. 마귀나 마귀나 다 거기서 거기니까요.”"]],[["“Well then,”","“그래 그러면,” 돈키호테가 말을 이었다."],["continued Don Quixote, “now she has done sifting the corn and sent it to the mill; what did she do when she read the letter?”","“이제 그분께서 밀을 다 까부르시고 방앗간으로 보내셨겠다. 편지를 읽으시고는 무엇을 하시더냐?”"]],[["“As for the letter,” said Sancho, “she did not read it, for she said she could neither read nor write; instead of that she tore it up into small pieces, saying that she did not want to let anyone read it lest her secrets should become known in the village, and that what I had told her by word of mouth about the love your worship bore her, and the extraordinary penance you were doing for her sake, was enough; and, to make an end of it, she told me to tell your worship that she kissed your hands, and that she had a greater desire to see you than to write to you; and that therefore she entreated and commanded you, on sight of this present, to come out of these thickets, and to have done with carrying on absurdities, and to set out at once for El Toboso, unless something else of greater importance should happen, for she had a great desire to see your worship. She laughed greatly when I told her how your worship was called The Knight of the Rueful Countenance; I asked her if that Biscayan the other day had been there; and she told me he had, and that he was an honest fellow; I asked her too about the galley slaves, but she said she had not seen any as yet.”","“편지로 말씀드리면요,” 산초가 말했다. “읽지 않으셨습니다요. 글도 못 읽고 쓰지도 못한다고 하시면서요. 그러는 대신 잘게 찢어 버리시던데요. 마을에 비밀이 새어 나갈까 봐 아무한테도 못 읽히겠다 하시면서, 나리께서 그분께 품으신 사랑이며 그분을 위해 하고 계시는 그 대단한 고행에 대해 제가 말로 전해 드린 걸로 충분하다고요. 그러고는 끝으로, 나리께 손에 입맞춤을 보낸다고, 그리고 편지를 쓰기보다 만나 뵙고 싶은 마음이 더 크다고 전하라 하시더군요. 그러니 이 전갈을 받으시는 즉시 이 덤불숲에서 나오셔서, 그 터무니없는 짓들을 그만두시고, 더 중대한 다른 일이 생기지 않는 한 당장 엘 토보소로 떠나시기를 간청하고 또 명하신다고요. 나리를 무척 뵙고 싶으시다면서요. 나리께서 ‘슬픈 몰골의 기사’라 불린다고 말씀드렸더니 그분이 무척 웃으셨습니다요. 저는 며칠 전 그 비스카야인이 거기 왔었는지 여쭤봤는데, 왔었다고, 정직한 친구더라고 하시더군요. 갤리선 노예들에 대해서도 여쭤봤는데, 아직 아무도 못 봤다고 하셨습니다요.”"]],[["“So far all goes well,”","“여기까지는 모든 게 잘되었구나.”"],["said Don Quixote; “but tell me what jewel was it that she gave thee on taking thy leave, in return for thy tidings of me?","돈키호테가 말했다. “한데 말해 보거라."],["For it is a usual and ancient custom with knights and ladies errant to give the squires, damsels, or dwarfs who bring tidings of their ladies to the knights, or of their knights to the ladies, some rich jewel as a guerdon for good news,’ and acknowledgment of the message.”","작별할 때, 나에 관한 소식을 전한 대가로 그분께서 네게 무슨 보석을 주시더냐? 기사와 귀부인을 섬기는 종자나 시녀나 난쟁이가 귀부인의 소식을 기사에게, 또는 기사의 소식을 귀부인에게 전하면, 좋은 소식에 대한 보답이자 전갈을 받았다는 표시로 값진 보석을 주는 것이 편력 기사와 귀부인들의 오래되고도 통상적인 관례이니라.”"]],[["“That is very likely,” said Sancho, “and a good custom it was, to my mind; but that must have been in days gone by, for now it would seem to be the custom only to give a piece of bread and cheese; because that was what my lady Dulcinea gave me over the top of the yard-wall when I took leave of her; and more by token it was sheep’s-milk cheese.”","“그럴 법한 일이지요.” 산초가 말했다. “제 생각에도 좋은 관례였고요. 한데 그건 옛날 옛적 일이었나 봅니다요. 지금은 빵과 치즈 한 조각을 주는 게 관례인 모양이니까요. 제가 작별할 때 둘시네아 마님이 마당 담장 너머로 제게 주신 게 그거였거든요. 게다가 더 말씀드리자면 양젖 치즈였고요.”"]],[["“She is generous in the extreme,”","“그분은 더없이 너그러우시니,” 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “and if she did not give thee a jewel of gold, no doubt it must have been because she had not one to hand there to give thee; but sleeves are good after Easter; I shall see her and all shall be made right.","“네게 황금 보석을 주지 않으셨다면, 그건 분명 그 자리에 줄 만한 것이 손에 없으셨기 때문일 게다. 하나 부활절 뒤에 받는 소매도 좋은 법이니, 내가 그분을 뵈면 모든 게 바로잡힐 것이니라."],["But knowest thou what amazes me, Sancho?","한데 산초, 무엇이 나를 놀라게 하는지 아느냐? 네가 공중을 가로질러 오가고 온 게 틀림없어 보인다."],["It seems to me thou must have gone and come through the air, for thou hast taken but little more than three days to go to El Toboso and return, though it is more than thirty leagues from here to there.","여기서 엘 토보소까지 삼십 레과가 넘는데도, 다녀오는 데 사흘 남짓밖에 안 걸렸으니 말이다. 그래서 내 친구이자 내 일을 돌봐 주는 현명한 마법사가(편력 기사라면 반드시 그런 자가 있게 마련이고 또 있어야 하니, 그렇지 않으면 나는 진정한 편력 기사가 아닐 게다), 바로 그자가 네가 알지 못하는 사이에 너를 여행하도록 도왔으리라 생각하는 쪽으로 마음이 기우는구나."],["From which I am inclined to think that the sage magician who is my friend, and watches over my interests (for of necessity there is and must be one, or else I should not be a right knight-errant), that this same, I say, must have helped thee to travel without thy knowledge; for some of these sages will catch up a knight-errant sleeping in his bed, and without his knowing how or in what way it happened, he wakes up the next day more than a thousand leagues away from the place where he went to sleep.","이런 현인들 가운데 더러는 자기 침대에서 자고 있는 편력 기사를 낚아채어, 그 기사가 어떻게 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지도 모르는 사이에, 잠든 곳에서 천 레과도 넘게 떨어진 곳에서 이튿날 깨어나게 한단다. 이것이 아니라면, 편력 기사들이 위기에 처한 서로를, 늘 그러듯 도와줄 수 없을 것이니라."],["And if it were not for this, knights-errant would not be able to give aid to one another in peril, as they do at every turn.","어느 기사가 아르메니아 산중에서 용이나 사나운 뱀이나 또 다른 기사와 싸우다가 전세가 불리해져 죽음의 문턱에 이르렀다 치자. 그런데 전혀 예기치 못한 순간에, 그를 마주하여 구름 위나 불수레 위에 또 다른 기사가, 그의 벗이, 바로 직전까지 잉글랜드에 있던 자가 나타나, 그의 편을 들어 죽음에서 그를 건져 내느니라."],["For a knight, maybe, is fighting in the mountains of Armenia with some dragon, or fierce serpent, or another knight, and gets the worst of the battle, and is at the point of death; but when he least looks for it, there appears over against him on a cloud, or chariot of fire, another knight, a friend of his, who just before had been in England, and who takes his part, and delivers him from death; and at night he finds himself in his own quarters supping very much to his satisfaction; and yet from one place to the other will have been two or three thousand leagues.","그러고는 밤이면 그자가 제 거처에서 흡족하게 저녁을 들고 있는 자신을 발견하게 되는데, 그럼에도 한곳에서 다른 곳까지는 이삼천 레과나 되었을 것이니라. 이 모든 일이 그 용맹한 기사들을 돌보는 현명한 마법사들의 술책과 솜씨로 이루어지는 게야."],["And all this is done by the craft and skill of the sage enchanters who take care of those valiant knights; so that, friend Sancho, I find no difficulty in believing that thou mayest have gone from this place to El Toboso and returned in such a short time, since, as I have said, some friendly sage must have carried thee through the air without thee perceiving it.”","그러니 산초, 네가 이곳에서 엘 토보소까지 다녀오는 데 그토록 짧은 시간이 걸렸다는 것을 나는 조금도 믿기 어렵지 않구나. 내가 말했듯, 어떤 친절한 현인이 네가 알아채지 못하는 사이에 너를 공중으로 실어 날랐을 게 틀림없으니 말이다.”"]],[["“That must have been it,”","“그게 틀림없습니다요.”"],["said Sancho, “for indeed Rocinante went like a gipsy’s ass with quicksilver in his ears.”","산초가 말했다. “정말이지 로시난테가 귀에 수은을 넣은 집시 당나귀처럼 내달렸으니까요.”"]],[["“Quicksilver!” said Don Quixote, “aye and what is more, a legion of devils, folk that can travel and make others travel without being weary, exactly as the whim seizes them. But putting this aside, what thinkest thou I ought to do about my lady’s command to go and see her? For though I feel that I am bound to obey her mandate, I feel too that I am debarred by the boon I have accorded to the princess that accompanies us, and the law of chivalry compels me to have regard for my word in preference to my inclination; on the one hand the desire to see my lady pursues and harasses me, on the other my solemn promise and the glory I shall win in this enterprise urge and call me; but what I think I shall do is to travel with all speed and reach quickly the place where this giant is, and on my arrival I shall cut off his head, and establish the princess peacefully in her realm, and forthwith I shall return to behold the light that lightens my senses, to whom I shall make such excuses that she will be led to approve of my delay, for she will see that it entirely tends to increase her glory and fame; for all that I have won, am winning, or shall win by arms in this life, comes to me of the favour she extends to me, and because I am hers.”","“수은이라!” 돈키호테가 말했다. “그렇고말고, 게다가 한 무리의 마귀들이렷다. 변덕이 동하는 대로 지치지도 않고 다니며 남도 다니게 만드는 족속들 말이다. 한데 이 일은 접어 두고, 그분을 뵈러 오라는 내 귀부인의 명에 대해 내가 어찌해야 마땅하다고 보느냐? 그분의 명령에 따라야 마땅하다고 느끼면서도, 우리와 함께하는 공주에게 베푼 은혜로 인해 막혀 있음 또한 느끼는구나. 기사도의 법은 내가 내 마음보다 내 약속을 더 중히 여기도록 강요하느니라. 한편으로는 내 귀부인을 뵙고 싶은 열망이 나를 쫓고 들볶으며, 다른 한편으로는 내 엄숙한 약속과 이 위업에서 얻을 영광이 나를 재촉하고 부른다. 하나 내가 하려는 바는 온 힘을 다해 서둘러 이 거인이 있는 곳에 빨리 다다라, 도착하는 즉시 그놈의 목을 베고 공주를 그 왕국에 평화로이 앉히는 것이며, 그런 뒤 곧장 돌아와 내 감각을 밝혀 주는 그 빛을 다시 우러르는 것이니라. 그분께는 이러한 변명을 드릴 것인즉, 그분께서 내 지체를 흔쾌히 받아들이시게 될 게다. 그것이 오롯이 그분의 영광과 명성을 드높이는 데로 향한다는 것을 그분께서 아실 테니 말이다. 내가 이 생에서 무기로 얻었고, 얻고 있고, 또 얻을 모든 것은 그분께서 내게 베푸시는 은총에서 비롯되며, 내가 그분의 것이기 때문이니라.”"]],[["“Ah!","“아이고, 나리의 머릿속이 어찌 이리 딱한 지경이람!” 산초가 말했다."],["what a sad state your worship’s brains are in!”","“말씀해 보세요, 나리. 그 먼 길을 헛걸음하시면서, 이렇게 부유하고 대단한 혼처를 놓치고 잃어버리실 작정이세요?"],["said Sancho.","결혼 지참금으로 한 왕국을 준다잖아요. 제가 듣기로 솔직히 그 둘레가 이만 레과가 넘는다는데, 인간이 살아가는 데 필요한 온갖 것이 넘쳐 나고, 포르투갈과 카스티야를 합친 것보다도 크다는데요."],["“Tell me, señor, do you mean to travel all that way for nothing, and to let slip and lose so rich and great a match as this where they give as a portion a kingdom that in sober truth I have heard say is more than twenty thousand leagues round about, and abounds with all things necessary to support human life, and is bigger than Portugal and Castile put together?","제발 그만하세요, 하느님 사랑으로요! 하신 말씀을 부끄러워하시고, 제 충고를 받아들이세요."],["Peace, for the love of God!","그리고 저를 용서하시고, 사제가 있는 첫 마을에서 당장 결혼하세요. 그게 아니면 여기 우리 학사 나리가 그 일을 멋들어지게 해 주실 겁니다요."],["Blush for what you have said, and take my advice, and forgive me, and marry at once in the first village where there is a curate; if not, here is our licentiate who will do the business beautifully; remember, I am old enough to give advice, and this I am giving comes pat to the purpose; for a sparrow in the hand is better than a vulture on the wing, and he who has the good to his hand and chooses the bad, that the good he complains of may not come to him.”","명심하세요, 저도 충고할 만큼 나이를 먹었고, 지금 드리는 이 충고는 딱 들어맞는 거랍니다요. 날아가는 독수리보다 손안의 참새가 나은 법이고, 좋은 것을 손에 쥐고도 나쁜 것을 고르는 자는, 그 한탄하는 좋은 것이 제게 오지 않을 테니까요.”"]],[["“Look here, Sancho,”","“이보게, 산초,” 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote.","“네가 내게 결혼하라고 권하는 것이, 거인을 죽이는 즉시 내가 왕이 되어 네게 은혜를 베풀고 약속한 것을 줄 수 있게 하려는 뜻이라면, 내 말하노니, 결혼하지 않고도 네 소원을 아주 쉽게 들어줄 수 있느니라."],["“If thou art advising me to marry, in order that immediately on slaying the giant I may become king, and be able to confer favours on thee, and give thee what I have promised, let me tell thee I shall be able very easily to satisfy thy desires without marrying; for before going into battle I will make it a stipulation that, if I come out of it victorious, even I do not marry, they shall give me a portion portion of the kingdom, that I may bestow it upon whomsoever I choose, and when they give it to me upon whom wouldst thou have me bestow it but upon thee?”","싸움에 나서기 전에 한 가지 조건을 걸어 둘 터인즉, 만일 내가 승리하여 거기서 나온다면, 설령 내가 결혼하지 않더라도 왕국의 한 부분을 내게 떼어 주어 내가 원하는 누구에게든 그것을 줄 수 있게 하라는 것이니라. 그리고 그것을 내게 주면 내가 너 말고 누구에게 주겠느냐?”"]],[["“That is plain speaking,” said Sancho; “but let your worship take care to choose it on the seacoast, so that if I don’t like the life, I may be able to ship off my black vassals and deal with them as I have said; don’t mind going to see my lady Dulcinea now, but go and kill this giant and let us finish off this business; for by God it strikes me it will be one of great honour and great profit.”","“그거 솔직한 말씀이네요.” 산초가 말했다. “한데 나리, 부디 바닷가에 있는 걸로 골라 주세요. 그래야 그 삶이 마음에 안 들면 제 검은 신하들을 배에 실어, 제가 말씀드린 대로 처분할 수 있을 테니까요. 둘시네아 마님은 지금 뵈러 가실 것 없이, 가셔서 이 거인을 죽이고 이 일을 끝장내시지요. 맹세코 제 생각엔 이게 큰 명예와 큰 이득이 될 일 같으니까요.”"]],[["“I hold thou art in the right of it, Sancho,” said Don Quixote, “and I will take thy advice as to accompanying the princess before going to see Dulcinea; but I counsel thee not to say anything to any one, or to those who are with us, about what we have considered and discussed, for as Dulcinea is so decorous that she does not wish her thoughts to be known it is not right that I or anyone for me should disclose them.”","“네 말이 옳다고 본다, 산초.” 돈키호테가 말했다. “둘시네아를 뵈러 가기 전에 공주와 동행하라는 네 충고를 따르마. 하나 우리가 헤아리고 의논한 바에 대해서는 아무에게도, 우리와 함께 있는 이들에게도 한마디 하지 말라고 일러 두겠노라. 둘시네아는 워낙 정숙하셔서 자기 생각이 알려지길 원치 않으시니, 나든 나를 대신한 누구든 그것을 드러내는 것은 옳지 않으니라.”"]],[["“Well then, if that be so,”","“그래 그게 그렇다면요,” 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “how is it that your worship makes all those you overcome by your arm go to present themselves before my lady Dulcinea, this being the same thing as signing your name to it that you love her and are her lover?","“나리께서 그 팔로 무찌른 자들을 모조리 둘시네아 마님 앞에 나가 자기를 바치라고 하시는 건 어찌 된 일입니까요? 그건 나리가 그분을 사랑하며 그분의 연인이라고 거기다 서명하시는 것과 똑같은 일 아니겠습니까요?"],["And as those who go must perforce kneel before her and say they come from your worship to submit themselves to her, how can the thoughts of both of you be hid?”","그리고 가야 하는 자들이 별수 없이 그분 앞에 무릎 꿇고 나리께서 보내셔서 복종하러 왔다고 말해야 하는데, 어떻게 두 분의 생각이 감춰지겠습니까요?”"]],[["“O, how silly and simple thou art!”","“오, 너는 어찌 이리 어리석고 단순하냐!”"],["said Don Quixote; “seest thou not, Sancho, that this tends to her greater exaltation?","돈키호테가 말했다. “산초, 그것이 그분을 더욱 드높이는 일임을 모르겠느냐?"],["For thou must know that according to our way of thinking in chivalry, it is a high honour to a lady to have many knights-errant in her service, whose thoughts never go beyond serving her for her own sake, and who look for no other reward for their great and true devotion than that she should be willing to accept them as her knights.”","기사도에서 우리가 생각하는 방식에 따르면, 한 귀부인에게 여러 편력 기사가 봉사하는 것은 그분께 더없는 영예가 되는 일이니라. 그 기사들의 생각이 오로지 그분 자체를 위해 그분을 섬기는 데서 한 걸음도 벗어나지 않으며, 그 위대하고 진실한 헌신에 대한 다른 어떤 보답도 바라지 않고, 다만 그분께서 그들을 당신의 기사로 받아들여 주시기만을 바라기 때문이니라.”"]],[["“It is with that kind of love,” said Sancho, “I have heard preachers say we ought to love our Lord, for himself alone, without being moved by the hope of glory or the fear of punishment; though for my part, I would rather love and serve him for what he could do.”","“바로 그런 사랑으로요,” 산초가 말했다. “설교자들이 우리가 주님을 사랑해야 한다고 말씀하시는 걸 들었습죠. 영광의 희망이나 벌의 두려움에 흔들리지 말고, 오직 그분 자체를 위해서요. 하긴 제 경우엔, 그분이 해 주실 수 있는 것 때문에 그분을 사랑하고 섬기는 편이 낫겠습니다만요.”"]],[["“The devil take thee for a clown!”","“이 촌뜨기 같은 놈, 마귀나 잡아가라!”"],["said Don Quixote, “and what shrewd things thou sayest at times!","돈키호테가 말했다. “한데 때로 어쩌면 그리 영리한 말을 하느냐!"],["One would think thou hadst studied.”","네가 공부깨나 한 줄 알겠구나.”"]],[["“In faith, then, I cannot even read.”","“참말로요, 저는 글도 못 읽는걸요.”"]],[["Master Nicholas here called out to them to wait a while, as they wanted to halt and drink at a little spring there was there.","이때 니콜라스 선생이 그들에게 잠깐 멈추라고 외쳤다. 거기 있는 작은 샘에서 멈춰 물을 마시려 했기 때문이다."],["Don Quixote drew up, not a little to the satisfaction of Sancho, for he was by this time weary of telling so many lies, and in dread of his master catching him tripping, for though he knew that Dulcinea was a peasant girl of El Toboso, he had never seen her in all his life.","돈키호테가 말을 세우니 산초로서는 적잖이 흡족했는데, 이즈음 그는 그토록 많은 거짓말을 늘어놓느라 지친 데다, 주인이 자신이 헛디딘 것을 잡아챌까 봐 겁이 났던 것이다. 둘시네아가 엘 토보소의 농사꾼 처녀라는 것은 알았으나, 평생 한 번도 본 적이 없었기 때문이다."],["Cardenio had now put on the clothes which Dorothea was wearing when they found her, and though they were not very good, they were far better than those he put off.","카르데니오는 어느덧 그들이 도로테아를 발견했을 때 그녀가 입고 있던 옷으로 갈아입었는데, 그리 좋은 옷은 아니었으나 그가 벗어 던진 것보다는 훨씬 나았다."],["They dismounted together by the side of the spring, and with what the curate had provided himself with at the inn they appeased, though not very well, the keen appetite they all of them brought with them.","그들은 다 함께 샘가에 내려, 사제가 주막에서 챙겨 온 것으로, 비록 넉넉히는 아니었으나, 다들 가져온 왕성한 식욕을 달랬다."]],[["While they were so employed there happened to come by a youth passing on his way, who stopping to examine the party at the spring, the next moment ran to Don Quixote and clasping him round the legs, began to weep freely, saying, “O, señor, do you not know me?","그들이 그러고 있는 동안, 마침 한 젊은이가 길을 가다 지나치게 되었는데, 샘가의 일행을 살피려고 멈춰 섰다가, 다음 순간 돈키호테에게 달려와 그의 다리를 끌어안고는 거침없이 울기 시작하며 말했다. “오, 나리, 저를 모르시겠어요?"],["Look at me well; I am that lad Andres that your worship released from the oak-tree where I was tied.”","잘 보세요. 저는 나리께서 묶여 있던 그 떡갈나무에서 풀어 주신 그 안드레스라는 아이입니다요.”"]],[["Don Quixote recognised him, and taking his hand he turned to those present and said: “That your worships may see how important it is to have knights-errant to redress the wrongs and injuries done by tyrannical and wicked men in this world, I may tell you that some days ago passing through a wood, I heard cries and piteous complaints as of a person in pain and distress; I immediately hastened, impelled by my bounden duty, to the quarter whence the plaintive accents seemed to me to proceed, and I found tied to an oak this lad who now stands before you, which in my heart I rejoice at, for his testimony will not permit me to depart from the truth in any particular.","돈키호테는 그를 알아보고, 그의 손을 잡고서 좌중을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. “이 세상에서 포악하고 사악한 자들이 저지르는 부당함과 해악을 바로잡는 데 편력 기사를 두는 것이 얼마나 중요한지를 여러분이 알 수 있도록 말씀드리겠소이다."],["He was, I say, tied to an oak, naked from the waist up, and a clown, whom I afterwards found to be his master, was scarifying him by lashes with the reins of his mare.","며칠 전 어느 숲을 지나는데, 고통과 곤경에 처한 사람의 것인 듯한 비명과 애처로운 하소연이 들려왔소. 나는 즉시 마땅한 의무에 떠밀려, 그 애처로운 소리가 들려오는 듯한 쪽으로 서둘러 갔더니, 지금 여러분 앞에 서 있는 이 아이가 떡갈나무에 묶여 있는 것을 발견했소이다."],["As soon as I saw him I asked the reason of so cruel a flagellation.","마음속으로 기쁘기 그지없으니, 이 아이의 증언이 내가 어떤 점에서도 진실에서 벗어나지 못하게 할 것이기 때문이오. 말하건대, 이 아이는 허리 위로 벗은 채 떡갈나무에 묶여 있었고, 나중에 그 주인임을 알게 된 한 촌뜨기가 암말 고삐로 채찍질을 하며 살갗이 벗겨지도록 매질하고 있었소."],["The boor replied that he was flogging him because he was his servant and because of carelessness that proceeded rather from dishonesty than stupidity; on which this boy said, ‘Señor, he flogs me only because I ask for my wages.’ The master made I know not what speeches and explanations, which, though I listened to them, I did not accept.","그를 보자마자 나는 어찌하여 그토록 잔혹하게 매질하는지 까닭을 물었소. 그 무지렁이는, 이 아이가 자기 하인이고, 어리석음보다는 부정직함에서 비롯된 부주의 때문에 매질한다고 대답했소."],["In short, I compelled the clown to unbind him, and to swear he would take him with him, and pay him real by real, and perfumed into the bargain.","그러자 이 소년이 ‘나리, 제가 품삯을 달라고 했다는 이유만으로 매질하는 겁니다요’라고 했소이다. 그 주인은 무어라 변명인지 해명인지를 늘어놓았으나, 나는 그것을 듣기는 했어도 받아들이지 않았소."],["Is not all this true, Andres my son?","요컨대 나는 그 촌뜨기에게 아이를 풀어 주게 하고, 그를 데려가 한 닢 한 닢 품삯을 치르되 향수까지 곁들여 주겠다고 맹세하게 했소. 그렇지 아니하냐, 안드레스 내 아들아?"],["Didst thou not mark with what authority I commanded him, and with what humility he promised to do all I enjoined, specified, and required of him?","내가 얼마나 위엄 있게 그에게 명했고, 그가 얼마나 공손하게 내가 명하고 지시하고 요구한 모든 것을 하겠다고 약속했는지 보지 않았느냐? 망설이지 말고 대답하거라."],["Answer without hesitation; tell these gentlemen what took place, that they may see that it is as great an advantage as I say to have knights-errant abroad.”","이 신사분들께 무슨 일이 있었는지 말씀드려라. 편력 기사가 세상에 나도는 것이 내가 말한 만큼이나 큰 이득임을 그분들이 알 수 있도록 말이다.”"]],[["“All that your worship has said is quite true,”","“나리께서 말씀하신 모든 게 다 사실입니다요.”"],["answered the lad; “but the end of the business turned out just the opposite of what your worship supposes.”","소년이 대답했다. “하지만 그 일의 끝은 나리께서 생각하시는 것과 정반대로 되어 버렸답니다요.”"]],[["“How!","“뭐라!"],["the opposite?”","정반대라고?”"],["said Don Quixote; “did not the clown pay thee then?”","돈키호테가 말했다. “그러면 그 촌뜨기가 네게 품삯을 치르지 않았더란 말이냐?”"]],[["“Not only did he not pay me,” replied the lad, “but as soon as your worship had passed out of the wood and we were alone, he tied me up again to the same oak and gave me a fresh flogging, that left me like a flayed Saint Bartholomew; and every stroke he gave me he followed up with some jest or gibe about having made a fool of your worship, and but for the pain I was suffering I should have laughed at the things he said. In short he left me in such a condition that I have been until now in a hospital getting cured of the injuries which that rascally clown inflicted on me then; for all which your worship is to blame; for if you had gone your own way and not come where there was no call for you, nor meddled in other people’s affairs, my master would have been content with giving me one or two dozen lashes, and would have then loosed me and paid me what he owed me; but when your worship abused him so out of measure, and gave him so many hard words, his anger was kindled; and as he could not revenge himself on you, as soon as he saw you had left him the storm burst upon me in such a way, that I feel as if I should never be a man again.”","“품삯을 치르지 않았을 뿐 아니라요,” 소년이 대답했다. “나리께서 숲을 빠져나가시고 우리 둘만 남게 되자마자, 저를 다시 그 똑같은 떡갈나무에 묶어 놓고는 새로 매질을 해서, 살가죽이 벗겨진 성 바르톨로메 꼴로 만들어 버렸습니다요. 한 대 칠 때마다 나리를 골탕 먹였다고 농지거리며 비웃음을 곁들였는데, 제가 겪던 고통만 아니었어도 그가 하는 말에 웃음이 터질 뻔했지요. 요컨대 그가 저를 그런 꼴로 만들어 놓는 바람에, 그 못된 촌뜨기가 그때 입힌 상처를 치료하느라 저는 지금까지 병원 신세를 졌습니다요. 이 모든 게 나리 탓이에요. 나리께서 가시던 길이나 가시고, 부르지도 않은 곳에 오시지 않고 남의 일에 끼어들지만 않으셨어도, 제 주인은 매 한두 다스 치는 걸로 만족하고는, 저를 풀어 주고 빚진 품삯을 치렀을 거예요. 그런데 나리께서 그를 그토록 도를 넘어 모욕하시고 그토록 심한 말을 많이 퍼부으시니, 그의 화가 불붙은 거지요. 나리한테는 앙갚음을 할 수 없으니, 나리께서 떠나신 걸 보자마자 그 폭풍이 제게 몰아쳤고, 그래서 저는 영영 사내구실을 못하게 될 것만 같습니다요.”"]],[["“The mischief,” said Don Quixote, “lay in my going away; for I should not have gone until I had seen thee paid; because I ought to have known well by long experience that there is no clown who will keep his word if he finds it will not suit him to keep it; but thou rememberest, Andres, that I swore if he did not pay thee I would go and seek him, and find him though he were to hide himself in the whale’s belly.”","“화근은,” 돈키호테가 말했다. “내가 떠나 버린 데 있었구나. 네가 품삯 받는 것을 보기 전에는 떠나지 말았어야 했어. 오랜 경험으로 미루어, 약속을 지키는 게 제게 이롭지 않다고 여기면 약속을 지킬 촌뜨기란 하나도 없다는 걸 진작 잘 알았어야 했는데 말이다. 하나 안드레스, 그가 네게 품삯을 치르지 않으면 내가 그를 찾아 나서서, 설령 그가 고래 뱃속에 숨더라도 기어이 찾아내겠다고 맹세한 것을 너도 기억할 게다.”"]],[["“That is true,”","“그건 사실이에요.”"],["said Andres; “but it was of no use.”","안드레스가 말했다. “하지만 아무 소용없었지요.”"]],[["“Thou shalt see now whether it is of use or not,”","“그게 소용이 있는지 없는지 이제 보게 되리라.”"],["said Don Quixote; and so saying, he got up hastily and bade Sancho bridle Rocinante, who was browsing while they were eating.","돈키호테가 이렇게 말하고는 황급히 일어나, 그들이 먹는 동안 풀을 뜯고 있던 로시난테에게 굴레를 씌우라고 산초에게 일렀다."],["Dorothea asked him what he meant to do.","도로테아가 그에게 무엇을 하려는 것이냐고 물었다."],["He replied that he meant to go in search of this clown and chastise him for such iniquitous conduct, and see Andres paid to the last maravedi, despite and in the teeth of all the clowns in the world.","그는 그 촌뜨기를 찾아 나서서 그토록 사악한 짓을 한 것을 벌하고, 세상의 모든 촌뜨기가 반대하고 가로막더라도 안드레스가 마지막 한 푼까지 품삯을 받는 것을 지켜보려는 것이라고 대답했다."],["To which she replied that he must remember that in accordance with his promise he could not engage in any enterprise until he had concluded hers; and that as he knew this better than anyone, he should restrain his ardour until his return from her kingdom.","이에 그녀는, 그가 한 약속에 따라 자신의 일을 마치기 전에는 어떤 위업에도 나설 수 없음을 명심해야 하며, 그가 이를 누구보다 잘 아는 만큼, 자기 왕국에서 돌아올 때까지 그 열정을 억눌러야 한다고 대꾸했다."]],[["“That is true,”","“그건 사실이오.” 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “and Andres must have patience until my return as you say, señora; but I once more swear and promise not to stop until I have seen him avenged and paid.”","“하여 안드레스는, 귀부인께서 말씀하신 대로 내가 돌아올 때까지 인내해야 한다. 하나 나는 다시 한번 맹세하고 약속하노니, 그가 원수를 갚고 품삯을 받는 것을 보기까지는 멈추지 않을 것이니라.”"]],[["“I have no faith in those oaths,” said Andres; “I would rather have now something to help me to get to Seville than all the revenges in the world; if you have here anything to eat that I can take with me, give it me, and God be with your worship and all knights-errant; and may their errands turn out as well for themselves as they have for me.”","“저는 그런 맹세들 안 믿어요.” 안드레스가 말했다. “세상의 그 모든 복수보다, 지금 세비야까지 갈 수 있게 도와줄 무언가가 차라리 더 낫겠어요. 여기 제가 가져갈 만한 먹을 게 있으면 주세요. 그리고 나리와 모든 편력 기사들에게 하느님이 함께하시기를요. 그들의 일이 제게 그랬던 만큼이나 그들 자신에게도 그렇게 잘 풀리기를 바랍니다요.”"]],[["Sancho took out from his store a piece of bread and another of cheese, and giving them to the lad he said, “Here, take this, brother Andres, for we have all of us a share in your misfortune.”","산초가 자기 짐에서 빵 한 조각과 치즈 한 조각을 꺼내 소년에게 주며 말했다. “자, 이걸 받게, 안드레스 형제. 우리 모두가 자네의 불행에 몫을 나눠 가졌으니 말이야.”"]],[["“Why, what share have you got?”","“아니, 무슨 몫을 가졌다는 거예요?”"]],[["“This share of bread and cheese I am giving you,”","“내가 자네한테 주는 이 빵과 치즈 몫이지.” 산초가 대답했다."],["answered Sancho; “and God knows whether I shall feel the want of it myself or not; for I would have you know, friend, that we squires to knights-errant have to bear a great deal of hunger and hard fortune, and even other things more easily felt than told.”","“이게 나중에 나한테 아쉬울지 어떨지는 하느님만 아실 일이고. 이보게 친구, 자네가 알아야 할 게, 우리처럼 편력 기사를 섬기는 종자들은 숱한 굶주림과 박복한 운명을, 게다가 말로 하기보다 몸으로 느끼기가 더 쉬운 다른 것들까지도 견뎌 내야 한다네.”"]],[["Andres seized his bread and cheese, and seeing that nobody gave him anything more, bent his head, and took hold of the road, as the saying is. However, before leaving he said, “For the love of God, sir knight-errant, if you ever meet me again, though you may see them cutting me to pieces, give me no aid or succour, but leave me to my misfortune, which will not be so great but that a greater will come to me by being helped by your worship, on whom and all the knights-errant that have ever been born God send his curse.”","안드레스는 빵과 치즈를 움켜쥐었고, 아무도 더 주는 것이 없음을 보자, 고개를 숙이고는, 흔히 말하듯 길을 잡아 나섰다. 그러나 떠나기 전에 그가 말했다. “하느님 사랑으로 부탁드리는데요, 편력 기사 나리, 혹시라도 다시 저를 만나시거든, 설령 사람들이 저를 갈가리 찢는 걸 보시더라도 제게 아무 도움도 구원도 베풀지 마시고, 그저 제 불행에 저를 내버려 두세요. 그래 봤자 나리께 도움받아 더 큰 불행이 제게 닥치는 것만큼 큰일은 아닐 테니까요. 나리와, 이제껏 태어난 모든 편력 기사들에게 하느님이 저주를 내리시기를요.”"]],[["Don Quixote was getting up to chastise him, but he took to his heels at such a pace that no one attempted to follow him; and mightily chapfallen was Don Quixote at Andres’ story, and the others had to take great care to restrain their laughter so as not to put him entirely out of countenance.","돈키호테가 그를 벌하려고 일어서려 했으나, 그가 어찌나 빠른 걸음으로 줄행랑을 쳤던지 아무도 뒤쫓으려 들지 않았다. 안드레스의 이야기에 돈키호테는 풀이 잔뜩 죽었고, 나머지 사람들은 그를 아주 무안하게 만들지 않으려고 웃음을 참느라 무척 애를 써야 했다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXXII. WHICH TREATS OF WHAT BEFELL DON QUIXOTE’S PARTY AT THE INN","# CHAPTER XXXII."]],[["Their dainty repast being finished, they saddled at once, and without any adventure worth mentioning they reached next day the inn, the object of Sancho Panza’s fear and dread; but though he would have rather not entered it, there was no help for it.","맛깔스러운 식사를 마치고 그들은 곧장 말에 안장을 얹었고, 이렇다 할 모험 하나 없이 이튿날 산초 판사가 두려워하고 무서워하던 그 객줏집에 다다랐다. 산초는 차라리 들어가지 않았으면 했으나 어쩔 도리가 없었다."],["The landlady, the landlord, their daughter, and Maritornes, when they saw Don Quixote and Sancho coming, went out to welcome them with signs of hearty satisfaction, which Don Quixote received with dignity and gravity, and bade them make up a better bed for him than the last time: to which the landlady replied that if he paid better than he did the last time she would give him one fit for a prince.","안주인과 주인, 그들의 딸과 마리토르네스는 돈키호테와 산초가 오는 것을 보고는 진심 어린 만족의 기색을 띠며 그들을 맞으러 나왔고, 돈키호테는 이를 위엄과 무게를 갖추어 받아들이며 지난번보다 더 나은 잠자리를 마련해 달라고 일렀다. 이에 안주인은 지난번보다 셈을 더 잘 치른다면 왕자에게나 어울릴 자리를 내드리겠다고 대답했다."],["Don Quixote said he would, so they made up a tolerable one for him in the same garret as before; and he lay down at once, being sorely shaken and in want of sleep.","돈키호테가 그러마고 하자, 그들은 전과 같은 다락방에 그런대로 쓸 만한 잠자리를 마련해 주었고, 그는 몹시 시달린 데다 잠이 절실했던지라 곧바로 드러누웠다."]],[["No sooner was the door shut upon him than the landlady made at the barber, and seizing him by the beard, said:","그의 등 뒤로 문이 닫히기가 무섭게 안주인은 이발사에게 달려들어 그의 수염을 움켜잡고 말했다."]],[["“By my faith you are not going to make a beard of my tail any longer; you must give me back my tail, for it is a shame the way that thing of my husband’s goes tossing about on the floor; I mean the comb that I used to stick in my good tail.”","\"맹세코 더는 내 꼬리로 당신 수염 노릇을 시킬 순 없어요. 내 꼬리를 돌려줘요. 내 남편의 그 물건이 바닥에 나뒹구는 꼴이 영 창피하니 말이에요. 내가 그 멀쩡한 꼬리에 꽂아 두던 빗 말이에요.\""]],[["But for all she tugged at it the barber would not give it up until the licentiate told him to let her have it, as there was now no further occasion for that stratagem, because he might declare himself and appear in his own character, and tell Don Quixote that he had fled to this inn when those thieves the galley slaves robbed him; and should he ask for the princess’s squire, they could tell him that she had sent him on before her to give notice to the people of her kingdom that she was coming, and bringing with her the deliverer of them all.","그러나 그녀가 아무리 잡아당겨도 이발사는 그것을 내주려 하지 않았는데, 마침내 석사가 이제는 그 술책이 더는 필요 없으니 그녀에게 돌려주라고 일렀다. 이제 그는 자기 정체를 밝히고 본래 모습으로 나서서, 그 갤리선 노예 도둑들이 자신을 털었을 때 이 객줏집으로 피신해 왔노라고 돈키호테에게 말하면 되었고, 만일 돈키호테가 공주의 종자를 찾거든, 공주가 자신이 모두의 구원자를 모시고 오고 있음을 미리 알리려고 그를 자기 나라 백성들에게 앞서 보냈다고 일러 주면 그만이었다."],["On this the barber cheerfully restored the tail to the landlady, and at the same time they returned all the accessories they had borrowed to effect Don Quixote’s deliverance.","이에 이발사는 흔쾌히 꼬리를 안주인에게 돌려주었고, 동시에 그들은 돈키호테를 구해 내려고 빌렸던 온갖 소품을 모두 돌려주었다."],["All the people of the inn were struck with astonishment at the beauty of Dorothea, and even at the comely figure of the shepherd Cardenio.","객줏집 사람들은 모두 도로테아의 아름다움에, 그리고 양치기로 분장한 카르데니오의 준수한 모습에까지 놀라움을 금치 못했다."],["The curate made them get ready such fare as there was in the inn, and the landlord, in hope of better payment, served them up a tolerably good dinner.","신부는 객줏집에 있는 대로 음식을 차리게 했고, 주인은 셈을 후하게 받을 기대로 그런대로 괜찮은 저녁을 차려 냈다."],["All this time Don Quixote was asleep, and they thought it best not to waken him, as sleeping would now do him more good than eating.","이러는 내내 돈키호테는 잠들어 있었고, 사람들은 그를 깨우지 않는 편이 낫다고 여겼는데, 이제는 먹는 것보다 자는 편이 그에게 더 이로울 것이었기 때문이다."]],[["While at dinner, the company consisting of the landlord, his wife, their daughter, Maritornes, and all the travellers, they discussed the strange craze of Don Quixote and the manner in which he had been found; and the landlady told them what had taken place between him and the carrier; and then, looking round to see if Sancho was there, when she saw he was not, she gave them the whole story of his blanketing, which they received with no little amusement.","저녁을 들며, 주인과 그 아내, 딸, 마리토르네스, 그리고 모든 길손이 함께한 자리에서, 그들은 돈키호테의 기이한 광기와 그가 발견된 경위를 두고 이야기를 나누었다. 안주인은 그와 짐꾼 사이에 있었던 일을 들려주었고, 이어 산초가 거기 있는지 둘러보다가 그가 없는 것을 보고는, 그가 담요로 키질을 당한 이야기를 죄다 들려주었으니, 사람들은 이를 적잖이 재미있어하며 들었다."],["But on the curate observing that it was the books of chivalry which Don Quixote had read that had turned his brain, the landlord said:","그러나 신부가 돈키호테가 읽은 기사도 책들이 그의 머리를 돌게 만든 것이라고 하자, 주인이 말했다."]],[["“I cannot understand how that can be, for in truth to my mind there is no better reading in the world, and I have here two or three of them, with other writings that are the very life, not only of myself but of plenty more; for when it is harvest-time, the reapers flock here on holidays, and there is always one among them who can read and who takes up one of these books, and we gather round him, thirty or more of us, and stay listening to him with a delight that makes our grey hairs grow young again. At least I can say for myself that when I hear of what furious and terrible blows the knights deliver, I am seized with the longing to do the same, and I would like to be hearing about them night and day.”","\"어찌 그럴 수가 있는지 도무지 모르겠습니다. 사실 제 생각으로는 세상에 그보다 나은 읽을거리가 없거든요. 저한테도 그런 책이 두세 권 있는데, 다른 글들과 더불어 그것들이야말로 저뿐 아니라 숱한 사람들의 그야말로 삶의 낙입니다. 추수철이 되면 일꾼들이 쉬는 날마다 여기로 몰려드는데, 그중에는 늘 글을 읽을 줄 아는 사람이 하나쯤 있어서 이런 책 한 권을 집어 들면, 우리 서른 명 넘는 사람이 그 둘레에 모여 앉아 그가 읽는 것을 듣지요. 그러면 어찌나 신이 나는지 우리 흰머리가 다시 젊어지는 것만 같습니다. 적어도 저로 말씀드리자면, 기사들이 얼마나 사납고 무시무시한 일격을 날리는지 들을 때면 저도 똑같이 해 보고 싶은 갈망에 사로잡혀, 밤이고 낮이고 그 이야기를 듣고 싶어진답니다.\""]],[["“And I just as much,”","\"저도 꼭 그래요.\" 안주인이 말했다."],["said the landlady, “because I never have a quiet moment in my house except when you are listening to some one reading; for then you are so taken up that for the time being you forget to scold.”","\"이 집에서 제가 마음 편한 때라곤 당신이 누군가 읽어 주는 걸 듣고 있을 때뿐이거든요. 그럴 때면 당신이 어찌나 푹 빠져 있는지 그동안만큼은 잔소리하는 것도 잊어버리니까요.\""]],[["“That is true,” said Maritornes; “and, faith, I relish hearing these things greatly too, for they are very pretty; especially when they describe some lady or another in the arms of her knight under the orange trees, and the duenna who is keeping watch for them half dead with envy and fright; all this I say is as good as honey.”","\"그건 맞아요.\" 마리토르네스가 말했다. \"그리고, 정말이지 저도 이런 이야기를 듣는 게 무척 좋아요. 어찌나 어여쁜지요. 특히 어느 귀부인이 오렌지나무 아래서 자기 기사의 품에 안겨 있고, 곁을 지키던 시녀는 부러움과 두려움으로 반쯤 넋이 나가 있는 대목을 그릴 때가 그래요. 그런 건 죄다 꿀처럼 달콤하다니까요.\""]],[["“And you, what do you think, young lady?”","\"그럼 아가씨는 어떻게 생각하나요?\""],["said the curate turning to the landlord’s daughter.","신부가 주인의 딸 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다."]],[["“I don’t know indeed, señor,” said she; “I listen too, and to tell the truth, though I do not understand it, I like hearing it; but it is not the blows that my father likes that I like, but the laments the knights utter when they are separated from their ladies; and indeed they sometimes make me weep with the pity I feel for them.”","\"저야 정말 모르겠어요, 나리.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저도 듣긴 하는데, 솔직히 말씀드리면 잘 알아듣지는 못해도 듣는 건 좋아요. 다만 저는 아버지가 좋아하시는 그런 일격이 좋은 게 아니라, 기사들이 자기 귀부인과 헤어져서 토해 내는 그 한탄이 좋아요. 정말이지 가끔은 그들이 가엾어서 눈물이 다 난다니까요.\""]],[["“Then you would console them if it was for you they wept, young lady?”","\"그럼 만일 그들이 아가씨를 위해 우는 거라면, 아가씨가 그들을 위로해 주겠군요?\""],["said Dorothea.","도로테아가 말했다."]],[["“I don’t know what I should do,”","\"제가 어떻게 할지는 모르겠어요.\""],["said the girl; “I only know that there are some of those ladies so cruel that they call their knights tigers and lions and a thousand other foul names: and Jesus!","처녀가 말했다. \"다만 그런 귀부인들 중에는 어찌나 모진 이들이 있는지, 자기 기사들을 호랑이니 사자니, 그 밖에도 온갖 험한 이름으로 부른다는 것만은 알아요."],["I don’t know what sort of folk they can be, so unfeeling and heartless, that rather than bestow a glance upon a worthy man they leave him to die or go mad.","아이고 맙소사! 그렇게 무정하고 매정해서, 의젓한 남자에게 눈길 한 번 던져 주느니 차라리 그를 죽게 내버려 두거나 미치게 만드는 그런 여자들이 대체 어떤 부류인지 저는 도무지 모르겠어요."],["I don’t know what is the good of such prudery; if it is for honour’s sake, why not marry them?","그런 내숭이 무슨 소용인지 모르겠어요. 그게 명예 때문이라면, 차라리 그들과 혼인하면 될 게 아니에요?"],["That’s all they want.”","그 남자들이 바라는 건 그게 다인데 말이에요.\""]],[["“Hush, child,”","\"쉿, 얘야.\" 안주인이 말했다."],["said the landlady; “it seems to me thou knowest a great deal about these things, and it is not fit for girls to know or talk so much.”","\"네가 이런 일들을 너무 많이 아는 것 같구나. 처녀가 그렇게 많이 알거나 떠들어 대는 건 합당치 않은 일이야.\""]],[["“As the gentleman asked me, I could not help answering him,”","\"나리가 물으시니 대답하지 않을 수가 없었던 거예요.\""],["said the girl.","처녀가 말했다."]],[["“Well then,”","\"그럼 어디,\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “bring me these books, señor landlord, for I should like to see them.”","\"그 책들을 내게 가져와 보시오, 주인 양반. 한번 보고 싶구려.\""]],[["“With all my heart,”","\"기꺼이 그러지요.\""],["said he, and going into his own room he brought out an old valise secured with a little chain, on opening which the curate found in it three large books and some manuscripts written in a very good hand. The first that he opened he found to be “Don Cirongilio of Thrace,”","그가 말하고는 자기 방으로 들어가 작은 사슬로 잠긴 낡은 여행 가방을 들고 나왔는데, 그것을 열어 보니 신부는 그 안에서 커다란 책 세 권과 아주 좋은 필체로 쓴 원고 몇 장을 찾아냈다."],["and the second “Don Felixmarte of Hircania,” and the other the “History of the Great Captain Gonzalo Hernandez de Cordova, with the Life of Diego Garcia de Paredes.”","처음 펼친 것은 「트라키아의 돈 시롱힐리오」였고, 두 번째는 「이르카니아의 돈 펠릭스마르테」였으며, 나머지 하나는 「위대한 장수 곤살로 에르난데스 데 코르도바의 일대기, 디에고 가르시아 데 파레데스의 생애를 덧붙임」이었다."]],[["When the curate read the two first titles he looked over at the barber and said, “We want my friend’s housekeeper and niece here now.”","신부가 앞의 두 제목을 읽고는 이발사 쪽을 건너다보며 말했다. \"지금 여기 우리 친구의 가정부와 조카딸이 있었으면 좋겠구려.\""]],[["“Nay,”","\"아닐세.\" 이발사가 말했다."],["said the barber, “I can do just as well to carry them to the yard or to the hearth, and there is a very good fire there.”","\"이것들을 마당으로든 화덕으로든 가져가는 건 나 혼자서도 충분히 할 수 있네. 마침 저기 불도 아주 잘 피워져 있고 말이야.\""]],[["“What!","\"뭐라고요!"],["your worship would burn my books!”","나리께서 제 책을 태우시겠다고요!\""],["said the landlord.","주인이 말했다."]],[["“Only these two,”","\"이 두 권만이오.\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “Don Cirongilio, and Felixmarte.”","\"「돈 시롱힐리오」와 「펠릭스마르테」 말이오.\""]],[["“Are my books, then, heretics or phlegmatics that you want to burn them?”","\"그럼 제 책들이 이단자나 점액질이라도 된단 말입니까, 태워 버리시게요?\""],["said the landlord.","주인이 말했다."]],[["“Schismatics you mean, friend,”","\"분파주의자를 말하는 게지, 이 친구야.\""],["said the barber, “not phlegmatics.”","이발사가 말했다. \"점액질이 아니라.\""]],[["“That’s it,”","\"그렇지요, 그거예요.\" 주인이 말했다."],["said the landlord; “but if you want to burn any, let it be that about the Great Captain and that Diego Garcia; for I would rather have a child of mine burnt than either of the others.”","\"하지만 굳이 태우시려거든 저 위대한 장수와 디에고 가르시아에 관한 걸 태우세요. 저는 다른 두 권 중 하나가 타느니 차라리 제 자식이 타는 걸 보는 편이 낫겠습니다.\""]],[["“Brother,” said the curate, “those two books are made up of lies, and are full of folly and nonsense; but this of the Great Captain is a true history, and contains the deeds of Gonzalo Hernandez of Cordova, who by his many and great achievements earned the title all over the world of the Great Captain, a famous and illustrious name, and deserved by him alone; and this Diego Garcia de Paredes was a distinguished knight of the city of Trujillo in Estremadura, a most gallant soldier, and of such bodily strength that with one finger he stopped a mill-wheel in full motion; and posted with a two-handed sword at the foot of a bridge he kept the whole of an immense army from passing over it, and achieved such other exploits that if, instead of his relating them himself with the modesty of a knight and of one writing his own history, some free and unbiassed writer had recorded them, they would have thrown into the shade all the deeds of the Hectors, Achilleses, and Rolands.”","\"이보시오.\" 신부가 말했다. \"그 두 권은 죄다 거짓으로 지어낸 것이라 어리석음과 헛소리로 가득하오. 그러나 이 위대한 장수에 관한 것은 참된 역사로서, 코르도바의 곤살로 에르난데스의 행적을 담고 있소. 그는 그 수많은 위대한 공적으로 온 세상에서 '위대한 장수'라는 칭호를 얻었으니, 이는 명망 높고 빛나는 이름이며 오로지 그만이 받아 마땅한 것이오. 그리고 이 디에고 가르시아 데 파레데스는 에스트레마두라의 트루히요 시 출신의 이름난 기사로, 더없이 용맹한 군인이요, 그 몸의 힘이 어찌나 셌던지 한껏 돌아가는 방앗간 바퀴를 손가락 하나로 멈춰 세웠다 하오. 또한 양손검을 들고 다리 어귀에 버티고 서서 거대한 군대 전체가 그 다리를 건너지 못하게 막았으며, 그 밖에도 갖가지 위업을 이루었으니, 만일 그가 기사다운 겸손과, 제 손으로 제 역사를 쓰는 자의 겸손으로 몸소 그것을 이야기한 것이 아니라 자유롭고 공평한 어느 필자가 그것을 기록했더라면, 헥토르며 아킬레우스며 롤랑의 모든 행적을 무색하게 만들었을 것이오.\""]],[["“Tell that to my father,”","\"그런 얘긴 우리 아버지한테나 하시지요.\""],["said the landlord.","주인이 말했다."],["“There’s a thing to be astonished at!","\"참 놀라울 일도 많네요! 방앗간 바퀴를 멈춰 세웠다니!"],["Stopping a mill-wheel!","맹세코 나리께서는 제가 읽은 「이르카니아의 펠릭스마르테」를 읽으셔야 합니다."],["By God your worship should read what I have read of Felixmarte of Hircania, how with one single backstroke he cleft five giants asunder through the middle as if they had been made of bean-pods like the little friars the children make; and another time he attacked a very great and powerful army, in which there were more than a million six hundred thousand soldiers, all armed from head to foot, and he routed them all as if they had been flocks of sheep.","그는 단 한 번의 후려치기로 거인 다섯을 한복판으로 두 동강 냈는데, 마치 아이들이 콩깍지로 만드는 그 작은 수도사 인형이라도 되는 양 쪼개 버렸지요. 또 한번은 어마어마하게 크고 강력한 군대를 공격했는데, 거기엔 머리부터 발끝까지 무장한 병사가 백육십만 명도 넘게 있었건만, 그는 마치 양 떼라도 되는 양 그들을 모조리 무찔러 버렸답니다."]],[["“And then, what do you say to the good Cirongilio of Thrace, that was so stout and bold; as may be seen in the book, where it is related that as he was sailing along a river there came up out of the midst of the water against him a fiery serpent, and he, as soon as he saw it, flung himself upon it and got astride of its scaly shoulders, and squeezed its throat with both hands with such force that the serpent, finding he was throttling it, had nothing for it but to let itself sink to the bottom of the river, carrying with it the knight who would not let go his hold; and when they got down there he found himself among palaces and gardens so pretty that it was a wonder to see; and then the serpent changed itself into an old ancient man, who told him such things as were never heard. Hold your peace, señor; for if you were to hear this you would go mad with delight. A couple of figs for your Great Captain and your Diego Garcia!”","게다가, 그 어찌나 굳세고 담대했던 착한 트라키아의 시롱힐리오는 또 어떻고요? 그 책에 다 나와 있지요. 그가 어느 강을 따라 항해하고 있는데, 물 한가운데서 불을 뿜는 뱀이 그에게 덤벼들었답니다. 그러자 그는 그것을 보자마자 그 위로 몸을 날려 비늘 덮인 어깨에 올라타고는, 두 손으로 그 목을 어찌나 세게 졸랐던지, 뱀은 자기가 목이 졸리는 걸 알고 별수 없이 강바닥으로 가라앉을 수밖에 없었는데, 그러면서 끝내 손을 놓지 않은 그 기사를 함께 끌고 내려갔지요. 그렇게 바닥에 다다르니 그는 보기에도 놀라울 만큼 아름다운 궁전과 정원들 사이에 있게 되었고, 그러자 뱀은 늙디늙은 노인으로 변해서는 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 이야기들을 그에게 들려주었답니다. 입 다물고 계시지요, 나리. 이걸 들으시면 너무 좋아서 미쳐 버리실 테니까요. 나리의 그 위대한 장수니 디에고 가르시아니 하는 것들은 무화과 두 알 값어치도 안 됩니다요!\""]],[["Hearing this Dorothea said in a whisper to Cardenio, “Our landlord is almost fit to play a second part to Don Quixote.”","이 말을 듣고 도로테아가 카르데니오에게 속삭여 말했다. \"우리 주인장은 돈키호테 다음 자리를 맡아도 거의 손색이 없겠어요.\""]],[["“I think so,”","\"제 생각도 그렇소.\""],["said Cardenio, “for, as he shows, he accepts it as a certainty that everything those books relate took place exactly as it is written down; and the barefooted friars themselves would not persuade him to the contrary.”","카르데니오가 말했다. \"보다시피 저 사람은 그 책들이 이야기하는 모든 것이 적힌 그대로 정확히 일어났다고 틀림없는 사실로 받아들이고 있으니, 맨발의 수도사들이 와도 그 반대를 믿게 하지는 못할 거요.\""]],[["“But consider, brother,” said the curate once more, “there never was any Felixmarte of Hircania in the world, nor any Cirongilio of Thrace, or any of the other knights of the same sort, that the books of chivalry talk of; the whole thing is the fabrication and invention of idle wits, devised by them for the purpose you describe of beguiling the time, as your reapers do when they read; for I swear to you in all seriousness there never were any such knights in the world, and no such exploits or nonsense ever happened anywhere.”","\"하지만 잘 생각해 보시오, 이 친구.\" 신부가 다시 한번 말했다. \"세상에 이르카니아의 펠릭스마르테 같은 자도, 트라키아의 시롱힐리오 같은 자도, 기사도 책들이 떠들어 대는 그런 부류의 다른 어떤 기사도 결코 있었던 적이 없소. 그 모든 것은 한가한 재주꾼들이 꾸며 내고 지어낸 것이니, 당신네 일꾼들이 책을 읽으며 그러듯, 바로 당신이 말한 그 목적, 곧 시간을 즐겁게 때우려는 목적으로 그들이 고안한 것이오. 진실로 맹세코 말하건대, 세상에 그런 기사는 결코 없었고, 그런 위업이나 헛소리는 어디서도 일어난 적이 없소.\""]],[["“Try that bone on another dog,” said the landlord; “as if I did not know how many make five, and where my shoe pinches me; don’t think to feed me with pap, for by God I am no fool. It is a good joke for your worship to try and persuade me that everything these good books say is nonsense and lies, and they printed by the license of the Lords of the Royal Council, as if they were people who would allow such a lot of lies to be printed all together, and so many battles and enchantments that they take away one’s senses.”","\"그런 뼈다귀는 다른 개한테나 던져 보시지요.\" 주인이 말했다. \"제가 마치 다섯이 얼마인지도, 제 신발 어디가 발을 죄는지도 모르는 줄 아십니까. 저를 미음으로 먹여 어르려 마시지요. 맹세코 저는 바보가 아닙니다. 이 훌륭한 책들이 하는 말이 죄다 헛소리요 거짓이라고 저를 설득하려 드시다니, 나리도 참 농담도 잘하시네요. 게다가 이것들은 왕실 심의회 나리들의 허가를 받아 인쇄된 책들 아닙니까. 마치 그분들이 그런 거짓말 덩어리를 한꺼번에, 사람 정신을 쏙 빼놓는 그 많은 전투와 마법들을 죄다 인쇄하도록 허락할 분들이기라도 한 것처럼 말이에요.\""]],[["“I have told you, friend,” said the curate, “that this is done to divert our idle thoughts; and as in well-ordered states games of chess, fives, and billiards are allowed for the diversion of those who do not care, or are not obliged, or are unable to work, so books of this kind are allowed to be printed, on the supposition that, what indeed is the truth, there can be nobody so ignorant as to take any of them for true stories; and if it were permitted me now, and the present company desired it, I could say something about the qualities books of chivalry should possess to be good ones, that would be to the advantage and even to the taste of some; but I hope the time will come when I can communicate my ideas to some one who may be able to mend matters; and in the meantime, señor landlord, believe what I have said, and take your books, and make up your mind about their truth or falsehood, and much good may they do you; and God grant you may not fall lame of the same foot your guest Don Quixote halts on.”","\"내가 말하지 않았소, 이 친구.\" 신부가 말했다. \"이런 것은 우리의 한가한 생각을 달래 주려고 만들어진 것이라고 말이오. 잘 다스려진 나라에서 일하기 싫어하거나, 일할 의무가 없거나, 일할 수 없는 이들의 소일거리로 장기며 공놀이며 당구가 허용되듯이, 이런 부류의 책들도 인쇄가 허용되는 것이오. 누구든 그것들을 참된 이야기로 받아들일 만큼 무지한 자는 없으리라는 전제 위에서 말인데, 사실이 진정 그렇소. 그리고 만일 지금 내게 허락이 주어지고 여기 계신 분들이 원하신다면, 좋은 기사도 책이 갖추어야 할 자질에 관해 한마디 할 수도 있겠으니, 그것이 어떤 이들에게는 유익하고 또 입맛에도 맞으리다. 하지만 언젠가 이 일을 바로잡을 수 있을 만한 누군가에게 내 생각을 전할 때가 오기를 바라오. 그동안에는, 주인 양반, 내가 한 말을 믿고 당신 책들을 도로 가져가서, 그것들의 진위에 대해서는 스스로 마음을 정하시오. 부디 그 책들이 당신께 큰 보탬이 되기를 바라며, 당신 손님 돈키호테가 절뚝거리는 바로 그 발로 당신이 절름발이가 되지 않도록 하느님께서 지켜 주시기를.\""]],[["“No fear of that,”","\"그런 걱정은 마십시오.\" 주인이 대답했다."],["returned the landlord; “I shall not be so mad as to make a knight-errant of myself; for I see well enough that things are not now as they used to be in those days, when they say those famous knights roamed about the world.”","\"제가 저 자신을 편력 기사로 만들 만큼 미치지는 않을 테니까요. 저 유명한 기사들이 세상을 떠돌아다녔다는 그 시절과 지금은 사정이 같지 않다는 걸 저도 잘 알고 있거든요.\""]],[["Sancho had made his appearance in the middle of this conversation, and he was very much troubled and cast down by what he heard said about knights-errant being now no longer in vogue, and all books of chivalry being folly and lies; and he resolved in his heart to wait and see what came of this journey of his master’s, and if it did not turn out as happily as his master expected, he determined to leave him and go back to his wife and children and his ordinary labour.","이 대화가 한창일 때 산초가 모습을 드러냈는데, 이제 편력 기사는 더 이상 유행이 아니며 기사도 책들은 죄다 어리석음이요 거짓이라는 말을 듣고는 몹시 심란해하고 풀이 죽었다. 그래서 그는 주인의 이번 여정이 어찌 되는지 두고 보기로 마음속으로 작정했고, 만일 그것이 주인이 기대하는 만큼 행복하게 풀리지 않는다면, 주인을 떠나 아내와 자식들과 본래의 생업으로 돌아가기로 결심했다."]],[["The landlord was carrying away the valise and the books, but the curate said to him, “Wait; I want to see what those papers are that are written in such a good hand.” The landlord taking them out handed them to him to read, and he perceived they were a work of about eight sheets of manuscript, with, in large letters at the beginning, the title of “Novel of the Ill-advised Curiosity.” The curate read three or four lines to himself, and said, “I must say the title of this novel does not seem to me a bad one, and I feel an inclination to read it all.” To which the landlord replied, “Then your reverence will do well to read it, for I can tell you that some guests who have read it here have been much pleased with it, and have begged it of me very earnestly; but I would not give it, meaning to return it to the person who forgot the valise, books, and papers here, for maybe he will return here some time or other; and though I know I shall miss the books, faith I mean to return them; for though I am an innkeeper, still I am a Christian.”","주인이 여행 가방과 책들을 들고 가려는데, 신부가 그에게 말했다. \"잠깐만 기다리시오. 그렇게 좋은 필체로 쓰인 저 종이들이 무엇인지 보고 싶구려.\" 주인이 그것들을 꺼내 읽어 보라며 그에게 건네주자, 그는 그것이 여덟 장쯤 되는 원고로, 첫머리에 큰 글씨로 「무모한 호기심에 관한 소설」이라는 제목이 붙어 있음을 알아보았다. 신부가 혼자 서너 줄을 읽고는 말했다. \"확실히 이 소설의 제목이 내게는 나빠 보이지 않는구려. 전부 읽어 보고 싶은 마음이 드는데.\" 이에 주인이 대답했다. \"그럼 나리께서 읽어 보시는 게 좋겠습니다. 여기서 그걸 읽은 몇몇 손님들이 무척 흡족해하며 그것을 제게 간곡히 졸라 댔다는 걸 말씀드릴 수 있거든요. 하지만 저는 내주지 않았는데, 여기다 여행 가방과 책들과 종이들을 두고 간 사람에게 돌려주려는 생각에서지요. 그 사람이 언젠가 다시 이리로 돌아올지도 모르니까요. 책들이 아쉽긴 하겠지만, 정말이지 저는 그걸 돌려줄 작정입니다. 제가 객줏집 주인이긴 해도 그래도 기독교인이니까요.\""]],[["“You are very right, friend,”","\"참으로 옳은 말이오, 이 친구.\""],["said the curate; “but for all that, if the novel pleases me you must let me copy it.”","신부가 말했다. \"하지만 그래도 그 소설이 내 마음에 들거든 베껴 가게는 해 주셔야 하오.\""]],[["“With all my heart,”","\"기꺼이 그러지요.\""],["replied the host.","주인이 대답했다."]],[["While they were talking Cardenio had taken up the novel and begun to read it, and forming the same opinion of it as the curate, he begged him to read it so that they might all hear it.","그들이 이야기를 나누는 동안 카르데니오는 그 소설을 집어 들어 읽기 시작했고, 신부와 똑같은 의견에 이르자, 모두가 함께 들을 수 있도록 그것을 읽어 달라고 신부에게 청했다."]],[["“I would read it,”","\"읽어 드리겠소.\""],["said the curate, “if the time would not be better spent in sleeping.”","신부가 말했다. \"그 시간을 차라리 자는 데 쓰는 편이 낫지 않다면 말이오.\""]],[["“It will be rest enough for me,”","\"제게는 충분한 쉼이 될 거예요.\" 도로테아가 말했다."],["said Dorothea, “to while away the time by listening to some tale, for my spirits are not yet tranquil enough to let me sleep when it would be seasonable.”","\"어떤 이야기를 들으며 시간을 보내는 게요. 제 마음이 아직, 마땅히 잘 시간에 잠을 청할 만큼 평온하지가 않거든요.\""]],[["“Well then, in that case,” said the curate, “I will read it, if it were only out of curiosity; perhaps it may contain something pleasant.”","\"그럼 좋소, 그렇다면,\" 신부가 말했다. \"읽어 드리리다. 그저 호기심에서라도 말이오. 어쩌면 뭔가 즐거운 것이 담겨 있을지도 모르지요.\""]],[["Master Nicholas added his entreaties to the same effect, and Sancho too; seeing which, and considering that he would give pleasure to all, and receive it himself, the curate said, “Well then, attend to me everyone, for the novel begins thus.”","니콜라스 선생도 같은 뜻으로 간청을 보탰고, 산초도 그러했다. 이를 보고, 또 모두에게 즐거움을 주고 자신도 즐거움을 얻으리라 헤아린 신부가 말했다. \"그럼 좋소, 모두들 내게 귀를 기울이시오. 소설은 이렇게 시작하오.\""]],[["# CHAPTER XXXIII. IN WHICH IS RELATED THE NOVEL OF “THE ILL-ADVISED CURIOSITY”","# CHAPTER XXXIII."]],[["In Florence, a rich and famous city of Italy in the province called Tuscany, there lived two gentlemen of wealth and quality, Anselmo and Lothario, such great friends that by way of distinction they were called by all that knew them “The Two Friends.”","이탈리아의 토스카나라 불리는 지방에 자리한 부유하고 이름난 도시 피렌체에, 안셀모와 로타리오라는 부와 가문을 겸비한 두 신사가 살았는데, 둘은 어찌나 막역한 사이였던지 그들을 아는 모든 이가 구별 삼아 \"두 친구\"라 불렀다."],["They were unmarried, young, of the same age and of the same tastes, which was enough to account for the reciprocal friendship between them.","둘은 미혼이었고, 젊었으며, 나이가 같고 취향도 같았으니, 그것만으로도 두 사람 사이의 우정이 서로 오가는 까닭을 충분히 설명할 만했다."],["Anselmo, it is true, was somewhat more inclined to seek pleasure in love than Lothario, for whom the pleasures of the chase had more attraction; but on occasion Anselmo would forego his own tastes to yield to those of Lothario, and Lothario would surrender his to fall in with those of Anselmo, and in this way their inclinations kept pace one with the other with a concord so perfect that the best regulated clock could not surpass it.","사실 안셀모는 로타리오보다 사랑에서 즐거움을 찾으려는 성향이 다소 강한 편이었고, 로타리오는 사냥의 즐거움에 더 끌렸으나, 때로는 안셀모가 자신의 취향을 접고 로타리오의 취향을 따라 주었고, 로타리오 또한 자신의 것을 양보하여 안셀모의 취향에 맞추어 주었으니, 이렇게 두 사람의 마음 기우는 바가 서로 발맞추어 나아가는 그 조화로움은 아무리 정교하게 맞춘 시계라도 따라올 수 없을 정도였다."]],[["Anselmo was deep in love with a high-born and beautiful maiden of the same city, the daughter of parents so estimable, and so estimable herself, that he resolved, with the approval of his friend Lothario, without whom he did nothing, to ask her of them in marriage, and did so, Lothario being the bearer of the demand, and conducting the negotiation so much to the satisfaction of his friend that in a short time he was in possession of the object of his desires, and Camilla so happy in having won Anselmo for her husband, that she gave thanks unceasingly to heaven and to Lothario, by whose means such good fortune had fallen to her.","안셀모는 같은 도시에 사는 고귀한 가문 출신의 아름다운 처녀를 깊이 사랑하고 있었으니, 그녀는 더없이 훌륭한 부모의 딸이었고 그녀 자신도 더없이 훌륭하여, 안셀모는 자신이 무슨 일을 하든 빠뜨리지 않던 벗 로타리오의 동의를 얻어 그 부모에게 그녀를 아내로 청하기로 결심했고, 실제로 그리하였는데, 로타리오가 그 청혼을 전하는 사자가 되어 벗이 흡족해할 만큼 일을 잘 매듭지어 준 덕분에 안셀모는 머지않아 그토록 바라던 바를 손에 넣게 되었으며, 카밀라 또한 안셀모를 남편으로 얻은 것이 어찌나 행복했던지, 그러한 행운이 자신에게 굴러든 것을 두고 하늘과 로타리오에게 끊임없이 감사를 드렸다."],["The first few days, those of a wedding being usually days of merry-making, Lothario frequented his friend Anselmo’s house as he had been wont, striving to do honour to him and to the occasion, and to gratify him in every way he could; but when the wedding days were over and the succession of visits and congratulations had slackened, he began purposely to leave off going to the house of Anselmo, for it seemed to him, as it naturally would to all men of sense, that friends’ houses ought not to be visited after marriage with the same frequency as in their masters’ bachelor days: because, though true and genuine friendship cannot and should not be in any way suspicious, still a married man’s honour is a thing of such delicacy that it is held liable to injury from brothers, much more from friends.","처음 며칠 동안은, 혼례 무렵이라는 것이 으레 즐거운 날들이기 마련인지라, 로타리오는 예전에 그러했듯 벗 안셀모의 집을 자주 드나들며 그를 빛내 주고 그 경사를 빛내 주려, 또 할 수 있는 온갖 방법으로 그를 기쁘게 해 주려 애썼다. 그러나 혼례의 날들이 다 지나가고 줄을 잇던 방문과 축하가 뜸해지자, 그는 일부러 안셀모의 집에 발길을 끊기 시작했으니, 분별 있는 모든 사람이 으레 그러하듯 그에게도 이렇게 여겨졌던 까닭이다."],["Anselmo remarked the cessation of Lothario’s visits, and complained of it to him, saying that if he had known that marriage was to keep him from enjoying his society as he used, he would have never married; and that, if by the thorough harmony that subsisted between them while he was a bachelor they had earned such a sweet name as that of “The Two Friends,”","즉 결혼한 뒤의 벗의 집은 그 주인이 총각이던 시절과 같은 빈도로 드나들 곳이 아니라는 것이었다. 참되고 진실한 우정이란 어떤 식으로도 의심을 품을 수 없고 또 품어서도 안 되는 것이지만, 그럼에도 결혼한 남자의 명예란 워낙 미묘한 것이어서 형제로 인해서도 상하기 쉽고, 벗으로 인해서는 더더욱 그러하다고 여겨지기 때문이었다."],["he should not allow a title so rare and so delightful to be lost through a needless anxiety to act circumspectly; and so he entreated him, if such a phrase was allowable between them, to be once more master of his house and to come in and go out as formerly, assuring him that his wife Camilla had no other desire or inclination than that which he would wish her to have, and that knowing how sincerely they loved one another she was grieved to see such coldness in him.","안셀모는 로타리오의 발길이 끊긴 것을 알아채고 그에게 그것을 두고 불평하며 말하기를, 결혼이 자신을 그의 벗과 어울리는 즐거움에서 멀어지게 할 줄 알았더라면 자기는 결코 결혼하지 않았을 것이며, 자신이 총각이던 시절 둘 사이에 흐르던 그 더할 나위 없는 조화로 인해 \"두 친구\"라는 그토록 달콤한 이름을 얻었던 터에, 신중하게 처신하려는 부질없는 걱정 때문에 그토록 드물고 그토록 기쁜 칭호를 잃어버리도록 내버려 두어서는 안 된다 하였다. 그러면서 둘 사이에 그런 표현이 허락된다면, 다시 한번 자기 집의 주인이 되어 예전처럼 드나들어 달라고 간청하면서, 자기 아내 카밀라는 그가 그녀에게 바라는 그 마음 외에는 다른 어떤 바람이나 기울임도 없으며, 두 사람이 얼마나 진심으로 서로를 아끼는지 알기에 그에게서 그토록 냉랭함을 보는 것이 슬프다고 단언했다."]],[["To all this and much more that Anselmo said to Lothario to persuade him to come to his house as he had been in the habit of doing, Lothario replied with so much prudence, sense, and judgment, that Anselmo was satisfied of his friend’s good intentions, and it was agreed that on two days in the week, and on holidays, Lothario should come to dine with him; but though this arrangement was made between them Lothario resolved to observe it no further than he considered to be in accordance with the honour of his friend, whose good name was more to him than his own.","이 모든 말에, 그리고 예전처럼 자기 집에 드나들도록 그를 설득하려고 안셀모가 로타리오에게 한 그보다 더 많은 말에, 로타리오는 어찌나 신중하고 사리에 밝으며 분별 있게 대답했던지, 안셀모는 벗의 선의를 믿게 되었고, 두 사람은 로타리오가 일주일에 이틀, 그리고 축일에 그와 함께 식사하러 오기로 합의를 보았다. 그러나 둘 사이에 이런 약조가 맺어졌어도, 로타리오는 자기 자신의 명예보다도 더 소중히 여기는 벗의 명예에 부합한다고 여겨지는 선을 넘어서는 그 약조를 지키지 않기로 마음먹었다."],["He said, and justly, that a married man upon whom heaven had bestowed a beautiful wife should consider as carefully what friends he brought to his house as what female friends his wife associated with, for what cannot be done or arranged in the market-place, in church, at public festivals or at stations (opportunities that husbands cannot always deny their wives), may be easily managed in the house of the female friend or relative in whom most confidence is reposed.","그는 이렇게 말했으니, 그 말은 옳았다. 하늘이 아름다운 아내를 점지해 준 결혼한 남자라면, 자기 아내가 어떤 여자 벗들과 어울리는지를 살피는 것만큼이나 자기가 어떤 친구들을 집에 들이는지도 신중히 헤아려야 한다는 것이었으니, 광장에서나 교회에서나 공공의 축제에서나 행렬 참배에서는 (남편이 늘 아내에게 거절할 수만은 없는 그런 기회들에서는) 도무지 할 수도 꾸밀 수도 없는 일이, 가장 미더운 여자 벗이나 친척의 집에서는 손쉽게 이루어질 수 있기 때문이었다."],["Lothario said, too, that every married man should have some friend who would point out to him any negligence he might be guilty of in his conduct, for it will sometimes happen that owing to the deep affection the husband bears his wife either he does not caution her, or, not to vex her, refrains from telling her to do or not to do certain things, doing or avoiding which may be a matter of honour or reproach to him; and errors of this kind he could easily correct if warned by a friend.","로타리오는 또 말하기를, 결혼한 남자라면 누구나 자기 처신에서 저지를지 모를 어떤 부주의든 일러 줄 벗 하나쯤은 두어야 한다고 했으니, 이따금 남편이 아내에게 품은 깊은 정 때문에 그녀에게 주의를 주지 않거나, 그녀의 비위를 상하게 하지 않으려고 어떤 일을 하라거나 하지 말라거나 일러 주기를 삼가는 일이 생기는데, 그것을 하느냐 마느냐가 그에게 명예가 되기도 비난거리가 되기도 하기 때문이며, 이런 종류의 잘못은 벗이 경고해 준다면 손쉽게 바로잡을 수 있기 때문이었다."],["But where is such a friend to be found as Lothario would have, so judicious, so loyal, and so true?","그러나 로타리오가 바라 마지않는, 그토록 분별 있고 그토록 충직하며 그토록 진실한 벗을 대체 어디서 찾을 수 있단 말인가?"]],[["Of a truth I know not; Lothario alone was such a one, for with the utmost care and vigilance he watched over the honour of his friend, and strove to diminish, cut down, and reduce the number of days for going to his house according to their agreement, lest the visits of a young man, wealthy, high-born, and with the attractions he was conscious of possessing, at the house of a woman so beautiful as Camilla, should be regarded with suspicion by the inquisitive and malicious eyes of the idle public.","* usque ad aras: 라틴어로 \"제단에 이르기까지\"라는 뜻으로, 신의 뜻에 어긋나지 않는 한계까지라는 의미. 참으로 나는 알지 못한다."],["For though his integrity and reputation might bridle slanderous tongues, still he was unwilling to hazard either his own good name or that of his friend; and for this reason most of the days agreed upon he devoted to some other business which he pretended was unavoidable; so that a great portion of the day was taken up with complaints on one side and excuses on the other.","로타리오만이 그러한 벗이었으니, 그는 더없는 정성과 경계심으로 벗의 명예를 지켜보며, 약조한 대로 그 집에 가는 날의 수를 줄이고 깎고 덜어 내려 애썼으니, 부유하고 고귀한 가문 출신에다 자신이 지녔다고 자각하는 매력까지 갖춘 젊은이가 카밀라처럼 아름다운 여인의 집을 드나드는 것이, 한가로운 세상 사람들의 캐묻기 좋아하고 심술궂은 눈에 의심스럽게 비칠까 저어한 까닭이었다. 비록 그의 올곧음과 평판이 헐뜯는 혀를 잠재울 수 있을지언정, 그는 자기 자신의 좋은 이름도 벗의 좋은 이름도 위태롭게 하고 싶지 않았던 것이다."],["It happened, however, that on one occasion when the two were strolling together outside the city, Anselmo addressed the following words to Lothario.","이런 까닭에 약조한 날들의 대부분을 그는 피치 못할 일이라 둘러댄 어떤 다른 볼일에 쏟았으니, 하루의 큰 부분이 한쪽에서는 불평으로, 다른 쪽에서는 변명으로 채워지곤 했다. 그러던 어느 날, 두 사람이 도시 밖을 함께 거닐고 있을 때, 안셀모가 로타리오에게 다음과 같은 말을 건넸다."]],[["“Thou mayest suppose, Lothario my friend, that I am unable to give sufficient thanks for the favours God has rendered me in making me the son of such parents as mine were, and bestowing upon me with no niggard hand what are called the gifts of nature as well as those of fortune, and above all for what he has done in giving me thee for a friend and Camilla for a wife--two treasures that I value, if not as highly as I ought, at least as highly as I am able. And yet, with all these good things, which are commonly all that men need to enable them to live happily, I am the most discontented and dissatisfied man in the whole world; for, I know not how long since, I have been harassed and oppressed by a desire so strange and so unusual, that I wonder at myself and blame and chide myself when I am alone, and strive to stifle it and hide it from my own thoughts, and with no better success than if I were endeavouring deliberately to publish it to all the world; and as, in short, it must come out, I would confide it to thy safe keeping, feeling sure that by this means, and by thy readiness as a true friend to afford me relief, I shall soon find myself freed from the distress it causes me, and that thy care will give me happiness in the same degree as my own folly has caused me misery.”","\"벗 로타리오여, 자네는 짐작하겠지. 내가, 내 부모 같은 분들의 아들로 태어나게 해 주시고, 흔히 자연의 선물이라 일컫는 것들과 운명의 선물이라 일컫는 것들을 인색함 없이 베풀어 주신 것, 그리고 무엇보다도 자네를 벗으로, 카밀라를 아내로 주신 것 — 내가 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼은 아닐지언정 적어도 내가 할 수 있는 만큼은 소중히 여기는 두 보배를 주신 것 — 에 대해 하느님께 드린 그 은혜에 충분한 감사를 드릴 길이 없다는 것을 말일세. 그런데도, 사람이 행복하게 살아가는 데 흔히 필요한 모든 것이라 할 이 온갖 좋은 것들을 다 가지고도, 나는 온 세상에서 가장 만족하지 못하고 가장 흡족하지 못한 사람일세. 언제부터인지는 모르겠으나, 어찌나 기이하고 어찌나 유별난 욕망에 시달리고 짓눌려 왔던지, 나는 나 자신이 놀랍기만 하고, 혼자 있을 때면 나 자신을 탓하고 꾸짖으며, 그것을 억누르고 내 생각에서조차 감추려 애쓰지만, 그것을 작정하고 온 세상에 떠벌리려 애쓰는 것보다 나을 게 없는 결과만 얻을 뿐일세. 그러니 결국 그것은 밖으로 나오고야 말 것이기에, 나는 그것을 자네의 미더운 간직에 맡기려 하네. 이렇게 하면, 그리고 참된 벗으로서 기꺼이 나를 덜어 주려는 자네의 마음으로, 나는 머지않아 그것이 내게 끼치는 괴로움에서 벗어나게 되리라 확신하며, 내 어리석음이 내게 안긴 비참함만큼이나 자네의 보살핌이 내게 행복을 주리라 믿네.\""]],[["The words of Anselmo struck Lothario with astonishment, unable as he was to conjecture the purport of such a lengthy preamble; and though be strove to imagine what desire it could be that so troubled his friend, his conjectures were all far from the truth, and to relieve the anxiety which this perplexity was causing him, he told him he was doing a flagrant injustice to their great friendship in seeking circuitous methods of confiding to him his most hidden thoughts, for he well knew he might reckon upon his counsel in diverting them, or his help in carrying them into effect.","안셀모의 말은 로타리오를 놀라움에 빠뜨렸으니, 그토록 긴 머리말의 속뜻을 도무지 짐작할 수 없었던 까닭이다. 그는 무슨 욕망이 자기 벗을 그토록 괴롭히는 것인지 헤아려 보려 애썼으나, 그 짐작은 모두 진실과는 거리가 멀었고, 이 어리둥절함이 자아내는 불안을 덜고자, 그는 안셀모에게 말하기를, 자신의 가장 은밀한 생각을 자기에게 털어놓는 데 빙빙 둘러 가는 방법을 쓰는 것은 둘의 위대한 우정에 대한 명백한 모욕을 저지르는 일이라 했으니, 그러한 생각을 떨쳐 버리는 데는 자신의 충고를, 또 그것을 이루는 데는 자신의 도움을 기댈 수 있음을 안셀모가 잘 알고 있는 까닭이었다."]],[["“That is the truth,” replied Anselmo, “and relying upon that I will tell thee, friend Lothario, that the desire which harasses me is that of knowing whether my wife Camilla is as good and as perfect as I think her to be; and I cannot satisfy myself of the truth on this point except by testing her in such a way that the trial may prove the purity of her virtue as the fire proves that of gold; because I am persuaded, my friend, that a woman is virtuous only in proportion as she is or is not tempted; and that she alone is strong who does not yield to the promises, gifts, tears, and importunities of earnest lovers; for what thanks does a woman deserve for being good if no one urges her to be bad, and what wonder is it that she is reserved and circumspect to whom no opportunity is given of going wrong and who knows she has a husband that will take her life the first time he detects her in an impropriety? I do not therefore hold her who is virtuous through fear or want of opportunity in the same estimation as her who comes out of temptation and trial with a crown of victory; and so, for these reasons and many others that I could give thee to justify and support the opinion I hold, I am desirous that my wife Camilla should pass this crisis, and be refined and tested by the fire of finding herself wooed and by one worthy to set his affections upon her; and if she comes out, as I know she will, victorious from this struggle, I shall look upon my good fortune as unequalled, I shall be able to say that the cup of my desire is full, and that the virtuous woman of whom the sage says ‘Who shall find her?’ has fallen to my lot. And if the result be the contrary of what I expect, in the satisfaction of knowing that I have been right in my opinion, I shall bear without complaint the pain which my so dearly bought experience will naturally cause me. And, as nothing of all thou wilt urge in opposition to my wish will avail to keep me from carrying it into effect, it is my desire, friend Lothario, that thou shouldst consent to become the instrument for effecting this purpose that I am bent upon, for I will afford thee opportunities to that end, and nothing shall be wanting that I may think necessary for the pursuit of a virtuous, honourable, modest and high-minded woman. And among other reasons, I am induced to entrust this arduous task to thee by the consideration that if Camilla be conquered by thee the conquest will not be pushed to extremes, but only far enough to account that accomplished which from a sense of honour will be left undone; thus I shall not be wronged in anything more than intention, and my wrong will remain buried in the integrity of thy silence, which I know well will be as lasting as that of death in what concerns me. If, therefore, thou wouldst have me enjoy what can be called life, thou wilt at once engage in this love struggle, not lukewarmly nor slothfully, but with the energy and zeal that my desire demands, and with the loyalty our friendship assures me of.”","\"그것이 맞는 말일세.\" 안셀모가 대답했다. \"바로 그것을 믿기에, 벗 로타리오여, 자네에게 말하겠네. 나를 시달리게 하는 그 욕망이란, 내 아내 카밀라가 내가 생각하는 만큼 선하고 완전한지 알고 싶다는 것일세. 그런데 나는 그녀를 어떤 식으로든 시험해 보지 않고는, 곧 불이 금의 순도를 증명하듯 그 시험이 그녀의 미덕의 순결함을 증명하지 않고는, 이 점에 관한 진실을 두고 마음을 놓을 수가 없네. 벗이여, 나는 여자란 유혹을 받느냐 받지 않느냐에 비례해서만 정숙한 것이고, 간곡한 구애자들의 약속과 선물과 눈물과 끈질긴 졸라댐에 굴하지 않는 여자만이 강한 여자라고 확신하기 때문일세. 아무도 나쁜 짓을 하라고 부추기지 않는데 선하게 군다고 해서 여자가 무슨 감사를 받을 만하며, 그릇될 기회조차 주어지지 않고, 단 한 번이라도 부적절한 짓을 들켰다 하면 목숨을 앗아 갈 남편이 있음을 아는 여자가 조심스럽고 신중하게 군다고 해서 그게 무어 놀라운 일이겠는가? 그러니 나는 두려움 때문에, 혹은 기회가 없어서 정숙한 여자를, 유혹과 시험을 거쳐 승리의 면류관을 쓰고 나온 여자와 같은 반열에 두지 않네. 그러므로 이런 이유들과, 내가 품은 견해를 정당화하고 떠받치기 위해 자네에게 댈 수 있는 다른 여러 이유로 인해, 나는 내 아내 카밀라가 이 고비를 넘기를, 그리고 자신이 구애를 받고 있음을, 그것도 그녀에게 마음을 둘 만한 자격 있는 자에게서 구애받고 있음을 깨닫는 그 불길로 단련되고 시험받기를 바라네. 만일 그녀가, 내가 알기로 그러하리라 믿듯이, 이 싸움에서 승리하고 나온다면, 나는 내 행운을 견줄 데 없는 것으로 여길 것이며, 내 욕망의 잔이 가득 찼노라고, 그리고 현자가 '누가 그런 여인을 찾으랴?' 하고 말한 그 정숙한 여인이 내 몫으로 떨어졌노라고 말할 수 있을 것이네. 그리고 만일 그 결과가 내 기대와 반대로 나온다 하더라도, 내 견해가 옳았음을 알게 되는 만족 속에서, 그토록 비싼 값을 치르고 얻은 경험이 으레 내게 안길 그 고통을 불평 없이 견디겠네. 그리고 내 바람에 맞서 자네가 내놓을 어떤 말도 내가 그것을 실행에 옮기는 것을 막지는 못할 터이니, 벗 로타리오여, 나는 자네가 내가 마음먹은 이 목적을 이루는 도구가 되어 주기를 바라네. 내가 그 일을 위해 자네에게 기회를 마련해 줄 것이고, 정숙하고 명예롭고 정결하며 고결한 여인을 좇는 데 필요하다고 내가 여기는 것이라면 무엇 하나 모자람이 없게 할 것이네. 그리고 여러 이유 가운데서도, 내가 이 고된 일을 자네에게 맡길 마음이 든 것은, 만일 카밀라가 자네에게 정복당한다 해도 그 정복이 끝까지 밀어붙여지지는 않을 것이고, 명예심에서 비롯되어 끝내 하지 않을 일이 이루어진 것으로 칠 만큼만 나아가리라는 점을 헤아렸기 때문일세. 그리하여 나는 의도 외에는 아무것도 더 침해받지 않을 것이고, 내가 입은 침해는 자네의 침묵의 올곧음 속에 묻힐 것인데, 나와 관계된 일에 관한 한 그 침묵이 죽음의 침묵만큼이나 오래가리라는 것을 나는 잘 아네. 그러니 자네가 나로 하여금 삶이라 부를 만한 것을 누리게 하려거든, 미적지근하게도 게으르게도 말고, 내 욕망이 요구하는 그 열의와 정성으로, 그리고 우리의 우정이 내게 확신케 하는 그 충직함으로, 당장 이 사랑의 싸움에 나서 주게.\""]],[["Such were the words Anselmo addressed to Lothario, who listened to them with such attention that, except to say what has been already mentioned, he did not open his lips until the other had finished. Then perceiving that he had no more to say, after regarding him for awhile, as one would regard something never before seen that excited wonder and amazement, he said to him, “I cannot persuade myself, Anselmo my friend, that what thou hast said to me is not in jest; if I thought that thou wert speaking seriously I would not have allowed thee to go so far; so as to put a stop to thy long harangue by not listening to thee I verily suspect that either thou dost not know me, or I do not know thee; but no, I know well thou art Anselmo, and thou knowest that I am Lothario; the misfortune is, it seems to me, that thou art not the Anselmo thou wert, and must have thought that I am not the Lothario I should be; for the things that thou hast said to me are not those of that Anselmo who was my friend, nor are those that thou demandest of me what should be asked of the Lothario thou knowest. True friends will prove their friends and make use of them, as a poet has said, usque ad aras; whereby he meant that they will not make use of their friendship in things that are contrary to God’s will. If this, then, was a heathen’s feeling about friendship, how much more should it be a Christian’s, who knows that the divine must not be forfeited for the sake of any human friendship? And if a friend should go so far as to put aside his duty to Heaven to fulfil his duty to his friend, it should not be in matters that are trifling or of little moment, but in such as affect the friend’s life and honour. Now tell me, Anselmo, in which of these two art thou imperilled, that I should hazard myself to gratify thee, and do a thing so detestable as that thou seekest of me? Neither forsooth; on the contrary, thou dost ask of me, so far as I understand, to strive and labour to rob thee of honour and life, and to rob myself of them at the same time; for if I take away thy honour it is plain I take away thy life, as a man without honour is worse than dead; and being the instrument, as thou wilt have it so, of so much wrong to thee, shall not I, too, be left without honour, and consequently without life? Listen to me, Anselmo my friend, and be not impatient to answer me until I have said what occurs to me touching the object of thy desire, for there will be time enough left for thee to reply and for me to hear.”","이것이 안셀모가 로타리오에게 건넨 말이었으니, 로타리오는 어찌나 주의 깊게 그 말을 들었던지, 앞서 언급한 것을 말한 것 외에는 상대가 다 마칠 때까지 입을 열지 않았다. 그러다 상대가 더 할 말이 없음을 알아채고는, 이전에 한 번도 본 적 없어 경이와 놀라움을 자아내는 무언가를 바라보듯 잠시 그를 바라보고 나서, 그에게 말했다. \"벗 안셀모여, 자네가 내게 한 말이 농담이 아니라고는 도무지 믿어지지가 않네. 만일 자네가 진지하게 말하고 있다고 여겼다면 나는 자네가 그토록 멀리까지 가도록 내버려 두지 않았을 것이네. 그래서 자네의 말을 듣지 않음으로써 자네의 긴 일장 연설을 멈추게 하려 했던 것일세. 정말이지 의심스럽군. 자네가 나를 모르거나, 아니면 내가 자네를 모르거나 둘 중 하나가 아닌가 싶네. 하지만 아닐세, 나는 자네가 안셀모임을 잘 알고, 자네는 내가 로타리오임을 알지. 불행한 것은, 내가 보기엔, 자네가 예전의 그 안셀모가 아니라는 것이고, 또 내가 마땅히 그러해야 할 그 로타리오가 아니라고 자네가 생각했음이 틀림없다는 것일세. 자네가 내게 한 말들은 내 벗이던 그 안셀모의 말이 아니며, 자네가 내게 요구하는 것들도 자네가 아는 그 로타리오에게 청할 만한 것이 아니기 때문일세. 참된 벗들은 시인이 말한 대로 '제단에 이르기까지(usque ad aras)' 서로를 시험하고 서로를 활용하는 법인데, 그 말로 그가 뜻한 바는, 그들이 하느님의 뜻에 어긋나는 일에는 우정을 쓰지 않으리라는 것이었네. 그러니 이것이 이교도가 우정에 대해 품은 마음이었다면, 인간의 어떤 우정을 위해서도 신성한 것을 저버려서는 안 됨을 아는 그리스도교도에게는 얼마나 더 그러해야 하겠는가? 그리고 벗이 자기 벗에 대한 의무를 다하고자 하늘에 대한 의무를 제쳐 두는 데까지 나아간다 하더라도, 그것은 하찮거나 사소한 일에서가 아니라, 벗의 목숨과 명예에 관계된 일에서여야 마땅하네. 자, 말해 보게, 안셀모. 이 둘 가운데 어느 것이 자네에게 위태로워서, 내가 자네를 기쁘게 해 주려고 나 자신을 위태롭게 하고, 자네가 내게 구하는 그토록 가증스러운 일을 해야 한단 말인가? 정말이지 둘 다 아닐세. 오히려, 내가 이해하는 한, 자네는 내게 자네의 명예와 목숨을 앗고, 동시에 나 자신의 명예와 목숨까지 앗으려 애쓰고 수고하라고 청하고 있는 것일세. 내가 자네의 명예를 앗는다면 자네의 목숨을 앗는 것이 분명하니, 명예 없는 사람은 죽은 자보다 못하기 때문일세. 그리고 자네가 그러기를 바라는 대로 내가 자네에게 그토록 큰 해악을 끼치는 도구가 된다면, 나 역시 명예 없이, 따라서 목숨 없이 남게 되지 않겠는가? 벗 안셀모여, 내 말을 들어 보게. 그리고 자네 욕망의 대상에 관해 내게 떠오르는 바를 다 말할 때까지는 답하려 조급해하지 말게. 자네가 답하고 내가 들을 시간은 충분히 남아 있을 테니 말일세.\""]],[["“Be it so,”","\"좋아.\""],["said Anselmo, “say what thou wilt.”","안셀모가 말했다. \"하고 싶은 말을 다 하게.\""]],[["Lothario then went on to say, “It seems to me, Anselmo, that thine is just now the temper of mind which is always that of the Moors, who can never be brought to see the error of their creed by quotations from the Holy Scriptures, or by reasons which depend upon the examination of the understanding or are founded upon the articles of faith, but must have examples that are palpable, easy, intelligible, capable of proof, not admitting of doubt, with mathematical demonstrations that cannot be denied, like, ‘If equals be taken from equals, the remainders are equal:’ and if they do not understand this in words, and indeed they do not, it has to be shown to them with the hands, and put before their eyes, and even with all this no one succeeds in convincing them of the truth of our holy religion.","그러자 로타리오가 말을 이었다. \"안셀모, 내가 보기에 자네는 지금 무어인들이 늘 지니는 그런 마음 상태에 있는 듯하네."],["This same mode of proceeding I shall have to adopt with thee, for the desire which has sprung up in thee is so absurd and remote from everything that has a semblance of reason, that I feel it would be a waste of time to employ it in reasoning with thy simplicity, for at present I will call it by no other name; and I am even tempted to leave thee in thy folly as a punishment for thy pernicious desire; but the friendship I bear thee, which will not allow me to desert thee in such manifest danger of destruction, keeps me from dealing so harshly by thee.","그들은 성경의 인용으로도, 깨달음의 검토에 기대거나 신앙 조항에 근거한 논리로도 결코 자기네 교리의 그릇됨을 깨닫게 할 수가 없고, 오직 손에 잡히고 쉽고 알기 쉬우며 증명할 수 있고 의심의 여지가 없는, '같은 것에서 같은 것을 빼면 그 나머지도 같다' 같은, 부인할 수 없는 수학적 논증을 갖춘 본보기라야만 받아들이지."],["And that thou mayest clearly see this, say, Anselmo, hast thou not told me that I must force my suit upon a modest woman, decoy one that is virtuous, make overtures to one that is pure-minded, pay court to one that is prudent?","그리고 그것을 말로 알아듣지 못하면 — 실제로 알아듣지 못하지 — 손으로 보여 주고, 눈앞에 들이대야 하는데, 그렇게 다 해 줘도 아무도 그들에게 우리의 거룩한 종교의 진리를 납득시키지 못하네. 자네에게도 나는 바로 이런 식으로 해야겠네."],["Yes, thou hast told me so.","자네 안에서 솟아난 그 욕망이 어찌나 터무니없고 이성의 그림자라 할 만한 모든 것에서 동떨어져 있는지, 자네의 어리석음 — 지금으로선 그것을 달리 부를 이름이 없으니 — 을 두고 논리를 펴는 데 시간을 들이는 것이 헛수고이리라 여겨질 정도일세. 자네의 그 해로운 욕망에 대한 벌로 자네를 그 어리석음 속에 그대로 내버려 두고 싶은 마음마저 드네."],["Then, if thou knowest that thou hast a wife, modest, virtuous, pure-minded and prudent, what is it that thou seekest?","하지만 그토록 명백한 파멸의 위험 속에 자네를 저버리도록 두지 않는, 자네에 대한 내 우정이, 자네를 그토록 매정하게 대하지 못하게 막는구먼. 그리고 자네가 이를 똑똑히 알도록 말해 보게, 안셀모."],["And if thou believest that she will come forth victorious from all my attacks--as doubtless she would--what higher titles than those she possesses now dost thou think thou canst bestow upon her then, or in what will she be better then than she is now?","자네는 내게, 정숙한 여인에게 억지로 구애를 들이대고, 미덕 있는 여인을 꾀어내고, 정결한 마음을 지닌 여인에게 수작을 걸고, 신중한 여인에게 구애하라고 말하지 않았는가? 그래, 자네는 내게 그렇게 말했네."],["Either thou dost not hold her to be what thou sayest, or thou knowest not what thou dost demand.","그렇다면, 자네가 정숙하고 미덕 있고 정결한 마음에 신중한 아내를 두었음을 안다면, 대체 자네가 구하는 것이 무엇이란 말인가? 그리고 그녀가 내 모든 공세에서 승리하고 나오리라 — 의심할 바 없이 그러하겠지만 — 믿는다면, 그때 자네는 그녀가 지금 지닌 것보다 더 높은 무슨 칭호를 그녀에게 내릴 수 있다고 생각하며, 그때 그녀가 지금보다 무엇이 더 나아진단 말인가?"],["If thou dost not hold her to be what thou why dost thou seek to prove her instead of treating her as guilty in the way that may seem best to thee?","자네는 그녀를 자네가 말하는 그런 여인으로 여기지 않거나, 아니면 자네가 무엇을 구하는지를 모르거나 둘 중 하날세. 자네가 그녀를 자네가 말하는 그런 여인으로 여기지 않는다면, 어찌하여 자네가 보기에 가장 좋을 방식으로 그녀를 죄 있는 자로 다루지 않고 굳이 그녀를 시험하려 든단 말인가?"],["but if she be as virtuous as thou believest, it is an uncalled-for proceeding to make trial of truth itself, for, after trial, it will but be in the same estimation as before.","하지만 그녀가 자네가 믿는 만큼 미덕 있는 여인이라면, 진리 그 자체를 시험해 보려는 것은 쓸데없는 짓일세. 시험을 거친 뒤에도 그것은 그저 이전과 같은 평가에 머물 것이기 때문이지."],["Thus, then, it is conclusive that to attempt things from which harm rather than advantage may come to us is the part of unreasoning and reckless minds, more especially when they are things which we are not forced or compelled to attempt, and which show from afar that it is plainly madness to attempt them.","그러니 결론은 분명하네. 이득보다 해가 올 법한 일을 시도하는 것은, 더구나 그것이 우리가 시도하도록 강요받거나 떠밀린 것도 아니고, 멀리서 보아도 시도하는 것이 명백히 미친 짓임이 드러나는 일이라면, 그것은 사리를 분별 못 하고 무모한 자들이나 하는 짓이라는 것일세."]],[["“Difficulties are attempted either for the sake of God or for the sake of the world, or for both; those undertaken for God’s sake are those which the saints undertake when they attempt to live the lives of angels in human bodies; those undertaken for the sake of the world are those of the men who traverse such a vast expanse of water, such a variety of climates, so many strange countries, to acquire what are called the blessings of fortune; and those undertaken for the sake of God and the world together are those of brave soldiers, who no sooner do they see in the enemy’s wall a breach as wide as a cannon ball could make, than, casting aside all fear, without hesitating, or heeding the manifest peril that threatens them, borne onward by the desire of defending their faith, their country, and their king, they fling themselves dauntlessly into the midst of the thousand opposing deaths that await them. Such are the things that men are wont to attempt, and there is honour, glory, gain, in attempting them, however full of difficulty and peril they may be; but that which thou sayest it is thy wish to attempt and carry out will not win thee the glory of God nor the blessings of fortune nor fame among men; for even if the issue he as thou wouldst have it, thou wilt be no happier, richer, or more honoured than thou art this moment; and if it be otherwise thou wilt be reduced to misery greater than can be imagined, for then it will avail thee nothing to reflect that no one is aware of the misfortune that has befallen thee; it will suffice to torture and crush thee that thou knowest it thyself. And in confirmation of the truth of what I say, let me repeat to thee a stanza made by the famous poet Luigi Tansillo at the end of the first part of his ‘Tears of Saint Peter,’ which says thus:","\"어려운 일은 하느님을 위해서든, 세상을 위해서든, 아니면 그 둘 다를 위해서든 시도되는 법일세. 하느님을 위해 떠맡는 일이란 성인들이 인간의 몸으로 천사의 삶을 살고자 할 때 시도하는 일이고, 세상을 위해 떠맡는 일이란 흔히 운명의 축복이라 일컫는 것을 얻으려고 그 광막한 바다와 그토록 다양한 기후, 그토록 많은 낯선 나라를 가로지르는 사람들의 일이며, 하느님과 세상을 함께 위해 떠맡는 일이란 용감한 병사들의 일이니, 그들은 적의 성벽에서 포탄이 뚫을 만한 너비의 구멍을 보자마자 온갖 두려움을 떨쳐 버리고, 망설임도 없이, 자신들을 위협하는 명백한 위험도 아랑곳없이, 자기 신앙과 조국과 임금을 지키려는 욕망에 떠밀려, 자신들을 기다리는 수천 가지 죽음의 한복판으로 겁 없이 몸을 던지지. 사람들이 으레 시도하는 일이란 바로 이런 것들이고, 아무리 어려움과 위험으로 가득하다 해도 그런 일을 시도하는 데는 명예와 영광과 이득이 따르네. 하지만 자네가 시도하고 이루기를 바란다고 말하는 그 일은 자네에게 하느님의 영광도, 운명의 축복도, 사람들 사이의 명성도 가져다주지 못할 것일세. 설령 그 결말이 자네 바라는 대로 나온다 해도, 자네는 지금 이 순간보다 더 행복해지지도, 더 부유해지지도, 더 명예로워지지도 않을 것이고, 만일 그렇지 않게 나온다면 자네는 상상할 수 있는 것보다 더 큰 비참함에 빠지게 될 걸세. 그때는 자네에게 닥친 불행을 아무도 모른다고 곱씹어 봤자 아무 소용이 없을 것이고, 자네 자신이 그것을 안다는 사실만으로도 자네를 괴롭히고 짓누르기에 충분할 테니 말일세. 그리고 내가 하는 말의 진실을 뒷받침하고자, 이름난 시인 루이지 탄실로가 그의 '성 베드로의 눈물' 제1부 끝에 지은 한 연을 자네에게 들려주겠네. 그 시는 이러하네."]],[["The anguish and the shame but greater grew In Peter’s heart as morning slowly came; No eye was there to see him, well he knew, Yet he himself was to himself a shame; Exposed to all men’s gaze, or screened from view, A noble heart will feel the pang the same; A prey to shame the sinning soul will be, Though none but heaven and earth its shame can see.","아침이 더디 밝아 올수록 베드로의 가슴속 번민과 수치는 더욱 자라났네. 보는 눈 하나 없음을 그는 잘 알았으나, 그 자신이 그 자신에게 수치였으니, 뭇사람의 눈앞에 드러나든, 시야에서 가려지든, 고귀한 마음은 한결같이 그 아픔을 느끼리라. 죄지은 영혼은 수치의 먹이가 되리니, 오직 하늘과 땅만이 그 수치를 본다 할지라도."]],[["Thus by keeping it secret thou wilt not escape thy sorrow, but rather thou wilt shed tears unceasingly, if not tears of the eyes, tears of blood from the heart, like those shed by that simple doctor our poet tells us of, that tried the test of the cup, which the wise Rinaldo, better advised, refused to do; for though this may be a poetic fiction it contains a moral lesson worthy of attention and study and imitation. Moreover by what I am about to say to thee thou wilt be led to see the great error thou wouldst commit.","이처럼 그것을 비밀로 간직한다 해도 자네는 자네의 슬픔에서 벗어나지 못할 것이며, 오히려 끊임없이 눈물을 흘릴 것이네. 눈의 눈물이 아니라면, 가슴에서 흐르는 피눈물을 말일세. 마치 우리 시인이 들려준 그 어수룩한 의사처럼 말이야. 더 분별 있던 현명한 리날도는 마다했던 그 잔의 시험을 그자는 해 보려 들었지. 비록 이것이 시인의 허구라 하더라도, 거기에는 주의와 연구와 본받음에 값할 만한 도덕적 교훈이 담겨 있네. 게다가 내가 이제 자네에게 하려는 말을 들으면, 자네는 자네가 저지르려는 그 큰 잘못을 보게 될 걸세."]],[["“Tell me, Anselmo, if Heaven or good fortune had made thee master and lawful owner of a diamond of the finest quality, with the excellence and purity of which all the lapidaries that had seen it had been satisfied, saying with one voice and common consent that in purity, quality, and fineness, it was all that a stone of the kind could possibly be, thou thyself too being of the same belief, as knowing nothing to the contrary, would it be reasonable in thee to desire to take that diamond and place it between an anvil and a hammer, and by mere force of blows and strength of arm try if it were as hard and as fine as they said? And if thou didst, and if the stone should resist so silly a test, that would add nothing to its value or reputation; and if it were broken, as it might be, would not all be lost? Undoubtedly it would, leaving its owner to be rated as a fool in the opinion of all. Consider, then, Anselmo my friend, that Camilla is a diamond of the finest quality as well in thy estimation as in that of others, and that it is contrary to reason to expose her to the risk of being broken; for if she remains intact she cannot rise to a higher value than she now possesses; and if she give way and be unable to resist, bethink thee now how thou wilt be deprived of her, and with what good reason thou wilt complain of thyself for having been the cause of her ruin and thine own. Remember there is no jewel in the world so precious as a chaste and virtuous woman, and that the whole honour of women consists in reputation; and since thy wife’s is of that high excellence that thou knowest, wherefore shouldst thou seek to call that truth in question? Remember, my friend, that woman is an imperfect animal, and that impediments are not to be placed in her way to make her trip and fall, but that they should be removed, and her path left clear of all obstacles, so that without hindrance she may run her course freely to attain the desired perfection, which consists in being virtuous. Naturalists tell us that the ermine is a little animal which has a fur of purest white, and that when the hunters wish to take it, they make use of this artifice. Having ascertained the places which it frequents and passes, they stop the way to them with mud, and then rousing it, drive it towards the spot, and as soon as the ermine comes to the mud it halts, and allows itself to be taken captive rather than pass through the mire, and spoil and sully its whiteness, which it values more than life and liberty. The virtuous and chaste woman is an ermine, and whiter and purer than snow is the virtue of modesty; and he who wishes her not to lose it, but to keep and preserve it, must adopt a course different from that employed with the ermine; he must not put before her the mire of the gifts and attentions of persevering lovers, because perhaps--and even without a perhaps--she may not have sufficient virtue and natural strength in herself to pass through and tread under foot these impediments; they must be removed, and the brightness of virtue and the beauty of a fair fame must be put before her. A virtuous woman, too, is like a mirror, of clear shining crystal, liable to be tarnished and dimmed by every breath that touches it. She must be treated as relics are; adored, not touched. She must be protected and prized as one protects and prizes a fair garden full of roses and flowers, the owner of which allows no one to trespass or pluck a blossom; enough for others that from afar and through the iron grating they may enjoy its fragrance and its beauty. Finally let me repeat to thee some verses that come to my mind; I heard them in a modern comedy, and it seems to me they bear upon the point we are discussing. A prudent old man was giving advice to another, the father of a young girl, to lock her up, watch over her and keep her in seclusion, and among other arguments he used these:","\"말해 보게, 안셀모. 만일 하늘이나 행운이 자네를 더없이 빼어난 품질의 다이아몬드의 임자이자 합당한 주인으로 만들어 주었고, 그것을 본 모든 보석 세공인이 그 빼어남과 순도에 만족하여 한목소리로 한뜻이 되어, 순도와 품질과 정교함에서 그것이 그런 종류의 보석이 될 수 있는 모든 것을 다 갖추었다고 말했으며, 자네 자신도 달리 알 길이 없어 같은 믿음을 지녔다 치세. 그런데 자네가 그 다이아몬드를 가져다 모루와 망치 사이에 끼워 놓고, 그저 두들기는 힘과 팔의 완력으로, 그것이 사람들 말대로 그토록 단단하고 그토록 빼어난지 시험해 보고 싶어 한다면, 그게 자네에게 사리에 맞는 일이겠는가? 그리고 자네가 그리했는데, 그 돌이 그토록 어리석은 시험을 견뎌 낸다 한들, 그것이 그 값이나 명성에 보태 주는 것은 아무것도 없을 것이며, 만일 그것이 깨진다면 — 그럴 수도 있을 테니 — 모든 것이 사라지지 않겠는가? 의심할 바 없이 그러할 것이고, 그 임자는 모든 이의 눈에 바보로 평가받게 될 걸세. 그러니 벗 안셀모여, 헤아려 보게. 카밀라는 자네의 평가에서나 다른 이들의 평가에서나 더없이 빼어난 품질의 다이아몬드라는 것을, 그리고 그녀를 깨질 위험에 내모는 것은 이치에 어긋난다는 것을 말일세. 그녀가 온전히 남는다 해도 지금 지닌 것보다 더 높은 값으로 오를 수는 없고, 만일 그녀가 무너져 버텨 내지 못한다면, 그때 자네가 어떻게 그녀를 잃게 될지, 그리고 그녀와 자네 자신의 파멸의 원인이 된 것을 두고 얼마나 마땅히 자네 자신을 원망하게 될지 지금 곰곰이 생각해 보게. 정숙하고 미덕 있는 여인만큼 세상에 귀한 보석은 없으며, 여자의 명예란 모두 평판에 달려 있음을 기억하게. 그리고 자네 아내의 평판이 자네가 아는 그토록 높은 경지에 있는데, 어찌하여 자네는 그 진실을 의문에 부치려 든단 말인가? 벗이여, 기억하게. 여자란 불완전한 동물이어서, 그녀가 발을 헛디뎌 넘어지게 할 걸림돌을 그녀의 길에 놓아서는 안 되고, 오히려 그것들을 치워, 그녀의 길에서 온갖 장애를 비워 주어, 거리낌 없이 자기 길을 자유로이 달려가 바라던 완성에 — 곧 미덕 있는 여인이 되는 데에 — 이르게 해야 한다는 것을 말일세. 박물학자들은 흰담비라는 작은 짐승이 더없이 새하얀 털을 지녔다고 일러 주는데, 사냥꾼들이 그것을 잡으려 할 때 이런 술책을 쓴다고 하네. 그것이 자주 드나드는 길목을 알아낸 다음, 그 길을 진흙으로 막아 놓고, 그것을 몰아세워 그 자리로 내모는 것이지. 그러면 흰담비는 진흙에 이르자마자 멈춰 서서, 그 진창을 지나 자기 흰빛을 망치고 더럽히느니 차라리 사로잡히기를 택한다네. 그것은 자기 흰빛을 목숨과 자유보다 더 소중히 여기기 때문이지. 미덕 있고 정숙한 여인은 흰담비와 같고, 정결의 미덕은 눈보다 더 희고 더 깨끗하네. 그러니 그녀가 그것을 잃지 않고 지니고 간직하기를 바라는 자라면, 흰담비에게 쓰는 방법과는 다른 방법을 택해야 하네. 끈질긴 구애자들의 선물과 정성이라는 진창을 그녀 앞에 놓아서는 안 되네. 어쩌면 — 아니 '어쩌면'이랄 것도 없이 — 그녀 자신에게 이 걸림돌들을 지나 발밑에 짓밟을 만한 미덕과 타고난 힘이 충분치 않을지도 모르기 때문일세. 그것들은 치워야 하고, 미덕의 광채와 아름다운 명예의 아름다움을 그녀 앞에 놓아 주어야 하네. 미덕 있는 여인은 또한 거울과 같아서, 맑게 빛나는 수정 같아, 그것에 닿는 입김 하나하나에 흐려지고 어두워지기 쉽다네. 그녀는 성유물처럼 다루어야 하니, 흠모하되 만지지는 말아야 하지. 그녀는 장미와 꽃이 가득한 아름다운 정원을 지키고 아끼듯 지키고 아껴야 하니, 그 정원의 임자는 누구도 들어와 꽃 한 송이 꺾지 못하게 하고, 다른 이들은 그저 멀리서 쇠창살 사이로 그 향기와 아름다움을 즐기는 것으로 족하게 하지. 끝으로, 내게 떠오르는 시 몇 구절을 자네에게 들려주겠네. 어느 현대 희극에서 들은 것인데, 우리가 논하는 요점에 들어맞는 듯하네. 한 신중한 노인이 어린 딸을 둔 아버지인 다른 이에게, 딸을 가두어 놓고 지켜보며 칩거시키라고 충고하면서, 여러 논거 가운데 이런 말들을 썼다네. 여인은 유리로 된 것, 허나 그 깨지기 쉬움을 너무 꼬치꼬치 시험하지 않는 것이 상책이라. 무슨 일이 벌어질지 누가 알랴?"]],[["Woman is a thing of glass; But her brittleness ‘tis best Not too curiously to test: Who knows what may come to pass?","깨뜨리기란 손쉬운 일, 고칠 수 없는 것을 매질에 내맡기고, 온전케 할 수 없는 것을 산산조각 내려는 건 어리석은 짓이라."]],[["Breaking is an easy matter, And it’s folly to expose What you cannot mend to blows; What you can’t make whole to shatter.","그러니 이것은 누구나 참으로 여길 만하고, 그 까닭은 보기에도 분명하니, 다나에가 있는 곳에는 황금의 소나기 또한 있는 법이라."]],[["This, then, all may hold as true, And the reason’s plain to see; For if Danaes there be, There are golden showers too.","* 다나에: 그리스 신화의 인물로, 아버지가 그녀를 청동 탑에 가두었으나 제우스가 황금 비로 변해 들어가 임신하게 했다."]],[["“All that I have said to thee so far, Anselmo, has had reference to what concerns thee; now it is right that I should say something of what regards myself; and if I be prolix, pardon me, for the labyrinth into which thou hast entered and from which thou wouldst have me extricate thee makes it necessary.","\"안셀모, 지금껏 자네에게 한 말은 모두 자네에게 관계된 것이었네. 이제 나 자신에게 관계된 것을 좀 말하는 것이 옳겠지. 그리고 내 말이 장황하더라도 너그러이 봐 주게. 자네가 들어선, 그리고 나더러 자네를 끌어내 달라고 하는 그 미궁이 그것을 어쩔 수 없게 만드니 말일세."]],[["“Thou dost reckon me thy friend, and thou wouldst rob me of honour, a thing wholly inconsistent with friendship; and not only dost thou aim at this, but thou wouldst have me rob thee of it also. That thou wouldst rob me of it is clear, for when Camilla sees that I pay court to her as thou requirest, she will certainly regard me as a man without honour or right feeling, since I attempt and do a thing so much opposed to what I owe to my own position and thy friendship. That thou wouldst have me rob thee of it is beyond a doubt, for Camilla, seeing that I press my suit upon her, will suppose that I have perceived in her something light that has encouraged me to make known to her my base desire; and if she holds herself dishonoured, her dishonour touches thee as belonging to her; and hence arises what so commonly takes place, that the husband of the adulterous woman, though he may not be aware of or have given any cause for his wife’s failure in her duty, or (being careless or negligent) have had it in his power to prevent his dishonour, nevertheless is stigmatised by a vile and reproachful name, and in a manner regarded with eyes of contempt instead of pity by all who know of his wife’s guilt, though they see that he is unfortunate not by his own fault, but by the lust of a vicious consort. But I will tell thee why with good reason dishonour attaches to the husband of the unchaste wife, though he know not that she is so, nor be to blame, nor have done anything, or given any provocation to make her so; and be not weary with listening to me, for it will be for thy good.","\"자네는 나를 자네의 벗으로 여기면서, 우정과는 전혀 어울리지 않는 것, 곧 내게서 명예를 앗으려 하고 있네. 그리고 자네는 그것만 노리는 것이 아니라, 나로 하여금 자네에게서도 명예를 앗게 하려 드는구먼. 자네가 내게서 명예를 앗으려 한다는 것은 분명하네. 카밀라가 내가 자네 요구대로 그녀에게 구애하는 것을 보면, 그녀는 분명 나를 명예도 올바른 마음도 없는 사람으로 여길 테니, 내가 나 자신의 처지와 자네의 우정에 진 빚에 그토록 어긋나는 일을 시도하고 저지르는 셈이기 때문일세. 자네가 나로 하여금 자네에게서도 명예를 앗게 하려 든다는 것 또한 의심할 바 없네. 카밀라는 내가 그녀에게 구애를 들이대는 것을 보면, 내가 그녀에게서 무언가 가벼운 구석을 알아채어 그것이 나로 하여금 내 천한 욕망을 그녀에게 드러내도록 부추겼다고 생각할 것이며, 만일 그녀가 자신이 욕보았다고 여긴다면, 그녀의 불명예는 그녀에게 속한 자네에게도 닿는 것일세. 그래서 흔히 일어나는 그 일이 생겨나는 것이지. 곧, 간음한 여자의 남편은, 자기 아내가 본분을 저버린 것을 알지 못했거나 그럴 빌미를 준 적이 없거나, 혹은 (부주의하거나 게을러서) 자기 불명예를 막을 힘이 있었음에도 그러지 못했더라도, 그럼에도 천하고 비난받을 이름으로 낙인찍히며, 자기 아내의 죄를 아는 모든 이에게서 동정 대신 멸시의 눈초리로, 어떤 의미에서, 여겨지게 되는 일 말일세. 그가 자기 잘못이 아니라 음란한 배우자의 욕정 때문에 불운을 당했음을 그들이 빤히 보면서도 말이지. 하지만 정숙치 못한 아내의 남편이, 그녀가 그러한 줄 알지 못하고, 탓할 데도 없으며, 그녀를 그리 만들 어떤 짓도 빌미도 준 적이 없는데도, 어찌하여 마땅한 이유로 불명예가 그에게 따라붙는지를 말해 주겠네. 듣기에 지치지 말게, 그것이 자네에게 이로울 테니 말일세."]],[["“When God created our first parent in the earthly paradise, the Holy Scripture says that he infused sleep into Adam and while he slept took a rib from his left side of which he formed our mother Eve, and when Adam awoke and beheld her he said, ‘This is flesh of my flesh, and bone of my bone.’ And God said ‘For this shall a man leave his father and his mother, and they shall be two in one flesh; and then was instituted the divine sacrament of marriage, with such ties that death alone can loose them. And such is the force and virtue of this miraculous sacrament that it makes two different persons one and the same flesh; and even more than this when the virtuous are married; for though they have two souls they have but one will. And hence it follows that as the flesh of the wife is one and the same with that of her husband the stains that may come upon it, or the injuries it incurs fall upon the husband’s flesh, though he, as has been said, may have given no cause for them; for as the pain of the foot or any member of the body is felt by the whole body, because all is one flesh, as the head feels the hurt to the ankle without having caused it, so the husband, being one with her, shares the dishonour of the wife; and as all worldly honour or dishonour comes of flesh and blood, and the erring wife’s is of that kind, the husband must needs bear his part of it and be held dishonoured without knowing it. See, then, Anselmo, the peril thou art encountering in seeking to disturb the peace of thy virtuous consort; see for what an empty and ill-advised curiosity thou wouldst rouse up passions that now repose in quiet in the breast of thy chaste wife; reflect that what thou art staking all to win is little, and what thou wilt lose so much that I leave it undescribed, not having the words to express it. But if all I have said be not enough to turn thee from thy vile purpose, thou must seek some other instrument for thy dishonour and misfortune; for such I will not consent to be, though I lose thy friendship, the greatest loss that I can conceive.”","\"하느님이 지상 낙원에서 우리 최초의 조상을 창조하셨을 때, 성경은 하느님이 아담에게 잠을 불어넣으시고, 그가 잠든 사이 그의 왼쪽 갈비뼈 하나를 취하여 그것으로 우리 어머니 하와를 빚으셨으며, 아담이 깨어나 그녀를 보고는 '이는 내 살 중의 살이요, 내 뼈 중의 뼈로다' 하고 말했다고 하네. 그리고 하느님이 말씀하시기를 '이러므로 남자가 그 아비와 어미를 떠나, 둘이 한 몸을 이룰지니라' 하셨네. 그리하여 결혼이라는 거룩한 성사가 세워졌으니, 오직 죽음만이 풀 수 있는 그런 매듭으로 맺어진 것일세. 그리고 이 기적과도 같은 성사의 힘과 효험이 어찌나 큰지, 그것은 서로 다른 두 사람을 하나의 똑같은 살로 만들어 주며, 미덕 있는 이들이 결혼하면 그 이상이 되니, 그들은 두 영혼을 가졌으되 단 하나의 의지만을 가지기 때문일세. 그리하여 따라 나오는 결론은, 아내의 살이 그 남편의 살과 하나이자 똑같은 것이므로, 그 살에 끼칠지 모를 얼룩이나 그것이 입는 손상이 남편의 살에 떨어진다는 것이며, 비록 그가, 앞서 말했듯, 그 빌미를 준 적이 없을지라도 그러하다는 것이네. 발이나 몸의 어느 부위의 아픔을 온몸이 느끼는 것은 모두가 한 몸이기 때문이고, 머리가 발목의 상처를 — 자기가 그것을 일으키지 않았는데도 — 느끼는 것처럼, 남편도 그녀와 하나이기에 아내의 불명예를 함께 나눠 지는 것일세. 그리고 모든 세속의 명예나 불명예가 살과 피에서 비롯되고, 그릇된 아내의 명예도 그러한 종류인지라, 남편은 어쩔 수 없이 그 몫을 짊어지고, 자신도 모르는 새 불명예를 짊어진 자로 여겨지게 되는 것일세. 그러니 보게, 안셀모. 자네가 자네 미덕 있는 배우자의 평안을 어지럽히려 함으로써 무릅쓰는 위험을 말일세. 자네가 그 얼마나 공허하고 분별없는 호기심 때문에 자네 정숙한 아내의 가슴속에 지금 고요히 잠들어 있는 정념들을 흔들어 깨우려 하는지를 말일세. 곰곰이 생각해 보게. 자네가 모든 것을 걸고 얻으려는 것은 보잘것없고, 자네가 잃을 것은 어찌나 큰지, 나는 그것을 표현할 말이 없어 차마 묘사하지 못하고 그냥 두겠네. 하지만 내가 한 말 모두가 자네를 그 천한 목적에서 돌이키기에 충분치 않다면, 자네는 자네 불명예와 불행을 위한 다른 도구를 찾아야 할 것이네. 나는 비록 내가 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 상실인 자네의 우정을 잃는다 해도, 그러한 도구가 되는 데는 동의하지 않을 테니 말일세.\""]],[["Having said this, the wise and virtuous Lothario was silent, and Anselmo, troubled in mind and deep in thought, was unable for a while to utter a word in reply; but at length he said, “I have listened, Lothario my friend, attentively, as thou hast seen, to what thou hast chosen to say to me, and in thy arguments, examples, and comparisons I have seen that high intelligence thou dost possess, and the perfection of true friendship thou hast reached; and likewise I see and confess that if I am not guided by thy opinion, but follow my own, I am flying from the good and pursuing the evil. This being so, thou must remember that I am now labouring under that infirmity which women sometimes suffer from, when the craving seizes them to eat clay, plaster, charcoal, and things even worse, disgusting to look at, much more to eat; so that it will be necessary to have recourse to some artifice to cure me; and this can be easily effected if only thou wilt make a beginning, even though it be in a lukewarm and make-believe fashion, to pay court to Camilla, who will not be so yielding that her virtue will give way at the first attack: with this mere attempt I shall rest satisfied, and thou wilt have done what our friendship binds thee to do, not only in giving me life, but in persuading me not to discard my honour. And this thou art bound to do for one reason alone, that, being, as I am, resolved to apply this test, it is not for thee to permit me to reveal my weakness to another, and so imperil that honour thou art striving to keep me from losing; and if thine may not stand as high as it ought in the estimation of Camilla while thou art paying court to her, that is of little or no importance, because ere long, on finding in her that constancy which we expect, thou canst tell her the plain truth as regards our stratagem, and so regain thy place in her esteem; and as thou art venturing so little, and by the venture canst afford me so much satisfaction, refuse not to undertake it, even if further difficulties present themselves to thee; for, as I have said, if thou wilt only make a beginning I will acknowledge the issue decided.”","이 말을 하고 나서 현명하고 미덕 있는 로타리오는 입을 다물었고, 안셀모는 마음이 어지럽고 깊은 생각에 잠겨 한동안 대답이라고 한마디도 내놓지 못했으나, 마침내 입을 열었다. \"벗 로타리오여, 자네가 본 대로 나는 자네가 내게 들려주기로 한 말을 주의 깊게 들었네. 그리고 자네의 논거와 본보기와 비유 속에서 나는 자네가 지닌 그 높은 지성과, 자네가 다다른 참된 우정의 완벽함을 보았네. 또한 내가 자네의 견해를 따르지 않고 내 견해를 좇는다면, 나는 선에서 달아나 악을 좇는 셈임을 나는 보고 또 인정하네. 사정이 그러하니, 자네는 기억해 주게. 내가 지금 여자들이 이따금 시달리는 그 병을 앓고 있다는 것을 말일세. 흙이나 회반죽이나 숯, 그보다 더 고약한, 보기에도 역겹고 먹기에는 더더욱 역겨운 것들을 먹고 싶은 갈망이 그들을 사로잡을 때의 그 병 말일세. 그러니 나를 낫게 하려면 어떤 술책에 기댈 수밖에 없는데, 자네가 그저 시작만 해 준다면, 비록 그것이 미적지근하고 흉내만 내는 식이라 해도, 카밀라에게 구애를 시작해 주기만 한다면 그것은 손쉽게 이루어질 것이네. 그녀는 첫 공격에 미덕이 무너질 만큼 호락호락하지 않을 테니 말일세. 이 시도만으로도 나는 만족할 것이고, 자네는 우리 우정이 자네에게 지운 의무를 다하게 될 것이니, 곧 내게 생명을 주는 것뿐 아니라, 내 명예를 버리지 말라고 나를 설득하는 일까지 해 주는 셈일세. 그리고 자네가 이를 해야만 하는 데는 단 한 가지 이유가 있으니, 곧 내가 지금처럼 이 시험을 해 보기로 작정한 이상, 자네가 나로 하여금 내 약점을 다른 이에게 드러내게 하여, 자네가 내가 잃지 않도록 애쓰는 그 명예를 위태롭게 두어서는 안 된다는 것일세. 그리고 자네가 그녀에게 구애하는 동안 자네 명예가 카밀라의 평가에서 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 높이 서지 못한다 해도, 그것은 거의, 아니 전혀 중요치 않네. 머지않아 그녀에게서 우리가 기대하는 그 한결같음을 발견하게 되면, 자네는 우리의 계략에 관한 있는 그대로의 진실을 그녀에게 일러 줄 수 있을 것이고, 그리하여 그녀의 존중 속에서 자네 자리를 되찾을 수 있을 테니 말일세. 그러니 자네가 거는 것은 그토록 적고, 그 모험으로 내게 그토록 큰 만족을 줄 수 있으니, 설령 더한 어려움이 자네 앞에 나타난다 해도 그것을 떠맡기를 마다하지 말아 주게. 내가 말했듯, 자네가 시작만 해 준다면 나는 그 일이 결판난 것으로 받아들이겠네.\""]],[["Lothario seeing the fixed determination of Anselmo, and not knowing what further examples to offer or arguments to urge in order to dissuade him from it, and perceiving that he threatened to confide his pernicious scheme to some one else, to avoid a greater evil resolved to gratify him and do what he asked, intending to manage the business so as to satisfy Anselmo without corrupting the mind of Camilla; so in reply he told him not to communicate his purpose to any other, for he would undertake the task himself, and would begin it as soon as he pleased.","로타리오는 안셀모의 확고한 결심을 보고, 그를 거기서 단념시키기 위해 더 내놓을 본보기도 들이댈 논거도 알지 못했으며, 그가 자기 그 해로운 계획을 다른 누군가에게 털어놓겠다고 으름장 놓는 것을 알아채고는, 더 큰 화를 막고자 그를 흡족하게 해 주고 그가 청한 바를 들어주기로 마음먹었으니, 카밀라의 마음을 더럽히지 않으면서 안셀모를 만족시키도록 그 일을 처리할 작정이었다. 그리하여 그는 답하기를, 그 뜻을 다른 누구에게도 알리지 말라고 하면서, 그 일을 자신이 떠맡아 그가 원하는 대로 곧 시작하겠노라 했다."],["Anselmo embraced him warmly and affectionately, and thanked him for his offer as if he had bestowed some great favour upon him; and it was agreed between them to set about it the next day, Anselmo affording opportunity and time to Lothario to converse alone with Camilla, and furnishing him with money and jewels to offer and present to her.","안셀모는 그를 따뜻하고 다정하게 껴안으며, 마치 그가 자기에게 무슨 큰 은혜라도 베푼 양 그 제안에 고마워했다. 그리고 두 사람 사이에 이튿날부터 일에 착수하기로 합의가 이루어졌으니, 안셀모가 로타리오에게 카밀라와 단둘이 이야기 나눌 기회와 시간을 마련해 주고, 그녀에게 내밀고 선사할 돈과 보석을 대 주기로 했다."],["He suggested, too, that he should treat her to music, and write verses in her praise, and if he was unwilling to take the trouble of composing them, he offered to do it himself.","안셀모는 또한 그가 그녀에게 음악을 들려주고, 그녀를 기리는 시구를 지어야 한다고 일렀으며, 만일 그가 시 짓는 수고를 마다하면 자기가 직접 짓겠다고 했다."],["Lothario agreed to all with an intention very different from what Anselmo supposed, and with this understanding they returned to Anselmo’s house, where they found Camilla awaiting her husband anxiously and uneasily, for he was later than usual in returning that day.","로타리오는 안셀모가 짐작하는 것과는 사뭇 다른 속셈으로 모든 것에 동의했고, 이런 양해 아래 두 사람은 안셀모의 집으로 돌아왔는데, 거기서 카밀라가 남편을 애타고 불안하게 기다리고 있는 것을 보았으니, 그날따라 그가 평소보다 늦게 돌아온 까닭이었다."],["Lothario repaired to his own house, and Anselmo remained in his, as well satisfied as Lothario was troubled in mind; for he could see no satisfactory way out of this ill-advised business.","로타리오는 제집으로 물러갔고, 안셀모는 제집에 남았으니, 로타리오가 마음이 어지러운 만큼이나 그는 흡족해 있었다. 로타리오로서는 이 분별없는 일에서 빠져나갈 만족스러운 길이 보이지 않았던 것이다."],["That night, however, he thought of a plan by which he might deceive Anselmo without any injury to Camilla.","그러나 그날 밤 그는 카밀라에게 아무런 해도 끼치지 않으면서 안셀모를 속일 만한 계책 하나를 생각해 냈다."],["The next day he went to dine with his friend, and was welcomed by Camilla, who received and treated him with great cordiality, knowing the affection her husband felt for him.","이튿날 그는 벗과 함께 식사하러 갔고, 남편이 그에게 품은 정을 아는 카밀라에게서 매우 다정한 환대를 받았다."],["When dinner was over and the cloth removed, Anselmo told Lothario to stay there with Camilla while he attended to some pressing business, as he would return in an hour and a half.","식사가 끝나고 상보가 걷히자, 안셀모는 로타리오에게, 자기가 급한 볼일을 보고 와야 하니 카밀라와 함께 거기 있으라고 하면서, 한 시간 반이면 돌아오겠다고 했다."],["Camilla begged him not to go, and Lothario offered to accompany him, but nothing could persuade Anselmo, who on the contrary pressed Lothario to remain waiting for him as he had a matter of great importance to discuss with him.","카밀라는 그에게 가지 말라고 청했고, 로타리오는 그를 따라가겠다고 했으나, 무엇으로도 안셀모를 설득할 수 없었으니, 그는 오히려 자기가 그와 의논할 매우 중대한 일이 있다며 로타리오더러 자기를 기다리며 남아 있으라고 다그쳤다."],["At the same time he bade Camilla not to leave Lothario alone until he came back.","동시에 그는 카밀라에게, 자기가 돌아올 때까지 로타리오를 혼자 두지 말라고 일렀다."],["In short he contrived to put so good a face on the reason, or the folly, of his absence that no one could have suspected it was a pretence.","요컨대 그는 자기 부재의 이유, 곧 그 어리석음에 어찌나 그럴듯한 낯빛을 입혔던지, 그것이 꾸며 낸 일이라고는 누구도 의심할 수 없었다."]],[["Anselmo took his departure, and Camilla and Lothario were left alone at the table, for the rest of the household had gone to dinner.","안셀모가 떠나고, 카밀라와 로타리오는 식탁에 단둘이 남았으니, 집안의 나머지 사람들은 식사하러 갔던 것이다."],["Lothario saw himself in the lists according to his friend’s wish, and facing an enemy that could by her beauty alone vanquish a squadron of armed knights; judge whether he had good reason to fear; but what he did was to lean his elbow on the arm of the chair, and his cheek upon his hand, and, asking Camilla’s pardon for his ill manners, he said he wished to take a little sleep until Anselmo returned.","로타리오는 벗의 바람대로 자신이 결투장에 들어선 처지임을, 그것도 그 아름다움만으로 무장한 기사 한 부대를 무찌를 수 있는 적을 마주하고 있음을 알았으니, 그가 두려워할 만한 까닭이 있었는지 어떤지는 판단해 보라. 허나 그가 한 일이란, 의자 팔걸이에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손에 뺨을 기댄 채, 카밀라에게 무례를 용서해 달라고 청하며, 안셀모가 돌아올 때까지 잠을 좀 자고 싶다고 한 것이었다."],["Camilla in reply said he could repose more at his ease in the reception-room than in his chair, and begged of him to go in and sleep there; but Lothario declined, and there he remained asleep until the return of Anselmo, who finding Camilla in her own room, and Lothario asleep, imagined that he had stayed away so long as to have afforded them time enough for conversation and even for sleep, and was all impatience until Lothario should wake up, that he might go out with him and question him as to his success.","카밀라는 대답하기를, 의자에서보다는 응접실에서 더 편히 쉴 수 있을 테니 그리로 들어가 주무시라고 청했으나, 로타리오는 사양하고는 안셀모가 돌아올 때까지 거기서 잠들어 있었다. 안셀모는 카밀라가 제 방에 있고 로타리오는 잠들어 있는 것을 보고, 자기가 그토록 오래 자리를 비워 둘에게 이야기를 나누고도 잠까지 잘 만큼 넉넉한 시간을 마련해 주었거니 생각하고는, 로타리오가 깨어나기를 못내 조바심치며 기다렸으니, 그와 함께 나가 성과가 어떠했는지 캐물으려는 것이었다."],["Everything fell out as he wished; Lothario awoke, and the two at once left the house, and Anselmo asked what he was anxious to know, and Lothario in answer told him that he had not thought it advisable to declare himself entirely the first time, and therefore had only extolled the charms of Camilla, telling her that all the city spoke of nothing else but her beauty and wit, for this seemed to him an excellent way of beginning to gain her good-will and render her disposed to listen to him with pleasure the next time, thus availing himself of the device the devil has recourse to when he would deceive one who is on the watch; for he being the angel of darkness transforms himself into an angel of light, and, under cover of a fair seeming, discloses himself at length, and effects his purpose if at the beginning his wiles are not discovered.","모든 일이 그가 바라던 대로 되어 갔다. 로타리오가 깨어나자 두 사람은 곧장 집을 나섰고, 안셀모는 알고 싶어 안달하던 것을 물었으며, 로타리오는 답하기를, 첫 번에 자신을 온전히 드러내는 것은 마땅치 않다고 여겼기에, 그저 카밀라의 매력을 칭송하면서, 온 도시가 그녀의 아름다움과 재치 외에는 아무것도 입에 올리지 않는다고 그녀에게 말해 주었을 뿐이라 했으니, 이것이 그녀의 호감을 사 다음번에 자신의 말을 기껍게 듣도록 마음을 기울이게 하는 데 더없이 좋은 시작이라 여겨진 까닭이며, 그리하여 그는 경계하고 있는 자를 속이려 할 때 악마가 기대는 그 술책을 써먹은 셈이었다."],["All this gave great satisfaction to Anselmo, and he said he would afford the same opportunity every day, but without leaving the house, for he would find things to do at home so that Camilla should not detect the plot.","악마는 어둠의 천사이면서도 빛의 천사로 둔갑하여, 그럴듯한 겉모습 아래 마침내 제 모습을 드러내고, 처음에 그 간계가 들통나지 않으면 제 목적을 이루는 법이니 말이다. 이 모든 것이 안셀모를 매우 흡족하게 했고, 그는 날마다 같은 기회를 마련해 주되, 집을 비우지는 않겠다고 했으니, 카밀라가 그 음모를 알아채지 못하도록 집 안에서 할 일들을 찾아내겠다는 것이었다."]],[["Thus, then, several days went by, and Lothario, without uttering a word to Camilla, reported to Anselmo that he had talked with her and that he had never been able to draw from her the slightest indication of consent to anything dishonourable, nor even a sign or shadow of hope; on the contrary, he said she would inform her husband of it.","이리하여 여러 날이 지나갔고, 로타리오는 카밀라에게 한마디도 건네지 않으면서, 안셀모에게는 자기가 그녀와 이야기를 나누었으나 명예롭지 못한 어떤 일에 대해서도 그녀에게서 동의의 기미라곤 손톱만큼도, 아니 희망의 표징이나 그림자조차도 끌어낼 수 없었으며, 오히려 그녀가 그것을 남편에게 일러바치겠다고 했다고 보고했다."]],[["“So far well,”","\"여기까지는 좋아.\" 안셀모가 말했다."],["said Anselmo; “Camilla has thus far resisted words; we must now see how she will resist deeds.","\"카밀라가 지금껏 말에는 버텨 냈으니, 이제 그녀가 행동에는 어떻게 버티는지 봐야겠네. 내일 자네에게 금화 이천 크라운을 줄 테니 그것을 내밀거나 아예 선사하고, 그만큼 더 줄 테니 그녀를 꾀어낼 보석을 사도록 하게."],["I will give you to-morrow two thousand crowns in gold for you to offer or even present, and as many more to buy jewels to lure her, for women are fond of being becomingly attired and going gaily dressed, and all the more so if they are beautiful, however chaste they may be; and if she resists this temptation, I will rest satisfied and will give you no more trouble.”","여자란 곱게 차려입고 화사하게 꾸미고 다니는 것을 좋아하는 법이고, 아름다운 여자일수록 더더욱 그러하니, 아무리 정숙하다 해도 그렇지. 그러니 그녀가 이 유혹에도 버텨 낸다면, 나는 만족하고 자네를 더는 성가시게 하지 않겠네.\""]],[["Lothario replied that now he had begun he would carry on the undertaking to the end, though he perceived he was to come out of it wearied and vanquished.","로타리오는 답하기를, 이제 시작한 이상 끝까지 그 일을 밀고 나가겠으나, 자기가 지치고 패하여 거기서 빠져나오게 되리라는 것을 알겠노라 했다."],["The next day he received the four thousand crowns, and with them four thousand perplexities, for he knew not what to say by way of a new falsehood; but in the end he made up his mind to tell him that Camilla stood as firm against gifts and promises as against words, and that there was no use in taking any further trouble, for the time was all spent to no purpose.","이튿날 그는 사천 크라운을 받았고, 그와 함께 사천 가지 난감함도 받았으니, 새로운 거짓말로 무어라 둘러대야 할지 알 수 없었던 까닭이다. 그러나 끝내 그는 마음을 정하고, 카밀라가 말에 버틴 것만큼이나 선물과 약속에도 굳건히 버티고 있으니, 더 수고할 필요가 없고, 시간만 헛되이 다 흘러가고 있다고 그에게 말하기로 했다."]],[["But chance, directing things in a different manner, so ordered it that Anselmo, having left Lothario and Camilla alone as on other occasions, shut himself into a chamber and posted himself to watch and listen through the keyhole to what passed between them, and perceived that for more than half an hour Lothario did not utter a word to Camilla, nor would utter a word though he were to be there for an age; and he came to the conclusion that what his friend had told him about the replies of Camilla was all invention and falsehood, and to ascertain if it were so, he came out, and calling Lothario aside asked him what news he had and in what humour Camilla was.","그런데 우연이 일을 다른 식으로 이끌어, 안셀모가 다른 때처럼 카밀라와 로타리오를 단둘이 남겨 둔 다음, 어느 방에 틀어박혀 열쇠 구멍으로 둘 사이에 오가는 일을 지켜보고 엿듣도록 자리를 잡았는데, 반 시간이 넘도록 로타리오가 카밀라에게 한마디도 건네지 않으며, 한평생 거기 있는다 해도 한마디도 건네지 않으리라는 것을 알아챘다. 그리하여 그는 카밀라의 대답에 관해 벗이 자기에게 들려준 것이 모두 꾸며 낸 거짓말이라는 결론에 이르렀고, 정말 그러한지 확인하려고 밖으로 나와 로타리오를 따로 불러내, 무슨 소식이 있으며 카밀라는 어떤 심기였는지 물었다."],["Lothario replied that he was not disposed to go on with the business, for she had answered him so angrily and harshly that he had no heart to say anything more to her.","로타리오는 답하기를, 자기는 그 일을 더 끌고 나갈 마음이 없으니, 그녀가 어찌나 노엽고 매몰차게 대답했던지 그녀에게 더 무슨 말을 할 엄두가 나지 않는다 했다."]],[["“Ah, Lothario, Lothario,”","\"아, 로타리오, 로타리오.\" 안셀모가 말했다."],["said Anselmo, “how ill dost thou meet thy obligations to me, and the great confidence I repose in thee!","\"자네는 나에 대한 의무를, 그리고 내가 자네에게 거는 그 큰 믿음을 어찌 이리도 못 갚는가! 나는 방금 이 열쇠 구멍으로 지켜보았는데, 자네가 카밀라에게 한마디도 하지 않는 것을 보았네."],["I have been just now watching through this keyhole, and I have seen that thou has not said a word to Camilla, whence I conclude that on the former occasions thou hast not spoken to her either, and if this be so, as no doubt it is, why dost thou deceive me, or wherefore seekest thou by craft to deprive me of the means I might find of attaining my desire?”","그러니 나는 이전에도 자네가 그녀에게 말을 건넨 적이 없다고 결론짓네. 그리고 그것이, 의심할 바 없이 그러하듯, 사실이라면, 어찌하여 자네는 나를 속이며, 어찌하여 자네는 술수를 부려 내가 내 욕망을 이룰 수단을 찾을 길을 앗아 가려 든단 말인가?\""]],[["Anselmo said no more, but he had said enough to cover Lothario with shame and confusion, and he, feeling as it were his honour touched by having been detected in a lie, swore to Anselmo that he would from that moment devote himself to satisfying him without any deception, as he would see if he had the curiosity to watch; though he need not take the trouble, for the pains he would take to satisfy him would remove all suspicions from his mind.","안셀모는 더는 말하지 않았으나, 로타리오를 수치와 당혹으로 뒤덮기에는 충분한 말을 한 셈이었으니, 그는 거짓말이 들통난 탓에 제 명예가 건드려진 듯 느끼고는, 안셀모에게 맹세하기를, 만일 그가 궁금하여 지켜본다면 알게 되겠지만, 그 순간부터 어떤 속임수도 없이 그를 만족시키는 일에 전념하겠다 했으며, 다만 그가 그런 수고를 할 것까지는 없으니, 자기가 그를 만족시키려 들이는 정성이 그의 마음에서 온갖 의심을 걷어 낼 것이라 했다."],["Anselmo believed him, and to afford him an opportunity more free and less liable to surprise, he resolved to absent himself from his house for eight days, betaking himself to that of a friend of his who lived in a village not far from the city; and, the better to account for his departure to Camilla, he so arranged it that the friend should send him a very pressing invitation.","안셀모는 그를 믿었고, 그에게 더 자유롭고 들킬 염려가 덜한 기회를 마련해 주고자, 도시에서 멀지 않은 마을에 사는 자기 벗의 집으로 가, 여드레 동안 집을 비우기로 마음먹었다. 그리고 카밀라에게 자기가 떠나는 까닭을 더 그럴듯하게 둘러대려고, 그 벗으로 하여금 자기에게 매우 간곡한 초대장을 보내도록 일을 꾸몄다."]],[["Unhappy, shortsighted Anselmo, what art thou doing, what art thou plotting, what art thou devising?","불행하고 앞 못 보는 안셀모여, 그대는 무슨 짓을 하고 있는가, 무엇을 꾀하고 있는가, 무엇을 꾸미고 있는가?"],["Bethink thee thou art working against thyself, plotting thine own dishonour, devising thine own ruin.","곰곰이 생각해 보라. 그대는 그대 자신을 거슬러 일하고 있고, 그대 자신의 불명예를 꾀하고 있으며, 그대 자신의 파멸을 꾸미고 있음을."],["Thy wife Camilla is virtuous, thou dost possess her in peace and quietness, no one assails thy happiness, her thoughts wander not beyond the walls of thy house, thou art her heaven on earth, the object of her wishes, the fulfilment of her desires, the measure wherewith she measures her will, making it conform in all things to thine and Heaven’s.","그대의 아내 카밀라는 미덕 있고, 그대는 평화롭고 고요하게 그녀를 가졌으며, 아무도 그대의 행복을 넘보지 않고, 그녀의 생각은 그대 집의 담장 너머로 떠도는 일이 없으며, 그대는 그녀에게 지상의 하늘이요, 그녀의 바람의 대상이요, 그녀의 욕망의 성취요, 그녀가 자기 뜻을 재는 자이니, 만사에서 그것을 그대의 뜻과 하늘의 뜻에 맞추는도다."],["If, then, the mine of her honour, beauty, virtue, and modesty yields thee without labour all the wealth it contains and thou canst wish for, why wilt thou dig the earth in search of fresh veins, of new unknown treasure, risking the collapse of all, since it but rests on the feeble props of her weak nature?","그러하거늘, 그녀의 명예와 아름다움과 미덕과 정숙함이라는 광맥이 그대에게 수고 없이 그것이 품은 온갖 부와 그대가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 내어 주는데, 어찌하여 그대는 새로운 광맥을, 알지 못하던 새 보화를 찾아 땅을 파헤쳐, 그것이 오직 그녀의 약한 천성이라는 무른 받침대에 얹혀 있을 뿐인데, 모든 것의 무너짐을 무릅쓰려 하는가? 곰곰이 생각해 보라."],["Bethink thee that from him who seeks impossibilities that which is possible may with justice be withheld, as was better expressed by a poet who said:","불가능한 것을 구하는 자에게는 가능한 것마저 마땅히 거두어들여질 수 있음을. 어느 시인이 더 잘 표현했으니, 그는 이렇게 말했다."]],[["‘Tis mine to seek for life in death, Health in disease seek I, I seek in prison freedom’s breath, In traitors loyalty.","죽음 속에서 생명을 찾는 것이 내 일이요, 병 속에서 건강을 나는 찾으며, 감옥 속에서 자유의 숨결을, 배신자들 속에서 충성을 찾노라."],["So Fate that ever scorns to grant Or grace or boon to me, Since what can never be I want, Denies me what might be.","하여 내게 은총도 은혜도 베풀기를 언제나 마다하는 운명은, 결코 있을 수 없는 것을 내가 바라기에, 있을 법한 것마저 내게 거절하는도다."]],[["The next day Anselmo took his departure for the village, leaving instructions with Camilla that during his absence Lothario would come to look after his house and to dine with her, and that she was to treat him as she would himself.","이튿날 안셀모는 마을로 떠나면서 카밀라에게 일러두기를, 자기가 없는 동안 로타리오가 집을 돌보고 그녀와 함께 식사하러 올 것이니, 그를 자기 대하듯 대접하라고 했다."],["Camilla was distressed, as a discreet and right-minded woman would be, at the orders her husband left her, and bade him remember that it was not becoming that anyone should occupy his seat at the table during his absence, and if he acted thus from not feeling confidence that she would be able to manage his house, let him try her this time, and he would find by experience that she was equal to greater responsibilities.","카밀라는 신중하고 올바른 마음을 지닌 여자가 으레 그러하듯, 남편이 자기에게 남긴 분부에 마음이 괴로워, 자기가 없는 동안 누군가가 식탁에서 그의 자리를 차지하는 것은 온당치 않다고 그에게 일러 주면서, 만일 그가 자기더러 집안을 꾸려 낼 수 있다는 믿음이 없어 이러는 것이라면, 이번 한 번 자기를 시험해 보라고, 그러면 자기가 더 큰 책임도 감당할 수 있음을 몸소 알게 되리라 했다."],["Anselmo replied that it was his pleasure to have it so, and that she had only to submit and obey.","안셀모는 답하기를, 그렇게 하는 것이 자기 뜻이니, 그녀는 그저 순순히 따르기만 하면 된다고 했다."],["Camilla said she would do so, though against her will.","카밀라는 비록 제 뜻과는 어긋나나 그리하겠노라 했다."]],[["Anselmo went, and the next day Lothario came to his house, where he was received by Camilla with a friendly and modest welcome; but she never suffered Lothario to see her alone, for she was always attended by her men and women servants, especially by a handmaid of hers, Leonela by name, to whom she was much attached (for they had been brought up together from childhood in her father’s house), and whom she had kept with her after her marriage with Anselmo. The first three days Lothario did not speak to her, though he might have done so when they removed the cloth and the servants retired to dine hastily; for such were Camilla’s orders; nay more, Leonela had directions to dine earlier than Camilla and never to leave her side. She, however, having her thoughts fixed upon other things more to her taste, and wanting that time and opportunity for her own pleasures, did not always obey her mistress’s commands, but on the contrary left them alone, as if they had ordered her to do so; but the modest bearing of Camilla, the calmness of her countenance, the composure of her aspect were enough to bridle the tongue of Lothario. But the influence which the many virtues of Camilla exerted in imposing silence on Lothario’s tongue proved mischievous for both of them, for if his tongue was silent his thoughts were busy, and could dwell at leisure upon the perfections of Camilla’s goodness and beauty one by one, charms enough to warm with love a marble statue, not to say a heart of flesh. Lothario gazed upon her when he might have been speaking to her, and thought how worthy of being loved she was; and thus reflection began little by little to assail his allegiance to Anselmo, and a thousand times he thought of withdrawing from the city and going where Anselmo should never see him nor he see Camilla. But already the delight he found in gazing on her interposed and held him fast. He put a constraint upon himself, and struggled to repel and repress the pleasure he found in contemplating Camilla; when alone he blamed himself for his weakness, called himself a bad friend, nay a bad Christian; then he argued the matter and compared himself with Anselmo; always coming to the conclusion that the folly and rashness of Anselmo had been worse than his faithlessness, and that if he could excuse his intentions as easily before God as with man, he had no reason to fear any punishment for his offence.","안셀모가 떠나고, 이튿날 로타리오가 그의 집으로 왔으니, 카밀라는 그를 다정하고 정숙한 환대로 맞이했으나, 결코 로타리오가 자기를 단둘이서 보도록 두지 않았으니, 그녀는 늘 남녀 하인들을, 특히 레오넬라라는 자기 시녀를 곁에 두었던 까닭이다. 그녀는 그 시녀를 무척 아꼈는데(어릴 적부터 친정에서 함께 자란 사이였기에), 안셀모와 결혼한 뒤에도 그녀를 곁에 두고 있었다. 처음 사흘 동안 로타리오는 그녀에게 말을 건네지 않았으니, 상보를 걷고 하인들이 서둘러 식사하러 물러갔을 때라면 그럴 수도 있었으련만, 그러지 않았다. 카밀라의 분부가 그러했기 때문이다. 아니 그뿐 아니라, 레오넬라는 카밀라보다 먼저 식사하고 결코 그녀 곁을 떠나지 말라는 분부를 받았던 것이다. 그러나 레오넬라는 제 마음에 더 드는 다른 일에 생각이 쏠려 있었고, 자기 즐거움을 위해 그 시간과 기회가 탐났던지라, 늘 제 안주인의 명을 따르지는 않았고, 오히려 그러라는 분부라도 받은 양 둘을 단둘이 남겨 두곤 했다. 그러나 카밀라의 정숙한 몸가짐과, 차분한 낯빛과, 의젓한 자태는 로타리오의 혀를 묶어 두기에 충분했다. 그러나 카밀라의 그 많은 미덕이 로타리오의 혀에 침묵을 강요하는 데 미친 그 영향은 두 사람 모두에게 해로운 것이 되고 말았으니, 그의 혀가 잠잠한 동안 그의 생각은 분주하여, 카밀라의 선함과 아름다움의 빼어남을 하나하나 한가로이 곱씹을 수 있었던 까닭이다. 그것은 살로 된 가슴은 말할 것도 없고 대리석상이라도 사랑으로 데우기에 족한 매력이었다. 로타리오는 그녀에게 말을 건넬 수도 있었을 그때에 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀가 얼마나 사랑받을 만한 여인인지 생각했다. 그리하여 그 헤아림은 안셀모에 대한 그의 충정을 차츰차츰 갉아먹기 시작했고, 그는 수천 번이나 도시를 떠나, 안셀모가 결코 자기를 보지 못하고 자기도 카밀라를 보지 못할 곳으로 가 버릴까 생각했다. 그러나 이미 그녀를 바라보는 데서 찾는 그 기쁨이 끼어들어 그를 단단히 붙들었다. 그는 자신을 다잡고, 카밀라를 바라보는 데서 얻는 그 즐거움을 물리치고 억누르려 안간힘을 썼다. 혼자 있을 때면 자기 약함을 탓하며 스스로를 못된 벗이라, 아니 못된 그리스도교도라 일컬었다. 그러다가는 그 문제를 따져 보고 자신을 안셀모와 견주어 보았는데, 늘 이르는 결론은 안셀모의 어리석음과 무모함이 자기 신의 없음보다 더 고약했다는 것이며, 만일 자기 의도를 사람 앞에서만큼이나 하느님 앞에서도 손쉽게 변명할 수 있다면, 자기 잘못에 대해 어떤 벌도 두려워할 이유가 없다는 것이었다."]],[["In short the beauty and goodness of Camilla, joined with the opportunity which the blind husband had placed in his hands, overthrew the loyalty of Lothario; and giving heed to nothing save the object towards which his inclinations led him, after Anselmo had been three days absent, during which he had been carrying on a continual struggle with his passion, he began to make love to Camilla with so much vehemence and warmth of language that she was overwhelmed with amazement, and could only rise from her place and retire to her room without answering him a word.","요컨대 카밀라의 아름다움과 선함이, 앞 못 보는 남편이 그의 손에 쥐여 준 기회와 어우러져, 로타리오의 충직함을 무너뜨리고 말았다. 그리하여 그는 자기 마음 기우는 바가 이끄는 그 대상 외에는 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 않게 되었고, 안셀모가 떠난 지 사흘이 지나, 그 사흘 동안 자기 정념과 끊임없는 싸움을 벌인 끝에, 어찌나 격렬하고 뜨거운 말로 카밀라에게 사랑을 호소하기 시작했던지, 그녀는 놀라움에 압도되어, 그저 제자리에서 일어나 그에게 한마디 대꾸도 없이 제 방으로 물러갈 수밖에 없었다."],["But the hope which always springs up with love was not weakened in Lothario by this repelling demeanour; on the contrary his passion for Camilla increased, and she discovering in him what she had never expected, knew not what to do; and considering it neither safe nor right to give him the chance or opportunity of speaking to her again, she resolved to send, as she did that very night, one of her servants with a letter to Anselmo, in which she addressed the following words to him.","그러나 사랑과 더불어 늘 솟아나는 그 희망은, 이 물리치는 태도에도 로타리오 안에서 수그러들지 않았고, 오히려 카밀라를 향한 그의 정념은 더해 갔으며, 그녀는 그에게서 결코 예상치 못한 것을 발견하고는 어찌할 바를 몰랐다. 그리고 그에게 다시 자기에게 말을 건넬 틈이나 기회를 주는 것은 안전하지도 옳지도 않다고 여겨, 바로 그날 밤 자기 하인 하나를 시켜 안셀모에게 편지를 보내기로 마음먹었으니, 거기서 그녀는 그에게 다음과 같은 말을 적어 보냈다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXXIV. IN WHICH IS CONTINUED THE NOVEL OF “THE ILL-ADVISED CURIOSITY”","# CHAPTER XXXIV."]],[["“It is commonly said that an army looks ill without its general and a castle without its castellan, and I say that a young married woman looks still worse without her husband unless there are very good reasons for it.","흔히들 군대는 장수가 없으면 볼품없고 성은 성주가 없으면 초라하다고 말하는데, 저는 갓 결혼한 젊은 아내가 남편 없이 지내는 모습이야말로, 그럴 만한 아주 합당한 사정이 없는 한 그보다 더 초라한 법이라 말하고 싶습니다."],["I find myself so ill at ease without you, and so incapable of enduring this separation, that unless you return quickly I shall have to go for relief to my parents’ house, even if I leave yours without a protector; for the one you left me, if indeed he deserved that title, has, I think, more regard to his own pleasure than to what concerns you: as you are possessed of discernment I need say no more to you, nor indeed is it fitting I should say more.”","저는 당신 없이 지내는 것이 너무도 괴롭고 이 이별을 견딜 힘조차 없어, 당신이 어서 돌아오지 않으신다면 당신의 집을 지킬 사람 하나 없이 비워 두는 한이 있더라도 친정으로 가서 위안을 구해야만 할 듯합니다. 당신이 제게 남겨 두신 그 사람으로 말하자면, 정녕 그런 호칭을 받을 자격이 있는지 모르겠으나, 제 생각에 그는 당신과 관계된 일보다 제 한 몸의 즐거움에 더 마음을 두는 사람이옵니다. 당신은 분별 있는 분이시니 더는 말씀드릴 필요가 없을 것이며, 또한 제가 더 말하는 것이 온당치도 않을 것입니다."]],[["Anselmo received this letter, and from it he gathered that Lothario had already begun his task and that Camilla must have replied to him as he would have wished; and delighted beyond measure at such intelligence he sent word to her not to leave his house on any account, as he would very shortly return.","안셀모는 이 편지를 받고서, 로타리오가 이미 그 임무에 착수했으며 카밀라가 그가 바라던 대로 그에게 응답했음이 틀림없다고 헤아렸다. 그리고 그러한 소식에 더없이 기뻐하며, 자신이 곧 돌아갈 터이니 무슨 일이 있어도 집을 떠나지 말라는 전갈을 그녀에게 보냈다."],["Camilla was astonished at Anselmo’s reply, which placed her in greater perplexity than before, for she neither dared to remain in her own house, nor yet to go to her parents’; for in remaining her virtue was imperilled, and in going she was opposing her husband’s commands.","카밀라는 안셀모의 답신에 깜짝 놀랐으니, 그 답신이 그녀를 전보다 더 큰 곤혹 속으로 몰아넣었기 때문이다. 그녀는 제 집에 머물 엄두도, 친정으로 갈 엄두도 나지 않았다."],["Finally she decided upon what was the worse course for her, to remain, resolving not to fly from the presence of Lothario, that she might not give food for gossip to her servants; and she now began to regret having written as she had to her husband, fearing he might imagine that Lothario had perceived in her some lightness which had impelled him to lay aside the respect he owed her; but confident of her rectitude she put her trust in God and in her own virtuous intentions, with which she hoped to resist in silence all the solicitations of Lothario, without saying anything to her husband so as not to involve him in any quarrel or trouble; and she even began to consider how to excuse Lothario to Anselmo when he should ask her what it was that induced her to write that letter.","머무르자니 그녀의 정절이 위태로웠고, 떠나자니 남편의 명령을 거스르는 일이었다. 마침내 그녀는 자신에게 더 나쁜 길, 곧 머무르는 쪽을 택하기로 하고, 하인들에게 입길에 오를 빌미를 주지 않으려 로타리오 앞에서 달아나지 않기로 마음먹었다."],["With these resolutions, more honourable than judicious or effectual, she remained the next day listening to Lothario, who pressed his suit so strenuously that Camilla’s firmness began to waver, and her virtue had enough to do to come to the rescue of her eyes and keep them from showing signs of a certain tender compassion which the tears and appeals of Lothario had awakened in her bosom.","그러고는 남편에게 그런 편지를 쓴 것을 후회하기 시작했으니, 혹여 로타리오가 자신에게서 어떤 경박함을 알아채고 그로 인해 그가 마땅히 지켜야 할 예의를 저버리게 되었다고 남편이 상상할까 두려웠던 것이다. 그러나 자신의 올곧음을 믿었기에 그녀는 하느님과 자신의 정숙한 뜻에 모든 것을 맡겼으며, 그것으로써 로타리오의 온갖 유혹에 묵묵히 맞서되, 남편을 어떤 다툼이나 곤경에 끌어들이지 않으려 그에게는 아무 말도 하지 않으리라 바랐다."],["Lothario observed all this, and it inflamed him all the more.","심지어 그녀는, 안셀모가 무엇 때문에 그런 편지를 쓰게 되었느냐고 물어 올 때 로타리오를 어떻게 변호해 줄지까지 궁리하기 시작했다."],["In short he felt that while Anselmo’s absence afforded time and opportunity he must press the siege of the fortress, and so he assailed her self-esteem with praises of her beauty, for there is nothing that more quickly reduces and levels the castle towers of fair women’s vanity than vanity itself upon the tongue of flattery.","이처럼 분별 있다거나 실효가 있다기보다는 차라리 갸륵하다 할 만한 결심들을 품은 채, 그녀는 이튿날 로타리오의 말에 귀를 기울이며 머물렀는데, 그가 어찌나 끈질기게 구애하던지 카밀라의 굳센 마음이 흔들리기 시작했고, 로타리오의 눈물과 호소가 그녀의 가슴속에 일깨운 어떤 애틋한 연민의 기색이 그녀의 두 눈에 드러나지 않도록 막아 내느라 그녀의 정절은 진땀을 빼야 했다. 로타리오는 이 모든 것을 눈여겨보았고, 그것이 그를 더욱 불타오르게 했다."],["In fact with the utmost assiduity he undermined the rock of her purity with such engines that had Camilla been of brass she must have fallen.","요컨대 그는 안셀모의 부재가 시간과 기회를 주는 동안 이 요새의 공략에 박차를 가해야 한다고 느꼈으며, 그리하여 그녀의 미모를 찬미함으로써 그녀의 자존심을 공략했으니, 아첨하는 혀에 실린 허영심만큼 아름다운 여인들의 허영이라는 성루를 그토록 빠르게 무너뜨리고 허무는 것은 없기 때문이다."],["He wept, he entreated, he promised, he flattered, he importuned, he pretended with so much feeling and apparent sincerity, that he overthrew the virtuous resolves of Camilla and won the triumph he least expected and most longed for.","실로 그는 더없는 집요함으로, 카밀라가 청동으로 빚어졌다 한들 끝내 무너지고 말았을 그런 공성 기계로 그녀의 순결이라는 바위를 허물어 갔다. 그는 울었고, 애원했고, 약속했고, 아첨했고, 졸라 댔고, 어찌나 절절하고 진실해 보이는 태도로 꾸며 댔던지, 끝내 카밀라의 정숙한 결심을 무너뜨리고 자신이 가장 기대하지 못했으면서도 가장 갈망하던 승리를 거두었다."],["Camilla yielded, Camilla fell; but what wonder if the friendship of Lothario could not stand firm?","카밀라는 굴복했고, 카밀라는 쓰러졌다. 그러나 로타리오의 우정이 굳건히 버티지 못한 것이 무에 그리 놀랄 일이겠는가?"],["A clear proof to us that the passion of love is to be conquered only by flying from it, and that no one should engage in a struggle with an enemy so mighty; for divine strength is needed to overcome his human power.","이는 우리에게, 사랑의 정념이란 오직 그것에서 달아남으로써만 이겨 낼 수 있으며, 그토록 막강한 적과는 그 누구도 싸움을 벌여서는 안 된다는 명백한 증거이니, 그의 인간적인 힘을 이겨 내자면 신성한 힘이 필요하기 때문이다."],["Leonela alone knew of her mistress’s weakness, for the two false friends and new lovers were unable to conceal it.","안주인의 약점을 안 사람은 레오넬라뿐이었으니, 이 거짓된 두 벗이자 새로운 연인은 그것을 도무지 감출 수 없었던 것이다."],["Lothario did not care to tell Camilla the object Anselmo had in view, nor that he had afforded him the opportunity of attaining such a result, lest she should undervalue his love and think that it was by chance and without intending it and not of his own accord that he had made love to her.","로타리오는 안셀모가 무엇을 노렸는지, 또 안셀모가 그에게 그런 결과에 이를 기회를 마련해 주었다는 사실을 카밀라에게 말하고 싶지 않았으니, 그녀가 그의 사랑을 하찮게 여겨, 그가 그녀에게 구애한 것이 우연이며 본의가 아니라 제 뜻에서 비롯된 것이 아니라고 생각할까 염려했기 때문이다."]],[["A few days later Anselmo returned to his house and did not perceive what it had lost, that which he so lightly treated and so highly prized.","며칠 뒤 안셀모는 제 집으로 돌아왔으나, 그토록 가벼이 다루면서도 그토록 높이 떠받들던 그것을 자신이 잃었음을 알아채지 못했다."],["He went at once to see Lothario, and found him at home; they embraced each other, and Anselmo asked for the tidings of his life or his death.","그는 곧장 로타리오를 보러 가서 집에 있는 그를 만났다. 두 사람은 서로 끌어안았고, 안셀모는 자신의 삶이냐 죽음이냐를 가를 소식을 물었다."]],[["“The tidings I have to give thee, Anselmo my friend,”","\"내가 자네에게 전할 소식은 말일세, 안셀모, 나의 벗이여,\""],["said Lothario, “are that thou dost possess a wife that is worthy to be the pattern and crown of all good wives.","로타리오가 말했다. \"자네가 모든 어진 아내의 본보기요 면류관이 될 만한 아내를 두었다는 것일세."],["The words that I have addressed to her were borne away on the wind, my promises have been despised, my presents have been refused, such feigned tears as I shed have been turned into open ridicule.","내가 그녀에게 건넨 말들은 바람결에 흩날려 가 버렸고, 내 약속은 멸시당했으며, 내 선물은 거절당했고, 내가 흘린 거짓 눈물은 노골적인 비웃음으로 돌아왔다네."],["In short, as Camilla is the essence of all beauty, so is she the treasure-house where purity dwells, and gentleness and modesty abide with all the virtues that can confer praise, honour, and happiness upon a woman.","요컨대 카밀라가 모든 아름다움의 정수이듯, 그녀는 또한 순결이 깃들어 사는 보고이자, 한 여인에게 칭송과 명예와 행복을 안겨 줄 수 있는 온갖 미덕과 더불어 온유함과 정숙함이 머무는 곳일세. 자네 돈을 도로 가져가게, 나의 벗이여."],["Take back thy money, my friend; here it is, and I have had no need to touch it, for the chastity of Camilla yields not to things so base as gifts or promises.","여기 있네, 손댈 일조차 없었으니, 카밀라의 정절은 선물이나 약속 따위의 천한 것에는 굴하지 않는다네. 만족하게, 안셀모, 더는 시험하려 들지 말게."],["Be content, Anselmo, and refrain from making further proof; and as thou hast passed dryshod through the sea of those doubts and suspicions that are and may be entertained of women, seek not to plunge again into the deep ocean of new embarrassments, or with another pilot make trial of the goodness and strength of the bark that Heaven has granted thee for thy passage across the sea of this world; but reckon thyself now safe in port, moor thyself with the anchor of sound reflection, and rest in peace until thou art called upon to pay that debt which no nobility on earth can escape paying.”","그리고 자네가 여자들에 대해 품고 있고 또 품을 수 있는 그 온갖 의심과 의혹의 바다를 발도 적시지 않고 건넜으니, 다시 새로운 곤혹의 깊은 대양으로 뛰어들려 하지도 말고, 또 다른 키잡이를 데려와 하늘이 자네에게 이 세상의 바다를 건너라고 허락하신 그 배의 견고함과 굳셈을 시험해 보려 하지도 말게. 도리어 이제 자네는 항구에 무사히 들었다 여기고, 건전한 성찰이라는 닻으로 자신을 단단히 매어 두고서, 이 땅의 어떤 고귀함으로도 면할 수 없는 그 빚을 갚으라는 부름을 받을 때까지 평안히 쉬게나.\""]],[["Anselmo was completely satisfied by the words of Lothario, and believed them as fully as if they had been spoken by an oracle; nevertheless he begged of him not to relinquish the undertaking, were it but for the sake of curiosity and amusement; though thenceforward he need not make use of the same earnest endeavours as before; all he wished him to do was to write some verses to her, praising her under the name of Chloris, for he himself would give her to understand that he was in love with a lady to whom he had given that name to enable him to sing her praises with the decorum due to her modesty; and if Lothario were unwilling to take the trouble of writing the verses he would compose them himself.","안셀모는 로타리오의 말에 완전히 흡족해하며, 그것이 마치 신탁에서 나온 말이기라도 한 듯 온전히 믿었다. 그럼에도 그는, 단지 호기심과 즐길 거리를 위해서라도 그 일을 그만두지 말아 달라고 그에게 청했다. 다만 이후로는 전처럼 진지한 노력을 기울일 필요는 없으며, 그가 바라는 것은 그저 클로리스라는 이름으로 그녀를 찬미하는 시 몇 편을 써 달라는 것뿐이라고 했다. 자신이 카밀라에게, 자기가 어떤 귀부인을 사랑하고 있는데 그녀의 정숙함에 걸맞은 예의를 갖춰 찬미할 수 있도록 그 부인에게 클로리스라는 이름을 붙여 주었노라 일러 두겠다는 것이었다. 그러면서 만일 로타리오가 그 시를 쓰는 수고를 들이기 싫다면 자신이 직접 짓겠노라고 했다."]],[["“That will not be necessary,”","\"그럴 것까지는 없네,\""],["said Lothario, “for the muses are not such enemies of mine but that they visit me now and then in the course of the year.","로타리오가 말했다. \"내가 시신(詩神)들과 그리 척진 사이는 아니어서, 그들이 한 해에 한두 번쯤은 나를 찾아오니 말일세."],["Do thou tell Camilla what thou hast proposed about a pretended amour of mine; as for the verses will make them, and if not as good as the subject deserves, they shall be at least the best I can produce.”","자네는 카밀라에게 내가 가짜 연애를 하고 있다고 자네가 일러 두기로 한 그 일을 전해 두게. 시로 말하자면 내가 지어 줄 터이니, 그 주제에 걸맞을 만큼 훌륭하지는 못하더라도 적어도 내가 지을 수 있는 최선의 것은 되리라.\""],["An agreement to this effect was made between the friends, the ill-advised one and the treacherous, and Anselmo returning to his house asked Camilla the question she already wondered he had not asked before--what it was that had caused her to write the letter she had sent him.","분별없는 자와 배신하는 자, 이 두 벗 사이에 이런 취지의 약조가 맺어졌고, 안셀모는 제 집으로 돌아가 카밀라에게, 그녀가 진작 묻지 않은 것을 이상히 여기던 바로 그 물음을 던졌으니, 곧 무엇이 그녀로 하여금 자신에게 보낸 그 편지를 쓰게 했느냐는 것이었다."],["Camilla replied that it had seemed to her that Lothario looked at her somewhat more freely than when he had been at home; but that now she was undeceived and believed it to have been only her own imagination, for Lothario now avoided seeing her, or being alone with her.","카밀라는, 로타리오가 그가 집에 있을 때보다 다소 거리낌 없이 자신을 쳐다보는 듯이 느껴졌으나, 이제는 오해가 풀려 그것이 그저 제 상상에 지나지 않았다고 믿게 되었다고 답했으니, 로타리오가 이제는 자신과 마주치거나 단둘이 있는 것을 피하기 때문이라 했다."],["Anselmo told her she might be quite easy on the score of that suspicion, for he knew that Lothario was in love with a damsel of rank in the city whom he celebrated under the name of Chloris, and that even if he were not, his fidelity and their great friendship left no room for fear.","안셀모는 그녀에게 그런 의심일랑 마음 놓아도 좋다고 일렀으니, 자신이 알기로 로타리오는 시내의 어느 지체 높은 처녀와 사랑에 빠져 그녀를 클로리스라는 이름으로 노래하고 있으며, 설령 그렇지 않다 해도 그의 신의와 두 사람의 두터운 우정으로 보아 두려워할 것이 조금도 없다고 했다."],["Had not Camilla, however, been informed beforehand by Lothario that this love for Chloris was a pretence, and that he himself had told Anselmo of it in order to be able sometimes to give utterance to the praises of Camilla herself, no doubt she would have fallen into the despairing toils of jealousy; but being forewarned she received the startling news without uneasiness.","그런데 만일 카밀라가 이 클로리스에 대한 사랑이 거짓이며, 로타리오가 때때로 카밀라 자신을 찬미하는 말을 입에 올릴 수 있도록 그 일을 안셀모에게 미리 일러 둔 것이라는 사실을 로타리오에게서 진작 전해 듣지 못했더라면, 그녀는 틀림없이 질투의 절망스러운 올가미에 빠지고 말았으리라. 그러나 미리 귀띔을 받은 터라 그녀는 그 놀라운 소식을 아무 불안 없이 받아들였다."]],[["The next day as the three were at table Anselmo asked Lothario to recite something of what he had composed for his mistress Chloris; for as Camilla did not know her, he might safely say what he liked.","이튿날 세 사람이 식탁에 둘러앉았을 때 안셀모는 로타리오에게, 연인 클로리스를 위해 지은 것 가운데 무엇이든 한 편 읊어 달라고 청했으니, 카밀라가 그녀를 알지 못하므로 무슨 말을 해도 거리낄 것이 없으리라는 것이었다."]],[["“Even did she know her,” returned Lothario, “I would hide nothing, for when a lover praises his lady’s beauty, and charges her with cruelty, he casts no imputation upon her fair name; at any rate, all I can say is that yesterday I made a sonnet on the ingratitude of this Chloris, which goes thus:","\"그녀가 카밀라 부인을 안다 해도,\" 로타리오가 답했다. \"나는 아무것도 감추지 않을 걸세. 연인이 제 여인의 아름다움을 찬미하고 그녀의 냉정함을 탓하는 것은 그녀의 고운 이름에 누를 끼치는 일이 아니니 말일세. 어쨌든 내가 말할 수 있는 것은, 어제 이 클로리스의 무정함을 두고 소네트 한 편을 지었는데 그것이 이러하다는 것일세.\""]],[["At midnight, in the silence, when the eyes Of happier mortals balmy slumbers close, The weary tale of my unnumbered woes To Chloris and to Heaven is wont to rise.","소네트\n한밤중, 고요 속에, 더 복된 인간들의 두 눈이 달콤한 잠으로 감길 적에, 헤아릴 길 없는 내 슬픔의 지친 사연은 클로리스에게로, 또 하늘로 솟아오르네."],["And when the light of day returning dyes The portals of the east with tints of rose, With undiminished force my sorrow flows In broken accents and in burning sighs.","그리고 돌아온 낮의 빛이 동녘의 문을 장밋빛으로 물들일 때면, 내 비탄은 줄지 않은 기세로 끊긴 가락과 타는 한숨이 되어 흐르네."],["And when the sun ascends his star-girt throne, And on the earth pours down his midday beams, Noon but renews my wailing and my tears; And with the night again goes up my moan.","태양이 별들로 둘러싸인 옥좌에 오르고, 대지 위에 한낮의 빛살을 쏟아부을 적에, 정오는 그저 내 통곡과 눈물을 새로이 할 뿐."],["Yet ever in my agony it seems To me that neither Heaven nor Chloris hears.”","그리고 밤과 더불어 또다시 내 신음이 솟아오르나, 내 고뇌 속에서 줄곧 내게 여겨지기로는 하늘도 클로리스도 듣지 않는 듯하구나."]],[["The sonnet pleased Camilla, and still more Anselmo, for he praised it and said the lady was excessively cruel who made no return for sincerity so manifest.","소네트는 카밀라를 흡족하게 했고, 안셀모는 더욱 그러했으니, 그는 그것을 칭찬하며 이토록 분명한 진심에 아무 응답도 하지 않는 그 여인은 지나치게 무정하다고 말했다."],["On which Camilla said, “Then all that love-smitten poets say is true?”","그러자 카밀라가 말했다. \"그렇다면 사랑에 빠진 시인들이 하는 말은 다 사실인가요?\""]],[["“As poets they do not tell the truth,”","\"시인으로서야 그들이 진실을 말하지는 않지요,\""],["replied Lothario; “but as lovers they are not more defective in expression than they are truthful.”","로타리오가 답했다. \"허나 연인으로서 그들은 그 표현이 모자란 만큼이나 진실하답니다.\""]],[["“There is no doubt of that,”","\"그건 의심할 여지가 없지,\" 안셀모가 거들었으니, 카밀라 앞에서 로타리오의 생각을 받쳐 주고 떠받들고자 애가 탄 것이었다."],["observed Anselmo, anxious to support and uphold Lothario’s ideas with Camilla, who was as regardless of his design as she was deep in love with Lothario; and so taking delight in anything that was his, and knowing that his thoughts and writings had her for their object, and that she herself was the real Chloris, she asked him to repeat some other sonnet or verses if he recollected any.","그러나 카밀라는 그의 속셈에는 아랑곳하지 않았으니, 그만큼 그녀는 로타리오에게 깊이 빠져 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 그에게 속한 것이라면 무엇이든 기뻐하고, 그의 생각과 글이 자신을 향한 것이며 자신이 바로 진짜 클로리스임을 알았기에, 그녀는 그에게 혹 기억나는 다른 소네트나 시가 있거든 한 편 더 읊어 달라고 청했다."]],[["“I do,”","\"있긴 하지요,\" 로타리오가 답했다."],["replied Lothario, “but I do not think it as good as the first one, or, more correctly speaking, less bad; but you can easily judge, for it is this.","\"다만 첫 번째 것만큼 훌륭하지는, 아니 더 정확히 말하면 그만큼 형편없지는 않은 듯합니다. 허나 부인께서 쉽게 판단하실 수 있을 터이니, 그것이 이러합니다.\""]],[["I know that I am doomed; death is to me As certain as that thou, ungrateful fair, Dead at thy feet shouldst see me lying, ere My heart repented of its love for thee.","소네트\n나는 아노라, 내 운명이 정해졌음을. 죽음은 내게 그대, 은혜 모르는 미인이여, 내 마음이 그대 향한 사랑을 뉘우치기 전에 그대 발치에 쓰러진 나를 보게 되리란 것만큼 확실하구나."],["If buried in oblivion I should be, Bereft of life, fame, favour, even there It would be found that I thy image bear Deep graven in my breast for all to see.","설령 망각 속에 파묻혀 목숨도, 명성도, 은총도 빼앗긴다 한들, 거기서조차 모두가 보도록 내 가슴 깊이 새겨진 그대의 모습을 내가 지니고 있음이 드러나리라."],["This like some holy relic do I prize To save me from the fate my truth entails, Truth that to thy hard heart its vigour owes.","이것을 나는 어떤 거룩한 성물처럼 소중히 여기노니, 내 진실이 불러온 운명에서 나를 건지려 함이라. 그 진실은 그대의 모진 마음에서 그 기세를 얻은 것이로다."],["Alas for him that under lowering skies, In peril o’er a trackless ocean sails, Where neither friendly port nor pole-star shows.”","아아, 가엾어라, 어둑한 하늘 아래, 자취 없는 대양 위를 위태로이 항해하는 자여, 정다운 항구도, 길잡이 북극성도 보이지 않는 그곳을."]],[["Anselmo praised this second sonnet too, as he had praised the first; and so he went on adding link after link to the chain with which he was binding himself and making his dishonour secure; for when Lothario was doing most to dishonour him he told him he was most honoured; and thus each step that Camilla descended towards the depths of her abasement, she mounted, in his opinion, towards the summit of virtue and fair fame.","안셀모는 이 두 번째 소네트도 첫 번째 것을 칭찬했듯 칭찬했고, 이렇게 그는 자신을 옭아매며 제 불명예를 확실히 다지는 그 사슬에 한 고리 또 한 고리를 잇따라 보태어 갔다. 로타리오가 그를 가장 욕되게 하고 있을 때 그는 도리어 그에게 자신이 가장 명예롭다고 말했으며, 그리하여 카밀라가 제 타락의 나락으로 한 발짝씩 내려갈 때마다, 그의 생각으로는 그녀가 미덕과 고운 명성의 정점을 향해 한 발짝씩 올라가고 있었던 것이다."]],[["It so happened that finding herself on one occasion alone with her maid, Camilla said to her, “I am ashamed to think, my dear Leonela, how lightly I have valued myself that I did not compel Lothario to purchase by at least some expenditure of time that full possession of me that I so quickly yielded him of my own free will.","어느 날 카밀라는 하녀와 단둘이 있게 되자 그녀에게 이렇게 말했다. \"레오넬라, 내 사랑하는 아이야, 생각하면 부끄럽구나."],["I fear that he will think ill of my pliancy or lightness, not considering the irresistible influence he brought to bear upon me.”","내가 어찌 그리 스스로를 가벼이 여겼던지, 적어도 얼마간의 시간이라도 들이게 하여 로타리오가 나를 온전히 차지하도록 하지 않고, 그토록 빨리 내 발로 그에게 내주고 말았으니 말이다. 그가 내게 끼친 그 거역할 수 없는 힘은 헤아리지 않은 채, 내 무름이나 경박함을 나쁘게 여길까 두렵구나.\""]],[["“Let not that trouble you, my lady,”","\"마님, 그런 일로 심려치 마옵소서,\" 레오넬라가 말했다."],["said Leonela, “for it does not take away the value of the thing given or make it the less precious to give it quickly if it be really valuable and worthy of being prized; nay, they are wont to say that he who gives quickly gives twice.”","\"정녕 값지고 귀히 여길 만한 것이라면, 그것을 빨리 내준다 하여 그 값어치가 깎이거나 덜 귀해지는 법은 없사옵니다. 아니, 외려 빨리 주는 자는 두 번 주는 것이라고들 하지 않사옵니까.\""]],[["“They say also,”","\"허나 또 이런 말도 있지,\""],["said Camilla, “that what costs little is valued less.”","카밀라가 말했다. \"값싸게 얻은 것은 덜 귀히 여겨진다고.\""]],[["“That saying does not hold good in your case,” replied Leonela, “for love, as I have heard say, sometimes flies and sometimes walks; with this one it runs, with that it moves slowly; some it cools, others it burns; some it wounds, others it slays; it begins the course of its desires, and at the same moment completes and ends it; in the morning it will lay siege to a fortress and by night will have taken it, for there is no power that can resist it; so what are you in dread of, what do you fear, when the same must have befallen Lothario, love having chosen the absence of my lord as the instrument for subduing you? and it was absolutely necessary to complete then what love had resolved upon, without affording the time to let Anselmo return and by his presence compel the work to be left unfinished; for love has no better agent for carrying out his designs than opportunity; and of opportunity he avails himself in all his feats, especially at the outset. All this I know well myself, more by experience than by hearsay, and some day, señora, I will enlighten you on the subject, for I am of your flesh and blood too. Moreover, lady Camilla, you did not surrender yourself or yield so quickly but that first you saw Lothario’s whole soul in his eyes, in his sighs, in his words, his promises and his gifts, and by it and his good qualities perceived how worthy he was of your love. This, then, being the case, let not these scrupulous and prudish ideas trouble your imagination, but be assured that Lothario prizes you as you do him, and rest content and satisfied that as you are caught in the noose of love it is one of worth and merit that has taken you, and one that has not only the four S’s that they say true lovers ought to have, but a complete alphabet; only listen to me and you will see how I can repeat it by rote. He is to my eyes and thinking, Amiable, Brave, Courteous, Distinguished, Elegant, Fond, Gay, Honourable, Illustrious, Loyal, Manly, Noble, Open, Polite, Quickwitted, Rich, and the S’s according to the saying, and then Tender, Veracious: X does not suit him, for it is a rough letter; Y has been given already; and Z Zealous for your honour.”","\"그 말은 마님의 경우에는 들어맞지 않사옵니다,\" 레오넬라가 답했다. \"사랑이란, 제가 듣기로는, 때로는 날고 때로는 걷는 것이어서, 이 사람에게는 내달리고 저 사람에게는 더디 가며, 누구는 식히고 누구는 불태우며, 누구에게는 상처를 입히고 누구는 죽이옵니다. 또 제 욕망의 길을 떠나는 바로 그 순간에 그것을 마치고 끝내기도 하며, 아침에 요새를 에워싸서는 밤이면 그것을 함락하기도 하니, 그것을 당해 낼 힘이란 없는 까닭이옵니다. 그러니 마님께서 무엇을 겁내시고 무엇을 두려워하시옵니까, 사랑이 나리의 부재를 마님을 굴복시킬 도구로 골랐으니 로타리오 님께도 똑같은 일이 닥쳤을 것이 분명한데 말이옵니다. 게다가 그때 사랑이 작정한 바를 그 자리에서 이루는 것은 절대로 필요한 일이었으니, 안셀모 나리께서 돌아오시어 그 존재로 일이 마무리되지 못한 채 남겨지도록 만들 틈을 주어서는 안 되었기 때문이옵니다. 사랑이 제 뜻을 이루는 데에 기회보다 더 나은 일꾼은 없으며, 사랑은 제 온갖 위업에서, 특히 그 시작에서 기회를 십분 이용하옵니다. 이 모든 것을 저 자신이 잘 아니, 풍문으로보다는 경험으로 아는 것이옵니다. 그리고 언젠가, 마님, 제가 이 일에 대해 마님을 깨우쳐 드리겠나이다. 저 또한 마님과 같은 살과 피를 지닌 사람이니 말이옵니다. 더구나, 카밀라 마님, 마님께서 그리 빨리 자신을 내주거나 굴복하신 것이 아니오라, 먼저 로타리오 님의 온 영혼을 그 두 눈에서, 그 한숨에서, 그 말과 약속과 선물에서 보셨고, 그것으로, 또 그분의 훌륭한 자질로 그분이 마님의 사랑을 받을 만큼 얼마나 합당한 분인지를 알아보셨던 것이옵니다. 사정이 이러하오니 그런 깐깐하고 새침한 생각으로 마님의 마음을 어지럽히지 마옵시고, 로타리오 님께서도 마님께서 그분을 귀히 여기시듯 마님을 귀히 여기심을 확신하시옵소서. 또 마님께서 사랑의 올가미에 걸리셨으되, 마님을 사로잡은 것이 값지고 빼어난 사람이며, 그것도 참된 연인이라면 갖추어야 한다는 그 네 가지 S뿐 아니라 알파벳 전부를 갖춘 사람임을 흡족히 여기시고 마음 놓으시옵소서. 제 말을 들어 보시면 제가 그것을 줄줄 외울 수 있음을 아시게 될 것이옵니다. 제 눈과 생각으로 그분은 다정하고(Amiable), 용감하고(Brave), 정중하고(Courteous), 출중하고(Distinguished), 우아하고(Elegant), 애틋하고(Fond), 쾌활하고(Gay), 명예롭고(Honourable), 빛나고(Illustrious), 충직하고(Loyal), 남자답고(Manly), 고결하고(Noble), 솔직하고(Open), 점잖고(Polite), 재치 있고(Quickwitted), 부유하며(Rich), 그리고 그 속담에 나오는 S들이 있고, 그다음으로 다정하고(Tender), 진실하옵니다(Veracious). X는 거친 글자라 그분께 어울리지 않사옵고, Y는 이미 나왔으며, Z는 마님의 명예를 위해 열성을 다하는(Zealous) 것이옵니다.\" * 네 가지 S: 당시 스페인에서 참된 연인이 갖추어야 한다고 일컬어지던 네 덕목으로, 모두 S로 시작하는 단어였다(현명하다sabio, 한결같다solo, 진중하다solícito, 비밀을 지킨다secreto 등으로 전해진다)."]],[["Camilla laughed at her maid’s alphabet, and perceived her to be more experienced in love affairs than she said, which she admitted, confessing to Camilla that she had love passages with a young man of good birth of the same city. Camilla was uneasy at this, dreading lest it might prove the means of endangering her honour, and asked whether her intrigue had gone beyond words, and she with little shame and much effrontery said it had; for certain it is that ladies’ imprudences make servants shameless, who, when they see their mistresses make a false step, think nothing of going astray themselves, or of its being known. All that Camilla could do was to entreat Leonela to say nothing about her doings to him whom she called her lover, and to conduct her own affairs secretly lest they should come to the knowledge of Anselmo or of Lothario. Leonela said she would, but kept her word in such a way that she confirmed Camilla’s apprehension of losing her reputation through her means; for this abandoned and bold Leonela, as soon as she perceived that her mistress’s demeanour was not what it was wont to be, had the audacity to introduce her lover into the house, confident that even if her mistress saw him she would not dare to expose him; for the sins of mistresses entail this mischief among others; they make themselves the slaves of their own servants, and are obliged to hide their laxities and depravities; as was the case with Camilla, who though she perceived, not once but many times, that Leonela was with her lover in some room of the house, not only did not dare to chide her, but afforded her opportunities for concealing him and removed all difficulties, lest he should be seen by her husband. She was unable, however, to prevent him from being seen on one occasion, as he sallied forth at daybreak, by Lothario, who, not knowing who he was, at first took him for a spectre; but, as soon as he saw him hasten away, muffling his face with his cloak and concealing himself carefully and cautiously, he rejected this foolish idea, and adopted another, which would have been the ruin of all had not Camilla found a remedy. It did not occur to Lothario that this man he had seen issuing at such an untimely hour from Anselmo’s house could have entered it on Leonela’s account, nor did he even remember there was such a person as Leonela; all he thought was that as Camilla had been light and yielding with him, so she had been with another; for this further penalty the erring woman’s sin brings with it, that her honour is distrusted even by him to whose overtures and persuasions she has yielded; and he believes her to have surrendered more easily to others, and gives implicit credence to every suspicion that comes into his mind. All Lothario’s good sense seems to have failed him at this juncture; all his prudent maxims escaped his memory; for without once reflecting rationally, and without more ado, in his impatience and in the blindness of the jealous rage that gnawed his heart, and dying to revenge himself upon Camilla, who had done him no wrong, before Anselmo had risen he hastened to him and said to him, “Know, Anselmo, that for several days past I have been struggling with myself, striving to withhold from thee what it is no longer possible or right that I should conceal from thee. Know that Camilla’s fortress has surrendered and is ready to submit to my will; and if I have been slow to reveal this fact to thee, it was in order to see if it were some light caprice of hers, or if she sought to try me and ascertain if the love I began to make to her with thy permission was made with a serious intention. I thought, too, that she, if she were what she ought to be, and what we both believed her, would have ere this given thee information of my addresses; but seeing that she delays, I believe the truth of the promise she has given me that the next time thou art absent from the house she will grant me an interview in the closet where thy jewels are kept (and it was true that Camilla used to meet him there); but I do not wish thee to rush precipitately to take vengeance, for the sin is as yet only committed in intention, and Camilla’s may change perhaps between this and the appointed time, and repentance spring up in its place. As hitherto thou hast always followed my advice wholly or in part, follow and observe this that I will give thee now, so that, without mistake, and with mature deliberation, thou mayest satisfy thyself as to what may seem the best course; pretend to absent thyself for two or three days as thou hast been wont to do on other occasions, and contrive to hide thyself in the closet; for the tapestries and other things there afford great facilities for thy concealment, and then thou wilt see with thine own eyes and I with mine what Camilla’s purpose may be. And if it be a guilty one, which may be feared rather than expected, with silence, prudence, and discretion thou canst thyself become the instrument of punishment for the wrong done thee.”","카밀라는 하녀의 알파벳에 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 그녀가 스스로 말한 것보다 연애에 더 노련함을 알아챘으니, 레오넬라도 이를 인정하여 같은 도시의 좋은 가문 출신 젊은이와 연애한 일이 있노라 카밀라에게 털어놓았다. 카밀라는 이 일로 마음이 편치 않았으니, 그것이 자신의 명예를 위태롭게 하는 빌미가 될까 두려워, 그 정분이 말로만 그친 것인지를 물었다. 그러자 레오넬라는 부끄러운 기색이라곤 거의 없이 뻔뻔스럽게 그 이상이었노라 답했으니, 안주인들의 경솔함이 하녀들을 파렴치하게 만드는 것은 분명한 일이어서, 하녀들은 안주인이 그릇된 발을 내딛는 것을 보면 저 자신이 엇나가는 것도, 그것이 알려지는 것도 대수롭지 않게 여기기 때문이다. 카밀라가 할 수 있는 일이라곤, 레오넬라에게 자신의 행실에 관해서는 그녀가 연인이라 부르는 자에게 아무 말도 하지 말 것이며, 그녀 자신의 일도 안셀모나 로타리오의 귀에 들어가지 않도록 은밀히 처신하라고 당부하는 것뿐이었다. 레오넬라는 그러겠노라 했으나, 그 약속을 지킨 방식이라는 것이, 자신으로 말미암아 평판을 잃게 되리라는 카밀라의 우려를 도리어 굳혀 주는 식이었다. 이 막돼먹고 뻔뻔한 레오넬라는 안주인의 행실이 예전 같지 않음을 알아채자마자, 안주인이 제 연인을 본다 한들 감히 그를 들춰내지 못하리라 믿고, 대담하게도 제 연인을 집 안에 끌어들였던 것이다. 안주인들의 죄는 여러 폐해 가운데 이런 화근도 불러오니, 곧 안주인들이 제 하인들의 노예가 되어 제 방종과 타락을 감추지 않을 수 없게 만드는 것이다. 카밀라가 바로 그러했으니, 그녀는 레오넬라가 집 안 어느 방에서 제 연인과 함께 있는 것을 한 번도 아니고 여러 번 알아챘으면서도, 감히 그녀를 꾸짖기는커녕 그를 숨겨 줄 기회를 마련해 주고 온갖 어려움을 치워 주었으니, 행여 남편의 눈에 그가 띌까 함이었다. 그러나 어느 날 동틀 무렵 그가 밖으로 빠져나가는 것을 로타리오에게 들키는 것까지는 막을 수 없었다. 로타리오는 그가 누구인지 알지 못해 처음에는 그를 유령으로 여겼으나, 망토로 얼굴을 가린 채 조심조심 신중하게 몸을 숨기며 서둘러 사라지는 것을 보고는 그 어리석은 생각을 떨쳐 버리고 다른 생각을 품게 되었으니, 카밀라가 방책을 마련해 내지 않았더라면 그것은 모든 것을 파멸로 이끌었을 생각이었다. 그처럼 부적절한 시각에 안셀모의 집에서 나오는 이 사내가 레오넬라 때문에 거기 들어왔을 수도 있다는 생각은 로타리오에게 떠오르지 않았고, 레오넬라라는 사람이 있다는 것조차 그는 기억하지 못했다. 그가 떠올린 것은 오직, 카밀라가 자신에게 경박하고 무르게 굴었듯이 다른 사내에게도 그러했으리라는 것뿐이었다. 길을 그르친 여인의 죄는 이런 더한 벌까지 함께 불러오니, 곧 그녀가 그 구애와 설득에 굴복해 준 바로 그 사내에게조차 그녀의 명예가 의심을 받게 되는 것이다. 그는 그녀가 다른 사내들에게는 더 쉽게 몸을 내주었으리라 믿고, 제 마음에 떠오르는 온갖 의심을 고스란히 믿어 버린다. 이 고비에서 로타리오의 분별력은 죄다 그를 저버린 듯했고, 그의 신중한 격언들은 모조리 기억에서 빠져나가 버린 듯했다. 한 번도 이성적으로 헤아려 보지 않은 채, 다시 두말없이, 제 마음을 갉아먹는 질투의 광기로 눈이 멀어 조급해진 그는, 아무런 잘못도 저지르지 않은 카밀라에게 복수하지 못해 안달이 나, 안셀모가 잠자리에서 일어나기도 전에 그에게 달려가 이렇게 말했다. \"알아 두게, 안셀모, 며칠 전부터 나는 자신과 씨름하며, 이제는 더 이상 자네에게 감출 수도, 감추는 것이 옳지도 않은 것을 자네에게 알리지 않으려 안간힘을 써 왔다네. 알아 두게, 카밀라의 요새가 함락되어 내 뜻에 따를 채비가 되어 있다네. 그리고 내가 이 사실을 자네에게 알리기를 더뎌한 것은, 그것이 그녀의 어떤 가벼운 변덕에 지나지 않는지, 아니면 그녀가 나를 떠보아 자네의 허락을 받아 내가 그녀에게 건네기 시작한 그 사랑이 진심에서 우러난 것인지를 알아내려는 것인지 살펴보고자 함이었다네. 또 나는, 그녀가 마땅히 그래야 할 사람이고 우리 둘 다 그녀를 그렇게 믿어 온 사람이라면, 진작 자네에게 내 구애를 일러바쳤으리라 생각했네. 그런데 그녀가 그러기를 미루는 것을 보니, 다음번에 자네가 집을 비우면 자네의 보석을 간직해 둔 골방에서 나와 밀회하겠노라 내게 한 그 약속이 참말이라 믿게 되었네(카밀라가 그곳에서 그와 만나곤 했던 것은 사실이었다). 허나 나는 자네가 황급히 복수에 나서기를 바라지는 않네. 그 죄가 아직은 마음속에서만 저질러진 것이며, 카밀라의 마음은 어쩌면 지금부터 약속한 그 시각 사이에 바뀌어 그 자리에 뉘우침이 싹틀 수도 있기 때문일세. 자네가 이제껏 늘 내 충고를 전부든 일부든 따라 왔으니, 이제 내가 일러 줄 이것도 따르고 새겨 두게. 그리하여 그릇됨 없이, 충분히 숙고하여, 무엇이 최선의 길로 보이는지 자네 스스로 흡족하게 판단할 수 있도록 말일세. 다른 때처럼 이삼 일 집을 비우는 척하고, 그 골방에 몸을 숨기게나. 그곳의 벽걸이며 다른 물건들이 자네가 숨기에 안성맞춤이니 말일세. 그러면 카밀라의 속셈이 무엇인지 자네는 자네 눈으로, 나는 내 눈으로 보게 될 걸세. 만일 그것이 죄지은 속셈이라면 — 바라기보다는 두려워해야 할 일이지만 — 자네는 침묵과 신중함과 분별로 자네 자신이 자네에게 가해진 그릇됨을 응징하는 도구가 될 수 있을 걸세.\""]],[["Anselmo was amazed, overwhelmed, and astounded at the words of Lothario, which came upon him at a time when he least expected to hear them, for he now looked upon Camilla as having triumphed over the pretended attacks of Lothario, and was beginning to enjoy the glory of her victory. He remained silent for a considerable time, looking on the ground with fixed gaze, and at length said, “Thou hast behaved, Lothario, as I expected of thy friendship: I will follow thy advice in everything; do as thou wilt, and keep this secret as thou seest it should be kept in circumstances so unlooked for.”","안셀모는 로타리오의 말에 어안이 벙벙해지고, 압도되고, 망연자실했으니, 그 말이 그가 그것을 들으리라 가장 기대하지 않던 때에 들이닥친 까닭이었다. 그도 그럴 것이 그는 이제 카밀라가 로타리오의 거짓 공세를 물리치고 승리를 거두었다고 여겨, 그 승리의 영광을 누리기 시작하던 참이었던 것이다. 그는 한동안 말없이 눈길을 땅에 박은 채 가만히 있더니, 마침내 이렇게 말했다. \"자네는, 로타리오, 내가 자네의 우정에 기대했던 그대로 처신했네. 나는 모든 일에서 자네 충고를 따르겠네. 자네 뜻대로 하게, 그리고 이토록 뜻밖의 상황에서 마땅히 지켜야 할 만큼 이 비밀을 지켜 주게.\""]],[["Lothario gave him his word, but after leaving him he repented altogether of what he had said to him, perceiving how foolishly he had acted, as he might have revenged himself upon Camilla in some less cruel and degrading way. He cursed his want of sense, condemned his hasty resolution, and knew not what course to take to undo the mischief or find some ready escape from it. At last he decided upon revealing all to Camilla, and, as there was no want of opportunity for doing so, he found her alone the same day; but she, as soon as she had the chance of speaking to him, said, “Lothario my friend, I must tell thee I have a sorrow in my heart which fills it so that it seems ready to burst; and it will be a wonder if it does not; for the audacity of Leonela has now reached such a pitch that every night she conceals a gallant of hers in this house and remains with him till morning, at the expense of my reputation; inasmuch as it is open to anyone to question it who may see him quitting my house at such unseasonable hours; but what distresses me is that I cannot punish or chide her, for her privity to our intrigue bridles my mouth and keeps me silent about hers, while I am dreading that some catastrophe will come of it.”","로타리오는 그에게 약속했으나, 그를 떠난 뒤로는 자신이 그에게 한 말을 송두리째 후회하게 되었으니, 자신이 얼마나 어리석게 굴었는지를 깨달은 것이다. 카밀라에게 그보다 덜 잔인하고 덜 모욕적인 어떤 방식으로도 복수할 수 있었을 터이기 때문이다. 그는 자신의 몰지각함을 저주하고, 성급한 결심을 책망했으나, 그 화근을 돌이키거나 거기서 빠져나갈 손쉬운 방도를 찾을 길을 알지 못했다. 마침내 그는 카밀라에게 모든 것을 털어놓기로 작정했고, 그럴 기회야 모자랄 것이 없었기에 바로 그날 단둘이 있는 그녀를 만났다. 그런데 그녀는 그에게 말할 기회가 생기자마자 이렇게 말했다. \"로타리오, 나의 벗이여, 당신에게 말해야겠어요. 내 마음속에 슬픔이 가득 차서 터질 듯하니, 정말로 터지지 않으면 다행일 거예요. 레오넬라의 뻔뻔함이 이제 극에 달해서, 밤마다 제 정부를 이 집에 숨겨 두고 아침까지 그와 함께 있으니, 내 평판을 희생시키고 있는 거예요. 그처럼 부적절한 시각에 그가 내 집을 나서는 걸 보는 사람이라면 누구든 내 평판을 의심할 수 있을 테니까요. 하지만 나를 가장 괴롭히는 건, 내가 그 애를 벌하거나 꾸짖을 수 없다는 거예요. 그 애가 우리 정분을 알고 있으니 내 입을 옭아매어 그 애 일에 대해 입을 다물게 하고, 그러는 사이 나는 이 일로 무슨 끔찍한 변고가 생길까 두려워하고 있어요.\""]],[["As Camilla said this Lothario at first imagined it was some device to delude him into the idea that the man he had seen going out was Leonela’s lover and not hers; but when he saw how she wept and suffered, and begged him to help her, he became convinced of the truth, and the conviction completed his confusion and remorse; however, he told Camilla not to distress herself, as he would take measures to put a stop to the insolence of Leonela. At the same time he told her what, driven by the fierce rage of jealousy, he had said to Anselmo, and how he had arranged to hide himself in the closet that he might there see plainly how little she preserved her fidelity to him; and he entreated her pardon for this madness, and her advice as to how to repair it, and escape safely from the intricate labyrinth in which his imprudence had involved him. Camilla was struck with alarm at hearing what Lothario said, and with much anger, and great good sense, she reproved him and rebuked his base design and the foolish and mischievous resolution he had made; but as woman has by nature a nimbler wit than man for good and for evil, though it is apt to fail when she sets herself deliberately to reason, Camilla on the spur of the moment thought of a way to remedy what was to all appearance irremediable, and told Lothario to contrive that the next day Anselmo should conceal himself in the place he mentioned, for she hoped from his concealment to obtain the means of their enjoying themselves for the future without any apprehension; and without revealing her purpose to him entirely she charged him to be careful, as soon as Anselmo was concealed, to come to her when Leonela should call him, and to all she said to him to answer as he would have answered had he not known that Anselmo was listening. Lothario pressed her to explain her intention fully, so that he might with more certainty and precaution take care to do what he saw to be needful.","카밀라가 이렇게 말하자 로타리오는 처음에는, 그것이 자신이 본 그 나가던 사내가 그녀의 정부가 아니라 레오넬라의 정부였다는 생각으로 자신을 속이려는 어떤 술책이려니 짐작했다. 그러나 그녀가 어찌나 흐느끼며 괴로워하고 또 자신에게 도와 달라 애원하는지를 보고는 그 말이 참임을 확신하게 되었고, 그 확신이 그의 혼란과 회한을 한층 더 깊게 했다. 그래도 그는 카밀라에게 너무 괴로워 말라고, 자신이 레오넬라의 무엄함을 막을 조치를 취하겠노라 일렀다. 그와 동시에 그는, 사나운 질투의 광기에 휘몰려 자신이 안셀모에게 무슨 말을 했는지, 또 그녀가 그에 대한 신의를 얼마나 지키지 못하는지 거기서 똑똑히 보겠다며 어떻게 골방에 숨기로 일을 꾸몄는지를 그녀에게 털어놓았다. 그러고는 이 광기에 대해 그녀의 용서를 빌고, 그것을 어떻게 바로잡을지, 또 자신의 경솔함이 자신을 빠뜨린 그 얽히고설킨 미궁에서 어떻게 무사히 벗어날지에 대한 그녀의 조언을 청했다. 카밀라는 로타리오의 말을 듣고 경악에 사로잡혔으나, 큰 노여움과 더불어 빼어난 분별로 그를 나무라며 그의 비열한 속셈과 그가 내린 어리석고 해로운 결단을 꾸짖었다. 그러나 여자는 선이든 악이든 본래 남자보다 더 날렵한 기지를 타고났으되, 다만 작정하고 차근차근 따져 들 때면 그것이 곧잘 어그러지는 법이라, 카밀라는 그 자리에서 누가 봐도 돌이킬 수 없을 일을 바로잡을 방도를 떠올리고는, 로타리오에게 이튿날 안셀모가 그가 말한 그 장소에 숨도록 일을 꾸미라고 일렀으니, 그가 숨어 있음으로써 앞으로 아무 두려움 없이 둘이 즐길 수 있는 길을 얻으리라 바란 까닭이었다. 그러고는 자신의 속셈을 그에게 온전히 밝히지는 않은 채, 안셀모가 숨자마자 레오넬라가 부르거든 자신에게로 오라고, 그리고 자신이 그에게 하는 모든 말에 대해, 안셀모가 듣고 있음을 모르는 것처럼 대답하라고 그에게 단단히 일렀다. 로타리오는 자신이 마땅히 해야 할 일을 한층 더 확실하고 빈틈없이 챙길 수 있도록 그녀에게 그 속셈을 온전히 밝혀 달라고 졸랐다."]],[["“I tell you,”","\"당신에게 말하지만,\""],["said Camilla, “there is nothing to take care of except to answer me what I shall ask you;”","카밀라가 말했다. \"내가 묻는 말에 대답하는 것 말고는 챙길 일이 전혀 없어요.\""],["for she did not wish to explain to him beforehand what she meant to do, fearing lest he should be unwilling to follow out an idea which seemed to her such a good one, and should try or devise some other less practicable plan.","그녀가 무엇을 하려는지 미리 그에게 설명하고 싶지 않았던 것은, 자신에게 그토록 훌륭해 보이는 생각을 그가 따르려 하지 않고 무언가 다른, 실행하기 어려운 계획을 꾀하거나 짜낼까 염려한 까닭이었다."]],[["Lothario then retired, and the next day Anselmo, under pretence of going to his friend’s country house, took his departure, and then returned to conceal himself, which he was able to do easily, as Camilla and Leonela took care to give him the opportunity; and so he placed himself in hiding in the state of agitation that it may be imagined he would feel who expected to see the vitals of his honour laid bare before his eyes, and found himself on the point of losing the supreme blessing he thought he possessed in his beloved Camilla.","이윽고 로타리오가 물러갔고, 이튿날 안셀모는 친구의 시골 별장에 간다는 핑계로 길을 떠났다가 몸을 숨기러 되돌아왔는데, 카밀라와 레오넬라가 기회를 마련해 주려 마음 쓴 덕에 그 일은 어렵잖게 이루어졌다. 그리하여 그는, 제 명예의 급소가 제 눈앞에 발가벗겨지는 것을 보리라 예상하고, 사랑하는 카밀라에게서 자신이 지녔다 여기던 더없는 복을 잃기 직전에 처한 자라면 누구나 느낄 법한 그런 동요에 휩싸인 채 몸을 숨겼다."],["Having made sure of Anselmo’s being in his hiding-place, Camilla and Leonela entered the closet, and the instant she set foot within it Camilla said, with a deep sigh, “Ah!","안셀모가 은신처에 든 것을 확인한 뒤 카밀라와 레오넬라가 골방으로 들어왔고, 카밀라는 그 안에 발을 들여놓는 순간 깊은 한숨과 함께 말했다. \"아!"],["dear Leonela, would it not be better, before I do what I am unwilling you should know lest you should seek to prevent it, that you should take Anselmo’s dagger that I have asked of you and with it pierce this vile heart of mine?","사랑하는 레오넬라, 네가 막으려 들까 봐 알리고 싶지 않은 그 일을 내가 하기 전에, 차라리 네게 가져오라 한 안셀모의 단검을 받아 들고 그것으로 이 천한 내 심장을 꿰뚫는 편이 낫지 않겠느냐?"],["But no; there is no reason why I should suffer the punishment of another’s fault.","하지만 아니다. 내가 남의 잘못에 대한 벌을 받을 까닭은 없지."],["I will first know what it is that the bold licentious eyes of Lothario have seen in me that could have encouraged him to reveal to me a design so base as that which he has disclosed regardless of his friend and of my honour.","나는 먼저, 로타리오의 그 대담하고 음탕한 눈이 내게서 무엇을 보았기에 제 벗도 내 명예도 아랑곳없이 내게 드러낸 그토록 비열한 속셈을 감히 털어놓을 마음을 먹게 되었는지부터 알아야겠다. 창가로 가서 그를 부르거라, 레오넬라."],["Go to the window, Leonela, and call him, for no doubt he is in the street waiting to carry out his vile project; but mine, cruel it may be, but honourable, shall be carried out first.”","그가 제 더러운 계략을 실행하려고 길에서 기다리고 있는 것이 분명하니. 하지만 내 계략은, 잔인할지언정 명예로운 것이니, 그것이 먼저 실행되어야 한다.\""]],[["“Ah, señora,”","\"아, 마님,\""],["said the crafty Leonela, who knew her part, “what is it you want to do with this dagger?","제 배역을 잘 알고 있던 교활한 레오넬라가 말했다. \"이 단검으로 무엇을 하시려는 것이옵니까?"],["Can it be that you mean to take your own life, or Lothario’s?","마님 자신의 목숨을, 아니면 로타리오 님의 목숨을 거두려 하시는 것이옵니까?"],["for whichever you mean to do, it will lead to the loss of your reputation and good name.","어느 쪽이든 그것은 마님의 평판과 명성을 잃는 일로 이어질 것이옵니다."],["It is better to dissemble your wrong and not give this wicked man the chance of entering the house now and finding us alone; consider, señora, we are weak women and he is a man, and determined, and as he comes with such a base purpose, blind and urged by passion, perhaps before you can put yours into execution he may do what will be worse for you than taking your life.","차라리 마님의 그릇됨을 감추시고, 이 사악한 자에게 지금 집에 들어와 우리가 단둘이 있는 것을 보게 할 빌미를 주지 마옵소서. 헤아려 보옵소서, 마님, 우리는 약한 여인들이고 그는 사내이며, 더구나 작심한 사내인 데다, 그토록 비열한 속셈을 품고 눈이 멀어 정념에 내몰려 오는 터라, 어쩌면 마님께서 마님의 뜻을 실행하시기도 전에 그가 마님께 목숨을 거두는 것보다 더 끔찍한 짓을 저지를지도 모르옵니다."],["Ill betide my master, Anselmo, for giving such authority in his house to this shameless fellow!","이 파렴치한 자에게 제 집에서 그런 권한을 내주신 제 주인 안셀모 나리께 화가 미치기를!"],["And supposing you kill him, señora, as I suspect you mean to do, what shall we do with him when he is dead?”","그리고 마님께서, 제가 짐작하기로 하시려는 대로, 그를 죽이신다 한들, 그가 죽고 나면 우리가 그를 어찌하오리까?\""]],[["“What, my friend?”","\"어찌하다니, 얘야?\" 카밀라가 답했다."],["replied Camilla, “we shall leave him for Anselmo to bury him; for in reason it will be to him a light labour to hide his own infamy under ground.","\"그를 안셀모가 묻도록 남겨 두면 되지. 제 불명예를 땅속에 감추는 일쯤이야 그에게는 응당 수월한 수고가 될 테니."],["Summon him, make haste, for all the time I delay in taking vengeance for my wrong seems to me an offence against the loyalty I owe my husband.”","그를 불러 오너라, 어서 서둘러라. 내 그릇됨에 복수하기를 미루는 동안의 모든 시간이 내게는 남편에게 마땅히 바쳐야 할 충절을 거스르는 일로 여겨지는구나.\""]],[["Anselmo was listening to all this, and every word that Camilla uttered made him change his mind; but when he heard that it was resolved to kill Lothario his first impulse was to come out and show himself to avert such a disaster; but in his anxiety to see the issue of a resolution so bold and virtuous he restrained himself, intending to come forth in time to prevent the deed.","안셀모는 이 모든 것을 듣고 있었고, 카밀라가 입에 올리는 말 한마디 한마디가 그의 마음을 돌려놓았다. 그러나 로타리오를 죽이기로 작정했다는 말을 듣자 그의 첫 충동은 밖으로 나가 모습을 드러내어 그런 참사를 막는 것이었다."],["At this moment Camilla, throwing herself upon a bed that was close by, swooned away, and Leonela began to weep bitterly, exclaiming, “Woe is me!","그러나 그토록 대담하고 정숙한 결심의 결말을 보고 싶은 마음에 그는 자신을 억눌렀으니, 그 일이 벌어지는 것을 막기에 알맞은 때에 나설 작정이었다. 이때 카밀라가 곁에 있던 침상에 몸을 던지며 까무러쳤고, 레오넬라는 비통하게 울기 시작하며 외쳤다."],["that I should be fated to have dying here in my arms the flower of virtue upon earth, the crown of true wives, the pattern of chastity!”","\"아아, 가엾어라! 이 땅 위 미덕의 꽃이요, 참된 아내들의 면류관이요, 정절의 본보기이신 분이 내 품에서 숨을 거두시도록 정해졌다니!\""],["with more to the same effect, so that anyone who heard her would have taken her for the most tender-hearted and faithful handmaid in the world, and her mistress for another persecuted Penelope.","이런 식으로 더 많은 말을 늘어놓았으니, 그 소리를 들은 사람이라면 누구나 그녀를 세상에서 가장 마음 따뜻하고 충직한 하녀로, 그 안주인을 또 한 사람의 박해받는 페넬로페로 여겼으리라. * 페넬로페: 호메로스의 「오디세이아」에 나오는 오디세우스의 아내로, 남편이 떠난 오랜 세월 동안 숱한 구혼자들을 물리치며 정절을 지킨 여인."]],[["Camilla was not long in recovering from her fainting fit and on coming to herself she said, “Why do you not go, Leonela, to call hither that friend, the falsest to his friend the sun ever shone upon or night concealed?","카밀라는 오래지 않아 실신에서 깨어났고, 정신을 차리자 말했다. \"어찌 가지 않느냐, 레오넬라, 해가 비춘 적 있고 밤이 가린 적 있는 그 어떤 자보다도 제 벗에게 가장 거짓된 그 벗을 이리로 부르러 말이다."],["Away, run, haste, speed!","어서 가거라, 뛰거라, 서둘러라, 빨리!"],["lest the fire of my wrath burn itself out with delay, and the righteous vengeance that I hope for melt away in menaces and maledictions.”","미적거리는 사이에 내 분노의 불길이 스스로 사그라들고, 내가 바라는 의로운 복수가 위협과 저주 속에 녹아 사라지지 않도록.\""]],[["“I am just going to call him, señora,”","\"지금 부르러 가옵니다, 마님,\" 레오넬라가 말했다."],["said Leonela; “but you must first give me that dagger, lest while I am gone you should by means of it give cause to all who love you to weep all their lives.”","\"허나 먼저 제게 그 단검을 주셔야 하옵니다. 제가 가 있는 동안 그것으로 마님을 사랑하는 모든 이가 평생 울게 할 빌미를 마님께서 만드실까 두렵사오니.\""]],[["“Go in peace, dear Leonela, I will not do so,”","\"평안히 가거라, 사랑하는 레오넬라, 그러지 않으마,\""],["said Camilla, “for rash and foolish as I may be, to your mind, in defending my honour, I am not going to be so much so as that Lucretia who they say killed herself without having done anything wrong, and without having first killed him on whom the guilt of her misfortune lay.","카밀라가 말했다. \"네 생각에 내가 내 명예를 지키느라 무모하고 어리석을지 모르나, 나는 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않고서, 또 제 불행의 죄가 얹힌 그 사내를 먼저 죽이지도 않고서 스스로 목숨을 끊었다는 그 루크레티아만큼은 되지 않으련다."],["I shall die, if I am to die; but it must be after full vengeance upon him who has brought me here to weep over audacity that no fault of mine gave birth to.”","죽어야 한다면 죽겠지만, 아무런 내 잘못도 낳지 않은 그 만행을 두고 나를 이리로 끌어와 울게 만든 그자에게 온전히 복수한 다음이라야 하느니라.\""]],[["Leonela required much pressing before she would go to summon Lothario, but at last she went, and while awaiting her return Camilla continued, as if speaking to herself, “Good God!","* 루크레티아: 고대 로마의 정숙한 여인으로, 능욕을 당한 뒤 가족에게 복수를 당부하고 스스로 목숨을 끊었다. 그녀의 죽음은 로마 왕정을 무너뜨리는 계기가 되었다고 전해진다."],["would it not have been more prudent to have repulsed Lothario, as I have done many a time before, than to allow him, as I am now doing, to think me unchaste and vile, even for the short time I must wait until I undeceive him?","레오넬라는 로타리오를 부르러 가기까지 한참을 졸라야 했으나, 마침내 그녀가 떠났고, 그녀가 돌아오기를 기다리는 동안 카밀라는 혼잣말하듯 말을 이었다. \"맙소사!"],["No doubt it would have been better; but I should not be avenged, nor the honour of my husband vindicated, should he find so clear and easy an escape from the strait into which his depravity has led him.","내가 전에 여러 번 그랬듯 로타리오를 물리치는 편이, 지금처럼 그가 나를 부정하고 천한 여자로 여기게 — 그의 오해를 풀어 줄 때까지 그 잠깐 동안일지언정 — 내버려 두는 것보다 더 현명하지 않았겠는가? 의심할 바 없이 그편이 나았으리라."],["Let the traitor pay with his life for the temerity of his wanton wishes, and let the world know (if haply it shall ever come to know) that Camilla not only preserved her allegiance to her husband, but avenged him of the man who dared to wrong him.","그러나 그러면 나는 복수를 못 했을 것이고, 그가 제 타락이 자신을 몰아넣은 그 궁지에서 그토록 빤하고 손쉬운 빠져나갈 길을 찾아낸다면 내 남편의 명예도 씻기지 못했으리라. 그 배신자로 하여금 제 음탕한 욕망의 무모함을 목숨으로 갚게 하고, 카밀라가 남편에 대한 충절을 지켰을 뿐 아니라 감히 남편을 욕보이려 한 자에게 남편의 원수를 갚았음을 세상이 알게 하리라(혹여 언젠가 세상이 그것을 알게 된다면)."],["Still, I think it might be better to disclose this to Anselmo.","그래도 이 일을 안셀모에게 털어놓는 편이 나으리라는 생각이 드는구나."],["But then I have called his attention to it in the letter I wrote to him in the country, and, if he did nothing to prevent the mischief I there pointed out to him, I suppose it was that from pure goodness of heart and trustfulness he would not and could not believe that any thought against his honour could harbour in the breast of so stanch a friend; nor indeed did I myself believe it for many days, nor should I have ever believed it if his insolence had not gone so far as to make it manifest by open presents, lavish promises, and ceaseless tears.","하지만 시골에 있는 그에게 쓴 편지에서 내가 이미 그 일에 주의를 환기시켰는데, 그가 내가 거기서 일러 준 그 화근을 막으려 아무것도 하지 않았다면, 그것은 순전한 마음의 선량함과 미더움에서, 그토록 굳건한 벗의 가슴속에 제 명예를 거스르는 어떤 생각이 깃들 수 있으리라고는 믿으려 하지도, 믿을 수도 없었던 까닭이리라. 사실 나 자신도 여러 날 동안 그것을 믿지 않았고, 그의 무엄함이 노골적인 선물과 아낌없는 약속과 그칠 줄 모르는 눈물로 그것을 드러낼 만큼 멀리 나가지 않았더라면 결코 믿지 않았으리라."],["But why do I argue thus?","그러나 어찌 내가 이리 따지고만 있는가?"],["Does a bold determination stand in need of arguments?","대담한 결단에 따짐 따위가 필요하던가? 결코 그렇지 않지."],["Surely not. Then traitors avaunt!","그러니 배신자들이여, 물러가라!"],["Vengeance to my aid!","복수여, 나를 도우라!"],["Let the false one come, approach, advance, die, yield up his life, and then befall what may.","그 거짓된 자로 하여금 오게 하라, 다가오게 하라, 나아오게 하라, 죽게 하라, 제 목숨을 내놓게 하라, 그런 다음에는 무슨 일이 일어나든 일어나라."],["Pure I came to him whom Heaven bestowed upon me, pure I shall leave him; and at the worst bathed in my own chaste blood and in the foul blood of the falsest friend that friendship ever saw in the world;”","하늘이 내게 내려 주신 그분께 나는 정결하게 왔고, 정결하게 그분 곁을 떠나리라. 최악의 경우라도 나 자신의 정결한 피와, 우정이 이 세상에서 본 가장 거짓된 벗의 더러운 피에 젖은 채로.\""],["and as she uttered these words she paced the room holding the unsheathed dagger, with such irregular and disordered steps, and such gestures that one would have supposed her to have lost her senses, and taken her for some violent desperado instead of a delicate woman.","그리고 그녀는 이 말을 입에 올리며, 칼집에서 뺀 단검을 든 채 어찌나 불규칙하고 어지러운 걸음으로, 또 어찌나 격한 몸짓으로 방 안을 거니는지, 누가 보았다면 그녀가 제정신을 잃었다 여기고, 가녀린 여인이 아니라 어떤 사나운 광인으로 여겼으리라."]],[["Anselmo, concealed behind some tapestries where he had hidden himself, beheld and was amazed at all, and already felt that what he had seen and heard was a sufficient answer to even greater suspicions; and he would have been now well pleased if the proof afforded by Lothario’s coming were dispensed with, as he feared some sudden mishap; but as he was on the point of showing himself and coming forth to embrace and undeceive his wife he paused as he saw Leonela returning, leading Lothario. Camilla when she saw him, drawing a long line in front of her on the floor with the dagger, said to him, “Lothario, pay attention to what I say to thee: if by any chance thou darest to cross this line thou seest, or even approach it, the instant I see thee attempt it that same instant will I pierce my bosom with this dagger that I hold in my hand; and before thou answerest me a word I desire thee to listen to a few from me, and afterwards thou shalt reply as may please thee. First, I desire thee to tell me, Lothario, if thou knowest my husband Anselmo, and in what light thou regardest him; and secondly I desire to know if thou knowest me too. Answer me this, without embarrassment or reflecting deeply what thou wilt answer, for they are no riddles I put to thee.”","벽걸이 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 안셀모는 이 모든 것을 지켜보며 경탄을 금치 못했고, 자신이 보고 들은 것이 그보다 더 큰 의심에까지 넉넉한 답이 됨을 이미 절감했다. 그리하여 그는 로타리오가 와서 보여 줄 증거 따위는 없어도 좋겠다 싶었으니, 무슨 갑작스러운 변고가 생길까 두려웠던 것이다. 그러나 그가 막 모습을 드러내어 밖으로 나가 아내를 끌어안고 오해를 풀어 주려던 찰나, 레오넬라가 로타리오를 데리고 돌아오는 것을 보고는 멈칫했다. 카밀라는 그를 보자 단검으로 바닥에 자기 앞으로 길게 줄을 하나 그으며 그에게 말했다. \"로타리오, 내가 당신에게 하는 말을 새겨들어요. 혹시라도 당신이 감히 저 줄을 넘거나 그것에 다가오기라도 한다면, 당신이 그러려는 것을 내가 보는 그 순간 나는 내 손에 쥔 이 단검으로 내 가슴을 꿰뚫겠어요. 그리고 당신이 내게 한마디 대답하기 전에, 나는 당신이 내 말 몇 마디를 듣기를 바라요. 그런 다음에 당신 좋을 대로 대답하면 돼요. 먼저, 로타리오, 당신에게 묻겠어요. 당신이 내 남편 안셀모를 아는지, 또 그를 어떻게 여기는지 말해 주세요. 그리고 둘째로, 당신이 나 또한 아는지 알고 싶어요. 이에 답해 주세요, 당황하지도 말고 무어라 답할지 깊이 헤아리지도 말고. 내가 당신에게 던지는 것은 수수께끼가 아니니까요.\""]],[["Lothario was not so dull but that from the first moment when Camilla directed him to make Anselmo hide himself he understood what she intended to do, and therefore he fell in with her idea so readily and promptly that between them they made the imposture look more true than truth; so he answered her thus: “I did not think, fair Camilla, that thou wert calling me to ask questions so remote from the object with which I come; but if it is to defer the promised reward thou art doing so, thou mightst have put it off still longer, for the longing for happiness gives the more distress the nearer comes the hope of gaining it; but lest thou shouldst say that I do not answer thy questions, I say that I know thy husband Anselmo, and that we have known each other from our earliest years; I will not speak of what thou too knowest, of our friendship, that I may not compel myself to testify against the wrong that love, the mighty excuse for greater errors, makes me inflict upon him. Thee I know and hold in the same estimation as he does, for were it not so I had not for a lesser prize acted in opposition to what I owe to my station and the holy laws of true friendship, now broken and violated by me through that powerful enemy, love.”","로타리오는 그리 둔한 자가 아니어서, 카밀라가 그에게 안셀모를 숨도록 만들라고 지시한 첫 순간부터 그녀가 무엇을 하려는지 알아챘던 터라, 그녀의 생각에 어찌나 선뜻 척척 맞장구를 쳤던지 둘이서 그 거짓을 진실보다 더 진실해 보이게 만들었다. 그리하여 그는 이렇게 답했다. \"아름다운 카밀라, 나는 당신이 내가 온 목적과는 그토록 동떨어진 물음을 던지려고 나를 부른 줄은 몰랐소. 그러나 만일 약속한 보상을 미루려 그러는 것이라면, 더 오래 미뤄도 좋았으리다. 행복에 대한 갈망이란 그것을 얻을 희망이 가까워질수록 더 큰 괴로움을 안기는 법이니 말이오. 허나 당신이, 내가 당신의 물음에 답하지 않는다고 하지 않도록, 내 말하리다. 나는 당신의 남편 안셀모를 알며, 우리는 가장 어린 시절부터 서로를 알아 왔소. 우리의 우정에 대해서는, 당신도 아는 그것에 대해서는 말하지 않으리니, 더 큰 잘못들에 대한 막강한 핑계인 사랑이 그에게 끼치게 만든 그 그릇됨을 거슬러 나 스스로 증언하도록 나를 몰아세우지 않으려 함이오. 당신을 나는 알며, 그가 당신을 여기는 것과 똑같이 여기고 있소. 만일 그렇지 않았더라면, 나는 더 못한 보상을 위해서라면 내 신분에 진 의무와, 이제 그 막강한 적 사랑으로 말미암아 내가 깨뜨리고 짓밟은 참된 우정의 거룩한 법도를 거스르며 행동하지는 않았을 것이오.\""]],[["“If thou dost confess that,”","\"당신이 그것을 시인한다면,\" 카밀라가 응수했다."],["returned Camilla, “mortal enemy of all that rightly deserves to be loved, with what face dost thou dare to come before one whom thou knowest to be the mirror wherein he is reflected on whom thou shouldst look to see how unworthily thou him?","\"마땅히 사랑받아야 할 모든 것의 철천지원수여, 무슨 낯으로 감히, 당신이 마땅히 우러러보아야 할 그분이 비치는 거울임을, 그리하여 당신이 얼마나 그분께 부당하게 굴었는지를 비추어 보아야 할 그 거울임을 당신이 아는 사람 앞에 나설 수 있단 말이에요? 그러나, 아아 가엾어라, 무엇이 당신으로 하여금 당신 자신이 마땅히 지켜야 할 바를 그토록 가벼이 여기게 했는지 이제 알겠어요."],["But, woe is me, I now comprehend what has made thee give so little heed to what thou owest to thyself; it must have been some freedom of mine, for I will not call it immodesty, as it did not proceed from any deliberate intention, but from some heedlessness such as women are guilty of through inadvertence when they think they have no occasion for reserve.","그것은 분명 내 어떤 거리낌 없는 행동이었을 거예요. 그것을 부정함이라 부르지는 않겠어요."],["But tell me, traitor, when did I by word or sign give a reply to thy prayers that could awaken in thee a shadow of hope of attaining thy base wishes?","어떤 작정한 의도에서 나온 것이 아니라, 여자들이 굳이 삼갈 필요가 없다 여길 때 무심결에 저지르는 그런 부주의에서 나온 것이니까요. 하지만 말해 봐요, 배신자여, 내가 언제 말이나 몸짓으로 당신의 그 비열한 욕망을 이룰 수 있으리라는 한 가닥 희망의 그림자라도 당신 속에 일깨울 만한 응답을 당신의 간청에 한 적이 있던가요?"],["When were not thy professions of love sternly and scornfully rejected and rebuked?","당신의 사랑 고백이 언제 매몰차고 경멸스럽게 거절당하고 책망당하지 않았던가요? 당신의 잦은 맹세와 그보다 더 잦은 선물이 언제 믿어지거나 받아들여진 적이 있던가요?"],["When were thy frequent pledges and still more frequent gifts believed or accepted?","그러나 나는 어떤 희망에 떠받쳐지지 않고서는 누구도 오래도록 사랑을 얻으려는 시도를 이어 갈 수 없다고 여기는지라, 당신의 그 뻔뻔함의 탓을 나 자신에게 돌리려 해요. 분명 내 어떤 경솔함이 이제껏 줄곧 당신의 희망을 키워 주었을 테니까요."],["But as I am persuaded that no one can long persevere in the attempt to win love unsustained by some hope, I am willing to attribute to myself the blame of thy assurance, for no doubt some thoughtlessness of mine has all this time fostered thy hopes; and therefore will I punish myself and inflict upon myself the penalty thy guilt deserves.","그러니 나는 나 자신을 벌하고, 당신의 죄가 마땅히 받아야 할 그 벌을 나 자신에게 내리겠어요. 그리고 내가 나 자신에게 이토록 가차 없는 만큼 당신에게도 결코 다르게 굴 수 없음을 당신이 알도록, 나는 당신을 불러, 당신이 할 수 있는 온갖 집요함으로 욕보였고 또 나 역시 — 내가 빌미를 주었다면 — 당신의 비열한 속셈을 부추기고 묵인할 만한 빌미를 매번 피하지 못한 부주의로 욕보인, 내 존귀한 남편의 다친 명예에 바칠 그 제물의 증인이 되게 하려는 거예요."],["And that thou mayest see that being so relentless to myself I cannot possibly be otherwise to thee, I have summoned thee to be a witness of the sacrifice I mean to offer to the injured honour of my honoured husband, wronged by thee with all the assiduity thou wert capable of, and by me too through want of caution in avoiding every occasion, if I have given any, of encouraging and sanctioning thy base designs.","다시 한번 말하건대, 내 어떤 경솔함이 당신 속에 이 무도한 생각들을 낳았으리라는 내 마음속 의심, 그것이 나를 가장 괴롭히는 것이며 내 손으로 가장 벌하고 싶은 것이에요. 다른 어떤 응징의 도구를 쓴다면 내 잘못이 어쩌면 더 널리 알려질지도 모르니까요."],["Once more I say the suspicion in my mind that some imprudence of mine has engendered these lawless thoughts in thee, is what causes me most distress and what I desire most to punish with my own hands, for were any other instrument of punishment employed my error might become perhaps more widely known; but before I do so, in my death I mean to inflict death, and take with me one that will fully satisfy my longing for the revenge I hope for and have; for I shall see, wheresoever it may be that I go, the penalty awarded by inflexible, unswerving justice on him who has placed me in a position so desperate.”","그러나 그러기 전에, 나는 내 죽음 속에서 죽음을 내려, 내가 바라고 또 품은 그 복수에 대한 갈망을 온전히 채워 줄 자를 데려가겠어요. 내가 어디로 가든 그곳에서, 나를 이토록 절망스러운 처지에 몰아넣은 자에게 굽힘 없고 흔들림 없는 정의가 내린 그 형벌을 보게 될 테니까요.\""]],[["As she uttered these words, with incredible energy and swiftness she flew upon Lothario with the naked dagger, so manifestly bent on burying it in his breast that he was almost uncertain whether these demonstrations were real or feigned, for he was obliged to have recourse to all his skill and strength to prevent her from striking him; and with such reality did she act this strange farce and mystification that, to give it a colour of truth, she determined to stain it with her own blood; for perceiving, or pretending, that she could not wound Lothario, she said, “Fate, it seems, will not grant my just desire complete satisfaction, but it will not be able to keep me from satisfying it partially at least;” and making an effort to free the hand with the dagger which Lothario held in his grasp, she released it, and directing the point to a place where it could not inflict a deep wound, she plunged it into her left side high up close to the shoulder, and then allowed herself to fall to the ground as if in a faint.","이 말을 입에 올리며 그녀는 믿기 어려운 기세와 날쌤으로, 칼집에서 뺀 단검을 들고 로타리오에게 달려들었는데, 어찌나 대놓고 그것을 그의 가슴에 박으려 드는지 그는 이 거동이 참인지 거짓인지 도무지 가늠할 수 없을 지경이었으니, 그녀가 자신을 찌르지 못하도록 막느라 제 온갖 솜씨와 힘에 기대지 않을 수 없었던 것이다. 그리고 그녀가 이 기이한 광대극이자 속임수를 어찌나 진짜처럼 연기했던지, 그녀는 그것에 진실의 빛깔을 입히려 자기 자신의 피로 그것을 물들이기로 작정했다. 로타리오에게 상처를 입힐 수 없음을 알아채자, 혹은 알아챈 척하며 그녀가 말했다. \"운명이, 보아하니, 내 정당한 소원을 온전히 채워 주지는 않으려나 보다. 그러나 적어도 그 일부나마 채우는 것까지 막지는 못하리라.\" 그러고는 로타리오가 움켜쥐고 있던, 단검 든 손을 빼내려 안간힘을 써서 그것을 풀어내고는, 칼끝을 깊은 상처를 입힐 수 없는 자리로 향하게 하여 어깨에 바싹 붙은 왼쪽 옆구리 위쪽에 그것을 찔러 넣고, 그런 다음 까무러친 듯 바닥에 쓰러졌다."]],[["Leonela and Lothario stood amazed and astounded at the catastrophe, and seeing Camilla stretched on the ground and bathed in her blood they were still uncertain as to the true nature of the act.","레오넬라와 로타리오는 이 참변에 어안이 벙벙하고 망연자실하여, 카밀라가 제 피에 젖은 채 바닥에 뻗어 있는 것을 보고도 그 행위의 참된 본질이 무엇인지 여전히 가늠하지 못했다. 로타리오는 겁에 질려 숨을 헐떡이며 단검을 뽑으려 황급히 달려갔으나, 상처가 얼마나 얕은지를 보고는 두려움이 가셨고, 다시 한번 아름다운 카밀라의 영민함과 침착함과 재빠른 기지에 감탄했다."],["Lothario, terrified and breathless, ran in haste to pluck out the dagger; but when he saw how slight the wound was he was relieved of his fears and once more admired the subtlety, coolness, and ready wit of the fair Camilla; and the better to support the part he had to play he began to utter profuse and doleful lamentations over her body as if she were dead, invoking maledictions not only on himself but also on him who had been the means of placing him in such a position: and knowing that his friend Anselmo heard him he spoke in such a way as to make a listener feel much more pity for him than for Camilla, even though he supposed her dead.","그러고는 자신이 맡은 배역을 한층 더 잘 떠받치고자, 마치 그녀가 죽기라도 한 듯 그녀의 몸 위로 넘쳐흐르는 구슬픈 비탄을 쏟아 내기 시작했으니, 자기 자신뿐 아니라 자신을 이런 처지에 몰아넣은 장본인에게까지 저주를 퍼부었다. 그리고 자신의 벗 안셀모가 듣고 있음을 알았기에, 그는 듣는 이로 하여금 — 비록 그가 카밀라를 죽었다 여길지라도 — 카밀라보다 자신을 훨씬 더 가엾이 여기게 만들 만한 식으로 말했다."],["Leonela took her up in her arms and laid her on the bed, entreating Lothario to go in quest of some one to attend to her wound in secret, and at the same time asking his advice and opinion as to what they should say to Anselmo about his lady’s wound if he should chance to return before it was healed.","레오넬라는 그녀를 팔에 안아 침상에 누이고, 로타리오에게 누군가를 찾아가 은밀히 그녀의 상처를 돌보아 줄 사람을 데려와 달라고 애원하는 한편, 혹여 상처가 아물기 전에 안셀모가 돌아온다면 그의 부인의 상처에 대해 뭐라 말해야 할지 그의 조언과 의견을 구했다. 그는 좋을 대로 말하면 된다고, 자신은 쓸모 있을 만한 조언을 해 줄 형편이 못 된다고 답했다."],["He replied they might say what they liked, for he was not in a state to give advice that would be of any use; all he could tell her was to try and stanch the blood, as he was going where he should never more be seen; and with every appearance of deep grief and sorrow he left the house; but when he found himself alone, and where there was nobody to see him, he crossed himself unceasingly, lost in wonder at the adroitness of Camilla and the consistent acting of Leonela.","그가 그녀에게 일러 줄 수 있는 것이라곤 그저 피를 멎게 해 보라는 것뿐이며, 자신은 다시는 모습을 보이지 않을 곳으로 가겠노라 했다. 그러고는 깊은 비탄과 슬픔에 잠긴 온갖 기색을 띤 채 집을 나섰다."],["He reflected how convinced Anselmo would be that he had a second Portia for a wife, and he looked forward anxiously to meeting him in order to rejoice together over falsehood and truth the most craftily veiled that could be imagined.","그러나 홀로, 아무도 자신을 보지 않는 곳에 이르자 그는 카밀라의 능란함과 레오넬라의 한결같은 연기에 경탄하여 정신을 못 차린 채 쉴 새 없이 성호를 그었다. 그는 안셀모가 자신이 또 한 사람의 포르티아를 아내로 두었다고 얼마나 굳게 믿을지를 곱씹으며, 상상할 수 있는 가장 교묘하게 가려진 거짓과 진실을 두고 함께 기뻐할 수 있도록 그를 만날 일을 애타게 고대했다."]],[["Leonela, as he told her, stanched her lady’s blood, which was no more than sufficed to support her deception; and washing the wound with a little wine she bound it up to the best of her skill, talking all the time she was tending her in a strain that, even if nothing else had been said before, would have been enough to assure Anselmo that he had in Camilla a model of purity.","* 포르티아: 고대 로마의 정치가 카토의 딸이자 브루투스의 아내로, 정절과 굳센 절개로 이름난 여인이다. 레오넬라는 그가 일러 준 대로 안주인의 피를 멎게 했는데, 그것은 그녀의 속임수를 떠받치는 데 꼭 필요한 만큼에 지나지 않았다."],["To Leonela’s words Camilla added her own, calling herself cowardly and wanting in spirit, since she had not enough at the time she had most need of it to rid herself of the life she so much loathed.","그러고는 포도주를 조금 발라 상처를 씻고 제 솜씨껏 그것을 동여매면서, 안주인을 돌보는 내내, 설령 앞서 다른 말이 일절 없었다 한들 그것만으로도 카밀라가 순결의 본보기임을 안셀모에게 확신시키기에 넉넉했을 그런 어조로 이야기를 늘어놓았다."],["She asked her attendant’s advice as to whether or not she ought to inform her beloved husband of all that had happened, but the other bade her say nothing about it, as she would lay upon him the obligation of taking vengeance on Lothario, which he could not do but at great risk to himself; and it was the duty of a true wife not to give her husband provocation to quarrel, but, on the contrary, to remove it as far as possible from him.","레오넬라의 말에 카밀라는 자기 말을 보태어, 자신을 겁쟁이요 기개가 모자란 여자라 일컬었으니, 그토록 진저리 나는 목숨을 떨쳐 버려야 할 가장 절박한 그 순간에 그럴 만한 기개가 없었다는 것이었다. 그녀는 일어난 모든 일을 사랑하는 남편에게 알려야 할지 말지 시중드는 하녀의 조언을 구했으나, 레오넬라는 그것에 대해 아무 말도 말라고 일렀으니, 그러면 남편에게 로타리오에게 복수해야 할 의무를 지우게 될 터인데, 그가 그 일을 하자면 제 한 몸이 큰 위험을 무릅써야 하기 때문이며, 참된 아내의 도리란 남편에게 다툼의 빌미를 주는 것이 아니라 도리어 그것을 그에게서 되도록 멀리 치워 주는 것이라는 까닭에서였다."]],[["Camilla replied that she believed she was right and that she would follow her advice, but at any rate it would be well to consider how she was to explain the wound to Anselmo, for he could not help seeing it; to which Leonela answered that she did not know how to tell a lie even in jest.","카밀라는 그녀의 말이 옳다 여기며 그 조언을 따르겠노라 답했으나, 어쨌든 안셀모에게 그 상처를 어떻게 둘러댈지 궁리해 두는 것이 좋겠다고, 그가 그것을 보지 않을 수 없을 터이기 때문이라고 했다. 이에 레오넬라는 자신은 농담으로라도 거짓말을 할 줄 모른다고 답했다."]],[["“How then can I know, my dear?”","\"그럼 나라고 어찌 알겠느냐, 얘야?\""],["said Camilla, “for I should not dare to forge or keep up a falsehood if my life depended on it.","카밀라가 말했다. \"나는 목숨이 달렸다 한들 거짓을 꾸미거나 둘러댈 엄두조차 못 낼 텐데."],["If we can think of no escape from this difficulty, it will be better to tell him the plain truth than that he should find us out in an untrue story.”","이 곤경에서 빠져나갈 길을 도무지 떠올릴 수 없다면, 그가 우리의 거짓 이야기를 들춰내게 하느니 차라리 그에게 있는 그대로 사실을 말하는 편이 나으리라.\""]],[["“Be not uneasy, señora,”","\"심려치 마옵소서, 마님,\" 레오넬라가 말했다."],["said Leonela; “between this and to-morrow I will think of what we must say to him, and perhaps the wound being where it is it can be hidden from his sight, and Heaven will be pleased to aid us in a purpose so good and honourable.","\"오늘부터 내일 사이에 제가 나리께 무어라 말씀드려야 할지 궁리해 두겠나이다. 어쩌면 상처가 있는 자리가 그러하니 나리의 눈에 띄지 않게 감출 수 있을 것이고, 이토록 선하고 명예로운 뜻을 이루도록 하늘이 기꺼이 우리를 도우실 것이옵니다."],["Compose yourself, señora, and endeavour to calm your excitement lest my lord find you agitated; and leave the rest to my care and God’s, who always supports good intentions.”","마음을 가다듬으소서, 마님, 그리고 나리께서 마님의 동요하신 모습을 보시지 않도록 흥분을 가라앉히도록 애써 보옵소서. 나머지는 제 손과 하느님께 맡기소서, 그분은 언제나 선한 뜻을 지지하시니 말이옵니다.\""]],[["Anselmo had with the deepest attention listened to and seen played out the tragedy of the death of his honour, which the performers acted with such wonderfully effective truth that it seemed as if they had become the realities of the parts they played.","안셀모는 더없는 주의를 기울여, 자기 명예의 죽음이라는 그 비극이 펼쳐지는 것을 듣고 또 지켜보았으니, 그 연기자들이 어찌나 놀랍도록 실감 나는 진실로 그 비극을 연기했던지 그들이 제가 맡은 배역 그 자체가 되어 버린 듯했다. 그는 밤이, 그리고 집을 빠져나가 자신의 좋은 벗 로타리오를 만나러 가서, 그와 더불어 아내의 순결을 입증한 데서 얻은 그 값진 진주를 두고 기쁨을 쏟아 낼 기회가 오기를 애타게 바랐다."],["He longed for night and an opportunity of escaping from the house to go and see his good friend Lothario, and with him give vent to his joy over the precious pearl he had gained in having established his wife’s purity.","안주인과 하녀 둘 다 그가 빠져나갈 시간과 기회를 마련해 주려 마음을 썼고, 그는 그것을 틈타 빠져나가 곧장 로타리오를 찾아 나섰는데, 그를 찾아냈을 때 어떻게 그를 끌어안았는지, 또 마음의 기쁨에 겨워 그에게 무슨 말들을 했는지, 카밀라에게 어떤 찬사를 바쳤는지는 이루 다 형용할 수 없으리라. 로타리오는 이 모든 것을 들으면서도 아무런 기쁨도 내비치지 못했으니, 제 벗이 얼마나 속고 있는지, 또 자신이 얼마나 불명예스럽게 그를 욕보였는지를 잊을 수 없었던 까닭이다."],["Both mistress and maid took care to give him time and opportunity to get away, and taking advantage of it he made his escape, and at once went in quest of Lothario, and it would be impossible to describe how he embraced him when he found him, and the things he said to him in the joy of his heart, and the praises he bestowed upon Camilla; all which Lothario listened to without being able to show any pleasure, for he could not forget how deceived his friend was, and how dishonourably he had wronged him; and though Anselmo could see that Lothario was not glad, still he imagined it was only because he had left Camilla wounded and had been himself the cause of it; and so among other things he told him not to be distressed about Camilla’s accident, for, as they had agreed to hide it from him, the wound was evidently trifling; and that being so, he had no cause for fear, but should henceforward be of good cheer and rejoice with him, seeing that by his means and adroitness he found himself raised to the greatest height of happiness that he could have ventured to hope for, and desired no better pastime than making verses in praise of Camilla that would preserve her name for all time to come.","그리고 안셀모는 로타리오가 기뻐하지 않음을 알아챌 수 있었으되, 그저 그가 카밀라에게 상처를 입힌 채 두고 왔고 또 자신이 그 장본인이었던 탓이려니 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 여러 말 가운데, 카밀라의 사고를 두고 괴로워 말라고, 그것을 자기에게 숨기기로 둘이 합의한 것을 보면 그 상처가 분명 대수롭지 않은 것일 테니, 그렇다면 두려워할 까닭이 없고, 이제부터는 기운을 내어 자기와 더불어 기뻐해야 마땅하다고 그에게 일렀다."],["Lothario commended his purpose, and promised on his own part to aid him in raising a monument so glorious.","그의 솜씨와 능란함 덕에 자신이 감히 바랄 수 있었던 행복의 최고봉에 올랐음을 알게 되었으니, 카밀라의 이름을 길이길이 남길, 그녀를 찬미하는 시를 짓는 것보다 더 좋은 소일거리는 바라지 않는다는 것이었다. 로타리오는 그 뜻을 칭송하며, 그토록 영광스러운 기념비를 세우는 데 제 몫을 다해 그를 돕겠노라 약속했다."]],[["And so Anselmo was left the most charmingly hoodwinked man there could be in the world.","그리하여 안셀모는 이 세상에 다시없을 만큼 더없이 곱게 눈이 가려진 사내로 남게 되었다."],["He himself, persuaded he was conducting the instrument of his glory, led home by the hand him who had been the utter destruction of his good name; whom Camilla received with averted countenance, though with smiles in her heart.","제 영광의 도구를 이끌어 가고 있다고 믿은 그 자신이, 제 좋은 명성을 송두리째 무너뜨린 장본인의 손을 잡고 집으로 데려갔으니, 카밀라는 마음속으로는 미소를 머금은 채 고개를 돌린 얼굴로 그를 맞이했다."],["The deception was carried on for some time, until at the end of a few months Fortune turned her wheel and the guilt which had been until then so skilfully concealed was published abroad, and Anselmo paid with his life the penalty of his ill-advised curiosity.","이 속임수는 얼마간 이어졌으나, 몇 달이 지난 끝에 운명이 제 수레바퀴를 돌리니, 그때껏 그토록 교묘히 감추어졌던 그 죄가 만천하에 드러났고, 안셀모는 제 분별없는 호기심의 대가를 목숨으로 치렀다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXXV. WHICH TREATS OF THE HEROIC AND PRODIGIOUS BATTLE DON QUIXOTE HAD WITH CERTAIN SKINS OF RED WINE, AND BRINGS THE NOVEL OF “THE ILL-ADVISED CURIOSITY” TO A CLOSE","# CHAPTER XXXV."]],[["There remained but little more of the novel to be read, when Sancho Panza burst forth in wild excitement from the garret where Don Quixote was lying, shouting, “Run, sirs! quick; and help my master, who is in the thick of the toughest and stiffest battle I ever laid eyes on.","소설은 이제 읽을 것이 얼마 남지 않았는데, 그때 산초 판사가 돈키호테가 누워 있던 다락방에서 격한 흥분에 휩싸여 뛰쳐나오며 소리쳤다. \"여러분, 어서 달려가세요! 빨리, 우리 주인님을 도와주세요. 제가 평생 본 적 없는 가장 지독하고 사나운 싸움 한복판에 계십니다."],["By the living God he has given the giant, the enemy of my lady the Princess Micomicona, such a slash that he has sliced his head clean off as if it were a turnip.”","살아 계신 하느님께 맹세코, 우리 미코미코나 공주님의 원수인 그 거인에게 어찌나 칼을 휘두르셨는지, 무 자르듯 단번에 머리를 싹둑 베어 버리셨다니까요.\""]],[["“What are you talking about, brother?”","\"이 사람아, 대체 무슨 소리를 하는 게냐?\""],["said the curate, pausing as he was about to read the remainder of the novel.","소설의 나머지를 막 읽으려던 신부가 멈추고 말했다."],["“Are you in your senses, Sancho?","\"산초, 정신이 있는 게냐?"],["How the devil can it be as you say, when the giant is two thousand leagues away?”","그 거인이 이천 레과나 떨어진 곳에 있는데, 어떻게 자네 말대로 될 수 있단 말이냐?\""]],[["Here they heard a loud noise in the chamber, and Don Quixote shouting out, “Stand, thief, brigand, villain; now I have got thee, and thy scimitar shall not avail thee!”","이때 방 안에서 요란한 소리가 들리더니, 돈키호테가 고함치는 것이었다. \"멈춰라, 도둑놈아, 강도야, 악당아."],["And then it seemed as though he were slashing vigorously at the wall.","이제 내 손에 잡혔으니, 네 언월도 따위는 아무 소용이 없을 것이다!\" 그러더니 마치 벽을 향해 힘차게 칼질을 해 대는 듯한 소리가 들렸다."]],[["“Don’t stop to listen,” said Sancho, “but go in and part them or help my master: though there is no need of that now, for no doubt the giant is dead by this time and giving account to God of his past wicked life; for I saw the blood flowing on the ground, and the head cut off and fallen on one side, and it is as big as a large wine-skin.”","\"가만히 서서 듣고 계실 게 아니라요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"들어가서 둘을 떼어 놓든지 우리 주인님을 도우시라고요. 하기야 이제 그럴 것도 없겠네요. 분명 거인은 지금쯤 죽어서, 지나온 죄 많은 생을 하느님께 고하고 있을 테니까요. 제가 봤어요, 피가 바닥에 흐르고 잘려 나간 머리가 한쪽에 나뒹구는데, 그게 큼직한 포도주 가죽 부대만큼이나 컸다니까요.\""]],[["“May I die,” said the landlord at this, “if Don Quixote or Don Devil has not been slashing some of the skins of red wine that stand full at his bed’s head, and the spilt wine must be what this good fellow takes for blood;” and so saying he went into the room and the rest after him, and there they found Don Quixote in the strangest costume in the world. He was in his shirt, which was not long enough in front to cover his thighs completely and was six fingers shorter behind; his legs were very long and lean, covered with hair, and anything but clean; on his head he had a little greasy red cap that belonged to the host, round his left arm he had rolled the blanket of the bed, to which Sancho, for reasons best known to himself, owed a grudge, and in his right hand he held his unsheathed sword, with which he was slashing about on all sides, uttering exclamations as if he were actually fighting some giant: and the best of it was his eyes were not open, for he was fast asleep, and dreaming that he was doing battle with the giant. For his imagination was so wrought upon by the adventure he was going to accomplish, that it made him dream he had already reached the kingdom of Micomicon, and was engaged in combat with his enemy; and believing he was laying on the giant, he had given so many sword cuts to the skins that the whole room was full of wine. On seeing this the landlord was so enraged that he fell on Don Quixote, and with his clenched fist began to pummel him in such a way, that if Cardenio and the curate had not dragged him off, he would have brought the war of the giant to an end. But in spite of all the poor gentleman never woke until the barber brought a great pot of cold water from the well and flung it with one dash all over his body, on which Don Quixote woke up, but not so completely as to understand what was the matter. Dorothea, seeing how short and slight his attire was, would not go in to witness the battle between her champion and her opponent. As for Sancho, he went searching all over the floor for the head of the giant, and not finding it he said, “I see now that it’s all enchantment in this house; for the last time, on this very spot where I am now, I got ever so many thumps without knowing who gave them to me, or being able to see anybody; and now this head is not to be seen anywhere about, though I saw it cut off with my own eyes and the blood running from the body as if from a fountain.”","이 말에 주인이 말했다. \"내가 죽어도 좋소이다, 만일 저 돈키호테인지 돈악마인지 하는 자가 침대 머리맡에 그득히 세워 둔 적포도주 가죽 부대들을 칼질해 놓지 않았다면 말이오. 그러니 저 양반이 피라고 여기는 게 틀림없이 쏟아진 포도주일 게요.\" 이렇게 말하면서 그는 방으로 들어갔고 나머지 사람들도 뒤따랐다. 거기서 그들은 세상에서 가장 기이한 차림새를 한 돈키호테를 발견했다. 그는 셔츠 바람이었는데, 그 셔츠는 앞쪽이 넓적다리를 다 덮을 만큼 길지 못했고 뒤쪽은 여섯 손가락만큼이나 더 짧았다. 그의 다리는 무척 길고 비쩍 말랐으며 털로 덮여 있어 도무지 깨끗하다고는 할 수 없었다. 머리에는 주인의 것인 기름때 묻은 작고 빨간 모자를 쓰고 있었고, 왼팔에는 침대 담요를 둘둘 감고 있었으니, 산초가 자기만 아는 이유로 원한을 품고 있던 바로 그 담요였다. 오른손에는 칼집에서 뽑은 칼을 들고 사방으로 휘둘러 대며, 마치 실제로 어떤 거인과 싸우기라도 하는 양 고함을 질러 댔다. 그런데 무엇보다 가관인 것은 그의 두 눈이 감겨 있었다는 사실이니, 그는 곤히 잠들어 거인과 전투를 벌이는 꿈을 꾸고 있었던 것이다. 자신이 이루어 낼 모험에 그의 상상력이 어찌나 강하게 사로잡혔던지, 그는 이미 미코미콘 왕국에 다다라 원수와 전투를 벌이고 있는 꿈을 꾸기에 이르렀고, 거인에게 칼질을 퍼붓고 있다고 믿으며 가죽 부대들에 어찌나 많은 칼을 휘둘렀던지 방 안이 온통 포도주 천지가 되어 버린 것이었다. 이 광경을 본 주인은 어찌나 분이 치밀었던지 돈키호테에게 달려들어 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 마구 후려치기 시작했으니, 만일 카르데니오와 신부가 그를 끌어내지 않았더라면 그가 거인과의 전쟁을 끝장내 버렸을 것이다. 그러나 이 모든 일에도 불구하고 가엾은 양반은 도무지 깨어나지 않았으니, 이발사가 우물에서 차가운 물을 큰 항아리로 길어 와 그의 온몸에 단번에 끼얹고 나서야 돈키호테는 잠에서 깨어났다. 다만 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지를 알아차릴 만큼 온전히 깬 것은 아니었다. 도로테아는 그의 옷차림이 어찌나 짧고 빈약한지를 보고는, 자신의 기사와 자신의 적수 사이의 싸움을 구경하러 들어가려 하지 않았다. 산초로 말하자면, 거인의 머리를 찾느라 온 바닥을 헤집고 다녔으나 끝내 찾지 못하자 이렇게 말했다. \"이제야 알겠네요, 이 집은 죄다 마법이 걸려 있다니까요. 지난번에도 바로 지금 제가 서 있는 이 자리에서, 누가 때리는지도 모르고 아무도 보이지 않는 채로 무수히 두들겨 맞았잖습니까. 그런데 지금 이 머리도 어디에서도 보이질 않네요, 제 두 눈으로 똑똑히 잘려 나가는 걸 보았고 몸에서 샘솟듯 피가 흐르는 것도 보았는데 말입니다.\""]],[["“What blood and fountains are you talking about, enemy of God and his saints?”","\"무슨 피며 무슨 샘 타령이냐, 이 하느님과 그 성인들의 원수 같은 놈아?\""],["said the landlord.","주인이 말했다."],["“Don’t you see, you thief, that the blood and the fountain are only these skins here that have been stabbed and the red wine swimming all over the room?--and I wish I saw the soul of him that stabbed them swimming in hell.”","\"이 도둑놈아, 그 피니 샘이니 하는 게 죄다 여기 칼에 찔린 이 가죽 부대들이고 방 안에 온통 떠다니는 적포도주라는 게 보이지 않느냐? 차라리 이걸 찔러 놓은 놈의 영혼이 지옥에서 헤엄치는 꼴을 보았으면 좋겠다.\""]],[["“I know nothing about that,”","\"전 그런 건 아무것도 모릅니다.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho; “all I know is it will be my bad luck that through not finding this head my county will melt away like salt in water;”--for Sancho awake was worse than his master asleep, so much had his master’s promises addled his wits.","\"제가 아는 거라고는, 이 머리를 못 찾는 바람에 제 영지가 소금이 물에 녹듯 사라져 버린다면 그게 제 운수 사나운 팔자라는 것뿐이에요.\" 깨어 있는 산초가 잠든 주인보다 더 딱한 지경이었으니, 주인의 약속이 그토록 그의 정신을 흐려 놓았던 것이다."]],[["The landlord was beside himself at the coolness of the squire and the mischievous doings of the master, and swore it should not be like the last time when they went without paying; and that their privileges of chivalry should not hold good this time to let one or other of them off without paying, even to the cost of the plugs that would have to be put to the damaged wine-skins. The curate was holding Don Quixote’s hands, who, fancying he had now ended the adventure and was in the presence of the Princess Micomicona, knelt before the curate and said, “Exalted and beauteous lady, your highness may live from this day forth fearless of any harm this base being could do you; and I too from this day forth am released from the promise I gave you, since by the help of God on high and by the favour of her by whom I live and breathe, I have fulfilled it so successfully.”","주인은 종자의 천연덕스러움과 그 주인의 고약한 짓거리에 제정신이 아니었다. 그는 지난번에 셈도 치르지 않고 떠나 버린 것처럼 되지는 않을 것이며, 이번에는 기사도의 특권 따위가 통하여 둘 중 누구도 셈을 치르지 않고 빠져나가게 두지 않겠노라고, 망가진 가죽 부대에 박아 넣어야 할 마개 값까지도 받아 내겠노라고 맹세했다. 신부는 돈키호테의 두 손을 붙잡고 있었는데, 돈키호테는 이제 모험을 끝내고 미코미코나 공주 앞에 있다고 여기며 신부 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 말했다. \"고귀하고 아름다우신 공주님, 전하께서는 오늘부터 이 비천한 자가 끼칠 그 어떤 해도 두려워하지 않으시고 살아가실 수 있나이다. 그리고 저 또한 오늘부터 공주님께 드린 약속에서 풀려났으니, 높으신 하느님의 도움과, 제가 살아 숨 쉬는 까닭이 되는 그분의 은총으로 그 약속을 이토록 훌륭히 완수하였기 때문입니다.\""]],[["“Did not I say so?”","\"제가 그렇게 말씀드리지 않았습니까?\""],["said Sancho on hearing this.","이 말을 듣고 산초가 말했다. \"보세요, 전 취하지 않았다니까요."],["“You see I wasn’t drunk; there you see my master has already salted the giant; there’s no doubt about the bulls; my county is all right!”","저기 보시다시피 우리 주인님께서 벌써 거인을 소금에 절여 놓으셨잖습니까. 황소들은 의심할 여지가 없고, 제 영지도 멀쩡합니다!\""]],[["Who could have helped laughing at the absurdities of the pair, master and man?","주인과 하인, 이 한 쌍의 터무니없는 짓거리에 누군들 웃음을 참을 수 있었으랴? 과연 다들 웃었으니, 스스로를 저주하던 주인만은 예외였다."],["And laugh they did, all except the landlord, who cursed himself; but at length the barber, Cardenio, and the curate contrived with no small trouble to get Don Quixote on the bed, and he fell asleep with every appearance of excessive weariness.","그러나 마침내 이발사와 카르데니오와 신부는 적잖은 수고 끝에 돈키호테를 침대에 눕히는 데 성공했고, 그는 몹시 지친 기색이 역력한 채 잠이 들었다. 그들은 그를 자도록 두고 여관 문간으로 나와, 거인의 머리를 찾지 못한 산초 판사를 위로했다."],["They left him to sleep, and came out to the gate of the inn to console Sancho Panza on not having found the head of the giant; but much more work had they to appease the landlord, who was furious at the sudden death of his wine-skins; and said the landlady half scolding, half crying, “At an evil moment and in an unlucky hour he came into my house, this knight-errant--would that I had never set eyes on him, for dear he has cost me; the last time he went off with the overnight score against him for supper, bed, straw, and barley, for himself and his squire and a hack and an ass, saying he was a knight adventurer--God send unlucky adventures to him and all the adventurers in the world--and therefore not bound to pay anything, for it was so settled by the knight-errantry tariff: and then, all because of him, came the other gentleman and carried off my tail, and gives it back more than two cuartillos the worse, all stripped of its hair, so that it is no use for my husband’s purpose; and then, for a finishing touch to all, to burst my wine-skins and spill my wine!","그러나 그보다 훨씬 더 애를 먹은 것은 자기 가죽 부대들의 갑작스러운 죽음에 노발대발하는 주인을 달래는 일이었다. 안주인은 반은 꾸짖고 반은 울먹이며 말했다."],["I wish I saw his own blood spilt!","\"이 편력 기사가 우리 집에 들어온 게 흉한 순간이요 불길한 시각이었지."],["But let him not deceive himself, for, by the bones of my father and the shade of my mother, they shall pay me down every quarto; or my name is not what it is, and I am not my father’s daughter.”","차라리 그자에게 눈길 한 번 주지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을, 그자 때문에 큰 손해를 봤으니 말이오. 지난번에는 저 자신과 종자, 말과 당나귀 몫으로 든 저녁 식사며 잠자리며 짚이며 보리 값을 외상으로 달아 놓고 떠나 버렸지."],["All this and more to the same effect the landlady delivered with great irritation, and her good maid Maritornes backed her up, while the daughter held her peace and smiled from time to time.","자기는 모험을 찾아다니는 기사라며—하느님께서 그자와 세상 모든 모험가에게 불운한 모험을 내려 주시길—그러니 아무것도 갚을 의무가 없다는 거였소, 기사도 요금표에 그렇게 정해져 있다나. 그러더니 죄다 그자 때문에 그 다른 양반이 와서 내 꼬리를 가져가더니, 털이 죄다 벗겨진 채 두 쿠아르티요어치도 넘게 망가져서 돌려주는 바람에 우리 남편이 쓰려던 데에는 아무 쓸모도 없게 돼 버렸지."],["The curate smoothed matters by promising to make good all losses to the best of his power, not only as regarded the wine-skins but also the wine, and above all the depreciation of the tail which they set such store by.","게다가 모든 일에 마무리 손질이라도 하듯, 내 가죽 부대를 터뜨리고 포도주를 쏟아 버리다니! 차라리 그자 제 피가 쏟아지는 꼴을 보았으면 좋겠소!"],["Dorothea comforted Sancho, telling him that she pledged herself, as soon as it should appear certain that his master had decapitated the giant, and she found herself peacefully established in her kingdom, to bestow upon him the best county there was in it.","하지만 그자가 착각은 말아야 할 거요. 내 아버지의 유골과 내 어머니의 혼령에 걸고, 한 푼도 빠짐없이 받아 내고야 말 테니까."],["With this Sancho consoled himself, and assured the princess she might rely upon it that he had seen the head of the giant, and more by token it had a beard that reached to the girdle, and that if it was not to be seen now it was because everything that happened in that house went by enchantment, as he himself had proved the last time he had lodged there.","안 그러면 내 이름이 내 이름이 아니고, 내가 내 아버지의 딸이 아닐 거요.\" 안주인은 이 모든 것과 그 비슷한 말을 더 늘어놓으며 잔뜩 골을 냈고, 착한 하녀 마리토르네스도 거들었으며, 딸은 잠자코 이따금 미소만 지었다."],["Dorothea said she fully believed it, and that he need not be uneasy, for all would go well and turn out as he wished.","신부는 가죽 부대뿐 아니라 포도주, 그리고 무엇보다 그들이 그토록 애지중지하던 꼬리의 손상까지 모든 손실을 자기 능력껏 보상해 주겠노라 약속하여 일을 무마했다. 도로테아는 산초를 위로하며, 그의 주인이 거인의 목을 베었음이 확실해지고 자신이 자기 왕국에 평화로이 자리 잡게 되는 즉시, 그 안에서 가장 좋은 영지를 그에게 내리겠노라 다짐했다."],["All therefore being appeased, the curate was anxious to go on with the novel, as he saw there was but little more left to read.","이에 산초는 마음을 달랬고, 자기는 거인의 머리를 분명히 보았으며, 더구나 허리띠까지 닿는 수염이 달려 있더라고, 그리고 지금 그것이 보이지 않는다면 그건 이 집에서 벌어지는 모든 일이 마법으로 돌아가기 때문이며, 지난번에 그곳에 묵었을 때 자신이 직접 겪어 본 바이기도 하다고 공주에게 장담했다. 도로테아는 그 말을 충분히 믿으며, 모든 것이 잘되어 그가 바라는 대로 풀릴 테니 걱정할 것 없다고 말했다."],["Dorothea and the others begged him to finish it, and he, as he was willing to please them, and enjoyed reading it himself, continued the tale in these words:","이리하여 모두가 가라앉자, 신부는 이제 읽을 것이 얼마 남지 않은 것을 보고 소설을 계속 읽어 나가고 싶어 했다. 도로테아와 다른 이들이 끝까지 읽어 달라 청하니, 그도 그들을 기쁘게 해 주고 싶었고 자신도 읽는 것이 즐거웠던지라, 이런 말로 이야기를 이어 갔다."]],[["The result was, that from the confidence Anselmo felt in Camilla’s virtue, he lived happy and free from anxiety, and Camilla purposely looked coldly on Lothario, that Anselmo might suppose her feelings towards him to be the opposite of what they were; and the better to support the position, Lothario begged to be excused from coming to the house, as the displeasure with which Camilla regarded his presence was plain to be seen.","그 결과, 카밀라의 정절에 대한 안셀모의 믿음으로 그는 행복하게 근심 없이 살았고, 카밀라는 안셀모가 자신의 감정을 실제와는 정반대로 여기도록 일부러 로타리오를 냉랭하게 대했다. 그리고 그 처지를 더 잘 떠받치기 위해 로타리오는, 카밀라가 자신의 존재를 못마땅해하는 기색이 뻔히 보인다며 그 집에 드나드는 일을 면제해 달라고 청했다."],["But the befooled Anselmo said he would on no account allow such a thing, and so in a thousand ways he became the author of his own dishonour, while he believed he was insuring his happiness.","그러나 어리석게 속아 넘어간 안셀모는 그런 일은 결코 용납할 수 없다고 했으니, 이리하여 그는 자신의 행복을 보장하고 있다고 믿으면서 천 가지 방식으로 제 불명예의 장본인이 되어 갔다. 한편 레오넬라는 자신이 정사를 이어 갈 권한을 쥐었다고 여기는 데서 오는 만족감이 어찌나 높아졌던지, 다른 모든 것을 아랑곳하지 않고 거리낌 없이 제 욕망을 좇았으니, 제 안주인이 자신을 감싸 주고 심지어 어떻게 하면 안전하게 일을 처리할 수 있는지까지 일러 주리라 자신했던 것이다."],["Meanwhile the satisfaction with which Leonela saw herself empowered to carry on her amour reached such a height that, regardless of everything else, she followed her inclinations unrestrainedly, feeling confident that her mistress would screen her, and even show her how to manage it safely.","마침내 어느 날 밤 안셀모는 레오넬라의 방에서 발소리를 들었고, 누구인지 보려고 들어가려 했으나 문이 안에서 막혀 있는 것을 알고는 더욱 기를 쓰고 열려 했다. 그가 힘을 써서 문을 밀어젖히고 방에 들어선 순간, 마침 한 남자가 창문을 넘어 길거리로 뛰어내리는 것이 보였다."],["At last one night Anselmo heard footsteps in Leonela’s room, and on trying to enter to see who it was, he found that the door was held against him, which made him all the more determined to open it; and exerting his strength he forced it open, and entered the room in time to see a man leaping through the window into the street.","그는 그자를 붙잡거나 누구인지 알아내려고 재빨리 달려갔으나 둘 다 이루지 못했으니, 레오넬라가 그에게 두 팔을 두르고 매달리며 외쳤기 때문이다. \"진정하세요, 나리."],["He ran quickly to seize him or discover who he was, but he was unable to effect either purpose, for Leonela flung her arms round him crying, “Be calm, señor; do not give way to passion or follow him who has escaped from this; he belongs to me, and in fact he is my husband.”","분에 못 이겨 저기 도망친 자를 뒤쫓지 마세요. 그이는 제 사람, 사실은 제 남편이랍니다.\""]],[["Anselmo would not believe it, but blind with rage drew a dagger and threatened to stab Leonela, bidding her tell the truth or he would kill her.","안셀모는 그 말을 믿지 않고 분노로 눈이 멀어 단검을 뽑아 들고는 레오넬라를 찌르겠다고 위협하며, 사실대로 말하지 않으면 죽여 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 그녀는 두려움에 사로잡혀 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 외쳤다."],["She, in her fear, not knowing what she was saying, exclaimed, “Do not kill me, señor, for I can tell you things more important than any you can imagine.”","\"저를 죽이지 마세요, 나리. 나리께서 상상하실 수 있는 그 어떤 것보다도 더 중대한 일들을 말씀드릴 수 있으니까요.\""]],[["“Tell me then at once or thou diest,”","\"그렇다면 당장 말하라, 아니면 죽으리라.\""],["said Anselmo.","안셀모가 말했다."]],[["“It would be impossible for me now,” said Leonela, “I am so agitated: leave me till to-morrow, and then you shall hear from me what will fill you with astonishment; but rest assured that he who leaped through the window is a young man of this city, who has given me his promise to become my husband.”","\"지금은 도저히 안 되겠어요.\" 레오넬라가 말했다. \"제가 너무 떨려서요. 내일까지 저를 내버려 두세요, 그러면 나리를 깜짝 놀라게 할 이야기를 제게서 들으실 거예요. 다만 창문으로 뛰어내린 자가 이 도시의 청년이며, 제 남편이 되기로 약속한 사람이라는 것만은 믿어 주세요.\""]],[["Anselmo was appeased with this, and was content to wait the time she asked of him, for he never expected to hear anything against Camilla, so satisfied and sure of her virtue was he; and so he quitted the room, and left Leonela locked in, telling her she should not come out until she had told him all she had to make known to him.","안셀모는 이 말에 마음이 가라앉아 그녀가 청한 시간을 기다리는 데 만족했으니, 그는 카밀라의 정절을 그토록 흡족해하고 확신하던 터라 그녀에게 불리한 말은 무엇도 들으리라고는 결코 예상하지 못했던 것이다. 그리하여 그는 방을 나서며 레오넬라를 가둔 채, 자기에게 밝혀야 할 모든 것을 다 말하기 전까지는 나오지 말라고 일러두었다."],["He went at once to see Camilla, and tell her, as he did, all that had passed between him and her handmaid, and the promise she had given him to inform him matters of serious importance.","그는 곧장 카밀라를 만나러 가서, 자기와 그녀의 하녀 사이에 오간 모든 일과, 중대한 일들을 알려 주겠다던 그녀의 약속을 그대로 들려주었다."]],[["There is no need of saying whether Camilla was agitated or not, for so great was her fear and dismay, that, making sure, as she had good reason to do, that Leonela would tell Anselmo all she knew of her faithlessness, she had not the courage to wait and see if her suspicions were confirmed; and that same night, as soon as she thought that Anselmo was asleep, she packed up the most valuable jewels she had and some money, and without being observed by anybody escaped from the house and betook herself to Lothario’s, to whom she related what had occurred, imploring him to convey her to some place of safety or fly with her where they might be safe from Anselmo.","카밀라가 동요했는지 아닌지는 말할 필요도 없으니, 그녀의 두려움과 낭패감이 어찌나 컸던지, 레오넬라가 자신의 부정에 관해 아는 모든 것을 안셀모에게 일러바칠 것이라고—그럴 만한 충분한 이유가 있었으므로—확신하고는, 제 의심이 사실로 드러나는지를 차마 기다려 볼 용기가 없었던 것이다. 그리하여 바로 그날 밤, 안셀모가 잠들었다 싶자마자 그녀는 가진 것 중 가장 값진 보석들과 약간의 돈을 챙겨, 아무에게도 들키지 않고 집을 빠져나가 로타리오에게로 달려갔다."],["The state of perplexity to which Camilla reduced Lothario was such that he was unable to utter a word in reply, still less to decide upon what he should do.","그녀는 그에게 벌어진 일을 이야기하며, 자신을 어딘가 안전한 곳으로 데려다주거나 함께 달아나 안셀모의 손이 미치지 못할 곳으로 가 달라고 애원했다. 카밀라가 로타리오를 몰아넣은 당혹스러움이 어찌나 컸던지, 그는 대답으로 한마디도 내뱉지 못했으며 하물며 무엇을 해야 할지조차 정하지 못했다."],["At length he resolved to conduct her to a convent of which a sister of his was prioress; Camilla agreed to this, and with the speed which the circumstances demanded, Lothario took her to the convent and left her there, and then himself quitted the city without letting anyone know of his departure.","마침내 그는 자기 누이가 원장으로 있는 한 수녀원으로 그녀를 데려가기로 결심했다. 카밀라가 이에 동의하자, 로타리오는 사정이 요구하는 신속함으로 그녀를 수녀원으로 데려가 그곳에 남겨 두고는, 자신은 누구에게도 떠난다는 사실을 알리지 않은 채 도시를 떠나 버렸다."]],[["As soon as daylight came Anselmo, without missing Camilla from his side, rose cager to learn what Leonela had to tell him, and hastened to the room where he had locked her in.","날이 밝자마자 안셀모는 곁에 카밀라가 없는 것도 알아차리지 못한 채, 레오넬라가 자기에게 들려줄 이야기를 알고 싶은 마음에 일어나 그녀를 가두어 둔 방으로 서둘러 갔다. 그는 문을 열고 들어갔으나 레오넬라는 보이지 않았다."],["He opened the door, entered, but found no Leonela; all he found was some sheets knotted to the window, a plain proof that she had let herself down from it and escaped.","그가 발견한 것이라고는 창문에 매듭지어 묶인 시트 몇 장뿐이었으니, 그녀가 그것을 타고 내려가 도망쳤다는 명백한 증거였다. 그는 불안해하며 카밀라에게 알리러 돌아갔으나, 침대에도 집 안 어디에도 그녀가 보이지 않자 어찌할 바를 몰랐다."],["He returned, uneasy, to tell Camilla, but not finding her in bed or anywhere in the house he was lost in amazement.","그는 집안의 하인들에게 그녀에 관해 물었으나 누구도 설명해 주지 못했다. 카밀라를 찾아 나서던 중 우연히 그는 그녀의 상자들이 열려 있고 보석 대부분이 사라진 것을 보게 되었다."],["He asked the servants of the house about her, but none of them could give him any explanation.","그제야 그는 자신의 치욕을 온전히 깨달았으며, 레오넬라가 자기 불행의 원인이 아니라는 것도 알게 되었다."],["As he was going in search of Camilla it happened by chance that he observed her boxes were lying open, and that the greater part of her jewels were gone; and now he became fully aware of his disgrace, and that Leonela was not the cause of his misfortune; and, just as he was, without delaying to dress himself completely, he repaired, sad at heart and dejected, to his friend Lothario to make known his sorrow to him; but when he failed to find him and the servants reported that he had been absent from his house all night and had taken with him all the money he had, he felt as though he were losing his senses; and to make all complete on returning to his own house he found it deserted and empty, not one of all his servants, male or female, remaining in it.","그리하여 그는 옷도 채 제대로 갖춰 입지 않은 그대로, 슬픔에 잠기고 의기소침하여 자기 벗 로타리오에게 그 슬픔을 털어놓으러 갔다. 그러나 그를 찾지 못하고, 하인들이 그가 밤새 집을 비웠으며 가진 돈을 모조리 가지고 갔다고 전하자, 그는 제정신을 잃는 듯한 기분이 들었다."],["He knew not what to think, or say, or do, and his reason seemed to be deserting him little by little.","그리고 모든 것의 마무리를 짓듯, 제 집으로 돌아왔을 때 그는 집이 텅 비어 버려 남녀 하인 가운데 하나도 남아 있지 않은 것을 보았다. 그는 무엇을 생각해야 할지, 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 무엇을 해야 할지 알 수 없었고, 그의 이성은 조금씩 그를 떠나가는 듯했다."],["He reviewed his position, and saw himself in a moment left without wife, friend, or servants, abandoned, he felt, by the heaven above him, and more than all robbed of his honour, for in Camilla’s disappearance he saw his own ruin.","그는 자신의 처지를 돌아보며, 한순간에 아내도 벗도 하인도 없이 버려진 자신을, 머리 위의 하늘에마저 버림받았다고 느끼는 자신을, 그리고 그 무엇보다도 명예를 빼앗긴 자신을 보았으니, 카밀라가 사라진 데서 그는 곧 제 자신의 파멸을 보았던 것이다. 오랜 숙고 끝에 그는 마침내, 이 불행의 얽힘이 꾸며질 빌미를 스스로 마련하던 동안 머물렀던 벗의 마을로 가기로 결심했다."],["After long reflection he resolved at last to go to his friend’s village, where he had been staying when he afforded opportunities for the contrivance of this complication of misfortune.","그는 집 문들을 잠그고 말에 올라, 꺾인 마음으로 길을 나섰다. 그러나 절반도 채 가지 못해 제 상념에 시달린 나머지, 말에서 내려 나무에 말을 매어 두고 그 밑동에 몸을 던지고는 가슴 미어지는 가련한 한숨을 토해 내야 했다."],["He locked the doors of his house, mounted his horse, and with a broken spirit set out on his journey; but he had hardly gone half-way when, harassed by his reflections, he had to dismount and tie his horse to a tree, at the foot of which he threw himself, giving vent to piteous heartrending sighs; and there he remained till nearly nightfall, when he observed a man approaching on horseback from the city, of whom, after saluting him, he asked what was the news in Florence.","그는 거의 해 질 녘까지 그곳에 머물렀는데, 그때 도시 쪽에서 한 남자가 말을 타고 다가오는 것이 보였다. 그는 그에게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 피렌체에 무슨 소식이 있는지를 물었다."]],[["The citizen replied, “The strangest that have been heard for many a day; for it is reported abroad that Lothario, the great friend of the wealthy Anselmo, who lived at San Giovanni, carried off last night Camilla, the wife of Anselmo, who also has disappeared. All this has been told by a maid-servant of Camilla’s, whom the governor found last night lowering herself by a sheet from the windows of Anselmo’s house. I know not indeed, precisely, how the affair came to pass; all I know is that the whole city is wondering at the occurrence, for no one could have expected a thing of the kind, seeing the great and intimate friendship that existed between them, so great, they say, that they were called ‘The Two Friends.’”","그 시민이 대답했다. \"여러 날 만에 들은 가장 기이한 소식입죠. 산조반니에 살던 부유한 안셀모의 둘도 없는 벗 로타리오가 지난밤 안셀모의 아내 카밀라를 데리고 달아났는데, 그 아내도 함께 사라졌다고 온 동네에 소문이 자자합니다. 이 모든 게 카밀라의 하녀 입에서 나온 이야긴데, 그 하녀를 총독이 지난밤 안셀모의 집 창문에서 시트를 타고 내려오던 걸 붙잡았답니다. 사실 그 일이 어찌 된 영문인지는 저도 정확히 모릅니다. 제가 아는 거라곤 온 도시가 이 일에 놀라워한다는 것뿐인데요, 그 둘 사이에 오갔던 그 크고도 깊은 우정으로 보아 누구도 이런 일을 예상치 못했을 테니까요. 그 우정이 어찌나 컸던지 사람들이 그들을 '두 친구'라고 불렀다지 않습니까.\""]],[["“Is it known at all,”","\"로타리오와 카밀라가 어느 길로 갔는지는 도무지 알 수 없소?\""],["said Anselmo, “what road Lothario and Camilla took?”","안셀모가 물었다."]],[["“Not in the least,”","\"전혀요.\""],["said the citizen, “though the governor has been very active in searching for them.”","시민이 말했다. \"총독이 그들을 찾느라 무척 분주하긴 했습니다만.\""]],[["“God speed you, señor,”","\"하느님의 가호가 함께하시길, 나리.\""],["said Anselmo.","안셀모가 말했다."]],[["“God be with you,”","\"하느님이 당신과 함께하시길.\""],["said the citizen and went his way.","시민은 이렇게 말하고 제 갈 길을 갔다."]],[["This disastrous intelligence almost robbed Anselmo not only of his senses but of his life.","이 참담한 소식은 안셀모에게서 제정신뿐 아니라 목숨마저 거의 앗아 갈 뻔했다."],["He got up as well as he was able and reached the house of his friend, who as yet knew nothing of his misfortune, but seeing him come pale, worn, and haggard, perceived that he was suffering some heavy affliction.","그는 간신히 몸을 일으켜 벗의 집에 다다랐는데, 그 벗은 아직 안셀모의 불행을 전혀 모르고 있었으나, 그가 창백하고 초췌하며 수척한 모습으로 오는 것을 보고는 그가 어떤 무거운 고통을 겪고 있음을 알아차렸다."],["Anselmo at once begged to be allowed to retire to rest, and to be given writing materials.","안셀모는 곧장 쉬게 해 달라고, 그리고 글 쓸 도구를 달라고 청했다."],["His wish was complied with and he was left lying down and alone, for he desired this, and even that the door should be locked.","그의 청은 받아들여졌고, 그가 그러기를 바랐던지라—심지어 문도 잠가 주기를 바랐던지라—그는 홀로 누운 채 남겨졌다."],["Finding himself alone he so took to heart the thought of his misfortune that by the signs of death he felt within him he knew well his life was drawing to a close, and therefore he resolved to leave behind him a declaration of the cause of his strange end.","홀로 있게 되자 그는 제 불행에 대한 생각을 어찌나 마음 깊이 새겼던지, 제 안에서 느껴지는 죽음의 징후로 자기 목숨이 끝나 가고 있음을 잘 알게 되었고, 그리하여 자신의 기이한 죽음의 원인을 밝히는 글을 남기기로 결심했다."],["He began to write, but before he had put down all he meant to say, his breath failed him and he yielded up his life, a victim to the suffering which his ill-advised curiosity had entailed upon him.","그는 글을 쓰기 시작했으나, 하려던 말을 다 적어 내려가기도 전에 숨이 끊어져, 그릇된 호기심이 그에게 안긴 고통의 희생양이 되어 목숨을 내놓고 말았다."],["The master of the house observing that it was now late and that Anselmo did not call, determined to go in and ascertain if his indisposition was increasing, and found him lying on his face, his body partly in the bed, partly on the writing-table, on which he lay with the written paper open and the pen still in his hand.","집주인은 이제 시간이 늦었는데도 안셀모가 부르지 않는 것을 보고, 들어가서 그의 병세가 더해 가는지 알아보기로 마음먹었다. 들어가 보니 그는 엎드린 채, 몸의 일부는 침대에, 일부는 글 쓰던 책상 위에 걸쳐져 있었고, 그 책상 위에는 글 쓴 종이가 펼쳐져 있었으며 펜은 여전히 그의 손에 쥐어져 있었다."],["Having first called to him without receiving any answer, his host approached him, and taking him by the hand, found that it was cold, and saw that he was dead.","주인은 먼저 그를 불러 보았으나 아무 대답이 없자, 다가가 그의 손을 잡아 보고는 그것이 차갑게 식은 것을 느꼈고, 그가 죽었음을 알았다."],["Greatly surprised and distressed he summoned the household to witness the sad fate which had befallen Anselmo; and then he read the paper, the handwriting of which he recognised as his, and which contained these words:","크게 놀라고 비탄에 잠긴 그는 집안 식구들을 불러 안셀모에게 닥친 슬픈 운명을 함께 지켜보게 했다. 그러고 나서 그는 그 종이를 읽었는데, 그 필체가 안셀모의 것임을 알아보았으며, 거기에는 이런 말이 적혀 있었다."]],[["“A foolish and ill-advised desire has robbed me of life.","\"어리석고 그릇된 욕망이 내게서 목숨을 앗아 갔다. 내 죽음의 소식이 카밀라의 귀에 닿거든, 내가 그녀를 용서한다는 것을 알게 하라."],["If the news of my death should reach the ears of Camilla, let her know that I forgive her, for she was not bound to perform miracles, nor ought I to have required her to perform them; and since I have been the author of my own dishonour, there is no reason why-”","그녀는 기적을 행할 의무가 없었고, 나 또한 그녀에게 기적을 행하라 요구하지 말았어야 했으니. 그리고 내가 내 불명예의 장본인이었으니, 무슨 까닭으로—\""]],[["So far Anselmo had written, and thus it was plain that at this point, before he could finish what he had to say, his life came to an end.","안셀모는 여기까지 썼으니, 이로써 분명한 것은 하려던 말을 채 끝맺기도 전에 이 대목에서 그의 목숨이 다했다는 사실이다."],["The next day his friend sent intelligence of his death to his relatives, who had already ascertained his misfortune, as well as the convent where Camilla lay almost on the point of accompanying her husband on that inevitable journey, not on account of the tidings of his death, but because of those she received of her lover’s departure.","이튿날 그의 벗은 그의 죽음을 친척들에게 알렸는데, 그들은 이미 그의 불행을 알고 있었으며, 카밀라가 누워 있는 수녀원에도 알렸으니, 그녀는 거의 남편을 따라 그 피할 수 없는 여정에 오를 지경에 이르러 있었다."],["Although she saw herself a widow, it is said she refused either to quit the convent or take the veil, until, not long afterwards, intelligence reached her that Lothario had been killed in a battle in which M.","그것은 남편의 죽음을 알리는 소식 때문이 아니라, 자기 연인이 떠났다는 소식을 받았기 때문이었다. 비록 자신이 과부가 된 처지였으나, 그녀는 수녀원을 떠나는 것도 베일을 쓰는 것도 거부했다고 전한다."],["de Lautrec had been recently engaged with the Great Captain Gonzalo Fernandez de Cordova in the kingdom of Naples, whither her too late repentant lover had repaired.","그러다 얼마 지나지 않아, 너무 늦게 뉘우친 그녀의 연인이 찾아갔던 나폴리 왕국에서, 라우트레크 공이 위대한 장군 곤살로 페르난데스 데 코르도바와 근래 벌인 전투 중에 로타리오가 전사했다는 소식이 그녀에게 닿았다."],["On learning this Camilla took the veil, and shortly afterwards died, worn out by grief and melancholy.","이를 알게 된 카밀라는 베일을 썼고, 얼마 후 슬픔과 우울에 시달리다 세상을 떠났다."],["This was the end of all three, an end that came of a thoughtless beginning.","이것이 세 사람 모두의 끝이었으니, 분별없는 시작에서 비롯된 끝이었다."]],[["“I like this novel,”","\"나는 이 소설이 마음에 드네.\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate; “but I cannot persuade myself of its truth; and if it has been invented, the author’s invention is faulty, for it is impossible to imagine any husband so foolish as to try such a costly experiment as Anselmo’s.","\"허나 그것이 사실이라고는 도무지 믿어지지 않으이. 그리고 만일 지어낸 것이라면 작자의 창작에 결함이 있는 것이, 안셀모처럼 그토록 값비싼 시험을 해 보려는 어리석은 남편이 있으리라고는 도저히 상상할 수 없기 때문일세."],["If it had been represented as occurring between a gallant and his mistress it might pass; but between husband and wife there is something of an impossibility about it.","만일 한량과 그 정부 사이에 일어난 일로 그려졌다면 그럴듯하게 넘어갈 만하지. 허나 남편과 아내 사이라니, 거기에는 불가능한 구석이 있단 말일세."],["As to the way in which the story is told, however, I have no fault to find.”","다만 이야기를 풀어 나간 방식으로 말하자면, 나로서는 흠잡을 데가 없네.\""]],[["# CHAPTER XXXVI. WHICH TREATS OF MORE CURIOUS INCIDENTS THAT OCCURRED AT THE INN","# CHAPTER XXXVI."]],[["Just at that instant the landlord, who was standing at the gate of the inn, exclaimed, “Here comes a fine troop of guests; if they stop here we may say gaudeamus.”","바로 그 순간, 객줏집 문간에 서 있던 주인이 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"근사한 손님 한 무리가 오고 있구먼. 저 사람들이 여기서 묵는다면 가우데아무스라 할 만하지.\" * 가우데아무스(gaudeamus): 라틴어로 \"기뻐하자\"라는 뜻."]],[["“What are they?”","\"어떤 사람들이오?\""],["said Cardenio.","카르데니오가 물었다."]],[["“Four men,” said the landlord, “riding a la jineta, with lances and bucklers, and all with black veils, and with them there is a woman in white on a side-saddle, whose face is also veiled, and two attendants on foot.”","\"네 사람이오.\" 주인이 말했다. \"라 히네타식으로 말을 타고, 창과 둥근 방패를 들었으며, 모두 검은 베일을 쓰고 있소. 그들과 함께 흰옷을 입은 여인이 한 사람 곁안장에 앉아 있는데 그 얼굴 역시 베일에 가려져 있고, 걸어서 따라오는 시종이 둘이오.\" * 라 히네타(a la jineta): 등자를 짧게 하고 무릎을 굽혀 타는 무어식 승마법."]],[["“Are they very near?”","\"아주 가까이 왔소?\""],["said the curate.","신부가 물었다."]],[["“So near,”","\"어찌나 가까운지,\""],["answered the landlord, “that here they come.”","주인이 대답했다. \"벌써 이리 들어오는구먼.\""]],[["Hearing this Dorothea covered her face, and Cardenio retreated into Don Quixote’s room, and they hardly had time to do so before the whole party the host had described entered the inn, and the four that were on horseback, who were of highbred appearance and bearing, dismounted, and came forward to take down the woman who rode on the side-saddle, and one of them taking her in his arms placed her in a chair that stood at the entrance of the room where Cardenio had hidden himself.","이 말을 듣고 도로테아는 얼굴을 가렸고, 카르데니오는 돈키호테의 방으로 물러났으니, 그들이 그리할 겨를도 채 없이 주인이 묘사한 그 무리 전체가 객줏집으로 들어섰다. 말을 타고 있던 네 사람은 기품 있는 용모와 태도를 지녔는데, 말에서 내려 곁안장에 탄 여인을 부축해 내리려고 앞으로 나섰다."],["All this time neither she nor they had removed their veils or spoken a word, only on sitting down on the chair the woman gave a deep sigh and let her arms fall like one that was ill and weak.","그중 한 사람이 여인을 두 팔로 받아 안아, 카르데니오가 몸을 숨긴 방 입구에 놓인 의자에 앉혔다. 그동안 내내 여인도 그들도 베일을 벗지 않았고 한마디도 하지 않았으며, 다만 의자에 앉으면서 여인이 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 병들고 쇠약한 사람처럼 두 팔을 늘어뜨릴 뿐이었다."],["The attendants on foot then led the horses away to the stable.","걸어서 따라온 시종들은 그러고 나서 말들을 마구간으로 끌고 갔다."],["Observing this the curate, curious to know who these people in such a dress and preserving such silence were, went to where the servants were standing and put the question to one of them, who answered him.","이를 지켜보던 신부는 이런 차림에 이토록 침묵을 지키는 사람들이 대체 누구인지 궁금하여, 하인들이 서 있는 곳으로 가서 그중 한 사람에게 물었고, 그가 대답했다."]],[["“Faith, sir, I cannot tell you who they are, I only know they seem to be people of distinction, particularly he who advanced to take the lady you saw in his arms; and I say so because all the rest show him respect, and nothing is done except what he directs and orders.”","\"솔직히 말씀드리자면, 나리, 저들이 누구인지 저로서는 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다. 다만 신분이 높은 분들 같다는 것만 알지요. 특히 보신 그 부인을 두 팔로 안아 내린 분이 그렇습니다. 이렇게 말씀드리는 까닭은 나머지 사람들이 모두 그분께 경의를 표하고, 그분이 지시하고 명령하는 대로만 모든 일이 이루어지기 때문입니다.\""]],[["“And the lady, who is she?”","\"그러면 그 부인은 누구요?\""],["asked the curate.","신부가 물었다."]],[["“That I cannot tell you either,” said the servant, “for I have not seen her face all the way: I have indeed heard her sigh many times and utter such groans that she seems to be giving up the ghost every time; but it is no wonder if we do not know more than we have told you, as my comrade and I have only been in their company two days, for having met us on the road they begged and persuaded us to accompany them to Andalusia, promising to pay us well.”","\"그것 역시 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다.\" 하인이 말했다. \"오는 길 내내 그분의 얼굴을 보지 못했으니까요. 한숨을 여러 번 내쉬고 매번 숨이 넘어갈 듯한 신음을 토하는 것은 분명히 들었습니다만, 저희가 말씀드린 것 이상을 모른다 해도 이상할 게 없습지요. 제 동료와 저는 저분들과 함께한 지 겨우 이틀밖에 안 됐으니 말입니다. 길에서 저희를 만나, 보수를 두둑이 주겠노라 약속하며 안달루시아까지 동행해 달라 간청하고 설득했거든요.\""]],[["“And have you heard any of them called by his name?”","\"그리고 그들 가운데 누가 이름으로 불리는 것을 들은 적이 있소?\""],["asked the curate.","신부가 물었다."]],[["“No, indeed,” replied the servant; “they all preserve a marvellous silence on the road, for not a sound is to be heard among them except the poor lady’s sighs and sobs, which make us pity her; and we feel sure that wherever it is she is going, it is against her will, and as far as one can judge from her dress she is a nun or, what is more likely, about to become one; and perhaps it is because taking the vows is not of her own free will, that she is so unhappy as she seems to be.”","\"아닙니다, 정말로.\" 하인이 대답했다. \"길에서 다들 놀랍도록 침묵을 지켜서, 그 가엾은 부인의 한숨과 흐느낌 말고는 그들 사이에서 아무 소리도 들리지 않습니다. 그 소리가 저희를 가엾게 만들지요. 그리고 저희는 그분이 어디로 가든 본인의 뜻과는 어긋나는 길이라고 확신합니다. 차림새로 미루어 판단하건대 그분은 수녀이거나, 더 그럴듯하게는 수녀가 되려는 분 같습니다. 어쩌면 서원하는 것이 본인의 자유로운 뜻이 아니어서 저렇게 불행해 보이는지도 모르지요.\""]],[["“That may well be,”","\"능히 그럴 만하오.\""],["said the curate, and leaving them he returned to where Dorothea was, who, hearing the veiled lady sigh, moved by natural compassion drew near to her and said, “What are you suffering from, señora?","신부는 이렇게 말하고 그들을 떠나 도로테아가 있는 곳으로 돌아왔다. 도로테아는 베일을 쓴 부인이 한숨 쉬는 소리를 듣고 타고난 연민에 이끌려 그녀에게 다가가 말했다."],["If it be anything that women are accustomed and know how to relieve, I offer you my services with all my heart.”","\"무엇 때문에 그리 괴로워하십니까, 세뇨라? 여인들이 으레 덜어 드릴 줄 아는 그런 것이라면, 진심을 다해 제 도움을 드리겠습니다.\""]],[["To this the unhappy lady made no reply; and though Dorothea repeated her offers more earnestly she still kept silence, until the gentleman with the veil, who, the servant said, was obeyed by the rest, approached and said to Dorothea, “Do not give yourself the trouble, señora, of making any offers to that woman, for it is her way to give no thanks for anything that is done for her; and do not try to make her answer unless you want to hear some lie from her lips.”","이 말에 불행한 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 도로테아가 더욱 간곡히 거듭 청해도 여전히 침묵을 지켰다. 그러다 마침내, 하인이 나머지 사람들이 복종한다고 한 그 베일 쓴 신사가 다가와 도로테아에게 말했다. \"수고스럽게 저 여인에게 무엇을 베풀려 애쓰지 마십시오, 세뇨라. 누가 무엇을 해 주어도 고마워할 줄 모르는 것이 저 여인의 버릇입니다. 그리고 입에서 거짓말이나 듣고 싶지 않거든 대답을 시키려 들지 마십시오.\""]],[["“I have never told a lie,” was the immediate reply of her who had been silent until now; “on the contrary, it is because I am so truthful and so ignorant of lying devices that I am now in this miserable condition; and this I call you yourself to witness, for it is my unstained truth that has made you false and a liar.”","\"저는 한 번도 거짓을 말한 적이 없습니다.\" 지금껏 침묵하던 여인이 즉각 대답했다. \"오히려 제가 너무도 진실하고 거짓을 꾸미는 술수에 너무도 어두운 탓에 지금 이 비참한 처지에 빠진 것이지요. 이 점은 당신 자신을 증인으로 부르겠습니다. 당신을 거짓되고 거짓말쟁이로 만든 것은 다름 아닌 더럽혀지지 않은 저의 진실이니까요.\""]],[["Cardenio heard these words clearly and distinctly, being quite close to the speaker, for there was only the door of Don Quixote’s room between them, and the instant he did so, uttering a loud exclamation he cried, “Good God!","카르데니오는 그 말을 또렷하고 분명하게 들었으니, 말하는 이와 아주 가까이 있었던 데다 그들 사이에는 돈키호테 방의 문 하나밖에 없었기 때문이다. 그는 그 말을 듣는 순간 큰 소리로 외쳤다."],["what is this I hear?","\"맙소사! 이게 무슨 소리인가?"],["What voice is this that has reached my ears?”","내 귀에 들려온 이 목소리는 대체 누구의 것인가?\""],["Startled at the voice the lady turned her head; and not seeing the speaker she stood up and attempted to enter the room; observing which the gentleman held her back, preventing her from moving a step.","그 목소리에 놀란 부인은 고개를 돌렸으나, 말한 이가 보이지 않자 자리에서 일어나 방으로 들어가려 했다. 이를 본 신사가 그녀를 붙잡아 한 발짝도 움직이지 못하게 막았다."],["In her agitation and sudden movement the silk with which she had covered her face fell off and disclosed a countenance of incomparable and marvellous beauty, but pale and terrified; for she kept turning her eyes, everywhere she could direct her gaze, with an eagerness that made her look as if she had lost her senses, and so marked that it excited the pity of Dorothea and all who beheld her, though they knew not what caused it.","그녀가 동요하여 갑작스레 몸을 움직이는 바람에 얼굴을 가렸던 비단이 흘러내려, 비할 데 없이 놀랍도록 아름다우나 창백하고 겁에 질린 얼굴이 드러났다. 그녀는 눈길이 닿는 곳마다 두 눈을 이리저리 돌렸는데, 그 간절함이 마치 제정신을 잃은 듯 보일 만큼 두드러져, 영문을 알지 못하면서도 도로테아와 그 모습을 본 모든 이의 연민을 자아냈다."],["The gentleman grasped her firmly by the shoulders, and being so fully occupied with holding her back, he was unable to put a hand to his veil which was falling off, as it did at length entirely, and Dorothea, who was holding the lady in her arms, raising her eyes saw that he who likewise held her was her husband, Don Fernando.","신사는 그녀의 양어깨를 단단히 붙들었고, 그녀를 막느라 온통 정신이 팔린 나머지, 흘러내리는 자신의 베일에는 손을 쓸 수가 없어 마침내 그것이 완전히 떨어지고 말았다. 그 부인을 두 팔로 안고 있던 도로테아가 눈을 들어 보니, 마찬가지로 그녀를 붙들고 있는 이가 바로 자기 남편 돈 페르난도였다."],["The instant she recognised him, with a prolonged plaintive cry drawn from the depths of her heart, she fell backwards fainting, and but for the barber being close by to catch her in his arms, she would have fallen completely to the ground.","그를 알아본 순간, 도로테아는 가슴 깊은 곳에서 길게 끌려 나온 애처로운 비명을 지르며 뒤로 넘어가 정신을 잃었으니, 마침 가까이 있던 이발사가 두 팔로 받아 안지 않았더라면 그대로 땅바닥에 쓰러지고 말았을 것이다."],["The curate at once hastened to uncover her face and throw water on it, and as he did so Don Fernando, for he it was who held the other in his arms, recognised her and stood as if death-stricken by the sight; not, however, relaxing his grasp of Luscinda, for it was she that was struggling to release herself from his hold, having recognised Cardenio by his voice, as he had recognised her.","신부가 곧장 달려가 그녀의 얼굴을 드러내고 물을 끼얹었는데, 그가 그리하는 동안 다른 여인을 안고 있던 돈 페르난도가 — 바로 그였다 — 도로테아를 알아보고는, 그 광경에 죽은 사람처럼 굳어 서 있었다. 그러면서도 루신다를 붙든 손은 늦추지 않았으니, 카르데니오가 자신을 알아본 것처럼 그녀 역시 목소리로 카르데니오를 알아보고는 그의 손아귀에서 벗어나려 몸부림치고 있었기 때문이다."],["Cardenio also heard Dorothea’s cry as she fell fainting, and imagining that it came from his Luscinda burst forth in terror from the room, and the first thing he saw was Don Fernando with Luscinda in his arms.","카르데니오도 도로테아가 기절하며 쓰러질 때 지른 비명을 듣고는, 그것이 자기 루신다에게서 나온 것이라 여겨 공포에 사로잡혀 방에서 뛰쳐나왔는데, 가장 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은 루신다를 두 팔로 안고 있는 돈 페르난도였다."],["Don Fernando, too, knew Cardenio at once; and all three, Luscinda, Cardenio, and Dorothea, stood in silent amazement scarcely knowing what had happened to them.","돈 페르난도 역시 카르데니오를 단번에 알아보았다. 그리하여 루신다, 카르데니오, 도로테아 셋은 자기들에게 무슨 일이 일어난 것인지 거의 알지 못한 채 말없이 놀라 서 있었다."]],[["They gazed at one another without speaking, Dorothea at Don Fernando, Don Fernando at Cardenio, Cardenio at Luscinda, and Luscinda at Cardenio. The first to break silence was Luscinda, who thus addressed Don Fernando: “Leave me, Señor Don Fernando, for the sake of what you owe to yourself; if no other reason will induce you, leave me to cling to the wall of which I am the ivy, to the support from which neither your importunities, nor your threats, nor your promises, nor your gifts have been able to detach me. See how Heaven, by ways strange and hidden from our sight, has brought me face to face with my true husband; and well you know by dear-bought experience that death alone will be able to efface him from my memory. May this plain declaration, then, lead you, as you can do nothing else, to turn your love into rage, your affection into resentment, and so to take my life; for if I yield it up in the presence of my beloved husband I count it well bestowed; it may be by my death he will be convinced that I kept my faith to him to the last moment of life.”","그들은 말 한마디 없이 서로를 바라보았으니, 도로테아는 돈 페르난도를, 돈 페르난도는 카르데니오를, 카르데니오는 루신다를, 루신다는 카르데니오를 바라보았다. 침묵을 처음으로 깨뜨린 이는 루신다였으니, 그녀는 돈 페르난도에게 이렇게 말했다. \"저를 놓아주세요, 세뇨르 돈 페르난도, 당신 자신에게 진 도리를 봐서라도요. 다른 어떤 이유로도 마음이 움직이지 않으신다면, 제가 담쟁이덩굴로서 매달려 있는 그 벽에, 당신의 끈질긴 청도, 위협도, 약속도, 선물도 저를 떼어 내지 못한 그 버팀목에 제가 매달리도록 놓아주세요. 보세요, 하늘이 우리 눈에는 낯설고 감추어진 방식으로 저를 제 진정한 남편과 얼굴을 마주하도록 이끌어 주었으니까요. 그리고 죽음만이 그를 제 기억에서 지울 수 있으리라는 것을, 당신은 비싼 값을 치르고 얻은 경험으로 잘 알고 계시지요. 그러니 이 명백한 선언이, 달리 어쩔 도리가 없는 당신을 움직여, 당신의 사랑을 분노로, 당신의 애정을 원한으로 바꾸어 제 목숨을 거두시게 하기를 바랍니다. 제가 사랑하는 남편 앞에서 목숨을 내놓을 수 있다면 그것은 값지게 바쳐진 것이라 여기겠습니다. 제 죽음으로 그가, 제가 생의 마지막 순간까지 그에 대한 신의를 지켰음을 확신하게 될지도 모르니까요.\""]],[["Meanwhile Dorothea had come to herself, and had heard Luscinda’s words, by means of which she divined who she was; but seeing that Don Fernando did not yet release her or reply to her, summoning up her resolution as well as she could she rose and knelt at his feet, and with a flood of bright and touching tears addressed him thus:","그러는 사이 도로테아는 정신을 차려 루신다의 말을 들었고, 그 말로써 그녀가 누구인지 짐작했다. 그러나 돈 페르난도가 여전히 그녀를 놓아주지도, 그녀에게 대답하지도 않는 것을 보고는, 할 수 있는 한 결심을 가다듬어 일어나 그의 발 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 맑고 가슴을 울리는 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 그에게 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“If, my lord, the beams of that sun that thou holdest eclipsed in thine arms did not dazzle and rob thine eyes of sight thou wouldst have seen by this time that she who kneels at thy feet is, so long as thou wilt have it so, the unhappy and unfortunate Dorothea. I am that lowly peasant girl whom thou in thy goodness or for thy pleasure wouldst raise high enough to call herself thine; I am she who in the seclusion of innocence led a contented life until at the voice of thy importunity, and thy true and tender passion, as it seemed, she opened the gates of her modesty and surrendered to thee the keys of her liberty; a gift received by thee but thanklessly, as is clearly shown by my forced retreat to the place where thou dost find me, and by thy appearance under the circumstances in which I see thee. Nevertheless, I would not have thee suppose that I have come here driven by my shame; it is only grief and sorrow at seeing myself forgotten by thee that have led me. It was thy will to make me thine, and thou didst so follow thy will, that now, even though thou repentest, thou canst not help being mine. Bethink thee, my lord, the unsurpassable affection I bear thee may compensate for the beauty and noble birth for which thou wouldst desert me. Thou canst not be the fair Luscinda’s because thou art mine, nor can she be thine because she is Cardenio’s; and it will be easier, remember, to bend thy will to love one who adores thee, than to lead one to love thee who abhors thee now. Thou didst address thyself to my simplicity, thou didst lay siege to my virtue, thou wert not ignorant of my station, well dost thou know how I yielded wholly to thy will; there is no ground or reason for thee to plead deception, and if it be so, as it is, and if thou art a Christian as thou art a gentleman, why dost thou by such subterfuges put off making me as happy at last as thou didst at first? And if thou wilt not have me for what I am, thy true and lawful wife, at least take and accept me as thy slave, for so long as I am thine I will count myself happy and fortunate. Do not by deserting me let my shame become the talk of the gossips in the streets; make not the old age of my parents miserable; for the loyal services they as faithful vassals have ever rendered thine are not deserving of such a return; and if thou thinkest it will debase thy blood to mingle it with mine, reflect that there is little or no nobility in the world that has not travelled the same road, and that in illustrious lineages it is not the woman’s blood that is of account; and, moreover, that true nobility consists in virtue, and if thou art wanting in that, refusing me what in justice thou owest me, then even I have higher claims to nobility than thine. To make an end, señor, these are my last words to thee: whether thou wilt, or wilt not, I am thy wife; witness thy words, which must not and ought not to be false, if thou dost pride thyself on that for want of which thou scornest me; witness the pledge which thou didst give me, and witness Heaven, which thou thyself didst call to witness the promise thou hadst made me; and if all this fail, thy own conscience will not fail to lift up its silent voice in the midst of all thy gaiety, and vindicate the truth of what I say and mar thy highest pleasure and enjoyment.”","\"나리, 그대가 두 팔 안에 가두어 빛을 가린 저 태양의 광채가 그대의 눈을 부시게 하여 시력을 앗아 가지 않았다면, 그대는 이미 그대의 발 앞에 무릎 꿇은 이 여인이, 그대가 그리 원하시는 한 불행하고 가엾은 도로테아임을 알아보셨을 것입니다. 저는 그대가 선의로, 혹은 그대의 즐거움을 위해 그대의 것이라 일컬을 만큼 높이 끌어올리려 하셨던 그 미천한 시골 처녀입니다. 저는 그대의 집요한 청과, 진실하고 다정해 보이던 그 열정의 목소리에 정숙의 문을 열고 그대에게 제 자유의 열쇠를 내어 줄 때까지, 순결의 은둔 속에서 만족스러운 삶을 살던 바로 그 여인입니다. 그대가 받은 그 선물에 아무런 고마움도 표하지 않았음은, 그대가 저를 발견한 이곳으로 제가 떠밀려 와야 했다는 사실로, 또 제가 지금 보는 이 처지에 그대가 나타났다는 사실로 명백히 드러나지요. 그렇지만 제가 수치심에 떠밀려 이곳에 왔다고 여기지는 말아 주십시오. 저를 이끈 것은 오직 그대에게 잊혔다는 것을 보는 데서 오는 비탄과 슬픔뿐입니다. 저를 그대의 것으로 삼는 것이 그대의 뜻이었고, 그대가 그 뜻을 따랐으니, 이제 그대가 후회한다 해도 제 것이 되지 않을 수 없습니다. 생각해 보세요, 나리, 제가 그대에게 품은 더없는 애정이, 그대가 저를 버리고 좇으려는 아름다움과 고귀한 혈통을 보상해 줄 수 있을지도 모릅니다. 그대는 제 것이니 아름다운 루신다의 것이 될 수 없고, 그녀는 카르데니오의 것이니 그대의 것이 될 수 없습니다. 그러니 기억하세요, 지금 그대를 혐오하는 이를 그대를 사랑하도록 이끄는 것보다, 그대를 흠모하는 이를 사랑하도록 그대의 뜻을 굽히는 편이 더 쉬울 것입니다. 그대는 저의 순진함을 노렸고, 저의 정절을 포위했으며, 저의 신분을 모르지 않았고, 제가 그대의 뜻에 온전히 굴복한 것을 잘 알고 계십니다. 그러니 그대가 속았다고 호소할 근거도 이유도 없습니다. 만일 그렇다면, 정말 그러하니, 그대가 신사인 만큼 그리스도교도라면, 어찌하여 그러한 핑계로, 처음에 저를 행복하게 했던 것처럼 끝내 저를 행복하게 해 주는 일을 미루십니까? 그리고 제가 진정하고 적법한 그대의 아내라는 본모습으로 저를 받아들이지 않으시겠다면, 적어도 저를 그대의 노예로라도 받아 주세요. 제가 그대의 것인 한, 저는 스스로를 행복하고 운 좋은 사람으로 여길 테니까요. 저를 버려 제 수치가 거리의 입방아꾼들의 이야깃거리가 되게 하지 마세요. 제 부모님의 노년을 비참하게 만들지 마세요. 충실한 봉신으로서 그분들이 그대의 가문에 늘 바쳐 온 충성스러운 섬김은 그런 보답을 받을 만한 것이 아니니까요. 그리고 그대의 피를 제 피와 섞는 것이 그대의 혈통을 천하게 만든다고 여기신다면, 같은 길을 걷지 않은 고귀한 가문이 세상에 거의 혹은 전혀 없음을, 또 명문가에서 중요한 것은 여자의 피가 아님을 헤아려 주세요. 더욱이 참된 고귀함은 덕에 있으니, 그대가 그 덕을 결여하여 마땅히 제게 갚아야 할 것을 거부한다면, 그렇다면 저야말로 그대보다 고귀함에 대한 더 높은 권리를 지닌 셈입니다. 마지막으로 말씀드립니다, 세뇨르, 이것이 그대에게 드리는 저의 마지막 말입니다. 그대가 원하든 원치 않든, 저는 그대의 아내입니다. 그 결여 때문에 그대가 저를 업신여기는 바로 그것, 곧 그대가 자랑으로 삼는다면 거짓이어서는 안 되고 또 그래서도 안 될 그대의 말이 증인입니다. 그대가 제게 준 맹세가 증인이며, 그대가 제게 한 약속의 증인으로 그대 자신이 불러낸 하늘이 증인입니다. 이 모든 것이 무력하다 해도, 그대 자신의 양심이 그대의 온갖 환락 한가운데서 그 말없는 목소리를 높여, 제가 한 말의 진실을 입증하고 그대의 더없는 쾌락과 향락을 망치는 일만큼은 결코 그치지 않을 것입니다.\""]],[["All this and more the injured Dorothea delivered with such earnest feeling and such tears that all present, even those who came with Don Fernando, were constrained to join her in them.","상처 입은 도로테아는 이 모든 말과 그 이상을 너무도 간절한 심정으로, 너무도 많은 눈물과 함께 토해 내어, 돈 페르난도와 함께 온 자들까지 그 자리에 있던 모든 이가 그녀와 더불어 눈물 흘리지 않을 수 없었다."],["Don Fernando listened to her without replying, until, ceasing to speak, she gave way to such sobs and sighs that it must have been a heart of brass that was not softened by the sight of so great sorrow.","돈 페르난도는 아무 대답도 없이 그녀의 말을 듣고만 있다가, 그녀가 말을 마치고 흐느낌과 한숨을 터뜨리니, 그토록 큰 슬픔의 광경에 누그러지지 않는다면 그것은 청동으로 된 심장이라 할 만했다."],["Luscinda stood regarding her with no less compassion for her sufferings than admiration for her intelligence and beauty, and would have gone to her to say some words of comfort to her, but was prevented by Don Fernando’s grasp which held her fast.","루신다는 그녀의 고통을 향한 연민과 그녀의 지성과 미모를 향한 감탄을 똑같이 품고 그녀를 바라보았으며, 위로의 말이라도 건네려고 다가가려 했으나, 자신을 단단히 붙든 돈 페르난도의 손에 가로막혔다."],["He, overwhelmed with confusion and astonishment, after regarding Dorothea for some moments with a fixed gaze, opened his arms, and, releasing Luscinda, exclaimed:","그는 당혹과 경악에 압도되어 한동안 도로테아를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 두 팔을 벌려 루신다를 놓아주며 외쳤다."]],[["“Thou hast conquered, fair Dorothea, thou hast conquered, for it is impossible to have the heart to deny the united force of so many truths.”","\"그대가 이겼소, 아름다운 도로테아, 그대가 이겼소. 이토록 많은 진실이 한데 모인 힘을 모질게 부정할 마음이란 도무지 가질 수 없으니 말이오.\""]],[["Luscinda in her feebleness was on the point of falling to the ground when Don Fernando released her, but Cardenio, who stood near, having retreated behind Don Fernando to escape recognition, casting fear aside and regardless of what might happen, ran forward to support her, and said as he clasped her in his arms, “If Heaven in its compassion is willing to let thee rest at last, mistress of my heart, true, constant, and fair, nowhere canst thou rest more safely than in these arms that now receive thee, and received thee before when fortune permitted me to call thee mine.”","돈 페르난도가 놓아주자 루신다는 기력이 다해 막 땅에 쓰러지려 했으나, 알아볼까 두려워 돈 페르난도 뒤로 물러나 가까이 서 있던 카르데니오가 두려움을 떨치고 무슨 일이 벌어지든 아랑곳없이 달려 나와 그녀를 부축하고는, 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안으며 말했다. \"하늘이 자비를 베풀어 그대가 마침내 쉬게 하려 하신다면, 내 마음의 주인이여, 진실하고 한결같으며 아름다운 이여, 지금 그대를 받아 안고, 운명이 그대를 내 것이라 부르도록 허락했던 그때에도 그대를 받아 안았던 이 두 팔 안에서보다 더 안전하게 쉴 곳은 어디에도 없으리다.\""]],[["At these words Luscinda looked up at Cardenio, at first beginning to recognise him by his voice and then satisfying herself by her eyes that it was he, and hardly knowing what she did, and heedless of all considerations of decorum, she flung her arms around his neck and pressing her face close to his, said, “Yes, my dear lord, you are the true master of this your slave, even though adverse fate interpose again, and fresh dangers threaten this life that hangs on yours.”","이 말에 루신다는 카르데니오를 올려다보았으니, 처음에는 목소리로 그를 알아보기 시작하다가 이윽고 두 눈으로 그가 틀림없음을 확인하고는, 자기가 무엇을 하는지도 거의 모른 채, 또 예법에 관한 온갖 고려도 아랑곳없이 두 팔을 그의 목에 두르고 얼굴을 그의 얼굴에 바짝 대고 말했다. \"그래요, 나의 사랑하는 주인이여, 비록 모진 운명이 다시 끼어들어 당신의 목숨에 매달린 이 목숨을 새로운 위험들이 위협한다 해도, 당신은 이 노예의 진정한 주인이십니다.\""]],[["A strange sight was this for Don Fernando and those that stood around, filled with surprise at an incident so unlooked for. Dorothea fancied that Don Fernando changed colour and looked as though he meant to take vengeance on Cardenio, for she observed him put his hand to his sword; and the instant the idea struck her, with wonderful quickness she clasped him round the knees, and kissing them and holding him so as to prevent his moving, she said, while her tears continued to flow, “What is it thou wouldst do, my only refuge, in this unforeseen event? Thou hast thy wife at thy feet, and she whom thou wouldst have for thy wife is in the arms of her husband: reflect whether it will be right for thee, whether it will be possible for thee to undo what Heaven has done, or whether it will be becoming in thee to seek to raise her to be thy mate who in spite of every obstacle, and strong in her truth and constancy, is before thine eyes, bathing with the tears of love the face and bosom of her lawful husband. For God’s sake I entreat of thee, for thine own I implore thee, let not this open manifestation rouse thy anger; but rather so calm it as to allow these two lovers to live in peace and quiet without any interference from thee so long as Heaven permits them; and in so doing thou wilt prove the generosity of thy lofty noble spirit, and the world shall see that with thee reason has more influence than passion.”","이는 돈 페르난도와 둘레에 서 있던 이들에게 기이한 광경이었으니, 그토록 뜻밖의 사건에 모두 놀라움으로 가득 찼다. 도로테아는 돈 페르난도의 안색이 변하며 카르데니오에게 복수하려는 듯이 보인다고 여겼으니, 그가 칼에 손을 가져가는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 그 생각이 떠오르는 순간 그녀는 놀라운 민첩함으로 그의 무릎을 끌어안고, 거기에 입을 맞추며 그가 움직이지 못하게 붙들고는, 여전히 눈물을 흘리며 말했다. \"이 뜻밖의 일에 무엇을 하시려는 것입니까, 저의 유일한 피난처여? 그대의 아내가 그대의 발 앞에 있고, 그대가 아내로 삼으려던 이는 자기 남편의 품에 안겨 있습니다. 하늘이 이룬 일을 그대가 되돌리는 것이 옳은 일일지, 아니 그것이 그대에게 가능한 일이기나 한지, 또 온갖 장애에도 불구하고 자신의 진실과 한결같음으로 굳건하여 그대의 눈앞에서 사랑의 눈물로 적법한 남편의 얼굴과 가슴을 적시고 있는 그 여인을 그대의 짝으로 끌어올리려 애쓰는 것이 그대에게 어울리는 일일지 헤아려 보세요. 하느님의 이름으로 그대에게 간청하며, 그대 자신을 위해 그대에게 애원합니다. 이 공공연한 사실의 드러남이 그대의 분노를 일깨우지 않게 하시고, 오히려 그 분노를 가라앉혀, 하늘이 허락하는 한 이 두 연인이 그대의 어떤 간섭도 없이 평화롭고 조용히 살아가도록 해 주세요. 그리하시면 그대는 고귀하고 드높은 정신의 너그러움을 입증하실 것이며, 그대에게는 정념보다 이성이 더 큰 힘을 지녔음을 온 세상이 보게 될 것입니다.\""]],[["All the time Dorothea was speaking, Cardenio, though he held Luscinda in his arms, never took his eyes off Don Fernando, determined, if he saw him make any hostile movement, to try and defend himself and resist as best he could all who might assail him, though it should cost him his life.","도로테아가 말하는 내내, 카르데니오는 루신다를 두 팔에 안고 있으면서도 돈 페르난도에게서 눈을 떼지 않았으니, 그가 적대적인 움직임을 보이기만 하면 제 목숨을 내놓는 한이 있더라도 스스로를 지키려 애쓰고, 자신을 공격할지 모르는 모든 자에게 힘닿는 데까지 맞서리라 작정하고 있었다. 그러나 이제 돈 페르난도의 벗들은 물론, 줄곧 그 자리에 있던 신부와 이발사가, 또 충직한 산초 판사도 잊지 않고 달려 나와 돈 페르난도를 빙 둘러싸고는, 도로테아의 눈물을 헤아려 그녀의 정당한 희망이 좌절되지 않게 해 달라고 간청했으니, 그들이 굳게 믿기로 그녀가 한 말은 다름 아닌 진실이었기 때문이다."],["But now Don Fernando’s friends, as well as the curate and the barber, who had been present all the while, not forgetting the worthy Sancho Panza, ran forward and gathered round Don Fernando, entreating him to have regard for the tears of Dorothea, and not suffer her reasonable hopes to be disappointed, since, as they firmly believed, what she said was but the truth; and bidding him observe that it was not, as it might seem, by accident, but by a special disposition of Providence that they had all met in a place where no one could have expected a meeting.","그리고 그들은 그에게 일러두기를, 이렇게 보일지 모르나 그들이 아무도 만남을 기대할 수 없었던 한 장소에서 다 함께 마주친 것은 우연이 아니라 섭리의 특별한 안배에 의한 것임을 헤아리라고 했다. 또 신부는 그에게 일깨우기를, 오직 죽음만이 루신다를 카르데니오에게서 떼어 놓을 수 있으며, 설령 어떤 칼이 그들을 갈라놓는다 해도 그들은 그 죽음을 더없이 행복하게 여길 것이라 했다."],["And the curate bade him remember that only death could part Luscinda from Cardenio; that even if some sword were to separate them they would think their death most happy; and that in a case that admitted of no remedy his wisest course was, by conquering and putting a constraint upon himself, to show a generous mind, and of his own accord suffer these two to enjoy the happiness Heaven had granted them.","그러니 아무런 처방도 없는 경우에 그가 취할 가장 현명한 길은, 스스로를 이기고 절제함으로써 너그러운 마음을 보여, 자진하여 이 두 사람이 하늘이 베푼 행복을 누리게 하는 것이라 했다. 또 그는 돈 페르난도에게 도로테아의 아름다움에 눈을 돌려 보라 했으니, 그러면 그녀에 견줄 만한 이조차 거의 없거니와 그녀를 넘어설 이는 더더욱 없음을 알게 되리라 했다."],["He bade him, too, turn his eyes upon the beauty of Dorothea and he would see that few if any could equal much less excel her; while to that beauty should be added her modesty and the surpassing love she bore him.","게다가 그 아름다움에 더하여 그녀의 정숙함과 그녀가 그에게 품은 그지없는 사랑까지 헤아려야 한다고 했다. 그러나 이 모든 것에 더하여 신부는 그에게, 그가 신사이자 그리스도교도임을 자랑으로 삼는다면 맹세한 말을 지키는 것 외에 달리 할 도리가 없음을 일깨웠다."],["But besides all this, he reminded him that if he prided himself on being a gentleman and a Christian, he could not do otherwise than keep his plighted word; and that in doing so he would obey God and meet the approval of all sensible people, who know and recognised it to be the privilege of beauty, even in one of humble birth, provided virtue accompany it, to be able to raise itself to the level of any rank, without any slur upon him who places it upon an equality with himself; and furthermore that when the potent sway of passion asserts itself, so long as there be no mixture of sin in it, he is not to be blamed who gives way to it.","그리고 그리함으로써 그는 하느님께 순종하고 분별 있는 모든 이의 인정을 얻게 되리라 했으니, 그들은 미천한 가문의 여인일지라도 덕이 함께한다면 그 아름다움이, 자신과 동등한 자리에 그녀를 두는 이에게 아무런 흠도 되지 않으면서 어떤 신분의 수준에라도 스스로를 끌어올릴 수 있음이 아름다움의 특권임을 알고 또 인정하는 사람들이었다. 더 나아가 그는, 정념의 강력한 힘이 작용할 때, 거기에 죄가 섞여 있지 않은 한 그에 굴복하는 자는 비난받을 일이 아니라고 했다."]],[["To be brief, they added to these such other forcible arguments that Don Fernando’s manly heart, being after all nourished by noble blood, was touched, and yielded to the truth which, even had he wished it, he could not gainsay; and he showed his submission, and acceptance of the good advice that had been offered to him, by stooping down and embracing Dorothea, saying to her, “Rise, dear lady, it is not right that what I hold in my heart should be kneeling at my feet; and if until now I have shown no sign of what I own, it may have been by Heaven’s decree in order that, seeing the constancy with which you love me, I may learn to value you as you deserve. What I entreat of you is that you reproach me not with my transgression and grievous wrong-doing; for the same cause and force that drove me to make you mine impelled me to struggle against being yours; and to prove this, turn and look at the eyes of the now happy Luscinda, and you will see in them an excuse for all my errors: and as she has found and gained the object of her desires, and I have found in you what satisfies all my wishes, may she live in peace and contentment as many happy years with her Cardenio, as on my knees I pray Heaven to allow me to live with my Dorothea;” and with these words he once more embraced her and pressed his face to hers with so much tenderness that he had to take great heed to keep his tears from completing the proof of his love and repentance in the sight of all. Not so Luscinda, and Cardenio, and almost all the others, for they shed so many tears, some in their own happiness, some at that of the others, that one would have supposed a heavy calamity had fallen upon them all. Even Sancho Panza was weeping; though afterwards he said he only wept because he saw that Dorothea was not as he fancied the queen Micomicona, of whom he expected such great favours. Their wonder as well as their weeping lasted some time, and then Cardenio and Luscinda went and fell on their knees before Don Fernando, returning him thanks for the favour he had rendered them in language so grateful that he knew not how to answer them, and raising them up embraced them with every mark of affection and courtesy.","요컨대 그들은 이런 말에 다른 강력한 논거들까지 보태었고, 결국 고귀한 피로 길러진 돈 페르난도의 사내다운 마음은 감동하여, 설령 원했다 해도 거스를 수 없었을 진실에 굴복했다. 그는 몸을 굽혀 도로테아를 끌어안음으로써, 자신에게 베풀어진 좋은 충고를 받아들이고 굴복했음을 보이며 그녀에게 말했다. \"일어나시오, 친애하는 부인. 내가 가슴에 품은 이가 내 발 앞에 무릎 꿇고 있는 것은 옳지 않소. 그리고 지금까지 내가 마음에 품은 것을 아무런 내색도 하지 않았다면, 그것은 어쩌면 하늘의 뜻이었을지도 모르오. 그대가 나를 사랑하는 그 한결같음을 보고, 그대가 받아 마땅한 만큼 그대를 귀히 여기는 법을 내가 배우도록 말이오. 내가 그대에게 간청하는 것은, 내 잘못과 모진 악행을 두고 나를 책망하지 말아 달라는 것이오. 나로 하여금 그대를 내 것으로 삼게 한 바로 그 까닭과 힘이, 그대의 것이 되지 않으려 발버둥치게 만들었던 것이니 말이오. 이를 입증하려거든, 돌아서서 이제 행복해진 루신다의 눈을 보시오. 그러면 그 눈 속에서 내 모든 잘못에 대한 변명을 보게 될 것이오. 그녀가 자신이 바라던 바를 찾아 얻었고, 나 또한 그대에게서 내 모든 소원을 채워 줄 것을 찾았으니, 그녀가 자기 카르데니오와 더불어 평화롭고 만족스럽게 오래도록 행복한 세월을 살기를 바라며, 나도 내 도로테아와 더불어 살도록 하늘이 허락해 주기를 무릎 꿇고 비는 바이오.\" 이렇게 말하고 그는 다시 한번 그녀를 끌어안고는 너무도 다정하게 자기 얼굴을 그녀의 얼굴에 댔으니, 모두가 보는 앞에서 눈물이 그의 사랑과 뉘우침의 증거를 마저 완성해 버리지 않도록 무던히도 조심해야 했다. 루신다와 카르데니오, 그리고 나머지 거의 모든 사람은 그렇지 않았으니, 어떤 이는 제 행복에, 어떤 이는 남의 행복에 어찌나 많은 눈물을 흘렸던지, 그들 모두에게 무거운 재앙이 닥쳤다고 여겨질 정도였다. 산초 판사조차 울고 있었다. 다만 그는 나중에 말하기를, 도로테아가 자기가 생각하던 미코미코나 여왕이 아니라는 것을 알았기 때문에 운 것일 뿐이라 했으니, 그는 그 여왕에게서 그토록 큰 은혜를 기대하고 있었던 것이다. 그들의 놀라움도, 눈물도 한동안 이어지다가, 이윽고 카르데니오와 루신다가 돈 페르난도 앞에 무릎 꿇고, 그가 베풀어 준 은혜에 너무도 감사에 찬 말로 사례하니, 그는 무어라 대답해야 할지 알지 못한 채 그들을 일으켜 세워 온갖 애정과 예의의 표시로 끌어안았다."]],[["He then asked Dorothea how she had managed to reach a place so far removed from her own home, and she in a few fitting words told all that she had previously related to Cardenio, with which Don Fernando and his companions were so delighted that they wished the story had been longer; so charmingly did Dorothea describe her misadventures.","그러고 나서 그는 도로테아에게, 어떻게 자기 집에서 그토록 멀리 떨어진 곳까지 올 수 있었는지 물었고, 그녀는 앞서 카르데니오에게 들려준 그 모든 이야기를 몇 마디 알맞은 말로 전했다. 그 이야기에 돈 페르난도와 그의 동행들은 어찌나 즐거워했던지 이야기가 더 길었더라면 좋았겠다고 바랄 정도였으니, 그만큼 도로테아는 자신의 불운한 일들을 매혹적으로 풀어놓았다."],["When she had finished Don Fernando recounted what had befallen him in the city after he had found in Luscinda’s bosom the paper in which she declared that she was Cardenio’s wife, and never could be his.","그녀가 이야기를 마치자 돈 페르난도는, 루신다의 가슴속에서 그녀가 카르데니오의 아내이며 결코 자신의 아내가 될 수 없다고 선언한 그 종이를 발견한 뒤 도시에서 자신에게 일어난 일을 이야기했다. 그는 그녀를 죽이려 했고, 그녀의 부모가 막지 않았더라면 그리했을 것이며, 분노와 수치심에 가득 차 그 집을 떠나 더 알맞은 기회가 오면 복수하리라 마음먹었다고 했다."],["He said he meant to kill her, and would have done so had he not been prevented by her parents, and that he quitted the house full of rage and shame, and resolved to avenge himself when a more convenient opportunity should offer.","이튿날 그는 루신다가 아버지의 집에서 사라졌고 아무도 그녀가 어디로 갔는지 모른다는 것을 알게 되었다. 마침내 몇 달이 지난 끝에 그는, 그녀가 한 수녀원에 있으며 카르데니오와 함께 살 수 없다면 평생 그곳에 머무를 작정이라는 것을 알아냈다."],["The next day he learned that Luscinda had disappeared from her father’s house, and that no one could tell whither she had gone.","그리고 이를 알자마자 그는 이 세 신사를 동행으로 삼아 그녀가 있는 곳에 다다랐으나, 자신이 거기 있음이 알려지면 수녀원에서 더 엄중한 경계를 펼칠까 두려워 그녀에게 말을 거는 것은 피했다."],["Finally, at the end of some months he ascertained that she was in a convent and meant to remain there all the rest of her life, if she were not to share it with Cardenio; and as soon as he had learned this, taking these three gentlemen as his companions, he arrived at the place where she was, but avoided speaking to her, fearing that if it were known he was there stricter precautions would be taken in the convent; and watching a time when the porter’s lodge was open he left two to guard the gate, and he and the other entered the convent in quest of Luscinda, whom they found in the cloisters in conversation with one of the nuns, and carrying her off without giving her time to resist, they reached a place with her where they provided themselves with what they required for taking her away; all which they were able to do in complete safety, as the convent was in the country at a considerable distance from the city.","그러다 문지기의 방이 열려 있는 때를 노려 둘을 남겨 문을 지키게 하고, 자신과 나머지 한 사람이 루신다를 찾아 수녀원으로 들어갔으니, 그녀가 수녀 한 사람과 회랑에서 이야기를 나누고 있는 것을 발견하여, 저항할 틈도 주지 않고 그녀를 데리고 나와, 그녀를 데려가는 데 필요한 것들을 갖춘 한 장소에 이르렀다고 했다. 이 모든 일을 그들이 완벽히 안전하게 해낼 수 있었던 것은, 그 수녀원이 도시에서 상당히 떨어진 시골에 있었기 때문이다."],["He added that when Luscinda found herself in his power she lost all consciousness, and after returning to herself did nothing but weep and sigh without speaking a word; and thus in silence and tears they reached that inn, which for him was reaching heaven where all the mischances of earth are over and at an end.","그는 덧붙이기를, 루신다가 자신이 그의 손아귀에 들어왔음을 알았을 때 온통 정신을 잃었고, 정신을 차린 뒤로는 한마디 말도 없이 울고 한숨짓는 일밖에 하지 않았다고 했다. 그리하여 그들은 침묵과 눈물 속에서 그 객줏집에 다다랐으니, 그에게 그곳은 땅 위의 온갖 불행이 다 끝나고 마는 천국에 다다른 것이나 마찬가지였다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXXVII. IN WHICH IS CONTINUED THE STORY OF THE FAMOUS PRINCESS MICOMICONA, WITH OTHER DROLL ADVENTURES","# CHAPTER XXXVII."]],[["To all this Sancho listened with no little sorrow at heart to see how his hopes of dignity were fading away and vanishing in smoke, and how the fair Princess Micomicona had turned into Dorothea, and the giant into Don Fernando, while his master was sleeping tranquilly, totally unconscious of all that had come to pass.","이 모든 것을 산초는 적잖이 가슴 아파하며 듣고 있었으니, 자신의 영화로운 신분에 대한 희망이 연기처럼 스러져 사라지는 것을, 그리고 아름다운 미코미코나 공주가 도로테아로 변하고 거인이 돈 페르난도로 변해 버린 것을 지켜보았기 때문이다. 한편 그의 주인은 그동안 벌어진 모든 일을 까맣게 모른 채 평온히 잠들어 있었다."],["Dorothea was unable to persuade herself that her present happiness was not all a dream; Cardenio was in a similar state of mind, and Luscinda’s thoughts ran in the same direction.","도로테아는 지금의 이 행복이 한낱 꿈이 아니라는 사실을 스스로도 믿을 수가 없었고, 카르데니오 역시 비슷한 심정이었으며, 루신다의 생각도 같은 곳을 향해 달려가고 있었다."],["Don Fernando gave thanks to Heaven for the favour shown to him and for having been rescued from the intricate labyrinth in which he had been brought so near the destruction of his good name and of his soul; and in short everybody in the inn was full of contentment and satisfaction at the happy issue of such a complicated and hopeless business.","돈 페르난도는 자신에게 베풀어진 은총에 대해, 그리고 자신의 명예와 영혼을 파멸 직전까지 몰아넣었던 그 얽히고설킨 미궁에서 구원받은 것에 대해 하늘에 감사를 드렸다. 요컨대 객줏집에 있던 모든 이가 이토록 복잡하고 가망 없던 일이 행복한 결말에 이른 것에 만족과 흡족함으로 가득 차 있었다."],["The curate as a sensible man made sound reflections upon the whole affair, and congratulated each upon his good fortune; but the one that was in the highest spirits and good humour was the landlady, because of the promise Cardenio and the curate had given her to pay for all the losses and damage she had sustained through Don Quixote’s means.","분별 있는 사람인 신부는 이 모든 사태에 대해 건전한 성찰을 펼치며 저마다의 행운을 축하해 주었다. 그러나 가장 들뜨고 기분 좋아한 이는 객줏집 안주인이었으니, 돈키호테 탓에 입은 손실과 손해를 모두 갚아 주겠노라 카르데니오와 신부가 약속해 준 까닭이었다."],["Sancho, as has been already said, was the only one who was distressed, unhappy, and dejected; and so with a long face he went in to his master, who had just awoke, and said to him:","앞서 말했듯이 괴로워하고 불행해하며 풀이 죽은 이는 오직 산초뿐이었다. 그리하여 그는 침울한 얼굴로, 막 잠에서 깨어난 주인에게 들어가 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“Sir Rueful Countenance, your worship may as well sleep on as much as you like, without troubling yourself about killing any giant or restoring her kingdom to the princess; for that is all over and settled now.”","\"슬픈 몰골의 기사 나리, 나리께서야 마음껏 더 주무셔도 되겠습니다요. 거인을 죽이느니 공주님께 왕국을 되찾아 주느니 하는 일로 더는 골치 썩이실 것 없으니까요. 그건 다 끝났고 죄다 정리됐거든요.\""]],[["“I should think it was,” replied Don Quixote, “for I have had the most prodigious and stupendous battle with the giant that I ever remember having had all the days of my life; and with one back-stroke-swish!--I brought his head tumbling to the ground, and so much blood gushed forth from him that it ran in rivulets over the earth like water.”","\"나도 그렇게 여기노라.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내 평생 기억하는 한 가장 어마어마하고 엄청난 싸움을 그 거인과 벌였느니라. 단번에 내리치는 한 칼에 — 휙! — 그 머리를 땅바닥에 굴러떨어지게 했으니, 그자에게서 어찌나 피가 솟구쳐 나왔던지 물처럼 땅 위를 개울 이루며 흘렀느니라.\""]],[["“Like red wine, your worship had better say,” replied Sancho; “for I would have you know, if you don’t know it, that the dead giant is a hacked wine-skin, and the blood four-and-twenty gallons of red wine that it had in its belly, and the cut-off head is the bitch that bore me; and the devil take it all.”","\"붉은 포도주처럼이라고 하시는 게 나을 겁니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"혹시 모르신다면 일러 드리지요. 그 죽은 거인이라는 건 칼질당한 포도주 부대였고, 그 피라는 건 부대 속에 들었던 붉은 포도주 스물네 갤런이었습니다요. 게다가 잘려 나간 머리란 건 저를 낳아 준 어미 암캐였으니, 그 모든 게 악마한테나 가 버리라죠.\""]],[["“What art thou talking about, fool?”","\"무슨 소리를 지껄이는 게냐, 이 멍청아?\""],["said Don Quixote; “art thou in thy senses?”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"네 제정신이냐?\""]],[["“Let your worship get up,”","\"나리, 일어나 보시지요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “and you will see the nice business you have made of it, and what we have to pay; and you will see the queen turned into a private lady called Dorothea, and other things that will astonish you, if you understand them.”","\"그러면 나리께서 무슨 멋들어진 일을 저질러 놓으셨는지, 또 우리가 뭘 물어 줘야 하는지 보실 수 있을 겁니다요. 그러면 여왕이 도로테아라는 평범한 귀부인으로 변한 것도 보실 테고, 알아들으시기만 한다면 깜짝 놀라실 다른 일들도 보시게 될 겁니다요.\""]],[["“I shall not be surprised at anything of the kind,”","\"나는 그 따위 일에는 전혀 놀라지 않을 것이니라.\" 돈키호테가 대꾸했다."],["returned Don Quixote; “for if thou dost remember the last time we were here I told thee that everything that happened here was a matter of enchantment, and it would be no wonder if it were the same now.”","\"우리가 지난번에 여기 왔을 때 내가 네게 말하지 않았더냐, 여기서 일어나는 모든 일은 마법의 소관이라고. 그러니 지금도 똑같다 한들 조금도 이상할 게 없느니라.\""]],[["“I could believe all that,” replied Sancho, “if my blanketing was the same sort of thing also; only it wasn’t, but real and genuine; for I saw the landlord, who is here to-day, holding one end of the blanket and jerking me up to the skies very neatly and smartly, and with as much laughter as strength; and when it comes to be a case of knowing people, I hold for my part, simple and sinner as I am, that there is no enchantment about it at all, but a great deal of bruising and bad luck.”","\"저도 그 모든 걸 믿을 수 있겠습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제가 담요 키질당한 것도 그런 종류의 일이었다면 말입죠. 하지만 그건 그렇지가 않았고, 진짜배기에 틀림없는 일이었습니다요. 오늘도 여기 있는 객줏집 주인이 담요 한쪽 끝을 붙잡고서 저를 아주 깔끔하고 날렵하게, 힘쓰는 만큼이나 웃어 가며 하늘로 휙휙 차올리는 걸 제 눈으로 봤거든요. 그리고 사람을 알아본다는 점에 대해서 말씀드리자면, 저야 단순하고 죄 많은 인간입니다만, 거기엔 마법이라곤 전혀 없고 오로지 멍투성이에다 지독한 불운만 잔뜩 있었다는 게 제 생각입니다요.\""]],[["“Well, well, God will give a remedy,” said Don Quixote; “hand me my clothes and let me go out, for I want to see these transformations and things thou speakest of.”","\"좋다, 좋아. 하느님께서 해결책을 주시리라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내 옷을 가져오너라, 그리고 나가게 해 다오. 네가 말하는 그 변신이니 뭐니 하는 것들을 보고 싶으니라.\""]],[["Sancho fetched him his clothes; and while he was dressing, the curate gave Don Fernando and the others present an account of Don Quixote’s madness and of the stratagem they had made use of to withdraw him from that Pena Pobre where he fancied himself stationed because of his lady’s scorn.","산초는 그에게 옷을 가져다주었다. 그가 옷을 입는 동안 신부는 돈 페르난도와 그 자리에 있던 다른 이들에게 돈키호테의 광기에 대해, 그리고 그가 자기 귀부인의 멸시 때문에 머물러 있노라 여겼던 그 페냐 포브레에서 그를 빼내려고 자기들이 썼던 계략에 대해 이야기해 주었다."],["He described to them also nearly all the adventures that Sancho had mentioned, at which they marvelled and laughed not a little, thinking it, as all did, the strangest form of madness a crazy intellect could be capable of.","그는 또한 산초가 언급했던 거의 모든 모험을 그들에게 들려주었고, 그들은 그 이야기에 적잖이 놀라며 웃어 댔으니, 정신 나간 자의 머리가 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 기이한 형태의 광기라고 모두가 입을 모아 여겼다."],["But now, the curate said, that the lady Dorothea’s good fortune prevented her from proceeding with their purpose, it would be necessary to devise or discover some other way of getting him home.","그러나 이제, 신부가 말하기를, 도로테아 부인의 행운으로 인해 본래의 계획을 더 진행할 수 없게 되었으니, 그를 집으로 데려갈 다른 방도를 궁리하거나 찾아내는 것이 필요하리라고 했다."]],[["Cardenio proposed to carry out the scheme they had begun, and suggested that Luscinda would act and support Dorothea’s part sufficiently well.","카르데니오는 자기들이 시작했던 계획을 그대로 밀고 나가자고 제안하면서, 루신다가 도로테아의 배역을 충분히 잘 연기하고 떠받쳐 줄 거라고 의견을 냈다."]],[["“No,” said Don Fernando, “that must not be, for I want Dorothea to follow out this idea of hers; and if the worthy gentleman’s village is not very far off, I shall be happy if I can do anything for his relief.”","\"아니오.\" 돈 페르난도가 말했다. \"그건 안 될 일이오. 나는 도로테아가 자기의 이 생각을 끝까지 이어 가기를 바라니까요. 그리고 이 훌륭하신 양반의 마을이 그리 멀지 않다면, 그분을 구하기 위해 내가 뭔가 할 수 있다면 기쁘겠소이다.\""]],[["“It is not more than two days’ journey from this,”","\"여기서 이틀 길밖에 안 됩니다.\""],["said the curate.","신부가 말했다."]],[["“Even if it were more,”","\"설령 더 멀다 해도,\""],["said Don Fernando, “I would gladly travel so far for the sake of doing so good a work.”","돈 페르난도가 말했다. \"이렇게 좋은 일을 위해서라면 그만한 거리야 기꺼이 가겠소이다.\""]],[["At this moment Don Quixote came out in full panoply, with Mambrino’s helmet, all dinted as it was, on his head, his buckler on his arm, and leaning on his staff or pike.","바로 그 순간 돈키호테가 완전 무장을 갖추고 나왔으니, 온통 우그러진 그대로의 맘브리노 투구를 머리에 쓰고, 둥근 방패를 팔에 끼고, 자기 지팡이인지 창인지에 몸을 기댄 채였다."],["The strange figure he presented filled Don Fernando and the rest with amazement as they contemplated his lean yellow face half a league long, his armour of all sorts, and the solemnity of his deportment.","그가 드러낸 기이한 형상은 돈 페르난도와 나머지 사람들을 경탄으로 가득 채웠으니, 반 레구아는 됨 직하게 길쭉한 그의 비쩍 마른 누런 얼굴과, 온갖 종류가 뒤섞인 갑주와, 그 거동의 엄숙함을 바라보면서였다."],["They stood silent waiting to see what he would say, and he, fixing his eyes on the fair Dorothea, addressed her with great gravity and composure:","그들은 그가 무슨 말을 할지 보려고 잠자코 기다렸고, 그는 아름다운 도로테아에게 두 눈을 고정한 채 더없이 무게 있고 침착한 태도로 그녀에게 말을 건넸다."]],[["“I am informed, fair lady, by my squire here that your greatness has been annihilated and your being abolished, since, from a queen and lady of high degree as you used to be, you have been turned into a private maiden. If this has been done by the command of the magician king your father, through fear that I should not afford you the aid you need and are entitled to, I may tell you he did not know and does not know half the mass, and was little versed in the annals of chivalry; for, if he had read and gone through them as attentively and deliberately as I have, he would have found at every turn that knights of less renown than mine have accomplished things more difficult: it is no great matter to kill a whelp of a giant, however arrogant he may be; for it is not many hours since I myself was engaged with one, and-I will not speak of it, that they may not say I am lying; time, however, that reveals all, will tell the tale when we least expect it.”","\"아름다운 부인이여, 여기 있는 제 종자에게서 듣자 하니, 부인의 위대함이 산산이 무너지고 부인의 존재가 폐기되었다 하더이다. 한때 그러하셨던 여왕이자 고귀한 신분의 귀부인에서, 평범한 처녀로 변하셨다 하니 말이외다. 만일 이것이 마법사인 부왕의 명령으로 이루어진 일이라면, 제가 부인께 마땅히 드려야 할, 또 부인께서 받으실 자격이 있는 도움을 드리지 못할까 두려워서 그리하신 것이라면, 한 말씀 올리건대 그분께서는 미사의 절반도 알지 못하셨고 지금도 알지 못하시며, 기사도의 연대기에 거의 통달하지 못하셨던 것이외다. 그분께서 만일 제가 한 만큼 주의 깊고 신중하게 그것들을 읽고 통독하셨더라면, 저보다 명성이 덜한 기사들도 더 어려운 일들을 이루어 냈음을 곳곳에서 발견하셨을 터이니 말이외다. 거인 새끼 한 마리쯤 죽이는 일은, 그자가 아무리 오만방자할지언정 대단한 일이 아니외다. 저 자신이 한 거인과 맞붙은 지가 채 몇 시간도 지나지 않았으니 말이외다. 한데 — 그 이야기는 하지 않으리다. 제가 거짓말을 한다고들 할까 싶어서이외다. 허나 모든 것을 드러내는 시간이, 우리가 가장 뜻하지 않은 때에 그 사연을 일러 줄 것이외다.\""]],[["“You were engaged with a couple of wine-skins, and not a giant,” said the landlord at this; but Don Fernando told him to hold his tongue and on no account interrupt Don Quixote, who continued, “I say in conclusion, high and disinherited lady, that if your father has brought about this metamorphosis in your person for the reason I have mentioned, you ought not to attach any importance to it; for there is no peril on earth through which my sword will not force a way, and with it, before many days are over, I will bring your enemy’s head to the ground and place on yours the crown of your kingdom.”","\"당신이 맞붙은 건 포도주 부대 두 짝이지 거인이 아니었소.\" 이때 객줏집 주인이 말했다. 그러나 돈 페르난도가 그에게 입을 다물라고, 무슨 일이 있어도 돈키호테의 말을 끊지 말라고 일렀고, 돈키호테는 말을 이었다. \"결론으로 말씀드리건대, 고귀하시되 상속권을 빼앗기신 부인이여, 만일 부왕께서 제가 말씀드린 그 이유로 부인의 몸에 이런 변신을 일으키신 것이라면, 부인께서는 그것에 조금도 의미를 두지 마셔야 하외다. 이 땅 위에 제 검이 길을 뚫지 못할 위험이란 없으며, 그 검으로써 며칠 지나지 않아 부인의 적의 머리를 땅바닥에 굴러떨어지게 하고 부인의 머리 위에 왕국의 왕관을 씌워 드리겠나이다.\""]],[["Don Quixote said no more, and waited for the reply of the princess, who aware of Don Fernando’s determination to carry on the deception until Don Quixote had been conveyed to his home, with great ease of manner and gravity made answer, “Whoever told you, valiant Knight of the Rueful Countenance, that I had undergone any change or transformation did not tell you the truth, for I am the same as I was yesterday. It is true that certain strokes of good fortune, that have given me more than I could have hoped for, have made some alteration in me; but I have not therefore ceased to be what I was before, or to entertain the same desire I have had all through of availing myself of the might of your valiant and invincible arm. And so, señor, let your goodness reinstate the father that begot me in your good opinion, and be assured that he was a wise and prudent man, since by his craft he found out such a sure and easy way of remedying my misfortune; for I believe, señor, that had it not been for you I should never have lit upon the good fortune I now possess; and in this I am saying what is perfectly true; as most of these gentlemen who are present can fully testify. All that remains is to set out on our journey to-morrow, for to-day we could not make much way; and for the rest of the happy result I am looking forward to, I trust to God and the valour of your heart.”","돈키호테는 더 말하지 않고 공주의 대답을 기다렸다. 공주는 돈키호테가 집으로 옮겨질 때까지 이 속임수를 이어 가려는 돈 페르난도의 결심을 알아챘기에, 더없이 편안한 태도와 무게 있는 자세로 대답했다. \"용감하신 슬픈 몰골의 기사님, 제가 무슨 변화나 변신을 겪었다고 말씀드린 자가 누구든 간에 그자는 기사님께 진실을 말씀드린 것이 아닙니다. 저는 어제와 똑같은 사람이니까요. 제가 바랄 수 있었던 것 이상을 안겨 준 행운의 어떤 손길들이 제게 약간의 변화를 일으킨 것은 사실입니다. 허나 그렇다고 제가 이전의 저이기를 그만둔 것도 아니며, 기사님의 용맹하고 무적인 팔의 힘을 빌리고자 줄곧 품어 온 그 한결같은 소망을 거둔 것도 아닙니다. 그러니 기사님, 부디 너그러우심으로 저를 낳아 주신 아버지를 다시 좋게 보아 주시고, 그분이 현명하고 신중한 분이셨음을 믿어 주십시오. 그분의 술책으로 제 불행을 바로잡을 그토록 확실하고 손쉬운 방도를 찾아내신 것이니까요. 기사님, 저는 믿습니다. 기사님이 아니셨던들 지금 제가 누리는 이 행운에 결코 다다르지 못했으리라고 말입니다. 이 점에서 저는 완벽한 진실을 말씀드리고 있으며, 여기 계신 이분들 대부분이 충분히 증언해 주실 수 있을 것입니다. 이제 남은 일이라곤 내일 우리의 여정을 떠나는 것뿐입니다. 오늘은 멀리 갈 수 없을 테니까요. 그리고 제가 고대하는 그 행복한 결말의 나머지에 대해서는, 하느님과 기사님 마음속의 용맹을 믿겠습니다.\""]],[["So said the sprightly Dorothea, and on hearing her Don Quixote turned to Sancho, and said to him, with an angry air, “I declare now, little Sancho, thou art the greatest little villain in Spain.","발랄한 도로테아가 이렇게 말하자, 그 말을 들은 돈키호테는 산초를 돌아보며 노여운 기색으로 말했다. \"꼬마 산초야, 내 이제 단언하노니, 너는 스페인에서 가장 큰 꼬마 악당이로다."],["Say, thief and vagabond, hast thou not just now told me that this princess had been turned into a maiden called Dorothea, and that the head which I am persuaded I cut off from a giant was the bitch that bore thee, and other nonsense that put me in the greatest perplexity I have ever been in all my life?","말해 보아라, 이 도둑놈에 떠돌이 부랑자야, 방금 네놈이 내게 이 공주가 도로테아라는 처녀로 변했다고, 그리고 내가 거인에게서 베어 냈다고 굳게 믿고 있는 그 머리가 너를 낳은 어미 암캐였다고, 그밖에 내 평생 가장 큰 혼란에 빠뜨릴 온갖 헛소리를 지껄이지 않았더냐? 내 맹세하노니\""],["I vow” (and here he looked to heaven and ground his teeth) “I have a mind to play the mischief with thee, in a way that will teach sense for the future to all lying squires of knights-errant in the world.”","(여기서 그는 하늘을 우러러보며 이를 갈았다) \"내 너를 흠씬 혼내 줄 마음이 있으니, 그리하여 앞으로 이 세상 편력기사들의 거짓말쟁이 종자들 모두에게 분별을 가르쳐 줄 작정이로다.\""]],[["“Let your worship be calm, señor,” returned Sancho, “for it may well be that I have been mistaken as to the change of the lady princess Micomicona; but as to the giant’s head, or at least as to the piercing of the wine-skins, and the blood being red wine, I make no mistake, as sure as there is a God; because the wounded skins are there at the head of your worship’s bed, and the wine has made a lake of the room; if not you will see when the eggs come to be fried; I mean when his worship the landlord calls for all the damages: for the rest, I am heartily glad that her ladyship the queen is as she was, for it concerns me as much as anyone.”","\"나리, 부디 진정하십시오.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"제가 미코미코나 공주 마님의 변화에 대해서는 착각했을 수도 있습니다요. 허나 거인의 머리에 대해서는, 아니 적어도 포도주 부대를 꿰뚫은 일이며 그 피가 붉은 포도주였던 일에 대해서는 하느님이 계신 것만큼이나 확실하게 저는 착각하지 않았습니다요. 그 찢어진 부대들이 바로 나리 침대 머리맡에 있고, 포도주가 방 안을 호수로 만들어 놨거든요. 못 믿으시겠거든 달걀이 부쳐질 때 보시면 됩니다요. 그러니까 객줏집 주인 나리께서 손해 배상을 죄다 청구하실 때 말입죠. 그밖의 일에 대해서는, 여왕 마님께서 본래 그대로이신 게 진심으로 기쁩니다요. 그게 누구 못지않게 저한테도 상관있는 일이거든요.\""]],[["“I tell thee again, Sancho, thou art a fool,”","\"다시 말하노니 산초야, 너는 멍청이로다.\""],["said Don Quixote; “forgive me, and that will do.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"나를 용서해 다오, 그러면 됐느니라.\""]],[["“That will do,” said Don Fernando; “let us say no more about it; and as her ladyship the princess proposes to set out to-morrow because it is too late to-day, so be it, and we will pass the night in pleasant conversation, and to-morrow we will all accompany Señor Don Quixote; for we wish to witness the valiant and unparalleled achievements he is about to perform in the course of this mighty enterprise which he has undertaken.”","\"그러면 됐습니다.\" 돈 페르난도가 말했다. \"그 일에 대해서는 더 말하지 맙시다. 그리고 공주 마님께서 오늘은 너무 늦었으니 내일 출발하기로 제안하셨으니, 그렇게 합시다. 우리는 즐거운 대화로 밤을 지새우고, 내일 우리 모두 돈키호테 님을 모시고 가겠소이다. 그분께서 떠맡으신 이 위대한 과업을 펼치시는 동안 행하실 그 용감하고 비할 데 없는 위업을 우리가 직접 보고 싶으니 말이외다.\""]],[["“It is I who shall wait upon and accompany you,”","\"여러분을 받들어 모시고 동행할 사람은 바로 저이외다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “and I am much gratified by the favour that is bestowed upon me, and the good opinion entertained of me, which I shall strive to justify or it shall cost me my life, or even more, if it can possibly cost me more.”","\"제게 베풀어진 호의와, 제게 품어 주신 좋은 생각에 깊이 감사드리며, 그것에 부응하고자 애쓰겠나이다. 그러지 못한다면 제 목숨을 내놓겠으며, 목숨보다 더한 것을 내놓을 수 있다면 그것마저 내놓겠나이다.\""]],[["Many were the compliments and expressions of politeness that passed between Don Quixote and Don Fernando; but they were brought to an end by a traveller who at this moment entered the inn, and who seemed from his attire to be a Christian lately come from the country of the Moors, for he was dressed in a short-skirted coat of blue cloth with half-sleeves and without a collar; his breeches were also of blue cloth, and his cap of the same colour, and he wore yellow buskins and had a Moorish cutlass slung from a baldric across his breast. Behind him, mounted upon an ass, there came a woman dressed in Moorish fashion, with her face veiled and a scarf on her head, and wearing a little brocaded cap, and a mantle that covered her from her shoulders to her feet. The man was of a robust and well-proportioned frame, in age a little over forty, rather swarthy in complexion, with long moustaches and a full beard, and, in short, his appearance was such that if he had been well dressed he would have been taken for a person of quality and good birth. On entering he asked for a room, and when they told him there was none in the inn he seemed distressed, and approaching her who by her dress seemed to be a Moor he her down from saddle in his arms. Luscinda, Dorothea, the landlady, her daughter and Maritornes, attracted by the strange, and to them entirely new costume, gathered round her; and Dorothea, who was always kindly, courteous, and quick-witted, perceiving that both she and the man who had brought her were annoyed at not finding a room, said to her, “Do not be put out, señora, by the discomfort and want of luxuries here, for it is the way of road-side inns to be without them; still, if you will be pleased to share our lodging with us (pointing to Luscinda) perhaps you will have found worse accommodation in the course of your journey.”","돈키호테와 돈 페르난도 사이에 오간 찬사와 정중한 인사말은 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 많았다. 그러나 그것들은 바로 그 순간 객줏집으로 들어선 한 나그네로 인해 끝나고 말았으니, 그는 차림새로 보아 무어인들의 나라에서 막 건너온 기독교도인 듯했다. 그는 반소매에 깃이 없는, 자락이 짧은 푸른 천 외투를 걸치고 있었고, 바지 역시 푸른 천이었으며, 모자도 같은 빛깔이었다. 발에는 노란 반장화를 신고 있었고, 가슴을 가로지른 어깨띠에 무어인의 언월도를 매달고 있었다. 그의 뒤로는 한 여인이 당나귀에 올라타 따라왔는데, 무어식 차림에 얼굴은 베일로 가리고 머리에는 스카프를 둘렀으며, 작은 비단 모자를 쓰고 어깨에서 발까지 덮는 망토를 두르고 있었다. 그 남자는 다부지고 균형 잡힌 체격이었고, 나이는 마흔을 조금 넘긴 듯했으며, 안색은 다소 거뭇하고, 긴 콧수염에 풍성한 턱수염을 길렀으니, 요컨대 그의 풍모는 옷만 잘 차려입었던들 지체 높고 가문 좋은 인물로 여겨질 만한 것이었다. 그는 들어서며 방을 청했고, 객줏집에 빈방이 없다는 말을 듣자 난처해하는 기색이더니, 차림새로 보아 무어 여인인 듯한 그녀에게 다가가 두 팔로 안아 안장에서 내려놓았다. 루신다와 도로테아, 객줏집 안주인과 그 딸, 그리고 마리토르네스는 그 기이하고 자기들에게는 전혀 새로운 차림새에 이끌려 그녀 주위로 모여들었다. 그리고 언제나 친절하고 정중하며 재치 빠른 도로테아는, 그녀와 그녀를 데려온 남자가 방을 찾지 못해 곤란해함을 알아채고는 그녀에게 말했다. \"부인, 여기 불편하고 호사스럽지 못한 것에 마음 쓰지 마십시오. 길가 객줏집이란 으레 그런 것들이 없게 마련이니까요. 그래도 부인께서 저희와 숙소를 함께 쓰는 것을 좋아해 주신다면(그녀는 루신다를 가리켰다), 아마 여행 중에 이보다 못한 잠자리를 만나신 적도 있으실 겁니다.\""]],[["To this the veiled lady made no reply; all she did was to rise from her seat, crossing her hands upon her bosom, bowing her head and bending her body as a sign that she returned thanks.","이에 베일을 쓴 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그녀가 한 일이라곤 자리에서 일어나 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고 고개를 숙이며 몸을 굽혀, 감사를 표한다는 표시를 하는 것뿐이었다."],["From her silence they concluded that she must be a Moor and unable to speak a Christian tongue.","그 침묵으로 미루어 사람들은 그녀가 무어인인 데다 기독교도의 말을 할 줄 모르는 것이 틀림없다고 결론지었다."]],[["At this moment the captive came up, having been until now otherwise engaged, and seeing that they all stood round his companion and that she made no reply to what they addressed to her, he said, “Ladies, this damsel hardly understands my language and can speak none but that of her own country, for which reason she does not and cannot answer what has been asked of her.”","바로 그 순간 그때까지 다른 일에 매여 있던 그 포로가 다가왔고, 모두가 자기 동행 곁에 둘러서 있는데도 그녀가 자기들이 건넨 말에 아무 대답도 하지 않는 것을 보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"부인들, 이 처녀는 제 말을 거의 알아듣지 못하고 자기 나라 말밖에는 할 줄 모릅니다. 그런 까닭에 여러분께서 물으신 것에 대답하지 못하고, 또 대답할 수도 없는 것입니다.\""]],[["“Nothing has been asked of her,”","\"저희가 그녀에게 물은 것은 아무것도 없습니다.\" 루신다가 대답했다."],["returned Luscinda; “she has only been offered our company for this evening and a share of the quarters we occupy, where she shall be made as comfortable as the circumstances allow, with the good-will we are bound to show all strangers that stand in need of it, especially if it be a woman to whom the service is rendered.”","\"그저 오늘 저녁 저희와 함께 지내자고, 저희가 묵는 자리를 나누자고 권했을 뿐입니다. 그곳에서 형편이 허락하는 한 편안히 지내시게 해 드릴 것이며, 도움이 필요한 모든 나그네에게, 특히 그 도움을 받는 이가 여인이라면 마땅히 베풀어야 할 호의로써 그리할 것입니다.\""]],[["“On her part and my own, señora,”","\"그녀를 대신해서, 또 저 자신을 위해서, 부인\" 포로가 대답했다."],["replied the captive, “I kiss your hands, and I esteem highly, as I ought, the favour you have offered, which, on such an occasion and coming from persons of your appearance, is, it is plain to see, a very great one.”","\"부인의 손에 입 맞추며, 베풀어 주신 호의를 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 높이 받들겠습니다. 이러한 처지에, 또 부인 같은 풍모의 분들에게서 받은 것이니, 보다시피 참으로 크나큰 호의가 아닐 수 없습니다.\""]],[["“Tell me, señor,”","\"한 가지 여쭙겠는데요, 선생님\""],["said Dorothea, “is this lady a Christian or a Moor?","도로테아가 말했다. \"이 부인은 기독교도인가요, 아니면 무어인인가요?"],["for her dress and her silence lead us to imagine that she is what we could wish she was not.”","그 차림새와 침묵으로 미루어, 저희가 바라지 않는 쪽이 아닐까 짐작하게 되어서요.\""]],[["“In dress and outwardly,” said he, “she is a Moor, but at heart she is a thoroughly good Christian, for she has the greatest desire to become one.”","\"옷차림과 겉모습으로는\" 그가 말했다. \"무어인입니다. 하지만 마음속으로는 더없이 훌륭한 기독교도입니다. 기독교도가 되고 싶은 간절한 소망을 품고 있으니까요.\""]],[["“Then she has not been baptised?”","\"그렇다면 아직 세례를 받지 못했나요?\""],["returned Luscinda.","루신다가 되물었다."]],[["“There has been no opportunity for that,” replied the captive, “since she left Algiers, her native country and home; and up to the present she has not found herself in any such imminent danger of death as to make it necessary to baptise her before she has been instructed in all the ceremonies our holy mother Church ordains; but, please God, ere long she shall be baptised with the solemnity befitting her which is higher than her dress or mine indicates.”","\"그럴 기회가 없었습니다.\" 포로가 대답했다. \"그녀가 자기 고향이자 보금자리인 알제를 떠난 이래로 말입니다. 그리고 지금까지 우리 거룩한 어머니이신 교회가 정한 모든 의식을 다 배우기 전에 서둘러 세례를 베풀어야 할 만큼 임박한 죽음의 위험에 처한 적도 없었습니다. 다만 하느님께서 허락하신다면, 머지않아 그녀의 옷차림이나 제 옷차림이 가리키는 것보다 훨씬 높은, 그녀에게 걸맞은 엄숙함으로 세례를 받게 될 것입니다.\""]],[["By these words he excited a desire in all who heard him, to know who the Moorish lady and the captive were, but no one liked to ask just then, seeing that it was a fitter moment for helping them to rest themselves than for questioning them about their lives.","이 말로 그는 듣는 모든 이에게, 그 무어 부인과 포로가 누구인지 알고 싶은 욕구를 불러일으켰다. 그러나 그때만큼은 아무도 선뜻 묻고 싶어 하지 않았으니, 그들의 삶에 대해 캐물을 때라기보다는 그들이 좀 쉬도록 도와줄 더 알맞은 때라고 여긴 까닭이었다."],["Dorothea took the Moorish lady by the hand and leading her to a seat beside herself, requested her to remove her veil.","도로테아는 무어 부인의 손을 잡아 자기 곁의 자리로 이끌고는 베일을 벗어 달라고 청했다."],["She looked at the captive as if to ask him what they meant and what she was to do.","그녀는 그게 무슨 뜻인지, 자기가 어찌해야 하는지 묻는 듯 포로를 바라보았다."],["He said to her in Arabic that they asked her to take off her veil, and thereupon she removed it and disclosed a countenance so lovely, that to Dorothea she seemed more beautiful than Luscinda, and to Luscinda more beautiful than Dorothea, and all the bystanders felt that if any beauty could compare with theirs it was the Moorish lady’s, and there were even those who were inclined to give it somewhat the preference.","그가 아랍어로 베일을 벗어 달라는 청이라고 말해 주자, 그녀는 베일을 벗었고, 그러자 어찌나 사랑스러운 얼굴이 드러났던지, 도로테아에게는 그녀가 루신다보다 더 아름다워 보였고, 루신다에게는 도로테아보다 더 아름다워 보였으며, 곁에 있던 모든 이가 자기들의 아름다움에 견줄 만한 아름다움이 있다면 그것은 바로 이 무어 부인의 것이라고 느꼈고, 심지어 그쪽에 다소 우위를 두고 싶어 하는 이들마저 있었다."],["And as it is the privilege and charm of beauty to win the heart and secure good-will, all forthwith became eager to show kindness and attention to the lovely Moor.","그리고 마음을 사로잡고 호의를 얻어 내는 것이야말로 아름다움의 특권이자 매력인지라, 모두가 곧장 이 사랑스러운 무어 여인에게 친절과 관심을 보이고 싶어 안달이 났다."]],[["Don Fernando asked the captive what her name was, and he replied that it was Lela Zoraida; but the instant she heard him, she guessed what the Christian had asked, and said hastily, with some displeasure and energy, “No, not Zoraida; Maria, Maria!”","돈 페르난도가 포로에게 그녀의 이름이 무엇이냐고 묻자, 그는 렐라 소라이다라고 대답했다. 그러나 그 말을 듣는 순간 그녀는 그 기독교도가 무엇을 물었는지 알아채고는, 약간의 언짢음과 격한 기색으로 황급히 말했다."],["giving them to understand that she was called “Maria”","\"아뇨, 소라이다 아녜요."],["and not “Zoraida.”","마리아, 마리아!\""],["These words, and the touching earnestness with which she uttered them, drew more than one tear from some of the listeners, particularly the women, who are by nature tender-hearted and compassionate.","자신은 \"소라이다\"가 아니라 \"마리아\"라고 불린다는 뜻을 알린 것이었다. 이 말과, 그녀가 그것을 내뱉는 그 가슴 뭉클한 진심은 듣고 있던 몇몇 사람에게서, 특히 천성이 다정하고 가여워할 줄 아는 여인들에게서 한 방울 이상의 눈물을 자아냈다."],["Luscinda embraced her affectionately, saying, “Yes, yes, Maria, Maria,”","루신다가 그녀를 다정하게 끌어안으며 \"그래요, 그래요, 마리아, 마리아\"라고 말하자, 무어 여인은 \"그래요, 그래요, 마리아."],["to which the Moor replied, “Yes, yes, Maria; Zoraida macange,” which means “not Zoraida.”","소라이다 마칸헤\"라고 대답했으니, 이는 \"소라이다 아니에요\"라는 뜻이었다."]],[["Night was now approaching, and by the orders of those who accompanied Don Fernando the landlord had taken care and pains to prepare for them the best supper that was in his power.","이제 밤이 다가오고 있었고, 돈 페르난도를 수행한 이들의 분부에 따라 객줏집 주인은 자기 능력껏 가장 훌륭한 저녁상을 차리려고 정성과 수고를 기울였다."],["The hour therefore having arrived they all took their seats at a long table like a refectory one, for round or square table there was none in the inn, and the seat of honour at the head of it, though he was for refusing it, they assigned to Don Quixote, who desired the lady Micomicona to place herself by his side, as he was her protector.","그리하여 때가 이르자 모두 수도원 식당의 것 같은 긴 식탁에 자리를 잡았으니, 객줏집에는 둥글거나 네모난 식탁이 없었던 까닭이다. 식탁 상석의 영예로운 자리는, 그가 한사코 사양했음에도 돈키호테에게 배정되었고, 그는 미코미코나 부인에게 자기 곁에 앉으라고 청했으니, 자신이 그녀의 보호자였기 때문이다."],["Luscinda and Zoraida took their places next her, opposite to them were Don Fernando and Cardenio, and next the captive and the other gentlemen, and by the side of the ladies, the curate and the barber.","루신다와 소라이다가 그녀 옆에 자리를 잡았고, 그들 맞은편에는 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오가, 그 옆에는 포로와 다른 신사들이, 그리고 부인들 옆에는 신부와 이발사가 앉았다."],["And so they supped in high enjoyment, which was increased when they observed Don Quixote leave off eating, and, moved by an impulse like that which made him deliver himself at such length when he supped with the goatherds, begin to address them:","그리하여 그들은 더없이 즐겁게 저녁을 들었는데, 그 즐거움은 돈키호테가 식사를 멈추고서, 염소지기들과 저녁을 들 때 그를 그토록 길게 늘어놓게 만들었던 것과 같은 충동에 이끌려 그들에게 말을 건네기 시작하는 것을 보았을 때 한층 더해졌다."]],[["“Verily, gentlemen, if we reflect upon it, great and marvellous are the things they see, who make profession of the order of knight-errantry. Say, what being is there in this world, who entering the gate of this castle at this moment, and seeing us as we are here, would suppose or imagine us to be what we are? Who would say that this lady who is beside me was the great queen that we all know her to be, or that I am that Knight of the Rueful Countenance, trumpeted far and wide by the mouth of Fame? Now, there can be no doubt that this art and calling surpasses all those that mankind has invented, and is the more deserving of being held in honour in proportion as it is the more exposed to peril. Away with those who assert that letters have the preeminence over arms; I will tell them, whosoever they may be, that they know not what they say. For the reason which such persons commonly assign, and upon which they chiefly rest, is, that the labours of the mind are greater than those of the body, and that arms give employment to the body alone; as if the calling were a porter’s trade, for which nothing more is required than sturdy strength; or as if, in what we who profess them call arms, there were not included acts of vigour for the execution of which high intelligence is requisite; or as if the soul of the warrior, when he has an army, or the defence of a city under his care, did not exert itself as much by mind as by body. Nay; see whether by bodily strength it be possible to learn or divine the intentions of the enemy, his plans, stratagems, or obstacles, or to ward off impending mischief; for all these are the work of the mind, and in them the body has no share whatever. Since, therefore, arms have need of the mind, as much as letters, let us see now which of the two minds, that of the man of letters or that of the warrior, has most to do; and this will be seen by the end and goal that each seeks to attain; for that purpose is the more estimable which has for its aim the nobler object. The end and goal of letters--I am not speaking now of divine letters, the aim of which is to raise and direct the soul to Heaven; for with an end so infinite no other can be compared--I speak of human letters, the end of which is to establish distributive justice, give to every man that which is his, and see and take care that good laws are observed: an end undoubtedly noble, lofty, and deserving of high praise, but not such as should be given to that sought by arms, which have for their end and object peace, the greatest boon that men can desire in this life. The first good news the world and mankind received was that which the angels announced on the night that was our day, when they sang in the air, ‘Glory to God in the highest, and peace on earth to men of good-will;’ and the salutation which the great Master of heaven and earth taught his disciples and chosen followers when they entered any house, was to say, ‘Peace be on this house;’ and many other times he said to them, ‘My peace I give unto you, my peace I leave you, peace be with you;’ a jewel and a precious gift given and left by such a hand: a jewel without which there can be no happiness either on earth or in heaven. This peace is the true end of war; and war is only another name for arms. This, then, being admitted, that the end of war is peace, and that so far it has the advantage of the end of letters, let us turn to the bodily labours of the man of letters, and those of him who follows the profession of arms, and see which are the greater.”","\"진실로, 여러분, 우리가 곰곰이 생각해 보면, 편력기사도의 길에 몸담은 자들이 보는 것들이란 위대하고 경이로운 것이외다. 말해 보시오, 이 세상에 어떤 존재가, 바로 이 순간 이 성의 문을 들어서서 여기 있는 우리를 본다면, 우리를 지금의 우리와 같은 사람들이라고 짐작하거나 상상할 수 있겠소이까? 제 곁에 있는 이 부인이 우리 모두가 아는 그 위대한 여왕이라고, 또는 제가 명성의 입을 통해 멀리까지 나팔처럼 울려 퍼진 그 슬픈 몰골의 기사라고 누가 말하겠소이까? 자, 이 기예와 소명이 인류가 고안해 낸 다른 모든 것을 능가한다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없으며, 위험에 더 많이 노출되는 만큼 더더욱 영예롭게 여겨질 자격이 있는 것이외다. 학문이 무예보다 우월하다고 주장하는 자들은 물러가시오. 그자들이 누구든 간에 제가 일러 주겠소이다. 그들은 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지조차 모른다고 말이외다. 그런 자들이 흔히 내세우는, 또 주로 거기에 기대는 이유란, 정신의 노고가 육체의 노고보다 크며 무예는 육체에만 일거리를 준다는 것이외다. 마치 그 소명이 짐꾼의 일이어서 억센 힘 말고는 더 필요한 게 없다는 듯이, 또는 우리 무예를 업으로 삼는 자들이 무예라 부르는 것 안에 높은 지력을 요하는 용맹한 행위들이 들어 있지 않다는 듯이 말이외다. 또는 전사가 군대를 거느리거나 한 도시의 방어를 맡았을 때 그 영혼이 육체만큼이나 정신으로도 진력하지 않는다는 듯이 말이외다. 천만에! 육체의 힘만으로 적의 의도며 계획이며 책략이며 장애물을 배우거나 알아챌 수 있는지, 또는 닥쳐오는 화를 막아 낼 수 있는지 보시오. 이 모든 것은 정신의 일이며, 거기에 육체는 아무런 몫도 없는 것이외다. 그러므로 무예가 학문 못지않게 정신을 필요로 하니, 이제 둘 가운데 어느 정신이, 즉 학자의 정신과 전사의 정신 가운데 어느 쪽이 할 일이 더 많은지 봅시다. 이것은 저마다가 다다르려고 하는 목적과 목표를 보면 알 수 있을 것이외다. 더 고귀한 대상을 목표로 삼는 목적이 더 가치 있는 법이니 말이외다. 학문의 목적과 목표는 — 지금 저는 신성한 학문을 말하는 게 아니외다. 그 목표는 영혼을 일으켜 하늘로 인도하는 것이니, 그토록 무한한 목적에는 다른 무엇도 견줄 수 없는 까닭이외다 — 인간의 학문을 말하는 것인데, 그 목적은 분배적 정의를 세우고, 각 사람에게 그의 몫을 주며, 좋은 법이 지켜지는지 보살피는 것이외다. 이는 의심할 바 없이 고귀하고 드높으며 큰 칭송을 받을 만한 목적이외다. 허나 무예가 추구하는 것에 돌려야 할 칭송에는 미치지 못하니, 무예는 그 목적이자 대상으로 평화를, 곧 인간이 이 삶에서 바랄 수 있는 가장 큰 축복을 두는 까닭이외다. 세상과 인류가 받은 첫 기쁜 소식은 천사들이 우리에게 낮이 된 그 밤에 알린 것이니, 그들이 공중에서 '지극히 높은 곳에서는 하느님께 영광이요, 땅에서는 선의의 사람들에게 평화로다'라고 노래했을 때 말이외다. 또한 하늘과 땅의 위대한 스승께서 그분의 제자들과 택하신 추종자들에게 어느 집에 들어가든 하라고 가르치신 인사도, '이 집에 평화가 깃들지어다'라는 것이었으며, 그분은 그밖에도 여러 번 그들에게 '내 평화를 너희에게 주노라, 내 평화를 너희에게 남기노라, 평화가 너희와 함께할지어다'라고 말씀하셨으니, 그러한 손으로 주고 남기신 보석이자 귀한 선물이외다. 그것이 없이는 땅에서도 하늘에서도 행복이 있을 수 없는 보석이외다. 이 평화야말로 전쟁의 참된 목적이며, 전쟁이란 무예의 또 다른 이름일 뿐이외다. 그러니 전쟁의 목적이 평화이며, 그 점에서 무예가 학문의 목적보다 우위에 있음을 인정한다면, 이제 학자의 육체적 노고와 무예의 길을 따르는 자의 육체적 노고로 눈을 돌려, 어느 쪽이 더 큰지 봅시다.\""]],[["Don Quixote delivered his discourse in such a manner and in such correct language, that for the time being he made it impossible for any of his hearers to consider him a madman; on the contrary, as they were mostly gentlemen, to whom arms are an appurtenance by birth, they listened to him with great pleasure as he continued: “Here, then, I say is what the student has to undergo; first of all poverty: not that all are poor, but to put the case as strongly as possible: and when I have said that he endures poverty, I think nothing more need be said about his hard fortune, for he who is poor has no share of the good things of life. This poverty he suffers from in various ways, hunger, or cold, or nakedness, or all together; but for all that it is not so extreme but that he gets something to eat, though it may be at somewhat unseasonable hours and from the leavings of the rich; for the greatest misery of the student is what they themselves call ‘going out for soup,’ and there is always some neighbour’s brazier or hearth for them, which, if it does not warm, at least tempers the cold to them, and lastly, they sleep comfortably at night under a roof. I will not go into other particulars, as for example want of shirts, and no superabundance of shoes, thin and threadbare garments, and gorging themselves to surfeit in their voracity when good luck has treated them to a banquet of some sort. By this road that I have described, rough and hard, stumbling here, falling there, getting up again to fall again, they reach the rank they desire, and that once attained, we have seen many who have passed these Syrtes and Scyllas and Charybdises, as if borne flying on the wings of favouring fortune; we have seen them, I say, ruling and governing the world from a chair, their hunger turned into satiety, their cold into comfort, their nakedness into fine raiment, their sleep on a mat into repose in holland and damask, the justly earned reward of their virtue; but, contrasted and compared with what the warrior undergoes, all they have undergone falls far short of it, as I am now about to show.”","돈키호테는 자기 일장 연설을 어찌나 그럴듯한 태도로, 또 어찌나 정확한 언어로 펼쳤던지, 적어도 그동안만큼은 듣는 이들 가운데 누구도 그를 미치광이로 여길 수 없게 만들었다. 오히려 그들 대부분이 무예가 태생적으로 따라붙는 신사들이었던지라, 그가 말을 이어 가는 동안 큰 즐거움으로 그에게 귀 기울였다. \"그러니 여기서, 제가 말씀드리건대, 학생이 견뎌야 할 것은 이것이외다. 무엇보다도 먼저 가난이외다. 모든 학생이 가난하다는 게 아니라, 경우를 가능한 한 강하게 들자는 것이외다. 그리고 그가 가난을 견딘다고 말했으니, 그의 가혹한 운명에 대해서는 더 말할 게 없으리라 봅니다. 가난한 자는 삶의 좋은 것들을 누리지 못하는 법이니 말이외다. 이 가난을 그는 갖가지 모양으로 겪으니, 굶주림이든 추위든 헐벗음이든, 혹은 그 모두든 말이외다. 허나 그렇다 해도 그것이 그토록 극심하지는 않으니, 다소 때아닌 시각에, 또 부자들이 먹다 남긴 것에서일망정 먹을 것은 좀 얻게 되는 까닭이외다. 학생의 가장 큰 비참함이란 그들 자신이 '수프 얻으러 나간다'고 부르는 것인데, 그들에게는 언제나 이웃의 화로나 화덕이 있어, 비록 몸을 데워 주지는 못해도 적어도 추위를 누그러뜨려는 주고, 끝으로 밤에는 지붕 아래서 편안히 잠드는 것이외다. 그밖의 자질구레한 것들, 이를테면 셔츠가 없다거나 신발이 넉넉지 못하다거나 옷이 얇고 다 해졌다거나, 운 좋게 어떤 잔치에 초대받기라도 하면 게걸스럽게 배 터지도록 처먹는다거나 하는 것들에 대해서는 더 들어가지 않겠소이다. 제가 묘사한 이 길, 거칠고 험난한 이 길로, 여기서 비틀거리고 저기서 넘어지고 다시 일어났다가 또 넘어지면서, 그들은 바라던 지위에 다다르며, 일단 그것에 이르면, 우리는 그 시르티스와 스킬라와 카립디스를 마치 순풍의 행운이 날개를 달아 준 듯 헤쳐 나간 이들을 많이 보아 왔소이다. 제가 말하건대, 그들이 의자에 앉아 세상을 다스리고 통치하며, 그 굶주림이 포만으로, 그 추위가 안락으로, 그 헐벗음이 고운 의복으로, 거적 위의 잠이 고운 삼베와 다마스크 천 위의 안식으로 바뀐 것을 보아 왔으니, 이는 그들의 덕에 대한 정당히 얻은 보상이외다. 허나 전사가 견뎌 내는 것과 견주고 비교해 보면, 그들이 견뎌 낸 모든 것은 거기에 훨씬 못 미치니, 이제 제가 그것을 보여 드리겠소이다.\" * 시르티스, 스킬라, 카립디스: 고대 지중해 항해의 위험한 곳들로, 시르티스는 북아프리카 연안의 모래톱이고, 스킬라와 카립디스는 그리스 신화에 나오는 좁은 해협의 두 괴물이다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXXVIII. WHICH TREATS OF THE CURIOUS DISCOURSE DON QUIXOTE DELIVERED ON ARMS AND LETTERS","# CHAPTER XXXVIII."]],[["Continuing his discourse Don Quixote said: “As we began in the student’s case with poverty and its accompaniments, let us see now if the soldier is richer, and we shall find that in poverty itself there is no one poorer; for he is dependent on his miserable pay, which comes late or never, or else on what he can plunder, seriously imperilling his life and conscience; and sometimes his nakedness will be so great that a slashed doublet serves him for uniform and shirt, and in the depth of winter he has to defend himself against the inclemency of the weather in the open field with nothing better than the breath of his mouth, which I need not say, coming from an empty place, must come out cold, contrary to the laws of nature. To be sure he looks forward to the approach of night to make up for all these discomforts on the bed that awaits him, which, unless by some fault of his, never sins by being over narrow, for he can easily measure out on the ground as he likes, and roll himself about in it to his heart’s content without any fear of the sheets slipping away from him. Then, after all this, suppose the day and hour for taking his degree in his calling to have come; suppose the day of battle to have arrived, when they invest him with the doctor’s cap made of lint, to mend some bullet-hole, perhaps, that has gone through his temples, or left him with a crippled arm or leg. Or if this does not happen, and merciful Heaven watches over him and keeps him safe and sound, it may be he will be in the same poverty he was in before, and he must go through more engagements and more battles, and come victorious out of all before he betters himself; but miracles of that sort are seldom seen. For tell me, sirs, if you have ever reflected upon it, by how much do those who have gained by war fall short of the number of those who have perished in it? No doubt you will reply that there can be no comparison, that the dead cannot be numbered, while the living who have been rewarded may be summed up with three figures. All which is the reverse in the case of men of letters; for by skirts, to say nothing of sleeves, they all find means of support; so that though the soldier has more to endure, his reward is much less. But against all this it may be urged that it is easier to reward two thousand soldiers, for the former may be remunerated by giving them places, which must perforce be conferred upon men of their calling, while the latter can only be recompensed out of the very property of the master they serve; but this impossibility only strengthens my argument.","돈키호테는 이야기를 이어 가며 말했다. \"학생의 경우를 가난과 그에 딸린 것들에서 시작했으니, 이제 군인이 더 부유한지 어디 한번 보십시다. 그러면 가난 그 자체에서도 군인보다 더 가난한 자는 없음을 알게 될 것입니다. 군인은 늦게 오거나 아예 오지 않는 그 변변찮은 봉급에 매여 있거나, 아니면 제 목숨과 양심을 심히 위태롭게 하며 약탈할 수 있는 것에 의지해야 하니 말입니다. 게다가 때로는 헐벗음이 어찌나 심한지 갈라진 더블릿 한 벌이 군복이자 셔츠 노릇을 하고, 한겨울에는 허허벌판에서 제 입김 말고는 더 나은 것도 없이 혹독한 날씨에 맞서 몸을 지켜야 하니, 굳이 말할 것도 없거니와 텅 빈 곳에서 나오는 그 입김은 자연의 법칙을 거슬러 차갑게 나올 수밖에 없는 것입니다. 그래도 그는 밤이 오기를 손꼽아 기다립니다. 자신을 기다리는 침상에서 이 모든 불편을 메우려는 것이지요. 그 침상은 그 자신의 잘못이 아니고서야 결코 너무 좁다는 죄를 짓는 법이 없으니, 땅바닥에 마음대로 제 몸을 재어 누울 수 있고, 시트가 흘러내릴까 봐 두려워할 일도 없이 마음껏 몸을 뒤척일 수 있기 때문입니다. 그런데 이 모든 것을 겪은 뒤, 이제 자기 직업에서 학위를 받을 날과 시각이 왔다고 칩시다. 곧 전투의 날이 와서, 어쩌면 관자놀이를 꿰뚫고 지나간 어떤 총탄 구멍을 꿰매기 위해, 혹은 그를 한쪽 팔이나 다리 못 쓰는 불구로 만들 거즈로 지은 박사모를 그에게 씌워 주는 날이 왔다고 칩시다. 혹 이런 일이 일어나지 않고 자비로운 하늘이 그를 지켜보며 무사히 멀쩡하게 보살펴 준다 하더라도, 그는 전과 똑같은 가난 속에 있을 것이며, 더 많은 교전과 더 많은 전투를 거치고 그 모든 것에서 승리하여 나와야만 비로소 형편이 나아질 것이나, 그런 종류의 기적은 좀처럼 보기 어려운 법입니다. 여러분, 한번 곰곰이 생각해 본 적이 있다면 말씀해 보십시오. 전쟁으로 이득을 본 자들의 수가 거기서 죽어 간 자들의 수에 비해 얼마나 모자란지 말입니다. 의심할 바 없이 여러분은, 비교 자체가 안 된다고, 죽은 자들은 헤아릴 수조차 없는데 상을 받은 산 자들은 세 자릿수면 다 셀 수 있다고 답하실 것입니다. 이 모든 것이 학식 있는 자들의 경우에는 정반대입니다. 그들은 소매는 말할 것도 없고 옷자락만으로도 다들 먹고살 방도를 찾으니 말입니다. 그러니 군인이 더 많이 견뎌야 하면서도 그 보상은 훨씬 적습니다. 그러나 이 모든 것에 맞서, 군인 이천 명에게 상을 주기가 더 쉽다는 반론을 펼 수도 있습니다. 전자는 자리를 줌으로써 보상할 수 있고, 그 자리는 마땅히 그들의 직업에 종사하는 자들에게 내려져야 하는 것이나, 후자는 그들이 섬기는 주인의 바로 그 재산에서밖에 보상받을 길이 없기 때문입니다. 하지만 이 불가능함이야말로 오히려 내 논지를 굳혀 줄 따름입니다."]],[["“Putting this, however, aside, for it is a puzzling question for which it is difficult to find a solution, let us return to the superiority of arms over letters, a matter still undecided, so many are the arguments put forward on each side; for besides those I have mentioned, letters say that without them arms cannot maintain themselves, for war, too, has its laws and is governed by them, and laws belong to the domain of letters and men of letters.","\"그러나 이 문제는 풀어내기 어려운 까다로운 물음이니 한쪽으로 제쳐 두고, 무기가 학문보다 우월하다는 문제로 돌아갑시다. 이것은 아직 결판이 나지 않은 문제이니, 양쪽에서 내세우는 논거가 그토록 많기 때문입니다."],["To this arms make answer that without them laws cannot be maintained, for by arms states are defended, kingdoms preserved, cities protected, roads made safe, seas cleared of pirates; and, in short, if it were not for them, states, kingdoms, monarchies, cities, ways by sea and land would be exposed to the violence and confusion which war brings with it, so long as it lasts and is free to make use of its privileges and powers.","내가 이미 언급한 것들 말고도, 학문은 자기 없이는 무기가 스스로를 지탱할 수 없다고 말합니다. 전쟁에도 그 나름의 법이 있어 그 법의 지배를 받는데, 법이란 학문과 학식 있는 자들의 영역에 속하기 때문이라는 것이지요."],["And then it is plain that whatever costs most is valued and deserves to be valued most.","이에 무기는, 자기 없이는 법이 유지될 수 없다고 답합니다."],["To attain to eminence in letters costs a man time, watching, hunger, nakedness, headaches, indigestions, and other things of the sort, some of which I have already referred to.","무기로써 나라가 지켜지고, 왕국이 보전되며, 도시가 보호받고, 길이 안전해지고, 바다에서 해적이 소탕되기 때문입니다. 한마디로, 무기가 없다면 나라도, 왕국도, 군주국도, 도시도, 바다와 육지의 길도, 전쟁이 지속되며 그 특권과 권력을 마음껏 휘두르는 한, 전쟁이 몰고 오는 폭력과 혼란에 고스란히 내맡겨지고 말 것이라는 말이지요."],["But for a man to come in the ordinary course of things to be a good soldier costs him all the student suffers, and in an incomparably higher degree, for at every step he runs the risk of losing his life.","그리고 가장 값비싼 것이 가장 귀하게 여겨지고 또 가장 귀하게 여겨질 만하다는 것은 명백한 이치입니다. 학문에서 탁월함에 이르려면 시간과 밤샘과 굶주림과 헐벗음과 두통과 소화불량을, 그리고 이런 종류의 다른 것들을 — 그중 몇 가지는 이미 내가 언급한 바 있거니와 — 치러야 합니다."],["For what dread of want or poverty that can reach or harass the student can compare with what the soldier feels, who finds himself beleaguered in some stronghold mounting guard in some ravelin or cavalier, knows that the enemy is pushing a mine towards the post where he is stationed, and cannot under any circumstances retire or fly from the imminent danger that threatens him?","그러나 한 사람이 통상적인 과정을 밟아 훌륭한 군인이 되기까지는, 학생이 겪는 모든 것을 그것도 비할 바 없이 더한 정도로 치러야 하니, 걸음마다 제 목숨을 잃을 위험을 무릅쓰기 때문입니다. 학생을 덮치거나 괴롭힐 수 있는 그 어떤 궁핍과 가난의 두려움인들, 어느 요새에 갇힌 채 어느 반월보(半月堡)나 능보(稜堡)에서 보초를 서다가, 적이 자기가 배치된 초소를 향해 갱도를 파 들어오고 있음을 알면서도, 어떤 경우에도 임박한 위험에서 물러나거나 달아날 수 없는 군인이 느끼는 것에 견줄 수 있겠습니까?"],["All he can do is to inform his captain of what is going on so that he may try to remedy it by a counter-mine, and then stand his ground in fear and expectation of the moment when he will fly up to the clouds without wings and descend into the deep against his will.","그가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 자기 대장에게 알려, 대장이 맞갱도를 파서 막아 보도록 하는 것뿐이며, 그러고는 날개도 없이 구름 위로 솟구쳐 올랐다가 제 뜻과는 어긋나게 깊은 땅속으로 떨어질 그 순간을 두려움과 기다림 속에서 버티고 서 있는 것뿐입니다. 만일 이것이 하찮은 위험으로 보인다면, 망망대해 한복판에서 뱃머리와 뱃머리를 맞댄 두 갤리선이, 서로 맞물리고 뒤엉킨 채 맞부딪치는 일이 이에 견줄 만한지, 아니 이를 능가하는지 어디 한번 봅시다."],["And if this seems a trifling risk, let us see whether it is equalled or surpassed by the encounter of two galleys stem to stem, in the midst of the open sea, locked and entangled one with the other, when the soldier has no more standing room than two feet of the plank of the spur; and yet, though he sees before him threatening him as many ministers of death as there are cannon of the foe pointed at him, not a lance length from his body, and sees too that with the first heedless step he will go down to visit the profundities of Neptune’s bosom, still with dauntless heart, urged on by honour that nerves him, he makes himself a target for all that musketry, and struggles to cross that narrow path to the enemy’s ship.","그때 군인은 충각(衝角) 발판의 두 자 너비밖에 디딜 자리가 없는데도, 자기 몸에서 창 한 자루 길이도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 적의 대포가 겨눠진 그 수만큼이나 많은 죽음의 사자(使者)들이 눈앞에서 자신을 위협하는 것을 보면서도, 또 첫걸음을 부주의하게 내디뎠다가는 넵투누스 품속의 심연으로 곤두박질쳐 들어가리라는 것을 보면서도, 여전히 두려움 없는 가슴으로, 자신을 다잡아 주는 명예에 떠밀려, 그 모든 총탄의 표적이 되기를 마다하지 않고 적선으로 건너가려 그 좁은 길을 가로지르려 발버둥 칩니다. 더욱 놀라운 것은, 한 사람이 세상이 끝나는 날까지 다시는 떠오르지 못할 심연으로 가라앉기가 무섭게 또 다른 자가 그 자리를 차지하고, 그자 또한 적처럼 자기를 기다리는 바닷속으로 떨어지면, 또 다른 자가, 그리고 또 다른 자가 그 죽음들 사이에 한순간의 틈도 없이 뒤를 잇는다는 것입니다."],["And what is still more marvellous, no sooner has one gone down into the depths he will never rise from till the end of the world, than another takes his place; and if he too falls into the sea that waits for him like an enemy, another and another will succeed him without a moment’s pause between their deaths: courage and daring the greatest that all the chances of war can show.","전쟁의 온갖 운수가 보여 줄 수 있는 가장 위대한 용기와 담력이지요. 저 악마 같은 포병 기계들의 무시무시한 광포함을 알지 못했던, 복받은 시대들은 행복했습니다."],["Happy the blest ages that knew not the dread fury of those devilish engines of artillery, whose inventor I am persuaded is in hell receiving the reward of his diabolical invention, by which he made it easy for a base and cowardly arm to take the life of a gallant gentleman; and that, when he knows not how or whence, in the height of the ardour and enthusiasm that fire and animate brave hearts, there should come some random bullet, discharged perhaps by one who fled in terror at the flash when he fired off his accursed machine, which in an instant puts an end to the projects and cuts off the life of one who deserved to live for ages to come.","그것을 발명한 자는, 내 확신컨대, 자신의 악마 같은 발명에 대한 응보를 받으며 지옥에 있을 것입니다. 그자는 그 발명으로, 비천하고 비겁한 팔이 용맹한 신사의 목숨을 빼앗기 쉽도록 만들어 놓았으니, 용감한 가슴을 불태우고 살아 움직이게 하는 그 열정과 격정이 한껏 타오르는 와중에, 어디서 어떻게 날아오는지도 모르는 어떤 난데없는 총알이 — 어쩌면 그 저주받은 기계를 쏘고 나서 그 섬광에 겁먹어 달아난 자가 쏘았을 그 총알이 — 날아들어, 다가올 여러 세대를 살 자격이 있는 자의 계획을 순식간에 끝장내고 그 목숨을 끊어 버리는 것입니다."],["And thus when I reflect on this, I am almost tempted to say that in my heart I repent of having adopted this profession of knight-errant in so detestable an age as we live in now; for though no peril can make me fear, still it gives me some uneasiness to think that powder and lead may rob me of the opportunity of making myself famous and renowned throughout the known earth by the might of my arm and the edge of my sword.","그리하여 이를 곰곰이 헤아릴 때면, 나는 지금 우리가 살고 있는 이토록 가증스러운 시대에 편력 기사라는 이 직업을 택한 것을 내 마음속으로 후회한다고 거의 말하고 싶어집니다. 그 어떤 위험도 나를 두렵게 할 수는 없으나, 화약과 납덩이가 내 팔의 힘과 내 칼날로써 온 세상에 이름을 떨치고 명성을 얻을 기회를 앗아 갈지도 모른다는 생각을 하면 다소 마음이 편치 않기 때문입니다."],["But Heaven’s will be done; if I succeed in my attempt I shall be all the more honoured, as I have faced greater dangers than the knights-errant of yore exposed themselves to.”","그러나 하늘의 뜻대로 될지니, 만일 내가 뜻을 이룬다면 나는 옛날의 편력 기사들이 무릅썼던 것보다 더 큰 위험에 맞섰던 만큼 그만큼 더 큰 영예를 누리게 될 것입니다.\""]],[["All this lengthy discourse Don Quixote delivered while the others supped, forgetting to raise a morsel to his lips, though Sancho more than once told him to eat his supper, as he would have time enough afterwards to say all he wanted.","이 기나긴 이야기를 돈키호테는 다른 이들이 저녁을 먹는 동안 늘어놓았으니, 산초가 한 번도 아니고 여러 번이나 하고 싶은 말은 나중에 얼마든지 할 시간이 있을 테니 어서 저녁이나 드시라고 일렀건만, 그는 음식 한 조각 입에 가져가는 것조차 잊고 있었다."],["It excited fresh pity in those who had heard him to see a man of apparently sound sense, and with rational views on every subject he discussed, so hopelessly wanting in all, when his wretched unlucky chivalry was in question.","그의 말을 듣던 이들은 새삼 연민에 사로잡혔으니, 분별이 멀쩡해 보이고 논하는 모든 주제마다 사리에 맞는 견해를 지닌 사람이, 그 가련하고 운수 사나운 기사도가 화제에 오르기만 하면 어쩌면 그토록 가망 없이 모든 분별을 잃어버리는지를 보았기 때문이다."],["The curate told him he was quite right in all he had said in favour of arms, and that he himself, though a man of letters and a graduate, was of the same opinion.","신부는 그가 무기를 옹호하여 한 말이 모두 지당하며, 자신 또한 학식 있는 사람이요 학위를 받은 자이지만 같은 의견이라고 말해 주었다."]],[["They finished their supper, the cloth was removed, and while the hostess, her daughter, and Maritornes were getting Don Quixote of La Mancha’s garret ready, in which it was arranged that the women were to be quartered by themselves for the night, Don Fernando begged the captive to tell them the story of his life, for it could not fail to be strange and interesting, to judge by the hints he had let fall on his arrival in company with Zoraida.","그들은 저녁 식사를 마치고 식탁보를 걷었으며, 여관 안주인과 그녀의 딸과 마리토르네스가 라만차의 돈키호테가 묵을 다락방을 — 그날 밤 여자들끼리만 그곳에서 묵기로 정해져 있었다 — 준비하는 동안, 돈 페르난도는 포로에게 그의 생애 이야기를 들려 달라고 청했다. 그가 소라이다와 함께 도착했을 때 흘린 몇 마디 암시로 미루어 보건대 그 이야기가 기이하고 흥미롭지 않을 리 없었기 때문이다."],["To this the captive replied that he would very willingly yield to his request, only he feared his tale would not give them as much pleasure as he wished; nevertheless, not to be wanting in compliance, he would tell it.","이에 포로는 기꺼이 그 청에 응하겠으나, 다만 자기 이야기가 그들이 바라는 만큼 즐거움을 주지는 못할까 봐 염려된다고 답했다. 그럼에도 응하는 데 인색하지 않으려 이야기를 들려주겠노라 했다."],["The curate and the others thanked him and added their entreaties, and he finding himself so pressed said there was no occasion to ask, where a command had such weight, and added, “If your worships will give me your attention you will hear a true story which, perhaps, fictitious ones constructed with ingenious and studied art cannot come up to.”","신부와 다른 이들이 고마워하며 거듭 청을 더하자, 그는 이토록 간곡히 청하는 마당에, 그것도 명령이 이토록 무게를 지니는 자리에서 굳이 청할 것까지도 없다고 하면서 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"여러분께서 귀를 기울여 주신다면, 어쩌면 기발하고 공들인 기교로 지어낸 허구의 이야기들도 따라오지 못할 진실한 이야기를 듣게 되실 것입니다.\""],["These words made them settle themselves in their places and preserve a deep silence, and he seeing them waiting on his words in mute expectation, began thus in a pleasant quiet voice.","이 말에 모두는 제자리에 자세를 고쳐 앉고는 깊은 침묵을 지켰으며, 그는 그들이 말없는 기대 속에 자신의 말을 기다리는 것을 보고는 유쾌하고 차분한 목소리로 이렇게 이야기를 시작했다."]],[["# CHAPTER XXXIX. WHEREIN THE CAPTIVE RELATES HIS LIFE AND ADVENTURES","# CHAPTER XXXIX."]],[["My family had its origin in a village in the mountains of Leon, and nature had been kinder and more generous to it than fortune; though in the general poverty of those communities my father passed for being even a rich man; and he would have been so in reality had he been as clever in preserving his property as he was in spending it.","우리 집안은 레온 산악 지방의 어느 마을에서 비롯되었는데, 자연은 운명보다 우리 집안에 더 너그럽고 더 후하였다. 그렇지만 그런 고장의 전반적인 가난 속에서도 내 아버지는 부유한 사람으로까지 통하였으니, 재산을 지키는 데도 재산을 쓰는 데 못지않게 영리하였더라면 실제로 그러하였을 것이다."],["This tendency of his to be liberal and profuse he had acquired from having been a soldier in his youth, for the soldier’s life is a school in which the niggard becomes free-handed and the free-handed prodigal; and if any soldiers are to be found who are misers, they are monsters of rare occurrence.","후하고 헤프게 베푸는 이 성향은 젊은 시절 군인으로 지낸 데서 몸에 밴 것이었다. 군인의 삶이란 인색한 자를 손이 큰 사람으로, 손이 큰 사람을 낭비가로 만드는 학교이기 때문이다."],["My father went beyond liberality and bordered on prodigality, a disposition by no means advantageous to a married man who has children to succeed to his name and position.","혹여 구두쇠인 군인이 있다 하더라도 그것은 좀처럼 보기 드문 괴물 같은 존재이다. 내 아버지는 후함을 넘어 낭비벽에 가까웠으니, 이는 자신의 이름과 지위를 이어받을 자식들을 둔 결혼한 남자에게는 결코 이로울 것이 없는 기질이었다."],["My father had three, all sons, and all of sufficient age to make choice of a profession.","내 아버지에게는 세 자식이 있었는데, 모두 아들이었고 모두 직업을 택할 만큼 나이가 들어 있었다."],["Finding, then, that he was unable to resist his propensity, he resolved to divest himself of the instrument and cause of his prodigality and lavishness, to divest himself of wealth, without which Alexander himself would have seemed parsimonious; and so calling us all three aside one day into a room, he addressed us in words somewhat to the following effect:","그리하여 자신의 성벽을 도무지 거스를 수 없음을 깨달은 아버지는, 그 낭비와 사치의 도구이자 원인이 되는 것에서 스스로를 떼어 내기로, 곧 알렉산드로스 자신조차 인색해 보이게 만들 그 재산에서 스스로를 떼어 내기로 결심하였다. 그리하여 어느 날 우리 셋을 모두 한 방으로 불러들여 대략 다음과 같은 말로 우리에게 이야기하였다."]],[["“My sons, to assure you that I love you, no more need be known or said than that you are my sons; and to encourage a suspicion that I do not love you, no more is needed than the knowledge that I have no self-control as far as preservation of your patrimony is concerned; therefore, that you may for the future feel sure that I love you like a father, and have no wish to ruin you like a stepfather, I propose to do with you what I have for some time back meditated, and after mature deliberation decided upon. You are now of an age to choose your line of life or at least make choice of a calling that will bring you honour and profit when you are older; and what I have resolved to do is to divide my property into four parts; three I will give to you, to each his portion without making any difference, and the other I will retain to live upon and support myself for whatever remainder of life Heaven may be pleased to grant me. But I wish each of you on taking possession of the share that falls to him to follow one of the paths I shall indicate. In this Spain of ours there is a proverb, to my mind very true--as they all are, being short aphorisms drawn from long practical experience--and the one I refer to says, ‘The church, or the sea, or the king’s house;’ as much as to say, in plainer language, whoever wants to flourish and become rich, let him follow the church, or go to sea, adopting commerce as his calling, or go into the king’s service in his household, for they say, ‘Better a king’s crumb than a lord’s favour.’ I say so because it is my will and pleasure that one of you should follow letters, another trade, and the third serve the king in the wars, for it is a difficult matter to gain admission to his service in his household, and if war does not bring much wealth it confers great distinction and fame. Eight days hence I will give you your full shares in money, without defrauding you of a farthing, as you will see in the end. Now tell me if you are willing to follow out my idea and advice as I have laid it before you.”","\"내 아들들아, 내가 너희를 사랑한다는 것을 확실히 알려 주는 데는 너희가 내 아들이라는 사실 외에 더 알거나 말할 것이 없으며, 내가 너희를 사랑하지 않는다는 의심을 품게 하는 데는 너희 유산을 지키는 일에 관한 한 내게 자제력이 없다는 사실을 아는 것 외에 더 필요한 것이 없다. 그러므로 너희가 앞으로 내가 너희를 아버지로서 사랑하며 계부처럼 너희를 망치고자 하는 마음이 없음을 확실히 느끼도록, 나는 얼마 전부터 곰곰이 생각해 오다가 충분히 숙고한 끝에 결정한 바를 너희에게 행하고자 한다. 너희는 이제 살아갈 길을 택할 나이, 적어도 나이가 들었을 때 명예와 이익을 가져다줄 천직을 택할 나이가 되었다. 내가 하기로 결심한 것은 내 재산을 네 몫으로 나누는 일이니, 세 몫은 차별 없이 너희 각자에게 그 몫을 주고, 나머지 한 몫은 하늘이 내게 베풀어 주실 남은 생이 얼마든 그동안 살아가며 나 자신을 부양하는 데 쓰려고 남겨 두겠다. 다만 나는 너희가 각자 자기에게 떨어진 몫을 차지하거든 내가 일러 줄 길 가운데 하나를 따르기를 바란다. 우리 이 스페인에는 내 생각에 매우 참된 — 모든 속담이 다 그러하듯, 오랜 실제 경험에서 끌어낸 짧은 격언이기에 — 속담이 하나 있으니, 내가 말하려는 그것은 이렇게 이른다. '교회냐, 바다냐, 임금의 집이냐.' 더 쉬운 말로 하자면, 번창하여 부자가 되고자 하는 자는 교회를 따르거나, 상업을 천직으로 삼아 바다로 나가거나, 임금의 궁에서 봉직하라는 뜻이다. 사람들이 말하기를 '귀족의 호의보다 임금의 빵 부스러기가 낫다'고 하기 때문이다. 내가 이렇게 말하는 까닭은, 너희 가운데 하나는 학문을 따르고, 또 하나는 장사를, 셋째는 전쟁에서 임금을 섬기기를 내가 바라고 또 그것이 내 기쁨이기 때문이다. 임금의 궁 안에서 봉직 자리를 얻기란 어려운 일이며, 전쟁이 큰 재물을 가져다주지는 않더라도 큰 명망과 명성을 안겨 주기 때문이다. 지금부터 여드레 후에 내가 너희에게 한 푼도 속이지 않고 돈으로 너희 몫을 온전히 내주겠으니, 끝에 가서 너희가 보게 될 것이다. 자, 이제 내가 너희 앞에 펼쳐 놓은 대로 내 생각과 충고를 따를 마음이 있는지 말해 보아라.\""]],[["Having called upon me as the eldest to answer, I, after urging him not to strip himself of his property but to spend it all as he pleased, for we were young men able to gain our living, consented to comply with his wishes, and said that mine were to follow the profession of arms and thereby serve God and my king.","맏이인 내게 먼저 답하라 하셨기에, 나는 아버지께 재산을 다 떨어내지 마시고 마음껏 다 쓰시라고, 우리는 제 손으로 벌어 살아갈 수 있는 젊은이들이라고 청한 끝에, 아버지의 뜻을 따르기로 하고 내 뜻은 무기의 길을 따라 그로써 하느님과 임금을 섬기는 데 있다고 말하였다. 둘째 형제도 같은 제안을 받아들여 자기에게 떨어진 몫을 장사에 실어 인도 제도로 가기로 정하였다."],["My second brother having made the same proposal, decided upon going to the Indies, embarking the portion that fell to him in trade.","막내, 내 생각에는 가장 현명한 그 아이는 차라리 교회를 따르거나 살라망카에 가서 학업을 마치겠다고 하였다."],["The youngest, and in my opinion the wisest, said he would rather follow the church, or go to complete his studies at Salamanca.","우리가 합의에 이르러 각자의 직업을 택하자마자 아버지는 우리 모두를 껴안으셨고, 말씀하신 그 짧은 기간 안에 약속한 모든 것을 실행에 옮기셨다."],["As soon as we had come to an understanding, and made choice of our professions, my father embraced us all, and in the short time he mentioned carried into effect all he had promised; and when he had given to each his share, which as well as I remember was three thousand ducats apiece in cash (for an uncle of ours bought the estate and paid for it down, not to let it go out of the family), we all three on the same day took leave of our good father; and at the same time, as it seemed to me inhuman to leave my father with such scanty means in his old age, I induced him to take two of my three thousand ducats, as the remainder would be enough to provide me with all a soldier needed.","그리하여 각자에게 그 몫을 — 내 기억으로는 각각 현금 삼천 두카도였다(우리 외삼촌 한 분이 재산이 집안 밖으로 나가지 않도록 그 토지를 사들여 즉석에서 값을 치렀기 때문이다) — 내주셨을 때, 우리 셋은 같은 날 우리의 인자하신 아버지께 작별을 고하였다. 그와 동시에, 그토록 빈약한 형편으로 노년의 아버지를 두고 떠나는 것이 인정 없는 일로 여겨졌기에, 나는 내 삼천 두카도 가운데 이천 두카도를 받으시도록 아버지를 설득하였으니, 나머지만으로도 군인에게 필요한 모든 것을 갖추기에 넉넉할 터였기 때문이다."],["My two brothers, moved by my example, gave him each a thousand ducats, so that there was left for my father four thousand ducats in money, besides three thousand, the value of the portion that fell to him which he preferred to retain in land instead of selling it.","두 형제도 내 본보기에 마음이 움직여 각각 천 두카도씩 아버지께 드렸고, 그리하여 아버지께는 현금 사천 두카도가, 그 밖에 토지로 남겨 두기를 택하신 자기 몫의 값인 삼천 두카도가 남게 되었다."],["Finally, as I said, we took leave of him, and of our uncle whom I have mentioned, not without sorrow and tears on both sides, they charging us to let them know whenever an opportunity offered how we fared, whether well or ill.","마침내 앞서 말한 대로 우리는 양쪽 모두 슬픔과 눈물 없이는 아니게, 아버지와 또 내가 말한 외삼촌께 작별을 고하였다. 그분들은 우리에게 잘 지내든 못 지내든 기회가 닿는 대로 어찌 지내는지 알려 달라고 당부하셨다."],["We promised to do so, and when he had embraced us and given us his blessing, one set out for Salamanca, the other for Seville, and I for Alicante, where I had heard there was a Genoese vessel taking in a cargo of wool for Genoa.","우리는 그러마고 약속하였고, 아버지께서 우리를 껴안고 축복을 내려 주신 뒤, 하나는 살라망카로, 또 하나는 세비야로, 나는 제노바로 양모를 싣는 제노바 배가 있다고 들은 알리칸테로 떠났다."]],[["It is now some twenty-two years since I left my father’s house, and all that time, though I have written several letters, I have had no news whatever of him or of my brothers; my own adventures during that period I will now relate briefly. I embarked at Alicante, reached Genoa after a prosperous voyage, and proceeded thence to Milan, where I provided myself with arms and a few soldier’s accoutrements; thence it was my intention to go and take service in Piedmont, but as I was already on the road to Alessandria della Paglia, I learned that the great Duke of Alva was on his way to Flanders. I changed my plans, joined him, served under him in the campaigns he made, was present at the deaths of the Counts Egmont and Horn, and was promoted to be ensign under a famous captain of Guadalajara, Diego de Urbina by name. Some time after my arrival in Flanders news came of the league that his Holiness Pope Pius V of happy memory, had made with Venice and Spain against the common enemy, the Turk, who had just then with his fleet taken the famous island of Cyprus, which belonged to the Venetians, a loss deplorable and disastrous. It was known as a fact that the Most Serene Don John of Austria, natural brother of our good king Don Philip, was coming as commander-in-chief of the allied forces, and rumours were abroad of the vast warlike preparations which were being made, all which stirred my heart and filled me with a longing to take part in the campaign which was expected; and though I had reason to believe, and almost certain promises, that on the first opportunity that presented itself I should be promoted to be captain, I preferred to leave all and betake myself, as I did, to Italy; and it was my good fortune that Don John had just arrived at Genoa, and was going on to Naples to join the Venetian fleet, as he afterwards did at Messina. I may say, in short, that I took part in that glorious expedition, promoted by this time to be a captain of infantry, to which honourable charge my good luck rather than my merits raised me; and that day--so fortunate for Christendom, because then all the nations of the earth were disabused of the error under which they lay in imagining the Turks to be invincible on sea--on that day, I say, on which the Ottoman pride and arrogance were broken, among all that were there made happy (for the Christians who died that day were happier than those who remained alive and victorious) I alone was miserable; for, instead of some naval crown that I might have expected had it been in Roman times, on the night that followed that famous day I found myself with fetters on my feet and manacles on my hands.","내가 아버지의 집을 떠난 지도 이제 한 스물두 해가 되었는데, 그동안 편지를 여러 통 썼는데도 아버지나 형제들에 관한 소식은 전혀 듣지 못하였다. 그 세월 동안 나 자신이 겪은 모험을 이제 간략히 이야기하겠다. 나는 알리칸테에서 배를 타고 순조로운 항해 끝에 제노바에 닿았고, 거기서 밀라노로 나아가 거기서 무기와 약간의 군인 장비를 갖추었다. 거기서 나는 피에몬테로 가서 복무할 생각이었으나, 이미 알레산드리아 델라 팔리아로 가는 길에 들어섰을 때, 위대한 알바 공작이 플랑드르로 가는 중이라는 소식을 들었다. 나는 계획을 바꾸어 그에게 합류하여 그가 벌인 전역에서 그의 휘하에 복무하였고, 에흐몬트 백작과 호른 백작의 죽음을 곁에서 보았으며, 과달라하라의 이름난 대장 디에고 데 우르비나라는 분 밑에서 기수로 승진하였다. 내가 플랑드르에 도착한 지 얼마 뒤, 복된 기억의 교황 성하 피우스 5세께서 공동의 적인 튀르크인에 맞서 베네치아 및 스페인과 동맹을 맺었다는 소식이 전해졌다. 그 튀르크인은 마침 그때 베네치아인의 소유였던 이름난 키프로스 섬을 함대로 빼앗았으니, 통탄스럽고 참담한 손실이었다. 우리 인자하신 임금 펠리페 폐하의 서출 동생인 지존하신 돈 후안 데 아우스트리아께서 동맹군의 총사령관으로 오신다는 것이 사실로 알려졌고, 엄청난 전쟁 준비가 이루어지고 있다는 소문이 떠돌았으니, 이 모든 것이 내 가슴을 들쑤셔 예상되는 전역에 참가하고 싶은 갈망으로 나를 가득 채웠다. 비록 첫 기회가 닿는 대로 대장으로 승진하리라 믿을 만한 이유가, 거의 확실한 약속이 있었음에도, 나는 모든 것을 버리고 이탈리아로 가는 쪽을 택하여 실제로 그리하였다. 그리고 운 좋게도 돈 후안께서 마침 제노바에 막 도착하시어 나폴리로 가서 베네치아 함대에 합류하려 하셨으니, 훗날 메시나에서 실제로 그리하셨다. 한마디로, 나는 그 영광스러운 원정에 참가하였는데, 그때쯤에는 보병 대장으로 승진하여 있었으니, 내 공로보다는 내 행운이 나를 그 명예로운 직책으로 끌어올린 것이었다. 그리고 그날 — 그리스도교 세계에 그토록 다행스러웠던 그날, 그때 비로소 땅 위의 모든 민족이 튀르크인이 바다에서 무적이라 여기던 그 오류에서 깨어났기 때문이다 — 내가 말하건대 오스만의 오만과 거만이 꺾인 그날, 거기서 행복을 얻은 모든 이들 가운데(그날 죽은 그리스도교도들은 살아남아 승리한 자들보다 더 행복하였으니) 나 홀로 비참하였다. 로마 시대였다면 내가 기대하였을 법한 어떤 해전의 관(冠) 대신, 그 이름난 날의 뒤를 이은 밤에 나는 발에 족쇄를 차고 손에 수갑을 찬 신세가 되어 있었던 것이다."]],[["It happened in this way: El Uchali, the king of Algiers, a daring and successful corsair, having attacked and taken the leading Maltese galley (only three knights being left alive in it, and they badly wounded), the chief galley of John Andrea, on board of which I and my company were placed, came to its relief, and doing as was bound to do in such a case, I leaped on board the enemy’s galley, which, sheering off from that which had attacked it, prevented my men from following me, and so I found myself alone in the midst of my enemies, who were in such numbers that I was unable to resist; in short I was taken, covered with wounds; El Uchali, as you know, sirs, made his escape with his entire squadron, and I was left a prisoner in his power, the only sad being among so many filled with joy, and the only captive among so many free; for there were fifteen thousand Christians, all at the oar in the Turkish fleet, that regained their longed-for liberty that day.","일은 이렇게 일어났다. 알제의 왕이자 대담하고 성공적인 해적인 엘 우찰리가 선두에 선 몰타의 갤리선을 공격하여 빼앗았는데(그 안에는 세 기사만이 살아남았고, 그들도 심한 부상을 입었다), 나와 내 부대가 타고 있던 후안 안드레아의 기함이 그것을 구원하러 다가갔다. 그러한 경우에 마땅히 해야 할 바를 행하여, 나는 적의 갤리선으로 뛰어올랐는데, 그 배가 공격해 오던 배에서 비켜 가는 바람에 내 부하들이 나를 따라오지 못하게 되었고, 그리하여 나는 적들 한가운데 홀로 남게 되었으니, 그 수가 너무도 많아 도저히 맞설 수가 없었다. 한마디로 나는 온몸에 상처를 입은 채 사로잡혔다. 여러분도 아시다시피 엘 우찰리는 자기 함대 전부를 이끌고 달아났고, 나는 그의 손아귀에 포로로 남겨졌으니, 그토록 많은 기쁨에 찬 이들 가운데 홀로 슬픈 자였고, 그토록 많은 자유로운 이들 가운데 홀로 사로잡힌 자였다. 그날 튀르크 함대에서 노를 젓던 그리스도교도 일만 오천 명이 그토록 갈망하던 자유를 되찾았기 때문이다."]],[["They carried me to Constantinople, where the Grand Turk, Selim, made my master general at sea for having done his duty in the battle and carried off as evidence of his bravery the standard of the Order of Malta.","그들은 나를 콘스탄티노플로 끌고 갔고, 거기서 대(大)튀르크인 셀림은 내 주인을 해전에서 임무를 다하고 그 용맹의 증거로 몰타 기사단의 군기를 빼앗아 온 공로로 해군 총사령관에 앉혔다."],["The following year, which was the year seventy-two, I found myself at Navarino rowing in the leading galley with the three lanterns.","그 이듬해, 곧 칠십이 년에 나는 나바리노에서 등불 셋을 단 선두 갤리선에서 노를 젓고 있었다."],["There I saw and observed how the opportunity of capturing the whole Turkish fleet in harbour was lost; for all the marines and janizzaries that belonged to it made sure that they were about to be attacked inside the very harbour, and had their kits and pasamaques, or shoes, ready to flee at once on shore without waiting to be assailed, in so great fear did they stand of our fleet.","거기서 나는 항구 안에 든 튀르크 함대 전부를 사로잡을 기회가 어떻게 사라졌는지를 보고 또 지켜보았다. 거기 속한 모든 해병과 예니체리들이 바로 그 항구 안에서 공격을 받으리라 확신하고는, 공격당하기를 기다리지도 않고 즉시 뭍으로 달아나려고 짐과 파사마케스, 곧 신발을 준비해 두고 있었으니, 그만큼 우리 함대를 크게 두려워하고 있었던 것이다."],["But Heaven ordered it otherwise, not for any fault or neglect of the general who commanded on our side, but for the sins of Christendom, and because it was God’s will and pleasure that we should always have instruments of punishment to chastise us.","그러나 하늘은 그것을 달리 정하셨으니, 우리 편을 지휘한 사령관의 어떤 잘못이나 태만 때문이 아니라, 그리스도교 세계의 죄 때문이요, 우리를 징벌할 회초리를 우리가 늘 지니게 하시는 것이 하느님의 뜻이요 기쁨이었기 때문이다."],["As it was, El Uchali took refuge at Modon, which is an island near Navarino, and landing forces fortified the mouth of the harbour and waited quietly until Don John retired.","사실대로 말하자면, 엘 우찰리는 나바리노 가까운 섬인 모돈으로 피신하여, 병력을 상륙시켜 항구 어귀를 요새화하고는 돈 후안이 물러갈 때까지 조용히 기다렸다."],["On this expedition was taken the galley called the Prize, whose captain was a son of the famous corsair Barbarossa.","이 원정에서는 '프레미오'라 불리는 갤리선이 나포되었는데, 그 선장은 이름난 해적 바르바로사의 아들이었다."],["It was taken by the chief Neapolitan galley called the She-wolf, commanded by that thunderbolt of war, that father of his men, that successful and unconquered captain Don Alvaro de Bazan, Marquis of Santa Cruz; and I cannot help telling you what took place at the capture of the Prize.","그것을 나포한 것은 '암늑대'라 불리는 나폴리의 기함으로, 전쟁의 벼락이자 부하들의 아버지요 성공적이며 한 번도 정복당하지 않은 대장, 산타크루스 후작 돈 알바로 데 바산이 지휘하는 배였다. 나는 그 '프레미오'의 나포 때 일어난 일을 여러분께 말씀드리지 않을 수 없다."]],[["The son of Barbarossa was so cruel, and treated his slaves so badly, that, when those who were at the oars saw that the She-wolf galley was bearing down upon them and gaining upon them, they all at once dropped their oars and seized their captain who stood on the stage at the end of the gangway shouting to them to row lustily; and passing him on from bench to bench, from the poop to the prow, they so bit him that before he had got much past the mast his soul had already got to hell; so great, as I said, was the cruelty with which he treated them, and the hatred with which they hated him.","바르바로사의 아들은 어찌나 잔인하고 노예들을 어찌나 모질게 다루었던지, 노를 젓던 자들이 '암늑대' 갤리선이 자기들을 향해 짓쳐 와 거리를 좁혀 오는 것을 보자, 일제히 노를 놓아 버리고는, 통로 끝 발판 위에 서서 힘껏 노를 저으라고 고함치던 자기 선장을 붙잡아, 고물에서 이물까지 자리에서 자리로 넘겨 가며 어찌나 물어뜯었던지, 그가 돛대를 채 많이 지나기도 전에 그 영혼은 이미 지옥에 가 있었다. 내가 말한 대로, 그가 그들을 다룬 잔인함과 그들이 그를 미워한 증오가 그만큼 컸던 것이다."]],[["We returned to Constantinople, and the following year, seventy-three, it became known that Don John had seized Tunis and taken the kingdom from the Turks, and placed Muley Hamet in possession, putting an end to the hopes which Muley Hamida, the cruelest and bravest Moor in the world, entertained of returning to reign there.","우리는 콘스탄티노플로 돌아왔고, 그 이듬해 칠십삼 년에 돈 후안이 튀니스를 빼앗아 튀르크인에게서 그 왕국을 거두고 물레이 하메트를 그 자리에 앉혀, 세상에서 가장 잔인하고 가장 용맹한 무어인 물레이 하미다가 거기서 다시 다스리고자 품던 희망에 종지부를 찍었다는 것이 알려졌다."],["The Grand Turk took the loss greatly to heart, and with the cunning which all his race possess, he made peace with the Venetians (who were much more eager for it than he was), and the following year, seventy-four, he attacked the Goletta and the fort which Don John had left half built near Tunis.","대튀르크인은 그 손실을 몹시 가슴 아파하였고, 그 종족 모두가 지닌 교활함으로 베네치아인과 화친을 맺었으며(그들이 그보다 화친에 훨씬 더 열을 올렸다), 그 이듬해 칠십사 년에 골레타와 돈 후안이 튀니스 가까이에 반쯤 짓다 만 요새를 공격하였다. 이 모든 일이 벌어지는 동안 나는 자유에 대한 아무런 희망도 없이 노에 매여 일하고 있었다."],["While all these events were occurring, I was labouring at the oar without any hope of freedom; at least I had no hope of obtaining it by ransom, for I was firmly resolved not to write to my father telling him of my misfortunes.","적어도 몸값으로 자유를 얻을 희망은 없었으니, 내 불행을 알리는 편지를 아버지께 쓰지 않겠다고 굳게 결심하고 있었기 때문이다. 마침내 골레타가 함락되고 요새가 함락되었는데, 그 진지들 앞에는 정규 튀르크 병사 칠만 오천 명과, 아프리카 곳곳에서 온 사만 명이 넘는 무어인과 아랍인이 있었으며, 이 대군의 행렬에는 그토록 많은 군수품과 전쟁 기계, 그리고 그토록 많은 공병이 따라와 그들이 맨손으로도 한 줌씩 흙을 던져 골레타와 요새를 덮어 버릴 수 있을 정도였다."],["At length the Goletta fell, and the fort fell, before which places there were seventy-five thousand regular Turkish soldiers, and more than four hundred thousand Moors and Arabs from all parts of Africa, and in the train of all this great host such munitions and engines of war, and so many pioneers that with their hands they might have covered the Goletta and the fort with handfuls of earth.","가장 먼저 함락된 것은 골레타였으니, 그때까지 난공불락으로 여겨지던 곳이었다. 그곳이 함락된 것은 할 수 있고 또 마땅히 해야 할 모든 일을 다 한 수비병들의 어떤 잘못 때문이 아니라, 거기 사막의 모래에서는 참호를 얼마나 쉽게 팔 수 있는지를 경험이 증명하였기 때문이다."],["The first to fall was the Goletta, until then reckoned impregnable, and it fell, not by any fault of its defenders, who did all that they could and should have done, but because experiment proved how easily entrenchments could be made in the desert sand there; for water used to be found at two palms depth, while the Turks found none at two yards; and so by means of a quantity of sandbags they raised their works so high that they commanded the walls of the fort, sweeping them as if from a cavalier, so that no one was able to make a stand or maintain the defence.","거기서는 두 뼘 깊이만 파도 물이 나오곤 하였으나, 튀르크인은 두 발(척) 깊이를 파도 물을 찾지 못하였다. 그리하여 그들은 수많은 모래 자루로 자기들 진지를 어찌나 높이 쌓아 올렸던지, 요새의 성벽을 내려다보며 마치 기마대(騎馬臺)에서 쏘듯 성벽을 휩쓸었으니, 아무도 버티고 서거나 방어를 지탱할 수 없었다."]],[["It was a common opinion that our men should not have shut themselves up in the Goletta, but should have waited in the open at the landing-place; but those who say so talk at random and with little knowledge of such matters; for if in the Goletta and in the fort there were barely seven thousand soldiers, how could such a small number, however resolute, sally out and hold their own against numbers like those of the enemy?","우리 편 사람들이 골레타 안에 틀어박힐 것이 아니라 상륙 지점의 탁 트인 곳에서 기다렸어야 했다는 것이 흔한 의견이었다. 그러나 그렇게 말하는 자들은 함부로 지껄이는 것이요 그러한 일에 관한 지식이 거의 없는 것이니, 골레타와 요새 안에 병사가 겨우 칠천 명밖에 없었는데, 그토록 적은 수가 아무리 결연한들 어찌 밖으로 쳐 나가 적의 그런 수효를 상대로 버틸 수 있었겠는가?"],["And how is it possible to help losing a stronghold that is not relieved, above all when surrounded by a host of determined enemies in their own country?","또 구원받지 못하는 요새를, 그것도 자기들 땅에서 결연한 적의 대군에 에워싸인 요새를 어찌 잃지 않을 수 있었겠는가?"],["But many thought, and I thought so too, that it was special favour and mercy which Heaven showed to Spain in permitting the destruction of that source and hiding place of mischief, that devourer, sponge, and moth of countless money, fruitlessly wasted there to no other purpose save preserving the memory of its capture by the invincible Charles V; as if to make that eternal, as it is and will be, these stones were needed to support it.","그러나 많은 이들이 생각하였고 나도 그리 생각하였으니, 그 화근의 근원이요 소굴, 그 게걸스러운 먹보, 해면(海綿), 헤아릴 수 없는 돈을 좀먹는 좀벌레, 무적의 카를 5세께서 그곳을 함락하신 기억을 보존하는 것 말고는 아무런 목적도 없이 부질없이 거기 탕진된 그 돈, 그것의 파괴를 허락하신 것은 하늘이 스페인에 베푸신 각별한 은총이요 자비였다. 마치 그 기억을, 지금도 그러하고 앞으로도 그러할 그 영원함을 떠받치는 데 이 돌들이 필요하기라도 한 양 말이다."],["The fort also fell; but the Turks had to win it inch by inch, for the soldiers who defended it fought so gallantly and stoutly that the number of the enemy killed in twenty-two general assaults exceeded twenty-five thousand.","요새도 함락되었으나, 튀르크인은 그것을 한 치 한 치 따내야만 하였으니, 그것을 지키던 병사들이 어찌나 용맹하고 굳세게 싸웠던지 스물두 차례의 전면 공격에서 죽은 적의 수가 이만 오천을 넘었기 때문이다."],["Of three hundred that remained alive not one was taken unwounded, a clear and manifest proof of their gallantry and resolution, and how sturdily they had defended themselves and held their post.","살아남은 삼백 명 가운데 부상 없이 사로잡힌 자는 하나도 없었으니, 그들이 얼마나 용맹하고 결연하였는지, 또 얼마나 굳세게 스스로를 지키고 자기 자리를 지켜 냈는지의 분명하고 뚜렷한 증거였다."],["A small fort or tower which was in the middle of the lagoon under the command of Don Juan Zanoguera, a Valencian gentleman and a famous soldier, capitulated upon terms.","석호 한가운데 있던, 발렌시아의 신사이자 이름난 군인 돈 후안 사노게라의 지휘 아래 있던 작은 요새 혹은 망루는 조건을 걸고 항복하였다."],["They took prisoner Don Pedro Puertocarrero, commandant of the Goletta, who had done all in his power to defend his fortress, and took the loss of it so much to heart that he died of grief on the way to Constantinople, where they were carrying him a prisoner.","그들은 자기 요새를 지키려 온 힘을 다하였고 그것을 잃은 것을 어찌나 가슴 아파하였던지 포로로 콘스탄티노플로 끌려가던 도중 슬픔에 겨워 죽고 만 골레타의 사령관 돈 페드로 푸에르토카레로를 사로잡았다. 그들은 또 요새의 사령관인 가브리오 세르베욘이라는 자도 사로잡았는데, 밀라노의 신사로 뛰어난 공병이자 매우 용감한 군인이었다."],["They also took the commandant of the fort, Gabrio Cerbellon by name, a Milanese gentleman, a great engineer and a very brave soldier.","이 두 요새에서는 명망 있는 인물이 많이 스러졌으니, 그 가운데 성 요한 기사단의 기사 파가노 도리아가 있었다."],["In these two fortresses perished many persons of note, among whom was Pagano Doria, knight of the Order of St.","그는 너그러운 기질의 인물이었으니, 그 이름난 형 후안 안드레아 도리아에게 베푼 지극한 후함으로 그것이 드러났다."],["John, a man of generous disposition, as was shown by his extreme liberality to his brother, the famous John Andrea Doria; and what made his death the more sad was that he was slain by some Arabs to whom, seeing that the fort was now lost, he entrusted himself, and who offered to conduct him in the disguise of a Moor to Tabarca, a small fort or station on the coast held by the Genoese employed in the coral fishery.","그의 죽음을 더욱 슬프게 한 것은, 이제 요새가 함락된 것을 보고 그가 몸을 의탁한 어떤 아랍인들에게 죽임을 당하였다는 사실이다. 그들은 그를 무어인으로 변장시켜, 산호 채취에 종사하는 제노바인들이 차지하고 있던 해안의 작은 요새 혹은 거점인 타바르카까지 데려다 주겠다고 제안하였다."],["These Arabs cut off his head and carried it to the commander of the Turkish fleet, who proved on them the truth of our Castilian proverb, that “though the treason may please, the traitor is hated;”","이 아랍인들은 그의 목을 베어 튀르크 함대 사령관에게 가져갔는데, 그 사령관은 그들에게 우리 카스티야 속담, 곧 \"반역은 마음에 들지언정 반역자는 미움을 받는다\"는 말이 참됨을 입증해 보였다."],["for they say he ordered those who brought him the present to be hanged for not having brought him alive.","그 선물을 가져온 자들을 그를 산 채로 데려오지 않았다는 이유로 목매달라 명하였다고들 하기 때문이다."]],[["Among the Christians who were taken in the fort was one named Don Pedro de Aguilar, a native of some place, I know not what, in Andalusia, who had been ensign in the fort, a soldier of great repute and rare intelligence, who had in particular a special gift for what they call poetry.","요새에서 사로잡힌 그리스도교도들 가운데 돈 페드로 데 아길라르라는 이가 있었는데, 안달루시아의 어느 곳인지는 모르겠으나 그 출신으로, 요새의 기수였으며, 명망이 높고 보기 드문 지성을 갖춘 군인이요, 특히 이른바 시(詩)에 각별한 재능을 지닌 자였다. 내가 이렇게 말하는 까닭은 그의 운명이 그를 내 갤리선, 내 노 젓는 자리로 데려와 같은 주인의 노예로 만들었기 때문이다."],["I say so because his fate brought him to my galley and to my bench, and made him a slave to the same master; and before we left the port this gentleman composed two sonnets by way of epitaphs, one on the Goletta and the other on the fort; indeed, I may as well repeat them, for I have them by heart, and I think they will be liked rather than disliked.","그리고 우리가 그 항구를 떠나기 전 이 신사는 비문(碑文) 삼아 소네트 두 편을 지었으니, 하나는 골레타에 관한 것이요 다른 하나는 요새에 관한 것이었다. 사실 내가 그것을 읊어 드리는 것도 좋겠으니, 외고 있는 데다, 싫어하기보다는 좋아하시리라 생각하기 때문이다."]],[["The instant the captive mentioned the name of Don Pedro de Aguilar, Don Fernando looked at his companions and they all three smiled; and when he came to speak of the sonnets one of them said, “Before your worship proceeds any further I entreat you to tell me what became of that Don Pedro de Aguilar you have spoken of.”","포로가 돈 페드로 데 아길라르라는 이름을 입에 올리는 순간, 돈 페르난도는 일행을 바라보았고 세 사람 모두 미소 지었다. 그리고 그가 소네트 이야기에 이르렀을 때 그들 가운데 하나가 말하였다. \"더 이야기를 이어 가시기 전에, 말씀하신 그 돈 페드로 데 아길라르가 어찌 되었는지 제게 일러 주시기를 청합니다.\""]],[["“All I know is,” replied the captive, “that after having been in Constantinople two years, he escaped in the disguise of an Arnaut, in company with a Greek spy; but whether he regained his liberty or not I cannot tell, though I fancy he did, because a year afterwards I saw the Greek at Constantinople, though I was unable to ask him what the result of the journey was.”","\"제가 아는 것이라고는,\" 포로가 대답하였다. \"그가 콘스탄티노플에서 두 해를 지낸 뒤 어느 그리스인 첩자와 함께 아르나우트로 변장하여 달아났다는 것뿐입니다. 그가 자유를 되찾았는지 어떤지는 말씀드릴 수 없으나, 그러하였으리라 짐작합니다. 한 해 뒤에 콘스탄티노플에서 그 그리스인을 보았기 때문인데, 다만 그 여정의 결말이 어찌 되었는지는 물어볼 수가 없었습니다.\""]],[["“Well then, you are right,”","\"그렇다면 그대 말이 옳습니다.\""],["returned the gentleman, “for that Don Pedro is my brother, and he is now in our village in good health, rich, married, and with three children.”","그 신사가 대답하였다. \"그 돈 페드로는 제 형제이니, 지금 우리 마을에서 건강히, 부유하게, 결혼하여 세 자식을 두고 지내고 있습니다.\""]],[["“Thanks be to God for all the mercies he has shown him,”","\"그분께 베푸신 모든 자비에 대해 하느님께 감사드립니다.\""],["said the captive; “for to my mind there is no happiness on earth to compare with recovering lost liberty.”","포로가 말하였다. \"제 생각에는 잃었던 자유를 되찾는 것에 견줄 만한 행복이 이 땅에는 없기 때문입니다.\""]],[["“And what is more,”","\"게다가 더한 것은,\""],["said the gentleman, “I know the sonnets my brother made.”","그 신사가 말하였다. \"제 형제가 지은 그 소네트들을 제가 알고 있다는 것입니다.\""]],[["“Then let your worship repeat them,”","\"그렇다면 그것을 읊어 주십시오.\""],["said the captive, “for you will recite them better than I can.”","포로가 말하였다. \"저보다 그대께서 더 잘 읊으실 테니 말입니다.\""]],[["“With all my heart,”","\"기꺼이 그러지요.\""],["said the gentleman; “that on the Goletta runs thus.”","그 신사가 말하였다. \"골레타에 관한 것은 이렇습니다.\""]],[["# CHAPTER XL. IN WHICH THE STORY OF THE CAPTIVE IS CONTINUED","# CHAPTER XL."]],[["“Blest souls, that, from this mortal husk set free, In guerdon of brave deeds beatified, Above this lowly orb of ours abide Made heirs of heaven and immortality, With noble rage and ardour glowing ye Your strength, while strength was yours, in battle plied, And with your own blood and the foeman’s dyed The sandy soil and the encircling sea.","“이 죽음의 껍데기에서 벗어나 자유로워진 복된 영혼들이여, 용맹한 행적의 보답으로 축복받아, 우리네 이 비천한 땅덩이를 넘어 저 위에 머물러 하늘과 불멸의 상속자가 되었도다, 고귀한 분노와 불타는 열정으로 빛나며 그대들은 힘이 있는 동안 그 힘을 전장에서 떨쳤고, 그대들 자신의 피와 적의 피로 모래 흙과 사방을 두른 바다를 물들였노라."],["It was the ebbing life-blood first that failed The weary arms; the stout hearts never quailed.","먼저 다한 것은 지쳐 가던 핏기였으니 지친 팔이 먼저 꺾였을 뿐, 굳센 심장은 결코 떨지 않았도다."],["Though vanquished, yet ye earned the victor’s crown: Though mourned, yet still triumphant was your fall For there ye won, between the sword and wall, In Heaven glory and on earth renown.”","패하였어도 그대들은 승자의 관을 얻었으며, 애도받으면서도 그대들의 쓰러짐은 여전히 개선이었으니, 칼과 성벽 사이에서 그대들은 그곳에서 하늘에서는 영광을, 땅에서는 명성을 얻었음이라.”"],["“That is it exactly, according to my recollection,”","“바로 그것이오, 내 기억으로는.”"],["said the captive.","포로가 말했다."]],[["“Well then, that on the fort,”","“그렇다면 요새에 관한 것은,”"],["said the gentleman, “if my memory serves me, goes thus:","그 신사가 말했다, “내 기억이 맞다면 이렇게 가오."]],[["“Up from this wasted soil, this shattered shell, Whose walls and towers here in ruin lie, Three thousand soldier souls took wing on high, In the bright mansions of the blest to dwell.","“황폐해진 이 땅, 산산이 부서진 이 껍데기, 그 성벽과 망루가 여기 폐허로 누워 있는 이곳으로부터 삼천의 병사 혼들이 높이 날아올라, 복된 자들의 밝은 거처에 깃들었도다."],["The onslaught of the foeman to repel By might of arm all vainly did they try, And when at length ‘twas left them but to die, Wearied and few the last defenders fell.","적의 맹습을 물리치고자 팔의 힘으로 헛되이 애를 다 썼으나, 마침내 죽는 일 외에는 남은 것이 없게 되었을 때, 지치고 얼마 남지 않은 마지막 수호자들이 쓰러졌노라."],["And this same arid soil hath ever been A haunt of countless mournful memories, As well in our day as in days of yore.","그리고 이 메마른 땅은 예나 지금이나 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 슬픈 추억의 터전이었으되, 우리 시대에도 옛 시절에도 한결같았도다."],["But never yet to Heaven it sent, I ween, From its hard bosom purer souls than these, Or braver bodies on its surface bore.”","허나 그 단단한 품에서 하늘로 보낸 것 중에 이들보다 더 순결한 혼은 없었으리라, 짐작건대, 또한 그 표면에 이보다 더 용맹한 육신을 떠받친 적도 없었으리라.”"]],[["The sonnets were not disliked, and the captive was rejoiced at the tidings they gave him of his comrade, and continuing his tale, he went on to say:","소네트들은 미움을 받지 않았고, 포로는 그것들이 전해 주는 자기 전우의 소식에 기뻐하며, 이야기를 이어 가 이렇게 말했다."]],[["The Goletta and the fort being thus in their hands, the Turks gave orders to dismantle the Goletta--for the fort was reduced to such a state that there was nothing left to level--and to do the work more quickly and easily they mined it in three places; but nowhere were they able to blow up the part which seemed to be the least strong, that is to say, the old walls, while all that remained standing of the new fortifications that the Fratin had made came to the ground with the greatest ease.","골레타와 요새가 이렇게 그들의 손에 들어가자, 튀르크인들은 골레타를 헐어 버리라는 명령을 내렸으니 — 요새는 이미 평평하게 할 것이 하나도 남지 않을 만큼 무너져 버린 상태였기 때문이오 — 일을 더 빠르고 쉽게 하려고 세 군데에 폭약을 묻었으나, 가장 약해 보이던 부분, 곧 옛 성벽만은 어디서도 날려 버릴 수가 없었고, 반면 프라틴이 쌓아 올린 새 방어 시설 가운데 서 있던 것은 모조리 더없이 쉽게 땅바닥에 주저앉았소."],["Finally the fleet returned victorious and triumphant to Constantinople, and a few months later died my master, El Uchali, otherwise Uchali Fartax, which means in Turkish “the scabby renegade;”","마침내 함대는 승리와 개선을 안고 콘스탄티노플로 돌아갔고, 몇 달 뒤 내 주인이 죽었으니, 곧 엘 우찰리, 다른 이름으로는 우찰리 파르탁스라 불리는 자인데, 그것은 튀르크 말로 \"딱지투성이 배교자\"라는 뜻이오. 실제로 그러했소."],["for that he was; it is the practice with the Turks to name people from some defect or virtue they may possess; the reason being that there are among them only four surnames belonging to families tracing their descent from the Ottoman house, and the others, as I have said, take their names and surnames either from bodily blemishes or moral qualities. This “scabby one”","튀르크인들은 사람이 지닌 어떤 결점이나 미덕을 따서 이름을 붙이는 관습이 있는데, 그 까닭은 그들 가운데 오스만 가문에서 혈통을 잇는 가문에 속한 성은 단 네 개뿐이고, 나머지는 내가 말한 대로 신체의 흠이나 도덕적 자질에서 이름과 성을 따 오기 때문이오."],["rowed at the oar as a slave of the Grand Signor’s for fourteen years, and when over thirty-four years of age, in resentment at having been struck by a Turk while at the oar, turned renegade and renounced his faith in order to be able to revenge himself; and such was his valour that, without owing his advancement to the base ways and means by which most favourites of the Grand Signor rise to power, he came to be king of Algiers, and afterwards general-on-sea, which is the third place of trust in the realm.","이 \"딱지투성이\"는 십사 년 동안 대제후의 노예로 노를 저었고, 서른네 살이 넘었을 무렵, 노를 젓던 중에 어느 튀르크인에게 얻어맞은 데 앙심을 품고는, 복수할 수 있도록 배교하여 자기 신앙을 저버렸소. 그리고 그 용맹이 어찌나 대단했던지, 대제후의 총신 대부분이 권력에 오르는 그 비천한 방편과 수단에 기대지 않고도 알제의 왕이 되었고, 그 후에는 해군 총사령관이 되었으니, 이는 그 왕국에서 세 번째로 높은 신임의 자리요."],["He was a Calabrian by birth, and a worthy man morally, and he treated his slaves with great humanity.","그는 태생이 칼라브리아 사람으로 도덕적으로는 훌륭한 인물이었으며, 노예들을 매우 인간적으로 대했소."],["He had three thousand of them, and after his death they were divided, as he directed by his will, between the Grand Signor (who is heir of all who die and shares with the children of the deceased) and his renegades.","그에게는 노예가 삼천 명 있었는데, 그가 죽은 뒤 유언으로 지시한 대로 대제후(죽는 모든 자의 상속자가 되어 고인의 자식들과 함께 나누어 가지는 자요)와 그의 배교자들 사이에 나뉘었소. 나는 어느 베네치아 출신 배교자의 몫으로 떨어졌는데, 그자는 배에서 사환 노릇을 하던 중 우찰리에게 붙잡혀 그토록 총애를 받아 그의 가장 사랑받는 젊은이 가운데 하나가 되었던 인물이오."],["I fell to the lot of a Venetian renegade who, when a cabin boy on board a ship, had been taken by Uchali and was so much beloved by him that he became one of his most favoured youths.","그는 내가 본 가운데 가장 잔인한 배교자가 되었으니, 이름은 하산 아가요, 큰 부자가 되어 알제의 왕이 되었소. 나는 그와 함께 콘스탄티노플에서 그곳으로 갔는데, 스페인에 그렇게 가까이 가게 되어 차라리 기뻤소."],["He came to be the most cruel renegade I ever saw: his name was Hassan Aga, and he grew very rich and became king of Algiers.","내 불행한 처지를 누군가에게 편지로 알리려는 뜻에서가 아니라, 콘스탄티노플에서보다 알제에서 운이 좀 더 너그러워질지 시험해 보려는 것이었소. 콘스탄티노플에서는 천 가지 방법으로 달아나려 했으나 끝내 적당한 때나 기회를 찾지 못했기 때문이오."],["With him I went there from Constantinople, rather glad to be so near Spain, not that I intended to write to anyone about my unhappy lot, but to try if fortune would be kinder to me in Algiers than in Constantinople, where I had attempted in a thousand ways to escape without ever finding a favourable time or chance; but in Algiers I resolved to seek for other means of effecting the purpose I cherished so dearly; for the hope of obtaining my liberty never deserted me; and when in my plots and schemes and attempts the result did not answer my expectations, without giving way to despair I immediately began to look out for or conjure up some new hope to support me, however faint or feeble it might be.","그러나 알제에서는 내가 그토록 간절히 품어 온 목적을 이룰 다른 수단을 찾아보기로 결심했으니, 자유를 얻으리라는 희망이 결코 나를 떠난 적이 없었기 때문이오. 그래서 내 음모와 계략과 시도가 기대에 부응하지 못했을 때도, 절망에 빠지는 일 없이 나는 곧장 나를 지탱해 줄 어떤 새로운 희망을, 그것이 아무리 희미하고 가냘프더라도 찾거나 지어내기 시작했소."]],[["In this way I lived on immured in a building or prison called by the Turks a bano in which they confine the Christian captives, as well those that are the king’s as those belonging to private individuals, and also what they call those of the Almacen, which is as much as to say the slaves of the municipality, who serve the city in the public works and other employments; but captives of this kind recover their liberty with great difficulty, for, as they are public property and have no particular master, there is no one with whom to treat for their ransom, even though they may have the means. To these banos, as I have said, some private individuals of the town are in the habit of bringing their captives, especially when they are to be ransomed; because there they can keep them in safety and comfort until their ransom arrives. The king’s captives also, that are on ransom, do not go out to work with the rest of the crew, unless when their ransom is delayed; for then, to make them write for it more pressingly, they compel them to work and go for wood, which is no light labour.","이렇게 나는 튀르크인들이 바뇨라 부르는 건물, 곧 감옥에 갇혀 살았는데, 거기에는 그리스도교도 포로들을 가두어 두니, 왕에게 속한 자들이나 사사로운 개인에게 속한 자들이나, 또 그들이 알마센이라 부르는 자들도 함께였소. 알마센이란 말하자면 시 소속 노예들로, 공공 토목 공사나 그 밖의 일에 동원되어 도시에 봉사하는 자들이오. 다만 이런 부류의 포로들은 자유를 되찾기가 몹시 어려우니, 그들은 공공의 재산이라 특정한 주인이 없어, 설령 몸값을 치를 방편이 있다 해도 그 몸값을 두고 흥정할 상대가 없기 때문이오. 내가 말했듯이 이 바뇨에는 마을의 사사로운 개인들이 자기 포로들을, 특히 몸값을 받고 풀어 줄 포로들을 데려다 두는 관습이 있었소. 거기서라면 몸값이 도착할 때까지 그들을 안전하고 편안하게 둘 수 있었기 때문이오. 몸값이 걸린 왕의 포로들 역시 다른 선원들과 함께 일하러 나가지 않았으나, 몸값이 지체될 경우에는 예외였소. 그럴 때는 몸값을 더 다급하게 재촉하는 편지를 쓰게 하려고 그들을 일하게 하고 땔나무를 하러 보냈는데, 그것은 결코 가벼운 노역이 아니었소."]],[["I, however, was one of those on ransom, for when it was discovered that I was a captain, although I declared my scanty means and want of fortune, nothing could dissuade them from including me among the gentlemen and those waiting to be ransomed. They put a chain on me, more as a mark of this than to keep me safe, and so I passed my life in that bano with several other gentlemen and persons of quality marked out as held to ransom; but though at times, or rather almost always, we suffered from hunger and scanty clothing, nothing distressed us so much as hearing and seeing at every turn the unexampled and unheard-of cruelties my master inflicted upon the Christians. Every day he hanged a man, impaled one, cut off the ears of another; and all with so little provocation, or so entirely without any, that the Turks acknowledged he did it merely for the sake of doing it, and because he was by nature murderously disposed towards the whole human race. The only one that fared at all well with him was a Spanish soldier, something de Saavedra by name, to whom he never gave a blow himself, or ordered a blow to be given, or addressed a hard word, although he had done things that will dwell in the memory of the people there for many a year, and all to recover his liberty; and for the least of the many things he did we all dreaded that he would be impaled, and he himself was in fear of it more than once; and only that time does not allow, I could tell you now something of what that soldier did, that would interest and astonish you much more than the narration of my own tale.","그러나 나는 몸값이 걸린 포로 가운데 하나였소. 내가 대위라는 것이 발각되자, 비록 내 빈약한 형편과 재산 없음을 밝혔건만, 무엇으로도 그들을 설득하여 나를 신사들과 몸값을 기다리는 자들 무리에서 빼낼 수가 없었기 때문이오. 그들은 내게 사슬을 채웠는데, 안전하게 붙잡아 두려는 것보다는 이런 표시로서의 의미가 더 컸소. 그렇게 나는 그 바뇨에서 몸값에 걸린 자로 지목된 다른 여러 신사와 지체 높은 사람들과 더불어 삶을 이어 갔소. 그러나 때때로, 아니 거의 언제나 우리는 굶주림과 헐벗음에 시달렸으되, 무엇보다 우리를 괴롭힌 것은 내 주인이 그리스도교도들에게 가하는, 유례없고 듣도 보도 못한 잔혹함을 사사건건 듣고 보는 일이었소. 그는 날마다 한 사람을 목매달고, 한 사람을 꼬챙이에 꿰고, 또 한 사람의 귀를 잘랐소. 그것도 어찌나 하찮은 도발에, 아니 전혀 아무런 도발도 없이 그러했던지, 튀르크인들조차 그가 그저 그 짓을 하기 위해, 또 천성이 온 인류를 향해 살의에 차 있어서 그러는 것이라고 인정했소. 그자에게서 그나마 잘 대접받은 유일한 사람은 데 사아베드라라는 이름의 어느 스페인 병사였는데, 그자는 결코 그에게 직접 매를 들거나 매를 들게 시키거나 모진 말 한마디 던지지 않았소. 그가 자기 자유를 되찾으려고, 그곳 사람들의 기억 속에 여러 해 남을 만한 일들을 저질렀건만 말이오. 그가 한 그 많은 일 가운데 가장 사소한 것만으로도 우리는 모두 그가 꼬챙이에 꿰이지나 않을까 두려워했고, 그 자신도 한두 번 그것을 겁냈소. 다만 시간이 허락지 않으니 그렇지, 그 병사가 한 일에 관해서는 지금 내 이야기를 늘어놓는 것보다 여러분에게 훨씬 더 흥미롭고 놀라울 무언가를 들려 드릴 수 있을 것이오."]],[["To go on with my story; the courtyard of our prison was overlooked by the windows of the house belonging to a wealthy Moor of high position; and these, as is usual in Moorish houses, were rather loopholes than windows, and besides were covered with thick and close lattice-work.","이야기를 계속하자면, 우리 감옥의 안뜰은 지체 높은 어느 부유한 무어인의 집 창문들에서 내려다보였소. 그 창문들은 무어인 집에서 흔히 그렇듯 창이라기보다는 총안에 가까웠고, 게다가 두껍고 촘촘한 격자로 덮여 있었소."],["It so happened, then, that as I was one day on the terrace of our prison with three other comrades, trying, to pass away the time, how far we could leap with our chains, we being alone, for all the other Christians had gone out to work, I chanced to raise my eyes, and from one of these little closed windows I saw a reed appear with a cloth attached to the end of it, and it kept waving to and fro, and moving as if making signs to us to come and take it.","그러던 어느 날, 내가 다른 세 전우와 함께 우리 감옥 옥상에서 시간을 보내려고 사슬을 찬 채 얼마나 멀리 뛸 수 있는지를 겨루고 있었는데 — 다른 그리스도교도들은 모두 일하러 나가고 우리만 있었소 — 마침 눈을 들어 보니, 그 작고 닫힌 창문 가운데 하나에서 갈대가 하나 나타났고 그 끝에는 천이 매여 있었으며, 그것이 이리저리 흔들리며 우리더러 와서 가져가라고 신호하는 듯 움직였소."],["We watched it, and one of those who were with me went and stood under the reed to see whether they would let it drop, or what they would do, but as he did so the reed was raised and moved from side to side, as if they meant to say “no”","우리는 그것을 지켜보았고, 나와 함께 있던 자들 가운데 하나가 가서 갈대 아래에 서서 그것을 떨어뜨릴지, 아니면 어떻게 하는지를 보려 했소. 그러나 그가 그러자 갈대는 들어 올려져 좌우로 흔들렸으니, 마치 고개를 저어 \"아니다\"라고 말하려는 듯했소."],["by a shake of the head. The Christian came back, and it was again lowered, making the same movements as before.","그 그리스도교도가 돌아오자 갈대는 다시 내려졌고 전과 같은 움직임을 했소."],["Another of my comrades went, and with him the same happened as with the first, and then the third went forward, but with the same result as the first and second.","내 다른 전우 하나가 갔으나 첫째와 똑같은 일이 벌어졌고, 그다음에 셋째가 나아갔으되 첫째와 둘째와 똑같은 결과였소."],["Seeing this I did not like not to try my luck, and as soon as I came under the reed it was dropped and fell inside the bano at my feet.","이를 보고 나는 내 운을 시험해 보고 싶어졌고, 내가 갈대 아래로 다가서자마자 그것이 떨어져 내 발치에, 바뇨 안쪽으로 떨어졌소."],["I hastened to untie the cloth, in which I perceived a knot, and in this were ten cianis, which are coins of base gold, current among the Moors, and each worth ten reals of our money.","나는 서둘러 천을 풀었는데, 거기 매듭이 하나 있는 것을 알아챘고, 그 안에는 시아니 열 닢이 들어 있었소. 그것은 무어인들 사이에 통용되는 질 낮은 금화로, 한 닢이 우리 돈으로 십 레알 값어치가 나가는 것이었소."]],[["It is needless to say I rejoiced over this godsend, and my joy was not less than my wonder as I strove to imagine how this good fortune could have come to us, but to me specially; for the evident unwillingness to drop the reed for any but me showed that it was for me the favour was intended.","이 뜻밖의 선물에 내가 기뻐했음은 말할 것도 없거니와, 이 행운이 어찌하여 우리에게, 특히 내게 찾아왔는지 짐작해 보려 애쓰는 동안 그 기쁨은 놀라움 못지않았소. 나를 제외한 누구에게도 갈대를 떨어뜨리지 않으려는 그 명백한 거부가, 그 호의가 나를 향한 것임을 보여 주었기 때문이오."],["I took my welcome money, broke the reed, and returned to the terrace, and looking up at the window, I saw a very white hand put out that opened and shut very quickly.","나는 그 반가운 돈을 받아 갈대를 부러뜨리고 옥상으로 돌아왔는데, 창문을 올려다보니 아주 흰 손 하나가 나와 매우 재빨리 열렸다 닫혔소. 이로써 우리는 이런 친절을 베푼 것이 그 집에 사는 어떤 여인임이 틀림없다고 짐작하거나 상상했고, 우리가 그것을 고마워한다는 것을 보이려고 무어인들의 방식을 따라 머리를 숙이고 몸을 굽히고 두 팔을 가슴에 엇갈려 절을 했소."],["From this we gathered or fancied that it must be some woman living in that house that had done us this kindness, and to show that we were grateful for it, we made salaams after the fashion of the Moors, bowing the head, bending the body, and crossing the arms on the breast.","잠시 후 같은 창문에서 갈대로 만든 작은 십자가가 나왔다가 곧 거두어졌소. 이 표시로 우리는 어떤 그리스도교도 여인이 그 집에 포로로 있으며, 우리에게 그토록 친절을 베푼 이가 바로 그 여인이라고 믿게 되었소."],["Shortly afterwards at the same window a small cross made of reeds was put out and immediately withdrawn.","그러나 우리가 본 그 손의 흰빛과 팔찌들은 그 생각을 떨치게 했으니, 그래도 우리는 그 여인이 주인들이 흔히, 그것도 기꺼이 정실로 삼는 그리스도교도 배교 여인 가운데 하나일지도 모른다고 여겼소. 그들은 그런 여인을 자기 나라 여인보다 더 좋아하기 때문이오."],["This sign led us to believe that some Christian woman was a captive in the house, and that it was she who had been so good to us; but the whiteness of the hand and the bracelets we had perceived made us dismiss that idea, though we thought it might be one of the Christian renegades whom their masters very often take as lawful wives, and gladly, for they prefer them to the women of their own nation.","우리의 온갖 추측은 진실과는 한참 동떨어진 것이었소. 그리하여 그때부터 우리의 유일한 일거리는 십자가가 우리에게 나타났던 그 창문을, 마치 우리의 북극성인 양 지켜보며 응시하는 것이었소."],["In all our conjectures we were wide of the truth; so from that time forward our sole occupation was watching and gazing at the window where the cross had appeared to us, as if it were our pole-star; but at least fifteen days passed without our seeing either it or the hand, or any other sign and though meanwhile we endeavoured with the utmost pains to ascertain who it was that lived in the house, and whether there were any Christian renegade in it, nobody could ever tell us anything more than that he who lived there was a rich Moor of high position, Hadji Morato by name, formerly alcaide of La Pata, an office of high dignity among them.","그러나 적어도 보름이 지나도록 우리는 그것도, 그 손도, 그 어떤 다른 표시도 보지 못했소. 그동안 우리는 그 집에 사는 이가 누구인지, 그 안에 그리스도교도 배교자가 있는지를 알아내려고 온갖 애를 다 썼으나, 거기 사는 자가 하지 모라토라는 이름의 지체 높은 부유한 무어인으로, 전에는 라 파타의 알카이데를 지냈고 그것이 그들 사이에서 매우 높은 지위의 직책이라는 것 외에는 아무도 더 일러 줄 수 있는 자가 없었소."],["But when we least thought it was going to rain any more cianis from that quarter, we saw the reed suddenly appear with another cloth tied in a larger knot attached to it, and this at a time when, as on the former occasion, the bano was deserted and unoccupied.","그런데 그 방면에서 더는 시아니가 비처럼 쏟아지지 않으리라 생각하던 바로 그때, 우리는 갈대가 불쑥 나타나 거기에 또 다른 천이 더 큰 매듭으로 매여 있는 것을 보았으니, 이번에도 앞서처럼 바뇨가 텅 비어 아무도 없을 때였소."]],[["We made trial as before, each of the same three going forward before I did; but the reed was delivered to none but me, and on my approach it was let drop.","우리는 전처럼 시험을 해 보았는데, 나보다 먼저 같은 세 사람이 차례로 나아갔소. 그러나 갈대는 나 말고는 누구에게도 건네지지 않았고, 내가 다가서자 그것이 떨어졌소."],["I untied the knot and I found forty Spanish gold crowns with a paper written in Arabic, and at the end of the writing there was a large cross drawn.","나는 매듭을 풀어 스페인 금화 사십 에스쿠도와 함께 아랍어로 적힌 종이 한 장을 발견했는데, 그 글의 끝에는 큰 십자가가 하나 그려져 있었소. 나는 그 십자가에 입을 맞추고 에스쿠도를 챙긴 뒤 옥상으로 돌아왔고, 우리 모두 절을 했소."],["I kissed the cross, took the crowns and returned to the terrace, and we all made our salaams; again the hand appeared, I made signs that I would read the paper, and then the window was closed.","다시 그 손이 나타났고, 나는 종이를 읽겠다는 신호를 보냈으며, 그러자 창문이 닫혔소. 우리는 벌어진 일에 기쁨이 가득하면서도 모두 어리둥절했소."],["We were all puzzled, though filled with joy at what had taken place; and as none of us understood Arabic, great was our curiosity to know what the paper contained, and still greater the difficulty of finding some one to read it.","우리 가운데 누구도 아랍어를 알지 못했기에, 그 종이에 무엇이 담겨 있는지 알고 싶은 호기심이 컸고, 그것을 읽어 줄 누군가를 찾는 어려움은 더 컸소. 마침내 나는 무르시아 태생의 어느 배교자에게 털어놓기로 결심했소."],["At last I resolved to confide in a renegade, a native of Murcia, who professed a very great friendship for me, and had given pledges that bound him to keep any secret I might entrust to him; for it is the custom with some renegades, when they intend to return to Christian territory, to carry about them certificates from captives of mark testifying, in whatever form they can, that such and such a renegade is a worthy man who has always shown kindness to Christians, and is anxious to escape on the first opportunity that may present itself.","그자는 내게 대단한 우정을 표했고, 내가 맡길지 모를 어떤 비밀이든 지키겠다고 다짐하는 담보를 준 적이 있었소. 어떤 배교자들은 그리스도교 영토로 돌아갈 작정일 때, 이름난 포로들에게서 받은 증명서를 몸에 지니고 다니는 관습이 있는데, 그 증명서에는 가능한 어떤 형식으로든 아무개라는 배교자가 늘 그리스도교도들에게 친절을 베풀어 온 훌륭한 사람이며, 기회가 닿는 대로 달아나기를 간절히 바라고 있다고 증언되어 있소."],["Some obtain these testimonials with good intentions, others put them to a cunning use; for when they go to pillage on Christian territory, if they chance to be cast away, or taken prisoners, they produce their certificates and say that from these papers may be seen the object they came for, which was to remain on Christian ground, and that it was to this end they joined the Turks in their foray.","어떤 자들은 좋은 뜻으로 이런 증명서를 얻지만, 어떤 자들은 그것을 교활하게 써먹소. 그들이 그리스도교 영토로 약탈하러 갔다가 어쩌다 난파하거나 포로로 붙잡히면, 그 증명서를 내밀며 이 종이들로 자기가 온 목적을 알 수 있으리라 말하니, 곧 그리스도교 땅에 머물려는 것이었고, 바로 이 목적을 위해 튀르크인들의 약탈 행각에 가담했다는 것이오."],["In this way they escape the consequences of the first outburst and make their peace with the Church before it does them any harm, and then when they have the chance they return to Barbary to become what they were before.","이런 식으로 그들은 첫 분노의 결과를 피하고 그것이 해를 끼치기 전에 교회와 화해한 다음, 기회가 닿으면 다시 바르바리로 돌아가 이전의 자기로 되돌아가는 것이오."],["Others, however, there are who procure these papers and make use of them honestly, and remain on Christian soil.","그러나 이런 종이를 구해 정직하게 써먹어 그리스도교 땅에 머무는 자들도 있소."],["This friend of mine, then, was one of these renegades that I have described; he had certificates from all our comrades, in which we testified in his favour as strongly as we could; and if the Moors had found the papers they would have burned him alive.","그러니 내 이 친구는 내가 말한 그런 배교자 가운데 하나였소. 그는 우리 전우 모두에게서 받은 증명서를 가지고 있었는데, 거기서 우리는 할 수 있는 한 강력하게 그를 위해 증언했고, 만일 무어인들이 그 종이를 발견했더라면 그를 산 채로 불태웠을 것이오."]],[["I knew that he understood Arabic very well, and could not only speak but also write it; but before I disclosed the whole matter to him, I asked him to read for me this paper which I had found by accident in a hole in my cell.","나는 그가 아랍어를 매우 잘 이해하고, 말할 뿐 아니라 쓸 줄도 안다는 것을 알고 있었소. 그러나 그에게 모든 일을 털어놓기 전에, 나는 내 감방의 어느 구멍에서 우연히 발견한 이 종이를 읽어 달라고 청했소."],["He opened it and remained some time examining it and muttering to himself as he translated it.","그는 그것을 펼치고는 한동안 그것을 살피며 번역하느라 혼자 중얼거렸소."],["I asked him if he understood it, and he told me he did perfectly well, and that if I wished him to tell me its meaning word for word, I must give him pen and ink that he might do it more satisfactorily.","나는 그에게 그것을 이해하겠느냐고 물었고, 그는 완벽하게 이해한다며, 만일 그 뜻을 한 마디 한 마디 일러 주기를 바란다면 더 만족스럽게 할 수 있도록 펜과 잉크를 달라고 했소."],["We at once gave him what he required, and he set about translating it bit by bit, and when he had done he said:","우리는 즉시 그가 청한 것을 주었고, 그는 그것을 한 토막씩 번역하기 시작했으며, 다 마치고 나서 이렇게 말했소."]],[["“All that is here in Spanish is what the Moorish paper contains, and you must bear in mind that when it says ‘Lela Marien’ it means ‘Our Lady the Virgin Mary.’”","“이 무어인 종이에 담긴 내용을 모두 스페인어로 옮겼소. 그리고 ‘렐라 마리엔’이라 한 곳은 ‘우리의 성모 마리아님’을 뜻한다는 것을 명심해 두시오.”"]],[["We read the paper and it ran thus:","우리는 그 종이를 읽었는데, 이렇게 적혀 있었소."]],[["“When I was a child my father had a slave who taught me to pray the Christian prayer in my own language, and told me many things about Lela Marien.","“제가 어릴 적에 제 아버지에게는 노예가 하나 있었는데, 그 여자가 제게 제 말로 그리스도교의 기도를 외는 법을 가르쳐 주었고 렐라 마리엔에 관해 많은 것을 일러 주었습니다."],["The Christian died, and I know that she did not go to the fire, but to Allah, because since then I have seen her twice, and she told me to go to the land of the Christians to see Lela Marien, who had great love for me.","그 그리스도교도 여자는 죽었으나, 저는 그 여자가 불로 가지 않고 알라께로 갔음을 압니다. 그 뒤로 제가 그 여자를 두 번 보았는데, 저더러 그리스도교도들의 땅으로 가서 저를 무척 사랑하시는 렐라 마리엔을 뵈라고 말했기 때문입니다."],["I know not how to go.","저는 어떻게 가야 할지 모릅니다."],["I have seen many Christians, but except thyself none has seemed to me to be a gentleman.","그리스도교도를 많이 보았으나, 당신 말고는 누구도 신사처럼 보이지 않았습니다. 저는 젊고 아름다우며, 가지고 갈 돈이 넉넉합니다."],["I am young and beautiful, and have plenty of money to take with me.","우리가 어떻게 갈 수 있을지 당신이 궁리해 낼 수 있을지 보십시오."],["See if thou canst contrive how we may go, and if thou wilt thou shalt be my husband there, and if thou wilt not it will not distress me, for Lela Marien will find me some one to marry me.","그러기를 바라신다면 당신은 그곳에서 제 남편이 될 것이고, 바라지 않으셔도 저는 괴롭지 않을 것이니, 렐라 마리엔께서 저와 혼인할 누군가를 찾아 주실 것이기 때문입니다. 이것은 제가 손수 썼습니다."],["I myself have written this: have a care to whom thou givest it to read: trust no Moor, for they are all perfidious.","누구에게 이것을 읽게 하는지 조심하십시오. 어떤 무어인도 믿지 마십시오, 그들은 모두 배신을 일삼는 자들이니까요."],["I am greatly troubled on this account, for I would not have thee confide in anyone, because if my father knew it he would at once fling me down a well and cover me with stones.","저는 이 일로 몹시 근심하고 있으니, 당신이 누구에게도 털어놓지 않으셨으면 합니다. 제 아버지가 이를 알면 당장 저를 우물에 던지고 돌로 덮어 버릴 것이기 때문입니다."],["I will put a thread to the reed; tie the answer to it, and if thou hast no one to write for thee in Arabic, tell it to me by signs, for Lela Marien will make me understand thee.","제가 갈대에 실을 매어 두겠으니, 거기에 답을 매십시오. 그리고 당신에게 아랍어로 써 줄 사람이 없으면 신호로 제게 일러 주십시오, 렐라 마리엔께서 제가 당신을 알아듣게 해 주실 것입니다."],["She and Allah and this cross, which I often kiss as the captive bade me, protect thee.”","그분과 알라와, 포로가 일러 준 대로 제가 자주 입 맞추는 이 십자가가 당신을 지켜 주시기를.”"]],[["Judge, sirs, whether we had reason for surprise and joy at the words of this paper; and both one and the other were so great, that the renegade perceived that the paper had not been found by chance, but had been in reality addressed to some one of us, and he begged us, if what he suspected were the truth, to trust him and tell him all, for he would risk his life for our freedom; and so saying he took out from his breast a metal crucifix, and with many tears swore by the God the image represented, in whom, sinful and wicked as he was, he truly and faithfully believed, to be loyal to us and keep secret whatever we chose to reveal to him; for he thought and almost foresaw that by means of her who had written that paper, he and all of us would obtain our liberty, and he himself obtain the object he so much desired, his restoration to the bosom of the Holy Mother Church, from which by his own sin and ignorance he was now severed like a corrupt limb.","여러분, 이 종이의 말에 우리가 놀라고 기뻐할 만했는지 헤아려 보시오. 그 놀라움과 기쁨이 어찌나 컸던지, 배교자는 그 종이가 우연히 발견된 것이 아니라 실은 우리 가운데 누군가에게 보내진 것임을 알아챘소."],["The renegade said this with so many tears and such signs of repentance, that with one consent we all agreed to tell him the whole truth of the matter, and so we gave him a full account of all, without hiding anything from him.","그래서 그는 자기가 의심하는 바가 사실이라면 자기를 믿고 모든 것을 말해 달라며, 우리의 자유를 위해서라면 목숨을 걸겠다고 간청했소. 그러면서 그는 가슴에서 쇠로 된 십자가상을 꺼내 많은 눈물을 흘리며, 그 형상이 표상하는 하느님께, 죄 많고 사악한 자신이지만 진실하고 충실하게 믿는 그 하느님께 맹세하여, 우리에게 충실하고 우리가 밝히기로 택한 것은 무엇이든 비밀로 지키겠다고 했소."],["We pointed out to him the window at which the reed appeared, and he by that means took note of the house, and resolved to ascertain with particular care who lived in it.","그가 그렇게 생각하고 거의 예감하기를, 그 종이를 쓴 그 여인을 통해 자기와 우리 모두가 자유를 얻고, 자신 또한 그토록 갈망하던 것, 곧 제 죄와 무지로 인해 지금은 썩은 가지처럼 잘려 나가 있는 거룩한 어머니 교회의 품으로 되돌아가는 일을 이루리라 했기 때문이오. 배교자는 어찌나 많은 눈물과 참회의 기색으로 이 말을 했던지, 우리는 한마음으로 이 일의 모든 진실을 그에게 말하기로 동의했고, 그리하여 그에게 아무것도 숨기지 않고 모든 것을 낱낱이 들려주었소."],["We agreed also that it would be advisable to answer the Moorish lady’s letter, and the renegade without a moment’s delay took down the words I dictated to him, which were exactly what I shall tell you, for nothing of importance that took place in this affair has escaped my memory, or ever will while life lasts.","우리는 그에게 갈대가 나타났던 창문을 가리켜 보였고, 그는 그것으로 그 집을 눈여겨 두었으며, 거기에 누가 사는지 각별히 주의를 기울여 알아내기로 결심했소. 또한 우리는 그 무어인 여인의 편지에 답하는 것이 좋겠다고 합의했고, 배교자는 한순간도 지체 없이 내가 불러 주는 말을 받아 적었으니, 그것은 바로 내가 여러분에게 들려 드릴 그대로요."],["This, then, was the answer returned to the Moorish lady:","이 일에서 일어난 중요한 일 가운데 무엇 하나 내 기억에서 빠져나간 것이 없고, 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 결코 빠져나가지 않을 것이기 때문이오. 그러니 무어인 여인에게 보낸 답은 이러했소."]],[["“The true Allah protect thee, Lady, and that blessed Marien who is the true mother of God, and who has put it into thy heart to go to the land of the Christians, because she loves thee.","“참된 알라께서 당신을 지켜 주시기를, 부인이여, 그리고 하느님의 참된 어머니이시며 당신을 사랑하시기에 그리스도교도들의 땅으로 가도록 당신 마음에 그 뜻을 심어 주신 저 복된 마리엔께서 지켜 주시기를. 그분께서 당신에게 내리신 그 명을 당신이 어떻게 이행할 수 있는지 보여 주시기를 그분께 간청하십시오."],["Entreat her that she be pleased to show thee how thou canst execute the command she gives thee, for she will, such is her goodness.","그분의 선하심이 그러하시니 반드시 그리하실 것입니다. 저로 말하자면, 또 저와 함께 있는 이 모든 그리스도교도를 대신하여, 죽음에 이르기까지 당신을 위해 우리가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 하겠다고 약속합니다."],["On my own part, and on that of all these Christians who are with me, I promise to do all that we can for thee, even to death.","제게 편지하기를 거르지 마시고 당신이 어찌하실 작정인지 알려 주십시오. 그러면 저는 늘 답하겠습니다."],["Fail not to write to me and inform me what thou dost mean to do, and I will always answer thee; for the great Allah has given us a Christian captive who can speak and write thy language well, as thou mayest see by this paper; without fear, therefore, thou canst inform us of all thou wouldst.","위대하신 알라께서 당신의 언어를 잘 말하고 쓸 줄 아는 그리스도교도 포로를 우리에게 주셨으니, 이 종이로 보시는 바와 같습니다. 그러니 두려움 없이 당신은 원하는 바를 모두 우리에게 알리실 수 있습니다."],["As to what thou sayest, that if thou dost reach the land of the Christians thou wilt be my wife, I give thee my promise upon it as a good Christian; and know that the Christians keep their promises better than the Moors.","당신이 그리스도교도들의 땅에 닿으면 제 아내가 되겠다 하신 말씀에 대해서는, 선량한 그리스도교도로서 그것을 약속드립니다. 그리스도교도는 무어인보다 약속을 더 잘 지킨다는 것을 아셔야 합니다."],["Allah and Marien his mother watch over thee, my Lady.”","알라와 그분의 어머니 마리엔께서 당신을 보살피시기를, 부인이여.”"]],[["The paper being written and folded I waited two days until the bano was empty as before, and immediately repaired to the usual walk on the terrace to see if there were any sign of the reed, which was not long in making its appearance.","종이를 써서 접은 뒤 나는 이틀을 기다려 바뇨가 전처럼 텅 비기를 기다렸고, 곧장 옥상의 늘 다니던 산책길로 가서 갈대의 기미가 있는지 살폈는데, 그것은 오래지 않아 나타났소. 나는 그것을 보자마자, 누가 그것을 내미는지는 분간할 수 없었으나, 실을 매라는 신호로 종이를 들어 보였소."],["As soon as I saw it, although I could not distinguish who put it out, I showed the paper as a sign to attach the thread, but it was already fixed to the reed, and to it I tied the paper; and shortly afterwards our star once more made its appearance with the white flag of peace, the little bundle.","그러나 실은 이미 갈대에 매여 있었고, 거기에 나는 종이를 묶었소. 그리고 잠시 후 우리의 별이 다시금 나타났으니, 평화의 흰 깃발인 그 작은 꾸러미와 함께였소."],["It was dropped, and I picked it up, and found in the cloth, in gold and silver coins of all sorts, more than fifty crowns, which fifty times more strengthened our joy and doubled our hope of gaining our liberty.","그것이 떨어졌고 나는 그것을 집어 들었는데, 천 안에서 온갖 종류의 금화와 은화로 오십 에스쿠도가 넘는 돈을 발견했소. 그것은 우리의 기쁨을 쉰 배나 더 굳세게 했고 자유를 얻으리라는 우리의 희망을 갑절로 키웠소."],["That very night our renegade returned and said he had learned that the Moor we had been told of lived in that house, that his name was Hadji Morato, that he was enormously rich, that he had one only daughter the heiress of all his wealth, and that it was the general opinion throughout the city that she was the most beautiful woman in Barbary, and that several of the viceroys who came there had sought her for a wife, but that she had been always unwilling to marry; and he had learned, moreover, that she had a Christian slave who was now dead; all which agreed with the contents of the paper.","바로 그날 밤 우리 배교자가 돌아와 말하기를, 우리가 들은 그 무어인이 그 집에 사는데, 이름은 하지 모라토이고, 엄청난 부자이며, 그 모든 재산의 상속녀인 외동딸이 하나 있는데, 그 도시 전체의 한결같은 평판으로 그 여인이 바르바리에서 가장 아름다운 여인이라 하고, 그곳에 부임한 여러 부왕이 그 여인을 아내로 구했으나 그 여인은 늘 혼인하기를 마다해 왔다는 것을 알아냈다 했소. 게다가 그 여인에게는 그리스도교도 노예 여자가 하나 있었는데 지금은 죽었다는 것도 알아냈으니, 이 모든 것이 그 종이의 내용과 들어맞았소."],["We immediately took counsel with the renegade as to what means would have to be adopted in order to carry off the Moorish lady and bring us all to Christian territory; and in the end it was agreed that for the present we should wait for a second communication from Zoraida (for that was the name of her who now desires to be called Maria), because we saw clearly that she and no one else could find a way out of all these difficulties.","우리는 곧장 배교자와 더불어 그 무어인 여인을 빼내어 우리 모두를 그리스도교 영토로 데려가려면 어떤 수단을 써야 할지 의논했고, 결국 당분간은 소라이다(지금은 마리아라 불리기를 바라는 그 여인의 이름이 그러하오)에게서 두 번째 연락이 오기를 기다리기로 합의했으니, 우리는 이 모든 난관에서 빠져나갈 길을 찾을 수 있는 이는 그 여인이지 다른 누구도 아님을 똑똑히 보았기 때문이오."],["When we had decided upon this the renegade told us not to be uneasy, for he would lose his life or restore us to liberty.","이렇게 결정하고 나자 배교자는 우리더러 마음을 졸이지 말라며, 목숨을 잃든지 아니면 우리를 자유로 되돌려 놓겠다고 했소."],["For four days the bano was filled with people, for which reason the reed delayed its appearance for four days, but at the end of that time, when the bano was, as it generally was, empty, it appeared with the cloth so bulky that it promised a happy birth.","나흘 동안 바뇨가 사람들로 가득 찼던 까닭에 갈대는 나흘이나 나타나기를 미루었으나, 그 기간이 끝나고 바뇨가 으레 그렇듯 텅 비었을 때, 갈대가 천을 어찌나 불룩하게 매단 채 나타났던지 행복한 출산을 예고하는 듯했소."],["Reed and cloth came down to me, and I found another paper and a hundred crowns in gold, without any other coin.","갈대와 천이 내게 내려왔고, 나는 또 다른 종이 한 장과 함께 다른 동전은 하나 없이 금화로 백 에스쿠도를 발견했소."],["The renegade was present, and in our cell we gave him the paper to read, which was to this effect:","배교자가 그 자리에 있어, 우리 감방에서 그에게 읽으라고 종이를 건넸는데, 그 내용은 이러했소."]],[["“I cannot think of a plan, señor, for our going to Spain, nor has Lela Marien shown me one, though I have asked her.","“세뇨르, 저는 우리가 스페인으로 갈 방도를 생각해 낼 수가 없으며, 렐라 마리엔께서도 비록 제가 여쭈었으나 한 가지도 보여 주지 않으셨습니다."],["All that can be done is for me to give you plenty of money in gold from this window.","할 수 있는 일이라고는 제가 이 창문에서 당신에게 금화를 넉넉히 드리는 것뿐입니다."],["With it ransom yourself and your friends, and let one of you go to the land of the Christians, and there buy a vessel and come back for the others; and he will find me in my father’s garden, which is at the Babazon gate near the seashore, where I shall be all this summer with my father and my servants.","그것으로 당신과 당신 벗들의 몸값을 치르고, 당신들 가운데 하나가 그리스도교도들의 땅으로 가서 거기서 배를 한 척 사 다른 이들을 위해 돌아오게 하십시오. 그러면 그는 저를 제 아버지의 정원에서 찾게 될 것이니, 그것은 바닷가 가까운 바바손 문 곁에 있고, 저는 이번 여름 내내 제 아버지와 하인들과 함께 그곳에 있을 것입니다."],["You can carry me away from there by night without any danger, and bring me to the vessel.","당신은 거기서 아무 위험 없이 밤에 저를 데리고 나와 배로 데려갈 수 있습니다."],["And remember thou art to be my husband, else I will pray to Marien to punish thee.","그리고 당신이 제 남편이 되어야 함을 기억하십시오, 그러지 않으면 저는 마리엔께 당신을 벌하시라 기도할 것입니다."],["If thou canst not trust anyone to go for the vessel, ransom thyself and do thou go, for I know thou wilt return more surely than any other, as thou art a gentleman and a Christian.","만일 배를 가지러 갈 누군가를 믿을 수 없다면, 당신 자신의 몸값을 치르고 당신이 가십시오. 당신은 신사이며 그리스도교도이니, 다른 누구보다 더 확실히 돌아오시리라는 것을 제가 알기 때문입니다."],["Endeavour to make thyself acquainted with the garden; and when I see thee walking yonder I shall know that the bano is empty and I will give thee abundance of money.","그 정원을 잘 알아 두도록 애쓰십시오. 그리고 당신이 저쪽에서 거니는 것을 제가 보면, 바뇨가 비었음을 알고 당신에게 돈을 넉넉히 드리겠습니다."],["Allah protect thee, señor.”","알라께서 당신을 지켜 주시기를, 세뇨르.”"]],[["These were the words and contents of the second paper, and on hearing them, each declared himself willing to be the ransomed one, and promised to go and return with scrupulous good faith; and I too made the same offer; but to all this the renegade objected, saying that he would not on any account consent to one being set free before all went together, as experience had taught him how ill those who have been set free keep promises which they made in captivity; for captives of distinction frequently had recourse to this plan, paying the ransom of one who was to go to Valencia or Majorca with money to enable him to arm a bark and return for the others who had ransomed him, but who never came back; for recovered liberty and the dread of losing it again efface from the memory all the obligations in the world. And to prove the truth of what he said, he told us briefly what had happened to a certain Christian gentleman almost at that very time, the strangest case that had ever occurred even there, where astonishing and marvellous things are happening every instant. In short, he ended by saying that what could and ought to be done was to give the money intended for the ransom of one of us Christians to him, so that he might with it buy a vessel there in Algiers under the pretence of becoming a merchant and trader at Tetuan and along the coast; and when master of the vessel, it would be easy for him to hit on some way of getting us all out of the bano and putting us on board; especially if the Moorish lady gave, as she said, money enough to ransom all, because once free it would be the easiest thing in the world for us to embark even in open day; but the greatest difficulty was that the Moors do not allow any renegade to buy or own any craft, unless it be a large vessel for going on roving expeditions, because they are afraid that anyone who buys a small vessel, especially if he be a Spaniard, only wants it for the purpose of escaping to Christian territory. This however he could get over by arranging with a Tagarin Moor to go shares with him in the purchase of the vessel, and in the profit on the cargo; and under cover of this he could become master of the vessel, in which case he looked upon all the rest as accomplished. But though to me and my comrades it had seemed a better plan to send to Majorca for the vessel, as the Moorish lady suggested, we did not dare to oppose him, fearing that if we did not do as he said he would denounce us, and place us in danger of losing all our lives if he were to disclose our dealings with Zoraida, for whose life we would have all given our own. We therefore resolved to put ourselves in the hands of God and in the renegade’s; and at the same time an answer was given to Zoraida, telling her that we would do all she recommended, for she had given as good advice as if Lela Marien had delivered it, and that it depended on her alone whether we were to defer the business or put it in execution at once. I renewed my promise to be her husband; and thus the next day that the bano chanced to be empty she at different times gave us by means of the reed and cloth two thousand gold crowns and a paper in which she said that the next Juma, that is to say Friday, she was going to her father’s garden, but that before she went she would give us more money; and if it were not enough we were to let her know, as she would give us as much as we asked, for her father had so much he would not miss it, and besides she kept all the keys.","이것이 두 번째 종이의 말과 내용이었는데, 그것을 듣자 각자 자기가 몸값으로 풀려나는 자가 되겠노라 하며, 한 치의 어김없는 신의로 가서 돌아오겠다고 약속했소. 나 역시 같은 제안을 했소. 그러나 이 모든 것에 배교자가 반대하며 말하기를, 모두가 함께 떠나기 전에 한 사람이 먼저 풀려나는 것에는 어떤 일이 있어도 동의할 수 없다고 했소. 자유를 얻은 자들이 사로잡혀 있을 때 한 약속을 얼마나 형편없이 지키는지 경험으로 알았기 때문이라 했소. 지체 높은 포로들이 흔히 이런 방책에 의지하여, 발렌시아나 마요르카로 가서 배 한 척을 무장시키고 자기 몸값을 치러 준 다른 이들을 위해 돌아올 수 있도록 돈과 함께 한 사람의 몸값을 치렀으나, 그자가 결코 돌아오지 않는 일이 있었으니, 되찾은 자유와 그것을 다시 잃을 두려움이 세상의 온갖 의무를 기억에서 지워 버리기 때문이라 했소. 그리고 자기 말이 진실임을 입증하려고, 그는 거의 바로 그 무렵 어느 그리스도교도 신사에게 일어난 일을, 놀랍고 신기한 일이 매 순간 벌어지는 그곳에서조차 일찍이 일어난 가장 기이한 사건을 간략히 들려주었소. 요컨대 그는 결론짓기를, 할 수 있고 또 마땅히 해야 할 일은, 우리 그리스도교도 가운데 하나의 몸값으로 쓰려던 돈을 자기에게 주어, 자기가 테투안과 그 연안을 오가는 상인이자 무역상이 되는 척하며 그것으로 여기 알제에서 배를 한 척 사는 것이라 했소. 그리하여 배의 주인이 되면, 우리 모두를 바뇨에서 빼내어 배에 태울 어떤 방도를 찾는 것이 그에게는 쉬운 일일 것이라 했소. 특히 그 무어인 여인이 말한 대로 모두의 몸값을 치를 만한 돈을 준다면 더욱 그러하니, 일단 자유로워지면 우리가 환한 대낮에라도 배에 오르는 일은 세상에서 가장 쉬운 일일 것이기 때문이라 했소. 다만 가장 큰 어려움은, 무어인들은 어떤 배교자에게도 배를 사거나 소유하는 것을 허락하지 않는다는 점인데, 약탈 원정을 나가는 큰 배라면 또 모를까, 작은 배를 사는 자는, 특히 스페인 사람이라면, 오로지 그리스도교 영토로 달아나려는 목적으로 그것을 원할 뿐이라 두려워하기 때문이라 했소. 그러나 이것은 타가린 무어인 하나와 함께 배 구입과 화물의 이문을 나누기로 약정하여 넘어설 수 있다고 했소. 그리고 이를 빌미로 그가 배의 주인이 될 수 있을 것이니, 그렇게 되면 나머지 모든 일은 다 이루어진 것이나 다름없다고 보았소. 그러나 나와 내 전우들에게는 그 무어인 여인이 제안한 대로 마요르카로 사람을 보내 배를 구하는 편이 더 나은 계획으로 여겨졌으되, 우리는 감히 그에게 반대하지 못했소. 만일 그가 말한 대로 하지 않으면 그가 우리를 밀고하여 우리 모두 목숨을 잃을 위험에 빠뜨릴지도 모른다는 두려움 때문이었으니, 그가 소라이다와 우리의 거래를 폭로하기라도 한다면 — 그 여인의 목숨을 위해서라면 우리 모두 제 목숨을 다 내놓았을 것이오 — 큰일이었기 때문이오. 그리하여 우리는 우리 자신을 하느님과 배교자의 손에 맡기기로 결심했소. 동시에 소라이다에게 답을 보내, 우리가 그녀가 권한 모든 것을 하겠으며, 그녀가 마치 렐라 마리엔께서 전해 주신 것처럼 훌륭한 조언을 주었으니, 일을 미룰지 곧장 실행에 옮길지는 오직 그녀에게 달렸다고 일렀소. 나는 그녀의 남편이 되겠다는 약속을 다시 새겼소. 그리하여 이튿날 바뇨가 마침 비었을 때, 그녀는 갈대와 천을 통해 여러 차례에 걸쳐 우리에게 금화 이천 에스쿠도와 함께 종이 한 장을 주었는데, 거기서 그녀는 다음 후마, 곧 금요일에 자기 아버지의 정원으로 가는데, 그 전에 우리에게 돈을 더 주겠다고 했소. 그리고 그것으로 부족하면 알려 달라며, 우리가 청하는 만큼 주겠으니, 자기 아버지가 가진 것이 하도 많아 알아채지도 못할 것이고, 게다가 모든 열쇠를 자기가 지니고 있다고 했소."]],[["We at once gave the renegade five hundred crowns to buy the vessel, and with eight hundred I ransomed myself, giving the money to a Valencian merchant who happened to be in Algiers at the time, and who had me released on his word, pledging it that on the arrival of the first ship from Valencia he would pay my ransom; for if he had given the money at once it would have made the king suspect that my ransom money had been for a long time in Algiers, and that the merchant had for his own advantage kept it secret. In fact my master was so difficult to deal with that I dared not on any account pay down the money at once. The Thursday before the Friday on which the fair Zoraida was to go to the garden she gave us a thousand crowns more, and warned us of her departure, begging me, if I were ransomed, to find out her father’s garden at once, and by all means to seek an opportunity of going there to see her. I answered in a few words that I would do so, and that she must remember to commend us to Lela Marien with all the prayers the captive had taught her. This having been done, steps were taken to ransom our three comrades, so as to enable them to quit the bano, and lest, seeing me ransomed and themselves not, though the money was forthcoming, they should make a disturbance about it and the devil should prompt them to do something that might injure Zoraida; for though their position might be sufficient to relieve me from this apprehension, nevertheless I was unwilling to run any risk in the matter; and so I had them ransomed in the same way as I was, handing over all the money to the merchant so that he might with safety and confidence give security; without, however, confiding our arrangement and secret to him, which might have been dangerous.","우리는 곧장 배교자에게 배를 사라고 오백 에스쿠도를 주었고, 팔백 에스쿠도로 나는 내 몸값을 치렀으니, 마침 그때 알제에 있던 발렌시아의 어느 상인에게 돈을 주었소. 그는 자기 말만 믿고 나를 풀려나게 해 주었으니, 발렌시아에서 첫 배가 도착하면 내 몸값을 치르겠다고 약조한 것이오. 만일 그가 돈을 당장 치렀더라면, 왕은 내 몸값이 오래전부터 알제에 있었는데도 상인이 제 잇속을 챙기려고 그것을 비밀로 했다고 의심했을 것이오. 사실 내 주인이 어찌나 상대하기 까다로운 자였던지, 나는 어떤 일이 있어도 감히 돈을 당장 내려놓을 수 없었소. 아름다운 소라이다가 정원으로 가기로 한 금요일 전 목요일에, 그녀는 우리에게 천 에스쿠도를 더 주고 자기가 떠난다는 것을 일러 주며, 내가 몸값을 치르고 풀려났거든 곧장 자기 아버지의 정원을 알아내어 무슨 수를 써서라도 거기로 가 자기를 만날 기회를 찾으라고 간청했소. 나는 몇 마디로 그리하겠노라 답하고, 포로가 그녀에게 가르쳐 준 모든 기도를 다해 우리를 렐라 마리엔께 부탁드리는 것을 잊지 말라고 했소. 이 일이 이루어지고 나서, 우리 세 전우의 몸값을 치를 조치를 취하여 그들이 바뇨를 떠날 수 있게 했소. 행여 내가 몸값을 치르고 풀려나는데 자기들은 돈이 마련되어 있는데도 그러지 못하는 것을 보고 그 일로 소동을 일으켜, 악마가 그들을 부추겨 소라이다를 해칠 무슨 짓을 하게 할까 염려해서였소. 비록 그들의 신분으로 보아 이런 우려에서 벗어날 만했으나, 그래도 나는 이 일에서 어떤 위험도 무릅쓰고 싶지 않았소. 그래서 나는 그들도 나와 같은 방식으로 몸값을 치르게 하여, 그 상인에게 모든 돈을 넘겨 그가 안전하고 자신 있게 담보를 설 수 있도록 했소. 다만 우리의 계획과 비밀은 그에게 털어놓지 않았으니, 그것은 위험할 수도 있었기 때문이오."]],[["# CHAPTER XLI. IN WHICH THE CAPTIVE STILL CONTINUES HIS ADVENTURES","# CHAPTER XLI."]],[["Before fifteen days were over our renegade had already purchased an excellent vessel with room for more than thirty persons; and to make the transaction safe and lend a colour to it, he thought it well to make, as he did, a voyage to a place called Shershel, twenty leagues from Algiers on the Oran side, where there is an extensive trade in dried figs.","보름이 채 지나기도 전에 우리의 배교자는 벌써 서른 명도 더 태울 수 있는 훌륭한 배 한 척을 사들였다. 그리고 그 거래를 안전하게 만들고 그럴듯한 명분을 붙이기 위해 그는 알제에서 오랑 쪽으로 스무 레과쯤 떨어진 셰르셀이라는 곳으로 항해를 다녀오는 것이 좋겠다고 여겨 실제로 그리했는데, 그곳은 말린 무화과 거래가 활발한 곳이었다."],["Two or three times he made this voyage in company with the Tagarin already mentioned.","그는 두세 차례 앞서 말한 타가린 사람과 함께 이 항해를 했다."],["The Moors of Aragon are called Tagarins in Barbary, and those of Granada Mudejars; but in the Kingdom of Fez they call the Mudejars Elches, and they are the people the king chiefly employs in war.","아라곤의 무어인들을 바르바리아에서는 타가린이라 부르고 그라나다의 무어인들은 무데하르라 부르나, 페스 왕국에서는 무데하르를 엘체라 부르며, 이들은 왕이 전쟁에 주로 부리는 사람들이다. 이야기를 이어 가자면, 그는 배를 몰고 지날 때마다 소라이다가 기다리는 정원에서 석궁 두 번 쏠 거리도 안 되는 어느 후미에 닻을 내렸다."],["To proceed: every time he passed with his vessel he anchored in a cove that was not two crossbow shots from the garden where Zoraida was waiting; and there the renegade, together with the two Moorish lads that rowed, used purposely to station himself, either going through his prayers, or else practising as a part what he meant to perform in earnest.","그리고 거기서 배교자는 노를 젓는 두 무어 청년과 함께 일부러 머무르며 기도를 올리거나, 아니면 본격적으로 행하려는 일을 미리 연습 삼아 해 보곤 했다. 그렇게 그는 소라이다의 정원으로 가서 과일을 청했고, 그녀의 아버지는 그를 알아보지 못한 채 그것을 내주었다."],["And thus he would go to Zoraida’s garden and ask for fruit, which her father gave him, not knowing him; but though, as he afterwards told me, he sought to speak to Zoraida, and tell her who he was, and that by my orders he was to take her to the land of the Christians, so that she might feel satisfied and easy, he had never been able to do so; for the Moorish women do not allow themselves to be seen by any Moor or Turk, unless their husband or father bid them: with Christian captives they permit freedom of intercourse and communication, even more than might be considered proper.","그러나 그가 나중에 내게 말하기로는, 소라이다에게 말을 걸어 자기가 누구인지, 그리고 내 명령에 따라 그녀를 기독교도들의 땅으로 데려가기로 되어 있으니 안심하고 마음 놓아도 좋다는 것을 일러 주려 애썼으나 끝내 그리할 수 없었다고 했다. 무어 여인들은 남편이나 아버지가 시키지 않는 한 어떤 무어인이나 튀르크인에게도 자기 모습을 보이지 않기 때문이다."],["But for my part I should have been sorry if he had spoken to her, for perhaps it might have alarmed her to find her affairs talked of by renegades.","다만 기독교도 포로들과는 격의 없이 어울리고 이야기 나누는 것을 허락하니, 어쩌면 온당하다 여겨질 정도를 넘어설 만큼이다. 그러나 내 처지에서는 그가 그녀에게 말을 걸지 않은 것이 차라리 다행스러웠을 것이니, 자기 일이 배교자들 입에 오르내리는 것을 알면 그녀가 놀랐을지도 모르기 때문이다."],["But God, who ordered it otherwise, afforded no opportunity for our renegade’s well-meant purpose; and he, seeing how safely he could go to Shershel and return, and anchor when and how and where he liked, and that the Tagarin his partner had no will but his, and that, now I was ransomed, all we wanted was to find some Christians to row, told me to look out for any I should be willing to take with me, over and above those who had been ransomed, and to engage them for the next Friday, which he fixed upon for our departure.","그러나 일을 달리 마련하신 하느님께서는 우리 배교자의 선의에 찬 뜻이 이루어질 기회를 주지 않으셨다. 그리고 그는 셰르셀을 얼마나 안전하게 오갈 수 있는지, 또 언제 어떻게 어디서든 마음대로 닻을 내릴 수 있는지, 동료인 타가린이 오로지 자기 뜻만을 따른다는 것을, 그리고 내 몸값이 치러진 지금 우리에게 필요한 것은 노를 저을 기독교도 몇 사람을 찾는 일뿐임을 알고는, 몸값을 치른 자들 외에 내가 함께 데려갈 만한 자들이 있으면 눈여겨봐 두었다가 다음 금요일을 기해 그들을 끌어들이라고 내게 말했다."],["On this I spoke to twelve Spaniards, all stout rowers, and such as could most easily leave the city; but it was no easy matter to find so many just then, because there were twenty ships out on a cruise and they had taken all the rowers with them; and these would not have been found were it not that their master remained at home that summer without going to sea in order to finish a galliot that he had upon the stocks.","그는 그날을 우리의 출발일로 정했던 것이다. 그래서 나는 모두가 건장한 노잡이이고 도시를 가장 수월하게 빠져나올 수 있는 열두 명의 스페인 사람에게 이야기를 했다."],["To these men I said nothing more than that the next Friday in the evening they were to come out stealthily one by one and hang about Hadji Morato’s garden, waiting for me there until I came.","그러나 바로 그때 그만한 수를 찾는 것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었으니, 스무 척의 배가 순항을 나가 노잡이들을 죄다 데려갔기 때문이다. 만일 그들의 주인이 선대에 올려놓은 갤리엇 한 척을 마무리하느라 그해 여름 바다에 나가지 않고 집에 머물지 않았더라면, 이들조차 찾지 못했을 것이다."],["These directions I gave each one separately, with orders that if they saw any other Christians there they were not to say anything to them except that I had directed them to wait at that spot.","나는 이 사람들에게 다만 다음 금요일 저녁에 한 사람씩 몰래 빠져나와 하지 모라토의 정원 근처를 서성이며 내가 올 때까지 거기서 기다리라고만 일렀다. 이 지시를 나는 각자에게 따로따로 주었으며, 만일 거기서 다른 기독교도들을 보더라도 내가 그 자리에서 기다리라고 시켰다는 말 외에는 아무 말도 하지 말라고 명했다."]],[["This preliminary having been settled, another still more necessary step had to be taken, which was to let Zoraida know how matters stood that she might be prepared and forewarned, so as not to be taken by surprise if we were suddenly to seize upon her before she thought the Christians’ vessel could have returned.","이 준비를 마치고 나니, 그보다 더 긴요한 또 한 가지 일을 해 두어야 했다. 그것은 일이 어찌 돌아가는지를 소라이다에게 알려, 그녀가 미리 대비하고 마음의 준비를 갖추어, 기독교도의 배가 돌아올 수 있으리라 생각지도 못한 때에 우리가 갑작스레 그녀를 붙들더라도 놀라지 않게 하는 것이었다."],["I determined, therefore, to go to the garden and try if I could speak to her; and the day before my departure I went there under the pretence of gathering herbs.","그리하여 나는 정원에 가서 그녀에게 말을 걸 수 있을지 시험해 보기로 마음먹고, 출발 전날 약초를 캔다는 핑계로 그리로 갔다."],["The first person I met was her father, who addressed me in the language that all over Barbary and even in Constantinople is the medium between captives and Moors, and is neither Morisco nor Castilian, nor of any other nation, but a mixture of all languages, by means of which we can all understand one another.","거기서 처음 마주친 사람은 그녀의 아버지였는데, 그는 바르바리아 전역, 심지어 콘스탄티노플에서도 포로와 무어인 사이의 소통 수단으로 쓰이는 언어로 내게 말을 걸었다. 그 언어는 모리스코 말도 카스티야 말도, 다른 어느 나라 말도 아니요 모든 언어가 뒤섞인 것으로, 이를 통해 우리는 모두 서로를 알아들을 수 있다."],["In this sort of language, I say, he asked me what I wanted in his garden, and to whom I belonged.","그러니까 그는 이런 식의 언어로 내가 자기 정원에서 무엇을 원하는지, 또 누구의 소속인지를 물었다."],["I replied that I was a slave of the Arnaut Mami (for I knew as a certainty that he was a very great friend of his), and that I wanted some herbs to make a salad.","나는 아르나우트 마미의 노예라고 대답했다(나는 그가 이 사람의 아주 절친한 벗임을 확실히 알고 있었기 때문이다). 그리고 샐러드를 만들 약초를 좀 얻고 싶다고 했다."],["He asked me then whether I were on ransom or not, and what my master demanded for me.","그러자 그는 내가 몸값을 치르기로 되어 있는지, 또 내 주인이 나를 두고 얼마를 요구하는지 물었다."],["While these questions and answers were proceeding, the fair Zoraida, who had already perceived me some time before, came out of the house in the garden, and as Moorish women are by no means particular about letting themselves be seen by Christians, or, as I have said before, at all coy, she had no hesitation in coming to where her father stood with me; moreover her father, seeing her approaching slowly, called to her to come.","이런 문답이 오가는 동안, 얼마 전부터 이미 나를 알아본 아리따운 소라이다가 정원 안의 집에서 나왔다. 무어 여인들은 기독교도에게 자기 모습을 보이는 것을 전혀 꺼리지 않으며, 앞서 말했듯이 조금도 수줍어하지 않으므로, 그녀는 망설임 없이 자기 아버지가 나와 함께 서 있는 곳으로 다가왔다."],["It would be beyond my power now to describe to you the great beauty, the high-bred air, the brilliant attire of my beloved Zoraida as she presented herself before my eyes.","게다가 그녀의 아버지도 그녀가 천천히 다가오는 것을 보고 어서 오라고 불렀다. 그녀가 내 눈앞에 모습을 드러냈을 때의 그 대단한 아름다움, 그 기품 어린 자태, 그 눈부신 차림새를 지금 여러분께 묘사하는 일은 내 능력 밖일 것이다."],["I will content myself with saying that more pearls hung from her fair neck, her ears, and her hair than she had hairs on her head.","다만 그녀의 고운 목과 귀와 머리에 머리카락 수보다 더 많은 진주가 매달려 있었다고 말하는 것으로 만족하겠다."],["On her ankles, which as is customary were bare, she had carcajes (for so bracelets or anklets are called in Morisco) of the purest gold, set with so many diamonds that she told me afterwards her father valued them at ten thousand doubloons, and those she had on her wrists were worth as much more.","관습대로 맨살을 드러낸 발목에는 가장 순도 높은 황금으로 만든 카르카헤(모리스코 말로 팔찌나 발찌를 그렇게 부른다)를 차고 있었는데, 거기에 박힌 다이아몬드가 어찌나 많은지 그녀가 나중에 말하기로는 자기 아버지가 그것을 만 도블론으로 쳤다 했으며, 그녀의 손목에 찬 것도 그만한 값어치가 나갔다."],["The pearls were in profusion and very fine, for the highest display and adornment of the Moorish women is decking themselves with rich pearls and seed-pearls; and of these there are therefore more among the Moors than among any other people.","진주는 무수히 많고 매우 훌륭했으니, 무어 여인들이 가장 으뜸으로 치는 과시와 치장은 값진 진주와 잔진주로 몸을 꾸미는 것이어서, 그렇기에 진주는 다른 어느 민족보다도 무어인들 사이에 더 많다. 소라이다의 아버지는 알제에서 가장 많고 가장 순도 높은 진주를 가졌다는 평판이 있었으며, 또한 이십만 스페인 크라운이 넘는 재산을 지녔다는 평판도 있었다."],["Zoraida’s father had to the reputation of possessing a great number, and the purest in all Algiers, and of possessing also more than two hundred thousand Spanish crowns; and she, who is now mistress of me only, was mistress of all this.","그리고 이제는 오로지 나만의 주인인 그녀가 이 모든 것의 주인이었다. 이렇듯 치장한 그녀가 아름다웠을지 아닐지, 또 그녀가 영화로운 시절에 어떠했을지는, 그토록 많은 고초를 겪고도 그녀에게 남아 있는 아름다움으로 미루어 짐작할 수 있으리라."],["Whether thus adorned she would have been beautiful or not, and what she must have been in her prosperity, may be imagined from the beauty remaining to her after so many hardships; for, as everyone knows, the beauty of some women has its times and its seasons, and is increased or diminished by chance causes; and naturally the emotions of the mind will heighten or impair it, though indeed more frequently they totally destroy it.","누구나 알다시피 어떤 여인들의 아름다움은 때와 철을 타며, 우연한 사정으로 더해지기도 덜해지기도 하기 때문이다. 그리고 마음의 격정은 당연히 그것을 드높이기도 망치기도 하지만, 실로 더 흔하게는 그것을 송두리째 무너뜨린다."],["In a word she presented herself before me that day attired with the utmost splendour, and supremely beautiful; at any rate, she seemed to me the most beautiful object I had ever seen; and when, besides, I thought of all I owed to her I felt as though I had before me some heavenly being come to earth to bring me relief and happiness.","한마디로 그날 그녀는 더없이 화려하게 차려입고 지극히 아름다운 모습으로 내 앞에 나타났다. 어쨌든 내게는 그녀가 일찍이 본 가장 아름다운 것으로 여겨졌으며, 게다가 내가 그녀에게 진 모든 빚을 떠올리자, 나는 마치 내게 위안과 행복을 가져다주러 땅으로 내려온 어떤 천상의 존재를 눈앞에 둔 듯한 기분이 들었다."]],[["As she approached her father told her in his own language that I was a captive belonging to his friend the Arnaut Mami, and that I had come for salad.","그녀가 다가오자 아버지는 자기네 말로, 내가 자기 벗 아르나우트 마미에게 속한 포로이며 샐러드 거리를 얻으러 왔다고 그녀에게 일러 주었다."]],[["She took up the conversation, and in that mixture of tongues I have spoken of she asked me if I was a gentleman, and why I was not ransomed.","그녀는 대화를 이어받아, 내가 앞서 말한 그 뒤섞인 언어로 내게 양반인지, 또 왜 몸값을 치르지 않았는지 물었다."]],[["I answered that I was already ransomed, and that by the price it might be seen what value my master set on me, as I had given one thousand five hundred zoltanis for me; to which she replied, “Hadst thou been my father’s, I can tell thee, I would not have let him part with thee for twice as much, for you Christians always tell lies about yourselves and make yourselves out poor to cheat the Moors.”","나는 이미 몸값을 치렀으며, 그 값으로 미루어 내 주인이 나를 얼마나 값있게 여겼는지 알 수 있을 것이라고, 내가 천오백 솔타니를 치렀노라고 대답했다. 그러자 그녀가 대답하기를, \"그대가 내 아버지의 것이었다면, 내 그대에게 말해 두거니와, 그 갑절을 준대도 그분이 그대를 내놓게 하지 않았을 거예요. 그대들 기독교도는 늘 자기 자신에 대해 거짓을 늘어놓고, 무어인을 속이려고 스스로를 가난한 척하니까요\" 했다."]],[["“That may be, lady,”","\"그럴지도 모르지요, 아가씨.\" 내가 말했다."],["said I; “but indeed I dealt truthfully with my master, as I do and mean to do with everybody in the world.”","\"그러나 저는 정말로 제 주인에게 진실되게 대했습니다. 세상 모든 이에게 그러하듯, 또 앞으로도 그러려 하듯 말입니다.\""]],[["“And when dost thou go?”","\"그래, 그대는 언제 떠나죠?\""],["said Zoraida.","소라이다가 말했다."]],[["“To-morrow, I think,”","\"내일인 듯합니다.\""],["said I, “for there is a vessel here from France which sails to-morrow, and I think I shall go in her.”","내가 말했다. \"여기 프랑스에서 온 배 한 척이 내일 떠나는데, 저는 그 배로 갈까 합니다.\""]],[["“Would it not be better,”","\"스페인에서 오는 배들이 도착하기를 기다렸다가 그들과 함께 가는 편이 낫지 않겠어요?\""],["said Zoraida, “to wait for the arrival of ships from Spain and go with them and not with the French who are not your friends?”","소라이다가 말했다. \"그대들의 벗도 아닌 프랑스 사람들과 가느니 말이에요.\""]],[["“No,” said I; “though if there were intelligence that a vessel were now coming from Spain it is true I might, perhaps, wait for it; however, it is more likely I shall depart to-morrow, for the longing I feel to return to my country and to those I love is so great that it will not allow me to wait for another opportunity, however more convenient, if it be delayed.”","\"아니요.\" 내가 말했다. \"지금 스페인에서 배가 오고 있다는 소식이라도 있다면야 어쩌면 기다릴 수도 있겠지요. 그러나 아무래도 내일 떠날 듯합니다. 제 나라와 제가 사랑하는 이들에게 돌아가고픈 그리움이 너무도 커서, 설령 더 편한 기회라 해도 그것이 늦춰진다면 그것을 기다리도록 저를 내버려 두지 않을 테니까요.\""]],[["“No doubt thou art married in thine own country,”","\"그대는 분명 제 나라에 아내를 두고 왔겠지요.\""],["said Zoraida, “and for that reason thou art anxious to go and see thy wife.”","소라이다가 말했다. \"그래서 가서 아내를 보고 싶어 안달하는 거고요.\""]],[["“I am not married,”","\"저는 혼인하지 않았습니다.\""],["I replied, “but I have given my promise to marry on my arrival there.”","내가 대답했다. \"다만 그곳에 도착하면 혼인하겠노라 약조를 해 두었지요.\""]],[["“And is the lady beautiful to whom thou hast given it?”","\"그래, 그대가 약조한 그 여인은 아름다운가요?\""],["said Zoraida.","소라이다가 말했다."]],[["“So beautiful,”","\"어찌나 아름다운지,\""],["said I, “that, to describe her worthily and tell thee the truth, she is very like thee.”","내가 말했다. \"그녀를 합당하게 묘사하고 그대에게 진실을 말하자면, 그녀는 그대와 아주 닮았습니다.\""]],[["At this her father laughed very heartily and said, “By Allah, Christian, she must be very beautiful if she is like my daughter, who is the most beautiful woman in all this kingdom: only look at her well and thou wilt see I am telling the truth.”","이 말에 그녀의 아버지가 자못 흐뭇하게 웃으며 말했다. \"알라께 맹세코, 기독교도여, 내 딸을 닮았다면 그녀는 분명 매우 아름답겠구먼. 내 딸은 이 왕국 전체에서 가장 아름다운 여인이거든. 그저 그 아이를 잘 보기만 해도 내가 진실을 말하고 있음을 알 게야.\""]],[["Zoraida’s father as the better linguist helped to interpret most of these words and phrases, for though she spoke the bastard language, that, as I have said, is employed there, she expressed her meaning more by signs than by words.","소라이다의 아버지는 더 나은 통역자였던지라 이 말과 표현들의 대부분을 풀어 주는 데 도움을 주었다. 앞서 말했듯 그녀가 그곳에서 쓰이는 그 잡탕 언어를 말하기는 했어도, 그녀는 자기 뜻을 말보다는 손짓으로 더 많이 나타냈기 때문이다."]],[["While we were still engaged in this conversation, a Moor came running up, exclaiming that four Turks had leaped over the fence or wall of the garden, and were gathering the fruit though it was not yet ripe. The old man was alarmed and Zoraida too, for the Moors commonly, and, so to speak, instinctively have a dread of the Turks, but particularly of the soldiers, who are so insolent and domineering to the Moors who are under their power that they treat them worse than if they were their slaves. Her father said to Zoraida, “Daughter, retire into the house and shut thyself in while I go and speak to these dogs; and thou, Christian, pick thy herbs, and go in peace, and Allah bring thee safe to thy own country.”","우리가 아직 이런 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 한 무어인이 뛰어와 외치기를, 튀르크인 넷이 정원의 울타리인지 담을 넘어 들어와 아직 익지도 않은 과일을 따 모으고 있다는 것이었다. 노인은 놀랐고 소라이다도 그러했으니, 무어인들은 으레, 말하자면 본능적으로 튀르크인을 두려워하기 때문이며, 특히 그 군졸들을 두려워한다. 그들은 자기 세력 아래 있는 무어인들에게 어찌나 오만방자하고 위세를 부리는지, 노예보다도 더 못하게 대하는 것이다. 그녀의 아버지가 소라이다에게 말했다. \"얘야, 내가 가서 저 개들에게 한마디 하는 동안 집 안으로 들어가 문을 걸어 잠그고 있거라. 그리고 그대, 기독교도여, 약초를 캐서 평안히 가게나. 알라께서 그대를 그대의 고향까지 무사히 데려다주시길 비네.\""]],[["I bowed, and he went away to look for the Turks, leaving me alone with Zoraida, who made as if she were about to retire as her father bade her; but the moment he was concealed by the trees of the garden, turning to me with her eyes full of tears she said, “Tameji, cristiano, tameji?” that is to say, “Art thou going, Christian, art thou going?”","나는 절을 했고, 그는 튀르크인들을 찾으러 가 버려, 나를 소라이다와 단둘이 남겨 두었다. 그녀는 아버지의 분부대로 물러나려는 양했으나, 그가 정원의 나무들에 가려 보이지 않게 된 순간, 눈물이 가득한 눈으로 내게 돌아서며 말했다. \"타메히, 크리스티아노, 타메히?\" 다시 말해 \"가시나요, 기독교도여, 가시나요?\" 하는 뜻이었다."]],[["I made answer, “Yes, lady, but not without thee, come what may: be on the watch for me on the next Juma, and be not alarmed when thou seest us; for most surely we shall go to the land of the Christians.”","나는 대답했다. \"그렇습니다, 아가씨. 그러나 무슨 일이 있어도 그대 없이는 가지 않겠습니다. 다음 주마에 저를 기다려 주십시오. 그리고 우리를 보거든 놀라지 마십시오. 우리는 틀림없이 기독교도들의 땅으로 갈 것이니까요.\""]],[["This I said in such a way that she understood perfectly all that passed between us, and throwing her arm round my neck she began with feeble steps to move towards the house; but as fate would have it (and it might have been very unfortunate if Heaven had not otherwise ordered it), just as we were moving on in the manner and position I have described, with her arm round my neck, her father, as he returned after having sent away the Turks, saw how we were walking and we perceived that he saw us; but Zoraida, ready and quickwitted, took care not to remove her arm from my neck, but on the contrary drew closer to me and laid her head on my breast, bending her knees a little and showing all the signs and tokens of fainting, while I at the same time made it seem as though I were supporting her against my will. Her father came running up to where we were, and seeing his daughter in this state asked what was the matter with her; she, however, giving no answer, he said, “No doubt she has fainted in alarm at the entrance of those dogs,” and taking her from mine he drew her to his own breast, while she sighing, her eyes still wet with tears, said again, “Ameji, cristiano, ameji”--“Go, Christian, go.” To this her father replied, “There is no need, daughter, for the Christian to go, for he has done thee no harm, and the Turks have now gone; feel no alarm, there is nothing to hurt thee, for as I say, the Turks at my request have gone back the way they came.”","나는 우리 사이에 오간 모든 말을 그녀가 온전히 알아듣도록 이렇게 말했다. 그러자 그녀는 내 목에 팔을 두르고 비척거리는 걸음으로 집을 향해 걷기 시작했다. 그러나 운명이 그렇게 정한 탓에(하늘이 달리 마련해 주시지 않았더라면 매우 불운한 일이 될 뻔했다), 내가 묘사한 그대로의 모양과 자세로, 곧 그녀가 내 목에 팔을 두른 채 우리가 막 걸음을 옮기던 바로 그때, 튀르크인들을 돌려보내고 돌아오던 그녀의 아버지가 우리가 어떻게 걷고 있는지를 보았고, 우리도 그가 우리를 본 것을 알아챘다. 그러나 재빠르고 기민한 소라이다는 내 목에서 팔을 떼지 않으려 조심했고, 오히려 내게 더 바싹 다가와 머리를 내 가슴에 기대고는 무릎을 살짝 굽혀 기절하는 온갖 기색과 시늉을 보였다. 나는 그 사이 마치 마지못해 그녀를 부축하는 척했다. 그녀의 아버지가 우리가 있는 곳으로 달려와 자기 딸이 이런 상태인 것을 보고 무슨 일이냐고 물었다. 그러나 그녀가 아무 대답도 하지 않자 그가 말했다. \"저 개들이 들어오는 바람에 놀라 기절한 게 틀림없구나.\" 그러고는 그녀를 내게서 떼어 자기 가슴에 끌어안았다. 그녀는 여전히 눈물에 젖은 눈으로 한숨지으며 다시 말했다. \"아메히, 크리스티아노, 아메히.\" 곧 \"가세요, 기독교도여, 가세요\"라는 뜻이었다. 이에 그녀의 아버지가 대답했다. \"얘야, 기독교도가 갈 필요는 없다. 그는 네게 아무런 해도 끼치지 않았고, 튀르크인들은 이제 가 버렸으니 말이다. 놀라지 말거라, 너를 해칠 것은 아무것도 없으니. 내가 말했듯이 튀르크인들은 내 청대로 왔던 길로 돌아갔단다.\""]],[["“It was they who terrified her, as thou hast said, señor,” said I to her father; “but since she tells me to go, I have no wish to displease her: peace be with thee, and with thy leave I will come back to this garden for herbs if need be, for my master says there are nowhere better herbs for salad then here.”","\"말씀하신 대로 그녀를 놀라게 한 것은 그들입니다, 나리.\" 내가 그녀의 아버지에게 말했다. \"그러나 그녀가 가라고 하니 저는 그녀의 뜻을 거스르고 싶지 않습니다. 평안하시기를 빌며, 허락하신다면 필요할 때 이 정원으로 약초를 캐러 다시 오겠습니다. 제 주인께서 샐러드용 약초로는 여기보다 더 나은 데가 없다고 하시니 말입니다.\""]],[["“Come back for any thou hast need of,”","\"필요한 것이 있거든 언제든 다시 오게나.\" 하지 모라토가 대답했다."],["replied Hadji Morato; “for my daughter does not speak thus because she is displeased with thee or any Christian: she only meant that the Turks should go, not thou; or that it was time for thee to look for thy herbs.”","\"내 딸이 이렇게 말하는 것은 그대나 다른 어떤 기독교도가 못마땅해서가 아닐세. 그저 그대가 아니라 튀르크인들더러 가라는 뜻이었거나, 아니면 그대가 약초를 찾으러 갈 때가 되었다는 말이었을 뿐이지.\""]],[["With this I at once took my leave of both; and she, looking as though her heart were breaking, retired with her father.","이 말과 함께 나는 곧 두 사람에게 작별을 고했다. 그리고 그녀는 가슴이 찢어질 듯한 얼굴로 아버지와 함께 물러갔다."],["While pretending to look for herbs I made the round of the garden at my ease, and studied carefully all the approaches and outlets, and the fastenings of the house and everything that could be taken advantage of to make our task easy.","나는 약초를 찾는 척하면서 정원을 여유롭게 한 바퀴 돌며, 모든 진입로와 출구, 집의 잠금장치, 그리고 우리의 일을 수월하게 할 수 있도록 이용할 만한 모든 것을 주의 깊게 살펴 두었다."]],[["Having done so I went and gave an account of all that had taken place to the renegade and my comrades, and looked forward with impatience to the hour when, all fear at an end, I should find myself in possession of the prize which fortune held out to me in the fair and lovely Zoraida.","그렇게 하고 나서 나는 가서 일어난 모든 일을 배교자와 동료들에게 일러 주었고, 두려움이 모두 사라지고 운명이 아리땁고 사랑스러운 소라이다라는 모습으로 내게 내미는 그 상을 손에 넣게 될 시각을 애타게 기다렸다. 마침내 시간이 흘러 우리가 그토록 고대하던 정해진 날이 왔다."],["The time passed at length, and the appointed day we so longed for arrived; and, all following out the arrangement and plan which, after careful consideration and many a long discussion, we had decided upon, we succeeded as fully as we could have wished; for on the Friday following the day upon which I spoke to Zoraida in the garden, the renegade anchored his vessel at nightfall almost opposite the spot where she was.","우리는 신중한 숙고와 숱한 긴 논의 끝에 결정한 그 절차와 계획을 모두 따랐고, 바랄 수 있는 만큼 온전히 성공했다. 내가 정원에서 소라이다와 이야기를 나눈 날 이후 다음 금요일, 배교자는 해 질 무렵 그녀가 있던 곳 거의 맞은편에 배의 닻을 내렸다."],["The Christians who were to row were ready and in hiding in different places round about, all waiting for me, anxious and elated, and eager to attack the vessel they had before their eyes; for they did not know the renegade’s plan, but expected that they were to gain their liberty by force of arms and by killing the Moors who were on board the vessel.","노를 저을 기독교도들은 준비를 갖추고 사방 여기저기에 숨어, 모두 나를 기다리며 초조하면서도 들떠 있었고, 눈앞에 있는 배를 덮치고 싶어 안달했다. 그들은 배교자의 계획을 알지 못한 채, 무력으로, 그리고 배에 타고 있는 무어인들을 죽여 자유를 얻으리라 여겼던 것이다."],["As soon, then, as I and my comrades made our appearance, all those that were in hiding seeing us came and joined us.","그리하여 나와 동료들이 모습을 드러내자, 숨어 있던 자들이 모두 우리를 보고 나와 우리에게 합류했다."],["It was now the time when the city gates are shut, and there was no one to be seen in all the space outside.","이제 도시의 성문이 닫히는 시각이라, 성문 밖 온 공간에는 보이는 사람이 하나도 없었다."],["When we were collected together we debated whether it would be better first to go for Zoraida, or to make prisoners of the Moorish rowers who rowed in the vessel; but while we were still uncertain our renegade came up asking us what kept us, as it was now the time, and all the Moors were off their guard and most of them asleep.","우리가 한데 모이자 우리는 먼저 소라이다를 데리러 가는 것이 나을지, 아니면 배에서 노를 젓는 무어 노잡이들을 포로로 잡는 것이 나을지를 의논했다. 그러나 우리가 아직 결정을 못 내리고 있을 때 배교자가 다가와 무엇 때문에 지체하느냐고, 이제 때가 되었으며 무어인들이 모두 방심하고 대부분 잠들어 있다고 물었다."],["We told him why we hesitated, but he said it was of more importance first to secure the vessel, which could be done with the greatest ease and without any danger, and then we could go for Zoraida.","우리가 망설이는 까닭을 일러 주자, 그는 먼저 배를 확보하는 것이 더 중요하다고, 그것은 더없이 손쉽게 아무런 위험 없이 해낼 수 있는 일이며 그러고 나서 소라이다를 데리러 가면 된다고 말했다."],["We all approved of what he said, and so without further delay, guided by him we made for the vessel, and he leaping on board first, drew his cutlass and said in Morisco, “Let no one stir from this if he does not want it to cost him his life.”","우리는 모두 그의 말에 찬성했고, 그리하여 더 지체하지 않고 그의 인도를 받아 배로 향했다. 그가 먼저 배에 뛰어올라 단검을 빼 들고는 모리스코 말로 말했다."],["By this almost all the Christians were on board, and the Moors, who were fainthearted, hearing their captain speak in this way, were cowed, and without any one of them taking to his arms (and indeed they had few or hardly any) they submitted without saying a word to be bound by the Christians, who quickly secured them, threatening them that if they raised any kind of outcry they would be all put to the sword.","\"목숨이 아깝거든 누구도 여기서 꼼짝 마라.\" 이 말과 함께 거의 모든 기독교도가 배에 올랐고, 겁이 많은 무어인들은 자기네 선장이 이런 식으로 말하는 것을 듣고는 기가 꺾여, 누구 하나 무기를 드는 일도 없이(실은 무기랄 것도 거의 없었지만) 군소리 없이 기독교도들에게 결박되었다."],["This having been accomplished, and half of our party being left to keep guard over them, the rest of us, again taking the renegade as our guide, hastened towards Hadji Morato’s garden, and as good luck would have it, on trying the gate it opened as easily as if it had not been locked; and so, quite quietly and in silence, we reached the house without being perceived by anybody.","기독교도들은 그들을 재빨리 묶으며, 조금이라도 소리를 지르면 모조리 칼로 베어 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 이 일을 끝내고 우리 일행의 절반을 남겨 그들을 지키게 한 뒤, 나머지 우리는 다시 배교자를 길잡이로 삼아 하지 모라토의 정원으로 서둘러 갔다."],["The lovely Zoraida was watching for us at a window, and as soon as she perceived that there were people there, she asked in a low voice if we were “Nizarani,”","운 좋게도 문을 밀어 보니 마치 잠겨 있지 않았던 것처럼 쉽게 열렸다. 그리하여 우리는 아주 조용히, 소리 없이, 아무에게도 들키지 않고 집에 다다랐다."],["as much as to say or ask if we were Christians. I answered that we were, and begged her to come down.","아리따운 소라이다는 창가에서 우리를 지켜보고 있었고, 거기 사람들이 있는 것을 알아채자마자 낮은 목소리로 우리가 \"니사라니\"냐고 물었으니, 곧 우리가 기독교도냐고 묻는 셈이었다."],["As soon as she recognised me she did not delay an instant, but without answering a word came down immediately, opened the door and presented herself before us all, so beautiful and so richly attired that I cannot attempt to describe her.","나는 그렇다고 대답하며 그녀에게 내려오라고 청했다. 그녀는 나를 알아보자마자 한순간도 지체하지 않고, 한마디 대답도 없이 곧장 내려와 문을 열고 우리 모두 앞에 모습을 드러냈는데, 어찌나 아름답고 어찌나 화려하게 차려입었는지 나로서는 묘사할 엄두조차 나지 않는다."],["The moment I saw her I took her hand and kissed it, and the renegade and my two comrades did the same; and the rest, who knew nothing of the circumstances, did as they saw us do, for it only seemed as if we were returning thanks to her, and recognising her as the giver of our liberty.","그녀를 본 순간 나는 그녀의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추었고, 배교자와 내 두 동료도 똑같이 했다. 그리고 사정을 전혀 모르는 나머지 사람들도 우리가 하는 것을 보고 그대로 따라 했으니, 그것은 마치 우리가 그녀에게 감사를 표하며 그녀를 우리 자유의 베푼 이로 알아보는 것처럼만 보였다."],["The renegade asked her in the Morisco language if her father was in the house.","배교자가 모리스코 말로 그녀의 아버지가 집에 있느냐고 물었다."],["She replied that he was and that he was asleep.","그녀는 계시며 잠들어 있다고 대답했다."]],[["“Then it will be necessary to waken him and take him with us,”","\"그러면 그분을 깨워 우리와 함께 데려가야 하겠습니다.\""],["said the renegade, “and everything of value in this fair mansion.”","배교자가 말했다. \"그리고 이 좋은 저택 안의 값진 것은 모조리 말입니다.\""]],[["“Nay,”","\"안 됩니다.\" 그녀가 말했다."],["said she, “my father must not on any account be touched, and there is nothing in the house except what I shall take, and that will be quite enough to enrich and satisfy all of you; wait a little and you shall see,”","\"제 아버지께는 무슨 일이 있어도 손을 대서는 안 돼요. 그리고 집 안에는 제가 가져갈 것 말고는 아무것도 없으나, 그것만으로도 여러분 모두를 넉넉히 부유하게 하고 만족시키기에 충분할 거예요."],["and so saying she went in, telling us she would return immediately and bidding us keep quiet making any noise.","잠시만 기다리시면 보게 될 거예요.\" 그렇게 말하고 그녀는 안으로 들어가며, 곧 돌아오겠으니 소리를 내지 말고 조용히 있으라고 우리에게 당부했다."]],[["I asked the renegade what had passed between them, and when he told me, I declared that nothing should be done except in accordance with the wishes of Zoraida, who now came back with a little trunk so full of gold crowns that she could scarcely carry it.","나는 배교자에게 둘 사이에 무슨 말이 오갔는지 물었고, 그가 말해 주자, 나는 무슨 일이든 소라이다의 뜻에 따르지 않고서는 아무것도 해서는 안 된다고 단언했다. 이제 그녀가 황금 크라운이 가득 차서 들기조차 힘들 만큼 묵직한 작은 궤짝 하나를 들고 돌아왔다."],["Unfortunately her father awoke while this was going on, and hearing a noise in the garden, came to the window, and at once perceiving that all those who were there were Christians, raising a prodigiously loud outcry, he began to call out in Arabic, “Christians, Christians!","불행히도 이 일이 벌어지는 동안 그녀의 아버지가 잠에서 깨어 정원에서 나는 소리를 듣고 창가로 와서는, 거기 있는 자들이 모두 기독교도임을 단번에 알아채고 엄청나게 큰 소리를 지르며 아랍어로 외치기 시작했다. \"기독교도다, 기독교도!"],["thieves, thieves!”","도둑이다, 도둑이야!\""],["by which cries we were all thrown into the greatest fear and embarrassment; but the renegade seeing the danger we were in and how important it was for him to effect his purpose before we were heard, mounted with the utmost quickness to where Hadji Morato was, and with him went some of our party; I, however, did not dare to leave Zoraida, who had fallen almost fainting in my arms.","이 외침에 우리는 모두 더없는 두려움과 당혹에 빠졌다. 그러나 우리가 처한 위험과, 들키기 전에 자기 목적을 이루는 것이 자신에게 얼마나 중요한지를 안 배교자는 더없이 재빨리 하지 모라토가 있는 곳으로 올라갔고, 그와 함께 우리 일행 몇이 따라 올라갔다."],["To be brief, those who had gone upstairs acted so promptly that in an instant they came down, carrying Hadji Morato with his hands bound and a napkin tied over his mouth, which prevented him from uttering a word, warning him at the same time that to attempt to speak would cost him his life.","그러나 나는 거의 기절한 채 내 품에 쓰러져 있던 소라이다를 차마 떠날 수 없었다. 간단히 말해, 위로 올라간 자들이 어찌나 신속하게 움직였던지 눈 깜짝할 사이에 하지 모라토를 데리고 내려왔는데, 그의 두 손은 묶이고 입에는 천이 동여매여 한마디도 내뱉지 못하게 되어 있었으며, 동시에 입을 열려 들면 목숨을 잃게 되리라는 경고도 받은 터였다."],["When his daughter caught sight of him she covered her eyes so as not to see him, and her father was horror-stricken, not knowing how willingly she had placed herself in our hands.","그의 딸은 아버지를 보자 차마 보지 못해 두 눈을 가렸고, 그녀가 얼마나 기꺼이 우리 손에 몸을 맡겼는지 알 리 없는 아버지는 경악에 사로잡혔다."],["But it was now most essential for us to be on the move, and carefully and quickly we regained the vessel, where those who had remained on board were waiting for us in apprehension of some mishap having befallen us.","그러나 이제 우리가 서둘러 움직이는 것이 무엇보다 긴요했으므로, 우리는 조심스럽고도 재빠르게 배로 돌아왔다. 거기서는 배에 남아 있던 자들이 우리에게 무슨 변고라도 생겼을까 염려하며 우리를 기다리고 있었다."],["It was barely two hours after night set in when we were all on board the vessel, where the cords were removed from the hands of Zoraida’s father, and the napkin from his mouth; but the renegade once more told him not to utter a word, or they would take his life.","우리가 모두 배에 오른 것은 밤이 든 지 겨우 두 시간쯤 지나서였다. 거기서 소라이다 아버지의 손에서 밧줄을 풀고 입에서 천을 떼어 냈으나, 배교자는 한마디라도 내뱉으면 목숨을 빼앗겠다고 그에게 다시 한번 일렀다."],["He, when he saw his daughter there, began to sigh piteously, and still more when he perceived that I held her closely embraced and that she lay quiet without resisting or complaining, or showing any reluctance; nevertheless he remained silent lest they should carry into effect the repeated threats the renegade had addressed to him.","그는 거기 자기 딸이 있는 것을 보자 가엾게 한숨짓기 시작했고, 내가 그녀를 꼭 끌어안고 있으며 그녀가 저항하거나 하소연하거나 꺼리는 기색도 없이 가만히 안겨 있는 것을 알아챘을 때 더욱더 그러했다. 그럼에도 그는 배교자가 거듭 던진 위협을 그들이 실행에 옮길까 두려워 입을 다물고 있었다."]],[["Finding herself now on board, and that we were about to give way with the oars, Zoraida, seeing her father there, and the other Moors bound, bade the renegade ask me to do her the favour of releasing the Moors and setting her father at liberty, for she would rather drown herself in the sea than suffer a father that had loved her so dearly to be carried away captive before her eyes and on her account. The renegade repeated this to me, and I replied that I was very willing to do so; but he replied that it was not advisable, because if they were left there they would at once raise the country and stir up the city, and lead to the despatch of swift cruisers in pursuit, and our being taken, by sea or land, without any possibility of escape; and that all that could be done was to set them free on the first Christian ground we reached. On this point we all agreed; and Zoraida, to whom it was explained, together with the reasons that prevented us from doing at once what she desired, was satisfied likewise; and then in glad silence and with cheerful alacrity each of our stout rowers took his oar, and commending ourselves to God with all our hearts, we began to shape our course for the island of Majorca, the nearest Christian land. Owing, however, to the Tramontana rising a little, and the sea growing somewhat rough, it was impossible for us to keep a straight course for Majorca, and we were compelled to coast in the direction of Oran, not without great uneasiness on our part lest we should be observed from the town of Shershel, which lies on that coast, not more than sixty miles from Algiers. Moreover we were afraid of meeting on that course one of the galliots that usually come with goods from Tetuan; although each of us for himself and all of us together felt confident that, if we were to meet a merchant galliot, so that it were not a cruiser, not only should we not be lost, but that we should take a vessel in which we could more safely accomplish our voyage. As we pursued our course Zoraida kept her head between my hands so as not to see her father, and I felt that she was praying to Lela Marien to help us.","이제 배에 오른 자신을 발견하고 우리가 막 노를 저으려는 것을 본 소라이다는, 거기 자기 아버지가 있고 다른 무어인들이 묶여 있는 것을 보고는, 배교자를 시켜 내게 부탁하기를, 무어인들을 풀어 주고 자기 아버지를 자유롭게 해 주는 호의를 베풀어 달라고 했다. 자기를 그토록 애틋이 사랑한 아버지가 자기 눈앞에서, 자기 탓에 포로로 끌려가는 것을 보느니 차라리 바다에 몸을 던져 죽는 편이 낫겠다는 것이었다. 배교자가 이를 내게 거듭 전했고, 나는 기꺼이 그리하겠다고 대답했다. 그러나 그는 그것이 현명하지 못하다고 했으니, 만일 그들을 거기 남겨 둔다면 그들이 곧 온 고장을 들쑤시고 도시를 소동에 빠뜨려, 빠른 순항선들을 추격에 내보내게 하고, 그리하여 우리가 바다에서든 뭍에서든 빠져나갈 길 없이 붙잡히게 될 것이기 때문이라고 했다. 그러니 할 수 있는 일이라고는 우리가 처음으로 닿는 기독교 땅에서 그들을 풀어 주는 것뿐이라고 했다. 이 점에 대해 우리는 모두 동의했다. 그리고 그녀가 바라는 일을 당장 할 수 없게 만든 이유와 함께 사정을 설명받은 소라이다도 마찬가지로 수긍했다. 그리하여 기쁜 침묵 속에서, 즐거운 민첩함으로 우리의 건장한 노잡이들이 저마다 노를 잡았고, 우리는 온 마음을 다해 하느님께 우리를 의탁하며 가장 가까운 기독교 땅인 마요르카섬을 향해 뱃길을 잡기 시작했다. 그러나 트라몬타나 바람이 조금 일고 바다가 다소 거칠어진 탓에 마요르카로 곧장 항로를 잡기란 불가능했고, 우리는 오랑 방향을 따라 연안을 항해할 수밖에 없었다. 알제에서 육십 마일도 채 떨어지지 않은 그 연안에 자리한 셰르셀 마을에서 우리가 눈에 띌까 큰 불안을 느끼면서 말이다. 게다가 우리는 그 항로에서, 흔히 테투안에서 물자를 싣고 오는 갤리엇 한 척과 마주칠까 두려웠다. 다만 우리 각자, 또 우리 모두가 한데 모여 자신했던 것은, 만일 우리가 순항선이 아닌 상선 갤리엇과 마주친다면 우리가 잃을 것이 없을 뿐 아니라, 오히려 우리의 항해를 더 안전하게 이룰 수 있는 배 한 척을 빼앗게 되리라는 것이었다. 항로를 따라가는 동안 소라이다는 아버지를 보지 않으려고 내 두 손 사이에 머리를 묻고 있었고, 나는 그녀가 우리를 도와 달라고 렐라 마리엔에게 기도하고 있음을 느꼈다."]],[["We might have made about thirty miles when daybreak found us some three musket-shots off the land, which seemed to us deserted, and without anyone to see us.","우리가 한 삼십 마일쯤 갔을 무렵 동이 트면서 우리가 뭍에서 총 세 방 쏠 거리쯤 떨어져 있음을 알게 되었는데, 뭍은 인적이 끊긴 듯했고 우리를 볼 사람도 없는 듯했다."],["For all that, however, by hard rowing we put out a little to sea, for it was now somewhat calmer, and having gained about two leagues the word was given to row by batches, while we ate something, for the vessel was well provided; but the rowers said it was not a time to take any rest; let food be served out to those who were not rowing, but they would not leave their oars on any account.","그래도 우리는 힘껏 노를 저어 바다 쪽으로 조금 나아갔으니, 이제 다소 잔잔해진 까닭이었다. 두 레과쯤 나아가고 나서, 우리가 무언가를 먹는 동안 패를 나누어 노를 젓자는 말이 나왔으니, 배에는 양식이 넉넉히 갖추어져 있었다."],["This was done, but now a stiff breeze began to blow, which obliged us to leave off rowing and make sail at once and steer for Oran, as it was impossible to make any other course.","그러나 노잡이들은 지금은 쉴 때가 아니라고 했다. 노를 젓지 않는 자들에게는 음식을 나누어 주되, 자기들은 무슨 일이 있어도 노를 놓지 않겠다는 것이었다."],["All this was done very promptly, and under sail we ran more than eight miles an hour without any fear, except that of coming across some vessel out on a roving expedition.","그대로 했으나, 이제 거센 바람이 불기 시작해 우리는 노 젓기를 그만두고 곧장 돛을 올려 오랑을 향해 키를 잡을 수밖에 없었으니, 다른 항로를 잡기란 불가능했기 때문이다."],["We gave the Moorish rowers some food, and the renegade comforted them by telling them that they were not held as captives, as we should set them free on the first opportunity.","이 모든 것을 매우 신속히 해냈고, 돛을 올린 채 우리는 시속 팔 마일이 넘게 달렸으니, 떠돌이 약탈을 나선 어떤 배와 마주칠까 하는 두려움 외에는 아무 걱정이 없었다. 우리는 무어 노잡이들에게 음식을 좀 주었고, 배교자는 그들이 포로로 잡혀 있는 것이 아니라 첫 기회가 닿는 대로 풀어 주겠노라 일러 주며 그들을 달랬다."]],[["The same was said to Zoraida’s father, who replied, “Anything else, Christian, I might hope for or think likely from your generosity and good behaviour, but do not think me so simple as to imagine you will give me my liberty; for you would have never exposed yourselves to the danger of depriving me of it only to restore it to me so generously, especially as you know who I am and the sum you may expect to receive on restoring it; and if you will only name that, I here offer you all you require for myself and for my unhappy daughter there; or else for her alone, for she is the greatest and most precious part of my soul.”","소라이다의 아버지에게도 같은 말을 했더니 그가 대답했다. \"기독교도여, 그대들의 너그러움과 훌륭한 처신에서 다른 것이라면 무엇이든 바라거나 그럴 법하다 여길 수 있겠으나, 그대들이 내게 자유를 주리라 여길 만큼 나를 그리 순진한 자로 보지는 마시오. 그대들은 한낱 그것을 이리도 너그러이 되돌려 주려고 그것을 내게서 빼앗는 위험에 스스로를 내맡겼을 리 없으니, 더구나 내가 누구인지, 또 그것을 되돌려 줌으로써 그대들이 받을 만한 금액이 얼마인지 알고 있으면서 말이오. 그러니 그대들이 그 액수를 말하기만 한다면, 나는 여기서 나 자신과 저기 있는 불행한 내 딸을 위해 그대들이 요구하는 모든 것을 내놓겠소. 아니면 저 아이 하나만을 위해서라도 그러겠소. 저 아이는 내 영혼의 가장 크고 가장 소중한 부분이니 말이오.\""]],[["As he said this he began to weep so bitterly that he filled us all with compassion and forced Zoraida to look at him, and when she saw him weeping she was so moved that she rose from my feet and ran to throw her arms round him, and pressing her face to his, they both gave way to such an outburst of tears that several of us were constrained to keep them company.","이 말을 하면서 그는 어찌나 비통하게 울기 시작했던지 우리 모두를 가엾은 마음으로 채웠고, 소라이다로 하여금 그를 바라보지 않을 수 없게 만들었다. 그녀는 아버지가 우는 것을 보자 어찌나 마음이 움직였던지 내 발치에서 일어나 그에게 달려가 두 팔로 끌어안았고, 자기 얼굴을 그의 얼굴에 맞대니, 두 사람은 그토록 격하게 울음을 터뜨려 우리 가운데 여럿도 그들과 함께 울지 않을 수 없었다."]],[["But when her father saw her in full dress and with all her jewels about her, he said to her in his own language, “What means this, my daughter?","그러나 그녀의 아버지는 그녀가 성장(盛裝)을 하고 온갖 보석을 몸에 두른 것을 보자 자기네 말로 그녀에게 말했다. \"이게 무슨 일이냐, 내 딸아?"],["Last night, before this terrible misfortune in which we are plunged befell us, I saw thee in thy everyday and indoor garments; and now, without having had time to attire thyself, and without my bringing thee any joyful tidings to furnish an occasion for adorning and bedecking thyself, I see thee arrayed in the finest attire it would be in my power to give thee when fortune was most kind to us.","우리가 빠져든 이 끔찍한 불행이 닥치기 전인 어젯밤만 해도 나는 너의 평상복, 집에서 입는 옷차림을 보았다. 그런데 지금, 옷을 차려입을 겨를도 없었고, 내가 너를 단장하고 꾸밀 구실이 될 만한 어떤 기쁜 소식을 가져다준 것도 아닌데, 운수가 우리에게 가장 후했던 시절 내가 너에게 줄 수 있었을 가장 좋은 차림으로 단장한 너를 보는구나."],["Answer me this; for it causes me greater anxiety and surprise than even this misfortune itself.”","내게 이것을 답해 보아라. 이는 이 불행 자체보다도 내게 더 큰 근심과 놀라움을 안기는 일이니라.\""]],[["The renegade interpreted to us what the Moor said to his daughter; she, however, returned him no answer.","배교자가 무어인이 자기 딸에게 한 말을 우리에게 통역해 주었다. 그러나 그녀는 그에게 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."],["But when he observed in one corner of the vessel the little trunk in which she used to keep her jewels, which he well knew he had left in Algiers and had not brought to the garden, he was still more amazed, and asked her how that trunk had come into our hands, and what there was in it.","한데 그가 배 한구석에서 그녀가 보석을 넣어 두던 그 작은 궤짝을 보았는데, 그것을 알제에 두고 정원으로 가져오지 않았음을 잘 알고 있던 터라, 그는 더욱더 놀라 그 궤짝이 어떻게 우리 손에 들어왔으며 그 안에 무엇이 들었느냐고 그녀에게 물었다. 이에 소라이다가 대답하기를 기다리지 않고 배교자가 대답했다."],["To which the renegade, without waiting for Zoraida to reply, made answer, “Do not trouble thyself by asking thy daughter Zoraida so many questions, señor, for the one answer I will give thee will serve for all; I would have thee know that she is a Christian, and that it is she who has been the file for our chains and our deliverer from captivity.","\"그대의 딸 소라이다에게 그리 많은 것을 묻느라 애쓰지 마시오, 나리. 내가 그대에게 줄 한 가지 대답이 그 모든 것을 갈음할 테니 말이오."],["She is here of her own free will, as glad, I imagine, to find herself in this position as he who escapes from darkness into the light, from death to life, and from suffering to glory.”","그대가 알아야 할 것은, 저 아이가 기독교도이며, 우리의 사슬을 끊어 준 줄칼이요 포로 신세에서 우리를 건져 낸 구원자가 바로 저 아이라는 사실이오. 저 아이는 제 발로 여기 왔으며, 내 짐작에는, 어둠에서 빛으로, 죽음에서 생명으로, 고통에서 영광으로 벗어난 자처럼 자신이 이 처지에 놓인 것을 기뻐하고 있소.\""]],[["“Daughter, is this true, what he says?”","\"딸아, 저자가 하는 말이 정녕 사실이냐?\""],["cried the Moor.","무어인이 외쳤다."]],[["“It is,”","\"사실이에요.\""],["replied Zoraida.","소라이다가 대답했다."]],[["“That thou art in truth a Christian,”","\"네가 정녕 기독교도라는 것이,\""],["said the old man, “and that thou hast given thy father into the power of his enemies?”","노인이 말했다. \"그리고 네가 네 아비를 그 원수들의 손에 넘겨주었다는 것이?\""]],[["To which Zoraida made answer, “A Christian I am, but it is not I who have placed thee in this position, for it never was my wish to leave thee or do thee harm, but only to do good to myself.”","이에 소라이다가 대답했다. \"제가 기독교도인 것은 맞아요. 그러나 아버지를 이 처지에 놓이게 한 것은 제가 아니에요. 저는 결코 아버지를 떠나거나 아버지께 해를 끼치고 싶었던 게 아니라, 다만 제 자신에게 좋은 일을 하고 싶었을 뿐이니까요.\""]],[["“And what good hast thou done thyself, daughter?”","\"그래, 네가 네 자신에게 무슨 좋은 일을 했단 말이냐, 딸아?\""],["said he.","그가 말했다."]],[["“Ask thou that,”","\"그건,\" 그녀가 말했다."],["said she, “of Lela Marien, for she can tell thee better than I.”","\"렐라 마리엔께 여쭈어 보세요. 저보다 그분이 더 잘 일러 주실 거예요.\""]],[["The Moor had hardly heard these words when with marvellous quickness he flung himself headforemost into the sea, where no doubt he would have been drowned had not the long and full dress he wore held him up for a little on the surface of the water.","무어인은 이 말을 듣기가 무섭게 놀랍도록 잽싸게 머리부터 바다에 몸을 던졌고, 그가 입은 길고 풍성한 옷이 잠시 그를 물 위에 떠받쳐 주지 않았더라면 틀림없이 익사했을 것이다."],["Zoraida cried aloud to us to save him, and we all hastened to help, and seizing him by his robe we drew him in half drowned and insensible, at which Zoraida was in such distress that she wept over him as piteously and bitterly as though he were already dead.","소라이다가 우리에게 그를 구해 달라고 큰 소리로 외쳤고, 우리는 모두 서둘러 도우러 가서 그의 옷자락을 붙잡아 반쯤 익사해 정신을 잃은 그를 끌어 올렸다. 이에 소라이다는 어찌나 비통해하던지 그가 이미 죽기라도 한 양 가엾고 애절하게 그를 두고 울었다."],["We turned him upon his face and he voided a great quantity of water, and at the end of two hours came to himself.","우리는 그를 엎어 놓았고 그는 엄청난 양의 물을 토해 냈으며, 두 시간이 지나서야 정신을 차렸다."],["Meanwhile, the wind having changed we were compelled to head for the land, and ply our oars to avoid being driven on shore; but it was our good fortune to reach a creek that lies on one side of a small promontory or cape, called by the Moors that of the “Cava rumia,”","그러는 사이 바람이 바뀌어 우리는 뭍을 향해 뱃머리를 돌리고 노를 부지런히 저어 해안으로 떠밀리지 않도록 해야 했다. 그러나 다행히도 우리는 작은 곶이나 갑(岬)의 한쪽에 자리한 후미에 다다랐는데, 무어인들은 그곳을 \"카바 루미아\"의 곶이라 부르니, 우리말로 \"사악한 기독교도 여인\"이라는 뜻이다."],["which in our language means “the wicked Christian woman;” for it is a tradition among them that La Cava, through whom Spain was lost, lies buried at that spot; “cava”","그들 사이에는 스페인이 멸망하게 된 원인인 라 카바가 그 자리에 묻혀 있다는 전설이 있기 때문이다."],["in their language meaning “wicked woman,” and “rumia”","그들 말로 \"카바\"는 \"사악한 여인\"을, \"루미아\"는 \"기독교도\"를 뜻한다."],["“Christian;” moreover, they count it unlucky to anchor there when necessity compels them, and they never do so otherwise.","게다가 그들은 어쩔 수 없이 그곳에 닻을 내리는 것을 불길하게 여기며, 그렇지 않고서는 결코 거기 닻을 내리지 않는다."],["For us, however, it was not the resting-place of the wicked woman but a haven of safety for our relief, so much had the sea now got up.","그러나 우리에게 그곳은 사악한 여인의 안식처가 아니라 우리를 구해 줄 안전한 피난처였으니, 이제 바다가 그만큼 험해진 까닭이었다."],["We posted a look-out on shore, and never let the oars out of our hands, and ate of the stores the renegade had laid in, imploring God and Our Lady with all our hearts to help and protect us, that we might give a happy ending to a beginning so prosperous.","우리는 뭍에 망보는 자를 세워 두고 결코 노를 손에서 놓지 않았으며, 배교자가 마련해 둔 양식을 먹으면서, 이토록 순조롭게 시작한 일에 행복한 결말을 줄 수 있도록 우리를 도와 지켜 주십사 하느님과 성모님께 온 마음으로 빌었다."],["At the entreaty of Zoraida orders were given to set on shore her father and the other Moors who were still bound, for she could not endure, nor could her tender heart bear to see her father in bonds and her fellow-countrymen prisoners before her eyes.","소라이다의 간청에 따라, 아직 묶여 있던 그녀의 아버지와 다른 무어인들을 뭍에 내려 주라는 명이 내려졌으니, 그녀는 자기 아버지가 결박당하고 동포들이 자기 눈앞에서 포로가 되어 있는 것을 차마 견딜 수도, 그 여린 마음으로 두고 볼 수도 없었기 때문이다."],["We promised her to do this at the moment of departure, for as it was uninhabited we ran no risk in releasing them at that place.","우리는 떠날 순간에 그리하겠노라 그녀에게 약속했으니, 그곳은 사람이 살지 않아 거기서 그들을 풀어 주어도 아무런 위험이 없었기 때문이다."]],[["Our prayers were not so far in vain as to be unheard by Heaven, for after a while the wind changed in our favour, and made the sea calm, inviting us once more to resume our voyage with a good heart.","우리의 기도가 하늘에 닿지 않을 만큼 헛되지는 않았던지, 얼마 뒤 바람이 우리에게 유리하게 바뀌어 바다를 잔잔하게 만들었고, 우리더러 다시 한번 기운차게 항해를 이어 가라고 부추겼다."],["Seeing this we unbound the Moors, and one by one put them on shore, at which they were filled with amazement; but when we came to land Zoraida’s father, who had now completely recovered his senses, he said:","이를 보고 우리는 무어인들의 결박을 풀어 한 사람씩 뭍에 내려 주었으니, 그들은 경악으로 가득 찼다. 그러나 우리가 이제 완전히 정신을 차린 소라이다의 아버지를 뭍에 내리려 하자 그가 말했다."]],[["“Why is it, think ye, Christians, that this wicked woman is rejoiced at your giving me my liberty?","\"기독교도들이여, 이 사악한 계집이 그대들이 내게 자유를 주는 것을 기뻐하는 까닭이 무엇이라 생각하오?"],["Think ye it is because of the affection she bears me?","저 아이가 내게 품은 정 때문이라 여기오?"],["Nay verily, it is only because of the hindrance my presence offers to the execution of her base designs.","천만에, 결코 아니오. 그저 내가 있으면 저 아이의 비열한 음모를 실행하는 데 방해가 되기 때문일 뿐이오."],["And think not that it is her belief that yours is better than ours that has led her to change her religion; it is only because she knows that immodesty is more freely practised in your country than in ours.”","또 그대들 종교가 우리 것보다 낫다고 믿어서 저 아이가 제 신앙을 바꾸었다고 여기지 마시오. 그저 그대들 나라에서 우리 나라에서보다 음란함이 더 거리낌 없이 행해진다는 것을 저 아이가 알기 때문일 뿐이오.\""],["Then turning to Zoraida, while I and another of the Christians held him fast by both arms, lest he should do some mad act, he said to her, “Infamous girl, misguided maiden, whither in thy blindness and madness art thou going in the hands of these dogs, our natural enemies?","그러고는 내가 또 다른 기독교도 한 사람과 함께, 그가 무슨 미친 짓이라도 할까 봐 그의 두 팔을 단단히 붙든 가운데, 그가 그녀에게 돌아서서 말했다. \"치욕스러운 계집아, 길 잃은 처녀야, 너는 눈멀고 미쳐서 우리의 천적인 이 개들의 손에 끌려 어디로 가려는 것이냐?"],["Cursed be the hour when I begot thee!","너를 낳은 시각이 저주받을지어다!"],["Cursed the luxury and indulgence in which I reared thee!”","너를 호사와 응석 속에 길러 낸 일이 저주받을지어다!\""]],[["But seeing that he was not likely soon to cease I made haste to put him on shore, and thence he continued his maledictions and lamentations aloud; calling on Mohammed to pray to Allah to destroy us, to confound us, to make an end of us; and when, in consequence of having made sail, we could no longer hear what he said we could see what he did; how he plucked out his beard and tore his hair and lay writhing on the ground. But once he raised his voice to such a pitch that we were able to hear what he said. “Come back, dear daughter, come back to shore; I forgive thee all; let those men have the money, for it is theirs now, and come back to comfort thy sorrowing father, who will yield up his life on this barren strand if thou dost leave him.”","그러나 그가 좀처럼 멈출 것 같지 않은 것을 보고 나는 서둘러 그를 뭍에 내려놓았고, 거기서도 그는 큰 소리로 저주와 한탄을 이어 갔다. 마호메트더러 우리를 멸하고, 혼란에 빠뜨리고, 끝장내 달라고 알라께 빌라고 부르짖으면서 말이다. 그리고 돛을 올린 탓에 그가 하는 말이 더는 들리지 않게 되었을 때도, 우리는 그가 무엇을 하는지는 볼 수 있었다. 그가 어떻게 제 수염을 쥐어뜯고 머리카락을 잡아 뜯으며 땅바닥에 몸부림치며 누웠는지를 말이다. 그러나 한번은 그가 어찌나 높이 목청을 돋우었던지 우리가 그의 말을 들을 수 있었다. \"돌아오너라, 사랑하는 딸아, 뭍으로 돌아오너라. 내 너의 모든 것을 용서하마. 저 사내들에게 돈은 주어 버려라, 이제 그건 저들의 것이니. 그리고 돌아와 슬픔에 잠긴 네 아비를 위로해 다오. 네가 떠난다면 이 메마른 해변에서 목숨을 내놓고 말 네 아비를 말이다.\""]],[["All this Zoraida heard, and heard with sorrow and tears, and all she could say in answer was, “Allah grant that Lela Marien, who has made me become a Christian, give thee comfort in thy sorrow, my father. Allah knows that I could not do otherwise than I have done, and that these Christians owe nothing to my will; for even had I wished not to accompany them, but remain at home, it would have been impossible for me, so eagerly did my soul urge me on to the accomplishment of this purpose, which I feel to be as righteous as to thee, dear father, it seems wicked.”","이 모든 것을 소라이다는 들었으니, 슬픔과 눈물로 들었다. 그리고 그녀가 대답으로 할 수 있었던 말이라고는 이것뿐이었다. \"알라께서 부디, 저를 기독교도가 되게 하신 렐라 마리엔으로 하여금 아버지의 슬픔에 위안을 주시게 하소서. 알라께서는 제가 한 것 말고는 달리 어쩔 수 없었음을, 그리고 이 기독교도들이 제 뜻에 빚진 것이 아무것도 없음을 아십니다. 제가 그들을 따라가지 않고 집에 남기를 바랐다 한들, 그것은 제게 불가능했을 테니까요. 제 영혼이 어찌나 간절히 저를 이 목적의 성취로 떠밀었던지요. 사랑하는 아버지께는 그것이 사악해 보이는 만큼이나 제게는 그것이 의로운 것으로 느껴지니까요.\""]],[["But neither could her father hear her nor we see him when she said this; and so, while I consoled Zoraida, we turned our attention to our voyage, in which a breeze from the right point so favoured us that we made sure of finding ourselves off the coast of Spain on the morrow by daybreak. But, as good seldom or never comes pure and unmixed, without being attended or followed by some disturbing evil that gives a shock to it, our fortune, or perhaps the curses which the Moor had hurled at his daughter (for whatever kind of father they may come from these are always to be dreaded), brought it about that when we were now in mid-sea, and the night about three hours spent, as we were running with all sail set and oars lashed, for the favouring breeze saved us the trouble of using them, we saw by the light of the moon, which shone brilliantly, a square-rigged vessel in full sail close to us, luffing up and standing across our course, and so close that we had to strike sail to avoid running foul of her, while they too put the helm hard up to let us pass. They came to the side of the ship to ask who we were, whither we were bound, and whence we came, but as they asked this in French our renegade said, “Let no one answer, for no doubt these are French corsairs who plunder all comers.”","그러나 그녀가 이 말을 할 때 아버지는 그녀의 말을 들을 수도 없었고 우리도 그를 볼 수 없었다. 그리하여 내가 소라이다를 달래는 동안 우리는 우리의 항해에 마음을 쏟았고, 알맞은 방향에서 부는 바람이 어찌나 우리를 도왔던지 우리는 이튿날 동틀 무렵이면 틀림없이 스페인 연안에 다다라 있으리라 믿어 의심치 않았다. 그러나 좋은 일이란 좀처럼, 아니 결코 순수하고 섞임 없이 오는 법이 없고, 거기에 충격을 주는 어떤 어지러운 재앙이 따르거나 뒤이어 오기 마련이라, 우리의 운명, 아니 어쩌면 무어인이 제 딸에게 퍼부은 저주가(어떤 아비에게서 나온 것이든 이런 저주는 늘 두려워할 만하니) 일을 이렇게 만들었다. 우리가 이제 바다 한복판에 있고 밤이 세 시간쯤 지났을 무렵, 돛을 모두 올리고 노는 묶어 둔 채(순풍이 노를 쓸 수고를 덜어 준 까닭이었다) 달리고 있을 때, 휘영청 밝게 빛나는 달빛으로 우리는 돛을 모두 펼친 사각돛배 한 척이 우리 가까이에서 바람을 거슬러 오르며 우리 항로를 가로질러 다가오는 것을 보았다. 어찌나 가까웠던지 우리는 그 배와 충돌하지 않으려 돛을 내려야 했고, 그들 또한 우리를 지나가게 하려고 키를 한껏 위로 꺾었다. 그들은 배 옆으로 다가와 우리가 누구이며 어디로 향하고 어디서 왔는지를 물었으나, 그것을 프랑스어로 물었으므로 우리 배교자가 말했다. \"아무도 대답하지 마시오. 이들은 틀림없이 오는 자마다 약탈하는 프랑스 해적들이오.\""]],[["Acting on this warning no one answered a word, but after we had gone a little ahead, and the vessel was now lying to leeward, suddenly they fired two guns, and apparently both loaded with chain-shot, for with one they cut our mast in half and brought down both it and the sail into the sea, and the other, discharged at the same moment, sent a ball into our vessel amidships, staving her in completely, but without doing any further damage.","이 경고에 따라 아무도 한마디 대답하지 않았으나, 우리가 조금 앞서 나가 그 배가 이제 바람 아래쪽에 놓이게 되었을 때, 갑자기 그들이 대포 두 방을 쏘았는데 둘 다 사슬탄을 장전한 듯했다. 한 방으로는 우리 돛대를 반으로 잘라 돛대와 돛을 함께 바다로 떨어뜨렸고, 동시에 발사된 다른 한 방은 우리 배 한가운데에 포탄을 박아 배를 완전히 부수었으나 더 이상의 손상은 입히지 않았다."],["We, however, finding ourselves sinking began to shout for help and call upon those in the ship to pick us up as we were beginning to fill.","그러나 우리는 배가 가라앉는 것을 알고는 도움을 청해 소리치기 시작했고, 우리가 물에 잠겨 가니 배에 탄 자들에게 우리를 건져 달라고 외쳤다."],["They then lay to, and lowering a skiff or boat, as many as a dozen Frenchmen, well armed with match-locks, and their matches burning, got into it and came alongside; and seeing how few we were, and that our vessel was going down, they took us in, telling us that this had come to us through our incivility in not giving them an answer.","그러자 그들은 배를 세우고 작은 거룻배인지 보트를 내렸으며, 화승총으로 단단히 무장하고 화승에 불을 붙인 프랑스인 열두 명쯤이 거기 올라타 우리 곁으로 다가왔다. 그리고 우리 수가 얼마 안 되고 우리 배가 가라앉고 있는 것을 보고는, 이 일이 우리가 자기들에게 대답하지 않은 무례 탓에 닥친 것이라 말하며 우리를 거두어 주었다."],["Our renegade took the trunk containing Zoraida’s wealth and dropped it into the sea without anyone perceiving what he did.","우리 배교자는 소라이다의 재물이 담긴 궤짝을 들어, 자기가 무엇을 하는지 아무도 알아채지 못하게 바다에 떨어뜨렸다."],["In short we went on board with the Frenchmen, who, after having ascertained all they wanted to know about us, rifled us of everything we had, as if they had been our bitterest enemies, and from Zoraida they took even the anklets she wore on her feet; but the distress they caused her did not distress me so much as the fear I was in that from robbing her of her rich and precious jewels they would proceed to rob her of the most precious jewel that she valued more than all.","요컨대 우리는 프랑스인들과 함께 배에 올랐는데, 그들은 우리에 관해 알고 싶은 모든 것을 알아내고 나자, 마치 우리의 가장 지독한 원수이기라도 한 양 우리가 가진 모든 것을 털어 갔으며, 소라이다에게서는 발에 차고 있던 발찌마저 빼앗았다. 그러나 그들이 그녀에게 끼친 괴로움도, 그들이 그녀의 값지고 귀한 보석을 약탈한 데서 더 나아가 그녀가 모든 것보다 더 소중히 여기는 가장 귀한 보석마저 약탈하지나 않을까 하는 내 두려움만큼 나를 괴롭히지는 못했다."],["The desires, however, of those people do not go beyond money, but of that their covetousness is insatiable, and on this occasion it was carried to such a pitch that they would have taken even the clothes we wore as captives if they had been worth anything to them.","그러나 그자들의 욕망은 돈을 넘어서지 않으며, 그 점에 있어서 그들의 탐욕은 채울 길이 없으니, 이때에도 그것이 어찌나 극에 달했던지, 그들에게 조금이라도 값나가는 것이었다면 우리가 포로로 입고 있던 옷가지마저 빼앗아 갔을 것이다."],["It was the advice of some of them to throw us all into the sea wrapped up in a sail; for their purpose was to trade at some of the ports of Spain, giving themselves out as Bretons, and if they brought us alive they would be punished as soon as the robbery was discovered; but the captain (who was the one who had plundered my beloved Zoraida) said he was satisfied with the prize he had got, and that he would not touch at any Spanish port, but pass the Straits of Gibraltar by night, or as best he could, and make for La Rochelle, from which he had sailed.","그들 가운데 더러는 우리를 모두 돛에 싸서 바다에 던져 버리자고 권했으니, 그들의 속셈은 스페인의 어느 항구들에서 자기들을 브르타뉴 사람으로 내세우며 거래를 하려는 것이었는데, 만일 우리를 산 채로 데려간다면 약탈이 발각되는 즉시 벌을 받게 되리라는 것이었다. 그러나 선장은(내 사랑하는 소라이다를 약탈한 바로 그자였다) 자기가 얻은 노획물에 만족한다고, 자기는 스페인의 어떤 항구에도 들르지 않고 밤에, 혹은 할 수 있는 한 어떻게든 지브롤터 해협을 지나, 자기가 출항한 라로셸로 향하겠다고 말했다."],["So they agreed by common consent to give us the skiff belonging to their ship and all we required for the short voyage that remained to us, and this they did the next day on coming in sight of the Spanish coast, with which, and the joy we felt, all our sufferings and miseries were as completely forgotten as if they had never been endured by us, such is the delight of recovering lost liberty.","그리하여 그들은 공동의 합의로, 자기들 배에 딸린 거룻배와 우리에게 남은 짧은 항해에 필요한 모든 것을 우리에게 주기로 했다. 그들은 스페인 연안이 보이게 된 이튿날 그리해 주었고, 그 연안과 우리가 느낀 기쁨에 우리의 모든 고초와 비참함은 마치 우리가 단 한 번도 그것을 견딘 적 없었던 양 완전히 잊혀졌으니, 잃었던 자유를 되찾는다는 것은 그토록 큰 기쁨이다."]],[["It may have been about mid-day when they placed us in the boat, giving us two kegs of water and some biscuit; and the captain, moved by I know not what compassion, as the lovely Zoraida was about to embark, gave her some forty gold crowns, and would not permit his men to take from her those same garments which she has on now.","그들이 우리를 보트에 태운 것은 정오 무렵이었을 것이며, 물 두 통과 비스킷 약간을 주었다. 그리고 선장은 무슨 측은한 마음이 일었던지, 아리따운 소라이다가 막 배에 오르려 할 때 그녀에게 황금 크라운 마흔 닢쯤을 주었고, 부하들이 그녀가 지금 입고 있는 바로 그 옷가지를 빼앗는 것을 허락하지 않았다."],["We got into the boat, returning them thanks for their kindness to us, and showing ourselves grateful rather than indignant.","우리는 그들이 우리에게 베푼 친절에 고마움을 표하며, 분개하기보다 감사하는 모습을 보이며 보트에 올랐다."],["They stood out to sea, steering for the straits; we, without looking to any compass save the land we had before us, set ourselves to row with such energy that by sunset we were so near that we might easily, we thought, land before the night was far advanced.","그들은 해협을 향해 키를 잡고 바다로 나아갔다. 우리는 눈앞에 둔 뭍 말고는 어떤 나침반도 보지 않은 채, 어찌나 힘차게 노를 저었던지 해 질 무렵에는 밤이 깊기 전에 쉬이 상륙할 수 있으리라 여길 만큼 가까이 다가가 있었다."],["But as the moon did not show that night, and the sky was clouded, and as we knew not whereabouts we were, it did not seem to us a prudent thing to make for the shore, as several of us advised, saying we ought to run ourselves ashore even if it were on rocks and far from any habitation, for in this way we should be relieved from the apprehensions we naturally felt of the prowling vessels of the Tetuan corsairs, who leave Barbary at nightfall and are on the Spanish coast by daybreak, where they commonly take some prize, and then go home to sleep in their own houses.","그러나 그날 밤은 달이 뜨지 않았고 하늘은 구름에 덮였으며, 우리가 대체 어디쯤에 있는지 알 수 없었던지라, 우리 가운데 여럿이 권한 대로 해안으로 향하는 것은 현명한 일로 여겨지지 않았다. 그들은 설령 바위투성이에 인가에서 멀리 떨어진 곳일지라도 배를 뭍에 올려야 한다고 했으니, 이렇게 하면 우리가 으레 느끼던 두려움, 곧 어두워질 무렵 바르바리아를 떠나 동틀 녘이면 스페인 연안에 와 있는 테투안 해적들의 떠도는 배들에 대한 두려움에서 벗어날 수 있다는 것이었다."],["But of the conflicting counsels the one which was adopted was that we should approach gradually, and land where we could if the sea were calm enough to permit us.","그자들은 그곳에서 흔히 노획물을 얻고는 제집에서 잠을 자러 돌아간다. 그러나 엇갈린 권고들 가운데 채택된 것은, 우리가 차츰차츰 다가가, 바다가 잔잔해 허락한다면 가능한 곳에 상륙하자는 것이었다."],["This was done, and a little before midnight we drew near to the foot of a huge and lofty mountain, not so close to the sea but that it left a narrow space on which to land conveniently.","그대로 했고, 자정이 조금 못 되어 우리는 어느 거대하고 우뚝한 산기슭에 다가갔는데, 바다에 그리 바싹 붙어 있지는 않아 상륙하기에 알맞은 좁은 자리를 남겨 두고 있었다."],["We ran our boat up on the sand, and all sprang out and kissed the ground, and with tears of joyful satisfaction returned thanks to God our Lord for all his incomparable goodness to us on our voyage.","우리는 보트를 모래밭에 올리고 모두 뛰어내려 땅에 입을 맞추었으며, 기쁨에 찬 흡족함의 눈물을 흘리며 우리 항해 내내 우리에게 베푸신 비할 데 없는 자비를 두고 우리 주 하느님께 감사를 드렸다."],["We took out of the boat the provisions it contained, and drew it up on the shore, and then climbed a long way up the mountain, for even there we could not feel easy in our hearts, or persuade ourselves that it was Christian soil that was now under our feet.","우리는 보트에서 거기 실린 양식을 꺼내고 보트를 뭍으로 끌어 올린 다음, 산을 따라 한참을 올라갔으니, 거기서조차 마음을 놓을 수도, 이제 우리 발밑에 있는 것이 기독교 땅임을 스스로 믿을 수도 없었기 때문이다."]],[["The dawn came, more slowly, I think, than we could have wished; we completed the ascent in order to see if from the summit any habitation or any shepherds’ huts could be discovered, but strain our eyes as we might, neither dwelling, nor human being, nor path nor road could we perceive.","동이 텄으니, 내 생각에는 우리가 바랐을 법한 것보다 더 더디게 텄다. 우리는 정상에서 어떤 인가나 양치기들의 오두막이라도 보이는지 알아보려고 끝까지 올라갔으나, 아무리 눈을 부릅떠도 살림집도, 사람도, 오솔길도, 큰길도 보이지 않았다."],["However, we determined to push on farther, as it could not but be that ere long we must see some one who could tell us where we were.","그래도 우리는 더 멀리 나아가기로 했으니, 머지않아 우리가 어디 있는지 일러 줄 누군가를 틀림없이 만나게 되리라 여긴 까닭이었다. 그러나 무엇보다 나를 가장 괴롭힌 것은 소라이다가 그 험한 땅을 걸어서 가는 것을 보는 일이었다."],["But what distressed me most was to see Zoraida going on foot over that rough ground; for though I once carried her on my shoulders, she was more wearied by my weariness than rested by the rest; and so she would never again allow me to undergo the exertion, and went on very patiently and cheerfully, while I led her by the hand.","한번은 내가 그녀를 어깨에 메었으나, 그녀는 쉼으로 쉰 것보다 내 고단함에 더 고단해했으므로, 그녀는 다시는 내게 그 수고를 하게 하려 들지 않았고, 내가 손을 잡고 이끄는 동안 매우 참을성 있고 명랑하게 걸어갔다. 우리가 사 분의 일 레과쯤 채 안 되게 갔을 때 작은 방울 소리가 우리 귀에 들려왔으니, 가까운 데 가축 떼가 있다는 분명한 증거였다."],["We had gone rather less than a quarter of a league when the sound of a little bell fell on our ears, a clear proof that there were flocks hard by, and looking about carefully to see if any were within view, we observed a young shepherd tranquilly and unsuspiciously trimming a stick with his knife at the foot of a cork tree.","그래서 보이는 데 무엇이 있나 주의 깊게 둘러보니, 한 젊은 양치기가 코르크나무 아래에서 태평스럽고 아무 의심 없이 칼로 막대기를 다듬고 있는 것이 보였다. 우리가 그를 부르자, 그는 고개를 들고 날렵하게 벌떡 일어났다."],["We called to him, and he, raising his head, sprang nimbly to his feet, for, as we afterwards learned, the first who presented themselves to his sight were the renegade and Zoraida, and seeing them in Moorish dress he imagined that all the Moors of Barbary were upon him; and plunging with marvellous swiftness into the thicket in front of him, he began to raise a prodigious outcry, exclaiming, “The Moors--the Moors have landed!","나중에 알게 된 바로는, 그의 눈에 처음 들어온 것이 배교자와 소라이다였고, 그들이 무어인 차림을 한 것을 보고는 바르바리아의 무어인들이 죄다 자기에게 들이닥치는 줄로 여겼던 것이다. 그리하여 그는 놀라운 빠르기로 앞에 있는 덤불 속으로 뛰어들며 엄청난 비명을 지르기 시작했다."],["To arms, to arms!”","\"무어인이다, 무어인이 상륙했다! 무기를 들어라, 무기를!\""],["We were all thrown into perplexity by these cries, not knowing what to do; but reflecting that the shouts of the shepherd would raise the country and that the mounted coast-guard would come at once to see what was the matter, we agreed that the renegade must strip off his Turkish garments and put on a captive’s jacket or coat which one of our party gave him at once, though he himself was reduced to his shirt; and so commending ourselves to God, we followed the same road which we saw the shepherd take, expecting every moment that the coast-guard would be down upon us.","이 외침에 우리는 모두 어찌할 바를 몰라 당혹에 빠졌으나, 양치기의 고함이 온 고장을 들쑤셔 말을 탄 연안 경비대가 무슨 일인지 보러 곧장 들이닥치리라 생각하고는, 배교자가 튀르크 옷을 벗고 우리 일행 가운데 한 사람이 곧 그에게 내준 포로의 윗옷이나 외투를 입어야 한다고 의견을 모았다. 비록 그 옷을 내준 자 자신은 셔츠 바람이 되고 말았지만 말이다."],["Nor did our expectation deceive us, for two hours had not passed when, coming out of the brushwood into the open ground, we perceived some fifty mounted men swiftly approaching us at a hand-gallop.","그리하여 우리는 하느님께 우리를 의탁하며, 양치기가 간 것과 같은 길을 따라가면서, 연안 경비대가 금방이라도 우리를 덮치리라 시시각각 예상했다. 우리의 예상은 빗나가지 않았으니, 두 시간이 채 지나기도 전에 우리가 덤불에서 빈 땅으로 나섰을 때 쉰 명쯤 되는 기마병이 가벼운 구보로 우리를 향해 빠르게 다가오는 것을 보았던 것이다."],["As soon as we saw them we stood still, waiting for them; but as they came close and, instead of the Moors they were in quest of, saw a set of poor Christians, they were taken aback, and one of them asked if it could be we who were the cause of the shepherd having raised the call to arms.","그들을 보자마자 우리는 가만히 서서 그들을 기다렸다. 그러나 그들은 가까이 다가와, 자기들이 찾던 무어인 대신 가련한 기독교도 한 무리를 보고는 어리둥절해했고, 그중 하나가 양치기로 하여금 무기를 들라 외치게 한 원인이 우리냐고 물었다."],["I said “Yes,” and as I was about to explain to him what had occurred, and whence we came and who we were, one of the Christians of our party recognised the horseman who had put the question to us, and before I could say anything more he exclaimed:","내가 \"그렇소\"라고 하며 무슨 일이 있었는지, 또 우리가 어디서 왔으며 누구인지를 그에게 설명하려는데, 우리 일행 가운데 한 기독교도가 우리에게 그 물음을 던진 기마병을 알아보고는, 내가 한마디 더 하기도 전에 외쳤다."]],[["“Thanks be to God, sirs, for bringing us to such good quarters; for, if I do not deceive myself, the ground we stand on is that of Velez Malaga unless, indeed, all my years of captivity have made me unable to recollect that you, señor, who ask who we are, are Pedro de Bustamante, my uncle.”","\"하느님께 감사드립니다, 여러분, 우리를 이리도 좋은 곳으로 데려다주셔서요. 제가 틀리지 않았다면, 우리가 서 있는 이 땅은 벨레스말라가의 땅입니다. 하기야 그 숱한 포로살이의 세월이 제 기억을 흐려 놓지 않았다면 말이지요. 우리가 누구인지 물으시는 나리, 당신은 바로 제 외삼촌 페드로 데 부스타만테이십니다.\""]],[["The Christian captive had hardly uttered these words, when the horseman threw himself off his horse, and ran to embrace the young man, crying:","기독교도 포로가 이 말을 채 입에 올리기 무섭게, 그 기마병은 말에서 몸을 던져 내려 젊은이를 끌어안으러 달려가며 외쳤다."]],[["“Nephew of my soul and life!","\"내 영혼이요 생명인 조카야! 이제야 너를 알아보겠구나."],["I recognise thee now; and long have I mourned thee as dead, I, and my sister, thy mother, and all thy kin that are still alive, and whom God has been pleased to preserve that they may enjoy the happiness of seeing thee.","오랫동안 나는 너를 죽은 줄 알고 애통해했단다, 나도, 네 어미인 내 누이도, 아직 살아 있는 네 모든 일가붙이도 말이다. 하느님께서 그들을 살려 두신 것은 너를 보는 기쁨을 누리게 하시려는 뜻이었구나."],["We knew long since that thou wert in Algiers, and from the appearance of thy garments and those of all this company, I conclude that ye have had a miraculous restoration to liberty.”","우리는 네가 알제에 있다는 것을 오래전부터 알고 있었다. 그리고 네 옷차림과 이 일행 모두의 옷차림을 보니, 너희가 기적처럼 자유를 되찾았으리라 짐작이 가는구나.\""]],[["“It is true,”","\"사실입니다.\""],["replied the young man, “and by-and-by we will tell you all.”","젊은이가 대답했다. \"이따가 모두 말씀드리지요.\""]],[["As soon as the horsemen understood that we were Christian captives, they dismounted from their horses, and each offered his to carry us to the city of Velez Malaga, which was a league and a half distant.","기마병들이 우리가 기독교도 포로들임을 알아채자마자, 그들은 말에서 내려 저마다 자기 말을 내주며 우리를 한 레과 반쯤 떨어진 벨레스말라가 시로 데려다주려 했다."],["Some of them went to bring the boat to the city, we having told them where we had left it; others took us up behind them, and Zoraida was placed on the horse of the young man’s uncle.","그중 몇은 우리가 보트를 둔 곳을 일러 주자 그것을 시로 가져오러 갔고, 다른 자들은 우리를 자기들 뒤에 태웠으며, 소라이다는 젊은이의 외삼촌의 말에 태워졌다. 온 마을 사람들이 우리를 맞으러 나왔으니, 이때쯤에는 앞서 달려간 자에게서 우리의 도착 소식을 들었던 것이다."],["The whole town came out to meet us, for they had by this time heard of our arrival from one who had gone on in advance.","그들은 풀려난 포로들이나 포로가 된 무어인들을 보고 놀라지는 않았으니, 그 연안 사람들은 이쪽도 저쪽도 보는 데 익숙했기 때문이다."],["They were not astonished to see liberated captives or captive Moors, for people on that coast are well used to see both one and the other; but they were astonished at the beauty of Zoraida, which was just then heightened, as well by the exertion of travelling as by joy at finding herself on Christian soil, and relieved of all fear of being lost; for this had brought such a glow upon her face, that unless my affection for her were deceiving me, I would venture to say that there was not a more beautiful creature in the world--at least, that I had ever seen.","그러나 그들은 소라이다의 아름다움에 놀랐는데, 그 아름다움은 마침 그때 여행의 노고와 더불어 자신이 기독교 땅에 와 있음을, 또 길을 잃을 모든 두려움에서 벗어났음을 알게 된 기쁨으로 한껏 돋보였던 것이다. 이것이 그녀의 얼굴에 그토록 환한 빛을 가져다주었으니, 그녀를 향한 내 애정이 나를 속이는 것이 아니라면, 나는 감히 세상에 그녀보다 더 아름다운 존재는 없다고, 적어도 내가 본 적이 있는 한에서는 그렇다고 말하리라."],["We went straight to the church to return thanks to God for the mercies we had received, and when Zoraida entered it she said there were faces there like Lela Marien’s.","우리는 우리가 받은 자비를 두고 하느님께 감사를 드리러 곧장 성당으로 갔는데, 소라이다는 성당에 들어서자 거기 렐라 마리엔 같은 얼굴들이 있다고 말했다."],["We told her they were her images; and as well as he could the renegade explained to her what they meant, that she might adore them as if each of them were the very same Lela Marien that had spoken to her; and she, having great intelligence and a quick and clear instinct, understood at once all he said to her about them.","우리는 그것이 그녀의 성상(聖像)이라고 일러 주었고, 배교자는 할 수 있는 한, 그녀가 그것들 하나하나를 마치 자기에게 말을 건넸던 바로 그 렐라 마리엔처럼 경배할 수 있도록, 그것들이 무엇을 뜻하는지 설명해 주었다. 그녀는 빼어난 지력과 빠르고 또렷한 직관을 지녔던지라, 그것들에 관해 그가 한 말을 단번에 모두 알아들었다."],["Thence they took us away and distributed us all in different houses in the town; but as for the renegade, Zoraida, and myself, the Christian who came with us brought us to the house of his parents, who had a fair share of the gifts of fortune, and treated us with as much kindness as they did their own son.","거기서 사람들은 우리를 데려가 마을의 여러 집에 나누어 머물게 했으나, 배교자와 소라이다와 나로 말하자면, 우리와 함께 온 그 기독교도가 우리를 자기 부모의 집으로 데려갔으니, 그들은 운수의 선물을 어지간히 누리는 처지였고 우리를 제 친자식 대하듯 친절히 대접해 주었다."]],[["We remained six days in Velez, at the end of which the renegade, having informed himself of all that was requisite for him to do, set out for the city of Granada to restore himself to the sacred bosom of the Church through the medium of the Holy Inquisition.","우리는 벨레스에서 엿새를 머물렀고, 그 끝에 배교자는 자기가 해야 할 모든 일을 알아 둔 뒤, 성스러운 종교 재판소를 통해 자신을 거룩한 교회의 품으로 되돌리려고 그라나다 시로 떠났다. 풀려난 다른 포로들도 저마다 자기에게 가장 좋아 보이는 길로 떠나갔고, 소라이다와 나만 남았는데, 가진 것이라고는 그 프랑스인의 호의가 소라이다에게 베푼 크라운뿐이었으니, 나는 그것으로 그녀가 타고 다니는 짐승을 샀다."],["The other released captives took their departures, each the way that seemed best to him, and Zoraida and I were left alone, with nothing more than the crowns which the courtesy of the Frenchman had bestowed upon Zoraida, out of which I bought the beast on which she rides; and, I for the present attending her as her father and squire and not as her husband, we are now going to ascertain if my father is living, or if any of my brothers has had better fortune than mine has been; though, as Heaven has made me the companion of Zoraida, I think no other lot could be assigned to me, however happy, that I would rather have.","그리고 나는 지금으로서는 그녀를 남편으로서가 아니라 아버지요 종자(從者)로서 모시면서, 우리는 이제 내 아버지가 살아 계신지, 또는 내 형제들 가운데 누가 나보다 나은 운을 누렸는지를 알아보러 가는 길이다. 비록 하늘이 나를 소라이다의 길동무로 삼아 주셨으니, 아무리 행복한 다른 운명이 내게 주어진다 한들 이보다 더 갖고 싶은 것은 없으리라 여기지만 말이다."],["The patience with which she endures the hardships that poverty brings with it, and the eagerness she shows to become a Christian, are such that they fill me with admiration, and bind me to serve her all my life; though the happiness I feel in seeing myself hers, and her mine, is disturbed and marred by not knowing whether I shall find any corner to shelter her in my own country, or whether time and death may not have made such changes in the fortunes and lives of my father and brothers, that I shall hardly find anyone who knows me, if they are not alive.","그녀가 가난이 안기는 고초를 견뎌 내는 그 인내심과, 기독교도가 되고자 보이는 그 간절함은 나를 경탄으로 가득 채우고, 평생 그녀를 섬기도록 나를 묶어 놓을 만한 것이다. 비록 내가 그녀의 것이요 그녀가 내 것임을 보는 데서 느끼는 행복은, 내 고향에 그녀를 깃들게 할 한 귀퉁이라도 찾을 수 있을지, 또는 세월과 죽음이 내 아버지와 형제들의 살림과 목숨에 어떤 변화를 일으켜, 그들이 살아 있지 않다면 나를 알아볼 이를 좀처럼 찾지 못하게 되지나 않았을지 알 수 없어 흐트러지고 망쳐지기는 하지만 말이다."]],[["I have no more of my story to tell you, gentlemen; whether it be an interesting or a curious one let your better judgments decide; all I can say is I would gladly have told it to you more briefly; although my fear of wearying you has made me leave out more than one circumstance.","여러분, 제 이야기는 이제 더 들려드릴 것이 없습니다. 그것이 흥미로운 이야기인지 진기한 이야기인지는 여러분의 더 나은 판단에 맡기겠습니다. 다만 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 것은, 저로서는 기꺼이 더 짧게 들려드리고 싶었다는 것입니다. 비록 여러분을 지루하게 할까 하는 두려움 탓에 한 가지 이상의 정황을 빼놓고 말하기는 했지만 말입니다."]],[["# CHAPTER XLII. WHICH TREATS OF WHAT FURTHER TOOK PLACE IN THE INN, AND OF SEVERAL OTHER THINGS WORTH KNOWING","# CHAPTER XLII."]],[["With these words the captive held his peace, and Don Fernando said to him, “In truth, captain, the manner in which you have related this remarkable adventure has been such as befitted the novelty and strangeness of the matter.","포로가 이 말과 함께 입을 다물자, 돈 페르난도가 그에게 말했다. \"정말이지, 대위님, 이 보기 드문 모험을 들려주신 방식이야말로 그 사안의 새로움과 기이함에 꼭 어울리는 것이었습니다."],["The whole story is curious and uncommon, and abounds with incidents that fill the hearers with wonder and astonishment; and so great is the pleasure we have found in listening to it that we should be glad if it were to begin again, even though to-morrow were to find us still occupied with the same tale.”","그 이야기는 처음부터 끝까지 신기하고 보기 드물며, 듣는 이들을 경이와 놀라움으로 가득 채우는 사건들로 넘쳐납니다. 그리고 우리가 그것을 듣는 데서 얻은 즐거움이 어찌나 큰지, 내일까지 같은 이야기에 매달려 있게 된다 해도 다시 처음부터 시작해 주신다면 기쁘기 그지없겠습니다.\""],["And while he said this Cardenio and the rest of them offered to be of service to him in any way that lay in their power, and in words and language so kindly and sincere that the captain was much gratified by their good-will.","그가 이렇게 말하는 동안 카르데니오와 나머지 사람들도 자기들의 힘이 닿는 한 어떤 식으로든 그를 돕겠노라 나섰는데, 그 말과 표현이 어찌나 다정하고 진실되던지 대위는 그들의 호의에 크게 감사한 마음이 들었다."],["In particular Don Fernando offered, if he would go back with him, to get his brother the marquis to become godfather at the baptism of Zoraida, and on his own part to provide him with the means of making his appearance in his own country with the credit and comfort he was entitled to.","특히 돈 페르난도는, 만약 그가 자신과 함께 돌아간다면 형인 후작을 소라이다의 세례 대부로 모시게 해 주겠으며, 자신으로서는 그가 마땅히 누려야 할 명예와 안락을 갖추어 고향에 모습을 드러낼 수 있도록 방편을 마련해 주겠노라 제안했다."],["For all this the captive returned thanks very courteously, although he would not accept any of their generous offers.","이 모든 것에 대해 포로는 매우 정중히 감사의 뜻을 표했으나, 그들의 너그러운 제안 가운데 어느 것도 받아들이려 하지 않았다."]],[["By this time night closed in, and as it did, there came up to the inn a coach attended by some men on horseback, who demanded accommodation; to which the landlady replied that there was not a hand’s breadth of the whole inn unoccupied.","이러는 사이 밤이 깊어졌고, 어둠이 내려앉을 무렵 마차 한 대가 말 탄 사람 몇을 거느리고 객줏집으로 다가와 묵을 곳을 청했다. 이에 안주인은 객줏집 전체에 손바닥 한 뼘만 한 빈자리도 없다고 답했다."]],[["“Still, for all that,”","\"그래도 어쨌든,\""],["said one of those who had entered on horseback, “room must be found for his lordship the Judge here.”","말을 타고 들어선 사람들 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"여기 판관 나리를 모실 방은 마련되어야 하오.\""]],[["At this name the landlady was taken aback, and said, “Señor, the fact is I have no beds; but if his lordship the Judge carries one with him, as no doubt he does, let him come in and welcome; for my husband and I will give up our room to accommodate his worship.”","이 호칭에 안주인은 흠칫 놀라며 말했다. \"나리, 사실 침대가 하나도 없습니다요. 하지만 판관 나리께서 분명 그러하시듯 침대를 지니고 다니신다면, 어서 드시지요. 저와 제 남편이 나리께 자리를 내어 드리고자 저희 방을 비우겠습니다요.\""]],[["“Very good, so be it,”","\"좋소, 그리합시다.\" 종자가 말했다."],["said the squire; but in the meantime a man had got out of the coach whose dress indicated at a glance the office and post he held, for the long robe with ruffled sleeves that he wore showed that he was, as his servant said, a Judge of appeal.","그러나 그러는 사이 한 남자가 마차에서 내렸는데, 그 차림새만 보아도 그가 어떤 직책과 자리에 있는 사람인지 한눈에 알 수 있었으니, 그가 걸친 주름 소맷자락이 달린 긴 법복이 그가 종자의 말대로 항소 판관임을 드러내고 있었기 때문이다. 그는 여행복 차림에 열여섯 살쯤 되어 보이는 한 어린 처녀를 손으로 이끌고 있었는데, 그 처녀가 어찌나 기품 있고 어찌나 아름다우며 어찌나 우아하던지 그녀가 모습을 드러내자 모두가 감탄으로 가득 찼고, 만일 그 객줏집에 있던 도로테아와 루신다와 소라이다를 보지 못했더라면 이 처녀의 아름다움 같은 것은 좀처럼 찾아보기 어려우리라 여겼을 것이다."],["He led by the hand a young girl in a travelling dress, apparently about sixteen years of age, and of such a high-bred air, so beautiful and so graceful, that all were filled with admiration when she made her appearance, and but for having seen Dorothea, Luscinda, and Zoraida, who were there in the inn, they would have fancied that a beauty like that of this maiden’s would have been hard to find.","돈키호테는 판관이 그 젊은 처녀와 함께 들어설 때 그 자리에 있었는데, 그를 보자마자 이렇게 말했다. \"나리께서는 안심하시고 이 성으로 드셔서 편히 쉬시지요."],["Don Quixote was present at the entrance of the Judge with the young lady, and as soon as he saw him he said, “Your worship may with confidence enter and take your ease in this castle; for though the accommodation be scanty and poor, there are no quarters so cramped or inconvenient that they cannot make room for arms and letters; above all if arms and letters have beauty for a guide and leader, as letters represented by your worship have in this fair maiden, to whom not only ought castles to throw themselves open and yield themselves up, but rocks should rend themselves asunder and mountains divide and bow themselves down to give her a reception.","비록 묵을 곳이 비좁고 누추하나, 무예와 학문을 위해 자리를 내어 주지 못할 만큼 옹색하거나 불편한 거처란 없는 법이니 말입니다. 더구나 무예와 학문이 아름다움을 길잡이요 인도자로 삼고 있다면야 더욱 그러하니, 나리께서 대변하시는 학문이 이 아리따운 처녀를 인도자로 두고 있은즉, 그녀에게는 성들이 마땅히 활짝 문을 열고 스스로를 내어 줄 뿐 아니라, 바위들이 갈라져 쪼개지고 산들이 둘로 나뉘어 몸을 숙여 그녀를 맞아들여야 할 것입니다."],["Enter, your worship, I say, into this paradise, for here you will find stars and suns to accompany the heaven your worship brings with you, here you will find arms in their supreme excellence, and beauty in its highest perfection.”","드시지요, 나리, 이 낙원으로 드시지요. 여기서 나리께서는 나리께서 함께 가져오신 하늘을 수행할 별들과 태양들을 보시게 될 것이며, 여기서 나리께서는 그 지극한 탁월함에 이른 무예와 그 최고의 완성에 다다른 아름다움을 보시게 될 것입니다.\""]],[["The Judge was struck with amazement at the language of Don Quixote, whom he scrutinized very carefully, no less astonished by his figure than by his talk; and before he could find words to answer him he had a fresh surprise, when he saw opposite to him Luscinda, Dorothea, and Zoraida, who, having heard of the new guests and of the beauty of the young lady, had come to see her and welcome her; Don Fernando, Cardenio, and the curate, however, greeted him in a more intelligible and polished style. In short, the Judge made his entrance in a state of bewilderment, as well with what he saw as what he heard, and the fair ladies of the inn gave the fair damsel a cordial welcome. On the whole he could perceive that all who were there were people of quality; but with the figure, countenance, and bearing of Don Quixote he was at his wits’ end; and all civilities having been exchanged, and the accommodation of the inn inquired into, it was settled, as it had been before settled, that all the women should retire to the garret that has been already mentioned, and that the men should remain outside as if to guard them; the Judge, therefore, was very well pleased to allow his daughter, for such the damsel was, to go with the ladies, which she did very willingly; and with part of the host’s narrow bed and half of what the Judge had brought with him, they made a more comfortable arrangement for the night than they had expected.","판관은 돈키호테의 말에 깜짝 놀라, 그의 말 못지않게 그의 모습에도 적잖이 놀라며 그를 매우 찬찬히 뜯어보았다. 그리고 미처 그에게 답할 말을 찾기도 전에 또 한 번 놀랄 일이 생겼으니, 자기 맞은편에 루신다와 도로테아와 소라이다가 있는 것을 본 것이다. 그들은 새 손님들과 어린 처녀의 아름다움에 관한 소식을 듣고 그녀를 보고 또 맞이하려고 나와 있었던 것이다. 그러나 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오와 신부는 그를 보다 알아들을 만하고 세련된 투로 맞이했다. 요컨대 판관은 자기가 본 것에도 들은 것에도 어리둥절해하며 안으로 들어섰고, 객줏집의 아리따운 부인들은 그 아리따운 아가씨를 따뜻이 맞아 주었다. 대체로 그는 거기 있는 이들이 모두 신분 있는 사람들임을 알아챌 수 있었으나, 돈키호테의 모습과 얼굴과 거동만큼은 도무지 종잡을 수가 없었다. 모든 인사가 오가고 객줏집의 잠자리에 관한 의논이 끝나자, 앞서 정해진 대로 모든 여인은 이미 언급된 그 다락방으로 물러가고 남자들은 마치 그들을 지키기라도 하듯 바깥에 머물기로 결정되었다. 그리하여 판관은 자기 딸을 — 그 아가씨가 바로 그의 딸이었던 것이다 — 기꺼이 부인들과 함께 가도록 허락했고, 딸도 매우 흔쾌히 그리했다. 그리고 주인의 좁은 침대 일부와 판관이 가져온 것의 절반으로 그들은 예상했던 것보다 더 편안한 잠자리를 마련했다."]],[["The captive, whose heart had leaped within him the instant he saw the Judge, telling him somehow that this was his brother, asked one of the servants who accompanied him what his name was, and whether he knew from what part of the country he came.","포로는 판관을 보는 순간 가슴이 뛰며 어쩐지 이 사람이 자기 형이라는 느낌이 들었던 터라, 그를 따라온 하인들 가운데 하나에게 그의 이름이 무엇이며 어느 고장 출신인지 아느냐고 물었다."],["The servant replied that he was called the Licentiate Juan Perez de Viedma, and that he had heard it said he came from a village in the mountains of Leon.","하인은 그가 후안 페레스 데 비에드마 학사라 불리며, 레온 산지의 어느 마을 출신이라는 말을 들은 적이 있다고 답했다."],["From this statement, and what he himself had seen, he felt convinced that this was his brother who had adopted letters by his father’s advice; and excited and rejoiced, he called Don Fernando and Cardenio and the curate aside, and told them how the matter stood, assuring them that the judge was his brother.","이 말과 그 자신이 직접 본 것으로 미루어, 그는 이 사람이 아버지의 권유로 학문의 길을 택한 자기 형임을 확신하게 되었다. 그리하여 흥분과 기쁨에 차서 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오와 신부를 한쪽으로 불러 사정이 어떠한지를 말하고, 그 판관이 자기 형이라고 단언했다."],["The servant had further informed him that he was now going to the Indies with the appointment of Judge of the Supreme Court of Mexico; and he had learned, likewise, that the young lady was his daughter, whose mother had died in giving birth to her, and that he was very rich in consequence of the dowry left to him with the daughter.","하인은 또한 그가 지금 멕시코 대법원 판관으로 임명되어 인도 제도로 가는 길이라는 것과, 그 어린 처녀가 그의 딸인데 그 어머니는 딸을 낳다가 세상을 떠났으며, 딸과 함께 남겨진 지참금 덕분에 그가 매우 부유하다는 것도 알려 주었다."],["He asked their advice as to what means he should adopt to make himself known, or to ascertain beforehand whether, when he had made himself known, his brother, seeing him so poor, would be ashamed of him, or would receive him with a warm heart.","그는 자기를 어떻게 알려야 할지, 혹은 자기를 알린 뒤에 그토록 가난한 자기 모습을 보고 형이 자기를 부끄러워할지 아니면 따뜻한 마음으로 맞아 줄지를 미리 알아내려면 어떤 방법을 써야 할지에 관해 그들의 조언을 구했다."]],[["“Leave it to me to find out that,”","\"그건 제가 알아내도록 맡겨 주십시오.\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate; “though there is no reason for supposing, señor captain, that you will not be kindly received, because the worth and wisdom that your brother’s bearing shows him to possess do not make it likely that he will prove haughty or insensible, or that he will not know how to estimate the accidents of fortune at their proper value.”","\"하기야, 대위님, 형님께서 따뜻이 맞아 주지 않으시리라 짐작할 이유라곤 없습니다. 형님의 거동에 드러난 그 인품과 지혜로 보건대 그분이 거만하거나 무정하게 굴거나, 운명의 부침을 그 마땅한 값으로 헤아릴 줄 모르실 분 같지는 않으니 말입니다.\""]],[["“Still,”","\"그래도,\""],["said the captain, “I would not make myself known abruptly, but in some indirect way.”","대위가 말했다. \"제 신분을 불쑥 드러내기보다는 어떤 에둘러 가는 방식으로 하고 싶습니다.\""]],[["“I have told you already,”","\"이미 말씀드렸지 않습니까.\""],["said the curate, “that I will manage it in a way to satisfy us all.”","신부가 말했다. \"우리 모두가 만족할 만한 방식으로 제가 처리하겠습니다.\""]],[["By this time supper was ready, and they all took their seats at the table, except the captive, and the ladies, who supped by themselves in their own room.","이러는 사이 저녁 식사가 준비되어, 포로와 부인들을 제외한 모두가 식탁에 둘러앉았다. 부인들은 자기들 방에서 따로 식사를 했다."],["In the middle of supper the curate said:","식사 도중에 신부가 말했다."]],[["“I had a comrade of your worship’s name, Señor Judge, in Constantinople, where I was a captive for several years, and that same comrade was one of the stoutest soldiers and captains in the whole Spanish infantry; but he had as large a share of misfortune as he had of gallantry and courage.”","\"제가 여러 해 포로로 있던 콘스탄티노플에서, 판관님과 같은 이름을 가진 동료가 한 사람 있었습니다. 그 동료는 스페인 보병 전체에서 가장 굳세고 용맹한 군인이자 대위 가운데 하나였지요. 하지만 그는 용맹과 용기를 지닌 만큼이나 큰 몫의 불운도 지니고 있었습니다.\""]],[["“And how was the captain called, señor?”","\"그 대위는 어떻게 불렸습니까, 나리?\""],["asked the Judge.","판관이 물었다."]],[["“He was called Ruy Perez de Viedma,”","\"루이 페레스 데 비에드마라 불렸습니다.\" 신부가 답했다."],["replied the curate, “and he was born in a village in the mountains of Leon; and he mentioned a circumstance connected with his father and his brothers which, had it not been told me by so truthful a man as he was, I should have set down as one of those fables the old women tell over the fire in winter; for he said his father had divided his property among his three sons and had addressed words of advice to them sounder than any of Cato’s.","\"그리고 레온 산지의 어느 마을에서 태어났지요. 그는 자기 아버지와 형제들에 얽힌 한 가지 사연을 들려주었는데, 만일 그토록 진실한 사람이 직접 들려준 것이 아니었더라면 저는 그것을 겨울철 노파들이 화롯가에서 늘어놓는 옛이야기 가운데 하나로 치부했을 것입니다."],["But I can say this much, that the choice he made of going to the wars was attended with such success, that by his gallant conduct and courage, and without any help save his own merit, he rose in a few years to be captain of infantry, and to see himself on the high-road and in position to be given the command of a corps before long; but Fortune was against him, for where he might have expected her favour he lost it, and with it his liberty, on that glorious day when so many recovered theirs, at the battle of Lepanto.","그가 말하기를, 자기 아버지가 세 아들에게 재산을 나누어 주면서 카토의 그 어떤 가르침보다도 더 사리에 맞는 충고의 말을 들려주었다는 것입니다. 다만 이것만은 말할 수 있으니, 그가 전쟁터로 나가기로 한 선택은 그토록 큰 성공이 따라서, 그 용맹한 처신과 용기로, 그리고 오로지 자신의 공적 외에는 아무런 도움도 없이, 그는 몇 해 만에 보병 대위에 올랐고, 머지않아 군단의 지휘권을 받게 될 출셋길에 오른 자기 모습을 보게 되었습니다."],["I lost mine at the Goletta, and after a variety of adventures we found ourselves comrades at Constantinople.","그러나 운명은 그를 거슬렀으니, 그 운명의 은총을 기대할 만한 바로 그곳에서 그는 그것을 잃고 말았고, 그와 더불어 자유까지 잃었습니다. 그토록 많은 이들이 자유를 되찾은 그 영광스러운 날, 레판토 해전에서 말입니다."],["Thence he went to Algiers, where he met with one of the most extraordinary adventures that ever befell anyone in the world.”","저는 라 골레타에서 제 자유를 잃었고, 여러 곡절을 겪은 끝에 우리는 콘스탄티노플에서 동료가 되었습니다. 거기서 그는 알제로 갔는데, 그곳에서 세상 누구에게도 일찍이 일어난 적 없는 더없이 기이한 모험 가운데 하나를 겪었습니다.\""]],[["Here the curate went on to relate briefly his brother’s adventure with Zoraida; to all which the Judge gave such an attentive hearing that he never before had been so much of a hearer.","여기서 신부는 자기 형이 소라이다와 겪은 모험을 간추려 이야기해 나갔다. 판관은 그 모든 것에 어찌나 귀 기울여 듣던지 일찍이 그처럼 열심히 들어 본 적이 없을 정도였다."],["The curate, however, only went so far as to describe how the Frenchmen plundered those who were in the boat, and the poverty and distress in which his comrade and the fair Moor were left, of whom he said he had not been able to learn what became of them, or whether they had reached Spain, or been carried to France by the Frenchmen.","그러나 신부는 프랑스인들이 보트에 탄 이들의 것을 약탈한 일과, 그의 동료와 아리따운 무어 여인이 빠지게 된 가난과 곤경을 묘사하는 데까지만 이야기했고, 그들이 어떻게 되었는지, 스페인에 닿았는지, 아니면 프랑스인들에게 프랑스로 끌려갔는지는 알아낼 수 없었노라고 말했다."]],[["The captain, standing a little to one side, was listening to all the curate said, and watching every movement of his brother, who, as soon as he perceived the curate had made an end of his story, gave a deep sigh and said with his eyes full of tears, “Oh, señor, if you only knew what news you have given me and how it comes home to me, making me show how I feel it with these tears that spring from my eyes in spite of all my worldly wisdom and self-restraint!","대위는 한쪽으로 조금 물러나 서서 신부가 하는 말을 모두 듣고 있었고, 자기 형의 일거수일투족을 지켜보고 있었다. 형은 신부가 이야기를 끝냈음을 알아채자마자 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 눈에 눈물을 가득 머금은 채 말했다."],["That brave captain that you speak of is my eldest brother, who, being of a bolder and loftier mind than my other brother or myself, chose the honourable and worthy calling of arms, which was one of the three careers our father proposed to us, as your comrade mentioned in that fable you thought he was telling you.","\"오, 나리, 나리께서 제게 어떤 소식을 주셨는지, 그것이 제게 얼마나 사무치는지 아신다면! 제 온갖 세상 지혜와 자제력에도 불구하고 제 눈에서 솟구치는 이 눈물로 제 심정을 드러내고 마는군요!"],["I followed that of letters, in which God and my own exertions have raised me to the position in which you see me.","나리께서 말씀하신 그 용감한 대위는 제 맏형입니다. 그는 저나 제 다른 형제보다 더 담대하고 더 고상한 뜻을 품고 있어서, 아버지께서 우리에게 제시하신 세 가지 길 가운데 명예롭고 떳떳한 무예의 길을 택했지요."],["My second brother is in Peru, so wealthy that with what he has sent to my father and to me he has fully repaid the portion he took with him, and has even furnished my father’s hands with the means of gratifying his natural generosity, while I too have been enabled to pursue my studies in a more becoming and creditable fashion, and so to attain my present standing.","나리의 그 동료분이, 나리께서 옛이야기를 들려주는 줄로 여기셨던 그 사연 속에서 말한 그대로입니다. 저는 학문의 길을 따랐는데, 하느님과 저 자신의 노력으로 지금 나리께서 보시는 이 자리에까지 오르게 되었습니다."],["My father is still alive, though dying with anxiety to hear of his eldest son, and he prays God unceasingly that death may not close his eyes until he has looked upon those of his son; but with regard to him what surprises me is, that having so much common sense as he had, he should have neglected to give any intelligence about himself, either in his troubles and sufferings, or in his prosperity, for if his father or any of us had known of his condition he need not have waited for that miracle of the reed to obtain his ransom; but what now disquiets me is the uncertainty whether those Frenchmen may have restored him to liberty, or murdered him to hide the robbery.","제 둘째 형은 페루에 있는데, 어찌나 부유한지 아버지와 제게 보내 준 것으로 자기가 가지고 간 몫을 충분히 갚고도, 아버지께서 타고난 너그러움을 베푸실 방편까지 손에 쥐여 드렸으며, 저 또한 보다 격에 맞고 떳떳한 방식으로 학업을 이어 가 지금의 지위에 이를 수 있었습니다. 아버지께서는 아직 살아 계시나, 맏아들의 소식을 듣고자 하는 애간장에 거의 돌아가실 지경이며, 아들의 두 눈을 한 번 보기 전에는 죽음이 자기 두 눈을 감기지 않게 해 달라고 하느님께 쉼 없이 기도드리고 계십니다."],["All this will make me continue my journey, not with the satisfaction in which I began it, but in the deepest melancholy and sadness. Oh dear brother!","그런데 형에 관해 제가 놀라운 것은, 그토록 분별 있는 사람이었으면서 자기 고난과 고통 속에서든 형통할 때든 자신에 관해 아무런 소식도 전하기를 소홀히 했다는 점입니다."],["that I only knew where thou art now, and I would hasten to seek thee out and deliver thee from thy sufferings, though it were to cost me suffering myself!","만일 아버지나 우리 가운데 누구라도 그의 처지를 알았더라면 그는 몸값을 마련하려고 그 갈대의 기적 따위를 기다릴 필요도 없었을 테니 말입니다. 그러나 지금 저를 불안하게 하는 것은, 그 프랑스인들이 형을 풀어 주었는지, 아니면 도둑질을 숨기려고 죽여 버렸는지 알 수 없다는 것입니다."],["Oh that I could bring news to our old father that thou art alive, even wert thou the deepest dungeon of Barbary; for his wealth and my brother’s and mine would rescue thee thence!","이 모든 것 때문에 저는 처음 시작했을 때의 그 만족감이 아니라 더없이 깊은 우수와 슬픔 속에서 제 여정을 이어 가게 될 것입니다. 오, 사랑하는 형이여!"],["Oh beautiful and generous Zoraida, that I could repay thy good goodness to a brother!","형이 지금 어디 있는지 알기만 한다면, 비록 제 자신이 고통을 치르더라도 서둘러 형을 찾아 그 고통에서 건져 낼 텐데! 오, 형이 살아 있다는 소식을 우리 늙으신 아버지께 전할 수만 있다면, 설령 형이 바르바리의 가장 깊은 토굴에 갇혀 있다 한들 형의 재산과 제 재산으로 거기서 형을 구해 낼 텐데!"],["That I could be present at the new birth of thy soul, and at thy bridal that would give us all such happiness!”","오, 아름답고 너그러운 소라이다여, 한 형제에게 베푼 그대의 선한 덕에 보답할 수만 있다면! 그대 영혼의 새로운 탄생에, 그리고 우리 모두에게 그토록 큰 행복을 안겨 줄 그대의 혼례에 함께할 수만 있다면!\""]],[["All this and more the Judge uttered with such deep emotion at the news he had received of his brother that all who heard him shared in it, showing their sympathy with his sorrow.","판관은 형에 관해 들은 소식에 어찌나 깊이 감격했던지 이 모든 말과 그 이상의 말을 토해 냈고, 듣는 이들 모두가 그 마음을 함께 나누며 그의 슬픔에 동조하는 빛을 보였다."],["The curate, seeing, then, how well he had succeeded in carrying out his purpose and the captain’s wishes, had no desire to keep them unhappy any longer, so he rose from the table and going into the room where Zoraida was he took her by the hand, Luscinda, Dorothea, and the Judge’s daughter following her.","그러자 신부는 자기 목적과 대위의 바람을 얼마나 훌륭히 이루어 냈는가를 보고는 더 이상 그들을 슬픔 속에 두고 싶지 않아, 식탁에서 일어나 소라이다가 있는 방으로 가서 그녀의 손을 잡았고, 루신다와 도로테아와 판관의 딸이 그 뒤를 따랐다."],["The captain was waiting to see what the curate would do, when the latter, taking him with the other hand, advanced with both of them to where the Judge and the other gentlemen were and said, “Let your tears cease to flow, Señor Judge, and the wish of your heart be gratified as fully as you could desire, for you have before you your worthy brother and your good sister-in-law.","대위는 신부가 어찌하나 지켜보며 기다리고 있었는데, 신부가 다른 손으로 그를 붙들고는 두 사람을 데리고 판관과 다른 신사들이 있는 곳으로 나아가 말했다. \"판관님, 이제 눈물을 거두십시오."],["He whom you see here is the Captain Viedma, and this is the fair Moor who has been so good to him.","그리고 바라시던 만큼 흡족하게 마음의 소원을 이루십시오. 바로 판관님 앞에 그 훌륭한 형님과 어진 형수님이 계시니 말입니다."],["The Frenchmen I told you of have reduced them to the state of poverty you see that you may show the generosity of your kind heart.”","여기 보시는 이분이 비에드마 대위이며, 이 사람이 그에게 그토록 잘해 준 그 아리따운 무어 여인입니다. 제가 말씀드린 그 프랑스인들이 이들을 지금 보시는 가난한 처지로 몰아넣었으니, 판관님의 어진 마음의 너그러움을 베푸시기 바랍니다.\""]],[["The captain ran to embrace his brother, who placed both hands on his breast so as to have a good look at him, holding him a little way off but as soon as he had fully recognised him he clasped him in his arms so closely, shedding such tears of heartfelt joy, that most of those present could not but join in them.","대위는 달려가 형을 끌어안으려 했으나, 형은 그를 잘 바라보려고 두 손을 그의 가슴에 짚어 조금 떼어 놓았다. 그러나 그가 누구인지 온전히 알아보자마자 형은 두 팔로 그를 어찌나 꼭 껴안고 진심에서 우러난 기쁨의 눈물을 어찌나 쏟던지, 거기 있던 이들 대부분이 함께 눈물을 흘리지 않을 수 없었다."],["The words the brothers exchanged, the emotion they showed can scarcely be imagined, I fancy, much less put down in writing.","두 형제가 주고받은 말과 그들이 드러낸 감격은 짐작하기조차 어려우리니, 글로 적어 내기는 더더욱 어려우리라 여겨진다."],["They told each other in a few words the events of their lives; they showed the true affection of brothers in all its strength; then the judge embraced Zoraida, putting all he possessed at her disposal; then he made his daughter embrace her, and the fair Christian and the lovely Moor drew fresh tears from every eye.","그들은 몇 마디로 서로 자기 삶에 일어난 일들을 들려주었고, 형제의 참된 애정을 그 온 힘을 다해 드러내 보였다. 이윽고 판관은 소라이다를 껴안으며 자기가 가진 모든 것을 그녀가 마음대로 쓰도록 내어 주었고, 그런 다음 자기 딸로 하여금 그녀를 껴안게 하니, 그 아리따운 기독교 처녀와 사랑스러운 무어 여인의 모습에 다시금 모든 이의 눈에서 새 눈물이 솟았다."],["And there was Don Quixote observing all these strange proceedings attentively without uttering a word, and attributing the whole to chimeras of knight-errantry.","그리고 그 자리에는 돈키호테가 있어, 이 모든 기이한 일들을 한마디 말도 없이 유심히 지켜보며 그 전부를 편력 기사도의 환영(幻影)들 탓으로 돌리고 있었다."],["Then they agreed that the captain and Zoraida should return with his brother to Seville, and send news to his father of his having been delivered and found, so as to enable him to come and be present at the marriage and baptism of Zoraida, for it was impossible for the Judge to put off his journey, as he was informed that in a month from that time the fleet was to sail from Seville for New Spain, and to miss the passage would have been a great inconvenience to him.","이윽고 그들은, 대위와 소라이다가 형과 함께 세비야로 돌아가, 그가 풀려나 찾아졌다는 소식을 아버지께 전하여 아버지가 와서 소라이다의 혼례와 세례에 함께할 수 있도록 하기로 합의했다. 판관으로서는 여정을 미루기가 불가능했으니, 그때부터 한 달 뒤에 함대가 세비야에서 누에바 에스파냐를 향해 출항한다는 통지를 받은 터라, 그 항해를 놓치면 그에게 큰 낭패가 되었을 것이기 때문이다."],["In short, everybody was well pleased and glad at the captive’s good fortune; and as now almost two-thirds of the night were past, they resolved to retire to rest for the remainder of it.","요컨대, 모두가 포로의 좋은 운수에 흡족해하고 기뻐했다. 그리고 이제 밤이 거의 삼분의 이가 지난지라, 그들은 남은 밤 동안 잠자리에 들어 쉬기로 했다."],["Don Quixote offered to mount guard over the castle lest they should be attacked by some giant or other malevolent scoundrel, covetous of the great treasure of beauty the castle contained.","돈키호테는 성안에 든 그 막대한 아름다움의 보물을 탐낸 어떤 거인이나 다른 악독한 자가 그들을 습격하지 못하도록 성을 지키며 보초를 서겠노라 자청했다."],["Those who understood him returned him thanks for this service, and they gave the Judge an account of his extraordinary humour, with which he was not a little amused.","그를 아는 이들은 이 봉사에 감사를 표했고, 판관에게 그의 별난 기질에 관해 이야기해 주니 판관도 적잖이 흥겨워했다."],["Sancho Panza alone was fuming at the lateness of the hour for retiring to rest; and he of all was the one that made himself most comfortable, as he stretched himself on the trappings of his ass, which, as will be told farther on, cost him so dear.","오직 산초 판사만은 잠자리에 들 시각이 너무 늦은 데 부아가 나 있었다. 그러면서도 그는 누구보다 더 편안히 자리를 잡았으니, 자기 당나귀의 마구 위에 몸을 쭉 뻗었던 것인데, 뒤에 이야기되겠지만 이것이 그에게 톡톡히 값비싼 대가를 치르게 했다."]],[["The ladies, then, having retired to their chamber, and the others having disposed themselves with as little discomfort as they could, Don Quixote sallied out of the inn to act as sentinel of the castle as he had promised.","그리하여 부인들이 자기들 방으로 물러가고 나머지 사람들도 되도록 불편하지 않게 자리를 잡고 나자, 돈키호테는 약속한 대로 성의 보초를 서려고 객줏집 밖으로 나갔다. 그런데 동이 트기 조금 전, 어찌나 곱고 감미로운 목소리가 부인들의 귀에 닿았던지, 모두가 그것에 귀를 곤두세우지 않을 수 없었는데, 특히나 잠이 깨어 있던 도로테아가 그러했으니, 그녀 곁에는 도냐 클라라 데 비에드마가 — 판관의 딸이 그렇게 불렸던 것이다 — 잠들어 누워 있었다."],["It happened, however, that a little before the approach of dawn a voice so musical and sweet reached the ears of the ladies that it forced them all to listen attentively, but especially Dorothea, who had been awake, and by whose side Dona Clara de Viedma, for so the Judge’s daughter was called, lay sleeping.","그토록 감미롭게 노래하는 이가 누구인지 아무도 짐작할 수 없었고, 그 목소리에는 어떤 악기의 반주도 곁들여 있지 않았다. 어느 순간에는 노래하는 이가 안뜰에 있는 것 같다가도, 또 어느 순간에는 마구간에 있는 것 같았다."],["No one could imagine who it was that sang so sweetly, and the voice was unaccompanied by any instrument.","그렇게 모두가 어리둥절해하며 온통 귀를 기울이고 있을 때, 카르데니오가 문간으로 와서 말했다."],["At one moment it seemed to them as if the singer were in the courtyard, at another in the stable; and as they were all attention, wondering, Cardenio came to the door and said, “Listen, whoever is not asleep, and you will hear a muleteer’s voice that enchants as it chants.”","\"잠들지 않은 사람은 들어 보시오. 노래하면서 사람을 호리는 어느 노새몰이꾼의 목소리를 듣게 될 테니.\""]],[["“We are listening to it already, señor,”","\"이미 듣고 있답니다, 나리.\""],["said Dorothea; on which Cardenio went away; and Dorothea, giving all her attention to it, made out the words of the song to be these:","도로테아가 말했다. 이에 카르데니오는 자리를 떴고, 도로테아는 그 목소리에 온통 정신을 쏟아 그 노랫말이 이러함을 알아들었다."]],[["# CHAPTER XLIII. WHEREIN IS RELATED THE PLEASANT STORY OF THE MULETEER, TOGETHER WITH OTHER STRANGE THINGS THAT CAME TO PASS IN THE INN","# CHAPTER XLIII."]],[["Ah me, Love’s mariner am I On Love’s deep ocean sailing; I know not where the haven lies, I dare not hope to gain it.","아, 나는 사랑의 뱃사람, 사랑의 깊은 바다를 항해하는 자. 어디에 항구가 있는지 알지 못하고, 그곳에 닿으리라 감히 바라지도 못하네."]],[["One solitary distant star Is all I have to guide me, A brighter orb than those of old That Palinurus lighted.","외로이 멀리 떠 있는 별 하나가 나를 인도하는 전부이니, 옛날 팔리누루스를 비추던 그 어떤 별보다도 밝구나."]],[["And vaguely drifting am I borne, I know not where it leads me; I fix my gaze on it alone, Of all beside it heedless.","정처 없이 떠밀려 가는 나는, 그것이 나를 어디로 데려가는지 알지 못한 채, 오직 그 별 하나에만 눈길을 박고, 그 밖의 모든 것엔 무심하기만 하네."]],[["But over-cautious prudery, And coyness cold and cruel, When most I need it, these, like clouds, Its longed-for light refuse me.","허나 지나치게 조심스러운 정숙함과 차갑고 모진 새침함이, 내가 가장 그것을 필요로 할 때면, 구름처럼 그토록 갈망하던 그 빛을 내게서 가려 버리네."]],[["Bright star, goal of my yearning eyes As thou above me beamest, When thou shalt hide thee from my sight I’ll know that death is near me.","밝은 별이여, 내 그리워하는 눈길의 목표여, 그대 내 머리 위에서 빛날 때, 그대 내 시야에서 몸을 감추는 그날이면 죽음이 내게 가까이 왔음을 나는 알리라."]],[["The singer had got so far when it struck Dorothea that it was not fair to let Clara miss hearing such a sweet voice, so, shaking her from side to side, she woke her, saying:","노래하는 이가 여기까지 불렀을 때, 도로테아는 클라라가 이토록 감미로운 목소리를 듣지 못하고 놓치게 두는 것이 온당치 못하다는 생각이 들어, 그녀를 이리저리 흔들어 깨우며 말했다."]],[["“Forgive me, child, for waking thee, but I do so that thou mayest have the pleasure of hearing the best voice thou hast ever heard, perhaps, in all thy life.”","\"얘야, 깨워서 미안하구나. 허나 네 평생에 들어 본 것 중 가장 아름다운 목소리일지도 모를 노래를 듣는 즐거움을 네게 주려고 그러는 거란다.\""]],[["Clara awoke quite drowsy, and not understanding at the moment what Dorothea said, asked her what it was; she repeated what she had said, and Clara became attentive at once; but she had hardly heard two lines, as the singer continued, when a strange trembling seized her, as if she were suffering from a severe attack of quartan ague, and throwing her arms round Dorothea she said:","클라라는 잠에 몹시 취한 채로 깨어나, 그 순간 도로테아가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아듣지 못해 무슨 일이냐고 물었다. 도로테아가 했던 말을 되풀이하자, 클라라는 이내 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 노래하는 이가 계속 부르는 두 줄을 채 듣기도 전에, 마치 심한 사일열에 시달리기라도 하듯 야릇한 떨림이 그녀를 사로잡았고, 그녀는 도로테아에게 두 팔을 두르며 말했다."]],[["“Ah, dear lady of my soul and life!","\"아, 제 영혼과 생명의 다정하신 분이여!"],["why did you wake me?","어찌하여 저를 깨우셨나요?"],["The greatest kindness fortune could do me now would be to close my eyes and ears so as neither to see or hear that unhappy musician.”","지금 운명이 제게 베풀 수 있는 가장 큰 자비라면, 제 눈과 귀를 닫아 저 불행한 악사를 보지도 듣지도 못하게 하는 것일 텐데요.\""]],[["“What art thou talking about, child?”","\"얘야, 대체 무슨 말을 하는 게냐?\""],["said Dorothea.","도로테아가 말했다."],["“Why, they say this singer is a muleteer!”","\"아니, 저 노래하는 이는 노새몰이꾼이라던데!\""]],[["“Nay, he is the lord of many places,”","\"아니에요, 그분은 여러 영지의 주인이세요.\""],["replied Clara, “and that one in my heart which he holds so firmly shall never be taken from him, unless he be willing to surrender it.”","클라라가 대답했다. \"그리고 제 마음속의 한 영지는 그분이 그토록 굳건히 차지하고 계시니, 그분 스스로 내어 주실 마음이 없는 한 결코 빼앗기지 않을 거예요.\""]],[["Dorothea was amazed at the ardent language of the girl, for it seemed to be far beyond such experience of life as her tender years gave any promise of, so she said to her:","도로테아는 그 소녀의 열렬한 말에 깜짝 놀랐으니, 그것은 그 어린 나이가 약속할 법한 인생의 경험을 한참 넘어선 듯 보였기에, 그녀에게 말했다."]],[["“You speak in such a way that I cannot understand you, Señora Clara; explain yourself more clearly, and tell me what is this you are saying about hearts and places and this musician whose voice has so moved you? But do not tell me anything now; I do not want to lose the pleasure I get from listening to the singer by giving my attention to your transports, for I perceive he is beginning to sing a new strain and a new air.”","\"클라라 아가씨, 무슨 말씀인지 알아들을 수가 없게 말씀하시는군요. 좀 더 분명하게 일러 주세요. 마음이며 영지며, 그리고 그 목소리가 아가씨를 그토록 흔들어 놓은 이 악사에 관해 무슨 말씀을 하시는지 말이에요. 하지만 지금은 아무 말도 하지 마세요. 아가씨의 그 격정에 정신을 쏟느라 노래하는 이의 노래를 듣는 즐거움을 잃고 싶지 않으니까요. 보아하니 그가 새로운 가락과 새로운 곡조를 부르기 시작하는 모양이네요.\""]],[["“Let him, in Heaven’s name,”","\"맙소사, 그러시든지요.\" 클라라가 대꾸했다."],["returned Clara; and not to hear him she stopped both ears with her hands, at which Dorothea was again surprised; but turning her attention to the song she found that it ran in this fashion:","그러고는 그의 노래를 듣지 않으려고 두 손으로 양쪽 귀를 틀어막았으니, 이에 도로테아는 다시금 놀랐다. 그러나 노래에 주의를 돌리자, 그 가락이 이러하게 흘러가는 것을 알게 되었다."]],[["Sweet Hope, my stay, That onward to the goal of thy intent Dost make thy way, Heedless of hindrance or impediment, Have thou no fear If at each step thou findest death is near.","감미로운 희망이여, 나의 버팀목이여, 그대가 품은 뜻의 목표를 향해 앞으로 길을 헤쳐 나아가며, 방해도 장애도 아랑곳하지 않는구나. 두려워 말지니, 한 걸음마다 죽음이 가까이 있음을 보더라도."]],[["No victory, No joy of triumph doth the faint heart know; Unblest is he That a bold front to Fortune dares not show, But soul and sense In bondage yieldeth up to indolence.","어떤 승리도, 승전의 어떤 기쁨도 약한 마음은 알지 못하나니, 복되지 못한 자로다, 운명에 담대한 낯을 감히 내보이지 못하고, 영혼과 감각을 나태에 묶어 내어 주는 자는."]],[["If Love his wares Do dearly sell, his right must be contest; What gold compares With that whereon his stamp he hath imprest?","사랑이 제 물건을 비싸게 판다면, 그 값어치는 다투어 마땅하니, 사랑이 제 도장을 찍어 놓은 것에 어떤 황금이 견주리오?"],["And all men know What costeth little that we rate but low.","또한 모든 이가 알거니, 값싸게 얻은 것은 우리가 하찮게 여기는 법."]],[["Love resolute Knows not the word “impossibility;” And though my suit Beset by endless obstacles I see, Yet no despair Shall hold me bound to earth while heaven is there.","결연한 사랑은 \"불가능\"이라는 말을 알지 못하나니, 내 구애가 끝없는 장애에 둘러싸여 있음을 보더라도, 어떤 절망도 하늘이 저기 있는 한 나를 땅에 묶어 두지 못하리라."]],[["Here the voice ceased and Clara’s sobs began afresh, all which excited Dorothea’s curiosity to know what could be the cause of singing so sweet and weeping so bitter, so she again asked her what it was she was going to say before.","여기서 목소리가 그쳤고 클라라의 흐느낌이 다시 시작되었으니, 이 모든 것이 도로테아의 호기심을 부추겨, 어찌하여 노래는 그리도 감미롭고 울음은 그리도 쓰라린지 그 까닭을 알고 싶게 했다. 그래서 도로테아는 아까 무슨 말을 하려 했었는지 다시 물었다."],["On this Clara, afraid that Luscinda might overhear her, winding her arms tightly round Dorothea put her mouth so close to her ear that she could speak without fear of being heard by anyone else, and said:","이에 클라라는 루신다가 엿들을까 두려워, 두 팔을 도로테아에게 단단히 감고 입을 그녀의 귀에 바짝 갖다 대어 다른 누구에게도 들릴 염려 없이 말할 수 있게 한 뒤 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“This singer, dear señora, is the son of a gentleman of Aragon, lord of two villages, who lives opposite my father’s house at Madrid; and though my father had curtains to the windows of his house in winter, and lattice-work in summer, in some way--I know not how--this gentleman, who was pursuing his studies, saw me, whether in church or elsewhere, I cannot tell, and, in fact, fell in love with me, and gave me to know it from the windows of his house, with so many signs and tears that I was forced to believe him, and even to love him, without knowing what it was he wanted of me. One of the signs he used to make me was to link one hand in the other, to show me he wished to marry me; and though I should have been glad if that could be, being alone and motherless I knew not whom to open my mind to, and so I left it as it was, showing him no favour, except when my father, and his too, were from home, to raise the curtain or the lattice a little and let him see me plainly, at which he would show such delight that he seemed as if he were going mad. Meanwhile the time for my father’s departure arrived, which he became aware of, but not from me, for I had never been able to tell him of it. He fell sick, of grief I believe, and so the day we were going away I could not see him to take farewell of him, were it only with the eyes. But after we had been two days on the road, on entering the posada of a village a day’s journey from this, I saw him at the inn door in the dress of a muleteer, and so well disguised, that if I did not carry his image graven on my heart it would have been impossible for me to recognise him. But I knew him, and I was surprised, and glad; he watched me, unsuspected by my father, from whom he always hides himself when he crosses my path on the road, or in the posadas where we halt; and, as I know what he is, and reflect that for love of me he makes this journey on foot in all this hardship, I am ready to die of sorrow; and where he sets foot there I set my eyes. I know not with what object he has come; or how he could have got away from his father, who loves him beyond measure, having no other heir, and because he deserves it, as you will perceive when you see him. And moreover, I can tell you, all that he sings is out of his own head; for I have heard them say he is a great scholar and poet; and what is more, every time I see him or hear him sing I tremble all over, and am terrified lest my father should recognise him and come to know of our loves. I have never spoken a word to him in my life; and for all that I love him so that I could not live without him. This, dear señora, is all I have to tell you about the musician whose voice has delighted you so much; and from it alone you might easily perceive he is no muleteer, but a lord of hearts and towns, as I told you already.”","\"다정하신 부인, 이 노래하는 이는 아라곤 출신 어느 신사의 아들로, 마을 두 곳의 영주이신 분의 자제예요. 그 신사는 마드리드에 있는 제 아버지 집 맞은편에 사시는데요, 비록 제 아버지가 겨울이면 집 창문에 커튼을, 여름이면 격자창을 쳐 두셨음에도, 어찌 된 영문인지 — 저도 모르겠어요 — 학업을 닦고 있던 이 신사분이, 교회에서였는지 다른 곳에서였는지는 말씀드릴 수 없지만, 아무튼 저를 보셨고, 그러다 저와 사랑에 빠지셨어요. 그리고 자기 집 창문에서 그토록 많은 신호와 눈물로 그 마음을 제게 알리시기에, 저는 그분을 믿지 않을 수 없었고, 심지어 그분이 제게서 무엇을 바라는지도 모른 채 그분을 사랑하지 않을 수 없었어요. 그분이 제게 보내곤 하던 신호 중 하나는 한 손을 다른 손에 맞잡아, 저와 혼인하고 싶다는 뜻을 보이는 것이었어요. 그렇게 될 수만 있다면 저야 기뻤겠지만, 외톨이에다 어머니도 없는 처지라 누구에게 제 마음을 털어놓아야 할지 몰라, 그저 그대로 두고 그분께 아무 호의도 보이지 않았어요. 다만 제 아버지와 그분의 아버지가 모두 집을 비우셨을 때면, 커튼이나 격자창을 조금 들어 올려 그분이 저를 똑똑히 볼 수 있게 해 드렸는데, 그러면 그분은 미쳐 버릴 것만 같은 기쁨을 내보이셨어요. 그러는 사이 제 아버지가 떠나실 때가 다가왔고, 그분이 그것을 알게 되셨지만 제게서 들으신 건 아니었어요. 저는 그것을 그분께 도무지 알릴 수가 없었으니까요. 그분은, 제 생각엔 슬픔 때문에 병이 나셨고, 그래서 저희가 떠나던 날 저는 그분을 뵙고 작별 인사를 — 그저 눈으로라도 — 드릴 수가 없었어요. 그런데 길을 떠난 지 이틀이 지나, 여기서 하루 거리에 있는 어느 마을의 여인숙에 들어서는데, 저는 여관 문간에서 노새몰이꾼 차림을 한 그분을 보았어요. 어찌나 변장을 잘하셨던지, 제가 그분의 모습을 마음에 새겨 두고 다니지 않았더라면 알아보지 못했을 거예요. 하지만 저는 그분을 알아보았고, 놀라우면서도 기뻤어요. 그분은 제 아버지 모르게 저를 지켜보셨는데, 길에서나 저희가 묵는 여인숙에서나 제 앞을 지날 때면 늘 아버지를 피해 몸을 숨기시거든요. 그분이 어떤 분인지 알고, 또 저를 사랑하는 마음에 이 모든 고생을 무릅쓰며 걸어서 이 여행을 하신다는 걸 떠올리면, 저는 슬픔에 죽을 것만 같아요. 그래서 그분이 발을 딛는 곳마다 제 눈길을 두지요. 그분이 무슨 뜻으로 오셨는지, 또 어떻게 자기 아버지에게서 빠져나오실 수 있었는지 저는 모르겠어요. 그 아버지는 다른 상속자도 없거니와 그분이 그럴 만한 자식이기에 한없이 그분을 사랑하시거든요. 그건 그분을 보시면 부인께서도 아시게 될 거예요. 게다가 말씀드릴 수 있는 건, 그분이 부르는 노래는 죄다 그분의 머릿속에서 나온 것이라는 거예요. 사람들이 그분을 대단한 학자이자 시인이라 말하는 것을 제가 들었거든요. 더구나 저는 그분을 보거나 그분의 노래를 들을 때마다 온몸이 떨리고, 제 아버지가 그분을 알아보고 저희 사랑을 알게 되실까 봐 겁이 나요. 저는 평생 그분과 단 한 마디도 나눈 적이 없어요. 그런데도 그분 없이는 살 수 없을 만큼 그분을 사랑한답니다. 다정하신 부인, 이것이 그 목소리로 부인을 그토록 기쁘게 한 그 악사에 관해 제가 드릴 수 있는 이야기 전부예요. 그리고 이것만으로도 그분이 노새몰이꾼이 아니라, 제가 이미 말씀드렸듯 마음과 마을의 주인이라는 것을 부인께서는 어렵잖이 아실 수 있을 거예요.\""]],[["“Say no more, Dona Clara,” said Dorothea at this, at the same time kissing her a thousand times over, “say no more, I tell you, but wait till day comes; when I trust in God to arrange this affair of yours so that it may have the happy ending such an innocent beginning deserves.”","\"클라라 아가씨, 더는 말씀하지 마세요.\" 이 말에 도로테아가 그녀에게 천 번이나 입 맞추며 말했다. \"더는 말씀하지 마시라니까요. 날이 밝을 때까지 기다리세요. 그러면 저는 하느님께서 이 아가씨의 일을 마련해 주시리라 믿어요. 이토록 순결한 시작에 어울리는 행복한 결말을 맺도록 말이에요.\""]],[["“Ah, señora,” said Dona Clara, “what end can be hoped for when his father is of such lofty position, and so wealthy, that he would think I was not fit to be even a servant to his son, much less wife? And as to marrying without the knowledge of my father, I would not do it for all the world. I would not ask anything more than that this youth should go back and leave me; perhaps with not seeing him, and the long distance we shall have to travel, the pain I suffer now may become easier; though I daresay the remedy I propose will do me very little good. I don’t know how the devil this has come about, or how this love I have for him got in; I such a young girl, and he such a mere boy; for I verily believe we are both of an age, and I am not sixteen yet; for I will be sixteen Michaelmas Day, next, my father says.”","\"아, 부인.\" 도냐 클라라가 말했다. \"그분의 아버지가 그토록 높은 지위에 계시고 그토록 부유하시어, 저 같은 건 그분 아들의 아내는커녕 하녀가 될 자격조차 없다 여기실 텐데, 무슨 결말을 바랄 수 있겠어요? 그리고 제 아버지 몰래 혼인하는 일은, 세상을 다 준다 해도 하지 않을 거예요. 제가 바라는 건 그저 이 젊은이가 돌아가서 저를 떠나는 것뿐이에요. 어쩌면 그분을 보지 않고, 또 저희가 가야 할 그 먼 길을 가다 보면, 지금 제가 겪는 이 고통이 한결 가벼워질지도 몰라요. 하긴 제가 내놓은 그 처방이 제게 별 도움이 안 되리란 건 짐작하지만요. 어쩌다 이런 일이 생겼는지, 그분을 향한 이 사랑이 어떻게 들어왔는지 도무지 모르겠어요. 저는 이렇게 어린 계집애이고 그분은 이렇게 한낱 소년인데요. 정말이지 저희 둘은 동갑이라고 생각해요. 저는 아직 열여섯도 안 됐거든요. 아버지 말씀으로는 다가오는 미카엘 축일에야 열여섯이 된다고 하셨어요.\""]],[["Dorothea could not help laughing to hear how like a child Dona Clara spoke.","도로테아는 도냐 클라라가 어찌나 어린아이처럼 말하는지 웃지 않을 수 없었다. \"이제 잠을 청합시다, 아가씨.\""],["“Let us go to sleep now, señora,”","그녀가 말했다. \"밤이 얼마 안 남은 듯하니까요."],["said she, “for the little of the night that I fancy is left to us: God will soon send us daylight, and we will set all to rights, or it will go hard with me.”","하느님께서 이내 날을 밝혀 주실 테고, 그러면 우리가 만사를 바로잡지요. 그러지 못하면 제 탓이 클 거예요.\""]],[["With this they fell asleep, and deep silence reigned all through the inn.","이 말과 함께 두 사람은 잠이 들었고, 깊은 정적이 여인숙 전체를 다스렸다."],["The only persons not asleep were the landlady’s daughter and her servant Maritornes, who, knowing the weak point of Don Quixote’s humour, and that he was outside the inn mounting guard in armour and on horseback, resolved, the pair of them, to play some trick upon him, or at any rate to amuse themselves for a while by listening to his nonsense.","잠들지 않은 사람이라곤 여관 안주인의 딸과 그 하녀 마리토르네스뿐이었으니, 둘은 돈키호테의 별난 기질의 약점을 익히 알고 있던 데다, 그가 갑옷을 입고 말에 올라 여인숙 밖에서 보초를 서고 있다는 것도 알았기에, 그에게 무슨 장난을 치거나, 아니면 적어도 그의 허튼소리를 들으며 한동안 재미나 보자고 둘이서 작정했다. 마침 여인숙을 통틀어 바깥을 향해 난 창이라곤, 짚을 던져 내보내는 데 쓰던 짚 광 벽의 구멍 하나밖에 없었다."],["As it so happened there was not a window in the whole inn that looked outwards except a hole in the wall of a straw-loft through which they used to throw out the straw.","이 구멍에 두 명의 반쪽짜리 아가씨가 자리를 잡고, 말 위에 앉아 창을 짚은 채 이따금 어찌나 깊고 구슬픈 한숨을 내쉬는지 그때마다 제 영혼을 뿌리째 뽑아내는 듯한 돈키호테를 살펴보았다."],["At this hole the two demi-damsels posted themselves, and observed Don Quixote on his horse, leaning on his pike and from time to time sending forth such deep and doleful sighs, that he seemed to pluck up his soul by the roots with each of them; and they could hear him, too, saying in a soft, tender, loving tone, “Oh my lady Dulcinea del Toboso, perfection of all beauty, summit and crown of discretion, treasure house of grace, depositary of virtue, and finally, ideal of all that is good, honourable, and delectable in this world!","또한 그가 부드럽고 다정하고 사랑이 담긴 어조로 이렇게 말하는 것도 들을 수 있었다. \"오, 나의 여인 둘시네아 델 토보소여, 온갖 아름다움의 극치요, 분별의 정점이자 면류관이며, 우아함의 보고요, 미덕의 곳간이며, 끝으로 이 세상 온갖 선하고 명예롭고 즐거운 것의 이상이시여!"],["What is thy grace doing now?","그대 고귀하신 분께서는 지금 무엇을 하고 계신지요?"],["Art thou, perchance, mindful of thy enslaved knight who of his own free will hath exposed himself to so great perils, and all to serve thee?","혹여 그대는, 오로지 그대를 섬기기 위해 자청하여 이토록 큰 위험에 몸을 내던진, 그대에게 사로잡힌 기사를 마음에 두고 계신지요? 오, 세 얼굴을 가진 빛이여, 그녀의 소식을 내게 전해 다오!"],["Give me tidings of her, oh luminary of the three faces!","어쩌면 이 순간, 그녀를 시샘하며 그대는 그녀를 바라보고 있을지도 모르지. 그녀가 호화로운 궁전의 어느 회랑을 이리저리 거닐든, 어느 발코니에 기대어, 자신의 순결과 위엄을 지키면서도 어떻게 하면 그녀를 위해 견디는 이 가련한 내 가슴의 고통을 덜어 줄 수 있을지, 어떤 영광이 내 고난을 보답할지, 어떤 안식이 내 수고를, 끝으로 어떤 죽음이 내 삶을, 또 어떤 보상이 내 봉사를 갚아 줄지 골똘히 헤아리는 동안에 말이지."],["Perhaps at this moment, envious of hers, thou art regarding her, either as she paces to and fro some gallery of her sumptuous palaces, or leans over some balcony, meditating how, whilst preserving her purity and greatness, she may mitigate the tortures this wretched heart of mine endures for her sake, what glory should recompense my sufferings, what repose my toil, and lastly what death my life, and what reward my services?","그리고 그대, 오, 태양이여, 그대는 지금 의심할 바 없이 일찍 떠올라 내 여인을 보러 나오려고 서둘러 그대의 준마들에 마구를 채우고 있으렷다. 그대가 그녀를 보거든 부디 내 대신 그녀에게 인사를 전해 다오."],["And thou, oh sun, that art now doubtless harnessing thy steeds in haste to rise betimes and come forth to see my lady; when thou seest her I entreat of thee to salute her on my behalf: but have a care, when thou shalt see her and salute her, that thou kiss not her face; for I shall be more jealous of thee than thou wert of that light-footed ingrate that made thee sweat and run so on the plains of Thessaly, or on the banks of the Peneus (for I do not exactly recollect where it was thou didst run on that occasion) in thy jealousy and love.”","허나 조심할지니, 그녀를 보고 인사할 때 그녀의 얼굴에 입 맞추지는 말지어다. 그러지 않으면 나는, 그대가 질투와 사랑에 빠져 테살리아 들판에서, 아니면 페네우스 강가에서(그때 그대가 어디서 달렸는지 정확히 기억나지는 않지만) 그대를 그토록 땀 흘리며 달리게 했던 그 발 빠른 배은망덕한 여인에게 품었던 것보다 더한 질투를 그대에게 품게 될 테니까.\""]],[["Don Quixote had got so far in his pathetic speech when the landlady’s daughter began to signal to him, saying, “Señor, come over here, please.”","돈키호테가 그 애절한 연설을 여기까지 이어 갔을 때, 여관 안주인의 딸이 그에게 신호를 보내기 시작하며 말했다. \"기사님, 이리로 좀 와 주세요.\""]],[["At these signals and voice Don Quixote turned his head and saw by the light of the moon, which then was in its full splendour, that some one was calling to him from the hole in the wall, which seemed to him to be a window, and what is more, with a gilt grating, as rich castles, such as he believed the inn to be, ought to have; and it immediately suggested itself to his imagination that, as on the former occasion, the fair damsel, the daughter of the lady of the castle, overcome by love for him, was once more endeavouring to win his affections; and with this idea, not to show himself discourteous, or ungrateful, he turned Rocinante’s head and approached the hole, and as he perceived the two wenches he said:","이 신호와 목소리에 돈키호테는 고개를 돌렸고, 마침 휘영청 만월의 광채로 빛나던 달빛에 비추어, 누군가가 벽의 그 구멍에서 자기를 부르고 있음을 보았다. 그에게는 그것이 창문처럼 보였고, 더구나 — 그가 여인숙을 그렇게 믿었듯 — 화려한 성이라면 으레 갖추었을 법한 금빛 격자가 달린 창문으로 보였다. 그러자 즉시, 지난번처럼, 이 성주의 따님인 아름다운 아가씨가 그를 향한 사랑에 사로잡혀 다시 한번 그의 애정을 얻으려 애쓰고 있다는 생각이 그의 상상에 떠올랐다. 이런 생각에, 무례하거나 배은망덕하게 보이지 않으려고, 그는 로시난테의 머리를 돌려 그 구멍으로 다가갔고, 두 처녀를 알아보자 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“I pity you, beauteous lady, that you should have directed your thoughts of love to a quarter from whence it is impossible that such a return can be made to you as is due to your great merit and gentle birth, for which you must not blame this unhappy knight-errant whom love renders incapable of submission to any other than her whom, the first moment his eyes beheld her, he made absolute mistress of his soul. Forgive me, noble lady, and retire to your apartment, and do not, by any further declaration of your passion, compel me to show myself more ungrateful; and if, of the love you bear me, you should find that there is anything else in my power wherein I can gratify you, provided it be not love itself, demand it of me; for I swear to you by that sweet absent enemy of mine to grant it this instant, though it be that you require of me a lock of Medusa’s hair, which was all snakes, or even the very beams of the sun shut up in a vial.”","\"아름다운 아가씨여, 그대의 큰 덕망과 고귀한 가문에 마땅한 보답을 도무지 돌려드릴 수 없는 자에게 사랑의 정념을 두셨다니, 가엾기 그지없습니다. 허나 그 일로 이 불행한 편력 기사를 탓하셔서는 아니 됩니다. 사랑이 이 기사로 하여금, 처음 눈으로 보았던 그 순간에 자기 영혼의 절대적 여주인으로 삼은 그분 외에는 누구에게도 굴복할 수 없게 만들었기 때문입니다. 고귀한 아가씨여, 저를 용서하시고 그대의 처소로 물러가소서. 그리고 그대의 정념을 더는 토로하시어 저로 하여금 더한층 배은망덕한 모습을 보이도록 몰아가지 마소서. 만일 저를 향한 그 사랑으로, 사랑 그 자체만 아니라면, 제 능력이 닿는 한도 안에서 그대를 만족시켜 드릴 수 있는 다른 무언가가 있다 여기신다면, 제게 청하소서. 제 그 감미롭고도 멀리 떨어진 적을 두고 그대에게 맹세하노니, 비록 그대가 온통 뱀으로 된 메두사의 머리카락 한 타래를 요구하시든, 아니 병 속에 가둔 태양의 빛줄기 그 자체를 요구하시든, 당장 이 순간 들어드리겠나이다.\""]],[["“My mistress wants nothing of that sort, sir knight,”","\"기사님, 저희 아씨께서는 그런 건 아무것도 원하지 않으세요.\""],["said Maritornes at this.","이때 마리토르네스가 말했다."]],[["“What then, discreet dame, is it that your mistress wants?”","\"그렇다면 분별 있는 부인, 그대의 아씨께서 원하시는 것이 무엇이오?\""],["replied Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 대꾸했다."]],[["“Only one of your fair hands,”","\"그저 기사님의 고운 손 한쪽이에요.\" 마리토르네스가 말했다."],["said Maritornes, “to enable her to vent over it the great passion passion which has brought her to this loophole, so much to the risk of her honour; for if the lord her father had heard her, the least slice he would cut off her would be her ear.”","\"그 손에다 아씨께서, 그 큰 정념을 — 이 구멍까지 이끌려 나와 명예를 그토록 위태롭게 하면서까지 품게 된 그 정념을 — 쏟아 내실 수 있게요. 만일 영주이신 아버님께서 아씨의 소리를 들으셨더라면, 잘라 내실 것 중 가장 작은 것이 아씨의 귀였을 테니까요.\""]],[["“I should like to see that tried,”","\"그런 짓을 한번 시도하는 꼴을 보았으면 좋겠소.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “but he had better beware of that, if he does not want to meet the most disastrous end that ever father in the world met for having laid hands on the tender limbs of a love-stricken daughter.”","\"허나 그자는 그런 짓을 삼가는 편이 나을 거요. 사랑에 빠진 딸의 보드라운 사지에 손을 댔다가, 세상의 어떤 아비도 맞은 적 없는 가장 처참한 최후를 맞고 싶지 않다면 말이오.\""]],[["Maritornes felt sure that Don Quixote would present the hand she had asked, and making up her mind what to do, she got down from the hole and went into the stable, where she took the halter of Sancho Panza’s ass, and in all haste returned to the hole, just as Don Quixote had planted himself standing on Rocinante’s saddle in order to reach the grated window where he supposed the lovelorn damsel to be; and giving her his hand, he said, “Lady, take this hand, or rather this scourge of the evil-doers of the earth; take, I say, this hand which no other hand of woman has ever touched, not even hers who has complete possession of my entire body. I present it to you, not that you may kiss it, but that you may observe the contexture of the sinews, the close network of the muscles, the breadth and capacity of the veins, whence you may infer what must be the strength of the arm that has such a hand.”","마리토르네스는 돈키호테가 자기가 청한 손을 내밀 것이라 확신하고, 무엇을 할지 마음을 정한 뒤, 구멍에서 내려와 마구간으로 들어가 산초 판사의 당나귀 고삐를 집어 들고는, 마침 돈키호테가 사랑에 애태우는 아가씨가 있다고 여긴 그 격자창에 닿으려고 로시난테의 안장 위에 올라선 바로 그 순간에, 한껏 서둘러 구멍으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 그에게 손을 내밀며 그가 말했다. \"아가씨여, 이 손을, 아니 차라리 땅 위 악인들을 응징하는 이 채찍을 받으소서. 자, 받으소서. 이 손은 그 어떤 여인의 손도 닿은 적이 없으니, 내 온몸을 완전히 차지하고 계신 그분의 손조차도 닿은 적이 없는 손입니다. 입 맞추라고 이 손을 내미는 것이 아니라, 힘줄의 짜임새와 근육의 촘촘한 얽힘과 핏줄의 너비와 굵기를 살펴보시라고 내미는 것이니, 그로써 그대는 이런 손을 가진 팔의 힘이 어떠할지 헤아릴 수 있으리다.\""]],[["“That we shall see presently,”","\"그건 곧 알게 되겠지요.\""],["said Maritornes, and making a running knot on the halter, she passed it over his wrist and coming down from the hole tied the other end very firmly to the bolt of the door of the straw-loft.","마리토르네스가 말하고는, 고삐에 매듭 올가미를 지어 그것을 그의 손목에 걸고, 구멍에서 내려와 그 다른 끝을 짚 광 문빗장에 아주 단단히 잡아맸다."]],[["Don Quixote, feeling the roughness of the rope on his wrist, exclaimed, “Your grace seems to be grating rather than caressing my hand; treat it not so harshly, for it is not to blame for the offence my resolution has given you, nor is it just to wreak all your vengeance on so small a part; remember that one who loves so well should not revenge herself so cruelly.”","손목에 닿는 밧줄의 거칠음을 느낀 돈키호테가 부르짖었다. \"그대께서는 제 손을 어루만지신다기보다 갈아 대시는 듯하구려. 그토록 모질게 다루지 마소서. 제 결심이 그대를 노엽게 한 그 잘못에 이 손은 죄가 없으며, 그토록 작은 부분에 그대의 온갖 복수를 퍼붓는 것도 옳지 않소이다. 그토록 깊이 사랑하는 이라면 그토록 잔혹하게 복수해서는 아니 됨을 기억하소서.\""]],[["But there was nobody now to listen to these words of Don Quixote’s, for as soon as Maritornes had tied him she and the other made off, ready to die with laughing, leaving him fastened in such a way that it was impossible for him to release himself.","그러나 이제 돈키호테의 이 말을 들어 줄 사람은 아무도 없었으니, 마리토르네스는 그를 묶자마자 다른 처녀와 함께 웃다 죽을 지경이 되어 달아나 버렸고, 그가 스스로 풀려나는 것이 도무지 불가능한 꼴로 그를 매어 둔 채 떠난 것이다."]],[["He was, as has been said, standing on Rocinante, with his arm passed through the hole and his wrist tied to the bolt of the door, and in mighty fear and dread of being left hanging by the arm if Rocinante were to stir one side or the other; so he did not dare to make the least movement, although from the patience and imperturbable disposition of Rocinante, he had good reason to expect that he would stand without budging for a whole century. Finding himself fast, then, and that the ladies had retired, he began to fancy that all this was done by enchantment, as on the former occasion when in that same castle that enchanted Moor of a carrier had belaboured him; and he cursed in his heart his own want of sense and judgment in venturing to enter the castle again, after having come off so badly the first time; it being a settled point with knights-errant that when they have tried an adventure, and have not succeeded in it, it is a sign that it is not reserved for them but for others, and that therefore they need not try it again. Nevertheless he pulled his arm to see if he could release himself, but it had been made so fast that all his efforts were in vain. It is true he pulled it gently lest Rocinante should move, but try as he might to seat himself in the saddle, he had nothing for it but to stand upright or pull his hand off. Then it was he wished for the sword of Amadis, against which no enchantment whatever had any power; then he cursed his ill fortune; then he magnified the loss the world would sustain by his absence while he remained there enchanted, for that he believed he was beyond all doubt; then he once more took to thinking of his beloved Dulcinea del Toboso; then he called to his worthy squire Sancho Panza, who, buried in sleep and stretched upon the pack-saddle of his ass, was oblivious, at that moment, of the mother that bore him; then he called upon the sages Lirgandeo and Alquife to come to his aid; then he invoked his good friend Urganda to succour him; and then, at last, morning found him in such a state of desperation and perplexity that he was bellowing like a bull, for he had no hope that day would bring any relief to his suffering, which he believed would last for ever, inasmuch as he was enchanted; and of this he was convinced by seeing that Rocinante never stirred, much or little, and he felt persuaded that he and his horse were to remain in this state, without eating or drinking or sleeping, until the malign influence of the stars was overpast, or until some other more sage enchanter should disenchant him.","앞서 말했듯이 그는 로시난테 위에 서 있었고, 팔은 구멍으로 통과시킨 채 손목은 문빗장에 묶여 있어, 로시난테가 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 한 발짝이라도 움직이면 팔에 매달린 신세가 될까 봐 극심한 두려움과 공포에 떨었다. 그리하여 그는 손톱만큼도 움직이려 들지 못했다. 비록 로시난테의 참을성 있고 흔들림 없는 성품으로 보아, 한 세기 내내 꿈쩍 않고 서 있으리라 충분히 기대할 만했지만 말이다. 자기가 단단히 묶여 있고 아가씨들이 물러갔음을 알게 되자, 그는 이 모든 것이 마법으로 벌어진 일이라 여기기 시작했으니, 지난번 바로 그 성에서 그 마법에 걸린 무어인 같은 짐꾼이 자기를 흠씬 두들겨 팼던 때처럼 말이다. 그리고 그는, 처음에 그토록 호되게 당하고서도 또다시 이 성에 들어오는 모험을 무릅쓴 자신의 분별없음과 판단력 부족을 마음속으로 저주했다. 편력 기사들 사이에서는, 일단 어떤 모험을 시도했다가 성공하지 못하면 그것은 그들을 위한 것이 아니라 다른 이들을 위해 마련된 모험이라는 표시이므로 다시 시도할 필요가 없다는 것이 정해진 이치였기 때문이다. 그럼에도 그는 풀려날 수 있을까 하여 팔을 잡아당겨 보았으나, 너무도 단단히 매여 있어 온갖 애를 써 보았자 헛수고였다. 사실 그는 로시난테가 움직일까 봐 조심스레 잡아당기긴 했으나, 안장에 다시 앉아 보려고 아무리 애를 써도 별수 없이 그저 꼿꼿이 서 있거나 손을 잡아 떼어 내는 수밖에 없었다. 바로 그때 그는, 그 어떤 마법도 힘을 쓰지 못하던 아마디스의 검을 갈망했다. 그러고는 자기 불운을 저주했다. 그러고는 자기가 여기 마법에 걸려 있는 동안 세상이 입게 될 손실이 얼마나 클지 부풀려 생각했으니, 자기가 의심할 바 없이 마법에 걸려 있다고 믿었기 때문이다. 그러고는 다시 한번 사랑하는 둘시네아 델 토보소를 떠올리기 시작했다. 그러고는 충직한 종자 산초 판사를 불렀으나, 그는 당나귀의 짐안장 위에 큰대자로 뻗어 잠에 파묻힌 채 그 순간 자기를 낳아 준 어미조차 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 그러고는 현자 리르간데오와 알키페에게 도와달라고 외쳤다. 그러고는 자기의 좋은 벗 우르간다에게 구원을 청했다. 그러고는 마침내, 아침이 되도록 그는 절망과 당혹에 빠져 황소처럼 울부짖고 있었으니, 그날이 자기 고통에 어떤 위안도 가져다주지 못하리라 여겼기 때문이다. 자기가 마법에 걸린 이상 그 고통이 영원히 계속되리라 믿었던 것이다. 로시난테가 많이든 적게든 도무지 꿈쩍하지 않는 것을 보고 그는 이 믿음을 더욱 굳혔으니, 자기와 자기 말이 흉성(凶星)의 사악한 기운이 다 지나가거나, 혹은 더 현명한 어떤 마법사가 마법을 풀어 줄 때까지 먹지도 마시지도 자지도 못한 채 이 꼴로 남아 있어야 하리라 믿어 버린 것이다."]],[["But he was very much deceived in this conclusion, for daylight had hardly begun to appear when there came up to the inn four men on horseback, well equipped and accoutred, with firelocks across their saddle-bows. They called out and knocked loudly at the gate of the inn, which was still shut; on seeing which, Don Quixote, even there where he was, did not forget to act as sentinel, and said in a loud and imperious tone, “Knights, or squires, or whatever ye be, ye have no right to knock at the gates of this castle; for it is plain enough that they who are within are either asleep, or else are not in the habit of throwing open the fortress until the sun’s rays are spread over the whole surface of the earth. Withdraw to a distance, and wait till it is broad daylight, and then we shall see whether it will be proper or not to open to you.”","그러나 그는 이 결론에서 크게 속고 있었으니, 날이 채 밝기도 전에 잘 갖춰 입고 무장한 네 명의 사내가 말을 타고 안장 앞에 화승총을 가로질러 걸친 채 여인숙으로 다가왔기 때문이다. 그들은 아직 닫혀 있던 여인숙 문을 향해 큰 소리로 외치며 요란하게 두드렸다. 이를 본 돈키호테는, 자기가 있던 그곳에서도 보초 노릇 하기를 잊지 않고, 크고 위엄 있는 어조로 말했다. \"기사들이든, 종자들이든, 그대들이 무엇이든 간에, 그대들에게는 이 성의 문을 두드릴 권리가 없소. 안에 있는 이들이 잠들어 있거나, 아니면 햇살이 온 땅 위에 두루 퍼지기 전에는 이 요새를 열어젖히는 법이 없음이 자명하지 않소. 멀찍이 물러가, 날이 환히 밝을 때까지 기다리시오. 그런 다음에야 그대들에게 문을 여는 것이 마땅한지 아닌지 우리가 보게 될 것이오.\""]],[["“What the devil fortress or castle is this,”","\"대체 이게 무슨 빌어먹을 요새고 성이라고,\""],["said one, “to make us stand on such ceremony?","한 사람이 말했다. \"우리한테 이런 격식을 차리게 한단 말이오?"],["If you are the innkeeper bid them open to us; we are travellers who only want to feed our horses and go on, for we are in haste.”","당신이 여관 주인이거든 문을 열라고 일러 주시오. 우리는 그저 말에게 여물이나 먹이고 가던 길을 가려는 나그네들이고, 갈 길이 바쁘오.\""]],[["“Do you think, gentlemen, that I look like an innkeeper?”","\"나리들, 내가 여관 주인처럼 보이시오?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“I don’t know what you look like,”","\"당신이 무엇처럼 보이는지는 모르겠소.\""],["replied the other; “but I know that you are talking nonsense when you call this inn a castle.”","그 사람이 대답했다. \"허나 이 여인숙을 성이라 부르다니 허튼소리를 하고 있다는 건 알겠소.\""]],[["“A castle it is,”","\"성이 맞소.\" 돈키호테가 받아쳤다."],["returned Don Quixote, “nay, more, one of the best in this whole province, and it has within it people who have had the sceptre in the hand and the crown on the head.”","\"아니, 그뿐 아니라 이 지방 전체에서 손꼽히는 성 중 하나요. 그리고 그 안에는 손에 왕홀을 쥐고 머리에 왕관을 얹어 본 사람들이 있소.\""]],[["“It would be better if it were the other way,” said the traveller, “the sceptre on the head and the crown in the hand; but if so, may be there is within some company of players, with whom it is a common thing to have those crowns and sceptres you speak of; for in such a small inn as this, and where such silence is kept, I do not believe any people entitled to crowns and sceptres can have taken up their quarters.”","\"차라리 그 반대라면 나을 텐데,\" 나그네가 말했다. \"머리에 왕홀을, 손에 왕관을 말이오. 하지만 그렇다면 안에 어쩌면 어느 배우 패거리가 있는 모양이군. 그들이야 당신이 말하는 그 왕관이며 왕홀을 갖고 다니는 게 흔한 일이니 말이오. 이렇게 조그만 여인숙에, 게다가 이토록 조용한 곳에, 왕관과 왕홀을 누릴 자격이 있는 사람들이 묵고 있으리라곤 믿기지 않으니까.\""]],[["“You know but little of the world,”","\"그대는 세상을 거의 모르는구려.\""],["returned Don Quixote, “since you are ignorant of what commonly occurs in knight-errantry.”","돈키호테가 되받았다. \"편력 기사도에서 흔히 일어나는 일을 모르는 걸 보니 말이오.\""]],[["But the comrades of the spokesman, growing weary of the dialogue with Don Quixote, renewed their knocks with great vehemence, so much so that the host, and not only he but everybody in the inn, awoke, and he got up to ask who knocked. It happened at this moment that one of the horses of the four who were seeking admittance went to smell Rocinante, who melancholy, dejected, and with drooping ears stood motionless, supporting his sorely stretched master; and as he was, after all, flesh, though he looked as if he were made of wood, he could not help giving way and in return smelling the one who had come to offer him attentions. But he had hardly moved at all when Don Quixote lost his footing; and slipping off the saddle, he would have come to the ground, but for being suspended by the arm, which caused him such agony that he believed either his wrist would be cut through or his arm torn off; and he hung so near the ground that he could just touch it with his feet, which was all the worse for him; for, finding how little was wanted to enable him to plant his feet firmly, he struggled and stretched himself as much as he could to gain a footing; just like those undergoing the torture of the strappado, when they are fixed at “touch and no touch,” who aggravate their own sufferings by their violent efforts to stretch themselves, deceived by the hope which makes them fancy that with a very little more they will reach the ground.","그러나 말하던 사내의 동료들은 돈키호테와의 입씨름에 진력이 나, 다시 어찌나 거세게 문을 두드렸던지, 주인은 물론이고 여인숙 안의 모든 이가 잠에서 깨어났고, 주인은 일어나 누가 문을 두드리는지 물으려 했다. 마침 이 순간, 안으로 들어가기를 청하던 네 사람의 말 가운데 한 마리가 로시난테의 냄새를 맡으러 다가갔다. 로시난테는 우울하고 풀 죽은 채 귀를 늘어뜨리고 꿈쩍도 않고 서서, 호되게 늘어진 주인을 떠받치고 있었다. 그러나 나무로 만들어진 것처럼 보일지언정 결국 살과 피로 된 짐승인지라, 어쩔 수 없이 그만 몸을 내주어, 자기에게 관심을 보이러 온 그 말에게 답례로 냄새를 맡았다. 그런데 그가 조금이라도 움직이자마자 돈키호테는 발 디딘 곳을 잃고 안장에서 미끄러져 땅에 떨어졌을 것이나, 팔로 매달린 덕에 그러지 못했다. 그 바람에 그는 손목이 잘려 나가거나 팔이 뜯겨 나가리라 여겨질 만큼 극심한 고통을 겪었다. 그리고 그는 땅에서 어찌나 가까이 매달려 있던지 발끝으로 겨우 땅에 닿을 정도였는데, 이것이 그에게는 오히려 더 나빴다. 발을 단단히 디디는 데 얼마나 모자라는지를 알아채자, 그는 발 디딜 자리를 얻으려고 안간힘을 다해 버둥거리며 몸을 뻗었으니, 마치 스트라파도 고문을 당하는 자들이 \"닿을 듯 말 듯한\" 높이에 매달려, 조금만 더 하면 땅에 닿으리라는 헛된 희망에 속아 미친 듯이 몸을 뻗으며 제 고통을 한층 키우는 꼴과 꼭 같았다. * 스트라파도: 두 손을 뒤로 묶어 도르래로 매달아 올렸다가 떨어뜨리는 옛 고문 방식."]],[["# CHAPTER XLIV. IN WHICH ARE CONTINUED THE UNHEARD-OF ADVENTURES OF THE INN","# CHAPTER XLIV."]],[["So loud, in fact, were the shouts of Don Quixote, that the landlord opening the gate of the inn in all haste, came out in dismay, and ran to see who was uttering such cries, and those who were outside joined him.","이렇듯 돈키호테가 지른 고함 소리가 어찌나 컸던지, 여관 주인이 부랴부랴 여관 문을 열고 당황한 얼굴로 뛰쳐나와 누가 그런 고함을 지르는지 보려 달려갔고, 바깥에 있던 자들도 그를 뒤따랐다."],["Maritornes, who had been by this time roused up by the same outcry, suspecting what it was, ran to the loft and, without anyone seeing her, untied the halter by which Don Quixote was suspended, and down he came to the ground in the sight of the landlord and the travellers, who approaching asked him what was the matter with him that he shouted so.","그 무렵 같은 고함 소리에 잠이 깬 마리토르네스는 무슨 일인지 짐작하고 다락으로 달려가, 아무도 보지 못하게 돈키호테가 매달려 있던 밧줄을 풀어 주었다. 그리하여 그는 여관 주인과 길손들이 보는 앞에서 땅으로 떨어졌고, 그들이 다가와 무슨 일로 그토록 고함을 질렀느냐고 물었다."],["He without replying a word took the rope off his wrist, and rising to his feet leaped upon Rocinante, braced his buckler on his arm, put his lance in rest, and making a considerable circuit of the plain came back at a half-gallop exclaiming:","그는 한마디 대꾸도 없이 손목에 묶인 밧줄을 풀고는 벌떡 일어나 로시난테에 뛰어올라, 방패를 팔에 끼고 창을 겨드랑이에 받친 채 들판을 한참 빙 돌더니 절반쯤 질주하며 돌아와 외쳤다."]],[["“Whoever shall say that I have been enchanted with just cause, provided my lady the Princess Micomicona grants me permission to do so, I give him the lie, challenge him and defy him to single combat.”","\"누구든 내가 마땅한 이유로 마법에 걸렸다고 말하는 자가 있다면, 미코미코나 공주 마님께서 내게 허락해 주시는 한, 나는 그가 거짓말쟁이라 선언하고, 일대일 결투를 청하며 도전하노라.\""]],[["The newly arrived travellers were amazed at the words of Don Quixote; but the landlord removed their surprise by telling them who he was, and not to mind him as he was out of his senses. They then asked the landlord if by any chance a youth of about fifteen years of age had come to that inn, one dressed like a muleteer, and of such and such an appearance, describing that of Dona Clara’s lover. The landlord replied that there were so many people in the inn he had not noticed the person they were inquiring for; but one of them observing the coach in which the Judge had come, said, “He is here no doubt, for this is the coach he is following: let one of us stay at the gate, and the rest go in to look for him; or indeed it would be as well if one of us went round the inn, lest he should escape over the wall of the yard.” “So be it,” said another; and while two of them went in, one remained at the gate and the other made the circuit of the inn; observing all which, the landlord was unable to conjecture for what reason they were taking all these precautions, though he understood they were looking for the youth whose description they had given him.","새로 도착한 길손들은 돈키호테의 말에 어리둥절했으나, 여관 주인이 그가 누구인지 일러 주며 제정신이 아니니 신경 쓰지 말라 하여 그들의 놀라움을 풀어 주었다. 그러자 그들은 여관 주인에게 혹시 열다섯 살쯤 된 젊은이가 이 여관에 들지 않았는지, 노새몰이꾼 차림에 이러이러한 모습을 한 자가 오지 않았는지 물으며 도냐 클라라의 연인의 생김새를 일러 주었다. 여관 주인은 여관에 사람이 하도 많아 그들이 찾는 자를 미처 눈여겨보지 못했노라 대답했다. 그런데 그들 가운데 하나가 판사가 타고 온 마차를 알아보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"그자는 틀림없이 여기 있소. 이게 바로 그가 따라온 마차니 말이오. 우리 중 하나는 문간에 남고 나머지는 안으로 들어가 그를 찾읍시다. 아니, 그자가 마당 담을 넘어 달아나지 못하게 누군가가 여관을 한 바퀴 도는 것이 좋겠소.\" \"그럽시다.\" 다른 자가 말했다. 그리하여 둘은 안으로 들어가고, 하나는 문간에 남고 또 하나는 여관 둘레를 돌았는데, 이 모든 것을 지켜보던 여관 주인은 그들이 일러 준 생김새의 젊은이를 찾고 있다는 것은 알았으나, 무슨 까닭에 이토록 온갖 조심을 다하는지는 도무지 짐작할 수가 없었다."]],[["It was by this time broad daylight; and for that reason, as well as in consequence of the noise Don Quixote had made, everybody was awake and up, but particularly Dona Clara and Dorothea; for they had been able to sleep but badly that night, the one from agitation at having her lover so near her, the other from curiosity to see him. Don Quixote, when he saw that not one of the four travellers took any notice of him or replied to his challenge, was furious and ready to die with indignation and wrath; and if he could have found in the ordinances of chivalry that it was lawful for a knight-errant to undertake or engage in another enterprise, when he had plighted his word and faith not to involve himself in any until he had made an end of the one to which he was pledged, he would have attacked the whole of them, and would have made them return an answer in spite of themselves. But considering that it would not become him, nor be right, to begin any new emprise until he had established Micomicona in her kingdom, he was constrained to hold his peace and wait quietly to see what would be the upshot of the proceedings of those same travellers; one of whom found the youth they were seeking lying asleep by the side of a muleteer, without a thought of anyone coming in search of him, much less finding him.","이 무렵 날은 훤히 밝았다. 그런 까닭에, 또한 돈키호테가 일으킨 소동 탓에 모두가 잠에서 깨어 일어나 있었는데, 특히 도냐 클라라와 도로테아가 그러했으니, 둘은 그날 밤 잠을 제대로 이루지 못했던 것이다. 하나는 연인이 그토록 가까이 있다는 데서 오는 설렘 때문이었고, 다른 하나는 그를 보고 싶은 호기심 때문이었다. 돈키호테는 네 명의 길손 가운데 어느 누구도 자기를 거들떠보지 않고 도전에 응하지도 않는 것을 보자 분통이 터져, 분노와 노여움으로 죽을 지경이 되었다. 만일 그가 기사도의 법규 가운데, 편력 기사가 어떤 임무에 자신의 말과 신의를 걸어 그 일을 끝마치기 전에는 다른 어떤 일에도 얽혀들지 않겠다고 맹세한 처지에서도 또 다른 일을 떠맡거나 거기에 뛰어드는 것이 마땅하다는 조항을 찾아낼 수 있었더라면, 그는 그들 전부를 공격하여 그들로 하여금 본의 아니게라도 대답하게 만들었으리라. 그러나 미코미코나를 그 왕국에 복위시키기 전에는 새로운 일을 시작하는 것이 자신에게 어울리지도, 옳지도 않다는 데 생각이 미치자, 그는 입을 다물고 가만히 기다리며 그 길손들의 일이 어찌 결말이 날지 지켜볼 수밖에 없었다. 그들 가운데 하나가 찾던 젊은이를 발견했는데, 그는 누가 자기를 찾으러 오리라고는, 더더구나 찾아내리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못한 채 노새몰이꾼 곁에서 곤히 잠들어 있었다."]],[["The man laid hold of him by the arm, saying, “It becomes you well indeed, Señor Don Luis, to be in the dress you wear, and well the bed in which I find you agrees with the luxury in which your mother reared you.”","그 사내는 그의 팔을 붙잡고 말했다. \"돈 루이스 도련님, 참으로 잘 어울리는 차림새를 하고 계시는군요. 어머님께서 호사스럽게 길러 주신 분이 누우신 잠자리치고는 어찌나 걸맞은지요.\""]],[["The youth rubbed his sleepy eyes and stared for a while at him who held him, but presently recognised him as one of his father’s servants, at which he was so taken aback that for some time he could not find or utter a word; while the servant went on to say, “There is nothing for it now, Señor Don Luis, but to submit quietly and return home, unless it is your wish that my lord, your father, should take his departure for the other world, for nothing else can be the consequence of the grief he is in at your absence.”","젊은이는 졸린 눈을 비비며 자기를 붙든 자를 한참 바라보다가, 이내 그가 아버지의 하인 가운데 하나임을 알아보고는 어찌나 놀랐던지 한동안 한마디도 찾아 입에 올리지 못했다. 그러는 사이 하인은 말을 이었다. \"돈 루이스 도련님, 이제는 별수 없으니 순순히 따라 집으로 돌아가시지요. 도련님의 부재로 상심에 잠기신 아버님께서 저세상으로 떠나시기를 바라지 않으신다면 말입니다. 그러지 않으시면 그것 말고 다른 결말은 있을 수 없으니까요.\""]],[["“But how did my father know that I had gone this road and in this dress?”","\"한데 내가 이 길로, 이런 차림으로 떠난 줄을 아버지께서 어찌 아셨단 말이냐?\""],["said Don Luis.","돈 루이스가 말했다."]],[["“It was a student to whom you confided your intentions,” answered the servant, “that disclosed them, touched with pity at the distress he saw your father suffer on missing you; he therefore despatched four of his servants in quest of you, and here we all are at your service, better pleased than you can imagine that we shall return so soon and be able to restore you to those eyes that so yearn for you.”","\"도련님이 속내를 털어놓으셨던 그 학생입니다.\" 하인이 대답했다. \"그자가 도련님을 잃고 괴로워하시는 아버님의 모습에 측은한 마음이 들어 다 일러바쳤지요. 그래서 아버님께서 하인 넷을 도련님을 찾으라 보내셨으니, 이제 저희 모두 도련님을 모시려 여기 와 있습니다. 이토록 빨리 돌아가 도련님을 그토록 애타게 기다리는 그 눈앞에 다시 모실 수 있게 되었으니, 상상도 못 하실 만큼 기쁘기 그지없습니다.\""]],[["“That shall be as I please, or as heaven orders,”","\"그건 내 뜻에 달렸거나, 하늘이 정하는 대로 될 일이다.\""],["returned Don Luis.","돈 루이스가 대꾸했다."]],[["“What can you please or heaven order,” said the other, “except to agree to go back?","\"돌아가는 데 동의하시는 것 말고 도련님이 무엇을 뜻하실 수 있으며, 하늘인들 무엇을 정할 수 있겠습니까?\""],["Anything else is impossible.”","하인이 말했다. \"그 밖의 일은 있을 수 없습니다.\""]],[["All this conversation between the two was overheard by the muleteer at whose side Don Luis lay, and rising, he went to report what had taken place to Don Fernando, Cardenio, and the others, who had by this time dressed themselves; and told them how the man had addressed the youth as “Don,”","돈 루이스가 곁에 누워 있던 노새몰이꾼이 두 사람의 이 모든 대화를 엿들었는데, 그는 일어나 그동안 옷을 차려입은 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오와 다른 이들에게 가서 무슨 일이 있었는지 알렸다."],["and what words had passed, and how he wanted him to return to his father, which the youth was unwilling to do.","그 사내가 젊은이를 \"돈\"이라 부른 것이며, 오간 말들이며, 사내가 젊은이를 아버지에게 돌려보내려 하나 젊은이는 그러기를 꺼린다는 것을 일러 주었다."],["With this, and what they already knew of the rare voice that heaven had bestowed upon him, they all felt very anxious to know more particularly who he was, and even to help him if it was attempted to employ force against him; so they hastened to where he was still talking and arguing with his servant.","이 이야기에다, 하늘이 그에게 내려 준 그 보기 드문 목소리에 관해 이미 알고 있던 터라, 그들 모두 그가 누구인지 좀 더 자세히 알고 싶은 마음이 간절해졌고, 만일 그에게 무력을 쓰려 한다면 그를 도와주고 싶기까지 했다. 그리하여 그들은 그가 아직 하인과 이야기를 나누며 옥신각신하고 있는 곳으로 서둘러 갔다."],["Dorothea at this instant came out of her room, followed by Dona Clara all in a tremor; and calling Cardenio aside, she told him in a few words the story of the musician and Dona Clara, and he at the same time told her what had happened, how his father’s servants had come in search of him; but in telling her so, he did not speak low enough but that Dona Clara heard what he said, at which she was so much agitated that had not Dorothea hastened to support her she would have fallen to the ground.","바로 그때 도로테아가 자기 방에서 나왔고, 온몸을 떠는 도냐 클라라가 그 뒤를 따랐다. 도로테아는 카르데니오를 따로 불러 몇 마디로 그 악사와 도냐 클라라의 사연을 들려주었고, 카르데니오 역시 그녀에게 무슨 일이 있었는지, 그의 아버지의 하인들이 그를 찾으러 왔다는 것을 일러 주었는데, 그렇게 말하면서 목소리를 충분히 낮추지 못한 탓에 도냐 클라라가 그 말을 듣고 말았다."],["Cardenio then bade Dorothea return to her room, as he would endeavour to make the whole matter right, and they did as he desired.","그녀가 어찌나 동요했던지 도로테아가 얼른 부축하지 않았더라면 땅바닥에 쓰러질 뻔했다."],["All the four who had come in quest of Don Luis had now come into the inn and surrounded him, urging him to return and console his father at once and without a moment’s delay.","그러자 카르데니오는 도로테아에게 자기가 이 모든 일을 잘 수습해 보겠노라며 방으로 돌아가라 일렀고, 그들은 그의 말대로 했다."],["He replied that he could not do so on any account until he had concluded some business in which his life, honour, and heart were at stake.","돈 루이스를 찾으러 온 네 사람은 이제 모두 여관 안으로 들어와 그를 에워싸고, 당장 한시도 지체 없이 돌아가 아버지를 위로해 드리라고 다그쳤다."],["The servants pressed him, saying that most certainly they would not return without him, and that they would take him away whether he liked it or not.","그는 자신의 목숨과 명예와 마음이 걸린 어떤 일을 매듭짓기 전에는 무슨 일이 있어도 그럴 수 없다고 대답했다. 하인들은 그를 두고는 결단코 돌아가지 않을 것이며, 그가 좋든 싫든 데려가겠다며 그를 몰아세웠다."]],[["“You shall not do that,”","\"그렇게는 못 한다.\" 돈 루이스가 대꾸했다."],["replied Don Luis, “unless you take me dead; though however you take me, it will be without life.”","\"나를 죽여서 데려간다면 모를까. 어떤 식으로 데려가든 살아 있는 나를 데려가지는 못할 것이다.\""]],[["By this time most of those in the inn had been attracted by the dispute, but particularly Cardenio, Don Fernando, his companions, the Judge, the curate, the barber, and Don Quixote; for he now considered there was no necessity for mounting guard over the castle any longer.","이 무렵에는 여관 안에 있던 대부분의 사람이 이 다툼에 이끌려 모여들었는데, 특히 카르데니오와 돈 페르난도와 그의 일행, 판사, 신부, 이발사, 그리고 돈키호테가 그러했다. 돈키호테는 이제 더는 성을 지키고 망볼 필요가 없다고 여겼던 것이다."],["Cardenio being already acquainted with the young man’s story, asked the men who wanted to take him away, what object they had in seeking to carry off this youth against his will.","카르데니오는 이미 젊은이의 사연을 알고 있던 터라, 그를 데려가려는 사내들에게 어찌하여 이 젊은이를 본인의 뜻을 거슬러 끌고 가려 하느냐고 물었다."]],[["“Our object,”","\"저희 목적은,\""],["said one of the four, “is to save the life of his father, who is in danger of losing it through this gentleman’s disappearance.”","네 사람 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"이 도련님의 실종으로 목숨을 잃을 지경에 이르신 그분의 아버님 목숨을 구하는 것입니다.\""]],[["Upon this Don Luis exclaimed, “There is no need to make my affairs public here; I am free, and I will return if I please; and if not, none of you shall compel me.”","이 말에 돈 루이스가 소리쳤다. \"여기서 내 일을 떠벌릴 필요는 없다. 나는 자유로운 몸이니, 내가 원하면 돌아갈 것이고, 원치 않으면 너희 가운데 누구도 나를 억지로 끌고 가지 못한다.\""]],[["“Reason will compel your worship,”","\"이치가 도련님을 끌고 갈 것입니다.\""],["said the man, “and if it has no power over you, it has power over us, to make us do what we came for, and what it is our duty to do.”","사내가 말했다. \"이치가 도련님께는 힘을 못 쓴다 해도 저희에게는 힘을 쓸 테니, 저희가 온 까닭, 저희가 마땅히 해야 할 일을 하게 만들 것입니다.\""]],[["“Let us hear what the whole affair is about,”","\"대체 이게 다 무슨 일인지 들어 봅시다.\""],["said the Judge at this; but the man, who knew him as a neighbour of theirs, replied, “Do you not know this gentleman, Señor Judge?","이때 판사가 말했다. 그러자 그를 이웃으로 알아본 사내가 대답했다."],["He is the son of your neighbour, who has run away from his father’s house in a dress so unbecoming his rank, as your worship may perceive.”","\"판사님, 이분을 모르시겠습니까? 댁의 이웃 어른의 아드님이신데, 보시다시피 신분에 도무지 어울리지 않는 차림으로 아버님 댁에서 도망쳐 나온 것입니다.\""]],[["The judge on this looked at him more carefully and recognised him, and embracing him said, “What folly is this, Señor Don Luis, or what can have been the cause that could have induced you to come here in this way, and in this dress, which so ill becomes your condition?”","이 말에 판사는 그를 더 유심히 살펴 알아보고는 그를 끌어안으며 말했다. \"돈 루이스, 이게 대체 무슨 어리석은 짓인가, 무슨 까닭이 있어 이런 모양으로, 자네 처지에 그토록 어울리지 않는 이런 차림으로 여기까지 오게 되었단 말인가?\""]],[["Tears came into the eyes of the young man, and he was unable to utter a word in reply to the Judge, who told the four servants not to be uneasy, for all would be satisfactorily settled; and then taking Don Luis by the hand, he drew him aside and asked the reason of his having come there.","젊은이는 눈에 눈물이 차올라 판사의 물음에 한마디도 대꾸하지 못했다. 판사는 네 하인에게 마음 놓으라며, 모든 일이 흡족하게 매듭지어질 것이라 일렀다. 그러고는 돈 루이스의 손을 잡고 한쪽으로 데려가 그가 여기까지 온 까닭을 물었다."]],[["But while he was questioning him they heard a loud outcry at the gate of the inn, the cause of which was that two of the guests who had passed the night there, seeing everybody busy about finding out what it was the four men wanted, had conceived the idea of going off without paying what they owed; but the landlord, who minded his own affairs more than other people’s, caught them going out of the gate and demanded his reckoning, abusing them for their dishonesty with such language that he drove them to reply with their fists, and so they began to lay on him in such a style that the poor man was forced to cry out, and call for help. The landlady and her daughter could see no one more free to give aid than Don Quixote, and to him the daughter said, “Sir knight, by the virtue God has given you, help my poor father, for two wicked men are beating him to a mummy.”","그런데 그가 그렇게 묻고 있는 사이, 여관 문간에서 요란한 고함 소리가 들려왔다. 그 까닭인즉, 그곳에서 밤을 보낸 손님 둘이 모두가 네 사내가 무엇을 원하는지 알아내느라 분주한 틈을 타, 외상값을 치르지 않고 슬그머니 떠날 궁리를 했던 것이다. 그러나 남의 일보다 제 일을 더 챙기는 여관 주인이 문간을 나서는 그들을 붙잡아 셈을 치르라 요구하며 그들의 파렴치함을 어찌나 험한 말로 욕했던지, 그들이 주먹으로 응수하게 만들었고, 그리하여 그들은 가엾은 주인이 비명을 지르며 도움을 청하지 않을 수 없을 만큼 호되게 두들겨 패기 시작했다. 여관 안주인과 그 딸은 돈키호테말고는 도움을 줄 만큼 한가한 사람이 아무도 없다고 보았기에, 딸이 그에게 말했다. \"기사 나리, 하느님께서 나리께 주신 그 힘으로 부디 가엾은 제 아버지를 도와주세요. 못된 사내 둘이 아버지를 떡이 되도록 두들겨 패고 있어요.\""]],[["To which Don Quixote very deliberately and phlegmatically replied, “Fair damsel, at the present moment your request is inopportune, for I am debarred from involving myself in any adventure until I have brought to a happy conclusion one to which my word has pledged me; but that which I can do for you is what I will now mention: run and tell your father to stand his ground as well as he can in this battle, and on no account to allow himself to be vanquished, while I go and request permission of the Princess Micomicona to enable me to succour him in his distress; and if she grants it, rest assured I will relieve him from it.”","이에 돈키호테는 자못 차분하고 태연하게 대꾸했다. \"고운 아가씨, 지금 이 순간 그대의 청은 때에 맞지 않소. 나는 내 말이 걸린 어떤 일을 행복한 결말로 이끌기 전에는 어떤 모험에도 얽혀드는 것이 금지되어 있기 때문이오. 다만 그대를 위해 내가 할 수 있는 것은 이제 말해 줄 터이니, 어서 달려가 그대의 아버지에게 이 싸움에서 힘닿는 대로 버티며 결코 패배를 당하지 말라 이르시오. 그동안 나는 미코미코나 공주께 가서 그분의 곤경에서 그를 구할 수 있도록 허락을 청하겠소. 만일 공주께서 허락하신다면, 내 반드시 그를 그 곤경에서 건져 내리라 믿어도 좋소.\""]],[["“Sinner that I am,”","\"이 죄 많은 몸이여!\""],["exclaimed Maritornes, who stood by; “before you have got your permission my master will be in the other world.”","곁에 서 있던 마리토르네스가 소리쳤다. \"나리가 그 허락을 얻기도 전에 우리 주인은 저세상에 가 있을 거예요.\""]],[["“Give me leave, señora, to obtain the permission I speak of,”","\"부인, 내가 말한 그 허락을 얻을 수 있게 해 주시오.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다."],["returned Don Quixote; “and if I get it, it will matter very little if he is in the other world; for I will rescue him thence in spite of all the same world can do; or at any rate I will give you such a revenge over those who shall have sent him there that you will be more than moderately satisfied;”","\"내가 허락을 얻기만 한다면, 그가 저세상에 가 있다 한들 별 상관이 없소. 온 세상이 막아선다 해도 내가 그를 거기서 구해 낼 것이며, 적어도 그를 그리로 보낸 자들에게 그대가 어지간히는 만족하고도 남을 만큼 톡톡히 원수를 갚아 주겠소.\""],["and without saying anything more he went and knelt before Dorothea, requesting her Highness in knightly and errant phrase to be pleased to grant him permission to aid and succour the castellan of that castle, who now stood in grievous jeopardy.","그러고는 더 말하지 않고 도로테아 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 편력 기사다운 격식 갖춘 말로 공주 마님께, 지금 참혹한 위기에 처한 그 성의 성주를 돕고 구할 수 있도록 허락해 주시기를 청했다."],["The princess granted it graciously, and he at once, bracing his buckler on his arm and drawing his sword, hastened to the inn-gate, where the two guests were still handling the landlord roughly; but as soon as he reached the spot he stopped short and stood still, though Maritornes and the landlady asked him why he hesitated to help their master and husband.","공주는 흔쾌히 허락해 주었고, 그는 즉시 방패를 팔에 끼고 칼을 빼어 들고는 여관 문간으로 달려갔는데, 그곳에서는 여전히 두 손님이 여관 주인을 거칠게 다루고 있었다. 그러나 그는 그 자리에 이르자마자 우뚝 멈춰 꼼짝 않고 서 있었으니, 마리토르네스와 안주인이 어찌하여 주인이자 남편을 돕기를 머뭇거리느냐고 물었다."]],[["“I hesitate,” said Don Quixote, “because it is not lawful for me to draw sword against persons of squirely condition; but call my squire Sancho to me; for this defence and vengeance are his affair and business.”","\"내가 머뭇거리는 것은,\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"종자 신분의 자들을 상대로 칼을 뽑는 것이 나에게는 허락되지 않은 일이기 때문이오. 그러니 내 종자 산초를 불러 주시오. 이 방어와 복수는 그의 소관이요 그가 할 일이니 말이오.\""]],[["Thus matters stood at the inn-gate, where there was a very lively exchange of fisticuffs and punches, to the sore damage of the landlord and to the wrath of Maritornes, the landlady, and her daughter, who were furious when they saw the pusillanimity of Don Quixote, and the hard treatment their master, husband and father was undergoing. But let us leave him there; for he will surely find some one to help him, and if not, let him suffer and hold his tongue who attempts more than his strength allows him to do; and let us go back fifty paces to see what Don Luis said in reply to the Judge whom we left questioning him privately as to his reasons for coming on foot and so meanly dressed.","여관 문간의 사정은 이러했으니, 그곳에서는 주먹질과 매질이 자못 활기차게 오가 여관 주인은 심하게 상하고, 마리토르네스와 안주인과 그 딸은 노여워했다. 그들은 돈키호테의 비겁함과, 주인이자 남편이자 아버지인 자가 당하는 모진 꼴을 보고 분통이 터졌던 것이다. 그러나 그는 거기 내버려 두자. 그를 도와줄 누군가가 분명 나타날 터이고, 그렇지 않다면 제 힘이 닿지 않는 일을 무리하게 떠맡으려는 자는 고통을 겪으며 입을 다물고 있어야 하리라. 우리는 쉰 걸음쯤 뒤로 돌아가, 우리가 두고 온, 판사가 따로 그를 데려가 무슨 까닭으로 걸어서 그토록 초라한 차림으로 왔느냐고 캐묻던 곳에서 돈 루이스가 무어라 대답했는지 살펴보기로 하자."]],[["To which the youth, pressing his hand in a way that showed his heart was troubled by some great sorrow, and shedding a flood of tears, made answer:","이에 젊은이는, 어떤 크나큰 슬픔으로 마음이 괴롭다는 것을 드러내듯 판사의 손을 꼭 쥐고 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 대답했다."]],[["“Señor, I have no more to tell you than that from the moment when, through heaven’s will and our being near neighbours, I first saw Dona Clara, your daughter and my lady, from that instant I made her the mistress of my will, and if yours, my true lord and father, offers no impediment, this very day she shall become my wife.","\"나리, 제가 더 드릴 말씀은 없습니다. 다만 하늘의 뜻으로, 또 저희가 가까운 이웃인 덕분에, 제가 나리의 따님이자 제 연인이신 도냐 클라라를 처음 본 그 순간부터 저는 그분을 제 마음의 주인으로 삼았으며, 진정한 제 어른이자 아버지이신 나리께서 가로막지만 않으신다면, 바로 오늘 그분을 제 아내로 맞고자 합니다."],["For her I left my father’s house, and for her I assumed this disguise, to follow her whithersoever she may go, as the arrow seeks its mark or the sailor the pole-star.","그분을 위해 저는 아버지의 집을 떠났고, 그분을 위해 이 변장을 했으니, 화살이 과녁을 좇고 뱃사람이 북극성을 좇듯 그분이 어디로 가시든 따르려 함이었습니다."],["She knows nothing more of my passion than what she may have learned from having sometimes seen from a distance that my eyes were filled with tears.","그분은 제 열정에 대해, 이따금 멀리서 제 눈에 눈물이 가득 고인 것을 보아 알아챘을 법한 것 말고는 아무것도 모르십니다. 나리께서는 이미 제 부모의 재산과 고귀한 가문, 그리고 제가 그 외아들이라는 것을 알고 계십니다."],["You know already, señor, the wealth and noble birth of my parents, and that I am their sole heir; if this be a sufficient inducement for you to venture to make me completely happy, accept me at once as your son; for if my father, influenced by other objects of his own, should disapprove of this happiness I have sought for myself, time has more power to alter and change things, than human will.”","만일 이것이 나리께서 저를 온전히 행복하게 해 주시려 마음먹으실 만한 충분한 이유가 된다면, 부디 저를 지금 당장 사위로 받아 주십시오. 설령 제 아버지께서 당신만의 다른 뜻에 이끌려 제가 스스로 구한 이 행복을 못마땅하게 여기신다 해도, 세월은 인간의 의지보다 더 큰 힘으로 일을 뒤바꾸고 변화시키는 법이니까요.\""]],[["With this the love-smitten youth was silent, while the Judge, after hearing him, was astonished, perplexed, and surprised, as well at the manner and intelligence with which Don Luis had confessed the secret of his heart, as at the position in which he found himself, not knowing what course to take in a matter so sudden and unexpected.","이 말과 함께 사랑에 빠진 젊은이는 입을 다물었고, 그 말을 들은 판사는 돈 루이스가 제 마음속 비밀을 털어놓은 그 태도와 영민함에도, 또 자신이 처하게 된 그 입장에도 깜짝 놀라며 어리둥절하고 당혹스러워했으니, 이토록 갑작스럽고 뜻밖의 일에 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 알 수 없었던 것이다."],["All the answer, therefore, he gave him was to bid him to make his mind easy for the present, and arrange with his servants not to take him back that day, so that there might be time to consider what was best for all parties.","그리하여 그가 그에게 해 준 대답이라고는, 지금으로서는 마음을 편히 가지고, 하인들과 잘 의논하여 그날은 자기를 데려가지 않도록 해서, 모두에게 무엇이 가장 좋을지 헤아릴 여유를 두라는 것뿐이었다."],["Don Luis kissed his hands by force, nay, bathed them with his tears, in a way that would have touched a heart of marble, not to say that of the Judge, who, as a shrewd man, had already perceived how advantageous the marriage would be to his daughter; though, were it possible, he would have preferred that it should be brought about with the consent of the father of Don Luis, who he knew looked for a title for his son.","돈 루이스는 억지로 그의 손에 입을 맞추었을 뿐 아니라, 대리석 같은 마음이라도 — 영민한 사람으로서 이 혼인이 제 딸에게 얼마나 이로운지 이미 알아챈 판사의 마음은 말할 것도 없고 — 감동시킬 만큼 눈물로 그 손을 적셨다. 다만 가능하기만 하다면, 판사는 아들에게 작위를 바라고 있음을 그가 아는 돈 루이스의 아버지의 동의를 얻어 이 혼인이 이루어지기를 더 바랐다."]],[["The guests had by this time made peace with the landlord, for, by persuasion and Don Quixote’s fair words more than by threats, they had paid him what he demanded, and the servants of Don Luis were waiting for the end of the conversation with the Judge and their master’s decision, when the devil, who never sleeps, contrived that the barber, from whom Don Quixote had taken Mambrino’s helmet, and Sancho Panza the trappings of his ass in exchange for those of his own, should at this instant enter the inn; which said barber, as he led his ass to the stable, observed Sancho Panza engaged in repairing something or other belonging to the pack-saddle; and the moment he saw it he knew it, and made bold to attack Sancho, exclaiming, “Ho, sir thief, I have caught you! hand over my basin and my pack-saddle, and all my trappings that you robbed me of.”","그 무렵 손님들은 이미 여관 주인과 화해한 뒤였으니, 협박보다는 설득과 돈키호테의 그럴듯한 말솜씨 덕분에 그가 요구한 셈을 치렀던 것이다. 돈 루이스의 하인들은 판사와의 대화가 끝나고 주인의 결정이 내려지기를 기다리고 있었는데, 결코 잠드는 법이 없는 악마가 일을 꾸며, 돈키호테가 맘브리노의 투구를 빼앗고 산초 판사가 제 당나귀 마구와 맞바꿔 마구를 가져왔던 그 이발사가 바로 이 순간 여관으로 들어서게 만들었다. 그 이발사는 당나귀를 마구간으로 끌고 가다가, 산초 판사가 길마에 딸린 무언가를 손보고 있는 것을 보았는데, 그것을 보는 순간 한눈에 알아보고는 대담하게 산초에게 달려들며 소리쳤다. \"어이, 이 도둑놈아, 내가 너를 잡았다! 네놈이 나한테서 훔쳐 간 내 대야와 길마와 내 마구를 죄다 내놓아라.\""]],[["Sancho, finding himself so unexpectedly assailed, and hearing the abuse poured upon him, seized the pack-saddle with one hand, and with the other gave the barber a cuff that bathed his teeth in blood.","산초는 이렇게 느닷없이 공격을 당하고 자기에게 퍼붓는 욕설을 듣자, 한 손으로는 길마를 움켜쥐고 다른 손으로는 이발사에게 한 대 갈겨 그 이를 피로 적셨다."],["The barber, however, was not so ready to relinquish the prize he had made in the pack-saddle; on the contrary, he raised such an outcry that everyone in the inn came running to know what the noise and quarrel meant.","그러나 이발사는 길마라는 전리품을 그리 쉽사리 내줄 생각이 없었으니, 오히려 어찌나 큰 소리로 고함을 질렀던지 여관 안의 모두가 무슨 소란이며 다툼인가 알아보려 달려왔다."],["“Here, in the name of the king and justice!”","\"여기, 국왕과 정의의 이름으로!\""],["he cried, “this thief and highwayman wants to kill me for trying to recover my property.”","그가 외쳤다. \"이 도둑놈, 노상강도가 제 물건을 되찾으려는 나를 죽이려 합니다!\""]],[["“You lie,”","\"거짓말 마라.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “I am no highwayman; it was in fair war my master Don Quixote won these spoils.”","\"나는 노상강도가 아니다. 우리 주인 돈키호테께서 정당한 싸움으로 이 전리품을 얻으신 것이다.\""]],[["Don Quixote was standing by at the time, highly pleased to see his squire’s stoutness, both offensive and defensive, and from that time forth he reckoned him a man of mettle, and in his heart resolved to dub him a knight on the first opportunity that presented itself, feeling sure that the order of chivalry would be fittingly bestowed upon him.","마침 곁에 서 있던 돈키호테는 제 종자가 공격에서나 방어에서나 야무진 것을 보고 무척 흐뭇해하며, 그때부터 그를 기개 있는 사내로 여기고는, 기회가 오는 대로 첫 번째 기회에 그를 기사로 서임하리라 마음속으로 작정했으니, 기사도의 위계가 그에게 합당하게 내려지리라 확신했던 것이다."]],[["In the course of the altercation, among other things the barber said, “Gentlemen, this pack-saddle is mine as surely as I owe God a death, and I know it as well as if I had given birth to it, and here is my ass in the stable who will not let me lie; only try it, and if it does not fit him like a glove, call me a rascal; and what is more, the same day I was robbed of this, they robbed me likewise of a new brass basin, never yet handselled, that would fetch a crown any day.”","옥신각신하는 가운데 이발사는 다른 말들과 함께 이렇게 말했다. \"여러분, 제가 하느님께 죽음을 빚지고 있는 것만큼이나 이 길마는 분명 제 것이며, 제가 직접 낳기라도 한 듯 훤히 압니다. 여기 마구간에 제 당나귀가 있으니 그놈이 거짓이 아님을 증명해 줄 겁니다. 한번 채워 보시지요, 만일 장갑처럼 꼭 맞지 않거든 저를 악당이라 부르십시오. 게다가 이것을 도둑맞은 바로 그날, 그놈들은 제 새 놋쇠 대야도 함께 훔쳐 갔는데, 아직 한 번도 써 본 적 없는, 어느 날이든 1크라운은 족히 받을 만한 물건이었습니다.\""]],[["At this Don Quixote could not keep himself from answering; and interposing between the two, and separating them, he placed the pack-saddle on the ground, to lie there in sight until the truth was established, and said, “Your worships may perceive clearly and plainly the error under which this worthy squire lies when he calls a basin which was, is, and shall be the helmet of Mambrino which I won from him in air war, and made myself master of by legitimate and lawful possession.","이 말에 돈키호테는 대꾸하지 않을 수 없어, 두 사람 사이에 끼어들어 그들을 떼어 놓고는 진실이 가려질 때까지 모두가 볼 수 있도록 길마를 땅에 내려놓으며 말했다. \"여러분께서는 이 훌륭한 종자가 빠져 있는 잘못을 똑똑히, 그리고 분명히 아실 수 있을 것이오."],["With the pack-saddle I do not concern myself; but I may tell you on that head that my squire Sancho asked my permission to strip off the caparison of this vanquished poltroon’s steed, and with it adorn his own; I allowed him, and he took it; and as to its having been changed from a caparison into a pack-saddle, I can give no explanation except the usual one, that such transformations will take place in adventures of chivalry.","그는 내가 정당한 싸움에서 그에게서 빼앗아 적법하고 합법적인 소유로 내 것이 된, 과거에도 맘브리노의 투구였고 지금도 그러하며 앞으로도 그러할 것을 대야라고 부르고 있소. 길마에 대해서는 내가 관여할 바 아니나, 다만 그 점에 관해 말할 수 있는 것은, 내 종자 산초가 이 패배한 겁쟁이의 말에서 마구를 벗겨 제 것을 꾸미게 해 달라고 내게 청하기에 내가 허락했고 그가 그것을 가져갔다는 사실이오."],["To confirm all which, run, Sancho my son, and fetch hither the helmet which this good fellow calls a basin.”","그것이 마구에서 길마로 바뀐 데 대해서는, 기사도의 모험에서는 그런 변신이 일어나기 마련이라는 흔한 설명 말고는 달리 드릴 말씀이 없소. 이 모든 것을 확인하기 위해, 산초 내 아들아, 어서 달려가 이 선량한 자가 대야라 부르는 그 투구를 이리로 가져오너라.\""]],[["“Egad, master,”","\"이런, 나리.\""],["said Sancho, “if we have no other proof of our case than what your worship puts forward, Mambrino’s helmet is just as much a basin as this good fellow’s caparison is a pack-saddle.”","산초가 말했다. \"우리 처지를 증명할 거리가 나리께서 내놓으시는 것밖에 없다면, 맘브리노의 투구는 이 선량한 자의 마구가 길마인 것만큼이나 영락없이 대야입니다요.\""]],[["“Do as I bid thee,”","\"시키는 대로 하여라.\""],["said Don Quixote; “it cannot be that everything in this castle goes by enchantment.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"이 성의 모든 것이 마법으로 돌아간다고는 할 수 없을 테니 말이다.\""]],[["Sancho hastened to where the basin was, and brought it back with him, and when Don Quixote saw it, he took hold of it and said:","산초는 대야가 있는 곳으로 달려가 그것을 가지고 돌아왔고, 돈키호테는 그것을 보자 손에 들고 말했다."]],[["“Your worships may see with what a face this squire can assert that this is a basin and not the helmet I told you of; and I swear by the order of chivalry I profess, that this helmet is the identical one I took from him, without anything added to or taken from it.”","\"여러분께서는 이 종자가 무슨 낯짝으로 이것이 대야이지 내가 말한 그 투구가 아니라고 우길 수 있는지 보실 것이오. 그리고 내가 받든 기사도의 위계를 걸고 맹세하건대, 이 투구는 내가 그에게서 빼앗은 바로 그것으로, 거기에 무엇 하나 더하거나 덜어 낸 것이 없소.\""]],[["“There is no doubt of that,” said Sancho, “for from the time my master won it until now he has only fought one battle in it, when he let loose those unlucky men in chains; and if had not been for this basin-helmet he would not have come off over well that time, for there was plenty of stone-throwing in that affair.”","\"그건 의심할 여지가 없지요.\" 산초가 말했다. \"주인님이 그것을 얻으신 때부터 지금까지 그것을 쓰고 싸우신 전투라고는 단 한 번뿐이니, 그 불운한 쇠사슬에 묶인 자들을 풀어 주셨을 때 말입니다요. 만일 이 대야 투구가 아니었다면 그때 주인님은 그리 무사히 빠져나오지 못하셨을 겁니다요. 그 일에서는 돌팔매질이 어찌나 쏟아졌는지 모르니까요.\""]],[["# CHAPTER XLV. IN WHICH THE DOUBTFUL QUESTION OF MAMBRINO’S HELMET AND THE PACK-SADDLE IS FINALLY SETTLED, WITH OTHER ADVENTURES THAT OCCURRED IN TRUTH AND EARNEST","# CHAPTER XLV."]],[["“What do you think now, gentlemen,”","이발사가 말했다. \"여러분, 이제 어떻게들 생각하십니까?"],["said the barber, “of what these gentles say, when they want to make out that this is a helmet?”","이 양반들이 이것을 투구라고 우기려 드는 이 일을 두고 말입니다.\""]],[["“And whoever says the contrary,”","\"누구든 그 반대를 말하는 자가 있다면,\""],["said Don Quixote, “I will let him know he lies if he is a knight, and if he is a squire that he lies again a thousand times.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그가 기사라면 거짓을 말하고 있음을 알려 주겠고, 종자라면 천 번이고 거짓을 말하고 있음을 알려 주겠노라.\""]],[["Our own barber, who was present at all this, and understood Don Quixote’s humour so thoroughly, took it into his head to back up his delusion and carry on the joke for the general amusement; so addressing the other barber he said:","이 모든 일에 함께 있으면서 돈키호테의 별난 기질을 속속들이 꿰뚫고 있던 우리 편 이발사는, 모두의 흥을 돋우기 위해 그의 망상에 장단을 맞추어 이 익살을 계속 끌고 가야겠다는 생각이 들었다. 그래서 다른 이발사를 향해 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“Señor barber, or whatever you are, you must know that I belong to your profession too, and have had a licence to practise for more than twenty years, and I know the implements of the barber craft, every one of them, perfectly well; and I was likewise a soldier for some time in the days of my youth, and I know also what a helmet is, and a morion, and a headpiece with a visor, and other things pertaining to soldiering, I meant to say to soldiers’ arms; and I say-saving better opinions and always with submission to sounder judgments--that this piece we have now before us, which this worthy gentleman has in his hands, not only is no barber’s basin, but is as far from being one as white is from black, and truth from falsehood; I say, moreover, that this, although it is a helmet, is not a complete helmet.”","\"이발사 양반, 아니 댁이 뭐든 간에, 알아 두시오. 나도 댁과 같은 직업에 몸담은 사람으로, 이십 년 넘게 면허를 받아 일해 왔으니, 이발 일에 쓰는 연장이라면 하나도 빠짐없이 더할 나위 없이 잘 안다오. 그뿐 아니라 젊은 시절 한동안 군인 노릇도 했던지라, 투구가 무엇인지, 챙 없는 투구가 무엇인지, 면갑이 달린 투구가 무엇인지, 그 밖에 군대에 관한 것들, 아니 군인들의 무장에 관한 것들도 잘 안다오. 그래서 내가 말하는데 — 더 나은 견해는 존중하며, 언제나 더 건전한 판단에는 따른다는 전제하에 — 지금 우리 앞에 있는 이 물건, 이 훌륭한 양반이 손에 들고 있는 이것은 이발사의 대야가 아닐 뿐 아니라, 흰빛이 검은빛과 다르고 진실이 거짓과 다른 만큼이나 대야와는 거리가 멀다오. 게다가 내가 말하건대, 이것은 비록 투구이기는 하나 온전한 투구는 아니라오.\""]],[["“Certainly not,”","\"그야 물론 아니지요.\""],["said Don Quixote, “for half of it is wanting, that is to say the beaver.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그 절반, 그러니까 턱받이가 빠져 있으니 말이오.\""]],[["“It is quite true,”","\"정말 맞는 말이군요.\""],["said the curate, who saw the object of his friend the barber; and Cardenio, Don Fernando and his companions agreed with him, and even the Judge, if his thoughts had not been so full of Don Luis’s affair, would have helped to carry on the joke; but he was so taken up with the serious matters he had on his mind that he paid little or no attention to these facetious proceedings.","친구인 이발사가 무엇을 노리는지 알아챈 신부가 말했다. 카르데니오와 돈 페르난도와 그 일행도 그의 말에 맞장구를 쳤고, 판관조차도 돈 루이스의 일로 머릿속이 그토록 가득 차 있지만 않았더라면 이 익살을 거드는 데 한몫 거들었으련만, 그는 마음에 담아 둔 진지한 문제에 너무나 골몰해 있던 터라 이 우스꽝스러운 일에는 거의, 아니 전혀 관심을 두지 않았다."]],[["“God bless me!”","이때 그들의 놀림감이 된 이발사가 외쳤다."],["exclaimed their butt the barber at this; “is it possible that such an honourable company can say that this is not a basin but a helmet?","\"맙소사! 이렇게 점잖은 분들이 이것을 대야가 아니라 투구라고 말씀하시다니, 이게 있을 법한 일입니까?"],["Why, this is a thing that would astonish a whole university, however wise it might be!","이건 대학 전체를, 그것이 아무리 지혜롭다 한들 깜짝 놀라게 할 일이올시다!"],["That will do; if this basin is a helmet, why, then the pack-saddle must be a horse’s caparison, as this gentleman has said.”","됐습니다. 이 대야가 투구라면, 그럼 이 양반이 말한 대로 길마는 말의 마갑(馬甲)일 수밖에 없겠군요.\""]],[["“To me it looks like a pack-saddle,”","\"내게는 길마로 보이는구려.\""],["said Don Quixote; “but I have already said that with that question I do not concern myself.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"허나 그 문제에는 내가 관여하지 않겠다고 이미 말한 바 있소.\""]],[["“As to whether it be pack-saddle or caparison,”","\"그것이 길마인지 마갑인지에 관해서는,\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “it is only for Señor Don Quixote to say; for in these matters of chivalry all these gentlemen and I bow to his authority.”","\"오직 돈키호테 님께서 판단하실 일입니다. 이런 기사도에 관한 문제에 있어서는 여기 계신 모든 분들과 나는 그분의 권위에 머리를 숙이니까요.\""]],[["“By God, gentlemen,”","\"맹세코, 여러분,\""],["said Don Quixote, “so many strange things have happened to me in this castle on the two occasions on which I have sojourned in it, that I will not venture to assert anything positively in reply to any question touching anything it contains; for it is my belief that everything that goes on within it goes by enchantment.","돈키호테가 말했다. \"내가 이 성에 두 번 머무는 동안 하도 기이한 일들을 많이 겪은지라, 이 안에 있는 무엇에 관한 물음이든 단정 지어 답하기를 감히 시도하지 않겠소이다."],["The first time, an enchanted Moor that there is in it gave me sore trouble, nor did Sancho fare well among certain followers of his; and last night I was kept hanging by this arm for nearly two hours, without knowing how or why I came by such a mishap.","그 안에서 벌어지는 모든 일이 마법으로 일어난다는 것이 내 믿음이기 때문이오. 처음에는 이 안에 있는 마법에 걸린 무어인 하나가 나를 심히 괴롭혔고, 산초 또한 어떤 무리의 손에 봉변을 당했소."],["So that now, for me to come forward to give an opinion in such a puzzling matter, would be to risk a rash decision.","그리고 어젯밤에는 이 팔로 두 시간 가까이 매달려 있었으되, 어찌하여 또 무슨 까닭으로 그런 봉변을 당했는지 알지 못하오."],["As regards the assertion that this is a basin and not a helmet I have already given an answer; but as to the question whether this is a pack-saddle or a caparison I will not venture to give a positive opinion, but will leave it to your worships’ better judgment.","그러니 지금 내가 나서서 이토록 어려운 문제에 의견을 내놓는다면, 이는 경솔한 판단을 무릅쓰는 일이 되겠소. 이것이 투구가 아니라 대야라는 주장에 관해서는 이미 답을 드렸소만, 이것이 길마인지 마갑인지에 관해서는 감히 단정적인 의견을 내놓지 않고 여러분의 더 나은 판단에 맡기겠소."],["Perhaps as you are not dubbed knights like myself, the enchantments of this place have nothing to do with you, and your faculties are unfettered, and you can see things in this castle as they really and truly are, and not as they appear to me.”","어쩌면 여러분은 나처럼 기사로 서임되지 않았기에 이곳의 마법이 여러분과는 아무 상관이 없고, 그래서 여러분의 분별력이 얽매이지 않아 이 성 안의 사물을 내게 보이는 대로가 아니라 참되고 진실한 모습 그대로 볼 수 있을지도 모르겠소.\""]],[["“There can be no question,”","이에 돈 페르난도가 말했다."],["said Don Fernando on this, “but that Señor Don Quixote has spoken very wisely, and that with us rests the decision of this matter; and that we may have surer ground to go on, I will take the votes of the gentlemen in secret, and declare the result clearly and fully.”","\"돈키호테 님께서 참으로 지혜롭게 말씀하셨고, 이 문제의 판결이 우리에게 달려 있다는 데에는 의문의 여지가 없습니다. 우리가 더 확실한 근거 위에 설 수 있도록, 내가 여러분의 의견을 은밀히 모아 그 결과를 분명하고 빠짐없이 선언하겠습니다.\""]],[["To those who were in the secret of Don Quixote’s humour all this afforded great amusement; but to those who knew nothing about it, it seemed the greatest nonsense in the world, in particular to the four servants of Don Luis, as well as to Don Luis himself, and to three other travellers who had by chance come to the inn, and had the appearance of officers of the Holy Brotherhood, as indeed they were; but the one who above all was at his wits’ end, was the barber basin, there before his very eyes, had been turned into Mambrino’s helmet, and whose pack-saddle he had no doubt whatever was about to become a rich caparison for a horse. All laughed to see Don Fernando going from one to another collecting the votes, and whispering to them to give him their private opinion whether the treasure over which there had been so much fighting was a pack-saddle or a caparison; but after he had taken the votes of those who knew Don Quixote, he said aloud, “The fact is, my good fellow, that I am tired collecting such a number of opinions, for I find that there is not one of whom I ask what I desire to know, who does not tell me that it is absurd to say that this is the pack-saddle of an ass, and not the caparison of a horse, nay, of a thoroughbred horse; so you must submit, for, in spite of you and your ass, this is a caparison and no pack-saddle, and you have stated and proved your case very badly.”","돈키호테의 별난 기질을 속속들이 아는 이들에게는 이 모든 것이 큰 즐거움을 주었으나, 그것을 전혀 모르는 이들에게는 세상에서 가장 터무니없는 헛소리로만 보였으니, 특히 돈 루이스의 하인 넷과 돈 루이스 본인, 그리고 우연히 객줏집에 들른 다른 세 나그네에게 그러했다. 그 세 사람은 산타 에르만다드의 관원처럼 보였는데 실제로도 그러했다. 그러나 그 누구보다도 어찌할 바를 모른 것은 그 이발사였으니, 바로 그의 눈앞에서 자기 대야가 맘브리노의 투구로 둔갑하고, 자기 길마가 의심할 여지 없이 곧 호화로운 말의 마갑으로 바뀌려는 참이었던 것이다. 돈 페르난도가 이 사람 저 사람을 돌며 표를 거두면서, 그토록 다투어 온 이 보물이 길마인지 마갑인지 각자의 은밀한 의견을 일러 달라고 귓속말로 속삭이는 모습을 보고 모두가 웃었다. 그런데 돈키호테를 아는 이들의 표를 다 거둔 뒤 그는 큰 소리로 말했다. \"이보게, 실은 이렇게 많은 의견을 거두느라 지쳤네. 내가 알고자 하는 바를 묻는 이마다 하나같이, 이것을 말의 마갑이, 그것도 순종 말의 마갑이 아니라 당나귀의 길마라고 하는 것은 터무니없는 소리라고 답하지 않는 이가 없으니 말일세. 그러니 자네는 승복해야 하네. 자네와 자네 당나귀가 아무리 떼를 써도 이것은 길마가 아니라 마갑이며, 자네는 자네 주장을 아주 형편없이 펼치고 또 입증한 셈이니 말일세.\""]],[["“May I never share heaven,” said the poor barber, “if your worships are not all mistaken; and may my soul appear before God as that appears to me a pack-saddle and not a caparison; but, ‘laws go,’-I say no more; and indeed I am not drunk, for I am fasting, except it be from sin.”","\"제가 천국을 누리지 못해도 좋습니다.\" 가엾은 이발사가 말했다. \"여러분 모두가 잘못 보신 게 아니라면 말입니다. 또 제 영혼이 하느님 앞에 나아가지 못해도 좋습니다. 제게 저것이 마갑이 아니라 길마로 보이지 않는다면 말입니다. 하지만 '법은 법대로 가는 법'이니 — 더는 말하지 않겠습니다. 그리고 저는 정말이지 취하지 않았습니다. 저는 단식 중이거든요, 죄에서 단식하는 것만 빼면 말입니다.\""]],[["The simple talk of the barber did not afford less amusement than the absurdities of Don Quixote, who now observed:","이발사의 천진한 말은 돈키호테의 터무니없는 소리 못지않은 즐거움을 안겨 주었는데, 이번에는 돈키호테가 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“There is no more to be done now than for each to take what belongs to him, and to whom God has given it, may St.","\"이제 더 할 일이라고는, 각자 자기 것을 가져가는 것뿐이오."],["Peter add his blessing.”","하느님께서 그것을 누구에게 주셨든, 성 베드로께서 그분의 축복을 더해 주시기를.\""]],[["But said one of the four servants, “Unless, indeed, this is a deliberate joke, I cannot bring myself to believe that men so intelligent as those present are, or seem to be, can venture to declare and assert that this is not a basin, and that not a pack-saddle; but as I perceive that they do assert and declare it, I can only come to the conclusion that there is some mystery in this persistence in what is so opposed to the evidence of experience and truth itself; for I swear by”--and here he rapped out a round oath-“all the people in the world will not make me believe that this is not a barber’s basin and that a jackass’s pack-saddle.”","그러나 네 하인 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"이것이 작정하고 꾸민 농담이 아닌 다음에야, 여기 계신 분들처럼 지혜롭거나 지혜로워 보이는 분들이 감히 이것을 대야가 아니라거니 저것을 길마가 아니라거니 선언하고 단언하실 수 있다는 것을, 저로서는 도무지 믿을 수가 없습니다. 하지만 그분들이 정말로 그렇게 단언하고 선언하시는 걸 보니, 저는 다만, 경험과 진실 그 자체의 증거에 그토록 어긋나는 것을 이렇게 고집하는 데에는 무슨 까닭이 숨어 있으리라는 결론에 이를 수밖에 없군요. 맹세코\" — 여기서 그는 걸쭉한 욕설을 한바탕 내뱉었다 — \"세상 모든 사람이 달려들어도 저로 하여금 이것이 이발사의 대야가 아니고 저것이 당나귀의 길마가 아니라고 믿게 만들지는 못할 겁니다.\""]],[["“It might easily be a she-ass’s,”","\"그게 암나귀 것일 수도 있지요.\""],["observed the curate.","신부가 한마디 했다."]],[["“It is all the same,”","\"그게 그거지요.\""],["said the servant; “that is not the point; but whether it is or is not a pack-saddle, as your worships say.”","하인이 말했다. \"그게 요점이 아니라, 여러분 말씀처럼 그것이 길마냐 아니냐 하는 게 요점이지요.\""]],[["On hearing this one of the newly arrived officers of the Brotherhood, who had been listening to the dispute and controversy, unable to restrain his anger and impatience, exclaimed, “It is a pack-saddle as sure as my father is my father, and whoever has said or will say anything else must be drunk.”","이 말을 듣고, 그 다툼과 옥신각신을 듣고 있던, 새로 도착한 산타 에르만다드의 관원 가운데 하나가 분노와 조바심을 누르지 못하고 외쳤다. \"내 아버지가 내 아버지인 것만큼이나 분명히 이건 길마요. 그리고 다른 소리를 했거나 앞으로 할 자는 술에 취한 게 틀림없소.\""]],[["“You lie like a rascally clown,” returned Don Quixote; and lifting his pike, which he had never let out of his hand, he delivered such a blow at his head that, had not the officer dodged it, it would have stretched him at full length. The pike was shivered in pieces against the ground, and the rest of the officers, seeing their comrade assaulted, raised a shout, calling for help for the Holy Brotherhood. The landlord, who was of the fraternity, ran at once to fetch his staff of office and his sword, and ranged himself on the side of his comrades; the servants of Don Luis clustered round him, lest he should escape from them in the confusion; the barber, seeing the house turned upside down, once more laid hold of his pack-saddle and Sancho did the same; Don Quixote drew his sword and charged the officers; Don Luis cried out to his servants to leave him alone and go and help Don Quixote, and Cardenio and Don Fernando, who were supporting him; the curate was shouting at the top of his voice, the landlady was screaming, her daughter was wailing, Maritornes was weeping, Dorothea was aghast, Luscinda terror-stricken, and Dona Clara in a faint. The barber cudgelled Sancho, and Sancho pommelled the barber; Don Luis gave one of his servants, who ventured to catch him by the arm to keep him from escaping, a cuff that bathed his teeth in blood; the Judge took his part; Don Fernando had got one of the officers down and was belabouring him heartily; the landlord raised his voice again calling for help for the Holy Brotherhood; so that the whole inn was nothing but cries, shouts, shrieks, confusion, terror, dismay, mishaps, sword-cuts, fisticuffs, cudgellings, kicks, and bloodshed; and in the midst of all this chaos, complication, and general entanglement, Don Quixote took it into his head that he had been plunged into the thick of the discord of Agramante’s camp; and, in a voice that shook the inn like thunder, he cried out:","\"이 비열한 촌놈 같으니, 거짓을 지껄이는구나.\" 돈키호테가 받아쳤다. 그러고는 한 번도 손에서 놓지 않았던 장창을 치켜들어 관원의 머리를 향해 어찌나 세게 후려쳤던지, 관원이 몸을 피하지 않았더라면 그를 그 자리에 길게 뻗게 만들었을 것이다. 장창은 땅바닥에 부딪쳐 산산조각이 났고, 동료가 공격당하는 것을 본 나머지 관원들은 고함을 지르며 산타 에르만다드에 도움을 청했다. 그 형제단의 일원이던 객줏집 주인은 즉시 직권을 나타내는 지팡이와 칼을 가지러 달려가 동료들 편에 가담했다. 돈 루이스의 하인들은 그가 이 혼란을 틈타 빠져나가지 못하도록 그를 빙 둘러쌌다. 집 안이 발칵 뒤집힌 것을 본 이발사는 다시 한번 자기 길마를 움켜잡았고 산초도 똑같이 했다. 돈키호테는 칼을 빼어 들고 관원들에게 돌진했다. 돈 루이스는 하인들에게 자기를 내버려 두고 가서 돈키호테와, 그를 거들고 있는 카르데니오와 돈 페르난도를 도우라고 소리쳤다. 신부는 목청껏 고함을 지르고, 안주인은 비명을 질러 대고, 그 딸은 울부짖고, 마리토르네스는 흐느끼고, 도로테아는 아연실색하고, 루신다는 공포에 사로잡히고, 도냐 클라라는 까무러쳤다. 이발사는 산초를 몽둥이질하고, 산초는 이발사를 주먹으로 쥐어박았다. 돈 루이스는 자기를 붙잡아 도망치지 못하게 하려고 감히 팔을 움켜쥔 하인 하나에게 주먹을 한 방 먹여 그 이빨을 피로 적셨다. 판관이 그를 거들었다. 돈 페르난도는 관원 하나를 쓰러뜨려 흠씬 두들겨 패고 있었다. 객줏집 주인은 다시 목소리를 높여 산타 에르만다드에 도움을 청했다. 그리하여 객줏집 안은 온통 울부짖음과 고함과 비명과 혼란과 공포와 경악과 봉변과 칼부림과 주먹질과 몽둥이질과 발길질과 유혈뿐이었다. 그리고 이 모든 아수라장과 뒤엉킴과 총체적 난장판 한가운데서, 돈키호테는 자신이 아그라만테 진영의 불화 한복판에 던져졌다는 생각이 머릿속에 들었다. 그래서 객줏집을 천둥처럼 뒤흔드는 목소리로 그가 외쳤다."]],[["“Hold all, let all sheathe their swords, let all be calm and attend to me as they value their lives!”","\"모두 멈추라, 모두 칼을 거두라, 모두 진정하고, 목숨을 아낀다면 내 말에 귀를 기울이라!\""]],[["All paused at his mighty voice, and he went on to say, “Did I not tell you, sirs, that this castle was enchanted, and that a legion or so of devils dwelt in it? In proof whereof I call upon you to behold with your own eyes how the discord of Agramante’s camp has come hither, and been transferred into the midst of us. See how they fight, there for the sword, here for the horse, on that side for the eagle, on this for the helmet; we are all fighting, and all at cross purposes. Come then, you, Señor Judge, and you, señor curate; let the one represent King Agramante and the other King Sobrino, and make peace among us; for by God Almighty it is a sorry business that so many persons of quality as we are should slay one another for such trifling cause.” The officers, who did not understand Don Quixote’s mode of speaking, and found themselves roughly handled by Don Fernando, Cardenio, and their companions, were not to be appeased; the barber was, however, for both his beard and his pack-saddle were the worse for the struggle; Sancho like a good servant obeyed the slightest word of his master; while the four servants of Don Luis kept quiet when they saw how little they gained by not being so. The landlord alone insisted upon it that they must punish the insolence of this madman, who at every turn raised a disturbance in the inn; but at length the uproar was stilled for the present; the pack-saddle remained a caparison till the day of judgment, and the basin a helmet and the inn a castle in Don Quixote’s imagination.","그 우렁찬 목소리에 모두가 멈추자, 그는 이어서 말했다. \"내가 여러분에게 말하지 않았소이까, 여러분, 이 성이 마법에 걸렸으며 그 안에 일개 군단쯤 되는 악마들이 깃들어 있다고 말이오? 그 증거로, 아그라만테 진영의 불화가 어떻게 이리로 옮겨 와 우리 한복판에 자리 잡았는지 여러분의 두 눈으로 직접 보시라고 청하는 바요. 보시오, 저들이 어떻게 싸우는지를. 저기서는 칼을 두고, 여기서는 말을 두고, 저쪽에서는 독수리를 두고, 이쪽에서는 투구를 두고 싸우고 있소. 우리 모두가 싸우고 있고, 모두가 서로 엇갈려 있소. 자, 그러니 판관 님과 신부 님께서 이리 오시오. 한 분은 아그라만테 왕을, 다른 한 분은 소브리노 왕을 맡으셔서 우리 사이에 화평을 이루어 주시오. 전능하신 하느님께 맹세코, 우리처럼 지체 높은 사람들이 이토록 하찮은 까닭으로 서로를 죽이려 든다는 것은 참으로 한심한 노릇이외다.\" 돈키호테의 말투를 알아듣지 못하고, 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오와 그 일행에게 호되게 당한 처지였던 관원들은 좀처럼 진정하려 들지 않았다. 그러나 이발사는 진정했으니, 수염도 길마도 그 다툼으로 더 망가진 탓이었다. 산초는 충실한 종답게 주인의 말 한마디 한마디에 고분고분 따랐고, 돈 루이스의 네 하인은 그러지 않아 봐야 득 될 것이 없음을 보고 잠잠해졌다. 오직 객줏집 주인만이, 걸핏하면 객줏집에 소동을 일으키는 이 미친자의 무례함을 반드시 벌해야 한다고 우겨 댔다. 그러나 마침내 소란은 당분간 잠잠해졌고, 길마는 최후의 심판 날까지 마갑으로 남았으며, 대야는 투구로, 객줏집은 성으로 돈키호테의 상상 속에 남았다."]],[["All having been now pacified and made friends by the persuasion of the Judge and the curate, the servants of Don Luis began again to urge him to return with them at once; and while he was discussing the matter with them, the Judge took counsel with Don Fernando, Cardenio, and the curate as to what he ought to do in the case, telling them how it stood, and what Don Luis had said to him.","판관과 신부의 설득으로 모두가 진정되어 화해한 뒤, 돈 루이스의 하인들은 다시 그에게 당장 자기들과 함께 돌아가자고 재촉하기 시작했다. 그가 그들과 그 문제를 의논하는 동안, 판관은 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오와 신부에게 이 일을 어떻게 처리해야 할지 의논을 구하며, 사정이 어떠한지와 돈 루이스가 자기에게 한 말을 일러 주었다."],["It was agreed at length that Don Fernando should tell the servants of Don Luis who he was, and that it was his desire that Don Luis should accompany him to Andalusia, where he would receive from the marquis his brother the welcome his quality entitled him to; for, otherwise, it was easy to see from the determination of Don Luis that he would not return to his father at present, though they tore him to pieces.","마침내, 돈 페르난도가 돈 루이스의 하인들에게 자신이 누구인지를 밝히고, 돈 루이스가 자기를 따라 안달루시아로 가는 것이 자기 뜻이며, 거기서 그가 자기 형인 후작에게서 그의 지체에 걸맞은 환대를 받게 되리라고 말하기로 합의가 이루어졌다. 그러지 않으면, 돈 루이스가 비록 갈가리 찢긴다 해도 지금은 아버지에게 돌아가지 않으리라는 그 단호함으로 보아, 사정이 어떨지는 빤히 알 수 있었기 때문이다."],["On learning the rank of Don Fernando and the resolution of Don Luis the four then settled it between themselves that three of them should return to tell his father how matters stood, and that the other should remain to wait upon Don Luis, and not leave him until they came back for him, or his father’s orders were known.","돈 페르난도의 지위와 돈 루이스의 결심을 알게 된 네 하인은 자기들끼리 이렇게 정했으니, 그중 셋은 돌아가 아버지에게 사정이 어떠한지 알리고, 나머지 하나는 남아서 돈 루이스의 시중을 들며, 그들이 그를 데리러 돌아오거나 아버지의 분부를 알게 될 때까지 그의 곁을 떠나지 않기로 했다."],["Thus by the authority of Agramante and the wisdom of King Sobrino all this complication of disputes was arranged; but the enemy of concord and hater of peace, feeling himself slighted and made a fool of, and seeing how little he had gained after having involved them all in such an elaborate entanglement, resolved to try his hand once more by stirring up fresh quarrels and disturbances.","이렇게 아그라만테의 권위와 소브리노 왕의 지혜로 이 모든 얽히고설킨 다툼이 가지런히 정리되었다. 그러나 화합의 적이요 평화를 미워하는 자는, 자신이 무시당하고 우롱당했다고 느끼며, 그 모두를 그토록 공들인 뒤엉킴 속에 몰아넣고도 얻은 것이 거의 없음을 보고서, 다시 한번 새로운 다툼과 소동을 부추겨 솜씨를 발휘해 보기로 작정했다."]],[["It came about in this wise: the officers were pacified on learning the rank of those with whom they had been engaged, and withdrew from the contest, considering that whatever the result might be they were likely to get the worst of the battle; but one of them, the one who had been thrashed and kicked by Don Fernando, recollected that among some warrants he carried for the arrest of certain delinquents, he had one against Don Quixote, whom the Holy Brotherhood had ordered to be arrested for setting the galley slaves free, as Sancho had, with very good reason, apprehended. Suspecting how it was, then, he wished to satisfy himself as to whether Don Quixote’s features corresponded; and taking a parchment out of his bosom he lit upon what he was in search of, and setting himself to read it deliberately, for he was not a quick reader, as he made out each word he fixed his eyes on Don Quixote, and went on comparing the description in the warrant with his face, and discovered that beyond all doubt he was the person described in it. As soon as he had satisfied himself, folding up the parchment, he took the warrant in his left hand and with his right seized Don Quixote by the collar so tightly that he did not allow him to breathe, and shouted aloud, “Help for the Holy Brotherhood! and that you may see I demand it in earnest, read this warrant which says this highwayman is to be arrested.”","일은 이렇게 벌어졌다. 관원들은 자기들이 맞붙은 상대의 지위를 알고는 진정하여, 결과가 어찌 되든 이 싸움에서 자기들이 가장 큰 손해를 보기 쉽다고 여겨 다툼에서 물러났다. 그러나 그들 가운데 하나, 곧 돈 페르난도에게 두들겨 맞고 걷어차인 그 관원이, 자기가 지닌 몇몇 범죄자 체포 영장 가운데 돈키호테를 잡으라는 영장이 하나 있다는 사실을 떠올렸다. 산초가 아주 그럴 만한 까닭으로 염려했던 대로, 산타 에르만다드는 갤리선 노예들을 풀어 준 죄로 돈키호테를 체포하라고 명했던 것이다. 그래서 사정이 어떠한지를 짐작한 그는 돈키호테의 용모가 일치하는지 확인하고 싶어졌다. 그는 품에서 양피지 한 장을 꺼내어 찾던 것을 찾아내고는, 글 읽는 솜씨가 빠르지 못한 터라 천천히 읽기 시작하여, 한 단어 한 단어 더듬어 가며 돈키호테에게 눈길을 고정하고는, 영장에 적힌 인상착의를 그의 얼굴과 견주어 보았다. 그리하여 그가 의심할 여지 없이 거기 묘사된 바로 그 인물임을 알아냈다. 확신을 얻자마자 그는 양피지를 접어 왼손에 영장을 들고 오른손으로 돈키호테의 멱살을 어찌나 단단히 거머쥐었던지 숨도 쉬지 못하게 하고는 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"산타 에르만다드에 도움을 청하노라! 그리고 내가 진심으로 청하는 것임을 알고자 한다면, 이 노상강도를 체포하라고 적힌 이 영장을 읽어 보시오.\""]],[["The curate took the warrant and saw that what the officer said was true, and that it agreed with Don Quixote’s appearance, who, on his part, when he found himself roughly handled by this rascally clown, worked up to the highest pitch of wrath, and all his joints cracking with rage, with both hands seized the officer by the throat with all his might, so that had he not been helped by his comrades he would have yielded up his life ere Don Quixote released his hold. The landlord, who had perforce to support his brother officers, ran at once to aid them. The landlady, when she saw her husband engaged in a fresh quarrel, lifted up her voice afresh, and its note was immediately caught up by Maritornes and her daughter, calling upon heaven and all present for help; and Sancho, seeing what was going on, exclaimed, “By the Lord, it is quite true what my master says about the enchantments of this castle, for it is impossible to live an hour in peace in it!”","신부가 영장을 받아 보니 관원의 말이 사실이며 돈키호테의 모습과 일치했다. 한편 돈키호테로 말하자면, 이 비열한 촌놈에게 거칠게 다루어지는 처지가 되자 분노가 극에 달해, 격노로 온 관절이 우두둑거리며 두 손으로 있는 힘껏 관원의 목을 거머쥐었으니, 만일 동료들이 돕지 않았더라면 그는 돈키호테가 손을 놓기도 전에 목숨을 내놓고 말았을 것이다. 부득이 동료 관원들을 거들어야 했던 객줏집 주인이 즉시 그들을 도우러 달려갔다. 남편이 또다시 싸움에 휘말린 것을 본 안주인은 새삼 목소리를 높였고, 그 가락을 마리토르네스와 그 딸이 곧장 받아, 하늘과 그 자리에 있는 모든 이에게 도움을 청했다. 벌어지는 일을 본 산초가 외쳤다. \"맹세코, 이 성의 마법에 관한 우리 주인 나리 말씀이 다 옳구먼. 여기서는 단 한 시간도 편안히 살 수가 없으니 말이야!\""]],[["Don Fernando parted the officer and Don Quixote, and to their mutual contentment made them relax the grip by which they held, the one the coat collar, the other the throat of his adversary; for all this, however, the officers did not cease to demand their prisoner and call on them to help, and deliver him over bound into their power, as was required for the service of the King and of the Holy Brotherhood, on whose behalf they again demanded aid and assistance to effect the capture of this robber and footpad of the highways.","돈 페르난도가 관원과 돈키호테를 떼어 놓고, 양쪽 다 만족하도록 한쪽은 멱살을, 다른 쪽은 상대의 목을 쥐고 있던 손아귀의 힘을 풀게 했다. 그래도 관원들은 자기들의 죄수를 내놓으라고 요구하기를 멈추지 않고 도와 달라 외치며, 국왕과 산타 에르만다드를 위해 요구되는 대로 그를 결박하여 자기들 손에 넘기라고 했다. 그러면서 그들은 이 도둑이자 노상강도를 붙잡을 수 있도록 다시 한번 산타 에르만다드를 대신하여 지원과 협조를 요청했다."]],[["Don Quixote smiled when he heard these words, and said very calmly, “Come now, base, ill-born brood; call ye it highway robbery to give freedom to those in bondage, to release the captives, to succour the miserable, to raise up the fallen, to relieve the needy?","돈키호테는 이 말을 듣고 빙긋 웃더니 아주 침착하게 말했다. \"자, 이리 와 보라, 비천하고 천한 핏줄의 무리여."],["Infamous beings, who by your vile grovelling intellects deserve that heaven should not make known to you the virtue that lies in knight-errantry, or show you the sin and ignorance in which ye lie when ye refuse to respect the shadow, not to say the presence, of any knight-errant!","속박된 자들에게 자유를 주고, 사로잡힌 이들을 풀어 주고, 비참한 이들을 구하고, 쓰러진 이들을 일으켜 세우고, 궁핍한 이들을 돕는 것을 너희가 노상강도질이라 부르느냐?"],["Come now; band, not of officers, but of thieves; footpads with the licence of the Holy Brotherhood; tell me who was the ignoramus who signed a warrant of arrest against such a knight as I am?","파렴치한 것들이여, 너희의 그 천박하고 비루한 지력 때문에, 하늘이 너희에게 편력 기사도에 깃든 미덕을 알려 주지 않아 마땅하고, 어떤 편력 기사의 현존은 말할 것도 없고 그 그림자조차 존중하기를 거부하는 너희가 어떤 죄와 무지 속에 빠져 있는지를 보여 주지 않아 마땅하도다! 자, 이리 와 보라."],["Who was he that did not know that knights-errant are independent of all jurisdictions, that their law is their sword, their charter their prowess, and their edicts their will?","관원이 아니라 도둑의 무리여, 산타 에르만다드의 면허를 받은 노상강도들이여. 나 같은 기사를 잡으라는 체포 영장에 서명한 그 무지렁이가 대체 누구인지 내게 말해 보라."],["Who, I say again, was the fool that knows not that there are no letters patent of nobility that confer such privileges or exemptions as a knight-errant acquires the day he is dubbed a knight, and devotes himself to the arduous calling of chivalry?","편력 기사가 모든 사법권에서 벗어나 있으며, 그들의 법은 그들의 칼이요, 그들의 헌장은 그들의 무용이요, 그들의 칙령은 그들의 의지임을 모르는 자가 대체 누구더냐? 다시 묻거니와, 편력 기사가 기사로 서임되어 기사도라는 그 고된 소명에 헌신하는 그날 얻게 되는 특권이나 면제만 한 것을 부여하는 귀족 특허장은 어디에도 없음을 모르는 그 어리석은 자가 대체 누구더냐?"],["What knight-errant ever paid poll-tax, duty, queen’s pin-money, king’s dues, toll or ferry?","어느 편력 기사가 인두세나, 통행세나, 왕비의 용돈이나, 국왕의 세금이나, 통과세나, 나룻세를 낸 적이 있더냐?"],["What tailor ever took payment of him for making his clothes?","어느 재단사가 그의 옷을 지어 주고 삯을 받은 적이 있더냐?"],["What castellan that received him in his castle ever made him pay his shot?","자기 성에 그를 맞아들인 어느 성주가 그에게 셈을 치르게 한 적이 있더냐?"],["What king did not seat him at his table?","어느 왕이 그를 자기 식탁에 앉히지 않았더냐?"],["What damsel was not enamoured of him and did not yield herself up wholly to his will and pleasure?","어느 처녀가 그에게 반하여 자신을 온전히 그의 뜻과 기쁨에 내맡기지 않았더냐?"],["And, lastly, what knight-errant has there been, is there, or will there ever be in the world, not bold enough to give, single-handed, four hundred cudgellings to four hundred officers of the Holy Brotherhood if they come in his way?”","그리고 마지막으로, 산타 에르만다드의 관원 사백 명이 제 앞길을 막아선다면 혼자서 그 사백 명에게 사백 번의 몽둥이질을 안겨 줄 만큼 담대하지 못한 편력 기사가 이 세상에 있었던 적이, 또 있을 적이 대체 어디 있더냐?\""]],[["# CHAPTER XLVI. OF THE END OF THE NOTABLE ADVENTURE OF THE OFFICERS OF THE HOLY BROTHERHOOD; AND OF THE GREAT FEROCITY OF OUR WORTHY KNIGHT, DON QUIXOTE","# CHAPTER XLVI."]],[["While Don Quixote was talking in this strain, the curate was endeavouring to persuade the officers that he was out of his senses, as they might perceive by his deeds and his words, and that they need not press the matter any further, for even if they arrested him and carried him off, they would have to release him by-and-by as a madman; to which the holder of the warrant replied that he had nothing to do with inquiring into Don Quixote’s madness, but only to execute his superior’s orders, and that once taken they might let him go three hundred times if they liked.","돈키호테가 이런 투로 이야기하는 동안, 신부는 그가 제정신이 아님을 그의 행동과 말에서 알 수 있을 것이라며 성동포단 관원들을 설득하려 애썼고, 더 이상 이 일을 밀어붙일 필요가 없다고, 설령 그를 체포해 데려간다 한들 미치광이라 하여 머지않아 풀어 주어야 할 터이니 그렇다고 일렀다. 이에 영장을 쥔 자는 돈키호테가 미쳤는지 아닌지를 따지는 일은 자기와 상관없는 노릇이며, 자기는 그저 상관의 명령을 집행할 뿐이고, 일단 잡아넣은 뒤에는 그쪽에서 원한다면 삼백 번이라도 놓아주든 말든 마음대로 하라고 대꾸했다."]],[["“For all that,”","\"그렇다 해도,\" 신부가 말했다."],["said the curate, “you must not take him away this time, nor will he, it is my opinion, let himself be taken away.”","\"이번만은 그를 데려가서는 안 될 것이오. 또 내 생각엔 그가 순순히 끌려가도록 가만있지도 않을 것이고.\""]],[["In short, the curate used such arguments, and Don Quixote did such mad things, that the officers would have been more mad than he was if they had not perceived his want of wits, and so they thought it best to allow themselves to be pacified, and even to act as peacemakers between the barber and Sancho Panza, who still continued their altercation with much bitterness. In the end they, as officers of justice, settled the question by arbitration in such a manner that both sides were, if not perfectly contented, at least to some extent satisfied; for they changed the pack-saddles, but not the girths or head-stalls; and as to Mambrino’s helmet, the curate, under the rose and without Don Quixote’s knowing it, paid eight reals for the basin, and the barber executed a full receipt and engagement to make no further demand then or thenceforth for evermore, amen. These two disputes, which were the most important and gravest, being settled, it only remained for the servants of Don Luis to consent that three of them should return while one was left to accompany him whither Don Fernando desired to take him; and good luck and better fortune, having already begun to solve difficulties and remove obstructions in favour of the lovers and warriors of the inn, were pleased to persevere and bring everything to a happy issue; for the servants agreed to do as Don Luis wished; which gave Dona Clara such happiness that no one could have looked into her face just then without seeing the joy of her heart. Zoraida, though she did not fully comprehend all she saw, was grave or gay without knowing why, as she watched and studied the various countenances, but particularly her Spaniard’s, whom she followed with her eyes and clung to with her soul. The gift and compensation which the curate gave the barber had not escaped the landlord’s notice, and he demanded Don Quixote’s reckoning, together with the amount of the damage to his wine-skins, and the loss of his wine, swearing that neither Rocinante nor Sancho’s ass should leave the inn until he had been paid to the very last farthing. The curate settled all amicably, and Don Fernando paid; though the Judge had also very readily offered to pay the score; and all became so peaceful and quiet that the inn no longer reminded one of the discord of Agramante’s camp, as Don Quixote said, but of the peace and tranquillity of the days of Octavianus: for all which it was the universal opinion that their thanks were due to the great zeal and eloquence of the curate, and to the unexampled generosity of Don Fernando.","요컨대 신부가 이런저런 논리를 펴고 돈키호테가 어찌나 미친 짓들을 해 댔던지, 관원들이 만일 그의 모자란 지각을 알아채지 못했다면 그보다 더 미친 자들이 되었을 판이었다. 그리하여 그들은 마음을 누그러뜨리는 편이 상책이라 여겼고, 나아가 아직도 모진 악다구니로 옥신각신하고 있던 이발사와 산초 판사 사이를 중재하는 노릇까지 맡기로 했다. 결국 그들은 법을 집행하는 관원답게 양쪽이 완전히 만족하지는 못하더라도 적어도 어느 정도는 흡족하도록 중재로 문제를 매듭지었으니, 길마는 서로 바꾸되 뱃대끈이나 굴레는 바꾸지 않기로 했다. 그리고 맘브리노의 투구에 대해서는, 신부가 돈키호테 모르게 슬그머니 대야 값으로 8레알을 치렀고, 이발사는 영수증을 끝까지 작성하여 지금이나 앞으로나 영원토록 더는 아무런 청구도 하지 않겠노라 다짐했으니, 아멘이었다. 가장 중대하고 심각하던 이 두 분쟁이 해결되고 나자, 이제 남은 일이라곤 돈루이스의 하인들이 셋은 돌아가고 하나만 남아 돈페르난도가 데려가고자 하는 곳까지 그를 따르도록 동의하는 것뿐이었다. 그리고 행운과 더 나은 운명이 이미 객줏집의 연인들과 무사들을 위하여 곤경을 풀고 장애를 치워 주기 시작했던 터라, 끝까지 그 일을 밀고 나가 모든 것을 행복한 결말로 이끌어 주기를 기꺼워했으니, 하인들이 돈루이스가 바라는 대로 하기로 동의했던 것이다. 이에 도냐클라라는 어찌나 행복했던지, 그 순간 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다본 사람이라면 누구든 그 가슴속 기쁨을 보지 않을 수 없었을 것이다. 소라이다는 비록 자기가 보는 모든 것을 온전히 헤아리지는 못했으나, 까닭도 모른 채 진지해지기도 하고 즐거워지기도 하면서, 여러 사람의 얼굴을, 특히 자기 눈으로 좇고 영혼으로 매달리던 그 에스파냐 사람의 얼굴을 지켜보며 살폈다. 신부가 이발사에게 준 선물이자 보상은 주인장의 눈을 벗어나지 못했으니, 그는 돈키호테의 셈과 더불어 자기 가죽 부대가 입은 손해와 흘려 버린 포도주 값까지 청구하면서, 마지막 한 푼까지 받아 내기 전에는 로시난테도 산초의 나귀도 객줏집을 떠나지 못하리라 맹세했다. 신부가 모든 것을 원만히 처리하고 돈페르난도가 값을 치렀다. 비록 판사도 기꺼이 셈을 치르겠노라 선뜻 나섰지만 말이다. 그리하여 모두가 어찌나 평화롭고 고요해졌던지, 돈키호테의 말마따나 객줏집은 더 이상 아그라만테 진영의 불화를 떠올리게 하지 않고 옥타비아누스 시절의 평화와 안녕을 떠올리게 했다. 이 모든 일에 대해 그 공이 신부의 크나큰 열성과 능변에, 그리고 돈페르난도의 유례없는 너그러움에 돌아가야 한다는 것이 모두의 한결같은 의견이었다."]],[["Finding himself now clear and quit of all quarrels, his squire’s as well as his own, Don Quixote considered that it would be advisable to continue the journey he had begun, and bring to a close that great adventure for which he had been called and chosen; and with this high resolve he went and knelt before Dorothea, who, however, would not allow him to utter a word until he had risen; so to obey her he rose, and said, “It is a common proverb, fair lady, that ‘diligence is the mother of good fortune,’ and experience has often shown in important affairs that the earnestness of the negotiator brings the doubtful case to a successful termination; but in nothing does this truth show itself more plainly than in war, where quickness and activity forestall the devices of the enemy, and win the victory before the foe has time to defend himself. All this I say, exalted and esteemed lady, because it seems to me that for us to remain any longer in this castle now is useless, and may be injurious to us in a way that we shall find out some day; for who knows but that your enemy the giant may have learned by means of secret and diligent spies that I am going to destroy him, and if the opportunity be given him he may seize it to fortify himself in some impregnable castle or stronghold, against which all my efforts and the might of my indefatigable arm may avail but little? Therefore, lady, let us, as I say, forestall his schemes by our activity, and let us depart at once in quest of fair fortune; for your highness is only kept from enjoying it as fully as you could desire by my delay in encountering your adversary.”","이제 자기 것이든 종자의 것이든 모든 다툼에서 깨끗이 풀려난 것을 알게 된 돈키호테는, 시작했던 여정을 계속하여 자기가 부름받고 뽑힌 그 위대한 모험을 끝맺는 것이 좋겠다고 생각했다. 이 드높은 결심을 품고 그는 도로테아 앞에 나아가 무릎을 꿇었는데, 그녀는 그가 일어서기 전에는 한마디도 입을 떼지 못하게 했다. 하여 그 명을 따라 그는 일어나서 말했다. \"아름다운 공주님, '근면은 행운의 어머니'라는 흔한 속담이 있고, 또 중대한 일들에서 협상하는 자의 열성이 의심스러운 사안을 성공적인 결말로 이끈다는 것을 경험이 자주 보여 주었습니다. 그러나 이 진리가 가장 분명히 드러나는 것은 다름 아닌 전쟁에서이니, 거기서는 신속함과 민첩함이 적의 책략을 미리 막아 내고, 적이 미처 방비할 틈을 갖기도 전에 승리를 거두게 마련입니다. 고귀하고 존경받는 공주님, 제가 이 모든 말씀을 드리는 까닭은, 우리가 지금 이 성에 더 머무는 것이 무익할뿐더러 언젠가 알게 될 어떤 방식으로 우리에게 해로울 수도 있다고 여겨지기 때문입니다. 누가 압니까, 공주님의 원수인 그 거인이 은밀하고 부지런한 첩자들을 통해 제가 자기를 무찌르러 간다는 것을 알아챘을지, 그리고 기회가 주어진다면 그 틈을 타 난공불락의 성이나 요새에 틀어박혀, 저의 온갖 노력과 지칠 줄 모르는 제 팔의 힘으로도 어쩌지 못하게 만들지 말입니다. 그러니 공주님, 제가 말씀드린 대로 우리의 민첩함으로 그자의 책략을 미리 막고, 당장 떠나 행운을 찾아 나섭시다. 공주님께서 바라시는 만큼 흡족하게 그 행운을 누리지 못하시는 것은 오로지 제가 공주님의 적수와 맞붙기를 미루고 있는 탓이니 말입니다.\""]],[["Don Quixote held his peace and said no more, calmly awaiting the reply of the beauteous princess, who, with commanding dignity and in a style adapted to Don Quixote’s own, replied to him in these words, “I give you thanks, sir knight, for the eagerness you, like a good knight to whom it is a natural obligation to succour the orphan and the needy, display to afford me aid in my sore trouble; and heaven grant that your wishes and mine may be realised, so that you may see that there are women in this world capable of gratitude; as to my departure, let it be forthwith, for I have no will but yours; dispose of me entirely in accordance with your good pleasure; for she who has once entrusted to you the defence of her person, and placed in your hands the recovery of her dominions, must not think of offering opposition to that which your wisdom may ordain.”","돈키호테는 입을 다물고 더는 말하지 않은 채, 아리따운 공주의 대답을 차분히 기다렸다. 공주는 위엄을 갖춘 당당한 태도로, 돈키호테 자신의 말투에 걸맞은 투로 이렇게 답했다. \"기사님, 고아와 궁핍한 자를 돕는 것이 천성의 의무인 훌륭한 기사답게, 이 쓰라린 곤경에 처한 저를 도우려 보여 주시는 그 열의에 감사드립니다. 부디 하늘이 기사님과 저의 소원을 이루어 주시어, 이 세상에 감사할 줄 아는 여인이 있음을 기사님께서 보실 수 있기를 바랍니다. 제 출발로 말하자면, 당장 떠나도록 하지요. 저에게는 기사님의 뜻 외에 다른 뜻이 없으니, 기사님 좋으실 대로 저를 온전히 처분하소서. 일찍이 기사님께 제 한 몸의 보호를 맡기고 제 영토의 회복을 그 손에 맡긴 여인으로서, 기사님의 지혜가 명하시는 바에 거스를 생각은 결코 품지 않을 것이옵니다.\""]],[["“On, then, in God’s name,”","\"그렇다면 하느님의 이름으로 나아갑시다.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “for, when a lady humbles herself to me, I will not lose the opportunity of raising her up and placing her on the throne of her ancestors.","\"한 귀부인이 제게 몸을 낮추실 때, 저는 그분을 일으켜 세워 조상의 옥좌에 앉혀 드릴 기회를 놓치지 않겠습니다. 당장 떠납시다."],["Let us depart at once, for the common saying that in delay there is danger, lends spurs to my eagerness to take the road; and as neither heaven has created nor hell seen any that can daunt or intimidate me, saddle Rocinante, Sancho, and get ready thy ass and the queen’s palfrey, and let us take leave of the castellan and these gentlemen, and go hence this very instant.”","지체하는 데 위험이 도사린다는 흔한 말이 길을 떠나려는 제 열의에 박차를 가하니 말입니다. 하늘도 저를 두렵게 하거나 겁먹게 할 자를 창조하지 않았고 지옥도 그런 자를 본 적이 없으니, 산초야, 로시난테에 안장을 얹고, 네 나귀와 여왕님의 말도 채비하여, 성주님과 이 신사분들께 작별을 고하고 바로 이 순간 떠나도록 하자.\""]],[["Sancho, who was standing by all the time, said, shaking his head, “Ah!","내내 곁에 서 있던 산초가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."],["master, master, there is more mischief in the village than one hears of, begging all good bodies’ pardon.”","\"아이고, 주인 나리, 나리, 착한 분들께는 죄송한 말씀이지만, 동네에는 소문으로 들리는 것보다 훨씬 더 못된 일들이 있는 법이라고요.\""]],[["“What mischief can there be in any village, or in all the cities of the world, you booby, that can hurt my reputation?”","\"이 멍청아, 어느 동네에서든, 아니 세상의 모든 도시를 통틀어서든, 내 명예를 해칠 만한 못된 일이 무엇이 있단 말이냐?\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."]],[["“If your worship is angry,”","\"나리께서 화를 내신다면,\""],["replied Sancho, “I will hold my tongue and leave unsaid what as a good squire I am bound to say, and what a good servant should tell his master.”","산초가 대꾸했다. \"저는 입을 다물고, 훌륭한 종자로서 마땅히 드려야 할 말씀, 또 충직한 하인이 제 주인께 일러 드려야 할 말씀을 하지 않고 그냥 두겠습니다요.\""]],[["“Say what thou wilt,”","\"무엇이든 하고 싶은 말을 하거라.\" 돈키호테가 받았다."],["returned Don Quixote, “provided thy words be not meant to work upon my fears; for thou, if thou fearest, art behaving like thyself; but I like myself, in not fearing.”","\"다만 네 말이 내 두려움을 부추기려는 것만 아니라면 말이다. 네가 두려워한다면 그건 너다운 처신이요, 나는 두려워하지 않으니 나다운 것이로다.\""]],[["“It is nothing of the sort, as I am a sinner before God,”","\"하느님 앞의 죄인인 제가 말씀드리건대, 그런 게 결코 아닙니다요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “but that I take it to be sure and certain that this lady, who calls herself queen of the great kingdom of Micomicon, is no more so than my mother; for, if she was what she says, she would not go rubbing noses with one that is here every instant and behind every door.”","\"다만 제 생각엔, 자기를 위대한 미코미콘 왕국의 여왕이라 일컫는 이 부인이 제 어머니가 여왕인 것이나 진배없다는 게 확실하고 분명하다는 겁니다요. 만약 부인이 정말 그렇게 말씀하시는 분이라면, 여기 매 순간 같이 있는 어떤 사람이랑, 그것도 문짝 뒤에서마다 코를 비벼 대고 있지는 않으실 테니까요.\""]],[["Dorothea turned red at Sancho’s words, for the truth was that her husband Don Fernando had now and then, when the others were not looking, gathered from her lips some of the reward his love had earned, and Sancho seeing this had considered that such freedom was more like a courtesan than a queen of a great kingdom; she, however, being unable or not caring to answer him, allowed him to proceed, and he continued, “This I say, señor, because, if after we have travelled roads and highways, and passed bad nights and worse days, one who is now enjoying himself in this inn is to reap the fruit of our labours, there is no need for me to be in a hurry to saddle Rocinante, put the pad on the ass, or get ready the palfrey; for it will be better for us to stay quiet, and let every jade mind her spinning, and let us go to dinner.”","산초의 말에 도로테아의 얼굴이 붉어졌으니, 사실인즉 그녀의 남편 돈페르난도가 이따금 다른 이들이 보지 않을 때 그녀의 입술에서 제 사랑이 얻어 낸 보답의 일부를 거두어들였던 것이고, 이를 본 산초가 그런 스스럼없는 행동은 위대한 왕국의 여왕보다는 화류계 여인에게나 어울린다고 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 그에게 대꾸할 수 없었던 건지 아니면 대꾸하기를 개의치 않았던 건지, 그냥 계속 지껄이게 내버려 두었고, 그는 말을 이었다. \"제가 이런 말씀을 드리는 까닭은요, 나리, 우리가 큰길 작은 길 가리지 않고 두루 돌아다니고, 험한 밤과 더 험한 낮을 겪고 나서, 지금 이 객줏집에서 흥겹게 놀고 있는 어떤 자가 우리 수고의 열매를 거두어 갈 거라면, 제가 서둘러 로시난테에 안장을 얹고 나귀에 길마를 채우고 말을 채비할 필요가 없다는 거지요. 우리는 가만히 머무르면서, 갈보들은 제 실 잣는 일이나 신경 쓰게 두고, 우리는 밥이나 먹으러 가는 편이 더 나을 테니까요.\""]],[["Good God, what was the indignation of Don Quixote when he heard the audacious words of his squire!","맙소사, 자기 종자의 그 무엄한 말을 들었을 때 돈키호테의 분노가 어떠했겠는가!"],["So great was it, that in a voice inarticulate with rage, with a stammering tongue, and eyes that flashed living fire, he exclaimed, “Rascally clown, boorish, insolent, and ignorant, ill-spoken, foul-mouthed, impudent backbiter and slanderer!","어찌나 컸던지, 그는 분노로 말이 제대로 나오지 않는 목소리로, 혀를 더듬거리며, 생생한 불길이 번뜩이는 눈으로 외쳤다. \"이 못돼 먹은 촌놈, 무지렁이에다 건방지고 무식하며, 입버릇 고약하고 입 더러우며, 뻔뻔스러운 험담꾼이요 비방꾼 같으니라고!"],["Hast thou dared to utter such words in my presence and in that of these illustrious ladies?","네 감히 내 앞에서, 그리고 이 고귀하신 부인들 앞에서 그따위 말을 입에 담느냐?"],["Hast thou dared to harbour such gross and shameless thoughts in thy muddled imagination?","네 감히 그 흐리멍덩한 머릿속에 그토록 천하고 파렴치한 생각을 품는단 말이냐?"],["Begone from my presence, thou born monster, storehouse of lies, hoard of untruths, garner of knaveries, inventor of scandals, publisher of absurdities, enemy of the respect due to royal personages!","내 앞에서 썩 꺼지거라, 이 타고난 괴물 같은 놈, 거짓의 곳간이요 허위의 창고요 악행의 곡간이며, 추문을 지어내는 자요 망발을 퍼뜨리는 자요, 왕가에 마땅히 바칠 존경의 원수 같은 놈아!"],["Begone, show thyself no more before me under pain of my wrath;”","꺼져라, 내 노여움을 사기 싫거든 다시는 내 앞에 모습을 보이지 마라.\""],["and so saying he knitted his brows, puffed out his cheeks, gazed around him, and stamped on the ground violently with his right foot, showing in every way the rage that was pent up in his heart; and at his words and furious gestures Sancho was so scared and terrified that he would have been glad if the earth had opened that instant and swallowed him, and his only thought was to turn round and make his escape from the angry presence of his master.","이렇게 말하면서 그는 눈썹을 찌푸리고 두 볼을 부풀리며 주위를 둘러보더니, 오른발로 땅을 사납게 굴러, 가슴속에 응어리진 분노를 온갖 방식으로 드러냈다. 그의 말과 격분한 몸짓에 산초는 어찌나 겁에 질리고 두려웠던지, 그 순간 땅이 갈라져 자기를 삼켜 주었으면 좋겠다 싶을 지경이었으니, 그의 머릿속에는 오직 돌아서서 노여워하는 주인 앞에서 달아날 생각뿐이었다."]],[["But the ready-witted Dorothea, who by this time so well understood Don Quixote’s humour, said, to mollify his wrath, “Be not irritated at the absurdities your good squire has uttered, Sir Knight of the Rueful Countenance, for perhaps he did not utter them without cause, and from his good sense and Christian conscience it is not likely that he would bear false witness against anyone. We may therefore believe, without any hesitation, that since, as you say, sir knight, everything in this castle goes and is brought about by means of enchantment, Sancho, I say, may possibly have seen, through this diabolical medium, what he says he saw so much to the detriment of my modesty.”","그러나 이때쯤이면 돈키호테의 성미를 너무도 잘 헤아리고 있던 재치 있는 도로테아가 그의 노여움을 누그러뜨리려고 말했다. \"슬픈 몰골의 기사님, 충직한 종자가 내뱉은 망발에 노여워하지 마십시오. 어쩌면 그가 까닭 없이 그런 말을 한 것은 아닐 테고, 그의 건전한 분별력과 기독교도다운 양심으로 보아 누구에게든 거짓 증언을 할 사람 같지는 않으니 말입니다. 그러니 우리는 조금도 망설일 것 없이 이렇게 믿어도 좋을 것입니다. 기사님께서 말씀하신 대로 이 성안의 모든 일이 마법으로 행해지고 이루어지는 것이라면, 산초도 그 악마 같은 매개를 통해 제 정숙함을 그토록 해칠 만한 광경을 정말로 보았을 수도 있다고 말입니다.\""]],[["“I swear by God Omnipotent,” exclaimed Don Quixote at this, “your highness has hit the point; and that some vile illusion must have come before this sinner of a Sancho, that made him see what it would have been impossible to see by any other means than enchantments; for I know well enough, from the poor fellow’s goodness and harmlessness, that he is incapable of bearing false witness against anybody.”","\"전능하신 하느님께 맹세코,\" 이 말에 돈키호테가 외쳤다. \"공주님께서 정곡을 찌르셨습니다. 이 죄인 같은 산초 앞에 어떤 사악한 환영이 나타나, 마법이 아니고서는 도저히 볼 수 없었을 것을 그에게 보이게 한 게 분명합니다. 저 가엾은 녀석의 선량함과 무해함으로 보아, 그가 누구에게든 거짓 증언을 할 위인이 못 된다는 것을 저는 잘 알고 있으니 말입니다.\""]],[["“True, no doubt,”","\"의심할 바 없이 옳은 말씀이오.\""],["said Don Fernando, “for which reason, Señor Don Quixote, you ought to forgive him and restore him to the bosom of your favour, sicut erat in principio, before illusions of this sort had taken away his senses.”","돈페르난도가 말했다. \"그러니 돈키호테 님, 그대는 마땅히 그를 용서하고, 이런 종류의 환영이 그의 지각을 앗아 가기 전 그대로(시쿠트 에라트 인 프린키피오), 그를 그대의 은총의 품으로 되돌려 주셔야 할 것이오.\""]],[["Don Quixote said he was ready to pardon him, and the curate went for Sancho, who came in very humbly, and falling on his knees begged for the hand of his master, who having presented it to him and allowed him to kiss it, gave him his blessing and said, “Now, Sancho my son, thou wilt be convinced of the truth of what I have many a time told thee, that everything in this castle is done by means of enchantment.”","* 시쿠트 에라트 인 프린키피오(sicut erat in principio): 라틴어로 \"처음에 그러했던 것처럼\"이라는 뜻. 돈키호테가 그를 용서할 마음이 있다고 말하자, 신부가 산초를 데리러 갔다. 산초는 매우 겸손하게 들어와 무릎을 꿇고 주인의 손을 청했고, 주인은 그 손을 내밀어 입맞추게 한 다음, 그에게 축복을 내리며 말했다. \"자, 내 아들 산초야, 이제 너도 내가 너에게 여러 번 일러 준 말의 진실을 확신하게 될 게다. 이 성안의 모든 일은 마법으로 이루어진다는 것을 말이다.\""]],[["“So it is, I believe,”","\"그런 것 같습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho, “except the affair of the blanket, which came to pass in reality by ordinary means.”","\"다만 그 담요 키질 사건만은 빼고요. 그건 평범한 방법으로 실제로 일어난 일이었으니까요.\""]],[["“Believe it not,”","\"믿지 마라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “for had it been so, I would have avenged thee that instant, or even now; but neither then nor now could I, nor have I seen anyone upon whom to avenge thy wrong.”","\"만일 그게 그랬다면, 나는 그 순간에, 아니 지금이라도 네 원수를 갚아 주었을 게다. 하나 그때도 지금도 나는 그럴 수 없었고, 네 모욕을 갚아 줄 상대를 본 적도 없으니 말이다.\""]],[["They were all eager to know what the affair of the blanket was, and the landlord gave them a minute account of Sancho’s flights, at which they laughed not a little, and at which Sancho would have been no less out of countenance had not his master once more assured him it was all enchantment.","모두들 그 담요 키질 사건이 무엇인지 알고 싶어 했고, 주인장이 산초가 공중에 키질당하던 이야기를 시시콜콜 들려주자 다들 적잖이 웃었다. 만일 주인이 다시 한번 그 모든 것이 마법이라고 단언해 주지 않았더라면 산초도 그에 못지않게 무안했을 터였다."],["For all that his simplicity never reached so high a pitch that he could persuade himself it was not the plain and simple truth, without any deception whatever about it, that he had been blanketed by beings of flesh and blood, and not by visionary and imaginary phantoms, as his master believed and protested.","그러나 산초의 단순함이 결코 그렇게까지 높은 경지에 이르지는 못하여, 자기가 주인이 믿고 우기는 대로 환영이나 상상 속의 유령들이 아니라 살과 피를 가진 자들에게 담요 키질을 당했다는 것이, 아무런 속임수도 끼어들지 않은 명백하고 단순한 진실이 아니라고 스스로를 납득시킬 수는 없었다."]],[["The illustrious company had now been two days in the inn; and as it seemed to them time to depart, they devised a plan so that, without giving Dorothea and Don Fernando the trouble of going back with Don Quixote to his village under pretence of restoring Queen Micomicona, the curate and the barber might carry him away with them as they proposed, and the curate be able to take his madness in hand at home; and in pursuance of their plan they arranged with the owner of an oxcart who happened to be passing that way to carry him after this fashion. They constructed a kind of cage with wooden bars, large enough to hold Don Quixote comfortably; and then Don Fernando and his companions, the servants of Don Luis, and the officers of the Brotherhood, together with the landlord, by the directions and advice of the curate, covered their faces and disguised themselves, some in one way, some in another, so as to appear to Don Quixote quite different from the persons he had seen in the castle. This done, in profound silence they entered the room where he was asleep, taking his his rest after the past frays, and advancing to where he was sleeping tranquilly, not dreaming of anything of the kind happening, they seized him firmly and bound him fast hand and foot, so that, when he awoke startled, he was unable to move, and could only marvel and wonder at the strange figures he saw before him; upon which he at once gave way to the idea which his crazed fancy invariably conjured up before him, and took it into his head that all these shapes were phantoms of the enchanted castle, and that he himself was unquestionably enchanted as he could neither move nor help himself; precisely what the curate, the concoctor of the scheme, expected would happen. Of all that were there Sancho was the only one who was at once in his senses and in his own proper character, and he, though he was within very little of sharing his master’s infirmity, did not fail to perceive who all these disguised figures were; but he did not dare to open his lips until he saw what came of this assault and capture of his master; nor did the latter utter a word, waiting to the upshot of his mishap; which was that bringing in the cage, they shut him up in it and nailed the bars so firmly that they could not be easily burst open.","이 고귀한 일행은 이제 객줏집에서 이틀을 묵었고, 떠날 때가 된 듯 여겨지자, 도로테아와 돈페르난도가 미코미코나 여왕을 복위시킨다는 구실로 돈키호테와 함께 그의 마을까지 되돌아가는 수고를 끼치지 않으면서도, 신부와 이발사가 애초에 작정했던 대로 그를 데려가, 신부가 집에서 그의 광증을 다스릴 수 있도록 하는 계획을 궁리해 냈다. 그리하여 그 계획에 따라, 마침 그 길로 지나가던 어느 소달구지 주인과 흥정하여 이런 식으로 그를 운반하기로 했다. 그들은 나무 살창으로 우리 같은 것을 만들었는데, 돈키호테를 넉넉히 들어앉힐 만큼 컸다. 그러고 나서 돈페르난도와 그 일행, 돈루이스의 하인들, 성동포단 관원들, 그리고 주인장까지, 신부의 지시와 조언에 따라 얼굴을 가리고 저마다 이렇게 저렇게 변장을 하여, 돈키호테에게는 성안에서 보았던 사람들과는 전혀 딴판으로 보이게 했다. 이렇게 한 다음, 그들은 깊은 침묵 속에서 그가 지난번 싸움들을 치른 뒤 잠들어 쉬고 있는 방으로 들어가, 그가 이런 일이 벌어지리라곤 꿈에도 모른 채 평온히 잠든 곳으로 다가가서, 그를 단단히 붙잡아 손발을 꽁꽁 묶었다. 하여 그가 깜짝 놀라 깨어났을 때는 꼼짝달싹할 수 없었고, 그저 눈앞에 보이는 괴이한 형상들에 어리둥절해 의아해할 따름이었다. 이에 그는 곧장, 그의 미친 상상이 어김없이 떠올리던 그 생각에 빠져들어, 이 모든 형상이 마법에 걸린 성의 유령들이며, 자기 자신도 움직일 수도 어쩔 수도 없으니 의심할 바 없이 마법에 걸린 것이라고 머릿속에 단정해 버렸다. 그것이야말로 이 계략을 꾸민 신부가 일어나리라 예상했던 바로 그것이었다. 거기 있던 모든 이 가운데 산초만이 유일하게 제정신이요 제 본래 성품 그대로였는데, 그는 비록 주인의 병에 거의 함께 빠져들 뻔하긴 했어도, 이 변장한 형상들이 모두 누구인지 알아채지 못하지는 않았다. 그러나 그는 주인을 이렇게 습격하고 사로잡은 일이 어찌 되는지 지켜볼 때까지는 감히 입을 떼지 못했고, 주인 역시 자기에게 닥친 재난의 결말을 기다리며 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그 결말인즉, 그들이 우리를 들여와 그를 그 안에 가두고 살창을 어찌나 단단히 못 박았던지 쉬이 부수고 나올 수 없게 만든 것이었다."]],[["They then took him on their shoulders, and as they passed out of the room an awful voice--as much so as the barber, not he of the pack-saddle but the other, was able to make it--was heard to say, “O Knight of the Rueful Countenance, let not this captivity in which thou art placed afflict thee, for this must needs be, for the more speedy accomplishment of the adventure in which thy great heart has engaged thee; the which shall be accomplished when the raging Manchegan lion and the white Tobosan dove shall be linked together, having first humbled their haughty necks to the gentle yoke of matrimony. And from this marvellous union shall come forth to the light of the world brave whelps that shall rival the ravening claws of their valiant father; and this shall come to pass ere the pursuer of the flying nymph shall in his swift natural course have twice visited the starry signs. And thou, O most noble and obedient squire that ever bore sword at side, beard on face, or nose to smell with, be not dismayed or grieved to see the flower of knight-errantry carried away thus before thy very eyes; for soon, if it so please the Framer of the universe, thou shalt see thyself exalted to such a height that thou shalt not know thyself, and the promises which thy good master has made thee shall not prove false; and I assure thee, on the authority of the sage Mentironiana, that thy wages shall be paid thee, as thou shalt see in due season. Follow then the footsteps of the valiant enchanted knight, for it is expedient that thou shouldst go to the destination assigned to both of you; and as it is not permitted to me to say more, God be with thee; for I return to that place I wot of;” and as he brought the prophecy to a close he raised his voice to a high pitch, and then lowered it to such a soft tone, that even those who knew it was all a joke were almost inclined to take what they heard seriously.","그러고 나서 그들이 그를 어깨에 둘러메고 방을 나서는데, 무시무시한 목소리가 — 길마 다툼을 벌인 그 이발사가 아니라 다른 이발사가 낼 수 있는 한껏 무시무시한 목소리로 — 이렇게 말하는 것이 들려왔다. \"오, 슬픈 몰골의 기사여, 그대가 처한 이 사로잡힘을 괴로워하지 말지어다. 이는 그대의 위대한 가슴이 떠맡은 모험을 더 빨리 이루기 위하여 마땅히 그리되어야 하는 일이니라. 그 모험은 사납게 날뛰는 라만차의 사자와 새하얀 토보소의 비둘기가, 먼저 그 거만한 목을 혼인의 부드러운 멍에에 숙인 다음 서로 맺어질 때 이루어지리라. 그리고 이 경이로운 결합에서 용맹한 아비의 게걸스러운 발톱에 견줄 만한 늠름한 새끼들이 세상의 빛 속으로 태어나리라. 또 이는 달아나는 요정을 쫓는 자가 그 빠른 천체의 운행으로 별자리들을 두 번 거듭 찾기도 전에 이루어지리라. 그리고 그대여, 일찍이 옆구리에 칼을 차고 얼굴에 수염을 기르고 냄새 맡을 코를 지녔던 그 누구보다도 고귀하고 순종하는 종자여, 편력 기사도의 꽃이 그대의 바로 눈앞에서 이렇게 실려 가는 것을 보더라도 낙담하거나 슬퍼하지 말지어다. 머지않아, 만물을 빚으신 분께서 그리 기꺼워하신다면, 그대는 그대 자신도 알아보지 못할 만큼 높은 자리에 오른 그대 자신을 보게 될 것이요, 그대의 훌륭한 주인이 그대에게 한 약속들도 헛되지 않으리라. 또한 나는 현자 멘티로니아나의 권위를 빌려 그대에게 단언하노니, 그대의 품삯은 때가 되면 그대 눈으로 보게 되듯 어김없이 치러지리라. 그러니 그 용맹하고 마법에 걸린 기사의 발자취를 따르라. 그대 두 사람에게 정해진 행선지로 그대가 가는 것이 마땅하니 말이다. 내게 더 말하는 것이 허락되지 않았으므로, 하느님께서 그대와 함께하시기를. 나는 내가 아는 그곳으로 돌아가노라.\" 그러고는 예언을 끝맺으면서 목소리를 높은 가락으로 끌어올렸다가, 다시 어찌나 부드러운 어조로 낮추었던지, 이 모든 것이 한낱 장난임을 아는 이들조차 들리는 말을 진지하게 받아들이고픈 마음이 들 지경이었다."]],[["Don Quixote was comforted by the prophecy he heard, for he at once comprehended its meaning perfectly, and perceived it was promised to him that he should see himself united in holy and lawful matrimony with his beloved Dulcinea del Toboso, from whose blessed womb should proceed the whelps, his sons, to the eternal glory of La Mancha; and being thoroughly and firmly persuaded of this, he lifted up his voice, and with a deep sigh exclaimed, “Oh thou, whoever thou art, who hast foretold me so much good, I implore of thee that on my part thou entreat that sage enchanter who takes charge of my interests, that he leave me not to perish in this captivity in which they are now carrying me away, ere I see fulfilled promises so joyful and incomparable as those which have been now made me; for, let this but come to pass, and I shall glory in the pains of my prison, find comfort in these chains wherewith they bind me, and regard this bed whereon they stretch me, not as a hard battle-field, but as a soft and happy nuptial couch; and touching the consolation of Sancho Panza, my squire, I rely upon his goodness and rectitude that he will not desert me in good or evil fortune; for if, by his ill luck or mine, it may not happen to be in my power to give him the island I have promised, or any equivalent for it, at least his wages shall not be lost; for in my will, which is already made, I have declared the sum that shall be paid to him, measured, not by his many faithful services, but by the means at my disposal.”","돈키호테는 들려온 예언에 위로를 받았으니, 곧장 그 뜻을 완벽하게 헤아렸기 때문이다. 그는 자기가 사랑하는 토보소의 둘시네아와 거룩하고 합법적인 혼인으로 맺어진 자신을 보게 되리라는 것, 그리고 그 복된 태에서 새끼들, 곧 그의 아들들이 나와 라만차의 영원한 영광이 되리라는 것을 약속받았음을 알아차렸다. 이를 철저하고 굳건하게 확신한 그는 목소리를 높여 깊은 한숨과 함께 외쳤다. \"오, 그대가 누구이든, 내게 그토록 많은 길사를 예언해 준 그대여, 그대에게 간청하노니, 나를 대신하여 내 안위를 맡아 보살피는 저 현명한 마법사에게 청해 주시오. 지금 그들이 나를 실어 가는 이 사로잡힘 속에서, 방금 내게 주어진 그토록 기쁘고 비할 데 없는 약속들이 이루어지는 것을 내가 보기도 전에, 그가 나를 죽도록 내버려 두지 말기를. 그것만 이루어진다면, 나는 이 감옥의 고통을 영광으로 여기고, 나를 묶은 이 사슬에서 위안을 찾으며, 그들이 나를 뉘어 놓은 이 잠자리도 딱딱한 싸움터가 아니라 부드럽고 행복한 신혼의 잠자리로 여길 것이오. 또 내 종자 산초 판사의 위안으로 말하자면, 나는 그의 선량함과 정직함을 믿으니, 그가 좋은 운에서든 나쁜 운에서든 나를 저버리지 않으리라. 설령 그의 불운으로든 나의 불운으로든, 내가 그에게 약속한 섬이나 그에 맞먹는 무엇을 줄 수 없게 되더라도, 적어도 그의 품삯만은 잃지 않을 것이니, 이미 작성해 둔 내 유언장에, 그의 수많은 충직한 봉사가 아니라 내가 가진 재력에 맞추어 그에게 치러야 할 액수를 밝혀 두었기 때문이오.\""]],[["Sancho bowed his head very respectfully and kissed both his hands, for, being tied together, he could not kiss one; and then the apparitions lifted the cage upon their shoulders and fixed it upon the ox-cart.","산초는 매우 공손하게 고개를 숙이고 두 손에 입을 맞추었으니, 두 손이 함께 묶여 있어 한 손에만 입맞출 수는 없었던 까닭이다. 그러고 나서 그 유령들이 우리를 어깨에 둘러메어 소달구지 위에 고정시켰다."]],[["# CHAPTER XLVII. OF THE STRANGE MANNER IN WHICH DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA WAS CARRIED AWAY ENCHANTED, TOGETHER WITH OTHER REMARKABLE INCIDENTS","# CHAPTER XLVII."]],[["When Don Quixote saw himself caged and hoisted on the cart in this way, he said, “Many grave histories of knights-errant have I read; but never yet have I read, seen, or heard of their carrying off enchanted knights-errant in this fashion, or at the slow pace that these lazy, sluggish animals promise; for they always take them away through the air with marvellous swiftness, enveloped in a dark thick cloud, or on a chariot of fire, or it may be on some hippogriff or other beast of the kind; but to carry me off like this on an ox-cart!","돈키호테가 이런 식으로 우리에 갇혀 수레 위에 올려진 자기 모습을 보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"편력 기사에 관한 진중한 역사를 나는 숱하게 읽어 왔으나, 마법에 걸린 편력 기사를 이런 식으로, 또 이 게으르고 굼뜬 짐승들이 약속하는 이런 느린 걸음으로 데려갔다는 이야기는 읽은 적도, 본 적도, 들은 적도 없도다."],["By God, it puzzles me!","본디 마법에 걸린 기사는 언제나 신비로운 신속함으로 검고 짙은 구름에 휩싸인 채 공중을 가로질러, 혹은 불의 전차에 실려, 어쩌면 어떤 히포그리프나 그와 비슷한 짐승에 태워져 끌려가는 법이거늘, 나를 이렇게 황소 수레에 실어 데려가다니!"],["But perhaps the chivalry and enchantments of our day take a different course from that of those in days gone by; and it may be, too, that as I am a new knight in the world, and the first to revive the already forgotten calling of knight-adventurers, they may have newly invented other kinds of enchantments and other modes of carrying off the enchanted.","맙소사, 도무지 알 수가 없구나! 그러나 어쩌면 오늘날의 기사도와 마법은 지난날의 그것과는 다른 길을 따르는지도 모를 일이며, 또 내가 이 세상의 새로운 기사이자 이미 잊힌 모험 기사라는 소명을 되살린 첫 번째 사람이기에, 어쩌면 그들이 새로이 다른 종류의 마법과 마법에 걸린 자를 데려가는 다른 방식을 고안해 낸 것인지도 모르지."],["What thinkest thou of the matter, Sancho my son?”","산초야, 너는 이 일을 어찌 생각하느냐?\""]],[["“I don’t know what to think,”","\"무얼 어떻게 생각해야 할지 모르겠습니다요.\" 산초가 대답했다."],["answered Sancho, “not being as well read as your worship in errant writings; but for all that I venture to say and swear that these apparitions that are about us are not quite catholic.”","\"저야 나리만큼 편력 기사에 관한 글을 두루 읽지 못했으니까요. 하지만 그래도 한마디 감히 말씀드리고 또 맹세컨대, 우리를 에워싸고 있는 이 유령들은 그리 가톨릭답지가 못합니다요.\""]],[["“Catholic!”","\"가톨릭답다고!\""],["said Don Quixote.","돈키호테가 말했다."],["“Father of me!","\"이런 한심한 녀석 같으니!"],["how can they be Catholic when they are all devils that have taken fantastic shapes to come and do this, and bring me to this condition?","저것들이 어찌 가톨릭답겠느냐? 저것들은 모두 환상 같은 형상을 뒤집어쓰고 와서 이런 짓을 벌이며 나를 이 지경으로 몰아넣은 악마들인 것을."],["And if thou wouldst prove it, touch them, and feel them, and thou wilt find they have only bodies of air, and no consistency except in appearance.”","정 그걸 증명하고 싶거든 저것들을 만져 보고 더듬어 보거라. 그러면 저것들이 한낱 공기로 된 몸뚱이뿐이며, 겉모습 말고는 아무 실체도 없음을 알게 되리라.\""]],[["“By God, master,”","\"맙소사, 나리.\" 산초가 대꾸했다."],["returned Sancho, “I have touched them already; and that devil, that goes about there so busily, has firm flesh, and another property very different from what I have heard say devils have, for by all accounts they all smell of brimstone and other bad smells; but this one smells of amber half a league off.”","\"저는 벌써 그것들을 만져 보았습니다요. 그런데 저기서 저리도 분주히 돌아다니는 저 악마는 단단한 살을 지녔고, 게다가 제가 악마에 대해 들어 온 것과는 아주 딴판인 성질을 하나 가지고 있습니다요."],["Sancho was here speaking of Don Fernando, who, like a gentleman of his rank, was very likely perfumed as Sancho said.","듣자 하니 악마들은 죄다 유황 냄새며 온갖 고약한 냄새를 풍긴다는데, 저 녀석은 반 레과 밖에서도 호박 향을 풍기지 뭡니까요.\" 산초가 여기서 말한 것은 돈 페르난도를 두고 한 것이었으니, 그는 그만한 신분의 신사답게 산초가 말한 대로 향수를 뿌렸을 법했다."]],[["“Marvel not at that, Sancho my friend,” said Don Quixote; “for let me tell thee devils are crafty; and even if they do carry odours about with them, they themselves have no smell, because they are spirits; or, if they have any smell, they cannot smell of anything sweet, but of something foul and fetid; and the reason is that as they carry hell with them wherever they go, and can get no ease whatever from their torments, and as a sweet smell is a thing that gives pleasure and enjoyment, it is impossible that they can smell sweet; if, then, this devil thou speakest of seems to thee to smell of amber, either thou art deceiving thyself, or he wants to deceive thee by making thee fancy he is not a devil.”","\"그것을 이상히 여기지 마라, 내 벗 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 말했다. \"내 너에게 일러두건대, 악마들이란 교활한 것들이라, 설령 향기를 몸에 지니고 다닌다 해도 그것들 자체는 아무 냄새도 없으니, 그것들은 영혼이기 때문이니라. 혹 냄새가 난다 해도 무언가 향긋한 냄새가 날 수는 없고 무언가 더럽고 역겨운 냄새가 날 따름이니, 그 까닭은 이러하다. 그것들은 어디를 가든 지옥을 함께 짊어지고 다니며 그 고통에서 조금도 벗어날 길이 없는데, 향긋한 냄새란 즐거움과 기쁨을 주는 것인즉, 그것들이 향긋한 냄새를 풍긴다는 것은 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이로다. 그러니 네가 말하는 저 악마에게서 호박 향이 나는 듯이 여겨진다면, 네가 스스로를 속이고 있거나, 아니면 그가 제 딴에는 악마가 아니라고 너로 하여금 믿게 하여 너를 속이려는 것이니라.\""]],[["Such was the conversation that passed between master and man; and Don Fernando and Cardenio, apprehensive of Sancho’s making a complete discovery of their scheme, towards which he had already gone some way, resolved to hasten their departure, and calling the landlord aside, they directed him to saddle Rocinante and put the pack-saddle on Sancho’s ass, which he did with great alacrity.","주인과 종 사이에 오간 대화가 이러했다. 한편 돈 페르난도와 카르데니오는 산초가 이미 어느 정도 눈치를 챈 터라 자기들의 계략을 완전히 들춰낼까 염려하여 서둘러 출발하기로 마음먹고, 주막 주인을 한쪽으로 불러 로시난테에 안장을 얹고 산초의 당나귀에 길마를 지우라 일렀더니, 그가 매우 기민하게 그 일을 해치웠다."],["In the meantime the curate had made an arrangement with the officers that they should bear them company as far as his village, he paying them so much a day.","그사이 신부는 관원들과 약조를 맺어, 하루에 얼마씩을 지불하는 조건으로 그들이 자기 마을까지 동행하게 하였다."],["Cardenio hung the buckler on one side of the bow of Rocinante’s saddle and the basin on the other, and by signs commanded Sancho to mount his ass and take Rocinante’s bridle, and at each side of the cart he placed two officers with their muskets; but before the cart was put in motion, out came the landlady and her daughter and Maritornes to bid Don Quixote farewell, pretending to weep with grief at his misfortune; and to them Don Quixote said:","카르데니오는 로시난테 안장의 앞고리 한쪽에 작은 방패를, 다른 쪽에 대야를 매달고는, 산초에게 손짓으로 명하여 당나귀에 올라타 로시난테의 고삐를 잡게 하였으며, 수레 양옆에는 머스켓 총을 든 관원 둘씩을 배치하였다. 그러나 수레가 움직이기에 앞서 주막 안주인과 그 딸, 그리고 마리토르네스가 나와 돈키호테에게 작별을 고하면서, 그의 불운을 슬퍼하는 척 눈물을 흘리는 시늉을 하니, 돈키호테가 그들에게 이렇게 말했다."]],[["“Weep not, good ladies, for all these mishaps are the lot of those who follow the profession I profess; and if these reverses did not befall me I should not esteem myself a famous knight-errant; for such things never happen to knights of little renown and fame, because nobody in the world thinks about them; to valiant knights they do, for these are envied for their virtue and valour by many princes and other knights who compass the destruction of the worthy by base means. Nevertheless, virtue is of herself so mighty, that, in spite of all the magic that Zoroaster its first inventor knew, she will come victorious out of every trial, and shed her light upon the earth as the sun does upon the heavens. Forgive me, fair ladies, if, through inadvertence, I have in aught offended you; for intentionally and wittingly I have never done so to any; and pray to God that he deliver me from this captivity to which some malevolent enchanter has consigned me; and should I find myself released therefrom, the favours that ye have bestowed upon me in this castle shall be held in memory by me, that I may acknowledge, recognise, and requite them as they deserve.”","\"울지 마시오, 착한 부인들이여, 이 모든 재난은 내가 받드는 이 직업을 따르는 자들의 운명이오. 만일 이러한 역경이 내게 닥치지 않는다면 나는 스스로를 이름난 편력 기사라 여기지 못할 것이오. 명성도 이름도 변변찮은 기사에게는 이런 일이 결코 일어나지 않으니, 세상의 그 누구도 그런 자들은 마음에 두지 않기 때문이오. 그러나 용맹한 기사에게는 이런 일이 일어나는 법이니, 그들의 덕과 용맹을 시기하는 숱한 군주와 다른 기사들이 비열한 수단으로 훌륭한 자들을 파멸시키려 꾀하는 까닭이오. 그럼에도 덕이란 그 자체로 너무도 강대하여, 그 최초의 창시자인 조로아스터가 알았던 온갖 마법에도 불구하고 모든 시련에서 승리하여 나올 것이며, 태양이 하늘에 빛을 뿌리듯 땅 위에 그 빛을 뿌리리다. 아리따운 부인들이여, 혹 내가 부주의로 무엇이든 그대들의 마음을 상하게 한 일이 있다면 부디 용서하시오. 의도적으로 알고서 그런 짓을 한 적은 누구에게도 결코 없었으니 말이오. 그리고 어느 사악한 마법사가 나를 가두어 놓은 이 속박에서 하느님께서 나를 구해 주시기를 기도해 주시오. 만일 내가 여기서 풀려나게 된다면, 그대들이 이 성에서 내게 베풀어 준 은혜를 마음에 새겨 두었다가, 그것이 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼 알아주고 갚아 드리리다.\""]],[["While this was passing between the ladies of the castle and Don Quixote, the curate and the barber bade farewell to Don Fernando and his companions, to the captain, his brother, and the ladies, now all made happy, and in particular to Dorothea and Luscinda.","성의 부인들과 돈키호테 사이에 이런 일이 오가는 동안, 신부와 이발사는 돈 페르난도와 그 일행에게, 그리고 대위와 그 형제와 부인들에게, 또 이제 모두가 행복하게 된 그들에게, 특히 도로테아와 루신다에게 작별을 고하였다."],["They all embraced one another, and promised to let each other know how things went with them, and Don Fernando directed the curate where to write to him, to tell him what became of Don Quixote, assuring him that there was nothing that could give him more pleasure than to hear of it, and that he too, on his part, would send him word of everything he thought he would like to know, about his marriage, Zoraida’s baptism, Don Luis’s affair, and Luscinda’s return to her home.","그들은 모두 서로 부둥켜안고, 앞으로 일이 어찌 되어 가는지 서로에게 알리기로 약속하였으며, 돈 페르난도는 돈키호테가 어찌 되었는지 알려 달라고 신부에게 자기에게 편지를 보낼 곳을 일러 주면서, 그 소식을 듣는 것보다 더 기쁜 일은 없으리라 장담하였고, 자기 또한 그의 결혼이며 소라이다의 세례, 돈 루이스의 일, 루신다가 친정으로 돌아간 일 등, 신부가 알고 싶어 할 만한 모든 일을 빠짐없이 전해 주겠노라 하였다."],["The curate promised to comply with his request carefully, and they embraced once more, and renewed their promises.","신부는 그 청을 빈틈없이 따르겠노라 약속하였고, 그들은 다시 한 번 부둥켜안고 약속을 거듭 다졌다."]],[["The landlord approached the curate and handed him some papers, saying he had discovered them in the lining of the valise in which the novel of “The Ill-advised Curiosity”","주막 주인이 신부에게 다가와 종이 몇 장을 건네면서, 「무모한 호기심」이라는 소설이 들어 있던 가방의 안감 속에서 이것들을 발견했는데, 주인이 그 뒤로 돌아오지 않았으니 신부가 죄다 가져가도 된다고 말했다. 자기는 글을 읽을 줄 모르니 그것들이 필요 없다는 것이었다."],["had been found, and that he might take them all away with him as their owner had not since returned; for, as he could not read, he did not want them himself. The curate thanked him, and opening them he saw at the beginning of the manuscript the words, “Novel of Rinconete and Cortadillo,”","신부는 그에게 고마움을 표하고 그것들을 펼쳐 보니, 원고 첫머리에 \"린코네테와 코르타디요 이야기\"라는 글자가 적혀 있어 그것이 소설임을 알아차렸고, 「무모한 호기심」이 훌륭했던 만큼 이것도 그러하리라 짐작하였으니, 둘 다 아마도 같은 작가의 작품일 듯하였기 때문이다."],["by which he perceived that it was a novel, and as that of “The Ill-advised Curiosity”","그리하여 그는 그것을 챙겨 두었다가 틈이 나면 읽으려 하였다."],["had been good he concluded this would be so too, as they were both probably by the same author; so he kept it, intending to read it when he had an opportunity.","그러고 나서 그는 말에 올랐고, 그의 벗 이발사도 똑같이 하였는데, 둘 다 돈키호테에게 알아보이지 않으려 얼굴을 가리고서 수레의 뒤를 따라 나섰다."],["He then mounted and his friend the barber did the same, both masked, so as not to be recognised by Don Quixote, and set out following in the rear of the cart.","행렬의 순서는 이러하였다."],["The order of march was this: first went the cart with the owner leading it; at each side of it marched the officers of the Brotherhood, as has been said, with their muskets; then followed Sancho Panza on his ass, leading Rocinante by the bridle; and behind all came the curate and the barber on their mighty mules, with faces covered, as aforesaid, and a grave and serious air, measuring their pace to suit the slow steps of the oxen.","맨 앞에는 주인이 끄는 수레가 가고, 그 양옆에는 앞서 말한 대로 머스켓 총을 든 성동포단(聖同胞團)의 관원들이 행진하였으며, 그 뒤로는 산초 판사가 당나귀를 타고 로시난테의 고삐를 잡고 따랐고, 맨 뒤에는 신부와 이발사가 앞서 말한 대로 얼굴을 가린 채, 진중하고 엄숙한 자태로 그 늠름한 노새에 올라타, 황소들의 느린 걸음에 보조를 맞추어 따라왔다."],["Don Quixote was seated in the cage, with his hands tied and his feet stretched out, leaning against the bars as silent and as patient as if he were a stone statue and not a man of flesh.","돈키호테는 두 손이 묶이고 두 발을 쭉 뻗은 채 우리 안에 앉아, 마치 살과 피로 된 사람이 아니라 돌로 된 조각상이기라도 한 양 말없이 참을성 있게 창살에 기대어 있었다."],["Thus slowly and silently they made, it might be, two leagues, until they reached a valley which the carter thought a convenient place for resting and feeding his oxen, and he said so to the curate, but the barber was of opinion that they ought to push on a little farther, as at the other side of a hill which appeared close by he knew there was a valley that had more grass and much better than the one where they proposed to halt; and his advice was taken and they continued their journey.","이렇게 느릿느릿 말없이 그들은 두 레과쯤 나아가, 마침내 어느 골짜기에 이르렀는데, 수레 주인이 보기에 그곳이 쉬면서 황소들에게 먹이를 줄 만한 자리인 듯하여 그렇게 신부에게 말하였으나, 이발사의 생각으로는 조금 더 나아가야 할 듯하였으니, 가까이 보이는 언덕 너머에 그들이 멈추려는 곳보다 풀이 더 많고 훨씬 좋은 골짜기가 있음을 그가 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그의 조언이 받아들여져 그들은 길을 계속 갔다."]],[["Just at that moment the curate, looking back, saw coming on behind them six or seven mounted men, well found and equipped, who soon overtook them, for they were travelling, not at the sluggish, deliberate pace of oxen, but like men who rode canons’ mules, and in haste to take their noontide rest as soon as possible at the inn which was in sight not a league off.","바로 그때 신부가 뒤를 돌아보니, 잘 갖추어 입고 채비를 단단히 한 예닐곱 명의 말 탄 사람들이 뒤따라오고 있었는데, 그들은 황소의 굼뜨고 느릿한 걸음이 아니라 참사회원의 노새를 탄 사람들답게 달려왔으니, 한 레과도 채 안 되는 곳에 보이는 주막에서 한시바삐 한낮의 휴식을 취하려고 서두르는 까닭에 이내 그들을 따라잡았다. 발 빠른 나그네들이 느린 일행을 따라잡자 정중한 인사가 오갔는데, 새로 온 이들 가운데 하나는 실은 톨레도의 참사회원으로서 그를 수행하는 다른 이들의 우두머리였으니, 그가 그 행렬의 정연한 차림새며, 수레와 관원들과 산초와 로시난테와 신부와 이발사, 그리고 무엇보다 우리에 갇혀 결박당한 돈키호테를 살펴보고는, 저 사람을 어찌하여 저런 식으로 데려가는지 그 까닭을 묻지 않을 수 없었다."],["The quick travellers came up with the slow, and courteous salutations were exchanged; and one of the new comers, who was, in fact, a canon of Toledo and master of the others who accompanied him, observing the regular order of the procession, the cart, the officers, Sancho, Rocinante, the curate and the barber, and above all Don Quixote caged and confined, could not help asking what was the meaning of carrying the man in that fashion; though, from the badges of the officers, he already concluded that he must be some desperate highwayman or other malefactor whose punishment fell within the jurisdiction of the Holy Brotherhood.","다만 관원들의 휘장으로 미루어 그는 이미 저 사람이 처벌이 성동포단의 관할에 속하는 어떤 막무가내 노상강도이거나 다른 흉악범이려니 짐작하고 있었다. 그가 물음을 던진 관원 가운데 하나가 대답하였다."],["One of the officers to whom he had put the question, replied, “Let the gentleman himself tell you the meaning of his going this way, señor, for we do not know.”","\"나리, 저 양반이 어찌하여 이런 식으로 끌려가는지는 저 양반 본인더러 말하게 하십시오. 저희는 모릅니다.\""]],[["Don Quixote overheard the conversation and said, “Haply, gentlemen, you are versed and learned in matters of errant chivalry?","돈키호테가 그 말을 엿듣고 말했다. \"여러분, 혹 편력 기사도의 일에 정통하고 박식하신지요?"],["Because if you are I will tell you my misfortunes; if not, there is no good in my giving myself the trouble of relating them;”","만일 그러하시다면 제 불운을 들려드리겠으나, 그렇지 않다면 굳이 수고로이 그것을 늘어놓아 봐야 아무 소용이 없을 테니 말입니다.\""],["but here the curate and the barber, seeing that the travellers were engaged in conversation with Don Quixote, came forward, in order to answer in such a way as to save their stratagem from being discovered.","그러나 이때 신부와 이발사는 나그네들이 돈키호테와 대화를 나누는 것을 보고는, 자기들의 계략이 발각되지 않도록 적당히 대답하려고 앞으로 나섰다."]],[["The canon, replying to Don Quixote, said, “In truth, brother, I know more about books of chivalry than I do about Villalpando’s elements of logic; so if that be all, you may safely tell me what you please.”","참사회원이 돈키호테에게 대답하여 말했다. \"사실, 형제여, 나는 비얄판도의 논리학 입문서보다 기사도 책에 대해 더 많이 알고 있소. 그러니 그게 전부라면 무엇이든 안심하고 내게 말씀하셔도 좋소.\""]],[["“In God’s name, then, señor,” replied Don Quixote; “if that be so, I would have you know that I am held enchanted in this cage by the envy and fraud of wicked enchanters; for virtue is more persecuted by the wicked than loved by the good. I am a knight-errant, and not one of those whose names Fame has never thought of immortalising in her record, but of those who, in defiance and in spite of envy itself, and all the magicians that Persia, or Brahmans that India, or Gymnosophists that Ethiopia ever produced, will place their names in the temple of immortality, to serve as examples and patterns for ages to come, whereby knights-errant may see the footsteps in which they must tread if they would attain the summit and crowning point of honour in arms.”","\"그렇다면, 나리, 하느님의 이름으로 말씀드리겠습니다.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"정 그러하시다면 알아 두십시오. 저는 사악한 마법사들의 시기와 술수로 인해 이 우리에 마법으로 갇혀 있는 것입니다. 덕이란 선한 자들에게 사랑받는 것보다 악한 자들에게 핍박받는 일이 더 많은 법이니까요. 저는 편력 기사이되, 명성이 그 기록에 결코 불멸로 남길 생각조차 하지 않은 그런 자들의 하나가 아니라, 시기 그 자체와, 페르시아가 낳은 온갖 마법사며 인도가 낳은 브라만이며 에티오피아가 낳은 나체 수행자들 모두에게 맞서고 또 그들을 무릅쓰고서, 다가올 시대를 위한 본보기와 귀감이 되도록 불멸의 신전에 제 이름을 새길 그런 자들의 하나입니다. 그리하여 편력 기사들이 무예에서 명예의 정점이자 최고의 영광에 이르고자 한다면 마땅히 밟아야 할 발자취를 거기서 보게 할 것입니다.\""]],[["“What Señor Don Quixote of La Mancha says,”","\"라만차의 돈키호테 나리께서 하시는 말씀은,\" 신부가 말했다."],["observed the curate, “is the truth; for he goes enchanted in this cart, not from any fault or sins of his, but because of the malevolence of those to whom virtue is odious and valour hateful.","\"사실입니다. 그분은 어떤 잘못이나 죄 때문이 아니라, 덕을 가증스러워하고 용맹을 미워하는 자들의 악의 때문에 이 수레에 마법으로 갇혀 가시는 것입니다."],["This, señor, is the Knight of the Rueful Countenance, if you have ever heard him named, whose valiant achievements and mighty deeds shall be written on lasting brass and imperishable marble, notwithstanding all the efforts of envy to obscure them and malice to hide them.”","나리, 이분이 바로 슬픈 몰골의 기사이시니, 혹 그 이름을 들어 보신 적이 있다면 아실 것입니다. 그분의 용맹한 위업과 위대한 행적은, 그것을 가리려는 시기와 숨기려는 악의의 온갖 노력에도 불구하고, 길이 남을 청동과 썩지 않을 대리석에 새겨질 것입니다.\""]],[["When the canon heard both the prisoner and the man who was at liberty talk in such a strain he was ready to cross himself in his astonishment, and could not make out what had befallen him; and all his attendants were in the same state of amazement.","참사회원은 갇힌 자와 풀려난 자가 다 같이 이런 투로 말하는 것을 듣고는 놀란 나머지 성호를 그을 지경이었으며, 자기에게 무슨 일이 닥쳤는지 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었다. 그를 수행하던 자들도 모두 똑같이 어리둥절한 상태였다."]],[["At this point Sancho Panza, who had drawn near to hear the conversation, said, in order to make everything plain, “Well, sirs, you may like or dislike what I am going to say, but the fact of the matter is, my master, Don Quixote, is just as much enchanted as my mother.","이때 대화를 들으려고 가까이 다가와 있던 산초 판사가, 모든 것을 분명히 밝히려고 이렇게 말했다. \"여보십시오, 나리들, 제가 이제부터 드릴 말씀이 마음에 드시든 안 드시든 간에, 사실인즉슨 제 주인 돈키호테 나리는 우리 어머니나 마찬가지로 마법에 걸린 게 아닙니다요."],["He is in his full senses, he eats and he drinks, and he has his calls like other men and as he had yesterday, before they caged him.","멀쩡한 정신에다 잡수시고 마시시고, 어제 우리에 가두기 전과 똑같이 다른 사람들처럼 볼일도 다 보십니다요."],["And if that’s the case, what do they mean by wanting me to believe that he is enchanted?","그런데 사정이 이러한데, 어찌하여 저더러 나리가 마법에 걸렸다고 믿으라는 겁니까요?"],["For I have heard many a one say that enchanted people neither eat, nor sleep, nor talk; and my master, if you don’t stop him, will talk more than thirty lawyers.”","저는 마법에 걸린 사람은 먹지도, 자지도, 말하지도 못한다고 하는 말을 숱하게 들었습니다요. 한데 제 주인은 누가 말리지 않으면 변호사 서른 명보다 더 떠들어 대실 분입죠.\""],["Then turning to the curate he exclaimed, “Ah, señor curate, señor curate!","그러더니 신부 쪽으로 돌아서서 그가 외쳤다. \"아이고, 신부님, 신부님!"],["do you think I don’t know you?","제가 신부님을 못 알아볼 줄 아십니까요?"],["Do you think I don’t guess and see the drift of these new enchantments?","제가 이 새로운 마법인지 뭔지의 속셈을 알아채지도 보지도 못할 줄 아십니까요?"],["Well then, I can tell you I know you, for all your face is covered, and I can tell you I am up to you, however you may hide your tricks.","그렇다면 말씀드립니다만, 얼굴을 죄다 가리셨어도 저는 신부님을 알아봤고, 아무리 그 수작을 감추셔도 제가 다 꿰뚫어 보고 있습니다요."],["After all, where envy reigns virtue cannot live, and where there is niggardliness there can be no liberality.","결국 시기가 판치는 곳에 덕이 살아남을 수 없고, 인색함이 있는 곳에 너그러움이 있을 수 없는 법이지요."],["Ill betide the devil!","망할 놈의 악마 같으니!"],["if it had not been for your worship my master would be married to the Princess Micomicona this minute, and I should be a count at least; for no less was to be expected, as well from the goodness of my master, him of the Rueful Countenance, as from the greatness of my services.","신부님만 아니었던들 지금쯤 제 주인은 미코미코나 공주님과 혼인하셨을 테고, 저는 적어도 백작쯤은 되었을 겁니다요. 슬픈 몰골의 기사이신 제 주인의 그 어지심으로 보나, 제가 바친 그 큰 공로로 보나, 그만한 일이야 당연히 기대할 만했으니까요."],["But I see now how true it is what they say in these parts, that the wheel of fortune turns faster than a mill-wheel, and that those who were up yesterday are down to-day.","한데 이제 보니 이 고장에서 흔히들 하는 말, 즉 운명의 수레바퀴는 물레방아 바퀴보다 더 빨리 돌아가고, 어제 위에 있던 자가 오늘은 아래로 떨어진다는 말이 참으로 옳습니다요."],["I am sorry for my wife and children, for when they might fairly and reasonably expect to see their father return to them a governor or viceroy of some island or kingdom, they will see him come back a horse-boy.","제 처자식이 가엾기 그지없습니다요. 그것들이 마땅히 제 아비가 어느 섬이나 왕국의 총독이나 부왕이 되어 돌아오기를 기대할 만하였거늘, 도리어 말구종이 되어 돌아오는 꼴을 보게 될 테니 말입니다요."],["I have said all this, señor curate, only to urge your paternity to lay to your conscience your ill-treatment of my master; and have a care that God does not call you to account in another life for making a prisoner of him in this way, and charge against you all the succours and good deeds that my lord Don Quixote leaves undone while he is shut up.","신부님, 제가 이 모든 말씀을 드리는 것은, 오로지 신부님께서 제 주인을 학대하신 일을 양심에 비추어 보시라고 촉구하기 위함입니다요. 그리고 이렇게 그분을 가둠으로써, 또 제 주인 돈키호테 나리께서 갇혀 계시는 동안 베풀지 못하고 마는 온갖 구원과 선행을 신부님 탓으로 돌려, 하느님께서 저세상에서 신부님께 그 책임을 물으시지 않도록 조심하십시오.\""]],[["“Trim those lamps there!”","\"저기 등잔 심지나 손질해라!\""],["exclaimed the barber at this; “so you are of the same fraternity as your master, too, Sancho?","이때 이발사가 외쳤다. \"그래, 산초, 너도 네 주인과 한패였더냐?"],["By God, I begin to see that you will have to keep him company in the cage, and be enchanted like him for having caught some of his humour and chivalry.","맙소사, 이제 보니 너도 그 주인의 광기와 기사도 기질을 옮아 받아서, 그와 함께 우리에 갇혀 마법에 걸려야 할 모양이로구나."],["It was an evil hour when you let yourself be got with child by his promises, and that island you long so much for found its way into your head.”","네가 그자의 약속에 넘어가 배가 부르도록 내버려 두고, 네가 그토록 갈망하는 그 섬이 네 머릿속에 들어앉은 것이 다 액운이었던 게야.\""]],[["“I am not with child by anyone,” returned Sancho, “nor am I a man to let myself be got with child, if it was by the King himself. Though I am poor I am an old Christian, and I owe nothing to nobody, and if I long for an island, other people long for worse. Each of us is the son of his own works; and being a man I may come to be pope, not to say governor of an island, especially as my master may win so many that he will not know whom to give them to. Mind how you talk, master barber; for shaving is not everything, and there is some difference between Peter and Peter. I say this because we all know one another, and it will not do to throw false dice with me; and as to the enchantment of my master, God knows the truth; leave it as it is; it only makes it worse to stir it.”","\"저는 누구의 씨로도 배가 부른 적이 없습니다요.\" 산초가 대꾸했다. \"설령 임금님이 그러시려 한들 제가 배부르도록 내버려 둘 사람도 아니고요. 비록 가난하나마 저는 유서 깊은 기독교도이고, 누구에게도 빚진 게 없습니다요. 제가 섬을 갈망한다 한들, 남들은 더 못한 것을 갈망하기도 합니다요. 우리는 저마다 제가 한 일의 자식인 법이고, 사람으로 태어난 이상 섬의 총독은 그만두고 교황도 될 수 있는 것 아닙니까요. 더구나 제 주인께서는 하도 많은 섬을 얻으셔서 누구한테 줘야 할지 모르실 지경일 텐데요. 말조심하십시오, 이발사 양반. 면도질이 전부가 아니고, 베드로와 베드로 사이에도 차이가 있는 법이니까요. 제가 이런 말씀을 드리는 것은 우리가 다 서로를 알기 때문이며, 저한테 가짜 주사위를 굴려 봐야 통하지 않는다는 말입니다요. 그리고 제 주인의 마법으로 말할 것 같으면, 진실은 하느님께서 아십니다요. 그냥 내버려 두십시오. 들쑤셔 봐야 더 나빠질 뿐이니까요.\""]],[["The barber did not care to answer Sancho lest by his plain speaking he should disclose what the curate and he himself were trying so hard to conceal; and under the same apprehension the curate had asked the canon to ride on a little in advance, so that he might tell him the mystery of this man in the cage, and other things that would amuse him.","이발사는 산초에게 대꾸하고 싶지 않았으니, 그가 직설적으로 떠들어 대다가 신부와 자기가 그토록 애써 감추려는 것을 폭로해 버릴까 염려한 까닭이었다. 그리고 같은 염려에서 신부는 참사회원에게 조금 앞서 가 달라고 청하여, 이 우리에 갇힌 사람의 사연이며 그를 즐겁게 해 줄 다른 일들을 들려주려 하였다."],["The canon agreed, and going on ahead with his servants, listened with attention to the account of the character, life, madness, and ways of Don Quixote, given him by the curate, who described to him briefly the beginning and origin of his craze, and told him the whole story of his adventures up to his being confined in the cage, together with the plan they had of taking him home to try if by any means they could discover a cure for his madness.","참사회원이 응하여 종자들과 함께 앞으로 나아가서는, 신부가 들려주는 돈키호테의 성품과 삶과 광기와 행적에 관한 이야기에 귀 기울여 들었다. 신부는 그의 광기의 시작과 기원을 간략히 일러 주고, 그가 우리에 갇히기까지의 모험담을 죄다 들려주었으며, 어떻게든 그 광기를 고칠 방도를 찾을 수 있을까 하여 그를 집으로 데려가려는 계획까지 함께 이야기하였다."],["The canon and his servants were surprised anew when they heard Don Quixote’s strange story, and when it was finished he said, “To tell the truth, señor curate, I for my part consider what they call books of chivalry to be mischievous to the State; and though, led by idle and false taste, I have read the beginnings of almost all that have been printed, I never could manage to read any one of them from beginning to end; for it seems to me they are all more or less the same thing; and one has nothing more in it than another; this no more than that.","참사회원과 그 종자들은 돈키호테의 기이한 사연을 듣고 새삼 놀랐는데, 이야기가 끝나자 그가 말했다."],["And in my opinion this sort of writing and composition is of the same species as the fables they call the Milesian, nonsensical tales that aim solely at giving amusement and not instruction, exactly the opposite of the apologue fables which amuse and instruct at the same time.","\"사실대로 말하자면, 신부님, 나로서는 사람들이 기사도 책이라 부르는 것이 나라에 해롭다고 봅니다. 그리고 한가하고 그릇된 취향에 이끌려 인쇄된 거의 모든 책의 첫머리는 읽어 보았으나, 그중 단 한 권도 처음부터 끝까지 읽어 낼 수가 없었습니다."],["And though it may be the chief object of such books to amuse, I do not know how they can succeed, when they are so full of such monstrous nonsense.","내가 보기에 그것들은 죄다 크든 작든 똑같은 것이어서, 이것이 저것보다 나을 게 없고, 저것이 이것보다 나을 게 없으니 말입니다."],["For the enjoyment the mind feels must come from the beauty and harmony which it perceives or contemplates in the things that the eye or the imagination brings before it; and nothing that has any ugliness or disproportion about it can give any pleasure.","그리고 내 생각에 이런 종류의 글과 작품은 사람들이 밀레토스풍이라 부르는 우화와 같은 부류로서, 오로지 즐거움만 주고 가르침은 주지 않으려는 터무니없는 이야기들인즉, 즐거움과 가르침을 동시에 주는 교훈 우화와는 정반대입니다. 그리고 설령 그런 책들의 으뜸가는 목적이 즐거움을 주는 것이라 하더라도, 그토록 괴이한 헛소리로 가득 차 있으니 어찌 그 목적을 이룰 수 있을지 모르겠습니다."],["What beauty, then, or what proportion of the parts to the whole, or of the whole to the parts, can there be in a book or fable where a lad of sixteen cuts down a giant as tall as a tower and makes two halves of him as if he was an almond cake?","마음이 느끼는 즐거움이란 눈이나 상상이 앞에 펼쳐 보이는 사물에서 마음이 지각하거나 관조하는 아름다움과 조화로움에서 비롯되어야 하는 법이며, 무엇이든 추함이나 부조화를 지닌 것은 아무런 즐거움도 줄 수 없습니다."],["And when they want to give us a picture of a battle, after having told us that there are a million of combatants on the side of the enemy, let the hero of the book be opposed to them, and we have perforce to believe, whether we like it or not, that the said knight wins the victory by the single might of his strong arm.","그렇다면 열여섯 살짜리 소년이 탑처럼 키 큰 거인을 베어 마치 아몬드 과자라도 되는 양 두 동강 내어 버리는 책이나 이야기에 무슨 아름다움이, 또 부분과 전체, 전체와 부분 사이에 무슨 균형이 있을 수 있겠습니까? 또 그들이 전투의 모습을 그려 보이고자 할 때, 적군 쪽에 백만 명의 전사가 있다고 일러 놓고는, 책의 주인공을 그들에게 맞서게 하니, 우리는 좋든 싫든 그 기사가 제 억센 팔의 힘 하나만으로 승리를 거둔다고 어쩔 수 없이 믿어야 합니다."],["And then, what shall we say of the facility with which a born queen or empress will give herself over into the arms of some unknown wandering knight?","그리고 또, 타고난 여왕이나 황후가 일면식도 없는 어느 떠돌이 기사의 품에 제 몸을 던져 버리는 그 손쉬움에 대해서는 무어라 해야겠습니까?"],["What mind, that is not wholly barbarous and uncultured, can find pleasure in reading of how a great tower full of knights sails away across the sea like a ship with a fair wind, and will be to-night in Lombardy and to-morrow morning in the land of Prester John of the Indies, or some other that Ptolemy never described nor Marco Polo saw?","기사들로 가득 찬 거대한 탑이 순풍을 받은 배처럼 바다를 가로질러 떠가서, 오늘 밤은 롬바르디아에, 내일 아침은 인도의 사제왕 요한의 나라에, 혹은 프톨레마이오스도 기술한 적 없고 마르코 폴로도 본 적 없는 어떤 다른 나라에 이른다는 이야기를 읽고 즐거움을 느낄 마음이, 도무지 야만스럽고 무지하지 않은 다음에야 어디 있겠습니까?"],["And if, in answer to this, I am told that the authors of books of the kind write them as fiction, and therefore are not bound to regard niceties of truth, I would reply that fiction is all the better the more it looks like truth, and gives the more pleasure the more probability and possibility there is about it.","그리고 만일 이에 대한 답으로, 그런 종류의 책을 쓰는 작가들은 그것을 허구로 쓰는 것이니 진실의 세세한 부분까지 따질 의무는 없다고 한다면, 나는 이렇게 대답하겠습니다. 허구란 진실에 가까워 보일수록 더 훌륭하며, 개연성과 가능성이 있을수록 더 큰 즐거움을 주는 법이라고 말입니다."],["Plots in fiction should be wedded to the understanding of the reader, and be constructed in such a way that, reconciling impossibilities, smoothing over difficulties, keeping the mind on the alert, they may surprise, interest, divert, and entertain, so that wonder and delight joined may keep pace one with the other; all which he will fail to effect who shuns verisimilitude and truth to nature, wherein lies the perfection of writing.","허구 속의 줄거리는 독자의 이해에 맞아떨어져야 하며, 불가능한 것을 그럴듯하게 만들고 어려운 것을 매끄럽게 다듬으며 마음을 긴장 속에 붙들어 둠으로써, 놀라게 하고 흥미를 끌고 즐겁게 하고 재미있게 하여, 경탄과 기쁨이 서로 발맞추어 나아가도록 짜여야 합니다. 그런데 글쓰기의 완성이 깃든 핍진성과 자연에 대한 충실함을 외면하는 자는 이 모든 것을 이루어 내지 못할 것입니다."],["I have never yet seen any book of chivalry that puts together a connected plot complete in all its numbers, so that the middle agrees with the beginning, and the end with the beginning and middle; on the contrary, they construct them with such a multitude of members that it seems as though they meant to produce a chimera or monster rather than a well-proportioned figure.","나는 아직껏 모든 부분이 완결된, 짜임새 있는 줄거리를 갖추어, 중간이 처음과 들어맞고 끝이 처음 및 중간과 들어맞는 기사도 책을 단 한 권도 본 적이 없습니다. 도리어 그들은 그토록 많은 부분으로 그것을 짜 맞추니, 균형 잡힌 형상이 아니라 키마이라나 괴물을 빚어내려는 것처럼 보일 지경입니다."],["And besides all this they are harsh in their style, incredible in their achievements, licentious in their amours, uncouth in their courtly speeches, prolix in their battles, silly in their arguments, absurd in their travels, and, in short, wanting in everything like intelligent art; for which reason they deserve to be banished from the Christian commonwealth as a worthless breed.”","게다가 이 모든 것에 더하여, 그것들은 문체가 거칠고, 위업은 믿기 어려우며, 연애는 음란하고, 궁정의 언사는 투박하며, 전투는 장황하고, 논변은 어리석으며, 여행은 황당하니, 한마디로 지성적인 기예라 할 만한 모든 것이 결여되어 있습니다. 그런 까닭에 그것들은 쓸모없는 종자(種子)로서 기독교 공화국에서 추방당해 마땅합니다.\""]],[["The curate listened to him attentively and felt that he was a man of sound understanding, and that there was good reason in what he said; so he told him that, being of the same opinion himself, and bearing a grudge to books of chivalry, he had burned all Don Quixote’s, which were many; and gave him an account of the scrutiny he had made of them, and of those he had condemned to the flames and those he had spared, with which the canon was not a little amused, adding that though he had said so much in condemnation of these books, still he found one good thing in them, and that was the opportunity they afforded to a gifted intellect for displaying itself; for they presented a wide and spacious field over which the pen might range freely, describing shipwrecks, tempests, combats, battles, portraying a valiant captain with all the qualifications requisite to make one, showing him sagacious in foreseeing the wiles of the enemy, eloquent in speech to encourage or restrain his soldiers, ripe in counsel, rapid in resolve, as bold in biding his time as in pressing the attack; now picturing some sad tragic incident, now some joyful and unexpected event; here a beauteous lady, virtuous, wise, and modest; there a Christian knight, brave and gentle; here a lawless, barbarous braggart; there a courteous prince, gallant and gracious; setting forth the devotion and loyalty of vassals, the greatness and generosity of nobles. “Or again,” said he, “the author may show himself to be an astronomer, or a skilled cosmographer, or musician, or one versed in affairs of state, and sometimes he will have a chance of coming forward as a magician if he likes. He can set forth the craftiness of Ulysses, the piety of Æneas, the valour of Achilles, the misfortunes of Hector, the treachery of Sinon, the friendship of Euryalus, the generosity of Alexander, the boldness of Caesar, the clemency and truth of Trajan, the fidelity of Zopyrus, the wisdom of Cato, and in short all the faculties that serve to make an illustrious man perfect, now uniting them in one individual, again distributing them among many; and if this be done with charm of style and ingenious invention, aiming at the truth as much as possible, he will assuredly weave a web of bright and varied threads that, when finished, will display such perfection and beauty that it will attain the worthiest object any writing can seek, which, as I said before, is to give instruction and pleasure combined; for the unrestricted range of these books enables the author to show his powers, epic, lyric, tragic, or comic, and all the moods the sweet and winning arts of poesy and oratory are capable of; for the epic may be written in prose just as well as in verse.”","신부는 그의 말에 주의 깊게 귀 기울이며, 그가 건전한 분별을 갖춘 사람이요 그의 말에 충분한 일리가 있음을 느꼈다. 그리하여 그는 자기도 같은 의견이며 기사도 책에 원한을 품고 있던 터라, 무수히 많던 돈키호테의 책들을 죄다 불태워 버렸노라 일러 주었다. 또 자기가 그것들을 어떻게 점검하였는지, 어떤 것을 불길에 처넣고 어떤 것을 살려 두었는지에 관한 이야기를 들려주니, 참사회원은 적잖이 재미있어하였다. 그러더니 참사회원은, 자기가 그 책들을 그토록 책망하기는 하였으나 그래도 한 가지 좋은 점은 발견하였다고 덧붙였으니, 그것은 바로 재능 있는 지성이 스스로를 펼쳐 보일 기회를 그 책들이 제공한다는 점이었다. 그 책들은 펜이 자유로이 누빌 수 있는 넓고 광활한 들판을 펼쳐 보이는바, 난파며 폭풍이며 격전이며 전투를 그려 내고, 용맹한 대장을 갖추는 데 필요한 온갖 자질을 두루 지닌 그러한 인물을 묘사하되, 적의 술책을 미리 내다보는 데 명민하고, 병사들을 북돋우거나 다잡는 언변에 능하며, 책략에 노련하고, 결단에 신속하며, 때를 기다리는 데도 공격을 밀어붙이는 데도 한결같이 대담한 모습으로 그려 낸다는 것이었다. 또 어느 때는 어떤 슬프고 비극적인 사건을, 또 어느 때는 어떤 기쁘고 뜻밖의 사건을 그려 보이고, 여기서는 아름답고 정숙하며 지혜롭고 겸손한 귀부인을, 저기서는 용감하고 점잖은 기독교도 기사를, 여기서는 무법하고 야만스러운 허풍쟁이를, 저기서는 정중하고 늠름하며 기품 있는 군주를 그려 내며, 봉신들의 헌신과 충성을, 귀족들의 위대함과 너그러움을 펼쳐 보인다는 것이었다. \"혹은 또,\" 그가 말했다. \"작가가 스스로 천문학자임을, 혹은 능숙한 우주지리학자나 음악가, 또는 국정에 통달한 자임을 보여 줄 수도 있고, 때로는 원한다면 마법사로 나설 기회도 가질 것입니다. 그는 율리시스의 교활함, 아이네아스의 효심, 아킬레우스의 용맹, 헥토르의 불운, 시논의 배신, 에우리알루스의 우정, 알렉산드로스의 너그러움, 카이사르의 대담함, 트라야누스의 관용과 진실함, 조피로스의 충성, 카토의 지혜를, 한마디로 한 출중한 인물을 완전하게 만드는 데 이바지하는 온갖 자질을, 어느 때는 한 사람에게 한데 모으고 어느 때는 여럿에게 나누어 펼쳐 보일 수 있습니다. 그리고 만일 이것을 문체의 매력과 기발한 창의로써, 되도록 진실을 겨냥하여 해낸다면, 그는 분명 밝고 갖가지 빛깔의 실로 짠 직물 한 폭을 엮어 낼 것이며, 그것이 완성되면 그 완벽함과 아름다움이 너무도 빛나, 어떤 글이든 추구할 수 있는 가장 값진 목적, 곧 앞서 말한 대로 가르침과 즐거움을 함께 주는 그 목적에 다다를 것입니다. 이런 책들의 거침없는 범위는 작가로 하여금 서사시적, 서정시적, 비극적, 희극적인 자기 역량을, 그리고 시와 웅변의 달콤하고 매혹적인 기예가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 정조(情調)를 펼쳐 보일 수 있게 하니, 서사시란 운문으로 쓸 수 있는 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 산문으로도 쓸 수 있는 것이기 때문입니다.\""]],[["# CHAPTER XLVIII. IN WHICH THE CANON PURSUES THE SUBJECT OF THE BOOKS OF CHIVALRY, WITH OTHER MATTERS WORTHY OF HIS WIT","# CHAPTER XLVIII."]],[["“It is as you say, señor canon,” said the curate; “and for that reason those who have hitherto written books of the sort deserve all the more censure for writing without paying any attention to good taste or the rules of art, by which they might guide themselves and become as famous in prose as the two princes of Greek and Latin poetry are in verse.”","신부가 말했다. \"참사회원 님 말씀이 옳습니다. 바로 그런 이유로 지금까지 그런 부류의 책을 써 온 자들은 더욱 큰 비난을 받아 마땅하지요. 좋은 취향이나 예술의 규칙에는 조금도 마음을 두지 않고 써 댔으니 말입니다. 그 규칙을 길잡이로 삼았더라면 그리스와 라틴 시의 두 거장이 운문에서 그러하듯, 그들도 산문에서 명성을 떨칠 수 있었을 텐데요.\""]],[["“I myself, at any rate,” said the canon, “was once tempted to write a book of chivalry in which all the points I have mentioned were to be observed; and if I must own the truth I have more than a hundred sheets written; and to try if it came up to my own opinion of it, I showed them to persons who were fond of this kind of reading, to learned and intelligent men as well as to ignorant people who cared for nothing but the pleasure of listening to nonsense, and from all I obtained flattering approval; nevertheless I proceeded no farther with it, as well because it seemed to me an occupation inconsistent with my profession, as because I perceived that the fools are more numerous than the wise; and, though it is better to be praised by the wise few than applauded by the foolish many, I have no mind to submit myself to the stupid judgment of the silly public, to whom the reading of such books falls for the most part.","참사회원이 말했다. \"저로 말하자면, 한때 제가 말씀드린 그 모든 요점을 지킨 기사도 책을 한 권 써 보고 싶은 유혹을 받은 적이 있습니다. 사실을 털어놓자면 이미 백 장이 넘게 써 두었지요. 그리고 그것이 제 스스로의 평가에 부합하는지 시험해 보려고, 이런 종류의 읽을거리를 좋아하는 사람들에게, 곧 학식 있고 지적인 이들에게나, 허튼소리를 듣는 즐거움밖에는 아무것도 바라지 않는 무지한 자들에게나 두루 보여 주었습니다. 그랬더니 모두에게서 듣기 좋은 칭찬을 얻었지요. 그럼에도 저는 그 일을 더 진척시키지 않았습니다. 한편으로는 그것이 제 직분에 어울리지 않는 일로 여겨졌기 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 어리석은 자가 현명한 자보다 수가 많음을 알았기 때문입니다. 비록 어리석은 다수에게 갈채받는 것보다 현명한 소수에게 칭찬받는 편이 낫다고는 하나, 저는 이런 책을 읽는 일이 대개 떨어지고 마는 그 분별없는 대중의 우둔한 판단에 저 자신을 내맡길 마음이 없습니다."]],[["“But what most of all made me hold my hand and even abandon all idea of finishing it was an argument I put to myself taken from the plays that are acted now-a-days, which was in this wise: if those that are now in vogue, as well those that are pure invention as those founded on history, are, all or most of them, downright nonsense and things that have neither head nor tail, and yet the public listens to them with delight, and regards and cries them up as perfection when they are so far from it; and if the authors who write them, and the players who act them, say that this is what they must be, for the public wants this and will have nothing else; and that those that go by rule and work out a plot according to the laws of art will only find some half-dozen intelligent people to understand them, while all the rest remain blind to the merit of their composition; and that for themselves it is better to get bread from the many than praise from the few; then my book will fare the same way, after I have burnt off my eyebrows in trying to observe the principles I have spoken of, and I shall be ‘the tailor of the corner.’ And though I have sometimes endeavoured to convince actors that they are mistaken in this notion they have adopted, and that they would attract more people, and get more credit, by producing plays in accordance with the rules of art, than by absurd ones, they are so thoroughly wedded to their own opinion that no argument or evidence can wean them from it.","\"하지만 무엇보다도 제 손을 멈추게 하고 그것을 완성할 생각조차 아예 버리게 만든 것은, 요즘 상연되는 연극을 두고 제 스스로에게 던진 논거였습니다. 그것은 이런 식이었지요. 지금 유행하는 연극들이, 순전한 창작이든 역사에 바탕을 둔 것이든, 그 전부 혹은 대부분이 한낱 허튼소리요 머리도 꼬리도 없는 것들인데도 대중은 그것을 기뻐하며 듣고, 완벽함과는 거리가 멀건만 완벽한 것으로 여기고 떠받들며 칭송한다면, 또 그것을 쓰는 작가들과 그것을 연기하는 배우들이 대중이 이것을 원하고 다른 것은 받아들이려 하지 않으니 마땅히 이래야 한다고 말한다면, 또 규칙에 따라 예술의 법도대로 줄거리를 짜는 자들은 그것을 알아볼 만한 지각 있는 사람을 기껏해야 대여섯이나 찾을 뿐이고 나머지는 모두 그 작품의 가치에 눈먼 채로 남아 있다면, 또 그들로서는 소수에게 칭찬받기보다 다수에게서 빵을 얻는 편이 낫다면, 제가 앞서 말씀드린 원칙을 지키느라 눈썹을 다 태우고 난 뒤에도 제 책 역시 똑같은 길을 걷게 될 것이요, 저는 '길모퉁이의 재단사'* 꼴이 되고 말 것이라는 논거였습니다. 그래서 제가 더러 배우들에게 그들이 받아들인 이 관념이 잘못된 것이라고, 터무니없는 연극보다 예술의 규칙에 맞는 연극을 내놓는 편이 더 많은 사람을 끌어모으고 더 큰 신망을 얻으리라고 설득해 보려 했으나, 그들은 자기네 생각에 어찌나 단단히 매여 있는지 어떤 논거나 증거로도 거기서 떼어 낼 수가 없었습니다."]],[["“I remember saying one day to one of these obstinate fellows, ‘Tell me, do you not recollect that a few years ago, there were three tragedies acted in Spain, written by a famous poet of these kingdoms, which were such that they filled all who heard them with admiration, delight, and interest, the ignorant as well as the wise, the masses as well as the higher orders, and brought in more money to the performers, these three alone, than thirty of the best that have been since produced?’","* 길모퉁이의 재단사: 공짜로 일해 주고도 실을 제 돈으로 댔다는 스페인 속담 속 인물로, 헛수고하는 사람을 빗댄 말. \"하루는 그런 고집불통 가운데 한 사람에게 이렇게 말한 기억이 납니다. '말해 보시오, 몇 해 전에 이 왕국의 한 이름난 시인이 쓴 비극 세 편이 스페인에서 상연되었던 것을 기억하지 못하시오? 그것들은 듣는 사람을 모두, 무지한 자나 현명한 자나, 천한 무리나 높은 신분이나 가릴 것 없이 감탄과 기쁨과 흥미로 가득 채웠고, 그 세 편만으로도 이후 나온 가장 훌륭한 연극 서른 편을 합친 것보다 더 많은 돈을 흥행주들에게 벌어다 주지 않았소?'"]],[["“‘No doubt,’ replied the actor in question, ‘you mean the “Isabella,”","\"그러자 그 배우가 대답하더군요."],["the “Phyllis,” and the “Alexandra.”’","'「이사벨라」와 「필리스」와 「알렉산드라」를 두고 하시는 말씀이겠지요.'"]],[["“‘Those are the ones I mean,’ said I; ‘and see if they did not observe the principles of art, and if, by observing them, they failed to show their superiority and please all the world; so that the fault does not lie with the public that insists upon nonsense, but with those who don’t know how to produce something else.","\"제가 말했지요. '바로 그것들이오."],["“The Ingratitude Revenged” was not nonsense, nor was there any in “The Numantia,”","그것들이 예술의 원칙을 지키지 않았는지, 또 그 원칙을 지킴으로써 그 우월함을 드러내고 온 세상을 즐겁게 하는 데 실패했는지 보시오."],["nor any to be found in “The Merchant Lover,” nor yet in “The Friendly Fair Foe,”","그러니 허튼소리를 고집하는 것은 대중의 잘못이 아니라, 다른 무언가를 만들어 낼 줄 모르는 자들의 잘못이오."],["nor in some others that have been written by certain gifted poets, to their own fame and renown, and to the profit of those that brought them out;’ some further remarks I added to these, with which, I think, I left him rather dumbfoundered, but not so satisfied or convinced that I could disabuse him of his error.”","「복수당한 배은망덕」도 허튼소리가 아니었고, 「누만시아」에도 그런 것은 없었으며, 「상인이 된 연인」에도, 「다정한 어여쁜 원수」에도, 또 어떤 재능 있는 시인들이 자기네의 명성과 영예를 위해, 그리고 그것을 무대에 올린 자들의 이익을 위해 쓴 다른 몇몇 작품에도 그런 것은 없었소.' 여기에 몇 마디 더 덧붙였더니, 제 생각에는 그를 다소 말문이 막히게 만들기는 했으나, 그의 오류를 깨우쳐 줄 만큼 그가 흡족해하거나 수긍한 것은 아니었습니다.\""]],[["“You have touched upon a subject, señor canon,”","여기서 신부가 끼어들었다. \"참사회원 님께서는 한 가지 화제를 건드리셨는데, 그것은 제가 오늘날 유행하는 연극에 대해 품고 있는 해묵은 적개심을 일깨우는군요."],["observed the curate here, “that has awakened an old enmity I have against the plays in vogue at the present day, quite as strong as that which I bear to the books of chivalry; for while the drama, according to Tully, should be the mirror of human life, the model of manners, and the image of the truth, those which are presented now-a-days are mirrors of nonsense, models of folly, and images of lewdness.","제가 기사도 책에 품은 적개심에 못지않게 강한 것입니다. 툴리우스에 따르면 연극은 인생의 거울이요, 풍속의 모범이며, 진리의 형상이어야 하건만, 요즘 상연되는 것들은 허튼소리의 거울이요, 어리석음의 모범이며, 음란함의 형상이니 말입니다."],["For what greater nonsense can there be in connection with what we are now discussing than for an infant to appear in swaddling clothes in the first scene of the first act, and in the second a grown-up bearded man?","우리가 지금 논하는 것과 관련하여, 첫 막 첫 장면에서 강보에 싸인 갓난아이가 등장했다가 두 번째 장면에서 수염 난 다 자란 어른으로 나오는 것보다 더한 허튼소리가 어디 있겠습니까? 또 늙은이를 허세 부리는 칼잡이로, 젊은이를 겁쟁이로, 하인이 고상한 말을 늘어놓고, 시동이 현명한 충고를 하며, 왕이 짐꾼 노릇을 하고, 공주가 부엌데기가 되어 우리 앞에 나서는 것보다 더한 터무니없음이 어디 있겠습니까?"],["Or what greater absurdity can there be than putting before us an old man as a swashbuckler, a young man as a poltroon, a lackey using fine language, a page giving sage advice, a king plying as a porter, a princess who is a kitchen-maid?","또 그들이 그려 내는 사건이 일어날 법한, 혹은 일어날 수 있는 시간에 그들이 기울이는 주의에 대해서는 무어라 말해야 할까요? 제가 본 어떤 연극은 첫 막이 유럽에서 시작되어, 두 번째 막은 아시아에서, 세 번째 막은 아프리카에서 끝나더군요."],["And then what shall I say of their attention to the time in which the action they represent may or can take place, save that I have seen a play where the first act began in Europe, the second in Asia, the third finished in Africa, and no doubt, had it been in four acts, the fourth would have ended in America, and so it would have been laid in all four quarters of the globe?","그리고 만일 네 막짜리였다면 네 번째 막은 틀림없이 아메리카에서 끝나, 그리하여 지구의 네 귀퉁이 모두를 무대로 삼았을 것입니다. 또 인생에의 충실함이 연극이 무엇보다 염두에 두어야 할 것이라면, 사건이 페핀 왕이나 샤를마뉴 시대에 일어나는 것으로 설정되어 있는데 그 안의 주인공으로는 부용의 고드프루아처럼 십자가를 들고 예루살렘에 들어가 성묘를 되찾은 헤라클리우스 황제를 내세우니, 그 둘 사이에는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 세월이 가로놓여 있는 터에, 어떻게 평범한 지각을 가진 사람치고 만족할 수 있겠습니까?"],["And if truth to life is the main thing the drama should keep in view, how is it possible for any average understanding to be satisfied when the action is supposed to pass in the time of King Pepin or Charlemagne, and the principal personage in it they represent to be the Emperor Heraclius who entered Jerusalem with the cross and won the Holy Sepulchre, like Godfrey of Bouillon, there being years innumerable between the one and the other?","또 연극이 허구에 바탕을 두면서 거기에 역사적 사실을 끌어들이거나, 서로 다른 사람들에게 서로 다른 때에 일어났던 일들의 토막을 뒤섞어 놓는데, 그 모든 것이 개연성의 기미라고는 전혀 없을뿐더러, 어느 모로 보나 변명의 여지 없는 명백한 오류들로 가득하다면 어떻겠습니까? 게다가 가장 고약한 것은, 이것이 완벽함이요 이것을 넘어선 것은 무엇이든 꾸며 낸 세련됨이라고 말하는 무지한 자들이 있다는 사실입니다."],["or, if the play is based on fiction and historical facts are introduced, or bits of what occurred to different people and at different times mixed up with it, all, not only without any semblance of probability, but with obvious errors that from every point of view are inexcusable?","그리고 성극으로 눈을 돌리면, 그 안에서 그들은 어떤 기적들을 지어내는지요! 한 성인의 기적을 다른 성인에게 갖다 붙이며, 얼마나 외경스럽고 엉성하게 꾸며 낸 사건들을 만들어 내는지요!"],["And the worst of it is, there are ignorant people who say that this is perfection, and that anything beyond this is affected refinement.","심지어 세속극에서도 그들은 아무런 까닭이나 목적도 없이 기적을 끌어들이는데, 그저 그들이 변신이라 부르는 그런 기적이 어리석은 사람들을 놀라게 하고 연극으로 끌어들이는 데 요긴하리라 여길 따름입니다. 이 모든 것이 진리를 해치고 역사를 그르치며, 더 나아가 스페인 재사들에게 치욕을 안기는 데로 기울지요."],["And then if we turn to sacred dramas--what miracles they invent in them!","연극의 법도를 빈틈없이 지키는 외국인들이 우리가 내놓는 연극의 터무니없음과 허튼소리를 볼 때면 우리를 야만스럽고 무지한 자들로 여기니 말입니다. 잘 다스려지는 정부가 연극을 대중 앞에 상연하도록 허락할 때 주로 염두에 두는 목적은, 때때로 해롭지 않은 오락거리로 사람들을 즐겁게 하고, 게으름이 자아내기 쉬운 그 사악한 기질로부터 그들을 지키려는 데 있으며, 또 이것은 좋든 나쁘든 어떤 종류의 연극으로든 이룰 수 있으니, 법도를 정하거나 그것을 쓰고 연기하는 자들을 마땅한 방식대로 만들도록 매어 둘 필요가 없다는 변명도 충분치 않습니다."],["What apocryphal, ill-devised incidents, attributing to one saint the miracles of another!","제가 말씀드렸듯이, 추구하는 목적은 어떤 종류의 연극으로든 확보될 수 있다는 변명 말입니다. 이에 대해 저는 이렇게 답하겠습니다."],["And even in secular plays they venture to introduce miracles without any reason or object except that they think some such miracle, or transformation as they call it, will come in well to astonish stupid people and draw them to the play.","그와 똑같은 목적은, 비할 데 없이 더 훌륭하게, 그렇지 못한 연극보다는 좋은 연극을 통해 이루어지리라고요. 예술적이고 제대로 짜인 연극을 듣고 나면, 듣는 이는 익살에 흥이 돋고, 진지한 대목에 가르침을 받으며, 사건들에 감탄으로 가득 차고, 논변으로 지혜가 날카로워지며, 술수에 경각심을 얻고, 본보기로 한층 현명해지며, 악덕에 맞서 분기하고, 미덕을 사랑하며 자리를 뜰 것입니다."],["All this tends to the prejudice of the truth and the corruption of history, nay more, to the reproach of the wits of Spain; for foreigners who scrupulously observe the laws of the drama look upon us as barbarous and ignorant, when they see the absurdity and nonsense of the plays we produce.","이 모든 길로 좋은 연극은, 듣는 이가 아무리 무뚝뚝하고 둔하다 한들 그의 마음을 자극할 것입니다. 그리고 온갖 불가능한 일 가운데 가장 큰 것은, 이 모든 자질을 갖춘 연극이, 그것이 결여된 연극보다, 곧 요즘 흔히 상연되는 대다수의 연극보다 훨씬 더 즐겁게 하고 만족시키고 기쁘게 하지 못하리라는 것입니다."],["Nor will it be a sufficient excuse to say that the chief object well-ordered governments have in view when they permit plays to be performed in public is to entertain the people with some harmless amusement occasionally, and keep it from those evil humours which idleness is apt to engender; and that, as this may be attained by any sort of play, good or bad, there is no need to lay down laws, or bind those who write or act them to make them as they ought to be made, since, as I say, the object sought for may be secured by any sort.","또 이 일로 그것을 쓰는 시인들을 탓할 것도 못 됩니다. 그들 가운데에는 자기 결함을 더없이 잘 알고 마땅히 무엇을 해야 하는지를 아는 이들도 있으니 말입니다."],["To this I would reply that the same end would be, beyond all comparison, better attained by means of good plays than by those that are not so; for after listening to an artistic and properly constructed play, the hearer will come away enlivened by the jests, instructed by the serious parts, full of admiration at the incidents, his wits sharpened by the arguments, warned by the tricks, all the wiser for the examples, inflamed against vice, and in love with virtue; for in all these ways a good play will stimulate the mind of the hearer be he ever so boorish or dull; and of all impossibilities the greatest is that a play endowed with all these qualities will not entertain, satisfy, and please much more than one wanting in them, like the greater number of those which are commonly acted now-a-days.","다만 연극이 사고파는 상품이 되어 버린 까닭에, 그들은 배우들이 이런 식으로 된 것이 아니면 사려 하지 않는다고, 그것도 사실대로 말합니다. 그래서 시인은 자기 작품에 값을 치를 배우의 요구에 자신을 맞추려 애쓰는 것이지요."],["Nor are the poets who write them to be blamed for this; for some there are among them who are perfectly well aware of their faults, and know what they ought to do; but as plays have become a salable commodity, they say, and with truth, that the actors will not buy them unless they are after this fashion; and so the poet tries to adapt himself to the requirements of the actor who is to pay him for his work.","이것이 진실임은, 이 왕국의 더없이 풍요로운 한 재사가 그토록 찬란하게, 그토록 우아하고 흥겹게, 그토록 다듬어진 운율로, 그토록 정선된 언어로, 그토록 심오한 성찰로, 한마디로 웅변과 격조 높은 문체가 그토록 풍부하게 써낸 수많은 연극을 보면 알 수 있습니다. 그는 그 명성으로 온 세상을 가득 채웠으나, 배우들의 취향에 맞추려는 그의 욕심 탓에, 그 작품들 가운데 몇몇이 그러하듯 모두가 마땅한 만큼 완벽함에 다가가지는 못했지요."],["And that this is the truth may be seen by the countless plays that a most fertile wit of these kingdoms has written, with so much brilliancy, so much grace and gaiety, such polished versification, such choice language, such profound reflections, and in a word, so rich in eloquence and elevation of style, that he has filled the world with his fame; and yet, in consequence of his desire to suit the taste of the actors, they have not all, as some of them have, come as near perfection as they ought.","또 어떤 이들은 어찌나 경솔하게 연극을 쓰는지, 그것이 상연된 뒤에 배우들은 어느 왕이나 누군가에게 거슬리거나 어느 귀족 가문에 모욕이 될 만한 것을 연기했다는 이유로 처벌받을까 두려워 달아나 몸을 숨겨야 하는데, 실제로 그런 일이 자주 있었습니다. 이 모든 폐단, 그리고 제가 말씀드리지 않은 더 많은 폐단은, 수도에 모든 연극이 상연되기 전에 검토할 만한 지각 있고 분별 있는 사람이 하나 있다면 사라질 것입니다."],["Others write plays with such heedlessness that, after they have been acted, the actors have to fly and abscond, afraid of being punished, as they often have been, for having acted something offensive to some king or other, or insulting to some noble family.","수도 자체에서 만들어진 것뿐 아니라 스페인 전역에서 상연될 모든 연극을 말이지요. 그의 승인과 검인과 서명이 없이는 어떤 지방 관청도 어떤 연극의 상연을 허락하지 않도록 하는 것입니다."],["All which evils, and many more that I say nothing of, would be removed if there were some intelligent and sensible person at the capital to examine all plays before they were acted, not only those produced in the capital itself, but all that were intended to be acted in Spain; without whose approval, seal, and signature, no local magistracy should allow any play to be acted.","그렇게 되면 배우들은 자기네 연극을 수도로 보내도록 신경 쓸 것이고, 안전하게 그것을 상연할 수 있을 것이며, 그것을 쓰는 자들은 그 일을 아는 자의 엄정한 검토에 자기 작품을 제출해야 한다는 두려움에, 더 신중을 기하고 자기 일에 더 공을 들일 것입니다. 그리하여 좋은 연극이 만들어지고 그것이 노리는 목적도 흐뭇하게 이루어지겠지요."],["In that case actors would take care to send their plays to the capital, and could act them in safety, and those who write them would be more careful and take more pains with their work, standing in awe of having to submit it to the strict examination of one who understood the matter; and so good plays would be produced and the objects they aim at happily attained; as well the amusement of the people, as the credit of the wits of Spain, the interest and safety of the actors, and the saving of trouble in inflicting punishment on them.","사람들의 오락도, 스페인 재사들의 신망도, 배우들의 이익과 안전도, 또 그들에게 처벌을 내리는 수고를 더는 일도 말입니다. 그리고 만일 같은 사람이나 다른 누군가에게 새로 쓰인 기사도 책을 검토할 권한이 주어진다면, 틀림없이 참사회원 님께서 묘사하신 그 모든 완벽함을 갖춘 책들이 나타나, 우리 언어를 웅변의 우아하고 값진 보배로 풍요롭게 하고, 새로 나올 책들의 빛 앞에서 낡은 책들을 어둠 속으로 몰아넣을 것입니다."],["And if the same or some other person were authorised to examine the newly written books of chivalry, no doubt some would appear with all the perfections you have described, enriching our language with the gracious and precious treasure of eloquence, and driving the old books into obscurity before the light of the new ones that would come out for the harmless entertainment, not merely of the idle but of the very busiest; for the bow cannot be always bent, nor can weak human nature exist without some lawful amusement.”","그 새 책들은 한가한 자들뿐 아니라 더없이 바쁜 자들에게까지도 해롭지 않은 즐거움을 주려고 나올 테니까요. 활시위를 늘 당겨 놓을 수만은 없고, 연약한 인간 본성도 얼마간의 정당한 오락 없이는 존재할 수 없는 법이니 말입니다.\""]],[["The canon and the curate had proceeded thus far with their conversation, when the barber, coming forward, joined them, and said to the curate, “This is the spot, señor licentiate, that I said was a good one for fresh and plentiful pasture for the oxen, while we take our noontide rest.”","참사회원과 신부가 대화를 여기까지 이어 왔을 때, 이발사가 앞으로 나와 그들에게 다가서며 신부에게 말했다. \"면허 신부 님, 여기가 한낮의 휴식을 취하는 동안 소들에게 싱싱하고 넉넉한 꼴을 먹이기에 좋다고 제가 말씀드린 그 자리입니다.\""]],[["“And so it seems,” returned the curate, and he told the canon what he proposed to do, on which he too made up his mind to halt with them, attracted by the aspect of the fair valley that lay before their eyes; and to enjoy it as well as the conversation of the curate, to whom he had begun to take a fancy, and also to learn more particulars about the doings of Don Quixote, he desired some of his servants to go on to the inn, which was not far distant, and fetch from it what eatables there might be for the whole party, as he meant to rest for the afternoon where he was; to which one of his servants replied that the sumpter mule, which by this time ought to have reached the inn, carried provisions enough to make it unnecessary to get anything from the inn except barley.","\"그런 것 같군.\" 신부가 대답하고는 자기가 하려는 바를 참사회원에게 일러 주었다. 그러자 참사회원도 눈앞에 펼쳐진 아름다운 골짜기의 풍광에 이끌려 그들과 함께 멈추기로 마음을 굳혔다. 그 풍광을 즐기는 동시에, 자기가 차츰 호감을 품게 된 신부와의 대화를 누리고, 또 돈키호테의 행적에 관해 더 자세한 사정을 알기 위해서였다. 그는 하인 몇을 가까이 있는 주막으로 보내, 일행 전부가 먹을 만한 먹을거리를 거기서 가져오게 했다. 자기는 오후 동안 있던 자리에서 쉬려 했기 때문이다. 그러자 하인 하나가 대답하기를, 이맘때면 벌써 주막에 닿았을 짐 노새가 보리 말고는 주막에서 아무것도 구할 필요가 없을 만큼 넉넉한 양식을 싣고 있다고 했다."]],[["“In that case,”","\"그렇다면,\""],["said the canon, “take all the beasts there, and bring the sumpter mule back.”","참사회원이 말했다. \"짐승들을 모두 그리로 데려가고, 짐 노새는 도로 끌고 오너라.\""]],[["While this was going on, Sancho, perceiving that he could speak to his master without having the curate and the barber, of whom he had his suspicions, present all the time, approached the cage in which Don Quixote was placed, and said, “Señor, to ease my conscience I want to tell you the state of the case as to your enchantment, and that is that these two here, with their faces covered, are the curate of our village and the barber; and I suspect they have hit upon this plan of carrying you off in this fashion, out of pure envy because your worship surpasses them in doing famous deeds; and if this be the truth it follows that you are not enchanted, but hoodwinked and made a fool of. And to prove this I want to ask you one thing; and if you answer me as I believe you will answer, you will be able to lay your finger on the trick, and you will see that you are not enchanted but gone wrong in your wits.”","이런 일이 벌어지는 동안, 산초는 자기가 의심을 품고 있던 신부와 이발사가 줄곧 곁에 있지 않은 틈을 타 주인에게 말을 건넬 수 있겠다 싶어, 돈키호테가 갇혀 있는 우리로 다가가 말했다. \"나리, 제 양심을 편하게 하려고 나리께 마법에 관한 사정이 어떤지 말씀드리고 싶습니다요. 그게 다름이 아니라, 여기 얼굴을 가린 저 두 사람은 우리 마을 신부님과 이발사거든요. 제 짐작에는, 나리께서 그분들보다 이름난 위업을 더 잘 이루신다는 이유로, 순전한 시샘에서 이런 식으로 나리를 끌고 가려는 꾀를 짜낸 듯합니다요. 그리고 이게 사실이라면, 나리께서는 마법에 걸리신 게 아니라 눈가림을 당하고 바보 취급을 당하시는 셈이지요. 이걸 증명하려고 나리께 한 가지 여쭤보고 싶습니다요. 그래서 제가 믿는 대로 나리께서 대답해 주시면, 나리께서는 그 속임수에 손가락을 짚을 수 있을 것이고, 나리께서 마법에 걸리신 게 아니라 그저 정신이 어긋나셨음을 아시게 될 겁니다요.\""]],[["“Ask what thou wilt, Sancho my son,” returned Don Quixote, “for I will satisfy thee and answer all thou requirest. As to what thou sayest, that these who accompany us yonder are the curate and the barber, our neighbours and acquaintances, it is very possible that they may seem to be those same persons; but that they are so in reality and in fact, believe it not on any account; what thou art to believe and think is that, if they look like them, as thou sayest, it must be that those who have enchanted me have taken this shape and likeness; for it is easy for enchanters to take any form they please, and they may have taken those of our friends in order to make thee think as thou dost, and lead thee into a labyrinth of fancies from which thou wilt find no escape though thou hadst the cord of Theseus; and they may also have done it to make me uncertain in my mind, and unable to conjecture whence this evil comes to me; for if on the one hand thou dost tell me that the barber and curate of our village are here in company with us, and on the other I find myself shut up in a cage, and know in my heart that no power on earth that was not supernatural would have been able to shut me in, what wouldst thou have me say or think, but that my enchantment is of a sort that transcends all I have ever read of in all the histories that deal with knights-errant that have been enchanted? So thou mayest set thy mind at rest as to the idea that they are what thou sayest, for they are as much so as I am a Turk. But touching thy desire to ask me something, say on, and I will answer thee, though thou shouldst ask questions from this till to-morrow morning.”","\"무엇이든 묻고 싶은 대로 물어라, 내 아들 산초야.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"내 너를 흡족하게 해 주고 네가 청하는 모든 것에 답해 주마. 네가 말하는바, 저기 우리와 동행하는 저들이 우리 이웃이자 아는 사이인 신부와 이발사라는 것은, 그들이 바로 그 사람들로 보일 수도 있는 일이니 충분히 가능하다. 허나 그들이 실제로, 사실상 그 사람들이라는 것은 결코 믿지 마라. 네가 믿고 생각해야 할 것은, 네 말대로 그들이 그들처럼 보인다면, 그것은 필시 나를 마법에 건 자들이 이 모습과 형상을 빌려 쓴 것이라는 점이다. 마법사들이 마음에 드는 어떤 형상이든 빌려 쓰기란 쉬운 일이니, 그들은 네가 지금처럼 생각하도록 만들고, 테세우스의 실타래를 가졌다 한들 빠져나올 수 없는 환상의 미궁으로 너를 끌어들이려고 우리 벗들의 형상을 빌렸을 수도 있다. 또한 그들은 내 마음을 어지럽혀, 이 화가 어디에서 내게 오는지 짐작조차 못 하게 하려고 그리했을 수도 있다. 한편으로 네가 우리 마을 이발사와 신부가 여기 우리와 함께 있다고 말하는데, 다른 한편으로 나 자신은 우리에 갇혀 있고, 초자연적인 것이 아닌 이 세상의 어떤 힘으로도 나를 가둘 수 없었으리라는 것을 내 마음속으로 알고 있으니, 마법에 걸린 편력 기사들을 다룬 그 모든 이야기에서 내가 읽은 모든 것을 뛰어넘는 종류의 마법이 내게 걸린 것이라고 말하고 생각하는 것 말고, 네가 나더러 무어라 말하거나 생각하라는 것이냐? 그러니 그들이 네 말하는 그런 자들이라는 생각일랑 마음을 놓아도 좋다. 내가 터키 사람인 만큼이나 그들도 그러한 자들이니 말이다. 다만 내게 무언가를 묻고 싶다는 네 바람으로 말하자면, 어서 말해 보아라. 네가 지금부터 내일 아침까지 질문을 던진다 한들 내 답해 주마.\""]],[["“May Our Lady be good to me!”","\"성모님, 저를 어여삐 여기소서!\""],["said Sancho, lifting up his voice; “and is it possible that your worship is so thick of skull and so short of brains that you cannot see that what I say is the simple truth, and that malice has more to do with your imprisonment and misfortune than enchantment?","산초가 목소리를 높여 말했다. \"나리께서 머리가 어찌나 둔하고 골이 어찌나 모자라시기에, 제 말이 꾸밈없는 진실이라는 것을, 또 나리께서 갇히고 불행을 겪으시는 데에는 마법보다 악의가 더 큰 몫을 했다는 것을 보지 못하신단 말입니까요?"],["But as it is so, I will prove plainly to you that you are not enchanted.","허나 그러시다니, 나리께서 마법에 걸리지 않으셨음을 제가 환히 증명해 드리겠습니다요."],["Now tell me, so may God deliver you from this affliction, and so may you find yourself when you least expect it in the arms of my lady Dulcinea—”","자, 말씀해 주십시오. 하느님께서 나리를 이 고난에서 건져 주시기를, 또 나리께서 미처 생각지도 못하실 때 둘시네아 마님의 품에 안기시게 되기를 빌면서——\""]],[["“Leave off conjuring me,”","\"내게 주문 외우는 짓일랑 그만두어라.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “and ask what thou wouldst know; I have already told thee I will answer with all possible precision.”","\"그리고 알고 싶은 것을 물어라. 내 이미 더없이 정확하게 답해 주겠다고 일렀느니라.\""]],[["“That is what I want,”","\"바로 그걸 바라는 겁니다요.\""],["said Sancho; “and what I would know, and have you tell me, without adding or leaving out anything, but telling the whole truth as one expects it to be told, and as it is told, by all who profess arms, as your worship professes them, under the title of knights-errant—”","산초가 말했다. \"그리고 제가 알고 싶고 나리께 말씀해 주십사 하는 것은, 무엇 하나 보태거나 빼지 마시고, 으레 그러리라 기대되는 대로, 또 나리께서 표방하시는 그 편력 기사라는 칭호 아래 무예를 표방하는 모든 이가 으레 그러하듯, 온전한 진실을 말씀해 주시는 것입니다요——\""]],[["“I tell thee I will not lie in any particular,”","\"내 어떤 점에서도 거짓말하지 않으리라 말하지 않았느냐.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “finish thy question; for in truth thou weariest me with all these asseverations, requirements, and precautions, Sancho.”","\"어서 질문을 끝맺어라. 산초야, 정말이지 이 온갖 다짐이며 요구며 조심성으로 네가 나를 지치게 하는구나.\""]],[["“Well, I rely on the goodness and truth of my master,”","\"그럼, 저는 제 주인 나리의 선하심과 진실하심을 믿겠습니다요.\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho; “and so, because it bears upon what we are talking about, I would ask, speaking with all reverence, whether since your worship has been shut up and, as you think, enchanted in this cage, you have felt any desire or inclination to go anywhere, as the saying is?”","\"그러니, 우리가 이야기하던 것과 관련된 일이라, 더없이 공손하게 여쭙겠습니다요. 나리께서 이 우리에 갇히신 뒤로, 또 나리 생각대로 마법에 걸리신 뒤로, 흔히들 말하듯 어디 좀 가고 싶은 마음이나 욕구를 느끼신 적이 있으신지요?\""]],[["“I do not understand ‘going anywhere,’”","\"'어디 좀 가다'라는 말은 못 알아듣겠구나.\""],["said Don Quixote; “explain thyself more clearly, Sancho, if thou wouldst have me give an answer to the point.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"산초야, 내가 요점에 맞는 답을 해 주기를 바란다면, 좀 더 분명히 말해 보아라.\""]],[["“Is it possible,”","\"이럴 수가 있나요.\""],["said Sancho, “that your worship does not understand ‘going anywhere’?","산초가 말했다. \"나리께서 '어디 좀 가다'라는 말을 못 알아들으신다니요?"],["Why, the schoolboys know that from the time they were babes.","아니, 학교 아이들도 갓난쟁이 적부터 그건 압니다요. 그럼, 이렇게 말씀드리지요."],["Well then, you must know I mean have you had any desire to do what cannot be avoided?”","나리께서 피할 수 없는 그 일을 하고 싶은 마음이 드신 적이 있느냐는 겁니다요.\""]],[["“Ah!","\"아! 이제 알아듣겠다, 산초야.\""],["now I understand thee, Sancho,”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"그래, 자주 있었고, 바로 이 순간에도 그렇구나."],["said Don Quixote; “yes, often, and even this minute; get me out of this strait, or all will not go right.”","나를 이 곤경에서 꺼내 다오. 그러지 않으면 만사가 순조롭지 못할 테니.\""]],[["# CHAPTER XLIX. WHICH TREATS OF THE SHREWD CONVERSATION WHICH SANCHO PANZA HELD WITH HIS MASTER DON QUIXOTE","# CHAPTER XLIX."]],[["“Aha, I have caught you,” said Sancho; “this is what in my heart and soul I was longing to know. Come now, señor, can you deny what is commonly said around us, when a person is out of humour, ‘I don’t know what ails so-and-so, that he neither eats, nor drinks, nor sleeps, nor gives a proper answer to any question; one would think he was enchanted’? From which it is to be gathered that those who do not eat, or drink, or sleep, or do any of the natural acts I am speaking of-that such persons are enchanted; but not those that have the desire your worship has, and drink when drink is given them, and eat when there is anything to eat, and answer every question that is asked them.”","\"아, 이제 당신을 붙잡았습니다.\" 산초가 말했다. \"이것이야말로 제 마음속 깊이 알고 싶어 하던 것입니다. 자, 나리, 우리 주변에서 누군가 기분이 언짢을 때면 흔히들 하는 말을 부정하실 수 있겠습니까? '아무개가 무슨 탈이 났는지 모르겠어, 먹지도 않고, 마시지도 않고, 잠도 안 자고, 무엇을 물어도 제대로 대답을 안 하니. 마법에라도 걸린 게 아닌가 싶어' 하는 그런 말 말입니다. 거기서 알 수 있는 것은, 먹지도 마시지도 자지도 않고, 제가 말씀드리는 그런 자연스러운 일을 하나도 하지 않는 사람들, 그런 사람들이 마법에 걸린 거라는 겁니다. 나리처럼 욕구가 있어서, 마실 것을 주면 마시고, 먹을 것이 있으면 먹고, 묻는 말마다 다 대답하는 사람은 아니란 말이지요.\""]],[["“What thou sayest is true, Sancho,” replied Don Quixote; “but I have already told thee there are many sorts of enchantments, and it may be that in the course of time they have been changed one for another, and that now it may be the way with enchanted people to do all that I do, though they did not do so before; so it is vain to argue or draw inferences against the usage of the time. I know and feel that I am enchanted, and that is enough to ease my conscience; for it would weigh heavily on it if I thought that I was not enchanted, and that in a faint-hearted and cowardly way I allowed myself to lie in this cage, defrauding multitudes of the succour I might afford to those in need and distress, who at this very moment may be in sore want of my aid and protection.”","\"네 말이 옳다, 산초.\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"허나 이미 너에게 말하지 않았느냐, 마법에도 여러 종류가 있으며, 세월이 흐르는 동안 그것들이 서로 뒤바뀌었을 수도 있고, 이제는 마법에 걸린 자들이 내가 하는 모든 일을 하는 것이 그 방식이 되었을지도 모른다고 말이다. 비록 예전에는 그러지 않았다 해도 말이지. 그러니 시대의 관행을 두고 따지거나 추론을 끌어내는 것은 헛된 일이다. 나는 내가 마법에 걸려 있음을 알고 또 느끼고 있으며, 그것으로 내 양심을 편케 하기에 족하다. 만일 내가 마법에 걸린 것이 아니라고, 그저 겁 많고 비겁하게 이 우리 속에 누워 곤경과 고통에 처한 이들에게 베풀 수 있는 도움을 수많은 사람들에게서 빼앗고 있다고 여긴다면, 바로 이 순간에도 내 도움과 보호를 절실히 필요로 하고 있을 그들을 생각하면, 그 무게가 내 양심을 무겁게 짓누를 것이기 때문이다.\""]],[["“Still for all that,” replied Sancho, “I say that, for your greater and fuller satisfaction, it would be well if your worship were to try to get out of this prison (and I promise to do all in my power to help, and even to take you out of it), and see if you could once more mount your good Rocinante, who seems to be enchanted too, he is so melancholy and dejected; and then we might try our chance in looking for adventures again; and if we have no luck there will be time enough to go back to the cage; in which, on the faith of a good and loyal squire, I promise to shut myself up along with your worship, if so be you are so unfortunate, or I so stupid, as not to be able to carry out my plan.”","\"그렇다 해도 말입니다.\" 산초가 대답했다. \"더 크고 더 온전한 만족을 위해서, 나리께서 이 감옥에서 빠져나오려 애써 보시는 게 좋겠습니다(저는 제 힘이 닿는 한 도와드리겠다고, 아니 직접 꺼내 드리겠다고 약속합니다). 그래서 마법에 걸린 것처럼 저리도 우울하고 풀 죽어 보이는 그 훌륭한 로시난테에 다시 한번 올라타실 수 있는지 보시는 겁니다. 그런 다음 우리는 다시 모험을 찾아 운을 시험해 보면 됩니다. 운이 따르지 않으면 그때 우리로 돌아갈 시간은 충분합니다. 만일 나리께서 운이 너무 없으시거나 제가 너무 어리석어 제 계획을 이뤄 내지 못하게 된다면, 착하고 충직한 종자의 신의를 걸고, 저도 나리와 함께 그 안에 갇히겠다고 약속드립니다.\""]],[["“I am content to do as thou sayest, brother Sancho,”","\"네 말대로 하기로 하마, 산초 형제여.\" 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote, “and when thou seest an opportunity for effecting my release I will obey thee absolutely; but thou wilt see, Sancho, how mistaken thou art in thy conception of my misfortune.”","\"네가 나를 풀어 줄 기회를 보거든 나는 너에게 절대적으로 따르겠다. 허나 산초야, 너는 곧 알게 될 게다, 네가 내 불운을 두고 얼마나 잘못 생각하고 있는지를 말이다.\""]],[["The knight-errant and the ill-errant squire kept up their conversation till they reached the place where the curate, the canon, and the barber, who had already dismounted, were waiting for them.","편력 기사와 불운한 종자는 이미 말에서 내려 그들을 기다리고 있던 신부와 참사회원과 이발사가 있는 곳에 다다를 때까지 그렇게 대화를 이어 갔다."],["The carter at once unyoked the oxen and left them to roam at large about the pleasant green spot, the freshness of which seemed to invite, not enchanted people like Don Quixote, but wide-awake, sensible folk like his squire, who begged the curate to allow his master to leave the cage for a little; for if they did not let him out, the prison might not be as clean as the propriety of such a gentleman as his master required.","짐수레꾼은 곧 소들의 멍에를 풀어 그 기분 좋은 푸른 풀밭을 마음껏 거닐도록 놓아주었으니, 그 싱그러움은 돈키호테처럼 마법에 걸린 사람이 아니라 그의 종자처럼 정신이 또렷하고 분별 있는 사람을 부르는 듯했다. 종자는 신부에게 자기 주인을 잠시 우리에서 나오게 해 달라고 청했다."],["The curate understood him, and said he would very gladly comply with his request, only that he feared his master, finding himself at liberty, would take to his old courses and make off where nobody could ever find him again.","그를 내보내 주지 않으면 그런 신사 분의 체통이 요구하는 만큼 우리가 깨끗이 유지되지 못할 것이기 때문이었다. 신부는 그 뜻을 알아듣고, 기꺼이 청을 들어주고 싶지만 다만 자기 주인이 자유로워지면 옛 버릇이 도져 아무도 다시는 찾지 못할 곳으로 달아날까 두렵다고 말했다."]],[["“I will answer for his not running away,”","\"그분이 달아나지 않으리란 것은 제가 책임지겠습니다.\""],["said Sancho.","산초가 말했다."]],[["“And I also,”","\"나도 그러겠소.\""],["said the canon, “especially if he gives me his word as a knight not to leave us without our consent.”","참사회원이 말했다. \"특히 우리의 동의 없이는 우리를 떠나지 않겠다고 기사로서 약조해 준다면 말이오.\""]],[["Don Quixote, who was listening to all this, said, “I give it;-moreover one who is enchanted as I am cannot do as he likes with himself; for he who had enchanted him could prevent his moving from one place for three ages, and if he attempted to escape would bring him back flying.”--And that being so, they might as well release him, particularly as it would be to the advantage of all; for, if they did not let him out, he protested he would be unable to avoid offending their nostrils unless they kept their distance.","이 모든 말을 듣고 있던 돈키호테가 말했다. \"그 약조를 드리겠소. 더구나 나처럼 마법에 걸린 자는 제 뜻대로 제 몸을 어찌할 수가 없소. 그를 마법에 건 자가 그를 세 시대 동안 한곳에서 움직이지 못하게 할 수도 있고, 만일 그가 달아나려 든다면 날아오게 하여 도로 끌고 올 수도 있는 까닭이오.\" 그러니 사정이 그러하므로 그들이 자기를 풀어 주어도 무방하며, 특히 그것이 모두에게 이로운 일이라고 했다. 만일 자기를 내보내 주지 않는다면, 그들이 멀찍이 떨어져 있지 않는 한 그들의 콧구멍을 거스르는 일을 피할 수 없으리라고 그는 단언했다."]],[["The canon took his hand, tied together as they both were, and on his word and promise they unbound him, and rejoiced beyond measure he was to find himself out of the cage.","참사회원은 둘 다 묶여 있는 채로 그의 손을 잡았고, 그의 말과 약속을 믿고 그를 풀어 주었으니, 그는 우리 밖으로 나오게 된 것이 이루 말할 수 없이 기뻤다. 그가 맨 처음 한 일은 온몸을 쭉 펴는 것이었고, 그런 다음 로시난테가 서 있는 곳으로 가서 그 엉덩이를 두어 번 두드리며 말했다."],["The first thing he did was to stretch himself all over, and then he went to where Rocinante was standing and giving him a couple of slaps on the haunches said, “I still trust in God and in his blessed mother, O flower and mirror of steeds, that we shall soon see ourselves, both of us, as we wish to be, thou with thy master on thy back, and I mounted upon thee, following the calling for which God sent me into the world.”","\"준마들의 꽃이요 거울인 그대여, 나는 여전히 하느님과 그 복되신 어머니를 믿노라. 우리 둘 다 머지않아 바라던 그 모습으로 다시 서게 되리니, 그대는 네 주인을 등에 태우고, 나는 그대 위에 올라타, 하느님께서 나를 이 세상에 보내신 그 소명을 좇으리라.\""],["And so saying, accompanied by Sancho, he withdrew to a retired spot, from which he came back much relieved and more eager than ever to put his squire’s scheme into execution.","그러고는 그렇게 말하며 산초를 데리고 외진 곳으로 물러갔다가, 한결 홀가분해져서, 종자의 계획을 실행에 옮기고 싶은 마음이 그 어느 때보다 간절해진 채로 돌아왔다."]],[["The canon gazed at him, wondering at the extraordinary nature of his madness, and that in all his remarks and replies he should show such excellent sense, and only lose his stirrups, as has been already said, when the subject of chivalry was broached.","참사회원은 그를 바라보며, 그 광기의 유별난 성질에 놀랐으니, 그의 모든 말과 대답에서 그토록 훌륭한 분별을 보이다가도, 앞서 말한 대로 기사도라는 주제가 나오기만 하면 그만 등자를 놓치는 것이었다."],["And so, moved by compassion, he said to him, as they all sat on the green grass awaiting the arrival of the provisions:","그리하여 연민에 사로잡혀, 모두가 푸른 풀밭에 앉아 음식이 도착하기를 기다리는 동안 그는 돈키호테에게 말했다."]],[["“Is it possible, gentle sir, that the nauseous and idle reading of books of chivalry can have had such an effect on your worship as to upset your reason so that you fancy yourself enchanted, and the like, all as far from the truth as falsehood itself is?","\"점잖으신 분이여, 그 메스껍고 부질없는 기사도 책 읽기가 당신의 이성을 뒤엎어 스스로 마법에 걸렸다고 믿게 하고, 거짓 그 자체만큼이나 진실에서 동떨어진 그런 온갖 것들을 믿게 할 만큼 당신에게 그토록 큰 영향을 미쳤다는 것이 가능한 일입니까?"],["How can there be any human understanding that can persuade itself there ever was all that infinity of Amadises in the world, or all that multitude of famous knights, all those emperors of Trebizond, all those Felixmartes of Hircania, all those palfreys, and damsels-errant, and serpents, and monsters, and giants, and marvellous adventures, and enchantments of every kind, and battles, and prodigious encounters, splendid costumes, love-sick princesses, squires made counts, droll dwarfs, love letters, billings and cooings, swashbuckler women, and, in a word, all that nonsense the books of chivalry contain?","세상에 그 무한히 많은 아마디스가 있었다거나, 그 수많은 이름난 기사들, 그 모든 트레비손다의 황제들, 히르카니아의 펠릭스마르테들, 그 모든 말들과 편력하는 시녀들과 뱀들과 괴물들과 거인들, 온갖 종류의 놀라운 모험과 마법, 전투와 굉장한 격돌, 화려한 의상, 사랑에 빠진 공주들, 백작이 된 종자들, 익살맞은 난쟁이들, 연애편지, 사랑의 속삭임, 칼 휘두르는 여인들, 한마디로 기사도 책에 담긴 그 모든 허튼소리가 다 있었다고 스스로 납득시킬 수 있는 인간의 지성이 어디 있겠습니까? 저로 말하자면, 그것들을 읽을 때 그것들이 모두 거짓이요 부질없는 것임을 멈춰 생각하지만 않는다면 어느 정도 즐거움을 얻는다고는 말할 수 있습니다."],["For myself, I can only say that when I read them, so long as I do not stop to think that they are all lies and frivolity, they give me a certain amount of pleasure; but when I come to consider what they are, I fling the very best of them at the wall, and would fling it into the fire if there were one at hand, as richly deserving such punishment as cheats and impostors out of the range of ordinary toleration, and as founders of new sects and modes of life, and teachers that lead the ignorant public to believe and accept as truth all the folly they contain.","그러나 그것들이 무엇인지를 따져 보기에 이르면, 그중 가장 훌륭한 것이라도 벽에 내던지고, 곁에 불이 있다면 불 속에 던져 넣고 싶어집니다. 보통의 관용 범위를 벗어난 사기꾼이요 협잡꾼으로서, 새로운 종파와 삶의 방식을 세운 자요, 무지한 대중을 이끌어 그 안에 담긴 온갖 어리석음을 진실로 믿고 받아들이게 하는 선생으로서, 그런 벌을 받아 마땅하니 말입니다."],["And such is their audacity, they even dare to unsettle the wits of gentlemen of birth and intelligence, as is shown plainly by the way they have served your worship, when they have brought you to such a pass that you have to be shut up in a cage and carried on an ox-cart as one would carry a lion or a tiger from place to place to make money by showing it.","게다가 그 책들의 대담함이 어찌나 큰지, 가문 좋고 지성 있는 신사들의 정신마저 어지럽힐 엄두를 내니, 그것들이 당신을 어떤 지경에 이르게 했는지 — 당신을 우리에 가두고, 돈벌이를 위해 사자나 호랑이를 이곳저곳 끌고 다니며 구경시키듯 소달구지에 실어 옮기게 만든 그 처지가 — 명백히 보여 주는 바입니다."],["Come, Señor Don Quixote, have some compassion for yourself, return to the bosom of common sense, and make use of the liberal share of it that heaven has been pleased to bestow upon you, employing your abundant gifts of mind in some other reading that may serve to benefit your conscience and add to your honour.","자, 돈키호테 님, 당신 자신에게 좀 연민을 가지시고, 상식의 품으로 돌아오시어, 하늘이 당신에게 기꺼이 베풀어 주신 그 넉넉한 몫을 활용하십시오. 당신의 풍부한 정신적 재능을 당신의 양심에 이로움을 주고 당신의 명예를 더해 줄 만한 다른 책 읽기에 쓰시면서 말입니다."],["And if, still led away by your natural bent, you desire to read books of achievements and of chivalry, read the Book of Judges in the Holy Scriptures, for there you will find grand reality, and deeds as true as they are heroic.","그래도 여전히 타고난 성향에 이끌려 무훈과 기사도에 관한 책을 읽고 싶으시거든, 성경의 사사기를 읽으십시오. 거기서 당신은 웅장한 실제와, 영웅적인 만큼이나 진실한 행적들을 보게 될 것입니다."],["Lusitania had a Viriatus, Rome a Caesar, Carthage a Hannibal, Greece an Alexander, Castile a Count Fernan Gonzalez, Valencia a Cid, Andalusia a Gonzalo Fernandez, Estremadura a Diego Garcia de Paredes, Jerez a Garci Perez de Vargas, Toledo a Garcilaso, Seville a Don Manuel de Leon, to read of whose valiant deeds will entertain and instruct the loftiest minds and fill them with delight and wonder.","루시타니아에는 비리아투스가 있었고, 로마에는 카이사르가, 카르타고에는 한니발이, 그리스에는 알렉산드로스가, 카스티야에는 페르난 곤살레스 백작이, 발렌시아에는 시드가, 안달루시아에는 곤살로 페르난데스가, 에스트레마두라에는 디에고 가르시아 데 파레데스가, 헤레스에는 가르시 페레스 데 바르가스가, 톨레도에는 가르실라소가, 세비야에는 돈 마누엘 데 레온이 있었으니, 그들의 용맹한 행적을 읽는 것은 더없이 고매한 정신마저 즐겁게 하고 가르치며 기쁨과 경이로 채워 줄 것입니다. 여기에야말로, 돈키호테 님, 당신의 건전한 지성에 합당한 읽을거리가 있습니다."],["Here, Señor Don Quixote, will be reading worthy of your sound understanding; from which you will rise learned in history, in love with virtue, strengthened in goodness, improved in manners, brave without rashness, prudent without cowardice; and all to the honour of God, your own advantage and the glory of La Mancha, whence, I am informed, your worship derives your birth.”","그것을 통해 당신은 역사에 박식해지고, 미덕을 사랑하며, 선함이 굳건해지고, 행실이 나아지며, 무모하지 않으면서 용감하고, 비겁하지 않으면서 신중한 사람으로 일어서게 될 것입니다. 이 모든 것이 하느님의 영광이요, 당신 자신의 이로움이며, 제가 듣기로 당신께서 태어나신 곳인 라만차의 영예가 될 것입니다.\""]],[["Don Quixote listened with the greatest attention to the canon’s words, and when he found he had finished, after regarding him for some time, he replied to him:","돈키호테는 참사회원의 말을 더없이 주의 깊게 듣고 있다가, 그가 말을 마쳤음을 알자 한동안 그를 바라본 뒤 이렇게 대답했다."]],[["“It appears to me, gentle sir, that your worship’s discourse is intended to persuade me that there never were any knights-errant in the world, and that all the books of chivalry are false, lying, mischievous and useless to the State, and that I have done wrong in reading them, and worse in believing them, and still worse in imitating them, when I undertook to follow the arduous calling of knight-errantry which they set forth; for you deny that there ever were Amadises of Gaul or of Greece, or any other of the knights of whom the books are full.”","\"점잖으신 분이여, 제가 보기에 당신의 말씀은 세상에 편력 기사란 결코 없었으며, 기사도 책이란 모두 거짓되고 허위에 가득 차며 해롭고 나라에 쓸모없는 것이라고, 그래서 제가 그것들을 읽은 것이 잘못이고, 믿은 것은 더 큰 잘못이며, 그 책들이 펼쳐 보인 편력 기사라는 그 험난한 소명을 좇기로 했을 때 그것들을 흉내 낸 것은 더더욱 큰 잘못이라고 저를 설득하려는 데 그 뜻이 있는 듯합니다. 당신은 갈리아의 아마디스나 그리스의 아마디스, 혹은 그 책들에 가득한 다른 어떤 기사도 결코 없었다고 부정하시니 말입니다.\""]],[["“It is all exactly as you state it,”","\"모두 당신이 말씀하신 그대로입니다.\""],["said the canon; to which Don Quixote returned, “You also went on to say that books of this kind had done me much harm, inasmuch as they had upset my senses, and shut me up in a cage, and that it would be better for me to reform and change my studies, and read other truer books which would afford more pleasure and instruction.”","참사회원이 말하자, 돈키호테가 받아 말했다. \"당신은 또한 이런 종류의 책들이 제 정신을 뒤엎고 저를 우리에 가두었으니 제게 큰 해를 끼쳤다고, 그래서 제가 마음을 고쳐 공부를 바꾸어, 더 큰 즐거움과 가르침을 줄 더 진실한 다른 책들을 읽는 편이 나으리라는 말씀까지 하셨습니다.\""]],[["“Just so,”","\"바로 그렇습니다.\""],["said the canon.","참사회원이 말했다."]],[["“Well then,”","\"그렇다면,\""],["returned Don Quixote, “to my mind it is you who are the one that is out of his wits and enchanted, as you have ventured to utter such blasphemies against a thing so universally acknowledged and accepted as true that whoever denies it, as you do, deserves the same punishment which you say you inflict on the books that irritate you when you read them.","돈키호테가 받아 말했다. \"제가 보기에는 오히려 당신이야말로 제정신이 아니고 마법에 걸린 사람입니다."],["For to try to persuade anybody that Amadis, and all the other knights-adventurers with whom the books are filled, never existed, would be like trying to persuade him that the sun does not yield light, or ice cold, or earth nourishment.","진실로 그토록 널리 인정되고 받아들여진 것에 대해 그런 신성모독을 감히 입에 올리셨으니 말입니다. 당신처럼 그것을 부정하는 자는 누구든, 당신이 읽다가 거슬리는 책들에 가한다고 말씀하신 바로 그 벌을 받아 마땅합니다."],["What wit in the world can persuade another that the story of the Princess Floripes and Guy of Burgundy is not true, or that of Fierabras and the bridge of Mantible, which happened in the time of Charlemagne?","아마디스와, 그 책들에 가득한 다른 모든 모험 기사들이 결코 존재하지 않았다고 누군가를 설득하려는 것은, 해가 빛을 주지 않는다거나, 얼음이 차갑지 않다거나, 땅이 양식을 내지 않는다고 그를 설득하려는 것과 마찬가지일 것입니다. 세상의 어떤 지혜가, 플로리페스 공주와 부르고뉴의 기 이야기가 진실이 아니라거나, 샤를마뉴 시대에 일어난 피에라브라스와 만티블레 다리 이야기가 진실이 아니라고 남을 납득시킬 수 있겠습니까?"],["For by all that is good it is as true as that it is daylight now; and if it be a lie, it must be a lie too that there was a Hector, or Achilles, or Trojan war, or Twelve Peers of France, or Arthur of England, who still lives changed into a raven, and is unceasingly looked for in his kingdom.","좋은 모든 것을 두고 맹세컨대, 그것은 지금이 대낮인 것만큼이나 진실입니다. 만일 그것이 거짓이라면, 헥토르나 아킬레우스나 트로이 전쟁이나 프랑스의 열두 용사나, 아직도 까마귀로 변한 채 살아 있어 그 왕국에서 끊임없이 사람들이 찾고 있다는 잉글랜드의 아서가 있었다는 것 또한 거짓이어야 할 것입니다."],["One might just as well try to make out that the history of Guarino Mezquino, or of the quest of the Holy Grail, is false, or that the loves of Tristram and the Queen Yseult are apocryphal, as well as those of Guinevere and Lancelot, when there are persons who can almost remember having seen the Dame Quintanona, who was the best cupbearer in Great Britain.","차라리 과리노 메스키노의 이야기나 성배를 찾아 떠난 탐색의 이야기가 거짓이라거나, 트리스탄과 이졸데 왕비의 사랑이, 그리고 기네비어와 랜슬롯의 사랑이 거짓이라고 우기는 편이 나을 것입니다. 그레이트브리튼 최고의 술 시중꾼이었던 킨타뇨나 부인을 보았던 일을 거의 기억할 수 있는 사람들이 있는데도 말입니다."],["And so true is this, that I recollect a grandmother of mine on the father’s side, whenever she saw any dame in a venerable hood, used to say to me, ‘Grandson, that one is like Dame Quintanona,’ from which I conclude that she must have known her, or at least had managed to see some portrait of her.","이것이 어찌나 진실인지, 저는 친가 쪽 제 할머니 한 분을 기억하는데, 그분은 점잖은 두건을 쓴 부인을 볼 때마다 제게 이렇게 말씀하시곤 했습니다. '얘야, 저분이 킨타뇨나 부인을 닮았구나.'"],["Then who can deny that the story of Pierres and the fair Magalona is true, when even to this day may be seen in the king’s armoury the pin with which the valiant Pierres guided the wooden horse he rode through the air, and it is a trifle bigger than the pole of a cart?","그래서 저는 할머니께서 그분을 아셨거나, 적어도 그분의 초상을 어떻게든 보신 적이 있었으리라고 결론짓습니다. 그러니 피에레스와 아리따운 마갈로나의 이야기가 진실임을 누가 부정할 수 있겠습니까?"],["And alongside of the pin is Babieca’s saddle, and at Roncesvalles there is Roland’s horn, as large as a large beam; whence we may infer that there were Twelve Peers, and a Pierres, and a Cid, and other knights like them, of the sort people commonly call adventurers.","오늘날까지도 왕의 무기고에는 용맹한 피에레스가 공중을 가르며 탔던 그 나무 말을 조종하던 핀이 보이는데, 그것은 짐수레의 끌채보다 조금 더 클 정도입니다. 그 핀 곁에는 바비에카의 안장이 있고, 론세스바예스에는 큰 들보만큼이나 큰 롤단의 뿔나팔이 있으니, 그로 미루어 우리는 열두 용사와 피에레스와 시드와, 흔히 사람들이 모험가라 부르는 그런 부류의 다른 기사들이 있었음을 추론할 수 있습니다."],["Or perhaps I shall be told, too, that there was no such knight-errant as the valiant Lusitanian Juan de Merlo, who went to Burgundy and in the city of Arras fought with the famous lord of Charny, Mosen Pierres by name, and afterwards in the city of Basle with Mosen Enrique de Remesten, coming out of both encounters covered with fame and honour; or adventures and challenges achieved and delivered, also in Burgundy, by the valiant Spaniards Pedro Barba and Gutierre Quixada (of whose family I come in the direct male line), when they vanquished the sons of the Count of San Polo.","어쩌면 누군가는 제게, 부르고뉴로 가서 아라스 시에서 샤르니의 이름난 영주, 모센 피에레스라는 자와 싸우고, 그 뒤 바젤 시에서 모센 엔리케 데 레메스텐과 맞붙어 두 격전 모두에서 명성과 영예로 뒤덮여 나온 그 용맹한 루시타니아인 후안 데 메를로 같은 편력 기사는 없었다고 말할지도 모릅니다. 또 역시 부르고뉴에서 산 폴로 백작의 아들들을 무찔렀던 용맹한 스페인 사람 페드로 바르바와 구티에레 키하다(저는 이 가문의 직계 남자 혈통으로 이어집니다)가 이루고 응한 모험과 도전들도 없었다고 말이지요."],["I shall be told, too, that Don Fernando de Guevara did not go in quest of adventures to Germany, where he engaged in combat with Micer George, a knight of the house of the Duke of Austria.","또한 제게, 돈 페르난도 데 게바라가 모험을 찾아 독일로 가서 오스트리아 공작 가문의 기사인 미세르 호르헤와 결투를 벌인 일도 없었다고 말할 것입니다. '파소'의 그 사람, 수에로 데 키뇨네스의 마상 시합과, 카스티야 기사 돈 곤살로 데 구스만에 맞선 모센 루이스 데 팔세스의 무훈도 한낱 우스갯거리에 불과했다고 말하겠지요."],["I shall be told that the jousts of Suero de Quinones, him of the ‘Paso,’ and the emprise of Mosen Luis de Falces against the Castilian knight, Don Gonzalo de Guzman, were mere mockeries; as well as many other achievements of Christian knights of these and foreign realms, which are so authentic and true, that, I repeat, he who denies them must be totally wanting in reason and good sense.”","그 밖에도 이 나라와 외국 영토의 기독교 기사들이 이룬 수많은 다른 행적들도 그러하다고 말입니다. 그것들은 어찌나 확실하고 진실한지, 거듭 말하거니와, 그것들을 부정하는 자는 이성과 양식이 완전히 결여된 자임이 틀림없습니다.\""]],[["The canon was amazed to hear the medley of truth and fiction Don Quixote uttered, and to see how well acquainted he was with everything relating or belonging to the achievements of his knight-errantry; so he said in reply:","참사회원은 돈키호테가 쏟아 내는 진실과 허구의 뒤범벅을 듣고, 또 그가 자기 편력 기사도의 행적과 관련되거나 거기 속한 모든 것에 대해 얼마나 훤히 꿰고 있는지를 보고 깜짝 놀랐다. 그래서 그는 이렇게 대답했다."]],[["“I cannot deny, Señor Don Quixote, that there is some truth in what you say, especially as regards the Spanish knights-errant; and I am willing to grant too that the Twelve Peers of France existed, but I am not disposed to believe that they did all the things that the Archbishop Turpin relates of them. For the truth of the matter is they were knights chosen by the kings of France, and called ‘Peers’ because they were all equal in worth, rank and prowess (at least if they were not they ought to have been), and it was a kind of religious order like those of Santiago and Calatrava in the present day, in which it is assumed that those who take it are valiant knights of distinction and good birth; and just as we say now a Knight of St. John, or of Alcantara, they used to say then a Knight of the Twelve Peers, because twelve equals were chosen for that military order. That there was a Cid, as well as a Bernardo del Carpio, there can be no doubt; but that they did the deeds people say they did, I hold to be very doubtful. In that other matter of the pin of Count Pierres that you speak of, and say is near Babieca’s saddle in the Armoury, I confess my sin; for I am either so stupid or so short-sighted, that, though I have seen the saddle, I have never been able to see the pin, in spite of it being as big as your worship says it is.”","\"돈키호테 님, 당신 말씀에 어느 정도 진실이 있음은 부정할 수 없습니다. 특히 스페인 편력 기사들에 관한 한 그러합니다. 또 프랑스의 열두 용사가 존재했다는 것도 기꺼이 인정하겠습니다. 다만 대주교 투르핀이 그들에 관해 이야기한 그 모든 일을 그들이 다 했다고는 믿고 싶지 않습니다. 사실인즉슨 그들은 프랑스 왕들이 가려 뽑은 기사들로서, 모두가 가치와 지위와 무용에서 동등했기에(적어도 그렇지 않았다면 응당 그래야 했기에) '용사'라 불렸던 것입니다. 그것은 오늘날의 산티아고나 칼라트라바 같은 일종의 종교 기사단이었으니, 그 단에 드는 자는 명망 있고 가문 좋은 용맹한 기사라고 여겨졌습니다. 또 지금 우리가 성 요한의 기사니 알칸타라의 기사니 하고 말하듯, 그들은 그때 열두 용사의 기사라 말했던 것이니, 그 무사 기단을 위해 동등한 열둘을 가려 뽑았기 때문입니다. 시드가 있었다는 것, 또 베르나르도 델 카르피오가 있었다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 그러나 사람들이 말하는 그런 행적을 그들이 했다는 것은 저로서는 매우 의심스럽게 여겨집니다. 당신이 말씀하신, 무기고에서 바비에카의 안장 곁에 있다는 그 피에레스 백작의 핀이라는 또 다른 문제에 대해서는, 제 죄를 고백하겠습니다. 저는 너무 어리석거나 너무 눈이 어두운 탓인지, 안장은 보았으나, 당신께서 말씀하신 만큼 그토록 크다는데도 그 핀만은 도무지 본 적이 없으니 말입니다.\""]],[["“For all that it is there, without any manner of doubt,”","\"그래도 그것은 그곳에 분명히 있습니다, 조금도 의심할 바 없이.\""],["said Don Quixote; “and more by token they say it is inclosed in a sheath of cowhide to keep it from rusting.”","돈키호테가 말했다. \"더구나 듣자니 그것은 녹슬지 않도록 쇠가죽 칼집에 넣어져 있다고 합니다.\""]],[["“All that may be,”","\"그럴 수도 있겠지요.\""],["replied the canon; “but, by the orders I have received, I do not remember seeing it.","참사회원이 대답했다. \"그러나 제가 받은 성직을 두고 말하건대, 저는 그것을 본 기억이 없습니다."],["However, granting it is there, that is no reason why I am bound to believe the stories of all those Amadises and of all that multitude of knights they tell us about, nor is it reasonable that a man like your worship, so worthy, and with so many good qualities, and endowed with such a good understanding, should allow himself to be persuaded that such wild crazy things as are written in those absurd books of chivalry are really true.”","어쨌든 그것이 그곳에 있다고 인정한다 해도, 그것이 그 모든 아마디스들과, 그들이 우리에게 이야기하는 그 수많은 기사들의 이야기를 제가 마땅히 믿어야 할 이유가 되지는 않습니다. 또한 당신처럼 훌륭하고 그토록 많은 좋은 자질을 갖추고, 그토록 뛰어난 지성을 타고난 분이, 그런 허무맹랑한 기사도 책에 적힌 그 거칠고 미친 것들이 정말로 진실이라고 스스로 납득하도록 내버려 두는 것은 도무지 사리에 맞지 않습니다.\""]],[["# CHAPTER L. OF THE SHREWD CONTROVERSY WHICH DON QUIXOTE AND THE CANON HELD, TOGETHER WITH OTHER INCIDENTS","# CHAPTER L."]],[["“A good joke, that!” returned Don Quixote. “Books that have been printed with the king’s licence, and with the approbation of those to whom they have been submitted, and read with universal delight, and extolled by great and small, rich and poor, learned and ignorant, gentle and simple, in a word by people of every sort, of whatever rank or condition they may be--that these should be lies! And above all when they carry such an appearance of truth with them; for they tell us the father, mother, country, kindred, age, place, and the achievements, step by step, and day by day, performed by such a knight or knights! Hush, sir; utter not such blasphemy; trust me I am advising you now to act as a sensible man should; only read them, and you will see the pleasure you will derive from them. For, come, tell me, can there be anything more delightful than to see, as it were, here now displayed before us a vast lake of bubbling pitch with a host of snakes and serpents and lizards, and ferocious and terrible creatures of all sorts swimming about in it, while from the middle of the lake there comes a plaintive voice saying: ‘Knight, whosoever thou art who beholdest this dread lake, if thou wouldst win the prize that lies hidden beneath these dusky waves, prove the valour of thy stout heart and cast thyself into the midst of its dark burning waters, else thou shalt not be worthy to see the mighty wonders contained in the seven castles of the seven Fays that lie beneath this black expanse;’ and then the knight, almost ere the awful voice has ceased, without stopping to consider, without pausing to reflect upon the danger to which he is exposing himself, without even relieving himself of the weight of his massive armour, commending himself to God and to his lady, plunges into the midst of the boiling lake, and when he little looks for it, or knows what his fate is to be, he finds himself among flowery meadows, with which the Elysian fields are not to be compared.","“이거 참 우습기도 하구려!” 돈키호테가 대꾸했다. “국왕의 허가를 받아 인쇄되고, 심의를 맡은 이들의 인준을 거쳐, 만인이 즐겁게 읽고, 신분 높은 자나 낮은 자나, 부자나 가난한 자나, 학식 있는 자나 무지한 자나, 양반이나 평민이나, 한마디로 어떤 계급이든 어떤 처지든 온갖 부류의 사람들이 입을 모아 칭송하는 책들이라오. 그런 책들이 거짓이라니! 더구나 그토록 진실의 외양을 갖추고 있는데 말이오. 그 책들은 우리에게 어느 기사 혹은 기사들의 아버지와 어머니, 나라와 친족, 나이와 출신지, 그리고 그들이 한 발짝 한 발짝 날마다 이룬 위업들을 일러 주지 않소! 자, 그만하시오, 선생. 그런 불경한 말을 입에 담지 마시구려. 나를 믿고, 지금 내가 충고하는 대로 분별 있는 사람이 마땅히 할 일을 하시오. 그저 그 책들을 읽어 보시오. 그러면 그 책들에서 얻게 될 즐거움을 알게 될 것이오. 자, 어디 한번 말해 보시오. 바로 지금 우리 눈앞에 이런 광경이 펼쳐지는 것보다 더 흐뭇한 일이 어디 있겠소? 부글부글 끓어오르는 역청의 드넓은 호수가 펼쳐지고, 그 안에는 뱀과 살무사와 도마뱀과 온갖 사납고 무시무시한 짐승들이 우글우글 헤엄쳐 다니고, 그 호수 한복판에서 구슬픈 목소리가 들려오는 것이오. ‘기사여, 그대가 누구이든 이 무시무시한 호수를 바라보는 자여, 만일 그대가 이 어두운 물결 아래 숨겨진 상을 얻고자 한다면, 그대의 굳센 심장의 용맹을 증명하여 이 컴컴하고 불타는 물 한가운데로 몸을 던지라. 그러지 않으면 그대는 이 검은 수면 아래 일곱 요정의 일곱 성에 깃든 위대한 경이들을 볼 자격이 없으리라.’ 그러면 기사는 그 끔찍한 목소리가 채 그치기도 전에, 멈춰 서서 생각해 보지도 않고, 자신이 처할 위험을 헤아려 보려 잠시 멈추지도 않고, 심지어 묵직한 갑옷의 무게마저 벗어 내지 않은 채, 하느님과 자기 귀부인께 자신을 맡기고 그 펄펄 끓는 호수 한복판으로 뛰어든다오. 그리고 미처 예상치도 못하고 자기 운명이 어찌 될지 알지도 못하는 사이에, 엘리시온 들판도 견줄 수 없을 만큼 꽃이 만발한 풀밭 가운데에 있는 자신을 발견하게 되는 것이오."]],[["“The sky seems more transparent there, and the sun shines with a strange brilliancy, and a delightful grove of green leafy trees presents itself to the eyes and charms the sight with its verdure, while the ear is soothed by the sweet untutored melody of the countless birds of gay plumage that flit to and fro among the interlacing branches.","“그곳에선 하늘이 더욱 투명해 보이고, 태양은 야릇한 광채로 빛나며, 푸르고 잎이 무성한 나무들의 아늑한 숲이 눈앞에 펼쳐져 그 싱그러움으로 시선을 사로잡는다오. 한편 귀로는 얽히고설킨 가지 사이를 이리저리 날아다니는, 화려한 깃털을 두른 수많은 새들의 달콤하고 꾸밈없는 가락이 어루만져 주지요."],["Here he sees a brook whose limpid waters, like liquid crystal, ripple over fine sands and white pebbles that look like sifted gold and purest pearls.","여기에는 맑은 물이 액체 수정처럼 고운 모래와, 체로 거른 황금이나 더없이 순결한 진주처럼 보이는 하얀 자갈 위로 잔물결을 일으키며 흐르는 시냇물이 보이고."],["There he perceives a cunningly wrought fountain of many-coloured jasper and polished marble; here another of rustic fashion where the little mussel-shells and the spiral white and yellow mansions of the snail disposed in studious disorder, mingled with fragments of glittering crystal and mock emeralds, make up a work of varied aspect, where art, imitating nature, seems to have outdone it.","저기에는 갖가지 빛깔의 벽옥과 윤을 낸 대리석으로 정교하게 빚어 낸 샘이 눈에 들어오며, 또 여기에는 시골풍으로 만든 또 다른 샘이 있어, 작은 홍합 껍데기와 나선형으로 말린 희고 노란 달팽이 집들이 일부러 흐트러뜨린 듯 배치되어 반짝이는 수정 조각과 가짜 에메랄드와 어우러지니, 그것은 자연을 본뜬 기교가 도리어 자연을 능가한 듯한 다채로운 작품을 이루고 있다오."]],[["“Suddenly there is presented to his sight a strong castle or gorgeous palace with walls of massy gold, turrets of diamond and gates of jacinth; in short, so marvellous is its structure that though the materials of which it is built are nothing less than diamonds, carbuncles, rubies, pearls, gold, and emeralds, the workmanship is still more rare. And after having seen all this, what can be more charming than to see how a bevy of damsels comes forth from the gate of the castle in gay and gorgeous attire, such that, were I to set myself now to depict it as the histories describe it to us, I should never have done; and then how she who seems to be the first among them all takes the bold knight who plunged into the boiling lake by the hand, and without addressing a word to him leads him into the rich palace or castle, and strips him as naked as when his mother bore him, and bathes him in lukewarm water, and anoints him all over with sweet-smelling unguents, and clothes him in a shirt of the softest sendal, all scented and perfumed, while another damsel comes and throws over his shoulders a mantle which is said to be worth at the very least a city, and even more? How charming it is, then, when they tell us how, after all this, they lead him to another chamber where he finds the tables set out in such style that he is filled with amazement and wonder; to see how they pour out water for his hands distilled from amber and sweet-scented flowers; how they seat him on an ivory chair; to see how the damsels wait on him all in profound silence; how they bring him such a variety of dainties so temptingly prepared that the appetite is at a loss which to select; to hear the music that resounds while he is at table, by whom or whence produced he knows not. And then when the repast is over and the tables removed, for the knight to recline in the chair, picking his teeth perhaps as usual, and a damsel, much lovelier than any of the others, to enter unexpectedly by the chamber door, and herself by his side, and begin to tell him what the castle is, and how she is held enchanted there, and other things that amaze the knight and astonish the readers who are perusing his history.","“그러다 문득 그의 눈앞에 단단한 성, 혹은 화려한 궁전이 나타나는데, 그 성벽은 묵직한 황금으로 되어 있고, 망루는 다이아몬드, 성문은 히아신스 보석으로 되어 있소. 요컨대 그 구조가 어찌나 경이로운지, 그것을 지은 재료가 다이아몬드, 홍옥, 루비, 진주, 황금, 에메랄드에 다름 아니건만, 그 솜씨는 그보다도 더 희귀하다오. 그리고 이 모든 것을 본 다음, 한 무리의 처녀들이 화려하고 곱게 차려입고 성문에서 걸어 나오는 모습을 보는 것보다 더 흐뭇한 일이 어디 있겠소? 그 차림새는 어찌나 곱던지, 이제 내가 그 옛이야기들이 우리에게 묘사해 주는 그대로 그것을 그려 내려 든다면 결코 끝맺지 못할 것이오. 그러고는 그들 가운데 으뜸으로 보이는 처녀가 펄펄 끓는 호수에 몸을 던진 그 대담한 기사의 손을 잡고, 한마디 말도 건네지 않은 채 그를 으리으리한 궁전, 혹은 성 안으로 이끌어, 어머니가 그를 낳았을 때처럼 발가벗기고는, 미지근한 물에 목욕시키고, 향기로운 연고를 온몸에 발라 주며, 더없이 부드러운 비단 셔츠를 입혀 주는데, 그 셔츠는 온통 향기를 머금고 있소. 그러는 동안 또 다른 처녀가 와서 그의 어깨에 망토를 걸쳐 주는데, 그 망토는 적어도 도시 하나 값어치는 되고, 어쩌면 그보다 더하다고들 한다오. 그러니 이 모든 일이 끝난 뒤에 그 처녀들이 그를 또 다른 방으로 이끌고, 거기에서 그가 어찌나 멋들어지게 차려진 식탁들을 보았던지 경탄과 경이로 가득 차게 되었다는 이야기를 들려줄 때, 그것이 또 얼마나 흐뭇하겠소. 그들이 호박과 향기로운 꽃에서 증류해 낸 물을 그의 두 손에 부어 주는 모습이며, 상아 의자에 그를 앉히는 모습이며, 처녀들이 깊은 침묵 속에서 그의 시중을 드는 모습이며, 너무도 먹음직스럽게 차려진 갖가지 진미를 가져다주어 어느 것을 골라야 할지 입맛이 갈피를 못 잡는 모습이며, 그가 식탁에 앉아 있는 동안 누가 어디서 빚어내는지도 모를 음악이 울려 퍼지는 소리를 듣는 모습까지 말이오. 그러고 나서 식사가 끝나고 식탁들이 치워지면, 기사는 여느 때처럼 어쩌면 이를 쑤시며 의자에 기대어 있고, 다른 어느 처녀보다도 한결 어여쁜 처녀가 뜻밖에 방문으로 들어와 그의 곁에 앉아서는, 이 성이 무엇인지, 그리고 자신이 어떻게 이곳에 마법에 걸려 갇혀 있는지를 이야기하기 시작하고, 그 밖에 기사를 놀라게 하고 그의 이야기를 읽는 독자들을 경탄케 하는 온갖 것들을 들려준다오."]],[["“But I will not expatiate any further upon this, as it may be gathered from it that whatever part of whatever history of a knight-errant one reads, it will fill the reader, whoever he be, with delight and wonder; and take my advice, sir, and, as I said before, read these books and you will see how they will banish any melancholy you may feel and raise your spirits should they be depressed. For myself I can say that since I have been a knight-errant I have become valiant, polite, generous, well-bred, magnanimous, courteous, dauntless, gentle, patient, and have learned to bear hardships, imprisonments, and enchantments; and though it be such a short time since I have seen myself shut up in a cage like a madman, I hope by the might of my arm, if heaven aid me and fortune thwart me not, to see myself king of some kingdom where I may be able to show the gratitude and generosity that dwell in my heart; for by my faith, señor, the poor man is incapacitated from showing the virtue of generosity to anyone, though he may possess it in the highest degree; and gratitude that consists of disposition only is a dead thing, just as faith without works is dead. For this reason I should be glad were fortune soon to offer me some opportunity of making myself an emperor, so as to show my heart in doing good to my friends, particularly to this poor Sancho Panza, my squire, who is the best fellow in the world; and I would gladly give him a county I have promised him this ever so long, only that I am afraid he has not the capacity to govern his realm.”","“그러나 이 일을 더 길게 늘어놓지는 않겠소. 다만 이로써 알 수 있는 것은, 편력기사의 그 어떤 이야기든 그 어느 대목을 읽더라도, 읽는 이가 누구든 간에 그를 즐거움과 경이로 가득 채워 주리라는 점이오. 그러니 내 충고를 들으시오, 선생. 앞서 말했듯이 이 책들을 읽어 보시오. 그러면 이 책들이 선생이 느낄 법한 어떤 우울도 어떻게 몰아내 주는지, 의기소침할 때면 어떻게 기운을 북돋아 주는지 알게 될 것이오. 나로 말할 것 같으면, 편력기사가 된 이후로 나는 용감하고 정중하고 너그럽고 예의 바르고 도량이 넓고 공손하고 두려움을 모르고 온화하고 인내심 있는 사람이 되었으며, 온갖 고난과 옥살이와 마법을 견디는 법을 배웠다고 말할 수 있소. 그리고 내가 미치광이처럼 우리에 갇힌 신세가 된 지 비록 이토록 얼마 되지 않았지만, 하늘이 나를 돕고 운명이 나를 가로막지 않는다면, 이 팔의 힘으로 어느 왕국의 왕이 되어 내 마음속에 깃든 감사와 너그러움을 보일 수 있게 되기를 바라오. 정말이지, 선생, 가난한 자는 비록 너그러움의 미덕을 더없이 드높이 지니고 있다 한들 누구에게도 그것을 베풀 수 없는 처지이고, 마음가짐만으로 그치는 감사란 죽은 것이니, 행함이 없는 믿음이 죽은 것과 마찬가지요. 이런 까닭에 운명이 머지않아 내게 황제가 될 어떤 기회를 베풀어 주어, 내가 벗들에게, 특히 세상에서 둘도 없이 좋은 친구인 내 종자 이 가엾은 산초 판사에게 선을 베풀며 내 마음을 보일 수 있게 된다면 더없이 기쁘겠소. 진작부터 그에게 약속해 둔 백작령을 기꺼이 그에게 주고 싶으나, 다만 그가 자기 영지를 다스릴 만한 역량이 없을까 봐 그것이 걱정될 따름이오.”"]],[["Sancho partly heard these last words of his master, and said to him, “Strive hard you, Señor Don Quixote, to give me that county so often promised by you and so long looked for by me, for I promise you there will be no want of capacity in me to govern it; and even if there is, I have heard say there are men in the world who farm seigniories, paying so much a year, and they themselves taking charge of the government, while the lord, with his legs stretched out, enjoys the revenue they pay him, without troubling himself about anything else. That’s what I’ll do, and not stand haggling over trifles, but wash my hands at once of the whole business, and enjoy my rents like a duke, and let things go their own way.”","산초는 주인의 이 마지막 말을 어느 정도 알아듣고는 그에게 말했다. “돈키호테 나리, 그토록 자주 약속하셨고 제가 그토록 오래 기다려 온 그 백작령을 부디 힘써 주십시오. 약속하건대 저는 그것을 다스리는 데 역량이 모자라지 않을 것입니다요. 설령 모자란다 해도, 세상에는 영지를 한 해 얼마씩 바치고 도지로 부쳐서, 정작 통치는 자기들이 도맡아 하고, 영주는 다리를 쭉 뻗고 앉아 그들이 바치는 수익을 누리며 다른 일에는 신경도 쓰지 않는다는 자들이 있다고 들었습니다요. 저도 바로 그렇게 할 작정입니다요. 자질구레한 일로 흥정하며 다투지 않고, 단번에 그 골치 아픈 일에서 손을 씻어 버리고는, 공작님처럼 제 소작료나 누리면서 만사는 제멋대로 흘러가게 둘 작정입니다요.”"]],[["“That, brother Sancho,” said the canon, “only holds good as far as the enjoyment of the revenue goes; but the lord of the seigniory must attend to the administration of justice, and here capacity and sound judgment come in, and above all a firm determination to find out the truth; for if this be wanting in the beginning, the middle and the end will always go wrong; and God as commonly aids the honest intentions of the simple as he frustrates the evil designs of the crafty.”","“산초 형제,” 사제 참사회원이 말했다. “그것은 어디까지나 수익을 누리는 데에만 들어맞는 이야기일세. 그러나 영지의 영주는 재판을 다스리는 일에 마음을 써야 하는데, 여기에는 역량과 건전한 판단력이 필요하고, 무엇보다도 진실을 밝혀내려는 굳건한 결의가 필요하다네. 처음에 이것이 없으면 중간도 끝도 언제나 어그러지고 말 테니까. 하느님께서는 흔히 단순한 자들의 정직한 뜻을 도우시는 만큼이나, 교활한 자들의 사악한 책략을 좌절시키신다네.”"]],[["“I don’t understand those philosophies,” returned Sancho Panza; “all I know is I would I had the county as soon as I shall know how to govern it; for I have as much soul as another, and as much body as anyone, and I shall be as much king of my realm as any other of his; and being so I should do as I liked, and doing as I liked I should please myself, and pleasing myself I should be content, and when one is content he has nothing more to desire, and when one has nothing more to desire there is an end of it; so let the county come, and God he with you, and let us see one another, as one blind man said to the other.”","“저는 그런 철학들은 모르겠습니다요.” 산초 판사가 대꾸했다. “제가 아는 거라곤, 그것을 다스릴 줄 알게 되는 즉시 그 백작령을 갖고 싶다는 것뿐입니다요. 저도 남만큼 영혼이 있고, 누구 못지않게 몸뚱이도 있으니, 다른 왕이 자기 왕국의 왕이듯 저도 제 영지의 왕이 될 것입니다요. 그리되면 저는 하고 싶은 대로 할 것이고, 하고 싶은 대로 하면 제 마음에 들 것이고, 제 마음에 들면 만족할 것이고, 사람이 만족하면 더 바랄 것이 없고, 더 바랄 것이 없으면 그것으로 끝입니다요. 그러니 어서 그 백작령이나 들어오라지요. 그럼 안녕히, 또 봅시다, 어느 장님이 다른 장님에게 말했듯이 말입니다요.”"]],[["“That is not bad philosophy thou art talking, Sancho,”","“자네가 늘어놓는 것이 그리 나쁜 철학은 아닐세, 산초.”"],["said the canon; “but for all that there is a good deal to be said on this matter of counties.”","사제 참사회원이 말했다. “하지만 그래도 이 백작령 문제에 관해서는 할 말이 적지 않다네.”"]],[["To which Don Quixote returned, “I know not what more there is to be said; I only guide myself by the example set me by the great Amadis of Gaul, when he made his squire count of the Insula Firme; and so, without any scruples of conscience, I can make a count of Sancho Panza, for he is one of the best squires that ever knight-errant had.”","이에 돈키호테가 대꾸했다. “더 할 말이 무엇이 있는지 나는 모르겠소. 나로 말하면 그저 저 위대한 ‘갈리아의 아마디스’가 자기 종자를 인술라 피르메의 백작으로 삼았을 때 내게 보여 준 본보기를 따를 따름이오. 그러니 양심에 아무런 거리낌 없이 나는 산초 판사를 백작으로 삼을 수 있소. 그는 일찍이 편력기사가 거느린 종자 가운데 가장 훌륭한 자 중 하나이기 때문이오.”"]],[["The canon was astonished at the methodical nonsense (if nonsense be capable of method) that Don Quixote uttered, at the way in which he had described the adventure of the knight of the lake, at the impression that the deliberate lies of the books he read had made upon him, and lastly he marvelled at the simplicity of Sancho, who desired so eagerly to obtain the county his master had promised him.","사제 참사회원은 돈키호테가 늘어놓은, 조리 있는 헛소리(헛소리에 조리가 있을 수 있다면)에 놀랐고, 그가 호수의 기사 모험을 묘사한 방식에 놀랐으며, 그가 읽은 책들의 그 작정하고 꾸며 낸 거짓말들이 그에게 남긴 인상에 놀랐고, 마지막으로 주인이 약속한 백작령을 그토록 간절히 얻고자 하는 산초의 순박함에 경탄해 마지않았다."]],[["By this time the canon’s servants, who had gone to the inn to fetch the sumpter mule, had returned, and making a carpet and the green grass of the meadow serve as a table, they seated themselves in the shade of some trees and made their repast there, that the carter might not be deprived of the advantage of the spot, as has been already said.","이즈음 짐 싣는 노새를 가지러 주막으로 갔던 사제 참사회원의 하인들이 돌아왔고, 양탄자 한 장과 풀밭의 푸른 잔디를 식탁 삼아 그들은 몇몇 나무 그늘에 자리를 잡고 거기에서 식사를 했는데, 앞서 말한 대로 마부가 그 좋은 자리의 이점을 빼앗기지 않도록 하기 위함이었다."],["As they were eating they suddenly heard a loud noise and the sound of a bell that seemed to come from among some brambles and thick bushes that were close by, and the same instant they observed a beautiful goat, spotted all over black, white, and brown, spring out of the thicket with a goatherd after it, calling to it and uttering the usual cries to make it stop or turn back to the fold.","그들이 먹고 있는데 별안간 큰 소리와 함께 방울 소리가 들려왔으니, 그것은 가까이 있던 가시덤불과 무성한 수풀 사이에서 나는 듯했다. 바로 그 순간 그들은 온통 검고 희고 갈색 얼룩이 진 아름다운 염소 한 마리가 덤불에서 튀어나오고, 그 뒤를 염소지기 하나가 쫓아오며 멈추거나 우리로 돌아오게 하려고 으레 내지르는 소리를 외쳐 대는 것을 보았다."],["The fugitive goat, scared and frightened, ran towards the company as if seeking their protection and then stood still, and the goatherd coming up seized it by the horns and began to talk to it as if it were possessed of reason and understanding: “Ah wanderer, wanderer, Spotty, Spotty; how have you gone limping all this time?","달아나던 염소는 겁에 질려 마치 그들에게 보호를 청하기라도 하듯 일행 쪽으로 달려오더니 그 자리에 멈춰 섰고, 다가온 염소지기는 그 뿔을 붙잡고는 마치 그 짐승이 이성과 분별을 갖추기라도 한 양 말을 걸기 시작했다. “아, 떠돌이야, 떠돌이야, 얼룩아, 얼룩아, 그동안 어찌하여 이리 절뚝거리며 다녔느냐?"],["What wolves have frightened you, my daughter?","무슨 늑대가 너를 놀라게 했느냐, 내 딸아?"],["Won’t you tell me what is the matter, my beauty?","무슨 일인지 내게 말해 주지 않으련, 내 어여쁜 것아?"],["But what else can it be except that you are a she, and cannot keep quiet?","허나 네가 암컷이어서 가만히 있지 못하는 것 말고 무슨 까닭이 더 있겠느냐?"],["A plague on your humours and the humours of those you take after!","네 변덕과, 네가 닮은 것들의 변덕에 염병이나 들어라! 돌아오너라, 돌아와, 내 귀여운 것아."],["Come back, come back, my darling; and if you will not be so happy, at any rate you will be safe in the fold or with your companions; for if you who ought to keep and lead them, go wandering astray, what will become of them?”","네가 그리 행복하지 못할지언정, 적어도 우리 안에서나 네 동무들과 함께라면 안전할 것이 아니냐. 마땅히 저들을 지키고 이끌어야 할 네가 길을 잃고 헤맨다면, 저들은 대체 어찌 되겠느냐?”"]],[["The goatherd’s talk amused all who heard it, but especially the canon, who said to him, “As you live, brother, take it easy, and be not in such a hurry to drive this goat back to the fold; for, being a female, as you say, she will follow her natural instinct in spite of all you can do to prevent it.","염소지기의 말은 그것을 듣는 모든 이를 즐겁게 했으나, 특히 사제 참사회원이 그러하여 그에게 말했다. “여보게, 자네 목숨을 두고 부탁하건대, 마음을 좀 누그러뜨리고, 이 염소를 우리로 몰아넣으려고 그리 서두르지 말게나."],["Take this morsel and drink a sup, and that will soothe your irritation, and in the meantime the goat will rest herself,”","자네 말마따나 암컷이니, 자네가 아무리 막으려 애쓴들 그 타고난 본성을 따를 것이 아닌가. 이 고기 한 점 들고 한 모금 마시게나."],["and so saying, he handed him the loins of a cold rabbit on a fork.","그러면 화가 가라앉을 것이고, 그동안 염소도 쉴 것일세.” 이렇게 말하며 그는 차게 식은 토끼 뒷다리 살을 포크에 꽂아 그에게 건넸다."]],[["The goatherd took it with thanks, and drank and calmed himself, and then said, “I should be sorry if your worships were to take me for a simpleton for having spoken so seriously as I did to this animal; but the truth is there is a certain mystery in the words I used.","염소지기는 고맙다며 그것을 받아 들고는, 한 모금 마시고 마음을 가라앉힌 뒤에 말했다. “제가 이 짐승에게 그토록 진지하게 말을 걸었다고 하여 나리들께서 저를 얼간이로 여기신다면 섭섭하겠습니다."],["I am a clown, but not so much of one but that I know how to behave to men and to beasts.”","하오나 사실인즉, 제가 쓴 그 말에는 어떤 사연이 깃들어 있답니다. 저는 촌사람이지만, 사람과 짐승에게 어떻게 처신해야 할지 모를 만큼 그리 촌스럽지는 않습니다.”"]],[["“That I can well believe,”","“그야 나도 능히 믿네.”"],["said the curate, “for I know already by experience that the woods breed men of learning, and shepherds’ harbour philosophers.”","신부가 말했다. “이미 경험으로 알고 있거니와, 숲은 학식 있는 사람들을 길러 내고, 목동들의 오두막은 철학자들을 품는 법이니까.”"]],[["“At all events, señor,”","“어쨌든, 나리,” 염소지기가 대꾸했다."],["returned the goatherd, “they shelter men of experience; and that you may see the truth of this and grasp it, though I may seem to put myself forward without being asked, I will, if it will not tire you, gentlemen, and you will give me your attention for a little, tell you a true story which will confirm this gentleman’s word (and he pointed to the curate) as well as my own.”","“그곳들은 경험 많은 사람들을 품지요. 그리고 이것이 사실임을 나리들께서 보고 또 헤아리실 수 있도록, 비록 청하지도 않았는데 제가 주제넘게 나서는 듯 보일지라도, 나리들께서 싫증 내지 않으시고 잠시 귀를 기울여 주신다면, 저 나리의 말씀과(그러면서 그는 신부를 가리켰다) 제 말을 아울러 입증해 줄 참된 이야기 하나를 들려 드리겠습니다.”"]],[["To this Don Quixote replied, “Seeing that this affair has a certain colour of chivalry about it, I for my part, brother, will hear you most gladly, and so will all these gentlemen, from the high intelligence they possess and their love of curious novelties that interest, charm, and entertain the mind, as I feel quite sure your story will do. So begin, friend, for we are all prepared to listen.”","이에 돈키호테가 대답했다. “이 일에 어딘가 기사도의 빛깔이 깃들어 있는 듯하니, 나로서는, 여보게, 자네 이야기를 더없이 기쁘게 듣겠고, 여기 이 신사분들도 모두 그러할 것이오. 이분들은 드높은 지성을 지닌 데다, 마음을 끌고 매혹하고 즐겁게 해 주는 진기한 이야기들을 사랑하는 분들이니 말이오. 자네 이야기가 분명 그러하리라 나는 확신하오. 그러니 시작하시게, 친구. 우리 모두 들을 채비가 되어 있으니.”"]],[["“I draw my stakes,” said Sancho, “and will retreat with this pasty to the brook there, where I mean to victual myself for three days; for I have heard my lord, Don Quixote, say that a knight-errant’s squire should eat until he can hold no more, whenever he has the chance, because it often happens them to get by accident into a wood so thick that they cannot find a way out of it for six days; and if the man is not well filled or his alforjas well stored, there he may stay, as very often he does, turned into a dried mummy.”","“저는 판돈을 거두겠습니다요.” 산초가 말했다. “그러고는 이 고기 파이를 들고 저기 시냇가로 물러가, 사흘치 양식을 든든히 채워 둘 작정입니다요. 우리 주인이신 돈키호테 나리께서 말씀하시기를, 편력기사의 종자는 기회가 닿을 때마다 더는 들어가지 않을 만큼 먹어 두어야 한다고 하셨거든요. 흔히 우연찮게 어찌나 빽빽한 숲에 들어서는지, 엿새가 지나도록 빠져나갈 길을 찾지 못하는 일이 잦은데, 그때 사람이 든든히 배를 채워 두지 않았거나 자루를 잘 채워 두지 않았으면, 거기서 흔히 그렇듯 바싹 마른 미라가 되어 버릴 수도 있다고 하셨으니까요.”"]],[["“Thou art in the right of it, Sancho,”","“네 말이 옳다, 산초.” 돈키호테가 말했다."],["said Don Quixote; “go where thou wilt and eat all thou canst, for I have had enough, and only want to give my mind its refreshment, as I shall by listening to this good fellow’s story.”","“가고 싶은 데로 가서 먹을 수 있는 만큼 먹어라. 나는 이미 충분하니, 그저 이 착한 친구의 이야기를 들으며 마음에 새 기운을 불어넣어 주고 싶을 따름이다.”"]],[["“It is what we shall all do,”","“우리 모두 그렇게 할 것이오.”"],["said the canon; and then begged the goatherd to begin the promised tale.","사제 참사회원이 말하고는, 염소지기에게 약속한 이야기를 시작해 달라고 청했다."]],[["The goatherd gave the goat which he held by the horns a couple of slaps on the back, saying, “Lie down here beside me, Spotty, for we have time enough to return to our fold.” The goat seemed to understand him, for as her master seated himself, she stretched herself quietly beside him and looked up in his face to show him she was all attention to what he was going to say, and then in these words he began his story.","염소지기는 뿔을 잡고 있던 염소의 등을 두어 번 토닥이며 말했다. “여기 내 곁에 누우려무나, 얼룩아. 우리 우리로 돌아갈 시간은 넉넉하니 말이다.” 염소는 그의 말을 알아듣는 듯했으니, 주인이 자리에 앉자 그 곁에 얌전히 몸을 누이고는, 이제부터 그가 하려는 말에 온통 귀를 기울이고 있음을 보이려는 양 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그러고는 다음과 같은 말로 그는 이야기를 시작했다."]],[["# CHAPTER LI. WHICH DEALS WITH WHAT THE GOATHERD TOLD THOSE WHO WERE CARRYING OFF DON QUIXOTE","# CHAPTER LI."]],[["Three leagues from this valley there is a village which, though small, is one of the richest in all this neighbourhood, and in it there lived a farmer, a very worthy man, and so much respected that, although to be so is the natural consequence of being rich, he was even more respected for his virtue than for the wealth he had acquired.","이 골짜기에서 삼 레구아 떨어진 곳에 마을이 하나 있는데, 비록 작기는 하나 이 일대에서 가장 부유한 마을 가운데 하나입니다. 그곳에 한 농부가 살았으니, 매우 훌륭한 사람으로 어찌나 존경받았던지, 부유하면 자연히 그리되기 마련이긴 하지만, 그는 자신이 쌓은 재산보다도 그 덕성으로 인해 한층 더 존경을 받았습니다."],["But what made him still more fortunate, as he said himself, was having a daughter of such exceeding beauty, rare intelligence, gracefulness, and virtue, that everyone who knew her and beheld her marvelled at the extraordinary gifts with which heaven and nature had endowed her.","그러나 그를 더더욱 복된 사람으로 만든 것은, 그 자신의 말마따나, 빼어난 아름다움과 보기 드문 지혜와 우아함과 덕성을 두루 갖춘 딸을 두었다는 점이었으니, 그녀를 알고 또 본 사람이라면 누구나 하늘과 자연이 그녀에게 베푼 그 비범한 천품에 경탄해 마지않았습니다."],["As a child she was beautiful, she continued to grow in beauty, and at the age of sixteen she was most lovely.","어린아이 적부터 그녀는 아름다웠고, 자라면서 더욱 아름다워졌으며, 열여섯 나이에 이르러서는 더없이 어여뻤습니다."],["The fame of her beauty began to spread abroad through all the villages around--but why do I say the villages around, merely, when it spread to distant cities, and even made its way into the halls of royalty and reached the ears of people of every class, who came from all sides to see her as if to see something rare and curious, or some wonder-working image?","그녀의 아름다움에 관한 명성이 주변의 모든 마을로 퍼져 나가기 시작했지요. 아니, 어찌 주변 마을이라고만 하겠습니까, 그 명성이 먼 도시들에까지 미치고, 심지어 왕궁의 홀에까지 흘러들어 온갖 계층 사람들의 귀에 닿았으니, 사람들은 무언가 희귀하고 진기한 것이라도, 혹은 기적을 행하는 성상이라도 보러 오듯 사방에서 그녀를 보러 몰려왔던 것입니다."]],[["Her father watched over her and she watched over herself; for there are no locks, or guards, or bolts that can protect a young girl better than her own modesty.","그녀의 아버지는 그녀를 지켜보았고 그녀 또한 스스로를 지켰으니, 젊은 처녀를 그 자신의 정숙함보다 더 잘 지켜 줄 수 있는 자물쇠도, 감시인도, 빗장도 없는 법이지요. 아버지의 재산과 딸의 아름다움은 같은 마을 이웃들뿐 아니라 외지 사람들까지 그녀를 아내로 삼고자 찾아오게 만들었습니다."],["The wealth of the father and the beauty of the daughter led many neighbours as well as strangers to seek her for a wife; but he, as one might well be who had the disposal of so rich a jewel, was perplexed and unable to make up his mind to which of her countless suitors he should entrust her.","그러나 그토록 값진 보석을 처분할 권한을 쥔 자가 으레 그러하듯, 그는 어찌할 바를 몰라 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 구혼자 가운데 누구에게 딸을 맡겨야 할지 마음을 정하지 못했습니다. 저 또한 이 지극히 자연스러운 소망을 품은 많은 이들 가운데 하나였는데, 그녀의 아버지가 제가 누구인지를 알고 있었던 데다, 저는 같은 마을 사람이요, 순수한 혈통에, 한창나이였으며, 재산도 매우 넉넉했기에 큰 기대를 품고 있었습니다."],["I was one among the many who felt a desire so natural, and, as her father knew who I was, and I was of the same town, of pure blood, in the bloom of life, and very rich in possessions, I had great hopes of success.","같은 마을 출신에 같은 조건을 갖춘 또 다른 사람도 그녀를 구했으니, 이로 인해 그녀 아버지의 선택은 저울 위에 매달린 듯 기울지 못했지요. 그는 우리 둘 중 누구에게든 딸을 잘 시집보내는 것이라 여겼기 때문입니다."],["There was another of the same place and qualifications who also sought her, and this made her father’s choice hang in the balance, for he felt that on either of us his daughter would be well bestowed; so to escape from this state of perplexity he resolved to refer the matter to Leandra (for that is the name of the rich damsel who has reduced me to misery), reflecting that as we were both equal it would be best to leave it to his dear daughter to choose according to her inclination--a course that is worthy of imitation by all fathers who wish to settle their children in life.","그리하여 이 곤혹스러운 처지에서 벗어나고자 그는 이 일을 레안드라에게 맡기기로 결심했는데(저를 이 비참한 처지로 몰아넣은 그 부유한 처녀의 이름이 바로 레안드라입니다), 우리 둘이 동등하니 사랑하는 딸이 제 마음 가는 대로 고르게 하는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 생각했던 것이지요. 이는 자식을 한세상 자리 잡게 해 주고자 하는 모든 아버지가 본받을 만한 처사입니다."],["I do not mean that they ought to leave them to make a choice of what is contemptible and bad, but that they should place before them what is good and then allow them to make a good choice as they please.","제 말은 자식들에게 천하고 나쁜 것을 고르게 내버려 두라는 뜻이 아니라, 좋은 것을 그들 앞에 차려 놓고 그런 다음 제 마음껏 좋은 선택을 하도록 허락하라는 뜻입니다. 레안드라가 누구를 골랐는지는 저도 모릅니다."],["I do not know which Leandra chose; I only know her father put us both off with the tender age of his daughter and vague words that neither bound him nor dismissed us.","다만 그녀의 아버지가 딸이 아직 어리다는 핑계와, 자신을 묶지도 않고 우리를 물리치지도 않는 모호한 말로 우리 둘을 미루어 두었다는 것만은 압니다. 제 연적의 이름은 안셀모요, 저 자신은 에우헤니오라 합니다."],["My rival is called Anselmo and I myself Eugenio--that you may know the names of the personages that figure in this tragedy, the end of which is still in suspense, though it is plain to see it must be disastrous.","이 비극에 등장하는 인물들의 이름을 아시도록 말씀드리는 것이지요. 그 비극의 결말은 아직 매듭지어지지 않았으나, 참담할 수밖에 없으리라는 것은 빤히 내다보입니다."]],[["About this time there arrived in our town one Vicente de la Roca, the son of a poor peasant of the same town, the said Vicente having returned from service as a soldier in Italy and divers other parts.","이 무렵 우리 마을에 비센테 데 라 로카라는 자가 들어왔는데, 그는 같은 마을의 가난한 농부의 아들로, 이탈리아를 비롯한 여러 곳에서 병사로 복무하고 돌아온 자였습니다. 한 대위가 우연히 자기 중대를 이끌고 그곳을 지나다가, 그가 열두 살 남짓한 소년이었을 적에 우리 마을에서 데려갔던 것인데, 이제 열두 해가 지나 그 젊은이가 병사의 군복 차림으로 돌아온 것입니다."],["A captain who chanced to pass that way with his company had carried him off from our village when he was a boy of about twelve years, and now twelve years later the young man came back in a soldier’s uniform, arrayed in a thousand colours, and all over glass trinkets and fine steel chains.","갖가지 빛깔로 치장하고, 온몸에 유리 장신구며 번쩍이는 강철 사슬을 두른 채로 말이지요. 오늘은 이 화려한 옷을 입고 나타나고, 내일은 또 저 옷을 입고 나타나곤 했는데, 하나같이 얄팍하고 번지르르하기만 할 뿐 알맹이도 없고 값어치도 더더욱 없는 것들이었습니다."],["To-day he would appear in one gay dress, to-morrow in another; but all flimsy and gaudy, of little substance and less worth.","천성이 짓궂은 데다 할 일이 없으면 짓궂음 그 자체가 되어 버리는 농사꾼들은 이 모든 것을 눈여겨보아, 그의 화려한 옷과 장신구를 하나하나 따져 보고는, 그가 빛깔이 다른 세 벌의 옷에 거기 맞춘 대님과 양말을 가졌을 뿐임을 알아냈지요. 그런데 그가 그것들을 어찌나 요리조리 짜 맞추고 바꿔 입었던지, 일일이 세어 보지 않았더라면 누구든 그가 열 벌 넘는 옷과 스무 개의 깃 장식을 늘어놓고 자랑했다고 맹세했을 정도였습니다."],["The peasant folk, who are naturally malicious, and when they have nothing to do can be malice itself, remarked all this, and took note of his finery and jewellery, piece by piece, and discovered that he had three suits of different colours, with garters and stockings to match; but he made so many arrangements and combinations out of them, that if they had not counted them, anyone would have sworn that he had made a display of more than ten suits of clothes and twenty plumes.","제가 옷에 관해 들려드리는 이 모든 이야기를 쓸데없거나 장황한 것으로 여기지는 마십시오, 이 이야기와 큰 관련이 있으니까요. 그는 우리 광장의 큰 포플러나무 아래 의자에 앉아, 자기 무용담을 늘어놓아 우리 모두가 입을 떡 벌린 채 거기에 매달리게 만들곤 했습니다."],["Do not look upon all this that I am telling you about the clothes as uncalled for or spun out, for they have a great deal to do with the story.","그가 가 보지 못한 나라가 이 지구상에 없었고, 끼어들지 않은 전투가 없었습니다. 그는 모로코와 튀니스에 있는 무어인보다 더 많은 무어인을 죽였고, 제 입으로 떠벌리는 바에 따르면 가르실라소며 디에고 가르시아 데 파레데스며 그 밖에 그가 들먹인 천 명의 인물보다 더 많은 일대일 결투를 치렀으되, 그 모든 싸움에서 피 한 방울 흘리지 않고 승리하여 빠져나왔다고 했지요."],["He used to seat himself on a bench under the great poplar in our plaza, and there he would keep us all hanging open-mouthed on the stories he told us of his exploits.","한편으로는 상처 자국을 내보이기도 했는데, 분간조차 되지 않는 것을 두고 그는 여러 교전과 전투에서 입은 총상이라고 했습니다. 끝으로 그는 무지막지하게 뻔뻔스럽게도 자기와 동등한 사람들, 심지어 그가 어떤 자인지 다 아는 사람들에게까지 \"너\"라고 불러대며, 제 팔이 곧 제 아비요 제 무공이 곧 제 가문이며, 자기는 병사이니 임금 그 자신만큼이나 훌륭하다고 큰소리쳤습니다."],["There was no country on the face of the globe he had not seen, nor battle he had not been engaged in; he had killed more Moors than there are in Morocco and Tunis, and fought more single combats, according to his own account, than Garcilaso, Diego Garcia de Paredes and a thousand others he named, and out of all he had come victorious without losing a drop of blood.","게다가 이런 허풍에 더해 그는 음악도 조금 할 줄 알아, 기타를 어찌나 멋들어지게 켜는지 어떤 이들은 그가 기타를 말하게 만든다고까지 했지요. 그의 재주는 여기서 그치지 않았으니, 그는 시인 노릇도 조금 했던지라 마을에서 일어나는 사소한 일마다 일 레구아 길이의 발라드를 지어내곤 했습니다."],["On the other hand he showed marks of wounds, which, though they could not be made out, he said were gunshot wounds received in divers encounters and actions.","자, 그래서 제가 말씀드린 이 병사, 이 비센테 데 라 로카, 이 허풍쟁이요 멋쟁이요 음악가요 시인인 자를, 레안드라는 광장이 내려다보이는 자기 집 창문에서 자주 보고 또 지켜보았습니다. 그의 화려한 옷의 번쩍임이 그녀의 마음을 사로잡았고, 그의 발라드가 그녀를 호렸으며(그는 자기가 지은 발라드마다 스무 부씩 나눠 주었으니까요), 그가 제 입으로 들려주는 무용담이 그녀의 귀에 와닿았습니다."],["Lastly, with monstrous impudence he used to say “you”","요컨대, 의심할 바 없이 악마가 그렇게 꾸며 놓은 것이겠지요, 그가 감히 그녀에게 구애할 생각을 떠올리기도 전에 그녀가 먼저 그에게 반해 버린 것입니다. 그런데 사랑의 일에서 여인의 마음이 한편이 되어 주는 것보다 더 쉽게 결말로 치닫는 것은 없는 법이라, 레안드라와 비센테는 아무런 어려움 없이 서로 뜻을 통하게 되었습니다."],["to his equals and even those who knew what he was, and declare that his arm was his father and his deeds his pedigree, and that being a soldier he was as good as the king himself.","그리하여 그녀의 그 숱한 구혼자 가운데 누구도 그녀의 속셈을 눈치채기도 전에, 그녀는 이미 그 일을 실행에 옮겨, 끔찍이도 사랑하는 아버지의 집을 떠나(어머니는 없었으니까요) 그 병사와 함께 마을에서 자취를 감추고 말았으니, 그 병사로 말하면 자신이 떠벌린 그 많은 모험 가운데 어느 것에서보다도 이 거사에서 더 의기양양하게 빠져나온 셈이었지요. 온 마을과 이 일을 들은 모든 이가 이 사건에 경악했습니다."],["And to add to these swaggering ways he was a trifle of a musician, and played the guitar with such a flourish that some said he made it speak; nor did his accomplishments end here, for he was something of a poet too, and on every trifle that happened in the town he made a ballad a league long.","저는 망연자실했고, 안셀모는 벼락 맞은 듯했으며, 그녀의 아버지는 슬픔에 잠겼고, 친척들은 분개했으며, 관청은 온통 들끓었고, 형제단의 관원들은 무장에 나섰습니다. 그들은 길이란 길은 다 뒤지고, 숲이며 사방을 다 수색하여, 사흘 만에 산속 동굴에서 그 경솔한 레안드라를 찾아냈는데, 속옷만 걸친 채 집에서 가지고 나온 돈과 값진 보석은 죄다 빼앗긴 모습이었습니다."]],[["This soldier, then, that I have described, this Vicente de la Roca, this bravo, gallant, musician, poet, was often seen and watched by Leandra from a window of her house which looked out on the plaza.","* 형제단: 산타 에르만다드(Santa Hermandad). 스페인의 시골 지역 치안을 맡던 일종의 무장 자경 조직."],["The glitter of his showy attire took her fancy, his ballads bewitched her (for he gave away twenty copies of every one he made), the tales of his exploits which he told about himself came to her ears; and in short, as the devil no doubt had arranged it, she fell in love with him before the presumption of making love to her had suggested itself to him; and as in love-affairs none are more easily brought to an issue than those which have the inclination of the lady for an ally, Leandra and Vicente came to an understanding without any difficulty; and before any of her numerous suitors had any suspicion of her design, she had already carried it into effect, having left the house of her dearly beloved father (for mother she had none), and disappeared from the village with the soldier, who came more triumphantly out of this enterprise than out of any of the large number he laid claim to.","그들은 그녀를 가엾은 아버지에게 데려와 그 불행에 관해 캐물었는데, 그녀는 다그치지 않아도 순순히 털어놓기를, 비센테 데 라 로카가 자기를 속였으며, 결혼해 주겠다는 약속으로 자기를 꾀어 아버지의 집을 떠나게 했으니, 자기를 온 세상에서 가장 부유하고 가장 즐거운 도시인 나폴리로 데려가겠노라 했다는 것입니다."],["All the village and all who heard of it were amazed at the affair; I was aghast, Anselmo thunderstruck, her father full of grief, her relations indignant, the authorities all in a ferment, the officers of the Brotherhood in arms. They scoured the roads, they searched the woods and all quarters, and at the end of three days they found the flighty Leandra in a mountain cave, stript to her shift, and robbed of all the money and precious jewels she had carried away from home with her.","그리고 자기는 어리석게도 속아 넘어가 그 말을 믿고는 아버지의 것을 훔쳐, 자취를 감춘 그날 밤 그 모두를 그에게 넘겨주었으며, 그가 자기를 험준한 산으로 끌고 가 그들이 찾아낸 그 동굴 안에 가두었다고 했지요."],["They brought her back to her unhappy father, and questioned her as to her misfortune, and she confessed without pressure that Vicente de la Roca had deceived her, and under promise of marrying her had induced her to leave her father’s house, as he meant to take her to the richest and most delightful city in the whole world, which was Naples; and that she, ill-advised and deluded, had believed him, and robbed her father, and handed over all to him the night she disappeared; and that he had carried her away to a rugged mountain and shut her up in the cave where they had found her.","그녀는 또 말하기를, 그 병사가 자기의 정조만은 빼앗지 않은 채, 가진 것을 죄다 앗아 가고는 자기를 동굴에 남겨 둔 채 달아났다고 했으니, 이것이 또 모두를 한층 더 놀라게 했습니다. 그 젊은이의 절제를 믿기란 우리로서는 쉬운 일이 아니었으나, 그녀가 어찌나 간곡하게 단언하던지 그것이 비탄에 잠긴 아버지를 위로하는 데에 도움이 되었습니다."],["She said, moreover, that the soldier, without robbing her of her honour, had taken from her everything she had, and made off, leaving her in the cave, a thing that still further surprised everybody.","한번 잃으면 결코 되찾을 수 없는 그 보석이 딸에게 남겨졌으니, 빼앗긴 것쯤은 아무것도 아니라고 그가 여겼던 것이지요."],["It was not easy for us to credit the young man’s continence, but she asserted it with such earnestness that it helped to console her distressed father, who thought nothing of what had been taken since the jewel that once lost can never be recovered had been left to his daughter.","레안드라가 모습을 드러낸 바로 그날, 그녀의 아버지는 그녀를 우리 눈앞에서 거두어, 이 근처의 어느 마을 수도원에 가두려고 데려가 버렸습니다. 그녀가 저지른 불명예를 세월이 얼마간 닳아 없애 주기를 바라면서 말이지요."],["The same day that Leandra made her appearance her father removed her from our sight and took her away to shut her up in a convent in a town near this, in the hope that time may wear away some of the disgrace she has incurred.","레안드라의 어린 나이는 그녀의 잘못에 대한 변명거리가 되어 주었으니, 적어도 그녀가 선한지 악한지가 아무래도 상관없는 이들에게는 그러했습니다."],["Leandra’s youth furnished an excuse for her fault, at least with those to whom it was of no consequence whether she was good or bad; but those who knew her shrewdness and intelligence did not attribute her misdemeanour to ignorance but to wantonness and the natural disposition of women, which is for the most part flighty and ill-regulated.","그러나 그녀의 영악함과 지혜를 아는 이들은 그녀의 잘못을 무지의 탓으로 돌리지 않고, 방종과 여자의 천성 탓으로 돌렸으니, 그 천성이란 대개 들뜨고 절제가 없는 법이지요."]],[["Leandra withdrawn from sight, Anselmo’s eyes grew blind, or at any rate found nothing to look at that gave them any pleasure, and mine were in darkness without a ray of light to direct them to anything enjoyable while Leandra was away.","레안드라가 눈앞에서 사라지자 안셀모의 눈은 멀어 버린 듯, 아니 적어도 어떤 즐거움도 주지 못하는 것 외에는 바라볼 것을 찾지 못하게 되었고, 제 눈 또한 레안드라가 떠나 있는 동안 어떤 즐거운 것으로도 이끌어 줄 한 줄기 빛도 없는 어둠 속에 잠겼습니다. 우리의 우울은 더욱 깊어지고, 우리의 인내는 더욱 줄어들었지요."],["Our melancholy grew greater, our patience grew less; we cursed the soldier’s finery and railed at the carelessness of Leandra’s father.","우리는 그 병사의 화려한 옷을 저주하고, 레안드라 아버지의 부주의함을 욕했습니다. 마침내 안셀모와 저는 마을을 떠나 이 골짜기로 오기로 뜻을 모았으니, 그는 자기 소유의 큰 양 떼를 치고 저는 제 소유의 큰 염소 떼를 치면서, 우리는 나무들 사이에서 우리의 슬픔을 토해 내고, 함께 아름다운 레안드라를 찬미하거나 그녀를 책망하거나, 아니면 홀로 한숨지으며 외로이 하늘을 향해 우리의 하소연을 쏟아 내며 세월을 보냅니다."],["At last Anselmo and I agreed to leave the village and come to this valley; and, he feeding a great flock of sheep of his own, and I a large herd of goats of mine, we pass our life among the trees, giving vent to our sorrows, together singing the fair Leandra’s praises, or upbraiding her, or else sighing alone, and to heaven pouring forth our complaints in solitude.","우리를 본받아 레안드라의 다른 연인들도 여럿 이 거친 산으로 와서 우리의 생활 방식을 따랐는데, 그 수가 어찌나 많은지 이곳이 목가적인 아르카디아로 변해 버린 듯, 양치기와 양 우리로 가득합니다. 이곳에는 아름다운 레안드라의 이름이 들리지 않는 자리가 한 군데도 없지요."],["Following our example, many more of Leandra’s lovers have come to these rude mountains and adopted our mode of life, and they are so numerous that one would fancy the place had been turned into the pastoral Arcadia, so full is it of shepherds and sheep-folds; nor is there a spot in it where the name of the fair Leandra is not heard.","여기서는 한 사람이 그녀를 저주하며 변덕스럽고 경박하고 정숙하지 못하다 하고, 저기서는 또 한 사람이 그녀를 연약하고 들떴다고 나무랍니다. 이쪽은 그녀를 용서하고 사면하는가 하면, 저쪽은 그녀를 내치고 욕합니다."],["Here one curses her and calls her capricious, fickle, and immodest, there another condemns her as frail and frivolous; this pardons and absolves her, that spurns and reviles her; one extols her beauty, another assails her character, and in short all abuse her, and all adore her, and to such a pitch has this general infatuation gone that there are some who complain of her scorn without ever having exchanged a word with her, and even some that bewail and mourn the raging fever of jealousy, for which she never gave anyone cause, for, as I have already said, her misconduct was known before her passion.","한 사람은 그녀의 아름다움을 추켜올리고, 또 한 사람은 그녀의 품성을 헐뜯습니다. 요컨대 모두가 그녀를 욕하면서도 모두가 그녀를 흠모하니, 이 보편적인 미혹이 어찌나 극에 달했던지, 그녀와 단 한마디도 나눠 본 적 없으면서 그녀의 냉대를 한탄하는 자들이 있는가 하면, 그녀가 누구에게도 빌미를 준 적 없는 질투의 격렬한 열병을 슬퍼하며 애통해하는 자들마저 있습니다."],["There is no nook among the rocks, no brookside, no shade beneath the trees that is not haunted by some shepherd telling his woes to the breezes; wherever there is an echo it repeats the name of Leandra; the mountains ring with “Leandra,”","앞서 말씀드렸듯이 그녀의 비행은 그녀의 정열에 앞서 알려졌으니까요. 바위 틈새며 시냇가며 나무 아래 그늘 어디든, 어느 양치기가 산들바람에 제 시름을 풀어놓지 않는 자리가 없습니다."],["“Leandra”","메아리가 있는 곳이라면 어디든 레안드라의 이름을 되울리고, 산들은 \"레안드라\""],["murmur the brooks, and Leandra keeps us all bewildered and bewitched, hoping without hope and fearing without knowing what we fear.","소리로 울려 퍼지며, 시냇물은 \"레안드라\" 하고 속삭이니, 레안드라는 우리 모두를 얼떨떨하게 또 호린 채로 붙들어 두어, 희망 없이 희망하고 무엇을 두려워하는지도 모르면서 두려워하게 만듭니다."],["Of all this silly set the one that shows the least and also the most sense is my rival Anselmo, for having so many other things to complain of, he only complains of separation, and to the accompaniment of a rebeck, which he plays admirably, he sings his complaints in verses that show his ingenuity.","이 어리석은 무리 가운데 가장 분별이 없으면서도 또한 가장 분별이 있는 자가 제 연적 안셀모인데, 그는 한탄할 다른 일이 그토록 많은데도 오로지 이별만을 한탄하며, 자기가 솜씨 좋게 켜는 레벡 반주에 맞추어 제 재치를 드러내는 시로 그 한탄을 노래하지요. 저는 다른, 더 쉬운, 제 생각으로는 더 현명한 길을 택했으니, 그것은 곧 여자들의 경박함과 변덕과 표리부동과 깨어진 약속과 지키지 않은 맹세를, 한마디로 그들이 제 애정과 마음을 정함에 있어 보이는 그 분별 없음을 욕하는 것입니다."],["I follow another, easier, and to my mind wiser course, and that is to rail at the frivolity of women, at their inconstancy, their double dealing, their broken promises, their unkept pledges, and in short the want of reflection they show in fixing their affections and inclinations.","여러분, 이것이 바로 제가 조금 전에 올라오면서 이 염소에게 퍼부었던 말과 표현의 까닭입니다. 이놈이 암컷인지라 제 우리에서 으뜸가는 놈이긴 해도 저는 이놈을 경멸하지요."],["This, sirs, was the reason of words and expressions I made use of to this goat when I came up just now; for as she is a female I have a contempt for her, though she is the best in all my fold.","이것이 제가 들려드리겠다고 약속한 이야기입니다. 이야기를 들려드리느라 지루하게 해 드렸다면, 여러분을 대접하는 일에는 굼뜨지 않겠습니다."],["This is the story I promised to tell you, and if I have been tedious in telling it, I will not be slow to serve you; my hut is close by, and I have fresh milk and dainty cheese there, as well as a variety of toothsome fruit, no less pleasing to the eye than to the palate.","제 오두막이 바로 가까이에 있으니, 그곳에는 신선한 우유와 맛깔스러운 치즈가 있고, 보기에도 입에도 두루 즐거운 갖가지 맛난 과일도 있습니다. * 레벡: 활로 켜는 중세의 현악기."]],[["# CHAPTER LII. OF THE QUARREL THAT DON QUIXOTE HAD WITH THE GOATHERD, TOGETHER WITH THE RARE ADVENTURE OF THE PENITENTS, WHICH WITH AN EXPENDITURE OF SWEAT HE BROUGHT TO A HAPPY CONCLUSION","# CHAPTER LII."]],[["The goatherd’s tale gave great satisfaction to all the hearers, and the canon especially enjoyed it, for he had remarked with particular attention the manner in which it had been told, which was as unlike the manner of a clownish goatherd as it was like that of a polished city wit; and he observed that the curate had been quite right in saying that the woods bred men of learning. They all offered their services to Eugenio but he who showed himself most liberal in this way was Don Quixote, who said to him, “Most assuredly, brother goatherd, if I found myself in a position to attempt any adventure, I would, this very instant, set out on your behalf, and would rescue Leandra from that convent (where no doubt she is kept against her will), in spite of the abbess and all who might try to prevent me, and would place her in your hands to deal with her according to your will and pleasure, observing, however, the laws of chivalry which lay down that no violence of any kind is to be offered to any damsel. But I trust in God our Lord that the might of one malignant enchanter may not prove so great but that the power of another better disposed may prove superior to it, and then I promise you my support and assistance, as I am bound to do by my profession, which is none other than to give aid to the weak and needy.”","염소지기의 이야기는 듣고 있던 모든 이를 크게 만족시켰는데, 그중에서도 특히 신부 참사회원이 즐거워했으니, 그는 이야기가 펼쳐지는 방식을 각별한 주의를 기울여 눈여겨보았던 것이다. 그것은 촌스러운 염소지기의 말투와는 도무지 닮지 않았고, 오히려 세련된 도회지 재담가의 말투에 가까웠다. 그리하여 그는 숲이 학식 있는 사람들을 길러 낸다고 한 신부의 말이 참으로 옳았다고 여겼다. 모두가 에우헤니오에게 도움을 베풀겠다고 나섰으나, 그중에서도 이 점에서 가장 후하게 자기를 내보인 이는 돈키호테였으니, 그는 그에게 이렇게 말했다. \"분명히 말하건대, 염소지기 형제여, 만일 내가 어떤 모험에 나설 만한 처지에 있었다면, 나는 바로 이 순간 그대를 위하여 길을 떠나, 레안드라를 그 수도원에서 — 틀림없이 제 뜻과는 달리 갇혀 있을 그곳에서 — 구해 냈을 것이오. 원장 수녀를 비롯하여 나를 막으려는 그 누구도 아랑곳하지 않고 말이오. 그러고는 그녀를 그대의 손에 맡겨 그대의 뜻과 마음대로 처분하게 하였을 것이나, 다만 그 어떤 처녀에게도 어떠한 폭력도 행해서는 안 된다고 정한 기사도의 법도만은 지키게 했을 것이오. 그러나 나는 우리 주 하느님을 믿거니와, 한 사악한 마법사의 힘이 제아무리 크다 한들, 더 선한 뜻을 품은 다른 마법사의 힘이 그보다 우월하지 못할 만큼은 아닐 것이오. 그때가 오면 나는 그대에게 나의 지지와 도움을 약속하리니, 이는 내 본분이 명하는 바요, 그 본분이란 다름 아니라 약하고 궁핍한 자를 돕는 일이기 때문이오.\""]],[["The goatherd eyed him, and noticing Don Quixote’s sorry appearance and looks, he was filled with wonder, and asked the barber, who was next him, “Señor, who is this man who makes such a figure and talks in such a strain?”","염소지기는 그를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 돈키호테의 초라한 행색과 몰골을 알아채고는 경이로움에 사로잡혀, 곁에 있던 이발사에게 물었다. \"나리, 저런 꼴을 하고 저런 투로 말하는 저 사람은 대체 누굽니까?\""]],[["“Who should it be,”","\"누구긴 누구겠나,\" 이발사가 말했다."],["said the barber, “but the famous Don Quixote of La Mancha, the undoer of injustice, the righter of wrongs, the protector of damsels, the terror of giants, and the winner of battles?”","\"저 유명한 라만차의 돈키호테가 아닌가. 불의를 무너뜨리고, 그릇된 것을 바로잡으며, 처녀들을 보호하고, 거인들을 두려움에 떨게 하며, 싸움마다 이기는 분이지.\""]],[["“That,” said the goatherd, “sounds like what one reads in the books of the knights-errant, who did all that you say this man does; though it is my belief that either you are joking, or else this gentleman has empty lodgings in his head.”","\"그거 참,\" 염소지기가 말했다. \"편력 기사 이야기책에서나 읽을 법한 말이로군요. 그 기사들이야말로 나리께서 저 사람이 한다고 하신 그 모든 일을 했으니 말입니다. 하지만 제 생각엔, 나리께서 농을 하고 계시거나, 아니면 저 양반 머릿속이 텅 빈 셋방 같은 게 아닐까 싶습니다.\""]],[["“You are a great scoundrel,”","\"이 고약한 놈 같으니,\""],["said Don Quixote, “and it is you who are empty and a fool.","돈키호테가 말했다. \"텅 비고 어리석은 건 바로 너로다."],["I am fuller than ever was the whoreson bitch that bore you;”","나로 말하면 너를 낳은 그 화냥년의 암캐보다도 가득 차 있느니라.\""],["and passing from words to deeds, he caught up a loaf that was near him and sent it full in the goatherd’s face, with such force that he flattened his nose; but the goatherd, who did not understand jokes, and found himself roughly handled in such good earnest, paying no respect to carpet, tablecloth, or diners, sprang upon Don Quixote, and seizing him by the throat with both hands would no doubt have throttled him, had not Sancho Panza that instant come to the rescue, and grasping him by the shoulders flung him down on the table, smashing plates, breaking glasses, and upsetting and scattering everything on it.","그러고는 말에서 행동으로 옮겨, 곁에 있던 빵 덩이를 집어 들어 염소지기의 얼굴 한복판에 정통으로 날렸는데, 어찌나 세게 던졌던지 그의 코가 납작해지고 말았다. 그러나 농담을 알아듣지 못하는 데다 정말로 호되게 당했다고 여긴 염소지기는, 깔개도 식탁보도 식사하던 이들도 아랑곳하지 않고 돈키호테에게 달려들어 두 손으로 그의 멱살을 움켜쥐었으니, 만일 그 순간 산초 판사가 구하러 오지 않았던들 틀림없이 그를 목 졸라 죽였으리라."],["Don Quixote, finding himself free, strove to get on top of the goatherd, who, with his face covered with blood, and soundly kicked by Sancho, was on all fours feeling about for one of the table-knives to take a bloody revenge with.","산초는 그의 어깨를 붙들어 식탁 위로 내동댕이쳤고, 그 바람에 접시가 깨지고 잔이 부서지며 식탁 위의 온갖 것이 뒤엎어져 흩어졌다. 자유로워진 돈키호테는 염소지기 위에 올라타려고 안간힘을 썼고, 얼굴이 온통 피투성이가 되고 산초에게 흠씬 걷어차인 염소지기는 네 발로 기어 다니며 피로 복수하려고 식탁 칼 하나를 더듬어 찾고 있었다."],["The canon and the curate, however, prevented him, but the barber so contrived it that he got Don Quixote under him, and rained down upon him such a shower of fisticuffs that the poor knight’s face streamed with blood as freely as his own.","그러나 신부 참사회원과 신부가 그것을 막았고, 한편 이발사는 용케 솜씨를 부려 돈키호테를 제 밑에 깔고는 그에게 주먹세례를 어찌나 퍼부었던지, 가엾은 기사의 얼굴에서도 제 얼굴 못지않게 피가 줄줄 흘렀다."],["The canon and the curate were bursting with laughter, the officers were capering with delight, and both the one and the other hissed them on as they do dogs that are worrying one another in a fight.","신부 참사회원과 신부는 웃음을 터뜨려 배를 잡았고, 형제단 관원들은 좋아라 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰었으며, 이쪽이고 저쪽이고 마치 싸우며 서로 물어뜯는 개들을 부추기듯 그들을 부추겼다."],["Sancho alone was frantic, for he could not free himself from the grasp of one of the canon’s servants, who kept him from going to his master’s assistance.","오직 산초만이 미칠 듯이 안달했으니, 그는 신부 참사회원의 하인 하나가 붙잡고 놓아주지 않아 주인을 도우러 갈 수 없었던 것이다."]],[["At last, while they were all, with the exception of the two bruisers who were mauling each other, in high glee and enjoyment, they heard a trumpet sound a note so doleful that it made them all look in the direction whence the sound seemed to come.","마침내, 서로 두들겨 패고 있던 두 싸움꾼을 빼고 모두가 한껏 흥에 겨워 즐기고 있을 때, 나팔 소리가 어찌나 구슬프게 울렸던지 그 모든 이가 소리 나는 듯한 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다."],["But the one that was most excited by hearing it was Don Quixote, who though sorely against his will he was under the goatherd, and something more than pretty well pummelled, said to him, “Brother devil (for it is impossible but that thou must be one since thou hast had might and strength enough to overcome mine), I ask thee to agree to a truce for but one hour for the solemn note of yonder trumpet that falls on our ears seems to me to summon me to some new adventure.”","그러나 그 소리를 듣고 가장 흥분한 이는 돈키호테였으니, 그는 자기 뜻과는 너무도 어긋나게 염소지기 밑에 깔려 어지간히 흠씬 두들겨 맞은 처지였음에도 그에게 이렇게 말했다. \"악마 형제여(그대가 악마가 아니고서야 내 힘을 누를 만큼 강하고 힘셀 수는 없는 노릇이니), 그대에게 청하건대 단 한 시간만 휴전에 동의해 다오."],["The goatherd, who was by this time tired of pummelling and being pummelled, released him at once, and Don Quixote rising to his feet and turning his eyes to the quarter where the sound had been heard, suddenly saw coming down the slope of a hill several men clad in white like penitents.","우리 귀에 들려오는 저 나팔의 엄숙한 소리가 내게는 어떤 새로운 모험으로 나를 부르는 것만 같으니 말이다.\" 이때쯤이면 두들겨 패고 또 두들겨 맞는 데 지칠 대로 지친 염소지기는 즉시 그를 놓아주었고, 돈키호테는 벌떡 일어나 소리가 들려온 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸다가, 문득 언덕 비탈을 내려오는 여러 사람을 보았으니, 그들은 고행자들처럼 흰옷을 입고 있었다."]],[["The fact was that the clouds had that year withheld their moisture from the earth, and in all the villages of the district they were organising processions, rogations, and penances, imploring God to open the hands of his mercy and send the rain; and to this end the people of a village that was hard by were going in procession to a holy hermitage there was on one side of that valley.","사실인즉, 그해에는 구름이 땅에 비를 내려 주지 않아, 그 지방의 모든 마을에서 행렬과 기도 행진과 고행을 벌이며 하느님께 자비의 손을 열어 비를 내려 주십사 빌고 있었다. 그리하여 가까이 있던 한 마을 사람들이 그 골짜기 한편에 있는 어느 성스러운 암자로 행렬을 지어 가는 중이었다."],["Don Quixote when he saw the strange garb of the penitents, without reflecting how often he had seen it before, took it into his head that this was a case of adventure, and that it fell to him alone as a knight-errant to engage in it; and he was all the more confirmed in this notion, by the idea that an image draped in black they had with them was some illustrious lady that these villains and discourteous thieves were carrying off by force.","돈키호테는 고행자들의 기이한 차림을 보자, 전에도 그런 모습을 얼마나 자주 보았던가는 돌이켜 생각하지도 않고, 이것이야말로 모험의 한 경우이며 편력 기사인 자기 혼자만이 떠맡아야 할 일이라고 단정해 버렸다. 게다가 그들이 지니고 가는, 검은 천을 두른 어떤 성상(聖像)이 저 비열한 자들과 무례한 도적들이 강제로 끌고 가는 어느 고귀한 부인이라는 생각이 들면서, 이 확신은 한층 더 굳어졌다."],["As soon as this occurred to him he ran with all speed to Rocinante who was grazing at large, and taking the bridle and the buckler from the saddle-bow, he had him bridled in an instant, and calling to Sancho for his sword he mounted Rocinante, braced his buckler on his arm, and in a loud voice exclaimed to those who stood by, “Now, noble company, ye shall see how important it is that there should be knights in the world professing the of knight-errantry; now, I say, ye shall see, by the deliverance of that worthy lady who is borne captive there, whether knights-errant deserve to be held in estimation,”","이런 생각이 머리에 떠오르자마자 그는 마음대로 풀밭을 뜯고 있던 로시난테에게 온 힘을 다해 달려가, 안장 앞가지에서 고삐와 방패를 집어 들고는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 말에 굴레를 씌웠으며, 산초를 불러 칼을 받아 들고 로시난테에 올라타 방패를 팔에 동여매고는, 곁에 서 있던 이들에게 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"자, 고귀한 분들이여, 이제 그대들은 편력 기사도를 받드는 기사들이 이 세상에 있다는 것이 얼마나 중요한 일인지 보게 되리라."],["and so saying he brought his legs to bear on Rocinante--for he had no spurs--and at a full canter (for in all this veracious history we never read of Rocinante fairly galloping) set off to encounter the penitents, though the curate, the canon, and the barber ran to prevent him.","이제, 내 말하노니, 그대들은 저기 사로잡혀 끌려가는 저 가련한 부인을 구해 냄으로써, 편력 기사들이 존경받아 마땅한 존재인지 아닌지를 보게 되리라.\" 그러고는 두 다리로 로시난테의 옆구리를 죄었으니 — 박차가 없었던 까닭이다 — 전속력으로 달려(이 진실한 이야기를 통틀어 로시난테가 제대로 질주했다는 대목은 한 번도 읽은 적이 없으므로) 고행자들과 맞서러 나아갔다."],["But it was out of their power, nor did he even stop for the shouts of Sancho calling after him, “Where are you going, Señor Don Quixote?","신부와 신부 참사회원과 이발사가 그를 막으려고 달려갔으나, 그것은 그들의 힘에 부치는 일이었고, 산초가 뒤에서 외쳐 부르는 소리에도 그는 멈추지 않았다. \"어디로 가시는 겁니까, 돈키호테 나리?"],["What devils have possessed you to set you on against our Catholic faith?","대체 무슨 악마가 씌었기에 우리 가톨릭 신앙에 맞서려 드십니까? 빌어먹을, 정신 차리세요, 저건 고행자들의 행렬이고, 저 받침대에 모시고 가는 부인은 티 없으신 동정 마리아의 거룩한 성상이란 말입니다."],["Plague take me!","무슨 짓을 하시는지 조심하세요, 나리."],["mind, that is a procession of penitents, and the lady they are carrying on that stand there is the blessed image of the immaculate Virgin.","이번만큼은 나리께서 무슨 일을 벌이시는지도 모르신다고 단언해도 좋을 지경입니다.\" 그러나 산초의 수고는 헛일이었으니, 그의 주인은 이 천을 둘러쓴 무리와 맞붙어 검은 옷의 부인을 구해 내겠다는 일념에 사로잡혀 한마디도 듣지 못했고, 설령 들었다 한들 임금이 명령했다 해도 돌아서지 않았으리라."],["Take care what you are doing, señor, for this time it may be safely said you don’t know what you are about.”","그는 행렬에 다가가 로시난테의 고삐를 당겨 세웠는데, 로시난테로서도 이미 걸음을 조금 늦추고 싶어 안달이 나 있던 참이었다. 그러고는 쉬고 들뜬 목소리로 외쳤다."],["Sancho laboured in vain, for his master was so bent on coming to quarters with these sheeted figures and releasing the lady in black that he did not hear a word; and even had he heard, he would not have turned back if the king had ordered him.","\"그대들, 얼굴을 가린 자들이여, 어쩌면 떳떳한 백성이 못 되어 그러는 것이렷다. 정신 차리고 내가 그대들에게 하려는 말을 들으라.\""],["He came up with the procession and reined in Rocinante, who was already anxious enough to slacken speed a little, and in a hoarse, excited voice he exclaimed, “You who hide your faces, perhaps because you are not good subjects, pay attention and listen to what I am about to say to you.”","가장 먼저 멈춰 선 이들은 성상을 메고 가던 자들이었고, 호칭 기도를 노래하던 네 명의 성직자 가운데 하나가, 돈키호테의 괴이한 몰골과 로시난테의 비쩍 마른 꼴, 그 밖에 눈에 띈 우스꽝스러운 별난 점들에 놀라 그에게 대답했다. \"형제여, 우리에게 할 말이 있거든 어서 하시오."],["The first to halt were those who were carrying the image, and one of the four ecclesiastics who were chanting the Litany, struck by the strange figure of Don Quixote, the leanness of Rocinante, and the other ludicrous peculiarities he observed, said in reply to him, “Brother, if you have anything to say to us say it quickly, for these brethren are whipping themselves, and we cannot stop, nor is it reasonable we should stop to hear anything, unless indeed it is short enough to be said in two words.”","이 형제들이 제 몸에 채찍질을 하고 있는 터라 우리는 멈출 수가 없고, 무슨 말을 들으려고 멈추는 것도 도리가 아니오. 정 그렇다면 두 마디로 끝낼 만큼 짧은 것이라야 하오.\""]],[["“I will say it in one,” replied Don Quixote, “and it is this; that at once, this very instant, ye release that fair lady whose tears and sad aspect show plainly that ye are carrying her off against her will, and that ye have committed some scandalous outrage against her; and I, who was born into the world to redress all such like wrongs, will not permit you to advance another step until you have restored to her the liberty she pines for and deserves.”","\"한마디로 하겠소,\" 돈키호테가 대답했다. \"그것은 이러하오. 당장, 바로 이 순간에 저 아름다운 부인을 풀어 주시오. 그분의 눈물과 슬픈 모습이 그대들이 그분을 뜻에 거슬러 끌고 가며 그분께 어떤 파렴치한 폭행을 저질렀음을 분명히 드러내고 있소. 그리고 바로 이런 온갖 그릇된 일을 바로잡고자 세상에 태어난 나는, 그대들이 그분께 그분이 애타게 그리며 누려 마땅한 자유를 되돌려 줄 때까지는 단 한 걸음도 더 나아가도록 허락하지 않겠소.\""]],[["From these words all the hearers concluded that he must be a madman, and began to laugh heartily, and their laughter acted like gunpowder on Don Quixote’s fury, for drawing his sword without another word he made a rush at the stand.","이 말을 듣고 모두는 그가 미친 사람임에 틀림없다고 결론짓고는 한바탕 호탕하게 웃기 시작했는데, 그 웃음은 돈키호테의 분노에 화약처럼 불을 댕겼으니, 그는 더 말할 것도 없이 칼을 빼 들고 받침대로 돌진했다."],["One of those who supported it, leaving the burden to his comrades, advanced to meet him, flourishing a forked stick that he had for propping up the stand when resting, and with this he caught a mighty cut Don Quixote made at him that severed it in two; but with the portion that remained in his hand he dealt such a thwack on the shoulder of Don Quixote’s sword arm (which the buckler could not protect against the clownish assault) that poor Don Quixote came to the ground in a sad plight.","그것을 떠받치던 자들 가운데 하나가 짐을 동료들에게 맡기고 그를 맞으러 나서서, 받침대를 쉴 때 받쳐 두려고 가지고 있던 끝이 갈라진 막대기를 휘둘렀는데, 이 막대기로 돈키호테가 내리친 굉장한 일격을 받아 막대기가 두 동강이 났다. 그러나 손에 남은 그 토막으로 그는 돈키호테의 칼 잡은 팔 어깨에 어찌나 호된 일격을 가했던지(방패도 이 촌스러운 공격만은 막아 내지 못했다), 가엾은 돈키호테는 비참한 꼴로 땅바닥에 나동그라지고 말았다."]],[["Sancho Panza, who was coming on close behind puffing and blowing, seeing him fall, cried out to his assailant not to strike him again, for he was poor enchanted knight, who had never harmed anyone all the days of his life; but what checked the clown was, not Sancho’s shouting, but seeing that Don Quixote did not stir hand or foot; and so, fancying he had killed him, he hastily hitched up his tunic under his girdle and took to his heels across the country like a deer.","씩씩거리며 헐떡이며 바로 뒤따라오던 산초 판사는 그가 쓰러지는 것을 보고는, 공격한 자에게 더는 치지 말라고 소리쳤으니, 그는 가엾은 마법에 걸린 기사로서 한평생 누구도 해친 적이 없는 분이라는 것이었다. 그러나 그 촌놈을 멈추게 한 것은 산초의 외침이 아니라, 돈키호테가 손도 발도 까딱하지 않는 것을 본 일이었다. 그리하여 그를 죽였다고 여긴 그는 허둥지둥 옷자락을 허리띠 밑으로 걷어 올리고는 사슴처럼 들판을 가로질러 줄행랑을 쳤다."]],[["By this time all Don Quixote’s companions had come up to where he lay; but the processionists seeing them come running, and with them the officers of the Brotherhood with their crossbows, apprehended mischief, and clustering round the image, raised their hoods, and grasped their scourges, as the priests did their tapers, and awaited the attack, resolved to defend themselves and even to take the offensive against their assailants if they could.","이때쯤 돈키호테의 일행이 모두 그가 쓰러진 곳으로 달려왔다. 그러나 행렬을 짓던 이들은 그들이 달려오는 것을, 게다가 석궁을 든 형제단 관원들까지 함께 오는 것을 보고는 좋지 않은 일을 짐작하고, 성상 둘레로 모여들어 두건을 치켜올리고 채찍을 움켜쥐었으며, 사제들도 촛대를 굳게 쥐고는 공격에 대비하여, 스스로를 지킬 뿐 아니라 가능하다면 공격해 온 자들에게 맞받아 나설 작정으로 기다렸다."],["Fortune, however, arranged the matter better than they expected, for all Sancho did was to fling himself on his master’s body, raising over him the most doleful and laughable lamentation that ever was heard, for he believed he was dead.","그러나 운명은 그들이 예상한 것보다 일을 더 잘 풀어 주었으니, 산초가 한 일이라고는 주인의 몸뚱이에 몸을 내던지고 그 위에서 일찍이 들어 본 적 없을 만큼 구슬프고도 우스꽝스러운 통곡을 늘어놓은 것뿐이었으니, 그는 주인이 죽은 줄로만 알았던 것이다."],["The curate was known to another curate who walked in the procession, and their recognition of one another set at rest the apprehensions of both parties; the first then told the other in two words who Don Quixote was, and he and the whole troop of penitents went to see if the poor gentleman was dead, and heard Sancho Panza saying, with tears in his eyes, “Oh flower of chivalry, that with one blow of a stick hast ended the course of thy well-spent life!","신부는 행렬 속에 걷던 또 다른 신부와 아는 사이였는데, 두 사람이 서로 알아보면서 양쪽의 불안이 가라앉았다. 그러고 나서 앞의 신부가 뒤의 신부에게 돈키호테가 누구인지를 두 마디로 일러 주자, 그와 고행자 무리 전체가 그 가엾은 양반이 죽었는지 보러 갔다가, 산초 판사가 눈물을 글썽이며 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."],["Oh pride of thy race, honour and glory of all La Mancha, nay, of all the world, that for want of thee will be full of evil-doers, no longer in fear of punishment for their misdeeds!","\"오, 기사도의 꽃이시여, 막대기 한 방에 그토록 잘 쓰신 생애의 길을 마치셨단 말입니까! 오, 그대 가문의 자랑이시여, 온 라만차의, 아니 온 세상의 명예요 영광이시여, 그대가 없으니 세상은 악행을 저지르고도 더는 그 죄에 대한 벌을 두려워하지 않는 자들로 가득 차겠나이다!"],["Oh thou, generous above all the Alexanders, since for only eight months of service thou hast given me the best island the sea girds or surrounds!","오, 모든 알렉산드로스보다도 너그러우신 분이시여, 고작 여덟 달의 봉직에 그대는 바다가 두르고 에워싼 가장 좋은 섬을 제게 주셨나이다!"],["Humble with the proud, haughty with the humble, encounterer of dangers, endurer of outrages, enamoured without reason, imitator of the good, scourge of the wicked, enemy of the mean, in short, knight-errant, which is all that can be said!”","오만한 자에게 겸손하고, 겸손한 자에게 오만하며, 위험에 맞서고, 모욕을 견디며, 까닭 없이 사랑에 빠지고, 선한 이를 본받으며, 악한 자를 채찍질하고, 비열한 자의 적이셨던, 한마디로 편력 기사이셨으니, 그 이상 무슨 말이 더 필요하겠나이까!\""]],[["At the cries and moans of Sancho, Don Quixote came to himself, and the first word he said was, “He who lives separated from you, sweetest Dulcinea, has greater miseries to endure than these.","산초의 울부짖음과 신음 소리에 돈키호테는 정신을 차렸고, 그가 맨 처음 한 말은 이러했다. \"그대에게서 떨어져 사는 자는, 더없이 어여쁜 둘시네아여, 이런 것들보다 더 큰 비참함을 견뎌야 한다오."],["Aid me, friend Sancho, to mount the enchanted cart, for I am not in a condition to press the saddle of Rocinante, as this shoulder is all knocked to pieces.”","산초 친구여, 저 마법에 걸린 수레에 오르도록 나를 도와다오. 이 어깨가 산산조각이 나서 로시난테의 안장을 짓누를 형편이 못 되니 말이다.\""]],[["“That I will do with all my heart, señor,”","\"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다요, 나리,\" 산초가 말했다."],["said Sancho; “and let us return to our village with these gentlemen, who seek your good, and there we will prepare for making another sally, which may turn out more profitable and creditable to us.”","\"그리고 나리를 위하는 이분들과 함께 우리 마을로 돌아가시지요. 거기서 또 한 번 출정을 준비하면 우리에게 더 득이 되고 더 명예로운 일이 될지도 모르지 않습니까.\""]],[["“Thou art right, Sancho,”","\"네 말이 옳다, 산초,\""],["returned Don Quixote; “It will be wise to let the malign influence of the stars which now prevails pass off.”","돈키호테가 대답했다. \"지금 기승을 부리는 별들의 사악한 기운이 지나가도록 두는 것이 현명한 일이겠지.\""]],[["The canon, the curate, and the barber told him he would act very wisely in doing as he said; and so, highly amused at Sancho Panza’s simplicities, they placed Don Quixote in the cart as before.","신부 참사회원과 신부와 이발사는 그가 말한 대로 하는 것이 참으로 현명한 처사라고 일러 주었다. 그리하여 그들은 산초 판사의 어수룩한 말들에 한껏 즐거워하며 돈키호테를 전처럼 수레에 실었다."],["The procession once more formed itself in order and proceeded on its road; the goatherd took his leave of the party; the officers of the Brotherhood declined to go any farther, and the curate paid them what was due to them; the canon begged the curate to let him know how Don Quixote did, whether he was cured of his madness or still suffered from it, and then begged leave to continue his journey; in short, they all separated and went their ways, leaving to themselves the curate and the barber, Don Quixote, Sancho Panza, and the good Rocinante, who regarded everything with as great resignation as his master.","행렬은 다시금 차례를 갖추어 제 길을 따라 나아갔고, 염소지기는 일행에게 작별을 고했으며, 형제단 관원들은 더 가지 않겠다고 하여 신부가 그들에게 마땅한 삯을 치렀다. 신부 참사회원은 신부에게 돈키호테의 형편이 어떤지, 그의 광기가 나았는지 아니면 여전히 앓고 있는지 알려 달라고 청한 다음, 길을 마저 가게 해 달라고 양해를 구했다."],["The carter yoked his oxen and made Don Quixote comfortable on a truss of hay, and at his usual deliberate pace took the road the curate directed, and at the end of six days they reached Don Quixote’s village, and entered it about the middle of the day, which it so happened was a Sunday, and the people were all in the plaza, through which Don Quixote’s cart passed.","요컨대 그들은 모두 헤어져 제 갈 길을 갔고, 신부와 이발사, 돈키호테와 산초 판사, 그리고 제 주인 못지않게 무던히 모든 것을 받아들이던 착한 로시난테만이 남았다. 수레꾼은 소들에 멍에를 메우고 돈키호테를 건초 더미 위에 편안히 뉘인 뒤, 늘 그렇듯 느릿한 걸음으로 신부가 일러 준 길을 잡아 나아갔으며, 엿새 만에 그들은 돈키호테의 마을에 다다라 한낮 무렵 마을로 들어섰다."],["They all flocked to see what was in the cart, and when they recognised their townsman they were filled with amazement, and a boy ran off to bring the news to his housekeeper and his niece that their master and uncle had come back all lean and yellow and stretched on a truss of hay on an ox-cart.","마침 그날이 일요일이라 사람들이 모두 광장에 나와 있었는데, 돈키호테의 수레가 그 광장을 가로질러 지나갔다. 모두가 수레에 무엇이 실렸는지 보려고 몰려들었고, 그것이 제 고장 사람임을 알아보고는 경이로움에 사로잡혔으니, 한 소년이 달려가 가정부와 조카딸에게 그들의 주인이자 외삼촌이 비쩍 마르고 누렇게 뜬 몰골로 소달구지의 건초 더미 위에 뻗은 채 돌아왔다는 소식을 전했다."],["It was piteous to hear the cries the two good ladies raised, how they beat their breasts and poured out fresh maledictions on those accursed books of chivalry; all which was renewed when they saw Don Quixote coming in at the gate.","그 두 착한 여인이 내지른 울부짖음을, 가슴을 치며 저 저주받은 기사도 책들에 새삼스레 악담을 퍼붓는 모습을 듣자니 참으로 가여웠는데, 그 모든 것이 돈키호테가 문으로 들어서는 것을 보자 다시금 터져 나왔다."]],[["At the news of Don Quixote’s arrival Sancho Panza’s wife came running, for she by this time knew that her husband had gone away with him as his squire, and on seeing Sancho, the first thing she asked him was if the ass was well.","돈키호테가 도착했다는 소식에 산초 판사의 아내가 달려왔으니, 그녀는 이미 제 남편이 그의 종자로 따라나섰다는 것을 알고 있던 터라, 산초를 보자마자 맨 처음 물은 것은 나귀가 잘 있느냐는 것이었다."],["Sancho replied that he was, better than his master was.","산초는 나귀가 잘 있노라고, 주인보다 더 잘 있노라고 대답했다."]],[["“Thanks be to God,”","\"하느님께 감사할 일이네요,\" 그녀가 말했다."],["said she, “for being so good to me; but now tell me, my friend, what have you made by your squirings?","\"내게 이리 잘해 주시니. 하지만 이제 말해 봐요, 여보, 종노릇으로 뭘 벌어 왔어요?"],["What gown have you brought me back?","내게 무슨 치마를 가져왔어요?"],["What shoes for your children?”","애들 신발은요?\""]],[["“I bring nothing of that sort, wife,”","\"그런 건 아무것도 안 가져왔소, 여보,\""],["said Sancho; “though I bring other things of more consequence and value.”","산초가 말했다. \"그래도 더 중하고 값진 다른 것들을 가져왔지.\""]],[["“I am very glad of that,”","\"그거 참 반가운 소리네요,\" 그의 아내가 대꾸했다."],["returned his wife; “show me these things of more value and consequence, my friend; for I want to see them to cheer my heart that has been so sad and heavy all these ages that you have been away.”","\"그 더 값지고 중하다는 것들을 보여 줘요, 여보. 당신이 떠나 있던 그 오랜 세월 내내 슬프고 무겁기만 했던 내 마음을 좀 달래게 보고 싶으니까요.\""]],[["“I will show them to you at home, wife,” said Sancho; “be content for the present; for if it please God that we should again go on our travels in search of adventures, you will soon see me a count, or governor of an island, and that not one of those everyday ones, but the best that is to be had.”","\"집에 가서 보여 주리다, 여보,\" 산초가 말했다. \"지금은 그쯤으로 만족하구려. 하느님 뜻이 닿아 우리가 다시 모험을 찾아 길을 떠나게 된다면, 머지않아 내가 백작이 되거나 어느 섬의 총독이 된 걸 보게 될 테니. 그것도 흔해 빠진 그런 섬이 아니라 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 섬 말이오.\""]],[["“Heaven grant it, husband,”","\"하늘이 그리되게 해 주시기를, 여보,\""],["said she, “for indeed we have need of it.","그녀가 말했다. \"정말이지 우리에게 그게 필요하니까요."],["But tell me, what’s this about islands, for I don’t understand it?”","하지만 말해 봐요, 그 섬이라는 게 대체 무슨 소리예요? 도무지 못 알아듣겠으니.\""]],[["“Honey is not for the mouth of the ass,”","\"꿀이 나귀 입에 어울리는 건 아니지,\" 산초가 대꾸했다."],["returned Sancho; “all in good time thou shalt see, wife--nay, thou wilt be surprised to hear thyself called ‘your ladyship’ by all thy vassals.”","\"때가 되면 다 보게 될 거요, 여보. 아니, 당신은 온 신하들이 당신을 '마님'이라 부르는 걸 듣고 깜짝 놀라게 될 거외다.\""]],[["“What are you talking about, Sancho, with your ladyships, islands, and vassals?”","\"무슨 소리예요, 산초, 마님이니 섬이니 신하니 하면서?\""],["returned Teresa Panza--for so Sancho’s wife was called, though they were not relations, for in La Mancha it is customary for wives to take their husbands’ surnames.","테레사 판사가 대꾸했다 — 산초의 아내는 그렇게 불렸으니, 둘이 친척 사이는 아니었으나, 라만차에서는 아내가 남편의 성을 따르는 것이 관습이었던 까닭이다."]],[["“Don’t be in such a hurry to know all this, Teresa,”","\"이 모든 걸 그리 서둘러 알려고 하지 마오, 테레사,\""],["said Sancho; “it is enough that I am telling you the truth, so shut your mouth.","산초가 말했다. \"내가 당신에게 진실을 말하고 있다는 것으로 족하니, 입을 다물구려."],["But I may tell you this much by the way, that there is nothing in the world more delightful than to be a person of consideration, squire to a knight-errant, and a seeker of adventures.","다만 지나는 말로 이만큼은 일러 줄 수 있소. 세상에 편력 기사의 종자요 모험을 찾아다니는 자로서 존중받는 사람이 되는 것보다 더 즐거운 일은 없다는 것을."],["To be sure most of those one finds do not end as pleasantly as one could wish, for out of a hundred, ninety-nine will turn out cross and contrary.","하기야 마주치는 모험들 대부분이 바라는 만큼 유쾌하게 끝나지는 않지. 백 개 가운데 아흔아홉은 어긋나고 뒤틀려 버리니 말이오."],["I know it by experience, for out of some I came blanketed, and out of others belaboured.","나는 그걸 몸소 겪어 안다오. 어떤 데서는 담요에 싸여 키질을 당해 나왔고, 또 어떤 데서는 흠씬 두들겨 맞고 나왔으니까."],["Still, for all that, it is a fine thing to be on the look-out for what may happen, crossing mountains, searching woods, climbing rocks, visiting castles, putting up at inns, all at free quarters, and devil take the maravedi to pay.”","그래도 그 모든 것에도 불구하고, 무슨 일이 일어날까 살피는 것은 멋진 일이오. 산을 넘고, 숲을 뒤지고, 바위를 기어오르고, 성을 찾아가고, 객줏집에 묵으면서, 그 모든 걸 공짜로 하고, 마라베디 한 푼 낼 일은 악마에게나 던져 주는 거지.\""]],[["While this conversation passed between Sancho Panza and his wife, Don Quixote’s housekeeper and niece took him in and undressed him and laid him in his old bed.","산초 판사와 그의 아내 사이에 이런 이야기가 오가는 동안, 돈키호테의 가정부와 조카딸은 그를 안으로 들여 옷을 벗기고 옛 침대에 뉘었다."],["He eyed them askance, and could not make out where he was.","그는 그들을 곁눈질로 바라보았으나 자기가 어디에 있는지조차 가늠하지 못했다."],["The curate charged his niece to be very careful to make her uncle comfortable and to keep a watch over him lest he should make his escape from them again, telling her what they had been obliged to do to bring him home.","신부는 조카딸에게 외삼촌을 편안히 모시는 데 각별히 마음을 쓰고, 그가 다시 그들에게서 달아나지 못하도록 잘 지켜보라고 단단히 당부하면서, 그를 집으로 데려오기 위해 자기들이 어쩔 수 없이 했던 일을 일러 주었다."],["On this the pair once more lifted up their voices and renewed their maledictions upon the books of chivalry, and implored heaven to plunge the authors of such lies and nonsense into the midst of the bottomless pit.","이 말에 두 여인은 다시금 목소리를 높여 기사도 책들에 악담을 퍼붓고, 그런 거짓말과 헛소리를 지어낸 자들을 바닥 모를 구렁텅이 한가운데로 처넣어 달라고 하늘에 빌었다."],["They were, in short, kept in anxiety and dread lest their uncle and master should give them the slip the moment he found himself somewhat better, and as they feared so it fell out.","요컨대 그들은 외삼촌이자 주인이 몸이 좀 나아지는 순간 자기들을 따돌리고 빠져나가지 않을까 하는 근심과 두려움에 사로잡혀 있었는데, 그들이 두려워하던 그대로 일은 벌어지고 말았다."]],[["But the author of this history, though he has devoted research and industry to the discovery of the deeds achieved by Don Quixote in his third sally, has been unable to obtain any information respecting them, at any rate derived from authentic documents; tradition has merely preserved in the memory of La Mancha the fact that Don Quixote, the third time he sallied forth from his home, betook himself to Saragossa, where he was present at some famous jousts which came off in that city, and that he had adventures there worthy of his valour and high intelligence. Of his end and death he could learn no particulars, nor would he have ascertained it or known of it, if good fortune had not produced an old physician for him who had in his possession a leaden box, which, according to his account, had been discovered among the crumbling foundations of an ancient hermitage that was being rebuilt; in which box were found certain parchment manuscripts in Gothic character, but in Castilian verse, containing many of his achievements, and setting forth the beauty of Dulcinea, the form of Rocinante, the fidelity of Sancho Panza, and the burial of Don Quixote himself, together with sundry epitaphs and eulogies on his life and character; but all that could be read and deciphered were those which the trustworthy author of this new and unparalleled history here presents. And the said author asks of those that shall read it nothing in return for the vast toil which it has cost him in examining and searching the Manchegan archives in order to bring it to light, save that they give him the same credit that people of sense give to the books of chivalry that pervade the world and are so popular; for with this he will consider himself amply paid and fully satisfied, and will be encouraged to seek out and produce other histories, if not as truthful, at least equal in invention and not less entertaining. The first words written on the parchment found in the leaden box were these:","그러나 이 이야기의 작가는 돈키호테가 세 번째 출정에서 이룬 행적을 밝혀내고자 연구와 노고를 쏟아부었음에도, 적어도 믿을 만한 문헌에서 비롯된 정보라고 할 만한 것은 그 행적에 관하여 무엇 하나 얻어 내지 못했다. 다만 전승이 라만차의 기억 속에 보존해 온 사실이라고는, 돈키호테가 세 번째로 집을 나섰을 때 사라고사로 발걸음을 옮겨, 그 도시에서 열린 몇몇 이름난 마상 시합에 참석했으며, 거기서 그의 용맹과 드높은 지혜에 걸맞은 모험들을 겪었다는 것뿐이다. 그의 최후와 죽음에 관하여는 어떤 자세한 내막도 알아낼 수 없었을 것이며, 만일 행운이 그에게 한 늙은 의사를 보내 주지 않았던들 그것을 확인하지도, 알지도 못했으리라. 그 의사가 한 납 상자를 가지고 있었는데, 그의 말에 따르면 그 상자는 다시 짓고 있던 어느 옛 암자의 허물어진 기초 사이에서 발견되었다고 한다. 그 상자 안에서는 고딕 문자로 쓰였으되 카스티야어 운문으로 된 양피지 사본 몇 점이 나왔는데, 거기에는 그의 행적 가운데 많은 것이 담겨 있었고, 둘시네아의 아름다움과 로시난테의 생김새, 산초 판사의 충직함, 그리고 돈키호테 자신의 매장이, 그의 생애와 성품에 관한 갖가지 묘비명과 찬사와 더불어 적혀 있었다. 그러나 읽고 판독할 수 있었던 것이라고는, 이 새롭고도 견줄 데 없는 이야기의 믿음직한 작가가 여기 내놓는 것들뿐이었다. 그리고 이 작가는, 그것을 세상에 드러내기 위하여 라만차의 문서고를 살피고 뒤지는 데 들인 막대한 노고에 대하여, 그것을 읽을 이들에게 그 어떤 보답도 바라지 않거니와, 다만 세상에 널리 퍼져 그토록 인기를 누리는 기사도 책들에 분별 있는 이들이 보내는 것과 똑같은 신뢰를 자기에게도 보내 주기만을 바랄 뿐이다. 이만하면 그는 스스로 넉넉히 보상받았고 충분히 만족했다고 여길 것이며, 비록 이만큼 진실하지는 못할지언정 적어도 착상에서는 견줄 만하고 재미에서도 못지않은 다른 이야기들을 찾아내어 내놓을 용기를 얻을 것이다. 납 상자에서 발견된 양피지에 맨 처음 적혀 있던 말은 이러했다."]],[["THE ACADEMICIANS OF ARGAMASILLA, A VILLAGE OF LA MANCHA, ON THE LIFE AND DEATH OF DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA, HOC SCRIPSERUNT MONICONGO, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA,","라만차의 한 마을 아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원들이 라만차의 돈키호테의 삶과 죽음에 관하여 이를 적었노라 아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원, 모니콩고,"]],[["# ON THE TOMB OF DON QUIXOTE EPITAPH","# ON THE TOMB OF DON QUIXOTE EPITAPH"]],[["The scatterbrain that gave La Mancha more Rich spoils than Jason’s; who a point so keen Had to his wit, and happier far had been If his wit’s weathercock a blunter bore; The arm renowned far as Gaeta’s shore, Cathay, and all the lands that lie between; The muse discreet and terrible in mien As ever wrote on brass in days of yore; He who surpassed the Amadises all, And who as naught the Galaors accounted, Supported by his love and gallantry: Who made the Belianises sing small, And sought renown on Rocinante mounted; Here, underneath this cold stone, doth he lie.","돈키호테의 무덤에 부쳐 묘비명 이아손의 것보다도 더 풍성한 노획물을 라만차에 안겨 준 그 얼빠진 자, 그 재치 어찌나 날카로웠던지, 차라리 그 재치의 풍향계가 더 무딘 데를 가리켰던들 더 행복했으리. 가에타의 기슭만큼, 카타이만큼, 그 사이에 놓인 온 땅만큼 멀리 떨치던 그 팔뚝. 옛적에 청동에 새겼던 그 어떤 시신(詩神)만큼 신중하고도 그 위세 무시무시한 시신. 사랑과 무용에 떠받쳐 모든 아마디스를 능가하고, 갈라오르 무리를 한낱 아무것도 아닌 듯 여기며, 벨리아니스 무리를 풀 죽게 만들고, 로시난테에 올라 명성을 좇던 그가, 여기, 이 차가운 돌 아래에 누워 있노라."]],[["PANIAGUADO, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, IN LAUDEM DULCINEAE DEL TOBOSO","아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원, 파니아구아도, 엘 토보소의 둘시네아를 기리며"]],[["She, whose full features may be here descried, High-bosomed, with a bearing of disdain, Is Dulcinea, she for whom in vain The great Don Quixote of La Mancha sighed.","소네트 여기 그려진 두툼한 이목구비, 봉긋한 가슴, 멸시하는 듯한 자태를 지닌 그녀는 둘시네아, 위대한 라만차의 돈키호테가 그녀를 위하여 헛되이 한숨짓던 바로 그녀라."],["For her, Toboso’s queen, from side to side He traversed the grim sierra, the champaign Of Aranjuez, and Montiel’s famous plain: On Rocinante oft a weary ride.","엘 토보소의 여왕인 그녀를 위하여 그는 이 끝에서 저 끝까지, 험준한 산맥과 아랑후에스의 들판, 몬티엘의 이름난 벌판을 로시난테에 올라 곧잘 고달프게 누볐노라."],["Malignant planets, cruel destiny, Pursued them both, the fair Manchegan dame, And the unconquered star of chivalry.","사악한 별들, 잔혹한 운명이 저 아름다운 라만차의 부인과 정복되지 않는 기사도의 별, 그 둘을 뒤쫓았더라."],["Nor youth nor beauty saved her from the claim Of death; he paid love’s bitter penalty, And left the marble to preserve his name.","젊음도 미모도 그녀를 죽음의 손아귀에서 구해 내지 못하였고, 그는 사랑의 쓰디쓴 대가를 치르고 제 이름을 간직하라 대리석을 남겼노라."]],[["CAPRICHOSO, A MOST ACUTE ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, IN PRAISE OF ROCINANTE, STEED OF DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA","아르가마시야의 더없이 예리한 아카데미 회원, 카프리초소, 라만차의 돈키호테의 군마 로시난테를 기리며"]],[["On that proud throne of diamantine sheen, Which the blood-reeking feet of Mars degrade, The mad Manchegan’s banner now hath been By him in all its bravery displayed.","소네트 전쟁의 신 마르스의 피 냄새 풍기는 발이 욕보이는, 저 금강석처럼 빛나는 드높은 옥좌 위에, 이제 미친 라만차 사나이의 깃발이 그에 의해 그 온갖 위풍으로 펄럭였노라."],["There hath he hung his arms and trenchant blade Wherewith, achieving deeds till now unseen, He slays, lays low, cleaves, hews; but art hath made A novel style for our new paladin.","거기에 그는 제 무기와 잘 드는 칼날을 걸었으니, 이제껏 보지 못한 위업을 이루며 그것으로 그는 베고, 쓰러뜨리고, 가르고, 찍었더라. 허나 무예는 우리의 새 용사를 위한 새로운 양식을 빚어냈도다."],["If Amadis be the proud boast of Gaul, If by his progeny the fame of Greece Through all the regions of the earth be spread, Great Quixote crowned in grim Bellona’s hall To-day exalts La Mancha over these, And above Greece or Gaul she holds her head.","아마디스가 갈리아의 드높은 자랑이라면, 그의 후예들로 그리스의 명성이 온 누리의 방방곡곡에 퍼졌다면, 무시무시한 벨로나의 전당에서 왕관을 쓴 위대한 키호테는 오늘 이들 위에 라만차를 드높이며, 그녀는 그리스도 갈리아도 위에 고개를 쳐드노라."],["Nor ends his glory here, for his good steed Doth Brillador and Bayard far exceed; As mettled steeds compared with Rocinante, The reputation they have won is scanty.","그의 영광은 여기서 그치지 않으니, 그의 좋은 군마는 브리야도르와 바야르를 한참 능가하는도다. 혈기 넘치는 군마들도 로시난테에 견주면, 그들이 얻은 명성은 보잘것없으니."]],[["The worthy Sancho Panza here you see; A great soul once was in that body small, Nor was there squire upon this earthly ball So plain and simple, or of guile so free.","아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원, 부를라도르, 산초 판사에 관하여 소네트 여기 그대가 보는 이는 갸륵한 산초 판사라. 그 작은 몸뚱이 안에 한때 큰 영혼이 깃들었고, 이 지상의 공 위에 그토록 꾸밈없고 소박하며 간계라곤 없는 종자는 다시 없었더라."],["Within an ace of being Count was he, And would have been but for the spite and gall Of this vile age, mean and illiberal, That cannot even let a donkey be.","백작이 되기 일보 직전까지 갔으니, 이 비열하고 옹졸하며 천박한 시대의 악의와 앙심만 아니었던들 백작이 되었으련만, 이 시대는 나귀 한 마리조차 가만두지 못하는도다."],["For mounted on an ass (excuse the word), By Rocinante’s side this gentle squire Was wont his wandering master to attend.","나귀에 올라(이 말을 용서하시라) 로시난테의 곁에서 이 온순한 종자는 떠도는 제 주인을 따르곤 하였더라."],["Delusive hopes that lure the common herd With promises of ease, the heart’s desire, In shadows, dreams, and smoke ye always end.","편안함과 마음의 소망을 약속하며 무리를 꾀어내는 헛된 희망이여, 너희는 늘 그림자와 꿈과 연기 속에서 스러지는도다."]],[["CACHIDIABLO, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DON QUIXOTE EPITAPH","아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원, 카치디아블로, 돈키호테의 무덤에 부쳐 묘비명"]],[["# CACHIDIABLO, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DON QUIXOTE EPITAPH","# CACHIDIABLO, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DON QUIXOTE EPITAPH"]],[["The knight lies here below, Ill-errant and bruised sore, Whom Rocinante bore In his wanderings to and fro.","여기 이 아래 기사가 누워 있으니, 편력에 서툴고 호되게 멍든 자라, 로시난테가 그를 태우고 이리저리 떠돌았더라."],["By the side of the knight is laid Stolid man Sancho too, Than whom a squire more true Was not in the esquire trade.","기사의 곁에는 우직한 사내 산초 또한 뉘어 있으니, 종자라는 생업에서 그보다 더 진실한 종자는 없었더라."]],[["TIQUITOC, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DULCINEA DEL TOBOSO","아르가마시야의 아카데미 회원, 티키톡, 엘 토보소의 둘시네아의 무덤에 부쳐"]],[["# TIQUITOC, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DULCINEA DEL TOBOSO","# TIQUITOC, ACADEMICIAN OF ARGAMASILLA, ON THE TOMB OF DULCINEA DEL TOBOSO"]],[["EPITAPH Here Dulcinea lies.","묘비명 여기 둘시네아가 누워 있노라."],["Plump was she and robust: Now she is ashes and dust: The end of all flesh that dies.","토실토실하고 튼실하던 그녀, 이제는 재요 티끌이니, 죽는 모든 육신의 끝이라."],["A lady of high degree, With the port of a lofty dame, And the great Don Quixote’s flame, And the pride of her village was she.","드높은 신분의 부인으로, 당당한 귀부인의 자태를 지니고, 위대한 돈키호테의 연정의 대상이요, 제 마을의 자랑이었더라."]],[["These were all the verses that could be deciphered; the rest, the writing being worm-eaten, were handed over to one of the Academicians to make out their meaning conjecturally. We have been informed that at the cost of many sleepless nights and much toil he has succeeded, and that he means to publish them in hopes of Don Quixote’s third sally.","이것이 판독해 낼 수 있었던 시구의 전부였다. 나머지는 글씨가 좀먹은 까닭에, 그 뜻을 어림으로 헤아려 보라고 아카데미 회원 가운데 한 사람에게 넘겨졌다. 우리가 전해 들은 바로는, 그가 숱한 불면의 밤과 많은 노고를 치른 끝에 그 일을 해냈으며, 돈키호테의 세 번째 출정을 기대하며 그것을 펴낼 작정이라고 한다. \"어쩌면 다른 이가 더 나은 가락으로 노래하리라.\" * 포르세 알트로 칸테라 콘 밀리오르 플레트로(Forse altro cantera con miglior plectro): 이탈리아어로 \"어쩌면 다른 이가 더 나은 채(撥)로 노래하리라\"라는 뜻으로, 아리오스토의 「광란의 오를란도」에 나오는 구절."]]];
